if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-4599"]="# CHAPTER I. THE SQUIRE OF ALLINGTON.\n\nOf course there was a Great House at Allington. How otherwise should there have been a Small House? Our story will, as its name imports, have its closest relations with those who lived in the less dignified domicile of the two; but it will have close relations also with the more dignified, and it may be well that I should, in the first instance, say a few words as to the Great House and its owner.\n\nThe squires of Allington had been squires of Allington since squires, such as squires are now, were first known in England. From father to son, and from uncle to nephew, and, in one instance, from second cousin to second cousin, the sceptre had descended in the family of the Dales; and the acres had remained intact, growing in value and not decreasing in number, though guarded by no entail and protected by no wonderful amount of prudence or wisdom. The estate of Dale of Allington had been coterminous with the parish of Allington for some hundreds of years; and though, as I have said, the race of squires had possessed nothing of superhuman discretion, and had perhaps been guided in their walks through life by no very distinct principles, still there had been with them so much of adherence to a sacred law, that no acre of the property had ever been parted from the hands of the existing squire. Some futile attempts had been made to increase the territory, as indeed had been done by Kit Dale, the father of Christopher Dale, who will appear as our squire of Allington when the persons of our drama are introduced. Old Kit Dale, who had married money, had bought outlying farms,--a bit of ground here and a bit there,--talking, as he did so, much of political influence and of the good old Tory cause. But these farms and bits of ground had gone again before our time. To them had been attached no religion. When old Kit had found himself pressed in that matter of the majority of the Nineteenth Dragoons, in which crack regiment his second son made for himself quite a career, he found it easier to sell than to save--seeing that that which he sold was his own and not the patrimony of the Dales. At his death the remainder of these purchases had gone. Family arrangements required completion, and Christopher Dale required ready money. The outlying farms flew away, as such new purchases had flown before; but the old patrimony of the Dales remained untouched, as it had ever remained.\n\nIt had been a religion among them; and seeing that the worship had been carried on without fail, that the vestal fire had never gone down upon the hearth, I should not have said that the Dales had walked their ways without high principle. To this religion they had all adhered, and the new heir had ever entered in upon his domain without other encumbrances than those with which he himself was then already burdened. And yet there had been no entail. The idea of an entail was not in accordance with the peculiarities of the Dale mind. It was necessary to the Dale religion that each squire should have the power of wasting the acres of Allington,--and that he should abstain from wasting them. I remember to have dined at a house, the whole glory and fortune of which depended on the safety of a glass goblet. We all know the story. If the luck of Edenhall should be shattered, the doom of the family would be sealed. Nevertheless I was bidden to drink out of the fatal glass, as were all guests in that house. It would not have contented the chivalrous mind of the master to protect his doom by lock and key and padded chest. And so it was with the Dales of Allington. To them an entail would have been a lock and key and a padded chest; but the old chivalry of their house denied to them the use of such protection.\n\nI have spoken something slightingly of the acquirements and doings of the family; and indeed their acquirements had been few and their doings little. At Allington, Dale of Allington had always been known as a king. At Guestwick, the neighbouring market town, he was a great man--to be seen frequently on Saturdays, standing in the market-place, and laying down the law as to barley and oxen among men who knew usually more about barley and oxen than did he. At Hamersham, the assize town, he was generally in some repute, being a constant grand juror for the county, and a man who paid his way. But even at Hamersham the glory of the Dales had, at most periods, begun to pale, for they had seldom been widely conspicuous in the county, and had earned no great reputation by their knowledge of jurisprudence in the grand jury room. Beyond Hamersham their fame had not spread itself.\n\nThey had been men generally built in the same mould, inheriting each from his father the same virtues and the same vices,--men who would have lived, each, as his father had lived before him, had not the new ways of the world gradually drawn away with them, by an invisible magnetism, the upcoming Dale of the day,--not indeed in any case so moving him as to bring him up to the spirit of the age in which he lived, but dragging him forward to a line in advance of that on which his father had trodden. They had been obstinate men; believing much in themselves; just according to their ideas of justice; hard to their tenants--but not known to be hard even by the tenants themselves, for the rules followed had ever been the rules on the Allington estate; imperious to their wives and children, but imperious within bounds, so that no Mrs. Dale had fled from her lord's roof, and no loud scandals had existed between father and sons; exacting in their ideas as to money, expecting that they were to receive much and to give little, and yet not thought to be mean, for they paid their way, and gave money in parish charity and in county charity. They had ever been steady supporters of the Church, graciously receiving into their parish such new vicars as, from time to time, were sent to them from King's College, Cambridge, to which establishment the gift of the living belonged;--but, nevertheless, the Dales had ever carried on some unpronounced warfare against the clergyman, so that the intercourse between the lay family and the clerical had seldom been in all respects pleasant.\n\nSuch had been the Dales of Allington, time out of mind, and such in all respects would have been the Christopher Dale of our time, had he not suffered two accidents in his youth. He had fallen in love with a lady who obstinately refused his hand, and on her account he had remained single; that was his first accident. The second had fallen upon him with reference to his father's assumed wealth. He had supposed himself to be richer than other Dales of Allington when coming in upon his property, and had consequently entertained an idea of sitting in Parliament for his county. In order that he might attain this honour he had allowed himself to be talked by the men of Hamersham and Guestwick out of his old family politics, and had declared himself a Liberal. He had never gone to the poll, and, indeed, had never actually stood for the seat. But he had come forward as a liberal politician, and had failed; and, although it was well known to all around that Christopher Dale was in heart as thoroughly conservative as any of his forefathers, this accident had made him sour and silent on the subject of politics, and had somewhat estranged him from his brother squires.\n\nIn other respects our Christopher Dale was, if anything, superior to the average of the family. Those whom he did love he loved dearly. Those whom he hated he did not ill-use beyond the limits of justice. He was close in small matters of money, and yet in certain family arrangements he was, as we shall see, capable of much liberality. He endeavoured to do his duty in accordance with his lights, and had succeeded in weaning himself from personal indulgences, to which during the early days of his high hopes he had become accustomed. And in that matter of his unrequited love he had been true throughout. In his hard, dry, unpleasant way he had loved the woman; and when at last he learned to know that she would not have his love, he had been unable to transfer his heart to another. This had happened just at the period of his father's death, and he had endeavoured to console himself with politics, with what fate we have already seen. A constant, upright, and by no means insincere man was our Christopher Dale,--thin and meagre in his mental attributes, by no means even understanding the fulness of a full man, with power of eye-sight very limited in seeing aught which was above him, but yet worthy of regard in that he had realized a path of duty and did endeavour to walk therein. And, moreover, our Mr. Christopher Dale was a gentleman.\n\nSuch in character was the squire of Allington, the only regular inhabitant of the Great House. In person, he was a plain, dry man, with short grizzled hair and thick grizzled eyebrows. Of beard, he had very little, carrying the smallest possible gray whiskers, which hardly fell below the points of his ears. His eyes were sharp and expressive, and his nose was straight and well formed,--as was also his chin. But the nobility of his face was destroyed by a mean mouth with thin lips; and his forehead, which was high and narrow, though it forbad you to take Mr. Dale for a fool, forbad you also to take him for a man of great parts, or of a wide capacity. In height, he was about five feet ten; and at the time of our story was as near to seventy as he was to sixty. But years had treated him very lightly, and he bore few signs of age. Such in person was Christopher Dale, Esq., the squire of Allington, and owner of some three thousand a year, all of which proceeded from the lands of that parish.\n\nAnd now I will speak of the Great House of Allington. After all, it was not very great; nor was it surrounded by much of that exquisite nobility of park appurtenance which graces the habitations of most of our old landed proprietors. But the house itself was very graceful. It had been built in the days of the early Stuarts, in that style of architecture to which we give the name of the Tudors. On its front it showed three pointed roofs, or gables, as I believe they should be called; and between each gable a thin tall chimney stood, the two chimneys thus raising themselves just above the three peaks I have mentioned. I think that the beauty of the house depended much on those two chimneys; on them, and on the mullioned windows with which the front of the house was closely filled. The door, with its jutting porch, was by no means in the centre of the house. As you entered, there was but one window on your right hand, while on your left there were three. And over these there was a line of five windows, one taking its place above the porch. We all know the beautiful old Tudor window, with its stout stone mullions and its stone transoms, crossing from side to side at a point much nearer to the top than to the bottom. Of all windows ever invented it is the sweetest. And here, at Allington, I think their beauty was enhanced by the fact that they were not regular in their shape. Some of these windows were long windows, while some of them were high. That to the right of the door, and that at the other extremity of the house, were among the former. But the others had been put in without regard to uniformity, a long window here, and a high window there, with a general effect which could hardly have been improved. Then above, in the three gables, were three other smaller apertures. But these also were mullioned, and the entire frontage of the house was uniform in its style.\n\nRound the house there were trim gardens, not very large, but worthy of much note in that they were so trim,--gardens with broad gravel paths, with one walk running in front of the house so broad as to be fitly called a terrace. But this, though in front of the house, was sufficiently removed from it to allow of a coach-road running inside it to the front door. The Dales of Allington had always been gardeners, and their garden was perhaps more noted in the county than any other of their properties. But outside the gardens no pretensions had been made to the grandeur of a domain. The pastures round the house were but pretty fields, in which timber was abundant. There was no deer-park at Allington; and though the Allington woods were well known, they formed no portion of a whole of which the house was a part. They lay away, out of sight, a full mile from the back of the house; but not on that account of less avail for the fitting preservation of foxes.\n\nAnd the house stood much too near the road for purposes of grandeur, had such purposes ever swelled the breast of any of the squires of Allington. But I fancy that our ideas of rural grandeur have altered since many of our older country seats were built. To be near the village, so as in some way to afford comfort, protection, and patronage, and perhaps also with some view to the pleasantness of neighbourhood for its own inmates, seemed to be the object of a gentleman when building his house in the old days. A solitude in the centre of a wide park is now the only site that can be recognized as eligible. No cottage must be seen, unless the cottage orné of the gardener. The village, if it cannot be abolished, must be got out of sight. The sound of the church bells is not desirable, and the road on which the profane vulgar travel by their own right must be at a distance. When some old Dale of Allington built his house, he thought differently. There stood the church and there the village, and, pleased with such vicinity, he sat himself down close to his God and to his tenants.\n\nAs you pass along the road from Guestwick into the village you see the church near to you on your left hand; but the house is hidden from the road. As you approach the church, reaching the gate of it which is not above two hundred yards from the high road, you see the full front of the Great House. Perhaps the best view of it is from the churchyard. The lane leading up to the church ends in a gate, which is the entrance into Mr. Dale's place. There is no lodge there, and the gate generally stands open,--indeed, always does so, unless some need of cattle grazing within requires that it should be closed. But there is an inner gate, leading from the home paddock through the gardens to the house, and another inner gate, some thirty yards farther on, which will take you into the farm-yard. Perhaps it is a defect at Allington that the farm-yard is very close to the house. But the stables, and the straw-yards, and the unwashed carts, and the lazy lingering cattle of the homestead, are screened off by a row of chestnuts, which, when in its glory of flower, in the early days of May, no other row in England can surpass in beauty. Had any one told Dale of Allington--this Dale or any former Dale--that his place wanted wood, he would have pointed with mingled pride and disdain to his belt of chestnuts.\n\nOf the church itself I will say the fewest possible number of words. It was a church such as there are, I think, thousands in England--low, incommodious, kept with difficulty in repair, too often pervious to the wet, and yet strangely picturesque, and correct too, according to great rules of architecture. It was built with a nave and aisles, visibly in the form of a cross, though with its arms clipped down to the trunk, with a separate chancel, with a large square short tower, and with a bell-shaped spire, covered with lead and irregular in its proportions. Who does not know the low porch, the perpendicular Gothic window, the flat-roofed aisles, and the noble old gray tower of such a church as this? As regards its interior, it was dusty; it was blocked up with high-backed ugly pews; the gallery in which the children sat at the end of the church, and in which two ancient musicians blew their bassoons, was all awry, and looked as though it would fall; the pulpit was an ugly useless edifice, as high nearly as the roof would allow, and the reading-desk under it hardly permitted the parson to keep his head free from the dangling tassels of the cushion above him. A clerk also was there beneath him, holding a third position somewhat elevated; and upon the whole things there were not quite as I would have had them. But, nevertheless, the place looked like a church, and I can hardly say so much for all the modern edifices which have been built in my days towards the glory of God. It looked like a church, and not the less so because in walking up the passage between the pews the visitor trod upon the brass plates which dignified the resting-places of the departed Dales of old.\n\nBelow the church, and between that and the village, stood the vicarage, in such position that the small garden of the vicarage stretched from the churchyard down to the backs of the village cottages. This was a pleasant residence, newly built within the last thirty years, and creditable to the ideas of comfort entertained by the rich collegiate body from which the vicars of Allington always came. Doubtless we shall in the course of our sojourn at Allington visit the vicarage now and then, but I do not know that any further detailed account of its comforts will be necessary to us.\n\nPassing by the lane leading to the vicarage, the church and to the house, the high road descends rapidly to a little brook which runs through the village. On the right as you descend you will have seen the \"Red Lion,\" and will have seen no other house conspicuous in any way. At the bottom, close to the brook, is the post-office, kept surely by the crossest old woman in all those parts. Here the road passes through the water, the accommodation of a narrow wooden bridge having been afforded for those on foot. But before passing the stream, you will see a cross street, running to the left, as had run that other lane leading to the house. Here, as this cross street rises the hill, are the best houses in the village. The baker lives here, and that respectable woman, Mrs. Frummage, who sells ribbons, and toys, and soap, and straw bonnets, with many other things too long to mention. Here, too, lives an apothecary, whom the veneration of this and neighbouring parishes has raised to the dignity of a doctor. And here also, in the smallest but prettiest cottage that can be imagined, lives Mrs. Hearn, the widow of a former vicar, on terms, however, with her neighbour the squire which I regret to say are not as friendly as they should be. Beyond this lady's modest residence, Allington Street, for so the road is called, turns suddenly round towards the church, and at the point of the turn is a pretty low iron railing with a gate, and with a covered way, which leads up to the front door of the house which stands there. I will only say here, at this fag end of a chapter, that it is the Small House at Allington. Allington Street, as I have said, turns short round towards the church at this point, and there ends at a white gate, leading into the churchyard by a second entrance.\n\nSo much it was needful that I should say of Allington Great House, of the Squire, and of the village. Of the Small House, I will speak separately in a further chapter.\n\n# CHAPTER II. THE TWO PEARLS OF ALLINGTON.\n\n\"But Mr. Crosbie is only a mere clerk.\"\n\nThis sarcastic condemnation was spoken by Miss Lilian Dale to her sister Isabella, and referred to a gentleman with whom we shall have much concern in these pages. I do not say that Mr. Crosbie will be our hero, seeing that that part in the drama will be cut up, as it were, into fragments. Whatever of the magnificent may be produced will be diluted and apportioned out in very moderate quantities among two or more, probably among three or four, young gentlemen--to none of whom will be vouchsafed the privilege of much heroic action.\n\n\"I don't know what you call a mere clerk, Lily. Mr. Fanfaron is a mere barrister, and Mr. Boyce is a mere clergyman.\" Mr. Boyce was the vicar of Allington, and Mr. Fanfaron was a lawyer who had made his way over to Allington during the last assizes. \"You might as well say that Lord De Guest is a mere earl.\"\n\n\"So he is--only a mere earl. Had he ever done anything except have fat oxen, one wouldn't say so. You know what I mean by a mere clerk? It isn't much in a man to be in a public office, and yet Mr. Crosbie gives himself airs.\"\n\n\"You don't suppose that Mr. Crosbie is the same as John Eames,\" said Bell, who, by her tone of voice, did not seem inclined to undervalue the qualifications of Mr. Crosbie. Now John Eames was a young man from Guestwick, who had been appointed to a clerkship in the Income-tax Office, with eighty pounds a year, two years ago.\n\n\"Then Johnny Eames is a mere clerk,\" said Lily; \"and Mr. Crosbie is-- After all, Bell, what is Mr. Crosbie, if he is not a mere clerk? Of course, he is older than John Eames; and, as he has been longer at it, I suppose he has more than eighty pounds a year.\"\n\n\"I am not in Mr. Crosbie's confidence. He is in the General Committee Office, I know; and, I believe, has pretty nearly the management of the whole of it. I have heard Bernard say that he has six or seven young men under him, and that--; but, of course, I don't know what he does at his office.\"\n\n\"I'll tell you what he is, Bell; Mr. Crosbie is a swell.\" And Lilian Dale was right; Mr. Crosbie was a swell.\n\nAnd here I may perhaps best explain who Bernard was, and who was Mr. Crosbie. Captain Bernard Dale was an officer in the corps of Engineers, was the first cousin of the two girls who have been speaking, and was nephew and heir presumptive to the squire. His father, Colonel Dale, and his mother, Lady Fanny Dale, were still living at Torquay--an effete, invalid, listless couple, pretty well dead to all the world beyond the region of the Torquay card-tables. He it was who had made for himself quite a career in the Nineteenth Dragoons. This he did by eloping with the penniless daughter of that impoverished earl, the Lord De Guest. After the conclusion of that event circumstances had not afforded him the opportunity of making himself conspicuous; and he had gone on declining gradually in the world's esteem--for the world had esteemed him when he first made good his running with the Lady Fanny--till now, in his slippered years, he and his Lady Fanny were unknown except among those Torquay Bath chairs and card-tables. His elder brother was still a hearty man, walking in thick shoes, and constant in his saddle; but the colonel, with nothing beyond his wife's title to keep his body awake, had fallen asleep somewhat prematurely among his slippers. Of him and of Lady Fanny, Bernard Dale was the only son. Daughters they had had; some were dead, some married, and one living with them among the card-tables. Of his parents Bernard had latterly not seen much; not more, that is, than duty and a due attention to the fifth commandment required of him. He also was making a career for himself, having obtained a commission in the Engineers, and being known to all his compeers as the nephew of an earl, and as the heir to a property of three thousand a year. And when I say that Bernard Dale was not inclined to throw away any of these advantages, I by no means intend to speak in his dispraise. The advantage of being heir to a good property is so manifest--the advantages over and beyond those which are merely fiscal--that no man thinks of throwing them away, or expects another man to do so. Moneys in possession or in expectation do give a set to the head, and a confidence to the voice, and an assurance to the man, which will help him much in his walk in life--if the owner of them will simply use them, and not abuse them. And for Bernard Dale I will say that he did not often talk of his uncle the earl. He was conscious that his uncle was an earl, and that other men knew the fact. He knew that he would not otherwise have been elected at the Beaufort, or at that most aristocratic of little clubs called Sebright's. When noble blood was called in question he never alluded specially to his own, but he knew how to speak as one of whom all the world was aware on which side he had been placed by the circumstances of his birth. Thus he used his advantage, and did not abuse it. And in his profession he had been equally fortunate. By industry, by a small but wakeful intelligence, and by some aid from patronage, he had got on till he had almost achieved the reputation of talent. His name had become known among scientific experimentalists, not as that of one who had himself invented a cannon or an antidote to a cannon, but as of a man understanding in cannons and well fitted to look at those invented by others; who would honestly test this or that antidote; or, if not honestly, seeing that such thin-minded men can hardly go to the proof of any matter without some pre-judgment in their minds, at any rate with such appearance of honesty that the world might be satisfied. And in this way Captain Dale was employed much at home, about London; and was not called on to build barracks in Nova Scotia, or to make roads in the Punjaub.\n\nHe was a small slight man, smaller than his uncle, but in face very like him. He had the same eyes, and nose, and chin, and the same mouth; but his forehead was better,--less high and pointed, and better formed about the brows. And then he wore moustaches, which somewhat hid the thinness of his mouth. On the whole, he was not ill-looking; and, as I have said before, he carried with him an air of self-assurance and a confident balance, which in itself gives a grace to a young man.\n\nHe was staying at the present time in his uncle's house, during the delicious warmth of the summer,--for, as yet, the month of July was not all past; and his intimate friend, Adolphus Crosbie, who was or was not a mere clerk as my readers may choose to form their own opinions on that matter, was a guest in the house with him. I am inclined to say that Adolphus Crosbie was not a mere clerk; and I do not think that he would have been so called, even by Lily Dale, had he not given signs to her that he was a \"swell.\" Now a man in becoming a swell,--a swell of such an order as could possibly be known to Lily Dale,--must have ceased to be a mere clerk in that very process. And, moreover, Captain Dale would not have been Damon to any Pythias, of whom it might fairly be said that he was a mere clerk. Nor could any mere clerk have got himself in either at the Beaufort or at Sebright's. The evidence against that former assertion made by Lily Dale is very strong; but then the evidence as to her latter assertion is as strong. Mr. Crosbie certainly was a swell. It is true that he was a clerk in the General Committee Office. But then, in the first place, the General Committee Office is situated in Whitehall; whereas poor John Eames was forced to travel daily from his lodgings in Burton Crescent, ever so far beyond Russell Square, to his dingy room in Somerset House. And Adolphus Crosbie, when very young, had been a private secretary, and had afterwards mounted up in his office to some quasi authority and senior-clerkship, bringing him in seven hundred a year, and giving him a status among assistant secretaries and the like, which even in an official point of view was something. But the triumphs of Adolphus Crosbie had been other than these. Not because he had been intimate with assistant secretaries, and was allowed in Whitehall a room to himself with an arm-chair, would he have been entitled to stand upon the rug at Sebright's and speak while rich men listened,--rich men, and men also who had handles to their names! Adolphus Crosbie had done more than make minutes with discretion on the papers of the General Committee Office. He had set himself down before the gates of the city of fashion, and had taken them by storm; or, perhaps, to speak with more propriety, he had picked the locks and let himself in. In his walks of life he was somebody in London. A man at the West End who did not know who was Adolphus Crosbie knew nothing. I do not say that he was the intimate friend of many great men; but even great men acknowledged the acquaintance of Adolphus Crosbie, and he was to be seen in the drawing-rooms, or at any rate on the staircases, of Cabinet Ministers.\n\nLilian Dale, dear Lily Dale--for my reader must know that she is to be very dear, and that my story will be nothing to him if he do not love Lily Dale--Lilian Dale had discovered that Mr. Crosbie was a swell. But I am bound to say that Mr. Crosbie did not habitually proclaim the fact in any offensive manner; nor in becoming a swell had he become altogether a bad fellow. It was not to be expected that a man who was petted at Sebright's should carry himself in the Allington drawing-room as would Johnny Eames, who had never been petted by any one but his mother. And this fraction of a hero of ours had other advantages to back him, over and beyond those which fashion had given him. He was a tall, well-looking man, with pleasant eyes and an expressive mouth,--a man whom you would probably observe in whatever room you might meet him. And he knew how to talk, and had in him something which justified talking. He was no butterfly or dandy, who flew about in the world's sun, warmed into prettiness by a sunbeam. Crosbie had his opinion on things,--on politics, on religion, on the philanthropic tendencies of the age, and had read something here and there as he formed his opinion. Perhaps he might have done better in the world had he not been placed so early in life in that Whitehall public office. There was that in him which might have earned better bread for him in an open profession.\n\nBut in that matter of his bread the fate of Adolphus Crosbie had by this time been decided for him, and he had reconciled himself to fate that was now inexorable. Some very slight patrimony, a hundred a year or so, had fallen to his share. Beyond that he had his salary from his office, and nothing else; and on his income, thus made up, he had lived as a bachelor in London, enjoying all that London could give him as a man in moderately easy circumstances, and looking forward to no costly luxuries,--such as a wife, a house of his own, or a stable full of horses. Those which he did enjoy of the good things of the world would, if known to John Eames, have made him appear fabulously rich in the eyes of that brother clerk. His lodgings in Mount Street were elegant in their belongings. During three months of the season in London he called himself the master of a very neat hack. He was always well dressed, though never over-dressed. At his clubs he could live on equal terms with men having ten times his income. He was not married. He had acknowledged to himself that he could not marry without money; and he would not marry for money. He had put aside from him, as not within his reach, the comforts of marriage. But-- We will not, however, at the present moment inquire more curiously into the private life and circumstances of our new friend Adolphus Crosbie.\n\nAfter the sentence pronounced against him by Lilian, the two girls remained silent for awhile. Bell was, perhaps, a little angry with her sister. It was not often that she allowed herself to say much in praise of any gentleman; and, now that she had spoken a word or two in favour of Mr. Crosbie, she felt herself to be rebuked by her sister for this unwonted enthusiasm. Lily was at work on a drawing, and in a minute or two had forgotten all about Mr. Crosbie; but the injury remained on Bell's mind, and prompted her to go back to the subject. \"I don't like those slang words, Lily.\"\n\n\"What slang words?\"\n\n\"You know what you called Bernard's friend.\"\n\n\"Oh; a swell. I fancy I do like slang. I think it's awfully jolly to talk about things being jolly. Only that I was afraid of your nerves I should have called him stunning. It's so slow, you know, to use nothing but words out of a dictionary.\"\n\n\"I don't think it's nice in talking of gentlemen.\"\n\n\"Isn't it? Well, I'd like to be nice--if I knew how.\"\n\nIf she knew how! There is no knowing how, for a girl, in that matter. If nature and her mother have not done it for her, there is no hope for her on that head. I think I may say that nature and her mother had been sufficiently efficacious for Lilian Dale in this respect.\n\n\"Mr. Crosbie is, at any rate, a gentleman, and knows how to make himself pleasant. That was all that I meant. Mamma said a great deal more about him than I did.\"\n\n\"Mr. Crosbie is an Apollo; and I always look upon Apollo as the greatest--you know what--that ever lived. I mustn't say the word, because Apollo was a gentleman.\"\n\nAt this moment, while the name of the god was still on her lips, the high open window of the drawing-room was darkened, and Bernard entered, followed by Mr. Crosbie.\n\n\"Who is talking about Apollo?\" said Captain Dale.\n\nThe girls were both stricken dumb. How would it be with them if Mr. Crosbie had heard himself spoken of in those last words of poor Lily's? This was the rashness of which Bell was ever accusing her sister, and here was the result! But, in truth, Bernard had heard nothing more than the name, and Mr. Crosbie, who had been behind him, had heard nothing.\n\n\"'As sweet and musical as bright Apollo's lute, strung with his hair,'\" said Mr. Crosbie, not meaning much by the quotation, but perceiving that the two girls had been in some way put out and silenced.\n\n\"What very bad music it must have made,\" said Lily; \"unless, indeed, his hair was very different from ours.\"\n\n\"It was all sunbeams,\" suggested Bernard. But by that time Apollo had served his turn, and the ladies welcomed their guests in the proper form.\n\n\"Mamma is in the garden,\" said Bell, with that hypocritical pretence so common with young ladies when young gentlemen call; as though they were aware that mamma was the object specially sought.\n\n\"Picking peas, with a sun-bonnet on,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Let us by all means go and help her,\" said Mr. Crosbie; and then they issued out into the garden.\n\nThe gardens of the Great House of Allington and those of the Small House open on to each other. A proper boundary of thick laurel hedge, and wide ditch, and of iron spikes guarding the ditch, there is between them; but over the wide ditch there is a foot-bridge, and at the bridge there is a gate which has no key; and for all purposes of enjoyment the gardens of each house are open to the other. And the gardens of the Small House are very pretty. The Small House itself is so near the road that there is nothing between the dining-room windows and the iron rail but a narrow edge rather than border, and a little path made with round fixed cobble stones, not above two feet broad, into which no one but the gardener ever makes his way. The distance from the road to the house is not above five or six feet, and the entrance from the gate is shut in by a covered way. But the garden behind the house, on to which the windows from the drawing-room open, is to all the senses as private as though there were no village of Allington, and no road up to the church within a hundred yards of the lawn. The steeple of the church, indeed, can be seen from the lawn, peering, as it were, between the yew-trees which stand in the corner of the churchyard adjoining to Mrs. Dale's wall. But none of the Dale family have any objection to the sight of that steeple. The glory of the Small House at Allington certainly consists in its lawn, which is as smooth, as level, and as much like velvet as grass has ever yet been made to look. Lily Dale, taking pride in her own lawn, has declared often that it is no good attempting to play croquet up at the Great House. The grass, she says, grows in tufts, and nothing that Hopkins, the gardener, can or will do has any effect upon the tufts. But there are no tufts at the Small House. As the squire himself has never been very enthusiastic about croquet, the croquet implements have been moved permanently down to the Small House, and croquet there has become quite an institution.\n\nAnd while I am on the subject of the garden I may also mention Mrs. Dale's conservatory, as to which Bell was strenuously of opinion that the Great House had nothing to offer equal to it--\"For flowers, of course, I mean,\" she would say, correcting herself; for at the Great House there was a grapery very celebrated. On this matter the squire would be less tolerant than as regarded the croquet, and would tell his niece that she knew nothing about flowers. \"Perhaps not, uncle Christopher,\" she would say. \"All the same, I like our geraniums best;\" for there was a spice of obstinacy about Miss Dale,--as, indeed, there was in all the Dales, male and female, young and old.\n\nIt may be as well to explain that the care of this lawn and of this conservatory, and, indeed, of the entire garden belonging to the Small House, was in the hands of Hopkins, the head gardener to the Great House; and it was so simply for this reason, that Mrs. Dale could not afford to keep a gardener herself. A working lad, at ten shillings a week, who cleaned the knives and shoes, and dug the ground, was the only male attendant on the three ladies. But Hopkins, the head gardener of Allington, who had men under him, was as widely awake to the lawn and the conservatory of the humbler establishment as he was to the grapery, peach-walls, and terraces of the grander one. In his eyes it was all one place. The Small House belonged to his master, as indeed did the very furniture within it; and it was lent, not let, to Mrs. Dale. Hopkins, perhaps, did not love Mrs. Dale, seeing that he owed her no duty as one born a Dale. The two young ladies he did love, and also snubbed in a very peremptory way sometimes. To Mrs. Dale he was coldly civil, always referring to the squire if any direction worthy of special notice as concerning the garden was given to him.\n\nAll this will serve to explain the terms on which Mrs. Dale was living at the Small House,--a matter needful of explanation sooner or later. Her husband had been the youngest of three brothers, and in many respects the brightest. Early in life he had gone up to London, and there had done well as a land surveyor. He had done so well that Government had employed him, and for some three or four years he had enjoyed a large income, but death had come suddenly on him, while he was only yet ascending the ladder; and, when he died, he had hardly begun to realize the golden prospects which he had seen before him. This had happened some fifteen years before our story commenced, so that the two girls hardly retained any memory of their father. For the first five years of her widowhood, Mrs. Dale, who had never been a favourite of the squire's, lived with her two little girls in such modest way as her very limited means allowed. Old Mrs. Dale, the squire's mother, then occupied the Small House. But when old Mrs. Dale died, the squire offered the place rent-free to his sister-in-law, intimating to her that her daughters would obtain considerable social advantages by living at Allington. She had accepted the offer, and the social advantages had certainly followed. Mrs. Dale was poor, her whole income not exceeding three hundred a year, and therefore her own style of living was of necessity very unassuming; but she saw her girls becoming popular in the county, much liked by the families around them, and enjoying nearly all the advantages which would have accrued to them had they been the daughters of Squire Dale of Allington. Under such circumstances it was little to her whether or no she were loved by her brother-in-law, or respected by Hopkins. Her own girls loved her, and respected her, and that was pretty much all that she demanded of the world on her own behalf.\n\nAnd uncle Christopher had been very good to the girls in his own obstinate and somewhat ungracious manner. There were two ponies in the stables of the Great House, which they were allowed to ride, and which, unless on occasions, nobody else did ride. I think he might have given the ponies to the girls, but he thought differently. And he contributed to their dresses, sending them home now and again things which he thought necessary, not in the pleasantest way in the world. Money he never gave them, nor did he make them any promises. But they were Dales, and he loved them; and with Christopher Dale to love once was to love always. Bell was his chief favourite, sharing with his nephew Bernard the best warmth of his heart. About these two he had his projects, intending that Bell should be the future mistress of the Great House of Allington; as to which project, however, Miss Dale was as yet in very absolute ignorance.\n\nWe may now, I think, go back to our four friends, as they walked out upon the lawn. They were understood to be on a mission to assist Mrs. Dale in the picking of the peas; but pleasure intervened in the way of business, and the young people, forgetting the labours of their elder, allowed themselves to be carried away by the fascinations of croquet. The iron hoops and the sticks were fixed. The mallets and the balls were lying about; and then the party was so nicely made up! \"I haven't had a game of croquet yet,\" said Mr. Crosbie. It cannot be said that he had lost much time, seeing that he had only arrived before dinner on the preceding day. And then the mallets were in their hands in a moment.\n\n\"We'll play sides, of course,\" said Lily. \"Bernard and I'll play together.\" But this was not allowed. Lily was well known to be the queen of the croquet ground; and as Bernard was supposed to be more efficient than his friend, Lily had to take Mr. Crosbie as her partner. \"Apollo can't get through the hoops,\" Lily said afterwards to her sister; \"but then how gracefully he fails to do it!\" Lily, however, had been beaten, and may therefore be excused for a little spite against her partner. But it so turned out that before Mr. Crosbie took his final departure from Allington he could get through the hoops; and Lily, though she was still queen of the croquet ground, had to acknowledge a male sovereign in that dominion.\n\n\"That's not the way we played at--,\" said Crosbie, at one point of the game, and then stopped himself.\n\n\"Where was that?\" said Bernard.\n\n\"A place I was at last summer,--in Shropshire.\"\n\n\"Then they don't play the game, Mr. Crosbie, at the place you were at last summer,--in Shropshire,\" said Lily.\n\n\"You mean Lady Hartletop's,\" said Bernard. Now, the Marchioness of Hartletop was a very great person indeed, and a leader in the fashionable world.\n\n\"Oh! Lady Hartletop's!\" said Lily. \"Then I suppose we must give in;\" which little bit of sarcasm was not lost upon Mr. Crosbie, and was put down by him in the tablets of his mind as quite undeserved. He had endeavoured to avoid any mention of Lady Hartletop and her croquet ground, and her ladyship's name had been forced upon him. Nevertheless, he liked Lily Dale through it all. But he thought that he liked Bell the best, though she said little; for Bell was the beauty of the family.\n\nDuring the game Bernard remembered that they had especially come over to bid the three ladies to dinner at the house on that day. They had all dined there on the day before, and the girls' uncle had now sent directions to them to come again. \"I'll go and ask mamma about it,\" said Bell, who was out first. And then she returned, saying, that she and her sister would obey their uncle's behest; but that her mother would prefer to remain at home. \"There are the peas to be eaten, you know,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Send them up to the Great House,\" said Bernard.\n\n\"Hopkins would not allow it,\" said Lily. \"He calls that a mixing of things. Hopkins doesn't like mixings.\" And then when the game was over, they sauntered about, out of the small garden into the larger one, and through the shrubberies, and out upon the fields, where they found the still lingering remnants of the haymaking. And Lily took a rake, and raked for two minutes; and Mr. Crosbie, making an attempt to pitch the hay into the cart, had to pay half-a-crown for his footing to the haymakers; and Bell sat quiet under a tree, mindful of her complexion; whereupon Mr. Crosbie, finding the hay-pitching not much to his taste, threw himself under the same tree also, quite after the manner of Apollo, as Lily said to her mother late in the evening. Then Bernard covered Lily with hay, which was a great feat in the jocose way for him; and Lily in returning the compliment, almost smothered Mr. Crosbie,--by accident.\n\n\"Oh, Lily,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I'm sure I beg your pardon, Mr. Crosbie. It was Bernard's fault. Bernard, I never will come into a hayfield with you again.\" And so they all became very intimate; while Bell sat quietly under the tree, listening to a word or two now and then as Mr. Crosbie chose to speak them. There is a kind of enjoyment to be had in society, in which very few words are necessary. Bell was less vivacious than her sister Lily; and when, an hour after this, she was dressing herself for dinner, she acknowledged that she had passed a pleasant afternoon, though Mr. Crosbie had not said very much.\n\n# CHAPTER III. THE WIDOW DALE OF ALLINGTON.\n\nAs Mrs. Dale, of the Small House, was not a Dale by birth, there can be no necessity for insisting on the fact that none of the Dale peculiarities should be sought for in her character. These peculiarities were not, perhaps, very conspicuous in her daughters, who had taken more in that respect from their mother than from their father; but a close observer might recognize the girls as Dales. They were constant, perhaps obstinate, occasionally a little uncharitable in their judgment, and prone to think that there was a great deal in being a Dale, though not prone to say much about it. But they had also a better pride than this, which had come to them as their mother's heritage.\n\nMrs. Dale was certainly a proud woman,--not that there was anything appertaining to herself in which she took a pride. In birth she had been much lower than her husband, seeing that her grandfather had been almost nobody. Her fortune had been considerable for her rank in life, and on its proceeds she now mainly depended; but it had not been sufficient to give any of the pride of wealth. And she had been a beauty; according to my taste, was still very lovely; but certainly at this time of life, she, a widow of fifteen years' standing, with two grown-up daughters, took no pride in her beauty. Nor had she any conscious pride in the fact that she was a lady. That she was a lady, inwards and outwards, from the crown of her head to the sole of her feet, in head, in heart, and in mind, a lady by education and a lady by nature, a lady also by birth in spite of that deficiency respecting her grandfather, I hereby state as a fact--meo periculo. And the squire, though he had no special love for her, had recognized this, and in all respects treated her as his equal.\n\nBut her position was one which required that she should either be very proud or else very humble. She was poor, and yet her daughters moved in a position which belongs, as a rule, to the daughters of rich men only. This they did as nieces of the childless squire of Allington, and as his nieces she felt that they were entitled to accept his countenance and kindness, without loss of self-respect either to her or to them. She would have ill done her duty as a mother to them had she allowed any pride of her own to come between them and such advantage in the world as their uncle might be able to give them. On their behalf she had accepted the loan of the house in which she lived, and the use of many of the appurtenances belonging to her brother-in-law; but on her own account she had accepted nothing. Her marriage with Philip Dale had been disliked by his brother the squire, and the squire, while Philip was still living, had continued to show that his feelings in this respect were not to be overcome. They never had been overcome; and now, though the brother-in-law and sister-in-law had been close neighbours for years, living as one may say almost in the same family, they had never become friends. There had not been a word of quarrel between them. They met constantly. The squire had unconsciously come to entertain a profound respect for his brother's widow. The widow had acknowledged to herself the truth of the affection shown by the uncle to her daughters. But yet they had never come together as friends. Of her own money matters Mrs. Dale had never spoken a word to the squire. Of his intention respecting the girls the squire had never spoken a word to the mother. And in this way they had lived and were living at Allington.\n\nThe life which Mrs. Dale led was not altogether an easy life,--was not devoid of much painful effort on her part. The theory of her life one may say was this--that she should bury herself in order that her daughters might live well above ground. And in order to carry out this theory, it was necessary that she should abstain from all complaint or show of uneasiness before her girls. Their life above ground would not be well if they understood that their mother, in this underground life of hers, was enduring any sacrifice on their behalf. It was needful that they should think that the picking of peas in a sun-bonnet, or long readings by her own fire-side, and solitary hours spent in thinking, were specially to her mind. \"Mamma doesn't like going out.\" \"I don't think mamma is happy anywhere out of her own drawing-room.\" I do not say that the girls were taught to say such words, but they were taught to have thoughts which led to such words, and in the early days of their going out into the world used so to speak of their mother. But a time came to them before long,--to one first and then to the other, in which they knew that it was not so, and knew also all that their mother had suffered for their sakes.\n\nAnd in truth Mrs. Dale could have been as young in heart as they were. She, too, could have played croquet, and have coquetted with a haymaker's rake, and have delighted in her pony, ay, and have listened to little nothings from this and that Apollo, had she thought that things had been conformable thereto. Women at forty do not become ancient misanthropes, or stern Rhadamanthine moralists, indifferent to the world's pleasures--no, not even though they be widows. There are those who think that such should be the phase of their minds. I profess that I do not so think. I would have women, and men also, young as long as they can be young. It is not that a woman should call herself in years younger than her father's family Bible will have her to be. Let her who is forty call herself forty; but if she can be young in spirit at forty, let her show that she is so.\n\nI think that Mrs. Dale was wrong. She would have joined that party on the croquet ground, instead of remaining among the pea-sticks in her sun-bonnet, had she done as I would have counselled her. Not a word was spoken among the four that she did not hear. Those pea-sticks were only removed from the lawn by a low wall and a few shrubs. She listened, not as one suspecting, but simply as one loving. The voices of her girls were very dear to her, and the silver ringing tones of Lily's tongue were as sweet to her ears as the music of the gods. She heard all that about Lady Hartletop, and shuddered at Lily's bold sarcasm. And she heard Lily say that mamma would stay at home and eat the peas, and said to herself sadly that that was now her lot in life.\n\n\"Dear darling girl,--and so it should be!\"\n\nIt was thus her thoughts ran. And then, when her ear had traced them, as they passed across the little bridge into the other grounds, she returned across the lawn to the house with her burden on her arm, and sat herself down on the step of the drawing-room window, looking out on the sweet summer flowers and the smooth surface of the grass before her.\n\nHad not God done well for her to place her where she was? Had not her lines been set for her in pleasant places? Was she not happy in her girls,--her sweet, loving, trusting, trusty children? As it was to be that her lord, that best half of herself, was to be taken from her in early life, and that the springs of all the lighter pleasures were to be thus stopped for her, had it not been well that in her bereavement so much had been done to soften her lot in life and give it grace and beauty? 'Twas so, she argued with herself, and yet she acknowledged to herself that she was not happy. She had resolved, as she herself had said often, to put away childish things, and now she pined for those things which she so put from her. As she sat she could still hear Lily's voice as they went through the shrubbery,--hear it when none but a mother's ears would have distinguished the sound. Now that those young men were at the Great House it was natural that her girls should be there too. The squire would not have had young men to stay with him had there been no ladies to grace his table. But for her,--she knew that no one would want her there. Now and again she must go, as otherwise her very existence, without going, would be a thing disagreeably noticeable. But there was no other reason why she should join the party; nor in joining it would she either give or receive pleasure. Let her daughters eat from her brother's table and drink of his cup. They were made welcome to do so from the heart. For her there was no such welcome as that at the Great House,--nor at any other house, or any other table!\n\n\"Mamma will stay at home to eat the peas.\"\n\nAnd then she repeated to herself the words which Lily had spoken, sitting there, leaning with her elbow on her knee, and her head upon her hand.\n\n\"Please, ma'am, cook says, can we have the peas to shell?\" and then her reverie was broken.\n\nWhereupon Mrs. Dale got up and gave over her basket. \"Cook knows that the young ladies are going to dine at the Great House?\"\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"She needn't mind getting dinner for me. I will have tea early.\" And so, after all, Mrs. Dale did not perform that special duty appointed for her.\n\nBut she soon set herself to work upon another duty. When a family of three persons has to live upon an income of three hundred a year, and, nevertheless, makes some pretence of going into society, it has to be very mindful of small details, even though that family may consist only of ladies. Of this Mrs. Dale was well aware, and as it pleased her that her daughters should be nice and fresh, and pretty in their attire, many a long hour was given up to that care. The squire would send them shawls in winter, and had given them riding habits, and had sent them down brown silk for dresses from London,--so limited in quantity that the due manufacture of two dresses out of the material had been found to be beyond the art of woman, and the brown silk garments had been a difficulty from that day to this,--the squire having a good memory in such matters, and being anxious to see the fruits of his liberality. All this was doubtless of assistance, but had the squire given the amount which he so expended in money to his nieces, the benefit would have been greater. As it was, the girls were always nice and fresh and pretty, they themselves not being idle in that matter; but their tire-woman in chief was their mother. And now she went up to their room and got out their muslin frocks, and--but, perhaps, I should not tell such tales!--She, however, felt no shame in her work, as she sent for a hot iron, and with her own hands smoothed out the creases, and gave the proper set to the crimp flounces, and fixed a new ribbon where it was wanted, and saw that all was as it should be. Men think but little how much of this kind is endured that their eyes may be pleased, even though it be but for an hour.\n\n\"Oh! mamma, how good you are,\" said Bell, as the two girls came in, only just in time to make themselves ready for returning to dinner.\n\n\"Mamma is always good,\" said Lily. \"I wish, mamma, I could do the same for you oftener,\" and then she kissed her mother. But the squire was exact about dinner, so they dressed themselves in haste, and went off again through the garden, their mother accompanying them to the little bridge.\n\n\"Your uncle did not seem vexed at my not coming?\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"We have not seen him, mamma,\" said Lily. \"We have been ever so far down the fields, and forgot altogether what o'clock it was.\"\n\n\"I don't think uncle Christopher was about the place, or we should have met him,\" said Bell.\n\n\"But I am vexed with you, mamma. Are not you, Bell? It is very bad of you to stay here all alone, and not come.\"\n\n\"I suppose mamma likes being at home better than up at the Great House,\" said Bell, very gently; and as she spoke she was holding her mother's hand.\n\n\"Well; good-by, dears. I shall expect you between ten and eleven. But don't hurry yourselves if anything is going on.\" And so they went, and the widow was again alone. The path from the bridge ran straight up towards the back of the Great House, so that for a moment or two she could see them as they tripped on almost in a run. And then she saw their dresses flutter as they turned sharp round, up the terrace steps. She would not go beyond the nook among the laurels by which she was surrounded, lest any one should see her as she looked after her girls. But when the last flutter of the pink muslin had been whisked away from her sight, she felt it hard that she might not follow them. She stood there, however, without advancing a step. She would not have Hopkins telling how she watched her daughters as they went from her own home to that of her brother-in-law. It was not within the capacity of Hopkins to understand why she watched them.\n\n\"Well, girls, you're not much too soon. I think your mother might have come with you,\" said uncle Christopher. And this was the manner of the man. Had he known his own wishes he must have acknowledged to himself that he was better pleased that Mrs. Dale should stay away. He felt himself more absolutely master and more comfortably at home at his own table without her company than with it. And yet he frequently made a grievance of her not coming, and himself believed in that grievance.\n\n\"I think mamma was tired,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Hem. It's not so very far across from one house to the other. If I were to shut myself up whenever I'm tired-- But never mind. Let's go to dinner. Mr. Crosbie, will you take my niece Lilian.\" And then, offering his own arm to Bell, he walked off to the dining-room.\n\n\"If he scolds mamma any more, I'll go away,\" said Lily to her companion; by which it may be seen that they had all become very intimate during the long day that they had passed together.\n\nMrs. Dale, after remaining for a moment on the bridge, went in to her tea. What succedaneum of mutton chop or broiled ham she had for the roast duck and green peas which were to have been provided for the family dinner we will not particularly inquire. We may, however, imagine that she did not devote herself to her evening repast with any peculiar energy of appetite. She took a book with her as she sat herself down,--some novel, probably, for Mrs. Dale was not above novels,--and read a page or two as she sipped her tea. But the book was soon laid on one side, and the tray on which the warm plate had become cold was neglected, and she threw herself back in her own familiar chair, thinking of herself, and of her girls, and thinking also what might have been her lot in life had he lived who had loved her truly during the few years that they had been together.\n\nIt is especially the nature of a Dale to be constant in his likings and his dislikings. Her husband's affection for her had been unswerving,--so much so that he had quarrelled with his brother because his brother would not express himself in brotherly terms about his wife; but, nevertheless, the two brothers had loved each other always. Many years had now gone by since these things had occurred, but still the same feelings remained. When she had first come down to Allington she had resolved to win the squire's regard, but she had now long known that any such winning was out of the question; indeed, there was no longer a wish for it. Mrs. Dale was not one of those soft-hearted women who sometimes thank God that they can love any one. She could once have felt affection for her brother-in-law,--affection, and close, careful, sisterly friendship; but she could not do so now. He had been cold to her, and had with perseverance rejected her advances. That was now seven years since; and during those years Mrs. Dale had been, at any rate, as cold to him as he had been to her.\n\nBut all this was very hard to bear. That her daughters should love their uncle was not only reasonable, but in every way desirable. He was not cold to them. To them he was generous and affectionate. If she were only out of the way, he would have taken them to his house as his own, and they would in all respects have stood before the world as his adopted children. Would it not be better if she were out of the way?\n\nIt was only in her most dismal moods that this question would get itself asked within her mind, and then she would recover herself, and answer it stoutly with an indignant protest against her own morbid weakness. It would not be well that she should be away from her girls,--not though their uncle should have been twice a better uncle; not though, by her absence, they might become heiresses of all Allington. Was it not above everything to them that they should have a mother near them? And as she asked of herself that morbid question,--wickedly asked it, as she declared to herself,--did she not know that they loved her better than all the world beside, and would prefer her caresses and her care to the guardianship of any uncle, let his house be ever so great? As yet they loved her better than all the world beside. Of other love, should it come, she would not be jealous. And if it should come, and should be happy, might there not yet be a bright evening of life for herself? If they should marry, and if their lords would accept her love, her friendship, and her homage, she might yet escape from the deathlike coldness of that Great House, and be happy in some tiny cottage, from which she might go forth at times among those who would really welcome her. A certain doctor there was, living not very far from Allington, at Guestwick, as to whom she had once thought that he might fill that place of son-in-law,--to be well-beloved. Her quiet, beautiful Bell had seemed to like the man; and he had certainly done more than seem to like her. But now, for some weeks past, this hope, or rather this idea, had faded away. Mrs. Dale had never questioned her daughter on the matter; she was not a woman prone to put such questions. But during the month or two last past, she had seen with regret that Bell looked almost coldly on the man whom her mother favoured.\n\nIn thinking of all this the long evening passed away, and at eleven o'clock she heard the coming steps across the garden. The young men had, of course, accompanied the girls home; and as she stepped out from the still open window of her own drawing-room, she saw them all on the centre of the lawn before her.\n\n\"There's mamma,\" said Lily. \"Mamma, Mr. Crosbie wants to play croquet by moonlight.\"\n\n\"I don't think there is light enough for that,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"There is light enough for him,\" said Lily, \"for he plays quite independently of the hoops; don't you, Mr. Crosbie?\"\n\n\"There's very pretty croquet light, I should say,\" said Mr. Crosbie, looking up at the bright moon; \"and then it is so stupid going to bed.\"\n\n\"Yes, it is stupid going to bed,\" said Lily; \"but people in the country are stupid, you know. Billiards, that you can play all night by gas, is much better, isn't it?\"\n\n\"Your arrows fall terribly astray there, Miss Dale, for I never touch a cue; you should talk to your cousin about billiards.\"\n\n\"Is Bernard a great billiard player?\" asked Bell.\n\n\"Well, I do play now and again; about as well as Crosbie does croquet. Come, Crosbie, we'll go home and smoke a cigar.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Lily; \"and then, you know, we stupid people can go to bed. Mamma, I wish you had a little smoking-room here for us. I don't like being considered stupid.\" And then they parted,--the ladies going into the house, and the two men returning across the lawn.\n\n\"Lily, my love,\" said Mrs. Dale, when they were all together in her bedroom, \"it seems to me that you are very hard upon Mr. Crosbie.\"\n\n\"She has been going on like that all the evening,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I'm sure we are very good friends,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Oh, very!\" said Bell.\n\n\"Now, Bell, you're jealous; you know you are.\" And then, seeing that her sister was in some slight degree vexed, she went up to her and kissed her. \"She shan't be called jealous; shall she, mamma?\"\n\n\"I don't think she deserves it,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Now, you don't mean to say that you think I meant anything,\" said Lily. \"As if I cared a buttercup about Mr. Crosbie.\"\n\n\"Or I either, Lily.\"\n\n\"Of course you don't. But I do care for him very much, mamma. He is such a duck of an Apollo. I shall always call him Apollo; Phoebus Apollo! And when I draw his picture he shall have a mallet in his hand instead of a bow. Upon my word I am very much obliged to Bernard for bringing him down here; and I do wish he was not going away the day after to-morrow.\"\n\n\"The day after to-morrow!\" said Mrs. Dale. \"It was hardly worth coming for two days.\"\n\n\"No, it wasn't,--disturbing us all in our quiet little ways just for such a spell as that,--not giving one time even to count his rays.\"\n\n\"But he says he shall perhaps come again,\" said Bell.\n\n\"There is that hope for us,\" said Lily. \"Uncle Christopher asked him to come down when he gets his long leave of absence. This is only a short sort of leave. He is better off than poor Johnny Eames. Johnny Eames only has a month, but Mr. Crosbie has two months just whenever he likes it; and seems to be pretty much his own master all the year round besides.\"\n\n\"And uncle Christopher asked him to come down for the shooting in September,\" said Bell.\n\n\"And though he didn't say he'd come I think he meant it,\" said Lily. \"There is that hope for us, mamma.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have to draw Apollo with a gun instead of a mallet.\"\n\n\"That is the worst of it, mamma. We shan't see much of him or of Bernard either. They wouldn't let us go out into the woods as beaters, would they?\"\n\n\"You'd make too much noise to be of any use.\"\n\n\"Should I? I thought the beaters had to shout at the birds. I should get very tired of shouting at birds, so I think I'll stay at home and look after my clothes.\"\n\n\"I hope he will come, because uncle Christopher seems to like him so much,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I wonder whether a certain gentleman at Guestwick will like his coming,\" said Lily. And then, as soon as she had spoken the words, she looked at her sister, and saw that she had grieved her.\n\n\"Lily, you let your tongue run too fast,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"I didn't mean anything, Bell,\" said Lily. \"I beg your pardon.\"\n\n\"It doesn't signify,\" said Bell. \"Only Lily says things without thinking.\" And then that conversation came to an end, and nothing more was said among them beyond what appertained to their toilet, and a few last words at parting. But the two girls occupied the same room, and when their own door was closed upon them, Bell did allude to what had passed with some spirit.\n\n\"Lily, you promised me,\" she said, \"that you would not say anything more to me about Dr. Crofts.\"\n\n\"I know I did, and I was very wrong. I beg your pardon, Bell; and I won't do it again,--not if I can help it.\"\n\n\"Not help it, Lily!\"\n\n\"But I'm sure I don't know why I shouldn't speak of him,--only not in the way of laughing at you. Of all the men I ever saw in my life I like him best. And only that I love you better than I love myself I could find it in my heart to grudge you his--\"\n\n\"Lily, what did you promise just now?\"\n\n\"Well; after to-night. And I don't know why you should turn against him.\"\n\n\"I have never turned against him or for him.\"\n\n\"There's no turning about him. He'd give his left hand if you'd only smile on him. Or his right either,--and that's what I should like to see; so now you've heard it.\"\n\n\"You know you are talking nonsense.\"\n\n\"So I should like to see it. And so would mamma too, I'm sure; though I never heard her say a word about him. In my mind he's the finest fellow I ever saw. What's Mr. Apollo Crosbie to him? And now, as it makes you unhappy, I'll never say another word about him.\"\n\nAs Bell wished her sister good-night with perhaps more than her usual affection, it was evident that Lily's words and eager tone had in some way pleased her, in spite of their opposition to the request which she had made. And Lily was aware that it was so.\n\n# CHAPTER IV. MRS. ROPER'S BOARDING-HOUSE.\n\nI have said that John Eames had been petted by none but his mother, but I would not have it supposed, on this account, that John Eames had no friends. There is a class of young men who never get petted, though they may not be the less esteemed, or perhaps loved. They do not come forth to the world as Apollos, nor shine at all, keeping what light they may have for inward purposes. Such young men are often awkward, ungainly, and not yet formed in their gait; they straggle with their limbs, and are shy; words do not come to them with ease, when words are required, among any but their accustomed associates. Social meetings are periods of penance to them, and any appearance in public will unnerve them. They go much about alone, and blush when women speak to them. In truth, they are not as yet men, whatever the number may be of their years; and, as they are no longer boys, the world has found for them the ungraceful name of hobbledehoy.\n\nSuch observations, however, as I have been enabled to make on this matter have led me to believe that the hobbledehoy is by no means the least valuable species of the human race. When I compare the hobbledehoy of one or two and twenty to some finished Apollo of the same age, I regard the former as unripe fruit, and the latter as fruit that is ripe. Then comes the question as to the two fruits. Which is the better fruit, that which ripens early--which is, perhaps, favoured with some little forcing apparatus, or which, at least, is backed by the warmth of a southern wall; or that fruit of slower growth, as to which nature works without assistance, on which the sun operates in its own time,--or perhaps never operates if some ungenial shade has been allowed to interpose itself? The world, no doubt, is in favour of the forcing apparatus or of the southern wall. The fruit comes certainly, and at an assured period. It is spotless, speckless, and of a certain quality by no means despicable. The owner has it when he wants it, and it serves its turn. But, nevertheless, according to my thinking, the fullest flavour of the sun is given to that other fruit,--is given in the sun's own good time, if so be that no ungenial shade has interposed itself. I like the smack of the natural growth, and like it, perhaps, the better because that which has been obtained has been obtained without favour.\n\nBut the hobbledehoy, though he blushes when women address him, and is uneasy even when he is near them, though he is not master of his limbs in a ball-room, and is hardly master of his tongue at any time, is the most eloquent of beings, and especially eloquent among beautiful women. He enjoys all the triumphs of a Don Juan, without any of Don Juan's heartlessness, and is able to conquer in all encounters, through the force of his wit and the sweetness of his voice. But this eloquence is heard only by his own inner ears, and these triumphs are the triumphs of his imagination.\n\nThe true hobbledehoy is much alone, not being greatly given to social intercourse even with other hobbledehoys--a trait in his character which I think has hardly been sufficiently observed by the world at large. He has probably become a hobbledehoy instead of an Apollo, because circumstances have not afforded him much social intercourse; and, therefore, he wanders about in solitude, taking long walks, in which he dreams of those successes which are so far removed from his powers of achievement. Out in the fields, with his stick in his hand, he is very eloquent, cutting off the heads of the springing summer weeds, as he practises his oratory with energy. And thus he feeds an imagination for which those who know him give him but scanty credit, and unconsciously prepares himself for that latter ripening, if only the ungenial shade will some day cease to interpose itself.\n\nSuch hobbledehoys receive but little petting, unless it be from a mother; and such a hobbledehoy was John Eames when he was sent away from Guestwick to begin his life in the big room of a public office in London. We may say that there was nothing of the young Apollo about him. But yet he was not without friends--friends who wished him well, and thought much of his welfare. And he had a younger sister who loved him dearly, who had no idea that he was a hobbledehoy, being somewhat of a hobbledehoya herself. Mrs. Eames, their mother, was a widow, living in a small house in Guestwick, whose husband had been throughout his whole life an intimate friend of our squire. He had been a man of many misfortunes, having begun the world almost with affluence, and having ended it in poverty. He had lived all his days in Guestwick, having at one time occupied a large tract of land, and lost much money in experimental farming; and late in life he had taken a small house on the outskirts of the town, and there had died, some two years previously to the commencement of this story. With no other man had Mr. Dale lived on terms so intimate; and when Mr. Eames died Mr. Dale acted as executor under his will, and as guardian to his children. He had, moreover, obtained for John Eames that situation under the Crown which he now held.\n\nAnd Mrs. Eames had been and still was on very friendly terms with Mrs. Dale. The squire had never taken quite kindly to Mrs. Eames, whom her husband had not met till he was already past forty years of age. But Mrs. Dale had made up by her kindness to the poor forlorn woman for any lack of that cordiality which might have been shown to her from the Great House. Mrs. Eames was a poor forlorn woman--forlorn even during the time of her husband's life, but very wobegone now in her widowhood. In matters of importance the squire had been kind to her; arranging for her her little money affairs, advising her about her house and income, also getting for her that appointment for her son. But he snubbed her when he met her, and poor Mrs. Eames held him in great awe. Mrs. Dale held her brother-in-law in no awe, and sometimes gave to the widow from Guestwick advice quite at variance to that given by the squire. In this way there had grown up an intimacy between Bell and Lily and the young Eames, and either of the girls was prepared to declare that Johnny Eames was her own and well-loved friend. Nevertheless, they spoke of him occasionally with some little dash of merriment--as is not unusual with pretty girls who have hobbledehoys among their intimate friends, and who are not themselves unaccustomed to the grace of an Apollo.\n\nI may as well announce at once that John Eames, when he went up to London, was absolutely and irretrievably in love with Lily Dale. He had declared his passion in the most moving language a hundred times; but he had declared it only to himself. He had written much poetry about Lily, but he kept his lines safe under double lock and key. When he gave the reins to his imagination, he flattered himself that he might win not only her but the world at large also by his verses; but he would have perished rather than exhibit them to human eye. During the last ten weeks of his life at Guestwick, while he was preparing for his career in London, he hung about Allington, walking over frequently and then walking back again; but all in vain. During these visits he would sit in Mrs. Dale's drawing-room, speaking but little, and addressing himself usually to the mother; but on each occasion, as he started on his long, hot walk, he resolved that he would say something by which Lily might know of his love. When he left for London that something had not been said.\n\nHe had not dreamed of asking her to be his wife. John Eames was about to begin the world with eighty pounds a year, and an allowance of twenty more from his mother's purse. He was well aware that with such an income he could not establish himself as a married man in London, and he also felt that the man who might be fortunate enough to win Lily for his wife should be prepared to give her every soft luxury that the world could afford. He knew well that he ought not to expect any assurance of Lily's love; but, nevertheless, he thought it possible that he might give her an assurance of his love. It would probably be in vain. He had no real hope, unless when he was in one of those poetic moods. He had acknowledged to himself, in some indistinct way, that he was no more than a hobbledehoy, awkward, silent, ungainly, with a face unfinished, as it were, or unripe. All this he knew, and knew also that there were Apollos in the world who would be only too ready to carry off Lily in their splendid cars. But not the less did he make up his mind that having loved her once, it behoved him, as a true man, to love her on to the end.\n\nOne little word he had said to her when they parted, but it had been a word of friendship rather than of love. He had strayed out after her on to the lawn, leaving Bell alone in the drawing-room. Perhaps Lily had understood something of the boy's feeling, and had wished to speak kindly to him at parting, or almost more than kindly. There is a silent love which women recognize, and which in some silent way they acknowledge,--giving gracious but silent thanks for the respect which accompanies it.\n\n\"I have come to say good-by, Lily,\" said Johnny Eames, following the girl down one of the paths.\n\n\"Good-by, John,\" said she, turning round. \"You know how sorry we are to lose you. But it's a great thing for you to be going up to London.\"\n\n\"Well; yes. I suppose it is. I'd sooner remain here, though.\"\n\n\"What! stay here, doing nothing! I am sure you would not.\"\n\n\"Of course, I should like to do something. I mean--\"\n\n\"You mean that it is painful to part with old friends; and I'm sure that we all feel that at parting with you. But you'll have a holiday sometimes, and then we shall see you.\"\n\n\"Yes; of course, I shall see you then. I think, Lily, I shall care more about seeing you than anybody.\"\n\n\"Oh, no, John. There'll be your own mother and sister.\"\n\n\"Yes; there'll be mother and Mary, of course. But I will come over here the very first day,--that is, if you'll care to see me?\"\n\n\"We shall care to see you very much. You know that. And--dear John, I do hope you'll be happy.\"\n\nThere was a tone in her voice as she spoke which almost upset him; or, I should rather say, which almost put him up upon his legs and made him speak; but its ultimate effect was less powerful. \"Do you?\" said he, as he held her hand for a few happy seconds. \"And I'm sure I hope you'll always be happy. Good-by, Lily.\" Then he left her, returning to the house, and she continued her walk, wandering down among the trees in the shrubbery, and not showing herself for the next half hour. How many girls have some such lover as that,--a lover who says no more to them than Johnny Eames then said to Lily Dale, who never says more than that? And yet when, in after years, they count over the names of all who have loved them, the name of that awkward youth is never forgotten.\n\nThat farewell had been spoken nearly two years since, and Lily Dale was then seventeen. Since that time, John Eames had been home once, and during his month's holiday had often visited Allington. But he had never improved upon that occasion of which I have told. It had seemed to him that Lily was colder to him than in old days, and he had become, if anything, more shy in his ways with her. He was to return to Guestwick again during this autumn; but, to tell honestly the truth in the matter, Lily Dale did not think or care very much for his coming. Girls of nineteen do not care for lovers of one-and-twenty, unless it be when the fruit has had the advantage of some forcing apparatus or southern wall.\n\nJohn Eames's love was still as hot as ever, having been sustained on poetry, and kept alive, perhaps, by some close confidence in the ears of a brother clerk; but it is not to be supposed that during these two years he had been a melancholy lover. It might, perhaps, have been better for him had his disposition led him to that line of life. Such, however, had not been the case. He had already abandoned the flute on which he had learned to sound three sad notes before he left Guestwick, and, after the fifth or sixth Sunday, he had relinquished his solitary walks along the towing-path of the Regent's Park Canal. To think of one's absent love is very sweet; but it becomes monotonous after a mile or two of a towing-path, and the mind will turn away to Aunt Sally, the Cremorne Gardens, and financial questions. I doubt whether any girl would be satisfied with her lover's mind if she knew the whole of it.\n\n\"I say, Caudle, I wonder whether a fellow could get into a club?\"\n\nThis proposition was made, on one of those Sunday walks, by John Eames to the friend of his bosom, a brother clerk, whose legitimate name was Cradell, and who was therefore called Caudle by his friends.\n\n\"Get into a club? Fisher in our room belongs to a club.\"\n\n\"That's only a chess-club. I mean a regular club.\"\n\n\"One of the swell ones at the West End?\" said Cradell, almost lost in admiration at the ambition of his friend.\n\n\"I shouldn't want it to be particularly swell. If a man isn't a swell, I don't see what he gets by going among those who are. But it is so uncommon slow at Mother Roper's.\" Now Mrs. Roper was a respectable lady, who kept a boarding-house in Burton Crescent, and to whom Mrs. Eames had been strongly recommended when she was desirous of finding a specially safe domicile for her son. For the first year of his life in London John Eames had lived alone in lodgings; but that had resulted in discomfort, solitude, and, alas! in some amount of debt, which had come heavily on the poor widow. Now, for the second year, some safer mode of life was necessary. She had learned that Mrs. Cradell, the widow of a barrister, who had also succeeded in getting her son into the Income-tax Office, had placed him in charge of Mrs. Roper; and she, with many injunctions to that motherly woman, submitted her own boy to the same custody.\n\n\"And about going to church?\" Mrs. Eames had said to Mrs. Roper.\n\n\"I don't suppose I can look after that, ma'am,\" Mrs. Roper had answered, conscientiously. \"Young gentlemen choose mostly their own churches.\"\n\n\"But they do go?\" asked the mother, very anxious in her heart as to this new life in which her boy was to be left to follow in so many things the guidance of his own lights.\n\n\"They who have been brought up steady do so, mostly.\"\n\n\"He has been brought up steady, Mrs. Roper. He has, indeed. And you won't give him a latch-key?\"\n\n\"Well, they always do ask for it.\"\n\n\"But he won't insist, if you tell him that I had rather that he shouldn't have one.\"\n\nMrs. Roper promised accordingly, and Johnny Eames was left under her charge. He did ask for the latch-key, and Mrs. Roper answered as she was bidden. But he asked again, having been sophisticated by the philosophy of Cradell, and then Mrs. Roper handed him the key. She was a woman who plumed herself on being as good as her word, not understanding that any one could justly demand from her more than that. She gave Johnny Eames the key, as doubtless she had intended to do; for Mrs. Roper knew the world, and understood that young men without latch-keys would not remain with her.\n\n\"I thought you didn't seem to find it so dull since Amelia came home,\" said Cradell.\n\n\"Amelia! What's Amelia to me? I have told you everything, Cradell, and yet you can talk to me about Amelia Roper!\"\n\n\"Come now, Johnny--\" He had always been called Johnny, and the name had gone with him to his office. Even Amelia Roper had called him Johnny on more than one occasion before this. \"You were as sweet to her the other night as though there were no such person as L. D. in existence.\" John Eames turned away and shook his head. Nevertheless, the words of his friend were grateful to him. The character of a Don Juan was not unpleasant to his imagination, and he liked to think that he might amuse Amelia Roper with a passing word, though his heart was true to Lilian Dale. In truth, however, many more of the passing words had been spoken by the fair Amelia than by him.\n\nMrs. Roper had been quite as good as her word when she told Mrs. Eames that her household was composed of herself, of a son who was in an attorney's office, of an ancient maiden cousin, named Miss Spruce, who lodged with her, and of Mr. Cradell. The divine Amelia had not then been living with her, and the nature of the statement which she was making by no means compelled her to inform Mrs. Eames that the young lady would probably return home in the following winter. A Mr. and Mrs. Lupex had also joined the family lately, and Mrs. Roper's house was now supposed to be full.\n\nAnd it must be acknowledged that Johnny Eames had, in certain unguarded moments, confided to Cradell the secret of a second weaker passion for Amelia. \"She is a fine girl,--a deuced fine girl!\" Johnny Eames had said, using a style of language which he had learned since he left Guestwick and Allington. Mr. Cradell, also, was an admirer of the fair sex; and, alas! that I should say so, Mrs. Lupex, at the present moment, was the object of his admiration. Not that he entertained the slightest idea of wronging Mr. Lupex,--a man who was a scene-painter, and knew the world. Mr. Cradell admired Mrs. Lupex as a connoisseur, not simply as a man. \"By heavens! Johnny, what a figure that woman has!\" he said, one morning, as they were walking to their office.\n\n\"Yes; she stands well on her pins.\"\n\n\"I should think she did. If I understand anything of form,\" said Cradell, \"that woman is nearly perfect. What a torso she has!\"\n\nFrom which expression, and from the fact that Mrs. Lupex depended greatly upon her stays and crinoline for such figure as she succeeded in displaying, it may, perhaps, be understood that Mr. Cradell did not understand much about form.\n\n\"It seems to me that her nose isn't quite straight,\" said Johnny Eames. Now, it undoubtedly was the fact that the nose on Mrs. Lupex's face was a little awry. It was a long, thin nose, which, as it progressed forward into the air, certainly had a preponderating bias towards the left side.\n\n\"I care more for figure than face,\" said Cradell. \"But Mrs. Lupex has fine eyes--very fine eyes.\"\n\n\"And knows how to use them, too,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Why shouldn't she? And then she has lovely hair.\"\n\n\"Only she never brushes it in the morning.\"\n\n\"Do you know, I like that kind of deshabille,\" said Cradell. \"Too much care always betrays itself.\"\n\n\"But a woman should be tidy.\"\n\n\"What a word to apply to such a creature as Mrs. Lupex! I call her a splendid woman. And how well she was got up last night. Do you know, I've an idea that Lupex treats her very badly. She said a word or two to me yesterday that--,\" and then he paused. There are some confidences which a man does not share even with his dearest friend.\n\n\"I rather fancy it's quite the other way,\" said Eames.\n\n\"How the other way?\"\n\n\"That Lupex has quite as much as he likes of Mrs. L. The sound of her voice sometimes makes me shake in my shoes, I know.\"\n\n\"I like a woman with spirit,\" said Cradell.\n\n\"Oh, so do I. But one may have too much of a good thing. Amelia did tell me;--only you won't mention it.\"\n\n\"Of course, I won't.\"\n\n\"She told me that Lupex sometimes was obliged to run away from her. He goes down to the theatre, and remains there two or three days at a time. Then she goes to fetch him, and there is no end of a row in the house.\"\n\n\"The fact is, he drinks,\" said Cradell. \"By George, I pity a woman whose husband drinks--and such a woman as that, too!\"\n\n\"Take care, old fellow, or you'll find yourself in a scrape.\"\n\n\"I know what I'm at. Lord bless you, I'm not going to lose my head because I see a fine woman.\"\n\n\"Or your heart either?\"\n\n\"Oh, heart! There's nothing of that kind of thing about me. I regard a woman as a picture or a statue. I dare say I shall marry some day, because men do; but I've no idea of losing myself about a woman.\"\n\n\"I'd lose myself ten times over for--\"\n\n\"L. D.,\" said Cradell.\n\n\"That I would. And yet I know I shall never have her. I'm a jolly, laughing sort of fellow; and yet, do you know, Caudle, when that girl marries, it will be all up with me. It will, indeed.\"\n\n\"Do you mean that you'll cut your throat?\"\n\n\"No; I shan't do that. I shan't do anything of that sort; and yet it will be all up with me.\"\n\n\"You are going down there in October;--why don't you ask her to have you?\"\n\n\"With ninety pounds a year!\" His grateful country had twice increased his salary at the rate of five pounds each year. \"With ninety pounds a year, and twenty allowed me by my mother!\"\n\n\"She could wait, I suppose. I should ask her, and no mistake. If one is to love a girl, it's no good one going on in that way!\"\n\n\"It isn't much good, certainly,\" said Johnny Eames. And then they reached the door of the Income-tax Office, and each went away to his own desk.\n\nFrom this little dialogue, it may be imagined that though Mrs. Roper was as good as her word, she was not exactly the woman whom Mrs. Eames would have wished to select as a protecting angel for her son. But the truth I take to be this, that protecting angels for widows' sons, at forty-eight pounds a year, paid quarterly, are not to be found very readily in London. Mrs. Roper was not worse than others of her class. She would much have preferred lodgers who were respectable to those who were not so,--if she could only have found respectable lodgers as she wanted them. Mr. and Mrs. Lupex hardly came under that denomination; and when she gave them up her big front bedroom at a hundred a year, she knew she was doing wrong. And she was troubled, too, about her own daughter Amelia, who was already over thirty years of age. Amelia was a very clever young woman, who had been, if the truth must be told, first young lady at a millinery establishment in Manchester. Mrs. Roper knew that Mrs. Eames and Mrs. Cradell would not wish their sons to associate with her daughter. But what could she do? She could not refuse the shelter of her own house to her own child, and yet her heart misgave her when she saw Amelia flirting with young Eames.\n\n\"I wish, Amelia, you wouldn't have so much to say to that young man.\"\n\n\"Laws, mother.\"\n\n\"So I do. If you go on like that, you'll put me out of both my lodgers.\"\n\n\"Go on like what, mother? If a gentleman speaks to me, I suppose I'm to answer him? I know how to behave myself, I believe.\" And then she gave her head a toss. Whereupon her mother was silent; for her mother was afraid of her.\n\n# CHAPTER V. ABOUT L. D.\n\nApollo Crosbie left London for Allington on the 31st of August, intending to stay there four weeks, with the declared intention of recruiting his strength by an absence of two months from official cares, and with no fixed purpose as to his destiny for the last of those two months. Offers of hospitality had been made to him by the dozen. Lady Hartletop's doors, in Shropshire, were open to him, if he chose to enter them. He had been invited by the Countess De Courcy to join her suite at Courcy Castle. His special friend Montgomerie Dobbs had a place in Scotland, and then there was a yachting party by which he was much wanted. But Mr. Crosbie had as yet knocked himself down to none of these biddings, having before him when he left London no other fixed engagement than that which took him to Allington. On the first of October we shall also find ourselves at Allington in company with Johnny Eames; and Apollo Crosbie will still be there,--by no means to the comfort of our friend from the Income-tax Office.\n\nJohnny Eames cannot be called unlucky in that matter of his annual holiday, seeing that he was allowed to leave London in October, a month during which few chose to own that they remain in town. For myself, I always regard May as the best month for holiday-making; but then no Londoner cares to be absent in May. Young Eames, though he lived in Burton Crescent and had as yet no connection with the West End, had already learned his lesson in this respect. \"Those fellows in the big room want me to take May,\" he had said to his friend Cradell. \"They must think I'm uncommon green.\"\n\n\"It's too bad,\" said Cradell. \"A man shouldn't be asked to take his leave in May. I never did, and what's more, I never will. I'd go to the Board first.\"\n\nEames had escaped this evil without going to the Board, and had succeeded in obtaining for himself for his own holiday that month of October, which, of all months, is perhaps the most highly esteemed for holiday purposes. \"I shall go down by the mail-train to-morrow night,\" he said to Amelia Roper, on the evening before his departure. At that moment he was sitting alone with Amelia in Mrs. Roper's back drawing-room. In the front room Cradell was talking to Mrs. Lupex; but as Miss Spruce was with them, it may be presumed that Mr. Lupex need have had no cause for jealousy.\n\n\"Yes,\" said Amelia; \"I know how great is your haste to get down to that fascinating spot. I could not expect that you would lose one single hour in hurrying away from Burton Crescent.\"\n\nAmelia Roper was a tall, well-grown young woman, with dark hair and dark eyes;--not handsome, for her nose was thick, and the lower part of her face was heavy, but yet not without some feminine attractions. Her eyes were bright; but then, also, they were mischievous. She could talk fluently enough; but then, also, she could scold. She could assume sometimes the plumage of a dove; but then again she could occasionally ruffle her feathers like an angry kite. I am quite prepared to acknowledge that John Eames should have kept himself clear of Amelia Roper; but then young men so frequently do those things which they should not do!\n\n\"After twelve months up here in London one is glad to get away to one's own friends,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Your own friends, Mr. Eames! What sort of friends? Do you suppose I don't know?\"\n\n\"Well, no. I don't think you do know.\"\n\n\"L. D.!\" said Amelia, showing that Lily had been spoken of among people who should never have been allowed to hear her name. But perhaps, after all, no more than those two initials were known in Burton Crescent. From the tone which was now used in naming them, it was sufficiently manifest that Amelia considered herself to be wronged by their very existence.\n\n\"L. S. D.,\" said Johnny, attempting the line of a witty, gay young spendthrift. \"That's my love--pounds, shillings, and pence; and a very coy mistress she is.\"\n\n\"Nonsense, sir. Don't talk to me in that way. As if I didn't know where your heart was. What right had you to speak to me if you had an L. D. down in the country?\"\n\nIt should be here declared on behalf of poor John Eames that he had not ever spoken to Amelia--he had not spoken to her in any such phrase as her words seemed to imply. But then he had written to her a fatal note of which we will speak further before long, and that perhaps was quite as bad,--or worse.\n\n\"Ha, ha, ha!\" laughed Johnny. But the laugh was assumed, and not assumed with ease.\n\n\"Yes, sir; it's a laughing matter to you, I dare say. It is very easy for a man to laugh under such circumstances;--that is to say, if he is perfectly heartless,--if he's got a stone inside his bosom instead of flesh and blood. Some men are made of stone, I know, and are troubled with no feelings.\"\n\n\"What is it you want me to say? You pretend to know all about it, and it wouldn't be civil in me to contradict you.\"\n\n\"What is it I want? You know very well what I want; or rather, I don't want anything. What is it to me? It is nothing to me about L. D. You can go down to Allington and do what you like for me. Only I hate such ways.\"\n\n\"What ways, Amelia?\"\n\n\"What ways! Now, look here, Johnny: I'm not going to make a fool of myself for any man. When I came home here three months ago--and I wish I never had;\"--she paused here a moment, waiting for a word of tenderness; but as the word of tenderness did not come, she went on--\"but when I did come home, I didn't think there was a man in all London could make me care for him,--that I didn't. And now you're going away, without so much as hardly saying a word to me.\" And then she brought out her handkerchief.\n\n\"What am I to say, when you keep on scolding me all the time?\"\n\n\"Scolding you!--And me too! No, Johnny, I ain't scolding you, and don't mean to. If it's to be all over between us, say the word, and I'll take myself away out of the house before you come back again. I've had no secrets from you. I can go back to my business in Manchester, though it is beneath my birth, and not what I've been used to. If L. D. is more to you than I am, I won't stand in your way. Only say the word.\"\n\nL. D. was more to him than Amelia Roper,--ten times more to him. L. D. would have been everything to him, and Amelia Roper was worse than nothing. He felt all this at the moment, and struggled hard to collect an amount of courage that would make him free.\n\n\"Say the word,\" said she, rising on her feet before him, \"and all between you and me shall be over. I have got your promise, but I'd scorn to take advantage. If Amelia hasn't got your heart, she'd despise to take your hand. Only I must have an answer.\"\n\nIt would seem that an easy way of escape was offered to him; but the lady probably knew that the way as offered by her was not easy to such an one as John Eames.\n\n\"Amelia,\" he said, still keeping his seat.\n\n\"Well, sir?\"\n\n\"You know I love you.\"\n\n\"And about L. D.?\"\n\n\"If you choose to believe all the nonsense that Cradell puts into your head, I can't help it. If you like to make yourself jealous about two letters, it isn't my fault.\"\n\n\"And you love me?\" said she.\n\n\"Of course I love you.\" And then, upon hearing these words, Amelia threw herself into his arms.\n\nAs the folding doors between the two rooms were not closed, and as Miss Spruce was sitting in her easy chair immediately opposite to them, it was probable that she saw what passed. But Miss Spruce was a taciturn old lady, not easily excited to any show of surprise or admiration; and as she had lived with Mrs. Roper for the last twelve years, she was probably well acquainted with her daughter's ways.\n\n\"You'll be true to me?\" said Amelia, during the moment of that embrace--\"true to me for ever?\"\n\n\"Oh, yes; that's a matter of course,\" said Johnny Eames. And then she liberated him; and the two strolled into the front sitting-room.\n\n\"I declare, Mr. Eames,\" said Mrs. Lupex, \"I'm glad you've come. Here's Mr. Cradell does say such queer things.\"\n\n\"Queer things!\" said Cradell. \"Now, Miss Spruce, I appeal to you--Have I said any queer things?\"\n\n\"If you did, sir, I didn't notice them,\" said Miss Spruce.\n\n\"I noticed them, then,\" said Mrs. Lupex. \"An unmarried man like Mr. Cradell has no business to know whether a married lady wears a cap or her own hair--has he, Mr. Eames?\"\n\n\"I don't think I ever know,\" said Johnny, not intending any sarcasm on Mrs. Lupex.\n\n\"I dare say not, sir,\" said the lady. \"We all know where your attention is riveted. If you were to wear a cap, my dear, somebody would see the difference very soon--wouldn't they, Miss Spruce?\"\n\n\"I dare say they would,\" said Miss Spruce.\n\n\"If I could look as nice in a cap as you do, Mrs. Lupex, I'd wear one to-morrow,\" said Amelia, who did not wish to quarrel with the married lady at the present moment. There were occasions, however, on which Mrs. Lupex and Miss Roper were by no means so gracious to each other.\n\n\"Does Lupex like caps?\" asked Cradell.\n\n\"If I wore a plumed helmet on my head, it's my belief he wouldn't know the difference; nor yet if I had got no head at all. That's what comes of getting married. If you'll take my advice, Miss Roper, you'll stay as you are; even though somebody should break his heart about it. Wouldn't you, Miss Spruce?\"\n\n\"Oh, as for me, I'm an old woman, you know,\" said Miss Spruce, which was certainly true.\n\n\"I don't see what any woman gets by marrying,\" continued Mrs. Lupex. \"But a man gains everything. He don't know how to live, unless he's got a woman to help him.\"\n\n\"But is love to go for nothing?\" said Cradell.\n\n\"Oh, love! I don't believe in love. I suppose I thought I loved once, but what did it come to after all? Now, there's Mr. Eames--we all know he's in love.\"\n\n\"It comes natural to me, Mrs. Lupex. I was born so,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"And there's Miss Roper--one never ought to speak free about a lady, but perhaps she's in love too.\"\n\n\"Speak for yourself, Mrs. Lupex,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"There's no harm in saying that, is there? I'm sure, if you ain't, you're very hard-hearted; for, if ever there was a true lover, I believe you've got one of your own. My!--if there's not Lupex's step on the stair! What can bring him home at this hour? If he's been drinking, he'll come home as cross as anything.\" Then Mr. Lupex entered the room, and the pleasantness of the party was destroyed.\n\nIt may be said that neither Mrs. Cradell nor Mrs. Eames would have placed their sons in Burton Crescent if they had known the dangers into which the young men would fall. Each, it must be acknowledged, was imprudent; but each clearly saw the imprudence of the other. Not a week before this, Cradell had seriously warned his friend against the arts of Miss Roper. \"By George, Johnny, you'll get yourself entangled with that girl.\"\n\n\"One always has to go through that sort of thing,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Yes; but those who go through too much of it never get out again. Where would you be if she got a written promise of marriage from you?\"\n\nPoor Johnny did not answer this immediately, for in very truth Amelia Roper had such a document in her possession.\n\n\"Where should I be?\" said he. \"Among the breaches of promise, I suppose.\"\n\n\"Either that, or else among the victims of matrimony. My belief of you is, that if you gave such a promise, you'd carry it out.\"\n\n\"Perhaps I should,\" said Johnny; \"but I don't know. It's a matter of doubt what a man ought to do in such a case.\"\n\n\"But there's been nothing of that kind yet?\"\n\n\"Oh dear, no!\"\n\n\"If I was you, Johnny, I'd keep away from her. It's very good fun, of course, that sort of thing; but it is so uncommon dangerous! Where would you be now with such a girl as that for your wife?\"\n\nSuch had been the caution given by Cradell to his friend. And now, just as he was starting for Allington, Eames returned the compliment. They had gone together to the Great Western station at Paddington, and Johnny tendered his advice as they were walking together up and down the platform.\n\n\"I say, Caudle, old boy, you'll find yourself in trouble with that Mrs. Lupex, if you don't take care of yourself.\"\n\n\"But I shall take care of myself. There's nothing so safe as a little nonsense with a married woman. Of course, it means nothing, you know, between her and me.\"\n\n\"I don't suppose it does mean anything. But she's always talking about Lupex being jealous; and if he was to cut up rough, you wouldn't find it pleasant.\"\n\nCradell, however, seemed to think that there was no danger. His little affair with Mrs. Lupex was quite platonic and safe. As for doing any real harm, his principles, as he assured his friend, were too high. Mrs. Lupex was a woman of talent, whom no one seemed to understand, and, therefore, he had taken some pleasure in studying her character. It was merely a study of character, and nothing more. Then the friends parted, and Eames was carried away by the night mail-train down to Guestwick.\n\nHow his mother was up to receive him at four o'clock in the morning, how her maternal heart was rejoicing at seeing the improvement in his gait, and the manliness of appearance imparted to him by his whiskers, I need not describe at length. Many of the attributes of a hobbledehoy had fallen from him, and even Lily Dale might now probably acknowledge that he was no longer a boy. All which might be regarded as good, if only in putting off childish things he had taken up things which were better than childish.\n\nOn the very first day of his arrival he made his way over to Allington. He did not walk on this occasion as he had used to do in the old happy days. He had an idea that it might not be well for him to go into Mrs. Dale's drawing-room with the dust of the road on his boots, and the heat of the day on his brow. So he borrowed a horse and rode over, taking some pride in a pair of spurs which he had bought in Piccadilly, and in his kid gloves, which were brought out new for the occasion. Alas, alas! I fear that those two years in London have not improved John Eames; and yet I have to acknowledge that John Eames is one of the heroes of my story.\n\nOn entering Mrs. Dale's drawing-room he found Mrs. Dale and her eldest daughter. Lily at the moment was not there, and as he shook hands with the other two, of course, he asked for her.\n\n\"She is only in the garden,\" said Bell. \"She will be here directly.\"\n\n\"She has walked across to the Great House with Mr. Crosbie,\" said Mrs. Dale; \"but she is not going to remain. She will be so glad to see you, John! We all expected you to-day.\"\n\n\"Did you?\" said Johnny, whose heart had been plunged into cold water at the mention of Mr. Crosbie's name. He had been thinking of Lilian Dale ever since his friend had left him on the railway platform; and, as I beg to assure all ladies who may read my tale, the truth of his love for Lily had moulted no feather through that unholy liaison between him and Miss Roper. I fear that I shall be disbelieved in this; but it was so. His heart was and ever had been true to Lilian, although he had allowed himself to be talked into declarations of affection by such a creature as Amelia Roper. He had been thinking of his meeting with Lily all the night and throughout the morning, and now he heard that she was walking alone about the gardens with a strange gentleman. That Mr. Crosbie was very grand and very fashionable he had heard, but he knew no more of him. Why should Mr. Crosbie be allowed to walk with Lily Dale? And why should Mrs. Dale mention the circumstance as though it were quite a thing of course? Such mystery as there was in this was solved very quickly.\n\n\"I'm sure Lily won't object to my telling such a dear friend as you what has happened,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"She is engaged to be married to Mr. Crosbie.\"\n\nThe water into which Johnny's heart had been plunged now closed over his head and left him speechless. Lily Dale was engaged to be married to Mr. Crosbie! He knew that he should have spoken when he heard the tidings. He knew that the moments of silence as they passed by told his secret to the two women before him,--that secret which it would now behove him to conceal from all the world. But yet he could not speak.\n\n\"We are all very well pleased at the match,\" said Mrs. Dale, wishing to spare him.\n\n\"Nothing can be nicer than Mr. Crosbie,\" said Bell. \"We have often talked about you, and he will be so happy to know you.\"\n\n\"He won't know much about me,\" said Johnny; and even in speaking these few senseless words--words which he uttered because it was necessary that he should say something--the tone of his voice was altered. He would have given the world to have been master of himself at this moment, but he felt that he was utterly vanquished.\n\n\"There is Lily coming across the lawn,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Then I'd better go,\" said Eames. \"Don't say anything about it; pray don't.\" And then, without waiting for another word, he escaped out of the drawing-room.\n\n# CHAPTER VI. BEAUTIFUL DAYS.\n\nI am well aware that I have not as yet given any description of Bell and Lilian Dale, and equally well aware that the longer the doing so is postponed the greater the difficulty becomes. I wish it could be understood without any description that they were two pretty, fair-haired girls, of whom Bell was the tallest and the prettiest, whereas Lily was almost as pretty as her sister, and perhaps was more attractive.\n\nThey were fair-haired girls, very like each other, of whom I have before my mind's eye a distinct portrait, which I fear I shall not be able to draw in any such manner as will make it distinct to others. They were something below the usual height, being slight and slender in all their proportions. Lily was the shorter of the two, but the difference was so trifling that it was hardly remembered unless the two were together. And when I said that Bell was the prettier, I should, perhaps, have spoken more justly had I simply declared that her features were more regular than her sister's. The two girls were very fair, so that the soft tint of colour which relieved the whiteness of their complexion was rather acknowledged than distinctly seen. It was there, telling its own tale of health, as its absence would have told a tale of present or coming sickness; and yet nobody could ever talk about the colour in their cheeks. The hair of the two girls was so alike in hue and texture, that no one, not even their mother, could say that there was a difference. It was not flaxen hair, and yet it was very light. Nor did it approach to auburn; and yet there ran through it a golden tint that gave it a distinct brightness of its own. But with Bell it was more plentiful than with Lily, and therefore Lily would always talk of her own scanty locks, and tell how beautiful were those belonging to her sister. Nevertheless Lily's head was quite as lovely as her sister's; for its form was perfect, and the simple braids in which they both wore their hair did not require any great exuberance in quantity. Their eyes were brightly blue; but Bell's were long, and soft, and tender, often hardly daring to raise themselves to your face; while those of Lily were rounder, but brighter, and seldom kept by any want of courage from fixing themselves where they pleased. And Lily's face was perhaps less oval in its form--less perfectly oval--than her sister's. The shape of the forehead was, I think, the same, but with Bell the chin was something more slender and delicate. But Bell's chin was unmarked, whereas on her sister's there was a dimple which amply compensated for any other deficiency in its beauty. Bell's teeth were more even than her sister's; but then she showed her teeth more frequently. Her lips were thinner and, as I cannot but think, less expressive. Her nose was decidedly more regular in its beauty, for Lily's nose was somewhat broader than it should have been. It may, therefore, be understood that Bell would be considered the beauty by the family.\n\nBut there was, perhaps, more in the general impression made by these girls, and in the whole tone of their appearance, than in the absolute loveliness of their features or the grace of their figures. There was about them a dignity of demeanour devoid of all stiffness or pride, and a maidenly modesty which gave itself no airs. In them was always apparent that sense of security which women should receive from an unconscious dependence on their own mingled purity and weakness. These two girls were never afraid of men,--never looked as though they were so afraid. And I may say that they had little cause for that kind of fear to which I allude. It might be the lot of either of them to be ill-used by a man, but it was hardly possible that either of them should ever be insulted by one. Lily, as may, perhaps, have been already seen, could be full of play, but in her play she never so carried herself that any one could forget what was due to her.\n\nAnd now Lily Dale was engaged to be married, and the days of her playfulness were over. It sounds sad, this sentence against her, but I fear that it must be regarded as true. And when I think that it is true,--when I see that the sportiveness and kitten-like gambols of girlhood should be over, and generally are over, when a girl has given her troth, it becomes a matter of regret to me that the feminine world should be in such a hurry after matrimony. I have, however, no remedy to offer for the evil; and, indeed, am aware that the evil, if there be an evil, is not well expressed in the words I have used. The hurry is not for matrimony, but for love. Then, the love once attained, matrimony seizes it for its own, and the evil is accomplished.\n\nAnd Lily Dale was engaged to be married to Adolphus Crosbie,--to Apollo Crosbie, as she still called him, confiding her little joke to his own ears. And to her he was an Apollo, as a man who is loved should be to the girl who loves him. He was handsome, graceful, clever, self-confident, and always cheerful when she asked him to be cheerful. But he had also his more serious moments, and could talk to her of serious matters. He would read to her, and explain to her things which had hitherto been too hard for her young intelligence. His voice, too, was pleasant, and well under command. It could be pathetic if pathos were required, or ring with laughter as merry as her own. Was not such a man fit to be an Apollo to such a girl, when once the girl had acknowledged to herself that she loved him?\n\nShe had acknowledged it to herself, and had acknowledged it to him,--as the reader will perhaps say without much delay. But the courtship had so been carried on that no delay had been needed. All the world had smiled upon it. When Mr. Crosbie had first come among them at Allington, as Bernard's guest, during those few days of his early visit, it had seemed as though Bell had been chiefly noticed by him. And Bell in her own quiet way had accepted his admiration, saying nothing of it and thinking but very little. Lily was heart-free at the time, and had ever been so. No first shadow from Love's wing had as yet been thrown across the pure tablets of her bosom. With Bell it was not so,--not so in absolute strictness. Bell's story, too, must be told, but not on this page. But before Crosbie had come among them, it was a thing fixed in her mind that such love as she had felt must be overcome and annihilated. We may say that it had been overcome and annihilated, and that she would have sinned in no way had she listened to vows from this new Apollo. It is almost sad to think that such a man might have had the love of either of such girls, but I fear that I must acknowledge that it was so. Apollo, in the plenitude of his power, soon changed his mind; and before the end of his first visit, had transferred the distant homage which he was then paying from the elder to the younger sister. He afterwards returned, as the squire's guest, for a longer sojourn among them, and at the end of the first month had already been accepted as Lily's future husband.\n\nIt was beautiful to see how Bell changed in her mood towards Crosbie and towards her sister as soon as she perceived how the affair was going. She was not long in perceiving it, having caught the first glimpses of the idea on that evening when they both dined at the Great House, leaving their mother alone to eat or to neglect the peas. For some six or seven weeks Crosbie had been gone, and during that time Bell had been much more open in speaking of him than her sister. She had been present when Crosbie had bid them good-by, and had listened to his eagerness as he declared to Lily that he should soon be back again at Allington. Lily had taken this very quietly, as though it had not belonged at all to herself; but Bell had seen something of the truth, and, believing in Crosbie as an earnest, honest man, had spoken kind words of him, fostering any little aptitude for love which might already have formed itself in Lily's bosom.\n\n\"But he is such an Apollo, you know,\" Lily had said.\n\n\"He is a gentleman; I can see that.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes; a man can't be an Apollo unless he's a gentleman.\"\n\n\"And he's very clever.\"\n\n\"I suppose he is clever.\" There was nothing more said about his being a mere clerk. Indeed, Lily had changed her mind on that subject. Johnny Eames was a mere clerk; whereas Crosbie, if he was to be called a clerk at all, was a clerk of some very special denomination. There may be a great difference between one clerk and another! A Clerk of the Council and a parish clerk are very different persons. Lily had got some such idea as this into her head as she attempted in her own mind to rescue Mr. Crosbie from the lower orders of the Government service.\n\n\"I wish he were not coming,\" Mrs. Dale had said to her eldest daughter.\n\n\"I think you are wrong, mamma.\"\n\n\"But if she should become fond of him, and then--\"\n\n\"Lily will never become really fond of any man till he shall have given her proper reason. And if he admires her, why should they not come together?\"\n\n\"But she is so young, Bell.\"\n\n\"She is nineteen; and if they were engaged, perhaps, they might wait for a year or so. But it's no good talking in that way, mamma. If you were to tell Lily not to give him encouragement, she would not speak to him.\"\n\n\"I should not think of interfering.\"\n\n\"No, mamma; and therefore it must take its course. For myself, I like Mr. Crosbie very much.\"\n\n\"So do I, my dear.\"\n\n\"And so does my uncle. I wouldn't have Lily take a lover of my uncle's choosing.\"\n\n\"I should hope not.\"\n\n\"But it must be considered a good thing if she happens to choose one of his liking.\"\n\nIn this way the matter had been talked over between the mother and her elder daughter. Then Mr. Crosbie had come; and before the end of the first month his declared admiration for Lily had proved the correctness of her sister's foresight. And during that short courtship all had gone well with the lovers. The squire from the first had declared himself satisfied with the match, informing Mrs. Dale, in his cold manner, that Mr. Crosbie was a gentleman with an income sufficient for matrimony.\n\n\"It would be close enough in London,\" Mrs. Dale had said.\n\n\"He has more than my brother had when he married,\" said the squire.\n\n\"If he will only make her as happy as your brother made me,--while it lasted!\" said Mrs. Dale, as she turned away her face to conceal a tear that was coming. And then there was nothing more said about it between the squire and his sister-in-law. The squire spoke no word as to assistance in money matters,--did not even suggest that he would lend a hand to the young people at starting, as an uncle in such a position might surely have done. It may well be conceived that Mrs. Dale herself said nothing on the subject. And, indeed, it may be conceived, also, that the squire, let his intentions be what they might, would not divulge them to Mrs. Dale. This was uncomfortable, but the position was one that was well understood between them.\n\nBernard Dale was still at Allington, and had remained there through the period of Crosbie's absence. Whatever words Mrs. Dale might choose to speak on the matter would probably be spoken to him; but, then, Bernard could be quite as close as his uncle. When Crosbie returned, he and Bernard had, of course, lived much together; and, as was natural, there came to be close discussion between them as to the two girls, when Crosbie allowed it to be understood that his liking for Lily was becoming strong.\n\n\"You know, I suppose, that my uncle wishes me to marry the elder one,\" Bernard had said.\n\n\"I have guessed as much.\"\n\n\"And I suppose the match will come off. She's a pretty girl, and as good as gold.\"\n\n\"Yes, she is.\"\n\n\"I don't pretend to be very much in love with her. It's not my way, you know. But, some of these days, I shall ask her to have me, and I suppose it'll all go right. The governor has distinctly promised to allow me eight hundred a year off the estate, and to take us in for three months every year if we wish it. I told him simply that I couldn't do it for less, and he agreed with me.\"\n\n\"You and he get on very well together.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes! There's never been any fal-lal between us about love, and duty, and all that. I think we understand each other, and that's everything. He knows the comfort of standing well with the heir, and I know the comfort of standing well with the owner.\" It must be admitted, I think, that there was a great deal of sound, common sense about Bernard Dale.\n\n\"What will he do for the younger sister?\" asked Crosbie; and, as he asked the important question, a close observer might have perceived that there was some slight tremor in his voice.\n\n\"Ah! that's more than I can tell you. If I were you, I should ask him. The governor is a plain man, and likes plain business.\"\n\n\"I suppose you couldn't ask him?\"\n\n\"No; I don't think I could. It is my belief that he will not let her go by any means empty-handed.\"\n\n\"Well, I should suppose not.\"\n\n\"But remember this, Crosbie,--I can say nothing to you on which you are to depend. Lily, also, is as good as gold; and, as you seem to be fond of her, I should ask the governor, if I were you, in so many words, what he intends to do. Of course, it's against my interest, for every shilling he gives Lily will ultimately come out of my pocket. But I'm not the man to care about that, as you know.\"\n\nWhat might be Crosbie's knowledge on this subject we will not here inquire; but we may say that it would have mattered very little to him out of whose pocket the money came, so long as it went into his own. When he felt quite sure of Lily,--having, in fact, received Lily's permission to speak to her uncle, and Lily's promise that she would herself speak to her mother,--he did tell the squire what was his intention. This he did in an open, manly way, as though he felt that in asking for much he also offered to give much.\n\n\"I have nothing to say against it,\" said the squire.\n\n\"And I have your permission to consider myself as engaged to her?\"\n\n\"If you have hers and her mother's. Of course you are aware that I have no authority over her.\"\n\n\"She would not marry without your sanction.\"\n\n\"She is very good to think so much of her uncle,\" said the squire; and his words as he spoke them sounded very cold in Crosbie's ears. After that Crosbie said nothing about money, having to confess to himself that he was afraid to do so. \"And what would be the use?\" said he to himself, wishing to make excuses for what he felt to be weak in his own conduct. \"If he should refuse to give her a shilling I could not go back from it now.\" And then some ideas ran across his mind as to the injustice to which men are subjected in this matter of matrimony. A man has to declare himself before it is fitting that he should make any inquiry about a lady's money; and then, when he has declared himself, any such inquiry is unavailing. Which consideration somewhat cooled the ardour of his happiness. Lily Dale was very pretty, very nice, very refreshing in her innocence, her purity, and her quick intelligence. No amusement could be more deliciously amusing than that of making love to Lily Dale. Her way of flattering her lover without any intention of flattery on her part, had put Crosbie into a seventh heaven. In all his experience he had known nothing like it. \"You may be sure of this,\" she had said,--\"I shall love you with all my heart and all my strength.\" It was very nice;--but then what were they to live upon? Could it be that he, Adolphus Crosbie, should settle down on the north side of the New Road, as a married man, with eight hundred a year? If indeed the squire would be as good to Lily as he had promised to be to Bell, then indeed things might be made to arrange themselves.\n\nBut there was no such drawback on Lily's happiness. Her ideas about money were rather vague, but they were very honest. She knew she had none of her own, but supposed it was a husband's duty to find what would be needful. She knew she had none of her own, and was therefore aware that she ought not to expect luxuries in the little household that was to be prepared for her. She hoped, for his sake, that her uncle might give some assistance, but was quite prepared to prove that she could be a good poor man's wife. In the old colloquies on such matters between her and her sister, she had always declared that some decent income should be considered as indispensable before love could be entertained. But eight hundred a year had been considered as doing much more than fulfilling this stipulation. Bell had had high-flown notions as to the absolute glory of poverty. She had declared that income should not be considered at all. If she had loved a man, she could allow herself to be engaged to him, even though he had no income. Such had been their theories; and as regarded money, Lily was quite contented with the way in which she had carried out her own.\n\nIn these beautiful days there was nothing to check her happiness. Her mother and sister united in telling her that she had done well,--that she was happy in her choice, and justified in her love. On that first day, when she told her mother all, she had been made exquisitely blissful by the way in which her tidings had been received.\n\n\"Oh! mamma, I must tell you something,\" she said, coming up to her mother's bedroom, after a long ramble with Mr. Crosbie through those Allington fields.\n\n\"Is it about Mr. Crosbie?\"\n\n\"Yes, mamma.\" And then the rest had been said through the medium of warm embraces and happy tears rather than by words.\n\nAs she sat in her mother's room, hiding her face on her mother's shoulders, Bell had come, and had knelt at her feet.\n\n\"Dear Lily,\" she had said, \"I am so glad.\" And then Lily remembered how she had, as it were, stolen her lover from her sister, and she put her arms round Bell's neck and kissed her.\n\n\"I knew how it was going to be from the very first,\" said Bell. \"Did I not, mamma?\"\n\n\"I'm sure I didn't,\" said Lily. \"I never thought such a thing was possible.\"\n\n\"But we did,--mamma and I.\"\n\n\"Did you?\" said Lily.\n\n\"Bell told me that it was to be so,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"But I could hardly bring myself at first to think that he was good enough for my darling.\"\n\n\"Oh, mamma! you must not say that. You must think that he is good enough for anything.\"\n\n\"I will think that he is very good.\"\n\n\"Who could be better? And then, when you remember all that he is to give up for my sake!--And what can I do for him in return? What have I got to give him?\"\n\nNeither Mrs. Dale nor Bell could look at the matter in this light, thinking that Lily gave quite as much as she received. But they both declared that Crosbie was perfect, knowing that by such assurances only could they now administer to Lily's happiness; and Lily, between them, was made perfect in her happiness, receiving all manner of encouragement in her love, and being nourished in her passion by the sympathy and approval of her mother and sister.\n\nAnd then had come that visit from Johnny Eames. As the poor fellow marched out of the room, giving them no time to say farewell, Mrs. Dale and Bell looked at each other sadly; but they were unable to concoct any arrangement, for Lily had run across the lawn, and was already on the ground before the window.\n\n\"As soon as we got to the end of the shrubbery there were uncle Christopher and Bernard close to us; so I told Adolphus he might go on by himself.\"\n\n\"And who do you think has been here?\" said Bell. But Mrs. Dale said nothing. Had time been given to her to use her own judgment, nothing should have been said at that moment as to Johnny's visit.\n\n\"Has anybody been here since I went? Whoever it was didn't stay very long.\"\n\n\"Poor Johnny Eames,\" said Bell. Then the colour came up into Lily's face, and she bethought herself in a moment that the old friend of her young days had loved her, that he, too, had had hopes as to his love, and that now he had heard tidings which would put an end to such hopes. She understood it all in a moment, but understood also that it was necessary that she should conceal such understanding.\n\n\"Dear Johnny!\" she said. \"Why did he not wait for me?\"\n\n\"We told him you were out,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"He will be here again before long, no doubt.\"\n\n\"And he knows--?\"\n\n\"Yes; I thought you would not object to my telling him.\"\n\n\"No, mamma; of course not. And he has gone back to Guestwick?\"\n\nThere was no answer given to this question, nor were there any further words then spoken about Johnny Eames. Each of these women understood exactly how the matter stood, and each knew that the others understood it. The young man was loved by them all, but not loved with that sort of admiring affection which had been accorded to Mr. Crosbie. Johnny Eames could not have been accepted as a suitor by their pet. Mrs. Dale and Bell both felt that. And yet they loved him for his love, and for that distant, modest respect which had restrained him from any speech regarding it. Poor Johnny! But he was young,--hardly as yet out of his hobbledehoyhood,--and he would easily recover this blow, remembering, and perhaps feeling to his advantage, some slight touch of its passing romance. It is thus women think of men who love young and love in vain.\n\nBut Johnny Eames himself, as he rode back to Guestwick, forgetful of his spurs, and with his gloves stuffed into his pocket, thought of the matter very differently. He had never promised to himself any success as to his passion for Lily, and had, indeed, always acknowledged that he could have no hope; but now, that she was actually promised to another man, and as good as married, he was not the less broken-hearted because his former hopes had not been high. He had never dared to speak to Lily of his love, but he was conscious that she knew it, and he did not now dare to stand before her as one convicted of having loved in vain. And then, as he rode back, he thought also of his other love, not with many of those pleasant thoughts which Lotharios and Don Juans may be presumed to enjoy when they contemplate their successes. \"I suppose I shall marry her, and there'll be an end of me,\" he said to himself, as he remembered a short note which he had once written to her in his madness. There had been a little supper at Mrs. Roper's, and Mrs. Lupex and Amelia had made the punch. After supper, he had been by some accident alone with Amelia in the dining-parlour; and when, warmed by the generous god, he had declared his passion, she had shaken her head mournfully, and had fled from him to some upper region, absolutely refusing his proffered embrace. But on the same night, before his head had found its pillow, a note had come to him, half repentant, half affectionate, half repellent,--\"If, indeed, he would swear to her that his love was honest and manly, then, indeed, she might even yet,--see him through the chink of the doorway with the purport of telling him that he was forgiven.\" Whereupon, a perfidious pencil being near to his hand, he had written the requisite words. \"My only object in life is to call you my own for ever.\" Amelia had her misgivings whether such a promise, in order that it might be used as legal evidence, should not have been written in ink. It was a painful doubt; but nevertheless she was as good as her word, and saw him through the chink, forgiving him for his impetuosity in the parlour with, perhaps, more clemency than a mere pardon required. \"By George! how well she looked with her hair all loose,\" he said to himself, as he at last regained his pillow, still warm with the generous god. But now, as he thought of that night, returning on his road from Allington to Guestwick, those loose, floating locks were remembered by him with no strong feeling as to their charms. And he thought also of Lily Dale, as she was when he had said farewell to her on that day before he first went up to London. \"I shall care more about seeing you than anybody,\" he had said; and he had often thought of the words since, wondering whether she had understood them as meaning more than an assurance of ordinary friendship. And he remembered well the dress she had then worn. It was an old brown merino, which he had known before, and which, in truth, had nothing in it to recommend it specially to a lover's notice. \"Horrid old thing!\" had been Lily's own verdict respecting the frock, even before that day. But she had hallowed it in his eyes, and he would have been only too happy to have worn a shred of it near his heart, as a talisman. How wonderful in its nature is that passion of which men speak when they acknowledge to themselves that they are in love. Of all things, it is, under one condition, the most foul, and under another, the most fair. As that condition is, a man shows himself either as a beast or as a god! And so we will let poor Johnny Eames ride back to Guestwick, suffering much in that he had loved basely--and suffering much, also, in that he had loved nobly.\n\nLily, as she had tripped along through the shrubbery, under her lover's arm, looking up, every other moment, into his face, had espied her uncle and Bernard. \"Stop,\" she had said, giving him a little pull at the arm; \"I won't go on. Uncle is always teasing me with some old-fashioned wit. And I've had quite enough of you to-day, sir. Mind you come over to-morrow before you go to your shooting.\" And so she had left him.\n\nWe may as well learn here what was the question in dispute between the uncle and cousin, as they were walking there on the broad gravel path behind the Great House. \"Bernard,\" the old man had said, \"I wish this matter could be settled between you and Bell.\"\n\n\"Is there any hurry about it, sir?\"\n\n\"Yes, there is hurry; or, rather, as I hate hurry in all things, I would say that there is ground for despatch. Mind, I do not wish to drive you. If you do not like your cousin, say so.\"\n\n\"But I do like her; only I have a sort of feeling that these things grow best by degrees. I quite share your dislike to being in a hurry.\"\n\n\"But time enough has been taken now. You see, Bernard, I am going to make a great sacrifice of income on your behalf.\"\n\n\"I am sure I am very grateful.\"\n\n\"I have no children, and have therefore always regarded you as my own. But there is no reason why my brother Philip's daughter should not be as dear to me as my brother Orlando's son.\"\n\n\"Of course not, sir; or, rather, his two daughters.\"\n\n\"You may leave that matter to me, Bernard. The younger girl is going to marry this friend of yours, and as he has a sufficient income to support a wife, I think that my sister-in-law has good reason to be satisfied by the match. She will not be expected to give up any part of her small income, as she must have done had Lily married a poor man.\"\n\n\"I suppose she could hardly give up much.\"\n\n\"People must be guided by circumstances. I am not disposed to put myself in the place of a parent to them both. There is no reason why I should, and I will not encourage false hopes. If I knew that this matter between you and Bell was arranged, I should have reason to feel satisfied with what I was doing.\" From all which Bernard began to perceive that poor Crosbie's expectations in the matter of money would not probably receive much gratification. But he also perceived--or thought that he perceived--a kind of threat in this warning from his uncle. \"I have promised you eight hundred a year with your wife,\" the warning seemed to say. \"But if you do not at once accept it, or let me feel that it will be accepted, it may be well for me to change my mind--especially as this other niece is about to be married. If I am to give you so large a fortune with Bell, I need do nothing for Lily. But if you do not choose to take Bell and the fortune, why then--\" And so on. It was thus that Bernard read his uncle's caution, as they walked together on the broad gravel path.\n\n\"I have no desire to postpone the matter any longer,\" said Bernard. \"I will propose to Bell at once, if you wish it.\"\n\n\"If your mind be quite made up, I cannot see why you should delay it.\"\n\nAnd then, having thus arranged that matter, they received their future relative with kind smiles and soft words.\n\n# CHAPTER VII. THE BEGINNING OF TROUBLES.\n\nLily, as she parted with her lover in the garden, had required of him to attend upon her the next morning as he went to his shooting, and in obedience to this command he appeared on Mrs. Dale's lawn after breakfast, accompanied by Bernard and two dogs. The men had guns in their hands, and were got up with all proper sporting appurtenances, but it so turned out that they did not reach the stubble-fields on the farther side of the road until after luncheon. And may it not be fairly doubted whether croquet is not as good as shooting when a man is in love?\n\nIt will be said that Bernard Dale was not in love; but they who bring such accusation against him, will bring it falsely. He was in love with his cousin Bell according to his manner and fashion. It was not his nature to love Bell as John Eames loved Lily; but then neither would his nature bring him into such a trouble as that which the charms of Amelia Roper had brought upon the poor clerk from the Income-tax Office. Johnny was susceptible, as the word goes; whereas Captain Dale was a man who had his feelings well under control. He was not one to make a fool of himself about a girl, or to die of a broken heart; but, nevertheless, he would probably love his wife when he got a wife, and would be a careful father to his children.\n\nThey were very intimate with each other now,--these four. It was Bernard and Adolphus, or sometimes Apollo, and Bell and Lily among them; and Crosbie found it to be pleasant enough. A new position of life had come upon him, and one exceeding pleasant; but, nevertheless, there were moments in which cold fits of a melancholy nature came upon him. He was doing the very thing which throughout all the years of his manhood he had declared to himself that he would not do. According to his plan of life he was to have eschewed marriage, and to have allowed himself to regard it as a possible event only under the circumstances of wealth, rank, and beauty all coming in his way together. As he had expected no such glorious prize, he had regarded himself as a man who would reign at the Beaufort and be potent at Sebright's to the end of his chapter. But now--\n\nIt was the fact that he had fallen from his settled position, vanquished by a silver voice, a pretty wit, and a pair of moderately bright eyes. He was very fond of Lily, having in truth a stronger capability for falling in love than his friend Captain Dale; but was the sacrifice worth his while? This was the question which he asked himself in those melancholy moments; while he was lying in bed, for instance, awake in the morning, when he was shaving himself, and sometimes also when the squire was prosy after dinner. At such times as these, while he would be listening to Mr. Dale, his self-reproaches would sometimes be very bitter. Why should he undergo this, he, Crosbie of Sebright's, Crosbie of the General Committee Office, Crosbie who would allow no one to bore him between Charing Cross and the far end of Bayswater,--why should he listen to the long-winded stories of such a one as Squire Dale? If, indeed, the squire intended to be liberal to his niece, then it might be very well. But as yet the squire had given no sign of such intention, and Crosbie was angry with himself in that he had not had the courage to ask a question on that subject.\n\nAnd thus the course of love was not all smooth to our Apollo. It was still pleasant for him when he was there on the croquet ground, or sitting in Mrs. Dale's drawing-room with all the privileges of an accepted lover. It was pleasant to him also as he sipped the squire's claret, knowing that his coffee would soon be handed to him by a sweet girl who would have tripped across the two gardens on purpose to perform for him this service. There is nothing pleasanter than all this, although a man when so treated does feel himself to look like a calf at the altar, ready for the knife, with blue ribbons round his horns and neck. Crosbie felt that he was such a calf,--and the more calf-like, in that he had not as yet dared to ask a question about his wife's fortune. \"I will have it out of the old fellow this evening,\" he said to himself, as he buttoned on his dandy shooting gaiters that morning.\n\n\"How nice he looks in them,\" Lily said to her sister afterwards, knowing nothing of the thoughts which had troubled her lover's mind while he was adorning his legs.\n\n\"I suppose we shall come back this way,\" Crosbie said, as they prepared to move away on their proper business when lunch was over.\n\n\"Well, not exactly!\" said Bernard. \"We shall make our way round by Darvell's farm, and so back by Gruddock's. Are the girls going to dine up at the Great House to-day?\"\n\nThe girls declared that they were not going to dine up at the Great House,--that they did not intend going to the Great House at all that evening.\n\n\"Then, as you won't have to dress, you might as well meet us at Gruddock's gate, at the back of the farmyard. We'll be there exactly at half-past five.\"\n\n\"That is to say, we're to be there at half-past five, and you'll keep us waiting for three-quarters of an hour,\" said Lily. Nevertheless the arrangement as proposed was made, and the two ladies were not at all unwilling to make it. It is thus that the game is carried on among unsophisticated people who really live in the country. The farmyard gate at Farmer Gruddock's has not a fitting sound as a trysting-place in romance, but for people who are in earnest it does as well as any oak in the middle glade of a forest. Lily Dale was quite in earnest--and so indeed was Adolphus Crosbie,--only with him the earnest was beginning to take that shade of brown which most earnest things have to wear in this vale of tears. With Lily it was as yet all rose-coloured. And Bernard Dale was also in earnest. Throughout this morning he had stood very near to Bell on the lawn, and had thought that his cousin did not receive his little whisperings with any aversion. Why should she? Lucky girl that she was, thus to have eight hundred a year pinned to her skirt!\n\n\"I say, Dale,\" Crosbie said, as in the course of their day's work they had come round upon Gruddock's ground, and were preparing to finish off his turnips before they reached the farm-yard gate. And now, as Crosbie spoke, they stood leaning on the gate, looking at the turnips while the two dogs squatted on their haunches. Crosbie had been very silent for the last mile or two, and had been making up his mind for this conversation. \"I say, Dale,--your uncle has never said a word to me yet as to Lily's fortune.\"\n\n\"As to Lily's fortune! The question is whether Lily has got a fortune.\"\n\n\"He can hardly expect that I am to take her without something. Your uncle is a man of the world and he knows--\"\n\n\"Whether or no my uncle is a man of the world, I will not say; but you are, Crosbie, whether he is or not. Lily, as you have always known, has nothing of her own.\"\n\n\"I am not talking of Lily's own. I'm speaking of her uncle. I have been straightforward with him; and when I became attached to your cousin I declared what I meant at once.\"\n\n\"You should have asked him the question, if you thought there was any room for such a question.\"\n\n\"Thought there was any room! Upon my word, you are a cool fellow.\"\n\n\"Now look here, Crosbie; you may say what you like about my uncle, but you must not say a word against Lily.\"\n\n\"Who is going to say a word against her? You can little understand me if you don't know that the protection of her name against evil words is already more my care than it is yours. I regard Lily as my own.\"\n\n\"I only meant to say, that any discontent you may feel as to her money, or want of money, you must refer to my uncle, and not to the family at the Small House.\"\n\n\"I am quite well aware of that.\"\n\n\"And though you are quite at liberty to say what you like to me about my uncle, I cannot say that I can see that he has been to blame.\"\n\n\"He should have told me what her prospects are.\"\n\n\"But if she have got no prospects! It cannot be an uncle's duty to tell everybody that he does not mean to give his niece a fortune. In point of fact, why should you suppose that he has such an intention?\"\n\n\"Do you know that he has not? because you once led me to believe that he would give his niece money.\"\n\n\"Now, Crosbie, it is necessary that you and I should understand each other in this matter--\"\n\n\"But did you not?\"\n\n\"Listen to me for a moment. I never said a word to you about my uncle's intentions in any way, until after you had become fully engaged to Lily with the knowledge of us all. Then, when my belief on the subject could make no possible difference in your conduct, I told you that I thought my uncle would do something for her. I told you so because I did think so;--and as your friend, I should have told you what I thought in any matter that concerned your interest.\"\n\n\"And now you have changed your opinion?\"\n\n\"I have changed my opinion; but very probably without sufficient ground.\"\n\n\"That's hard upon me.\"\n\n\"It may be hard to bear disappointment; but you cannot say that anybody has ill-used you.\"\n\n\"And you don't think he will give her anything?\"\n\n\"Nothing that will be of much moment to you.\"\n\n\"And I'm not to say that that's hard? I think it confounded hard. Of course I must put off my marriage.\"\n\n\"Why do you not speak to my uncle?\"\n\n\"I shall do so. To tell the truth, I think it would have come better from him; but that is a matter of opinion. I shall tell him very plainly what I think about it; and if he is angry, why, I suppose I must leave his house; that will be all.\"\n\n\"Look here, Crosbie; do not begin your conversation with the purpose of angering him. He is not a bad-hearted man, but is very obstinate.\"\n\n\"I can be quite as obstinate as he is.\" And, then, without further parley, they went in among the turnips, and each swore against his luck as he missed his birds. There are certain phases of mind in which a man can neither ride nor shoot, nor play a stroke at billiards, nor remember a card at whist,--and to such a phase of mind had come both Crosbie and Dale after their conversation over the gate.\n\nThey were not above fifteen minutes late at the trysting-place, but nevertheless, punctual though they had been, the girls were there before them. Of course the first inquiries were made about the game, and of course the gentlemen declared that the birds were scarcer than they had ever been before, that the dogs were wilder, and their luck more excruciatingly bad,--to all which apologies very little attention was paid. Lily and Bell had not come there to inquire after partridges, and would have forgiven the sportsmen even though no single bird had been killed. But they could not forgive the want of good spirits which was apparent.\n\n\"I declare I don't know what's the matter with you,\" Lily said to her lover.\n\n\"We have been over fifteen miles of ground, and--\"\n\n\"I never knew anything so lackadaisical as you gentlemen from London. Been over fifteen miles of ground! Why, uncle Christopher would think nothing of that.\"\n\n\"Uncle Christopher is made of sterner stuff than we are,\" said Crosbie. \"They used to be born so sixty or seventy years ago.\" And then they walked on through Gruddock's fields, and the home paddocks, back to the Great House, where they found the squire standing in the front of the porch.\n\nThe walk had not been so pleasant as they had all intended that it should be when they made their arrangements for it. Crosbie had endeavoured to recover his happy state of mind, but had been unsuccessful; and Lily, fancying that her lover was not all that he should be, had become reserved and silent. Bernard and Bell had not shared this discomfiture, but then Bernard and Bell were, as a rule, much more given to silence than the other two.\n\n\"Uncle,\" said Lily, \"these men have shot nothing, and you cannot conceive how unhappy they are in consequence. It's all the fault of the naughty partridges.\"\n\n\"There are plenty of partridges if they knew how to get them,\" said the squire.\n\n\"The dogs are uncommonly wild,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"They are not wild with me,\" said the squire; \"nor yet with Dingles.\" Dingles was the squire's gamekeeper. \"The fact is, you young men, nowadays, expect to have dogs trained to do all the work for you. It's too much labour for you to walk up to your game. You'll be late for dinner, girls, if you don't look sharp.\"\n\n\"We're not coming up this evening, sir,\" said Bell.\n\n\"And why not?\"\n\n\"We're going to stay with mamma.\"\n\n\"And why will not your mother come with you? I'll be whipped if I can understand it. One would have thought that under the present circumstances she would have been glad to see you all as much together as possible.\"\n\n\"We're together quite enough,\" said Lily. \"And as for mamma, I suppose she thinks--\" And then she stopped herself, catching the glance of Bell's imploring eye. She was going to make some indignant excuse for her mother,--some excuse which would be calculated to make her uncle angry. It was her practice to say such sharp words to him, and consequently he did not regard her as warmly as her more silent and more prudent sister. At the present moment he turned quickly round and went into the house; and then, with a very few words of farewell, the two young men followed him. The girls went back over the little bridge by themselves, feeling that the afternoon had not gone off altogether well.\n\n\"You shouldn't provoke him, Lily,\" said Bell.\n\n\"And he shouldn't say those things about mamma. It seems to me that you don't mind what he says.\"\n\n\"Oh, Lily.\"\n\n\"No more you do. He makes me so angry that I cannot hold my tongue. He thinks that because all the place is his, he is to say just what he likes. Why should mamma go up there to please his humours?\"\n\n\"You may be sure that mamma will do what she thinks best. She is stronger-minded than uncle Christopher, and does not want any one to help her. But, Lily, you shouldn't speak as though I were careless about mamma. You didn't mean that, I know.\"\n\n\"Of course I didn't.\" Then the two girls joined their mother in their own little domain; but we will return to the men at the Great House.\n\nCrosbie, when he went up to dress for dinner, fell into one of those melancholy fits of which I have spoken. Was he absolutely about to destroy all the good that he had done for himself throughout the past years of his hitherto successful life? or rather, as he at last put the question to himself more strongly,--was it not the case that he had already destroyed all that success? His marriage with Lily, whether it was to be for good or bad, was now a settled thing, and was not regarded as a matter admitting of any doubt. To do the man justice, I must declare that in all these moments of misery he still did the best he could to think of Lily herself as of a great treasure which he had won,--as of a treasure which should, and perhaps would, compensate him for his misery. But there was the misery very plain. He must give up his clubs, and his fashion, and all that he had hitherto gained, and be content to live a plain, humdrum, domestic life, with eight hundred a year, and a small house, full of babies. It was not the kind of Elysium for which he had tutored himself. Lily was very nice, very nice indeed. She was, as he said to himself, \"by odds, the nicest girl that he had ever seen.\" Whatever might now turn up, her happiness should be his first care. But as for his own,--he began to fear that the compensation would hardly be perfect. \"It is my own doing,\" he said to himself, intending to be rather noble in the purport of his soliloquy, \"I have trained myself for other things,--very foolishly. Of course I must suffer,--suffer damnably. But she shall never know it. Dear, sweet, innocent, pretty little thing!\" And then he went on about the squire, as to whom he felt himself entitled to be indignant by his own disinterested and manly line of conduct towards the niece. \"But I will let him know what I think about it,\" he said. \"It's all very well for Dale to say that I have been treated fairly. It isn't fair for a man to put forward his niece under false pretences. Of course I thought that he intended to provide for her.\" And then, having made up his mind in a very manly way that he would not desert Lily altogether after having promised to marry her, he endeavoured to find consolation in the reflection that he might, at any rate, allow himself two years' more run as a bachelor in London. Girls who have to get themselves married without fortunes always know that they will have to wait. Indeed, Lily had already told him, that as far as she was concerned, she was in no hurry. He need not, therefore, at once withdraw his name from Sebright's. Thus he endeavoured to console himself, still, however, resolving that he would have a little serious conversation with the squire that very evening as to Lily's fortune.\n\nAnd what was the state of Lily's mind at the same moment, while she, also, was performing some slight toilet changes preparatory to their simple dinner at the Small House?\n\n\"I didn't behave well to him,\" she said to herself; \"I never do. I forget how much he is giving up for me; and then, when anything annoys him, I make it worse instead of comforting him.\" And upon that she made accusation against herself that she did not love him half enough,--that she did not let him see how thoroughly and perfectly she loved him. She had an idea of her own, that as a girl should never show any preference for a man till circumstances should have fully entitled him to such manifestation, so also should she make no drawback on her love, but pour it forth for his benefit with all her strength, when such circumstances had come to exist. But she was ever feeling that she was not acting up to her theory, now that the time for such practice had come. She would unwittingly assume little reserves, and make small pretences of indifference in spite of her own judgment. She had done so on this afternoon, and had left him without giving him her hand to press, without looking up into his face with an assurance of love, and therefore she was angry with herself. \"I know I shall teach him to hate me,\" she said out loud to Bell.\n\n\"That would be very sad,\" said Bell; \"but I don't see it.\"\n\n\"If you were engaged to a man you would be much better to him. You would not say so much, but what you did say would be all affection. I am always making horrid little speeches, for which I should like to cut out my tongue afterwards.\"\n\n\"Whatever sort of speeches they are, I think that he likes them.\"\n\n\"Does he? I'm not all so sure of that, Bell. Of course I don't expect that he is to scold me,--not yet, that is. But I know by his eye when he is pleased and when he is displeased.\"\n\nAnd then they went down to their dinner.\n\nUp at the Great House the three gentlemen met together in apparent good humour. Bernard Dale was a man of an equal temperament, who rarely allowed any feeling, or even any annoyance, to interfere with his usual manner,--a man who could always come to table with a smile, and meet either his friend or his enemy with a properly civil greeting. Not that he was especially a false man. There was nothing of deceit in his placidity of demeanour. It arose from true equanimity; but it was the equanimity of a cold disposition rather than of one well ordered by discipline. The squire was aware that he had been unreasonably petulant before dinner, and having taken himself to task in his own way, now entered the dining-room with the courteous greeting of a host. \"I find that your bag was not so bad after all,\" he said, \"and I hope that your appetite is at least as good as your bag.\"\n\nCrosbie smiled, and made himself pleasant, and said a few flattering words. A man who intends to take some very decided step in an hour or two generally contrives to bear himself in the meantime as though the trifles of the world were quite sufficient for him. So he praised the squire's game; said a good-natured word as to Dingles, and bantered himself as to his own want of skill. Then all went merry,--not quite as a marriage bell; but still merry enough for a party of three gentlemen.\n\nBut Crosbie's resolution was fixed; and as soon, therefore, as the old butler was permanently gone, and the wine steadily in transit upon the table, he began his task, not without some apparent abruptness. Having fully considered the matter, he had determined that he would not wait for Bernard Dale's absence. He thought it possible that he might be able to fight his battle better in Bernard's presence than he could do behind his back.\n\n\"Squire,\" he began. They all called him squire when they were on good terms together, and Crosbie thought it well to begin as though there was nothing amiss between them. \"Squire, of course I am thinking a good deal at the present moment as to my intended marriage.\"\n\n\"That's natural enough,\" said the squire.\n\n\"Yes, by George! sir, a man doesn't make a change like that without finding that he has got something to think of.\"\n\n\"I suppose not,\" said the squire. \"I never was in the way of getting married myself, but I can easily understand that.\"\n\n\"I've been the luckiest fellow in the world in finding such a girl as your niece--\" Whereupon the squire bowed, intending to make a little courteous declaration that the luck in the matter was on the side of the Dales. \"I know that,\" continued Crosbie. \"She is exactly everything that a girl ought to be.\"\n\n\"She is a good girl,\" said Bernard.\n\n\"Yes; I think she is,\" said the squire.\n\n\"But it seems to me,\" said Crosbie, finding that it was necessary to dash at once headlong into the water, \"that something ought to be said as to my means of supporting her properly.\"\n\nThen he paused for a moment, expecting that the squire would speak. But the squire sat perfectly still, looking intently at the empty fireplace and saying nothing. \"Of supporting her,\" continued Crosbie, \"with all those comforts to which she has been accustomed.\"\n\n\"She has never been used to expense,\" said the squire. \"Her mother, as you doubtless know, is not a rich woman.\"\n\n\"But living here, Lily has had great advantages,--a horse to ride, and all that sort of thing.\"\n\n\"I don't suppose she expects a horse in the park,\" said the squire, with a very perceptible touch of sarcasm in his voice.\n\n\"I hope not,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"I believe she has had the use of one of the ponies here sometimes, but I hope that has not made her extravagant in her ideas. I did not think that there was anything of that nonsense about either of them.\"\n\n\"Nor is there,--as far as I know.\"\n\n\"Nothing of the sort,\" said Bernard.\n\n\"But the long and the short of it is this, sir!\" and Crosbie, as he spoke, endeavoured to maintain his ordinary voice and usual coolness, but his heightened colour betrayed that he was nervous. \"Am I to expect any accession of income with my wife?\"\n\n\"I have not spoken to my sister-in-law on the subject,\" said the squire; \"but I should fear that she cannot do much.\"\n\n\"As a matter of course, I would not take a shilling from her,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Then that settles it,\" said the squire.\n\nCrosbie paused a moment, during which his colour became very red. He unconsciously took up an apricot and eat it, and then he spoke out. \"Of course I was not alluding to Mrs. Dale's income; I would not, on any account, disturb her arrangements. But I wished to learn, sir, whether you intend to do anything for your niece.\"\n\n\"In the way of giving her a fortune? Nothing at all. I intend to do nothing at all.\"\n\n\"Then I suppose we understand each other,--at last,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"I should have thought that we might have understood each other at first,\" said the squire. \"Did I ever make you any promise, or give you any hint that I intended to provide for my niece? Have I ever held out to you any such hope? I don't know what you mean by that word 'at last'--unless it be to give offence.\"\n\n\"I meant the truth, sir;--I meant this--that seeing the manner in which your nieces lived with you, I thought it probable that you would treat them both as though they were your daughters. Now I find out my mistake;--that is all!\"\n\n\"You have been mistaken,--and without a shadow of excuse for your mistake.\"\n\n\"Others have been mistaken with me,\" said Crosbie, forgetting, on the spur of the moment, that he had no right to drag the opinion of any other person into the question.\n\n\"What others?\" said the squire, with anger; and his mind immediately betook itself to his sister-in-law.\n\n\"I do not want to make any mischief,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"If anybody connected with my family has presumed to tell you that I intended to do more for my niece Lilian than I have already done, such person has not only been false, but ungrateful. I have given to no one any authority to make any promise on behalf of my niece.\"\n\n\"No such promise has been made. It was only a suggestion,\" said Crosbie.\n\nHe was not in the least aware to whom the squire was alluding in his anger; but he perceived that his host was angry, and having already reflected that he should not have alluded to the words which Bernard Dale had spoken in his friendship, he resolved to name no one. Bernard, as he sat by listening, knew exactly how the matter stood; but, as he thought, there could be no reason why he should subject himself to his uncle's ill-will, seeing that he had committed no sin.\n\n\"No such suggestion should have been made,\" said the squire. \"No one has had a right to make such a suggestion. No one has been placed by me in a position to make such a suggestion to you without manifest impropriety. I will ask no further questions about it; but it is quite as well that you should understand at once that I do not consider it to be my duty to give my niece Lilian a fortune on her marriage. I trust that your offer to her was not made under any such delusion.\"\n\n\"No, sir; it was not,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Then I suppose that no great harm has been done. I am sorry if false hopes have been given to you; but I am sure you will acknowledge that they were not given to you by me.\"\n\n\"I think you have misunderstood me, sir. My hopes were never very high; but I thought it right to ascertain your intentions.\"\n\n\"Now you know them. I trust, for the girl's sake, that it will make no difference to her. I can hardly believe that she has been to blame in the matter.\"\n\nCrosbie hastened at once to exculpate Lily; and then, with more awkward blunders than a man should have made who was so well acquainted with fashionable life as the Apollo of the Beaufort, he proceeded to explain that, as Lily was to have nothing, his own pecuniary arrangements would necessitate some little delay in their marriage.\n\n\"As far as I myself am concerned,\" said the squire, \"I do not like long engagements. But I am quite aware that in this matter I have no right to interfere, unless, indeed--\" and then he stopped himself.\n\n\"I suppose it will be well to fix some day; eh, Crosbie?\" said Bernard.\n\n\"I will discuss that matter with Mrs. Dale,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"If you and she understand each other,\" said the squire, \"that will be sufficient. Shall we go into the drawing-room now, or out upon the lawn?\"\n\nThat evening, as Crosbie went to bed, he felt that he had not gained the victory in his encounter with the squire.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII. IT CANNOT BE.\n\nOn the following morning at breakfast each of the three gentlemen at the Great House received a little note on pink paper, nominally from Mrs. Dale, asking them to drink tea at the Small House on that day week. At the bottom of the note which Lily had written for Mr. Crosbie was added: \"Dancing on the lawn, if we can get anybody to stand up. Of course you must come, whether you like it or not. And Bernard also. Do your possible to talk my uncle into coming.\" And this note did something towards re-creating good-humour among them at the breakfast-table. It was shown to the squire, and at last he was brought to say that he would perhaps go to Mrs. Dale's little evening-party.\n\nIt may be well to explain that this promised entertainment had been originated with no special view to the pleasure of Mr. Crosbie, but altogether on behalf of poor Johnny Eames. What was to be done in that matter? This question had been fully discussed between Mrs. Dale and Bell, and they had come to the conclusion that it would be best to ask Johnny over to a little friendly gathering, in which he might be able to meet Lily with some strangers around them. In this way his embarrassment might be overcome. It would never do, as Mrs. Dale said, that he should be suffered to stay away, unnoticed by them. \"When the ice is once broken he won't mind it,\" said Bell. And, therefore, early in the day, a messenger was sent over to Guestwick, who returned with a note from Mrs. Eames, saying that she would come on the evening in question, with her son and daughter. They would keep the fly and get back to Guestwick the same evening. This was added, as an offer had been made of beds for Mrs. Eames and Mary.\n\nBefore the evening of the party another memorable occurrence had taken place at Allington, which must be described, in order that the feelings of the different people on that evening may be understood. The squire had given his nephew to understand that he wished to have that matter settled as to his niece Bell; and as Bernard's views were altogether in accordance with the squire's, he resolved to comply with his uncle's wishes. The project with him was not a new thing. He did love his cousin quite sufficiently for purposes of matrimony, and was minded that it would be a good thing for him to marry. He could not marry without money, but this marriage would give him an income without the trouble of intricate settlements, or the interference of lawyers hostile to his own interests. It was possible that he might do better; but then it was possible also that he might do much worse; and, in addition to this, he was fond of his cousin. He discussed the matter within himself, very calmly; made some excellent resolutions as to the kind of life which it would behove him to live as a married man; settled on the street in London in which he would have his house, and behaved very prettily to Bell for four or five days running. That he did not make love to her, in the ordinary sense of the word, must, I suppose, be taken for granted, seeing that Bell herself did not recognize the fact. She had always liked her cousin, and thought that in these days he was making himself particularly agreeable.\n\nOn the evening before the party the girls were at the Great House, having come up nominally with the intention of discussing the expediency of dancing on the lawn. Lily had made up her mind that it was to be so, but Bell had objected that it would be cold and damp, and that the drawing-room would be nicer for dancing.\n\n\"You see we've only got four young gentlemen and one ungrown,\" said Lily; \"and they will look so stupid standing up all properly in a room, as though we had a regular party.\"\n\n\"Thank you for the compliment,\" said Crosbie, taking off his straw hat.\n\n\"So you will; and we girls will look more stupid still. But out on the lawn it won't look stupid at all. Two or three might stand up on the lawn, and it would be jolly enough.\"\n\n\"I don't quite see it,\" said Bernard.\n\n\"Yes, I think I see it,\" said Crosbie. \"The unadaptability of the lawn for the purpose of a ball--\"\n\n\"Nobody is thinking of a ball,\" said Lily, with mock petulance.\n\n\"I'm defending you, and yet you won't let me speak. The unadaptability of the lawn for the purposes of a ball will conceal the insufficiency of four men and a boy as a supply of male dancers. But, Lily, who is the ungrown gentleman? Is it your old friend Johnny Eames?\"\n\nLily's voice became sobered as she answered him.\n\n\"Oh, no; I did not mean Mr. Eames. He is coming, but I did not mean him. Dick Boyce, Mr. Boyce's son, is only sixteen. He is the ungrown gentleman.\"\n\n\"And who is the fourth adult?\"\n\n\"Dr. Crofts, from Guestwick. I do hope you will like him, Adolphus. We think he is the very perfection of a man.\"\n\n\"Then of course I shall hate him; and be very jealous, too!\"\n\nAnd then that pair went off together, fighting their own little battle on that head, as turtle-doves will sometimes do. They went off, and Bernard was left with Bell standing together over the ha-ha fence which divides the garden at the back of the house from the field.\n\n\"Bell,\" he said, \"they seem very happy, don't they?\"\n\n\"And they ought to be happy now, oughtn't they? Dear Lily! I hope he will be good to her. Do you know, Bernard, though he is your friend, I am very, very anxious about it. It is such a vast trust to put in a man when we do not quite know him.\"\n\n\"Yes, it is; but they'll do very well together. Lily will be happy enough.\"\n\n\"And he?\"\n\n\"I suppose he'll be happy, too. He'll feel himself a little straightened as to income at first, but that will all come round.\"\n\n\"If he is not, she will be wretched.\"\n\n\"They will do very well. Lily must be prepared to make the money go as far as she can, that's all.\"\n\n\"Lily won't feel the want of money. It is not that. But if he lets her know that she has made him a poor man, then she will be unhappy. Is he extravagant, Bernard?\"\n\nBut Bernard was anxious to discuss another subject, and therefore would not speak such words of wisdom as to Lily's engagement as might have been expected from him had he been in a different frame of mind.\n\n\"No, I should say not,\" said he. \"But, Bell--\"\n\n\"I do not know that we could have acted otherwise than we have done, and yet I fear that we have been rash. If he makes her unhappy, Bernard, I shall never forgive you.\"\n\nBut as she said this she put her hand lovingly upon his arm, as a cousin might do, and spoke in a tone which divested her threat of its acerbity.\n\n\"You must not quarrel with me, Bell, whatever may happen. I cannot afford to quarrel with you.\"\n\n\"Of course I was not in earnest as to that.\"\n\n\"You and I must never quarrel, Bell; at least, I hope not. I could bear to quarrel with any one rather than with you.\" And then, as he spoke, there was something in his voice which gave the girl some slight, indistinct warning of what might be his intention. Not that she said to herself at once, that he was going to make her an offer of his hand,--now, on the spot; but she felt that he intended something beyond the tenderness of ordinary cousinly affection.\n\n\"I hope we shall never quarrel,\" she said. But as she spoke, her mind was settling itself,--forming its resolution, and coming to a conclusion as to the sort of love which Bernard might, perhaps, expect. And it formed another conclusion; as to the sort of love which might be given in return.\n\n\"Bell,\" he said, \"you and I have always been dear friends.\"\n\n\"Yes; always.\"\n\n\"Why should we not be something more than friends?\"\n\nTo give Captain Dale his due I must declare that his voice was perfectly natural as he asked this question, and that he showed no signs of nervousness, either in his face or limbs. He had made up his mind to do it on that occasion, and he did it without any signs of outward disturbance. He asked his question, and then he waited for his answer. In this he was rather hard upon his cousin; for, though the question had certainly been asked in language that could not be mistaken, still the matter had not been put forward with all that fulness which a young lady, under such circumstances, has a right to expect.\n\nThey had sat down on the turf close to the ha-ha, and they were so near that Bernard was able to put out his hand with the view of taking that of his cousin within his own. But she contrived to keep her hands locked together, so that he merely held her gently by the wrist.\n\n\"I don't quite understand, Bernard,\" she said, after a minute's pause.\n\n\"Shall we be more than cousins? Shall we be man and wife?\"\n\nNow, at least, she could not say that she did not understand. If the question was ever asked plainly, Bernard Dale had asked it plainly. Shall we be man and wife? Few men, I fancy, dare to put it all at once in so abrupt a way, and yet I do not know that the English language affords any better terms for the question.\n\n\"Oh, Bernard! you have surprised me.\"\n\n\"I hope I have not pained you, Bell. I have been long thinking of this, but I am well aware that my own manner, even to you, has not been that of a lover. It is not in me to smile and say soft things, as Crosbie can. But I do not love you the less on that account. I have looked about for a wife, and I have thought that if I could gain you I should be very fortunate.\"\n\nHe did not then say anything about his uncle, and the eight hundred a year; but he fully intended to do so as soon as an opportunity should serve. He was quite of opinion that eight hundred a year and the good-will of a rich uncle were strong grounds for matrimony,--were grounds even for love; and he did not doubt but his cousin would see the matter in the same light.\n\n\"You are very good to me--more than good. Of course I know that. But, oh, Bernard! I did not expect this a bit.\"\n\n\"But you will answer me, Bell! Or if you would like time to think, or to speak to my aunt, perhaps you will answer me to-morrow?\"\n\n\"I think I ought to answer you now.\"\n\n\"Not if it be a refusal, Bell. Think well of it before you do that. I should have told you that our uncle wishes this match, and that he will remove any difficulty there might be about money.\"\n\n\"I do not care for money.\"\n\n\"But, as you were saying about Lily, one has to be prudent. Now, in our marriage, everything of that kind would be well arranged. My uncle has promised me that he would at once allow us--\"\n\n\"Stop, Bernard. You must not be led to suppose that any offer made by my uncle would help to purchase-- Indeed, there can be no need for us to talk about money.\"\n\n\"I wished to let you know the facts of the case, exactly as they are. And as to our uncle, I cannot but think that you would be glad, in such a matter, to have him on your side.\"\n\n\"Yes, I should be glad to have him on my side; that is, if I were going-- But my uncle's wishes could not influence my decision. The fact is, Bernard--\"\n\n\"Well, dearest, what is the fact?\"\n\n\"I have always regarded you rather as a brother than as anything else.\"\n\n\"But that regard may be changed.\"\n\n\"No; I think not. Bernard, I will go further and speak on at once. It cannot be changed. I know myself well enough to say that with certainty. It cannot be changed.\"\n\n\"You mean that you cannot love me?\"\n\n\"Not as you would have me do. I do love you very dearly,--very dearly, indeed. I would go to you in any trouble, exactly as I would go to a brother.\"\n\n\"And must that be all, Bell?\"\n\n\"Is not that all the sweetest love that can be felt? But you must not think me ungrateful, or proud. I know well that you are--are proposing to do for me much more than I deserve. Any girl might be proud of such an offer. But, dear Bernard--\"\n\n\"Bell, before you give me a final answer, sleep upon this and talk it over with your mother. Of course you were unprepared, and I cannot expect that you should promise me so much without a moment's consideration.\"\n\n\"I was unprepared, and therefore I have not answered you as I should have done. But as it has gone so far, I cannot let you leave me in uncertainty. It is not necessary that I should keep you waiting. In this matter I do know my own mind. Dear Bernard, indeed, indeed it cannot be as you have proposed.\"\n\nShe spoke in a low voice, and in a tone that had in it something of almost imploring humility; but, nevertheless, it conveyed to her cousin an assurance that she was in earnest; an assurance also that that earnest would not readily be changed. Was she not a Dale? And when did a Dale change his mind? For a while he sat silent by her; and she too, having declared her intention, refrained from further words. For some minutes they thus remained, looking down into the ha-ha. She still kept her old position, holding her hands clasped together over her knees; but he was now lying on his side, supporting his head upon his arm, with his face indeed turned towards her, but with his eyes fixed upon the grass. During this time, however, he was not idle. His cousin's answer, though it had grieved him, had not come upon him as a blow stunning him for a moment, and rendering him unfit for instant thought. He was grieved, more grieved than he had thought he would have been. The thing that he had wanted moderately, he now wanted the more in that it was denied to him. But he was able to perceive the exact truth of his position, and to calculate what might be his chances if he went on with his suit, and what his advantage if he at once abandoned it.\n\n\"I do not wish to press you unfairly, Bell; but may I ask if any other preference--\"\n\n\"There is no other preference,\" she answered. And then again they were silent for a minute or two.\n\n\"My uncle will be much grieved at this,\" he said at last.\n\n\"If that be all,\" said Bell, \"I do not think that we need either of us trouble ourselves. He can have no right to dispose of our hearts.\"\n\n\"I understand the taunt, Bell.\"\n\n\"Dear Bernard, there was no taunt. I intended none.\"\n\n\"I need not speak of my own grief. You cannot but know how deep it must be. Why should I have submitted myself to this mortification had not my heart been concerned? But that I will bear, if I must bear it--\" And then he paused, looking up at her.\n\n\"It will soon pass away,\" she said.\n\n\"I will accept it at any rate without complaint. But as to my uncle's feelings, it is open to me to speak, and to you, I should think, to listen without indifference. He has been kind to us both, and loves us two above any other living beings. It's not surprising that he should wish to see us married, and it will not be surprising if your refusal should be a great blow to him.\"\n\n\"I shall be sorry--very sorry.\"\n\n\"I also shall be sorry. I am now speaking of him. He has set his heart upon it; and as he has but few wishes, few desires, so is he the more constant in those which he expresses. When he knows this, I fear that we shall find him very stern.\"\n\n\"Then he will be unjust.\"\n\n\"No; he will not be unjust. He is always a just man. But he will be unhappy, and will, I fear, make others unhappy. Dear Bell, may not this thing remain for a while unsettled? You will not find that I take advantage of your goodness. I will not intrude it on you again,--say for a fortnight,--or till Crosbie shall be gone.\"\n\n\"No, no, no,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Why are you so eager in your noes? There can be no danger in such delay. I will not press you,--and you can let my uncle think that you have at least taken time for consideration.\"\n\n\"There are things as to which one is bound to answer at once. If I doubted myself, I would let you persuade me. But I do not doubt myself, and I should be wrong to keep you in suspense. Dear, dearest Bernard, it cannot be; and as it cannot be, you, as my brother, would bid me say so clearly. It cannot be.\"\n\nAs she made this last assurance, they heard the steps of Lily and her lover close to them, and they both felt that it would be well that their intercourse should thus be brought to a close. Neither had known how to get up and leave the place, and yet each had felt that nothing further could then be said.\n\n\"Did you ever see anything so sweet and affectionate and romantic?\" said Lily, standing over them and looking at them. \"And all the while we have been so practical and worldly. Do you know, Bell, that Adolphus seems to think we can't very well keep pigs in London. It makes me so unhappy.\"\n\n\"It does seem a pity,\" said Crosbie, \"for Lily seems to know all about pigs.\"\n\n\"Of course I do. I haven't lived in the country all my life for nothing. Oh, Bernard, I should so like to see you rolled down into the bottom of the ha-ha. Just remain there, and we'll do it between us.\"\n\nWhereupon Bernard got up, as did Bell also, and they all went in to tea.\n\n# CHAPTER IX. MRS. DALE'S LITTLE PARTY.\n\nThe next day was the day of the party. Not a word more was said on that evening between Bell and her cousin, at least, not a word more of any peculiar note; and when Crosbie suggested to his friend on the following morning that they should both step down and see how the preparations were getting on at the Small House, Bernard declined.\n\n\"You forget, my dear fellow, that I'm not in love as you are,\" said he.\n\n\"But I thought you were,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"No; not at all as you are. You are an accepted lover, and will be allowed to do anything,--whip the creams, and tune the piano, if you know how. I'm only a half sort of lover, meditating a mariage de convenance to oblige an uncle, and by no means required by the terms of my agreement to undergo a very rigid amount of drill. Your position is just the reverse.\" In saying all which Captain Dale was no doubt very false; but if falseness can be forgiven to a man in any position, it may be forgiven in that which he then filled. So Crosbie went down to the Small House alone.\n\n\"Dale wouldn't come,\" said he, speaking to the three ladies together, \"I suppose he's keeping himself up for the dance on the lawn.\"\n\n\"I hope he will be here in the evening,\" said Mrs. Dale. But Bell said never a word. She had determined, that under the existing circumstances, it would be only fair to her cousin that his offer and her answer to it should be kept secret. She knew why Bernard did not come across from the Great House with his friend, but she said nothing of her knowledge. Lily looked at her, but looked without speaking; and as for Mrs. Dale, she took no notice of the circumstance. Thus they passed the afternoon together without further mention of Bernard Dale; and it may be said, at any rate of Lily and Crosbie, that his presence was not missed.\n\nMrs. Eames, with her son and daughter, were the first to come. \"It is so nice of you to come early,\" said Lily, trying on the spur of the moment to say something which should sound pleasant and happy, but in truth using that form of welcome which to my ears sounds always the most ungracious. \"Ten minutes before the time named; and, of course, you must have understood that I meant thirty minutes after it!\" That is my interpretation of the words when I am thanked for coming early. But Mrs. Eames was a kind, patient, unexacting woman, who took all civil words as meaning civility. And, indeed, Lily had meant nothing else.\n\n\"Yes; we did come early,\" said Mrs. Eames, \"because Mary thought she would like to go up into the girls' room and just settle her hair, you know.\"\n\n\"So she shall,\" said Lily, who had taken Mary by the hand.\n\n\"And we knew we shouldn't be in the way. Johnny can go out into the garden if there's anything left to be done.\"\n\n\"He shan't be banished unless he likes it,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"If he finds us women too much for his unaided strength--\"\n\nJohn Eames muttered something about being very well as he was, and then got himself into an arm-chair. He had shaken hands with Lily, trying as he did so to pronounce articulately a little speech which he had prepared for the occasion. \"I have to congratulate you, Lily, and I hope with all my heart that you will be happy.\" The words were simple enough, and were not ill-chosen, but the poor young man never got them spoken. The word \"congratulate\" did reach Lily's ears, and she understood it all;--both the kindness of the intended speech and the reason why it could not be spoken.\n\n\"Thank you, John,\" she said; \"I hope I shall see so much of you in London. It will be so nice to have an old Guestwick friend near me.\" She had her own voice, and the pulses of her heart better under command than had he; but she also felt that the occasion was trying to her. The man had loved her honestly and truly,--still did love her, paying her the great homage of bitter grief in that he had lost her. Where is the girl who will not sympathize with such love and such grief, if it be shown only because it cannot be concealed, and be declared against the will of him who declares it?\n\nThen came in old Mrs. Hearn, whose cottage was not distant two minutes' walk from the Small House. She always called Mrs. Dale \"my dear,\" and petted the girls as though they had been children. When told of Lily's marriage, she had thrown up her hands with surprise, for she had still left in some corner of her drawers remnants of sugar-plums which she had bought for Lily. \"A London man is he? Well, well. I wish he lived in the country. Eight hundred a year, my dear?\" she had said to Mrs. Dale. \"That sounds nice down here, because we are all so poor. But I suppose eight hundred a year isn't very much up in London?\"\n\n\"The squire's coming, I suppose, isn't he?\" said Mrs. Hearn, as she seated herself on the sofa close to Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Yes, he'll be here by-and-by; unless he changes his mind, you know. He doesn't stand on ceremony with me.\"\n\n\"He change his mind! When did you ever know Christopher Dale change his mind?\"\n\n\"He is pretty constant, Mrs. Hearn.\"\n\n\"If he promised to give a man a penny, he'd give it. But if he promised to take away a pound, he'd take it, though it cost him years to get it. He's going to turn me out of my cottage, he says.\"\n\n\"Nonsense, Mrs. Hearn!\"\n\n\"Jolliffe came and told me\"--Jolliffe, I should explain, was the bailiff,--\"that if I didn't like it as it was, I might leave it, and that the squire could get double the rent for it. Now all I asked was that he should do a little painting in the kitchen; and the wood is all as black as his hat.\"\n\n\"I thought it was understood you were to paint inside.\"\n\n\"How can I do it, my dear, with a hundred and forty pounds for everything? I must live, you know! And he that has workmen about him every day of the year! And was that a message to send to me, who have lived in the parish for fifty years? Here he is.\" And Mrs. Hearn majestically raised herself from her seat as the squire entered the room.\n\nWith him entered Mr. and Mrs. Boyce, from the parsonage, with Dick Boyce, the ungrown gentleman, and two girl Boyces, who were fourteen and fifteen years of age. Mrs. Dale, with the amount of good-nature usual on such occasions, asked reproachfully why Jane, and Charles, and Florence, and Bessy, did not come,--Boyce being a man who had his quiver full of them,--and Mrs. Boyce, giving the usual answer, declared that she already felt that they had come as an avalanche.\n\n\"But where are the--the--the young men?\" asked Lily, assuming a look of mock astonishment.\n\n\"They'll be across in two or three hours' time,\" said the squire. \"They both dressed for dinner, and, as I thought, made themselves very smart; but for such a grand occasion as this they thought a second dressing necessary. How do you do, Mrs. Hearn? I hope you are quite well. No rheumatism left, eh?\" This the squire said very loud into Mrs. Hearn's ear. Mrs. Hearn was perhaps a little hard of hearing; but it was very little, and she hated to be thought deaf. She did not, moreover, like to be thought rheumatic. This the squire knew, and therefore his mode of address was not good-natured.\n\n\"You needn't make me jump so, Mr. Dale. I'm pretty well now, thank ye. I did have a twinge in the spring,--that cottage is so badly built for draughts! 'I wonder you can live in it,' my sister said to me the last time she was over. I suppose I should be better off over with her at Hamersham, only one doesn't like to move, you know, after living fifty years in one parish.\"\n\n\"You mustn't think of going away from us,\" Mrs. Boyce said, speaking by no means loud, but slowly and plainly, hoping thereby to flatter the old woman. But the old woman understood it all. \"She's a sly creature, is Mrs. Boyce,\" Mrs. Hearn said to Mrs. Dale, before the evening was out. There are some old people whom it is very hard to flatter, and with whom it is, nevertheless, almost impossible to live unless you do flatter them.\n\nAt last the two heroes came in across the lawn at the drawing-room window; and Lily, as they entered, dropped a low curtsey before them, gently swelling down upon the ground with her light muslin dress, till she looked like some wondrous flower that had bloomed upon the carpet, and putting her two hands, with the backs of her fingers pressed together, on the buckle of her girdle, she said, \"We are waiting upon your honours' kind grace, and feel how much we owe to you for favouring our poor abode.\" And then she gently rose up again, smiling, oh, so sweetly, on the man she loved, and the puffings and swellings went out of her muslin.\n\nI think there is nothing in the world so pretty as the conscious little tricks of love played off by a girl towards the man she loves, when she has made up her mind boldly that all the world may know that she has given herself away to him.\n\nI am not sure that Crosbie liked it all as much as he should have done. The bold assurance of her love when they two were alone together he did like. What man does not like such assurances on such occasions? But perhaps he would have been better pleased had Lily shown more reticence,--been more secret, as it were, as to her feelings, when others were around them. It was not that he accused her in his thoughts of any want of delicacy. He read her character too well;--was, if not quite aright in his reading of it, at least too nearly so to admit of his making against her any such accusation as that. It was the calf-like feeling that was disagreeable to him. He did not like to be presented, even to the world of Allington, as a victim caught for the sacrifice, and bound with ribbon for the altar. And then there lurked behind it all a feeling that it might be safer that the thing should not be so openly manifested before all the world. Of course, everybody knew that he was engaged to Lily Dale; nor had he, as he said to himself, perhaps too frequently, the slightest idea of breaking from that engagement. But then the marriage might possibly be delayed. He had not discussed that matter yet with Lily, having, indeed, at the first moment of his gratified love, created some little difficulty for himself by pressing for an early day. \"I will refuse you nothing,\" she had said to him; \"but do not make it too soon.\" He saw, therefore, before him some little embarrassment, and was inclined to wish that Lily would abstain from that manner which seemed to declare to all the world that she was about to be married immediately. \"I must speak to her to-morrow,\" he said to himself, as he accepted her salute with a mock gravity equal to her own.\n\nPoor Lily! How little she understood as yet what was passing through his mind. Had she known his wish she would have wrapped up her love carefully in a napkin, so that no one should have seen it,--no one but he, when he might choose to have the treasure uncovered for his sight. And it was all for his sake that she had been thus open in her ways. She had seen girls who were half ashamed of their love; but she would never be ashamed of hers or of him. She had given herself to him; and now all the world might know it, if all the world cared for such knowledge. Why should she be ashamed of that which, to her thinking, was so great an honour to her? She had heard of girls who would not speak of their love, arguing to themselves cannily that there may be many a slip between the cup and the lip. There could be no need of any such caution with her. There could surely be no such slip! Should there be such a fall,--should any such fate, either by falseness or misfortune, come upon her,--no such caution could be of service to save her. The cup would have been so shattered in its fall that no further piecing of its parts would be in any way possible. So much as this she did not exactly say to herself; but she felt it all, and went bravely forward,--bold in her love, and careful to hide it from none who chanced to see it.\n\nThey had gone through the ceremony with the cake and teacups, and had decided that, at any rate, the first dance or two should be held upon the lawn when the last of the guests arrived.\n\n\"Oh, Adolphus, I am so glad he has come,\" said Lily. \"Do try to like him.\" Of Dr. Crofts, who was the new comer, she had sometimes spoken to her lover, but she had never coupled her sister's name with that of the doctor, even in speaking to him. Nevertheless, Crosbie had in some way conceived the idea that this Crofts either had been, or was, or was to be, in love with Bell; and as he was prepared to advocate his friend Dale's claims in that quarter, he was not particularly anxious to welcome the doctor as a thoroughly intimate friend of the family. He knew nothing as yet of Dale's offer, or of Bell's refusal, but he was prepared for war, if war should be necessary. Of the squire, at the present moment, he was not very fond; but if his destiny intended to give him a wife out of this family, he should prefer the owner of Allington and nephew of Lord De Guest as a brother-in-law to a village doctor,--as he took upon himself, in his pride, to call Dr. Crofts.\n\n\"It is very unfortunate,\" said he, \"but I never do like Paragons.\"\n\n\"But you must like this Paragon. Not that he is a Paragon at all, for he smokes and hunts, and does all manner of wicked things.\" And then she went forward to welcome her friend.\n\nDr. Crofts was a slight, spare man, about five feet nine in height, with very bright dark eyes, a broad forehead, with dark hair that almost curled, but which did not come so forward over his brow as it should have done for purposes of beauty,--with a thin well-cut nose, and a mouth that would have been perfect had the lips been a little fuller. The lower part of his face, when seen alone, had in it somewhat of sternness, which, however, was redeemed by the brightness of his eyes. And yet an artist would have declared that the lower features of his face were by far the more handsome.\n\nLily went across to him and greeted him heartily, declaring how glad she was to have him there. \"And I must introduce you to Mr. Crosbie,\" she said, as though she was determined to carry her point. The two men shook hands with each other, coldly, without saying a word, as young men are apt to do when they are brought together in that way. Then they separated at once, somewhat to the disappointment of Lily. Crosbie stood off by himself, both his eyes turned up towards the ceiling, and looking as though he meant to give himself airs; while Crofts got himself quickly up to the fireplace, making civil little speeches to Mrs. Dale, Mrs. Boyce, and Mrs. Hearn. And then at last he made his way round to Bell.\n\n\"I am so glad,\" he said, \"to congratulate you on your sister's engagement.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Bell; \"we knew that you would be glad to hear of her happiness.\"\n\n\"Indeed, I am glad; and thoroughly hope that she may be happy. You all like him, do you not?\"\n\n\"We like him very much.\"\n\n\"And I am told that he is well off. He is a very fortunate man,--very fortunate,--very fortunate.\"\n\n\"Of course we think so,\" said Bell. \"Not, however, because he is rich.\"\n\n\"No; not because he is rich. But because, being worthy of such happiness, his circumstances should enable him to marry, and to enjoy it.\"\n\n\"Yes, exactly,\" said Bell. \"That is just it.\" Then she sat down, and in sitting down put an end to the conversation. \"That is just it,\" she had said. But as soon as the words were spoken she declared to herself that it was not so, and that Crofts was wrong. \"We love him,\" she said to herself, \"not because he is rich enough to marry without anxious thought, but because he dares to marry although he is not rich.\" And then she told herself that she was angry with the doctor.\n\nAfter that Dr. Crofts got off towards the door, and stood there by himself, leaning against the wall, with the thumbs of both his hands stuck into the armholes of his waistcoat. People said that he was a shy man. I suppose he was shy, and yet he was a man that was by no means afraid of doing anything that he had to do. He could speak before a multitude without being abashed, whether it was a multitude of men or of women. He could be very fixed too in his own opinion, and eager, if not violent, in the prosecution of his purpose. But he could not stand and say little words, when he had in truth nothing to say. He could not keep his ground when he felt that he was not using the ground upon which he stood. He had not learned the art of assuming himself to be of importance in whatever place he might find himself. It was this art which Crosbie had learned, and by this art that he had flourished. So Crofts retired and leaned against the wall near the door; and Crosbie came forward and shone like an Apollo among all the guests. \"How is it that he does it?\" said John Eames to himself, envying the perfect happiness of the London man of fashion.\n\nAt last Lily got the dancers out upon the lawn, and then they managed to go through one quadrille. But it was found that it did not answer. The music of the single fiddle which Crosbie had hired from Guestwick was not sufficient for the purpose; and then the grass, though it was perfect for purposes of croquet, was not pleasant to the feet for dancing.\n\n\"This is very nice,\" said Bernard to his cousin. \"I don't know anything that could be nicer; but perhaps--\"\n\n\"I know what you mean,\" said Lily. \"But I shall stay here. There's no touch of romance about any of you. Look at the moon there at the back of the steeple. I don't mean to go in all night.\" Then she walked off by one of the paths, and her lover went after her.\n\n\"Don't you like the moon?\" she said, as she took his arm, to which she was now so accustomed that she hardly thought of it as she took it.\n\n\"Like the moon?--well; I fancy I like the sun better. I don't quite believe in moonlight. I think it does best to talk about when one wants to be sentimental.\"\n\n\"Ah; that is just what I fear. That is what I say to Bell when I tell her that her romance will fade as the roses do. And then I shall have to learn that prose is more serviceable than poetry, and that the mind is better than the heart, and--and that money is better than love. It's all coming, I know; and yet I do like the moonlight.\"\n\n\"And the poetry,--and the love?\"\n\n\"Yes. The poetry much, and the love more. To be loved by you is sweeter even than any of my dreams,--is better than all the poetry I have read.\"\n\n\"Dearest Lily,\" and his unchecked arm stole round her waist.\n\n\"It is the meaning of the moonlight, and the essence of the poetry,\" continued the impassioned girl. \"I did not know then why I liked such things, but now I know. It was because I longed to be loved.\"\n\n\"And to love.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes. I would be nothing without that. But that, you know, is your delight,--or should be. The other is mine. And yet it is a delight to love you; to know that I may love you.\"\n\n\"You mean that this is the realization of your romance.\"\n\n\"Yes; but it must not be the end of it, Adolphus. You must like the soft twilight, and the long evenings when we shall be alone; and you must read to me the books I love, and you must not teach me to think that the world is hard, and dry, and cruel,--not yet. I tell Bell so very often; but you must not say so to me.\"\n\n\"It shall not be dry and cruel, if I can prevent it.\"\n\n\"You understand what I mean, dearest. I will not think it dry and cruel, even though sorrow should come upon us, if you-- I think you know what I mean.\"\n\n\"If I am good to you.\"\n\n\"I am not afraid of that;--I am not the least afraid of that. You do not think that I could ever distrust you? But you must not be ashamed to look at the moonlight, and to read poetry, and to--\"\n\n\"To talk nonsense, you mean.\"\n\nBut as he said it, he pressed her closer to his side, and his tone was pleasant to her.\n\n\"I suppose I'm talking nonsense now?\" she said, pouting. \"You liked me better when I was talking about the pigs; didn't you?\"\n\n\"No; I like you best now.\"\n\n\"And why didn't you like me then? Did I say anything to offend you?\"\n\n\"I like you best now, because--\"\n\nThey were standing in the narrow pathway of the gate leading from the bridge into the gardens of the Great House, and the shadow of the thick-spreading laurels was around them. But the moonlight still pierced brightly through the little avenue, and she, as she looked up to him, could see the form of his face and the loving softness of his eye.\n\n\"Because--,\" said he; and then he stooped over her and pressed her closely, while she put up her lips to his, standing on tip-toe that she might reach to his face.\n\n\"Oh, my love!\" she said. \"My love! my love!\"\n\nAs Crosbie walked back to the Great House that night, he made a firm resolution that no consideration of worldly welfare should ever induce him to break his engagement with Lily Dale. He went somewhat further also, and determined that he would not put off the marriage for more than six or eight months, or, at the most, ten, if he could possibly get his affairs arranged in that time. To be sure, he must give up everything,--all the aspirations and ambition of his life; but then, as he declared to himself somewhat mournfully, he was prepared to do that. Such were his resolutions, and, as he thought of them in bed, he came to the conclusion that few men were less selfish than he was.\n\n\"But what will they say to us for staying away?\" said Lily, recovering herself. \"And I ought to be making the people dance, you know. Come along, and do make yourself nice. Do waltz with Mary Eames;--pray, do. If you don't, I won't speak to you all night!\"\n\nActing under which threat, Crosbie did, on his return, solicit the honour of that young lady's hand, thereby elating her into a seventh heaven of happiness. What could the world afford better than a waltz with such a partner as Adolphus Crosbie? And poor Mary Eames could waltz well; though she could not talk much as she danced, and would pant a good deal when she stopped. She put too much of her energy into the motion, and was too anxious to do the mechanical part of the work in a manner that should be satisfactory to her partner. \"Oh! thank you;--it's very nice. I shall be able to go on--again directly.\" Her conversation with Crosbie did not get much beyond that, and yet she felt that she had never done better than on this occasion.\n\nThough there were, at most, not above five couples of dancers, and though they who did not dance, such as the squire and Mr. Boyce, and a curate from a neighbouring parish, had, in fact, nothing to amuse them, the affair was kept on very merrily for a considerable number of hours. Exactly at twelve o'clock there was a little supper, which, no doubt, served to relieve Mrs. Hearn's ennui, and at which Mrs. Boyce also seemed to enjoy herself. As to the Mrs. Boyces on such occasions, I profess that I feel no pity. They are generally happy in their children's happiness, or if not, they ought to be. At any rate, they are simply performing a manifest duty, which duty, in their time, was performed on their behalf. But on what account do the Mrs. Hearns betake themselves to such gatherings? Why did that ancient lady sit there hour after hour yawning, longing for her bed, looking every ten minutes at her watch, while her old bones were stiff and sore, and her old ears pained with the noise? It could hardly have been simply for the sake of the supper. After the supper, however, her maid took her across to her cottage, and Mrs. Boyce also then stole away home, and the squire went off with some little parade, suggesting to the young men that they should make no noise in the house as they returned. But the poor curate remained, talking a dull word every now and then to Mrs. Dale, and looking on with tantalized eyes at the joys which the world had prepared for others than him. I must say that I think that public opinion and the bishops together are too hard upon curates in this particular.\n\nIn the latter part of the night's delight, when time and practice had made them all happy together, John Eames stood up for the first time to dance with Lily. She had done all she could, short of asking him, to induce him to do her this favour; for she felt that it would be a favour. How great had been the desire on his part to ask her, and, at the same time, how great the repugnance, Lily, perhaps, did not quite understand. And yet she understood much of it. She knew that he was not angry with her. She knew that he was suffering from the injured pride of futile love, almost as much as from the futile love itself. She wished to put him at his ease in this; but she did not quite give him credit for the full sincerity, and the upright, uncontrolled heartiness of his feelings.\n\nAt length he did come up to her, and though, in truth, she was engaged, she at once accepted his offer. Then she tripped across the room. \"Adolphus,\" she said, \"I can't dance with you, though I said I would. John Eames has asked me, and I haven't stood up with him before. You understand, and you'll be a good boy, won't you?\"\n\nCrosbie, not being in the least jealous, was a good boy, and sat himself down to rest, hidden behind a door.\n\nFor the first few minutes the conversation between Eames and Lily was of a very matter-of-fact kind. She repeated her wish that she might see him in London, and he said that of course he should come and call. Then there was silence for a little while, and they went through their figure dancing.\n\n\"I don't at all know yet when we are to be married,\" said Lily, as soon as they were again standing together.\n\n\"No; I dare say not,\" said Eames.\n\n\"But not this year, I suppose. Indeed, I should say, of course not.\"\n\n\"In the spring, perhaps,\" suggested Eames. He had an unconscious desire that it might be postponed to some Greek kalends, and yet he did not wish to injure Lily.\n\n\"The reason I mention it is this, that we should be so very glad if you could be here. We all love you so much, and I should so like to have you here on that day.\"\n\nWhy is it that girls so constantly do this,--so frequently ask men who have loved them to be present at their marriages with other men? There is no triumph in it. It is done in sheer kindness and affection. They intend to offer something which shall soften and not aggravate the sorrow that they have caused. \"You can't marry me yourself,\" the lady seems to say. \"But the next greatest blessing which I can offer you shall be yours,--you shall see me married to somebody else.\" I fully appreciate the intention, but in honest truth, I doubt the eligibility of the proffered entertainment.\n\nOn the present occasion John Eames seemed to be of this opinion, for he did not at once accept the invitation.\n\n\"Will you not oblige me so far as that?\" said she softly.\n\n\"I would do anything to oblige you,\" said he gruffly; \"almost anything.\"\n\n\"But not that?\"\n\n\"No; not that. I could not do that.\" Then he went off upon his figure, and when they were next both standing together, they remained silent till their turn for dancing had again come. Why was it, that after that night Lily thought more of John Eames than ever she had thought before;--felt for him, I mean, a higher respect, as for a man who had a will of his own?\n\nAnd in that quadrille Crofts and Bell had been dancing together, and they also had been talking of Lily's marriage. \"A man may undergo what he likes for himself,\" he had said, \"but he has no right to make a woman undergo poverty.\"\n\n\"Perhaps not,\" said Bell.\n\n\"That which is no suffering for a man,--which no man should think of for himself,--will make a hell on earth for a woman.\"\n\n\"I suppose it would,\" said Bell, answering him without a sign of feeling in her face or voice. But she took in every word that he spoke, and disputed their truth inwardly with all the strength of her heart and mind, and with the very vehemence of her soul. \"As if a woman cannot bear more than a man!\" she said to herself, as she walked the length of the room alone, when she had got herself free from the doctor's arm.\n\n# CHAPTER X. MRS. LUPEX AND AMELIA ROPER.\n\nI should simply mislead a confiding reader if I were to tell him that Mrs. Lupex was an amiable woman. Perhaps the fact that she was not amiable is the one great fault that should be laid to her charge; but that fault had spread itself so widely, and had cropped forth in so many different places of her life, like a strong rank plant that will show itself all over a garden, that it may almost be said that it made her odious in every branch of life, and detestable alike to those who knew her little and to those who knew her much. If a searcher could have got at the inside spirit of the woman, that searcher would have found that she wished to go right,--that she did make, or at any rate promise to herself that she would make, certain struggles to attain decency and propriety. But it was so natural to her to torment those whose misfortune brought them near to her, and especially that wretched man who in an evil day had taken her to his bosom as his wife, that decency fled from her, and propriety would not live in her quarters.\n\nMrs. Lupex was, as I have already described her, a woman not without some feminine attraction in the eyes of those who like morning negligence and evening finery, and do not object to a long nose somewhat on one side. She was clever in her way, and could say smart things. She could flatter also, though her very flattery had always in it something that was disagreeable. And she must have had some power of will, as otherwise her husband would have escaped from her before the days of which I am writing. Otherwise, also, she could hardly have obtained her footing and kept it in Mrs. Roper's drawing-room. For though the hundred pounds a year, either paid, or promised to be paid, was matter with Mrs. Roper of vast consideration, nevertheless the first three months of Mrs. Lupex's sojourn in Burton Crescent were not over before the landlady of that house was most anxiously desirous of getting herself quit of her married boarders.\n\nI shall perhaps best describe a little incident that had occurred in Burton Crescent during the absence of our friend Eames, and the manner in which things were going on in that locality, by giving at length two letters which Johnny received by post at Guestwick on the morning after Mrs. Dale's party. One was from his friend Cradell, and the other from the devoted Amelia. In this instance I will give that from the gentleman first, presuming that I shall best consult my reader's wishes by keeping the greater delicacy till the last.\n\nIncome-tax Office, September, 186--.\n\nMY DEAR JOHNNY,--We have had a terrible affair in the Crescent; and I really hardly know how to tell you; and yet I must do it, for I want your advice. You know the sort of standing that I was on with Mrs. Lupex, and perhaps you remember what we were saying on the platform at the station. I have, no doubt, been fond of her society, as I might be of that of any other friend. I knew, of course, that she was a fine woman; and if her husband chose to be jealous, I couldn't help that. But I never intended anything wrong; and, if it was necessary, couldn't I call you as a witness to prove it? I never spoke a word to her out of Mrs. Roper's drawing-room; and Miss Spruce, or Mrs. Roper, or somebody has always been there. You know he drinks horribly sometimes, but I do not think he ever gets downright drunk. Well, he came home last night about nine o'clock after one of these bouts. From what Jemima says [Jemima was Mrs. Roper's parlour-maid], I believe he had been at it down at the theatre for three days. We hadn't seen him since Tuesday. He went straight into the parlour and sent up Jemima to me, to say that he wanted to see me. Mrs. Lupex was in the room and heard the girl summon me, and, jumping up, she declared that if there was going to be blood shed she would leave the house. There was nobody else in the room but Miss Spruce, and she didn't say a word, but took her candle and went upstairs. You must own it looked very uncomfortable. What was I to do with a drunken man down in the parlour? However, she seemed to think I ought to go. \"If he comes up here,\" said she, \"I shall be the victim. You little know of what that man is capable when his wrath has been inflamed by wine!\" Now, I think you are aware that I am not likely to be very much afraid of any man; but why was I to be got into a row in such a way as this? I hadn't done anything. And then, if there was to be a quarrel, and anything was to come of it, as she seemed to expect,--like bloodshed, I mean, or a fight, or if he were to knock me on the head with the poker, where should I be at my office? A man in a public office, as you and I are, can't quarrel like anybody else. It was this that I felt so much at the moment. \"Go down to him,\" said she, \"unless you wish to see me murdered at your feet.\" Fisher says, that if what I say is true, they must have arranged it all between them. I don't think that; for I do believe that she really is fond of me. And then everybody knows that they never do agree about anything. But she certainly did implore me to go down to him. Well, I went down; and, as I got to the bottom of the stairs, where I found Jemima, I heard him walking up and down the parlour. \"Take care of yourself, Mr. Cradell,\" said the girl; and I could see by her face that she was in a terrible fright.\n\nAt that moment I happened to see my hat on the hall table, and it occurred to me that I ought to put myself into the hands of a friend. Of course, I was not afraid of that man in the dining-room; but should I have been justified in engaging in a struggle, perhaps for dear life, in Mrs. Roper's house? I was bound to think of her interests. So I took up my hat, and deliberately walked out of the front door. \"Tell him,\" said I to Jemima, \"that I'm not at home.\" And so I went away direct to Fisher's, meaning to send him back to Lupex as my friend; but Fisher was at his chess-club.\n\nAs I thought there was no time to be lost on such an occasion as this, I went down to the club and called him out. You know what a cool fellow Fisher is. I don't suppose anything would ever excite him. When I told him the story, he said that he would sleep upon it; and I had to walk up and down before the club while he finished his game. Fisher seemed to think that I might go back to Burton Crescent; but, of course, I knew that that would be out of the question. So it ended in my going home and sleeping on his sofa, and sending for some of my things in the morning. I wanted him to get up and see Lupex before going to the office this morning. But he said it would be better to put it off, and so he will call upon him at the theatre immediately after office hours.\n\nI want you to write to me at once saying what you know about the matter. I ask you, as I don't want to lug in any of the other people at Roper's. It is very uncomfortable, as I can't exactly leave her at once because of last quarter's money, otherwise I should cut and run; for the house is not the sort of place either for you or me. You may take my word for that, Master Johnny. And I could tell you something, too, about A. R., only I don't want to make mischief. But do you write immediately. And now I think of it, you had better write to Fisher, so that he can show your letter to Lupex,--just saying, that to the best of your belief there had never been anything between her and me but mere friendship; and that, of course, you, as my friend, must have known everything. Whether I shall go back to Roper's to-night will depend on what Fisher says after the interview.\n\nGood-by, old fellow! I hope you are enjoying yourself, and that L. D. is quite well.--Your sincere friend,\n\nJohn Eames read this letter over twice before he opened that from Amelia. He had never yet received a letter from Miss Roper; and felt very little of that ardour for its perusal which young men generally experience on the receipt of a first letter from a young lady. The memory of Amelia was at the present moment distasteful to him; and he would have thrown the letter unopened into the fire, had he not felt it might be dangerous to do so. As regarded his friend Cradell, he could not but feel ashamed of him,--ashamed of him, not for running away from Mr. Lupex, but for excusing his escape on false pretences.\n\nAnd then, at last, he opened the letter from Amelia. \"Dearest John,\" it began; and as he read the words, he crumpled the paper up between his fingers. It was written in a fair female hand, with sharp points instead of curves to the letters, but still very legible, and looking as though there were a decided purport in every word of it.\n\nDEAREST JOHN,--It feels so strange to me to write to you in such language as this. And yet you are dearest, and have I not a right to call you so? And are you not my own, and am not I yours?\n\nAgain he crunched the paper up in his hand, and, as he did so, he muttered words which I need not repeat at length. But still he went on with his letter.\n\nI know that we understand each other perfectly, and when that is the case, heart should be allowed to speak openly to heart. Those are my feelings, and I believe that you will find them reciprocal in your own bosom. Is it not sweet to be loved? I find it so. And, dearest John, let me assure you, with open candour, that there is no room for jealousy in this breast with regard to you. I have too much confidence for that, I can assure you, both in your honour and in my own--I would say charms, only you would call me vain. You must not suppose that I meant what I said about L. D. Of course, you will be glad to see the friends of your childhood; and it would be far from your Amelia's heart to begrudge you such delightful pleasure. Your friends will, I hope, some day be my friends. [Another crunch.] And if there be any one among them, any real L. D. whom you have specially liked, I will receive her to my heart, specially also.\n\nThis assurance on the part of his Amelia was too much for him, and he threw the letter from him, thinking whence he might get relief--whether from suicide or from the colonies; but presently he took it up again, and drained the bitter cup to the bottom.\n\nAnd if I seemed petulant to you before you went away, you must forgive your own Amelia. I had nothing before me but misery for the month of your absence. There is no one here congenial to my feelings,--of course not. And you would not wish me to be happy in your absence,--would you? I can assure you, let your wishes be what they may, I never can be happy again unless you are with me. Write to me one little line, and tell me that you are grateful to me for my devotion.\n\nAnd now, I must tell you that we have had a sad affair in the house; and I do not think that your friend Mr. Cradell has behaved at all well. You remember how he has been always going on with Mrs. Lupex. Mother was quite unhappy about it, though she didn't like to say anything. Of course, when a lady's name is concerned, it is particular. But Lupex has become dreadful jealous during the last week; and we all knew that something was coming. She is an artful woman, but I don't think she meant anything bad--only to drive her husband to desperation. He came here yesterday in one of his tantrums, and wanted to see Cradell; but he got frightened, and took his hat and went off. Now, that wasn't quite right. If he was innocent, why didn't he stand his ground and explain the mistake? As mother says, it gives the house such a name. Lupex swore last night that he'd be off to the Income-tax Office this morning, and have Cradell out before all the commissioners, and clerks, and everybody. If he does that, it will get into the papers, and all London will be full of it. She would like it, I know; for all she cares for is to be talked about; but only think what it will be for mother's house. I wish you were here; for your high prudence and courage would set everything right at once,--at least, I think so.\n\nI shall count the minutes till I get an answer to this, and shall envy the postman who will have your letter before it will reach me. Do write at once. If I do not hear by Monday morning I shall think that something is the matter. Even though you are among your dear old friends, surely you can find a moment to write to your own Amelia.\n\nMother is very unhappy about this affair of the Lupexes. She says that if you were here to advise her she should not mind it so much. It is very hard upon her, for she does strive to make the house respectable and comfortable for everybody. I would send my duty and love to your dear mamma, if I only knew her, as I hope I shall do one day, and to your sister, and to L. D. also, if you like to tell her how we are situated together. So, now, no more from your\n\nAlways affectionate sweetheart,\n\nPoor Eames did not feel the least gratified by any part of this fond letter; but the last paragraph of it was the worst. Was it to be endured by him that this woman should send her love to his mother and to his sister, and even to Lily Dale! He felt that there was a pollution in the very mention of Lily's name by such an one as Amelia Roper. And yet Amelia Roper was, as she had assured him,--his own. Much as he disliked her at the present moment, he did believe that he was--her own. He did feel that she had obtained a certain property in him, and that his destiny in life would tie him to her. He had said very few words of love to her at any time--very few, at least, that were themselves of any moment; but among those few there had undoubtedly been one or two in which he had told her that he loved her. And he had written to her that fatal note! Upon the whole, would it not be as well for him to go out to the great reservoir behind Guestwick, by which the Hamersham Canal was fed with its waters, and put an end to his miserable existence?\n\nOn that same day he did write a letter to Fisher, and he wrote also to Cradell. As to those letters he felt no difficulty. To Fisher he declared his belief that Cradell was innocent as he was himself as regarded Mrs. Lupex. \"I don't think he is the sort of man to make up to a married woman,\" he said, somewhat to Cradell's displeasure, when the letter reached the Income-tax Office; for that gentleman was not averse to the reputation for success in love which the little adventure was, as he thought, calculated to give him among his brother clerks. At the first bursting of the shell, when that desperately jealous man was raging in the parlour, incensed by the fumes both of wine and love, Cradell had felt that the affair was disagreeably painful. But on the morning of the third day--for he had passed two nights on his friend Fisher's sofa--he had begun to be somewhat proud of it, and did not dislike to hear Mrs. Lupex's name in the mouths of the other clerks. When, therefore, Fisher read to him the letter from Guestwick, he hardly was pleased with his friend's tone. \"Ha, ha, ha,\" said he, laughing. \"That's just what I wanted him to say. Make up to a married woman, indeed. No; I'm the last man in London to do that sort of thing.\"\n\n\"Upon my word, Caudle, I think you are,\" said Fisher; \"the very last man.\"\n\nAnd then poor Cradell was not happy. On that afternoon he boldly went to Burton Crescent, and ate his dinner there. Neither Mr. nor Mrs. Lupex were to be seen, nor were their names mentioned to him by Mrs. Roper. In the course of the evening he did pluck up courage to ask Miss Spruce where they were; but that ancient lady merely shook her head solemnly, and declared that she knew nothing about such goings on--no, not she.\n\nBut what was John Eames to do as to that letter from Amelia Roper? He felt that any answer to it would be very dangerous, and yet that he could not safely leave it unanswered. He walked off by himself across Guestwick Common, and through the woods of Guestwick Manor, up by the big avenue of elms in Lord De Guest's park, trying to resolve how he might rescue himself from this scrape. Here, over the same ground, he had wandered scores of times in his earlier years, when he knew nothing beyond the innocency of his country home, thinking of Lily Dale, and swearing to himself that she should be his wife. Here he had strung together his rhymes, and fed his ambition with high hopes, building gorgeous castles in the air, in all of which Lilian reigned as a queen; and though in those days he had known himself to be awkward, poor, uncared for by any in the world except his mother and his sister, yet he had been happy in his hopes--happy in his hopes, even though he had never taught himself really to believe that they would be realized. But now there was nothing in his hopes or thoughts to make him happy. Everything was black, and wretched, and ruinous. What would it matter, after all, even if he should marry Amelia Roper, seeing that Lily was to be given to another? But then the idea of Amelia as he had seen her that night through the chink in the door came upon his memory, and he confessed to himself that life with such a wife as that would be a living death.\n\nAt one moment he thought that he would tell his mother everything, and leave her to write an answer to Amelia's letter. Should the worst come to the worst, the Ropers could not absolutely destroy him. That they could bring an action against him, and have him locked up for a term of years, and dismissed from his office, and exposed in all the newspapers, he seemed to know. That might all, however, be endured, if only the gauntlet could be thrown down for him by some one else. The one thing which he felt that he could not do was, to write to a girl whom he had professed to love, and tell her that he did not love her. He knew that he could not himself form such words upon the paper; nor, as he was well aware, could he himself find the courage to tell her to her face that he had changed his mind. He knew that he must become the victim of his Amelia, unless he could find some friendly knight to do battle in his favour; and then again he thought of his mother.\n\nBut when he returned home he was as far as ever from any resolve to tell her how he was situated. I may say that his walk had done him no good, and that he had not made up his mind to anything. He had been building those pernicious castles in the air during more than half the time; not castles in the building of which he could make himself happy, as he had done in the old days, but black castles, with cruel dungeons, into which hardly a ray of light could find its way. In all these edifices his imagination pictured to him Lily as the wife of Mr. Crosbie. He accepted that as a fact, and then went to work in his misery, making her as wretched as himself, through the misconduct and harshness of her husband. He tried to think, and to resolve what he would do; but there is no task so hard as that of thinking, when the mind has an objection to the matter brought before it. The mind, under such circumstances, is like a horse that is brought to the water, but refuses to drink. So Johnny returned to his home, still doubting whether or no he would answer Amelia's letter. And if he did not answer it, how would he conduct himself on his return to Burton Crescent?\n\nI need hardly say that Miss Roper, in writing her letter, had been aware of all this, and that Johnny's position had been carefully prepared for him by--his affectionate sweetheart.\n\n# CHAPTER XI. SOCIAL LIFE.\n\nMr. and Mrs. Lupex had eaten a sweetbread together in much connubial bliss on that day which had seen Cradell returning to Mrs. Roper's hospitable board. They had together eaten a sweetbread, with some other delicacies of the season, in the neighbourhood of the theatre, and had washed down all unkindness with bitter beer and brandy-and-water. But of this reconciliation Cradell had not heard; and when he saw them come together into the drawing-room, a few minutes after the question he had addressed to Miss Spruce, he was certainly surprised.\n\nLupex was not an ill-natured man, nor one naturally savage by disposition. He was a man fond of sweetbread and little dinners, and one to whom hot brandy-and-water was too dear. Had the wife of his bosom been a good helpmate to him, he might have gone through the world, if not respectably, at any rate without open disgrace. But she was a woman who left a man no solace except that to be found in brandy-and-water. For eight years they had been man and wife; and sometimes--I grieve to say it--he had been driven almost to hope that she would commit a married woman's last sin, and leave him. In his misery, any mode of escape would have been welcome to him. Had his energy been sufficient he would have taken his scene-painting capabilities off to Australia,--or to the farthest shifting of scenes known on the world's stage. But he was an easy, listless, self-indulgent man; and at any moment, let his misery be as keen as might be, a little dinner, a few soft words, and a glass of brandy-and-water would bring him round. The second glass would make him the fondest husband living; but the third would restore to him the memory of all his wrongs, and give him courage against his wife or all the world,--even to the detriment of the furniture around him, should a stray poker chance to meet his hand. All these peculiarities of his character were not, however, known to Cradell; and when our friend saw him enter the drawing-room with his wife on his arm, he was astonished.\n\n\"Mr. Cradell, your hand,\" said Lupex, who had advanced as far as the second glass of brandy-and-water, but had not been allowed to go beyond it. \"There has been a misunderstanding between us; let it be forgotten.\"\n\n\"Mr. Cradell, if I know him,\" said the lady, \"is too much the gentleman to bear any anger when a gentleman has offered him his hand.\"\n\n\"Oh, I'm sure,\" said Cradell, \"I'm quite--indeed, I'm delighted to find there's nothing wrong after all.\" And then he shook hands with both of them; whereupon Miss Spruce got up, curtseyed low, and also shook hands with the husband and wife.\n\n\"You're not a married man, Mr. Cradell,\" said Lupex, \"and, therefore, you cannot understand the workings of a husband's heart. There have been moments when my regard for that woman has been too much for me.\"\n\n\"Now, Lupex, don't,\" said she, playfully tapping him with an old parasol which she still held.\n\n\"And I do not hesitate to say that my regard for her was too much for me on that night when I sent for you to the dining-room.\"\n\n\"I'm glad it's all put right now,\" said Cradell.\n\n\"Very glad, indeed,\" said Miss Spruce.\n\n\"And, therefore, we need not say any more about it,\" said Mrs. Lupex.\n\n\"One word,\" said Lupex, waving his hand. \"Mr. Cradell, I greatly rejoice that you did not obey my summons on that night. Had you done so,--I confess it now,--had you done so, blood would have been the consequence. I was mistaken. I acknowledge my mistake;--but blood would have been the consequence.\"\n\n\"Dear, dear, dear,\" said Miss Spruce.\n\n\"Miss Spruce,\" continued Lupex, \"there are moments when the heart becomes too strong for a man.\"\n\n\"I dare say,\" said Miss Spruce.\n\n\"Now, Lupex, that will do,\" said his wife.\n\n\"Yes; that will do. But I think it right to tell Mr. Cradell that I am glad he did not come to me. Your friend, Mr. Cradell, did me the honour of calling on me at the theatre yesterday, at half-past four; but I was in the slings then, and could not very well come down to him. I shall be happy to see you both any day at five, and to bury all unkindness with a chop and glass at the Pot and Poker, in Bow-street.\"\n\n\"I'm sure you're very kind,\" said Cradell.\n\n\"And Mrs. Lupex will join us. There's a delightful little snuggery upstairs at the Pot and Poker; and if Miss Spruce will condescend to--\"\n\n\"Oh, I'm an old woman, sir.\"\n\n\"No--no--no,\" said Lupex, \"I deny that. Come, Cradell, what do you say?--just a snug little dinner for four, you know.\"\n\nIt was, no doubt, pleasant to see Mr. Lupex in his present mood,--much pleasanter than in that other mood of which blood would have been the consequence; but pleasant as he now was, it was, nevertheless, apparent that he was not quite sober. Cradell, therefore, did not settle the day for the little dinner; but merely remarked that he should be very happy at some future day.\n\n\"And now, Lupex, suppose you get off to bed,\" said his wife. \"You've had a very trying day, you know.\"\n\n\"And you, ducky?\"\n\n\"I shall come presently. Now don't be making a fool of yourself, but get yourself off. Come--\" and she stood close up against the open door, waiting for him to pass.\n\n\"I rather think I shall remain where I am, and have a glass of something hot,\" said he.\n\n\"Lupex, do you want to aggravate me again?\" said the lady, and she looked at him with a glance of her eye which he thoroughly understood. He was not in a humour for fighting, nor was he at present desirous of blood; so he resolved to go. But as he went he prepared himself for new battles. \"I shall do something desperate, I am sure; I know I shall,\" he said, as he pulled off his boots.\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Cradell,\" said Mrs. Lupex as soon as she had closed the door behind her retreating husband, \"how am I ever to look you in the face again after the events of these last memorable days?\" And then she seated herself on the sofa, and hid her face in a cambric handkerchief.\n\n\"As for that,\" said Cradell, \"what does it signify,--among friends like us, you know?\"\n\n\"But that it should be known at your office,--as of course it is, because of the gentleman that went down to him at the theatre!--I don't think I shall ever survive it.\"\n\n\"You see I was obliged to send somebody, Mrs. Lupex.\"\n\n\"I'm not finding fault, Mr. Cradell. I know very well that in my melancholy position I have no right to find fault, and I don't pretend to understand gentlemen's feelings towards each other. But to have had my name mentioned up with yours in that way is-- Oh! Mr. Cradell, I don't know how I'm ever to look you in the face again.\" And again she buried hers in her pocket-handkerchief.\n\n\"Handsome is as handsome does,\" said Miss Spruce; and there was that in her tone of voice which seemed to convey much hidden meaning.\n\n\"Exactly so, Miss Spruce,\" said Mrs. Lupex; \"and that's my only comfort at the present moment. Mr. Cradell is a gentleman who would scorn to take advantage--I'm quite sure of that.\" And then she did contrive to look at him over the edge of the hand which held the handkerchief.\n\n\"That I wouldn't, I'm sure,\" said Cradell. \"That is to say--\" And then he paused. He did not wish to get into a scrape about Mrs. Lupex. He was by no means anxious to encounter her husband in one of his fits of jealousy. But he did like the idea of being talked of as the admirer of a married woman, and he did like the brightness of the lady's eyes. When the unfortunate moth in his semi-blindness whisks himself and his wings within the flame of the candle, and finds himself mutilated and tortured, he even then will not take the lesson, but returns again and again till he is destroyed. Such a moth was poor Cradell. There was no warmth to be got by him from that flame. There was no beauty in the light,--not even the false brilliance of unhallowed love. Injury might come to him,--a pernicious clipping of the wings, which might destroy all power of future flight; injury, and not improbably destruction, if he should persevere. But one may say that no single hour of happiness could accrue to him from his intimacy with Mrs. Lupex. He felt for her no love. He was afraid of her, and, in many respects, disliked her. But to him, in his moth-like weakness, ignorance, and blindness, it seemed to be a great thing that he should be allowed to fly near the candle. Oh! my friends, if you will but think of it, how many of you have been moths, and are now going about ungracefully with wings more or less burnt off, and with bodies sadly scorched!\n\nBut before Mr. Cradell could make up his mind whether or no he would take advantage of the present opportunity for another dip into the flame of the candle,--in regard to which proceeding, however, he could not but feel that the presence of Miss Spruce was objectionable,--the door of the room was opened, and Amelia Roper joined the party.\n\n\"Oh, indeed; Mrs. Lupex,\" she said. \"And Mr. Cradell!\"\n\n\"And Miss Spruce, my dear,\" said Mrs. Lupex, pointing to the ancient lady.\n\n\"I'm only an old woman,\" said Miss Spruce.\n\n\"Oh, yes; I see Miss Spruce,\" said Amelia. \"I was not hinting at anything, I can assure you.\"\n\n\"I should think not, my dear,\" said Mrs. Lupex.\n\n\"Only I didn't know that you two were quite-- That is, when last I heard about it, I fancied-- But if the quarrel's made up, there's nobody more rejoiced than I am.\"\n\n\"The quarrel is made up,\" said Cradell.\n\n\"If Mr. Lupex is satisfied, I'm sure I am,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"Mr. Lupex is satisfied,\" said Mrs. Lupex; \"and let me tell you, my dear, seeing that you are expecting to get married yourself--\"\n\n\"Mrs. Lupex, I'm not expecting to get married,--not particularly, by any means.\"\n\n\"Oh, I thought you were. And let me tell you, that when you've got a husband of your own, you won't find it so easy to keep everything straight. That's the worst of these lodgings; if there is any little thing, everybody knows it. Don't they, Miss Spruce?\"\n\n\"Lodgings is so much more comfortable than housekeeping,\" said Miss Spruce, who lived rather in fear of her relatives, the Ropers.\n\n\"Everybody knows it; does he?\" said Amelia. \"Why, if a gentleman will come home at night tipsy and threaten to murder another gentleman in the same house; and if a lady--\" And then Amelia paused, for she knew that the line-of-battle ship which she was preparing to encounter had within her much power of fighting.\n\n\"Well, miss,\" said Mrs. Lupex, getting on her feet, \"and what of the lady?\"\n\nNow we may say that the battle had begun, and that the two ships were pledged by the general laws of courage and naval warfare to maintain the contest till one of them should be absolutely disabled, if not blown up or sunk. And at this moment it might be difficult for a bystander to say with which of the combatants rested the better chance of permanent success. Mrs. Lupex had doubtless on her side more matured power, a habit of fighting which had given her infinite skill, a courage which deadened her to the feeling of all wounds while the heat of the battle should last, and a recklessness which made her almost indifferent whether she sank or swam. But then Amelia carried the greater guns, and was able to pour in heavier metal than her enemy could use; and she, too, swam in her own waters. Should they absolutely come to grappling and boarding, Amelia would no doubt have the best of it; but Mrs. Lupex would probably be too crafty to permit such a proceeding as that. She was, however, ready for the occasion, and greedy for the fight.\n\n\"And what of the lady?\" said she, in a tone of voice that admitted of no pacific rejoinder.\n\n\"A lady, if she is a lady,\" said Amelia, \"will know how to behave herself.\"\n\n\"And you're going to teach me, are you, Miss Roper? I'm sure I'm ever so much obliged to you. It's Manchester manners, I suppose, that you prefer?\"\n\n\"I prefer honest manners, Mrs. Lupex, and decent manners, and manners that won't shock a whole house full of people; and I don't care whether they come from Manchester or London.\"\n\n\"Milliner's manners, I suppose?\"\n\n\"I don't care whether they are milliner's manners or theatrical, Mrs. Lupex, as long as they're not downright bad manners--as yours are, Mrs. Lupex. And now you've got it. What are you going on for in this way with that young man, till you'll drive your husband into a madhouse with drink and jealousy?\"\n\n\"Miss Roper! Miss Roper!\" said Cradell; \"now really--\"\n\n\"Don't mind her, Mr. Cradell,\" said Mrs. Lupex; \"she's not worthy for you to speak to. And as to that poor fellow Eames, if you've any friendship for him, you'll let him know what she is. My dear, how's Mr. Juniper, of Grogram's house, at Salford? I know all about you, and so shall John Eames, too--poor unfortunate fool of a fellow! Telling me of drink and jealousy, indeed!\"\n\n\"Yes, telling you! And now you've, mentioned Mr. Juniper's name, Mr. Eames, and Mr. Cradell too, may know the whole of it. There's been nothing about Mr. Juniper that I'm ashamed of.\"\n\n\"It would be difficult to make you ashamed of anything, I believe.\"\n\n\"But let me tell you this, Mrs. Lupex, you're not going to destroy the respectability of this house by your goings on.\"\n\n\"It was a bad day for me when I let Lupex bring me into it.\"\n\n\"Then pay your bill, and walk out of it,\" said Amelia, waving her hand towards the door. \"I'll undertake to say there shan't be any notice required. Only you pay mother what you owe, and you're free to go at once.\"\n\n\"I shall go just when I please, and not one hour before. Who are you, you gipsy, to speak to me in this way?\"\n\n\"And as for going, go you shall, if we have to call in the police to make you.\"\n\nAmelia, as at this period of the fight she stood fronting her foe with her arms akimbo, certainly seemed to have the best of the battle. But the bitterness of Mrs. Lupex's tongue had hardly yet produced its greatest results. I am inclined to think that the married lady would have silenced her who was single, had the fight been allowed to rage,--always presuming that no resort to grappling-irons took place. But at this moment Mrs. Roper entered the room, accompanied by her son, and both the combatants for a moment retreated.\n\n\"Amelia, what's all this?\" said Mrs. Roper, trying to assume a look of agonized amazement.\n\n\"Ask Mrs. Lupex,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"And Mrs. Lupex will answer,\" said that lady. \"Your daughter has come in here, and attacked me--in such language--before Mr. Cradell, too--\"\n\n\"Why doesn't she pay what she owes, and leave the house?\" said Amelia.\n\n\"Hold your tongue,\" said her brother. \"What she owes is no affair of yours.\"\n\n\"But it's an affair of mine, when I'm insulted by such a creature as that.\"\n\n\"Creature!\" said Mrs. Lupex. \"I'd like to know which is most like a creature! But I'll tell you, what it is, Amelia Roper--\" Here, however, her eloquence was stopped, for Amelia had disappeared through the door, having been pushed out of the room by her brother. Whereupon Mrs. Lupex, having found a sofa convenient for the service, betook herself to hysterics. There for the moment we will leave her, hoping that poor Mrs. Roper was not kept late out of her bed.\n\n\"What a deuce of a mess Eames will make of it if he marries that girl!\" Such was Cradell's reflection as he betook himself to his own room. But of his own part in the night's transactions he was rather proud than otherwise, feeling that the married lady's regard for him had been the cause of the battle which had raged. So, likewise, did Paris derive much gratification from the ten years' siege of Troy.\n\n# CHAPTER XII. LILIAN DALE BECOMES A BUTTERFLY.\n\nAnd now we will go back to Allington. The same morning that brought to John Eames the two letters which were given in the last chapter but one, brought to the Great House, among others, the following epistle for Adolphus Crosbie. It was from a countess, and was written on pink paper, beautifully creamlaid and scented, ornamented with a coronet and certain singularly-entwined initials. Altogether, the letter was very fashionable and attractive, and Adolphus Crosbie was by no means sorry to receive it.\n\nCourcy Castle, September, 186--.\n\nMY DEAR MR. CROSBIE,--We have heard of you from the Gazebees, who have come down to us, and who tell us that you are rusticating at a charming little village, in which, among other attractions, there are wood nymphs and water nymphs, to whom much of your time is devoted. As this is just the thing for your taste, I would not for worlds disturb you; but if you should ever tear yourself away from the groves and fountains of Allington, we shall be delighted to welcome you here, though you will find us very unromantic after your late Elysium.\n\nLady Dumbello is coming to us, who I know is a favourite of yours. Or is it the other way, and are you a favourite of hers? I did ask Lady Hartletop, but she cannot get away from the poor marquis, who is, you know, so very infirm. The duke isn't at Gatherum at present, but, of course, I don't mean that that has anything to do with dear Lady Hartletop's not coming to us. I believe we shall have the house full, and shall not want for nymphs either, though I fear they will not be of the wood and water kind. Margaretta and Alexandrina particularly want you to come, as they say you are so clever at making a houseful of people go off well If you can give us a week before you go back to manage the affairs of the nation, pray do.\n\nYours very sincerely,\n\nThe Countess De Courcy was a very old friend of Mr. Crosbie's; that is to say, as old friends go in the world in which he had been living. He had known her for the last six or seven years, and had been in the habit of going to all her London balls, and dancing with her daughters everywhere, in a most good-natured and affable way. He had been intimate, from old family relations, with Mr. Mortimer Gazebee, who, though only an attorney of the more distinguished kind, had married the countess's eldest daughter, and now sat in Parliament for the city of Barchester, near to which Courcy Castle was situated. And, to tell the truth honestly at once, Mr. Crosbie had been on terms of great friendship with Lady De Courcy's daughters, the Ladies Margaretta and Alexandrina--perhaps especially so with the latter, though I would not have my readers suppose by my saying so that anything more tender than friendship had ever existed between them.\n\nCrosbie said nothing about the letter on that morning; but during the day, or, perhaps, as he thought over the matter in bed, he made up his mind that he would accept Lady De Courcy's invitation. It was not only that he would be glad to see the Gazebees, or glad to stay in the same house with that great master in the high art of fashionable life, Lady Dumbello, or glad to renew his friendship with the Ladies Margaretta and Alexandrina. Had he felt that the circumstances of his engagement with Lily made it expedient for him to stay with her till the end of his holidays, he could have thrown over the De Courcys without a struggle. But he told himself that it would be well for him now to tear himself away from Lily; or perhaps he said that it would be well for Lily that he should be torn away. He must not teach her to think that they were to live only in the sunlight of each other's eyes during those months, or perhaps years, which must elapse before their engagement could be carried out. Nor must he allow her to suppose that either he or she were to depend solely upon the other for the amusements and employments of life. In this way he argued the matter very sensibly within his own mind, and resolved, without much difficulty, that he would go to Courcy Castle, and bask for a week in the sunlight of the fashion which would be collected there. The quiet humdrum of his own fireside would come upon him soon enough!\n\n\"I think I shall leave you on Wednesday, sir,\" Crosbie said to the squire at breakfast on Sunday morning.\n\n\"Leave us on Wednesday!\" said the squire, who had an old-fashioned idea that people who were engaged to marry each other should remain together as long as circumstances could be made to admit of their doing so. \"Nothing wrong, is there?\"\n\n\"O dear, no! But everything must come to an end some day; and as I must make one or two short visits before I get back to town, I might as well go on Wednesday. Indeed, I have made it as late as I possibly could.\"\n\n\"Where do you go from here?\" asked Bernard.\n\n\"Well, as it happens, only into the next county,--to Courcy Castle.\" And then there was nothing more said about the matter at that breakfast-table.\n\nIt had become their habit to meet together on the Sunday mornings before church, on the lawn belonging to the Small House, and on this day the three gentlemen walked down together, and found Lily and Bell already waiting for them. They generally had some few minutes to spare on those occasions before Mrs. Dale summoned them to pass through the house to church, and such was the case at present. The squire at these times would stand in the middle of the grass-plot, surveying his grounds, and taking stock of the shrubs, and flowers, and fruit-trees round him; for he never forgot that it was all his own, and would thus use this opportunity, as he seldom came down to see the spot on other days. Mrs. Dale, as she would see him from her own window while she was tying on her bonnet, would feel that she knew what was passing through his mind, and would regret that circumstances had forced her to be beholden to him for such assistance. But, in truth, she did not know all that he thought at such times. \"It is mine,\" he would say to himself, as he looked around on the pleasant place. \"But it is well for me that they should enjoy it. She is my brother's widow, and she is welcome;--very welcome.\" I think that if those two persons had known more than they did of each other's hearts and minds they might have loved each other better.\n\nAnd then Crosbie told Lily of his intention. \"On Wednesday!\" she said, turning almost pale with emotion as she heard this news. He had told her abruptly, not thinking, probably, that such tidings would affect her so strongly.\n\n\"Well, yes. I have written to Lady De Courcy and said Wednesday. It wouldn't do for me exactly to drop everybody, and perhaps--\"\n\n\"Oh, no! And, Adolphus, you don't suppose I begrudge your going. Only it does seem so sudden; does it not?\"\n\n\"You see, I've been here over six weeks.\"\n\n\"Yes; you've been very good. When I think of it, what a six weeks it has been! I wonder whether the difference seems to you as great as it does to me. I've left off being a grub, and begun to be a butterfly.\"\n\n\"But you mustn't be a butterfly when you're married, Lily.\"\n\n\"No; not in that sense. But I meant that my real position in the world,--that for which I would fain hope that I was created,--opened to me only when I knew you and knew that you loved me. But mamma is calling us, and we must go through to church. Going on Wednesday! There are only three days more, then!\"\n\n\"Yes, just three days,\" he said, as he took her on his arm and passed through the house on to the road.\n\n\"And when are we to see you again?\" she asked, as they reached the churchyard.\n\n\"Ah, who is to say that yet? We must ask the Chairman of Committees when he will let me go again.\" Then there was nothing more said, and they all followed the squire through the little porch and up to the big family-pew in which they all sat. Here the squire took his place in one special corner which he had occupied ever since his father's death, and from which he read the responses loudly and plainly,--so loudly and plainly, that the parish clerk could by no means equal him, though with emulous voice he still made the attempt. \"T' squire'd like to be squire, and parson, and clerk, and everything; so a would,\" the poor clerk would say, when complaining of the ill-usage which he suffered.\n\nIf Lily's prayers were interrupted by her new sorrow, I think that her fault in that respect would be forgiven. Of course she had known that Crosbie was not going to remain at Allington much longer. She knew quite as well as he did the exact day on which his leave of absence came to its end, and the hour at which it behoved him to walk into his room at the General Committee Office. She had taught herself to think that he would remain with them up to the end of his vacation, and now she felt as a schoolboy would feel who was told suddenly, a day or two before the time, that the last week of his holidays was to be taken from him. The grievance would have been slight had she known it from the first; but what schoolboy could stand such a shock, when the loss amounted to two-thirds of his remaining wealth? Lily did not blame her lover. She did not even think that he ought to stay. She would not allow herself to suppose that he could propose anything that was unkind. But she felt her loss, and more than once, as she knelt at her prayers, she wiped a hidden tear from her eyes.\n\nCrosbie also was thinking of his departure more than he should have done during Mr. Boyce's sermon. \"It's easy listening to him,\" Mrs. Hearn used to say of her husband's successor. \"It don't give one much trouble following him into his arguments.\" Mr. Crosbie perhaps found the difficulty greater than did Mrs. Hearn, and would have devoted his mind more perfectly to the discourse had the argument been deeper. It is very hard, that necessity of listening to a man who says nothing. On this occasion Crosbie ignored the necessity altogether, and gave up his mind to the consideration of what it might be expedient that he should say to Lily before he went. He remembered well those few words which he had spoken in the first ardour of his love, pleading that an early day might be fixed for their marriage. And he remembered, also, how prettily Lily had yielded to him. \"Only do not let it be too soon,\" she had said. Now he must unsay what he had then said. He must plead against his own pleadings, and explain to her that he desired to postpone the marriage rather than to hasten it--a task which, I presume, must always be an unpleasant one for any man engaged to be married. \"I might as well do it at once,\" he said to himself, as he bobbed his head forward into his hands by way of returning thanks for the termination of Mr. Boyce's sermon.\n\nAs he had only three days left, it was certainly as well that he should do this at once. Seeing that Lily had no fortune, she could not in justice complain of a prolonged engagement. That was the argument which he used in his own mind. But he as often told himself that she would have very great ground of complaint if she were left for a day unnecessarily in doubt as to this matter. Why had he rashly spoken those hasty words to her in his love, betraying himself into all manner of scrapes, as a schoolboy might do, or such a one as Johnny Eames? What an ass he had been not to have remembered himself and to have been collected,--not to have bethought himself on the occasion of all that might be due to Adolphus Crosbie! And then the idea came upon him whether he had not altogether made himself an ass in this matter. And as he gave his arm to Lily outside the church-door, he shrugged his shoulders while making that reflection. \"It is too late now,\" he said to himself; and then turned round and made some sweet little loving speech to her. Adolphus Crosbie was a clever man; and he meant also to be a true man,--if only the temptations to falsehood might not be too great for him.\n\n\"Lily,\" he said to her, \"will you walk in the fields after lunch?\"\n\nWalk in the fields with him! Of course she would. There were only three days left, and would she not give up to him every moment of her time, if he would accept of all her moments? And then they lunched at the Small House, Mrs. Dale having promised to join the dinner-party at the squire's table. The squire did not eat any lunch, excusing himself on the plea that lunch in itself was a bad thing. \"He can eat lunch at his own house,\" Mrs. Dale afterwards said to Bell. \"And I've often seen him take a glass of sherry.\" While thinking of this, Mrs. Dale made her own dinner. If her brother-in-law would not eat at her board, neither would she eat at his.\n\nAnd then in a few minutes Lily had on her hat, in place of that decorous, church-going bonnet which Crosbie was wont to abuse with a lover's privilege, feeling well assured that he might say what he liked of the bonnet as long as he would praise the hat. \"Only three days,\" she said, as she walked down with him across the lawn at a quick pace. But she said it in a voice which made no complaint,--which seemed to say simply this,--that as the good time was to be so short, they must make the most of it. And what compliment could be paid to a man so sweet as that? What flattery could be more gratifying? All my earthly heaven is with you; and now, for the delight of these immediately present months or so, there are left to me but three days of this heaven! Come, then; I will make the most of what happiness is given to me. Crosbie felt it all as she felt it, and recognized the extent of the debt he owed her. \"I'll come down to them for a day at Christmas, though it be only for a day,\" he said to himself. Then he reflected that as such was his intention, it might be well for him to open his present conversation with a promise to that effect.\n\n\"Yes, Lily; there are only three days left now. But I wonder whether--I suppose you'll all be at home at Christmas?\"\n\n\"At home at Christmas?--of course we shall be at home. You don't mean to say you'll come to us!\"\n\n\"Well; I think I will, if you'll have me.\"\n\n\"Oh! that will make such a difference. Let me see. That will only be three months. And to have you here on Christmas Day! I would sooner have you then than on any other day in the year.\"\n\n\"It will only be for one day, Lily. I shall come to dinner on Christmas Eve, and must go away the day after.\"\n\n\"But you will come direct to our house!\"\n\n\"If you can spare me a room.\"\n\n\"Of course we can. So we could now. Only when you came, you know--\" Then she looked up into his face and smiled.\n\n\"When I came, I was the squire's friend and your cousin's, rather than yours. But that's all changed now.\"\n\n\"Yes; you're my friend now,--mine specially. I'm to be now and always your own special, dearest friend;--eh, Adolphus?\" And then she exacted from him the repetition of the promise which he had so often given her.\n\nBy this time they had passed through the grounds of the Great House and were in the fields. \"Lily,\" said he, speaking rather suddenly, and making her feel by his manner that something of importance was to be said; \"I want to say a few words to you about,--business.\" And he gave a little laugh as he spoke the last word, making her fully understand that he was not quite at his ease.\n\n\"Of course I'll listen. And, Adolphus, pray don't be afraid about me. What I mean is, don't think that I can't bear cares and troubles. I can bear anything as long as you love me. I say that because I'm afraid I seemed to complain about your going. I didn't mean to.\"\n\n\"I never thought you complained, dearest. Nothing can be better than you are at all times and in every way. A man would be very hard to please if you didn't please him.\"\n\n\"If I can only please you--\"\n\n\"You do please me, in everything. Dear Lily, I think I found an angel when I found you. But now about this business. Perhaps I'd better tell you everything.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, tell me everything.\"\n\n\"But then you mustn't misunderstand me. And if I talk about money, you mustn't suppose that it has anything to do with my love for you.\"\n\n\"I wish for your sake that I wasn't such a little pauper.\"\n\n\"What I mean to say is this, that if I seem to be anxious about money, you must not suppose that that anxiety bears any reference whatever to my affection for you. I should love you just the same, and look forward just as much to my happiness in marrying you, whether you were rich or poor. You understand that?\"\n\nShe did not quite understand him; but she merely pressed his arm, so as to encourage him to go on. She presumed that he intended to tell her something as to their future mode of life--something which he supposed it might not be pleasant for her to hear, and she was determined to show him that she would receive it pleasantly.\n\n\"You know,\" said he, \"how anxious I have been that our marriage should not be delayed. To me, of course, it must be everything now to call you my own as soon as possible.\" In answer to which little declaration of love, she merely pressed his arm again, the subject being one on which she had not herself much to say.\n\n\"Of course I must be very anxious, but I find it not so easy as I expected.\"\n\n\"You know what I said, Adolphus. I said that I thought we had better wait. I'm sure mamma thinks so. And if we can only see you now and then--\"\n\n\"That will be a matter of course. But, as I was saying-- Let me see. Yes,--all that waiting will be intolerable to me. It is such a bore for a man when he has made up his mind on such a matter as marriage, not to make the change at once, especially when he is going to take to himself such a little angel as you are,\" and as he spoke these loving words, his arm was again put round her waist; \"but--\" and then he stopped. He wanted to make her understand that this change of intention on his part was caused by the unexpected misconduct of her uncle. He desired that she should know exactly how the matter stood; that he had been led to suppose that her uncle would give her some small fortune; that he had been disappointed, and had a right to feel the disappointment keenly; and that in consequence of this blow to his expectations, he must put off his marriage. But he wished her also to understand at the same time that this did not in the least mar his love for her; that he did not join her at all in her uncle's fault. All this he was anxious to convey to her, but he did not know how to get it said in a manner that would not be offensive to her personally, and that should not appear to accuse himself of sordid motives. He had begun by declaring that he would tell her all; but sometimes it is not easy, that task of telling a person everything. There are things which will not get themselves told.\n\n\"You mean, dearest,\" said she, \"that you cannot afford to marry at once.\"\n\n\"Yes; that is it. I had expected that I should be able, but--\"\n\nDid any man in love ever yet find himself able to tell the lady whom he loved that he was very much disappointed on discovering that she had got no money? If so, his courage, I should say, was greater than his love. Crosbie found himself unable to do it, and thought himself cruelly used because of the difficulty. The delay to which he intended to subject her was occasioned, as he felt, by the squire, and not by himself. He was ready to do his part, if only the squire had been willing to do the part which properly belonged to him. The squire would not; and, therefore, neither could he,--not as yet. Justice demanded that all this should be understood; but when he came to the telling of it, he found that the story would not form itself properly. He must let the thing go, and bear the injustice, consoling himself as best he might by the reflection that he at least was behaving well in the matter.\n\n\"It won't make me unhappy, Adolphus.\"\n\n\"Will it not?\" said he. \"As regards myself, I own that I cannot bear the delay with so much indifference.\"\n\n\"Nay, my love; but you should not misunderstand me,\" she said, stopping and facing him on the path in which they were walking. \"I suppose I ought to protest, according to the common rules, that I would rather wait. Young ladies are expected to say so. If you were pressing me to marry at once, I should say so, no doubt. But now, as it is, I will be more honest. I have only one wish in the world, and that is, to be your wife,--to be able to share everything with you. The sooner we can be together the better it will be,--at any rate, for me. There; will that satisfy you?\"\n\n\"My own, own Lily!\"\n\n\"Yes, your own Lily. You shall have no cause to doubt me, dearest. But I do not expect that I am to have everything exactly as I want it. I say again, that I shall not be unhappy in waiting. How can I be unhappy while I feel certain of your love? I was disappointed just now when you said that you were going so soon; and I am afraid I showed it. But those little things are more unendurable than the big things.\"\n\n\"Yes; that's very true.\"\n\n\"But there are three more days, and I mean to enjoy them so much! And then you will write to me: and you will come at Christmas. And next year, when you have your holiday, you will come down to us again; will you not?\"\n\n\"You may be quite sure of that.\"\n\n\"And so the time will go by till it suits you to come and take me. I shall not be unhappy.\"\n\n\"I, at any rate, shall be impatient.\"\n\n\"Ah, men always are impatient. It is one of their privileges, I suppose. And I don't think that a man ever has the same positive and complete satisfaction in knowing that he is loved, which a girl feels. You are my bird that I have shot with my own gun; and the assurance of my success is sufficient for my happiness.\"\n\n\"You have bowled me over, and know that I can't get up again.\"\n\n\"I don't know about can't. I would let you up quick enough, if you wished it.\"\n\nHow he made his loving assurance that he did not wish it, never would or could wish it, the reader will readily understand. And then he considered that he might as well leave all those money questions as they now stood. His real object had been to convince her that their joint circumstances did not admit of an immediate marriage; and as to that she completely understood him. Perhaps, during the next three days, some opportunity might arise for explaining the whole matter to Mrs. Dale. At any rate, he had declared his own purpose honestly, and no one could complain of him.\n\nOn the following day they all rode over to Guestwick together,--the all consisting of the two girls, with Bernard and Crosbie. Their object was to pay two visits,--one to their very noble and highly exalted ally, the Lady Julia De Guest; and the other to their much humbler and better known friend, Mrs. Eames. As Guestwick Manor lay on their road into the town, they performed the grander ceremony the first. The present Earl De Guest, brother of that Lady Fanny who ran away with Major Dale, was an unmarried nobleman, who devoted himself chiefly to the breeding of cattle. And as he bred very good cattle, taking infinite satisfaction in the employment, devoting all his energies thereto, and abstaining from all prominently evil courses, it should be acknowledged that he was not a bad member of society. He was a thorough-going old Tory, whose proxy was always in the hand of the leader of his party; and who seldom himself went near the metropolis, unless called thither by some occasion of cattle-showing. He was a short, stumpy man, with red cheeks and a round face; who was usually to be seen till dinner-time dressed in a very old shooting coat, with breeches, gaiters, and very thick shoes. He lived generally out of doors, and was almost as great in the preserving of game as in the breeding of oxen. He knew every acre of his own estate, and every tree upon it, as thoroughly as a lady knows the ornaments in her drawing-room. There was no gap in a fence of which he did not remember the exact bearings, no path hither or thither as to which he could not tell the why and the wherefore. He had been in his earlier years a poor man as regarded his income,--very poor, seeing that he was an earl. But he was not at present by any means an impoverished man, having been taught a lesson by the miseries of his father and grandfather, and having learned to live within his means. Now, as he was going down the vale of years, men said that he was becoming rich, and that he had ready money to spend,--a position in which no Lord De Guest had found himself for many generations back. His father and grandfather had been known as spendthrifts; and now men said that this earl was a miser.\n\nThere was not much of nobility in his appearance; but they greatly mistook Lord De Guest who conceived that on that account his pride of place was not dear to his soul. His peerage dated back to the time of King John, and there were but three lords in England whose patents had been conferred before his own. He knew what privileges were due to him on behalf of his blood, and was not disposed to abate one jot of them. He was not loud in demanding them. As he went through the world he sent no trumpeters to the right or left, proclaiming that the Earl De Guest was coming. When he spread his board for his friends, which he did but on rare occasions, he entertained them simply, with a mild, tedious, old-fashioned courtesy. We may say that, if properly treated, the earl never walked over anybody. But he could, if ill-treated, be grandly indignant; and if attacked, could hold his own against all the world. He knew himself to be every inch an earl, pottering about after his oxen with his muddy gaiters and red cheeks, as much as though he were glittering with stars in courtly royal ceremonies among his peers at Westminster;--ay, more an earl than any of those who use their nobility for pageant purposes. Woe be to him who should mistake that old coat for a badge of rural degradation! Now and again some unlucky wight did make such a mistake, and had to do his penance very uncomfortably.\n\nWith the earl lived a maiden sister, the Lady Julia. Bernard Dale's father had, in early life, run away with one sister, but no suitor had been fortunate enough to induce the Lady Julia to run with him. Therefore she still lived, in maiden blessedness, as mistress of Guestwick Manor; and as such had no mean opinion of the high position which destiny had called upon her to fill. She was a tedious, dull, virtuous old woman, who gave herself infinite credit for having remained all her days in the home of her youth, probably forgetting, in her present advanced years, that her temptations to leave it had not been strong or numerous. She generally spoke of her sister Fanny with some little contempt, as though that poor lady had degraded herself in marrying a younger brother. She was as proud of her own position as was the earl her brother, but her pride was maintained with more of outward show and less of inward nobility. It was hardly enough for her that the world should know that she was a De Guest, and therefore she had assumed little pompous ways and certain airs of condescension which did not make her popular with her neighbours.\n\nThe intercourse between Guestwick Manor and Allington was not very frequent or very cordial. Soon after the running away of the Lady Fanny, the two families had agreed to acknowledge their connection with each other, and to let it be known by the world that they were on friendly terms. Either that course was necessary to them, or the other course, of letting it be known that they were enemies. Friendship was the less troublesome, and therefore the two families called on each other from time to time, and gave each other dinners about once a year. The earl regarded the squire as a man who had deserted his politics, and had thereby forfeited the respect due to him as an hereditary land magnate; and the squire was wont to be-little the earl as one who understood nothing of the outer world. At Guestwick Manor Bernard was to some extent a favourite. He was actually a relative, having in his veins blood of the De Guests, and was not the less a favourite because he was the heir to Allington, and because the blood of the Dales was older even than that of the noble family to which he was allied. When Bernard should come to be the squire, then indeed there might be cordial relations between Guestwick Manor and Allington; unless, indeed, the earl's heir and the squire's heir should have some fresh cause of ill-will between themselves.\n\nThey found Lady Julia sitting in her drawing-room alone, and introduced to her Mr. Crosbie in due form. The fact of Lily's engagement was of course known at the manor, and it was quite understood that her intended husband was now brought over that he might be looked at and approved. Lady Julia made a very elaborate curtsey, and expressed a hope that her young friend might be made happy in that sphere of life to which it had pleased God to call her.\n\n\"I hope I shall, Lady Julia,\" said Lily, with a little laugh; \"at any rate I mean to try.\"\n\n\"We all try, my dear, but many of us fail to try with sufficient energy of purpose. It is only by doing our duty that we can hope to be happy, whether in single life or in married.\"\n\n\"Miss Dale means to be a dragon of perfection in the performance of hers,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"A dragon!\" said Lady Julia. \"No; I hope Miss Lily Dale will never become a dragon.\" And then she turned to her nephew. It may be as well to say at once that she never forgave Mr. Crosbie the freedom of the expression which he had used. He had been in the drawing-room of Guestwick Manor for two minutes only, and it did not become him to talk about dragons. \"Bernard,\" she said, \"I heard from your mother yesterday. I am afraid she does not seem to be very strong.\" And then there was a little conversation, not very interesting in its nature, between the aunt and the nephew as to the general health of Lady Fanny.\n\n\"I didn't know my aunt was so unwell,\" said Bell.\n\n\"She isn't ill,\" said Bernard. \"She never is ill; but then she is never well.\"\n\n\"Your aunt,\" said Lady Julia, seeming to put a touch of sarcasm into the tone of her voice as she repeated the word--\"your aunt has never enjoyed good health since she left this house; but that is a long time ago.\"\n\n\"A very long time,\" said Crosbie, who was not accustomed to be left in his chair silent. \"You, Dale, at any rate, can hardly remember it.\"\n\n\"But I can remember it,\" said Lady Julia, gathering herself up. \"I can remember when my sister Fanny was recognized as the beauty of the country. It is a dangerous gift, that of beauty.\"\n\n\"Very dangerous,\" said Crosbie. Then Lily laughed again, and Lady Julia became more angry than ever. What odious man was this whom her neighbours were going to take into their very bosom! But she had heard of Mr. Crosbie before, and Mr. Crosbie also had heard of her.\n\n\"By-the-by, Lady Julia,\" said he, \"I think I know some very dear friends of yours.\"\n\n\"Very dear friends is a very strong word. I have not many very dear friends.\"\n\n\"I mean the Gazebees. I have heard Mortimer Gazebee and Lady Amelia speak of you.\"\n\nWhereupon Lady Julia confessed that she did know the Gazebees. Mr. Gazebee, she said, was a man who in early life had wanted many advantages, but still he was a very estimable person. He was now in Parliament, and she understood that he was making himself useful. She had not quite approved of Lady Amelia's marriage at the time, and so she had told her very old friend Lady De Courcy; but-- And then Lady Julia said many words in praise of Mr. Gazebee, which seemed to amount to this; that he was an excellent sort of man, with a full conviction of the too great honour done to him by the earl's daughter who had married him, and a complete consciousness that even that marriage had not put him on a par with his wife's relations, or even with his wife. And then it came out that Lady Julia in the course of the next week was going to meet the Gazebees at Courcy Castle.\n\n\"I am delighted to think that I shall have the pleasure of seeing you there,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Indeed!\" said Lady Julia.\n\n\"I am going to Courcy on Wednesday. That, I fear, will be too early to allow of my being of any service to your ladyship.\"\n\nLady Julia drew herself up, and declined the escort which Mr. Crosbie had seemed to offer. It grieved her to find that Lily Dale's future husband was an intimate friend of her friend's, and it especially grieved her to find that he was now going to that friend's house. It was a grief to her, and she showed that it was. It also grieved Crosbie to find that Lady Julia was to be a fellow guest with himself at Courcy Castle; but he did not show it. He expressed nothing but smiles and civil self-congratulation on the matter, pretending that he would have much delight in again meeting Lady Julia; but, in truth, he would have given much could he have invented any manoeuvre by which her ladyship might have been kept at home.\n\n\"What a horrid old woman she is,\" said Lily, as they rode back down the avenue. \"I beg your pardon, Bernard; for, of course, she is your aunt.\"\n\n\"Yes; she is my aunt; and though I am not very fond of her, I deny that she is a horrid old woman. She never murdered anybody, or robbed anybody, or stole away any other woman's lover.\"\n\n\"I should think not,\" said Lily.\n\n\"She says her prayers earnestly, I have no doubt,\" continued Bernard, \"and gives away money to the poor, and would sacrifice to-morrow any desire of her own to her brother's wish. I acknowledge that she is ugly, and pompous, and that, being a woman, she ought not to have such a long black beard on her upper lip.\"\n\n\"I don't care a bit about her beard,\" said Lily. \"But why did she tell me to do my duty? I didn't go there to have a sermon preached to me.\"\n\n\"And why did she talk about beauty being dangerous?\" said Bell. \"Of course, we all knew what she meant.\"\n\n\"I didn't know at all what she meant,\" said Lily; \"and I don't know now.\"\n\n\"I think she's a charming woman, and I shall be especially civil to her at Lady De Courcy's,\" said Crosbie.\n\nAnd in this way, saying hard things of the poor old spinster whom they had left, they made their way into Guestwick, and again dismounted at Mrs. Eames's door.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII. A VISIT TO GUESTWICK.\n\nAs the party from Allington rode up the narrow High-street of Guestwick, and across the market square towards the small, respectable, but very dull row of new houses in which Mrs. Eames lived, the people of Guestwick were all aware that Miss Lily Dale was escorted by her future husband. The opinion that she had been a very fortunate girl was certainly general among the Guestwickians, though it was not always expressed in open or generous terms. \"It was a great match for her,\" some said, but shook their heads at the same time, hinting that Mr. Crosbie's life in London was not all that it should be, and suggesting that she might have been more safe had she been content to bestow herself upon some country neighbour of less dangerous pretensions. Others declared that it was no such great match after all. They knew his income to a penny, and believed that the young people would find it very difficult to keep a house in London unless the old squire intended to assist them. But, nevertheless, Lily was envied as she rode through the town with her handsome lover by her side.\n\nAnd she was very happy. I will not deny that she had some feeling of triumphant satisfaction in the knowledge that she was envied. Such a feeling on her part was natural, and is natural to all men and women who are conscious that they have done well in the adjustment of their own affairs. As she herself had said, he was her bird, the spoil of her own gun, the product of such capacity as she had in her, on which she was to live, and, if possible, to thrive during the remainder of her life. Lily fully recognized the importance of the thing she was doing, and, in soberest guise, had thought much of this matter of marriage. But the more she thought of it the more satisfied she was that she was doing well. And yet she knew that there was a risk. He who was now everything to her might die; nay, it was possible that he might be other than she thought him to be; that he might neglect her, desert her, or misuse her. But she had resolved to trust in everything, and, having so trusted, she would not provide for herself any possibility of retreat. Her ship should go out into the middle ocean, beyond all ken of the secure port from which it had sailed; her army should fight its battle with no hope of other safety than that which victory gives. All the world might know that she loved him if all the world chose to inquire about the matter. She triumphed in her lover, and did not deny even to herself that she was triumphant.\n\nMrs. Eames was delighted to see them. It was so good in Mr. Crosbie to come over and call upon such a poor, forlorn woman as her, and so good in Captain Dale; so good also in the dear girls, who, at the present moment, had so much to make them happy at home at Allington! Little things, accounted as bare civilities by others, were esteemed as great favours by Mrs. Eames.\n\n\"And dear Mrs. Dale? I hope she was not fatigued when we kept her up the other night so unconscionably late?\" Bell and Lily both assured her that their mother was none the worse for what she had gone through; and then Mrs. Eames got up and left the room, with the declared purpose of looking for John and Mary, but bent, in truth, on the production of some cake and sweet wine which she kept under lock and key in the little parlour.\n\n\"Don't let's stay here very long,\" whispered Crosbie.\n\n\"No, not very long,\" said Lily. \"But when you come to see my friends you mustn't be in a hurry, Mr. Crosbie.\"\n\n\"He had his turn with Lady Julia,\" said Bell, \"and we must have ours now.\"\n\n\"At any rate, Mrs. Eames won't tell us to do our duty and to beware of being too beautiful,\" said Lily.\n\nMary and John came into the room before their mother returned; then came Mrs. Eames, and a few minutes afterwards the cake and wine arrived. It certainly was rather dull, as none of the party seemed to be at their ease. The grandeur of Mr. Crosbie was too great for Mrs. Eames and her daughter, and John was almost silenced by the misery of his position. He had not yet answered Miss Roper's letter, nor had he even made up his mind whether he would answer it or no. And then the sight of Lily's happiness did not fill him with all that friendly joy which he should perhaps have felt as the friend of her childhood. To tell the truth, he hated Crosbie, and so he had told himself; and had so told his sister also very frequently since the day of the party.\n\n\"I tell you what it is, Molly,\" he had said, \"if there was any way of doing it, I'd fight that man.\"\n\n\"What; and make Lily wretched?\"\n\n\"She'll never be happy with him. I'm sure she won't. I don't want to do her any harm, but yet I'd like to fight that man,--if I only knew how to manage it.\"\n\nAnd then he bethought himself that if they could both be slaughtered in such an encounter it would be the only fitting termination to the present state of things. In that way, too, there would be an escape from Amelia, and, at the present moment, he saw none other.\n\nWhen he entered the room he shook hands with all the party from Allington, but, as he told his sister afterwards, his flesh crept when he touched Crosbie. Crosbie, as he contemplated the Eames family sitting stiff and ill at ease in their own drawing-room chairs, made up his mind that it would be well that his wife should see as little of John Eames as might be when she came to London;--not that he was in any way jealous of her lover. He had learned everything from Lily,--all, at least, that Lily knew,--and regarded the matter rather as a good joke. \"Don't see him too often,\" he had said to her, \"for fear he should make an ass of himself.\" Lily had told him everything,--all that she could tell; but yet he did not in the least comprehend that Lily had, in truth, a warm affection for the young man whom he despised.\n\n\"Thank you, no,\" said Crosbie. \"I never do take wine in the middle of the day.\"\n\n\"But a bit of cake?\" And Mrs. Eames by her look implored him to do her so much honour. She implored Captain Dale, also, but they were both inexorable. I do not know that the two girls were at all more inclined to eat and drink than the two men; but they understood that Mrs. Eames would be broken-hearted if no one partook of her delicacies. The little sacrifices of society are all made by women, as are also the great sacrifices of life. A man who is good for anything is always ready for his duty, and so is a good woman always ready for a sacrifice.\n\n\"We really must go now,\" said Bell, \"because of the horses.\" And under this excuse they got away.\n\n\"You will come over before you go back to London, John?\" said Lily, as he came out with the intention of helping her mount, from which purpose, however, he was forced to recede by the iron will of Mr. Crosbie.\n\n\"Yes, I'll come over again--before I go. Good-by.\"\n\n\"Good-by, John,\" said Bell. \"Good-by, Eames,\" said Captain Dale. Crosbie, as he seated himself in the saddle, made the very slightest sign of recognition, to which his rival would not condescend to pay any attention. \"I'll manage to have a fight with him in some way,\" said Eames to himself as he walked back through the passage of his mother's house. And Crosbie, as he settled his feet in the stirrups, felt that he disliked the young man more and more. It would be monstrous to suppose that there could be aught of jealousy in the feeling; and yet he did dislike him very strongly, and felt almost angry with Lily for asking him to come again to Allington. \"I must put an end to all that,\" he said to himself as he rode silently out of town.\n\n\"You must not snub my friends, sir,\" said Lily, smiling as she spoke, but yet with something of earnestness in her voice. They were out of the town by this time, and Crosbie had hardly uttered a word since they had left Mrs. Eames's door. They were now on the high road, and Bell and Bernard Dale were somewhat in advance of them.\n\n\"I never snub anybody,\" said Crosbie, petulantly; \"that is, unless they have absolutely deserved snubbing.\"\n\n\"And have I deserved it? Because I seem to have got it,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Nonsense, Lily. I never snubbed you yet, and I don't think it likely that I shall begin. But you ought not to accuse me of not being civil to your friends. In the first place I am as civil to them as my nature will allow me to be. And, in the second place--\"\n\n\"Well; in the second place--?\"\n\n\"I am not quite sure that you are very wise to encourage that young man's--friendship just at present.\"\n\n\"That means, I suppose, that I am very wrong to do so?\"\n\n\"No, dearest, it does not mean that. If I meant so I would tell you so honestly. I mean just what I say. There can, I suppose, be no doubt that he has filled himself with some kind of romantic attachment for you,--a foolish kind of love which I don't suppose he ever expected to gratify, but the idea of which lends a sort of grace to his life. When he meets some young woman fit to be his wife he will forget all about it, but till then he will go about fancying himself a despairing lover. And then such a young man as John Eames is very apt to talk of his fancies.\"\n\n\"I don't believe for a moment that he would mention my name to any one.\"\n\n\"But, Lily, perhaps I may know more of young men than you do.\"\n\n\"Yes, of course you do.\"\n\n\"And I can assure you that they are generally too well inclined to make free with the names of girls whom they think that they like. You must not be surprised if I am unwilling that any man should make free with your name.\"\n\nAfter this Lily was silent for a minute or two. She felt that an injustice was being done to her and she was not inclined to put up with it, but she could not quite see where the injustice lay. A great deal was owing from her to Crosbie. In very much she was bound to yield to him, and she was anxious to do on his behalf even more than her duty. But yet she had a strong conviction that it would not be well that she should give way to him in everything. She wished to think as he thought as far as possible, but she could not say that she agreed with him when she knew that she differed from him. John Eames was an old friend whom she could not abandon, and so much at the present time she felt herself obliged to say.\n\n\"But, Adolphus--\"\n\n\"Well, dearest?\"\n\n\"You would not wish me to be unkind to so very old a friend as John Eames? I have known him all my life, and we have all of us had a very great regard for the whole family. His father was my uncle's most particular friend.\"\n\n\"I think, Lily, you must understand what I mean. I don't want you to quarrel with any of them, or to be what you call unkind. But you need not give special and pressing invitations to this young man to come and see you before he goes back to London, and then to come and see you directly you get to London. You tell me that he has some kind of romantic idea of being in love with you;--of being in despair because you are not in love with him. It's all great nonsense, no doubt, but it seems to me that under such circumstances you'd better--just leave him alone.\"\n\nAgain Lily was silent. These were her three last days, in which it was her intention to be especially happy, but above all things to make him especially happy. On no account would she say to him sharp words, or encourage in her own heart a feeling of animosity against him, and yet she believed him to be wrong; and so believing could hardly bring herself to bear the injury. Such was her nature, as a Dale. And let it be remembered that very many who can devote themselves for great sacrifices, cannot bring themselves to the endurance of little injuries. Lily could have given up any gratification for her lover, but she could not allow herself to have been in the wrong, believing herself to have been in the right.\n\n\"I have asked him now, and he must come,\" she said.\n\n\"But do not press him to come any more.\"\n\n\"Certainly not, after what you have said, Adolphus. If he comes over to Allington, he will see me in mamma's house, to which he has always been made welcome by her. Of course I understand perfectly--\"\n\n\"You understand what, Lily?\"\n\nBut she had stopped herself, fearing that she might say that which would be offensive to him if she continued.\n\n\"What is it you understand, Lily?\"\n\n\"Do not press me to go on, Adolphus. As far as I can, I will do all that you want me to do.\"\n\n\"You meant to say that when you find yourself an inmate of my house, as a matter of course you could not ask your own friends to come and see you. Was that gracious?\"\n\n\"Whatever I may have meant to say, I did not say that. Nor in truth did I mean it. Pray don't go on about it now. These are to be our last days, you know, and we shouldn't waste them by talking of things that are unpleasant. After all poor Johnny Eames is nothing to me; nothing, nothing. How can any one be anything to me when I think of you?\"\n\nBut even this did not bring Crosbie back at once into a pleasant humour. Had Lily yielded to him and confessed that he was right, he would have made himself at once as pleasant as the sun in May. But this she had not done. She had simply abstained from her argument because she did not choose to be vexed, and had declared her continued purpose of seeing Eames on his promised visit. Crosbie would have had her acknowledge herself wrong, and would have delighted in the privilege of forgiving her. But Lily Dale was one who did not greatly relish forgiveness, or any necessity of being forgiven. So they rode on, if not in silence, without much joy in their conversation. It was now late on the Monday afternoon, and Crosbie was to go early on the Wednesday morning. What if these three last days should come to be marred with such terrible drawbacks as these!\n\nBernard Dale had not spoken a word to his cousin of his suit, since they had been interrupted by Crosbie and Lily as they were lying on the bank by the ha-ha. He had danced with her again and again at Mrs. Dale's party, and had seemed to revert to his old modes of conversation without difficulty. Bell, therefore, had believed the matter to be over, and was thankful to her cousin, declaring within her own bosom that the whole matter should be treated by her as though it had never happened. To no one,--not even to her mother, would she tell it. To such reticence she bound herself for his sake, feeling that he would be best pleased that it should be so. But now as they rode on together, far in advance of the other couple, he again returned to the subject.\n\n\"Bell,\" said he, \"am I to have any hope?\"\n\n\"Any hope as to what, Bernard?\"\n\n\"I hardly know whether a man is bound to take a single answer on such a subject. But this I know, that if a man's heart is concerned, he is not very willing to do so.\"\n\n\"When that answer has been given honestly and truly--\"\n\n\"Oh, no doubt. I don't at all suppose that you were dishonest or false when you refused to allow me to speak to you.\"\n\n\"But, Bernard, I did not refuse to allow you to speak to me.\"\n\n\"Something very like it. But, however, I have no doubt you were true enough. But, Bell, why should it be so? If you were in love with any one else I could understand it.\"\n\n\"I am not in love with any one else.\"\n\n\"Exactly. And there are so many reasons why you and I should join our fortunes together.\"\n\n\"It cannot be a question of fortune, Bernard.\"\n\n\"Do listen to me. Do let me speak, at any rate. I presume I may at least suppose that you do not dislike me.\"\n\n\"Oh, no.\"\n\n\"And though you might not be willing to accept any man's hand merely on a question of fortune, surely the fact that our marriage would be in every way suitable as regards money should not set you against it. Of my own love for you I will not speak further, as I do not doubt that you believe what I say; but should you not question your own feelings very closely before you determine to oppose the wishes of all those who are nearest to you?\"\n\n\"Do you mean mamma, Bernard?\"\n\n\"Not her especially, though I cannot but think she would like a marriage that would keep all the family together, and would give you an equal claim to the property to that which I have.\"\n\n\"That would not have a feather's-weight with mamma.\"\n\n\"Have you asked her?\"\n\n\"No, I have mentioned the matter to no one.\"\n\n\"Then you cannot know. And as to my uncle, I have the means of knowing that it is the great desire of his life. I must say that I think some consideration for him should induce you to pause before you give a final answer, even though no consideration for me should have any weight with you.\"\n\n\"I would do more for you than for him,--much more.\"\n\n\"Then do this for me. Allow me to think that I have not yet had an answer to my proposal; give me to this day month, to Christmas; till any time that you like to name, so that I may think that it is not yet settled, and may tell uncle Christopher that such is the case.\"\n\n\"Bernard, it would be useless.\"\n\n\"It would at any rate show him that you are willing to think of it.\"\n\n\"But I am not willing to think of it;--not in that way. I do know my own mind thoroughly, and I should be very wrong if I were to deceive you.\"\n\n\"And you wish me to give that as your only answer to my uncle?\"\n\n\"To tell the truth, Bernard, I do not much care what you may say to my uncle in this matter. He can have no right to interfere in the disposal of my hand, and therefore I need not regard his wishes on the subject. I will explain to you in one word what my feelings are about it. I would accept no man in opposition to mamma's wishes; but not even for her could I accept any man in opposition to my own. But as concerns my uncle, I do not feel myself called on to consult him in any way on such a matter.\"\n\n\"And yet he is the head of our family.\"\n\n\"I don't care anything about the family,--not in that way.\"\n\n\"And he has been very generous to you all.\"\n\n\"That I deny. He has not been generous to mamma. He is very hard and ungenerous to mamma. He lets her have that house because he is anxious that the Dales should seem to be respectable before the world; and she lives in it, because she thinks it better for us that she should do so. If I had my way, she should leave it to-morrow--or, at any rate, as soon as Lily is married. I would much sooner go into Guestwick, and live as the Eames do.\"\n\n\"I think you are ungrateful, Bell.\"\n\n\"No; I am not ungrateful. And as to consulting, Bernard,--I should be much more inclined to consult you than him about my marriage. If you would let me look on you altogether as a brother, I should think little of promising to marry no one whom you did not approve.\"\n\nBut such an agreement between them would by no means have suited Bernard's views. He had thought, some four or five weeks back, that he was not personally very anxious for this match. He had declared to himself that he liked his cousin well enough; that it would be a good thing for him to settle himself; that his uncle was reasonable in his wishes and sufficiently liberal in his offers; and that, therefore, he would marry. It had hardly occurred to him as probable that his cousin would reject so eligible an offer, and had certainly never occurred to him that he would have to suffer anything from such rejection. He had entertained none of that feeling of which lovers speak when they declare that they are staking their all upon the hazard of a die. It had not seemed to him that he was staking anything, as he gently told his tale of languid love, lying on the turf by the ha-ha. He had not regarded the possibility of disappointment, of sorrow, and of a deeply-vexed mind. He would have felt but little triumph if accepted, and had not thought that he could be humiliated by any rejection. In this frame of mind he had gone to his work; but now he found, to his own surprise, that this girl's answer had made him absolutely unhappy. Having expressed a wish for this thing, the very expression of the wish made him long to possess it. He found, as he rode along silently by her side, that he was capable of more earnestness of desire than he had known himself to possess. He was at this moment unhappy, disappointed, anxious, distrustful of the future, and more intent on one special toy than he had ever been before, even as a boy. He was vexed, and felt himself to be sore at heart. He looked round at her, as she sat silent, quiet, and somewhat sad upon her pony, and declared to himself that she was very beautiful,--that she was a thing to be gained if still there might be the possibility of gaining her. He felt that he really loved her, and yet he was almost angry with himself for so feeling. Why had he subjected himself to this numbing weakness? His love had never given him any pleasure. Indeed he had never hitherto acknowledged it; but now he was driven to do so on finding it to be the source of trouble and pain. I think it is open to us to doubt whether, even yet, Bernard Dale was in love with his cousin; whether he was not rather in love with his own desire. But against himself he found a verdict that he was in love, and was angry with himself and with all the world.\n\n\"Ah, Bell,\" he said, coming close up to her, \"I wish you could understand how I love you.\" And, as he spoke, his cousin unconsciously recognized more of affection in his tone, and less of that spirit of bargaining which had seemed to pervade all his former pleas, than she had ever found before.\n\n\"And do I not love you? Have I not offered to be to you in all respects as a sister?\"\n\n\"That is nothing. Such an offer to me now is simply laughing at me. Bell, I tell you what,--I will not give you up. The fact is, you do not know me yet,--not know me as you must know any man before you choose him for your husband. You and Lily are not alike in this. You are cautious, doubtful of yourself, and perhaps, also, somewhat doubtful of others. My heart is set upon this, and I shall still try to succeed.\"\n\n\"Ah, Bernard, do not say that! Believe me, when I tell you that it can never be.\"\n\n\"No; I will not believe you. I will not allow myself to be made utterly wretched. I tell you fairly that I will not believe you. I may surely hope if I choose to hope. No, Bell, I will never give you up,--unless, indeed, I should see you become another man's wife.\"\n\nAs he said this, they all turned in through the squire's gate, and rode up to the yard in which it was their habit to dismount from their horses.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV. JOHN EAMES TAKES A WALK.\n\nJohn Eames watched the party of cavaliers as they rode away from his mother's door, and then started upon a solitary walk, as soon as the noise of the horses' hoofs had passed away out of the street. He was by no means happy in his mind as he did so. Indeed, he was overwhelmed with care and trouble, and as he went along very gloomy thoughts passed through his mind. Had he not better go to Australia, or Vancouver's Island, or--? I will not name the places which the poor fellow suggested to himself as possible terminations of the long journeys which he might not improbably be called upon to take. That very day, just before the Dales had come in, he had received a second letter from his darling Amelia, written very closely upon the heels of the first. Why had he not answered her? Was he ill? Was he untrue? No; she would not believe that, and therefore fell back upon the probability of his illness. If it was so, she would rush down to see him. Nothing on earth should keep her from the bedside of her betrothed. If she did not get an answer from her beloved John by return of post, she would be down with him at Guestwick by the express train. Here was a position for such a young man as John Eames! And of Amelia Roper we may say that she was a young woman who would not give up her game, as long as the least chance remained of her winning it. \"I must go somewhere,\" John said to himself, as he put on his slouched hat and wandered forth through the back streets of Guestwick. What would his mother say when she heard of Amelia Roper? What would she say when she saw her?\n\nHe walked away towards the Manor, so that he might roam about the Guestwick woods in solitude. There was a path with a stile, leading off from the high road, about half a mile beyond the lodges through which the Dales had ridden up to the house, and by this path John Eames turned in, and went away till he had left the Manor house behind him, and was in the centre of the Guestwick woods. He knew the whole ground well, having roamed there ever since he was first allowed to go forth upon his walks alone. He had thought of Lily Dale by the hour together, as he had lost himself among the oak-trees; but in those former days he had thought of her with some pleasure. Now he could only think of her as of one gone from him for ever; and then he had also to think of her whom he had taken to himself in Lily's place.\n\nYoung men, very young men,--men so young that it may be almost a question whether or no they have as yet reached their manhood,--are more inclined to be earnest and thoughtful when alone than they ever are when with others, even though those others be their elders. I fancy that, as we grow old ourselves, we are apt to forget that it was so with us; and, forgetting it, we do not believe that it is so with our children. We constantly talk of the thoughtlessness of youth. I do not know whether we might not more appropriately speak of its thoughtfulness. It is, however, no doubt, true that thought will not at once produce wisdom. It may almost be a question whether such wisdom as many of us have in our mature years has not come from the dying out of the power of temptation, rather than as the results of thought and resolution. Men, full fledged and at their work, are, for the most part, too busy for much thought; but lads, on whom the work of the world has not yet fallen with all its pressure,--they have time for thinking.\n\nAnd thus John Eames was thoughtful. They who knew him best accounted him to be a gay, good-hearted, somewhat reckless young man, open to temptation, but also open to good impressions; as to whom no great success could be predicated, but of whom his friends might fairly hope that he might so live as to bring upon them no disgrace and not much trouble. But, above all things, they would have called him thoughtless. In so calling him, they judged him wrong. He was ever thinking,--thinking much of the world as it appeared to him, and of himself as he appeared to the world; and thinking, also, of things beyond the world. What was to be his fate here and hereafter? Lily Dale was gone from him, and Amelia Roper was hanging round his neck like a millstone! What, under such circumstances, was to be his fate here and hereafter?\n\nWe may say that the difficulties in his way were not as yet very great. As to Lily, indeed, he had no room for hope; but, then, his love for Lily had, perhaps, been a sentiment rather than a passion. Most young men have to go through that disappointment, and are enabled to bear it without much injury to their prospects or happiness. And in after-life the remembrance of such love is a blessing rather than a curse, enabling the possessor of it to feel that in those early days there was something within him of which he had no cause to be ashamed. I do not pity John Eames much in regard to Lily Dale. And then, as to Amelia Roper,--had he achieved but a tithe of that lady's experience in the world, or possessed a quarter of her audacity, surely such a difficulty as that need not have stood much in his way! What could Amelia do to him if he fairly told her that he was not minded to marry her? In very truth he had never promised to do so. He was in no way bound to her, not even by honour. Honour, indeed, with such as her! But men are cowards before women until they become tyrants; and are easy dupes, till of a sudden they recognize the fact that it is pleasanter to be the victimizer than the victim,--and as easy. There are men, indeed, who never learn the latter lesson.\n\nBut, though the cause for fear was so slight, poor John Eames was thoroughly afraid. Little things which, in connection with so deep a sorrow as his, it is almost ridiculous to mention, added to his embarrassments, and made an escape from them seem to him to be impossible. He could not return to London without going to Burton Crescent, because his clothes were there, and because he owed to Mrs. Roper some small sum of money which on his return to London he would not have immediately in his pocket. He must therefore meet Amelia, and he knew that he had not the courage to tell a girl, face to face, that he did not love her, after he had been once induced to say that he did do so. His boldest conception did not go beyond the writing of a letter in which he would renounce her, and removing himself altogether from that quarter of the town in which Burton Crescent was situated. But then about his clothes, and that debt of his? And what if Amelia should in the meantime come down to Guestwick and claim him? Could he in his mother's presence declare that she had no right to make such claim? The difficulties, in truth, were not very great, but they were too heavy for that poor young clerk from the Income-tax Office.\n\nYou will declare that he must have been a fool and a coward. Yet he could read and understand Shakspeare. He knew much,--by far too much,--of Byron's poetry by heart. He was a deep critic, often writing down his criticisms in a lengthy journal which he kept. He could write quickly, and with understanding; and I may declare that men at his office had already ascertained that he was no fool. He knew his business, and could do it,--as many men failed to do who were much less foolish before the world. And as to that matter of cowardice, he would have thought it the greatest blessing in the world to be shut up in a room with Crosbie, having permission to fight with him till one of them should have been brought by stress of battle to give up his claim to Lily Dale. Eames was no coward. He feared no man on earth. But he was terribly afraid of Amelia Roper.\n\nHe wandered about through the old Manor woods very ill at ease. The post from Guestwick went out at seven, and he must at once make up his mind whether or no he would write to Amelia on that day. He must also make up his mind as to what he would say to her. He felt that he should at least answer her letter, let his answer be what it might. Should he promise to marry her,--say, in ten or twelve years' time? Should he tell her that he was a blighted being, unfit for love, and with humility entreat of her that he might be excused? Or should he write to her mother, telling her that Burton Crescent would not suit him any longer, promising her to send the balance on receipt of his next payment, and asking her to send his clothes in a bundle to the Income-tax Office? Or should he go home to his own mother, and boldly tell it all to her?\n\nHe at last resolved that he must write the letter, and as he composed it in his mind he sat himself down beneath an old tree which stood on a spot at which many of the forest tracks met and crossed each other. The letter, as he framed it here, was not a bad letter, if only he could have got it written and posted. Every word of it he chose with precision, and in his mind he emphasized every expression which told his mind clearly and justified his purpose. \"He acknowledged himself to have been wrong in misleading his correspondent, and allowing her to imagine that she possessed his heart. He had not a heart at her disposal. He had been weak not to write to her before, having been deterred from doing so by the fear of giving her pain; but now he felt that he was bound in honour to tell her the truth. Having so told her, he would not return to Burton Crescent, if it would pain her to see him there. He would always have a deep regard for her,\"--Oh, Johnny!--\"and would hope anxiously that her welfare in life might be complete.\" That was the letter, as he wrote it on the tablets of his mind under the tree; but the getting it put on to paper was a task, as he knew, of greater difficulty. Then, as he repeated it to himself, he fell asleep.\n\n\"Young man,\" said a voice in his ears as he slept. At first the voice spoke as a voice from his dream without waking him, but when it was repeated, he sat up and saw that a stout gentleman was standing over him. For a moment he did not know where he was, or how he had come there; nor could he recollect, as he saw the trees about him, how long he had been in the wood. But he knew the stout gentleman well enough, though he had not seen him for more than two years. \"Young man,\" said the voice, \"if you want to catch rheumatism, that's the way to do it. Why, it's young Eames, isn't it?\"\n\n\"Yes, my lord,\" said Johnny, raising himself up so that he was now sitting, instead of lying, as he looked up into the earl's rosy face.\n\n\"I knew your father, and a very good man he was; only he shouldn't have taken to farming. People think they can farm without learning the trade, but that's a very great mistake. I can farm, because I've learned it. Don't you think you'd better get up?\" Whereupon Johnny raised himself to his feet. \"Not but what you're very welcome to lie there if you like it. Only, in October, you know--\"\n\n\"I'm afraid I'm trespassing, my lord,\" said Eames. \"I came in off the path, and--\"\n\n\"You're welcome; you're very welcome. If you'll come up to the house, I'll give you some luncheon.\" This hospitable offer, however, Johnny declined, alleging that it was late, and that he was going home to dinner.\n\n\"Come along,\" said the earl. \"You can't go any shorter way than by the house. Dear, dear, how well I remember your father. He was a much cleverer man than I am,--very much; but he didn't know how to send a beast to market any better than a child. By-the-by, they have put you into a public office, haven't they?\"\n\n\"Yes, my lord.\"\n\n\"And a very good thing, too,--a very good thing, indeed. But why were you asleep in the wood? It isn't warm, you know. I call it rather cold.\" And the earl stopped, and looked at him, scrutinizing him, as though resolved to inquire into so deep a mystery.\n\n\"I was taking a walk, and thinking of something, I sat down.\"\n\n\"Leave of absence, I suppose?\"\n\n\"Yes, my lord.\"\n\n\"Have you got into trouble? You look as though you were in trouble. Your poor father used to be in trouble.\"\n\n\"I haven't taken to farming,\" said Johnny, with an attempt at a smile.\n\n\"Ha, ha, ha,--quite right. No, don't take to farming. Unless you learn it, you know, you might just as well take to shoemaking;--just the same. You haven't got into trouble, then; eh?\"\n\n\"No, my lord, not particularly.\"\n\n\"Not particularly! I know very well that young men do get into trouble when they get up to London. If you want any--any advice, or that sort of thing, you may come to me; for I knew your father well. Do you like shooting?\"\n\n\"I never did shoot anything.\"\n\n\"Well, perhaps better not. To tell the truth, I'm not very fond of young men who take to shooting without having anything to shoot at. By-the-by, now I think of it, I'll send your mother some game.\" It may, however, here be fair to mention that game very often came from Guestwick Manor to Mrs. Eames. \"And look here, cold pheasant for breakfast is the best thing I know of. Pheasants at dinner are rubbish,--mere rubbish. Here we are at the house. Will you come in and have a glass of wine?\"\n\nBut this John Eames declined, pleasing the earl better by doing so than he would have done by accepting it. Not that the lord was inhospitable or insincere in his offer, but he preferred that such a one as John Eames should receive his proffered familiarity without too much immediate assurance. He felt that Eames was a little in awe of his companion's rank, and he liked him the better for it. He liked him the better for it, and was a man apt to remember his likings. \"If you won't come in, good-by,\" and he gave Johnny his hand.\n\n\"Good evening, my lord,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"And remember this; it is the deuce of a thing to have rheumatism in your loins. I wouldn't go to sleep under a tree, if I were you,--not in October. But you're always welcome to go anywhere about the place.\"\n\n\"Thank you, my lord.\"\n\n\"And if you should take to shooting,--but I dare say you won't; and if you come to trouble, and want advice, or that sort of thing, write to me. I knew your father well.\" And so they parted, Eames returning on his road towards Guestwick.\n\nFor some reason, which he could not define, he felt better after his interview with the earl. There had been something about the fat, good-natured, sensible old man, which had cheered him, in spite of his sorrow. \"Pheasants for dinner are rubbish,--mere rubbish,\" he said to himself, over and over again, as he went along the road; and they were the first words which he spoke to his mother, after entering the house.\n\n\"I wish we had some of that sort of rubbish,\" said she.\n\n\"So you will, to-morrow;\" and then he described to her his interview.\n\n\"The earl was, at any rate, quite right about lying upon the ground. I wonder you can be so foolish. And he is right about your poor father too. But you have got to change your boots; and we shall be ready for dinner almost immediately.\"\n\nBut Johnny Eames, before he sat down to dinner, did write his letter to Amelia, and did go out to post it with his own hands,--much to his mother's annoyance. But the letter would not get itself written in that strong and appropriate language which had come to him as he was roaming through the woods. It was a bald letter, and somewhat cowardly withal.\n\nDEAR AMELIA [the letter ran],--I have received both of yours; and did not answer the first because I felt that there was a difficulty in expressing what I wish to say; and now it will be better that you should allow the subject to stand over till I am back in town. I shall be there in ten days from this. I have been quite well, and am so; but of course am much obliged by your inquiries. I know you will think this very cold; but when I tell you everything, you will agree with me that it is best. If I were to marry, I know that we should be unhappy, because we should have nothing to live on. If I have ever said anything to deceive you, I beg your pardon with all my heart;--but perhaps it will be better to let the subject remain till we shall meet again in London.\n\nBelieve me to be Your most sincere friend, And I may say admirer,--[Oh, John Eames!]\n\n# CHAPTER XV. THE LAST DAY.\n\nLast days are wretched days; and so are last moments wretched moments. It is not the fact that the parting is coming which makes these days and moments so wretched, but the feeling that something special is expected from them, which something they always fail to produce. Spasmodic periods of pleasure, of affection, or even of study, seldom fail of disappointment when premeditated. When last days are coming, they should be allowed to come and to glide away without special notice or mention. And as for last moments, there should be none such. Let them ever be ended, even before their presence has been acknowledged.\n\nBut Lily Dale had not yet been taught these lessons by her world's experience, and she expected that this sweetest cup of which she had ever drank should go on being sweet--sweeter and still sweeter--as long as she could press it to her lips. How the dregs had come to mix themselves with the last drops we have already seen; and on that same day--on the Monday evening--the bitter task still remained; for Crosbie, as they walked about through the gardens in the evening, found other subjects on which he thought it necessary to give her sundry hints, intended for her edification, which came to her with much of the savour of a lecture. A girl, when she is thoroughly in love, as surely was the case with Lily, likes to receive hints as to her future life from the man to whom she is devoted; but she would, I think, prefer that such hints should be short, and that the lesson should be implied rather than declared;--that they should, in fact, be hints and not lectures. Crosbie, who was a man of tact, who understood the world and had been dealing with women for many years, no doubt understood all this as well as we do. But he had come to entertain a notion that he was an injured man, that he was giving very much more than was to be given to him, and that therefore he was entitled to take liberties which might not fairly be within the reach of another lover. My reader will say that in all this he was ungenerous. Well; he was ungenerous. I do not know that I have ever said that much generosity was to be expected from him. He had some principles of right and wrong under the guidance of which it may perhaps be hoped that he will not go utterly astray; but his past life had not been of a nature to make him unselfish. He was ungenerous, and Lily felt it, though she would not acknowledge it even to herself. She had been very open with him,--acknowledging the depth of her love for him; telling him that he was now all in all to her; that life without his love would be impossible to her: and in a certain way he took advantage of these strong avowals, treating her as though she were a creature utterly in his power;--as indeed she was.\n\nOn that evening he said no more of Johnny Eames, but said much of the difficulty of a man establishing himself with a wife in London, who had nothing but his own moderate income on which to rely. He did not in so many words tell her that if her friends could make up for her two or three thousand pounds,--that being much less than he had expected when he first made his offer,--this terrible difficulty would be removed; but he said enough to make her understand that the world would call him very imprudent in taking a girl who had nothing. And as he spoke of these things, Lily remaining for the most part silent as he did so, it occurred to him that he might talk to her freely of his past life,--more freely than he would have done had he feared that he might lose her by any such disclosures. He had no fear of losing her. Alas! might it not be possible that he had some such hope!\n\nHe told her that his past life had been expensive; that, though he was not in debt, he had lived up to every shilling that he had, and that he had contracted habits of expenditure which it would be almost impossible for him to lay aside at a day's notice. Then he spoke of entanglements, meaning, as he did so, to explain more fully what were their nature,--but not daring to do so when he found that Lily was altogether in the dark as to what he meant. No; he was not a generous man,--a very ungenerous man. And yet, during all this time, he thought that he was guided by principle. \"It will be best that I should be honest with her,\" he said to himself. And then he told himself, scores of times, that when making his offer he had expected, and had a right to expect, that she would not be penniless. Under those circumstances he had done the best he could for her--offering her his heart honestly, with a quick readiness to make her his own at the earliest day that she might think possible. Had he been more cautious, he need not have fallen into this cruel mistake; but she, at any rate, could not quarrel with him for his imprudence. And still he was determined to stand by his engagement and willing to marry her, although, as he the more thought of it, he felt the more strongly that he would thereby ruin his prospects, and thrust beyond his own reach all those good things which he had hoped to win. As he continued to talk to her he gave himself special credit for his generosity, and felt that he was only doing his duty by her in pointing out to her all the difficulties which lay in the way of their marriage.\n\nAt first Lily said some words intended to convey an assurance that she would be the most economical wife that man ever had, but she soon ceased from such promises as these. Her perceptions were keen, and she discovered that the difficulties of which he was afraid were those which he must overcome before his marriage, not any which might be expected to overwhelm him after it. \"A cheap and nasty ménage would be my aversion,\" he said to her. \"It is that which I want to avoid,--chiefly for your sake.\" Then she promised him that she would wait patiently for his time--\"even though it should be for seven years,\" she said, looking up into his face and trying to find there some sign of approbation. \"That's nonsense,\" he said. \"People are not patriarchs now-a-days. I suppose we shall have to wait two years. And that's a deuce of a bore,--a terrible bore.\" And there was that in the tone of his voice which grated on her feelings, and made her wretched for the moment.\n\nAs he parted with her for the night on her own side of the little bridge which led from one garden to the other, he put his arm round her to embrace her and kiss her, as he had often done at that spot. It had become a habit with them to say their evening farewells there, and the secluded little nook amongst the shrubs was inexpressibly dear to Lily. But on the present occasion she made an effort to avoid his caress. She turned from him--very slightly, but it was enough, and he felt it. \"Are you angry with me?\" he said. \"Oh, no! Adolphus; how can I be angry with you?\" And then she turned to him and gave him her face to kiss almost before he had again asked for it. \"He shall not at any rate think that I am unkind to him,--and it will not matter now,\" she said to herself, as she walked slowly across the lawn, in the dark, up to her mother's drawing-room window.\n\n\"Well, dearest,\" said Mrs. Dale, who was there alone; \"did the beards wag merry in the Great Hall this evening?\" That was a joke with them, for neither Crosbie nor Bernard Dale used a razor at his toilet.\n\n\"Not specially merry. And I think it was my fault, for I have a headache. Mamma, I believe I will go at once to bed.\"\n\n\"My darling, is there anything wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing, mamma. But we had such a long ride; and then Adolphus is going, and of course we have so much to say. To-morrow will be the last day, for I shall only just see him on Wednesday morning; and as I want to be well, if possible, I'll go to bed.\" And so she took her candle and went.\n\nWhen Bell came up, Lily was still awake, but she begged her sister not to disturb her. \"Don't talk to me, Bell,\" she said. \"I'm trying to make myself quiet, and I half feel that I should get childish if I went on talking. I have almost more to think of than I know how to manage.\" And she strove, not altogether unsuccessfully, to speak with a cheery tone, as though the cares which weighed upon her were not unpleasant in their nature. Then her sister kissed her and left her to her thoughts.\n\nAnd she had great matter for thinking; so great, that many hours sounded in her ears from the clock on the stairs before she brought her thoughts to a shape that satisfied herself. She did so bring them at last, and then she slept. She did so bring them, toiling over her work with tears that made her pillow wet, with heart-burning and almost with heart-breaking, with much doubting, and many anxious, eager inquiries within her own bosom as to that which she ought to do, and that which she could endure to do. But at last her resolve was taken, and then she slept.\n\nIt had been agreed between them that Crosbie should come down to the Small House on the next day after breakfast, and remain there till the time came for riding. But Lily determined to alter this arrangement, and accordingly put on her hat immediately after breakfast, and posted herself at the bridge, so as to intercept her lover as he came. He soon appeared with his friend Dale, and she at once told him her purpose.\n\n\"I want to have a talk with you, Adolphus, before you go in to mamma; so come with me into the field.\"\n\n\"All right,\" said he.\n\n\"And Bernard can finish his cigar on the lawn. Mamma and Bell will join him there.\"\n\n\"All right,\" said Bernard. So they separated; and Crosbie went away with Lily into the field where they had first learned to know each other in those haymaking days.\n\nShe did not say much till they were well away from the house; but answered what words he chose to speak,--not knowing very well of what he spoke. But when she considered that they had reached the proper spot, she began very abruptly.\n\n\"Adolphus,\" she said, \"I have something to say to you,--something to which you must listen very carefully.\" Then he looked at her, and at once knew that she was in earnest.\n\n\"This is the last day on which I could say it,\" she continued; \"and I am very glad that I have not let the last day go by without saying it. I should not have known how to put it in a letter.\"\n\n\"What is it, Lily?\"\n\n\"And I do not know that I can say it properly; but I hope that you will not be hard upon me. Adolphus, if you wish that all this between us should be over, I will consent.\"\n\n\"Lily!\"\n\n\"I mean what I say. If you wish it, I will consent; and when I have said so, proposing it myself, you may be quite sure that I shall never blame you, if you take me at my word.\"\n\n\"Are you tired of me, Lily?\"\n\n\"No. I shall never be tired of you,--never weary with loving you. I did not wish to say so now; but I will answer your question boldly. Tired of you! I fancy that a girl can never grow tired of her lover. But I would sooner die in the struggle than be the cause of your ruin. It would be better,--in every way better.\"\n\n\"I have said nothing of being ruined.\"\n\n\"But listen to me. I should not die if you left me,--not be utterly broken-hearted. Nothing on earth can I ever love as I have loved you. But I have a God and a Saviour that will be enough for me. I can turn to them with content, if it be well that you should leave me. I have gone to them, and--\" But at this moment she could utter no more words. She had broken down in her effort, losing her voice through the strength of her emotion. As she did not choose that he should see her overcome, she turned from him and walked away across the grass.\n\nOf course he followed her; but he was not so quick after her, but that time had been given to her to recover herself. \"It is true,\" she said. \"I have the strength of which I tell you. Though I have given myself to you as your wife, I can bear to be divorced from you now,--now. And, my love, though it may sound heartless, I would sooner be so divorced from you, than cling to you as a log that must drag you down under the water, and drown you in trouble and care. I would;--indeed I would. If you go, of course that kind of thing is over for me. But the world has more than that,--much more; and I would make myself happy;--yes, my love, I would be happy. You need not fear that.\"\n\n\"But, Lily, why is all this said to me here to-day?\"\n\n\"Because it is my duty to say it. I understand all your position now, though it is only now. It never flashed on me till yesterday. When you proposed to me, you thought that I,--that I had some fortune.\"\n\n\"Never mind that now, Lily.\"\n\n\"But you did. I see it all now. I ought perhaps to have told you that it was not so. There has been the mistake, and we are both sufferers. But we need not make the suffering deeper than needs be. My love, you are free,--from this moment. And even my heart shall not blame you for accepting your freedom.\"\n\n\"And are you afraid of poverty?\" he asked her.\n\n\"I am afraid of poverty for you. You and I have lived differently. Luxuries, of which I know nothing, have been your daily comforts. I tell you I can bear to part with you, but I cannot bear to become the source of your unhappiness. Yes; I will bear it; and none shall dare in my hearing to speak against you. I have brought you here to say the word; nay, more than that,--to advise you to say it.\"\n\nHe stood silent for a moment, during which he held her by the hand. She was looking into his face, but he was looking away into the clouds; striving to appear as though he was the master of the occasion. But during those moments his mind was wracked with doubt. What if he should take her at her word? Some few would say bitter things against him, but such bitter things had been said against many another man without harming him. Would it not be well for both if he should take her at her word? She would recover and love again, as other girls had done; and as for him, he would thus escape from the ruin at which he had been gazing for the last week past. For it was ruin,--utter ruin. He did love her; so he declared to himself. But was he a man who ought to throw the world away for love? Such men there were; but was he one of them? Could he be happy in that small house, somewhere near the New Road, with five children and horrid misgivings as to the baker's bill? Of all men living, was not he the last that should have allowed himself to fall into such a trap? All this passed through his mind as he turned his face up to the clouds with a look that was intended to be grand and noble.\n\n\"Speak to me, Adolphus, and say that it shall be so.\"\n\nThen his heart misgave him, and he lacked the courage to extricate himself from his trouble; or, as he afterwards said to himself, he had not the heart to do it. \"If I understand you, rightly, Lily, all this comes from no want of love on your own part?\"\n\n\"Want of love on my part? But you should not ask me that.\"\n\n\"Until you tell me that there is such a want, I will agree to no parting.\" Then he took her hand and put it within his arm. \"No, Lily; whatever may be our cares and troubles, we are bound together,--indissolubly.\"\n\n\"Are we?\" said she; and as she spoke, her voice trembled, and her hand shook.\n\n\"Much too firmly for any such divorce as that. No, Lily, I claim the right to tell you all my troubles; but I shall not let you go.\"\n\n\"But, Adolphus--\" and the hand on his arm was beginning to cling to it again.\n\n\"Adolphus,\" said he, \"has got nothing more to say on that subject. He exercises the right which he believes to be his own, and chooses to retain the prize which he has won.\"\n\nShe was now clinging to him in very truth. \"Oh, my love!\" she said. \"I do not know how to say it again. It is of you that I am thinking;--of you, of you!\"\n\n\"I know you are; but you have misunderstood me a little; that's all.\"\n\n\"Have I? Then listen to me again, once more, my heart's own darling, my love, my husband, my lord! If I cannot be to you at once like Ruth, and never cease from coming after you, my thoughts to you shall be like those of Ruth:--if aught but death part thee and me, may God do so to me and more also.\" Then she fell upon his breast and wept.\n\nHe still hardly understood the depth of her character. He was not himself deep enough to comprehend it all. But yet he was awed by her great love, and exalted to a certain solemnity of feeling which for the time made him rejoice in his late decision. For a few hours he was minded to throw the world behind him, and wear this woman, as such a woman should be worn,--as a comforter to him in all things, and a strong shield against great troubles. \"Lily,\" he said, \"my own Lily!\"\n\n\"Yes, your own, to take when you please, and leave untaken while you please; and as much your own in one way as in the other.\" Then she looked up again, and essayed to laugh as she did so. \"You will think I am frantic, but I am so happy. I don't care about your going now; indeed I don't. There; you may go now, this minute, if you like it.\" And she withdrew her hand from him. \"I feel so differently from what I have done for the last few days. I am so glad you have spoken to me as you did. Of course I ought to bear all those things with you. But I cannot be unhappy about it now. I wonder if I went to work and made a lot of things, whether that would help?\"\n\n\"A set of shirts for me, for instance?\"\n\n\"I could do that, at any rate.\"\n\n\"It may come to that yet, some of these days.\"\n\n\"I pray God that it may.\" Then again she was serious, and the tears came once more into her eyes. \"I pray God that it may. To be of use to you,--to work for you,--to do something for you that may have in it some sober, earnest purport of usefulness;--that is what I want above all things. I want to be with you at once that I may be of service to you. Would that you and I were alone together, that I might do everything for you. I sometimes think that a very poor man's wife is the happiest, because she does do everything.\"\n\n\"You shall do everything very soon,\" said he; and then they sauntered along pleasantly through the morning hours, and when they again appeared at Mrs. Dale's table, Mrs. Dale and Bell were astonished at Lily's brightness. All her old ways had seemed to return to her, and she made her little saucy speeches to Mr. Crosbie as she had used to do when he was first becoming fascinated by her sweetness. \"You know that you'll be such a swell when you get to that countess's house that you'll forget all about Allington.\"\n\n\"Of course I shall,\" said he.\n\n\"And the paper you write upon will be all over coronets,--that is, if ever you do write. Perhaps you will to Bernard some day, just to show that you are staying at a castle.\"\n\n\"You certainly don't deserve that he should write to you,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"I don't expect it for a moment,--not till he gets back to London and finds that he has nothing else to do at his office. But I should so like to see how you and Lady Julia get on together. It was quite clear that she regarded you as an ogre; didn't she, Bell?\"\n\n\"So many people are ogres to Lady Julia,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I believe Lady Julia to be a very good woman,\" said Mrs. Dale, \"and I won't have her abused.\"\n\n\"Particularly before poor Bernard, who is her pet nephew,\" said Lily. \"I dare say Adolphus will become a pet too when she has been a week with him at Courcy Castle. Do try and cut Bernard out.\"\n\nFrom all which Mrs. Dale learned that some care which had sat heavy on Lily's heart was now lightened, if not altogether removed. She had asked no questions of her daughter, but she had perceived during the past few days that Lily was in trouble, and she knew that such trouble had arisen from her engagement. She had asked no questions, but of course she had been told what was Mr. Crosbie's income, and had been made to understand that it was not to be considered as amply sufficient for all the wants of matrimony. There was little difficulty in guessing what was the source of Lily's care, and as little in now perceiving that something had been said between them by which that care had been relieved.\n\nAfter that they all rode, and the afternoon went by pleasantly. It was the last day indeed, but Lily had determined that she would not be sad. She had told him that he might go now, and that she would not be discontented at his going. She knew that the morrow would be very blank to her; but she struggled to live up to the spirit of her promise, and she succeeded. They all dined at the Great House, even Mrs. Dale doing so upon this occasion. When they had come in from the garden in the evening, Crosbie talked more to Mrs. Dale than he did even to Lily, while Lily sat a little distant, listening with all her ears, sometimes saying a low-toned word, and happy beyond expression in the feeling that her mother and her lover should understand each other. And it must be understood that Crosbie at this time was fully determined to conquer the difficulties of which he had thought so much, and to fix the earliest day which might be possible for his marriage. The solemnity of that meeting in the field still hung about him, and gave to his present feelings a manliness and a truth of purpose which were too generally wanting to them. If only those feelings would last! But now he talked to Mrs. Dale about her daughter, and about their future prospects, in a tone which he could not have used had not his mind for the time been true to her. He had never spoken so freely to Lily's mother, and at no time had Mrs. Dale felt for him so much of a mother's love. He apologized for the necessity of some delay, arguing that he could not endure to see his young wife without the comfort of a home of her own, and that he was now, as he always had been, afraid of incurring debt. Mrs. Dale disliked waiting engagements,--as do all mothers,--but she could not answer unkindly to such pleading as this.\n\n\"Lily is so very young,\" she said, \"that she may well wait for a year or so.\"\n\n\"For seven years,\" said Lily, jumping up and whispering into her mother's ear. \"I shall hardly be six-and-twenty then, which is not at all too old.\"\n\nAnd so the evening passed away very pleasantly.\n\n\"God bless you, Adolphus!\" Mrs. Dale said to him, as she parted with him at her own door. It was the first time that she had called him by his Christian name. \"I hope you understand how much we are trusting to you.\"\n\n\"I do,--I do,\" said he, as he pressed her hand. Then as he walked back alone, he swore to himself, binding himself to the oath with all his heart, that he would be true to those women,--both to the daughter and to the mother; for the solemnity of the morning was still upon him.\n\nHe was to start the next morning before eight, Bernard having undertaken to drive him over to the railway at Guestwick. The breakfast was on the table shortly after seven; and just as the two men had come down, Lily entered the room, with her hat and shawl. \"I said I would be in to pour out your tea,\" said she; and then she sat herself down over against the teapot.\n\nIt was a silent meal, for people do not know what to say in those last minutes. And Bernard, too, was there; proving how true is the adage which says, that two are company, but that three are not. I think that Lily was wrong to come up on that last morning; but she would not hear of letting him start without seeing him, when her lover had begged her not to put herself to so much trouble. Trouble! Would she not have sat up all night to see even the last of the top of his hat?\n\nThen Bernard, muttering something about the horse, went away. \"I have only one minute to speak to you,\" said she, jumping up, \"and I have been thinking all night of what I had to say. It is so easy to think, and so hard to speak.\"\n\n\"My darling, I understand it all.\"\n\n\"But you must understand this, that I will never distrust you. I will never ask you to give me up again, or say that I could be happy without you. I could not live without you; that is, without the knowledge that you are mine. But I will never be impatient, never. Pray, pray believe me! Nothing shall make me distrust you.\"\n\n\"Dearest Lily, I will endeavour to give you no cause.\"\n\n\"I know you will not; but I specially wanted to tell you that. And you will write,--very soon?\"\n\n\"Directly I get there.\"\n\n\"And as often as you can. But I won't bother you; only your letters will make me so happy. I shall be so proud when they come to me. I shall be afraid of writing too much to you, for fear I should tire you.\"\n\n\"You will never do that.\"\n\n\"Shall I not? But you must write first, you know. If you could only understand how I shall live upon your letters! And now good-by. There are the wheels. God bless you, my own, my own!\" And she gave herself up into his arms, as she had given herself up into his heart.\n\nShe stood at the door as the two men got into the gig, and, as it passed down through the gate, she hurried out upon the terrace, from whence she could see it for a few yards down the lane. Then she ran from the terrace to the gate, and, hurrying through the gate, made her way into the churchyard, from the farther corner of which she could see the heads of the two men till they had made the turn into the main road beyond the parsonage. There she remained till the very sound of the wheels no longer reached her ears, stretching her eyes in the direction they had taken. Then she turned round slowly and made her way out at the churchyard gate, which opened on to the road close to the front door of the Small House.\n\n\"I should like to punch his head,\" said Hopkins, the gardener, to himself, as he saw the gig driven away and saw Lily trip after it, that she might see the last of him whom it carried. \"And I wouldn't think nothing of doing it; no more I wouldn't,\" Hopkins added in his soliloquy. It was generally thought about the place that Miss Lily was Hopkins's favourite; though he showed it chiefly by snubbing her more frequently than he snubbed her sister.\n\nLily had evidently intended to return home through the front door; but she changed her purpose before she reached the house, and made her way slowly back through the churchyard, and by the gate of the Great House, and by the garden at the back of it, till she crossed the little bridge. But on the bridge she rested awhile, leaning against the railing as she had often leant with him, and thinking of all that had passed since that July day on which she had first met him. On no spot had he so often told her of his love as on this, and nowhere had she so eagerly sworn to him that she would be his own dutiful loving wife.\n\n\"And by God's help so I will,\" she said to herself, as she walked firmly up to the house. \"He has gone, mamma,\" she said, as she entered the breakfast-room. \"And now we'll go back to our work-a-day ways; it has been all Sunday for me for the last six weeks.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XVI. MR. CROSBIE MEETS AN OLD CLERGYMAN ON HIS WAY TO COURCY CASTLE.\n\nFor the first mile or two of their journey Crosbie and Bernard Dale sat, for the most part, silent in their gig. Lily, as she ran down to the churchyard corner and stood there looking after them with her loving eyes, had not been seen by them. But the spirit of her devotion was still strong upon them both, and they felt that it would not be well to strike at once into any ordinary topic of conversation. And, moreover, we may presume that Crosbie did feel much at thus parting from such a girl as Lily Dale, with whom he had lived in close intercourse for the last six weeks, and whom he loved with all his heart,--with all the heart that he had for such purposes. In those doubts as to his marriage which had troubled him he had never expressed to himself any disapproval of Lily. He had not taught himself to think that she was other than he would have her be, that he might thus give himself an excuse for parting from her. Not as yet, at any rate, had he had recourse to that practice, so common with men who wish to free themselves from the bonds with which they have permitted themselves to be bound. Lily had been too sweet to his eyes, to his touch, to all his senses for that. He had enjoyed too keenly the pleasure of being with her, and of hearing her tell him that she loved him, to allow of his being personally tired of her. He had not been so spoilt by his club life but that he had taken exquisite pleasure in all her nice country ways, and soft, kind-hearted, womanly humour. He was by no means tired of Lily. Better than any of his London pleasures was this pleasure of making love in the green fields to Lily Dale. It was the consequences of it that affrighted him. Babies with their belongings would come; and dull evenings, over a dull fire, or else the pining grief of a disappointed woman. He would be driven to be careful as to his clothes, because the ordering of a new coat would entail a serious expenditure. He could go no more among countesses and their daughters, because it would be out of the question that his wife should visit at their houses. All the victories that he had ever won must be given up. He was thinking of this even while the gig was going round the corner near the parsonage house, and while Lily's eyes were still blessed with some view of his departing back; but he was thinking, also, that moment, that there might be other victory in store for him; that it might be possible for him to learn to like that fireside, even though babies should be there, and a woman opposite to him intent on baby cares. He was struggling as best he knew how; for the solemnity which Lily had imparted to him had not yet vanished from his spirit.\n\n\"I hope that, upon the whole, you feel contented with your visit?\" said Bernard to him, at last.\n\n\"Contented? Of course I do.\"\n\n\"That is easily said; and civility to me, perhaps, demands as much. But I know that you have, to some extent, been disappointed.\"\n\n\"Well; yes. I have been disappointed as regards money. It is of no use denying it.\"\n\n\"I should not mention it now, only that I want to know that you exonerate me.\"\n\n\"I have never blamed you;--neither you, nor anybody else; unless, indeed, it has been myself.\"\n\n\"You mean that you regret what you've done?\"\n\n\"No; I don't mean that. I am too devotedly attached to that dear girl whom we have just left to feel any regret that I have engaged myself to her. But I do think that had I managed better with your uncle things might have been different.\"\n\n\"I doubt it. Indeed I know that it is not so; and can assure you that you need not make yourself unhappy on that score. I had thought, as you well know, that he would have done something for Lily;--something, though not as much as he always intended to do for Bell. But you may be sure of this; that he had made up his mind as to what he would do. Nothing that you or I could have said would have changed him.\"\n\n\"Well; we won't say anything more about it,\" said Crosbie.\n\nThen they went on again in silence, and arrived at Guestwick in ample time for the train.\n\n\"Let me know as soon as you get to town,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Oh, of course. I'll write to you before that.\"\n\nAnd so they parted. As Dale turned and went, Crosbie felt that he liked him less than he had done before; and Bernard, also, as he was driving him, came to the conclusion that Crosbie would not be so good a fellow as a brother-in-law as he had been as a chance friend. \"He'll give us trouble, in some way; and I'm sorry that I brought him down.\" That was Dale's inward conviction in the matter.\n\nCrosbie's way from Guestwick lay, by railway, to Barchester, the cathedral city lying in the next county, from whence he purposed to have himself conveyed over to Courcy. There had, in truth, been no cause for his very early departure, as he was aware that all arrivals at country houses should take place at some hour not much previous to dinner. He had been determined to be so soon upon the road by a feeling that it would be well for him to get over those last hours. Thus he found himself in Barchester at eleven o'clock, with nothing on his hands to do; and, having nothing else to do, he went to church. There was a full service at the cathedral, and as the verger marshalled him up to one of the empty stalls, a little spare old man was beginning to chant the Litany. \"I did not mean to fall in for all this,\" said Crosbie, to himself, as he settled himself with his arms on the cushion. But the peculiar charm of that old man's voice soon attracted him;--a voice that, though tremulous, was yet strong; and he ceased to regret the saint whose honour and glory had occasioned the length of that day's special service.\n\n\"And who is the old gentleman who chanted the Litany?\" he asked the verger afterwards, as he allowed himself to be shown round the monuments of the cathedral.\n\n\"That's our precentor, sir; Mr. Harding. You must have heard of Mr. Harding.\" But Crosbie, with a full apology, confessed his ignorance.\n\n\"Well, sir; he's pretty well known too, tho' he is so shy like. He's father-in-law to our dean, sir; and father-in-law to Archdeacon Grantly also.\"\n\n\"His daughters have all gone into the profession, then?\"\n\n\"Why, yes; but Miss Eleanor--for I remember her before she was married at all,--when they lived at the hospital--\"\n\n\"At the hospital?\"\n\n\"Hiram's hospital, sir. He was warden, you know. You should go and see the hospital, sir, if you never was there before. Well, Miss Eleanor,--that was his youngest,--she married Mr. Bold as her first. But now she's the dean's lady.\"\n\n\"Oh; the dean's lady, is she?\"\n\n\"Yes, indeed. And what do you think, sir? Mr. Harding might have been dean himself if he'd liked. They did offer it to him.\"\n\n\"And he refused it?\"\n\n\"Indeed he did, sir.\"\n\n\"Nolo decanari. I never heard of that before. What made him so modest?\"\n\n\"Just that, sir; because he is modest. He's past his seventy now,--ever so much; but he's just as modest as a young girl. A deal more modest than some of them. To see him and his granddaughter together!\"\n\n\"And who is his granddaughter?\"\n\n\"Why, Lady Dumbello, as will be the Marchioness of Hartletop.\"\n\n\"I know Lady Dumbello,\" said Crosbie; not meaning, however, to boast to the verger of his noble acquaintance.\n\n\"Oh, do you, sir?\" said the man, unconsciously touching his hat at this sign of greatness in the stranger; though in truth he had no love for her ladyship. \"Perhaps you're going to be one of the party at Courcy Castle.\"\n\n\"Well, I believe I am.\"\n\n\"You'll find her ladyship there before you. She lunched with her aunt at the deanery as she went through, yesterday; finding it too much trouble to go out to her father's, at Plumstead. Her father is the archdeacon, you know. They do say,--but her ladyship is your friend!\"\n\n\"No friend at all; only a very slight acquaintance. She's quite as much above my line as she is above her father's.\"\n\n\"Well, she is above them all. They say she would hardly as much as speak to the old gentleman.\"\n\n\"What, her father?\"\n\n\"No, Mr. Harding; he that chanted the Litany just now. There he is, sir, coming out of the deanery.\"\n\nThey were now standing at the door leading out from one of the transepts, and Mr. Harding passed them as they were speaking together. He was a little, withered, shambling old man, with bent shoulders, dressed in knee-breeches and long black gaiters, which hung rather loosely about his poor old legs,--rubbing his hands one over the other as he went. And yet he walked quickly; not tottering as he walked, but with an uncertain, doubtful step. The verger, as Mr. Harding passed, put his hand to his head, and Crosbie also raised his hat. Whereupon Mr. Harding raised his, and bowed, and turned round as though he were about to speak. Crosbie felt that he had never seen a face on which traits of human kindness were more plainly written. But the old man did not speak. He turned his body half round, and then shambled back, as though ashamed of his intention, and passed on.\n\n\"He is of that sort that they make the angels of,\" said the verger. \"But they can't make many if they want them all as good as he is. I'm much obliged to you, sir.\" And he pocketed the half-crown which Crosbie gave him.\n\n\"So that's Lady Dumbello's grandfather,\" said Crosbie, to himself, as he walked slowly round the close towards the hospital, by the path which the verger had shown him. He had no great love for Lady Dumbello, who had dared to snub him,--even him. \"They may make an angel of the old gentleman,\" he continued to say; \"but they'll never succeed in that way with the granddaughter.\"\n\nHe sauntered slowly on over a little bridge; and at the gate of the hospital he again came upon Mr. Harding. \"I was going to venture in,\" said he, \"to look at the place. But perhaps I shall be intruding?\"\n\n\"No, no; by no means,\" said Mr. Harding. \"Pray come in. I cannot say that I am just at home here. I do not live here,--not now. But I know the ways of the place well, and can make you welcome. That's the warden's house. Perhaps we won't go in so early in the day, as the lady has a very large family. An excellent lady, and a dear friend of mine,--as is her husband.\"\n\n\"And he is warden, you say?\"\n\n\"Yes, warden of the hospital. You see the house, sir. Very pretty, isn't it? Very pretty. To my idea it's the prettiest built house I ever saw.\"\n\n\"I won't go quite so far as that,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"But you would if you'd lived there twelve years, as I did. I lived in that house twelve years, and I don't think there's so sweet a spot on the earth's surface. Did you ever see such turf as that?\"\n\n\"Very nice indeed,\" said Crosbie, who began to make a comparison with Mrs. Dale's turf at the Small House, and to determine that the Allington turf was better than that of the hospital.\n\n\"I had that turf laid down myself. There were borders there when I first came, with hollyhocks, and those sort of things. The turf was an improvement.\"\n\n\"There's no doubt of that, I should say.\"\n\n\"The turf was an improvement, certainly. And I planted those shrubs, too. There isn't such a Portugal laurel as that in the county.\"\n\n\"Were you warden here, sir?\" And Crosbie, as he asked the question, remembered that, in his very young days, he had heard of some newspaper quarrel which had taken place about Hiram's hospital at Barchester.\n\n\"Yes, sir. I was warden here for twelve years. Dear, dear, dear! If they had put any gentleman here that was not on friendly terms with me it would have made me very unhappy,--very. But, as it is, I go in and out just as I like; almost as much as I did before they-- But they didn't turn me out. There were reasons which made it best that I should resign.\"\n\n\"And you live at the deanery now, Mr. Harding?\"\n\n\"Yes; I live at the deanery now. But I am not dean, you know. My son-in-law, Dr. Arabin, is the dean. I have another daughter married in the neighbourhood, and can truly say that my lines have fallen to me in pleasant places.\"\n\nThen he took Crosbie in among the old men, into all of whose rooms he went. It was an almshouse for aged men of the city, and before Crosbie had left him Mr. Harding had explained all the circumstances of the hospital, and of the way in which he had left it. \"I didn't like going, you know; I thought it would break my heart. But I could not stay when they said such things as that;--I couldn't stay. And, what is more, I should have been wrong to stay. I see it all now. But when I went out under that arch, Mr. Crosbie, leaning on my daughter's arm, I thought that my heart would have broken.\" And the tears even now ran down the old man's cheeks as he spoke.\n\nIt was a long story, and it need not be repeated here. And there was no reason why it should have been told to Mr. Crosbie, other than this,--that Mr. Harding was a fond garrulous old man, who loved to indulge his mind in reminiscences of the past. But this was remarked by Crosbie; that, in telling his story, no word was said by Mr. Harding injurious to any one. And yet he had been injured,--injured very deeply. \"It was all for the best,\" he said at last; \"especially as the happiness has not been denied to me of making myself at home at the old place. I would take you into the house, which is very comfortable,--very; only it is not always convenient early in the day, where there's a large family.\" In hearing which Crosbie was again made to think of his own future home and limited income.\n\nHe had told the old clergyman who he was, and that he was on his way to Courcy. \"Where, as I understand, I shall meet a granddaughter of yours.\"\n\n\"Yes, yes; she is my grandchild. She and I have got into different walks of life now, so that I don't see much of her. They tell me that she does her duty well in that sphere of life to which it has pleased God to call her.\"\n\n\"That depends,\" thought Crosbie, \"on what the duties of a viscountess may be supposed to be.\" But he wished his new friend good-by, without saying anything further as to Lady Dumbello, and, at about six o'clock in the evening, had himself driven up under the portico of Courcy Castle.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII. COURCY CASTLE.\n\nCourcy Castle was very full. In the first place, there was a great gathering there of all the Courcy family. The earl was there,--and the countess, of course. At this period of the year Lady De Courcy was always at home; but the presence of the earl himself had heretofore been by no means so certain. He was a man who had been much given to royal visitings and attendances, to parties in the Highlands, to--no doubt necessary--prolongations of the London season, to sojournings at certain German watering-places, convenient, probably, in order that he might study the ways and ceremonies of German Courts,--and to various other absences from home, occasioned by a close pursuit of his own special aims in life; for the Earl De Courcy had been a great courtier. But of late gout, lumbago, and perhaps also some diminution in his powers of making himself generally agreeable, had reconciled him to domestic duties, and the earl spent much of his time at home. The countess, in former days, had been heard to complain of her lord's frequent absence. But it is hard to please some women,--and now she would not always be satisfied with his presence.\n\nAnd all the sons and daughters were there,--excepting Lord Porlock, the eldest, who never met his father. The earl and Lord Porlock were not on terms, and indeed hated each other as only such fathers and such sons can hate. The Honourable George De Courcy was there with his bride, he having lately performed a manifest duty, in having married a young woman with money. Very young she was not,--having reached some years of her life in advance of thirty; but then, neither was the Honourable George very young; and in this respect the two were not ill-sorted. The lady's money had not been very much,--perhaps thirty thousand pounds or so. But then the Honourable George's money had been absolutely none. Now he had an income on which he could live, and therefore his father and mother had forgiven him all his sins, and taken him again to their bosom. And the marriage was matter of great moment, for the elder scion of the house had not yet taken to himself a wife, and the De Courcy family might have to look to this union for an heir. The lady herself was not beautiful, or clever, or of imposing manners--nor was she of high birth. But neither was she ugly, nor unbearably stupid. Her manners were, at any rate, innocent; and as to her birth,--seeing that, from the first, she was not supposed to have had any,--no disappointment was felt. Her father had been a coal-merchant. She was always called Mrs. George, and the effort made respecting her by everybody in and about the family was to treat her as though she were a figure of a woman, a large well-dressed resemblance of a being, whom it was necessary for certain purposes that the De Courcys should carry in their train. Of the Honourable George we may further observe, that, having been a spendthrift all his life, he had now become strictly parsimonious. Having reached the discreet age of forty, he had at last learned that beggary was objectionable; and he, therefore, devoted every energy of his mind to saving shillings and pence wherever pence and shillings might be saved. When first this turn came upon him both his father and mother were delighted to observe it; but, although it had hardly yet lasted over twelve months, some evil results were beginning to appear. Though possessed of an income, he would take no steps towards possessing himself of a house. He hung by the paternal mansion, either in town or country; drank the paternal wines, rode the paternal horses, and had even contrived to obtain his wife's dresses from the maternal milliner. In the completion of which little last success, however, some slight family dissent had showed itself.\n\nThe Honourable John, the third son, was also at Courcy. He had as yet taken to himself no wife, and as he had not hitherto made himself conspicuously useful in any special walk of life his family were beginning to regard him as a burden. Having no income of his own to save, he had not copied his brother's virtue of parsimony; and, to tell the truth plainly, had made himself so generally troublesome to his father, that he had been on more than one occasion threatened with expulsion from the family roof. But it is not easy to expel a son. Human fledglings cannot be driven out of the nest like young birds. An Honourable John turned adrift into absolute poverty will make himself heard of in the world,--if in no other way, by his ugliness as he starves. A thorough-going ne'er-do-well in the upper classes has eminent advantages on his side in the battle which he fights against respectability. He can't be sent to Australia against his will. He can't be sent to the poor-house without the knowledge of all the world. He can't be kept out of tradesmen's shops; nor, without terrible scandal, can he be kept away from the paternal properties. The earl had threatened, and snarled, and shown his teeth; he was an angry man, and a man who could look very angry; with eyes which could almost become red, and a brow that wrinkled itself in perpendicular wrinkles, sometimes very terrible to behold. But he was an inconsistent man, and the Honourable John had learned to measure his father, and in an accurate balance.\n\nI have mentioned the sons first, because it is to be presumed that they were the elder, seeing that their names were mentioned before those of their sisters in all the peerages. But there were four daughters,--the Ladies Amelia, Rosina, Margaretta, and Alexandrina. They, we may say, were the flowers of the family, having so lived that they had created none of those family feuds which had been so frequent between their father and their brothers. They were discreet, high-bred women, thinking, perhaps, a little too much of their own position in the world, and somewhat apt to put a wrong value on those advantages which they possessed, and on those which they did not possess. The Lady Amelia was already married, having made a substantial if not a brilliant match with Mr. Mortimer Gazebee, a flourishing solicitor, belonging to a firm which had for many years acted as agents to the De Courcy property. Mortimer Gazebee was now member of Parliament for Barchester, partly through the influence of his father-in-law. That this should be so was a matter of great disgust to the Honourable George, who thought that the seat should have belonged to him. But as Mr. Gazebee had paid the very heavy expenses of the election out of his own pocket, and as George De Courcy certainly could not have paid them, the justice of his claim may be questionable. Lady Amelia Gazebee was now the happy mother of many babies, whom she was wont to carry with her on her visits to Courcy Castle, and had become an excellent partner to her husband. He would perhaps have liked it better if she had not spoken so frequently to him of her own high position as the daughter of an earl, or so frequently to others of her low position as the wife of an attorney. But, on the whole, they did very well together, and Mr. Gazebee had gotten from his marriage quite as much as he expected when he made it.\n\nThe Lady Rosina was very religious; and I do not know that she was conspicuous in any other way, unless it might be that she somewhat resembled her father in her temper. It was of the Lady Rosina that the servants were afraid, especially with reference to that so-called day of rest which, under her dominion, had become to many of them a day of restless torment. It had not always been so with the Lady Rosina; but her eyes had been opened by the wife of a great church dignitary in the neighbourhood, and she had undergone regeneration. How great may be the misery inflicted by an energetic, unmarried, healthy woman in that condition,--a woman with no husband, or children, or duties, to distract her from her work--I pray that my readers may never know.\n\nThe Lady Margaretta was her mother's favourite, and she was like her mother in all things,--except that her mother had been a beauty. The world called her proud, disdainful, and even insolent; but the world was not aware that in all that she did she was acting in accordance with a principle which had called for much self-abnegation. She had considered it her duty to be a De Courcy and an earl's daughter at all times; and consequently she had sacrificed to her idea of duty all popularity, adulation, and such admiration as would have been awarded to her as a well-dressed, tall, fashionable, and by no means stupid young woman. To be at all times in something higher than they who were manifestly below her in rank,--that was the effort that she was ever making. But she had been a good daughter, assisting her mother, as best she might, in all family troubles, and never repining at the cold, colourless, unlovely life which had been vouchsafed to her.\n\nAlexandrina was the beauty of the family, and was in truth the youngest. But even she was not very young, and was beginning to make her friends uneasy lest she, too, should let the precious season of hay-harvest run by without due use of her summer's sun. She had, perhaps, counted too much on her beauty, which had been beauty according to law rather than beauty according to taste, and had looked, probably, for too bounteous a harvest. That her forehead, and nose, and cheeks, and chin were well-formed, no man could deny. Her hair was soft and plentiful. Her teeth were good, and her eyes were long and oval. But the fault of her face was this,--that when you left her you could not remember it. After a first acquaintance you could meet her again and not know her. After many meetings you would fail to carry away with you any portrait of her features. But such as she had been at twenty, such was she now at thirty. Years had not robbed her face of its regularity, or ruffled the smoothness of her too even forehead. Rumour had declared that on more than one, or perhaps more than two occasions, Lady Alexandrina had been already induced to plight her troth in return for proffered love; but we all know that Rumour, when she takes to such topics, exaggerates the truth, and sets down much in malice. The lady was once engaged, the engagement lasting for two years, and the engagement had been broken off, owing to some money difficulties between the gentlemen of the families. Since that she had become somewhat querulous, and was supposed to be uneasy on that subject of her haymaking. Her glass and her maid assured her that her sun shone still as brightly as ever; but her spirit was becoming weary with waiting, and she dreaded lest she should become a terror to all, as was her sister Rosina, or an object of interest to none, as was Margaretta. It was from her especially that this message had been sent to our friend Crosbie; for, during the last spring in London, she and Crosbie had known each other well. Yes, my gentle readers; it is true, as your heart suggests to you. Under such circumstances Mr. Crosbie should not have gone to Courcy Castle.\n\nSuch was the family circle of the De Courcys. Among their present guests I need not enumerate many. First and foremost in all respects was Lady Dumbello, of whose parentage and position a few words were said in the last chapter. She was a lady still very young, having as yet been little more than two years married. But in those two years her triumphs had been many;--so many, that in the great world her standing already equalled that of her celebrated mother-in-law, the Marchioness of Hartletop, who, for twenty years, had owned no greater potentate than herself in the realms of fashion. But Lady Dumbello was every inch as great as she; and men said, and women also, that the daughter-in-law would soon be the greater.\n\n\"I'll be hanged if I can understand how she does it,\" a certain noble peer had once said to Crosbie, standing at the door of Sebright's, during the latter days of the last season. \"She never says anything to any one. She won't speak ten words a whole night through.\"\n\n\"I don't think she has an idea in her head,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Let me tell you that she must be a very clever woman,\" continued the noble peer. \"No fool could do as she does. Remember, she's only a parson's daughter; and as for beauty--\"\n\n\"I don't admire her for one,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"I don't want to run away with her, if you mean that,\" said the peer; \"but she is handsome, no doubt. I wonder whether Dumbello likes it.\"\n\nDumbello did like it. It satisfied his ambition to be led about as the senior lacquey in his wife's train. He believed himself to be a great man because the world fought for his wife's presence; and considered himself to be distinguished even among the eldest sons of marquises, by the greatness reflected from the parson's daughter whom he had married. He had now been brought to Courcy Castle, and felt himself proud of his situation because Lady Dumbello had made considerable difficulty in according this week to the Countess De Courcy.\n\nAnd Lady Julia De Guest was already there, the sister of the other old earl who lived in the next county. She had only arrived on the day before, but had been quick in spreading the news as to Crosbie's engagement. \"Engaged to one of the Dales, is he?\" said the countess, with a pretty little smile, which showed plainly that the matter was one of no interest to herself. \"Has she got any money?\"\n\n\"Not a shilling, I should think,\" said the Lady Julia.\n\n\"Pretty, I suppose?\" suggested the countess.\n\n\"Why, yes; she is pretty--and a nice girl. I don't know whether her mother and uncle were very wise in encouraging Mr. Crosbie. I don't hear that he has anything special to recommend him,--in the way of money I mean.\"\n\n\"I dare say it will come to nothing,\" said the countess, who liked to hear of girls being engaged and then losing their promised husbands. She did not know that she liked it, but she did; and already had pleasure in anticipating poor Lily's discomfiture. But not the less was she angry with Crosbie, feeling that he was making his way into her house under false pretences.\n\nAnd Alexandrina also was angry when Lady Julia repeated the same tidings in her hearing. \"I really don't think we care very much about it, Lady Julia,\" said she, with a little toss of her head. \"That's three times we've been told of Miss Dale's good fortune.\"\n\n\"The Dales are related to you, I think?\" said Margaretta.\n\n\"Not at all,\" said Lady Julia, bristling up. \"The lady whom Mr. Crosbie proposes to marry is in no way connected with us. Her cousin, who is the heir to the Allington property, is my nephew by his mother.\" And then the subject was dropped.\n\nCrosbie, on his arrival, was shown up into his room, told the hour of dinner, and left to his devices. He had been at the castle before, and knew the ways of the house. So he sat himself down to his table, and began a letter to Lily. But he had not proceeded far, not having as yet indeed made up his mind as to the form in which he would commence it, but was sitting idly with the pen in his hand, thinking of Lily, and thinking also how such houses as this in which he now found himself would be soon closed against him, when there came a rap at his door, and before he could answer the Honourable John entered the room.\n\n\"Well, old fellow,\" said the Honourable John, \"how are you?\"\n\nCrosbie had been intimate with John De Courcy, but never felt for him either friendship or liking. Crosbie did not like such men as John De Courcy; but nevertheless, they called each other old fellow, poked each other's ribs, and were very intimate.\n\n\"Heard you were here,\" continued the Honourable John; \"so I thought I would come up and look after you. Going to be married, ain't you?\"\n\n\"Not that I know of,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Come, we know better than that. The women have been talking about it for the last three days. I had her name quite pat yesterday, but I've forgot it now. Hasn't got a tanner; has she?\" And the Honourable John had now seated himself upon the table.\n\n\"You seem to know a great deal more about it than I do.\"\n\n\"It is that old woman from Guestwick who told us, then. The women will be at you at once, you'll find. If there's nothing in it, it's what I call a d---- shame. Why should they always pull a fellow to pieces in that way? They were going to marry me the other day!\"\n\n\"Were they indeed, though?\"\n\n\"To Harriet Twistleton. You know Harriet Twistleton? An uncommon fine girl, you know. But I wasn't going to be caught like that. I'm very fond of Harriet,--in my way, you know; but they don't catch an old bird like me with chaff.\"\n\n\"I condole with Miss Twistleton for what she has lost.\"\n\n\"I don't know about condoling. But upon my word that getting married is a very slow thing. Have you seen George's wife?\"\n\nCrosbie declared that he had not as yet had that pleasure.\n\n\"She's here now, you know. I wouldn't have taken her, not if she'd had ten times thirty thousand pounds. By Jove, no. But he likes it well enough. Would you believe it now?--he cares for nothing on earth except money. You never saw such a fellow. But I'll tell you what, his nose will be out of joint yet, for Porlock is going to marry. I heard it from Colepepper, who almost lives with Porlock. As soon as Porlock heard that she was in the family way he immediately made up his mind to cut him out.\"\n\n\"That was a great sign of brotherly love,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"I knew he'd do it,\" said John; \"and so I told George before he got himself spliced. But he would go on. If he'd remained as he was for four or five years longer there would have been no danger;--for Porlock, you know, is leading the deuce of a life. I shouldn't wonder if he didn't reform now, and take to singing psalms or something of that sort.\"\n\n\"There's no knowing what a man may come to in this world.\"\n\n\"By George, no. But I'll tell you what, they'll find no change in me. If I marry it will not be with the intention of giving up life. I say, old fellow, have you got a cigar here?\"\n\n\"What, to smoke up here, do you mean?\"\n\n\"Yes; why not? we're ever so far from the women.\"\n\n\"Not whilst I am occupier of this room. Besides, it's time to dress for dinner.\"\n\n\"Is it? So it is, by George! But I mean to have a smoke first, I can tell you. So it's all a lie about your being engaged; eh?\"\n\n\"As far as I know, it is,\" said Crosbie. And then his friend left him.\n\nWhat was he to do at once, now, this very day, as to his engagement? He had felt sure that the report of it would be carried to Courcy by Lady Julia De Guest, but he had not settled down upon any resolution as to what he would do in consequence. It had not occurred to him that he would immediately be charged with the offence, and called upon to plead guilty or not guilty. He had never for a moment meditated any plea of not guilty, but he was aware of an aversion on his part to declare himself as engaged to Lilian Dale. It seemed that by doing so he would cut himself off at once from all pleasure at such houses as Courcy Castle; and, as he argued to himself, why should he not enjoy the little remnant of his bachelor life? As to his denying his engagement to John De Courcy,--that was nothing. Any one would understand that he would be justified in concealing a fact concerning himself from such a one as he. The denial repeated from John's mouth would amount to nothing,--even among John's own sisters. But now it was necessary that Crosbie should make up his mind as to what he would say when questioned by the ladies of the house. If he were to deny the fact to them the denial would be very serious. And, indeed, was it possible that he should make such denial with Lady Julia opposite to him?\n\nMake such a denial! And was it the fact that he could wish to do so,--that he should think of such falsehood, and even meditate on the perpetration of such cowardice? He had held that young girl to his heart on that very morning. He had sworn to her, and had also sworn to himself, that she should have no reason for distrusting him. He had acknowledged most solemnly to himself that, whether for good or for ill, he was bound to her; and could it be that he was already calculating as to the practicability of disowning her? In doing so must he not have told himself that he was a villain? But in truth he made no such calculation. His object was to banish the subject, if it were possible to do so; to think of some answer by which he might create a doubt. It did not occur to him to tell the countess boldly that there was no truth whatever in the report, and that Miss Dale was nothing to him. But might he not skilfully laugh off the subject, even in the presence of Lady Julia? Men who were engaged did so usually, and why should not he? It was generally thought that solicitude for the lady's feelings should prevent a man from talking openly of his own engagement. Then he remembered the easy freedom with which his position had been discussed throughout the whole neighbourhood of Allington, and felt for the first time that the Dale family had been almost indelicate in their want of reticence. \"I suppose it was done to tie me the faster,\" he said to himself, as he pulled out the ends of his cravat. \"What a fool I was to come here, or indeed to go anywhere, after settling myself as I have done.\" And then he went down into the drawing-room.\n\nIt was almost a relief to him when he found that he was not charged with his sin at once. He himself had been so full of the subject that he had expected to be attacked at the moment of his entrance. He was, however, greeted without any allusion to the matter. The countess, in her own quiet way, shook hands with him as though she had seen him only the day before. The earl, who was seated in his arm-chair, asked some one, out loud, who the stranger was, and then, with two fingers put forth, muttered some apology for a welcome. But Crosbie was quite up to that kind of thing. \"How do, my lord?\" he said, turning his face away to some one else as he spoke; and then he took no further notice of the master of the house. \"Not know him, indeed!\" Crippled though he was by his matrimonial bond, Crosbie felt that, at any rate as yet, he was the earl's equal in social importance. After that, he found himself in the back part of the drawing-room, away from the elder people, standing with Lady Alexandrina, with Miss Gresham, a cousin of the De Courcys, and sundry other of the younger portion of the assembled community.\n\n\"So you have Lady Dumbello here?\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Oh, yes; the dear creature!\" said Lady Margaretta. \"It was so good of her to come, you know.\"\n\n\"She positively refused the Duchess of St. Bungay,\" said Alexandrina. \"I hope you perceive how good we've been to you in getting you to meet her. People have actually asked to come.\"\n\n\"I am grateful; but, in truth, my gratitude has more to do with Courcy Castle and its habitual inmates, than with Lady Dumbello. Is he here?\"\n\n\"Oh, yes! he's in the room somewhere. There he is, standing up by Lady Clandidlem. He always stands in that way before dinner. In the evening he sits down much after the same fashion.\"\n\nCrosbie had seen him on first entering the room, and had seen every individual in it. He knew better than to omit the duty of that scrutinizing glance; but it sounded well in his line not to have observed Lord Dumbello.\n\n\"And her ladyship is not down?\" said he.\n\n\"She is generally last,\" said Lady Margaretta.\n\n\"And yet she has always three women to dress her,\" said Alexandrina.\n\n\"But when finished, what a success it is!\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Indeed it is!\" said Margaretta, with energy. Then the door was opened, and Lady Dumbello entered the room.\n\nThere was immediately a commotion among them all. Even the gouty old lord shuffled up out of his chair, and tried, with a grin, to look sweet and pleasant. The countess came forward, looking very sweet and pleasant, making little complimentary speeches, to which the viscountess answered simply by a gracious smile. Lady Clandidlem, though she was very fat and heavy, left the viscount, and got up to join the group. Baron Potsneuf, a diplomatic German of great celebrity, crossed his hands upon his breast and made a low bow. The Honourable George, who had stood silent for the last quarter of an hour, suggested to her ladyship that she must have found the air rather cold; and the Ladies Margaretta and Alexandrina fluttered up with little complimentary speeches to their dear Lady Dumbello, hoping this and beseeching that, as though the \"Woman in White\" before them had been the dearest friend of their infancy.\n\nShe was a woman in white, being dressed in white silk, with white lace over it, and with no other jewels upon her person than diamonds. Very beautifully she was dressed; doing infinite credit, no doubt, to those three artists who had, between them, succeeded in turning her out of hand. And her face, also, was beautiful, with a certain cold, inexpressive beauty. She walked up the room very slowly, smiling here and smiling there; but still with very faint smiles, and took the place which her hostess indicated to her. One word she said to the countess and two to the earl. Beyond that she did not open her lips. All the homage paid to her she received as though it were clearly her due. She was not in the least embarrassed, nor did she show herself to be in the slightest degree ashamed of her own silence. She did not look like a fool, nor was she even taken for a fool; but she contributed nothing to society but her cold, hard beauty, her gait, and her dress. We may say that she contributed enough, for society acknowledged itself to be deeply indebted to her.\n\nThe only person in the room who did not move at Lady Dumbello's entrance was her husband. But he remained unmoved from no want of enthusiasm. A spark of pleasure actually beamed in his eye as he saw the triumphant entrance of his wife. He felt that he had made a match that was becoming to him as a great nobleman, and that the world was acknowledging that he had done his duty. And yet Lady Dumbello had been simply the daughter of a country parson, of a clergyman who had reached no higher rank than that of an archdeacon. \"How wonderfully well that woman has educated her,\" the countess said that evening in her dressing-room, to Margaretta. The woman alluded to was Mrs. Grantly, the wife of the parson and mother of Lady Dumbello.\n\nThe old earl was very cross because destiny and the table of precedence required him to take out Lady Clandidlem to dinner. He almost insulted her, as she kindly endeavoured to assist him in his infirm step rather than to lean upon him.\n\n\"Ugh!\" he said, \"it's a bad arrangement that makes two old people like you and me be sent out together to help each other.\"\n\n\"Speak for yourself,\" said her ladyship, with a laugh. \"I, at any rate, can get about without any assistance,\"--which, indeed, was true enough.\n\n\"It's well for you!\" growled the earl, as he got himself into his seat.\n\nAnd after that he endeavoured to solace his pain by a flirtation with Lady Dumbello on his left. The earl's smiles and the earl's teeth, when he whispered naughty little nothings to pretty young women, were phenomena at which men might marvel. Whatever those naughty nothings were on the present occasion, Lady Dumbello took them all with placidity, smiling graciously, but speaking hardly more than monosyllables.\n\nLady Alexandrina fell to Crosbie's lot, and he felt gratified that it was so. It might be necessary for him, as a married man, to give up such acquaintances as the De Courcys, but he should like, if possible, to maintain a friendship with Lady Alexandrina. What a friend Lady Alexandrina would be for Lily, if any such friendship were only possible! What an advantage would such an alliance confer upon that dear little girl;--for, after all, though the dear little girl's attractions were very great, he could not but admit to himself that she wanted a something,--a way of holding herself and of speaking, which some people call style. Lily might certainly learn a great deal from Lady Alexandrina; and it was this conviction, no doubt, which made him so sedulous in pleasing that lady on the present occasion.\n\nAnd she, as it seemed, was well inclined to be pleased. She said no word to him during dinner about Lily; and yet she spoke about the Dales, and about Allington, showing that she knew in what quarters he had been staying, and then she alluded to their last parties in London,--those occasions on which, as Crosbie now remembered, the intercourse between them had almost been tender. It was manifest to him that at any rate she did not wish to quarrel with him. It was manifest, also, that she had some little hesitation in speaking to him about his engagement. He did not for a moment doubt that she was aware of it. And in this way matters went on between them till the ladies left the room.\n\n\"So you're going to be married, too,\" said the Honourable George, by whose side Crosbie found himself seated when the ladies were gone. Crosbie was employing himself upon a walnut, and did not find it necessary to make any answer.\n\n\"It's the best thing a fellow can do,\" continued George; \"that is, if he has been careful to look to the main chance,--if he hasn't been caught napping, you know. It doesn't do for a man to go hanging on by nothing till he finds himself an old man.\"\n\n\"You've feathered your own nest, at any rate.\"\n\n\"Yes; I've got something in the scramble, and I mean to keep it. Where will John be when the governor goes off the hooks? Porlock wouldn't give him a bit of bread and cheese and a glass of beer to save his life;--that is to say, not if he wanted it.\"\n\n\"I'm told your elder brother is going to be married.\"\n\n\"You've heard that from John. He's spreading that about everywhere to take a rise out of me. I don't believe a word of it. Porlock never was a marrying man;--and, what's more, from all I hear, I don't think he'll live long.\"\n\nIn this way Crosbie escaped from his own difficulty; and when he rose from the dinner-table had not as yet been driven to confess anything to his own discredit.\n\nBut the evening was not yet over. When he returned to the drawing-room he endeavoured to avoid any conversation with the countess herself, believing that the attack would more probably come from her than from her daughter. He, therefore, got into conversation first with one and then with another of the girls, till at last he found himself again alone with Alexandrina.\n\n\"Mr. Crosbie,\" she said, in a low voice, as they were standing together over one of the distant tables, with their backs to the rest of the company, \"I want you to tell me something about Miss Lilian Dale.\"\n\n\"About Miss Lilian Dale!\" he said, repeating her words.\n\n\"Is she very pretty?\"\n\n\"Yes; she certainly is pretty.\"\n\n\"And very nice, and attractive, and clever,--and all that is delightful? Is she perfect?\"\n\n\"She is very attractive,\" said he; \"but I don't think she's perfect.\"\n\n\"And what are her faults?\"\n\n\"That question is hardly fair, is it? Suppose any one were to ask me what were your faults, do you think I should answer the question?\"\n\n\"I am quite sure you would, and make a very long list of them, too. But as to Miss Dale, you ought to think her perfect. If a gentleman were engaged to me, I should expect him to swear before all the world that I was the very pink of perfection.\"\n\n\"But supposing the gentleman were not engaged to you?\"\n\n\"That would be a different thing.\"\n\n\"I am not engaged to you,\" said Crosbie. \"Such happiness and such honour are, I fear, very far beyond my reach. But, nevertheless, I am prepared to testify as to your perfection anywhere.\"\n\n\"And what would Miss Dale say?\"\n\n\"Allow me to assure you that such opinions as I may choose to express of my friends will be my own opinions, and not depend on those of any one else.\"\n\n\"And you think, then, that you are not bound to be enslaved as yet? How many more months of such freedom are you to enjoy?\"\n\nCrosbie remained silent for a minute before he answered, and then he spoke in a serious voice. \"Lady Alexandrina,\" said he, \"I would beg from you a great favour.\"\n\n\"What is the favour, Mr. Crosbie?\"\n\n\"I am quite in earnest. Will you be good enough, kind enough, enough my friend, not to connect my name again with that of Miss Dale while I am here?\"\n\n\"Has there been a quarrel?\"\n\n\"No; there has been no quarrel. I cannot explain to you now why I make this request; but to you I will explain it before I go.\"\n\n\"Explain it to me!\"\n\n\"I have regarded you as more than an acquaintance,--as a friend. In days now past there were moments when I was almost rash enough to hope that I might have said even more than that. I confess that I had no warrant for such hopes, but I believe that I may still look on you as a friend?\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, certainly,\" said Alexandrina, in a very low voice, and with a certain amount of tenderness in her tone. \"I have always regarded you as a friend.\"\n\n\"And therefore I venture to make the request. The subject is not one on which I can speak openly, without regret, at the present moment. But to you, at least, I promise that I will explain it all before I leave Courcy.\"\n\nHe at any rate succeeded in mystifying Lady Alexandrina. \"I don't believe he is engaged a bit,\" she said to Lady Amelia Gazebee that night.\n\n\"Nonsense, my dear. Lady Julia wouldn't speak of it in that certain way if she didn't know. Of course he doesn't wish to have it talked about.\"\n\n\"If ever he has been engaged to her, he has broken it off again,\" said Lady Alexandrina.\n\n\"I dare say he will, my dear, if you give him encouragement,\" said the married sister, with great sisterly good-nature.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII. LILY DALE'S FIRST LOVE-LETTER.\n\nCrosbie was rather proud of himself when he went to bed. He had succeeded in baffling the charge made against him, without saying anything as to which his conscience need condemn him. So, at least, he then told himself. The impression left by what he had said would be that there had been some question of an engagement between him and Lilian Dale, but that nothing at this moment was absolutely fixed. But in the morning his conscience was not quite so clear. What would Lily think and say if she knew it all? Could he dare to tell her, or to tell any one the real state of his mind?\n\nAs he lay in bed, knowing that an hour remained to him before he need encounter the perils of his tub, he felt that he hated Courcy Castle and its inmates. Who was there, among them all, that was comparable to Mrs. Dale and her daughters? He detested both George and John. He loathed the earl. As to the countess herself, he was perfectly indifferent, regarding her as a woman whom it was well to know, but as one only to be known as the mistress of Courcy Castle and a house in London. As to the daughters, he had ridiculed them all from time to time--even Alexandrina, whom he now professed to love. Perhaps in some sort of way he had a weak fondness for her;--but it was a fondness that had never touched his heart. He could measure the whole thing at its worth,--Courcy Castle with its privileges, Lady Dumbello, Lady Clandidlem, and the whole of it. He knew that he had been happier on that lawn at Allington, and more contented with himself, than ever he had been even under Lady Hartletop's splendid roof in Shropshire. Lady Dumbello was satisfied with these things, even in the inmost recesses of her soul; but he was not a male Lady Dumbello. He knew that there was something better, and that that something was within his reach.\n\nBut, nevertheless, the air of Courcy was too much for him. In arguing the matter with himself he regarded himself as one infected with a leprosy from which there could be no recovery, and who should, therefore, make his whole life suitable to the circumstances of that leprosy. It was of no use for him to tell himself that the Small House at Allington was better than Courcy Castle. Satan knew that heaven was better than hell; but he found himself to be fitter for the latter place. Crosbie ridiculed Lady Dumbello, even there among her friends, with all the cutting words that his wit could find; but, nevertheless, the privilege of staying in the same house with her was dear to him. It was the line of life into which he had fallen, and he confessed inwardly that the struggle to extricate himself would be too much for him. All that had troubled him while he was yet at Allington, but it overwhelmed him almost with dismay beneath the hangings of Courcy Castle.\n\nHad he not better run from the place at once? He had almost acknowledged to himself that he repented his engagement with Lilian Dale, but he still was resolved that he would fulfil it. He was bound in honour to marry \"that little girl,\" and he looked sternly up at the drapery over his head, as he assured himself that he was a man of honour. Yes; he would sacrifice himself. As he had been induced to pledge his word, he would not go back from it. He was too much of a man for that!\n\nBut had he not been wrong to refuse the result of Lily's wisdom when she told him in the field that it would be better for them to part? He did not tell himself that he had refused her offer merely because he had not the courage to accept it on the spur of the moment. No. \"He had been too good to the poor girl to take her at her word.\" It was thus he argued on the matter within his own breast. He had been too true to her; and now the effect would be that they would both be unhappy for life! He could not live in content with a family upon a small income. He was well aware of that. No one could be harder upon him in that matter than was he himself. But it was too late now to remedy the ill effects of an early education.\n\nIt was thus that he debated the matter as he lay in bed,--contradicting one argument by another over and over again; but still in all of them teaching himself to think that this engagement of his was a misfortune. Poor Lily! Her last words to him had conveyed an assurance that she would never distrust him. And she also, as she lay wakeful in her bed on this the first morning of his absence, thought much of their mutual vows. How true she would be to them! How she would be his wife with all her heart and spirit! It was not only that she would love him;--but in her love she would serve him to her utmost; serve him as regarded this world, and if possible as regarded the next.\n\n\"Bell,\" she said, \"I wish you were going to be married too.\"\n\n\"Thank'ye, dear,\" said Bell. \"Perhaps I shall some day.\"\n\n\"Ah; but I'm not joking. It seems such a serious thing. And I can't expect you to talk to me about it now as you would if you were in the same position yourself. Do you think I shall make him happy?\"\n\n\"Yes, I do, certainly.\"\n\n\"Happier than he would be with any one else that he might meet? I dare not think that. I think I could give him up to-morrow, if I could see any one that would suit him better.\" What would Lily have said had she been made acquainted with all the fascinations of Lady Alexandrina De Courcy?\n\nThe countess was very civil to him, saying nothing about his engagement, but still talking to him a good deal about his sojourn at Allington. Crosbie was a pleasant man for ladies in a large house. Though a sportsman, he was not so keen a sportsman as to be always out with the gamekeepers. Though a politician, he did not sacrifice his mornings to the perusal of blue-books or the preparation of party tactics. Though a reading man, he did not devote himself to study. Though a horseman, he was not often to be found in the stables. He could supply conversation when it was wanted, and could take himself out of the way when his presence among the women was not needed. Between breakfast and lunch on the day following his arrival he talked a good deal to the countess, and made himself very agreeable. She continued to ridicule him gently for his prolonged stay among so primitive and rural a tribe of people as the Dales, and he bore her little sarcasm with the utmost good-humour.\n\n\"Six weeks at Allington without a move! Why, Mr. Crosbie, you must have felt yourself to be growing there.\"\n\n\"So I did--like an ancient tree. Indeed, I was so rooted that I could hardly get away.\"\n\n\"Was the house full of people all the time?\"\n\n\"There was nobody there but Bernard Dale, Lady Julia's nephew.\"\n\n\"Quite a case of Damon and Pythias. Fancy your going down to the shades of Allington to enjoy the uninterrupted pleasures of friendship for six weeks.\"\n\n\"Friendship and the partridges.\"\n\n\"There was nothing else, then?\"\n\n\"Indeed there was. There was a widow with two very nice daughters, living, not exactly in the same house, but on the same grounds.\"\n\n\"Oh, indeed. That makes such a difference; doesn't it? You are not a man to bear much privation on the score of partridges, nor a great deal, I imagine, for friendship. But when you talk of pretty girls--\"\n\n\"It makes a difference, doesn't it?\"\n\n\"A very great difference. I think I have heard of that Mrs. Dale before. And so her girls are nice?\"\n\n\"Very nice indeed.\"\n\n\"Play croquet, I suppose, and eat syllabub on the lawn? But, really, didn't you get very tired of it?\"\n\n\"Oh dear, no. I was happy as the day was long.\"\n\n\"Going about with a crook, I suppose?\"\n\n\"Not exactly a live crook; but doing all that kind of thing. I learned a great deal about pigs.\"\n\n\"Under the guidance of Miss Dale?\"\n\n\"Yes; under the guidance of Miss Dale.\"\n\n\"I'm sure one is very much obliged to you for tearing yourself away from such charms, and coming to such unromantic people as we are. But I fancy men always do that sort of thing once or twice in their lives,--and then they talk of their souvenirs. I suppose it won't go beyond a souvenir with you.\"\n\nThis was a direct question, but still admitted of a fencing answer. \"It has, at any rate, given me one,\" said he, \"which will last me my life!\"\n\nThe countess was quite contented. That Lady Julia's statement was altogether true she had never for a moment doubted. That Crosbie should become engaged to a young lady in the country, whereas he had shown signs of being in love with her daughter in London, was not at all wonderful. Nor, in her eyes, did such practice amount to any great sin. Men did so daily, and girls were prepared for their so doing. A man in her eyes was not to be regarded as safe from attack because he was engaged. Let the young lady who took upon herself to own him have an eye to that. When she looked back on the past careers of her own flock, she had to reckon more than one such disappointment for her own daughters. Others besides Alexandrina had been so treated. Lady De Courcy had had her grand hopes respecting her girls, and after them moderate hopes, and again after them bitter disappointments. Only one had been married, and she was married to an attorney. It was not to be supposed that she would have any very high-toned feelings as to Lily's rights in this matter.\n\nSuch a man as Crosbie was certainly no great match for an earl's daughter. Such a marriage, indeed, would, one may say, be but a poor triumph. When the countess, during the last season in town, had observed how matters were going with Alexandrina, she had cautioned her child, taking her to task for her imprudence. But the child had been at this work for fourteen years, and was weary of it. Her sisters had been at the work longer, and had almost given it up in despair. Alexandrina did not tell her parent that her heart was now beyond her control, and that she had devoted herself to Crosbie for ever; but she pouted, saying that she knew very well what she was about, scolding her mother in return, and making Lady De Courcy perceive that the struggle was becoming very weary. And then there were other considerations. Mr. Crosbie had not much certainly in his own possession, but he was a man out of whom something might be made by family influence and his own standing. He was not a hopeless, ponderous man, whom no leaven could raise. He was one of whose position in society the countess and her daughters need not be ashamed. Lady De Courcy had given no expressed consent to the arrangement, but it had come to be understood between her and her daughter that the scheme was to be entertained as admissible.\n\nThen came these tidings of the little girl down at Allington. She felt no anger against Crosbie. To be angry on such a subject would be futile, foolish, and almost indecorous. It was a part of the game which was as natural to her as fielding is to a cricketer. One cannot have it all winnings at any game. Whether Crosbie should eventually become her own son-in-law or not it came to her naturally, as a part of her duty in life, to bowl down the stumps of that young lady at Allington. If Miss Dale knew the game well and could protect her own wicket, let her do so.\n\nShe had no doubt as to Crosbie's engagement with Lilian Dale, but she had as little as to his being ashamed of that engagement. Had he really cared for Miss Dale he would not have left her to come to Courcy Castle. Had he been really resolved to marry her, he would not have warded all questions respecting his engagement with fictitious answers. He had amused himself with Lily Dale, and it was to be hoped that the young lady had not thought very seriously about it. That was the most charitable light in which Lady De Courcy was disposed to regard the question.\n\nIt behoved Crosbie to write to Lily Dale before dinner. He had promised to do so immediately on his arrival, and he was aware that he would be regarded as being already one day beyond his promise. Lily had told him that she would live upon his letters, and it was absolutely necessary that he should furnish her with her first meal. So he betook himself to his room in sufficient time before dinner, and got out his pen, ink, and paper.\n\nHe got out his pen, ink, and paper, and then he found that his difficulties were beginning. I beg that it may be understood that Crosbie was not altogether a villain. He could not sit down and write a letter as coming from his heart, of which as he wrote it he knew the words to be false. He was an ungenerous, worldly, inconstant man, very prone to think well of himself, and to give himself credit for virtues which he did not possess; but he could not be false with premeditated cruelty to a woman he had sworn to love. He could not write an affectionate, warm-hearted letter to Lily, without bringing himself, at any rate for the time, to feel towards her in an affectionate, warm-hearted way. Therefore he now sat himself to work, while his pen yet remained dry in his hand, to remodel his thoughts, which had been turned against Lily and Allington by the craft of Lady De Courcy. It takes some time before a man can do this. He has to struggle with himself in a very uncomfortable way, making efforts which are often unsuccessful. It is sometimes easier to lift a couple of hundredweights than to raise a few thoughts in one's mind which at other moments will come galloping in without a whistle.\n\nHe had just written the date of his letter when a little tap came at his door, and it was opened.\n\n\"I say, Crosbie,\" said the Honourable John, \"didn't you say something yesterday about a cigar before dinner?\"\n\n\"Not a word,\" said Crosbie, in rather an angry tone.\n\n\"Then it must have been me,\" said John. \"But bring your case with you, and come down to the harness-room, if you won't smoke here. I've had a regular little snuggery fitted up there; and we can go in and see the fellows making up the horses.\"\n\nCrosbie wished the Honourable John at the mischief.\n\n\"I have letters to write,\" said he. \"Besides, I never smoke before dinner.\"\n\n\"That's nonsense. I've smoked hundreds of cigars with you before dinner. Are you going to turn curmudgeon, too, like George and the rest of them? I don't know what's coming to the world! I suppose the fact is, that little girl at Allington won't let you smoke.\"\n\n\"The little girl at Allington--\" began Crosbie; and then he reflected that it would not be well for him to say anything to his present companion about that little girl. \"I'll tell you what it is,\" said he. \"I really have got letters to write which must go by this post. There's my cigar-case on the dressing-table.\"\n\n\"I hope it will be long before I'm brought to such a state,\" said John, taking up the cigars in his hand.\n\n\"Let me have the case back,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"A present from the little girl, I suppose?\" said John. \"All right, old fellow! you shall have it.\"\n\n\"There would be a nice brother-in-law for a man,\" said Crosbie to himself, as the door closed behind the retreating scion of the De Courcy family. And then, again, he took up his pen. The letter must be written, and therefore he threw himself upon the table, resolved that the words should come and the paper be filled.\n\nCourcy Castle, October, 186--.\n\nDEAREST LILY,--This is the first letter I ever wrote to you, except those little notes when I sent you my compliments discreetly,--and it sounds so odd. You will think that this does not come as soon as it should; but the truth is that after all I only got in here just before dinner yesterday. I stayed ever so long in Barchester, and came across such a queer character. For you must know I went to church, and afterwards fraternized with the clergyman who did the service; such a gentle old soul,--and, singularly enough, he is the grandfather of Lady Dumbello, who is staying here. I wonder what you'd think of Lady Dumbello, or how you'd like to be shut up in the same house with her for a week?\n\nBut with reference to my staying at Barchester, I must tell you the truth now, though I was a gross impostor the day that I went away. I wanted to avoid a parting on that last morning, and therefore I started much sooner than I need have done. I know you will be very angry with me; but open confession is good for the soul. You frustrated all my little plan by your early rising; and as I saw you standing on the terrace, looking after us as we went, I acknowledged that you had been right, and that I was wrong. When the time came, I was very glad to have you with me at the last moment.\n\nMy own dearest Lily, you cannot think how different this place is from the two houses at Allington, or how much I prefer the sort of life which belongs to the latter. I know that I have been what the world calls worldly, but you will have to cure me of that. I have questioned myself very much since I left you, and I do not think that I am quite beyond the reach of a cure. At any rate, I will put myself trustingly into the doctor's hands. I know it is hard for a man to change his habits; but I can with truth say this for myself, that I was happy at Allington, enjoying every hour of the day, and that here I am ennuyé by everybody and nearly by everything. One of the girls of the house I do like; but as to other people, I can hardly find a companion among them, let alone a friend. However, it would not have done for me to have broken away from all such alliances too suddenly.\n\nWhen I get up to London--and now I really am anxious to get there--I can write to you more at my ease, and more freely than I do here. I know that I am hardly myself among these people,--or rather, I am hardly myself as you know me, and as I hope you always will know me. But, nevertheless, I am not so overcome by the miasma but what I can tell you how truly I love you. Even though my spirit should be here, which it is not, my heart would be on the Allington lawns. That dear lawn and that dear bridge!\n\nGive my kind love to Bell and your mother. I feel already that I might almost say my mother. And Lily, my darling, write to me at once. I expect your letters to me to be longer, and better, and brighter than mine to you. But I will endeavour to make mine nicer when I get back to town.\n\nGod bless you. Yours, with all my heart,\n\nAs he had waxed warm with his writing he had forced himself to be affectionate, and, as he flattered himself, frank and candid. Nevertheless, he was partly conscious that he was preparing for himself a mode of escape in those allusions of his to his own worldliness; if escape should ultimately be necessary. \"I have tried,\" he would then say; \"I have struggled honestly, with my best efforts for success; but I am not good enough for such success.\" I do not intend to say that he wrote with a premeditated intention of thus using his words; but as he wrote them he could not keep himself from reflecting that they might be used in that way.\n\nHe read his letter over, felt satisfied with it, and resolved that he might now free his mind from that consideration for the next forty-eight hours. Whatever might be his sins he had done his duty by Lily! And with this comfortable reflection he deposited his letter in the Courcy Castle letter-box.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX. THE SQUIRE MAKES A VISIT TO THE SMALL HOUSE.\n\nMrs. Dale acknowledged to herself that she had not much ground for hoping that she should ever find in Crosbie's house much personal happiness for her future life. She did not dislike Mr. Crosbie, nor in any great degree mistrust him; but she had seen enough of him to make her certain that Lily's future home in London could not be a home for her. He was worldly, or, at least, a man of the world. He would be anxious to make the most of his income, and his life would be one long struggle, not perhaps for money, but for those things which money only can give. There are men to whom eight hundred a year is great wealth, and houses to which it brings all the comforts that life requires. But Crosbie was not such a man, nor would his house be such a house. Mrs. Dale hoped that Lily would be happy with him, and satisfied with his modes of life, and she strove to believe that such would be the case; but as regarded herself she was forced to confess that in such a marriage her child would be much divided from her. That pleasant abode to which she had long looked forward that she might have a welcome there in coming years should be among fields and trees, not in some narrow London street. Lily must now become a city lady; but Bell would still be left to her, and it might still be hoped that Bell would find for herself some country home.\n\nSince the day on which Lily had first told her mother of her engagement, Mrs. Dale had found herself talking much more fully and more frequently with Bell than with her younger daughter. As long as Crosbie was at Allington this was natural enough. He and Lily were of course together, while Bell remained with her mother. But the same state of things continued even after Crosbie was gone. It was not that there was any coolness or want of affection between the mother and daughter, but that Lily's heart was full of her lover, and that Mrs. Dale, though she had given her cordial consent to the marriage, felt that she had but few points of sympathy with her future son-in-law. She had never said, even to herself, that she disliked him; nay, she had sometimes declared to herself that she was fond of him. But, in truth, he was not a man after her own heart. He was not one who could ever be to her as her own son and her own child.\n\nBut she and Bell would pass hours together talking of Lily's prospects. \"It seems so strange to me,\" said Mrs. Dale, \"that she of all girls should have been fancied by such a man as Mr. Crosbie, or that she should have liked him. I cannot imagine Lily living in London.\"\n\n\"If he is good and affectionate to her she will be happy wherever he is,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I hope so;--I'm sure I hope so. But it seems as though she will be so far separated from us. It is not the distance, but the manner of life which makes the separation. I hope you'll never be taken so far from me.\"\n\n\"I don't think I shall allow myself to be taken up to London,\" said Bell, laughing. \"But one can never tell. If I do you must follow us, mamma.\"\n\n\"I do not want another Mr. Crosbie for you, dear.\"\n\n\"But perhaps I may want one for myself. You need not tremble quite yet, however. Apollos do not come this road every day.\"\n\n\"Poor Lily! Do you remember when she first called him Apollo? I do, well. I remember his coming here the day after Bernard brought him down, and how you were playing on the lawn, while I was in the other garden. I little thought then what it would come to.\"\n\n\"But, mamma, you don't regret it?\"\n\n\"Not if it's to make her happy. If she can be happy with him, of course I shall not regret it; not though he were to take her to the world's end away from us. What else have I to look for but that she and you should both be happy?\"\n\n\"Men in London are happy with their wives as well as men in the country.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes; of all women I should be the first to acknowledge that.\"\n\n\"And as to Adolphus himself, I do not know why we should distrust him.\"\n\n\"No, my dear; there is no reason. If I did distrust him, I should not have given so ready an assent to the marriage. But, nevertheless--\"\n\n\"The truth is, you don't like him, mamma.\"\n\n\"Not so cordially as I hope I may like any man whom you may choose for your husband.\"\n\nAnd Lily, though she said nothing on the subject to Mrs. Dale, felt that her mother was in some degree estranged from her. Crosbie's name was frequently mentioned between them, but in the tone of Mrs. Dale's voice, and in her manner when she spoke of him, there was lacking that enthusiasm and heartiness which real sympathy would have produced. Lily did not analyse her own feelings, or closely make inquiry as to those of her mother, but she perceived that it was not all as she would have wished it to have been. \"I know mamma does not love him,\" she said to Bell on the evening of the day on which she received Crosbie's first letter.\n\n\"Not as you do, Lily; but she does love him.\"\n\n\"Not as I do! To say that is nonsense, Bell; of course she does not love him as I do. But the truth is she does not love him at all. Do you think I cannot see it?\"\n\n\"I'm afraid that you see too much.\"\n\n\"She never says a word against him; but if she really liked him she would sometimes say a word in his favour. I do not think she would ever mention his name unless you or I spoke of him before her. If she did not approve of him, why did she not say so sooner?\"\n\n\"That's hardly fair upon mamma,\" said Bell, with some earnestness. \"She does not disapprove of him, and she never did. You know mamma well enough to be sure that she would not interfere with us in such a matter without very strong reason. As regards Mr. Crosbie, she gave her consent without a moment's hesitation.\"\n\n\"Yes, she did.\"\n\n\"How can you say, then, that she disapproves of him?\"\n\n\"I didn't mean to find fault with mamma. Perhaps it will come all right.\"\n\n\"It will come all right.\" But Bell, though she made this very satisfactory promise, was as well aware as either of the others that the family would be divided when Crosbie should have married Lily and taken her off to London.\n\nOn the following morning Mrs. Dale and Bell were sitting together. Lily was above in her own room, either writing to her lover, or reading his letter, or thinking of him, or working for him. In some way she was employed on his behalf, and with this object she was alone. It was now the middle of October, and the fire was lit in Mrs. Dale's drawing-room. The window which opened upon the lawn was closed, the heavy curtains had been put back in their places, and it had been acknowledged as an unwelcome fact that the last of the summer was over. This was always a sorrow to Mrs. Dale; but it is one of those sorrows which hardly admit of open expression.\n\n\"Bell,\" she said, looking up suddenly; \"there's your uncle at the window. Let him in.\" For now, since the putting up of the curtains, the window had been bolted as well as closed. So Bell got up, and opened a passage for the squire's entrance. It was not often that he came down in this way, and when he did do so it was generally for some purpose which had been expressed before.\n\n\"What! fires already?\" said he. \"I never have fires at the other house in the morning till the first of November. I like to see a spark in the grate after dinner.\"\n\n\"I like a fire when I'm cold,\" said Mrs. Dale. But this was a subject on which the squire and his sister-in-law had differed before, and as Mr. Dale had some business in hand, he did not now choose to waste his energy in supporting his own views on the question of fires.\n\n\"Bell, my dear,\" said he, \"I want to speak to your mother for a minute or two on a matter of business. You wouldn't mind leaving us for a little while, would you?\" Whereupon Bell collected up her work and went upstairs to her sister. \"Uncle Christopher is below with mamma,\" said she, \"talking about business. I suppose it is something to do with your marriage.\" But Bell was wrong. The squire's visit had no reference to Lily's marriage.\n\nMrs. Dale did not move or speak a word when Bell was gone, though it was evident that the squire paused in order that she might ask some question of him. \"Mary,\" said he, at last, \"I'll tell you what it is that I have come to say to you.\" Whereupon she put the piece of needlework which was in her hands down upon the work-basket before her, and settled herself to listen to him.\n\n\"I wish to speak to you about Bell.\"\n\n\"About Bell?\" said Mrs. Dale, as though much surprised that he should have anything to say to her respecting her eldest daughter.\n\n\"Yes, about Bell. Here's Lily going to be married, and it will be well that Bell should be married too.\"\n\n\"I don't see that at all,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"I am by no means in a hurry to be rid of her.\"\n\n\"No, I dare say not. But, of course, you only regard her welfare, and I can truly say that I do the same. There would be no necessity for hurry as to a marriage for her under ordinary circumstances, but there may be circumstances to make such a thing desirable, and I think that there are.\" It was evident from the squire's tone and manner that he was very much in earnest; but it was also evident that he found some difficulty in opening out the budget with which he had prepared himself. He hesitated a little in his voice, and seemed to be almost nervous. Mrs. Dale, with some little spice of ill-nature, altogether abstained from assisting him. She was jealous of interference from him about her girls, and though she was of course bound to listen to him, she did so with a prejudice against and almost with a resolve to oppose anything that he might say. When he had finished his little speech about circumstances, the squire paused again; but Mrs. Dale still sat silent, with her eyes fixed upon his face.\n\n\"I love your children very dearly,\" said he, \"though I believe you hardly give me credit for doing so.\"\n\n\"I am sure you do,\" said Mrs. Dale, \"and they are both well aware of it.\"\n\n\"And I am very anxious that they should be comfortably established in life. I have no children of my own, and those of my two brothers are everything to me.\"\n\nMrs. Dale had always considered it as a matter of course that Bernard should be the squire's heir, and had never felt that her daughters had any claim on that score. It was a well-understood thing in the family that the senior male Dale should have all the Dale property and all the Dale money. She fully recognized even the propriety of such an arrangement. But it seemed to her that the squire was almost guilty of hypocrisy in naming his nephew and his two nieces together, as though they were the joint heirs of his love. Bernard was his adopted son, and no one had begrudged to the uncle the right of making such adoption. Bernard was everything to him, and as being his heir was bound to obey him in many things. But her daughters were no more to him than any nieces might be to any uncle. He had nothing to do with their disposal in marriage; and the mother's spirit was already up in arms and prepared to do battle for her own independence, and for that of her children. \"If Bernard would marry well,\" said she, \"I have no doubt it would be a comfort to you,\"--meaning to imply thereby that the squire had no right to trouble himself about any other marriage.\n\n\"That's just it,\" said the squire. \"It would be a great comfort to me. And if he and Bell could make up their minds together, it would, I should think, be a great comfort to you also.\"\n\n\"Bernard and Bell!\" exclaimed Mrs. Dale. No idea of such a union had ever yet come upon her, and now in her surprise she sat silent. She had always liked Bernard Dale, having felt for him more family affection than for any other of the Dale family beyond her own hearth. He had been very intimate in her house, having made himself almost as a brother to her girls. But she had never thought of him as a husband for either of them.\n\n\"Then Bell has not spoken to you about it,\" said the squire.\n\n\"Never a word.\"\n\n\"And you had never thought about it?\"\n\n\"Certainly not.\"\n\n\"I have thought about it a great deal. For some years I have always been thinking of it. I have set my heart upon it, and shall be very unhappy if it cannot be brought about. They are both very dear to me,--dearer than anybody else. If I could see them man and wife, I should not much care then how soon I left the old place to them.\"\n\nThere was a purer touch of feeling in this than the squire had ever before shown in his sister-in-law's presence, and more heartiness than she had given him the credit of possessing. And she could not but acknowledge to herself that her own child was included in this unexpected warmth of love, and that she was bound at any rate to entertain some gratitude for such kindness.\n\n\"It is good of you to think of her,\" said the mother; \"very good.\"\n\n\"I think a great deal about her,\" said the squire. \"But that does not much matter now. The fact is, that she has declined Bernard's offer.\"\n\n\"Has Bernard offered to her?\"\n\n\"So he tells me; and she has refused him. It may perhaps be natural that she should do so, never having taught herself to look at him in the light of a lover. I don't blame her at all. I am not angry with her.\"\n\n\"Angry with her! No. You can hardly be angry with her for not being in love with her cousin.\"\n\n\"I say that I am not angry with her. But I think she might undertake to consider the question. You would like such a match, would you not?\"\n\nMrs. Dale did not at first make any answer, but began to revolve the thing in her mind, and to look at it in various points of view. There was a great deal in such an arrangement which at the first sight recommended it to her very strongly. All the local circumstances were in its favour. As regarded herself it would promise to her all that she had ever desired. It would give her a prospect of seeing very much of Lily; for if Bell were settled at the old family house, Crosbie would naturally be much with his friend. She liked Bernard also; and for a moment or two fancied, as she turned it all over in her mind, that, even yet, if such a marriage were to take place, there might grow up something like true regard between her and the old squire. How happy would be her old age in that Small House, if Bell with her children were living so close to her!\n\n\"Well?\" said the squire, who was looking very intently into her face.\n\n\"I was thinking,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"Do you say that she has already refused him?\"\n\n\"I am afraid she has; but then you know--\"\n\n\"It must of course be left for her to judge.\"\n\n\"If you mean that she cannot be made to marry her cousin, of course we all know she can't.\"\n\n\"I mean rather more than that.\"\n\n\"What do you mean, then?\"\n\n\"That the matter must be left altogether to her own decision; that no persuasion must be used by you or me. If he can persuade her, indeed--\"\n\n\"Yes, exactly. He must persuade her. I quite agree with you that he should have liberty to plead his own cause. But look you here, Mary;--she has always been a very good child to you--\"\n\n\"Indeed she has.\"\n\n\"And a word from you would go a long way with her,--as it ought. If she knows that you would like her to marry her cousin, it will make her think it her duty--\"\n\n\"Ah! but that is just what I cannot try to make her think.\"\n\n\"Will you let me speak, Mary? You take me up and scold me before the words are half out of my mouth. Of course I know that in these days a young lady is not to be compelled into marrying anybody;--not but that, as far as I can see, they did better than they do now when they had not quite so much of their own way.\"\n\n\"I never would take upon myself to ask a child to marry any man.\"\n\n\"But you may explain to her that it is her duty to give such a proposal much thought before it is absolutely refused. A girl either is in love or she is not. If she is, she is ready to jump down a man's throat; and that was the case with Lily.\"\n\n\"She never thought of the man till he had proposed to her fully.\"\n\n\"Well, never mind now. But if a girl is not in love, she thinks she is bound to swear and declare that she never will be so.\"\n\n\"I don't think Bell ever declared anything of the kind.\"\n\n\"Yes, she did. She told Bernard that she didn't love him and couldn't love him,--and, in fact, that she wouldn't think anything more about it. Now, Mary, that's what I call being headstrong and positive. I don't want to drive her, and I don't want you to drive her. But here is an arrangement which for her will be a very good one; you must admit that. We all know that she is on excellent terms with Bernard. It isn't as though they had been falling out and hating each other all their lives. She told him that she was very fond of him, and talked nonsense about being his sister, and all that.\"\n\n\"I don't see that it was nonsense at all.\"\n\n\"Yes, it was nonsense,--on such an occasion. If a man asks a girl to marry him, he doesn't want her to talk to him about being his sister. I think it is nonsense. If she would only consider about it properly she would soon learn to love him.\"\n\n\"That lesson, if it be learned at all, must be learned without any tutor.\"\n\n\"You won't do anything to help me then?\"\n\n\"I will, at any rate, do nothing to mar you. And, to tell the truth, I must think over the matter fully before I can decide what I had better say to Bell about it. From her not speaking to me--\"\n\n\"I think she ought to have told you.\"\n\n\"No, Mr. Dale. Had she accepted him, of course she would have told me. Had she thought of doing so she might probably have consulted me. But if she made up her mind that she must reject him--\"\n\n\"She oughtn't to have made up her mind.\" \"But if she did, it seems natural to me that she should speak of it to no one. She might probably think that Bernard would be as well pleased that it should not be known.\"\n\n\"Psha,--known!--of course it will be known. As you want time to consider of it, I will say nothing more now. If she were my daughter, I should have no hesitation in telling her what I thought best for her welfare.\"\n\n\"I have none; though I may have some in making up my mind as to what is best for her welfare. But, Mr. Dale, you may be sure of this; I will speak to her very earnestly of your kindness and love for her. And I wish you would believe that I feel your regard for her very strongly.\"\n\nIn answer to this he merely shook his head, and hummed and hawed. \"You would be glad to see them married, as regards yourself?\" he asked.\n\n\"Certainly I would,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"I have always liked Bernard, and I believe my girl would be safe with him. But then, you see, it's a question on which my own likings or dislikings should not have any bearing.\"\n\nAnd so they parted, the squire making his way back again through the drawing-room window. He was not above half pleased with his interview; but then he was a man for whom half-pleasure almost sufficed. He rarely indulged any expectation that people would make themselves agreeable to him. Mrs. Dale, since she had come to the Small House, had never been a source of satisfaction to him, but he did not on that account regret that he had brought her there. He was a constant man; urgent in carrying out his own plans, but not sanguine in doing so, and by no means apt to expect that all things would go smooth with him. He had made up his mind that his nephew and his niece should be married, and should he ultimately fail in this, such failure would probably embitter his future life;--but it was not in the nature of the man to be angry in the meantime, or to fume and scold because he met with opposition. He had told Mrs. Dale that he loved Bell dearly. So he did, though he seldom spoke to her with much show of special regard, and never was soft and tender with her. But, on the other hand, he did not now love her the less because she opposed his wishes. He was a constant, undemonstrative man, given rather to brooding than to thinking; harder in his words than in his thoughts, with more of heart than others believed, or than he himself knew; but, above all, he was a man who having once desired a thing would desire it always.\n\nMrs. Dale, when she was left alone, began to turn over the question in her mind in a much fuller manner than the squire's presence had as yet made possible for her. Would not such a marriage as this be for them all the happiest domestic arrangement which circumstances could afford? Her daughter would have no fortune, but here would be prepared for her all the comforts which fortune can give. She would be received into her uncle's house, not as some penniless, portionless bride whom Bernard might have married and brought home, but as the wife whom of all others Bernard's friends had thought desirable for him. And then, as regarded Mrs. Dale herself, there would be nothing in such a marriage which would not be delightful to her. It would give a realization to all her dreams of future happiness.\n\nBut, as she said to herself over and over again, all that must go for nothing. It must be for Bell, and for her only, to answer Bernard's question. In her mind there was something sacred in that idea of love. She would regard her daughter almost as a castaway if she were to marry any man without absolutely loving him,--loving him as Lily loved her lover, with all her heart and all her strength.\n\nWith such a conviction as this strong upon her, she felt that she could not say much to Bell that would be of any service.\n\n# CHAPTER XX. DR. CROFTS.\n\nIf there was anything in the world as to which Isabella Dale was quite certain, it was this--that she was not in love with Dr. Crofts. As to being in love with her cousin Bernard, she had never had occasion to ask herself any question on that head. She liked him very well, but she had never thought of marrying him; and now, when he made his proposal, she could not bring herself to think of it. But as regards Dr. Crofts, she had thought of it, and had made up her mind;--in the manner above described.\n\nIt may be said that she could not have been justified in discussing the matter even within her own bosom, unless authorized to do so by Dr. Crofts himself. Let it then be considered that Dr. Crofts had given her some such authority. This may be done in more ways than one; and Miss Dale could not have found herself asking herself questions about him, unless there had been fitting occasion for her to do so.\n\nThe profession of a medical man in a small provincial town is not often one which gives to its owner in early life a large income. Perhaps in no career has a man to work harder for what he earns, or to do more work without earning anything. It has sometimes seemed to me as though the young doctors and the old doctors had agreed to divide between them the different results of their profession,--the young doctors doing all the work and the old doctors taking all the money. If this be so it may account for that appearance of premature gravity which is borne by so many of the medical profession. Under such an arrangement a man may be excused for a desire to put away childish things very early in life.\n\nDr. Crofts had now been practising in Guestwick nearly seven years, having settled himself in that town when he was twenty-three years old, and being at this period about thirty. During those seven years his skill and industry had been so fully admitted that he had succeeded in obtaining the medical care of all the paupers in the union, for which work he was paid at the rate of one hundred pounds a year. He was also assistant-surgeon at a small hospital which was maintained in that town, and held two or three other similar public positions, all of which attested his respectability and general proficiency. They, moreover, thoroughly saved him from any of the dangers of idleness; but, unfortunately, they did not enable him to regard himself as a successful professional man. Whereas old Dr. Gruffen, of whom but few people spoke well, had made a fortune in Guestwick, and even still drew from the ailments of the town a considerable and hardly yet decreasing income. Now this was hard upon Dr. Crofts--unless there was existing some such well-understood arrangement as that above named.\n\nHe had been known to the family of the Dales long previous to his settlement at Guestwick, and had been very intimate with them from that time to the present day. Of all the men, young or old, whom Mrs. Dale counted among her intimate friends, he was the one whom she most trusted and admired. And he was a man to be trusted by those who knew him well. He was not bright and always ready, as was Crosbie, nor had he all the practical worldly good sense of Bernard Dale. In mental power I doubt whether he was superior to John Eames;--to John Eames, such as he might become when the period of his hobbledehoyhood should have altogether passed away. But Crofts, compared with the other three, as they all were at present, was a man more to be trusted than any of them. And there was, moreover, about him an occasional dash of humour, without which Mrs. Dale would hardly have regarded him with that thorough liking which she had for him. But it was a quiet humour, apt to show itself when he had but one friend with him, rather than in general society. Crosbie, on the other hand, would be much more bright among a dozen, than he could with a single companion. Bernard Dale was never bright; and as for Johnny Eames--; but in this matter of brightness, Johnny Eames had not yet shown to the world what his character might be.\n\nIt was now two years since Crofts had been called upon for medical advice on behalf of his friend Mrs. Dale. She had then been ill for a long period--some two or three months, and Dr. Crofts had been frequent in his visits at Allington. At that time he became very intimate with Mrs. Dale's daughters, and especially so with the eldest. Young unmarried doctors ought perhaps to be excluded from houses in which there are young ladies. I know, at any rate, that many sage matrons hold very strongly to that opinion, thinking, no doubt, that doctors ought to get themselves married before they venture to begin working for a living. Mrs. Dale, perhaps, regarded her own girls as still merely children, for Bell, the elder, was then hardly eighteen; or perhaps she held imprudent and heterodox opinions on this subject; or it may be that she selfishly preferred Dr. Crofts, with all the danger to her children, to Dr. Gruffen, with all the danger to herself. But the result was that the young doctor one day informed himself, as he was riding back to Guestwick, that much of his happiness in this world would depend on his being able to marry Mrs. Dale's eldest daughter. At that time his total income amounted to little more than two hundred a year, and he had resolved within his own mind that Dr. Gruffen was esteemed as much the better doctor by the general public opinion of Guestwick, and that Dr. Gruffen's sandy-haired assistant would even have a better chance of success in the town than himself, should it ever come to pass that the doctor was esteemed too old for personal practice. Crofts had no fortune of his own, and he was aware that Miss Dale had none. Then, under those circumstances, what was he to do?\n\nIt is not necessary that we should inquire at any great length into those love passages of the doctor's life which took place three years before the commencement of this narrative. He made no declaration to Bell; but Bell, young as she was, understood well that he would fain have done so, had not his courage failed him, or rather had not his prudence prevented him. To Mrs. Dale he did speak, not openly avowing his love even to her, but hinting at it, and then talking to her of his unsatisfied hopes and professional disappointments. \"It is not that I complain of being poor as I am,\" said he; \"or at any rate, not so poor that my poverty must be any source of discomfort to me; but I could hardly marry with such an income as I have at present.\"\n\n\"But it will increase, will it not?\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"It may some day, when I am becoming an old man,\" he said. \"But of what use will it be to me then?\"\n\nMrs. Dale could not tell him that, as far as her voice in the matter went, he was welcome to woo her daughter and marry her, poor as he was, and doubly poor as they would both be together on such a pittance. He had not even mentioned Bell's name, and had he done so she could only have bade him wait and hope. After that he said nothing further to her upon the subject. To Bell he spoke no word of overt love; but on an autumn day, when Mrs. Dale was already convalescent, and the repetition of his professional visits had become unnecessary, he got her to walk with him through the half-hidden shrubbery paths, and then told her things which he should never have told her, if he really wished to bind her heart to his. He repeated that story of his income, and explained to her that his poverty was only grievous to him in that it prevented him from thinking of marriage. \"I suppose it must,\" said Bell. \"I should think it wrong to ask any lady to share such an income as mine,\" said he. Whereupon Bell had suggested to him that some ladies had incomes of their own, and that he might in that way get over the difficulty. \"I should be afraid of myself in marrying a girl with money,\" said he; \"besides, that is altogether out of the question now.\" Of course Bell did not ask him why it was out of the question, and for a time they went on walking in silence. \"It is a hard thing to do,\" he then said,--not looking at her, but looking at the gravel on which he stood. \"It is a hard thing to do, but I will determine to think of it no further. I believe a man may be as happy single as he may married,--almost.\" \"Perhaps more so,\" said Bell. Then the doctor left her, and Bell, as I have said before, made up her mind with great firmness that she was not in love with him. I may certainly say that there was nothing in the world as to which she was so certain as she was of this.\n\nAnd now, in these days, Dr. Crofts did not come over to Allington very often. Had any of the family in the Small House been ill, he would have been there of course. The squire himself employed the apothecary in the village, or if higher aid was needed, would send for Dr. Gruffen. On the occasion of Mrs. Dale's party, Crofts was there, having been specially invited; but Mrs. Dale's special invitations to her friends were very few, and the doctor was well aware that he must himself make occasion for going there if he desired to see the inmates of the house. But he very rarely made such occasion, perhaps feeling that he was more in his element at the workhouse and the hospital.\n\nJust at this time, however, he made one very great and unexpected step towards success in his profession. He was greatly surprised one morning by being summoned to the Manor House to attend upon Lord De Guest. The family at the Manor had employed Dr. Gruffen for the last thirty years, and Crofts, when he received the earl's message, could hardly believe the words. \"The earl ain't very bad,\" said the servant, \"but he would be glad to see you if possible a little before dinner.\"\n\n\"You're sure he wants to see me?\" said Crofts.\n\n\"Oh, yes; I'm sure enough of that, sir.\"\n\n\"It wasn't Dr. Gruffen?\"\n\n\"No, sir; it wasn't Dr. Gruffen. I believe his lordship's had about enough of Dr. Gruffen. The doctor took to chaffing his lordship one day.\"\n\n\"Chaffed his lordship;--his hands and feet, and that sort of thing?\" suggested the doctor.\n\n\"Hands and feet!\" said the man. \"Lord bless you, sir, he poked his fun at him, just as though he was nobody. I didn't hear, but Mrs. Connor says that my lord's back was up terribly high.\" And so Dr. Crofts got on his horse and rode up to Guestwick Manor.\n\nThe earl was alone, Lady Julia having already gone to Courcy Castle. \"How d'ye do, how d'ye do?\" said the earl. \"I'm not very ill, but I want to get a little advice from you. It's quite a trifle, but I thought it well to see somebody.\" Whereupon Dr. Crofts of course declared that he was happy to wait upon his lordship.\n\n\"I know all about you, you know,\" said the earl. \"Your grandmother Stoddard was a very old friend of my aunt's. You don't remember Lady Jemima?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Crofts. \"I never had that honour.\"\n\n\"An excellent old woman, and knew your grandmother Stoddard well. You see, Gruffen has been attending us for I don't know how many years; but upon my word--\" and then the earl stopped himself.\n\n\"It's an ill wind that blows nobody any good,\" said Crofts, with a slight laugh.\n\n\"Perhaps it 'll blow me some good, for Gruffen never did me any. The fact is this; I'm very well, you know;--as strong as a horse.\"\n\n\"You look pretty well.\"\n\n\"No man could be better,--not of my age. I'm sixty, you know.\"\n\n\"You don't look as though you were ailing.\"\n\n\"I'm always out in the open air, and that, I take it, is the best thing for a man.\"\n\n\"There's nothing like plenty of exercise, certainly.\"\n\n\"And I'm always taking exercise,\" said the earl. \"There isn't a man about the place works much harder than I do. And, let me tell you, sir, when you undertake to keep six or seven hundred acres of land in your own hand, you must look after it, unless you mean to lose money by it.\"\n\n\"I've always heard that your lordship is a good farmer.\"\n\n\"Well, yes; wherever the grass may grow about my place, it doesn't grow under my feet. You won't often find me in bed at six o'clock, I can tell you.\"\n\nAfter this Dr. Crofts ventured to ask his lordship as to what special physical deficiency his own aid was invoked at the present time.\n\n\"Ah, I was just coming to that,\" said the earl. \"They tell me it's a very dangerous practice to go to sleep after dinner.\"\n\n\"It's not very uncommon at any rate,\" said the doctor.\n\n\"I suppose not; but Lady Julia is always at me about it. And, to tell the truth, I think I sleep almost too sound when I get to my arm-chair in the drawing-room. Sometimes my sister really can't wake me;--so, at least, she says.\"\n\n\"And how's your appetite at dinner?\"\n\n\"Oh, I'm quite right there. I never eat any luncheon, you know, and enjoy my dinner thoroughly. Then I drink three or four glasses of port wine--\"\n\n\"And feel sleepy afterwards?\"\n\n\"That's just it,\" said the earl.\n\nIt is not perhaps necessary that we should inquire what was the exact nature of the doctor's advice; but it was, at any rate, given in such a way that the earl said he would be glad to see him again.\n\n\"And look here, Doctor Crofts, I'm all alone just at present. Suppose you come over and dine with me to-morrow; then, if I should go to sleep, you know, you'll be able to let me know whether Lady Julia doesn't exaggerate. Just between ourselves, I don't quite believe all she says about my--my snoring, you know.\"\n\nWhether it was that the earl restrained his appetite when at dinner under the doctor's eyes, or whether the mid-day mutton chop which had been ordered for him had the desired effect, or whether the doctor's conversation was more lively than that of the Lady Julia, we will not say; but the earl, on the evening in question, was triumphant. As he sat in his easy-chair after dinner he hardly winked above once or twice; and when he had taken the large bowl of tea, which he usually swallowed in a semi-somnolent condition, he was quite lively.\n\n\"Ah, yes,\" he said, jumping up and rubbing his eyes; \"I think I do feel lighter. I enjoy a snooze after dinner; I do indeed; I like it; but then, when one comes to go to bed, one does it in such a sneaking sort of way, as though one were in disgrace! And my sister, she thinks it a crime--literally a sin, to go to sleep in a chair. Nobody ever caught her napping! By-the-by, Dr. Crofts, did you know that Mr. Crosbie whom Bernard Dale brought down to Allington? Lady Julia and he are staying at the same house now.\"\n\n\"I met him once at Mrs. Dale's.\"\n\n\"Going to marry one of the girls, isn't he?\"\n\nWhereupon Dr. Crofts explained that Mr. Crosbie was engaged to Lilian Dale.\n\n\"Ah, yes; a nice girl, I'm told. You know all those Dales are connections of ours. My sister Fanny married their uncle Orlando. My brother-in-law doesn't like travelling, and so I don't see very much of him; but of course I'm interested about the family.\"\n\n\"They're very old friends of mine,\" said Crofts.\n\n\"Yes, I daresay. There are two girls, are there not?\"\n\n\"Yes, two.\"\n\n\"And Miss Lily is the youngest. There's nothing about the elder one getting married, is there?\"\n\n\"I've not heard anything of it.\"\n\n\"A very pretty girl she is, too. I remember seeing her at her uncle's last year. I shouldn't wonder if she were to marry her cousin Bernard. He is to have the property, you know; and he's my nephew.\"\n\n\"I'm not quite sure that it's a good thing for cousins to marry,\" said Crofts.\n\n\"They do, you know, very often; and it suits some family arrangements. I suppose Dale must provide for them, and that would take one off his hands without any trouble.\"\n\nDr. Crofts didn't exactly see the matter in this light, but he was not anxious to argue it very closely with the earl. \"The younger one,\" he said, \"has provided for herself.\"\n\n\"What; by getting a husband? But I suppose Dale must give her something. They're not married yet, you know, and, from what I hear, that fellow may prove a slippery customer. He'll not marry her unless old Dale gives her something. You'll see if he does. I'm told that he has got another string to his bow at Courcy Castle.\"\n\nSoon after this, Crofts took his horse and rode home, having promised the earl that he would dine with him again before long.\n\n\"It'll be a great convenience to me if you'd come about that time,\" said the earl, \"and as you're a bachelor perhaps you won't mind it. You'll come on Thursday at seven, will you? Take care of yourself. It's as dark as pitch. John, go and open the first gates for Dr. Crofts.\" And then the earl took himself off to bed.\n\nCrofts, as he rode home, could not keep his mind from thinking of the two girls at Allington. \"He'll not marry her unless old Dale gives her something.\" Had it come to that with the world, that a man must be bribed into keeping his engagement with a lady? Was there no romance left among mankind,--no feeling of chivalry? \"He's got another string to his bow at Courcy Castle,\" said the earl; and his lordship seemed to be in no degree shocked as he said it. It was in this tone that men spoke of women now-a-days, and yet he himself had felt such awe of the girl he loved, and such a fear lest he might injure her in her worldly position, that he had not dared to tell her that he loved her.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI. JOHN EAMES ENCOUNTERS TWO ADVENTURES, AND DISPLAYS GREAT COURAGE IN BOTH.\n\nLily thought that her lover's letter was all that it should be. She was not quite aware what might be the course of post between Courcy and Allington, and had not, therefore, felt very grievously disappointed when the letter did not come on the very first day. She had, however, in the course of the morning, walked down to the post-office, in order that she might be sure that it was not remaining there.\n\n\"Why, miss, they be all delivered; you know that,\" said Mrs. Crump, the post-mistress.\n\n\"But one might be left behind, I thought.\"\n\n\"John Postman went up to the house this very day, with a newspaper for your mamma. I can't make letters for people if folks don't write them.\".\n\n\"But they are left behind sometimes, Mrs. Crump. He wouldn't come up with one letter if he'd got nothing else for anybody in the street.\"\n\n\"Indeed but he would then. I wouldn't let him leave a letter here no how, nor yet a paper. It's no good you're coming down here for letters, Miss Lily. If he don't write to you, I can't make him do it.\" And so poor Lily went home discomforted.\n\nBut the letter came on the next morning, and all was right. According to her judgment it lacked nothing, either in fulness or in affection. When he told her how he had planned his early departure in order that he might avoid the pain of parting with her on the last moment, she smiled and pressed the paper, and rejoiced inwardly that she had got the better of him as to that manoeuvre. And then she kissed the words which told her that he had been glad to have her with him at the last moment. When he declared that he had been happier at Allington than he was at Courcy, she believed him thoroughly, and rejoiced that it should be so. And when he accused himself of being worldly, she excused him, persuading herself that he was nearly perfect in this respect as in others. Of course a man living in London, and having to earn his bread out in the world, must be more worldly than a country girl; but the fact of his being able to love such a girl, to choose such a one for his wife,--was not that alone sufficient proof that the world had not enslaved him? \"My heart is on the Allington lawns,\" he said; and then, as she read the words, she kissed the paper again.\n\nIn her eyes, and to her ears, and to her heart, the letter was a beautiful letter. I believe there is no bliss greater than that which a thorough love-letter gives to a girl who knows that in receiving it she commits no fault,--who can open it before her father and mother with nothing more than the slight blush which the consciousness of her position gives her. And of all love-letters the first must be the sweetest! What a value there is in every word! How each expression is scanned and turned to the best account! With what importance are all those little phrases invested, which too soon become mere phrases, used as a matter of course. Crosbie had finished his letter by bidding God bless her; \"And you too,\" said Lily, pressing the letter to her bosom.\n\n\"Does he say anything particular?\" asked Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Yes, mamma; it's all very particular.\"\n\n\"But there's nothing for the public ear.\"\n\n\"He sends his love to you and Bell.\"\n\n\"We are very much obliged to him.\"\n\n\"So you ought to be. And he says that he went to church going through Barchester, and that the clergyman was the grandfather of that Lady Dumbello. When he got to Courcy Castle Lady Dumbello was there.\"\n\n\"What a singular coincidence!\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"I won't tell you a word more about his letter,\" said Lily. So she folded it up, and put it in her pocket. But as soon as she found herself alone in her own room, she had it out again, and read it over some half-a-dozen times.\n\nThat was the occupation of her morning;--that, and the manufacture of some very intricate piece of work which was intended for the adornment of Mr. Crosbie's person. Her hands, however, were very full of work;--or, rather, she intended that they should be full. She would take with her to her new home, when she was married, all manner of household gear, the produce of her own industry and economy. She had declared that she wanted to do something for her future husband, and she would begin that something at once. And in this matter she did not belie her promises to herself, or allow her good intentions to evaporate unaccomplished. She soon surrounded herself with harder tasks than those embroidered slippers with which she indulged herself immediately after his departure. And Mrs. Dale and Bell,--though in their gentle way they laughed at her,--nevertheless they worked with her, sitting sternly to their long tasks, in order that Crosbie's house might not be empty when their darling should go to take her place there as his wife.\n\nBut it was absolutely necessary that the letter should be answered. It would in her eyes have been a great sin to have let that day's post go without carrying a letter from her to Courcy Castle,--a sin of which she felt no temptation to be guilty. It was an exquisite pleasure to her to seat herself at her little table, with her neat desk and small appurtenances for epistle-craft, and to feel that she had a letter to write in which she had truly much to say. Hitherto her correspondence had been uninteresting and almost weak in its nature. From her mother and sister she had hardly yet been parted; and though she had other friends, she had seldom found herself with very much to tell them by post. What could she communicate to Mary Eames at Guestwick, which should be in itself exciting as she wrote it? When she wrote to John Eames, and told \"Dear John\" that mamma hoped to have the pleasure of seeing him to tea at such an hour, the work of writing was of little moment to her, though the note when written became one of the choicest treasures of him to whom it was addressed.\n\nBut now the matter was very different. When she saw the words \"Dearest Adolphus\" on the paper before her, she was startled with their significance. \"And four months ago I had never even heard of him,\" she said to herself, almost with awe. And now he was more to her, and nearer to her, than even was her sister or her mother! She recollected how she had laughed at him behind his back, and called him a swell on the first day of his coming to the Small House, and how, also, she had striven, in her innocent way, to look her best when called upon to go out and walk with the stranger from London. He was no longer a stranger now, but her own dearest friend.\n\nShe had put down her pen that she might think of all this--by no means for the first time--and then resumed it with a sudden start as though fearing that the postman might be in the village before her letter was finished. \"Dearest Adolphus, I need not tell you how delighted I was when your letter was brought to me this morning.\" But I will not repeat the whole of her letter here. She had no incident to relate, none even so interesting as that of Mr. Crosbie's encounter with Mr. Harding at Barchester. She had met no Lady Dumbello, and had no counterpart to Lady Alexandrina, of whom, as a friend, she could say a word in praise. John Eames's name she did not mention, knowing that John Eames was not a favourite with Mr. Crosbie; nor had she anything to say of John Eames, that had not been already said. He had, indeed, promised to come over to Allington; but this visit had not been made when Lily wrote her first letter to Crosbie. It was a sweet, good, honest love-letter, full of assurances of unalterable affection and unlimited confidence, indulging in a little quiet fun as to the grandees of Courcy Castle, and ending with a promise that she would be happy and contented if she might receive his letters constantly, and live with the hope of seeing him at Christmas.\n\n\"I am in time, Mrs. Crump, am I not?\" she said, as she walked into the post-office.\n\n\"Of course you be,--for the next half-hour. T' postman--he bain't stirred from t' ale'us yet. Just put it into t' box, wull ye?\"\n\n\"But you won't leave it there?\"\n\n\"Leave it there! Did you ever hear the like of that? If you're afeared to put it in, you can take it away; that's all about it, Miss Lily.\" And then Mrs. Crump turned away to her avocations at the washing-tub. Mrs. Crump had a bad temper, but perhaps she had some excuse. A separate call was made upon her time with reference to almost every letter brought to her office, and for all this, as she often told her friends in profound disgust, she received as salary no more than \"tuppence farden a day. It don't find me in shoe-leather; no more it don't.\" As Mrs. Crump was never seen out of her own house, unless it was in church once a month, this latter assertion about her shoe-leather, could hardly have been true.\n\nLily had received another letter, and had answered it before Eames made his promised visit to Allington. He, as will be remembered, had also had a correspondence. He had answered Miss Roper's letter, and had since that been living in fear of two things; in a lesser fear of some terrible rejoinder from Amelia, and in a greater fear of a more terrible visit from his lady-love. Were she to swoop down in very truth upon his Guestwick home, and declare herself to his mother and sister as his affianced bride, what mode of escape would then be left for him? But this she had not yet done, nor had she even answered his cruel missive.\n\n\"What an ass I am to be afraid of her!\" he said to himself as he walked along under the elms of Guestwick manor, which overspread the road to Allington. When he first went over to Allington after his return home, he had mounted himself on horseback, and had gone forth brilliant with spurs, and trusting somewhat to the glories of his dress and gloves. But he had then known nothing of Lily's engagement. Now he was contented to walk; and as he had taken up his slouched hat and stick in the passage of his mother's house, he had been very indifferent as to his appearance. He walked quickly along the road, taking for the first three miles the shade of the Guestwick elms, and keeping his feet on the broad greensward which skirts the outside of the earl's palings. \"What an ass I am to be afraid of her!\" And as he swung his big stick in his hand, striking a tree here and there, and knocking the stones from his path, he began to question himself in earnest, and to be ashamed of his position in the world. \"Nothing on earth shall make me marry her,\" he said; \"not if they bring a dozen actions against me. She knows as well as I do, that I have never intended to marry her. It's a cheat from beginning to end. If she comes down here, I'll tell her so before my mother.\" But as the vision of her sudden arrival came before his eyes, he acknowledged to himself that he still held her in great fear. He had told her that he loved her. He had written as much as that. If taxed with so much, he must confess his sin.\n\nThen, by degrees, his mind turned away from Amelia Roper to Lily Dale, not giving him a prospect much more replete with enjoyment than that other one. He had said that he would call at Allington before he returned to town, and he was now redeeming his promise. But he did not know why he should go there. He felt that he should sit silent and abashed in Mrs. Dale's drawing-room, confessing by his demeanour that secret which it behoved him now to hide from every one. He could not talk easily before Lily, nor could he speak to her of the only subject which would occupy his thoughts when in her presence. If indeed, he might find her alone-- But, perhaps that might be worse for him than any other condition.\n\nWhen he was shown into the drawing-room there was nobody there. \"They were here a minute ago, all three,\" said the servant girl. \"If you'll walk down the garden, Mr. John, you'll be sure to find some of 'em.\" So John Eames, with a little hesitation, walked down the garden.\n\nFirst of all he went the whole way round the walks, meeting nobody. Then he crossed the lawn, returning again to the farther end; and there, emerging from the little path which led from the Great House, he encountered Lily alone. \"Oh, John,\" she said, \"how d'ye do? I'm afraid you did not find anybody in the house. Mamma and Bell are with Hopkins, away in the large kitchen-garden.\"\n\n\"I've just come over,\" said Eames, \"because I promised. I said I'd come before I went back to London.\"\n\n\"And they'll be very glad to see you, and so am I. Shall we go after them into the other grounds? But perhaps you walked over and are tired.\"\n\n\"I did walk,\" said Eames; \"not that I am very tired.\" But in truth he did not wish to go after Mrs. Dale, though he was altogether at a loss as to what he would say to Lily while remaining with her. He had fancied that he would like to have some opportunity of speaking to her alone before he went away;--of making some special use of the last interview which he should have with her before she became a married woman. But now the opportunity was there, and he hardly dared to avail himself of it.\n\n\"You'll stay and dine with us,\" said Lily.\n\n\"No, I'll not do that, for I especially told my mother that I would be back.\"\n\n\"I'm sure it was very good of you to walk so far to see us. If you really are not tired, I think we will go to mamma, as she would be very sorry to miss you.\"\n\nThis she said, remembering at the moment what had been Crosbie's injunctions to her about John Eames. But John had resolved that he would say those words which he had come to speak, and that, as Lily was there with him, he would avail himself of the chance which fortune had given him.\n\n\"I don't think I'll go into the squire's garden,\" he said.\n\n\"Uncle Christopher is not there. He is about the farm somewhere.\"\n\n\"If you don't mind, Lily, I think I'll stay here. I suppose they'll be back soon. Of course I should like to see them before I go away to London. But, Lily, I came over now chiefly to see you. It was you who asked me to promise.\"\n\nHad Crosbie been right in those remarks of his? Had she been imprudent in her little endeavour to be cordially kind to her old friend? \"Shall we go into the drawing-room?\" she said, feeling that she would be in some degree safer there than out among the shrubs and paths of the garden. And I think she was right in this. A man will talk of love out among the lilacs and roses, who would be stricken dumb by the demure propriety of the four walls of a drawing-room. John Eames also had some feeling of this kind, for he determined to remain out in the garden, if he could so manage it.\n\n\"I don't want to go in unless you wish it,\" he said. \"Indeed, I'd rather stay here. So, Lily, you're going to be married?\" And thus he rushed at once into the middle of his discourse.\n\n\"Yes,\" said she, \"I believe I am.\"\n\n\"I have not told you yet that I congratulated you.\"\n\n\"I have known very well that you did so in your heart. I have always been sure that you wished me well.\"\n\n\"Indeed I have. And if congratulating a person is hoping that she may always be happy, I do congratulate you. But, Lily--\" And then he paused, abashed by the beauty, purity, and woman's grace which had forced him to love her.\n\n\"I think I understand all that you would say. I do not want ordinary words to tell me that I am to count you among my best friends.\"\n\n\"No, Lily; you don't understand all that I would say. You have never known how often and how much I have thought of you; how dearly I have loved you.\"\n\n\"John, you must not talk of that now.\"\n\n\"I cannot go without telling you. When I came over here, and Mrs. Dale told me that you were to be married to that man--\"\n\n\"You must not speak of Mr. Crosbie in that way,\" she said, turning upon him almost fiercely.\n\n\"I did not mean to say anything disrespectful of him to you. I should hate myself if I were to do so. Of course you like him better than anybody else?\"\n\n\"I love him better than all the world besides.\"\n\n\"And so do I love you better than all the world besides.\" And as he spoke he got up from his seat and stood before her. \"I know how poor I am, and unworthy of you; and only that you are engaged to him, I don't suppose that I should now tell you. Of course you couldn't accept such a one as me. But I have loved you ever since you remember; and now that you are going to be his wife, I cannot but tell you that it is so. You will go and live in London; but as to my seeing you there, it will be impossible. I could not go into that man's house.\"\n\n\"Oh, John.\"\n\n\"No, never; not if you become his wife. I have loved you as well as he does. When Mrs. Dale told me of it, I thought I should have fallen. I went away without seeing you because I was unable to speak to you. I made a fool of myself, and have been a fool all along. I am foolish now to tell you this, but I cannot help it.\"\n\n\"You will forget it all when you meet some girl that you can really love.\"\n\n\"And have I not really loved you? Well, never mind. I have said what I came to say, and I will now go. If it ever happens that we are down in the country together, perhaps I may see you again; but never in London. Good-by, Lily.\" And he put out his hand to her.\n\n\"And won't you stay for mamma?\" she said.\n\n\"No. Give her my love, and to Bell. They understand all about it. They will know why I have gone. If ever you should want anybody to do anything for you, remember that I will do it, whatever it is.\" And as he paced away from her across the lawn, the special deed in her favour to which his mind was turned,--that one thing which he most longed to do on her behalf,--was an act of corporal chastisement upon Crosbie. If Crosbie would but ill-treat her,--ill-treat her with some antenuptial barbarity,--and if only he could be called in to avenge her wrongs! And as he made his way back along the road towards Guestwick, he built up within his own bosom a castle in the air, for her part in which Lily Dale would by no means have thanked him.\n\nLily when she was left alone burst into tears. She had certainly said very little to encourage her forlorn suitor, and had so borne herself during the interview that even Crosbie could hardly have been dissatisfied; but now that Eames was gone her heart became very tender towards him. She felt that she did love him also;--not at all as she loved Crosbie, but still with a love that was tender, soft, and true. If Crosbie could have known all her thoughts at that moment, I doubt whether he would have liked them. She burst into tears, and then hurried away into some nook where she could not be seen by her mother and Bell on their return.\n\nEames went on his way, walking very quietly, swinging his stick and kicking through the dust, with his heart full of the scene which had just passed. He was angry with himself, thinking that he had played his part badly, accusing himself in that he had been rough to her, and selfish in the expression of his love; and he was angry with her because she had declared to him that she loved Crosbie better than all the world besides. He knew that of course she must do so;--that at any rate it was to be expected that such was the case. Yet, he thought, she might have refrained from saying so to him. \"She chooses to scorn me now,\" he said to himself; \"but the time may come when she will wish that she had scorned him.\" That Crosbie was wicked, bad, and selfish, he believed most fully. He felt sure that the man would ill-use her and make her wretched. He had some slight doubt whether he would marry her, and from this doubt he endeavoured to draw a scrap of comfort. If Crosbie would desert her, and if to him might be accorded the privilege of beating the man to death with his fists because of this desertion, then the world would not be quite blank for him. In all this he was no doubt very cruel to Lily;--but then had not Lily been very cruel to him?\n\nHe was still thinking of these things when he came to the first of the Guestwick pastures. The boundary of the earl's property was very plainly marked, for with it commenced also the shady elms along the roadside, and the broad green margin of turf, grateful equally to those who walked and to those who rode. Eames had got himself on to the grass, but, in the fulness of his thoughts, was unconscious of the change in his path, when he was startled by a voice in the next field and the loud bellowing of a bull. Lord De Guest's choice cattle he knew were there, and there was one special bull which was esteemed by his lordship as of great value, and regarded as a high favourite. The people about the place declared that the beast was vicious, but Lord De Guest had often been heard to boast that it was never vicious with him. \"The boys tease him, and the men are almost worse than the boys,\" said the earl; \"but he'll never hurt any one that has not hurt him.\" Guided by faith in his own teaching the earl had taught himself to look upon his bull as a large, horned, innocent lamb of the flock.\n\nAs Eames paused on the road, he fancied that he recognized the earl's voice, and it was the voice of one in distress. Then the bull's roar sounded very plain in his ear, and almost close; upon hearing which he rushed on to the gate, and, without much thinking what he was doing, vaulted over it, and advanced a few steps into the field.\n\n\"Halloo!\" shouted the earl. \"There's a man. Come on.\" And then his continued shoutings hardly formed themselves into intelligible words; but Eames plainly understood that he was invoking assistance under great pressure and stress of circumstances. The bull was making short runs at his owner, as though determined in each run to have a toss at his lordship; and at each run the earl would retreat quickly for a few paces, but he retreated always facing his enemy, and as the animal got near to him, would make digs at his face with the long spud which he carried in his hand. But in thus making good his retreat he had been unable to keep in a direct line to the gate, and there seemed to be great danger lest the bull should succeed in pressing him up against the hedge. \"Come on!\" shouted the earl, who was fighting his battle manfully, but was by no means anxious to carry off all the laurels of the victory himself. \"Come on, I say!\" Then he stopped in his path, shouted into the bull's face, brandished his spud, and threw about his arms, thinking that he might best dismay the beast by the display of these warlike gestures.\n\nJohnny Eames ran on gallantly to the peer's assistance, as he would have run to that of any peasant in the land. He was one to whom I should be perhaps wrong to attribute at this period of his life the gift of very high courage. He feared many things which no man should fear; but he did not fear personal mishap or injury to his own skin and bones. When Cradell escaped out of the house in Burton Crescent, making his way through the passage into the outer air, he did so because he feared that Lupex would beat him or kick him, or otherwise ill-use him. John Eames would also have desired to escape under similar circumstances; but he would have so desired because he could not endure to be looked upon in his difficulties by the people of the house, and because his imagination would have painted the horrors of a policeman dragging him off with a black eye and a torn coat. There was no one to see him now, and no policeman to take offence. Therefore he rushed to the earl's assistance, brandishing his stick, and roaring in emulation of the bull.\n\nWhen the animal saw with what unfairness he was treated, and that the number of his foes was doubled, while no assistance had lent itself on his side, he stood for a while, disgusted by the injustice of humanity. He stopped, and throwing his head up to the heavens, bellowed out his complaint. \"Don't come close!\" said the earl, who was almost out of breath. \"Keep a little apart. Ugh! ugh! whoop, whoop!\" And he threw up his arms manfully, jobbing about with his spud, ever and anon rubbing the perspiration from off his eyebrows with the back of his hand.\n\nAs the bull stood pausing, meditating whether under such circumstances flight would not be preferable to gratified passion, Eames made a rush in at him, attempting to hit him on the head. The earl, seeing this, advanced a step also, and got his spud almost up to the animal's eye. But these indignities the beast could not stand. He made a charge, bending his head first towards John Eames, and then, with that weak vacillation which is as disgraceful in a bull as in a general, he changed his purpose, and turned his horns upon his other enemy. The consequence was that his steps carried him in between the two, and that the earl and Eames found themselves for a while behind his tail.\n\n\"Now for the gate,\" said the earl.\n\n\"Slowly does it; slowly does it; don't run!\" said Johnny, assuming in the heat of the moment a tone of counsel which would have been very foreign to him under other circumstances.\n\nThe earl was not a whit offended. \"All right,\" said he, taking with a backward motion the direction of the gate. Then as the bull again faced towards him, he jumped from the ground, labouring painfully with arms and legs, and ever keeping his spud well advanced against the foe. Eames, holding his position a little apart from his friend, stooped low and beat the ground with his stick, and as though defying the creature. The bull felt himself defied, stood still and roared, and then made another vacillating attack.\n\n\"Hold on till we reach the gate,\" said Eames.\n\n\"Ugh! ugh! Whoop! whoop!\" shouted the earl. And so gradually they made good their ground.\n\n\"Now get over,\" said Eames, when they had both reached the corner of the field in which the gate stood.\n\n\"And what'll you do?\" said the earl.\n\n\"I'll go at the hedge to the right.\" And Johnny as he spoke dashed his stick about, so as to monopolize, for a moment, the attention of the brute. The earl made a spring at the gate, and got well on to the upper rung. The bull seeing that his prey was going, made a final rush upon the earl and struck the timber furiously with his head, knocking his lordship down on the other side. Lord De Guest was already over, but not off the rail; and thus, though he fell, he fell in safety on the sward beyond the gate. He fell in safety, but utterly exhausted. Eames, as he had purposed, made a leap almost sideways at a thick hedge which divided the field from one of the Guestwick copses. There was a fairly broad ditch, and on the other side a quickset hedge, which had, however, been weakened and injured by trespassers at this corner, close to the gate. Eames was young and active and jumped well. He jumped so well that he carried his body full into the middle of the quickset, and then scrambled through to the other side, not without much injury to his clothes, and some damage also to his hands and face.\n\nThe beast, recovering from his shock against the wooden bars, looked wistfully at his last retreating enemy, as he still struggled amidst the bushes. He looked at the ditch and at the broken hedge, but he did not understand how weak were the impediments in his way. He had knocked his head against the stout timber, which was strong enough to oppose him, but was dismayed by the brambles which he might have trodden under foot without an effort. How many of us are like the bull, turning away conquered by opposition which should be as nothing to us, and breaking our feet, and worse still, our hearts, against rocks of adamant. The bull at last made up his mind that he did not dare to face the hedge; so he gave one final roar, and then turning himself round, walked placidly back amidst the herd.\n\nJohnny made his way on to the road by a stile that led out of the copse, and was soon standing over the earl, while the blood ran down his cheeks from the scratches. One of the legs of his trowsers had been caught by a stake, and was torn from the hip downward, and his hat was left in the field, the only trophy for the bull. \"I hope you're not hurt, my lord,\" he said.\n\n\"Oh dear, no; but I'm terribly out of breath. Why, you're bleeding all over. He didn't get at you, did he?\"\n\n\"It's only the thorns in the hedge,\" said Johnny, passing his hand over his face. \"But I've lost my hat.\"\n\n\"There are plenty more hats,\" said the earl.\n\n\"I think I'll have a try for it,\" said Johnny, with whom the means of getting hats had not been so plentiful as with the earl. \"He looks quiet now.\" And he moved towards the gate.\n\nBut Lord De Guest jumped upon his feet, and seized the young man by the collar of his coat. \"Go after your hat!\" said he. \"You must be a fool to think of it. If you're afraid of catching cold, you shall have mine.\"\n\n\"I'm not the least afraid of catching cold,\" said Johnny. \"Is he often like that, my lord?\" And he made a motion with his head towards the bull.\n\n\"The gentlest creature alive; he's like a lamb generally--just like a lamb. Perhaps he saw my red pocket-handkerchief.\" And Lord De Guest showed his friend that he carried such an article. \"But where should I have been if you hadn't come up?\"\n\n\"You'd have got to the gate, my lord.\"\n\n\"Yes; with my feet foremost, and four men carrying me. I'm very thirsty. You don't happen to carry a flask, do you?\"\n\n\"No, my lord, I don't.\"\n\n\"Then we'll make the best of our way home, and have a glass of wine there.\" And on this occasion his lordship intended that his offer should be accepted.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII. LORD DE GUEST AT HOME.\n\nThe earl and John Eames, after their escape from the bull, walked up to the Manor House together. \"You can write a note to your mother, and I'll send it by one of the boys,\" said the earl. This was his lordship's answer when Eames declined to dine at the Manor House, because he would be expected home.\n\n\"But I'm so badly off for clothes, my lord,\" pleaded Johnny. \"I tore my trowsers in the hedge.\"\n\n\"There will be nobody there beside us two and Dr. Crofts. The doctor will forgive you when he hears the story; and as for me, I didn't care if you hadn't a stitch to your back. You'll have company back to Guestwick, so come along.\"\n\nEames had no further excuse to offer, and therefore did as he was bidden. He was by no means as much at home with the earl now as during those minutes of the combat. He would rather have gone home, being somewhat ashamed of being seen in his present tattered and bare-headed condition by the servants of the house; and moreover, his mind would sometimes revert to the scene which had taken place in the garden at Allington. But he found himself obliged to obey the earl, and so he walked on with him through the woods.\n\nThe earl did not say very much, being tired and somewhat thoughtful. In what little he did say he seemed to be specially hurt by the ingratitude of the bull towards himself. \"I never teased him, or annoyed him in any way.\"\n\n\"I suppose they are dangerous beasts?\" said Eames.\n\n\"Not a bit of it, if they're properly treated. It must have been my handkerchief, I suppose. I remember that I did blow my nose.\"\n\nHe hardly said a word in the way of thanks to his assistant. \"Where should I have been if you had not come to me?\" he had exclaimed immediately after his deliverance; but having said that he didn't think it necessary to say much more to Eames. But he made himself very pleasant, and by the time he had reached the house his companion was almost glad that he had been forced to dine at the Manor House. \"And now we'll have a drink,\" said the earl. \"I don't know how you feel, but I never was so thirsty in my life.\"\n\nTwo servants immediately showed themselves, and evinced some surprise at Johnny's appearance. \"Has the gentleman hurt hisself, my lord?\" asked the butler, looking at the blood upon our friend's face.\n\n\"He has hurt his trowsers the worst, I believe,\" said the earl. \"And if he was to put on any of mine they'd be too short and too big, wouldn't they? I am sorry you should be so uncomfortable, but you mustn't mind it for once.\"\n\n\"I don't mind it a bit,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"And I'm sure I don't,\" said the earl. \"Mr. Eames is going to dine here, Vickers.\"\n\n\"Yes, my lord.\"\n\n\"And his hat is down in the middle of the nineteen acres. Let three or four men go for it.\"\n\n\"Three or four men, my lord!\"\n\n\"Yes,--three or four men. There's something gone wrong with that bull. And you must get a boy with a pony to take a note into Guestwick, to Mrs. Eames. Oh dear, I'm better now,\" and he put down the tumbler from which he'd been drinking. \"Write your note here, and then we'll go and see my pet pheasants before dinner.\"\n\nVickers and the footman knew that something had happened of much moment, for the earl was usually very particular about his dinner-table. He expected every guest who sat there to be dressed in such guise as the fashion of the day demanded; and he himself, though his morning costume was by no means brilliant, never dined, even when alone, without having put himself into a suit of black, with a white cravat, and having exchanged the old silver hunting-watch which he carried during the day tied round his neck by a bit of old ribbon, for a small gold watch, with a chain and seals, which in the evening always dangled over his waistcoat. Dr. Gruffen had once been asked to dinner at Guestwick Manor. \"Just a bachelor's chop,\" said the earl; \"for there's nobody at home but myself.\" Whereupon Dr. Gruffen had come in coloured trowsers,--and had never again been asked to dine at Guestwick Manor. All this Vickers knew well; and now his lordship had brought young Eames home to dine with him with his clothes all hanging about him in a manner which Vickers declared in the servants' hall wasn't more than half decent. Therefore, they all knew that something very particular must have happened. \"It's some trouble about the bull, I know,\" said Vickers;--\"but bless you, the bull couldn't have tore his things in that way!\"\n\nEames wrote his note, in which he told his mother that he had had an adventure with Lord De Guest, and that his lordship had insisted on bringing him home to dinner. \"I have torn my trowsers all to pieces,\" he added in a postscript, \"and have lost my hat. Everything else is all right.\" He was not aware that the earl also sent a short note to Mrs. Eames.\n\nDEAR MADAM [ran the earl's note],--\n\nYour son has, under Providence, probably saved my life. I will leave the story for him to tell. He has been good enough to accompany me home, and will return to Guestwick after dinner with Dr. Crofts, who dines here. I congratulate you on having a son with so much cool courage and good feeling.\n\nYour very faithful servant,\n\nGuestwick Manor, Thursday, October, 186--.\n\nAnd then they went to see the pheasants. \"Now, I'll tell you what,\" said the earl. \"I advise you to take to shooting. It's the amusement of a gentleman when a man chances to have the command of game.\"\n\n\"But I'm always up in London.\"\n\n\"No, you're not. You're not up in London now. You always have your holidays. If you choose to try it, I'll see that you have shooting enough while you're here. It's better than going to sleep under the trees. Ha, ha, ha! I wonder what made you lay yourself down there. You hadn't been fighting a bull that day?\"\n\n\"No, my lord. I hadn't seen the bull then.\"\n\n\"Well; you think of what I've been saying. When I say a thing, I mean it. You shall have shooting enough, if you have a mind to try it.\" Then they looked at the pheasants, and pottered about the place till the earl said it was time to dress for dinner. \"That's hard upon you, isn't it?\" said he. \"But, at any rate, you can wash your hands, and get rid of the blood. I'll be down in the little drawing-room five minutes before seven, and I suppose I'll find you there.\"\n\nAt five minutes before seven Lord De Guest came into the small drawing-room, and found Johnny seated there, with a book before him. The earl was a little fussy, and showed by his manner that he was not quite at his ease, as some men do when they have any piece of work on hand which is not customary to them. He held something in his hand, and shuffled a little as he made his way up the room. He was dressed, as usual, in black; but his gold chain was not, as usual, dangling over his waistcoat.\n\n\"Eames,\" he said, \"I want you to accept a little present from me,--just as a memorial of our affair with the bull. It will make you think of it sometimes, when I'm perhaps gone.\"\n\n\"Oh, my lord--\"\n\n\"It's my own watch, that I have been wearing for some time; but I've got another;--two or three, I believe, somewhere upstairs. You mustn't refuse me. I can't bear being refused. There are two or three little seals, too, which I have worn. I have taken off the one with my arms, because that's of no use to you, and it is to me. It doesn't want a key, but winds up at the handle, in this way,\" and the earl proceeded to explain the nature of the toy.\n\n\"My lord, you think too much of what happened to-day,\" said Eames, stammering.\n\n\"No, I don't; I think very little about it. I know what I think of. Put the watch in your pocket before the doctor comes. There; I hear his horse. Why didn't he drive over, and then he could have taken you back?\"\n\n\"I can walk very well.\"\n\n\"I'll make that all right. The servant shall ride Crofts' horse, and bring back the little phaeton. How d'you do, doctor? You know Eames, I suppose? You needn't look at him in that way. His leg is not broken; it's only his trowsers.\" And then the earl told the story of the bull.\n\n\"Johnny will become quite a hero in town,\" said Crofts.\n\n\"Yes; I fear he'll get the most of the credit; and yet I was at it twice as long as he was. I'll tell you what, young men, when I got to that gate I didn't think I'd breath enough left in me to get over it. It's all very well jumping into a hedge when you're only two-and-twenty; but when a man comes to be sixty he likes to take his time about such things. Dinner ready, is it? So am I. I quite forgot that mutton chop of yours to-day, doctor. But I suppose a man may eat a good dinner after a fight with a bull?\"\n\nThe evening passed by without any very pleasurable excitement, and I regret to say that the earl went fast to sleep in the drawing-room as soon as he had swallowed his cup of coffee. During dinner he had been very courteous to both his guests, but towards Eames he had used a good-humoured and almost affectionate familiarity. He had quizzed him for having been found asleep under the tree, telling Crofts that he had looked very forlorn,--\"So that I haven't a doubt about his being in love,\" said the earl. And he had asked Johnny to tell the name of the fair one, bringing up the remnants of his half-forgotten classicalities to bear out the joke. \"If I am to take more of the severe Falernian,\" said he, laying his hand on the decanter of port, \"I must know the lady's name. Whoever she be, I'm well sure you need not blush for her. What! you refuse to tell! Then I'll drink no more.\" And so the earl had walked out of the dining-room; but not till he had perceived by his guest's cheeks that the joke had been too true to be pleasant. As he went, however, he leaned with his hand on Eames's shoulder, and the servants looking on saw that the young man was to be a favourite. \"He'll make him his heir,\" said Vickers. \"I shouldn't wonder a bit if he don't make him his heir.\" But to this the footman objected, endeavouring to prove to Mr. Vickers that, in accordance with the law of the land, his lordship's second cousin, once removed, whom the earl had never seen, but whom he was supposed to hate, must be his heir. \"A hearl can never choose his own heir, like you or me,\" said the footman, laying down the law. \"Can't he though really, now? That's very hard on him; isn't it?\" said the pretty housemaid. \"Psha,\" said Vickers: \"you know nothing about it. My lord could make young Eames his heir to-morrow; that is, the heir of his property. He couldn't make him a hearl, because that must go to the heirs of his body. As to his leaving him the place here, I don't just know how that'd be; and I'm sure Richard don't.\"\n\n\"But suppose he hasn't got any heirs of his body?\" asked the pretty housemaid, who was rather fond of putting down Mr. Vickers.\n\n\"He must have heirs of his body,\" said the butler. \"Everybody has 'em. If a man don't know 'em himself, the law finds 'em out.\" And then Mr. Vickers walked away, avoiding further dispute.\n\nIn the meantime, the earl was asleep upstairs, and the two young men from Guestwick did not find that they could amuse themselves with any satisfaction. Each took up a book; but there are times at which a man is quite unable to read, and when a book is only a cover for his idleness or dulness. At last, Dr. Crofts suggested, in a whisper, that they might as well begin to think of going home.\n\n\"Eh; yes; what?\" said the earl: \"I'm not asleep.\" In answer to which the doctor said that he thought he'd go home, if his lordship would let him order his horse. But the earl was again fast bound in slumber, and took no further notice of the proposition.\n\n\"Perhaps we could get off without waking him,\" suggested Eames, in a whisper.\n\n\"Eh; what?\" said the earl. So they both resumed their books, and submitted themselves to their martyrdom for a further period of fifteen minutes. At the expiration of that time, the footman brought in tea.\n\n\"Eh, what? tea!\" said the earl. \"Yes, we'll have a little tea. I've heard every word you've been saying.\" It was that assertion on the part of the earl which always made Lady Julia so angry. \"You cannot have heard what I have been saying, Theodore, because I have said nothing,\" she would reply. \"But I should have heard it if you had,\" the earl would rejoin, snappishly. On the present occasion neither Crofts nor Eames contradicted him, and he took his tea and swallowed it while still three parts asleep.\n\n\"If you'll allow me, my lord, I think I'll order my horse,\" said the doctor.\n\n\"Yes; horse--yes--\" said the earl, nodding.\n\n\"But what are you to do, Eames, if I ride?\" said the doctor.\n\n\"I'll walk,\" whispered Eames, in his very lowest voice.\n\n\"What--what--what?\" said the earl, jumping up on his feet. \"Oh, ah, yes; going away, are you? I suppose you might as well, as sit here and see me sleeping. But, doctor--I didn't snore, did I?\"\n\n\"Only occasionally.\"\n\n\"Not loud, did I? Come, Eames, did I snore loud?\"\n\n\"Well, my lord, you did snore rather loud two or three times.\"\n\n\"Did I?\" said the earl, in a voice of great disappointment. \"And yet, do you know, I heard every word you said.\"\n\nThe small phaeton had been already ordered, and the two young men started back to Guestwick together, a servant from the house riding the doctor's horse behind them. \"Look here, Eames,\" said the earl, as they parted on the steps of the hall door. \"You're going back to town the day after to-morrow, you say, so I shan't see you again?\"\n\n\"No, my lord,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Look you here, now. I shall be up for the Cattle-show before Christmas. You must dine with me at my hotel, on the twenty-second of December, Pawkins's, in Jermyn Street; seven o'clock, sharp. Mind you do not forget, now. Put it down in your pocket-book when you get home. Good-by, doctor; good-by. I see I must stick to that mutton chop in the middle of the day.\" And then they drove off.\n\n\"He'll make him his heir for certain,\" said Vickers to himself, as he slowly returned to his own quarters.\n\n\"You were returning from Allington, I suppose,\" said Crofts, \"when you came across Lord De Guest and the bull?\"\n\n\"Yes: I just walked over to say good-by to them.\"\n\n\"Did you find them all well?\"\n\n\"I only saw one. The other two were out.\"\n\n\"Mrs. Dale, was it?\"\n\n\"No; it was Lily.\"\n\n\"Sitting alone, thinking of her fine London lover, of course? I suppose we ought to look upon her as a very lucky girl. I have no doubt she thinks herself so.\"\n\n\"I'm sure I don't know,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"I believe he's a very good young man,\" said the doctor; \"but I can't say I quite liked his manner.\"\n\n\"I should think not,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"But then in all probability he did not like mine a bit better, or perhaps yours either. And if so it's all fair.\"\n\n\"I don't see that it's a bit fair. He's a snob,\" said Eames; \"and I don't believe that I am.\" He had taken a glass or two of the earl's \"severe Falernian,\" and was disposed to a more generous confidence, and perhaps also to stronger language, than might otherwise have been the case.\n\n\"No; I don't think he is a snob,\" said Crofts. \"Had he been so, Mrs. Dale would have perceived it.\"\n\n\"You'll see,\" said Johnny, touching up the earl's horse with energy as he spoke. \"You'll see. A man who gives himself airs is a snob; and he gives himself airs. And I don't believe he's a straightforward fellow. It was a bad day for us all when he came among them at Allington.\"\n\n\"I can't say that I see that.\"\n\n\"I do. But mind, I haven't spoken a word of this to any one. And I don't mean. What would be the good? I suppose she must marry him now?\"\n\n\"Of course she must.\"\n\n\"And be wretched all her life. Oh-h-h-h!\" and he muttered a deep groan. \"I'll tell you what it is, Crofts. He is going to take the sweetest girl out of this country that ever was in it, and he don't deserve her.\"\n\n\"I don't think she can be compared to her sister,\" said Crofts slowly.\n\n\"What; not Lily?\" said Eames, as though the proposition made by the doctor were one that could not hold water for a minute.\n\n\"I have always thought that Bell was the more admired of the two,\" said Crofts.\n\n\"I'll tell you what,\" said Eames. \"I have never yet set my eyes on any human creature whom I thought so beautiful as Lily Dale. And now that beast is going to marry her! I'll tell you what, Crofts; I'll manage to pick a quarrel with him yet.\" Whereupon the doctor, seeing the nature of the complaint from which his companion was suffering, said nothing more, either about Lily or about Bell.\n\nSoon after this Eames was at his own door, and was received there by his mother and sister with all the enthusiasm due to a hero. \"He has saved the earl's life!\" Mrs. Eames had exclaimed to her daughter on reading Lord De Guest's note. \"Oh, goodness!\" and she threw herself back upon the sofa almost in a fainting condition.\n\n\"Saved Lord De Guest's life!\" said Mary.\n\n\"Yes--under Providence,\" said Mrs. Eames, as though that latter fact added much to her son's good deed.\n\n\"But how did he do it?\"\n\n\"By cool courage and good feeling--so his lordship says. But I wonder how he really did do it?\"\n\n\"Whatever way it was, he's torn all his clothes and lost his hat,\" said Mary.\n\n\"I don't care a bit about that,\" said Mrs. Eames. \"I wonder whether the earl has any interest at the Income-tax.\n\n\"What a thing it would be if he could get Johnny a step. It would be seventy pounds a year at once. He was quite right to stay and dine when his lordship asked him. And so Dr. Crofts is there. It couldn't have been anything in the doctoring way, I suppose.\"\n\n\"No, I should say not; because of what he says of his trowsers.\" And so the two ladies were obliged to wait for John's return.\n\n\"How did you do it, John?\" said his mother, embracing him, as soon as the door was opened.\n\n\"How did you save the earl's life?\" said Mary, who was standing behind her mother.\n\n\"Would his lordship really have been killed, if it had not been for you?\" asked Mrs. Eames.\n\n\"And was he very much hurt?\" asked Mary.\n\n\"Oh, bother,\" said Johnny, on whom the results of the day's work, together with the earl's Falernian, had made some still remaining impression. On ordinary occasions, Mrs. Eames would have felt hurt at being so answered by her son; but at the present moment she regarded him as standing so high in general favour that she took no offence. \"Oh, Johnny, do tell us. Of course we must be very anxious to know it all.\"\n\n\"There's nothing to tell, except that a bull ran at the earl, as I was going by; so I went into the field and helped him, and then he made me stay and dine with him.\"\n\n\"But his lordship says that you saved his life,\" said Mary.\n\n\"Under Providence,\" added their mother.\n\n\"At any rate, he has given me a gold watch and chain,\" said Johnny, drawing the present out of his pocket. \"I wanted a watch badly. All the same, I didn't like taking it.\"\n\n\"It would have been very wrong to refuse,\" said his mother. \"And I am so glad you have been so fortunate. And look here, Johnny: when a friend like that comes in your way, don't turn your back on him.\" Then, at last, he thawed beneath their kindness, and told them the whole of the story. I fear that in recounting the earl's efforts with the spud, he hardly spoke of his patron with all that deference which would have been appropriate.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII. MR. PLANTAGENET PALLISER.\n\nA week passed over Mr. Crosbie's head at Courcy Castle without much inconvenience to him from the well-known fact of his matrimonial engagement. Both George De Courcy and John De Courcy had in their different ways charged him with his offence, and endeavoured to annoy him by recurring to the subject; but he did not care much for the wit or malice of George or John De Courcy. The countess had hardly alluded to Lily Dale after those few words which she said on the first day of his visit, and seemed perfectly willing to regard his doings at Allington as the occupation natural to a young man in such a position. He had been seduced down to a dull country house, and had, as a matter of course, taken to such amusements as the place afforded. He had shot the partridges and made love to the young lady, taking those little recreations as compensation for the tedium of the squire's society. Perhaps he had gone a little too far with the young lady; but then no one knew better than the countess how difficult it is for a young man to go far enough without going too far. It was not her business to make herself a censor on a young man's conduct. The blame, no doubt, rested quite as much with Miss Dale as with him. She was quite sorry that any young lady should be disappointed; but if girls will be imprudent, and set their caps at men above their mark, they must encounter disappointment. With such language did Lady De Courcy speak of the affair among her daughters, and her daughters altogether agreed with her that it was out of the question that Mr. Crosbie should marry Lily Dale. From Alexandrina he encountered during the week none of that raillery which he had expected. He had promised to explain to her before he left the castle all the circumstances of his acquaintance with Lily, and she at last showed herself determined to demand the fulfilment of this promise; but, previous to that, she said nothing to manifest either offence or a lessened friendship. And I regret to say, that in the intercourse which had taken place between them, that friendship was by no means less tender that it had been in London.\n\n\"And when will you tell me what you promised?\" she asked him one afternoon, speaking in a low voice, as they were standing together at the window of the billiard-room, in that idle half-hour which always occurs before the necessity for dinner preparation has come. She had been riding and was still in her habit, and he had returned from shooting. She knew that she looked more than ordinarily well in her tall straight hat and riding gear, and was wont to hang about the house, walking skilfully with her upheld drapery, during this period of the day. It was dusk, but not dark, and there was no artificial light in the billiard-room. There had been some pretence of knocking about the balls, but it had been only pretence. \"Even Diana,\" she had said, \"could not have played billiards in a habit.\" Then she had put down her mace, and they had stood talking together in the recess of a large bow-window.\n\n\"And what did I promise?\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"You know well enough. Not that it is a matter of any special interest to me; only, as you undertook to promise, of course my curiosity has been raised.\"\n\n\"If it be of no special interest,\" said Crosbie, \"you will not object to absolve me from my promise.\"\n\n\"That is just like you,\" she said. \"And how false you men always are. You made up your mind to buy my silence on a distasteful subject by pretending to offer me your future confidence; and now you tell me that you do not mean to confide in me.\"\n\n\"You begin by telling me that the matter is one that does not in the least interest you.\"\n\n\"That is so false again! You know very well what I meant. Do you remember what you said to me the day you came? and am I not bound to tell you after that, that your marriage with this or that young lady is not matter of special interest to me? Still, as your friend--\"\n\n\"Well, as my friend!\"\n\n\"I shall be glad to know--. But I am not going to beg for your confidence; only I tell you this fairly, that no man is so mean in my eyes as a man who fights under false colours.\"\n\n\"And am I fighting under false colours?\"\n\n\"Yes, you are.\" And now, as she spoke, the Lady Alexandrina blushed beneath her hat; and dull as was the remaining light of the evening, Crosbie, looking into her face, saw her heightened colour. \"Yes, you are. A gentleman is fighting under false colours who comes into a house like this, with a public rumour of his being engaged, and then conducts himself as though nothing of the kind existed. Of course, it is not anything to me specially; but that is fighting under false colours. Now, sir, you may redeem the promise you made me when you first came here,--or you may let it alone.\"\n\nIt must be acknowledged that the lady was fighting her battle with much courage, and also with some skill. In three or four days Crosbie would be gone; and this victory, if it were ever to be gained, must be gained in those three or four days. And if there were to be no victory, then it would be only fair that Crosbie should be punished for his duplicity, and that she should be avenged as far as any revenge might be in her power. Not that she meditated any deep revenge, or was prepared to feel any strong anger. She liked Crosbie as well as she had ever liked any man. She believed that he liked her also. She had no conception of any very strong passion, but conceived that a married life was more pleasant than one of single bliss. She had no doubt that he had promised to make Lily Dale his wife, but so had he previously promised her, or nearly so. It was a fair game, and she would win it if she could. If she failed, she would show her anger; but she would show it in a mild, weak manner,--turning up her nose at Lily before Crosbie's face, and saying little things against himself behind his back. Her wrath would not carry her much beyond that.\n\n\"Now, sir, you may redeem the promise you made me when you first came here,--or you may let it alone.\" So she spoke, and then she turned her face away from him, gazing out into the darkness.\n\n\"Alexandrina!\" he said.\n\n\"Well, sir? But you have no right to speak to me in that style. You know that you have no right to call me by my name in that way!\"\n\n\"You mean that you insist upon your title?\"\n\n\"All ladies insist on what you call their title, from gentlemen, except under the privilege of greater intimacy than you have the right to claim. You did not call Miss Dale by her Christian name till you had obtained permission, I suppose?\"\n\n\"You used to let me call you so.\"\n\n\"Never! Once or twice, when you have done so, I have not forbidden it, as I should have done. Very well, sir, as you have nothing to tell me, I will leave you. I must confess that I did not think you were such a coward.\" And she prepared to go, gathering up the skirts of her habit, and taking up the whip which she had laid on the window-sill.\n\n\"Stay a moment, Alexandrina,\" he said; \"I am not happy, and you should not say words intended to make me more miserable.\"\n\n\"And why are you unhappy?\"\n\n\"Because-- I will tell you instantly, if I may believe that I am telling you only, and not the whole household.\"\n\n\"Of course I shall not talk of it to others. Do you think that I cannot keep a secret?\"\n\n\"It is because I have promised to marry one woman, and because I love another. I have told you everything now; and if you choose to say again that I am fighting under false colours I will leave the castle before you can see me again.\"\n\n\"Mr. Crosbie!\"\n\n\"Now you know it all, and may imagine whether or no I am very happy. I think you said it was time to dress;--suppose we go?\" And without further speech the two went off to their separate rooms.\n\nCrosbie, as soon as he was alone in his chamber, sat himself down in his arm-chair, and went to work striving to make up his mind as to his future conduct. It must not be supposed that the declaration just made by him had been produced solely by his difficulty at the moment. The atmosphere of Courcy Castle had been at work upon him for the last week past. And every word that he had heard, and every word that he had spoken, had tended to destroy all that was good and true within him, and to foster all that was selfish and false. He had said to himself a dozen times during that week that he never could be happy with Lily Dale, and that he never could make her happy. And then he had used the old sophistry in his endeavour to teach himself that it was right to do that which he wished to do. Would it not be better for Lily that he should desert her, than marry her against the dictates of his own heart? And if he really did not love her, would he not be committing a greater crime in marrying her than in deserting her? He confessed to himself that he had been very wrong in allowing the outer world to get such a hold upon him that the love of a pure girl like Lily could not suffice for his happiness. But there was the fact, and he found himself unable to contend against it. If by any absolute self-sacrifice he could secure Lily's well-being, he would not hesitate for a moment. But would it be well to sacrifice her as well as himself?\n\nHe had discussed the matter in this way within his own breast, till he had almost taught himself to believe that it was his duty to break off his engagement with Lily; and he had also almost taught himself to believe that a marriage with a daughter of the house of Courcy would satisfy his ambition and assist him in his battle with the world. That Lady Alexandrina would accept him he felt certain, if he could only induce her to forgive him for his sin in becoming engaged to Miss Dale. How very prone she would be to forgiveness in this matter, he had not divined, having not as yet learned how easily such a woman can forgive such a sin, if the ultimate triumph be accorded to herself.\n\nAnd there was another reason which operated much with Crosbie, urging him on in his present mood and wishes, though it should have given an exactly opposite impulse to his heart. He had hesitated as to marrying Lily Dale at once, because of the smallness of his income. Now he had a prospect of considerable increase to that income. One of the commissioners at his office had been promoted to some greater commissionership, and it was understood by everybody that the secretary at the General Committee Office would be the new commissioner. As to that there was no doubt. But then the question had arisen as to the place of secretary. Crosbie had received two or three letters on the subject, and it seemed that the likelihood of his obtaining this step in the world was by no means slight. It would increase his official income from seven hundred a year to twelve, and would place him altogether above the world. His friend, the present secretary, had written to him, assuring him that no other probable competitor was spoken of as being in the field against him. If such good fortune awaited him, would it not smooth any present difficulty which lay in the way of his marriage with Lily Dale? But, alas, he had not looked at the matter in that light! Might not the countess help him to this preferment? And if his destiny intended for him the good things of this world,--secretaryships, commissionerships, chairmanships, and such like, would it not be well that he should struggle on in his upward path by such assistance as good connections might give him?\n\nHe sat thinking over it all in his own room on that evening. He had written twice to Lily since his arrival at Courcy Castle. His first letter has been given. His second was written much in the same tone; though Lily, as she had read it, had unconsciously felt somewhat less satisfied than she had been with the first. Expressions of love were not wanting, but they were vague and without heartiness. They savoured of insincerity, though there was nothing in the words themselves to convict them. Few liars can lie with the full roundness and self-sufficiency of truth; and Crosbie, bad as he was, had not yet become bad enough to reach that perfection. He had said nothing to Lily of the hopes of promotion which had been opened to him; but he had again spoken of his own worldliness--acknowledging that he received an unsatisfying satisfaction from the pomps and vanities of Courcy Castle. In fact he was paving the way for that which he had almost resolved that he would do, now he had told Lady Alexandrina that he loved her; and he was obliged to confess to himself that the die was cast.\n\nAs he thought of all this, there was not wanting to him some of the satisfaction of an escape. Soon after making that declaration of love at Allington he had begun to feel that in making it he had cut his throat. He had endeavoured to persuade himself that he could live comfortably with his throat cut in that way; and as long as Lily was with him he would believe that he could do so; but as soon as he was again alone he would again accuse himself of suicide. This was his frame of mind even while he was yet at Allington, and his ideas on the subject had become stronger during his sojourn at Courcy. But the self-immolation had not been completed, and he now began to think that he could save himself. I need hardly say that this was not all triumph to him. Even had there been no material difficulty as to his desertion of Lily,--no uncle, cousin, and mother whose anger he must face,--no vision of a pale face, more eloquent of wrong in its silence than even uncle, cousin, and mother, with their indignant storm of words,--he was not altogether heartless. How should he tell all this to the girl who had loved him so well; who had so loved him, that, as he himself felt, her love would fashion all her future life either for weal or for woe? \"I am unworthy of her, and will tell her so,\" he said to himself. How many a false hound of a man has endeavoured to salve his own conscience by such mock humility? But he acknowledged at this moment, as he rose from his seat to dress himself, that the die was cast, and that it was open to him now to say what he pleased to Lady Alexandrina. \"Others have gone through the same fire before,\" he said to himself, as he walked downstairs, \"and have come out scatheless.\" And then he recalled to himself the names of various men of high repute in the world who were supposed to have committed in their younger days some such little mistake as that into which he had been betrayed.\n\nIn passing through the hall he overtook Lady Julia De Guest, and was in time to open for her the door of the drawing-room. He then remembered that she had come into the billiard-room at one side, and had gone out at the other, while he was standing with Alexandrina at the window. He had not, however, then thought much of Lady Julia; and as he now stood for her to pass by him through the door-way, he made to her some indifferent remark.\n\nBut Lady Julia was on some subjects a stern woman, and not without a certain amount of courage. In the last week she had seen what had been going on, and had become more and more angry. Though she had disowned any family connection with Lily Dale, nevertheless she now felt for her sympathy and almost affection. Nearly every day she had repeated stiffly to the countess some incident of Crosbie's courtship and engagement to Miss Dale,--speaking of it as with absolute knowledge, as a thing settled at all points. This she had done to the countess alone, in the presence of the countess and Alexandrina, and also before all the female guests of the castle. But what she had said was received simply with an incredulous smile. \"Dear me! Lady Julia,\" the countess had replied at last, \"I shall begin to think you are in love with Mr. Crosbie yourself; you harp so constantly on this affair of his. One would think that young ladies in your part of the world must find it very difficult to get husbands, seeing that the success of one young lady is trumpeted so loudly.\" For the moment, Lady Julia was silenced; but it was not easy to silence her altogether when she had a subject for speech near her heart.\n\nAlmost all the Courcy world were assembled in the drawing-room as she now walked into the room with Crosbie at her heels. When she found herself near the crowd she turned round, and addressed him in a voice more audible than that generally required for purposes of drawing-room conversation. \"Mr. Crosbie,\" she said, \"have you heard lately from our dear friend, Lily Dale?\" And she looked him full in the face, in a manner more significant, probably, than even she had intended it to be. There was, at once, a general hush in the room, and all eyes were turned upon her and upon him.\n\nCrosbie instantly made an effort to bear the attack gallantly, but he felt that he could not quite command his colour, or prevent a sudden drop of perspiration from showing itself upon his brow. \"I had a letter from Allington yesterday,\" he said. \"I suppose you have heard of your brother's encounter with the bull?\"\n\n\"The bull!\" said Lady Julia. And it was instantly manifest to all that her attack had been foiled and her flank turned.\n\n\"Good gracious! Lady Julia, how very odd you are!\" said the countess.\n\n\"But what about the bull?\" asked the Honourable George.\n\n\"It seems that the earl was knocked down in the middle of one of his own fields.\"\n\n\"Oh, dear!\" exclaimed Alexandrina. And sundry other exclamations were made by all the assembled ladies.\n\n\"But he wasn't hurt,\" said Crosbie. \"A young man named Eames seems to have fallen from the sky and carried off the earl on his back.\"\n\n\"Ha, ha, ha, ha!\" growled the other earl, as he heard of the discomfiture of his brother peer.\n\nLady Julia, who had received her own letters that day from Guestwick, knew that nothing of importance had happened to her brother; but she felt that she was foiled for that time.\n\n\"I hope that there has not really been any accident,\" said Mr. Gazebee, with a voice of great solicitude.\n\n\"My brother was quite well last night, thank you,\" said she. And then the little groups again formed themselves, and Lady Julia was left alone on the corner of a sofa.\n\n\"Was that all an invention of yours, sir?\" said Alexandrina to Crosbie.\n\n\"Not quite. I did get a letter this morning from my friend Bernard Dale,--that old harridan's nephew; and Lord De Guest has been worried by some of his animals. I wish I had told her that his stupid old neck had been broken.\"\n\n\"Fie, Mr. Crosbie!\"\n\n\"What business has she to interfere with me?\"\n\n\"But I mean to ask the same question that she asked, and you won't put me off with a cock-and-bull story like that.\" But then, as she was going to ask the question, dinner was announced.\n\n\"And is it true that De Guest has been tossed by a bull?\" said the earl, as soon as the ladies were gone. He had spoken nothing during dinner except what words he had muttered into the ear of Lady Dumbello. It was seldom that conversation had many charms for him in his own house; but there was a savour of pleasantry in the idea of Lord De Guest having been tossed, by which even he was tickled.\n\n\"Only knocked down, I believe,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Ha, ha, ha!\" growled the earl; then he filled his glass, and allowed some one else to pass the bottle. Poor man! There was not much left to him now in the world which did amuse him.\n\n\"I don't see anything to laugh at,\" said Plantagenet Palliser, who was sitting at the earl's right hand, opposite to Lord Dumbello.\n\n\"Don't you?\" said the earl. \"Ha, ha, ha!\"\n\n\"I'll be shot if I do. From all I hear De Guest is an uncommon good farmer. And I don't see the joke of tossing a farmer merely because he's a nobleman also. Do you?\" and he turned round to Mr. Gazebee, who was sitting on the other side. The earl was an earl, and was also Mr. Gazebee's father-in-law. Mr. Plantagenet Palliser was the heir to a dukedom. Therefore, Mr. Gazebee merely simpered, and did not answer the question put to him. Mr. Palliser said nothing more about it, nor did the earl; and then the joke died away.\n\nMr. Plantagenet Palliser was the Duke of Omnium's heir--heir to that nobleman's title and to his enormous wealth; and, therefore, was a man of mark in the world. He sat in the House of Commons, of course. He was about five-and-twenty years of age, and was, as yet, unmarried. He did not hunt or shoot or keep a yacht, and had been heard to say that he had never put a foot upon a race-course in his life. He dressed very quietly, never changing the colour or form of his garments; and in society was quiet, reserved, and very often silent. He was tall, slight, and not ill-looking; but more than this cannot be said for his personal appearance--except, indeed, this, that no one could mistake him for other than a gentleman. With his uncle, the duke, he was on good terms--that is to say, they had never quarrelled. A very liberal allowance had been made to the nephew; but the two relatives had no tastes in common, and did not often meet. Once a year Mr. Palliser visited the duke at his great country seat for two or three days, and usually dined with him two or three times during the season in London. Mr. Palliser sat for a borough which was absolutely under the duke's command; but had accepted his seat under the distinct understanding that he was to take whatever part in politics might seem good to himself. Under these well-understood arrangements, the duke and his heir showed to the world quite a pattern of a happy family. \"So different to the earl and Lord Porlock!\" the people of West Barsetshire used to say. For the estates, both of the duke and of the earl, were situated in the western division of that county.\n\nMr. Palliser was chiefly known to the world as a rising politician. We may say that he had everything at his command, in the way of pleasure, that the world could offer him. He had wealth, position, power, and the certainty of attaining the highest rank among, perhaps, the most brilliant nobility of the world. He was courted by all who could get near enough to court him. It is hardly too much to say that he might have selected a bride from all that was most beautiful and best among English women. If he would have bought race-horses, and have expended thousands on the turf, he would have gratified his uncle by doing so. He might have been the master of hounds, or the slaughterer of hecatombs of birds. But to none of these things would he devote himself. He had chosen to be a politician, and in that pursuit he laboured with a zeal and perseverance which would have made his fortune at any profession or in any trade. He was constant in committee-rooms up to the very middle of August. He was rarely absent from any debate of importance, and never from any important division. Though he seldom spoke, he was always ready to speak if his purpose required it. No man gave him credit for any great genius--few even considered that he could become either an orator or a mighty statesman. But the world said that he was a rising man, and old Nestor of the Cabinet looked on him as one who would be able, at some far future day, to come among them as a younger brother. Hitherto he had declined such inferior offices as had been offered to him, biding his time carefully; and he was as yet tied hand and neck to no party, though known to be liberal in all his political tendencies. He was a great reader--not taking up a book here, and another there, as chance brought books before him, but working through an enormous course of books, getting up the great subject of the world's history--filling himself full of facts--though perhaps not destined to acquire the power of using those facts otherwise than as precedents. He strove also diligently to become a linguist--not without success, as far as a competent understanding of various languages. He was a thin-minded, plodding, respectable man, willing to devote all his youth to work, in order that in old age he might be allowed to sit among the Councillors of the State.\n\nHitherto his name had not been coupled by the world with that of any woman whom he had been supposed to admire; but latterly it had been observed that he had often been seen in the same room with Lady Dumbello. It had hardly amounted to more than this; but when it was remembered how undemonstrative were the two persons concerned--how little disposed was either of them to any strong display of feeling--even this was thought matter to be mentioned. He certainly would speak to her from time to time almost with an air of interest; and Lady Dumbello, when she saw that he was in the room, would be observed to raise her head with some little show of life, and to look round as though there were something there on which it might be worth her while to allow her eyes to rest. When such innuendoes were abroad, no one would probably make more of them than Lady De Courcy. Many, when they heard that Mr. Palliser was to be at the castle, had expressed their surprise at her success in that quarter. Others, when they learned that Lady Dumbello had consented to become her guest, had also wondered greatly. But when it was ascertained that the two were to be there together, her good-natured friends had acknowledged that she was a very clever woman. To have either Mr. Palliser or Lady Dumbello would have been a feather in her cap; but to succeed in getting both, by enabling each to know that the other would be there, was indeed a triumph. As regards Lady Dumbello, however, the bargain was not fairly carried out; for, after all, Mr. Palliser came to Courcy Castle only for two nights and a day, and during the whole of that day he was closeted with sundry large blue-books. As for Lady De Courcy, she did not care how he might be employed. Blue-books and Lady Dumbello were all the same to her. Mr. Palliser had been at Courcy Castle, and neither enemy nor friend could deny the fact.\n\nThis was his second evening; and as he had promised to meet his constituents at Silverbridge at one P.M. on the following day, with the view of explaining to them his own conduct and the political position of the world in general; and as he was not to return from Silverbridge to Courcy, Lady Dumbello, if she made any way at all, must take advantage of the short gleam of sunshine which the present hour afforded her. No one, however, could say that she showed any active disposition to monopolize Mr. Palliser's attention. When he sauntered into the drawing-room she was sitting, alone, in a large, low chair, made without arms, so as to admit the full expansion of her dress, but hollowed and round at the back, so as to afford her the support that was necessary to her. She had barely spoken three words since she had left the dining-room, but the time had not passed heavily with her. Lady Julia had again attacked the countess about Lily Dale and Mr. Crosbie, and Alexandrina, driven almost to rage, had stalked off to the farther end of the room, not concealing her special concern in the matter.\n\n\"How I do wish they were married and done with,\" said the countess; \"and then we should hear no more about them.\"\n\nAll of which Lady Dumbello heard and understood; and in all of it she took a certain interest. She remembered such things, learning thereby who was who, and regulating her own conduct by what she learned. She was by no means idle at this or at other such times, going through, we may say, a considerable amount of really hard work in her manner of working. There she had sat speechless, unless when acknowledging by a low word of assent some expression of flattery from those around her. Then the door opened, and when Mr. Palliser entered she raised her head, and the faintest possible gleam of satisfaction might have been discerned upon her features. But she made no attempt to speak to him; and when, as he stood at the table, he took up a book and remained thus standing for a quarter of an hour, she neither showed nor felt any impatience. After that Lord Dumbello came in, and he stood at the table without a book. Even then Lady Dumbello felt no impatience.\n\nPlantagenet Palliser skimmed through his little book, and probably learned something. When he put it down he sipped a cup of tea, and remarked to Lady De Courcy that he believed it was only twelve miles to Silverbridge.\n\n\"I wish it was a hundred and twelve,\" said the countess.\n\n\"In that case I should be forced to start to-night,\" said Mr. Palliser.\n\n\"Then I wish it was a thousand and twelve,\" said Lady De Courcy.\n\n\"In that case I should not have come at all,\" said Mr. Palliser. He did not mean to be uncivil, and had only stated a fact.\n\n\"The young men are becoming absolute bears,\" said the countess to her daughter Margaretta.\n\nHe had been in the room nearly an hour when he did at last find himself standing close to Lady Dumbello: close to her, and without any other very near neighbour.\n\n\"I should hardly have expected to find you here,\" he said.\n\n\"Nor I you,\" she answered.\n\n\"Though, for the matter of that, we are both near our own homes.\"\n\n\"I am not near mine.\"\n\n\"I meant Plumstead; your father's place.\"\n\n\"Yes; that was my home once.\"\n\n\"I wish I could show you my uncle's place. The castle is very fine, and he has some good pictures.\"\n\n\"So I have heard.\"\n\n\"Do you stay here long?\"\n\n\"Oh, no. I go to Cheshire the day after to-morrow. Lord Dumbello is always there when the hunting begins.\"\n\n\"Ah, yes; of course. What a happy fellow he is; never any work to do! His constituents never trouble him, I suppose?\"\n\n\"I don't think they ever do, much.\"\n\nAfter that Mr. Palliser sauntered away again, and Lady Dumbello passed the rest of the evening in silence. It is to be hoped that they both were rewarded by that ten minutes of sympathetic intercourse for the inconvenience which they had suffered in coming to Courcy Castle.\n\nBut that which seems so innocent to us had been looked on in a different light by the stern moralists of that house.\n\n\"By Jove!\" said the Honourable George to his cousin, Mr. Gresham, \"I wonder how Dumbello likes it.\"\n\n\"It seems to me that Dumbello takes it very easily.\"\n\n\"There are some men who will take anything easily,\" said George, who, since his own marriage, had learned to have a holy horror of such wicked things.\n\n\"She's beginning to come out a little,\" said Lady Clandidlem to Lady De Courcy, when the two old women found themselves together over a fire in some back sitting-room. \"Still waters always run deep, you know.\"\n\n\"I shouldn't at all wonder if she were to go off with him,\" said Lady De Courcy.\n\n\"He'll never be such a fool as that,\" said Lady Clandidlem.\n\n\"I believe men will be fools enough for anything,\" said Lady De Courcy. \"But, of course, if he did, it would come to nothing afterwards. I know one who would not be sorry. If ever a man was tired of a woman, Lord Dumbello is tired of her.\"\n\nBut in this, as in almost everything else, the wicked old woman spoke scandal. Lord Dumbello was still proud of his wife, and as fond of her as a man can be of a woman whose fondness depends upon mere pride.\n\nThere had not been much that was dangerous in the conversation between Mr. Palliser and Lady Dumbello, but I cannot say the same as to that which was going on at the same moment between Crosbie and Lady Alexandrina. She, as I have said, walked away in almost open dudgeon when Lady Julia recommenced her attack about poor Lily, nor did she return to the general circle during the evening. There were two large drawing-rooms at Courcy Castle, joined together by a narrow link of a room, which might have been called a passage, had it not been lighted by two windows coming down to the floor, carpeted as were the drawing-rooms, and warmed with a separate fireplace. Hither she betook herself, and was soon followed by her married sister Amelia.\n\n\"That woman almost drives me mad,\" said Alexandrina, as they stood together with their toes upon the fender.\n\n\"But, my dear, you of all people should not allow yourself to be driven mad on such a subject.\"\n\n\"That's all very well, Amelia.\"\n\n\"The question is this, my dear,--what does Mr. Crosbie mean to do?\"\n\n\"How should I know?\"\n\n\"If you don't know, it will be safer to suppose that he is going to marry this girl; and in that case--\"\n\n\"Well, what in that case? Are you going to be another Lady Julia? What do I care about the girl?\"\n\n\"I don't suppose you care much about the girl; and if you care as little about Mr. Crosbie, there's an end of it; only in that case, Alexandrina--\"\n\n\"Well, what in that case?\"\n\n\"You know I don't want to preach to you. Can't you tell me at once whether you really like him? You and I have always been good friends.\" And the married sister put her arm affectionately round the waist of her who wished to be married.\n\n\"I like him well enough.\"\n\n\"And has he made any declaration to you?\"\n\n\"In a sort of a way he has. Hark, here he is!\" And Crosbie, coming in from the larger room, joined the sisters at the fireplace.\n\n\"We were driven away by the clack of Lady Julia's tongue,\" said the elder.\n\n\"I never met such a woman,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"There cannot well be many like her,\" said Alexandrina. And after that they all stood silent for a minute or two. Lady Amelia Gazebee was considering whether or no she would do well to go and leave the two together. If it were intended that Mr. Crosbie should marry her sister, it would certainly be well to give him an opportunity of expressing such a wish on his own part. But if Alexandrina was simply making a fool of herself, then it would be well for her to stay. \"I suppose she would rather I should go,\" said the elder sister to herself; and then, obeying the rule which should guide all our actions from one to another, she went back and joined the crowd.\n\n\"Will you come on into the other room?\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"I think we are very well here,\" Alexandrina replied.\n\n\"But I wish to speak to you,--particularly,\" said he.\n\n\"And cannot you speak here?\"\n\n\"No. They will be passing backwards and forwards.\" Lady Alexandrina said nothing further, but led the way into the other large room. That also was lighted, and there were in it four or five persons. Lady Rosina was reading a work on the Millennium, with a light to herself in one corner. Her brother John was asleep in an arm-chair, and a young gentleman and lady were playing chess. There was, however, ample room for Crosbie and Alexandrina to take up a position apart.\n\n\"And now, Mr. Crosbie, what have you got to say to me? But, first, I mean to repeat Lady Julia's question, as I told you that I should do.--When did you hear last from Miss Dale?\"\n\n\"It is cruel in you to ask me such a question, after what I have already told you. You know that I have given to Miss Dale a promise of marriage.\"\n\n\"Very well, sir. I don't see why you should bring me in here to tell me anything that is so publicly known as that. With such a herald as Lady Julia it was quite unnecessary.\"\n\n\"If you can only answer me in that tone I will make an end of it at once. When I told you of my engagement, I told you also that another woman possessed my heart. Am I wrong to suppose that you knew to whom I alluded?\"\n\n\"Indeed, I did not, Mr. Crosbie. I am no conjuror, and I have not scrutinized you so closely as your friend Lady Julia.\"\n\n\"It is you that I love. I am sure I need hardly say so now.\"\n\n\"Hardly, indeed,--considering that you are engaged to Miss Dale.\"\n\n\"As to that I have, of course, to own that I have behaved foolishly;--worse than foolishly, if you choose to say so. You cannot condemn me more absolutely than I condemn myself. But I have made up my mind as to one thing. I will not marry where I do not love.\" Oh, if Lily could have heard him as he then spoke! \"It would be impossible for me to speak in terms too high of Miss Dale; but I am quite sure that I could not make her happy as her husband.\"\n\n\"Why did you not think of that before you asked her?\" said Alexandrina. But there was very little of condemnation in her tone.\n\n\"I ought to have done so; but it is hardly for you to blame me with severity. Had you, when we were last together in London--had you been less--\"\n\n\"Less what?\"\n\n\"Less defiant,\" said Crosbie, \"all this might perhaps have been avoided.\"\n\nLady Alexandrina could not remember that she had been defiant; but, however, she let that pass. \"Oh, yes; of course it was my fault.\"\n\n\"I went down there to Allington with my heart ill at ease, and now I have fallen into this trouble. I tell you all as it has happened. It is impossible that I should marry Miss Dale. It would be wicked in me to do so, seeing that my heart belongs altogether to another. I have told you who is that other; and now may I hope for an answer?\"\n\n\"An answer to what?\"\n\n\"Alexandrina, will you be my wife?\"\n\nIf it had been her object to bring him to a point-blank declaration and proposition of marriage, she had certainly achieved her object now. And she had that trust in her own power of management and in her mother's, that she did not fear that in accepting him she would incur the risk of being served as he was serving Lily Dale. She knew her own position and his too well for that. If she accepted him she would in due course of time become his wife,--let Miss Dale and all her friends say what they might to the contrary. As to that head she had no fear. But nevertheless she did not accept him at once. Though she wished for the prize, her woman's nature hindered her from taking it when it was offered to her.\n\n\"How long is it, Mr. Crosbie,\" she said, \"since you put the same question to Miss Dale?\"\n\n\"I have told you everything, Alexandrina,--as I promised that I would do. If you intend to punish me for doing so--\"\n\n\"And I might ask another question. How long will it be before you put the same question to some other girl?\"\n\nHe turned round as though to walk away from her in anger; but when he had gone half the distance to the door he returned.\n\n\"By heaven!\" he said, and he spoke somewhat roughly, too, \"I'll have an answer. You at any rate have nothing with which to reproach me. All that I have done wrong, I have done through you, or on your behalf. You have heard my proposal. Do you intend to accept it?\"\n\n\"I declare you startle me. If you demanded my money or my life, you could not be more imperious.\"\n\n\"Certainly not more resolute in my determination.\"\n\n\"And if I decline the honour?\"\n\n\"I shall think you the most fickle of your sex.\"\n\n\"And if I were to accept it?\"\n\n\"I would swear that you were the best, the dearest, and the sweetest of women.\"\n\n\"I would rather have your good opinion than your bad, certainly,\" said Lady Alexandrina. And then it was understood by both of them that that affair was settled. Whenever she was called on in future to speak of Lily, she always called her, \"that poor Miss Dale;\" but she never again spoke a word of reproach to her future lord about that little adventure. \"I shall tell mamma, to-night,\" she said to him, as she bade him good-night in some sequestered nook to which they had betaken themselves. Lady Julia's eye was again on them as they came out from the sequestered nook, but Alexandrina no longer cared for Lady Julia.\n\n\"George, I cannot quite understand about that Mr. Palliser. Isn't he to be a duke, and oughtn't he to be a lord now?\" This question was asked by Mrs. George De Courcy of her husband, when they found themselves together in the seclusion of the nuptial chamber.\n\n\"Yes; he'll be Duke of Omnium when the old fellow dies. I think he's one of the slowest fellows I ever came across. He'll take deuced good care of the property, though.\"\n\n\"But, George, do explain it to me. It is so stupid not to understand, and I am afraid of opening my mouth for fear of blundering.\"\n\n\"Then keep your mouth shut, my dear. You'll learn all those sort of things in time, and nobody notices it if you don't say anything.\"\n\n\"Yes, but, George;--I don't like to sit silent all the night. I'd sooner be up here with a novel if I can't speak about anything.\"\n\n\"Look at Lady Dumbello. She doesn't want to be always talking.\"\n\n\"Lady Dumbello is very different from me. But do tell me, who is Mr. Palliser?\"\n\n\"He's the duke's nephew. If he were the duke's son, he would be the Marquis of Silverbridge.\"\n\n\"And will he be plain Mister till his uncle dies?\"\n\n\"Yes, a very plain Mister.\"\n\n\"What a pity for him. But, George,--if I have a baby, and if he should be a boy, and if--\"\n\n\"Oh, nonsense; it will be time enough to talk of that when he comes. I'm going to sleep.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV. A MOTHER-IN-LAW AND A FATHER-IN-LAW.\n\nOn the following morning Mr. Plantagenet Palliser was off upon his political mission before breakfast;--either that, or else some private comfort was afforded to him in guise of solitary rolls and coffee. The public breakfast at Courcy Castle was going on at eleven o'clock, and at that hour Mr. Palliser was already closeted with the Mayor of Silverbridge.\n\n\"I must get off by the 3.45 train,\" said Mr. Palliser. \"Who is there to speak after me?\"\n\n\"Well, I shall say a few words; and Growdy,--he'll expect them to listen to him. Growdy has always stood very firm by his grace, Mr. Palliser.\"\n\n\"Mind we are in the room sharp at one. And you can have a fly, for me to get away to the station, ready in the yard. I won't go a moment before I can help. I shall be just an hour and a half myself. No, thank you, I never take any wine in the morning.\" And I may here state that Mr. Palliser did get away by the 3.45 train, leaving Mr. Growdy still talking on the platform. Constituents must be treated with respect; but time has become so scarce now-a-days that that respect has to be meted out by the quarter of an hour with parsimonious care.\n\nIn the meantime there was more leisure at Courcy Castle. Neither the countess nor Lady Alexandrina came down to breakfast, but their absence gave rise to no special remark. Breakfast at the castle was a morning meal at which people showed themselves, or did not show themselves, as it pleased them. Lady Julia was there looking very glum, and Crosbie was sitting next to his future sister-in-law Margaretta, who already had placed herself on terms of close affection with him. As he finished his tea she whispered into his ear, \"Mr. Crosbie, if you could spare half an hour, mamma would so like to see you in her own room.\" Crosbie declared that he would be delighted to wait upon her, and did in truth feel some gratitude in being welcomed as a son-in-law into the house. And yet he felt also that he was being caught, and that in ascending into the private domains of the countess he would be setting the seal upon his own captivity.\n\nNevertheless, he went with a smiling face and a light step, Lady Margaretta ushering him the way. \"Mamma,\" said she; \"I have brought Mr. Crosbie up to you. I did not know that you were here, Alexandrina, or I should have warned him.\"\n\nThe countess and her youngest daughter had been breakfasting together in the elder lady's sitting-room, and were now seated in a very graceful and well-arranged deshabille. The tea-cups out of which they had been drinking were made of some elegant porcelain, the teapot and cream-jug were of chased silver and as delicate in their way. The remnant of food consisted of morsels of French roll which had not even been allowed to crumble themselves in a disorderly fashion, and of infinitesimal pats of butter. If the morning meal of the two ladies had been as unsubstantial as the appearance of the fragments indicated, it must be presumed that they intended to lunch early. The countess herself was arrayed in an elaborate morning wrapper of figured silk, but the simple Alexandrina wore a plain white muslin peignoir, fastened with pink ribbon. Her hair, which she usually carried in long rolls, now hung loose over her shoulders, and certainly added something to her stock of female charms. The countess got up as Crosbie entered and greeted him with an open hand; but Alexandrina kept her seat, and merely nodded at him a little welcome. \"I must run down again,\" said Margaretta, \"or I shall have left Amelia with all the cares of the house upon her.\"\n\n\"Alexandrina has told me all about it,\" said the countess, with her sweetest smile; \"and I have given her my approval. I really do think you will suit each other very well.\"\n\n\"I am very much obliged to you,\" said Crosbie. \"I'm sure at any rate of this,--that she will suit me very well.\"\n\n\"Yes; I think she will. She is a good sensible girl.\"\n\n\"Psha, mamma; pray don't go on in that Goody Twoshoes sort of way.\"\n\n\"So you are, my dear. If you were not it would not be well for you to do as you are going to do. If you were giddy and harum-scarum, and devoted to rank and wealth and that sort of thing, it would not be well for you to marry a commoner without fortune. I'm sure Mr. Crosbie will excuse me for saying so much as that.\"\n\n\"Of course I know,\" said Crosbie, \"that I had no right to look so high.\"\n\n\"Well; we'll say nothing more about it,\" said the countess.\n\n\"Pray don't,\" said Alexandrina. \"It sounds so like a sermon.\"\n\n\"Sit down, Mr. Crosbie,\" said the countess, \"and let us have a little conversation. She shall sit by you, if you like it. Nonsense, Alexandrina,--if he asks it!\"\n\n\"Don't, mamma;--I mean to remain where I am.\"\n\n\"Very well, my dear;--then remain where you are. She is a wilful girl, Mr. Crosbie; as you will say when you hear that she has told me all that you told her last night.\" Upon hearing this, he changed colour a little, but said nothing. \"She has told me,\" continued the countess, \"about that young lady at Allington. Upon my word, I'm afraid you have been very naughty.\"\n\n\"I have been foolish, Lady De Courcy.\"\n\n\"Of course; I did not mean anything worse than that. Yes, you have been foolish;--amusing yourself in a thoughtless way, you know, and, perhaps, a little piqued because a certain lady was not to be won so easily as your Royal Highness wished. Well, now, all that must be settled, you know, as quickly as possible. I don't want to ask any indiscreet questions; but if the young lady has really been left with any idea that you meant anything, don't you think you should undeceive her at once?\"\n\n\"Of course he will, mamma.\"\n\n\"Of course you will; and it will be a great comfort to Alexandrina to know that the matter is arranged. You hear what Lady Julia is saying almost every hour of her life. Now, of course, Alexandrina does not care what an old maid like Lady Julia may say; but it will be better for all parties that the rumour should be put a stop to. If the earl were to hear it, he might, you know--\" And the countess shook her head, thinking that she could thus best indicate what the earl might do, if he were to take it into his head to do anything.\n\nCrosbie could not bring himself to hold any very confidential intercourse with the countess about Lily; but he gave a muttered assurance that he should, as a matter of course, make known the truth to Miss Dale with as little delay as possible. He could not say exactly when he would write, nor whether he would write to her or to her mother; but the thing should be done immediately on his return to town.\n\n\"If it will make the matter easier, I will write to Mrs. Dale,\" said the countess. But to this scheme Mr. Crosbie objected very strongly.\n\nAnd then a few words were said about the earl. \"I will tell him this afternoon,\" said the countess; \"and then you can see him to-morrow morning. I don't suppose he will say very much, you know; and perhaps he may think,--you won't mind my saying it, I'm sure,--that Alexandrina might have done better. But I don't believe that he'll raise any strong objection. There will be something about settlements, and that sort of thing, of course.\" Then the countess went away, and Alexandrina was left with her lover for half an hour. When the half-hour was over, he felt that he would have given all that he had in the world to have back the last four-and-twenty hours of his existence. But he had no hope. To jilt Lily Dale would, no doubt, be within his power, but he knew that he could not jilt Lady Alexandrina De Courcy.\n\nOn the next morning at twelve o'clock he had his interview with the father, and a very unpleasant interview it was. He was ushered into the earl's room, and found the great peer standing on the rug, with his back to the fire, and his hands in his breeches pockets.\n\n\"So you mean to marry my daughter?\" said he. \"I'm not very well, as you see; I seldom am.\"\n\nThese last words were spoken in answer to Crosbie's greeting. Crosbie had held out his hand to the earl, and had carried his point so far that the earl had been forced to take one of his own out of his pocket, and give it to his proposed son-in-law.\n\n\"If your lordship has no objection. I have, at any rate, her permission to ask for yours.\"\n\n\"I believe you have not any fortune, have you? She's got none; of course you know that?\"\n\n\"I have a few thousand pounds, and I believe she has as much.\"\n\n\"About as much as will buy bread to keep the two of you from starving. It's nothing to me. You can marry her if you like; only, look here, I'll have no nonsense. I've had an old woman in with me this morning,--one of those that are here in the house,--telling me some story about some other girl that you have made a fool of. It's nothing to me how much of that sort of thing you may have done, so that you do none of it here. But,--if you play any prank of that kind with me, you'll find that you've made a mistake.\"\n\nCrosbie hardly made any answer to this, but got himself out of the room as quickly as he could.\n\n\"You'd better talk to Gazebee about the trifle of money you've got,\" said the earl. Then he dismissed the subject from his mind, and no doubt imagined that he had fully done his duty by his daughter.\n\nOn the day after this, Crosbie was to go. On the last afternoon, shortly before dinner, he was waylaid by Lady Julia, who had passed the day in preparing traps to catch him.\n\n\"Mr. Crosbie,\" she said, \"let me have one word with you. Is this true?\"\n\n\"Lady Julia,\" he said, \"I really do not know why you should inquire into my private affairs.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir, you do know; you know very well. That poor young lady who has no father and no brother, is my neighbour, and her friends are my friends. She is a friend of my own, and being an old woman, I have a right to speak for her. If this is true, Mr. Crosbie, you are treating her like a villain.\"\n\n\"Lady Julia, I really must decline to discuss the matter with you.\"\n\n\"I'll tell everybody what a villain you are; I will, indeed;--a villain and a poor weak silly fool. She was too good for you; that's what she was.\" Crosbie, as Lady Julia was addressing to him the last words, hurried upstairs away from her, but her ladyship, standing on a landing-place, spoke up loudly, so that no word should be lost on her retreating enemy.\n\n\"We positively must get rid of that woman,\" the countess, who heard it all, said to Margaretta. \"She is disturbing the house and disgracing herself every day.\"\n\n\"She went to papa this morning, mamma.\"\n\n\"She did not get much by that move,\" said the countess.\n\nOn the following morning Crosbie returned to town, but just before he left the castle he received a third letter from Lily Dale. \"I have been rather disappointed at not hearing this morning,\" said Lily, \"for I thought the postman would have brought me a letter. But I know you'll be a better boy when you get back to London, and I won't scold you. Scold you, indeed! No; I'll never scold you, not though I shouldn't hear for a month.\"\n\nHe would have given all that he had in the world, three times told, if he could have blotted out that visit to Courcy Castle from the past facts of his existence.\n\n# CHAPTER XXV. ADOLPHUS CROSBIE SPENDS AN EVENING AT HIS CLUB.\n\nCrosbie, as he was being driven from the castle to the nearest station, in a dog-cart hired from the hotel, could not keep himself from thinking of that other morning, not yet a fortnight past, on which he had left Allington; and as he thought of it he knew that he was a villain. On this morning Alexandrina had not come out from the house to watch his departure, and catch the last glance of his receding figure. As he had not started very early she had sat with him at the breakfast-table; but others also had sat there, and when he got up to go, she did no more than smile softly and give him her hand. It had been already settled that he was to spend his Christmas at Courcy; as it had been also settled that he was to spend it at Allington.\n\nLady Amelia was, of all the family, the most affectionate to him, and perhaps of them all she was the one whose affection was worth the most. She was not a woman endowed with a very high mind or with very noble feelings. She had begun life trusting to the nobility of her blood for everything, and declaring somewhat loudly among her friends that her father's rank and her mother's birth imposed on her the duty of standing closely by her own order. Nevertheless, at the age of thirty-three she had married her father's man of business, under circumstances which were not altogether creditable to her. But she had done her duty in her new sphere of life with some constancy and a fixed purpose; and now that her sister was going to marry, as she had done, a man much below herself in social standing, she was prepared to do her duty as a sister and a sister-in-law.\n\n\"We shall be up in town in November, and of course you'll come to us at once. Albert Villa, you know, in Hamilton Terrace, St. John's Wood. We dine at seven, and on Sundays at two; and you'll always find a place. Mind you come to us, and make yourself quite at home. I do so hope you and Mortimer will get on well together.\"\n\n\"I'm sure we shall,\" said Crosbie. But he had had higher hopes in marrying into this noble family than that of becoming intimate with Mortimer Gazebee. What those hopes were he could hardly define to himself now that he had brought himself so near to the fruition of them. Lady De Courcy had certainly promised to write to her first cousin who was Under-Secretary of State for India, with reference to that secretaryship at the General Committee Office; but Crosbie, when he came to weigh in his mind what good might result to him from this, was disposed to think that his chance of obtaining the promotion would be quite as good without the interest of the Under-Secretary of State for India as with it. Now that he belonged, as we may say, to this noble family, he could hardly discern what were the advantages which he had expected from this alliance. He had said to himself that it would be much to have a countess for a mother-in-law; but now, even already, although the possession to which he had looked was not yet garnered, he was beginning to tell himself that the thing was not worth possessing.\n\nAs he sat in the train, with a newspaper in his hand, he went on acknowledging to himself that he was a villain. Lady Julia had spoken the truth to him on the stairs at Courcy, and so he confessed over and over again. But he was chiefly angry with himself for this,--that he had been a villain without gaining anything by his villany; that he had been a villain, and was to lose so much by his villany. He made comparison between Lily and Alexandrina, and owned to himself, over and over again, that Lily would make the best wife that a man could take to his bosom. As to Alexandrina, he knew the thinness of her character. She would stick by him, no doubt; and in a circuitous, discontented, unhappy way, would probably be true to her duties as a wife and mother. She would be nearly such another as Lady Amelia Gazebee. But was that a prize sufficiently rich to make him contented with his own prowess and skill in winning it? And was that a prize sufficiently rich to justify him to himself for his terrible villany? Lily Dale he had loved; and he now declared to himself that he could have continued to love her through his whole life. But what was there for any man to love in Alexandrina De Courcy?\n\nWhile resolving, during his first four or five days at the castle, that he would throw Lily Dale overboard, he had contrived to quiet his conscience by inward allusions to sundry heroes of romance. He had thought of Lothario, Don Juan, and of Lovelace; and had told himself that the world had ever been full of such heroes. And the world, too, had treated such heroes well; not punishing them at all as villains, but caressing them rather, and calling them curled darlings. Why should not he be a curled darling as well as another? Ladies had ever been fond of the Don Juan character, and Don Juan had generally been popular with men also. And then he named to himself a dozen modern Lotharios,--men who were holding their heads well above water, although it was known that they had played this lady false, and brought that other one to death's door, or perhaps even to death itself. War and love were alike, and the world was prepared to forgive any guile to militants in either camp.\n\nBut now that he had done the deed he found himself forced to look at it from quite another point of view. Suddenly that character of Lothario showed itself to him in a different light, and one in which it did not please him to look at it as belonging to himself. He began to feel that it would be almost impossible for him to write that letter to Lily, which it was absolutely necessary that he should write. He was in a position in which his mind would almost turn itself to thoughts of self-destruction as the only means of escape. A fortnight ago he was a happy man, having everything before him that a man ought to want; and now--now that he was the accepted son-in-law of an earl, and the confident expectant of high promotion--he was the most miserable, degraded wretch in the world!\n\nHe changed his clothes at his lodgings in Mount Street and went down to his club to dinner. He could, at any rate, do nothing that night. His letter to Allington must, no doubt, be written at once; but, as he could not send it before the next night's post, he was not forced to set to work upon it that evening. As he walked along Piccadilly on his way to St. James's Square, it occurred to him that it might be well to write a short line to Lily, telling her nothing of the truth,--a note written as though his engagement with her was still unbroken, but yet written with care, saying nothing about that engagement, so as to give him a little time. Then he thought that he would telegraph to Bernard and tell everything to him. Bernard would, of course, be prepared to avenge his cousin in some way, but for such vengeance Crosbie felt that he should care little. Lady Julia had told him that Lily was without father or brother, thereby accusing him of the basest cowardice. \"I wish she had a dozen brothers,\" he said to himself. But he hardly knew why he expressed such a wish.\n\nHe returned to London on the last day of October, and he found the streets at the West End nearly deserted. He thought, therefore, that he should be quite alone at his club, but as he entered the dinner room he saw one of his oldest and most intimate friends standing before the fire. Fowler Pratt was the man who had first brought him into Sebright's, and had given him almost his earliest start on his successful career in life. Since that time he and his friend Fowler Pratt had lived in close communion, though Pratt had always held a certain ascendancy in their friendship. He was in age a few years senior to Crosbie, and was in truth a man of better parts. But he was less ambitious, less desirous of shining in the world, and much less popular with men in general. He was possessed of a moderate private fortune on which he lived in a quiet, modest manner, and was unmarried, not likely to marry, inoffensive, useless, and prudent. For the first few years of Crosbie's life in London he had lived very much with his friend Pratt, and had been accustomed to depend much on his friend's counsel; but latterly, since he had himself become somewhat noticeable, he had found more pleasure in the society of such men as Dale, who were not his superiors either in age or wisdom. But there had been no coolness between him and Pratt, and now they met with perfect cordiality.\n\n\"I thought you were down in Barsetshire,\" said Pratt.\n\n\"And I thought you were in Switzerland.\"\n\n\"I have been in Switzerland,\" said Pratt.\n\n\"And I have been in Barsetshire,\" said Crosbie. Then they ordered their dinner together.\n\n\"And so you're going to be married?\" said Pratt, when the waiter had carried away the cheese.\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\n\"Well, but you are? Never mind who told me, if I was told the truth.\"\n\n\"But if it be not true?\"\n\n\"I have heard it for the last month,\" said Pratt, \"and it has been spoken of as a thing certain; and it is true; is it not?\"\n\n\"I believe it is,\" said Crosbie, slowly.\n\n\"Why, what on earth is the matter with you, that you speak of it in that way? Am I to congratulate you, or am I not? The lady, I'm told, is a cousin of Dale's.\"\n\nCrosbie had turned his chair from the table round to the fire, and said nothing in answer to this. He sat with his glass of sherry in his hand, looking at the coals, and thinking whether it would not be well that he should tell the whole story to Pratt. No one could give him better advice; and no one, as far as he knew his friend, would be less shocked at the telling of such a story. Pratt had no romance about women, and had never pretended to very high sentiments.\n\n\"Come up into the smoking-room and I'll tell you all about it,\" said Crosbie. So they went off together, and, as the smoking-room was untenanted, Crosbie was able to tell his story.\n\nHe found it very hard to tell;--much harder than he had beforehand fancied. \"I have got into terrible trouble,\" he began by saying. Then he told how he had fallen suddenly in love with Lily, how he had been rash and imprudent, how nice she was--\"infinitely too good for such a man as I am,\" he said;--how she had accepted him, and then how he had repented. \"I should have told you beforehand,\" he then said, \"that I was already half engaged to Lady Alexandrina De Courcy.\" The reader, however, will understand that this half-engagement was a fiction.\n\n\"And now you mean that you are altogether engaged to her?\"\n\n\"Exactly so.\"\n\n\"And that Miss Dale must be told that, on second thoughts, you have changed your mind?\"\n\n\"I know that I have behaved very badly,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Indeed you have,\" said his friend.\n\n\"It is one of those troubles in which a man finds himself involved almost before he knows where he is.\"\n\n\"Well; I can't look at it exactly in that light. A man may amuse himself with a girl, and I can understand his disappointing her and not offering to marry her,--though even that sort of thing isn't much to my taste. But, by George, to make an offer of marriage to such a girl as that in September, to live for a month in her family as her affianced husband, and then coolly go away to another house in October, and make an offer to another girl of higher rank--\"\n\n\"You know very well that that has had nothing to do with it.\"\n\n\"It looks very like it. And how are you going to communicate these tidings to Miss Dale?\"\n\n\"I don't know,\" said Crosbie, who was beginning to be very sore.\n\n\"And you have quite made up your mind that you'll stick to the earl's daughter?\"\n\nThe idea of jilting Alexandrina instead of Lily had never as yet presented itself to Crosbie, and now, as he thought of it, he could not perceive that it was feasible.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said, \"I shall marry Lady Alexandrina;--that is, if I do not cut the whole concern, and my own throat into the bargain.\"\n\n\"If I were in your shoes I think I should cut the whole concern. I could not stand it. What do you mean to say to Miss Dale's uncle?\"\n\n\"I don't care a ---- for Miss Dale's uncle,\" said Crosbie. \"If he were to walk in at that door this moment, I would tell him the whole story, without--\"\n\nAs he was yet speaking, one of the club servants opened the door of the smoking-room, and seeing Crosbie seated in a lounging-chair near the fire, went up to him with a gentleman's card. Crosbie took the card and read the name. \"Mr. Dale, Allington.\"\n\n\"The gentleman is in the waiting-room,\" said the servant.\n\nCrosbie for the moment was struck dumb. He had declared that very moment that he should feel no personal disinclination to meet Mr. Dale, and now that gentleman was within the walls of the club, waiting to see him!\n\n\"Who's that?\" asked Pratt. And then Crosbie handed him the card. \"Whew-w-w-hew,\" whistled Pratt.\n\n\"Did you tell the gentleman I was here?\" asked Crosbie.\n\n\"I said I thought you were upstairs, sir.\"\n\n\"That will do,\" said Pratt. \"The gentleman will no doubt wait for a minute.\" And then the servant went out of the room. \"Now, Crosbie, you must make up your mind. By one of these women and all her friends you will ever be regarded as a rascal, and they of course will look out to punish you with such punishment as may come to their hands. You must now choose which shall be the sufferer.\"\n\nThe man was a coward at heart. The reflection that he might, even now, at this moment, meet the old squire on pleasant terms,--or at any rate not on terms of defiance, pleaded more strongly in Lily's favour than had any other argument since Crosbie had first made up his mind to abandon her. He did not fear personal ill-usage;--he was not afraid lest he should be kicked or beaten; but he did not dare to face the just anger of the angry man.\n\n\"If I were you,\" said Pratt, \"I would not go down to that man at the present moment for a trifle.\"\n\n\"But what can I do?\"\n\n\"Shirk away out of the club. Only if you do that it seems to me that you'll have to go on shirking for the rest of your life.\"\n\n\"Pratt, I must say that I expected something more like friendship from you.\"\n\n\"What can I do for you? There are positions in which it is impossible to help a man. I tell you plainly that you have behaved very badly. I do not see that I can help you.\"\n\n\"Would you see him?\"\n\n\"Certainly not, if I am to be expected to take your part.\"\n\n\"Take any part you like,--only tell him the truth.\"\n\n\"And what is the truth?\"\n\n\"I was part engaged to that other girl before; and then, when I came to think of it, I knew that I was not fit to marry Miss Dale. I know I have behaved badly; but, Pratt, thousands have done the same thing before.\"\n\n\"I can only say that I have not been so unfortunate as to reckon any of those thousands among my friends.\"\n\n\"You mean to tell me, then, that you are going to turn your back on me?\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"I haven't said anything of the kind. I certainly won't undertake to defend you, for I don't see that your conduct admits of defence. I will see this gentleman if you wish it, and tell him anything that you may desire me to tell him.\"\n\nAt this moment the servant returned with a note for Crosbie. Mr. Dale had called for paper and envelope, and sent up to him the following missive:--\"Do you intend to come down to me? I know that you are in the house.\" \"For heaven's sake go to him,\" said Crosbie. \"He is well aware that I was deceived about his niece,--that I thought he was to give her some fortune. He knows all about that, and that when I learned from him that she was to have nothing--\"\n\n\"Upon my word, Crosbie, I wish you could find another messenger.\"\n\n\"Ah! you do not understand,\" said Crosbie in his agony. \"You think that I am inventing this plea about her fortune now. It isn't so. He will understand. We have talked all this over before, and he knew how terribly I was disappointed. Shall I wait for you here, or will you come to my lodgings? Or I will go down to the Beaufort, and will wait for you there.\" And it was finally arranged that he should get himself out of this club and wait at the other for Pratt's report of the interview.\n\n\"Do you go down first,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Yes: I had better,\" said Pratt. \"Otherwise you may be seen. Mr. Dale would have his eye upon you, and there would be a row in the house.\" There was a smile of sarcasm on Pratt's face as he spoke which angered Crosbie even in his misery, and made him long to tell his friend that he would not trouble him with this mission,--that he would manage his own affairs himself; but he was weakened and mentally humiliated by the sense of his own rascality, and had already lost the power of asserting himself, and of maintaining his ascendancy. He was beginning to recognize the fact that he had done that for which he must endure to be kicked, to be kicked morally if not materially; and that it was no longer possible for him to hold his head up without shame.\n\nPratt took Mr. Dale's note in his hand and went down into the stranger's room. There he found the squire standing, so that he could see through the open door of the room to the foot of the stairs down which Crosbie must descend before he could leave the club. As a measure of first precaution the ambassador closed the door; then he bowed to Mr. Dale, and asked him if he would take a chair.\n\n\"I wanted to see Mr. Crosbie,\" said the squire.\n\n\"I have your note to that gentleman in my hand,\" said he. \"He has thought it better that you should have this interview with me;--and under all the circumstances perhaps it is better.\"\n\n\"Is he such a coward that he dare not see me?\"\n\n\"There are some actions, Mr. Dale, that will make a coward of any man. My friend Crosbie is, I take it, brave enough in the ordinary sense of the word, but he has injured you.\"\n\n\"It is all true, then?\"\n\n\"Yes, Mr. Dale; I fear it is all true.\"\n\n\"And you call that man your friend! Mr.--; I don't know what your name is.\"\n\n\"Pratt;--Fowler Pratt. I have known Crosbie for fourteen years,--ever since he was a boy; and it is not my way, Mr. Dale, to throw over an old friend under any circumstances.\"\n\n\"Not if he committed a murder.\"\n\n\"No; not though he committed a murder.\"\n\n\"If what I hear is true, this man is worse than a murderer.\"\n\n\"Of course, Mr. Dale, I cannot know what you have heard. I believe that Mr. Crosbie has behaved very badly to your niece, Miss Dale; I believe that he was engaged to marry her, or, at any rate, that some such proposition had been made.\"\n\n\"Proposition! Why, sir, it was a thing so completely understood that everybody knew it in the county. It was so positively fixed that there was no secret about it. Upon my honour, Mr. Pratt, I can't as yet understand it. If I remember right, it's not a fortnight since he left my house at Allington,--not a fortnight. And that poor girl was with him on the morning of his going as his betrothed bride. Not a fortnight since! And now I've had a letter from an old family friend telling me that he is going to marry one of Lord De Courcy's daughters! I went instantly off to Courcy, and found that he had started for London. Now, I have followed him here; and you tell me it's all true.\"\n\n\"I am afraid it is, Mr. Dale; too true.\"\n\n\"I don't understand it; I don't, indeed. I cannot bring myself to believe that the man who was sitting the other day at my table should be so great a scoundrel. Did he mean it all the time that he was there?\"\n\n\"No; certainly not. Lady Alexandrina De Courcy was, I believe, an old friend of his;--with whom, perhaps, he had had some lover's quarrel. On his going to Courcy they made it up; and this is the result.\"\n\n\"And that is to be sufficient for my poor girl?\"\n\n\"You will, of course, understand that I am not defending Mr. Crosbie. The whole affair is very sad,--very sad, indeed. I can only say, in his excuse, that he is not the first man who has behaved badly to a lady.\"\n\n\"And that is his message to me, is it? And that is what I am to tell my niece? You have been deceived by a scoundrel. But what then? You are not the first! Mr. Pratt, I give you my word as a gentleman, I do not understand it. I have lived a good deal out of the world, and am, therefore, perhaps, more astonished than I ought to be.\"\n\n\"Mr. Dale, I feel for you--\"\n\n\"Feel for me! What is to become of my girl? And do you suppose that I will let this other marriage go on; that I will not tell the De Courcys, and all the world at large, what sort of a man this is;--that I will not get at him to punish him? Does he think that I will put up with this?\"\n\n\"I do not know what he thinks; I must only beg that you will not mix me up in the matter--as though I were a participator in his offence.\"\n\n\"Will you tell him from me that I desire to see him?\"\n\n\"I do not think that that would do any good.\"\n\n\"Never mind, sir; you have brought me his message; will you have the goodness now to take back mine to him?\"\n\n\"Do you mean at once--this evening,--now?\"\n\n\"Yes, at once--this evening,--now;--this minute.\"\n\n\"Ah; he has left the club; he is not here now; he went when I came to you.\"\n\n\"Then he is a coward as well as a scoundrel.\" In answer to which assertion, Mr. Fowler Pratt merely shrugged his shoulders.\n\n\"He is a coward as well as a scoundrel. Will you have the kindness to tell your friend from me that he is a coward and a scoundrel,--and a liar, sir.\"\n\n\"If it be so, Miss Dale is well quit of her engagement.\"\n\n\"That is your consolation, is it? That may be all very well now-a-days; but when I was a young man, I would sooner have burnt out my tongue than have spoken in such a way on such a subject. I would, indeed. Good-night, Mr. Pratt. Pray make your friend understand that he has not yet seen the last of the Dales; although, as you hint, the ladies of that family will no doubt have learned that he is not fit to associate with them.\" Then, taking up his hat, the squire made his way out of the club.\n\n\"I would not have done it,\" said Pratt to himself, \"for all the beauty, and all the wealth, and all the rank that ever were owned by a woman.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXVI. LORD DE COURCY IN THE BOSOM OF HIS FAMILY.\n\nLady Julia De Guest had not during her life written many letters to Mr. Dale of Allington, nor had she ever been very fond of him. But when she felt certain how things were going at Courcy, or rather, as we may say, how they had already gone, she took pen in hand, and sat herself to work, doing, as she conceived, her duty by her neighbour.\n\nMY DEAR MR. DALE [she said],\n\nI believe I need make no secret of having known that your niece Lilian is engaged to Mr. Crosbie, of London. I think it proper to warn you that if this be true Mr. Crosbie is behaving himself in a very improper manner here. I am not a person who concerns myself much in the affairs of other people; and under ordinary circumstances, the conduct of Mr. Crosbie would be nothing to me,--or, indeed, less than nothing; but I do to you as I would wish that others should do unto me. I believe it is only too true that Mr. Crosbie has proposed to Lady Alexandrina De Courcy, and been accepted by her. I think you will believe that I would not say this without warrant, and if there be anything in it, it may be well, for the poor young lady's sake, that you should put yourself in the way of learning the truth.\n\nBelieve me to be yours sincerely,\n\nCourcy Castle, Thursday.\n\nThe squire had never been very fond of any of the De Guest family, and had, perhaps, liked Lady Julia the least of them all. He was wont to call her a meddling old woman,--remembering her bitterness and pride in those now long bygone days in which the gallant major had run off with Lady Fanny. When he first received this letter, he did not, on the first reading of it, believe a word of its contents. \"Cross-grained old harridan,\" he said out loud to his nephew. \"Look what that aunt of yours has written to me.\" Bernard read the letter twice, and as he did so his face became hard and angry.\n\n\"You don't mean to say you believe it?\" said the squire.\n\n\"I don't think it will be safe to disregard it.\"\n\n\"What! you think it possible that your friend is doing as she says?\"\n\n\"It is certainly possible. He was angry when he found that Lily had no fortune.\"\n\n\"Heavens, Bernard! And you can speak of it in that way?\"\n\n\"I don't say that it is true; but I think we should look to it. I will go to Courcy Castle and learn the truth.\"\n\nThe squire at last decided that he would go. He went to Courcy Castle, and found that Crosbie had started two hours before his arrival. He asked for Lady Julia, and learned from her that Crosbie had actually left the house as the betrothed husband of Lady Alexandrina.\n\n\"The countess, I am sure, will not contradict it, if you will see her,\" said Lady Julia. But this the squire was unwilling to do. He would not proclaim the wretched condition of his niece more loudly than was necessary, and therefore he started on his pursuit of Crosbie. What was his success on that evening we have already learned.\n\nBoth Lady Alexandrina and her mother heard of Mr. Dale's arrival at the castle, but nothing was said between them on the subject. Lady Amelia Gazebee heard of it also, and she ventured to discuss the matter with her sister.\n\n\"You don't know exactly how far it went, do you?\"\n\n\"No; yes;--not exactly, that is,\" said Alexandrina.\n\n\"I suppose he did say something about marriage to the girl?\"\n\n\"Yes, I'm afraid he did.\"\n\n\"Dear, dear! It's very unfortunate. What sort of people are those Dales? I suppose he talked to you about them.\"\n\n\"No, he didn't; not very much. I daresay she is an artful, sly thing! It's a great pity men should go on in such a way.\"\n\n\"Yes, it is,\" said Lady Amelia. \"And I do suppose that in this case the blame has been more with him than with her. It's only right I should tell you that.\"\n\n\"But what can I do?\"\n\n\"I don't say you can do anything; but it's as well you should know.\"\n\n\"But I don't know, and you don't know; and I can't see that there is any use talking about it now. I knew him a long while before she did, and if she has allowed him to make a fool of her, it isn't my fault.\"\n\n\"Nobody says it is, my dear.\"\n\n\"But you seem to preach to me about it. What can I do for the girl? The fact is, he don't care for her a bit, and never did.\"\n\n\"Then he shouldn't have told her that he did.\"\n\n\"That's all very well, Amelia; but people don't always do exactly all that they ought to do. I suppose Mr. Crosbie isn't the first man that has proposed to two ladies. I dare say it was wrong, but I can't help it. As to Mr. Dale coming here with a tale of his niece's wrongs, I think it very absurd,--very absurd indeed. It makes it look as though there had been a scheme to catch Mr. Crosbie, and it's my belief that there was such a scheme.\"\n\n\"I only hope that there'll be no quarrel.\"\n\n\"Men don't fight duels now-a-days, Amelia.\"\n\n\"But do you remember what Frank Gresham did to Mr. Moffat when he behaved so badly to poor Augusta?\"\n\n\"Mr. Crosbie isn't afraid of that kind of thing. And I always thought that Frank was very wrong,--very wrong indeed. What's the good of two men beating each other in the street?\"\n\n\"Well; I'm sure I hope there'll be no quarrel. But I own I don't like the look of it. You see the uncle must have known all about it, and have consented to the marriage, or he would not have come here.\"\n\n\"I don't see that it can make any difference to me, Amelia.\"\n\n\"No, my dear, I don't see that it can. We shall be up in town soon, and I will see as much as possible of Mr. Crosbie. The marriage, I hope, will take place soon.\"\n\n\"He talks of February.\"\n\n\"Don't put it off, Alley, whatever you do. There are so many slips, you know, in these things.\"\n\n\"I'm not a bit afraid of that,\" said Alexandrina, sticking up her head.\n\n\"I daresay not; and you may be sure that we will keep an eye on him. Mortimer will get him up to dine with us as often as possible, and as his leave of absence is all over, he can't get out of town. He's to be here at Christmas, isn't he?\"\n\n\"Of course he is.\"\n\n\"Mind you keep him to that. And as to these Dales, I would be very careful, if I were you, not to say anything unkind of them to any one. It sounds badly in your position.\" And with this last piece of advice Lady Amelia Gazebee allowed the subject to drop.\n\nOn that day Lady Julia returned to her own home. Her adieux to the whole family at Courcy Castle were very cold, but about Mr. Crosbie and his lady-love at Allington she said no further word to any of them. Alexandrina did not show herself at all on the occasion, and indeed had not spoken to her enemy since that evening on which she had felt herself constrained to retreat from the drawing-room.\n\n\"Good-by,\" said the countess. \"You have been so good to come, and we have enjoyed it so much.\"\n\n\"I thank you very much. Good morning,\" said Lady Julia, with a stately courtesy.\n\n\"Pray remember me to your brother. I wish we could have seen him; I hope he has not been hurt by the--the bull.\" And then Lady Julia went her way.\n\n\"What a fool I have been to have that woman in the house,\" said the countess, before the door was closed behind her guest's back.\n\n\"Indeed you have,\" said Lady Julia, screaming back through the passage. Then there was a long silence, then a suppressed titter, and after that a loud laugh.\n\n\"Oh, mamma, what shall we do?\" said Lady Amelia.\n\n\"Do!\" said Margaretta; \"why should we do anything? She has heard the truth for once in her life.\"\n\n\"Dear Lady Dumbello, what will you think of us?\" said the countess, turning round to another guest, who was also just about to depart. \"Did any one ever know such a woman before?\"\n\n\"I think she's very nice,\" said Lady Dumbello, smiling.\n\n\"I can't quite agree with you there,\" said Lady Clandidlem. \"But I do believe she means to do her best. She is very charitable, and all that sort of thing.\"\n\n\"I'm sure I don't know,\" said Rosina. \"I asked her for a subscription to the mission for putting down the Papists in the west of Ireland, and she refused me point-blank.\"\n\n\"Now, my dear, if you're quite ready,\" said Lord Dumbello, coming into the room. Then there was another departure; but on this occasion the countess waited till the doors were shut, and the retreating footsteps were no longer heard. \"Have you observed,\" said she to Lady Clandidlem, \"that she has not held her head up since Mr. Palliser went away?\"\n\n\"Indeed I have,\" said Lady Clandidlem. \"As for poor Dumbello, he's the blindest creature I ever saw in my life.\"\n\n\"We shall hear of something before next May,\" said Lady De Courcy, shaking her head; \"but for all that she'll never be Duchess of Omnium.\"\n\n\"I wonder what your mamma will say of me when I go away to-morrow,\" said Lady Clandidlem to Margaretta, as they walked across the hall together.\n\n\"She won't say that you are going to run away with any gentleman,\" said Margaretta.\n\n\"At any rate not with the earl,\" said Lady Clandidlem. \"Ha, ha, ha! Well, we are all very good-natured, are we not? The best is that it means nothing.\"\n\nThus by degrees all the guests went, and the family of the De Courcys was left to the bliss of their own domestic circle. This, we may presume, was not without its charms, seeing that there were so many feelings in common between the mother and her children. There were drawbacks to it, no doubt, arising perhaps chiefly from the earl's bodily infirmities. \"When your father speaks to me,\" said Mrs. George to her husband, \"he puts me in such a shiver that I cannot open my mouth to answer him.\"\n\n\"You should stand up to him,\" said George. \"He can't hurt you, you know. Your money's your own; and if I'm ever to be the heir, it won't be by his doing.\"\n\n\"But he gnashes his teeth at me.\"\n\n\"You shouldn't care for that, if he don't bite. He used to gnash them at me; and when I had to ask him for money I didn't like it; but now I don't mind him a bit. He threw the peerage at me one day, but it didn't go within a yard of my head.\"\n\n\"If he throws anything at me, George, I shall drop upon the spot.\"\n\nBut the countess had a worse time with the earl than any of her children. It was necessary that she should see him daily, and necessary also that she should say much that he did not like to hear, and make many petitions that caused him to gnash his teeth. The earl was one of those men who could not endure to live otherwise than expensively, and yet was made miserable by every recurring expense. He ought to have known by this time that butchers, and bakers, and corn-chandlers, and coal-merchants will not supply their goods for nothing; and yet it always seemed as though he had expected that at this special period they would do so. He was an embarrassed man, no doubt, and had not been fortunate in his speculations at Newmarket or Homburg; but, nevertheless, he had still the means of living without daily torment; and it must be supposed that his self-imposed sufferings, with regard to money, rose rather from his disposition than his necessities. His wife never knew whether he were really ruined, or simply pretending it. She had now become so used to her position in this respect, that she did not allow fiscal considerations to mar her happiness. Food and clothing had always come to her,--including velvet gowns, new trinkets, and a man-cook,--and she presumed that they would continue to come. But that daily conference with her husband was almost too much for her. She struggled to avoid it; and, as far as the ways and means were concerned, would have allowed them to arrange themselves, if he would only have permitted it. But he insisted on seeing her daily in his own sitting-room; and she had acknowledged to her favourite daughter, Margaretta, that those half-hours would soon be the death of her. \"I sometimes feel,\" she said, \"that I am going mad before I can get out.\" And she reproached herself, probably without reason, in that she had brought much of this upon herself. In former days the earl had been constantly away from home, and the countess had complained. Like many other women she had not known when she was well off. She had complained, urging upon her lord that he should devote more of his time to his own hearth. It is probable that her ladyship's remonstrances had been less efficacious than the state of his own health in producing that domestic constancy which he now practised; but it is certain that she looked back with bitter regret to the happy days when she was deserted, jealous, and querulous. \"Don't you wish we could get Sir Omicron to order him to the German Spas?\" she had said to Margaretta. Now Sir Omicron was the great London physician, and might, no doubt, do much in that way.\n\nBut no such happy order had as yet been given; and, as far as the family could foresee, paterfamilias intended to pass the winter with them at Courcy. The guests, as I have said, were all gone, and none but the family were in the house when her ladyship waited upon her lord one morning at twelve o'clock, a few days after Mr. Dale's visit to the castle. He always breakfasted alone, and after breakfast found in a French novel and a cigar what solace those innocent recreations were still able to afford him. When the novel no longer excited him and when he was saturated with smoke, he would send for his wife. After that, his valet would dress him. \"She gets it worse than I do,\" the man declared in the servants' hall; \"and minds it a deal more. I can give warning, and she can't.\"\n\n\"Better? No, I ain't better,\" the husband said, in answer to his wife's inquiries. \"I never shall be better while you keep that cook in the kitchen.\"\n\n\"But where are we to get another if we send him away?\"\n\n\"It's not my business to find cooks. I don't know where you're to get one. It's my belief you won't have a cook at all before long. It seems you have got two extra men into the house without telling me.\"\n\n\"We must have servants, you know, when there is company. It wouldn't do to have Lady Dumbello here, and no one to wait on her.\"\n\n\"Who asked Lady Dumbello? I didn't.\"\n\n\"I'm sure, my dear, you liked having her here.\"\n\n\"D---- Lady Dumbello!\" and then there was a pause. The countess had no objection whatsoever to the above proposition, and was rejoiced that that question of the servants was allowed to slip aside, through the aid of her ladyship.\n\n\"Look at that letter from Porlock,\" said the earl; and he pushed over to the unhappy mother a letter from her eldest son. Of all her children he was the one she loved the best; but him she was never allowed to see under her own roof. \"I sometimes think that he is the greatest rascal with whom I ever had occasion to concern myself,\" said the earl.\n\nShe took the letter and read it. The epistle was certainly not one which a father could receive with pleasure from his son; but the disagreeable nature of its contents was the fault rather of the parent than of the child. The writer intimated that certain money due to him had not been paid with necessary punctuality, and that unless he received it, he should instruct his lawyer to take some authorized legal proceedings. Lord De Courcy had raised certain moneys on the family property, which he could not have raised without the co-operation of his heir, and had bound himself, in return for that co-operation, to pay a certain fixed income to his eldest son. This he regarded as an allowance from himself; but Lord Porlock regarded it as his own, by lawful claim. The son had not worded his letter with any affectionate phraseology. \"Lord Porlock begs to inform Lord De Courcy--\" Such had been the commencement.\n\n\"I suppose he must have his money; else how can he live?\" said the countess, trembling.\n\n\"Live!\" shouted the earl. \"And so you think it proper that he should write such a letter as that to his father!\"\n\n\"It is all very unfortunate,\" she replied.\n\n\"I don't know where the money's to come from. As for him, if he were starving, it would serve him right. He's a disgrace to the name and the family. From all I hear, he won't live long.\"\n\n\"Oh, De Courcy, don't talk of it in that way!\"\n\n\"What way am I to talk of it? If I say that he's my greatest comfort, and living as becomes a nobleman, and is a fine healthy man of his age, with a good wife and a lot of legitimate children, will that make you believe it? Women are such fools. Nothing that I say will make him worse than he is.\"\n\n\"But he may reform.\"\n\n\"Reform! He's over forty, and when I last saw him he looked nearly sixty. There;--you may answer his letter; I won't.\"\n\n\"And about the money?\"\n\n\"Why doesn't he write to Gazebee about his dirty money? Why does he trouble me? I haven't got his money. Ask Gazebee about his money. I won't trouble myself about it.\" Then there was another pause, during which the countess folded the letter, and put it in her pocket.\n\n\"How long is George going to remain here with that woman?\" he asked.\n\n\"I'm sure she is very harmless,\" pleaded the countess.\n\n\"I always think when I see her that I'm sitting down to dinner with my own housemaid. I never saw such a woman. How he can put up with it! But I don't suppose he cares for anything.\"\n\n\"It has made him very steady.\"\n\n\"Steady!\"\n\n\"And as she will be confined before long it may be as well that she should remain here. If Porlock doesn't marry, you know--\"\n\n\"And so he means to live here altogether, does he? I'll tell you what it is,--I won't have it. He's better able to keep a house over his own head and his wife's than I am to do it for them, and so you may tell them. I won't have it. D'ye hear?\" Then there was another short pause. \"D'ye hear?\" he shouted at her.\n\n\"Yes; of course I hear. I was only thinking you wouldn't wish me to turn them out, just as her confinement is coming on.\"\n\n\"I know what that means. Then they'd never go. I won't have it; and if you don't tell them I will.\" In answer to this Lady De Courcy promised that she would tell them, thinking perhaps that the earl's mode of telling might not be beneficial in that particular epoch which was now coming in the life of Mrs. George.\n\n\"Did you know,\" said he, breaking out on a new subject, \"that a man had been here named Dale, calling on somebody in this house?\" In answer to which the countess acknowledged that she had known it.\n\n\"Then why did you keep it from me?\" And that gnashing of the teeth took place which was so specially objectionable to Mrs. George.\n\n\"It was a matter of no moment. He came to see Lady Julia De Guest.\"\n\n\"Yes; but he came about that man Crosbie.\"\n\n\"I suppose he did.\"\n\n\"Why have you let that girl be such a fool? You'll find he'll play her some knave's trick.\"\n\n\"Oh dear, no.\"\n\n\"And why should she want to marry such a man as that?\"\n\n\"He's quite a gentleman, you know, and very much thought of in the world. It won't be at all bad for her, poor thing. It is so very hard for a girl to get married now-a-days without money.\"\n\n\"And so they're to take up with anybody. As far as I can see, this is a worse affair than that of Amelia.\"\n\n\"Amelia has done very well, my dear.\"\n\n\"Oh, if you call it doing well for your girls, I don't. I call it doing uncommon badly; about as bad as they well can do. But it's your affair. I have never meddled with them, and don't intend to do it now.\"\n\n\"I really think she'll be happy, and she is devotedly attached to the young man.\"\n\n\"Devotedly attached to the young man!\" The tone and manner in which the earl repeated these words were such as to warrant an opinion that his lordship might have done very well on the stage had his attention been called to that profession. \"It makes me sick to hear people talk in that way. She wants to get married, and she's a fool for her pains;--I can't help that; only remember that I'll have no nonsense here about that other girl. If he gives me trouble of that sort, by ----, I'll be the death of him. When is the marriage to be?\"\n\n\"They talk of February.\"\n\n\"I won't have any tomfoolery and expense. If she chooses to marry a clerk in an office, she shall marry him as clerks are married.\"\n\n\"He'll be the secretary before that, De Courcy.\"\n\n\"What difference does that make? Secretary, indeed! What sort of men do you suppose secretaries are? A beggar that came from nobody knows where! I won't have any tomfoolery;--d'ye hear?\" Whereupon the countess said that she did hear, and soon afterwards managed to escape. The valet then took his turn; and repeated, after his hour of service, that \"Old Nick\" in his tantrums had been more like the Prince of Darkness than ever.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVII. \"ON MY HONOUR, I DO NOT UNDERSTAND IT.\"\n\nIn the meantime Lady Alexandrina endeavoured to realize to herself all the advantages and disadvantages of her own position. She was not possessed of strong affections, nor of depth of character, nor of high purpose; but she was no fool, nor was she devoid of principle. She had asked herself many times whether her present life was so happy as to make her think that a permanent continuance in it would suffice for her desires, and she had always replied to herself that she would fain change to some other life if it were possible. She had also questioned herself as to her rank, of which she was quite sufficiently proud, and had told herself that she could not degrade herself in the world without a heavy pang. But she had at last taught herself to believe that she had more to gain by becoming the wife of such a man as Crosbie than by remaining as an unmarried daughter of her father's house. There was much in her sister Amelia's position which she did not envy, but there was less to envy in that of her sister Rosina. The Gazebee house in St. John's Wood Road was not so magnificent as Courcy Castle; but then it was less dull, less embittered by torment, and was moreover her sister's own.\n\n\"Very many do marry commoners,\" she had said to Margaretta.\n\n\"Oh, yes, of course. It makes a difference, you know, when a man has a fortune.\"\n\nOf course it did make a difference. Crosbie had no fortune, was not even so rich as Mr. Gazebee, could keep no carriage, and would have no country house. But then he was a man of fashion, was more thought of in the world than Mr. Gazebee, might probably rise in his own profession,--and was at any rate thoroughly presentable. She would have preferred a gentleman with £5,000 a year; but then as no gentleman with £5,000 a year came that way, would she not be happier with Mr. Crosbie than she would be with no husband at all? She was not very much in love with Mr. Crosbie, but she thought that she could live with him comfortably, and that on the whole it would be a good thing to be married.\n\nAnd she made certain resolves as to the manner in which she would do her duty by her husband. Her sister Amelia was paramount in her own house, ruling indeed with a moderate, endurable dominion, and ruling much to her husband's advantage. Alexandrina feared that she would not be allowed to rule, but she could at any rate try. She would do all in her power to make him comfortable, and would be specially careful not to irritate him by any insistence on her own higher rank. She would be very meek in this respect; and if children should come she would be as painstaking about them as though her own father had been merely a clergyman or a lawyer. She thought also much about poor Lilian Dale, asking herself sundry questions, with an idea of being high-principled as to her duty in that respect. Was she wrong in taking Mr. Crosbie away from Lilian Dale? In answer to these questions she was able to assure herself comfortably that she was not wrong. Mr. Crosbie would not, under any circumstances, marry Lilian Dale. He had told her so more than once, and that in a solemn way. She could therefore be doing no harm to Lilian Dale. If she entertained any inner feeling that Crosbie's fault in jilting Lilian Dale was less than it would have been had she herself not been an earl's daughter,--that her own rank did in some degree extenuate her lover's falseness,--she did not express it in words even to herself.\n\nShe did not get very much sympathy from her own family. \"I'm afraid he does not think much of his religious duties. I'm told that young men of that sort seldom do,\" said Rosina. \"I don't say you're wrong,\" said Margaretta. \"By no means. Indeed I think less of it now than I did when Amelia did the same thing. I shouldn't do it myself, that's all.\" Her father told her that he supposed she knew her own mind. Her mother, who endeavoured to comfort and in some sort to congratulate her, nevertheless, harped constantly on the fact that she was marrying a man without rank and without a fortune. Her congratulations were apologetic, and her comfortings took the guise of consolation. \"Of course you won't be rich, my dear; but I really think you'll do very well. Mr. Crosbie may be received anywhere, and you never need be ashamed of him.\" By which the countess implied that her elder married daughter was occasionally called on to be ashamed of her husband. \"I wish he could keep a carriage for you, but perhaps that will come some day.\" Upon the whole Alexandrina did not repent, and stoutly told her father that she did know her own mind.\n\nDuring all this time Lily Dale was as yet perfect in her happiness. That delay of a day or two in the receipt of the expected letter from her lover had not disquieted her. She had promised him that she would not distrust him, and she was firmly minded to keep her promises. Indeed no idea of breaking it came to her at this time. She was disappointed when the postman would come and bring no letter for her,--disappointed, as is the husbandman when the longed-for rain does not come to refresh the parched earth; but she was in no degree angry. \"He will explain it,\" she said to herself. And she assured Bell that men never recognized the hunger and thirst after letters which women feel when away from those whom they love.\n\nThen they heard at the Small House that the squire had gone away from Allington. During the last few days Bernard had not been much with them, and now they heard the news, not through their cousin, but from Hopkins. \"I really can't undertake to say, Miss Bell, where the master's gone to. It's not likely the master'd tell me where he was going to; not unless it was about seeds, or the likes of that.\"\n\n\"He has gone very suddenly,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Well, miss, I've nothing to say to that. And why shouldn't he go sudden if he likes? I only know he had his gig, and went to the station. If you was to bury me alive I couldn't tell you more.\"\n\n\"I should like to try,\" said Lily as they walked away. \"He is such a cross old thing. I wonder whether Bernard has gone with my uncle.\" And then they thought no more about it.\n\nOn the day after that Bernard came down to the Small House, but he said nothing by way of accounting for the squire's absence. \"He is in London, I know,\" said Bernard.\n\n\"I hope he'll call on Mr. Crosbie,\" said Lily. But on this subject Bernard said not a word. He did ask Lily whether she had heard from Adolphus, in answer to which she replied, with as indifferent a voice as she could assume, that she had not had a letter that morning.\n\n\"I shall be angry with him if he's not a good correspondent,\" said Mrs. Dale, when she and Lily were alone together.\n\n\"No, mamma, you mustn't be angry with him. I won't let you be angry with him. Please to remember he's my lover and not yours.\"\n\n\"But I can see you when you watch for the postman.\"\n\n\"I won't watch for the postman any more if it makes you have bad thoughts about him. Yes, they are bad thoughts. I won't have you think that he doesn't do everything that is right.\"\n\nOn the next morning the postman brought a letter, or rather a note, and Lily at once saw that it was from Crosbie. She had contrived to intercept it near the back door, at which the postman called, so that her mother should not watch her watchings, nor see her disappointment if none should come. \"Thank you, Jane,\" she said, very calmly, when the eager, kindly girl ran to her with the little missive; and she walked off to some solitude, trying to hide her impatience. The note had seemed so small that it amazed her; but when she opened it the contents amazed her more. There was neither beginning nor end. There was no appellation of love, and no signature. It contained but two lines. \"I will write to you at length to-morrow. This is my first day in London, and I have been so driven about that I cannot write.\" That was all, and it was scrawled on half a sheet of note-paper. Why, at any rate, had he not called her his dearest Lily? Why had he not assured her that he was ever her own? Such expressions, meaning so much, may be conveyed in a glance of the pen. \"Ah,\" she said, \"if he knew how I hunger and thirst after his love!\"\n\nShe had but a moment left to her before she must join her mother and sister, and she used that moment in remembering her promise. \"I know it is all right,\" she said to herself. \"He does not think of these things as I do. He had to write at the last moment,--as he was leaving his office.\" And then with a quiet, smiling face, she walked into the breakfast-parlour.\n\n\"What does he say, Lily?\" asked Bell.\n\n\"What would you give to know?\" said Lily.\n\n\"I wouldn't give twopence for the whole of it,\" said Bell.\n\n\"When you get anybody to write to you letters, I wonder whether you'll show them to everybody?\"\n\n\"But if there's any special London news, I suppose we might hear it,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"But suppose there's no special London news, mamma. The poor man had only been in town one day, you know: and there never is any news at this time of the year.\"\n\n\"Had he seen uncle Christopher?\"\n\n\"I don't think he had; but he doesn't say. We shall get all the news from him when he comes. He cares much more about London news than Adolphus does.\" And then there was no more said about the letter.\n\nBut Lily had read her two former letters over and over again at the breakfast-table; and though she had not read them aloud, she had repeated many words out of them, and had so annotated upon them that her mother, who had heard her, could have almost re-written them. Now, she did not even show the paper; and then her absence, during which she had read the letter, had hardly exceeded a minute or two. All this Mrs. Dale observed, and she knew that her daughter had been again disappointed.\n\nIn fact that day Lily was very serious, but she did not appear to be unhappy. Early after breakfast Bell went over to the parsonage, and Mrs. Dale and her youngest daughter sat together over their work. \"Mamma,\" she said, \"I hope you and I are not to be divided when I go to live in London.\"\n\n\"We shall never be divided in heart, my love.\"\n\n\"Ah, but that will not be enough for happiness, though perhaps enough to prevent absolute unhappiness. I shall want to see you, touch you, and pet you as I do now.\" And she came and knelt on the cushion at her mother's feet.\n\n\"You will have some one else to caress and pet,--perhaps many others.\"\n\n\"Do you mean to say that you are going to throw me off, mamma?\"\n\n\"God forbid, my darling. It is not mothers that throw off their children. What shall I have left when you and Bell are gone from me?\"\n\n\"But we will never be gone. That's what I mean. We are to be just the same to you always, even though we are married. I must have my right to be here as much as I have it now; and, in return, you shall have your right to be there. His house must be a home to you,--not a cold place which you may visit now and again, with your best clothes on. You know what I mean, when I say that we must not be divided.\"\n\n\"But Lily--\"\n\n\"Well, mamma?\"\n\n\"I have no doubt we shall be happy together,--you and I.\"\n\n\"But you were going to say more than that.\"\n\n\"Only this,--that your house will be his house, and will be full without me. A daughter's marriage is always a painful parting.\"\n\n\"Is it, mamma?\"\n\n\"Not that I would have it otherwise than it is. Do not think that I would wish to keep you at home with me. Of course you will both marry and leave me. I hope that he to whom you are going to devote yourself may be spared to love you and protect you.\" Then the widow's heart became too full, and she put away her child from her that she might hide her face.\n\n\"Mamma, mamma, I wish I was not going from you.\"\n\n\"No, Lily; do not say that. I should not be contented with life if I did not see both my girls married. I think that it is the only lot which can give to a woman perfect content and satisfaction. I would have you both married. I should be the most selfish being alive if I wished otherwise.\"\n\n\"Bell will settle herself near you, and then you will see more of her and love her better than you do me.\"\n\n\"I shall not love her better.\"\n\n\"I wish she would marry some London man, and then you would come with us, and be near to us. Do you know, mamma, I sometimes think you don't like this place here.\"\n\n\"Your uncle has been very kind to give it to us.\"\n\n\"I know he has; and we have been very happy here. But if Bell should leave you--\"\n\n\"Then should I go also. Your uncle has been very kind, but I sometimes feel that his kindness is a burden which I should not be strong enough to bear solely on my own shoulders. And what should keep me here, then?\" Mrs. Dale as she said this felt that the \"here\" of which she spoke extended beyond the limits of the home which she held through the charity of her brother-in-law. Might not all the world, as far as she was concerned in it, be contained in that \"here\"? How was she to live if both her children should be taken away from her? She had already realized the fact that Crosbie's house could never be a home to her,--never even a temporary home. Her visits there must be of that full-dressed nature to which Lily had alluded. It was impossible that she could explain this to Lily. She would not prophesy that the hero of her girl's heart would be inhospitable to his wife's mother; but such had been her reading of Crosbie's character. Alas, alas, as matters were to go, his hospitality or inhospitality would be matter of small moment to them.\n\nAgain in the afternoon the two sisters were together, and Lily was still more serious than her wont. It might almost have been gathered from her manner that this marriage of hers was about to take place at once, and that she was preparing to leave her home. \"Bell,\" she said, \"I wonder why Dr. Crofts never comes to see us now?\"\n\n\"It isn't a month since he was here, at our party.\"\n\n\"A month! But there was a time when he made some pretext for being here every other day.\"\n\n\"Yes, when mamma was ill.\"\n\n\"Ay, and since mamma was well, too. But I suppose I must not break the promise you made me give you. He's not to be talked about even yet, is he?\"\n\n\"I didn't say he was not to be talked about. You know what I meant, Lily; and what I meant then, I mean now.\"\n\n\"And how long will it be before you mean something else? I do hope it will come some day,--I do indeed.\"\n\n\"It never will, Lily. I once fancied that I cared for Dr. Crofts, but it was only fancy. I know it, because--\" She was going to explain that her knowledge on that point was assured to her, because since that day she had felt that she might have learned to love another man. But that other man had been Mr. Crosbie, and so she stopped herself.\n\n\"I wish he would come and ask you himself.\"\n\n\"He will never do so. He would never ask such a question without encouragement, and I shall give him none. Nor will he ever think of marrying till he can do so without,--without what he thinks to be imprudence as regards money. He has courage enough to be poor himself without unhappiness, but he has not courage to endure poverty with a wife. I know well what his feelings are.\"\n\n\"Well, we shall see,\" said Lily. \"I shouldn't wonder if you were married first now, Bell. For my part I'm quite prepared to wait for three years.\"\n\nLate on that evening the squire returned to Allington, Bernard having driven over to meet him at the station. He had telegraphed to his nephew that he would be back by a late train, and no more than this had been heard from him since he went. On that day Bernard had seen none of the ladies at the Small House. With Bell at the present moment it was impossible that he should be on easy terms. He could not meet her alone without recurring to the one special subject of interest between them, and as to that he did not choose to speak without much forethought. He had not known himself, when he had gone about his wooing so lightly, thinking it a slight thing, whether or no he might be accepted. Now it was no longer a slight thing to him. I do not know that it was love that made him so eager; not good, honest, downright love. But he had set his heart upon the object, and with the wilfulness of a Dale was determined that it should be his. He had no remotest idea of giving up his cousin, but he had at last persuaded himself that she was not to be won without some toil, and perhaps also some delay.\n\nNor had he been in a humour to talk either to Mrs. Dale or to Lily. He feared that Lady Julia's news was true,--that at any rate there might be in it something of truth; and while thus in doubt he could not go down to the Small House. So he hung about the place by himself, with a cigar in his mouth, fearing that something evil was going to happen, and when the message came for him, almost shuddered as he seated himself in the gig. What would it become him to do in this emergency if Crosbie had truly been guilty of the villany with which Lady Julia had charged him? Thirty years ago he would have called the man out, and shot at him till one of them was hit. Now-a-days it was hardly possible for a man to do that; and yet what would the world say of him if he allowed such an injury as this to pass without vengeance?\n\nHis uncle, as he came forth from the station with his travelling-bag in his hand, was stern, gloomy, and silent. He came out and took his place in the gig almost without speaking. There were strangers about, and therefore his nephew at first could ask no question, but as the gig turned the corner out of the station-house yard he demanded the news.\n\n\"What have you heard?\" he said.\n\nBut even then the squire did not answer at once. He shook his head, and turned away his face, as though he did not choose to be interrogated.\n\n\"Have you seen him, sir?\" asked Bernard.\n\n\"No, he has not dared to see me.\"\n\n\"Then it is true?\"\n\n\"True?--yes, it is all true. Why did you bring the scoundrel here? It has been your fault.\"\n\n\"No, sir; I must contradict that. I did not know him for a scoundrel.\"\n\n\"But it was your duty to have known him before you brought him here among them. Poor girl! how is she to be told?\"\n\n\"Then she does not know it?\"\n\n\"I fear not. Have you seen them?\"\n\n\"I saw them yesterday, and she did not know it then; she may have heard it to-day.\"\n\n\"I don't think so. I believe he has been too great a coward to write to her. A coward indeed! How can any man find the courage to write such a letter as that?\"\n\nBy degrees the squire told his tale. How he had gone to Lady Julia, had made his way to London, had tracked Crosbie to his club, and had there learned the whole truth from Crosbie's friend, Fowler Pratt, we already know. \"The coward escaped me while I was talking to the man he sent down,\" said the squire. \"It was a concerted plan, and I think he was right. I should have brained him in the hall of the club.\" On the following morning Pratt had called upon him at his inn with Crosbie's apology. \"His apology!\" said the squire. \"I have it in my pocket. Poor reptile; wretched worm of a man! I cannot understand it. On my honour, Bernard, I do not understand it. I think men are changed since I knew much of them. It would have been impossible for me to write such a letter as that.\" He went on telling how Pratt had brought him this letter, and had stated that Crosbie declined an interview. \"The gentleman had the goodness to assure me that no good could come from such a meeting. 'You mean,' I answered, 'that I cannot touch pitch and not be defiled!' He acknowledged that the man was pitch. Indeed, he could not say a word for his friend.\"\n\n\"I know Pratt. He is a gentleman. I am sure he would not excuse him.\"\n\n\"Excuse him! How could any one excuse him? Words could not be found to excuse him.\" And then he sat silent for some half mile. \"On my honour, Bernard, I can hardly yet bring myself to believe it. It is so new to me. It makes me feel that the world is changed, and that it is no longer worth a man's while to live in it.\"\n\n\"And he is engaged to this other girl?\"\n\n\"Oh, yes; with the full consent of the family. It is all arranged, and the settlements, no doubt, in the lawyer's hands by this time. He must have gone away from here determined to throw her over. Indeed, I don't suppose he ever meant to marry her. He was just passing away his time here in the country.\"\n\n\"He meant it up to the time of his leaving.\"\n\n\"I don't think it. Had he found me able and willing to give her a fortune he might, perhaps, have married her. But I don't think he meant it for a moment after I told him that she would have nothing. Well, here we are. I may truly say that I never before came back to my own house with so sore a heart.\"\n\nThey sat silently over their supper, the squire showing more open sorrow than might have been expected from his character. \"What am I to say to them in the morning?\" he repeated over and over again. \"How am I to do it? And if I tell the mother, how is she to tell her child?\"\n\n\"Do you think that he has given no intimation of his purpose?\"\n\n\"As far as I can tell, none. That man Pratt knew that he had not done so yesterday afternoon. I asked him what were the intentions of his blackguard friend, and he said that he did not know--that Crosbie would probably have written to me. Then he brought me this letter. There it is,\" and the squire threw the letter over the table; \"read it and let me have it back. He thinks probably that the trouble is now over as far as he is concerned.\"\n\nIt was a vile letter to have written--not because the language was bad, or the mode of expression unfeeling, or the facts falsely stated--but because the thing to be told was in itself so vile. There are deeds which will not bear a gloss--sins as to which the perpetrator cannot speak otherwise than as a reptile; circumstances which change a man and put upon him the worthlessness of vermin. Crosbie had struggled hard to write it, going home to do it after his last interview on that night with Pratt. But he had sat moodily in his chair at his lodgings, unable to take the pen in his hand. Pratt was to come to him at his office on the following morning, and he went to bed resolving that he would write it at his desk. On the next day Pratt was there before a word of it had been written.\n\n\"I can't stand this kind of thing,\" said Pratt. \"If you mean me to take it, you must write it at once.\" Then, with inward groaning, Crosbie sat himself at his table, and the words at last were forthcoming. Such words as they were! \"I know that I can have no excuse to make to you--or to her. But, circumstanced as I now am, the truth is the best. I feel that I should not make Miss Dale happy; and, therefore, as an honest man, I think I best do my duty by relinquishing the honour which she and you had proposed for me.\" There was more of it, but we all know of what words such letters are composed, and how men write when they feel themselves constrained to write as reptiles.\n\n\"As an honest man!\" repeated the squire. \"On my honour, Bernard, as a gentleman, I do not understand it. I cannot believe it possible that the man who wrote that letter was sitting the other day as a guest at my table.\"\n\n\"What are we to do to him?\" said Bernard, after a while.\n\n\"Treat him as you would a rat. Throw your stick at him, if he comes under your feet; but beware, above all things, that he does not get into your house. That is too late for us now.\"\n\n\"There must be more than that, uncle.\"\n\n\"I don't know what more. There are deeds for committing which a man is doubly damned, because he has screened himself from overt punishment by the nature of his own villany. We have to remember Lily's name, and do what may best tend to her comfort. Poor girl! poor girl!\"\n\nThen they were silent, till the squire rose and took his bed candle. \"Bernard,\" he said, \"let my sister-in-law know early to-morrow that I will see her here, if she will be good enough to come to me after breakfast. Do not have anything else said at the Small House. It may be that he has written to-day.\"\n\nThen the squire went to bed, and Bernard sat over the dining-room fire, meditating on it all. How would the world expect that he should behave to Crosbie? and what should he do when he met Crosbie at the club?\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII. THE BOARD.\n\nCrosbie, as we already know, went to his office in Whitehall on the morning after his escape from Sebright's, at which establishment he left the Squire of Allington in conference with Fowler Pratt. He had seen Fowler Pratt again that same night, and the course of the story will have shown what took place at that interview.\n\nHe went early to his office, knowing that he had before him the work of writing two letters, neither of which would run very glibly from his pen. One was to be his missive to the squire, to be delivered by his friend; the other, that fatal epistle to poor Lily, which, as the day passed away, he found himself utterly unable to accomplish. The letter to the squire he did write, under certain threats; and, as we have seen, was considered to have degraded himself to the vermin rank of humanity by the meanness of his production.\n\nBut on reaching his office he found that other cares awaited him,--cares which he would have taken much delight in bearing, had the state of his mind enabled him to take delight in anything. On entering the lobby of his office, at ten o'clock, he became aware that he was received by the messengers assembled there with almost more than their usual deference. He was always a great man at the General Committee Office; but there are shades of greatness and shades of deference, which, though quite beyond the powers of definition, nevertheless manifest themselves clearly to the experienced ear and eye. He walked through to his own apartment, and there found two official letters addressed to him lying on his table. The first which came to hand, though official, was small, and marked private, and it was addressed in the handwriting of his old friend, Butterwell, the outgoing secretary. \"I shall see you in the morning, nearly as soon as you get this,\" said the semi-official note; \"but I must be the first to congratulate you on the acquisition of my old shoes. They will be very easy in the wearing to you, though they pinched my corns a little at first. I dare say they want new soling, and perhaps they are a little down at heels; but you will find some excellent cobbler to make them all right, and will give them a grace in the wearing which they have sadly lacked since they came into my possession. I wish you much joy with them,\" &c., &c. He then opened the larger official letter, but that had now but little interest for him. He could have made a copy of the contents without seeing them. The Board of Commissioners had had great pleasure in promoting him to the office of secretary, vacated by the promotion of Mr. Butterwell to a seat at their own Board; and then the letter was signed by Mr. Butterwell himself.\n\nHow delightful to him would have been this welcome on his return to his office had his heart in other respects been free from care! And as he thought of this, he remembered all Lily's charms. He told himself how much she excelled the noble scion of the De Courcy stock, with whom he was now destined to mate himself; how the bride he had rejected excelled the one he had chosen in grace, beauty, faith, freshness, and all feminine virtues. If he could only wipe out the last fortnight from the facts of his existence! But fortnights such as those are not to be wiped out,--not even with many sorrowful years of tedious scrubbing.\n\nAnd at this moment it seemed to him as though all those impediments which had frightened him when he had thought of marrying Lily Dale were withdrawn. That which would have been terrible with seven or eight hundred a year, would have been made delightful with twelve or thirteen. Why had his fate been so unkind to him? Why had not this promotion come to him but one fortnight earlier? Why had it not been declared before he had made his visit to that terrible castle? He even said to himself that if he had positively known the fact before Pratt had seen Mr. Dale, he would have sent a different message to the squire, and would have braved the anger of all the race of the De Courcys. But in that he lied to himself, and he knew that he did so. An earl, in his imagination, was hedged by so strong a divinity, that his treason towards Alexandrina could do no more than peep at what it would. It had been considered but little by him, when the project first offered itself to his mind, to jilt the niece of a small rural squire; but it was not in him to jilt the daughter of a countess.\n\nThat house full of babies in St. John's Wood appeared to him now under a very different guise from that which it wore as he sat in his room at Courcy Castle on the evening of his arrival there. Then such an establishment had to him the flavour of a graveyard. It was as though he were going to bury himself alive. Now that it was out of his reach, he thought of it as a paradise upon earth. And then he considered what sort of a paradise Lady Alexandrina would make for him. It was astonishing how ugly was the Lady Alexandrina, how old, how graceless, how destitute of all pleasant charm, seen through the spectacles which he wore at the present moment.\n\nDuring his first hour at the office he did nothing. One or two of the younger clerks came in and congratulated him with much heartiness. He was popular at his office, and they had got a step by his promotion. Then he met one or two of the elder clerks, and was congratulated with much less heartiness. \"I suppose it's all right,\" said one bluff old gentleman. \"My time is gone by, I know. I married too early to be able to wear a good coat when I was young, and I never was acquainted with any lords or lords' families.\" The sting of this was the sharper because Crosbie had begun to feel how absolutely useless to him had been all that high interest and noble connection which he had formed. He had really been promoted because he knew more about his work than any of the other men, and Lady De Courcy's influential relation at the India Board had not yet even had time to write a note upon the subject.\n\nAt eleven Mr. Butterwell came into Crosbie's room, and the new secretary was forced to clothe himself in smiles. Mr. Butterwell was a pleasant, handsome man of about fifty, who had never yet set the Thames on fire, and had never attempted to do so. He was perhaps a little more civil to great men and a little more patronizing to those below him than he would have been had he been perfect. But there was something frank and English even in his mode of bowing before the mighty ones, and to those who were not mighty he was rather too civil than either stern or supercilious. He knew that he was not very clever, but he knew also how to use those who were clever. He seldom made any mistake, and was very scrupulous not to tread on men's corns. Though he had no enemies, yet he had a friend or two; and we may therefore say of Mr. Butterwell that he had walked his path in life discreetly. At the age of thirty-five he had married a lady with some little fortune, and now he lived a pleasant, easy, smiling life in a villa at Putney. When Mr. Butterwell heard, as he often did hear, of the difficulty which an English gentleman has of earning his bread in his own country, he was wont to look back on his own career with some complacency. He knew that he had not given the world much; yet he had received largely, and no one had begrudged it to him. \"Tact,\" Mr. Butterwell used to say to himself, as he walked along the paths of his Putney villa. \"Tact. Tact. Tact.\"\n\n\"Crosbie,\" he said, as he entered the room cheerily, \"I congratulate you with all my heart. I do, indeed. You have got the step early in life, and you deserve it thoroughly;--much better than I did when I was appointed to the same office.\"\n\n\"Oh, no,\" said Crosbie, gloomily.\n\n\"But I say, Oh, yes. We are deuced lucky to have such a man, and so I told the commissioners.\"\n\n\"I'm sure I'm very much obliged to you.\"\n\n\"I've known it all along,--before you left even. Sir Raffle Buffle had told me he was to go to the Income-tax Office. The chair is two thousand there, you know; and I had been promised the first seat at the Board.\"\n\n\"Ah;--I wish I'd known,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"You are much better as you are,\" said Butterwell. \"There's no pleasure like a surprise! Besides, one knows a thing of that kind, and yet doesn't know it. I don't mind saying now that I knew it,--swearing that I knew it,--but I wouldn't have said so to a living being the day before yesterday. There are such slips between the cups and the lips. Suppose Sir Raffle had not gone to the Income-tax!\"\n\n\"Exactly so,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"But it's all right now. Indeed I sat at the Board yesterday, though I signed the letter afterwards. I'm not sure that I don't lose more than I gain.\"\n\n\"What! with three hundred a year more and less work?\"\n\n\"Ah, but look at the interest of the thing. The secretary sees everything and knows everything. But I'm getting old, and, as you say, the lighter work will suit me. By the by, will you come down to Putney to-morrow? Mrs. Butterwell will be delighted to see the new secretary. There's nobody in town now, so you can have no ground for refusing.\"\n\nBut Mr. Crosbie did find some ground for refusing. It would have been impossible for him to have sat and smiled at Mrs. Butterwell's table in his present frame of mind. In a mysterious, half-explanatory manner, he let Mr. Butterwell know that private affairs of importance made it absolutely necessary that he should remain that evening in town. \"And indeed,\" as he said, \"he was not his own master just at present.\"\n\n\"By the by,--of course not. I had quite forgotten to congratulate you on that head. So you're going to be married? Well; I'm very glad, and hope you'll be as lucky as I have been.\"\n\n\"Thank you,\" said Crosbie, again rather gloomily.\n\n\"A young lady from near Guestwick, isn't it; or somewhere in those parts?\"\n\n\"N--no,\" stammered Crosbie. \"The lady comes from Barsetshire.\"\n\n\"Why, I heard the name. Isn't she a Bell, or Tait, or Ball, or some such name as that?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Crosbie, assuming what boldness he could command. \"Her name is De Courcy.\"\n\n\"One of the earl's daughters?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Oh, I beg your pardon. I'd heard wrong. You're going to be allied to a very noble family, and I am heartily glad to hear of your success in life.\" Then Butterwell shook him very cordially by the hand,--having offered him no such special testimony of approval when under the belief that he was going to marry a Bell, a Tait, or a Ball. All the same, Mr. Butterwell began to think that there was something wrong. He had heard from an indubitable source that Crosbie had engaged himself to a niece of a squire with whom he had been staying near Guestwick,--a girl without any money; and Mr. Butterwell, in his wisdom, had thought his friend Crosbie to be rather a fool for his pains. But now he was going to marry one of the De Courcys! Mr. Butterwell was rather at his wits' ends.\n\n\"Well; we shall be sitting at two, you know, and of course you'll come to us. If you're at leisure before that I'll make over what papers I have to you. I've not been a Lord Eldon in my office, and they won't break your back.\"\n\nImmediately after that Fowler Pratt had been shown into Crosbie's room, and Crosbie had written the letter to the squire under Pratt's eye.\n\nHe could take no joy in his promotion. When Pratt left him he tried to lighten his heart. He endeavoured to throw Lily and her wrongs behind him, and fix his thoughts on his advancing successes in life; but he could not do it. A self-imposed trouble will not allow itself to be banished. If a man lose a thousand pounds by a friend's fault, or by a turn in the wheel of fortune, he can, if he be a man, put his grief down and trample it under foot; he can exorcise the spirit of his grievance, and bid the evil one depart from out of his house. But such exorcism is not to be used when the sorrow has come from a man's own folly and sin;--especially not if it has come from his own selfishness. Such are the cases which make men drink; which drive them on to the avoidance of all thought; which create gamblers and reckless prodigals; which are the promoters of suicide. How could he avoid writing this letter to Lily? He might blow his brains out, and so let there be an end of it all. It was to such reflections that he came, when he sat himself down endeavouring to reap satisfaction from his promotion.\n\nBut Crosbie was not a man to commit suicide. In giving him his due I must protest that he was too good for that. He knew too well that a pistol-bullet could not be the be-all and the end-all here, and there was too much manliness in him for so cowardly an escape. The burden must be borne. But how was he to bear it? There he sat till it was two o'clock, neglecting Mr. Butterwell and his office papers, and not stirring from his seat till a messenger summoned him before the Board. The Board, as he entered the room, was not such a Board as the public may, perhaps, imagine such Boards to be. There was a round table, with a few pens lying about, and a comfortable leathern arm-chair at the side of it, farthest from the door. Sir Raffle Buffle was leaving his late colleagues, and was standing with his back to the fire-place, talking very loudly. Sir Raffle was a great bully, and the Board was uncommonly glad to be rid of him; but as this was to be his last appearance at the Committee Office, they submitted to his voice meekly. Mr. Butterwell was standing close to him, essaying to laugh mildly at Sir Raffle's jokes. A little man, hardly more than five feet high, with small but honest-looking eyes, and close-cut hair, was standing behind the arm-chair, rubbing his hands together, and longing for the departure of Sir Raffle, in order that he might sit down. This was Mr. Optimist, the new chairman, in praise of whose appointment the Daily Jupiter had been so loud, declaring that the present Minister was showing himself superior to all Ministers who had ever gone before him, in giving promotion solely on the score of merit. The Daily Jupiter, a fortnight since, had published a very eloquent article, strongly advocating the claims of Mr. Optimist, and was naturally pleased to find that its advice had been taken. Has not an obedient Minister a right to the praise of those powers which he obeys?\n\nMr. Optimist was, in truth, an industrious little gentleman, very well connected, who had served the public all his life, and who was, at any rate, honest in his dealings. Nor was he a bully, such as his predecessor. It might, however, be a question whether he carried guns enough for the command in which he was now to be employed. There was but one other member of the Board, Major Fiasco by name, a discontented, broken-hearted, silent man, who had been sent to the General Committee Office some few years before because he was not wanted anywhere else. He was a man who had intended to do great things when he entered public life, and had possessed the talent and energy for things moderately great. He had also possessed to a certain extent the ear of those high in office; but, in some way, matters had not gone well with him, and in running his course he had gone on the wrong side of the post. He was still in the prime of life, and yet all men knew that Major Fiasco had nothing further to expect from the public or from the Government. Indeed, there were not wanting those who said that Major Fiasco was already in receipt of a liberal income, for which he gave no work in return; that he merely filled a chair for four hours a day four or five days a week, signing his name to certain forms and documents, reading, or pretending to read, certain papers, but, in truth, doing no good. Major Fiasco, on the other hand, considered himself to be a deeply injured individual, and he spent his life in brooding over his wrongs. He believed now in nothing and in nobody. He had begun public life striving to be honest, and he now regarded all around him as dishonest. He had no satisfaction in any man other than that which he found when some event would show to him that this or that other compeer of his own had proved himself to be self-interested, false, or fraudulent. \"Don't tell me, Butterwell,\" he would say--for with Mr. Butterwell he maintained some semi-official intimacy, and he would take that gentleman by the button-hole, holding him close. \"Don't tell me. I know what men are. I've seen the world. I've been looking at things with my eyes open. I knew what he was doing.\" And then he would tell of the sly deed of some official known well to them both, not denouncing it by any means, but affecting to take it for granted that the man in question was a rogue. Butterwell would shrug his shoulders, and laugh gently, and say that, upon his word, he didn't think the world so bad as Fiasco made it out to be.\n\nNor did he; for Butterwell believed in many things. He believed in his Putney villa on this earth, and he believed also that he might achieve some sort of Putney villa in the world beyond without undergoing present martyrdom. His Putney villa first, with all its attendant comforts, and then his duty to the public afterwards. It was thus that Mr. Butterwell regulated his conduct; and as he was solicitous that the villa should be as comfortable a home to his wife as to himself, and that it should be specially comfortable to his friends, I do not think that we need quarrel with his creed.\n\nMr. Optimist believed in everything, but especially he believed in the Prime Minister, in the Daily Jupiter, in the General Committee Office, and in himself. He had long thought that everything was nearly right; but now that he himself was chairman at the General Committee Office, he was quite sure that everything must be right. In Sir Raffle Buffle, indeed, he had never believed; and now it was, perhaps, the greatest joy of his life that he should never again be called upon to hear the tones of that terrible knight's hated voice.\n\nSeeing who were the components of the new Board, it may be presumed that Crosbie would look forward to enjoying a not uninfluential position in his office. There were, indeed, some among the clerks who did not hesitate to say that the new secretary would have it pretty nearly all his own way. As for \"Old Opt,\" there would be, they said, no difficulty about him. Only tell him that such and such a decision was his own, and he would be sure to believe the teller. Butterwell was not fond of work, and had been accustomed to lean upon Crosbie for many years. As for Fiasco, he would be cynical in words, but wholly indifferent in deed. If the whole office were made to go to the mischief, Fiasco, in his own grim way, would enjoy the confusion.\n\n\"Wish you joy, Crosbie,\" said Sir Raffle, standing up on the rug, waiting for the new secretary to go up to him and shake hands. But Sir Raffle was going, and the new secretary did not indulge him.\n\n\"Thank ye, Sir Raffle,\" said Crosbie, without going near the rug.\n\n\"Mr. Crosbie, I congratulate you most sincerely,\" said Mr. Optimist. \"Your promotion has been the result altogether of your own merit. You have been selected for the high office which you are now called upon to fill solely because it has been thought that you are the most fit man to perform the onerous duties attached to it. Hum--h-m--ha. As regards my share in the recommendation which we found ourselves bound to submit to the Treasury, I must say that I never felt less hesitation in my life, and I believe I may declare as much as regards the other members of the Board.\" And Mr. Optimist looked around him for approving words. He had come forward from his standing ground behind his chair to welcome Crosbie, and had shaken his hand cordially. Fiasco also had risen from his seat, and had assured Crosbie in a whisper that he had feathered his nest uncommon well. Then he had sat down again.\n\n\"Indeed you may, as far as I am concerned,\" said Butterwell.\n\n\"I told the Chancellor of the Exchequer,\" said Sir Raffle, speaking very loud and with much authority, \"that unless he had some first-rate man to send from elsewhere I could name a fitting candidate. 'Sir Raffle,' he said, 'I mean to keep it in the office, and therefore shall be glad of your opinion.' 'In that case, Mr. Chancellor,' said I, 'Mr. Crosbie must be the man.' 'Mr. Crosbie shall be the man,' said the Chancellor. And Mr. Crosbie is the man.\"\n\n\"Your friend Sark spoke to Lord Brock about it,\" said Fiasco. Now the Earl of Sark was a young nobleman of much influence at the present moment, and Lord Brock was the Prime Minister. \"You should thank Lord Sark.\"\n\n\"Had as much to do with it as if my footman had spoken,\" said Sir Raffle.\n\n\"I am very much obliged to the Board for their good opinion,\" said Crosbie, gravely. \"I am obliged to Lord Sark as well,--and also to your footman, Sir Raffle, if, as you seem to say, he has interested himself in my favour.\"\n\n\"I didn't say anything of the kind,\" said Sir Raffle. \"I thought it right to make you understand that it was my opinion, given, of course, officially, which prevailed with the Chancellor of the Exchequer. Well, gentlemen, as I shall be wanted in the city, I will say good morning to you. Is my carriage ready, Boggs?\" Upon which the attendant messenger opened the door, and the great Sir Raffle Buffle took his final departure from the scene of his former labours.\n\n\"As to the duties of your new office\"--and Mr. Optimist continued his speech, taking no other notice of the departure of his enemy than what was indicated by an increased brightness of his eye and a more satisfactory tone of voice--\"you will find yourself quite familiar with them.\"\n\n\"Indeed he will,\" said Butterwell.\n\n\"And I am quite sure that you will perform them with equal credit to yourself, satisfaction to the department, and advantage to the public. We shall always be glad to have your opinion on any subject of importance that may come before us; and as regards the internal discipline of the office, we feel that we may leave it safely in your hands. In any matter of importance you will, of course, consult us, and I feel very confident that we shall go on together with great comfort and with mutual confidence.\" Then Mr. Optimist looked at his brother commissioners, sat down in his arm-chair, and taking in his hands some papers before him, began the routine business of the day.\n\nIt was nearly five o'clock when, on this special occasion, the secretary returned from the board-room to his own office. Not for a moment had the weight been off his shoulders while Sir Raffle had been bragging or Mr. Optimist making his speech. He had been thinking, not of them, but of Lily Dale; and though they had not discovered his thoughts, they had perceived that he was hardly like himself.\n\n\"I never saw a man so little elated by good fortune in my life,\" said Mr. Optimist.\n\n\"Ah, he's got something on his mind,\" said Butterwell. \"He's going to be married, I believe.\"\n\n\"If that's the case, it's no wonder he shouldn't be elated,\" said Major Fiasco, who was himself a bachelor.\n\nWhen in his own room again, Crosbie at once seized on a sheet of note-paper, as though by hurrying himself on with it he could get that letter to Allington written. But though the paper was before him, and the pen in his hand, the letter did not, would not, get itself written. With what words was he to begin it? To whom should it be written? How was he to declare himself the villain which he had made himself? The letters from his office were taken away every night shortly after six, and at six o'clock he had not written a word. \"I will do it at home to-night,\" he said to himself, and then, tearing off a scrap of paper, he scratched those few lines which Lily received, and which she had declined to communicate to her mother or sister. Crosbie, as he wrote them, conceived that they would in some way prepare the poor girl for the coming blow,--that they would, at any rate, make her know that all was not right; but in so supposing he had not counted on the constancy of her nature, nor had he thought of the promise which she had given him that nothing should make her doubt him. He wrote the scrap, and then taking his hat walked off through the gloom of the November evening up Charing Cross and St. Martin's Lane, towards the Seven Dials and Bloomsbury, into regions of the town with which he had no business, and which he never frequented. He hardly knew where he went or wherefore. How was he to escape from the weight of the burden which was now crushing him? It seemed to him as though he would change his position with thankfulness for that of the junior clerk in his office, if only that junior clerk had upon his mind no such betrayal of trust as that of which he was guilty.\n\nAt half-past seven he found himself at Sebright's, and there he dined. A man will dine, even though his heart be breaking. Then he got into a cab, and had himself taken home to Mount Street. During his walk he had sworn to himself that he would not go to bed that night till the letter was written and posted. It was twelve before the first words were marked on the paper, and yet he kept his oath. Between two and three, in the cold moonlight, he crawled out and deposited his letter in the nearest post-office.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX. JOHN EAMES RETURNS TO BURTON CRESCENT.\n\nJohn Eames and Crosbie returned to town on the same day. It will be remembered how Eames had assisted Lord De Guest in the matter of the bull, and how great had been the earl's gratitude on the occasion. The memory of this, and the strong encouragement which he received from his mother and sister for having made such a friend by his gallantry, lent some slight satisfaction to his last hours at home. But his two misfortunes were too serious to allow of anything like real happiness. He was leaving Lily behind him, engaged to be married to a man whom he hated, and he was returning to Burton Crescent, where he would have to face Amelia Roper,--Amelia either in her rage or in her love. The prospect of Amelia in her rage was very terrible to him; but his greatest fear was of Amelia in her love. He had in his letter declined matrimony; but what if she talked down all his objections, and carried him off to church in spite of himself!\n\nWhen he reached London and got into a cab with his portmanteau, he could hardly fetch up courage to bid the man drive him to Burton Crescent. \"I might as well go to an hotel for the night,\" he said to himself, \"and then I can learn how things are going on from Cradell at the office.\" Nevertheless, he did give the direction to Burton Crescent, and when it was once given felt ashamed to change it. But, as he was driven up to the well-known door, his heart was so low within him that he might almost be said to have lost it. When the cabman demanded whether he should knock, he could not answer; and when the maid-servant at the door greeted him, he almost ran away.\n\n\"Who's at home?\" said he, asking the question in a very low voice.\n\n\"There's missus,\" said the girl, \"and Miss Spruce, and Mrs. Lupex. He's away somewhere, in his tantrums again; and there's Mr.--\"\n\n\"Is Miss Roper here?\" he said, still whispering.\n\n\"Oh, yes! Miss Mealyer's here,\" said the girl, speaking in a cruelly loud voice. \"She was in the dining-room just now, putting out the table. Miss Mealyer!\" And the girl, as she called out the name, opened the dining-room door. Johnny Eames felt that his knees were too weak to support him.\n\nBut Miss Mealyer was not in the dining-room. She had perceived the advancing cab of her sworn adorer, and had thought it expedient to retreat from her domestic duties, and fortify herself among her brushes and ribbons. Had it been possible that she should know how very weak and cowardly was the enemy against whom she was called upon to put herself in action, she might probably have fought her battle somewhat differently, and have achieved a speedy victory, at the cost of an energetic shot or two. But she did not know. She thought it probable that she might obtain power over him and manage him; but it did not occur to her that his legs were so weak beneath him that she might almost blow him over with a breath. None but the worst and most heartless of women know the extent of their own power over men;--as none but the worst and most heartless of men know the extent of their power over women. Amelia Roper was not a good specimen of the female sex, but there were worse women than her.\n\n\"She ain't there, Mr. Eames; but you'll see her in the drawen-room,\" said the girl. \"And it's she'll be glad to see you back again, Mr. Eames.\" But he scrupulously passed the door of the upstairs sitting-room, not even looking within it, and contrived to get himself into his own chamber without having encountered anybody. \"Here's yer 'ot water, Mr. Eames,\" said the girl, coming up to him after an interval of half-an-hour; \"and dinner'll be on the table in ten minutes. Mr. Cradell is come in, and so is missus's son.\"\n\nIt was still open to him to go out and dine at some eating-house in the Strand. He could start out, leaving word that he was engaged, and so postpone the evil hour. He had almost made up his mind to do so, and certainly would have done it, had not the sitting-room door opened as he was on the landing-place. The door opened, and he found himself confronting the assembled company. First came Cradell, and leaning on his arm, I regret to say, was Mrs. Lupex,--Egyptia conjux! Then there came Miss Spruce with young Roper; Amelia and her mother brought up the rear together. There was no longer question of flight now; and poor Eames, before he knew what he was doing, was carried down into the dining-room with the rest of the company. They were all glad to see him, and welcomed him back warmly, but he was so much beside himself that he could not ascertain whether Amelia's voice was joined with the others. He was already seated at table, and had before him a plate of soup, before he recognized the fact that he was sitting between Mrs. Roper and Mrs. Lupex. The latter lady had separated herself from Mr. Cradell as she entered the room. \"Under all the circumstances perhaps it will be better for us to be apart,\" she said. \"A lady can't make herself too safe; can she, Mrs. Roper? There's no danger between you and me, is there, Mr. Eames,--specially when Miss Amelia is opposite?\" The last words, however, were intended to be whispered into his ear.\n\nBut Johnny made no answer to her; contenting himself for the moment with wiping the perspiration from his brow. There was Amelia opposite to him, looking at him--the very Amelia to whom he had written, declining the honour of marrying her. Of what her mood towards him might be, he could form no judgment from her looks. Her face was simply stern and impassive, and she seemed inclined to eat her dinner in silence. A slight smile of derision had passed across her face as she heard Mrs. Lupex whisper, and it might have been discerned that her nose, at the same time, became somewhat elevated; but she said not a word.\n\n\"I hope you've enjoyed yourself, Mr. Eames, among the vernal beauties of the country,\" said Mrs. Lupex.\n\n\"Very much, thank you,\" he replied.\n\n\"There's nothing like the country at this autumnal season of the year. As for myself, I've never been accustomed to remain in London after the breaking up of the beau monde. We've usually been to Broadstairs, which is a very charming place, with most elegant society, but now--\" and she shook her head, by which all the company knew that she intended to allude to the sins of Mr. Lupex.\n\n\"I'd never wish to sleep out of London for my part,\" said Mrs. Roper. \"When a woman's got a house over her head, I don't think her mind's ever easy out of it.\"\n\nShe had not intended any reflection on Mrs. Lupex for not having a house of her own, but that lady immediately bristled up. \"That's just what the snails say, Mrs. Roper. And as for having a house of one's own, it's a very good thing, no doubt, sometimes; but that's according to circumstances. It has suited me lately to live in lodgings, but there's no knowing whether I mayn't fall lower than that yet, and have--\" but here she stopped herself, and looking over at Mr. Cradell nodded her head.\n\n\"And have to let them,\" said Mrs. Roper. \"I hope you'll be more lucky with your lodgers than I have been with some of mine. Jemima, hand the potatoes to Miss Spruce. Miss Spruce, do let me send you a little more gravy? There's plenty here, really.\" Mrs. Roper was probably thinking of Mr. Todgers.\n\n\"I hope I shall,\" said Mrs. Lupex. \"But, as I was saying, Broadstairs is delightful. Were you ever at Broadstairs, Mr. Cradell?\"\n\n\"Never, Mrs. Lupex. I generally go abroad in my leave. One sees more of the world, you know. I was at Dieppe last June, and found that very delightful--though rather lonely. I shall go to Ostend this year; only December is so late for Ostend. It was a deuced shame my getting December, wasn't it, Johnny?\"\n\n\"Yes, it was,\" said Eames. \"I managed better.\"\n\n\"And what have you been doing, Mr. Eames?\" said Mrs. Lupex, with one of her sweetest smiles. \"Whatever it may have been, you've not been false to the cause of beauty, I'm sure.\" And she looked over to Amelia with a knowing smile. But Amelia was engaged upon her plate, and went on with her dinner without turning her eyes either on Mrs. Lupex or on John Eames.\n\n\"I haven't done anything particular,\" said Eames. \"I've just been staying with my mother.\"\n\n\"We've been very social here, haven't we, Miss Amelia?\" continued Mrs. Lupex. \"Only now and then a cloud comes across the heavens, and the lights at the banquet are darkened.\" Then she put her handkerchief up to her eyes, sobbing deeply, and they all knew that she was again alluding to the sins of her husband.\n\nAs soon as dinner was over the ladies with young Mr. Roper retired, and Eames and Cradell were left to take their wine over the dining-room fire,--or their glass of gin and water, as it might be. \"Well, Caudle, old fellow,\" said one. \"Well, Johnny, my boy,\" said the other. \"What's the news at the office?\" said Eames.\n\n\"Muggeridge has been playing the very mischief.\" Muggeridge was the second clerk in Cradell's room. \"We're going to put him into Coventry and not speak to him except officially. But to tell you the truth, my hands have been so full here at home, that I haven't thought much about the office. What am I to do about that woman?\"\n\n\"Do about her? How do about her?\"\n\n\"Yes; what am I to do about her? How am I to manage with her? There's Lupex off again in one of his fits of jealousy.\"\n\n\"But it's not your fault, I suppose?\"\n\n\"Well; I can't just say. I am fond of her, and that's the long and the short of it; deuced fond of her.\"\n\n\"But, my dear Caudle, you know she's that man's wife.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, I know all about it. I'm not going to defend myself. It's wrong, I know,--pleasant, but wrong. But what's a fellow to do? I suppose in strict morality I ought to leave the lodgings. But, by George, I don't see why a man's to be turned out in that way. And then I couldn't make a clean score with old mother Roper. But I say, old fellow, who gave you the gold chain?\"\n\n\"Well; it was an old family friend at Guestwick; or rather, I should say, a man who said he knew my father.\"\n\n\"And he gave you that because he knew your governor! Is there a watch to it?\"\n\n\"Yes, there's a watch. It wasn't exactly that. There was some trouble about a bull. To tell the truth, it was Lord De Guest; the queerest fellow, Caudle, you ever met in your life; but such a trump. I've got to go and dine with him at Christmas.\" And then the old story of the bull was told.\n\n\"I wish I could find a lord in a field with a bull,\" said Cradell. We may, however, be permitted to doubt whether Mr. Cradell would have earned a watch even if he had had his wish.\n\n\"You see,\" continued Cradell, reverting to the subject on which he most delighted to talk, \"I'm not responsible for that man's ill-conduct.\"\n\n\"Does anybody say you are?\"\n\n\"No; nobody says so. But people seem to think so. When he is by I hardly speak to her. She is thoughtless and giddy, as women are, and takes my arm, and that kind of thing, you know. It makes him mad with rage, but upon my honour I don't think she means any harm.\"\n\n\"I don't suppose she does,\" said Eames.\n\n\"Well; she may or she mayn't. I hope with all my heart she doesn't.\"\n\n\"And where is he now?\"\n\n\"This is between ourselves, you know; but she went to find him this afternoon. Unless he gives her money she can't stay here, nor, for the matter of that, will she be able to go away. If I mention something to you, you won't tell any one?\"\n\n\"Of course I won't.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't have it known to any one for the world. I've lent her seven pounds ten. It's that which makes me so short with mother Roper.\"\n\n\"Then I think you're a fool for your pains.\"\n\n\"Ah, that's so like you. I always said you'd no feeling of real romance. If I cared for a woman I'd give her the coat off my back.\"\n\n\"I'd do better than that,\" said Johnny. \"I'd give her the heart out of my body. I'd be chopped up alive for a girl I loved; but it shouldn't be for another man's wife.\"\n\n\"That's a matter of taste. But she's been to Lupex to-day at that house he goes to in Drury Lane. She had a terrible scene there. He was going to commit suicide in the middle of the street, and she declares that it all comes from jealousy. Think what a time I have of it--standing always, as one may say, on gunpowder. He may turn up here any moment, you know. But, upon my word, for the life of me I cannot desert her. If I were to turn my back on her she wouldn't have a friend in the world. And how's L. D.? I'll tell you what it is--you'll have some trouble with the divine Amelia.\"\n\n\"Shall I?\"\n\n\"By Jove, you will. But how's L. D. all this time?\"\n\n\"L. D. is engaged to be married to a man named Adolphus Crosbie,\" said poor Johnny, slowly. \"If you please, we will not say any more about her.\"\n\n\"Whew--w--w! That's what makes you so down in the mouth! L. D. going to marry Crosbie! Why, that's the man who is to be the new secretary at the General Committee Office. Old Huffle Scuffle, who was their chair, has come to us, you know. There's been a general move at the G. C., and this Crosbie has got to be secretary. He's a lucky chap, isn't he?\"\n\n\"I don't know anything about his luck. He's one of those fellows that make me hate them the first time I look at them. I've a sort of a feeling that I shall live to kick him some day.\"\n\n\"That's the time, is it? Then I suppose Amelia will have it all her own way now.\"\n\n\"I'll tell you what, Caudle. I'd sooner get up through the trap-door, and throw myself off the roof into the area, than marry Amelia Roper.\"\n\n\"Have you and she had any conversation since you came back?\"\n\n\"Not a word.\"\n\n\"Then I tell you fairly you've got trouble before you. Amelia and Maria--Mrs. Lupex, I mean--are as thick as thieves just at present, and they have been talking you over. Maria--that is, Mrs. Lupex--lets it all out to me. You'll have to mind where you are, old fellow.\"\n\nEames was not inclined to discuss the matter any further, so he finished his toddy in silence. Cradell, however, who felt that there was something in his affairs of which he had reason to be proud, soon returned to the story of his own very extraordinary position. \"By Jove, I don't know that a man was ever so circumstanced,\" he said. \"She looks to me to protect her, and yet what can I do?\"\n\nAt last Cradell got up, and declared that he must go to the ladies. \"She's so nervous, that unless she has some one to countenance her she becomes unwell.\"\n\nEames declared his purpose of going to the divan, or to the theatre, or to take a walk in the streets. The smiles of beauty had no longer charms for him in Burton Crescent.\n\n\"They'll expect you to take a cup of tea the first night,\" said Cradell; but Eames declared that they might expect it.\n\n\"I'm in no humour for it,\" said he. \"I'll tell you what, Cradell, I shall leave this place, and take rooms for myself somewhere. I'll never go into a lodging-house again.\"\n\nAs he so spoke, he was standing at the dining-room door; but he was not allowed to escape in this easy way. Jemima, as he went out into the passage, was there with a three-cornered note in her hand. \"From Miss Mealyer,\" she said. \"Miss Mealyer is in the back parlour all by herself.\"\n\nPoor Johnny took the note, and read it by the lamp over the front door.\n\n\"Are you not going to speak to me on the day of your return? It cannot be that you will leave the house without seeing me for a moment. I am in the back parlour.\"\n\nWhen he had read these words, he paused in the passage, with his hat on. Jemima, who could not understand why any young man should hesitate as to seeing his lady-love in the back parlour alone, whispered to him again, in her audible way, \"Miss Mealyer is there, sir; and all the rest on 'em's upstairs!\" So compelled, Eames put down his hat, and walked with slow steps into the back parlour.\n\nHow was it to be with the enemy? Was he to encounter Amelia in anger, or Amelia in love? She had seemed to be stern and defiant when he had ventured to steal a look at her across the dining-table, and now he expected that she would turn upon him with loud threatenings and protestations as to her wrongs. But it was not so. When he entered the room she was standing with her back to him, leaning on the mantel-piece, and at the first moment she did not essay to speak. He walked into the middle of the room and stood there, waiting for her to begin.\n\n\"Shut the door!\" she said, looking over her shoulder. \"I suppose you don't want the girl to hear all you've got to say to me!\"\n\nThen he shut the door; but still Amelia stood with her back to him, leaning upon the mantel-piece.\n\nIt did not seem that he had much to say, for he remained perfectly silent.\n\n\"Well!\" said Amelia, after a long pause, and she then again looked over her shoulder. \"Well, Mr. Eames!\"\n\n\"Jemima gave me your note, and so I've come,\" said he.\n\n\"And is this the way we meet!\" she exclaimed, turning suddenly upon him, and throwing her long black hair back over her shoulders. There certainly was some beauty about her. Her eyes were large and bright, and her shoulders were well turned. She might have done as an artist's model for a Judith, but I doubt whether any man, looking well into her face, could think that she would do well as a wife. \"Oh, John, is it to be thus, after love such as ours?\" And she clasped her hands together, and stood before him.\n\n\"I don't know what you mean,\" said Eames.\n\n\"If you are engaged to marry L. D., tell me so at once. Be a man, and speak out, sir.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Eames; \"I am not engaged to marry the lady to whom you allude.\"\n\n\"On your honour?\"\n\n\"I won't have her spoken about. I'm not going to marry her, and that's enough.\"\n\n\"Do you think that I wish to speak of her? What can L. D. be to me as long as she is nothing to you? Oh, Johnny, why did you write me that heartless letter?\" Then she leaned upon his shoulder--or attempted to do so.\n\nI cannot say that Eames shook her off, seeing that he lacked the courage to do so; but he shuffled his shoulder about so that the support was uneasy to her, and she was driven to stand erect again. \"Why did you write that cruel letter?\" she said again.\n\n\"Because I thought it best, Amelia. What's a man to do with ninety pounds a year, you know?\"\n\n\"But your mother allows you twenty.\"\n\n\"And what's a man to do with a hundred and ten?\"\n\n\"Rising five pounds every year,\" said the well-informed Amelia. \"Of course we should live here, with mamma, and you would just go on paying her as you do now. If your heart was right, Johnny, you wouldn't think so much about money. If you loved me--as you said you did--\" Then a little sob came, and the words were stopped. The words were stopped, but she was again upon his shoulder. What was he to do? In truth, his only wish was to escape, and yet his arm, quite in opposition to his own desires, found its way round her waist. In such a combat a woman has so many points in her favour! \"Oh, Johnny,\" she said again, as soon as she felt the pressure of his arm. \"Gracious, what a beautiful watch you've got,\" and she took the trinket out of his pocket. \"Did you buy that?\"\n\n\"No; it was given to me.\"\n\n\"John Eames, did L. D. give it you?\"\n\n\"No, no, no,\" he shouted, stamping on the floor as he spoke.\n\n\"Oh, I beg your pardon,\" said Amelia, quelled for the moment by his energy. \"Perhaps it was your mother.\"\n\n\"No; it was a man. Never mind about the watch now.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't mind anything, Johnny, if you would tell me that you loved me again. Perhaps I oughtn't to ask you, and it isn't becoming in a lady; but how can I help it, when you know you've got my heart. Come upstairs and have tea with us now, won't you?\"\n\nWhat was he to do? He said that he would go up and have tea; and as he led her to the door he put down his face and kissed her. Oh, Johnny Eames! But then a woman in such a contest has so many points in her favour.\n\n# CHAPTER XXX. IS IT FROM HIM?\n\nI have already declared that Crosbie wrote and posted the fatal letter to Allington, and we must now follow it down to that place. On the morning following the squire's return to his own house, Mrs. Crump, the post-mistress at Allington, received a parcel by post directed to herself. She opened it, and found an enclosure addressed to Mrs. Dale, with a written request that she would herself deliver it into that lady's own hand at once. This was Crosbie's letter.\n\n\"It's from Miss Lily's gentleman,\" said Mrs. Crump, looking at the handwriting. \"There's something up, or he wouldn't be writing to her mamma in this way.\" But Mrs. Crump lost no time in putting on her bonnet, and trudging up with the letter to the Small House. \"I must see the missus herself,\" said Mrs. Crump. Whereupon Mrs. Dale was called downstairs into the hall, and there received the packet. Lily was in the breakfast-parlour, and had seen the post-mistress arrive;--had seen also that she carried a letter in her hand. For a moment she had thought that it was for her, and imagined that the old woman had brought it herself from simple good-nature. But Lily, when she heard her mother mentioned, instantly withdrew and shut the parlour door. Her heart misgave her that something was wrong, but she hardly tried to think what it might be. After all, the regular postman might bring the letter she herself expected. Bell was not yet downstairs, and she stood alone over the tea-cups on the breakfast-table, feeling that there was something for her to fear. Her mother did not come at once into the room, but, after a pause of a moment or two, went again upstairs. So she remained, either standing against the table, or at the window, or seated in one of the two arm-chairs, for a space of ten minutes, when Bell entered the room.\n\n\"Isn't mamma down yet?\" said Bell.\n\n\"Bell,\" said Lily, \"something has happened. Mamma has got a letter.\"\n\n\"Happened! What has happened? Is anybody ill? Who is the letter from?\" And Bell was going to return through the door in search of her mother.\n\n\"Stop, Bell,\" said Lily. \"Do not go to her yet. I think it's from--Adolphus.\"\n\n\"Oh, Lily, what do you mean?\"\n\n\"I don't know, dear. We'll wait a little longer. Don't look like that, Bell.\" And Lily strove to appear calm, and strove almost successfully.\n\n\"You have frightened me so,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I am frightened myself. He only sent me one line yesterday, and now he has sent nothing. If some misfortune should have happened to him! Mrs. Crump brought down the letter herself to mamma, and that is so odd, you know.\"\n\n\"Are you sure it was from him?\"\n\n\"No; I have not spoken to her. I will go up to her now. Don't you come, Bell. Oh! Bell, do not look so unhappy.\" She then went over and kissed her sister, and after that, with very gentle steps, made her way up to her mother's room. \"Mamma, may I come in?\" she said.\n\n\"Oh! my child!\"\n\n\"I know it is from him, mamma. Tell me all at once.\"\n\nMrs. Dale had read the letter. With quick, glancing eyes, she had made herself mistress of its whole contents, and was already aware of the nature and extent of the sorrow which had come upon them. It was a sorrow that admitted of no hope. The man who had written that letter could never return again; nor if he should return could he be welcomed back to them. The blow had fallen, and it was to be borne. Inside the letter to herself had been a very small note addressed to Lily. \"Give her the enclosed,\" Crosbie had said in his letter, \"if you do not now think it wrong to do so. I have left it open, that you may read it.\" Mrs. Dale, however, had not yet read it, and she now concealed it beneath her handkerchief.\n\nI will not repeat at length Crosbie's letter to Mrs. Dale. It covered four sides of letter-paper, and was such a letter that any man who wrote it must have felt himself to be a rascal. We saw that he had difficulty in writing it, but the miracle was, that any man could have found it possible to write it. \"I know you will curse me,\" said he; \"and I deserve to be cursed. I know that I shall be punished for this, and I must bear my punishment. My worst punishment will be this,--that I never more shall hold up my head again.\" And then, again, he said:--\"My only excuse is my conviction that I should never make her happy. She has been brought up as an angel, with pure thoughts, with holy hopes, with a belief in all that is good, and high, and noble. I have been surrounded through my whole life by things low, and mean, and ignoble. How could I live with her, or she with me? I know now that this is so; but my fault has been that I did not know it when I was there with her. I choose to tell you all,\" he continued, towards the end of the letter, \"and therefore I let you know that I have engaged myself to marry another woman. Ah! I can foresee how bitter will be your feelings when you read this: but they will not be so bitter as mine while I write it. Yes; I am already engaged to one who will suit me, and whom I may suit. You will not expect me to speak ill of her who is to be near and dear to me. But she is one with whom I may mate myself without an inward conviction that I shall destroy all her happiness by doing so. Lilian,\" he said, \"shall always have my prayers; and I trust that she may soon forget, in the love of an honest man, that she ever knew one so dishonest as--Adolphus Crosbie.\"\n\nOf what like must have been his countenance as he sat writing such words of himself under the ghastly light of his own small, solitary lamp? Had he written his letter at his office, in the day-time, with men coming in and out of his room, he could hardly have written of himself so plainly. He would have bethought himself that the written words might remain, and be read hereafter by other eyes than those for which they were intended. But, as he sat alone, during the small hours of the night, almost repenting of his sin with true repentance, he declared to himself that he did not care who might read them. They should, at any rate, be true. Now they had been read by her to whom they had been addressed, and the daughter was standing before the mother to hear her doom.\n\n\"Tell me all at once,\" Lily had said; but in what words was her mother to tell her?\n\n\"Lily,\" she said, rising from her seat, and leaving the two letters on the couch; that addressed to the daughter was hidden beneath a handkerchief, but that which she had read she left open and in sight. She took both the girl's hands in hers as she looked into her face, and spoke to her. \"Lily, my child!\" Then she burst into sobs, and was unable to tell her tale.\n\n\"Is it from him, mamma? May I read it? He cannot be--\"\n\n\"It is from Mr. Crosbie.\"\n\n\"Is he ill, mamma? Tell me at once. If he is ill I will go to him.\"\n\n\"No, my darling, he is not ill. Not yet;--do not read it yet. Oh, Lily! It brings bad news; very bad news.\"\n\n\"Mamma, if he is not in danger, I can read it. Is it bad to him, or only bad to me?\"\n\nAt this moment the servant knocked, and not waiting for an answer half opened the door.\n\n\"If you please, ma'am, Mr. Bernard is below, and wants to speak to you.\"\n\n\"Mr. Bernard! ask Miss Bell to see him.\"\n\n\"Miss Bell is with him, ma'am, but he says that he specially wants to speak to you.\"\n\nMrs. Dale felt that she could not leave Lily alone. She could not take the letter away, nor could she leave her child with the letter open.\n\n\"I cannot see him,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"Ask him what it is. Tell him I cannot come down just at present.\" And then the servant went, and Bernard left his message with Bell.\n\n\"Bernard,\" she had said, \"do you know of anything? Is there anything wrong about Mr. Crosbie?\" Then, in a few words, he told her all, and understanding why his aunt had not come down to him, he went back to the Great House. Bell, almost stupefied by the tidings, seated herself at the table unconsciously, leaning upon her elbows.\n\n\"It will kill her,\" she said to herself. \"My Lily, my darling Lily! It will surely kill her!\"\n\nBut the mother was still with the daughter, and the story was still untold.\n\n\"Mamma,\" said Lily, \"whatever it is, I must, of course, be made to know it. I begin to guess the truth. It will pain you to say it. Shall I read the letter?\"\n\nMrs. Dale was astonished at her calmness. It could not be that she had guessed the truth, or she would not stand like that, with tearless eyes and unquelled courage before her.\n\n\"You shall read it, but I ought to tell you first. Oh, my child, my own one!\" Lily was now leaning against the bed, and her mother was standing over her, caressing her.\n\n\"Then tell me,\" said she. \"But I know what it is. He has thought it all over while away from me, and he finds that it must not be as we have supposed. Before he went I offered to release him, and now he knows that he had better accept my offer. Is it so, mamma?\" In answer to this Mrs. Dale did not speak, but Lily understood from her signs that it was so.\n\n\"He might have written it to me, myself,\" said Lily, very proudly. \"Mamma, we will go down to breakfast. He has sent nothing to me, then?\"\n\n\"There is a note. He bids me read it, but I have not opened it. It is here.\"\n\n\"Give it me,\" said Lily, almost sternly. \"Let me have his last words to me;\" and she took the note from her mother's hands.\n\n\"Lily,\" said the note, \"your mother will have told you all. Before you read these few words you will know that you have trusted one who was quite untrustworthy. I know that you will hate me.--I cannot even ask you to forgive me. You will let me pray that you may yet be happy.--A. C.\"\n\nShe read these few words, still leaning against the bed. Then she got up, and walking to a chair, seated herself with her back to her mother. Mrs. Dale moving silently after her stood over the back of the chair, not daring to speak to her. So she sat for some five minutes, with her eyes fixed upon the open window, and with Crosbie's note in her hand.\n\n\"I will not hate him, and I do forgive him,\" she said at last, struggling to command her voice, and hardly showing that she could not altogether succeed in her attempt. \"I may not write to him again, but you shall write and tell him so. Now we will go down to breakfast.\" And so saying, she got up from her chair.\n\nMrs. Dale almost feared to speak to her, her composure was so complete, and her manner so stern and fixed. She hardly knew how to offer pity and sympathy, seeing that pity seemed to be so little necessary, and that even sympathy was not demanded. And she could not understand all that Lily had said. What had she meant by the offer to release him? Had there, then, been some quarrel between them before he went? Crosbie had made no such allusion in his letter. But Mrs. Dale did not dare to ask any questions.\n\n\"You frighten me, Lily,\" she said. \"Your very calmness frightens me.\"\n\n\"Dear mamma!\" and the poor girl absolutely smiled as she embraced her mother. \"You need not be frightened by my calmness. I know the truth well. I have been very unfortunate;--very. The brightest hopes of my life are all gone;--and I shall never again see him whom I love beyond all the world!\" Then at last she broke down, and wept in her mother's arms.\n\nThere was not a word of anger spoken then against him who had done all this. Mrs. Dale felt that she did not dare to speak in anger against him, and words of anger were not likely to come from poor Lily. She, indeed, hitherto did not know the whole of his offence, for she had not read his letter.\n\n\"Give it me, mamma,\" she said at last. \"It has to be done sooner or later.\"\n\n\"Not now, Lily. I have told you all,--all that you need know at present.\"\n\n\"Yes; now, mamma,\" and again that sweet silvery voice became stern. \"I will read it now, and there shall be an end.\" Whereupon Mrs. Dale gave her the letter and she read it in silence. Her mother, though standing somewhat behind her, watched her narrowly as she did so. She was now lying over upon the bed, and the letter was on the pillow, as she propped herself upon her arm. Her tears were running, and ever and again she would stop to dry her eyes. Her sobs, too, were very audible, but she went on steadily with her reading till she came to the line on which Crosbie told that he had already engaged himself to another woman. Then her mother could see that she paused suddenly, and that a shudder slightly convulsed all her limbs.\n\n\"He has been very quick,\" she said, almost in a whisper; and then she finished the letter. \"Tell him, mamma,\" she said, \"that I do forgive him, and I will not hate him. You will tell him that,--from me; will you not?\" And then she raised herself from the bed.\n\nMrs. Dale would give her no such assurance. In her present mood her feelings against Crosbie were of a nature which she herself hardly could understand or analyze. She felt that if he were present she could almost fly at him as would a tigress. She had never hated before as she now hated this man. He was to her a murderer, and worse than a murderer. He had made his way like a wolf into her little fold, and torn her ewe-lamb and left her maimed and mutilated for life. How could a mother forgive such an offence as that, or consent to be the medium through which forgiveness should be expressed?\n\n\"You must, mamma; or, if you do not, I shall do so. Remember that I love him. You know what it is to have loved one single man. He has made me very unhappy; I hardly know yet how unhappy. But I have loved him, and do love him. I believe, in my heart, that he still loves me. Where this has been there must not be hatred and unforgiveness.\"\n\n\"I will pray that I may become able to forgive him,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"But you must write to him those words. Indeed you must, mamma! 'She bids me tell you that she has forgiven you, and will not hate you.' Promise me that!\"\n\n\"I can make no promise now, Lily. I will think about it, and endeavour to do my duty.\"\n\nLily was now seated, and was holding the skirt of her mother's dress.\n\n\"Mamma,\" she said, looking up into her mother's face, \"you must be very good to me now; and I must be very good to you. We shall be always together now. I must be your friend and counsellor; and be everything to you, more than ever. I must fall in love with you now;\" and she smiled again, and the tears were almost dry upon her cheeks.\n\nAt last they went down to the breakfast-room, from which Bell had not moved. Mrs. Dale entered the room first, and Lily followed, hiding herself for a moment behind her mother. Then she came forward boldly, and taking Bell in her arms, clasped her close to her bosom.\n\n\"Bell,\" she said, \"he has gone.\"\n\n\"Lily! Lily! Lily!\" said Bell, weeping.\n\n\"He has gone! We shall talk it over in a few days, and shall know how to do so without losing ourselves in misery. To-day we will say no more about it. I am so thirsty, Bell; do give me my tea;\" and she sat herself down at the breakfast-table.\n\nLily's tea was given to her, and she drank it. Beyond that I cannot say that any of them partook with much heartiness of the meal. They sat there, as they would have sat if no terrible thunderbolt had fallen among them, and no word further was spoken about Crosbie and his conduct. Immediately after breakfast they went into the other room, and Lily, as was her wont, sat herself immediately down to her drawing. Her mother looked at her with wistful eyes, longing to bid her spare herself, but she shrank from interfering with her. For a quarter of an hour Lily sat over her board, with her brush or pencil in her hand, and then she rose up and put it away.\n\n\"It is no good pretending,\" she said. \"I am only spoiling the things; but I will be better to-morrow. I'll go away and lie down by myself, mamma.\" And so she went.\n\nSoon after this Mrs. Dale took her bonnet and went up to the Great House, having received her brother-in-law's message from Bell.\n\n\"I know what he has to tell me,\" she said; \"but I might as well go. It will be necessary that we should speak to each other about it.\" So she walked across the lawn, and up into the hall of the Great House. \"Is my brother in the book-room?\" she said to one of the maids; and then knocking at the door, went in unannounced.\n\nThe squire rose from his arm-chair, and came forward to meet her.\n\n\"Mary,\" he said, \"I believe you know it all.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"You can read that,\" and she handed him Crosbie's letter. \"How was one to know that any man could be so wicked as that?\"\n\n\"And she has heard it?\" asked the squire. \"Is she able to bear it?\"\n\n\"Wonderfully! She has amazed me by her strength. It frightens me; for I know that a relapse must come. She has never sunk for a moment beneath it. For myself, I feel as though it were her strength that enables me to bear my share of it.\" And then she described to the squire all that had taken place that morning.\n\n\"Poor child!\" said the squire. \"Poor child! What can we do for her? Would it be good for her to go away for a time? She is a sweet, good, lovely girl, and has deserved better than that. Sorrow and disappointment come to us all; but they are doubly heavy when they come so early.\"\n\nMrs. Dale was almost surprised at the amount of sympathy which he showed.\n\n\"And what is to be his punishment?\" she asked.\n\n\"The scorn which men and women will feel for him; those, at least, whose esteem or scorn are matters of concern to any one. I know no other punishment. You would not have Lily's name brought before a tribunal of law?\"\n\n\"Certainly not that.\"\n\n\"And I will not have Bernard calling him out. Indeed, it would be for nothing; for in these days a man is not expected to fight duels.\"\n\n\"You cannot think that I would wish that.\"\n\n\"What punishment is there, then? I know of none. There are evils which a man may do, and no one can punish him. I know of nothing. I went up to London after him, but he contrived to crawl out of my way. What can you do to a rat but keep clear of him?\"\n\nMrs. Dale had felt in her heart that it would be well if Crosbie could be beaten till all his bones were sore. I hardly know whether such should have been a woman's thought, but it was hers. She had no wish that he should be made to fight a duel. In that there would have been much that was wicked, and in her estimation nothing that was just. But she felt that if Bernard would thrash the coward for his cowardice she would love her nephew better than ever she had loved him. Bernard also had considered it probable that he might be expected to horsewhip the man who had jilted his cousin, and, as regarded the absolute bodily risk, he would not have felt any insuperable objection to undertake the task. But such a piece of work was disagreeable to him in many ways. He hated the idea of a row at his club. He was most desirous that his cousin's name should not be made public. He wished to avoid anything that might be impolitic. A wicked thing had been done, and he was quite ready to hate Crosbie as Crosbie ought to be hated; but as regarded himself, it made him unhappy to think that the world might probably expect him to punish the man who had so lately been his friend. And then he did not know where to catch him, or how to thrash him when caught. He was very sorry for his cousin, and felt strongly that Crosbie should not be allowed to escape. But what was he to do?\n\n\"Would she like to go anywhere?\" said the squire again, anxious, if he could, to afford solace by some act of generosity. At this moment he would have settled a hundred a year for life upon his niece if by so doing he could have done her any good.\n\n\"She will be better at home,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"Poor thing. For a while she will wish to avoid going out.\"\n\n\"I suppose so;\" and then there was a pause. \"I'll tell you what, Mary; I don't understand it. On my honour I don't understand it. It is to me as wonderful as though I had caught the man picking my pence out of my pocket. I don't think any man in the position of a gentleman would have done such a thing when I was young. I don't think any man would have dared to do it. But now it seems that a man may act in that way and no harm come to him. He had a friend in London who came to me and talked about it as though it were some ordinary, everyday transaction of life. Yes; you may come in, Bernard. The poor child knows it all now.\"\n\nBernard offered to his aunt what of solace and sympathy he had to offer, and made some sort of half-expressed apology for having introduced this wolf into their flock. \"We always thought very much of him at his club,\" said Bernard.\n\n\"I don't know much about your London clubs now-a-days,\" said his uncle, \"nor do I wish to do so if the society of that man can be endured after what he has now done.\"\n\n\"I don't suppose half-a-dozen men will ever know anything about it,\" said Bernard.\n\n\"Umph!\" ejaculated the squire. He could not say that he wished Crosbie's villany to be widely discussed, seeing that Lily's name was so closely connected with it. But yet he could not support the idea that Crosbie should not be punished by the frown of the world at large. It seemed to him that from this time forward any man speaking to Crosbie should be held to have disgraced himself by so doing.\n\n\"Give her my best love,\" he said, as Mrs. Dale got up to take her leave; \"my very best love. If her old uncle can do anything for her she has only to let me know. She met the man in my house, and I feel that I owe her much. Bid her come and see me. It will be better for her than moping at home. And Mary\"--this he said to her, whispering into her ear--\"think of what I said to you about Bell.\"\n\nMrs. Dale, as she walked back to her own house, acknowledged to herself that her brother-in-law's manner was different to her from anything that she had hitherto known of him.\n\nDuring the whole of that day Crosbie's name was not mentioned at the Small House. Neither of the girls stirred out, and Bell spent the greater part of the afternoon sitting, with her arm round her sister's waist, upon the sofa. Each of them had a book; but though there was little spoken, there was as little read. Who can describe the thoughts that were passing through Lily's mind as she remembered the hours which she had passed with Crosbie, of his warm assurances of love, of his accepted caresses, of her uncontrolled and acknowledged joy in his affection? It had all been holy to her then; and now those things which were then sacred had been made almost disgraceful by his fault. And yet as she thought of this she declared to herself over and over again that she would forgive him;--nay, that she had forgiven him. \"And he shall know it, too,\" she said, speaking almost out loud.\n\n\"Lily, dear Lily,\" said Bell, \"turn your thoughts away from it for a while, if you can.\"\n\n\"They won't go away,\" said Lily. And that was all that was said between them on the subject.\n\nEverybody would know it! I doubt whether that must not be one of the bitterest drops in the cup which a girl in such circumstances is made to drain. Lily perceived early in the day that the parlour-maid well knew that she had been jilted. The girl's manner was intended to convey sympathy; but it did convey pity; and Lily for a moment felt angry. But she remembered that it must be so, and smiled upon the girl, and spoke kindly to her. What mattered it? All the world would know it in a day or two.\n\nOn the following day she went up, by her mother's advice, to see her uncle.\n\n\"My child,\" said he, \"I am sorry for you. My heart bleeds for you.\"\n\n\"Uncle,\" she said, \"do not mind it. Only do this for me,--do not talk about it,--I mean to me.\"\n\n\"No, no; I will not. That there should ever have been in my house so great a rascal--\"\n\n\"Uncle! uncle! I will not have that! I will not listen to a word against him from any human being,--not a word! Remember that!\" And her eyes flashed as she spoke.\n\nHe did not answer her, but took her hand and pressed it, and then she left him. \"The Dales were ever constant!\" he said to himself, as he walked up and down the terrace before his house. \"Ever constant!\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI. THE WOUNDED FAWN.\n\nNearly two months passed away, and it was now Christmas time at Allington. It may be presumed that there was no intention at either house that the mirth should be very loud. Such a wound as that received by Lily Dale was one from which recovery could not be quick, and it was felt by all the family that a weight was upon them which made gaiety impracticable. As for Lily herself it may be said that she bore her misfortune with all a woman's courage. For the first week she stood up as a tree that stands against the wind, which is soon to be shivered to pieces because it will not bend. During that week her mother and sister were frightened by her calmness and endurance. She would perform her daily task. She would go out through the village, and appear at her place in church on the first Sunday. She would sit over her book of an evening, keeping back her tears; and would chide her mother and sister when she found that they were regarding her with earnest anxiety.\n\n\"Mamma, let it all be as though it had never been,\" she said.\n\n\"Ah, dear! if that were but possible!\"\n\n\"God forbid that it should be possible inwardly,\" Lily replied, \"but it is possible outwardly. I feel that you are more tender to me than you used to be, and that upsets me. If you would only scold me because I am idle, I should soon be better.\" But her mother could not speak to her as she perhaps might have spoken had no grief fallen upon her pet. She could not cease from those anxious tender glances which made Lily know that she was looked on as a fawn wounded almost to death.\n\nAt the end of the first week she gave way. \"I won't get up, Bell,\" she said one morning, almost petulantly. \"I am ill;--I had better lie here out of the way. Don't make a fuss about it. I'm stupid and foolish, and that makes me ill.\"\n\nThereupon Mrs. Dale and Bell were frightened, and looked into each other's blank faces, remembering stories of poor broken-hearted girls who had died because their loves had been unfortunate,--as small wax tapers whose lights are quenched if a breath of wind blows upon them too strongly. But then Lily was in truth no such slight taper as that. Nor was she the stem that must be broken because it will not bend. She bent herself to the blast during that week of illness, and then arose with her form still straight and graceful, and with her bright light unquenched.\n\nAfter that she would talk more openly to her mother about her loss,--openly and with a true appreciation of the misfortune which had befallen her; but with an assurance of strength which seemed to ridicule the idea of a broken heart. \"I know that I can bear it,\" she said, \"and that I can bear it without lasting unhappiness. Of course I shall always love him, and must feel almost as you felt when you lost my father.\"\n\nIn answer to this Mrs. Dale could say nothing. She could not speak out her thoughts about Crosbie, and explain to Lily that he was unworthy of her love. Love does not follow worth, and is not given to excellence;--nor is it destroyed by ill-usage, nor killed by blows and mutilation. When Lily declared that she still loved the man who had so ill-used her, Mrs. Dale would be silent. Each perfectly understood the other, but on that matter even they could not interchange their thoughts with freedom.\n\n\"You must promise never to be tired of me, mamma,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Mothers do not often get tired of their children, whatever the children may do of their mothers.\"\n\n\"I'm not so sure of that when the children turn out old maids. And I mean to have a will of my own, too, mamma; and a way also, if it be possible. When Bell is married I shall consider it a partnership, and I shan't do what I'm told any longer.\"\n\n\"Forewarned will be forearmed.\"\n\n\"Exactly;--and I don't want to take you by surprise. For a year or two longer, till Bell is gone, I mean to be dutiful; but it would be very stupid for a girl to be dutiful all her life.\"\n\nAll of which Mrs. Dale understood thoroughly. It amounted to an assertion on Lily's part that she had loved once and could never love again; that she had played her game, hoping, as other girls hope, that she might win the prize of a husband; but that, having lost, she could never play the game again. It was that inward conviction on Lily's part which made her say such words to her mother. But Mrs. Dale would by no means allow herself to share this conviction. She declared to herself that time would cure Lily's wound, and that her child might yet be crowned by the bliss of a happy marriage. She would not in her heart consent to that plan in accordance with which Lily's destiny in life was to be regarded as already fixed. She had never really liked Crosbie as a suitor, and would herself have preferred John Eames, with all the faults of his hobbledehoyhood on his head. It might yet come to pass that John Eames' love might be made happy.\n\nBut in the meantime Lily, as I have said, had become strong in her courage, and recommenced the work of living with no lackadaisical self-assurance that because she had been made more unhappy than others, therefore she should allow herself to be more idle. Morning and night she prayed for him, and daily, almost hour by hour, she assured herself that it was still her duty to love him. It was hard, this duty of loving, without any power of expressing such love. But still she would do her duty.\n\n\"Tell me at once, mamma,\" she said one morning, \"when you hear that the day is fixed for his marriage. Pray don't keep me in the dark.\"\n\n\"It is to be in February,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"But let me know the day. It must not be to me like ordinary days. But do not look unhappy, mamma; I am not going to make a fool of myself. I shan't steal off and appear in the church like a ghost.\" And then, having uttered her little joke, a sob came, and she hid her face on her mother's bosom. In a moment she raised it again. \"Believe me, mamma, that I am not unhappy,\" she said.\n\nAfter the expiration of that second week Mrs. Dale did write a letter to Crosbie:\n\nI suppose [she said] it is right that I should acknowledge the receipt of your letter. I do not know that I have aught else to say to you. It would not become me as a woman to say what I think of your conduct, but I believe that your conscience will tell you the same things. If it do not, you must, indeed, be hardened. I have promised my child that I will send to you a message from her. She bids me tell you that she has forgiven you, and that she does not hate you. May God also forgive you, and may you recover his love.\n\nI beg that no rejoinder may be made to this letter, either to myself or to any of my family.\n\nThe squire wrote no answer to the letter which he had received, nor did he take any steps towards the immediate punishment of Crosbie. Indeed he had declared that no such steps could be taken, explaining to his nephew that such a man could be served only as one serves a rat.\n\n\"I shall never see him,\" he said once again; \"if I did, I should not scruple to hit him on the head with my stick; but I should think ill of myself to go after him with such an object.\"\n\nAnd yet it was a terrible sorrow to the old man that the scoundrel who had so injured him and his should escape scot-free. He had not forgiven Crosbie. No idea of forgiveness had ever crossed his mind. He would have hated himself had he thought it possible that he could be induced to forgive such an injury. \"There is an amount of rascality in it,--of low meanness, which I do not understand,\" he would say over and over again to his nephew. And then as he would walk alone on the terrace he would speculate within his own mind whether Bernard would take any steps towards avenging his cousin's injury. \"He is right,\" he would say to himself; \"Bernard is quite right. But when I was young I could not have stood it. In those days a gentleman might have a fellow out who had treated him as he has treated us. A man was satisfied in feeling that he had done something. I suppose the world is different now-a-days.\" The world is different; but the squire by no means acknowledged in his heart that there had been any improvement.\n\nBernard also was greatly troubled in his mind. He would have had no objection to fight a duel with Crosbie, had duels in these days been possible. But he believed them to be no longer possible,--at any rate without ridicule. And if he could not fight the man, in what other way was he to punish him? Was it not the fact that for such a fault the world afforded no punishment? Was it not in the power of a man like Crosbie to amuse himself for a week or two at the expense of a girl's happiness for life, and then to escape absolutely without any ill effects to himself? \"I shall be barred out of my club lest I should meet him,\" Bernard said to himself, \"but he will not be barred out.\" Moreover, there was a feeling within him that the matter would be one of triumph to Crosbie rather than otherwise. In having secured for himself the pleasure of his courtship with such a girl as Lily Dale, without encountering the penalty usually consequent upon such amusement, he would be held by many as having merited much admiration. He had sinned against all the Dales, and yet the suffering arising from his sin was to fall upon the Dales exclusively. Such was Bernard's reasoning, as he speculated on the whole affair, sadly enough,--wishing to be avenged, but not knowing where to look for vengeance. For myself I believe him to have been altogether wrong as to the light in which he supposed that Crosbie's falsehood would be regarded by Crosbie's friends. Men will still talk of such things lightly, professing that all is fair in love as it is in war, and speaking almost with envy of the good fortunes of a practised deceiver. But I have never come across the man who thought in this way with reference to an individual case. Crosbie's own judgment as to the consequences to himself of what he had done was more correct than that formed by Bernard Dale. He had regarded the act as venial as long as it was still to do,--while it was still within his power to leave it undone; but from the moment of its accomplishment it had forced itself upon his own view in its proper light. He knew that he had been a scoundrel, and he knew that other men would so think of him. His friend Fowler Pratt, who had the reputation of looking at women simply as toys, had so regarded him. Instead of boasting of what he had done, he was as afraid of alluding to any matter connected with his marriage as a man is of talking of the articles which he has stolen. He had already felt that men at his club looked askance at him; and, though he was no coward as regarded his own skin and bones, he had an undefined fear lest some day he might encounter Bernard Dale purposely armed with a stick. The squire and his nephew were wrong in supposing that Crosbie was unpunished.\n\nAnd as the winter came on he felt that he was closely watched by the noble family of De Courcy. Some of that noble family he had already learned to hate cordially. The Honourable John came up to town in November, and persecuted him vilely;--insisted on having dinners given to him at Sebright's, of smoking throughout the whole afternoon in his future brother-in-law's rooms, and on borrowing his future brother-in-law's possessions; till at last Crosbie determined that it would be wise to quarrel with the Honourable John,--and he quarrelled with him accordingly, turning him out of his rooms, and telling him in so many words that he would have no more to do with him.\n\n\"You'll have to do it, as I did,\" Mortimer Gazebee had said to him; \"I didn't like it because of the family, but Lady Amelia told me that it must be so.\" Whereupon Crosbie took the advice of Mortimer Gazebee.\n\nBut the hospitality of the Gazebees was perhaps more distressing to him than even the importunities of the Honourable John. It seemed as though his future sister-in-law was determined not to leave him alone. Mortimer was sent to fetch him up for the Sunday afternoons, and he found that he was constrained to go to the villa in St. John's Wood, even in opposition to his own most strenuous will. He could not quite analyze the circumstances of his own position, but he felt as though he were a cock with his spurs cut off,--as a dog with his teeth drawn. He found himself becoming humble and meek. He had to acknowledge to himself that he was afraid of Lady Amelia, and almost even afraid of Mortimer Gazebee. He was aware that they watched him, and knew all his goings out and comings in. They called him Adolphus, and made him tame. That coming evil day in February was dinned into his ears. Lady Amelia would go and look at furniture for him, and talked by the hour about bedding and sheets. \"You had better get your kitchen things at Tomkins'. They're all good, and he'll give you ten per cent. off if you pay him ready money,--which of course you will, you know!\" Was it for this that he had sacrificed Lily Dale?--for this that he had allied himself with the noble house of De Courcy?\n\nMortimer had been at him about the settlements from the very first moment of his return to London, and had already bound him up hand and foot. His life was insured, and the policy was in Mortimer's hands. His own little bit of money had been already handed over to be tied up with Lady Alexandrina's little bit. It seemed to him that in all the arrangements made the intention was that he should die off speedily, and that Lady Alexandrina should be provided with a decent little income, sufficient for St. John's Wood. Things were to be so settled that he could not even spend the proceeds of his own money, or of hers. They were to go, under the fostering hand of Mortimer Gazebee, in paying insurances. If he would only die the day after his marriage, there would really be a very nice sum of money for Alexandrina, almost worthy of the acceptance of an earl's daughter. Six months ago he would have considered himself able to turn Mortimer Gazebee round his finger on any subject that could be introduced between them. When they chanced to meet Gazebee had been quite humble to him, treating him almost as a superior being. He had looked down on Gazebee from a very great height. But now it seemed as though he were powerless in this man's hands.\n\nBut perhaps the countess had become his greatest aversion. She was perpetually writing to him little notes in which she gave him multitudes of commissions, sending him about as though he had been her servant. And she pestered him with advice which was even worse than her commissions, telling him of the style of life in which Alexandrina would expect to live, and warning him very frequently that such an one as he could not expect to be admitted within the bosom of so noble a family without paying very dearly for that inestimable privilege. Her letters had become odious to him, and he would chuck them on one side, leaving them for the whole day unopened. He had already made up his mind that he would quarrel with the countess also, very shortly after his marriage; indeed, that he would separate himself from the whole family if it were possible. And yet he had entered into this engagement mainly with the view of reaping those advantages which would accrue to him from being allied to the De Courcys! The squire and his nephew were wretched in thinking that this man was escaping without punishment, but they might have spared themselves that misery.\n\nIt had been understood from the first that he was to spend his Christmas at Courcy Castle. From this undertaking it was quite out of his power to enfranchise himself: but he resolved that his visit should be as short as possible. Christmas Day unfortunately came on a Monday, and it was known to the De Courcy world that Saturday was almost a dies non at the General Committee Office. As to those three days there was no escape for him; but he made Alexandrina understand that the three Commissioners were men of iron as to any extension of those three days. \"I must be absent again in February, of course,\" he said, almost making his wail audible in the words he used, \"and therefore it is quite impossible that I should stay now beyond the Monday.\" Had there been attractions for him at Courcy Castle I think he might have arranged with Mr. Optimist for a week or ten days. \"We shall be all alone,\" the countess wrote to him, \"and I hope you will have an opportunity of learning more of our ways than you have ever really been able to do as yet.\" This was bitter as gall to him. But in this world all valuable commodities have their price; and when men such as Crosbie aspire to obtain for themselves an alliance with noble families, they must pay the market price for the article which they purchase.\n\n\"You'll all come up and dine with us on Monday,\" the squire said to Mrs. Dale, about the middle of the previous week.\n\n\"Well, I think not,\" said Mrs. Dale; \"we are better, perhaps, as we are.\"\n\nAt this moment the squire and his sister-in-law were on much more friendly terms than had been usual with them, and he took her reply in good part, understanding her feeling. Therefore, he pressed his request, and succeeded.\n\n\"I think you're wrong,\" he said; \"I don't suppose that we shall have a very merry Christmas. You and the girls will hardly have that whether you eat your pudding here or at the Great House. But it will be better for us all to make the attempt. It's the right thing to do. That's the way I look at it.\"\n\n\"I'll ask Lily,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Do, do. Give her my love, and tell her from me that, in spite of all that has come and gone, Christmas Day should still be to her a day of rejoicing. We'll dine about three, so that the servants can have the afternoon.\"\n\n\"Of course we'll go,\" said Lily; \"why not? We always do. And we'll have blind-man's-buff with all the Boyces, as we had last year, if uncle will ask them up.\" But the Boyces were not asked up for that occasion.\n\nBut Lily, though she put on it all so brave a face, had much to suffer, and did in truth suffer greatly. If you, my reader, ever chanced to slip into the gutter on a wet day, did you not find that the sympathy of the bystanders was by far the severest part of your misfortune? Did you not declare to yourself that all might yet be well, if the people would only walk on and not look at you? And yet you cannot blame those who stood and pitied you; or, perhaps, essayed to rub you down, and assist you in the recovery of your bedaubed hat. You, yourself, if you see a man fall, cannot walk by as though nothing uncommon had happened to him. It was so with Lily. The people of Allington could not regard her with their ordinary eyes. They would look at her tenderly, knowing that she was a wounded fawn, and thus they aggravated the soreness of her wound. Old Mrs. Hearn condoled with her, telling her that very likely she would be better off as she was. Lily would not lie about it in any way. \"Mrs. Hearn,\" she said, \"the subject is painful to me.\" Mrs. Hearn said no more about it, but on every meeting between them she looked the things she did not say. \"Miss Lily!\" said Hopkins, one day, \"Miss Lily!\"--and as he looked up into her face a tear had almost formed itself in his old eye--\"I knew what he was from the first. Oh, dear! oh, dear! if I could have had him killed!\" \"Hopkins, how dare you?\" said Lily. \"If you speak to me again in such a way, I will tell my uncle.\" She turned away from him; but immediately turned back again, and put out her little hand to him. \"I beg your pardon,\" she said. \"I know how kind you are, and I love you for it.\" And then she went away. \"I'll go after him yet, and break the dirty neck of him,\" said Hopkins to himself, as he walked down the path.\n\nShortly before Christmas Day she called with her sister at the vicarage. Bell, in the course of the visit, left the room with one of the Boyce girls, to look at the last chrysanthemums of the year. Then Mrs. Boyce took advantage of the occasion to make her little speech. \"My dear Lily,\" she said, \"you will think me cold if I do not say one word to you.\" \"No, I shall not,\" said Lily, almost sharply, shrinking from the finger that threatened to touch her sore. \"There are things which should never be talked about.\" \"Well, well; perhaps so,\" said Mrs. Boyce. But for a minute or two she was unable to fall back upon any other topic, and sat looking at Lily with painful tenderness. I need hardly say what were Lily's sufferings under such a gaze; but she bore it, acknowledging to herself in her misery that the fault did not lie with Mrs. Boyce. How could Mrs. Boyce have looked at her otherwise than tenderly?\n\nIt was settled, then, that Lily was to dine up at the Great House on Christmas Day, and thus show to the Allington world that she was not to be regarded as a person shut out from the world by the depth of her misfortune. That she was right there can, I think, be no doubt; but as she walked across the little bridge, with her mother and sister, after returning from church, she would have given much to be able to have turned round, and have gone to bed instead of to her uncle's dinner.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII. PAWKINS'S IN JERMYN STREET.\n\nThe show of fat beasts in London took place this year on the twentieth day of December, and I have always understood that a certain bullock exhibited by Lord De Guest was declared by the metropolitan butchers to have realized all the possible excellences of breeding, feeding, and condition. No doubt the butchers of the next half-century will have learned much better, and the Guestwick beast, could it be embalmed and then produced, would excite only ridicule at the agricultural ignorance of the present age; but Lord De Guest took the praise that was offered to him, and found himself in a seventh heaven of delight. He was never so happy as when surrounded by butchers, graziers, and salesmen who were able to appreciate the work of his life, and who regarded him as a model nobleman. \"Look at that fellow,\" he said to Eames, pointing to the prize bullock. Eames had joined his patron at the show after his office hours, looking on upon the living beef by gaslight. \"Isn't he like his sire? He was got by Lambkin, you know.\"\n\n\"Lambkin,\" said Johnny, who had not as yet been able to learn much about the Guestwick stock.\n\n\"Yes, Lambkin. The bull that we had the trouble with. He has just got his sire's back and fore-quarters. Don't you see?\"\n\n\"I daresay,\" said Johnny, who looked very hard, but could not see.\n\n\"It's very odd,\" exclaimed the earl, \"but do you know, that bull has been as quiet since that day,--as quiet as--as anything. I think it must have been my pocket-handkerchief.\"\n\n\"I daresay it was,\" said Johnny;--\"or perhaps the flies.\"\n\n\"Flies!\" said the earl, angrily. \"Do you suppose he isn't used to flies? Come away. I ordered dinner at seven, and it's past six now. My brother-in-law, Colonel Dale, is up in town, and he dines with us.\" So he took Johnny's arm, and led him off through the show, calling his attention as he went to several beasts which were inferior to his own.\n\nAnd then they walked down through Portman Square and Grosvenor Square, and across Piccadilly to Jermyn Street. John Eames acknowledged to himself that it was odd that he should have an earl leaning on his arm as he passed along through the streets. At home, in his own life, his daily companions were Cradell and Amelia Roper, Mrs. Lupex and Mrs. Roper. The difference was very great, and yet he found it quite as easy to talk to the earl as to Mrs. Lupex.\n\n\"You know the Dales down at Allington, of course,\" said the earl.\n\n\"Oh, yes, I know them.\"\n\n\"But, perhaps, you never met the colonel.\"\n\n\"I don't think I ever did.\"\n\n\"He's a queer sort of fellow;--very well in his way, but he never does anything. He and my sister live at Torquay, and as far as I can find out, they neither of them have any occupation of any sort. He's come up to town now because we both had to meet our family lawyers and sign some papers, but he looks on the journey as a great hardship. As for me, I'm a year older than he is, but I wouldn't mind going up and down from Guestwick every day.\"\n\n\"It's looking after the bull that does it,\" said Eames.\n\n\"By George! you're right, Master Johnny. My sister and Crofts may tell me what they like, but when a man's out in the open air for eight or nine hours every day, it doesn't much matter where he goes to sleep after that. This is Pawkins's,--capital good house, but not so good as it used to be while old Pawkins was alive. Show Mr. Eames up into a bedroom to wash his hands.\"\n\nColonel Dale was much like his brother in face, but was taller, even thinner, and apparently older. When Eames went into the sitting-room, the colonel was there alone, and had to take upon himself the trouble of introducing himself. He did not get up from his arm-chair, but nodded gently at the young man. \"Mr. Eames, I believe? I knew your father at Guestwick, a great many years ago;\" then he turned his face back towards the fire and sighed.\n\n\"It's got very cold this afternoon,\" said Johnny, trying to make conversation.\n\n\"It's always cold in London,\" said the colonel.\n\n\"If you had to be here in August you wouldn't say so.\"\n\n\"God forbid,\" said the colonel, and he sighed again, with his eyes fixed upon the fire. Eames had heard of the very gallant way in which Orlando Dale had persisted in running away with Lord De Guest's sister, in opposition to very terrible obstacles, and as he now looked at the intrepid lover, he thought that there must have been a great change since those days. After that nothing more was said till the earl came down.\n\nPawkins's house was thoroughly old-fashioned in all things, and the Pawkins of that day himself stood behind the earl's elbow when the dinner began, and himself removed the cover from the soup tureen. Lord De Guest did not require much personal attention, but he would have felt annoyed if this hadn't been done. As it was he had a civil word to say to Pawkins about the fat cattle, thereby showing that he did not mistake Pawkins for one of the waiters. Pawkins then took his lordship's orders about the wine and retired.\n\n\"He keeps up the old house pretty well,\" said the earl to his brother-in-law. \"It isn't like what it was thirty years ago, but then everything of that sort has got worse and worse.\"\n\n\"I suppose it has,\" said the colonel.\n\n\"I remember when old Pawkins had as good a glass of port as I've got at home,--or nearly. They can't get it now, you know.\"\n\n\"I never drink port,\" said the colonel. \"I seldom take anything after dinner, except a little negus.\"\n\nHis brother-in-law said nothing, but made a most eloquent grimace as he turned his face towards his soup-plate. Eames saw it, and could hardly refrain from laughing. When, at half-past nine o'clock, the colonel retired from the room, the earl, as the door was closed, threw up his hands, and uttered the one word \"negus!\" Then Eames took heart of grace and had his laughter out.\n\nThe dinner was very dull, and before the colonel went to bed Johnny regretted that he had been induced to dine at Pawkins's. It might be a very fine thing to be asked to dinner with an earl, and John Eames had perhaps received at his office some little accession of dignity from the circumstance, of which he had been not unpleasantly aware; but, as he sat at the table, on which there were four or five apples and a plate of dried nuts, looking at the earl, as he endeavoured to keep his eyes open, and at the colonel, to whom it seemed absolutely a matter of indifference whether his companions were asleep or awake, he confessed to himself that the price he was paying was almost too dear. Mrs. Roper's tea-table was not pleasant to him, but even that would have been preferable to the black dinginess of Pawkins's mahogany, with the company of two tired old men, with whom he seemed to have no mutual subject of conversation. Once or twice he tried a word with the colonel, for the colonel sat with his eyes open looking at the fire. But he was answered with monosyllables, and it was evident to him that the colonel did not wish to talk. To sit still, with his hands closed over each other on his lap, was work enough for Colonel Dale during his after-dinner hours.\n\nBut the earl knew what was going on. During that terrible conflict between him and his slumber, in which the drowsy god fairly vanquished him for some twenty minutes, his conscience was always accusing him of treating his guests badly. He was very angry with himself, and tried to arouse himself and talk. But his brother-in-law would not help him in his efforts; and even Eames was not bright in rendering him assistance. Then for twenty minutes he slept soundly, and at the end of that he woke himself with one of his own snorts. \"By George!\" he said, jumping up and standing on the rug, \"we'll have some coffee;\" and after that he did not sleep any more.\n\n\"Dale,\" said he, \"won't you take some more wine?\"\n\n\"Nothing more,\" said the colonel, still looking at the fire, and shaking his head very slowly.\n\n\"Come, Johnny, fill your glass.\" He had already got into the way of calling his young friend Johnny, having found that Mrs. Eames generally spoke of her son by that name.\n\n\"I have been filling my glass all the time,\" said Eames, taking the decanter again in his hand as he spoke.\n\n\"I'm glad you've found something to amuse you, for it has seemed to me that you and Dale haven't had much to say to each other. I've been listening all the time.\"\n\n\"You've been asleep,\" said the colonel.\n\n\"Then there's been some excuse for my holding my tongue,\" said the earl. \"By-the-by, Dale, what do you think of that fellow Crosbie?\"\n\nEames' ears were instantly on the alert, and the spirit of dullness vanished from him.\n\n\"Think of him?\" said the colonel.\n\n\"He ought to have every bone in his skin broken,\" said the earl.\n\n\"So he ought,\" said Eames, getting up from his chair in his eagerness, and speaking in a tone somewhat louder than was perhaps becoming in the presence of his seniors. \"So he ought, my lord. He is the most abominable rascal that ever I met in my life. I wish I was Lily Dale's brother.\" Then he sat down again, remembering that he was speaking in the presence of Lily's uncle, and of the father of Bernard Dale, who might be supposed to occupy the place of Lily's brother.\n\nThe colonel turned his head round, and looked at the young man with surprise. \"I beg your pardon, sir,\" said Eames, \"but I have known Mrs. Dale and your nieces all my life.\"\n\n\"Oh, have you?\" said the colonel. \"Nevertheless it is, perhaps, as well not to make too free with a young lady's name. Not that I blame you in the least, Mr. Eames.\"\n\n\"I should think not,\" said the earl. \"I honour him for his feeling. Johnny, my boy, if ever I am unfortunate enough to meet that man, I shall tell him my mind, and I believe you will do the same.\" On hearing this John Eames winked at the earl, and made a motion with his head towards the colonel, whose back was turned to him. And then the earl winked back at Eames.\n\n\"De Guest,\" said the colonel, \"I think I'll go upstairs; I always have a little arrowroot in my own room.\"\n\n\"I'll ring the bell for a candle,\" said the host. Then the colonel went, and as the door was closed behind him, the earl raised his two hands and uttered that single word, \"negus!\" Whereupon Johnny burst out laughing, and coming round to the fire, sat himself down in the arm-chair which the colonel had left.\n\n\"I've no doubt it's all right,\" said the earl; \"but I shouldn't like to drink negus myself, nor yet to have arrowroot up in my bedroom.\"\n\n\"I don't suppose there's any harm in it.\"\n\n\"Oh dear, no; I wonder what Pawkins says about him. But I suppose they have them of all sorts in an hotel.\"\n\n\"The waiter didn't seem to think much of it when he brought it.\"\n\n\"No, no. If he'd asked for senna and salts, the waiter wouldn't have showed any surprise. By-the-by, you touched him up about that poor girl.\"\n\n\"Did I, my lord? I didn't mean it.\"\n\n\"You see he's Bernard Dale's father, and the question is, whether Bernard shouldn't punish the fellow for what he has done. Somebody ought to do it. It isn't right that he should escape. Somebody ought to let Mr. Crosbie know what a scoundrel he has made himself.\"\n\n\"I'd do it to-morrow, only I'm afraid--\"\n\n\"No, no, no,\" said the earl; \"you are not the right person at all. What have you got to do with it? You've merely known them as family friends, but that's not enough.\"\n\n\"No, I suppose not,\" said Eames, sadly.\n\n\"Perhaps it's best as it is,\" said the earl. \"I don't know that any good would be got by knocking him over the head. And if we are to be Christians, I suppose we ought to be Christians.\"\n\n\"What sort of a Christian has he been?\"\n\n\"That's true enough; and if I was Bernard, I should be very apt to forget my Bible lessons about meekness.\"\n\n\"Do you know, my lord, I should think it the most Christian thing in the world to pitch into him; I should, indeed. There are some things for which a man ought to be beaten black and blue.\"\n\n\"So that he shouldn't do them again?\"\n\n\"Exactly. You might say it isn't Christian to hang a man.\"\n\n\"I'd always hang a murderer. It wasn't right to hang men for stealing sheep.\"\n\n\"Much better hang such a fellow as Crosbie,\" said Eames.\n\n\"Well, I believe so. If any fellow wanted now to curry favour with the young lady, what an opportunity he'd have.\"\n\nJohnny remained silent for a moment or two before he answered. \"I'm not so sure of that,\" he said, mournfully, as though grieving at the thought that there was no chance of currying favour with Lily by thrashing her late lover.\n\n\"I don't pretend to know much about girls,\" said Lord De Guest; \"but I should think it would be so. I should fancy that nothing would please her so much as hearing that he had caught it, and that all the world knew that he'd caught it.\" The earl had declared that he didn't know much about girls, and in so saying, he was no doubt right.\n\n\"If I thought so,\" said Eames, \"I'd find him out to-morrow.\"\n\n\"Why so? what difference does it make to you?\" Then there was another pause, during which Johnny looked very sheepish. \"You don't mean to say that you're in love with Miss Lily Dale?\"\n\n\"I don't know much about being in love with her,\" said Johnny, turning very red as he spoke. And then he made up his mind, in a wild sort of way, to tell all the truth to his friend. Pawkins's port wine may, perhaps, have had something to do with the resolution. \"But I'd go through fire and water for her, my lord. I knew her years before he had ever seen her, and have loved her a great deal better than he will ever love any one. When I heard that she had accepted him, I had half a mind to cut my own throat,--or else his.\"\n\n\"Highty tighty,\" said the earl.\n\n\"It's very ridiculous, I know,\" said Johnny, \"and of course she would never have accepted me.\"\n\n\"I don't see that at all.\"\n\n\"I haven't a shilling in the world.\"\n\n\"Girls don't care much for that.\"\n\n\"And then a clerk in the Income-tax Office! It's such a poor thing.\"\n\n\"The other fellow was only a clerk in another office.\"\n\nThe earl living down at Guestwick did not understand that the Income-tax Office in the city, and the General Committee Office at Whitehall, were as far apart as Dives and Lazarus, and separated by as impassable a gulf.\n\n\"Oh, yes,\" said Johnny; \"but his office is another kind of thing, and then he was a swell himself.\"\n\n\"By George, I don't see it,\" said the earl.\n\n\"I don't wonder a bit at her accepting a fellow like that. I hated him the first moment I saw him; but that's no reason she should hate him. He had that sort of manner, you know. He was a swell, and girls like that kind of thing. I never felt angry with her, but I could have eaten him.\" As he spoke he looked as though he would have made some such attempt had Crosbie been present.\n\n\"Did you ever ask her to have you?\" said the earl.\n\n\"No; how could I ask her, when I hadn't bread to give her?\"\n\n\"And you never told her--that you were in love with her, I mean, and all that kind of thing.\"\n\n\"She knows it now,\" said Johnny; \"I went to say good-by to her the other day,--when I thought she was going to be married. I could not help telling her then.\"\n\n\"But it seems to me, my dear fellow, that you ought to be very much obliged to Crosbie;--that is to say, if you've a mind to--\"\n\n\"I know what you mean, my lord. I am not a bit obliged to him. It's my belief that all this will about kill her. As to myself, if I thought she'd ever have me--\"\n\nThen he was again silent, and the earl could see that the tears were in his eyes.\n\n\"I think I begin to understand it,\" said the earl, \"and I'll give you a bit of advice. You come down and spend your Christmas with me at Guestwick.\"\n\n\"Oh, my lord!\"\n\n\"Never mind my-lording me, but do as I tell you. Lady Julia sent you a message, though I forgot all about it till now. She wants to thank you herself for what you did in the field.\"\n\n\"That's all nonsense, my lord.\"\n\n\"Very well; you can tell her so. You may take my word for this, too,--my sister hates Crosbie quite as much as you do. I think she'd 'pitch into him,' as you call it, herself, if she knew how. You come down to Guestwick for the Christmas, and then go over to Allington and tell them all plainly what you mean.\"\n\n\"I couldn't say a word to her now.\"\n\n\"Say it to the squire, then. Go to him, and tell him what you mean,--holding your head up like a man. Don't talk to me about swells. The man who means honestly is the best swell I know. He's the only swell I recognize. Go to old Dale, and say you come from me,--from Guestwick Manor. Tell him that if he'll put a little stick under the pot to make it boil, I'll put a bigger one. He'll understand what that means.\"\n\n\"Oh, no, my lord.\"\n\n\"But I say, oh, yes;\" and the earl, who was now standing on the rug before the fire, dug his hands deep down into his trousers' pockets. \"I'm very fond of that girl, and would do much for her. You ask Lady Julia if I didn't say so to her before I ever knew of your casting a sheep's-eye that way. And I've a sneaking kindness for you too, Master Johnny. Lord bless you, I knew your father as well as I ever knew any man; and to tell the truth, I believe I helped to ruin him. He held land of me, you know, and there can't be any doubt that he did ruin himself. He knew no more about a beast when he'd done, than--than--than that waiter. If he'd gone on to this day he wouldn't have been any wiser.\"\n\nJohnny sat silent, with his eyes full of tears. What was he to say to his friend?\n\n\"You come down with me,\" continued the earl, \"and you'll find we'll make it all straight. I daresay you're right about not speaking to the girl just at present. But tell everything to the uncle, and then to the mother. And, above all things, never think that you're not good enough yourself. A man should never think that. My belief is that in life people will take you very much at your own reckoning. If you are made of dirt, like that fellow Crosbie, you'll be found out at last, no doubt. But then I don't think you are made of dirt.\"\n\n\"I hope not.\"\n\n\"And so do I. You can come down, I suppose, with me the day after to-morrow?\"\n\n\"I'm afraid not. I have had all my leave.\"\n\n\"Shall I write to old Buffle, and ask it as a favour?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Johnny; \"I shouldn't like that. But I'll see to-morrow, and then I'll let you know. I can go down by the mail train on Saturday, at any rate.\"\n\n\"That won't be comfortable. See and come with me if you can. Now, good-night, my dear fellow, and remember this,--when I say a thing I mean it. I think I may boast that I never yet went back from my word.\"\n\nThe earl as he spoke gave his left hand to his guest, and looking somewhat grandly up over the young man's head, he tapped his own breast thrice with his right hand. As he went through the little scene, John Eames felt that he was every inch an earl.\n\n\"I don't know what to say to you, my lord.\"\n\n\"Say nothing,--not a word more to me. But say to yourself that faint heart never won fair lady. Good-night, my dear boy, good-night. I dine out to-morrow, but you can call and let me know at about six.\"\n\nEames then left the room without another word, and walked out into the cold air of Jermyn Street. The moon was clear and bright, and the pavement in the shining light seemed to be as clean as a lady's hand. All the world was altered to him since he had entered Pawkins's Hotel. Was it then possible that Lily Dale might even yet become his wife? Could it be true that he, even now, was in a position to go boldly to the Squire of Allington, and tell him what were his views with reference to Lily? And how far would he be justified in taking the earl at his word? Some incredible amount of wealth would be required before he could marry Lily Dale. Two or three hundred pounds a year at the very least! The earl could not mean him to understand that any such sum as that would be made up with such an object! Nevertheless he resolved as he walked home to Burton Crescent that he would go down to Guestwick, and that he would obey the earl's behest. As regarded Lily herself he felt that nothing could be said to her for many a long day as yet.\n\n\"Oh, John, how late you are!\" said Amelia, slipping out from the back parlour as he let himself in with his latch-key.\n\n\"Yes, I am;--very late,\" said John, taking his candle, and passing her by on the stairs without another word.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII. \"THE TIME WILL COME.\"\n\n\"Did you hear that young Eames is staying at Guestwick Manor?\"\n\nAs these were the first words which the squire spoke to Mrs. Dale as they walked together up to the Great House, after church, on Christmas Day, it was clear enough that the tidings of Johnny's visit, when told to him, had made some impression.\n\n\"At Guestwick Manor!\" said Mrs. Dale. \"Dear me! Do you hear that, Bell? There's promotion for Master Johnny!\"\n\n\"Don't you remember, mamma,\" said Bell, \"that he helped his lordship in his trouble with the bull?\"\n\nLily, who remembered accurately all the passages of her last interview with John Eames, said nothing, but felt, in some sort, sore at the idea that he should be so near her at such a time. In some unconscious way she had liked him for coming to her and saying all that he did say. She valued him more highly after that scene than she did before. But now, she would feel herself injured and hurt if he ever made his way into her presence under circumstances as they existed.\n\n\"I should not have thought that Lord De Guest was the man to show so much gratitude for so slight a favour,\" said the squire. \"However, I'm going to dine there to-morrow.\"\n\n\"To meet young Eames?\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Yes,--especially to meet young Eames. At least, I've been very specially asked to come, and I've been told that he is to be there.\"\n\n\"And is Bernard going?\"\n\n\"Indeed I'm not,\" said Bernard. \"I shall come over and dine with you.\"\n\nA half-formed idea flitted across Lily's mind, teaching her to imagine for a moment that she might possibly be concerned in this arrangement. But the thought vanished as quickly as it came, merely leaving some soreness behind it. There are certain maladies which make the whole body sore. The patient, let him be touched on any point,--let him even be nearly touched,--will roar with agony as though his whole body had been bruised. So it is also with maladies of the mind. Sorrows such as that of poor Lily's leave the heart sore at every point, and compel the sufferer to be ever in fear of new wounds. Lily bore her cross bravely and well; but not the less did it weigh heavily upon her at every turn because she had the strength to walk as though she did not bear it. Nothing happened to her, or in her presence, that did not in some way connect itself with her misery. Her uncle was going over to meet John Eames at Lord De Guest's. Of course the men there would talk about her, and all such talking was an injury to her.\n\nThe afternoon of that day did not pass away brightly. As long as the servants were in the room the dinner went on much as other dinners. At such times a certain amount of hypocrisy must always be practised in closely domestic circles. At mixed dinner-parties people can talk before Richard and William the same words that they would use if Richard and William were not there. People so mixed do not talk together their inward home thoughts. But when close friends are together, a little conscious reticence is practised till the door is tiled. At such a meeting as this that conscious reticence was of service, and created an effect which was salutary. When the door was tiled, and when the servants were gone, how could they be merry together? By what mirth should the beards be made to wag on that Christmas Day?\n\n\"My father has been up in town,\" said Bernard. \"He was with Lord De Guest at Pawkins's.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you go and see him?\" asked Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Well, I don't know. He did not seem to wish it. I shall go down to Torquay in February. I must be up in London, you know, in a fortnight, for good.\" Then they were all silent again for a few minutes. If Bernard could have owned the truth, he would have acknowledged that he had not gone up to London, because he did not yet know how to treat Crosbie when he should meet him. His thoughts on this matter threw some sort of shadow across poor Lily's mind, making her feel that her wound was again opened.\n\n\"I want him to give up his profession altogether,\" said the squire, speaking firmly and slowly. \"It would be better, I think, for both of us that he should do so.\"\n\n\"Would it be wise at his time of life,\" said Mrs. Dale, \"and when he has been doing so well?\"\n\n\"I think it would be wise. If he were my son it would be thought better that he should live here upon the property, among the people who are to become his tenants, than remain up in London, or perhaps be sent to India. He has one profession as the heir of this place, and that, I think, should be enough.\"\n\n\"I should have but an idle life of it down here,\" said Bernard.\n\n\"That would be your own fault. But if you did as I would have you, your life would not be idle.\" In this he was alluding to Bernard's proposed marriage, but as to that nothing further could be said in Bell's presence. Bell understood it all, and sat quite silent, with demure countenance;--perhaps even with something of sternness in her face.\n\n\"But the fact is,\" said Mrs. Dale, speaking in a low tone, and having well considered what she was about to say, \"that Bernard is not exactly the same as your son.\"\n\n\"Why not?\" said the squire. \"I have even offered to settle the property on him if he will leave the service.\"\n\n\"You do not owe him so much as you would owe your son; and, therefore, he does not owe you as much as he would owe his father.\"\n\n\"If you mean that I cannot constrain him, I know that well enough. As regards money, I have offered to do for him quite as much as any father would feel called upon to do for an only son.\"\n\n\"I hope you don't think me ungrateful,\" said Bernard.\n\n\"No, I do not; but I think you unmindful. I have nothing more to say about it, however;--not about that. If you should marry--\" And then he stopped himself, feeling that he could not go on in Bell's presence.\n\n\"If he should marry,\" said Mrs. Dale, \"it may well be that his wife would like a house of her own.\"\n\n\"Wouldn't she have this house?\" said the squire, angrily. \"Isn't it big enough? I only want one room for myself, and I'd give up that if it were necessary.\"\n\n\"That's nonsense,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"It isn't nonsense,\" said the squire.\n\n\"You'll be squire of Allington for the next twenty years,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"And as long as you are the squire, you'll be master of this house; at least, I hope so. I don't approve of monarchs abdicating in favour of young people.\"\n\n\"I don't think uncle Christopher would look at all well like Charles the Fifth,\" said Lily.\n\n\"I would always keep a cell for you, my darling, if I did,\" said the squire, regarding her with that painful, special tenderness. Lily, who was sitting next to Mrs. Dale, put her hand out secretly and got hold of her mother's, thereby indicating that she did not intend to occupy the cell offered to her by her uncle; or to look to him as the companion of her monastic seclusion. After that there was nothing more then said as to Bernard's prospects.\n\n\"Mrs. Hearn is dining at the vicarage, I suppose?\" asked the squire.\n\n\"Yes; she went in after church,\" said Bell. \"I saw her go with Mrs. Boyce.\"\n\n\"She told me she never would dine with them again after dark in winter,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"The last time she was there, the boy let the lamp blow out as she was going home, and she lost her way. The truth was, she was angry because Mr. Boyce didn't go with her.\"\n\n\"She's always angry,\" said the squire. \"She hardly speaks to me now. When she paid her rent the other day to Jolliffe, she said she hoped it would do me much good; as though she thought me a brute for taking it.\"\n\n\"So she does,\" said Bernard.\n\n\"She's very old, you know,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I'd give her the house for nothing, if I were you, uncle,\" said Lily.\n\n\"No, my dear; if you were me you would not. I should be very wrong to do so. Why should Mrs. Hearn have her house for nothing, any more than her meat or her clothes? It would be much more reasonable were I to give her so much money into her hand yearly; but it would be wrong in me to do so, seeing that she is not an object of charity;--and it would be wrong in her to take it.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't take it,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"I don't think she would. But if she did, I'm sure she would grumble because it wasn't double the amount. And if Mr. Boyce had gone home with her, she would have grumbled because he walked too fast.\"\n\n\"She is very old,\" said Bell, again.\n\n\"But, nevertheless, she ought to know better than to speak disparagingly of me to my servants. She should have more respect for herself.\" And the squire showed by the tone of his voice that he thought very much about it.\n\nIt was very long and very dull that Christmas evening, making Bernard feel strongly that he would be very foolish to give up his profession, and tie himself down to a life at Allington. Women are more accustomed than men to long, dull, unemployed hours; and, therefore, Mrs. Dale and her daughters bore the tedium courageously. While he yawned, stretched himself, and went in and out of the room, they sat demurely, listening as the squire laid down the law on small matters, and contradicting him occasionally when the spirit of either of them prompted her specially to do so. \"Of course you know much better than I do,\" he would say. \"Not at all,\" Mrs. Dale would answer. \"I don't pretend to know anything about it. But--\" So the evening wore itself away; and when the squire was left alone at half-past nine, he did not feel that the day had passed badly with him. That was his style of life, and he expected no more from it than he got. He did not look to find things very pleasant, and, if not happy, he was, at any rate, contented.\n\n\"Only think of Johnny Eames being at Guestwick Manor!\" said Bell, as they were going home.\n\n\"I don't see why he shouldn't be there,\" said Lily. \"I would rather it should be he than I, because Lady Julia is so grumpy.\"\n\n\"But asking your uncle Christopher especially to meet him!\" said Mrs. Dale. \"There must be some reason for it.\" Then Lily felt the soreness come upon her again, and spoke no further upon the subject.\n\nWe all know that there was a special reason, and that Lily's soreness was not false in its mysterious forebodings. Eames, on the evening after his dinner at Pawkins's, had seen the earl, and explained to him that he could not leave town till the Saturday evening; but that he could remain over the Tuesday. He must be at his office by twelve on Wednesday, and could manage to do that by an early train from Guestwick.\n\n\"Very well, Johnny,\" said the earl, talking to his young friend with the bedroom candle in his hand, as he was going up to dress. \"Then I'll tell you what; I've been thinking of it. I'll ask Dale to come over to dinner on Tuesday; and if he'll come, I'll explain the whole matter to him myself. He's a man of business, and he'll understand. If he won't come, why then you must go over to Allington, and find him, if you can, on the Tuesday morning; or I'll go to him myself, which will be better. You mustn't keep me now, as I am ever so much too late.\"\n\nEames did not attempt to keep him, but went away feeling that the whole matter was being arranged for him in a very wonderful way. And when he got to Allington he found that the squire had accepted the earl's invitation. Then he declared to himself that there was no longer any possibility of retractation for him. Of course he did not wish to retract. The one great longing of his life was to call Lily Dale his own. But he felt afraid of the squire,--that the squire would despise him and snub him, and that the earl would perceive that he had made a mistake when he saw how his client was scorned and snubbed. It was arranged that the earl was to take the squire into his own room for a few minutes before dinner, and Johnny felt that he would be hardly able to stand his ground in the drawing-room when the two old men should make their appearance together.\n\nHe got on very well with Lady Julia, who gave herself no airs, and made herself very civil. Her brother had told her the whole story, and she felt as anxious as he did to provide Lily with another husband in place of that horrible man Crosbie. \"She has been very fortunate in her escape,\" she said to her brother; \"very fortunate.\" The earl agreed with this, saying that in his opinion his own favourite Johnny would make much the nicer lover of the two. But Lady Julia had her doubts as to Lily's acquiescence. \"But, Theodore, he must not speak to Miss Lilian Dale herself about it yet a while.\"\n\n\"No,\" said the earl; \"not for a month or so.\"\n\n\"He will have a better chance if he can remain silent for six months,\" said Lady Julia.\n\n\"Bless my soul! somebody else will have picked her up before that,\" said the earl.\n\nIn answer to this Lady Julia merely shook her head.\n\nJohnny went over to his mother on Christmas Day after church, and was received by her and by his sister with great honour. And she gave him many injunctions as to his behaviour at the earl's table, even descending to small details about his boots and linen. But Johnny had already begun to feel at the Manor that, after all, people are not so very different in their ways of life as they are supposed to be. Lady Julia's manners were certainly not quite those of Mrs. Roper; but she made the tea very much in the way in which it was made at Burton Crescent, and Eames found that he could eat his egg, at any rate on the second morning, without any tremor in his hand, in spite of the coronet on the silver egg-cup. He did feel himself to be rather out of his place in the Manor pew on the Sunday, conceiving that all the congregation was looking at him; but he got over this on Christmas Day, and sat quite comfortably in his soft corner during the sermon, almost going to sleep. And when he walked with the earl after church to the gate over which the noble peer had climbed in his agony, and inspected the hedge through which he had thrown himself, he was quite at home with his little jokes, bantering his august companion as to the mode of his somersault. But be it always remembered that there are two modes in which a young man may be free and easy with his elder and superior,--the mode pleasant and the mode offensive. Had it been in Johnny's nature to try the latter, the earl's back would soon have been up at once, and the play would have been over. But it was not in Johnny's nature to do so, and therefore it was that the earl liked him.\n\nAt last came the hour of dinner on Tuesday, or at least the hour at which the squire had been asked to show himself at the Manor House. Eames, as by agreement with his patron, did not come down so as to show himself till after the interview. Lady Julia, who had been present at their discussions, had agreed to receive the squire; and then a servant was to ask him to step into the earl's own room. It was pretty to see the way in which the three conspired together, planning and plotting with an eagerness that was beautifully green and fresh.\n\n\"He can be as cross as an old stick when he likes it,\" said the earl, speaking of the squire; \"and we must take care not to rub him the wrong way.\"\n\n\"I shan't know what to say to him when I come down,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Just shake hands with him and don't say anything,\" said Lady Julia.\n\n\"I'll give him some port wine that ought to soften his heart,\" said the earl, \"and then we'll see how he is in the evening.\"\n\nEames heard the wheels of the squire's little open carriage and trembled. The squire, unconscious of all schemes, soon found himself with Lady Julia, and within two minutes of his entrance was walked off to the earl's private room. \"Certainly,\" he said, \"certainly;\" and followed the man-servant. The earl, as he entered, was standing in the middle of the room, and his round rosy face was a picture of good-humour.\n\n\"I'm very glad you've come, Dale,\" said he. \"I've something I want to say to you.\"\n\nMr. Dale, who neither in heart nor in manner was so light a man as the earl, took the proffered hand of his host, and bowed his head slightly, signifying that he was willing to listen to anything.\n\n\"I think I told you,\" continued the earl, \"that young John Eames is down here; but he goes back to-morrow, as they can't spare him at his office. He's a very good fellow,--as far as I am able to judge, an uncommonly good young man. I've taken a great fancy to him myself.\"\n\nIn answer to this Mr. Dale did not say much. He sat down, and in some general terms expressed his good-will towards all the Eames family.\n\n\"As you know, Dale, I'm a very bad hand at talking, and therefore I won't beat about the bush in what I've got to say at present. Of course we've all heard of that scoundrel Crosbie, and the way he has treated your niece Lilian.\"\n\n\"He is a scoundrel,--an unmixed scoundrel. But the less we say about that the better. It is ill mentioning a girl's name in such a matter as that.\"\n\n\"But, my dear Dale, I must mention it at the present moment. Dear young child, I would do anything to comfort her! And I hope that something may be done to comfort her. Do you know that that young man was in love with her long before Crosbie ever saw her?\"\n\n\"What;--John Eames!\"\n\n\"Yes, John Eames. And I wish heartily for his sake that he had won her regard before she had met that rascal whom you had to stay down at your house.\"\n\n\"A man cannot help these things, De Guest,\" said the squire.\n\n\"No, no, no! There are such men about the world, and it is impossible to know them at a glance. He was my nephew's friend, and I am not going to say that my nephew was in fault. But I wish,--I only say that I wish,--she had first known what are this young man's feelings towards her.\"\n\n\"But she might not have thought of him as you do.\"\n\n\"He is an uncommonly good-looking young fellow; straight made, broad in the chest, with a good, honest eye, and a young man's proper courage. He has never been taught to give himself airs like a dancing monkey; but I think he's all the better for that.\"\n\n\"But it's too late now, De Guest.\"\n\n\"No, no; that's just where it is. It mustn't be too late! That child is not to lose her whole life because a villain has played her false. Of course she'll suffer. Just at present it wouldn't do, I suppose, to talk to her about a new sweetheart. But, Dale, the time will come; the time will come;--the time always does come.\"\n\n\"It has never come to you and me,\" said the squire, with the slightest possible smile on his dry cheeks. The story of their lives had been so far the same; each had loved, and each had been disappointed, and then each had remained single through life.\n\n\"Yes, it has,\" said the earl, with no slight touch of feeling and even of romance in what he said. \"We have retricked our beams in our own ways, and our lives have not been desolate. But for her,--you and her mother will look forward to see her married some day.\"\n\n\"I have not thought about it.\"\n\n\"But I want you to think about it. I want to interest you in this fellow's favour; and in doing so, I mean to be very open with you. I suppose you'll give her something?\"\n\n\"I don't know, I'm sure,\" said the squire, almost offended at an inquiry of such a nature.\n\n\"Well, then, whether you do or not, I'll give him something,\" said the earl. \"I shouldn't have ventured to meddle in the matter had I not intended to put myself in such a position with reference to him as would justify me in asking the question.\" And the peer as he spoke drew himself up to his full height. \"If such a match can be made, it shall not be a bad marriage for your niece in a pecuniary point of view. I shall have pleasure in giving to him; but I shall have more pleasure if she can share what I give.\"\n\n\"She ought to be very much obliged to you,\" said the squire.\n\n\"I think she would be if she knew young Eames. I hope the day may come when she will be so. I hope that you and I may see them happy together, and that you too may thank me for having assisted in making them so. Shall we go in to Lady Julia now?\" The earl had felt that he had not quite succeeded; that his offer had been accepted somewhat coldly, and had not much hope that further good could be done on that day, even with the help of his best port wine.\n\n\"Half a moment,\" said the squire. \"There are matters as to which I never find myself able to speak quickly, and this certainly seems to be one of them. If you will allow me I will think over what you have said, and then see you again.\"\n\n\"Certainly, certainly.\"\n\n\"But for your own part in the matter, for your great generosity and kind heart, I beg to offer you my warmest thanks.\" Then the squire bowed low, and preceded the earl out of the room.\n\nLord De Guest still felt that he had not succeeded. We may probably say, looking at the squire's character and peculiarities, that no marked success was probable at the first opening-out of such a subject. He had said of himself that he was never able to speak quickly in matters of moment; but he would more correctly have described his own character had he declared that he could not think of them quickly. As it was, the earl was disappointed; but had he been able to read the squire's mind, his disappointment would have been less strong. Mr. Dale knew well enough that he was being treated well, and that the effort being made was intended with kindness to those belonging to him; but it was not in his nature to be demonstrative and quick at expressions of gratitude. So he entered the drawing-room with a cold, placid face, leading Eames, and Lady Julia also, to suppose that no good had been done.\n\n\"How do you do, sir?\" said Johnny, walking up to him in a wild sort of manner,--going through a premeditated lesson, but doing it without any presence of mind.\n\n\"How do you do, Eames?\" said the squire, speaking with a very cold voice. And then there was nothing further said till the dinner was announced.\n\n\"Dale, I know you drink port,\" said the earl when Lady Julia left them. \"If you say you don't like that, I shall say you know nothing about it.\"\n\n\"Ah! that's the '20,\" said the squire, tasting it.\n\n\"I should rather think it is,\" said the earl. \"I was lucky enough to get it early, and it hasn't been moved for thirty years. I like to give it to a man who knows it, as you do, at the first glance. Now there's my friend Johnny there; it's thrown away upon him.\"\n\n\"No, my lord, it is not. I think it's uncommonly nice.\"\n\n\"Uncommonly nice! So is champagne, or ginger-beer, or lollipops,--for those who like them. Do you mean to tell me you can taste wine with half a pickled orange in your mouth?\"\n\n\"It'll come to him soon enough,\" said the squire.\n\n\"Twenty port won't come to him when he is as old as we are,\" said the earl, forgetting that by that time sixty port will be as wonderful to the then living seniors of the age as was his own pet vintage to him.\n\nThe good wine did in some sort soften the squire; but, as a matter of course, nothing further was said as to the new matrimonial scheme. The earl did observe, however, that Mr. Dale was civil, and even kind, to his own young friend, asking a question here and there as to his life in London, and saying something about the work at the Income-tax Office.\n\n\"It is hard work,\" said Eames. \"If you're under the line, they make a great row about it, send for you, and look at you as though you'd been robbing the bank; but they think nothing of keeping you till five.\"\n\n\"But how long do you have for lunch and reading the papers?\" said the earl.\n\n\"Not ten minutes. We take a paper among twenty of us for half the day. That's exactly nine minutes to each; and as for lunch, we only have a biscuit dipped in ink.\"\n\n\"Dipped in ink!\" said the squire.\n\n\"It comes to that, for you have to be writing while you munch it.\"\n\n\"I hear all about you,\" said the earl; \"Sir Raffle Buffle is an old crony of mine.\"\n\n\"I don't suppose he ever heard my name as yet,\" said Johnny. \"But do you really know him well, Lord De Guest?\"\n\n\"Haven't seen him these thirty years; but I did know him.\"\n\n\"We call him old Huffle Scuffle.\"\n\n\"Huffle Scuffle! Ha, ha, ha! He always was Huffle Scuffle; a noisy, pretentious, empty-headed fellow. But I oughtn't to say so before you, young man. Come, we'll go into the drawing-room.\"\n\n\"And what did he say?\" asked Lady Julia, as soon as the squire was gone.\n\nThere was no attempt at concealment, and the question was asked in Johnny's presence.\n\n\"Well, he did not say much. And coming from him, that ought to be taken as a good sign. He is to think of it, and let me see him again. You hold your head up, Johnny, and remember that you shan't want a friend on your side. Faint heart never won fair lady.\"\n\nAt seven o'clock on the following morning Eames started on his return journey, and was at his desk at twelve o'clock,--as per agreement with his taskmaster at the Income-tax Office.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIV. THE COMBAT.\n\nI have said that John Eames was at his office punctually at twelve; but an incident had happened before his arrival there very important in the annals which are now being told,--so important that it is essentially necessary that it should be described with some minuteness of detail.\n\nLord De Guest, in the various conversations which he had had with Eames as to Lily Dale and her present position, had always spoken of Crosbie with the most vehement abhorrence. \"He is a damned blackguard,\" said the earl, and the fire had come out of his round eyes as he spoke. Now the earl was by no means given to cursing and swearing, in the sense which is ordinarily applied to these words. When he made use of such a phrase as that quoted above, it was to be presumed that he in some sort meant what he said; and so he did, and had intended to signify that Crosbie by his conduct had merited all such condemnation as was the fitting punishment for blackguardism of the worst description.\n\n\"He ought to have his neck broken,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"I don't know about that,\" said the earl. \"The present times have become so pretty behaved that corporal punishment seems to have gone out of fashion. I shouldn't care so much about that, if any other punishment had taken its place. But it seems to me that a blackguard such as Crosbie can escape now altogether unscathed.\"\n\n\"He hasn't escaped yet,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Don't you go and put your finger in the pie and make a fool of yourself,\" said the earl. If it had behoved any one to resent in any violent fashion the evil done by Crosbie, Bernard Dale, the earl's nephew, should have been the avenger. This the earl felt, but under these circumstances he was disposed to think that there should be no such violent vengeance. \"Things were different when I was young,\" he said to himself. But Eames gathered from the earl's tone that the earl's words were not strictly in accordance with his thoughts, and he declared to himself over and over again that Crosbie had not yet escaped.\n\nHe got into the train at Guestwick, taking a first-class ticket, because the earl's groom in livery was in attendance upon him. Had he been alone he would have gone in a cheaper carriage. Very weak in him, was it not? little also, and mean? My friend, can you say that you would not have done the same at his age? Are you quite sure that you would not do the same now that you are double his age? Be that as it may, Johnny Eames did that foolish thing, and gave the groom in livery half-a-crown into the bargain.\n\n\"We shall have you down again soon, Mr. John,\" said the groom, who seemed to understand that Mr. Eames was to be made quite at home at the manor.\n\nHe went fast to sleep in the carriage, and did not awake till the train was stopped at the Barchester Junction.\n\n\"Waiting for the up-train from Barchester, sir,\" said the guard. \"They're always late.\" Then he went to sleep again, and was aroused in a few minutes by some one entering the carriage in a great hurry. The branch train had come in, just as the guardians of the line then present had made up their minds that the passengers on the main line should not be kept waiting any longer. The transfer of men, women, and luggage was therefore made in great haste, and they who were now taking their new seats had hardly time to look about them. An old gentleman, very red about the gills, first came into Johnny's carriage, which up to that moment he had shared with an old lady. The old gentleman was abusing everybody, because he was hurried, and would not take himself well into the compartment, but stuck in the doorway, standing on the step.\n\n\"Now, sir, when you're quite at leisure,\" said a voice behind the old man, which instantly made Eames start up in his seat.\n\n\"I'm not at all at leisure,\" said the old man; \"and I'm not going to break my legs if I know it.\"\n\n\"Take your time, sir,\" said the guard.\n\n\"So I mean,\" said the old man, seating himself in the corner nearest to the open door, opposite to the old lady. Then Eames saw plainly that it was Crosbie who had first spoken, and that he was getting into the carriage.\n\nCrosbie at the first glance saw no one but the old gentleman and the old lady, and he immediately made for the unoccupied corner seat. He was busy with his umbrella and his dressing-bag, and a little flustered by the pushing and hurrying. The carriage was actually in motion before he perceived that John Eames was opposite to him: Eames had, instinctively, drawn up his legs so as not to touch him. He felt that he had become very red in the face, and to tell the truth, the perspiration had broken out upon his brow. It was a great occasion,--great in its imminent trouble, and great in its opportunity for action. How was he to carry himself at the first moment of his recognition by his enemy, and what was he to do afterwards?\n\nIt need hardly be explained that Crosbie had also been spending his Christmas with a certain earl of his acquaintance, and that he too was returning to his office. In one respect he had been much more fortunate than poor Eames, for he had been made happy with the smiles of his lady love. Alexandrina and the countess had fluttered about him softly, treating him as a tame chattel, now belonging to the noble house of De Courcy, and in this way he had been initiated into the inner domesticities of that illustrious family. The two extra men-servants, hired to wait upon Lady Dumbello, had vanished. The champagne had ceased to flow in a perennial stream. Lady Rosina had come out from her solitude, and had preached at him constantly. Lady Margaretta had given him some lessons in economy. The Honourable John, in spite of a late quarrel, had borrowed five pounds from him. The Honourable George had engaged to come and stay with his sister during the next May. The earl had used a father-in-law's privilege, and had called him a fool. Lady Alexandrina had told him more than once, in rather a tart voice, that this must be done, and that that must be done; and the countess had given him her orders as though it was his duty, in the course of nature, to obey every word that fell from her. Such had been his Christmas delights; and now, as he returned back from the enjoyment of them, he found himself confronted in the railway carriage with Johnny Eames!\n\nThe eyes of the two met, and Crosbie made a slight inclination of his head. To this Eames gave no acknowledgment whatever, but looked straight into the other's face. Crosbie immediately saw that they were not to know each other, and was well contented that it should be so. Among all his many troubles, the enmity of John Eames did not go for much. He showed no appearance of being disconcerted, though our friend had shown much. He opened his bag, and taking out a book was soon deeply engaged in it, pursuing his studies as though the man opposite was quite unknown to him. I will not say that his mind did not run away from his book, for indeed there were many things of which he found it impossible not to think; but it did not revert to John Eames. Indeed, when the carriages reached Paddington, he had in truth all but forgotten him; and as he stepped out of the carriage, with his bag in his hand, was quite free from any remotest trouble on his account.\n\nBut it had not been so with Eames himself. Every moment of the journey had for him been crowded with thought as to what he would do now that chance had brought his enemy within his reach. He had been made quite wretched by the intensity of his thinking; and yet, when the carriages stopped, he had not made up his mind. His face had been covered with perspiration ever since Crosbie had come across him, and his limbs had hardly been under his own command. Here had come to him a great opportunity, and he felt so little confidence in himself that he almost knew that he would not use it properly. Twice and thrice he had almost flown at Crosbie's throat in the carriage, but he was restrained by an idea that the world and the police would be against him if he did such a thing in the presence of that old lady.\n\nBut when Crosbie turned his back upon him, and walked out, it was absolutely necessary that he should do something. He was not going to let the man escape, after all that he had said as to the expediency of thrashing him. Any other disgrace would be preferable to that. Fearing, therefore, lest his enemy should be too quick for him, he hurried out after him, and only just gave Crosbie time to turn round and face the carriages before he was upon him. \"You confounded scoundrel!\" he screamed out. \"You confounded scoundrel!\" and seized him by the throat, throwing himself upon him, and almost devouring him by the fury of his eyes.\n\nThe crowd upon the platform was not very dense, but there were quite enough of people to make a very respectable audience for this little play. Crosbie, in his dismay, retreated a step or two, and his retreat was much accelerated by the weight of Eames's attack. He endeavoured to free his throat from his foe's grasp; but in that he failed entirely. For the minute, however, he did manage to escape any positive blow, owing his safety in that respect rather to Eames's awkwardness than to his own efforts. Something about the police he was just able to utter, and there was, as a matter of course, an immediate call for a supply of those functionaries. In about three minutes three policemen, assisted by six porters, had captured our poor friend Johnny; but this had not been done quick enough for Crosbie's purposes. The bystanders, taken by surprise, had allowed the combatants to fall back upon Mr. Smith's book-stall, and there Eames laid his foe prostrate among the newspapers, falling himself into the yellow shilling-novel depot by the over fury of his own energy; but as he fell, he contrived to lodge one blow with his fist in Crosbie's right eye,--one telling blow; and Crosbie had, to all intents and purposes, been thrashed.\n\n\"Con--founded scoundrel, rascal, blackguard!\" shouted Johnny, with what remnants of voice were left to him, as the police dragged him off. \"If you only knew--what he's--done.\" But in the meantime the policemen held him fast.\n\nAs a matter of course the first burst of public sympathy went with Crosbie. He had been assaulted, and the assault had come from Eames. In the British bosom there is so firm a love of well-constituted order, that these facts alone were sufficient to bring twenty knights to the assistance of the three policemen and the six porters; so that for Eames, even had he desired it, there was no possible chance of escape. But he did not desire it. One only sorrow consumed him at present. He had, as he felt, attacked Crosbie, but had attacked him in vain. He had had his opportunity, and had misused it. He was perfectly unconscious of that happy blow, and was in absolute ignorance of the great fact that his enemy's eye was already swollen and closed, and that in another hour it would be as black as his hat.\n\n\"He is a con--founded rascal!\" ejaculated Eames, as the policemen and porters hauled him about. \"You don't know what he's done.\"\n\n\"No, we don't,\" said the senior constable; \"but we know what you have done. I say, Bushers, where's that gentleman? He'd better come along with us.\"\n\nCrosbie had been picked up from among the newspapers by another policeman and two or three other porters, and was attended also by the guard of the train, who knew him, and knew that he had come up from Courcy Castle. Three or four hangers-on were standing also around him, together with a benevolent medical man who was proposing to him an immediate application of leeches. If he could have done as he wished, he would have gone his way quietly, allowing Eames to do the same. A great evil had befallen him, but he could in no way mitigate that evil by taking the law of the man who had attacked him. To have the thing as little talked about as possible should be his endeavour. What though he should have Eames locked up and fined, and scolded by a police magistrate? That would not in any degree lessen his calamity. If he could have parried the attack, and got the better of his foe; if he could have administered the black eye instead of receiving it, then indeed he could have laughed the matter off at his club, and his original crime would have been somewhat glozed over by his success in arms. But such good fortune had not been his. He was forced, however, on the moment to decide as to what he would do.\n\n\"We've got him here in custody, sir,\" said Bushers, touching his hat. It had become known from the guard that Crosbie was somewhat of a big man, a frequent guest at Courcy Castle, and of repute and station in the higher regions of the Metropolitan world. \"The magistrates will be sitting at Paddington, now, sir--or will be by the time we get there.\"\n\nBy this time some mighty railway authority had come upon the scene and made himself cognizant of the facts of the row,--a stern official who seemed to carry the weight of many engines on his brow; one at the very sight of whom smokers would drop their cigars, and porters close their fists against sixpences; a great man with an erect chin, a quick step, and a well-brushed hat powerful with an elaborately upturned brim. This was the platform-superintendent, dominant even over the policemen.\n\n\"Step into my room, Mr. Crosbie,\" he said. \"Stubbs, bring that man in with you.\" And then, before Crosbie had been able to make up his mind as to any other line of conduct, he found himself in the superintendent's room, accompanied by the guard, and by the two policemen who conducted Johnny Eames between them.\n\n\"What's all this?\" said the superintendent, still keeping on his hat, for he was aware how much of the excellence of his personal dignity was owing to the arrangement of that article; and as he spoke he frowned upon the culprit with his utmost severity. \"Mr. Crosbie, I am very sorry that you should have been exposed to such brutality on our platform.\"\n\n\"You don't know what he has done,\" said Johnny. \"He is the most confounded scoundrel living. He has broken--\" But then he stopped himself. He was going to tell the superintendent that the confounded scoundrel had broken a beautiful young lady's heart; but he bethought himself that he would not allude more specially to Lily Dale in that hearing.\n\n\"Do you know who he is, Mr. Crosbie?\" said the superintendent.\n\n\"Oh, yes,\" said Crosbie, whose eye was already becoming blue. \"He is a clerk in the Income-tax Office, and his name is Eames. I believe you had better leave him to me.\"\n\nBut the superintendent at once wrote down the words \"Income-tax Office--Eames,\" on his tablet. \"We can't allow a row like that to take place on our platform and not notice it. I shall bring it before the directors. It's a most disgraceful affair, Mr. Eames--most disgraceful.\"\n\nBut Johnny by this time had perceived that Crosbie's eye was in a state which proved satisfactorily that his morning's work had not been thrown away, and his spirits were rising accordingly. He did not care two straws for the superintendent or even for the policemen, if only the story could be made to tell well for himself hereafter. It was his object to have thrashed Crosbie, and now, as he looked at his enemy's face, he acknowledged that Providence had been good to him.\n\n\"That's your opinion,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Yes, sir, it is,\" said the superintendent; \"and I shall know how to represent the matter to your superiors, young man.\"\n\n\"You don't know all about it,\" said Eames; \"and I don't suppose you ever will. I had made up my mind what I'd do the first time I saw that scoundrel there; and now I've done it. He'd have got much worse in the railway carriage, only there was a lady there.\"\n\n\"Mr. Crosbie, I really think we had better take him before the magistrates.\"\n\nTo this, however, Crosbie objected. He assured the superintendent that he would himself know how to deal with the matter--which, however, was exactly what he did not know. Would the superintendent allow one of the railway servants to get a cab for him, and to find his luggage? He was very anxious to get home without being subjected to any more of Mr. Eames's insolence.\n\n\"You haven't done with Mr. Eames's insolence yet, I can tell you. All London shall hear of it, and shall know why. If you have any shame in you, you shall be ashamed to show your face.\"\n\nUnfortunate man! Who can say that punishment--adequate punishment--had not overtaken him? For the present, he had to sneak home with a black eye, with the knowledge inside him that he had been whipped by a clerk in the Income-tax Office; and for the future--he was bound over to marry Lady Alexandrina De Courcy!\n\nHe got himself smuggled off in a cab, without being forced to go again upon the platform--his luggage being brought to him by two assiduous porters. But in all this there was very little balm for his hurt pride. As he ordered the cabman to drive to Mount Street, he felt that he had ruined himself by that step in life which he had taken at Courcy Castle. Whichever way he looked he had no comfort. \"D---- the fellow!\" he said, almost out loud in the cab; but though he did with his outward voice allude to Eames, the curse in his inner thoughts was uttered against himself.\n\nJohnny was allowed to make his way down to the platform, and there find his own carpet-bag. One young porter, however, came up and fraternized with him.\n\n\"You guve it him tidy just at that last moment, sir. But, laws, sir, you should have let out at him at fust. What's the use of clawing a man's neck-collar?\"\n\nIt was then a quarter past eleven, but, nevertheless, Eames appeared at his office precisely at twelve.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV. VÆ VICTIS.\n\nCrosbie had two engagements for that day; one being his natural engagement to do his work at his office, and the other an engagement, which was now very often becoming as natural, to dine at St. John's Wood with Lady Amelia Gazebee. It was manifest to him when he looked at himself in the glass that he could keep neither of these engagements. \"Oh, laws, Mr. Crosbie,\" the woman of the house exclaimed when she saw him.\n\n\"Yes, I know,\" said he. \"I've had an accident and got a black eye. What's a good thing for it?\"\n\n\"Oh! an accident!\" said the woman, who knew well that that mark had been made by another man's fist. \"They do say that a bit of raw beef is about the best thing. But then it must be held on constant all the morning.\"\n\nAnything would be better than leeches, which tell long-enduring tales, and therefore Crosbie sat through the greater part of the morning holding the raw beef to his eye. But it was necessary that he should write two notes as he held it, one to Mr. Butterwell at his office, and the other to his future sister-in-law. He felt that it would hardly be wise to attempt any entire concealment of the nature of his catastrophe, as some of the circumstances would assuredly become known. If he said that he had fallen over the coal-scuttle, or on to the fender, thereby cutting his face, people would learn that he had fibbed, and would learn also that he had had some reason for fibbing. Therefore he constructed his notes with a phraseology that bound him to no details. To Butterwell he said that he had had an accident--or rather a row--and that he had come out of it with considerable damage to his frontispiece. He intended to be at the office on the next day, whether able to appear decently there or not. But for the sake of decency he thought it well to give himself that one half-day's chance. Then to the Lady Amelia he also said that he had had an accident, and had been a little hurt. \"It is nothing at all serious, and affects only my appearance, so that I had better remain in for a day. I shall certainly be with you on Sunday. Don't let Gazebee trouble himself to come to me, as I shan't be at home after to-day.\" Gazebee did trouble himself to come to Mount Street so often, and South Audley Street, in which was Mr. Gazebee's office, was so disagreeably near to Mount Street, that Crosbie inserted this in order to protect himself if possible. Then he gave special orders that he was to be at home to no one, fearing that Gazebee would call for him after the hours of business--to make him safe and carry him off bodily to St. John's Wood.\n\nThe beefsteak and the dose of physic and the cold-water application which was kept upon it all night was not efficacious in dispelling that horrid, black-blue colour by ten o'clock on the following morning.\n\n\"It certainly have gone down, Mr. Crosbie; it certainly have,\" said the mistress of the lodgings, touching the part affected with her finger. \"But the black won't go out of them all in a minute; it won't indeed. Couldn't you just stay in one more day?\"\n\n\"But will one day do it, Mrs. Phillips?\"\n\nMrs. Phillips couldn't take upon herself to say that it would. \"They mostly come with little red streaks across the black before they goes away,\" said Mrs. Phillips, who would seem to have been the wife of a prize-fighter, so well was she acquainted with black eyes.\n\n\"And that won't be till to-morrow,\" said Crosbie, affecting to be mirthful in his agony.\n\n\"Not till the third day;--and then they wears themselves out, gradual. I never knew leeches do any good.\"\n\nHe stayed at home the second day, and then resolved that he would go to his office, black eye and all. In that morning's newspaper he saw an account of the whole transaction, saying how Mr. C---- of the office of General Committees, who was soon about to lead to the hymeneal altar the beautiful daughter of the Earl De C----, had been made the subject of a brutal personal attack on the platform of the Great Western Railway Station, and how he was confined to his room from the injuries which he had received. The paragraph went on to state that the delinquent had, as it was believed, dared to raise his eyes to the same lady, and that his audacity had been treated with scorn by every member of the noble family in question. \"It was, however, satisfactory to know,\" so said the newspaper, \"that Mr. C---- had amply avenged himself, and had so flogged the young man in question, that he had been unable to stir from his bed since the occurrence.\"\n\nOn reading this Crosbie felt that it would be better that he should show himself at once, and tell as much of the truth as the world would be likely to ascertain at last without his telling. So on that third morning he put on his hat and gloves, and had himself taken to his office, though the red-streaky period of his misfortune had hardly even yet come upon him. The task of walking along the office passage, through the messengers' lobby, and into his room, was very disagreeable. Of course everybody looked at him, and of course he failed in his attempt to appear as though he did not mind it. \"Boggs,\" he said to one of the men as he passed by, \"just see if Mr. Butterwell is in his room,\" and then, as he expected, Mr. Butterwell came to him after the expiration of a few minutes.\n\n\"Upon my word, that is serious,\" said Mr. Butterwell, looking into the secretary's damaged face. \"I don't think I would have come out if I had been you.\"\n\n\"Of course it's disagreeable,\" said Crosbie; \"but it's better to put up with it. Fellows do tell such horrid lies if a man isn't seen for a day or two. I believe it's best to put a good face upon it.\"\n\n\"That's more than you can do just at present, eh, Crosbie?\" And then Mr. Butterwell tittered. \"But how on earth did it happen? The paper says that you pretty well killed the fellow who did it.\"\n\n\"The paper lies, as papers always do. I didn't touch him at all.\"\n\n\"Didn't you, though? I should like to have had a poke at him after getting such a tap in the face as that.\"\n\n\"The policemen came, and all that sort of thing. One isn't allowed to fight it out in a row of that kind as one would have to do on Salisbury heath. Not that I mean to say that I could lick the fellow. How's a man to know whether he can or not?\"\n\n\"How, indeed, unless he gets a licking,--or gives it? But who was he, and what's this about his having been scorned by the noble family?\"\n\n\"Trash and lies, of course. He had never seen any of the De Courcy people.\"\n\n\"I suppose the truth is, it was about that other--eh, Crosbie? I knew you'd find yourself in some trouble before you'd done.\"\n\n\"I don't know what it was about, or why he should have made such a brute of himself. You have heard about those people at Allington?\"\n\n\"Oh, yes; I have heard about them.\"\n\n\"God knows, I didn't mean to say anything against them. They knew nothing about it.\"\n\n\"But the young fellow knew them? Ah, yes, I see all about it. He wants to step into your shoes. I can't say that he sets about it in a bad way. But what do you mean to do?\"\n\n\"Nothing.\"\n\n\"Nothing! Won't that look queer? I think I should have him before the magistrates.\"\n\n\"You see, Butterwell, I am bound to spare that girl's name. I know I have behaved badly.\"\n\n\"Well, yes; I fear you have.\"\n\nMr. Butterwell said this with some considerable amount of decision in his voice, as though he did not intend to mince matters, or in any way to hide his opinion. Crosbie had got into a way of condemning himself in this matter of his marriage, but was very anxious that others, on hearing such condemnation from him, should say something in the way of palliating his fault. It would be so easy for a friend to remark that such little peccadilloes were not altogether uncommon, and that it would sometimes happen in life that people did not know their own minds. He had hoped for some such benevolence from Fowler Pratt, but had hoped in vain. Butterwell was a good-natured, easy man, anxious to stand well with all about him, never pretending to any very high tone of feeling or of morals; and yet Butterwell would say no word of comfort to him. He could get no one to slur over his sin for him, as though it were no sin,--only an unfortunate mistake; no one but the De Courcys, who had, as it were, taken possession of him and swallowed him alive.\n\n\"It can't be helped now,\" said Crosbie. \"But as for that fellow who made such a brutal attack on me the other morning, he knows that he is safe behind her petticoats. I can do nothing which would not make some mention of her name necessary.\"\n\n\"Ah, yes; I see,\" said Butterwell. \"It's very unfortunate; very. I don't know that I can do anything for you. Will you come before the Board to-day?\"\n\n\"Yes; of course I shall,\" said Crosbie, who was becoming very sore. His sharp ear had told him that all Butterwell's respect and cordiality were gone,--at any rate for the time. Butterwell, though holding the higher official rank, had always been accustomed to treat him as though he, the inferior, were to be courted. He had possessed, and had known himself to possess, in his office as well as in the outside world, a sort of rank much higher than that which from his position he could claim legitimately. Now he was being deposed. There could be no better touchstone in such a matter than Butterwell. He would go as the world went, but he would perceive almost intuitively how the world intended to go. \"Tact, tact, tact,\" as he was in the habit of saying to himself when walking along the paths of his Putney villa. Crosbie was now secretary, whereas a few months before he had been simply a clerk; but, nevertheless, Mr. Butterwell's instinct told him that Crosbie had fallen. Therefore he declined to offer any sympathy to the man in his misfortune, and felt aware, as he left the secretary's room, that it might probably be some time before he visited it again.\n\nCrosbie resolved in his soreness that henceforth he would brazen it out. He would go to the Board, with as much indifference as to his black eye as he was able to assume, and if any one said aught to him he would be ready with his answer. He would go to his club, and let him who intended to show him any slight beware of him in his wrath. He could not turn upon John Eames, but he could turn upon others if it were necessary. He had not gained for himself a position before the world, and held it now for some years, to allow himself to be crushed at once because he had made a mistake. If the world, his world, chose to go to war with him, he would be ready for the fight. As for Butterwell,--Butterwell the incompetent, Butterwell the vapid,--for Butterwell, who in every little official difficulty had for years past come to him, he would let Butterwell know what it was to be thus disloyal to one who had condescended to be his friend. He would show them all at the Board that he scorned them, and could be their master. Then, too, as he was making some other resolves as to his future conduct, he made one or two resolutions respecting the De Courcy people. He would make it known to them that he was not going to be their very humble servant. He would speak out his mind with considerable plainness; and if upon that they should choose to break off this \"alliance,\" they might do so; he would not break his heart. And as he leaned back in his arm-chair, thinking of all this, an idea made its way into his brain,--a floating castle in the air, rather than the image of a thing that might by possibility be realized; and in this castle in the air he saw himself kneeling again at Lily's feet, asking her pardon, and begging that he might once more be taken to her heart.\n\n\"Mr. Crosbie is here to-day,\" said Mr. Butterwell to Mr. Optimist.\n\n\"Oh, indeed,\" said Mr. Optimist, very gravely; for he had heard all about the row at the railway station.\n\n\"They've made a monstrous show of him.\"\n\n\"I am very sorry to hear it. It's so--so--so-- If it were one of the younger clerks, you know, we should tell him that it was discreditable to the department.\"\n\n\"If a man gets a blow in the eye, he can't help it, you know. He didn't do it himself, I suppose,\" said Major Fiasco.\n\n\"I am well aware that he didn't do it himself,\" continued Mr. Optimist; \"but I really think that, in his position, he should have kept himself out of any such encounter.\"\n\n\"He would have done so if he could, with all his heart,\" said the major. \"I don't suppose he liked being thrashed any better than I should.\"\n\n\"Nobody gives me a black eye,\" said Mr. Optimist.\n\n\"Nobody has as yet,\" said the major.\n\n\"I hope they never will,\" said Mr. Butterwell. Then, the hour for their meeting having come round, Mr. Crosbie came into the Board-room.\n\n\"We have been very sorry to hear of this misfortune,\" said Mr. Optimist, very gravely.\n\n\"Not half so sorry as I have been,\" said Crosbie, with a laugh. \"It's an uncommon nuisance to have a black eye, and to go about looking like a prize-fighter.\"\n\n\"And like a prize-fighter that didn't win his battle, too,\" said Fiasco.\n\n\"I don't know that there's much difference as to that,\" said Crosbie. \"But the whole thing is a nuisance, and, if you please, we won't say anything more about it.\"\n\nMr. Optimist almost entertained an opinion that it was his duty to say something more about it. Was not he the chief Commissioner, and was not Mr. Crosbie secretary to the Board? Ought he, looking at their respective positions, to pass over without a word of notice such a manifest impropriety as this? Would not Sir Raffle Buffle have said something had Mr. Butterwell, when secretary, come to the office with a black eye? He wished to exercise all the full rights of a chairman; but, nevertheless, as he looked at the secretary he felt embarrassed, and was unable to find the proper words. \"H--m, ha, well; we'll go to business now, if you please,\" he said, as though reserving to himself the right of returning to the secretary's black eye when the more usual business of the Board should be completed. But when the more usual business of the Board had been completed, the secretary left the room without any further reference to his eye.\n\nCrosbie, when he got back to his own apartment, found Mortimer Gazebee waiting there for him.\n\n\"My dear fellow,\" said Gazebee, \"this is a very nasty affair.\"\n\n\"Uncommonly nasty,\" said Crosbie; \"so nasty that I don't mean to talk about it to anybody.\"\n\n\"Lady Amelia is quite unhappy.\" He always called her Lady Amelia, even when speaking of her to his own brothers and sisters. He was too well behaved to take the liberty of calling an earl's daughter by her plain Christian name, even though that earl's daughter was his own wife. \"She fears that you have been a good deal hurt.\"\n\n\"Not at all hurt; but disfigured, as you see.\"\n\n\"And so you beat the fellow well that did it?\"\n\n\"No, I didn't,\" said Crosbie, very angrily. \"I didn't beat him at all. You don't believe everything you read in the newspapers, do you?\"\n\n\"No, I don't believe everything. Of course I didn't believe about his having aspired to an alliance with Lady Alexandrina. That was untrue, of course.\" Mr. Gazebee showed by the tone of his voice that imprudence so unparalleled as that was quite incredible.\n\n\"You shouldn't believe anything; except this,--that I have got a black eye.\"\n\n\"You certainly have got that. Lady Amelia thinks you would be more comfortable if you would come up to us this evening. You can't go out, of course; but Lady Amelia said, very good-naturedly, that you need not mind with her.\"\n\n\"Thank you, no; I'll come on Sunday.\"\n\n\"Of course Lady Alexandrina will be very anxious to hear from her sister; and Lady Amelia begged me very particularly to press you to come.\"\n\n\"Thank you, no; not to-day.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\n\"Oh, simply because I shall be better at home.\"\n\n\"How can you be better at home? You can have anything that you want. Lady Amelia won't mind, you know.\"\n\nAnother beefsteak to his eye, as he sat in the drawing-room, a cold-water bandage, or any little medical appliance of that sort;--these were the things which Lady Amelia would, in her domestic good nature, condescend not to mind!\n\n\"I won't trouble her this evening,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Well, upon my word, I think you're wrong. All manner of stories will get down to Courcy Castle, and to the countess's ears; and you don't know what harm may come of it. Lady Amelia thinks she had better write and explain it; but she can't do so till she has heard something about it from you.\"\n\n\"Look here, Gazebee. I don't care one straw what story finds its way down to Courcy Castle.\"\n\n\"But if the earl were to hear anything, and be offended?\"\n\n\"He may recover from his offence as he best likes.\"\n\n\"My dear fellow; that's talking wildly, you know.\"\n\n\"What on earth do you suppose the earl can do to me? Do you think I'm going to live in fear of Lord De Courcy all my life, because I'm going to marry his daughter? I shall write to Alexandrina myself to-day, and you can tell her sister so. I'll be up to dinner on Sunday, unless my face makes it altogether out of the question.\"\n\n\"And you won't come in time for church?\"\n\n\"Would you have me go to church with such a face as this?\"\n\nThen Mr. Mortimer Gazebee went, and when he got home he told his wife that Crosbie was taking things with a high hand. \"The fact is, my dear, that he's ashamed of himself, and therefore tries to put a bold face upon it.\"\n\n\"It was very foolish of him throwing himself in the way of that young man,--very; and so I shall tell him on Sunday. If he chooses to give himself airs to me, I shall make him understand that he is very wrong. He should remember now that the way in which he conducts himself is a matter of moment to all our family.\"\n\n\"Of course he should,\" said Mr. Gazebee.\n\nWhen the Sunday came the red-streaky period had arrived, but had by no means as yet passed away. The men at the office had almost become used to it; but Crosbie, in spite of his determination to go down to the club, had not yet shown himself elsewhere. Of course he did not go to church, but at five he made his appearance at the house in St. John's Wood. They always dined at five on Sundays, having some idea that by doing so they kept the Sabbath better than they would have done had they dined at seven. If keeping the Sabbath consists in going to bed early, or is in any way assisted by such a practice, they were right. To the cook that semi-early dinner might perhaps be convenient, as it gave her an excuse for not going to church in the afternoon, as the servants' and children's dinner gave her a similar excuse in the morning. Such little attempts at goodness,--proceeding half the way, or perhaps, as in this instance, one quarter of the way, on the disagreeable path towards goodness,--are very common with respectable people, such as Lady Amelia. If she would have dined at one o'clock, and have eaten cold meat, one perhaps might have felt that she was entitled to some praise.\n\n\"Dear, dear, dear; this is very sad, isn't it, Adolphus?\" she said on first seeing him.\n\n\"Well, it is sad, Amelia,\" he said. He always called her Amelia, because she called him Adolphus; but Gazebee himself was never quite pleased when he heard it. Lady Amelia was older than Crosbie, and entitled to call him anything she liked; but he should have remembered the great difference in their rank. \"It is sad, Amelia,\" he said. \"But will you oblige me in one thing?\"\n\n\"What thing, Adolphus?\"\n\n\"Not to say a word more about it. The black eye is a bad thing, no doubt, and has troubled me much; but the sympathy of my friends has troubled me a great deal more. I had all the family commiseration from Gazebee on Friday, and if it is repeated again, I shall lie down and die.\"\n\n\"Shall 'oo die, uncle Dolphus, 'cause 'oo've got a bad eye?\" asked De Courcy Gazebee, the eldest hope of the family, looking up into his face.\n\n\"No, my hero,\" said Crosbie, taking the boy up into his arms, \"not because I've got a black eye. There isn't very much harm in that, and you'll have a great many before you leave school. But because the people will go on talking about it.\"\n\n\"But aunt Dina on't like 'oo, if oo've got an ugly bad eye.\"\n\n\"But, Adolphus,\" said Lady Amelia, settling herself for an argument, \"that's all very well, you know--and I'm sure I'm very sorry to cause you any annoyance,--but really one doesn't know how to pass over such a thing without speaking of it. I have had a letter from mamma.\"\n\n\"I hope Lady De Courcy is quite well.\"\n\n\"Quite well, thank you. But as a matter of course she is very anxious about this affair. She had read what has been said in the newspapers, and it may be necessary that Mortimer should take it up, as the family solicitor.\"\n\n\"Quite out of the question,\" said Adolphus.\n\n\"I don't think I should advise any such step as that,\" said Gazebee.\n\n\"Perhaps not; very likely not. But you cannot be surprised, Mortimer, that my mother under such circumstances should wish to know what are the facts of the case.\"\n\n\"Not at all surprised,\" said Gazebee.\n\n\"Then once for all, I'll tell you the facts. As I got out of the train a man I'd seen once before in my life made an attack upon me, and before the police came up, I got a blow in the face. Now you know all about it.\"\n\nAt that moment dinner was announced. \"Will you give Lady Amelia your arm?\" said the husband.\n\n\"It's a very sad occurrence,\" said Lady Amelia with a slight toss of her head, \"and, I'm afraid, will cost my sister a great deal of vexation.\"\n\n\"You agree with De Courcy, do you, that aunt Dina won't like me with an ugly black eye?\"\n\n\"I really don't think it's a joking matter,\" said the Lady Amelia. And then there was nothing more said about it during the dinner.\n\nThere was nothing more said about it during the dinner, but it was plain enough from Lady Amelia's countenance that she was not very well pleased with her future brother-in-law's conduct. She was very hospitable to him, pressing him to eat; but even in doing that she made repeated little references to his present unfortunate state. She told him that she did not think fried plum-pudding would be bad for him, but that she would recommend him not to drink port-wine after dinner. \"By-the-by, Mortimer, you'd better have some claret up,\" she remarked. \"Adolphus shouldn't take anything that is heating.\"\n\n\"Thank you,\" said Crosbie. \"I'll have some brandy-and-water, if Gazebee will give it me.\"\n\n\"Brandy-and-water!\" said Lady Amelia. Crosbie in truth was not given to the drinking of brandy-and-water; but he was prepared to call for raw gin, if he were driven much further by Lady Amelia's solicitude.\n\nAt these Sunday dinners the mistress of the house never went away into the drawing-room, and the tea was always brought into them at the table on which they had dined. It was another little step towards keeping holy the first day of the week. When Lady Rosina was there, she was indulged with the sight of six or seven solid good books which were laid upon the mahogany as soon as the bottles were taken off it. At her first prolonged visit she had obtained for herself the privilege of reading a sermon; but as on such occasions both Lady Amelia and Mr. Gazebee would go to sleep,--and as the footman had also once shown a tendency that way,--the sermon had been abandoned. But the master of the house, on these evenings, when his sister-in-law was present, was doomed to sit in idleness, or else to find solace in one of the solid good books. But Lady Rosina just now was in the country, and therefore the table was left unfurnished.\n\n\"And what am I to say to my mother?\" said Lady Amelia, when they were alone.\n\n\"Give her my kindest regards,\" said Crosbie. It was quite clear, both to the husband and to the wife, that he was preparing himself for rebellion against authority.\n\nFor some ten minutes there was nothing said. Crosbie amused himself by playing with the boy whom he called Dicksey, by way of a nickname for De Courcy.\n\n\"Mamma, he calls me Dicksey. Am I Dicksey? I'll call 'oo old Cross, and then aunt Dina 'on't like 'oo.\"\n\n\"I wish you would not call the child nicknames, Adolphus. It seems as though you would wish to cast a slur upon the one which he bears.\"\n\n\"I should hardly think that he would feel disposed to do that,\" said Mr. Gazebee.\n\n\"Hardly, indeed,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"It has never yet been disgraced in the annals of our country by being made into a nickname,\" said the proud daughter of the house. She was probably unaware that among many of his associates her father had been called Lord De Curse'ye, from the occasional energy of his language. \"And any such attempt is painful in my ears. I think something of my family, I can assure you, Adolphus, and so does my husband.\"\n\n\"A very great deal,\" said Mr. Gazebee.\n\n\"So do I of mine,\" said Crosbie. \"That's natural to all of us. One of my ancestors came over with William the Conqueror. I think he was one of the assistant cooks in the king's tent.\"\n\n\"A cook!\" said young De Courcy.\n\n\"Yes, my boy, a cook. That was the way most of our old families were made noble. They were cooks, or butlers to the kings--or sometimes something worse.\"\n\n\"But your family isn't noble?\"\n\n\"No--I'll tell you how that was. The king wanted this cook to poison half-a-dozen of his officers who wished to have a way of their own; but the cook said, 'No, my Lord King; I am a cook, not an executioner.' So they sent him into the scullery, and when they called all the other servants barons and lords, they only called him Cookey. They've changed the name to Crosbie since that, by degrees.\"\n\nMr. Gazebee was awestruck, and the face of the Lady Amelia became very dark. Was it not evident that this snake, when taken into their innermost bosoms that they might there warm him, was becoming an adder, and preparing to sting them? There was very little more conversation that evening, and soon after the story of the cook, Crosbie got up and went away to his own home.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVI. \"SEE, THE CONQUERING HERO COMES.\"\n\nJohn Eames had reached his office precisely at twelve o'clock, but when he did so he hardly knew whether he was standing on his heels or his head. The whole morning had been to him one of intense excitement, and latterly, to a certain extent, one of triumph. But he did not at all know what might be the results. Would he be taken before a magistrate and locked up? Would there be a row at the office? Would Crosbie call him out, and, if so, would it be incumbent on him to fight a duel with pistols? What would Lord De Guest say--Lord De Guest, who had specially warned him not to take upon himself the duty of avenging Lily's wrongs? What would all the Dale family say of his conduct? And, above all, what would Lily say and think? Nevertheless, the feeling of triumph was predominant; and now, at this interval of time, he was beginning to remember with pleasure the sensation of his fist as it went into Crosbie's eye.\n\nDuring his first day at the office he heard nothing about the affair, nor did he say a word of it to any one. It was known in his room that he had gone down to spend his Christmas holiday with Lord De Guest, and he was treated with some increased consideration accordingly. And, moreover, I must explain, in order that I may give Johnny Eames his due, he was gradually acquiring for himself a good footing among the Income-tax officials. He knew his work, and did it with some manly confidence in his own powers, and also with some manly indifference to the occasional frowns of the mighty men of the department. He was, moreover, popular--being somewhat of a radical in his official demeanour, and holding by his own rights, even though mighty men should frown. In truth, he was emerging from his hobbledehoyhood and entering upon his young manhood, having probably to go through much folly and some false sentiment in that period of his existence, but still with fair promise of true manliness beyond, to those who were able to read the signs of his character.\n\nMany questions on that first day were asked him about the glories of his Christmas, but he had very little to say on the subject. Indeed nothing could have been much more commonplace than his Christmas visit, had it not been for the one great object which had taken him down to that part of the country, and for the circumstance with which his holiday had been ended. On neither of these subjects was he disposed to speak openly; but as he walked home to Burton Crescent with Cradell, he did tell him of the affair with Crosbie.\n\n\"And you went in at him on the station?\" asked Cradell, with admiring doubt.\n\n\"Yes, I did. If I didn't do it there, where was I to do it? I'd said I would, and therefore when I saw him I did it.\" Then the whole affair was told as to the black eye, the police, and the superintendent. \"And what's to come next?\" asked our hero.\n\n\"Well, he'll put it in the hands of a friend, of course; as I did with Fisher in that affair with Lupex. And, upon my word, Johnny, I shall have to do something of the kind again. His conduct last night was outrageous; would you believe it--\"\n\n\"Oh, he's a fool.\"\n\n\"He's a fool you wouldn't like to meet when he's in one of his mad fits, I can tell you that. I absolutely had to sit up in my own bedroom all last night. Mother Roper told me that if I remained in the drawing-room she would feel herself obliged to have a policeman in the house. What could I do, you know? I made her have a fire for me, of course.\"\n\n\"And then you went to bed.\"\n\n\"I waited ever so long, because I thought that Maria would want to see me. At last she sent me a note. Maria is so imprudent, you know. If he had found anything in her writing, it would have been terrible, you know,--quite terrible. And who can say whether Jemima mayn't tell?\"\n\n\"And what did she say?\"\n\n\"Come; that's tellings, Master Johnny. I took very good care to take it with me to the office this morning, for fear of accidents.\"\n\nBut Eames was not so widely awake to the importance of his friend's adventures as he might have been had he not been weighted with adventures of his own.\n\n\"I shouldn't care so much,\" said he, \"about that fellow Crosbie going to a friend, as I should about his going to a police magistrate.\"\n\n\"He'll put it in a friend's hands, of course,\" said Cradell, with the air of a man who from experience was well up in such matters. \"And I suppose you'll naturally come to me. It's a deuced bore to a man in a public office, and all that kind of thing, of course. But I'm not the man to desert my friend. I'll stand by you, Johnny, my boy.\"\n\n\"Oh, thank you,\" said Eames, \"I don't think that I shall want that.\"\n\n\"You must be ready with a friend, you know.\"\n\n\"I should write down to a man I know in the country, and ask his advice,\" said Eames; \"an older sort of friend, you know.\"\n\n\"By Jove, old fellow, take care what you're about. Don't let them say of you that you show the white feather. Upon my honour, I'd sooner have anything said of me than that. I would, indeed,--anything.\"\n\n\"I'm not afraid of that,\" said Eames, with a touch of scorn in his voice. \"There isn't much thought about white feathers now-a-days,--not in the way of fighting duels.\"\n\nAfter that, Cradell managed to carry back the conversation to Mrs. Lupex and his own peculiar position, and as Eames did not care to ask from his companion further advice in his own matters, he listened nearly in silence till they reached Burton Crescent.\n\n\"I hope you found the noble earl well,\" said Mrs. Roper to him, as soon as they were all seated at dinner.\n\n\"I found the noble earl pretty well, thank you,\" said Johnny.\n\nIt had become plainly understood by all the Roperites that Eames's position was quite altered since he had been honoured with the friendship of Lord De Guest. Mrs. Lupex, next to whom he always sat at dinner, with a view to protecting her as it were from the dangerous neighbourhood of Cradell, treated him with a marked courtesy. Miss Spruce always called him \"sir.\" Mrs. Roper helped him the first of the gentlemen, and was mindful about his fat and gravy, and Amelia felt less able than she was before to insist upon the possession of his heart and affections. It must not be supposed that Amelia intended to abandon the fight, and allow the enemy to walk off with his forces; but she felt herself constrained to treat him with a deference that was hardly compatible with the perfect equality which should attend any union of hearts.\n\n\"It is such a privilege to be on visiting terms with the nobility,\" said Mrs. Lupex. \"When I was a girl, I used to be very intimate--\"\n\n\"You ain't a girl any longer, and so you'd better not talk about it,\" said Lupex. Mr. Lupex had been at that little shop in Drury Lane after he came down from his scene-painting.\n\n\"My dear, you needn't be a brute to me before all Mrs. Roper's company. If, led away by feelings which I will not now describe, I left my proper circles in marrying you, you need not before all the world teach me how much I have to regret.\" And Mrs. Lupex, putting down her knife and fork, applied her handkerchief to her eyes.\n\n\"That's pleasant for a man over his meals, isn't it?\" said Lupex, appealing to Miss Spruce. \"I have plenty of that kind of thing, and you can't think how I like it.\"\n\n\"Them whom God has joined together, let no man put asunder,\" said Miss Spruce. \"As for me myself, I'm only an old woman.\"\n\nThis little ebullition threw a gloom over the dinner-table, and nothing more was said on the occasion as to the glories of Eames's career. But, in the course of the evening, Amelia heard of the encounter which had taken place at the railway station, and at once perceived that she might use the occasion for her own purposes.\n\n\"John,\" she whispered to her victim, finding an opportunity for coming upon him when almost alone, \"what is this I hear? I insist upon knowing. Are you going to fight a duel?\"\n\n\"Nonsense,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"But it is not nonsense. You don't know what my feelings will be, if I think that such a thing is going to happen. But then you are so hard-hearted!\"\n\n\"I ain't hard-hearted a bit, and I'm not going to fight a duel.\"\n\n\"But is it true that you beat Mr. Crosbie at the station?\"\n\n\"It is true. I did beat him.\"\n\n\"Oh, John! not that I mean to say you were wrong, and indeed I honour you for the feeling. There can be nothing so dreadful as a young man's deceiving a young woman and leaving her after he has won her heart--particularly when she has had his promise in plain words, or, perhaps, even in black and white.\" John thought of that horrid, foolish, wretched note which he had written. \"And a poor girl, if she can't right herself by a breach of promise, doesn't know what to do. Does she, John?\"\n\n\"A girl who'd right herself that way wouldn't be worth having.\"\n\n\"I don't know about that. When a poor girl is in such a position, she has to be aided by her friends. I suppose, then, Miss Lily Dale won't bring a breach of promise against him.\"\n\nThis mention of Lily's name in such a place was sacrilege in the ears of poor Eames. \"I cannot tell,\" said he, \"what may be the intention of the lady of whom you speak. But from what I know of her friends, I should not think that she will be disgraced by such a proceeding.\"\n\n\"That may be all very well for Miss Lily Dale--\" Amelia said, and then she hesitated. It would not be well, she thought, absolutely to threaten him as yet,--not as long as there was any possibility that he might be won without a threat. \"Of course I know all about it,\" she continued. \"She was your L. D., you know. Not that I was ever jealous of her. To you she was no more than one of childhood's friends. Was she, Johnny?\"\n\nHe stamped his foot upon the floor, and then jumped up from his seat. \"I hate all that sort of twaddle about childhood's friends, and you know I do. You'll make me swear that I'll never come into this room again.\"\n\n\"Johnny!\"\n\n\"So I will. The whole thing makes me sick. And as for that Mrs. Lupex--\"\n\n\"If this is what you learn, John, by going to a lord's house, I think you had better stay at home with your own friends.\"\n\n\"Of course I had;--much better stay at home with my own friends. Here's Mrs. Lupex, and at any rate I can't stand her.\" So he went off, and walked round the Crescent, and down to the New Road, and almost into the Regent's Park, thinking of Lily Dale and of his own cowardice with Amelia Roper.\n\nOn the following morning he received a message, at about one o'clock, by the mouth of the Board-room messenger, informing him that his presence was required in the Board-room. \"Sir Raffle Buffle has desired your presence, Mr. Eames.\"\n\n\"My presence, Tupper! what for?\" said Johnny, turning upon the messenger almost with dismay.\n\n\"Indeed I can't say, Mr. Eames; but Sir Raffle Buffle has desired your presence in the Board-room.\"\n\nSuch a message as that in official life always strikes awe into the heart of a young man. And yet, young men generally come forth from such interviews without having received any serious damage, and generally talk about the old gentlemen whom they have encountered with a good deal of light-spirited sarcasm,--or chaff, as it is called in the slang phraseology of the day. It is that same \"majesty which doth hedge a king\" that does it. The turkey-cock in his own farmyard is master of the occasion, and the thought of him creates fear. A bishop in his lawn, a judge on the bench, a chairman in the big room at the end of a long table, or a policeman with his bull's-eye lamp upon his beat, can all make themselves terrible by means of those appanages of majesty which have been vouchsafed to them. But how mean is the policeman in his own home, and how few thought much of Sir Raffle Buffle as he sat asleep after dinner in his old slippers! How well can I remember the terror created within me by the air of outraged dignity with which a certain fine old gentleman, now long since gone, could rub his hands slowly, one on the other, and look up to the ceiling, slightly shaking his head, as though lost in the contemplation of my iniquities! I would become sick in my stomach, and feel as though my ankles had been broken. That upward turn of the eye unmanned me so completely that I was speechless as regarded any defence. I think that that old man could hardly have known the extent of his own power.\n\nOnce upon a time a careless lad, having the charge of a bundle of letters addressed to the King,--petitions and such like, which in the course of business would not get beyond the hands of some lord-in-waiting's deputy assistant,--sent the bag which contained them to the wrong place; to Windsor, perhaps, if the Court were in London; or to St. James's, if it were at Windsor. He was summoned; and the great man of the occasion contented himself with holding his hands up to the heavens as he stood up from his chair, and exclaiming twice, \"Mis-sent the Monarch's pouch! Mis-sent the Monarch's pouch!\" That young man never knew how he escaped from the Board-room; but for a time he was deprived of all power of exertion, and could not resume his work till he had had six months' leave of absence, and been brought round upon rum and asses' milk. In that instance the peculiar use of the word Monarch had a power which the official magnate had never contemplated. The story is traditional; but I believe that the circumstance happened as lately as in the days of George the Third.\n\nJohn Eames could laugh at the present chairman of the Income-tax Office with great freedom, and call him old Huffle Scuffle, and the like; but now that he was sent for, he also, in spite of his radical propensities, felt a little weak about his ankle joints. He knew, from the first hearing of the message, that he was wanted with reference to that affair at the railway station. Perhaps there might be a rule that any clerk should be dismissed who used his fists in any public place. There were many rules entailing the punishment of dismissal for many offences,--and he began to think that he did remember something of such a regulation. However, he got up, looked once around him upon his friends, and then followed Tupper into the Board-room.\n\n\"There's Johnny been sent for by old Scuffles,\" said one clerk.\n\n\"That's about his row with Crosbie,\" said another. \"The Board can't do anything to him for that.\"\n\n\"Can't it?\" said the first. \"Didn't young Outonites have to resign because of that row at the Cider Cellars, though his cousin, Sir Constant Outonites, did all that he could for him?\"\n\n\"But he was regularly up the spout with accommodation bills.\"\n\n\"I tell you that I wouldn't be in Eames's shoes for a trifle. Crosbie is secretary at the Committee Office, where Scuffles was chairman before he came here; and of course they're as thick as thieves. I shouldn't wonder if they didn't make him go down and apologize.\"\n\n\"Johnny won't do that,\" said the other.\n\nIn the meantime John Eames was standing in the august presence. Sir Raffle Buffle was throned in his great oak arm-chair at the head of a long table in a very large room; and by him, at the corner of the table, was seated one of the assistant secretaries of the office. Another member of the Board was also at work upon the long table; but he was reading and signing papers at some distance from Sir Raffle, and paid no heed whatever to the scene. The assistant secretary, looking on, could see that Sir Raffle was annoyed by this want of attention on the part of his colleague, but all this was lost upon Eames.\n\n\"Mr. Eames?\" said Sir Raffle, speaking with a peculiarly harsh voice, and looking at the culprit through a pair of gold-rimmed glasses, which he perched for the occasion upon his big nose. \"Isn't that Mr. Eames?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said the assistant secretary, \"this is Eames.\"\n\n\"Ah!\"--and then there was a pause. \"Come a little nearer, Mr. Eames, will you?\" and Johnny drew nearer, advancing noiselessly over the Turkey carpet.\n\n\"Let me see; in the second class, isn't he? Ah! Do you know, Mr. Eames, that I have received a letter from the secretary to the Directors of the Great Western Railway Company, detailing circumstances which,--if truly stated in that letter,--redound very much to your discredit?\"\n\n\"I did get into a row there yesterday, sir.\"\n\n\"Got into a row! It seems to me that you have got into a very serious row, and that I must tell the Directors of the Great Western Railway Company that the law must be allowed to take its course.\"\n\n\"I shan't mind that, sir, in the least,\" said Eames, brightening up a little under this view of the case.\n\n\"Not mind that, sir!\" said Sir Raffle--or rather, he shouted out the words at the offender before him. I am inclined to think that he overdid it, missing the effect which a milder tone might have attained. Perhaps there was lacking to him some of that majesty of demeanour and dramatic propriety of voice which had been so efficacious in the little story as to the King's bag of letters. As it was, Johnny gave a slight jump, but after his jump he felt better than he had been before. \"Not mind, sir, being dragged before the criminal tribunals of your country, and being punished as a felon,--or rather as a misdemeanour,--for an outrage committed on a public platform! Not mind it! What do you mean, sir?\"\n\n\"I mean, that I don't think the magistrate would say very much about it, sir. And I don't think Mr. Crosbie would come forward.\"\n\n\"But Mr. Crosbie must come forward, young man. Do you suppose that an outrage against the peace of the Metropolis is to go unpunished because he may not wish to pursue the matter? I'm afraid you must be very ignorant, young man.\"\n\n\"Perhaps I am,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Very ignorant indeed,--very ignorant indeed. And are you aware, sir, that it would become a question with the Commissioners of this Board whether you could be retained in the service of this department if you were publicly punished by a police magistrate for such a disgraceful outrage as that?\"\n\nJohnny looked round at the other Commissioner, but that gentleman did not raise his face from his papers.\n\n\"Mr. Eames is a very good clerk,\" whispered the assistant secretary, but in a voice which made his words audible to Eames; \"one of the best young men we have,\" he added, in a voice which was not audible.\n\n\"Oh,--ah; very well. Now, I'll tell you what, Mr. Eames, I hope this will be a lesson to you,--a very serious lesson.\"\n\nThe assistant secretary, leaning back in his chair so as to be a little behind the head of Sir Raffle, did manage to catch the eye of the other Commissioner. The other Commissioner, barely looking round, smiled a little, and then the assistant secretary smiled also. Eames saw this, and he smiled too.\n\n\"Whether any ulterior consequences may still await the breach of the peace of which you have been guilty, I am not yet prepared to say,\" continued Sir Raffle. \"You may go now.\"\n\nAnd Johnny returned to his own place, with no increased reverence for the dignity of the chairman.\n\nOn the following morning one of his colleagues showed him with great glee the passage in the newspaper which informed the world that he had been so desperately beaten by Crosbie that he was obliged to keep his bed at this present time in consequence of the flogging that he had received. Then his anger was aroused, and he bounced about the big room of the Income-tax Office, regardless of assistant secretaries, head clerks, and all other official grandees whatsoever, denouncing the iniquities of the public press, and declaring his opinion that it would be better to live in Russia than in a country which allowed such audacious falsehoods to be propagated.\n\n\"He never touched me, Fisher; I don't think he ever tried; but, upon my honour, he never touched me.\"\n\n\"But, Johnny, it was bold in you to make up to Lord De Courcy's daughter,\" said Fisher.\n\n\"I never saw one of them in my life.\"\n\n\"He's going it altogether among the aristocracy, now,\" said another; \"I suppose you wouldn't look at anybody under a viscount?\"\n\n\"Can I help what that thief of an editor puts into his paper? Flogged! Huffle Scuffle told me I was a felon, but that wasn't half so bad as this fellow;\" and Johnny kicked the newspaper across the room.\n\n\"Indict him for a libel,\" said Fisher.\n\n\"Particularly for saying you wanted to marry a countess's daughter,\" said another clerk.\n\n\"I never heard such a scandal in my life,\" declared a third; \"and then to say that the girl wouldn't look at you.\"\n\nBut not the less was it felt by all in the office that Johnny Eames was becoming a leading man among them, and that he was one with whom each of them would be pleased to be intimate. And even among the grandees this affair of the railway station did him no real harm. It was known that Crosbie had deserved to be thrashed, and known that Eames had thrashed him. It was all very well for Sir Raffle Buffle to talk of police magistrates and misdemeanours, but all the world at the Income-tax Office knew very well that Eames had come out from that affair with his head upright, and his right foot foremost.\n\n\"Never mind about the newspaper,\" a thoughtful old senior clerk said to him. \"As he did get the licking and you didn't, you can afford to laugh at the newspaper.\"\n\n\"And you wouldn't write to the editor?\"\n\n\"No, no; certainly not. No one thinks of defending himself to a newspaper except an ass;--unless it be some fellow who wants to have his name puffed. You may write what's as true as the gospel, but they'll know how to make fun of it.\"\n\nJohnny therefore gave up his idea of an indignant letter to the editor, but he felt that he was bound to give some explanation of the whole matter to Lord De Guest. The affair had happened as he was coming from the earl's house, and all his own concerns had now been made so much a matter of interest to his kind friend, that he thought that he could not with propriety leave the earl to learn from the newspapers either the facts or the falsehoods. And, therefore, before he left his office he wrote the following letter:--\n\nIncome-tax Office, December 29, 186--.\n\nHe thought a good deal about the style in which he ought to address the peer, never having hitherto written to him. He began, \"My dear Lord,\" on one sheet of paper, and then put it aside, thinking that it looked over-bold.\n\nAs you have been so very kind to me, I feel that I ought to tell you what happened the other morning at the railway station, as I was coming back from Guestwick. That scoundrel Crosbie got into the same carriage with me at the Barchester Junction, and sat opposite to me all the way up to London. I did not speak a word to him, or he to me; but when he got out at the Paddington Station, I thought I ought not to let him go away, so I-- I can't say that I thrashed him as I wished to do; but I made an attempt, and I did give him a black eye. A whole quantity of policemen got round us, and I hadn't a fair chance. I know you will think that I was wrong, and perhaps I was; but what could I do when he sat opposite to me there for two hours, looking as though he thought himself the finest fellow in all London?\n\nThey've put a horrible paragraph into one of the newspapers, saying that I got so \"flogged\" that I haven't been able to stir since. It is an atrocious falsehood, as is all the rest of the newspaper account. I was not touched. He was not nearly so bad a customer as the bull, and seemed to take it all very quietly. I must acknowledge, though, that he didn't get such a beating as he deserved.\n\nYour friend Sir R. B. sent for me this morning, and told me I was a felon. I didn't seem to care much for that, for he might as well have called me a murderer or a burglar; but I shall care very much indeed if I have made you angry with me. But what I most fear is the anger of some one else,--at Allington.\n\nBelieve me to be, my Lord, Yours very much obliged and most sincerely,\n\n\"I knew he'd do it if ever he got the opportunity,\" said the earl when he had read his letter; and he walked about his room striking his hands together, and then thrusting his thumbs into his waistcoat-pockets. \"I knew he was made of the right stuff,\" and the earl rejoiced greatly in the prowess of his favourite. \"I'd have done it myself if I'd seen him. I do believe I would.\" Then he went back to the breakfast-room and told Lady Julia. \"What do you think?\" said he; \"Johnny Eames has come across Crosbie, and given him a desperate beating.\"\n\n\"No!\" said Lady Julia, putting down her newspaper and spectacles, and expressing by the light of her eyes anything but Christian horror at the wickedness of the deed.\n\n\"But he has, though. I knew he would if he saw him.\"\n\n\"Beaten him! Actually beaten him!\"\n\n\"Sent him home to Lady Alexandrina with two black eyes.\"\n\n\"Two black eyes! What a young pickle! But did he get hurt himself?\"\n\n\"Not a scratch, he says.\"\n\n\"And what'll they do to him?\"\n\n\"Nothing. Crosbie won't be fool enough to do anything. A man becomes an outlaw when he plays such a game as he has played. Anybody's hand may be raised against him with impunity. He can't show his face, you know. He can't come forward and answer questions as to what he has done. There are offences which the law can't touch, but which outrage public feeling so strongly that any one may take upon himself the duty of punishing them. He has been thrashed, and that will stick to him till he dies.\"\n\n\"Do tell Johnny from me that I hope he didn't get hurt,\" said Lady Julia. The old lady could not absolutely congratulate him on his feat of arms, but she did the next thing to it.\n\nBut the earl did congratulate him, with a full open assurance of his approval.\n\n\"I hope,\" he said, \"I should have done the same at your age, under similar circumstances, and I'm very glad that he proved less difficult than the bull. I'm quite sure you didn't want any one to help you with Master Crosbie. As for that other person at Allington, if I understand such matters at all, I think she will forgive you.\" It may, however, be a question whether the earl did understand such matters at all. And then he added, in a postscript: \"When you write to me again,--and don't be long first, begin your letter, 'My dear Lord De Guest,'--that is the proper way.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII. AN OLD MAN'S COMPLAINT.\n\n\"Have you been thinking again of what I was saying to you, Bell?\" Bernard said to his cousin one morning.\n\n\"Thinking of it, Bernard? Why should I think more of it? I had hoped that you had forgotten it yourself.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said; \"I am not so easy-hearted as that. I cannot look on such a thing as I would the purchase of a horse, which I could give up without sorrow if I found that the animal was too costly for my purse. I did not tell you that I loved you till I was sure of myself, and having made myself sure I cannot change at all.\"\n\n\"And yet you would have me change.\"\n\n\"Yes, of course I would. If your heart be free now, it must of course be changed before you come to love any man. Such change as that is to be looked for. But when you have loved, then it will not be easy to change you.\"\n\n\"But I have not.\"\n\n\"Then I have a right to hope. I have been hanging on here, Bell, longer than I ought to have done, because I could not bring myself to leave you without speaking of this again. I did not wish to seem to you to be importunate--\"\n\n\"If you could only believe me in what I say.\"\n\n\"It is not that I do not believe. I am not a puppy or a fool, to flatter myself that you must be in love with me. I believe you well enough. But still it is possible that your mind may alter.\"\n\n\"It is impossible.\"\n\n\"I do not know whether my uncle or your mother have spoken to you about this.\"\n\n\"Such speaking would have no effect.\"\n\nIn fact, her mother had spoken to her, but she truly said that such speaking would have no effect. If her cousin could not win the battle by his own skill, he might have been quite sure, looking at her character as it was known to him, that he would not be able to win it by the skill of others.\n\n\"We have all been made very unhappy,\" he went on to say, \"by this calamity which has fallen on poor Lily.\"\n\n\"And because she has been deceived by the man she did love, I am to make matters square by marrying a man I--\" and then she paused. \"Dear Bernard, you should not drive me to say words which will sound harsh to you.\"\n\n\"No words can be harsher than those which you have already spoken. But, Bell, at any rate, you may listen to me.\"\n\nThen he told her how desirable it was with reference to all the concerns of the Dale family that she should endeavour to look favourably on his proposition. It would be good for them all, he said, especially for Lily, as to whom, at the present moment, their uncle felt so kindly. He, as Bernard pleaded, was so anxious at heart for this marriage, that he would do anything that was asked of him if he were gratified. But if he were not gratified in this, he would feel that he had ground for displeasure.\n\nBell, as she had been desired to listen, did listen very patiently. But when her cousin had finished, her answer was very short. \"Nothing that my uncle can say, or think, or do, can make any difference in this,\" said she.\n\n\"You will think nothing, then, of the happiness of others.\"\n\n\"I would not marry a man I did not love, to ensure any amount of happiness to others;--at least I know I ought not to do so. But I do not believe I should ensure any one's happiness by this marriage. Certainly not yours.\"\n\nAfter this Bernard had acknowledged to himself that the difficulties in his way were great. \"I will go away till next autumn,\" he said to his uncle.\n\n\"If you would give up your profession and remain here, she would not be so perverse.\"\n\n\"I cannot do that, sir. I cannot risk the well-being of my life on such a chance.\" Then his uncle had been angry with him, as well as with his niece. In his anger he determined that he would go again to his sister-in-law, and, after some unreasonable fashion, he resolved that it would become him to be very angry with her also, if she declined to assist him with all her influence as a mother.\n\n\"Why should they not both marry?\" he said to himself. Lord De Guest's offer as to young Eames had been very generous. As he had then declared, he had not been able to express his own opinion at once; but on thinking over what the earl had said, he had found himself very willing to heal the family wound in the manner proposed, if any such healing might be possible. That, however, could not be done quite as yet. When the time should come, and he thought it might come soon,--perhaps in the spring, when the days should be fine and the evenings again long,--he would be willing to take his share with the earl in establishing that new household. To Crosbie he had refused to give anything, and there was upon his conscience a shade of remorse in that he had so refused. But if Lily could be brought to love this other man, he would be more open-handed. She should have her share as though she was in fact his daughter. But then, if he intended to do so much for them at the Small House, should not they in return do something also for him? So thinking, he went again to his sister-in-law, determined to explain his views, even though it might be at the risk of some hard words between them. As regarded himself, he did not much care for hard words spoken to him. He almost expected that people's words should be hard and painful. He did not look for the comfort of affectionate soft greetings, and perhaps would not have appreciated them had they come to him. He caught Mrs. Dale walking in the garden, and brought her into his own room, feeling that he had a better chance there than in her own house. She, with an old dislike to being lectured in that room, had endeavoured to avoid the interview, but had failed.\n\n\"So I met John Eames at the manor,\" he had said to her in the garden.\n\n\"Ah, yes; and how did he get on there? I cannot conceive poor Johnny keeping holiday with the earl and his sister. How did he behave to them, and how did they behave to him?\"\n\n\"I can assure you he was very much at home there.\"\n\n\"Was he, indeed? Well, I hope it will do him good. He is, I'm sure, a very good young man; only rather awkward.\"\n\n\"I didn't think him awkward at all. You'll find, Mary, that he'll do very well;--a great deal better than his father did.\"\n\n\"I'm sure I hope he may.\" After that Mrs. Dale made her attempt to escape; but the squire had taken her prisoner, and led her captive into the house. \"Mary,\" he said, as soon as he had induced her to sit down, \"it is time that this should be settled between my nephew and niece.\"\n\n\"I am afraid there will be nothing to settle.\"\n\n\"What do you mean;--that you disapprove of it?\"\n\n\"By no means,--personally. I should approve of it very strongly. But that has nothing to do with the question.\"\n\n\"Yes, it has. I beg your pardon, but it must have, and should have a great deal to do with it. Of course, I am not saying that anybody should now ever be compelled to marry anybody.\"\n\n\"I hope not.\"\n\n\"I never said that they ought, and never thought so. But I do think that the wishes of all her family should have very great weight with a girl that has been well brought up.\"\n\n\"I don't know whether Bell has been well brought up; but in such a matter as this nobody's wishes would weigh a feather with her; and, indeed, I could not take upon myself even to express a wish. To you I can say that I should have been very happy if she could have regarded her cousin as you wish her to do.\"\n\n\"You mean that you are afraid to tell her so?\"\n\n\"I am afraid to do what I think is wrong, if you mean that.\"\n\n\"I don't think it would be wrong, and therefore I shall speak to her myself.\"\n\n\"You must do as you like about that, Mr. Dale; I can't prevent you. I shall think you wrong to harass her on such a matter, and I fear also that her answer will not be satisfactory to you. If you choose to tell her your opinion, you must do so. Of course I shall think you wrong, that's all.\"\n\nMrs. Dale's voice as she said this was stern enough, and so was her countenance. She could not forbid the uncle to speak his mind to his niece, but she especially disliked the idea of any interference with her daughter. The squire got up and walked about the room, trying to compose himself that he might answer her rationally, but without anger.\n\n\"May I go now?\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"May you go? Of course you may go if you like it. If you think that I am intruding upon you in speaking to you of the welfare of your two girls, whom I endeavour to regard as my own daughters,--except in this, that I know they have never been taught to love me,--if you think that it is an interference on my part to show anxiety for their welfare, of course you may go.\"\n\n\"I did not mean to say anything to hurt you, Mr. Dale.\"\n\n\"Hurt me! What does it signify whether I am hurt or not? I have no children of my own, and of course my only business in life is to provide for my nephews and nieces. I am an old fool if I expect that they are to love me in return, and if I venture to express a wish I am interfering and doing wrong! It is hard,--very hard. I know well that they have been brought up to dislike me, and yet I am endeavouring to do my duty by them.\"\n\n\"Mr. Dale, that accusation has not been deserved. They have not been brought up to dislike you. I believe that they have both loved and respected you as their uncle; but such love and respect will not give you a right to dispose of their hands.\"\n\n\"Who wants to dispose of their hands?\"\n\n\"There are some things in which I think no uncle,--no parent,--should interfere, and of all such things this is the chief. If after that you may choose to tell her your wishes, of course you can do so.\"\n\n\"It will not be much good after you have set her against me.\"\n\n\"Mr. Dale, you have no right to say such things to me, and you are very unjust in doing so. If you think that I have set my girls against you, it will be much better that we should leave Allington altogether. I have been placed in circumstances which have made it difficult for me to do my duty to my children; but I have endeavoured to do it, not regarding my own personal wishes. I am quite sure, however, that it would be wrong in me to keep them here, if I am to be told by you that I have taught them to regard you unfavourably. Indeed, I cannot suffer such a thing to be said to me.\"\n\nAll this Mrs. Dale said with an air of decision, and with a voice expressing a sense of injury received, which made the squire feel that she was very much in earnest.\n\n\"Is it not true,\" he said, defending himself, \"that in all that relates to the girls you have ever regarded me with suspicion?\"\n\n\"No, it is not true.\" And then she corrected herself, feeling that there was something of truth in the squire's last assertion. \"Certainly not with suspicion,\" she said. \"But as this matter has gone so far, I will explain what my real feelings have been. In worldly matters you can do much for my girls, and have done much.\"\n\n\"And wish to do more,\" said the squire.\n\n\"I am sure you do. But I cannot on that account give up my place as their only living parent. They are my children, and not yours. And even could I bring myself to allow you to act as their guardian and natural protector, they would not consent to such an arrangement. You cannot call that suspicion.\"\n\n\"I can call it jealousy.\"\n\n\"And should not a mother be jealous of her children's love?\"\n\nDuring all this time the squire was walking up and down the room with his hands in his trousers pockets. And when Mrs. Dale had last spoken, he continued his walk for some time in silence.\n\n\"Perhaps it is well that you should have spoken out,\" he said.\n\n\"The manner in which you accused me made it necessary.\"\n\n\"I did not intend to accuse you, and I do not do so now; but I think that you have been, and that you are, very hard to me,--very hard indeed. I have endeavoured to make your children, and yourself also, sharers with me in such prosperity as has been mine. I have striven to add to your comfort and to their happiness. I am most anxious to secure their future welfare. You would have been very wrong had you declined to accept this on their behalf; but I think that in return for it you need not have begrudged me the affection and obedience which generally follows from such good offices.\"\n\n\"Mr. Dale, I have begrudged you nothing of this.\"\n\n\"I am hurt;--I am hurt,\" he continued. And she was surprised by his look of pain even more than by the unaccustomed warmth of his words. \"What you have said has, I have known, been the case all along. But though I had felt it to be so, I own that I am hurt by your open words.\"\n\n\"Because I have said that my own children must ever be my own?\"\n\n\"Ah, you have said more than that. You and the girls have been living here, close to me, for--how many years is it now?--and during all those years there has grown up for me no kindly feeling. Do you think that I cannot hear, and see, and feel? Do you suppose that I am a fool and do not know? As for yourself you would never enter this house if you did not feel yourself constrained to do so for the sake of appearances. I suppose it is all as it should be. Having no children of my own, I owe the duty of a parent to my nieces; but I have no right to expect from them in return either love, regard, or obedience. I know I am keeping you here against your will, Mary. I won't do so any longer.\" And he made a sign to her that she was to depart.\n\nAs she rose from her seat her heart was softened towards him. In these latter days he had shown much kindness to the girls,--a kindness that was more akin to the gentleness of love than had ever come from him before. Lily's fate had seemed to melt even his sternness, and he had striven to be tender in his words and ways. And now he spoke as though he had loved the girls, and had loved them in vain. Doubtless he had been a disagreeable neighbour to his sister-in-law, making her feel that it was never for her personally that he had opened his hand. Doubtless he had been moved by an unconscious desire to undermine and take upon himself her authority with her own children. Doubtless he had looked askance at her from the first day of her marriage with his brother. She had been keenly alive to all this since she had first known him, and more keenly alive to it than ever since the failure of those efforts she had made to live with him on terms of affection, made during the first year or two of her residence at the Small House. But, nevertheless, in spite of all, her heart bled for him now. She had gained her victory over him, having fully held her own position with her children; but now that he complained that he had been beaten in the struggle, her heart bled for him.\n\n\"My brother,\" she said, and as she spoke she offered him her hands, \"it may be that we have not thought as kindly of each other as we should have done.\"\n\n\"I have endeavoured,\" said the old man. \"I have endeavoured--\" And then he stopped, either hindered by some excess of emotion, or unable to find the words which were necessary for the expression of his meaning.\n\n\"Let us endeavour once again,--both of us.\"\n\n\"What, begin again at near seventy! No, Mary, there is no more beginning again for me. All this shall make no difference to the girls. As long as I am here they shall have the house. If they marry, I will do for them what I can. I believe Bernard is much in earnest in his suit, and if Bell will listen to him, she shall still be welcomed here as mistress of Allington. What you have said shall make no difference;--but as to beginning again, it is simply impossible.\"\n\nAfter that Mrs. Dale walked home through the garden by herself. He had studiously told her that that house in which they lived should be lent, not to her, but to her children, during his lifetime. He had positively declined the offer of her warmer regard. He had made her understand that they were to look on each other almost as enemies; but that she, enemy as she was, should still be allowed the use of his munificence, because he chose to do his duty by his nieces!\n\n\"It will be better for us that we shall leave it,\" she said to herself as she seated herself in her own arm-chair over the drawing-room fire.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVIII. DOCTOR CROFTS IS CALLED IN.\n\nMrs. Dale had not sat long in her drawing-room before tidings were brought to her which for a while drew her mind away from that question of her removal. \"Mamma,\" said Bell, entering the room, \"I really do believe that Jane has got scarlatina.\" Jane, the parlour-maid, had been ailing for the last two days, but nothing serious had hitherto been suspected.\n\nMrs. Dale instantly jumped up. \"Who is with her?\" she asked.\n\nIt appeared from Bell's answer that both she and Lily had been with the girl, and that Lily was still in the room. Whereupon Mrs. Dale ran upstairs, and there was on the sudden a commotion in the house. In an hour or so the village doctor was there, and he expressed an opinion that the girl's ailment was certainly scarlatina. Mrs. Dale, not satisfied with this, sent off a boy to Guestwick for Dr. Crofts, having herself maintained an opposition of many years' standing against the medical reputation of the apothecary, and gave a positive order to the two girls not to visit poor Jane again. She herself had had scarlatina, and might do as she pleased. Then, too, a nurse was hired.\n\nAll this changed for a few hours the current of Mrs. Dale's thoughts: but in the evening she went back to the subject of her morning conversation, and before the three ladies went to bed, they held together an open council of war upon the subject. Dr. Crofts had been found to be away from Guestwick, and word had been sent on his behalf that he would be over at Allington early on the following morning. Mrs. Dale had almost made up her mind that the malady of her favourite maid was not scarlatina, but had not on that account relaxed her order as to the absence of her daughters from the maid's bedside.\n\n\"Let us go at once,\" said Bell, who was even more opposed to any domination on the part of her uncle than was her mother. In the discussion which had been taking place between them the whole matter of Bernard's courtship had come upon the carpet. Bell had kept her cousin's offer to herself as long as she had been able to do so; but since her uncle had pressed the subject upon Mrs. Dale, it was impossible for Bell to remain silent any longer. \"You do not want me to marry him, mamma; do you?\" she had said, when her mother had spoken with some show of kindness towards Bernard. In answer to this, Mrs. Dale had protested vehemently that she had no such wish, and Lily, who still held to her belief in Dr. Crofts, was almost equally animated. To them all, the idea that their uncle should in any way interfere in their own views of life, on the strength of the pecuniary assistance which they had received from him, was peculiarly distasteful. But it was especially distasteful that he should presume to have even an opinion as to their disposition in marriage. They declared to each other that their uncle could have no right to object to any marriage which either of them might contemplate as long as their mother should approve of it. The poor old squire had been right in saying that he was regarded with suspicion. He was so regarded. The fault had certainly been his own, in having endeavoured to win the daughters without thinking it worth his while to win the mother. The girls had unconsciously felt that the attempt was made, and had vigorously rebelled against it. It had not been their fault that they had been brought to live in their uncle's house, and made to ride on his ponies, and to eat partially of his bread. They had so eaten, and so lived, and declared themselves to be grateful. The squire was good in his way, and they recognized his goodness; but not on that account would they transfer to him one jot of the allegiance which as children they owed to their mother. When she told them her tale, explaining to them the words which their uncle had spoken that morning, they expressed their regret that he should be so grieved; but they were strong in assurances to their mother that she had been sinned against, and was not sinning.\n\n\"Let us go at once,\" said Bell.\n\n\"It is much easier said than done, my dear.\"\n\n\"Of course it is, mamma; else we shouldn't be here now. What I mean is this,--let us take some necessary first step at once. It is clear that my uncle thinks that our remaining here should give him some right over us. I do not say that he is wrong to think so. Perhaps it is natural. Perhaps, in accepting his kindness, we ought to submit ourselves to him. If that be so, it is a conclusive reason for our going.\"\n\n\"Could we not pay him rent for the house,\" said Lily, \"as Mrs. Hearn does? You would like to remain here, mamma, if you could do that?\"\n\n\"But we could not do that, Lily. We must choose for ourselves a smaller house than this, and one that is not burdened with the expense of a garden. Even if we paid but a moderate rent for this place, we should not have the means of living here.\"\n\n\"Not if we lived on toast and tea?\" said Lily, laughing.\n\n\"But I should hardly wish you to live upon toast and tea; and indeed I fancy that I should get tired of such a diet myself.\"\n\n\"Never, mamma,\" said Lily. \"As for me, I confess to a longing after mutton chops; but I don't think you would ever want such vulgar things.\"\n\n\"At any rate, it would be impossible to remain here,\" said Bell. \"Uncle Christopher would not take rent from mamma; and even if he did, we should not know how to go on with our other arrangements after such a change. No; we must give up the dear old Small House.\"\n\n\"It is a dear old house,\" said Lily, thinking, as she spoke, more of those late scenes in the garden, when Crosbie had been with them in the autumn months, than of any of the former joys of her childhood.\n\n\"After all, I do not know that I should be right to move,\" said Mrs. Dale, doubtingly.\n\n\"Yes, yes,\" said both the girls at once. \"Of course you will be right, mamma; there cannot be a doubt about it, mamma. If we can get any cottage, or even lodgings, that would be better than remaining here, now that we know what uncle Christopher thinks of it.\"\n\n\"It will make him very unhappy,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\nBut even this argument did not in the least move the girls. They were very sorry that their uncle should be unhappy. They would endeavour to show him by some increased show of affection that their feelings towards him were not unkind. Should he speak to them they would endeavour to explain to him that their thoughts towards him were altogether affectionate. But they could not remain at Allington increasing their load of gratitude, seeing that he expected a certain payment which they did not feel themselves able to render.\n\n\"We should be robbing him, if we stayed here,\" Bell declared;--\"wilfully robbing him of what he believes to be his just share of the bargain.\"\n\nSo it was settled among them that notice should be given to their uncle of their intention to quit the Small House of Allington.\n\nAnd then came the question as to their new home. Mrs. Dale was aware that her income was at any rate better than that possessed by Mrs. Eames, and therefore she had fair ground for presuming that she could afford to keep a house at Guestwick. \"If we do go away, that is what we must do,\" she said.\n\n\"And we shall have to walk out with Mary Eames, instead of Susan Boyce,\" said Lily. \"It won't make so much difference after all.\"\n\n\"In that respect we shall gain as much as we lose,\" said Bell.\n\n\"And then it will be so nice to have the shops,\" said Lily, ironically.\n\n\"Only we shall never have any money to buy anything,\" said Bell.\n\n\"But we shall see more of the world,\" said Lily. \"Lady Julia's carriage comes into town twice a week, and the Miss Gruffens drive about in great style. Upon the whole, we shall gain a great deal; only for the poor old garden. Mamma, I do think I shall break my heart at parting with Hopkins; and as to him, I shall be disappointed in mankind if he ever holds his head up again after I am gone.\"\n\nBut in truth there was very much of sadness in their resolution, and to Mrs. Dale it seemed as though she were managing matters badly for her daughters, and allowing poverty and misfortune to come upon them through her own fault. She well knew how great a load of sorrow was lying on Lily's heart, hidden beneath those little attempts at pleasantry which she made. When she spoke of being disappointed in mankind, Mrs. Dale could hardly repress an outward shudder that would betray her thoughts. And now she was consenting to take them forth from their comfortable home, from the luxury of their lawns and gardens, and to bring them to some small dingy corner of a provincial town,--because she had failed to make herself happy with her brother-in-law. Could she be right to give up all the advantages which they enjoyed at Allington,--advantages which had come to them from so legitimate a source,--because her own feelings had been wounded? In all their future want of comfort, in the comfortless dowdiness of the new home to which she would remove them, would she not always blame herself for having brought them to that by her own false pride? And yet it seemed to her that she now had no alternative. She could not now teach her daughters to obey their uncle's wishes in all things. She could not make Bell understand that it would be well that she should marry Bernard because the squire had set his heart on such a marriage. She had gone so far that she could not now go back.\n\n\"I suppose we must move at Lady-day?\" said Bell, who was in favour of instant action. \"If so, had you not better let uncle Christopher know at once?\"\n\n\"I don't think that we can find a house by that time.\"\n\n\"We can get in somewhere,\" continued Bell. \"There are plenty of lodgings in Guestwick, you know.\" But the sound of the word lodgings was uncomfortable in Mrs. Dale's ears.\n\n\"If we are to go, let us go at once,\" said Lily. \"We need not stand much upon the order of our going.\"\n\n\"Your uncle will be very much shocked,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"He cannot say that it is your fault,\" said Bell.\n\nIt was thus agreed between them that the necessary information should be at once given to the squire, and that the old, well-loved house should be left for ever. It would be a great fall in a worldly point of view,--from the Allington Small House to an abode in some little street of Guestwick. At Allington they had been county people,--raised to a level with their own squire and other squires by the circumstance of their residence; but at Guestwick they would be small even among the people of the town. They would be on an equality with the Eameses, and much looked down upon by the Gruffens. They would hardly dare to call any more at Guestwick Manor, seeing that they certainly could not expect Lady Julia to call upon them at Guestwick. Mrs. Boyce no doubt would patronize them, and they could already anticipate the condolence which would be offered to them by Mrs. Hearn. Indeed such a movement on their part would be tantamount to a confession of failure in the full hearing of so much of the world as was known to them.\n\nI must not allow my readers to suppose that these considerations were a matter of indifference to any of the ladies at the Small House. To some women of strong mind, of highly-strung philosophic tendencies, such considerations might have been indifferent. But Mrs. Dale was not of this nature, nor were her daughters. The good things of the world were good in their eyes, and they valued the privilege of a pleasant social footing among their friends. They were by no means capable of a wise contempt of the advantages which chance had hitherto given to them. They could not go forth rejoicing in the comparative poverty of their altered condition. But then, neither could they purchase those luxuries which they were about to abandon at the price which was asked for them.\n\n\"Had you not better write to my uncle?\" said one of the girls. But to this Mrs. Dale objected that she could not make a letter on such a subject clearly intelligible, and that therefore she would see the squire on the following morning. \"It will be very dreadful,\" she said, \"but it will soon be over. It is not what he will say at the moment that I fear so much, as the bitter reproaches of his face when I shall meet him afterwards.\" So, on the following morning, she again made her way, and now without invitation, to the squire's study.\n\n\"Mr. Dale,\" she began, starting upon her work with some confusion in her manner, and hurry in her speech, \"I have been thinking over what we were saying together yesterday, and I have come to a resolution which I know I ought to make known to you without a moment's delay.\"\n\nThe squire also had thought of what had passed between them, and had suffered much as he had done so; but he had thought of it without acerbity or anger. His thoughts were ever gentler than his words, and his heart softer than any exponent of his heart that he was able to put forth. He wished to love his brother's children, and to be loved by them; but even failing that, he wished to do good to them. It had not occurred to him to be angry with Mrs. Dale after that interview was over. The conversation had not gone pleasantly with him; but then he hardly expected that things would go pleasantly. No idea had occurred to him that evil could come upon any of the Dale ladies from the words which had then been spoken. He regarded the Small House as their abode and home as surely as the Great House was his own. In giving him his due, it must be declared that any allusion to their holding these as a benefit done to them by him had been very far from his thoughts. Mrs. Hearn, who held her cottage at half its real value, grumbled almost daily at him as her landlord; but it never occurred to him that therefore he should raise her rent, or that in not doing so he was acting with special munificence. It had ever been to him a grumbling, cross-grained, unpleasant world; and he did not expect from Mrs. Hearn, or from his sister-in-law, anything better than that to which he had ever been used.\n\n\"It will make me very happy,\" said he, \"if it has any bearing on Bell's marriage with her cousin.\"\n\n\"Mr. Dale, that is out of the question. I would not vex you by saying so if I were not certain of it; but I know my child so well!\"\n\n\"Then we must leave it to time, Mary.\"\n\n\"Yes, of course; but no time will suffice to make Bell change her mind. We will, however, leave the subject. And now, Mr. Dale, I have to tell you of something else;--we have resolved to leave the Small House.\"\n\n\"Resolved on what?\" said the squire, turning his eyes full upon her.\n\n\"We have resolved to leave the Small House.\"\n\n\"Leave the Small House!\" he said, repeating her words; \"and where on earth do you mean to go?\"\n\n\"We think we shall go into Guestwick.\"\n\n\"And why?\"\n\n\"Ah, that is so hard to explain. If you would only accept the fact as I tell it to you, and not ask for the reasons which have guided me!\"\n\n\"But that is out of the question, Mary. In such a matter as that I must ask your reasons; and I must tell you also that, in my opinion, you will not be doing your duty to your daughters in carrying out such an intention, unless your reasons are very strong indeed.\"\n\n\"But they are very strong,\" said Mrs. Dale; and then she paused.\n\n\"I cannot understand it,\" said the squire. \"I cannot bring myself to believe that you are really in earnest. Are you not comfortable there?\"\n\n\"More comfortable than we have any right to be with our means.\"\n\n\"But I thought you always did very nicely with your money. You never get into debt.\"\n\n\"No; I never get into debt. It is not that, exactly. The fact is, Mr. Dale, we have no right to live there without paying rent; but we could not afford to live there if we did pay rent.\"\n\n\"Who has talked about rent?\" he said, jumping up from his chair. \"Some one has been speaking falsehoods of me behind my back.\" No gleam of the real truth had yet come to him. No idea had reached his mind that his relatives thought it necessary to leave his house in consequence of any word that he himself had spoken. He had never considered himself to have been in any special way generous to them, and would not have thought it reasonable that they should abandon the house in which they had been living, even if his anger against them had been strong and hot. \"Mary,\" he said, \"I must insist upon getting to the bottom of this. As for your leaving the house, it is out of the question. Where can you be better off, or so well? As to going into Guestwick, what sort of life would there be for the girls? I put all that aside as out of the question; but I must know what has induced you to make such a proposition. Tell me honestly,--has any one spoken evil of me behind my back?\"\n\nMrs. Dale had been prepared for opposition and for reproach; but there was a decision about the squire's words, and an air of masterdom in his manner, which made her recognize more fully than she had yet done the difficulty of her position. She almost began to fear that she would lack power to carry out her purpose.\n\n\"Indeed, it is not so, Mr. Dale.\"\n\n\"Then what is it?\"\n\n\"I know that if I attempt to tell you, you will be vexed, and will contradict me.\"\n\n\"Vexed I shall be, probably.\"\n\n\"And yet I cannot help it. Indeed, I am endeavouring to do what is right by you and by the children.\"\n\n\"Never mind me; your duty is to think of them.\"\n\n\"Of course it is; and in doing this they most cordially agree with me.\"\n\nIn using such argument as that, Mrs. Dale showed her weakness, and the squire was not slow to take advantage of it. \"Your duty is to them,\" he said; \"but I do not mean by that that your duty is to let them act in any way that may best please them for the moment. I can understand that they should be run away with by some romantic nonsense, but I cannot understand it of you.\"\n\n\"The truth is this, Mr. Dale. You think that my children owe to you that sort of obedience which is due to a parent, and as long as they remain here, accepting from your hands so large a part of their daily support, it is perhaps natural that you should think so. In this unhappy affair about Bell--\"\n\n\"I have never said anything of the kind,\" said the squire, interrupting her.\n\n\"No; you have not said so. And I do not wish you to think that I make any complaint. But I feel that it is so, and they feel it. And, therefore, we have made up our minds to go away.\"\n\nMrs. Dale, as she finished, was aware that she had not told her story well, but she had acknowledged to herself that it was quite out of her power to tell it as it should be told. Her main object was to make her brother-in-law understand that she certainly would leave his house, and to make him understand this with as little pain to himself as possible. She did not in the least mind his thinking her foolish, if only she could so carry her point as to be able to tell her daughters on her return that the matter was settled. But the squire, from his words and manners, seemed indisposed to give her this privilege.\n\n\"Of all the propositions which I ever heard,\" said he, \"it is the most unreasonable. It amounts to this, that you are too proud to live rent-free in a house which belongs to your husband's brother, and therefore you intend to subject yourself and your children to the great discomfort of a very straitened income. If you yourself only were concerned I should have no right to say anything; but I think myself bound to tell you that, as regards the girls, everybody that knows you will think you to have been very wrong. It is in the natural course of things that they should live in that house. The place has never been let. As far as I know, no rent has ever been paid for the house since it was built. It has always been given to some member of the family, who has been considered as having the best right to it. I have considered your footing there as firm as my own here. A quarrel between me and your children would be to me a great calamity, though, perhaps, they might be indifferent to it. But if there were such a quarrel it would afford no reason for their leaving that house. Let me beg you to think over the matter again.\"\n\nThe squire could assume an air of authority on certain occasions, and he had done so now. Mrs. Dale found that she could only answer him by a simple repetition of her own intention; and, indeed, failed in making him any serviceable answer whatsoever.\n\n\"I know that you are very good to my girls,\" she said.\n\n\"I will say nothing about that,\" he answered; not thinking at that moment of the Small House, but of the full possession which he had desired to give to the elder of all the privileges which should belong to the mistress of Allington,--thinking also of the means by which he was hoping to repair poor Lily's shattered fortunes. What words were further said had no great significance, and Mrs. Dale got herself away, feeling that she had failed. As soon as she was gone the squire arose, and putting on his great-coat, went forth with his hat and stick to the front of the house. He went out in order that his thoughts might be more free, and that he might indulge in that solace which an injured man finds in contemplating his injury. He declared to himself that he was very hardly used,--so hardly used, that he almost began to doubt himself and his own motives. Why was it that the people around him disliked him so strongly,--avoided him and thwarted him in the efforts which he made for their welfare? He offered to his nephew all the privileges of a son,--much more indeed than the privileges of a son,--merely asking in return that he would consent to live permanently in the house which was to be his own. But his nephew refused. \"He cannot bear to live with me,\" said the old man to himself sorely. He was prepared to treat his nieces with more generosity than the daughters of the House of Allington had usually received from their fathers; and they repelled his kindness, running away from him, and telling him openly that they would not be beholden to him. He walked slowly up and down the terrace, thinking of this very bitterly. He did not find in the contemplation of his grievance all that solace which a grievance usually gives, because he accused himself in his thoughts rather than others. He declared to himself that he was made to be hated, and protested to himself that it would be well that he should die and be buried out of memory, so that the remaining Dales might have a better chance of living happily; and then as he thus discussed all this within his own bosom, his thoughts were very tender, and though he was aggrieved, he was most affectionate to those who had most injured him. But it was absolutely beyond his power to reproduce outwardly, with words and outward signs, such thoughts and feelings.\n\nIt was now very nearly the end of the year, but the weather was still soft and open. The air was damp rather than cold, and the lawns and fields still retained the green tints of new vegetation. As the squire was walking on the terrace Hopkins came up to him, and touching his hat, remarked that they should have frost in a day or two.\n\n\"I suppose we shall,\" said the squire.\n\n\"We must have the mason to the flues of that little grape-house, sir, before I can do any good with a fire there.\"\n\n\"Which grape-house?\" said the squire, crossly.\n\n\"Why, the grape-house in the other garden, sir. It ought to have been done last year by rights.\" This Hopkins said to punish his master for being cross to him. On that matter of the flues of Mrs. Dale's grape-house he had, with much consideration, spared his master during the last winter, and he felt that this ought to be remembered now. \"I can't put any fire in it, not to do any real good, till something's done. That's sure.\"\n\n\"Then don't put any fire in it,\" said the squire.\n\nNow the grapes in question were supposed to be peculiarly fine, and were the glory of the garden of the Small House. They were always forced, though not forced so early as those at the Great House, and Hopkins was in a state of great confusion.\n\n\"They'll never ripen, sir; not the whole year through.\"\n\n\"Then let them be unripe,\" said the squire, walking about.\n\nHopkins did not at all understand it. The squire in his natural course was very unwilling to neglect any such matter as this, but would be specially unwilling to neglect anything touching the Small House. So Hopkins stood on the terrace, raising his hat and scratching his head. \"There's something wrong amongst them,\" said he to himself, sorrowfully.\n\nBut when the squire had walked to the end of the terrace and had turned upon the path which led round the side of the house, he stopped and called to Hopkins.\n\n\"Have what is needful done to the flue,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes, sir; very well, sir. It'll only be re-setting the bricks. Nothing more ain't needful, just this winter.\"\n\n\"Have the place put in perfect order while you're about it,\" said the squire, and then he walked away.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIX. DR. CROFTS IS TURNED OUT.\n\n\"Have you heard the news, my dear, from the Small House?\" said Mrs. Boyce to her husband, some two or three days after Mrs. Dale's visit to the squire. It was one o'clock, and the parish pastor had come in from his ministrations to dine with his wife and children.\n\n\"What news?\" said Mr. Boyce, for he had heard none.\n\n\"Mrs. Dale and the girls are going to leave the Small House; they're going into Guestwick to live.\"\n\n\"Mrs. Dale going away; nonsense!\" said the vicar. \"What on earth should take her into Guestwick? She doesn't pay a shilling of rent where she is.\"\n\n\"I can assure you it's true, my dear. I was with Mrs. Hearn just now, and she had it direct from Mrs. Dale's own lips. Mrs. Hearn said she'd never been taken so much aback in her whole life. There's been some quarrel, you may be sure of that.\"\n\nMr. Boyce sat silent, pulling off his dirty shoes preparatory to his dinner. Tidings so important, as touching the social life of his parish, had not come to him for many a day, and he could hardly bring himself to credit them at so short a notice.\n\n\"Mrs. Hearn says that Mrs. Dale spoke ever so firmly about it, as though determined that nothing should change her.\"\n\n\"And did she say why?\"\n\n\"Well, not exactly. But Mrs. Hearn said she could understand there had been words between her and the squire. It couldn't be anything else, you know. Probably it had something to do with that man Crosbie.\"\n\n\"They'll be very pushed about money,\" said Mr. Boyce, thrusting his feet into his slippers.\n\n\"That's just what I said to Mrs. Hearn. And those girls have never been used to anything like real economy. What's to become of them I don't know;\" and Mrs. Boyce, as she expressed her sympathy for her dear friends, received considerable comfort from the prospect of their future poverty. It always is so, and Mrs. Boyce was not worse than her neighbours.\n\n\"You'll find they'll make it up before the time comes,\" said Mr. Boyce, to whom the excitement of such a change in affairs was almost too good to be true.\n\n\"I am afraid not,\" said Mrs. Boyce; \"I'm afraid not. They are both so determined. I always thought that riding and giving the girls hats and habits was injurious. It was treating them as though they were the squire's daughters, and they were not the squire's daughters.\"\n\n\"It was almost the same thing.\"\n\n\"But now we see the difference,\" said the judicious Mrs. Boyce. \"I often said that dear Mrs. Dale was wrong, and it turns out that I was right. It will make no difference to me, as regards calling on them and that sort of thing.\"\n\n\"Of course it won't.\"\n\n\"Not but what there must be a difference, and a very great difference too. It will be a terrible come down for poor Lily, with the loss of her fine husband and all.\"\n\nAfter dinner, when Mr. Boyce had again gone forth upon his labours, the same subject was discussed between Mrs. Boyce and her daughters, and the mother was very careful to teach her children that Mrs. Dale would be just as good a person as ever she had been, and quite as much a lady, even though she should live in a very dingy house at Guestwick; from which lesson the Boyce girls learned plainly that Mrs. Dale, with Bell and Lily, were about to have a fall in the world, and that they were to be treated accordingly.\n\nFrom all this, it will be discovered that Mrs. Dale had not given way to the squire's arguments, although she had found herself unable to answer them. As she had returned home she had felt herself to be almost vanquished, and had spoken to the girls with the air and tone of a woman who hardly knew in which course lay the line of her duty. But they had not seen the squire's manner on the occasion, nor heard his words, and they could not understand that their own purpose should be abandoned because he did not like it. So they talked their mother into fresh resolves, and on the following morning she wrote a note to her brother-in-law, assuring him that she had thought much of all that he had said, but again declaring that she regarded herself as bound in duty to leave the Small House. To this he had returned no answer, and she had communicated her intention to Mrs. Hearn, thinking it better that there should be no secret in the matter.\n\n\"I am sorry to hear that your sister-in-law is going to leave us,\" Mr. Boyce said to the squire that same afternoon.\n\n\"Who told you that?\" asked the squire, showing by his tone that he by no means liked the topic of conversation which the parson had chosen.\n\n\"Well, I had it from Mrs. Boyce, and I think Mrs. Hearn told her.\"\n\n\"I wish Mrs. Hearn would mind her own business, and not spread idle reports.\"\n\nThe squire said nothing more, and Mr. Boyce felt that he had been very unjustly snubbed.\n\nDr. Crofts had come over and pronounced as a fact that it was scarlatina. Village apothecaries are generally wronged by the doubts which are thrown upon them, for the town doctors when they come always confirm what the village apothecaries have said.\n\n\"There can be no doubt as to its being scarlatina,\" the doctor declared; \"but the symptoms are all favourable.\"\n\nThere was, however, much worse coming than this. Two days afterwards Lily found herself to be rather unwell. She endeavoured to keep it to herself, fearing that she should be brought under the doctor's notice as a patient; but her efforts were unavailing, and on the following morning it was known that she had also taken the disease. Dr. Crofts declared that everything was in her favour. The weather was cold. The presence of the malady in the house had caused them all to be careful, and, moreover, good advice was at hand at once. The doctor begged Mrs. Dale not to be uneasy, but he was very eager in begging that the two sisters might not be allowed to be together. \"Could you not send Bell into Guestwick,--to Mrs. Eames's?\" said he. But Bell did not choose to be sent to Mrs. Eames's, and was with great difficulty kept out of her mother's bedroom, to which Lily as an invalid was transferred.\n\n\"If you will allow me to say so,\" he said to Bell, on the second day after Lily's complaint had declared itself, \"you are wrong to stay here in the house.\"\n\n\"I certainly shall not leave mamma, when she has got so much upon her hands,\" said Bell.\n\n\"But if you should be taken ill she would have more on her hands,\" pleaded the doctor.\n\n\"I could not do it,\" Bell replied. \"If I were taken over to Guestwick, I should be so uneasy that I should walk back to Allington the first moment that I could escape from the house.\"\n\n\"I think your mother would be more comfortable without you.\"\n\n\"And I think she would be more comfortable with me. I don't ever like to hear of a woman running away from illness; but when a sister or a daughter does so, it is intolerable.\" So Bell remained, without permission indeed to see her sister, but performing various outside administrations which were much needed.\n\nAnd thus all manner of trouble came upon the inhabitants of the Small House, falling upon them as it were in a heap together. It was as yet barely two months since those terrible tidings had come respecting Crosbie; tidings which, it was felt at the time, would of themselves be sufficient to crush them; and now to that misfortune other misfortunes had been added,--one quick upon the heels of another. In the teeth of the doctor's kind prophecy Lily became very ill, and after a few days was delirious. She would talk to her mother about Crosbie, speaking of him as she used to speak in the autumn that was passed. But even in her madness she remembered that they had resolved to leave their present home; and she asked the doctor twice whether their lodgings in Guestwick were ready for them.\n\nIt was thus that Crofts first heard of their intention. Now, in these days of Lily's worst illness, he came daily over to Allington, remaining there, on one occasion, the whole night. For all this he would take no fee;--nor had he ever taken a fee from Mrs. Dale. \"I wish you would not come so often,\" Bell said to him one evening, as he stood with her at the drawing-room fire, after he had left the patient's room; \"you are overloading us with obligations.\" On that day Lily was over the worst of the fever, and he had been able to tell Mrs. Dale that he did not think that she was now in danger.\n\n\"It will not be necessary much longer,\" he said; \"the worst of it is over.\"\n\n\"It is such a luxury to hear you say so. I suppose we shall owe her life to you; but nevertheless--\"\n\n\"Oh, no; scarlatina is not such a terrible thing now as it used to be.\"\n\n\"Then why should you have devoted your time to her as you have done? It frightens me when I think of the injury we must have done you.\"\n\n\"My horse has felt it more than I have,\" said the doctor, laughing. \"My patients at Guestwick are not so very numerous.\" Then, instead of going, he sat himself down. \"And it is really true,\" he said, \"that you are all going to leave this house?\"\n\n\"Quite true. We shall do so at the end of March, if Lily is well enough to be moved.\"\n\n\"Lily will be well long before that, I hope; not, indeed, that she ought to be moved out of her own rooms for many weeks to come yet.\"\n\n\"Unless we are stopped by her we shall certainly go at the end of March.\" Bell now had also sat down, and they both remained for some time looking at the fire in silence.\n\n\"And why is it, Bell?\" he said, at last. \"But I don't know whether I have a right to ask.\"\n\n\"You have a right to ask any question about us,\" she said. \"My uncle is very kind. He is more than kind; he is generous. But he seems to think that our living here gives him a right to interfere with mamma. We don't like that, and, therefore, we are going.\"\n\nThe doctor still sat on one side of the fire, and Bell still sat opposite to him; but the conversation did not form itself very freely between them. \"It is bad news,\" he said, at last.\n\n\"At any rate, when we are ill you will not have so far to come and see us.\"\n\n\"Yes, I understand. That means that I am ungracious not to congratulate myself on having you all so much nearer to me; but I do not in the least. I cannot bear to think of you as living anywhere but here at Allington. Dales will be out of their place in a street at Guestwick.\"\n\n\"That's hard upon the Dales, too.\"\n\n\"It is hard upon them. It's a sort of offshoot from that very tyrannical law of noblesse oblige. I don't think you ought to go away from Allington, unless the circumstances are very imperative.\"\n\n\"But they are very imperative.\"\n\n\"In that case, indeed!\" And then again he fell into silence.\n\n\"Have you never seen that mamma is not happy here?\" she said, after another pause. \"For myself, I never quite understood it all before as I do now; but now I see it.\"\n\n\"And I have seen it;--have seen at least what you mean. She has led a life of restraint; but then, how frequently is such restraint the necessity of a life? I hardly think that your mother would move on that account.\"\n\n\"No. It is on our account. But this restraint, as you call it, makes us unhappy, and she is governed by seeing that. My uncle is generous to her as regards money; but in other things,--in matters of feeling,--I think he has been ungenerous.\"\n\n\"Bell,\" said the doctor; and then he paused.\n\nShe looked up at him, but made no answer. He had always called her by her Christian name, and they two had ever regarded each other as close friends. At the present moment she had forgotten all else besides this, and yet she had infinite pleasure in sitting there and talking to him.\n\n\"I am going to ask you a question which perhaps I ought not to ask, only that I have known you so long that I almost feel that I am speaking to a sister.\"\n\n\"You may ask me what you please,\" said she.\n\n\"It is about your cousin Bernard.\"\n\n\"About Bernard!\" said Bell.\n\nIt was now dusk; and as they were sitting without other light than that of the fire, she knew that he could not discern the colour which covered her face as her cousin's name was mentioned. But, had the light of day pervaded the whole room, I doubt whether Crofts would have seen that blush, for he kept his eyes firmly fixed upon the fire.\n\n\"Yes, about Bernard. I don't know whether I ought to ask you.\"\n\n\"I'm sure I can't say,\" said Bell, speaking words of the nature of which she was not conscious.\n\n\"There has been a rumour in Guestwick that he and you--\"\n\n\"It is untrue,\" said Bell; \"quite untrue. If you hear it repeated, you should contradict it. I wonder why people should say such things.\"\n\n\"It would have been an excellent marriage;--all your friends must have approved it.\"\n\n\"What do you mean, Dr. Crofts? How I do hate those words, 'an excellent marriage.' In them is contained more of wicked worldliness than any other words that one ever hears spoken. You want me to marry my cousin simply because I should have a great house to live in, and a coach. I know that you are my friend; but I hate such friendship as that.\"\n\n\"I think you misunderstand me, Bell. I mean that it would have been an excellent marriage, provided you had both loved each other.\"\n\n\"No, I don't misunderstand you. Of course it would be an excellent marriage, if we loved each other. You might say the same if I loved the butcher or the baker. What you mean is, that it makes a reason for loving him.\"\n\n\"I don't think I did mean that.\"\n\n\"Then you mean nothing.\"\n\nAfter that, there were again some minutes of silence during which Dr. Crofts got up to go away. \"You have scolded me very dreadfully,\" he said, with a slight smile, \"and I believe I have deserved it for interfering--\"\n\n\"No; not at all for interfering.\"\n\n\"But at any rate you must forgive me before I go.\"\n\n\"I won't forgive you at all, unless you repent of your sins, and alter altogether the wickedness of your mind. You will become very soon as bad as Dr. Gruffen.\"\n\n\"Shall I?\"\n\n\"Oh, but I will forgive you; for after all, you are the most generous man in the world.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes; of course I am. Well,--good-by.\"\n\n\"But, Dr. Crofts, you should not suppose others to be so much more worldly than yourself. You do not care for money so very much--\"\n\n\"But I do care very much.\"\n\n\"If you did, you would not come here for nothing day after day.\"\n\n\"I do care for money very much. I have sometimes nearly broken my heart because I could not get opportunities of earning it. It is the best friend that a man can have--\"\n\n\"Oh, Dr. Crofts!\"\n\n\"--the best friend that a man can have, if it be honestly come by. A woman can hardly realize the sorrow which may fall upon a man from the want of such a friend.\"\n\n\"Of course a man likes to earn a decent living by his profession; and you can do that.\"\n\n\"That depends upon one's ideas of decency.\"\n\n\"Ah! mine never ran very high. I've always had a sort of aptitude for living in a pigsty;--a clean pigsty, you know, with nice fresh bean straw to lie upon. I think it was a mistake when they made a lady of me. I do, indeed.\"\n\n\"I do not,\" said Dr. Crofts.\n\n\"That's because you don't quite know me yet. I've not the slightest pleasure in putting on three different dresses a day. I do it very often because it comes to me to do it, from the way in which we have been taught to live. But when we get to Guestwick I mean to change all that; and if you come in to tea, you'll see me in the same brown frock that I wear in the morning,--unless, indeed, the morning work makes the brown frock dirty. Oh, Dr. Crofts! you'll have it pitch-dark riding home under the Guestwick elms.\"\n\n\"I don't mind the dark,\" he said; and it seemed as though he hardly intended to go even yet.\n\n\"But I do,\" said Bell, \"and I shall ring for candles.\" But he stopped her as she put her hand out to the bell-pull.\n\n\"Stop a moment, Bell. You need hardly have the candles before I go, and you need not begrudge my staying either, seeing that I shall be all alone at home.\"\n\n\"Begrudge your staying!\"\n\n\"But, however, you shall begrudge it, or else make me very welcome.\" He still held her by the wrist, which he had caught as he prevented her from summoning the servant.\n\n\"What do you mean?\" said she. \"You know you are welcome to us as flowers in May. You always were welcome; but now, when you have come to us in our trouble-- At any rate, you shall never say that I turn you out.\"\n\n\"Shall I never say so?\" And still he held her by the wrist. He had kept his chair throughout, but she was standing before him,--between him and the fire. But she, though he held her in this way, thought little of his words, or of his action. They had known each other with great intimacy, and though Lily would still laugh at her, saying that Dr. Crofts was her lover, she had long since taught herself that no such feeling as that would ever exist between them.\n\n\"Shall I never say so, Bell? What if so poor a man as I ask for the hand that you will not give to so rich a man as your cousin Bernard?\"\n\nShe instantly withdrew her arm and moved back very quickly a step or two across the rug. She did it almost with the motion which she might have used had he insulted her; or had a man spoken such words who would not, under any circumstances, have a right to speak them.\n\n\"Ah, yes! I thought it would be so,\" he said. \"I may go now, and may know that I have been turned out.\"\n\n\"What is it you mean, Dr. Crofts? What is it you are saying? Why do you talk that nonsense, trying to see if you can provoke me?\"\n\n\"Yes; it is nonsense. I have no right to address you in that way, and certainly should not have done it now that I am in your house in the way of my profession. I beg your pardon.\" Now he also was standing, but he had not moved from his side of the fireplace. \"Are you going to forgive me before I go?\"\n\n\"Forgive you for what?\" said she.\n\n\"For daring to love you; for having loved you almost as long as you can remember; for loving you better than all beside. This alone you should forgive; but will you forgive me for having told it?\"\n\nHe had made her no offer, nor did she expect that he was about to make one. She herself had hardly yet realized the meaning of his words, and she certainly had asked herself no question as to the answer which she should give to them. There are cases in which lovers present themselves in so unmistakeable a guise, that the first word of open love uttered by them tells their whole story, and tells it without the possibility of a surprise. And it is generally so when the lover has not been an old friend, when even his acquaintance has been of modern date. It had been so essentially in the case of Crosbie and Lily Dale. When Crosbie came to Lily and made his offer, he did it with perfect ease and thorough self-possession, for he almost knew that it was expected. And Lily, though she had been flurried for a moment, had her answer pat enough. She already loved the man with all her heart, delighted in his presence, basked in the sunshine of his manliness, rejoiced in his wit, and had tuned her ears to the tone of his voice. It had all been done, and the world expected it. Had he not made his offer, Lily would have been ill-treated;--though, alas, alas, there was future ill-treatment, so much heavier, in store for her! But there are other cases in which a lover cannot make himself known as such without great difficulty, and when he does do so, cannot hope for an immediate answer in his favour. It is hard upon old friends that this difficulty should usually fall the heaviest upon them. Crofts had been so intimate with the Dale family that very many persons had thought it probable that he would marry one of the girls. Mrs. Dale herself had thought so, and had almost hoped it. Lily had certainly done both. These thoughts and hopes had somewhat faded away, but yet their former existence should have been in the doctor's favour. But now, when he had in some way spoken out, Bell started back from him and would not believe that he was in earnest. She probably loved him better than any man in the world, and yet, when he spoke to her of love, she could not bring herself to understand him.\n\n\"I don't know what you mean, Dr. Crofts; indeed I do not,\" she said.\n\n\"I had meant to ask you to be my wife; simply that. But you shall not have the pain of making me a positive refusal. As I rode here to-day I thought of it. During my frequent rides of late I have thought of little else. But I told myself that I had no right to do it. I have not even a house in which it would be fit that you should live.\"\n\n\"Dr. Crofts, if I loved you,--if I wished to marry you--\" and then she stopped herself.\n\n\"But you do not?\"\n\n\"No; I think not. I suppose not. No. But in any way no consideration about money has anything to do with it.\"\n\n\"But I am not that butcher or that baker whom you could love?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Bell; and then she stopped herself from further speech, not as intending to convey all her answer in that one word, but as not knowing how to fashion any further words.\n\n\"I knew it would be so,\" said the doctor.\n\nIt will, I fear, be thought by those who condescend to criticize this lover's conduct and his mode of carrying on his suit, that he was very unfit for such work. Ladies will say that he wanted courage, and men will say that he wanted wit. I am inclined, however, to believe that he behaved as well as men generally do behave on such occasions, and that he showed himself to be a good average lover. There is your bold lover, who knocks his lady-love over as he does a bird, and who would anathematize himself all over, and swear that his gun was distraught, and look about as though he thought the world was coming to an end, if he missed to knock over his bird. And there is your timid lover, who winks his eyes when he fires, who has felt certain from the moment in which he buttoned on his knickerbockers that he at any rate would kill nothing, and who, when he hears the loud congratulations of his friends, cannot believe that he really did bag that beautiful winged thing by his own prowess. The beautiful winged thing which the timid man carries home in his bosom, declining to have it thrown into a miscellaneous cart, so that it may never be lost in a common crowd of game, is better to him than are the slaughtered hecatombs to those who kill their birds by the hundred.\n\nBut Dr. Crofts had so winked his eye, that he was not in the least aware whether he had winged his bird or no. Indeed, having no one at hand to congratulate him, he was quite sure that the bird had flown away uninjured into the next field. \"No\" was the only word which Bell had given in answer to his last sidelong question, and No is not a comfortable word to lovers. But there had been that in Bell's No which might have taught him that the bird was not escaping without a wound, if he had still had any of his wits about him.\n\n\"Now I will go,\" said he. Then he paused for an answer, but none came. \"And you will understand what I meant when I spoke of being turned out.\"\n\n\"Nobody--turns you out.\" And Bell, as she spoke, had almost descended to a sob.\n\n\"It is time, at any rate, that I should go; is it not? And, Bell, don't suppose that this little scene will keep me away from your sister's bedside. I shall be here to-morrow, and you will find that you will hardly know me again for the same person.\" Then in the dark he put out his hand to her.\n\n\"Good-by,\" she said, giving him her hand. He pressed hers very closely, but she, though she wished to do so, could not bring herself to return the pressure. Her hand remained passive in his, showing no sign of offence; but it was absolutely passive.\n\n\"Good-by, dearest friend,\" he said.\n\n\"Good-by,\" she answered,--and then he was gone.\n\nShe waited quite still till she heard the front-door close after him, and then she crept silently up to her own bedroom, and sat herself down in a low rocking-chair over the fire. It was in accordance with a custom already established that her mother should remain with Lily till the tea was ready downstairs; for in these days of illness such dinners as were provided were eaten early. Bell, therefore, knew that she had still some half-hour of her own, during which she might sit and think undisturbed.\n\nAnd what naturally should have been her first thoughts?--that she had ruthlessly refused a man who, as she now knew, loved her well, and for whom she had always felt at any rate the warmest friendship? Such were not her thoughts, nor were they in any way akin to this. They ran back instantly to years gone by,--over long years, as her few years were counted,--and settled themselves on certain halcyon days, in which she had dreamed that he had loved her, and had fancied that she had loved him. How she had schooled herself for those days since that, and taught herself to know that her thoughts had been over-bold! And now it had all come round. The only man that she had ever liked had loved her. Then there came to her a memory of a certain day, in which she had been almost proud to think that Crosbie had admired her, in which she had almost hoped that it might be so; and as she thought of this she blushed, and struck her foot twice upon the floor. \"Dear Lily,\" she said to herself--\"poor Lily!\" But the feeling which induced her then to think of her sister had had no relation to that which had first brought Crosbie into her mind.\n\nAnd this man had loved her through it all,--this priceless, peerless man,--this man who was as true to the backbone as that other man had shown himself to be false; who was as sound as the other man had proved himself to be rotten. A smile came across her face as she sat looking at the fire, thinking of this. A man had loved her, whose love was worth possessing. She hardly remembered whether or no she had refused him or accepted him. She hardly asked herself what she would do. As to all that it was necessary that she should have many thoughts, but the necessity did not press upon her quite immediately. For the present, at any rate, she might sit and triumph;--and thus triumphant she sat there till the old nurse came in and told her that her mother was waiting for her below.\n\n# CHAPTER XL. PREPARATIONS FOR THE WEDDING.\n\nThe fourteenth of February was finally settled as the day on which Mr. Crosbie was to be made the happiest of men. A later day had been at first named, the twenty-seventh or twenty-eighth having been suggested as an improvement over the first week in March; but Lady Amelia had been frightened by Crosbie's behaviour on that Sunday evening, and had made the countess understand that there should be no unnecessary delay. \"He doesn't scruple at that kind of thing,\" Lady Amelia had said in one of her letters, showing perhaps less trust in the potency of her own rank than might have been expected from her. The countess, however, had agreed with her, and when Crosbie received from his mother-in-law a very affectionate epistle, setting forth all the reasons which would make the fourteenth so much more convenient a day than the twenty-eighth, he was unable to invent an excuse for not being made happy a fortnight earlier than the time named in the bargain. His first impulse had been against yielding, arising from some feeling which made him think that more than the bargain ought not to be exacted. But what was the use to him of quarrelling? What the use, at least, of quarrelling just then? He believed that he could more easily enfranchise himself from the De Courcy tyranny when he should be once married than he could do now. When Lady Alexandrina should be his own he would let her know that he intended to be her master. If in doing so it would be necessary that he should divide himself altogether from the De Courcys, such division should be made. At the present moment he would yield to them, at any rate in this matter. And so the fourteenth of February was fixed for the marriage.\n\nIn the second week in January Alexandrina came up to look after her things; or, in more noble language, to fit herself with becoming bridal appanages. As she could not properly do all this work alone, or even under the surveillance and with the assistance of a sister, Lady De Courcy was to come up also. But Alexandrina came first, remaining with her sister in St. John's Wood till the countess should arrive. The countess had never yet condescended to accept of her son-in-law's hospitality, but always went to the cold, comfortless house in Portman Square,--the house which had been the De Courcy town family mansion for many years, and which the countess would long since have willingly exchanged for some abode on the other side of Oxford Street; but the earl had been obdurate; his clubs and certain lodgings which he had occasionally been wont to occupy, were on the right side of Oxford Street; why should he change his old family residence? So the countess was coming up to Portman Square, not having been even asked on this occasion to St. John's Wood.\n\n\"Don't you think we'd better,\" Mr. Gazebee had said to his wife, almost trembling at the renewal of his own proposition.\n\n\"I think not, my dear,\" Lady Amelia had answered. \"Mamma is not very particular; but there are little things, you know--\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, of course,\" said Mr. Gazebee; and then the conversation had been dropped. He would most willingly have entertained his august mother-in-law during her visit to the metropolis, and yet her presence in his house would have made him miserable as long as she remained there.\n\nBut for a week Alexandrina sojourned under Mr. Gazebee's roof, during which time Crosbie was made happy with all the delights of an expectant bridegroom. Of course he was given to understand that he was to dine at the Gazebees' every day, and spend all his evenings there; and, under the circumstances, he had no excuse for not doing so. Indeed, at the present moment, his hours would otherwise have hung heavily enough upon his hands. In spite of his bold resolution with reference to his eye, and his intention not to be debarred from the pleasures of society by the marks of the late combat, he had not, since that occurrence, frequented his club very closely; and though London was now again becoming fairly full, he did not find himself going out so much as had been his wont. The brilliance of his coming marriage did not seem to have added much to his popularity; in fact, the world,--his world,--was beginning to look coldly at him. Therefore that daily attendance at St. John's Wood was not felt to be so irksome as might have been expected.\n\nA residence had been taken for the couple in a very fashionable row of buildings abutting upon the Bayswater Road, called Princess Royal Crescent. The house was quite new, and the street being unfinished had about it a strong smell of mortar, and a general aspect of builders' poles and brickbats; but nevertheless, it was acknowledged to be a quite correct locality. From one end of the crescent a corner of Hyde Park could be seen, and the other abutted on a very handsome terrace indeed, in which lived an ambassador,--from South America,--a few bankers' senior clerks, and a peer of the realm. We know how vile is the sound of Baker Street, and how absolutely foul to the polite ear is the name of Fitzroy Square. The houses, however, in those purlieus are substantial, warm, and of good size. The house in Princess Royal Crescent was certainly not substantial, for in these days substantially-built houses do not pay. It could hardly have been warm, for, to speak the truth, it was even yet not finished throughout; and as for the size, though the drawing-room was a noble apartment, consisting of a section of the whole house, with a corner cut out for the staircase, it was very much cramped in its other parts, and was made like a cherub, in this respect, that it had no rear belonging to it. \"But if you have no private fortune of your own, you cannot have everything,\" as the countess observed when Crosbie objected to the house because a closet under the kitchen-stairs was to be assigned to him as his own dressing-room.\n\nWhen the question of the house was first debated, Lady Amelia had been anxious that St. John's Wood should be selected as the site, but to this Crosbie had positively objected.\n\n\"I think you don't like St. John's Wood,\" Lady Amelia had said to him somewhat sternly, thinking to awe him into a declaration that he entertained no general enmity to the neighbourhood. But Crosbie was not weak enough for this.\n\n\"No; I do not,\" he said. \"I have always disliked it. It amounts to a prejudice, I daresay. But if I were made to live here I am convinced I should cut my throat in the first six months.\"\n\nLady Amelia had then drawn herself up, declaring her sorrow that her house should be so hateful to him.\n\n\"Oh, dear, no,\" said he. \"I like it very much for you, and enjoy coming here of all things. I speak only of the effect which living here myself would have upon me.\"\n\nLady Amelia was quite clever enough to understand it all; but she had her sister's interest at heart, and therefore persevered in her affectionate solicitude for her brother-in-law, giving up that point as to St. John's Wood. Crosbie himself had wished to go to one of the new Pimlico squares down near Vauxhall Bridge and the river, actuated chiefly by consideration of the enormous distance lying between that locality and the northern region in which Lady Amelia lived; but to this Lady Alexandrina had objected strongly. If, indeed, they could have achieved Eaton Square, or a street leading out of Eaton Square,--if they could have crept on to the hem of the skirt of Belgravia,--the bride would have been delighted. And at first she was very nearly being taken in with the idea that such was the proposal made to her. Her geographical knowledge of Pimlico had not been perfect, and she had nearly fallen into a fatal error. But a friend had kindly intervened. \"For heaven's sake, my dear, don't let him take you anywhere beyond Eccleston Square!\" had been exclaimed to her in dismay by a faithful married friend. Thus warned, Alexandrina had been firm, and now their tent was to be pitched in Princess Royal Crescent, from one end of which the Hyde Park may be seen.\n\nThe furniture had been ordered chiefly under the inspection, and by the experience, of the Lady Amelia. Crosbie had satisfied himself by declaring that she at any rate could get the things cheaper than he could buy them, and that he had no taste for such employment. Nevertheless, he had felt that he was being made subject to tyranny and brought under the thumb of subjection. He could not go cordially into this matter of beds and chairs, and, therefore, at last deputed the whole matter to the De Courcy faction. And for this there was another reason, not hitherto mentioned. Mr. Mortimer Gazebee was finding the money with which all the furniture was being bought. He, with an honest but almost unintelligible zeal for the De Courcy family, had tied up every shilling on which he could lay his hand as belonging to Crosbie, in the interest of Lady Alexandrina. He had gone to work for her, scraping here and arranging there, strapping the new husband down upon the grindstone of his matrimonial settlement, as though the future bread of his, Gazebee's, own children were dependent on the validity of his legal workmanship. And for this he was not to receive a penny, or gain any advantage, immediate or ulterior. It came from his zeal,--his zeal for the coronet which Lord De Courcy wore. According to his mind an earl and an earl's belongings were entitled to such zeal. It was the theory in which he had been educated, and amounted to a worship which, unconsciously, he practised. Personally, he disliked Lord De Courcy, who ill-treated him. He knew that the earl was a heartless, cruel, bad man. But as an earl he was entitled to an amount of service which no commoner could have commanded from Mr. Gazebee. Mr. Gazebee, having thus tied up all the available funds in favour of Lady Alexandrina's seemingly expected widowhood, was himself providing the money with which the new house was to be furnished. \"You can pay me a hundred and fifty a year with four per cent. till it is liquidated,\" he had said to Crosbie; and Crosbie had assented with a grunt. Hitherto, though he had lived in London expensively, and as a man of fashion, he had never owed any one anything. He was now to begin that career of owing. But when a clerk in a public office marries an earl's daughter, he cannot expect to have everything his own way.\n\nLady Amelia had bought the ordinary furniture--the beds, the stair-carpets, the washing-stands, and the kitchen things. Gazebee had got a bargain of the dinner-table and sideboard. But Lady Alexandrina herself was to come up with reference to the appurtenances of the drawing-room. It was with reference to matters of costume that the countess intended to lend her assistance--matters of costume as to which the bill could not be sent in to Gazebee, and be paid for by him with five per cent. duly charged against the bridegroom. The bridal trousseau must be produced by De Courcy's means, and, therefore, it was necessary that the countess herself should come upon the scene. \"I will have no bills, d'ye hear?\" snarled the earl, gnashing and snapping upon his words with one specially ugly black tooth. \"I won't have any bills about this affair.\" And yet he made no offer of ready money. It was very necessary under such circumstances that the countess herself should come upon the scene. An ambiguous hint had been conveyed to Mr. Gazebee, during a visit of business which he had lately made to Courcy Castle, that the milliner's bills might as well be pinned on to those of the furniture-makers, the crockery-mongers, and the like. The countess, putting it in her own way, had gently suggested that the fashion of the thing had changed lately, and that such an arrangement was considered to be the proper thing among people who lived really in the world. But Gazebee was a clear-headed, honest man; and he knew the countess. He did not think that such an arrangement could be made on the present occasion. Whereupon the countess pushed her suggestion no further, but made up her mind that she must come up to London herself.\n\nIt was pleasant to see the Ladies Amelia and Alexandrina, as they sat within a vast emporium of carpets in Bond Street, asking questions of the four men who were waiting upon them, putting their heads together and whispering, calculating accurately as to extra twopences a yard, and occasioning as much trouble as it was possible for them to give. It was pleasant because they managed their large hoops cleverly among the huge rolls of carpets, because they were enjoying themselves thoroughly, and taking to themselves the homage of the men as clearly their due. But it was not so pleasant to look at Crosbie, who was fidgeting to get away to his office, to whom no power of choosing in the matter was really given, and whom the men regarded as being altogether supernumerary. The ladies had promised to be at the shop by half-past ten, so that Crosbie should reach his office at eleven--or a little after. But it was nearly eleven before they left the Gazebee residence, and it was very evident that half-an-hour among the carpets would be by no means sufficient. It seemed as though miles upon miles of gorgeous colouring were unrolled before them; and then when any pattern was regarded as at all practicable, it was unrolled backwards and forwards till a room was nearly covered by it. Crosbie felt for the men who were hauling about the huge heaps of material; but Lady Amelia sat as composed as though it were her duty to inspect every yard of stuff in the warehouse. \"I think we'll look at that one at the bottom again.\" Then the men went to work and removed a mountain. \"No, my dear, that green in the scroll-work won't do. It would fly directly, if any hot water were spilt.\" The man smiling ineffably, declared that that particular green never flew anywhere. But Lady Amelia paid no attention to him, and the carpet for which the mountain had been removed became part of another mountain.\n\n\"That might do,\" said Alexandrina, gazing upon a magnificent crimson ground through which rivers of yellow meandered, carrying with them in their streams an infinity of blue flowers. And as she spoke she held her head gracefully on one side, and looked down upon the carpet doubtingly. Lady Amelia poked it with her parasol as though to test its durability, and whispered something about yellows showing the dirt. Crosbie took out his watch and groaned.\n\n\"It's a superb carpet, my lady, and about the newest thing we have. We put down four hundred and fifty yards of it for the Duchess of South Wales, at Cwddglwlch Castle, only last month. Nobody has had it since, for it has not been in stock.\" Whereupon Lady Amelia again poked it, and then got up and walked upon it. Lady Alexandrina held her head a little more on one side.\n\n\"Five and three?\" said Lady Amelia.\n\n\"Oh, no, my lady; five and seven; and the cheapest carpet we have in the house. There is twopence a yard more in the colour; there is, indeed.\"\n\n\"And the discount?\" asked Lady Amelia.\n\n\"Two and a half, my lady.\"\n\n\"Oh dear, no,\" said Lady Amelia. \"I always have five per cent. for immediate payment--quite immediate, you know.\" Upon which the man declared the question must be referred to his master. Two and a half was the rule of the house. Crosbie, who had been looking out of the window, said that upon his honour he couldn't wait any longer.\n\n\"And what do you think of it, Adolphus?\" asked Alexandrina.\n\n\"Think of what?\"\n\n\"Of the carpet--this one, you know!\"\n\n\"Oh--what do I think of the carpet? I don't think I quite like all these yellow bands; and isn't it too red? I should have thought something brown with a small pattern would have been better. But, upon my word, I don't much care.\"\n\n\"Of course he doesn't,\" said Lady Amelia. Then the two ladies put their heads together for another five minutes, and the carpet was chosen--subject to that question of the discount. \"And now about the rug,\" said Lady Amelia. But here Crosbie rebelled, and insisted that he must leave them and go to his office. \"You can't want me about the rug,\" he said. \"Well, perhaps not,\" said Lady Amelia. But it was manifest that Alexandrina did not approve of being thus left by her male attendant.\n\nThe same thing happened in Oxford Street with reference to the chairs and sofas, and Crosbie began to wish that he were settled, even though he should have to dress himself in the closet below the kitchen-stairs. He was learning to hate the whole household in St. John's Wood, and almost all that belonged to it. He was introduced there to little family economies of which hitherto he had known nothing, and which were disgusting to him, and the necessity for which was especially explained to him. It was to men placed as he was about to place himself that these economies were so vitally essential--to men who with limited means had to maintain a decorous outward face towards the fashionable world. Ample supplies of butchers' meat and unlimited washing-bills might be very well upon fifteen hundred a year to those who went out but seldom, and who could use the first cab that came to hand when they did go out. But there were certain things that Lady Alexandrina must do, and therefore the strictest household economy became necessary. Would Lily Dale have required the use of a carriage, got up to look as though it were private, at the expense of her husband's beefsteaks and clean shirts? That question and others of that nature were asked by Crosbie within his own mind, not unfrequently.\n\nBut, nevertheless, he tried to love Alexandrina, or rather to persuade himself that he loved her. If he could only get her away from the De Courcy faction, and especially from the Gazebee branch of it, he would break her of all that. He would teach her to sit triumphantly in a street cab, and to cater for her table with a plentiful hand. Teach her!--at some age over thirty; and with such careful training as she had already received! Did he intend to forbid her ever again to see her relations, ever to go to St. John's Wood, or to correspond with the countess and Lady Margaretta? Teach her, indeed! Had he yet to learn that he could not wash a blackamoor white?--that he could not have done so even had he himself been well adapted for the attempt, whereas he was in truth nearly as ill adapted as a man might be? But who could pity him? Lily, whom he might have had in his bosom, would have been no blackamoor.\n\nThen came the time of Lady De Courcy's visit to town, and Alexandrina moved herself off to Portman Square. There was some apparent comfort in this to Crosbie, for he would thereby be saved from those daily dreary journeys up to the north-west. I may say that he positively hated that windy corner near the church, round which he had to walk in getting to the Gazebee residence, and that he hated the lamp which guided him to the door, and the very door itself. This door stood buried as it were in a wall, and opened on to a narrow passage which ran across a so-called garden, or front yard, containing on each side two iron receptacles for geraniums, painted to look like Palissy ware, and a naked female on a pedestal. No spot in London was, as he thought, so cold as the bit of pavement immediately in front of that door. And there he would be kept five, ten, fifteen minutes, as he declared--though I believe in my heart that the time never exceeded three,--while Richard was putting off the trappings of his work and putting on the trappings of his grandeur.\n\nIf people would only have their doors opened to you by such assistance as may come most easily and naturally to the work! I stood lately for some minutes on a Tuesday afternoon at a gallant portal, and as I waxed impatient a pretty maiden came and opened it. She was a pretty maiden, though her hands and face and apron told tales of the fire-grates. \"Laws, sir,\" she said, \"the visitors' day is Wednesday; and if you would come then, there would be the man in livery!\" She took my card with the corner of her apron, and did just as well as the man in livery; but what would have happened to her had her little speech been overheard by her mistress?\n\nCrosbie hated the house in St. John's Wood, and therefore the coming of the countess was a relief to him. Portman Square was easily to be reached, and the hospitalities of the countess would not be pressed upon him so strongly as those of the Gazebees. When he first called he was shown into the great family dining-room, which looked out towards the back of the house. The front windows were, of course, closed, as the family was not supposed to be in London. Here he remained in the room for some quarter of an hour, and then the countess descended upon him in all her grandeur. Perhaps he had never before seen her so grand. Her dress was very large, and rustled through the broad doorway, as if demanding even a broader passage. She had on a wonder of a bonnet, and a velvet mantle that was nearly as expansive as her petticoats. She threw her head a little back as she accosted him, and he instantly perceived that he was enveloped in the fumes of an affectionate but somewhat contemptuous patronage. In old days he had liked the countess, because her manner to him had always been flattering. In his intercourse with her he had been able to feel that he gave quite as much as he got, and that the countess was aware of the fact. In all the circumstances of their acquaintance the ascendancy had been with him, and therefore the acquaintance had been a pleasant one. The countess had been a good-natured, agreeable woman, whose rank and position had made her house pleasant to him; and therefore he had consented to shine upon her with such light as he had to give. Why was it that the matter was reversed, now that there was so much stronger a cause for good feeling between them? He knew that there was such change, and with bitter internal upbraidings he acknowledged to himself that this woman was getting the mastery over him. As the friend of the countess he had been a great man in her eyes;--in all her little words and looks she had acknowledged his power; but now, as her son-in-law, he was to become a very little man,--such as was Mortimer Gazebee!\n\n\"My dear Adolphus,\" she said, taking both his hands, \"the day is coming very near now; is it not?\"\n\n\"Very near, indeed,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes, it is very near. I hope you feel yourself a happy man.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, that's of course.\"\n\n\"It ought to be. Speaking very seriously, I mean that it ought to be a matter of course. She is everything that a man should desire in a wife. I am not alluding now to her rank, though of course you feel what a great advantage she gives you in this respect.\"\n\nCrosbie muttered something as to his consciousness of having drawn a prize in the lottery; but he so muttered it as not to convey to the lady's ears a proper sense of his dependent gratitude. \"I know of no man more fortunate than you have been,\" she continued; \"and I hope that my dear girl will find that you are fully aware that it is so. I think that she is looking rather fagged. You have allowed her to do more than was good for her in the way of shopping.\"\n\n\"She has done a good deal, certainly,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"She is so little used to anything of that kind! But of course, as things have turned out, it was necessary that she should see to these things herself.\"\n\n\"I rather think she liked it,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"I believe she will always like doing her duty. We are just going now to Madame Millefranc's, to see some silks;--perhaps you would wish to go with us?\"\n\nJust at this moment Alexandrina came into the room, and looked as though she were in all respects a smaller edition of her mother. They were both well-grown women, with handsome, large figures, and a certain air about them which answered almost for beauty. As to the countess, her face, on close inspection, bore, as it was entitled to do, deep signs of age; but she so managed her face that any such close inspection was never made; and her general appearance for her time of life was certainly good. Very little more than this could be said in favour of her daughter.\n\n\"Oh dear, no, mamma,\" she said, having heard her mother's last words. \"He's the worst person in a shop in the world. He likes nothing, and dislikes nothing. Do you, Adolphus?\"\n\n\"Indeed I do. I like all the cheap things, and dislike all the dear things.\"\n\n\"Then you certainly shall not go with us to Madame Millefranc's,\" said Alexandrina.\n\n\"It would not matter to him there, you know, my dear,\" said the countess, thinking perhaps of the suggestion she had lately made to Mr. Gazebee.\n\nOn this occasion Crosbie managed to escape, simply promising to return to Portman Square in the evening after dinner. \"By-the-by, Adolphus,\" said the countess, as he handed her into the hired carriage which stood at the door, \"I wish you would go to Lambert's, on Ludgate Hill, for me. He has had a bracelet of mine for nearly three months. Do, there's a good creature. Get it if you can, and bring it up this evening.\"\n\nCrosbie, as he made his way back to his office, swore that he would not do the bidding of the countess. He would not trudge off into the city after her trinkets. But at five o'clock, when he left his office, he did go there. He apologized to himself by saying that he had nothing else to do, and bethought himself that at the present moment his lady mother-in-law's smiles might be more convenient than her frowns. So he went to Lambert's, on Ludgate Hill, and there learned that the bracelet had been sent down to Courcy Castle full two months since.\n\nAfter that he dined at his club, at Sebright's. He dined alone, sitting by no means in bliss with his half-pint of sherry on the table before him. A man now and then came up and spoke to him, one a few words, and another a few, and two or three congratulated him as to his marriage; but the club was not the same thing to him as it had formerly been. He did not stand in the centre of the rug, speaking indifferently to all or any around him, ready with his joke, and loudly on the alert with the last news of the day. How easy it is to be seen when any man has fallen from his pride of place, though the altitude was ever so small, and the fall ever so slight. Where is the man who can endure such a fall without showing it in his face, in his voice, in his step, and in every motion of every limb? Crosbie knew that he had fallen, and showed that he knew it by the manner in which he ate his mutton chop.\n\nAt half-past eight he was again in Portman Square, and found the two ladies crowding over a small fire in a small back drawing-room. The furniture was all covered with brown holland, and the place had about it that cold comfortless feeling which uninhabited rooms always produce. Crosbie, as he had walked from the club up to Portman Square, had indulged in some serious thoughts. The kind of life which he had hitherto led had certainly passed away from him. He could never again be the pet of a club, or indulged as one to whom all good things were to be given without any labour at earning them on his own part. Such for some years had been his good fortune, but such could be his good fortune no longer. Was there anything within his reach which he might take in lieu of that which he had lost? He might still be victorious at his office, having more capacity for such victory than others around him. But such success alone would hardly suffice for him. Then he considered whether he might not even yet be happy in his own home,--whether Alexandrina, when separated from her mother, might not become such a wife as he could love. Nothing softens a man's feelings so much as failure, or makes him turn so anxiously to an idea of home as buffetings from those he meets abroad. He had abandoned Lily because his outer world had seemed to him too bright to be deserted. He would endeavour to supply her place with Alexandrina, because his outer world had seemed to him too harsh to be supported. Alas! alas! a man cannot so easily repent of his sins, and wash himself white from their stains!\n\nWhen he entered the room the two ladies were sitting over the fire, as I have stated, and Crosbie could immediately perceive that the spirit of the countess was not serene. In fact there had been a few words between the mother and child on that matter of the trousseau, and Alexandrina had plainly told her mother that if she were to be married at all she would be married with such garments belonging to her as were fitting for an earl's daughter. It was in vain that her mother had explained with many circumlocutional phrases, that the fitness in this respect should be accommodated rather to the plebeian husband than to the noble parent. Alexandrina had been very firm, and had insisted on her rights, giving the countess to understand that if her orders for finery were not complied with, she would return as a spinster to Courcy, and prepare herself for partnership with Rosina.\n\n\"My dear,\" said the countess, piteously, \"you can have no idea of what I shall have to go through with your father. And, of course, you could get all these things afterwards.\"\n\n\"Papa has no right to treat me in such a way. And if he would not give me any money himself, he should have let me have some of my own.\"\n\n\"Ah, my dear, that was Mr. Gazebee's fault.\"\n\n\"I don't care whose fault it was. It certainly was not mine. I won't have him to tell me\"--\"him\" was intended to signify Adolphus Crosbie--\"that he had to pay for my wedding-clothes.\"\n\n\"Of course not that, my dear.\"\n\n\"No; nor yet for the things which I wanted immediately. I'd much rather go and tell him at once that the marriage must be put off.\"\n\nAlexandrina of course carried her point, the countess reflecting with a maternal devotion equal almost to that of the pelican, that the earl could not do more than kill her. So the things were ordered as Alexandrina chose to order them, and the countess desired that the bills might be sent in to Mr. Gazebee. Much self-devotion had been displayed by the mother, but the mother thought that none had been displayed by the daughter, and therefore she had been very cross with Alexandrina.\n\nCrosbie, taking a chair, sat himself between them, and in a very good-humoured tone explained the little affair of the bracelet. \"Your ladyship's memory must have played you false,\" said he, with a smile.\n\n\"My memory is very good,\" said the countess; \"very good indeed. If Twitch got it, and didn't tell me, that was not my fault.\" Twitch was her ladyship's lady's-maid. Crosbie, seeing how the land lay, said nothing more about the bracelet.\n\nAfter a minute or two he put out his hand to take that of Alexandrina. They were to be married now in a week or two, and such a sign of love might have been allowed to him, even in the presence of the bride's mother. He did succeed in getting hold of her fingers, but found in them none of the softness of a response. \"Don't,\" said Lady Alexandrina, withdrawing her hand; and the tone of her voice as she spoke the word was not sweet to his ears. He remembered at the moment a certain scene which took place one evening at the little bridge at Allington, and Lily's voice, and Lily's words, and Lily's passion, as he caressed her: \"Oh, my love, my love, my love!\"\n\n\"My dear,\" said the countess, \"they know how tired I am. I wonder whether they are going to give us any tea.\" Whereupon Crosbie rang the bell, and, on resuming his chair, moved it a little farther away from his lady-love.\n\nPresently the tea was brought to them by the housekeeper's assistant, who did not appear to have made herself very smart for the occasion, and Crosbie thought that he was de trop. This, however, was a mistake on his part. As he had been admitted into the family, such little matters were no longer subject of care. Two or three months since, the countess would have fainted at the idea of such a domestic appearing with a tea-tray before Mr. Crosbie. Now, however, she was utterly indifferent to any such consideration. Crosbie was to be admitted into the family, thereby becoming entitled to certain privileges,--and thereby also becoming subject to certain domestic drawbacks. In Mrs. Dale's little household there had been no rising to grandeur; but then, also, there had never been any bathos of dirt. Of this also Crosbie thought as he sat with his tea in his hand.\n\nHe soon, however, got himself away. When he rose to go Alexandrina also rose, and he was permitted to press his nose against her cheekbone by way of a salute.\n\n\"Good-night, Adolphus,\" said the countess, putting out her hand to him. \"But stop a minute; I know there is something I want you to do for me. But you will look in as you go to your office to-morrow morning.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XLI. DOMESTIC TROUBLES.\n\nWhen Crosbie was making his ineffectual inquiry after Lady De Courcy's bracelet at Lambert's, John Eames was in the act of entering Mrs. Roper's front door in Burton Crescent.\n\n\"Oh, John, where's Mr. Cradell?\" were the first words which greeted him, and they were spoken by the divine Amelia. Now, in her usual practice of life, Amelia did not interest herself much as to the whereabouts of Mr. Cradell.\n\n\"Where's Cradell?\" said Eames, repeating the question. \"Upon my word, I don't know. I walked to the office with him, but I haven't seen him since. We don't sit in the same room, you know.\"\n\n\"John!\" and then she stopped.\n\n\"What's up now?\" said John.\n\n\"John! That woman's off and left her husband. As sure as your name's John Eames, that foolish fellow has gone off with her.\"\n\n\"What, Cradell? I don't believe it.\"\n\n\"She went out of this house at two o'clock in the afternoon, and has never been back since.\" That, certainly, was only four hours from the present time, and such an absence from home in the middle of the day was but weak evidence on which to charge a married woman with the great sin of running off with a lover. This Amelia felt, and therefore she went on to explain. \"He's there upstairs in the drawing-room, the very picture of disconsolateness.\"\n\n\"Who,--Cradell?\"\n\n\"Lupex is. He's been drinking a little, I'm afraid; but he's very unhappy, indeed. He had an appointment to meet his wife here at four o'clock, and when he came he found her gone. He rushed up into their room, and now he says she has broken open a box he had and taken off all his money.\"\n\n\"But he never had any money.\"\n\n\"He paid mother some the day before yesterday.\"\n\n\"That's just the reason he shouldn't have any to-day.\"\n\n\"She certainly has taken things she wouldn't have taken if she'd merely gone out shopping or anything like that, for I've been up in the room and looked about it. She'd three necklaces. They weren't much account; but she must have them all on, or else have got them in her pocket.\"\n\n\"Cradell has never gone off with her in that way. He may be a fool--\"\n\n\"Oh, he is, you know. I've never seen such a fool about a woman as he has been.\"\n\n\"But he wouldn't be a party to stealing a lot of trumpery trinkets, or taking her husband's money. Indeed, I don't think he has anything to do with it.\" Then Eames thought over the circumstances of the day, and remembered that he had certainly not seen Cradell since the morning. It was that public servant's practice to saunter into Eames's room in the middle of the day, and there consume bread and cheese and beer,--in spite of an assertion which Johnny had once made as to crumbs of biscuit bathed in ink. But on this special day he had not done so. \"I can't think he has been such a fool as that,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"But he has,\" said Amelia. \"It's dinner-time now, and where is he? Had he any money left, Johnny?\"\n\nSo interrogated, Eames disclosed a secret confided to him by his friend which no other circumstances would have succeeded in dragging from his breast.\n\n\"She borrowed twelve pounds from him about a fortnight since, immediately after quarter-day. And she owed him money, too, before that.\"\n\n\"Oh, what a soft!\" exclaimed Amelia; \"and he hasn't paid mother a shilling for the last two months!\"\n\n\"It was his money, perhaps, that Mrs. Roper got from Lupex the day before yesterday. If so, it comes to the same thing as far as she is concerned, you know.\"\n\n\"And what are we to do now?\" said Amelia, as she went before her lover upstairs. \"Oh, John, what will become of me if ever you serve me in that way? What should I do if you were to go off with another lady?\"\n\n\"Lupex hasn't gone off,\" said Eames, who hardly knew what to say when the matter was brought before him with so closely personal a reference.\n\n\"But it's the same thing,\" said Amelia. \"Hearts is divided. Hearts that have been joined together ought never to be divided; ought they?\" And then she hung upon his arm just as they got to the drawing-room door.\n\n\"Hearts and darts are all my eye,\" said Johnny. \"My belief is that a man had better never marry at all. How d'you do, Mr. Lupex? Is anything the matter?\"\n\nMr. Lupex was seated on a chair in the middle of the room, and was leaning with his head over the back of it. So despondent was he in his attitude that his head would have fallen off and rolled on to the floor, had it followed the course which its owner seemed to intend that it should take. His hands hung down also along the back legs of the chair, till his fingers almost touched the ground, and altogether his appearance was pendent, drooping, and wobegone. Miss Spruce was seated in one corner of the room, with her hands folded in her lap before her, and Mrs. Roper was standing on the rug with a look of severe virtue on her brow,--of virtue which, to judge by its appearance, was very severe. Nor was its severity intended to be exercised solely against Mrs. Lupex. Mrs. Roper was becoming very tired of Mr. Lupex also, and would not have been unhappy if he also had run away,--leaving behind him so much of his property as would have paid his bill.\n\nMr. Lupex did not stir when first addressed by John Eames, but a certain convulsive movement was to be seen on the back of his head, indicating that this new arrival in the drawing-room had produced a fresh accession of agony. The chair, too, quivered under him, and his fingers stretched themselves nearer to the ground and shook themselves.\n\n\"Mr. Lupex, we're going to dinner immediately,\" said Mrs. Roper. \"Mr. Eames, where is your friend, Mr. Cradell?\"\n\n\"Upon my word I don't know,\" said Eames.\n\n\"But I know,\" said Lupex, jumping up and standing at his full height, while he knocked down the chair which had lately supported him. \"The traitor to domestic bliss! I know. And wherever he is, he has that false woman in his arms. Would he were here!\" And as he expressed the last wish he went through a motion with his hands and arms which seemed intended to signify that if that unfortunate young man were in the company he would pull him in pieces and double him up, and pack him close, and then despatch his remains off, through infinite space, to the Prince of Darkness. \"Traitor,\" he exclaimed, as he finished the process. \"False traitor! Foul traitor! And she too!\" Then, as he thought of this softer side of the subject, he prepared himself to relapse again on to the chair. Finding it on the ground he had to pick it up. He did pick it up, and once more flung away his head over the back of it, and stretched his finger-nails almost down to the carpet.\n\n\"James,\" said Mrs. Roper to her son, who was now in the room, \"I think you'd better stay with Mr. Lupex while we are at dinner. Come, Miss Spruce, I'm very sorry that you should be annoyed by this kind of thing.\"\n\n\"It don't hurt me,\" said Miss Spruce, preparing to leave the room. \"I'm only an old woman.\"\n\n\"Annoyed!\" said Lupex, raising himself again from his chair, not perhaps altogether disposed to remain upstairs while the dinner, for which it was intended that he should some day pay, was being eaten below. \"Annoyed! It is a profound sorrow to me that any lady should be annoyed by my misfortunes. As regards Miss Spruce, I look upon her character with profound veneration.\"\n\n\"You needn't mind me; I'm only an old woman,\" said Miss Spruce.\n\n\"But, by heavens, I do mind!\" exclaimed Lupex; and hurrying forward he seized Miss Spruce by the hand. \"I shall always regard age as entitled--\" But the special privileges which Mr. Lupex would have accorded to age were never made known to the inhabitants of Mrs. Roper's boarding-house, for the door of the room was again opened at this moment, and Mr. Cradell entered.\n\n\"Here you are, old fellow, to answer for yourself,\" said Eames.\n\nCradell, who had heard something as he came in at the front door, but had not heard that Lupex was in the drawing-room, made a slight start backwards when he saw that gentleman's face. \"Upon my word and honour,\" he began;--but he was able to carry his speech no further. Lupex, dropping the hand of the elderly lady whom he reverenced, was upon him in an instant, and Cradell was shaking beneath his grasp like an aspen leaf,--or rather not like an aspen leaf, unless an aspen leaf when shaken is to be seen with its eyes shut, its mouth open, and its tongue hanging out.\n\n\"Come, I say,\" said Eames, stepping forward to his friend's assistance; \"this won't do at all, Mr. Lupex. You've been drinking. You'd better wait till to-morrow morning, and speak to Cradell then.\"\n\n\"To-morrow morning, viper,\" shouted Lupex, still holding his prey, but looking back at Eames over his shoulder. Who the viper was had not been clearly indicated. \"When will he restore to me my wife? When will he restore to me my honour?\"\n\n\"Upon-on-on-on my--\" It was for the moment in vain that poor Mr. Cradell endeavoured to asseverate his innocence, and to stake his honour upon his own purity as regarded Mrs. Lupex. Lupex still held to his enemy's cravat, though Eames had now got him by the arm, and so far impeded his movements as to hinder him from proceeding to any graver attack.\n\n\"Jemima, Jemima, Jemima!\" shouted Mrs. Roper. \"Run for the police; run for the police!\" But Amelia, who had more presence of mind than her mother, stopped Jemima as she was making to one of the front windows. \"Keep where you are,\" said Amelia. \"They'll come quiet in a minute or two.\" And Amelia no doubt was right. Calling for the police when there is a row in the house is like summoning the water-engines when the soot is on fire in the kitchen chimney. In such cases good management will allow the soot to burn itself out, without aid from the water-engines. In the present instance the police were not called in, and I am inclined to think that their presence would not have been advantageous to any of the party.\n\n\"Upon-my-honour--I know nothing about her,\" were the first words which Cradell was able to articulate, when Lupex, under Eames's persuasion, at last relaxed his hold.\n\nLupex turned round to Miss Spruce with a sardonic grin. \"You hear his words,--this enemy to domestic bliss,--Ha, ha! man, tell me whither you have conveyed my wife!\"\n\n\"If you were to give me the Bank of England I don't know,\" said Cradell.\n\n\"And I'm sure he does not know,\" said Mrs. Roper, whose suspicions against Cradell were beginning to subside. But as her suspicions subsided, her respect for him decreased. Such was the case also with Miss Spruce, and with Amelia, and with Jemima. They had all thought him to be a great fool for running away with Mrs. Lupex, but now they were beginning to think him a poor creature because he had not done so. Had he committed that active folly he would have been an interesting fool. But now, if, as they all suspected, he knew no more about Mrs. Lupex than they did, he would be a fool without any special interest whatever.\n\n\"Of course he doesn't,\" said Eames.\n\n\"No more than I do,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"His very looks show him innocent,\" said Mrs. Roper.\n\n\"Indeed they do,\" said Miss Spruce.\n\nLupex turned from one to the other as they thus defended the man whom he suspected, and shook his head at each assertion that was made. \"And if he doesn't know who does?\" he asked. \"Haven't I seen it all for the last three months? Is it reasonable to suppose that a creature such as she, used to domestic comforts all her life, should have gone off in this way, at dinner-time, taking with her my property and all her jewels, and that nobody should have instigated her; nobody assisted her! Is that a story to tell to such a man as me! You may tell it to the marines!\" Mr. Lupex, as he made this speech, was walking about the room, and as he finished it he threw his pocket-handkerchief with violence on to the floor. \"I know what to do, Mrs. Roper,\" he said. \"I know what steps to take. I shall put the affair into the hands of my lawyer to-morrow morning.\" Then he picked up his handkerchief and walked down into the dining-room.\n\n\"Of course you know nothing about it?\" said Eames to his friend, having run upstairs for the purpose of saying a word to him while he washed his hands.\n\n\"What,--about Maria? I don't know where she is, if you mean that.\"\n\n\"Of course I mean that. What else should I mean? And what makes you call her Maria?\"\n\n\"It is wrong. I admit it's wrong. The word will come out, you know.\"\n\n\"Will come out! I'll tell you what it is, old fellow, you'll get yourself into a mess, and all for nothing. That fellow will have you up before the police for stealing his things--\"\n\n\"But, Johnny--\"\n\n\"I know all about it. Of course you have not stolen them, and of course there was nothing to steal. But if you go on calling her Maria you'll find that he'll have a pull on you. Men don't call other men's wives names for nothing.\"\n\n\"Of course we've been friends,\" said Cradell, who rather liked this view of the matter.\n\n\"Yes,--you have been friends! She's diddled you out of your money, and that's the beginning and the end of it. And now, if you go on showing off your friendship, you'll be done out of more money. You're making an ass of yourself. That's the long and the short of it.\"\n\n\"And what have you made of yourself with that girl? There are worse asses than I am yet, Master Johnny.\" Eames, as he had no answer ready to this counter attack, left the room and went downstairs. Cradell soon followed him, and in a few minutes they were all eating their dinner together at Mrs. Roper's hospitable table.\n\nImmediately after dinner Lupex took himself away, and the conversation upstairs became general on the subject of the lady's departure.\n\n\"If I was him I'd never ask a question about her, but let her go,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"Yes; and then have all her bills following you, wherever you went,\" said Amelia's brother.\n\n\"I'd sooner have her bills than herself,\" said Eames.\n\n\"My belief is, that she's been an ill-used woman,\" said Cradell. \"If she had a husband that she could respect and have loved, and all that sort of thing, she would have been a charming woman.\"\n\n\"She's every bit as bad as he is,\" said Mrs. Roper.\n\n\"I can't agree with you, Mrs. Roper,\" continued the lady's champion. \"Perhaps I ought to understand her position better than any one here, and--\"\n\n\"Then that's just what you ought not to do, Mr. Cradell,\" said Mrs. Roper. And now the lady of the house spoke out her mind with much maternal dignity and with some feminine severity. \"That's just what a young man like you has no business to know. What's a married woman like that to you, or you to her; or what have you to do with understanding her position? When you've a wife of your own, if ever you do have one, you'll find you'll have trouble enough then without anybody else interfering with you. Not but what I believe you're innocent as a lamb about Mrs. Lupex; that is, as far as any harm goes. But you've got yourself into all this trouble by meddling, and was like enough to get yourself choked upstairs by that man. And who's to wonder when you go on pretending to be in love with a woman in that way, and she old enough to be your mother? What would your mamma say if she saw you at it?\"\n\n\"Ha, ha, ha!\" laughed Cradell.\n\n\"It's all very well your laughing, but I hate such folly. If I see a young man in love with a young woman, I respect him for it;\" and then she looked at Johnny Eames. \"I respect him for it,--even though he may now and then do things as he shouldn't. They most of 'em does that. But to see a young man like you, Mr. Cradell, dangling after an old married woman, who doesn't know how to behave herself; and all just because she lets him to do it;--ugh!--an old broomstick with a petticoat on would do just as well! It makes me sick to see it, and that's the truth of it. I don't call it manly; and it ain't manly, is it, Miss Spruce?\"\n\n\"Of course I know nothing about it,\" said the lady to whom the appeal was thus made. \"But a young gentleman should keep himself to himself till the time comes for him to speak out,--begging your pardon all the same, Mr. Cradell.\"\n\n\"I don't see what a married woman should want with any one after her but her own husband,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"And perhaps not always that,\" said John Eames.\n\nIt was about an hour after this when the front-door bell was rung, and a scream from Jemima announced to them all that some critical moment had arrived. Amelia, jumping up, opened the door, and then the rustle of a woman's dress was heard on the lower stairs. \"Oh, laws, ma'am, you have given us sich a turn,\" said Jemima. \"We all thought you was run away.\"\n\n\"It's Mrs. Lupex,\" said Amelia. And in two minutes more that ill-used lady was in the room.\n\n\"Well, my dears,\" said she, gaily, \"I hope nobody has waited dinner.\"\n\n\"No; we didn't wait dinner,\" said Mrs. Roper, very gravely.\n\n\"And where's my Orson? Didn't he dine at home? Mr. Cradell, will you oblige me by taking my shawl? But perhaps you had better not. People are so censorious; ain't they, Miss Spruce? Mr. Eames shall do it; and everybody knows that that will be quite safe. Won't it, Miss Amelia?\"\n\n\"Quite, I should think,\" said Amelia. And Mrs. Lupex knew that she was not to look for an ally in that quarter on the present occasion. Eames got up to take the shawl, and Mrs. Lupex went on.\n\n\"And didn't Orson dine at home? Perhaps they kept him down at the theatre. But I've been thinking all day what fun it would be when he thought his bird was flown.\"\n\n\"He did dine at home,\" said Mrs. Roper; \"and he didn't seem to like it. There wasn't much fun, I can assure you.\"\n\n\"Ah, wasn't there, though? I believe that man would like to have me tied to his button-hole. I came across a few friends,--lady friends, Mr. Cradell, though two of them had their husbands; so we made a party, and just went down to Hampton Court. So my gentleman has gone again, has he? That's what I get for gadding about myself, isn't it, Miss Spruce?\"\n\nMrs. Roper, as she went to bed that night, made up her mind that, whatever might be the cost and trouble of doing so, she would lose no further time in getting rid of her married guests.\n\n# CHAPTER XLII. LILY'S BEDSIDE.\n\nLily Dale's constitution was good, and her recovery was retarded by no relapse or lingering debility; but, nevertheless, she was forced to keep her bed for many days after the fever had left her. During all this period Dr. Crofts came every day. It was in vain that Mrs. Dale begged him not to do so; telling him in simple words that she felt herself bound not to accept from him all this continuation of his unremunerated labours now that the absolute necessity for them was over. He answered her only by little jokes, or did not answer her at all; but still he came daily, almost always at the same hour, just as the day was waning, so that he could sit for a quarter of an hour in the dusk, and then ride home to Guestwick in the dark. At this time Bell had been admitted into her sister's room, and she would always meet Dr. Crofts at Lily's bedside; but she never sat with him alone, since the day on which he had offered her his love with half-articulated words, and she had declined it with words also half-articulated. She had seen him alone since that, on the stairs, or standing in the hall, but she had not remained with him, talking to him after her old fashion, and no further word of his love had been spoken in speech either half or wholly articulate.\n\nNor had Bell spoken of what had passed to any one else. Lily would probably have told both her mother and sister instantly; but then no such scene as that which had taken place with Bell would have been possible with Lily. In whatever way the matter might have gone with her, there would certainly have been some clear tale to tell when the interview was over. She would have known whether or no she loved the man, or could love him, and would have given him some true and intelligible answer. Bell had not done so, but had given him an answer which, if true, was not intelligible, and if intelligible was not true. And yet, when she had gone away to think over what had passed, she had been happy and satisfied, and almost triumphant. She had never yet asked herself whether she expected anything further from Dr. Crofts, nor what that something further might be,--and yet she was happy!\n\nLily had now become pert and saucy in her bed, taking upon herself the little airs which are allowed to a convalescent invalid as compensation for previous suffering and restraint. She pretended to much anxiety on the subject of her dinner, and declared that she would go out on such or such a day, let Dr. Crofts be as imperious as he might. \"He's an old savage, after all,\" she said to her sister, one evening, after he was gone, \"and just as bad as the rest of them.\"\n\n\"I do not know who the rest of them are,\" said Bell, \"but at any rate he's not very old.\"\n\n\"You know what I mean. He's just as grumpy as Dr. Gruffen, and thinks everybody is to do what he tells them. Of course, you take his part.\"\n\n\"And of course you ought, seeing how good he has been.\"\n\n\"And of course I should, to anybody but you. I do like to abuse him to you.\"\n\n\"Lily, Lily!\"\n\n\"So I do. It's so hard to knock any fire out of you, that when one does find the place where the flint lies, one can't help hammering at it. What did he mean by saying that I shouldn't get up on Sunday? Of course I shall get up if I like it.\"\n\n\"Not if mamma asks you not?\"\n\n\"Oh, but she won't, unless he interferes and dictates to her. Oh, Bell, what a tyrant he would be if he were married!\"\n\n\"Would he?\"\n\n\"And how submissive you would be, if you were his wife! It's a thousand pities that you are not in love with each other;--that is, if you are not.\"\n\n\"Lily, I thought that there was a promise between us about that.\"\n\n\"Ah! but that was in other days. Things are all altered since that promise was given,--all the world has been altered.\" And as she said this the tone of her voice was changed, and it had become almost sad. \"I feel as though I ought to be allowed now to speak about anything I please.\"\n\n\"You shall, if it pleases you, my pet.\"\n\n\"You see how it is, Bell; I can never again have anything of my own to talk about.\"\n\n\"Oh, my darling, do not say that.\"\n\n\"But it is so, Bell; and why not say it? Do you think I never say it to myself in the hours when I am all alone, thinking over it--thinking, thinking, thinking. You must not,--you must not grudge to let me talk of it sometimes.\"\n\n\"I will not grudge you anything;--only I cannot believe that it must be so always.\"\n\n\"Ask yourself, Bell, how it would be with you. But I sometimes fancy that you measure me differently from yourself.\"\n\n\"Indeed I do, for I know how much better you are.\"\n\n\"I am not so much better as to be ever able to forget all that. I know I never shall do so. I have made up my mind about it clearly and with an absolute certainty.\"\n\n\"Lily, Lily, Lily! pray do not say so.\"\n\n\"But I do say it. And yet I have not been very mopish and melancholy; have I, Bell? I do think I deserve some little credit, and yet, I declare, you won't allow me the least privilege in the world.\"\n\n\"What privilege would you wish me to give you?\"\n\n\"To talk about Dr. Crofts.\"\n\n\"Lily, you are a wicked, wicked tyrant.\" And Bell leaned over her, and fell upon her, and kissed her, hiding her own face in the gloom of the evening. After that it came to be an accepted understanding between them that Bell was not altogether indifferent to Dr. Crofts.\n\n\"You heard what he said, my darling,\" Mrs. Dale said the next day, as the three were in the room together after Dr. Crofts was gone. Mrs. Dale was standing on one side of the bed, and Bell on the other, while Lily was scolding them both. \"You can get up for an hour or two to-morrow, but he thinks you had better not go out of the room.\"\n\n\"What would be the good of that, mamma? I am so tired of looking always at the same paper. It is such a tiresome paper. It makes one count the pattern over and over again. I wonder how you ever can live here.\"\n\n\"I've got used to it, you see.\"\n\n\"I never can get used to that sort of thing; but go on counting, and counting, and counting. I'll tell you what I should like; and I'm sure it would be the best thing, too.\"\n\n\"And what would you like?\" said Bell.\n\n\"Just to get up at nine o'clock to-morrow, and go to church as though nothing had happened. Then, when Dr. Crofts came in the evening, you would tell him I was down at the school.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't quite advise that,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"It would give him such a delightful start. And when he found I didn't die immediately, as of course I ought to do according to rule, he would be so disgusted.\"\n\n\"It would be very ungrateful, to say the least of it,\" said Bell.\n\n\"No, it wouldn't, a bit. He needn't come, unless he likes it. And I don't believe he comes to see me at all. It's all very well, mamma, your looking in that way; but I'm sure it's true. And I'll tell you what I'll do, I'll pretend to be bad again, otherwise the poor man will be robbed of his only happiness.\"\n\n\"I suppose we must allow her to say what she likes till she gets well,\" said Mrs. Dale, laughing. It was now nearly dark, and Mrs. Dale did not see that Bell's hand had crept under the bed-clothes, and taken hold of that of her sister. \"It's true, mamma,\" continued Lily, \"and I defy her to deny it. I would forgive him for keeping me in bed if he would only make her fall in love with him.\"\n\n\"She has made a bargain, mamma,\" said Bell, \"that she is to say whatever she likes till she gets well.\"\n\n\"I am to say whatever I like always; that was the bargain, and I mean to stand to it.\"\n\nOn the following Sunday Lily did get up, but did not leave her mother's bedroom. There she was, seated in that half-dignified and half-luxurious state which belongs to the first getting up of an invalid, when Dr. Crofts called. There she had eaten her tiny bit of roast mutton, and had called her mother a stingy old creature, because she would not permit another morsel; and there she had drunk her half glass of port wine, pretending that it was very bad, and twice worse than the doctor's physic; and there, Sunday though it was, she had fully enjoyed the last hour of daylight, reading that exquisite new novel which had just completed itself, amidst the jarring criticisms of the youth and age of the reading public.\n\n\"I am quite sure she was right in accepting him, Bell,\" she said, putting down the book as the light was fading, and beginning to praise the story.\n\n\"It was a matter of course,\" said Bell. \"It always is right in the novels. That's why I don't like them. They are too sweet.\"\n\n\"That's why I do like them, because they are so sweet. A sermon is not to tell you what you are, but what you ought to be, and a novel should tell you not what you are to get, but what you'd like to get.\"\n\n\"If so, then, I'd go back to the old school, and have the heroine really a heroine, walking all the way up from Edinburgh to London, and falling among thieves; or else nursing a wounded hero, and describing the battle from the window. We've got tired of that; or else the people who write can't do it now-a-days. But if we are to have real life, let it be real.\"\n\n\"No, Bell, no!\" said Lily. \"Real life sometimes is so painful.\" Then her sister, in a moment, was down on the floor at her feet, kissing her hand and caressing her knees, and praying that the wound might be healed.\n\nOn that morning Lily had succeeded in inducing her sister to tell her all that had been said by Dr. Crofts. All that had been said by herself also, Bell had intended to tell; but when she came to this part of the story, her account was very lame. \"I don't think I said anything,\" she said. \"But silence always gives consent. He'll know that,\" Lily had rejoined. \"No, he will not; my silence didn't give any consent; I'm sure of that. And he didn't think that it did.\" \"But you didn't mean to refuse him?\" \"I think I did. I don't think I knew what I meant; and it was safer, therefore, to look no, than to look yes. If I didn't say it, I'm sure I looked it.\" \"But you wouldn't refuse him now?\" asked Lily. \"I don't know,\" said Bell. \"It seems as though I should want years to make up my mind; and he won't ask me again.\"\n\nBell was still at her sister's feet, caressing them, and praying with all her heart that that wound might be healed in due time, when Mrs. Dale came in and announced the doctor's daily visit. \"Then I'll go,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Indeed you won't,\" said Lily. \"He's coming simply to make a morning call, and nobody need run away. Now, Dr. Crofts, you need not come and stand over me with your watch, for I won't let you touch my hand except to shake hands with me;\" and then she held her hand out to him. \"And all you'll know of my tongue you'll learn from the sound.\"\n\n\"I don't care in the least for your tongue.\"\n\n\"I dare say not, and yet you may some of these days. I can speak out, if I like it; can't I, mamma?\"\n\n\"I should think Dr. Crofts knows that by this time, my dear.\"\n\n\"I don't know. There are some things gentlemen are very slow to learn. But you must sit down, Dr. Crofts, and make yourself comfortable and polite; for you must understand that you are not master here any longer. I am out of bed now, and your reign is over.\"\n\n\"That's the gratitude of the world, all through,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Who is ever grateful to a doctor? He only cures you that he may triumph over some other doctor, and declare, as he goes by Dr. Gruffen's door, 'There, had she called you in, she'd have been dead before now; or else would have been ill for twelve months.' Don't you jump for joy when Dr. Gruffen's patients die?\"\n\n\"Of course I do--out in the market-place, so that everybody shall see me,\" said the doctor.\n\n\"Lily, how can you say such shocking things?\" said her sister.\n\nThen the doctor did sit down, and they were all very cosy together over the fire, talking about things which were not medical, or only half medical in their appliance. By degrees the conversation came round to Mrs. Eames and to John Eames. Two or three days since, Crofts had told Mrs. Dale of that affair at the railway station, of which up to that time she had heard nothing. Mrs. Dale, when she was assured that young Eames had given Crosbie a tremendous thrashing--the tidings of the affair which had got themselves substantiated at Guestwick so described the nature of the encounter--could not withhold some meed of applause.\n\n\"Dear boy!\" she said, almost involuntarily. \"Dear boy! it came from the honestness of his heart!\" And then she gave special injunctions to the doctor--injunctions which were surely unnecessary--that no word of the matter should be whispered before Lily.\n\n\"I was at the manor, yesterday,\" said the doctor, \"and the earl would talk about nothing but Master Johnny. He says he's the finest fellow going.\" Whereupon Mrs. Dale touched him with her foot, fearing that the conversation might be led away in the direction of Johnny's prowess.\n\n\"I am so glad,\" said Lily. \"I always knew that they'd find John out at last.\"\n\n\"And Lady Julia is just as fond of him,\" said the doctor.\n\n\"Dear me!\" said Lily. \"Suppose they were to make up a match!\"\n\n\"Lily, how can you be so absurd?\"\n\n\"Let me see; what relation would he be to us? He would certainly be Bernard's uncle, and uncle Christopher's half brother-in-law. Wouldn't it be odd?\"\n\n\"It would rather,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"I hope he'll be civil to Bernard. Don't you, Bell? Is he to give up the Income-tax Office, Dr. Crofts?\"\n\n\"I didn't hear that that was settled yet.\" And so they went on talking about John Eames.\n\n\"Joking apart,\" said Lily, \"I am very glad that Lord De Guest has taken him by the hand. Not that I think an earl is better than anybody else, but because it shows that people are beginning to understand that he has got something in him. I always said that they who laughed at John would see him hold up his head yet.\" All which words sank deep into Mrs. Dale's mind. If only, in some coming time, her pet might be taught to love this new young hero! But then would not that last heroic deed of his militate most strongly against any possibility of such love!\n\n\"And now I may as well be going,\" said the doctor, rising from his chair. At this time Bell had left the room, but Mrs. Dale was still there.\n\n\"You need not be in such a hurry, especially this evening,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Why especially this evening?\"\n\n\"Because it will be the last. Sit down again, Dr. Crofts. I've got a little speech to make to you. I've been preparing it all the morning, and you must give me an opportunity of speaking it.\"\n\n\"I'll come the day after to-morrow, and I'll hear it then.\"\n\n\"But I choose, sir, that you should hear it now. Am I not to be obeyed when I first get up on to my own throne? Dear, dear Dr. Crofts, how am I to thank you for all that you have done?\"\n\n\"How are any of us to thank him?\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"I hate thanks,\" said the doctor. \"One kind glance of the eye is worth them all, and I've had many such in this house.\"\n\n\"You have our hearts' love, at any rate,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"God bless you all!\" said he, as he prepared to go.\n\n\"But I haven't made my speech yet,\" said Lily. \"And to tell the truth, mamma, you must go away, or I shall never be able to make it. It's very improper, is it not, turning you out, but it shall only take three minutes.\" Then Mrs. Dale, with some little joking word, left the room; but, as she left it, her mind was hardly at ease. Ought she to have gone, leaving it to Lily's discretion to say what words she might think fit to Dr. Crofts? Hitherto she had never doubted her daughters--not even their discretion; and therefore it had been natural to her to go when she was bidden. But as she went downstairs she had her doubts whether she was right or no.\n\n\"Dr. Crofts,\" said Lily, as soon as they were alone. \"Sit down there, close to me. I want to ask you a question. What was it you said to Bell when you were alone with her the other evening in the parlour?\"\n\nThe doctor sat for a moment without answering, and Lily, who was watching him closely, could see by the light of the fire that he had been startled--had almost shuddered as the question was asked him.\n\n\"What did I say to her?\" and he repeated her words in a very low voice. \"I asked her if she could love me, and be my wife.\"\n\n\"And what answer did she make to you?\"\n\n\"What answer did she make? She simply refused me.\"\n\n\"No, no, no; don't believe her, Dr. Crofts. It was not so;--I think it was not so. Mind you, I can say nothing as coming from her. She has not told me her own mind. But if you really love her, she will be mad to refuse you.\"\n\n\"I do love her, Lily; that at any rate is true.\"\n\n\"Then go to her again. I am speaking for myself now. I cannot afford to lose such a brother as you would be. I love you so dearly that I cannot spare you. And she,--I think she'll learn to love you as you would wish to be loved. You know her nature, how silent she is, and averse to talk about herself. She has confessed nothing to me but this,--that you spoke to her and took her by surprise. Are we to have another chance? I know how wrong I am to ask such a question. But, after all, is not the truth the best?\"\n\n\"Another chance!\"\n\n\"I know what you mean, and I think she is worthy to be your wife. I do, indeed; and if so, she must be very worthy. You won't tell of me, will you now, doctor?\"\n\n\"No; I won't tell of you.\"\n\n\"And you'll try again?\"\n\n\"Yes; I'll try again.\"\n\n\"God bless you, my brother! I hope,--I hope you'll be my brother.\" Then, as he put out his hand to her once more, she raised her head towards him, and he, stooping down, kissed her forehead. \"Make mamma come to me,\" were the last words she spoke as he went out at the door.\n\n\"So you've made your speech,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Yes, mamma.\"\n\n\"I hope it was a discreet speech.\"\n\n\"I hope it was, mamma. But it has made me so tired, and I believe I'll go to bed. Do you know I don't think I should have done much good down at the school to-day?\"\n\nThen Mrs. Dale, in her anxiety to repair what injury might have been done to her daughter by over-exertion, omitted any further mention of the farewell speech.\n\nDr. Crofts as he rode home enjoyed but little of the triumph of a successful lover. \"It may be that she's right,\" he said to himself; \"and, at any rate, I'll ask again.\" Nevertheless, that \"No\" which Bell had spoken, and had repeated, still sounded in his ears harsh and conclusive. There are men to whom a peal of noes rattling about their ears never takes the sound of a true denial, and others to whom the word once pronounced, be it whispered ever so softly, comes as though it were an unchangeable verdict from the supreme judgment-seat.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIII. FIE, FIE!\n\nWill any reader remember the loves,--no, not the loves; that word is so decidedly ill-applied as to be incapable of awakening the remembrance of any reader; but the flirtations--of Lady Dumbello and Mr. Plantagenet Palliser? Those flirtations, as they had been carried on at Courcy Castle, were laid bare in all their enormities to the eye of the public, and it must be confessed that if the eye of the public was shocked, that eye must be shocked very easily.\n\nBut the eye of the public was shocked, and people who were particular as to their morals said very strange things. Lady De Courcy herself said very strange things indeed, shaking her head, and dropping mysterious words; whereas Lady Clandidlem spoke much more openly, declaring her opinion that Lady Dumbello would be off before May. They both agreed that it would not be altogether bad for Lord Dumbello that he should lose his wife, but shook their heads very sadly when they spoke of poor Plantagenet Palliser. As to the lady's fate, that lady whom they had both almost worshipped during the days at Courcy Castle,--they did not seem to trouble themselves about that.\n\nAnd it must be admitted that Mr. Palliser had been a little imprudent,--imprudent, that is, if he knew anything about the rumours afloat,--seeing that soon after his visit at Courcy Castle he had gone down to Lady Hartletop's place in Shropshire, at which the Dumbellos intended to spend the winter, and on leaving it had expressed his intention of returning in February. The Hartletop people had pressed him very much,--the pressure having come with peculiar force from Lord Dumbello. Therefore it is reasonable to suppose that the Hartletop people had at any rate not heard of the rumour.\n\nMr. Plantagenet Palliser spent his Christmas with his uncle, the Duke of Omnium, at Gatherum Castle. That is to say, he reached the castle in time for dinner on Christmas eve, and left it on the morning after Christmas day. This was in accordance with the usual practice of his life, and the tenants, dependants, and followers of the Omnium interest were always delighted to see this manifestation of a healthy English domestic family feeling between the duke and his nephew. But the amount of intercourse on such occasions between them was generally trifling. The duke would smile as he put out his right hand to his nephew, and say,--\n\n\"Well, Plantagenet,--very busy, I suppose?\"\n\nThe duke was the only living being who called him Plantagenet to his face, though there were some scores of men who talked of Planty Pal behind his back. The duke had been the only living being so to call him. Let us hope that it still was so, and that there had arisen no feminine exception, dangerous in its nature and improper in its circumstances.\n\n\"Well, Plantagenet,\" said the duke, on the present occasion, \"very busy, I suppose?\"\n\n\"Yes, indeed, duke,\" said Mr. Palliser. \"When a man gets the harness on him he does not easily get quit of it.\"\n\nThe duke remembered that his nephew had made almost the same remark at his last Christmas visit.\n\n\"By-the-by,\" said the duke, \"I want to say a word or two to you before you go.\"\n\nSuch a proposition on the duke's part was a great departure from his usual practice, but the nephew of course undertook to obey his uncle's behests.\n\n\"I'll see you before dinner to-morrow,\" said Plantagenet.\n\n\"Ah, do,\" said the duke. \"I'll not keep you five minutes.\" And at six o'clock on the following afternoon the two were closeted together in the duke's private room.\n\n\"I don't suppose there is much in it,\" began the duke, \"but people are talking about you and Lady Dumbello.\"\n\n\"Upon my word, people are very kind.\" And Mr. Palliser bethought himself of the fact,--for it certainly was a fact,--that people for a great many years had talked about his uncle and Lady Dumbello's mother-in-law.\n\n\"Yes; kind enough; are they not? You've just come from Hartlebury, I believe.\" Hartlebury was the Marquis of Hartletop's seat in Shropshire.\n\n\"Yes, I have. And I'm going there again in February.\"\n\n\"Ah, I'm sorry for that. Not that I mean, of course, to interfere with your arrangements. You will acknowledge that I have not often done so, in any matter whatever.\"\n\n\"No; you have not,\" said the nephew, comforting himself with an inward assurance that no such interference on his uncle's part could have been possible.\n\n\"But in this instance it would suit me, and I really think it would suit you too, that you should be as little at Hartlebury as possible. You have said you would go there, and of course you will go. But if I were you, I would not stay above a day or two.\"\n\nMr. Plantagenet Palliser received everything he had in the world from his uncle. He sat in Parliament through his uncle's interest, and received an allowance of ever so many thousand a year which his uncle could stop to-morrow by his mere word. He was his uncle's heir, and the dukedom, with certain entailed properties, must ultimately fall to him, unless his uncle should marry and have a son. But by far the greater portion of the duke's property was unentailed; the duke might probably live for the next twenty years or more; and it was quite possible that, if offended, he might marry and become a father. It may be said that no man could well be more dependent on another than Plantagenet Palliser was upon his uncle; and it may be said also that no father or uncle ever troubled his heir with less interference. Nevertheless, the nephew immediately felt himself aggrieved by this allusion to his private life, and resolved at once that he would not submit to such surveillance.\n\n\"I don't know how long I shall stay,\" said he; \"but I cannot say that my visit will be influenced one way or the other by such a rumour as that.\"\n\n\"No; probably not. But it may perhaps be influenced by my request.\" And the duke, as he spoke, looked a little savage.\n\n\"You wouldn't ask me to regard a report that has no foundation.\"\n\n\"I am not asking about its foundation. Nor do I in the least wish to interfere with your manner in life.\" By which last observation the duke intended his nephew to understand that he was quite at liberty to take away any other gentleman's wife, but that he was not at liberty to give occasion even for a surmise that he wanted to take Lord Dumbello's wife. \"The fact is this, Plantagenet. I have for many years been intimate with that family. I have not many intimacies, and shall probably never increase them. Such friends as I have, I wish to keep, and you will easily perceive that any such report as that which I have mentioned, might make it unpleasant for me to go to Hartlebury, or for the Hartlebury people to come here.\" The duke certainly could not have spoken plainer, and Mr. Palliser understood him thoroughly. Two such alliances between the two families could not be expected to run pleasantly together, and even the rumour of any such second alliance might interfere with the pleasantness of the former one.\n\n\"That's all,\" said the duke.\n\n\"It's a most absurd slander,\" said Mr. Palliser.\n\n\"I dare say. Those slanders always are absurd; but what can we do? We can't tie up people's tongues.\" And the duke looked as though he wished to have the subject considered as finished, and to be left alone.\n\n\"But we can disregard them,\" said the nephew, indiscreetly.\n\n\"You may. I have never been able to do so. And yet, I believe, I have not earned for myself the reputation of being subject to the voices of men. You think that I am asking much of you; but you should remember that hitherto I have given much and have asked nothing. I expect you to oblige me in this matter.\"\n\nThen Mr. Plantagenet Palliser left the room, knowing that he had been threatened. What the duke had said amounted to this.--If you go on dangling after Lady Dumbello, I'll stop the seven thousand a year which I give you. I'll oppose your next return at Silverbridge, and I'll make a will and leave away from you Matching and The Horns,--a beautiful little place in Surrey, the use of which had been already offered to Mr. Palliser in the event of his marriage; all the Littlebury estate in Yorkshire, and the enormous Scotch property. Of my personal goods, and money invested in loans, shares, and funds, you shall never touch a shilling, or the value of a shilling. And, if I find that I can suit myself, it may be that I'll leave you plain Mr. Plantagenet Palliser, with a little first cousin for the head of your family.\n\nThe full amount of this threat Mr. Palliser understood, and, as he thought of it, he acknowledged to himself that he had never felt for Lady Dumbello anything like love. No conversation between them had ever been warmer than that of which the reader has seen a sample. Lady Dumbello had been nothing to him. But now,--now that the matter had been put before him in this way, might it not become him, as a gentleman, to fall in love with so very beautiful a woman, whose name had already been linked with his own? We all know that story of the priest, who, by his question in the confessional, taught the ostler to grease the horses' teeth. \"I never did yet,\" said the ostler, \"but I'll have a try at it.\" In this case, the duke had acted the part of the priest, and Mr. Palliser, before the night was over, had almost become as ready a pupil as the ostler. As to the threat, it would ill become him, as a Palliser and a Plantagenet, to regard it. The duke would not marry. Of all men in the world he was the least likely to spite his own face by cutting off his own nose; and, for the rest of it, Mr. Palliser would take his chance. Therefore he went down to Hartlebury early in February, having fully determined to be very particular in his attentions to Lady Dumbello.\n\nAmong a houseful of people at Hartlebury, he found Lord Porlock, a slight, sickly, worn-out looking man, who had something about his eye of his father's hardness, but nothing in his mouth of his father's ferocity.\n\n\"So your sister is going to be married?\" said Mr. Palliser.\n\n\"Yes. One has no right to be surprised at anything they do, when one remembers the life their father leads them.\"\n\n\"I was going to congratulate you.\"\n\n\"Don't do that.\"\n\n\"I met him at Courcy, and rather liked him.\"\n\nMr. Palliser had barely spoken to Mr. Crosbie at Courcy, but then in the usual course of his social life he seldom did more than barely speak to anybody.\n\n\"Did you?\" said Lord Porlock. \"For the poor girl's sake I hope he's not a ruffian. How any man should propose to my father to marry a daughter out of his house, is more than I can understand. How was my mother looking?\"\n\n\"I didn't see anything amiss about her.\"\n\n\"I expect that he'll murder her some day.\" Then that conversation came to an end.\n\nMr. Palliser himself perceived--as he looked at her he could not but perceive--that a certain amount of social energy seemed to enliven Lady Dumbello when he approached her. She was given to smile when addressed, but her usual smile was meaningless, almost leaden, and never in any degree flattering to the person to whom it was accorded. Very many women smile as they answer the words which are spoken to them, and most who do so flatter by their smile. The thing is so common that no one thinks of it. The flattering pleases, but means nothing. The impression unconsciously taken simply conveys a feeling that the woman has made herself agreeable, as it was her duty to do,--agreeable, as far as that smile went, in some very infinitesimal degree. But she has thereby made her little contribution to society. She will make the same contribution a hundred times in the same evening. No one knows that she has flattered anybody; she does not know it herself; and the world calls her an agreeable woman. But Lady Dumbello put no flattery into her customary smiles. They were cold, unmeaning, accompanied by no special glance of the eye, and seldom addressed to the individual. They were given to the room at large; and the room at large, acknowledging her great pretensions, accepted them as sufficient. But when Mr. Palliser came near to her she would turn herself slightly, ever so slightly, on her seat, and would allow her eyes to rest for a moment upon his face. Then when he remarked that it had been rather cold, she would smile actually upon him as she acknowledged the truth of his observation. All this Mr. Palliser taught himself to observe, having been instructed by his foolish uncle in that lesson as to the greasing of the horses' teeth.\n\nBut, nevertheless, during the first week of his stay at Hartlebury, he did not say a word to her more tender than his observation about the weather. It is true that he was very busy. He had undertaken to speak upon the address, and as Parliament was now about to be opened, and as his speech was to be based upon statistics, he was full of figures and papers. His correspondence was pressing, and the day was seldom long enough for his purposes. He felt that the intimacy to which he aspired was hindered by the laborious routine of his life; but nevertheless he would do something before he left Hartlebury, to show the special nature of his regard. He would say something to her, that should open to her view the secret of--shall we say his heart? Such was his resolve, day after day. And yet day after day went by, and nothing was said. He fancied that Lord Dumbello was somewhat less friendly in his manner than he had been, that he put himself in the way and looked cross; but, as he declared to himself, he cared very little for Lord Dumbello's looks.\n\n\"When do you go to town?\" he said to her one evening.\n\n\"Probably in April. We certainly shall not leave Hartlebury before that.\"\n\n\"Ah, yes. You stay for the hunting.\"\n\n\"Yes; Lord Dumbello always remains here through March. He may run up to town for a day or two.\"\n\n\"How comfortable! I must be in London on Thursday, you know.\"\n\n\"When Parliament meets, I suppose?\"\n\n\"Exactly. It is such a bore; but one has to do it.\"\n\n\"When a man makes a business of it, I suppose he must.\"\n\n\"Oh, dear, yes; it's quite imperative.\" Then Mr. Palliser looked round the room, and thought he saw Lord Dumbello's eye fixed upon him. It was really very hard work. If the truth must be told, he did not know how to begin. What was he to say to her? How was he to commence a conversation that should end by being tender? She was very handsome certainly, and for him she could look interesting; but for his very life he did not know how to begin to say anything special to her. A liaison with such a woman as Lady Dumbello,--platonic, innocent, but nevertheless very intimate,--would certainly lend a grace to his life, which, under its present circumstances, was rather dry. He was told,--told by public rumour which had reached him through his uncle,--that the lady was willing. She certainly looked as though she liked him; but how was he to begin? The art of startling the House of Commons and frightening the British public by the voluminous accuracy of his statistics he had already learned; but what was he to say to a pretty woman?\n\n\"You'll be sure to be in London in April?\"\n\nThis was on another occasion.\n\n\"Oh, yes; I think so.\"\n\n\"In Carlton Gardens, I suppose.\"\n\n\"Yes; Lord Dumbello has got a lease of the house now.\"\n\n\"Has he, indeed? Ah, it's an excellent house. I hope I shall be allowed to call there sometimes.\"\n\n\"Certainly,--only I know you must be so busy.\"\n\n\"Not on Saturdays and Sundays.\"\n\n\"I always receive on Sundays,\" said Lady Dumbello. Mr. Palliser felt that there was nothing peculiarly gracious in this. A permission to call when all her other acquaintances would be there, was not much; but still, perhaps, it was as much as he could expect to obtain on that occasion. He looked up and saw that Lord Dumbello's eyes were again upon him, and that Lord Dumbello's brow was black. He began to doubt whether a country house, where all the people were thrown together, was the best place in the world for such manoeuvring. Lady Dumbello was very handsome, and he liked to look at her, but he could not find any subject on which to interest her in that drawing-room at Hartlebury. Later in the evening he found himself saying something to her about the sugar duties, and then he knew that he had better give it up. He had only one day more, and that was required imperatively for his speech. The matter would go much easier in London, and he would postpone it till then. In the crowded rooms of London private conversation would be much easier, and Lord Dumbello wouldn't stand over and look at him. Lady Dumbello had taken his remarks about the sugar very kindly, and had asked for a definition of an ad valorem duty. It was a nearer approach to a real conversation than he had ever before made; but the subject had been unlucky, and could not, in his hands, be brought round to anything tender; so he resolved to postpone his gallantry till the London spring should make it easy, and felt as he did so, that he was relieved for the time from a heavy weight.\n\n\"Good-by, Lady Dumbello,\" he said, on the next evening. \"I start early to-morrow morning.\"\n\n\"Good-by, Mr. Palliser.\"\n\nAs she spoke she smiled ever so sweetly, but she certainly had not learned to call him Plantagenet as yet. He went up to London and immediately got himself to work. The accurate and voluminous speech came off with considerable credit to himself,--credit of that quiet, enduring kind which is accorded to such men. The speech was respectable, dull, and correct. Men listened to it, or sat with their hats over their eyes, asleep, pretending to do so; and the Daily Jupiter in the morning had a leading article about it, which, however, left the reader at its close altogether in doubt whether Mr. Palliser might be supposed to be a great financial pundit or no. Mr. Palliser might become a shining light to the moneyed world, and a glory to the banking interests; he might be a future Chancellor of the Exchequer. But then again, it might turn out that, in these affairs, he was a mere ignis fatuus, a blind guide,--a man to be laid aside as very respectable, but of no depth. Who, then, at the present time, could judiciously risk his credit by declaring whether Mr. Palliser understood his subject or did not understand it? We are not content in looking to our newspapers for all the information that earth and human intellect can afford; but we demand from them what we might demand if a daily sheet could come to us from the world of spirits. The result, of course, is this,--that the papers do pretend that they have come daily from the world of spirits; but the oracles are very doubtful, as were those of old.\n\nPlantagenet Palliser, though he was contented with this article, felt, as he sat in his chambers in the Albany, that something else was wanting to his happiness. This sort of life was all very well. Ambition was a grand thing, and it became him, as a Palliser and a future peer, to make politics his profession. But might he not spare an hour or two for Amaryllis in the shade? Was it not hard, this life of his? Since he had been told that Lady Dumbello smiled upon him, he had certainly thought more about her smiles than had been good for his statistics. It seemed as though a new vein in his body had been brought into use, and that blood was running where blood had never run before. If he had seen Lady Dumbello before Dumbello had seen her, might he not have married her? Ah! in such case as that, had she been simply Miss Grantly, or Lady Griselda Grantly, as the case might have been, he thought he might have been able to speak to her with more ease. As it was, he certainly had found the task difficult, down in the country, though he had heard of men of his class doing the same sort of thing all his life. For my own part, I believe that the reputed sinners are much more numerous than the sinners.\n\nAs he sat there, a certain Mr. Fothergill came in upon him. Mr. Fothergill was a gentleman who managed most of his uncle's ordinary affairs,--a clever fellow, who knew on which side his bread was buttered. Mr. Fothergill was naturally anxious to stand well with the heir; but to stand well with the owner was his business in life, and with that business he never allowed anything to interfere. On this occasion Mr. Fothergill was very civil, complimenting his future possible patron on his very powerful speech, and predicting for him political power with much more certainty than the newspapers which had, or had not, come from the world of spirits. Mr. Fothergill had come in to say a word or two about some matter of business. As all Mr. Palliser's money passed through Mr. Fothergill's hands, and as his electioneering interests were managed by Mr. Fothergill, Mr. Fothergill not unfrequently called to say a necessary word or two. When this was done he said another word or two, which might be necessary or not, as the case might be.\n\n\"Mr. Palliser,\" said he, \"I wonder you don't think of marrying. I hope you'll excuse me.\"\n\nMr. Palliser was by no means sure that he would excuse him, and sat himself suddenly upright in his chair in a manner that was intended to exhibit a first symptom of outraged dignity. But, singularly enough, he had himself been thinking of marriage at that moment. How would it have been with him had he known the beautiful Griselda before the Dumbello alliance had been arranged? Would he have married her? Would he have been comfortable if he had married her? Of course he could not marry now, seeing that he was in love with Lady Dumbello, and that the lady in question, unfortunately, had a husband of her own; but though he had been thinking of marrying, he did not like to have the subject thus roughly thrust before his eyes, and, as it were, into his very lap by his uncle's agent. Mr. Fothergill, no doubt, saw the first symptom of outraged dignity, for he was a clever, sharp man. But, perhaps, he did not in truth much regard it. Perhaps he had received instructions which he was bound to regard above all other matters.\n\n\"I hope you'll excuse me, Mr. Palliser, I do, indeed; but I say it because I am half afraid of some,--some,--some diminution of good feeling, perhaps, I had better call it, between you and your uncle. Anything of that kind would be such a monstrous pity.\"\n\n\"I am not aware of any such probability.\"\n\nThis Mr. Palliser said with considerable dignity; but when the words were spoken he bethought himself whether he had not told a fib.\n\n\"No; perhaps not. I trust there is no such probability. But the duke is a very determined man if he takes anything into his head;--and then he has so much in his power.\"\n\n\"He has not me in his power, Mr. Fothergill.\"\n\n\"No, no, no. One man does not have another in his power in this country,--not in that way; but then you know, Mr. Palliser, it would hardly do to offend him; would it?\"\n\n\"I would rather not offend him, as is natural. Indeed, I do not wish to offend any one.\"\n\n\"Exactly so; and least of all the duke, who has the whole property in his own hands. We may say the whole, for he can marry to-morrow if he pleases. And then his life is so good. I don't know a stouter man of his age, anywhere.\"\n\n\"I'm very glad to hear it.\"\n\n\"I'm sure you are, Mr. Palliser. But if he were to take offence, you know?\"\n\n\"I should put up with it.\"\n\n\"Yes, exactly; that's what you would do. But it would be worth while to avoid it, seeing how much he has in his power.\"\n\n\"Has the duke sent you to me now, Mr. Fothergill?\"\n\n\"No, no, no,--nothing of the sort. But he dropped words the other day which made me fancy that he was not quite,--quite,--quite at ease about you. I have long known that he would be very glad indeed to see an heir born to the property. The other morning,--I don't know whether there was anything in it,--but I fancied he was going to make some change in the present arrangements. He did not do it, and it might have been fancy. Only think, Mr. Palliser, what one word of his might do! If he says a word, he never goes back from it.\" Then, having said so much, Mr. Fothergill went his way.\n\nMr. Palliser understood the meaning of all this very well. It was not the first occasion on which Mr. Fothergill had given him advice,--advice such as Mr. Fothergill himself had no right to give him. He always received such counsel with an air of half-injured dignity, intending thereby to explain to Mr. Fothergill that he was intruding. But he knew well whence the advice came; and though, in all such cases, he had made up his mind not to follow such counsel, it had generally come to pass that Mr. Palliser's conduct had more or less accurately conformed itself to Mr. Fothergill's advice. A word from the duke might certainly do a great deal! Mr. Palliser resolved that in that affair of Lady Dumbello he would follow his own devices. But, nevertheless, it was undoubtedly true that a word from the duke might do a great deal!\n\nWe, who are in the secret, know how far Mr. Palliser had already progressed in his iniquitous passion before he left Hartlebury. Others, who were perhaps not so well informed, gave him credit for a much more advanced success. Lady Clandidlem, in her letter to Lady De Courcy, written immediately after the departure of Mr. Palliser, declared that, having heard of that gentleman's intended matutinal departure, she had confidently expected to learn at the breakfast-table that Lady Dumbello had flown with him. From the tone of her ladyship's language, it seemed as though she had been robbed of an anticipated pleasure by Lady Dumbello's prolonged sojourn in the halls of her husband's ancestors. \"I feel, however, quite convinced,\" said Lady Clandidlem, \"that it cannot go on longer than the spring. I never yet saw a man so infatuated as Mr. Palliser. He did not leave her for one moment all the time he was here. No one but Lady Hartletop would have permitted it. But, you know, there is nothing so pleasant as good old family friendships.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XLIV. VALENTINE'S DAY AT ALLINGTON.\n\nLily had exacted a promise from her mother before her illness, and during the period of her convalescence often referred to it, reminding her mother that that promise had been made, and must be kept. Lily was to be told the day on which Crosbie was to be married. It had come to the knowledge of them all that the marriage was to take place in February. But this was not sufficient for Lily. She must know the day.\n\nAnd as the time drew nearer,--Lily becoming stronger the while, and less subject to medical authority,--the marriage of Crosbie and Alexandrina was spoken of much more frequently at the Small House. It was not a subject which Mrs. Dale or Bell would have chosen for conversation; but Lily would refer to it. She would begin by doing so almost in a drolling strain, alluding to herself as a forlorn damsel in a play-book; and then she would go on to speak of his interests as a matter which was still of great moment to her. But in the course of such talking she would too often break down, showing by some sad word or melancholy tone how great was the burden on her heart. Mrs. Dale and Bell would willingly have avoided the subject, but Lily would not have it avoided. For them it was a very difficult matter on which to speak in her hearing. It was not permitted to them to say a word of abuse against Crosbie, as to whom they thought that no word of condemnation could be sufficiently severe; and they were forced to listen to such excuses for his conduct as Lily chose to manufacture, never daring to point out how vain those excuses were.\n\nIndeed, in those days Lily reigned as a queen at the Small House. Ill-usage and illness together falling into her hands had given her such power, that none of the other women were able to withstand it. Nothing was said about it; but it was understood by them all, Jane and the cook included, that Lily was for the time paramount. She was a dear, gracious, loving, brave queen, and no one was anxious to rebel;--only that those praises of Crosbie were so very bitter in the ears of her subjects. The day was named soon enough, and the tidings came down to Allington. On the fourteenth of February, Crosbie was to be made a happy man. This was not known to the Dales till the twelfth, and they would willingly have spared the knowledge then, had it been possible to spare it. But it was not so, and on that evening Lily was told.\n\nDuring these days, Bell used to see her uncle daily. Her visits were made with the pretence of taking to him information as to Lily's health; but there was perhaps at the bottom of them a feeling that, as the family intended to leave the Small House at the end of March, it would be well to let the squire know that there was no enmity in their hearts against him. Nothing more had been said about their moving,--nothing, that is, from them to him. But the matter was going on, and he knew it. Dr. Crofts was already in treaty on their behalf for a small furnished house at Guestwick. The squire was very sad about it,--very sad indeed. When Hopkins spoke to him on the subject, he sharply desired that faithful gardener to hold his tongue, giving it to be understood that such things were not to be made matter of talk by the Allington dependants till they had been officially announced. With Bell during these visits he never alluded to the matter. She was the chief sinner, in that she had refused to marry her cousin, and had declined even to listen to rational counsel upon the matter. But the squire felt that he could not discuss the subject with her, seeing that he had been specially informed by Mrs. Dale that his interference would not be permitted; and then he was perhaps aware that if he did discuss the subject with Bell, he would not gain much by such discussion. Their conversation, therefore, generally fell upon Crosbie, and the tone in which he was mentioned in the Great House was very different from that assumed in Lily's presence.\n\n\"He'll be a wretched man,\" said the squire, when he told Bell of the day that had been fixed.\n\n\"I don't want him to be wretched,\" said Bell. \"But I can hardly think that he can act as he has done without being punished.\"\n\n\"He will be a wretched man. He gets no fortune with her, and she will expect everything that fortune can give. I believe, too, that she is older than he is. I cannot understand it. Upon my word, I cannot understand how a man can be such a knave and such a fool. Give my love to Lily. I'll see her to-morrow or the next day. She's well rid of him; I'm sure of that;--though I suppose it would not do to tell her so.\"\n\nThe morning of the fourteenth came upon them at the Small House, as comes the morning of those special days which have been long considered, and which are to be long remembered. It brought with it a hard, bitter frost,--a black, biting frost,--such a frost as breaks the water-pipes, and binds the ground to the hardness of granite. Lily, queen as she was, had not yet been allowed to go back to her own chamber, but occupied the larger bed in her mother's room, her mother sleeping on a smaller one.\n\n\"Mamma,\" she said, \"how cold they'll be!\" Her mother had announced to her the fact of the black frost, and these were the first words she spoke.\n\n\"I fear their hearts will be cold also,\" said Mrs. Dale. She ought not to have said so. She was transgressing the acknowledged rule of the house in saying any word that could be construed as being inimical to Crosbie or his bride. But her feeling on the matter was too strong, and she could not restrain herself.\n\n\"Why should their hearts be cold? Oh, mamma, that is a terrible thing to say. Why should their hearts be cold?\"\n\n\"I hope it may not be so.\"\n\n\"Of course you do; of course we all hope it. He was not cold-hearted, at any rate. A man is not cold-hearted, because he does not know himself. Mamma, I want you to wish for their happiness.\"\n\nMrs. Dale was silent for a minute or two before she answered this, but then she did answer it. \"I think I do,\" said she. \"I think I do wish for it.\"\n\n\"I am very sure that I do,\" said Lily.\n\nAt this time Lily had her breakfast upstairs, but went down into the drawing-room in the course of the morning.\n\n\"You must be very careful in wrapping yourself as you go downstairs,\" said Bell, who stood by the tray on which she had brought up the toast and tea. \"The cold is what you would call awful.\"\n\n\"I should call it jolly,\" said Lily, \"if I could get up and go out. Do you remember lecturing me about talking slang the day that he first came?\"\n\n\"Did I, my pet?\"\n\n\"Don't you remember, when I called him a swell? Ah, dear! so he was. That was the mistake, and it was all my own fault, as I had seen it from the first.\"\n\nBell for a moment turned her face away, and beat with her foot against the ground. Her anger was more difficult of restraint than was even her mother's,--and now, not restraining it, but wishing to hide it, she gave it vent in this way.\n\n\"I understand, Bell. I know what your foot means when it goes in that way; and you shan't do it. Come here, Bell, and let me teach you Christianity. I'm a fine sort of teacher, am I not? And I did not quite mean that.\"\n\n\"I wish I could learn it from some one,\" said Bell. \"There are circumstances in which what we call Christianity seems to me to be hardly possible.\"\n\n\"When your foot goes in that way it is a very unchristian foot, and you ought to keep it still. It means anger against him, because he discovered before it was too late that he would not be happy,--that is, that he and I would not be happy together if we were married.\"\n\n\"Don't scrutinize my foot too closely, Lily.\"\n\n\"But your foot must bear scrutiny, and your eyes, and your voice. He was very foolish to fall in love with me. And so was I very foolish to let him love me, at a moment's notice,--without a thought as it were. I was so proud of having him, that I gave myself up to him all at once, without giving him a chance of thinking of it. In a week or two it was done. Who could expect that such an engagement should be lasting?\"\n\n\"And why not? That is nonsense, Lily. But we will not talk about it.\"\n\n\"Ah, but I want to talk about it. It was as I have said, and if so, you shouldn't hate him because he did the only thing which he honestly could do when he found out his mistake.\"\n\n\"What; become engaged again within a week!\"\n\n\"There had been a very old friendship, Bell; you must remember that. But I was speaking of his conduct to me, and not of his conduct to--\" And then she remembered that that other lady might at this very moment possess the name which she had once been so proud to think that she would bear herself. \"Bell,\" she said, stopping her other speech suddenly, \"at what o'clock do people get married in London?\"\n\n\"Oh, at all manner of hours,--any time before twelve. They will be fashionable, and will be married late.\"\n\n\"You don't think she's Mrs. Crosbie yet, then?\"\n\n\"Lady Alexandrina Crosbie,\" said Bell, shuddering.\n\n\"Yes, of course; I forgot. I should so like to see her. I feel such an interest about her. I wonder what coloured hair she has. I suppose she is a sort of Juno of a woman,--very tall and handsome. I'm sure she has not got a pug-nose like me. Do you know what I should really like, only of course it's not possible;--to be godmother to his first child.\"\n\n\"Oh, Lily!\"\n\n\"I should. Don't you hear me say that I know it's not possible? I'm not going up to London to ask her. She'll have all manner of grandees for her godfathers and godmothers. I wonder what those grand people are really like.\"\n\n\"I don't think there's any difference. Look at Lady Julia.\"\n\n\"Oh, she's not a grand person. It isn't merely having a title. Don't you remember that he told us that Mr. Palliser is about the grandest grandee of them all. I suppose people do learn to like them. He always used to say that he had been so long among people of that sort, that it would be very difficult for him to divide himself off from them. I should never have done for that kind of thing; should I?\"\n\n\"There is nothing I despise so much as what you call that kind of thing.\"\n\n\"Do you? I don't. After all, think how much work they do. He used to tell me of that. They have all the governing in their hands, and get very little money for doing it.\"\n\n\"Worse luck for the country.\"\n\n\"The country seems to do pretty well. But you're a radical, Bell. My belief is, you wouldn't be a lady if you could help it.\"\n\n\"I'd sooner be an honest woman.\"\n\n\"And so you are,--my own dear, dearest, honest Bell,--and the fairest lady that I know. If I were a man, Bell, you are just the girl that I should worship.\"\n\n\"But you are not a man; so it's no good.\"\n\n\"But you mustn't let your foot go astray in that way; you mustn't, indeed. Somebody said, that whatever is, is right, and I declare I believe it.\"\n\n\"I'm sometimes inclined to think, that whatever is, is wrong.\"\n\n\"That's because you're a radical. I think I'll get up now, Bell; only it's so frightfully cold that I'm afraid.\"\n\n\"There's a beautiful fire,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Yes; I see. But the fire won't go all around me, like the bed does. I wish I could know the very moment when they're at the altar. It's only half-past ten yet.\"\n\n\"I shouldn't be at all surprised if it's over.\"\n\n\"Over! What a word that is! A thing like that is over, and then all the world cannot put it back again. What if he should be unhappy after all?\"\n\n\"He must take his chance,\" said Bell, thinking within her own mind that that chance would be a very bad one.\n\n\"Of course he must take his chance. Well,--I'll get up now.\" And then she took her first step out into the cold world beyond her bed. \"We must all take our chance. I have made up my mind that it will be at half-past eleven.\"\n\nWhen half-past eleven came, she was seated in a large easy chair over the drawing-room fire, with a little table by her side, on which a novel was lying. She had not opened her book that morning, and had been sitting for some time perfectly silent, with her eyes closed, and her watch in her hand.\n\n\"Mamma,\" she said at last, \"it is over now, I'm sure.\"\n\n\"What is over, my dear?\"\n\n\"He has made that lady his wife. I hope God will bless them, and I pray that they may be happy.\" As she spoke these words, there was an unwonted solemnity in her tone which startled Mrs. Dale and Bell.\n\n\"I also will hope so,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"And now, Lily, will it not be well that you should turn your mind away from the subject, and endeavour to think of other things?\"\n\n\"But I can't, mamma. It is so easy to say that; but people can't choose their own thoughts.\"\n\n\"They can usually direct them as they will, if they make the effort.\"\n\n\"But I can't make the effort. Indeed, I don't know why I should. It seems natural to me to think about him, and I don't suppose it can be very wrong. When you have had so deep an interest in a person, you can't drop him all of a sudden.\" Then there was again silence, and after a while Lily took up her novel. She made that effort of which her mother had spoken, but she made it altogether in vain. \"I declare, Bell,\" she said, \"it's the greatest rubbish I ever attempted to read.\" This was specially ungrateful, because Bell had recommended the book. \"All the books have got to be so stupid! I think I'll read Pilgrim's Progress again.\"\n\n\"What do you say to Robinson Crusoe?\" said Bell.\n\n\"Or Paul and Virginia?\" said Lily. \"But I believe I'll have Pilgrim's Progress. I never can understand it, but I rather think that makes it nicer.\"\n\n\"I hate books I can't understand,\" said Bell. \"I like a book to be clear as running water, so that the whole meaning may be seen at once.\"\n\n\"The quick seeing of the meaning must depend a little on the reader, must it not?\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"The reader mustn't be a fool, of course,\" said Bell.\n\n\"But then so many readers are fools,\" said Lily. \"And yet they get something out of their reading. Mrs. Crump is always poring over the Revelations, and nearly knows them by heart. I don't think she could interpret a single image, but she has a hazy, misty idea of the truth. That's why she likes it,--because it's too beautiful to be understood; and that's why I like Pilgrim's Progress.\" After which Bell offered to get the book in question.\n\n\"No, not now,\" said Lily. \"I'll go on with this, as you say it's so grand. The personages are always in their tantrums, and go on as though they were mad. Mamma, do you know where they're going for the honeymoon?\"\n\n\"No, my dear.\"\n\n\"He used to talk to me about going to the lakes.\" And then there was another pause, during which Bell observed that her mother's face became clouded with anxiety. \"But I won't think of it any more,\" continued Lily; \"I will fix my mind to something.\" And then she got up from her chair. \"I don't think it would have been so difficult if I had not been ill.\"\n\n\"Of course it would not, my darling.\"\n\n\"And I'm going to be well again now, immediately. Let me see: I was told to read Carlyle's History of the French Revolution, and I think I'll begin now.\" It was Crosbie who had told her to read the book, as both Bell and Mrs. Dale were well aware. \"But I must put it off till I can get it down from the other house.\"\n\n\"Jane shall fetch it, if you really want it,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Bell shall get it, when she goes up in the afternoon; will you, Bell? And I'll try to get on with this stuff in the meantime.\" Then again she sat with her eyes fixed upon the pages of the book. \"I'll tell you what, mamma,--you may have some comfort in this: that when to-day's gone by, I shan't make a fuss about any other day.\"\n\n\"Nobody thinks that you are making a fuss, Lily.\"\n\n\"Yes, but I am. Isn't it odd, Bell, that it should take place on Valentine's day? I wonder whether it was so settled on purpose, because of the day. Oh, dear, I used to think so often of the letter that I should get from him on this day, when he would tell me that I was his valentine. Well; he's got another--valen--tine--now.\" So much she said with articulate voice, and then she broke down, bursting out into convulsive sobs, and crying in her mother's arms as though she would break her heart. And yet her heart was not broken, and she was still strong in that resolve which she had made, that her grief should not overpower her. As she had herself said, the thing would not have been so difficult, had she not been weakened by illness.\n\n\"Lily, my darling; my poor, ill-used darling.\"\n\n\"No, mamma, I won't be that.\" And she struggled grievously to get the better of the hysterical attack which had overpowered her. \"I won't be regarded as ill-used; not as specially ill-used. But I am your darling, your own darling. Only I wish you'd beat me and thump me when I'm such a fool, instead of pitying me. It's a great mistake being soft to people when they make fools of themselves. There, Bell; there's your stupid book, and I won't have any more of it. I believe it was that that did it.\" And she pushed the book away from her.\n\nAfter this little scene she said no further word about Crosbie and his bride on that day, but turned the conversation towards the prospect of their new house at Guestwick.\n\n\"It will be a great comfort to be nearer Dr. Crofts; won't it, Bell?\"\n\n\"I don't know,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Because if we are ill, he won't have such a terrible distance to come.\"\n\n\"That will be a comfort for him, I should think,\" said Bell, very demurely.\n\nIn the evening the first volume of the French Revolution had been procured, and Lily stuck to her reading with laudable perseverance; till at eight her mother insisted on her going to bed, queen as she was.\n\n\"I don't believe a bit, you know, that the king was such a bad man as that,\" she said.\n\n\"I do,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Ah, that's because you're a radical. I never will believe that kings are so much worse than other people. As for Charles the First, he was about the best man in history.\"\n\nThis was an old subject of dispute; but Lily on the present occasion was allowed her own way,--as being an invalid.\n\n# CHAPTER XLV. VALENTINE'S DAY IN LONDON.\n\nThe fourteenth of February in London was quite as black, and cold, and as wintersome as it was at Allington, and was, perhaps, somewhat more melancholy in its coldness. Nevertheless Lady Alexandrina De Courcy looked as bright as bridal finery could make her, when she got out of her carriage and walked into St. James's church at eleven o'clock on that morning.\n\nIt had been finally arranged that the marriage should take place in London. There were certainly many reasons which would have made a marriage from Courcy Castle more convenient. The De Courcy family were all assembled at their country family residence, and could therefore have been present at the ceremony without cost or trouble. The castle too was warm with the warmth of life, and the pleasantness of home would have lent a grace to the departure of one of the daughters of the house. The retainers and servants were there, and something of the rich mellowness of a noble alliance might have been felt, at any rate by Crosbie, at a marriage so celebrated. And it must have been acknowledged, even by Lady De Courcy, that the house in Portman Square was very cold--that a marriage from thence would be cold,--that there could be no hope of attaching to it any honour and glory, or of making it resound with fashionable éclat in the columns of the Morning Post. But then, had they been married in the country, the earl would have been there; whereas there was no probability of his travelling up to London for the purpose of being present on such an occasion.\n\nThe earl was very terrible in these days, and Alexandrina, as she became confidential in her communications with her future husband, spoke of him as of an ogre, who could not by any means be avoided in all the concerns of life, but whom one might shun now and again by some subtle device and careful arrangement of favourable circumstances. Crosbie had more than once taken upon himself to hint that he did not specially regard the ogre, seeing that for the future he could keep himself altogether apart from the malicious monster's dominions.\n\n\"He will not come to me in our new home,\" he had said to his love, with some little touch of affection. But to this view of the case Lady Alexandrina had demurred. The ogre in question was not only her parent, but was also a noble peer, and she could not agree to any arrangement by which their future connection with the earl, and with nobility in general, might be endangered. Her parent, doubtless, was an ogre, and in his ogreship could make himself very terrible to those near him; but then might it not be better for them to be near to an earl who was an ogre, than not to be near to any earl at all? She had therefore signified to Crosbie that the ogre must be endured.\n\nBut, nevertheless, it was a great thing to be rid of him on that happy occasion. He would have said very dreadful things,--things so dreadful that there might have been a question whether the bridegroom could have borne them. Since he had heard of Crosbie's accident at the railway station, he had constantly talked with fiendish glee of the beating which had been administered to his son-in-law. Lady De Courcy in taking Crosbie's part, and maintaining that the match was fitting for her daughter, had ventured to declare before her husband that Crosbie was a man of fashion, and the earl would now ask, with a loathsome grin, whether the bridegroom's fashion had been improved by his little adventure at Paddington. Crosbie, to whom all this was not repeated, would have preferred a wedding in the country. But the countess and Lady Alexandrina knew better.\n\nThe earl had strictly interdicted any expenditure, and the countess had of necessity construed this as forbidding any unnecessary expense. \"To marry a girl without any immediate cost was a thing which nobody could understand,\" as the countess remarked to her eldest daughter.\n\n\"I would really spend as little as possible,\" Lady Amelia had answered. \"You see, mamma, there are circumstances about it which one doesn't wish to have talked about just at present. There's the story of that girl,--and then that fracas at the station. I really think it ought to be as quiet as possible.\" The good sense of Lady Amelia was not to be disputed, as her mother acknowledged. But then if the marriage were managed in any notoriously quiet way, the very notoriety of that quiet would be as dangerous as an attempt at loud glory. \"But it won't cost as much,\" said Amelia. And thus it had been resolved that the wedding should be very quiet.\n\nTo this Crosbie had assented very willingly, though he had not relished the manner in which the countess had explained to him her views.\n\n\"I need not tell you, Adolphus,\" she had said, \"how thoroughly satisfied I am with this marriage. My dear girl feels that she can be happy as your wife, and what more can I want? I declared to her and to Amelia that I was not ambitious, for their sakes, and have allowed them both to please themselves.\"\n\n\"I hope they have pleased themselves,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"I trust so; but nevertheless,--I don't know whether I make myself understood?\"\n\n\"Quite so, Lady De Courcy. If Alexandrina were going to marry the eldest son of a marquis, you would have a longer procession to church than will be necessary when she marries me.\"\n\n\"You put it in such an odd way, Adolphus.\"\n\n\"It's all right so long as we understand each other. I can assure you I don't want any procession at all. I should be quite contented to go down with Alexandrina, arm in arm, like Darby and Joan, and let the clerk give her away.\"\n\nWe may say that he would have been much better contented could he have been allowed to go down the street without any encumbrance on his arm. But there was no possibility now for such deliverance as that.\n\nBoth Lady Amelia and Mr. Gazebee had long since discovered the bitterness of his heart and the fact of his repentance, and Gazebee had ventured to suggest to his wife that his noble sister-in-law was preparing for herself a life of misery.\n\n\"He'll become quiet and happy when he's used to it,\" Lady Amelia had replied, thinking, perhaps, of her own experiences.\n\n\"I don't know, my dear; he's not a quiet man. There's something in his eye which tells me that he could be very hard to a woman.\"\n\n\"It has gone too far now for any change,\" Lady Amelia had answered.\n\n\"Well; perhaps it has.\"\n\n\"And I know my sister so well; she would not hear of it. I really think they will do very well when they become used to each other.\"\n\nMr. Gazebee, who also had had his own experiences, hardly dared to hope so much. His home had been satisfactory to him, because he had been a calculating man, and having made his calculation correctly was willing to take the net result. He had done so all his life with success. In his house his wife was paramount,--as he very well knew. But no effort on his wife's part, had she wished to make such effort, could have forced him to spend more than two-thirds of his income. Of this she also was aware, and had trimmed her sails accordingly, likening herself to him in this respect. But of such wisdom, and such trimmings, and such adaptability, what likelihood was there with Mr. Crosbie and Lady Alexandrina?\n\n\"At any rate, it is too late now,\" said Lady Amelia, thus concluding the conversation.\n\nBut nevertheless, when the last moment came, there was some little attempt at glory. Who does not know the way in which a lately married couple's little dinner-party stretches itself out from the pure simplicity of a fried sole and a leg of mutton to the attempt at clear soup, the unfortunately cold dish of round balls which is handed about after the sole, and the brightly red jelly, and beautifully pink cream, which are ordered, in the last agony of ambition, from the next pastrycook's shop?\n\n\"We cannot give a dinner, my dear, with only cook and Sarah.\"\n\nIt has thus begun, and the husband has declared that he has no such idea. \"If Phipps and Dowdney can come here and eat a bit of mutton, they are very welcome; if not, let them stay away. And you might as well ask Phipps's sister; just to have some one to go with you into the drawing-room.\"\n\n\"I'd much rather go alone, because then I can read,\"--or sleep, we may say.\n\nBut her husband has explained that she would look friendless in this solitary state, and therefore Phipps's sister has been asked. Then the dinner has progressed down to those costly jellies which have been ordered in a last agony. There has been a conviction on the minds of both of them that the simple leg of mutton would have been more jolly for them all. Had those round balls not been carried about by a hired man; had simple mutton with hot potatoes been handed to Miss Phipps by Sarah, Miss Phipps would not have simpered with such unmeaning stiffness when young Dowdney spoke to her. They would have been much more jolly. \"Have a bit more mutton, Phipps; and where do you like it?\" How pleasant it sounds! But we all know that it is impossible. My young friend had intended this, but his dinner had run itself away to cold round balls and coloured forms from the pastrycook. And so it was with the Crosbie marriage.\n\nThe bride must leave the church in a properly appointed carriage, and the postboys must have wedding favours. So the thing grew; not into noble proportions, not into proportions of true glory, justifying the attempt and making good the gala. A well-cooked rissole, brought pleasantly to you, is good eating. A gala marriage, when everything is in keeping, is excellent sport. Heaven forbid that we should have no gala marriages. But the small spasmodic attempt, made in opposition to manifest propriety, made with an inner conviction of failure,--that surely should be avoided in marriages, in dinners, and in all affairs of life.\n\nThere were bridesmaids and there was a breakfast. Both Margaretta and Rosina came up to London for the occasion, as did also a first cousin of theirs, one Miss Gresham, a lady whose father lived in the same county. Mr. Gresham had married a sister of Lord De Courcy's, and his services were also called into requisition. He was brought up to give away the bride, because the earl,--as the paragraph in the newspaper declared,--was confined at Courcy Castle by his old hereditary enemy, the gout. A fourth bridesmaid also was procured, and thus there was a bevy, though not so large a bevy as is now generally thought to be desirable. There were only three or four carriages at the church, but even three or four were something. The weather was so frightfully cold that the light-coloured silks of the ladies carried with them a show of discomfort. Girls should be very young to look nice in light dresses on a frosty morning, and the bridesmaids at Lady Alexandrina's wedding were not very young. Lady Rosina's nose was decidedly red. Lady Margaretta was very wintry, and apparently very cross. Miss Gresham was dull, tame, and insipid; and the Honourable Miss O'Flaherty, who filled the fourth place, was sulky at finding that she had been invited to take a share in so very lame a performance.\n\nBut the marriage was made good, and Crosbie bore up against his misfortunes like a man. Montgomerie Dobbs and Fowler Pratt both stood by him, giving him, let us hope, some assurance that he was not absolutely deserted by all the world,--that he had not given himself up, bound hand and foot, to the De Courcys, to be dealt with in all matters as they might please. It was that feeling which had been so grievous to him,--and that other feeling, cognate to it, that if he should ultimately succeed in rebelling against the De Courcys, he would find himself a solitary man.\n\n\"Yes; I shall go,\" Fowler Pratt had said to Montgomerie Dobbs. \"I always stick to a fellow if I can. Crosbie has behaved like a blackguard, and like a fool also; and he knows that I think so. But I don't see why I should drop him on that account. I shall go as he has asked me.\"\n\n\"So shall I,\" said Montgomerie Dobbs, who considered that he would be safe in doing whatever Fowler Pratt did, and who remarked to himself that after all Crosbie was marrying the daughter of an earl.\n\nThen, after the marriage, came the breakfast, at which the countess presided with much noble magnificence. She had not gone to church, thinking, no doubt, that she would be better able to maintain her good humour at the feast, if she did not subject herself to the chance of lumbago in the church. At the foot of the table sat Mr. Gresham, her brother-in-law, who had undertaken to give the necessary toast and make the necessary speech. The Honourable John was there, saying all manner of ill-natured things about his sister and new brother-in-law, because he had been excluded from his proper position at the foot of the table. But Alexandrina had declared that she would not have the matter entrusted to her brother. The Honourable George would not come, because the countess had not asked his wife.\n\n\"Maria may be slow, and all that sort of thing,\" George had said; \"but she is my wife. And she had got what they haven't. Love me, love my dog, you know.\" So he had stayed down at Courcy,--very properly as I think.\n\nAlexandrina had wished to go away before breakfast, and Crosbie would not have cared how early an escape had been provided for him; but the countess had told her daughter that if she would not wait for the breakfast, there should be no breakfast at all, and in fact no wedding; nothing but a simple marriage. Had there been a grand party, that going away of the bride and bridegroom might be very well; but the countess felt that on such an occasion as this nothing but the presence of the body of the sacrifice could give any reality to the festivity. So Crosbie and Lady Alexandrina Crosbie heard Mr. Gresham's speech, in which he prophesied for the young couple an amount of happiness and prosperity almost greater than is compatible with the circumstances of humanity. His young friend Crosbie, whose acquaintance he had been delighted to make, was well known as one of the rising pillars of the State. Whether his future career might be parliamentary, or devoted to the permanent Civil Service of the country, it would be alike great, noble, and prosperous. As to his dear niece, who was now filling that position in life which was most beautiful and glorious for a young woman,--she could not have done better. She had preferred genius to wealth,--so said Mr. Gresham,--and she would find her fitting reward. As to her finding her fitting reward, whatever her preferences may have been, there Mr. Gresham was no doubt quite right. On that head I myself have no doubt whatever. After that Crosbie returned thanks, making a much better speech than nine men do out of ten on such occasions, and then the thing was over. No other speaking was allowed, and within half an hour from that time, he and his bride were in the post-chaise, being carried away to the Folkestone railway station; for that place had been chosen as the scene of their honeymoon. It had been at one time intended that the journey to Folkestone should be made simply as the first stage to Paris, but Paris and all foreign travelling had been given up by degrees.\n\n\"I don't care a bit about France,--we have been there so often,\" Alexandrina said.\n\nShe had wished to be taken to Naples, but Crosbie had made her understand at the first whispering of the word, that Naples was quite out of the question. He must look now in all things to money. From the very first outset of his career he must save a shilling wherever a shilling could be saved. To this view of life no opposition was made by the De Courcy interest. Lady Amelia had explained to her sister that they ought so to do their honeymooning that it should not cost more than if they began keeping house at once. Certain things must be done which, no doubt, were costly in their nature. The bride must take with her a well-dressed lady's-maid. The rooms at the Folkestone hotel must be large, and on the first floor. A carriage must be hired for her use while she remained; but every shilling must be saved the spending of which would not make itself apparent to the outer world. Oh, deliver us from the poverty of those who, with small means, affect a show of wealth! There is no whitening equal to that of sepulchres whited as they are whited!\n\nBy the proper administration of a slight bribe Crosbie secured for himself and his wife a compartment in the railway carriage to themselves. And as he seated himself opposite to Alexandrina, having properly tucked her up with all her bright-coloured trappings, he remembered that he had never in truth been alone with her before. He had danced with her frequently, and been left with her for a few minutes between the figures. He had flirted with her in crowded drawing-rooms, and had once found a moment at Courcy Castle to tell her that he was willing to marry her in spite of his engagement with Lilian Dale. But he had never walked with her for hours together as he had walked with Lily. He had never talked to her about government, and politics, and books, nor had she talked to him of poetry, of religion, and of the little duties and comforts of life. He had known the Lady Alexandrina for the last six or seven years; but he had never known her,--perhaps never would know her,--as he had learned to know Lily Dale within the space of two months.\n\nAnd now that she was his wife, what was he to say to her? They two had commenced a partnership which was to make of them for the remaining term of their lives one body and one flesh. They were to be all-in-all to each other. But how was he to begin this all-in-all partnership? Had the priest, with his blessing, done it so sufficiently that no other doing on Crosbie's own part was necessary? There she was, opposite to him, his very actual wife,--bone of his bone; and what was he to say to her? As he settled himself on his seat, taking over his own knees a part of a fine fur rug trimmed with scarlet, with which he had covered her other mufflings, he bethought himself how much easier it would have been to talk to Lily. And Lily would have been ready with all her ears, and all her mind, and all her wit, to enter quickly upon whatever thoughts had occurred to him. In that respect Lily would have been a wife indeed,--a wife that would have transferred herself with quick mental activity into her husband's mental sphere. Had he begun about his office Lily would have been ready for him, but Alexandrina had never yet asked him a single question about his official life. Had he been prepared with a plan for to-morrow's happiness Lily would have taken it up eagerly, but Alexandrina never cared for such trifles.\n\n\"Are you quite comfortable?\" he said, at last.\n\n\"Oh, yes, quite, thank you. By-the-by, what did you do with my dressing-case?\"\n\nAnd that question she did ask with some energy.\n\n\"It is under you. You can have it as foot-stool if you like it.\"\n\n\"Oh, no; I should scratch it. I was afraid that if Hannah had it, it might be lost.\" Then again there was silence, and Crosbie again considered as to what he would next say to his wife.\n\nWe all know the advice given us of old as to what we should do under such circumstances; and who can be so thoroughly justified in following that advice as a newly-married husband? So he put out his hand for hers and drew her closer to him.\n\n\"Take care of my bonnet,\" she said, as she felt the motion of the railway carriage when he kissed her. I don't think he kissed her again till he had landed her and her bonnet safely at Folkestone. How often would he have kissed Lily, and how pretty would her bonnet have been when she reached the end of her journey, and how delightfully happy would she have looked when she scolded him for bending it! But Alexandrina was quite in earnest about her bonnet; by far too much in earnest for any appearance of happiness.\n\nSo he sat without speaking, till the train came to the tunnel.\n\n\"I do so hate tunnels,\" said Alexandrina.\n\nHe had half intended to put out his hand again, under some mistaken idea that the tunnel afforded him an opportunity. The whole journey was one long opportunity, had he desired it; but his wife hated tunnels, and so he drew his hand back again. Lily's little fingers would have been ready for his touch. He thought of this, and could not help thinking of it.\n\nHe had The Times newspaper in his dressing-bag. She also had a novel with her. Would she be offended if he took out the paper and read it? The miles seemed to pass by very slowly, and there was still another hour down to Folkestone. He longed for his Times, but resolved at last that he would not read unless she read first. She also had remembered her novel; but by nature she was more patient than he, and she thought that on such a journey any reading might perhaps be almost improper. So she sat tranquilly, with her eyes fixed on the netting over her husband's head.\n\nAt last he could stand it no longer, and he dashed off into a conversation, intended to be most affectionate and serious.\n\n\"Alexandrina,\" he said, and his voice was well-tuned for the tender serious manner, had her ears been alive to such tuning. \"Alexandrina, this is a very important step that you and I have taken to-day.\"\n\n\"Yes; it is, indeed,\" said she.\n\n\"I trust we shall succeed in making each other happy.\"\n\n\"Yes; I hope we shall.\"\n\n\"If we both think seriously of it, and remember that that is our chief duty, we shall do so.\"\n\n\"Yes, I suppose we shall. I only hope we shan't find the house very cold. It is so new, and I am so subject to colds in my head. Amelia says we shall find it very cold; but then she was always against our going there.\"\n\n\"The house will do very well,\" said Crosbie. And Alexandrina could perceive that there was something of the master in his tone as he spoke.\n\n\"I am only telling you what Amelia said,\" she replied.\n\nHad Lily been his bride, and had he spoken to her of their future life and mutual duties, how she would have kindled to the theme! She would have knelt at his feet on the floor of the carriage, and, looking up into his face, would have promised him to do her best,--her best,--her very best. And with what an eagerness of inward resolution would she have determined to keep her promise. He thought of all this now, but he knew that he ought not to think of it. Then, for some quarter of an hour, he did take out his newspaper, and she, when she saw him do so, did take out her novel.\n\nHe took out his newspaper, but he could not fix his mind upon the politics of the day. Had he not made a terrible mistake? Of what use to him in life would be that thing of a woman that sat opposite to him? Had not a great punishment come upon him, and had he not deserved the punishment? In truth, a great punishment had come upon him. It was not only that he had married a woman incapable of understanding the higher duties of married life, but that he himself would have been capable of appreciating the value of a woman who did understand them. He would have been happy with Lily Dale; and therefore we may surmise that his unhappiness with Lady Alexandrina would be the greater. There are men who, in marrying such as Lady Alexandrina De Courcy, would get the article best suited to them, as Mortimer Gazebee had done in marrying her sister. Miss Griselda Grantly, who had become Lady Dumbello, though somewhat colder and somewhat cleverer than Lady Alexandrina, had been of the same sort. But in marrying her, Lord Dumbello had got the article best suited to him;--if only the ill-natured world would allow him to keep the article. It was in this that Crosbie's failure had been so grievous,--that he had seen and approved the better course, but had chosen for himself to walk in that which was worse. During that week at Courcy Castle,--the week which he passed there immediately after his second visit to Allington,--he had deliberately made up his mind that he was more fit for the bad course than for the good one. The course was now before him, and he had no choice but to walk in it.\n\nIt was very cold when they got to Folkestone, and Lady Alexandrina shivered as she stepped into the private-looking carriage which had been sent to the station for her use.\n\n\"We shall find a good fire in the parlour at the hotel,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"Oh, I hope so,\" said Alexandrina, \"and in the bedroom too.\"\n\nThe young husband felt himself to be offended, but he hardly knew why. He felt himself to be offended, and with difficulty induced himself to go through all those little ceremonies the absence of which would have been remarked by everybody. He did his work, however, seeing to all her shawls and wrappings, speaking with good-nature to Hannah, and paying special attention to the dressing-case.\n\n\"What time would you like to dine?\" he asked, as he prepared to leave her alone with Hannah in the bedroom.\n\n\"Whenever you please; only I should like some tea and bread-and-butter presently.\"\n\nCrosbie went into the sitting-room, ordered the tea and bread-and-butter, ordered also the dinner, and then stood himself up with his back to the fire, in order that he might think a little of his future career.\n\nHe was a man who had long since resolved that his life should be a success. It would seem that all men would so resolve, if the matter were simply one of resolution. But the majority of men, as I take it, make no such resolution, and very many men resolve that they will be unsuccessful. Crosbie, however, had resolved on success, and had done much towards carrying out his purpose. He had made a name for himself, and had acquired a certain fame. That, however, was, as he acknowledged to himself, departing from him. He looked the matter straight in the face, and told himself that his fashion must be abandoned; but the office remained to him. He might still rule over Mr. Optimist, and make a subservient slave of Butterwell. That must be his line in life now, and to that line he would endeavour to be true. As to his wife and his home,--he would look to them for his breakfast, and perhaps his dinner. He would have a comfortable arm-chair, and if Alexandrina should become a mother, he would endeavour to love his children; but above all things he would never think of Lily. After that he stood and thought of her for half an hour.\n\n\"If you please, sir, my lady wants to know at what time you have ordered dinner.\"\n\n\"At seven, Hannah.\"\n\n\"My lady says she is very tired, and will lie down till dinner-time.\"\n\n\"Very well, Hannah. I will go into her room when it is time to dress. I hope they are making you comfortable downstairs?\"\n\nThen Crosbie strolled out on the pier in the dusk of the cold winter evening.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVI. JOHN EAMES AT HIS OFFICE.\n\nMr. Crosbie and his wife went upon their honeymoon tour to Folkestone in the middle of February, and returned to London about the end of March. Nothing of special moment to the interests of our story occurred during those six weeks, unless the proceedings of the young married couple by the sea-side may be thought to have any special interest. With regard to those proceedings I can only say that Crosbie was very glad when they were brought to a close. All holiday-making is hard work, but holiday-making with nothing to do is the hardest work of all. At the end of March they went into their new house, and we will hope that Lady Alexandrina did not find it very cold.\n\nDuring this time Lily's recovery from her illness was being completed. She had no relapse, nor did anything occur to create a new fear on her account. But, nevertheless, Dr. Crofts gave it as his opinion that it would be inexpedient to move her into a fresh house at Lady-day. March is not a kindly month for invalids; and therefore with some regret on the part of Mrs. Dale, with much impatience on that of Bell, and with considerable outspoken remonstrance from Lily herself, the squire was requested to let them remain through the month of April. How the squire received this request, and in what way he assented to the doctor's reasoning, will be told in the course of a chapter or two.\n\nIn the meantime John Eames had continued his career in London without much immediate satisfaction to himself, or to the lady who boasted to be his heart's chosen queen. Miss Amelia Roper, indeed, was becoming very cross, and in her ill-temper was playing a game that was tending to create a frightful amount of hot water in Burton Crescent. She was devoting herself to a flirtation with Mr. Cradell, not only under the immediate eyes of Johnny Eames, but also under those of Mrs. Lupex. John Eames, the blockhead, did not like it. He was above all things anxious to get rid of Amelia and her claims; so anxious, that on certain moody occasions he would threaten himself with diverse tragical terminations to his career in London. He would enlist. He would go to Australia. He would blow out his brains. He would have \"an explanation\" with Amelia, tell her that she was a vixen, and proclaim his hatred. He would rush down to Allington and throw himself in despair at Lily's feet. Amelia was the bugbear of his life. Nevertheless, when she flirted with Cradell, he did not like it, and was ass enough to speak to Cradell about it.\n\n\"Of course I don't care,\" he said, \"only it seems to me that you are making a fool of yourself.\"\n\n\"I thought you wanted to get rid of her.\"\n\n\"She's nothing on earth to me; only it does, you know--\"\n\n\"Does do what?\" asked Cradell.\n\n\"Why, if I was to be fal-lalling with that married woman, you wouldn't like it. That's all about it. Do you mean to marry her?\"\n\n\"What!--Amelia?\"\n\n\"Yes; Amelia.\"\n\n\"Not if I know it.\"\n\n\"Then if I were you I would leave her alone. She's only making a fool of you.\"\n\nEames's advice may have been good, and the view taken by him of Amelia's proceedings may have been correct; but as regarded his own part in the affair, he was not wise. Miss Roper, no doubt, wished to make him jealous; and she succeeded in the teeth of his aversion to her and of his love elsewhere. He had no desire to say soft things to Miss Roper. Miss Roper, with all her skill, could not extract a word pleasantly soft from him once a week. But, nevertheless, soft words to her and from her in another quarter made him uneasy. Such being the case, must we not acknowledge that John Eames was still floundering in the ignorance of his hobbledehoyhood?\n\nThe Lupexes at this time still held their ground in the Crescent, although repeated warnings to go had been given them. Mrs. Roper, though she constantly spoke of sacrificing all that they owed her, still hankered, with a natural hankering, after her money. And as each warning was accompanied by a demand for payment, and usually produced some slight subsidy on account, the thing went on from week to week; and at the beginning of April Mr. and Mrs. Lupex were still boarders at Mrs. Roper's house.\n\nEames had heard nothing from Allington since the time of his Christmas visit, and his subsequent correspondence with Lord De Guest. In his letters from his mother he was told that game came frequently from Guestwick Manor, and in this way he knew that he was not forgotten by the earl. But of Lily he had heard not a word,--except, indeed, the rumour, which had now become general, that the Dales from the Small House were about to move themselves into Guestwick. When first he learned this he construed the tidings as favourable to himself, thinking that Lily, removed from the grandeur of Allington, might possibly be more easily within his reach; but, latterly, he had given up any such hope as that, and was telling himself that his friend at the Manor had abandoned all idea of making up the marriage. Three months had already elapsed since his visit. Five months had passed since Crosbie had surrendered his claim. Surely such a knave as Crosbie might be forgotten in five months! If any steps could have been taken through the squire, surely three months would have sufficed for them! It was very manifest to him that there was no ground of hope for him at Allington, and it would certainly be well for him to go off to Australia. He would go to Australia, but he would thrash Cradell first for having dared to interfere with Amelia Roper. That, generally, was the state of his mind during the first week in April.\n\nThen there came to him a letter from the earl which instantly effected a great change in all his feelings; which taught him to regard Australia as a dream, and almost put him into a good humour with Cradell. The earl had by no means lost sight of his friend's interests at Allington; and, moreover, those interests were now backed by an ally who in this matter must be regarded as much more powerful than the earl. The squire had given in his consent to the Eames alliance.\n\nThe earl's letter was as follows:--\n\nGuestwick Manor, April 7, 18--.\n\nI told you to write to me again, and you haven't done it. I saw your mother the other day, or else you might have been dead for anything I knew. A young man always ought to write letters when he is told to do so.\n\nEames, when he had got so far, felt himself rather aggrieved by this rebuke, knowing that he had abstained from writing to his patron simply from an unwillingness to intrude upon him with his letters. \"By Jove, I'll write to him every week of his life, till he's sick of me,\" Johnny said to himself when he found himself thus instructed as to a young man's duties.\n\nAnd now I have got to tell you a long story, and I should like it much better if you were down here, so that I might save myself the trouble; but you would think me ill-natured if I were to keep you waiting. I happened to meet Mr. Dale the other day, and he said that he should be very glad if a certain young lady would make up her mind to listen to a certain young friend of mine. So I asked him what he meant to do about the young lady's fortune, and he declared himself willing to give her a hundred a year during his life, and to settle four thousand pounds upon her after his death. I said that I would do as much on my part by the young man; but as two hundred a year, with your salary, would hardly give you enough to begin with, I'll make mine a hundred and fifty. You'll be getting up in your office soon, and with five hundred a year you ought to be able to get along; especially as you need not insure your life. I should live somewhere near Bloomsbury Square at first, because I'm told you can get a house for nothing. After all, what's fashion worth? You can bring your wife down here in the autumn, and have some shooting. She won't let you go to sleep under the trees, I'll be bound.\n\nBut you must look after the young lady. You will understand that no one has said a word to her about it; or, if they have, I don't know it. You'll find the squire on your side, that's all. Couldn't you manage to come down this Easter? Tell old Buffle, with my compliments, that I want you. I'll write to him if you like it. I did know him at one time, though I can't say I was ever very fond of him. It stands to reason that you can't get on with Miss Lily without seeing her; unless, indeed, you like better to write to her, which always seems to me to be very poor sort of fun. You'd much better come down, and go a-wooing in the regular old-fashioned way. I need not tell you that Lady Julia will be delighted to see you. You are a prime favourite with her since that affair at the railway station. She thinks a great deal more about that than she does about the bull.\n\nNow, my dear fellow, you know all about it, and I shall take it very much amiss of you if you don't answer my letter soon.\n\nYour very sincere friend,\n\nWhen Eames had finished this letter, sitting at his office-desk, his surprise and elation were so great that he hardly knew where he was or what he ought to do. Could it be the truth that Lily's uncle had not only consented that the match should be made, but that he had also promised to give his niece a considerable fortune? For a few minutes it seemed to Johnny as though all obstacles to his happiness were removed, and that there was no impediment between him and an amount of bliss of which he had hitherto hardly dared to dream. Then, when he considered the earl's munificence, he almost cried. He found that he could not compose his mind to think, or even his hand to write. He did not know whether it would be right in him to accept such pecuniary liberality from any living man, and almost thought that he should feel himself bound to reject the earl's offer. As to the squire's money, that he knew he might accept. All that comes in the shape of a young woman's fortune may be taken by any man.\n\nHe would certainly answer the earl's letter, and that at once. He would not leave the office till he had done so. His friend should have cause to bring no further charge against him of that kind. And then again he reverted to the injustice which had been done to him in the matter of letter-writing--as if that consideration were of moment in such a state of circumstances as was now existing. But at last his thoughts brought themselves to the real question at issue. Would Lily Dale accept him? After all, the realization of his good fortune depended altogether upon her feelings; and, as he remembered this, his mind misgave him sorely. It was filled not only with a young lover's ordinary doubts,--with the fear and trembling incidental to the bashfulness of hobbledehoyhood--but with an idea that that affair with Crosbie would still stand in his way. He did not, perhaps, rightly understand all that Lily had suffered, but he conceived it to be probable that there had been wounds which even the last five months might not yet have cured. Could it be that she would allow him to cure these wounds? As he thought of this he felt almost crushed to the earth by an indomitable bashfulness and conviction of his own unworthiness. What had he to offer worthy of the acceptance of such a girl as Lilian Dale?\n\nI fear that the Crown did not get out of John Eames an adequate return for his salary on that day. So adequate, however, had been the return given by him for some time past, that promotion was supposed throughout the Income-tax Office to be coming in his way, much to the jealousy of Cradell, Fisher, and others, his immediate compeers and cronies. And the place assigned to him by rumour was one which was generally regarded as a perfect Elysium upon earth in the Civil Service world. He was, so rumour said, to become private secretary to the First Commissioner. He would be removed by such a change as this from the large uncarpeted room in which he at present sat; occupying the same desk with another man to whom he had felt himself to be ignominiously bound, as dogs must feel when they are coupled. This room had been the bear-garden of the office. Twelve or fourteen men sat in it. Large pewter pots were brought into it daily at one o'clock, giving it an air that was not aristocratic. The senior of the room, one Mr. Love, who was presumed to have it under his immediate dominion, was a clerk of the ancient stamp, dull, heavy, unambitious, living out on the farther side of Islington, and unknown beyond the limits of his office to any of his younger brethren. He was generally regarded as having given a bad tone to the room. And then the clerks in this room would not unfrequently be blown up,--with very palpable blowings up,--by an official swell, a certain chief clerk, named Kissing, much higher in standing though younger in age than the gentleman of whom we have before spoken. He would hurry in, out of his own neighbouring chamber, with quick step and nose in the air, shuffling in his office slippers, looking on each occasion as though there were some cause to fear that the whole Civil Service were coming to an abrupt termination, and would lay about him with hard words, which some of those in the big room did not find it very easy to bear. His hair was always brushed straight up, his eyes were always very wide open,--and he usually carried a big letter-book with him, keeping in it a certain place with his finger. This book was almost too much for his strength, and he would flop it down, now on this man's desk and now on that man's, and in a long career of such floppings had made himself to be very much hated. On the score of some old grudge he and Mr. Love did not speak to each other; and for this reason, on all occasions of fault-finding, the blown-up young man would refer Mr. Kissing to his enemy.\n\n\"I know nothing about it,\" Mr. Love would say, not lifting his face from his desk for a moment.\n\n\"I shall certainly lay the matter before the Board,\" Mr. Kissing would reply, and would then shuffle out of the room with the big book.\n\nSometimes Mr. Kissing would lay the matter before the Board, and then he, and Mr. Love, and two or three delinquent clerks would be summoned thither. It seldom led to much. The delinquent clerks would be cautioned. One Commissioner would say a word in private to Mr. Love, and another a word in private to Mr. Kissing. Then, when left alone, the Commissioners would have their little jokes, saying that Kissing, they feared, went by favour; and that Love should still be lord of all. But these things were done in the mild days, before Sir Raffle Buffle came to the Board.\n\nThere had been some fun in this at first; but of late John Eames had become tired of it. He disliked Mr. Kissing, and the big book out of which Mr. Kissing was always endeavouring to convict him of some official sin, and had got tired of that joke of setting Kissing and Love by the ears together. When the Assistant Secretary first suggested to him that Sir Raffle had an idea of selecting him as private secretary, and when he remembered the cosy little room, all carpeted, with a leathern arm-chair and a separate washing-stand, which in such case would be devoted to his use, and remembered also that he would be put into receipt of an additional hundred a year, and would stand in the way of still better promotion, he was overjoyed. But there were certain drawbacks. The present private secretary,--who had been private secretary also to the late First Commissioner,--was giving up his Elysium because he could not endure the tones of Sir Raffle's voice. It was understood that Sir Raffle required rather more of a private secretary, in the way of obsequious attendance, than was desirable, and Eames almost doubted his own fitness for the place.\n\n\"And why should he choose me?\" he had asked the Assistant Secretary.\n\n\"Well, we have talked it over together, and I think that he prefers you to any other that has been named.\"\n\n\"But he was so very hard upon me about the affair at the railway station.\"\n\n\"I think he has heard more about that since; I think that some message has reached him from your friend, Earl De Guest.\"\n\n\"Oh, indeed!\" said Johnny, beginning to comprehend what it was to have an earl for his friend. Since his acquaintance with the nobleman had commenced, he had studiously avoided all mention of the earl's name at his office; and yet he received almost daily intimation that the fact was well known there, and not a little considered.\n\n\"But he is so very rough,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"You can put up with that,\" said his friend the Assistant Secretary. \"His bark is worse than his bite, as you know; and then a hundred a year is worth having.\" Eames was at that moment inclined to take a gloomy view of life in general, and was disposed to refuse the place, should it be offered to him. He had not then received the earl's letter; but now, as he sat with that letter open before him, lying in the drawer beneath his desk so that he could still read it as he leaned back in his chair, he was enabled to look at things in general through a different atmosphere. In the first place, Lilian Dale's husband ought to have a room to himself, with a carpet and an arm-chair; and then that additional hundred a year would raise his income at once to the sum as to which the earl had made some sort of stipulation. But could he get that leave of absence at Easter? If he consented to be Sir Raffle's private secretary, he would make that a part of the bargain.\n\nAt this moment the door of the big room was opened, and Mr. Kissing shuffled in with very quick little steps. He shuffled in, and coming direct up to John's desk, flopped his ledger down upon it before its owner had had time to close the drawer which contained the precious letter.\n\n\"What have you got in that drawer, Mr. Eames?\"\n\n\"A private letter, Mr. Kissing.\"\n\n\"Oh;--a private letter!\" said Mr. Kissing, feeling strongly convinced there was a novel hidden there, but not daring to express his belief. \"I have been half the morning, Mr. Eames, looking for this letter to the Admiralty, and you've put it under S!\" A bystander listening to Mr. Kissing's tone would have been led to believe that the whole Income-tax Office was jeopardized by the terrible iniquity thus disclosed.\n\n\"Somerset House,\" pleaded Johnny.\n\n\"Psha;--Somerset House! Half the offices in London--\"\n\n\"You'd better ask Mr. Love,\" said Eames. \"It's all done under his special instructions.\" Mr. Kissing looked at Mr. Love, and Mr. Love looked steadfastly at his desk. \"Mr. Love knows all about the indexing,\" continued Johnny. \"He's index master general to the department.\"\n\n\"No, I'm not, Mr. Eames,\" said Mr. Love, who rather liked John Eames, and hated Mr. Kissing with his whole heart. \"But I believe the indexes, on the whole, are very well done in this room. Some people don't know how to find letters.\"\n\n\"Mr. Eames,\" began Mr. Kissing, still pointing with a finger of bitter reproach to the misused S, and beginning an oration which was intended for the benefit of the whole room, and for the annihilation of old Mr. Love, \"if you have yet to learn that the word Admiralty begins with A and not with S, you have much to learn which should have been acquired before you first came into this office. Somerset House is not a department.\" Then he turned round to the room at large, and repeated the last words, as though they might become very useful if taken well to heart--\"Is not a department. The Treasury is a department; the Home Office is a department; the India Board is a department--\"\n\n\"No, Mr. Kissing, it isn't,\" said a young clerk from the other end of the room.\n\n\"You know very well what I mean, sir. The India Office is a department.\"\n\n\"There's no Board, sir.\"\n\n\"Never mind; but how any gentleman who has been in the service three months,--not to say three years,--can suppose Somerset House to be a department, is beyond my comprehension. If you have been improperly instructed--\"\n\n\"We shall know all about it another time,\" said Eames. \"Mr. Love will make a memorandum of it.\"\n\n\"I shan't do anything of the kind,\" said Mr. Love.\n\n\"If you have been wrongly instructed,--\" Mr. Kissing began again, stealing a glance at Mr. Love as he did so; but at this moment the door was again opened, and a messenger summoned Johnny to the presence of the really great man. \"Mr. Eames, to wait upon Sir Raffle.\" Upon hearing this Johnny immediately started, and left Mr. Kissing and the big book in possession of his desk. How the battle was waged, and how it raged in the large room, we cannot stop to hear, as it is necessary that we should follow our hero into the presence of Sir Raffle Buffle.\n\n\"Ah, Eames,--yes,\" said Sir Raffle, looking up from his desk when the young man entered; \"just wait half a minute, will you?\" And the knight went to work at his papers, as though fearing that any delay in what he was doing might be very prejudicial to the nation at large. \"Ah, Eames,--well,--yes,\" he said again, as he pushed away from him, almost with a jerk, the papers on which he had been writing. \"They tell me that you know the business of this office pretty well.\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir,\" said Eames.\n\n\"Well, yes; some of it. But you'll have to understand the whole of it if you come to me. And you must be very sharp about it too. You know that FitzHoward is leaving me?\"\n\n\"I have heard of it, sir.\"\n\n\"A very excellent young man, though perhaps not-- But we won't mind that. The work is a little too much for him, and he's going back into the office. I believe Lord De Guest is a friend of yours; isn't he?\"\n\n\"Yes; he is a friend of mine, certainly. He's been very kind to me.\"\n\n\"Ah, well. I've known the earl for many years,--for very many years; and intimately at one time. Perhaps you may have heard him mention my name?\"\n\n\"Yes, I have, Sir Raffle.\"\n\n\"We were intimate once, but those things go off, you know. He's been the country mouse and I've been the town mouse. Ha, ha, ha! You may tell him that I say so. He won't mind that coming from me.\"\n\n\"Oh, no; not at all,\" said Eames.\n\n\"Mind you tell him when you see him. The earl is a man for whom I've always had a great respect,--a very great respect,--I may say regard. And now, Eames, what do you say to taking FitzHoward's place? The work is hard. It is fair that I should tell you that. The work will, no doubt, be very hard. I take a greater share of what's going than my predecessors have done; and I don't mind telling you that I have been sent here, because a man was wanted who would do that.\" The voice of Sir Raffle, as he continued, became more and more harsh, and Eames began to think how wise FitzHoward had been. \"I mean to do my duty, and I shall expect that my private secretary will do his. But, Mr. Eames, I never forget a man. Whether he be good or bad, I never forget a man. You don't dislike late hours, I suppose.\"\n\n\"Coming late to the office, you mean? Oh, no, not in the least.\"\n\n\"Staying late,--staying late. Six or seven o'clock if necessary,--putting your shoulder to the wheel when the coach gets into the mud. That's what I've been doing all my life. They've known what I am very well. They've always kept me for the heavy roads. If they paid, in the Civil Service, by the hour, I believe I should have drawn a larger income than any man in it. If you take the vacant chair in the next room you'll find it's no joke. It's only fair that I should tell you that.\"\n\n\"I can work as hard as any man,\" said Eames.\n\n\"That's right. That's right. Stick to that and I'll stick to you. It will be a great gratification to me to have by me a friend of my old friend De Guest. Tell him I say so. And now you may as well get into harness at once. FitzHoward is there. You can go in to him, and at half-past four exactly I'll see you both. I'm very exact, mind,--very;--and therefore you must be exact.\" Then Sir Raffle looked as though he desired to be left alone.\n\n\"Sir Raffle, there's one favour I want to ask of you,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"And what's that?\"\n\n\"I am most anxious to be absent for a fortnight or three weeks, just at Easter. I shall want to go in about ten days.\"\n\n\"Absent for three weeks at Easter, when the parliamentary work is beginning! That won't do for a private secretary.\"\n\n\"But it's very important, Sir Raffle.\"\n\n\"Out of the question, Eames; quite out of the question.\"\n\n\"It's almost life and death to me.\"\n\n\"Almost life and death. Why, what are you going to do?\" With all his grandeur and national importance, Sir Raffle would be very curious as to little people.\n\n\"Well, I can't exactly tell you, and I'm not quite sure myself.\"\n\n\"Then don't talk nonsense. It's impossible that I should spare my private secretary just at that time of the year. I couldn't do it. The service won't admit of it. You're not entitled to leave at that season. Private secretaries always take their leave in the autumn.\"\n\n\"I should like to be absent in the autumn too, but--\"\n\n\"It's out of the question, Mr. Eames.\"\n\nThen John Eames reflected that it behoved him in such an emergency to fire off his big gun. He had a great dislike to firing this big gun, but, as he said to himself, there are occasions which make a big gun very necessary. \"I got a letter from Lord De Guest this morning, pressing me very much to go to him at Easter. It's about business,\" added Johnny. \"If there was any difficulty, he said, he should write to you.\"\n\n\"Write to me,\" said Sir Raffle, who did not like to be approached too familiarly in his office, even by an earl.\n\n\"Of course I shouldn't tell him to do that. But, Sir Raffle, if I remained out there, in the office,\" and Johnny pointed towards the big room with his head, \"I could choose April for my month. And as the matter is so important to me, and to the earl--\"\n\n\"What can it be?\" said Sir Raffle.\n\n\"It's quite private,\" said John Eames.\n\nHereupon Sir Raffle became very petulant, feeling that a bargain was being made with him. This young man would only consent to become his private secretary upon certain terms! \"Well, go in to FitzHoward now. I can't lose all my day in this way.\"\n\n\"But I shall be able to get away at Easter?\"\n\n\"I don't know. We shall see about it. But don't stand talking there now.\" Then John Eames went into FitzHoward's room, and received that gentleman's congratulations on his appointment. \"I hope you like being rung for, like a servant, every minute, for he's always ringing that bell. And he'll roar at you till you're deaf. You must give up all dinner engagements, for though there is not much to do, he'll never let you go. I don't think anybody ever asks him out to dinner, for he likes being here till seven. And you'll have to write all manner of lies about big people. And, sometimes, when he has sent Rafferty out about his private business, he'll ask you to bring him his shoes.\" Now Rafferty was the First Commissioner's messenger.\n\nIt must be remembered, however, that this little account was given by an outgoing and discomfited private secretary. \"A man is not asked to bring another man his shoes,\" said Eames to himself, \"until he shows himself fit for that sort of business.\" Then he made within his own breast a little resolution about Sir Raffle's shoes.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII. THE NEW PRIVATE SECRETARY.\n\nIncome-tax Office, April 8, 18--.\n\nI hardly know how to answer your letter, it is so very kind--more than kind. And about not writing before,--I must explain that I have not liked to trouble you with letters. I should have seemed to be encroaching if I had written much. Indeed it didn't come from not thinking about you. And first of all, about the money,--as to your offer, I mean. I really feel that I do not know what I ought to say to you about it, without appearing to be a simpleton. The truth is, I don't know what I ought to do, and can only trust to you not to put me wrong. I have an idea that a man ought not to accept a present of money, unless from his father, or somebody like that. And the sum you mention is so very large that it makes me wish you had not named it. If you choose to be so generous, would it not be better that you should leave it me in your will?\n\n\"So that he might always want me to be dying,\" said Lord De Guest, as he read the letter out loud to his sister.\n\n\"I'm sure he wouldn't want that,\" said Lady Julia. \"But you may live for twenty-five years, you know.\"\n\n\"Say fifty,\" said the earl. And then he continued the reading of his letter.\n\nBut all that depends so much upon another person, that it is hardly worth while talking about it. Of course I am very much obliged to Mr. Dale,--very much indeed,--and I think that he is behaving very handsomely to his niece. But whether it will do me any good, that is quite another thing. However, I shall certainly accept your kind invitation for Easter, and find out whether I have a chance or not. I must tell you that Sir Raffle Buffle has made me his private secretary, by which I get a hundred a year. He says he was a great crony of yours many years ago, and seems to like talking about you very much. You will understand what all that means. He has sent you ever so many messages, but I don't suppose you will care to get them. I am to go to him to-morrow, and from all I hear I shall have a hard time of it.\n\n\"By George, he will,\" said the earl. \"Poor fellow!\"\n\n\"But I thought a private secretary never had anything to do,\" said Lady Julia.\n\n\"I shouldn't like to be private secretary to Sir Raffle, myself. But he's young, and a hundred a year is a great thing. How we all of us used to hate that man. His voice sounded like a bell with a crack in it. We always used to be asking for some one to muffle the Buffle. They call him Huffle Scuffle at his office. Poor Johnny!\" Then he finished the letter:--\n\nI told him that I must have leave of absence at Easter, and he at first declared that it was impossible. But I shall carry my point about that. I would not stay away to be made private secretary to the Prime Minister; and yet I almost feel that I might as well stay away for any good that I shall do.\n\nGive my kind regards to Lady Julia, and tell her how very much obliged to her I am. I cannot express the gratitude which I owe to you. But pray believe me, my dear Lord De Guest, always very faithfully yours,\n\nIt was late before Eames had finished his letter. He had been making himself ready for his exodus from the big room, and preparing his desk and papers for his successor. About half-past five Cradell came up to him, and suggested that they should walk home together.\n\n\"What! you here still?\" said Eames. \"I thought you always went at four.\" Cradell had remained, hanging about the office, in order that he might walk home with the new private secretary. But Eames did not desire this. He had much of which he desired to think alone, and would fain have been allowed to walk by himself.\n\n\"Yes; I had things to do. I say, Johnny, I congratulate you most heartily; I do, indeed.\"\n\n\"Thank you, old fellow!\"\n\n\"It is such a grand thing, you know. A hundred a year and all at once! And then such a snug room to yourself,--and that fellow, Kissing, never can come near you. He has been making himself such a beast all day. But, Johnny, I always knew you'd come to something more than common. I always said so.\"\n\n\"There's nothing uncommon about this; except that Fitz says that old Huffle Scuffle makes himself uncommon nasty.\"\n\n\"Never mind what Fitz says. It's all jealousy. You'll have it all your own way, if you look sharp. I think you always do have it all your own way. Are you nearly ready?\"\n\n\"Well,--not quite. Don't wait for me, Caudle.\"\n\n\"Oh, I'll wait. I don't mind waiting. They'll keep dinner for us if we both stay. Besides, what matters? I'd do more than that for you.\"\n\n\"I have some idea of working on till eight, and having a chop sent in,\" said Johnny. \"Besides--I've got somewhere to call, by myself.\"\n\nThen Cradell almost cried. He remained silent for two or three minutes, striving to master his emotion; and at last, when he did speak, had hardly succeeded in doing so. \"Oh, Johnny,\" he said, \"I know what that means. You are going to throw me over because you are getting up in the world. I have always stuck to you, through everything; haven't I?\"\n\n\"Don't make yourself a fool, Caudle.\"\n\n\"Well; so I have. And if they had made me private secretary, I should have been just the same to you as ever. You'd have found no change in me.\"\n\n\"What a goose you are. Do you say I'm changed, because I want to dine in the city?\"\n\n\"It's all because you don't want to walk home with me, as we used to do. I'm not such a goose but what I can see. But, Johnny-- I suppose I mustn't call you Johnny, now.\"\n\n\"Don't be such a--con-founded--\" Then Eames got up, and walked about the room. \"Come along,\" said he, \"I don't care about staying, and don't mind where I dine.\" And he bustled away with his hat and gloves, hardly giving Cradell time to catch him before he got out into the streets. \"I tell you what it is, Caudle,\" said he, \"all that kind of thing is disgusting.\"\n\n\"But how would you feel,\" whimpered Cradell, who had never succeeded in putting himself quite on a par with his friend, even in his own estimation, since that glorious victory at the railway station. If he could only have thrashed Lupex as Johnny had thrashed Crosbie; then indeed they might have been equal,--a pair of heroes. But he had not done so. He had never told himself that he was a coward, but he considered that circumstances had been specially unkind to him. \"But how would you feel,\" he whimpered, \"if the friend whom you liked better than anybody else in the world, turned his back upon you?\"\n\n\"I haven't turned my back upon you; except that I can't get you to walk fast enough. Come along, old fellow, and don't talk confounded nonsense. I hate all that kind of thing. You never ought to suppose that a man will give himself airs, but wait till he does. I don't believe I shall remain with old Scuffles above a month or two. From all that I can hear that's as much as any one can bear.\"\n\nThen Cradell by degrees became happy and cordial, and during the whole walk flattered Eames with all the flattery of which he was master. And Johnny, though he did profess himself to be averse to \"all that kind of thing,\" was nevertheless open to flattery. When Cradell told him that though FitzHoward could not manage the Tartar knight, he might probably do so; he was inclined to believe what Cradell said. \"And as to getting him his shoes,\" said Cradell, \"I don't suppose he'd ever think of asking you to do such a thing, unless he was in a very great hurry, or something of that kind.\"\n\n\"Look here, Johnny,\" said Cradell, as they got into one of the streets bordering on Burton Crescent, \"you know the last thing in the world I should like to do would be to offend you.\"\n\n\"All right, Caudle,\" said Eames, going on, whereas his companion had shown a tendency towards stopping.\n\n\"Look here, now; if I have vexed you about Amelia Roper, I'll make you a promise never to speak to her again.\"\n\n\"D---- Amelia Roper,\" said Eames, suddenly stopping himself and stopping Cradell as well. The exclamation was made in a deep angry voice which attracted the notice of one or two who were passing. Johnny was very wrong,--wrong to utter any curse;--very wrong to ejaculate that curse against a human being; and especially wrong to fulminate it against a woman--a woman whom he had professed to love! But he did do so, and I cannot tell my story thoroughly without repeating the wicked word.\n\nCradell looked up at him and stared. \"I only meant to say,\" said Cradell, \"I'll do anything you like in the matter.\"\n\n\"Then never mention her name to me again. And as to talking to her, you may talk to her till you're both blue in the face, if you please.\"\n\n\"Oh;--I didn't know. You didn't seem to like it the other day.\"\n\n\"I was a fool the other day,--a confounded fool. And so I have been all my life. Amelia Roper! Look here, Caudle; if she makes up to you this evening, as I've no doubt she will, for she seems to be playing that game constantly now, just let her have her fling. Never mind me; I'll amuse myself with Mrs. Lupex, or Miss Spruce.\"\n\n\"But there'll be the deuce to pay with Mrs. Lupex. She's as cross as possible already whenever Amelia speaks to me. You don't know what a jealous woman is, Johnny.\" Cradell had got upon what he considered to be his high ground. And on that he felt himself equal to any man. It was no doubt true that Eames had thrashed a man, and that he had not; it was true also that Eames had risen to very high place in the social world, having become a private secretary; but for a dangerous, mysterious, overwhelming, life-enveloping intrigue--was not he the acknowledged hero of such an affair? He had paid very dearly, both in pocket and in comfort, for the blessing of Mrs. Lupex's society; but he hardly considered that he had paid too dearly. There are certain luxuries which a man will find to be expensive; but, for all that, they may be worth their price. Nevertheless as he went up the steps of Mrs. Roper's house he made up his mind that he would oblige his friend. The intrigue might in that way become more mysterious, and more life-enveloping; whereas it would not become more dangerous, seeing that Mr. Lupex could hardly find himself to be aggrieved by such a proceeding.\n\nThe whole number of Mrs. Roper's boarders were assembled at dinner that day. Mr. Lupex seldom joined that festive board, but on this occasion he was present, appearing from his voice and manner to be in high good-humour. Cradell had communicated to the company in the drawing-room the great good fortune which had fallen upon his friend, and Johnny had thereby become the mark of a certain amount of hero-worship.\n\n\"Oh, indeed!\" said Mrs. Roper. \"An 'appy woman your mother will be when she hears it. But I always said you'd come down right side uppermost.\"\n\n\"Handsome is as handsome does,\" said Miss Spruce.\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Eames!\" exclaimed Mrs. Lupex, with graceful enthusiasm, \"I wish you joy from the very depth of my heart. It is such an elegant appointment.\"\n\n\"Accept the hand of a true and disinterested friend,\" said Lupex. And Johnny did accept the hand, though it was very dirty and stained all over with paint.\n\nAmelia stood apart and conveyed her congratulations by a glance,--or, I might better say, by a series of glances. \"And now,--now will you not be mine,\" the glances said; \"now that you are rolling in wealth and prosperity?\" And then before they went downstairs she did whisper one word to him. \"Oh, I am so happy, John;--so very happy.\"\n\n\"Bother!\" said Johnny, in a tone quite loud enough to reach the lady's ear. Then making his way round the room, he gave his arm to Miss Spruce. Amelia, as she walked downstairs alone, declared to herself that she would wring his heart. She had been employed in wringing it for some days past, and had been astonished at her own success. It had been clear enough to her that Eames had been piqued by her overtures to Cradell, and she had therefore to play out that game.\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Cradell,\" she said, as she took her seat next to him. \"The friends I like are the friends that remain always the same. I hate your sudden rises. They do so often make a man upsetting.\"\n\n\"I should like to try, myself, all the same,\" said Cradell.\n\n\"Well, I don't think it would make any difference in you; I don't indeed. And of course your time will come too. It's that earl as has done it,--he that was worried by the bull. Since we have known an earl we have been so mighty fine.\" And Amelia gave her head a little toss, and then smiled archly, in a manner which, to Cradell's eyes, was really very becoming. But he saw that Mrs. Lupex was looking at him from the other side of the table, and he could not quite enjoy the goods which the gods had provided for him.\n\nWhen the ladies left the dining-room Lupex and the two young men drew their chairs near the fire, and each prepared for himself a moderate potation. Eames made a little attempt at leaving the room, but he was implored by Lupex with such earnest protestations of friendship to remain, and was so weakly fearful of being charged with giving himself airs, that he did as he was desired.\n\n\"And here, Mr. Eames, is to your very good health,\" said Lupex, raising to his mouth a steaming goblet of gin-and-water, \"and wishing you many years to enjoy your official prosperity.\"\n\n\"Thank ye,\" said Eames. \"I don't know much about the prosperity, but I'm just as much obliged.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir; when I see a young man of your age beginning to rise in the world, I know he'll go on. Now look at me, Mr. Eames. Mr. Cradell, here's your very good health, and may all unkindness be drowned in the flowing bowl-- Look at me, Mr. Eames. I've never risen in the world; I've never done any good in the world, and never shall.\"\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Lupex, don't say that.\"\n\n\"Ah, but I do say it. I've always been pulling the devil by the tail, and never yet got as much as a good hold on to that. And I'll tell you why; I never got a chance when I was young. If I could have got any big fellow, a star, you know, to let me paint his portrait when I was your age,--such a one, let us say, as your friend Sir Raffle--\"\n\n\"What a star!\" said Cradell.\n\n\"Well, I suppose he's pretty much known in the world, isn't he? Or Lord Derby, or Mr. Spurgeon. You know what I mean. If I'd got such a chance as that when I was young, I should never have been doing jobs of scene-painting at the minor theatres at so much a square yard. You've got the chance now, but I never had it.\"\n\nWhereupon Mr. Lupex finished his first measure of gin-and-water.\n\n\"It's a very queer thing,--life is,\" continued Lupex; and, though he did not at once go to work boldly at the mixing of another glass of toddy, he began gradually, and as if by instinct, to finger the things which would be necessary for that operation. \"A very queer thing. Now, remember, young gentlemen, I'm not denying that success in life will depend upon good conduct;--of course it does; but, then, how often good conduct comes from success! Should I have been what I am now, do you suppose, if some big fellow had taken me by the hand when I was struggling to make an artist of myself? I could have drunk claret and champagne just as well as gin-and-water, and worn ruffles to my shirt as gracefully as many a fellow who used to be very fond of me, and now won't speak to me if he meets me in the streets. I never got a chance,--never.\"\n\n\"But it's not too late yet, Mr. Lupex,\" said Eames.\n\n\"Yes, it is, Eames,--yes, it is.\" And now Mr. Lupex had grasped the gin-bottle. \"It's too late now. The game's over, and the match is lost. The talent is here. I'm as sure of that now as ever I was. I've never doubted my own ability,--never for a moment. There are men this very day making a thousand a year off their easels who haven't so good and true an eye in drawing as I have, or so good a feeling in colours. I could name them; only I won't.\"\n\n\"And why shouldn't you try again?\" said Eames.\n\n\"If I were to paint the finest piece that ever delighted the eye of man, who would come and look at it? Who would have enough belief in me to come as far as this place and see if it were true? No, Eames; I know my own position and my own ways, and I know my own weakness. I couldn't do a day's work now, unless I were certain of getting a certain number of shillings at the end of it. That's what a man comes to when things have gone against him.\"\n\n\"But I thought men got lots of money by scene-painting?\"\n\n\"I don't know what you may call lots, Mr. Cradell; I don't call it lots. But I'm not complaining. I know who I have to thank; and if ever I blow my own brains out I shan't be putting the blame on the wrong shoulders. If you'll take my advice,\"--and now he turned round to Eames,--\"you'll beware of marrying too soon in life.\"\n\n\"I think a man should marry early, if he marries well,\" said Eames.\n\n\"Don't misunderstand me,\" continued Lupex. \"It isn't about Mrs. L. I'm speaking. I've always regarded my wife as a very fascinating woman.\"\n\n\"Hear, hear, hear!\" said Cradell, thumping the table.\n\n\"Indeed she is,\" said Eames.\n\n\"And when I caution you against marrying, don't you misunderstand me. I've never said a word against her to any man, and never will. If a man don't stand by his wife, whom will he stand by? I blame no one but myself. But I do say this; I never had a chance;--I never had a chance;--never had a chance.\" And as he repeated the words for the third time, his lips were already fixed to the rim of his tumbler.\n\nAt this moment the door of the dining-room was opened, and Mrs. Lupex put in her head.\n\n\"Lupex,\" she said, \"what are you doing?\"\n\n\"Yes, my dear. I can't say I'm doing anything at the present moment. I was giving a little advice to these young gentlemen.\"\n\n\"Mr. Cradell, I wonder at you. And, Mr. Eames, I wonder at you, too,--in your position! Lupex, come upstairs at once.\" She then stepped into the room and secured the gin-bottle.\n\n\"Oh, Mr. Cradell, do come here,\" said Amelia, in her liveliest tone, as soon as the men made their appearance above. \"I've been waiting for you this half-hour. I've got such a puzzle for you.\" And she made way for him to a chair which was between herself and the wall. Cradell looked half afraid of his fortunes as he took the proffered seat; but he did take it, and was soon secured from any positive physical attack by the strength and breadth of Miss Roper's crinoline.\n\n\"Dear me! Here's a change,\" said Mrs. Lupex, out loud.\n\nJohnny Eames was standing close, and whispered into her ear, \"Changes are so pleasant sometimes! Don't you think so? I do.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII. NEMESIS.\n\nCrosbie had now settled down to the calm realities of married life, and was beginning to think that the odium was dying away which for a week or two had attached itself to him, partly on account of his usage of Miss Dale, but more strongly in consequence of the thrashing which he had received from John Eames. Not that he had in any way recovered his former tone of life, or that he ever hoped to do so. But he was able to go in and out of his club without embarrassment. He could talk with his wonted voice, and act with his wonted authority at his office. He could tell his friends, with some little degree of pleasure in the sound, that Lady Alexandrina would be very happy to see them. And he could make himself comfortable in his own chair after dinner, with his slippers and his newspaper. He could make himself comfortable, or at any rate could tell his wife that he did so.\n\nIt was very dull. He was obliged to acknowledge to himself, when he thought over the subject, that the life which he was leading was dull. Though he could go into his club without annoyance, nobody there ever thought of asking him to join them at dinner. It was taken for granted that he was going to dine at home; and in the absence of any provocation to the contrary, he always did dine at home. He had now been in his house for three weeks, and had been asked with his wife to a few bridal dinner-parties, given chiefly by friends of the De Courcy family. Except on such occasions he never passed an evening out of his own house, and had not yet, since his marriage, dined once away from his wife. He told himself that his good conduct in this respect was the result of his own resolution; but, nevertheless, he felt that there was nothing else left for him to do. Nobody asked him to go to the theatre. Nobody begged him to drop in of an evening. Men never asked him why he did not play a rubber. He would generally saunter into Sebright's after he left his office, and lounge about the room for half an hour, talking to a few men. Nobody was uncivil to him. But he knew that the whole thing was changed, and he resolved, with some wisdom, to accommodate himself to his altered circumstances.\n\nLady Alexandrina also found her new life rather dull, and was sometimes inclined to be a little querulous. She would tell her husband that she never got out, and would declare, when he offered to walk with her, that she did not care for walking in the streets. \"I don't exactly see, then, where you are to walk,\" he once replied. She did not tell him that she was fond of riding, and that the Park was a very fitting place for such exercise; but she looked it, and he understood her. \"I'll do all I can for her,\" he said to himself; \"but I'll not ruin myself.\" \"Amelia is coming to take me for a drive,\" she said another time. \"Ah, that'll be very nice,\" he answered. \"No; it won't be very nice,\" said Alexandrina. \"Amelia is always shopping and bargaining with the tradespeople. But it will be better than being kept in the house without ever stirring out.\"\n\nThey breakfasted nominally at half-past nine; in truth, it was always nearly ten, as Lady Alexandrina found it difficult to get herself out of her room. At half-past ten punctually he left his house for his office. He usually got home by six, and then spent the greatest part of the hour before dinner in the ceremony of dressing. He went, at least, into his dressing-room, after speaking a few words to his wife, and there remained, pulling things about, clipping his nails, looking over any paper that came in his way, and killing the time. He expected his dinner punctually at seven, and began to feel a little cross if he were kept waiting. After dinner, he drank one glass of wine in company with his wife, and one other by himself, during which latter ceremony he would stare at the hot coals, and think of the thing he had done. Then he would go upstairs, and have, first a cup of coffee, and then a cup of tea. He would read his newspaper, open a book or two, hide his face when he yawned, and try to make believe that he liked it. She had no signs or words of love for him. She never sat on his knee, or caressed him. She never showed him that any happiness had come to her in being allowed to live close to him. They thought that they loved each other:--each thought so; but there was no love, no sympathy, no warmth. The very atmosphere was cold;--so cold that no fire could remove the chill.\n\nIn what way would it have been different had Lily Dale sat opposite to him there as his wife, instead of Lady Alexandrina? He told himself frequently that either with one or with the other life would have been the same; that he had made himself for a while unfit for domestic life, and that he must cure himself of that unfitness. But though he declared this to himself in one set of half-spoken thoughts, he would also declare to himself in another set, that Lily would have made the whole house bright with her brightness; that had he brought her home to his hearth, there would have been a sun shining on him every morning and every evening. But, nevertheless, he strove to do his duty, and remembered that the excitement of official life was still open to him. From eleven in the morning till five in the afternoon he could still hold a position which made it necessary that men should regard him with respect, and speak to him with deference. In this respect he was better off than his wife, for she had no office to which she could betake herself.\n\n\"Yes,\" she said to Amelia, \"it is all very nice, and I don't mind the house being damp; but I get so tired of being alone.\"\n\n\"That must be the case with women who are married to men of business.\"\n\n\"Oh, I don't complain. Of course I knew what I was about. I suppose it won't be so very dull when everybody is up in London.\"\n\n\"I don't find the season makes much difference to us after Christmas,\" said Amelia; \"but no doubt London is gayer in May. You'll find you'll like it better next year; and perhaps you'll have a baby, you know.\"\n\n\"Psha!\" ejaculated Lady Alexandrina; \"I don't want a baby, and don't suppose I shall have one.\"\n\n\"It's always something to do, you know.\"\n\nLady Alexandrina, though she was not of an energetic temperament, could not but confess to herself that she had made a mistake. She had been tempted to marry Crosbie because Crosbie was a man of fashion, and now she was told that the London season would make no difference to her;--the London season which had hitherto always brought to her the excitement of parties, if it had not given her the satisfaction of amusement. She had been tempted to marry because it appeared to her that a married woman could enjoy society with less restraint than a girl who was subject to her mother or her chaperon; that she would have more freedom of action as a married woman; and now she was told that she must wait for a baby before she could have anything to do. Courcy Castle was sometimes dull, but Courcy Castle would have been better than this.\n\nWhen Crosbie returned home after this little conversation about the baby, he was told by his wife that they were to dine with the Gazebees on the next Sunday. On hearing this he shook his head with vexation. He knew, however, that he had no right to make complaint, as he had been only taken to St. John's Wood once since they had come home from their marriage trip. There was, however, one point as to which he could grumble. \"Why, on earth, on Sunday?\"\n\n\"Because Amelia asked me for Sunday. If you are asked for Sunday, you cannot say you'll go on Monday.\"\n\n\"It is so terrible on a Sunday afternoon. At what hour?\"\n\n\"She said half-past five.\"\n\n\"Heavens and earth! What are we to do all the evening?\"\n\n\"It is not kind of you, Adolphus, to speak in that way of my relations.\"\n\n\"Come, my love, that's a joke; as if I hadn't heard you say the same thing twenty times. You've complained of having to go up there much more bitterly than I ever did. You know I like your sister, and, in his way, Gazebee is a very good fellow; but after three or four hours, one begins to have had enough of him.\"\n\n\"It can't be much duller than it is--;\" but Lady Alexandrina stopped herself before she finished her speech.\n\n\"One can always read at home, at any rate,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"One can't always be reading. However, I have said you would go. If you choose to refuse, you must write and explain.\"\n\nWhen the Sunday came the Crosbies of course did go to St. John's Wood, arriving punctually at that door which he so hated at half-past five. One of the earliest resolutions which he made when he first contemplated the De Courcy match, was altogether hostile to the Gazebees. He would see but very little of them. He would shake himself free of that connexion. It was not with that branch of the family that he desired an alliance. But now, as things had gone, that was the only branch of the family with which he seemed to be allied. He was always hearing of the Gazebees. Amelia and Alexandrina were constantly together. He was now dragged there to a Sunday dinner; and he knew that he should often be dragged there,--that he could not avoid such draggings. He already owed money to Mortimer Gazebee, and was aware that his affairs had been allowed to fall into that lawyer's hands in such a way that he could not take them out again. His house was very thoroughly furnished, and he knew that the bills had been paid; but he had not paid them; every shilling had been paid through Mortimer Gazebee.\n\n\"Go with your mother and aunt, De Courcy,\" the attorney said to the lingering child after dinner; and then Crosbie was left alone with his wife's brother-in-law. This was the period of the St. John's Wood purgatory which was so dreadful to him. With his sister-in-law he could talk, remembering perhaps always that she was an earl's daughter. But with Gazebee he had nothing in common. And he felt that Gazebee, who had once treated him with great deference, had now lost all such feeling. Crosbie had once been a man of fashion in the estimation of the attorney, but that was all over. Crosbie, in the attorney's estimation, was now simply the secretary of a public office,--a man who owed him money. The two had married sisters, and there was no reason why the light of the prosperous attorney should pale before that of the civil servant, who was not very prosperous. All this was understood thoroughly by both the men.\n\n\"There's terrible bad news from Courcy,\" said the attorney, as soon as the boy was gone.\n\n\"Why; what's the matter?\"\n\n\"Porlock has married--that woman, you know.\"\n\n\"Nonsense.\"\n\n\"He has. The old lady has been obliged to tell me, and she's nearly broken-hearted about it. But that's not the worst of it to my mind. All the world knows that Porlock had gone to the mischief. But he is going to bring an action against his father for some arrears of his allowance, and he threatens to have everything out in court, if he doesn't get his money.\"\n\n\"But is there money due to him?\"\n\n\"Yes, there is. A couple of thousand pounds or so. I suppose I shall have to find it. But, upon my honour, I don't know where it's to come from; I don't, indeed. In one way or another, I've paid over fourteen hundred pounds for you.\"\n\n\"Fourteen hundred pounds!\"\n\n\"Yes, indeed;--what with the insurance and the furniture, and the bill from our house for the settlements. That's not paid yet, but it's the same thing. A man doesn't get married for nothing, I can tell you.\"\n\n\"But you've got security.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes; I've got security. But the thing is the ready money. Our house has advanced so much on the Courcy property, that they don't like going any further; and therefore it is that I have to do this myself. They'll all have to go abroad,--that'll be the end of it. There's been such a scene between the earl and George. George lost his temper and told the earl that Porlock's marriage was his fault. It has ended in George with his wife being turned out.\"\n\n\"He has money of his own.\"\n\n\"Yes, but he won't spend it. He's coming up here, and we shall find him hanging about us. I don't mean to give him a bed here, and I advise you not to do so either. You'll not get rid of him if you do.\"\n\n\"I have the greatest possible dislike to him.\"\n\n\"Yes; he's a bad fellow. So is John. Porlock was the best, but he's gone altogether to ruin. They've made a nice mess of it between them; haven't they?\"\n\nThis was the family for whose sake Crosbie had jilted Lily Dale! His single and simple ambition had been that of being an earl's son-in-law. To achieve that it had been necessary that he should make himself a villain. In achieving it he had gone through all manner of dirt and disgrace. He had married a woman whom he knew he did not love. He was thinking almost hourly of a girl whom he had loved, whom he did love, but whom he had so injured, that, under no circumstances, could he be allowed to speak to her again. The attorney there--who sat opposite to him, talking about his thousands of pounds with that disgusting assumed solicitude which such men put on, when they know very well what they are doing--had made a similar marriage. But he had known what he was about. He had got from his marriage all that he had expected. But what had Crosbie got?\n\n\"They're a bad set,--a bad set,\" said he in his bitterness.\n\n\"The men are,\" said Gazebee, very comfortably.\n\n\"H--m,\" said Crosbie. It was manifest to Gazebee that his friend was expressing a feeling that the women, also, were not all that they should be, but he took no offence, though some portion of the censure might thereby be supposed to attach to his own wife.\n\n\"The countess means well,\" said Gazebee. \"But she's had a hard life of it,--a very hard life. I've heard him call her names that would frighten a coal-heaver. I have, indeed. But he'll die soon, and then she'll be comfortable. She has three thousand a year jointure.\"\n\nHe'll die soon, and then she'll be comfortable! That was one phase of married life. As Crosbie's mind dwelt upon the words, he remembered Lily's promise made in the fields, that she would do everything for him. He remembered her kisses; the touch of her fingers; the low silvery laughing voice; the feel of her dress as she would press close to him. After that he reflected whether it would not be well that he too should die, so that Alexandrina might be comfortable. She and her mother might be very comfortable together, with plenty of money, at Baden-Baden!\n\nThe squire at Allington, and Mrs. Dale, and Lady Julia De Guest, had been, and still were, uneasy in their minds because no punishment had fallen upon Crosbie,--no vengeance had overtaken him in consequence of his great sin. How little did they know about it! Could he have been prosecuted and put into prison, with hard labour, for twelve months, the punishment would not have been heavier. He would, in that case, at any rate, have been saved from Lady Alexandrina.\n\n\"George and his wife are coming up to town; couldn't we ask them to come to us for a week or so?\" said his wife to him, as soon as they were in the fly together, going home.\n\n\"No,\" shouted Crosbie; \"we will do no such thing.\" There was not another word said on the subject,--nor on any other subject till they got home. When they reached their house Alexandrina had a headache, and went up to her room immediately. Crosbie threw himself into a chair before the remains of a fire in the dining-room, and resolved that he would cut the whole De Courcy family altogether. His wife, as his wife, should obey him. She should obey him--or else leave him and go her way by herself, leaving him to go his way. There was an income of twelve hundred a year. Would it not be a fine thing for him if he could keep six hundred for himself and return to his old manner of life. All his old comforts of course he would not have,--nor the old esteem and regard of men. But the luxury of a club dinner he might enjoy. Unembarrassed evenings might be his,--with liberty to him to pass them as he pleased. He knew many men who were separated from their wives, and who seemed to be as happy as their neighbours. And then he remembered how ugly Alexandrina had been this evening, wearing a great tinsel coronet full of false stones, with a cold in her head which had reddened her nose. There had, too, fallen upon her in these her married days a certain fixed dreary dowdiness. She certainly was very plain! So he said to himself, and then he went to bed. I myself am inclined to think that his punishment was sufficiently severe.\n\nThe next morning his wife still complained of headache, so that he breakfasted alone. Since that positive refusal which he had given to her proposition for inviting her brother, there had not been much conversation between them. \"My head is splitting, and Sarah shall bring some tea and toast up to me, if you will not mind it.\"\n\nHe did not mind it in the least, and ate his breakfast by himself, with more enjoyment than usually attended that meal.\n\nIt was clear to him that all the present satisfaction of his life must come to him from his office work. There are men who find it difficult to live without some source of daily comfort, and he was such a man. He could hardly endure his life unless there were some page in it on which he could look with gratified eyes. He had always liked his work, and he now determined that he would like it better than ever. But in order that he might do so it was necessary that he should have much of his own way. According to the theory of his office, it was incumbent on him as Secretary simply to take the orders of the Commissioners, and see that they were executed; and to such work as this his predecessor had strictly confined himself. But he had already done more than this, and had conceived the ambition of holding the Board almost under his thumb. He flattered himself that he knew his own work and theirs better than they knew either, and that by a little management he might be their master. It is not impossible that such might have been the case had there been no fracas at the Paddington station; but, as we all know, the dominant cock of the farmyard must be ever dominant. When he shall once have had his wings so smeared with mud as to give him even the appearance of adversity, no other cock will ever respect him again. Mr. Optimist and Mr. Butterwell knew very well that their secretary had been cudgelled, and they could not submit themselves to a secretary who had been so treated.\n\n\"Oh, by-the-by, Crosbie,\" said Butterwell, coming into his room, soon after his arrival at his office on that day of his solitary breakfast, \"I want to say just a few words to you.\" And Butterwell turned round and closed the door, the lock of which had not previously been fastened. Crosbie, without much thinking, immediately foretold himself the nature of the coming conversation.\n\n\"Do you know--\" said Butterwell, beginning.\n\n\"Sit down, won't you?\" said Crosbie, seating himself as he spoke. If there was to be a contest, he would make the best fight he could. He would show a better spirit here than he had done on the railway platform. Butterwell did sit down, and felt as he did so, that the very motion of sitting took away some of his power. He ought to have sent for Crosbie into his own room. A man, when he wishes to reprimand another, should always have the benefit of his own atmosphere.\n\n\"I don't want to find any fault,\" Butterwell began.\n\n\"I hope you have not any cause,\" said Crosbie.\n\n\"No, no; I don't say that I have. But we think at the Board--\"\n\n\"Stop, stop, Butterwell. If anything unpleasant is coming, it had better come from the Board. I should take it in better spirit; I should, indeed.\"\n\n\"What takes place at the Board must be official.\"\n\n\"I shall not mind that in the least. I should rather like it than otherwise.\"\n\n\"It simply amounts to this,--that we think you are taking a little too much on yourself. No doubt, it's a fault on the right side, and arises from your wishing to have the work well done.\"\n\n\"And if I don't do it, who will?\" asked Crosbie.\n\n\"The Board is very well able to get through all that appertains to it. Come, Crosbie, you and I have known each other a great many years, and it would be a pity that we should have any words. I have come to you in this way because it would be disagreeable to you to have any question raised officially. Optimist isn't given to being very angry, but he was downright angry yesterday. You had better take what I say in good part, and go along a little quieter.\"\n\nBut Crosbie was not in a humour to take anything quietly. He was sore all over, and prone to hit out at everybody that he met. \"I have done my duty to the best of my ability, Mr. Butterwell,\" he said, \"and I believe I have done it well. I believe I know my duty here as well as any one can teach me. If I have done more than my share of work, it is because other people have done less than theirs.\" As he spoke, there was a black cloud upon his brow, and the Commissioner could perceive that the Secretary was very wrathful.\n\n\"Oh! very well,\" said Butterwell, rising from his chair. \"I can only, under such circumstances, speak to the Chairman, and he will tell you what he thinks at the Board. I think you're foolish; I do, indeed. As for myself, I have only meant to act kindly by you.\" After that, Mr. Butterwell took himself off.\n\nOn the same afternoon, Crosbie was summoned into the Board-room in the usual way, between two and three. This was a daily occurrence, as he always sat for about an hour with two out of the three Commissioners, after they had fortified themselves with a biscuit and a glass of sherry. On the present occasion, the usual amount of business was transacted, but it was done in a manner which made Crosbie feel that they did not all stand together on their usual footing. The three Commissioners were all there. The Chairman gave his directions in a solemn, pompous voice, which was by no means usual to him when he was in good humour. The Major said little or nothing; but there was a gleam of satisfied sarcasm in his eye. Things were going wrong at the Board, and he was pleased. Mr. Butterwell was exceedingly civil in his demeanour, and rather more than ordinarily brisk. As soon as the regular work of the day was over, Mr. Optimist shuffled about on his chair, rising from his seat, and then sitting down again. He looked through a lot of papers close to his hand, peering at them over his spectacles. Then he selected one, took off his spectacles, leaned back in his chair, and began his little speech.\n\n\"Mr. Crosbie,\" he said, \"we are all very much gratified,--very much gratified, indeed,--by your zeal and energy in the service.\"\n\n\"Thank you, sir,\" said Crosbie; \"I am fond of the service.\"\n\n\"Exactly, exactly; we all feel that. But we think that you,--if I were to say take too much upon yourself, I should say, perhaps, more than we mean.\"\n\n\"Don't say more than you mean, Mr. Optimist.\" Crosbie's eyes, as he spoke, gleamed slightly with his momentary triumph; as did also those of Major Fiasco.\n\n\"No, no, no,\" said Mr. Optimist; \"I would say rather less than more to so very good a public servant as yourself. But you, doubtless, understand me?\"\n\n\"I don't think I do quite, sir. If I have not taken too much on me, what is it that I have done that I ought not to have done?\"\n\n\"You have given directions in many cases for which you ought first to have received authority. Here is an instance,\" and the selected paper was at once brought out.\n\nIt was a matter in which the Secretary had been manifestly wrong according to written law, and he could not defend it on its own merits.\n\n\"If you wish me,\" said he, \"to confine myself exactly to the positive instructions of the office, I will do so; but I think you will find it inconvenient.\"\n\n\"It will be far the best,\" said Mr. Optimist.\n\n\"Very well,\" said Mr. Crosbie, \"it shall be done.\" And he at once determined to make himself as unpleasant to the three gentlemen in the room as he might find it within his power to do. He could make himself very unpleasant, but the unpleasantness would be as much to him as to them.\n\nNothing would now go right with him. He could look in no direction for satisfaction. He sauntered into Sebright's, as he went home, but he could not find words to speak to any one about the little matters of the day. He went home, and his wife, though she was up, complained still of her headache.\n\n\"I haven't been out of the house all day,\" she said, \"and that has made it worse.\"\n\n\"I don't know how you are to get out if you won't walk,\" he answered.\n\nThen there was no more said between them till they sat down to their meal.\n\nHad the squire at Allington known all, he might, I think, have been satisfied with the punishment which Crosbie had encountered.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIX. PREPARATIONS FOR GOING.\n\n\"Mamma, read that letter.\"\n\nIt was Mrs. Dale's eldest daughter who spoke to her, and they were alone together in the parlour at the Small House. Mrs. Dale took the letter and read it very carefully. She then put it back into its envelope and returned it to Bell.\n\n\"It is, at any rate, a good letter, and, as I believe, tells the truth.\"\n\n\"I think it tells a little more than the truth, mamma. As you say, it is a well-written letter. He always writes well when he is in earnest. But yet--\"\n\n\"Yet what, my dear?\"\n\n\"There is more head than heart in it.\"\n\n\"If so, he will suffer the less; that is, if you are quite resolved in the matter.\"\n\n\"I am quite resolved, and I do not think he will suffer much. He would not, I suppose, have taken the trouble to write like that, if he did not wish this thing.\"\n\n\"I am quite sure that he does wish it, most earnestly; and that he will be greatly disappointed.\"\n\n\"As he would be if any other scheme did not turn out to his satisfaction; that is all.\"\n\nThe letter, of course, was from Bell's cousin Bernard, and containing the strongest plea he was able to make in favour of his suit for her hand. Bernard Dale was better able to press such a plea by letter than by spoken words. He was a man capable of doing anything well in the doing of which a little time for consideration might be given to him; but he had not in him that power of passion which will force a man to eloquence in asking for that which he desires to obtain. His letter on this occasion was long, and well argued. If there was little in it of passionate love, there was much of pleasant flattery. He told Bell how advantageous to both their families their marriage would be; he declared to her that his own feeling in the matter had been rendered stronger by absence; he alluded without boasting to his past career of life as her best guarantee for his future conduct; he explained to her that if this marriage could be arranged there need then, at any rate, be no further question as to his aunt removing with Lily from the Small House; and then he told her that his affection for herself was the absorbing passion of his existence. Had the letter been written with the view of obtaining from a third person a favourable verdict as to his suit, it would have been a very good letter indeed; but there was not a word in it that could stir the heart of such a girl as Bell Dale.\n\n\"Answer him kindly,\" Mrs. Dale said.\n\n\"As kindly as I know how,\" said Bell. \"I wish you would write the letter, mamma.\"\n\n\"I fear that would not do. What I should say would only tempt him to try again.\"\n\nMrs. Dale knew very well,--had known for some months past,--that Bernard's suit was hopeless. She felt certain, although the matter had not been discussed between them, that whenever Dr. Crofts might choose to come again and ask for her daughter's hand he would not be refused. Of the two men she probably liked Dr. Crofts the best; but she liked them both, and she could not but remember that the one, in a worldly point of view, would be a very poor match, whereas the other would, in all respects, be excellent. She would not, on any account, say a word to influence her daughter, and knew, moreover, that no word which she could say would influence her; but she could not divest herself of some regret that it should be so.\n\n\"I know what you would wish, mamma,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I have but one wish, dearest, and that is for your happiness. May God preserve you from any such fate as Lily's. When I tell you to write kindly to your cousin, I simply mean that I think him to have deserved a kind reply by his honesty.\"\n\n\"It shall be as kind as I can make it, mamma; but you know what the lady says in the play,--how hard it is to take the sting from that word 'no.'\" Then Bell walked out alone for a while, and on her return got her desk and wrote her letter. It was very firm and decisive. As for that wit which should pluck the sting \"from such a sharp and waspish word as 'no,'\" I fear she had it not. \"It will be better to make him understand that I, also, am in earnest,\" she said to herself; and in this frame of mind she wrote her letter. \"Pray do not allow yourself to think that what I have said is unfriendly,\" she added, in a postscript. \"I know how good you are, and I know the great value of what I refuse; but in this matter it must be my duty to tell you the simple truth.\"\n\nIt had been decided between the squire and Mrs. Dale that the removal from the Small House to Guestwick was not to take place till the first of May. When he had been made to understand that Dr. Crofts had thought it injudicious that Lily should be taken out of their present house in March, he had used all the eloquence of which he was master to induce Mrs. Dale to consent to abandon her project. He had told her that he had always considered that house as belonging, of right, to some other of the family than himself; that it had always been so inhabited, and that no squire of Allington had for years past taken rent for it. \"There is no favour conferred,--none at all,\" he had said; but speaking nevertheless in his usual sharp, ungenial tone.\n\n\"There is a favour, a great favour, and great generosity,\" Mrs. Dale had replied. \"And I have never been too proud to accept it; but when I tell you that we think we shall be happier at Guestwick, you will not refuse to let us go. Lily has had a great blow in that house, and Bell feels that she is running counter to your wishes on her behalf,--wishes that are so very kind!\"\n\n\"No more need be said about that. All that may come right yet, if you will remain where you are.\"\n\nBut Mrs. Dale knew that \"all that\" could never come right, and persisted. Indeed, she would hardly have dared to tell her girls that she had yielded to the squire's entreaties. It was just then, at that very time, that the squire was, as it were, in treaty with the earl about Lily's fortune; and he did feel it hard that he should be opposed in such a way by his own relatives at the moment when he was behaving towards them with so much generosity. But in his arguments about the house he said nothing of Lily, or her future prospects.\n\nThey were to move on the first of May, and one week of April was already past. The squire had said nothing further on the matter after the interview with Mrs. Dale to which allusion has just been made. He was vexed and sore at the separation, thinking that he was ill-used by the feeling which was displayed by this refusal. He had done his duty by them, as he thought; indeed more than his duty, and now they told him that they were leaving him because they could no longer bear the weight of an obligation conferred by his hands. But in truth he did not understand them; nor did they understand him. He had been hard in his manner, and had occasionally domineered, not feeling that his position, though it gave him all the privileges of a near and a dear friend, did not give him the authority of a father or a husband. In that matter of Bernard's proposed marriage he had spoken as though Bell should have considered his wishes before she refused her cousin. He had taken upon himself to scold Mrs. Dale, and had thereby given offence to the girls, which they at the time had found it utterly impossible to forgive.\n\nBut they were hardly better satisfied in the matter than was he; and now that the time had come, though they could not bring themselves to go back from their demand, almost felt that they were treating the squire with cruelty. When their decision had been made,--while it had been making,--he had been stern and hard to them. Since that he had been softened by Lily's misfortune, and softened also by the anticipated loneliness which would come upon him when they should be gone from his side. It was hard upon him that they should so treat him when he was doing his best for them all! And they also felt this, though they did not know the extent to which he was anxious to go in serving them. When they had sat round the fire planning the scheme of their removal, their hearts had been hardened against him, and they had resolved to assert their independence. But now, when the time for action had come, they felt that their grievances against him had already been in a great measure assuaged. This tinged all that they did with a certain sadness; but still they continued their work.\n\nWho does not know how terrible are those preparations for house-moving;--how infinite in number are the articles which must be packed, how inexpressibly uncomfortable is the period of packing, and how poor and tawdry is the aspect of one's belongings while they are thus in a state of dislocation? Now-a-days people who understand the world, and have money commensurate with their understanding, have learned the way of shunning all these disasters, and of leaving the work to the hands of persons paid for doing it. The crockery is left in the cupboards, the books on the shelves, the wine in the bins, the curtains on their poles, and the family that is understanding goes for a fortnight to Brighton. At the end of that time the crockery is comfortably settled in other cupboards, the books on other shelves, the wine in other bins, the curtains are hung on other poles, and all is arranged. But Mrs. Dale and her daughters understood nothing of such a method of moving as this. The assistance of the village carpenter in filling certain cases that he had made was all that they knew how to obtain beyond that of their own two servants. Every article had to pass through the hands of some one of the family; and as they felt almost overwhelmed by the extent of the work to be done, they began it much sooner than was necessary, so that it became evident as they advanced in their work, that they would have to pass a dreadfully dull, stupid, uncomfortable week at last, among their boxes and cases, in all the confusion of dismantled furniture.\n\nAt first an edict had gone forth that Lily was to do nothing. She was an invalid, and was to be petted and kept quiet. But this edict soon fell to the ground, and Lily worked harder than either her mother or her sister. In truth she was hardly an invalid any longer, and would not submit to an invalid's treatment. She felt herself that for the present constant occupation could alone save her from the misery of looking back,--and she had conceived an idea that the harder that occupation was, the better it would be for her. While pulling down the books, and folding the linen, and turning out from their old hiding-places the small long-forgotten properties of the household, she would be as gay as ever she had been in old times. She would talk over her work, standing with flushed cheek and laughing eyes among the dusty ruins around her, till for a moment her mother would think that all was well within her. But then at other moments, when the reaction came, it would seem as though nothing were well. She could not sit quietly over the fire, with quiet rational work in her hands, and chat in a rational quiet way. Not as yet could she do so. Nevertheless it was well with her,--within her own bosom. She had declared to herself that she would conquer her misery,--as she had also declared to herself during her illness that her misfortune should not kill her,--and she was in the way to conquer it. She told herself that the world was not over for her because her sweet hopes had been frustrated. The wound had been deep and very sore, but the flesh of the patient had been sound and healthy, and her blood pure. A physician having knowledge in such cases would have declared, after long watching of her symptoms, that a cure was probable. Her mother was the physician who watched her with the closest eyes; and she, though she was sometimes driven to doubt, did hope, with stronger hope from day to day, that her child might live to remember the story of her love without abiding agony.\n\nThat nobody should talk to her about it,--that had been the one stipulation which she had seemed to make, not sending forth a request to that effect among her friends in so many words, but showing by certain signs that such was her stipulation. A word to that effect she had spoken to her uncle,--as may be remembered, which word had been regarded with the closest obedience. She had gone out into her little world very soon after the news of Crosbie's falsehood had reached her,--first to church and then among the people of the village, resolving to carry herself as though no crushing weight had fallen upon her. The village people had understood it all, listening to her and answering her without the proffer of any outspoken parley.\n\n\"Lord bless 'ee,\" said Mrs. Crump, the postmistress,--and Mrs. Crump was supposed to have the sourest temper in Allington,--\"whenever I look at thee, Miss Lily, I thinks that surely thee is the beautifulest young 'ooman in all these parts.\"\n\n\"And you are the crossest old woman,\" said Lily, laughing, and giving her hand to the postmistress.\n\n\"So I be,\" said Mrs. Crump. \"So I be.\" Then Lily sat down in the cottage and asked after her ailments. With Mrs. Hearn it was the same. Mrs. Hearn, after that first meeting which has been already mentioned, petted and caressed her, but spoke no further word of her misfortune. When Lily called a second time upon Mrs. Boyce, which she did boldly by herself, that lady did begin one other word of commiseration. \"My dearest Lily, we have all been made so unhappy--\" So far Mrs. Boyce got, sitting close to Lily and striving to look into her face; but Lily, with a slightly heightened colour, turned sharp round upon one of the Boyce girls, tearing Mrs. Boyce's commiseration into the smallest shreds. \"Minnie,\" she said, speaking quite loud, almost with girlish ecstasy, \"what do you think Tartar did yesterday? I never laughed so much in my life.\" Then she told a ludicrous story about a very ugly terrier which belonged to the squire. After that even Mrs. Boyce made no further attempt. Mrs. Dale and Bell both understood that such was to be the rule--the rule even to them. Lily would speak to them occasionally on the matter,--to one of them at a time, beginning with some almost single word of melancholy resignation, and then would go on till she opened her very bosom before them; but no such conversation was ever begun by them. But now, in these busy days of the packing, that topic seemed to have been banished altogether.\n\n\"Mamma,\" she said, standing on the top rung of a house-ladder, from which position she was handing down glass out of a cupboard, \"are you sure that these things are ours? I think some of them belong to the house.\"\n\n\"I'm sure about that bowl at any rate, because it was my mother's before I was married.\"\n\n\"Oh, dear, what should I do if I were to break it? Whenever I handle anything very precious I always feel inclined to throw it down and smash it. Oh! it was as nearly gone as possible, mamma; but that was your fault.\"\n\n\"If you don't take care you'll be nearly gone yourself. Do take hold of something.\"\n\n\"Oh, Bell, here's the inkstand for which you've been moaning for three years.\"\n\n\"I haven't been moaning for three years; but who could have put it up there?\"\n\n\"Catch it,\" said Lily; and she threw the bottle down on to a pile of carpets.\n\nAt this moment a step was heard in the hall, and the squire entered through the open door of the room. \"So you're all at work,\" said he.\n\n\"Yes, we're at work,\" said Mrs. Dale, almost with a tone of shame. \"If it is to be done it is as well that it should be got over.\"\n\n\"It makes me wretched enough,\" said the squire. \"But I didn't come to talk about that. I've brought you a note from Lady Julia De Guest, and I've had one from the earl. They want us all to go there and stay the week after Easter.\"\n\nMrs. Dale and the girls, when this very sudden proposition was made to them, all remained fixed in their places, and, for a moment, were speechless. Go and stay a week at Guestwick Manor! The whole family! Hitherto the intercourse between the Manor and the Small House had been confined to morning calls, very far between. Mrs. Dale had never dined there, and had latterly even deputed the calling to her daughters. Once Bell had dined there with her uncle, the squire, and once Lily had gone over with her uncle Orlando. Even this had been long ago, before they were quite brought out, and they had regarded the occasion with the solemn awe of children. Now, at this time of their flitting into some small mean dwelling at Guestwick, they had previously settled among themselves that that affair of calling at the Manor might be allowed to drop. Mrs. Eames never called, and they were descending to the level of Mrs. Eames. \"Perhaps we shall get game sent to us, and that will be better,\" Lily had said. And now, at this very moment of their descent in life, they were all asked to go and stay a week at the Manor! Stay a week with Lady Julia! Had the Queen sent the Lord Chamberlain down to bid them all go to Windsor Castle it could hardly have startled them more at the first blow. Bell had been seated on the folded carpet when her uncle had entered, and now had again sat herself in the same place. Lily was still standing at the top of the ladder, and Mrs. Dale was at the foot with one hand on Lily's dress. The squire had told his story very abruptly, but he was a man who, having a story to tell, knew nothing better than to tell it out abruptly, letting out everything at the first moment.\n\n\"Wants us all!\" said Mrs. Dale. \"How many does the all mean?\" Then she opened Lady Julia's note and read it, not moving from her position at the foot of the ladder.\n\n\"Do let me see, mamma,\" said Lily; and then the note was handed up to her. Had Mrs. Dale well considered the matter she might probably have kept the note to herself for a while, but the whole thing was so sudden that she had not considered the matter well.\n\nMY DEAR MRS. DALE [the letter ran],\n\nI send this inside a note from my brother to Mr. Dale. We particularly want you and your two girls to come to us for a week from the seventeenth of this month. Considering our near connection we ought to have seen more of each other than we have done for years past, and of course it has been our fault. But it is never too late to amend one's ways; and I hope you will receive my confession in the true spirit of affection in which it is intended, and that you will show your goodness by coming to us. I will do all I can to make the house pleasant to your girls, for both of whom I have much real regard.\n\nI should tell you that John Eames will be here for the same week. My brother is very fond of him, and thinks him the best young man of the day. He is one of my heroes, too, I must confess.\n\nVery sincerely yours,\n\nLily, standing on the ladder, read the letter very attentively. The squire meanwhile stood below speaking a word or two to his sister-in-law and niece. No one could see Lily's face, as it was turned away towards the window, and it was still averted when she spoke. \"It is out of the question that we should go, mamma;--that is, all of us.\"\n\n\"Why out of the question?\" said the squire.\n\n\"A whole family!\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"That is just what they want,\" said the squire.\n\n\"I should like of all things to be left alone for a week,\" said Lily, \"if mamma and Bell would go.\"\n\n\"That wouldn't do at all,\" said the squire. \"Lady Julia specially wants you to be one of the party.\"\n\nThe thing had been badly managed altogether. The reference in Lady Julia's note to John Eames had explained to Lily the whole scheme at once, and had so opened her eyes that all the combined influence of the Dale and De Guest families could not have dragged her over to the Manor.\n\n\"Why not do?\" said Lily. \"It would be out of the question a whole family going in that way, but it would be very nice for Bell.\"\n\n\"No, it would not,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Don't be ungenerous about it, my dear,\" said the squire, turning to Bell; \"Lady Julia means to be kind. But, my darling,\" and the squire turned again towards Lily, addressing her, as was his wont in these days, with an affection that was almost vexatious to her; \"but, my darling, why should you not go? A change of scene like that will do you all the good in the world, just when you are getting well. Mary, tell the girls that they ought to go.\"\n\nMrs. Dale stood silent, again reading the note, and Lily came down from the ladder. When she reached the floor she went directly up to her uncle, and taking his hand turned him round with herself towards one of the windows, so that they stood with their backs to the room. \"Uncle,\" she said, \"do not be angry with me. I can't go;\" and then she put up her face to kiss him.\n\nHe stooped and kissed her and still held her hand. He looked into her face and read it all. He knew well, now, why she could not go; or, rather, why she herself thought that she could not go. \"Cannot you, my darling?\" he said.\n\n\"No, uncle. It is very kind,--very kind; but I cannot go. I am not fit to go anywhere.\"\n\n\"But you should get over that feeling. You should make a struggle.\"\n\n\"I am struggling, and I shall succeed; but I cannot do it all at once. At any rate I could not go there. You must give my love to Lady Julia, and not let her think me cross. Perhaps Bell will go.\"\n\nWhat would be the good of Bell's going--or the good of his putting himself out of the way, by a visit which would of itself be so tiresome to him, if the one object of the visit could not be carried out? The earl and his sister had planned the invitation with the express intention of bringing Lily and Eames together. It seemed that Lily was firm in her determination to resist this intention; and, if so, it would be better that the whole thing should fall to the ground. He was very vexed, and yet he was not angry with her. Everybody lately had opposed him in everything. All his intended family arrangements had gone wrong. But yet he was seldom angry respecting them. He was so accustomed to be thwarted that he hardly expected success. In this matter of providing Lily with a second lover, he had not come forward of his own accord. He had been appealed to by his neighbour the earl, and had certainly answered the appeal with much generosity. He had been induced to make the attempt with eagerness, and a true desire for its accomplishment; but in this, as in all his own schemes, he was met at once by opposition and failure.\n\n\"I will leave you to talk it over among yourselves,\" he said. \"But, Mary, you had better see me before you send your answer. If you will come up by-and-by, Ralph shall take the two notes over together in the afternoon.\" So saying, he left the Small House, and went back to his own solitary home.\n\n\"Lily, dear,\" said Mrs. Dale, as soon as the front door had been closed, \"this is meant for kindness to you,--for most affectionate kindness.\"\n\n\"I know it, mamma; and you must go to Lady Julia, and must tell her that I know it. You must give her my love. And, indeed, I do love her now. But--\"\n\n\"You won't go, Lily?\" said Mrs. Dale, beseechingly.\n\n\"No, mamma; certainly I will not go.\" Then she escaped out of the room by herself, and for the next hour neither of them dared to go to her.\n\n# CHAPTER L. MRS. DALE IS THANKFUL FOR A GOOD THING.\n\nOn that day they dined early at the Small House, as they had been in the habit of doing since the packing had commenced. And after dinner Mrs. Dale went through the gardens, up to the other house, with a written note in her hand. In that note she had told Lady Julia, with many protestations of gratitude, that Lily was unable to go out so soon after her illness, and that she herself was obliged to stay with Lily. She explained also, that the business of moving was in hand, and that, therefore, she could not herself accept the invitation. But her other daughter, she said, would be very happy to accompany her uncle to Guestwick Manor. Then, without closing her letter, she took it up to the squire in order that it might be decided whether it would or would not suit his views. It might well be that he would not care to go to Lord De Guest's with Bell alone.\n\n\"Leave it with me,\" he said; \"that is, if you do not object.\"\n\n\"Oh dear, no!\"\n\n\"I'll tell you the plain truth at once, Mary. I shall go over myself with it, and see the earl. Then I will decline it or not, according to what passes between me and him. I wish Lily would have gone.\"\n\n\"Ah! she could not.\"\n\n\"I wish she could. I wish she could. I wish she could.\" As he repeated the words over and over again, there was an eagerness in his voice that filled Mrs. Dale's heart with tenderness towards him.\n\n\"The truth is,\" said Mrs. Dale, \"she could not go there to meet John Eames.\"\n\n\"Oh, I know,\" said the squire: \"I understand it. But that is just what we want her to do. Why should she not spend a week in the same house with an honest young man whom we all like.\"\n\n\"There are reasons why she would not wish it.\"\n\n\"Ah, exactly; the very reasons which should make us induce her to go there if we can. Perhaps I had better tell you all. Lord De Guest has taken him by the hand, and wishes him to marry. He has promised to settle on him an income which will make him comfortable for life.\"\n\n\"That is very generous; and I am delighted to hear it,--for John's sake.\"\n\n\"And they have promoted him at his office.\"\n\n\"Ah! then he will do well.\"\n\n\"He will do very well. He is private secretary now to their head man. And, Mary, so that she, Lily, should not be empty-handed if this marriage can be arranged, I have undertaken to settle a hundred a year on her,--on her and her children, if she will accept him. Now you know it all. I did not mean to tell you; but it is as well that you should have the means of judging. That other man was a villain. This man is honest. Would it not be well that she should learn to like him? She always did like him, I thought, before that other fellow came down here among us.\"\n\n\"She has always liked him--as a friend.\"\n\n\"She will never get a better lover.\"\n\nMrs. Dale sat silent, thinking over it all. Every word that the squire said was true. It would be a healing of wounds most desirable and salutary; an arrangement advantageous to them all; a destiny for Lily most devoutly to be desired,--if only it were possible. Mrs. Dale firmly believed that if her daughter could be made to accept John Eames as her second lover in a year or two all would be well. Crosbie would then be forgotten or thought of without regret, and Lily would become the mistress of a happy home. But there are positions which cannot be reached, though there be no physical or material objection in the way. It is the view which the mind takes of a thing which creates the sorrow that arises from it. If the heart were always malleable and the feelings could be controlled, who would permit himself to be tormented by any of the reverses which affection meets? Death would create no sorrow; ingratitude would lose its sting; and the betrayal of love would do no injury beyond that which it might entail upon worldly circumstances. But the heart is not malleable; nor will the feelings admit of such control.\n\n\"It is not possible for her,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"I fear it is not possible. It is too soon.\"\n\n\"Six months,\" pleaded the squire.\n\n\"It will take years,--not months,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"And she will lose all her youth.\"\n\n\"Yes; he has done all that by his treachery. But it is done, and we cannot now go back. She loves him yet as dearly as she ever loved him.\"\n\nThen the squire muttered certain words below his breath,--ejaculations against Crosbie, which were hardly voluntary; but even as involuntary ejaculations were very improper. Mrs. Dale heard them, and was not offended either by their impropriety or their warmth. \"But you can understand,\" she said, \"that she cannot bring herself to go there.\" The squire struck the table with his fist, and repeated his ejaculations. If he could only have known how very disagreeable Lady Alexandrina was making herself, his spirit might, perhaps, have been less vehemently disturbed. If, also, he could have perceived and understood the light in which an alliance with the De Courcy family was now regarded by Crosbie, I think that he would have received some consolation from that consideration. Those who offend us are generally punished for the offence they give; but we so frequently miss the satisfaction of knowing that we are avenged! It is arranged, apparently, that the injurer shall be punished, but that the person injured shall not gratify his desire for vengeance.\n\n\"And will you go to Guestwick yourself?\" asked Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"I will take the note,\" said the squire, \"and will let you know to-morrow. The earl has behaved so kindly that every possible consideration is due to him. I had better tell him the whole truth, and go or stay, as he may wish. I don't see the good of going. What am I to do at Guestwick Manor? I did think that if we had all been there it might have cured some difficulties.\"\n\nMrs. Dale got up to leave him, but she could not go without saying some word of gratitude for all that he had attempted to do for them. She well knew what he meant by the curing of difficulties. He had intended to signify that had they lived together for a week at Guestwick the idea of flitting from Allington might possibly have been abandoned. It seemed now to Mrs. Dale as though her brother-in-law were heaping coals of fire on her head in return for that intention. She felt half-ashamed of what she was doing, almost acknowledging to herself that she should have borne with his sternness in return for the benefits he had done to her daughters. Had she not feared their reproaches she would, even now, have given way.\n\n\"I do not know what I ought to say to you for your kindness.\"\n\n\"Say nothing,--either for my kindness or unkindness; but stay where you are, and let us live like Christians together, striving to think good and not evil.\" These were kind, loving words, showing in themselves a spirit of love and forbearance; but they were spoken in a harsh, unsympathizing voice, and the speaker, as he uttered them, looked gloomily at the fire. In truth the squire, as he spoke, was half-ashamed of the warmth of what he said.\n\n\"At any rate I will not think evil,\" Mrs. Dale answered, giving him her hand. After that she left him, and returned home. It was too late for her to abandon her project of moving and remain at the Small House; but as she went across the garden she almost confessed to herself that she repented of what she was doing.\n\nIn these days of the cold early spring, the way from the lawn into the house, through the drawing-room window, was not as yet open, and it was necessary to go round by the kitchen-garden on to the road, and thence in by the front door; or else to pass through the back door, and into the house by the kitchen. This latter mode of entrance Mrs. Dale now adopted; and as she made her way into the hall Lily came upon her, with very silent steps, out from the parlour, and arrested her progress. There was a smile upon Lily's face as she lifted up her finger as if in caution, and no one looking at her would have supposed that she was herself in trouble. \"Mamma,\" she said, pointing to the drawing-room door, and speaking almost in a whisper, \"you must not go in there; come into the parlour.\"\n\n\"Who's there? Where's Bell?\" and Mrs. Dale went into the parlour as she was bidden. \"But who is there?\" she repeated.\n\n\"He's there!\"\n\n\"Who is he?\"\n\n\"Oh, mamma, don't be a goose! Dr. Crofts is there, of course. He's been nearly an hour. I wonder how he is managing, for there is nothing on earth to sit upon but the old lump of a carpet. The room is strewed about with crockery, and Bell is such a figure! She has got on your old checked apron, and when he came in she was rolling up the fire-irons in brown paper. I don't suppose she was ever in such a mess before. There's one thing certain,--he can't kiss her hand.\"\n\n\"It's you are the goose, Lily.\"\n\n\"But he's in there certainly, unless he has gone out through the window, or up the chimney.\"\n\n\"What made you leave them?\"\n\n\"He met me here, in the passage, and spoke to me ever so seriously. 'Come in,' I said, 'and see Bell packing the pokers and tongs.' 'I will go in,' he said, 'but don't come with me.' He was ever so serious, and I'm sure he had been thinking of it all the way along.\"\n\n\"And why should he not be serious?\"\n\n\"Oh, no, of course he ought to be serious; but are you not glad, mamma? I am so glad. We shall live alone together, you and I; but she will be so close to us! My belief is that he'll stay there for ever unless somebody does something. I have been so tired of waiting and looking out for you. Perhaps he's helping her to pack the things. Don't you think we might go in; or would it be ill-natured?\"\n\n\"Lily, don't be in too great a hurry to say anything. You may be mistaken, you know; and there's many a slip between the cup and the lip.\"\n\n\"Yes, mamma, there is,\" said Lily, putting her hand inside her mother's arm, \"that's true enough.\"\n\n\"Oh, my darling, forgive me,\" said the mother, suddenly remembering that the use of the old proverb at the present moment had been almost cruel.\n\n\"Do not mind it,\" said Lily, \"it does not hurt me, it does me good; that is to say, when there is nobody by except yourself. But, with God's help, there shall be no slip here, and she shall be happy. It is all the difference between one thing done in a hurry, and another done with much thinking. But they'll remain there for ever if we don't go in. Come, mamma, you open the door.\"\n\nThen Mrs. Dale did open the door, giving some little premonitory notice with the handle, so that the couple inside might be warned of approaching footsteps. Crofts had not escaped, either through the window or up the chimney, but was seated in the middle of the room on an empty box, just opposite to Bell, who was seated upon the lump of carpeting. Bell still wore the checked apron as described by her sister. What might have been the state of her hands I will not pretend to say; but I do not believe that her lover had found anything amiss with them. \"How do you do, doctor?\" said Mrs. Dale, striving to use her accustomed voice, and to look as though there were nothing of special importance in his visit. \"I have just come down from the Great House.\"\n\n\"Mamma,\" said Bell, jumping up, \"you must not call him doctor any more.\"\n\n\"Must I not? Has any one undoctored him?\"\n\n\"Oh, mamma, you understand,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I understand,\" said Lily, going up to the doctor, and giving him her cheek to kiss, \"he is to be my brother, and I mean to claim him as such from this moment. I expect him to do everything for us, and not to call a moment of his time his own.\"\n\n\"Mrs. Dale,\" said the doctor, \"Bell has consented that it shall be so, if you will consent.\"\n\n\"There is but little doubt of that,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"We shall not be rich--\" began the doctor.\n\n\"I hate to be rich,\" said Bell. \"I hate even to talk about it. I don't think it quite manly even to think about it; and I'm sure it isn't womanly.\"\n\n\"Bell was always a fanatic in praise of poverty,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"No; I'm no fanatic. I'm very fond of money earned. I would like to earn some myself if I knew how.\"\n\n\"Let her go out and visit the lady patients,\" said Lily. \"They do in America.\"\n\nThen they all went into the parlour and sat round the fire talking as though they were already one family. The proceeding, considering the nature of it,--that a young lady, acknowledged to be of great beauty and known to be of good birth, had on the occasion been asked and given in marriage,--was carried on after a somewhat humdrum fashion, and in a manner that must be called commonplace. How different had it been when Crosbie had made his offer! Lily for the time had been raised to a pinnacle,--a pinnacle which might be dangerous, but which was, at any rate, lofty. With what a pretty speech had Crosbie been greeted! How it had been felt by all concerned that the fortunes of the Small House were in the ascendant,--felt, indeed, with some trepidation, but still with much inward triumph. How great had been the occasion, forcing Lily almost to lose herself in wonderment at what had occurred! There was no great occasion now, and no wonderment. No one, unless it was Crofts, felt very triumphant. But they were all very happy, and were sure that there was safety in their happiness. It was but the other day that one of them had been thrown rudely to the ground through the treachery of a lover, but yet none of them feared treachery from this lover. Bell was as sure of her lot in life as though she were already being taken home to her modest house in Guestwick. Mrs. Dale already looked upon the man as her son, and the party of four as they sat round the fire grouped themselves as though they already formed one family.\n\nBut Bell was not seated next to her lover. Lily, when she had once accepted Crosbie, seemed to think that she could never be too near to him. She had been in no wise ashamed of her love, and had shown it constantly by some little caressing motion of her hand, leaning on his arm, looking into his face, as though she were continually desirous of some palpable assurance of his presence. It was not so at all with Bell. She was happy in loving and in being loved, but she required no overt testimonies of affection. I do not think it would have made her unhappy if some sudden need had required that Crofts should go to India and back before they were married. The thing was settled, and that was enough for her. But, on the other hand, when he spoke of the expediency of an immediate marriage, she raised no difficulty. As her mother was about to go into a new residence, it might be as well that that residence should be fitted to the wants of two persons instead of three. So they talked about chairs and tables, carpets and kitchens, in a most unromantic, homely, useful manner! A considerable portion of the furniture in the house they were now about to leave belonged to the squire,--or to the house rather, as they were in the habit of saying. The older and more solid things,--articles of household stuff that stand the wear of half a century,--had been in the Small House when they came to it. There was, therefore, a question of buying new furniture for a house in Guestwick,--a question not devoid of importance to the possessor of so moderate an income as that owned by Mrs. Dale. In the first month or two they were to live in lodgings, and their goods were to be stored in some friendly warehouse. Under such circumstances would it not be well that Bell's marriage should be so arranged that the lodging question might not be in any degree complicated by her necessities? This was the last suggestion made by Dr. Crofts, induced no doubt by the great encouragement he had received.\n\n\"That would be hardly possible,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"It only wants three weeks;--and with the house in such a condition!\"\n\n\"James is joking,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I was not joking at all,\" said the doctor.\n\n\"Why not send for Mr. Boyce, and carry her off at once on a pillion behind you?\" said Lily. \"It's just the sort of thing for primitive people to do, like you and Bell. All the same, Bell, I do wish you could have been married from this house.\"\n\n\"I don't think it will make much difference,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Only if you would have waited till summer we would have had such a nice party on the lawn. It sounds so ugly, being married from lodgings; doesn't it, mamma?\"\n\n\"It doesn't sound at all ugly to me,\" said Bell.\n\n\"I shall always call you Dame Commonplace when you're married,\" said Lily.\n\nThen they had tea, and after tea Dr. Crofts got on his horse and rode back to Guestwick.\n\n\"Now may I talk about him?\" said Lily, as soon as the door was closed behind his back.\n\n\"No; you may not.\"\n\n\"As if I hadn't known it all along! And wasn't it hard to bear that you should have scolded me with such pertinacious austerity, and that I wasn't to say a word in answer!\"\n\n\"I don't remember the austerity,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Nor yet Lily's silence,\" said Bell.\n\n\"But it's all settled now,\" said Lily, \"and I'm downright happy. I never felt more satisfaction,--never, Bell!\"\n\n\"Nor did I,\" said her mother; \"I may truly say that I thank God for this good thing.\"\n\n# CHAPTER LI. JOHN EAMES DOES THINGS WHICH HE OUGHT NOT TO HAVE DONE.\n\nJohn Eames succeeded in making his bargain with Sir Raffle Buffle. He accepted the private secretaryship on the plainly expressed condition that he was to have leave of absence for a fortnight towards the end of April. Having arranged this he took an affectionate leave of Mr. Love, who was really much affected at parting with him, discussed valedictory pots of porter in the big room, over which many wishes were expressed that he might be enabled to compass the length and breadth of old Huffle's feet, uttered a last cutting joke at Mr. Kissing as he met that gentleman hurrying through the passages with an enormous ledger in his hands, and then took his place in the comfortable arm-chair which FitzHoward had been forced to relinquish.\n\n\"Don't tell any of the fellows,\" said Fitz, \"but I'm going to cut the concern altogether. My governor wouldn't let me stop here in any other place than that of private secretary.\"\n\n\"Ah, your governor is a swell,\" said Eames.\n\n\"I don't know about that,\" said FitzHoward. \"Of course he has a good deal of family interest. My cousin is to come in for St. Bungay at the next election, and then I can do better than remain here.\"\n\n\"That's a matter of course,\" said Eames. \"If my cousin were Member for St. Bungay, I'd never stand anything east of Whitehall.\"\n\n\"And I don't mean,\" said FitzHoward. \"This room, you know, is all very nice; but it is a bore coming into the City every day. And then one doesn't like to be rung for like a servant. Not that I mean to put you out of conceit with it.\"\n\n\"It will do very well for me,\" said Eames. \"I never was very particular.\" And so they parted, Eames assuming the beautiful arm-chair and the peril of being asked to carry Sir Raffle's shoes, while FitzHoward took the vacant desk in the big room till such time as some member of his family should come into Parliament for the borough of St. Bungay.\n\nBut Eames, though he drank the porter, and quizzed FitzHoward, and gibed at Kissing, did not seat himself in his new arm-chair without some serious thoughts. He was aware that his career in London had not hitherto been one on which he could look back with self-respect. He had lived with friends whom he did not esteem; he had been idle, and sometimes worse than idle; and he had allowed himself to be hampered by the pretended love of a woman for whom he had never felt any true affection, and by whom he had been cozened out of various foolish promises which even yet were hanging over his head. As he sat with Sir Raffle's notes before him, he thought almost with horror of the men and women in Burton Crescent. It was now about three years since he had first known Cradell, and he shuddered as he remembered how very poor a creature was he whom he had chosen for his bosom friend. He could not make for himself those excuses which we can make for him. He could not tell himself that he had been driven by circumstances to choose a friend, before he had learned to know what were the requisites for which he should look. He had lived on terms of closest intimacy with this man for three years, and now his eyes were opening themselves to the nature of his friend's character. Cradell was in age three years his senior. \"I won't drop him,\" he said to himself; \"but he is a poor creature.\" He thought, too, of the Lupexes, of Miss Spruce, and of Mrs. Roper, and tried to imagine what Lily Dale would do if she found herself among such people. It would be impossible that she should ever so find herself. He might as well ask her to drink at the bar of a gin-shop as to sit down in Mrs. Roper's drawing-room. If destiny had in store for him such good fortune as that of calling Lily his own, it was necessary that he should altogether alter his mode of life.\n\nIn truth his hobbledehoyhood was dropping off from him, as its old skin drops from a snake. Much of the feeling and something of the knowledge of manhood was coming on him, and he was beginning to recognize to himself that the future manner of his life must be to him a matter of very serious concern. No such thought had come near him when he first established himself in London. It seems to me that in this respect the fathers and mothers of the present generation understand but little of the inward nature of the young men for whom they are so anxious. They give them credit for so much that it is impossible they should have, and then deny them credit for so much that they possess! They expect from them when boys the discretion of men,--that discretion which comes from thinking; but will not give them credit for any of that power of thought which alone can ultimately produce good conduct. Young men are generally thoughtful,--more thoughtful than their seniors; but the fruit of their thought is not as yet there. And then so little is done for the amusement of lads who are turned loose into London at nineteen or twenty. Can it be that any mother really expects her son to sit alone evening after evening in a dingy room drinking bad tea, and reading good books? And yet it seems that mothers do so expect,--the very mothers who talk about the thoughtlessness of youth! O ye mothers who from year to year see your sons launched forth upon the perils of the world, and who are so careful with your good advice, with under flannel shirting, with books of devotion and tooth-powder, does it never occur to you that provision should be made for amusement, for dancing, for parties, for the excitement and comfort of women's society? That excitement your sons will have, and if it be not provided by you of one kind, will certainly be provided by themselves of another kind. If I were a mother sending lads out into the world, the matter most in my mind would be this,--to what houses full of nicest girls could I get them admission, so that they might do their flirting in good company.\n\nPoor John Eames had been so placed that he had been driven to do his flirting in very bad company, and he was now fully aware that it had been so. It wanted but two days to his departure for Guestwick Manor, and as he sat breathing a while after the manufacture of a large batch of Sir Raffle's notes, he made up his mind that he would give Mrs. Roper notice before he started, that on his return to London he would be seen no more in Burton Crescent. He would break his bonds altogether asunder, and if there should be any penalty for such breaking he would pay it in what best manner he might be able. He acknowledged to himself that he had been behaving badly to Amelia, confessing, indeed, more sin in that respect than he had in truth committed; but this, at any rate, was clear to him, that he must put himself on a proper footing in that quarter before he could venture to speak to Lily Dale.\n\nAs he came to a definite conclusion on this subject the little handbell which always stood on Sir Raffle's table was sounded, and Eames was called into the presence of the great man. \"Ah,\" said Sir Raffle, leaning back in his arm-chair, and stretching himself after the great exertions which he had been making--\"Ah, let me see! You are going out of town the day after to-morrow.\"\n\n\"Yes, Sir Raffle, the day after to-morrow.\"\n\n\"Ah! it's a great annoyance,--a very great annoyance. But on such occasions I never think of myself. I never have done so, and don't suppose I ever shall. So you're going down to my old friend De Guest?\"\n\nEames was always angered when his new patron Sir Raffle talked of his old friendship with the earl, and never gave the Commissioner any encouragement. \"I am going down to Guestwick,\" said he.\n\n\"Ah! yes; to Guestwick Manor? I don't remember that I was ever there. I daresay I may have been, but one forgets those things.\"\n\n\"I never heard Lord De Guest speak of it.\"\n\n\"Oh, dear, no. Why should his memory be better than mine? Tell him, will you, how very glad I shall be to renew our old intimacy. I should think nothing of running down to him for a day or two in the dull time of the year,--say in September or October. It's rather a coincidence our both being interested about you,--isn't it?\"\n\n\"I'll be sure to tell him.\"\n\n\"Mind you do. He's one of our most thoroughly independent noblemen, and I respect him very highly. Let me see; didn't I ring my bell? What was it I wanted? I think I rang my bell.\"\n\n\"You did ring your bell.\"\n\n\"Ah, yes; I know. I am going away, and I wanted my--would you tell Rafferty to bring me--my boots?\" Whereupon Johnny rang the bell--not the little handbell, but the other bell. \"And I shan't be here to-morrow,\" continued Sir Raffle. \"I'll thank you to send my letters up to the square; and if they should send down from the Treasury;--but the Chancellor would write, and in that case you'll send up his letter at once by a special messenger, of course.\"\n\n\"Here's Rafferty,\" said Eames, determined that he would not even sully his lips with speaking of Sir Raffle's boots.\n\n\"Oh, ah, yes; Rafferty, bring me my boots.\"\n\n\"Anything else to say?\" asked Eames.\n\n\"No, nothing else. Of course you'll be careful to leave everything straight behind you.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes; I'll leave it all straight.\" Then Eames withdrew, so that he might not be present at the interview between Sir Raffle and his boots. \"He'll not do,\" said Sir Raffle to himself. \"He'll never do. He's not quick enough,--has no go in him. He's not man enough for the place. I wonder why the earl has taken him by the hand in that way.\"\n\nSoon after the little episode of the boots Eames left his office, and walked home alone to Burton Crescent. He felt that he had gained a victory in Sir Raffle's room, but the victory there had been easy. Now he had another battle on his hands, in which, as he believed, the achievement of victory would be much more difficult. Amelia Roper was a person much more to be feared than the Chief Commissioner. He had one strong arrow in his quiver on which he would depend, if there should come to him the necessity of giving his enemy a death-wound. During the last week she had been making powerful love to Cradell, so as to justify the punishment of desertion from a former lover. He would not throw Cradell in her teeth if he could help it; but it was incumbent on him to gain a victory, and if the worst should come to the worst, he must use such weapons as destiny and the chance of war had given him.\n\nHe found Mrs. Roper in the dining-room as he entered, and immediately began his work. \"Mrs. Roper,\" he said, \"I'm going out of town the day after to-morrow.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, Mr. Eames, we know that. You're going as a visitor to the noble mansion of the Earl De Guest.\"\n\n\"I don't know about the mansion being very noble, but I'm going down into the country for a fortnight. When I come back--\"\n\n\"When you come back, Mr. Eames, I hope you'll find your room a deal more comfortable. I know it isn't quite what it should be for a gentleman like you, and I've been thinking for some time past--\"\n\n\"But, Mrs. Roper, I don't mean to come back here any more. It's just that that I want to say to you.\"\n\n\"Not come back to the crescent!\"\n\n\"No, Mrs. Roper. A fellow must move sometimes, you know; and I'm sure I've been very constant to you for a long time.\"\n\n\"But where are you going, Mr. Eames?\"\n\n\"Well; I haven't just made up my mind as yet. That is, it will depend on what I may do,--on what friends of mine may say down in the country. You'll not think I'm quarrelling with you, Mrs. Roper.\"\n\n\"It's them Lupexes as have done it,\" said Mrs. Roper, in her deep distress.\n\n\"No, indeed, Mrs. Roper, nobody has done it.\"\n\n\"Yes, it is; and I'm not going to blame you, Mr. Eames. They've made the house unfit for any decent young gentleman like you. I've been feeling that all along; but it's hard upon a lone woman like me, isn't it, Mr. Eames?\"\n\n\"But, Mrs. Roper, the Lupexes have had nothing to do with my going.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, they have; I understand it all. But what could I do, Mr. Eames? I've been giving them warning every week for the last six months; but the more I give them warning, the more they won't go. Unless I were to send for a policeman, and have a row in the house--\"\n\n\"But I haven't complained of the Lupexes, Mrs. Roper.\"\n\n\"You wouldn't be quitting without any reason, Mr. Eames. You are not going to be married in earnest, are you, Mr. Eames?\"\n\n\"Not that I know of.\"\n\n\"You may tell me; you may, indeed. I won't say a word,--not to anybody. It hasn't been my fault about Amelia. It hasn't really.\"\n\n\"Who says there's been any fault?\"\n\n\"I can see, Mr. Eames. Of course it didn't do for me to interfere. And if you had liked her, I will say I believe she'd have made as good a wife as any young man ever took; and she can make a few pounds go farther than most girls. You can understand a mother's feelings; and if there was to be anything, I couldn't spoil it; could I, now?\"\n\n\"But there isn't to be anything.\"\n\n\"So I've told her for months past. I'm not going to say anything to blame you; but young men ought to be very particular; indeed they ought.\" Johnny did not choose to hint to the disconsolate mother that it also behoved young women to be very particular, but he thought it. \"I've wished many a time, Mr. Eames, that she had never come here; indeed I have. But what's a mother to do? I couldn't put her outside the door.\" Then Mrs. Roper raised her apron up to her eyes, and began to sob.\n\n\"I'm very sorry if I've made any mischief,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"It hasn't been your fault,\" continued the poor woman, from whom, as her tears became uncontrollable, her true feelings forced themselves and the real outpouring of her feminine nature. \"Nor it hasn't been my fault. But I knew what it would come to when I saw how she was going on; and I told her so. I knew you wouldn't put up with the likes of her.\"\n\n\"Indeed, Mrs. Roper, I've always had a great regard for her, and for you too.\"\n\n\"But you weren't going to marry her. I've told her so all along, and I've begged her not to do it,--almost on my knees I have; but she wouldn't be said by me. She never would. She's always been that wilful that I'd sooner have her away from me than with me. Though she's a good young woman in the house,--she is, indeed, Mr. Eames;--and there isn't a pair of hands in it that works so hard; but it was no use my talking.\"\n\n\"I don't think any harm has been done.\"\n\n\"Yes, there has; great harm. It has made the place not respectable. It's the Lupexes is the worst. There's Miss Spruce, who has been with me for nine years,--ever since I've had the house,--she's been telling me this morning that she means to go into the country. It's all the same thing. I understand it. I can see it. The house isn't respectable, as it should be; and your mamma, if she were to know all, would have a right to be angry with me. I did mean to be respectable, Mr. Eames; I did indeed.\"\n\n\"Miss Spruce will think better of it.\"\n\n\"You don't know what I've had to go through. There's none of them pays, not regular,--only she and you. She's been like the Bank of England, has Miss Spruce.\"\n\n\"I'm afraid I've not been very regular, Mrs. Roper.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, you have. I don't think of a pound or two more or less at the end of a quarter, if I'm sure to have it some day. The butcher,--he understands one's lodgers just as well as I do,--if the money's really coming, he'll wait; but he won't wait for such as them Lupexes, whose money's nowhere. And there's Cradell; would you believe it, that fellow owes me eight and twenty pounds!\"\n\n\"Eight and twenty pounds!\"\n\n\"Yes, Mr. Eames, eight and twenty pounds! He's a fool. It's them Lupexes as have had his money. I know it. He don't talk of paying, and going away. I shall be just left with him and the Lupexes on my hands; and then the bailiffs may come and sell every stick about the place. I won't say nay to them.\" Then she threw herself into the old horsehair arm-chair, and gave way to her womanly sorrow.\n\n\"I think I'll go upstairs, and get ready for dinner,\" said Eames.\n\n\"And you must go away when you come back?\" said Mrs. Roper.\n\n\"Well, yes, I'm afraid I must. I meant you to have a month's warning from to-day. Of course I shall pay for the month.\"\n\n\"I don't want to take any advantage; indeed, I don't. But I do hope you'll leave your things. You can have them whenever you like. If Chumpend knows that you and Miss Spruce are both going, of course he'll be down upon me for his money.\" Chumpend was the butcher. But Eames made no answer to this piteous plea. Whether or no he could allow his old boots to remain in Burton Crescent for the next week or two, must depend on the manner in which he might be received by Amelia Roper this evening.\n\nWhen he came down to the drawing-room, there was no one there but Miss Spruce. \"A fine day, Miss Spruce,\" said he.\n\n\"Yes, Mr. Eames, it is a fine day for London; but don't you think the country air is very nice?\"\n\n\"Give me the town,\" said Johnny, wishing to say a good word for poor Mrs. Roper, if it were possible.\n\n\"You're a young man, Mr. Eames; but I'm only an old woman. That makes a difference,\" said Miss Spruce.\n\n\"Not much,\" said Johnny, meaning to be civil. \"You don't like to be dull any more than I do.\"\n\n\"I like to be respectable, Mr. Eames. I always have been respectable, Mr. Eames.\" This the old woman said almost in a whisper, looking anxiously to see that the door had not been opened to other listening ears.\n\n\"I'm sure Mrs. Roper is very respectable.\"\n\n\"Yes; Mrs. Roper is respectable, Mr. Eames; but there are some here that-- Hush-sh-sh!\" And the old lady put her finger up to her lips. The door opened and Mrs. Lupex swam into the room.\n\n\"How d'ye do, Miss Spruce? I declare you're always first. It's to get a chance of having one of the young gentlemen to yourself, I believe. What's the news in the city to-day, Mr. Eames? In your position now of course you hear all the news.\"\n\n\"Sir Raffle Buffle has got a new pair of shoes. I don't know that for certain, but I guess it from the time it took him to put them on.\"\n\n\"Ah! now you're quizzing. That's always the way with you gentlemen when you get a little up in the world. You don't think women are worth talking to then, unless just for a joke or so.\"\n\n\"I'd a great deal sooner talk to you, Mrs. Lupex, than I would to Sir Raffle Buffle.\"\n\n\"It's all very well for you to say that. But we women know what such compliments as those mean;--don't we, Miss Spruce? A woman that's been married five years as I have--or I may say six,--doesn't expect much attention from young men. And though I was young when I married--young in years, that is,--I'd seen too much and gone through too much to be young in heart.\" This she said almost in a whisper; but Miss Spruce heard it, and was confirmed in her belief that Burton Crescent was no longer respectable.\n\n\"I don't know what you were then, Mrs. Lupex,\" said Eames; \"but you're young enough now for anything.\"\n\n\"Mr. Eames, I'd sell all that remains of my youth at a cheap rate,--at a very cheap rate, if I could only be sure of--\"\n\n\"Sure of what, Mrs. Lupex?\"\n\n\"The undivided affection of the one person that I loved. That is all that is necessary to a woman's happiness.\"\n\n\"And isn't Lupex--\"\n\n\"Lupex! But, hush, never mind. I should not have allowed myself to be betrayed into an expression of feeling. Here's your friend Mr. Cradell. Do you know I sometimes wonder what you find in that man to be so fond of him.\" Miss Spruce saw it all, and heard it all, and positively resolved upon moving herself to those two small rooms at Dulwich.\n\nHardly a word was exchanged between Amelia and Eames before dinner. Amelia still devoted herself to Cradell, and Johnny saw that that arrow, if it should be needed, would be a strong weapon. Mrs. Roper they found seated at her place at the dining-table, and Eames could perceive the traces of her tears. Poor woman! Few positions in life could be harder to bear than hers! To be ever tugging at others for money that they could not pay; to be ever tugged at for money which she could not pay; to desire respectability for its own sake, but to be driven to confess that it was a luxury beyond her means; to put up with disreputable belongings for the sake of lucre, and then not to get the lucre, but be driven to feel that she was ruined by the attempt! How many Mrs. Ropers there are who from year to year sink down and fall away, and no one knows whither they betake themselves! One fancies that one sees them from time to time at the corners of the streets in battered bonnets and thin gowns, with the tattered remnants of old shawls upon their shoulders, still looking as though they had within them a faint remembrance of long-distant respectability. With anxious eyes they peer about, as though searching in the streets for other lodgers. Where do they get their daily morsels of bread, and their poor cups of thin tea,--their cups of thin tea, with perhaps a pennyworth of gin added to it, if Providence be good! Of this state of things Mrs. Roper had a lively appreciation, and now, poor woman, she feared that she was reaching it, by the aid of the Lupexes. On the present occasion she carved her joint of meat in silence, and sent out her slices to the good guests that would leave her, and to the bad guests that would remain, with apathetic impartiality. What was the use now of doing favour to one lodger or disfavour to another? Let them take their mutton,--they who would pay for it and they who would not. She would not have the carving of many more joints in that house if Chumpend acted up to all the threats which he had uttered to her that morning.\n\nThe reader may, perhaps, remember the little back room behind the dining parlour. A description was given in some former pages of an interview which was held between Amelia and her lover. It was in that room that all the interviews of Mrs. Roper's establishment had their existence. A special room for interviews is necessary in all households of a mixed nature. If a man lives alone with his wife, he can have his interviews where he pleases. Sons and daughters, even when they are grown up, hardly create the necessity of an interview-chamber, though some such need may be felt if the daughters are marriageable and independent in their natures. But when the family becomes more complicated than this, if an extra young man be introduced, or an aunt comes into residence, or grown up children by a former wife interfere with the domestic simplicity, then such accommodation becomes quite indispensable. No woman would think of taking in lodgers without such a room; and this room there was at Mrs. Roper's, very small and dingy, but still sufficient,--just behind the dining parlour and opposite to the kitchen stairs. Hither, after dinner, Amelia was summoned. She had just seated herself between Mrs. Lupex and Miss Spruce, ready to do battle with the former because she would stay, and with the latter because she would go, when she was called out by the servant girl.\n\n\"Miss Mealyer, Miss Mealyer,--sh--sh--sh!\" And Amelia, looking round, saw a large red hand beckoning to her. \"He's down there,\" said Jemima, as soon as her young mistress had joined her, \"and wants to see you most partic'lar.\"\n\n\"Which of 'em?\" asked Amelia, in a whisper.\n\n\"Why, Mr. Heames, to be sure. Don't you go and have anythink to say to the other one, Miss Mealyer, pray don't; he ain't no good; he ain't indeed.\"\n\nAmelia stood still for a moment on the landing, calculating whether it would be well for her to have the interview, or well to decline it. Her objects were two;--or, rather, her object was in its nature twofold. She was, naturally, anxious to drive John Eames to desperation; and anxious also, by some slight added artifice, to make sure of Cradell if Eames's desperation did not have a very speedy effect. She agreed with Jemima's criticism in the main, but she did not go quite so far as to think that Cradell was no good at all. Let it be Eames, if Eames were possible; but let the other string be kept for use if Eames were not possible. Poor girl! in coming to this resolve she had not done so without agony. She had a heart, and with such power as it gave her, she loved John Eames. But the world had been hard to her; knocking her about hither and thither unmercifully; threatening, as it now threatened, to take from her what few good things she enjoyed. When a girl is so circumstanced she cannot afford to attend to her heart. She almost resolved not to see Eames on the present occasion, thinking that he might be made the more desperate by such refusal, and remembering also that Cradell was in the house and would know of it.\n\n\"He's there a-waiting, Miss Mealyer. Why don't yer come down?\" and Jemima plucked her young mistress by the arm.\n\n\"I am coming,\" said Amelia. And with dignified steps she descended to the interview.\n\n\"Here she is, Mr. Heames,\" said the girl. And then Johnny found himself alone with his lady-love.\n\n\"You have sent for me, Mr. Eames,\" she said, giving her head a little toss, and turning her face away from him. \"I was engaged upstairs, but I thought it uncivil not to come down to you as you sent for me so special.\"\n\n\"Yes, Miss Roper, I did want to see you very particularly.\"\n\n\"Oh, dear!\" she exclaimed, and he understood fully that the exclamation referred to his having omitted the customary use of her Christian name.\n\n\"I saw your mother before dinner, and I told her that I am going away the day after to-morrow.\"\n\n\"We all know about that;--to the earl's, of course!\" And then there was another chuck of her head.\n\n\"And I told her also that I had made up my mind not to come back to Burton Crescent.\"\n\n\"What! leave the house altogether!\"\n\n\"Well; yes. A fellow must make a change sometimes, you know.\"\n\n\"And where are you going, John?\"\n\n\"That I don't know as yet.\"\n\n\"Tell me the truth, John; are you going to be married? Are you--going--to marry--that young woman,--Mr. Crosbie's leavings? I demand to have an answer at once. Are you going to marry her?\"\n\nHe had determined very resolutely that nothing she might say should make him angry, but when she thus questioned him about \"Crosbie's leavings\" he found it very difficult to keep his temper. \"I have not come,\" said he, \"to speak to you about any one but ourselves.\"\n\n\"That put-off won't do with me, sir. You are not to treat any girl you may please in that sort of way;--oh, John!\" Then she looked at him as though she did not know whether to fly at him and cover him with kisses, or to fly at him and tear his hair.\n\n\"I know I haven't behaved quite as I should have done,\" he began.\n\n\"Oh, John!\" and she shook her head. \"You mean, then, to tell me that you are going to marry her?\"\n\n\"I mean to say nothing of the kind. I only mean to say that I am going away from Burton Crescent.\"\n\n\"John Eames, I wonder what you think will come to you! Will you answer me this; have I had a promise from you,--a distinct promise, over and over again, or have I not?\"\n\n\"I don't know about a distinct promise--\"\n\n\"Well, well! I did think that you was a gentleman that would not go back from your word. I did think that. I did think that you would never put a young lady to the necessity of bringing forward her own letters to prove that she is not expecting more than she has a right! You don't know! And that, after all that has been between us! John Eames!\" And again it seemed to him as though she were about to fly.\n\n\"I tell you that I know I haven't behaved well. What more can I say?\"\n\n\"What more can you say? Oh, John! to ask me such a question! If you were a man you would know very well what more to say. But all you private secretaries are given to deceit, as the sparks fly upwards. However, I despise you,--I do, indeed. I despise you.\"\n\n\"If you despise me, we might as well shake hands and part at once. I daresay that will be best. One doesn't like to be despised, of course; but sometimes one can't help it.\" And then he put out his hand to her.\n\n\"And is this to be the end of all?\" she said, taking it.\n\n\"Well, yes; I suppose so. You say I'm despised.\"\n\n\"You shouldn't take up a poor girl in that way for a sharp word,--not when she is suffering as I am made to suffer. If you only think of it,--think what I have been expecting!\" And now Amelia began to cry, and to look as though she were going to fall into his arms.\n\n\"It is better to tell the truth,\" he said; \"isn't it?\"\n\n\"But it shouldn't be the truth.\"\n\n\"But it is the truth. I couldn't do it. I should ruin myself and you too, and we should never be happy.\"\n\n\"I should be happy,--very happy indeed.\" At this moment the poor girl's tears were unaffected, and her words were not artful. For a minute or two her heart,--her actual heart,--was allowed to prevail.\n\n\"It cannot be, Amelia. Will you not say good-by?\"\n\n\"Good-by,\" she said, leaning against him as she spoke.\n\n\"I do so hope you will be happy,\" he said. And then, putting his arm round her waist, he kissed her; which he certainly ought not to have done.\n\nWhen the interview was over, he escaped out into the crescent, and as he walked down through the squares,--Woburn Square, and Russell Square, and Bedford Square,--towards the heart of London, he felt himself elated almost to a state of triumph. He had got himself well out of his difficulties, and now he would be ready for his love-tale to Lily.\n\n# CHAPTER LII. THE FIRST VISIT TO THE GUESTWICK BRIDGE.\n\nWhen John Eames arrived at Guestwick Manor, he was first welcomed by Lady Julia. \"My dear Mr. Eames,\" she said, \"I cannot tell you how glad we are to see you.\" After that she always called him John, and treated him throughout his visit with wonderful kindness. No doubt that affair of the bull had in some measure produced this feeling; no doubt, also, she was well disposed to the man who she hoped might be accepted as a lover by Lily Dale. But I am inclined to think that the fact of his having beaten Crosbie had been the most potential cause of this affection for our hero on the part of Lady Julia. Ladies,--especially discreet old ladies, such as Lady Julia De Guest,--are bound to entertain pacific theories, and to condemn all manner of violence. Lady Julia would have blamed any one who might have advised Eames to commit an assault upon Crosbie. But, nevertheless, deeds of prowess are still dear to the female heart, and a woman, be she ever so old and discreet, understands and appreciates the summary justice which may be done by means of a thrashing. Lady Julia, had she been called upon to talk of it, would undoubtedly have told Eames that he had committed a fault in striking Mr. Crosbie; but the deed had been done, and Lady Julia became very fond of John Eames.\n\n\"Vickers shall show you your room, if you like to go upstairs; but you'll find my brother close about the house if you choose to go out; I saw him not half an hour since.\" But John seemed to be well satisfied to sit in the arm-chair over the fire, and talk to his hostess; so neither of them moved.\n\n\"And now that you're a private secretary, how do you like it?\"\n\n\"I like the work well enough; only I don't like the man, Lady Julia. But I shouldn't say so, because he is such an intimate friend of your brother's.\"\n\n\"An intimate friend of Theodore's!--Sir Raffle Buffle!\" Lady Julia stiffened her back and put on a serious face, not being exactly pleased at being told that the Earl De Guest had any such intimate friend.\n\n\"At any rate he tells me so about four times a day, Lady Julia. And he particularly wants to come down here next September.\"\n\n\"Did he tell you that, too?\"\n\n\"Indeed he did. You can't believe what a goose he is! Then his voice sounds like a cracked bell; it's the most disagreeable voice you ever heard in your life. And one has always to be on one's guard lest he should make one do something that is--is--that isn't quite the thing for a gentleman. You understand;--what the messenger ought to do.\"\n\n\"You shouldn't be too much afraid of your own dignity.\"\n\n\"No, I'm not. If Lord De Guest were to ask me to fetch him his shoes, I'd run to Guestwick and back for them and think nothing of it,--just because I know he's my friend. He'd have a right to send me. But I'm not going to do such things as that for Sir Raffle Buffle.\"\n\n\"Fetch him his shoes!\"\n\n\"That's what FitzHoward had to do, and he didn't like it.\"\n\n\"Isn't Mr. FitzHoward nephew to the Duchess of St. Bungay?\"\n\n\"Nephew, or cousin, or something.\"\n\n\"Dear me!\" said Lady Julia, \"what a horrible man!\" And in this way John Eames and her ladyship became very intimate.\n\nThere was no one at dinner at the Manor that day but the earl and his sister and their single guest. The earl when he came in was very warm in his welcome, slapping his young friend on the back, and poking jokes at him with a good-humoured if not brilliant pleasantry.\n\n\"Thrashed anybody lately, John?\"\n\n\"Nobody to speak of,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Brought your nightcap down for your out-o'-doors nap?\"\n\n\"No; but I've got a grand stick for the bull,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Ah! that's no joke now, I can tell you,\" said the earl. \"We had to sell him, and it half broke my heart. We don't know what had come to him, but he became quite unruly after that;--knocked Darvell down in the straw-yard! It was a very bad business,--a very bad business, indeed! Come, go and dress. Do you remember how you came down to dinner that day? I shall never forget how Crofts stared at you. Come, you've only got twenty minutes, and you London fellows always want an hour.\"\n\n\"He's entitled to some consideration now he's a private secretary,\" said Lady Julia.\n\n\"Bless us all! yes; I forgot that. Come, Mr. Private Secretary, don't stand on the grandeur of your neck-tie to-day, as there's nobody here but ourselves. You shall have an opportunity to-morrow.\"\n\nThen Johnny was handed over to the groom of the chambers, and exactly in twenty minutes he re-appeared in the drawing-room.\n\nAs soon as Lady Julia had left them after dinner, the earl began to explain his plan for the coming campaign. \"I'll tell you now what I have arranged,\" said he. \"The squire is to be here to-morrow with his eldest niece,--your Miss Lily's sister, you know.\"\n\n\"What, Bell?\"\n\n\"Yes, with Bell, if her name is Bell. She's a very pretty girl, too. I don't know whether she's not the prettiest of the two, after all.\"\n\n\"That's a matter of opinion.\"\n\n\"Just so, Johnny; and do you stick to your own. They're coming here for three or four days. Lady Julia did ask Mrs. Dale and Lily. I wonder whether you'll let me call her Lily?\"\n\n\"Oh, dear! I wish I might have the power of letting you.\"\n\n\"That's just the battle that you've got to fight. But the mother and the younger sister wouldn't come. Lady Julia says it's all right;--that, as a matter of course, she wouldn't come when she heard you were to be here. I don't quite understand it. In my days the young girls were ready enough to go where they knew they'd meet their lovers, and I never thought any the worse of them for it.\"\n\n\"It wasn't because of that,\" said Eames.\n\n\"That's what Lady Julia says, and I always find her to be right in things of that sort. And she says you'll have a better chance in going over there than you would here, if she were in the same house with you. If I was going to make love to a girl, of course I'd sooner have her close to me,--staying in the same house. I should think it the best fun in the world. And we might have had a dance, and all that kind of thing. But I couldn't make her come, you know.\"\n\n\"Oh, no; of course not.\"\n\n\"And Lady Julia thinks that it's best as it is. You must go over, you know, and get the mother on your side, if you can. I take it, the truth is this;--you mustn't be angry with me, you know, for saying it.\"\n\n\"You may be sure of that.\"\n\n\"I suppose she was fond of that fellow, Crosbie. She can't be very fond of him now, I should think, after the way he has treated her; but she'll find a difficulty in making her confession that she really likes you better than she ever liked him. Of course that's what you'll want her to say.\"\n\n\"I want her to say that she'll be my wife,--some day.\"\n\n\"And when she has agreed to the some day, then you'll begin to press her to agree to your day;--eh, sir? My belief is you'll bring her round. Poor girl! why should she break her heart when a decent fellow like you will only be too glad to make her a happy woman?\" And in this way the earl talked to Eames till the latter almost believed that the difficulties were vanishing from out of his path. \"Could it be possible,\" he asked himself, as he went to bed, \"that in a fortnight's time Lily Dale should have accepted him as her future husband?\" Then he remembered that day on which Crosbie, with the two girls, had called at his mother's house, when in the bitterness of his heart, he had sworn to himself that he would always regard Crosbie as his enemy. Since then the world had gone well with him; and he had no longer any very bitter feeling against Crosbie. That matter had been arranged on the platform of the Paddington Station. He felt that if Lily would now accept him he could almost shake hands with Crosbie. The episode in his life and in Lily's would have been painful; but he would learn to look back upon that without regret, if Lily could be taught to believe that a kind fate had at last given her to the better of her two lovers. \"I'm afraid she won't bring herself to forget him,\" he had said to the earl. \"She'll only be too happy to forget him,\" the earl had answered, \"if you can induce her to begin the attempt. Of course it is very bitter at first;--all the world knew about it; but, poor girl, she is not to be wretched for ever, because of that. Do you go about your work with some little confidence, and I doubt not but what you'll have your way. You have everybody in your favour,--the squire, her mother, and all.\" While such words as these were in his ears how could he fail to hope and to be confident? While he was sitting cozily over his bedroom fire he resolved that it should be as the earl had said. But when he got up on the following morning, and stood shivering as he came out of his bath, he could not feel the same confidence. \"Of course I shall go to her,\" he said to himself, \"and make a plain story of it. But I know what her answer will be. She will tell me that she cannot forget him.\" Then his feelings towards Crosbie were not so friendly as they had been on the previous evening.\n\nHe did not visit the Small House on that, his first day. It had been thought better that he should first meet the squire and Bell at Guestwick Manor, so he postponed his visit to Mrs. Dale till the next morning.\n\n\"Go when you like,\" said the earl. \"There's the brown cob for you to do what you like with him while you are here.\"\n\n\"I'll go and see my mother,\" said John; \"but I won't take the cob to-day. If you'll let me have him to-morrow, I'll ride to Allington.\" So he walked off to Guestwick by himself.\n\nHe knew well every yard of the ground over which he went, remembering every gate and stile and greensward from the time of his early boyhood. And now as he went along through his old haunts, he could not but look back and think of the thoughts which had filled his mind in his earlier wanderings. As I have said before, in some of these pages, no walks taken by the man are so crowded with thought as those taken by the boy. He had been early taught to understand that the world to him would be very hard; that he had nothing to look to but his own exertions, and that those exertions would not, unfortunately, be backed by any great cleverness of his own. I do not know that anybody had told him that he was a fool; but he had come to understand, partly through his own modesty, and partly, no doubt, through the somewhat obtrusive diffidence of his mother, that he was less sharp than other lads. It is probably true that he had come to his sharpness later in life than is the case with many young men. He had not grown on the sunny side of the wall. Before that situation in the Income-tax Office had fallen in his way, very humble modes of life had offered themselves,--or, rather, had not offered themselves for his acceptance. He had endeavoured to become an usher at a commercial seminary, not supposed to be in a very thriving condition; but he had been, luckily, found deficient in his arithmetic. There had been some chance of his going into the leather-warehouse of Messrs. Basil and Pigskin, but those gentlemen had required a premium, and any payment of that kind had been quite out of his mother's power. A country attorney, who had known the family for years, had been humbly solicited, the widow almost kneeling before him with tears, to take Johnny by the hand and make a clerk of him; but the attorney had discovered that Master Johnny Eames was not supposed to be sharp, and would have none of him. During those days, those gawky, gainless, un-admired days, in which he had wandered about the lanes of Guestwick as his only amusement, and had composed hundreds of rhymes in honour of Lily Dale which no human eye but his own had ever seen, he had come to regard himself as almost a burden upon the earth. Nobody seemed to want him. His own mother was very anxious; but her anxiety seemed to him to indicate a continual desire to get rid of him. For hours upon hours he would fill his mind with castles in the air, dreaming of wonderful successes in the midst of which Lily Dale always reigned as a queen. He would carry on the same story in his imagination from month to month, almost contenting himself with such ideal happiness. Had it not been for the possession of that power, what comfort could there have been to him in his life? There are lads of seventeen who can find happiness in study, who can busy themselves in books and be at their ease among the creations of other minds. These are they who afterwards become well-informed men. It was not so with John Eames. He had never been studious. The perusal of a novel was to him in those days a slow affair; and of poetry he read but little, storing up accurately in his memory all that he did read. But he created for himself his own romance, though to the eye a most unromantic youth; and he wandered through the Guestwick woods with many thoughts of which they who knew him best knew nothing. All this he thought of now as, with devious steps, he made his way towards his old home;--with very devious steps, for he went backwards through the woods by a narrow path which led right away from the town down to a little water-course, over which stood a wooden foot-bridge with a rail. He stood on the centre of the plank, at a spot which he knew well, and rubbing his hand upon the rail, cleansed it for the space of a few inches of the vegetable growth\n\nwas still the word LILY. When he cut those letters she had been almost a child. \"I wonder whether she will come here with me and let me show it to her,\" he said to himself. Then he took out his knife and cleared the cuttings of the letters, and having done so, leaned upon the rail, and looked down upon the running water. How well things in the world had gone for him! How well! And yet what would it all be if Lily would not come to him? How well the world had gone for him! In those days when he stood there carving the girl's name everybody had seemed to regard him as a heavy burden, and he had so regarded himself. Now he was envied by many, respected by many, taken by the hand as a friend by those high in the world's esteem. When he had come near the Guestwick Mansion in his old walks,--always, however, keeping at a great distance lest the grumpy old lord should be down upon him and scold him,--he had little dreamed that he and the grumpy old lord would ever be together on such familiar terms, that he would tell to that lord more of his private thoughts than to any other living being; yet it had come to that. The grumpy old lord had now told him that that gift of money was to be his whether Lily Dale accepted him or no. \"Indeed, the thing's done,\" said the grumpy lord, pulling out from his pocket certain papers, \"and you've got to receive the dividends as they become due.\" Then, when Johnny had expostulated,--as, indeed, the circumstances had left him no alternative but to expostulate,--the earl had roughly bade him hold his tongue, telling him that he would have to fetch Sir Raffle's boots directly he got back to London. So the conversation had quickly turned itself away to Sir Raffle, whom they had both ridiculed with much satisfaction. \"If he finds his way down here in September, Master Johnny, or in any other month either, you may fit my head with a foolscap. Not remember, indeed! Is it not wonderful that any man should make himself so mean a fool?\" All this was thought over again, as Eames leaned upon the bridge. He remembered every word, and remembered many other words,--earlier words, spoken years ago, filling him with desolation as to the prospects of his life. It had seemed that his friends had united in prophesying that the outlook into the world for him was hopeless, and that the earning of bread must be for ever beyond his power. And now his lines had fallen to him in very pleasant places, and he was among those whom the world had determined to caress. And yet, what would it all be if Lily would not share his happiness? When he had carved that name on the rail, his love for Lily had been an idea. It had now become a reality which might probably be full of pain. If it were so,--if such should be the result of his wooing,--would not those old dreamy days have been better than these--the days of his success?\n\nIt was one o'clock by the time that he reached his mother's house, and he found her and his sister in a troubled and embarrassed state. \"Of course you know, John,\" said his mother, as soon as their first embraces were over, \"that we are going to dine at the Manor this evening?\" But he did not know it, neither the earl nor Lady Julia having said anything on the subject. \"Of course we are going,\" said Mrs. Eames, \"and it was so very kind. But I've never been out to such a house for so many years, John, and I do feel in such a twitter. I dined there once, soon after we were married; but I never have been there since that.\"\n\n\"It's not the earl I mind, but Lady Julia,\" said Mary Eames.\n\n\"She's the most good-natured woman in the world,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Oh, dear; people say she is so cross!\"\n\n\"That's because people don't know her. If I was asked who is the kindest-hearted woman I know in the world, I think I should say Lady Julia De Guest. I think I should.\"\n\n\"Ah! but then they're so fond of you,\" said the admiring mother. \"You saved his lordship's life,--under Providence.\"\n\n\"That's all bosh, mother. You ask Dr. Crofts. He knows them as well as I do.\"\n\n\"Dr. Crofts is going to marry Bell Dale,\" said Mary; and then the conversation was turned from the subject of Lady Julia's perfections, and the awe inspired by the earl.\n\n\"Crofts going to marry Bell!\" exclaimed Eames, thinking almost with dismay of the doctor's luck in thus getting himself accepted all at once, while he had been suing with the constancy almost of a Jacob.\n\n\"Yes,\" said Mary; \"and they say that she has refused her cousin Bernard, and that, therefore, the squire is taking away the house from them. You know they're all coming into Guestwick.\"\n\n\"Yes, I know they are. But I don't believe that the squire is taking away the house.\"\n\n\"Why should they come then? Why should they give up such a charming place as that?\"\n\n\"Rent-free!\" said Mrs. Eames.\n\n\"I don't know why they should come away, but I can't believe the squire is turning them out; at any rate not for that reason.\" The squire was prepared to advocate John's suit, and therefore John was bound to do battle on the squire's behalf.\n\n\"He is a very stern man,\" said Mrs. Eames, \"and they say that since that affair of poor Lily's he has been more cross than ever with them. As far as I know, it was not Lily's fault.\"\n\n\"Poor Lily!\" said Mary. \"I do pity her. If I was her I should hardly know how to show my face; I shouldn't, indeed.\"\n\n\"And why shouldn't she show her face?\" said John, in an angry tone. \"What has she done to be ashamed of? Show her face indeed! I cannot understand the spite which one woman will sometimes have to another.\"\n\n\"There is no spite, John; and it's very wrong of you to say so,\" said Mary, defending herself. \"But it is a very unpleasant thing for a girl to be jilted. All the world knows that she was engaged to him.\"\n\n\"And all the world knows--\" But he would not proceed to declare that all the world knew also that Crosbie had been well thrashed for his baseness. It would not become him to mention that even before his mother and sister. All the world did know it; all the world that cared to know anything of the matter;--except Lily Dale herself. Nobody had ever yet told Lily Dale of that occurrence at the Paddington Railway Station, and it was well for John that her friends and his had been so discreet.\n\n\"Oh, of course you are her champion,\" said Mary. \"And I didn't mean to say anything unkind. Indeed I didn't. Of course it was a misfortune.\"\n\n\"I think it was the best piece of good fortune that could have happened to her, not to marry a d---- scoundrel like--\"\n\n\"Oh, John!\" exclaimed Mrs. Eames.\n\n\"I beg your pardon, mother. But it isn't swearing to call such a man as that a d---- scoundrel.\" And he particularly emphasized the naughty word, thinking that thereby he would add to its import, and take away from its naughtiness. \"But we won't talk any more about him. I hate the man's very name. I hated him the first moment that I saw him, and knew that he was a blackguard from his look. And I don't believe a word about the squire having been cross to them. Indeed I know he has been the reverse of cross. So Bell is going to marry Dr. Crofts!\"\n\n\"There is no doubt on earth about that,\" said Mary. \"And they say that Bernard Dale is going abroad with his regiment.\"\n\nThen John discussed with his mother his duties as private secretary, and his intention of leaving Mrs. Roper's house. \"I suppose it isn't nice enough for you now, John,\" said his mother.\n\n\"It never was very nice, mother, to tell you the truth. There were people there--. But you mustn't think I am turning up my nose because I'm getting grand. I don't want to live any better than we all lived at Mrs. Roper's; but she took in persons that were not agreeable. There is a Mr. and Mrs. Lupex there.\" Then he described something of their life in Burton Crescent, but did not say much about Amelia Roper. Amelia Roper had not made her appearance in Guestwick, as he had once feared that she would do; and therefore it did not need that he should at present make known to his mother that episode in his life.\n\nWhen he got back to the Manor House he found that Mr. Dale and his niece had arrived. They were both sitting with Lady Julia when he went into the morning room, and Lord De Guest was standing over the fire talking to them. Eames as he came among them felt terribly conscious of his position, as though all there were aware that he had been brought down from London on purpose to make a declaration of love;--as, indeed, all of them were aware of that fact. Bell, though no one had told her so in direct words, was as sure of it as the others.\n\n\"Here comes the prince of matadores,\" said the earl.\n\n\"No, my lord; you're the prince. I'm only your first follower.\" Though he could contrive that his words should be gay, his looks were sheepish, and when he gave his hand to the squire it was only by a struggle that he could bring himself to look straight into the old man's face.\n\n\"I'm very glad to see you, John,\" said the squire, \"very glad indeed.\"\n\n\"And so am I,\" said Bell. \"I have been so happy to hear that you have been promoted at your office, and so is mamma.\"\n\n\"I hope Mrs. Dale is quite well,\" said he;--\"and Lily.\" The word had been pronounced, but it had been done with so manifest an effort that all in the room were conscious of it, and paused as Bell prepared her little answer.\n\n\"My sister has been very ill, you know,--with scarlatina. But she has recovered with wonderful quickness, and is nearly well again now. She will be so glad to see you if you will go over.\"\n\n\"Yes; I shall certainly go over,\" said John.\n\n\"And now shall I show you your room, Miss Dale?\" said Lady Julia. And so the party was broken up, and the ice had been broken.\n\n# CHAPTER LIII. LOQUITUR HOPKINS.\n\nThe squire had been told that his niece Bell had accepted Dr. Crofts, and he had signified a sort of acquiescence in the arrangement, saying that if it were to be so, he had nothing to say against Dr. Crofts. He spoke this in a melancholy tone of voice, wearing on his face that look of subdued sorrow which was now almost habitual to him. It was to Mrs. Dale that he spoke on the subject. \"I could have wished that it might have been otherwise,\" he said, \"as you are well aware. I had family reasons for wishing that it might be otherwise. But I have nothing to say against it. Dr. Crofts, as her husband, shall be welcome to my house.\" Mrs. Dale, who had expected much worse than this, began to thank him for his kindness, and to say that she also would have preferred to see her daughter married to her cousin. \"But in such a matter the decision should be left entirely to the girl. Don't you think so?\"\n\n\"I have not a word to say against her,\" he repeated. Then Mrs. Dale left him, and told her daughter that her uncle's manner of receiving the news had been, for him, very gracious. \"You were his favourite, but Lily will be so now,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"I don't care a bit about that;--or, rather, I do care, and think it will be in every way better. But as I, who am the naughty one, will go away, and as Lily, who is the good one, will remain with you, doesn't it almost seem a pity that you should be leaving the house?\"\n\nMrs. Dale thought it was almost a pity, but she could not say so now. \"You think Lily will remain,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes, mamma; I feel sure she will.\"\n\n\"She was always very fond of John Eames;--and he is doing so well.\"\n\n\"It will be of no use, mamma. She is fond of him,--very fond. In a sort of a way she loves him--so well, that I feel sure she never mentions his name without some inward reference to her old childish thoughts and fancies. If he had come before Mr. Crosbie it would have all been well with her. But she cannot do it now. Her pride would prevent her, even if her heart permitted it. Oh! dear; it's very wrong of me to say so, after all that I have said before; but I almost wish you were not going. Uncle Christopher seems to be less hard than he used to be; and as I was the sinner, and as I am disposed of--\"\n\n\"It is too late now, my dear.\"\n\n\"And we should neither of us have the courage to mention it to Lily,\" said Bell.\n\nOn the following morning the squire sent for his sister-in-law, as it was his wont to do when necessity came for any discussion on matters of business. This was perfectly understood between them, and such sending was not taken as indicating any lack of courtesy on the part of Mr. Dale. \"Mary,\" he said, as soon as Mrs. Dale was seated, \"I shall do for Bell exactly what I have proposed to do for Lily. I had intended more than that once, of course. But then it would all have gone into Bernard's pocket; as it is, I shall make no difference between them. They shall each have a hundred a year,--that is, when they marry. You had better tell Crofts to speak to me.\"\n\n\"Mr. Dale, he doesn't expect it. He does not expect a penny.\"\n\n\"So much the better for him; and, indeed, so much the better for her. He won't make her the less welcome to his home because she brings some assistance to it.\"\n\n\"We have never thought of it,--any of us. The offer has come so suddenly that I don't know what I ought to say.\"\n\n\"Say--nothing. If you choose to make me a return for it--; but I am only doing what I conceive to be my duty, and have no right to ask for a kindness in return.\"\n\n\"But what kindness can we show you, Mr. Dale?\"\n\n\"Remain in that house.\" In saying these last words he spoke as though he were again angry,--as though he were again laying down the law to them,--as though he were telling her of a duty which was due to him and incumbent on her. His voice was as stern and his face as acid as ever. He said that he was asking for a kindness; but surely no man ever asked for kindness in a voice so peremptory. \"Remain in that house.\" Then he turned himself in towards his table as though he had no more to say.\n\nBut Mrs. Dale was beginning, now at last, to understand something of his mind and real character. He could be affectionate and forbearing in his giving; but when asking, he could not be otherwise than stern. Indeed, he could not ask; he could only demand.\n\n\"We have done so much now,\" Mrs. Dale began to plead.\n\n\"Well, well, well. I did not mean to speak about that. Things are unpacked easier than they are packed. But, however-- Never mind. Bell is to go with me this afternoon to Guestwick Manor. Let her be up here at two. Grimes can bring her box round, I suppose.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes: of course.\"\n\n\"And don't be talking to her about money before she starts. I had rather you didn't;--you understand. But when you see Crofts, tell him to come to me. Indeed, he'd better come at once, if this thing is to go on quickly.\"\n\nIt may easily be understood that Mrs. Dale would disobey the injunctions contained in the squire's last words. It was quite out of the question that she should return to her daughters and not tell them the result of her morning's interview with their uncle. A hundred a year in the doctor's modest household would make all the difference between plenty and want, between modest plenty and endurable want. Of course she told them, giving Bell to understand that she must dissemble so far as to pretend ignorance of the affair.\n\n\"I shall thank him at once,\" said Bell; \"and tell him that I did not at all expect it, but am not too proud to accept it.\"\n\n\"Pray don't, my dear; not just now. I am breaking a sort of promise in telling you at all,--only I could not keep it to myself. And he has so many things to worry him! Though he says nothing about it now, he has half broken his heart about you and Bernard.\" Then, too, Mrs. Dale told the girls what request the squire had just made, and the manner in which he had made it. \"The tone of his voice as he spoke brought tears into my eyes. I almost wish we had not done anything.\"\n\n\"But, mamma,\" said Lily, \"what difference can it make to him? You know that our presence near him was always a trouble to him. He never really wanted us. He liked to have Bell there when he thought that Bell would marry his pet.\"\n\n\"Don't be unkind, Lily.\"\n\n\"I don't mean to be unkind. Why shouldn't Bernard be his pet? I love Bernard dearly, and always thought it the best point in uncle Christopher that he was so fond of him. I knew, you know, that it was no use. Of course I knew it, as I understood all about--somebody else. But Bernard is his pet.\"\n\n\"He's fond of you all, in his own way,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"But is he fond of you?--that's the question,\" said Lily. \"We could have forgiven him anything done to us, and have put up with any words he might have spoken to us, because he regards us as children. His giving a hundred a year to Bell won't make you comfortable in this house if he still domineers over you. If a neighbour be neighbourly, near neighbourhood is very nice. But uncle Christopher has not been neighbourly. He has wanted to be more than an uncle to us, on condition that he might be less than a brother to you. Bell and I have always felt that his regard on such terms was not worth having.\"\n\n\"I almost feel that we have been wrong,\" said Mrs. Dale; \"but in truth I never thought that the matter would be to him one of so much moment.\"\n\nWhen Bell had gone, Mrs. Dale and Lily were not disposed to continue with much energy the occupation on which they had all been employed for some days past. There had been life and excitement in the work when they had first commenced their packing, but now it was grown wearisome, dull, and distasteful. Indeed so much of it was done that but little was left to employ them, except those final strappings and fastenings, and that last collection of odds and ends which could not be accomplished till they were absolutely on the point of starting. The squire had said that unpacking would be easier than packing, and Mrs. Dale, as she wandered about among the hampers and cases, began to consider whether the task of restoring all the things to their old places would be very disagreeable. She said nothing of this to Lily, and Lily herself, whatever might be her thoughts, made no such suggestion to her mother.\n\n\"I think Hopkins will miss us more than any one else,\" she said. \"Hopkins will have no one to scold.\"\n\nJust at that moment Hopkins appeared at the parlour window, and signified his desire for a conference.\n\n\"You must come round,\" said Lily. \"It's too cold for the window to be opened. I always like to get him into the house, because he feels himself a little abashed by the chairs and tables; or, perhaps, it is the carpet that is too much for him. Out on the gravel-walks he is such a terrible tyrant, and in the greenhouse he almost tramples upon one!\"\n\nHopkins, when he did appear at the parlour door, seemed by his manner to justify Lily's discretion. He was not at all masterful in his tone or bearing, and seemed to pay to the chairs and tables all the deference which they could have expected.\n\n\"So you be going in earnest, ma'am,\" he said, looking down at Mrs. Dale's feet.\n\nAs Mrs. Dale did not answer him at once, Lily spoke:--\"Yes, Hopkins, we are going in a very few days, now. We shall see you sometimes, I hope, over at Guestwick.\"\n\n\"Humph!\" said Hopkins. \"So you be really going! I didn't think it'd ever come to that, miss; I didn't indeed,--and no more it oughtn't; but of course it isn't for me to speak.\"\n\n\"People must change their residence sometimes, you know,\" said Mrs. Dale, using the same argument by which Eames had endeavoured to excuse his departure to Mrs. Roper.\n\n\"Well, ma'am; it ain't for me to say anything. But this I will say, I've lived here about t' squire's place, man and boy, jist all my life, seeing I was born here, as you knows, Mrs. Dale; and of all the bad things I ever see come about the place, this is a sight the worst.\"\n\n\"Oh, Hopkins!\"\n\n\"The worst of all, ma'am; the worst of all! It'll just kill t' squire! There's ne'ery doubt in the world about that. It'll be the very death of t' old man.\"\n\n\"That's nonsense, Hopkins,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Very well, miss. I don't say but what it is nonsense; only you'll see. There's Mr. Bernard,--he's gone away; and by all accounts he never did care very much for the place. They all say he's a-going to the Hingies. And Miss Bell is going to be married,--which is all proper, in course; why shouldn't she? And why shouldn't you, too, Miss Lily?\"\n\n\"Perhaps I shall, some day, Hopkins.\"\n\n\"There's no day like the present, Miss Lily. And I do say this, that the man as pitched into him would be the man for my money.\" This, which Hopkins spoke in the excitement of the moment, was perfectly unintelligible to Lily, and Mrs. Dale, who shuddered as she heard him, said not a word to call for any explanation. \"But,\" continued Hopkins, \"that's all as it may be, Miss Lily, and you be in the hands of Providence,--as is others.\"\n\n\"Exactly so, Hopkins.\"\n\n\"But why should your mamma be all for going away? She ain't going to marry no one. Here's the house, and there's she, and there's t' squire; and why should she be for going away? So much going away all at once can't be for any good. It's just a breaking up of everything, as though nothing wasn't good enough for nobody. I never went away, and I can't abide it.\"\n\n\"Well, Hopkins; it's settled now,\" said Mrs. Dale, \"and I'm afraid it can't be unsettled.\"\n\n\"Settled;--well. Tell me this: do you expect, Mrs. Dale, that he's to live there all alone by hisself without any one to say a cross word to,--unless it be me or Dingles; for Jolliffe's worse than nobody, he's so mortial cross hisself. Of course he can't stand it. If you goes away, Mrs. Dale, Mister Bernard, he'll be squire in less than twelve months. He'll come back from the Hingies, then, I suppose?\"\n\n\"I don't think my brother-in-law will take it in that way, Hopkins.\"\n\n\"Ah, ma'am, you don't know him,--not as I knows him;--all the ins and outs and crinks and crannies of him. I knows him as I does the old apple-trees that I've been a-handling for forty year. There's a deal of bad wood about them old cankered trees, and some folk say they ain't worth the ground they stand on; but I know where the sap runs, and when the fruit-blossom shows itself I know where the fruit will be the sweetest. It don't take much to kill one of them old trees,--but there's life in 'm yet if they be well handled.\"\n\n\"I'm sure I hope my brother's life may be long spared to him,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Then don't be taking yourself away, ma'am, into them gashly lodgings at Guestwick. I says they are gashly for the likes of a Dale. It is not for me to speak, ma'am, of course. And I only came up now just to know what things you'd like with you out of the greenhouse.\"\n\n\"Oh, nothing, Hopkins, thank you,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"He told me to put up for you the best I could pick, and I means to do it;\" and Hopkins, as he spoke, indicated by a motion of his head that he was making reference to the squire.\n\n\"We shan't have any place for them,\" said Lily.\n\n\"I must send a few, miss, just to cheer you up a bit. I fear you'll be very dolesome there. And the doctor,--he ain't got what you can call a regular garden, but there is a bit of a place behind.\"\n\n\"But we wouldn't rob the dear old place,\" said Lily.\n\n\"For the matter of that what does it signify? T' squire'll be that wretched he'll turn sheep in here to destroy the place, or he'll have the garden ploughed. You see if he don't. As for the place, the place is clean done for, if you leave it. You don't suppose he'll go and let the Small House to strangers. T' squire ain't one of that sort any ways.\"\n\n\"Ah me!\" exclaimed Mrs. Dale, as soon as Hopkins had taken himself off.\n\n\"What is it, mamma? He's a dear old man, but surely what he says cannot make you really unhappy.\"\n\n\"It is so hard to know what one ought to do. I did not mean to be selfish, but it seems to me as though I were doing the most selfish thing in the world.\"\n\n\"Nay, mamma; it has been anything but selfish. Besides, it is we that have done it; not you.\"\n\n\"Do you know, Lily, that I also have that feeling as to breaking up one's old mode of life of which Hopkins spoke. I thought that I should be glad to escape from this place, but now that the time has come I dread it.\"\n\n\"Do you mean that you repent?\"\n\nMrs. Dale did not answer her daughter at once, fearing to commit herself by words which could not be retracted. But at last she said, \"Yes, Lily; I think I do repent. I think that it has not been well done.\"\n\n\"Then let it be undone,\" said Lily.\n\nThe dinner-party at Guestwick Manor on that day was not very bright, and yet the earl had done all in his power to make his guests happy. But gaiety did not come naturally to his house, which, as will have been seen, was an abode very unlike in its nature to that of the other earl at Courcy Castle. Lady De Courcy at any rate understood how to receive and entertain a house full of people, though the practice of doing so might give rise to difficult questions in the privacy of her domestic relations. Lady Julia did not understand it; but then Lady Julia was never called upon to answer for the expense of extra servants, nor was she asked about twice a week who the ---- was to pay the wine-merchant's bill? As regards Lord De Guest and the Lady Julia themselves, I think they had the best of it; but I am bound to admit, with reference to chance guests, that the house was dull. The people who were now gathered at the earl's table could hardly have been expected to be very sprightly when in company with each other. The squire was not a man much given to general society, and was unused to amuse a table full of people. On the present occasion he sat next to Lady Julia, and from time to time muttered a few words to her about the state of the country. Mrs. Eames was terribly afraid of everybody there, and especially of the earl, next to whom she sat, and whom she continually called \"my lord,\" showing by her voice as she did so that she was almost alarmed by the sound of her own voice. Mr. and Mrs. Boyce were there, the parson sitting on the other side of Lady Julia, and the parson's wife on the other side of the earl. Mrs. Boyce was very studious to show that she was quite at home, and talked perhaps more than any one else; but in doing so she bored the earl most exquisitely, so that he told John Eames the next morning that she was worse than the bull. The parson ate his dinner, but said little or nothing between the two graces. He was a heavy, sensible, slow man, who knew himself and his own powers. \"Uncommon good stewed beef,\" he said, as he went home; \"why can't we have our beef stewed like that?\" \"Because we don't pay our cook sixty pounds a year,\" said Mrs. Boyce. \"A woman with sixteen pounds can stew beef as well as a woman with sixty,\" said he; \"she only wants looking after.\" The earl himself was possessed of a sort of gaiety. There was about him a lightness of spirit which often made him an agreeable companion to one single person. John Eames conceived him to be the most sprightly old man of his day,--an old man with the fun and frolic almost of a boy. But this spirit, though it would show itself before John Eames, was not up to the entertainment of John Eames's mother and sister, together with the squire, the parson, and the parson's wife of Allington. So that the earl was overweighted and did not shine on this occasion at his own dinner-table. Dr. Crofts, who had also been invited, and who had secured the place which was now peculiarly his own, next to Bell Dale, was no doubt happy enough; as, let us hope, was the young lady also; but they added very little to the general hilarity of the company. John Eames was seated between his own sister and the parson, and did not at all enjoy his position. He had a full view of the doctor's felicity, as the happy pair sat opposite to him, and conceived himself to be hardly treated by Lily's absence.\n\nThe party was certainly very dull, as were all such dinners at Guestwick Manor. There are houses, which, in their every-day course, are not conducted by any means in a sad or unsatisfactory manner,--in which life, as a rule, runs along merrily enough; but which cannot give a dinner-party; or, I might rather say, should never allow themselves to be allured into the attempt. The owners of such houses are generally themselves quite aware of the fact, and dread the dinner which they resolved to give quite as much as it is dreaded by their friends. They know that they prepare for their guests an evening of misery, and for themselves certain long hours of purgatory which are hardly to be endured. But they will do it. Why that long table, and all those supernumerary glasses and knives and forks, if they are never to be used? That argument produces all this misery; that and others cognate to it. On the present occasion, no doubt, there were excuses to be made. The squire and his niece had been invited on special cause, and their presence would have been well enough. The doctor added in would have done no harm. It was good-natured, too, that invitation given to Mrs. Eames and her daughter. The error lay in the parson and his wife. There was no necessity for their being there, nor had they any ground on which to stand, except the party-giving ground. Mr. and Mrs. Boyce made the dinner-party, and destroyed the social circle. Lady Julia knew that she had been wrong as soon as she had sent out the note.\n\nNothing was said on that evening which has any bearing on our story. Nothing, indeed, was said which had any bearing on anything. The earl's professed object had been to bring the squire and young Eames together; but people are never brought together on such melancholy occasions. Though they sip their port in close contiguity, they are poles asunder in their minds and feelings. When the Guestwick fly came for Mrs. Eames, and the parson's pony phaeton came for him and Mrs. Boyce, a great relief was felt; but the misery of those who were left had gone too far to allow of any reaction on that evening. The squire yawned, and the earl yawned, and then there was an end of it for that night.\n\n# CHAPTER LIV. THE SECOND VISIT TO THE GUESTWICK BRIDGE.\n\nBell had declared that her sister would be very happy to see John Eames if he would go over to Allington, and he had replied that of course he would go there. So much having been, as it were, settled, he was able to speak of his visit as a matter of course at the breakfast-table, on the morning after the earl's dinner-party. \"I must get you to come round with me, Dale, and see what I am doing to the land,\" the earl said. And then he proposed to order saddle-horses. But the squire preferred walking, and in this way they were disposed of soon after breakfast.\n\nJohn had it in his mind to get Bell to himself for half an hour, and hold a conference with her; but it either happened that Lady Julia was too keen in her duties as a hostess, or else, as was more possible, Bell avoided the meeting. No opportunity for such an interview offered itself, though he hung about the drawing-room all the morning. \"You had better wait for luncheon, now,\" Lady Julia said to him about twelve. But this he declined; and taking himself away hid himself about the place for the next hour and a half. During this time he considered much whether it would be better for him to ride or walk. If she should give him any hope, he could ride back triumphant as a field-marshal. Then the horse would be delightful to him. But if she should give him no hope,--if it should be his destiny to be rejected utterly on that morning,--then the horse would be terribly in the way of his sorrow. Under such circumstances what could he do but roam wide about across the fields, resting when he might choose to rest, and running when it might suit him to run. \"And she is not like other girls,\" he thought to himself. \"She won't care for my boots being dirty.\" So at last he elected to walk.\n\n\"Stand up to her boldly, man,\" the earl had said to him. \"By George, what is there to be afraid of? It's my belief they'll give most to those who ask for most. There's nothing sets 'em against a man like being sheepish.\" How the earl knew so much, seeing that he had not himself given signs of any success in that walk of life, I am not prepared to say. But Eames took his advice as being in itself good, and resolved to act upon it. \"Not that any resolution will be of any use,\" he said to himself, as he walked along. \"When the moment comes I know that I shall tremble before her, and I know that she'll see it; but I don't think it will make any difference in her.\"\n\nHe had last seen her on the lawn behind the Small House, just at that time when her passion for Crosbie was at the strongest. Eames had gone thither impelled by a foolish desire to declare to her his hopeless love, and she had answered him by telling him that she loved Mr. Crosbie better than all the world besides. Of course she had done so, at that time; but, nevertheless, her manner of telling him had seemed to him to be cruel. And he also had been cruel. He had told her that he hated Crosbie,--calling him \"that man,\" and assuring her that no earthly consideration should induce him to go into \"that man's house.\" Then he had walked away moodily wishing him all manner of evil. Was it not singular that all the evil things which he, in his mind, had meditated for the man, had fallen upon him. Crosbie had lost his love! He had so proved himself to be a villain that his name might not be so much as mentioned! He had been ignominiously thrashed! But what good would all this be if his image were still dear to Lily's heart? \"I told her that I loved her then,\" he said to himself, \"though I had no right to do so. At any rate I have a right to tell her now.\"\n\nWhen he reached Allington he did not go in through the village and up to the front of the Small House by the cross street, but turned by the church gate and passed over the squire's terrace, and by the end of the Great House through the garden. Here he encountered Hopkins. \"Why, if that b'aint Mr. Eames!\" said the gardener. \"Mr. John, may I make so bold!\" and Hopkins held out a very dirty hand, which Eames of course took, unconscious of the cause of this new affection.\n\n\"I'm just going to call at the Small House, and I thought I'd come this way.\"\n\n\"To be sure; this way, or that way, or any way, who's so welcome, Mr. John? I envies you; I envies you more than I envies any man. If I could a got him by the scuff of the neck, I'd a treated him jist like any wermin;--I would, indeed! He was wermin! I ollays said it. I hated him ollays; I did indeed, Mr. John, from the first moment when he used to be nigging away at them foutry balls, knocking them in among the rhododendrons, as though there weren't no flower blossoms for next year. He never looked at one as though one were a Christian; did he, Mr. John?\"\n\n\"I wasn't very fond of him myself, Hopkins.\"\n\n\"Of course you weren't very fond of him. Who was?--only she, poor young lady. She'll be better now, Mr. John, a deal better. He wasn't a wholesome lover,--not like you are. Tell me, Mr. John, did you give it him well when you got him? I heard you did;--two black eyes, and all his face one mash of gore!\" And Hopkins, who was by no means a young man, stiffly put himself into a fighting attitude.\n\nEames passed on over the little bridge, which seemed to be in a state of fast decay, unattended to by any friendly carpenter, now that the days of its use were so nearly at an end; and on into the garden, lingering on the spot where he had last said farewell to Lily. He looked about as though he expected still to find her there; but there was no one to be seen in the garden, and no sound to be heard. As every step brought him nearer to her whom he was seeking, he became more and more conscious of the hopelessness of his errand. Him she had never loved, and why should he venture to hope that she would love him now? He would have turned back had he not been aware that his promise to others required that he should persevere. He had said that he would do this thing, and he would be as good as his word. But he hardly ventured to hope that he might be successful. In this frame of mind he slowly made his way up across the lawn.\n\n\"My dear, there is John Eames,\" said Mrs. Dale, who had first seen him from the parlour window.\n\n\"Don't go, mamma.\"\n\n\"I don't know; perhaps it will be better that I should.\"\n\n\"No, mamma, no; what good can it do? It can do no good. I like him as well as I can like any one. I love him dearly. But it can do no good. Let him come in here, and be very kind to him; but do not go away and leave us. Of course I knew he would come, and I shall be very glad to see him.\"\n\nThen Mrs. Dale went round to the other room, and admitted her visitor through the window of the drawing-room. \"We are in terrible confusion, John, are we not?\"\n\n\"And so you are really going to live in Guestwick?\"\n\n\"Well, it looks like it, does it not? But, to tell you a secret,--only it must be a secret; you must not mention it at Guestwick Manor; even Bell does not know;--we have half made up our minds to unpack all our things and stay where we are.\"\n\nEames was so intent on his own purpose, and so fully occupied with the difficulty of the task before him, that he could hardly receive Mrs. Dale's tidings with all the interest which they deserved. \"Unpack them all again,\" he said. \"That will be very troublesome. Is Lily with you, Mrs. Dale?\"\n\n\"Yes, she is in the parlour. Come and see her.\" So he followed Mrs. Dale through the hall, and found himself in the presence of his love.\n\n\"How do you do, John?\" \"How do you do, Lily?\" We all know the way in which such meetings are commenced. Each longed to be tender and affectionate to the other,--each in a different way; but neither knew how to throw any tenderness into this first greeting. \"So you're staying at the Manor House,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Yes; I'm staying there. Your uncle and Bell came yesterday afternoon.\"\n\n\"Have you heard about Bell?\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Oh, yes; Mary told me. I'm so glad of it. I always liked Dr. Crofts very much. I have not congratulated her, because I didn't know whether it was a secret. But Crofts was there last night, and if it is a secret he didn't seem to be very careful about keeping it.\"\n\n\"It is no secret,\" said Mrs. Dale. \"I don't know that I am fond of such secrets.\" But as she said this, she thought of Crosbie's engagement, which had been told to every one, and of its consequences.\n\n\"Is it to be soon?\" he asked.\n\n\"Well, yes; we think so. Of course nothing is settled.\"\n\n\"It was such fun,\" said Lily. \"James, who took, at any rate, a year or two to make his proposal, wanted to be married the next day afterwards.\"\n\n\"No, Lily; not quite that.\"\n\n\"Well, mamma, it was very nearly that. He thought it could all be done this week. It has made us so happy, John! I don't know anybody I should so much like for a brother. I'm very glad you like him;--very glad. I hope you'll be friends always.\" There was some little tenderness in this,--as John acknowledged to himself.\n\n\"I'm sure we shall,--if he likes it. That is, if I ever happen to see him. I'll do anything for him I can if he ever comes up to London. Wouldn't it be a good thing, Mrs. Dale, if he settled himself in London?\"\n\n\"No, John; it would be a very bad thing. Why should he wish to rob me of my daughter?\"\n\nMrs. Dale was speaking of her eldest daughter; but the very allusion to any such robbery covered John Eames's face with a blush, made him hot up to the roots of his hair, and for the moment silenced him.\n\n\"You think he would have a better career in London?\" said Lily, speaking under the influence of her superior presence of mind.\n\nShe had certainly shown defective judgment in desiring her mother not to leave them alone; and of this Mrs. Dale soon felt herself aware. The thing had to be done, and no little precautionary measure, such as this of Mrs. Dale's enforced presence, would prevent it. Of this Mrs. Dale was well aware; and she felt, moreover, that John was entitled to an opportunity of pleading his own cause. It might be that such opportunity would avail him nothing, but not the less should he have it of right, seeing that he desired it. But yet Mrs. Dale did not dare to get up and leave the room. Lily had asked her not to do so, and at the present period of their lives all Lily's requests were sacred. They continued for some time to talk of Crofts and his marriage; and when that subject was finished, they discussed their own probable,--or, as it seemed now, improbable,--removal to Guestwick. \"It's going too far, mamma,\" said Lily, \"to say that you think we shall not go. It was only last night that you suggested it. The truth is, John, that Hopkins came in and discoursed with the most wonderful eloquence. Nobody dared to oppose Hopkins. He made us almost cry; he was so pathetic.\"\n\n\"He has just been talking to me, too,\" said John, \"as I came through the squire's garden.\"\n\n\"And what has he been saying to you?\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Oh, I don't know; not much.\" John, however, remembered well, at this moment, all that the gardener had said to him. Did she know of that encounter between him and Crosbie? and if she did know of it, in what light did she regard it?\n\nThey had sat thus for an hour together, and Eames was not as yet an inch nearer to his object. He had sworn to himself that he would not leave the Small House without asking Lily to be his wife. It seemed to him as though he would be guilty of falsehood towards the earl if he did so. Lord De Guest had opened his house to him, and had asked all the Dales there, and had offered himself up as a sacrifice at the cruel shrine of a serious dinner-party, to say nothing of that easier and lighter sacrifice which he had made in a pecuniary point of view, in order that this thing might be done. Under such circumstances Eames was too honest a man not to do it, let the difficulties in his way be what they might.\n\nHe had sat there for an hour, and Mrs. Dale still remained with her daughter. Should he get up boldly and ask Lily to put on her bonnet and come out into the garden? As the thought struck him, he rose and grasped at his hat. \"I am going to walk back to Guestwick,\" said he.\n\n\"It was very good of you to come so far to see us.\"\n\n\"I was always fond of walking,\" he said. \"The earl wanted me to ride, but I prefer being on foot when I know the country, as I do here.\"\n\n\"Have a glass of wine before you go.\"\n\n\"Oh, dear, no. I think I'll go back through the squire's fields, and out on the road at the white gate. The path is quite dry now.\"\n\n\"I dare say it is,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Lily, I wonder whether you would come as far as that with me.\" As the request was made Mrs. Dale looked at her daughter almost beseechingly. \"Do, pray do,\" said he; \"it is a beautiful day for walking.\"\n\nThe path proposed lay right across the field into which Lily had taken Crosbie when she made her offer to let him off from his engagement. Could it be possible that she should ever walk there again with another lover? \"No, John,\" she said; \"not to-day, I think. I am almost tired, and I had rather not go out.\"\n\n\"It would do you good,\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"I don't want to be done good to, mamma. Besides, I should have to come back by myself.\"\n\n\"I'll come back with you,\" said Johnny.\n\n\"Oh, yes; and then I should have to go again with you. But, John, really I don't wish to walk to-day.\" Whereupon John Eames again put down his hat.\n\n\"Lily,\" said he; and then he stopped. Mrs. Dale walked away to the window, turning her back upon her daughter and visitor. \"Lily, I have come over here on purpose to speak to you. Indeed, I have come down from London only that I might see you.\"\n\n\"Have you, John?\"\n\n\"Yes, I have. You know well all that I have got to tell you. I loved you before he ever saw you; and now that he has gone, I love you better than I ever did. Dear Lily!\" and he put out his hand to her.\n\n\"No, John; no,\" she answered.\n\n\"Must it be always no?\"\n\n\"Always no to that. How can it be otherwise? You would not have me marry you while I love another!\"\n\n\"But he is gone. He has taken another wife.\"\n\n\"I cannot change myself because he is changed. If you are kind to me you will let that be enough.\"\n\n\"But you are so unkind to me!\"\n\n\"No, no; oh, I would wish to be so kind to you! John, here; take my hand. It is the hand of a friend who loves you, and will always love you. Dear John, I will do anything,--everything for you but that.\"\n\n\"There is only one thing,\" said he, still holding her by the hand, but with his face turned from her.\n\n\"Nay; do not say so. Are you worse off than I am? I could not have that one thing, and I was nearer to my heart's longings than you have ever been. I cannot have that one thing; but I know that there are other things, and I will not allow myself to be broken-hearted.\"\n\n\"You are stronger than I am,\" he said.\n\n\"Not stronger, but more certain. Make yourself as sure as I am, and you, too, will be strong. Is it not so, mamma?\"\n\n\"I wish it could be otherwise;--I wish it could be otherwise! If you can give him any hope--\"\n\n\"Mamma!\"\n\n\"Tell me that I may come again,--in a year,\" he pleaded.\n\n\"I cannot tell you so. You may not come again,--not in this way. Do you remember what I told you before, in the garden; that I loved him better than all the world besides? It is still the same. I still love him better than all the world. How, then, can I give you any hope?\"\n\n\"But it will not be so for ever, Lily.\"\n\n\"For ever! Why should he not be mine as well as hers when that for ever comes? John, if you understand what it is to love, you will say nothing more of it. I have spoken to you more openly about this than I have ever done to anybody, even to mamma, because I have wished to make you understand my feelings. I should be disgraced in my own eyes if I admitted the love of another man, after--after--. It is to me almost as though I had married him. I am not blaming him, remember. These things are different with a man.\"\n\nShe had not dropped his hand, and as she made her last speech was sitting in her old chair with her eyes fixed upon the ground. She spoke in a low voice, slowly, almost with difficulty; but still the words came very clearly, with a clear, distinct voice which caused them to be remembered with accuracy, both by Eames and Mrs. Dale. To him it seemed to be impossible that he should continue his suit after such a declaration. To Mrs. Dale they were terrible words, speaking of a perpetual widowhood, and telling of an amount of suffering greater even than that which she had anticipated. It was true that Lily had never said so much to her as she had now said to John Eames, or had attempted to make so clear an exposition of her own feelings. \"I should be disgraced in my own eyes if I admitted the love of another man!\" They were terrible words, but very easy to be understood. Mrs. Dale had felt, from the first, that Eames was coming too soon, that the earl and the squire together were making an effort to cure the wound too quickly after its infliction; that time should have been given to her girl to recover. But now the attempt had been made, and words had been forced from Lily's lips, the speaking of which would never be forgotten by herself.\n\n\"I knew that it would be so,\" said John.\n\n\"Ah, yes; you know it, because your heart understands my heart. And you will not be angry with me, and say naughty, cruel words, as you did once before. We will think of each other, John, and pray for each other; and will always love one another. When we do meet let us be glad to see each other. No other friend shall ever be dearer to me than you are. You are so true and honest! When you marry I will tell your wife what an infinite blessing God has given her.\"\n\n\"You shall never do that.\"\n\n\"Yes, I will. I understand what you mean; but yet I will.\"\n\n\"Good-by, Mrs. Dale,\" he said.\n\n\"Good-by, John. If it could have been otherwise with her, you should have had all my best wishes in the matter. I would have loved you dearly as my son; and I will love you now.\" Then she put up her lips and kissed his face.\n\n\"And so will I love you,\" said Lily, giving him her hand again. He looked longingly into her face as though he had thought it possible that she also might kiss him: then he pressed her hand to his lips, and without speaking any further farewell, took up his hat and left the room.\n\n\"Poor fellow!\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"They should not have let him come,\" said Lily. \"But they don't understand. They think that I have lost a toy, and they mean to be good-natured, and to give me another.\" Very shortly after that Lily went away by herself, and sat alone for hours; and when she joined her mother again at tea-time, nothing further was said of John Eames's visit.\n\nHe made his way out by the front door, and through the churchyard, and in this way on to the field through which he had asked Lily to walk with him. He hardly began to think of what had passed till he had left the squire's house behind him. As he made his way through the tombstones he paused and read one, as though it interested him. He stood a moment under the tower looking up at the clock, and then pulled out his own watch, as though to verify the one by the other. He made, unconsciously, a struggle to drive away from his thoughts the facts of the late scene, and for some five or ten minutes he succeeded. He said to himself a word or two about Sir Raffle and his letters, and laughed inwardly as he remembered the figure of Rafferty bringing in the knight's shoes. He had gone some half mile upon his way before he ventured to stand still and tell himself that he had failed in the great object of his life.\n\nYes; he had failed: and he acknowledged to himself, with bitter reproaches, that he had failed, now and for ever. He told himself that he had obtruded upon her in her sorrow with an unmannerly love, and rebuked himself as having been not only foolish but ungenerous. His friend the earl had been wont, in his waggish way, to call him the conquering hero, and had so talked him out of his common sense as to have made him almost think that he would be successful in his suit. Now, as he told himself that any such success must have been impossible, he almost hated the earl for having brought him to this condition. A conquering hero, indeed! How should he manage to sneak back among them all at the Manor House, crestfallen and abject in his misery? Everybody knew the errand on which he had gone, and everybody must know of his failure. How could he have been such a fool as to undertake such a task under the eyes of so many lookers-on? Was it not the case that he had so fondly expected success, as to think only of his triumph in returning, and not of his more probable disgrace? He had allowed others to make a fool of him, and had so made a fool of himself that now all hope and happiness were over for him. How could he escape at once out of the country,--back to London? How could he get away without saying a word further to any one? That was the thought that at first occupied his mind.\n\nHe crossed the road at the end of the squire's property, where the parish of Allington divides itself from that of Abbot's Guest in which the earl's house stands, and made his way back along the copse which skirted the field in which they had encountered the bull, into the high woods which were at the back of the park. Ah, yes; it had been well for him that he had not come out on horseback. That ride home along the high road and up to the Manor House stables would, under his present circumstances, have been almost impossible to him. As it was, he did not think it possible that he should return to his place in the earl's house. How could he pretend to maintain his ordinary demeanour under the eyes of those two old men? It would be better for him to get home to his mother,--to send a message from thence to the Manor, and then to escape back to London. So thinking, but with no resolution made, he went on through the woods, and down from the hill back towards the town till he again came to the little bridge over the brook. There he stopped and stood a while with his broad hand spread over the letters which he had cut in those early days, so as to hide them from his sight. \"What an ass I have been,--always and ever!\" he said to himself.\n\nIt was not only of his late disappointment that he was thinking, but of his whole past life. He was conscious of his hobbledehoyhood,--of that backwardness on his part in assuming manhood which had rendered him incapable of making himself acceptable to Lily before she had fallen into the clutches of Crosbie. As he thought of this he declared to himself that if he could meet Crosbie again he would again thrash him,--that he would so belabour him as to send him out of the world, if such sending might possibly be done by fair beating, regardless whether he himself might be called upon to follow him. Was it not hard that for the two of them,--for Lily and for him also,--there should be such punishment because of the insincerity of that man? When he had thus stood upon the bridge for some quarter of an hour, he took out his knife, and, with deep, rough gashes in the wood, cut out Lily's name from the rail.\n\nHe had hardly finished, and was still looking at the chips as they were being carried away by the stream, when a gentle step came close up to him, and turning round, he saw that Lady Julia was on the bridge. She was close to him, and had already seen his handiwork. \"Has she offended you, John?\" she said.\n\n\"Oh, Lady Julia!\"\n\n\"Has she offended you?\"\n\n\"She has refused me, and it is all over.\"\n\n\"It may be that she has refused you, and that yet it need not be all over. I am sorry that you have cut out the name, John. Do you mean to cut it out from your heart?\"\n\n\"Never. I would if I could, but I never shall.\"\n\n\"Keep to it as to a great treasure. It will be a joy to you in after years, and not a sorrow. To have loved truly, even though you shall have loved in vain, will be a consolation when you are as old as I am. It is something to have had a heart.\"\n\n\"I don't know. I wish that I had none.\"\n\n\"And, John;--I can understand her feeling now; and indeed, I thought all through that you were asking her too soon; but the time may yet come when she will think better of your wishes.\"\n\n\"No, no; never. I begin to know her now.\"\n\n\"If you can be constant in your love you may win her yet. Remember how young she is; and how young you both are. Come again in two years' time, and then, when you have won her, you shall tell me that I have been a good old woman to you both.\"\n\n\"I shall never win her, Lady Julia.\" As he spoke these last words the tears were running down his cheeks, and he was weeping openly in presence of his companion. It was well for him that she had come upon him in his sorrow. When he once knew that she had seen his tears, he could pour out to her the whole story of his grief; and as he did so she led him back quietly to the house.\n\n# CHAPTER LV. NOT VERY FIE FIE AFTER ALL.\n\nIt will perhaps be remembered that terrible things had been foretold as about to happen between the Hartletop and Omnium families. Lady Dumbello had smiled whenever Mr. Plantagenet Palliser had spoken to her. Mr. Palliser had confessed to himself that politics were not enough for him, and that Love was necessary to make up the full complement of his happiness. Lord Dumbello had frowned latterly when his eyes fell on the tall figure of the duke's heir; and the duke himself,--that potentate, generally so mighty in his silence,--the duke himself had spoken. Lady De Courcy and Lady Clandidlem were, both of them, absolutely certain that the thing had been fully arranged. I am, therefore, perfectly justified in stating that the world was talking about the loves,--the illicit loves,--of Mr. Palliser and Lady Dumbello.\n\nAnd the talking of the world found its way down to that respectable country parsonage in which Lady Dumbello had been born, and from which she had been taken away to those noble halls which she now graced by her presence. The talking of the world was heard at Plumstead Episcopi, where still lived Archdeacon Grantly, the lady's father; and was heard also at the deanery of Barchester, where lived the lady's aunt and grandfather. By whose ill-mannered tongue the rumour was spread in these ecclesiastical regions it boots not now to tell. But it may be remembered that Courcy Castle was not far from Barchester, and that Lady De Courcy was not given to hide her lights under a bushel.\n\nIt was a terrible rumour. To what mother must not such a rumour respecting her daughter be very terrible? In no mother's ears could it have sounded more frightfully than it did in those of Mrs. Grantly. Lady Dumbello, the daughter, might be altogether worldly; but Mrs. Grantly had never been more than half worldly. In one moiety of her character, her habits, and her desires, she had been wedded to things good in themselves,--to religion, to charity, and to honest-hearted uprightness. It is true that the circumstances of her life had induced her to serve both God and Mammon, and that, therefore, she had gloried greatly in the marriage of her daughter with the heir of a marquis. She had revelled in the aristocratic elevation of her child, though she continued to dispense books and catechisms with her own hands to the children of the labourers of Plumstead Episcopi. When Griselda first became Lady Dumbello the mother feared somewhat lest her child should find herself unequal to the exigencies of her new position. But the child had proved herself more than equal to them, and had mounted up to a dizzy height of success, which brought to the mother great glory and great fear also. She delighted to think that her Griselda was great even among the daughters of marquises; but she trembled as she reflected how deadly would be the fall from such a height--should there ever be a fall!\n\nBut she had never dreamed of such a fall as this! She would have said,--indeed, she often had said,--to the archdeacon that Griselda's religious principles were too firmly fixed to be moved by outward worldly matters; signifying, it may be, her conviction that that teaching of Plumstead Episcopi had so fastened her daughter into a groove, that all the future teaching of Hartlebury would not suffice to undo the fastenings. When she had thus boasted no such idea as that of her daughter running from her husband's house had ever come upon her; but she had alluded to vices of a nature kindred to that vice,--to vices into which other aristocratic ladies sometimes fell, who had been less firmly grooved; and her boastings had amounted to this,--that she herself had so successfully served God and Mammon together, that her child might go forth and enjoy all worldly things without risk of damage to things heavenly. Then came upon her this rumour. The archdeacon told her in a hoarse whisper that he had been recommended to look to it, that it was current through the world that Griselda was about to leave her husband.\n\n\"Nothing on earth shall make me believe it,\" said Mrs. Grantly. But she sat alone in her drawing-room afterwards and trembled. Then came her sister, Mrs. Arabin, the dean's wife, over to the parsonage, and in half-hidden words told the same story. She had heard it from Mrs. Proudie, the bishop's wife. \"That woman is as false as the father of falsehoods,\" said Mrs. Grantly. But she trembled the more; and as she prepared her parish work, could think of nothing but her child. What would be all her life to come, what would have been all that was past of her life, if this thing should happen to her? She would not believe it; but yet she trembled the more as she thought of her daughter's exaltation, and remembered that such things had been done in that world to which Griselda now belonged. Ah! would it not have been better for them if they had not raised their heads so high! And she walked out alone among the tombs of the neighbouring churchyard, and stood over the grave in which had been laid the body of her other daughter. Could be it that the fate of that one had been the happier.\n\nVery few words were spoken on the subject between her and the archdeacon, and yet it seemed agreed among them that something should be done. He went up to London, and saw his daughter,--not daring, however, to mention such a subject. Lord Dumbello was cross with him, and very uncommunicative. Indeed both the archdeacon and Mrs. Grantly had found that their daughter's house was not comfortable to them, and as they were sufficiently proud among their own class they had not cared to press themselves on the hospitality of their son-in-law. But he had been able to perceive that all was not right in the house in Carlton Gardens. Lord Dumbello was not gracious with his wife, and there was something in the silence, rather than in the speech, of men, which seemed to justify the report which had reached him.\n\n\"He is there oftener than he should be,\" said the archdeacon. \"And I am sure of this, at least, that Dumbello does not like it.\"\n\n\"I will write to her,\" said Mrs. Grantly at last. \"I am still her mother;--I will write to her. It may be that she does not know what people say of her.\"\n\nAnd Mrs. Grantly did write.\n\nPlumstead, April, 186--.\n\nIt seems sometimes that you have been moved so far away from me that I have hardly a right to concern myself more in the affairs of your daily life, and I know that it is impossible that you should refer to me for advice or sympathy, as you would have done had you married some gentleman of our own standing. But I am quite sure that my child does not forget her mother, or fail to look back upon her mother's love; and that she will allow me to speak to her if she be in trouble, as I would to any other child whom I had loved and cherished. I pray God that I may be wrong in supposing that such trouble is near you. If I am so you will forgive me my solicitude.\n\nRumours have reached us from more than one quarter that--Oh! Griselda, I hardly know in what words to conceal and yet to declare that which I have to write. They say that you are intimate with Mr. Palliser, the nephew of the duke, and that your husband is much offended. Perhaps I had better tell you all, openly, cautioning you not to suppose that I have believed it. They say that it is thought that you are going to put yourself under Mr. Palliser's protection. My dearest child, I think you can imagine with what an agony I write these words,--with what terrible grief I must have been oppressed before I could have allowed myself to entertain the thoughts which have produced them. Such things are said openly in Barchester, and your father, who has been in town and has seen you, feels himself unable to tell me that my mind may be at rest.\n\nI will not say to you a word as to the injury in a worldly point of view which would come to you from any rupture with your husband. I believe that you can see what would be the effect of so terrible a step quite as plainly as I can show it you. You would break the heart of your father, and send your mother to her grave;--but it is not even on that that I may most insist. It is this,--that you would offend your God by the worst sin that a woman can commit, and cast yourself into a depth of infamy in which repentance before God is almost impossible, and from which escape before man is not permitted.\n\nI do not believe it, my dearest, dearest child,--my only living daughter; I do not believe what they have said to me. But as a mother I have not dared to leave the slander unnoticed. If you will write to me and say that it is not so, you will make me happy again, even though you should rebuke me for my suspicion.\n\nBelieve that at all times, and under all circumstances, I am still your loving mother, as I was in other days.\n\nWe will now go back to Mr. Palliser as he sat in his chambers at the Albany, thinking of his love. The duke had cautioned him, and the duke's agent had cautioned him; and he, in spite of his high feeling of independence, had almost been made to tremble. All his thousands a year were in the balance, and perhaps everything on which depended his position before the world. But, nevertheless, though he did tremble, he resolved to persevere. Statistics were becoming dry to him, and love was very sweet. Statistics, he thought, might be made as enchanting as ever, if only they could be mingled with love. The mere idea of loving Lady Dumbello had seemed to give a salt to his life of which he did not now know how to rob himself. It is true that he had not as yet enjoyed many of the absolute blessings of love, seeing that his conversations with Lady Dumbello had never been warmer than those which have been repeated in these pages; but his imagination had been at work; and now that Lady Dumbello was fully established at her house in Carlton Gardens, he was determined to declare his passion on the first convenient opportunity. It was sufficiently manifest to him that the world expected him to do so, and that the world was already a little disposed to find fault with the slowness of his proceedings.\n\nHe had been once at Carlton Gardens since the season had commenced, and the lady had favoured him with her sweetest smile. But he had only been half a minute alone with her, and during that half-minute had only time to remark that he supposed she would now remain in London for the season.\n\n\"Oh, yes,\" she had answered, \"we shall not leave till July.\" Nor could he leave till July, because of the exigencies of his statistics. He therefore had before him two, if not three, clear months in which to manoeuvre, to declare his purposes, and prepare for the future events of his life. As he resolved on a certain morning that he would say his first tender word to Lady Dumbello that very night, in the drawing-room of Lady De Courcy, where he knew that he should meet her, a letter came to him by the post. He well knew the hand and the intimation which it would contain. It was from the duke's agent, Mr. Fothergill, and informed him that a certain sum of money had been placed to his credit at his banker's. But the letter went further, and informed him also that the duke had given his agent to understand that special instructions would be necessary before the next quarterly payment could be made. Mr. Fothergill said nothing further, but Mr. Palliser understood it all. He felt his blood run cold round his heart; but, nevertheless, he determined that he would not break his word to Lady De Courcy that night.\n\nAnd Lady Dumbello received her letter also on the same morning. She was being dressed as she read it, and the maidens who attended her found no cause to suspect that anything in the letter had excited her ladyship. Her ladyship was not often excited, though she was vigilant in exacting from them their utmost cares. She read her letter, however, very carefully, and as she sat beneath the toilet implements of her maidens thought deeply of the tidings which had been brought to her. She was angry with no one;--she was thankful to no one. She felt no special love for any person concerned in the matter. Her heart did not say, \"Oh, my lord and husband!\" or, \"Oh, my lover!\" or, \"Oh, my mother, the friend of my childhood!\" But she became aware that matter for thought had been brought before her, and she did think. \"Send my love to Lord Dumbello,\" she said, when the operations were nearly completed, \"and tell him that I shall be so glad to see him if he will come to me while I am at breakfast.\"\n\n\"Yes, my lady.\" And then the message came back: \"His lordship would be with her ladyship certainly.\"\n\n\"Gustavus,\" she said, as soon as she had seated herself discreetly in her chair, \"I have had a letter from my mother, which you had better read;\" and she handed to him the document. \"I do not know what I have done to deserve such suspicions from her; but she lives in the country, and has probably been deceived by ill-natured people. At any rate you must read it, and tell me what I should do.\"\n\nWe may predicate from this that Mr. Palliser's chance of being able to shipwreck himself upon that rock was but small, and that he would, in spite of himself, be saved from his uncle's anger. Lord Dumbello took the letter and read it very slowly, standing, as he did so, with his back to the fire. He read it very slowly, and his wife, though she never turned her face directly upon his, could perceive that he became very red, that he was fluttered and put beyond himself, and that his answer was not ready. She was well aware that his conduct to her during the last three months had been much altered from his former usages; that he had been rougher with her in his speech when alone, and less courteous in his attention when in society; but she had made no complaint or spoken a word to show him that she had marked the change. She had known, moreover, the cause of his altered manner, and having considered much, had resolved that she would live it down. She had declared to herself that she had done no deed and spoken no word that justified suspicion, and therefore she would make no change in her ways, or show herself to be conscious that she was suspected. But now,--having her mother's letter in her hand,--she could bring him to an explanation without making him aware that she had ever thought that he had been jealous of her. To her, her mother's letter was a great assistance. It justified a scene like this, and enabled her to fight her battle after her own fashion. As for eloping with any Mr. Palliser, and giving up the position which she had won;--no, indeed! She had been fastened in her grooves too well for that! Her mother, in entertaining any fear on such a subject, had shown herself to be ignorant of the solidity of her daughter's character.\n\n\"Well, Gustavus,\" she said at last. \"You must say what answer I shall make, or whether I shall make any answer.\" But he was not even yet ready to instruct her. So he unfolded the letter and read it again, and she poured out for herself a cup of tea.\n\n\"It's a very serious matter,\" said he.\n\n\"Yes, it is serious; I could not but think such a letter from my mother to be serious. Had it come from any one else I doubt whether I should have troubled you; unless, indeed, it had been from any as near to you as she is to me. As it is, you cannot but feel that I am right.\"\n\n\"Right! Oh, yes, you are right,--quite right to tell me; you should tell me everything. D---- them!\" But whom he meant to condemn he did not explain.\n\n\"I am above all things averse to cause you trouble,\" she said. \"I have seen some little things of late--\"\n\n\"Has he ever said anything to you?\"\n\n\"Who,--Mr. Palliser? Never a word.\"\n\n\"He has hinted at nothing of this kind?\"\n\n\"Never a word. Had he done so, I must have made you understand that he could not have been allowed again into my drawing-room.\" Then again he read the letter, or pretended to do so.\n\n\"Your mother means well,\" he said.\n\n\"Oh, yes, she means well. She has been foolish to believe the tittle-tattle that has reached her,--very foolish to oblige me to give you this annoyance.\"\n\n\"Oh, as for that, I'm not annoyed. By Jove, no. Come, Griselda, let us have it all out; other people have said this, and I have been unhappy. Now, you know it all.\"\n\n\"Have I made you unhappy?\"\n\n\"Well, no; not you. Don't be hard upon me when I tell you the whole truth. Fools and brutes have whispered things that have vexed me. They may whisper till the devil fetches them, but they shan't annoy me again. Give me a kiss, my girl.\" And he absolutely put out his arms and embraced her. \"Write a good-natured letter to your mother, and ask her to come up for a week in May. That'll be the best thing; and then she'll understand. By Jove, it's twelve o'clock. Good-by.\"\n\nLady Dumbello was well aware that she had triumphed, and that her mother's letter had been invaluable to her. But it had been used, and therefore she did not read it again. She ate her breakfast in quiet comfort, looking over a milliner's French circular as she did so; and then, when the time for such an operation had fully come, she got to her writing-table and answered her mother's letter.\n\nDEAR MAMMA [she said],\n\nI thought it best to show your letter at once to Lord Dumbello. He said that people would be ill-natured, and seemed to think that the telling of such stories could not be helped. As regards you, he was not a bit angry, but said that you and papa had better come to us for a week about the end of next month. Do come. We are to have rather a large dinner-party on the 23rd. His Royal Highness is coming, and I think papa would like to meet him. Have you observed that those very high bonnets have all gone out: I never liked them; and as I had got a hint from Paris, I have been doing my best to put them down. I do hope nothing will prevent your coming.\n\nYour affectionate daughter,\n\nCarlton Gardens, Wednesday.\n\nMrs. Grantly was aware, from the moment in which she received the letter, that she had wronged her daughter by her suspicions. It did not occur to her to disbelieve a word that was said in the letter, or an inference that was implied. She had been wrong, and rejoiced that it was so. But nevertheless there was that in the letter which annoyed and irritated her, though she could not explain to herself the cause of her annoyance. She had thrown all her heart into that which she had written, but in the words which her child had written not a vestige of heart was to be found. In that reconciling of God and Mammon which Mrs. Grantly had carried on so successfully in the education of her daughter, the organ had not been required, and had become withered, if not defunct, through want of use.\n\n\"We will not go there, I think,\" said Mrs. Grantly, speaking to her husband.\n\n\"Oh dear, no; certainly not. If you want to go to town at all, I will take rooms for you. And as for his Royal Highness--! I have a great respect for his Royal Highness, but I do not in the least desire to meet him at Dumbello's table.\"\n\nAnd so that matter was settled, as regarded the inhabitants of Plumstead Episcopi.\n\nAnd whither did Lord Dumbello betake himself when he left his wife's room in so great a hurry at twelve o'clock? Not to the Park, nor to Tattersall's, nor to a Committee-room of the House of Commons, nor yet to the bow-window of his club. But he went straight to a great jeweller's in Ludgate-hill, and there purchased a wonderful green necklace, very rare and curious, heavy with green sparkling drops, with three rows of shining green stones embedded in chaste gold,--a necklace amounting almost to a jewelled cuirass in weight and extent. It had been in all the exhibitions, and was very costly and magnificent. While Lady Dumbello was still dressing in the evening this was brought to her with her lord's love, as his token of renewed confidence; and Lady Dumbello, as she counted the sparkles, triumphed inwardly, telling herself that she had played her cards well.\n\nBut while she counted the sparkles produced by her full reconciliation with her lord, poor Plantagenet Palliser was still trembling in his ignorance. If only he could have been allowed to see Mrs. Grantly's letter, and the lady's answer, and the lord's present! But no such seeing was vouchsafed to him, and he was carried off in his brougham to Lady De Courcy's house, twittering with expectant love, and trembling with expectant ruin. To this conclusion he had come at any rate, that if anything was to be done, it should be done now. He would speak a word of love, and prepare his future in accordance with the acceptance it might receive.\n\nLady De Courcy's rooms were very crowded when he arrived there. It was the first great crushing party of the season, and all the world had been collected into Portman Square. Lady De Courcy was smiling as though her lord had no teeth, as though her eldest son's condition was quite happy, and all things were going well with the De Courcy interests. Lady Margaretta was there behind her, bland without and bitter within; and Lady Rosina also, at some further distance, reconciled to this world's vanity and finery because there was to be no dancing. And the married daughters of the house were there also, striving to maintain their positions on the strength of their undoubted birth, but subjected to some snubbing by the lowness of their absolute circumstances. Gazebee was there, happy in the absolute fact of his connection with an earl, and blessed with the consideration that was extended to him as an earl's son-in-law. And Crosbie, also, was in the rooms,--was present there, though he had sworn to himself that he would no longer dance attendance on the countess, and that he would sever himself away from the wretchedness of the family. But if he gave up them and their ways, what else would then be left to him? He had come, therefore, and now stood alone, sullen, in a corner, telling himself that all was vanity. Yes; to the vain all will be vanity; and to the poor of heart all will be poor.\n\nLady Dumbello was there in a small inner room, seated on a couch to which she had been brought on her first arrival at the house, and on which she would remain till she departed. From time to time some very noble or very elevated personage would come before her and say a word, and she would answer that elevated personage with another word; but nobody had attempted with her the task of conversation. It was understood that Lady Dumbello did not converse,--unless it were occasionally with Mr. Palliser.\n\nShe knew well that Mr. Palliser was to meet her there. He had told her expressly that he should do so, having inquired, with much solicitude, whether she intended to obey the invitation of the countess. \"I shall probably be there,\" she had said, and now had determined that her mother's letter and her husband's conduct to her should not cause her to break her word. Should Mr. Palliser \"forget\" himself, she would know how to say a word to him as she had known how to say a word to her husband. Forget himself! She was very sure that Mr. Palliser had been making up his mind to forget himself for some months past.\n\nHe did come to her, and stood over her, looking unutterable things. His unutterable things, however, were so looked, that they did not absolutely demand notice from the lady. He did not sigh like a furnace, nor open his eyes upon her as though there were two suns in the firmament above her head, nor did he beat his breast or tear his hair. Mr. Palliser had been brought up in a school which delights in tranquillity, and never allows its pupils to commit themselves either to the sublime or to the ridiculous. He did look an unutterable thing or two; but he did it with so decorous an eye, that the lady, who was measuring it all with great accuracy, could not, as yet, declare that Mr. Palliser had \"forgotten himself.\"\n\nThere was room by her on the couch, and once or twice, at Hartlebury, he had ventured so to seat himself. On the present occasion, however, he could not do so without placing himself manifestly on her dress. She would have known how to fill a larger couch even than that,--as she would have known, also, how to make room,--had it been her mind to do so. So he stood still over her, and she smiled at him. Such a smile! It was cold as death, flattering no one, saying nothing, hideous in its unmeaning, unreal grace. Ah! how I hate the smile of a woman who smiles by rote! It made Mr. Palliser feel very uncomfortable;--but he did not analyze it, and persevered.\n\n\"Lady Dumbello,\" he said, and his voice was very low, \"I have been looking forward to meeting you here.\"\n\n\"Have you, Mr. Palliser? Yes; I remember that you asked me whether I was coming.\"\n\n\"I did. Hm--Lady Dumbello!\" and he almost trenched upon the outside verge of that schooling which had taught him to avoid both the sublime and the ridiculous. But he had not forgotten himself as yet, and so she smiled again.\n\n\"Lady Dumbello, in this world in which we live, it is so hard to get a moment in which we can speak.\" He had thought that she would move her dress, but she did not.\n\n\"Oh, I don't know,\" she said; \"one doesn't often want to say very much, I think.\"\n\n\"Ah, no; not often, perhaps. But when one does want! How I do hate these crowded rooms!\" Yet, when he had been at Hartlebury he had resolved that the only ground for him would be the crowded drawing-room of some large London house. \"I wonder whether you ever desire anything beyond them?\"\n\n\"Oh, yes,\" said she; \"but I confess that I am fond of parties.\"\n\nMr. Palliser looked round and thought that he saw that he was unobserved. He had made up his mind as to what he would do, and he was determined to do it. He had in him none of that readiness which enables some men to make love and carry off their Dulcineas at a moment's notice, but he had that pluck which would have made himself disgraceful in his own eyes if he omitted to do that as to the doing of which he had made a solemn resolution. He would have preferred to do it sitting, but, faute de mieux, seeing that a seat was denied to him, he would do it standing.\n\n\"Griselda,\" he said,--and it must be admitted that his tone was not bad. The word sank softly into her ear, like small rain upon moss, and it sank into no other ear. \"Griselda!\"\n\n\"Mr. Palliser!\" said she;--and though she made no scene, though she merely glanced upon him once, he could see that he was wrong.\n\n\"May I not call you so?\"\n\n\"Certainly not. Shall I ask you to see if my people are there?\" He stood a moment before her hesitating. \"My carriage, I mean.\" As she gave the command she glanced at him again, and then he obeyed her orders.\n\nWhen he returned she had left her seat; but he heard her name announced on the stairs, and caught a glance of the back of her head as she made her way gracefully down through the crowd. He never attempted to make love to her again, utterly disappointing the hopes of Lady De Courcy, Mrs. Proudie, and Lady Clandidlem.\n\nAs I would wish those who are interested in Mr. Palliser's fortunes to know the ultimate result of this adventure, and as we shall not have space to return to his affairs in this little history, I may, perhaps, be allowed to press somewhat forward, and tell what Fortune did for him before the close of that London season. Everybody knows that in that spring Lady Glencora MacCluskie was brought out before the world, and it is equally well known that she, as the only child of the late Lord of the Isles, was the great heiress of the day. It is true that the hereditary possession of Skye, Staffa, Mull, Arran, and Bute went, with the title, to the Marquis of Auldreekie, together with the counties of Caithness and Ross-shire. But the property in Fife, Aberdeen, Perth, and Kincardine-shire, comprising the greater part of those counties, and the coal-mines in Lanark, as well as the enormous estate within the city of Glasgow, were unentailed, and went to the Lady Glencora. She was a fair girl, with bright blue eyes and short wavy flaxen hair, very soft to the eye. The Lady Glencora was small in stature, and her happy round face lacked, perhaps, the highest grace of female beauty. But there was ever a smile upon it, at which it was very pleasant to look; and the intense interest with which she would dance, and talk, and follow up every amusement that was offered her, was very charming. The horse she rode was the dearest love--oh! she loved him so dearly! And she had a little dog that was almost as dear as the horse. The friend of her youth, Sabrina Scott, was--oh, such a girl! And her cousin, the little Lord of the Isles, the heir of the marquis, was so gracious and beautiful that she was always covering him with kisses. Unfortunately he was only six, so that there was hardly a possibility that the properties should be brought together.\n\nBut Lady Glencora, though she was so charming, had even in this, her first outset upon the world, given great uneasiness to her friends, and caused the Marquis of Auldreekie to be almost wild with dismay. There was a terribly handsome man about town, who had spent every shilling that anybody would give him, who was very fond of brandy, who was known, but not trusted, at Newmarket, who was said to be deep in every vice, whose father would not speak to him;--and with him the Lady Glencora was never tired of dancing. One morning she had told her cousin the marquis, with a flashing eye,--for the round blue eye could flash,--that Burgo Fitzgerald was more sinned against than sinning. Ah me! what was a guardian marquis, anxious for the fate of the family property, to do under such circumstances as that?\n\nBut before the end of the season the marquis and the duke were both happy men, and we will hope that the Lady Glencora also was satisfied. Mr. Plantagenet Palliser had danced with her twice, and had spoken his mind. He had an interview with the marquis, which was pre-eminently satisfactory, and everything was settled. Glencora no doubt told him how she had accepted that plain gold ring from Burgo Fitzgerald, and how she had restored it; but I doubt whether she ever told him of that wavy lock of golden hair which Burgo still keeps in his receptacle for such treasures.\n\n\"Plantagenet,\" said the duke, with quite unaccustomed warmth, \"in this, as in all things, you have shown yourself to be everything that I could desire. I have told the marquis that Matching Priory, with the whole estate, should be given over to you at once. It is the most comfortable country-house I know. Glencora shall have The Horns as her wedding present.\"\n\nBut the genial, frank delight of Mr. Fothergill pleased Mr. Palliser the most. The heir of the Pallisers had done his duty, and Mr. Fothergill was unfeignedly a happy man.\n\n# CHAPTER LVI. SHOWING HOW MR. CROSBIE BECAME AGAIN A HAPPY MAN.\n\nIt has been told in the last chapter how Lady De Courcy gave a great party in London in the latter days of April, and it may therefore be thought that things were going well with the De Courcys; but I fear the inference would be untrue. At any rate, things were not going well with Lady Alexandrina, for she, on her mother's first arrival in town, had rushed to Portman-square with a long tale of her sufferings.\n\n\"Oh, mamma! you would not believe it; but he hardly ever speaks to me.\"\n\n\"My dear, there are worse faults in a man than that.\"\n\n\"I am alone there all the day. I never get out. He never offers to get me a carriage. He asked me to walk with him once last week, when it was raining. I saw that he waited till the rain began. Only think, I have not been out three evenings this month,--except to Amelia's; and now he says he won't go there any more, because a fly is so expensive. You can't believe how uncomfortable the house is.\"\n\n\"I thought you chose it, my dear.\"\n\n\"I looked at it, but, of course, I didn't know what a house ought to be. Amelia said it wasn't nice, but he would have it. He hates Amelia. I'm sure of that, for he says everything he can to snub her and Mr. Gazebee. Mr. Gazebee is as good as he, at any rate. What do you think? He has given Richard warning to go. You never saw him, but he was a very good servant. He has given him warning, and he is not talking of getting another man. I won't live with him without somebody to wait upon me.\"\n\n\"My dearest girl, do not think of such a thing as leaving him.\"\n\n\"But I will think of it, mamma. You do not know what my life is in that house. He never speaks to me,--never. He comes home before dinner at half-past six, and when he has just shown himself he goes to his dressing-room. He is always silent at dinner-time, and after dinner he goes to sleep. He breakfasts always at nine, and goes away at half-past nine, though I know he does not get to his office till eleven. If I want anything, he says that it cannot be afforded. I never thought before that he was stingy, but I am sure now that he must be a miser at heart.\"\n\n\"It is better so than a spendthrift, Alexandrina.\"\n\n\"I don't know that it is better. He could not make me more unhappy than I am. Unhappy is no word for it. What can I do, shut up in such a house as that by myself from nine o'clock in the morning till six in the evening? Everybody knows what he is, so that nobody will come to see me. I tell you fairly, mamma, I will not stand it. If you cannot help me, I will look for help elsewhere.\"\n\nIt may, at any rate, be said that things were not going well with that branch of the De Courcy family. Nor, indeed, was it going well with some other branches. Lord Porlock had married, not having selected his partner for life from the choicest cream of the aristocratic circles, and his mother, while endeavouring to say a word in his favour, had been so abused by the earl that she had been driven to declare that she could no longer endure such usage. She had come up to London in direct opposition to his commands, while he was fastened to his room by gout; and had given her party in defiance of him, so that people should not say, when her back was turned, that she had slunk away in despair.\n\n\"I have borne it,\" she said to Margaretta, \"longer than any other woman in England would have done. While I thought that any of you would marry--\"\n\n\"Oh, don't talk of that, mamma,\" said Margaretta, putting a little scorn into her voice. She had not been quite pleased that even her mother should intimate that all her chance was over, and yet she herself had often told her mother that she had given up all thought of marrying.\n\n\"Rosina will go to Amelia's,\" the countess continued; \"Mr. Gazebee is quite satisfied that it should be so, and he will take care that she shall have enough to cover her own expenses. I propose that you and I, dear, shall go to Baden-Baden.\"\n\n\"And about money, mamma?\"\n\n\"Mr. Gazebee must manage it. In spite of all that your father says, I know that there must be money. The expense will be much less so than in our present way.\"\n\n\"And what will papa do himself?\"\n\n\"I cannot help it, my dear. No one knows what I have had to bear. Another year of it would kill me. His language has become worse and worse, and I fear every day that he is going to strike me with his crutch.\"\n\nUnder all these circumstances it cannot be said that the De Courcy interests were prospering.\n\nBut Lady De Courcy, when she had made up her mind to go to Baden-Baden, had by no means intended to take her youngest daughter with her. She had endured for years, and now Alexandrina was unable to endure for six months. Her chief grievance, moreover, was this,--that her husband was silent. The mother felt that no woman had a right to complain much of any such sorrow as that. If her earl had sinned only in that way, she would have been content to have remained by him till the last!\n\nAnd yet I do not know whether Alexandrina's life was not quite as hard as that of her mother. She barely exceeded the truth when she said that he never spoke to her. The hours with her in her new comfortless house were very long,--very long and very tedious. Marriage with her had by no means been the thing that she had expected. At home, with her mother, there had always been people around her, but they had not always been such as she herself would have chosen for her companions. She had thought that, when married, she could choose and have those about her who were congenial to her; but she found that none came to her. Her sister, who was a wiser woman than she, had begun her married life with a definite idea, and had carried it out; but this poor creature found herself, as it were, stranded. When once she had conceived it in her heart to feel anger against her husband,--and she had done so before they had been a week together,--there was no love to bring her back to him again. She did not know that it behoved her to look pleased when he entered the room, and to make him at any rate think that his presence gave her happiness. She became gloomy before she reached her new house, and never laid her gloom aside. He would have made a struggle for some domestic comfort, had any seemed to be within his reach. As it was, he struggled for domestic propriety, believing that he might so best bolster up his present lot in life. But the task became harder and harder to him, and the gloom became denser and more dense. He did not think of her unhappiness, but of his own; as she did not think of his tedium, but of hers. \"If this be domestic felicity!\" he would say to himself, as he sat in his arm-chair, striving to fix his attention upon a book.\n\n\"If this be the happiness of married life!\" she thought, as she remained listless, without even the pretence of a book, behind her teacups. In truth she would not walk with him, not caring for such exercise round the pavement of a London square; and he had resolutely determined that she should not run into debt for carriage hire. He was not a curmudgeon with his money; he was no miser. But he had found that in marrying an earl's daughter he had made himself a poor man, and he was resolved that he would not also be an embarrassed man.\n\nWhen the bride heard that her mother and sister were about to escape to Baden-Baden, there rushed upon her a sudden hope that she might be able to accompany the flight. She would not be parted from her husband, or at least not so parted that the world should suppose that they had quarrelled. She would simply go away and make a long visit,--a very long visit. Two years ago a sojourn with her mother and Margaretta at Baden-Baden would not have offered to her much that was attractive; but now, in her eyes, such a life seemed to be a life in Paradise. In truth, the tedium of those hours in Princess Royal Crescent had been very heavy.\n\nBut how could she contrive that it should be so? That conversation with her mother had taken place on the day preceding the party, and Lady De Courcy had repeated it with dismay to Margaretta.\n\n\"Of course he would allow her an income,\" Margaretta had coolly said.\n\n\"But, my dear, they have been married only ten weeks.\"\n\n\"I don't see why people are to be made absolutely wretched because they are married,\" Margaretta answered. \"I don't want to persuade her to leave him, but if what she says is true, it must be very uncomfortable.\"\n\nCrosbie had consented to go to the party in Portman-square, but had not greatly enjoyed himself on that festive occasion. He had stood about moodily, speaking hardly a word to any one. His whole aspect of life seemed to have been altered during the last few months. It was here, in such spots as this that he had been used to find his glory. On such occasions he had shone with peculiar light, making envious the hearts of many who watched the brilliance of his career as they stood around in dull quiescence. But now no one in those rooms had been more dull, more silent, or less courted than he; and yet he was established there as the son-in-law of that noble house. \"Rather slow work; isn't it?\" Gazebee had said to him, having, after many efforts, succeeded in reaching his brother-in-law in a corner. In answer to this Crosbie had only grunted. \"As for myself,\" continued Gazebee, \"I would a deal sooner be at home with my paper and slippers. It seems to me these sort of gatherings don't suit married men.\" Crosbie had again grunted, and had then escaped into another corner.\n\nCrosbie and his wife went home together in a cab,--speechless both of them. Alexandrina hated cabs,--but she had been plainly told that in such vehicles, and in such vehicles only, could she be allowed to travel. On the following morning he was at the breakfast-table punctually by nine, but she did not make her appearance till after he had gone to his office. Soon after that, however, she was away to her mother and her sister; but she was seated grimly in her drawing-room when he came in to see her, on his return to his house. Having said some word which might be taken for a greeting, he was about to retire; but she stopped him with a request that he would speak to her.\n\n\"Certainly,\" said he. \"I was only going to dress. It is nearly the half-hour.\"\n\n\"I won't keep you very long, and if dinner is a few minutes late it won't signify. Mamma and Margaretta are going to Baden-Baden.\"\n\n\"To Baden-Baden, are they?\"\n\n\"Yes; and they intend to remain there--for a considerable time.\" There was a little pause, and Alexandrina found it necessary to clear her voice and to prepare herself for further speech by a little cough. She was determined to make her proposition, but was rather afraid of the manner in which it might be first received.\n\n\"Has anything happened at Courcy Castle?\" Crosbie asked.\n\n\"No; that is, yes; there may have been some words between papa and mamma; but I don't quite know. That, however, does not matter now. Mamma is going, and purposes to remain there for the rest of the year.\"\n\n\"And the house in town will be given up.\"\n\n\"I suppose so, but that will be as papa chooses. Have you any objection to my going with mamma?\"\n\nWhat a question to be asked by a bride of ten weeks' standing! She had hardly been above a month with her husband in her new house, and she was now asking permission to leave it, and to leave him also, for an indefinite number of months--perhaps for ever. But she showed no excitement as she made her request. There was neither sorrow, nor regret, nor hope in her face. She had not put on half the animation which she had once assumed in asking for the use, twice a week, of a carriage done up to look as though it were her own private possession. Crosbie had then answered her with great sternness, and she had wept when his refusal was made certain to her. But there was to be no weeping now. She meant to go,--with his permission if he would accord it, and without it if he should refuse it. The question of money was no doubt important, but Gazebee should manage that,--as he managed all those things.\n\n\"Going with them to Baden-Baden?\" said Crosbie. \"For how long?\"\n\n\"Well; it would be no use unless it were for some time.\"\n\n\"For how long a time do you mean, Alexandrina? Speak out what you really have to say. For a month?\"\n\n\"Oh, more than that.\"\n\n\"For two months, or six, or as long as they may stay there?\"\n\n\"We could settle that afterwards, when I am there.\" During all this time she did not once look into his face, though he was looking hard at her throughout.\n\n\"You mean,\" said he, \"that you wish to go away from me.\"\n\n\"In one sense it would be going away, certainly.\"\n\n\"But in the ordinary sense? is it not so? When you talk of going to Baden-Baden for an unlimited number of months, have you any idea of coming back again?\"\n\n\"Back to London, you mean?\"\n\n\"Back to me,--to my house,--to your duties as a wife! Why cannot you say at once what it is you want? You wish to be separated from me?\"\n\n\"I am not happy here,--in this house.\"\n\n\"And who chose the house? Did I want to come here? But it is not that. If you are not happy here, what could you have in any other house to make you happy?\"\n\n\"If you were left alone in this room for seven or eight hours at a time, without a soul to come to you, you would know what I mean. And even after that, it is not much better. You never speak to me when you are here.\"\n\n\"Is it my fault that nobody comes to you? The fact is, Alexandrina, that you will not reconcile yourself to the manner of life which is suitable to my income. You are wretched because you cannot have yourself driven round the Park. I cannot find you a carriage, and will not attempt to do so. You may go to Baden-Baden, if you please;--that is, if your mother is willing to take you.\"\n\n\"Of course I must pay my own expenses,\" said Alexandrina. But to this he made no answer on the moment. As soon as he had given his permission he had risen from his seat and was going, and her last words only caught him in the doorway. After all, would not this be the cheapest arrangement that he could make? As he went through his calculations he stood up with his elbow on the mantel-piece in his dressing-room. He had scolded his wife because she had been unhappy with him; but had he not been quite as unhappy with her? Would it not be better that they should part in this quiet, half-unnoticed way;--that they should part and never again come together? He was lucky in this, that hitherto had come upon them no prospect of any little Crosbie to mar the advantages of such an arrangement. If he gave her four hundred a year, and allowed Gazebee two more towards the paying off of encumbrances, he would still have six on which to enjoy himself in London. Of course he could not live as he had lived in those happy days before his marriage, nor, independently of the cost, would such a mode of life be within his reach. But he might go to his club for his dinners; he might smoke his cigar in luxury; he would not be bound to that wooden home which, in spite of all his resolutions, had become almost unendurable to him. So he made his calculations, and found that it would be well that his bride should go. He would give over his house and furniture to Gazebee, allowing Gazebee to do as he would about that. To be once more a bachelor, in lodgings, with six hundred a year to spend on himself, seemed to him now such a prospect of happiness that he almost became light-hearted as he dressed himself. He would let her go to Baden-Baden.\n\nThere was nothing said about it at dinner, nor did he mention the subject again till the servant had left the tea-things on the drawing-room table. \"You can go with your mother if you like it,\" he then said.\n\n\"I think it will be best,\" she answered.\n\n\"Perhaps it will. At any rate you shall suit yourself.\"\n\n\"And about money?\"\n\n\"You had better leave me to speak to Gazebee about that.\"\n\n\"Very well. Will you have some tea?\" And then the whole thing was finished.\n\nOn the next day she went after lunch to her mother's house, and never came back again to Princess Royal Crescent. During that morning she packed up those things which she cared to pack herself, and sent her sisters there, with an old family servant, to bring away whatever else might be supposed to belong to her. \"Dear, dear,\" said Amelia, \"what trouble I had in getting these things together for them, and only the other day. I can't but think she's wrong to go away.\"\n\n\"I don't know,\" said Margaretta. \"She has not been so lucky as you have in the man she has married. I always felt that she would find it difficult to manage him.\"\n\n\"But, my dear, she has not tried. She has given up at once. It isn't management that was wanting. The fact is that when Alexandrina began she didn't make up her mind to the kind of thing she was coming to. I did. I knew it wasn't to be all party-going and that sort of thing. But I must own that Crosbie isn't the same sort of man as Mortimer. I don't think I could have gone on with him. You might as well have those small books put up; he won't care about them.\" And in this way Crosbie's house was dismantled.\n\nShe saw him no more, for he made no farewell visit to the house in Portman Square. A note had been brought to him at his office: \"I am here with mamma, and may as well say good-by now. We start on Tuesday. If you wish to write, you can send your letters to the housekeeper here. I hope you will make yourself comfortable, and that you will be well. Yours affectionately, A. C.\" He made no answer to it, but went that day and dined at his club.\n\n\"I haven't seen you this age,\" said Montgomerie Dobbs.\n\n\"No. My wife is going abroad with her mother, and while she is away I shall come back here again.\"\n\nThere was nothing more said to him, and no one ever made any inquiry about his domestic affairs. It seemed to him now as though he had no friend sufficiently intimate with him to ask him after his wife or family. She was gone, and in a month's time he found himself again in Mount Street,--beginning the world with five hundred a year, not six. For Mr. Gazebee, when the reckoning came, showed him that a larger income at the present moment was not possible for him. The countess had for a long time refused to let Lady Alexandrina go with her on so small a pittance as four hundred and fifty;--and then were there not the insurances to be maintained?\n\nBut I think he would have consented to accept his liberty with three hundred a year,--so great to him was the relief.\n\n# CHAPTER LVII. LILIAN DALE VANQUISHES HER MOTHER.\n\nMrs. Dale had been present during the interview in which John Eames had made his prayer to her daughter, but she had said little or nothing on that occasion. All her wishes had been in favour of the suitor, but she had not dared to express them, neither had she dared to leave the room. It had been hard upon him to be thus forced to declare his love in the presence of a third person, but he had done it, and had gone away with his answer. Then, when the thing was over, Lily, without any communion with her mother, took herself off, and was no more seen till the evening hours had come on, in which it was natural that they should be together again. Mrs. Dale, when thus alone, had been able to think of nothing but this new suit for her daughter's hand. If only it might be accomplished! If any words from her to Lily might be efficacious to such an end! And yet, hitherto, she had been afraid almost to utter a word.\n\nShe knew that it was very difficult. She declared to herself over and over that he had come too soon,--that the attempt had been made too quickly after that other shipwreck. How was it possible that the ship should put to sea again at once, with all her timbers so rudely strained? And yet, now that the attempt had been made, now that Eames had uttered his request and been sent away with an answer, she felt that she must at once speak to Lily on the subject, if ever she were to speak upon it. She thought that she understood her child and all her feelings. She recognized the violence of the shock which must be encountered before Lily could be brought to acknowledge such a change in her heart. But if the thing could be done, Lily would be a happy woman. When once done it would be in all respects a blessing. And if it were not done, might not Lily's life be blank, lonely, and loveless to the end? Yet when Lily came down in the evening, with some light, half-joking word on her lips, as was usual to her, Mrs. Dale was still afraid to venture upon her task.\n\n\"I suppose, mamma, we may consider it as a settled thing that everything must be again unpacked, and that the lodging scheme will be given up.\"\n\n\"I don't know that, my dear.\"\n\n\"Oh, but I do--after what you said just now. What geese everybody will think us!\"\n\n\"I shouldn't care a bit for that, if we didn't think ourselves geese, or if your uncle did not think us so.\"\n\n\"I believe he would think we were swans. If I had ever thought he would be so much in earnest about it, or that he would ever have cared about our being here, I would never have voted for going. But he is so strange. He is affectionate when he ought to be angry, and ill-natured when he ought to be gentle and kind.\"\n\n\"He has, at any rate, given us reason to feel sure of his affection.\"\n\n\"For us girls, I never doubted it. But, mamma, I don't think I could face Mrs. Boyce. Mrs. Hearn and Mrs. Crump would be very bad, and Hopkins would come down upon us terribly when he found that we had given way. But Mrs. Boyce would be worse than any of them. Can't you fancy the tone of her congratulations?\"\n\n\"I think I should survive Mrs. Boyce.\"\n\n\"Ah, yes; because we should have to go and tell her. I know your cowardice of old, mamma; don't I? And Bell wouldn't care a bit, because of her lover. Mrs. Boyce will be nothing to her. It is I that must bear it all. Well, I don't mind; I'll vote for staying if you will promise to be happy here. Oh, mamma, I'll vote for anything if you will be happy.\"\n\n\"And will you be happy?\"\n\n\"Yes, as happy as the day is long. Only I know we shall never see Bell. People never do see each other when they live just at that distance. It's too near for long visits, and too far for short visits. I'll tell you what; we might make arrangements each to walk half-way, and meet at the corner of Lord De Guest's wood. I wonder whether they'd let us put up a seat there. I think we might have a little house and carry sandwiches and a bottle of beer. Couldn't we see something of each other in that way?\"\n\nThus it came to be the fixed idea of both of them that they would abandon their plan of migrating to Guestwick, and on this subject they continued to talk over their tea-table; but on that evening Mrs. Dale ventured to say nothing about John Eames.\n\nBut they did not even yet dare to commence the work of reconstructing their old home. Bell must come back before they would do that, and the express assent of the squire must be formally obtained. Mrs. Dale must, in a degree, acknowledge herself to have been wrong, and ask to be forgiven for her contumacy.\n\n\"I suppose the three of us had better go up in sackcloth, and throw ashes on our foreheads as we meet Hopkins in the garden,\" said Lily, \"and then I know he'll heap coals of fire on our heads by sending us an early dish of peas. And Dingles would bring us in a pheasant, only that pheasants don't grow in May.\"\n\n\"If the sackcloth doesn't take an unpleasanter shape than that, I shan't mind it.\"\n\n\"That's because you've got no delicate feelings. And then uncle Christopher's gratitude!\"\n\n\"Ah! I shall feel that.\"\n\n\"But, mamma, we'll wait till Bell comes home. She shall decide. She is going away, and therefore she'll be free from prejudice. If uncle offers to paint the house,--and I know he will,--then I shall be humbled to the dust.\"\n\nBut yet Mrs. Dale had said nothing on the subject which was nearest to her heart. When Lily in pleasantry had accused her of cowardice, her mind had instantly gone off to that other matter, and she had told herself that she was a coward. Why should she be afraid of offering her counsel to her own child? It seemed to her as though she had neglected some duty in allowing Crosbie's conduct to have passed away without hardly a word of comment on it between herself and Lily. Should she not have forced upon her daughter's conviction the fact that Crosbie had been a villain, and as such should be discarded from her heart? As it was, Lily had spoken the simple truth when she told John Eames that she was dealing more openly with him on that affair of her engagement than she had ever dealt, even with her mother. Thinking of this as she sat in her own room that night, before she allowed herself to rest, Mrs. Dale resolved that on the next morning she would endeavour to make Lily see as she saw and think as she thought.\n\nShe let breakfast pass by before she began her task, and even then she did not rush at it at once. Lily sat herself down to her work when the teacups were taken away, and Mrs. Dale went down to her kitchen as was her wont. It was nearly eleven before she seated herself in the parlour, and even then she got her work-box before her and took out her needle.\n\n\"I wonder how Bell gets on with Lady Julia,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Very well, I'm sure.\"\n\n\"Lady Julia won't bite her, I know, and I suppose her dismay at the tall footmen has passed off by this time.\"\n\n\"I don't know that they have any tall footmen.\"\n\n\"Short footmen then,--you know what I mean; all the noble belongings. They must startle one at first, I'm sure, let one determine ever so much not to be startled. It's a very mean thing, no doubt, to be afraid of a lord merely because he is a lord; yet I'm sure I should be afraid at first, even of Lord De Guest, if I were staying in the house.\"\n\n\"It's well you didn't go then.\"\n\n\"Yes, I think it is. Bell is of a firmer mind, and I dare say she'll get over it after the first day. But what on earth does she do there? I wonder whether they mend their stockings in such a house as that.\"\n\n\"Not in public, I should think.\"\n\n\"In very grand houses they throw them away at once, I suppose. I've often thought about it. Do you believe the Prime Minister ever has his shoes sent to a cobbler?\"\n\n\"Perhaps a regular shoemaker will condescend to mend a Prime Minister's shoes.\"\n\n\"You do think they are mended then? But who orders it? Does he see himself when there's a little hole coming, as I do? Does an archbishop allow himself so many pairs of gloves in a year?\"\n\n\"Not very strictly, I should think.\"\n\n\"Then I suppose it comes to this, that he has a new pair whenever he wants them. But what constitutes the want? Does he ever say to himself that they'll do for another Sunday? I remember the bishop coming here once, and he had a hole at the end of his thumb. I was going to be confirmed, and I remember thinking that he ought to have been smarter.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you offer to mend it?\"\n\n\"I shouldn't have dared for all the world.\"\n\nThe conversation had commenced itself in a manner that did not promise much assistance to Mrs. Dale's project. When Lily got upon any subject, she was not easily induced to leave it, and when her mind had twisted itself in one direction, it was difficult to untwist it. She was now bent on a consideration of the smaller social habits of the high and mighty among us, and was asking her mother whether she supposed that the royal children ever carried halfpence in their pockets, or descended so low as fourpenny-bits.\n\n\"I suppose they have pockets like other children,\" said Lily.\n\nBut her mother stopped her suddenly,--\n\n\"Lily, dear, I want to say something to you about John Eames.\"\n\n\"Mamma, I'd sooner talk about the Royal Family just at present.\"\n\n\"But, dear, you must forgive me if I persist. I have thought much about it, and I'm sure you will not oppose me when I am doing what I think to be my duty.\"\n\n\"No, mamma; I won't oppose you, certainly.\"\n\n\"Since Mr. Crosbie's conduct was made known to you, I have mentioned his name in your hearing very seldom.\"\n\n\"No, mamma, you have not. And I have loved you so dearly for your goodness to me. Do not think that I have not understood and known how generous you have been. No other mother ever was so good as you have been. I have known it all, and thought of it every day of my life, and thanked you in my heart for your trusting silence. Of course, I understand your feelings. You think him bad and you hate him for what he has done.\"\n\n\"I would not willingly hate any one, Lily.\"\n\n\"Ah, but you do hate him. If I were you, I should hate him; but I am not you, and I love him. I pray for his happiness every night and morning, and for hers. I have forgiven him altogether, and I think that he was right. When I am old enough to do so without being wrong, I will go to him and tell him so. I should like to hear of all his doings and all his success, if it were only possible. How, then, can you and I talk about him? It is impossible. You have been silent and I have been silent,--let us remain silent.\"\n\n\"It is not about Mr. Crosbie that I wish to speak. But I think you ought to understand that conduct such as his will be rebuked by all the world. You may forgive him, but you should acknowledge--\"\n\n\"Mamma, I don't want to acknowledge anything;--not about him. There are things as to which a person cannot argue.\" Mrs. Dale felt that this present matter was one as to which she could not argue. \"Of course, mamma,\" continued Lily, \"I don't want to oppose you in anything, but I think we had better be silent about this.\"\n\n\"Of course I am thinking only of your future happiness.\"\n\n\"I know you are; but pray believe me that you need not be alarmed. I do not mean to be unhappy. Indeed, I think I may say I am not unhappy; of course I have been unhappy,--very unhappy. I did think that my heart would break. But that has passed away, and I believe I can be as happy as my neighbours. We're all of us sure to have some troubles, as you used to tell us when we were children.\"\n\nMrs. Dale felt that she had begun wrong, and that she would have been able to make better progress had she omitted all mention of Crosbie's name. She knew exactly what it was that she wished to say,--what were the arguments which she desired to expound before her daughter; but she did not know what language to use, or how she might best put her thoughts into words. She paused for a while, and Lily went on with her work as though the conversation was over. But the conversation was not over.\n\n\"It was about John Eames, and not about Mr. Crosbie, that I wished to speak to you.\"\n\n\"Oh, mamma!\"\n\n\"My dear, you must not hinder me in doing what I think to be a duty. I heard what he said to you and what you replied, and of course I cannot but have my mind full of the subject. Why should you set yourself against him in so fixed a manner?\"\n\n\"Because I love another man.\" These words she spoke out loud, in a steady, almost dogged tone, with a certain show of audacity,--as though aware that the declaration was unseemly, but resolved that, though unseemly, it must be made.\n\n\"But, Lily, that love, from its very nature, must cease; or, rather, such love is not the same as that you felt when you thought that you were to be his wife.\"\n\n\"Yes, it is. If she died, and he came to me in five years' time, I would still take him. I should think myself constrained to take him.\"\n\n\"But she is not dead, nor likely to die.\"\n\n\"That makes no difference. You don't understand me, mamma.\"\n\n\"I think I do, and I want you to understand me also. I know how difficult is your position; I know what your feelings are; but I know this also, that if you could reason with yourself, and bring yourself in time to receive John Eames as a dear friend--\"\n\n\"I did receive him as a dear friend. Why not? He is a dear friend. I love him heartily,--as you do.\"\n\n\"You know what I mean?\"\n\n\"Yes, I do; and I tell you it is impossible.\"\n\n\"If you would make the attempt, all this misery would soon be forgotten. If once you could bring yourself to regard him as a friend, who might become your husband, all this would be changed,--and I should see you happy!\"\n\n\"You are strangely anxious to be rid of me, mamma!\"\n\n\"Yes, Lily;--to be rid of you in that way. If I could see you put your hand in his as his promised wife, I think that I should be the happiest woman in the world.\"\n\n\"Mamma, I cannot make you happy in that way. If you really understood my feelings, my doing as you propose would make you very unhappy. I should commit a great sin,--the sin against which women should be more guarded than against any other. In my heart I am married to that other man. I gave myself to him, and loved him, and rejoiced in his love. When he kissed me I kissed him again, and I longed for his kisses. I seemed to live only that he might caress me. All that time I never felt myself to be wrong,--because he was all in all to me. I was his own. That has been changed,--to my great misfortune; but it cannot be undone or forgotten. I cannot be the girl I was before he came here. There are things that will not have themselves buried and put out of sight, as though they had never been. I am as you are, mamma,--widowed. But you have your daughter, and I have my mother. If you will be contented, so will I.\" Then she got up and threw herself on her mother's neck.\n\nMrs. Dale's argument was over now. To such an appeal as that last made by Lily no rejoinder on her part was possible. After that she was driven to acknowledge to herself that she must be silent. Years as they rolled on might make a change, but no reasoning could be of avail. She embraced her daughter, weeping over her,--whereas Lily's eyes were dry. \"It shall be as you will,\" Mrs. Dale murmured.\n\n\"Yes, as I will. I shall have my own way; shall I not? That is all I want; to be a tyrant over you, and make you do my bidding in everything, as a well-behaved mother should do. But I won't be stern in my orderings. If you will only be obedient, I will be so gracious to you! There's Hopkins again. I wonder whether he has come to knock us down and trample upon us with another speech.\"\n\nHopkins knew very well to which window he must come, as only one of the rooms was at the present time habitable. He came up to the dining-room, and almost flattened his nose against the glass.\n\n\"Well, Hopkins,\" said Lily, \"here we are.\" Mrs. Dale had turned her face away, for she knew that the tears were still on her cheek.\n\n\"Yes, miss, I see you. I want to speak to your mamma, miss.\"\n\n\"Come round,\" said Lily, anxious to spare her mother the necessity of showing herself at once. \"It's too cold to open the window; come round, and I'll open the door.\"\n\n\"Too cold!\" muttered Hopkins, as he went. \"They'll find it a deal colder in lodgings at Guestwick.\" However, he went round through the kitchen, and Lily met him in the hall.\n\n\"Well, Hopkins, what is it? Mamma has got a headache.\"\n\n\"Got a headache, has she? I won't make her headache no worse. It's my opinion that there's nothing for a headache so good as fresh air. Only some people can't abear to be blowed upon, not for a minute. If you don't let down the lights in a greenhouse more or less every day, you'll never get any plants,--never;--and it's just the same with the grapes. Is I to go back and say as how I couldn't see her?\"\n\n\"You can come in if you like; only be quiet, you know.\"\n\n\"Ain't I ollays quiet, miss? Did anybody ever hear me rampage? If you please, ma'am, the squire's come home.\"\n\n\"What, home from Guestwick? Has he brought Miss Bell?\"\n\n\"He ain't brought none but hisself, 'cause he come on horseback; and it's my belief he's going back almost immediate. But he wants you to come to him, Mrs. Dale.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, I'll come at once.\"\n\n\"He bade me say with his kind love. I don't know whether that makes any difference.\"\n\n\"At any rate, I'll come, Hopkins.\"\n\n\"And I ain't to say nothing about the headache?\"\n\n\"About what?\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"No, no, no,\" said Lily. \"Mamma will be there at once. Go and tell my uncle, there's a good man,\" and she put up her hand and backed him out of the room.\n\n\"I don't believe she's got no headache at all,\" said Hopkins, grumbling, as he returned through the back premises. \"What lies gentlefolks do tell! If I said I'd a headache when I ought to be out among the things, what would they say to me? But a poor man mustn't never lie, nor yet drink, nor yet do nothing.\" And so he went back with his message.\n\n\"What can have brought your uncle home?\" said Mrs. Dale.\n\n\"Just to look after the cattle, and to see that the pigs are not all dead. My wonder is that he should ever have gone away.\"\n\n\"I must go up to him at once.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, of course.\"\n\n\"And what shall I say about the house?\"\n\n\"It's not about that,--at least I think not. I don't think he'll speak about that again till you speak to him.\"\n\n\"But if he does?\"\n\n\"You must put your trust in Providence. Declare you've got a bad headache, as I told Hopkins just now; only you would throw me over by not understanding. I'll walk with you down to the bridge.\" So they went off together across the lawn.\n\nBut Lily was soon left alone, and continued her walk, waiting for her mother's return. As she went round and round the gravel paths, she thought of the words that she had said to her mother. She had declared that she also was widowed. \"And so it should be,\" she said, debating the matter with herself. \"What can a heart be worth if it can be transferred hither and thither as circumstances and convenience and comfort may require? When he held me here in his arms\"--and, as the thoughts ran through her brain, she remembered the very spot on which they had stood--\"oh, my love!\" she had said to him then as she returned his kisses--\"oh, my love, my love, my love!\" \"When he held me here in his arms, I told myself that it was right, because he was my husband. He has changed, but I have not. It might be that I should have ceased to love him, and then I should have told him so. I should have done as he did.\" But, as she came to this, she shuddered, thinking of the Lady Alexandrina. \"It was very quick,\" she said, still speaking to herself; \"very, very. But then men are not the same as women.\" And she walked on eagerly, hardly remembering where she was, thinking over it all, as she did daily; remembering every little thought and word of those few eventful months in which she had learned to regard Crosbie as her husband and master. She had declared that she had conquered her unhappiness; but there were moments in which she was almost wild with misery. \"Tell me to forget him!\" she said. \"It is the one thing which will never be forgotten.\"\n\nAt last she heard her mother's step coming down across the squire's garden, and she took up her post at the bridge.\n\n\"Stand and deliver,\" she said, as her mother put her foot upon the plank. \"That is, if you've got anything worth delivering. Is anything settled?\"\n\n\"Come up to the house,\" said Mrs. Dale, \"and I'll tell you all.\"\n\n# CHAPTER LVIII. THE FATE OF THE SMALL HOUSE.\n\nThere was something in the tone of Mrs. Dale's voice, as she desired her daughter to come up to the house, and declared that her budget of news should be opened there, which at once silenced Lily's assumed pleasantry. Her mother had been away fully two hours, during which Lily had still continued her walk round the garden, till at last she had become impatient for her mother's footstep. Something serious must have been said between her uncle and her mother during those long two hours. The interviews to which Mrs. Dale was occasionally summoned at the Great House did not usually exceed twenty minutes, and the upshot would be communicated to the girls in a turn or two round the garden; but in the present instance Mrs. Dale positively declined to speak till she was seated within the house.\n\n\"Did he come over on purpose to see you, mamma?\"\n\n\"Yes, my dear, I believe so. He wished to see you, too; but I asked his permission to postpone that till after I had talked to you.\"\n\n\"To see me, mamma? About what?\"\n\n\"To kiss you, and bid you love him; solely for that. He has not a word to say to you that will vex you.\"\n\n\"Then I will kiss him, and love him, too.\"\n\n\"Yes, you will when I have told you all. I have promised him solemnly to give up all idea of going to Guestwick. So that is over.\"\n\n\"Oh, oh! And we may begin to unpack at once? What an episode in one's life!\"\n\n\"We may certainly unpack, for I have pledged myself to him; and he is to go into Guestwick himself and arrange about the lodgings.\"\n\n\"Does Hopkins know it?\"\n\n\"I should think not yet.\"\n\n\"Nor Mrs. Boyce! Mamma, I don't believe I shall be able to survive this next week. We shall look such fools! I'll tell you what we'll do;--it will be the only comfort I can have;--we'll go to work and get everything back into its place before Bell comes home, so as to surprise her.\"\n\n\"What! in two days?\"\n\n\"Why not? I'll make Hopkins come and help, and then he'll not be so bad. I'll begin at once and go to the blankets and beds, because I can undo them myself.\"\n\n\"But I haven't half told you all; and, indeed, I don't know how to make you understand what passed between us. He is very unhappy about Bernard; Bernard has determined to go abroad, and may be away for years.\"\n\n\"One can hardly blame a man for following up his profession.\"\n\n\"There was no blaming. He only said that it was very sad for him that, in his old age, he should be left alone. This was before there was any talk about our remaining. Indeed he seemed determined not to ask that again as a favour. I could see that in his eye, and I understood it from his tone. He went on to speak of you and Bell, saying how well he loved you both; but that, unfortunately, his hopes regarding you had not been fulfilled.\"\n\n\"Ah, but he shouldn't have had hopes of that sort.\"\n\n\"Listen, my dear, and I think that you will not feel angry with him. He said that he felt his house had never been pleasant to you. Then there followed words which I could not repeat, even if I could remember them. He said much about myself, regretting that the feeling between us had not been more kindly. 'But my heart,' he said, 'has ever been kinder than my words.' Then I got up from where I was seated, and going over to him, I told him that we would remain here.\"\n\n\"And what did he say?\"\n\n\"I don't know what he said. I know that I was crying, and that he kissed me. It was the first time in his life. I know that he was pleased,--beyond measure pleased. After a while he became animated, and talked of doing ever so many things. He promised that very painting of which you spoke.\"\n\n\"Ah, yes, I knew it; and Hopkins will be here with the peas before dinner-time to-morrow, and Dingles with his shoulders smothered with rabbits. And then Mrs. Boyce! Mamma, he didn't think of Mrs. Boyce; or, in very charity of heart, he would still have maintained his sadness.\"\n\n\"Then he did not think of her; for when I left him he was not at all sad. But I haven't told you half yet.\"\n\n\"Dear me, mamma; was there more than that?\"\n\n\"And I've told it all wrong; for what I've got to tell now was said before a word was spoken about the house. He brought it in just after what he said about Bernard. He said that Bernard would, of course, be his heir.\"\n\n\"Of course he will.\"\n\n\"And that he should think it wrong to encumber the property with any charges for you girls.\"\n\n\"Mamma, did any one ever--\"\n\n\"Stop, Lily, stop; and make your heart kinder towards him if you can.\"\n\n\"It is kind; only I hate to be told that I'm not to have a lot of money, as though I had ever shown a desire for it. I have never envied Bernard his man-servant, or his maid-servant, or his ox, or his ass, or anything that is his. To tell the truth I didn't even wish it to be Bell's, because I knew well that there was somebody she would like a great deal better than ever she could like Bernard.\"\n\n\"I shall never get to the end of my story.\"\n\n\"Yes, you will, mamma, if you persevere.\"\n\n\"The long and the short of it is this, that he has given Bell three thousand pounds, and has given you three thousand also.\"\n\n\"But why me, mamma?\" said Lily, and the colour of her cheeks became red as she spoke. There should if possible be nothing more said about John Eames; but whatever might or might not be the necessity of speaking, at any rate, let there be no mistake. \"But why me, mamma?\"\n\n\"Because, as he explained to me, he thinks it right to do the same by each of you. The money is yours at this moment,--to buy hair-pins with, if you please. I had no idea that he could command so large a sum.\"\n\n\"Three thousand pounds! The last money he gave me was half-a-crown, and I thought that he was so stingy! I particularly wanted ten shillings. I should have liked it so much better now if he had given me a nice new five-pound note.\"\n\n\"You'd better tell him so.\"\n\n\"No; because then he'd give me that too. But with five pounds I should have the feeling that I might do what I liked with it;--buy a dressing-case, and a thing for a squirrel to run round in. But nobody ever gives girls money like that, so that they can enjoy it.\"\n\n\"Oh, Lily; you ungrateful child!\"\n\n\"No, I deny it. I'm not ungrateful. I'm very grateful, because his heart was softened--and because he cried and kissed you. I'll be ever so good to him! But how I'm to thank him for giving me three thousand pounds, I cannot think. It's a sort of thing altogether beyond my line of life. It sounds like something that's to come to me in another world, but which I don't want quite yet. I am grateful, but with a misty, mazy sort of gratitude. Can you tell me how soon I shall have a new pair of Balmoral boots because of this money? If that were brought home to me I think it would enliven my gratitude.\"\n\nThe squire, as he rode back to Guestwick, fell again from that animation, which Mrs. Dale had described, into his natural sombre mood. He thought much of his past life, declaring to himself the truth of those words in which he had told his sister-in-law that his heart had ever been kinder than his words. But the world, and all those nearest to him in the world, had judged him always by his words rather than by his heart. They had taken the appearance, which he could not command or alter, rather than the facts, of which he had been the master. Had he not been good to all his relations?--and yet was there one among them that cared for him? \"I'm almost sorry that they are going to stay,\" he said to himself;--\"I know that I shall disappoint them.\" Yet when he met Bell at the Manor House he accosted her cheerily, telling her with much appearance of satisfaction that that flitting into Guestwick was not to be accomplished.\n\n\"I am so glad,\" said she. \"It is long since I wished it.\"\n\n\"And I do not think your mother wishes it now.\"\n\n\"I am sure she does not. It was all a misunderstanding from the first. When some of us could not do all that you wished, we thought it better--\" Then Bell paused, finding that she would get herself into a mess if she persevered.\n\n\"We will not say any more about it,\" said the squire. \"The thing is over, and I am very glad that it should be so pleasantly settled. I was talking to Dr. Crofts yesterday.\"\n\n\"Were you, uncle?\"\n\n\"Yes; and he is to come and stay with me the day before he is married. We have arranged it all. And we'll have the breakfast up at the Great House. Only you must fix the day. I should say some time in May. And, my dear, you'll want to make yourself fine; here's a little money for you. You are to spend that before your marriage, you know.\" Then he shambled away, and as soon as he was alone, again became sad and despondent. He was a man for whom we may predicate some gentle sadness and continued despondency to the end of his life's chapter.\n\nWe left John Eames in the custody of Lady Julia, who had overtaken him in the act of erasing Lily's name from the railing which ran across the brook. He had been premeditating an escape home to his mother's house in Guestwick, and thence back to London, without making any further appearance at the Manor House. But as soon as he heard Lady Julia's step, and saw her figure close upon him, he knew that his retreat was cut off from him. So he allowed himself to be led away quietly up to the house. With Lady Julia herself he openly discussed the whole matter,--telling her that his hopes were over, his happiness gone, and his heart half-broken. Though he would perhaps have cared but little for her congratulations in success, he could make himself more amenable to consolation and sympathy from her than from any other inmate in the earl's house. \"I don't know what I shall say to your brother,\" he whispered to her, as they approached the side door at which she intended to enter.\n\n\"Will you let me break it to him? After that he will say a few words to you of course, but you need not be afraid of him.\"\n\n\"And Mr. Dale?\" said Johnny. \"Everybody has heard about it. Everybody will know what a fool I have made myself.\" She suggested that the earl should speak to the squire, assured him that nobody would think him at all foolish, and then left him to make his way up to his own bedroom. When there he found a letter from Cradell, which had been delivered in his absence; but the contents of that letter may best be deferred to the next chapter. They were not of a nature to give him comfort or to add to his sorrow.\n\nAbout an hour before dinner there was a knock at his door, and the earl himself, when summoned, made his appearance in the room. He was dressed in his usual farming attire, having been caught by Lady Julia on his first approach to the house, and had come away direct to his young friend, after having been duly trained in what he ought to say by his kind-hearted sister. I am not, however, prepared to declare that he strictly followed his sister's teaching in all that he said upon the occasion.\n\n\"Well, my boy,\" he began, \"so the young lady has been perverse.\"\n\n\"Yes, my lord. That is, I don't know about being perverse. It is all over.\"\n\n\"That's as may be, Johnny. As far as I know, not half of them accept their lovers the first time of asking.\"\n\n\"I shall not ask her again.\"\n\n\"Oh, yes, you will. You don't mean to say you are angry with her for refusing you.\"\n\n\"Not in the least. I have no right to be angry. I am only angry with myself for being such a fool, Lord De Guest. I wish I had been dead before I came down here on this errand. Now I think of it, I know there are so many things which ought to have made me sure how it would be.\"\n\n\"I don't see that at all. You come down again,--let me see,--it's May now. Say you come when the shooting begins in September. If we can't get you leave of absence in any other way, we'll make old Buffle come too. Only, by George, I believe he'd shoot us all. But never mind; we'll manage that. You keep up your spirits till September, and then we'll fight the battle in another way. The squire shall get up a little party for the bride, and my lady Lily must go then. You shall meet her so; and then we'll shoot over the squire's land. We'll bring you together so; you see if we don't. Lord bless me! Refused once! My belief is, that in these days a girl thinks nothing of a man till she has refused him half-a-dozen times.\"\n\n\"I don't think Lily is at all like that.\"\n\n\"Look here, Johnny. I have not a word to say against Miss Lily. I like her very much, and think her one of the nicest girls I know. When she's your wife, I'll love her dearly, if she'll let me. But she's made of the same stuff as other girls, and will act in the same way. Things have gone a little astray among you, and they won't right themselves all in a minute. She knows now what your feelings are, and she'll go on thinking of it, till at last you'll be in her thoughts more than that other fellow. Don't tell me about her becoming an old maid, because at her time of life she has been so unfortunate as to come across a false-hearted man like that. It may take a little time; but if you'll carry on and not be down-hearted, you'll find it will all come right in the end. Everybody doesn't get all that they want in a minute. How I shall quiz you about all this when you have been two or three years married!\"\n\n\"I don't think I shall ever be able to ask her again; and I feel sure, if I do, that her answer will be the same. She told me in so many words--; but never mind, I cannot repeat her words.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to repeat them; nor yet to heed them beyond their worth. Lily Dale is a very pretty girl; clever, too, I believe, and good, I'm sure; but her words are not more sacred than those of other men or women. What she has said to you now, she means, no doubt; but the minds of men and women are prone to change, especially when such changes are conducive to their own happiness.\"\n\n\"At any rate I'll never forget your kindness, Lord De Guest.\"\n\n\"And there is one other thing I want to say to you, Johnny. A man should never allow himself to be cast down by anything,--not outwardly, to the eyes of other men.\"\n\n\"But how is he to help it?\"\n\n\"His pluck should prevent him. You were not afraid of a roaring bull, nor yet of that man when you thrashed him at the railway station. You've pluck enough of that kind. You must now show that you've that other kind of pluck. You know the story of the boy who would not cry though the wolf was gnawing him underneath his frock. Most of us have some wolf to gnaw us somewhere; but we are generally gnawed beneath our clothes, so that the world doesn't see; and it behoves us so to bear it that the world shall not suspect. The man who goes about declaring himself to be miserable will be not only miserable, but contemptible as well.\"\n\n\"But the wolf hasn't gnawed me beneath my clothes; everybody knows it.\"\n\n\"Then let those who do know it learn that you are able to bear such wounds without outward complaint. I tell you fairly that I cannot sympathize with a lackadaisical lover.\"\n\n\"I know that I have made myself ridiculous to everybody. I wish I had never come here. I wish you had never seen me.\"\n\n\"Don't say that, my dear boy; but take my advice for what it is worth. And remember what it is that I say; with your grief I do sympathize, but not with any outward expression of it;--not with melancholy looks, and a sad voice, and an unhappy gait. A man should always be able to drink his wine and seem to enjoy it. If he can't, he is so much less of a man than he would be otherwise,--not so much more, as some people seem to think. Now get yourself dressed, my dear fellow, and come down to dinner as though nothing had happened to you.\"\n\nAs soon as the earl was gone John looked at his watch and saw that it still wanted some forty minutes to dinner. Fifteen minutes would suffice for him to dress, and therefore there was time sufficient for him to seat himself in his arm-chair and think over it all. He had for a moment been very angry when his friend had told him that he could not sympathize with a lackadaisical lover. It was an ill-natured word. He felt it to be so when he heard it, and so he continued to think during the whole of the half-hour that he sat in that chair. But it probably did him more good than any word that the earl had ever spoken to him,--or any other word that he could have used. \"Lackadaisical! I'm not lackadaisical,\" he said to himself, jumping up from his chair, and instantly sitting down again. \"I didn't say anything to him. I didn't tell him. Why did he come to me?\" And yet, though he endeavoured to abuse Lord De Guest in his thoughts, he knew that Lord De Guest was right, and that he was wrong. He knew that he had been lackadaisical, and was ashamed of himself; and at once resolved that he would henceforth demean himself as though no calamity had happened to him. \"I've a good mind to take him at his word, and drink wine till I'm drunk.\" Then he strove to get up his courage by a song.\n\nIf she be not fair for me, What care I how--\n\n\"But I do care. What stuff it is a man writing poetry and putting into it such lies as that! Everybody knows that he did care,--that is, if he wasn't a heartless beast.\"\n\nBut nevertheless, when the time came for him to go down into the drawing-room he did make the effort which his friend had counselled, and walked into the room with less of that hang-dog look than the earl and Lady Julia had expected. They were both there, as was also the squire, and Bell followed him in less than a minute.\n\n\"You haven't seen Crofts to-day, John, have you?\" said the earl.\n\n\"No; I haven't been anywhere his way!\"\n\n\"His way! His ways are every way, I take it. I wanted him to come and dine, but he seemed to think it improper to eat two dinners in the same house two days running. Isn't that his theory, Miss Dale?\"\n\n\"I'm sure I don't know, Lord De Guest. At any rate, it isn't mine.\"\n\nSo they went to their feast, and before his last chance was over John Eames found himself able to go through the pretence of enjoying his roast mutton.\n\nThere can, I think, be no doubt that in all such calamities as that which he was now suffering, the agony of the misfortune is much increased by the conviction that the facts of the case are known to those round about the sufferer. A most warm-hearted and intensely-feeling young gentleman might, no doubt, eat an excellent dinner after being refused by the girl of his devotions, provided that he had reason to believe that none of those in whose company he ate it knew anything of his rejection. But the same warm-hearted and intensely-feeling young gentleman would find it very difficult to go through the ceremony with any appearance of true appetite or gastronomic enjoyment, if he were aware that all his convives knew all the facts of his little misfortune. Generally, we may suppose, a man in such condition goes to his club for his dinner, or seeks consolation in the shades of some adjacent Richmond or Hampton Court. There he meditates on his condition in silence, and does ultimately enjoy his little plate of whitebait, his cutlet and his moderate pint of sherry. He probably goes alone to the theatre, and, in his stall, speculates with a somewhat bitter sarcasm on the vanity of the world. Then he returns home, sad indeed, but with a moderated sadness, and as he puffs out the smoke of his cigar at the open window,--with perhaps the comfort of a little brandy-and-water at his elbow,--swears to himself that, \"By Jove, he'll have another try for it.\" Alone, a man may console himself, or among a crowd of unconscious mortals; but it must be admitted that the position of John Eames was severe. He had been invited down there to woo Lily Dale, and the squire and Bell had been asked to be present at the wooing. Had it all gone well, nothing could have been nicer. He would have been the hero of the hour, and everybody would have sung for him his song of triumph. But everything had not gone well, and he found it very difficult to carry himself otherwise than lackadaisically. On the whole, however, his effort was such that the earl gave him credit for his demeanour, and told him when parting with him for the night that he was a fine fellow, and that everything should go right with him yet.\n\n\"And you mustn't be angry with me for speaking harshly to you,\" he said.\n\n\"I wasn't a bit angry.\"\n\n\"Yes, you were; and I rather meant that you should be. But you mustn't go away in dudgeon.\"\n\nHe stayed at the Manor House one day longer, and then he returned to his room at the Income-tax Office, to the disagreeable sound of Sir Raffle's little bell, and the much more disagreeable sound of Sir Raffle's big voice.\n\n# CHAPTER LIX. JOHN EAMES BECOMES A MAN.\n\nEames, when he was half way up to London in the railway carriage, took out from his pocket a letter and read it. During the former portion of his journey he had been thinking of other things; but gradually he had resolved that it would be better for him not to think more of those other things for the present, and therefore he had recourse to his letter by way of dissipating his thoughts. It was from Cradell, and ran as follows:--\n\nIncome-Tax Office, May --, 186--.\n\nMY DEAR JOHN,--I hope the tidings which I have to give you will not make you angry, and that you will not think I am untrue to the great friendship which I have for you because of that which I am now going to tell you. There is no man--[and the word man was underscored]--there is no man whose regard I value so highly as I do yours; and though I feel that you can have no just ground to be displeased with me after all that I have heard you say on many occasions, nevertheless, in matters of the heart it is very hard for one person to understand the sentiments of another, and when the affections of a lady are concerned, I know that quarrels will sometimes arise.\n\nEames, when he had got so far as this, on the first perusal of the letter, knew well what was to follow. \"Poor Caudle!\" he said to himself; \"he's hooked, and he'll never get himself off the hook again.\"\n\nBut let that be as it may, the matter has now gone too far for any alteration to be made by me; nor would any mere earthly inducement suffice to change me. The claims of friendship are very strong, but those of love are paramount. Of course I know all that has passed between you and Amelia Roper. Much of this I had heard from you before, but the rest she has now told me with that pure-minded honesty which is the most remarkable feature in her character. She has confessed that at one time she felt attached to you, and that she was induced by your perseverance to allow you to regard her as your fiancy. [Fancy-girl he probably conceived to be the vulgar English for the elegant term which he used.] But all that must be over between you now. Amelia has promised to be mine--[this also was underscored]--and mine I intend that she shall be. That you may find in the kind smiles of L. D. consolation for any disappointment which this may occasion you, is the ardent wish of your true friend,\n\nP.S.--Perhaps I had better tell you the whole. Mrs. Roper has been in some trouble about her house. She is a little in arrears with her rent, and some bills have not been paid. As she explained that she has been brought into this by those dreadful Lupexes I have consented to take the house into my own hands, and have given bills to one or two tradesmen for small amounts. Of course she will take them up, but it was the credit that was wanting. She will carry on the house, but I shall, in fact, be the proprietor. I suppose it will not suit you now to remain here, but don't you think I might make it comfortable enough for some of our fellows; say half-a-dozen, or so? That is Mrs. Roper's idea, and I certainly think it is not a bad one. Our first efforts must be to get rid of the Lupexes. Miss Spruce goes next week. In the meantime we are all taking our meals up in our own rooms, so that there is nothing for the Lupexes to eat. But they don't seem to mind that, and still keep the sitting-room and best bedroom. We mean to lock them out after Tuesday, and send all their boxes to the public-house.\n\nPoor Cradell! Eames, as he threw himself back upon his seat and contemplated the depth of misfortune into which his friend had fallen, began to be almost in love with his own position. He himself was, no doubt, a very miserable fellow. There was only one thing in life worth living for, and that he could not get. He had been thinking for the last three days of throwing himself before a locomotive steam-engine, and was not quite sure that he would not do it yet; but, nevertheless, his place was a place among the gods as compared to that which poor Cradell had selected for himself. To be not only the husband of Amelia Roper, but to have been driven to take upon himself as his bride's fortune the whole of his future mother-in-law's debts! To find himself the owner of a very indifferent lodging-house;--the owner as regarded all responsibility, though not the owner as regarded any possible profit! And then, above and almost worse than all the rest, to find himself saddled with the Lupexes in the beginning of his career! Poor Cradell indeed!\n\nEames had not taken his things away from the lodging-house before he left London, and therefore determined to drive to Burton Crescent immediately on his arrival, not with the intention of remaining there, even for a night, but that he might bid them farewell, speak his congratulations to Amelia, and arrange for his final settlement with Mrs. Roper. It should have been explained in the last chapter that the earl had told him before parting with him that his want of success with Lily would make no difference as regarded money. John had, of course, expostulated, saying that he did not want anything, and would not, under his existing circumstances, accept anything; but the earl was a man who knew how to have his own way, and in this matter did have it. Our friend, therefore, was a man of wealth when he returned to London, and could tell Mrs. Roper that he would send her a cheque for her little balance as soon as he reached his office.\n\nHe arrived in the middle of the day,--not timing his return at all after the usual manner of Government clerks, who generally manage to reach the metropolis not more than half an hour before the moment at which they are bound to show themselves in their seats. But he had come back two days before he was due, and had run away from the country as though London in May to him were much pleasanter than the woods and fields. But neither had London nor the woods and fields any influence on his return. He had gone down that he might throw himself at the feet of Lily Dale,--gone down, as he now confessed to himself, with hopes almost triumphant, and he had returned because Lily Dale would not have him at her feet. \"I loved him,--him, Crosbie,--better than all the world besides. It is still the same. I still love him better than all the world.\" Those were the words which had driven him back to London; and having been sent away with such words as those, it was little matter to him whether he reached his office a day or two sooner or later. The little room in the city, even with the accompaniment of Sir Raffle's bell and Sir Raffle's voice, would be now more congenial to him than Lady Julia's drawing-room. He would therefore present himself to Sir Raffle on that very afternoon, and expel some interloper from his seat. But he would first call in Burton Crescent and say farewell to the Ropers.\n\nThe door was opened for him by the faithful Jemima. \"Mr. Heames, Mr. Heames! ho dear, ho dear!\" and the poor girl, who had always taken his side in the adventures of the lodging-house, raised her hands on high and lamented the fate which had separated her favourite from its fortunes. \"I suppose you knows it all, Mister Johnny?\" Mister Johnny said that he believed he did know it all, and asked for the mistress of the house. \"Yes, sure enough, she's at home. She don't dare stir out much, 'cause of them Lupexes. Ain't this a pretty game? No dinner and no nothink! Them boxes is Miss Spruce's. She's agoing now, this minute. You'll find 'em all upstairs in the drawen-room.\" So upstairs into the drawing-room he went, and there he found the mother and daughter, and with them Miss Spruce, tightly packed up in her bonnet and shawl. \"Don't, mother,\" Amelia was saying; \"what's the good of going on in that way? If she chooses to go, let her go.\"\n\n\"But she's been with me now so many years,\" said Mrs. Roper, sobbing; \"and I've always done everything for her! Haven't I, now, Sally Spruce?\" It struck Eames immediately that, though he had been an inmate in the house for two years, he had never before heard that maiden lady's Christian name. Miss Spruce was the first to see Eames as he entered the room. It is probable that Mrs. Roper's pathos might have produced some answering pathos on her part had she remained unobserved, but the sight of a young man brought her back to her usual state of quiescence. \"I'm only an old woman,\" said she; \"and here's Mr. Eames come back again.\"\n\n\"How d'ye do, Mrs. Roper? how d'ye do,--Amelia? how d'ye do, Miss Spruce?\" and he shook hands with them all.\n\n\"Oh, laws,\" said Mrs. Roper, \"you have given me such a start!\"\n\n\"Dear me, Mr. Eames; only think of your coming back in that way,\" said Amelia.\n\n\"Well, what way should I come back? You didn't hear me knock at the door, that's all. So Miss Spruce is really going to leave you?\"\n\n\"Isn't it dreadful, Mr. Eames? Nineteen years we've been together;--taking both houses together, Miss Spruce, we have, indeed.\" Miss Spruce, at this point, struggled very hard to convince John Eames that the period in question had in truth extended over only eighteen years, but Mrs. Roper was authoritative, and would not permit it. \"It's nineteen years if it's a day. No one ought to know dates if I don't, and there isn't one in the world understands her ways unless it's me. Haven't I been up to your bedroom every night, and with my own hand given you--\" But she stopped herself, and was too good a woman to declare before a young man what had been the nature of her nightly ministrations to her guest.\n\n\"I don't think you'll be so comfortable anywhere else, Miss Spruce,\" said Eames.\n\n\"Comfortable! of course she won't,\" said Amelia. \"But if I was mother I wouldn't have any more words about it.\"\n\n\"It isn't the money I'm thinking of, but the feeling of it,\" said Mrs. Roper. \"The house will be so lonely like. I shan't know myself; that I shan't. And now that things are all settled so pleasantly, and that the Lupexes must go on Tuesday-- I'll tell you what, Sally; I'll pay for the cab myself, and I'll start off to Dulwich by the omnibus to-morrow, and settle it all out of my own pocket. I will indeed. Come; there's the cab. Let me go down, and send him away.\"\n\n\"I'll do that,\" said Eames. \"It's only sixpence, off the stand,\" Mrs. Roper called to him as he left the room. But the cabman got a shilling, and John, as he returned, found Jemima in the act of carrying Miss Spruce's boxes back to her room. \"So much the better for poor Caudle,\" said he to himself. \"As he has gone into the trade it's well that he should have somebody that will pay him.\"\n\nMrs. Roper followed Miss Spruce up the stairs and Johnny was left with Amelia. \"He's written to you, I know,\" said she, with her face turned a little away from him. She was certainly very handsome, but there was a hard, cross, almost sullen look about her, which robbed her countenance of all its pleasantness. And yet she had no intention of being sullen with him.\n\n\"Yes,\" said John. \"He has told me how it's all going to be.\"\n\n\"Well?\" she said.\n\n\"Well?\" said he.\n\n\"Is that all you've got to say?\"\n\n\"I'll congratulate you, if you'll let me.\"\n\n\"Psha;--congratulations! I hate such humbug. If you've no feelings about it, I'm sure that I've none. Indeed I don't know what's the good of feelings. They never did me any good. Are you engaged to marry L. D.?\"\n\n\"No, I am not.\"\n\n\"And you've nothing else to say to me?\"\n\n\"Nothing,--except my hopes for your happiness. What else can I say? You are engaged to marry my friend Cradell, and I think it will be a happy match.\"\n\nShe turned away her face further from him, and the look of it became even more sullen. Could it be possible that at such a moment she still had a hope that he might come back to her?\n\n\"Good-by, Amelia,\" he said, putting out his hand to her.\n\n\"And this is to be the last of you in this house!\"\n\n\"Well, I don't know about that. I'll come and call upon you, if you'll let me, when you're married.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said, \"that there may be rows in the house, and noise, and jealousy,--as there have been with that wicked woman upstairs. Not if I know it, you won't! John Eames, I wish I'd never seen you. I wish we might have both fallen dead when we first met. I didn't think ever to have cared for a man as I have cared for you. It's all trash and nonsense and foolery; I know that. It's all very well for young ladies as can sit in drawing-rooms all their lives, but when a woman has her way to make in the world it's all foolery. And such a hard way too to make as mine is!\"\n\n\"But it won't be hard now.\"\n\n\"Won't it? But I think it will. I wish you would try it. Not that I'm going to complain. I never minded work, and as for company, I can put up with anybody. The world's not to be all dancing and fiddling for the likes of me. I know that well enough. But--\" and then she paused.\n\n\"What's the 'but' about, Amelia?\"\n\n\"It's like you to ask me; isn't it?\" To tell the truth he should not have asked her. \"Never mind. I'm not going to have any words with you. If you've been a knave I've been a fool, and that's worse.\"\n\n\"But I don't think I have been a knave.\"\n\n\"I've been both,\" said the girl; \"and both for nothing. After that you may go. I've told you what I am, and I'll leave you to name yourself. I didn't think it was in me to have been such a fool. It's that that frets me. Never mind, sir; it's all over now, and I wish you good-by.\"\n\nI do not think that there was the slightest reason why John should have again kissed her at parting, but he did so. She bore it, not struggling with him; but she took his caress with sullen endurance. \"It'll be the last,\" she said. \"Good-by, John Eames.\"\n\n\"Good-by, Amelia. Try to make him a good wife and then you'll be happy.\" She turned up her nose at this, assuming a look of unutterable scorn. But she said nothing further, and then he left the room. At the parlour door he met Mrs. Roper, and had his parting words with her.\n\n\"I am so glad you came,\" said she. \"It was just that word you said that made Miss Spruce stay. Her money is so ready, you know! And so you've had it all out with her about Cradell. She'll make him a good wife, she will indeed;--much better than you've been giving her credit for.\"\n\n\"I don't doubt she'll be a very good wife.\"\n\n\"You see, Mr. Eames, it's all over now, and we understand each other; don't we? It made me very unhappy when she was setting her cap at you; it did indeed. She is my own daughter, and I couldn't go against her;--could I? But I knew it wasn't in any way suiting. Laws, I know the difference. She's good enough for him any day of the week, Mr. Eames.\"\n\n\"That she is,--Saturdays or Sundays,\" said Johnny, not knowing exactly what he ought to say.\n\n\"So she is; and if he does his duty by her she won't go astray in hers by him. And as for you, Mr. Eames, I am sure I've always felt it an honour and a pleasure to have you in the house; and if ever you could use a good word in sending to me any of your young men, I'd do by them as a mother should; I would indeed. I know I've been to blame about those Lupexes, but haven't I suffered for it, Mr. Eames? And it was difficult to know at first; wasn't it? And as to you and Amelia, if you would send any of your young men to try, there couldn't be anything more of that kind, could there? I know it hasn't all been just as it should have been;--that is as regards you; but I should like to hear you say that you've found me honest before you went. I have tried to be honest, I have indeed.\"\n\nEames assured her that he was convinced of her honesty, and that he had never thought of impugning her character either in regard to those unfortunate people, the Lupexes, or in reference to other matters. \"He did not think,\" he said, \"that any young men would consult him as to their lodgings; but if he could be of any service to her, he would.\" Then he bade her good-by, and having bestowed half-a-sovereign on the faithful Jemima, he took a long farewell of Burton Crescent. Amelia had told him not to come and see her when she should be married, and he had resolved that he would take her at her word. So he walked off from the Crescent, not exactly shaking the dust from his feet, but resolving that he would know no more either of its dust or of its dirt. Dirt enough he had encountered there certainly, and he was now old enough to feel that the inmates of Mrs. Roper's house had not been those among whom a resting-place for his early years should judiciously have been sought. But he had come out of the fire comparatively unharmed, and I regret to say that he felt but little for the terrible scorchings to which his friend had been subjected and was about to subject himself. He was quite content to look at the matter exactly as it was looked at by Mrs. Roper. Amelia was good enough for Joseph Cradell--any day of the week. Poor Cradell, of whom in these pages after this notice no more will be heard! I cannot but think that a hard measure of justice was meted out to him, in proportion to the extent of his sins. More weak and foolish than our friend and hero he had been, but not to my knowledge more wicked. But it is to the vain and foolish that the punishments fall;--and to them they fall so thickly and constantly that the thinker is driven to think that vanity and folly are of all sins those which may be the least forgiven. As for Cradell I may declare that he did marry Amelia, that he did, with some pride, take the place of master of the house at the bottom of Mrs. Roper's table, and that he did make himself responsible for all Mrs. Roper's debts. Of his future fortunes there is not space to speak in these pages.\n\nGoing away from the Crescent Eames had himself driven to his office, which he reached just as the men were leaving it, at four o'clock. Cradell was gone, so that he did not see him on that afternoon; but he had an opportunity of shaking hands with Mr. Love, who treated him with all the smiling courtesy due to an official bigwig,--for a private secretary, if not absolutely a big-wig, is semi-big, and entitled to a certain amount of reverence;--and he passed Mr. Kissing in the passage, hurrying along as usual with a huge book under his arm. Mr. Kissing, hurried as he was, stopped his shuffling feet; but Eames only looked at him, hardly honouring him with the acknowledgment of a nod of his head. Mr. Kissing, however, was not offended; he knew that the private secretary of the First Commissioner had been the guest of an earl; and what more than a nod could be expected from him? After that John made his way into the august presence of Sir Raffle, and found that great man putting on his shoes in the presence of FitzHoward. FitzHoward blushed; but the shoes had not been touched by him, as he took occasion afterwards to inform John Eames.\n\nSir Raffle was all smiles and civility. \"Delighted to see you back, Eames: am, upon my word; though I and FitzHoward have got on capitally in your absence; haven't we, FitzHoward?\"\n\n\"Oh, yes,\" drawled FitzHoward. \"I haven't minded it for a time, just while Eames has been away.\"\n\n\"You're much too idle to keep at it, I know; but your bread will be buttered for you elsewhere, so it doesn't signify. My compliments to the duchess when you see her.\" Then FitzHoward went. \"And how's my dear old friend?\" asked Sir Raffle, as though of all men living Lord De Guest were the one for whom he had the strongest and the oldest love. And yet he must have known that John Eames knew as much about it as he did himself. But there are men who have the most lively gratification in calling lords and marquises their friends, though they know that nobody believes a word of what they say,--even though they know how great is the odium they incur, and how lasting is the ridicule which their vanity produces. It is a gentle insanity which prevails in the outer courts of every aristocracy; and as it brings with itself considerable annoyance and but a lukewarm pleasure, it should not be treated with too keen a severity.\n\n\"And how's my dear old friend?\" Eames assured him that his dear old friend was all right, that Lady Julia was all right, that the dear old place was all right. Sir Raffle now spoke as though the \"dear old place\" were quite well known to him. \"Was the game doing pretty well? Was there a promise of birds?\" Sir Raffle's anxiety was quite intense, and expressed with almost familiar affection. \"And, by-the-by, Eames, where are you living at present?\"\n\n\"Well, I'm not settled. I'm at the Great Western Railway Hotel at this moment.\"\n\n\"Capital house, very; only it's expensive if you stay there the whole season.\" Johnny had no idea of remaining there beyond one night, but he said nothing as to this. \"By-the-by, you might as well come and dine with us to-morrow. Lady Buffle is most anxious to know you. There'll be one or two with us. I did ask my friend Dumbello, but there's some nonsense going on in the House, and he thinks that he can't get away.\" Johnny was more gracious than Lord Dumbello, and accepted the invitation. \"I wonder what Lady Buffle will be like?\" he said to himself, as he walked away from the office.\n\nHe had turned into the Great Western Hotel, not as yet knowing where to look for a home; and there we will leave him, eating his solitary mutton-chop at one of those tables which are so comfortable to the eye, but which are so comfortless in reality. I speak not now with reference to the excellent establishment which has been named, but to the nature of such tables in general. A solitary mutton-chop in an hotel coffee-room is not a banquet to be envied by any god; and if the mutton-chop be converted into soup, fish, little dishes, big dishes, and the rest, the matter becomes worse and not better. What comfort are you to have, seated alone on that horsehair chair, staring into the room and watching the waiters as they whisk about their towels? No one but an Englishman has ever yet thought of subjecting himself to such a position as that! But here we will leave John Eames, and in doing so I must be allowed to declare that only now, at this moment, has he entered on his manhood. Hitherto he has been a hobbledehoy,--a calf, as it were, who had carried his calfishness later into life than is common with calves; but who did not, perhaps, on that account, give promise of making a worse ox than the rest of them. His life hitherto, as recorded in these pages, had afforded him no brilliant success, had hardly qualified him for the role of hero which he has been made to play. I feel that I have been in fault in giving such prominence to a hobbledehoy, and that I should have told my story better had I brought Mr. Crosbie more conspicuously forward on my canvas. He at any rate has gotten to himself a wife--as a hero always should do; whereas I must leave my poor friend Johnny without any matrimonial prospects.\n\nIt was thus that he thought of himself as he sat moping over his solitary table in the hotel coffee-room. He acknowledged to himself that he had not hitherto been a man; but at the same time he made some resolution which, I trust, may assist him in commencing his manhood from this date.\n\n# CHAPTER LX. CONCLUSION.\n\nIt was early in June that Lily went up to her uncle at the Great House, pleading for Hopkins,--pleading that to Hopkins might be restored all the privileges of head gardener at the Great House. There was some absurdity in this, seeing that he had never really relinquished his privileges; but the manner of the quarrel had been in this wise.\n\nThere was in those days, and had been for years, a vexed question between Hopkins and Jolliffe the bailiff on the matter of--stable manure. Hopkins had pretended to the right of taking what he required from the farmyard, without asking leave of any one. Jolliffe in return had hinted, that if this were so, Hopkins would take it all. \"But I can't eat it,\" Hopkins had said. Jolliffe merely grunted, signifying by the grunt, as Hopkins thought, that though a gardener couldn't eat a mountain of manure fifty feet long and fifteen high--couldn't eat in the body,--he might convert it into things edible for his own personal use. And so there had been a great feud. The unfortunate squire had of course been called on to arbitrate, and having postponed his decision by every contrivance possible to him, had at last been driven by Jolliffe to declare that Hopkins should take nothing that was not assigned to him. Hopkins, when the decision was made known to him by his master, bit his old lips, and turned round upon his old heel, speechless. \"You'll find it's so at all other places,\" said the squire, apologetically. \"Other places!\" sneered Hopkins. Where would he find other gardeners like himself? It is hardly necessary to declare that from that moment he resolved that he would abide by no such order. Jolliffe on the next morning informed the squire that the order had been broken, and the squire fretted and fumed, wishing that Jolliffe were well buried under the mountain in question. \"If they all is to do as they like,\" said Jolliffe, \"then nobody won't care for nobody.\" The squire understood that an order if given must be obeyed, and therefore, with many inner groanings of the spirit, resolved that war must be waged against Hopkins.\n\nOn the following morning he found the old man himself wheeling a huge barrow of manure round from the yard into the kitchen-garden. Now, on ordinary occasions, Hopkins was not required to do with his own hands work of that description. He had a man under him who hewed wood, and carried water, and wheeled barrows,--one man always, and often two. The squire knew when he saw him that he was sinning, and bade him stop upon his road.\n\n\"Hopkins,\" he said, \"why didn't you ask for what you wanted, before you took it?\" The old man put down the barrow on the ground, looked up in his master's face, spat into his hands, and then again resumed his barrow. \"Hopkins, that won't do,\" said the squire. \"Stop where you are.\"\n\n\"What won't do?\" said Hopkins, still holding the barrow from the ground, but not as yet progressing.\n\n\"Put it down, Hopkins,\" and Hopkins did put it down. \"Don't you know that you are flatly disobeying my orders?\"\n\n\"Squire, I've been here about this place going on nigh seventy years.\"\n\n\"If you've been going on a hundred and seventy it wouldn't do that there should be more than one master. I'm the master here, and I intend to be so to the end. Take that manure back into the yard.\"\n\n\"Back into the yard?\" said Hopkins, very slowly.\n\n\"Yes; back into the yard.\"\n\n\"What,--afore all their faces?\"\n\n\"Yes; you've disobeyed me before all their faces?\"\n\nHopkins paused a moment, looking away from the squire, and shaking his head as though he had need of deep thought, but by the aid of deep thought had come at last to a right conclusion. Then he resumed the barrow, and putting himself almost into a trot, carried away his prize into the kitchen-garden. At the pace which he went it would have been beyond the squire's power to stop him, nor would Mr. Dale have wished to come to a personal encounter with his servant. But he called after the man in dire wrath that if he were not obeyed the disobedient servant should rue the consequences for ever. Hopkins, equal to the occasion, shook his head as he trotted on, deposited his load at the foot of the cucumber-frames, and then at once returning to his master, tendered to him the key of the greenhouse.\n\n\"Master,\" said Hopkins, speaking as best he could with his scanty breath, \"there it is;--there's the key; of course I don't want no warning, and doesn't care about my week's wages. I'll be out of the cottage afore night, and as for the work'us, I suppose they'll let me in at once, if your honour'll give 'em a line.\"\n\nNow as Hopkins was well known by the squire to be the owner of three or four hundred pounds, the hint about the workhouse must be allowed to have been melodramatic.\n\n\"Don't be a fool,\" said the squire, almost gnashing his teeth.\n\n\"I know I've been a fool,\" said Hopkins, \"about that 'ere doong; my feelings has been too much for me. When a man's feelings has been too much for him, he'd better just take hisself off, and lie in the work'us till he dies.\" And then he again tendered the key. But the squire did not take the key, and so Hopkins went on. \"I s'pose I'd better just see to the lights and the like of that, till you've suited yourself, Mr. Dale. It 'ud be a pity all them grapes should go off, and they, as you may say, all one as fit for the table. It's a long way the best crop I ever see on 'em. I've been that careful with 'em that I haven't had a natural night's rest, not since February. There ain't nobody about this place as understands grapes, nor yet anywhere nigh that could be got at. My lord's head man is wery ignorant; but even if he knew ever so, of course he couldn't come here. I suppose I'd better keep the key till you're suited, Mr. Dale.\"\n\nThen for a fortnight there was an interregnum in the gardens, terrible in the annals of Allington. Hopkins lived in his cottage indeed, and looked most sedulously after the grapes. In looking after the grapes, too, he took the greenhouses under his care; but he would have nothing to do with the outer gardens, took no wages, returning the amount sent to him back to the squire, and insisted with everybody that he had been dismissed. He went about with some terrible horticultural implement always in his hand, with which it was said that he intended to attack Jolliffe; but Jolliffe prudently kept out of his way.\n\nAs soon as it had been resolved by Mrs. Dale and Lily that the flitting from the Small House at Allington was not to be accomplished, Lily communicated the fact to Hopkins.\n\n\"Miss,\" said he, \"when I said them few words to you and your mamma, I knew that you would listen to reason.\"\n\nThis was no more than Lily had expected; that Hopkins should claim the honour of having prevailed by his arguments was a matter of course.\n\n\"Yes,\" said Lily; \"we've made up our minds to stay. Uncle wishes it.\"\n\n\"Wishes it! Laws, miss; it ain't only wishes. And we all wishes it. Why, now, look at the reason of the thing. Here's this here house--\"\n\n\"But, Hopkins, it's decided. We're going to stay. What I want to know is this; can you come at once and help me to unpack?\"\n\n\"What! this very evening, as is--\"\n\n\"Yes, now; we want to have the things about again before they come back from Guestwick.\"\n\nHopkins scratched his head and hesitated, not wishing to yield to any proposition that could be considered as childish; but he gave way at last, feeling that the work itself was a good work. Mrs. Dale also assented, laughing at Lily for her folly as she did so, and in this way the things were unpacked very quickly, and the alliance between Lily and Hopkins became, for the time, very close. This work of unpacking and resettling was not yet over, when the battle of the manure broke out, and therefore it was that Hopkins, when his feelings had become altogether too much for him \"about the doong,\" came at last to Lily, and laying down at her feet all the weight and all the glory of his sixty odd years of life, implored her to make matters straight for him. \"It's been a killing me, miss, so it has; to see the way they've been a cutting that 'sparagus. It ain't cutting at all. It's just hocking it up;--what is fit, and what isn't, all together. And they've been a-putting the plants in where I didn't mean 'em, though they know'd I didn't mean 'em. I've stood by, miss, and said never a word. I'd a died sooner. But, Miss Lily, what my sufferings have been, 'cause of my feelings getting the better of me about that--you know, miss--nobody will ever tell;--nobody--nobody--nobody.\" Then Hopkins turned away and wept.\n\n\"Uncle,\" said Lily, creeping close up against his chair, \"I want to ask you a great favour.\"\n\n\"A great favour. Well, I don't think I shall refuse you anything at present. It isn't to ask another earl to the house,--is it?\"\n\n\"Another earl!\" said Lily.\n\n\"Yes; haven't you heard? Miss Bell has been here this morning, insisting that I should have over Lord De Guest and his sister for the marriage. It seems that there was some scheming between Bell and Lady Julia.\"\n\n\"Of course you'll ask them.\"\n\n\"Of course I must. I've no way out of it. It'll be all very well for Bell, who'll be off to Wales with her lover; but what am I to do with the earl and Lady Julia, when they're gone? Will you come and help me?\"\n\nIn answer to this, Lily of course promised that she would come and help. \"Indeed,\" said she, \"I thought we were all asked up for the day. And now for my favour. Uncle, you must forgive poor Hopkins.\"\n\n\"Forgive a fiddlestick!\" said the squire.\n\n\"No, but you must. You can't think how unhappy he is.\"\n\n\"How can I forgive a man who won't forgive me. He goes prowling about the place doing nothing; and he sends me back his wages, and he looks as though he were going to murder some one; and all because he wouldn't do as he was told. How am I to forgive such a man as that?\"\n\n\"But, uncle, why not?\"\n\n\"It would be his forgiving me. He knows very well that he may come back whenever he pleases; and, indeed, for the matter of that he has never gone away.\"\n\n\"But he is so very unhappy.\"\n\n\"What can I do to make him happier?\"\n\n\"Just go down to his cottage and tell him that you forgive him.\"\n\n\"Then he'll argue with me.\"\n\n\"No; I don't think he will. He is too much down in the world for arguing now.\"\n\n\"Ah! you don't know him as I do. All the misfortunes in the world wouldn't stop that man's conceit. Of course I'll go if you ask me, but it seems to me that I'm made to knock under to everybody. I hear a great deal about other people's feelings, but I don't know that mine are very much thought of.\" He was not altogether in a happy mood, and Lily almost regretted that she had persevered; but she did succeed in carrying him off across the garden to the cottage, and as they went together she promised him that she would think of him always,--always. The scene with Hopkins cannot be described now, as it would take too many of our few remaining pages. It resulted, I am afraid I must confess, in nothing more triumphant to the squire than a treaty of mutual forgiveness. Hopkins acknowledged, with much self-reproach, that his feelings had been too many for him; but then, look at his provocation! He could not keep his tongue from that matter, and certainly said as much in his own defence as he did in confession of his sins. The substantial triumph was altogether his, for nobody again ever dared to interfere with his operations in the farmyard. He showed his submission to his master mainly by consenting to receive his wages for the two weeks which he had passed in idleness.\n\nOwing to this little accident, Lily was not so much oppressed by Hopkins as she had expected to be in that matter of their altered plans; but this salvation did not extend to Mrs. Hearn, to Mrs. Crump, or, above all, to Mrs. Boyce. They, all of them, took an interest more or less strong in the Hopkins controversy; but their interest in the occupation of the Small House was much stronger, and it was found useless to put Mrs. Hearn off with the gardener's persistent refusal of his wages, when she was big with inquiry whether the house was to be painted inside, as well as out. \"Ah,\" said she, \"I think I'll go and look at lodgings at Guestwick myself, and pack up some of my beds.\" Lily made no answer to this, feeling that it was a part of that punishment which she had expected. \"Dear, dear,\" said Mrs. Crump to the two girls; \"well, to be sure, we should 'a been 'lone without 'ee, and mayhap we might 'a got worse in your place; but why did 'ee go and fasten up all your things in them big boxes, just to unfasten 'em all again?\"\n\n\"We changed our minds, Mrs. Crump,\" said Bell, with some severity.\n\n\"Yees, I know ye changed your mindses. Well, it's all right for loiks o' ye, no doubt; but if we changes our mindses, we hears of it.\"\n\n\"So, it seems, do we!\" said Lily. \"But never mind, Mrs. Crump. Do you send us our letters up early, and then we won't quarrel.\"\n\n\"Oh, letters! Drat them for letters. I wish there weren't no sich things. There was a man here yesterday with his imperence. I don't know where he come from,--down from Lun'on, I b'leeve: and this was wrong, and that was wrong, and everything was wrong; and then he said he'd have me discharged the sarvice.\"\n\n\"Dear me, Mrs. Crump; that wouldn't do at all.\"\n\n\"Discharged the sarvice! Tuppence farden a day. So I told 'un to discharge hisself, and take all the old bundles and things away upon his shoulders. Letters indeed! What business have they with post-missusses, if they cannot pay 'em better nor tuppence farden a day?\" And in this way, under the shelter of Mrs. Crump's storm of wrath against the inspector who had visited her, Lily and Bell escaped much that would have fallen upon their own heads; but Mrs. Boyce still remained. I may here add, in order that Mrs. Crump's history may be carried on to the farthest possible point, that she was not \"discharged the sarvice,\" and that she still receives her twopence farthing a day from the Crown. \"That's a bitter old lady,\" said the inspector to the man who was driving him. \"Yes, sir; they all says the same about she. There ain't none of 'em get much change out of Mrs. Crump.\"\n\nBell and Lily went together also to Mrs. Boyce's. \"If she makes herself very disagreeable, I shall insist upon talking of your marriage,\" said Lily.\n\n\"I've not the slightest objection,\" said Bell; \"only I don't know what there can be to say about it. Marrying the doctor is such a very commonplace sort of thing.\"\n\n\"Not a bit more commonplace than marrying the parson,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Oh, yes, it is. Parsons' marriages are often very grand affairs. They come in among county people. That's their luck in life. Doctors never do; nor lawyers. I don't think lawyers ever get married in the country. They're supposed to do it up in London. But a country doctor's wedding is not a thing to be talked about much.\"\n\nMrs. Boyce probably agreed in this view of the matter, seeing that she did not choose the coming marriage as her first subject of conversation. As soon as the two girls were seated she flew away immediately to the house, and began to express her very great surprise,--her surprise and her joy also,--at the sudden change which had been made in their plans. \"It is so much nicer, you know,\" said she, \"that things should be pleasant among relatives.\"\n\n\"Things always have been tolerably pleasant with us,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Oh, yes; I'm sure of that. I've always said it was quite a pleasure to see you and your uncle together. And when we heard about your all having to leave--\"\n\n\"But we didn't have to leave, Mrs. Boyce. We were going to leave because we thought mamma would be more comfortable in Guestwick; and now we're not going to leave, because we've all 'changed our mindses,' as Mrs. Crump calls it.\"\n\n\"And is it true the house is going to be painted?\" asked Mrs. Boyce.\n\n\"I believe it is true,\" said Lily.\n\n\"Inside and out?\"\n\n\"It must be done some day,\" said Bell.\n\n\"Yes, to be sure; but I must say it is generous of the squire. There's such a deal of wood-work about your house. I know I wish the Ecclesiastical Commissioners would paint ours; but nobody ever does anything for the clergy. I'm sure I'm delighted you're going to stay. As I said to Mr. Boyce, what should we ever have done without you? I believe the squire had made up his mind that he would not let the place.\"\n\n\"I don't think he ever has let it.\"\n\n\"And if there was nobody in it, it would all go to rack and ruin; wouldn't it? Had your mamma to pay anything for the lodgings she engaged at Guestwick?\"\n\n\"Upon my word, I don't know. Bell can tell you better about that than I, as Dr. Crofts settled it. I suppose Dr. Crofts tells her everything.\" And so the conversation was changed, and Mrs. Boyce was made to understand that whatever further mystery there might be, it would not be unravelled on that occasion.\n\nIt was settled that Dr. Crofts and Bell should be married about the middle of June, and the squire determined to give what grace he could to the ceremony by opening his own house on the occasion. Lord De Guest and Lady Julia were invited by special arrangement between her ladyship and Bell, as has been before explained. The colonel also with Lady Fanny came up from Torquay on the occasion, this being the first visit made by the colonel to his paternal roof for many years. Bernard did not accompany his father. He had not yet gone abroad, but there were circumstances which made him feel that he would not find himself comfortable at the wedding. The service was performed by Mr. Boyce, assisted, as the County Chronicle very fully remarked, by the Reverend John Joseph Jones, M.A., late of Jesus College, Cambridge, and curate of St. Peter's, Northgate, Guestwick; the fault of which little advertisement was this,--that as none of the readers of the paper had patience to get beyond the Reverend John Joseph Jones, the fact of Bell's marriage with Dr. Crofts was not disseminated as widely as might have been wished.\n\nThe marriage went off very nicely. The squire was upon his very best behaviour, and welcomed his guests as though he really enjoyed their presence there in his halls. Hopkins, who was quite aware that he had been triumphant, decorated the old rooms with mingled flowers and greenery with an assiduous care which pleased the two girls mightily. And during this work of wreathing and decking there was one little morsel of feeling displayed which may as well be told in these last lines. Lily had been encouraging the old man while Bell for a moment had been absent.\n\n\"I wish it had been for thee, my darling!\" he said; \"I wish it had been for thee!\"\n\n\"It is much better as it is, Hopkins,\" she answered, solemnly.\n\n\"Not with him, though,\" he went on, \"not with him. I wouldn't 'a hung a bough for him. But with t'other one.\"\n\nLily said no word further. She knew that the man was expressing the wishes of all around her. She said no word further, and then Bell returned to them.\n\nBut no one at the wedding was so gay as Lily,--so gay, so bright, and so wedding-like. She flirted with the old earl till he declared that he would marry her himself. No one seeing her that evening, and knowing nothing of her immediate history, would have imagined that she herself had been cruelly jilted some six or eight months ago. And those who did know her could not imagine that what she then suffered had hit her so hard, that no recovery seemed possible for her. But though no recovery, as she herself believed, was possible for her--though she was as a man whose right arm had been taken from him in the battle, still all the world had not gone with that right arm. The bullet which had maimed her sorely had not touched her life, and she scorned to go about the world complaining either by word or look of the injury she had received. \"Wives when they have lost their husbands still eat and laugh,\" she said to herself, \"and he is not dead like that.\" So she resolved that she would be happy, and I here declare that she not only seemed to carry out her resolution, but that she did carry it out in very truth. \"You're a dear good man, and I know you'll be good to her,\" she said to Crofts just as he was about to start with his bride.\n\n\"I'll try, at any rate,\" he answered.\n\n\"And I shall expect you to be good to me too. Remember you have married the whole family; and, sir, you mustn't believe a word of what that bad man says in his novels about mothers-in-law. He has done a great deal of harm, and shut half the ladies in England out of their daughters' houses.\"\n\n\"He shan't shut Mrs. Dale out of mine.\"\n\n\"Remember he doesn't. Now, good-by.\" So the bride and bridegroom went off, and Lily was left to flirt with Lord De Guest.\n\nOf whom else is it necessary that a word or two should be said before I allow the weary pen to fall from my hand? The squire, after much inward struggling on the subject, had acknowledged to himself that his sister-in-law had not received from him that kindness which she had deserved. He had acknowledged this, purporting to do his best to amend his past errors; and I think I may say that his efforts in that line would not be received ungraciously by Mrs. Dale. I am inclined therefore to think that life at Allington, both at the Great House and at the Small, would soon become pleasanter than it used to be in former days. Lily soon got the Balmoral boots, or, at least, soon learned that the power of getting them as she pleased had devolved upon her from her uncle's gift; so that she talked even of buying the squirrel's cage; but I am not aware that her extravagance led her as far as that.\n\nLord De Courcy we left suffering dreadfully from gout and ill-temper at Courcy Castle. Yes, indeed! To him in his latter days life did not seem to offer much that was comfortable. His wife had now gone from him, and declared positively to her son-in-law that no earthly consideration should ever induce her to go back again;--\"not if I were to starve!\" she said. By which she intended to signify that she would be firm in her resolve, even though she should thereby lose her carriage and horses. Poor Mr. Gazebee went down to Courcy, and had a dreadful interview with the earl; but matters were at last arranged, and her ladyship remained at Baden-Baden in a state of semi-starvation. That is to say, she had but one horse to her carriage.\n\nAs regards Crosbie, I am inclined to believe that he did again recover his power at his office. He was Mr. Butterwell's master, and the master also of Mr. Optimist, and the major. He knew his business, and could do it, which was more, perhaps, than might fairly be said of any of the other three. Under such circumstances he was sure to get in his hand, and lead again. But elsewhere his star did not recover its ascendancy. He dined at his club almost daily, and there were those with whom he habitually formed some little circle. But he was not the Crosbie of former days,--the Crosbie known in Belgravia and in St. James's Street. He had taken his little vessel bravely out into the deep waters, and had sailed her well while fortune stuck close to him. But he had forgotten his nautical rules, and success had made him idle. His plummet and lead had not been used, and he had kept no look-out ahead. Therefore the first rock he met shivered his bark to pieces. His wife, the Lady Alexandrina, is to be seen in the one-horse carriage with her mother at Baden-Baden.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-4599"]="# 제1장. 앨링턴의 지주\n\n물론 앨링턴에는 큰 집이 있었다. 그렇지 않고서야 어떻게 작은 집이 있을 수 있겠는가? 우리 이야기는 그 제목이 일러 주는 대로 두 집 가운데 덜 위엄 있는 거처에 살던 이들과 가장 가까운 관계를 맺을 것이다. 그러나 더 위엄 있는 쪽과도 밀접한 관계를 맺을 터이니, 우선은 내가 큰 집과 그 주인에 대해 몇 마디 해 두는 편이 좋겠다.\n\n앨링턴의 지주들은, 오늘날과 같은 지주라는 것이 잉글랜드에 처음 알려진 이래로 줄곧 앨링턴의 지주였다. 아버지에게서 아들에게로, 삼촌에게서 조카에게로, 그리고 한 번은 육촌에게서 육촌에게로, 그 홀은 데일 집안 안에서 내려왔다. 토지는 온전한 채로 남아 값이 오르기만 했을 뿐 그 수가 줄지 않았는데, 한정상속으로 묶인 것도 아니었고 대단한 신중함이나 지혜가 지켜 준 것도 아니었다. 앨링턴 데일 가의 영지는 수백 년 동안 앨링턴 교구와 경계를 같이해 왔다. 그리고 내가 말했듯이 이 지주 일족이 인간을 넘어서는 분별을 지녔던 것도 아니고, 살아가는 길에서 아주 뚜렷한 원칙의 인도를 받았던 것도 아마 아니겠지만, 그럼에도 그들에게는 신성한 법을 지키는 마음이 어찌나 깊었던지 재산의 단 한 에이커도 당대 지주의 손에서 떨어져 나간 적이 없었다. 영토를 늘리려는 헛된 시도가 몇 번 있기는 했으니, 크리스토퍼 데일의 아버지 킷 데일이 실제로 그런 일을 했다. 크리스토퍼 데일은 우리 극의 인물들이 소개될 때 우리의 앨링턴 지주로 등장할 사람이다. 돈 있는 여자와 결혼했던 늙은 킷 데일은 외곽의 농장들을 사들였다. 여기 한 뙈기 저기 한 뙈기 하는 식이었는데, 그러면서 그는 정치적 영향력이니 좋았던 옛 토리당의 대의니 하는 말을 많이 했다. 그러나 그 농장들과 땅뙈기들은 우리 시대가 오기 전에 이미 다시 나가 버렸다. 거기에는 아무런 신앙도 결부되어 있지 않았던 것이다. 제십구용기병연대의 소령 자리 문제로 늙은 킷이 몰리게 되었을 때 — 그 정예 연대에서 그의 둘째 아들은 제법 출셋길을 닦고 있었다 — 그는 지키기보다 파는 편이 쉽다는 것을 알았다. 파는 물건이 데일 가의 세습 재산이 아니라 제 것이었으니 그럴 만도 했다. 그가 죽자 그렇게 사들인 것의 나머지도 없어졌다. 집안일을 정리하자면 마무리가 필요했고, 크리스토퍼 데일에게는 당장 쓸 현금이 필요했다. 외곽의 농장들은 그렇게 새로 사들인 땅이 전에도 그랬듯 훌훌 날아가 버렸다. 그러나 데일 가의 오래된 세습 재산은 여태 그래 왔듯 손 하나 대지 않은 채 남았다.\n\n그것은 그들 사이에서 하나의 신앙이었다. 그리고 그 예배가 한 번도 거르는 일 없이 이어졌음을, 화덕의 성화가 한 번도 꺼진 적이 없음을 생각하면, 데일 가 사람들이 높은 원칙 없이 제 길을 걸었다고 나는 말하지 말았어야 했다. 이 신앙에는 그들 모두가 매달렸고, 새 상속인은 언제나 자기가 그때 이미 짊어지고 있던 것 말고는 다른 부담 없이 제 영지에 들어섰다. 그런데도 한정상속은 없었다. 한정상속이라는 발상은 데일 가 특유의 기질과 맞지 않았다. 데일 가의 신앙에는 지주마다 앨링턴의 토지를 탕진할 권능을 가질 것, 그러면서도 탕진하기를 삼갈 것이 필요했다. 나는 어느 집에서 저녁을 먹은 일을 기억한다. 그 집의 온 영광과 재산이 유리잔 하나의 무사함에 달려 있었다. 그 이야기는 우리 모두가 안다. 에덴홀의 행운이 깨어지면 그 집안의 운명도 끝장이라는 것이다. 그런데도 그 집의 모든 손님이 그러했듯 나 또한 그 운명의 잔으로 마시라는 권을 받았다. 자물쇠와 열쇠와 솜을 댄 궤짝으로 제 운명을 지키는 것은 주인의 기사다운 마음에 흡족하지 않았으리라. 앨링턴의 데일 가도 그러했다. 그들에게 한정상속이란 자물쇠요 열쇠요 솜을 댄 궤짝이었을 텐데, 집안의 오랜 기사도가 그런 보호물을 쓰는 것을 그들에게 허락하지 않았다.\n\n나는 이 집안의 재주와 행적을 얼마간 대수롭지 않게 말했거니와, 실제로 그들의 재주는 적었고 행적은 보잘것없었다. 앨링턴에서 앨링턴의 데일 어른은 언제나 왕으로 통했다. 이웃한 장터 마을 게스트윅에서 그는 대단한 인물이었다. 토요일이면 장터에 서서 보리와 소에 대해, 보통 자기보다 그것들을 더 잘 아는 사람들 앞에서 법을 늘어놓는 모습을 자주 볼 수 있었다. 순회재판이 열리는 고을 해머샴에서도 그는 대체로 얼마간 평판이 있었으니, 주의 대배심원을 늘 맡았고 제 빚을 갚고 사는 사람이었기 때문이다. 그러나 해머샴에서조차 데일 가의 영광은 대개의 시기에 이미 빛이 바래기 시작한 터였다. 그들이 주 안에서 널리 두드러진 적이 좀처럼 없었던 데다, 대배심원실에서 법률 지식으로 큰 명성을 얻은 일도 없었기 때문이다. 해머샴 너머로는 그들의 이름이 퍼지지 않았다.\n\n그들은 대체로 같은 틀에서 나온 사람들이었고, 저마다 아버지에게서 같은 미덕과 같은 악덕을 물려받았다. 세상의 새 풍조가 눈에 보이지 않는 자력으로 그때그때의 신진 데일을 조금씩 끌어가지만 않았다면, 저마다 제 아버지가 살았던 그대로 살았을 사람들이었다. 그 힘이 어느 경우에도 그를 당대의 정신에까지 끌어올릴 만큼 움직인 것은 아니었으나, 아버지가 밟았던 자리보다는 한 발 앞선 선까지 그를 끌고 나가기는 했다. 그들은 고집 센 사람들이었다. 스스로를 크게 믿었고, 저희 나름의 정의 관념에 비추어서는 공정했으며, 소작인들에게 모질었으나 정작 소작인들 자신은 그것을 모질다고 여기지 않았으니 지켜지는 규칙이 언제나 앨링턴 영지의 규칙이었기 때문이다. 아내와 자식들에게 오만했으되 그 오만함에는 한도가 있어서, 지아비의 지붕 아래에서 달아난 데일 부인은 하나도 없었고 아버지와 아들 사이에 요란한 추문이 인 적도 없었다. 돈에 관해서는 깐깐하여 많이 받고 적게 주기를 바랐으나 그렇다고 인색하다는 말을 듣지는 않았으니, 제 빚을 갚고 살았고 교구 자선과 주 자선에 돈을 냈기 때문이다. 그들은 언제나 교회를 한결같이 받쳐 왔고, 목사 임명권이 딸린 케임브리지 킹스 칼리지에서 때때로 보내오는 새 교구 목사들을 제 교구에 정중히 맞아들였다. 그럼에도 데일 가는 늘 성직자를 상대로 선포되지 않은 전쟁을 벌였으므로, 속가와 성직자 집안 사이가 모든 면에서 유쾌했던 적은 좀처럼 없었다.\n\n앨링턴의 데일 가는 아득한 옛날부터 그러했고, 우리 시대의 크리스토퍼 데일도 젊은 시절에 두 번의 사고를 겪지만 않았다면 모든 면에서 그러했을 것이다. 그는 한 여인과 사랑에 빠졌는데 그 여인이 한사코 그의 손을 마다했고, 그 때문에 그는 홀몸으로 남았다. 그것이 첫 번째 사고였다. 두 번째는 아버지의 재산이 넉넉하리라 짐작한 데서 그에게 닥쳤다. 그는 재산을 물려받으며 자기가 앨링턴의 다른 데일들보다 부자라고 생각했고, 그래서 제 주를 대표해 의회에 앉겠다는 생각을 품었다. 그 명예를 얻기 위해 그는 해머샴과 게스트윅 사람들의 말에 넘어가 집안 대대의 정견을 버렸고, 스스로를 자유당원이라 선언하기에 이르렀다. 그는 투표소까지 간 적이 없고, 실은 그 의석에 실제로 출마한 적조차 없다. 그러나 자유주의 정치가로 나섰다가 실패했다. 그리고 크리스토퍼 데일이 마음속으로는 그 어느 조상 못지않게 철저한 보수주의자임을 주변 사람들이 다 알고 있었음에도, 이 사고는 그를 정치 이야기에 대해 시큰둥하고 말 없는 사람으로 만들었으며 이웃 지주들에게서 그를 얼마간 멀어지게 했다.\n\n다른 점에서 보면 우리의 크리스토퍼 데일은 굳이 따지자면 집안의 평균보다 나은 사람이었다. 사랑하는 이는 애틋하게 사랑했다. 미워하는 이라도 정의의 한도를 넘어서까지 함부로 대하지는 않았다. 자잘한 돈 문제에는 인색했으나, 앞으로 보게 되겠지만 어떤 집안일에서는 크게 너그러울 줄도 알았다. 그는 제가 아는 빛에 따라 제 도리를 다하려 애썼고, 큰 기대에 부풀었던 젊은 시절에 몸에 밴 개인적인 탐닉에서 스스로를 떼어 놓는 데 성공했다. 그리고 보답받지 못한 그 사랑에서 그는 시종 한결같았다. 그 딱딱하고 메마르고 유쾌하지 못한 방식으로나마 그는 그 여인을 사랑했으며, 마침내 그 여인이 제 사랑을 받아 주지 않으리라는 것을 알게 되었을 때 그는 마음을 다른 사람에게 옮길 수가 없었다. 이 일은 바로 그의 아버지가 죽던 무렵에 일어났고, 그는 정치로 스스로를 달래 보려 했다가 어떤 운명을 맞았는지는 우리가 이미 본 대로다. 우리의 크리스토퍼 데일은 한결같고 올곧으며 결코 거짓되지 않은 사람이었다. 정신의 자질로 보면 얄팍하고 빈약하여 온전한 사람의 그 충만함이라는 것을 도무지 이해하지 못했고, 제 머리 위에 있는 것을 알아보는 눈의 힘은 몹시 좁았다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 존중받을 만했으니, 도리의 길을 알아보았고 그 길로 걸으려 애썼기 때문이다. 게다가 우리의 크리스토퍼 데일 씨는 신사였다.\n\n성품으로 보면 그러한 사람이 앨링턴의 지주, 큰 집의 유일한 상주자였다. 생김새로 보면 그는 수수하고 메마른 남자로, 희끗한 머리는 짧았고 희끗한 눈썹은 두툼했다. 수염은 거의 없어서 될 수 있는 대로 작은 잿빛 구레나룻을 길렀는데 귀 끝 아래로는 좀처럼 내려오지 않았다. 눈은 날카롭고 표정이 풍부했으며, 코는 곧고 잘생겼고 턱도 그러했다. 그러나 그 얼굴의 고귀함은 입술이 얇은 옹색한 입 때문에 망가졌다. 높고 좁은 이마는 데일 씨를 바보로 여기지 못하게 하는 한편, 그를 크게 뛰어난 사람이나 폭넓은 그릇으로 여기지도 못하게 했다. 키는 오 피트 십 인치쯤 되었고, 우리 이야기가 벌어지는 무렵에는 예순보다 일흔에 더 가까웠다. 그러나 세월은 그를 아주 가볍게 다루어서 그에게는 늙은 티가 별로 없었다. 몸으로 보면 그러한 사람이 앨링턴의 지주이자 연 삼천 파운드가량의 소유자였던 크리스토퍼 데일 님이었으니, 그 수입은 모두 그 교구의 토지에서 나왔다.\n\n이제 앨링턴의 큰 집에 대해 이야기하겠다. 따지고 보면 그리 크지 않았고, 우리네 오래된 지주들 대부분의 거처를 빛내 주는 그 절묘하게 고귀한 사냥터 부속물에 둘러싸인 것도 아니었다. 그러나 집 자체는 무척 우아했다. 스튜어트 왕조 초기에, 우리가 튜더 양식이라 이름 붙이는 그 건축 양식으로 지어진 것이었다. 정면에는 뾰족한 지붕이 셋, 그러니까 박공이라 불러야 마땅할 것이 셋 보였고, 박공과 박공 사이마다 가늘고 높은 굴뚝이 서 있어서 그 두 굴뚝이 앞서 말한 세 봉우리보다 조금 더 높이 솟아 있었다. 나는 이 집의 아름다움이 그 두 굴뚝에 크게 기대고 있었다고 생각한다. 그 굴뚝들과, 집 정면을 빽빽이 채운 멀리언 창들에 말이다. 튀어나온 현관이 딸린 문은 결코 집 한가운데 있지 않았다. 들어서면 오른편에는 창이 하나뿐인데 왼편에는 셋이 있었다. 그리고 그 위로는 창 다섯이 한 줄로 늘어서, 그중 하나가 현관 위에 자리를 잡았다. 튼튼한 돌 멀리언과 돌 트랜섬이 아래쪽보다 훨씬 위쪽에 가까운 지점에서 좌우로 가로지르는 그 아름다운 옛 튜더 창을 우리는 다 안다. 여태 고안된 온갖 창 가운데 그것이 가장 어여쁘다. 그리고 여기 앨링턴에서는 그 창들이 모양이 고르지 않다는 사실 덕분에 아름다움이 더해졌다고 나는 생각한다. 그 창들 가운데 어떤 것은 옆으로 길었고 어떤 것은 위로 높았다. 문 오른편의 것과 집 반대편 끝의 것은 앞엣것에 속했다. 그러나 나머지는 획일성을 아랑곳하지 않고 끼워 넣어져서 여기에 긴 창 하나 저기에 높은 창 하나 하는 식이었는데, 전체 효과는 도저히 더 낫게 하기 어려웠다. 그리고 그 위 세 박공에는 더 작은 구멍이 셋 나 있었다. 그러나 이것들 또한 멀리언 창이었으므로 집 정면 전체가 한 양식으로 통일되어 있었다.\n\n집 둘레에는 잘 손질된 정원이 있었다. 아주 넓지는 않았으나 그토록 잘 손질되었다는 점에서 크게 눈여겨볼 만했다. 널찍한 자갈길이 난 정원이었고, 그중 집 앞으로 난 산책길 하나는 어찌나 넓은지 테라스라 불러도 마땅했다. 그러나 이 길은 집 앞에 있으면서도 집에서 넉넉히 떨어져 있어서 그 안쪽으로 마차길이 현관까지 이어질 수 있었다. 앨링턴의 데일 가는 대대로 정원을 가꾸는 사람들이었고, 그들의 정원은 아마도 그들의 다른 어떤 재산보다 주 안에서 이름이 나 있었다. 그러나 정원 밖으로는 대영지의 위엄을 부리려는 시도가 없었다. 집 둘레의 목초지는 그저 어여쁜 들판일 뿐이었고 거기에는 나무가 넉넉했다. 앨링턴에는 사슴 사냥터가 없었다. 앨링턴 숲이 이름나 있기는 했으나, 그 숲은 집이 한 부분을 이루는 어떤 전체의 일부가 아니었다. 숲은 집 뒤편에서 족히 일 마일 떨어진 곳에 눈에 띄지 않게 물러나 있었다. 그렇다고 여우를 알맞게 보존하는 데 쓸모가 덜한 것은 아니었다.\n\n그리고 그 집은 위엄을 부리기에는 길에서 너무 가까이 서 있었다 — 앨링턴의 지주들 가운데 누구라도 그런 뜻으로 가슴이 부풀었던 적이 있다면 말이다. 그러나 시골의 위엄이라는 우리 관념은 우리네 오래된 시골 저택이 많이 지어지던 때 이후로 달라진 것이 아닌가 싶다. 마을 가까이 있어서 어떤 식으로든 편의와 보호와 후원을 베풀 수 있도록, 또 어쩌면 집 안 사람들 자신을 위해서도 이웃이 있는 편이 즐거우리라는 생각에서, 옛날에는 신사가 집을 지을 때 그것을 목표로 삼았던 듯하다. 이제는 널따란 사냥터 한복판의 호젓함만이 알맞은 자리로 인정받는다. 정원사의 아담한 별채가 아니라면 오두막은 하나도 보여서는 안 된다. 마을은, 없애 버릴 수 없다면 눈에 띄지 않는 곳으로 치워야 한다. 교회 종소리는 바람직하지 않고, 속된 무리가 제 권리로 다니는 길은 멀찍이 떨어져 있어야 한다. 앨링턴의 어느 옛 데일이 제 집을 지을 때 그는 다르게 생각했다. 저기에 교회가 있고 저기에 마을이 있으니, 그런 이웃함이 흐뭇하여 그는 제 하느님과 제 소작인들 곁에 바짝 붙어 앉았던 것이다.\n\n게스트윅에서 마을로 들어오는 길을 따라가면 왼편 가까이에 교회가 보이지만 집은 길에서 가려져 있다. 교회로 다가가 한길에서 이백 야드도 채 안 되는 교회 문에 이르면 큰 집의 정면이 온전히 보인다. 아마도 그 집을 보는 가장 좋은 자리는 교회 묘지일 것이다. 교회로 올라가는 좁은 길은 문 하나에서 끝나는데, 그것이 데일 씨 저택으로 들어가는 입구다. 거기에는 문지기 집이 없고 문은 대개 열려 있다. 아니, 안에서 풀을 뜯는 가축 때문에 닫아 두어야 할 일이 없는 한 늘 열려 있다. 그러나 안쪽에 또 문이 있어서 안뜰 목장에서 정원을 지나 집으로 이어지고, 거기서 서른 야드쯤 더 가면 또 다른 안쪽 문이 있어 농장 마당으로 데려간다. 아마도 농장 마당이 집과 아주 가까운 것은 앨링턴의 흠일 것이다. 그러나 마구간과 짚가리 마당과 씻지 않은 수레와 농가의 굼뜬 소들은 밤나무가 늘어선 줄이 가려 준다. 오월 초순 꽃이 한창 필 때면 잉글랜드의 어느 나무줄도 그 아름다움을 넘어서지 못한다. 앨링턴의 데일 어른에게 — 지금의 데일이든 앞선 어느 데일이든 — 당신 저택에는 나무가 아쉽다고 말하는 사람이 있었다면, 그는 자랑과 경멸이 뒤섞인 낯으로 제 밤나무 띠를 가리켰을 것이다.\n\n교회 자체에 대해서는 될 수 있는 대로 적은 말을 하겠다. 잉글랜드에 수천은 있으리라 싶은 그런 교회였다. 낮고 비좁고 손보아 가며 간신히 버티고 너무 자주 비가 새었으나, 이상하게도 그림 같았고 건축의 큰 법도로 보아 반듯하기도 했다. 신랑과 측랑을 갖추어 눈에 보이게 십자가 모양으로 지었으되 그 팔은 몸통에 붙도록 짧게 잘려 있었고, 따로 떨어진 성단소가 있었으며, 크고 네모지고 나지막한 탑과 납을 입힌 종 모양 첨탑이 있었는데 그 비례는 고르지 않았다. 낮은 현관, 수직 양식의 고딕 창, 지붕이 평평한 측랑, 그리고 이런 교회의 그 의젓한 잿빛 옛 탑을 누가 모르겠는가? 내부로 말하자면 먼지투성이였다. 등받이 높은 흉한 신도석들이 자리를 다 막고 있었다. 교회 끝에서 아이들이 앉고 두 늙은 악사가 바순을 부는 회랑은 온통 비뚤어져서 곧 무너질 것만 같았다. 설교단은 흉하고 쓸모없는 구조물로 지붕이 허락하는 만큼 거의 높았고, 그 아래 성서대에서는 목사가 머리 위 방석에 달랑거리는 술 장식에 머리가 걸리지 않게 두기가 힘들 지경이었다. 그 아래에는 서기도 하나 있어서 얼마간 높은 세 번째 자리를 차지했다. 그리고 대체로 그곳의 물건들은 내가 바랐을 모양과 딱히 같지 않았다. 그러나 그럼에도 그곳은 교회처럼 보였고, 하느님의 영광을 위해 내 시대에 지어진 근래의 건물들 모두에 대해서는 나는 그만큼도 말하기 어렵다. 그곳은 교회처럼 보였으며, 신도석 사이 통로를 걸어 올라가노라면 찾아온 이가 옛 데일 가 사람들이 잠든 자리를 기리는 놋쇠판을 밟게 된다는 사실 때문에 더욱 그러했다.\n\n교회 아래, 교회와 마을 사이에는 목사관이 서 있었는데, 그 자리가 어떠했는가 하면 목사관의 작은 정원이 교회 묘지에서부터 마을 오두막들 뒤편까지 뻗어 있었다. 지난 삼십 년 안에 새로 지은 쾌적한 집으로, 앨링턴의 교구 목사들이 늘 나오던 그 부유한 대학 단체가 지닌 안락함의 관념에 걸맞은 것이었다. 우리가 앨링턴에 머무는 동안 이따금 목사관을 찾게 되기야 하겠지만, 그 안락함에 대해 더 자세한 설명이 우리에게 필요하리라고는 생각하지 않는다.\n\n목사관과 교회와 저택으로 이어지는 좁은 길을 지나면 한길은 마을을 가로질러 흐르는 작은 개울까지 가파르게 내려간다. 내려가노라면 오른편에 「붉은 사자」가 보였을 것이고, 그 밖에 이렇다 하게 눈에 띄는 집은 보지 못했을 것이다. 개울 바로 곁 맨 아래에는 우체국이 있는데, 그 근방을 통틀어 가장 심술궂은 노파가 지키고 있음이 틀림없다. 여기서 길은 물을 가로질러 지나가고, 걸어가는 사람들을 위해서는 좁은 나무다리라는 편의가 마련되어 있다. 그러나 개울을 건너기 전에 왼편으로 갈라져 나가는 길이 하나 보일 텐데, 저택으로 이어지던 그 다른 좁은 길이 그러했듯이 말이다. 이 가로지르는 길이 언덕을 오르는 곳에 마을에서 가장 좋은 집들이 있다. 빵집 주인이 여기 살고, 리본과 장난감과 비누와 밀짚 보닛을, 그리고 일일이 들자면 너무 긴 다른 여러 물건을 파는 저 점잖은 여인 프러미지 부인도 여기 산다. 여기에는 약제사도 사는데, 이 교구와 이웃 교구들의 우러름이 그를 의사의 지위로 높여 놓았다. 그리고 여기, 상상할 수 있는 가장 작지만 가장 어여쁜 오두막에는 헌 부인이 사는데, 전임 교구 목사의 미망인으로 이웃인 지주와의 사이는 유감스럽게도 마땅히 그러해야 할 만큼 사이좋지 못하다. 이 부인의 소박한 거처를 지나면 앨링턴 스트리트가 — 이 길을 그렇게 부른다 — 갑자기 교회 쪽으로 돌아가는데, 그 굽이 지점에는 나지막하고 어여쁜 철제 난간이 문과 함께 있고 지붕 덮인 길이 나 있어 거기 서 있는 집의 현관으로 이어진다. 나는 여기 이 장의 끄트머리에서 그것이 앨링턴의 작은 집이라는 말만 해 두겠다. 앞서 말했듯 앨링턴 스트리트는 이 지점에서 교회 쪽으로 짧게 돌아 나가고, 흰 문 앞에서 끝나는데 그 문은 두 번째 입구가 되어 교회 묘지로 들어간다.\n\n앨링턴의 큰 집과 지주와 마을에 대해서는 이만큼 말해 둘 필요가 있었다. 작은 집에 대해서는 뒤에 따로 한 장을 두어 이야기하겠다.\n\n# 제2장. 앨링턴의 두 진주\n\n“하지만 크로즈비 씨는 일개 서기일 뿐이잖아.”\n\n이 신랄한 단죄를 입에 올린 사람은 릴리언 데일 양이었고, 상대는 그 언니 이저벨라였으며, 문제의 신사는 앞으로 이 지면에서 우리와 적잖은 인연을 맺게 될 사람이다. 나는 크로즈비 씨가 우리의 주인공이 되리라고는 말하지 않겠다. 이 극에서 그 배역은 말하자면 여러 조각으로 쪼개질 것이기 때문이다. 웅장한 무엇인가가 만들어진다 하더라도 그것은 묽게 희석되어 둘 이상의, 아마도 서넛쯤 되는 젊은 신사들에게 아주 알맞은 분량씩 나뉘어 돌아갈 터이고, 그들 가운데 누구에게도 영웅다운 행적을 넉넉히 누릴 특권은 주어지지 않을 것이다.\n\n“일개 서기라는 게 무슨 뜻인지 나는 모르겠구나, 릴리. 팬퍼런 씨는 일개 법정 변호사고, 보이스 씨는 일개 목사인걸.” 보이스 씨는 앨링턴의 교구 목사였고, 팬퍼런 씨는 지난번 순회재판 때 앨링턴까지 걸음을 한 법률가였다. “그런 식이면 드 게스트 경은 일개 백작일 뿐이라고 해도 되겠네.”\n\n“실제로 그렇잖아 — 일개 백작일 뿐이지. 살진 황소를 기르는 것 말고 무언가 해 본 일이 있었다면 그런 말을 하지도 않았을 거야. 내가 일개 서기라고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지 언니도 알잖아. 관청에 다닌다는 건 사람에게 그리 대단한 일이 못 되는데, 그런데도 크로즈비 씨는 잔뜩 티를 낸단 말이야.”\n\n“크로즈비 씨가 존 임스와 같은 사람이라고 생각하는 건 아니겠지.” 벨이 말했다. 그 말투로 보아 벨은 크로즈비 씨의 자질을 낮게 볼 마음이 조금도 없어 보였다. 존 임스로 말하자면 게스트윅 출신의 젊은이로, 이 년 전에 연봉 팔십 파운드를 받는 소득세 사무국 서기 자리를 얻은 참이었다.\n\n“그럼 조니 임스는 일개 서기지.” 릴리가 말했다. “그리고 크로즈비 씨는 — 결국, 언니, 크로즈비 씨가 일개 서기가 아니면 대체 뭐야? 물론 존 임스보다야 나이가 위고, 그 일을 더 오래 해 왔으니 팔십 파운드보다는 많이 받겠지.”\n\n“나는 크로즈비 씨의 속사정을 아는 사람이 아니야. 일반위원회 사무국에 있다는 건 알고, 내 짐작에는 그 일 전체를 거의 도맡아 관리하는 것 같더구나. 그분 밑에 젊은 사람이 예닐곱은 있고, 또 — 라고 버나드가 말하는 걸 들은 적이 있어. 하지만 물론 그분이 사무실에서 무슨 일을 하는지 내가 알 리는 없지.”\n\n“그럼 그분이 뭔지 내가 말해 줄게, 언니. 크로즈비 씨는 멋쟁이야.” 그리고 릴리언 데일의 말이 옳았다. 크로즈비 씨는 멋쟁이였다.\n\n여기서 버나드가 누구이고 크로즈비 씨가 누구인지를 설명해 두는 것이 가장 좋겠다. 버나드 데일 대위는 공병대의 장교였고, 지금까지 이야기를 나눈 두 아가씨의 사촌이었으며, 지주의 조카이자 추정 상속인이었다. 그의 아버지인 데일 대령과 어머니인 레이디 패니 데일은 아직 토키에 살고 있었는데, 기력이 다하고 병약하고 무기력한 부부로서 토키의 카드 탁자 언저리를 벗어난 세상에 대해서는 거의 죽은 것이나 다름없었다. 제19 용기병 연대에서 제법 이름을 낸 사람이 바로 그 아버지였다. 그가 그리한 방법이란, 저 궁핍한 백작 드 게스트 경의 무일푼 딸과 사랑의 도피를 한 것이었다. 그 사건이 끝난 뒤로는 남의 눈에 띌 만한 기회가 그에게 다시 오지 않았고, 그리하여 그는 세상의 존경으로부터 조금씩 미끄러져 내려갔다 — 처음 레이디 패니를 손에 넣었을 때만 해도 세상은 그를 존경했던 것이다 — 마침내 실내화나 끌고 지내는 이 나이에 이르러, 그와 그의 레이디 패니는 토키의 그 환자용 바퀴의자와 카드 탁자 사이가 아니고서는 아무도 모르는 사람이 되어 버렸다. 그의 형은 아직도 정정하여 두꺼운 구두를 신고 걸어 다니고 늘 말안장에 올랐지만, 대령은 아내의 작위 말고는 제 몸을 깨워 둘 것이 아무것도 없었던 터라 실내화들 사이에서 다소 이르게 잠들어 버린 셈이었다. 그와 레이디 패니 사이의 유일한 아들이 버나드 데일이었다. 딸들도 있기는 했다. 더러는 죽었고, 더러는 시집갔으며, 하나는 카드 탁자 곁에서 부모와 함께 살고 있었다. 근래에 버나드는 부모를 그리 자주 보지 않았다. 말하자면 자식의 도리와 제5계명에 마땅한 주의를 기울이는 데 필요한 정도 이상은 아니었다. 그 역시 제 앞길을 스스로 닦고 있었으니, 공병대의 임관을 얻어 냈고, 동료들 사이에서는 모두 백작의 조카이자 연 삼천 파운드짜리 재산의 상속인으로 알려져 있었다. 그리고 버나드 데일이 이런 이점들을 어느 하나 내던질 마음이 없었다고 말한다고 해서, 나는 결코 그를 헐뜯을 뜻이 없다. 좋은 재산의 상속인이라는 이점은 너무나 뚜렷해서 — 단순히 금전적인 것을 넘어서는 이점들까지 있어서 — 그것을 내던질 생각을 하는 사람도 없고, 남이 그러기를 기대하는 사람도 없다. 손에 쥔 돈이든 장차 들어올 돈이든, 그것은 고개를 반듯이 들게 하고 목소리에 자신을 실어 주며 사람에게 확신을 주어, 세상을 걸어 나가는 데 크게 도움이 된다 — 가진 사람이 그것을 그저 쓰기만 하고 함부로 남용하지만 않는다면 말이다. 그리고 버나드 데일에 대해서라면, 그는 백작인 삼촌 이야기를 자주 입에 올리지는 않았다고 말해 두겠다. 삼촌이 백작이라는 것도, 다른 사람들이 그 사실을 안다는 것도 그는 의식하고 있었다. 그렇지 않았더라면 보퍼트에도, 또 세브라이트라 불리는 저 가장 귀족적인 작은 클럽에도 뽑혀 들어가지 못했으리라는 것을 그는 알았다. 고귀한 혈통이 화제에 오를 때 그는 결코 제 혈통을 콕 집어 말하지 않았지만, 태생의 사정이 자기를 어느 쪽에 놓아 두었는지를 온 세상이 알고 있다는 듯한 투로 말할 줄은 알았다. 이런 식으로 그는 제 이점을 쓰되 남용하지는 않았다. 직업에서도 그는 마찬가지로 운이 좋았다. 근면함으로, 크지는 않으나 늘 깨어 있는 지력으로, 그리고 얼마간 후원의 힘을 빌려 그는 거의 재능 있는 사람이라는 평판을 얻는 데까지 나아갔다. 그의 이름은 과학 실험가들 사이에 알려지게 되었는데, 대포를 발명했다거나 대포를 막을 방책을 고안했다는 사람으로서가 아니라, 대포에 밝고 남이 발명한 것을 살펴보기에 알맞은 사람으로서였다. 이런저런 방책을 정직하게 시험해 줄 사람으로서, 혹은 정직하지는 않더라도 — 그런 얄팍한 사람들이 무슨 일이든 미리 마음속에 판단을 세워 두지 않고 검증에 임하기란 좀처럼 어려운 노릇이니 — 적어도 세상이 만족할 만큼은 정직해 보이는 사람으로서였다. 그리하여 데일 대위는 주로 본국에서, 런던 근방에서 일했고, 노바스코샤에 병영을 짓거나 펀자브에 도로를 놓으라는 부름을 받지 않았다.\n\n그는 몸집이 작고 마른 사내였다. 삼촌보다 작았지만 얼굴은 삼촌을 무척 닮았다. 눈도, 코도, 턱도, 입도 같았다. 다만 이마는 그의 것이 나아서, 덜 높고 덜 뾰족했으며 눈썹 언저리의 생김새가 더 좋았다. 게다가 그는 콧수염을 길렀는데, 그것이 얇은 입술을 어느 정도 가려 주었다. 대체로 그는 못생긴 편이 아니었고, 앞서 말했듯이 자신에 찬 태도와 흔들림 없는 균형을 몸에 지니고 있었으니, 그것만으로도 젊은 사내에게는 하나의 기품이 된다.\n\n그는 지금 여름의 그 감미로운 온기 속에서 — 아직 칠월이 다 가지 않았으므로 — 삼촌의 집에 머물고 있었다. 그리고 그의 절친한 벗 어돌퍼스 크로즈비도 함께 그 집의 손님으로 와 있었으니, 그가 일개 서기인지 아닌지는 내 독자들이 그 문제에 대해 각자 판단을 세우면 될 일이다. 나로서는 어돌퍼스 크로즈비가 일개 서기가 아니었다고 말하고 싶은 쪽이다. 그리고 그가 릴리 데일에게 자기가 「멋쟁이」라는 표를 내지 않았더라면, 그 릴리 데일에게조차 그런 말을 듣지 않았으리라고 생각한다. 사람이 멋쟁이가 되는 과정에서 — 릴리 데일이 알 만한 정도의 멋쟁이라면 — 바로 그 과정에서 이미 일개 서기이기를 그만두게 마련이다. 더구나 데일 대위가, 일개 서기라는 말을 들어 마땅한 사내를 상대로 다몬과 피티아스 같은 벗이 되어 줄 리도 없었다. 또 일개 서기라면 보퍼트든 세브라이트든 발을 들이지 못했을 것이다. 릴리 데일이 앞서 내린 단정에 반하는 증거는 이토록 강하다. 그러나 그녀의 뒤엣말을 뒷받침하는 증거 또한 그만큼 강하다. 크로즈비 씨는 분명 멋쟁이였다. 그가 일반위원회 사무국의 서기인 것은 사실이다. 그러나 우선 첫째로, 일반위원회 사무국은 화이트홀에 있다. 반면 가엾은 존 임스는 러셀 스퀘어보다도 한참 더 먼 버턴 크레슨트의 하숙에서 서머싯 하우스의 침침한 방까지 날마다 걸어 다녀야 했다. 게다가 어돌퍼스 크로즈비는 아주 젊은 시절에 개인 비서를 지냈고, 그 뒤 사무국에서 승진하여 준하급 결재권과 선임 서기 자리를 얻어 연 칠백 파운드를 받았으며, 차관보들 틈에서 어엿한 지위를 누렸으니 그것만 해도 관청의 눈으로 보아 무언가는 되는 일이었다. 그러나 어돌퍼스 크로즈비의 진짜 개가는 그런 것이 아니었다. 차관보들과 가깝게 지내고 화이트홀에서 안락의자가 놓인 제 방을 하나 받았다고 해서, 세브라이트의 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에 서서 부유한 자들이 — 부유하고 게다가 이름 앞에 붙는 칭호까지 가진 자들이! — 귀를 기울이는 가운데 말할 자격을 얻는 것은 아니다. 어돌퍼스 크로즈비는 일반위원회 사무국의 서류에 분별 있게 기록을 남기는 일 이상을 해냈다. 그는 유행의 도성 성문 앞에 진을 치고 앉아 그것을 급습해 함락시켰다. 아니, 더 정확히 말하자면 자물쇠를 따고 슬쩍 안으로 들어갔다. 자기가 걸어 다니는 세계에서 그는 런던의 어엿한 인물이었다. 웨스트 엔드에서 어돌퍼스 크로즈비가 누구인지 모르는 사람은 아무것도 모르는 사람이었다. 그가 많은 거물들과 절친했다는 말은 아니다. 그러나 거물들조차 어돌퍼스 크로즈비와 아는 사이임을 인정했고, 그는 각료들의 응접실에서, 적어도 그 계단께에서는 반드시 눈에 띄는 사람이었다.\n\n릴리언 데일, 사랑스러운 릴리 데일은 — 내 독자는 그녀가 앞으로 아주 사랑스러운 사람이 되리라는 것을, 그리고 그가 릴리 데일을 사랑하지 않는다면 내 이야기는 그에게 아무것도 아니리라는 것을 알아 두어야 한다 — 그 릴리언 데일은 크로즈비 씨가 멋쟁이임을 알아보았던 것이다. 그러나 크로즈비 씨가 그 사실을 남의 비위를 거스르는 식으로 늘 떠벌리고 다니지는 않았다는 말은 해 두어야겠다. 또 멋쟁이가 되면서 아주 못된 사람이 되어 버린 것도 아니었다. 세브라이트에서 떠받들리는 사내가 앨링턴의 응접실에서, 어머니 말고는 누구에게도 떠받들려 본 적 없는 조니 임스처럼 처신하기를 기대할 수는 없는 노릇이다. 그리고 우리의 이 조각난 주인공에게는 유행이 준 것 말고도 뒤를 받쳐 주는 이점이 더 있었다. 그는 키가 크고 잘생긴 사내로, 눈매가 서글서글하고 입매에는 표정이 있어서, 어느 방에서 마주치든 아마 눈여겨보게 되는 그런 사람이었다. 게다가 그는 말할 줄 알았고, 말할 만한 무언가를 안에 지니고 있었다. 그는 세상이라는 햇빛 속을 팔랑거리며 햇살 한 줄기에 데워져 예뻐 보이는 나비나 멋쟁이 한량 따위가 아니었다. 크로즈비에게는 정치에 대해, 종교에 대해, 이 시대의 박애적 경향에 대해 제 나름의 견해가 있었고, 그 견해를 세우는 동안 여기저기서 읽은 것도 있었다. 그가 그토록 이른 나이에 화이트홀의 저 관청에 들어앉지 않았더라면 세상에서 더 나은 일을 이루었을지도 모른다. 트인 직업에 나섰더라면 제 밥벌이를 더 훌륭히 해냈을 만한 무언가가 그 안에 있었다.\n\n그러나 그 밥벌이 문제에 관한 한 어돌퍼스 크로즈비의 운명은 이미 정해져 버렸고, 그도 이제는 돌이킬 수 없는 그 운명과 화해한 터였다. 아주 얼마 안 되는 유산이, 연 백 파운드 남짓이 그의 몫으로 떨어졌다. 그 밖에는 사무국에서 받는 봉급뿐 다른 것은 없었다. 그렇게 이루어진 수입으로 그는 런던에서 독신으로 살면서, 그럭저럭 여유 있는 형편의 사내에게 런던이 줄 수 있는 것을 다 누렸고, 아내라든가 제 집이라든가 말이 가득한 마구간 같은 값비싼 사치는 아예 바라보지 않았다. 그가 실제로 누린 세상의 좋은 것들만 해도, 존 임스가 알았더라면 그 동료 서기의 눈에는 그가 놀랍도록 부유한 사람으로 비쳤을 것이다. 마운트 스트리트의 그의 하숙은 세간이 우아했다. 런던의 사교철 석 달 동안 그는 아주 반듯한 삯말 한 필의 주인 노릇을 했다. 그는 언제나 옷을 잘 입었으나 결코 지나치게 차려입지 않았다. 클럽에서는 수입이 자기의 열 배인 사람들과 대등하게 어울려 지냈다. 그는 결혼하지 않았다. 돈 없이는 결혼할 수 없다고 스스로 인정했고, 돈 때문에 결혼하지는 않겠다고 마음먹고 있었다. 그는 결혼이 주는 안락을 제 손이 닿지 않는 것으로 여겨 옆으로 밀쳐 두었다. 그러나 — 아니, 우리의 새 친구 어돌퍼스 크로즈비의 사생활과 형편을 지금 이 자리에서 더 꼬치꼬치 캐고 들지는 않겠다.\n\n릴리언이 그에게 내린 선고가 있고 난 뒤, 두 아가씨는 한동안 말이 없었다. 벨은 아마 동생에게 조금 화가 나 있었을 것이다. 어느 신사에 대해서든 칭찬의 말을 길게 늘어놓도록 스스로를 놓아두는 일이 그녀에게는 흔치 않았는데, 이제 크로즈비 씨를 두둔하는 말을 한두 마디 했다가 그 유별난 열의 때문에 동생에게 핀잔을 들은 기분이었다. 릴리는 그림을 그리고 있었고, 일이 분 만에 크로즈비 씨 이야기는 죄다 잊어버렸다. 그러나 벨의 마음에는 앙금이 남아 그녀를 다시 그 화제로 돌아가게 만들었다. “난 그런 속된 말이 싫어, 릴리.”\n\n“속된 말이라니, 무슨?”\n\n“네가 버나드의 친구를 뭐라고 불렀는지 너도 알잖니.”\n\n“아, 멋쟁이 말이구나. 나는 속된 말이 좋은가 봐. 무엇이 신난다고 말하는 건 정말이지 굉장히 신나는 일이거든. 언니 신경이 어떨까 걱정만 아니었으면 나는 그분더러 죽여준다고 했을 거야. 사전에 실린 낱말만 쓰고 사는 건 얼마나 굼뜬 노릇이니.”\n\n“신사분들 이야기를 하면서 쓸 만한 말은 아닌 것 같아.”\n\n“점잖지 않다는 거야? 뭐, 나도 점잖았으면 좋겠어 — 어떻게 하는 건지 알기만 하면.”\n\n어떻게 하는 건지 알기만 하면! 그 문제에 관한 한 여자아이가 어떻게 하는지를 알아낼 길이란 없다. 천성과 어머니가 미리 그 일을 해 주지 않았다면 그 방면으로는 아무 가망이 없다. 릴리언 데일의 경우에는 천성과 어머니가 이 점에서 충분히 제 몫을 해 주었다고 나는 말할 수 있을 것 같다.\n\n“어쨌든 크로즈비 씨는 신사고, 사람을 유쾌하게 만드는 법을 아는 분이야. 내 말은 그뿐이었어. 그분 이야기라면 어머니가 나보다 훨씬 더 많이 하셨는걸.”\n\n“크로즈비 씨는 아폴로야. 그리고 나는 아폴로야말로 세상에 났던 이들 가운데 가장 대단한 — 언니도 그게 뭔지 알잖아 — 그것이라고 늘 생각해. 그 낱말은 입에 올리면 안 되겠지, 아폴로는 신사였으니까.”\n\n바로 그때, 그 신의 이름이 아직 릴리의 입술에 남아 있는 사이에 응접실의 높다란 열린 창이 어두워지더니 버나드가 들어섰고, 그 뒤를 크로즈비 씨가 따랐다.\n\n“누가 아폴로 이야기를 하고 있었지?” 데일 대위가 말했다.\n\n두 아가씨는 그만 말문이 막혔다. 가엾은 릴리의 그 마지막 말로 자기가 입에 오르내리는 것을 크로즈비 씨가 들었다면 어찌 될 것인가? 이것이야말로 벨이 늘 동생을 나무라던 그 경솔함이었고, 여기 그 결과가 있었다! 그러나 사실 버나드는 이름 말고는 아무것도 듣지 못했고, 그의 뒤에 있던 크로즈비 씨는 아무것도 듣지 못했다.\n\n““빛나는 아폴로의 비파, 그의 머리카락으로 줄을 맨 그것처럼 달콤하고 아름다운”.” 크로즈비 씨가 말했다. 그 인용에 별다른 뜻을 담은 것은 아니었고, 다만 두 아가씨가 무슨 까닭인지 당황하여 입을 다물었다는 것을 알아챈 것이었다.\n\n“그럼 음악이 무척 형편없었겠네요.” 릴리가 말했다. “그분 머리카락이 우리 것과 아주 달랐다면 모를까.”\n\n“온통 햇살이었지.” 버나드가 거들었다. 그러나 그쯤에서 아폴로는 제 소임을 다했고, 아가씨들은 격식대로 손님들을 맞았다.\n\n“어머니는 정원에 계세요.” 벨이 말했다. 젊은 신사들이 찾아왔을 때 젊은 아가씨들이 흔히 부리는 그 위선적인 시치미였으니, 마치 그 신사들이 특별히 찾는 대상이 어머니이기라도 한 것처럼 구는 것이었다.\n\n“챙 넓은 모자를 쓰고 완두콩을 따고 계세요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 우리도 어서 가서 거들어 드립시다.” 크로즈비 씨가 말했다. 그리고 그들은 정원으로 나갔다.\n\n앨링턴 큰 집의 정원과 작은 집의 정원은 서로 통해 있다. 둘 사이에는 두툼한 월계수 산울타리와 널찍한 도랑, 그리고 그 도랑을 지키는 쇠창살이 제대로 된 경계를 이루고 있다. 그러나 널찍한 도랑 위에는 판자다리가 놓여 있고 다리 어귀에는 열쇠 없는 쪽문이 하나 있어서, 즐기기 위한 용도로는 두 집의 정원이 서로에게 다 열려 있는 셈이다. 그리고 작은 집의 정원은 무척 예쁘다. 작은 집은 길에서 워낙 가까워, 식당 창과 쇠 난간 사이에는 화단이라기보다 가장자리라 할 좁은 띠 하나와, 둥근 자갈을 박아 만든 두 자도 안 되는 좁은 오솔길밖에 없다. 그 길로는 정원사 말고는 아무도 들어서지 않는다. 길에서 집까지는 대여섯 자밖에 안 되고, 대문에서 들어오는 통로는 지붕 덮인 길로 가려져 있다. 그러나 응접실 창이 열리는 집 뒤편의 정원은, 앨링턴이라는 마을도 없고 잔디밭에서 백 야드도 안 되는 곳에 교회로 오르는 길도 없는 양, 오감 어디로 보나 호젓하다. 하기야 교회 첨탑은 잔디밭에서 보인다. 데일 부인네 담장에 잇닿은 교회 묘지 모퉁이에 선 주목들 사이로 말하자면 고개를 내밀고 있는 것이다. 그러나 데일 집안사람 가운데 그 첨탑이 눈에 들어오는 것을 싫어하는 이는 아무도 없다. 앨링턴 작은 집의 자랑은 뭐니 뭐니 해도 그 잔디밭에 있으니, 풀로 만들어 낼 수 있는 한 가장 매끄럽고 가장 고르고 가장 벨벳 같은 잔디밭이다. 릴리 데일은 제 잔디밭을 자랑스러워하여, 큰 집에서 크로케를 해 보아야 아무 소용이 없다고 자주 말해 왔다. 거기는 풀이 다발다발 뭉쳐 자라는데, 정원사 홉킨스가 무슨 짓을 해도, 또 하려고 들어도 그 뭉치에는 아무 효험이 없다는 것이다. 그러나 작은 집에는 그런 뭉치가 없다. 지주 자신이 크로케에 그리 열을 올린 적이 없었으므로 크로케 도구는 아예 작은 집으로 옮겨졌고, 그리하여 그곳의 크로케는 하나의 제도가 되다시피 했다.\n\n정원 이야기가 나온 김에 데일 부인의 온실도 말해 두어야겠다. 벨은 이 온실에 관한 한 큰 집에 견줄 만한 것이 없다는 의견을 굽히지 않았다. “물론 꽃 이야기예요.” 그녀는 이렇게 제 말을 바로잡곤 했다. 큰 집에는 아주 이름난 포도 온실이 있었기 때문이다. 이 문제에 대해서만은 지주도 크로케 때만큼 너그럽지 못해서, 조카에게 너는 꽃을 하나도 모른다고 말하곤 했다. “모를지도 모르죠, 크리스토퍼 삼촌.” 그녀는 이렇게 말했다. “그래도 저는 우리 제라늄이 제일 좋아요.” 데일 양에게는 고집이라는 양념이 얼마쯤 들어 있었던 것이다. 하기야 남자든 여자든, 젊든 늙든 데일 집안사람이 다 그러했다.\n\n이 잔디밭과 이 온실, 아니 작은 집에 딸린 정원 전체를 돌보는 일이 큰 집의 수석 정원사 홉킨스의 손에 맡겨져 있었다는 것도 밝혀 두는 편이 좋겠다. 그리 된 까닭은 오로지, 데일 부인이 제 정원사를 따로 둘 형편이 못 되었기 때문이다. 주급 십 실링으로 칼과 신발을 닦고 땅을 파는 일꾼 아이 하나가 세 여인을 시중드는 유일한 남자였다. 그러나 밑에 사람을 여럿 부리는 앨링턴의 수석 정원사 홉킨스는, 큰 쪽의 포도 온실과 복숭아 담장과 계단식 화단에 그러하듯 이 조촐한 집의 잔디밭과 온실에도 눈을 부릅뜨고 있었다. 그의 눈에는 그것이 다 한 군데였다. 작은 집은 제 주인의 것이었고, 실은 그 안의 세간까지도 그러했다. 그것은 데일 부인에게 세를 준 것이 아니라 빌려준 것이었다. 홉킨스는 아마 데일 부인을 좋아하지는 않았을 것이다. 데일 집안에 나서 자란 사람이 아니니 자기가 그녀에게 도리를 다할 까닭이 없다고 여긴 것이다. 두 아가씨는 사랑했고, 때로는 아주 딱 잘라 면박을 주기도 했다. 데일 부인에게는 차갑게 예의만 차렸고, 정원에 관하여 특별히 눈여겨볼 만한 지시가 내려오면 언제나 지주에게 되물었다.\n\n이만하면 데일 부인이 작은 집에 살게 된 조건이 설명될 것이다. 언제고 한 번은 설명해 두어야 할 문제였다. 그녀의 남편은 삼 형제 가운데 막내였고, 여러 면에서 가장 총명했다. 그는 젊어서 런던으로 올라가 그곳에서 토지 측량사로 잘 풀렸다. 어찌나 잘 풀렸던지 정부가 그를 썼고, 서너 해 동안은 큰 수입을 누렸다. 그러나 아직 사다리를 올라가는 중일 뿐인데 죽음이 불쑥 그를 덮쳤고, 그가 눈을 감을 때 눈앞에 보이던 황금빛 앞날은 아직 손에 잡히기 시작도 하지 않은 참이었다. 이 일은 우리 이야기가 시작되기 열다섯 해쯤 전에 일어났으므로, 두 아가씨에게는 아버지에 대한 기억이랄 것이 거의 남아 있지 않다. 과부가 되고 처음 다섯 해 동안 데일 부인은 — 지주의 눈에 든 적이 한 번도 없었던 그녀는 — 두 어린 딸을 데리고 몹시 빠듯한 살림이 허락하는 대로 검소하게 살았다. 그때 작은 집에는 지주의 어머니인 늙은 데일 부인이 살고 있었다. 그러나 늙은 데일 부인이 세상을 뜨자 지주는 형수에게 그 집을 세 없이 쓰라고 내주며, 앨링턴에 살면 따님들이 사교상 적잖은 이득을 보게 되리라고 일러 주었다. 그녀는 그 제안을 받아들였고, 사교상의 이득은 과연 따라왔다. 데일 부인은 가난하여 수입을 다 합쳐도 연 삼백 파운드를 넘지 못했고, 따라서 자기 살림살이는 어쩔 수 없이 아주 수수했다. 그러나 그녀는 두 딸이 고을에서 사랑받고, 이웃한 집안들에게 크게 호감을 사며, 앨링턴의 데일 지주의 딸이었더라도 누렸을 만한 이점을 거의 다 누리는 것을 보았다. 사정이 이러하니, 시아주버니의 사랑을 받는지 못 받는지, 홉킨스에게 존경을 받는지 못 받는지는 그녀에게 대수로운 일이 아니었다. 제 딸들이 그녀를 사랑하고 존경했고, 그녀가 제 몫으로 세상에 요구하는 것은 그것이 거의 전부였다.\n\n그리고 크리스토퍼 삼촌은 제 나름의 고집스럽고 다소 무뚝뚝한 방식으로 두 조카딸에게 무척 잘해 주었다. 큰 집 마구간에는 조랑말 두 필이 있어 두 아가씨가 타도 좋게 되어 있었고, 어쩌다 있는 일을 빼면 다른 사람은 아무도 타지 않았다. 내 생각에는 그 조랑말을 아예 아가씨들에게 주어 버릴 수도 있었을 것 같지만, 그는 달리 생각했다. 또 그는 옷가지에도 보태 주어, 필요하다 싶은 것을 이따금 집으로 보내 주기는 했으되 세상에서 가장 기분 좋은 방식으로 그러지는 않았다. 돈은 결코 준 적이 없고, 약속 같은 것도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그 아이들은 데일이었고, 그는 그 아이들을 사랑했다. 그리고 크리스토퍼 데일로 말하자면 한번 사랑하면 언제까지고 사랑하는 사람이었다. 벨이 그가 가장 아끼는 아이였고, 조카 버나드와 더불어 그 마음의 가장 따뜻한 자리를 나누어 가졌다. 이 둘을 두고 그에게는 계획이 있었으니, 벨을 장차 앨링턴 큰 집의 안주인으로 앉힐 생각이었다. 그러나 그 계획에 대해 데일 양은 아직 아무것도 모르고 있었다.\n\n이제 잔디밭으로 걸어 나간 우리의 네 사람에게로 돌아가도 되겠다. 그들은 완두콩 따는 데일 부인을 거들러 나선 것으로 되어 있었다. 그러나 즐거움이 일 앞을 가로막았고, 젊은이들은 어른의 수고를 잊은 채 크로케의 매력에 마음을 빼앗겨 버렸다. 쇠고리와 말뚝은 이미 박혀 있었다. 나무망치와 공은 여기저기 흩어져 있었고, 게다가 편이 어찌나 알맞게 짜였는지! “저는 아직 크로케를 한 판도 못 해 봤습니다.” 크로즈비 씨가 말했다. 전날 저녁 식사 전에야 도착한 것을 생각하면 그가 시간을 많이 허비했다고는 할 수 없었다. 그러고는 이내 모두의 손에 나무망치가 들려 있었다.\n\n“물론 편을 갈라서 해야지.” 릴리가 말했다. “버나드하고 내가 한편이야.” 그러나 그것은 허락되지 않았다. 릴리가 크로케 마당의 여왕이라는 것은 널리 알려진 사실이었고, 버나드가 제 친구보다 솜씨가 낫다고들 여겼으므로 릴리가 크로즈비 씨를 짝으로 데려가야 했다. “아폴로는 고리를 못 빠져나가더라.” 릴리는 나중에 언니에게 이렇게 말했다. “그런데 못 빠져나가는 모양새가 어쩌면 그렇게 우아한지!” 하기야 릴리는 진 편이었으니 제 짝을 두고 얼마쯤 심술을 부린 것도 봐줄 만하다. 그러나 크로즈비 씨가 앨링턴을 아주 떠나기 전에 고리를 빠져나갈 수 있게 된 것은 사실이었고, 릴리는 여전히 크로케 마당의 여왕이기는 했으되 그 나라에 남자 군주가 하나 있음을 인정해야 했다.\n\n“우리가 그, 거기서 하던 방식은 이게 아닌데 —” 크로즈비가 놀이 도중에 이렇게 말하다가 스스로 입을 다물었다.\n\n“거기가 어딘데?” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“지난여름에 내가 가 있던 곳이야 — 슈롭셔에.”\n\n“그럼 크로즈비 씨가 지난여름에 가 계셨다는 그곳, 슈롭셔에서는 이 놀이를 할 줄 모르나 보네요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“레이디 하틀탑 댁 말이지.” 버나드가 말했다. 그런데 하틀탑 후작부인은 참으로 대단한 인물이자 유행 세계의 선두에 선 사람이었다.\n\n“어머! 레이디 하틀탑 댁이라니!” 릴리가 말했다. “그럼 우리는 두 손 들 수밖에 없겠네요.” 이 자그마한 빈정거림은 크로즈비 씨의 귀를 그냥 지나치지 않았고, 그는 그것을 부당한 소리라고 마음속 수첩에 적어 두었다. 그는 레이디 하틀탑과 그 댁의 크로케 마당 이야기가 나오지 않게 하려고 애쓴 참인데, 부인의 이름이 억지로 그에게 떠맡겨진 것이었다. 그럼에도 그는 그 모든 일에도 불구하고 릴리 데일이 마음에 들었다. 다만 말수는 적어도 벨이 가장 마음에 든다고 그는 생각했다. 벨이 그 집안의 미인이었기 때문이다.\n\n놀이가 한창일 때 버나드는 자기들이 세 부인을 그날 저택 만찬에 청하려고 일부러 건너왔다는 것을 떠올렸다. 그들은 바로 전날에도 다 함께 그곳에서 저녁을 먹었는데, 아가씨들의 삼촌이 다시 오라는 전갈을 보낸 것이었다. “제가 어머니께 여쭤 볼게요.” 벨이 이렇게 말하며 먼저 자리를 떴다. 그러고는 돌아와서, 자기와 동생은 삼촌의 분부를 따르겠지만 어머니는 집에 남아 계시고 싶어 한다고 전했다. “먹어야 할 완두콩이 있잖아요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“그건 큰 집으로 올려 보내.” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“홉킨스가 가만있지 않을걸.” 릴리가 말했다. “그건 이것저것 뒤섞는 짓이라고 하거든. 홉킨스는 뒤섞는 걸 싫어해.” 그러고 나서 놀이가 끝나자 그들은 작은 정원에서 큰 정원으로, 관목 숲을 지나 들판으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나갔고, 거기서 아직 남아 있는 건초 일의 끝자락과 마주쳤다. 릴리는 갈퀴를 하나 집어 이 분쯤 건초를 긁었다. 크로즈비 씨는 건초를 짐수레에 던져 올려 보려다가 건초 일꾼들에게 첫발 값으로 반 크라운을 물어야 했다. 벨은 살빛을 생각하여 나무 밑에 얌전히 앉아 있었는데, 그러자 건초 던지기가 그리 입에 맞지 않던 크로즈비 씨도 같은 나무 밑으로 몸을 던졌으니, 그야말로 아폴로다운 자세였다고 릴리는 그날 밤 늦게 어머니에게 말했다. 그러자 버나드가 릴리를 건초로 뒤덮었는데, 그로서는 익살로 치면 대단한 솜씨였다. 그리고 릴리는 그 답례를 하다가 크로즈비 씨를 거의 파묻어 버릴 뻔했다 — 실수로 말이다.\n\n“어머, 릴리.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“정말 죄송해요, 크로즈비 씨. 버나드 잘못이에요. 버나드, 다시는 너하고 건초밭에 오지 않을 거야.” 그리하여 그들은 모두 무척 허물없는 사이가 되었다. 그동안 벨은 나무 밑에 조용히 앉아, 크로즈비 씨가 이따금 골라 하는 한두 마디에 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 사람과 어울리는 데는 말이 거의 필요 없는 종류의 즐거움도 있는 법이다. 벨은 동생 릴리만큼 활달하지 않았다. 그리고 그로부터 한 시간 뒤 만찬을 위해 옷을 갈아입으면서, 그녀는 크로즈비 씨가 그리 많은 말을 하지는 않았어도 즐거운 오후를 보냈다고 스스로 인정했다.\n\n# 제3장. 앨링턴의 데일 미망인\n\n작은 집의 데일 부인은 태생이 데일 집안 사람이 아니었으므로, 그 성품에서 데일 집안 특유의 기질을 찾아서는 안 된다는 사실을 굳이 힘주어 말할 필요는 없을 것이다. 그 기질은 딸들에게서도 그다지 두드러지지 않았는데, 그 점에서 두 아이는 아버지보다 어머니를 더 많이 닮았기 때문이다. 그러나 눈이 밝은 사람이라면 이 처녀들에게서 데일 집안 사람을 알아보았을 것이다. 두 사람은 한결같았고, 어쩌면 고집스러웠으며, 이따금 남을 판단할 때 조금 야박했고, 데일 집안 사람이라는 데에는 대단한 무엇이 있다고 여기는 편이었다. 다만 그것을 입 밖에 내어 말하는 편은 아니었다. 그러나 두 사람에게는 이보다 나은 자긍심도 있었으니, 그것은 어머니에게서 물려받은 유산이었다.\n\n데일 부인은 분명 자긍심이 강한 여인이었다. 그렇다고 그녀 자신에게 자랑거리로 삼을 만한 무엇이 있어서는 아니었다. 태생으로 보자면 그녀는 남편보다 훨씬 아래였으니, 할아버지 되는 이는 거의 아무것도 아닌 사람이었다. 재산은 그녀의 신분치고는 상당했고 지금도 주로 그 수입에 기대어 살고 있었으나, 부에서 오는 자랑을 조금이라도 갖게 할 만큼은 아니었다. 그리고 그녀는 미인이었다. 내 취향으로 말하자면 지금도 여전히 무척 아름답다. 그러나 이 나이에 이르러, 미망인이 된 지 열다섯 해에 다 자란 딸이 둘이나 있는 그녀가 제 아름다움을 자랑으로 삼는 일은 분명 없었다. 자신이 숙녀라는 사실에 대해서도 의식적인 자부심 같은 것은 없었다. 그녀가 안팎으로, 정수리에서 발바닥까지, 머리로나 마음으로나 정신으로나 숙녀였다는 것, 교육으로도 숙녀요 천성으로도 숙녀였다는 것, 그리고 할아버지에 관한 그 결함에도 불구하고 태생으로도 숙녀였다는 것을, 나는 여기 사실로서 밝혀 둔다. 내가 책임을 지고서(meo periculo) 하는 말이다. 그리고 지주는, 비록 그녀에게 각별한 애정은 없었으나, 이 점을 인정하고 있었고 모든 면에서 그녀를 자기와 대등한 사람으로 대했다.\n\n그러나 그녀의 처지는 아주 자긍심이 높든가 아니면 아주 낮추어야 하든가, 둘 중 하나여야 하는 자리였다. 그녀는 가난했으나 딸들은 흔히 부잣집 딸들에게나 허락되는 자리에서 움직이고 있었다. 두 아이가 그럴 수 있는 것은 자식 없는 앨링턴 지주의 조카딸이기 때문이었고, 조카딸인 이상 삼촌의 호의와 친절을 받아들이더라도 그녀 자신에게나 아이들에게나 자존심이 상할 일은 없다고 그녀는 여겼다. 자기 자존심 때문에 아이들과, 삼촌이 아이들에게 줄 수 있는 세상에서의 이점 사이를 가로막았다면, 그녀는 어머니로서의 도리를 그르쳤을 것이다. 아이들을 위하여 그녀는 지금 살고 있는 집을 빌려 쓰는 것을 받아들였고, 시숙에게 딸린 여러 부속물을 쓰는 것도 받아들였다. 그러나 제 몫으로는 아무것도 받지 않았다. 필립 데일과의 혼인을 그의 형인 지주는 못마땅해했고, 필립이 살아 있는 동안에도 지주는 이 점에 대한 제 감정을 결코 거두지 않으리라는 태도를 계속 보였다. 그 감정은 끝내 거두어지지 않았다. 그리하여 이제 시숙과 제수가 여러 해 동안 가까운 이웃으로, 말하자면 거의 한 식구처럼 살아왔건만, 두 사람은 한 번도 벗이 되지 못했다. 둘 사이에 다툼의 말이 오간 적은 없었다. 두 사람은 끊임없이 만났다. 지주는 자기도 모르는 사이에 아우의 미망인에게 깊은 존경을 품게 되었다. 미망인은 삼촌이 제 딸들에게 보이는 애정이 참된 것임을 스스로 인정하고 있었다. 그런데도 두 사람은 끝내 벗으로 어울리지 못했다. 데일 부인은 제 돈 문제에 대해 지주에게 한마디도 한 적이 없었다. 지주는 아이들에 관한 제 뜻에 대해 어머니에게 한마디도 한 적이 없었다. 그런 식으로 두 사람은 앨링턴에서 살아왔고 또 살고 있었다.\n\n데일 부인이 꾸려 가는 삶은 결코 수월한 삶이 아니었다. 그녀 편에서 치르는 고통스러운 노력이 없지 않았다. 그 삶의 이치를 말하자면 이러했다. 딸들이 땅 위에서 잘 살게 하려고 자신은 땅속에 묻히는 것. 그리고 이 이치를 관철하려면 딸들 앞에서 어떤 불평도, 괴로운 기색도 내비치지 말아야 했다. 어머니가 이 땅속의 삶에서 자기들을 위해 무엇인가를 희생하고 있다는 것을 아이들이 알아차린다면, 아이들의 땅 위의 삶도 온전할 수 없을 터였다. 햇빛 가리개 모자를 쓰고 완두를 따는 일이나, 제 난롯가에서 오래 책을 읽는 일이나, 홀로 앉아 생각에 잠기는 시간이야말로 어머니가 각별히 좋아하는 것이라고 아이들이 믿어야 했다. “엄마는 밖에 나가는 걸 좋아하지 않으셔.” “엄마는 당신 응접실 말고는 어디서도 행복하지 않으신 것 같아.” 나는 아이들이 그런 말을 하도록 배웠다고 말하려는 것이 아니다. 다만 그런 말로 이어질 생각을 하도록 배웠다는 것이며, 세상에 나가기 시작한 초기에는 실제로 어머니를 두고 그렇게 말하곤 했다. 그러나 오래지 않아, 한 아이에게 먼저 그리고 그다음에 다른 아이에게, 그렇지 않다는 것을 그리고 어머니가 자기들을 위해 얼마나 견뎌 왔는지를 알게 되는 때가 찾아왔다.\n\n그리고 사실 데일 부인도 아이들만큼 마음이 젊을 수 있었다. 그녀 역시 크로케를 칠 수 있었고, 건초 갈퀴를 들고 아양을 떨 수도 있었고, 제 조랑말을 귀여워할 수도 있었고, 그렇다, 이런저런 아폴로가 늘어놓는 자잘한 허튼소리에 귀를 기울일 수도 있었다. 사정이 그러기에 알맞다고 여겼더라면 말이다. 마흔 살 여자가 늙어 빠진 인간 혐오자가 되거나, 세상의 즐거움에는 아랑곳하지 않는 라다만티스 같은 준엄한 도덕가가 되는 것은 아니다. 아니, 설령 미망인이라 해도 그렇지 않다. 그런 여자의 마음은 마땅히 그러해야 한다고 여기는 이들이 있다. 나는 그렇게 여기지 않는다고 분명히 말해 둔다. 나는 여자도 남자도 젊을 수 있는 동안은 젊기를 바란다. 그렇다고 여자가 제 아버지 집안 성경에 적힌 것보다 제 나이를 적게 부르라는 말은 아니다. 마흔인 사람은 마흔이라고 하라. 다만 마흔에도 마음이 젊을 수 있다면, 젊다는 것을 보여 주게 하라.\n\n나는 데일 부인이 잘못했다고 생각한다. 내가 권했던 대로 했더라면 그녀는 햇빛 가리개 모자를 쓰고 완두 지지대 사이에 남아 있는 대신 크로케 잔디밭의 그 무리에 끼었을 것이다. 네 사람 사이에 오간 말 가운데 그녀가 듣지 못한 것은 한마디도 없었다. 그 완두 지지대는 낮은 담과 관목 몇 그루로 잔디밭과 갈라져 있을 뿐이었다. 그녀는 의심하는 사람으로서가 아니라 그저 사랑하는 사람으로서 귀를 기울였다. 딸들의 목소리는 그녀에게 더없이 소중했고, 릴리의 은방울 같은 목소리는 신들의 음악처럼 귀에 달았다. 그녀는 레이디 하틀탑에 관한 이야기를 죄다 들었고, 릴리의 대담한 빈정거림에 몸을 떨었다. 그리고 엄마는 집에 남아 완두를 드실 거라는 릴리의 말도 들었고, 이제 그것이 제 인생의 몫이로구나 하고 슬프게 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n“사랑스러운 내 딸. 그래, 마땅히 그래야지!”\n\n그녀의 생각은 그렇게 흘렀다. 그러다 아이들이 작은 다리를 건너 저쪽 뜰로 넘어가는 것을 귀로 좇고 나서, 그녀는 팔에 짐을 낀 채 잔디밭을 가로질러 집으로 돌아왔고, 응접실 창문 앞 층계에 앉아 눈앞의 어여쁜 여름 꽃들과 매끄러운 잔디밭을 내다보았다.\n\n하느님께서 그녀를 지금 이 자리에 두신 것은 잘하신 일이 아니었던가? 그녀의 몫은 아름다운 곳에 정해진 것이 아니었던가? 그녀는 제 딸들로 하여, 그 사랑스럽고 다정하고 미더운 아이들로 하여 행복하지 않은가? 제 주인이자 자신의 더 나은 반쪽이 이른 나이에 그녀에게서 거두어지고 그리하여 가벼운 즐거움의 샘이 죄다 막혀 버리는 것이 정해진 일이었다면, 그 여읨 가운데서도 제 인생의 몫을 이만큼 부드럽게 하고 거기에 품위와 아름다움을 더해 준 것이 이토록 많았으니 다행이 아니었던가? 그렇지, 하고 그녀는 스스로와 따져 보았고, 그러면서도 자기가 행복하지 않다는 것을 스스로 인정했다. 그녀는 스스로 곧잘 말해 왔듯 어린아이의 것들을 버리기로 마음먹었건만, 이제 그렇게 제게서 밀어낸 그것들을 그리워하고 있었다. 앉아 있는 동안에도 관목숲을 지나가는 릴리의 목소리가 아직 들렸다. 어머니의 귀가 아니고서는 가려낼 수 없을 소리였다. 이제 그 젊은이들이 큰 집에 와 있으니 딸들이 그리로 가는 것은 당연했다. 식탁을 빛내 줄 여자들이 없었다면 지주도 젊은이들을 불러 묵게 하지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀 자신은, 거기서 아무도 자기를 바라지 않는다는 것을 알고 있었다. 이따금 한 번씩은 가야 했다. 그러지 않으면 가지 않는다는 사실 자체가 거북하게 눈에 띌 터였기 때문이다. 그러나 그 모임에 낄 다른 이유는 없었고, 낀다 한들 즐거움을 주지도 받지도 못할 것이었다. 딸들이야 시숙의 식탁에서 먹고 그 잔으로 마시라지. 아이들은 진심으로 환영받고 있었다. 그녀에게는 큰 집에도 그런 환영이 없었다. 다른 어느 집에도, 다른 어느 식탁에도!\n\n“엄마는 집에 남아서 완두를 드실 거야.”\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 머리를 받친 채 거기 앉아, 릴리가 한 그 말을 혼자 되뇌었다.\n\n“마님, 요리사가 완두를 까도 되겠느냐고 여쭙습니다요.” 그러자 그녀의 몽상이 깨졌다.\n\n그러자 데일 부인은 일어나 바구니를 넘겨주었다. “요리사도 아가씨들이 큰 집에서 저녁을 드시는 걸 알고 있지?”\n\n“네, 마님.”\n\n“내 저녁은 차릴 것 없다고 해라. 나는 차를 일찍 마시겠다.” 그리하여 결국 데일 부인은 자기에게 정해진 그 특별한 소임을 끝내 다하지 못했다.\n\n그러나 그녀는 곧 다른 소임에 착수했다. 세 식구가 한 해 삼백 파운드로 살아야 하면서도 그런대로 사교계에 나가는 시늉을 해야 한다면, 설령 그 식구가 여자들뿐이라 해도 자잘한 것들에 몹시 마음을 써야 하는 법이다. 데일 부인은 이를 잘 알고 있었고, 딸들이 단정하고 산뜻하고 어여쁜 차림이기를 바랐으므로 그 일에 긴 시간을 바쳤다. 지주는 겨울이면 숄을 보내 주었고, 승마복도 마련해 주었고, 런던에서 드레스감으로 갈색 비단을 내려보내 주기도 했다. 다만 그 양이 어찌나 빠듯했던지 그 감으로 드레스 두 벌을 제대로 지어내는 일은 여자의 재주로 될 일이 아니었고, 그리하여 그 갈색 비단옷은 그날 이후 지금까지 골칫거리로 남아 있었다. 지주는 그런 일에 기억력이 좋은 데다 제 후한 인심이 맺은 열매를 보고 싶어 안달했기 때문이다. 이 모든 것이 도움이 된 것은 틀림없으나, 지주가 그렇게 쓴 액수를 그냥 돈으로 조카딸들에게 주었더라면 이로움이 더 컸을 것이다. 어쨌든 두 처녀는 언제나 단정하고 산뜻하고 어여뻤으니, 자기들도 그 일에 게으르지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 그 옷시중의 으뜸은 어머니였다. 그리고 이제 그녀는 딸들 방으로 올라가 모슬린 드레스를 꺼냈고, 그리고, 아니 어쩌면 이런 이야기까지 늘어놓아서는 안 될는지도 모르겠다! 그러나 그녀는 제 일을 부끄러워하지 않았으니, 뜨거운 다리미를 가져오게 하여 제 손으로 구김을 펴고, 오글오글한 주름 장식을 제자리에 잡아 주고, 필요한 자리에 새 리본을 달고, 모든 것이 마땅한 대로 되었는지 살폈다. 남자들은 제 눈을 즐겁게 하려고, 그것도 겨우 한 시간을 위하여 이런 종류의 수고가 얼마나 많이 치러지는지 좀처럼 생각하지 않는다.\n\n“어머, 엄마, 정말 좋으셔라.” 두 처녀가 들어서며 벨이 말했다. 저녁 식사 자리로 돌아갈 채비를 겨우 할 만한 시각에 맞춰 온 것이었다.\n\n“엄마는 언제나 좋으신걸.” 릴리가 말했다. “엄마, 나도 더 자주 엄마한테 이렇게 해 드릴 수 있으면 좋겠어요.” 그러고는 어머니에게 입을 맞추었다. 그러나 지주는 저녁 시각에 엄격했으므로 두 사람은 서둘러 옷을 갈아입고 다시 정원을 지나 나섰고, 어머니는 작은 다리까지 따라 나왔다.\n\n“네 삼촌이 내가 안 간다고 언짢아하시지는 않더냐?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“삼촌은 뵙지도 못했어요, 엄마.” 릴리가 말했다. “저 아래 들판까지 한참 갔다 오느라 몇 시인지도 까맣게 잊고 있었지 뭐예요.”\n\n“크리스토퍼 삼촌은 근처에 안 계셨나 봐요. 계셨으면 마주쳤을 텐데.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“그런데 나는 엄마한테 화가 났어요. 언니도 그렇지 않아? 여기 혼자 남아서 안 오시겠다니 엄마 너무하셔.”\n\n“엄마는 큰 집보다 집에 계시는 게 더 좋으신가 봐.” 벨이 아주 부드럽게 말했다. 그렇게 말하면서 벨은 어머니의 손을 잡고 있었다.\n\n“자, 잘 다녀오너라, 얘들아. 열 시에서 열한 시 사이에 오겠거니 하고 기다리마. 하지만 무슨 일이 있거든 서두르지 말고.” 그렇게 두 사람은 떠났고 미망인은 다시 혼자가 되었다. 다리에서 큰 집 뒤편으로 길이 곧게 뻗어 있어서, 그녀는 아이들이 거의 뛰다시피 사뿐사뿐 걸어가는 모습을 한두 순간 볼 수 있었다. 그러다 두 아이가 홱 방향을 틀어 테라스 계단을 오를 때 옷자락이 펄럭이는 것이 보였다. 그녀는 제 몸을 둘러싼 월계수 사이의 그 후미진 자리에서 한 발짝도 더 나가려 하지 않았으니, 딸들의 뒷모습을 지켜보는 것을 누가 볼까 해서였다. 그러나 분홍 모슬린 자락이 마지막으로 한 번 펄럭이고 시야에서 사라져 버리자, 그녀는 아이들을 따라갈 수 없다는 것이 못내 서러웠다. 그래도 그녀는 한 걸음도 앞으로 나아가지 않고 그 자리에 서 있었다. 딸들이 제 집을 나서서 시숙의 집으로 가는 것을 자기가 지켜보더라고 홉킨스가 떠벌리게 할 수는 없었다. 그녀가 왜 지켜보는지를 헤아리는 것은 홉킨스의 능력 밖의 일이었다.\n\n“자, 얘들아, 그리 이른 편은 아니구나. 네 어머니도 같이 왔으면 좋았을 것을.” 크리스토퍼 삼촌이 말했다. 이것이 이 사람의 방식이었다. 제 속마음을 제대로 알았더라면 그는 데일 부인이 오지 않는 편이 자기에게 더 낫다는 것을 스스로 인정해야 했을 것이다. 그녀가 함께 있을 때보다 없을 때 그는 제 식탁의 주인 노릇을 더 확실히 했고 마음도 더 편했다. 그런데도 그는 그녀가 오지 않는 것을 자주 서운한 일로 삼았고, 스스로도 그 서운함을 믿었다.\n\n“엄마가 피곤하셨나 봐요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“흠. 이 집에서 저 집까지가 그리 먼 것도 아닌데. 나도 피곤할 때마다 방에 틀어박히기로 한다면. 아니, 됐다. 저녁이나 먹자꾸나. 크로즈비 씨, 내 조카 릴리언을 모셔 주시겠소.” 그러고는 벨에게 제 팔을 내밀고 식당으로 걸어갔다.\n\n“삼촌이 엄마를 또 나무라시면 난 가 버릴 거예요.” 릴리가 제 짝에게 말했다. 이로써 그들이 함께 보낸 그 긴 하루 동안 모두가 아주 허물없는 사이가 되었음을 알 수 있다.\n\n데일 부인은 다리 위에 잠시 더 머물렀다가 차를 마시러 들어갔다. 식구들 저녁상에 오르기로 되어 있던 오리구이와 풋완두 대신 그녀가 양갈비나 구운 햄 따위로 무엇을 때웠는지는 굳이 캐묻지 말기로 하자. 다만 그녀가 그 저녁 끼니에 유별난 식욕을 쏟지는 않았으리라고 짐작할 수는 있겠다. 그녀는 자리에 앉으면서 책 한 권을 들었고, 아마도 소설이었을 것이다, 데일 부인은 소설을 낮잡아 볼 사람이 아니었으므로, 차를 홀짝이며 한두 쪽을 읽었다. 그러나 책은 이내 한쪽으로 밀쳐졌고, 데워 두었던 접시가 식어 버린 쟁반은 버려둔 채, 그녀는 익숙한 제 의자에 몸을 기대고 자신에 대해, 딸들에 대해 생각했다. 그리고 함께 지낸 그 몇 해 동안 자기를 진심으로 사랑해 준 그이가 살아 있었더라면 제 인생의 몫이 어떠했을까 하는 생각도 했다.\n\n좋아하는 것도 싫어하는 것도 한결같은 것이 데일 집안 사람의 유별난 천성이다. 그녀에 대한 남편의 애정은 흔들린 적이 없었다. 어찌나 그러했던지 그는 제 형이 아내를 두고 형제다운 말을 해 주지 않는다 하여 형과 다투기까지 했다. 그럼에도 두 형제는 언제나 서로를 사랑했다. 그 일들이 있은 지 이제 여러 해가 지났으나 감정은 여전히 그대로였다. 앨링턴에 처음 내려왔을 때 그녀는 지주의 호감을 얻겠다고 마음먹었으나, 그런 것을 얻는 일은 이제 가망 없다는 것을 안 지 오래였다. 아니, 이제는 그럴 마음조차 없었다. 데일 부인은 아무나 사랑할 수 있음을 하느님께 감사하는 그런 마음 무른 여자들 축에 들지 않았다. 한때는 시숙에게 애정을, 애정과 함께 가깝고 살뜰한 누이 같은 우정을 품을 수도 있었으나 이제는 그럴 수 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 차가웠고, 그녀가 다가가는 것을 끈질기게 물리쳤다. 그것이 이제 칠 년 전 일이다. 그리고 그 세월 동안 데일 부인도 적어도 그가 자기에게 그러했던 만큼은 그에게 차가웠다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 것을 견디기란 몹시 힘든 일이었다. 딸들이 삼촌을 사랑하는 것은 사리에 맞을 뿐 아니라 모든 면에서 바람직했다. 그는 아이들에게는 차갑지 않았다. 아이들에게 그는 너그럽고 다정했다. 그녀만 비켜나 준다면 그는 아이들을 제 자식처럼 제 집으로 데려갔을 것이고, 아이들은 모든 점에서 세상 앞에 그의 양녀로 섰을 것이다. 그녀가 비켜나는 편이 더 낫지 않을까?\n\n이런 물음이 마음속에서 고개를 드는 것은 가장 울적한 때뿐이었고, 그러면 그녀는 곧 정신을 차려 제 병적인 나약함을 성내며 물리치고 단호히 답을 내놓았다. 딸들 곁을 떠나는 것은 옳지 않다. 설령 삼촌이 지금보다 곱절로 나은 삼촌이라 해도, 설령 자기가 없어짐으로써 아이들이 앨링턴 전부의 상속녀가 된다 해도 그렇다. 아이들에게 무엇보다 중요한 것은 곁에 어머니가 있다는 것이 아니던가? 그리고 그 병적인 물음을, 스스로 죄스럽게 던지는 것이라고 자인하면서도 그 물음을 던지면서, 그녀는 아이들이 세상 누구보다 자기를 사랑하며 아무리 대단한 집을 가진 삼촌의 보살핌보다도 제 어머니의 쓰다듬음과 보살핌을 더 좋아하리라는 것을 모르지 않았다. 아직까지는 아이들이 세상 누구보다 자기를 사랑했다. 다른 사랑이 온다 해도 그녀는 시샘하지 않을 것이었다. 그리고 그런 사랑이 오고 또 그것이 행복한 것이라면, 자기에게도 인생의 환한 저녁이 있지 않겠는가? 아이들이 시집을 가고 그 지아비 되는 이들이 자기의 사랑과 우정과 공경을 받아 준다면, 그녀도 저 큰 집의 주검 같은 냉랭함에서 벗어나 자그마한 오두막에서 행복할 수 있을 것이고, 이따금 자기를 정말로 반겨 줄 사람들 사이로 나갈 수도 있을 것이다. 앨링턴에서 그리 멀지 않은 게스트윅에 사는 어떤 의사가 있었는데, 그녀는 한때 그이가 그 사위 자리를 채워 줄 만하다고, 참으로 사랑받을 사위가 되리라고 생각한 적이 있었다. 조용하고 아름다운 벨이 그 사람을 좋아하는 듯했고, 그 사람 쪽에서는 분명 좋아하는 듯한 정도를 넘어섰다. 그러나 이제 몇 주 전부터 이 희망은, 아니 이 짐작은 스러져 가고 있었다. 데일 부인은 이 일로 딸에게 캐물은 적이 없었다. 그녀는 그런 물음을 즐겨 던지는 여자가 아니었다. 그러나 지난 한두 달 사이에 벨이 제 어머니가 마음에 들어 하는 그 사람을 오히려 냉랭하게 대하는 것을 그녀는 안타깝게 지켜보았다.\n\n이런 생각을 하는 사이에 긴 저녁이 지나갔고, 열한 시에 그녀는 정원을 건너오는 발소리를 들었다. 젊은이들이 물론 처녀들을 집까지 바래다준 것이었다. 그녀가 아직 열려 있던 제 응접실 창문으로 나서니, 앞쪽 잔디밭 한가운데에 네 사람이 다 모여 있는 것이 보였다.\n\n“엄마다.” 릴리가 말했다. “엄마, 크로즈비 씨가 달빛 아래서 크로케를 치고 싶으시대요.”\n\n“그러기엔 빛이 모자랄 것 같은데.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“저분한테는 빛이 넉넉해요.” 릴리가 말했다. “저분은 아치문하고는 아무 상관 없이 치시거든요. 안 그래요, 크로즈비 씨?”\n\n“크로케 치기에 아주 좋은 달빛인데요.” 크로즈비 씨가 환한 달을 올려다보며 말했다. “게다가 자러 가는 건 너무 시시하지 않습니까.”\n\n“네, 자러 가는 건 시시하죠.” 릴리가 말했다. “하지만 시골 사람들은 시시하답니다, 아시겠지만요. 가스등을 켜고 밤새도록 칠 수 있는 당구가 훨씬 낫지요, 안 그래요?”\n\n“그 화살은 영 빗나갔습니다, 데일 양. 저는 큐를 잡아 본 적이 없거든요. 당구 이야기는 사촌 되시는 분께 하셔야지요.”\n\n“버나드가 당구를 아주 잘 치나요?” 벨이 물었다.\n\n“글쎄, 이따금 치기는 하지. 크로즈비가 크로케 치는 만큼은 되려나. 자, 크로즈비, 우린 돌아가서 시가나 한 대 피우세.”\n\n“네.” 릴리가 말했다. “그럼 시시한 우리는 자러 가면 되겠네요. 엄마, 우리한테도 여기 자그마한 흡연실이 하나 있으면 좋겠어요. 시시한 사람 취급받는 건 싫거든요.” 그리하여 그들은 헤어졌다. 여자들은 집 안으로 들어가고, 두 남자는 잔디밭을 가로질러 돌아갔다.\n\n“릴리야.” 다들 어머니의 침실에 모였을 때 데일 부인이 말했다. “너 크로즈비 씨한테 너무 모질게 구는 것 같더구나.”\n\n“저녁 내내 저러고 있었어요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“우린 아주 좋은 친구인걸요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“오, 아주 좋고말고!” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“저것 봐, 언니는 시샘하는 거야. 언니도 알잖아.” 그러고는 언니가 조금 언짢아하는 것을 보고 다가가 입을 맞추었다. “언니를 시샘쟁이라고 부르면 안 되죠. 그렇죠, 엄마?”\n\n“그런 소리 들을 애는 아니지.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“자, 그럼 내가 무슨 뜻이 있어서 그런 줄 아시는 건 아니죠.” 릴리가 말했다. “크로즈비 씨 따위를 내가 미나리아재비 한 송이만큼이라도 마음에 두는 것처럼요.”\n\n“나도 마찬가지야, 릴리.”\n\n“물론 언니도 아니지. 하지만 나는 저분이 아주 마음에 들어요, 엄마. 그렇게 귀여운 아폴로가 어디 있담. 나는 앞으로 늘 저분을 아폴로라고 부를 거예요. 포이보스 아폴로! 그리고 저분 초상을 그릴 때는 활 대신에 크로케 망치를 손에 들려 드릴 테야. 정말이지 저분을 여기 데려와 준 버나드에게 나는 아주 고맙게 생각해요. 그리고 저분이 모레 떠나지 않으면 얼마나 좋을까.”\n\n“모레라고!” 데일 부인이 말했다. “이틀 있자고 온 보람은 별로 없구나.”\n\n“그러게 말이에요. 우리 조용한 살림을 그렇게 휘저어 놓고는 겨우 그만큼이라니. 저분 햇살이 몇 가닥이나 되는지 헤아려 볼 틈도 안 주고요.”\n\n“하지만 아마 또 오시겠다고 하셨어.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“우리한테 그 희망은 있네.” 릴리가 말했다. “크리스토퍼 삼촌이 긴 휴가를 얻거든 내려오라고 하셨어요. 이번은 그냥 짧은 휴가고요. 저분은 가엾은 조니 임스보다 형편이 훨씬 낫죠. 조니 임스는 한 달밖에 없는데, 크로즈비 씨는 마음 내킬 때 아무 때나 두 달이나 되고, 게다가 일 년 내내 거의 제 마음대로 지내는 것 같으니까요.”\n\n“그리고 크리스토퍼 삼촌이 구월에 사냥하러 내려오라고도 하셨어.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“오시겠다고 말씀은 안 하셨지만 그럴 뜻이었던 것 같아요.” 릴리가 말했다. “우리한테 그 희망은 있네요, 엄마.”\n\n“그럼 너는 아폴로에게 크로케 망치 대신 총을 들려서 그려야겠구나.”\n\n“그게 제일 나쁜 점이에요, 엄마. 저분도 버나드도 우리는 별로 못 볼 테니까요. 우리를 몰이꾼으로 숲에 데려가 주지는 않겠죠?”\n\n“네가 하도 시끄럽게 굴 테니 쓸모가 없을 게다.”\n\n“제가요? 몰이꾼은 새들한테 소리를 질러야 하는 줄 알았는데요. 새한테 소리 지르는 건 금방 지겨워질 테니, 그럼 저는 집에 남아서 옷이나 챙기는 게 낫겠네요.”\n\n“크리스토퍼 삼촌이 저분을 저렇게 마음에 들어 하시니 나는 저분이 왔으면 좋겠어.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“게스트윅의 어떤 신사분은 저분이 오는 걸 좋아하실지 모르겠네.” 릴리가 말했다. 그러고는 그 말을 내뱉자마자 언니를 바라보았고, 언니의 마음을 아프게 했다는 것을 알았다.\n\n“릴리야, 너는 혀를 너무 함부로 놀리는구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아무 뜻도 없었어, 언니.” 릴리가 말했다. “미안해.”\n\n“괜찮아.” 벨이 말했다. “릴리는 그저 생각 없이 말을 할 뿐이에요.” 그러고서 그 이야기는 끝이 났고, 그들 사이에는 몸단장에 관한 것과 헤어지며 나눈 몇 마디 말 외에는 더 오간 것이 없었다. 그러나 두 처녀는 같은 방을 썼고, 제 방문이 닫히자 벨은 아까 있었던 일을 제법 힘주어 다시 꺼냈다.\n\n“릴리, 너 나한테 약속했잖아.” 벨이 말했다. “크로프츠 선생 이야기는 다시는 나한테 하지 않겠다고.”\n\n“알아, 내가 그랬지. 내가 아주 잘못했어. 미안해, 언니. 다시는 안 그럴게. 참을 수 있는 한은.”\n\n“참을 수 있는 한이라니, 릴리!”\n\n“하지만 정말이지 내가 왜 그분 이야기를 하면 안 되는지 모르겠어. 언니를 놀리는 식만 아니라면 말이야. 내 평생 본 남자들 가운데 나는 그분이 제일 좋아. 그리고 내가 나 자신보다 언니를 더 사랑하지만 않았다면, 언니한테서 그분의——”\n\n“릴리, 방금 뭐라고 약속했지?”\n\n“알았어, 오늘 밤 이후로는. 그리고 나는 언니가 왜 그분한테 등을 돌리는지 모르겠어.”\n\n“나는 그분한테 등을 돌린 적도, 그분 편으로 돌아선 적도 없어.”\n\n“그분한테는 돌아설 것도 없어. 언니가 웃어 주기만 한다면 그분은 왼손이라도 내놓을 거야. 오른손이라도 내놓을걸. 그리고 나는 그걸 보고 싶어. 자, 이제 들었지.”\n\n“너 지금 허튼소리 하는 거 알잖아.”\n\n“어쨌든 나는 그걸 보고 싶어. 엄마도 분명 그러실 거야. 엄마가 그분 얘기를 한마디라도 하시는 건 들은 적이 없지만 말이야. 내 눈에 그분은 내가 본 가장 훌륭한 사람이야. 아폴로 크로즈비 씨가 그분한테 댈 게 뭐야? 자, 이 얘기가 언니를 언짢게 하니 나는 이제 다시는 그분 얘기를 하지 않을게.”\n\n벨이 여느 때보다 더한 애정을 담아 동생에게 잘 자라고 인사하는 것을 보면, 릴리의 말과 그 열띤 어조가, 자기가 한 부탁과는 어긋나는 것이었음에도 어떤 식으로든 벨을 기쁘게 했음이 분명했다. 그리고 릴리도 그것을 알고 있었다.\n\n# 제4장. 로퍼 부인의 하숙집\n\n나는 존 임스를 귀여워해 준 사람이 그 어머니 말고는 없었다고 말했지만, 그렇다고 해서 존 임스에게 친구가 없었다고 여겨지기를 바라지는 않는다. 세상에는 결코 귀여움을 받지 못하는 부류의 젊은이들이 있는데, 그렇다고 그들이 존중을 덜 받거나 어쩌면 사랑을 덜 받는 것도 아니다. 그들은 아폴로의 모습으로 세상에 나서지 않으며 도무지 빛나는 일이 없고, 제게 있을지 모를 빛이란 빛은 안으로 쓰려고 간직해 둔다. 그런 젊은이들은 흔히 어색하고 볼품없으며 걸음걸이도 아직 자리 잡히지 않았다. 팔다리는 제멋대로 뻗치고 수줍음을 타며, 말이 필요한 자리에서도 늘 어울리던 무리가 아니고서는 말이 쉬 나오지 않는다. 사교 모임은 그들에게 고행의 시간이고, 사람들 앞에 나서는 일이라면 무엇이든 그들의 기를 꺾어 놓는다. 그들은 혼자 다니는 일이 많고, 여자가 말을 걸면 얼굴을 붉힌다. 사실 그들은 나이가 몇이든 아직 어른이 아니다. 그러면서도 이미 소년은 아니기에, 세상은 그들에게 풋내기라는 볼품없는 이름을 붙여 주었다.\n\n그러나 이 문제를 두고 내가 할 수 있었던 관찰에 따르면, 풋내기란 결코 인류 가운데 가장 값어치 없는 부류가 아니라고 나는 믿게 되었다. 스물한둘 먹은 풋내기를 같은 나이의 다 여문 아폴로와 견주어 볼 때, 나는 앞엣것을 덜 익은 열매로, 뒤엣것을 다 익은 열매로 여긴다. 그러면 이 두 열매를 두고 물음이 하나 생긴다. 어느 쪽이 더 나은 열매인가. 일찍 익는 열매—아마 촉성 온실 장치의 덕을 좀 보았거나, 적어도 남향 담장의 온기를 등에 업은 열매인가. 아니면 더디 자라는 열매, 자연이 아무 도움 없이 손보고 햇볕이 제 때를 골라 내리쬐는 열매—혹은 짓궂은 그늘이 끼어들도록 내버려 두었다면 끝내 햇볕이 들지 않을지도 모르는 그 열매인가? 세상은 의심할 바 없이 촉성 장치나 남향 담장 편이다. 그 열매는 확실히 열리고, 열리는 때도 정해져 있다. 흠 하나 없고 티 하나 없으며 결코 얕볼 수 없는 품질을 지닌다. 주인은 원할 때 그것을 얻고, 그것은 제 몫을 한다. 그렇지만 그럼에도 불구하고, 내 생각으로는 햇볕의 맛을 가장 그득히 머금는 쪽은 저 다른 열매다. 짓궂은 그늘이 끼어들지만 않았다면, 햇볕이 스스로 고른 좋은 때에 그 맛이 깃드는 것이다. 나는 절로 자란 것의 그 맛을 좋아하고, 어쩌면 그렇게 얻은 것이 아무 특혜 없이 얻어졌기에 더 좋아하는지도 모른다.\n\n그러나 풋내기는, 여자가 말을 걸면 얼굴을 붉히고 여자 곁에 있기만 해도 불편해하며, 무도회장에서 제 팔다리를 다스리지 못하고 어느 때고 제 혀조차 거의 다스리지 못하지만, 실은 세상에서 가장 말 잘하는 존재이며 특히 아름다운 여인들 앞에서 그러하다. 그는 돈 후안의 승리란 승리는 다 누리면서도 돈 후안의 무정함은 조금도 없고, 재치의 힘과 목소리의 감미로움으로 어떤 겨룸에서든 이길 수 있다. 다만 그 웅변은 오직 제 안의 귀에만 들리고, 그 승리는 상상 속의 승리일 뿐이다.\n\n참된 풋내기는 홀로 있는 때가 많아서 다른 풋내기들과도 그리 어울리려 들지 않는데, 이는 세상 사람들이 아직 충분히 눈여겨보지 못한 그의 성품 가운데 하나라고 나는 생각한다. 그가 아폴로가 아니라 풋내기가 된 것은 아마도 사정이 그에게 어울릴 기회를 넉넉히 주지 않았기 때문일 것이다. 그래서 그는 홀로 이리저리 거닐며 먼 길을 걷고, 그 걸음 속에서 제 힘으로는 도저히 이룰 수 없는 온갖 성공을 꿈꾼다. 손에 지팡이를 들고 들판에 나서면 그는 아주 말을 잘하여, 돋아난 여름 잡초의 머리를 쳐 내면서 힘차게 웅변을 익힌다. 그렇게 그는 그를 아는 이들이 좀처럼 인정해 주지 않는 상상력을 길러 가며, 짓궂은 그늘이 언젠가 끼어들기를 그친다면 마침내 찾아올 그 늦은 무르익음을 저도 모르게 준비하는 것이다.\n\n그런 풋내기들은 어머니에게서가 아니고서는 귀여움을 거의 받지 못한다. 게스트윅을 떠나 런던의 어느 관청 큰 방에서 제 삶을 시작하도록 보내졌을 때의 존 임스가 바로 그런 풋내기였다. 그에게 젊은 아폴로다운 구석이라고는 하나도 없었다고 해도 좋을 것이다. 그렇지만 그에게 친구가 없지는 않았다. 그가 잘되기를 바라고 그의 앞날을 깊이 생각해 주는 친구들이 있었다. 그리고 그를 몹시 아끼는 여동생이 하나 있었는데, 저 자신이 얼마쯤은 여자 풋내기였던 까닭에 오빠가 풋내기라고는 꿈에도 생각하지 못했다. 그들의 어머니인 임스 부인은 미망인으로 게스트윅의 작은 집에 살았고, 그 남편은 평생을 두고 우리 지주의 절친한 벗이었다. 그는 불운이 많은 사람이어서 거의 넉넉하게 세상살이를 시작했으나 가난 속에 그 삶을 마쳤다. 그는 평생을 게스트윅에서 살면서 한때는 넓은 땅을 부치기도 했고 시험 삼아 하는 농사에 많은 돈을 잃었다. 그리고 만년에는 읍내 변두리에 작은 집을 한 채 얻었으며, 이 이야기가 시작되기 두 해쯤 전에 거기서 죽었다. 데일 씨가 그토록 가깝게 지낸 사람은 달리 없었다. 임스 씨가 죽자 데일 씨는 그 유언의 집행인이자 그 아이들의 후견인 노릇을 했다. 뿐만 아니라 존 임스가 지금 맡고 있는 그 관직 자리도 데일 씨가 얻어 준 것이었다.\n\n그리고 임스 부인은 데일 부인과 아주 다정한 사이였고 지금도 그러했다. 지주는 임스 부인을 그리 살갑게 대한 적이 없었는데, 그 남편이 이미 마흔을 넘긴 뒤에야 그녀를 만났던 터였다. 그러나 데일 부인은 큰 집에서 베풀어졌음 직한 살가움이 모자란 만큼을 그 가엾고 외로운 여인에게 제 친절로 메워 주었다. 임스 부인은 가엾고 외로운 여인이었다. 남편이 살아 있던 시절에도 외로웠지만 과부가 된 지금은 몹시 처량했다. 중요한 일에서는 지주가 그녀에게 친절했다. 그 얼마 안 되는 돈 문제를 정리해 주고, 집과 수입에 관해 조언해 주었으며, 아들의 그 자리도 얻어 주었다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 만나면 면박을 주었고, 가엾은 임스 부인은 그를 몹시 어려워했다. 데일 부인은 시아주버니를 조금도 어려워하지 않았고, 때로는 게스트윅의 그 과부에게 지주가 한 말과는 사뭇 어긋나는 조언을 해 주기도 했다. 이런 식으로 벨과 릴리와 젊은 임스 사이에 친밀함이 자라났고, 두 아가씨 모두 조니 임스가 제 소중한 벗이라고 말할 채비가 되어 있었다. 그럼에도 그들은 이따금 그를 두고 조금은 우스개 섞인 투로 이야기했는데, 절친한 벗 가운데 풋내기를 둔 예쁜 아가씨들, 그러면서 아폴로의 우아함에도 낯설지 않은 아가씨들에게는 드문 일이 아니다.\n\n이쯤에서 곧바로 밝혀 두는 편이 낫겠다. 존 임스는 런던으로 올라갈 때 릴리 데일에게 완전히, 그리고 돌이킬 수 없이 반해 있었다. 그는 세상에서 가장 감동적인 말로 제 연정을 백 번도 넘게 고백했다. 다만 그 고백을 들은 것은 저 자신뿐이었다. 그는 릴리를 두고 시를 많이 썼지만 그 시구들을 이중 자물쇠로 단단히 잠가 두었다. 상상에 고삐를 놓아 줄 때면 그는 이 시로 그녀뿐 아니라 온 세상까지 얻을 수 있으리라고 우쭐해했다. 그러나 그것을 사람 눈에 내보이느니 차라리 죽는 편을 택했을 것이다. 게스트윅에서 보낸 마지막 열 주 동안, 런던에서의 일자리를 준비하면서 그는 앨링턴 언저리를 맴돌며 자주 걸어갔다가 다시 걸어 돌아오곤 했다. 그러나 다 헛일이었다. 그렇게 찾아가면 그는 데일 부인의 응접실에 앉아 말은 거의 하지 않고 대개 어머니 쪽에다 말을 붙였다. 하지만 그 길고 더운 길을 나설 때마다 그는 릴리가 제 사랑을 알아차릴 만한 무언가를 말하리라 마음먹었다. 런던으로 떠날 때까지 그 무언가는 끝내 말해지지 않았다.\n\n그는 그녀에게 아내가 되어 달라고 청할 생각은 꿈에도 하지 않았다. 존 임스는 한 해 팔십 파운드에 어머니 지갑에서 나오는 이십 파운드를 더 받으며 세상살이를 시작하려는 참이었다. 그런 수입으로는 런던에서 살림을 차리고 아내를 둔 사내로 자리 잡을 수 없다는 것을 그는 잘 알았고, 또 릴리를 아내로 얻는 행운을 누릴 사내라면 세상이 줄 수 있는 온갖 안락을 그녀에게 베풀 채비가 되어 있어야 한다고 느꼈다. 릴리의 사랑에 대한 어떤 다짐도 바라서는 안 된다는 것을 그는 잘 알았다. 그러나 그럼에도 제 사랑을 그녀에게 다짐해 보일 수는 있으리라고 생각했다. 아마 헛일이 되겠지만. 저 시적인 기분에 잠겨 있을 때가 아니면 그에게 참된 희망은 없었다. 그는 저 자신이 어색하고 말없고 볼품없으며 얼굴마저 아직 마무리되지 않은, 말하자면 덜 익은 풋내기에 지나지 않는다는 것을 어렴풋하게나마 인정하고 있었다. 이 모든 것을 그는 알았고, 세상에는 릴리를 저 눈부신 마차에 태워 데려가고 싶어 안달할 아폴로들이 있다는 것도 알았다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서, 한번 그녀를 사랑한 이상 참된 사내로서 끝까지 그녀를 사랑하는 것이 도리라는 결심이 흔들리지는 않았다.\n\n헤어질 때 그는 그녀에게 짤막한 말 한마디를 건넸지만, 그것은 사랑의 말이라기보다 우정의 말이었다. 그는 벨을 응접실에 혼자 남겨 둔 채 그녀를 따라 잔디밭으로 나갔다. 어쩌면 릴리는 그 소년의 마음을 얼마쯤 알아차리고, 헤어지는 마당에 다정하게, 아니 다정한 것 이상으로 말해 주고 싶었는지도 모른다. 여자들이 알아보는 말없는 사랑이 있고, 그들은 어떤 말없는 방식으로 그것을 알은체하며, 그 사랑에 딸린 존중에 대해 곱지만 소리 없는 고마움을 표한다.\n\n“작별 인사를 하러 왔어요, 릴리.” 조니 임스가 오솔길 하나를 따라 아가씨의 뒤를 좇으며 말했다.\n\n“잘 가요, 존.” 그녀가 돌아서며 말했다. “우리가 얼마나 서운해하는지 아시죠. 그래도 런던에 올라가시는 건 대단한 일이에요.”\n\n“뭐, 그렇죠. 그런 것 같아요. 그래도 저는 여기 남는 편이 더 좋겠는데요.”\n\n“어머! 여기 남아서 아무 일도 안 하고요? 정말로 그러실 리가 없어요.”\n\n“물론 무언가 하고는 싶죠. 제 말은—”\n\n“오랜 친구와 헤어지는 게 괴롭다는 말씀이겠죠. 우리도 모두 그렇게 느끼고 있어요. 그래도 가끔은 휴가가 있을 테고, 그러면 뵐 수 있잖아요.”\n\n“네, 그럼요, 그때는 뵙겠죠. 릴리, 제 생각에는 누구보다도 릴리를 보는 게 제일 마음이 쓰일 것 같아요.”\n\n“어머, 아니에요, 존. 어머니도 계시고 동생도 있잖아요.”\n\n“네, 물론 어머니와 메리가 있죠. 하지만 저는 첫날로 곧장 이리 건너올 거예요. 그러니까, 릴리가 저를 보고 싶어 한다면 말이지만요.”\n\n“우리는 아주 많이 보고 싶어 할 거예요. 아시잖아요. 그리고—존, 부디 행복하시길 바라요.”\n\n그렇게 말할 때 그녀의 목소리에는 그를 거의 무너뜨릴 뻔한 어떤 가락이 있었다. 아니, 차라리 이렇게 말해야겠다. 그를 거의 두 다리로 일어서게 하여 말을 하게 만들 뻔한 가락이었다. 그러나 그 끝에 남은 효력은 그만큼 강하지 못했다. “그래요?” 그가 몇 초 동안 행복하게 그녀의 손을 잡은 채 말했다. “저도 릴리가 언제나 행복하기를 진심으로 바라요. 잘 있어요, 릴리.” 그러고는 그녀를 두고 집 쪽으로 돌아갔고, 그녀는 걸음을 이어 관목 숲의 나무들 사이로 내려가 그 뒤 반 시간 동안 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그때 조니 임스가 릴리 데일에게 한 말보다 더는 말하지 않는 연인, 끝내 그 이상은 결코 말하지 않는 그런 연인을 가진 아가씨가 얼마나 많은가? 그러면서도 훗날 저를 사랑했던 이들의 이름을 하나하나 꼽아 볼 때, 그 어색한 젊은이의 이름만은 결코 잊히지 않는 법이다.\n\n그 작별 인사가 오간 지도 벌써 이 년 가까이 되었고, 그때 릴리 데일은 열일곱이었다. 그 뒤로 존 임스는 한 번 고향에 다녀갔고, 한 달 남짓한 휴가 동안 앨링턴을 자주 찾았다. 그러나 앞서 이야기한 그 기회보다 더 나아간 적은 한 번도 없었다. 릴리가 예전보다 자기에게 차가워진 것처럼 여겨졌고, 그는 그녀 앞에서 오히려 전보다 더 수줍어졌다. 그는 이 가을에 다시 게스트윅에 내려올 참이었다. 그러나 이 일의 진실을 정직하게 말하자면, 릴리 데일은 그가 온다는 것을 그리 마음에 두지도 대수롭게 여기지도 않았다. 열아홉 먹은 아가씨는 스물한 살 먹은 연인에게 마음을 쓰지 않는 법이다. 그 열매가 촉성 장치나 남향 담장의 덕을 본 것이 아니라면 말이다.\n\n존 임스의 사랑은 여전히 예전만큼이나 뜨거웠다. 시로 떠받쳐지고, 어쩌면 동료 서기의 귀에다 은밀히 털어놓는 고백으로 목숨을 이어 온 사랑이었다. 그렇다고 이 두 해 동안 그가 우수에 잠긴 연인으로 지냈으리라고 생각해서는 안 된다. 아마 그의 기질이 그를 그런 삶 쪽으로 이끌었더라면 그에게 더 나았을지도 모른다. 그러나 사정은 그렇지 않았다. 그는 게스트윅을 떠나기 전에 구슬픈 가락 세 개를 낼 줄 알게 되었던 그 플루트를 이미 팽개쳤고, 다섯 번째인가 여섯 번째 일요일이 지난 뒤에는 리전트 파크 운하의 예선로를 따라 홀로 걷던 산책도 그만두었다. 곁에 없는 연인을 생각하는 일은 무척 달콤하다. 그러나 예선로를 한두 마일 걷다 보면 그것도 단조로워지고, 마음은 어느새 샐리 아줌마 놀이와 크리몬 정원과 돈 문제 쪽으로 돌아서고 만다. 어떤 아가씨든 제 연인의 마음속을 전부 알게 된다면 그 마음에 만족할 수 있을지 나는 의심스럽다.\n\n“이봐, 코들, 사람이 클럽에 들어갈 수도 있을까?”\n\n이 제안은 그런 일요일 산책 가운데 어느 날, 존 임스가 제 가슴속을 털어놓는 벗이자 동료 서기에게 건넨 것이었다. 그 벗의 본래 이름은 크래들이었고, 그래서 친구들은 그를 코들이라고 불렀다.\n\n“클럽에 들어간다고? 우리 방 피셔가 클럽 회원이잖아.”\n\n“그건 체스 클럽일 뿐이지. 나는 제대로 된 클럽 말이야.”\n\n“웨스트엔드에 있는 멋쟁이 클럽 가운데 하나 말이야?” 크래들이 벗의 야심에 거의 넋을 잃고 감탄하며 말했다.\n\n“딱히 멋쟁이 클럽일 것까지는 없어. 자기가 멋쟁이가 아니라면 멋쟁이들 틈에 끼어 봐야 얻을 게 뭐가 있겠어. 하지만 로퍼 아줌마네는 어지간히도 따분하단 말이야.” 그런데 로퍼 부인은 버턴 크레슨트에서 하숙집을 치는 점잖은 부인으로, 임스 부인이 아들을 특별히 안전한 거처에 두고 싶어 했을 때 강력히 추천받은 사람이었다. 런던 생활 첫해에 존 임스는 셋방에서 혼자 지냈다. 그러나 그 결과는 불편함과 외로움이었고, 아아, 얼마간의 빚까지 생겨 그것이 가엾은 과부에게 무겁게 얹혔다. 이제 둘째 해에는 좀 더 안전한 살림 방식이 필요했다. 임스 부인은 법정 변호사의 미망인인 크래들 부인 역시 아들을 소득세 사무국에 넣는 데 성공했고 그 아들을 로퍼 부인에게 맡겼다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그리하여 그녀도 그 모성 어린 여인에게 이런저런 당부를 잔뜩 하면서 제 아들을 같은 보호 아래 맡겼다.\n\n“그리고 교회 가는 일은요?” 임스 부인이 로퍼 부인에게 그렇게 말했었다.\n\n“그것까지는 제가 돌봐 드리지 못할 것 같습니다요, 마님.” 로퍼 부인이 양심껏 대답했었다. “젊은 신사분들은 대개 제 교회를 제가 고르시지요.”\n\n“그래도 가기는 하지요?” 어머니가 물었다. 아들이 이제 많은 일을 제 판단에 맡겨 따라가야 할 그 새 삶을 두고 그녀의 마음은 몹시 불안했다.\n\n“착실하게 자란 분들은 대개 그럽니다요.”\n\n“그 애는 착실하게 자랐어요, 로퍼 부인. 정말 그렇답니다. 그리고 그 애한테 대문 열쇠는 안 주시겠지요?”\n\n“글쎄요, 다들 꼭 달라고들 하기는 합니다요.”\n\n“하지만 제가 그 애가 그걸 갖지 않기를 바란다고 말씀해 주시면 그 애도 우기지는 않을 거예요.”\n\n로퍼 부인은 그러겠다고 약속했고, 조니 임스는 그녀의 보호 아래 맡겨졌다. 그는 정말로 열쇠를 달라고 했고, 로퍼 부인은 시킨 대로 대답했다. 그러나 그는 크래들의 궤변에 물이 들어 다시 청했고, 그러자 로퍼 부인은 그에게 열쇠를 건네주었다. 그녀는 제 말을 지키는 사람이라는 것을 자랑으로 삼는 여인이었고, 누구든 그 이상을 자기에게 요구할 수 있다고는 생각하지 않았다. 그녀는 조니 임스에게 열쇠를 주었는데, 처음부터 그럴 작정이었음이 틀림없다. 로퍼 부인은 세상을 알았고, 열쇠 없는 젊은이들은 제 집에 머물러 있지 않으리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다.\n\n“어밀리아가 집에 돌아온 뒤로는 그렇게 따분해 보이지 않던데.” 크래들이 말했다.\n\n“어밀리아라니! 어밀리아가 나한테 무슨 상관이야? 크래들, 내가 너한테 전부 털어놓았는데도 나한테 어밀리아 로퍼 이야기를 할 수 있어?”\n\n“이봐, 조니—” 그는 늘 조니라고 불렸고, 그 이름은 관청까지 그를 따라왔다. 어밀리아 로퍼조차 이 일이 있기 전에 여러 번 그를 조니라고 불렀다. “요전 날 밤에 너는 세상에 L. D.라는 사람이 있지도 않은 것처럼 그 아가씨한테 살갑게 굴었잖아.” 존 임스는 고개를 돌리고 머리를 내저었다. 그럼에도 벗의 그 말은 그에게 고마웠다. 돈 후안이라는 배역은 그의 상상에 그리 싫지 않았고, 마음은 릴리언 데일에게 참되면서도 지나가는 말 몇 마디로 어밀리아 로퍼를 즐겁게 해 줄 수 있다고 생각하는 것이 좋았다. 그러나 사실인즉 그 지나가는 말들은 그보다 어여쁜 어밀리아 쪽에서 훨씬 많이 나왔다.\n\n로퍼 부인은 제 식구가 자기 자신과, 변호사 사무실에 다니는 아들과, 함께 묵고 있는 스프루스 양이라는 나이 지긋한 노처녀 사촌과, 크래들 씨로 이루어져 있다고 임스 부인에게 말했을 때 어김없이 제 말 그대로였다. 그 거룩하신 어밀리아는 그때 그녀와 함께 살고 있지 않았고, 그녀가 하고 있던 그 이야기의 성격상 그 아가씨가 아마 다음 겨울에 집으로 돌아오리라는 것까지 임스 부인에게 알려야 할 까닭은 조금도 없었다. 루펙스 씨 부부도 근래에 이 식구에 끼어들었고, 이제 로퍼 부인의 집은 다 찬 것으로 여겨졌다.\n\n그리고 조니 임스가 어느 방심한 순간에 어밀리아를 향한, 그보다 약한 두 번째 연정의 비밀을 크래들에게 털어놓은 적이 있음은 인정해야겠다. “괜찮은 아가씨야, 되게 괜찮은 아가씨라고!” 게스트윅과 앨링턴을 떠난 뒤에 배운 말투를 써서 조니 임스는 그렇게 말했다. 크래들 씨 또한 여성을 흠모하는 사람이었다. 그리고, 아아, 이런 말을 해야 하다니, 지금으로 말하면 그 흠모의 대상은 루펙스 부인이었다. 그렇다고 그가 루펙스 씨에게 몹쓸 짓을 할 생각을 털끝만큼이라도 품었다는 것은 아니다. 루펙스 씨는 무대 배경 화가였고 세상 물정을 아는 사람이었다. 크래들 씨는 그저 사내로서가 아니라 감식가로서 루펙스 부인을 흠모했다. “이야, 조니, 저 여자 몸매 좀 보라고!” 어느 날 아침 관청으로 걸어가는 길에 그가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 다리 하나는 튼튼하게 잘 섰더군.”\n\n“그렇고말고. 내가 몸매라는 걸 조금이라도 안다면,” 크래들이 말했다. “저 여자는 거의 완벽해. 저 상반신 좀 보라니까!”\n\n이 표현으로 보아, 또 루펙스 부인이 그럭저럭 내보이는 데 성공한 그 몸매라는 것이 코르셋과 크리놀린에 크게 기대고 있었다는 사실로 보아, 크래들 씨가 몸매에 대해 그리 아는 것이 없었음을 아마 짐작할 수 있으리라.\n\n“내가 보기엔 코가 그리 반듯하지 않던데.” 조니 임스가 말했다. 그런데 루펙스 부인의 얼굴에 붙은 코가 조금 비뚤어져 있었다는 것은 의심할 여지 없는 사실이었다. 그것은 길고 가느다란 코였는데, 앞으로 허공을 향해 뻗어 나가면서 분명히 왼쪽으로 기우는 쪽이 우세했다.\n\n“나는 얼굴보다 몸매를 더 치거든.” 크래들이 말했다. “그래도 루펙스 부인은 눈이 곱잖아, 아주 고운 눈이지.”\n\n“게다가 그 눈을 쓸 줄도 알고.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“쓸 줄 알면 안 될 게 뭐야? 게다가 머리카락도 사랑스럽지.”\n\n“다만 아침에는 도무지 빗질을 안 하지.”\n\n“있잖아, 나는 그런 흐트러진 차림이 좋더라.” 크래들이 말했다. “너무 공을 들이면 늘 티가 나는 법이야.”\n\n“그래도 여자는 단정해야지.”\n\n“루펙스 부인 같은 분한테 단정이라니, 그게 무슨 말이야! 나는 그분을 훌륭한 여인이라고 부르겠어. 게다가 어젯밤엔 차림새가 얼마나 근사했다고. 있잖아, 나는 루펙스가 부인을 아주 못되게 대하는 것 같아. 어제 그분이 나한테 이런 말을 한두 마디 했는데—” 그러고는 그가 말을 멈추었다. 아무리 다정한 벗에게라도 나누지 않는 속내라는 것이 있는 법이다.\n\n“내 생각엔 오히려 그 반대인 것 같은데.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“반대라니 어떻게?”\n\n“루펙스 쪽이 루펙스 부인한테 실컷 시달린다는 말이지. 나는 가끔 그 목소리만 들려도 신발 속에서 다리가 다 떨린다니까.”\n\n“나는 기개 있는 여자가 좋아.” 크래들이 말했다.\n\n“아, 나도 그래. 하지만 좋은 것도 지나치면 탈이지. 어밀리아가 나한테 말해 줬는데, 다만 너 이 말은 옮기지 마.”\n\n“물론 안 옮기지.”\n\n“루펙스가 가끔은 부인한테서 달아나야만 한다더군. 극장으로 내려가서 한 번에 이삼 일씩 거기 눌러 있는다는 거야. 그러면 부인이 데리러 가고, 그러고 나면 집 안이 온통 난리가 끝이 없다지.”\n\n“실은 그자가 술을 마시는 거야.” 크래들이 말했다. “이런, 남편이 술을 마시는 여자는 정말 딱해. 더구나 그런 여인이 말이야!”\n\n“조심해, 이 친구야. 안 그러면 곤경에 빠질 거야.”\n\n“내가 뭘 하는지는 내가 알아. 원 참, 고운 여자 하나 봤다고 정신을 놓을 내가 아니야.”\n\n“마음도 놓지 않고?”\n\n“아, 마음이라니! 나한테 그런 건 없어. 나는 여자를 그림이나 조각처럼 보거든. 언젠가는 나도 장가를 들겠지, 사내라면 다들 그러니까. 하지만 여자 하나 때문에 나를 잃을 생각은 없어.”\n\n“나는 열 번이라도 나를 잃겠어, 그—”\n\n“L. D. 말이지.” 크래들이 말했다.\n\n“정말 그러겠어. 그러면서도 나는 그녀를 결코 얻지 못하리라는 걸 알아. 나는 유쾌하게 잘 웃는 축이지. 그런데 말이야, 코들, 그 아가씨가 시집가는 날이면 나는 끝장이야. 정말로 그래.”\n\n“네 목이라도 긋겠다는 말이야?”\n\n“아니, 그러지는 않아. 그런 짓은 하지 않을 거야. 그래도 나는 끝장이야.”\n\n“시월에 거기 내려간다며. 그럼 자기를 받아 달라고 청해 보지 그래?”\n\n“한 해 구십 파운드로!” 고마우신 나라가 해마다 오 파운드씩 두 차례 그의 봉급을 올려 주었던 것이다. “한 해 구십 파운드에, 어머니가 대 주시는 이십 파운드로!”\n\n“기다려 줄 수도 있잖아. 나 같으면 두말없이 청하겠어. 아가씨를 사랑할 바에야 그런 식으로 끌고 가 봐야 아무 소용 없어!”\n\n“소용이 없기야 없지, 그건 확실해.” 조니 임스가 말했다. 그러고 나서 그들은 소득세 사무국 문 앞에 이르렀고, 저마다 제 책상으로 흩어졌다.\n\n이 짤막한 대화만 보아도, 로퍼 부인이 제 말을 지키는 사람이기는 했으나 임스 부인이 제 아들의 수호천사로 고르고 싶어 했을 만한 여인은 아니었음을 짐작할 수 있을 것이다. 그러나 내가 보기에 진실은 이렇다. 한 해 사십팔 파운드를 분기마다 나누어 받고 과부의 아들을 지켜 줄 수호천사란 런던에서 그리 쉽사리 구해지지 않는다는 것이다. 로퍼 부인은 같은 처지의 다른 이들보다 나쁠 것이 없었다. 그녀는 점잖지 못한 하숙인보다 점잖은 하숙인을 훨씬 더 반겼을 것이다. 원하는 대로 점잖은 하숙인을 구할 수만 있었다면 말이다. 루펙스 씨 부부는 도무지 그 축에 들지 못했다. 그리고 한 해 백 파운드에 그들에게 앞쪽의 큰 침실을 내주었을 때, 그녀는 자기가 잘못하고 있음을 알고 있었다. 그녀는 이미 서른이 넘은 제 딸 어밀리아 때문에도 마음이 편치 않았다. 어밀리아는 아주 영리한 젊은 여자로, 사실대로 말하자면 맨체스터의 어느 모자 가게에서 수석 점원 아가씨 노릇을 하던 사람이었다. 로퍼 부인은 임스 부인도 크래들 부인도 제 아들이 자기 딸과 어울리기를 바라지 않으리라는 것을 알았다. 그러나 어쩌겠는가? 제 자식에게 제 집의 지붕을 마다할 수도 없는 노릇이고, 그러면서도 어밀리아가 젊은 임스와 시시덕거리는 것을 볼 때면 마음이 켕겼다.\n\n“어밀리아, 저 젊은이한테 그렇게 말을 많이 붙이지 않았으면 좋겠구나.”\n\n“원 참, 엄마도.”\n\n“정말 그렇다니까. 네가 그런 식으로 굴면 하숙인 둘을 다 놓치게 될 게다.”\n\n“어떤 식으로 군다는 거예요, 엄마? 신사분이 나한테 말을 걸면 대답은 해야 하는 것 아녜요? 내 처신쯤은 나도 안다고요.” 그러고는 고개를 홱 젖혔다. 그러자 어머니는 잠자코 있었다. 어머니가 딸을 무서워했기 때문이다.\n\n# 제5장. 「L. D.에 대하여」\n\n아폴로 크로즈비는 8월 31일에 런던을 떠나 앨링턴으로 향했다. 그곳에 4주를 머무를 작정이었고, 공무의 시달림에서 두 달간 벗어나 기력을 되찾겠다는 것이 그가 내세운 명분이었으며, 그 두 달 가운데 나머지 한 달을 어디서 보낼지에 대해서는 아직 정해 둔 바가 없었다. 그를 맞아들이겠다는 초대는 열두 통도 넘게 들어와 있었다. 슈롭셔에 있는 레이디 하틀탑의 저택 문은 그가 들어서기만 하면 언제든 열려 있었다. 드 코시 백작부인은 코시 성에서 자기 일행에 합류하라고 그를 청했다. 각별한 벗인 몽고메리 돕스는 스코틀랜드에 별장을 두고 있었고, 그 밖에 그가 오기를 몹시 바라는 요트 놀이 일행도 있었다. 그러나 크로즈비 씨는 아직 그 어느 부름에도 자신을 낙찰시키지 않은 채였으니, 런던을 떠날 때 그의 앞에 잡혀 있던 확정된 약속이라고는 그를 앨링턴으로 데려가는 그것 하나뿐이었다. 10월 1일이 되면 우리 역시 조니 임스와 더불어 앨링턴에 가 있게 될 것이다. 그리고 아폴로 크로즈비는 그때까지도 그곳에 있을 터인데, 소득세 사무국에서 온 우리 벗에게는 결코 마음 편할 일이 아니었다.\n\n연차 휴가라는 문제에 관한 한 조니 임스를 운 나쁜 사람이라 부를 수는 없다. 그는 10월에 런던을 떠나도 좋다는 허락을 받았고, 10월이란 시내에 남아 있다고 스스로 인정하려 드는 이가 거의 없는 달이기 때문이다. 나로 말하자면 휴가를 보내기에 가장 좋은 달은 언제나 5월이라고 여긴다. 하지만 런던 사람이라면 5월에 자리를 비우고 싶어 하는 법이 없다. 젊은 임스는 버턴 크레슨트에 살았고 아직 웨스트엔드와는 아무 연줄도 없었지만, 이 점에 관해서만은 이미 배울 것을 다 배워 두었다. “큰 방에 있는 그 작자들이 나더러 5월에 가라더군,” 그는 벗 크래들에게 그렇게 말한 적이 있었다. “나를 어지간히 어수룩한 놈으로 아는 모양이야.”\n\n“너무하잖아,” 크래들이 말했다. “5월에 휴가를 가라고 사람한테 요구할 일이 아니지. 나는 한 번도 그런 적 없고, 더구나 앞으로도 절대 그럴 생각이 없어. 그러느니 위원회에 올라가고 말겠어.”\n\n임스는 위원회까지 올라가지 않고도 이 재난을 피했고, 자기 휴가로 10월을 얻어 내는 데 성공했다. 10월은 온갖 달 가운데서도 휴가를 보내기에 가장 높이 쳐주는 달일 것이다. “내일 밤 우편 열차로 내려가려고요,” 그는 떠나기 전날 저녁 어밀리아 로퍼에게 그렇게 말했다. 그때 그는 로퍼 부인네 뒤쪽 응접실에서 어밀리아와 단둘이 앉아 있었다. 앞방에서는 크래들이 루펙스 부인과 이야기를 나누고 있었으나, 스프루스 양이 그들과 함께 있었으니 루펙스 씨로서는 질투할 까닭이 조금도 없었으리라고 짐작해도 좋았다.\n\n“그러시겠죠,” 어밀리아가 말했다. “그 매혹적인 고장으로 내려가고 싶어 얼마나 급하신지 잘 알아요. 버턴 크레슨트를 떠나느라 서두르시는 마당에 단 한 시간이라도 허비하실 리는 없겠죠.”\n\n어밀리아 로퍼는 키가 크고 몸이 잘 자란 젊은 여자로, 머리와 눈이 검었다. 미인이라고는 할 수 없었으니 코가 두툼했고 얼굴 아랫부분이 무거웠기 때문이다. 그렇다고 여자다운 매력이 전혀 없지는 않았다. 눈은 반짝였다. 다만 그 눈은 짓궂기도 했다. 말은 제법 거침없이 잘했다. 다만 잔소리도 잘했다. 어떤 때는 비둘기의 깃털을 두른 듯싶다가도, 또 어떤 때는 성난 솔개처럼 깃을 곤두세울 줄도 알았다. 존 임스가 어밀리아 로퍼와는 얽히지 말았어야 했다는 것을 나는 기꺼이 인정한다. 하지만 젊은 사내들이란 해서는 안 될 일을 얼마나 자주 저지르고 마는가!\n\n“런던에서 열두 달을 지내고 나면 제 벗들이 있는 곳으로 훌쩍 떠나는 게 반가운 법이죠,” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“당신 벗들이라고요, 임스 씨! 어떤 벗들인데요? 내가 모를 줄 아세요?”\n\n“글쎄요, 모르실걸요. 아신다고는 생각하지 않는데요.”\n\n“L. D.!” 어밀리아가 말했다. 이는 릴리의 이름이 결코 들어서는 안 될 사람들 사이에서 입에 오르내렸다는 뜻이었다. 하지만 어쩌면 버턴 크레슨트에 알려진 것이라고는 그 머리글자 두 자뿐이었는지도 모른다. 지금 그 글자를 부르는 말투로 보아, 어밀리아가 그 두 글자의 존재 자체를 자신에 대한 부당한 처사로 여기고 있다는 것만은 넉넉히 드러났다.\n\n“L. S. D.죠,” 조니가 재치 있고 쾌활한 젊은 난봉꾼 흉내를 내며 말했다. “그게 내 사랑입니다. 파운드, 실링, 펜스 말이에요. 게다가 여간 새침한 애인이 아니라니까요.”\n\n“실없는 소리 마세요. 나한테 그런 식으로 말하지 마시라고요. 내가 당신 마음이 어디 가 있는지 모르는 줄 아세요? 시골에 L. D.를 두고 있으면서 무슨 자격으로 나한테 말을 걸었나요?”\n\n가엾은 존 임스를 위하여 여기서 한마디 밝혀 두어야겠다. 그는 어밀리아에게 그런 말을 한 적이 없었다. 적어도 그녀의 말이 뜻하는 듯한 그런 투로 말한 적은 없었다. 그러나 그는 그녀에게 치명적인 쪽지 한 장을 써 보낸 일이 있었으니, 그 이야기는 머지않아 더 하게 될 것이다. 그리고 그것은 아마 말을 한 것이나 매한가지로 나빴을 터이다. 아니, 더 나빴을 것이다.\n\n“하하하!” 조니가 웃었다. 그러나 그 웃음은 꾸며 낸 것이었고, 게다가 편안하게 꾸며 낸 것도 아니었다.\n\n“그래요, 당신한테야 웃을 일이겠죠. 사내가 이런 판에 웃기란 아주 쉬운 일이죠. 그러니까 하는 말이지만, 마음이라고는 아예 없는 사람이라면 말이에요. 가슴속에 살과 피 대신 돌덩이가 들어앉은 사람이라면요. 어떤 사내들은 돌로 만들어져서 아무런 감정에도 시달리지 않는다는 걸 나도 알아요.”\n\n“나더러 대체 무슨 말을 하라는 겁니까? 다 알고 계신다면서요. 내가 아니라고 대들면 예의가 아니겠죠.”\n\n“내가 뭘 바라느냐고요? 내가 뭘 바라는지 잘 아시잖아요. 아니, 그보다 나는 아무것도 바라지 않아요. 나한테 무슨 상관이겠어요? L. D.가 나하고 무슨 상관이냐고요. 앨링턴에 내려가서 좋을 대로 하세요, 나는 아무래도 좋으니까. 다만 나는 그런 수작이 싫을 뿐이에요.”\n\n“무슨 수작 말입니까, 어밀리아?”\n\n“무슨 수작이냐고요! 자, 들어 봐요, 조니. 나는 어떤 사내를 위해서도 바보 노릇 할 생각이 없어요. 석 달 전에 내가 이 집에 돌아왔을 때, 아 정말이지 돌아오지 말걸 그랬어요.” 여기서 그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추고 다정한 한마디를 기다렸다. 그러나 다정한 한마디가 나오지 않자 그녀는 말을 이었다. “하지만 내가 정말 돌아왔을 때, 나는 온 런던을 뒤져도 내가 마음 쓸 만한 사내는 하나도 없을 줄 알았어요. 정말 그렇게 알았다고요. 그런데 이제 당신은 떠나면서, 나한테 말 한마디 제대로 건네지도 않는군요.” 그러고서 그녀는 손수건을 꺼냈다.\n\n“내내 나를 야단만 치시는데 무슨 말을 하란 말입니까?”\n\n“야단이라고요! 게다가 나까지 그런다고요! 아니에요, 조니, 나는 야단치는 게 아니고, 그럴 마음도 없어요. 우리 사이가 다 끝난 거라면 그렇다고 한마디만 하세요. 당신이 돌아오기 전에 내가 이 집에서 나가 버릴 테니까. 나는 당신한테 숨긴 게 하나도 없어요. 나는 맨체스터의 내 일자리로 돌아가면 그만이에요. 내 출신에 걸맞지 않고 여태 익숙하던 것도 아니지만 말이에요. L. D.가 나보다 당신한테 더 중하다면, 나는 당신 앞길을 막지 않겠어요. 그저 한마디만 하세요.”\n\nL. D.는 그에게 어밀리아 로퍼보다 더 중했다. 열 배는 더 중했다. L. D.는 그에게 전부였을 것이고, 어밀리아 로퍼는 아무것도 아닌 것만도 못했다. 그는 그 순간 이 모든 것을 느꼈고, 자신을 자유롭게 해 줄 만한 용기를 그러모으려고 안간힘을 썼다.\n\n“한마디만 하세요.” 그녀가 그의 앞에서 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. “그러면 당신과 나 사이의 모든 일이 끝나는 거예요. 나는 당신의 약속을 받아 두었지만, 그걸 빌미로 삼는 짓은 경멸해요. 어밀리아가 당신의 마음을 얻지 못했다면, 당신의 손을 잡는 일도 하찮게 여길 거예요. 다만 나는 대답을 들어야겠어요.”\n\n빠져나갈 손쉬운 길이 그에게 주어진 듯싶었다. 그러나 그 부인은 자기가 내미는 길이 존 임스 같은 사내에게는 결코 손쉽지 않다는 것을 아마 알고 있었을 것이다.\n\n“어밀리아,” 그가 여전히 자리에 앉은 채로 말했다.\n\n“네, 말씀하세요.”\n\n“내가 당신을 사랑하는 거 알잖아요.”\n\n“그럼 L. D.는요?”\n\n“크래들이 당신 머릿속에 집어넣는 그 헛소리를 다 믿겠다면 나로서도 어쩔 수 없죠. 글자 두 자를 가지고 스스로 질투하겠다면, 그건 내 잘못이 아니에요.”\n\n“그럼 나를 사랑하는 거예요?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“물론 당신을 사랑하죠.” 그리고 이 말을 듣자마자 어밀리아는 그의 품으로 몸을 던졌다.\n\n두 방 사이의 접이문은 닫혀 있지 않았고 스프루스 양은 바로 맞은편 안락의자에 앉아 있었으니, 그녀가 그 광경을 보았을 공산이 컸다. 그러나 스프루스 양은 말수가 적은 노부인이어서 놀라움이나 감탄을 좀처럼 겉으로 드러내는 법이 없었다. 게다가 지난 열두 해 동안 로퍼 부인과 함께 살아왔으니, 그 딸의 수법쯤은 아마 익히 알고 있었을 것이다.\n\n“나한테 변치 않을 거죠?” 그 포옹의 순간에 어밀리아가 말했다. “나한테 영원히 변치 않을 거죠?”\n\n“아, 그럼요. 그야 두말할 것도 없죠,” 조니 임스가 말했다. 그러자 그녀는 그를 놓아주었고, 두 사람은 앞쪽 거실로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어갔다.\n\n“어머, 임스 씨,” 루펙스 부인이 말했다. “오시니 반갑네요. 여기 크래들 씨가 하도 해괴한 말씀을 하셔서요.”\n\n“해괴한 말이라니요!” 크래들이 말했다. “자, 스프루스 양, 당신께 여쭙겠습니다. 제가 해괴한 말을 한 게 있습니까?”\n\n“그런 게 있었대도 나는 못 알아들었습니다,” 스프루스 양이 말했다.\n\n“그럼 내가 알아들었죠,” 루펙스 부인이 말했다. “크래들 씨처럼 장가도 안 든 남자가 유부녀의 머리에 얹힌 게 모자인지 제 머리카락인지 알아야 할 까닭이 어디 있느냐 말이에요. 안 그래요, 임스 씨?”\n\n“저는 그런 걸 알아본 적이 없는 것 같은데요,” 조니가 말했다. 루펙스 부인을 빈정댈 뜻은 조금도 없었다.\n\n“그러시겠죠,” 그 부인이 말했다. “당신 눈길이 어디에 못 박혀 있는지야 우리 모두 알고 있으니까요. 아가씨, 만약 아가씨가 모자를 쓴다면 누군가는 그 차이를 아주 금세 알아볼걸요. 그렇지 않겠어요, 스프루스 양?”\n\n“그렇겠지요,” 스프루스 양이 말했다.\n\n“제가 부인만큼 모자가 잘 어울린다면 내일이라도 하나 쓰겠어요, 루펙스 부인,” 어밀리아가 말했다. 지금 이 자리에서 그 유부녀와 다투고 싶지는 않았던 것이다. 그러나 루펙스 부인과 로퍼 양이 서로에게 결코 그렇게 상냥하지 않은 때도 있었다.\n\n“루펙스는 모자를 좋아합니까?” 크래들이 물었다.\n\n“내가 깃털 투구를 머리에 얹고 있어도 그이는 차이를 모를 거예요. 아니, 내 머리통이 통째로 없어져도 모를걸요. 시집을 가면 그렇게 되는 거랍니다. 내 말을 들으시려거든, 로퍼 양, 지금 그대로 계세요. 누가 그 일로 가슴이 찢어지는 한이 있더라도 말이에요. 안 그래요, 스프루스 양?”\n\n“아이고, 나로 말하면 늙은이 아닙니까,” 스프루스 양이 말했고, 그것은 분명 사실이었다.\n\n“여자가 시집가서 얻는 게 뭔지 나는 도무지 모르겠어요,” 루펙스 부인이 말을 이었다. “하지만 남자는 전부를 얻지요. 남자란 도와줄 여자가 없으면 사는 법도 모르는 것들이거든요.”\n\n“하지만 사랑은 아무것도 아니란 말입니까?” 크래들이 말했다.\n\n“아이고, 사랑이라니! 나는 사랑을 안 믿어요. 한때는 나도 사랑을 한다고 여겼겠지요. 그런데 결국 그게 뭐가 됐나요? 자, 여기 임스 씨를 보세요. 저이가 사랑에 빠진 건 우리 모두 알잖아요.”\n\n“저는 타고나기를 그렇게 타고났습니다, 루펙스 부인. 날 때부터 그랬어요,” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 여기 로퍼 양도요. 숙녀 이야기를 함부로 입에 올릴 일은 아니지만, 어쩌면 이 아가씨도 사랑에 빠졌을 거예요.”\n\n“댁 이야기나 하세요, 루펙스 부인,” 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“그렇게 말한다고 해로울 게 있나요? 정말이지, 아가씨가 아니라면 아가씨는 어지간히 모진 사람이에요. 세상에 참된 연인이라는 게 있다면 아가씨한테는 아가씨만의 연인이 하나 있으니 말이에요. 어머나! 저거 계단에 루펙스 발소리 아니에요! 이 시각에 무슨 일로 집에 들어온담? 술이라도 마셨으면 들어오자마자 어지간히 성을 낼 텐데.” 그러고 나서 루펙스 씨가 방으로 들어섰고, 그 자리의 흥겨움은 깨져 버렸다.\n\n크래들 부인도 임스 부인도 자기 아들이 어떤 위험에 빠져들지 알았더라면 그 아이들을 버턴 크레슨트에 두지는 않았으리라고 해도 좋을 것이다. 두 젊은이 다 신중하지 못했음은 인정해야겠으나, 둘 다 상대편의 경솔함만은 훤히 보고 있었다. 이 일이 있기 채 일주일도 전에 크래들은 자기 벗에게 로퍼 양의 술수를 조심하라고 정색하고 경고한 참이었다. “이봐 조니, 자네 그 처녀한테 단단히 얽히고 말 거야.”\n\n“누구나 한 번쯤은 그런 일을 겪게 마련이지,” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“그렇지. 하지만 너무 깊이 겪은 사람은 두 번 다시 못 빠져나와. 그 여자가 자네한테서 혼인 약속을 글로 받아 두기라도 하면 자네 신세가 어떻게 되겠나?”\n\n가엾은 조니는 여기에 곧바로 대답하지 못했다. 실은 어밀리아 로퍼가 바로 그런 문서를 손에 쥐고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n“내 신세가 어떻게 되겠느냐고?” 그가 말했다. “혼인 약속 위반 소송 축에 끼겠지 뭐.”\n\n“그렇게 되든지, 아니면 결혼의 희생자 축에 끼든지. 내가 자네를 보기에는, 자네가 그런 약속을 했다면 자네는 그걸 지킬 사람이야.”\n\n“그럴지도 모르지,” 조니가 말했다. “하지만 나도 모르겠어. 그런 경우에 사내가 어떻게 해야 옳은지는 아리송한 문제야.”\n\n“하지만 아직 그런 일은 없었단 말이지?”\n\n“저런, 그럼 없고말고!”\n\n“내가 자네라면, 조니, 나는 그 여자를 멀리하겠어. 물론 그런 일이야 아주 재미가 좋지. 하지만 얼마나 위험한지 몰라! 그런 처녀를 아내로 두고 나면 자네 신세가 지금 어떻게 되겠나?”\n\n크래들이 자기 벗에게 준 경고란 그런 것이었다. 그리고 이제 앨링턴으로 떠나려는 참에 임스가 그 답례를 했다. 두 사람은 함께 패딩턴의 그레이트웨스턴 역까지 갔고, 조니는 나란히 승강장을 오르내리며 자기 충고를 건넸다.\n\n“이봐 코들, 이 친구야, 자네 그 루펙스 부인 때문에 곤경에 빠질 거야. 몸조심하지 않으면 말이지.”\n\n“하지만 나는 몸조심할 걸세. 유부녀와의 실없는 장난만큼 안전한 게 또 어디 있나. 물론 그 여자와 나 사이에 아무 뜻도 없는 일이고 말고.”\n\n“나도 무슨 뜻이 있다고는 생각지 않아. 하지만 그 여자는 늘 루펙스가 질투한다는 말을 입에 달고 살잖나. 그 사람이 거칠게 나오기라도 하면 자네도 유쾌하진 않을걸.”\n\n그러나 크래들은 아무 위험도 없다고 여기는 듯했다. 루펙스 부인과의 그 자그마한 일은 어디까지나 정신적인 것이고 안전했다. 정말로 나쁜 짓을 저지르기로 말하면, 그가 벗에게 장담했듯이 그의 지조가 너무 높았다. 루펙스 부인은 재능 있는 여인인데 아무도 그녀를 이해하지 못하는 듯싶었고, 그래서 그는 그녀의 성격을 연구하는 데 얼마간 재미를 붙였을 뿐이었다. 그것은 그저 성격 연구일 뿐 그 이상은 아무것도 아니었다. 그러고서 두 벗은 헤어졌고, 임스는 밤 우편 열차에 실려 게스트윅으로 내려갔다.\n\n새벽 네 시에 그의 어머니가 어떻게 그를 맞으려고 일어나 있었는지, 그의 걸음걸이가 나아진 것과 구레나룻이 그에게 얹어 준 사내다운 풍채를 보고 그 어머니의 마음이 어떻게 기뻐 뛰었는지, 나는 길게 늘어놓지 않아도 되리라. 풋내기의 여러 표징이 그에게서 떨어져 나갔으니, 이제는 릴리 데일마저도 그가 더는 소년이 아니라고 아마 인정할 만했다. 이 모든 것을 좋게 여길 수 있을 터인데, 다만 어린아이의 것을 버리면서 그가 집어 든 것들이 어린아이의 것보다 나은 경우에나 그러했다.\n\n도착한 바로 그 첫날 그는 앨링턴으로 건너갔다. 이번에는 지난날의 행복하던 시절처럼 걸어가지 않았다. 길바닥 먼지를 장화에 묻히고 한낮의 열기를 이마에 얹은 채 데일 부인의 응접실로 들어서는 것은 좋지 않으리라는 생각이 들었던 것이다. 그래서 그는 말을 한 필 빌려 타고 갔는데, 피커딜리에서 사 둔 박차 한 벌과 이 자리를 위해 새로 꺼낸 새끼염소 가죽 장갑이 얼마간 자랑스러웠다. 아, 아! 런던에서 보낸 그 두 해가 존 임스를 낫게 만들지는 못했구나 싶다. 그러면서도 나는 존 임스가 내 이야기의 주인공 가운데 하나라는 것을 인정해야만 한다.\n\n데일 부인의 응접실로 들어서면서 그는 데일 부인과 그 맏딸을 보았다. 그 순간 릴리는 거기 없었고, 그래서 그는 다른 두 사람과 악수를 하면서 당연하게도 릴리는 어디 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n“정원에 나가 있을 뿐이에요,” 벨이 말했다. “곧 올 거예요.”\n\n“크로즈비 씨하고 큰 집까지 걸어갔단다,” 데일 부인이 말했다. “하지만 거기 머물러 있지는 않을 거야. 자네를 보면 얼마나 반가워하겠니, 존! 오늘 온다고 우리 모두 기다리고 있었단다.”\n\n“그러셨어요?” 조니가 말했다. 크로즈비 씨의 이름이 나오는 순간 그의 심장은 찬물 속으로 곤두박질쳐 있었다. 그는 철도 승강장에서 벗과 헤어진 뒤로 줄곧 릴리언 데일을 생각하고 있었다. 그리고 내 이야기를 읽어 줄 모든 숙녀들께 감히 장담하건대, 릴리를 향한 그의 사랑은 로퍼 양과의 그 불경한 관계로 인해 깃털 하나 빠지지 않은 채였다. 이 대목에서 내가 믿음을 얻지 못하리라는 것은 나도 안다. 그러나 사실이 그러했다. 어밀리아 로퍼 같은 여자에게 넘어가 애정을 고백하는 말까지 흘리고 다녔으면서도, 그의 마음은 지금도 언제나 릴리언에게 참되었다. 그는 밤새도록, 그리고 아침 내내 릴리와 만날 일을 생각하고 있었는데, 이제 그녀가 낯선 신사와 단둘이 정원을 거닐고 있다는 말을 들은 것이다. 크로즈비 씨가 아주 훌륭하고 아주 멋들어진 사람이라는 이야기는 들은 적이 있었으나, 그 밖에는 그에 관해 아는 것이 없었다. 어째서 크로즈비 씨가 릴리 데일과 함께 걷도록 허락받는단 말인가? 그리고 어째서 데일 부인은 그 일이 지극히 당연한 일이라도 되는 양 입에 올린단 말인가? 여기 서린 수수께끼는 아주 빠르게 풀렸다.\n\n“자네처럼 소중한 벗한테 무슨 일이 있었는지 알려 준다고 릴리가 싫어하지는 않을 게다,” 데일 부인이 말했다. “그 애가 크로즈비 씨와 혼인하기로 약혼했단다.”\n\n조니의 심장이 곤두박질쳤던 그 물이 이제 그의 머리 위까지 차올라 그를 말문이 막힌 채로 남겨 두었다. 릴리 데일이 크로즈비 씨와 혼인하기로 약혼했다니! 그 소식을 들었을 때 자기가 무슨 말이든 했어야 한다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 침묵의 순간이 흘러갈수록 그것이 앞에 앉은 두 여인에게 자신의 비밀을 일러바치고 있다는 것도, 그리고 그 비밀이란 이제 온 세상에 감추어야 할 것이 되었다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러나 그런데도 그는 입을 뗄 수가 없었다.\n\n“우리 모두 이 혼사를 아주 기뻐하고 있단다,” 데일 부인이 말했다. 그를 봐주고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n“크로즈비 씨보다 더 좋은 분은 없을 거예요,” 벨이 말했다. “우리는 자주 당신 이야기를 했어요. 그분도 당신을 알게 되면 아주 기뻐할 거예요.”\n\n“저를 알아 봐야 뭐 알 것도 없을걸요,” 조니가 말했다. 이 몇 마디 어이없는 말을 하는 동안에도, 무슨 말이든 해야 했기에 내뱉은 그 말을 하는 동안에도, 그의 목소리는 달라져 있었다. 이 순간 자신을 다스릴 수만 있다면 그는 온 세상이라도 내주었을 테지만, 자기가 여지없이 무너졌다는 것을 느낄 뿐이었다.\n\n“저기 릴리가 잔디밭을 가로질러 오는구나,” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그럼 저는 가 보는 게 낫겠습니다,” 임스가 말했다. “그 이야기는 아무 말씀도 마세요. 제발 부탁입니다.” 그러고서 그는 다른 말은 기다리지도 않고 응접실 밖으로 달아났다.\n\n# 제6장. 아름다운 나날\n\n나는 아직 벨과 릴리언 데일이 어떤 처녀들인지 한 번도 그려 보이지 않았음을 잘 알고 있으며, 그 일을 미루면 미룰수록 어려움이 커진다는 것 또한 잘 알고 있다. 아무런 묘사 없이도 이만큼은 이해되었으면 싶다. 두 사람은 머리 빛깔이 밝은 예쁜 처녀들이었고, 그중 벨이 키가 더 크고 더 예뻤으며, 릴리는 언니에 거의 못지않게 예뻤을뿐더러 어쩌면 사람을 끄는 힘은 더 있었다고.\n\n두 사람은 머리 빛깔이 밝은 처녀들로 서로 무척 닮았으며, 내 마음의 눈앞에는 그 또렷한 초상이 걸려 있으나, 그것을 남들에게도 또렷하게 보이도록 그려 낼 재간은 아무래도 내게 없을 듯싶다. 두 사람은 보통 키보다 조금 작았고, 몸의 모든 선이 가늘고 여렸다. 둘 중에서는 릴리가 더 작았으나 그 차이가 하도 미미해서 나란히 서 있지 않고서는 좀처럼 떠오르지 않았다. 그리고 벨이 더 예뻤다고 내가 말했을 때, 어쩌면 벨의 이목구비가 동생의 것보다 더 반듯했다고만 말하는 편이 더 옳았을지도 모른다. 두 처녀는 살결이 무척 희어서, 그 흰빛을 누그러뜨리는 부드러운 혈색은 또렷이 보인다기보다 그저 거기 있다고 인정되는 정도였다. 그것은 분명 거기 있어서 건강하다는 이야기를 제 나름으로 들려주었으니, 만일 그것이 없었다면 지금의 병이나 다가올 병을 이야기했을 터이다. 그러면서도 그 두 사람의 볼에 도는 빛깔을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵 말할 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 두 처녀의 머리카락은 빛깔과 결이 어찌나 닮았는지, 어머니조차도 다르다고 말할 수 없었다. 아마빛 머리는 아니었으되 매우 밝았다. 적갈색 쪽으로 기운 것도 아니었으나, 그 사이로 금빛이 감돌아 그것만의 또렷한 광채를 주었다. 다만 벨 쪽이 릴리보다 숱이 많아서, 릴리는 늘 제 머리가 성글고 볼품없다고 말하며 언니의 머리가 얼마나 아름다운지를 들려주곤 했다. 그러나 릴리의 머리도 언니 못지않게 사랑스러웠다. 두상의 생김이 완벽했고, 두 사람이 다 같이 하고 다니던 수수한 땋은 머리는 숱이 넘치도록 많을 것을 요구하지 않았기 때문이다. 두 사람의 눈은 맑은 푸른빛이었다. 다만 벨의 눈은 길고 부드럽고 순해서 상대의 얼굴로 들어 올려질 엄두조차 잘 내지 못했고, 릴리의 눈은 그보다 둥글되 더 반짝였으며, 용기가 모자라서 제가 보고 싶은 곳에 머무르기를 그만두는 일이란 좀처럼 없었다. 그리고 릴리의 얼굴은 언니의 것보다 갸름한 맛이 — 완벽한 달걀형에 가까운 맛이 — 아마 덜했을 것이다. 이마의 생김은 내 생각에 같았으나, 벨 쪽이 턱이 조금 더 가늘고 섬세했다. 그러나 벨의 턱에는 아무 자국이 없었던 데 반해 동생의 턱에는 보조개가 있어서, 그 아름다움에서 모자란 것이 무엇이든 넉넉히 메우고도 남았다. 벨의 치아는 동생의 것보다 고르게 났으나, 대신 벨은 이를 더 자주 드러냈다. 입술은 더 얇았고, 내 생각에는 아무래도 표정이 덜 실려 있었다. 코는 그 아름다움이 확실히 더 반듯했으니, 릴리의 코는 마땅한 정도보다 다소 넓적했기 때문이다. 그러니 집안에서 미인으로 꼽히는 쪽은 벨이었으리라고 이해하면 되겠다.\n\n그러나 이 처녀들이 주는 전체적인 인상과 그 자태 전반의 분위기에는, 이목구비의 절대적인 어여쁨이나 몸매의 우아함에 담긴 것보다 아마 더 많은 것이 담겨 있었다. 두 사람에게는 뻣뻣함이나 오만이라고는 조금도 없는 품위 있는 태도가 있었고, 티를 내지 않는 처녀다운 조심성이 있었다. 두 사람에게서는, 여자가 제 안에 뒤섞여 있는 순결과 연약함에 저도 모르게 기대는 데서 얻어야 마땅한 그 안심의 기색이 늘 드러나 보였다. 이 두 처녀는 남자를 두려워하는 법이 없었다. 두려워하는 것처럼 보이는 법도 없었다. 그리고 내가 말하는 그런 종류의 두려움을 품을 까닭이 두 사람에게는 거의 없었다고 해 두어도 좋겠다. 둘 중 누구인가가 어떤 남자에게 몹쓸 대접을 받는 일은 있을 수 있었겠으나, 둘 중 누구인가가 남자에게 모욕을 당하는 일은 좀처럼 있을 수 없었다. 릴리는, 어쩌면 이미 보셨을 터이지만, 장난기가 넘칠 수 있는 사람이었다. 그러나 그 장난 속에서도 그녀는 자기가 마땅히 받아야 할 대접이 무엇인지를 누구도 잊어버릴 만한 처신을 결코 하지 않았다.\n\n그리고 이제 릴리 데일은 약혼한 몸이 되었고, 장난스러운 나날은 끝이 났다. 그녀에게 내리는 이 선고가 서글프게 들리기는 하나, 나로서는 그것을 참말로 여길 수밖에 없을까 두렵다. 그리고 그것이 참말이라고 생각할 때 — 처녀 시절의 발랄함과 새끼 고양이 같은 재롱이 처녀가 언약을 준 순간 끝나야 마땅하고 또 대개 끝나 버리는 것을 볼 때 — 여자들의 세계가 혼인을 향해 이토록 서두른다는 사실이 내게는 안타깝게 다가온다. 그러나 나는 그 폐단에 내놓을 처방이 없고, 실은 그 폐단이, 폐단이라는 것이 있다손 치더라도, 내가 쓴 말로는 제대로 표현되지 않았음을 알고 있다. 서두르는 대상은 혼인이 아니라 사랑이다. 그러고 나면 일단 얻어진 사랑을 혼인이 제 것으로 낚아채고, 그렇게 폐단이 완성되는 것이다.\n\n그리고 릴리 데일이 약혼한 상대는 어돌퍼스 크로즈비였다 — 그녀가 여전히 그렇게 부르며 그 작은 농담을 그의 귀에만 들려주던, 아폴로 크로즈비였다. 그리고 그녀에게 그는 정말로 아폴로였으니, 사랑받는 남자란 그를 사랑하는 처녀에게 마땅히 그런 존재여야 하는 법이다. 그는 잘생기고, 몸가짐이 우아하고, 영리하고, 자신만만했으며, 그녀가 쾌활하기를 청하면 언제나 쾌활했다. 그러면서도 그에게는 좀 더 진지한 순간들이 있어서, 진지한 문제들을 그녀와 이야기할 줄도 알았다. 그는 그녀에게 책을 읽어 주었고, 그때까지 그녀의 어린 지혜로는 너무 어려웠던 것들을 풀어 설명해 주었다. 목소리 또한 듣기 좋았고 잘 다스려져 있었다. 애처로움이 필요하면 애처로울 수 있었고, 그녀 자신의 웃음만큼이나 흥겹게 울릴 수도 있었다. 그러한 처녀가 일단 제 마음속으로 그를 사랑한다고 인정한 마당에, 그러한 남자가 그 처녀의 아폴로가 되기에 마땅하지 않았겠는가?\n\n그녀는 제 마음속으로 그것을 인정했고, 또한 그에게도 인정했다 — 아마 독자들은 그리 오래 끌지도 않고서라고 말하리라. 그러나 구애가 그런 식으로 이어졌으니 오래 끌 필요가 없었던 것이다. 온 세상이 그 일에 미소를 지었다. 크로즈비 씨가 처음 앨링턴에서 버나드의 손님으로 그들 사이에 끼어들었던 그 짧은 첫 방문의 며칠 동안에는, 그가 주로 눈여겨보는 쪽이 벨인 듯이 보였다. 그리고 벨은 제 나름의 조용한 방식으로 그의 찬탄을 받아들이며 그것을 입에 올리지도, 그다지 마음에 두지도 않았다. 릴리는 그때 마음이 자유로웠고, 여태 늘 그러했다. 사랑의 날개가 드리우는 첫 그림자조차 아직 그 가슴의 깨끗한 서판 위에는 던져진 적이 없었다. 벨은 그렇지 않았다 — 엄밀히 따지자면 그렇지 않았다. 벨의 이야기 또한 언젠가 해야겠지만, 이 지면에서는 아니다. 그러나 크로즈비가 그들 사이에 끼어들기 전에, 자기가 느꼈던 그런 사랑은 이겨 내고 없애 버려야 한다는 것이 그녀의 마음속에 굳게 정해져 있었다. 그것은 이미 이겨 내고 없애 버린 것이었으니, 그녀가 이 새로운 아폴로의 맹세에 귀를 기울였다 한들 조금도 죄가 되지 않았으리라고 우리는 말할 수 있다. 그런 남자가 그런 처녀들 가운데 어느 쪽의 사랑이든 얻을 수 있었으리라는 것은 생각하기에 거의 서글픈 일이나, 나는 사실이 그러했음을 인정하지 않을 수 없을까 두렵다. 아폴로는 제 힘이 한창일 때 이내 마음을 바꾸었고, 첫 방문이 끝나기도 전에 그때껏 바치던 그 먼발치의 경의를 언니에게서 동생에게로 옮겨 놓았다. 그는 그 뒤 지주의 손님으로 다시 돌아와 그들 사이에서 더 오래 머물렀고, 한 달이 채 지나기 전에 이미 릴리의 장래 남편으로 받아들여져 있었다.\n\n일이 어느 쪽으로 흘러가는지를 알아채자마자 벨이 크로즈비를 대하는 마음과 동생을 대하는 마음을 어떻게 바꾸었는지는 보기에 아름다운 일이었다. 벨이 그것을 알아채기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았으니, 자매가 어머니를 홀로 남겨 두어 완두콩을 먹든 말든 내버려 둔 채 둘이서 큰 집에 저녁을 먹으러 갔던 그 저녁에 이미 그 생각의 첫 자락을 붙들었기 때문이다. 크로즈비가 떠나 있던 예닐곱 주 남짓 동안 벨은 동생보다 훨씬 거리낌 없이 그의 이야기를 했다. 벨은 크로즈비가 작별을 고하던 자리에 함께 있었고, 그가 릴리에게 곧 다시 앨링턴으로 돌아오겠노라고 다짐하던 그 간절함을 들었다. 릴리는 그것을 마치 저와는 아무 상관도 없는 일인 양 아주 담담하게 받아들였다. 그러나 벨은 사정의 얼마쯤을 보았고, 크로즈비를 성실하고 정직한 남자라 믿었으므로 그를 두고 좋은 말을 해 주면서, 이미 릴리의 가슴에 자라났을지 모르는 자그마한 사랑의 싹을 무엇이든 북돋아 주었다.\n\n“하지만 그분은 정말 아폴로잖아.” 릴리가 말했었다.\n\n“그분은 신사야. 그건 나도 알겠어.”\n\n“그럼, 물론이지. 신사가 아니고서는 아폴로가 될 수 없는걸.”\n\n“게다가 아주 영리하시고.”\n\n“영리하기야 하시겠지.” 그가 일개 서기일 뿐이라는 말은 더 나오지 않았다. 실은 릴리가 그 문제에 관해 마음을 바꾼 것이었다. 조니 임스는 일개 서기였다. 반면에 크로즈비는, 굳이 서기라고 불러야 한다면, 아주 특별한 부류에 드는 서기였다. 서기와 서기 사이에는 큰 차이가 있을 수 있는 법이다! 추밀원 서기관과 교구 서기는 아주 다른 사람이다. 릴리는 크로즈비 씨를 관청 근무의 하급 부류에서 제 마음속으로나마 건져 내려 애쓰다가 그런 식의 생각을 머릿속에 얻게 된 것이었다.\n\n“그 사람이 오지 않았으면 싶구나.” 데일 부인이 큰딸에게 말했었다.\n\n“어머니 생각이 틀리신 것 같아요.”\n\n“하지만 그 애가 그 사람을 좋아하게 되고, 그러고 나서 —”\n\n“릴리는 상대가 마땅한 이유를 주기 전까지는 어떤 남자도 정말로 좋아하게 되지 않아요. 그리고 그분이 릴리를 마음에 두고 있다면, 두 사람이 맺어지지 못할 이유가 무엇이겠어요?”\n\n“하지만 그 애는 너무 어리잖니, 벨.”\n\n“열아홉이에요. 그리고 약혼을 한다 해도 한 해쯤은 기다릴 수 있겠지요. 하지만 그런 식으로 말해 봐야 소용없어요, 어머니. 어머니가 릴리더러 그분에게 마음을 주는 티를 내지 말라고 하시면, 릴리는 아예 그분과 말을 하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“간섭할 생각은 없다.”\n\n“그럼요, 어머니. 그러니 일은 흘러가는 대로 두어야지요. 저로 말하자면 크로즈비 씨가 아주 마음에 들어요.”\n\n“나도 그렇단다, 얘야.”\n\n“삼촌도 그러시고요. 저라면 릴리가 삼촌이 골라 준 사람을 짝으로 맞게 하지는 않겠어요.”\n\n“그야 그래서는 안 되지.”\n\n“하지만 릴리가 고른 사람이 마침 삼촌 마음에도 드는 사람이라면, 그건 좋은 일로 여겨야겠지요.”\n\n이런 식으로 그 문제는 어머니와 큰딸 사이에서 이야기되었다. 그러고서 크로즈비 씨가 왔고, 첫 달이 다 가기도 전에 릴리를 향한 그의 공공연한 찬탄이 언니의 예감이 옳았음을 증명해 보였다. 그리고 그 짧은 구애 기간 동안 연인들에게는 모든 일이 순조로웠다. 지주는 처음부터 이 혼사에 만족한다고 밝히면서, 크로즈비 씨는 혼인을 감당하기에 넉넉한 수입을 지닌 신사라고 그 특유의 차가운 태도로 데일 부인에게 알렸다.\n\n“런던에서라면 빠듯하겠지요.” 데일 부인이 말했었다.\n\n“내 아우가 혼인할 때 가졌던 것보다는 많소.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“그이가 저를 행복하게 해 주었던 만큼만 그 애를 행복하게 해 준다면요 — 그것이 이어지는 동안만이라도!” 데일 부인은 이렇게 말하며 솟아오르는 눈물을 감추려 얼굴을 돌렸다. 그리고 그 일을 두고 지주와 그 제수 사이에 더 오간 말은 없었다. 지주는 돈 문제의 도움에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그런 처지의 삼촌이라면 응당 그리했음 직하게, 젊은 사람들이 살림을 시작할 때 손을 보태겠다는 뜻조차 비치지 않았다. 데일 부인 자신도 그 문제에 관해 아무 말도 하지 않았으리라는 것은 넉넉히 짐작할 만하다. 그리고 실은, 지주가 속으로 무슨 뜻을 품고 있었든 그것을 데일 부인에게 털어놓지는 않으리라는 것 또한 짐작할 만하다. 이는 거북한 일이었으나, 그 처지는 두 사람 사이에 서로 잘 이해되고 있는 것이었다.\n\n버나드 데일은 여전히 앨링턴에 있었고, 크로즈비가 자리를 비운 동안 내내 그곳에 머물러 있었다. 데일 부인이 그 문제에 관해 굳이 말을 꺼낸다면 아마 그 상대는 버나드였을 것이다. 그러나 버나드도 제 삼촌 못지않게 입이 무거울 수 있는 사람이었다. 크로즈비가 돌아오자 그와 버나드는 물론 많은 시간을 함께 보냈고, 크로즈비가 릴리를 향한 제 마음이 깊어지고 있음을 은근히 내비치자 자연스럽게 두 처녀를 두고 둘 사이에 속을 터놓는 이야기가 오가게 되었다.\n\n“자네도 알 테지만, 삼촌은 내가 큰딸과 혼인하기를 바라시네.” 버나드가 말했었다.\n\n“그쯤은 짐작하고 있었네.”\n\n“그리고 그 혼사는 성사되리라고 보네. 예쁜 처녀인 데다 더없이 착하지.”\n\n“그래, 그렇지.”\n\n“내가 그 애한테 몹시 반해 있는 척은 하지 않겠네. 그건 내 방식이 아니니까, 자네도 알겠지만. 하지만 조만간 나를 받아 달라고 청할 참이고, 다 잘 풀리리라 생각하네. 어르신은 영지에서 해마다 팔백 파운드를 떼어 주고, 우리가 원하면 해마다 석 달씩 집에 들이겠다고 분명히 약속하셨네. 나는 그저 그보다 적어서는 못 하겠다고 말씀드렸고, 어르신도 내 말에 수긍하셨지.”\n\n“자네와 어르신은 사이가 아주 좋군.”\n\n“그럼! 우리 사이에 사랑이니 도리니 하는 겉치레 소리가 오간 적은 한 번도 없네. 우리는 서로를 이해하고 있고, 그것이 전부라고 나는 생각해. 어르신은 상속인과 사이좋게 지내는 편안함을 아시고, 나는 소유주와 사이좋게 지내는 편안함을 알지.” 버나드 데일에게는 건전한 상식이 아주 많았다는 점을 인정해야 하리라고 나는 생각한다.\n\n“동생 쪽에는 어르신이 무엇을 해 주실까?” 크로즈비가 물었다. 그리고 그 중요한 물음을 던질 때, 눈 밝은 사람이었다면 그의 목소리에 희미한 떨림이 있음을 알아챘을 것이다.\n\n“아! 그건 내가 자네에게 말해 줄 수 있는 범위를 넘네. 내가 자네라면 어르신께 여쭙겠네. 어르신은 꾸밈없는 분이고, 꾸밈없는 거래를 좋아하시지.”\n\n“자네가 여쭤 볼 수는 없겠지?”\n\n“그래, 못 할 것 같네. 어르신이 그 애를 결코 빈손으로 보내지는 않으시리라는 것이 내 믿음일세.”\n\n“글쎄, 그러시지는 않겠지.”\n\n“하지만 이건 기억해 두게, 크로즈비 — 나는 자네가 기대를 걸 만한 말은 아무것도 해 줄 수 없네. 릴리도 더없이 착한 아이고, 자네가 그 애를 좋아하는 듯하니, 내가 자네라면 어르신께 어떻게 하실 작정이신지를 에두르지 말고 딱 잘라 여쭙겠네. 물론 그건 내 이익에 반하는 일이지. 어르신이 릴리에게 주시는 한 실링 한 실링이 결국은 내 주머니에서 나가는 것이니까. 하지만 자네도 알다시피 나는 그런 것을 마음에 두는 사람이 아닐세.”\n\n이 문제에 관해 크로즈비가 무엇을 알고 있었는지는 여기서 캐묻지 않기로 한다. 다만 그 돈이 누구의 주머니에서 나오는지는, 그것이 제 주머니로 들어오기만 한다면, 그에게 거의 아무 상관도 없었으리라는 말은 해 두어도 좋겠다. 릴리에 대해 완전히 확신이 서자 — 실은 그녀의 삼촌에게 말해도 좋다는 릴리의 허락과, 어머니에게는 자기가 직접 말하겠다는 릴리의 약속을 이미 받아 둔 터였다 — 그는 지주에게 제 뜻을 밝혔다. 그는 그 일을 드러내 놓고 사내답게 했으니, 많은 것을 청하는 만큼 자기 또한 많은 것을 내놓는 것이라고 느끼는 듯했다.\n\n“나로서는 반대할 말이 없네.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 제가 그 아가씨와 약혼한 사이라고 여겨도 좋다는 말씀이십니까?”\n\n“그 애와 그 애 어머니의 허락이 있다면 말이지. 물론 자네도 알다시피 나에게는 그 애에 대한 권한이 없네.”\n\n“그래도 어르신의 승낙 없이는 혼인하지 않을 것입니다.”\n\n“제 삼촌을 그토록 생각해 주다니 그 애가 참 기특하군.” 지주가 말했다. 그리고 그 말이 입에서 나올 때 크로즈비의 귀에는 그 소리가 몹시 차갑게 들렸다. 그 뒤로 크로즈비는 돈에 관해 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 그럴 엄두가 나지 않았음을 스스로 인정할 수밖에 없었다. “게다가 말해 본들 무슨 소용인가?” 그는 제 처신에서 약하다고 느껴지는 대목에 변명을 대고 싶은 마음으로 속으로 이렇게 되뇌었다. “저 어른이 그 애에게 한 실링도 못 주겠다고 하신들, 이제 와서 물러설 수는 없는 노릇 아닌가.” 그러고 나서 혼인이라는 이 문제에서 남자들이 겪게 되는 부당함에 관한 몇 가지 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다. 남자는 여자의 재산에 관해 무엇이든 물어보는 것이 온당해지기도 전에 먼저 제 뜻을 밝혀야 한다. 그리고 일단 제 뜻을 밝히고 나면, 그런 물음은 무엇이든 아무 소용이 없어진다. 이런 헤아림이 그의 행복이 지닌 열기를 얼마쯤 식혀 놓았다. 릴리 데일은 아주 예뻤고, 아주 고왔고, 그 천진함과 순결함과 재빠른 총명함으로 사람의 마음을 아주 시원하게 해 주었다. 릴리 데일에게 사랑을 속삭이는 일보다 더 달콤하게 즐거운 놀이는 있을 수 없었다. 아첨할 뜻이라고는 조금도 없이 제 연인을 추어올리는 그녀의 방식은 크로즈비를 하늘 꼭대기에 올려놓았다. 그가 겪어 온 모든 경험 가운데 그런 것은 없었다. “이것만은 믿으셔도 좋아요.” 그녀는 이렇게 말했었다. “저는 온 마음과 온 힘을 다해 당신을 사랑할 거예요.” 그것은 무척 좋았다. 그러나 그렇다면 두 사람은 무엇으로 먹고산단 말인가? 그, 어돌퍼스 크로즈비가 연 팔백 파운드를 가지고 아내를 거느린 사내로서 뉴 로드 북쪽에 자리를 잡는 일이 있을 수 있단 말인가? 만일 지주가 벨에게 해 주겠다고 약속한 만큼을 릴리에게도 해 준다면, 그렇다면 참으로 사정을 그럭저럭 꾸려 나갈 수도 있으리라.\n\n그러나 릴리의 행복에는 그런 걸림돌이 없었다. 돈에 관한 그녀의 생각은 다소 어렴풋했으나 무척 정직했다. 그녀는 제게 돈이 조금도 없다는 것을 알았고, 필요한 것을 마련하는 일은 남편의 몫이려니 여겼다. 그녀는 제게 돈이 조금도 없다는 것을 알았고, 따라서 자기를 위해 마련될 그 작은 살림에서 호사를 기대해서는 안 된다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그녀는 그를 위해 삼촌이 얼마쯤 도움을 주기를 바랐으나, 가난한 남자의 좋은 아내가 될 수 있음을 증명해 보일 각오도 넉넉히 되어 있었다. 그런 문제를 두고 언니와 나누던 예전의 이야기에서 그녀는 늘, 사랑을 품어도 좋으려면 그 전에 반듯한 수입이 있어야 하며 그것은 없어서는 안 될 조건이라고 잘라 말하곤 했다. 그러나 연 팔백 파운드라면 그 조건을 채우고도 훨씬 남는 것으로 여겨져 왔다. 벨은 가난의 절대적인 영광에 관해 드높은 생각을 품고 있었다. 벨은 수입 따위는 아예 셈에 넣어서는 안 된다고 잘라 말했었다. 자기가 어떤 남자를 사랑한다면 그에게 수입이 한 푼 없다 해도 그와 약혼할 수 있다고. 두 사람의 이론은 그러했다. 그리고 돈에 관해서라면 릴리는 제 이론을 몸소 지켜 낸 그 방식에 아주 만족하고 있었다.\n\n이 아름다운 나날 동안 그녀의 행복을 가로막는 것이라고는 아무것도 없었다. 어머니와 언니는 한목소리로, 그녀가 잘한 일이라고, 고른 것이 다행이라고, 그 사랑은 마땅한 것이라고 말해 주었다. 어머니에게 모든 것을 털어놓던 그 첫날, 그녀는 제 소식이 받아들여진 그 방식에 더없이 큰 기쁨을 누렸다.\n\n“오! 어머니, 드릴 말씀이 있어요.” 크로즈비 씨와 함께 앨링턴의 그 들판을 오래도록 거닐고 돌아온 뒤, 어머니의 침실로 올라가며 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“크로즈비 씨 이야기니?”\n\n“네, 어머니.” 그러고 나서 나머지는 말이 아니라 뜨거운 포옹과 행복한 눈물을 통해 이야기되었다.\n\n어머니의 방에 앉아 어머니의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻고 있는 사이 벨이 들어와 그녀의 발치에 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n“우리 릴리.” 벨이 말했다. “정말 잘됐어.” 그러자 릴리는 자기가 말하자면 언니에게서 그 연인을 훔쳐 낸 셈이라는 것을 떠올리고, 두 팔로 벨의 목을 감고 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“나는 처음부터 이렇게 될 줄 알았어.” 벨이 말했다. “그렇죠, 어머니?”\n\n“난 정말 몰랐는걸.” 릴리가 말했다. “그런 일이 있을 수 있으리라고는 생각도 못 했어.”\n\n“하지만 우리는 알았어 — 어머니하고 나는.”\n\n“그랬어?” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“벨이 그렇게 될 거라고 나한테 말해 주더구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “하지만 나는 처음에는 그 사람이 내 귀한 딸에게 걸맞을 만큼 훌륭한 사람이라고 좀처럼 생각하기 어려웠단다.”\n\n“어머, 어머니! 그런 말씀 하시면 안 돼요. 그분은 무엇에든 걸맞을 만큼 훌륭한 분이라고 생각하셔야죠.”\n\n“아주 좋은 사람이라고 생각하마.”\n\n“그분보다 나은 사람이 어디 있겠어요? 게다가 그분이 저를 위해 버리시는 것들을 다 떠올려 보면요! 그런데 저는 그 대신 그분께 무얼 해 드릴 수 있을까요? 제가 그분께 드릴 것이 무엇이 있나요?”\n\n데일 부인도 벨도 그 문제를 그런 식으로 볼 수는 없었으니, 릴리가 받는 만큼을 고스란히 주고 있다고 여겼기 때문이다. 그러나 두 사람은 다 같이 크로즈비가 나무랄 데 없는 사람이라고 잘라 말했다. 이제 릴리의 행복을 돌보아 줄 수 있는 길은 그런 다짐뿐임을 알았기 때문이다. 그리하여 릴리는 두 사람 사이에서 그 행복이 온전해졌고, 제 사랑에 온갖 격려를 받았으며, 어머니와 언니의 공감과 인정으로 그 애틋한 마음을 살찌워 갔다.\n\n그러고 나서 조니 임스의 그 방문이 있었다. 그 딱한 젊은이가 작별 인사를 할 틈도 주지 않고 방에서 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔을 때, 데일 부인과 벨은 서로 서글프게 바라보았다. 그러나 두 사람은 아무런 방도도 꾸며 낼 수가 없었으니, 릴리가 잔디밭을 가로질러 달려와 이미 창문 앞에 와 서 있었기 때문이다.\n\n“관목 숲 끝에 다다르자마자 크리스토퍼 삼촌하고 버나드가 우리 바로 옆에 있지 뭐예요. 그래서 어돌퍼스더러 혼자 더 가시라고 했어요.”\n\n“그런데 누가 다녀갔는지 알아?” 벨이 말했다. 그러나 데일 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 제 판단대로 할 겨를이 주어졌더라면 조니의 방문에 관해서는 그 순간 한마디도 나오지 않았어야 했다.\n\n“내가 나간 사이에 누가 왔었어? 누구였든 그리 오래 있지는 않았나 보네.”\n\n“가엾은 조니 임스.” 벨이 말했다. 그러자 릴리의 얼굴에 붉은빛이 올라왔고, 그녀는 어린 시절의 그 오랜 벗이 자기를 사랑했다는 것을, 그 또한 제 사랑에 무언가 바라는 바가 있었다는 것을, 그리고 이제 그가 그런 바람에 끝을 낼 소식을 들었다는 것을 한순간에 떠올렸다. 그녀는 그 모든 것을 한순간에 알아차렸으나, 그렇게 알아차렸다는 것을 감추어야 한다는 것 또한 알아차렸다.\n\n“조니도 참!” 그녀가 말했다. “왜 나를 기다려 주지 않았을까?”\n\n“네가 나갔다고 일러 주었단다.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “머지않아 또 올 게다, 틀림없이.”\n\n“그러면 그분도 아는 거예요 —?”\n\n“그래. 내가 말해도 네가 언짢아하지 않으리라고 생각했다.”\n\n“그럼요, 어머니. 물론이죠. 그러면 그분은 게스트윅으로 돌아갔나요?”\n\n이 물음에는 아무 대답도 주어지지 않았고, 조니 임스에 관해 그때 더 오간 말도 없었다. 이 여자들은 저마다 사정이 어떠한지를 정확히 알고 있었고, 저마다 나머지 사람들도 그것을 알고 있다는 것을 알았다. 그 젊은이는 세 사람 모두에게 사랑을 받았으나, 크로즈비 씨에게 주어졌던 그런 종류의 찬탄 어린 애정으로 사랑받는 것은 아니었다. 조니 임스는 저희 귀염둥이의 구혼자로 받아들여질 수는 없었을 것이다. 데일 부인도 벨도 그것을 느끼고 있었다. 그러면서도 두 사람은 그의 사랑 때문에, 또 그 사랑에 관해 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 못하게 그를 붙들어 둔 그 멀찍하고 조심스러운 존경 때문에 그를 사랑했다. 가엾은 조니! 그러나 그는 젊었다 — 아직 풋내기 시절을 겨우 벗어났을까 말까 한 나이였다 — 그러니 이 타격도 쉽게 이겨 내고서, 스쳐 지나간 그 낭만의 자그마한 자취를 기억하며 어쩌면 제게 득이 되는 것으로 느끼기까지 하리라. 젊어서 사랑하고 또 헛되이 사랑하는 남자들을 두고 여자들은 이런 식으로 생각하는 법이다.\n\n그러나 조니 임스 자신은, 박차 따위는 잊어버린 채 장갑을 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고 게스트윅으로 말을 달려 돌아가면서, 그 문제를 아주 다르게 생각하고 있었다. 그는 릴리를 향한 제 연정에 어떤 성공도 스스로에게 약속한 적이 없었고, 실은 아무런 희망도 품을 수 없다고 늘 인정해 왔다. 그러나 이제 그녀가 다른 남자에게 실제로 약속된 몸이 되어 혼인한 것이나 다름없게 되자, 예전의 희망이 드높지 않았다고 해서 그의 가슴이 덜 미어지는 것은 아니었다. 그는 릴리에게 제 사랑을 말할 엄두를 낸 적이 한 번도 없었으나 그녀가 그것을 알고 있다는 것은 의식하고 있었고, 이제 헛되이 사랑한 죄를 선고받은 자로서 그녀 앞에 설 엄두는 나지 않았다. 그러고 나서 말을 달려 돌아가는 동안 그는 제 또 다른 사랑도 생각했다. 다만 로타리오나 돈 후안 같은 자들이 제 성공을 헤아려 볼 때 누리리라 짐작되는 그 흐뭇한 생각들은 거의 없었다. “나는 그 여자와 혼인하게 되겠지, 그리고 그것으로 내 인생은 끝이겠지.” 언젠가 정신이 나가서 그녀에게 써 보낸 짧은 쪽지를 떠올리며 그는 속으로 이렇게 말했다. 로퍼 부인네에서 자그마한 야식 자리가 있었고, 루펙스 부인과 어밀리아가 펀치를 만들었다. 야식이 끝난 뒤 그는 어쩌다 보니 식당에 어밀리아와 단둘이 남게 되었다. 그리고 그 너그러운 신의 기운에 달아올라 제 연정을 고백했을 때, 그녀는 서글프게 고개를 저으며 그에게서 달아나 위층 어딘가로 올라가 버렸고, 그가 내민 포옹을 딱 잘라 마다했다. 그러나 바로 그날 밤, 그의 머리가 베개를 찾기도 전에 쪽지 한 장이 그에게 왔으니, 반은 뉘우치고 반은 다정하고 반은 밀쳐 내는 쪽지였다. “만일 참으로 그이가 제 사랑이 정직하고 사내다운 것임을 그녀에게 맹세해 준다면, 그렇다면 참으로 그녀는 그래도 아직은 — 용서받았음을 일러 주려는 뜻으로 문틈 사이로 그이를 만나 볼 수도 있으리라.” 그리하여 배신자 같은 연필이 마침 손 가까이 있었으므로 그는 요구되는 그 말을 써 보냈다. “내 삶의 오직 하나뿐인 목적은 그대를 영원히 내 사람이라 부르는 것입니다.” 어밀리아에게는 그런 약속이 법정 증거로 쓰이려면 잉크로 쓰였어야 하지 않았을까 하는 미심쩍음이 있었다. 그것은 괴로운 의심이었다. 그럼에도 그녀는 제 말을 지켰고 문틈 사이로 그를 만나 주었으며, 식당에서의 그 성급함을 두고 그를 용서하되 그저 용서받는 데 필요한 것보다는 아마 더 너그럽게 용서해 주었다. “저런! 머리를 다 풀어 내리니까 어찌나 예쁘던지.” 여전히 그 너그러운 신의 기운을 지닌 채 마침내 다시 베개를 찾았을 때 그는 속으로 이렇게 말했었다. 그러나 이제, 앨링턴에서 게스트윅으로 돌아가는 길 위에서 그날 밤을 떠올리자니, 풀어 내려 하늘거리던 그 머리채는 그 매력에 대한 아무런 강렬한 느낌도 없이 기억될 뿐이었다. 그리고 그는 릴리 데일도 생각했다 — 처음 런던으로 올라가기 전날 그녀에게 작별을 고하던 그때의 릴리를. “나는 다른 누구보다도 당신을 다시 보는 일을 마음에 두게 될 거예요.” 그는 그렇게 말했었고, 그 뒤로도 그 말을 자주 떠올리며 그녀가 그것을 여느 우정의 다짐 이상으로 알아들었을까 궁금해하곤 했다. 그리고 그때 그녀가 입고 있던 옷도 또렷이 기억났다. 그것은 전부터 알고 있던 낡은 갈색 메리노 원피스였고, 사실 연인의 눈길을 끌 만한 구석이라고는 조금도 없는 옷이었다. “지긋지긋하게 낡아빠진 것!” 그날이 오기 전부터 그 옷을 두고 릴리 자신이 내린 평결은 그러했다. 그러나 그녀는 그 옷을 그의 눈에 거룩한 것으로 만들어 놓았으니, 그는 그 자락 한 조각이라도 부적처럼 가슴에 지니고 다닐 수 있었다면 더없이 기뻤을 것이다. 남자들이 스스로 사랑에 빠졌음을 인정할 때 입에 올리는 그 연정이란 그 본성이 얼마나 놀라운 것인가. 모든 것 가운데 그것은 어떤 조건에서는 가장 더러운 것이고, 또 다른 조건에서는 가장 아름다운 것이다. 그 조건이 어떠하냐에 따라 남자는 스스로를 짐승으로도 드러내고 신으로도 드러내는 것이다! 그러니 가엾은 조니 임스로 하여금 게스트윅으로 말을 달려 돌아가게 두자 — 비천하게 사랑한 탓에 많이 괴로워하고, 또한 고결하게 사랑한 탓에 많이 괴로워하면서.\n\n릴리는 연인의 팔짱을 끼고 두어 걸음마다 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 관목 숲 사이를 가볍게 걸어가다가 삼촌과 버나드를 알아보았다. “멈춰요.” 그녀는 그의 팔을 살짝 잡아당기며 말했다. “저는 더 안 갈래요. 삼촌은 늘 그 케케묵은 재담으로 저를 놀리시거든요. 그리고 오늘은 당신을 볼 만큼 봤어요, 선생님. 내일은 사냥 나가시기 전에 꼭 건너오셔야 해요.” 그러고서 그녀는 그를 두고 떠났다.\n\n큰 집 뒤편의 널찍한 자갈길을 함께 걷던 삼촌과 사촌 사이에서 다투어지던 그 문제가 무엇이었는지는 여기서 알아 두는 편이 좋겠다. “버나드.” 노인이 말했었다. “이 문제가 너와 벨 사이에서 매듭지어졌으면 싶구나.”\n\n“그게 급한 일입니까, 어르신?”\n\n“그래, 급하다. 아니, 나는 매사에 서두르는 것을 싫어하니 차라리 서둘러야 할 까닭이 있다고 말해 두자꾸나. 명심해라, 나는 너를 몰아세울 뜻은 없다. 네 사촌이 마음에 들지 않는다면 그렇다고 말해라.”\n\n“마음에 들지 않는 게 아닙니다. 다만 이런 일은 차츰차츰 자라나는 편이 가장 좋다는 느낌이 좀 있을 뿐입니다. 서두르는 것을 싫어하시는 마음에는 저도 온전히 같은 생각이고요.”\n\n“하지만 이제 시간은 넉넉히 들였다. 알다시피, 버나드, 나는 너를 위해 수입에서 큰 희생을 치르려는 참이다.”\n\n“정말 감사하게 여기고 있습니다.”\n\n“나는 자식이 없으니 늘 너를 내 자식으로 여겨 왔다. 그러나 내 아우 필립의 딸이 내 아우 올랜도의 아들만큼 내게 귀하지 않을 까닭도 없지 않으냐.”\n\n“물론 그렇지요, 어르신. 아니, 딸이 아니라 두 딸이겠지요.”\n\n“그 문제는 내게 맡겨 두어라, 버나드. 작은아이는 네 그 친구와 혼인할 참이고, 그 사람은 아내를 건사하기에 넉넉한 수입이 있으니, 내 제수도 이 혼사에 만족할 만한 충분한 까닭이 있다고 나는 생각한다. 릴리가 가난한 남자와 혼인했더라면 반드시 그리해야 했을 터인데, 이제는 그 얼마 안 되는 제 수입에서 한 몫을 떼어 놓아야 할 일이 없을 테니 말이다.”\n\n“숙모님이 많이 내놓으실 형편도 못 되었겠지요.”\n\n“사람은 형편에 따라야 하는 법이다. 나는 그 두 아이 모두에게 아비 노릇을 할 마음은 없다. 그래야 할 까닭이 없고, 헛된 기대를 부추기지도 않겠다. 너와 벨 사이의 이 문제가 매듭지어졌다는 것을 알게 된다면, 나로서도 내가 하려는 일을 두고 흡족해할 까닭이 생기겠지.” 이 모든 말에서 버나드는, 돈 문제에 관한 가엾은 크로즈비의 기대가 아마 그리 많이 채워지지는 않으리라는 것을 알아차리기 시작했다. 그러나 그는 삼촌의 이 경고에서 일종의 위협 또한 알아차렸다 — 알아차렸다고 생각했다. “나는 네 아내와 함께 연 팔백 파운드를 주겠다고 약속했다.” 그 경고는 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. “그러나 네가 그것을 당장 받아들이지 않거나, 받아들일 것이라고 나를 느끼게 해 주지 않는다면, 내가 마음을 바꾸는 편이 나을지도 모르겠구나 — 더구나 이 또 다른 조카딸이 곧 혼인하려는 참이니 말이다. 벨과 함께 그토록 큰 재산을 네게 주어야 한다면 릴리에게는 아무것도 해 줄 필요가 없다. 그러나 네가 벨과 그 재산을 받지 않겠다면, 그렇다면 뭐 —” 그런 식이었다. 널찍한 자갈길을 함께 걸으며 버나드가 삼촌의 그 경계하는 말을 읽어 낸 방식은 그러했다.\n\n“더는 이 문제를 미룰 생각이 없습니다.” 버나드가 말했다. “원하신다면 당장 벨에게 청혼하겠습니다.”\n\n“네 마음이 아주 굳게 정해졌다면, 네가 그것을 미룰 까닭이 무엇인지 나는 모르겠구나.”\n\n그러고 나서 두 사람은 그 문제를 그렇게 매듭지은 뒤, 앞으로 친척이 될 그 사람을 다정한 미소와 부드러운 말로 맞이했다.\n\n# 제7장. 근심의 시작\n\n릴리는 정원에서 연인과 헤어지면서, 이튿날 아침 사냥을 나가는 길에 자기에게 들러 달라고 요구해 두었다. 그 명령에 순종하여 그는 아침 식사가 끝난 뒤 버나드와 개 두 마리를 데리고 데일 부인의 잔디밭에 나타났다. 두 사내는 손에 총을 들고 사냥에 어울리는 온갖 장비를 빠짐없이 갖추고 있었으나, 결국 길 건너편 그루터기 밭에 이른 것은 점심을 먹고 난 뒤였다. 그리고 사내가 사랑에 빠져 있을 때에는 크로케가 사냥 못지않게 좋은 것이 아니냐고 의심해 볼 만하지 않은가?\n\n버나드 데일은 사랑에 빠져 있지 않았다고들 말할 것이다. 그러나 그런 혐의를 그에게 씌우는 이들은 거짓을 말하는 것이다. 그는 그 나름의 방식과 유행대로 사촌 벨을 사랑하고 있었다. 존 임스가 릴리를 사랑하듯 벨을 사랑하는 것은 그의 천성이 아니었다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 그의 천성이 그를, 어밀리아 로퍼의 매력이 소득세 사무국의 가엾은 서기에게 안겨 준 것과 같은 곤경으로 몰아넣지도 않을 터였다. 흔히들 쓰는 말로 조니는 정에 약했다. 반면에 데일 대위는 제 감정을 능히 다스릴 줄 아는 사내였다. 그는 여자 하나를 두고 바보짓을 하거나 상심하여 죽을 사람은 아니었다. 그러나 그럼에도 아내를 얻게 되면 아마 제 아내를 사랑할 것이고, 제 자식들에게는 자상한 아버지가 될 사람이었다.\n\n이제 이 넷은 서로 무척 허물이 없어졌다. 그들 사이에서는 버나드, 어돌퍼스, 때로는 아폴로, 그리고 벨과 릴리였고, 크로즈비는 그것이 여간 즐겁지 않았다. 인생의 새로운 국면이 그에게 찾아왔고, 그것은 더할 나위 없이 즐거운 국면이었다. 그러나 그럼에도 우울한 성질의 싸늘한 발작이 그를 덮치는 순간들이 있었다. 그는 어른이 된 뒤로 줄곧 스스로에게 결코 하지 않겠노라 공언해 온 바로 그 일을 하고 있는 것이었다. 그가 세운 인생 계획에 따르자면 그는 결혼을 멀리해야 했고, 재산과 지위와 미모가 한꺼번에 제 앞에 굴러들어 오는 경우에 한해서만 그것을 있을 수 있는 일로 여겨야 했다. 그런 찬란한 상품을 기대해 본 적이 없었으므로, 그는 스스로를 제 인생의 마지막 장까지 보퍼트에서 군림하고 세브라이트에서 위세를 떨칠 사내로 여겨 왔다. 그런데 이제—\n\n그는 은방울 같은 목소리와 예쁜 재치와 어지간히 반짝이는 두 눈에 정복당해, 제가 굳혀 두었던 자리에서 굴러떨어진 것이었다. 그는 릴리를 매우 좋아했다. 실은 벗인 데일 대위보다도 사랑에 빠지는 능력이 강한 사람이었으니까. 그러나 그 희생이 치를 만한 값어치가 있는 것이었을까? 이것이 저 우울한 순간마다 그가 스스로에게 던지는 물음이었다. 이를테면 잠자리에 누워 아침에 눈을 뜨고 있을 때, 면도를 할 때, 그리고 이따금 저녁 식사 뒤 지주의 이야기가 장황해질 때. 그런 때에 데일 씨의 말을 듣고 앉아 있노라면 그의 자책은 이따금 몹시 쓰라렸다. 어째서 내가 이런 것을 견뎌야 하는가, 나, 세브라이트의 크로즈비가, 일반위원회 사무국의 크로즈비가, 채링 크로스에서 베이스워터 끝자락에 이르기까지 그 누구도 저를 지루하게 하도록 내버려 두지 않는 크로즈비가— 어째서 데일 지주 같은 사람의 장황한 이야기에 귀를 기울여야 하는가? 만일 지주가 정말로 제 조카딸에게 후하게 굴 작정이라면야 그것도 괜찮을 것이다. 그러나 지주는 아직 그런 뜻을 내비친 적이 없었고, 크로즈비는 그 문제에 대해 물어볼 용기를 내지 못한 자신에게 화가 났다.\n\n이리하여 사랑의 길은 우리의 아폴로에게 온통 순탄하기만 한 것은 아니었다. 크로케 마당에 나가 있을 때나, 받아들여진 연인의 온갖 특권을 누리며 데일 부인의 응접실에 앉아 있을 때는 여전히 그에게 즐거웠다. 지주의 클라레를 홀짝일 때에도 즐거웠으니, 오로지 그 시중을 들려고 두 정원을 총총 가로질러 온 사랑스러운 아가씨가 곧 제 손에 커피를 건네주리라는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 이보다 더 즐거운 일은 없다. 물론 그런 대접을 받는 사내는 뿔과 목에 푸른 리본을 감고 칼을 기다리며 제단 앞에 선 송아지가 된 것 같은 기분이 들기는 하지만 말이다. 크로즈비는 자기가 바로 그런 송아지라고 느꼈다. 아내가 될 사람의 재산에 대해 아직 감히 한마디도 묻지 못했다는 점에서 더욱더 송아지 같았다. “오늘 저녁에 그 노인네한테서 기어이 알아내고 말겠다.” 그날 아침 멋쟁이용 사냥 각반의 단추를 채우며 그는 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n“저이는 저걸 신으니 참 근사해 보이지.” 나중에 릴리가 언니에게 말했다. 제 연인이 다리를 치장하는 동안 그의 마음을 어지럽히던 생각 따위는 전혀 알지 못한 채였다.\n\n“돌아올 때도 이 길로 오겠지요.” 점심이 끝나고 본래의 볼일을 보러 나서려 하면서 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 꼭 그렇지는 않아!” 버나드가 말했다. “우리는 다벨 농장 쪽으로 돌아서 그러독 농장을 거쳐 돌아올 거야. 얘들아, 너희 오늘 큰 집에 저녁 먹으러 올라오니?”\n\n아가씨들은 큰 집에 저녁을 먹으러 올라가지 않겠다고, 오늘 저녁에는 아예 큰 집에 갈 생각이 없다고 잘라 말했다.\n\n“그럼 옷을 갈아입을 것도 없을 테니, 그러독네 문간, 농장 마당 뒤쪽으로 우리를 마중 나오는 게 어떠냐. 우리는 정확히 다섯 시 반에 거기 가 있을 테니.”\n\n“그러니까 우리는 다섯 시 반에 거기 가 있고, 오빠들은 우리를 사십오 분쯤 기다리게 하겠다는 말이지.” 릴리가 말했다. 그렇지만 약속은 제안된 대로 정해졌고, 두 아가씨도 그것이 조금도 싫지 않았다. 정말로 시골에 사는, 때 묻지 않은 사람들 사이에서 이 놀음은 이런 식으로 굴러가는 법이다. 그러독 농부네 농장 마당 문간이란 연애 소설의 밀회 장소로는 걸맞은 울림이 없지만, 진심인 사람들에게는 숲 한가운데 빈터의 어느 참나무 못지않게 훌륭한 구실을 한다. 릴리 데일은 아주 진심이었다. 어돌퍼스 크로즈비도 실은 진심이었다. 다만 그의 경우에는 그 진심이, 이 눈물의 골짜기에서 진심이라는 것들이 대개 걸치게 마련인 저 갈색빛을 띠기 시작하고 있었다. 릴리에게는 아직 모든 것이 장밋빛이었다. 그리고 버나드 데일 역시 진심이었다. 그날 아침 내내 그는 잔디밭에서 벨에게 아주 가까이 서 있었고, 사촌이 제 자잘한 속삭임을 그리 싫어하며 받아들이는 것 같지는 않다고 생각했다. 싫어할 까닭이 무엇이겠는가? 그리하여 연 팔백 파운드를 치맛자락에 달게 되었으니, 그 아가씨야말로 운 좋은 아가씨가 아닌가!\n\n“이보게, 데일.” 그날의 볼일을 보는 동안 둘이 그러독의 땅으로 돌아와, 농장 마당 문간에 이르기 전에 그의 순무밭을 마저 훑으려던 참에 크로즈비가 말했다. 그리고 이제 크로즈비가 말을 꺼내는 동안, 둘은 문에 몸을 기댄 채 순무밭을 바라보고 있었고 개 두 마리는 뒷다리를 접고 앉아 있었다. 크로즈비는 지난 한두 마일 동안 몹시 말이 없었고, 이 대화를 하려고 마음을 다잡던 참이었다. “이보게, 데일— 자네 삼촌은 릴리의 재산에 대해 아직 나한테 한마디도 하지 않았네.”\n\n“릴리의 재산이라니! 릴리에게 재산이 있기나 한가가 문제지.”\n\n“내가 아무것도 없이 그 사람을 데려가리라고 기대하실 리는 없지 않은가. 자네 삼촌은 세상 물정을 아는 분이고, 그러니 아실 텐데—”\n\n“우리 삼촌이 세상 물정을 아는 분인지 아닌지는 내가 말하지 않겠네. 하지만 크로즈비, 그분이 그렇든 아니든 자네는 세상 물정을 아는 사람이지. 자네도 늘 알고 있었다시피 릴리에게는 제 몫으로 가진 것이 없어.”\n\n“나는 릴리 제 몫을 말하는 게 아닐세. 그 애 삼촌을 말하는 거야. 나는 그분께 솔직했네. 자네 사촌에게 마음이 기울었을 때 나는 곧바로 내 뜻을 밝혔다고.”\n\n“그럴 여지가 있다고 생각했다면 그때 그분께 물어봤어야지.”\n\n“여지가 있다고 생각했다면이라니! 참말이지 자네는 태연한 친구로군.”\n\n“이보게 크로즈비, 우리 삼촌에 대해서는 자네 마음대로 말해도 좋지만, 릴리를 두고는 한마디도 안 되네.”\n\n“누가 그 사람을 두고 뭐라 한다는 건가? 험한 말로부터 그 이름을 지키는 일이 이미 자네보다 내 몫이 되었다는 걸 모른다면 자네는 나를 도무지 모르는 걸세. 나는 릴리를 내 사람으로 여기고 있어.”\n\n“내 말은 그저, 그 애의 돈이든 돈 없음이든 그것 때문에 불만이 있다면 그건 우리 삼촌한테 따질 일이지 작은 집 식구들한테 따질 일은 아니라는 걸세.”\n\n“그쯤은 나도 잘 알고 있네.”\n\n“그리고 우리 삼촌에 대해서라면 자네가 나한테 무슨 말을 하든 얼마든지 자유지만, 그분께 잘못이 있다고는 나로서는 도무지 보이지 않는군.”\n\n“그 애의 앞날이 어떻게 되는 건지 나한테 말해 주셨어야지.”\n\n“앞날이랄 게 없다면 어쩌겠나! 조카딸에게 재산을 줄 뜻이 없다는 것을 만나는 사람마다 붙들고 알릴 의무가 삼촌에게 있을 리는 없지. 실은 말이야, 자네는 어째서 그분께 그런 뜻이 있으리라고 생각했나?”\n\n“그런 뜻이 없다는 걸 자네가 아는가? 언젠가 자네가 나더러 그분이 조카딸에게 돈을 주시리라고 믿게 만들었지 않나.”\n\n“이보게 크로즈비, 이 문제만큼은 자네와 내가 서로 분명히 해 둘 필요가 있네—”\n\n“하지만 자네가 그러지 않았나?”\n\n“잠깐 내 말을 들어 보게. 자네가 우리 모두가 아는 가운데 릴리와 완전히 약혼하기 전까지, 나는 삼촌의 뜻에 대해 자네에게 단 한마디도 한 적이 없네. 그러고 나서, 이 문제에 대한 내 짐작이 자네의 처신을 조금도 바꿔 놓을 수 없게 된 다음에야, 나는 삼촌이 그 애에게 무언가 해 주시리라 생각한다고 말했지. 정말로 그렇게 생각했으니까 그리 말한 걸세. 그리고 벗으로서, 자네 이해가 걸린 일이라면 내가 생각하는 바를 자네에게 말해 주는 것이 마땅했으니까.”\n\n“그런데 지금은 생각이 바뀌었다?”\n\n“생각이 바뀌었네. 하지만 아마도 충분한 근거는 없이 바뀌었겠지.”\n\n“나로서는 가혹한 일이군.”\n\n“실망을 견디는 일이야 가혹할 수 있지. 하지만 누가 자네를 부당하게 대했다고는 말할 수 없을 걸세.”\n\n“그럼 자네는 그분이 그 애한테 아무것도 안 주시리라고 보는 건가?”\n\n“자네한테 그리 대수로울 만한 건 아무것도.”\n\n“그런데도 내가 가혹하다고 말해서는 안 된다? 나는 이건 지독하게 가혹하다고 보네. 물론 나는 결혼을 미룰 수밖에 없겠지.”\n\n“삼촌께 직접 말씀드리지 그러나?”\n\n“그럴 참일세. 사실을 말하자면 그 말씀이 그분 쪽에서 먼저 나왔어야 옳다고 보네만, 그건 보기 나름이겠지. 나는 내가 어찌 생각하는지 아주 분명히 말씀드릴 걸세. 그래서 그분이 노하시면, 뭐, 그 댁에서 나오는 수밖에 없겠지. 그뿐이야.”\n\n“이보게 크로즈비, 처음부터 그분을 노하게 할 작정으로 말을 꺼내지는 말게. 마음이 나쁜 분은 아니지만 몹시 완고하시니까.”\n\n“나도 그분 못지않게 완고할 수 있네.” 그러고는 더 실랑이할 것도 없이 둘은 순무밭으로 들어갔고, 새를 놓칠 때마다 각자 제 운수를 두고 욕지거리를 했다. 사람에게는 말도 못 타고 총도 못 쏘고 당구 한 큐도 제대로 치지 못하고 휘스트에서 패 한 장 기억하지 못하는 어떤 마음 상태라는 것이 있는데, 크로즈비와 데일은 문간에서 대화를 나눈 뒤 바로 그런 마음 상태에 이르러 있었다.\n\n두 사람이 밀회 장소에 늦은 것은 십오 분이 채 못 되었지만, 그렇게 시간을 지켰음에도 아가씨들이 먼저 와 있었다. 물론 첫 물음은 사냥거리에 관한 것이었고, 물론 신사들은 자고새가 그 어느 때보다 귀했고 개들은 더 사납게 날뛰었으며 자기들 운은 견딜 수 없으리만치 나빴노라고 늘어놓았다. 그 모든 변명에는 거의 아무도 귀를 기울이지 않았지만. 릴리와 벨은 자고새 소식을 들으러 나온 것이 아니었고, 새를 한 마리도 잡지 못했다 한들 사냥꾼들을 너그러이 용서했을 것이다. 그러나 눈에 훤히 드러나는 저 기운 없음만은 용서할 수가 없었다.\n\n“당신, 대체 왜 그러시는지 도무지 모르겠네요.” 릴리가 연인에게 말했다.\n\n“우리는 십오 마일이 넘는 땅을 걸었고, 게다가—”\n\n“런던서 오신 신사분들처럼 맥없는 사람들은 처음 봐요. 십오 마일이 넘는 땅을 걸었다니! 크리스토퍼 삼촌 같으면 그쯤은 아무것도 아니라고 하실걸요.”\n\n“크리스토퍼 삼촌은 우리보다 단단한 재료로 빚어진 분이지요.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “육칠십 년 전에는 사람들이 그렇게 태어나곤 했던 겁니다.” 그러고 나서 그들은 그러독의 밭과 집 옆 목초지를 지나 큰 집으로 걸어 돌아왔고, 거기서 현관 앞에 서 있는 지주와 마주쳤다.\n\n산책은 모두가 그 약속을 정할 때 기대했던 만큼 즐겁지 않았다. 크로즈비는 행복하던 마음을 되찾으려 애썼으나 뜻대로 되지 않았고, 릴리는 제 연인이 마땅히 그러해야 할 모습이 아니라고 여겨져 말수가 줄고 서먹해졌다. 버나드와 벨은 이 어색함을 함께 겪지 않았으나, 버나드와 벨은 본디 다른 두 사람보다 훨씬 더 말이 없는 편이었다.\n\n“삼촌.” 릴리가 말했다. “이분들이 아무것도 못 잡아 오셨는데, 그 때문에 얼마나 울적해하시는지 삼촌은 짐작도 못 하실 거예요. 죄다 못된 자고새들 탓이지요.”\n\n“잡을 줄만 알면 자고새야 얼마든지 있다.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“개들이 유난히 날뛰더군요.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“내가 데리고 나가면 안 날뛰네. 딩글스가 데리고 나가도 안 날뛰고.” 지주가 말했다. 딩글스는 지주의 사냥터지기였다. “실은 말이야, 요즘 젊은 사람들은 개가 일을 다 해 주도록 훈련되어 있기를 바란단 말이지. 제 발로 사냥감까지 걸어가는 건 너무 수고롭다 이거야. 얘들아, 꾸물대다간 저녁 시간에 늦겠구나.”\n\n“저희는 오늘 저녁엔 올라가지 않아요, 삼촌.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“어째서 안 온다는 게냐?”\n\n“어머니 곁에 있으려고요.”\n\n“네 어미는 왜 너희와 함께 오지 않는 게냐? 도무지 알 수가 없으니 답답해 죽을 노릇이다. 지금 같은 사정이라면 너희를 되도록 자주 한자리에 모아 놓고 보고 싶어 할 법도 하건만.”\n\n“저희끼리도 충분히 함께 있는걸요.” 릴리가 말했다. “그리고 어머니로 말하자면, 아마 어머니 생각에는—” 그러고 나서 그 애는 애원하는 듯한 벨의 눈짓을 알아채고 입을 다물었다. 어머니를 위해 성난 변명을 늘어놓으려던 참이었으니, 삼촌을 노하게 만들 것이 뻔한 변명이었다. 이렇게 날 선 말을 삼촌에게 하는 것이 릴리의 버릇이었고, 그래서 삼촌은 더 말수 적고 더 조심스러운 언니만큼 릴리를 따뜻이 여기지 않았다. 지금도 그는 홱 돌아서서 집 안으로 들어가 버렸고, 그러자 두 젊은이도 아주 짧은 작별 인사만 남기고 그를 따라 들어갔다. 아가씨들은 둘이서 작은 다리를 건너 돌아가면서, 이 오후가 도무지 좋게 끝나지 않았다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n“삼촌 화를 돋우면 안 돼, 릴리.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“삼촌도 어머니에 대해 그런 말씀은 하시면 안 되지. 언니는 삼촌이 무슨 말을 하든 아무렇지도 않은 것 같더라.”\n\n“어머나, 릴리.”\n\n“언니도 마찬가지야. 삼촌은 나를 화나게 만들어서 도저히 입을 다물고 있을 수가 없어. 이 땅이 전부 제 것이니 하고 싶은 말은 뭐든 해도 된다고 생각하시는 거야. 어머니가 왜 삼촌 비위 맞추러 저 위까지 올라가야 해?”\n\n“어머니는 당신이 가장 좋다고 여기시는 대로 하실 게 틀림없어. 어머니는 크리스토퍼 삼촌보다 마음이 굳센 분이고, 누구 도움도 바라지 않으셔. 하지만 릴리, 내가 어머니를 아끼지 않는다는 식으로 말하면 못써. 너도 그런 뜻은 아니었을 테지, 알아.”\n\n“당연히 아니지.” 그러고 나서 두 아가씨는 저희들만의 작은 영지에서 어머니와 합류했다. 그러나 우리는 큰 집의 남자들에게로 돌아가기로 하자.\n\n크로즈비는 저녁 식사를 위해 옷을 갈아입으러 올라갔다가 내가 앞서 이야기한 저 우울한 발작 가운데 하나에 빠져들었다. 그는 지금껏 성공적이었던 제 인생의 지난 세월 내내 스스로를 위해 이루어 놓은 모든 좋은 것을 기어이 허물어뜨리려는 참인가? 아니, 오히려, 마침내 그가 스스로에게 더 힘주어 물었듯이— 그는 이미 그 성공을 죄다 허물어뜨려 버린 것이 아닌가? 릴리와의 결혼은, 그것이 좋은 일이 되든 나쁜 일이 되든, 이제 정해진 일이었고 조금이라도 의심을 품어 볼 여지가 있는 문제로는 여겨지지 않았다. 이 사내에게 공정하려면 나는 이렇게 밝혀 두어야겠다. 이 모든 비참한 순간에도 그는 릴리라는 사람 자체를 제가 얻어 낸 큰 보물로— 제 비참함을 보상해 주어야 마땅한, 그리고 어쩌면 보상해 주게 될 보물로 여기려고 여전히 힘껏 애썼다는 것을. 그러나 그 비참함은 아주 뚜렷이 거기 있었다. 그는 클럽도, 유행도, 여태 얻어 낸 모든 것도 내려놓고, 연 팔백 파운드에 아기들로 가득한 조그만 집에서 밋밋하고 단조로운 가정생활을 하는 데 만족해야 할 터였다. 그것은 그가 스스로를 길들여 온 그런 종류의 엘리시움이 아니었다. 릴리는 아주 참했다, 정말이지 아주 참했다. 스스로에게 말했듯 그 애는 “단연코 여태 본 중에 가장 참한 아가씨”였다. 이제 무슨 일이 벌어지든 그 애의 행복이 그의 첫째 관심사가 될 것이었다. 그러나 제 자신의 행복으로 말하자면— 그는 그 보상이 아무래도 온전하지는 못하리라고 걱정하기 시작했다. “내가 자초한 일이다.” 제 독백의 취지를 자못 고결하게 만들 셈으로 그는 스스로에게 말했다. “나는 다른 것들에 맞추어 나를 길들여 왔지— 아주 어리석게도. 물론 나는 괴로워해야 한다— 지독하게 괴로워해야지. 하지만 그 애는 결코 그것을 알지 못할 것이다. 사랑스럽고 다정하고 순진한, 어여쁜 것!” 그러고 나서 그는 지주 이야기로 넘어갔는데, 조카딸에게 사심 없고 사내다운 처신을 해 온 제게는 노여워할 자격이 있다고 느꼈던 것이다. “하지만 내가 어떻게 생각하는지는 그분께 알려 드려야겠어.” 그는 말했다. “내가 공정한 대접을 받았다는 건 데일이야 얼마든지 할 수 있는 말이지. 하지만 거짓 구실을 내세워 제 조카딸을 내놓는 건 사내로서 공정한 일이 아니야. 물론 나는 그분이 그 애의 앞가림을 해 주실 작정이라고 생각했지.” 그러고는, 결혼을 약속해 놓고서 릴리를 아주 저버리지는 않겠다고 자못 사내답게 마음을 정한 뒤에, 그는 적어도 런던에서 총각으로 이태쯤은 더 지낼 수 있으리라는 생각에서 위안을 찾으려 했다. 재산 없이 시집을 가야 하는 아가씨들은 기다려야 하리라는 것을 언제나 알고 있는 법이다. 실제로 릴리는 이미 그에게, 자기로서는 조금도 서두를 것 없다고 말해 두었다. 그러니 세브라이트에서 당장 제 이름을 뺄 필요는 없었다. 이렇게 그는 스스로를 달래 보려 했으나, 그러면서도 여전히 바로 그날 저녁 릴리의 재산에 관해 지주와 진지한 이야기를 좀 나누어야겠다고 마음먹고 있었다.\n\n그러면 같은 순간 릴리의 마음은 어떠했을까? 그 애 역시 작은 집에서의 소박한 저녁 식사에 앞서 몸단장을 조금 손보고 있던 참이었는데.\n\n“나는 그이한테 잘하지 못했어.” 그 애는 혼잣말을 했다. “나는 늘 그래. 그이가 나를 위해 얼마나 많은 것을 내주고 있는지는 잊어버리고, 무슨 일로 그이가 언짢아하면 위로해 주기는커녕 더 언짢게 만들지.” 그러고서 그 애는, 자기가 그이를 반만큼도 사랑하지 않는다고— 자기가 그이를 얼마나 온전히 그리고 남김없이 사랑하는지 그이에게 보여 주지 않는다고 스스로를 나무랐다. 그 애에게는 나름의 생각이 있었다. 여자는 사정이 그럴 자격을 온전히 그 사람에게 줄 때까지 어떤 남자에게도 마음이 기운 티를 내서는 안 되지만, 그런 사정이 정말로 생기고 나면 사랑을 아끼는 일 없이 온 힘을 다해 그 사람을 위해 쏟아 부어야 한다는 것이었다. 그러나 그 애는 그렇게 실행할 때가 온 지금, 자기가 제 이론대로 하고 있지 못하다는 것을 늘 느끼고 있었다. 그 애는 저도 모르게 자잘하게 몸을 사렸고, 제 분별과는 어긋나게 아무렇지 않은 척을 조금씩 했다. 그날 오후에도 그렇게 하여, 손을 잡아 달라고 내밀지도 않고 사랑을 확인해 주는 눈으로 그이의 얼굴을 올려다보지도 않은 채 그이를 보내 버렸으니, 그래서 그 애는 스스로에게 화가 나 있었다. “나는 결국 그이가 나를 미워하게 가르치고 말 거야.” 그 애가 벨에게 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n“그건 아주 슬픈 일이겠구나.” 벨이 말했다. “하지만 나는 그렇게 보이지 않는걸.”\n\n“언니가 어떤 남자와 약혼을 했다면 언니는 그이한테 훨씬 잘했을 거야. 언니는 그렇게 말이 많지 않을 테고, 무슨 말을 하든 죄다 애정이 담겨 있겠지. 나는 늘 끔찍한 소리를 조금씩 해 놓고는 나중에 혀를 잘라 버리고 싶어져.”\n\n“어떤 소리든 간에 그이는 그걸 좋아하는 것 같더라.”\n\n“그럴까? 나는 그렇게까지 확신이 서지 않아, 언니. 물론 그이가 나를 꾸짖으리라고 기대하는 건 아니야— 적어도 아직은. 하지만 그이가 흡족해할 때와 언짢아할 때는 눈만 보면 알 수 있는걸.”\n\n그러고 나서 둘은 저녁을 먹으러 내려갔다.\n\n큰 집에서는 세 신사가 겉보기에는 기분 좋게 한자리에 모였다. 버나드 데일은 마음이 고른 사람이어서, 어떤 감정도, 심지어 짜증조차도 제 평소의 태도를 흐트러뜨리도록 좀처럼 내버려 두지 않았다. 언제나 미소를 띠고 식탁에 나올 수 있고, 벗이든 원수든 알맞게 예의 바른 인사로 맞을 수 있는 사람이었다. 그렇다고 그가 유난히 거짓된 사람이라는 말은 아니다. 그 잔잔한 몸가짐에는 속임수 같은 것이 없었다. 그것은 참된 평정심에서 나온 것이었으나, 수양으로 잘 다스려진 기질의 평정이라기보다는 차가운 기질의 평정이었다. 지주는 저녁 식사 전에 자기가 까닭 없이 성마르게 굴었음을 알고 있었고, 제 나름의 방식으로 스스로를 나무란 뒤에 이제 주인다운 정중한 인사를 건네며 식당으로 들어섰다. “듣자 하니 자네 사냥 자루도 그리 나쁘지는 않았다더군.” 그가 말했다. “입맛도 적어도 자루만큼은 좋기를 바라네.”\n\n크로즈비는 미소를 짓고 상냥하게 굴며 몇 마디 듣기 좋은 말을 했다. 한두 시간 뒤에 아주 단단히 마음먹은 걸음을 내디딜 작정인 사람은 대개 그때까지는 세상의 하찮은 일들만으로도 족하다는 듯이 처신하게 마련이다. 그리하여 그는 지주의 사냥터를 칭찬하고, 딩글스에 대해서도 너그러운 말을 한마디 하고, 제 서툰 솜씨를 두고는 스스로를 놀렸다. 그러자 모든 것이 즐거이 흘러갔다— 혼례의 종소리만큼까지는 아니었으나, 그래도 신사 셋의 모임치고는 넉넉히 즐거웠다.\n\n그러나 크로즈비의 결심은 굳어져 있었다. 그래서 늙은 집사가 아주 물러가고 포도주가 식탁 위를 꾸준히 오가게 되자마자 그는 제 볼일을 시작했으니, 얼마간 느닷없어 보이는 것을 면치는 못했다. 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에 그는 버나드 데일이 자리를 비울 때까지 기다리지 않기로 마음을 정했던 것이다. 등 뒤에서보다는 버나드가 있는 앞에서 이 싸움을 더 잘 치를 수 있으리라는 생각이 들었기 때문이다.\n\n“지주 어른.” 그가 입을 열었다. 사이가 좋을 때면 그들은 모두 그를 지주라고 불렀고, 크로즈비는 둘 사이에 아무 어긋난 일도 없는 듯이 시작하는 편이 좋겠다고 생각했다. “지주 어른, 물론 저는 요즈음 제 결혼에 대해 생각이 많습니다.”\n\n“그야 당연한 노릇이지.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“그럼요, 맹세코 그렇습니다! 사내가 그만한 변화를 겪으면서 생각할 거리가 생겼다는 것을 깨닫지 않을 도리는 없지요.”\n\n“그렇겠지.” 지주가 말했다. “나야 결혼할 처지에 놓여 본 적이 없네만, 그쯤은 쉽게 알 만하이.”\n\n“어르신의 조카딸 같은 아가씨를 만났으니 저는 세상에서 가장 운 좋은 사내입니다—” 그러자 지주가 고개를 숙였는데, 이 일에서 운이 좋은 쪽은 데일 집안이라는 뜻으로 조그맣게 예를 차려 말하려던 것이었다. “저도 압니다.” 크로즈비가 말을 이었다. “그 사람은 아가씨가 마땅히 갖추어야 할 모든 것을 그대로 갖추었지요.”\n\n“착한 아이지.” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 나도 그렇게 생각하네.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 제가 보기에는,” 이제는 단번에 물속으로 뛰어들어야겠다는 생각이 들어 크로즈비가 말했다. “그 사람을 제대로 건사할 제 살림 밑천에 대해서도 무언가 말이 있어야 할 것 같습니다.”\n\n그러고 나서 그는 지주가 말을 받으리라 기대하며 잠시 뜸을 들였다. 그러나 지주는 조금도 움직이지 않고 빈 벽난로만 골똘히 바라보며 아무 말도 하지 않았다. “그 사람을 건사한다는 건,” 크로즈비가 말을 이었다. “그 사람이 여태 익숙해져 있던 온갖 안락과 더불어 그리한다는 뜻입니다.”\n\n“그 애는 돈 쓰는 데 익숙해져 본 적이 없네.” 지주가 말했다. “자네도 익히 알다시피 그 어미는 넉넉한 여인이 아니니까.”\n\n“하지만 여기서 지내면서 릴리는 큰 혜택을 누렸지요— 타고 다닐 말이라든가, 그런 것들 말입니다.”\n\n“그 애가 공원에서 탈 말까지 바라리라고는 생각지 않네만.” 지주가 말했는데, 목소리에는 비아냥이 아주 뚜렷이 묻어났다.\n\n“그야 아니겠지요.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“여기 있는 조랑말 가운데 하나를 이따금 부린 것은 나도 아네만, 그 때문에 그 애 생각이 헤퍼지지는 않았기를 바라네. 나는 그 두 아이 어느 쪽에도 그런 실없는 구석이 있다고는 여기지 않았어.”\n\n“그런 것은 없습니다— 제가 아는 한은.”\n\n“전혀 그렇지 않습니다.” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 이러니저러니 해도 결국은 이것입니다, 어르신!” 그리고 크로즈비는 말하면서 여느 때의 목소리와 평소의 침착함을 지키려 애썼으나, 붉어진 낯빛이 그가 떨고 있음을 드러내 주었다. “제가 아내와 더불어 얼마간의 수입을 기대해도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“그 문제로 제수씨와 이야기해 본 적은 없네.” 지주가 말했다. “하지만 그 사람이 해 줄 수 있는 건 없을 것 같으이.”\n\n“물론 저야 그분께서 주시는 것은 한 실링도 받지 않을 겁니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 그것으로 끝난 일이군.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n크로즈비는 잠시 말을 멈추었고, 그동안 낯빛이 몹시 붉어졌다. 그는 저도 모르게 살구 하나를 집어 먹고는 이윽고 대놓고 말했다. “물론 저는 데일 부인의 수입을 두고 말씀드린 것이 아닙니다. 저는 무슨 일이 있어도 그분의 살림을 흔들 생각이 없습니다. 다만 제가 알고 싶었던 것은, 어르신께서 조카딸을 위해 무언가 해 주실 뜻이 있으신가 하는 것입니다.”\n\n“재산을 물려주는 식으로 말인가? 전혀 없네. 나는 아무것도 할 뜻이 없어.”\n\n“그렇다면 이제야 서로를 알게 된 셈이군요— 마침내 말입니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“나는 우리가 처음부터 서로를 알고 있었으리라 여겼네만.” 지주가 말했다. “내가 자네에게 무슨 약속을 한 적이 있나, 아니면 내 조카딸의 앞가림을 해 줄 뜻이 있다고 귀띔이라도 한 적이 있나? 내가 자네에게 그런 기대를 품게 한 일이 있는가? '마침내'라는 그 말을 자네가 무슨 뜻으로 하는지 나는 모르겠네— 사람을 거스르자는 것이 아니라면 말이야.”\n\n“저는 있는 그대로를 말씀드린 겁니다, 어르신. 제 뜻은 이렇습니다— 조카딸들이 어르신과 더불어 살아가는 모양을 보고서, 저는 어르신께서 두 분을 마치 친딸처럼 대해 주시리라 여겼습니다. 이제 제가 잘못 알았다는 것을 알게 되었지요— 그뿐입니다!”\n\n“자네가 잘못 안 걸세— 그리고 그 잘못에는 변명거리가 티끌만큼도 없어.”\n\n“저와 마찬가지로 잘못 안 사람들이 또 있습니다.” 크로즈비가 말했는데, 그 순간에 문득 잊어버렸던 것이다. 다른 어떤 사람의 생각을 이 문제로 끌어들일 권리가 자기에게는 없다는 것을.\n\n“또 누가 있단 말인가?” 지주가 성이 나서 말했다. 그리고 그의 마음은 곧바로 제수씨에게로 달려갔다.\n\n“저는 아무 말썽도 만들고 싶지 않습니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“내 집안과 이어진 누군가가 감히 자네에게, 내가 이미 해 준 것보다 더 많은 것을 조카딸 릴리언에게 해 줄 뜻이 있노라 말했다면, 그런 사람은 거짓될 뿐 아니라 은혜도 모르는 사람일세. 나는 그 누구에게도 내 조카딸을 대신해 무슨 약속을 할 권한을 준 적이 없네.”\n\n“그런 약속은 없었습니다. 그저 그럴 법하다는 말이었지요.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n그는 지주가 성이 나서 누구를 두고 말하는 것인지 조금도 알지 못했다. 그러나 주인이 노해 있음을 알아차렸고, 버나드 데일이 우정에서 해 준 말을 끌어대지 말았어야 했다고 이미 뉘우친 터라 아무 이름도 대지 않기로 마음먹었다. 곁에 앉아 듣고 있던 버나드는 사정이 어떻게 된 것인지 정확히 알고 있었다. 그러나 그의 생각으로는, 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않은 마당에 굳이 제 발로 삼촌의 미움을 살 까닭이 없었다.\n\n“그런 말은 나오지 말았어야 하네.” 지주가 말했다. “누구에게도 그런 말을 할 권리는 없었어. 나는 그 누구도, 뻔뻔스럽다는 소리를 듣지 않고서 자네에게 그런 말을 할 만한 자리에 앉혀 준 적이 없네. 나는 그 일에 대해 더는 묻지 않겠네. 다만 이것만은 지금 분명히 알아 두는 편이 좋겠지. 나는 내 조카딸 릴리언에게 혼인하면서 재산을 주는 것이 내 의무라고 여기지 않네. 자네가 그 애에게 청혼한 것이 그런 착각 위에서 한 일이 아니기를 바라네.”\n\n“아닙니다, 어르신. 그렇지 않았습니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 큰 해가 난 일은 없는 셈이군. 자네에게 헛된 기대를 품게 한 사람이 있었다면 안된 일이네. 하지만 그것을 품게 한 사람이 나는 아니라는 것쯤은 자네도 인정하리라 믿네.”\n\n“어르신께서 저를 오해하신 듯합니다. 제 기대는 결코 크지 않았습니다. 다만 어르신의 뜻을 확실히 알아 두는 것이 도리라고 생각했을 뿐입니다.”\n\n“이제 알았겠지. 그 아이를 봐서라도 이 일이 그 애에게 아무 차이도 만들지 않기를 바라네. 이 일에 그 애의 잘못이 있으리라고는 도무지 생각할 수 없으니까.”\n\n크로즈비는 곧바로 서둘러 릴리를 두둔했다. 그러고는, 보퍼트의 아폴로라 할 만큼 사교계 생활에 밝은 사람치고는 지나치게 어설프게 더듬거리면서, 릴리가 아무것도 받지 못하게 된 이상 자기 형편으로는 결혼을 얼마간 미룰 수밖에 없겠다고 설명했다.\n\n“내 개인적인 생각을 말하자면,” 지주가 말했다. “나는 긴 약혼을 좋아하지 않네. 하지만 이 문제에 대해서는 내가 참견할 권리가 없다는 것도 잘 알고 있지. 다만, 정말로—” 그러고서 그는 스스로 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“날을 하나 잡아 두는 게 좋지 않겠나. 어떤가, 크로즈비?” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“그 문제는 데일 부인과 상의하겠네.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“자네와 그 사람이 서로 뜻이 맞는다면,” 지주가 말했다. “그것으로 족하겠지. 이제 응접실로 갈까, 아니면 잔디밭으로 나갈까?”\n\n그날 저녁 잠자리에 들면서 크로즈비는, 지주와의 겨룸에서 자기가 이기지 못했다고 느꼈다.\n\n# 제8장. 그럴 수는 없어\n\n이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서 큰 집의 세 신사는 저마다 분홍빛 종이에 적힌 작은 쪽지를 한 장씩 받았다. 명목상으로는 데일 부인이 보낸 것으로, 그날로부터 일주일 뒤에 작은 집으로 차를 마시러 오라는 초대였다. 릴리가 크로즈비 씨 앞으로 쓴 쪽지의 아래쪽에는 이런 말이 덧붙어 있었다. “나서서 춤출 사람만 구할 수 있다면 잔디밭에서 춤을 출 거예요. 물론 좋든 싫든 오셔야 해요. 버나드도요. 우리 삼촌도 오시게끔 힘닿는 데까지 구슬려 보세요.” 그리고 이 쪽지는 아침 식탁의 분위기를 다시 누그러뜨리는 데 얼마간 도움이 되었다. 쪽지는 지주에게도 보여졌고, 마침내 그는 데일 부인의 작은 저녁 모임에 어쩌면 가 볼지도 모르겠다는 말까지 하게 되었다.\n\n이 약속된 모임이 크로즈비 씨를 즐겁게 해 주려는 뜻에서 나온 것이 결코 아니라, 오로지 가엾은 조니 임스를 위한 것이었다는 점은 밝혀 두는 편이 좋겠다. 그 일을 어떻게 하면 좋단 말인가? 이 문제는 데일 부인과 벨 사이에서 충분히 논의되었고, 두 사람은 조니를 조촐한 친목 모임에 불러 낯선 사람들이 여럿 있는 자리에서 릴리와 마주치게 하는 것이 가장 좋겠다는 결론에 이르렀다. 그렇게 하면 그의 겸연쩍음도 넘어갈 수 있을 터였다. 데일 부인의 말대로, 그가 저희들에게 아무런 관심도 받지 못한 채 멀찍이 떨어져 있도록 내버려 두어서는 결코 안 될 일이었다. “한번 어색한 고비만 깨지고 나면 그 애도 아무렇지 않을 거예요.” 벨이 말했다. 그리하여 그날 이른 시각에 심부름꾼 하나가 게스트윅으로 보내졌고, 그는 임스 부인의 답장을 들고 돌아왔다. 그날 저녁에 아들딸을 데리고 가겠다는 내용이었다. 삯마차를 붙들어 두었다가 그날 밤 안으로 게스트윅으로 돌아가겠다는 말도 덧붙어 있었다. 임스 부인과 메리에게 잠자리를 내어 주겠다는 제안이 있었기에 그렇게 적은 것이었다.\n\n모임이 열리는 저녁이 오기 전에 앨링턴에서는 또 하나 기억할 만한 일이 벌어졌는데, 그날 저녁에 이 사람 저 사람이 품게 되는 심정을 이해하려면 그 일을 먼저 이야기해 두어야 한다. 지주는 조카에게, 조카딸 벨에 관한 그 문제를 매듭지었으면 한다는 뜻을 알려 둔 터였다. 그리고 버나드의 생각은 지주의 생각과 온전히 맞아떨어졌으므로, 그는 삼촌의 바람대로 따르기로 마음먹었다. 그에게 이 계획은 새삼스러운 것이 아니었다. 그는 혼인을 목적으로 삼기에 충분할 만큼 사촌을 사랑하고 있었고, 결혼을 하는 편이 자기에게 좋은 일이라고 생각하던 참이었다. 돈 없이는 결혼할 수 없었으나, 이 결혼이라면 까다로운 재산 약정으로 골머리를 앓거나 제 이익에 맞서는 변호사들과 부딪치는 일 없이 수입을 얻을 수 있었다. 더 나은 자리를 구할 수도 있기는 했다. 그러나 그보다 훨씬 못한 자리를 얻게 될 수도 있는 노릇이었고, 게다가 그는 사촌을 좋아했다. 그는 이 문제를 아주 침착하게 혼자 따져 보았고, 아내를 둔 남자로서 마땅히 살아야 할 삶에 대해 훌륭한 결심 몇 가지를 세웠으며, 런던의 어느 거리에 집을 마련할지 정해 두었고, 나흘이고 닷새고 잇달아 벨에게 아주 상냥하게 굴었다. 그가 흔히 말하는 뜻으로의 구애를 한 것이 아니었다는 사실은, 벨 자신이 그것을 눈치채지 못했다는 점으로 미루어 당연한 일로 받아들여야 하리라고 나는 생각한다. 벨은 늘 사촌을 좋아해 왔고, 요즘 들어 그가 유난히 사근사근하게 군다고 여겼을 뿐이었다.\n\n모임 전날 저녁에 두 아가씨는 큰 집에 올라와 있었다. 명목은 잔디밭에서 춤을 추는 것이 마땅한지를 의논하러 왔다는 것이었다. 릴리는 꼭 그렇게 해야 한다고 마음을 굳혀 놓았으나, 벨은 밖이 춥고 눅눅할 테니 춤을 추기에는 응접실이 나을 것이라고 반대했다.\n\n“보다시피 우리한테는 젊은 신사가 넷뿐인 데다 하나는 아직 덜 자랐잖아요.” 릴리가 말했다. “그런 사람들이 방 안에서 격식을 차리고 나란히 서 있으면, 무슨 제대로 된 연회라도 되는 것처럼 우스꽝스러워 보일 거예요.”\n\n“칭찬 고맙습니다.” 크로즈비가 밀짚모자를 벗으며 말했다.\n\n“정말 그럴 거예요. 그리고 우리 여자들은 더 우스꽝스러워 보이겠지요. 하지만 잔디밭에 나가면 조금도 우습지 않아요. 두세 쌍쯤 잔디밭에 나서면 그것으로 충분히 즐거울 거예요.”\n\n“난 잘 모르겠는걸.” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“아니, 나는 알겠습니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “잔디밭이 무도회 자리로는 걸맞지 않다는 점이—”\n\n“아무도 무도회 이야기는 안 했어요.” 릴리가 짐짓 뾰로통하게 말했다.\n\n“내가 편을 들어 드리는 참인데도 말을 못 하게 하시는군요. 잔디밭이 무도회 자리로 걸맞지 않다는 점이, 남자 춤꾼이라고는 어른 넷에 아이 하나뿐인 부족함을 가려 줄 겁니다. 그런데 릴리, 덜 자란 신사라는 게 누구지요? 옛 친구 조니 임스입니까?”\n\n릴리는 그에게 대답하면서 목소리가 차분해졌다.\n\n“아, 아니에요. 임스 씨를 두고 한 말이 아니에요. 그분도 오시지만, 그분 이야기가 아니었어요. 보이스 씨 아들 딕 보이스가 이제 열여섯이거든요. 그 애가 덜 자란 신사예요.”\n\n“그럼 네 번째 어른은 누구지요?”\n\n“게스트윅에서 오시는 크로프츠 선생님이요. 어돌퍼스, 그분을 꼭 좋아해 주셨으면 좋겠어요. 우리는 그분이야말로 더할 나위 없이 완벽한 남자라고 생각하거든요.”\n\n“그렇다면 나는 당연히 그 사람을 미워하게 되겠군요. 게다가 몹시 질투도 하게 되겠고요!”\n\n그러고 나서 그 한 쌍은 그 문제를 두고 저희끼리 작은 싸움을 벌이며 함께 자리를 떴다. 멧비둘기 한 쌍이 이따금 그러하듯이 말이다. 두 사람이 떠나고 나자 버나드는 벨과 단둘이 남아, 집 뒤편의 정원과 들판을 갈라놓는 하하 도랑 위에 나란히 서 있게 되었다.\n\n“벨.” 그가 말했다. “저 둘은 무척 행복해 보이는구나, 그렇지?”\n\n“지금이야 행복해야 마땅하지 않겠니? 가엾은 릴리! 저 사람이 릴리에게 잘해 주었으면 좋겠어. 버나드, 저 사람이 네 친구이긴 하다만, 나는 그 일이 정말이지 몹시 마음에 걸려. 제대로 알지도 못하는 남자에게 사람을 맡긴다는 건 엄청난 믿음이잖니.”\n\n“그렇지. 하지만 둘은 아주 잘 지낼 거야. 릴리는 충분히 행복할 거고.”\n\n“저 사람은?”\n\n“저 친구도 행복할 테지. 처음에는 수입이 좀 빠듯하다고 느끼겠지만, 그런 건 다 풀리게 마련이야.”\n\n“저 사람이 행복하지 않으면 릴리는 비참해질 거야.”\n\n“둘은 아주 잘 지낼 거라니까. 릴리가 돈을 될 수 있는 대로 아껴 쓸 각오만 하면 돼. 그뿐이야.”\n\n“릴리는 돈이 아쉬운 줄 모를 애야. 그게 문제가 아니라, 저 사람이 자기 때문에 가난해졌다는 걸 릴리가 알게 만들면 그때는 릴리가 불행해진다는 거지. 저 사람은 헤픈 사람이니, 버나드?”\n\n그러나 버나드는 다른 이야기를 꺼내고 싶어 안달이 나 있었고, 그래서 릴리의 약혼에 대해 마음이 다른 데 가 있지 않았더라면 응당 그에게서 나왔을 법한 현명한 말들을 늘어놓지 않았다.\n\n“아니, 그렇지는 않을걸.” 그가 말했다. “그런데 벨—”\n\n“우리가 한 것 말고 달리 어떻게 할 수 있었을까 싶기는 해. 그래도 우리가 성급했던 게 아닌가 겁이 나. 저 사람이 릴리를 불행하게 만들면, 버나드, 나는 너를 절대로 용서하지 않을 거야.”\n\n그러나 이렇게 말하면서 벨은 사촌이 그러하듯 다정하게 그의 팔에 손을 얹었고, 그 목소리에는 제 위협에서 가시를 걷어 내는 무엇이 있었다.\n\n“무슨 일이 있어도 나하고 다투면 안 돼, 벨. 나는 너와 다툴 형편이 못 되거든.”\n\n“물론 진심으로 한 말은 아니야.”\n\n“너와 나는 결코 다투는 일이 없어야 해, 벨. 적어도 나는 그러기를 바라. 나는 다른 누구와 다투는 건 견딜 수 있어도 너와 다투는 건 못 견뎌.” 그리고 그가 이렇게 말할 때 그 목소리에는 무언가가 있어서, 그가 무엇을 마음먹고 있는지를 아가씨에게 어렴풋하고도 흐릿하게 일러 주었다. 그가 지금 이 자리에서 자기에게 청혼을 하려 한다는 생각이 대뜸 들었다는 것은 아니다. 다만 그가 여느 사촌 사이의 다정함을 넘어서는 무언가를 뜻하고 있다는 것을 벨은 느꼈다.\n\n“우리가 다투는 일은 결코 없기를 바라.” 벨이 말했다. 그러나 이렇게 말하는 동안 벨의 마음은 자리를 잡아 가고 있었다. 결심을 세우면서, 버나드가 어쩌면 바라고 있을지도 모를 사랑이 어떤 것인지에 대해 결론을 내리고 있었던 것이다. 그리고 그 마음은 또 하나의 결론에 이르렀다. 그 대가로 돌려줄 수 있는 사랑이 어떤 것인지에 대해서.\n\n“벨.” 그가 말했다. “너와 나는 언제나 다정한 벗이었지.”\n\n“그래, 언제나.”\n\n“그런데 우리가 벗보다 더한 사이가 되지 못할 것도 없지 않니?”\n\n데일 대위의 공정을 기하기 위해 나는 이렇게 밝혀 두어야겠다. 그가 이 물음을 던질 때 그의 목소리는 더할 나위 없이 자연스러웠고, 얼굴에도 손발에도 떨리는 기색이라고는 없었다. 그는 그 자리에서 그 일을 하기로 마음을 굳혀 두었고, 겉으로 조금도 흔들리는 기색 없이 그것을 해냈다. 그는 물음을 던졌고, 그런 다음 대답을 기다렸다. 이 점에서 그는 사촌에게 다소 가혹했다. 그 물음이 잘못 알아들을 여지가 없는 말로 던져진 것은 분명했으나, 그럼에도 그런 처지에 놓인 젊은 아가씨가 마땅히 기대할 만한 그 온갖 살뜰함을 갖추어 내놓인 것은 아니었기 때문이다.\n\n두 사람은 하하 도랑 바로 곁의 잔디에 앉아 있었는데, 워낙 가까이 있었으므로 버나드는 손을 뻗어 사촌의 손을 제 손 안에 쥘 수 있었다. 그러나 벨이 용케 두 손을 맞잡은 채로 두었기에 그는 그저 손목을 가만히 잡았을 뿐이었다.\n\n“무슨 말인지 잘 모르겠어, 버나드.” 잠깐 사이를 두었다가 벨이 말했다.\n\n“우리가 사촌보다 더한 사이가 되지 않겠느냐는 말이야. 우리가 부부가 되지 않겠니?”\n\n이제는 적어도 무슨 말인지 모르겠다고 할 수가 없었다. 이런 물음이 여태 분명하게 던져진 적이 있다면, 버나드 데일은 그것을 분명하게 던진 것이었다. 우리가 부부가 되지 않겠니? 내가 보기에 그렇게 대뜸 불쑥 다 털어놓을 만한 배짱을 가진 남자는 드물다. 그러면서도 나는 영어라는 말이 이 물음에 대해 그보다 나은 표현을 마련해 주고 있는지 알지 못한다.\n\n“어머, 버나드! 너 때문에 놀랐어.”\n\n“너를 아프게 하지는 않았기를 바라, 벨. 나는 오래전부터 이 일을 생각해 왔어. 하지만 내 태도가, 너에게조차도 연인의 태도는 아니었다는 걸 잘 알고 있어. 크로즈비처럼 웃음을 흘리며 달콤한 말을 하는 건 내게 없는 재주야. 그렇다고 해서 너를 덜 사랑하는 건 아니야. 나는 아내를 구하며 여기저기 둘러보았고, 너를 얻을 수 있다면 내가 아주 운이 좋은 사람이겠구나 생각했어.”\n\n그는 그때 삼촌에 대해서도, 연 팔백 파운드에 대해서도 아무 말을 하지 않았다. 그러나 기회가 닿는 대로 그 말을 할 작정이기는 했다. 연 팔백 파운드와 넉넉한 삼촌의 호의는 혼인의 든든한 근거가 되며, 심지어 사랑의 근거까지도 된다는 것이 그의 확고한 생각이었다. 그리고 사촌도 그 문제를 같은 눈으로 볼 것이라고 그는 믿어 의심치 않았다.\n\n“너는 내게 참 잘해 줘. 잘해 주는 정도가 아니지. 물론 나도 그건 알아. 하지만, 어머, 버나드! 이런 일은 조금도 생각지 못했어.”\n\n“그래도 대답은 해 줘야지, 벨! 아니면 생각할 시간을 갖고 싶다면, 혹은 숙모님께 여쭙고 싶다면, 내일 대답해 주어도 좋고.”\n\n“지금 대답하는 게 옳을 것 같아.”\n\n“거절이라면 그러지 마, 벨. 그런 대답을 하기 전에 잘 생각해 봐. 미리 말해 두었어야 했는데, 우리 삼촌이 이 혼사를 바라고 계셔. 돈 문제로 걸리는 것이 있다면 무엇이든 삼촌이 없애 주실 거야.”\n\n“나는 돈 같은 건 아무래도 좋아.”\n\n“하지만 네가 릴리를 두고 말했듯이, 사람은 신중해야 하잖니. 우리 결혼이라면 그런 종류의 일은 무엇이든 잘 정리될 거야. 삼촌이 나에게 약속하시기를, 곧바로 우리에게—”\n\n“그만해, 버나드. 우리 삼촌이 무엇을 내놓든 그것으로 나를 살 수 있다고 여기게 되면 곤란해— 아니, 우리가 돈 이야기를 할 까닭은 조금도 없어.”\n\n“나는 이 일의 사정을 있는 그대로 네가 알아 두었으면 했을 뿐이야. 그리고 삼촌 이야기라면, 이런 일에 삼촌이 네 편에 서 계시는 걸 너도 기뻐하리라고 생각할 수밖에 없거든.”\n\n“그래, 삼촌이 내 편에 서 계시면 기쁘겠지. 그러니까, 내가 그럴 마음이라면— 하지만 삼촌의 바람이 내 결정을 좌우할 수는 없어. 실은 말이야, 버나드—”\n\n“그래, 얘기해 봐. 실은 뭐라는 거야?”\n\n“나는 늘 너를 다른 무엇보다도 오라비처럼 여겨 왔어.”\n\n“하지만 그런 마음은 바뀔 수도 있잖아.”\n\n“아니, 그렇지 않을 거야. 버나드, 한 걸음 더 나아가서 지금 다 말할게. 그건 바뀔 수 없어. 내가 나를 아는 만큼은 아니까 확실히 말할 수 있어. 그건 바뀔 수 없어.”\n\n“나를 사랑할 수 없다는 말이니?”\n\n“네가 바라는 그런 식으로는 말이야. 나는 너를 정말 깊이 사랑해. 정말이지 깊이. 무슨 어려운 일이 생기면 나는 오라비를 찾아가듯 꼭 그렇게 너를 찾아갈 거야.”\n\n“그것뿐이어야만 하니, 벨?”\n\n“그거야말로 사람이 느낄 수 있는 사랑 가운데 가장 다정한 것 아닐까? 하지만 나를 은혜도 모르는 사람이라거나 도도한 사람이라고 여기지는 마. 네가 나에게— 내가 받을 자격보다 훨씬 더 큰 것을 해 주려 한다는 걸 나는 잘 알아. 어떤 아가씨라도 그런 청혼이라면 자랑스러워할 거야. 하지만, 버나드—”\n\n“벨, 마지막 대답을 주기 전에 하룻밤 자면서 생각해 보고 어머니와도 의논해 봐. 물론 너는 마음의 준비가 없었을 테고, 잠깐도 생각해 보지 않은 채 나에게 그런 큰 약속을 해 주기를 바랄 수는 없지.”\n\n“마음의 준비가 없었던 건 사실이야. 그래서 마땅히 했어야 할 대답을 못 하고 있었던 거고. 하지만 이야기가 여기까지 온 이상 너를 아리송한 채로 보낼 수는 없어. 너를 기다리게 할 까닭이 없거든. 이 일에 대해서는 나도 내 마음을 알아. 버나드, 정말이지, 정말이지 네가 말한 대로는 될 수 없어.”\n\n벨은 낮은 목소리로, 거의 애원하는 듯한 겸손함까지 어린 어조로 말했다. 그러나 그럼에도 그 목소리는 벨이 진심이라는 것을, 그리고 그 진심이 쉽사리 바뀌지 않으리라는 것을 사촌에게 일러 주었다. 벨도 데일 집안사람이 아니던가? 그리고 데일 집안사람이 언제 제 마음을 바꾼 적이 있던가? 한동안 그는 벨 곁에 말없이 앉아 있었다. 벨 또한 제 뜻을 밝혀 놓은 터라 더 말을 보태지 않았다. 그렇게 몇 분 동안 두 사람은 하하 도랑 아래를 내려다보며 그대로 있었다. 벨은 여전히 아까 그대로 두 손을 무릎 위에서 맞잡은 자세를 지키고 있었으나, 그는 이제 옆으로 누워 팔을 괴어 머리를 받치고 있었다. 얼굴은 벨 쪽으로 돌린 채였지만 눈은 풀밭에 못 박혀 있었다. 그러나 그 동안에도 그는 놀고 있지 않았다. 사촌의 대답은 그를 슬프게 하기는 했으나, 한순간 그를 멍하게 만들어 당장 아무 생각도 못 하게 하는 그런 타격으로 닥쳐오지는 않았다. 그는 슬펐다. 자기가 슬퍼하리라고 생각했던 것보다 더 슬펐다. 어지간히 갖고 싶었던 것이 이제 거절당하고 나니 그만큼 더 갖고 싶어졌던 것이다. 그러나 그는 제가 놓인 자리의 진상을 정확히 헤아릴 수 있었고, 구애를 밀고 나갈 경우의 승산이 얼마인지, 지금 당장 그만둘 경우의 이득이 무엇인지를 따져 볼 수도 있었다.\n\n“부당하게 너를 몰아붙일 생각은 없어, 벨. 그래도 물어봐도 될까, 혹시 마음이 가 있는 다른 사람이—”\n\n“마음이 가 있는 다른 사람은 없어.” 벨이 대답했다. 그러고 나서 두 사람은 다시 한두 분 동안 말이 없었다.\n\n“우리 삼촌이 이 일로 몹시 상심하실 거야.” 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n“그것뿐이라면.” 벨이 말했다. “우리 둘 다 마음 쓸 일은 아니라고 생각해. 삼촌이라고 우리 마음을 마음대로 처분할 권리가 있는 건 아니잖니.”\n\n“무엇을 빈정대는 건지 알겠어, 벨.”\n\n“버나드, 빈정댄 게 아니야. 그럴 뜻은 없었어.”\n\n“내 슬픔에 대해서는 말할 필요도 없겠지. 그게 얼마나 깊을지 너도 모를 리 없을 테니까. 내 마음이 걸려 있지 않았다면 무엇 하러 이런 창피를 자청했겠니? 하지만 그건 견뎌야 한다면 견딜게—” 그러고 나서 그는 말을 멈추고 벨을 올려다보았다.\n\n“곧 지나갈 거야.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“어쨌든 나는 군말 없이 받아들이겠어. 하지만 삼촌의 심정에 대해서라면 나도 말할 수 있고, 너도 무심하게 흘려듣지는 않으리라고 생각해. 삼촌은 우리 둘 모두에게 잘해 주셨고, 살아 있는 어느 누구보다도 우리 둘을 사랑하셔. 우리가 부부가 되는 걸 보고 싶어 하시는 것도 이상한 일이 아니고, 네 거절이 삼촌께 큰 타격이 된다 해도 이상한 일이 아닐 거야.”\n\n“나도 안타까울 거야. 아주 안타깝지.”\n\n“나도 안타깝겠지. 지금은 삼촌 이야기를 하는 거야. 삼촌은 이 일에 마음을 붙여 두셨어. 그리고 바라는 것도 원하는 것도 몇 안 되는 분이니만큼, 한번 입 밖에 낸 바람에는 그만큼 더 한결같으신 분이지. 이 일을 아시게 되면 삼촌이 아주 매몰차게 나오실까 봐 걱정이야.”\n\n“그렇다면 그건 부당한 일이지.”\n\n“아니, 삼촌은 부당하지 않으실 거야. 그분은 언제나 공정한 분이니까. 하지만 불행해하실 테고, 그러다 남까지 불행하게 만드실까 봐 나는 겁이 나. 벨, 이 일을 얼마 동안만 매듭짓지 않은 채로 두면 안 될까? 내가 네 마음씨 좋은 것을 이용해 먹는 일은 없을 거야. 다시는 이 일로 너를 성가시게 하지 않을게. 이를테면 보름 동안이라든가— 아니면 크로즈비가 떠날 때까지라든가.”\n\n“안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“어째서 그렇게 기를 쓰고 안 된다고만 하니? 그렇게 미룬다고 위험할 것도 없잖아. 너를 몰아붙이지 않을게. 그리고 너도 적어도 생각할 시간은 가져 보았다고 삼촌이 여기시게 할 수 있고.”\n\n“당장 대답해야만 하는 일도 있는 법이야. 내가 나를 의심스러워한다면 네 설득에 넘어가 주기라도 하겠지. 하지만 나는 나를 의심하지 않아. 그러니 너를 애태우게 두는 건 잘못이야. 버나드, 정말 아끼는 버나드, 그럴 수는 없어. 그리고 그럴 수 없는 이상, 네가 내 오라비라면 나더러 그렇게 분명히 말하라고 할 거야. 그럴 수는 없어.”\n\n벨이 이 마지막 다짐을 하는 순간, 두 사람은 릴리와 그 연인의 발소리가 가까이 다가오는 것을 들었다. 그리고 둘 다 이쯤에서 이야기가 끝나는 편이 좋겠다고 느꼈다. 어느 쪽도 자리에서 일어나 그곳을 떠날 방도를 찾지 못하고 있었으면서도, 저마다 이제 더 할 말이 없다고 느끼던 참이었기 때문이다.\n\n“이렇게 다정하고 애틋하고 낭만적인 광경을 본 적 있어?” 두 사람 위에 서서 그들을 내려다보며 릴리가 말했다. “그동안 우리는 어찌나 실속만 차리고 속물스러웠는지. 언니, 그거 알아? 어돌퍼스는 런던에서는 도무지 돼지를 칠 수가 없다고 생각하는 모양이야. 그래서 내가 어찌나 서운한지.”\n\n“딱하기는 하지요.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “릴리는 돼지에 대해서라면 모르는 게 없는 것 같으니까요.”\n\n“당연하지요. 평생을 시골에서 산 게 괜히 그런 줄 아세요. 아, 버나드, 나는 네가 저 하하 도랑 바닥으로 데굴데굴 굴러떨어지는 걸 정말이지 꼭 한번 보고 싶어. 거기 그대로 있기만 해. 우리 둘이서 힘을 합쳐 밀어 줄 테니까.”\n\n그리하여 버나드가 일어섰고, 벨도 따라 일어섰으며, 그들은 모두 차를 마시러 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n# 제9장. 데일 부인의 작은 잔치\n\n이튿날이 잔치가 열리는 날이었다. 그 저녁에 벨과 사촌 사이에는 더 이상 한마디도 오가지 않았다. 적어도 특별히 눈여겨볼 만한 말은 한마디도 없었다. 그리고 이튿날 아침에 크로즈비가 벗에게 둘이 함께 작은 집으로 내려가 준비가 어떻게 되어 가는지 보자고 했을 때, 버나드는 사양했다.\n\n“이 친구야, 나는 자네처럼 사랑에 빠진 사람이 아니라는 걸 잊었나 보군.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“나는 자네도 그런 줄 알았는데.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“아니야. 적어도 자네처럼은 아닐세. 자네는 승낙을 받은 구혼자이니 무엇이든 해도 되지. 크림을 젓는 일이든, 할 줄만 안다면 피아노 조율이든. 나는 절반짜리 구혼자에 지나지 않아. 삼촌 뜻을 받들어 정략결혼이나 궁리하는 처지이고, 우리가 맺은 약정 어디에도 그렇게까지 혹독한 훈련을 받으라는 조항은 없거든. 자네 처지는 그 정반대일세.” 이런 말을 하면서 데일 대위는 분명 몹시도 거짓되었다. 그러나 어떤 처지에 놓인 사람에게든 거짓이 용서될 수 있다면, 그 순간 그가 놓여 있던 처지에서는 용서받을 만도 했다. 그리하여 크로즈비는 혼자 작은 집으로 내려갔다.\n\n“데일이 오지 않겠다는군요.” 그가 세 부인에게 한꺼번에 말했다. “잔디밭에서 출 춤을 위해 몸을 아껴 두는 모양입니다.”\n\n“저녁에는 와 주었으면 좋겠군요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. 그러나 벨은 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 지금 형편에서 사촌의 청혼과 그에 대한 자신의 대답을 비밀로 해 두는 것이 사촌에 대한 도리라고 마음을 정해 두었다. 버나드가 왜 벗과 함께 큰 집에서 건너오지 않았는지 그녀는 알고 있었지만, 아는 바에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 릴리가 그녀를 쳐다보았으나 말없이 쳐다보기만 했다. 데일 부인으로 말하자면 그 일을 전혀 마음에 두지 않았다. 그리하여 그들은 버나드 데일의 이야기를 더 꺼내지 않은 채 오후를 함께 보냈다. 그리고 적어도 릴리와 크로즈비에 관한 한, 그가 없는 것이 아쉽지는 않았다고 말해 두어도 좋겠다.\n\n임스 부인이 아들딸을 데리고 제일 먼저 왔다. “일찍 와 주시다니 정말 좋아요.” 릴리는 그 자리에서 문득 무언가 상냥하고 즐겁게 들릴 말을 하려다가 그렇게 말했으나, 사실은 내 귀에 늘 가장 무례하게 들리는 그 인사법을 쓴 것이었다. “말한 시각보다 십 분이나 일찍 오셨네요. 물론 제가 말한 건 그보다 삼십 분 뒤라는 뜻인 줄 아셨어야지요!” 일찍 왔다고 고맙다는 인사를 들을 때 내가 그 말을 새기는 방식이 그러하다. 그러나 임스 부인은 친절하고 참을성 있고 까다롭지 않은 여인이어서, 예의 바른 말은 모두 예의로 받아들였다. 게다가 사실 릴리에게는 다른 뜻이 조금도 없었다.\n\n“그래요, 우리가 일찍 오기는 했지요.” 임스 부인이 말했다. “메리가 아가씨들 방에 올라가서 머리를 좀 매만지고 싶다고 해서요.”\n\n“그러시라고 해야지요.” 메리의 손을 잡으며 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 우리가 방해가 되지 않으리라는 것도 알았고요. 아직 할 일이 남았으면 조니는 정원에 나가 있으면 되니까요.”\n\n“본인이 좋다지 않는 한 내쫓지는 않을 거예요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “혼자 힘으로 감당하기에 여자들이 너무 많다 싶으면 몰라도……”\n\n존 임스는 이대로도 아주 좋다는 뜻으로 무언가 우물거리고는 안락의자에 몸을 파묻었다. 그는 릴리와 악수를 하면서, 이 자리를 위해 준비해 둔 짧은 인사말을 또박또박 발음해 보려고 애를 썼다. “축하합니다, 릴리. 진심으로 행복하시기를 바랍니다.” 말은 아주 단순했고 고른 낱말도 나쁘지 않았지만, 가엾은 젊은이는 끝내 그 말을 입 밖에 내지 못했다. “축하”라는 낱말만은 릴리의 귀에 닿았고, 그녀는 모든 것을 알아들었다. 하려 했던 그 인사의 다정함도, 그것을 끝내 말할 수 없었던 까닭도.\n\n“고마워요, 존.” 그녀가 말했다. “런던에서 자주 뵈었으면 좋겠어요. 게스트윅의 오랜 친구가 가까이 있으면 참 좋을 거예요.” 그녀는 그보다 목소리도, 심장의 박동도 더 잘 다스리고 있었다. 그러나 그녀 역시 이 자리가 견디기 힘들다고 느꼈다. 그 남자는 그녀를 정직하고 진실하게 사랑했으며, 지금도 사랑하고 있었다. 그녀를 잃었다는 쓰디쓴 슬픔으로 그녀에게 큰 경의를 바치고 있었다. 그러한 사랑과 그러한 슬픔이 오직 감출 수 없어서 드러나고, 드러내는 이의 뜻을 거슬러 스스로 밝혀지는 것이라면, 어느 처녀가 거기에 마음이 움직이지 않겠는가?\n\n이윽고 늙은 헌 부인이 들어왔다. 그녀의 오두막은 작은 집에서 걸어서 이 분도 걸리지 않는 곳에 있었다. 그녀는 늘 데일 부인을 “그대”라 불렀고, 아가씨들은 아직도 어린애인 양 귀여워했다. 릴리가 결혼한다는 말을 들었을 때 그녀는 놀라 두 손을 번쩍 들었다. 릴리에게 주려고 사 둔 사탕이 아직도 서랍 어느 구석엔가 남아 있었기 때문이다. “런던 사람이라고요? 저런, 저런. 시골에 사는 사람이었으면 좋았을 것을. 연 팔백 파운드라고요, 그대?” 그녀가 데일 부인에게 말했다. “여기서야 듣기 좋은 소리지요, 우리가 다들 가난하니까. 하지만 런던에서 연 팔백 파운드면 그리 큰돈은 아니지 싶은데?”\n\n“지주 어른도 오시겠지요, 안 그래요?” 헌 부인이 데일 부인 곁 소파에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n“네, 곧 오실 거예요. 마음이 바뀌지만 않는다면요. 저한테는 격식을 차리지 않는 분이니까요.”\n\n“그 양반이 마음을 바꿔요! 크리스토퍼 데일이 마음 바꾸는 걸 언제 한 번이라도 본 적이 있어요?”\n\n“한결같으신 분이지요, 헌 부인.”\n\n“누구한테 일 페니를 주기로 했으면 반드시 줄 거예요. 하지만 일 파운드를 빼앗기로 했으면 몇 해가 걸리더라도 기어이 빼앗고야 말지요. 나를 오두막에서 내쫓겠다고 하더라니까요.”\n\n“별말씀을 다 하시네요, 헌 부인!”\n\n“졸리프가 와서 말하기를”—졸리프란 영지 관리인이라는 것을 밝혀 두어야겠다—“지금 있는 그대로가 마음에 들지 않으면 나가도 좋다, 지주 어른은 그 집으로 두 배 세를 받을 수 있다고 하더군요. 내가 부탁한 거라고는 부엌에 칠이나 좀 해 달라는 것뿐이었는데. 나무가 온통 그 양반 모자처럼 새까맣거든요.”\n\n“안쪽 칠은 부인이 하시기로 되어 있는 줄 알았는데요.”\n\n“그대, 모든 걸 백사십 파운드로 꾸리면서 내가 어떻게 그걸 해요? 나도 살아야지요! 게다가 그 양반은 일 년 내내 날마다 일꾼들을 데리고 있는 사람이잖아요! 그리고 오십 년을 이 교구에서 살아온 나한테 그런 전갈을 보낼 일인가요? 저기 오시는군요.” 그러고서 헌 부인은 지주가 방으로 들어서자 자리에서 위엄 있게 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n그와 함께 목사관에서 보이스 씨 부부가, 아직 다 자라지 못한 도련님 딕 보이스와 열넷, 열다섯 살 난 보이스네 딸 둘을 데리고 들어왔다. 데일 부인은 이런 자리에서 흔히 그러하듯 넉넉한 인심을 부려, 제인이며 찰스며 플로렌스며 베시는 어째서 오지 않았느냐고 나무라듯 물었다. 보이스는 화살통을 그런 것들로 가득 채운 사람이었던 것이다. 그러자 보이스 부인은 늘 하는 대답으로, 자기들이 이미 눈사태처럼 몰려온 것 같다고 했다.\n\n“그런데 그, 그, 그 젊은 분들은 어디 계신가요?” 릴리가 짐짓 놀란 얼굴을 지으며 물었다.\n\n“두세 시간쯤 뒤에는 건너올 게다.” 지주가 말했다. “저녁 먹으려고 옷을 갈아입고 내가 보기엔 아주 말쑥해졌는데, 이런 대단한 자리에는 한 번 더 갈아입어야 한다는구나. 안녕하시오, 헌 부인? 몸은 편안하시겠지요. 류머티즘은 이제 없으시고, 응?” 지주는 이 말을 헌 부인의 귀에 대고 아주 큰 소리로 했다. 헌 부인은 어쩌면 귀가 조금 어두웠을지도 모른다. 그러나 아주 조금이었고, 그녀는 남들이 자기를 귀머거리로 여기는 것을 몹시 싫어했다. 게다가 류머티즘 환자로 여겨지는 것도 좋아하지 않았다. 이것을 지주는 알고 있었으니, 그의 인사법은 그리 인정 있는 것이 아니었다.\n\n“사람을 그렇게 놀라게 할 것 없어요, 데일 씨. 지금은 그런대로 괜찮아요, 고마워요. 봄에 한 번 쑤시기는 했지요. 그 오두막이 웃풍이 어찌나 심하게 지어졌는지! '거기서 어떻게 사는지 모르겠다'고 우리 언니가 지난번 다녀갈 때 그럽디다. 해머샴으로 언니한테 가 있는 편이 나을지도 모르지요. 다만 한 교구에서 오십 년을 살고 나면 옮기기가 싫어지는 법이라서요.”\n\n“우리 곁을 떠나실 생각은 마세요.” 보이스 부인이 말했다. 결코 큰 소리는 아니었지만 천천히 또박또박 말했으니, 그렇게 해서 노부인의 비위를 맞추려는 속셈이었다. 그러나 노부인은 그 속을 다 알아차렸다. “보이스 부인은 참 능청스러운 사람이에요.” 저녁이 다 가기 전에 헌 부인은 데일 부인에게 그렇게 말했다. 세상에는 비위를 맞추기가 몹시 어려우면서도 비위를 맞추지 않고는 도무지 함께 지낼 수 없는 노인들이 있는 법이다.\n\n마침내 두 영웅이 잔디밭을 가로질러 응접실 창으로 들어왔다. 그들이 들어서자 릴리는 그들 앞에서 깊이 무릎을 굽혀 절을 했으니, 얇은 모슬린 옷자락이 바닥으로 사뿐히 부풀어 내려 마치 양탄자 위에 피어난 신기한 꽃송이처럼 보일 지경이었다. 그러고는 두 손을 손등끼리 맞붙여 허리띠 죔쇠 위에 얹고서 말했다. “저희는 나리들의 은혜로운 왕림을 기다리고 있었사오며, 누추한 처소를 찾아 주신 은덕이 얼마나 큰지 잘 알고 있사옵니다.” 그런 다음 그녀는 사랑하는 남자를 향해 아, 그토록 달콤하게 미소 지으며 사뿐히 몸을 일으켰고, 모슬린의 부풀음과 물결도 함께 가라앉았다.\n\n한 처녀가 사랑하는 남자에게 부리는, 저 스스로도 의식하고 있는 자잘한 사랑의 재롱만큼 세상에 예쁜 것은 없다고 나는 생각한다. 자기를 그에게 내주었다는 것을 온 세상이 알아도 좋다고 당차게 마음을 정한 처녀라면 더욱 그렇다.\n\n크로즈비가 그 모든 것을 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 좋아했는지는 잘 모르겠다. 둘만 있을 때 그녀가 사랑을 거침없이 내보이는 것은 그도 좋아했다. 그런 자리에서 그런 확신을 좋아하지 않을 남자가 어디 있겠는가? 그러나 남들이 곁에 있을 때만은 릴리가 좀 더 말을 아꼈으면, 이를테면 자기 감정을 좀 더 감추어 주었으면 그는 더 흡족했을지도 모른다. 그가 마음속으로 그녀에게 조심성이 없다고 탓했다는 말은 아니다. 그는 그녀의 성품을 너무나 잘 읽고 있었다. 설령 그 읽음이 아주 정확하지는 않았다 해도, 적어도 그런 종류의 탓을 그녀에게 돌릴 만큼 어긋나 있지는 않았다. 그에게 거슬린 것은 송아지 같은 느낌이었다. 그는 비록 앨링턴 세상에 지나지 않는다 해도, 제물로 붙잡혀 제단에 바치려고 리본으로 묶인 짐승처럼 남들 앞에 내보여지는 것이 싫었다. 그리고 그 모든 것 밑바닥에는, 이 일이 온 세상 앞에 그토록 드러나게 밝혀지지 않는 편이 더 안전하리라는 느낌이 도사리고 있었다. 물론 그가 릴리 데일과 약혼했다는 것은 누구나 알고 있었다. 또 그 자신도, 어쩌면 너무 자주, 스스로에게 되뇌었듯이 그 약혼을 깨뜨릴 생각은 손톱만큼도 없었다. 그러나 혼인은 미루어질 수도 있는 일이었다. 그는 그 문제를 아직 릴리와 의논하지 않았다. 사실 사랑이 이루어진 첫 순간의 기쁨에 겨워 날을 이르게 잡자고 조른 탓에 스스로 조그마한 어려움을 만들어 놓은 터였다. “저는 당신께 아무것도 마다하지 않을 거예요.” 그때 그녀는 그에게 말했었다. “하지만 너무 이르게는 하지 말아요.” 그러니 그는 앞날에 얼마간의 곤란이 놓여 있음을 보았고, 릴리가 당장이라도 시집을 갈 것처럼 온 세상에 알리는 듯한 저 태도를 삼가 주었으면 하고 바라게 되었다. “내일은 저 사람한테 말해 두어야겠다.” 그는 그녀에 못지않게 짐짓 근엄한 태도로 그녀의 인사를 받으며 속으로 그렇게 되뇌었다.\n\n가엾은 릴리! 그의 마음속을 지나가는 것을 그녀는 아직 얼마나 모르고 있었던가. 그의 바람을 알았더라면 그녀는 자기 사랑을 냅킨에 고이 싸 두어, 아무도 그것을 보지 못하게 했으리라. 오직 그만이, 그가 그 보물을 드러내어 보고 싶을 때에만 보도록 했으리라. 그리고 그녀가 그토록 숨김없이 굴었던 것은 모두 그를 위해서였다. 그녀는 제 사랑을 반쯤 부끄러워하는 처녀들을 본 적이 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 결코 제 사랑도, 그도 부끄러워하지 않을 작정이었다. 그녀는 자기를 그에게 주었다. 그러니 온 세상이 그런 것을 알고 싶어 한다면 온 세상이 알아도 좋았다. 그녀가 보기에 그토록 큰 명예인 일을 어찌 부끄러워하겠는가? 그녀는 제 사랑을 입에 올리지 않는 처녀들 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다. 잔과 입술 사이에도 흘릴 일이 얼마든지 있다고 야무지게 따져 두는 처녀들 말이다. 그녀에게는 그런 조심이 필요할 까닭이 없었다. 그런 흘림이 있을 리 없지 않은가! 설령 그런 추락이 온다 해도, 거짓 때문이든 불행 때문이든 그런 운명이 그녀에게 닥친다면, 어떤 조심도 그녀를 구하는 데는 소용이 없을 터였다. 잔은 떨어지면서 산산이 부서져, 어떤 조각 맞추기로도 다시 이어 붙일 길이 없을 것이었다. 이만큼을 그녀가 또박또박 스스로에게 말했던 것은 아니다. 그러나 그녀는 그 모든 것을 느끼고 있었고, 사랑에 당당하게, 어쩌다 보게 되는 그 누구에게도 감추지 않으려 조심하며 씩씩하게 나아갔다.\n\n그들은 케이크와 찻잔의 예식을 마쳤고, 어쨌든 마지막 손님이 오면 적어도 첫 춤 한두 곡은 잔디밭에서 추기로 정해 두었다.\n\n“어돌퍼스, 저분이 오셔서 참 기뻐요.” 릴리가 말했다. “부디 좋아해 주세요.” 새로 온 사람은 크로프츠 선생이었다. 그녀는 이따금 연인에게 그의 이야기를 한 적이 있었으나, 그에게 말할 때조차 언니의 이름을 그 의사의 이름과 나란히 놓은 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그럼에도 크로즈비는 어쩐 일인지 이 크로프츠라는 사람이 벨을 사랑했거나, 사랑하고 있거나, 앞으로 사랑하게 되리라는 생각을 품고 있었다. 그리고 그는 그 자리에서 벗 데일의 권리를 편들 작정이었으므로, 이 의사를 집안의 아주 가까운 벗으로 반길 마음이 딱히 있지는 않았다. 그는 데일의 청혼도, 벨의 거절도 아직 전혀 몰랐다. 그러나 싸움이 필요하다면 싸울 채비는 되어 있었다. 지금으로서는 지주가 그리 마음에 들지는 않았다. 그러나 만일 운명이 이 집안에서 자기에게 아내를 줄 작정이라면, 시골 의사보다는—그는 오만하게도 크로프츠 선생을 그렇게 불렀다—앨링턴의 주인이자 드 게스트 경의 조카를 처남으로 삼는 편이 나았다.\n\n“참으로 딱한 일입니다만,” 그가 말했다. “저는 완벽한 분들을 도무지 좋아해 본 적이 없어서요.”\n\n“하지만 이 완벽한 분은 좋아하셔야 해요. 사실 조금도 완벽한 분이 아니지만요. 담배도 피우고 사냥도 하고 온갖 못된 짓을 다 하시거든요.” 그러고서 그녀는 벗을 맞으러 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n크로프츠 선생은 키가 오 피트 아홉 인치쯤 되는, 가늘고 여윈 사람이었다. 아주 반짝이는 검은 눈에 이마가 넓었고, 검은 머리카락은 거의 곱슬거리다시피 했으나 아름다움을 위해서라면 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 이마 앞으로 내려오지는 않았다. 코는 가늘고 선이 반듯했으며, 입술이 조금만 더 도톰했더라면 입매는 나무랄 데가 없었을 것이다. 얼굴의 아랫부분은 그것만 따로 보면 어딘가 엄한 데가 있었으나, 눈의 광채가 그것을 덜어 주었다. 그러면서도 화가라면 그의 얼굴에서 아랫부분이 훨씬 더 잘생겼다고 말했을 것이다.\n\n릴리는 그에게 건너가 진심으로 반기며, 그가 와 주어 얼마나 기쁜지 모르겠다고 말했다. “그리고 크로즈비 씨께 소개해 드려야겠어요.” 기어이 뜻을 이루고야 말겠다는 듯이 그녀가 말했다. 두 남자는 한마디도 없이 서먹하게 악수를 나누었다. 젊은 남자들이 그런 식으로 마주 세워지면 으레 그러하듯이. 그러고는 곧바로 서로 떨어져 버렸으니, 릴리에게는 다소 실망스러운 일이었다. 크로즈비는 저만치 홀로 서서 두 눈을 천장으로 치켜뜬 채 짐짓 거드름을 피우려는 듯한 낯빛이었다. 한편 크로프츠는 얼른 벽난로 쪽으로 가서 데일 부인과 보이스 부인과 헌 부인에게 예의 바른 짧은 인사말들을 건넸다. 그러고는 마침내 벨에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n“동생분의 약혼을 축하드리게 되어,” 그가 말했다. “참으로 기쁩니다.”\n\n“네.” 벨이 말했다. “그 애가 행복하다는 소식을 들으시면 기뻐하시리라는 걸 알고 있었어요.”\n\n“정말이지 기쁩니다. 그리고 부디 행복하시기를 진심으로 바랍니다. 다들 그분을 좋아하시지요?”\n\n“저희 모두 아주 좋아해요.”\n\n“그리고 형편도 넉넉한 분이라고 들었습니다. 참으로 운이 좋은 사람이에요. 참으로 운이 좋아요. 참으로 운이 좋습니다.”\n\n“물론 저희도 그렇게 생각해요.” 벨이 말했다. “다만 그분이 부자라서 그런 건 아니에요.”\n\n“그럼요, 부자라서가 아니지요. 그런 행복을 누릴 만한 사람인데, 마침 형편이 넉넉해 결혼을 하고 그 행복을 누릴 수 있게 되었으니 그런 거지요.”\n\n“네, 바로 그거예요.” 벨이 말했다. “바로 그거예요.” 그러고서 그녀는 자리에 앉았고, 앉음으로써 이야기를 끝내 버렸다. “바로 그거예요.” 그녀는 그렇게 말했다. 그러나 말이 입 밖으로 나오자마자 그녀는 그렇지 않다고, 크로프츠가 틀렸다고 속으로 되뇌었다. “우리가 그이를 사랑하는 건,” 그녀는 스스로에게 말했다. “걱정 없이 결혼할 만큼 넉넉해서가 아니라, 넉넉하지 않은데도 감히 결혼하려 들기 때문이야.” 그러고 나서 그녀는 자기가 그 의사에게 화가 나 있다고 스스로 인정했다.\n\n그 뒤로 크로프츠 선생은 문 쪽으로 물러나 벽에 기댄 채 홀로 서서, 두 손의 엄지를 조끼 겨드랑이 구멍에 찔러 넣고 있었다. 사람들은 그를 수줍음이 많은 사람이라고 했다. 아마 수줍음이 많기는 했을 것이다. 그러면서도 그는 해야 할 일이라면 무엇이든 조금도 두려워하지 않는 사람이었다. 그는 여럿 앞에서, 그것이 남자들의 무리든 여자들의 무리든 주눅 들지 않고 말할 수 있었다. 또 제 의견을 아주 굳게 지킬 줄도 알았고, 뜻한 바를 밀고 나갈 때는 사납지는 않을지언정 열렬했다. 그러나 정작 할 말이 없을 때 서서 자잘한 말을 늘어놓는 것만은 하지 못했다. 자기가 딛고 선 자리를 제대로 쓰고 있지 않다고 느끼면 그 자리를 지켜 낼 수가 없었다. 어느 자리에 가 있든 스스로를 중요한 사람으로 여기는 재주를 그는 배우지 못했다. 크로즈비가 배운 것이 바로 그 재주였고, 그가 번창한 것도 바로 그 재주 덕분이었다. 그리하여 크로프츠는 물러나 문 가까운 벽에 기대었고, 크로즈비는 앞으로 나와 손님들 사이에서 아폴로처럼 빛났다. “저 사람은 대체 어떻게 저러는 걸까?” 존 임스는 유행의 도시 런던 신사의 완벽한 행복을 부러워하며 속으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n마침내 릴리는 춤출 사람들을 잔디밭으로 끌어내는 데 성공했고, 그들은 카드리유를 한 곡 그럭저럭 마쳤다. 그러나 잘 되지 않는다는 것이 드러났다. 크로즈비가 게스트윅에서 불러온 바이올린 한 대의 연주로는 아무래도 모자랐다. 게다가 잔디는 크로케에는 더할 나위 없었지만 춤추는 발에는 편하지 않았다.\n\n“참 좋구나.” 버나드가 사촌에게 말했다. “이보다 더 좋을 게 있을까 싶어. 하지만 어쩌면……”\n\n“무슨 말인지 알아.” 릴리가 말했다. “하지만 난 여기 있을래. 너희들은 하나같이 낭만이라곤 눈곱만큼도 없어. 저기 첨탑 뒤에 뜬 달 좀 봐. 난 밤새도록 안 들어갈 거야.” 그러고서 그녀는 오솔길 하나를 따라 걸어갔고, 그녀의 연인이 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n“달이 좋지 않아요?” 그의 팔을 잡으며 그녀가 말했다. 이제는 하도 익숙해져서 잡으면서도 잡는다는 생각조차 들지 않는 팔이었다.\n\n“달이 좋으냐고요? 글쎄요, 나는 해가 더 좋은 것 같은데요. 나는 달빛이라는 걸 그다지 믿지 않아요. 감상에 젖고 싶을 때 화제로 삼기에나 가장 알맞지요.”\n\n“아, 제가 두려워하는 게 바로 그거예요. 벨한테도 그렇게 말한답니다. 언니의 낭만도 장미처럼 시들 거라고요. 그러고 나면 저도 배우게 되겠지요. 산문이 시보다 쓸모 있고, 머리가 가슴보다 낫고, 그리고, 그리고 돈이 사랑보다 낫다는 걸요. 다 오고야 말 일이라는 걸 알아요. 그런데도 저는 달빛이 좋아요.”\n\n“그리고 시도, 사랑도요?”\n\n“네. 시는 많이, 사랑은 더 많이요. 당신에게 사랑받는 건 제가 꾸던 어떤 꿈보다도 달콤해요. 제가 읽은 어떤 시보다도 좋고요.”\n\n“사랑하는 릴리.” 그러면서 거리낄 것 없는 그의 팔이 그녀의 허리를 감았다.\n\n“그게 바로 달빛의 뜻이고 시의 알맹이예요.” 열에 들뜬 처녀가 말을 이었다. “그때는 내가 왜 그런 것들을 좋아하는지 몰랐지만 이제는 알아요. 사랑받기를 애타게 바랐기 때문이었어요.”\n\n“그리고 사랑하기를요.”\n\n“아, 네. 그것이 없다면 저는 아무것도 아니겠지요. 하지만 그건 당신의 기쁨이잖아요. 아니, 당신의 기쁨이어야 하고요. 다른 쪽이 제 몫이지요. 그래도 당신을 사랑하는 건 기쁨이에요. 당신을 사랑해도 된다는 걸 아는 것도요.”\n\n“이것이 당신 낭만의 완성이라는 말이군요.”\n\n“네. 하지만 이게 낭만의 끝이 되어서는 안 돼요, 어돌퍼스. 당신도 부드러운 땅거미를 좋아하셔야 하고, 우리 둘만 있게 될 긴 저녁도 좋아하셔야 해요. 제가 좋아하는 책들을 읽어 주셔야 하고요. 그리고 세상이란 딱딱하고 메마르고 잔인한 곳이라고 저에게 가르치시면 안 돼요. 아직은요. 저는 벨에게 그런 말을 곧잘 하지만, 당신은 저에게 그런 말을 하시면 안 돼요.”\n\n“내가 막을 수 있는 한 메마르지도 잔인하지도 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“제 말뜻을 아시잖아요, 여보. 설령 슬픔이 우리에게 닥친다 해도 저는 세상을 메마르고 잔인하다고 여기지 않을 거예요, 당신만—— 제 말뜻을 아시리라 믿어요.”\n\n“내가 당신에게 잘해 준다면 말이지요.”\n\n“그건 두렵지 않아요. 조금도 두렵지 않아요. 제가 당신을 못 미더워할 수도 있다고 생각하시는 건 아니겠지요? 하지만 당신은 달빛을 바라보는 것도, 시를 읽는 것도, 그리고——”\n\n“허튼소리를 하는 것도 부끄러워하지 말라는 말이지요.”\n\n그러나 그렇게 말하면서 그는 그녀를 제 옆으로 더 바싹 끌어당겼고, 그의 말투는 그녀에게 다정하게 들렸다.\n\n“지금 제가 허튼소리를 하고 있나 보죠?” 그녀가 입을 삐죽이며 말했다. “돼지 이야기를 할 때가 더 좋으셨죠. 안 그래요?”\n\n“아니요. 나는 지금이 가장 좋아요.”\n\n“그러면 그때는 왜 제가 마음에 안 드셨어요? 제가 언짢은 말이라도 했나요?”\n\n“지금이 가장 좋은 건 말이지요——”\n\n두 사람은 다리에서 큰 집 정원으로 이어지는 문의 좁은 오솔길에 서 있었고, 우거진 월계수 그늘이 그들을 둘러싸고 있었다. 그러나 달빛은 여전히 그 작은 길로 환하게 스며들었고, 그녀는 그를 올려다보며 그의 얼굴 윤곽과 눈에 어린 사랑의 부드러움을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n“그건 말이지요——” 그가 말했다. 그러고는 그녀 위로 몸을 숙여 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았고, 그녀는 그의 얼굴에 닿으려고 발끝으로 서서 입술을 그에게 내밀었다.\n\n“아, 내 사랑!” 그녀가 말했다. “내 사랑! 내 사랑!”\n\n그날 밤 큰 집으로 돌아가면서 크로즈비는 세상의 어떤 이득도 자기로 하여금 릴리 데일과의 약혼을 깨뜨리게 하지는 못하리라고 굳게 마음먹었다. 그는 거기서 한 걸음 더 나아가, 그 안에 자기 일을 정리할 수만 있다면 혼인을 여섯 달이나 여덟 달, 아무리 늦어도 열 달 넘게는 미루지 않겠다고 작정했다. 물론 그러자면 모든 것을, 평생의 포부와 야심을 전부 버려야 할 터였다. 그러나 그는 다소 서글프게 스스로에게 밝혔듯이 그렇게 할 각오가 되어 있었다. 그의 결심이 그러했고, 잠자리에서 그 결심을 곱씹으면서 그는 자기만큼 이기적이지 않은 사람도 드물다는 결론에 이르렀다.\n\n“그런데 우리가 자리를 비웠다고 사람들이 뭐라고 할까요?” 정신을 차리며 릴리가 말했다. “게다가 저는 사람들을 춤추게 만들어야 하잖아요. 어서 가요, 그리고 좀 상냥하게 구세요. 메리 임스와 왈츠를 추세요. 부디 그래 주세요. 안 그러시면 밤새 말도 안 걸 거예요!”\n\n이 으름장에 따라 크로즈비는 돌아가서 그 젊은 아가씨의 손을 잡는 영광을 청했고, 그로써 그녀를 일곱째 하늘의 기쁨으로 들어 올려 주었다. 어돌퍼스 크로즈비 같은 상대와 추는 왈츠보다 세상이 내줄 수 있는 더 좋은 것이 무엇이겠는가? 그리고 가엾은 메리 임스는 왈츠를 잘 출 줄 알았다. 다만 춤을 추면서 말은 많이 하지 못했고, 멈추고 나면 숨을 몹시 헐떡였다. 그녀는 몸놀림에 너무 많은 기운을 쏟았고, 이 일의 기계적인 부분을 상대가 만족할 만하게 해내려고 지나치게 애를 썼다. “아! 고맙습니다. 참 좋아요. 곧, 곧 다시 출 수 있어요.” 크로즈비와 나눈 이야기는 그 언저리를 크게 벗어나지 못했지만, 그러면서도 그녀는 이날 밤만큼 잘해 본 적이 없다고 느꼈다.\n\n춤추는 짝이라야 많아야 다섯 쌍을 넘지 못했고, 지주와 보이스 씨와 이웃 교구에서 온 부목사처럼 춤추지 않는 사람들은 사실 심심풀이가 될 것이라곤 아무것도 없었지만, 그럼에도 잔치는 꽤 여러 시간 동안 아주 흥겹게 이어졌다. 열두 시 정각에는 간단한 야식이 나왔는데, 그것이 헌 부인의 무료함을 덜어 주었을 것은 틀림없고 보이스 부인 역시 그 자리를 즐기는 듯했다. 이런 자리의 보이스 부인들에 대해서라면 나는 조금도 딱하게 여기지 않는다고 밝혀 둔다. 그들은 대체로 제 자식들의 즐거움 속에서 즐거우며, 그렇지 않다면 마땅히 그러해야 한다. 어쨌든 그들은 명백한 의무를 다하고 있을 뿐이고, 그 의무는 그들이 젊었을 적에 남들이 그들을 위해 다해 주었던 것이다. 그러나 헌 부인들은 대체 무슨 까닭으로 그런 모임에 나오는 것일까? 어째서 저 늙은 부인은 하품을 하며 침대를 그리워하고 십 분마다 시계를 들여다보면서, 늙은 뼈마디가 뻣뻣하게 쑤시고 늙은 귀가 소음에 시달리는데도 몇 시간이고 그 자리에 앉아 있었을까? 설마 야식 하나 때문만은 아니었으리라. 어쨌든 야식이 끝나자 하녀가 그녀를 오두막까지 데려다주었고, 보이스 부인도 그때 슬며시 집으로 돌아갔으며, 지주는 젊은이들에게 돌아올 때 집 안에서 소란을 피우지 말라고 이르면서 자못 요란하게 자리를 떴다. 그러나 가엾은 부목사는 남아서 이따금 데일 부인에게 시시한 말 한마디씩을 건네며, 자기 아닌 다른 이들을 위해 세상이 마련해 둔 기쁨을 애타는 눈으로 바라보고만 있었다. 이 대목에서만은 세상의 여론과 주교들이 함께 부목사들에게 너무 모질다고 나는 말하지 않을 수 없다.\n\n밤의 즐거움이 무르익어 시간과 익숙함이 모두를 함께 즐겁게 만들어 놓았을 무렵, 존 임스는 처음으로 릴리와 춤을 추려고 일어섰다. 그녀는 대놓고 청하는 것만 빼고는 그가 이 호의를 베풀도록 할 수 있는 일을 다 해 두었다. 그것이 호의이리라고 느꼈기 때문이다. 그녀에게 청하고 싶은 마음이 그에게 얼마나 컸는지, 그러면서도 동시에 그 마음을 얼마나 크게 꺼렸는지 릴리는 아마 다 알지는 못했으리라. 그래도 그중 많은 것은 알고 있었다. 그가 자기에게 화가 난 것이 아니라는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그가 헛된 사랑 그 자체만큼이나 그 헛된 사랑으로 상한 자존심 때문에 괴로워하고 있다는 것도 알았다. 그녀는 그 점에서 그의 마음을 편하게 해 주고 싶었다. 그러나 그의 감정이 얼마나 온전히 진실한지, 얼마나 곧고 걷잡을 수 없이 뜨거운지까지는 그에게 온전히 인정해 주지 못했다.\n\n마침내 그가 그녀에게 다가왔고, 사실 그녀에게는 이미 약속한 짝이 있었지만 그녀는 그 자리에서 그의 청을 받아들였다. 그러고는 방을 가로질러 종종걸음을 쳤다. “어돌퍼스,” 그녀가 말했다. “약속은 했지만 당신과는 춤을 못 추겠어요. 존 임스가 청했는데 아직 그이와 한 번도 서 본 적이 없거든요. 아시겠지요, 착하게 굴어 주실 거죠?”\n\n조금도 질투 같은 것을 하지 않는 크로즈비는 착하게 굴었고, 문 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 앉아 쉬었다.\n\n처음 몇 분 동안 임스와 릴리가 나눈 이야기는 아주 대수롭지 않은 것들이었다. 그녀는 런던에서 그를 볼 수 있으면 좋겠다는 말을 되풀이했고, 그는 물론 찾아가겠다고 했다. 그러고는 잠시 말이 없었고, 두 사람은 춤의 대형을 따라 움직였다.\n\n“저희가 언제 결혼할지는 아직 저도 전혀 몰라요.” 다시 나란히 서게 되자마자 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“네, 그렇겠지요.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 올해는 아닐 거예요. 아니, 물론 아니라고 해야겠지요.”\n\n“어쩌면 봄쯤이겠지요.” 임스가 말해 보았다. 그는 그날이 하염없이 먼 훗날로 미루어졌으면 하는 마음을 저도 모르게 품고 있었으면서도, 릴리를 아프게 하고 싶지는 않았다.\n\n“제가 그 말씀을 드리는 까닭은 이래요. 그날 여기 와 주실 수 있다면 저희가 정말 기쁠 것 같아서요. 저희 모두 존 씨를 무척 좋아하고, 저도 그날 존 씨가 여기 계셨으면 좋겠어요.”\n\n어째서 처녀들은 이런 일을 그토록 자주 하는 것일까? 어째서 자기를 사랑했던 남자에게 다른 남자와 올리는 제 혼인 자리에 와 달라고 그토록 자주 청하는 것일까? 거기에는 아무런 우쭐함도 없다. 순전한 친절과 애정에서 하는 일이다. 그들은 자기가 일으킨 슬픔을 덧내지 않고 누그러뜨려 줄 무언가를 내주려는 것이다. “저와 결혼하실 수는 없지요.” 아가씨는 그렇게 말하는 듯하다. “하지만 제가 드릴 수 있는 그다음으로 큰 축복은 당신 것이 될 거예요. 제가 다른 사람과 혼인하는 걸 보실 수 있게요.” 나는 그 뜻을 온전히 헤아리지만, 솔직히 말하자면 그렇게 내놓은 대접이 과연 반가운 것일지는 의심스럽다.\n\n이번 자리에서 존 임스도 이런 생각이었던 모양이니, 그는 그 초대를 선뜻 받아들이지 않았다.\n\n“그만한 청도 들어주지 않으시겠어요?” 그녀가 나직이 말했다.\n\n“당신을 위해서라면 무엇이든 하겠습니다.” 그가 퉁명스럽게 말했다. “거의 무엇이든지요.”\n\n“하지만 그건 못 하시겠고요?”\n\n“네, 그건 못 합니다. 그것만은 할 수 없어요.” 그러고서 그는 제 대형을 따라 나아갔고, 다음번에 두 사람이 나란히 서게 되었을 때는 다시 춤출 차례가 돌아올 때까지 말없이 있었다. 어째서였을까, 그날 밤이 지난 뒤로 릴리가 그 어느 때보다도 존 임스를 마음에 두게 된 것은. 내 말은, 제 뜻을 지닌 남자에게 품는 더 높은 존경을 그에게 느끼게 된 것은.\n\n그리고 그 카드리유에서는 크로프츠와 벨이 함께 춤을 추고 있었고, 그들 역시 릴리의 혼인 이야기를 하던 참이었다. “남자는 제 몫으로 무엇이든 겪어 낼 수 있지요.” 그가 말했다. “하지만 여자에게 가난을 겪게 할 권리는 없습니다.”\n\n“그럴지도 모르지요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“남자에게는 고생 축에도 들지 않는 것이, 남자라면 제 몫으로는 생각조차 하지 말아야 할 것이, 여자에게는 이 땅 위의 지옥이 되고 맙니다.”\n\n“그렇겠지요.” 벨은 얼굴에도 목소리에도 아무런 감정의 기색 없이 그렇게 대답했다. 그러나 그녀는 그가 한 말을 한마디도 빠짐없이 새겨 두었고, 가슴과 머리의 온 힘으로, 영혼의 온 격렬함으로 그 말들이 옳지 않다고 속으로 반박했다. “여자가 남자보다 더 많이 견디지 못한다는 듯이!” 그녀는 의사의 팔에서 풀려나 홀로 방을 가로질러 걸으며 스스로에게 말했다.\n\n# 제10장. 루펙스 부인과 어밀리아 로퍼\n\n루펙스 부인이 상냥한 여인이었다고 말한다면, 나는 나를 믿어 주는 독자를 그저 그르치는 셈이 될 것이다. 어쩌면 그녀가 상냥하지 않았다는 사실이야말로 그녀에게 물어야 할 단 하나의 큰 허물인지도 모른다. 그러나 그 허물은 정원 어디에나 제 모습을 드러내고야 마는 억세고 무성한 잡초처럼 너무나 널리 퍼져 있었고 그녀의 삶의 온갖 자리에서 하도 여러 번 돋아났으므로, 그것이 그녀를 삶의 모든 갈래에서 밉살스러운 사람으로 만들었으며 그녀를 조금 아는 이에게나 많이 아는 이에게나 똑같이 넌더리 나는 사람으로 만들었다고 해도 좋을 지경이었다. 만일 어떤 탐색자가 이 여인의 속마음에까지 다다를 수 있었더라면, 그 탐색자는 그녀가 바르게 살고자 했다는 것을—즉 예절과 품위를 갖추려고 얼마간 애를 썼다는 것을, 적어도 그러겠노라고 스스로에게 약속은 했다는 것을 알아냈으리라. 그러나 불운하게도 제 곁에 오게 된 이들을, 그중에서도 재수 없는 날에 그녀를 아내로 제 품에 받아들인 그 가엾은 사내를 들볶는 일이 그녀에게는 하도 자연스러운 것이어서, 예절은 그녀에게서 달아났고 품위는 그녀가 거처하는 곳에 살려 하지 않았다.\n\n루펙스 부인은, 내가 이미 그려 보인 대로, 아침의 헝클어진 차림과 저녁의 화려한 치장을 좋아하고 한쪽으로 조금 비뚤어진 긴 코를 개의치 않는 사람들의 눈에는 여자다운 매력이 아주 없지는 않은 여인이었다. 그녀는 그녀 나름으로 영리했고 재치 있는 말을 할 줄 알았다. 아첨도 할 줄 알았으나, 그 아첨에는 언제나 어딘가 거슬리는 데가 있었다. 그리고 그녀에게는 얼마간 의지의 힘도 있었음이 분명하다. 그렇지 않았더라면 그녀의 남편은 내가 지금 쓰고 있는 시절이 오기 전에 이미 그녀에게서 달아나 버렸을 것이기 때문이다. 그렇지 않았더라면 또한 로퍼 부인네 응접실에 발을 붙이고 그 자리를 지켜 내지도 못했을 것이다. 실제로 치렀든 치르겠노라 약속만 했든 연 백 파운드라는 돈은 로퍼 부인에게 대단히 중요한 문제였지만, 그럼에도 루펙스 부인이 버턴 크레슨트에 머문 첫 석 달이 채 지나기도 전에 그 집 여주인은 혼인한 그 하숙인 부부를 제 집에서 떨쳐 버리기를 무엇보다도 간절히 바라게 되었으니 말이다.\n\n우리 벗 임스가 자리를 비운 사이 버턴 크레슨트에서 일어난 자그마한 사건과 그 동네에서 일이 되어 가던 형편을, 나는 아마도 조니가 데일 부인의 잔치가 있은 이튿날 아침 게스트윅에서 우편으로 받은 편지 두 통을 고스란히 옮겨 놓음으로써 가장 잘 그려 보일 수 있을 것이다. 하나는 그의 벗 크래들에게서 온 것이었고, 다른 하나는 헌신적인 어밀리아에게서 온 것이었다. 이 경우에 나는 신사 쪽 편지를 먼저 내놓겠으니, 더 섬세한 쪽을 마지막까지 아껴 두는 편이 내 독자의 바람에 가장 잘 들어맞으리라 여기기 때문이다.\n\n소득세 사무국, 186◯년 9월.\n\n내 소중한 조니에게.—크레슨트에서 끔찍한 일이 벌어졌어. 자네한테 이걸 어떻게 이야기해야 할지 정말 모르겠네. 그래도 이야기는 해야겠어. 자네 조언이 필요하니까. 내가 루펙스 부인과 어떤 사이로 지내 왔는지는 자네도 알 테고, 역 승강장에서 우리가 나눈 이야기도 어쩌면 기억하고 있을 거야. 물론 나는 그 부인과 어울리는 게 좋았어. 다른 어떤 벗과 어울리는 것이나 마찬가지로 말이지. 그 부인이 잘생긴 여자라는 것쯤이야 나도 알고 있었고, 남편이 굳이 질투를 하겠다면 그거야 내가 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이지. 하지만 나는 잘못된 일이라고는 조금도 뜻한 적이 없어. 그리고 필요하다면 자네를 증인으로 불러 그걸 증명할 수도 있지 않겠나? 나는 로퍼 부인네 응접실 밖에서 그 부인에게 말 한마디 건넨 적이 없고, 그 자리에는 늘 스프루스 양이든 로퍼 부인이든 누군가가 함께 있었어. 자네도 알다시피 그 사람은 이따금 지독하게 술을 마시지만, 아주 곤드레만드레가 되는 법은 없다고 나는 생각해. 그런데 어젯밤 아홉 시쯤에 그 사람이 그런 술판을 한바탕 벌이고 집에 돌아왔단 말이지. 제미마의 말로는[제미마는 로퍼 부인네 응접실 하녀야] 사흘 내내 극장에서 그러고 있었다는 거야. 우리는 화요일 이후로 그 사람을 보지 못했고. 그 사람은 곧장 아래층 거실로 들어가더니 제미마를 나한테 올려보내 나를 보자고 한다는 말을 전하게 했어. 루펙스 부인이 방 안에 있다가 하녀가 나를 부르는 소리를 듣고는 벌떡 일어나며, 피를 볼 일이 벌어질 거라면 자기는 이 집을 나가겠다고 선언하더군. 방에는 그 밖에 스프루스 양밖에 없었는데, 그 노부인은 한마디도 하지 않고 촛대를 들고 위층으로 올라가 버렸어. 이거 참 딱한 꼴이었다는 건 자네도 인정해야 할 거야. 아래층 거실에 술 취한 사내가 있는데 내가 어쩌란 말인가? 그런데도 부인은 내가 내려가야 한다고 여기는 눈치였어. “그이가 여기까지 올라오면,” 하고 부인이 말하더군. “희생되는 건 저예요. 그 사람이 술기운에 노여움이 타오르면 무슨 짓까지 할 수 있는지 당신은 조금도 모르세요!” 자, 나로 말하자면 어떤 사내를 두고도 그리 겁을 낼 사람이 아니라는 걸 자네도 알 거야. 하지만 어쩌자고 내가 이런 식으로 소동에 말려들어야 한단 말인가? 나는 아무 짓도 하지 않았는데. 게다가 만일 싸움이 벌어지고 부인이 예상하는 대로 무슨 일이 생긴다면—피를 본다든지, 주먹다짐을 한다든지, 아니면 그 사람이 부지깽이로 내 머리를 후려친다든지 한다면, 나는 사무국에서 어떻게 되겠나? 자네나 나처럼 관청에 몸담은 사람은 아무나처럼 싸움질을 할 수가 없어. 그 순간 내가 그토록 절실하게 느낀 것이 바로 그거였네. “내려가세요,” 부인이 말했어. “내가 당신 발치에서 살해되는 꼴을 보고 싶지 않으시다면요.” 피셔는 내 말이 사실이라면 그 두 사람이 미리 다 짜 놓은 것이 틀림없다고 하더군. 나는 그렇게 생각하지 않아. 그 부인이 나를 정말 좋아한다고 나는 믿으니까 말이야. 게다가 그 두 사람이 무슨 일에서든 마음이 맞는 법이 없다는 건 누구나 아는 사실이고. 어쨌든 부인은 나더러 내려가 달라고 간곡히 청했어. 그래서 나는 내려갔지. 그런데 계단 아래에 이르러 제미마와 마주쳤을 때, 그 사람이 거실 안을 이리저리 왔다 갔다 하는 소리가 들리더군. “조심하세요, 크래들 씨,” 하고 하녀가 말했는데, 얼굴만 봐도 그 애가 몹시 겁에 질려 있는 걸 알 수 있었어.\n\n바로 그때 현관 탁자에 놓인 내 모자가 눈에 띄었고, 나 자신을 벗의 손에 맡겨야겠다는 생각이 들었어. 물론 나는 식당에 있는 그 사내가 무섭지는 않았지. 하지만 로퍼 부인네 집 안에서, 어쩌면 목숨을 걸고 몸싸움을 벌이는 것이 과연 옳은 일이었겠나? 나는 그 부인의 이해도 생각해야 했네. 그래서 나는 모자를 집어 들고 유유히 대문으로 걸어 나왔어. “그 사람한테 전해라,” 나는 제미마에게 말했지. “나는 집에 없다고.” 그러고서 곧장 피셔의 집으로 갔네. 그를 내 입회인으로 삼아 루펙스에게 보낼 작정이었지. 그런데 피셔는 체스 클럽에 가 있더군.\n\n이런 일에는 지체할 겨를이 없다고 생각했기에 나는 그 클럽까지 찾아가 그를 불러냈어. 피셔가 얼마나 침착한 친구인지는 자네도 알지. 무슨 일이 있어도 흥분하는 법이 없는 사람이야. 내가 이야기를 들려주었더니 그는 하룻밤 자면서 생각해 보겠다고 하더군. 그래서 나는 그가 판을 끝낼 때까지 클럽 앞을 오락가락하며 기다려야 했네. 피셔는 내가 버턴 크레슨트로 돌아가도 괜찮으리라고 여기는 눈치였지만, 물론 나는 그게 말도 안 되는 소리라는 걸 알고 있었지. 그래서 결국 나는 그의 집으로 가서 소파에서 자고, 아침에 사람을 보내 내 물건 몇 가지를 가져오게 했어. 나는 그가 오늘 아침 출근하기 전에 일어나 루펙스를 만나 주기를 바랐네. 하지만 그는 미루는 편이 낫겠다고 했고, 그래서 근무 시간이 끝나는 대로 곧 극장으로 그를 찾아가겠다는 거야.\n\n자네가 이 일에 관해 아는 바를 곧바로 편지로 써 보내 주었으면 하네. 로퍼네 다른 사람들을 이 일에 끌어들이고 싶지 않아서 자네에게 부탁하는 걸세. 참 난처한 노릇이야. 지난 분기 셋돈 때문에 당장 그 집을 떠날 수가 없거든. 안 그랬으면 벌써 짐을 싸서 달아났을 거야. 그 집은 자네에게도 나에게도 어울리는 곳이 아니야. 내 말을 믿어도 좋네, 조니 도련님. 그리고 A. R.에 대해서도 자네한테 해 줄 이야기가 있지만, 공연히 말썽을 일으키고 싶지는 않군. 아무튼 곧바로 편지를 써 주게. 그리고 지금 생각해 보니 피셔에게 편지를 쓰는 편이 낫겠어. 그래야 그가 자네 편지를 루펙스에게 보여 줄 수 있을 테니까—자네가 아는 한 그 부인과 나 사이에는 그저 우정 말고는 아무것도 없었다는 것, 그리고 물론 자네는 내 벗이니 모든 것을 알고 있었을 것이라는 정도만 써 주면 되네. 내가 오늘 밤 로퍼네로 돌아갈지 어떨지는 그 면담이 끝난 뒤 피셔가 무어라고 하느냐에 달려 있어.\n\n잘 있게, 이 친구야! 즐겁게 지내고 있기를 바라고, L. D.도 잘 있기를 바라네.—자네의 진실한 벗,\n\n존 임스는 어밀리아에게서 온 편지를 뜯기 전에 이 편지를 두 번 읽었다. 그는 아직 로퍼 양에게서 편지를 받아 본 적이 없었으나, 젊은 사내가 젊은 아가씨에게서 첫 편지를 받았을 때 대개 느끼는 그 뜨거운 마음은 조금도 느끼지 못했다. 그 순간 어밀리아에 대한 기억은 그에게 역겨운 것이었고, 그렇게 하는 것이 위험하리라는 생각만 아니었더라면 그는 편지를 뜯지도 않은 채 불 속에 던져 버렸을 것이다. 벗 크래들로 말하자면 그가 부끄럽게 여겨지지 않을 수 없었다—루펙스 씨에게서 달아났다는 것이 부끄러운 것이 아니라, 그 도망을 거짓 구실로 변명한다는 것이 부끄러웠다.\n\n그러고서 마침내 그는 어밀리아의 편지를 뜯었다. “더없이 소중한 존” 하고 편지는 시작되었는데, 그 글자를 읽으면서 그는 종이를 손가락 사이에서 구겨 버렸다. 그것은 반듯한 여자 글씨로 쓰여 있었으니, 글자에 둥근 획 대신 뾰족한 모가 서 있었으나 그래도 읽기에는 아주 또렷했고, 한 낱말 한 낱말마다 단단히 정해 둔 속셈이 들어 있는 듯이 보였다.\n\n더없이 소중한 존에게.—당신에게 이런 말로 편지를 쓰자니 참 이상한 기분이 들어요. 하지만 당신은 정말 더없이 소중한 사람인걸요. 내게 그렇게 부를 권리가 없나요? 그리고 당신은 내 사람이 아닌가요? 나는 당신 사람이 아니고요?\n\n그는 다시 종이를 손안에 구겨 쥐었고, 그러면서 여기 길게 옮길 필요는 없는 말을 중얼거렸다. 그래도 그는 편지를 계속 읽어 나갔다.\n\n우리 둘이 서로를 더없이 잘 알고 있다는 것을 나는 알아요. 그리고 그럴 때에는 마음이 마음에게 거리낌 없이 말하도록 두어야 하지요. 그것이 내 심정이고, 당신의 가슴속에서도 같은 심정을 찾아내리라고 나는 믿어요. 사랑받는다는 건 달콤한 일이 아닌가요? 나는 그렇게 느껴요. 그리고 더없이 소중한 존, 터놓고 솔직하게 말씀드리지만, 이 가슴에는 당신을 두고 질투할 자리가 조금도 없답니다. 그러기에는 내가 당신의 명예를, 그리고 내 자신의—매력이라고 말하고 싶지만 그러면 당신이 나를 우쭐댄다고 하시겠죠—그것을 너무나 굳게 믿고 있는걸요. 정말이에요. 내가 L. D.에 대해 한 말을 진심이었다고 여기시면 안 돼요. 물론 당신은 어린 시절의 벗들을 만나 반가우시겠지요. 그런 즐거운 기쁨을 당신에게서 아까워하는 일 따위는 당신의 어밀리아의 마음과는 아주 거리가 먼 일이랍니다. 당신의 벗들이 언젠가는 내 벗들도 되었으면 해요. [또 한 번 종이가 구겨졌다.] 그리고 그들 가운데 당신이 각별히 좋아하시던 사람이 있다면, 어떤 진짜 L. D.가 있다면, 나는 그 사람도 각별히 내 마음에 받아들이겠어요.\n\n자기 어밀리아가 이렇게까지 장담하는 것은 그로서는 견디기 어려운 노릇이어서, 그는 편지를 내던지며 어디에서 구원을 얻을 수 있을까—자살에서 얻을까 아니면 식민지로 달아나 얻을까 하고 생각했다. 그러나 이내 다시 편지를 집어 들고 그 쓴 잔을 바닥까지 들이켰다.\n\n그리고 당신이 떠나시기 전에 내가 당신에게 짜증스럽게 굴었다면, 당신의 어밀리아를 용서해 주셔야 해요. 당신이 없는 그 한 달 동안 내 앞에 놓인 것이라고는 비참함밖에 없었으니까요. 여기에는 내 마음에 맞는 사람이 아무도 없어요—당연한 일이지요. 그리고 당신이 없는 동안 내가 행복하기를 당신도 바라지는 않으시겠죠? 정말이지, 당신이 무엇을 바라시든 당신이 곁에 있지 않고서는 나는 두 번 다시 행복해질 수 없어요. 한 줄만이라도 답장을 써 보내 주세요. 그리고 나의 이 헌신에 고마워하고 있다고 말해 주세요.\n\n그리고 이제 알려 드려야 할 것이, 이 집에서 딱한 일이 벌어졌다는 거예요. 나는 당신 벗 크래들 씨가 조금이라도 잘 처신했다고는 생각하지 않아요. 그이가 루펙스 부인과 늘 어떻게 지내 왔는지는 당신도 기억하시죠. 어머니는 그 일로 몹시 언짢아하셨지만 무슨 말씀을 하시기는 싫어하셨어요. 물론 부인네 이름이 걸린 일이니 조심스러운 노릇이지요. 그런데 지난주부터 루펙스가 지독하게 질투를 하기 시작했고, 우리 모두 무슨 일이 벌어지리라는 걸 알고 있었어요. 그 부인은 술수가 많은 여자이지만 나쁜 뜻이 있었던 것 같지는 않아요—그저 남편을 미치도록 몰아붙이려는 것뿐이었죠. 그 사람이 어제 여기에 발작을 일으킨 채 들어와서 크래들을 보자고 했는데, 크래들 씨는 겁을 먹고 모자를 집어 들고 나가 버렸어요. 그건 옳은 일이 아니었죠. 떳떳하다면 왜 자리를 지키고 서서 오해를 풀지 않았을까요? 어머니 말씀대로 그런 일은 이 집에 나쁜 소문을 안겨 주는 거예요. 루펙스는 어젯밤에 오늘 아침 소득세 사무국으로 쳐들어가서 위원들이며 서기들이며 모두가 보는 앞에서 크래들을 끌어내겠다고 큰소리를 쳤어요. 정말 그렇게 하면 그 일이 신문에까지 나서 온 런던이 그 이야기로 떠들썩해지겠죠. 그 부인이야 그걸 좋아하겠지요, 그 사람이 마음 쓰는 건 오로지 남들 입에 오르내리는 것뿐이니까요. 하지만 어머니 집이 어떻게 되겠는지 좀 생각해 보세요. 당신이 여기 계셨으면 좋겠어요. 당신의 높은 분별과 용기라면 모든 일을 단번에 바로잡아 놓으실 텐데요—적어도 나는 그렇게 생각해요.\n\n이 편지의 답장을 받을 때까지 나는 시간을 세고 있을 거예요. 그리고 나보다 먼저 당신 편지를 손에 쥘 우체부가 부러울 거예요. 부디 곧바로 써 주세요. 월요일 아침까지 소식이 없으면 나는 무슨 일이 생겼다고 생각하겠어요. 그리운 옛 벗들 사이에 계신다 해도 당신의 어밀리아에게 편지 쓸 짬쯤은 분명 내실 수 있을 거예요.\n\n어머니는 이 루펙스네 일로 몹시 마음이 상해 계세요. 당신이 여기 계셔서 조언해 주신다면 이렇게까지 힘들지는 않을 텐데 하고 말씀하세요. 어머니께는 정말 가혹한 일이에요. 모두를 위해 이 집을 번듯하고 편안하게 꾸리려고 그토록 애쓰시는데 말이에요. 당신의 사랑하는 어머님을 내가 알기만 한다면—언젠가는 알게 되기를 바라지만요—그분께 내 문안과 사랑을 보내 드리고 싶고, 당신 누이동생에게도, 그리고 우리가 서로 어떤 사이인지 그이에게 이야기하실 생각이 있으시다면 L. D.에게도 보내 드리고 싶어요. 그럼 이만, 당신의\n\n언제나 애정 어린 당신의 연인,\n\n가엾은 임스는 이 다정한 편지의 어느 대목에서도 조금도 흐뭇함을 느끼지 못했다. 그러나 그중에서도 마지막 문단이 가장 고약했다. 이런 여자가 자기 어머니에게, 자기 누이동생에게, 심지어 릴리 데일에게까지 사랑을 보낸다는 것을 그가 어찌 참을 수 있단 말인가! 어밀리아 로퍼 같은 사람이 릴리의 이름을 입에 올린다는 것만으로도 거기에는 더러움이 묻는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그런데도 어밀리아 로퍼는, 그녀가 그에게 장담한 대로, 그의 사람이었다. 지금 이 순간 그녀가 아무리 싫었다 해도 그는 자기가 그녀의 사람이라고 믿고 있었다. 그는 그녀가 자기에 대해 일종의 소유권을 얻어 냈으며 자기의 운명이 자기를 그녀에게 묶어 놓으리라고 느꼈다. 그는 지금껏 그녀에게 사랑의 말이라고는 거의 하지 않았다—적어도 그 자체로 무게가 나갈 만한 말은 거의 하지 않았다. 그러나 그 몇 마디 가운데 자기가 그녀를 사랑한다고 말한 것이 한두 번쯤 분명히 있었다. 게다가 그는 그녀에게 그 치명적인 쪽지를 써 보내지 않았던가! 이러니저러니 해도, 게스트윅 뒤편에 있는 저 큰 저수지로—해머샴 운하에 물을 대 주는 그 저수지로—나가서 이 비참한 목숨을 끝내 버리는 편이 그에게 낫지 않겠는가?\n\n그날 그는 피셔에게 편지를 썼고, 크래들에게도 편지를 썼다. 그 편지들에 대해서는 아무 어려움도 느끼지 않았다. 피셔에게 그는 루펙스 부인에 관한 한 크래들이 자기 자신만큼이나 결백하다고 믿는다고 밝혔다. “그 친구는 혼인한 여자에게 수작을 걸 만한 부류의 사내가 아닙니다.” 그는 그렇게 썼는데, 그 편지가 소득세 사무국에 이르렀을 때 크래들에게는 다소 언짢은 말이었다. 이 자그마한 모험이 제 딴에는 동료 서기들 사이에서 자기에게 연애에 성공한 사내라는 평판을 안겨 주게 되어 있다고 여겼고, 그 평판이 이 신사에게는 싫지 않았기 때문이다. 폭탄이 처음 터졌을 때, 그 지독하게 질투에 사로잡힌 사내가 술기운과 애욕에 함께 달아올라 아래층 거실에서 길길이 뛰던 그때에는 크래들도 이 일이 지긋지긋하게 괴롭다고 느꼈다. 그러나 사흘째 되는 날 아침—그는 벗 피셔의 소파에서 두 밤을 지냈다—그는 이 일이 다소 자랑스러워지기 시작했고, 다른 서기들의 입에서 루펙스 부인의 이름이 오르내리는 것도 싫지 않게 되었다. 그러니 피셔가 게스트윅에서 온 편지를 읽어 주었을 때 그는 벗의 말투가 그리 달갑지 않았다. “하하하,” 그가 웃으며 말했다. “바로 내가 저 친구한테 시키고 싶었던 말이 저거야. 혼인한 여자에게 수작이라니, 원. 아니지. 그런 짓을 할 사람으로 치면 나는 런던에서 맨 꼴찌일걸.”\n\n“참말이지, 코들, 나도 자네가 그렇다고 생각하네.” 피셔가 말했다. “맨 꼴찌지.”\n\n그러고 나서 가엾은 크래들은 마음이 편치 않았다. 그날 오후 그는 용감하게 버턴 크레슨트로 가서 거기서 저녁을 먹었다. 루펙스 씨도 루펙스 부인도 보이지 않았고, 로퍼 부인이 그에게 그들의 이름을 입에 올리지도 않았다. 저녁나절이 되어 그는 용기를 내어 스프루스 양에게 두 사람이 어디 있느냐고 물어보았다. 그러나 그 노부인은 그저 엄숙하게 고개를 저으며 자기는 그런 짓거리에 대해서는 아무것도 모른다고, 아무렴 자기는 모른다고 잘라 말했다.\n\n그런데 어밀리아 로퍼에게서 온 그 편지를 두고 존 임스는 어떻게 해야 한단 말인가? 그는 어떤 답장이든 매우 위험하리라고 느꼈으나, 그렇다고 답장 없이 내버려 두는 것도 안전하지 않다고 느꼈다. 그는 혼자서 게스트윅 공유지를 가로질러, 게스트윅 저택의 숲을 지나, 드 게스트 경의 정원에 늘어선 커다란 느릅나무 가로수 길을 따라 걸어 올라가면서 이 곤경에서 어떻게 빠져나갈 수 있을지 궁리해 보았다. 이곳 바로 이 땅 위를 그는 젊은 시절에 수십 번도 더 거닐었다. 시골집의 순진함 말고는 아무것도 알지 못하던 그 시절에, 릴리 데일을 생각하며 그녀를 제 아내로 삼겠노라고 스스로에게 맹세하면서. 이곳에서 그는 시구를 엮었고 드높은 희망으로 제 야심을 길렀으며 허공에 화려한 성을 지어 올렸으니, 그 성마다 릴리언이 여왕으로 군림하고 있었다. 그리고 그 시절 그는 자기가 어설프고 가난하며 어머니와 누이동생 말고는 세상 누구의 눈길도 받지 못한다는 것을 알고 있었으면서도 제 희망 속에서 행복했다—그 희망이 이루어지리라고 정말로 믿도록 스스로를 가르친 적은 한 번도 없었으면서도 그 희망 속에서 행복했던 것이다. 그러나 이제는 그의 희망에도 생각에도 그를 행복하게 해 줄 것이 아무것도 없었다. 모든 것이 캄캄하고 비참하고 무너져 내려 있었다. 릴리가 다른 사내에게 주어질 바에야, 그가 어밀리아 로퍼와 혼인한들 결국 무슨 상관이란 말인가? 그러나 그때 그날 밤 문틈으로 보았던 어밀리아의 모습이 기억에 떠올랐고, 그는 그런 아내와 함께하는 삶이란 살아 있는 죽음이 되리라고 스스로 시인했다.\n\n어느 순간 그는 어머니에게 모든 것을 털어놓고 어밀리아의 편지에 대한 답장은 어머니께 맡겨 버릴까 하고 생각했다. 최악의 경우가 닥친다 해도 로퍼네 사람들이 그를 아주 결딴낼 수는 없을 터였다. 그들이 그를 걸어 소송을 낼 수 있다는 것, 그를 몇 해 동안 옥에 가둘 수 있다는 것, 그를 사무국에서 쫓겨나게 하고 온갖 신문에 이름이 나게 할 수 있다는 것쯤은 그도 알고 있는 듯싶었다. 그러나 그 모든 것도 누군가 다른 사람이 그를 대신해 장갑을 던져 주기만 한다면 견뎌 낼 수 있을 것 같았다. 그가 도저히 할 수 없다고 느낀 단 한 가지는, 사랑한다고 공언했던 처녀에게 편지를 써서 이제는 사랑하지 않는다고 말하는 일이었다. 그런 말을 제 손으로 종이 위에 적을 수는 없다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 또한 그 자신이 잘 알고 있듯이, 마음이 변했노라고 그녀의 얼굴을 마주 보고 말할 용기도 낼 수 없었다. 자기 대신 싸워 줄 다정한 기사를 찾아내지 못하는 한 자기는 제 어밀리아의 제물이 되고 말리라는 것을 그는 알았다. 그러다가 그는 또다시 어머니를 떠올렸다.\n\n그러나 집에 돌아왔을 때 그는 자기 처지를 어머니에게 알리겠다는 결심에서 여전히 조금도 가까워지지 않은 채였다. 그 산책은 그에게 아무 도움이 되지 못했고 그는 아무것도 마음을 정하지 못했다고 말해 두어야겠다. 그는 그 시간의 절반이 넘도록 저 해로운 공중누각을 짓고 있었다. 그것도 옛날처럼 그 성을 지으면서 스스로 행복해질 수 있는 그런 성이 아니라, 잔혹한 지하 감옥이 딸린, 빛 한 줄기 겨우 들까 말까 한 캄캄한 성이었다. 그 모든 건물 안에서 그의 상상은 릴리를 크로즈비 씨의 아내로 그려 보였다. 그는 그것을 기정사실로 받아들이고 나서, 제 비참함 속에서 그 남편의 못된 행실과 모진 마음으로 그녀를 저 자신만큼이나 비참하게 만드는 일에 몰두했다. 그는 생각을 해 보려고, 어떻게 할지 마음을 정해 보려고 애썼다. 그러나 눈앞에 놓인 일에 마음이 도리질을 칠 때, 생각하는 일만큼 어려운 일은 없다. 그럴 때의 마음이란 물가에까지 끌려왔으나 마시기를 마다하는 말과 같다. 그리하여 조니는 어밀리아의 편지에 답장을 할지 말지 여전히 망설이면서 집으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 답장을 하지 않는다면, 버턴 크레슨트로 돌아갔을 때 그는 어떻게 처신할 것인가?\n\n로퍼 양이 편지를 쓰면서 이 모든 것을 알고 있었다는 것, 그리고 조니의 처지가 그를 위해—애정 어린 그의 연인에 의해—빈틈없이 마련되어 있었다는 것은 굳이 내가 말할 필요도 없으리라.\n\n# 제11장. 사교 생활\n\n루펙스 씨 부부는 크래들이 로퍼 부인의 후한 밥상으로 돌아오던 바로 그날, 부부다운 더없는 화목 속에서 함께 스위트브레드를 먹었다. 두 사람은 극장 근처에서 스위트브레드와 그 밖의 제철 별미 몇 가지를 나란히 먹었고, 온갖 앙금을 쓴 맥주와 물 탄 브랜디로 씻어 내렸다. 그러나 이 화해에 대해 크래들은 아무것도 들은 바가 없었다. 그래서 그가 스프루스 양에게 그 질문을 던지고 몇 분 지나지 않아 두 사람이 나란히 응접실로 들어오는 것을 보았을 때, 그는 분명 놀랐다.\n\n루펙스는 심성이 고약한 사람도 아니었고, 천성이 사나운 사람도 아니었다. 그는 스위트브레드와 자그마한 만찬을 좋아하는 사람이었고, 뜨겁게 데운 물 탄 브랜디가 너무나 소중한 사람이었다. 그의 품에 안긴 아내가 그에게 좋은 반려가 되어 주었더라면, 그는 세상을 살아가되 남의 존경까지는 못 받더라도 적어도 드러내 놓고 망신은 당하지 않은 채 지나갈 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 사내에게 물 탄 브랜디에서 얻는 것 말고는 아무 위안도 남겨 주지 않는 여자였다. 여덟 해 동안 두 사람은 부부로 지냈다. 그리고 때때로—이런 말을 하기가 나는 참으로 애석하지만—그는 그녀가 유부녀로서 지을 수 있는 마지막 죄를 짓고 자기를 떠나 주기를 바라다시피 하는 지경까지 몰리곤 했다. 그 비참함 속에서라면 어떤 탈출구든 그에게는 반가웠으리라. 기력만 넉넉했더라면 그는 무대 배경을 그리는 제 재주를 챙겨 오스트레일리아로—아니, 세상이라는 무대에 알려진 가장 먼 배경 전환의 자리로 떠나 버렸을 것이다. 그러나 그는 무르고 게으르고 제 몸을 아끼는 사람이었다. 그래서 어느 순간이든, 그의 비참함이 아무리 사무친다 한들, 자그마한 만찬 한 상과 몇 마디 부드러운 말과 물 탄 브랜디 한 잔이면 그를 도로 돌려놓을 수 있었다. 두 잔째는 그를 세상에서 가장 다정한 남편으로 만들어 주었다. 그러나 석 잔째는 그에게 자기가 당한 온갖 억울함의 기억을 되살려 주고, 아내에게든 온 세상에게든 맞설 용기를 주었다—손끝에 어쩌다 부지깽이라도 걸리는 날이면 주위의 가구까지 결딴내 가면서. 그러나 그의 성품이 지닌 이런 특징들을 크래들은 알지 못했다. 그리하여 우리 벗은 그가 아내를 팔에 낀 채 응접실로 들어서는 것을 보고 깜짝 놀랐던 것이다.\n\n“크래들 씨, 손을 주시오.” 루펙스가 말했다. 그는 물 탄 브랜디 두 잔째까지는 나아갔으나 그 이상은 허락받지 못한 참이었다. “우리 사이에 오해가 있었소. 그건 잊읍시다.”\n\n“내가 아는 크래들 씨라면,” 부인이 말했다. “신사가 손을 내미는 마당에 노여움을 품고 있기에는 너무나 신사다운 분이지요.”\n\n“아, 그럼요.” 크래들이 말했다. “저야 정말—아니, 결국 아무 일도 없었다니 참말 기쁩니다.” 그러고 나서 그는 두 사람과 차례로 악수했다. 그러자 스프루스 양이 일어나 깊이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는 역시 부부와 악수를 나누었다.\n\n“크래들 씨는 장가든 사람이 아니니,” 루펙스가 말했다. “남편 된 자의 가슴속에서 무엇이 움직이는지 이해하지 못하실 거요. 저 여자를 향한 내 마음이 나로서 감당하기에 너무 벅찼던 순간들이 있었소.”\n\n“자, 루펙스, 그만해요.” 그녀가 아직 손에 들고 있던 낡은 양산으로 그를 장난스레 툭툭 치며 말했다.\n\n“그리고 그날 밤 내가 당신을 식당으로 불러 내렸을 때, 그녀를 향한 내 마음이 나로서 감당하기에 너무 벅찼다는 말도 나는 서슴지 않고 하겠소.”\n\n“이제 다 잘 풀렸다니 기쁩니다.” 크래들이 말했다.\n\n“참말 다행입니다.” 스프루스 양이 말했다.\n\n“그러니 그 이야기는 더 할 것 없어요.” 루펙스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“한마디만.” 루펙스가 손을 내저으며 말했다. “크래들 씨, 그날 밤 당신이 내 부름에 응하지 않은 것을 나는 참으로 다행으로 여기오. 만일 응했더라면—이제야 털어놓지만—만일 응했더라면 피를 보고야 말았을 거요. 내가 잘못 알았소. 내 잘못은 인정하오. 하지만 피를 보고야 말았을 거요.”\n\n“저런, 저런, 저런.” 스프루스 양이 말했다.\n\n“스프루스 양,” 루펙스가 말을 이었다. “사람의 가슴이 그 사람으로서는 감당 못 할 만큼 거세지는 순간이 있는 법이지요.”\n\n“그렇겠지요.” 스프루스 양이 말했다.\n\n“자, 루펙스, 이제 됐어요.” 그의 아내가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 이제 됐지. 하지만 크래들 씨한테는 그가 나한테 오지 않아 다행이라고 말해 두는 것이 도리라고 생각하오. 크래들 씨, 당신 벗이 어제 네 시 반에 극장으로 나를 찾아오는 수고를 해 주셨소. 그런데 그때 나는 밧줄에 매달려 있던 참이라 도무지 내려갈 형편이 못 되었소. 언제든 다섯 시에 두 분을 다 뵈면 반갑겠고, 보 스트리트의 「냄비와 부지깽이」에서 고기 한 점에 술 한 잔으로 지난 앙금은 다 묻어 버립시다.”\n\n“참으로 친절하십니다.” 크래들이 말했다.\n\n“그리고 루펙스 부인도 우리와 함께할 거요. 「냄비와 부지깽이」 이층에는 아주 아늑한 골방이 하나 있소. 그리고 스프루스 양께서도 황송하게 함께해 주신다면—”\n\n“아이고, 나로 말하면 늙은 여자 아닙니까, 어르신.”\n\n“아니—아니—아니오.” 루펙스가 말했다. “그건 인정 못 하오. 자, 크래들, 어떻소?—넷이서 오붓하게 자그마한 만찬 한 상 말이오.”\n\n지금 이런 기분에 잠긴 루펙스 씨를 보는 일은 분명 유쾌했다—피를 보고야 말았을 그 다른 기분에 잠겼을 때보다야 훨씬 유쾌했다. 그러나 그가 지금 유쾌하기는 해도, 그럼에도 그가 아주 말짱한 정신은 아니라는 것이 뻔히 드러났다. 그래서 크래들은 그 자그마한 만찬의 날짜를 정하지 않고, 그저 언젠가 앞날에 기꺼이 그러겠노라고만 말해 두었다.\n\n“자 이제, 루펙스, 그만 자러 가는 게 좋겠어요.” 그의 아내가 말했다. “오늘 참 고단한 하루였잖아요.”\n\n“그럼 당신은, 여보?”\n\n“나도 곧 갈 거예요. 자, 우스운 꼴 보이지 말고 어서 올라가요. 어서—” 그러고서 그녀는 열린 문에 바싹 붙어 서서 그가 지나가기를 기다렸다.\n\n“나는 아무래도 여기 그대로 있으면서 뜨거운 걸 한 잔 할까 싶은데.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“루펙스, 또 나를 돋우고 싶은 거예요?” 부인이 그렇게 말하며 그를 흘겨보았는데, 그 눈길이 무슨 뜻인지 그는 훤히 알아들었다. 그는 싸울 기분이 아니었고, 지금은 피를 보고 싶은 마음도 없었다. 그래서 그는 가기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 그는 가면서 새로운 싸움을 준비했다. “나는 무슨 끔찍한 일을 저지르고 말 거야, 틀림없이. 내가 잘 알아.” 그는 장화를 벗으며 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“아이고, 크래들 씨,” 물러가는 남편의 등 뒤로 문을 닫자마자 루펙스 부인이 말했다. “이 잊지 못할 며칠 동안 일어난 일이 있고서야 내가 무슨 낯으로 당신 얼굴을 다시 본단 말이에요?” 그러고 나서 그녀는 소파에 앉아 고운 삼베 손수건에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n“그거야,” 크래들이 말했다. “우리 같은 벗들 사이에 무슨 대수겠습니까?”\n\n“하지만 그 일이 당신 관청에까지 알려졌으니—물론 알려졌겠죠, 그 신사분이 극장으로 그이를 찾아 내려갔으니까요!—나는 도무지 견디고 살아남을 것 같지가 않아요.”\n\n“보시다시피 저는 누구든 보내지 않을 수 없었습니다, 루펙스 부인.”\n\n“탓하는 게 아니에요, 크래들 씨. 이 서글픈 처지에 내가 남을 탓할 자격이 없다는 건 나도 잘 알아요. 그리고 신사분들끼리 서로 어떤 마음인지 안다고 내세울 생각도 없어요. 하지만 내 이름이 그런 식으로 당신 이름과 나란히 오르내렸다는 게—아아! 크래들 씨, 내가 무슨 낯으로 당신 얼굴을 다시 볼지 모르겠어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 또다시 제 얼굴을 손수건에 묻었다.\n\n“고운 것은 고운 행실에 달렸지요.” 스프루스 양이 말했다. 그 말투에는 감춰 둔 뜻이 잔뜩 실린 듯한 무엇이 있었다.\n\n“바로 그래요, 스프루스 양.” 루펙스 부인이 말했다. “그게 지금 이 순간 내 유일한 위안이에요. 크래들 씨는 남의 약점을 이용하는 짓 따위는 하찮게 여기실 신사분이니까요—그건 내가 잘 알지요.” 그러고서 그녀는 손수건을 쥔 손 너머로 그를 건너다보는 데 용케 성공했다.\n\n“그런 짓이야 제가 할 리 없지요, 정말입니다.” 크래들이 말했다. “그러니까 제 말은—” 그러고서 그는 말을 멈추었다. 그는 루펙스 부인 일로 곤경에 빠지고 싶지는 않았다. 질투가 발작한 그녀의 남편과 맞닥뜨리는 일이야 결코 바라지 않았다. 그러나 유부녀를 흠모하는 사내로 남의 입에 오르내리는 것은 마음에 들었고, 그 부인의 반짝이는 눈도 마음에 들었다. 반쯤 눈이 먼 가엾은 나방이 촛불의 불꽃 속으로 제 몸과 날개를 던져 넣었다가 몸이 상하고 지져지는 고통을 겪고도, 그러고서도 그 교훈을 받아들이지 못한 채 아주 결딴날 때까지 몇 번이고 되돌아가는 일이 있다. 가엾은 크래들이 바로 그런 나방이었다. 그 불꽃에서 그가 얻을 온기라고는 없었다. 그 불빛에는 아름다움이 없었다—떳떳지 못한 사랑의 그 거짓 광채조차 없었다. 그에게 올 것은 상함이었으니—앞으로 날 힘을 아주 없애 버릴, 날개를 못 쓰게 잘라 내는 상함이었다. 고집을 부린다면 상함, 그리고 십중팔구 파멸이었다. 그러나 루펙스 부인과의 친분에서 그에게 단 한 시간의 행복도 돌아올 수 없었다고 말해도 좋으리라. 그는 그녀에게 아무런 사랑도 느끼지 않았다. 그는 그녀가 두려웠고, 여러 가지로 그녀가 싫었다. 그러나 나방 같은 그의 나약함과 무지와 눈멂 속에서는, 촛불 가까이 날아다니도록 허락받는다는 것이 대단한 일로 여겨졌다. 아아, 내 벗들이여, 생각해 보기만 한다면, 그대들 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 나방이었으며, 지금 날개가 어지간히 타 버린 채 볼품없이 돌아다니고 있으며, 몸뚱이가 딱하게도 그을려 있는가!\n\n그러나 크래들 씨가 촛불의 불꽃 속으로 한 번 더 뛰어들 이번 기회를 붙잡을지 말지 마음을 정하기도 전에—하기야 그 일에 관해서는 스프루스 양이 그 자리에 있다는 것이 아무래도 거슬린다는 느낌을 지울 수 없었지만—방문이 열리고 어밀리아 로퍼가 이 자리에 끼어들었다.\n\n“어머, 이런. 루펙스 부인.” 그녀가 말했다. “게다가 크래들 씨까지!”\n\n“그리고 스프루스 양도 계세요, 아가씨.” 루펙스 부인이 그 노부인을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n“나는 그저 늙은 여자일 뿐입니다.” 스프루스 양이 말했다.\n\n“그럼요, 스프루스 양 계신 거 보여요.” 어밀리아가 말했다. “제가 무슨 뜻을 담아 한 말은 아니었어요, 정말이에요.”\n\n“그렇고말고요, 아가씨.” 루펙스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“다만 저는 두 분이 아주— 그러니까, 제가 마지막으로 들었을 때는 그런 줄로 알았거든요— 하지만 다툼이 다 풀렸다면 저만큼 기뻐할 사람도 없죠.”\n\n“다툼은 다 풀렸습니다.” 크래들이 말했다.\n\n“루펙스 씨가 흡족하시다면야 저도 그렇고말고요.” 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“루펙스 씨는 흡족해하세요.” 루펙스 부인이 말했다. “그리고 아가씨, 내가 한마디 해 두죠. 아가씨도 시집갈 날을 기다리는 처지인 모양이니—”\n\n“루펙스 부인, 저는 시집갈 날을 기다리는 처지가 아니에요—적어도 그리 대단히는 아니라고요.”\n\n“어머, 나는 그런 줄 알았죠. 그럼 이 말은 해 두겠어요. 아가씨가 제 남편이라는 걸 갖게 되면, 만사를 곧게 건사하기가 그리 쉽지 않다는 걸 알게 될 거예요. 하숙이란 게 그래서 못쓰는 거죠. 조그만 일이라도 있으면 온 집안이 다 알아 버리거든요. 안 그래요, 스프루스 양?”\n\n“하숙이 살림 나는 것보다야 훨씬 편하지요.” 스프루스 양이 말했다. 그녀는 친척인 로퍼네 사람들을 어지간히 어려워하며 살고 있었다.\n\n“온 집안이 다 안다고요?” 어밀리아가 말했다. “아니, 신사라는 사람이 밤중에 술에 취해 돌아와서 한집에 사는 다른 신사를 죽이겠다고 을러대는데야. 그리고 숙녀라는 사람이—” 그러고서 어밀리아는 말을 멈추었다. 자기가 맞서려고 나선 저 전열함이 만만찮은 싸움 실력을 품고 있다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n“자, 아가씨.” 루펙스 부인이 자리에서 일어서며 말했다. “그래, 숙녀라는 사람이 어쨌다는 거죠?”\n\n이제 우리는 싸움이 시작되었다고, 그리고 두 척의 배가 용기와 해전의 일반 법도에 따라 한쪽이 폭파되거나 침몰까지는 아니더라도 아주 못 쓰게 될 때까지 승부를 이어 가기로 서약했다고 말해도 좋다. 그리고 이 순간에는 지켜보는 사람으로서 두 싸움꾼 가운데 어느 쪽에 끝내 이길 승산이 더 있는지 말하기가 어려웠을 것이다. 루펙스 부인에게는 의심할 나위 없이 더 무르익은 힘과, 무한한 기량을 안겨 준 싸움의 습관과, 싸움의 열기가 이어지는 동안에는 어떤 상처의 아픔에도 무디게 만들어 주는 담력과, 자기가 가라앉든 뜨든 거의 아랑곳하지 않게 만드는 무모함이 있었다. 그러나 어밀리아 쪽은 더 큰 대포를 싣고 있었고, 적이 쓸 수 있는 것보다 더 무거운 쇳덩이를 퍼부을 수 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 제 앞바다에서 헤엄치고 있었다. 만일 정말로 갈고리를 걸고 배에 올라타는 데까지 간다면 어밀리아가 의심할 것 없이 이길 터였다. 그러나 루펙스 부인은 그런 일이 벌어지도록 내버려 두기에는 너무 노회할 것이었다. 어쨌거나 그녀는 이 판에 채비가 되어 있었고, 싸움에 굶주려 있었다.\n\n“그래, 숙녀라는 사람이 어쨌다는 거죠?” 그녀가 그렇게 말했는데, 그 말투는 순순한 대꾸 따위는 용납하지 않는 것이었다.\n\n“숙녀라면, 정말 숙녀라면 말이죠,” 어밀리아가 말했다. “제 몸가짐을 어떻게 해야 하는지 알 거예요.”\n\n“그래서 아가씨가 나를 가르치겠다는 거예요, 로퍼 양? 참으로 고맙기 그지없네요. 아가씨가 좋아하는 건 맨체스터 예절인 모양이죠?”\n\n“저는 정직한 예절이 좋아요, 루펙스 부인. 점잖은 예절이요. 온 집안 사람들을 기겁하게 하지 않는 예절 말이에요. 그게 맨체스터에서 왔든 런던에서 왔든 저는 상관 안 해요.”\n\n“모자 가게 예절이겠죠?”\n\n“모자 가게 예절이든 극장 예절이든 저는 상관 안 해요, 루펙스 부인. 아주 못돼먹은 예절만 아니라면요—부인 것처럼 말이에요, 루펙스 부인. 자, 이제 들으셨죠. 대체 저 젊은 남자하고 이런 식으로 계속 굴어서 뭘 하시려는 거예요? 남편을 술과 질투로 정신 병원에 처넣을 때까지 그러실 참인가요?”\n\n“로퍼 양! 로퍼 양!” 크래들이 말했다. “자, 이건 정말—”\n\n“저런 건 신경 쓰지 마세요, 크래들 씨.” 루펙스 부인이 말했다. “당신이 말을 섞을 만한 값어치도 없는 아이니까요. 그리고 저 딱한 임스 씨 말인데, 그이한테 조금이라도 우정이 있거든 저 아이가 어떤 사람인지 알려 주세요. 아가씨, 샐퍼드 그로그램 상회의 주니퍼 씨는 잘 계신가요? 나는 아가씨에 대해 다 알고 있고, 존 임스도 곧 다 알게 될 거예요—가엾고 딱한 바보 같으니! 나더러 술이니 질투니 하고 늘어놓다니, 참!”\n\n“그래요, 늘어놓았어요! 그리고 이제 부인이 주니퍼 씨 이름을 꺼내셨으니, 임스 씨도 크래들 씨도 그 이야기를 몽땅 다 아셔도 좋아요. 주니퍼 씨 일로 제가 부끄러워할 것은 하나도 없으니까요.”\n\n“아가씨를 무엇에든 부끄럽게 만들기란 어려운 일일 거예요, 내 생각에는.”\n\n“하지만 이 말은 해 두겠어요, 루펙스 부인. 부인이 그런 짓거리로 이 집의 체면을 망쳐 놓게 두지는 않을 거예요.”\n\n“루펙스가 나를 이 집에 데리고 들어온 그날이 나한테는 재수 없는 날이었죠.”\n\n“그럼 셈을 치르고 나가시죠.” 어밀리아가 문 쪽으로 손을 내저으며 말했다. “미리 알릴 것도 없다고 제가 책임지고 말씀드리죠. 어머니한테 진 빚만 갚으시면 지금 당장이라도 나가셔도 좋아요.”\n\n“나는 내가 좋을 때 나갈 거예요. 한 시간도 앞당겨서는 안 나가요. 이 집시 같은 계집애가 뭐기에 나한테 이런 식으로 말을 하는 거죠?”\n\n“그리고 나가는 것으로 말하면, 순경을 불러다 끌어내야 한다 해도 나가시게 만들 거예요.”\n\n싸움이 이쯤 이르렀을 때 두 손을 허리에 짚고 적을 마주 보고 선 어밀리아는 분명 이 전투에서 이기고 있는 것처럼 보였다. 그러나 루펙스 부인의 혀가 지닌 독기는 아직 그 최대의 결과를 내놓지 않은 참이었다. 나로서는 이 싸움이 계속 타오르도록 내버려 두었더라면 그 유부녀 쪽이 처녀 쪽의 입을 다물게 했으리라고 여기는 편이다—물론 갈고리를 걸고 배에 올라타는 데까지는 가지 않는다는 전제에서 말이다. 그러나 바로 그때 로퍼 부인이 아들을 데리고 방으로 들어왔고, 두 싸움꾼은 잠시 물러섰다.\n\n“어밀리아, 이게 다 무슨 일이냐?” 로퍼 부인이 몹시 놀라 괴로워하는 표정을 지어 보이려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n“루펙스 부인한테 물어보세요.” 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“루펙스 부인이 대답하지요.” 그 부인이 말했다. “댁의 따님이 이 방에 들어와서 나한테 대들었어요—그런 말버릇으로—그것도 크래들 씨 앞에서요—”\n\n“저 사람은 왜 진 빚을 갚고 이 집에서 나가지 않는대요?” 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“입 다물어.” 그녀의 오라비가 말했다. “저분이 뭘 빚졌든 네가 상관할 일이 아니야.”\n\n“하지만 내가 저런 것한테 모욕을 당했으니 내가 상관할 일이지.”\n\n“것이라니!” 루펙스 부인이 말했다. “누가 더 그런 것 같은지 나도 참 알고 싶네요! 하지만 내가 말해 주죠, 어밀리아 로퍼—” 그러나 여기서 그녀의 웅변은 멎었으니, 어밀리아가 오라비에게 떠밀려 방 밖으로 사라진 뒤였기 때문이다. 그러자 루펙스 부인은 쓰기에 알맞은 소파를 하나 찾아내어 발작에 몸을 맡겼다. 잠시 우리는 그녀를 그렇게 두고 떠나기로 하되, 가엾은 로퍼 부인이 잠자리에 들지 못한 채 너무 늦게까지 붙들려 있지 않았기를 바랄 뿐이다.\n\n“임스가 저 계집애하고 결혼하면 얼마나 지독한 난장판을 만들어 놓을까!” 자기 방으로 물러가면서 크래들이 한 생각은 그런 것이었다. 그러나 그날 밤의 일에서 자기가 맡은 몫에 대해서는 그는 부끄러워하기는커녕 오히려 자랑스러웠으니, 그 유부녀가 자기를 마음에 두었다는 것이 방금 맹렬히 타오른 저 전투의 원인이었다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 파리스 역시 십 년에 걸친 트로이 공성에서 그와 같이 큰 만족을 얻었던 것이다.\n\n# 제12장. 릴리언 데일이 나비가 되다\n\n이제 우리는 앨링턴으로 돌아가기로 하자. 존 임스에게 지지난 장에 실린 두 통의 편지를 가져다준 바로 그 아침에, 큰 집에도 여러 통의 편지에 섞여 어돌퍼스 크로즈비 앞으로 다음과 같은 서한이 도착했다. 그것은 어느 백작부인에게서 온 것으로, 분홍빛 종이에 씌어 있었는데, 결이 아름답게 잡힌 데다 향이 배어 있었고, 작위 관 문장과 기묘하게 뒤엉킨 머리글자들로 장식되어 있었다. 요컨대 그 편지는 대단히 세련되고 마음을 끄는 것이었으며, 어돌퍼스 크로즈비도 그것을 받고 결코 언짢아하지 않았다.\n\n코시 성, 186--년 9월.\n\n친애하는 크로즈비 씨께,--게이지비 부부에게서 당신 소식을 들었소. 그이들이 우리에게 내려와 있는데, 당신이 어느 매력적인 작은 마을에 묻혀 시골살이를 하고 있다더군요. 그 마을에는 여러 좋은 것들 가운데에서도 숲의 님프와 물의 님프가 있어서, 당신 시간의 태반을 거기에 바치고 있다고요. 그거야말로 당신 취향에 꼭 들어맞는 일이니 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 당신을 방해하고 싶지 않소. 다만 혹시라도 앨링턴의 숲과 샘에서 몸을 떼어낼 수 있게 되거든, 우리는 여기서 기꺼이 당신을 맞이하겠어요. 하기야 요즈음 당신이 지내던 엘리시온에 견주면 우리 쪽은 아주 낭만이 없다고 여겨지겠지만요.\n\n레이디 덤벨로가 우리에게 오기로 되어 있어요. 내가 알기로 당신이 아끼는 분이지요. 아니면 그 반대인가요, 당신이 그분에게 귀염을 받는 쪽인가요? 레이디 하틀탑에게도 청하기는 했으나, 그분은 가엾은 후작 곁을 떠나지 못한다는군요. 아시다시피 그이가 몹시 쇠약하니 말이오. 공작은 지금 개서럼에 있지 않소만, 물론 그것이 사랑스러운 레이디 하틀탑이 오지 못하는 일과 무슨 상관이 있다는 뜻은 아니라오. 집은 손님으로 가득 차게 될 듯하고, 님프도 아쉽지 않을 거예요. 다만 숲이나 물에 사는 종류는 아닐까 걱정이지만요. 마거리타와 알렉산드리나가 특히 당신이 와 주기를 바라요. 한 집 가득한 사람들을 잘 어우러지게 하는 데 당신만큼 능한 이가 없다고들 하니까요. 나라 살림을 챙기러 돌아가기 전에 우리에게 한 주를 내어줄 수 있거든, 부디 그리해 주오.\n\n진심을 담아,\n\n드 코시 백작부인은 크로즈비 씨의 아주 오랜 벗이었다. 물론 그가 살아온 세계에서 오랜 벗이라는 것이 어떤 것인지를 감안하고 하는 말이다. 그는 지난 예닐곱 해 동안 그 부인을 알고 지내며, 런던에서 열리는 부인의 무도회란 무도회에는 죄다 가고 어디서든 그 딸들과 춤을 추어 주는 것을 습관으로 삼아 왔다. 더없이 사람 좋고 싹싹한 태도로였다. 그는 또 집안끼리의 오랜 인연으로 모티머 게이지비 씨와도 친밀하게 지내 왔는데, 게이지비 씨는 좀 더 격이 높은 축에 드는 사무 변호사에 지나지 않으면서도 백작부인의 맏딸과 혼인하였고, 지금은 코시 성이 자리 잡은 인근의 도시 바체스터를 대표하여 의회에 앉아 있었다. 그리고 이참에 솔직하게 다 털어놓자면, 크로즈비 씨는 드 코시 백작부인의 딸들인 레이디 마거리타, 레이디 알렉산드리나와도 대단히 두터운 사이였다. 어쩌면 특히 뒤엣사람과 그러하였을 터인데, 그렇다고 해서 내 독자들이 내 이 말을 두고 두 사람 사이에 우정보다 더 다정한 무엇이 오간 적이 있으리라 짐작하는 일만은 없기를 바란다.\n\n크로즈비는 그날 아침에는 그 편지에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그날 하루가 가는 동안에, 혹은 어쩌면 잠자리에 누워 그 일을 곰곰이 헤아려 보는 동안에, 그는 드 코시 백작부인의 초대를 받아들이기로 마음을 정했다. 게이지비 부부를 만나는 것이 반가워서만도 아니고, 사교계 생활이라는 고등 기예의 저 위대한 대가 레이디 덤벨로와 한 지붕 밑에 머무는 것이 반가워서만도 아니며, 레이디 마거리타, 레이디 알렉산드리나와 우정을 새로이 하는 것이 반가워서만도 아니었다. 릴리와 약혼한 처지의 사정으로 보아 휴가가 끝날 때까지 그 애 곁에 머무는 편이 마땅하다고 느꼈다면, 그는 별다른 갈등 없이 드 코시 집안을 저버릴 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 스스로에게, 이제는 릴리에게서 몸을 떼어 놓는 편이 자기에게 좋겠다고 말했다. 아니, 어쩌면 자기가 떼어져 나가는 편이 릴리에게 좋겠다고 말했는지도 모른다. 약혼이 열매를 맺기까지 흘러가야 할 그 몇 달, 어쩌면 몇 해 동안, 두 사람이 오로지 서로의 눈에서 비쳐 오는 햇빛만으로 살아가는 것이라고 그 애가 여기도록 가르쳐서는 안 되었다. 또 삶의 즐거움과 일거리를 오로지 상대에게서만 구해야 하는 것이라고 그 애가 여기게 두어서도 안 되었다. 이런 식으로 그는 제 마음속에서 그 문제를 아주 사리에 닿게 따져 보았고, 별로 어렵지 않게 결심을 내렸다. 코시 성으로 가서 그곳에 모여들 화려한 사교계의 햇빛을 한 주 동안 쬐리라고. 제 집 난롯가의 조용하고 단조로운 나날은 머지않아 실컷 찾아올 터이니!\n\n“수요일에 떠날까 합니다, 어르신.” 일요일 아침 식탁에서 크로즈비가 지주에게 말했다.\n\n“수요일에 떠난다고!” 지주가 말했다. 그는 혼인을 약속한 두 사람이라면 사정이 허락하는 한 되도록 오래 함께 붙어 있어야 한다는 구식 생각을 지니고 있었다. “무슨 언짢은 일이라도 있는가?”\n\n“아이고, 아닙니다! 다만 모든 일에는 끝이 있는 법이고, 저도 시내로 돌아가기 전에 한두 군데 짧게 들를 데가 있으니 수요일에 나서는 편이 낫겠지요. 사실 미룰 수 있는 데까지 미룬 것입니다.”\n\n“여기서 어디로 가는데?” 버나드가 물었다.\n\n“글쎄, 마침 바로 옆 주(州)까지밖에 안 가. 코시 성으로.” 그러고는 그 아침 식탁에서 그 일에 관해 더는 아무 말도 오가지 않았다.\n\n일요일 아침이면 교회에 가기 전에 작은 집의 잔디밭에서 다 함께 모이는 것이 그들의 버릇이 되어 있었다. 이날도 세 신사가 나란히 걸어 내려가 보니 릴리와 벨이 벌써 나와 기다리고 있었다. 그런 자리에서는 대개 데일 부인이 집을 가로질러 교회로 나서자고 부를 때까지 몇 분쯤 여유가 있었고, 이날도 그러하였다. 지주는 그럴 때면 잔디밭 한복판에 서서 제 땅을 둘러보며 둘레의 관목이며 꽃이며 과실나무를 하나하나 눈으로 헤아리곤 했다. 그는 그것이 전부 제 것임을 한시도 잊은 적이 없었고, 여느 날에는 좀처럼 이곳까지 내려와 보는 일이 없었으므로 이런 기회를 그렇게 썼던 것이다. 데일 부인은 제 방 창가에서 보닛 끈을 매다가 그 모습을 내다볼 적마다, 시숙의 머릿속에 무엇이 오가는지 다 안다고 느꼈고, 형편에 밀려 그런 도움을 그에게 신세 지게 된 것을 서운해하곤 했다. 그러나 실상 부인은 그가 그럴 때 무슨 생각을 다 하는지는 알지 못했다. “이건 내 것이지.” 그는 그 정겨운 땅을 둘러보며 속으로 말하곤 했다. “하지만 저 사람들이 이걸 누리는 편이 내게도 좋은 일이야. 제수는 내 아우의 미망인이니 여기 있어도 좋아. 얼마든지 좋고말고.” 나는 이 두 사람이 서로의 마음과 생각을 지금보다 조금만 더 알았더라면 서로를 더 사랑할 수도 있었으리라 생각한다.\n\n그러고 나서 크로즈비는 릴리에게 제 뜻을 알렸다. “수요일에요!” 그 소식을 듣자 릴리는 감정이 북받쳐 얼굴빛이 거의 창백해지며 말했다. 그는 불쑥 말해 버렸던 것이니, 그런 기별이 그 애를 그토록 세게 흔들어 놓으리라고는 아마 생각하지 못했을 것이다.\n\n“그래요. 드 코시 백작부인께 편지를 써서 수요일이라고 했어요. 아무나 다 뿌리치고 지내는 건 나로서는 도리가 아니고, 또 어쩌면—”\n\n“아, 아니에요! 그리고 어돌퍼스, 제가 당신이 가시는 걸 아까워한다고는 생각지 마세요. 다만 너무 갑작스러워서요. 그렇지 않아요?”\n\n“보다시피 여기 여섯 주가 넘게 있었잖아요.”\n\n“네, 정말 잘해 주셨어요. 생각해 보면 그 여섯 주가 어떤 여섯 주였는지! 그 차이가 당신께도 제게만큼이나 크게 느껴지는지 궁금해요. 저는 애벌레 노릇을 그만두고 나비가 되기 시작했거든요.”\n\n“하지만 시집을 가면 나비 노릇은 하면 안 돼요, 릴리.”\n\n“네, 그런 뜻으로 한 말은 아니에요. 제가 말하려던 건, 이 세상에서 제가 놓일 참된 자리가—저는 그러라고 지어진 것이기를 바라는 그 자리가—당신을 알고 당신이 저를 사랑하신다는 걸 알고 나서야 비로소 열렸다는 거예요. 그런데 엄마가 부르시네요, 이제 집을 가로질러 교회로 가야 해요. 수요일에 가신다니! 그럼 사흘밖에 안 남았잖아요!”\n\n“그래요, 꼭 사흘이지요.” 그는 이렇게 말하며 릴리를 팔에 끼고 집을 가로질러 길로 나섰다.\n\n“그럼 언제 다시 뵐 수 있어요?” 교회 묘지에 이르렀을 때 릴리가 물었다.\n\n“아, 그거야 아직 누가 알겠어요? 언제 또 놓아 주실지는 위원회 의장께 여쭈어 봐야지요.” 그러고는 더 오간 말이 없었고, 모두들 지주를 따라 자그마한 현관을 지나 다들 함께 앉는 큼직한 가족석으로 올라갔다. 여기서 지주는 아버지가 세상을 뜬 뒤로 줄곧 차지해 온 특별한 한쪽 구석에 자리를 잡고서 응답송을 크고 또렷하게 읽었다. 어찌나 크고 또렷한지 교구 서기가 도무지 그를 당해 내지 못했는데, 그래도 서기는 지지 않으려는 목청으로 여전히 애를 썼다. “지주 어른께서는 지주도 되고 목사도 되고 서기도 되고 죄다 되고 싶으신 게죠. 암, 그러시고말고요.” 딱한 서기는 제가 겪는 그 몹쓸 꼴을 하소연할 적마다 이렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n릴리의 기도가 새로 생긴 시름 탓에 자꾸 끊겼다 하더라도, 나는 그 점에서 그 애의 허물은 용서받으리라고 생각한다. 크로즈비가 앨링턴에 그리 오래 더 머물지 않으리라는 것이야 물론 릴리도 알고 있었다. 그의 휴가가 끝나는 날짜도, 그가 일반위원회 사무국의 제 방으로 걸어 들어가야 하는 시각도, 릴리는 그 자신만큼이나 정확히 알고 있었다. 다만 그가 휴가가 다 끝날 때까지 자기들 곁에 머물러 있으리라고 제 마음에 새겨 두었던 터라, 지금 릴리는 방학이 끝나기 하루 이틀 전에 갑자기 마지막 한 주를 빼앗기게 되었다는 말을 들은 학생아이와 같은 심정이었다. 처음부터 알고 있었더라면 그쯤이야 대수롭지 않은 서운함이었으련만, 남은 재산의 삼분의 이를 잃는 셈인데 어느 학생아이가 그런 충격을 견뎌 낼 수 있겠는가? 릴리는 제 연인을 탓하지 않았다. 그가 남아 있어야 한다는 생각조차 하지 않았다. 그가 무엇이든 야박한 일을 하려 든다고는 스스로에게 도무지 여기게 하지 않았다. 그러나 잃은 것은 잃은 것이어서, 무릎을 꿇고 기도하는 동안에도 릴리는 한두 번 남몰래 눈물을 훔쳤다.\n\n크로즈비 또한 보이스 씨의 설교가 이어지는 동안 마땅한 정도를 넘어 제가 떠날 일만 생각하고 있었다. “그이 말은 듣기가 참 편하지요.” 헌 부인은 제 남편의 후임을 두고 늘 이렇게 말하곤 했다. “그이 논지를 좇아가느라 애쓸 일이 별로 없으니까요.” 크로즈비 씨는 어쩌면 헌 부인보다 그 어려움을 더 크게 느꼈을지도 모르고, 논지가 좀 더 깊었더라면 오히려 그 말씀에 마음을 온전히 바쳤을지도 모른다. 아무것도 말하지 않는 사람의 말을 듣고 앉아 있어야 한다는 것은 참으로 고된 노릇이다. 이날 크로즈비는 그 고역을 아예 모른 체하고, 떠나기 전에 릴리에게 무슨 말을 해 두는 것이 좋을지를 궁리하는 데에 마음을 다 내주었다. 그는 사랑에 처음 불이 붙었을 적에 혼인 날짜를 일찍 잡자고 조르며 했던 그 몇 마디를 또렷이 기억하고 있었다. 그리고 릴리가 얼마나 어여쁘게 제 뜻을 받아 주었는지도 기억하고 있었다. “다만 너무 서두르지는 마세요.” 릴리는 그렇게 말했더랬다. 이제 그는 그때 한 말을 도로 물러야 했다. 제가 편 주장을 제 손으로 꺾어야 했고, 혼인을 앞당기기보다 미루고 싶다는 뜻을 그 애에게 알려야 했다. 짐작건대 혼인을 약속한 남자에게 이보다 더 언짢은 일거리는 없을 것이다. “이왕이면 지금 당장 해치우는 게 낫겠어.” 보이스 씨의 설교가 끝난 데 대한 감사의 표시로 두 손에 이마를 파묻으며 그는 속으로 그렇게 말했다.\n\n남은 날이 사흘뿐이었으니 곧바로 해치우는 편이 나은 것은 분명했다. 릴리에게 재산이 없는 터에 약혼이 길어진다 한들 그 애가 억울해할 까닭은 없다. 그것이 그가 제 마음속에서 내세운 논리였다. 그러나 그 못지않게 자주 그는 스스로에게, 이 일을 하루라도 공연히 어정쩡하게 놓아둔다면 그 애가 서운해할 만한 큰 이유가 되리라고 말했다. 사랑에 겨워 어쩌자고 그 성급한 말을 함부로 내뱉어, 학생아이나 조니 임스 같은 자가 저지를 법한 온갖 낭패 속으로 스스로를 밀어 넣었단 말인가? 정신을 차리고 마음을 가다듬지 못했다니, 어돌퍼스 크로즈비라는 사람에게 마땅히 돌아가야 할 것이 무엇인지를 그 자리에서 헤아리지 못했다니, 이 얼마나 얼간이 같은 짓인가! 그러다 문득 이 일에서 저 자신을 통째로 얼간이로 만들어 버린 것이 아닌가 하는 생각이 그를 덮쳤다. 그리고 교회 문을 나서며 릴리에게 팔을 내어 줄 때, 그는 그런 생각을 하면서 어깨를 으쓱했다. “이제 와서는 늦었지.” 그는 속으로 그렇게 말하고는, 돌아서서 그 애에게 다정하고 어여쁜 말을 한마디 건넸다. 어돌퍼스 크로즈비는 영리한 사람이었다. 그리고 그는 진실한 사람이 되고자 하는 뜻도 있었다. 다만 거짓으로 이끄는 유혹이 그가 감당하지 못할 만큼 크지만 않다면 말이다.\n\n“릴리, 점심 뒤에 들판으로 나가 걸을래요?” 그가 릴리에게 말했다.\n\n그와 함께 들판을 걷다니! 물론 걸을 터였다. 남은 날이 사흘뿐인데, 그가 받아만 준다면 제 시간의 한 순간인들 그에게 내어 주지 않을 리가 있겠는가? 그러고 나서 그들은 작은 집에서 점심을 먹었다. 데일 부인은 지주의 식탁에서 열리는 만찬에는 함께하겠노라 약속해 둔 터였다. 지주는 점심에 아무것도 손을 대지 않았는데, 점심이라는 것 자체가 나쁜 것이라는 구실을 댔다. “점심이야 제 집에서 잡수시겠지.” 나중에 데일 부인이 벨에게 말했다. “게다가 셰리주를 한 잔 드시는 것도 여러 번 보았단다.” 이런 생각을 하면서 데일 부인은 제 몫의 저녁을 먹었다. 시숙이 제 상에서 먹지 않겠다면 저 역시 그의 상에서 먹지 않을 참이었다.\n\n그러고 몇 분 만에 릴리는 크로즈비가 연인의 특권으로 늘 흉을 보곤 하던 그 얌전한 교회용 보닛 대신 모자를 쓰고 나섰다. 그가 모자를 칭찬해 주기만 한다면 보닛이야 무슨 소리를 하든 좋다고 릴리는 굳게 여기고 있었다. “사흘뿐이에요.” 릴리는 그와 함께 잔디밭을 가로질러 빠른 걸음으로 내려가며 말했다. 그러나 그 목소리에는 원망이 담겨 있지 않았다. 그저 이렇게 말하는 듯할 뿐이었다—좋은 시간이 그토록 짧을 바에는 그 시간을 한껏 누려야 한다고. 그러니 한 남자에게 이보다 더한 찬사가 어디 있겠는가? 이보다 더 흐뭇한 아첨이 어디 있겠는가? 이 땅에서 제가 누릴 하늘은 온통 당신과 함께 있는데, 이제 앞으로 몇 달쯤 이어질 이 나날의 기쁨으로는 그 하늘이 사흘밖에 남지 않았다니! 자, 그렇다면 나는 내게 주어진 행복을 한껏 누리겠다. 크로즈비도 릴리가 느끼는 그대로를 느꼈고, 제가 그 애에게 진 빚이 얼마나 큰지도 알아차렸다. “크리스마스에는 하루라도 좋으니 저 사람들한테 내려와야겠어. 단 하루뿐일지라도.” 그는 속으로 그렇게 말했다. 그러고는 이왕 그럴 작정이라면 지금 나눌 이야기를 그 약속으로 여는 편이 좋겠다고 생각했다.\n\n“그래요, 릴리, 이제 사흘밖에 안 남았지요. 그런데 말이오—크리스마스에는 다들 집에 있겠지요?”\n\n“크리스마스에 집에 있느냐고요? 물론 집에 있지요. 설마 우리한테 오시겠다는 말씀은 아니겠죠!”\n\n“글쎄, 받아 주기만 한다면 그럴까 하는데요.”\n\n“어머! 그러면 얼마나 달라지는데요. 어디 보자. 그럼 석 달밖에 안 되네요. 게다가 크리스마스날에 당신이 여기 계시다니! 저는 한 해의 어느 날보다도 그날 당신이 계셨으면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“하루뿐일 거예요, 릴리. 크리스마스이브 저녁에 맞춰 와서 그 이튿날에는 떠나야 해요.”\n\n“그래도 곧장 우리 집으로 오시는 거죠!”\n\n“나한테 방을 하나 내어 줄 수 있다면요.”\n\n“당연히 내어 드리죠. 지금도 내어 드릴 수 있는걸요. 다만 처음 오셨을 때는, 아시다시피—” 그러고서 릴리는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 웃었다.\n\n“내가 왔을 때 나는 그대의 벗이라기보다 지주 어른과 그대 사촌의 벗이었지요. 하지만 이제는 다 달라졌어요.”\n\n“맞아요, 이제 당신은 제 벗이에요. 그것도 저만의 벗이요. 저는 지금도 앞으로도 늘 당신만의 각별하고 더없이 소중한 벗이 되는 거예요. 그렇죠, 어돌퍼스?” 그러고서 릴리는 그가 그토록 자주 해 주었던 그 약속을 다시 한번 그의 입으로 말하게 했다.\n\n이때쯤 두 사람은 큰 집의 뜰을 지나 들판에 나와 있었다. “릴리.” 그는 다소 불쑥 말을 꺼냈고, 그 말투로 무언가 중요한 이야기가 나오리라는 것을 릴리도 느꼈다. “그대에게 몇 마디 해 두고 싶은 게 있는데, 저—실무에 관한 이야기요.” 그러면서 그는 마지막 낱말을 뱉으며 짧게 웃었으니, 제가 편치 않은 처지라는 것을 릴리도 훤히 알아차릴 만했다.\n\n“그럼요, 듣고말고요. 그리고 어돌퍼스, 제 걱정은 부디 마세요. 제 말은, 제가 근심이나 고생을 못 견딘다고 여기지 마시라는 거예요. 당신이 저를 사랑해 주시는 한 저는 무엇이든 견딜 수 있어요. 이런 말을 하는 건, 아까 당신이 떠나신다고 할 때 제가 원망하는 것처럼 보였을까 봐 걱정돼서예요. 그럴 뜻은 아니었어요.”\n\n“그대가 원망한다고는 한 번도 생각하지 않았어요, 내 사랑. 언제 어느 모로 보나 그대보다 나은 사람은 있을 수 없지요. 그대가 마음에 들지 않는 남자라면 여간 까다로운 사람이 아닐 겁니다.”\n\n“제가 당신 마음에만 들 수 있다면—”\n\n“그대는 무엇 하나 빠짐없이 내 마음에 들어요. 사랑스러운 릴리, 나는 그대를 찾았을 때 천사를 하나 찾은 것이라고 생각해요. 그건 그렇고 이제 그 실무 이야기인데. 아무래도 전부 다 이야기하는 편이 낫겠소.”\n\n“네, 그럼요, 전부 다 이야기해 주세요.”\n\n“하지만 그러자면 그대가 나를 오해해서는 안 돼요. 그리고 내가 돈 이야기를 하더라도, 그것이 그대를 향한 내 사랑과 무슨 상관이 있는 것이라고 여겨서는 안 돼요.”\n\n“당신을 봐서라도 제가 이런 쬐그만 빈털터리가 아니었으면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“내가 하려는 말은 이거예요. 내가 돈 문제로 애를 태우는 것처럼 보이더라도, 그 걱정이 그대를 향한 내 애정과 조금이라도 관계가 있다고는 여기지 말라는 거지요. 그대가 부자든 가난뱅이든 나는 똑같이 그대를 사랑했을 것이고, 그대와 혼인해서 누릴 행복도 똑같이 손꼽아 기다렸을 거예요. 알아듣겠어요?”\n\n릴리는 그의 말을 온전히 알아듣지는 못했다. 그저 그가 말을 이어 가도록 북돋우려고 그의 팔을 꼭 눌렀을 뿐이다. 릴리는 그가 앞으로 두 사람이 어떻게 살아갈지에 관해 무언가를 말하려는 것이라고, 그것도 제가 듣기에 달갑지 않으리라고 그가 여기는 무언가를 말하려는 것이라고 짐작했고, 자기는 그것을 기꺼이 받아들이겠다는 것을 그에게 보여 주리라 마음먹었다.\n\n“그대도 알다시피.” 그가 말했다. “나는 우리 혼인이 미뤄지지 않기를 얼마나 애타게 바랐던가요. 물론 나로서는 하루라도 빨리 그대를 내 사람이라 부르는 것이 이제 전부일 수밖에 없지요.” 이 자그마한 사랑의 고백에 대해 릴리는 그저 다시 한번 그의 팔을 꼭 눌렀을 뿐이니, 그 대목은 저로서는 딱히 할 말이 많지 않은 이야기였다.\n\n“당연히 나는 몹시 애가 타지요. 그런데 생각만큼 쉽지가 않다는 걸 알게 되었어요.”\n\n“제가 뭐라고 했는지 아시잖아요, 어돌퍼스. 저는 좀 기다리는 편이 낫겠다고 했어요. 엄마도 분명 그렇게 생각하실 거예요. 그리고 이따금씩만이라도 당신을 뵐 수 있다면—”\n\n“그야 당연한 일이지요. 그런데, 아까 하던 말인데— 어디 보자. 그렇지, 그렇게 기다리는 일은 내게 견딜 수 없는 노릇일 거예요. 혼인 같은 문제에 마음을 굳혀 놓고서 당장 그 살림으로 바꾸지 못한다는 건 남자에게 여간 답답한 일이 아니지요. 더구나 그대 같은 작은 천사를 제 사람으로 맞아들이려는 참이라면 말이오.” 이 다정한 말을 하면서 그의 팔은 다시 릴리의 허리를 감았다. “하지만—” 그러고서 그는 말을 멈추었다. 그는 제 쪽에서 이렇게 뜻을 바꾸게 된 것이 릴리의 삼촌이 뜻밖에 처신을 잘못한 탓임을 그 애가 알아주기를 바랐다. 그는 일이 정확히 어떻게 되어 있는지를 그 애가 알아 주기를 바랐다. 자기는 삼촌이 얼마간의 재산을 그 애에게 떼어 주리라 여기게끔 되어 있었고, 그것이 어그러졌으니 그 낭패를 뼈저리게 느낄 만한 까닭이 있으며, 기대가 이렇게 꺾인 이상 혼인을 미룰 수밖에 없다는 것을 말이다. 그러나 그와 동시에 그는 이 일이 그 애를 향한 제 사랑을 조금도 상하게 하지 않는다는 것, 삼촌의 잘못을 두고 그 애까지 싸잡아 나무라는 것은 결코 아니라는 것도 알아 주기를 바랐다. 이 모든 것을 그는 그 애에게 전하고 싶었으나, 그 애 자신에게 무람없이 들리지도 않고 제가 돈에 눈이 어두운 것처럼 비치지도 않게 말할 방도를 도무지 찾지 못했다. 그는 전부 다 이야기하겠노라고 말문을 열었더랬다. 그러나 어떤 사람에게 전부 다 이야기한다는 그 일은 때로 그리 쉽지가 않다. 세상에는 제 입으로 말해지기를 한사코 마다하는 것들이 있는 법이다.\n\n“그러니까 당신 말씀은,” 릴리가 말했다. “지금 당장은 혼인할 형편이 못 된다는 거군요.”\n\n“그래요, 바로 그거예요. 될 줄로 알았는데, 그만—”\n\n사랑에 빠진 남자가 제가 사랑하는 여인에게, 그대에게 돈이 없다는 것을 알고 몹시 낙심했노라고 털어놓을 수 있었던 적이 이제껏 한 번이라도 있었던가? 만일 있었다면 그 사람은 사랑보다 용기가 더 컸던 것이라고 나는 말하겠다. 크로즈비는 그리할 수 없음을 깨달았고, 그 어려움 때문에 제가 모진 꼴을 당하고 있다고 여겼다. 그가 릴리에게 지우려 하는 그 기다림은 그가 느끼기에 저 자신이 아니라 지주가 만들어 낸 것이었다. 지주가 마땅히 제 몫으로 해야 할 일을 해 줄 마음만 있었다면 그도 제 몫을 할 준비가 되어 있었다. 그런데 지주가 하지 않으니 그도 할 수 없다는 것이다—아직은. 이 모든 것이 제대로 알려지는 것이 도리에 맞았다. 그러나 막상 말을 꺼내려 하니 그 이야기가 도무지 반듯하게 모양을 갖추어 주지 않았다. 그는 이 일을 그대로 흘려보내고 그 억울함을 감내하는 수밖에 없었고, 적어도 이 일에서 저만은 훌륭히 처신하고 있다는 생각으로 그럭저럭 스스로를 달랬다.\n\n“그렇다고 제가 불행해지지는 않아요, 어돌퍼스.”\n\n“그렇지 않아요?” 그가 말했다. “나로 말하자면, 그 기다림을 그렇게 아무렇지 않게 견딜 수는 없다는 걸 고백하겠어요.”\n\n“아니에요, 여보. 하지만 저를 오해하시면 안 돼요.” 릴리는 걸음을 멈추고 걷던 길에서 그를 마주 보고 서서 말했다. “저도 세상 흔한 법도대로라면 차라리 기다리는 편이 좋다고 우기는 시늉을 해야 마땅하겠지요. 젊은 아가씨란 으레 그렇게 말하는 법이니까요. 만일 당신이 당장 혼인하자고 저를 조르신다면 저도 틀림없이 그렇게 말할 거예요. 하지만 지금 형편이 이러하니 저는 좀 더 솔직해지겠어요. 저는 이 세상에 바라는 것이 딱 하나뿐이고, 그건 당신의 아내가 되는 것, 당신과 무엇이든 함께 나눌 수 있게 되는 거예요. 우리가 빨리 함께할수록 그만큼 좋아요. 적어도 제게는요. 자, 이만하면 마음이 놓이세요?”\n\n“내 것, 오직 내 것인 릴리!”\n\n“네, 당신의 릴리예요. 저를 의심하실 일은 없을 거예요, 여보. 하지만 저는 무엇이든 제가 바라는 그대로 되기를 기대하지는 않아요. 다시 말씀드리지만 저는 기다리는 일로 불행해지지 않아요. 당신의 사랑을 이렇게 확신하는데 제가 어떻게 불행해지겠어요? 아까 당신이 이렇게 빨리 떠나신다고 하셨을 때는 서운했고, 아마 그게 겉으로 드러났을 거예요. 하지만 그런 작은 일들이 큰일보다 오히려 더 견디기 어려운 법이니까요.”\n\n“그래요, 그건 정말 맞는 말이오.”\n\n“그래도 사흘이 더 남았고, 저는 그 사흘을 실컷 누릴 작정이에요! 그러고 나면 편지를 주실 거고, 크리스마스에 오실 거고요. 그리고 내년에 휴가가 나면 또 우리에게 내려오실 거죠. 그러실 거죠?”\n\n“그건 아주 마음 놓아도 좋아요.”\n\n“그렇게 시간이 흘러가다 보면 당신이 저를 데려가시기 좋은 때가 오겠지요. 저는 불행해지지 않아요.”\n\n“나는, 어쨌든, 애가 탈 거예요.”\n\n“아, 남자들은 늘 애가 타지요. 그것도 남자들 특권 가운데 하나인가 봐요. 그리고 저는 남자가 사랑받고 있다는 걸 아는 데에서 여자아이가 느끼는 만큼 또렷하고 온전한 만족을 느끼는 법은 없다고 생각해요. 당신은 제가 제 총으로 쏘아 맞힌 새예요. 그리고 저는 제가 맞혔다는 그 확신만으로도 충분히 행복하답니다.”\n\n“그대는 나를 굴려 넘어뜨려 놓고, 내가 다시 일어나지 못한다는 걸 아는 거로군요.”\n\n“못 한다는 건 잘 모르겠어요. 당신이 원하신다면 얼마든지 곧바로 일어나게 해 드릴 텐데요.”\n\n그가 자기는 그것을 원하지 않으며 앞으로도 결코 원하지 않을 것이고 원할 수도 없다는 다짐을 어떤 사랑스러운 말로 늘어놓았는지는 독자들이 어렵지 않게 짐작할 것이다. 그러고 나서 그는 저 돈 문제일랑 지금 있는 그대로 놓아두는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다. 그의 참뜻은 두 사람의 형편으로는 당장 혼인할 수 없다는 것을 그 애에게 납득시키는 데 있었고, 그 점이라면 그 애가 그를 온전히 알아들었다. 어쩌면 남은 사흘 사이에 데일 부인에게 이 일을 낱낱이 설명할 기회가 생길지도 모른다. 어쨌든 그는 제 뜻을 정직하게 밝혔으니 아무도 그를 탓할 수 없을 터였다.\n\n이튿날 그들은 다 함께 말을 타고 게스트윅으로 건너갔다. 다 함께라 함은 두 아가씨에 버나드와 크로즈비를 더한 것이다. 그들의 볼일은 두 군데를 찾아보는 것이었다. 하나는 지체 높고 더없이 고귀한 인척인 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트를 찾는 일이었고, 다른 하나는 훨씬 수수하고 훨씬 허물없는 벗인 임스 부인을 찾는 일이었다. 게스트윅 저택이 읍내로 들어가는 길목에 있었으므로 그들은 더 거창한 쪽의 예식부터 치렀다. 데일 소령과 달아나 버린 저 레이디 패니의 오라비인 현 드 게스트 백작은 혼인하지 않은 귀족으로, 주로 소 사육에 몰두하며 지내는 사람이었다. 그리고 그가 아주 좋은 소를 길러 내는 데다 그 일에 무한한 보람을 느끼며 온 힘을 거기에 쏟았고, 남 보기에 흉이 될 만한 못된 짓과는 담을 쌓고 살았으니, 그가 사회의 못난 구성원이 아니었다는 것은 인정해 주어야 마땅하다. 그는 철저한 구식 토리당원이어서 그의 위임장은 늘 제 당 지도자의 손에 들려 있었고, 가축 품평회 같은 일로 불려 가는 일이 아니고서는 좀처럼 몸소 서울로 올라가지 않았다. 그는 키가 작고 몸이 뭉툭하며 뺨이 붉고 얼굴이 둥근 사람으로, 저녁 식사 시간 전까지는 으레 아주 낡은 사냥 상의에 반바지와 각반과 몹시 두꺼운 구두 차림으로 눈에 띄었다. 그는 거의 바깥에서 살다시피 했고, 사냥감을 기르고 지키는 데에서도 황소를 길러 내는 데 못지않게 뛰어났다. 그는 제 영지의 땅 한 뙈기와 그 위에 선 나무 한 그루까지, 마치 부인네가 제 응접실의 장식품을 훤히 알듯 속속들이 알고 있었다. 울타리에 벌어진 틈이라면 그 자리를 정확히 기억하지 못하는 것이 없었고, 이리저리 난 오솔길이라면 그것이 어째서 거기 났고 무엇에 쓰이는지 대지 못하는 것이 없었다. 그는 젊은 시절에는 수입으로 보아 가난한 사람이었다. 백작이라는 것을 생각하면 몹시 가난했다. 그러나 아버지와 할아버지가 겪은 곤경에서 교훈을 얻어 제 분수 안에서 사는 법을 익힌 덕에 지금은 결코 궁색한 사람이 아니었다. 이제 나이의 내리막길을 걷는 그를 두고 사람들은 그가 부자가 되어 가고 있다고, 쓸 수 있는 현금을 쥐고 있다고 말했다. 지난 여러 대에 걸쳐 어떤 드 게스트 경도 누려 보지 못한 처지였다. 그의 아버지와 할아버지는 낭비꾼으로 이름났었다. 그런데 이제 사람들은 이 백작을 두고 구두쇠라고 말했다.\n\n그의 겉모습에는 귀족다운 구석이 별로 없었다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 제 지위에 대한 자부심이 그의 마음에 소중하지 않으리라 여긴다면 드 게스트 경을 크게 잘못 본 것이다. 그의 작위는 존 왕 시절까지 거슬러 올라갔고, 잉글랜드를 통틀어 그보다 먼저 특허장을 받은 귀족은 셋뿐이었다. 그는 제 핏줄에 힘입어 제게 돌아오는 특권이 무엇인지를 알고 있었고, 그 가운데 한 오라기도 양보할 마음이 없었다. 그는 그것을 요란하게 내세우지는 않았다. 세상을 살아가면서 그는 드 게스트 백작께서 납신다고 좌우에 알리는 나팔수를 앞세우는 일이 없었다. 벗들을 위해 상을 차리는 일은 아주 드물었고, 차릴 때에도 그저 온화하고 지루하며 구식인 예의로 소박하게 대접했다. 제대로만 대접받는다면 이 백작이 누구를 짓밟고 지나가는 일은 없었다고 말해도 좋을 것이다. 그러나 함부로 대접받으면 그는 당당하게 노여워할 줄 알았고, 공격을 받으면 온 세상을 상대로도 제 자리를 지켜 낼 수 있었다. 진창 묻은 각반에 붉은 뺨을 하고서 제 황소들 뒤꽁무니를 따라다닐 때에도, 그는 웨스트민스터에서 동료 귀족들 사이에 끼여 훈장을 번쩍이며 궁정의 왕실 의식에 참석할 때와 똑같이 제가 뼛속까지 백작임을 알고 있었다. 아니, 제 작위를 볼거리 삼아 쓰는 저들 누구보다도 더 백작이었다. 그 낡은 상의를 시골뜨기로 떨어진 표지로 잘못 아는 자에게 화 있을진저! 이따금 운 없는 작자가 그런 잘못을 저질렀고, 그때마다 아주 거북하게 그 값을 치러야 했다.\n\n백작과 함께 미혼의 누이 레이디 줄리아가 살고 있었다. 버나드 데일의 아버지는 젊은 시절 그 자매 가운데 하나와 달아났으나, 레이디 줄리아를 저와 함께 달아나게 할 만큼 운 좋은 구혼자는 없었다. 그리하여 그 여자는 여태 처녀의 복된 몸으로 게스트윅 저택의 안주인 노릇을 하며 살고 있었고, 그런 사람으로서 운명이 저를 불러 앉힌 그 높은 자리에 대해 결코 가볍지 않은 자부심을 품고 있었다. 그 여자는 지루하고 따분하며 흠잡을 데 없이 반듯한 노부인으로, 평생을 제가 나고 자란 집에서 지냈다는 것을 두고 스스로에게 한없는 공을 돌렸는데, 나이가 이만큼 든 지금에 와서는 아마도 그 집을 떠나고 싶게 만들 유혹이 저에게는 그다지 강하지도 잦지도 않았다는 사실을 잊고 있는 듯했다. 그 여자는 제 동생 패니 이야기를 할 때면 대체로 얼마간 업신여기는 투였으니, 마치 그 딱한 여동생이 아우뻘 되는 사내와 혼인하여 스스로 격을 떨어뜨렸다는 듯한 말투였다. 그 여자는 오라비인 백작에 못지않게 제 지위를 자랑스러워했으나, 그 자부심을 지탱하는 데에 겉치레는 더 많았고 속의 기품은 더 적었다. 제가 드 게스트 사람이라는 것을 세상이 알아주는 것만으로는 아무래도 모자랐던 모양이어서, 그 여자는 자잘하게 거드름 피우는 버릇과 짐짓 내려다보는 티를 몸에 붙였는데, 그것이 이웃들 사이에서 그 여자를 그리 달갑지 않은 사람으로 만들었다.\n\n게스트윅 저택과 앨링턴 사이의 왕래는 그리 잦지도 않았고 그리 살가운 것도 아니었다. 레이디 패니가 달아난 지 얼마 되지 않아 두 집안은 서로 인척임을 인정하고, 사이좋게 지낸다는 것을 세상이 알도록 하자고 합의를 보았다. 두 집안에게는 그 길이 아니면 서로 원수라는 것을 세상에 알리는 다른 길밖에 없었다. 벗이 되는 편이 성가심이 덜했고, 그리하여 두 집안은 이따금 서로를 찾아보고 한 해에 한 번쯤 서로에게 만찬을 베풀었다. 백작은 지주를 두고 제 정치적 신념을 저버렸으며 그로써 세습 대지주로서 마땅히 받아야 할 존경을 스스로 내던진 사람이라 여겼다. 그리고 지주는 백작을 두고 바깥세상이라고는 아무것도 모르는 사람이라며 깎아내리기 일쑤였다. 게스트윅 저택에서 버나드는 어느 정도 사랑을 받았다. 그는 실제로 혈족이어서 그 핏줄에 드 게스트의 피가 흐르고 있었고, 앨링턴의 상속인이라는 점, 그리고 데일 가의 피가 그와 이어진 저 귀족 가문의 피보다도 더 오래되었다는 점 때문에도 사랑을 덜 받을 까닭이 없었다. 버나드가 지주가 될 즈음이면 게스트윅 저택과 앨링턴 사이에 참으로 화목한 사이가 열릴지도 모를 일이었다. 다만 백작의 상속인과 지주의 상속인 사이에 또 새로운 앙금이 생기지만 않는다면 말이다.\n\n그들이 가 보니 레이디 줄리아는 응접실에 홀로 앉아 있었고, 그들은 격식을 갖추어 크로즈비 씨를 그 여자에게 소개했다. 릴리가 약혼했다는 소식은 물론 저택에도 알려져 있었으므로, 그 신랑감이 이제 사람들 눈에 뵈어 인정을 받으려고 데려와진 것이라는 사정도 훤히 이해되고 있었다. 레이디 줄리아는 몹시 공들인 무릎절을 하고서, 어린 벗이 하느님께서 부르시어 두신 그 삶의 자리에서 행복해지기를 바란다고 말했다.\n\n“그러기를 저도 바라요, 레이디 줄리아.” 릴리가 가볍게 웃으며 말했다. “아무튼 애는 써 볼 참이에요.”\n\n“우리 모두가 애는 쓴답니다, 아가씨. 다만 우리 가운데 많은 이가 그 뜻에 넉넉한 힘을 싣지 못하고 만답니다. 홀몸으로 살든 혼인해서 살든, 우리가 행복을 바랄 수 있는 길은 오직 제 본분을 다하는 것뿐이지요.”\n\n“데일 양은 제 본분을 다하는 데에서 완벽의 용이 될 작정이랍니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“용이라니요!” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다. “아니지요. 나는 릴리 데일 양이 결코 용이 되지 않기를 바라요.” 그러고서 그 여자는 조카에게로 몸을 돌렸다. 여기서 미리 말해 두는 편이 좋겠다. 그 여자는 크로즈비 씨가 함부로 놀린 그 말버릇을 끝내 용서하지 않았다. 그는 게스트윅 저택의 응접실에 들어온 지 겨우 이 분밖에 되지 않았으니, 용이 어떻고 하는 소리를 늘어놓는 것은 그의 분수에 맞지 않았다. “버나드.” 그 여자가 말했다. “어제 자네 어머니한테서 소식을 받았네. 아무래도 그리 튼튼해 보이지는 않으시더군.” 그러고서 이모와 조카 사이에 레이디 패니의 전반적인 건강에 관한 자잘한 이야기가, 그 내용이 그리 흥미롭지는 않은 이야기가 오갔다.\n\n“큰어머니가 그렇게 편찮으신 줄은 몰랐네요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“앓으시는 건 아니야.” 버나드가 말했다. “한 번도 앓으신 적은 없지. 그렇다고 성하신 적도 없지만.”\n\n“큰어머니라.” 레이디 줄리아가 그 낱말을 되뇌며 목소리에 얼마간 빈정거리는 기색을 담는 듯이 말했다. “—그 큰어머니는 이 집을 떠난 뒤로 한 번도 건강해 본 적이 없답니다. 하기야 그것도 아주 오래전 일이지요.”\n\n“아주 오래전이지요.” 의자에 앉아 잠자코 있는 데 익숙하지 않은 크로즈비가 말했다. “적어도 자네는, 데일, 그때 일을 기억할 수 없을 테고.”\n\n“하지만 나는 기억할 수 있어요.” 레이디 줄리아가 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. “내 동생 패니가 이 고장에서 제일가는 미인으로 꼽히던 때를 나는 기억한답니다. 아름다움이란 위험한 재주지요.”\n\n“아주 위험하지요.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그러자 릴리가 또 웃었고, 레이디 줄리아는 그 어느 때보다 노여워졌다. 제 이웃들이 제 품속에까지 들이려 하는 이 사내는 대체 얼마나 밉살스러운 자란 말인가! 그러나 그 여자는 전에도 크로즈비 씨에 관한 이야기를 들은 적이 있었고, 크로즈비 씨 또한 그 여자에 관한 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다.\n\n“그런데 말입니다, 레이디 줄리아.” 그가 말했다. “제가 마님의 아주 소중한 벗들을 몇 분 아는 듯합니다.”\n\n“아주 소중한 벗이라니, 그건 아주 센 말이로군요. 내게는 아주 소중한 벗이 그리 많지 않답니다.”\n\n“게이지비 부부 말입니다. 모티머 게이지비와 레이디 어밀리아가 마님 이야기를 하는 걸 들은 적이 있지요.”\n\n그 말에 레이디 줄리아는 게이지비 부부를 아는 것이 사실이라고 인정했다. 게이지비 씨는, 그 여자의 말로는, 젊은 시절에 여러 가지로 갖추지 못한 것이 많았던 사람이지만 그래도 아주 훌륭한 인물이라는 것이었다. 그는 지금 의회에 나가 있고, 듣자 하니 쓸모 있는 사람 노릇을 하고 있다고 했다. 그 여자는 당시 레이디 어밀리아의 혼인을 썩 마땅히 여기지는 않았고, 그래서 아주 오랜 벗인 드 코시 백작부인에게도 그렇게 말했더랬다는 것이다. 다만— 그러고서 레이디 줄리아는 게이지비 씨를 칭찬하는 말을 많이 늘어놓았는데, 그 말들을 그러모으면 결국 이런 뜻인 듯싶었다. 즉 그가 아주 훌륭한 축의 사람이어서, 백작의 딸이 저와 혼인해 준 것이 제게는 지나치게 큰 영광임을 온전히 확신하고 있으며, 그런 혼인을 하고서도 제가 아내의 친척들과, 아니 제 아내와도 어깨를 나란히 하게 된 것은 아님을 완전히 깨닫고 있는 사람이라는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 레이디 줄리아가 다음 주중에 코시 성에서 게이지비 부부를 만나기로 되어 있다는 사실이 드러났다.\n\n“거기서 마님을 뵙는 기쁨을 누리게 되겠다고 생각하니 참으로 반갑습니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그래요!” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다.\n\n“저는 수요일에 코시로 갑니다. 그러니 아무래도 너무 일러서 마님을 모시는 데 도움이 되어 드리지는 못하겠군요.”\n\n레이디 줄리아는 몸을 곧추세우고서, 크로즈비 씨가 내미는 듯이 보였던 그 동행을 사양했다. 릴리 데일의 미래의 남편이 제 벗과 가까운 사이라는 것을 알게 된 것이 그 여자로서는 언짢았고, 그가 이제 그 벗의 집으로 간다는 것을 알게 된 것은 더욱 언짢았다. 그것은 그 여자에게 언짢은 일이었고, 그 여자는 언짢다는 것을 겉으로 드러냈다. 레이디 줄리아가 코시 성에서 저와 함께 손님으로 묵게 된다는 것은 크로즈비에게도 언짢은 일이었으나, 그는 그것을 겉으로 드러내지 않았다. 그는 그 일에 대해 웃음과 점잖은 자축의 말밖에는 내비치지 않았고, 레이디 줄리아를 다시 뵙게 되어 무척 기쁘다는 시늉을 했다. 그러나 실상 그는 그 마님을 집에 붙잡아 둘 무슨 수라도 꾸며 낼 수만 있다면 큰 것이라도 내놓았을 것이다.\n\n“정말 지긋지긋한 할머니야.” 그들이 말을 타고 가로수길을 되돌아 내려가며 릴리가 말했다. “미안해, 버나드. 물론 저분은 네 이모님이시지만.”\n\n“그래, 내 이모님이지. 그리고 내가 그분을 그리 좋아하지는 않지만, 그분이 지긋지긋한 할머니라는 건 인정 못 하겠는걸. 그분은 누구를 죽인 적도 없고, 누구 것을 빼앗은 적도 없고, 남의 여자의 연인을 가로챈 적도 없어.”\n\n“그야 그렇겠지.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“그분이 기도를 정성껏 드린다는 건 의심할 여지가 없지.” 버나드가 말을 이었다. “게다가 가난한 이들에게 돈도 나눠 주고, 오라버니가 바라는 일이라면 제 소원쯤은 내일이라도 접어 버릴 분이야. 못생기고 거드름을 피운다는 건 나도 인정하지. 그리고 여자로서 윗입술에 그렇게 길고 검은 수염이 나 있어서는 안 된다는 것도.”\n\n“나는 그 수염 따위 아무래도 좋아.” 릴리가 말했다. “하지만 왜 나더러 본분을 다하라고 하시는 거야? 나는 설교를 들으러 거기 간 게 아닌데.”\n\n“그리고 왜 아름다움이 위험하다는 이야기를 꺼내셨을까?” 벨이 말했다. “물론 우리야 무슨 뜻인지 다 알았지만.”\n\n“나는 무슨 뜻인지 전혀 몰랐어.” 릴리가 말했다. “지금도 모르겠고.”\n\n“나는 그분이 매력적인 부인이라고 생각해요. 그러니 드 코시 백작부인 댁에서는 각별히 예의를 갖추어 대할 참이오.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n이런 식으로, 뒤에 두고 온 그 딱한 노처녀를 두고 모진 말을 주고받으며 그들은 게스트윅으로 들어섰고, 이번에는 임스 부인의 집 문 앞에서 말에서 내렸다.\n\n# 제13장. 게스트윅 방문\n\n앨링턴에서 온 일행이 게스트윅의 좁은 하이 스트리트를 따라 올라가 장터 광장을 가로질러, 임스 부인이 사는 저 반듯하되 몹시 따분한 새 집들의 짧은 줄을 향해 말을 몰아갈 때, 게스트윅 사람들은 릴리 데일 양이 장래의 남편에게 호위를 받고 있다는 것을 모두 알고 있었다. 그녀가 참으로 운 좋은 아가씨라는 의견은 게스트윅 사람들 사이에 분명 두루 퍼져 있었으나, 그것이 늘 드러내 놓고 너그러운 말로 표현되지는 않았다. “그 아가씨한테는 대단한 혼처지.” 어떤 이들은 그렇게 말하면서도 동시에 고개를 저었고, 크로즈비 씨의 런던 생활이 마땅히 그래야 할 만한 것은 못 된다고 넌지시 비추면서, 위험한 허세가 덜한 시골 이웃 가운데 누구에게 제 몸을 맡기는 것으로 만족했더라면 더 안전했을지도 모른다고 시사했다. 또 어떤 이들은 따지고 보면 그리 대단한 혼처도 아니라고 단언했다. 그들은 그의 수입을 한 푼까지 알고 있었고, 늙은 지주가 도와줄 생각이 아니라면 그 젊은 부부가 런던에서 집 한 채를 건사하기란 몹시 어려우리라고 믿었다. 그러나 그럼에도 릴리는 잘생긴 연인을 곁에 두고 읍내를 지나가는 동안 부러움을 샀다.\n\n그리고 그녀는 매우 행복했다. 나는 그녀가 부러움을 사고 있다는 것을 아는 데서 얼마간 의기양양한 만족을 느꼈다는 사실을 부인하지 않겠다. 그런 감정은 그녀에게 자연스러운 것이었고, 제 앞가림을 잘 매듭지었다고 자각하는 모든 남자와 여자에게 자연스러운 것이다. 그녀 자신이 말했듯이, 그는 그녀의 새였고, 그녀 자신의 총으로 잡은 노획물이었으며, 그녀 안에 있던 역량이 낳은 소산이었다. 그녀는 남은 평생을 그것에 기대어 살아가야 했고, 될 수 있으면 그것으로 번창해야 했다. 릴리는 자기가 하고 있는 일의 중대함을 충분히 알아보았고, 더없이 진지한 모습으로 이 혼인이라는 문제를 오래 생각해 왔다. 그러나 생각하면 할수록 그녀는 자기가 잘하고 있다는 데 더 만족했다. 그러면서도 그녀는 거기에 위험이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 이제 그녀에게 전부인 그 사람이 죽을 수도 있었다. 아니, 그가 그녀가 여기는 그런 사람이 아닐 수도 있었고, 그녀를 소홀히 하거나 버리거나 함부로 대할 수도 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 모든 것을 믿기로 마음먹었고, 그렇게 믿은 이상 자신을 위한 어떤 퇴로도 마련해 두지 않으려 했다. 그녀의 배는 떠나온 안전한 항구가 보이지도 않는 먼바다 한가운데로 나아가야 했고, 그녀의 군대는 승리가 주는 것 말고는 다른 안전을 바라지 않은 채 싸워야 했다. 온 세상이 이 일을 물어 오겠다면 온 세상이 그녀가 그를 사랑한다는 것을 알아도 좋았다. 그녀는 제 연인을 두고 의기양양했고, 스스로에게조차 자기가 의기양양하다는 것을 부인하지 않았다.\n\n임스 부인은 그들을 보고 기뻐 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다. 크로즈비 씨가 자기처럼 가엾고 외로운 여자를 굳이 찾아와 주다니 참으로 고마운 일이었고, 데일 대위도 참으로 고마웠다. 지금 이 순간 앨링턴 집에서 저희를 행복하게 해 줄 일이 그토록 많은 사랑스러운 아가씨들도 참으로 고마웠다! 남들이 그저 예의치레로 여기는 자잘한 일들이 임스 부인에게는 큰 은혜로 헤아려졌다.\n\n“그런데 그 고마우신 데일 부인은요? 요전 밤에 우리가 그렇게 터무니없이 늦게까지 붙들어 두었는데 지치시지는 않았는지요?” 벨과 릴리는 둘 다 어머니가 그 일로 조금도 상하지 않았다고 그녀를 안심시켰다. 그러자 임스 부인은 일어나 방을 나갔는데, 존과 메리를 찾으러 간다고 말은 했으나 실은 작은 응접실에 자물쇠를 채워 간수해 둔 케이크와 단 포도주를 내오려는 속셈이었다.\n\n“여기 너무 오래 있지는 맙시다.” 크로즈비가 속삭였다.\n\n“네, 아주 오래는 아니고요.” 릴리가 말했다. “하지만 제 친구들을 보러 오셨으면 서두르시면 안 돼요, 크로즈비 씨.”\n\n“이분은 레이디 줄리아하고 벌써 제 차례를 누리셨잖아.” 벨이 말했다. “이제는 우리 차례여야지.”\n\n“어쨌든 임스 부인은 우리한테 본분을 다하라느니 너무 아름다워지지 않게 조심하라느니 하지는 않으실 거야.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n메리와 존은 어머니가 돌아오기 전에 방으로 들어왔다. 그러고 나서 임스 부인이 왔고, 몇 분 뒤에 케이크와 포도주가 도착했다. 그 자리는 분명 다소 따분했으니, 일행 가운데 누구도 편안해 보이지 않았기 때문이다. 크로즈비 씨의 위풍은 임스 부인과 그 딸에게는 너무 벅찼고, 존은 제 처지의 비참함에 거의 입을 다물고 있었다. 그는 아직 로퍼 양의 편지에 답을 하지 않았고, 답을 할 것인지 말 것인지조차 마음을 정하지 못한 터였다. 게다가 릴리가 행복해하는 모습은 어린 시절의 벗으로서 마땅히 느꼈어야 할 그 정겨운 기쁨으로 그를 가득 채워 주지 못했다. 사실대로 말하자면 그는 크로즈비를 미워했고, 스스로에게 그렇게 말해 왔으며, 그 잔치가 있던 날 이후로 누이에게도 아주 자주 그렇게 말해 왔다.\n\n“내 말 좀 들어 봐, 몰리.” 그는 그렇게 말했었다. “할 방법만 있다면 나는 저 사람하고 결투를 하겠어.”\n\n“뭐? 그래서 릴리를 비참하게 만들려고?”\n\n“릴리는 저 사람하고는 결코 행복해지지 못해. 나는 확신해. 릴리한테 해를 끼치고 싶지는 않지만, 그래도 저 사람하고 한판 붙고 싶어—어떻게 해야 하는지만 알면 말이야.”\n\n그러고 나서 그는 만약 그런 맞대결에서 둘 다 죽어 없어질 수 있다면 그것이야말로 지금의 사정에 어울리는 유일한 결말이 되리라고 생각했다. 그렇게 되면 어밀리아에게서 벗어날 길도 생길 터였고, 지금 이 순간 그에게는 그 밖의 다른 길이 보이지 않았다.\n\n그가 방에 들어섰을 때 그는 앨링턴에서 온 일행 모두와 악수를 했으나, 나중에 누이에게 말한 대로 크로즈비를 만졌을 때는 살갗에 소름이 돋았다. 크로즈비는 임스 집안 사람들이 제 집 응접실 의자에 뻣뻣하고 거북하게 앉아 있는 것을 바라보면서, 아내가 런던에 오거든 존 임스를 되도록 적게 보게 하는 편이 좋겠다고 마음먹었다. 아내의 연모자를 조금이라도 질투해서가 아니었다. 그는 릴리에게서 모든 것을—적어도 릴리가 아는 모든 것을—들어 알고 있었고, 그 일을 오히려 좋은 우스갯거리로 여겼다. “그 사람을 너무 자주 만나지는 마요.” 그는 그녀에게 그렇게 말했었다. “바보짓을 할까 무서우니.” 릴리는 그에게 모든 것을—말할 수 있는 모든 것을—이야기했었다. 그러나 그는 릴리가 사실은 자기가 업신여기는 그 젊은이에게 따뜻한 애정을 품고 있다는 것을 조금도 깨닫지 못했다.\n\n“고맙지만 사양하겠습니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “저는 한낮에는 포도주를 입에 대는 법이 없어서요.”\n\n“그럼 케이크라도 한 조각?” 그러면서 임스 부인은 눈빛으로 그에게 그만한 영광이라도 베풀어 달라고 간청했다. 그녀는 데일 대위에게도 간청했으나 두 사람 다 요지부동이었다. 나는 그 두 아가씨가 두 남자보다 조금이라도 더 먹고 마실 마음이 있었는지 알지 못한다. 그러나 그들은 아무도 그 진미에 손을 대지 않으면 임스 부인이 가슴 아파하리라는 것을 알았다. 사교의 자잘한 희생은 모두 여자들이 치르며, 인생의 큰 희생도 마찬가지다. 무엇에든 쓸모 있는 남자는 언제나 제 본분을 다할 준비가 되어 있고, 착한 여자는 언제나 희생할 준비가 되어 있다.\n\n“이제 정말 가 봐야겠어요.” 벨이 말했다. “말들 때문에요.” 그리고 이 핑계로 그들은 자리를 떴다.\n\n“런던으로 돌아가기 전에 한 번 들르실 거죠, 존?” 릴리가 말했다. 그는 그녀가 말에 오르는 것을 거들 생각으로 따라 나왔으나, 크로즈비 씨의 무쇠 같은 뜻 앞에서 그 뜻을 접을 수밖에 없었다.\n\n“네, 다시 들르겠습니다—떠나기 전에요. 안녕히 가세요.”\n\n“안녕히 계세요, 존.” 벨이 말했다. “잘 있게, 임스.” 데일 대위가 말했다. 크로즈비는 안장에 자리를 잡으면서 알은체하는 시늉을 아주 희미하게 지어 보였으나, 그의 연적은 거기에 관심을 기울여 줄 만큼 몸을 낮추려 하지 않았다. “어떻게든 저 사람하고 한판 붙고 말겠다.” 임스는 어머니 집의 복도를 걸어 들어가면서 속으로 그렇게 말했다. 그리고 크로즈비는 등자에 발을 고쳐 넣으면서 그 젊은이가 점점 더 싫어지는 것을 느꼈다. 그 감정에 질투라고 할 만한 것이 조금이라도 섞여 있으리라 여긴다면 어처구니없는 일일 터였다. 그럼에도 그는 그가 몹시 싫었고, 그에게 다시 앨링턴으로 오라고 청한 릴리에게 화가 날 지경이었다. “이건 다 끝을 내야겠다.” 그는 말없이 읍내를 벗어나 말을 몰면서 속으로 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“제 친구들을 홀대하시면 안 돼요, 선생님.” 릴리가 말했다. 말하면서 웃고 있었으나 목소리에는 얼마간 진지한 기색이 있었다. 이때쯤 그들은 읍내를 벗어나 있었고, 크로즈비는 임스 부인 집 문을 나선 뒤로 거의 한마디도 하지 않았다. 이제 그들은 큰길에 올라 있었고, 벨과 버나드 데일은 그들보다 조금 앞서 가고 있었다.\n\n“나는 아무도 홀대하지 않아요.” 크로즈비가 짜증스레 말했다. “그러니까, 홀대받아 마땅한 사람이 아니라면 말이오.”\n\n“그럼 저는 마땅했나요? 제가 홀대를 받은 것 같아서요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리요, 릴리. 나는 아직 당신을 홀대한 적이 없고, 앞으로 그럴 성싶지도 않소. 하지만 당신 친구들에게 예의를 차리지 않는다고 나를 몰아세워서는 안 되지요. 첫째로 나는 내 천성이 허락하는 만큼 그들에게 예의를 차리고 있소. 그리고 둘째로는—”\n\n“그래요, 둘째로는요?”\n\n“나는 지금 이 시점에 당신이 그 젊은이의—우정을 북돋우는 것이 그리 현명한지 잘 모르겠소.”\n\n“그건 제가 그러는 게 아주 잘못이라는 말씀이겠지요?”\n\n“아니, 내 사랑, 그런 뜻이 아니오. 그런 뜻이었다면 정직하게 그렇게 말했을 거요. 나는 딱 말한 그대로를 뜻하오. 그가 당신에게 어떤 낭만적인 연정 같은 것을 가슴에 가득 채워 두고 있다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없을 거요. 이루어지리라 기대해 본 적도 없을 어리석은 사랑이지만, 그 생각이 그의 삶에 일종의 운치를 더해 주는 것이지. 아내 삼기에 알맞은 젊은 여자를 만나면 그는 그 일을 죄다 잊을 테지만, 그때까지는 스스로를 절망한 연인이라 여기며 돌아다닐 거요. 그리고 존 임스 같은 젊은이는 제 공상을 입 밖에 내기가 아주 쉽소.”\n\n“그가 제 이름을 누구에게든 입에 올리리라고는 한순간도 믿지 않아요.”\n\n“하지만 릴리, 아마 내가 당신보다 젊은 남자들을 더 잘 알 거요.”\n\n“네, 물론 그러시겠죠.”\n\n“그리고 장담하건대 그들은 대체로 저희가 좋아한다고 여기는 아가씨들의 이름을 함부로 입에 올리는 데 너무나 익숙하오. 어떤 사내도 당신 이름을 함부로 입에 올리는 것을 내가 원치 않는다고 해서 놀라서는 안 되오.”\n\n그 뒤로 릴리는 일이 분 동안 말이 없었다. 그녀는 자기가 부당한 일을 당하고 있다고 느꼈고 그것을 참고 넘길 마음이 들지 않았으나, 그 부당함이 어디에 있는지 딱 집어 볼 수가 없었다. 그녀가 크로즈비에게 빚진 것은 많았다. 아주 많은 일에서 그녀는 그에게 따라야 했고, 그를 위해서라면 제 본분보다 더한 일도 하고 싶었다. 그러면서도 그녀에게는 모든 일에서 그에게 굽히는 것이 좋지 않으리라는 굳은 확신이 있었다. 그녀는 될 수 있는 한 그가 생각하는 대로 생각하고 싶었으나, 그와 다르게 여긴다는 것을 알면서 그와 뜻이 같다고 말할 수는 없었다. 존 임스는 그녀가 저버릴 수 없는 오랜 벗이었고, 지금으로서는 그만큼은 말해야 한다고 그녀는 느꼈다.\n\n“하지만 어돌퍼스—”\n\n“왜 그러오, 내 사랑?”\n\n“존 임스처럼 아주 오랜 벗에게 제가 매정하게 굴기를 바라시지는 않겠지요? 저는 그 사람을 평생 알고 지냈고, 우리는 모두 그 집안 전체를 아주 크게 여겨 왔어요. 그의 아버지는 삼촌의 각별한 벗이었고요.”\n\n“릴리, 내 말뜻은 알아들었으리라 생각하오. 나는 당신이 그 사람들 누구와도 다투기를 바라지 않고, 당신 말대로 매정하게 굴기를 바라지도 않소. 하지만 이 젊은이에게 런던으로 돌아가기 전에 당신을 보러 오라고, 그러고 나서는 당신이 런던에 닿는 즉시 또 보러 오라고 특별히 다잡아 청할 것까지는 없지 않소. 당신은 그가 당신을 사랑한다는 어떤 낭만적인 생각을 품고 있다고, 당신이 자기를 사랑하지 않아서 절망에 빠져 있다고 나에게 말했소. 물론 죄다 터무니없는 소리요. 하지만 그런 사정이라면 내가 보기에 당신은—그냥 그 사람을 내버려 두는 편이 낫겠소.”\n\n릴리는 다시 말이 없었다. 이 사흘은 그녀의 마지막 사흘이었고, 그동안 그녀는 각별히 행복하려고, 그러나 무엇보다도 그를 각별히 행복하게 해 주려고 마음먹고 있었다. 무슨 일이 있어도 그녀는 그에게 모진 말을 하지 않을 것이고 제 마음속에 그를 향한 미움을 키우지도 않을 것이었다. 그러면서도 그녀는 그가 틀렸다고 여겼고, 그렇게 여기고 있으니 그 부당함을 참아 넘기기가 좀처럼 되지 않았다. 데일 집안 사람으로서 그것이 그녀의 천성이었다. 그리고 큰 희생에 자신을 바칠 수 있는 사람들 가운데 아주 많은 이가 작은 부당함은 견디지 못한다는 것을 기억해 두시라. 릴리는 연인을 위해서라면 어떤 즐거움도 내놓을 수 있었으나, 자기가 옳다고 믿으면서 자기가 틀렸었다고 하는 것만은 스스로에게 허락할 수 없었다.\n\n“이제 이미 청했으니 그 사람은 와야 해요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 더는 오라고 다잡지는 마시오.”\n\n“당연히 그러지 않겠어요, 어돌퍼스, 그렇게 말씀하셨으니. 그가 앨링턴으로 오면 어머니 집에서 저를 볼 거예요. 어머니는 늘 그를 반겨 맞아 주셨으니까요. 물론 저는 잘 알고 있어요—”\n\n“무엇을 안다는 말이오, 릴리?”\n\n그러나 그녀는 말을 이었다가는 그의 마음을 상하게 할 말을 하게 될까 두려워 스스로 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“당신이 안다는 것이 무엇이오, 릴리?”\n\n“말을 잇게 다잡지 마세요, 어돌퍼스. 제가 할 수 있는 한 당신이 바라시는 것은 다 하겠어요.”\n\n“내 집에 들어와 살게 되면 당연히 제 친구들을 보러 오라고 청하지 못하리라는 말을 하려던 거겠지. 그게 고운 마음이오?”\n\n“무슨 말을 하려 했든 저는 그렇게 말하지 않았어요. 사실 그런 뜻도 아니었고요. 제발 이제 그 이야기는 그만하세요. 이게 우리의 마지막 날들이잖아요. 그런데 불쾌한 이야기로 그 날들을 허비해서는 안 되지요. 따지고 보면 가엾은 조니 임스는 저에게 아무것도 아니에요. 아무것도, 아무것도. 제가 당신을 생각하는데 누가 저에게 무엇이 될 수 있겠어요?”\n\n그러나 이것으로도 크로즈비는 곧바로 유쾌한 기분으로 돌아오지는 못했다. 릴리가 그에게 굽히고 그가 옳았다고 인정했더라면 그는 당장 오월의 햇살처럼 환해졌을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 그렇게 하지 않았다. 그녀는 그저 언짢아지고 싶지 않아서 말싸움을 삼갔을 뿐이었고, 임스가 약속한 대로 찾아오면 만나겠다는 뜻은 그대로임을 밝혔던 것이다. 크로즈비는 그녀가 스스로 잘못했다고 인정해 주기를 바랐을 것이고, 그녀를 용서하는 특권을 마음껏 누렸을 것이다. 그러나 릴리 데일은 용서라는 것도, 용서를 받아야 하는 처지라는 것도 그리 반기지 않는 사람이었다. 그리하여 두 사람은 말없이는 아니더라도 이야기에 이렇다 할 즐거움 없이 말을 몰아갔다. 이제 월요일 오후 늦은 때였고, 크로즈비는 수요일 아침 일찍 떠나기로 되어 있었다. 그런데 이 마지막 사흘이 이런 끔찍한 걸림돌들로 얼룩지고 만다면 어찌한단 말인가!\n\n버나드 데일은 하하 도랑 옆 둑에 누워 있다가 크로즈비와 릴리에게 방해를 받은 뒤로 사촌에게 제 청혼 이야기를 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그는 데일 부인의 잔치에서 그녀와 몇 번이고 거듭 춤을 추었고, 어려움 없이 예전의 이야기 투로 돌아간 듯 보였다. 그래서 벨은 그 일이 끝났다고 믿었고 사촌에게 고마워하면서, 그 일 전체를 아예 없었던 일처럼 다루겠다고 제 가슴속으로 다짐했다. 누구에게도—어머니에게조차 그녀는 말하지 않을 것이었다. 그를 위해 그렇게 입을 다물기로 스스로를 묶었으니, 그가 그렇게 되기를 가장 기뻐하리라 느꼈기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 다른 두 사람보다 훨씬 앞서 나란히 말을 몰아가면서 그는 다시 그 이야기로 돌아왔다.\n\n“벨,” 그가 말했다. “나는 조금이라도 희망을 가져도 되는 거야?”\n\n“무엇에 대한 희망 말이야, 버나드?”\n\n“이런 문제에서 사내가 대답 한 번으로 물러서야 하는 것인지 나는 잘 모르겠어. 하지만 이것만은 알아. 사내의 마음이 걸린 일이라면 그러고 싶은 마음이 별로 들지 않는다는 것 말이야.”\n\n“그 대답이 정직하고 참되게 주어진 것이라면—”\n\n“아, 물론이지. 네가 나한테 말할 기회를 주지 않았을 때 정직하지 못했다거나 거짓이었다고는 조금도 생각하지 않아.”\n\n“하지만 버나드, 나는 너한테 말할 기회를 주지 않은 적이 없어.”\n\n“그와 아주 비슷한 일이었지. 하지만 어쨌든 네가 충분히 참되었다는 것은 의심하지 않아. 그런데 벨, 왜 그래야 하지? 네가 다른 누구를 사랑하고 있다면 나도 이해할 수 있을 텐데.”\n\n“나는 다른 누구도 사랑하고 있지 않아.”\n\n“바로 그거야. 그리고 너와 내가 서로의 앞날과 재산을 하나로 합쳐야 할 이유는 그토록 많고.”\n\n“재산 문제일 수는 없어, 버나드.”\n\n“내 말 좀 들어 줘. 하다못해 말이라도 하게 해 줘. 적어도 네가 나를 싫어하지는 않는다고 여겨도 되겠지.”\n\n“아, 그럴 리가.”\n\n“그리고 네가 그저 재산 문제만으로 어떤 사내의 손을 받아들일 마음이 없다 하더라도, 우리 혼인이 돈 문제에서 모든 면으로 알맞다는 사실이 너를 그 혼인에서 등 돌리게 만들어서는 안 되잖아. 너를 향한 내 사랑에 대해서는 더 말하지 않겠어. 내 말을 네가 믿어 주리라는 것을 의심하지 않으니까. 하지만 너와 가장 가까운 사람들 모두의 바람을 거스르기로 마음먹기 전에, 네 마음을 아주 꼼꼼히 캐물어 보아야 하지 않겠어?”\n\n“어머니를 말하는 거야, 버나드?”\n\n“특별히 숙모님을 말하는 건 아니야. 물론 온 집안을 한데 묶어 주고 너에게도 나와 똑같은 몫의 재산 권리를 주는 혼인이라면 숙모님도 좋아하시리라고 생각하지 않을 수 없지만.”\n\n“그건 어머니한테는 깃털만 한 무게도 없을 거야.”\n\n“여쭤 보기는 했어?”\n\n“아니, 나는 아무한테도 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않았어.”\n\n“그럼 알 수 없는 거지. 그리고 삼촌으로 말하자면, 나는 그것이 삼촌 평생의 큰 소원이라는 것을 알 만한 길이 있어. 나를 헤아리는 마음은 너에게 아무 무게가 없다 하더라도, 삼촌을 얼마쯤 헤아린다면 마지막 대답을 주기 전에 한 번 멈춰야 마땅하다고 나는 말하지 않을 수 없어.”\n\n“나는 삼촌보다 너를 위해 더 많은 것을 하겠어—훨씬 더.”\n\n“그럼 나를 위해 이것을 해 줘. 내 청혼에 아직 대답을 받지 못했다고 생각하게 해 줘. 오늘로부터 한 달 뒤까지, 크리스마스까지, 네가 대고 싶은 아무 때까지라도 좋아. 그래야 내가 아직 정해지지 않았다고 생각할 수 있고, 크리스토퍼 삼촌한테도 그렇다고 말할 수 있을 테니까.”\n\n“버나드, 그건 부질없는 일이야.”\n\n“어쨌든 네가 그 일을 생각해 볼 마음은 있다는 걸 삼촌한테 보여 주기는 하겠지.”\n\n“하지만 나는 그 일을 생각해 볼 마음이 없어—그런 식으로는. 나는 내 마음을 속속들이 알고 있고, 너를 속인다면 그건 아주 나쁜 짓이야.”\n\n“그럼 그것을 삼촌께 전할 네 유일한 대답으로 삼기를 바라는 거야?”\n\n“사실대로 말하면, 버나드, 나는 이 일로 네가 삼촌께 무슨 말을 하든 별로 개의치 않아. 삼촌한테는 내 혼사를 어떻게 하느냐에 끼어들 권리가 있을 수 없고, 그러니 나는 이 문제에서 삼촌의 바람을 헤아릴 필요가 없어. 이 일에 대한 내 마음을 한마디로 밝혀 줄게. 나는 어머니의 바람을 거슬러서는 어떤 남자도 받아들이지 않을 거야. 하지만 어머니를 위해서라도 내 마음을 거슬러서는 어떤 남자도 받아들일 수 없어. 그러나 삼촌으로 말하자면, 나는 이런 문제에서 어떤 식으로든 삼촌과 의논해야 할 처지라고 여기지 않아.”\n\n“그래도 삼촌은 우리 집안의 어른이잖아.”\n\n“나는 집안 같은 건 조금도 개의치 않아—그런 식으로는.”\n\n“그리고 삼촌은 너희 모두에게 아주 너그러우셨고.”\n\n“그건 아니야. 삼촌은 어머니에게 너그럽지 않았어. 삼촌은 어머니에게 아주 매정하고 인색해. 삼촌이 어머니에게 그 집을 쓰게 해 주는 건 데일 집안이 세상 앞에 번듯해 보이기를 바라서고, 어머니가 거기 사시는 건 그러는 편이 우리에게 낫다고 여기시기 때문이야. 내 뜻대로라면 어머니는 내일이라도 그 집을 나오셔야 해—아니면 적어도 릴리가 시집가는 대로 말이야. 나는 차라리 게스트윅으로 들어가서 임스네처럼 사는 편이 훨씬 좋겠어.”\n\n“나는 네가 은혜를 모른다고 생각해, 벨.”\n\n“아니, 나는 은혜를 모르는 게 아니야. 그리고 의논으로 말하자면, 버나드—내 혼사에 대해서라면 나는 삼촌보다 너와 의논하는 편이 훨씬 마음이 기울어. 네가 나로 하여금 너를 온전히 오라비로 여기게 해 준다면, 네가 찬성하지 않는 사람과는 아무와도 혼인하지 않겠다고 약속하는 것쯤은 대수롭지 않게 여기겠어.”\n\n그러나 두 사람 사이의 그런 약조는 버나드가 바라보는 바에 결코 맞지 않았다. 그는 너덧 주 전만 해도 이 혼인을 개인적으로 그리 간절히 바라지는 않는다고 생각했다. 그는 스스로에게, 사촌이 마음에 든다고, 자리를 잡는 것이 자기에게 좋은 일이라고, 삼촌의 바람은 사리에 맞고 내놓는 조건도 넉넉히 후하다고, 그러니 혼인하겠다고 말해 두었다. 사촌이 그토록 훌륭한 청혼을 물리치리라고는 좀처럼 생각해 본 적이 없었고, 그런 거절 때문에 자기가 무엇인가를 견뎌야 하리라고는 정말이지 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 연인들이 주사위 한 번 던지기에 제 전부를 건다고 말할 때의 그 감정 같은 것을 그는 조금도 품지 않았다. 하하 도랑 옆 잔디밭에 누워 나른한 사랑의 사연을 나직이 늘어놓을 때 그는 자기가 무엇인가를 걸고 있다고 느끼지 않았다. 실망과 슬픔과 깊이 상한 마음의 가능성을 그는 헤아려 보지 않았다. 받아들여진다 해도 그는 별로 의기양양하지 않았을 것이고, 거절당한다고 해서 자기가 모욕을 느낄 수 있으리라고는 생각하지도 않았다. 그런 마음가짐으로 그는 일에 나섰다. 그러나 이제 그는 이 아가씨의 대답이 자기를 아주 불행하게 만들었다는 것을 스스로도 놀랍게 깨달았다. 이것을 바란다고 입 밖에 낸 이상, 바란다고 말했다는 그 사실 자체가 그것을 손에 넣고 싶게 만들었다. 그는 말없이 그녀 곁에서 말을 몰아가면서, 자기가 스스로 알고 있던 것보다 더 간절한 열망을 품을 수 있는 사람임을 깨달았다. 그는 이 순간 불행했고, 실망했고, 불안했고, 앞날을 믿지 못했으며, 소년 시절에도 겪어 본 적 없을 만큼 단 하나의 장난감에 마음을 쏟고 있었다. 그는 속이 상했고 가슴이 쓰라린 것을 느꼈다. 그는 조랑말 위에 말없이 조용히, 그리고 얼마쯤 서글프게 앉아 있는 그녀를 돌아보고는, 그녀가 참으로 아름답다고, 아직도 손에 넣을 가망이 있다면 반드시 손에 넣어야 할 것이라고 스스로에게 단언했다. 그는 자기가 그녀를 정말로 사랑한다고 느꼈고, 그러면서도 그렇게 느끼는 자신에게 거의 화가 났다. 왜 그는 스스로를 이런 저리는 나약함에 내맡겼단 말인가? 그의 사랑은 그에게 아무런 기쁨도 준 적이 없었다. 사실 그는 여태껏 그것을 사랑이라 인정해 본 적조차 없었다. 그런데 이제 그것이 괴로움과 아픔의 근원임을 알게 되자 인정하지 않을 수 없게 된 것이다. 나는 여기까지 와서도 버나드 데일이 사촌을 사랑한 것인지, 오히려 제 욕망을 사랑한 것은 아닌지 우리가 의심해 볼 여지가 있다고 생각한다. 그러나 그는 자신을 상대로 자기가 사랑에 빠졌다는 판결을 내렸고, 자신에게도 온 세상에게도 화가 났다.\n\n“아, 벨.” 그가 그녀에게 바싹 다가서며 말했다. “내가 너를 얼마나 사랑하는지 네가 알아줄 수 있으면 좋겠어.” 그리고 그가 그렇게 말할 때, 사촌은 저도 모르게 그의 말투에서 여태 알던 것보다 더 많은 애정을, 그리고 그가 앞서 내놓은 모든 간청에 배어 있는 듯하던 그 흥정하는 기색은 여태 알던 것보다 덜한 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n“나도 너를 사랑하지 않는 게 아니잖아? 모든 면에서 누이가 되어 주겠다고 하지 않았어?”\n\n“그건 아무것도 아니야. 지금 나한테 그런 말을 하는 건 그냥 나를 비웃는 거야. 벨, 내 말 들어 봐—나는 너를 포기하지 않겠어. 사실은 네가 아직 나를 모르는 거야—어떤 사내를 남편으로 고르기 전에 반드시 알아야 하는 만큼은 나를 모르는 거지. 이 점에서 너와 릴리는 서로 달라. 너는 조심스럽고, 너 자신을 미덥지 않아 하고, 어쩌면 남들도 얼마쯤 미덥지 않아 하지. 내 마음은 이 일에 걸려 있고, 나는 그래도 이루어 보려고 애쓰겠어.”\n\n“아, 버나드, 그런 말 하지 마! 결코 그럴 수 없다고 말할 때는 내 말을 믿어 줘.”\n\n“아니, 나는 네 말을 믿지 않겠어. 나는 나 자신이 아주 비참해지도록 내버려 두지 않겠어. 솔직히 말하는데 나는 네 말을 믿지 않겠어. 내가 바라기로 마음먹었다면 바라도 되는 것 아니겠어. 아니야, 벨, 나는 결코 너를 포기하지 않겠어—네가 다른 남자의 아내가 되는 것을 정말로 보게 되지 않는 한은.”\n\n그가 이 말을 할 때 그들은 모두 지주의 문을 지나 안으로 들어섰고, 늘 말에서 내리곤 하던 마당까지 말을 몰아 올라갔다.\n\n# 제14장. 존 임스가 산책을 하다\n\n존 임스는 말 탄 일행이 어머니 집 문 앞에서 떠나가는 것을 지켜보다가, 말발굽 소리가 거리 밖으로 사라지자마자 홀로 산책을 나섰다. 그렇게 걸어 나서는 그의 마음은 결코 편치 않았다. 아니, 그는 근심과 걱정에 짓눌려 있었고, 길을 가는 동안 아주 우울한 생각들이 머릿속을 지나갔다. 차라리 오스트레일리아로 가는 편이 낫지 않을까, 아니면 밴쿠버섬으로, 아니면—? 이 가엾은 친구가 머지않아 떠나야 할지도 모른다고 여긴 그 긴 여행의 종착지로 스스로 꼽아 본 곳들의 이름을 나는 여기 적지 않겠다. 바로 그날, 데일 집안 사람들이 들이닥치기 조금 전에, 그는 사랑하는 어밀리아에게서 두 번째 편지를 받았다. 첫 번째 편지의 뒤를 바짝 좇아 쓰인 편지였다. 어찌하여 답장을 하지 않았느냐? 앓고 있느냐? 마음이 변한 것이냐? 아니, 그것만은 그녀도 믿지 않겠단다. 그리하여 그가 앓고 있으리라는 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 만일 그렇다면 그녀는 한달음에 내려와 그를 보겠노라고 했다. 세상 그 무엇도 약혼자의 머리맡에서 그녀를 떼어 놓지는 못하리라고. 사랑하는 존에게서 다음 우편으로 답장이 오지 않는다면, 그녀는 급행열차를 타고 게스트윅으로 그를 찾아 내려오겠노라고 했다. 존 임스 같은 젊은이에게 이 무슨 처지란 말인가! 그리고 어밀리아 로퍼로 말하자면, 그녀는 이길 가망이 티끌만큼이라도 남아 있는 한 판을 놓지 않는 젊은 여자였다고 우리는 말해 둘 수 있다. “어디로든 가야겠다.” 존은 챙 늘어진 모자를 눌러쓰고 게스트윅의 뒷골목으로 헤매어 나서면서 혼잣말을 했다. 어머니가 어밀리아 로퍼 이야기를 들으시면 무어라 하실까? 그녀를 직접 보시면 무어라 하실까?\n\n그는 게스트윅 숲속을 홀로 거닐 요량으로 저택 쪽을 향해 걸어갔다. 데일 집안 사람들이 말을 타고 집으로 올라간 그 문간채에서 반 마일쯤 더 간 곳에, 큰길에서 갈라져 나가는, 층계 달린 오솔길이 하나 있었다. 존 임스는 그 길로 접어들어, 저택을 뒤로 하고 게스트윅 숲 한가운데에 이를 때까지 걸어 들어갔다. 그는 혼자 산책을 나가도 좋다는 허락을 처음 받은 뒤로 줄곧 그곳을 쏘다녔던 터라 그 일대를 속속들이 알고 있었다. 참나무들 사이에서 저를 잊은 채 몇 시간이고 릴리 데일을 생각한 적도 있었다. 그러나 그 지난날에는 얼마간 즐거운 마음으로 그녀를 생각했다. 이제는 영영 저에게서 떠나가 버린 사람으로만 그녀를 생각할 수 있을 뿐이었다. 그리고 그다음에는, 릴리의 자리에 저 스스로 끌어들인 그 여자까지 생각해야 했다.\n\n젊은이들, 아주 젊은 사내들—아직 어른이 되었다고 할 수 있을지 없을지조차 의문일 만큼 젊은 사내들은, 남들과 함께 있을 때보다 혼자 있을 때 더 진지해지고 더 생각이 깊어지는 법이다. 그 남들이 저보다 나이 든 사람일 때조차 그러하다. 나는 우리가 나이를 먹어 가면서 우리에게도 그러했다는 사실을 곧잘 잊어버리고, 잊어버린 나머지 우리 아이들도 그러하리라고는 믿지 않게 되는 것이라고 생각한다. 우리는 젊음의 생각 없음을 노상 입에 올린다. 나로서는 오히려 젊음의 생각 많음을 말하는 편이 더 알맞지 않을까 싶다. 그러나 생각이 곧바로 지혜를 낳지는 않는다는 것 또한 의심할 바 없는 사실이다. 우리 가운데 많은 사람이 원숙한 나이에 이르러 지니게 된 지혜라는 것이, 생각과 결심의 열매라기보다는 유혹의 힘이 시들어 버린 데서 온 것은 아닌지 거의 의문스러울 지경이다. 다 자라 제 일에 매인 사내들은 대개 너무 바빠서 깊이 생각할 겨를이 없다. 그러나 세상일의 무게가 아직 온전히 내려앉지 않은 소년들에게는—그들에게는 생각할 시간이 있다.\n\n그리하여 존 임스는 생각이 많았다. 그를 가장 잘 안다는 이들은 그를 쾌활하고 마음씨 착하며 다소 무모한 젊은이, 유혹에도 쉬 넘어가지만 좋은 감화도 쉬 받아들이는 젊은이로 여겼다. 대단한 성공을 점칠 수는 없으나 그래도 벗들에게 망신을 안기지 않고 큰 말썽도 일으키지 않을 만큼은 살아가리라고 어지간히 기대해 볼 만한 사람이라고. 그러나 무엇보다도 그들은 그를 생각 없는 사람이라고 불렀을 것이다. 그렇게 부르는 데서 그들은 그를 잘못 보았다. 그는 늘 생각하고 있었다—제 눈에 비친 세상을, 그리고 세상의 눈에 비친 저를 골똘히 생각하고 있었다. 또한 세상 너머의 것들까지 생각하고 있었다. 이승에서, 그리고 저승에서 제 운명은 어찌 될 것인가? 릴리 데일은 저에게서 떠나갔고, 어밀리아 로퍼는 맷돌처럼 목에 매달려 있었다! 그런 처지에서 이승과 저승의 제 운명은 어찌 될 것인가?\n\n그의 앞을 가로막은 어려움이 아직 그리 대단한 것은 아니었다고 우리는 말할 수 있다. 릴리에 관해서라면 그에게 희망의 여지가 없기는 했다. 그러나 릴리를 향한 그의 사랑은 아마도 정념이라기보다는 감상에 가까웠다. 젊은이라면 대개 그런 실연을 한 번은 겪게 마련이고, 앞날이나 행복에 큰 손상을 입지 않고 그것을 견뎌 낸다. 그리고 훗날에 이르면 그런 사랑의 기억은 저주라기보다 축복이 되어, 그것을 지닌 사람으로 하여금 그 이른 시절에 제 안에 부끄러워할 것 없는 무언가가 있었음을 느끼게 해 준다. 릴리 데일에 관한 한 나는 존 임스를 그리 딱하게 여기지 않는다. 그리고 어밀리아 로퍼로 말하자면—만일 그가 그 여자가 세상에서 쌓은 경험의 십분의 일이라도 쌓았거나 그 여자의 대담함의 사분의 일이라도 지녔더라면, 그런 어려움쯤이야 그의 앞길을 그토록 가로막지는 못했으리라! 그녀와 결혼할 뜻이 없노라고 그가 딱 잘라 말해 버린다면 어밀리아가 그에게 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가? 실상 그는 그리하겠다고 약속한 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그는 어떤 식으로도 그녀에게 매여 있지 않았다. 명예로써조차 매여 있지 않았다. 명예라니, 그런 여자를 상대로! 그러나 사내란 여자 앞에서 폭군이 되기 전까지는 겁쟁이다. 그리고 희생시키는 편이 희생당하는 편보다 더 즐겁고 또 그만큼 쉽다는 사실을 문득 깨닫기 전까지는 손쉬운 봉이다. 물론 그 나중 교훈을 끝내 배우지 못하는 사내들도 있다.\n\n그러나 두려워할 까닭이 그토록 하잘것없었건만, 가엾은 존 임스는 철저히 겁에 질려 있었다. 그가 품은 그토록 깊은 슬픔과 나란히 놓고 보자면 입에 올리기조차 우스운 자잘한 일들이 그의 난처함을 보태어, 거기서 빠져나가는 일을 도무지 있을 수 없는 일처럼 보이게 만들었다. 그는 버턴 크레슨트에 들르지 않고서는 런던으로 돌아갈 수가 없었다. 옷가지가 거기 있었고, 또 로퍼 부인에게 얼마간의 돈을 빚지고 있는데 런던에 돌아가는 길로 곧장 주머니에서 꺼내 줄 형편이 못 되었기 때문이다. 그러니 그는 어밀리아를 만나야만 했다. 그리고 그는 한번 사랑한다고 말해 버린 뒤에 처녀의 얼굴을 마주하고 사랑하지 않는다고 말할 배짱이 저에게 없다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그가 낼 수 있는 가장 대담한 생각이라야 그녀를 단념하는 편지를 한 통 쓰고 버턴 크레슨트가 있는 그 동네에서 아주 거처를 옮겨 버리는 데까지가 고작이었다. 그러나 그러면 옷가지는? 또 그 빚은? 그리고 만일 그러는 사이에 어밀리아가 게스트윅으로 내려와 저를 제 것이라 주장하고 나선다면? 어머니가 보시는 앞에서 그녀에게 그런 주장을 할 권리가 없다고 잘라 말할 수 있을까? 어려움이란 것이 사실 그리 대단하지는 않았으나, 소득세 사무국의 그 가엾은 젊은 서기가 감당하기에는 너무나 무거웠다.\n\n그가 바보에 겁쟁이였음이 분명하다고 여러분은 말하리라. 그러나 그는 셰익스피어를 읽고 이해할 줄 알았다. 바이런의 시라면 많이도—너무나도 많이 외우고 있었다. 그는 깊이 있는 비평가였고, 저 스스로 적어 나가는 긴 일기에 그 비평을 곧잘 써 두기도 했다. 그는 글을 빠르게, 또 알아듣게 쓸 줄 알았다. 그리고 그가 결코 바보가 아니라는 것은 그의 사무국 사람들이 이미 알아보았다고 나는 잘라 말할 수 있다. 그는 제 일을 알았고 해낼 줄도 알았다—세상 눈에는 훨씬 덜 어리석어 보이는 많은 사람이 해내지 못한 그 일을. 그리고 겁쟁이 운운하는 문제로 말하자면, 그는 크로즈비와 한 방에 갇혀 둘 중 하나가 싸움에 지쳐 릴리 데일에 대한 제 권리를 내놓을 때까지 맞붙어도 좋다는 허락을 받는다면 그것을 세상에서 가장 큰 복으로 여겼을 것이다. 임스는 겁쟁이가 아니었다. 그는 세상 어떤 사내도 두려워하지 않았다. 그러나 어밀리아 로퍼만은 지독히도 두려웠다.\n\n그는 옛 저택 숲속을 마음 편치 못한 채로 이리저리 헤맸다. 게스트윅에서 우편은 일곱 시에 나갔고, 그러니 그날 어밀리아에게 편지를 쓸 것인지 말 것인지 당장 마음을 정해야 했다. 또 그녀에게 무슨 말을 할 것인지도 정해야 했다. 답장이 어떤 것이 되든 적어도 답장은 해야 한다는 생각이 들었다. 결혼하겠다고 약속할까—이를테면 열 해나 열두 해 뒤에? 자기는 사랑에 걸맞지 않은 시들어 버린 존재라고 말하고 겸손히 용서를 빌어 볼까? 아니면 그 어머니에게 편지를 써서 버턴 크레슨트가 이제 저에게는 맞지 않겠노라 하고, 다음 봉급을 받는 대로 남은 돈을 부치겠다고 약속하고, 옷가지는 보따리에 싸서 소득세 사무국으로 보내 달라고 부탁할까? 아니면 제 어머니에게 돌아가 다 털어놓아 버릴까?\n\n마침내 그는 편지를 써야만 한다고 마음을 정했다. 그리고 머릿속으로 편지를 지어 나가면서, 숲속 오솔길 여럿이 만나 서로 엇갈리는 자리에 서 있는 늙은 나무 한 그루 밑에 앉았다. 여기서 그가 엮어 낸 편지는, 종이에 옮겨 적어 부치기만 할 수 있었더라면 결코 나쁜 편지가 아니었다. 그는 낱말 하나하나를 꼼꼼히 골랐고, 제 뜻을 또렷이 전하고 제 목적을 옳게 밝혀 주는 표현마다 머릿속에서 힘주어 눌러 두었다. “그는 편지를 주고받는 상대를 그릇 이끌어 제 마음을 얻은 줄로 여기게 만든 것이 제 잘못이었음을 인정했다. 그의 마음은 그녀가 마음대로 할 수 있는 것이 아니었다. 진작 편지하지 않은 것은 그가 약했던 탓이니, 그녀에게 아픔을 줄까 두려워 그리하지 못했던 것이다. 그러나 이제 그는 명예를 걸고 그녀에게 진실을 말해야 한다고 느꼈다. 그렇게 말한 이상, 저를 그곳에서 보는 것이 그녀에게 괴로운 일이라면 그는 버턴 크레슨트로 돌아가지 않겠다. 그는 언제까지나 그녀를 깊이 아끼는 마음을 간직할 것이며”—아, 조니!—“그녀의 앞날이 온전히 복되기를 애타게 바라겠노라고.” 나무 밑에서 그가 마음의 서판에 적어 넣은 편지는 그러했다. 그러나 그것을 종이에 옮겨 적는 일은, 저도 알다시피 훨씬 더 어려운 일이었다. 그러다가 그것을 속으로 되뇌던 그는 잠이 들었다.\n\n“이보게, 젊은이.” 그가 잠들어 있는데 귓가에서 어떤 목소리가 말했다. 처음에 그 목소리는 그를 깨우지 않은 채 꿈속의 목소리처럼 들렸으나, 그것이 되풀이되자 그는 몸을 일으켜 앉았고 살진 신사 한 사람이 저를 굽어보고 서 있는 것을 보았다. 한순간 그는 저가 어디 있는지, 어떻게 거기 오게 되었는지 알지 못했다. 둘러선 나무들을 보면서도 저가 얼마나 오래 숲속에 있었는지 떠올릴 수 없었다. 그러나 두 해가 넘도록 뵌 적이 없었건만 그 살진 신사가 누구인지는 잘 알아보았다. “이보게, 젊은이.” 목소리가 말했다. “류머티즘에 걸리고 싶거든 딱 그렇게 하면 되네. 아니, 임스네 아들 아닌가?”\n\n“네, 백작님.” 조니가 이렇게 말하며 몸을 추슬러, 누워 있던 자세에서 이제는 앉은 자세가 되어 백작의 발그레한 얼굴을 올려다보았다.\n\n“자네 아버님을 내가 알았지, 아주 좋은 분이었네. 다만 농사에 손을 대지 말았어야 했어. 사람들은 배우지 않고도 농사를 지을 수 있는 줄 알지만, 그건 크게 잘못 아는 걸세. 나는 농사를 지을 줄 아네, 배웠으니까. 그만 일어나는 게 좋지 않겠나?” 그 말에 조니는 몸을 일으켜 섰다. “뭐, 자네가 좋다면 거기 누워 있어도 얼마든지 괜찮네만. 다만 시월이라는 게 말이야—”\n\n“함부로 들어와 죄송합니다, 백작님.” 임스가 말했다. “길에서 벗어나 들어왔다가, 그만—”\n\n“괜찮네, 얼마든지 괜찮아. 집까지 올라오면 점심을 좀 주겠네.” 그러나 이 후한 제안을 조니는 거절하며, 시간이 늦었고 저녁을 먹으러 집에 가는 길이라고 둘러댔다.\n\n“따라오게.” 백작이 말했다. “집을 지나는 길보다 가까운 길은 없네. 저런, 저런, 자네 아버님이 어찌나 또렷이 기억나는지. 나보다 훨씬 영리한 분이었지—훨씬 더. 그런데 짐승을 장에 내다 파는 일은 어린애만큼도 몰랐단 말이야. 참, 그러고 보니 자네를 관청에 넣어 주었다지, 그렇지 않은가?”\n\n“네, 백작님.”\n\n“그것도 아주 잘한 일이지—참으로 잘한 일이야. 그런데 어째서 숲속에서 잠이 들었나? 날이 따뜻하지도 않은데 말일세. 나는 도리어 춥다고 하겠네.” 그러고서 백작은 걸음을 멈추고, 그토록 깊은 수수께끼는 기어이 캐어 보고야 말겠다는 듯 그를 찬찬히 뜯어보며 바라보았다.\n\n“산책을 하다가, 무언가 생각할 것이 있어서 앉았습니다.”\n\n“휴가를 받았나 보군?”\n\n“네, 백작님.”\n\n“무슨 곤경에 빠진 겐가? 곤경에 빠진 얼굴인데. 자네 가엾은 아버님도 곧잘 곤경에 빠지곤 했지.”\n\n“저는 농사에 손을 대지는 않았습니다.” 조니가 웃어 보이려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n“하, 하, 하—옳은 말이야. 암, 농사에는 손대지 말게. 배우지 않고 할 바에야 구둣방을 차리는 편이 나을 걸세—꼭 마찬가지야. 그러니까 곤경에 빠진 건 아니란 말이지, 응?”\n\n“아닙니다, 백작님, 딱히 그런 건 아닙니다.”\n\n“딱히 그런 건 아니라니! 젊은 사람이 런던에 올라가면 곤경에 빠지게 마련이라는 걸 나도 잘 아네. 무엇이든—조언이든 그런 것이든 필요하거든 나를 찾아오게. 자네 아버님을 내가 잘 알았으니 말일세. 사냥은 좋아하나?”\n\n“저는 무얼 쏘아 본 적이 없습니다.”\n\n“그래, 어쩌면 안 하는 편이 낫지. 사실 나는 쏠 것도 없으면서 총질에 맛을 들이는 젊은이들을 그리 좋아하지 않네. 참, 그러고 보니 자네 어머님께 사냥한 고기를 좀 보내야겠군.” 그러나 여기서 밝혀 두는 것이 온당하겠는데, 게스트윅 저택에서 임스 부인에게 사냥한 고기가 오는 일은 아주 잦았다. “그리고 이보게, 아침 식사에는 식은 꿩고기만 한 것이 없네. 만찬에 꿩이라니 쓰레기지—순전히 쓰레기야. 자, 집에 다 왔네. 들어와서 포도주 한잔하지 않겠나?”\n\n그러나 이것도 존 임스는 거절했고, 그리함으로써 받아들였을 때보다 백작을 더 흡족하게 해 주었다. 백작이 손님 대접에 인색하다거나 마음에 없는 말을 한 것은 아니었다. 다만 그는 존 임스 같은 사람이라면 저가 베푼 친근함을 그 자리에서 넙죽 받아들이지는 않기를 바랐던 것이다. 백작은 임스가 저와 함께 걷는 이의 지체 앞에서 얼마간 주눅 들어 있음을 알아차렸고, 그 때문에 그가 더 마음에 들었다. 그 때문에 그가 더 마음에 들었거니와, 백작은 한번 마음에 든 것을 잘 잊지 않는 사람이었다. “들어오지 않겠다면 잘 가게.” 그러고서 그는 조니에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n“안녕히 계십시오, 백작님.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 이것만은 새겨 두게. 허리에 류머티즘이 오면 그야말로 지독한 일일세. 나 같으면 나무 밑에서 잠들지는 않겠네—적어도 시월에는 말이야. 하지만 이 근방이라면 어디든 자네는 언제든 와도 좋네.”\n\n“고맙습니다, 백작님.”\n\n“그리고 혹시라도 사냥에 맛을 들이거든—뭐, 자네는 안 그럴 성싶네만. 그리고 곤경에 빠져 조언이든 그런 것이든 필요하거든 내게 편지하게. 자네 아버님을 내가 잘 알았네.” 그리하여 두 사람은 헤어졌고, 임스는 게스트윅으로 가는 길로 돌아섰다.\n\n무슨 까닭인지는 저도 꼬집어 말할 수 없었으나, 백작과 이야기를 나누고 나서 그는 기분이 나아졌다. 그 뚱뚱하고 사람 좋고 지각 있는 노인에게는, 슬픔에 잠긴 그를 그런데도 북돋워 주는 무언가가 있었다. “만찬에 꿩이라니 쓰레기지—순전히 쓰레기야.” 그는 길을 가는 내내 몇 번이고 되뇌었다. 그리고 그것이 집에 들어서고 나서 어머니에게 처음으로 한 말이었다.\n\n“우리한테도 그런 쓰레기가 좀 있었으면 좋겠구나.” 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“내일이면 생길 겁니다.” 그러고서 그는 어머니에게 그 만남을 이야기해 드렸다.\n\n“땅바닥에 드러누운 일만은 백작님 말씀이 지당하시다. 어쩌면 그렇게 어리석을 수가 있느냐. 그리고 네 가엾은 아버지에 대해서도 그분 말씀이 옳고. 그건 그렇고 너는 신발을 갈아 신어야겠다. 저녁상은 곧 차려질 게다.”\n\n그러나 조니 임스는 저녁상에 앉기 전에 어밀리아에게 보낼 편지를 쓰기는 썼고, 제 손으로 부치러 나가기도 했다—어머니에게는 몹시도 못마땅한 일이었다. 그러나 그 편지는 숲속을 헤맬 때 떠올랐던 그 힘 있고 알맞은 말로는 도무지 쓰이지 않았다. 그것은 맹숭맹숭한 편지였고, 게다가 어딘가 비겁한 편지였다.\n\n친애하는 어밀리아에게 [편지는 이러했다],—보내신 두 통 다 받았습니다. 첫 번째 편지에 답하지 못한 것은 제가 하고 싶은 말을 표현하기가 어렵다고 느꼈기 때문입니다. 그리고 이제는 제가 시내로 돌아갈 때까지 그 이야기를 접어 두시는 편이 좋겠습니다. 저는 지금부터 열흘 뒤에 그곳에 가 있을 것입니다. 저는 아주 잘 지내 왔고 지금도 그러합니다. 물론 물어봐 주신 것은 대단히 고맙게 여깁니다. 이 말이 아주 쌀쌀맞게 들리리라는 것을 압니다. 그러나 제가 모든 것을 다 말씀드리면, 이것이 최선이라는 데 당신도 저와 같은 생각이 되실 것입니다. 만일 제가 결혼을 한다면 우리는 불행해지고 말리라는 것을 저는 압니다. 먹고살 것이 아무것도 없을 테니까요. 제가 혹 당신을 속이는 말을 한 적이 있다면 온 마음을 다해 용서를 빕니다.—그러나 아무래도 그 이야기는 우리가 런던에서 다시 만날 때까지 접어 두는 편이 좋겠습니다.\n\n믿어 주십시오 당신의 더없이 진실한 벗이며, 또한 흠모하는 사람이라고 해도 좋을,—[아, 존 임스!]\n\n# 제15장. 마지막 날\n\n마지막 날이란 비참한 날이다. 그리고 마지막 순간 또한 비참한 순간이다. 이런 날과 순간을 그토록 비참하게 만드는 것은 이별이 다가온다는 사실이 아니라, 그날과 그 순간에서 무언가 특별한 것이 기대된다는 느낌이다. 그런데 그 무언가는 언제나 나오지 않고 만다. 즐거움이든 애정이든 심지어 공부든, 발작하듯 몰아치는 시간은 미리 계획해 두면 실망으로 끝나지 않는 법이 드물다. 마지막 날이 다가올 때에는 특별히 알리거나 입에 올리지 말고 그저 오게 두었다가 그대로 흘러가게 두어야 한다. 그리고 마지막 순간으로 말하자면, 그런 것은 아예 없어야 한다. 그 존재를 인정하기도 전에 끝나 버리게 두라.\n\n그러나 릴리 데일은 아직 세상 경험으로부터 이런 교훈을 배우지 못했고, 그래서 자기가 지금껏 마셔 본 것 가운데 가장 달콤한 이 잔이 입술에 댈 수 있는 동안은 계속 달콤하기를, 더 달게 더욱더 달게 달기를 기대했다. 그 마지막 몇 방울에 어떻게 앙금이 섞여 들었는지는 우리가 이미 본 바와 같다. 그리고 바로 그날, 월요일 저녁에도 쓰디쓴 과업은 여전히 남아 있었다. 저녁에 정원을 거니는 동안 크로즈비가 다른 화제들을 찾아내어, 그녀를 깨우칠 요량으로 이런저런 암시를 주는 것이 필요하다고 여겼기 때문인데, 그 암시는 훈계의 냄새를 잔뜩 풍기며 그녀에게 다가왔다. 아가씨란, 릴리가 분명히 그러했듯 온전히 사랑에 빠져 있을 때에는, 자기가 몸과 마음을 바친 남자에게서 앞날의 삶에 관한 암시를 받는 것을 좋아한다. 그러나 내 생각으로는, 그런 암시가 짧기를, 그리고 그 가르침이 선언되기보다 넌지시 담기기를 바랄 것이다. 다시 말해 그것이 훈계가 아니라 암시이기를 바랄 것이다. 크로즈비는 요령 있는 남자였고, 세상을 알았으며, 여러 해 동안 여자들을 상대해 온 사람이었으니, 이 모든 것을 우리만큼이나 잘 알고 있었음에 틀림없다. 그러나 그는 자기가 손해를 보는 사람이라는 생각, 자기가 받는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이 주고 있다는 생각, 따라서 다른 연인에게라면 정당하게 허용되지 않을 방자함을 자기는 부려도 된다는 생각을 품게 되어 있었다. 내 독자는 이 모든 점에서 그가 너그럽지 못했다고 말할 것이다. 그렇다. 그는 너그럽지 못했다. 나는 그에게서 큰 너그러움을 기대할 만하다고 말한 적이 한 번도 없다고 생각한다. 그에게도 옳고 그름에 관한 몇 가지 원칙은 있었고, 그 인도를 받아 아주 못쓰게 빗나가지는 않으리라고 어쩌면 바라볼 수도 있었다. 그러나 그의 지난 삶은 그를 이기심 없는 사람으로 만들어 줄 만한 종류의 것이 아니었다. 그는 너그럽지 못했고, 릴리도 그것을 느꼈다. 다만 그녀는 자기 자신에게조차 그것을 인정하려 들지 않았다. 그녀는 그에게 아주 솔직했다. 그를 향한 자기 사랑의 깊이를 털어놓았고, 이제 그가 자기에게 전부라고, 그의 사랑 없는 삶은 자기로서는 불가능하다고 말했다. 그런데 그는 어떤 면에서 이 강렬한 고백들을 이용하여, 그녀가 온전히 자기 손아귀에 들어 있는 존재인 양 그녀를 대했다. 실제로 그녀는 그러했다.\n\n그날 저녁 그는 조니 임스에 관해서는 더 말하지 않았으나, 자기 자신의 그리 넉넉지 않은 수입 말고는 기댈 것이 없는 남자가 런던에서 아내를 두고 살림을 차리는 일이 얼마나 어려운지에 대해서는 많은 말을 했다. 그녀의 집안이 그녀 몫으로 이삼천 파운드쯤, 그러니까 그가 처음 청혼했을 때 기대했던 것보다는 훨씬 적은 액수나마 마련해 줄 수 있다면 이 무서운 어려움이 사라질 것이라고 대놓고 말하지는 않았다. 그러나 아무것도 가진 것 없는 아가씨를 데려간다면 세상이 그를 몹시 경솔하다고 부르리라는 것만은 그녀가 알아듣도록 충분히 말했다. 그리고 그가 이런 이야기를 하는 동안 릴리는 대체로 잠자코 있었는데, 그러다 문득 그는 자기 지난 삶에 관해서도 그녀에게 마음 놓고 이야기할 수 있겠다는 생각이 들었다. 그런 고백으로 그녀를 잃을지 모른다는 두려움이 있었다면 결코 그러지 못했을 만큼 마음 놓고 말이다. 그는 그녀를 잃을까 두려워하지 않았다. 아아! 어쩌면 그런 희망을 조금 품고 있었던 것은 아닐까!\n\n그는 그녀에게 자기 지난 삶이 돈이 많이 드는 삶이었다고 말했다. 빚은 없지만 가진 돈은 한 실링까지 다 쓰며 살아왔고, 하루아침에 내려놓기란 거의 불가능한 씀씀이 습관이 몸에 배었다고 했다. 그러고 나서 그는 얽힌 일들에 관해 말했는데, 말하면서는 그것이 어떤 성질의 것인지 더 자세히 밝힐 작정이었으나, 릴리가 자기 말뜻을 전혀 알아듣지 못하는 것을 보고는 감히 그러지 못했다. 아니, 그는 너그러운 남자가 아니었다. 지극히 너그럽지 못한 남자였다. 그런데도 이 모든 시간 내내 그는 자기가 원칙에 이끌려 움직이고 있다고 생각했다. “그녀에게 솔직한 편이 가장 낫다.” 그는 스스로에게 말했다. 그러고는 청혼할 때 자기는 그녀가 무일푼이 아니리라 기대했으며 또 그렇게 기대할 권리가 있었다고 수없이 되뇌었다. 그런 사정에서 그는 그녀를 위해 최선을 다한 것이었다. 정직하게 자기 마음을 바쳤고, 그녀가 가능하다고 여기는 가장 이른 날에 그녀를 자기 사람으로 삼으려고 선뜻 서둘렀으니 말이다. 좀 더 조심했더라면 이런 잔인한 실수에 빠지지 않아도 되었을 것이다. 그러나 적어도 그녀만은 그의 경솔함을 두고 그와 다툴 수 없을 터였다. 그리고 그는 여전히 약혼을 지키기로 마음먹고 있었고 그녀와 결혼할 뜻도 있었다. 다만 생각하면 할수록, 그렇게 함으로써 자기 앞날을 망치고 얻고자 바라 온 그 모든 좋은 것들을 제 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 밀어내 버리게 되리라는 느낌이 점점 강해질 뿐이었다. 그녀에게 이야기를 계속하는 동안 그는 자기 너그러움에 각별한 공을 돌렸고, 두 사람의 결혼 앞에 놓인 온갖 어려움을 그녀에게 짚어 주는 것이야말로 그녀에 대한 자기 의무를 다하는 일이라고 느꼈다.\n\n처음에 릴리는 자기가 세상 어느 남자가 가져 본 것보다 알뜰한 아내가 되겠다는 뜻의 말을 몇 마디 했으나, 곧 그런 약속을 그만두었다. 그녀는 눈치가 빨랐고, 그가 두려워하는 어려움이란 결혼한 뒤에 그를 짓누르리라 예상되는 것이 아니라 결혼 전에 그가 넘어야 할 것이라는 사실을 알아차렸다. “싸구려에 지저분한 살림살이는 내가 질색하는 것이오.” 그가 그녀에게 말했다. “내가 피하고 싶은 것이 바로 그거요. 무엇보다 당신을 위해서 말이오.” 그러자 그녀는 그의 때가 올 때까지 참을성 있게 기다리겠노라고 약속했다. “설령 칠 년이 걸린다 해도요.” 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 그의 얼굴을 올려다보고 거기서 무슨 인정의 기색이라도 찾아보려 했다. “그건 말도 안 되는 소리요.” 그가 말했다. “요즘 사람들은 족장 시대 사람이 아니오. 아마 이 년은 기다려야 할 거요. 그것만 해도 지독한 골칫거리지. 정말 끔찍한 골칫거리요.” 그리고 그의 목소리에는 그녀의 감정을 긁는 무언가가 있어서, 그 순간 그녀를 비참하게 만들었다.\n\n한쪽 정원에서 다른 쪽 정원으로 이어지는 작은 다리의 그녀 쪽 끝에서 밤 인사를 하며 헤어질 때, 그는 그 자리에서 여러 번 그랬듯 그녀를 껴안고 입 맞추려고 팔을 둘렀다. 거기서 저녁 작별 인사를 하는 것이 두 사람의 버릇이 되어 있었고, 관목 사이의 그 호젓한 구석은 릴리에게 말할 수 없이 소중했다. 그러나 이번만은 그녀가 그의 포옹을 피하려 했다. 그녀는 그에게서 몸을 돌렸다. 아주 조금이었지만 그것으로 충분했고, 그는 그것을 느꼈다. “나한테 화가 났소?” 그가 말했다. “아, 아니에요! 어돌퍼스. 제가 어떻게 당신한테 화를 내겠어요?” 그러고는 그를 향해 돌아서서, 그가 다시 청하기도 전에 얼굴을 내밀어 입 맞추게 했다. “적어도 내가 저이한테 매정하게 굴었다고는 생각하지 않게 해야지. 그리고 이제 와서 그게 무슨 상관이야.” 그녀는 어둠 속에서 잔디밭을 가로질러 어머니의 응접실 창문 쪽으로 천천히 걸어가며 스스로에게 말했다.\n\n“그래, 우리 딸.” 혼자 있던 데일 부인이 말했다. “오늘 저녁 큰 집 홀에서는 수염들이 즐겁게 흔들리더냐?” 그것은 두 사람 사이의 농담이었으니, 크로즈비도 버나드 데일도 몸단장할 때 면도칼을 쓰지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n“딱히 즐겁진 않았어요. 그리고 그건 제 탓이에요. 머리가 아파서요. 엄마, 저 그냥 바로 자러 갈까 봐요.”\n\n“얘야, 무슨 안 좋은 일이라도 있니?”\n\n“아무것도 아니에요, 엄마. 다만 오늘 말을 아주 오래 탔고요. 그리고 어돌퍼스가 떠나잖아요. 물론 우리도 할 이야기가 많고요. 내일이 마지막 날이에요. 수요일 아침에는 잠깐 얼굴만 볼 테니까요. 그러니 될 수 있으면 몸이 괜찮았으면 해서, 자러 갈래요.” 그러고서 그녀는 촛불을 들고 갔다.\n\n벨이 올라왔을 때 릴리는 아직 깨어 있었지만, 언니에게 자기를 건드리지 말아 달라고 부탁했다. “나한테 말 걸지 마, 언니.” 그녀가 말했다. “마음을 가라앉히려는 중인데, 계속 이야기하면 어린애처럼 굴게 될 것 같아. 감당할 수 있는 것보다 더 많이 생각할 게 있거든.” 그러고서 그녀는 자기를 짓누르는 근심이 성질상 언짢은 것은 아니라는 듯 명랑한 어조로 말하려고 애썼고, 아주 실패하지는 않았다. 그러자 언니는 그녀에게 입 맞추고 그녀를 그녀 자신의 생각에 맡겨 두었다.\n\n그리고 그녀에게는 생각할 거리가 컸다. 어찌나 컸던지, 계단의 시계가 여러 시각을 울려 그녀의 귀에 들려온 뒤에야 비로소 자기 마음에 흡족한 모양으로 생각을 정리할 수 있었다. 마침내 그녀는 그렇게 정리했고, 그러고서 잠이 들었다. 베개를 적시는 눈물과 함께, 가슴이 타고 거의 찢어지는 심정으로, 숱한 의심과 함께, 그리고 자기가 마땅히 해야 할 일과 자기가 견뎌 낼 수 있는 일에 관해 제 가슴속에 던진 많은 불안하고 간절한 물음과 함께, 그 일을 힘겹게 해내고서야 그녀는 생각을 그렇게 정리했다. 그러나 마침내 결심이 섰고, 그러고서 그녀는 잠들었다.\n\n크로즈비가 이튿날 아침 식사 뒤에 작은 집으로 내려와서 말을 타러 나갈 때까지 거기 머무르기로 두 사람 사이에 정해져 있었다. 그러나 릴리는 이 약속을 바꾸기로 마음먹고, 그리하여 아침을 먹자마자 모자를 쓰고 다리에 나가 서서 연인이 오는 길목을 지켰다. 그는 곧 친구 데일과 함께 나타났고, 그녀는 대뜸 자기 뜻을 그에게 말했다.\n\n“당신하고 이야기가 하고 싶어요, 어돌퍼스. 엄마한테 들어가시기 전에요. 그러니까 저랑 같이 들판으로 가요.”\n\n“좋소.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 버나드는 잔디밭에서 담배나 마저 피우면 돼. 엄마랑 언니가 그리로 나올 테니까.”\n\n“좋아.” 버나드가 말했다. 그렇게 그들은 갈라졌고, 크로즈비는 릴리와 함께 들판으로 갔다. 건초를 만들던 그 시절에 두 사람이 처음으로 서로를 알게 된 바로 그 들판이었다.\n\n집에서 멀찍이 벗어날 때까지 그녀는 별말을 하지 않았고, 그가 골라 하는 말에 대답만 했다. 그가 무슨 이야기를 하는지도 잘 모르는 채였다. 그러나 알맞은 자리에 이르렀다고 여겨지자 그녀는 아주 불쑥 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“어돌퍼스.” 그녀가 말했다. “당신한테 할 이야기가 있어요. 아주 주의 깊게 들어 주셔야 할 이야기예요.” 그러자 그는 그녀를 바라보았고, 곧 그녀가 진심임을 알았다.\n\n“이 이야기를 할 수 있는 건 오늘이 마지막 날이에요.” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “그리고 마지막 날을 그냥 흘려보내지 않고 이 말을 하게 되어 참 다행이에요. 편지로는 어떻게 써야 할지 몰랐을 거예요.”\n\n“무슨 이야기요, 릴리?”\n\n“그리고 제대로 말할 수 있을지도 모르겠어요. 하지만 저를 모질게 대하지는 말아 주세요. 어돌퍼스, 우리 사이의 이 모든 것을 끝내고 싶으시다면, 저는 그러겠어요.”\n\n“릴리!”\n\n“제 말은 진심이에요. 당신이 원하신다면 저는 그러겠어요. 그리고 제 입으로 먼저 그러자고 한 것이니, 당신이 제 말대로 하신대도 제가 결코 당신을 탓하지 않으리라는 것만은 꼭 믿으셔도 돼요.”\n\n“내가 싫증이 났소, 릴리?”\n\n“아니에요. 저는 절대 당신에게 싫증 나지 않을 거예요. 당신을 사랑하는 일에 결코 지치지 않을 거예요. 지금 그런 말을 하려던 건 아니지만, 물으시니 당돌하게 대답할게요. 당신한테 싫증이라니요! 저는 아가씨가 자기 연인에게 싫증을 낼 수 있으리라고는 생각하지 않아요. 하지만 저는 당신을 망치는 원인이 되느니 차라리 이 싸움 속에서 죽는 편을 택하겠어요. 그편이 나아요. 어느 모로 보나 그편이 나아요.”\n\n“나는 망한다는 말은 한 적이 없소.”\n\n“하지만 제 말을 들어 주세요. 당신이 저를 떠나신다 해도 저는 죽지 않아요. 아주 가슴이 무너져 버리지도 않을 거예요. 이 세상 무엇도 제가 당신을 사랑한 것처럼 사랑할 수는 없겠지요. 하지만 제게는 하느님이 계시고 구주가 계시니 그것으로 충분해요. 당신이 저를 떠나시는 게 옳다면, 저는 만족하며 그분들께로 돌아설 수 있어요. 저는 이미 그분들께 갔었고, 그리고….” 그러나 이 대목에서 그녀는 더 말을 잇지 못했다. 감정이 북받쳐 목소리를 잃고 말문이 막힌 것이다. 자기가 무너지는 모습을 그에게 보이고 싶지 않았으므로, 그녀는 그에게서 몸을 돌려 풀밭을 가로질러 걸어갔다.\n\n물론 그는 그녀를 뒤따랐다. 그러나 그리 빠르지 않아서, 그녀에게는 마음을 추스를 시간이 주어졌다. “정말이에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “제게는 말씀드린 그런 힘이 있어요. 저는 제 몸을 아내로 당신께 드렸지만, 지금은, 지금은 당신에게서 갈라지는 것을 견딜 수 있어요. 그리고 내 사랑, 매정하게 들릴지 몰라도, 저는 당신을 물속으로 끌어내려 근심과 걱정에 빠뜨려 익사시키는 통나무처럼 당신에게 매달리느니 차라리 그렇게 갈라지겠어요. 그렇게 하겠어요. 정말 그러겠어요. 당신이 가시면 물론 제게 그런 종류의 일은 다 끝나겠지요. 하지만 세상에는 그것 말고도 더 많은 것이, 훨씬 더 많은 것이 있어요. 그리고 저는 스스로 행복해지겠어요. 네, 내 사랑, 저는 행복할 거예요. 그 점은 걱정하지 않으셔도 돼요.”\n\n“하지만, 릴리, 어째서 이 모든 말을 오늘 여기서 나한테 하는 거요?”\n\n“그게 제 의무니까요. 저는 이제 당신의 처지를 다 알아요. 이제야 알게 되었지만요. 어제까지는 한 번도 생각이 미치지 않았어요. 당신이 제게 청혼하셨을 때, 당신은 제게, 제게 재산이 좀 있는 줄 아셨던 거예요.”\n\n“지금 그건 신경 쓰지 마시오, 릴리.”\n\n“하지만 그러셨잖아요. 이제 다 알겠어요. 어쩌면 제가 그렇지 않다고 당신께 말씀드렸어야 했어요. 착오가 있었고, 우리 둘 다 그 때문에 괴로워하고 있어요. 하지만 그 괴로움을 굳이 더 깊게 만들 필요는 없잖아요. 내 사랑, 당신은 자유로워요. 이 순간부터요. 그리고 당신이 그 자유를 받아들이신다 해도 제 마음조차 당신을 탓하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“그럼 당신은 가난이 두려운 거요?” 그가 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n“저는 당신이 가난해지는 게 두려워요. 당신과 저는 다르게 살아왔어요. 제가 전혀 알지 못하는 호사가 당신에게는 나날의 편안함이었고요. 말씀드리지만 저는 당신과 헤어지는 건 견딜 수 있어도, 당신을 불행하게 만드는 근원이 되는 것은 견딜 수 없어요. 그래요, 저는 견디겠어요. 그리고 제가 듣는 데서 아무도 감히 당신을 나쁘게 말하지 못하게 하겠어요. 저는 그 말을 하려고 당신을 여기로 데려왔어요. 아니, 그보다 더한 것을요. 그 말을 하시라고 권하려고요.”\n\n그는 잠시 말없이 서서 그녀의 손을 잡고 있었다. 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보고 있었으나 그는 구름 쪽으로 눈길을 돌린 채, 자기가 이 자리의 주인인 것처럼 보이려 애쓰고 있었다. 그러나 그 순간 그의 마음은 의심으로 갈가리 찢기고 있었다. 만일 그녀의 말대로 한다면 어떨까? 몇몇은 그를 두고 모진 말을 하겠지만, 그런 모진 말은 다른 많은 남자에게도 아무 해를 끼치지 않고 지나가지 않았던가. 그녀의 말대로 하는 편이 두 사람 모두에게 좋지 않을까? 그녀는 다른 아가씨들이 그랬듯 회복하여 다시 사랑하게 될 것이다. 그리고 그로 말하자면, 지난 한 주 내내 노려보고 있던 파멸에서 그렇게 벗어날 수 있을 것이다. 그것은 파멸이었으니까. 그야말로 완전한 파멸. 그는 그녀를 사랑했다. 적어도 그는 스스로에게 그렇게 단언했다. 그러나 그가 사랑을 위해 세상을 내던져야 할 남자인가? 그런 남자들이 있기는 했다. 그러나 그가 그런 사람 가운데 하나인가? 뉴 로드 근처 어딘가의 그 작은 집에서, 다섯 아이와 빵집 외상값에 대한 끔찍한 걱정을 안고 그가 행복할 수 있을까? 세상 모든 남자 가운데, 그런 덫에 스스로 빠지도록 두어서는 안 될 마지막 사람이 바로 그가 아니었던가? 웅장하고 고결해 보이려는 표정으로 구름 쪽으로 얼굴을 들고 있는 동안 이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다.\n\n“말씀해 주세요, 어돌퍼스. 그렇게 하자고요.”\n\n그러자 그는 겁이 났고, 자기 곤경에서 빠져나올 용기가 없었다. 혹은 나중에 스스로에게 말한 대로, 그렇게 할 만한 매정함이 없었다. “내가 제대로 알아들었다면, 릴리, 이 모든 말은 당신 쪽에 사랑이 모자라서 나온 것은 아니지요?”\n\n“제 쪽에 사랑이 모자라다니요? 그런 건 제게 물으시면 안 되죠.”\n\n“그런 것이 모자란다고 당신이 내게 말하기 전에는, 나는 어떤 이별에도 동의하지 않겠소.” 그러고서 그는 그녀의 손을 잡아 자기 팔에 끼워 넣었다. “아니오, 릴리. 우리에게 어떤 근심과 어려움이 있든, 우리는 함께 묶여 있소. 풀 수 없게 말이오.”\n\n“그런가요?” 그녀가 말했다. 그렇게 말하는 동안 그녀의 목소리가 떨렸고 손도 떨렸다.\n\n“그런 이별 따위로는 어림도 없이 단단히 묶여 있소. 아니오, 릴리. 나는 내 온갖 근심을 당신에게 말할 권리가 있다고 주장하오. 그러나 당신을 놓아주지는 않겠소.”\n\n“하지만, 어돌퍼스….” 그리고 그의 팔에 얹은 손이 다시 그 팔을 붙잡기 시작했다.\n\n“어돌퍼스는,” 그가 말했다. “그 문제에 관해서는 더 할 말이 없소. 그는 자기 것이라 믿는 권리를 행사하며, 자기가 얻어 낸 상을 지키기로 한다오.”\n\n이제 그녀는 정말로 그에게 매달리고 있었다. “아, 내 사랑!” 그녀가 말했다. “어떻게 다시 말해야 할지 모르겠어요. 제가 생각하는 건 당신이에요. 당신, 오직 당신이에요!”\n\n“나도 알고 있소. 다만 당신이 나를 조금 잘못 알아들었을 뿐이오. 그뿐이오.”\n\n“제가요? 그럼 다시 한번만 제 말을 들어 주세요. 내 마음의 임자, 내 사랑, 내 남편, 내 주인이여! 제가 곧바로 룻처럼 되어 당신을 따라나서기를 그치지 못하는 사람이 될 수는 없다 해도, 당신을 향한 제 마음만은 룻의 마음과 같을 거예요. 죽음 외에 무엇이 당신과 저를 갈라놓는다면, 하느님이 제게 벌을 내리시고 또 내리시기를.” 그러고서 그녀는 그의 가슴에 얼굴을 묻고 울었다.\n\n그는 여전히 그녀 성품의 깊이를 거의 이해하지 못했다. 그것을 다 헤아릴 만큼 그 자신이 깊지 못했다. 그러나 그래도 그는 그녀의 크나큰 사랑에 경외를 느꼈고, 어떤 엄숙한 감정으로 고양되어 그때만은 얼마 전에 내린 결심을 기뻐하게 되었다. 몇 시간 동안 그는 세상을 뒤로 내던지고, 이런 여자를 마땅히 지녀야 할 방식대로 이 여자를 지니고 살 마음이 되어 있었다. 즉 모든 일에서 자기를 위로해 주는 사람으로, 그리고 큰 어려움을 막아 주는 든든한 방패로 지니고 살 마음 말이다. “릴리.” 그가 말했다. “나의 릴리!”\n\n“네, 당신 것이에요. 원하실 때 가지시고, 원하실 때는 가지지 않고 두셔도 되는, 이래도 저래도 똑같이 당신 것이에요.” 그러고서 그녀는 다시 올려다보며 웃어 보이려 애썼다. “제가 정신이 나갔다고 생각하시겠지만, 저는 너무 행복해요. 이제는 당신이 떠나신대도 아무렇지 않아요. 정말 아무렇지 않아요. 자, 지금 당장이라도 가시고 싶으면 가셔도 돼요.” 그러고서 그녀는 그에게서 손을 뺐다. “지난 며칠 동안과는 마음이 아주 달라요. 당신이 제게 그렇게 말씀해 주셔서 정말 기뻐요. 물론 저는 그 모든 것을 당신과 함께 견뎌야지요. 하지만 이제는 그 때문에 불행할 수가 없어요. 제가 이것저것 잔뜩 만들어 두면 도움이 될까요?”\n\n“이를테면 내 셔츠 한 벌 같은 것 말이오?”\n\n“그 정도야 어쨌든 제가 할 수 있죠.”\n\n“머지않아 정말 그렇게 될지도 모르오.”\n\n“저는 그렇게 되기를 하느님께 기도해요.” 그러고서 그녀는 다시 진지해졌고 두 눈에 또다시 눈물이 고였다. “저는 그렇게 되기를 하느님께 기도해요. 당신께 쓸모가 되는 것, 당신을 위해 일하는 것, 무언가 차분하고 진지한 쓸모의 뜻이 담긴 일을 당신을 위해 하는 것, 그게 제가 무엇보다 바라는 거예요. 당신께 도움이 될 수 있도록 어서 당신 곁에 있고 싶어요. 당신과 제가 단둘이 있어서 제가 당신을 위해 모든 걸 할 수 있다면 좋겠어요. 저는 가끔 아주 가난한 남자의 아내가 제일 행복하지 않을까 생각해요. 정말로 모든 걸 다 하니까요.”\n\n“곧 당신이 다 하게 될 거요.” 그가 말했다. 그러고서 두 사람은 오전 내내 즐겁게 거닐었고, 다시 데일 부인의 식탁에 나타났을 때 데일 부인과 벨은 릴리의 밝은 모습에 놀랐다. 그녀의 옛 모습이 죄다 되돌아온 듯했고, 크로즈비 씨가 처음 그녀의 사랑스러움에 홀리던 무렵에 하던 것처럼 그녀는 그에게 앙큼한 말들을 던졌다. “그 백작부인 댁에 가시면 어찌나 멋쟁이가 되실지, 앨링턴은 죄다 잊어버리실걸요.”\n\n“물론 그럴 거요.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 편지지에는 온통 관 무늬가 박혀 있겠지요. 그러니까 편지를 쓰기라도 하신다면요. 어쩌면 언젠가 버나드에게는 쓰실지도 모르죠. 자기가 성에 묵고 있다는 걸 보여 주려고요.”\n\n“네가 그이한테서 편지를 받을 자격이 있다고는 못 하겠구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“저도 조금도 기대 안 해요. 런던에 돌아가서 사무실에 달리 할 일이 없다는 걸 알게 되기 전에는요. 하지만 당신하고 레이디 줄리아가 어떻게 지내실지는 정말 보고 싶어요. 그분이 당신을 도깨비처럼 여기는 게 아주 분명했죠. 안 그래, 언니?”\n\n“레이디 줄리아한테는 도깨비인 사람이 워낙 많으니까.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“나는 레이디 줄리아가 아주 좋은 분이라고 믿는다.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “그러니 그분 험담은 그만두렴.”\n\n“특히 그분의 귀염둥이 조카인 가엾은 버나드 앞에서는 더 그렇죠.” 릴리가 말했다. “코시 성에서 일주일만 같이 지내면 어돌퍼스도 귀염둥이가 될걸요. 어디 버나드를 밀어내 보세요.”\n\n이 모든 것으로부터 데일 부인은 릴리의 마음을 무겁게 짓누르던 어떤 근심이, 완전히 사라지지는 않았더라도 한결 가벼워졌음을 알았다. 그녀는 딸에게 아무것도 묻지 않았지만, 지난 며칠 동안 릴리가 괴로워하고 있음을 알아차렸고, 그 괴로움이 약혼에서 비롯되었다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그녀는 아무것도 묻지 않았으나, 물론 크로즈비 씨의 수입이 얼마인지는 들어서 알고 있었고, 그것이 혼인 생활의 온갖 필요를 넉넉히 채울 만한 액수로 여겨지지는 않는다는 것도 알게 되어 있었다. 릴리의 근심이 어디서 왔는지 짐작하기란 어렵지 않았고, 이제 두 사람 사이에 무슨 이야기가 오가서 그 근심이 덜어졌음을 알아채기도 어렵지 않았다.\n\n그러고 나서 다들 말을 타러 나갔고, 오후는 즐겁게 흘러갔다. 정말 마지막 날이었지만 릴리는 슬퍼하지 않기로 마음먹고 있었다. 이제 가셔도 된다고, 가셔도 서운해하지 않겠다고 그녀는 그에게 말해 두었다. 내일이 자기에게 몹시 텅 빈 날이 되리라는 것을 알았지만, 그녀는 자기 약속의 정신에 걸맞게 살아 보려고 애썼고 또 해냈다. 그들은 모두 큰 집에서 저녁을 먹었는데, 이번만은 데일 부인까지 그리했다. 저녁에 정원에서 들어왔을 때 크로즈비는 릴리에게보다 데일 부인에게 더 많은 말을 했고, 릴리는 조금 떨어져 앉아 온 귀를 기울이며 이따금 낮은 목소리로 한마디씩 거들었는데, 어머니와 연인이 서로를 알아 가는 것이 말할 수 없이 행복했다. 그리고 밝혀 두어야 할 것은, 이때 크로즈비가 그토록 골머리를 앓던 어려움들을 이겨 내고 결혼할 수 있는 가장 이른 날을 정하기로 온전히 마음먹고 있었다는 점이다. 들판에서 있었던 그 만남의 엄숙함이 아직 그에게 남아 있어서, 그의 지금 감정에 대체로 그에게는 모자랐던 사내다움과 진실한 목적을 부여하고 있었다. 그 감정이 오래가기만 한다면! 그러나 지금 그는 데일 부인에게 그 딸에 관해, 그리고 두 사람의 앞날에 관해, 한동안이나마 그의 마음이 그녀에게 참되지 않았더라면 쓸 수 없었을 어조로 이야기했다. 그는 릴리의 어머니에게 이토록 마음을 터놓고 말한 적이 없었고, 데일 부인 역시 어느 때보다 그에게 어머니의 사랑 같은 것을 느꼈다. 그는 얼마간 늦어질 수밖에 없는 사정을 사과하며, 자기 젊은 아내가 제 집의 안락함도 없이 지내는 것을 차마 볼 수 없고, 자기는 지금도 늘 그래 왔듯 빚지는 것이 두렵다고 말했다. 데일 부인은 기다리는 약혼을 싫어했다. 모든 어머니가 그렇듯이. 그러나 이런 간청에 매정하게 대답할 수는 없었다.\n\n“릴리가 아직 아주 어리니,” 그녀가 말했다. “일 년쯤은 기다려도 괜찮을 거예요.”\n\n“칠 년이라도요.” 릴리가 벌떡 일어나 어머니의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. “그때도 스물여섯이 채 안 될 텐데, 조금도 많은 나이가 아니잖아요.”\n\n그리하여 저녁은 아주 즐겁게 지나갔다.\n\n“하느님의 축복이 함께하기를, 어돌퍼스!” 데일 부인이 자기 집 문간에서 그와 헤어지며 말했다. 그를 세례명으로 부른 것은 그때가 처음이었다. “우리가 자네를 얼마나 믿고 맡기는지 알아주면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“압니다. 잘 압니다.” 그가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다. 그러고서 혼자 걸어 돌아가면서 그는 그 두 여자에게, 딸에게도 어머니에게도 참되겠노라고 온 마음을 다해 스스로에게 맹세하며 다짐했다. 그날 아침의 엄숙함이 아직 그에게 남아 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그는 이튿날 아침 여덟 시 전에 떠나기로 되어 있었고, 버나드가 게스트윅 철도역까지 그를 태워다 주기로 되어 있었다. 아침상은 일곱 시가 조금 지나 차려졌다. 그리고 두 남자가 막 내려왔을 때 릴리가 모자를 쓰고 숄을 두른 채 방으로 들어왔다. “차를 따라 드리러 오겠다고 했잖아요.” 그녀가 말하고는 찻주전자 맞은편에 자리를 잡고 앉았다.\n\n말수가 적은 식사였다. 그런 마지막 순간에는 사람들이 무슨 말을 해야 할지 모르는 법이니까. 게다가 버나드도 거기 있었으니, 둘이면 동무가 되어도 셋이면 그렇지 못하다는 옛말이 얼마나 옳은지 증명해 보이는 셈이었다. 내 생각에 릴리가 그 마지막 아침에 올라온 것은 잘못이었다. 그러나 연인이 그렇게까지 수고하지 말라고 청했는데도 그녀는 그가 떠나는 것을 보지 않고 보낸다는 말을 들으려 하지 않았다. 수고라니! 그의 모자 꼭대기의 마지막 모습이라도 보기 위해서라면 그녀가 밤을 꼬박 새우지 않았겠는가?\n\n그러다 버나드가 말에 관해 무어라 중얼거리며 자리를 떴다. “당신한테 말할 시간이 딱 일 분밖에 없네요.” 그녀가 벌떡 일어나며 말했다. “그리고 무슨 말을 해야 할지 밤새 생각했고요. 생각하기는 이렇게 쉬운데 말하기는 이렇게 어렵네요.”\n\n“내 사랑, 다 알고 있소.”\n\n“하지만 이건 알아주셔야 해요. 저는 절대 당신을 의심하지 않을 거예요. 다시는 저를 놓아 달라고 하지도, 당신 없이도 행복할 수 있다고 말하지도 않을 거예요. 저는 당신 없이는 살 수 없어요. 그러니까 당신이 제 사람이라는 걸 알지 못하고는요. 하지만 저는 절대로 조바심 내지 않을 거예요. 절대로요. 제발, 제발 믿어 주세요! 무슨 일이 있어도 저는 당신을 의심하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“가장 사랑하는 릴리, 그럴 일이 없도록 애쓰겠소.”\n\n“안 그러실 줄 알아요. 하지만 그 말을 꼭 하고 싶었어요. 그리고 편지 쓰시겠죠? 아주 곧요?”\n\n“도착하는 즉시 쓰겠소.”\n\n“그리고 되도록 자주요. 하지만 성가시게 굴진 않을게요. 다만 당신 편지가 저를 정말 행복하게 해 줄 거예요. 편지가 오면 얼마나 뿌듯할까요. 저는 당신을 지치게 할까 봐 너무 많이 쓰기가 겁날 거예요.”\n\n“그럴 일은 없을 거요.”\n\n“안 그럴까요? 하지만 아시다시피 먼저 쓰셔야 해요. 제가 당신 편지로 어떻게 살아갈지 조금이라도 아신다면! 자, 이제 안녕히 가세요. 마차 바퀴 소리가 나요. 하느님의 축복이 함께하기를, 내 사람, 내 사람!” 그러고서 그녀는 자기 마음을 그의 마음에 내맡겼듯이 자기 몸을 그의 두 팔에 내맡겼다.\n\n두 남자가 이륜마차에 오르는 동안 그녀는 문간에 서 있었고, 마차가 문을 지나 내려가자 서둘러 테라스로 나갔다. 거기서라면 마차가 오솔길을 얼마쯤 내려가는 모습을 볼 수 있었다. 그러고는 테라스에서 문으로 달려가, 문을 서둘러 빠져나가 교회 묘지로 들어갔다. 그 맨 끝 모퉁이에서라면 두 남자의 머리가 목사관 너머 큰길로 접어들 때까지 보였다. 그녀는 마차 바퀴 소리마저 더는 귀에 닿지 않을 때까지 거기 서서, 그들이 간 방향으로 눈길을 뻗고 있었다. 그러고서 천천히 몸을 돌려 교회 묘지 문으로 빠져나갔는데, 그 문은 작은 집 정문 바로 옆 길로 나 있었다.\n\n“저 사람 머리통을 한 대 쥐어박아 주고 싶습니다요.” 정원사 홉킨스가 이륜마차가 몰려 가는 것을, 그리고 릴리가 그 안에 실려 가는 사람의 마지막 모습이라도 보려고 종종걸음으로 뒤따르는 것을 보며 혼잣말을 했다. “그러고도 눈 하나 깜짝 안 하지요. 암, 안 하고말고요.” 홉킨스는 혼잣말로 이렇게 덧붙였다. 이 근방에서는 릴리 아가씨가 홉킨스의 귀염둥이라는 말이 대체로 돌았다. 다만 그는 주로 언니보다 그녀에게 더 자주 퉁을 놓는 것으로 그 마음을 드러내곤 했다.\n\n릴리는 분명히 정문으로 집에 돌아갈 작정이었으나, 집에 이르기 전에 마음을 바꾸어 교회 묘지를 지나, 큰 집 문을 지나, 그 뒤편 정원을 지나 천천히 되돌아가서 마침내 작은 다리를 건넜다. 그러나 다리 위에서 그녀는 그와 함께 자주 그랬듯 난간에 기대어 잠시 쉬며, 그를 처음 만난 그 칠월의 어느 날 이후로 있었던 모든 일을 생각했다. 그가 그녀에게 사랑을 고백한 곳 가운데 이곳만큼 자주 고백한 곳은 없었고, 그녀가 그의 충실하고 사랑하는 아내가 되겠노라고 그토록 간절히 맹세한 곳도 여기 말고는 없었다.\n\n“그리고 하느님이 도우시는 한 그렇게 하겠어.” 그녀는 집으로 씩씩하게 걸어 올라가며 스스로에게 말했다. “그이는 갔어요, 엄마.” 아침 식사 방으로 들어서며 그녀가 말했다. “이제 우리도 다시 일하는 나날로 돌아가요. 지난 여섯 주 동안 저한테는 온통 일요일이었으니까요.”\n\n# 제16장. 크로즈비 씨가 코시 성으로 가는 길에 늙은 목사를 만나다\n\n여정의 첫 한두 마일 동안 크로즈비와 버나드 데일은 이륜마차에 앉아 거의 내내 말이 없었다. 릴리가 교회 묘지 모퉁이까지 달려 내려가 거기 서서 사랑에 겨운 눈으로 두 사람의 뒷모습을 지켜보고 있었다는 것을 그들은 알지 못했다. 그러나 릴리의 그 지극한 마음이 뿜어낸 기운은 아직도 두 사람 위에 강하게 남아 있었고, 그래서 그들은 대뜸 여느 이야깃거리로 화제를 돌리는 것이 좋지 않으리라고 느꼈다. 게다가 우리는 이렇게 짐작해도 좋을 것이다. 크로즈비는 릴리 데일 같은 아가씨와, 지난 여섯 주 동안 살뜰히 어울려 지냈고 온 마음을 다해 사랑한 그 아가씨와 이렇게 헤어지는 것이 실로 견디기 어려웠다고 — 그런 데 쓸 마음이 그에게 있는 만큼은 그 전부를 다해서 사랑한 아가씨였다. 그를 괴롭히던 결혼에 대한 그 의심들 속에서도 그는 릴리를 못마땅해하는 뜻을 스스로에게 한 번도 내비친 적이 없었다. 릴리와 갈라설 구실을 제게 마련해 주려고, 그 아가씨가 자기가 바라는 바와는 다른 사람이라고 여기도록 제 마음을 길들이지도 않았다. 적어도 아직까지는, 제 발로 묶이기를 허락해 놓고서 그 굴레에서 벗어나고 싶어 하는 사내들이 그토록 흔히 쓰는 그 수법에 기대지 않은 것이다. 릴리는 그의 눈에도, 그의 손길에도, 그의 모든 감각에도 그러기에는 너무나 사랑스러웠다. 그는 릴리와 함께 있는 즐거움을, 그리고 사랑한다고 말해 주는 릴리의 목소리를 듣는 즐거움을 너무나 절실히 누렸으므로, 그 사람 자체에 싫증이 날 수는 없었다. 그는 클럽 생활에 찌들 대로 찌들지는 않았던 터라, 릴리의 그 점잖은 시골 사람다운 몸가짐과 부드럽고 다정하고 여자다운 익살에서 더없는 기쁨을 맛보았다. 그는 결코 릴리에게 싫증이 나지 않았다. 런던의 그 어떤 즐거움보다도, 푸른 들판에서 릴리 데일에게 사랑을 속삭이는 이 즐거움이 나았다. 그를 겁나게 한 것은 그 뒤에 따라올 결과였다. 아기들이 생기고 아기에게 딸린 온갖 것들이 따라올 터였다. 시들한 난롯불을 앞에 둔 시들한 저녁나절도, 아니면 실망한 여자의 애태우는 슬픔도 따라올 터였다. 옷차림에도 조심하지 않을 수 없게 될 것이었으니, 새 외투 한 벌을 맞추는 일이 만만찮은 지출을 뜻하게 될 터이기 때문이었다. 백작부인들과 그 딸들 틈에 드나드는 일도 더는 못 할 것이었으니, 제 아내가 그런 집에 인사를 다닌다는 것은 애당초 될 법하지도 않은 일일 터이기 때문이었다. 그가 지금까지 거둔 승리란 승리는 죄다 내놓아야 했다. 이륜마차가 목사관 곁의 모퉁이를 돌아 나가는 동안, 그러니까 릴리의 눈이 아직도 멀어져 가는 그의 등을 조금이나마 볼 수 있는 복을 누리고 있는 동안에도 그는 이런 것들을 생각하고 있었다. 그러나 그는 바로 그 순간 이런 생각도 하고 있었으니, 자기를 기다리는 또 다른 승리가 있을지도 모른다는 것, 아기들이 거기 있고 아기 시중에 여념이 없는 여자가 맞은편에 앉아 있다 한들 그 난롯가를 좋아하는 법을 배울 수도 있으리라는 것이었다. 그는 제가 아는 한 최선을 다해 싸우고 있었다. 릴리가 그에게 나누어 준 그 엄숙한 기운이 아직 그의 마음에서 사라지지 않은 까닭이었다.\n\n“그래도 대체로는, 이번 방문이 마음에 들었겠지?” 마침내 버나드가 그에게 말했다.\n\n“마음에 들었느냐고? 물론이지.”\n\n“그야 말이야 쉽지. 나한테 인사치레를 하자면 그쯤은 해야 할 테고. 하지만 자네가 어느 정도는 실망했다는 걸 나는 알고 있네.”\n\n“음, 그래. 돈 문제에서는 실망했지. 아니라고 우겨 봐야 소용없는 일이고.”\n\n“지금 그 얘기를 꺼낼 생각은 없었네만, 자네가 나한테는 허물을 돌리지 않는다는 걸 알고 싶어서 그러네.”\n\n“나는 자네를 탓한 적이 없어. 자네도, 다른 누구도. 굳이 탓한 사람이 있다면 나 자신뿐이지.”\n\n“자네가 한 일을 뉘우친다는 뜻인가?”\n\n“아니, 그런 뜻이 아니야. 우리가 방금 두고 온 그 어여쁜 아가씨에게 나는 너무나 마음을 쏟고 있어서, 그 사람과 약혼한 걸 뉘우친다는 마음은 조금도 없네. 다만 자네 삼촌을 내가 좀 더 잘 다루었더라면 사정이 달라졌을지도 모른다는 생각은 드는군.”\n\n“나는 그렇게 생각하지 않네. 아니, 그렇지 않다는 걸 나는 알고 있고, 그 일로 자네가 마음 상해할 것 없다고 장담할 수 있네. 자네도 잘 알다시피 나는 삼촌이 릴리를 위해서도 무엇인가 해 주시리라고 생각했었지. 벨을 위해 늘 하려고 마음먹어 온 것만큼은 아니더라도 무엇인가는 말일세. 하지만 이것만은 믿어도 좋아. 삼촌은 당신이 무엇을 할지 이미 마음을 정해 두고 계셨다는 것. 자네나 내가 무슨 말을 했더라도 그분 마음을 바꾸지는 못했을 걸세.”\n\n“음, 그 얘기는 이제 그만하세.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n그러고 나서 두 사람은 다시 말없이 길을 갔고, 기차 시각에 넉넉히 대어 게스트윅에 닿았다.\n\n“런던에 닿는 대로 소식 주게.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“아무렴, 그러지. 그 전에도 편지하겠네.”\n\n그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다. 데일이 돌아서서 가 버릴 때 크로즈비는 전보다 그가 덜 좋아졌다고 느꼈다. 그리고 버나드도 마차를 몰아 그를 데려다 주는 동안, 크로즈비가 우연히 사귄 벗으로서는 좋은 사람이었으나 처남매부 사이로는 그만큼 좋은 사람이 못 되리라는 결론에 이르렀다. “저 친구는 어떤 식으로든 우리에게 골칫거리를 안길 거야. 내가 저 친구를 데려온 것이 유감이군.” 그것이 이 일에 대한 데일의 속마음이었다.\n\n게스트윅을 떠난 크로즈비의 길은 철도를 타고 이웃 주에 있는 대성당 도시 바체스터로 이어졌고, 그는 거기서 마차를 얻어 타고 코시로 건너갈 작정이었다. 실은 그렇게 이른 시각에 떠날 까닭이라고는 없었다. 시골 저택을 찾아가는 손님은 누구나 만찬 시각에서 그리 앞서지 않은 때에 닿아야 한다는 것을 그도 알고 있었으니 말이다. 그가 그토록 서둘러 길에 나서기로 마음먹은 것은 그 마지막 몇 시간을 얼른 넘겨 버리는 편이 낫겠다는 느낌 때문이었다. 그리하여 그는 열한 시에 바체스터에 이르러 손에 아무 할 일도 없는 처지가 되었고, 달리 할 일이 없으므로 교회에 갔다. 대성당에서는 온전한 예배가 올려지고 있었고, 성당지기가 그를 이끌어 빈 성가대석 자리 하나에 앉히는 참에 자그마하고 여윈 노인 하나가 연도(連禱)를 선창하기 시작했다. “여기까지 걸려들 생각은 아니었는데.” 크로즈비는 방석 위에 팔을 올려놓고 자리를 잡으며 속으로 중얼거렸다. 그러나 그 노인의 목소리가 지닌 남다른 매력이 곧 그를 사로잡았다. 떨리면서도 힘이 있는 목소리였다. 그러자 그는 그날의 특별 예배를 그토록 길게 만든 그 성인을 원망하는 마음을 거두었다.\n\n“그런데 연도를 선창하신 저 노신사분은 뉘시오?” 뒤에 성당지기가 그를 이끌고 대성당의 기념비들을 구경시켜 주는 동안 그가 물었다.\n\n“저희 선창자 어른이시지요, 나리. 하딩 씨라고 합니다. 하딩 씨 말씀은 들어 보셨을 텐데요.” 그러나 크로즈비는 정중히 양해를 구하며 모른다고 털어놓았다.\n\n“그야, 나리, 저분도 꽤나 이름이 알려진 분입니다. 저리 수줍음을 타시기는 해도요. 저희 수석 사제님의 장인이시고, 그랜틀리 부주교님의 장인이시기도 합니다.”\n\n“그럼 따님들이 죄다 성직 쪽으로 시집을 갔다는 말이오?”\n\n“그야 그렇습니다. 하지만 엘리너 아가씨는 — 저는 그분이 시집가시기 훨씬 전부터 기억하고 있습니다, 다들 요양원에 사실 적에 말이지요 —”\n\n“요양원이라니?”\n\n“히램 요양원 말입니다, 나리. 그분이 거기 워든이셨지요. 나리, 여태 가 보신 적이 없으시면 그 요양원을 한번 들러 보셔야 합니다. 그래, 엘리너 아가씨는, 그분이 막내따님이신데, 처음에는 볼드 씨에게 시집을 가셨지요. 그렇지만 지금은 수석 사제님 부인이십니다.”\n\n“아, 수석 사제 부인이란 말이오?”\n\n“그렇고말고요. 그런데 나리, 이건 또 어떻게 생각하십니까? 하딩 씨께서는 마음만 잡수셨으면 당신이 수석 사제가 되실 수도 있었습니다. 그쪽에서 그 자리를 저분께 내놓았거든요.”\n\n“그런데 그것을 마다했다고?”\n\n“정말로 마다하셨습니다, 나리.”\n\n“놀로 데카나리라. 그런 말은 여태 들어 본 적이 없군. 무엇이 그분을 그토록 겸손하게 만들었소?”\n\n“바로 그것입지요, 나리. 겸손한 분이시니까요. 이제 연세가 일흔을 넘기셨습니다, 그것도 한참을요. 그런데도 어린 처녀만큼이나 겸손하십니다. 요새 처녀들 가운데 몇몇보다야 훨씬 더 겸손하시고요. 저분과 그 손녀분이 나란히 계신 걸 한번 보시면 압니다!”\n\n“그럼 그 손녀란 사람은 누구요?”\n\n“아, 레이디 덤벨로시지요. 앞으로 하틀탑 후작부인이 되실 분 말입니다.”\n\n“레이디 덤벨로라면 나도 아는 사람이오.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그렇다고 성당지기에게 제 고귀한 교분을 뽐낼 뜻이 있었던 것은 아니다.\n\n“아, 그러십니까, 나리?” 그 사내는 낯선 손님에게서 드러난 이 대단함의 표시에 저도 모르게 모자에 손을 가져다 대며 말했다. 실은 그 자신도 그 마님을 조금도 좋아하지 않았다. “아마 코시 성의 손님들 축에 끼시려나 봅니다.”\n\n“음, 그런 셈이오.”\n\n“가 보시면 그 마님이 나리보다 먼저 와 계실 겁니다. 어제 지나가시는 길에 수석사제관에서 이모님과 점심을 드셨지요. 플럼스테드의 친정아버님 댁까지 나가시기는 너무 번거롭다고 하시면서요. 그 아버님이 부주교님이시랍니다. 사람들 말로는 — 아 참, 그 마님이 나리와 아시는 사이라고 하셨지요!”\n\n“아는 사이라고 할 것도 없소. 그저 얼굴이나 겨우 아는 정도지. 그 사람은 제 아버지보다 높은 만큼이나 나보다도 높은 데 있는 사람이오.”\n\n“아무렴요, 그 마님은 죄다 위에 계시지요. 저 노신사분께는 말 한마디 건네시는 법도 거의 없다고들 합니다.”\n\n“뭐라고, 제 아버지한테?”\n\n“아니지요, 하딩 씨 말입니다. 방금 연도를 선창하신 그분요. 저기 계시네요, 나리. 수석사제관에서 나오십니다.”\n\n두 사람은 그때 익랑 가운데 한 곳에서 밖으로 통하는 문께에 서 있었는데, 그렇게 이야기를 주고받는 사이에 하딩 씨가 그 곁을 지나갔다. 그는 자그맣고 여위고 발을 끄는 노인으로, 어깨가 굽었고, 반바지에 긴 검정 각반을 차고 있었으며, 그 각반은 가엾은 노인의 다리께에 제법 헐렁하게 늘어져 있었다. 그는 걸어가면서 두 손을 맞잡아 연신 비벼 댔다. 그러면서도 걸음은 빨랐다. 비틀거리는 걸음은 아니었으나 어딘가 미덥지 못하고 머뭇거리는 걸음이었다. 하딩 씨가 지나갈 때 성당지기는 손을 머리에 가져다 댔고, 크로즈비도 모자를 들어 보였다. 그러자 하딩 씨도 제 모자를 들어 인사하고는, 무슨 말이라도 하려는 듯 몸을 돌렸다. 크로즈비는 사람의 다정함이 이보다 더 또렷하게 새겨진 얼굴을 여태 본 적이 없다고 느꼈다. 그러나 노인은 말을 하지 않았다. 그는 몸을 반쯤 돌렸다가, 제 마음이 부끄럽기라도 한 듯 발을 끌며 도로 돌아서서 그대로 지나가 버렸다.\n\n“천사를 만든다면 저런 분으로 만들 겁니다.” 성당지기가 말했다. “하지만 죄다 저분만큼 착한 사람으로 만들려 든다면 많이는 못 만들 테지요. 대단히 고맙습니다, 나리.” 그러고는 크로즈비가 건넨 반 크라운을 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n“그러니까 저이가 레이디 덤벨로의 할아버지란 말이지.” 크로즈비는 성당지기가 일러 준 길을 따라 요양원 쪽으로 대성당 구내를 천천히 돌아 걸으며 혼잣말을 했다. 그는 레이디 덤벨로를 조금도 좋아하지 않았으니, 그 여자가 감히 그를, 다른 사람도 아닌 그를 쌀쌀맞게 대한 일이 있었던 까닭이다. “저 노신사야 천사로 만들 수 있을지 모르지.” 그는 이어서 중얼거렸다. “하지만 그 손녀를 데리고는 결코 그렇게 되지 않을걸.”\n\n그는 작은 다리를 건너 어슬렁어슬렁 천천히 걸어갔다. 그리고 요양원 문간에서 다시 하딩 씨와 마주쳤다. “안을 좀 들여다볼까 하고 실례를 무릅쓸 참이었습니다.” 그가 말했다. “하지만 폐가 되려는지요?”\n\n“아니요, 아니요. 조금도 그렇지 않습니다.” 하딩 씨가 말했다. “어서 들어오세요. 내가 여기 주인이라고야 말할 수 없지요. 나는 여기 살지 않습니다. 지금은요. 그렇지만 이곳 사정은 훤히 알고 있으니 손님을 맞아들일 수는 있어요. 저것이 워든의 사택입니다. 그 댁 부인이 딸린 식구가 아주 많으니, 이렇게 이른 시각에 들어가지는 않는 편이 좋겠군요. 참으로 훌륭한 부인이고 나와는 각별한 벗이에요. 그 남편도 그렇고요.”\n\n“그러면 그 남편분이 워든이라는 말씀이지요?”\n\n“그렇습니다, 요양원의 워든이지요. 저 집이 보이시지요. 참 예쁘지 않습니까? 아주 예쁘지요. 내 생각으로는 여태 본 집 가운데 가장 예쁘게 지은 집입니다.”\n\n“거기까지야 말씀드리기 어렵겠습니다만.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“나처럼 저기서 열두 해를 살아 보셨다면 그런 말이 나올 겁니다. 나는 저 집에서 열두 해를 살았는데, 이 땅 위에 저만큼 다정한 자리가 또 있을까 싶어요. 저런 잔디를 어디서 보신 적이 있습니까?”\n\n“참으로 훌륭하군요.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그러면서 그는 작은 집에 있는 데일 부인의 잔디와 견주어 보기 시작했고, 앨링턴의 잔디가 이 요양원 잔디보다 낫다고 마음속으로 판정을 내렸다.\n\n“저 잔디는 내가 손수 깔았지요. 처음 왔을 적에는 저기에 화단이 있었습니다. 접시꽃이며 그런 것들이 심겨 있었지요. 잔디를 깐 것이 나아진 것이었어요.”\n\n“그야 두말할 것도 없겠지요.”\n\n“잔디를 깐 것은 분명 나아진 것이었지요. 그리고 저 관목들도 내가 심었습니다. 저만한 포르투갈월계수는 이 주 안에 또 없어요.”\n\n“선생께서 여기 워든이셨습니까?” 그렇게 물으면서 크로즈비는 아주 어릴 적에 바체스터의 히램 요양원을 두고 신문에서 벌어졌던 어떤 다툼 이야기를 들은 적이 있음을 떠올렸다.\n\n“그렇습니다. 나는 열두 해 동안 여기 워든이었지요. 저런, 저런, 저런! 만일 나와 사이가 좋지 않은 사람을 여기에 앉혀 놓았더라면 나는 몹시 불행했을 겁니다. 아주 몹시요. 그러나 이렇게 되었으니 나는 마음 내키는 대로 드나듭니다. 그들이 나를 — 그렇게 하기 전이나 거의 다름없이 말이지요. 아니, 그들이 나를 내쫓은 것은 아닙니다. 내가 물러나는 편이 가장 낫도록 만든 사정이 있었던 것이지요.”\n\n“그러면 지금은 수석사제관에서 지내시는군요, 하딩 씨?”\n\n“그렇습니다. 나는 지금 수석사제관에서 삽니다. 그렇다고 내가 수석 사제인 것은 아니에요. 내 사위인 애러빈 박사가 수석 사제이지요. 딸 하나는 이 근방으로 시집을 갔으니, 내 몫의 줄이 아름다운 곳에 떨어졌다고 참으로 말할 수 있겠습니다.”\n\n그러고 나서 그는 크로즈비를 구호 노인들 틈으로 데리고 들어가 그들의 방을 하나도 빼놓지 않고 돌았다. 그곳은 이 도시의 나이 든 남자들을 위한 구빈원이었고, 크로즈비가 그를 떠나기 전에 하딩 씨는 이 요양원의 사정이며 자기가 그곳을 떠나게 된 경위를 죄다 이야기해 주었다. “나는 떠나기가 싫었습니다. 가슴이 미어질 줄만 알았지요. 그렇지만 그런 말들이 오가는 마당에 남아 있을 수는 없었어요. 도저히 남아 있을 수 없었습니다. 게다가 남아 있었더라면 그것이 잘못이었을 겁니다. 이제는 다 알겠어요. 그러나 딸의 팔에 기대어 저 아치 밑을 걸어 나오던 그때에는, 크로즈비 씨, 나는 정말 가슴이 미어지는 줄만 알았습니다.” 그리고 그렇게 말하는 노인의 뺨으로는 지금도 눈물이 흘러내렸다.\n\n긴 이야기였으니, 여기서 되풀이할 것까지는 없다. 또 그 이야기를 크로즈비 씨에게 들려주어야 할 까닭이라고는 없었다. 굳이 있다면 이것뿐이니, 하딩 씨가 정 많고 말 많은 노인이어서 지난날을 되새기는 데 마음을 맡기기를 좋아했다는 것이다. 그러나 크로즈비는 이 점을 눈여겨보았다. 그 이야기를 하는 동안 하딩 씨의 입에서는 누구를 헐뜯는 말이 한마디도 나오지 않았다는 것을. 그러면서도 그는 해를 입은 사람이었다. 그것도 아주 깊이 해를 입은 사람이었다. “다 잘된 일이었지요.” 마침내 그가 말했다. “더구나 옛 자리에서 내 집처럼 지내는 낙까지 내게서 앗아 가지는 않았으니 말입니다. 저 집 안으로 모시고 들어가고 싶습니다만, 참으로 아늑한 집이지요, 아주요. 다만 식구가 많은 집에서는 이른 시각이 늘 편한 것은 아니라서요.” 그 말을 들으며 크로즈비는 다시금 제 앞날의 살림과 빠듯한 수입을 생각하게 되었다.\n\n그는 이 노목사에게 자기가 누구인지, 또 코시로 가는 길이라는 것을 이미 말해 둔 터였다. “거기서 선생의 손녀 되시는 분을 뵙게 되리라고 들었습니다.”\n\n“그렇지요, 그렇습니다. 그 애는 내 손녀입니다. 이제는 그 애와 내가 서로 다른 길을 걷게 되어 자주 보지는 못해요. 하느님께서 그 애를 불러 세우신 그 자리에서 제 본분을 잘 지키고 있다고들 하더군요.”\n\n“그거야,” 크로즈비는 속으로 생각했다. “자작부인의 본분이라는 것이 무엇이라고들 여기느냐에 달린 문제겠지.” 그러나 그는 레이디 덤벨로에 대해 더는 아무 말도 하지 않은 채 새로 사귄 벗에게 작별을 고했고, 저녁 여섯 시쯤에 마차를 몰아 코시 성의 현관 지붕 밑으로 들어섰다.\n\n# 제17장. 코시 성\n\n코시 성은 손님으로 가득 차 있었다. 우선 코시 집안사람들이 죄다 그곳에 모여 있었다. 백작이 와 있었고—물론 백작부인도 있었다. 해마다 이맘때면 드 코시 백작부인은 늘 집에 있었다. 그러나 백작 본인이 집에 있는 일은 지금까지 결코 그만큼 확실하지 않았다. 그는 왕실을 드나들며 시종 노릇을 하는 일에, 하일랜드의 모임에, 그리고—틀림없이 꼭 필요했을—런던 사교철의 연장에, 또 독일의 어느 온천장 체류에 몹시 마음을 쏟아 온 사람이었다. 그 온천장 체류란 아마도 독일 궁정의 예법과 의식을 연구하기에 편리했을 것이다. 그 밖에도 그는 제 인생의 특별한 목적을 바싹 좇느라 이런저런 일로 집을 비웠다. 드 코시 백작은 대단한 궁정인이었던 것이다. 그러나 근래에는 통풍과 요통이, 그리고 아마도 남들에게 두루 호감을 사는 능력이 얼마간 줄어든 것이 그를 집안의 의무와 화해시켜, 백작은 시간의 많은 부분을 집에서 보내게 되었다. 예전에 백작부인은 남편이 자주 집을 비운다고 불평하곤 했다. 그러나 어떤 여자들은 좀처럼 만족시키기 어려운 법이어서—이제 그녀는 남편이 집에 있는 것에도 늘 만족하지는 않았다.\n\n그리고 아들딸도 모두 와 있었다—다만 맏이인 폴록 경만은 빠졌으니, 그는 결코 아버지와 얼굴을 마주하지 않았다. 백작과 폴록 경은 사이가 틀어져 있었고, 실로 그런 아버지와 그런 아들만이 서로를 미워할 수 있는 방식으로 서로를 미워했다. 아너러블 조지 드 코시는 신부를 데리고 와 있었는데, 그는 얼마 전 돈 있는 젊은 여자와 결혼함으로써 누가 보아도 분명한 의무를 하나 이행한 참이었다. 아주 젊다고는 할 수 없었으니—서른을 몇 해 넘긴 나이였다. 그러나 아너러블 조지 역시 아주 젊지는 않았으므로, 그 점에서 두 사람은 어울리지 않는 짝이 아니었다. 여자의 돈은 그리 많지 않았다—아마 삼만 파운드쯤 되었을 것이다. 그러나 아너러블 조지의 돈은 그야말로 한 푼도 없었다. 이제 그에게는 그럭저럭 살아갈 만한 수입이 생겼고, 그리하여 아버지와 어머니는 그의 모든 죄를 용서하고 그를 다시 품에 받아들였다. 게다가 이 결혼은 대단히 중요한 일이었으니, 이 집안의 맏이가 아직 아내를 맞지 않은 터라 드 코시 집안은 상속자를 이 결합에서 찾아야 할지도 몰랐던 것이다. 여자 자신은 아름답지도, 영리하지도, 위엄 있는 태도를 갖추지도 않았고—가문이 높지도 않았다. 그러나 못생기지도 않았고, 견딜 수 없을 만큼 어리석지도 않았다. 적어도 그 태도는 순박했다. 그리고 가문으로 말하자면—애초부터 그런 것이 있으리라고 아무도 생각하지 않았으므로—실망할 일도 없었다. 그 아버지는 석탄 상인이었다. 사람들은 늘 그녀를 조지 부인이라 불렀고, 집안 안팎의 모든 사람이 그녀를 두고 기울인 노력이란, 그녀를 여자의 형상, 곧 잘 차려입은 커다란 사람 모양의 물건, 어떤 필요 때문에 드 코시 집안이 행렬에 데리고 다녀야만 하는 물건처럼 대하는 것이었다. 아너러블 조지에 대해서는 이런 말을 더 해 둘 수 있겠다. 그는 평생 낭비꾼으로 살아왔으면서 이제는 지독한 구두쇠가 되었다는 것이다. 분별이 든다는 마흔 살에 이르러 그는 마침내 빈털터리 신세가 못마땅한 것임을 배웠고, 그리하여 실링과 펜스를 아낄 수 있는 곳이면 어디서든 실링과 펜스를 아끼는 데 온 정신을 쏟았다. 이 변화가 처음 그에게 찾아왔을 때에는 아버지도 어머니도 그것을 보고 기뻐했다. 그러나 아직 열두 달이 채 지나지 않았는데도 몇 가지 나쁜 결과가 나타나기 시작했다. 수입이 있으면서도 그는 제 집을 마련할 생각을 조금도 하지 않았다. 그는 도시에서든 시골에서든 아버지의 저택에 눌어붙어 아버지의 포도주를 마시고 아버지의 말을 탔으며, 심지어 아내의 옷까지 어머니의 양장점에서 얻어 내는 재주를 부렸다. 다만 이 마지막의 자잘한 성공을 이루는 과정에서는 집안에 약간의 불화가 드러나기도 했다.\n\n셋째 아들인 아너러블 존도 코시에 와 있었다. 그는 아직 아내를 맞지 않았고, 어느 특정한 인생의 길에서도 눈에 띄게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되어 본 적이 없었으므로 집안에서는 그를 짐으로 여기기 시작하던 참이었다. 아껴 둘 제 수입이 없었으니 형의 인색이라는 미덕을 본받지도 못했고, 사실대로 툭 터놓고 말하자면 아버지에게 어찌나 두루 성가신 존재가 되었던지 한두 번이 아니게 집에서 쫓아내겠다는 위협을 받은 터였다. 그러나 아들을 내쫓기란 쉬운 일이 아니다. 사람의 새끼는 어린 새처럼 둥지 밖으로 몰아낼 수가 없다. 아너러블 존 하나를 알거지로 만들어 세상에 내던져 보라, 그는 어떻게든 세상에 제 소문을 낼 것이다—달리 방법이 없으면 굶주려 가는 그 흉한 꼴로라도. 상류층의 철저한 건달은 체면과 벌이는 싸움에서 제 편에 뛰어난 이점을 여럿 지니고 있다. 그는 제 뜻을 거슬러 오스트레일리아로 보내질 수 없다. 온 세상이 알지 못하게 구빈원으로 보내질 수도 없다. 상인들의 가게에 못 들어가게 막을 수도 없거니와, 무시무시한 추문 없이는 아버지의 영지에서 떼어 놓을 수도 없다. 백작은 위협하고 으르렁대며 이를 드러냈다. 그는 성마른 사람이었고, 몹시 성난 얼굴을 지을 줄 아는 사람이었다. 눈이 거의 붉어질 듯했고 이마에는 세로로 주름이 잡혀, 때로는 보기에 아주 무시무시했다. 그러나 그는 일관성이 없는 사람이었고, 아너러블 존은 아버지를 재는 법을, 그것도 아주 정확한 저울로 재는 법을 익힌 터였다.\n\n내가 아들들을 먼저 말한 것은 그들이 손위였으리라고 짐작되기 때문이다. 어느 귀족 명감을 보아도 그들의 이름이 누이들의 이름보다 앞에 적혀 있으니 말이다. 그러나 딸은 넷이었다—레이디 어밀리아, 레이디 로지나, 레이디 마거리타, 레이디 알렉산드리나. 그들은 이를테면 이 집안의 꽃이었으니, 아버지와 오라비들 사이에 그토록 잦았던 집안싸움을 하나도 일으키지 않고 살아왔던 것이다. 그들은 신중하고 교양 있는 여자들이었으나, 아마도 세상에서의 제 자리를 조금 지나치게 의식했고, 제가 가진 이점과 갖지 못한 이점에 그릇된 값을 매기는 버릇이 다소 있었다. 레이디 어밀리아는 이미 결혼한 몸으로, 눈부시지는 않아도 실속 있는 혼처를 잡아 모티머 게이지비 씨와 맺어졌다. 게이지비 씨는 잘나가는 사무 변호사로, 여러 해 동안 드 코시 영지의 대리인 노릇을 해 온 회사에 속해 있었다. 모티머 게이지비는 이제 바체스터의 하원 의원이었는데, 그 자리는 얼마간 장인의 힘 덕이었다. 이렇게 된 것이 아너러블 조지에게는 몹시 아니꼬운 일이었으니, 그 의석은 마땅히 제 것이 되었어야 한다고 여겼기 때문이다. 그러나 게이지비 씨가 선거의 아주 무거운 비용을 제 주머니에서 치렀고 조지 드 코시는 도저히 그 돈을 치를 수 없었을 터이니, 그 요구가 정당한지는 의심해 볼 만하다. 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비는 이제 여러 아기의 행복한 어머니가 되어 코시 성을 찾을 때면 아이들을 데리고 다니는 것이 버릇이었고, 남편에게는 더없이 훌륭한 반려가 되어 있었다. 다만 그녀가 남편에게는 백작의 딸이라는 제 높은 신분을 그토록 자주 말하지 않고, 남들에게는 변호사의 아내라는 제 낮은 신분을 그토록 자주 말하지 않았더라면 남편은 아마 더 마음이 편했을 것이다. 그러나 대체로 두 사람은 잘 지냈고, 게이지비 씨는 결혼할 때 기대했던 만큼은 충분히 이 결혼에서 얻어 냈다.\n\n레이디 로지나는 신앙심이 매우 깊었다. 그리고 그 밖의 어떤 점에서 그녀가 두드러졌는지 나는 알지 못하니, 굳이 든다면 성미가 아버지를 얼마간 닮았다는 것쯤이겠다. 하인들이 무서워한 사람은 바로 레이디 로지나였는데, 특히 이른바 안식의 날이라는 그날과 관련해서 그러했다. 그녀의 지배 아래 그날은 하인들 여럿에게 쉴 틈 없는 고문의 날이 되어 있었다. 레이디 로지나가 늘 그러했던 것은 아니다. 그러나 이웃에 사는 어느 높은 성직자의 부인이 그녀의 눈을 뜨게 해 주었고, 그리하여 그녀는 거듭남을 겪었던 것이다. 기운차고 결혼하지 않았으며 몸 성한 여자가 그런 상태에 빠졌을 때—남편도 자식도 의무도 없어 제 일에서 마음을 돌릴 것이 아무것도 없는 여자가—얼마나 큰 괴로움을 끼칠 수 있는지, 내 독자들은 결코 알게 되지 않기를 나는 빈다.\n\n레이디 마거리타는 어머니가 가장 아끼는 딸이었고, 모든 점에서 어머니를 닮았다—다만 어머니는 미인이었다는 점만 달랐다. 세상은 그녀를 오만하고 도도하며 심지어 무례하다고 했으나, 그녀가 하는 모든 일이 실은 많은 자기 부정을 요구하는 어떤 원칙에 따른 것이라는 사실을 세상은 알지 못했다. 그녀는 언제 어느 때나 드 코시 집안사람이자 백작의 딸로 있는 것이 제 의무라 여겨 왔고, 그리하여 그 의무의 관념에 인기와 찬사를, 그리고 잘 차려입고 키가 크며 세련되고 결코 어리석지 않은 젊은 여자로서 마땅히 받았을 만한 온갖 흠모를 제물로 바쳤다. 신분에서 저보다 명백히 아래인 자들보다 어느 때나 무언가 더 높은 데 있는 것—그것이 그녀가 늘 기울이고 있던 노력이었다. 그러나 그녀는 좋은 딸이어서 집안에 어려움이 있을 때마다 힘닿는 대로 어머니를 도왔고, 저에게 주어진 차갑고 빛깔 없고 사랑스럽지 못한 삶을 두고 결코 투덜대지 않았다.\n\n알렉산드리나는 이 집안의 미인이었고, 사실 막내였다. 그러나 그녀조차도 그리 젊지 않았고, 그래서 이 딸마저 여름 볕을 제대로 쓰지 못한 채 건초 거둘 귀한 철을 흘려보내는 것이 아닌가 하여 벗들이 불안해하기 시작하던 참이었다. 그녀는 아마도 제 아름다움을 너무 믿었던 듯한데, 그것은 취향에 맞는 아름다움이라기보다 법칙에 맞는 아름다움이었다. 그리고 아마도 너무 넉넉한 수확을 바랐던 듯하다. 그 이마와 코와 뺨과 턱이 잘생겼다는 것은 어떤 남자도 부인할 수 없었다. 머리칼은 부드럽고 숱이 많았다. 이는 고르고 눈은 길쭉한 달걀형이었다. 그러나 그 얼굴의 흠은 이것이었다—헤어지고 나면 그 얼굴이 떠오르지 않는다는 것. 한 번 안면을 트고 나서 다시 만나도 알아보지 못할 수 있었다. 여러 번 만난 뒤에도 그 이목구비의 초상 하나를 마음에 담아 오지 못했다. 그러나 스무 살 때 어떠했든, 서른 살인 지금도 꼭 그러했다. 세월도 그 얼굴에서 반듯함을 앗아 가지 못했고, 너무 매끈한 그 이마를 흐트러뜨리지 못했다. 레이디 알렉산드리나가 한 번이 아니라 어쩌면 두 번도 넘게, 바쳐진 사랑에 답하여 이미 정혼의 언약을 했다는 소문이 돌았다. 그러나 소문이라는 것이 그런 화제를 다룰 때면 사실을 부풀리고 많은 것을 악의로 적어 넣는다는 것을 우리는 다들 안다. 이 아가씨는 한 번 약혼한 일이 있었고, 그 약혼은 두 해 동안 이어지다가 양가의 남자들 사이에 돈 문제가 얽히는 바람에 깨졌다. 그 뒤로 그녀는 다소 투덜대는 사람이 되었고, 제 건초 거두기라는 그 문제로 마음이 편치 않다는 말이 있었다. 거울과 하녀는 그녀의 볕이 여전히 예전만큼 밝게 비친다고 장담해 주었다. 그러나 그 마음은 기다림에 지쳐 가고 있었고, 언니 로지나처럼 모두에게 무서운 사람이 되거나 마거리타처럼 아무의 관심도 끌지 못하는 사람이 될까 두려웠다. 우리의 벗 크로즈비에게 그 전갈이 보내진 것은 특히 그녀에게서 비롯된 일이었다. 지난봄 런던에서 그녀와 크로즈비는 서로를 잘 알고 지냈던 것이다. 그렇다, 나의 점잖은 독자들이여. 그대들의 마음이 그대들에게 일러 주는 대로다. 그런 사정이라면 크로즈비 씨는 코시 성에 가지 말았어야 했다.\n\n드 코시 집안의 식구들은 이러했다. 그때 와 있던 손님들 가운데 많은 이를 여기 늘어놓을 필요는 없겠다. 무엇으로 보나 첫째가고 으뜸가는 이는 레이디 덤벨로였으니, 그 태생과 처지에 대해서는 앞 장에서 몇 마디 적어 두었다. 그녀는 아직 아주 젊은 부인으로, 결혼한 지 겨우 두 해가 조금 넘었을 뿐이었다. 그러나 그 두 해 동안 그녀의 승리는 여러 번이었으니—어찌나 여러 번이었던지 큰 세상에서 그녀의 지위는 이미 이름 높은 시어머니 하틀탑 후작부인과 맞먹었다. 후작부인으로 말하자면 스무 해 동안 유행의 왕국에서 저보다 더 큰 세력자를 두어 본 적이 없는 사람이었다. 그러나 레이디 덤벨로는 한 치도 모자람 없이 그만큼 대단했고, 남자들도 여자들도 머지않아 며느리 쪽이 더 대단해지리라고 말했다.\n\n“저 여자가 대체 어떻게 저걸 해내는지 내가 알 수 있다면 목을 매달아도 좋소.” 지난 사교철이 저물어 갈 무렵 세브라이트 문간에 서서 어느 귀족이 크로즈비에게 이렇게 말한 적이 있었다. “아무한테도 아무 말을 안 하잖소. 하룻밤 내내 열 마디도 입 밖에 안 낼 거요.”\n\n“저 부인 머릿속에 무슨 생각이 하나라도 들어 있는 것 같지는 않군요.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“저 여자는 아주 영리한 여자임에 틀림없다는 걸 내 말해 두겠소.” 귀족이 말을 이었다. “바보라면 저렇게 못 하지. 생각해 보시오, 저 여자는 한낱 목사의 딸일 뿐이오. 게다가 미모로 말하자면—”\n\n“저는 저 부인의 미모를 흠모하는 축이 아닙니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그 여자를 데리고 달아나고 싶다는 말은 아니오, 그런 뜻이라면.” 귀족이 말했다. “그래도 잘생긴 건 틀림없소. 덤벨로가 이걸 좋아하는지 모르겠군.”\n\n덤벨로는 좋아했다. 아내의 행렬에서 우두머리 하인 노릇으로 끌려다니는 것이 그의 야심을 채워 주었다. 세상이 제 아내와 함께 있으려고 다투는 까닭에 그는 스스로를 대단한 사람이라 믿었고, 제가 아내로 맞은 목사의 딸에게서 비쳐 오는 광채 덕분에 후작의 맏아들들 가운데서도 저는 남다르다고 여겼다. 이제 그는 코시 성에 끌려와 있었는데, 레이디 덤벨로가 이 한 주를 드 코시 백작부인에게 내주기까지 여간 뜸을 들이지 않았던 까닭에 제 처지가 자랑스러웠다.\n\n그리고 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트가 벌써 와 있었으니, 이웃 주에 사는 또 한 사람의 늙은 백작의 누이였다. 그녀는 겨우 하루 전에 도착했으면서도 크로즈비의 약혼 소식은 재빠르게 퍼뜨려 놓은 터였다. “데일 집안의 누구와 약혼했다지요?” 백작부인이 예쁘장하게 살짝 웃으며 말했는데, 그 웃음은 이 일이 저에게는 아무 관심거리도 아님을 훤히 드러냈다. “돈은 좀 있소?”\n\n“한 푼도 없을 거예요.” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다.\n\n“예쁘기는 한 모양이지요?” 백작부인이 넌지시 물었다.\n\n“네, 뭐, 예쁘기는 해요—착한 아가씨고요. 다만 그 어머니와 삼촌이 크로즈비 씨를 그렇게 부추긴 것이 아주 현명한 일이었는지는 모르겠어요. 그 사람이 내세울 만한 것이 특별히 있다는 이야기는 못 들었으니까요—돈으로 말하자면 그렇다는 겁니다.”\n\n“아마 흐지부지되고 말 거요.” 백작부인이 말했다. 그녀는 아가씨들이 약혼했다가 약속된 남편을 잃었다는 이야기를 듣기 좋아하는 사람이었다. 스스로는 그것을 좋아하는 줄 몰랐으나 실은 좋아했다. 그리고 벌써 가여운 릴리가 낭패를 볼 일을 미리 그려 보며 즐거워하고 있었다. 그러나 그러면서도 크로즈비에게는 화가 났으니, 그가 거짓 구실로 제 집에 발을 들이고 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다.\n\n그리고 알렉산드리나도 레이디 줄리아가 제가 듣는 데서 같은 소식을 되풀이하자 화가 났다. “저희는 정말 그 일에 별로 관심이 없어요, 레이디 줄리아.” 그녀가 고개를 살짝 젖히며 말했다. “데일 양의 그 행운 이야기를 저희가 듣기는 이번이 세 번째예요.”\n\n“데일 집안은 댁과 친척이지요, 아마?” 마거리타가 말했다.\n\n“전혀 아니에요.” 레이디 줄리아가 발끈해서 말했다. “크로즈비 씨가 결혼하겠다는 아가씨는 우리와 아무 연고도 없습니다. 앨링턴 재산의 상속자인 그 아가씨의 사촌이 어머니 쪽으로 내 조카일 뿐이지요.” 그러고서 그 이야기는 끝났다.\n\n크로즈비는 도착하자 제 방으로 안내되어 만찬 시각을 전해 듣고는 알아서 하도록 내버려졌다. 그는 전에도 이 성에 와 본 적이 있어 이 집의 사정을 알고 있었다. 그래서 그는 탁자 앞에 앉아 릴리에게 보낼 편지를 쓰기 시작했다. 그러나 얼마 나아가지 못했으니, 실은 어떤 투로 첫머리를 열지조차 아직 정하지 못한 채 손에 펜을 들고 우두커니 앉아 릴리를 생각하고, 또 지금 제가 와 있는 이런 집들이 머지않아 저에게 문을 닫으리라는 것을 생각하고 있었는데, 그때 문을 두드리는 소리가 나더니 대답할 새도 없이 아너러블 존이 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n“여어, 이 사람아.” 아너러블 존이 말했다. “잘 지냈나?”\n\n크로즈비는 존 드 코시와 가깝게 지내 왔으나 그에게 우정도 호감도 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 크로즈비는 존 드 코시 같은 자들을 좋아하지 않았다. 그럼에도 두 사람은 서로를 이 사람 저 사람 하고 부르며 서로 옆구리를 찌르고 아주 허물없이 지냈다.\n\n“여기 와 있다는 소릴 들었지.” 아너러블 존이 말을 이었다. “그래서 올라와 얼굴이나 보자 했네. 장가간다며, 안 그런가?”\n\n“난 그런 줄 모르겠는데.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“이보게, 우린 그렇지 않다는 걸 다 아네. 여자들이 사흘째 그 얘기를 하고 있어. 어제만 해도 그 아가씨 이름이 입에 척 붙어 있었는데 지금은 잊어버렸군. 땡전 한 푼 없다지, 그렇지?” 그러면서 아너러블 존은 어느새 탁자 위에 걸터앉아 있었다.\n\n“나보다 자네가 훨씬 많이 아는 모양이군.”\n\n“그렇다면 우리한테 일러 준 건 게스트윅에서 온 그 노파로군. 여자들이 곧장 자네한테 달려들 걸세, 두고 보게. 아무것도 아닌 일이라면 그건 내가 보기엔 빌어먹을 창피한 노릇이지. 왜 늘 남을 그런 식으로 갈가리 찢어 놔야 한단 말인가? 저번에는 나를 장가보내려 들더라니까!”\n\n“그거 정말인가?”\n\n“해리엇 트위슬턴한테 말일세. 해리엇 트위슬턴 알지? 여간 근사한 아가씨가 아니라네. 하지만 난 그런 식으로 잡힐 사람이 아니지. 나야 해리엇을 무척 좋아해—내 나름대로 말일세. 그렇지만 나 같은 늙은 새는 쭉정이로 잡히지 않는 법이거든.”\n\n“트위슬턴 양이 놓친 것을 두고 내가 위로를 보내야겠군.”\n\n“위로랄 것까지야 있겠나. 그렇지만 정말이지 결혼이란 건 여간 김빠지는 노릇이 아니야. 조지 아내는 봤나?”\n\n크로즈비는 아직 그런 기쁨을 누려 보지 못했노라고 밝혔다.\n\n“지금 여기 와 있다네. 나 같으면 삼만 파운드의 열 곱절을 가졌다 해도 그 여자를 맞지 않았을 걸세. 맹세코 그렇지. 그런데 형은 꽤나 마음에 들어 하더군. 자네 믿기나 하겠나?—형은 이 세상에서 돈 말고는 아무것도 마음에 두지 않는다니까. 그런 사람은 자네도 본 적이 없을 걸세. 그런데 내 말해 두겠는데, 형의 코는 아직 납작해질 참이야. 폴록이 장가를 든다니 말일세. 폴록과 거의 살다시피 하는 콜페퍼한테서 들었네. 폴록은 그 여자가 아이를 뱄다는 소릴 듣자마자 당장 형을 제쳐 버리기로 마음먹었다는군.”\n\n“형제간의 우애가 참으로 대단하군.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그가 그럴 줄 난 알고 있었지.” 존이 말했다. “그래서 조지가 짝을 맺기 전에 내가 그렇게 말해 주었네. 그런데도 형은 굳이 밀고 나가더군. 사오 년만 더 지금 그대로 있었더라면 아무 위험도 없었을 걸세—폴록은, 알다시피, 지독한 생활을 하고 있으니까. 이제 와서 그가 마음을 고쳐먹고 찬송가나 부르고 앉아 있다 한들 난 놀라지 않겠네.”\n\n“사람이 이 세상에서 어떻게 될지는 아무도 모르는 법이지.”\n\n“암, 그렇고말고. 하지만 내 말해 두겠는데, 나한테서는 아무 변화도 못 볼 걸세. 내가 장가를 든다 해도 그건 인생을 접겠다는 뜻으로 드는 게 아니야. 그런데 이 사람아, 여기 시가 좀 있나?”\n\n“뭐, 여기서 피우겠다는 말인가?”\n\n“그래, 안 될 게 뭔가? 여자들한테서 이렇게나 멀리 떨어져 있는데.”\n\n“내가 이 방을 쓰고 있는 동안은 안 되네. 게다가 만찬 옷을 갈아입을 시각이야.”\n\n“그런가? 정말 그렇군, 이런! 그래도 난 먼저 한 대 피우고야 말겠네, 그건 장담하지. 그러니까 자네가 약혼했다는 건 죄다 헛소리다, 이 말이지?”\n\n“내가 아는 한은 그렇다네.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그러고서 그의 벗은 그를 두고 나갔다.\n\n당장, 오늘 바로 이날, 그는 제 약혼을 두고 어떻게 해야 할 것인가? 그 소문이 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트의 입을 통해 코시까지 실려 오리라는 것은 그도 확신하고 있었으나, 그에 따라 무엇을 할지에 대해서는 아무런 결심에도 이르지 못한 터였다. 도착하자마자 그 죄목으로 고발당하여 유죄냐 무죄냐를 답하라는 요구를 받으리라고는 생각해 보지 못했다. 무죄를 주장할 마음은 한순간도 품은 적이 없었으나, 릴리언 데일과 약혼했노라고 스스로 밝히는 일이 내키지 않는다는 것은 그도 알고 있었다. 그렇게 밝히면 코시 성 같은 집들에서 누리는 온갖 즐거움에서 당장 잘려 나가는 것만 같았다. 그리고 그가 스스로에게 따져 보았듯, 총각 생활의 이 짤막한 나머지를 즐기지 못할 까닭이 무엇이란 말인가? 존 드 코시에게 약혼을 부인한 것으로 말하자면—그것은 아무것도 아니었다. 그런 자에게 제 신상의 사실을 감추는 것이야 누구든 정당하다고 여길 터였다. 존의 입에서 되풀이되는 부인이란 아무 무게도 없을 것이다—존의 친누이들 사이에서조차도. 그러나 이제 크로즈비는 이 집 부인들에게서 질문을 받으면 무어라 말할지 마음을 정해야 했다. 그들에게 사실을 부인한다면 그 부인은 아주 심각한 일이 될 터였다. 게다가 레이디 줄리아를 맞은편에 두고 그런 부인을 한다는 것이 과연 될 법한 일이겠는가?\n\n그런 부인을 한다니! 그가 그러기를 바랄 수 있다는 것이, 그런 거짓을 생각하고 심지어 그런 비겁한 짓을 저지를 궁리까지 한다는 것이 사실이란 말인가? 바로 그날 아침에 그는 그 어린 아가씨를 제 가슴에 안았다. 그녀에게 맹세했고, 또 스스로에게도 맹세했다. 그녀가 그를 믿지 못할 까닭을 결코 주지 않겠노라고. 좋은 쪽으로든 나쁜 쪽으로든 자기가 그녀에게 매인 몸이라고 그는 더없이 엄숙하게 스스로 인정한 터였다. 그런데 벌써 그녀를 모른 체할 수 있을지를 셈하고 있다니, 있을 수 있는 일인가? 그런 짓을 하면서 스스로에게 나는 악당이라고 말하지 않을 수 있었겠는가? 그러나 실은 그는 그런 셈을 하고 있지 않았다. 그의 목적은 가능하기만 하다면 그 화제를 몰아내는 것, 의심을 자아낼 만한 대답을 하나 찾아내는 것이었다. 백작부인에게 대놓고 그 소문에는 아무 근거도 없으며 데일 양은 저와 아무 사이도 아니라고 말할 생각은 그에게 떠오르지 않았다. 그러나 레이디 줄리아가 있는 자리에서라도 그 화제를 솜씨 좋게 웃어넘길 수는 있지 않겠는가? 약혼한 남자들이 흔히 그렇게 하는데, 그라고 그러지 못할 까닭이 무엇인가? 아가씨의 마음을 헤아리는 배려 때문에 남자가 제 약혼을 드러내 놓고 말하지 않는 것이 보통이라고들 여겼다. 그러다가 그는 앨링턴 근방 온 동네에서 제 처지가 얼마나 스스럼없이 입에 오르내렸던가를 떠올렸고, 데일 집안이 입조심을 하지 않은 것이 거의 무례에 가까웠다는 생각을 난생처음으로 했다. “나를 더 단단히 붙들어 매려고 그런 게로군.” 그는 크라바트 끝자락을 잡아당기며 혼잣말을 했다. “여기 온 것이, 아니 그렇게 내 일을 정해 놓고 어디든 나다니는 것이 얼마나 어리석은 짓이었던가.” 그러고서 그는 응접실로 내려갔다.\n\n당장 제 죄로 추궁당하지 않는다는 것을 알았을 때 그는 거의 마음이 놓였다. 그 자신이 그 문제로 머리가 가득 차 있던 터라 들어서는 순간 공격을 받으리라 예상했던 것이다. 그러나 그는 그 일에 대한 아무런 언급도 없이 맞아들여졌다. 백작부인은 특유의 조용한 태도로, 마치 바로 어제 만났던 사람인 양 그와 악수했다. 안락의자에 앉아 있던 백작은 누군가에게 저 낯선 자가 누구냐고 큰 소리로 물었고, 그러고는 손가락 두 개를 내밀며 환영이랍시고 무어라 우물거렸다. 그러나 크로즈비는 그런 것쯤은 충분히 감당할 줄 알았다. “안녕하십니까, 백작님?” 그는 이렇게 말하면서 얼굴을 돌려 다른 누군가를 보았고, 그 뒤로는 이 집 주인에게 더는 아는 체하지 않았다. “나를 모른다고!” 혼인의 굴레에 매여 몸이 불편해졌다고는 하나, 크로즈비는 적어도 아직까지는 사교계의 무게에서 저와 백작이 대등하다고 느꼈다. 그러고 나서 그는 응접실 안쪽, 나이 든 사람들에게서 떨어진 자리에 서 있는 자신을 발견했으니, 레이디 알렉산드리나와 드 코시 집안의 사촌인 그레셤 양, 그리고 그 자리에 모인 젊은 축 몇몇과 함께였다.\n\n“레이디 덤벨로를 모셔 오셨군요?” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“네, 그럼요. 그 사랑스러운 분!” 레이디 마거리타가 말했다. “와 주신 것만 해도 얼마나 고마운 일인지 몰라요.”\n\n“세인트 벙게이 공작부인은 딱 잘라 거절하셨답니다.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다. “저희가 그분을 만나게 해 드리려고 크로즈비 씨한테 얼마나 잘해 드린 건지 알아주셔야 해요. 오게 해 달라고 청한 사람들이 실제로 있었으니까요.”\n\n“고맙습니다. 하지만 사실 제 고마움은 레이디 덤벨로보다는 코시 성과 이 성에 늘 머무는 분들과 더 관계가 깊습니다. 그분 남편도 와 있습니까?”\n\n“그럼요, 이 방 어딘가에 계세요. 저기 계시네요, 레이디 클랜디들럼 곁에 서 계시잖아요. 만찬 전이면 늘 저렇게 서 계세요. 저녁에는 거의 같은 모양새로 앉아 계시고요.”\n\n크로즈비는 방에 처음 들어설 때 이미 그를 보았고, 방 안의 사람 하나하나를 다 보아 두었다. 그는 그렇게 훑어보는 눈길의 의무를 빠뜨릴 만큼 어수룩하지 않았다. 그러나 덤벨로 경을 미처 보지 못했다고 말해 두는 편이 그가 걷는 길에서는 듣기에 좋았다.\n\n“그러면 그 부인께서는 아직 안 내려오셨습니까?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“대개 맨 마지막이세요.” 레이디 마거리타가 말했다.\n\n“그러면서도 옷 입혀 드리는 여자를 늘 셋이나 두고 계신답니다.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다.\n\n“그렇지만 다 끝나고 나면 그 얼마나 훌륭한 성공입니까!” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“정말 그래요!” 마거리타가 힘주어 말했다. 그때 문이 열리고 레이디 덤벨로가 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n곧 모두가 술렁였다. 통풍에 걸린 늙은 백작조차 의자에서 몸을 끌어 올려 이를 드러내 웃으며 상냥하고 다정해 보이려 애썼다. 백작부인은 앞으로 나서며 아주 상냥하고 다정한 낯빛으로 자잘한 인사치레의 말을 늘어놓았고, 자작부인은 그저 우아한 미소 하나로만 답했다. 레이디 클랜디들럼은 살이 몹시 찌고 몸이 무거운데도 자작 곁을 떠나 일어서서 그 무리에 끼었다. 이름 높은 독일 외교관 포츠뇌프 남작은 가슴 앞에 두 손을 모으고 깊이 허리를 숙였다. 십오 분째 잠자코 서 있던 아너러블 조지는 부인께서 바깥 공기가 꽤 쌀쌀하다고 느끼셨을 것 같다고 말을 건넸다. 그리고 레이디 마거리타와 레이디 알렉산드리나는 자잘한 인사치레의 말을 재잘거리며 사랑하는 레이디 덤벨로에게 팔랑팔랑 다가가 이것을 바라고 저것을 간청했으니, 마치 눈앞의 그 「흰옷의 여인」이 어린 시절부터 가장 소중히 여겨 온 벗이기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n그녀는 정말로 흰옷의 여인이었으니, 흰 비단옷을 입고 그 위에 흰 레이스를 걸쳤으며 몸에 지닌 보석이라고는 다이아몬드뿐이었다. 아주 아름답게 차려입어, 그녀를 셋이 힘을 합쳐 빚어내는 데 성공한 그 세 예술가에게 더없는 영예를 안겨 준 것이 틀림없었다. 그리고 그 얼굴 또한 아름다웠으니, 차갑고 아무것도 드러내지 않는 어떤 아름다움이었다. 그녀는 아주 천천히 방을 걸어 올라오며 여기서 한 번 저기서 한 번 미소 지었으나 그 미소는 여전히 아주 희미했고, 안주인이 가리켜 주는 자리에 가 앉았다. 백작부인에게 한마디, 백작에게 두 마디를 했다. 그 밖에는 입을 열지 않았다. 저에게 바쳐지는 모든 경의를 그녀는 분명히 제 몫인 양 받아들였다. 조금도 어색해하지 않았고, 제 침묵을 조금이라도 부끄러워하는 기색을 보이지도 않았다. 그녀는 바보처럼 보이지 않았고 바보로 여겨지지도 않았다. 그러나 그녀가 사교계에 내놓은 것이라고는 차갑고 단단한 아름다움과 걸음걸이와 옷차림뿐이었다. 그것으로 충분히 내놓은 것이라고 우리는 말해도 좋겠으니, 사교계 스스로가 그녀에게 깊이 빚졌노라고 인정했기 때문이다.\n\n레이디 덤벨로가 들어설 때 방 안에서 유일하게 꿈쩍하지 않은 사람은 그 남편이었다. 그러나 그가 움직이지 않은 것은 열의가 없어서가 아니었다. 아내가 의기양양하게 들어서는 것을 보았을 때 실로 기쁨의 불꽃 하나가 그의 눈에 반짝였다. 그는 대귀족인 저에게 걸맞은 혼인을 했으며 세상이 저를 두고 제 할 일을 했다고 인정해 주고 있다고 느꼈다. 그런데도 레이디 덤벨로는 그저 시골 목사의 딸이었을 뿐이니, 부주교보다 높은 자리에 올라 본 적 없는 성직자의 딸이었다. “그 여자가 딸을 참으로 놀랍도록 잘 길러 놓았지.” 그날 저녁 백작부인이 제 화장방에서 마거리타에게 이렇게 말했다. 여기서 그 여자란 그랜틀리 부인, 곧 그 목사의 아내이자 레이디 덤벨로의 어머니를 가리키는 말이었다.\n\n늙은 백작은 운명과 서열표가 저더러 레이디 클랜디들럼을 만찬석으로 데려가라 요구했으므로 몹시 심사가 뒤틀렸다. 그녀가 그에게 기대기는커녕 성치 못한 그의 걸음을 친절히 거들려 하자 그는 그녀에게 거의 무례한 말을 하다시피 했다.\n\n“으! 당신하고 나같이 늙은 사람 둘을 서로 부축하라고 함께 내보내다니, 이건 못된 짜임새요.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그건 백작님이나 그러시겠지요.” 부인이 웃으며 말했다. “저로 말하면 아무 도움 없이도 얼마든지 돌아다닐 수 있답니다.” 그리고 그것은 실로 맞는 말이었다.\n\n“당신은 팔자가 좋구려!” 백작이 제자리에 몸을 앉히며 으르렁거렸다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 왼쪽에 앉은 레이디 덤벨로와 시시덕거리며 제 고통을 달래 보려 했다. 백작이 예쁜 젊은 여자들에게 짓궂은 실없는 소리를 속삭일 때 짓는 그 웃음과 그 드러난 이빨은 남자들이 보고도 놀랄 만한 진기한 구경거리였다. 그 짓궂은 실없는 소리가 이번에는 무엇이었든 간에 레이디 덤벨로는 그것을 죄다 태연히 받아넘기며 우아하게 미소 지었으나, 입에서 나오는 말은 한두 마디 토막말이 고작이었다.\n\n레이디 알렉산드리나가 크로즈비의 짝이 되었고, 그는 그렇게 된 것이 흡족했다. 결혼한 몸이 되면 드 코시 집안 같은 사람들과의 교제는 포기해야 할지도 모르나, 될 수만 있다면 레이디 알렉산드리나와의 우정만은 지키고 싶었다. 그런 우정이 조금이라도 가능하기만 하다면 레이디 알렉산드리나는 릴리에게 얼마나 좋은 벗이 되어 줄 것인가! 그런 사귐이 그 사랑스러운 어린 아가씨에게 얼마나 큰 이득을 안겨 줄 것인가—사실 그 사랑스러운 어린 아가씨의 매력이 아무리 크다 해도, 그녀에게 무언가 모자란 것이, 곧 어떤 사람들이 품격이라 부르는 몸가짐과 말씨가 모자란다는 것을 그는 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 릴리는 분명 레이디 알렉산드리나에게서 많은 것을 배울 수 있으리라. 그리고 이날 저녁 그가 그 부인의 마음에 들려고 그토록 부지런히 애쓴 것도 틀림없이 이런 확신 때문이었다.\n\n그리고 그녀 쪽도 보아하니 기꺼이 흡족해할 마음이 있었다. 만찬 내내 그녀는 릴리에 대해 그에게 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그러면서도 데일 집안과 앨링턴 이야기를 꺼내어 그가 어느 고장에 머물다 왔는지 알고 있음을 드러내 보였고, 그러고는 런던에서 마지막으로 함께한 모임들을 넌지시 입에 올렸으니, 크로즈비가 이제 와 떠올리기로 그 자리들에서 두 사람 사이는 거의 은근한 데까지 갔던 것이다. 적어도 그녀가 그와 다툴 마음이 없다는 것은 그에게 분명했다. 또한 그의 약혼을 두고 그에게 말을 꺼내기를 그녀가 조금 망설이고 있다는 것도 분명했다. 그녀가 그 일을 알고 있다는 것을 그는 한순간도 의심하지 않았다. 그리고 이런 식으로 두 사람 사이에 시간이 흘러 마침내 부인들이 식당을 떠났다.\n\n“자네도 장가를 든다며?” 부인들이 나가고 나자 크로즈비는 아너러블 조지 곁에 앉아 있는 자신을 발견했는데, 그 조지가 이렇게 말했다. 크로즈비는 호두 하나를 까느라 여념이 없었고, 굳이 대답할 필요를 느끼지 못했다.\n\n“사내가 할 수 있는 일 가운데 그게 제일 낫지.” 조지가 말을 이었다. “물론 큰 몫을 챙기는 데 빈틈이 없었다면 말일세—졸다가 덜컥 잡혀 버린 게 아니라면 말이지. 아무것도 없이 매달려 지내다가 어느새 늙은이가 되어 있는 건 사내가 할 짓이 아니거든.”\n\n“어쨌든 자네는 제 둥지를 두둑이 깔아 놓았군.”\n\n“그럼, 아귀다툼 속에서 나도 뭔가 하나 챙겼고, 그걸 지킬 작정일세. 아버지가 세상을 뜨고 나면 존은 어디 가 있겠나? 폴록은 목숨이 걸린대도 그에게 빵 한 조각에 치즈 한 점, 맥주 한 잔 내주지 않을 걸세—말하자면 정말로 그것이 아쉬운 처지라면 안 준다는 뜻이지.”\n\n“자네 형이 장가를 든다는 얘길 들었네만.”\n\n“그건 존한테서 들었겠지. 그놈이 나를 놀려 먹으려고 그 소릴 사방에 퍼뜨리고 다닌다네. 난 한마디도 믿지 않아. 폴록은 애초에 장가들 위인이 아니었어—게다가 내가 듣는 이야기로 보아 그리 오래 살 것 같지도 않고.”\n\n이런 식으로 크로즈비는 제 곤경에서 벗어났다. 그리고 만찬 식탁에서 일어설 때까지도 그는 제 이름에 흠이 될 만한 것을 무엇 하나 고백하도록 내몰리지 않았다.\n\n그러나 저녁은 아직 끝나지 않았다. 응접실로 돌아갔을 때 그는 백작부인과 이야기를 나누는 일만은 피하려 애썼으니, 공격은 딸 쪽보다 어머니 쪽에서 올 공산이 크다고 믿었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그는 아가씨들과 차례로 하나씩 이야기를 나누다가 마침내 다시 알렉산드리나와 단둘이 있는 자신을 발견했다.\n\n“크로즈비 씨.” 두 사람이 저만치 떨어진 탁자 앞에 나머지 사람들에게 등을 돌린 채 나란히 서 있을 때 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “릴리언 데일 양에 대해 뭐라도 좀 얘기해 주셨으면 해요.”\n\n“릴리언 데일 양에 대해서라니요!” 그가 그녀의 말을 되뇌며 말했다.\n\n“아주 예쁜가요?”\n\n“네, 예쁜 것은 틀림없습니다.”\n\n“그리고 아주 점잖고, 매력 있고, 영리하고—즐겁지 않은 데라고는 하나도 없고요? 완벽한가요?”\n\n“아주 매력 있는 아가씨입니다.” 그가 말했다. “하지만 완벽하다고는 생각하지 않습니다.”\n\n“그러면 그 아가씨의 흠은 무엇이지요?”\n\n“그 물음은 공평하지 않군요, 그렇지 않습니까? 누가 저더러 당신의 흠은 무엇이냐고 묻는다면, 제가 그 물음에 대답할 것 같습니까?”\n\n“분명히 대답하실 거예요. 그것도 아주 긴 목록을 만들어서요. 하지만 데일 양으로 말하면, 크로즈비 씨는 그분을 완벽하다고 여기셔야 마땅해요. 어떤 신사가 저와 약혼했다면 저는 그이가 온 세상 앞에서 제가 완벽함의 정수라고 맹세해 주기를 바랄 거예요.”\n\n“그렇지만 그 신사가 당신과 약혼한 사이가 아니라면요?”\n\n“그건 다른 이야기지요.”\n\n“저는 당신과 약혼한 사이가 아닙니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “그런 행복과 그런 영예는 아무래도 제 손이 닿기에는 너무 먼 곳에 있겠지요. 그렇지만 그럼에도 저는 어디서든 당신의 완벽함을 증언할 준비가 되어 있습니다.”\n\n“그러면 데일 양은 뭐라고 할까요?”\n\n“제가 제 벗들에 대해 말하기로 한 의견은 어디까지나 저 자신의 의견이지 다른 누구의 의견에 매인 것이 아님을 분명히 말씀드리겠습니다.”\n\n“그러면 아직은 노예 신세가 될 일이 없다고 생각하시는군요? 그런 자유를 앞으로 몇 달이나 더 누리실 건가요?”\n\n크로즈비는 대답하기 전에 잠시 잠자코 있다가 진지한 목소리로 말했다. “레이디 알렉산드리나.” 그가 말했다. “당신께 큰 부탁 하나를 드리고 싶습니다.”\n\n“무슨 부탁이지요, 크로즈비 씨?”\n\n“저는 아주 진지합니다. 제가 여기 머무는 동안 제 이름을 데일 양의 이름과 다시는 엮지 말아 주실 만큼 너그럽고, 친절하고, 제 벗이 되어 주시겠습니까?”\n\n“다투기라도 하셨나요?”\n\n“아닙니다. 다툰 일은 없습니다. 제가 왜 이런 부탁을 드리는지 지금은 설명해 드릴 수 없습니다. 그러나 당신께는 떠나기 전에 설명해 드리겠습니다.”\n\n“저한테 설명을 해 주시겠다고요!”\n\n“저는 당신을 그저 아는 사이 이상으로—벗으로 여겨 왔습니다. 이제는 지나간 날들 가운데는 그보다 더한 것을 말해도 좋으리라 바랄 만큼 제가 거의 무모해졌던 순간들도 있었습니다. 그런 바람을 품을 근거가 저에게 없었음은 인정합니다. 그러나 지금도 여전히 당신을 벗으로 여겨도 좋다고 저는 믿습니다만?”\n\n“오, 그럼요, 물론이지요.” 알렉산드리나가 아주 낮은 목소리로, 그 어조에 얼마간 다정함을 담아 말했다. “저는 늘 크로즈비 씨를 벗으로 여겨 왔어요.”\n\n“그래서 감히 이런 부탁을 드리는 것입니다. 그 일은 지금 이 순간 제가 후회 없이 터놓고 말할 수 있는 화제가 아닙니다. 그러나 적어도 당신께만은 코시를 떠나기 전에 모두 설명하겠노라고 약속드립니다.”\n\n어쨌든 그는 레이디 알렉산드리나를 아리송하게 만드는 데 성공했다. “난 그이가 약혼했다고는 눈곱만큼도 믿지 않아요.” 그날 밤 그녀가 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비에게 말했다.\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리라오, 얘야. 레이디 줄리아가 알지도 못하면서 그렇게 딱 잘라 말할 리가 없지. 물론 그 사람이야 그 얘기가 오르내리는 걸 바라지 않겠지만.”\n\n“그이가 그 아가씨와 약혼한 적이 있다 해도 이미 파해 버린 거예요.” 레이디 알렉산드리나가 말했다.\n\n“네가 부추겨 주기만 하면 아마 그렇게 하겠지, 얘야.” 시집간 언니가 자매다운 큰 호의로 말했다.\n\n# 제18장. 릴리 데일의 첫 연애편지\n\n크로즈비는 잠자리에 들 때 제법 자기 자신이 자랑스러웠다. 그는 자기에게 겨누어진 추궁을 용케 따돌렸으면서도, 양심이 스스로를 나무랄 만한 말은 한마디도 하지 않았다. 적어도 그때 그는 스스로에게 그렇게 일러 두었다. 그가 한 말이 남길 인상은, 그와 릴리언 데일 사이에 약혼 비슷한 이야기가 오간 적은 있으나 이 순간 확정된 것은 아무것도 없다는 정도이리라. 그러나 아침이 되자 그의 양심은 그리 깨끗하지가 않았다. 릴리가 그 모든 것을 알게 된다면 무슨 생각을 하고 무슨 말을 할까? 그가 감히 그녀에게, 아니 그 누구에게라도 제 마음의 진짜 상태를 털어놓을 수 있을까?\n\n침대에 누운 채, 목욕통의 시련과 맞닥뜨리기까지 아직 한 시간이 남았음을 알면서, 그는 코시 성과 그 안에 사는 사람들이 밉다고 느꼈다. 그들 가운데 데일 부인과 그 딸들에 견줄 만한 사람이 누가 있단 말인가? 그는 조지도 존도 질색이었다. 백작은 진저리가 났다. 백작부인으로 말하자면 그는 완전히 무심했으니, 알아 두어서 나쁠 것 없는 여자이되 코시 성과 런던 저택의 안주인으로서만 알아 둘 여자로 여겼다. 딸들로 말하자면, 그는 때때로 그들 모두를 비웃어 왔다. 지금 사랑한다고 공언하는 알렉산드리나까지도. 어쩌면 어떤 식으로든 그녀에게 약한 애정 같은 것이 있었는지도 모른다. 그러나 그것은 한 번도 그의 가슴에 닿아 본 적 없는 애정이었다. 그는 그 모든 것의 값어치를 정확히 잴 수 있었다. 온갖 특권을 거느린 코시 성, 레이디 덤벨로, 레이디 클랜디들럼, 그리고 그 전부를. 그는 알고 있었다. 슈롭셔에 있는 레이디 하틀탑의 으리으리한 지붕 아래에서보다도 앨링턴의 그 잔디밭에서 자기가 더 행복했고 스스로에게 더 흡족했다는 것을. 레이디 덤벨로는 영혼의 가장 깊은 구석까지도 이런 것들로 만족하는 사람이었다. 그러나 그는 남자 레이디 덤벨로가 아니었다. 그는 더 나은 무엇이 있다는 것을, 그리고 그 무엇이 제 손 닿는 곳에 있다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n그러나 그럼에도 코시의 공기는 그에게 너무 벅찼다. 그 문제를 놓고 스스로와 따지는 동안 그는 자신을 두고, 나을 길이 없는 나병에 걸린 사람, 그러니 제 삶 전체를 그 나병의 형편에 맞추어야 할 사람이라고 여겼다. 앨링턴의 작은 집이 코시 성보다 낫다고 스스로에게 일러 봐야 소용이 없었다. 사탄도 천국이 지옥보다 낫다는 것쯤은 알았다. 다만 제가 뒤쪽 자리에 더 어울린다는 것을 알았을 뿐이다. 크로즈비는 레이디 덤벨로를, 그것도 그녀의 벗들 틈에 앉아서, 제 재치가 찾아낼 수 있는 온갖 신랄한 말로 비웃었다. 그러면서도 그녀와 같은 집에 묵는 특권은 그에게 소중했다. 그것이 그가 떨어져 들어온 삶의 길이었고, 거기서 빠져나오려는 몸부림은 제 힘에 부치리라고 그는 속으로 자인했다. 그 모든 것이 아직 앨링턴에 있을 때에도 그를 괴롭혔지만, 코시 성의 휘장 아래에서는 거의 낭패감이 될 만큼 그를 짓눌렀다.\n\n당장 이곳에서 달아나는 편이 낫지 않을까? 그는 릴리언 데일과의 약혼을 후회하고 있다고 거의 스스로 인정했으면서도, 그것을 지키겠다는 결심만은 여전했다. 그는 명예를 걸고 “그 어린 아가씨”와 결혼할 의무가 있었고, 자신이 명예를 아는 사내라고 스스로 다짐하며 머리 위의 휘장을 준엄하게 올려다보았다. 그렇다, 그는 자신을 희생하리라. 말을 주도록 이끌려 간 이상, 그 말을 물리지는 않으리라. 그는 그러기에는 너무나 사내다웠다!\n\n그러나 들판에서 릴리가 헤어지는 편이 서로에게 나으리라고 말했을 때, 그 지혜의 결론을 물리친 것은 잘못이 아니었을까? 그 자리에서 그것을 받아들일 용기가 없었기 때문에 그녀의 제안을 물리친 것이라고, 그는 스스로에게 말하지 않았다. 아니다. “그는 그 가엾은 아가씨에게 너무나 잘해 주었기에 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받을 수 없었던 것이다.” 그가 제 가슴속에서 이 문제를 따진 방식이 그러했다. 그는 그녀에게 너무 진실했다. 그리고 이제 그 결과로 두 사람은 평생 불행해지리라! 그는 적은 수입으로 식솔을 거느리고 만족스럽게 살 수 없었다. 그것을 그는 잘 알고 있었다. 그 점에 관해서라면 그 자신보다 더 그를 매섭게 몰아붙일 사람은 없었다. 그러나 어린 시절 교육이 남긴 나쁜 결과를 이제 와 고치기에는 너무 늦었다.\n\n침대에 누운 채 그가 이 문제를 따진 방식이 그러했다. 이 논리를 저 논리로 몇 번이고 되받아 뒤엎으면서도, 그 어느 논리로든 결국은 이 약혼이 불운이라고 스스로 믿게끔 가르치면서. 가엾은 릴리! 그에게 남긴 그녀의 마지막 말은 결코 그를 의심하지 않겠다는 다짐이었다. 그리고 그녀 역시, 그가 떠난 첫 아침 침대에 누워 잠들지 못한 채, 두 사람이 주고받은 맹세를 오래 생각했다. 그녀는 그 맹세에 얼마나 참되려 했던가! 온 마음과 온 정신을 다해 그의 아내가 되려 했던가! 그를 사랑하기만 하려는 것이 아니었다. 그 사랑 속에서 그녀는 힘 닿는 데까지 그를 섬기려 했다. 이 세상 일에서 그를 섬기고, 할 수만 있다면 저세상 일에서도 그를 섬기려 했다.\n\n“언니,” 그녀가 말했다. “언니도 시집을 갔으면 좋겠어.”\n\n“고맙구나, 얘.” 벨이 말했다. “언젠가는 나도 그리되겠지.”\n\n“아, 그런데 나 농담하는 거 아니야. 이건 참 엄청난 일 같아. 그리고 언니가 나랑 같은 처지도 아닌데, 지금 나한테 그 얘기를 그때처럼 해 주기를 바랄 수도 없고. 언니는 내가 그이를 행복하게 해 줄 것 같아?”\n\n“그럼, 정말 그럴 것 같구나.”\n\n“그이가 만날 수 있는 다른 어떤 사람과 있을 때보다도 더? 그렇게까지는 감히 못 믿겠어. 그이한테 더 어울릴 사람이 눈에 보이기만 한다면, 나는 내일이라도 그이를 놓아줄 수 있을 것 같아.” 만일 릴리가 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시의 온갖 매력을 알게 되었더라면 뭐라고 말했을까?\n\n백작부인은 그에게 무척 상냥했다. 약혼에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않으면서도, 앨링턴에 머물렀던 이야기는 꽤 많이 꺼냈다. 크로즈비는 큰 저택의 부인들에게 반가운 남자였다. 사냥을 즐기기는 하되 늘 사냥터지기를 따라 나가 있을 만큼 열심인 사냥꾼은 아니었다. 정치를 하기는 하되 청서(靑書)를 들여다보거나 당의 전술을 짜느라 오전을 바치지는 않았다. 책을 읽기는 하되 연구에 몰두하지는 않았다. 말을 타기는 하되 마구간에서 자주 눈에 띄지는 않았다. 이야기가 필요할 때면 이야기를 대 줄 줄 알았고, 부인들 틈에 제가 있을 필요가 없을 때면 슬며시 자리를 비켜 줄 줄도 알았다. 도착한 이튿날 아침 식사와 점심 사이에 그는 백작부인과 꽤 오래 이야기를 나누었고, 아주 마음에 드는 사람 노릇을 했다. 부인은 데일 집안처럼 촌스럽고 시골티 나는 족속들 틈에서 그리 오래 머물렀다고 그를 계속 부드럽게 놀려 댔고, 그는 그 자잘한 빈정거림을 더없이 좋은 낯으로 받아 넘겼다.\n\n“꼼짝도 않고 앨링턴에서 여섯 주라니! 저런, 크로즈비 씨, 거기서 뿌리가 돋는 기분이었겠구려.”\n\n“그랬지요. 늙은 나무처럼 말입니다. 정말이지 어찌나 뿌리가 박혔는지 좀처럼 빠져나올 수가 없었습니다.”\n\n“그동안 줄곧 그 집에 사람이 가득했소?”\n\n“레이디 줄리아의 조카인 버나드 데일 말고는 아무도 없었습니다.”\n\n“그야말로 다몬과 피티아스로군. 여섯 주 내내 방해라고는 없는 우정의 즐거움을 누리겠다고 앨링턴의 그늘까지 내려가다니, 생각 좀 해 보구려.”\n\n“우정과 자고새지요.”\n\n“그럼 그 밖에는 아무것도 없었소?”\n\n“아니, 있었습니다. 아주 참한 딸 둘을 둔 미망인이 하나 있었지요. 꼭 같은 집은 아니고, 같은 울안에 살고 있었습니다.”\n\n“오, 그러셨군. 그러면 사정이 아주 달라지지요, 안 그렇소? 그대는 자고새 때문에 궁핍을 견딜 사람도 아니고, 우정 때문이라 해도 그리 많이 견딜 사람은 아닐 성싶소. 하지만 예쁜 아가씨들 이야기가 나온다면—”\n\n“사정이 달라지지요, 그렇지 않습니까?”\n\n“아주 크게 달라지지요. 그 데일 부인이라는 사람 이야기는 전에도 들은 것 같소. 그래, 그 딸들이 참하다고?”\n\n“정말이지 아주 참합니다.”\n\n“크로케를 하고, 잔디밭에서 실러법을 먹고 그러겠지요? 그런데 참말이지, 지겨워지지는 않던가요?”\n\n“저런, 전혀요. 해가 긴 만큼 내내 행복했습니다.”\n\n“양치기 지팡이라도 짚고 돌아다녔겠구려?”\n\n“진짜 지팡이까지는 아니었습니다만, 그런 종류의 일은 다 했지요. 돼지에 관해서 아주 많이 배웠습니다.”\n\n“데일 양의 지도 아래서?”\n\n“예, 데일 양의 지도 아래서요.”\n\n“그런 매력에서 몸을 떼어 내어 우리처럼 낭만이라고는 없는 사람들에게 와 주셨으니 참으로 고마운 일이오. 하지만 사내들이란 평생에 한두 번은 꼭 그런 짓을 하는 법이지요. 그러고는 제 추억거리를 이야기하고 말이오. 그대에게도 추억거리 이상은 되지 않으리라 짐작하오만.”\n\n이것은 대놓고 던진 물음이었으나, 그래도 슬쩍 받아넘길 여지는 있었다. “어쨌든 하나는 남겨 주었지요.” 그가 말했다. “평생 가는 것으로 말입니다!”\n\n백작부인은 아주 흡족했다. 레이디 줄리아의 말이 전부 사실이라는 것을 그녀는 한순간도 의심한 적이 없었다. 크로즈비가 런던에서 제 딸에게 반한 기색을 보였으면서 시골 아가씨와 약혼했다는 것은 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니었다. 그녀가 보기에 그런 행실이 큰 죄가 되는 것도 아니었다. 사내들은 날마다 그런 짓을 했고, 아가씨들은 그러려니 하고 대비하는 법이었다. 그녀가 보기에 사내란 약혼했다고 해서 공격에서 안전하다고 여길 대상이 아니었다. 그를 제 것이라 떠맡은 아가씨가 알아서 그 점을 살필 일이었다. 제 새끼들이 걸어온 지난 이력을 돌이켜 보면, 딸들이 겪은 그런 낭패가 한 번으로 그치지 않았다. 알렉산드리나 말고 다른 딸들도 그런 꼴을 당했다. 드 코시 백작부인은 딸들을 두고 큰 희망을 품었고, 그다음에는 웬만한 희망을 품었으며, 그다음에는 다시 쓰디쓴 낭패를 맛보았다. 시집간 것은 하나뿐이었고, 그나마 사무 변호사에게 갔다. 이 문제에서 릴리의 권리를 두고 그녀가 무슨 고결한 감정을 품으리라고는 짐작할 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n크로즈비 같은 남자가 백작의 딸에게 대단한 혼처가 아닌 것은 분명했다. 그런 혼인이란 사실 초라한 승리에 지나지 않는다고 해도 좋으리라. 지난 사교철에 런던에서 알렉산드리나의 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 눈여겨보았을 때, 백작부인은 딸을 타이르며 그 경솔함을 나무랐다. 그러나 그 딸은 이 일에 십사 년을 매달려 온 터라 이제 진력이 나 있었다. 언니들은 더 오래 매달렸다가 거의 절망 속에 손을 놓아 버렸다. 알렉산드리나는 제 마음이 이제 제 뜻대로 되지 않으며 영원히 크로즈비에게 바쳐졌다는 말을 어머니에게 하지는 않았다. 다만 입을 삐죽이며 제가 무엇을 하는지 아주 잘 알고 있다고 말하고, 도리어 어머니를 나무라며, 이 싸움이 몹시 지겨워지고 있다는 것을 드 코시 백작부인이 알아차리게 만들었다. 게다가 따져 볼 것이 또 있었다. 크로즈비 씨가 제 손에 쥔 것이 많지 않은 것은 분명하나, 그는 집안의 영향력과 제 지위로 무언가를 만들어 낼 만한 사람이었다. 어떤 누룩으로도 부풀릴 수 없는, 가망 없이 둔중한 사내는 아니었다. 사교계에서의 그의 자리를 두고 백작부인이나 그 딸들이 부끄러워할 필요는 없는 사람이었다. 드 코시 백작부인이 이 일에 대놓고 승낙을 준 적은 없었으나, 그 계획을 받아들일 만한 것으로 여긴다는 데에 모녀 사이의 양해는 서 있었다.\n\n그러던 차에 저 앨링턴의 어린 아가씨 소식이 들려온 것이었다. 그녀는 크로즈비에게 화가 나지 않았다. 그런 일로 화를 내는 것은 부질없고 어리석으며 거의 예의에 어긋나는 짓이었다. 그것은 크리켓 선수에게 수비가 그러하듯 그녀에게는 지극히 자연스러운 경기의 한 부분이었다. 어떤 경기에서도 이기기만 할 수는 없는 법이다. 크로즈비가 끝내 제 사위가 되든 안 되든, 앨링턴의 그 젊은 아가씨의 삼주문을 공으로 쓰러뜨리는 일은 살아가는 도리의 한 부분으로서 자연스럽게 그녀에게 다가왔다. 데일 양이 경기를 잘 알아서 제 위킷을 지킬 수 있다면, 그렇게 하라지.\n\n크로즈비가 릴리언 데일과 약혼했다는 데에 그녀는 조금도 의심이 없었으나, 그가 그 약혼을 부끄러워한다는 데에도 그만큼 의심이 없었다. 정말로 데일 양을 아꼈다면 그녀를 두고 코시 성으로 오지는 않았으리라. 정말로 그녀와 결혼하기로 굳게 마음먹었다면, 약혼에 관한 물음들을 죄다 꾸며 낸 대답으로 막아 내지는 않았으리라. 그는 릴리 데일을 데리고 심심풀이를 한 것이고, 그 젊은 아가씨가 그 일을 그리 심각하게 여기지 않았기를 바랄 따름이었다. 드 코시 백작부인이 이 문제를 바라보려는 가장 너그러운 시선이 그러했다.\n\n크로즈비는 만찬 전에 릴리 데일에게 편지를 써야 했다. 도착하는 즉시 쓰겠다고 약속했으니, 이미 약속보다 하루가 늦은 것으로 여겨지리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 릴리는 그의 편지를 양식 삼아 살겠노라고 말했었고, 그러니 그가 그 첫 끼니를 차려 주는 일은 반드시 필요했다. 그래서 그는 만찬 전에 넉넉히 시간을 두고 제 방으로 물러가 펜과 잉크와 종이를 꺼냈다.\n\n펜과 잉크와 종이를 꺼내고 나서야 그는 제 고생이 이제부터 시작임을 알았다. 크로즈비가 아주 악당은 아니었다는 점만은 알아주기를 나는 바란다. 그는 쓰면서 그 말이 거짓임을 스스로 아는 편지를, 마음에서 우러난 것인 양 앉아서 써 내려갈 수는 없는 사람이었다. 그는 인색하고 세속적이며 변덕스러운 남자였고, 제 자신을 좋게 여기기를 몹시 좋아했으며, 지니지도 않은 미덕을 제 몫으로 셈하기 일쑤였다. 그러나 사랑하겠노라 맹세한 여자에게 미리 작정한 잔인함으로 거짓을 부릴 수는 없었다. 적어도 그동안만이라도 스스로를 다정하고 따뜻한 마음으로 릴리를 향하게 만들지 않고서는, 릴리에게 다정하고 따뜻한 편지를 쓸 수가 없었다. 그리하여 그는 펜이 아직 손안에서 마른 채로 있는 동안, 드 코시 백작부인의 술수로 릴리와 앨링턴에 등을 돌리게 된 제 생각을 다시 빚어내는 일에 앉은 채로 매달렸다. 사람이 이런 일을 해내려면 시간이 걸린다. 아주 불편한 방식으로 제 자신과 싸워야 하고, 그 애씀이 헛수고로 끝나는 일도 잦다. 다른 때 같으면 휘파람 한 번 불지 않아도 말달리듯 달려들어 오는 몇 가지 생각을 마음속에서 끌어올리기보다, 차라리 백 근짜리 짐 두 덩이를 들어 올리는 편이 쉬울 때가 있다.\n\n편지의 날짜를 막 적었을 때 문을 가볍게 두드리는 소리가 나더니 문이 열렸다.\n\n“이보게, 크로즈비.” 아너러블 존이 말했다. “어제 만찬 전에 시가 한 대 하자는 얘기를 하지 않았나?”\n\n“한마디도 안 했네.” 크로즈비가 제법 성난 어조로 말했다.\n\n“그럼 내가 그랬나 보군.” 존이 말했다. “아무튼 여기서 피우지 않겠다면 담배통을 들고 마구실로 내려오게. 거기다 제대로 된 아늑한 방을 하나 꾸며 놨거든. 들어가서 녀석들이 말 손질하는 것도 구경하고 말이야.”\n\n크로즈비는 아너러블 존이 어디 지옥에라도 가 있기를 바랐다.\n\n“편지를 써야 해서.” 그가 말했다. “게다가 나는 만찬 전에는 담배를 피우지 않네.”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리. 나는 자네와 만찬 전에 시가를 수백 대는 피웠네. 자네까지 조지며 그 패거리처럼 심술궂은 늙은이가 되려는 건가? 세상이 어떻게 돌아가려는지 알 수가 없군! 실은 앨링턴의 그 어린 아가씨가 자네한테 담배를 못 피우게 하는 게로군.”\n\n“앨링턴의 그 어린 아가씨는—” 크로즈비가 말을 꺼냈다가, 지금 앞에 있는 이 친구에게 그 어린 아가씨에 관해 무슨 말이든 하는 것은 좋지 않으리라는 데 생각이 미쳤다. “그럼 이렇게 하지.” 그가 말했다. “나는 정말로 이번 우편으로 부쳐야 할 편지가 있어. 내 시가 통은 화장대 위에 있네.”\n\n“나는 그런 꼴이 되기까지 한참 걸렸으면 좋겠군.” 존이 시가를 손에 집어 들며 말했다.\n\n“통은 돌려주게.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그 어린 아가씨한테 받은 선물인가 보지?” 존이 말했다. “알았네, 이 친구야! 돌려주지.”\n\n“사내가 얻기에 참 훌륭한 처남이겠군.” 물러가는 드 코시 가문의 자제 뒤로 문이 닫히자 크로즈비는 혼잣말을 했다. 그러고는 다시 펜을 들었다. 편지는 써야만 했고, 그래서 그는 말이 나오고 종이가 채워지고야 말리라 마음먹고 탁자 위로 몸을 내던졌다.\n\n코시 성, 186─년 10월.\n\n사랑하는 릴리에게. 이건 내가 그대에게 쓰는 첫 편지예요. 조심스레 안부를 전하던 그 짧은 쪽지들 말고는 말이지요. 그러니 어쩐지 참 이상한 기분이 듭니다. 이 편지가 마땅히 와야 할 때보다 늦었다고 그대는 생각하겠지요. 하지만 사실은 나도 어제 만찬 직전에야 겨우 이곳에 들어왔답니다. 바체스터에 한참을 머물렀고, 거기서 아주 별난 어른을 만났어요. 그러니까 내가 교회에 갔는데, 예배를 집전한 목사와 나중에 스스럼없이 어울리게 되었지요. 어찌나 온화한 노인인지. 그런데 참으로 묘하게도, 그분이 지금 여기 묵고 있는 레이디 덤벨로의 할아버지랍니다. 그대라면 레이디 덤벨로를 어떻게 볼지, 아니 그런 사람과 한집에 일주일이나 갇혀 지내는 걸 어떻게 여길지 궁금하군요.\n\n그런데 바체스터에 머문 일에 관해서는 이제 사실대로 말해야겠어요. 떠나던 날 나는 뻔뻔한 사기꾼이었으니까요. 나는 그 마지막 아침에 작별하는 일을 피하고 싶었고, 그래서 굳이 그럴 필요가 없는데도 훨씬 일찍 길을 나섰던 겁니다. 그대가 몹시 화를 내리라는 걸 압니다. 그러나 숨김없는 고백은 영혼에 이롭다고 하지요. 그대가 일찍 일어나는 바람에 내 자잘한 계획은 죄다 어그러졌어요. 그리고 우리가 떠나는 것을 테라스에 서서 바라보는 그대를 보면서, 나는 그대가 옳았고 내가 그르다는 것을 인정했습니다. 막상 그때가 되고 보니, 마지막 순간에 그대가 곁에 있어 준 것이 나는 몹시 기뻤어요.\n\n사랑하는 나의 릴리, 이곳이 앨링턴의 두 집과 얼마나 다른지, 그리고 내가 그쪽에 딸린 삶을 얼마나 더 좋아하는지 그대는 짐작도 못 할 겁니다. 내가 세상에서 이르는 대로 세속적인 사람이었다는 걸 압니다. 하지만 그대가 나를 그 병에서 고쳐 주어야 해요. 그대를 떠나온 뒤로 나는 스스로에게 무던히도 캐물었는데, 내가 아주 손쓸 수 없는 지경이라고는 생각지 않습니다. 어쨌든 나는 의사의 손에 나를 믿고 맡기렵니다. 사람이 제 버릇을 고치기가 어렵다는 건 알아요. 그래도 나 자신을 위해 이 말만은 참되게 할 수 있습니다. 나는 앨링턴에서 행복했고 하루의 매 시간을 즐겼으나, 여기서는 모든 사람에게, 그리고 거의 모든 것에 ennuyé, 곧 권태를 느낍니다. 이 집 딸들 가운데 하나는 내 마음에 듭니다. 그러나 다른 사람들로 말하자면 벗은커녕 말동무 하나 찾기가 어렵군요. 그렇지만 그런 교분을 전부 너무 갑작스레 끊어 버리는 것도 내게는 될 일이 아니었지요.\n\n런던으로 올라가면 — 이제는 정말이지 어서 그리로 가고 싶습니다 — 여기서보다 훨씬 마음 편하고 훨씬 자유롭게 그대에게 편지를 쓸 수 있을 거예요. 이 사람들 틈에서 나는 도무지 내가 아니라는 걸 압니다. 아니, 그대가 아는 나, 그리고 그대가 언제까지나 그렇게 알아 주기를 바라는 그 나는 도무지 아니라고 해야겠지요. 그래도 그 독기에 아주 넘어가 버린 것은 아니어서, 내가 그대를 얼마나 참되게 사랑하는지는 말할 수 있습니다. 설령 내 넋이 이곳에 있다 한들, 실은 그렇지도 않지만, 내 마음만은 앨링턴의 잔디밭에 가 있을 겁니다. 그 정다운 잔디밭과 그 정다운 다리!\n\n벨과 어머님께 내 다정한 안부를 전해 주세요. 나는 벌써 어머님을 내 어머니라고 불러도 되겠다 싶은 기분입니다. 그리고 릴리, 내 사랑, 당장 답장을 주어요. 그대가 내게 보내는 편지는 내가 그대에게 보내는 것보다 더 길고 더 좋고 더 밝으리라 기대합니다. 그래도 런던으로 돌아가면 내 편지도 더 나아지도록 애써 보지요.\n\n하느님의 축복이 그대에게 있기를. 온 마음을 다하여, 그대의,\n\n글을 써 내려가며 마음이 달아오르자 그는 애써 자신을 다정하게 만들었고, 스스로 흡족해한 대로 솔직하고 담백해졌다. 그러면서도 그는, 제 세속성을 넌지시 비친 그 대목들 속에 끝내 달아날 길이 필요해질 때를 위한 빠져나갈 구멍을 스스로 마련해 두고 있다는 것을 얼마쯤은 의식하고 있었다. “나는 애썼소.” 그때가 되면 그는 이렇게 말하리라. “나는 성공해 보려고 온 힘을 다해 정직하게 몸부림쳤소. 그러나 나는 그런 성공을 이룰 만큼 훌륭한 사람이 못 되오.” 그가 제 말을 그렇게 써먹으려는 속셈을 미리 품고 편지를 썼다는 말은 아니다. 다만 그 말을 써 내려가면서, 그것이 그런 식으로 쓰일 수도 있겠다는 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수 없었다는 것이다.\n\n그는 편지를 다시 읽어 보고 흡족해했으며, 이제 앞으로 마흔여덟 시간 동안은 그 생각에서 마음을 놓아도 되겠다고 작정했다. 제 죄가 무엇이든 릴리에 대한 도리는 다한 것이다! 그리고 이 흐뭇한 생각을 안고 그는 편지를 코시 성의 우편함에 넣었다.\n\n# 제19장. 지주가 작은 집을 찾아가다\n\n데일 부인은 크로즈비의 집에서 제 앞날의 삶을 위한 개인적인 행복을 많이 찾아내리라고 바랄 만한 근거가 별로 없다는 것을 스스로 인정했다. 그녀는 크로즈비 씨를 싫어하지도 않았고, 그를 크게 미덥지 않게 여기지도 않았다. 그러나 릴리가 런던에서 꾸릴 앞날의 집이 제게는 집이 될 수 없으리라는 것을 확신할 만큼은 그를 겪어 보았다. 그는 세속적인 사람, 적어도 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이었다. 그는 제 수입에서 뽑아낼 수 있는 것을 죄다 뽑아내려 애쓸 터였고, 그의 삶은 어쩌면 돈 그 자체를 위해서는 아닐지언정 오직 돈으로만 얻을 수 있는 것들을 위한 하나의 기나긴 싸움이 될 터였다. 연 팔백 파운드가 큰 재산인 사람들이 있고, 그 돈이 삶에 필요한 온갖 안락을 다 가져다주는 집들이 있다. 그러나 크로즈비는 그런 사람이 아니었고, 그의 집도 그런 집이 되지 않을 터였다. 데일 부인은 릴리가 그와 더불어 행복하기를, 그의 사는 방식에 만족하기를 바랐고, 또 그러하리라고 믿으려 애썼다. 그러나 제 자신에 관해서라면, 그런 혼인으로 제 아이가 저에게서 멀리 갈라져 나가리라는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 오는 세월에 제가 반갑게 맞아들여지기를 오래도록 고대해 온 그 정다운 거처는 들판과 나무들 사이에 있어야지 런던의 어느 비좁은 거리에 있어서는 안 되는 것이었다. 이제 릴리는 도시의 부인이 되어야 한다. 그러나 벨은 여전히 제 곁에 남을 것이고, 벨은 제 몫의 시골 보금자리를 찾아내리라고 아직은 바랄 수 있었다.\n\n릴리가 어머니에게 제 약혼을 처음 말한 날 이래로, 데일 부인은 어느새 작은딸보다 벨과 훨씬 더 속속들이, 더 자주 이야기를 나누고 있음을 깨달았다. 크로즈비가 앨링턴에 머무는 동안에는 그것이 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다. 그와 릴리가 함께 있는 것이 당연했고, 그동안 벨은 어머니와 함께 있었으니까. 그러나 크로즈비가 떠난 뒤에도 사정은 그대로였다. 어머니와 딸 사이에 서먹함이나 애정의 모자람이 있어서가 아니라, 릴리의 마음이 제 연인으로 가득 차 있었기 때문이고, 데일 부인으로 말하자면 그 혼인에 진심으로 동의를 해 주었으면서도 장차 사위가 될 사람과 통하는 데가 거의 없다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 그녀는 스스로에게조차 그가 싫다고 말한 적이 없었다. 아니, 때로는 그가 마음에 든다고 스스로에게 다짐하기까지 했다. 그러나 참으로 말하자면 그는 그녀의 마음에 맞는 사람이 아니었다. 그는 그녀에게 결코 제 아들이, 제 자식이 되어 줄 수 있는 사람이 아니었다.\n\n그러나 그녀와 벨은 릴리의 앞날을 이야기하며 몇 시간씩 함께 보내곤 했다. “나는 참 이상하구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “하필 그 아이가, 그 많은 처녀들 가운데 그 아이가 크로즈비 씨 같은 사람의 눈에 들었다는 것도, 그 아이가 그이를 좋아하게 되었다는 것도 말이다. 릴리가 런던에서 사는 모습이 도무지 그려지지 않는구나.”\n\n“그이가 그 애한테 착하고 다정하게만 해 준다면 어디서 살든 행복할 거예요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“나도 그러기를 바란다. 정말이지 그러기를 바라. 하지만 그 아이가 우리에게서 아주 멀리 갈라져 나가는 것만 같구나. 갈라놓는 것은 거리가 아니라 사는 방식이란다. 너만은 부디 나한테서 그렇게 멀리 데려가지지 않았으면 좋겠구나.”\n\n“저는 저를 런던으로 데려가게 놔두지는 않을 것 같아요.” 벨이 웃으며 말했다. “하지만 사람 일은 모르는 거죠. 만약 그렇게 되면 엄마도 우리를 따라오셔야 해요.”\n\n“너한테까지 크로즈비 씨 같은 사람이 또 오는 건 바라지 않는다, 얘야.”\n\n“하지만 저는 저대로 그런 사람을 바랄지도 모르잖아요. 그래도 아직 떨고 계실 것까지는 없어요. 아폴로가 날마다 이 길로 오는 건 아니니까요.”\n\n“가엾은 릴리! 그 애가 그이를 처음 아폴로라고 부르던 때가 기억나니? 나는 똑똑히 기억한다. 버나드가 그이를 데리고 내려온 이튿날 그이가 여기 왔던 것도, 내가 저쪽 정원에 있는 동안 너희가 잔디밭에서 놀고 있던 것도 기억나. 그때는 이런 일이 되리라고는 꿈에도 생각하지 못했지.”\n\n“그렇지만 엄마, 후회하시는 건 아니죠?”\n\n“그 애를 행복하게 해 줄 일이라면 후회할 게 없지. 그 애가 그이와 행복할 수 있다면야 물론 후회하지 않는다. 그이가 그 애를 우리에게서 땅끝까지 데려간다 해도 말이다. 그 애와 네가 둘 다 행복한 것 말고 내가 무엇을 더 바라겠니?”\n\n“런던 남자들도 시골 남자들 못지않게 아내와 행복하게 살아요.”\n\n“그럼, 그렇고말고. 여자들 가운데 그것을 맨 먼저 인정할 사람이 바로 나란다.”\n\n“그리고 어돌퍼스 그이만 놓고 보면 우리가 그이를 못 미더워할 까닭이 없잖아요.”\n\n“없지, 얘야. 아무 까닭도 없어. 내가 그이를 못 미더워했다면 그 혼인에 그렇게 선뜻 동의하지 않았을 게다. 하지만 그래도—”\n\n“사실은 엄마가 그이를 좋아하지 않으시는 거예요.”\n\n“네가 네 남편으로 고르는 사람이라면 누구든 내가 이만큼은 좋아했으면 하고 바라는데, 그만큼 살갑게는 아니로구나.”\n\n그리고 릴리는, 그 문제에 관해 데일 부인에게 한마디도 하지 않았으나, 어머니가 저에게서 어느 만큼 멀어져 있음을 느꼈다. 두 사람 사이에서 크로즈비의 이름은 자주 오르내렸다. 그러나 데일 부인의 목소리에도, 그를 이야기할 때의 태도에도, 참된 공감이라면 낳았을 그 열의와 살가움이 없었다. 릴리는 제 감정을 헤아려 보지도 않았고 어머니의 감정을 꼬치꼬치 캐물어 보지도 않았으나, 모든 것이 제가 바랐을 그대로는 아니라는 것을 알아차렸다. “엄마는 그이를 사랑하지 않으셔.” 크로즈비의 첫 편지를 받은 날 저녁에 릴리가 벨에게 말했다.\n\n“너만큼은 아니지, 릴리. 하지만 엄마도 그이를 사랑하셔.”\n\n“나만큼은 아니라니! 그런 말은 부질없어, 언니. 엄마가 나만큼 그이를 사랑하지 않는 거야 당연하지. 하지만 사실은 엄마가 그이를 도무지 사랑하지 않으신다는 거야. 내가 그걸 못 볼 것 같아?”\n\n“네가 너무 많은 걸 보는 것 같아 걱정이야.”\n\n“엄마는 그이를 나쁘게 말한 적이 한 번도 없어. 하지만 정말 그이가 마음에 들었다면 이따금은 그이를 두둔하는 말도 하셨을 거야. 언니나 내가 먼저 그이 이야기를 꺼내지 않으면 엄마는 그이 이름을 입에 올리지도 않으실걸. 그이가 마뜩잖으셨다면 왜 진작 말씀하지 않으셨을까?”\n\n“그건 엄마한테 온당한 말이 아니야.” 벨이 자못 진지하게 말했다. “엄마는 그이를 마뜩잖아하지 않으시고, 마뜩잖아하신 적도 없어. 너도 엄마를 잘 알잖니. 아주 강한 까닭이 없이는 그런 일에 우리한테 참견하실 분이 아니야. 크로즈비 씨에 관해서라면 엄마는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 승낙해 주셨어.”\n\n“그래, 그러셨지.”\n\n“그런데 어떻게 엄마가 그이를 마뜩잖아하신다고 말할 수 있니?”\n\n“엄마를 탓하려던 건 아니야. 아마 다 잘될 거야.”\n\n“다 잘될 거야.” 그러나 벨은 이렇게 아주 흡족한 다짐을 해 주면서도 다른 두 사람 못지않게 잘 알고 있었다. 크로즈비가 릴리와 혼인하여 그 애를 런던으로 데려가고 나면 이 집안이 갈라지리라는 것을.\n\n이튿날 아침 데일 부인과 벨은 함께 앉아 있었다. 릴리는 위층 제 방에서 연인에게 편지를 쓰고 있거나, 그의 편지를 읽고 있거나, 그를 생각하고 있거나, 그를 위해 무언가를 짓고 있었다. 어떻게든 그녀는 그를 위한 일에 매여 있었고, 그러느라 혼자 있었다. 때는 이제 10월 중순이었고, 데일 부인의 응접실에는 불이 지펴져 있었다. 잔디밭으로 열리던 창은 닫혔고, 두꺼운 커튼이 제자리에 다시 드리워졌으며, 여름이 마지막으로 다 갔다는 것이 반갑지 않은 사실로 받아들여져 있었다. 이것은 데일 부인에게 언제나 하나의 슬픔이었으나, 그것은 좀처럼 입 밖에 내어 말할 수 없는 종류의 슬픔이었다.\n\n“벨아.” 그녀가 문득 고개를 들며 말했다. “네 삼촌이 창밖에 와 계신다. 들어오시게 해라.” 커튼을 드리운 뒤로는 창을 닫아 두었을 뿐 아니라 빗장까지 질러 두었던 것이다. 그래서 벨이 일어나 지주가 들어올 길을 열어 주었다. 그가 이렇게 내려오는 일은 흔치 않았고, 내려올 때에는 대개 미리 말해 둔 어떤 볼일이 있어서였다.\n\n“아니, 벌써 불을 지폈소?” 그가 말했다. “나는 저쪽 집에서는 11월 초하루가 되기 전에는 아침에 불을 피우는 법이 없소. 나는 저녁 식사 뒤에 벽난로 속에 불티가 이는 걸 보는 게 좋더군.”\n\n“저는 추우면 불을 지피지요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. 그러나 이것은 지주와 그의 제수가 전에도 서로 뜻을 달리했던 문제였고, 데일 씨는 처리할 볼일이 있었으므로 이번에는 불에 관한 제 지론을 옹호하는 데 기운을 허비하고 싶지 않았다.\n\n“벨아, 얘야.” 그가 말했다. “네 어머니와 볼일로 잠깐 이야기를 좀 나눠야겠구나. 잠시만 자리를 비켜 주겠느냐?” 그 말에 벨은 제 바느질감을 챙겨 들고 위층 동생에게로 올라갔다. “크리스토퍼 삼촌이 아래층에서 엄마랑 계셔.” 그녀가 말했다. “볼일 이야기를 하신대. 네 혼사에 관한 일인가 봐.” 그러나 벨의 짐작은 빗나갔다. 지주의 방문은 릴리의 혼사와는 아무 상관이 없었다.\n\n벨이 나가고 나서도 데일 부인은 몸도 움직이지 않고 한마디도 하지 않았다. 지주가 그녀 쪽에서 무언가 물어 주기를 바라고 말을 멈춘 것이 뻔했는데도 그랬다. “제수씨.” 마침내 그가 말했다. “내가 무슨 말을 하러 왔는지 말하리다.” 그러자 그녀는 손에 들고 있던 바느질감을 앞에 놓인 반짇고리 위에 내려놓고 그의 말을 들을 자세를 갖추었다.\n\n“벨에 관해 이야기하고 싶소.”\n\n“벨에 관해서요?” 데일 부인이 말했다. 그가 제 맏딸에 관해 무슨 할 말이 있다는 것이 몹시 뜻밖이라는 투였다.\n\n“그렇소, 벨 말이오. 릴리가 시집을 가게 되었으니 벨도 시집을 가는 것이 좋을 거요.”\n\n“저는 도무지 그렇게 생각하지 않아요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “그 애를 서둘러 치워 버릴 마음은 조금도 없습니다.”\n\n“그렇겠지, 그럴 거요. 하지만 물론 제수씨는 그 아이의 앞날만 생각하는 것일 테고, 나 역시 그렇다고 참으로 말할 수 있소. 여느 형편이라면 그 아이의 혼사를 서두를 까닭이 없겠지만, 그런 일을 바랄 만하게 만드는 사정이라는 게 있을 수 있고, 나는 그런 사정이 있다고 생각하오.” 지주의 말투와 태도로 보아 그가 몹시 진지하다는 것이 뚜렷했다. 그러나 그가 채비해 온 보따리를 풀어놓기를 어려워한다는 것 또한 뚜렷했다. 목소리가 조금 머뭇거렸고, 거의 안절부절못하는 듯도 했다. 데일 부인은 조금은 심술궂은 마음으로 그를 도와주는 일이라고는 전혀 하지 않았다. 그녀는 제 딸들 일에 그가 참견하는 것을 시샘했고, 물론 그의 말을 들어 주어야 할 처지이기는 했으나, 그가 무슨 말을 하든 반대하겠다는 편견을, 거의 작정에 가까운 마음을 품고 들었다. 사정이 어떻고 하는 짤막한 말을 마친 뒤 지주는 다시 말을 멈추었다. 그러나 데일 부인은 여전히 그의 얼굴에 눈을 붙박은 채 말없이 앉아 있었다.\n\n“나는 제수씨의 아이들을 몹시 사랑하오.” 그가 말했다. “제수씨는 내가 그런다는 걸 좀처럼 인정해 주지 않는 것 같지만 말이오.”\n\n“그러시다는 걸 잘 알아요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “그 애들 둘 다 그걸 잘 알고 있고요.”\n\n“그리고 나는 그 아이들이 세상에서 편안하게 자리 잡기를 몹시 바라고 있소. 나에게는 내 자식이 없고, 내 두 아우의 자식들이 나에게는 전부요.”\n\n데일 부인은 버나드가 지주의 상속인이 되는 것을 늘 당연한 일로 여겨 왔고, 제 딸들이 그 몫에 무슨 권리가 있다고 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 데일 집안의 재산과 데일 집안의 돈은 모두 데일 가의 손위 남자에게 돌아가야 한다는 것이 이 집안에서 다들 잘 알고 있는 일이었다. 그녀는 그런 처사가 마땅하다는 것까지도 온전히 인정했다. 그러나 그가 조카 하나와 조카딸 둘을 마치 제 사랑의 공동 상속인이기라도 한 것처럼 나란히 부르는 것은 거의 위선에 가깝게 여겨졌다. 버나드는 그의 양자였고, 그 삼촌이 그런 양자를 들일 권리를 아무도 아까워하지 않았다. 버나드는 그에게 전부였고, 그의 상속인인 만큼 많은 일에서 그에게 순종할 의무가 있었다. 그러나 그녀의 딸들은 그에게 여느 삼촌에게 여느 조카딸이 되는 것 이상은 아무것도 아니었다. 그 아이들을 혼인으로 어디에 보내는가는 그가 상관할 일이 아니었다. 그리고 어머니의 마음은 벌써 제 몫의 독립을 위해, 또 제 아이들의 독립을 위해 싸울 태세로 곤두서 있었다. “버나드가 좋은 혼처를 만난다면 시아주버니께 위안이 되겠지요.” 그녀가 말했다. 지주에게는 그 밖의 어떤 혼사로도 골머리를 앓을 권리가 없다는 뜻을 담은 말이었다.\n\n“바로 그거요.” 지주가 말했다. “나에게 큰 위안이 될 거요. 그리고 그 아이와 벨이 서로 마음을 맞출 수만 있다면, 내 생각에는 제수씨에게도 큰 위안이 될 거요.”\n\n“버나드와 벨이라니요!” 데일 부인이 소리쳤다. 그런 짝을 지어 볼 생각은 여태 한 번도 그녀에게 떠오른 적이 없었고, 이제 놀라움에 그녀는 말없이 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 버나드 데일을 늘 좋아해 왔고, 제 집 울타리 밖의 데일 집안 사람들 가운데 누구보다도 그에게 집안다운 정을 느껴 왔다. 그는 그녀의 집을 무척 허물없이 드나들었고, 제 딸들에게 거의 오라비나 다름없이 굴어 왔다. 그러나 그를 두 아이 가운데 누구의 남편으로도 생각해 본 적은 한 번도 없었다.\n\n“그러면 벨이 제수씨에게 그 이야기를 하지 않았구려.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“한마디도 없었어요.”\n\n“그리고 제수씨도 그런 생각은 한 번도 해 본 적이 없고?”\n\n“물론 없지요.”\n\n“나는 그 생각을 아주 많이 했소. 몇 해째 늘 그 생각을 해 왔소. 거기에 내 마음을 걸어 두었고, 그것이 이루어지지 않는다면 나는 몹시 불행할 거요. 그 둘은 나에게 다 소중하오. 다른 누구보다도 소중하지. 그 둘이 부부가 된 것을 볼 수만 있다면, 그 옛집을 그 아이들에게 아무리 일찍 물려주고 떠나게 된들 나는 크게 개의치 않을 거요.”\n\n이 말에는 지주가 제 제수 앞에서 여태 보인 적이 없는 한결 맑은 감정의 결이 있었고, 그녀가 그에게 있으리라고 쳐 준 것보다 더한 살가움이 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 뜻밖의 이 따뜻한 사랑 안에 제 자식이 들어 있다는 것을, 그런 다정함에 어떻든 얼마간의 고마움은 품어야 마땅하다는 것을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“그 애를 그렇게 생각해 주시니 고맙습니다.” 어머니가 말했다. “참으로 고마운 일이에요.”\n\n“나는 그 아이 생각을 많이 하오.” 지주가 말했다. “하지만 지금 그건 그리 중요한 게 아니고. 실은 그 아이가 버나드의 청혼을 물리쳤소.”\n\n“버나드가 그 애한테 청혼을 했나요?”\n\n“그 아이가 나한테 그렇게 말하더군. 그리고 벨이 그 아이를 물리쳤다고. 여태 그 아이를 연인으로 볼 마음을 먹어 본 적이 없으니 그러는 것이 자연스러운 일인지도 모르지. 나는 벨을 조금도 나무라지 않소. 그 아이한테 화가 난 것도 아니고.”\n\n“화가 나시다니요! 그럴 리가요. 사촌을 사랑하지 않는다고 그 애한테 화를 내실 수야 없지요.”\n\n“나는 그 아이한테 화가 나지 않았다고 말하고 있소. 하지만 그 문제를 한번 헤아려 보겠다고는 할 수 있지 않겠소. 제수씨도 그런 혼처라면 좋아할 것 아니오?”\n\n데일 부인은 처음에는 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 그 일을 마음속에서 굴려 보며 이런저런 쪽에서 바라보기 시작했다. 그런 짜임에는 첫눈에도 그녀의 마음을 아주 강하게 끄는 데가 많았다. 이곳의 온갖 형편이 그것을 편들었다. 제 자신에 관해 말하자면 그것은 그녀가 여태 바라 온 모든 것을 약속해 주었다. 그것은 릴리를 아주 자주 볼 수 있는 앞날을 열어 줄 것이었다. 벨이 그 오래된 집안의 저택에 자리를 잡는다면 크로즈비도 제 벗을 찾아 자주 와 있을 것이기 때문이었다. 그녀는 버나드도 좋아했다. 그래서 마음속으로 이리저리 굴려 보는 동안 한두 순간은, 그런 혼인이 이루어진다면 이제라도 저와 늙은 지주 사이에 참된 정 비슷한 것이 자라날지도 모른다는 생각까지 들었다. 벨이 제 아이들을 데리고 저와 그토록 가까이 살아 준다면 저 작은 집에서의 늘그막이 얼마나 행복하겠는가!\n\n“어떻소?” 지주가 그녀의 얼굴을 몹시 뚫어지게 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n“생각을 좀 하고 있었어요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “그 애가 벌써 그이를 물리쳤다고 하셨지요?”\n\n“아무래도 그런 모양이오. 하지만 제수씨도 알다시피—”\n\n“그건 물론 그 애가 판단하도록 맡겨 두어야 할 일이지요.”\n\n“사촌과 억지로 혼인시킬 수는 없다는 뜻이라면, 그야 물론 우리 모두 그럴 수 없다는 걸 알고 있소.”\n\n“저는 그것보다 좀 더한 뜻으로 말씀드린 거예요.”\n\n“그러면 무슨 뜻이오?”\n\n“그 문제는 온전히 그 애가 결정하도록 맡겨 두어야 한다는 거예요. 시아주버니도 저도 그 애를 구슬려서는 안 된다는 거지요. 그이가 그 애를 구슬릴 수 있다면야 물론—”\n\n“그렇지, 바로 그거요. 그 아이가 벨을 구슬려야지. 그 아이가 제 뜻을 펼칠 자리를 얻어야 한다는 데는 나도 아주 동감이오. 하지만 이걸 좀 보시오, 제수씨. 그 아이는 늘 제수씨에게 아주 착한 딸이었소—”\n\n“그럼요, 그랬지요.”\n\n“그리고 제수씨의 한마디는 그 아이에게 큰 힘이 될 거요. 마땅히 그래야 하고. 제 어미가 사촌과 혼인하기를 바란다는 걸 알면 그 아이는 그것을 제 도리로 여길 테고—”\n\n“아! 하지만 바로 그것이 제가 그 애더러 그렇게 생각하도록 해 볼 수 없는 일이에요.”\n\n“내 말을 좀 끝까지 하게 해 주겠소, 제수씨? 내 입에서 말이 반도 나오기 전에 붙잡고 늘어져 나를 나무라는구려. 요즘 세상에 젊은 처녀더러 아무하고나 혼인하라고 억지로 시킬 수 없다는 거야 나도 물론 알고 있소. 하기야 내가 보기에는 처녀들이 지금처럼 제멋대로 하지 못하던 시절이 지금보다 나았지만.”\n\n“저는 자식더러 어떤 남자와 혼인하라고 청하는 일은 결코 떠맡지 않겠어요.”\n\n“하지만 그런 청혼을 아주 물리쳐 버리기 전에 깊이 생각해 보는 것이 제 도리라는 것쯤은 일러 줄 수 있지 않소. 처녀란 사랑에 빠져 있거나 아니거나 둘 중 하나요. 사랑에 빠져 있으면 사내의 목구멍으로라도 뛰어들 채비가 되어 있지. 릴리가 그런 경우였고.”\n\n“그 애는 그이가 온전히 청혼을 하기 전까지는 그이를 생각해 본 적도 없어요.”\n\n“뭐, 그건 이제 됐소. 하지만 처녀가 사랑에 빠져 있지 않으면, 저는 결코 사랑에 빠지지 않으리라고 맹세하고 다짐해야만 하는 줄로 안단 말이오.”\n\n“벨이 그런 걸 다짐했다고는 생각하지 않아요.”\n\n“아니, 다짐했소. 그 아이는 버나드한테 그를 사랑하지 않고 사랑할 수도 없다고, 그리고 사실 그 일은 더 생각해 보지도 않겠다고 말했소. 자, 제수씨, 그런 걸 두고 나는 고집스럽고 딱 잘라 말한다고 하는 거요. 나는 그 아이를 몰아붙일 생각이 없고, 제수씨가 몰아붙이는 것도 바라지 않소. 하지만 여기 그 아이에게 아주 좋은 자리가 마련되어 있단 말이오. 그건 제수씨도 인정해야지. 그 아이가 버나드와 더할 나위 없이 잘 지내 온 것은 우리 모두 알고 있소. 평생 서로 틀어지고 미워하며 지내 온 사이가 아니란 말이오. 그 아이는 버나드한테 그를 아주 좋아한다고 말했고, 누이가 되어 주겠다는 둥 부질없는 소리를 늘어놓았다더군.”\n\n“저는 그게 부질없는 소리라고는 조금도 생각하지 않아요.”\n\n“아니, 부질없는 소리요. 그런 자리에서는 말이오. 사내가 처녀한테 혼인해 달라고 청할 때는 누이가 되어 주겠다는 이야기를 듣자는 게 아니오. 나는 그게 부질없는 소리라고 보오. 그 아이가 그 일을 제대로 헤아려 보기만 한다면 곧 그를 사랑하는 법을 배울 텐데.”\n\n“그런 배움이란, 배워지는 것이라면, 어떤 스승도 없이 배워지는 것이지요.”\n\n“그러면 나를 도와주는 일은 아무것도 하지 않겠다는 거요?”\n\n“적어도 시아주버니를 그르치는 일은 아무것도 하지 않겠어요. 그리고 사실대로 말씀드리자면, 벨에게 이 일을 두고 무슨 말을 하는 것이 나을지 정하기 전에 저도 이 문제를 넉넉히 생각해 봐야겠어요. 그 애가 저한테 말하지 않은 걸 보면—”\n\n“그 아이는 제 어미한테 말했어야 하오.”\n\n“아니에요, 시아주버니. 그 애가 그이를 받아들였다면 물론 저한테 말했겠지요. 받아들일 생각이 있었다면 아마 저와 의논했을 거고요. 하지만 그이를 물리쳐야겠다고 마음을 굳혔다면—”\n\n“마음을 굳히지 말았어야지.” “하지만 굳혔다면, 아무한테도 그 이야기를 하지 않는 것이 저에게는 자연스러워 보여요. 그 애는 아마 버나드도 그 일이 알려지지 않는 편을 좋아하리라고 생각했을 거예요.”\n\n“쳇, 알려진다니! 그야 물론 알려지고말고. 생각할 시간이 필요하다니 지금은 더 말하지 않겠소. 벨이 내 딸이었다면 나는 그 아이의 앞날에 무엇이 가장 좋은지를 일러 주는 데 조금도 망설이지 않았을 거요.”\n\n“저도 망설이지 않아요. 다만 그 애의 앞날에 무엇이 가장 좋은지 마음을 정하는 데는 얼마간 망설일지도 모르지요. 하지만 시아주버니, 이것만은 믿으셔도 좋아요. 저는 그 애에게 시아주버니의 다정함과 사랑을 아주 간곡히 이야기하겠어요. 그리고 그 애를 아껴 주시는 그 마음을 제가 아주 강하게 느끼고 있다는 것도 믿어 주셨으면 해요.”\n\n이 말에 그는 그저 고개를 저으며 흥흥 헛기침만 했다. “제수씨 자신으로 말하자면, 그 아이들이 혼인하는 것을 기꺼워하겠소?” 그가 물었다.\n\n“물론 기꺼워하지요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “저는 버나드를 늘 좋아했고, 제 아이가 그이 곁에서 안전하리라고 믿어요. 하지만 아시다시피 이건 제가 좋아하고 싫어하는 것이 조금이라도 좌우해서는 안 되는 문제잖아요.”\n\n그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌고, 지주는 응접실 창을 통해 다시 밖으로 나갔다. 그는 이 만남이 절반쯤밖에 흡족하지 않았다. 그러나 그는 절반의 흡족만으로도 거의 넉넉한 사람이었다. 그는 사람들이 저에게 살갑게 굴어 주리라고 기대하는 일이 좀처럼 없었다. 데일 부인은 작은 집에 들어온 뒤로 한 번도 그에게 만족의 원천이 되어 준 적이 없었으나, 그렇다고 그녀를 그리로 데려온 것을 후회하지는 않았다. 그는 한결같은 사람이었다. 제 뜻을 밀고 나가는 데는 끈질겼으나 그러면서도 낙관하지 않았고, 모든 일이 저에게 순조로우리라고 기대하는 사람도 결코 아니었다. 그는 제 조카와 조카딸을 혼인시키기로 마음을 굳혔고, 끝내 이 일에 실패한다면 그 실패는 아마 그의 남은 삶을 쓰디쓰게 만들 것이었다. 그러나 그동안 성을 내거나, 반대에 부딪혔다고 씨근거리며 나무라는 것은 그의 됨됨이가 아니었다. 그는 데일 부인에게 벨을 몹시 사랑한다고 말했다. 정말 그러했다. 비록 그가 벨에게 각별한 정을 드러내며 말하는 일은 드물었고, 그녀에게 다정하고 부드러웠던 적은 한 번도 없었지만. 그러나 한편으로 벨이 제 뜻을 거스른다고 해서 이제 와 그녀를 덜 사랑하지도 않았다. 그는 한결같고 무뚝뚝한 사람이었으며, 생각하기보다는 곱씹는 쪽이었고, 속에 품은 것보다 입에 낸 말이 더 모질었으며, 남들이 믿는 것보다, 아니 제 자신이 아는 것보다 더 많은 마음을 지닌 사람이었다. 그러나 무엇보다도 그는 한번 바란 것은 언제까지나 바라는 사람이었다.\n\n데일 부인은 혼자 남게 되자 지주가 앞에 있을 때에 할 수 있었던 것보다 훨씬 더 넉넉하게 그 문제를 마음속에서 굴려 보기 시작했다. 이런 혼인이야말로 형편이 허락할 수 있는 것 가운데 그들 모두에게 가장 행복한 살림의 짜임이 아니겠는가? 제 딸은 지참할 재산이 없을 터이나, 재산이 줄 수 있는 온갖 안락이 이미 그 아이를 위해 마련되어 있을 것이었다. 그 아이는 버나드가 혼인해서 데려온 한 푼 없고 밑천 없는 신부로서가 아니라, 버나드의 벗들이 누구보다도 그에게 어울린다고 여겨 온 아내로서 삼촌의 집에 맞아들여질 것이었다. 그리고 데일 부인 자신에 관해 말하자면, 그런 혼인에는 그녀를 기쁘게 하지 않을 것이라고는 하나도 없었다. 그것은 앞날의 행복에 관한 그녀의 모든 꿈을 이루어 줄 것이었다.\n\n그러나 몇 번이고 스스로에게 되뇌었듯이, 그 모든 것은 셈에 넣어서는 안 되는 것이었다. 버나드의 물음에 답하는 것은 벨이라야 하고, 오직 벨뿐이라야 했다. 그녀의 마음속에서 사랑이라는 그 관념에는 무언가 거룩한 데가 있었다. 제 딸이 어떤 남자를 온전히 사랑하지도 않으면서 그와 혼인한다면, 그녀는 그 딸을 거의 몸을 버린 사람처럼 여길 것이었다. 릴리가 제 연인을 사랑하듯 온 마음과 온 힘을 다해 사랑하는 것이 아니라면 말이다.\n\n이런 확신이 이토록 강하게 그녀를 사로잡고 있었으므로, 그녀는 벨에게 무슨 말을 한들 도움이 될 것 같지 않다고 느꼈다.\n\n# 제20장. 크로프츠 선생\n\n이저벨라 데일이 이 세상에서 아주 확실하게 알고 있는 것이 하나라도 있었다면, 그것은 바로 이것이었다 — 자기는 크로프츠 선생과 사랑에 빠진 것이 아니라는 것. 사촌 버나드와 사랑에 빠졌느냐 하는 문제라면, 그 점에 관해서는 스스로에게 물음을 던져 볼 계기조차 가져 본 적이 없었다. 그를 무척 좋아하기는 했으나 그와 결혼하겠다는 생각은 해 본 적이 없었고, 이제 그가 청혼을 해 온 지금에도 그것을 마음에 담아 볼 수가 없었다. 그러나 크로프츠 선생에 관해서라면 그녀는 생각을 해 보았고, 마음을 정해 두었다 — 앞서 말한 그런 식으로.\n\n크로프츠 선생 자신에게서 그럴 만한 권한을 받은 것이 아니라면, 제 가슴속에서라도 그 문제를 따져 보는 일이 그녀에게 온당했을 리 없다고 말할 사람이 있을지도 모른다. 그렇다면 크로프츠 선생이 그녀에게 그만한 권한을 얼마쯤 주었다고 여겨 두기로 하자. 그런 일이 이루어지는 길은 한 가지만이 아니다. 그리고 그렇게 할 만한 마땅한 계기가 없었더라면, 데일 양이 스스로에게 그에 관한 물음을 던지고 있을 리도 없었을 것이다.\n\n작은 지방 도시의 의사라는 직업은 그 임자에게 젊은 시절부터 큰 수입을 안겨 주는 일이 흔치 않다. 아마 어떤 길에서도 사람이 버는 것에 견주어 이토록 고되게 일해야 하거나, 한 푼도 못 벌면서 이토록 많은 일을 해야 하는 경우는 없을 것이다. 나에게는 이따금, 젊은 의사와 늙은 의사가 저희 직업의 소출을 서로 나누어 갖기로 합의라도 한 것처럼 보였다 — 젊은 의사는 일을 죄다 하고, 늙은 의사는 돈을 죄다 가져가는 식으로. 만약 그렇다면, 의사라는 직업에 몸담은 이들 상당수가 지니고 있는 저 때 이른 근엄함도 그것으로 설명이 될 것이다. 그런 협정 아래에서라면 사람이 인생의 아주 이른 시기에 어린아이 같은 것들을 치워 버리고 싶어 한대도 나무랄 수 없는 일이다.\n\n크로프츠 선생은 이 무렵 게스트윅에서 개업한 지 거의 칠 년이 되어 가고 있었다. 스물세 살에 그 도시에 자리를 잡았으니 지금은 서른쯤 되어 있었다. 그 칠 년 동안 그의 의술과 근면은 워낙 널리 인정을 받아, 구빈 조합에 속한 극빈자 전부의 진료를 맡는 자리를 얻어 내는 데 성공했고, 그 일로 그가 받는 보수는 연 백 파운드였다. 또한 그 도시에 유지되고 있던 작은 병원의 부속 외과의였으며, 그 밖에도 비슷한 공직을 두세 개 더 맡고 있었으니, 그것들은 모두 그의 반듯함과 두루 능한 솜씨를 입증해 주는 것이었다. 게다가 그것들은 그를 게으름의 위험에서 완전히 지켜 주었다. 그러나 불행하게도, 그것들이 그로 하여금 스스로를 성공한 전문직 인사로 여기게 해 주지는 못했다. 반면에 좋게 말하는 사람이 거의 없던 늙은 그러펀 선생은 게스트윅에서 재산을 모았고, 지금까지도 이 도시의 온갖 병에서 상당한, 그리고 아직 별로 줄지도 않은 수입을 끌어내고 있었다. 자, 이것은 크로프츠 선생에게 가혹한 일이었다 — 앞서 말한 것과 같은 저 널리 이해되는 협정이 실제로 존재하는 것이 아니라면 말이다.\n\n그는 게스트윅에 자리 잡기 훨씬 전부터 데일 집안과 알고 지냈고, 그때부터 오늘에 이르기까지 그들과 아주 가깝게 지내 왔다. 데일 부인이 절친한 벗으로 꼽는 남자들 가운데, 젊은이든 늙은이든, 그녀가 가장 신뢰하고 가장 우러르는 사람이 바로 그였다. 그리고 그는 자기를 잘 아는 사람들에게 신뢰받을 만한 사람이었다. 그는 크로즈비처럼 재기가 번득이고 늘 재바르지는 않았고, 버나드 데일이 지닌 실제적이고 세속적인 분별을 다 갖추지도 못했다. 지력으로 말하자면 그가 존 임스보다 나았는지 나는 의심스럽다 — 풋내기 시절이 아주 지나가 버렸을 때의 존 임스 말이다. 그러나 크로프츠는, 지금 이 시점의 다른 세 사람과 견주자면, 그 누구보다도 더 믿을 만한 사람이었다. 게다가 그에게는 이따금 익살이 한 자락 번뜩이는 데가 있었으니, 그것이 없었더라면 데일 부인이 그를 그토록 진심으로 좋아하지는 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그것은 조용한 익살이어서, 여럿이 어울린 자리보다는 벗 하나를 앞에 두었을 때 곧잘 드러나곤 했다. 반면 크로즈비는 열두어 사람 사이에 있을 때가 단둘이 있을 때보다 훨씬 더 빛났다. 버나드 데일은 빛난 적이 없었다. 그리고 조니 임스로 말하자면 — 그러나 이 빛남이라는 문제에 관해서라면, 조니 임스는 아직 제 사람됨이 어떠한지를 세상에 보여 준 바가 없었다.\n\n크로프츠가 벗인 데일 부인을 위해 의사로서 불려 간 것은 이제 두 해 전의 일이었다. 그때 그녀는 오랫동안, 두석 달쯤 앓아누워 있었고, 크로프츠 선생은 앨링턴을 자주 드나들었다. 그 무렵 그는 데일 부인의 두 딸과 아주 가까워졌으며, 특히 큰딸과 그러했다. 결혼하지 않은 젊은 의사는 어쩌면 젊은 아가씨가 있는 집에서 배제되어야 마땅할지도 모른다. 적어도 나는 많은 슬기로운 부인네들이 그 견해를 아주 굳게 붙들고 있다는 것을 안다. 의사란 밥벌이를 시작하겠다고 나서기 전에 먼저 장가부터 들어야 한다고 여기는 것이 틀림없다. 데일 부인은 아마 제 딸들을 아직 어린애로만 여겼을 것이다. 큰딸 벨이 그때 겨우 열여덟이었으니 말이다. 아니면 이 문제에 관해 조심성 없고 정통에서 벗어난 견해를 지니고 있었는지도 모른다. 아니면 이기적이게도, 제 자신에게 온갖 위험이 따르는 그러펀 선생보다 제 아이들에게 온갖 위험이 따르는 크로프츠 선생을 더 좋아했는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 그 결과는 이러했다. 어느 날 이 젊은 의사는 게스트윅으로 말을 타고 돌아가면서, 이 세상에서 자기가 누릴 행복의 많은 부분이 데일 부인의 큰딸과 결혼할 수 있느냐에 달려 있다는 것을 스스로 깨달았다. 그때 그의 수입 전부는 연 이백 파운드가 조금 넘는 정도였고, 게스트윅의 일반 여론은 그러펀 선생을 훨씬 나은 의사로 쳐 준다는 것, 그리고 언젠가 그 의사가 몸소 진료를 보기에는 너무 늙었다는 말을 듣게 되는 날이 온다 해도 그러펀 선생의 모래빛 머리를 한 조수가 자기보다 이 도시에서 성공할 가망이 더 크리라는 것을, 그는 마음속으로 결론지어 두고 있었다. 크로프츠에게는 물려받은 재산이 없었고, 데일 양에게도 없다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 그렇다면 그런 형편에서 그가 어찌해야 한단 말인가?\n\n이 이야기가 시작되기 삼 년 전에 있었던 이 의사의 연애 대목을 우리가 길게 캐물을 필요는 없다. 그는 벨에게 아무런 고백도 하지 않았다. 그러나 벨은, 그토록 어린 나이였음에도, 용기가 그를 저버리지만 않았더라면, 아니 오히려 신중함이 그를 막지만 않았더라면 그가 기꺼이 그리했으리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 데일 부인에게는 그가 말을 하기는 했다. 그녀에게조차 제 사랑을 대놓고 밝히지는 않고 넌지시 비치기만 했으며, 그러고는 이루지 못한 희망과 직업에서 겪은 실망을 그녀에게 이야기했다. “제가 지금처럼 가난한 것을 두고 푸념하는 것은 아닙니다.” 그가 말했다. “적어도 제 가난이 저에게 불편의 씨앗이 될 만큼 그렇게 가난하지는 않습니다. 다만 지금 제가 가진 정도의 수입으로는 도무지 결혼을 할 수가 없지요.”\n\n“하지만 수입은 늘어나지 않겠어요?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“언젠가는 늘겠지요, 제가 늙은이가 되어 갈 무렵에는.” 그가 말했다. “하지만 그때 가서 그게 저에게 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까?”\n\n데일 부인으로서는, 자기 뜻이 미치는 한, 그가 지금처럼 가난하더라도, 또 그런 쥐꼬리만 한 수입으로 둘이 함께라면 갑절로 가난해질 터이지만, 얼마든지 제 딸에게 구애하고 결혼해도 좋다고 그에게 말해 줄 수가 없었다. 그는 벨의 이름을 입에 올리지도 않았고, 설령 올렸다 한들 그녀는 기다리며 희망을 품으라고 이를 수밖에 없었을 것이다. 그 뒤로 그는 그 문제에 관해 그녀에게 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 벨에게는 드러내 놓고 사랑을 말한 적이 한마디도 없었다. 그러나 어느 가을날, 데일 부인이 이미 회복기에 접어들어 그의 왕진이 더는 필요하지 않게 되었을 무렵, 그는 벨을 이끌고 반쯤 가려진 관목 숲 오솔길을 함께 걸었고, 그러고는 그녀의 마음을 제 마음에 묶어 두기를 정말로 바랐다면 결코 해서는 안 될 이야기들을 그녀에게 했다. 그는 제 수입 이야기를 되풀이했고, 자기가 가난한 것이 서러운 까닭은 오로지 그 때문에 결혼을 생각할 수 없다는 데 있노라고 설명했다. “그렇겠지요.” 벨이 말했다. “제 것과 같은 수입을 어느 부인에게 나누어 가지자고 청하는 건 잘못이라고 생각합니다.” 그가 말했다. 그러자 벨은 어떤 부인들은 제 몫의 수입을 가지고 있으니 그런 식으로 어려움을 넘길 수도 있지 않겠느냐고 말해 보았다. “돈을 가진 아가씨와 결혼한다면 저는 제 자신이 두려울 것 같습니다.” 그가 말했다. “게다가 그건 이제 아주 말도 안 되는 이야기지요.” 물론 벨은 어째서 말도 안 되는 이야기냐고 묻지 않았고, 두 사람은 한동안 말없이 걸었다. “쉽지 않은 일입니다.” 그가 이윽고 말했다. 그녀를 보는 것이 아니라 제가 딛고 선 자갈을 내려다보면서. “쉽지 않은 일이지만, 더는 그것을 생각하지 않기로 마음을 정하겠습니다. 사람은 혼자서도 결혼한 만큼 행복할 수 있다고 저는 믿습니다 — 거의는요.” “어쩌면 더 행복할지도 모르지요.” 벨이 말했다. 그러고 나서 의사는 그녀를 두고 떠났고, 벨은 앞서 내가 말한 대로 자기가 그를 사랑하는 것이 아니라고 아주 단단히 마음을 정했다. 이 세상에서 그녀가 이만큼 확신한 것은 달리 없었다고 나는 분명히 말할 수 있다.\n\n그리고 요즈음 크로프츠 선생은 앨링턴에 그리 자주 건너오지 않았다. 작은 집 식구 가운데 누가 앓았더라면 물론 그가 거기 있었을 것이다. 지주는 마을의 약제사를 썼고, 그보다 높은 도움이 필요하면 그러펀 선생을 부르러 보내곤 했다. 데일 부인의 모임이 있던 날 크로프츠는 특별히 초대를 받아 그 자리에 있었다. 그러나 데일 부인이 벗들에게 따로 초대를 보내는 일은 아주 드물었고, 그 집 사람들을 보고 싶으면 스스로 갈 구실을 만들어야 한다는 것을 의사는 잘 알고 있었다. 그러나 그는 그런 구실을 좀처럼 만들지 않았으니, 아마도 구빈원과 병원 쪽이 제게 더 어울리는 자리라고 느꼈기 때문이리라.\n\n그러나 바로 이 무렵 그는 제 직업에서 성공을 향해 아주 크고 뜻밖의 한 걸음을 내디뎠다. 어느 날 아침 그는 드 게스트 경을 진찰하러 저택으로 와 달라는 부름을 받고 몹시 놀랐다. 저택 사람들은 지난 삼십 년 동안 그러펀 선생을 써 왔던 터라, 백작의 전갈을 받은 크로프츠는 그 말을 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다. “백작님께서 많이 편찮으신 건 아닙니다요.” 하인이 말했다. “다만 되도록 만찬 전에 좀 뵈었으면 하십니다요.”\n\n“그분이 정말로 나를 보자고 하신 게 맞나?” 크로프츠가 말했다.\n\n“아, 예. 그건 틀림없습니다요, 나리.”\n\n“그러펀 선생이 아니고?”\n\n“예, 나리. 그러펀 선생이 아닙니다요. 백작님께서 그러펀 선생한테는 이제 신물이 나신 모양입니다요. 그 선생이 하루는 백작님을 주물러 대기 시작하셨거든요.”\n\n“백작님을 주물렀다니 — 손이며 발이며, 뭐 그런 것을 말인가?” 의사가 넌지시 물었다.\n\n“손이며 발이라니요!” 하인이 말했다. “어이구, 나리, 그 선생은 백작님이 아무것도 아닌 사람인 양 놀려 먹었습니다요. 저야 못 들었습니다만, 코너 부인 말로는 백작님께서 노발대발하셨답니다요.” 그리하여 크로프츠 선생은 말에 올라 게스트윅 저택으로 올라갔다.\n\n백작은 혼자였다. 레이디 줄리아는 이미 코시 성으로 떠난 뒤였다. “어서 오게, 어서 와.” 백작이 말했다. “나는 그리 아프지는 않네만, 자네한테 조언을 좀 얻고 싶어서 말이야. 아주 사소한 일이네만, 그래도 누구한테든 한번 보이는 게 좋겠다 싶었네.” 이에 크로프츠 선생은 물론 백작님을 모시게 되어 기쁘다고 말했다.\n\n“나는 자네에 대해 다 알고 있네.” 백작이 말했다. “자네 할머니 스토더드 부인은 우리 고모님의 아주 오랜 벗이었지. 레이디 제미마는 기억나지 않겠지?”\n\n“모릅니다.” 크로프츠가 말했다. “그런 영광은 누려 본 적이 없습니다.”\n\n“참으로 훌륭한 노부인이셨고, 자네 할머니 스토더드 부인을 잘 아셨네. 보게, 그러펀이 우리를 봐 준 게 도대체 몇 해나 되는지 나도 모를 지경일세만, 정말이지—” 그러고서 백작은 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“누구에게도 이롭지 않은 바람이란 없는 법이지요.” 크로프츠가 가볍게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“어쩌면 나한테는 좋은 바람을 불어 줄지도 모르지. 그러펀은 나한테 좋은 일이라곤 해 준 게 없으니 말이야. 사정은 이렇다네. 나는 아주 멀쩡해, 알겠나. 말처럼 튼튼하지.”\n\n“안색이 꽤 좋아 보이십니다.”\n\n“나보다 나은 사람은 없을 걸세 — 내 나이에는 말이지. 나는 예순일세, 알다시피.”\n\n“앓으시는 것처럼은 보이지 않습니다.”\n\n“나는 늘 바깥바람을 쐬고 사네. 그게 사람한테 제일 좋은 것이라고 나는 보네.”\n\n“운동을 넉넉히 하는 것만 한 게 없지요, 그야 물론입니다.”\n\n“그리고 나는 늘 몸을 움직인다네.” 백작이 말했다. “이 근방에서 나보다 더 고되게 일하는 사람은 없어. 그리고 말이지, 자네한테 하는 말이네만, 육칠백 에이커의 땅을 제 손으로 부치겠다고 나서면 그 땅을 잘 살펴야 하네. 손해를 볼 작정이 아니라면 말이야.”\n\n“백작님께서 훌륭한 농사꾼이시라는 말씀은 늘 들어 왔습니다.”\n\n“뭐, 그렇지. 내 땅 어디에 풀이 자란다 해도 내 발밑에서 자라지는 않네. 여섯 시에 내가 잠자리에 있는 걸 볼 일은 그리 흔치 않을 걸세, 정말이야.”\n\n이런 이야기가 오간 뒤에야 크로프츠 선생은 대체 몸의 어떤 이상 때문에 이번에 자기 도움을 청하신 것이냐고 백작에게 감히 물어보았다.\n\n“아, 그 이야기를 막 하려던 참이었네.” 백작이 말했다. “만찬을 들고 나서 잠드는 것이 아주 위험한 버릇이라고들 하더군.”\n\n“적어도 그리 드문 일은 아닙니다.” 의사가 말했다.\n\n“그렇겠지. 그런데 레이디 줄리아가 그 일로 늘 나를 들볶는다네. 그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 응접실 안락의자에 앉기만 하면 내가 너무 깊이 잠드는 것 같기는 해. 어떤 때는 누이가 나를 도무지 깨우지 못한다더군 — 적어도 저는 그렇게 말하네.”\n\n“만찬 때 식욕은 어떠십니까?”\n\n“아, 그건 아주 멀쩡하네. 나는 점심은 아예 안 먹는다는 걸 자네도 알겠지. 그래서 만찬을 아주 흡족하게 즐긴다네. 그러고 나서 포트와인을 서너 잔 마시는데—”\n\n“그러고 나면 졸리시고요?”\n\n“바로 그걸세.” 백작이 말했다.\n\n그 의사의 조언이 정확히 어떤 것이었는지까지 우리가 캐물을 필요는 아마 없을 것이다. 다만 그 조언은, 어쨌든, 백작이 그를 또 보고 싶다고 말할 만한 방식으로 주어진 것이었다.\n\n“그리고 이보게, 크로프츠 선생, 내가 지금 아주 혼자일세. 내일 건너와서 나와 함께 만찬을 들지 않겠나? 그러면 혹시 내가 잠이 들더라도 레이디 줄리아가 과장을 하는 것은 아닌지 자네가 알려 줄 수 있을 테니 말이야. 우리끼리 이야기네만, 내 — 내 코 고는 소리에 대해 저가 하는 말을 나는 다 믿지는 않는다네.”\n\n만찬 자리에서 의사의 눈길 아래 백작이 식욕을 억눌렀기 때문인지, 아니면 그를 위해 마련하도록 이른 한낮의 양갈비가 바라던 효과를 냈기 때문인지, 아니면 의사의 이야기가 레이디 줄리아의 이야기보다 더 활기찼기 때문인지는 우리가 말하지 않겠다. 어쨌든 그날 저녁 백작은 의기양양했다. 만찬 뒤 안락의자에 앉아 있는 동안 그는 한두 번밖에 눈을 껌뻑이지 않았고, 여느 때 같으면 반쯤 졸린 채로 삼키던 커다란 사발의 차를 다 마시고 났을 때에도 아주 생기가 돌았다.\n\n“아, 그렇군.” 그가 벌떡 일어나 눈을 비비며 말했다. “몸이 한결 가벼워진 것 같네. 나는 만찬 뒤에 한숨 자는 걸 즐기지. 정말 그래. 그게 좋아. 하지만 그러고 나서 잠자리에 들 때가 되면, 마치 무슨 죄라도 지은 사람처럼 슬금슬금 기어드는 꼴이 되거든! 그리고 우리 누이는 의자에서 조는 것을 죄악이라고, 말 그대로 죄라고 여긴다네. 저가 조는 걸 붙잡은 사람은 아무도 없지! 그건 그렇고, 크로프츠 선생, 버나드 데일이 앨링턴에 데려온 그 크로즈비 씨라는 사람을 자네도 아나? 레이디 줄리아가 지금 그 사람과 같은 집에 묵고 있다네.”\n\n“데일 부인 댁에서 한 번 만난 적이 있습니다.”\n\n“그 집 딸 가운데 하나와 결혼한다지, 아마?”\n\n이에 크로프츠 선생은 크로즈비 씨가 릴리언 데일과 약혼했다고 설명해 주었다.\n\n“아, 그래. 참한 아가씨라고들 하더군. 자네도 알겠지만 그 데일 집안 사람들은 죄다 우리 인척일세. 내 누이 패니가 그 애들의 삼촌 올랜도한테 시집을 갔거든. 내 매부는 여행을 좋아하지 않아서 나도 그를 자주 보지는 못하네만, 그래도 물론 그 집안 일에는 관심이 있지.”\n\n“그분들은 저와도 아주 오랜 벗입니다.” 크로프츠가 말했다.\n\n“그렇겠지. 딸이 둘이지, 아마?”\n\n“예, 둘입니다.”\n\n“그리고 릴리 양이 동생이고. 언니 쪽은 결혼 이야기가 없나?”\n\n“그런 이야기는 못 들었습니다.”\n\n“그 애도 아주 예쁘장한 아가씨지. 작년에 제 삼촌 집에서 본 기억이 나네. 사촌인 버나드와 결혼한다 해도 나는 놀라지 않겠어. 알다시피 그 재산은 버나드가 물려받을 것이고, 그 애는 내 조카이니 말이야.”\n\n“사촌끼리 결혼하는 것이 좋은 일인지 저는 잘 모르겠습니다.” 크로프츠가 말했다.\n\n“다들 곧잘 그러지 않나. 그리고 그게 집안 사정에 맞아떨어지기도 하지. 데일도 그 애들을 어떻게든 건사해 줘야 할 텐데, 그렇게 하면 아무 수고 없이 하나를 제 손에서 덜어 내는 셈이거든.”\n\n크로프츠 선생은 이 일을 그런 눈으로 보지는 않았으나, 백작과 그것을 꼬치꼬치 따지고 싶지는 않았다. “동생 쪽은” 하고 그가 말했다. “스스로 건사한 셈이지요.”\n\n“뭐라, 남편을 하나 얻어서 말인가? 하지만 데일이 그 애한테 무엇이든 좀 떼 줘야 할 걸세. 아직 혼인한 것도 아니고, 내가 듣기로는 그 친구가 만만찮은 사람일지도 모르거든. 늙은 데일이 그 애한테 뭘 좀 주지 않으면 그자는 그 애와 결혼하지 않을 걸세. 두고 보게, 정말 그런지. 코시 성에 활시위를 하나 더 걸어 두었다는 말을 나는 들었네.”\n\n이윽고 크로프츠는 말을 타고 집으로 돌아갔다. 머지않아 다시 와서 백작과 만찬을 들겠다고 약속한 뒤였다.\n\n“그맘때 와 주면 나한테는 아주 좋겠네.” 백작이 말했다. “그리고 자네는 총각이니 아마 개의치 않겠지. 목요일 일곱 시에 오겠나? 조심해서 가게. 칠흑같이 어두우니 말이야. 존, 가서 크로프츠 선생께 첫 번째 문을 열어 드려라.” 그러고 나서 백작은 잠자리로 물러갔다.\n\n크로프츠는 말을 타고 집으로 돌아오는 내내 앨링턴의 두 아가씨 생각을 마음에서 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. “늙은 데일이 그 애한테 뭘 좀 주지 않으면 그자는 그 애와 결혼하지 않을 걸세.” 세상이 이 지경에 이르렀단 말인가, 남자가 한 여인과 맺은 약혼을 지키려면 뇌물을 받아야만 한다는 지경에? 사람들 사이에 낭만이라고는 남아 있지 않단 말인가 — 기사도의 마음이라고는? “코시 성에 활시위를 하나 더 걸어 두었다지.” 백작은 그렇게 말했고, 그리 말하면서도 조금도 놀라는 기색이 아니었다. 요즈음 남자들이 여자에 대해 말하는 투가 이러했다. 그런데도 그 자신은 제가 사랑하는 아가씨에게 그토록 외경을 느꼈고, 세상살이의 처지에서 그녀를 해치게 될까 봐 그토록 두려워한 나머지, 그녀를 사랑한다는 말조차 감히 하지 못했던 것이다.\n\n# 제21장. 존 임스가 두 가지 모험을 만나 그 둘 다에서 대단한 용기를 보이다\n\n릴리는 연인의 편지가 편지로서 갖추어야 할 것을 모두 갖추었다고 생각했다. 코시와 앨링턴 사이에 우편이 어떤 길로 오가는지 그녀는 잘 알지 못했고, 그래서 바로 첫날에 편지가 오지 않았을 때에도 그리 몹시 실망하지는 않았다. 그렇지만 그녀는 그날 오전 중에 우체국까지 걸어 내려갔다. 편지가 거기 남아 있는 것은 아닌지 확인해 두고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n“아니 아가씨, 편지는 죄다 배달됐습니다요. 아시면서 그러시네.” 우체국을 맡아 보는 크럼프 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그래도 하나쯤 남겨질 수 있잖아요.”\n\n“우편배달부 존이 바로 오늘 아가씨 어머님께 신문을 갖다 드리러 댁까지 올라갔습니다요. 사람들이 편지를 안 쓰는 걸 저더러 만들어 내라 하실 수야 없지 않습니까요.”\n\n“그래도 가끔은 남겨지잖아요, 크럼프 부인. 그이도 온 거리에 다른 사람 것이 하나도 없으면 편지 한 통 때문에 올라오지는 않을 테니까요.”\n\n“아니고말고요, 그럴 때도 올라갑니다요. 저는 편지 한 통도 여기 두고 가게 놔두지 않습니다요. 신문도 마찬가지고요. 편지 때문에 여기까지 내려오셔야 아무 소용 없습니다요, 릴리 아가씨. 그분이 안 쓰시는 걸 제가 쓰게 만들 수는 없으니까요.” 그리하여 가엾은 릴리는 마음이 언짢은 채로 집에 돌아갔다.\n\n그러나 이튿날 아침에 편지가 왔고, 모든 것이 제자리를 찾았다. 그녀의 판단으로 그 편지에는 모자란 것이 없었다. 넉넉함으로 보나 애정으로 보나 그러했다. 마지막 순간에 그녀와 헤어지는 괴로움을 피하려고 일찍 떠날 궁리를 했노라고 그가 털어놓은 대목에서 그녀는 미소를 지으며 종이를 눌러 쥐었고, 그 술수에서 자기가 그를 이겼다는 사실에 속으로 흡족해했다. 그러고는 마지막 순간에 그녀가 곁에 있어 주어 기뻤노라고 적힌 낱말들에 입을 맞추었다. 코시에서보다 앨링턴에서 더 행복했다고 그가 잘라 말했을 때 그녀는 그 말을 고스란히 믿었고, 그러하다는 것이 기뻤다. 그리고 그가 스스로를 세속적이라고 나무랐을 때에는 그를 감싸며, 그가 이 점에서도 다른 점에서와 마찬가지로 거의 나무랄 데 없다고 자신을 설득했다. 런던에 살면서 세상에 나가 밥벌이를 해야 하는 남자라면 시골 처녀보다 세속적일 수밖에 없다. 그러나 그런 그가 그런 처녀를 사랑할 수 있다는 것, 그런 처녀를 아내로 고를 수 있다는 것—그것만으로도 세상이 그를 노예로 삼지 못했다는 충분한 증거가 아니겠는가? “내 마음은 앨링턴의 잔디밭에 있소.” 그가 그렇게 적어 놓았고, 그 낱말들을 읽으면서 그녀는 다시 한번 종이에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n그녀의 눈에도, 귀에도, 마음에도 그 편지는 아름다운 편지였다. 나는 제대로 된 연애편지 한 통이 처녀에게 주는 것보다 더 큰 기쁨은 없다고 믿는다. 그 편지를 받는 것이 아무 잘못도 아님을 아는 처녀, 제 처지를 의식하는 데서 오는 옅은 홍조 말고는 아무것도 없이 아버지와 어머니 앞에서 그것을 뜯어 볼 수 있는 처녀에게 말이다. 그리고 모든 연애편지 가운데 첫 편지가 가장 달콤할 수밖에 없다! 낱말 하나하나에 얼마나 큰 값이 들어 있는가! 표현 하나하나를 얼마나 뜯어보고 얼마나 좋은 쪽으로 새기는가! 머지않아 그저 흔한 문구가 되어 으레 쓰이고 말 그 자잘한 말마디들에 얼마나 큰 무게가 실리는가. 크로즈비는 하느님의 축복을 빌며 편지를 맺었다. “당신에게도요.” 릴리는 편지를 가슴에 꼭 누르며 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“뭐 특별한 말이라도 있니?” 데일 부인이 물었다.\n\n“네, 엄마. 온통 특별한 말인걸요.”\n\n“하지만 남들 귀에 들려줄 만한 건 없다는 게로구나.”\n\n“엄마랑 언니한테 사랑을 보낸대요.”\n\n“그것 참 고맙기도 하지.”\n\n“고마워하셔야죠. 그리고 바체스터를 지나는 길에 교회에 갔는데, 거기 목사님이 그 레이디 덤벨로의 외할아버지였대요. 그런데 코시 성에 닿아 보니 레이디 덤벨로가 거기 와 있었고요.”\n\n“참 묘한 우연이로구나!” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“편지 얘기는 이제 한마디도 더 안 할래요.” 릴리가 말했다. 그러고서 그녀는 편지를 접어 주머니에 넣었다. 그러나 제 방에 혼자 있게 되자마자 그것을 다시 꺼내어 예닐곱 번은 되풀이해 읽었다.\n\n그것이 그녀의 오전 일과였다. 그것과, 크로즈비 씨의 몸을 꾸미는 데 쓰일 예정인 아주 공들인 바느질 한 가지가. 그렇지만 그녀의 손은 일감으로 가득했다. 아니, 가득하게 만들 작정이었다. 혼인해서 새집으로 갈 때 그녀는 제 부지런함과 알뜰함이 낳은 온갖 살림살이를 가지고 갈 참이었다. 장차 남편이 될 사람을 위해 무언가를 하고 싶다고 그녀는 이미 선언해 두었고, 그 무언가를 당장 시작할 참이었다. 그리고 이 일에서 그녀는 스스로에게 한 약속을 저버리지 않았고, 좋은 뜻이 이루어지지 못한 채 김빠지도록 내버려 두지도 않았다. 그가 떠난 직후에 스스로를 달래며 수놓던 그 실내화보다 훨씬 고된 일감들을 그녀는 이내 제 둘레에 쌓아 놓았다. 그리고 데일 부인과 벨도—두 사람은 점잖은 방식으로 그녀를 놀리기는 했지만—그래도 그녀와 함께 일했다. 자기들의 귀염둥이가 아내로서 제자리를 차지하러 갈 때 크로즈비의 집이 텅 비어 있지 않도록, 긴 일감을 붙들고 엄숙하게 앉아서.\n\n그러나 그 편지에는 반드시 답장을 해야 했다. 그날 우편이 앨링턴에서 코시 성으로 그녀의 편지를 실어 가지 않은 채 지나가게 두는 것은 그녀 눈에는 큰 죄였을 터인데, 그런 죄를 지어 보고 싶은 마음은 조금도 들지 않았다. 제 자그마한 탁자 앞에 앉아 말끔한 문갑과 편지 쓰는 데 필요한 자잘한 도구들을 앞에 놓고, 정말로 할 말이 많은 편지를 쓸 수 있다고 느끼는 것은 그녀에게 더없는 기쁨이었다. 여태껏 그녀의 편지 왕래는 재미없고 알맹이도 거의 없다시피 했다. 어머니와 언니에게서는 아직 떨어져 지내 본 적이 거의 없었고, 다른 벗들이 있기는 했으나 우편으로 전할 만한 이야기가 그리 많았던 적도 별로 없었다. 게스트윅의 메리 임스에게 무슨 소식을 전한들 쓰는 사람 스스로 설렐 만한 것이 있었겠는가? 존 임스에게 편지를 써서 “존에게”라고 적고 엄마가 몇 시에 차를 들러 오시면 좋겠다고 하신다고 전할 때, 그 쪽지가 받는 사람에게는 더없이 귀한 보물이 되었을지언정 그것을 쓰는 일은 그녀에게 대수로울 것이 없었다.\n\n그러나 이번에는 사정이 아주 달랐다. 눈앞의 종이에 “사랑하는 어돌퍼스에게”라는 낱말이 적힌 것을 보았을 때 그녀는 그 무게에 스스로 흠칫했다. “넉 달 전만 해도 나는 이이의 이름조차 들어 본 적이 없었는데.” 그녀는 거의 경외에 가까운 마음으로 혼잣말을 했다. 그런데 이제 그는 언니보다도 어머니보다도 그녀에게 더 큰 사람이고 더 가까운 사람이었다! 그녀는 그가 작은 집에 처음 오던 날 등 뒤에서 그를 두고 웃으며 멋쟁이라 불렀던 일을 떠올렸고, 런던에서 온 낯선 이와 함께 나가 산책하라는 말을 들었을 때 순진하게도 제 딴에는 가장 예뻐 보이려고 애썼던 일도 떠올렸다. 이제 그는 더 이상 낯선 이가 아니라 그녀의 가장 소중한 벗이었다.\n\n이 모든 것을 곰곰 생각하려고 그녀는 펜을 내려놓았다가—결코 처음 있는 일은 아니었다—편지를 다 쓰기도 전에 우편배달부가 마을에 들어서지나 않을까 겁이 난 사람처럼 흠칫 놀라 다시 펜을 들었다. “사랑하는 어돌퍼스에게, 오늘 아침 당신의 편지를 받아 들었을 때 제가 얼마나 기뻤는지는 굳이 말씀드리지 않아도 되겠지요.” 그러나 그녀의 편지를 여기에 다 옮겨 적지는 않겠다. 그녀에게는 전할 사건이랄 것이 없었고, 크로즈비 씨가 바체스터에서 하딩 씨를 만난 일만큼 흥미로운 일조차 없었다. 그녀는 레이디 덤벨로를 만난 적도 없었고, 벗으로서 한마디 칭찬해 줄 만한 레이디 알렉산드리나에 견줄 사람도 없었다. 존 임스가 크로즈비 씨의 마음에 드는 사람이 아님을 알았으므로 그녀는 그 이름을 적지 않았고, 그렇다고 존 임스에 대해 이미 하지 않은 이야기가 남아 있는 것도 아니었다. 그가 앨링턴에 건너오겠다고 약속하기는 했으나, 릴리가 크로즈비에게 첫 편지를 쓸 때까지 그 방문은 아직 이루어지지 않고 있었다. 그것은 달콤하고 착하고 정직한 연애편지였다. 변치 않을 애정과 끝없는 믿음을 다짐하는 말로 가득했고, 코시 성의 높으신 분들을 두고 조용히 조금 놀리기도 했으며, 그의 편지를 늘 받을 수만 있다면, 그리고 성탄절에 그를 만나리라는 희망으로 살 수만 있다면 행복하고 만족하겠노라는 약속으로 끝을 맺었다.\n\n“늦지 않았죠, 크럼프 부인?” 우체국으로 들어서며 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“늦기는요. 앞으로 반 시간은 넉넉합니다요. 배달부 그 사람은 아직 술집에서 엉덩이도 안 뗐습니다요. 그냥 통에다 넣으시구려, 예?”\n\n“그래도 여기 그냥 두시진 않을 거죠?”\n\n“그냥 두다니요! 별말씀을 다 들어 봅니다요. 넣기가 겁나시면 도로 가져가시구려. 그뿐입니다요, 릴리 아가씨.” 그러고서 크럼프 부인은 빨래통 앞의 제 볼일로 돌아섰다. 크럼프 부인은 성미가 고약했으나 아마 그럴 만한 사정이 조금은 있었을 것이다. 그녀의 우체국에 들어오는 편지 거의 한 통마다 따로 그녀의 시간을 빼앗는 일이 생겼는데, 그 모든 일을 하고도 받는 삯이라고는—그녀가 이웃들에게 넌더리를 내며 자주 하던 말로—“하루에 이 펜스하고 한 파딩”이 고작이었다. “그걸로는 신발 가죽값도 안 나옵니다요. 암, 안 나오고말고요.” 그런데 크럼프 부인은 한 달에 한 번 교회에 갈 때 말고는 제 집 밖으로 나서는 것을 아무도 본 적이 없었으니, 신발 가죽에 대한 이 뒤엣말은 도무지 사실일 수가 없었다.\n\n임스가 약속한 앨링턴 방문을 하기까지 릴리는 또 한 통의 편지를 받았고 답장까지 해 두었다. 기억하시겠지만 그에게도 오간 편지가 있었다. 그는 로퍼 양의 편지에 답장을 했고, 그 뒤로 두 가지를 겁내며 지내고 있었다. 어밀리아에게서 무시무시한 반박이 날아올까 하는 것이 작은 겁이었고, 그 연인이 더 무시무시하게 찾아올까 하는 것이 큰 겁이었다. 그녀가 정말로 게스트윅의 그의 집에 들이닥쳐 어머니와 누이 앞에서 자기가 그의 약혼녀라고 선언한다면, 그때 그에게 무슨 달아날 길이 남아 있겠는가? 그러나 그녀는 아직 그러지 않았고, 그의 모진 편지에 답장조차 하지 않고 있었다.\n\n“저런 여자를 겁내다니 나도 참 얼간이지!” 앨링턴으로 가는 길 위에 그늘을 드리운 게스트윅 저택의 느릅나무 아래를 걸으며 그는 혼잣말을 했다. 집에 돌아온 뒤 처음으로 앨링턴에 건너갔을 때 그는 말에 올라 박차를 번쩍이며 나섰고, 옷과 장갑의 광채에 은근히 기대를 걸었더랬다. 그러나 그때 그는 릴리의 약혼에 대해 아무것도 모르고 있었다. 이제 그는 걸어가는 것으로 만족했고, 어머니 집 복도에서 챙 늘어진 모자와 지팡이를 집어 들 때에도 제 겉모양에는 도무지 무심했다. 그는 길을 따라 빠르게 걸었다. 처음 삼 마일은 게스트윅 느릅나무 그늘을 골라 걸었고, 백작의 울짱 바깥을 따라 뻗은 널찍한 잔디밭에 발을 두었다. “저런 여자를 겁내다니 나도 참 얼간이지!” 그러고서 그는 굵은 지팡이를 휘둘러 여기저기 나무를 치고 길에 놓인 돌을 쳐 내면서 진지하게 스스로를 따져 묻기 시작했고, 세상에서의 제 처지가 부끄러워졌다. “무슨 일이 있어도 그 여자와 혼인하지는 않겠어.” 그는 말했다. “열두 건의 소송을 걸어 온대도 안 해. 내가 그 여자와 혼인할 생각이 한 번도 없었다는 걸 그 여자도 나만큼 잘 알아. 처음부터 끝까지 속임수야. 여기까지 내려온다면 어머니 앞에서 그렇게 말해 주겠어.” 그러나 그녀가 불쑥 들이닥치는 모습이 눈앞에 떠오르자 그는 아직도 그녀를 몹시 두려워하고 있음을 스스로 인정했다. 그는 그녀를 사랑한다고 말한 적이 있었다. 그만큼은 글로도 적었다. 그만큼을 두고 따진다면 그는 제 죄를 고백하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그러다가 차츰 그의 마음은 어밀리아 로퍼에게서 릴리 데일에게로 옮겨 갔는데, 이쪽이라 해서 저쪽보다 딱히 더 즐거운 앞날을 보여 주지는 않았다. 그는 런던으로 돌아가기 전에 앨링턴에 들르겠다고 말해 두었고, 이제 그 약속을 지키는 참이었다. 그러나 자기가 왜 그리로 가야 하는지는 알 수 없었다. 데일 부인의 응접실에 말없이 주눅 든 채 앉아, 이제는 누구에게든 감추어야 마땅한 그 비밀을 몸가짐으로 고백하고 말리라는 느낌이 들었다. 그는 릴리 앞에서 편히 이야기할 수도 없었고, 그녀 앞에 있을 때 오로지 머릿속을 채울 그 한 가지 이야깃거리를 그녀에게 꺼낼 수도 없었다. 정말로 그녀를 혼자 만나게 된다면— 그러나 어쩌면 그것이 어떤 경우보다도 그에게 더 나쁠지도 몰랐다.\n\n그가 응접실로 안내되어 들어갔을 때 거기에는 아무도 없었다. “조금 전까지 세 분 다 여기 계셨습니다요.” 하녀가 말했다. “정원 쪽으로 내려가 보시면 누구든 꼭 만나실 겁니다요, 존 도련님.” 그리하여 존 임스는 조금 망설이다가 정원으로 내려갔다.\n\n먼저 그는 산책로를 빙 돌았으나 아무도 만나지 못했다. 그러고는 잔디밭을 가로질러 다시 저쪽 끝으로 돌아왔는데, 거기서 큰 집으로 통하는 좁은 길에서 나오던 릴리와 마주쳤다. 그녀는 혼자였다. “어머, 존.” 그녀가 말했다. “안녕하세요? 집에서 아무도 못 만나셨나 봐요. 엄마하고 언니는 홉킨스랑 저쪽 큰 채마밭에 가 있어요.”\n\n“약속을 했으니까 그냥 건너와 봤어요.” 임스가 말했다. “런던으로 돌아가기 전에 오겠다고 했잖아요.”\n\n“다들 무척 반가워하실 거예요. 저도 반갑고요. 저쪽 밭으로 두 분을 찾아가 볼까요? 아, 걸어오셨으면 힘드시겠네요.”\n\n“걷긴 걸었어요.” 임스가 말했다. “그렇다고 아주 힘든 건 아니고요.” 그러나 사실 그는 데일 부인을 찾아가고 싶지 않았다. 그러면서도 릴리와 함께 남아 무슨 말을 해야 할지는 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 떠나기 전에 그녀와 단둘이 이야기할 기회가 있었으면 좋겠다고 그는 마음속으로 그려 보았더랬다. 그녀가 혼인한 여인이 되기 전에 갖게 될 마지막 만남을 무언가 특별하게 써 보고 싶었던 것이다. 그런데 이제 그 기회가 눈앞에 있는데도 그것을 붙잡을 엄두가 좀처럼 나지 않았다.\n\n“저녁 잡숫고 가세요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“아니요, 그건 안 되겠어요. 어머니께 꼭 돌아오겠다고 다짐하고 나왔거든요.”\n\n“이렇게 먼 길을 걸어서 저희를 보러 와 주시다니 정말 고마워요. 정말로 힘들지 않으시다면 엄마한테 가 보면 좋겠어요. 못 뵈면 무척 서운해하실 테니까요.”\n\n그녀가 이렇게 말한 것은 존 임스에 대해 크로즈비가 당부했던 말이 그 순간 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그러나 존은 하려고 온 그 말을 하기로 마음먹은 터였고, 릴리가 이렇게 함께 있으니 운이 그에게 준 기회를 쓰기로 작정한 터였다.\n\n“지주 어른 정원으로는 들어가고 싶지 않아요.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“크리스토퍼 삼촌은 거기 안 계세요. 어디 농장 쪽에 계실 거예요.”\n\n“괜찮으시다면, 릴리, 저는 여기 있을게요. 곧 돌아오시겠지요. 물론 런던으로 가기 전에 두 분도 뵙고 싶어요. 하지만 릴리, 오늘 제가 건너온 건 무엇보다 릴리를 보러 온 거예요. 약속하라고 하신 것도 릴리였잖아요.”\n\n크로즈비가 했던 그 말들이 옳았던 것일까? 오랜 벗에게 다정하고 친절하게 대하려 했던 그 자그마한 노력이 그녀의 경솔함이었을까? “응접실로 들어갈까요?” 그녀가 말했다. 정원의 덤불과 오솔길 사이보다는 거기가 얼마쯤은 더 안전하리라고 느꼈던 것이다. 그리고 나는 이 점에서 그녀가 옳았다고 생각한다. 응접실 네 벽의 얌전한 예절 앞에서라면 입이 굳어 버렸을 남자도 라일락과 장미 사이에서는 사랑을 이야기하는 법이다. 존 임스에게도 그와 비슷한 느낌이 있었으니, 그는 될 수만 있다면 정원에 남아 있기로 마음먹었다.\n\n“릴리가 원하시는 게 아니라면 저는 들어가고 싶지 않아요.” 그가 말했다. “아니, 여기 있는 편이 좋겠어요. 그래서, 릴리, 혼인을 하신다고요?” 그러고서 그는 대뜸 하려던 이야기의 한복판으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n“네.” 그녀가 말했다. “그렇게 될 것 같아요.”\n\n“축하한다는 말을 아직 못 했네요.”\n\n“마음으로는 벌써 해 주신 걸 저는 잘 알고 있었어요. 언제나 제가 잘되기를 바라 주신다는 걸 늘 믿었으니까요.”\n\n“정말 그래요. 그리고 누군가를 축하한다는 것이 그 사람이 늘 행복하기를 바라는 것이라면, 저는 정말로 축하해요. 하지만 릴리—” 그러고서 그는 말을 멈추었다. 그로 하여금 그녀를 사랑하지 않을 수 없게 만들었던 그 아름다움과 순결함과 여인다운 기품 앞에서 기가 죽은 것이다.\n\n“하시려는 말씀은 다 알 것 같아요. 제가 존을 가장 좋은 벗으로 헤아리고 있다는 걸 알기 위해 여느 말이 필요하지는 않아요.”\n\n“아니에요, 릴리. 제가 하려는 말을 다 아시지는 못해요. 제가 얼마나 자주, 얼마나 많이 릴리를 생각했는지, 얼마나 애틋하게 사랑했는지 릴리는 한 번도 몰랐어요.”\n\n“존, 지금 그런 말씀은 하시면 안 돼요.”\n\n“말하지 않고는 갈 수가 없어요. 제가 여기 왔을 때, 데일 부인께서 릴리가 그 사람과 혼인하게 됐다고 말씀하셨을 때—”\n\n“크로즈비 씨를 그런 식으로 말씀하시면 안 돼요.” 그녀가 거의 사납게 그를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n“릴리 앞에서 그분을 함부로 말할 뜻은 없었어요. 그랬다면 저 자신이 미울 거예요. 물론 릴리야 다른 누구보다 그분이 좋으시겠지요?”\n\n“세상 누구보다 그분을 사랑해요.”\n\n“그리고 저도 세상 누구보다 릴리를 사랑해요.” 그렇게 말하면서 그는 자리에서 일어나 그녀 앞에 섰다. “제가 얼마나 가난한지, 얼마나 릴리에게 어울리지 않는지 저도 알아요. 그분과 약혼하지만 않으셨다면 지금 이런 말을 하지도 않았을 거예요. 물론 릴리가 저 같은 사람을 받아 주실 리는 없지요. 하지만 저는 릴리가 기억하는 그때부터 줄곧 릴리를 사랑해 왔고, 이제 그분의 아내가 되신다고 하니 그렇다는 말을 하지 않을 수가 없어요. 릴리는 런던에 가서 사시겠지요. 하지만 제가 거기서 릴리를 뵙는 일은 없을 거예요. 저는 그 사람 집에는 들어갈 수 없어요.”\n\n“아, 존.”\n\n“아니요, 절대로요. 릴리가 그분의 아내가 되신다면 절대로. 저도 그분만큼 릴리를 사랑했어요. 데일 부인께 그 말을 들었을 때 저는 쓰러지는 줄 알았어요. 릴리에게 말을 할 수가 없어서 뵙지도 않고 가 버렸고요. 저는 바보짓을 했고, 여태 줄곧 바보였어요. 이런 말을 하는 지금도 바보짓이지만 어쩔 수가 없어요.”\n\n“정말로 사랑할 수 있는 아가씨를 만나시면 다 잊어버리실 거예요.”\n\n“그럼 제가 릴리를 정말로 사랑한 게 아니었나요? 뭐, 됐어요. 하려던 말은 했으니 이제 가겠어요. 언젠가 시골에서 함께 있게 되면 어쩌면 다시 뵐지도 모르죠. 하지만 런던에서는 절대로요. 잘 있어요, 릴리.” 그러고서 그는 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n“엄마 오실 때까지 안 계실 거예요?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“네. 어머님께, 그리고 벨에게 제 안부를 전해 줘요. 두 분은 다 아시니까요. 제가 왜 갔는지도 아실 거예요. 혹시라도 무슨 일이든 해 줄 사람이 필요하거든, 제가 하겠다는 걸 기억해 주세요. 그게 무엇이든지요.” 그리고 그가 잔디밭을 가로질러 그녀에게서 멀어져 갈 때, 그녀를 위한 그 특별한 봉사로 그의 마음이 향한 것—그녀를 위해 그가 무엇보다 하고 싶어 하던 그 한 가지—은 크로즈비의 몸에 매질을 가하는 일이었다. 크로즈비가 그녀를 학대해 주기만 한다면, 혼인 전에 무슨 야만스러운 짓으로 그녀를 학대해 주기만 한다면, 그리고 그 억울함을 갚아 줄 사람으로 자기가 불려 나가기만 한다면! 그리고 게스트윅으로 향한 길을 되짚어 가면서 그는 제 가슴속에 공중누각을 하나 지어 올렸는데, 그 안에서 릴리 데일이 맡은 몫을 알았더라면 그녀는 결코 그에게 고마워하지 않았을 것이다.\n\n릴리는 혼자 남게 되자 울음을 터뜨렸다. 그 가엾은 구혼자를 부추길 만한 말은 분명 거의 하지 않았고, 그 만남 동안 몸가짐도 크로즈비조차 도무지 못마땅해할 수 없을 만큼 지켰다. 그러나 임스가 가 버리고 나자 그녀의 마음은 그에게 몹시 애틋해졌다. 그녀는 자기도 그를 사랑한다고 느꼈다. 크로즈비를 사랑하는 것과는 조금도 같지 않았으나, 그래도 애틋하고 부드럽고 참된 사랑이었다. 그 순간 그녀의 생각을 크로즈비가 모두 알 수 있었다면 과연 그것을 좋아했을지 나는 의심스럽다. 그녀는 울음을 터뜨렸고, 그러고는 어머니와 벨이 돌아왔을 때 눈에 띄지 않을 만한 구석으로 서둘러 숨어 들어갔다.\n\n임스는 지팡이를 휘두르고 먼지를 발로 차 올리며 아주 조용히 제 길을 갔는데, 가슴은 방금 지나간 장면으로 가득했다. 그는 스스로에게 화가 났다. 제 몫을 서투르게 해냈다고, 그녀에게 거칠게 굴었다고, 사랑을 말하면서 제 생각만 했다고 스스로를 나무랐다. 그리고 그녀에게도 화가 났다. 세상 누구보다 크로즈비를 사랑한다고 그녀가 그에게 잘라 말했기 때문이다. 그녀가 그럴 수밖에 없다는 것을, 적어도 그러하리라 여기는 것이 당연하다는 것을 그도 알고 있었다. 그래도, 하고 그는 생각했다. 하필 그 말을 자기에게 하지는 않을 수도 있지 않았는가. “저 사람은 지금 나를 업신여기기로 한 거야.” 그는 혼잣말을 했다. “하지만 차라리 그자를 업신여겼더라면 좋았을 것을 하고 바랄 날이 올지도 모르지.” 크로즈비가 못되고 나쁘고 이기적인 사람이라는 것을 그는 굳게 믿고 있었다. 그자가 그녀를 함부로 대하고 그녀를 비참하게 만들리라고 확신했다. 그자가 그녀와 혼인이나 할지도 조금은 의심스러웠는데, 그 의심에서 그는 한 조각 위안을 끌어내려 애썼다. 크로즈비가 그녀를 버린다면, 그리고 그 버림 때문에 제 주먹으로 그자를 때려죽일 특권이 자기에게 주어진다면, 그때는 세상이 아주 텅 비지는 않으리라. 이 모든 생각에서 그는 릴리에게 몹시 잔인했다. 그러나 릴리도 그에게 몹시 잔인하지 않았던가?\n\n그가 아직 이런 것들을 생각하고 있을 때 그는 게스트윅 목초지의 첫머리에 이르렀다. 백작 영지의 경계는 아주 뚜렷하게 표시되어 있었으니, 거기서부터 길가에 그늘을 드리우는 느릅나무와, 걷는 사람에게나 말 탄 사람에게나 똑같이 고마운 널찍하고 푸른 잔디 가장자리가 함께 시작되었기 때문이다. 임스는 어느새 잔디 위로 올라서 있었으나 생각에 파묻힌 나머지 제 발밑이 바뀐 줄도 몰랐는데, 그때 옆 밭에서 나는 사람 목소리와 황소가 크게 우는 소리에 화들짝 놀랐다. 드 게스트 경이 아끼는 소 떼가 거기 있다는 것을 그는 알고 있었고, 그중에는 백작이 큰 값어치가 있다고 여기며 유난히 마음에 들어 하는 황소가 한 마리 있었다. 그 고장 사람들은 그 짐승이 사납다고들 했으나, 드 게스트 경은 저한테는 한 번도 사나웠던 적이 없다고 자랑하는 말을 자주 했다. “애들이 저 녀석을 놀려 대고, 어른들은 애들보다 더하지.” 백작은 그렇게 말하곤 했다. “하지만 저 녀석은 저를 해치지 않은 사람은 결코 해치지 않네.” 제 가르침에 대한 믿음에 이끌려 백작은 제 황소를 크고 뿔 달린, 순한 양 떼의 어린양처럼 여기도록 스스로를 길들여 놓았던 것이다.\n\n임스가 길에 멈춰 섰을 때 그는 백작의 목소리를 알아들은 듯싶었는데, 그것은 위급에 처한 사람의 목소리였다. 이어 황소의 울부짖음이 아주 또렷하게, 거의 코앞에서 귀에 울렸다. 그 소리를 듣자 그는 문 쪽으로 달려가 제가 무엇을 하는지 별로 생각도 하지 않은 채 문을 훌쩍 뛰어넘어 밭 안으로 몇 걸음 들어섰다.\n\n“여보게!” 백작이 외쳤다. “사람이 있구먼. 이리 오게.” 그러고 나서 이어진 그의 외침은 알아들을 만한 말이 되지도 못했다. 그러나 임스는 그가 큰 위급과 압박 속에서 도움을 청하고 있다는 것만은 분명히 알아들었다. 황소는 제 주인에게 짧게짧게 달려들고 있었는데, 달려들 때마다 백작님을 뿔로 들이받고야 말겠다는 기세였다. 그리고 그때마다 백작은 몇 걸음씩 재빨리 물러섰으나, 물러설 때에도 늘 적을 마주 본 채였고, 짐승이 가까이 오면 손에 든 긴 잡초 괭이로 그 낯짝을 찔러 댔다. 그런데 그렇게 물러서느라 그는 문 쪽으로 곧게 길을 잡지 못했고, 자칫 황소가 그를 산울타리에 몰아붙이지나 않을까 하는 큰 위험이 있었다. “이리 오게!” 백작이 외쳤다. 그는 사내답게 싸우고 있었으나 승리의 월계관을 혼자 다 차지하고 싶은 마음은 조금도 없었다. “이리 오라니까!” 그러고서 그는 걸음을 멈추고 황소의 낯짝에 대고 고함을 지르며 괭이를 휘두르고 두 팔을 내저었다. 이렇게 싸움하는 몸짓을 보여 주는 것이 짐승의 기를 꺾는 데 가장 좋으리라 여긴 것이다.\n\n조니 임스는 이 땅의 어느 농사꾼을 도우러 달려갔을 법한 그대로 씩씩하게 그 귀족을 도우러 달려갔다. 그의 삶에서 이 무렵의 그가 아주 높은 용기를 지녔다고 말하는 것은 아마 잘못일 것이다. 그는 사내라면 두려워할 것 없는 것들을 많이도 두려워했다. 그러나 제 몸이 다치거나 제 살과 뼈가 상하는 것은 두려워하지 않았다. 크래들이 버턴 크레슨트의 집에서 복도를 지나 바깥바람 속으로 빠져나가 달아났을 때, 그것은 루펙스가 자기를 때리거나 걷어차거나 어떻게든 험하게 굴까 봐 겁이 났기 때문이었다. 존 임스라도 그런 처지였다면 달아나고 싶어 했을 것이다. 그러나 그가 그러고 싶어 했을 까닭은, 곤경에 빠진 제 꼴을 그 집 사람들이 들여다보는 것을 견딜 수 없어서였고, 순경이 눈에 시퍼렇게 멍이 들고 옷이 찢어진 자기를 끌고 가는 끔찍한 광경을 제 상상이 그려 보였을 것이기 때문이다. 지금은 그를 볼 사람도 없었고 트집 잡을 순경도 없었다. 그래서 그는 지팡이를 휘두르고 황소에 질세라 고함을 지르며 백작을 도우러 달려들었다.\n\n저를 얼마나 부당하게 대하는지, 적의 수는 갑절이 되었는데 제 편은 아무도 거들어 주지 않는다는 것을 그 짐승이 알아차렸을 때, 그는 인간의 불의에 넌더리가 나서 한동안 우두커니 서 있었다. 그는 걸음을 멈추더니 머리를 하늘로 쳐들고 제 억울함을 울부짖었다. “가까이 붙지 말게!” 백작이 말했다. 그는 숨이 거의 넘어갈 지경이었다. “조금 떨어져 있게. 어허! 어허! 훠이, 훠이!” 그러고서 그는 사내답게 두 팔을 쳐들고 괭이를 이리저리 내지르면서, 이따금 손등으로 눈썹에 맺힌 땀을 훔쳤다.\n\n황소가 멈춰 선 채 이런 판국에는 성깔을 채우는 것보다 달아나는 편이 낫지 않을까 궁리하고 있을 때, 임스가 그에게 달려들어 머리를 후려치려 했다. 이것을 본 백작도 한 걸음 나아가 괭이를 짐승의 눈앞까지 들이밀었다. 그러나 이런 모욕까지 그 짐승이 참아 낼 수는 없었다. 그는 먼저 존 임스 쪽으로 머리를 숙이고 돌진했는데, 그러다가 장군에게 그러하듯 황소에게도 부끄러운 그 우유부단으로 마음을 바꾸어 다른 적에게 뿔을 돌렸다. 그 바람에 그의 걸음은 두 사람 사이로 들어가게 되었고, 백작과 임스는 잠시 그의 꼬리 뒤에 있게 되었다.\n\n“이제 문으로.” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“천천히, 천천히요. 뛰지 마시고요!” 조니가 말했다. 그 순간의 열기에 휩쓸려, 다른 때 같으면 그에게 도무지 어울리지 않았을 훈수하는 말투를 쓰면서.\n\n백작은 조금도 기분 상해하지 않았다. “알았네.” 그가 이렇게 말하면서 뒷걸음으로 문 쪽 방향을 잡았다. 그러고서 황소가 다시 그를 마주 보자 그는 팔다리를 힘겹게 놀리며 땅에서 펄쩍 뛰었고, 괭이는 늘 적을 향해 잘 내밀어 두었다. 임스는 벗에게서 조금 떨어진 자리를 지키며 몸을 낮추고 지팡이로 땅을 두들겼는데, 마치 그 짐승에게 덤벼 보라는 듯한 시늉이었다. 황소는 저를 부추기는 것을 느끼고 우뚝 서서 울부짖더니 다시 또 오락가락하는 공격을 해 왔다.\n\n“문에 닿을 때까지 버티십시오.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“어허! 어허! 훠이! 훠이!” 백작이 고함을 질렀다. 그리하여 두 사람은 차츰 제 자리를 넓혀 갔다.\n\n“이제 넘으십시오.” 문이 서 있는 밭 모퉁이에 둘 다 이르렀을 때 임스가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 자네는 어떻게 하고?” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“저는 오른쪽 산울타리로 넘겠습니다.” 그렇게 말하면서 조니는 지팡이를 마구 휘둘러 잠시 그 짐승의 눈길을 혼자 붙들었다. 백작은 문으로 펄쩍 뛰어올라 윗가로대에 제대로 올라섰다. 제 먹잇감이 달아나는 것을 본 황소는 마지막으로 백작에게 달려들어 머리로 나무를 사납게 들이받았고, 그 바람에 백작님은 반대편으로 나가떨어졌다. 드 게스트 경은 이미 넘어가 있었으되 가로대에서 발을 뗀 것은 아니었다. 그리하여 그는 넘어지기는 했으나 문 너머 잔디밭에 무사히 떨어졌다. 무사히 떨어지기는 했으나 기운은 다 빠져 있었다. 임스는 작정한 대로 밭과 게스트윅 잡목숲 하나를 갈라놓은 두꺼운 산울타리를 향해 거의 옆으로 몸을 날렸다. 꽤 넓은 도랑이 있었고 그 너머에는 산사나무 울타리가 있었는데, 문 가까이의 이 모퉁이만은 함부로 넘나든 사람들 탓에 성기고 상해 있었다. 임스는 젊고 날래어 잘 뛰었다. 어찌나 잘 뛰었던지 그는 제 몸을 산사나무 울타리 한복판에 통째로 들이밀었고, 그러고 나서 반대편으로 기어 나왔는데, 그 통에 옷은 크게 상하고 손과 얼굴에도 얼마간 상처를 입었다.\n\n나무 빗장에 부딪힌 충격에서 깨어난 그 짐승은 아직 덤불 속에서 허우적대고 있는 마지막 적이 물러가는 것을 아쉬운 듯 바라보았다. 그는 도랑을 보고 성긴 울타리를 보았으나, 제 앞을 막은 것들이 얼마나 허술한지는 알지 못했다. 그는 튼튼한 나무에 머리를 부딪혔고 그 나무는 과연 그를 막아 낼 만했다. 그러나 힘들일 것도 없이 발밑에 짓밟아 버릴 수 있었을 가시덤불 앞에서는 기가 꺾이고 말았다. 우리 가운데 얼마나 많은 사람이 이 황소와 같은가. 우리에게 아무것도 아니어야 할 방해에 지고 돌아서며, 발을, 더 나쁘게는 마음을 금강석 같은 바위에 부딪혀 부수는 것이다. 마침내 황소는 그 울타리를 마주할 엄두가 나지 않는다고 마음을 정하고는, 마지막으로 한 번 울부짖고 몸을 돌려 무리 속으로 유유히 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n조니는 잡목숲에서 나가는 층계 울짱을 넘어 길로 나섰고, 이내 백작 옆에 서 있게 되었는데, 긁힌 자리에서 나온 피가 뺨을 타고 흐르고 있었다. 바지 한쪽은 말뚝에 걸려 엉덩이께부터 아래로 죽 찢어졌고, 모자는 밭에 남겨져 황소가 얻은 유일한 전리품이 되었다. “다치지 않으셨습니까, 백작님.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“어허, 다치기는. 그저 숨이 넘어가겠네. 아니, 자네 온통 피투성이 아닌가. 그 녀석이 자네를 받은 건 아니겠지?”\n\n“울타리 가시일 뿐입니다.” 조니가 손으로 얼굴을 훑으며 말했다. “그런데 모자를 잃어버렸습니다.”\n\n“모자야 얼마든지 있네.” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“한번 가서 가져와 볼까 합니다.” 조니가 말했다. 그에게는 모자를 마련할 방편이 백작만큼 넉넉하지 않았던 것이다. “이제 얌전해진 것 같은데요.” 그러고서 그는 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n그러나 드 게스트 경은 벌떡 일어나 그 젊은이의 웃옷 깃을 붙들었다. “모자를 가지러 간다고!” 그가 말했다. “그런 생각을 하다니 자네도 어지간한 바보로군. 감기가 들까 걱정이라면 내 것을 쓰게.”\n\n“감기는 조금도 걱정되지 않습니다.” 조니가 말했다. “저 녀석은 자주 저럽니까, 백작님?” 그러면서 그는 황소 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다.\n\n“세상에 저렇게 순한 짐승이 없네. 여느 때는 어린양 같지. 꼭 어린양 같아. 아마 내 빨간 손수건을 봤나 보네.” 그러고서 드 게스트 경은 자기가 그런 물건을 지니고 있음을 벗에게 보여 주었다. “그건 그렇고, 자네가 오지 않았더라면 나는 어디에 가 있었겠나?”\n\n“문까지는 가셨겠지요, 백작님.”\n\n“그래, 두 발을 앞으로 뻗고 장정 넷이 떠메고서 말이지. 목이 몹시 마르군. 자네 혹시 술병 같은 건 가지고 다니지 않겠지?”\n\n“예, 백작님, 없습니다.”\n\n“그럼 어서 집으로 가서 포도주나 한 잔 하세.” 그리고 이번만은 백작님도 제 청이 받아들여지게 할 작정이었다.\n\n# 제22장. 드 게스트 경의 집에서\n\n백작과 존 임스는 황소에게서 벗어난 뒤 함께 저택으로 걸어 올라갔다. “어머님께 쪽지를 한 장 써 두게. 그러면 내가 아이들 편에 보내 주겠네.” 백작이 말했다. 집에서 저를 기다릴 것이라는 이유로 임스가 저택에서 만찬을 들기를 사양했을 때 백작님이 내놓은 대답이 이것이었다.\n\n“하지만 제가 옷차림이 워낙 형편없어서요, 백작님.” 조니가 사정하듯 말했다. “울타리에서 바지를 찢어 먹었습니다.”\n\n“거기엔 우리 둘하고 크로프츠 선생 말고는 아무도 없네. 선생이야 이야기를 들으면 자네를 용서할 걸세. 나로 말하자면, 자네가 등에 실오라기 하나 걸치지 않았다 해도 개의치 않았을 걸세. 게스트윅으로 돌아갈 때 동행도 생길 테니, 자, 같이 가세.”\n\n임스는 더 내놓을 핑계가 없었으므로 시키는 대로 했다. 그는 지금 백작과 함께 있는 것이 싸움이 벌어지던 그 몇 분 동안만큼 편안하지가 않았다. 차라리 집으로 가고 싶었다. 이렇게 옷이 찢기고 모자도 없는 꼴을 그 집 하인들에게 보이는 것이 어쩐지 부끄러웠던 것이다. 게다가 그의 마음은 이따금 앨링턴의 정원에서 벌어졌던 그 장면으로 되돌아가곤 했다. 그러나 그는 백작에게 복종하지 않을 수 없다는 것을 알았고, 그리하여 백작과 함께 숲을 지나 걸어갔다.\n\n백작은 지치기도 했고 어쩐지 생각에 잠기기도 하여 말을 그리 많이 하지 않았다. 그 몇 마디 안 되는 말 속에서도 그는 황소가 저에게 보인 배은망덕이 유독 서운한 듯했다. “나는 저 녀석을 약 올린 적도, 어떤 식으로든 성가시게 군 적도 없단 말이야.”\n\n“그놈들이 원래 위험한 짐승 아닙니까?” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“제대로 다루기만 하면 조금도 그렇지 않네. 아무래도 내 손수건 탓이었나 보이. 내가 코를 풀었던 게 생각나는군.”\n\n그는 저를 도운 사람에게 고맙다는 말은 거의 한마디도 하지 않았다. “자네가 와 주지 않았더라면 나는 지금 어디에 있었겠나?” 위기를 벗어난 직후에 그는 그렇게 외쳤다. 그러나 그 말을 하고 나서는 임스에게 더 무슨 말을 해야 한다고 여기지 않았다. 그래도 그는 아주 유쾌하게 굴었고, 집에 이를 무렵에는 그와 동행한 젊은이도 저택에서 만찬을 들도록 떠밀린 것이 차라리 다행이다 싶어졌다. “자, 이제 한잔하세.” 백작이 말했다. “자네는 어떤지 모르겠네만, 나는 평생 이렇게 목말라 본 적이 없네.”\n\n하인 둘이 곧바로 모습을 드러냈고, 조니의 몰골을 보고 적잖이 놀랐다. “저 신사분께서 다치셨습니까요, 백작님?” 우리 벗의 얼굴에 묻은 피를 보며 집사가 물었다.\n\n“다친 건 바지 쪽이 더 심할 걸세.” 백작이 말했다. “게다가 저 사람이 내 옷을 입으면 너무 짧고 너무 헐렁하겠지, 안 그런가? 이렇게 불편하게 해서 미안하네만, 한 번쯤은 개의치 말게.”\n\n“저는 조금도 개의치 않습니다.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“나도 물론 개의치 않네.” 백작이 말했다. “임스 씨가 여기서 만찬을 드실 걸세, 비커스.”\n\n“예, 백작님.”\n\n“그리고 저 사람 모자가 십구 에이커 밭 한가운데에 떨어져 있네. 서넛 보내서 찾아오게 하게.”\n\n“서넛이나요, 백작님!”\n\n“그래,—서넛일세. 저 황소한테 무슨 탈이 났어. 그리고 조랑말을 태운 아이를 하나 구해서 게스트윅의 임스 부인께 쪽지를 전하게 해야겠네. 아이고, 이제 좀 살겠군.” 그러고서 그는 마시고 있던 큰 잔을 내려놓았다. “쪽지는 여기서 쓰게. 그러고 나서 만찬 전에 내가 아끼는 꿩들을 보러 가세.”\n\n비커스와 하인은 무언가 대단히 중대한 일이 벌어졌음을 알았다. 백작이 평소에 제 만찬 식탁에 관해서는 몹시 까다로웠기 때문이다. 그는 그 자리에 앉는 손님이면 누구든 그날의 유행이 요구하는 차림새를 갖추기를 바랐다. 그리고 그 자신도, 낮 동안의 옷차림이야 결코 화려한 편이 못 되었으나, 혼자 있을 때조차 검은 정장에 흰 크라바트를 갖추어 입지 않고서는 결코 만찬을 들지 않았으며, 낮에는 낡은 리본 끈으로 목에 매달아 지니고 다니던 낡은 은제 사냥용 회중시계를, 저녁이면 늘 조끼 위로 늘어뜨리는 사슬과 인장이 달린 작은 금시계로 바꾸어 차곤 했다. 언젠가 그러펀 선생이 게스트윅 저택의 만찬에 초대받은 적이 있었다. “그저 총각의 고기 한 점일세.” 백작은 그렇게 말했다. “집에 나 말고는 아무도 없으니 말이야.” 그런데 그러펀 선생은 색깔 있는 바지를 입고 왔고,—그 뒤로 다시는 게스트윅 저택의 만찬에 초대받지 못했다. 이 모든 것을 비커스는 잘 알고 있었다. 그런데 지금 백작님이 젊은 임스를 집으로 데려와 함께 만찬을 들자고 하시는데, 그 옷은 비커스가 하인들 방에서 반쯤도 점잖지 못하다고 잘라 말할 만한 꼴로 몸에 걸쳐져 있었다. 그러니 그들 모두가 무언가 아주 특별한 일이 일어났음이 틀림없다고 여겼다. “황소 때문에 무슨 일이 난 게지, 내가 알아.” 비커스가 말했다. “—하지만 이보게, 황소가 저 양반 옷을 저런 식으로 찢어 놨을 리는 없잖아!”\n\n임스는 쪽지를 썼는데, 드 게스트 경과 모험을 하나 겪었으며 백작님께서 기어이 자기를 집으로 데려와 만찬을 들게 하셨다고 어머니에게 알렸다. “바지를 온통 갈가리 찢었고,” 그는 추신에 이렇게 덧붙였다. “모자를 잃어버렸습니다. 그 밖에는 다 괜찮습니다.” 백작 역시 임스 부인에게 짧은 쪽지를 보냈다는 것을 그는 알지 못했다.\n\n부인께 [백작의 쪽지는 이러했다],—\n\n부인의 아드님이 하늘의 섭리 아래 아마도 제 목숨을 구했습니다. 그 이야기는 아드님이 직접 하도록 남겨 두겠습니다. 아드님은 고맙게도 저와 함께 집까지 와 주었고, 만찬 뒤에 여기서 만찬을 드는 크로프츠 선생과 함께 게스트윅으로 돌아갈 것입니다. 이토록 침착한 용기와 착한 마음을 지닌 아드님을 두신 것을 축하드립니다.\n\n부인의 참으로 충실한 종,\n\n게스트윅 저택, 186─년 시월 목요일.\n\n그러고 나서 두 사람은 꿩을 보러 갔다. “자, 내가 자네한테 할 말이 있네.” 백작이 말했다. “사냥에 맛을 들여 보라고 권하고 싶네. 어쩌다 사냥거리를 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 처지가 되면, 그건 신사의 놀이일세.”\n\n“하지만 저는 늘 런던에 올라가 있는걸요.”\n\n“아닐세, 그렇지 않네. 지금은 런던에 올라가 있지 않잖나. 자네도 늘 휴가가 있지 않은가. 한번 해 볼 마음이 있다면, 여기 있는 동안 쏠 것은 얼마든지 있게 해 주겠네. 나무 밑에서 잠드는 것보다야 낫지. 하하하! 대체 무슨 생각으로 거기 드러누워 있었던 겐가. 그날은 황소하고 싸운 것도 아니었을 텐데?”\n\n“예, 백작님. 그때는 아직 황소를 보지도 못했습니다.”\n\n“자, 내가 한 말을 잘 생각해 보게. 나는 말을 하면 그대로 하는 사람일세. 한번 해 볼 마음만 있다면 쏠 것은 얼마든지 있을 걸세.” 그러고 나서 두 사람은 꿩을 들여다보았고, 백작이 만찬 옷으로 갈아입을 때가 되었다고 말할 때까지 여기저기를 어슬렁거렸다. “자네한테는 그게 곤란한 일이겠군, 그렇지?” 그가 말했다. “하지만 어쨌든 손은 씻고 피는 좀 닦아 낼 수 있겠지. 나는 일곱 시 오 분 전에 작은 응접실로 내려갈 텐데, 자네도 거기 있으리라 생각하네.”\n\n일곱 시 오 분 전에 드 게스트 경이 작은 응접실로 들어와 보니, 조니가 책을 한 권 앞에 놓고 앉아 있었다. 백작은 어쩐지 부산스러웠고, 제게 익숙하지 않은 일거리를 손에 쥔 사람들이 더러 그러하듯이 마음이 썩 편치 않다는 것을 몸짓으로 드러내 보였다. 그는 손에 무언가를 쥐고 있었고, 방을 가로질러 올라오면서 발을 조금 끌었다. 그는 여느 때처럼 검은 옷을 입고 있었으나, 여느 때와 달리 금사슬은 조끼 위로 늘어져 있지 않았다.\n\n“임스.” 그가 말했다. “내가 주는 자그마한 선물을 하나 받아 주었으면 하네,—그저 우리가 황소와 벌인 일을 기억할 물건으로 말이야. 훗날 내가 세상을 떠난 뒤에도 이걸 보면 이따금 그 일이 생각날 걸세.”\n\n“아, 백작님—”\n\n“내가 얼마 전부터 차고 다니던 내 시계일세. 하지만 다른 게 또 있네;—위층 어딘가에 두세 개는 더 있을 걸세. 거절하면 안 되네. 나는 거절당하는 걸 못 견디는 사람이야. 내가 차던 작은 인장도 두어 개 달려 있네. 우리 집안 문장이 새겨진 건 떼어 냈네. 그건 자네한테는 아무 쓸모가 없고, 내게는 쓸모가 있으니 말이야. 이건 열쇠가 필요 없고 이렇게 손잡이를 돌려서 태엽을 감는 걸세.” 그러고서 백작은 이 장난감의 됨됨이를 설명하기 시작했다.\n\n“백작님, 오늘 있었던 일을 너무 대단하게 여기시는 겁니다.” 임스가 더듬거리며 말했다.\n\n“아닐세, 그렇지 않네. 나는 그 일은 거의 생각하지도 않네. 내가 무슨 생각을 하는지는 내가 알지. 선생이 오기 전에 시계를 주머니에 넣어 두게. 저기, 말발굽 소리가 들리는군. 왜 마차를 몰고 오지 않았을까, 그랬으면 자네를 태워다 줄 수 있었을 텐데?”\n\n“저는 걸어가도 아무렇지 않습니다.”\n\n“내가 다 알아서 해 줌세. 하인더러 크로프츠의 말을 타고 가서 작은 경마차를 몰고 오게 하지. 어서 오시게, 선생. 임스는 알 테지? 그런 눈으로 볼 것 없네. 다리가 부러진 게 아니라 바지가 그런 것뿐이니까.” 그러고 나서 백작은 황소 이야기를 들려주었다.\n\n“조니는 런던에 가면 아주 영웅이 되겠습니다.” 크로프츠가 말했다.\n\n“그래. 아무래도 공은 저 친구가 거의 다 가져갈 것 같아 걱정일세. 그런데도 내가 저 친구보다 두 배는 오래 그 일에 매달렸단 말이야. 내 말 좀 들어 보게, 젊은이들. 내가 그 문에 이르렀을 때는 그걸 넘어갈 숨이 남아 있지 않다 싶더군. 스물두 살일 때야 산울타리로 뛰어드는 것쯤 아무렇지도 않지. 하지만 예순이 되고 보면 그런 일에는 시간을 좀 들이고 싶어지는 법일세. 만찬 준비가 되었다고? 나도 준비가 됐네. 오늘 자네가 이르던 그 양갈비는 까맣게 잊고 있었네, 선생. 하지만 황소와 한판 붙고 나면 사람이 만찬을 든든히 들어도 되는 것 아니겠나?”\n\n저녁 시간은 그리 즐거울 것도 없이 지나갔고, 유감스럽게도 백작은 커피 한 잔을 들이켜자마자 응접실에서 곤히 잠들어 버렸다고 말해 두어야겠다. 만찬 동안 그는 두 손님 모두에게 아주 정중했으나, 임스에게는 사람 좋고 거의 다정하다 할 만한 허물없음을 보였다. 그는 임스가 나무 밑에서 잠든 채 발견된 일을 두고 놀려 대며, 크로프츠에게 저 친구가 아주 처량해 보이더라고 말했다,—“그러니 저 친구가 사랑에 빠졌다는 건 나는 의심하지 않네.” 백작은 그렇게 말했다. 그러고서 그는 반쯤 잊어버린 옛 고전 지식의 남은 조각들까지 끌어와 농담을 뒷받침하면서, 조니에게 그 어여쁜 이의 이름을 대 보라고 청했다. “내가 저 독한 팔레르눔을 더 들어야 한다면,” 그는 포도주 병에 손을 얹으며 말했다. “그 아가씨의 이름을 알아야겠네. 그이가 누구든 자네가 그이 때문에 낯을 붉힐 까닭이 없다는 건 내가 잘 아네. 뭐라고! 말하지 않겠다고! 그렇다면 나도 더는 마시지 않겠네.” 그러고서 백작은 만찬실에서 걸어 나갔다. 그러나 그전에 이미 손님의 뺨을 보고 그 농담이 유쾌하다 하기에는 너무 참말이었다는 것을 알아차린 뒤였다. 그렇지만 나가면서 그는 임스의 어깨에 손을 얹고 몸을 기댔고, 그것을 지켜보던 하인들은 이 젊은이가 총애를 받게 되리라는 것을 알아보았다. “나리께서 저분을 상속인으로 삼으실 거야.” 비커스가 말했다. “저분을 상속인으로 삼으신대도 나는 조금도 이상할 게 없어.” 그러나 이에 하인이 반대하며, 나라의 법에 따르자면 백작님이 한 번도 뵌 적이 없고 미워하신다고들 하는 백작님의 육촌쯤 되는 사람이 상속인이 될 수밖에 없다는 것을 비커스 씨에게 증명해 보이려 들었다. “백작 나리라고 해서 자네나 나처럼 제 상속인을 제 맘대로 고를 수 있는 게 아니라고.” 하인이 법을 늘어놓으며 말했다. “정말로 못 하신다고요? 그거 백작님께는 참 딱한 일이네요, 안 그래요?” 예쁜 하녀가 말했다. “쳇.” 비커스가 말했다. “자네는 아무것도 모르는군. 백작님은 당장 내일이라도 젊은 임스를 상속인으로 삼으실 수 있어. 그러니까 재산의 상속인 말이야. 백작으로 만들어 주실 수는 없지. 그건 몸에서 난 상속인한테 가야 하니까. 여기 이 저택을 남겨 주시는 문제라면, 그게 어찌 되는지는 나도 잘 모르겠고, 리처드도 모를 게 뻔해.”\n\n“그렇지만 몸에서 난 상속인이 없으시면 어떡해요?” 비커스 씨를 눌러 주기를 꽤 좋아하던 그 예쁜 하녀가 물었다.\n\n“몸에서 난 상속인은 있게 마련이야.” 집사가 말했다. “누구한테나 다 있는 거라고. 제 스스로 모른다 해도 법이 찾아내 주는 법이야.” 그러고 나서 비커스 씨는 더 다투는 일을 피해 자리를 떴다.\n\n그러는 동안 백작은 위층에서 잠들어 있었고, 게스트윅에서 온 두 젊은이는 도무지 무엇으로도 시간을 즐길 수가 없다는 것을 깨달았다. 저마다 책을 한 권씩 집어 들었다. 그러나 사람이 도무지 책을 읽을 수 없어 책이란 그저 제 게으름이나 따분함을 가려 주는 덮개에 지나지 않게 되는 때가 있는 법이다. 마침내 크로프츠 선생이 속삭이는 소리로, 이제 슬슬 집에 갈 생각을 하는 것이 어떻겠느냐고 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“어, 응, 뭐라고?” 백작이 말했다. “나 안 자네.” 이에 의사가 대답하기를, 백작님께서 말을 준비하라 일러 주신다면 이제 집으로 돌아갈까 한다고 했다. 그러나 백작은 다시 깊은 잠에 단단히 묶여 그 말에는 더 아무런 관심도 보이지 않았다.\n\n“깨우지 않고 빠져나갈 수 있지 않을까요?” 임스가 속삭였다.\n\n“어, 뭐라고?” 백작이 말했다. 그리하여 두 사람은 다시 책을 집어 들고 십오 분을 더 그 고행에 몸을 맡겼다. 그 시간이 다 지났을 무렵 하인이 차를 들여왔다.\n\n“어, 뭐야? 차라고!” 백작이 말했다. “그래, 차를 좀 들지. 자네들이 한 말은 한마디도 빠짐없이 다 들었네.” 백작의 이런 주장이야말로 레이디 줄리아를 늘 화나게 만드는 것이었다. “제가 한 말을 들으셨을 리가 없지요, 시어도어. 저는 아무 말도 하지 않았으니까요.” 그녀는 그렇게 대꾸하곤 했다. “하지만 네가 말을 했더라면 나는 들었을 게다.” 백작은 퉁명스럽게 되받곤 했다. 이날 저녁에는 크로프츠도 임스도 그의 말에 토를 달지 않았고, 그는 아직 사분의 삼쯤 잠든 채로 차를 받아 들이켰다.\n\n“허락해 주신다면, 백작님, 이제 제 말을 준비하라 이르겠습니다.” 의사가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 말—그래—” 백작이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n“그런데 내가 말을 타고 가면 자네는 어쩌겠나, 임스?” 의사가 말했다.\n\n“저는 걸어가지요.” 임스가 아주 낮은 목소리로 속삭였다.\n\n“뭐—뭐—뭐라고?” 백작이 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. “아, 그래, 그렇지. 가려는 게로군? 하기야 여기 앉아서 내가 자는 걸 지켜보느니 그러는 편이 낫겠지. 그런데 선생—내가 코를 골지는 않았지?”\n\n“가끔은 고셨습니다.”\n\n“크게는 아니었지? 자, 임스, 내가 크게 골던가?”\n\n“저, 백작님, 두세 번쯤은 꽤 크게 고셨습니다.”\n\n“그랬나?” 백작이 몹시 실망한 목소리로 말했다. “그런데도 말이야, 자네들이 한 말은 한마디도 빠짐없이 다 들었단 말일세.”\n\n작은 경마차는 이미 준비되어 있었고, 두 젊은이는 함께 게스트윅으로 돌아가는 길에 올랐다. 저택의 하인 하나가 그 뒤에서 의사의 말을 타고 따라왔다. “이보게, 임스.” 현관 계단에서 헤어지며 백작이 말했다. “모레 시내로 돌아간다고 했지. 그러면 다시는 못 보겠군?”\n\n“예, 백작님.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“자, 잘 들어 두게. 나는 크리스마스 전에 가축 품평회 때문에 올라갈 걸세. 십이월 이십이일에 내가 묵는 곳에서 나와 함께 만찬을 들어야 하네. 저민 스트리트의 포킨스일세. 일곱 시 정각이야. 잊어버리면 안 되네. 집에 가거든 수첩에 적어 두게. 잘 가게, 선생, 잘 가게. 아무래도 나는 한낮의 그 양갈비를 지켜야겠어.” 그러고 나서 그들은 마차를 몰고 떠났다.\n\n“틀림없이 저분을 상속인으로 삼으실 거야.” 비커스는 제 방으로 천천히 돌아가며 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n“앨링턴에서 돌아오는 길이었나 보군.” 크로프츠가 말했다. “드 게스트 경과 황소를 마주쳤을 때 말이야.”\n\n“예. 작별 인사나 하려고 잠깐 건너갔던 겁니다.”\n\n“다들 잘 있던가?”\n\n“한 분밖에 못 뵈었습니다. 나머지 두 분은 나가 계셨고요.”\n\n“데일 부인이었나?”\n\n“아뇨, 릴리였습니다.”\n\n“물론 혼자 앉아서 그 근사한 런던 연인을 생각하고 있었겠지? 우리야 그 아가씨를 아주 운 좋은 처녀로 보아야 하겠지. 저도 스스로 그렇게 여기고 있을 게 틀림없고.”\n\n“저야 잘 모르겠습니다.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“나는 그가 아주 훌륭한 젊은이라고 믿네.” 의사가 말했다. “하지만 그 사람 태도가 썩 마음에 들었다고는 말 못 하겠어.”\n\n“안 그러셨겠지요.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 그쪽도 십중팔구 내 태도가 조금도 더 마음에 들지 않았을 걸세. 자네 태도도 아마 마찬가지였겠지. 그렇다면야 피차 공평한 셈 아닌가.”\n\n“저는 그게 조금도 공평하다고 보지 않습니다. 그자는 속물이에요.” 임스가 말했다. “그리고 저는 그렇지 않다고 믿습니다.” 그는 백작의 저 “독한 팔레르눔”을 한두 잔 들이켠 터라, 여느 때 같았으면 그러지 않았을 만큼 마음을 넉넉히 털어놓고, 어쩌면 말도 더 거칠게 하고 싶어져 있었다.\n\n“아니, 나는 그가 속물이라고 생각하지 않네.” 크로프츠가 말했다. “그랬다면 데일 부인이 알아보셨을 걸세.”\n\n“두고 보세요.” 조니가 말하면서 백작의 말에 힘껏 채찍을 대었다. “두고 보시라고요. 거드름을 피우는 사람이 속물이지요. 그런데 그자는 거드름을 피웁니다. 그리고 저는 그자가 솔직한 사람이라고 믿지 않습니다. 그자가 앨링턴에서 그분들 사이에 끼어든 날은 우리 모두에게 나쁜 날이었어요.”\n\n“나는 그렇게는 보이지 않는걸.”\n\n“저는 그렇습니다. 하지만 잘 들어 두세요, 저는 이 이야기를 누구한테도 한마디도 하지 않았습니다. 앞으로도 할 생각이 없고요. 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까? 이제 그 아가씨는 그자와 결혼할 수밖에 없겠지요?”\n\n“물론 그래야지.”\n\n“그리고 평생을 비참하게 살겠지요. 아—아—아—아!” 그러고서 그는 깊은 신음을 흘렸다. “제가 말씀드리지요, 크로프츠. 그자는 이 고장에 있었던 아가씨 중에 가장 사랑스러운 아가씨를 데려가려는 겁니다. 그런데 그자는 그 아가씨를 가질 자격이 없어요.”\n\n“나는 그 아가씨가 언니에 견줄 만하다고는 생각하지 않네.” 크로프츠가 천천히 말했다.\n\n“예? 릴리가요?” 임스가 말했다. 마치 의사가 내놓은 그 말이 단 일 분도 버틸 수 없는 것이라는 듯이.\n\n“나는 늘 벨 쪽이 둘 중에 더 흠모를 받는다고 생각해 왔네.” 크로프츠가 말했다.\n\n“제가 말씀드리지요.” 임스가 말했다. “저는 여태껏 릴리 데일만큼 아름답다고 여긴 사람을 이 세상에서 본 적이 없습니다. 그런데 이제 저 짐승 같은 자가 그 아가씨와 결혼한다는 거예요! 제가 말씀드리지요, 크로프츠. 저는 기어이 그자와 시비를 붙고야 말 겁니다.” 이에 의사는 제 동행이 앓고 있는 병의 성격을 알아차리고, 릴리에 대해서도 벨에 대해서도 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n그러고 얼마 지나지 않아 임스는 제 집 문 앞에 이르렀고, 어머니와 누이는 영웅에게 마땅한 온갖 열광으로 그를 맞았다. “얘가 백작님의 목숨을 구했구나!” 드 게스트 경의 쪽지를 읽고서 임스 부인은 딸에게 그렇게 외쳤다. “아이고, 세상에!” 그러고서 그녀는 거의 정신을 잃다시피 하여 소파에 몸을 뒤로 던졌다.\n\n“드 게스트 경의 목숨을 구했다고요!” 메리가 말했다.\n\n“그래—하늘의 섭리 아래서 말이다.” 임스 부인이 말했다. 마치 그 뒷말이 제 아들의 갸륵한 일에 무게를 크게 더해 주기라도 하는 듯이.\n\n“그런데 어떻게 구했대요?”\n\n“침착한 용기와 착한 마음으로—백작님 말씀이 그렇구나. 그런데 대체 어떻게 한 걸까?”\n\n“어떻게 했든 옷은 죄다 찢어 놓고 모자는 잃어버렸대요.” 메리가 말했다.\n\n“나는 그런 건 조금도 아깝지 않다.” 임스 부인이 말했다. “백작님이 소득세 사무국에 무슨 연줄이라도 있으실까.\n\n“조니를 한 계단 올려 주실 수 있다면 얼마나 좋은 일이겠니. 당장 연 칠십 파운드가 될 텐데. 백작님이 청하셨을 때 남아서 만찬을 든 건 아주 잘한 일이야. 그리고 크로프츠 선생도 거기 계신다니. 설마 의술과 관계된 일은 아니었겠지.”\n\n“네, 아닐 거예요. 바지 이야기를 하는 걸 보면요.” 그리하여 두 부인은 존이 돌아오기를 기다릴 수밖에 없었다.\n\n“어떻게 한 게냐, 존?” 문이 열리자마자 그를 끌어안으며 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“오빠, 백작님 목숨을 어떻게 구한 거예요?” 어머니 뒤에 서 있던 메리가 말했다.\n\n“네가 아니었으면 백작님이 정말로 돌아가셨겠니?” 임스 부인이 물었다.\n\n“그리고 오빠는 많이 다쳤어요?” 메리가 물었다.\n\n“아, 귀찮아요.” 조니가 말했다. 그날 하루의 일에다 백작의 팔레르눔까지 겹쳐 아직도 얼마간 자취를 남기고 있었던 것이다. 여느 때 같았으면 임스 부인은 아들에게 그런 대답을 듣고 마음이 상했을 것이다. 그러나 지금 이 순간에는 아들이 세상의 총애를 한 몸에 받고 서 있다고 여겼으므로 조금도 언짢아하지 않았다. “아이고, 조니야, 어서 이야기해 다오. 우리야 물론 그 이야기를 전부 알고 싶어 안달일 수밖에 없지 않겠니.”\n\n“이야기할 게 없어요. 제가 지나가는데 황소가 백작님께 달려들기에 밭으로 들어가서 도와드렸고, 그랬더니 붙들어 두시고 함께 만찬을 들자고 하신 것뿐이에요.”\n\n“하지만 백작님은 오빠가 목숨을 구해 드렸다고 하셨잖아요.” 메리가 말했다.\n\n“하늘의 섭리 아래서 말이지.” 어머니가 덧붙였다.\n\n“어쨌든 금시계하고 사슬을 주셨어요.” 조니가 주머니에서 선물을 꺼내며 말했다. “시계가 몹시 갖고 싶긴 했어요. 그래도 받는 건 내키지 않았습니다.”\n\n“거절했으면 아주 잘못이었을 게다.” 어머니가 말했다. “그리고 네가 이렇게 운이 좋았다니 나는 참 기쁘구나. 그리고 얘야, 조니야, 그런 벗이 네 길에 나타나거든 등을 돌리면 안 된다.” 그러자 마침내 그는 두 사람의 다정함에 마음이 풀려 이야기 전부를 들려주었다. 다만 백작이 괭이를 들고 애쓰던 대목을 옮길 때, 제 후원자를 이야기하는 말투에 마땅히 있어야 할 만한 공경이 그리 담겨 있지 않았던 것 같아 나는 걱정스럽다.\n\n# 제23장. 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨\n\n크로즈비 씨는 혼약을 맺었다는, 이미 널리 알려진 사실 때문에 별다른 불편을 겪는 일도 없이 코시 성에서 한 주를 흘려보냈다. 조지 드 코시와 존 드 코시는 저마다 제 방식대로 그의 죄를 들추어 대며 그 화제를 자꾸 끄집어내어 그를 성가시게 하려 들었으나, 그는 조지나 존 드 코시의 재치나 심술 따위에는 별로 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 백작부인은 그가 찾아온 첫날에 했던 그 몇 마디 말고는 릴리 데일에 관해 거의 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 앨링턴에서 그가 한 짓을 그런 처지에 놓인 젊은 남자에게는 지극히 자연스러운 소일거리로 여기려는 듯했다. 그는 따분한 시골집으로 꾀여 내려갔고, 그러니 당연하게도 그곳에서 할 만한 놀이에 손을 댄 것이었다. 자고새를 쏘았고 젊은 아가씨에게 사랑을 속삭였으니, 지주와 어울려 지내는 지루함에 대한 보상으로 그런 자잘한 즐길 거리를 취한 것이었다. 어쩌면 그가 그 젊은 아가씨와 조금 지나치게 멀리 나갔는지도 모른다. 그러나 젊은 남자가 지나치게 멀리 가지 않으면서 충분히 멀리 가기가 얼마나 어려운 일인지를 백작부인보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없었다. 젊은 남자의 처신을 두고 검열관 노릇을 하는 것은 제가 할 일이 아니었다. 잘못은 틀림없이 그 못지않게 데일 양에게도 있었다. 어느 젊은 아가씨든 낭패를 보는 것은 안된 일이지만, 처녀들이 굳이 경솔하게 굴며 제 분수에 넘치는 남자에게 눈독을 들인다면 낭패를 겪을 수밖에 없는 법이다. 드 코시 백작부인은 딸들 사이에서 그 일을 두고 이런 식으로 말했고, 딸들도 크로즈비 씨가 릴리 데일과 혼인한다는 것은 말도 안 되는 일이라는 데 하나같이 어머니와 뜻을 같이했다. 알렉산드리나에게서는 그 한 주 동안 그가 예상했던 놀림을 조금도 받지 않았다. 그는 성을 떠나기 전에 릴리와 알고 지낸 내력을 모두 설명하겠노라 약속해 두었고, 그녀도 마침내 그 약속을 이행하라고 요구할 뜻을 내비쳤다. 그러나 그전까지는 노여움도, 우정이 식었다는 기색도 전혀 내비치지 않았다. 그리고 유감스럽게도 나는 이렇게 말해 두어야겠다. 그동안 두 사람 사이에 오간 것으로 보건대 그 우정은 런던에서 그러했던 것보다 결코 덜 살가워지지 않았다고.\n\n“그런데 약속하신 건 언제 이야기해 주실 건가요?” 어느 날 오후, 저녁 식사 준비를 서두를 필요가 아직 생기지 않은 그 한가한 삼십 분 동안, 두 사람이 당구실 창가에 나란히 서 있을 때 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 물었다. 그녀는 말을 타고 나갔다 온 참이라 아직 승마복 차림이었고, 그는 사냥에서 돌아온 참이었다. 그녀는 키 높은 곧은 모자와 승마 차림새를 하고 있을 때 제 모습이 여느 때보다 낫다는 것을 알고 있었고, 하루 중 이맘때면 자락을 손으로 받쳐 든 채 능숙한 걸음으로 집 안을 오가며 서성이는 버릇이 있었다. 어스름했으나 어둡지는 않았고, 당구실에는 켜 놓은 불이 없었다. 공을 쳐 보는 시늉을 얼마간 하기는 했으나 그저 시늉일 뿐이었다. “디아나라 한들 승마복 차림으로 당구를 칠 수는 없었을 거예요.” 그녀는 그렇게 말했더랬다. 그러고서 밀대를 내려놓았고, 두 사람은 큼직한 퇴창 안쪽에 들어서서 이야기를 나누며 서 있게 된 것이었다.\n\n“내가 무엇을 약속했지요?” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“잘 아시잖아요. 저한테 특별히 관심 있는 일이라서가 아니라, 굳이 약속을 하겠다고 나서셨으니 당연히 궁금증이 생긴 거지요.”\n\n“특별히 관심 있는 일이 아니라면,” 크로즈비가 말했다. “저를 그 약속에서 놓아주셔도 괜찮으시겠군요.”\n\n“꼭 당신다운 말씀이에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “남자들은 어쩌면 그렇게 늘 거짓되지요. 당신은 언젠가 속내를 털어놓겠다는 시늉을 해서 달갑잖은 화제에 대한 제 침묵을 사려고 마음먹었고, 이제 와서는 저에게 속내를 털어놓을 생각이 없다고 말하는군요.”\n\n“그 일이 조금도 관심 없는 일이라고 먼저 말씀하신 건 당신입니다.”\n\n“그것 또한 참 거짓된 말이에요! 제 말뜻을 잘 아시면서. 오시던 날 저에게 하신 말씀을 기억하세요? 그런 말을 들은 뒤라면, 당신이 이 아가씨와 혼인하든 저 아가씨와 혼인하든 저에게는 특별한 관심거리가 아니라고 말해 두는 것이 마땅하지 않나요? 그래도 당신 벗으로서는—”\n\n“그래서, 제 벗으로서는!”\n\n“알게 되면 기쁘겠지요—. 하지만 속내를 털어놓아 달라고 조르지는 않겠어요. 다만 이것만은 솔직히 말해 두지요. 거짓 깃발을 달고 싸우는 남자만큼 제 눈에 비열해 보이는 사람은 없다는 것을요.”\n\n“그럼 제가 거짓 깃발을 달고 싸우고 있다는 말씀입니까?”\n\n“네, 그래요.” 그리고 이렇게 말하는 동안 레이디 알렉산드리나는 모자 아래로 얼굴을 붉혔다. 저녁의 남은 빛이 흐릿했는데도 크로즈비는 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보다가 짙어진 홍조를 보았다. “네, 그래요. 약혼했다는 소문이 파다한 채로 이런 집에 들어와서는 그런 일이라곤 아예 없다는 듯이 처신하는 신사라면, 그건 거짓 깃발을 달고 싸우는 것이지요. 물론 저에게 특별히 무슨 상관이 있는 일은 아니에요. 하지만 그건 거짓 깃발을 달고 싸우는 겁니다. 자, 이제 처음 여기 오셨을 때 저에게 하신 약속을 지키시든지—아니면 그냥 내버려 두시든지 하세요.”\n\n이 부인이 제 싸움을 꽤 용기 있게, 게다가 얼마간 솜씨 있게 치르고 있었다는 것은 인정해야 한다. 사나흘 뒤면 크로즈비는 떠날 것이었다. 그러니 이 승리는, 얻을 수 있는 것이라면, 그 사나흘 안에 얻어 내야만 했다. 그리고 승리가 없을 것이라면, 그때는 크로즈비가 제 이중성 때문에 벌을 받고 그녀는 제 힘이 닿는 한 앙갚음을 하는 것이 마땅한 일이었다. 그렇다고 그녀가 무슨 깊은 앙갚음을 궁리했다거나 대단한 분노를 느낄 채비가 되어 있었다는 말은 아니다. 그녀는 여태까지 어떤 남자를 좋아해 본 만큼은 크로즈비를 좋아했다. 그도 저를 좋아한다고 믿었다. 아주 강렬한 정열이라는 것은 도무지 짐작조차 하지 못했으나, 혼인한 삶이 홀몸의 복락보다는 즐거우리라고 여겼다. 그가 릴리 데일을 아내로 삼겠다고 약속한 것은 의심하지 않았다. 그러나 그전에 저에게도 그렇게 약속했거나 그와 다름없는 말을 했던 것이다. 그것은 공정한 겨루기였고, 할 수만 있다면 그녀는 이길 작정이었다. 진다면 노여움을 드러낼 터였다. 그러나 온순하고 힘없는 방식으로, 즉 크로즈비의 면전에서 릴리를 두고 코를 치켜올리고 그의 등 뒤에서 그를 헐뜯는 자잘한 말이나 하는 식으로 드러낼 것이었다. 그녀의 분노가 그녀를 그보다 더 멀리 데려가지는 않을 터였다.\n\n“자, 이제 처음 여기 오셨을 때 저에게 하신 약속을 지키시든지—아니면 그냥 내버려 두시든지 하세요.” 그녀는 그렇게 말하고서 그에게서 얼굴을 돌려 어둠 속을 내다보았다.\n\n“알렉산드리나!” 그가 말했다.\n\n“네, 무슨 일이시죠? 하지만 당신은 저에게 그런 투로 말할 권리가 없어요. 그런 식으로 제 이름을 부를 권리가 없다는 걸 아시잖아요!”\n\n“작위를 붙여 부르라고 고집하신다는 말씀입니까?”\n\n“여자라면 누구나 신사에게서 당신이 작위라 부르는 그것을 붙여 듣기를 고집한답니다. 당신이 요구할 권리가 있는 것보다 더 가까운 사이라는 특권이 있지 않고서야 말이지요. 당신도 허락을 얻기 전에는 데일 양을 세례명으로 부르지 않으셨겠지요?”\n\n“전에는 그렇게 부르도록 허락해 주셨는데요.”\n\n“한 번도요! 당신이 그렇게 부른 적이 한두 번 있었을 때 제가 마땅히 금했어야 할 것을 금하지 않았을 뿐이에요. 좋아요, 저에게 하실 말씀이 없다니 그만 물러가겠어요. 당신이 이렇게 겁쟁이일 줄은 몰랐다고 고백해야겠군요.” 그러고서 그녀는 승마복 자락을 그러모으고 창턱에 얹어 두었던 채찍을 집어 들며 갈 채비를 했다.\n\n“잠깐만요, 알렉산드리나.” 그가 말했다. “저는 행복하지 않습니다. 저를 더 비참하게 만들려는 말씀은 하지 마십시오.”\n\n“그럼 왜 불행하신데요?”\n\n“그건— 이 집안 전체가 아니라 당신에게만 말하는 것이라고 믿어도 된다면 당장 말씀드리지요.”\n\n“물론 다른 사람들에게 옮기지 않아요. 제가 비밀 하나 지키지 못할 사람으로 보이나요?”\n\n“한 여자와 혼인하겠다고 약속해 놓고 다른 여자를 사랑하기 때문입니다. 이제 다 말씀드렸습니다. 그런데도 제가 거짓 깃발을 달고 싸운다고 또 말씀하시겠다면, 저는 당신이 저를 다시 보기 전에 이 성을 떠나겠습니다.”\n\n“크로즈비 씨!”\n\n“이제 다 아셨으니 제가 그리 행복한지 어떤지도 짐작하시겠지요. 옷 갈아입을 때가 되었다고 하신 것 같은데요—갈까요?” 그러고서 더는 말없이 두 사람은 각자의 방으로 갔다.\n\n크로즈비는 제 방에 혼자 남게 되자마자 안락의자에 앉아 앞으로 어떻게 처신할지 마음을 정하려 애쓰기 시작했다. 그가 방금 한 고백이 오로지 그 순간의 곤경 때문에 나온 것이라고 여겨서는 안 된다. 코시 성의 공기가 지난 한 주 내내 그에게 작용해 온 터였다. 그가 들은 말 하나하나, 그가 한 말 하나하나가 그의 안에 있던 선하고 참된 것을 모조리 허물고 이기적이고 거짓된 것을 모조리 북돋우는 쪽으로 작용했다. 그는 그 한 주 동안 열두 번도 더 스스로에게 말했다. 자기는 릴리 데일과 결코 행복할 수 없고 그녀를 결코 행복하게 해 줄 수도 없다고. 그러고는 제가 하고 싶은 일을 하는 것이 옳다고 스스로를 가르치려 애쓰면서 그 해묵은 궤변을 끌어 썼다. 제 마음의 명령을 거슬러 릴리와 혼인하는 것보다는 그녀를 버리는 편이 릴리에게 더 낫지 않겠는가? 그리고 그가 정말로 그녀를 사랑하지 않는다면, 그녀를 버리는 것보다 그녀와 혼인하는 것이 더 큰 죄를 짓는 일이 되지 않겠는가? 그는 바깥세상이 저를 그토록 단단히 옭아매도록 내버려 두어 릴리처럼 순결한 처녀의 사랑만으로는 제 행복이 채워지지 않게 만든 것이 아주 잘못이었다고 스스로 인정했다. 그러나 그것은 엄연한 사실이었고, 그는 그것과 맞서 싸울 힘이 제게 없음을 알았다. 어떤 절대적인 자기희생으로 릴리의 안녕을 지켜 줄 수 있다면 그는 한순간도 망설이지 않을 것이었다. 그러나 저와 함께 그녀까지 제물로 바치는 것이 과연 잘하는 일이겠는가?\n\n그는 제 가슴속에서 그 문제를 이런 식으로 따져 온 끝에, 릴리와의 약혼을 깨는 것이 제 의무라고 거의 스스로 믿게 만들어 놓았다. 그리고 코시 가문의 딸과 혼인하는 것이 제 야심을 채워 주고 세상과의 싸움에서 저를 도와주리라는 것도 거의 스스로 믿게 만들어 놓은 터였다. 데일 양과 약혼한 죄를 용서하도록 그녀를 이끌기만 한다면 레이디 알렉산드리나가 저를 받아들이리라는 것은 확실하다고 느꼈다. 다만 그 문제에서 그녀가 얼마나 선뜻 용서할 마음을 품고 있는지까지는 헤아리지 못했으니, 그런 여자가 그런 죄를—최후의 승리가 제게 돌아오기만 한다면—얼마나 쉽게 용서하는지 아직 배우지 못한 까닭이었다.\n\n그리고 크로즈비를 크게 움직인 또 하나의 이유가 있어 그를 지금의 심경과 바람 쪽으로 몰아갔으니, 마땅히 그의 마음에 정반대의 충동을 주었어야 할 것이었는데도 그러했다. 그는 수입이 적다는 이유로 릴리 데일과 당장 혼인하기를 망설였다. 그런데 이제 그 수입이 상당히 늘어날 전망이 생긴 것이다. 그가 다니는 사무국의 위원 하나가 더 높은 위원 자리로 승진했고, 일반위원회 사무국의 사무국장이 새 위원이 되리라는 것은 누구나 알고 있는 일이었다. 그 점에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그러나 그러자 사무국장 자리를 누가 맡느냐 하는 문제가 생겼다. 크로즈비는 그 일로 두세 통의 편지를 받은 터였고, 그가 이 출셋길의 한 계단을 얻을 가망은 결코 적지 않아 보였다. 그렇게 되면 관직에서 나오는 그의 수입이 연 칠백 파운드에서 천이백 파운드로 늘어나 그를 아주 세상 위로 올려놓을 것이었다. 지금 사무국장으로 있는 그의 벗이 편지를 보내, 그와 겨룰 만한 다른 경쟁자가 나섰다는 말은 들리지 않는다고 장담해 주었다. 그런 행운이 그를 기다리고 있다면, 릴리 데일과의 혼인을 가로막고 있는 지금의 어려움쯤은 말끔히 풀리지 않겠는가? 그러나 아아, 그는 그 일을 그런 빛 아래에서 보지 않았다! 백작부인이 그 승진에 도움을 줄 수 있지 않을까? 그리고 그의 운명이 그에게 이 세상의 좋은 것들—사무국장 자리며 위원 자리며 의장 자리며 그런 것들을 마련해 두었다면, 좋은 연줄이 줄 수 있는 도움을 받아 그 오르막길을 밀고 올라가는 것이 잘하는 일이 아니겠는가?\n\n그날 저녁 그는 제 방에 앉아 이 모든 것을 곰곰 생각했다. 코시 성에 온 뒤로 그는 릴리에게 두 번 편지를 썼다. 첫 번째 편지는 앞서 옮겨 두었다. 두 번째 편지도 거의 같은 투로 쓰였다. 다만 릴리는 그것을 읽으면서 자기도 모르게 첫 번째 편지만큼 흡족하지가 않았다. 사랑의 말이 모자라지는 않았으나 어렴풋했고 진심이 담기지 않았다. 말 자체에서 죄를 잡아낼 만한 것은 없었어도 어딘가 진실하지 못한 냄새가 났다. 진실이 지니는 그 온전한 원만함과 자족을 갖추고 거짓말을 할 수 있는 거짓말쟁이는 드물다. 크로즈비도 나쁜 사람이기는 했으나 아직 그런 완벽함에 이를 만큼 나빠지지는 않았던 것이다. 그는 저에게 열린 승진의 희망에 관해서는 릴리에게 한마디도 하지 않았다. 대신 제 세속됨을 또 한 번 이야기했으니, 코시 성의 화려함과 허영에서 흡족하지 못한 흡족을 얻고 있음을 인정한 것이다. 실은 이제 레이디 알렉산드리나에게 사랑한다고 말해 버린 마당에 저지르기로 거의 마음먹은 그 일을 위해 미리 길을 닦고 있었던 것이다. 그리고 그는 주사위가 던져졌음을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n이 모든 것을 생각하는 동안 그에게 도망쳐 나온 자의 만족 같은 것이 아주 없지는 않았다. 앨링턴에서 그 사랑 고백을 하고 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 그렇게 함으로써 제 목을 그어 버렸다고 느끼기 시작했더랬다. 그렇게 목이 그어진 채로도 그럭저럭 편안히 살 수 있으리라고 스스로를 설득해 보려 애썼고, 릴리가 곁에 있는 동안에는 그렇게 할 수 있다고 믿었다. 그러나 다시 혼자가 되기만 하면 또다시 스스로를 자살자라고 몰아세웠다. 이것이 아직 앨링턴에 있을 때부터의 그의 마음가짐이었고, 코시에 머무는 동안 그 생각은 더 굳어졌다. 그러나 제 몸을 제단에 바치는 일은 아직 다 끝나지 않았고, 이제 그는 저를 구할 수 있으리라 생각하기 시작했다. 이것이 그에게 온통 개가만은 아니었다는 말은 굳이 할 것도 없겠다. 릴리를 저버리는 데 아무런 실질적인 어려움이 없다 하더라도—맞서야 할 노여운 삼촌과 사촌과 어머니가 없다 하더라도, 삼촌과 사촌과 어머니가 성난 말의 소나기를 퍼붓는 것보다도 그 침묵으로 잘못을 더 웅변하는 창백한 얼굴이 눈앞에 떠오르지 않는다 하더라도—그는 아주 매정한 사람은 아니었다. 저를 그토록 잘 사랑해 준 처녀에게, 저 자신도 느끼다시피 그 사랑이 앞으로의 삶 전체를 좋은 쪽으로든 나쁜 쪽으로든 빚어 놓을 만큼 그렇게 저를 사랑해 준 처녀에게 이 모든 것을 어떻게 말한단 말인가? “나는 그녀에게 어울리지 않는 사람이고, 그렇다고 말해 주겠다.” 그는 스스로에게 그렇게 말했다. 사내라는 이름의 거짓된 사냥개들이 그런 짐짓 꾸민 겸손으로 제 양심을 달래려 든 적이 얼마나 많았던가? 그러나 옷을 갈아입으려고 자리에서 일어서면서 그는 이제 주사위가 던져졌음을, 그리고 이제 레이디 알렉산드리나에게 제 마음대로 무슨 말이든 할 수 있게 되었음을 인정했다. “전에도 같은 불길을 지나온 사람들이 있지.” 계단을 내려가면서 그는 스스로에게 말했다. “그러고도 멀쩡히 나왔고 말이야.” 그러고서 그는 젊은 시절에 저와 같은 자잘한 잘못을 저질렀으리라 여겨지는, 세상에서 이름 높은 여러 사람의 이름을 떠올려 보았다.\n\n현관홀을 지나다가 그는 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트를 따라잡았고, 마침 때맞추어 응접실 문을 열어 주었다. 그제야 그는 저와 알렉산드리나가 창가에 서 있는 동안 그녀가 당구실 한쪽 문으로 들어왔다가 다른 쪽 문으로 나갔다는 것이 떠올랐다. 그러나 그때는 레이디 줄리아를 그리 대수롭게 여기지 않았다. 그리고 이제 문간에 서서 그녀가 제 옆을 지나가기를 기다리며 그는 무어라 심상한 말을 건넸다.\n\n그러나 레이디 줄리아는 어떤 문제에서는 엄정한 여자였고 얼마간의 용기도 갖추고 있었다. 지난 한 주 동안 그녀는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지를 보았고 점점 더 화가 났다. 릴리 데일과는 아무 친척 관계도 없다고 잘라 말한 적이 있으면서도, 이제 그녀는 그 처녀에게 동정을, 아니 거의 애정을 느끼고 있었다. 거의 날마다 그녀는 크로즈비가 데일 양에게 구애하고 약혼한 일의 이런저런 대목을 백작부인에게 딱딱하게 되풀이해 말했다. 마치 낱낱이 다 알고 있다는 듯이, 무엇 하나 빠짐없이 다 정해진 일이라는 듯이 말이다. 백작부인과 단둘이 있을 때도 그렇게 했고, 백작부인과 알렉산드리나가 함께 있는 자리에서도, 성에 묵는 부인 손님들이 다 모인 앞에서도 그렇게 했다. 그러나 그녀가 한 말은 그저 못 믿겠다는 웃음으로 받아넘겨졌다. “어머나! 레이디 줄리아.” 마침내 백작부인이 대꾸했다. “나는 그대가 크로즈비 씨를 사랑하고 있다고 여기게 될 것 같소. 그이의 그 일을 어찌 그리 노상 되뇌시오. 처녀 하나가 거둔 성공을 그토록 요란하게 나발 불어 대는 걸 보면, 그대가 사는 고장에서는 아가씨들이 남편감 구하기가 여간 어렵지 않은가 보다고들 여기겠구려.” 그 순간에는 레이디 줄리아도 입을 다물었다. 그러나 마음에 가까운 이야깃거리가 있을 때 그녀를 아주 잠잠하게 만들기란 쉬운 일이 아니었다.\n\n크로즈비를 뒤에 달고 그녀가 응접실로 걸어 들어갔을 때 코시의 온 세계가 거의 다 그 방에 모여 있었다. 사람들 무리 가까이에 이르자 그녀는 몸을 돌려, 응접실에서 이야기를 나눌 때 흔히 필요한 것보다 훨씬 잘 들리는 목소리로 그에게 말을 건넸다. “크로즈비 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “우리의 소중한 벗 릴리 데일에게서 요즘 소식을 들으셨나요?” 그러고서 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았는데, 그 태도는 아마 그녀 자신이 뜻했던 것보다도 더 의미심장했다. 방 안은 대번에 물을 끼얹은 듯 조용해졌고 모든 눈이 그녀와 그에게 쏠렸다.\n\n크로즈비는 그 공격을 의젓하게 받아넘기려고 곧바로 애를 썼으나, 낯빛까지 제 뜻대로 다스릴 수는 없고 이마에 갑자기 돋는 땀방울이 드러나는 것도 막을 수 없다고 느꼈다. “어제 앨링턴에서 편지를 받았습니다.” 그가 말했다. “댁의 오라버님께서 황소와 맞닥뜨리신 일은 들으셨겠지요?”\n\n“황소라니요!” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다. 그리고 그녀의 공격이 막히고 옆구리가 뚫렸다는 것이 대번에 모두에게 뚜렷해졌다.\n\n“세상에! 레이디 줄리아, 어쩌면 그리 별나시오!” 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n“한데 그 황소 얘기는 뭔가?” 아너러블 조지가 물었다.\n\n“백작께서 당신 밭 한복판에서 나가떨어지셨다는 모양입니다.”\n\n“어머나!” 알렉산드리나가 외쳤다. 그리고 모여 있던 부인들이 저마다 이런저런 감탄을 터뜨렸다.\n\n“하지만 다치시지는 않았습니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “임스라는 젊은이가 하늘에서 뚝 떨어져 백작을 등에 업고 데려간 모양이더군요.”\n\n“하, 하, 하, 하!” 다른 백작이 제 동료 귀족의 낭패를 전해 듣고 으르렁대듯 웃었다.\n\n그날 게스트윅에서 제 앞으로 온 편지를 받아 본 레이디 줄리아는 오라비에게 대단한 일이라곤 아무것도 일어나지 않았음을 알고 있었다. 그러나 이번만은 저의 공격이 막혔음을 느꼈다.\n\n“정말로 무슨 사고가 있었던 것은 아니기를 바랍니다.” 게이지비 씨가 몹시 걱정스러운 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“제 오라비는 어젯밤까지 아주 잘 있었습니다, 고맙습니다.” 그녀가 말했다. 그러고 나서 작은 무리들이 다시 만들어졌고, 레이디 줄리아는 소파 한쪽 구석에 혼자 남겨졌다.\n\n“그건 다 지어내신 거예요?” 알렉산드리나가 크로즈비에게 말했다.\n\n“꼭 그런 건 아닙니다. 오늘 아침에 제 벗 버나드 데일에게서—저 늙은 마귀할멈의 조카지요—편지를 받은 건 사실이고, 드 게스트 경이 기르는 짐승 가운데 하나 때문에 시달린 것도 사실입니다. 그 미련한 늙은 목이 부러졌다고 말해 줄걸 그랬군요.”\n\n“저런, 크로즈비 씨!”\n\n“저 여자가 무슨 상관이라고 내 일에 끼어든답니까?”\n\n“하지만 저는 저분이 한 그 질문을 똑같이 물을 작정이에요. 그런 소 나오는 허튼소리로 저를 따돌리진 못하실 거예요.” 그러나 그녀가 그 질문을 하려는 참에 만찬이 알려졌다.\n\n“그래, 드 게스트가 황소에게 받혔다는 게 정말이오?” 부인들이 물러가자마자 백작이 말했다. 그는 만찬 내내 레이디 덤벨로의 귀에 대고 우물거린 몇 마디 말고는 아무 말도 하지 않은 터였다. 제 집에서 이야기라는 것이 그에게 큰 재미를 주는 일은 드물었다. 그러나 드 게스트 경이 뿔에 받혀 나가떨어졌다는 생각에는 익살스러운 맛이 있어서 그마저도 간지러웠던 것이다.\n\n“그저 나가떨어지기만 하신 모양입니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“하, 하, 하!” 백작이 으르렁대듯 웃었다. 그러고서 제 잔을 채우고 술병을 다른 사람에게 넘겼다. 가엾은 사람! 이제 이 세상에는 그를 즐겁게 해 주는 것이 별로 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n“나는 웃을 일이 도무지 보이지 않소.” 백작의 오른편, 덤벨로 경의 맞은편에 앉아 있던 플랜태저넷 팰리서가 말했다.\n\n“안 보이시오?” 백작이 말했다. “하, 하, 하!”\n\n“정말이지 하나도 모르겠소. 내가 듣기로 드 게스트는 보기 드물게 훌륭한 농사꾼이오. 농사짓는 사람이 귀족이기도 하다는 이유만으로 뿔에 받혔다는 것이 어디가 우스운지 나는 모르겠소. 그쪽은 아시겠소?” 그러면서 그는 반대쪽에 앉아 있던 게이지비 씨에게로 몸을 돌렸다. 백작은 백작이었고, 게이지비 씨의 장인이기도 했다. 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 공작 자리의 상속인이었다. 그러니 게이지비 씨는 그저 배시시 웃기만 할 뿐 저에게 던져진 질문에 답하지 않았다. 팰리서 씨도 그 일에 관해 더는 말하지 않았고 백작도 그러했다. 그리하여 그 농담은 사그라들었다.\n\n플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 옴니엄 공작의 상속인이었다. 그 귀인의 작위와 그 막대한 재산을 물려받을 사람이었으니, 따라서 세상에서 이름난 인물이었다. 물론 하원 의원이었다. 나이는 스물다섯쯤이었고 아직 혼인하지 않았다. 사냥개를 몰지도 총을 쏘지도 요트를 두지도 않았고, 평생 경마장에 발 한 번 들여놓은 적이 없다고 말하는 것을 들은 사람도 있었다. 옷차림은 몹시 수수해서 옷의 빛깔도 모양도 결코 바꾸는 법이 없었다. 사교 자리에서는 조용하고 삼가는 편이었으며 아주 자주 말이 없었다. 키가 크고 호리호리했으며 인물도 나쁘지 않았다. 그러나 그의 겉모습에 대해서는 그 이상 말할 것이 없다—다만 이것만은 덧붙여야겠다. 누구도 그를 신사가 아닌 다른 무엇으로 잘못 볼 수는 없었다는 것이다. 숙부인 공작과는 사이가 좋았다. 다시 말해 두 사람은 한 번도 다툰 적이 없었다. 조카에게는 아주 후한 생활비가 주어져 있었다. 그러나 이 두 친척은 취향이 도무지 겹치는 데가 없어 자주 만나지 않았다. 팰리서 씨는 해마다 한 번 공작의 큰 시골 저택을 이삼 일 찾았고, 런던 사교철에는 대개 두세 번 공작과 저녁을 들었다. 팰리서 씨는 전적으로 공작의 손아귀에 있는 자치구를 대표해 의석을 얻었으나, 정치에서는 제 눈에 옳아 보이는 편을 마음대로 들겠다는 분명한 양해 아래 그 자리를 받아들였다. 이렇게 서로 잘 알고 있는 약속 덕분에 공작과 그 상속인은 더할 나위 없이 화목한 집안의 본보기를 세상에 보여 주었다. “백작과 폴록 경하고는 어쩌면 그리 다른지!” 서부 바셋주 사람들은 그렇게들 말하곤 했다. 공작의 영지도 백작의 영지도 다 그 주의 서부에 있었기 때문이다.\n\n팰리서 씨는 세상에 주로 떠오르는 정치인으로 알려져 있었다. 즐길 거리라면 이 세상이 사람에게 줄 수 있는 것을 모조리 손안에 쥐고 있었다고 말해도 좋을 것이다. 재산이 있었고, 지위가 있었고, 권세가 있었으며, 아마도 이 세상에서 가장 빛나는 귀족 사회 안에서 가장 높은 신분에 오르리라는 것이 확실했다. 가까이 다가가 비위를 맞출 수 있는 사람이라면 누구나 그의 비위를 맞추었다. 영국 여인 가운데 가장 아름답고 가장 훌륭한 사람들 중에서 신부를 골라잡을 수 있었으리라고 해도 지나친 말은 아닐 것이다. 경주마를 사들이고 경마에 수천 파운드를 쏟아부었다면 숙부를 흡족하게 해 주었을 것이다. 사냥개 무리의 주인이 될 수도 있었고, 새를 백 마리씩 잡아 죽이는 사람이 될 수도 있었다. 그러나 그런 것에는 하나도 몸을 바치지 않았다. 그는 정치인이 되기를 택했고, 그 길에서 어떤 직업에서든 어떤 장사에서든 한밑천을 잡았을 만한 열성과 끈기로 애를 썼다. 팔월 한복판이 될 때까지도 그는 위원회실을 지켰다. 중요한 토론에는 좀처럼 빠지지 않았고 중요한 표결에는 한 번도 빠지지 않았다. 말은 드물게 했으나, 필요하다면 언제든 말할 채비가 되어 있었다. 그에게 대단한 천재성이 있다고 인정해 주는 사람은 없었다. 그가 웅변가나 위대한 정치가가 되리라고 여기는 사람도 거의 없었다. 그러나 세상은 그를 떠오르는 인물이라고 말했고, 내각의 늙은 네스토르들은 그를 언젠가 먼 훗날 아우뻘로 저희 틈에 끼어들 수 있는 사람으로 여겼다. 여태까지 그는 저에게 주어진 낮은 자리들은 사양하며 때를 조심스레 기다렸다. 그리고 정치적 성향은 온통 자유주의 쪽이라고 알려져 있었으나 아직 어느 당파에도 손발이 묶여 있지 않았다. 그는 책을 많이 읽는 사람이었다—우연히 눈앞에 오는 대로 이 책 한 권 저 책 한 권 집어 드는 식이 아니라, 어마어마한 분량의 책을 차례차례 헤쳐 나가며 세계사라는 큰 주제를 익히고 제 안을 사실로 가득 채우는 식이었다—다만 그 사실들을 선례로 쓰는 것 말고 달리 부릴 힘까지 얻을 운명은 아니었는지도 모른다. 또한 여러 나라 말에 능해지려고 부지런히 애썼고, 여러 언어를 그럭저럭 알아듣는 정도까지는 성과를 거두었다. 그는 생각의 폭이 좁고 우직하게 일하는 점잖은 사람이었으니, 늙어서 나라의 중신들 틈에 앉는 것이 허락되기만 한다면 제 젊음을 통째로 일에 바칠 각오가 되어 있는 사람이었다.\n\n여태까지 그의 이름이 그가 사모한다고 여겨지는 어떤 여자의 이름과 나란히 오르내린 적은 없었다. 그런데 근래 들어 그가 레이디 덤벨로와 같은 방에 있는 모습이 자주 눈에 띈다는 말이 돌기 시작했다. 그 이상은 거의 아무것도 아니었다. 그러나 이 두 사람이 얼마나 내색을 하지 않는 사람들인지—어느 쪽이든 감정을 강하게 드러내는 것을 얼마나 싫어하는지를 떠올려 보면, 그 정도만 해도 입에 올릴 만한 일이라고 여겨졌던 것이다. 그는 이따금 거의 관심 있는 기색을 띠고 그녀에게 말을 걸곤 했다. 그리고 레이디 덤벨로는 그가 방 안에 있는 것을 보면 얼마간 생기 있는 낌새로 고개를 들고, 마치 눈길을 얹어 둘 만한 무엇이 저기 있기라도 한 듯이 둘러보는 모습이 눈에 띄곤 했다. 그런 뒷말이 떠돌 때 그것을 드 코시 백작부인보다 더 크게 부풀릴 사람은 아마 없었을 것이다. 팰리서 씨가 성에 온다는 말을 듣고 그쪽 방면에서 거둔 그녀의 성공에 놀라움을 나타낸 사람이 많았다. 레이디 덤벨로가 그녀의 손님이 되기로 했다는 것을 알고 크게 놀란 사람들도 있었다. 그러나 그 둘이 함께 그곳에 온다는 것이 밝혀지자 마음씨 좋은 벗들은 그녀가 참으로 영리한 여자라고 인정했다. 팰리서 씨든 레이디 덤벨로든 하나만 모셔 와도 모자에 깃털 하나를 꽂는 셈이었을 것이다. 그러나 서로 상대가 온다는 것을 알려 주는 방식으로 둘 다 잡아채는 데 성공한 것은 참으로 개가였다. 다만 레이디 덤벨로 쪽에서 보자면 그 거래는 온전히 이행되지 않은 셈이었다. 결국 팰리서 씨는 이틀 밤과 하루만 묵으러 코시 성에 왔고, 그 하루 동안에는 온종일 이런저런 큼직한 청서에 파묻혀 방에 틀어박혀 있었으니 말이다. 드 코시 백작부인으로 말하자면 그가 무엇에 매달려 있든 상관하지 않았다. 그녀에게는 청서든 레이디 덤벨로든 매한가지였다. 팰리서 씨가 코시 성에 와 있었다는 것, 이는 적도 벗도 부인할 수 없는 사실이었으니까.\n\n이날이 그가 묵는 두 번째 저녁이었다. 그리고 그는 이튿날 낮 한 시에 실버브리지에서 저를 뽑아 준 사람들을 만나 제 처신과 세상 전반의 정치 형편을 설명해 주기로 약속해 둔 터였고, 실버브리지에서 코시로 돌아오지도 않을 것이었으므로, 레이디 덤벨로가 조금이라도 앞으로 나아갈 작정이라면 지금 이 시간이 그녀에게 내어 주는 그 짧은 볕뉘를 붙잡는 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 팰리서 씨의 관심을 독차지하려는 적극적인 기색을 그녀가 내비쳤다고 말할 사람은 아무도 없었을 것이다. 그가 어슬렁어슬렁 응접실로 들어섰을 때 그녀는 혼자 앉아 있었다. 옷자락을 활짝 펼칠 수 있도록 팔걸이 없이 만든 낮고 널찍한 의자였는데, 등받이 쪽은 옴폭하고 둥글게 되어 있어 그녀에게 꼭 필요한 받침이 되어 주었다. 식당을 나온 뒤로 그녀가 입 밖에 낸 말은 세 마디도 되지 않았으나, 그렇다고 시간이 지루하게 흐른 것은 아니었다. 레이디 줄리아가 릴리 데일과 크로즈비 씨를 두고 백작부인에게 또 한 번 덤벼들었고, 알렉산드리나는 화가 치밀다 못해 이 일에 저만이 각별히 마음을 쓰고 있다는 것을 감추지도 않은 채 방 저쪽 끝으로 성큼성큼 가 버린 참이었다.\n\n“그 사람들이 어서 혼인해서 다 끝나 버렸으면 좋겠소.” 백작부인이 말했다. “그러면 그 얘기를 더는 안 들어도 될 것 아니오.”\n\n이 모든 것을 레이디 덤벨로는 다 듣고 다 알아들었으며, 그 모두에 얼마간 관심을 두었다. 그녀는 그런 것들을 기억해 두었다가 누가 어떤 사람인지를 익혔고, 그렇게 익힌 것에 맞추어 제 처신을 다스렸다. 이런 때나 그 밖의 비슷한 자리에서 그녀는 결코 놀고 있는 것이 아니었으니, 제 나름의 일하는 방식으로 꽤나 고된 일을 적잖이 해내고 있었다고 말해도 좋을 것이다. 그녀는 그 자리에 말없이 앉아 있었고, 다만 곁에 있는 사람들이 늘어놓는 아첨에 나직한 한마디로 맞장구를 쳐 줄 때만 입을 열었다. 그러다 문이 열렸다. 팰리서 씨가 들어서자 그녀는 고개를 들었고, 그 얼굴에서 더할 나위 없이 희미한 만족의 빛을 알아볼 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 그에게 말을 걸려는 시늉조차 하지 않았다. 그가 탁자 곁에 서서 책 한 권을 집어 들고는 그대로 십오 분이나 서 있었을 때도, 그녀는 조바심을 내보이지도 않았고 느끼지도 않았다. 그 뒤에 덤벨로 경이 들어와 책도 없이 탁자 곁에 섰다. 그때도 레이디 덤벨로는 조바심을 내지 않았다.\n\n플랜태저넷 팰리서는 그 작은 책을 대충 훑어보았고, 아마 무언가를 배웠을 것이다. 책을 내려놓고 그는 차를 한 모금 홀짝이더니 드 코시 백작부인에게 실버브리지까지가 십이 마일밖에 안 되는 줄로 안다고 말했다.\n\n“백열두 마일이면 좋겠소.” 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 저는 오늘 밤에 떠나야 할 것입니다.” 팰리서 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 천십이 마일이면 좋겠구려.” 드 코시 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 저는 아예 오지도 않았을 것입니다.” 팰리서 씨가 말했다. 무례하게 굴 뜻은 없었고, 그저 사실을 말했을 뿐이었다.\n\n“요즘 젊은 남자들은 아주 곰이 다 되었구나.” 백작부인이 딸 마거리타에게 말했다.\n\n방에 들어온 지 거의 한 시간이 지나서야 그는 마침내 레이디 덤벨로 가까이에 서 있는 저를 발견했다. 그녀 곁에, 그것도 아주 가까이에 다른 사람이라고는 아무도 없이.\n\n“여기서 뵈리라고는 좀처럼 생각하지 못했소.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“저도 그랬어요.” 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“하기야 그 점을 따지자면 우리 둘 다 제집 가까이에 와 있는 셈이오만.”\n\n“저는 제집 가까이가 아니에요.”\n\n“플럼스테드를 말한 것이오. 그대 아버님 댁 말이오.”\n\n“네, 한때는 그곳이 제집이었지요.”\n\n“숙부님 댁을 보여 드릴 수 있으면 좋겠소. 그 성은 아주 훌륭하고, 좋은 그림도 몇 점 있소.”\n\n“그렇다고 들었어요.”\n\n“여기 오래 머무시오?”\n\n“아니에요. 모레 체셔로 갑니다. 덤벨로 경은 사냥철이 시작되면 늘 그곳에 있거든요.”\n\n“아, 그렇지요. 물론 그렇겠소. 참 팔자 좋은 사람이오. 할 일이라고는 도무지 없으니! 그를 뽑아 준 사람들이 그를 성가시게 하는 일은 없겠지요?”\n\n“그런 일은 별로 없는 것 같아요.”\n\n그러고서 팰리서 씨는 다시 어슬렁어슬렁 자리를 떴고, 레이디 덤벨로는 그날 저녁의 남은 시간을 잠자코 보냈다. 두 사람 다 코시 성까지 오느라 겪은 불편을 그 십 분 동안의 마음 맞는 교제로 보상받았기를 바랄 따름이다.\n\n그러나 우리 눈에 그토록 죄 없어 보이는 그 일을 그 집안의 준엄한 도덕가들은 다른 빛 아래에서 바라보았다.\n\n“이거 참!” 아너러블 조지가 사촌인 그레셤 씨에게 말했다. “덤벨로가 저걸 어떻게 여길지 궁금하군.”\n\n“내가 보기엔 덤벨로가 아주 태평하게 받아넘기는 것 같은데.”\n\n“무슨 일이든 태평하게 받아넘기는 사내들이 있지.” 조지가 말했다. 제가 혼인한 뒤로 그런 못된 일이라면 거룩한 진저리를 치는 법을 배운 사람이었다.\n\n“저 여자도 이제 슬슬 나오기 시작하는구려.” 뒤쪽 어느 거실 난롯가에 두 늙은 여자가 마주 앉게 되었을 때, 레이디 클랜디들럼이 드 코시 백작부인에게 말했다. “잔잔한 물이 늘 깊은 법 아니오.”\n\n“저 여자가 저이와 달아난다 해도 나는 조금도 놀라지 않겠소.” 드 코시 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n“저이가 그런 바보짓을 할 리야 있겠소.” 레이디 클랜디들럼이 말했다.\n\n“남자들은 무슨 짓이든 저지를 만큼 어리석다고 나는 믿소.” 드 코시 백작부인이 말했다. “하지만 물론 그이가 그런다 한들 나중에는 아무 일도 아닌 것이 되고 말 거요. 섭섭해하지 않을 사람을 나는 하나 알고 있소. 사내가 여자에게 물리는 일이 있다면, 덤벨로 경은 그 여자에게 물릴 대로 물렸다오.”\n\n그러나 이 일에서도 다른 거의 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 그 못된 늙은 여자는 헛소문을 옮긴 것이었다. 덤벨로 경은 여전히 제 아내를 자랑스러워했고, 그 애정이 오로지 자부심에만 기대고 있는 남자가 여자를 아낄 수 있는 만큼은 그녀를 아꼈다.\n\n팰리서 씨와 레이디 덤벨로 사이에 오간 이야기에는 위험한 구석이 별로 없었다. 그러나 바로 그 시각에 크로즈비와 레이디 알렉산드리나 사이에서 벌어지고 있던 이야기를 두고는 그렇게 말할 수가 없다. 앞서 말했다시피 그녀는 레이디 줄리아가 가엾은 릴리 이야기로 다시 공격을 시작하자 거의 대놓고 골이 나서 자리를 떴고, 그날 저녁 내내 사람들 무리로 돌아오지 않았다. 코시 성에는 큰 응접실이 둘 있었고, 그 둘을 좁다란 방 하나가 고리처럼 이어 주고 있었다. 바닥까지 내려오는 창이 둘 나 있어 밝고, 응접실과 마찬가지로 양탄자가 깔려 있으며, 벽난로가 따로 있어 데워지지 않았더라면 그저 복도라 불렸을 방이었다. 그녀는 그곳으로 물러갔고, 얼마 지나지 않아 혼인한 언니 어밀리아가 뒤따라왔다.\n\n“저 여자 때문에 미쳐 버릴 지경이야.” 두 사람이 발끝을 난로 울에 얹고 나란히 선 채로 알렉산드리나가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 얘, 다른 사람은 몰라도 네가 그런 일로 미쳐서야 되겠니.”\n\n“말이야 그렇지, 어밀리아 언니.”\n\n“문제는 이거야, 얘—크로즈비 씨가 어쩔 작정이냐는 것.”\n\n“내가 그걸 어떻게 알아?”\n\n“네가 모르겠거든 그이가 그 처녀와 혼인할 거라고 치는 편이 안전해. 그리고 그렇다면—”\n\n“그렇다면 뭐? 언니도 또 하나의 레이디 줄리아가 될 참이야? 내가 그 처녀를 무슨 상관한다고?”\n\n“네가 그 처녀를 상관하지 않는 건 알아. 그리고 크로즈비 씨도 그만큼 상관없다면 그것으로 끝이지. 다만 그렇다면, 알렉산드리나—”\n\n“그렇다면 뭐?”\n\n“너한테 설교하고 싶지 않다는 건 알잖니. 그이가 정말로 마음에 드는지 아닌지 그냥 대번에 말해 줄 수는 없어? 우리 둘은 늘 사이가 좋았잖니.” 그러고서 혼인한 언니는 혼인하고 싶어 하는 동생의 허리를 다정하게 팔로 감쌌다.\n\n“그이가 마음에 들어, 충분히.”\n\n“그럼 그이가 너한테 무슨 고백이라도 했니?”\n\n“어떻게 보면 했지. 쉿, 저기 오네!” 그러고서 더 큰 방에서 건너온 크로즈비가 난롯가의 두 자매와 어울렸다.\n\n“레이디 줄리아의 혀가 딸그락대는 통에 쫓겨 왔다오.” 언니가 말했다.\n\n“저런 여자는 처음 만나 봅니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“저런 여자가 여럿 있을 것 같지는 않네요.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다. 그러고서 세 사람은 일이 분 동안 말없이 서 있었다. 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비는 두 사람을 남겨 두고 자리를 뜨는 편이 좋을지 어떨지 궁리하고 있었다. 크로즈비 씨가 제 동생과 혼인하기로 되어 있는 것이라면, 그가 제 뜻을 스스로 말로 나타낼 기회를 주는 것이 분명 좋을 터였다. 그러나 알렉산드리나가 그저 바보짓을 하고 있는 것이라면 그때는 남아 있는 편이 좋을 것이었다. “내가 가 주기를 바라겠지.” 언니는 속으로 그렇게 말했다. 그러고는 사람과 사람 사이의 모든 행동을 이끌어야 마땅한 그 계율에 따라 되돌아가 사람들 무리에 끼었다.\n\n“저쪽 방으로 가시겠습니까?” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“여기도 아주 좋은 것 같은데요.” 알렉산드리나가 대답했다.\n\n“하지만 드릴 말씀이 있습니다—긴히요.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“여기서는 못 하시나요?”\n\n“못 합니다. 사람들이 이리로 저리로 오갈 테니까요.” 레이디 알렉산드리나는 더는 아무 말도 하지 않고 앞장서서 다른 큰 응접실로 갔다. 그곳에도 불이 켜져 있었고 네댓 사람이 있었다. 레이디 로지나는 한쪽 구석에서 저 혼자 등불을 켜 놓고 천년왕국에 관한 책을 읽고 있었다. 그녀의 오라비 존은 안락의자에서 잠들어 있었고, 젊은 신사와 아가씨가 체스를 두고 있었다. 그러나 크로즈비와 알렉산드리나가 따로 떨어져 자리를 잡을 만한 데는 넉넉했다.\n\n“자, 크로즈비 씨, 저에게 하실 말씀이 무엇인가요? 하지만 먼저, 그러겠다고 말씀드린 대로 레이디 줄리아가 한 그 질문을 그대로 되풀이하겠어요.—데일 양에게서 마지막으로 소식을 들으신 것이 언제죠?”\n\n“이미 말씀드린 것이 있는데도 그런 질문을 하시다니 잔인하십니다. 제가 데일 양에게 혼인 약속을 했다는 것을 아시지 않습니까.”\n\n“그렇군요. 그렇게 온 세상이 다 아는 이야기를 하려고 굳이 저를 여기까지 데려오실 까닭이 무엇인지 모르겠네요. 레이디 줄리아 같은 전령이 있는데 그럴 필요는 조금도 없었잖아요.”\n\n“그런 말투로밖에 대답해 주실 수 없다면 저는 이 일을 당장 끝내겠습니다. 제 약혼 이야기를 했을 때 저는 다른 여자가 제 마음을 차지하고 있다는 말도 함께 드렸습니다. 제가 누구를 가리킨 것인지 당신이 아셨으리라고 여기는 것이 잘못입니까?”\n\n“정말이지 몰랐어요, 크로즈비 씨. 저는 요술쟁이가 아니고, 당신 벗인 레이디 줄리아만큼 당신을 샅샅이 뜯어본 것도 아니니까요.”\n\n“제가 사랑하는 사람은 당신입니다. 이제 와서 굳이 말할 것도 없겠지요.”\n\n“정말이지 그렇겠네요—당신이 데일 양과 약혼했다는 것을 생각하면요.”\n\n“그 점에 대해서는 물론 제가 어리석게 굴었다고 인정해야겠지요—당신이 그렇게 말하고 싶으시다면 어리석은 것보다 더한 짓이었다고요. 제가 저를 단죄하는 것보다 더 모질게 저를 단죄하실 수는 없을 겁니다. 그러나 한 가지만은 마음을 정했습니다. 저는 사랑하지 않는 곳에서는 혼인하지 않겠습니다.” 아, 그때 그가 그렇게 말하는 것을 릴리가 들을 수 있었더라면! “데일 양을 아무리 높여 말한들 지나치지 않을 것입니다. 그러나 제가 그 사람의 남편이 되어 그 사람을 행복하게 해 줄 수는 없으리라는 것만은 아주 확실합니다.”\n\n“청혼하시기 전에는 왜 그 생각을 못 하셨나요?” 알렉산드리나가 말했다. 그러나 그 말투에는 나무라는 기색이 거의 없었다.\n\n“그랬어야 했지요. 하지만 당신이 저를 모질게 나무랄 처지는 아니지 않습니까. 우리가 지난번 런던에서 함께 있었을 때 당신이—당신이 조금만 덜—”\n\n“덜 무엇이요?”\n\n“덜 도도하셨더라면,” 크로즈비가 말했다. “이 모든 일을 어쩌면 피할 수 있었을 겁니다.”\n\n레이디 알렉산드리나는 제가 도도하게 굴었던 기억이 나지 않았다. 그러나 어쨌든 그것은 그냥 넘겼다. “아, 그렇지요. 물론 제 잘못이었겠지요.”\n\n“저는 마음이 편치 않은 채로 앨링턴에 내려갔고, 그러다 이 곤경에 빠진 것입니다. 있었던 그대로 다 말씀드립니다. 제가 데일 양과 혼인한다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일입니다. 제 마음이 온통 다른 사람의 것인데 그렇게 한다면 그것은 몹쓸 짓이 될 테지요. 그 다른 사람이 누구인지는 말씀드렸습니다. 이제 대답을 바라도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“무엇에 대한 대답이요?”\n\n“알렉산드리나, 제 아내가 되어 주시겠습니까?”\n\n그를 에두르지 않은 고백과 혼인 신청에까지 끌어내는 것이 그녀의 목적이었다면, 이제 그 목적은 분명히 이룬 셈이었다. 그리고 그녀는 제 수완과 제 어머니의 수완을 어찌나 믿었던지, 그를 받아들였다가 그가 릴리 데일에게 하고 있는 그 짓을 저도 당하게 될 위험을 겁내지 않았다. 그러기에는 제 처지도 그의 처지도 너무 잘 알고 있었다. 그를 받아들인다면 때가 되어 그의 아내가 될 것이었다—데일 양이며 그 벗들이 무어라 반대해 떠들어 대든 말이다. 그 점에서는 두려울 것이 없었다. 그런데도 그녀는 그를 대뜸 받아들이지는 않았다. 그 전리품을 바라면서도, 여자로 태어난 천성이 그것을 내미는 대로 냉큼 받아 쥐는 것을 가로막았다.\n\n“크로즈비 씨,” 그녀가 말했다. “똑같은 질문을 데일 양에게 하신 지는 얼마나 되었죠?”\n\n“저는 당신에게 모든 것을 말씀드렸습니다, 알렉산드리나—그러겠다고 약속한 대로요. 그렇게 한 것을 두고 저를 벌하실 작정이라면—”\n\n“그리고 저는 또 하나 물어볼 수도 있겠지요. 똑같은 질문을 또 다른 아가씨에게 하시기까지는 얼마나 걸릴까요?”\n\n그는 성이 나서 그녀에게서 걸어 나가려는 듯이 몸을 돌렸다. 그러나 문까지 절반쯤 갔을 때 되돌아왔다.\n\n“맹세코,” 그가 말했는데, 말투도 얼마간 거칠었다. “저는 대답을 듣고야 말겠습니다. 적어도 당신에게는 저를 나무랄 거리가 없습니다. 제가 저지른 잘못은 모두 당신 때문에, 그렇지 않으면 당신을 위해 저지른 것입니다. 제 청혼을 들으셨습니다. 받아들이실 작정입니까?”\n\n“정말이지 깜짝 놀랐네요. 제 돈이나 목숨을 내놓으라 하신다 해도 이보다 더 위압적일 수는 없겠어요.”\n\n“제 결심이 이보다 더 굳을 수는 없다는 것만은 분명합니다.”\n\n“제가 그 영광을 사양한다면요?”\n\n“당신을 여자들 가운데 가장 변덕스러운 사람이라 여기겠지요.”\n\n“그럼 제가 받아들인다면요?”\n\n“당신이 여자들 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 사랑스럽고 가장 다정한 사람이라고 맹세하겠지요.”\n\n“나쁘게 여기시는 것보다야 좋게 여기시는 편이 낫겠지요, 물론.” 레이디 알렉산드리나가 말했다. 그러고서 두 사람 다 그 일이 정해졌다는 것을 알아들었다. 앞으로 릴리 이야기를 해야 할 일이 있을 때면 그녀는 언제나 그녀를 ‘그 가엾은 데일 양’이라고 불렀다. 그러나 그 자잘한 사건을 두고 제 미래의 주인에게 나무라는 말을 두 번 다시 한마디도 하지 않았다. “오늘 밤에 어머니께 말씀드릴게요.” 두 사람이 물러가 있던 어느 후미진 구석에서 그에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하며 그녀가 말했다. 그 후미진 구석에서 두 사람이 나올 때 레이디 줄리아의 눈이 또 그들에게 쏠려 있었으나, 알렉산드리나는 이제 레이디 줄리아 따위는 아랑곳하지 않았다.\n\n“조지, 그 팰리서 씨라는 분은 도무지 모르겠어요. 공작이 될 사람 아닌가요? 그렇다면 지금도 경이어야 하는 것 아니에요?” 조지 드 코시 부인이 부부의 침실이라는 은밀한 곳에 남편과 단둘이 있게 되었을 때 던진 질문이었다.\n\n“그래, 그 늙은이가 죽으면 옴니엄 공작이 되지. 내가 여태 만나 본 사람 중에 제일 굼뜬 축에 드는 것 같더군. 하기야 재산 하나는 지독히도 잘 건사하겠지만.”\n\n“하지만 조지, 좀 설명해 줘요. 모르고 있으려니 어찌나 바보 같은지, 실수할까 무서워서 입도 못 떼겠어요.”\n\n“그럼 입을 다물고 있어, 여보. 그런 건 차차 다 알게 될 테고, 아무 말도 안 하면 아무도 눈치채지 못하니까.”\n\n“그건 그렇지만, 조지—밤새 잠자코 앉아 있는 건 싫어요. 아무 얘기도 못 할 바에는 차라리 여기 올라와서 소설이나 읽는 편이 낫겠어요.”\n\n“레이디 덤벨로를 봐. 그 사람은 노상 떠들고 싶어 하지 않잖아.”\n\n“레이디 덤벨로는 나하고 아주 다르지요. 그건 그렇고 좀 말해 줘요. 팰리서 씨는 대체 누구예요?”\n\n“공작의 조카야. 공작의 아들이었다면 실버브리지 후작이 되었겠지.”\n\n“그럼 숙부가 죽을 때까지는 그냥 밋밋한 씨로 불리는 거예요?”\n\n“그래, 아주 밋밋한 씨지.”\n\n“그이한테는 참 안됐네요. 그런데 조지—내가 아기를 낳고, 그 아기가 아들이고, 그리고 만약—”\n\n“아, 무슨 소리야. 그런 얘기는 그 녀석이 나오고 나서 해도 늦지 않아. 난 잘 테야.”\n\n# 제24장. 장모와 장인\n\n이튿날 아침 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 아침 식사도 하기 전에 정치 임무를 띠고 떠났다. 그게 아니라면, 혼자 먹는 롤빵과 커피라는 모양새로 그에게 어떤 사사로운 위안이 주어졌든가. 코시 성의 공식 아침 식사는 열한 시에 차려졌고, 그 시각에 팰리서 씨는 이미 실버브리지 시장과 단둘이 마주 앉아 밀담을 나누고 있었다.\n\n“나는 3시 45분 기차로 떠나야 하오.” 팰리서 씨가 말했다. “내 뒤에 연설할 사람은 누가 있소?”\n\n“글쎄요, 제가 몇 마디 하겠습니다. 그리고 그라우디가—그 사람은 사람들이 제 말을 들어 주기를 바랄 겁니다. 그라우디는 늘 공작 각하 편에 굳건히 서 온 사람입니다, 팰리서 씨.”\n\n“한 시 정각에는 회장에 들어가도록 신경 쓰시오. 그리고 내가 역으로 갈 삯마차를 마당에 대기시켜 두시오. 어쩔 수 없이 떠나야 할 때가 되기 전에는 한시도 먼저 자리를 뜨지 않겠소. 내가 맡을 몫은 딱 한 시간 반이오. 아니, 고맙지만 됐소. 나는 아침에는 술을 입에 대지 않소.” 그리고 여기서 나는, 팰리서 씨가 정말로 3시 45분 기차로 떠났고 그라우디 씨는 승강장에서 여전히 이야기를 늘어놓고 있었다는 사실을 밝혀 두어야겠다. 유권자들은 존중을 받아야 마땅하다. 그러나 요즈음은 시간이 하도 귀해져서 그 존중이라는 것도 십오 분 단위로 인색하게 아껴 가며 나누어 주어야 하는 것이다.\n\n그러는 동안 코시 성에는 한결 여유가 있었다. 백작부인도 레이디 알렉산드리나도 아침 식사에 내려오지 않았지만, 두 사람이 보이지 않는다고 해서 특별히 말이 나오지는 않았다. 성의 아침 식사란 사람들이 내키는 대로 얼굴을 내밀기도 하고 내밀지 않기도 하는 아침 끼니였다. 레이디 줄리아는 몹시 시무룩한 얼굴로 그 자리에 있었고, 크로즈비는 장차 처형이 될 마거리타 옆에 앉아 있었는데, 그녀는 벌써부터 그와 각별히 다정한 사이가 되어 있었다. 그가 차를 다 마시자 그녀가 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. “크로즈비 씨, 반 시간만 내실 수 있다면 어머니께서 자기 방에서 당신을 뵙고 싶어 하세요.” 크로즈비는 기꺼이 찾아뵙겠다고 말했고, 사위로서 이 집에 맞아들여지는 데 대해 실제로 얼마간 고마움을 느끼기도 했다. 그러면서도 그는 자기가 붙잡히고 있다는 것을, 그리고 백작부인의 사사로운 영역으로 올라가는 것이 곧 제 감금에 스스로 봉인을 찍는 일이라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n그럼에도 그는 미소 띤 얼굴로 가벼운 걸음을 옮겼고, 레이디 마거리타가 앞서 길을 안내했다. “어머니.” 그녀가 말했다. “크로즈비 씨를 모시고 왔어요. 알렉산드리나, 네가 여기 있는 줄은 몰랐구나. 알았으면 이분께 미리 일러 두었을 텐데.”\n\n백작부인과 그 막내딸은 어머니 쪽 거실에서 함께 아침을 먹고 있었고, 이제는 아주 우아하고 매무새를 잘 다듬은 실내복 차림으로 앉아 있었다. 두 사람이 마시던 찻잔은 고급 자기로 만든 것이었고, 찻주전자와 크림 단지는 돋을새김을 한 은제로 그 나름대로 섬세했다. 먹다 남은 것이라고는 부스러기 하나 흐트러뜨리는 일조차 허락받지 못한 프랑스식 롤빵 몇 조각과, 티끌만 한 버터 덩이 몇 개뿐이었다. 두 부인의 아침 끼니가 그 남은 조각들이 말해 주는 것만큼이나 부실한 것이었다면, 두 사람은 점심을 일찍 들 작정이었다고 보아야 할 것이다. 백작부인은 무늬 있는 비단으로 공들여 지은 아침 실내복을 걸치고 있었으나, 소박한 알렉산드리나는 분홍 리본으로 여민 밋밋한 흰 모슬린 실내 가운을 입고 있었다. 평소에는 길게 말아 올리고 다니던 머리채가 지금은 어깨 위로 풀어 내려져 있었는데, 그것이 그녀가 지닌 여성적 매력에 무언가를 확실히 보태 주고 있었다. 크로즈비가 들어서자 백작부인은 자리에서 일어나 손을 내밀며 그를 맞았다. 그러나 알렉산드리나는 앉은 자리를 지킨 채 그저 고개를 조금 끄덕여 인사를 대신했다. “저는 다시 내려가 봐야겠어요.” 마거리타가 말했다. “안 그러면 어밀리아 언니한테 집안일을 전부 떠맡기게 될 테니까요.”\n\n“알렉산드리나가 나한테 다 이야기했어요.” 백작부인이 더없이 상냥한 미소를 지으며 말했다. “그리고 나는 그 애한테 승낙을 해 주었지요. 정말이지 두 사람은 서로 아주 잘 어울릴 것 같군요.”\n\n“대단히 감사합니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “적어도 이것만은 확실합니다. 따님은 저한테 아주 잘 어울릴 겁니다.”\n\n“그럼요, 나도 그러리라 생각해요. 착하고 분별 있는 아이지요.”\n\n“쳇, 어머니, 제발 그런 굿디 투슈즈 같은 투로 말씀하지 마세요.”\n\n“너는 정말 그렇단다, 얘야. 그렇지 않았다면 네가 지금 하려는 일을 하는 것이 좋을 리 없지. 네가 경박하고 덜렁대는 아이였다면, 그리고 지위니 재산이니 하는 것에 마음을 온통 빼앗긴 아이였다면, 재산 없는 평민과 결혼하는 것이 너한테 좋을 리 없을 테고. 크로즈비 씨도 내가 이만큼 말하는 것쯤은 너그러이 봐주시리라 믿어요.”\n\n“저야 물론 압니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “제가 그토록 높은 곳을 넘볼 처지가 아니었다는 것을요.”\n\n“자, 그 이야기는 이제 그만합시다.” 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n“제발 그만하세요.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다. “꼭 설교 같잖아요.”\n\n“앉으세요, 크로즈비 씨.” 백작부인이 말했다. “우리 이야기나 좀 나눕시다. 원하신다면 저 애를 당신 곁에 앉히지요. 무슨 소리냐, 알렉산드리나. 이분이 청하시는데!”\n\n“그만두세요, 어머니. 저는 여기 그대로 있겠어요.”\n\n“좋아, 얘야. 그럼 그 자리에 그대로 있으렴. 고집이 센 아이예요, 크로즈비 씨. 어젯밤에 당신이 저 애한테 한 말을 저 애가 나한테 죄다 옮겼다는 걸 들으시면 당신도 그렇게 말씀하실 거예요.” 이 말을 듣고 그는 안색이 조금 변했으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다. “저 애가 나한테 이야기하더군요.” 백작부인이 말을 이었다. “앨링턴의 그 젊은 아가씨에 대해서요. 정말이지, 당신은 아주 짓궂게 굴었던 모양이에요.”\n\n“제가 어리석었습니다, 드 코시 백작부인.”\n\n“물론이지요. 나도 그보다 더 나쁜 뜻으로 말한 건 아니에요. 그래요, 당신은 어리석었어요. 아무 생각 없이 심심풀이를 한 거지요. 그리고 어쩌면, 어떤 아가씨가 전하께서 바라시는 만큼 쉽게 넘어오지 않아서 자존심이 조금 상했던 것일 테고요. 자, 이제 그 일은 되도록 빨리 정리되어야 해요. 나는 경솔한 질문을 하고 싶지는 않아요. 하지만 그 아가씨가 정말로 당신에게 무슨 뜻이 있었던 것으로 알고 남아 있다면, 당장 그 오해를 풀어 주어야 하지 않겠어요?”\n\n“물론 그러실 거예요, 어머니.”\n\n“물론 그러시겠지요. 그리고 그 일이 정리되었다는 걸 알면 알렉산드리나한테도 큰 위안이 될 거예요. 레이디 줄리아가 하루에도 몇 번씩 무슨 말을 하고 다니는지 당신도 들으셨겠지요. 물론 알렉산드리나야 레이디 줄리아 같은 노처녀가 무슨 말을 하든 개의치 않아요. 하지만 그 소문이 그치는 편이 모두에게 나을 거예요. 백작이 그 이야기를 듣기라도 하면, 그이는 말이지요—” 그러고 나서 백작부인은 고개를 내저었는데, 백작이 무슨 일이든 벌이기로 마음을 먹었을 때 어떻게 나올 수 있는지를 그렇게 하는 것이 가장 잘 내비치는 길이라고 여겼던 것이다.\n\n크로즈비는 릴리에 관해 백작부인과 마음을 터놓고 이야기할 마음이 도무지 나지 않았다. 그러나 그는 물론 되도록 지체 없이 데일 양에게 진실을 알리겠다고 웅얼거리듯 다짐했다. 언제 편지를 쓰겠다고도, 그녀에게 쓸지 그녀의 어머니에게 쓸지도 정확히 말할 수는 없었지만, 그 일만은 런던에 돌아가는 즉시 처리하겠다고 했다.\n\n“그러는 편이 일을 쉽게 만든다면, 내가 데일 부인에게 편지를 쓰지요.” 백작부인이 말했다. 그러나 이 계획에는 크로즈비 씨가 아주 강하게 반대했다.\n\n그러고 나서 백작에 관해 몇 마디가 오갔다. “오늘 오후에 내가 그이한테 말하겠어요.” 백작부인이 말했다. “그러면 당신은 내일 아침에 그이를 만나면 돼요. 그이가 별말은 하지 않을 거예요. 그리고 어쩌면—내가 이런 말을 해도 언짢아하지 않으시리라 믿어요—알렉산드리나가 더 나은 자리를 골랐을 수도 있었다고 생각할지도 모르지요. 하지만 그이가 크게 반대하리라고는 믿지 않아요. 물론 혼인 재산 계약이니 뭐니 하는 이야기는 좀 있을 거예요.” 그런 다음 백작부인은 자리를 떴고, 알렉산드리나는 반 시간 동안 연인과 함께 남겨졌다. 그 반 시간이 지났을 때, 그는 제 삶의 지난 스물네 시간을 되돌릴 수만 있다면 이 세상에서 가진 것을 전부 내놓아도 좋겠다고 느꼈다. 그러나 그에게는 아무런 희망이 없었다. 릴리 데일을 차 버리는 일이야 틀림없이 그의 힘으로 할 수 있는 일이었겠지만, 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시를 차 버릴 수는 없다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 열두 시에 그는 아버지 되는 이와 면담을 가졌는데, 그것은 대단히 불쾌한 면담이었다. 그는 백작의 방으로 안내되었고, 벽난로를 등진 채 난롯가 깔개 위에 서서 두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣고 있는 그 대단한 귀족과 마주쳤다.\n\n“그래, 자네가 내 딸과 결혼하겠다고?” 그가 말했다. “보다시피 나는 몸이 좋지 않네. 늘 그렇긴 하네만.”\n\n이 마지막 말은 크로즈비의 인사에 대한 대답이었다. 크로즈비가 백작에게 손을 내밀었고 그 수를 관철한 덕에, 백작은 어쩔 수 없이 주머니에서 한쪽 손을 꺼내어 사위가 되겠다는 사람에게 내어 주어야 했던 것이다.\n\n“백작님께서 반대하지 않으신다면 말입니다. 어쨌든 저는 따님의 허락을 받고서 백작님의 허락을 청하는 것입니다.”\n\n“자네한테는 재산이 없다지, 그렇지 않은가? 그 애도 없네. 물론 그건 알고 있겠지?”\n\n“저에게 몇천 파운드가 있고, 따님께도 그만큼 있는 줄로 압니다.”\n\n“둘이 굶어 죽지 않을 만큼 빵이나 사면 딱 맞겠군. 나야 알 바 아니네. 원한다면 그 애와 결혼하게. 다만, 이보게, 허튼짓은 용납하지 않겠네. 오늘 아침에 어떤 늙은 여자가 나를 찾아왔네—이 집에 묵고 있는 사람들 가운데 하나지—자네가 어떤 다른 아가씨를 우롱했다는 이야기를 늘어놓더군. 자네가 그런 짓을 얼마나 했든 나와는 상관없네. 여기서만 그러지 않으면 되네. 그러나—나한테 그런 수작을 부렸다가는 자네가 잘못 짚었다는 것을 알게 될 걸세.”\n\n크로즈비는 이 말에 이렇다 할 대답을 거의 하지 못한 채, 되도록 빨리 그 방에서 빠져나왔다.\n\n“자네가 가진 그 푼돈 이야기는 게이지비와 하는 편이 좋을 걸세.” 백작이 말했다. 그러고 나서 그는 그 문제를 머릿속에서 치워 버렸고, 딸에 대한 제 도리를 남김없이 다했다고 여겼음이 틀림없다.\n\n그 이튿날 크로즈비는 떠나기로 되어 있었다. 마지막 날 오후, 만찬을 얼마 앞두고 그는 레이디 줄리아에게 길목을 붙잡혔는데, 그녀는 그날 하루를 그를 붙잡을 덫을 놓는 데 다 쓴 참이었다.\n\n“크로즈비 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “한마디만 합시다. 이게 사실인가요?”\n\n“레이디 줄리아.” 그가 말했다. “어째서 제 사사로운 일을 캐물으시는지 저는 정말 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“아니요, 선생, 알고 계세요. 아주 잘 알고 계시죠. 아버지도 오라비도 없는 그 가엾은 아가씨는 내 이웃이고, 그 아이의 벗들은 내 벗들이에요. 그 아이는 나 자신의 벗이기도 하고, 나는 늙은 여자이니 그 아이를 대신해 말할 자격이 있어요. 이게 사실이라면, 크로즈비 씨, 당신은 그 아이를 악당처럼 대하고 있는 거예요.”\n\n“레이디 줄리아, 저는 그 문제를 부인과 논하는 것을 정말이지 사양하겠습니다.”\n\n“당신이 얼마나 악당인지 온 세상에 다 말하고 다니겠어요. 정말로 그러겠어요. 악당이자, 가엾고 나약한 얼간이라고요. 그 아이는 당신한테는 과분한 사람이었어요. 그 아이는 그런 사람이었다고요.” 레이디 줄리아가 그 마지막 말을 그에게 퍼붓고 있는 동안 크로즈비는 그녀를 피해 서둘러 위층으로 올라갔으나, 부인은 층계참에 버티고 서서 목소리를 크게 높였으니, 달아나는 적이 한 마디도 놓치지 않게 하려는 것이었다.\n\n“저 여자는 기어이 내보내야겠다.” 그 소리를 죄다 들은 백작부인이 마거리타에게 말했다. “날마다 집안을 어지럽히고 제 낯을 깎고 있으니 말이다.”\n\n“오늘 아침에는 아버지께 갔었어요, 어머니.”\n\n“그런다고 별 소득은 없었을 게다.” 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n그 이튿날 아침 크로즈비는 런던으로 돌아갔는데, 성을 떠나기 바로 직전에 그는 릴리 데일에게서 세 번째 편지를 받았다. “오늘 아침에 소식이 없어서 좀 서운했어요.” 릴리는 이렇게 적었다. “우편배달부가 편지를 가져다줄 줄 알았거든요. 하지만 런던에 돌아가시면 더 착한 아이가 되실 걸 아니까 꾸짖지 않을래요. 꾸짖다니요! 아니에요. 한 달 동안 소식이 없더라도 절대로 꾸짖지 않을 거예요.”\n\n코시 성으로의 그 방문을 제 삶의 지나간 사실들에서 지워 버릴 수만 있다면, 그는 이 세상에서 가진 것을 전부, 세 곱절로 내놓았을 것이다.\n\n# 제25장. 어돌퍼스 크로즈비가 클럽에서 저녁을 보내다\n\n크로즈비는 호텔에서 빌린 이륜 짐마차에 실려 성에서 가장 가까운 역으로 가는 동안, 아직 보름도 지나지 않은 그 다른 아침을, 곧 그가 앨링턴을 떠나던 아침을 도무지 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그것을 생각하는 동안 그는 자기가 악당임을 알았다. 이날 아침 알렉산드리나는 그가 떠나는 것을 지켜보려고, 멀어져 가는 그의 뒷모습을 마지막으로 한 번 담아 두려고 집에서 나오지 않았다. 그가 아주 이른 시각에 출발한 것은 아니었으므로 그녀가 아침 식탁에 그와 함께 앉아 있기는 했다. 그러나 그 자리에는 다른 사람들도 앉아 있었고, 그가 일어나 떠나려 할 때 그녀는 부드럽게 미소 지으며 손을 내미는 것 이상은 하지 않았다. 그가 성탄절을 코시에서 보내기로 하는 것은 이미 정해진 일이었다. 그가 그것을 앨링턴에서 보내기로 정해져 있었던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로.\n\n레이디 어밀리아는 그 집안 사람들 가운데 그에게 가장 다정했고, 어쩌면 그들 모두 가운데 그 애정이 가장 값진 사람이었을 것이다. 그녀는 아주 고결한 정신이나 아주 고매한 감정을 타고난 여자가 아니었다. 그녀는 무슨 일에나 제 핏줄의 고귀함에 기대는 것으로 인생을 시작했고, 아버지의 지위와 어머니의 가문이 제 계급 곁을 굳게 지킬 의무를 자신에게 지운다고 벗들 사이에서 제법 소리 높여 떠들어 왔다. 그럼에도 서른셋의 나이에 그녀는 아버지의 사무 대리인과 결혼했으니, 그 정황이 그녀에게 도무지 명예로운 것은 아니었다. 그러나 그녀는 새로 놓인 처지에서 제 의무를 얼마간의 한결같음과 확고한 목적을 가지고 다해 왔다. 그리고 이제 제 동생이 저와 마찬가지로 사회적 지위가 저보다 훨씬 낮은 남자와 결혼하려 하니, 그녀는 언니로서 또 처형으로서 제 의무를 다할 채비가 되어 있었다.\n\n“우리는 십일월에 런던으로 올라갈 텐데, 물론 그때는 곧장 우리 집으로 와야 하오. 세인트존스 우드, 해밀턴 테라스의 앨버트 빌라라오. 만찬은 일곱 시고, 일요일에는 두 시요. 언제라도 자리는 있을 거요. 꼭 우리 집에 오시오, 그리고 제집처럼 편히 지내구려. 크로즈비 씨와 모티머가 서로 잘 지내면 얼마나 좋겠소.”\n\n“분명 그럴 겁니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그러나 그가 이 귀족 집안과 혼인하면서 품었던 희망은 모티머 게이지비와 가까워지는 것보다는 높은 것이었다. 그 희망이 무엇이었는지는, 이제 그것을 손에 넣기 직전까지 스스로를 몰아 놓고 보니 그 자신도 좀처럼 규정할 수가 없었다. 드 코시 백작부인이 일반위원회 사무국의 그 사무국장 자리와 관련해 인도 담당 정무차관인 사촌에게 편지를 써 주겠다고 약속한 것은 분명했다. 그러나 크로즈비는 이 일로 자기에게 어떤 이득이 돌아올지 마음속으로 저울질해 보고는, 그 승진을 얻을 가망이란 인도 담당 정무차관의 후원이 있으나 없으나 매한가지겠다는 쪽으로 기울었다. 말하자면 이제 자신이 이 귀족 집안에 속하게 되고 보니, 그는 이 결연에서 기대했던 이점이 무엇이었는지 좀처럼 분간할 수가 없었다. 백작부인을 장모로 두는 것은 대단한 일이라고 그는 스스로에게 말해 왔다. 그러나 이제, 벌써부터, 눈독 들였던 그 소유물을 아직 거두어들이지도 않았는데 그는 그것이 가질 만한 물건이 못 된다고 스스로에게 말하기 시작하고 있었다.\n\n기차에 앉아 신문을 손에 들고서도 그는 자기가 악당이라고 계속 자인했다. 레이디 줄리아는 코시의 계단에서 그에게 진실을 말했던 것이고, 그렇게 그는 몇 번이고 되풀이해 고백했다. 그러나 그가 자신에게 가장 화가 난 것은 이것 때문이었다. 곧 악당 짓으로 얻은 것이라곤 아무것도 없이 악당이 되었다는 것, 악당이 되고서도 그 악당 짓으로 그토록 많은 것을 잃게 되었다는 것. 그는 릴리와 알렉산드리나를 견주어 보았고, 릴리야말로 사내가 제 품에 안을 수 있는 가장 좋은 아내가 되리라고 몇 번이고 스스로 인정했다. 알렉산드리나로 말하자면, 그는 그 사람됨이 얼마나 얄팍한지 알고 있었다. 물론 그녀는 그의 곁을 지킬 것이다. 그리고 빙 둘러 가는, 불평 많고 즐겁지 않은 방식으로나마 아내와 어머니로서의 제 의무에는 아마 충실할 것이다. 그녀는 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비와 거의 똑같은 또 하나의 사람이 될 것이다. 그러나 그것이 제 솜씨와 수완으로 얻어 낸 것을 스스로 흡족해할 만큼 값진 상이던가? 그리고 그것이 제 끔찍한 악당 짓을 스스로에게 정당화할 만큼 값진 상이던가? 릴리 데일을 그는 사랑했다. 그리고 이제 그는 평생토록 그녀를 계속 사랑할 수 있었으리라고 스스로에게 단언했다. 그러나 알렉산드리나 드 코시에게는 사내가 사랑할 만한 것이 대체 무엇이 있단 말인가?\n\n성에서 지낸 첫 나흘이나 닷새 동안 릴리 데일을 내던지기로 마음을 정하는 사이, 그는 소설 속 여러 영웅들을 마음속으로 끌어대며 제 양심을 달래 왔다. 그는 로타리오와 돈 후안과 러블레이스를 떠올렸고, 세상에는 언제나 그런 영웅들이 가득했다고 스스로에게 말했다. 게다가 세상은 그런 영웅들을 잘도 대접해 왔다. 악당이라며 벌하기는커녕 오히려 어루만졌고, 곱슬머리 총아라 불러 주었다. 어찌하여 자기라고 남들처럼 곱슬머리 총아가 되지 못하겠는가? 부인들은 언제나 돈 후안 같은 인물을 좋아했고, 돈 후안은 대체로 사내들 사이에서도 인기가 있었다. 그러고 나서 그는 요즘 세상의 로타리오를 열두어 명이나 손꼽아 보았다. 이 여자를 속이고 저 여자를 죽음의 문턱까지, 어쩌면 죽음 그 자체에까지 몰고 갔다는 것이 알려져 있는데도 물 위로 머리를 잘도 내밀고 다니는 사내들이었다. 전쟁과 사랑은 매한가지였고, 세상은 어느 진영의 싸움꾼에게든 어떤 술책이라도 용서할 채비가 되어 있었다.\n\n그러나 이제 그 일을 저지르고 나자 그는 전혀 다른 자리에서 그것을 바라보지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 문득 그 로타리오라는 인물이 다른 빛깔로 그에게 비쳤고, 그 빛깔 속에서 그것을 제 것으로 여기며 바라보는 일은 조금도 즐겁지 않았다. 그는 릴리에게 그 편지를, 반드시 써야만 하는 그 편지를 쓴다는 것이 자기로서는 거의 불가능하리라고 느끼기 시작했다. 그는 벗어날 유일한 길로 마음이 저절로 제 목숨을 끊는 생각 쪽으로 기울 지경의 처지에 놓여 있었다. 보름 전만 해도 그는 사내가 바랄 만한 것을 다 앞에 두고 있는 행복한 사람이었다. 그런데 이제—이제 백작의 사위로 받아들여지고 높은 승진을 자신 있게 기다리는 처지가 된 지금—그는 세상에서 가장 비참하고 타락한 가련한 자였다!\n\n그는 마운트 스트리트의 하숙에서 옷을 갈아입고 클럽으로 만찬을 하러 내려갔다. 어차피 그날 밤에는 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 앨링턴으로 보낼 편지는 물론 당장 써야 했다. 그러나 그것을 이튿날 밤 우편보다 먼저 부칠 수는 없었으니 그날 저녁에 굳이 붙들고 앉아야 할 까닭은 없었다. 피커딜리를 지나 세인트제임스 스퀘어로 가는 길에 그는 이런 생각이 들었다. 릴리에게 진실은 하나도 알리지 않은 짤막한 쪽지를 써 보내는 편이 좋겠다는 것이었다. 그녀와의 약혼이 아직 깨지지 않은 듯이 쓰되, 그 약혼에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않도록 조심해서 쓴 쪽지, 그리하여 자기에게 시간을 조금 벌어 줄 쪽지였다. 그러다가 그는 버나드에게 전보를 쳐서 그에게 모든 것을 털어놓을까 하는 생각도 했다. 버나드야 물론 어떤 식으로든 제 사촌의 원수를 갚으려 들 테지만, 그런 복수 따위는 크로즈비도 별로 마음에 두지 않을 것 같았다. 레이디 줄리아는 릴리에게 아버지도 오라비도 없다고 그에게 말했고, 그로써 그를 더없이 비열한 겁쟁이라고 몰아세웠던 것이다. “그 애한테 오라비가 열둘쯤 있었으면 좋았을 텐데.” 그는 혼잣말을 했다. 그러나 그가 어째서 그런 바람을 입에 올렸는지는 그 자신도 잘 알지 못했다.\n\n그는 시월의 마지막 날에 런던으로 돌아왔고, 웨스트엔드의 거리들이 거의 텅 비어 있는 것을 보았다. 그래서 그는 클럽에도 저 혼자뿐이려니 생각했는데, 만찬실로 들어서다가 가장 오래되고 가장 가까운 벗 하나가 벽난로 앞에 서 있는 것을 보았다. 파울러 프랫은 그를 세브라이트에 처음 데려다 놓은 사람이었고, 세상에서의 성공적인 이력에 첫걸음이라 할 것을 거의 마련해 준 사람이었다. 그때 이후로 그와 그의 벗 파울러 프랫은 가깝게 지내 왔다. 다만 그 우정에서 언제나 우위를 쥔 쪽은 프랫이었다. 그는 나이로 크로즈비보다 몇 살 위였고, 사실 재능도 더 나은 사람이었다. 그러나 그는 야심이 덜했고, 세상에서 빛나고자 하는 마음이 덜했으며, 사람들 사이에서의 인기는 훨씬 덜했다. 그는 얼마간의 개인 재산을 가지고 조용하고 수수하게 살았으며, 미혼이었고, 결혼할 성싶지도 않았고, 남에게 해를 끼치지 않았고, 쓸모가 없었고, 신중했다. 런던 생활 처음 몇 해 동안 크로즈비는 벗 프랫과 아주 많이 어울렸고 벗의 조언에 많이 기대는 데 익숙했다. 그러나 요즈음에는 저 자신이 제법 눈에 띄는 사람이 되고부터 데일 같은, 나이로나 지혜로나 저보다 나을 것이 없는 사내들과 어울리는 데서 더 즐거움을 찾았다. 그러나 그와 프랫 사이가 서먹해진 것은 아니었으므로, 이제 두 사람은 더할 나위 없이 반갑게 만났다.\n\n“자네 바셋주에 내려가 있는 줄 알았는데.” 프랫이 말했다.\n\n“나는 자네가 스위스에 있는 줄 알았고.”\n\n“스위스에 있었지.” 프랫이 말했다.\n\n“나는 바셋주에 있었고.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그러고서 두 사람은 함께 만찬을 시켰다.\n\n“그래서, 자네 결혼한다지?” 급사가 치즈를 물려 간 뒤에 프랫이 말했다.\n\n“누가 그러던가?”\n\n“아니, 하지만 하기는 하는 거지? 누가 말했든 무슨 상관인가, 들은 게 사실이라면.”\n\n“하지만 사실이 아니라면?”\n\n“지난 한 달 내내 들어 왔네.” 프랫이 말했다. “게다가 다들 확실한 일처럼 이야기하더군. 그러니 사실이지, 아닌가?”\n\n“그런 것 같네.” 크로즈비가 느릿하게 말했다.\n\n“아니, 대체 무슨 일이 있길래 그런 투로 말하는 건가? 축하를 해 줘야 하는 건가, 아닌가? 듣자니 그 아가씨가 데일의 사촌이라던데.”\n\n크로즈비는 식탁에서 의자를 돌려 벽난로 쪽으로 향했고, 이 말에는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그는 셰리주 잔을 손에 든 채 앉아서 석탄불을 들여다보며, 프랫에게 이야기를 전부 털어놓는 편이 낫지 않을까 생각하고 있었다. 그보다 더 나은 조언을 해 줄 사람은 없었다. 또 그가 제 벗을 아는 한, 그런 이야기를 들려주었을 때 그보다 덜 놀랄 사람도 없었다. 프랫은 여자에 대해 낭만이라곤 없었고, 아주 고상한 감정을 지닌 척한 적도 없었다.\n\n“흡연실로 올라가세. 죄다 이야기해 줄 테니.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그리하여 두 사람은 함께 올라갔고, 흡연실이 비어 있었으므로 크로즈비는 제 이야기를 할 수 있었다.\n\n털어놓기는 아주 어려웠다. 미리 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 어려웠다. “내가 지독한 곤경에 빠졌네.” 그는 이렇게 말문을 열었다. 그러고는 자기가 어떻게 릴리에게 느닷없이 반해 버렸는지, 자기가 얼마나 성급하고 경솔했는지, 그녀가 얼마나 좋은 사람인지—“나 같은 사내에게는 한없이 과분하지.” 그는 말했다—그녀가 어떻게 자기를 받아 주었는지, 그러고 나서 자기가 어떻게 후회했는지를 이야기했다. “미리 말해 두었어야 했는데,” 그는 이어서 말했다. “나는 이미 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시와 반쯤 약혼한 사이였네.” 그러나 독자는 이 반쯤의 약혼이 꾸며 낸 이야기라는 것을 이해할 것이다.\n\n“그런데 이제는 그 여자와 아주 약혼했다는 말인가?”\n\n“바로 그렇네.”\n\n“그리고 데일 양에게는 자네가 다시 생각해 보고 마음을 바꾸었다고 알려야 한다는 말이고?”\n\n“내가 아주 못되게 굴었다는 건 알고 있네.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“정말 그랬지.” 그의 벗이 말했다.\n\n“사내가 저 있는 자리를 알아차리기도 전에 벌써 휘말려 버리는 그런 곤경이 있는 법 아닌가.”\n\n“글쎄, 나는 도무지 그런 식으로는 볼 수가 없네. 사내가 아가씨와 재미 삼아 어울릴 수는 있지. 그러다 그 아가씨를 실망시키고 청혼하지 않는 것도 나로선 이해할 수 있어. 물론 그런 짓조차 내 취향에는 그리 맞지 않네만. 하지만 이거 참, 구월에 그런 아가씨에게 청혼을 하고, 한 달을 그 집안에서 약혼한 남편으로 지내 놓고서, 그러고는 시월에 태연히 다른 집으로 건너가 신분이 더 높은 다른 아가씨에게 청혼을 한다니—”\n\n“그 일과는 아무 상관이 없다는 걸 자네도 잘 알잖나.”\n\n“꼭 그런 것처럼 보이는걸. 그래서 이 소식을 데일 양에게 어떻게 전할 셈인가?”\n\n“모르겠네.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그는 점점 견디기가 괴로워지고 있었다.\n\n“그러면 백작의 딸 쪽을 붙들겠다고 아주 마음을 굳힌 건가?”\n\n릴리 대신 알렉산드리나를 차 버린다는 생각은 여태 크로즈비의 머리에 떠오른 적이 없었고, 이제 그것을 생각해 보아도 그는 그것이 될 법한 일이라고는 여겨지지 않았다.\n\n“그래.” 그는 말했다. “나는 레이디 알렉산드리나와 결혼하겠네. 이 판을 통째로 걷어차고 덤으로 내 목까지 긋지 않는다면 말이지.”\n\n“내가 자네 처지라면 이 판을 통째로 걷어찰 것 같네. 나라면 견디지 못할 걸세. 데일 양의 삼촌에게는 뭐라고 할 셈인가?”\n\n“데일 양의 삼촌 따위는 ——만큼도 상관없네.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “그 사람이 지금 이 순간 저 문으로 걸어 들어온다 해도 나는 이야기를 죄다 들려줄 걸세, 조금도—”\n\n그가 아직 말하고 있는 사이에 클럽의 하인 하나가 흡연실 문을 열었고, 크로즈비가 벽난로 가까운 안락의자에 앉아 있는 것을 보고는 신사의 명함 한 장을 들고 그에게 다가갔다. 크로즈비는 명함을 받아 이름을 읽었다. “앨링턴, 데일 씨.”\n\n“그 신사분은 대기실에 계십니다.” 하인이 말했다.\n\n크로즈비는 그 순간 입이 딱 막혔다. 바로 그 순간에 그는 데일 씨를 만나는 것이 개인적으로 조금도 꺼려지지 않는다고 큰소리를 쳤는데, 이제 그 신사가 클럽 담장 안에 들어와 그를 만나려고 기다리고 있는 것이었다!\n\n“누군가?” 프랫이 물었다. 그러자 크로즈비가 명함을 그에게 건넸다. “휘이—이—이—유.” 프랫이 휘파람을 불었다.\n\n“그 신사분께 내가 여기 있다고 말씀드렸나?” 크로즈비가 물었다.\n\n“위층에 계신 듯하다고 말씀드렸습니다, 나리.”\n\n“그만하면 됐네.” 프랫이 말했다. “그 신사분은 분명 잠시 기다려 주실 걸세.” 그러고서 하인은 방을 나갔다. “자, 크로즈비, 자네는 마음을 정해야 하네. 저 두 여자 가운데 하나와 그 여자의 벗들 모두에게 자네는 두고두고 악당으로 여겨질 테고, 그 사람들은 물론 제 손이 닿는 대로 벌을 주려고 벼를 걸세. 이제 자네는 어느 쪽을 그 고통받는 사람으로 삼을지 골라야 하네.”\n\n이 사내는 속으로 겁쟁이였다. 지금 이 순간에도 늙은 지주를 좋은 낯으로, 적어도 서로 등을 돌리지 않는 낯으로 만날 수 있을지 모른다는 생각은, 크로즈비가 릴리를 저버리기로 처음 마음먹은 뒤로 나온 그 어떤 논거보다도 릴리 쪽에 강하게 힘을 실어 주었다. 그는 몸이 험한 꼴을 당할까 두려운 것이 아니었다. 걷어차이거나 두들겨 맞을까 봐 겁내는 것이 아니었다. 다만 그는 그 노한 사람의 정당한 노여움을 마주 볼 엄두가 나지 않았다.\n\n“내가 자네라면,” 프랫이 말했다. “아무리 하찮은 일이 걸렸다 해도 지금 저 사람에게 내려가지는 않겠네.”\n\n“하지만 내가 어떻게 하면 좋겠나?”\n\n“클럽에서 슬그머니 빠져나가게. 다만 그렇게 하면 자네는 남은 평생을 슬그머니 빠져나가며 살아야 할 것 같네만.”\n\n“프랫, 나는 자네에게 좀 더 우정다운 것을 기대했다고 말해야겠네.”\n\n“내가 자네를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있겠나? 사내를 도울 길이 없는 처지라는 게 있는 법일세. 나는 자네에게 대놓고 말하네만, 자네는 아주 못되게 굴었어. 내가 자네를 도울 수 있으리라고는 보이지 않네.”\n\n“자네가 그 사람을 만나 주겠나?”\n\n“자네 편을 들라는 뜻이라면, 그건 못 하겠네.”\n\n“어느 편을 들든 좋으니—그저 사실을 말해 주게.”\n\n“그런데 사실이 무엇인가?”\n\n“나는 전부터 그 다른 아가씨와 반쯤 약혼한 사이였네. 그러다가 곰곰 생각해 보니 내가 데일 양과 결혼할 만한 사람이 못 된다는 걸 알게 된 걸세. 내가 못되게 굴었다는 건 알아. 하지만, 프랫, 전에도 수천 명이 똑같은 짓을 했네.”\n\n“나로서는 그 수천 명 가운데 하나라도 내 벗으로 꼽는 불운은 겪지 않았다고 말할 수밖에 없군.”\n\n“그러니까 자네는 나에게 등을 돌리겠다는 말인가?” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“나는 그런 말은 한 적 없네. 물론 자네를 변호하는 일은 맡지 않겠어. 자네가 한 짓은 변호할 여지가 있어 보이지 않으니까. 자네가 원한다면 그 신사분을 만나서, 자네가 전해 달라는 것은 무엇이든 전해 주겠네.”\n\n그때 하인이 크로즈비에게 보내는 쪽지를 하나 가지고 돌아왔다. 데일 씨가 종이와 봉투를 달라고 하여 그에게 다음과 같은 전갈을 올려 보낸 것이었다.—“내게 내려올 생각인가? 자네가 이 안에 있다는 것은 알고 있네.” “제발 저 사람에게 가 주게.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “저 사람도 내가 조카딸 일로 속았다는 걸, 그가 그 애에게 얼마쯤 재산을 줄 줄로 내가 알았다는 걸 잘 알고 있네. 그 일은 죄다 알고 있어. 그리고 그 애가 아무것도 받지 못한다는 걸 내가 그 사람에게서 듣고 나서—”\n\n“이거 참, 크로즈비, 자네가 다른 심부름꾼을 찾을 수 있으면 좋겠네.”\n\n“아! 자네는 이해하지 못하는군.” 크로즈비가 괴로움에 차서 말했다. “자네는 내가 지금 와서 그 애 재산 이야기를 핑계로 지어낸다고 여기지. 그렇지 않네. 그 사람은 알아들을 걸세. 우리는 전에 이 이야기를 다 나누었고, 그 사람은 내가 얼마나 끔찍하게 실망했는지 알고 있었네. 여기서 자네를 기다릴까, 아니면 자네가 내 하숙으로 오겠나? 아니면 내가 보퍼트로 내려가서 거기서 자네를 기다리겠네.” 그리하여 마침내 그가 이 클럽에서 빠져나가 다른 클럽에서 프랫의 면담 보고를 기다리기로 정해졌다.\n\n“자네가 먼저 내려가게.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 그러는 편이 낫겠지.” 프랫이 말했다. “안 그러면 자네가 눈에 띌 테니. 데일 씨가 자네를 노려보고 있을 테고, 그러면 클럽 안에서 소동이 벌어질 걸세.” 그가 이렇게 말할 때 프랫의 얼굴에는 비웃음의 미소가 떠올라 있었고, 그것이 비참한 처지에서도 크로즈비를 화나게 하여, 이런 심부름으로 벗을 성가시게 하지 않겠다고, 제 일은 제가 알아서 하겠다고 말하고 싶게 만들었다. 그러나 그는 제 악당 짓을 자각한 탓에 기운이 빠지고 마음이 꺾여 있었고, 저를 내세우고 우위를 지킬 힘은 이미 잃은 뒤였다. 그는 자기가 걷어차이는 것을 견뎌야 마땅한 짓을, 몸으로는 아니더라도 마음으로는 걷어차여야 마땅한 짓을 저질렀다는 것을, 그리하여 이제는 부끄러움 없이 머리를 들 수 없게 되었다는 것을 깨닫기 시작하고 있었다.\n\n프랫은 데일 씨의 쪽지를 손에 들고 손님 응접실로 내려갔다. 거기서 그는 지주가 서 있는 것을 보았는데, 방문이 열려 있어 크로즈비가 클럽을 나가려면 반드시 내려와야 하는 계단 아래쪽까지 내다보이는 자리였다. 사절은 첫 번째 방책으로 문을 닫았다. 그러고서 데일 씨에게 고개를 숙여 인사하고 자리에 앉으시겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n“나는 크로즈비 씨를 보러 왔소.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“그 신사분께 보내신 쪽지를 제가 들고 있습니다.” 그가 말했다. “그는 이 면담을 저와 하시는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다. 모든 정황을 보아 아마 그편이 나을 것입니다.”\n\n“그자는 나를 만날 엄두도 못 낼 만큼 겁쟁이란 말이오?”\n\n“데일 씨, 어떤 사람이라도 겁쟁이로 만들어 버리는 행실이라는 게 있습니다. 제 벗 크로즈비는 흔히 말하는 뜻으로는 제법 담대한 사람이라고 봅니다만, 그는 어르신께 해를 끼쳤습니다.”\n\n“그러면 다 사실이란 말이오?”\n\n“그렇습니다, 데일 씨. 유감이지만 다 사실입니다.”\n\n“그런데 당신은 그런 자를 벗이라 부르는구려! 그—, 성함이 어떻게 되는지 모르겠소만.”\n\n“프랫입니다. 파울러 프랫입니다. 저는 크로즈비를 열네 해째, 그가 소년이던 시절부터 알아 왔습니다. 그리고 데일 씨, 어떤 정황에서든 오랜 벗을 내던지는 것은 제 방식이 아닙니다.”\n\n“살인을 저질렀더라도 말이오?”\n\n“예. 살인을 저질렀더라도 그렇습니다.”\n\n“내가 들은 것이 사실이라면, 이 사내는 살인자보다 더 나쁘오.”\n\n“물론 데일 씨께서 무엇을 들으셨는지는 제가 알 수 없습니다. 저는 크로즈비 씨가 어르신의 조카딸 되시는 데일 양에게 아주 못되게 굴었다고 봅니다. 저는 그가 그분과 결혼하기로 약혼했다고, 아니 적어도 그런 제의 비슷한 것이 오갔다고 봅니다.”\n\n“제의라니! 여보시오, 그건 온 고을 사람이 다 알 만큼 완전히 통해 있던 일이오. 어찌나 확실하게 정해진 일이었던지 감출 것도 없었소. 명예를 걸고 말하는데, 프랫 씨, 나는 아직까지도 이 일을 이해할 수가 없소. 내 기억이 맞다면, 그가 앨링턴의 내 집을 떠난 지 보름도 되지 않았소—보름도 되지 않았단 말이오. 그리고 그 가엾은 아이는 그가 떠나던 날 아침에 약혼한 신부로서 그와 함께 있었소. 보름도 되지 않았소! 그런데 이제 집안의 오랜 벗한테서 편지를 받아 보니 그가 드 코시 백작의 딸들 가운데 하나와 결혼한다지 않소! 나는 곧장 코시로 달려갔고, 그가 런던으로 떠났다는 것을 알았소. 그래서 여기까지 그를 쫓아왔는데, 당신은 그게 다 사실이라고 하는구려.”\n\n“유감이지만 사실입니다, 데일 씨. 너무나 사실입니다.”\n\n“나는 이해할 수 없소. 정말이지 이해할 수 없소. 얼마 전까지 내 식탁에 앉아 있던 사람이 그토록 큰 악당일 수 있다는 것을 나는 도무지 믿을 수가 없소. 그는 거기 있는 내내 이럴 작정이었던 거요?”\n\n“아닙니다. 분명 그렇지 않습니다. 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시는 제가 알기로 그의 오랜 벗이었습니다. 어쩌면 그 사이에 연인들끼리의 다툼 같은 것이 있었겠지요. 코시로 가서 두 사람이 그것을 풀었고, 그래서 이런 결과가 된 것입니다.”\n\n“그러면 그것으로 내 가엾은 아이한테는 충분하다는 거요?”\n\n“물론 제가 크로즈비 씨를 변호하는 것이 아니라는 점은 이해해 주시겠지요. 이 일은 전부 아주 딱한 일입니다. 참으로 딱한 일이지요. 그를 위해 변명 삼아 드릴 수 있는 말이라고는, 숙녀에게 못되게 군 사내가 그가 처음은 아니라는 것뿐입니다.”\n\n“그러니까 그것이 나에게 보내는 그자의 전갈이란 말이오? 그리고 그것이 내가 조카딸한테 전해야 할 말이고? 너는 악당한테 속았다. 하지만 그게 어쨌다는 거냐? 네가 처음은 아니다! 프랫 씨, 나는 신사로서 내 말을 걸고 말하는데, 나는 이 일을 이해할 수가 없소. 나는 세상 물정과 꽤 동떨어져 살아왔으니, 아마 그래서 마땅한 것보다 더 놀라는 것인지도 모르겠소.”\n\n“데일 씨, 저도 안타깝게—”\n\n“안타깝다니! 내 아이는 어찌 되란 말이오? 그리고 당신은 내가 이 다른 결혼을 그냥 두고 볼 성싶소? 내가 드 코시 집안에도, 온 세상에도 이 사내가 어떤 자인지 알리지 않을 성싶소? 내가 그를 붙들어 벌하지 않을 성싶소? 그자는 내가 이 일을 참고 넘기리라 여기는 거요?”\n\n“그가 무슨 생각인지는 저도 모릅니다. 다만 제가 마치 그의 잘못에 한몫 낀 사람인 양 이 일에 저를 끌어넣지는 말아 주시기를 부탁드릴 뿐입니다.”\n\n“내가 그를 만나고 싶어 한다고 내 말을 그에게 전해 주겠소?”\n\n“그것이 무슨 도움이 되리라고는 생각지 않습니다.”\n\n“상관없소. 당신은 나에게 그의 전갈을 가져왔소. 이제 내 전갈도 그에게 가져다주는 수고를 해 주겠소?”\n\n“지금 당장, 오늘 저녁에, 지금 말씀입니까?”\n\n“그렇소, 당장, 오늘 저녁에, 지금—바로 이 순간에 말이오.”\n\n“아, 그는 클럽을 떠났습니다. 지금은 여기에 없습니다. 제가 어르신께 오는 사이에 갔습니다.”\n\n“그렇다면 그는 악당인 데다 겁쟁이이기도 하구려.” 이 단언에 대해 파울러 프랫 씨는 그저 어깨를 으쓱해 보였을 뿐이었다.\n\n“그는 악당인 데다 겁쟁이이기도 하오. 당신 벗에게 내 말을 전해 주는 친절을 베풀어 주겠소? 그가 겁쟁이이고 악당이며—또한 거짓말쟁이라고 말이오.”\n\n“그렇다면 데일 양은 그 약혼에서 벗어난 것이 다행입니다.”\n\n“그게 당신의 위로란 말이오? 요즘 세상에는 그런 말이 통할지도 모르겠소만, 내가 젊었을 적에는 그런 문제를 두고 그런 식으로 말하느니 차라리 내 혀를 태워 버렸을 거요. 정말 그랬을 거요. 안녕히 계시오, 프랫 씨. 부디 당신 벗에게, 그가 데일 집안 사람들을 아직 다 본 것이 아니라는 걸 일러 주시오. 하기야 당신이 넌지시 말한 대로, 그 집안 여자들이야 그가 저희와 어울릴 만한 사람이 못 된다는 걸 이제 알게 되었겠지만 말이오.” 그러고서 지주는 모자를 집어 들고 클럽 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n“나라면 그런 짓은 하지 않았을 텐데.” 프랫이 혼자 중얼거렸다. “여자가 지녔던 그 어떤 아름다움과, 그 어떤 재물과, 그 어떤 지위를 다 준다 해도.”\n\n# 제26장. 제 가족 품에 안긴 드 코시 백작\n\n레이디 줄리아 드 게스트는 평생 앨링턴의 데일 씨에게 편지를 그리 많이 쓴 적이 없었고, 그를 그리 좋아한 적도 없었다. 그러나 코시에서 일이 어떻게 돌아가고 있는지—아니, 우리로서는 이렇게 말해도 좋겠으니, 일이 이미 어떻게 되어 버렸는지—를 확실히 알게 되자, 그녀는 펜을 손에 들고 자리에 앉아, 스스로 생각하기에 이웃에 대한 제 도리를 다하는 일에 착수했다.\n\n친애하는 데일 씨께 [그녀는 이렇게 썼다],\n\n댁의 조카 릴리언 양이 런던의 크로즈비 씨와 약혼했다는 것을 제가 알고 있었다는 사실은 굳이 숨길 것이 없다고 봅니다. 만일 그것이 사실이라면, 크로즈비 씨가 이곳에서 매우 온당치 못한 처신을 하고 있음을 알려 드리는 것이 마땅하다고 생각합니다. 저는 남의 일에 그리 참견하는 사람이 아니며, 여느 때 같으면 크로즈비 씨의 행실 따위는 저와 아무 상관도 없을 것입니다—아니, 실로 아무 상관도 없는 것만도 못할 것입니다. 그러나 저는 남들이 저에게 해 주었으면 하는 대로 댁에게 하는 것입니다. 크로즈비 씨가 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시에게 청혼했고 그녀가 그것을 받아들였다는 것은 너무도 틀림없는 사실이라고 저는 믿습니다. 제가 근거 없이 이런 말을 할 사람이 아님은 댁도 믿어 주시리라 생각합니다. 그리고 만일 이 일에 조금이라도 사실이 있다면, 그 가엾은 아가씨를 위해서라도 댁이 몸소 나서서 진실을 알아보시는 편이 좋을 것입니다.\n\n진심을 담은 그대의 벗임을 믿어 주시기 바라며,\n\n코시 성, 목요일.\n\n지주는 드 게스트 집안의 누구도 그리 좋아한 적이 없었고, 그중에서도 레이디 줄리아를 아마 가장 덜 좋아했을 것이다. 그는 그녀를 참견 좋아하는 늙은 여자라 부르곤 했으니, 이제는 아득히 지나간 그 시절, 저 늠름한 소령이 레이디 패니를 데리고 달아났을 때 그녀가 보였던 독설과 오만을 잊지 않고 있었던 것이다. 이 편지를 처음 받아 들었을 때 그는 한 번 읽고 나서도 그 내용을 한마디도 믿지 않았다. “심술궂은 늙은 마귀할멈 같으니라고.” 그는 조카에게 소리 내어 말했다. “네 이모라는 사람이 나한테 뭐라고 써 보냈는지 좀 봐라.” 버나드는 그 편지를 두 번 읽었고, 읽어 가는 동안 그의 얼굴은 굳어지며 성난 빛을 띠었다.\n\n“설마 저 말을 믿는다는 건 아니겠지?” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“무시해도 괜찮을 일은 아닌 듯합니다.”\n\n“뭐라고! 네 친구가 저 여자 말대로 하고 있을 수도 있다고 보는 게냐?”\n\n“그럴 가능성은 분명히 있습니다. 릴리에게 재산이 한 푼도 없다는 걸 알았을 때 그 사람은 화를 냈으니까요.”\n\n“세상에, 버나드! 그런 일을 그런 투로 말할 수가 있느냐?”\n\n“사실이라는 말씀은 아닙니다. 다만 살펴보아야 한다는 것입니다. 제가 코시 성에 가서 진상을 알아보겠습니다.”\n\n지주는 마침내 제가 가겠다고 마음을 정했다. 그는 코시 성으로 갔고, 크로즈비가 제 도착보다 두 시간 앞서 떠났다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그는 레이디 줄리아를 찾았고, 크로즈비가 실제로 레이디 알렉산드리나의 약혼한 남편으로서 그 집을 떠났다는 것을 그녀에게서 들었다.\n\n“백작부인을 만나 보시면, 그분도 분명 이 말을 부인하지는 못할 겁니다.” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다. 그러나 지주는 그러고 싶지 않았다. 그는 조카딸의 비참한 처지를 필요 이상으로 요란하게 떠벌리고 싶지 않았고, 그리하여 크로즈비를 뒤쫓아 나섰다. 그날 저녁 그 추적이 어떤 성과를 거두었는지는 우리가 이미 본 대로다.\n\n레이디 알렉산드리나도 그 어머니도 데일 씨가 성에 왔다는 소식을 들었으나, 두 사람 사이에 그 일에 관한 이야기는 한마디도 오가지 않았다. 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비도 그 소식을 들었고, 그녀는 큰맘 먹고 동생과 그 문제를 이야기해 보았다.\n\n“일이 정확히 어디까지 갔는지는 모르지 않소?”\n\n“몰라요. 아니, 알아요—그러니까, 정확히는 아니고요.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다.\n\n“그이가 그 아가씨한테 혼인 이야기 비슷한 것은 했겠구려?”\n\n“네, 아마 그랬을 거예요.”\n\n“저런, 저런! 참으로 딱한 일이오. 그 데일 집안은 어떤 사람들이오? 그이가 그 집안 이야기를 했을 것 아니오.”\n\n“아니요, 하지 않았어요. 그리 많이는요. 아마 교활하고 능청스러운 계집애일 거예요. 사내들이 그런 식으로 굴다니 참 딱한 노릇이지요.”\n\n“그래요, 딱한 노릇이오.” 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다. “그리고 이번 일은 그 아가씨보다 그이 쪽에 허물이 더 많다고 나는 생각하오. 그 말만은 내가 해 두는 것이 옳겠지.”\n\n“하지만 제가 어쩌란 말이에요?”\n\n“무얼 할 수 있다는 말이 아니오. 다만 알고는 있는 편이 좋다는 것이오.”\n\n“하지만 저도 모르고 언니도 모르잖아요. 이제 와서 그 이야기를 해 봐야 무슨 소용이 있는지 모르겠어요. 저는 그 아가씨보다 훨씬 전부터 그이를 알았어요. 그 아가씨가 그이한테 놀아난 것이라면 그건 제 잘못이 아니에요.”\n\n“아무도 그렇다고는 하지 않소, 앨리.”\n\n“그런데 언니는 그 일로 저한테 설교를 하시는 것 같잖아요. 제가 그 아가씨한테 무엇을 해 줄 수 있어요? 사실 그이는 그 아가씨를 손톱만큼도 마음에 두지 않아요. 처음부터 그랬고요.”\n\n“그렇다면 마음에 둔다고 그 아가씨한테 말하지는 말았어야 하오.”\n\n“그거야 다 옳은 말이에요, 언니. 하지만 사람이 늘 마땅히 해야 할 일만 딱 그대로 하고 사는 건 아니잖아요. 크로즈비 씨가 두 아가씨한테 청혼한 첫 번째 남자도 아닐 테고요. 잘못된 일이라는 건 저도 알아요. 그렇지만 저로서는 어쩔 수가 없어요. 데일 씨가 제 조카딸이 억울한 일을 당했다는 이야기를 들고 여기까지 온 것으로 말하자면, 저는 그게 아주 우스꽝스러운 짓이라고 생각해요—정말이지 아주 우스꽝스러워요. 그건 마치 크로즈비 씨를 낚으려는 계략이 있었던 것처럼 보이게 하는데, 저는 정말 그런 계략이 있었다고 믿어요.”\n\n“나는 그저 싸움이나 나지 않기를 바랄 뿐이오.”\n\n“요새는 사내들이 결투 같은 건 하지 않아요, 언니.”\n\n“하지만 모팻 씨가 가엾은 오거스타에게 그렇게 못되게 굴었을 때 프랭크 그레셤이 그이한테 어떻게 했는지 기억하지 않소?”\n\n“크로즈비 씨는 그런 것쯤 두려워하지 않아요. 그리고 저는 늘 프랭크가 아주 잘못했다고 생각했어요—정말이지 아주 잘못했지요. 사내 둘이 길거리에서 서로 때린다고 무슨 좋은 일이 있어요?”\n\n“글쎄, 나야 싸움이 나지 않기를 진심으로 바라오. 그렇지만 솔직히 말해 나는 이 일의 모양새가 마음에 들지 않소. 보시오, 그 삼촌이라는 사람은 죄다 알고 있었고 그 혼인에 동의했던 것이 분명하오. 그렇지 않았다면 여기까지 오지도 않았을 테니까.”\n\n“그게 저한테 무슨 상관이 있는지 모르겠어요, 언니.”\n\n“그렇겠지, 앨리. 나도 상관이 있으리라고는 생각지 않소. 우리는 곧 런던으로 올라갈 테고, 나도 크로즈비 씨를 되도록 자주 볼 작정이오. 혼례가 어서 치러지면 좋겠구려.”\n\n“그이는 2월이라고 해요.”\n\n“무슨 일이 있어도 미루지는 마오, 앨리. 알다시피 이런 일에는 어그러질 구석이 참 많으니까.”\n\n“저는 그런 건 조금도 겁나지 않아요.” 알렉산드리나가 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들며 말했다.\n\n“그렇겠지. 그리고 우리가 그이를 눈여겨보고 있으리라는 것만은 믿어도 좋소. 모티머가 되도록 자주 그이를 불러 우리와 저녁을 들게 할 것이고, 휴가도 다 써 버렸으니 그이는 런던을 벗어나지도 못할 거요. 크리스마스에 여기 오기로 되어 있지 않소?”\n\n“물론이지요.”\n\n“꼭 그러도록 붙들어 두어요. 그리고 그 데일 집안 사람들 이야기인데, 내가 앨리라면 누구한테도 그이들을 두고 모진 말은 하지 않도록 아주 조심하겠소. 지금 앨리 처지에서는 남 듣기에 좋지 않으니까.” 그리고 이 마지막 충고를 끝으로 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비는 그 이야기가 흐지부지되도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n그날 레이디 줄리아는 제 집으로 돌아갔다. 코시 성의 온 식구에게 건넨 작별 인사는 몹시 쌀쌀했으나, 크로즈비 씨와 앨링턴에 있는 그의 연인에 대해서는 누구에게도 더 이상 한마디도 하지 않았다. 알렉산드리나는 그 자리에 아예 얼굴을 내밀지 않았고, 실로 응접실에서 물러나지 않을 수 없다고 느꼈던 그날 저녁 이후로 제 원수와 말을 섞은 적이 없었다.\n\n“안녕히 가세요.” 백작부인이 말했다. “와 주셔서 얼마나 좋았는지 몰라요. 저희도 아주 즐거웠답니다.”\n\n“대단히 고맙습니다. 안녕히 계십시오.” 레이디 줄리아가 위엄 있게 몸을 숙여 인사하며 말했다.\n\n“오라버님께 안부 전해 주세요. 그분도 뵈었더라면 좋았을 텐데요. 그—그 황소 때문에 다치신 데는 없기를 바라요.” 그러고서 레이디 줄리아는 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n“저런 여자를 집에 들이다니 내가 어리석었지.” 손님의 등 뒤로 문이 채 닫히기도 전에 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n“그렇고말고요.” 레이디 줄리아가 복도 저편에서 소리쳐 되받았다. 그러고는 긴 침묵이 흘렀고, 이어 참다 못한 킥킥거림이, 그다음에는 요란한 웃음소리가 터졌다.\n\n“어머, 어머니, 이를 어째요?” 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“어쩌긴!” 마거리타가 말했다. “우리가 뭘 어쩐단 말이에요? 저 사람이 평생 처음으로 진실을 들은 것뿐인데.”\n\n“어쩌면 좋아요, 레이디 덤벨로. 저희를 어떻게 보시겠어요?” 백작부인이 마침 떠나려던 또 다른 손님 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. “저런 여자를 여태 본 사람이 있을까요?”\n\n“아주 점잖은 분 같던데요.” 레이디 덤벨로가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n“그 점에서는 저는 좀 동의하기 어렵네요.” 레이디 클랜디들럼이 말했다. “하지만 저 사람이 나름대로 최선을 다하려 한다는 것만은 믿어요. 자선심도 많고, 뭐 그런 것들 말이지요.”\n\n“글쎄, 저는 잘 모르겠어요.” 로지나가 말했다. “아일랜드 서부의 교황파를 눌러 없애자는 선교회에 낼 기부금을 청했더니 저한테 딱 잘라 거절하던걸요.”\n\n“자, 여보, 준비가 다 되었으면.” 덤벨로 경이 방으로 들어서며 말했다. 그리하여 또 한 무리가 떠나갔다. 그러나 이번에는 백작부인도 문이 다 닫히고 물러가는 발소리가 더는 들리지 않을 때까지 기다렸다. “혹시 눈치채셨어요,” 그녀가 레이디 클랜디들럼에게 말했다. “저 부인이 팰리서 씨가 떠난 뒤로 도무지 고개를 들지 못한다는 걸?”\n\n“암요, 눈치채고말고요.” 레이디 클랜디들럼이 말했다. “가엾은 덤벨로로 말하자면, 내 평생 본 가운데 가장 눈먼 사람이지요.”\n\n“내년 오월이 오기 전에 무슨 소식이 들릴 거예요.” 레이디 드 코시가 고개를 저으며 말했다. “하지만 그 모든 일에도 저 부인은 결코 옴니엄 공작부인은 되지 못할 거예요.”\n\n“내일 내가 떠나고 나면 댁의 어머님은 나를 두고 뭐라고 하실까요.” 레이디 클랜디들럼이 마거리타와 함께 홀을 가로질러 걸으며 말했다.\n\n“어느 신사분과 달아날 참이라는 말씀만은 안 하실 거예요.” 마거리타가 말했다.\n\n“어쨌든 백작님과는 아니겠지요.” 레이디 클랜디들럼이 말했다. “하하하! 그래요, 우리 모두 참으로 마음씨가 곱지 않나요? 무엇보다 좋은 건 그게 다 아무 뜻도 없다는 거고요.”\n\n그리하여 손님들은 차츰 다 떠나고, 드 코시 집안사람들은 저희끼리의 단란한 가정의 행복 속에 남겨졌다. 어머니와 자식들 사이에 통하는 마음이 그토록 많았던 것을 보면, 이 행복에 나름의 매력이 없지는 않았으리라고 우리는 짐작해도 좋겠다. 물론 흠도 있었으니, 그것은 아마도 주로 백작의 병약한 몸에서 비롯한 것이었다. “아버님이 저한테 말을 거시면요,” 조지 부인이 남편에게 말했다. “어찌나 몸이 떨리는지 대답하려고 입을 열 수조차 없어요.”\n\n“당신이 맞서야 하오.” 조지가 말했다. “아버지는 당신을 어쩌지 못하오. 당신 돈은 당신 것이고, 내가 언젠가 상속자가 된다 한들 그건 아버지가 해 주셔서가 아닐 테니까.”\n\n“하지만 저한테 이를 가시는걸요.”\n\n“물지만 않는다면 상관할 것 없소. 나한테도 늘 이를 갈곤 했지. 돈을 달라고 해야 할 때는 나도 그게 싫었소. 그렇지만 이제는 조금도 개의치 않소. 언젠가는 귀족 명감을 나한테 집어던지기도 했는데, 내 머리에서 한 자도 못 미쳐 떨어지더군.”\n\n“저한테 무얼 집어던지시면요, 조지, 저는 그 자리에서 쓰러지고 말 거예요.”\n\n그러나 백작에게서 겪는 고초로 말하자면 백작부인이 어느 자식보다도 심했다. 그녀는 날마다 그를 보아야 했고, 그가 듣기 싫어하는 말을 숱하게 해야 했으며, 그로 하여금 이를 갈게 만드는 청을 여러 가지로 넣어야 했다. 백작은 사치스럽게 사는 것 말고는 견디지 못하면서도 새로 드는 비용 하나하나에 비참해지는 그런 부류의 사내였다. 푸주한이며 빵집이며 곡물상이며 석탄상이 제 물건을 거저 대 주지는 않는다는 것쯤은 이제 알 만도 하련만, 그런데도 그는 늘 이 특별한 시기만은 그들이 그렇게 해 주리라 기대하기라도 한 듯이 굴었다. 그가 돈에 쪼들리는 사람인 것은 틀림없었고, 뉴마켓이나 홈부르크에서 벌인 투기도 운이 따라 주지 않았다. 그럼에도 그에게는 날마다 이런 고문을 겪지 않고도 살아갈 만한 재력이 아직 남아 있었다. 그러니 돈에 관해 그가 스스로에게 지운 이 괴로움은 그의 형편에서라기보다 그의 성미에서 나온 것이라고 보아야 한다. 그의 아내는 그가 정말로 파산한 것인지 그저 그런 척하는 것인지 끝내 알지 못했다. 그녀는 이 점에서 제 처지에 하도 익어 버린 나머지, 돈 문제 따위가 제 행복을 망치도록 내버려 두지 않았다. 먹을 것과 입을 것은 늘 그녀에게 왔고—벨벳 가운이며 새 장신구며 남자 요리사까지 딸려서 왔고—앞으로도 계속 오리라고 그녀는 짐작했다. 그러나 남편과 날마다 갖는 그 회견만은 그녀에게 거의 견딜 수 없는 일이었다. 그녀는 그것을 피하려고 몸부림쳤으며, 살림 비용에 관한 한 그가 허락만 해 준다면 그것들이 저희끼리 알아서 정리되도록 내버려 두었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 날마다 제 거실에서 그녀를 보아야 한다고 고집했다. 그리고 그녀는 가장 아끼는 딸 마거리타에게, 그 반 시간이 머지않아 제 목숨을 앗아 가고 말 것이라고 털어놓은 터였다. “나는 이따금,” 그녀는 이렇게 말했다. “거기서 빠져나오기도 전에 미쳐 버릴 것 같은 기분이 든단다.” 그리고 그녀는, 아마도 까닭 없이, 이 일의 많은 부분을 제가 스스로 불러들였다며 자책했다. 지난날 백작은 늘 집을 비웠고, 백작부인은 그것을 두고 불평했다. 다른 많은 여자들처럼 그녀도 제가 얼마나 편한 처지인지를 알지 못했던 것이다. 그녀는 불평했고, 제 집 화롯가에 좀 더 많은 시간을 바치라고 남편을 다그쳤다. 지금 그가 실천하고 있는 저 가정에의 충실함을 낳은 것은 부인의 항의라기보다 그 자신의 건강 상태였을 가능성이 크다. 그러나 그녀가 버림받고 질투에 차서 푸념하던 저 행복한 나날을 이제 쓰디쓴 회한으로 돌아본 것만은 분명하다. “오미크론 경을 시켜 저이를 독일 온천장으로 보내라는 명을 내리게 할 수 있으면 얼마나 좋겠니?” 그녀는 마거리타에게 이렇게 말한 적이 있었다. 그런데 오미크론 경은 런던의 대단한 명의였으니, 아마도 그런 방면에서 큰 힘을 쓸 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n그러나 그런 행복한 명령은 아직 내려지지 않았다. 그리고 식구들이 내다볼 수 있는 한, 이 집 가장은 코시에서 저희와 함께 겨울을 날 작정이었다. 손님들은 내가 말한 대로 모두 떠났고, 데일 씨가 성에 다녀간 지 며칠 뒤 어느 날 아침 열두 시에 부인이 제 주인을 뵈러 갔을 때 집 안에는 식구들밖에 없었다. 그는 늘 혼자 아침을 들었고, 아침을 든 뒤에는 프랑스 소설과 시가 속에서, 그 무해한 소일거리들이 아직 그에게 줄 수 있는 만큼의 위안을 찾았다. 소설이 더는 그를 자극하지 못하고 연기에 흠뻑 절고 나면 그는 아내를 부르러 사람을 보냈다. 그다음에는 시종이 그의 옷을 입혔다. “마님이 저보다 더 심하게 당하십니다요.” 그 사내는 하인 방에서 이렇게 말하곤 했다. “게다가 훨씬 더 괴로워하시지요. 저야 그만두겠다고 통고라도 할 수 있지만 마님은 그러지도 못하시니까요.”\n\n“나아졌느냐고? 아니, 나아지긴 뭐가 나아졌소.” 아내가 안부를 묻자 남편은 이렇게 대답했다. “당신이 저 요리사를 부엌에 두고 있는 한 나는 결코 나아지지 않을 거요.”\n\n“하지만 그이를 내보내면 다른 사람을 어디서 구해요?”\n\n“요리사 구하는 건 내 일이 아니오. 어디서 구할지는 나도 모르오. 내 짐작으로는 머지않아 요리사고 뭐고 하나도 없게 될 거요. 게다가 나한테 말도 없이 하인을 둘이나 더 들였다더군.”\n\n“손님이 계실 때는 하인이 있어야 하잖아요. 레이디 덤벨로를 모셔 놓고 시중들 사람이 하나도 없으면 될 일이 아니지요.”\n\n“레이디 덤벨로는 누가 불렀소? 나는 아니오.”\n\n“여보, 당신도 그분이 와 계신 걸 좋아하셨잖아요.”\n\n“빌어먹을 레이디 덤벨로 같으니!” 그러고는 잠시 말이 끊겼다. 백작부인은 위의 명제에 조금도 이의가 없었고, 부인의 도움을 빌려 하인 문제가 슬며시 옆으로 밀려난 것을 기뻐했다.\n\n“폴록에게서 온 저 편지나 보시오.” 백작이 말하며, 그 불행한 어머니 앞으로 맏아들의 편지를 밀어 주었다. 자식들 가운데 그녀가 가장 사랑하는 것이 그였으나, 그를 제 지붕 아래에서 만나는 것만은 결코 허락되지 않았다. “나는 이따금 저 애가 내가 상대해 본 가운데 가장 몹쓸 놈이라는 생각이 드오.” 백작이 말했다.\n\n그녀는 편지를 받아 들고 읽었다. 그 서한은 아버지가 아들에게서 기꺼이 받아 볼 만한 것이 결코 아니었다. 그러나 그 내용이 불쾌한 것은 자식의 잘못이라기보다 어버이의 잘못이었다. 글쓴이는 제 앞으로 지불되어야 할 어떤 돈이 제때에 지불되지 않았음을 알리면서, 그것을 받지 못한다면 변호사에게 일러 법이 허락하는 절차를 밟게 하겠다고 통고하고 있었다. 드 코시 백작은 집안 재산을 담보로 얼마간의 돈을 끌어다 쓴 일이 있었는데, 그것은 상속자의 협조 없이는 끌어다 쓸 수 없는 것이었다. 그리하여 그는 그 협조의 대가로 맏아들에게 일정한 액수의 수입을 지불하기로 스스로 묶여 있었다. 이것을 그는 제가 주는 용돈으로 여겼으나, 폴록 경은 법으로 제 것이 된 제 몫으로 여겼다. 아들은 편지에 다정한 말이라고는 한마디도 쓰지 않았다. “폴록 경은 드 코시 경에게 알리는 바—” 서두가 그런 식이었다.\n\n“그 애도 제 돈은 받아야겠지요. 안 그러면 어떻게 살아요?” 백작부인이 떨면서 말했다.\n\n“살아!” 백작이 고함쳤다. “그러니까 당신은 저 애가 제 아비한테 저따위 편지를 쓰는 것이 온당하다고 여긴다는 말이오!”\n\n“모두 참으로 딱한 일이에요.” 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“그 돈이 대체 어디서 나온단 말이오. 그 애로 말하자면, 굶어 죽는다 해도 싸지. 이름에도 가문에도 수치일 뿐이오. 내가 듣기로는 오래 살지도 못할 거라더군.”\n\n“오, 드 코시, 그런 식으로 말하지 말아요!”\n\n“그럼 어떤 식으로 말하란 말이오? 그 애가 내 가장 큰 위안이고, 귀족답게 살고 있고, 제 나이에 걸맞게 몸이 튼튼하고, 착한 아내와 적자를 여럿 두었다고 내가 말하면, 당신이 그걸 믿기라도 하겠소? 여자들이란 참으로 바보들이오. 내가 무슨 말을 한들 그 애가 지금보다 더 나빠지지는 않소.”\n\n“그래도 마음을 고쳐먹을지 몰라요.”\n\n“고쳐먹어! 그 애는 마흔이 넘었고, 내가 마지막으로 보았을 때는 예순이 다 되어 보였소. 자—당신이 답장을 쓰시오. 나는 안 쓰겠소.”\n\n“그럼 돈은요?”\n\n“그 더러운 돈 이야기라면 왜 게이지비한테 쓰지 않는 거요? 왜 나를 성가시게 하지? 그 애 돈은 나한테 없소. 돈 이야기는 게이지비한테 물어보시오. 나는 그 일로 신경 쓰지 않겠소.” 그러고서 또 한 번 말이 끊겼고, 그동안 백작부인은 편지를 접어 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n“조지는 저 여자를 데리고 언제까지 여기 있을 작정이오?” 그가 물었다.\n\n“그 애는 정말 아무 해도 끼치지 않아요.” 백작부인이 사정하듯 말했다.\n\n“그 여자를 볼 때마다 나는 내 집 하녀와 마주 앉아 만찬을 드는 기분이오. 그런 여자는 난생처음 보오. 저 애는 어떻게 그걸 견딘단 말인가! 하기야 저 애는 아무것도 마음에 두지 않는 인간이지.”\n\n“그 덕에 조지가 아주 착실해졌어요.”\n\n“착실하다니!”\n\n“게다가 머지않아 해산할 몸이니 여기 머무는 편이 나을지도 몰라요. 폴록이 결혼하지 않으면, 아시다시피—”\n\n“그래, 아주 눌러살 작정이란 말이지? 내 분명히 말해 두겠소—나는 용납 못 하오. 제 머리와 제 아내 머리 위에 지붕을 얹는 일이라면, 내가 저것들을 위해 해 주는 것보다 저 애가 제 힘으로 하는 편이 훨씬 낫소. 그렇게 전하시오. 나는 용납 못 하오. 알아들었소?” 그러고서 또 한 번 짧은 사이가 있었다. “알아들었소?” 그가 아내에게 고함쳤다.\n\n“네, 물론 알아들어요. 다만 해산이 코앞인데 그 애들을 내쫓기를 당신이 바라지는 않으시리라 생각했을 뿐이에요.”\n\n“그게 무슨 뜻인지 나는 아오. 그러면 저것들은 영영 안 나갈 거요. 나는 용납 못 하오. 당신이 말하지 않으면 내가 하겠소.” 이에 드 코시 백작부인은 제가 말하겠노라 약속했으니, 아마도 백작이 말을 전하는 방식이 조지 부인의 삶에 이제 막 닥쳐오는 저 특별한 시기에는 이로울 것이 없으리라고 여겼기 때문일 것이다.\n\n“당신 알고 있었소,” 그가 느닷없이 새로운 화제를 꺼내며 말했다. “데일이라는 자가 여기 와서 이 집 누군가를 찾았다는 것을?” 이에 백작부인은 알고 있었노라고 시인했다.\n\n“그런데 왜 그것을 나한테 숨겼소?” 그러고는 조지 부인이 그토록 질색하는 저 이 가는 소리가 났다.\n\n“대수로운 일이 아니었어요. 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트를 만나러 온 것이니까요.”\n\n“그렇겠지. 하지만 크로즈비라는 그자 일로 온 것 아니오.”\n\n“그랬겠지요.”\n\n“어쩌자고 그 애를 그런 바보로 놔두었소? 그자가 그 애한테 무슨 사기꾼 짓을 할 게요, 두고 보시오.”\n\n“어머, 그럴 리가요.”\n\n“그리고 대체 왜 그 애가 그따위 사내와 결혼하고 싶어 한단 말이오?”\n\n“그이는 어엿한 신사고, 세상에서 아주 좋은 평을 듣고 있어요. 그 애한테 조금도 나쁜 혼처가 아니에요, 가엾은 것. 요새는 돈 없는 처녀가 시집가기가 여간 어려운 게 아니잖아요.”\n\n“그래서 아무하고나 붙어도 된다 이 말이오. 내가 보기엔 어밀리아 때보다 더 나쁜 일이오.”\n\n“어밀리아는 아주 잘했어요, 여보.”\n\n“오, 당신이 그걸 딸들이 잘한 일이라 부른다면 나는 아니오. 나는 여간 못한 일이 아니라고 하겠소. 할 수 있는 한 가장 못한 짓이지. 하지만 그건 당신 일이오. 나는 여태 그 애들 일에 참견한 적이 없고, 이제 와서 참견할 생각도 없소.”\n\n“저는 정말 그 애가 행복할 거라고 생각해요. 그리고 그 애는 그 젊은이에게 헌신적으로 마음을 바치고 있어요.”\n\n“그 젊은이에게 헌신적으로 마음을 바치고 있다니!” 백작이 이 말을 되뇌는 어조와 태도는, 만일 그가 무대라는 직업에 마음을 두었더라면 그 방면에서 크게 성공했으리라는 견해를 넉넉히 뒷받침할 만한 것이었다. “사람들이 그런 식으로 지껄이는 것을 들으면 나는 속이 뒤집히오. 그 애는 시집을 가고 싶은 것이고, 그 수고를 자청하는 걸 보면 바보요—그건 나도 어쩔 수 없소. 다만 그 다른 아가씨 일로 여기서 무슨 헛소리가 오가는 것만은 내가 결코 두고 보지 않겠다는 것을 기억해 두시오. 그자가 그런 종류의 말썽을 나한테 일으킨다면, ——에 걸고, 내가 그자를 죽여 놓고 말겠소. 혼례는 언제요?”\n\n“2월이라고들 해요.”\n\n“나는 어떤 법석도 낭비도 용납 못 하오. 그 애가 관청 서기와 결혼하겠다면, 서기들이 결혼하는 식으로 시집가야 할 거요.”\n\n“그전에 사무국장이 될 거예요, 드 코시.”\n\n“그게 무슨 차이가 있소? 사무국장이라니, 참! 사무국장이라는 자들이 대체 어떤 인간들인 줄 아시오? 어디서 굴러왔는지도 모를 거지 아니오! 나는 어떤 법석도 용납 못 하오—알아들었소?” 이에 백작부인은 알아들었다고 말했고, 얼마 지나지 않아 겨우 빠져나올 수 있었다. 그다음은 시종의 차례였다. 그리고 그는 제 근무 시간이 끝난 뒤에, “마왕 영감”이 성깔을 부리는 품이 그 어느 때보다도 암흑의 왕을 빼닮았더라고 되뇌었다.\n\n# 제27장. 「명예를 걸고, 나는 이해할 수가 없다」\n\n그러는 동안 레이디 알렉산드리나는 제 처지의 이로운 점과 이롭지 못한 점을 남김없이 헤아려 보려 애썼다. 그녀에게는 강한 애정도, 깊이 있는 성품도, 높은 뜻도 없었다. 그러나 바보는 아니었고, 원칙이 없는 사람도 아니었다. 지금의 삶이 이대로 내내 이어져도 제 바람에 넉넉하겠다 싶을 만큼 행복한 것인지를 그녀는 여러 번 스스로에게 물어 왔고, 그때마다 할 수만 있다면 다른 삶으로 기꺼이 바꾸겠노라고 스스로에게 대답해 왔다. 또한 그녀는 스스로 넉넉히 자랑스러워하는 제 신분에 대해서도 자문해 보았고, 세상에서 제 몸을 낮추자면 무거운 쓰라림 없이는 안 되리라고 스스로에게 말했다. 그러나 마침내 그녀는, 아버지 집안의 시집 안 간 딸로 남아 있기보다 크로즈비 같은 남자의 아내가 되는 편이 얻을 것이 더 많다고 스스로를 가르쳐 믿게 만들었다. 언니 어밀리아의 처지에는 부럽지 않은 구석이 많았으나, 언니 로지나의 처지에는 부러울 것이 그보다도 적었다. 세인트 존스 우드 로드의 게이지비 집은 코시 성만큼 웅장하지 않았다. 그러나 그 대신 덜 따분했고, 괴로움에 덜 시달렸으며, 게다가 언니 제 것이었다.\n\n“평민과 결혼하는 사람이야 아주 많잖아요.” 그녀는 마거리타에게 그렇게 말했었다.\n\n“아, 그럼요, 물론이죠. 하지만 남자한테 재산이 있으면 사정이 다르잖아요.”\n\n물론 사정이 달랐다. 크로즈비에게는 재산이 없었고, 게이지비 씨만큼 넉넉하지도 못했으며, 마차를 둘 수도 없었고, 시골 저택을 갖게 될 일도 없었다. 그러나 그는 유행을 아는 사람이었고, 세상에서 게이지비 씨보다 더 높이 쳐주는 사람이었으며, 아마도 제 직업에서 출세할 수도 있을 터였고—어쨌든 어디에 내놓아도 손색이 없었다. 그녀로서야 연 5,000파운드를 가진 신사가 더 좋았을 것이다. 그러나 연 5,000파운드를 가진 신사가 그쪽 길로 오지 않는 바에야, 남편이 아예 없는 것보다는 크로즈비 씨와 함께하는 편이 더 행복하지 않겠는가? 그녀는 크로즈비 씨를 그리 깊이 사랑하지는 않았지만, 그와 함께라면 편안히 살 수 있으리라 여겼고, 대체로 보아 결혼을 한다는 것은 좋은 일이라고 생각했다.\n\n그리고 그녀는 남편에게 제 도리를 어떤 식으로 다할 것인지에 대해 몇 가지를 마음에 정해 두었다. 언니 어밀리아는 제 집에서 으뜸가는 사람이었으니, 온건하고 견딜 만한 정도의 지배를 펴며 다스렸고, 그 다스림이 남편에게 크게 이롭기까지 했다. 알렉산드리나는 저에게는 다스리는 일이 허락되지 않으리라 짐작했지만, 어쨌든 해 보기는 할 참이었다. 그녀는 그를 편안하게 해 주는 데 힘닿는 대로 다 하겠다고, 그리고 특히 제 신분이 더 높다는 것을 내세워 그를 성나게 하는 일이 없도록 조심하겠다고 마음먹었다. 그 점에 대해서는 아주 얌전하게 굴 작정이었다. 그리고 아이들이 생긴다면, 마치 제 아버지가 일개 목사나 일개 변호사이기라도 했던 것처럼 아이들 일에 정성을 쏟으리라 했다. 그녀는 또 가엾은 릴리언 데일에 대해서도 많이 생각하며, 그 일에 관한 제 도리에서 높은 원칙을 지키겠다는 마음으로 스스로에게 이런저런 물음을 던져 보았다. 크로즈비 씨를 릴리언 데일에게서 빼앗은 것이 잘못인가? 이 물음들에 대해 그녀는 제가 잘못한 것이 아니라고 마음 편히 스스로를 안심시킬 수 있었다. 크로즈비 씨는 어떤 사정에서도 릴리언 데일과 결혼하지 않을 것이다. 그가 그녀에게 그렇게 말한 것이 한 번이 아니었고, 그것도 엄숙한 태도로 말했던 것이다. 그러니 그녀가 릴리언 데일에게 해를 끼치는 일일 리 없었다. 만일 그녀가 마음속 어딘가에서, 릴리언 데일을 저버린 크로즈비의 잘못이 제가 백작의 딸이 아니었더라면 더 컸으리라는 느낌을—제 신분이 어느 정도는 제 연인의 신의 없음을 덜어 준다는 느낌을 품고 있었다 하더라도, 그녀는 그것을 스스로에게조차 말로 옮기지는 않았다.\n\n그녀는 제 집안 사람들에게서 그리 큰 공감을 얻지 못했다. “그이가 신앙의 의무를 대수롭게 여기지 않는 것 같아 걱정이에요. 그런 부류의 젊은 남자들은 좀처럼 그러지 않는다더군요.” 로지나가 말했다. “네가 틀렸다는 말은 아니야.” 마거리타가 말했다. “결코 아니고말고. 사실 나는 어밀리아 언니가 같은 일을 했을 때보다 지금 이 일을 덜 대수롭게 여긴단다. 다만 나라면 하지 않겠다는 것뿐이야.” 아버지는 그녀에게 네가 네 마음을 알고 있는 모양이지 하고 말했다. 어머니는 그녀를 위로하고 어떻게든 축하해 주려 애쓰면서도, 신분도 재산도 없는 남자와 결혼하는 것이라는 사실을 끊임없이 되뇌었다. 그 축하는 변명 같았고, 그 위로는 딱하게 여기는 말투를 띠었다. “물론 너는 넉넉하게 살지는 못하겠지, 얘야. 하지만 나는 네가 아주 잘하는 거라고 정말로 생각한다. 크로즈비 씨는 어디에서든 받아들여질 사람이고, 너는 그이를 부끄러워할 일이 결코 없을 게다.” 이 말로 백작부인은 제 맏딸이 이따금 남편을 부끄러워해야 할 처지에 놓인다는 뜻을 내비쳤다. “그이가 너한테 마차 한 대쯤 마련해 줄 수 있으면 좋으련만. 하지만 어쩌면 언젠가는 그리되겠지.” 대체로 보아 알렉산드리나는 후회하지 않았고, 아버지에게 저는 제 마음을 알고 있노라고 당차게 말했다.\n\n그러는 내내 릴리 데일은 아직 온전한 행복 속에 있었다. 연인에게서 오리라던 편지가 하루 이틀 늦어진 일도 그녀의 마음을 어지럽히지는 않았다. 그녀는 그를 의심하지 않겠노라 약속했고, 제 약속을 지키겠다고 굳게 마음먹고 있었다. 실로 이때까지 그 약속을 깨겠다는 생각은 그녀에게 떠오르지도 않았다. 우편배달부가 와서 제게 올 편지를 가져오지 않을 때면 그녀는 실망했다—목마른 땅을 적셔 줄 그토록 기다리던 비가 오지 않을 때 농부가 실망하듯이. 그러나 그녀는 조금도 화가 나지는 않았다. “그이가 설명해 줄 거야.” 그녀는 스스로에게 말했다. 그러고는 벨에게, 사랑하는 이와 떨어져 있을 때 여자가 느끼는 편지에 대한 주림과 목마름을 남자들은 결코 알지 못한다고 잘라 말했다.\n\n그러던 참에 작은 집에서는 지주가 앨링턴을 떠났다는 소식을 들었다. 지난 며칠 동안 버나드는 그들과 그리 자주 어울리지 않았고, 그래서 이번 소식도 사촌을 통해서가 아니라 홉킨스에게서 들은 것이었다. “나리께서 어디로 가셨는지는 제가 정말이지 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다요, 벨 아가씨. 나리께서 어디 가신다고 저한테 일러 주실 리가 있습니까요. 씨앗이나 그런 것에 관한 일이라면 몰라도요.”\n\n“아주 갑작스럽게 떠나셨네요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“글쎄올시다, 아가씨, 그 일이야 제가 드릴 말씀이 없습니다요. 그리고 나리께서 그러고 싶으시면 갑작스럽게 가시면 어떻습니까요? 저야 나리께서 이륜마차를 타고 역으로 가셨다는 것밖에 모릅니다요. 저를 산 채로 파묻는다 해도 그보다 더는 말씀드릴 게 없습니다요.”\n\n“한번 해 보고 싶네.” 두 사람이 그 자리를 떠나며 릴리가 말했다. “저이는 어쩜 저렇게 심술궂은 늙은이일까. 버나드가 삼촌하고 같이 갔을까?” 그러고서 그들은 그 일을 더 생각하지 않았다.\n\n그 이튿날 버나드가 작은 집으로 내려왔으나, 지주가 자리를 비운 까닭에 대해서는 아무 설명도 하지 않았다. “런던에 계셔, 그건 알아.” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“크로즈비 씨를 찾아가 주셨으면 좋겠어.” 릴리가 말했다. 그러나 이 이야기에 대해 버나드는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그는 어돌퍼스에게서 소식이 있었느냐고 릴리에게 묻기는 했는데, 그 물음에 그녀는 되도록 아무렇지도 않은 목소리를 지어내며 그날 아침에는 편지를 받지 못했다고 대답했다.\n\n“편지를 잘 안 쓰는 사람이라면 내가 그이한테 화를 낼 테다.” 데일 부인이 릴리와 단둘이 있게 되었을 때 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 엄마, 그이한테 화내시면 안 돼요. 화내시게 두지 않을 거예요. 그이는 엄마 연인이 아니라 제 연인이라는 걸 기억해 주세요.”\n\n“하지만 네가 우편배달부를 기다리고 있는 게 눈에 보이는걸.”\n\n“엄마가 그이를 나쁘게 생각하시게 된다면 저는 이제 우편배달부를 기다리지 않을래요. 네, 그건 나쁜 생각이에요. 그이가 옳은 일을 다 하지 않는다고 엄마가 생각하시게 두지 않을 거예요.”\n\n이튿날 아침 우편배달부가 편지를, 아니 쪽지 한 장을 가져왔고, 릴리는 그것이 크로즈비에게서 온 것임을 대번에 알아보았다. 그녀는 어머니가 자기가 기다리는 모습을 지켜보지 못하도록, 그리고 아무것도 오지 않았을 때 자기가 실망하는 것을 보지 못하도록, 우편배달부가 들르는 뒷문 가까이에서 그것을 가로챌 궁리를 해 두었던 터였다. “고마워, 제인.” 마음씨 곱고 신이 난 하녀가 그 작은 편지를 들고 달려왔을 때 그녀는 아주 차분하게 말했다. 그러고는 조바심을 감추려 애쓰며 아무도 없는 곳으로 걸어갔다. 쪽지가 어찌나 작아 보이던지 그녀는 놀랐다. 그러나 그것을 펴 보았을 때 그 내용은 그녀를 더욱 놀라게 했다. 첫머리도 끝맺음도 없었다. 사랑을 담은 부름말도 없었고, 서명도 없었다. 거기에는 두 줄뿐이었다. “내일 길게 쓰겠소. 오늘이 런던에서의 첫날인데, 하도 이리저리 끌려다녀서 도무지 쓸 수가 없구려.” 그것이 전부였고, 그나마 편지지 반 장에 갈겨쓴 것이었다. 아무리 그렇더라도, 어째서 그는 그녀를 사랑하는 릴리라고 불러 주지 않았을까? 어째서 자기는 언제까지나 그대의 것이라고 다짐해 주지 않았을까? 그토록 많은 것을 담은 그런 말들은 펜을 한 번 놀리는 사이에도 전할 수 있는 것을. “아,” 그녀가 말했다. “내가 그이의 사랑을 얼마나 주리고 목말라하는지 그이가 알아준다면!”\n\n어머니와 언니에게 돌아가기까지 그녀에게 남은 시간은 잠깐뿐이었고, 그녀는 그 잠깐을 제 약속을 되새기는 데 썼다. “다 괜찮은 거야.” 그녀는 스스로에게 말했다. “그이는 이런 일들을 나처럼 생각하지 않는 것뿐이야. 마지막 순간에, 사무실을 나서면서 써야 했던 거지.” 그러고서 그녀는 조용히 미소 띤 얼굴로 아침 식사하는 방으로 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n“뭐라고 썼어, 릴리?” 벨이 물었다.\n\n“알면 뭘 줄 건데?” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“그거 전부라도 나는 이 펜스도 안 내겠어.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“언니도 누군가한테서 편지를 받게 되면, 그때는 그걸 아무한테나 다 보여 줄 참이야?”\n\n“그래도 런던의 무슨 특별한 소식이 있다면 우리도 들을 수 있지 않겠니.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 특별한 런던 소식이 없다면요, 엄마. 그 딱한 사람은 런던에 온 지 하루밖에 안 됐잖아요. 게다가 이맘때에는 원래 아무 소식도 없는걸요.”\n\n“크리스토퍼 삼촌은 만났다더냐?”\n\n“못 만난 것 같아요. 하지만 그런 말은 없어요. 삼촌이 오시면 소식은 다 삼촌한테서 듣게 될 거예요. 삼촌이 어돌퍼스보다 런던 소식에 훨씬 더 관심이 많으시니까요.” 그러고 나서 그 편지에 대해서는 더 이야기가 없었다.\n\n그러나 릴리는 앞서 온 편지 두 통은 아침 식탁에서 읽고 또 읽었었다. 소리 내어 읽지는 않았어도 거기서 여러 구절을 되뇌었고, 하도 이런저런 풀이를 곁들여 놓았기에, 그것을 들은 어머니는 그 편지를 거의 그대로 다시 써 낼 수도 있을 지경이었다. 그런데 이번에는 종이를 내보이지조차 않았다. 게다가 편지를 읽느라 자리를 비운 시간도 일이 분을 넘지 않았다. 데일 부인은 이 모든 것을 눈여겨보았고, 딸이 또 실망했다는 것을 알았다.\n\n실은 그날 릴리는 아주 진지했으나 불행해 보이지는 않았다. 아침을 먹고 얼마 되지 않아 벨이 목사관으로 건너갔고, 데일 부인과 막내딸은 일감을 앞에 두고 함께 앉아 있었다. “엄마,” 그녀가 말했다. “제가 런던에 가서 살게 되더라도 엄마와 제가 갈라지지는 않았으면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“마음이야 우리가 결코 갈라지지 않을 게다, 내 사랑.”\n\n“아, 하지만 그것만으로는 행복하기에 넉넉하지 않아요. 아주 불행해지는 것을 막기에는 넉넉할지 몰라도요. 저는 지금처럼 엄마를 보고, 만지고, 어루만지고 싶을 거예요.” 그러고서 그녀는 다가와 어머니 발치의 방석에 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n“너에게는 어루만지고 쓰다듬을 다른 사람이 생길 게다. 어쩌면 여럿이 생기겠지.”\n\n“그럼 엄마는 저를 내치시겠다는 말씀이에요?”\n\n“천만에, 우리 딸. 제 자식을 내치는 어미는 없단다. 너와 벨이 내게서 떠나고 나면 나한테 무엇이 남겠니?”\n\n“하지만 우리는 결코 떠나지 않을 거예요. 제 말이 그거예요. 우리는 결혼을 하더라도 엄마에게는 언제까지나 똑같아야 해요. 저는 지금만큼이나 여기 있을 권리를 가져야 하고, 그 대신 엄마는 저쪽에 계실 권리를 가지셔야 해요. 그이의 집이 엄마에게 한 집이어야 해요. 나들이옷을 차려입고 이따금 한 번씩 찾아가는 서먹한 곳이 아니라요. 우리가 갈라져서는 안 된다고 할 때 제가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 아시겠죠.”\n\n“하지만 릴리—”\n\n“네, 엄마?”\n\n“우리 둘이야 함께 행복하겠지. 너와 나는 말이다.”\n\n“하지만 그보다 더 하실 말씀이 있으셨잖아요.”\n\n“이것뿐이란다. 네 집은 그이의 집이 될 테고, 내가 없어도 가득 차 있을 게다. 딸을 시집보내는 일은 언제나 괴로운 이별이지.”\n\n“그런가요, 엄마?”\n\n“그렇다고 지금과 달라지기를 바라는 것은 아니란다. 내가 너를 집에 붙들어 두고 싶어 한다고는 생각하지 마라. 물론 너희 둘 다 결혼해서 나를 떠나겠지. 네가 몸과 마음을 바치려는 그 사람이 오래 살아남아 너를 사랑하고 지켜 주기를 바랄 뿐이다.” 그러고 나자 미망인의 가슴이 벅차올라, 그녀는 얼굴을 감추려고 아이를 제게서 떼어 놓았다.\n\n“엄마, 엄마, 저는 엄마 곁을 떠나지 않았으면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“아니다, 릴리. 그런 말은 하지 마라. 내 두 딸이 시집가는 것을 보지 못한다면 나는 이 삶에 만족하지 못할 게다. 나는 그것이야말로 여자에게 온전한 만족과 흡족함을 줄 수 있는 하나뿐인 몫이라고 생각한단다. 나는 너희 둘 다 결혼하기를 바란다. 그렇지 않기를 바란다면 나는 세상에서 가장 이기적인 사람일 게다.”\n\n“벨 언니는 엄마 가까이에 자리를 잡을 테고, 그러면 엄마는 저보다 언니를 더 자주 보고 더 아끼시게 되겠죠.”\n\n“벨을 더 아끼지는 않을 게다.”\n\n“언니가 런던 사람하고 결혼하면 좋겠어요. 그러면 엄마도 우리와 함께 오셔서 우리 가까이 계실 텐데. 있잖아요, 엄마, 저는 가끔 엄마가 이곳을 좋아하지 않으신다는 생각이 들어요.”\n\n“너희 삼촌이 이 집을 우리에게 주신 건 참으로 고마운 일이었단다.”\n\n“저도 알아요. 그리고 우리는 여기서 아주 행복했고요. 하지만 벨 언니가 엄마 곁을 떠난다면—”\n\n“그러면 나도 떠나야지. 너희 삼촌은 참으로 고마운 분이지만, 나는 이따금 그 고마움이 내 어깨만으로는 짊어질 힘이 모자란 짐이라는 느낌이 든단다. 그리고 그때가 되면 무엇이 나를 여기 붙들어 두겠니?” 데일 부인은 이렇게 말하면서, 자기가 말한 ‘여기’가 시숙의 자비로 얻어 지내는 이 집의 울타리 너머까지 뻗어 있음을 느꼈다. 자기가 몸담은 한에서의 온 세상이 그 ‘여기’ 안에 담겨 있는 것이 아니겠는가? 두 아이를 다 빼앗기고 나면 자기는 어떻게 살아야 하는가? 그녀는 크로즈비의 집이 자기에게 결코 한 집이 될 수 없으리라는 것을, 잠시 머무는 집조차 될 수 없으리라는 것을 이미 깨닫고 있었다. 그 집을 찾아가는 일은 릴리가 빗대어 말한 그 나들이옷 차림의 방문일 수밖에 없었다. 이것을 릴리에게 설명하기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 딸의 마음속 영웅이 아내의 어머니에게 매정하리라고 미리 점칠 생각은 없었다. 그러나 그녀가 읽어 낸 크로즈비의 사람됨이 그러했다. 아아, 슬프도다, 일이 앞으로 흘러갈 모양대로라면 그의 후한 대접이든 매정함이든 그들에게는 대수롭지 않은 일이 될 터였다.\n\n오후에 다시 두 자매가 함께 있게 되었는데, 릴리는 여느 때보다도 더 진지했다. 그 태도만 보아서는 그녀의 결혼이 당장이라도 치러질 참이고 그녀가 집을 떠날 채비를 하고 있다고 여겨질 정도였다. “언니,” 그녀가 말했다. “크로프츠 선생은 요즘 왜 통 우리를 보러 오지 않을까?”\n\n“여기 오신 지 한 달도 안 됐어. 우리 모임 때 오셨잖아.”\n\n“한 달이라니! 하지만 이틀에 한 번꼴로 무슨 핑계를 만들어서라도 여기 오시던 때가 있었잖아.”\n\n“그래, 엄마가 편찮으셨을 때지.”\n\n“그리고 엄마가 나으신 뒤에도 그랬어. 하지만 언니가 나한테 하게 만든 그 약속을 내가 깨서는 안 되겠지. 아직도 그분 이야기는 하면 안 되는 거야?”\n\n“이야기하면 안 된다고는 하지 않았어. 내가 무슨 뜻으로 그랬는지 너도 알잖니, 릴리. 그때 내 뜻이 지금도 내 뜻이고.”\n\n“그러면 언니 뜻이 달라지려면 얼마나 더 걸릴까? 언젠가는 그렇게 되면 좋겠어. 정말 그러면 좋겠어.”\n\n“결코 그렇게 되지 않을 거야, 릴리. 나도 한때는 크로프츠 선생을 마음에 두고 있다고 여겼지만, 그건 그저 여겨 본 것뿐이었어. 나는 그걸 알아. 왜냐하면—” 그녀는 그 점에 대한 자기 앎이 어째서 확실한지를 설명하려던 참이었다. 그날 이후로 자기가 다른 남자를 사랑하게 될 수도 있었겠다고 느꼈기 때문이라고. 그러나 그 다른 남자란 크로즈비 씨였으므로, 그녀는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“그분이 와서 언니한테 직접 물어봐 주면 좋겠어.”\n\n“그분은 결코 그러지 않을 거야. 부추김이 없으면 그런 물음은 결코 던지지 않을 분이고, 나는 부추길 마음이 없어. 그리고 돈 문제에서 경솔하다고 스스로 여기지 않을 만큼 되기 전에는 결혼을 생각조차 하지 않으실 거야. 그분은 혼자서 가난하게 지내면서도 불행해하지 않을 용기는 있지만, 아내를 데리고 가난을 견딜 용기는 없어. 나는 그분 마음을 잘 알아.”\n\n“그럼 두고 보자.” 릴리가 말했다. “이제 언니가 먼저 결혼한다 해도 나는 놀라지 않을 거야, 벨 언니. 내 쪽은 삼 년이라도 기다릴 각오가 아주 되어 있으니까.”\n\n그날 저녁 늦게 지주가 앨링턴으로 돌아왔고, 버나드가 이륜마차를 몰아 역으로 그를 맞으러 나갔다. 그는 늦은 기차로 돌아가겠다고 조카에게 전보를 쳤는데, 떠난 뒤로 그에게서 들려온 소식이라고는 그것뿐이었다. 그날 버나드는 작은 집의 여인들을 아무도 만나지 않았다. 지금 형편에서 벨과 허물없이 지내기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 그녀를 단둘이 만나면 두 사람 사이의 그 한 가지 관심사로 되돌아가지 않을 수 없었고, 그 일에 대해서는 깊이 헤아려 보지 않고 말할 마음이 없었다. 청혼을 그토록 가볍게, 대수롭지 않은 일로 여기며 밀고 나갔을 때만 해도, 받아들여지든 말든 그는 제 마음을 알지 못했다. 이제 그것은 그에게 더 이상 대수롭지 않은 일이 아니었다. 그를 그토록 조바심 나게 한 것이 사랑이었는지 나는 알지 못한다. 착하고 정직하고 온전한 사랑 말이다. 그러나 그는 그 목표에 마음을 붙박아 두었고, 데일 집안사람다운 고집으로 그것이 제 것이 되어야 한다고 굳게 마음먹고 있었다. 사촌을 포기할 생각은 털끝만큼도 없었으나, 그녀를 얻자면 얼마간의 수고가, 어쩌면 얼마간의 기다림도 있어야 하리라고 마침내 스스로를 납득시켜 두고 있었다.\n\n그는 데일 부인에게도 릴리에게도 말을 붙일 기분이 아니었다. 그는 레이디 줄리아의 소식이 사실일까 봐, 적어도 거기에 얼마쯤은 사실이 섞여 있을까 봐 두려웠다. 그렇게 미심쩍어하는 동안에는 작은 집으로 내려갈 수가 없었다. 그래서 그는 입에 시가을 문 채 혼자서 그 언저리를 서성거리며 무슨 나쁜 일이 벌어지려나 보다 하고 두려워했고, 자기를 부르는 전갈이 왔을 때는 이륜마차에 올라앉으며 하마터면 몸서리를 칠 뻔했다. 레이디 줄리아가 크로즈비에게 씌운 그 몹쓸 짓을 그가 정말로 저질렀다면, 이 다급한 처지에서 자기는 어떻게 하는 것이 마땅한가? 삼십 년 전이었다면 그는 그 사람을 결투에 불러내어 둘 중 하나가 총알을 맞을 때까지 쏘았을 것이다. 요즈음에는 사람이 그렇게 하기란 거의 불가능했다. 그런데도 이런 모욕을 앙갚음 없이 넘긴다면 세상은 그를 두고 무어라 할 것인가?\n\n삼촌은 여행 가방을 손에 들고 역에서 걸어 나오는데, 엄하고 침울하고 말이 없었다. 그는 나와서 거의 한마디도 없이 이륜마차에 자리를 잡았다. 둘레에 낯선 사람들이 있었으므로 조카는 처음에는 아무것도 물을 수 없었으나, 이륜마차가 역사 앞마당을 벗어나 모퉁이를 도는 참에 소식을 물었다.\n\n“무엇을 들으셨습니까?” 그가 말했다.\n\n그러나 그때에도 지주는 곧바로 대답하지 않았다. 그는 고개를 저었고, 캐물음을 받고 싶지 않다는 듯 얼굴을 돌렸다.\n\n“그를 만나셨습니까, 어르신?” 버나드가 물었다.\n\n“아니다. 그자는 감히 나를 만나지 못하더구나.”\n\n“그러면 사실이군요?”\n\n“사실이냐고? 그래, 죄다 사실이다. 어쩌자고 너는 그 악당을 여기 데려왔느냐? 이건 네 잘못이야.”\n\n“아닙니다, 어르신. 그 말씀에는 반박해야겠습니다. 저는 그가 악당인 줄 몰랐습니다.”\n\n“하지만 그를 여기 저 아이들 사이로 데려오기 전에 그가 어떤 사람인지 알아 두는 것이 네 도리였다. 가엾은 것! 그 아이에게 이 일을 어떻게 알린단 말이냐?”\n\n“그럼 그 아이는 아직 모른단 말씀입니까?”\n\n“모르는 듯싶어 걱정이구나. 너는 그 애들을 보았느냐?”\n\n“어제 보았습니다만, 그때는 몰랐습니다. 오늘 들었을지도 모르지요.”\n\n“그러지는 않았을 게다. 내 생각에 그자는 겁이 너무 많아서 그 아이에게 편지조차 못 썼을 거다. 겁쟁이라니, 참으로 겁쟁이지! 어떤 사내가 그런 편지를 쓸 용기를 낼 수 있단 말이냐?”\n\n지주는 차츰차츰 제 이야기를 들려주었다. 그가 레이디 줄리아를 찾아갔던 일, 런던으로 올라갔던 일, 크로즈비를 그의 클럽까지 뒤쫓아 갔던 일, 그리고 거기서 크로즈비의 벗 파울러 프랫에게서 진상을 죄다 알게 된 일은 우리가 이미 아는 바다. “그자가 내려보낸 사람과 내가 이야기하는 동안에 그 겁쟁이는 나를 피해 달아났다.” 지주가 말했다. “미리 짜 둔 계략이었지. 그리고 나는 그자가 옳았다고 본다. 나는 클럽 현관에서 그자의 머리통을 부숴 놓았을 테니까.” 이튿날 아침에 프랫이 크로즈비의 사과문을 들고 그가 묵는 여관으로 찾아왔었다. “사과문이라니!” 지주가 말했다. “그게 내 주머니에 있다. 가련한 파충류 같으니, 벌레만도 못한 가엾은 인간! 나는 이 일을 이해할 수가 없다. 명예를 걸고 말하는데, 버나드, 나는 이 일을 이해할 수가 없다. 내가 사람들을 잘 알던 시절 이후로 사람이 달라진 모양이다. 나로서는 그런 편지를 쓴다는 것이 있을 수 없는 일이었을 게다.” 그는 프랫이 이 편지를 가져왔고 크로즈비가 만나기를 마다한다고 전했다는 이야기를 이어 갔다. “그 신사분께서는 친절하게도 그런 만남에서는 좋은 것이 나올 수 없다고 나를 안심시켜 주더구나. ‘그러니까 자네 말은,’ 내가 대답했지, ‘내가 역청을 만지고서 더럽혀지지 않을 수는 없다는 뜻이로군!’ 그자도 그 사람이 역청이라는 것은 인정하더라. 참으로, 제 벗을 두고 한마디도 변명하지 못했으니까.”\n\n“저는 프랫을 압니다. 그는 신사입니다. 그가 그자를 두둔했을 리는 없다고 저는 확신합니다.”\n\n“두둔하다니! 누가 그자를 두둔할 수 있겠느냐? 그자를 두둔할 말이란 찾아낼 수 없을 게다.” 그러고서 그는 반 마일쯤 되는 거리를 말없이 앉아 있었다. “명예를 걸고 말하는데, 버나드, 나는 아직도 이 일을 좀처럼 믿을 수가 없구나. 나에게는 너무나 낯선 일이다. 세상이 달라졌다는 느낌, 이제는 사람이 이 세상에서 살아갈 값어치가 없다는 느낌이 든다.”\n\n“그러면 그자가 그 다른 아가씨와 약혼했다는 말씀입니까?”\n\n“오, 그렇다. 그 집안이 죄다 찬성한 채로 말이다. 다 정해졌고, 혼인 재산 계약서도 이맘때면 벌써 변호사 손에 넘어가 있겠지. 그자는 여기를 떠날 때 이미 그 아이를 저버리기로 작정하고 갔음이 틀림없다. 사실 나는 그자가 애초에 그 아이와 결혼할 마음을 품기나 했는지 모르겠다. 그자는 그저 시골에서 한때를 흘려보내고 있었던 게야.”\n\n“떠나실 때까지는 그럴 마음이 있었습니다.”\n\n“나는 그렇게 생각하지 않는다. 내가 그 아이에게 재산을 줄 수 있고 또 줄 마음도 있다는 것을 알았더라면 그자는 어쩌면 그 아이와 결혼했을지도 모르지. 하지만 그 아이에게 아무것도 없을 거라고 내가 말한 뒤로는 그자가 한순간도 그럴 마음이 없었다고 나는 생각한다. 자, 다 왔구나. 내 집으로 돌아오면서 이토록 마음이 쓰라렸던 적은 정말이지 여태 없었다.”\n\n두 사람은 말없이 저녁을 먹었는데, 지주는 그 성품에서 짐작할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 드러내 놓고 슬퍼했다. “아침에 그들에게 무어라고 말한단 말이냐?” 그는 몇 번이고 되뇌었다. “그 일을 내가 어떻게 한단 말이냐? 그리고 어미에게 말한들, 어미가 제 아이에게 그것을 어떻게 말한단 말이냐?”\n\n“그자가 제 뜻을 조금도 내비치지 않았다고 보십니까?”\n\n“내가 아는 한에서는 조금도 없다. 그 프랫이라는 사람도 어제 오후까지는 그자가 그러지 않았다는 것을 알고 있었다. 나는 그자의 그 불한당 같은 벗이 어쩔 작정인지 물었는데, 그는 모르겠다고, 크로즈비가 아마 내게 편지를 썼을 거라고 하더구나. 그러고 나서 그가 내게 이 편지를 가져왔다. 자, 여기 있다.” 그러면서 지주는 그 편지를 탁자 너머로 던졌다. “읽어 보고 나한테 도로 다오. 그자는 아마 이제 제 몫의 성가신 일은 끝났다고 여기고 있겠지.”\n\n그것은 써서는 안 될 더러운 편지였다. 말씨가 나빠서도, 표현하는 방식이 매정해서도, 사실을 거짓으로 적어서도 아니라, 알려야 할 그 일 자체가 그토록 더러웠기 때문이다. 아무리 겉을 발라도 가려지지 않는 짓들이 있다. 저지른 자가 파충류로서밖에는 달리 말할 수 없는 죄들이 있고, 사람을 바꾸어 놓고 그에게 해충 같은 하찮음을 씌워 버리는 사정들이 있는 것이다. 크로즈비는 그것을 쓰느라 몹시 애를 먹었다. 그날 밤 프랫과 마지막으로 만난 뒤에 그것을 쓰려고 집으로 갔던 것이다. 그러나 그는 하숙집 의자에 시무룩하게 앉은 채 펜을 손에 쥐지 못했다. 프랫이 이튿날 아침에 그의 사무실로 찾아오기로 되어 있었으므로, 그는 책상에서 그것을 쓰리라 마음먹고 잠자리에 들었다. 이튿날 프랫이 왔을 때에도 그것은 한 글자조차 쓰여 있지 않았다.\n\n“나는 이런 짓은 못 참겠네.” 프랫이 말했다. “내가 그것을 가져가기를 바란다면 자네는 지금 당장 써야 하네.” 그러자 크로즈비는 속으로 끙끙 앓으면서 제 탁자 앞에 앉았고, 마침내 말들이 나왔다. 그런 꼴의 말들이! “저로서는 어르신께—아니, 그분께도 드릴 변명이 있을 수 없다는 것을 압니다. 그러나 지금 제가 처한 사정에서는 진실이 가장 낫습니다. 저는 제가 데일 양을 행복하게 해 드리지 못하리라고 느낍니다. 그러므로 정직한 사람으로서, 그분과 어르신께서 제게 베풀고자 하신 그 영예를 내려놓는 것이 제 도리를 가장 잘 다하는 길이라고 생각합니다.” 그 뒤로도 더 있었으나, 그런 편지들이 어떤 말로 이루어지는지, 그리고 사람이 스스로 파충류로서 쓸 수밖에 없다고 느낄 때 어떻게 쓰는지는 우리 모두가 아는 바다.\n\n“정직한 사람으로서라니!” 지주가 되뇌었다. “명예를 걸고, 신사로서 말하는데, 버나드, 나는 이 일을 이해할 수가 없다. 저 편지를 쓴 사내가 바로 며칠 전에 내 식탁에 손님으로 앉아 있었다니, 나로서는 도무지 믿을 수가 없구나.”\n\n“우리가 그자를 어떻게 해야 합니까?” 얼마쯤 있다가 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“쥐를 다루듯 다루어라. 발밑으로 기어들거든 지팡이를 던지고. 그러나 무엇보다도 그자가 네 집 안으로 들어오지 못하게 조심해라. 우리에게는 이미 너무 늦은 일이다만.”\n\n“그것보다는 더 있어야 합니다, 삼촌.”\n\n“나도 더 무엇이 있는지 모르겠다. 저지르고 나면 갑절로 저주받는 짓들이 있는 법이다. 제 악행의 성질 그 자체 덕분에 드러내 놓고 받는 벌은 면해 버리니까 말이다. 우리는 릴리의 이름을 생각해야 하고, 그 아이가 편안해지는 데 가장 도움이 될 일을 해야 한다. 가엾은 것! 가엾은 것!”\n\n그러고서 두 사람은 말이 없었고, 이윽고 지주가 일어나 제 침실 촛대를 집어 들었다. “버나드,” 그가 말했다. “내일 아침 일찍 제수한테, 아침 식사 뒤에 여기로 와 주면 고맙겠으니 내가 여기서 만나겠다고 알려 다오. 작은 집에서 그 밖의 말은 하지 마라. 어쩌면 그자가 오늘 편지를 썼을지도 모르니.”\n\n그러고서 지주는 잠자리에 들었고, 버나드는 식당 난롯가에 앉아 이 모든 일을 곰곰 생각했다. 세상은 그가 크로즈비에게 어떻게 처신하기를 바랄 것인가? 그리고 클럽에서 크로즈비와 마주치면 자기는 어떻게 해야 하는가?\n\n# 제28장. 위원회\n\n크로즈비는, 우리가 이미 아는 대로, 세브라이트에서 빠져나온 이튿날 아침 화이트홀의 제 사무국으로 갔다. 그는 그 클럽에 앨링턴의 지주를 파울러 프랫과 이야기하도록 남겨 두고 나왔던 것이다. 그날 밤 그는 파울러 프랫을 다시 만났고, 그 자리에서 무슨 일이 오갔는지는 이야기의 흐름이 이미 보여 주었을 것이다.\n\n그는 일찍 사무국으로 나갔다. 편지 두 통을 써야 한다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문인데, 어느 쪽도 그의 펜에서 술술 흘러나올 편지는 아니었다. 하나는 벗의 손에 들려 지주에게 전할 서한이었고, 다른 하나는 가엾은 릴리에게 보낼 그 끔찍한 편지였는데, 하루가 다 저물도록 그는 도무지 그것을 써낼 수가 없었다. 지주에게 보내는 편지는 어떤 위협 아래 쓰기는 썼다. 그리고 우리가 본 대로, 그 글의 비루함 때문에 그는 인간 가운데 버러지의 등급으로 스스로를 떨어뜨렸다는 평을 들었다.\n\n그러나 사무국에 이르러 보니 다른 근심거리들이 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그의 마음이 무엇에서든 기쁨을 느낄 수 있는 상태이기만 했더라면 짊어지기에 몹시 즐거웠을 근심거리였다. 열 시에 사무국 현관으로 들어서면서 그는 거기 모여 있던 사환들이 여느 때보다도 더한 공손함으로 자기를 맞이한다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 일반위원회 사무국에서 늘 대단한 사람이었다. 그러나 대단함에도 등급이 있고 공손함에도 등급이 있어서, 도무지 말로 규정할 수는 없으되 겪어 본 귀와 눈에는 뚜렷이 드러나는 법이다. 그는 곧장 제 방으로 걸어 들어갔고, 거기 탁자 위에 제 앞으로 온 공문 두 통이 놓여 있는 것을 보았다. 먼저 손에 잡힌 것은 공문이기는 하되 작았고 사사로운 편지라는 표시가 붙어 있었으며, 겉봉의 글씨는 오랜 벗이자 물러나는 사무국장인 버터웰의 것이었다. “이 글을 받아 보는 것과 거의 동시에 아침에 만나게 되겠지만,” 그 반쯤 공적인 쪽지는 이렇게 말하고 있었다. “그래도 내 헌 신발을 물려받게 된 것을 축하하는 사람은 내가 첫 번째여야겠네. 처음에는 내 티눈을 조금 죄었네만, 자네가 신으면 아주 편할 걸세. 밑창은 새로 대야 할 테고 뒤축도 좀 닳았을 게야. 하지만 자네라면 훌륭한 구둣방을 찾아 다 손보게 하겠지. 그리고 그 신발이 내 손에 들어온 뒤로 애석하게도 없었던 품위를, 자네가 신음으로써 얹어 줄 걸세. 그 신발로 큰 기쁨을 누리기 바라네,” 등등, 등등. 그러고 나서 그는 더 큰 공문을 뜯었으나, 그것은 이제 그에게 거의 흥미가 없었다. 보지 않고도 그 내용을 그대로 베껴 쓸 수 있을 정도였다. 위원들의 위원회는 버터웰 씨가 그들 자신의 위원회 자리로 올라가면서 비게 된 사무국장직에 그를 승진시키게 되어 대단히 기쁘다는 것이었다. 그리고 그 편지에는 버터웰 씨 자신의 서명이 있었다.\n\n다른 일들에서 그의 마음이 근심으로부터 자유로웠더라면, 사무국으로 돌아온 그를 맞이한 이 환대가 얼마나 기꺼웠으랴! 그리고 그것을 생각하다가 그는 릴리의 온갖 사랑스러움을 떠올렸다. 이제 제 짝으로 삼을 운명이 된 드 코시 가문의 고귀한 자손보다 그 아이가 얼마나 나은지를, 그가 버린 신부가 그가 고른 신부보다 우아함에서, 아름다움에서, 신실함에서, 싱그러움에서, 그리고 여인의 온갖 덕에서 얼마나 나은지를 그는 스스로에게 일러 주었다. 지난 두 주만 제 삶의 사실에서 지워 버릴 수 있다면! 그러나 그런 두 주는 지워지지 않는 법이다. 여러 해를 슬픔 속에서 지겹도록 문질러 대도 지워지지 않는다.\n\n그리고 이 순간 그에게는, 릴리 데일과 혼인하는 일을 생각할 때 그를 겁먹게 했던 온갖 장애물이 죄다 걷힌 것처럼 보였다. 연 칠팔백 파운드로는 끔찍했을 일이 천이삼백 파운드로는 즐거운 일이 되었을 터였다. 어찌하여 운명은 그에게 그토록 모질었을까? 어찌하여 이 승진이 꼭 두 주만 일찍 오지 않았을까? 어찌하여 그가 그 무시무시한 성을 찾아가기 전에 발표되지 않았을까? 그는 심지어 스스로에게 이렇게까지 말했다. 프랫이 데일 씨를 만나기 전에 이 사실을 확실히 알았더라면 자기는 지주에게 다른 전갈을 보냈을 것이고, 드 코시 일족 전부의 노여움도 맞받았으리라고. 그러나 그 대목에서 그는 스스로에게 거짓말을 하고 있었고, 자기가 그런다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그의 상상 속에서 백작이란 어찌나 굳센 신성으로 둘려 있는지, 알렉산드리나에게 반역할 마음이 있어도 기껏 넘겨다보는 것이 고작이었다. 처음 그 계획이 머릿속에 떠올랐을 때 시골 소지주의 조카딸을 차 버리는 일은 그에게 대수롭지 않게 여겨졌다. 그러나 백작부인의 딸을 차 버리는 일은 그로서는 할 수 없는 짓이었다.\n\n세인트존스우드의 아이들로 가득한 그 집이 이제 그에게는, 코시 성에 닿던 날 저녁 제 방에 앉아 그려 보던 것과는 아주 다른 모습으로 보였다. 그때 그런 살림은 그에게 무덤 냄새를 풍겼다. 산 채로 제 몸을 파묻는 일 같았다. 이제 그것이 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 가 버리니, 그는 그것을 지상의 낙원으로 여겼다. 그러고는 레이디 알렉산드리나가 어떤 낙원을 만들어 줄 것인지를 헤아려 보았다. 지금 이 순간 그가 쓰고 있는 안경으로 내다보니, 레이디 알렉산드리나는 놀랍도록 못생겼고, 놀랍도록 늙었으며, 놀랍도록 볼품없었고, 사람을 끄는 매력이라고는 놀랍도록 없었다.\n\n사무국에서의 첫 한 시간 동안 그는 아무 일도 하지 않았다. 젊은 서기 한둘이 들어와 아주 진심으로 축하해 주었다. 그는 사무국에서 인기가 있었고, 그의 승진으로 그들도 한 계단씩 올라선 참이었다. 그다음에는 나이 든 서기 한둘을 만났는데, 그쪽의 축하에는 진심이 훨씬 덜했다. “다 옳은 일이겠지요.” 무뚝뚝한 노신사 하나가 말했다. “내 때는 지나갔다는 걸 나도 압니다. 나는 너무 일찍 혼인하는 바람에 젊었을 적에 좋은 외투 한 벌 걸칠 수가 없었고, 경이니 경의 집안이니 하는 이들과는 사귈 일이 없었지요.” 이 말의 가시가 더욱 따갑게 박힌 것은, 자기가 맺어 놓은 그 높은 연줄과 고귀한 인연이 실은 자기에게 얼마나 쓸모없었는지를 크로즈비가 막 깨닫기 시작한 참이었기 때문이다. 그가 승진한 것은 사실 다른 어느 누구보다 제 일을 잘 알았기 때문이었고, 인도청에 있다는 드 코시 백작부인의 세력 있는 친척은 아직 그 일로 쪽지 한 장 쓸 겨를조차 없었던 것이다.\n\n열한 시에 버터웰 씨가 크로즈비의 방으로 들어왔고, 새 사무국장은 억지로 웃음을 지어 걸쳐야 했다. 버터웰 씨는 쉰 줄의 유쾌하고 잘생긴 사람으로, 이제껏 세상을 놀라게 할 만한 일이라고는 해 본 적이 없었고 해 보려 한 적도 없었다. 그는 아마 완벽한 사람이었을 때보다 지체 높은 이들에게 조금 더 굽실거렸고 아랫사람들에게는 조금 더 생색을 냈을 것이다. 그러나 힘 있는 자들 앞에서 허리를 굽히는 그 태도에조차 어딘가 솔직하고 영국인다운 데가 있었고, 힘없는 이들에게는 매몰차거나 거만하기보다는 차라리 지나치게 공손했다. 그는 제가 그리 영리하지 못하다는 것을 알았고, 영리한 자들을 부리는 법도 알았다. 그는 좀처럼 실수를 하지 않았고, 남의 티눈을 밟지 않으려고 무척 조심했다. 원수는 하나도 없으면서 벗은 한둘 있었다. 그러니 버터웰 씨로 말하자면 인생의 길을 슬기롭게 걸어왔다고 해도 좋겠다. 서른다섯에 그는 재산을 조금 지닌 부인과 혼인했고, 지금은 퍼트니의 별장에서 유쾌하고 편안하고 웃음 띤 삶을 살고 있었다. 영국 신사가 제 나라에서 밥벌이하기가 얼마나 어려운가 하는 이야기를 들을 때면 — 그는 그런 이야기를 자주 들었다 — 버터웰 씨는 제가 걸어온 길을 얼마간 흐뭇하게 돌아보곤 했다. 그는 자기가 세상에 그리 많이 주지 않았음을 알았다. 그런데도 받기는 넉넉히 받았고, 아무도 그것을 아까워하지 않았다. “요령이지.” 버터웰 씨는 퍼트니 별장의 오솔길을 거닐며 스스로에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. “요령. 요령. 요령.”\n\n“크로즈비.” 그는 쾌활하게 방으로 들어서며 말했다. “진심으로 축하하네. 정말일세. 자네는 인생의 이른 나이에 한 계단을 올랐고, 그럴 자격이 충분하네. 나보다 훨씬 낫지. 나는 같은 자리에 임명됐을 때 자네만 못했으니.”\n\n“원, 별말씀을.” 크로즈비가 침울하게 말했다.\n\n“하지만 나는 별말이 아니라고 말하겠네. 우리는 이런 사람을 얻어 지독하게 운이 좋은 거야. 위원들에게도 그렇게 말해 두었네.”\n\n“정말 뭐라 감사드려야 할지 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“나는 진작부터 알고 있었네. 자네가 떠나기도 전부터 말이야. 래플 버플 경이 소득세 사무국으로 간다고 내게 말했거든. 거기 의장 자리는 이천 파운드일세, 자네도 알겠지만. 그리고 나는 위원회의 첫 자리를 약속받아 두었고.”\n\n“아, 미리 알았더라면 좋았을걸.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“모르는 편이 훨씬 낫네.” 버터웰이 말했다. “뜻밖의 일만 한 즐거움이 어디 있나! 게다가 그런 일이란 알면서도 또 모르는 것이거든. 이제야 알고 있었다고 말하는 거고, 알고 있었노라 맹세도 하겠네만, 그저께만 해도 살아 있는 누구에게도 그런 말은 하지 않았을 걸세. 잔과 입술 사이에 그런 미끄러짐이 얼마나 많은가. 래플 경이 소득세 사무국으로 가지 않았다고 생각해 보게!”\n\n“그렇지요.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 이제 다 됐네. 실은 내가 어제 위원회에 앉았지. 편지에 서명은 그 뒤에 했네만. 얻은 것보다 잃은 것이 더 많은 게 아닌가 싶기도 하이.”\n\n“무슨 말씀입니까! 연봉이 삼백 파운드 더 늘고 일은 줄었는데요?”\n\n“아, 하지만 일의 재미를 생각해 보게. 사무국장은 모든 것을 보고 모든 것을 알지. 그래도 나는 늙어 가고 있고, 자네 말대로 가벼운 일이 내게 맞을 걸세. 그건 그렇고, 내일 퍼트니로 내려오지 않겠나? 버터웰 부인이 새 사무국장을 보면 아주 기뻐할 걸세. 지금은 런던에 아무도 없으니 거절할 핑계도 없을 테고.”\n\n그러나 크로즈비 씨는 거절할 핑계를 기어이 찾아냈다. 지금 같은 마음으로는 버터웰 부인의 식탁에 앉아 웃고 있기란 도무지 불가능한 일이었다. 그는 알 듯 말 듯 하게, 반쯤만 털어놓는 투로, 중요한 사사로운 일이 있어 그날 저녁에는 반드시 시내에 남아 있어야 한다고 버터웰 씨에게 알렸다. 그리고 그가 말한 대로 “요즘은 제 몸이 제 것이 아니라서요.”\n\n“아, 그렇지, 그렇고말고. 그 일을 축하하는 것을 깜빡 잊었구먼. 그래, 혼인을 한다지? 그것참 반가운 일일세. 자네도 나만큼 운이 좋기를 바라네.”\n\n“고맙습니다.” 크로즈비가 다시 얼마간 침울하게 말했다.\n\n“게스트윅 가까이 사는 아가씨라던가? 아니면 그쯤 어디라던가?”\n\n“아, 아닙니다.” 크로즈비가 더듬거렸다. “그 아가씨는 바셋주 사람입니다.”\n\n“그것참, 나는 이름까지 들었는데. 벨이라던가, 테이트라던가, 볼이라던가, 그런 이름 아닌가?”\n\n“아닙니다.” 크로즈비가 낼 수 있는 대로 대담함을 그러모아 말했다. “드 코시라는 이름입니다.”\n\n“백작의 따님들 가운데 한 분인가?”\n\n“그렇습니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“이런, 미안하네. 내가 잘못 들었군. 자네는 아주 고귀한 집안과 인연을 맺게 되는 거로군. 자네가 인생에서 잘되어 간다니 진심으로 기쁘이.” 그러고서 버터웰은 그의 손을 아주 다정하게 흔들었다. 그가 벨이나 테이트나 볼과 혼인한다고 믿고 있었을 때에는 그런 각별한 찬동의 표시를 내놓지 않았던 것이다. 그러면서도 버터웰 씨는 무언가 잘못되었다는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 그는 아주 미덥고도 확실한 데서 이런 이야기를 들은 터였다. 크로즈비가 게스트윅 가까이 머무는 동안 그곳 지주의 조카딸과 약혼했다는 것, 그 아가씨는 돈이라고는 없다는 것. 그래서 버터웰 씨는 제 지혜를 다해, 벗 크로즈비가 사서 고생하는 얼간이라고 여겨 왔던 것이다. 그런데 이제 드 코시 집안의 딸과 혼인한다니! 버터웰 씨는 아주 어리둥절해졌다.\n\n“자, 우리는 두 시에 자리에 앉을 걸세, 알겠지. 물론 자네도 우리에게 와야 하고. 그 전에 틈이 나거든 내가 가진 서류를 자네에게 넘겨주겠네. 나는 이 자리에서 엘던 경 노릇을 하지는 않았으니, 그 서류들이 자네 허리를 부러뜨리지는 않을 걸세.”\n\n그 직후에 파울러 프랫이 크로즈비의 방으로 안내되어 들어왔고, 크로즈비는 프랫이 지켜보는 앞에서 지주에게 보내는 편지를 썼다.\n\n그는 제 승진에서 아무 기쁨도 얻을 수 없었다. 프랫이 떠난 뒤 그는 마음을 가볍게 해 보려 애썼다. 릴리와 그 아이가 당한 부당함을 등 뒤로 던져 버리고 인생에서 나아가는 성공에 생각을 붙들어 매려 했지만, 그렇게 되지 않았다. 제 손으로 불러들인 괴로움은 쫓겨나 주지 않는 법이다. 어떤 사람이 벗의 잘못으로, 혹은 운수의 수레바퀴가 돌아간 탓으로 천 파운드를 잃었다면, 그가 사내라면 그 슬픔을 내려놓고 발밑에 밟아 버릴 수 있다. 그는 제 억울함의 혼령을 쫓아내고 악한 것더러 제 집에서 나가라 명할 수 있다. 그러나 슬픔이 제 어리석음과 제 죄에서 온 것일 때에는 그런 축귀가 통하지 않는다. 더구나 제 이기심에서 온 것이라면 더욱 그렇다. 사람을 술로 몰아가는 것이 바로 그런 경우요, 사람을 아무 생각도 하지 않도록 내모는 것이 그런 경우요, 노름꾼과 앞뒤 없는 탕아를 만들어 내는 것, 자살을 부추기는 것이 그런 경우다. 릴리에게 이 편지를 쓰는 일을 어떻게 피할 수 있단 말인가? 제 머리를 쏘아 버리면 그것으로 죄다 끝이 나기는 하겠지. 제 승진에서 만족을 거두어 보려고 자리에 앉았을 때 그가 이른 생각이란 이런 것들이었다.\n\n그러나 크로즈비는 스스로 목숨을 끊을 사람이 아니었다. 그에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려주자면, 나는 그가 그러기에는 너무 괜찮은 사람이었다고 말해 두어야겠다. 그는 권총알 한 발이 이 세상에서 모든 것의 처음이자 끝이 될 수는 없다는 것을 너무나 잘 알았고, 그토록 비겁한 도피를 하기에는 그 안에 사내다움이 너무 많았다. 짐은 짊어져야 한다. 그러나 어떻게 짊어질 것인가? 그는 두 시가 될 때까지 거기 앉아 버터웰 씨도 사무국 서류도 내버려 둔 채, 사환이 와서 그를 위원회 앞으로 부를 때까지 제자리에서 꼼짝하지 않았다. 그가 방으로 들어섰을 때의 그 위원회는, 세상 사람들이 아마 그런 위원회를 어떠려니 상상하는 것과는 사뭇 다른 위원회였다. 둥근 탁자가 하나 있었고 그 위에 펜 몇 자루가 아무렇게나 놓여 있었으며, 문에서 가장 먼 쪽 곁에는 편안한 가죽 안락의자가 하나 있었다. 래플 버플 경은 옛 동료들과 작별하는 참이어서 벽난로에 등을 대고 서서 아주 큰 소리로 떠들고 있었다. 래플 경은 지독한 폭군이었고 위원회는 그를 치워 버리게 되어 여간 기쁘지 않았다. 그러나 이번이 그가 위원회 사무국에 모습을 보이는 마지막 자리인지라 다들 그 목소리를 얌전히 받아 주고 있었다. 버터웰 씨는 그의 곁에 바짝 붙어 서서 래플 경의 우스개에 부드럽게 웃어 주려 애쓰고 있었다. 키가 오 척을 겨우 넘을까 말까 한 자그마한 사람이, 작지만 정직해 보이는 눈에 머리를 짧게 깎고서, 안락의자 뒤에 서서 두 손을 비비며 래플 경이 어서 떠나 주기를, 그래야 자기가 앉을 수 있으니 애타게 바라고 있었다. 이 사람이 새 의장인 옵티미스트 씨였다. 《데일리 주피터》가 그의 임명을 두고 그토록 목청껏 칭송하면서, 지금의 총리대신은 오로지 공적만을 잣대로 승진을 시킨다는 점에서 앞서 간 어떤 총리대신보다도 뛰어남을 스스로 보여 주고 있다고 선언했던 바로 그 사람이다. 《데일리 주피터》는 두 주 전에 옵티미스트 씨의 자격을 힘껏 내세우는 매우 유창한 논설을 실었고, 제 충고가 받아들여진 것을 보고 흐뭇해한 것도 당연한 일이었다. 순종하는 대신이라면 제가 순종하는 그 권세로부터 칭찬을 받을 권리가 있지 않겠는가?\n\n옵티미스트 씨는 사실 부지런한 자그마한 신사로, 집안이 아주 좋았고, 평생 공직에서 나라를 섬겨 왔으며, 적어도 일 처리에 있어서는 정직한 사람이었다. 또 제 앞사람처럼 폭군도 아니었다. 다만 이제 맡게 된 자리를 감당할 만한 대포를 넉넉히 싣고 있느냐 하는 것은 물음거리일 수 있었다. 위원회에는 그 밖에 위원이 딱 한 사람 더 있었으니, 피아스코 소령이라는 이였다. 불평 많고 가슴이 무너진 채 말이 없는 사람으로, 몇 해 전에 달리 그를 원하는 데가 없다는 이유로 일반위원회 사무국에 보내진 이였다. 그는 공직에 발을 들일 때 큰일을 해내겠다고 마음먹었던 사람이었고, 웬만큼 큰일을 해낼 만한 재주와 기운도 지니고 있었다. 높은 자리에 있는 이들의 귀도 어느 정도까지는 얻고 있었다. 그러나 어쩌다 보니 일이 그에게 잘 풀리지 않았고, 제 길을 달리다가 기둥을 잘못된 쪽으로 돌고 말았다. 그는 아직 한창때였으나, 피아스코 소령이 이제 세상으로부터든 정부로부터든 더 기대할 것이 없다는 것은 누구나 아는 일이었다. 실로, 피아스코 소령은 아무 일도 해 주지 않으면서 후한 봉급만 받고 있다고, 그저 하루 네 시간씩 한 주에 나흘이나 닷새 의자를 차지하고 앉아 이런저런 서식과 문서에 제 이름을 적어 넣고, 이런저런 서류를 읽거나 읽는 시늉을 할 뿐 실은 아무 쓸모 있는 일도 하지 않는다고 말하는 이들도 없지 않았다. 반면에 피아스코 소령 자신은 스스로를 깊이 상처 입은 사람으로 여겨, 제가 당한 부당함을 곱씹으며 나날을 보냈다. 이제 그는 아무것도, 아무도 믿지 않았다. 그는 정직하려 애쓰며 공직 생활을 시작했으나, 이제는 제 둘레의 모든 사람을 부정직하다고 여겼다. 그는 어떤 사람에게서도 흡족함을 느끼지 못했고, 다만 어떤 일이 벌어져 제 또래의 이 아무개나 저 아무개가 잇속만 차리는 자요 거짓된 자요 사기꾼임이 드러날 때에만 흡족해했다. “나한테 그런 소리 마시게, 버터웰.” 그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 버터웰 씨와는 얼마간 반쯤 공적인 친분을 이어 오던 터라, 그는 그 신사의 단춧구멍을 붙들고 놓아 주지 않으며 말하곤 했다. “나한테 그런 소리 마시게. 나는 사람이라는 게 어떤지 알아. 세상을 다 봤네. 눈을 뜨고 세상일을 지켜봐 왔단 말일세. 그자가 무슨 짓을 하고 있었는지 나는 알고 있었네.” 그러고는 둘 다 잘 아는 어떤 관리의 교활한 짓거리를 일러바치되, 결코 그것을 성토하는 것은 아니고, 다만 문제의 그 사람이 악당이라는 것을 당연한 사실인 양 여기는 투로 말했다. 버터웰은 어깨를 으쓱하고 부드럽게 웃으며, 맹세코 자기는 세상이 피아스코가 말하는 것만큼 나쁘다고는 생각하지 않는다고 대답하곤 했다.\n\n실제로 그는 그렇게 생각하지 않았다. 버터웰은 여러 가지를 믿는 사람이었기 때문이다. 그는 이 땅에 있는 제 퍼트니 별장을 믿었고, 지금 순교를 겪지 않고도 저세상에서 어떤 종류의 퍼트니 별장을 얻을 수 있으리라는 것 또한 믿었다. 먼저 제 퍼트니 별장과 거기 딸린 온갖 안락함이요, 나라에 대한 의무는 그다음이었다. 버터웰 씨는 이렇게 제 처신을 다스렸다. 그리고 그는 그 별장이 저 자신에게만큼 아내에게도 편안한 집이 되기를, 또 벗들에게는 각별히 편안한 집이 되기를 마음 써 왔으니, 우리가 그의 신조를 두고 다툴 것까지는 없지 싶다.\n\n옵티미스트 씨는 모든 것을 믿었으나, 그중에서도 특히 총리대신을 믿었고, 《데일리 주피터》를 믿었고, 일반위원회 사무국을 믿었고, 저 자신을 믿었다. 그는 오래전부터 모든 것이 거의 옳게 되어 있다고 생각해 왔다. 그런데 이제 자기가 몸소 일반위원회 사무국의 의장이 되었으니, 모든 것이 반드시 옳게 되어 있으리라고 그는 아주 확신했다. 실로 래플 버플 경만은 한 번도 믿어 본 적이 없었다. 그러니 그 무시무시한 기사의 미운 목소리를 다시는 들을 일이 없으리라는 것이야말로 아마 그의 생애에서 가장 큰 기쁨이었을 것이다.\n\n새 위원회를 이루는 사람들이 누구인지를 보면, 크로즈비가 제 사무국에서 적잖이 영향력 있는 자리를 누리게 되리라고 기대했으리라 짐작할 만하다. 실제로 서기들 가운데에는 새 사무국장이 거의 모든 것을 제 뜻대로 하게 되리라고 서슴없이 말하는 이들도 있었다. 늙은 옵트로 말하자면 아무 어려움도 없으리라는 것이었다. 이러이러한 결정이 당신 자신의 결정이라고 일러 주기만 하면 그는 틀림없이 그 말을 믿어 준다는 것이다. 버터웰은 일을 좋아하지 않았고, 여러 해 동안 크로즈비에게 기대는 데 익숙해져 있었다. 피아스코로 말하자면 말로는 빈정댈지언정 행동으로는 전혀 아랑곳하지 않을 것이었다. 사무국 전체가 결딴이 난다 해도 피아스코는 제 특유의 음침한 방식으로 그 난장판을 즐길 사람이었다.\n\n“축하하네, 크로즈비.” 래플 경이 난로 앞 깔개를 밟고 서서, 새 사무국장이 제게로 다가와 손을 잡아 주기를 기다리며 말했다. 그러나 래플 경은 떠나는 사람이었고, 새 사무국장은 그 청을 들어주지 않았다.\n\n“고맙습니다, 래플 경.” 크로즈비는 깔개 쪽으로는 가지도 않은 채 말했다.\n\n“크로즈비 씨, 진심으로 축하드립니다.” 옵티미스트 씨가 말했다. “당신의 승진은 오로지 당신 자신의 공적에서 비롯된 결과입니다. 당신이 이제 맡게 된 높은 자리에 뽑힌 것은, 오직 거기 딸린 무거운 직무를 수행하기에 당신이 가장 적합한 사람이라고 여겨졌기 때문입니다. 흠, 으흠, 어험. 우리가 재무부에 올려야 한다고 여긴 그 추천에서 제가 맡은 몫으로 말하자면, 저는 평생 그때만큼 망설임이 적었던 적이 없다고 말해야겠습니다. 그리고 위원회의 다른 위원들에 대해서도 그만큼 말해도 좋으리라 믿습니다.” 그러고서 옵티미스트 씨는 찬동하는 말을 구하며 둘레를 둘러보았다. 그는 크로즈비를 맞이하려고 제 의자 뒤에 서 있던 자리에서 앞으로 나와 다정하게 손을 잡아 주었다. 피아스코 또한 제자리에서 일어나 제 둥지를 여간 잘 틀어 놓지 않았다고 크로즈비에게 속삭였다. 그러고는 다시 앉았다.\n\n“제게 관한 한 얼마든지 그렇게 말씀하셔도 좋습니다.” 버터웰이 말했다.\n\n“내가 재무장관에게 말했지.” 래플 경이 아주 큰 소리로, 대단한 권위를 실어 말했다. “다른 데서 데려올 일류 인재가 따로 없다면 내가 알맞은 후보를 하나 대겠노라고. ‘래플 경,’ 그가 말하더군. ‘나는 이 자리를 사무국 안에서 채울 생각이오. 그러니 당신의 의견을 듣는다면 반갑겠소.’ ‘그렇다면, 장관 각하,’ 내가 말했지. ‘크로즈비 씨가 그 사람이어야 합니다.’ ‘크로즈비 씨가 그 사람이 될 것이오.’ 장관이 말했네. 그리고 크로즈비 씨가 그 사람이 되었지.”\n\n“당신 친구 사크가 브록 경에게 그 이야기를 했다더군요.” 피아스코가 말했다. 사크 백작은 요즘 세력이 대단한 젊은 귀족이었고, 브록 경은 총리대신이었다. “사크 경께 고마워하셔야겠소.”\n\n“내 하인가 말한 것만큼이나 상관이 있었겠구먼.” 래플 경이 말했다.\n\n“위원회께서 저를 좋게 보아 주신 데 대해 대단히 감사드립니다.” 크로즈비가 정색하고 말했다. “사크 경께도 감사드립니다. 그리고 래플 경, 말씀하신 것처럼 그가 저를 위해 애써 주었다면 경의 하인에게도 감사드립니다.”\n\n“나는 그런 말을 한 적이 없네.” 래플 경이 말했다. “재무장관을 움직인 것이 물론 공적으로 개진된 내 의견이었다는 점을 자네가 알아 두는 것이 옳다고 여겼을 뿐이야. 자, 여러분, 나는 시내에서 부르는 데가 있으니 이만 작별 인사를 하겠소. 보그스, 내 마차는 준비됐나?” 그 말에 시중드는 사환이 문을 열었고, 위대한 래플 버플 경은 제가 예전에 수고하던 무대에서 마지막으로 퇴장했다.\n\n“그러면 새 자리의 직무에 대해서 말인데” — 옵티미스트 씨는 제 원수가 떠난 것을 두고, 눈빛이 한결 밝아지고 목소리가 한결 흡족해진 것 말고는 아무런 내색도 하지 않은 채 하던 말을 이었다 — “당신은 그 일에 아주 익숙하다는 것을 알게 될 겁니다.”\n\n“그야 물론이지요.” 버터웰이 말했다.\n\n“그리고 당신이 그 직무를 수행하여 스스로에게는 명예를, 부서에는 만족을, 나라에는 이로움을 똑같이 안겨 주리라고 나는 아주 확신합니다. 우리 앞에 놓이는 중요한 일이라면 언제든 당신의 의견을 들으면 기쁘겠습니다. 그리고 사무국 내부의 기강에 관해서는 안심하고 당신 손에 맡겨도 되겠다고 느낍니다. 중요한 일이 생기면 물론 우리와 의논하시겠지요. 나는 우리가 큰 편안함 속에서, 서로 믿으며 함께 잘해 나가리라고 아주 자신합니다.” 그러고서 옵티미스트 씨는 동료 위원들을 한 번 바라보고는 제 안락의자에 앉아, 앞에 놓인 서류를 손에 집어 들고 그날의 일상 업무를 시작했다.\n\n이 특별한 날에 사무국장이 위원회실에서 제 방으로 돌아온 것은 거의 다섯 시가 다 되어서였다. 래플 경이 큰소리를 치는 동안에도, 옵티미스트 씨가 연설을 하는 동안에도, 그 무게는 잠시도 그의 어깨에서 내려오지 않았다. 그는 그들이 아니라 릴리 데일을 생각하고 있었던 것이다. 그리고 그들은 그의 생각을 알아내지는 못했으되, 그가 어쩐지 평소의 그답지 않다는 것은 알아차렸다.\n\n“좋은 일을 만나고도 저렇게 신이 나지 않는 사람은 내 평생 처음 보오.” 옵티미스트 씨가 말했다.\n\n“아, 저 사람 마음에 무언가 걸리는 게 있소.” 버터웰이 말했다. “혼인을 한다지 아마.”\n\n“그렇다면야 신이 나지 않는 것도 이상할 게 없지요.” 저 자신이 독신인 피아스코 소령이 말했다.\n\n제 방에 돌아오자 크로즈비는 곧바로 편지지 한 장을 붙들었다. 서둘러 밀어붙이면 앨링턴으로 보낼 그 편지를 써낼 수 있기라도 하다는 듯이. 그러나 종이가 앞에 놓이고 펜이 손에 들려 있어도 편지는 써지지 않았고, 써지려 하지도 않았다. 무슨 말로 시작한단 말인가? 누구 앞으로 써야 하는가? 제가 스스로를 만들어 놓은 그 악당임을 어떻게 밝힌단 말인가? 사무국의 편지는 밤마다 여섯 시가 조금 지나면 걷어 가는데, 여섯 시가 되도록 그는 한 글자도 쓰지 못했다. “오늘 밤 집에서 쓰자.” 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 말하고는, 종이 한 귀퉁이를 찢어 릴리가 받았던 그 몇 줄을 갈겨썼다. 릴리가 어머니에게도 언니에게도 보이기를 마다한 그 쪽지였다. 크로즈비는 그것을 쓰면서, 그 몇 줄이 어떻게든 그 가엾은 아가씨로 하여금 다가올 타격을 미리 채비하게 해 주리라고, 적어도 무언가 잘못되었음을 알게는 해 주리라고 여겼다. 그러나 그렇게 여기면서 그는 그 아이의 한결같은 성품을 셈에 넣지 않았고, 무슨 일이 있어도 자기를 의심하지 않겠다던 그 아이의 약속도 떠올리지 못했다. 그는 그 쪽지를 써 놓고 모자를 집어 들고는, 십일월 저녁의 어스름을 헤치고 채링 크로스와 세인트 마틴스 레인을 거슬러 세븐 다이얼스와 블룸즈버리 쪽으로, 제게는 볼일도 없고 한 번도 드나든 적 없는 동네로 걸어 나갔다. 자기가 어디로 가는지도, 무엇 하러 가는지도 그는 거의 알지 못했다. 지금 저를 짓뭉개고 있는 이 짐의 무게에서 어떻게 벗어난단 말인가? 제 사무국의 말단 서기와 처지를 바꾸라 해도 그 말단 서기에게 제가 저지른 것 같은 믿음의 배신만 없다면 고맙게 바꾸겠다 싶었다.\n\n일곱 시 반에 그는 세브라이트에 이르렀고, 거기서 저녁을 먹었다. 사람은 가슴이 미어져도 저녁은 먹는 법이다. 그러고서 그는 삯마차를 잡아타고 마운트 스트리트의 제 집으로 갔다. 걷는 동안 그는 그날 밤 편지를 써서 부치기 전에는 잠자리에 들지 않겠노라고 스스로에게 맹세했다. 첫 낱말들이 종이에 적힌 것은 열두 시가 지나서였으나, 그래도 그는 제 맹세를 지켰다. 두 시와 세 시 사이, 차가운 달빛 속에서 그는 기어 나가 가장 가까운 우체국에 제 편지를 넣었다.\n\n# 제29장. 존 임스가 버턴 크레슨트로 돌아오다\n\n존 임스와 크로즈비는 같은 날 런던으로 돌아왔다. 임스가 황소 사건에서 드 게스트 백작을 도운 일과, 그때 백작이 얼마나 크게 고마워했는지는 다들 기억할 것이다. 그 일에 대한 기억과, 그런 용감한 행동으로 그런 벗을 얻었다고 어머니와 누이가 보내 준 열렬한 칭찬은 집에서 보내는 마지막 시간에 얼마간의 흐뭇함을 안겨 주었다. 그러나 그의 두 가지 불행은 참된 행복이라 할 만한 것을 조금이라도 허락하기에는 너무나 심각했다. 그는 자기가 미워하는 사내와 혼인하기로 약혼한 릴리를 뒤에 남겨 두고 떠나는 참이었고, 어밀리아 로퍼를 마주해야 하는 버턴 크레슨트로 돌아가는 참이었다—성이 난 어밀리아든, 사랑에 빠진 어밀리아든. 성이 난 어밀리아를 생각하면 몹시 무서웠다. 그러나 그가 가장 두려워한 것은 사랑에 빠진 어밀리아였다. 그는 편지에서 혼인을 사양했다. 그러나 그녀가 그의 반대를 죄다 말로 눌러 버리고, 그의 뜻과는 아랑곳없이 그를 교회로 끌고 가 버린다면 어쩐단 말인가!\n\n런던에 닿아 큰 여행 가방을 들고 삯마차에 올랐을 때, 그는 버턴 크레슨트로 가 달라고 마부에게 이르는 것조차 좀처럼 용기를 낼 수 없었다. “오늘 밤은 그냥 여관에서 자는 편이 나을지도 모르지.” 그는 혼잣말을 했다. “그러고 나서 사무국에서 크래들한테 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 들으면 될 테고.” 그런데도 그는 버턴 크레슨트라고 일러 주었고, 한번 그렇게 말하고 나자 바꾸기가 부끄러웠다. 그러나 그 낯익은 문 앞으로 마차가 다다랐을 때 그의 심장은 어찌나 내려앉았던지 아예 잃어버렸다고 해도 좋을 지경이었다. 마부가 문을 두드릴까요 하고 물었을 때 그는 대답을 하지 못했고, 문간에서 하녀가 그를 반겼을 때는 하마터면 달아날 뻔했다.\n\n“누구누구 계신가?” 그는 아주 낮은 목소리로 그렇게 물었다.\n\n“마님이 계시고요,” 하녀가 말했다. “스프루스 양이랑 루펙스 부인도 계십니다요. 그 양반은 또 심통이 나서 어디론가 가 버렸고요. 그리고 그—”\n\n“로퍼 양은 있나?” 그가 여전히 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n“아이참, 그럼요! 밀리어 아가씨도 계시지요.” 하녀가 잔인하리만큼 큰 목소리로 말했다. “방금까지 식당에서 상을 보고 계셨는걸요. 밀리어 아가씨!” 그러고서 하녀는 그 이름을 소리쳐 부르며 식당 문을 열어젖혔다. 조니 임스는 제 무릎이 몸을 버티기에는 너무 약해졌다고 느꼈다.\n\n그러나 밀리어 아가씨는 식당에 없었다. 그녀는 제게 맹세를 바친 흠모자의 마차가 다가오는 것을 알아차리고, 집안일에서 물러나 제 솔과 리본 틈에서 몸을 단장하는 편이 상책이라고 여겼던 것이다. 자기가 나서서 맞서야 할 적이 얼마나 약하고 얼마나 겁이 많은 사내인지 알 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 아마 싸움을 사뭇 다르게 치러 힘찬 한두 방으로 값싸게 속전속결의 승리를 거두었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 알지 못했다. 그녀는 그를 손아귀에 넣고 마음대로 부릴 수 있을지도 모른다고 여기기는 했다. 그러나 그의 다리가 어찌나 후들거리는지 숨 한 번 불면 넘어뜨릴 수 있으리라는 데까지는 생각이 미치지 못했다. 여자들 가운데 가장 못되고 가장 매정한 이들만이 남자에 대한 제 힘이 어디까지 미치는지를 안다. 남자들 가운데 가장 못되고 가장 매정한 이들만이 여자에 대한 제 힘이 어디까지 미치는지를 아는 것과 마찬가지다. 어밀리아 로퍼가 여성이라는 성의 훌륭한 본보기는 아니었으나, 그녀보다 못한 여자도 있었다.\n\n“거기엔 안 계십니다요, 임스 나리. 그래도 응접실에 가시면 뵐 수 있을 겁니다요.” 하녀가 말했다. “그리고 나리께서 돌아오신 걸 제일 반가워하실 분이 바로 그 아가씨지요, 임스 나리.” 그러나 그는 위층 거실 문 앞을 조심스레 지나쳐 안을 들여다보지도 않았고, 아무와도 마주치지 않은 채 용케 제 방으로 들어섰다. “더운물 여기 있습니다요, 임스 나리.” 반 시간쯤 지나 그에게 올라온 하녀가 말했다. “그리고 십 분 뒤면 저녁상이 차려집니다요. 크래들 씨도 들어오셨고, 마님 아드님도 오셨어요.”\n\n그에게는 아직 밖으로 나가 스트랜드의 어느 음식점에서 저녁을 먹을 길이 열려 있었다. 선약이 있다는 말만 남기고 나섰다가 그 고약한 시각을 뒤로 미룰 수도 있었다. 그는 그렇게 하기로 거의 마음을 굳혔고, 그가 층계참에 서 있을 때 거실 문이 열리지만 않았더라면 틀림없이 그렇게 했을 것이다. 문이 열렸고, 그는 모여 있던 사람들과 얼굴을 맞대고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 맨 앞에 크래들이 나왔고, 애석하게도 그 팔에 매달려 있는 것은 루펙스 부인이었다—아이깁티아 콘육스! 그다음에 스프루스 양이 젊은 로퍼와 함께 나왔고, 어밀리아와 그 어머니가 나란히 뒤를 따랐다. 이제 달아날 여지는 없었다. 가엾은 임스는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 깨닫기도 전에 나머지 사람들과 함께 식당으로 떠밀려 내려갔다. 다들 그를 보고 반가워하며 따뜻이 맞아 주었으나, 그는 넋이 나간 나머지 어밀리아의 목소리가 그 속에 섞여 있었는지조차 가려낼 수 없었다. 자기가 로퍼 부인과 루펙스 부인 사이에 앉아 있다는 사실을 알아차렸을 때 그는 이미 식탁 앞에 앉아 수프 접시를 앞에 두고 있었다. 뒤의 부인은 방에 들어서면서 크래들 씨에게서 제 몸을 떼어 놓았던 것이다. “여러 사정을 생각하면 우리는 떨어져 앉는 편이 나을 것 같아요.” 그녀가 말했다. “여자란 아무리 몸을 사려도 지나치지 않으니까요. 안 그래요, 로퍼 부인? 당신과 나 사이야 위험할 게 없지요, 그렇지 않아요, 임스 씨—더구나 어밀리아 양이 맞은편에 앉아 있는데야?” 그러나 마지막 말은 그의 귀에 대고 속삭인다고 한 것이었다.\n\n그러나 조니는 그녀에게 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 그저 이마의 땀을 닦는 것으로 그 순간을 넘겼다. 맞은편에 어밀리아가 앉아 그를 바라보고 있었다—그와 혼인하는 영광을 사양하겠노라고 그가 편지를 써 보낸 바로 그 어밀리아가. 그를 대하는 그녀의 심사가 어떤지는 그 낯빛으로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 그저 엄하고 무표정했으며, 그녀는 말없이 저녁을 먹을 작정인 듯했다. 루펙스 부인이 속삭이는 소리를 들었을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 얼핏 비웃음이 스쳤고, 동시에 그녀의 코끝이 조금 치켜 올라갔다는 것도 알아볼 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n“시골의 봄다운 아름다움 속에서 즐겁게 지내셨기를 바라요, 임스 씨.” 루펙스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아주 즐거웠습니다, 고맙습니다.” 그가 대답했다.\n\n“한 해 중에 이 가을철만큼 시골이 좋은 때도 없지요. 나로 말하자면 상류 사교계가 흩어진 뒤에 런던에 남아 있어 본 적이 한 번도 없어요. 우리는 대개 브로드스테어스로 가곤 했는데, 아주 매력적인 곳인 데다 사람들도 더없이 우아하답니다. 그런데 이제는—” 그러고서 그녀가 고개를 저었으므로, 좌중은 모두 그녀가 루펙스 씨의 죄상을 넌지시 가리키려 한다는 것을 알았다.\n\n“나 같으면 런던을 벗어나서 잠을 자고 싶은 마음은 없어요.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다. “여자란 제 머리 위에 제집 지붕이 있으면, 그 밖에 나가서는 결코 마음이 편하지 않은 법이거든요.”\n\n그녀는 루펙스 부인이 제집이 없다는 것을 빗대려던 것이 아니었으나, 그 부인은 대뜸 발끈했다. “그건 딱 달팽이가 하는 소리네요, 로퍼 부인. 그리고 제집을 갖는 일로 말하자면, 그야 물론 때로는 아주 좋은 일이지요. 하지만 그것도 사정에 따라 다른 법이에요. 나야 근래에 셋방살이가 형편에 맞았지만, 앞으로 그보다 더 떨어져서 셋방을 갖게 되지 말란 법도 없지요—” 그러나 그녀는 여기서 말을 멈추고 크래들 씨 쪽을 건너다보며 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n“그리고 그 방을 세놓아야 하게 되겠지요.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다. “나보다는 하숙인 복이 좋으시길 바라요. 나는 몇 사람한테 어지간히도 시달렸으니까요. 제미마, 스프루스 양께 감자를 갖다 드려라. 스프루스 양, 고기 국물을 좀 더 드리게 해 주세요. 여기 정말로 넉넉하답니다.” 로퍼 부인은 아마도 토저스 씨를 떠올리고 있었을 것이다.\n\n“나도 그러기를 바라요.” 루펙스 부인이 말했다. “하지만 아까 말씀드린 대로 브로드스테어스는 아주 좋아요. 브로드스테어스에 가 보신 적 있으세요, 크래들 씨?”\n\n“한 번도 없습니다, 루펙스 부인. 저는 휴가 때면 대개 외국으로 나갑니다. 아시다시피 세상을 더 넓게 볼 수 있으니까요. 지난 유월에는 디에프에 있었는데 아주 즐거웠지요. 다만 좀 쓸쓸하긴 했습니다. 올해는 오스텐드로 갈 참입니다. 다만 오스텐드에 십이월은 너무 늦은 철이지요. 내가 십이월에 걸린 건 지독하게 억울한 노릇이었어, 안 그런가, 조니?”\n\n“그래, 그랬지.” 임스가 말했다. “나는 더 잘 챙겼고.”\n\n“그러면 임스 씨는 무얼 하고 계셨나요?” 루펙스 부인이 더없이 상냥한 미소를 지으며 말했다. “무엇을 하셨든 미인을 받드는 대의를 저버리지는 않으셨을 테지요, 나는 그렇게 믿어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 뜻을 담은 미소를 지으며 어밀리아 쪽을 건너다보았다. 그러나 어밀리아는 제 접시에 열중해 있었고, 루펙스 부인에게도 존 임스에게도 눈길을 돌리지 않은 채 저녁을 계속 먹었다.\n\n“딱히 한 일은 아무것도 없습니다.” 임스가 말했다. “그저 어머니와 함께 지내다 왔지요.”\n\n“여기서는 우리끼리 아주 정답게 지냈지요, 안 그래요, 어밀리아 양?” 루펙스 부인이 말을 이었다. “다만 이따금 하늘에 구름이 끼고 잔칫상의 불빛이 어두워질 뿐이지요.” 그러고서 그녀는 손수건을 눈으로 가져가 깊이 흐느꼈고, 좌중은 모두 그녀가 또 제 남편의 죄상을 넌지시 가리키고 있음을 알았다.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나자마자 부인들은 젊은 로퍼 씨와 함께 물러갔고, 임스와 크래들만 남아 식당 난롯가에서 포도주를—혹은 형편에 따라 진에 물을 탄 잔을—들게 되었다. “이보게 코들, 이 친구야.” 한 사람이 말했다. “이보게 조니, 이 사람아.” 다른 사람이 말했다. “사무국에 무슨 소식은 없나?” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“머거리지가 아주 못된 짓을 하고 있어.” 머거리지는 크래들의 방 차석 서기였다. “우리는 그자를 따돌려서 공무가 아니면 말도 걸지 않기로 했네. 그렇지만 사실대로 말하자면 여기 집안일로 손이 어찌나 바빴는지 사무국 일은 별로 생각할 겨를도 없었어. 그 여자를 어떻게 하면 좋겠나?”\n\n“어떻게 하다니? 뭘 어떻게 한단 말인가?”\n\n“그러니까, 그 여자를 어떻게 해야 하느냐 말일세. 그 부인과 어떻게 꾸려 가야 하나? 루펙스가 또 질투 발작이 나서 나가 버렸단 말이지.”\n\n“하지만 자네 잘못은 아니잖나?”\n\n“글쎄, 그건 잘라 말하기 어렵네. 나는 그 부인이 좋아. 길게 말할 것도 짧게 말할 것도 없이 그게 전부야. 지독하게 좋단 말이지.”\n\n“하지만 이보게 코들, 그 부인은 그 사내의 아내가 아닌가.”\n\n“아, 그럼, 나도 다 알고 있네. 나를 두둔할 생각은 없어. 잘못된 일이지, 알고 있네—즐겁기는 하지만 잘못된 일이야. 그렇지만 사내가 어쩌겠나? 엄격한 도덕으로 따지자면 나는 이 하숙을 떠나야 마땅하겠지. 하지만 이런 참, 사내가 그런 식으로 쫓겨나야 할 까닭이 어디 있나. 게다가 로퍼 아줌마한테 셈을 깨끗이 치를 형편도 못 되고. 그런데 이보게, 그 금줄은 누가 준 건가?”\n\n“음, 게스트윅의 오랜 집안 벗이지. 아니, 좀 더 정확히 말하자면 우리 아버지를 알았다고 하는 사람이야.”\n\n“자네 아버님을 알았다고 그런 걸 주었단 말인가! 시계도 딸렸나?”\n\n“그래, 시계도 있네. 그런데 꼭 그런 사연은 아니었어. 황소 때문에 소동이 좀 있었거든. 사실대로 말하면 드 게스트 백작이야. 자네가 평생 만나 본 중에 제일 별난 양반이지, 코들. 그렇지만 참 대단한 분이야. 성탄절에 그 어른 댁에 가서 저녁을 먹기로 되어 있네.” 그러고서 황소에 얽힌 그 옛이야기를 다 들려주었다.\n\n“나도 들판에서 황소한테 쫓기는 귀족 나리 하나 만났으면 좋겠군.” 크래들이 말했다. 그러나 크래들 씨가 그 소원을 이루었다 한들 과연 시계를 벌어들였을지는 우리가 의심해 보아도 좋으리라.\n\n“알다시피 말이야.” 크래들이 자기가 이야기하기를 가장 즐기는 화제로 되돌아가며 말을 이었다. “그 사내가 못되게 구는 것까지 내가 책임질 일은 아니잖나.”\n\n“누가 자네더러 책임지라고 하던가?”\n\n“아니, 그렇게 말하는 사람이야 없지. 하지만 사람들이 그렇게 여기는 것 같단 말이야. 그 사내가 곁에 있을 때는 나도 그 부인에게 말을 거의 걸지 않아. 그 부인이야 여자들이 다 그렇듯 생각이 짧고 들떠 있어서 내 팔짱을 낀다든지 그런 짓을 하지, 자네도 알잖나. 그러면 그자는 분해서 미쳐 날뛰지. 하지만 내 명예를 걸고 말하는데, 나는 그 부인에게 나쁜 뜻이 있다고는 생각하지 않네.”\n\n“나도 그렇지 않다고 보네.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있지. 나는 아니기를 진심으로 바라네.”\n\n“그런데 그 사람은 지금 어디 있나?”\n\n“이건 우리끼리만 아는 얘긴데, 그 부인이 오늘 오후에 그 사내를 찾으러 갔네. 그자가 돈을 내주지 않으면 그 부인은 여기 머물 수도 없고, 하기야 그렇다고 떠날 수도 없는 형편이야. 내가 한 가지 이야기하면 자네 아무한테도 말하지 않겠나?”\n\n“물론 말하지 않겠네.”\n\n“이건 세상 누구에게도 알려지면 안 되네. 내가 그 부인에게 칠 파운드 십 실링을 빌려주었어. 로퍼 아줌마한테 셈이 그렇게 밀린 것도 그것 때문일세.”\n\n“그렇다면 자네는 애만 쓰고 바보짓을 한 걸세.”\n\n“아, 딱 자네다운 소리로군. 나는 늘 자네한테는 참된 낭만이라는 감정이 없다고 말해 왔지. 나 같으면 마음에 둔 여자한테는 등에 걸친 웃옷이라도 벗어 줄 걸세.”\n\n“나라면 그보다 더한 것도 하겠네.” 조니가 말했다. “내 몸에서 심장을 꺼내 주겠어. 사랑하는 아가씨를 위해서라면 산 채로 토막이 나도 좋아. 하지만 남의 아내를 위해서라면 안 될 말이지.”\n\n“그거야 취향 문제지. 아무튼 그 부인이 오늘 드루리 레인에 있는, 루펙스가 드나드는 그 집으로 그자를 찾아갔어. 거기서 지독한 소동이 벌어졌지. 그자는 길 한복판에서 목숨을 끊겠다고 나섰고, 부인은 그게 전부 질투에서 나온 일이라고 잘라 말하더군. 내가 어떤 세월을 보내고 있는지 좀 생각해 보게—말하자면 늘 화약 더미 위에 서 있는 셈이야. 그자가 언제 여기 들이닥칠지 모르는 노릇이지. 하지만 정말이지, 죽었다 깨어나도 나는 그 부인을 저버릴 수가 없네. 내가 그 부인에게 등을 돌린다면 그 부인한테는 이 세상에 벗이라고는 하나도 남지 않을 테니까. 그런데 L. D.는 어떻게 되었나? 내 한마디 해 두겠는데—자네는 그 거룩하신 어밀리아 때문에 곤경을 좀 치를 걸세.”\n\n“내가 그럴까?”\n\n“맹세코 그럴 걸세. 그런데 그동안 L. D.는 어떻게 되었나?”\n\n“L. D.는 어돌퍼스 크로즈비라는 사내와 혼인하기로 약혼했네.” 가엾은 조니가 천천히 말했다. “미안하지만 그 사람 이야기는 더 하지 마세.”\n\n“휘이—이—이! 그래서 자네가 그렇게 풀이 죽어 있었군! L. D.가 크로즈비와 혼인하다니! 아니, 그 사람이 바로 일반위원회 사무국의 새 사무국장이 될 사람 아닌가. 알다시피 거기 의장으로 있던 늙은 허플 스커플이 우리 쪽으로 왔거든. G. C.에 인사가 크게 돌았고, 그 크로즈비가 사무국장 자리를 차지하게 된 거야. 운 좋은 친구지, 안 그런가?”\n\n“그자의 운수 따위야 나는 아무것도 모르네. 그자는 처음 눈에 들어오는 순간부터 미워지는 그런 부류야. 언젠가 내가 살아서 그자를 걷어차게 되리라는 느낌 같은 게 있어.”\n\n“사정이 그렇게 되었단 말이지? 그럼 이제 어밀리아가 제 뜻대로 다 하겠는걸.”\n\n“내 한마디 해 두겠는데, 코들. 나는 어밀리아 로퍼와 혼인하느니 차라리 다락문으로 기어 올라가 지붕에서 지하 앞마당으로 몸을 던지겠네.”\n\n“돌아온 뒤로 그 아가씨와 무슨 이야기라도 나누었나?”\n\n“한마디도 안 했네.”\n\n“그렇다면 솔직히 말해 두는데 자네 앞에 곤경이 놓여 있어. 어밀리아와 머라이어가—루펙스 부인 말일세—요즘 도둑들처럼 짝짜꿍이 맞아서 자네 이야기를 해 왔거든. 머라이어가—그러니까 루펙스 부인이 나한테 죄다 흘려 준다네. 자네 발밑을 조심해야 할 걸세, 이 친구야.”\n\n임스는 그 문제를 더 이야기하고 싶지 않았으므로 말없이 제 토디를 마저 비웠다. 그러나 크래들은 제 형편 가운데 자랑할 만한 구석이 있다고 여기던 터라 이내 제 자신의 몹시 별난 처지에 얽힌 이야기로 돌아갔다. “맹세코 사내가 이런 처지에 놓인 적이 여태 있었나 싶네.” 그가 말했다. “그 부인은 나더러 지켜 달라고 기대는데, 그러면서 내가 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가?”\n\n마침내 크래들이 일어서더니 부인들에게 가 보아야겠다고 잘라 말했다. “그 부인은 어찌나 예민한지, 곁에서 힘이 되어 주는 사람이 없으면 몸이 상하고 만다네.”\n\n임스는 끽연실이든 극장이든 아니면 거리를 좀 걷든 하겠노라고 잘라 말했다. 버턴 크레슨트에서는 미인의 미소가 그에게 더는 아무런 매력이 없었다.\n\n“돌아온 첫날 밤이니 다들 자네가 차 한 잔은 들 거라고 기다릴 걸세.” 크래들이 말했다. 그러나 임스는 기다리려면 기다리라지 하고 잘라 말했다.\n\n“그럴 기분이 아닐세.” 그가 말했다. “내 한마디 해 두겠는데, 크래들, 나는 이 집을 떠나 어디든 따로 방을 얻을 참이야. 다시는 하숙집에 들어가지 않겠네.”\n\n그렇게 말할 때 그는 식당 문 앞에 서 있었다. 그러나 그렇게 쉽게 빠져나가도록 놓아주지는 않았다. 그가 복도로 나서자 제미마가 세모꼴로 접은 쪽지를 손에 들고 거기 서 있었다. “밀리어 아가씨한테서 온 겁니다요.” 그녀가 말했다. “밀리어 아가씨는 뒤쪽 응접실에 혼자 계십니다요.”\n\n가엾은 조니는 쪽지를 받아 들고 현관문 위에 걸린 등불에 비추어 읽었다.\n\n“돌아오신 날에 저에게 말도 걸지 않으실 건가요? 잠깐이라도 저를 보지 않고 이 집을 나서시다니 그럴 리는 없겠지요. 저는 뒤쪽 응접실에 있어요.”\n\n그 말을 다 읽고 나서 그는 모자를 쓴 채 복도에 우두커니 서 있었다. 제미마는 젊은 사내가 뒤쪽 응접실에서 제 연인을 단둘이 만나는 일을 두고 왜 망설이는지 도무지 알 수 없었으므로, 남들도 다 들리는 그 목소리로 다시 그에게 속삭였다. “밀리어 아가씨가 저기 계십니다요, 나리. 나머지 사람들은 죄다 위층에 있고요!” 그렇게 떠밀린 임스는 모자를 내려놓고 느린 걸음으로 뒤쪽 응접실로 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n적은 어떤 모습으로 나올 것인가? 그가 맞닥뜨릴 것은 성이 난 어밀리아인가, 사랑에 빠진 어밀리아인가? 식탁 너머로 슬쩍 눈길을 던져 보았을 때 그녀는 엄하고 도전적으로 보였고, 그래서 그는 이제 그녀가 제 억울함을 두고 큰 소리로 을러대고 따지고 들리라 예상했다. 그러나 그렇지 않았다. 그가 방으로 들어섰을 때 그녀는 그에게 등을 돌린 채 벽난로 선반에 기대어 서 있었고, 처음 한동안은 입을 열려고도 하지 않았다. 그는 방 한가운데로 걸어 들어가 거기 선 채 그녀가 시작하기를 기다렸다.\n\n“문 닫으세요!” 그녀가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 말했다. “저한테 하실 말씀을 하녀가 죄다 듣는 건 바라지 않으실 테니까요!”\n\n그래서 그는 문을 닫았다. 그러나 어밀리아는 여전히 그에게 등을 돌린 채 벽난로 선반에 기대어 서 있었다.\n\n그에게는 할 말이 그리 많지 않은 듯했다. 그는 여전히 완전히 잠자코 있었으니까.\n\n“어머!” 한참 만에 어밀리아가 그렇게 말하고는 다시 어깨 너머로 돌아보았다. “어머, 임스 씨!”\n\n“제미마가 쪽지를 주기에 이렇게 온 겁니다.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 우리가 만나는 방식이 이런 건가요!” 그녀가 갑자기 그를 향해 돌아서며, 길고 검은 머리채를 어깨 뒤로 넘기면서 외쳤다. 그녀에게 얼마간의 아름다움이 있는 것만은 분명했다. 눈은 크고 반짝였으며 어깨선도 고왔다. 화가가 유디트를 그리는 데 모델로 삼기에는 알맞았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 얼굴을 찬찬히 들여다본 사내라면 그녀가 아내 노릇을 잘하리라고 생각할 수 있었을지 나는 의심스럽다. “아, 존, 우리 같은 사랑을 나누고서도 이래야 하나요?” 그러고서 그녀는 두 손을 마주 잡고 그의 앞에 섰다.\n\n“무슨 말씀인지 모르겠는데요.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“L. D.와 혼인하기로 약혼하셨다면 지금 당장 그렇다고 말씀하세요. 사내답게 툭 털어놓으시라고요, 임스 씨.”\n\n“아닙니다.” 임스가 말했다. “나는 그쪽이 말하는 그 아가씨와 혼인하기로 약혼하지 않았어요.”\n\n“명예를 걸고요?”\n\n“그 사람 이야기는 입에 올리지 마세요. 나는 그 사람과 혼인하지 않을 거고, 그러면 된 거 아닙니까.”\n\n“내가 그 사람 이야기를 하고 싶어 한다고 생각하세요? 그 사람이 당신한테 아무것도 아닌 이상, L. D.가 나한테 무엇이겠어요? 아, 조니, 왜 나한테 그런 매정한 편지를 쓰셨나요?” 그러고서 그녀는 그의 어깨에 기댔다—아니, 기대려고 했다.\n\n임스가 그녀를 뿌리쳤다고는 말할 수 없다. 그에게는 그럴 용기가 없었으니까. 그러나 그가 어깨를 이리저리 들썩거리는 바람에 기대기가 불편해진 그녀는 하는 수 없이 다시 몸을 곧추세워야 했다. “왜 그런 모진 편지를 쓰셨어요?” 그녀가 다시 말했다.\n\n“그게 제일 낫다고 생각했으니까요, 어밀리아. 한 해 구십 파운드로 사내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요?”\n\n“하지만 어머님이 이십 파운드를 대 주시잖아요.”\n\n“그러면 백십 파운드로 사내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요?”\n\n“해마다 오 파운드씩 오르고요.” 사정에 밝은 어밀리아가 말했다. “물론 우리는 여기서 엄마와 함께 살 거고, 당신은 지금처럼 엄마한테 하숙비를 내면 그만이지요. 마음이 제자리에 있다면, 조니, 돈 생각을 그렇게까지 하지는 않을 거예요. 당신 말대로 정말 나를 사랑한다면—” 그러고서 작은 흐느낌이 새어 나오며 말이 끊겼다. 말은 끊겼으나 그녀는 다시 그의 어깨에 얹혀 있었다. 그가 어쩌겠는가? 사실 그의 유일한 바람은 달아나는 것이었는데도, 그의 팔은 제 뜻과는 아주 딴판으로 그녀의 허리를 감아 들어가고 말았다. 그런 싸움에서는 여자 쪽에 유리한 점이 어찌나 많은지! “아, 조니.” 그의 팔에 힘이 들어가는 것을 느끼자마자 그녀가 다시 말했다. “어머나, 참 고운 시계를 가지셨네요.” 그러고서 그녀는 그의 주머니에서 그 패물을 꺼냈다. “이거 사신 거예요?”\n\n“아니요, 받은 겁니다.”\n\n“존 임스, L. D.가 준 건가요?”\n\n“아니, 아니, 아닙니다.” 그가 말하면서 발로 바닥을 구르며 소리쳤다.\n\n“어머, 미안해요.” 어밀리아가 그 기세에 잠시 눌려 말했다. “어머님이 주셨나 보죠.”\n\n“아니요, 어떤 남자가 준 겁니다. 이제 시계 이야기는 그만하세요.”\n\n“나를 다시 사랑한다고만 말해 주시면 나는 아무래도 좋아요, 조니. 이런 걸 물으면 안 되는지도 모르고, 숙녀답지 못한 일인지도 모르지요. 하지만 내 마음을 당신이 가지고 있는 걸 아는데 내가 어쩌겠어요. 이제 위층으로 올라가서 우리와 차 한 잔 드세요, 네?”\n\n그가 어쩌겠는가? 그는 올라가서 차를 들겠다고 말했다. 그리고 그녀를 문으로 이끌면서 얼굴을 숙여 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다. 아, 조니 임스! 그러나 그런 겨루기에서는 여자 쪽에 유리한 점이 참으로 많은 법이다.\n\n# 제30장. 「그이에게서 온 건가요?」\n\n크로즈비가 앨링턴으로 그 치명적인 편지를 써서 부쳤다는 것은 내가 이미 말해 두었으니, 이제 우리는 그 편지를 따라 그곳까지 내려가 보아야 한다. 지주가 제 집으로 돌아온 이튿날 아침, 앨링턴의 우체국장 크럼프 부인은 자기 앞으로 온 소포를 우편으로 받았다. 부인이 그것을 뜯어 보니 안에는 데일 부인 앞으로 된 편지 한 통이 들어 있었고, 그것을 즉시 그 부인의 손에 직접 건네 달라는 부탁이 적혀 있었다. 이것이 크로즈비의 편지였다.\n\n“릴리 아가씨의 그 신사분한테서 온 것입니다요.” 크럼프 부인은 글씨를 들여다보며 말했다. “무슨 일이 난 게 틀림없습니다요. 안 그러고서야 그 어머니께 이런 식으로 편지를 쓸 리가 없지요.” 그러면서도 크럼프 부인은 지체 없이 모자를 눌러쓰고 편지를 든 채 작은 집까지 터덕터덕 걸어 올라갔다. “마님을 직접 뵈어야겠습니다요.” 크럼프 부인이 말했다. 그리하여 데일 부인이 아래층 현관으로 불려 내려와 그 꾸러미를 받았다. 릴리는 아침 식사 방에 있다가 우체국장이 오는 것을 보았고, 손에 편지를 들고 있는 것도 보았다. 잠깐 동안 릴리는 그것이 제게 온 것이라고, 그 노파가 그저 마음이 좋아 손수 가져다준 것이라고 생각했다. 그러나 어머니를 찾는다는 말을 듣자 릴리는 곧바로 물러나 방문을 닫았다. 무언가 잘못되었다는 예감에 가슴이 내려앉았으나, 그것이 무엇일지 굳이 헤아려 보려 하지는 않았다. 어차피 제가 기다리는 편지는 늘 오던 우편배달부가 가져올 수도 있는 것이었다. 벨은 아직 내려오지 않았고, 릴리는 아침 식탁의 찻잔들 앞에 혼자 서서 자기가 두려워해야 할 무언가가 있다고 느꼈다. 어머니는 곧바로 방으로 들어오지 않고, 잠깐 사이를 두었다가 다시 위층으로 올라갔다. 그리하여 릴리는 식탁에 기대섰다가, 창가에 갔다가, 안락의자 두 개 가운데 하나에 앉았다가 하면서 십 분쯤을 그렇게 있었고, 그때 벨이 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n“엄마는 아직 안 내려오셨니?” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“언니,” 릴리가 말했다. “무슨 일이 생겼어. 엄마한테 편지가 왔어.”\n\n“생겼다니! 무슨 일이 생겼다는 거야? 누가 아프기라도 해? 편지는 누구한테서 온 건데?” 그러면서 벨은 어머니를 찾으러 문으로 도로 나가려 했다.\n\n“잠깐, 언니.” 릴리가 말했다. “아직 엄마한테 가지 마. 그거, 어돌퍼스한테서 온 것 같아.”\n\n“아, 릴리, 그게 무슨 소리야?”\n\n“나도 몰라, 언니. 조금만 더 기다려 보자. 그런 얼굴 하지 마, 언니.” 그러면서 릴리는 침착해 보이려 애썼고, 거의 성공했다.\n\n“네가 나를 이렇게 겁먹게 만들잖니.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“나도 겁이 나. 어제는 그이가 겨우 한 줄 보냈는데, 오늘은 아무것도 안 보냈어. 그이한테 무슨 변고라도 생긴 거라면! 크럼프 부인이 편지를 손수 엄마한테 가져왔다는 것도 참 이상하잖아.”\n\n“정말 그이한테서 온 거야?”\n\n“아니, 엄마하고 얘기해 본 건 아니야. 지금 올라가 볼게. 언니는 오지 마. 아, 언니, 그렇게 슬픈 얼굴 하지 마.” 그러고서 릴리는 언니에게 다가가 입을 맞추고는, 아주 조용한 걸음으로 어머니 방까지 올라갔다. “엄마, 들어가도 돼요?” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“아! 내 딸아!”\n\n“그이한테서 온 거죠, 엄마. 한꺼번에 다 말해 주세요.”\n\n데일 부인은 편지를 이미 읽은 뒤였다. 빠르게 훑는 눈길로 그 내용 전부를 제 것으로 만들었고, 자기들에게 닥친 슬픔이 어떤 것이며 얼마나 큰 것인지 이미 알고 있었다. 그것은 희망이라고는 허락하지 않는 슬픔이었다. 그런 편지를 쓴 남자는 결코 다시 돌아올 수 없었다. 설령 돌아온다 한들 그를 다시 반겨 맞아들일 수는 없었다. 벼락은 이미 떨어졌고, 이제 견뎌 내는 수밖에 없었다. 제 앞으로 온 편지 속에는 릴리 앞으로 된 아주 작은 쪽지가 한 장 들어 있었다. “동봉한 것을 따님에게 주십시오.” 크로즈비는 편지에 그렇게 적었다. “그렇게 하는 것이 잘못이라고 여기지 않으신다면 말입니다. 부인께서 읽어 보실 수 있도록 봉하지 않고 두었습니다.” 그러나 데일 부인은 아직 그것을 읽지 않았고, 지금은 손수건 밑에 감추어 두었다.\n\n크로즈비가 데일 부인에게 보낸 편지를 여기에 길게 옮기지는 않겠다. 그것은 편지지 넉 장을 채웠고, 그런 편지를 쓴 사람이라면 누구든 스스로를 악당이라고 느끼지 않을 수 없는 그런 편지였다. 우리는 그가 그것을 쓰기 어려워했다는 것을 보았다. 그러나 놀라운 일은, 사람이 그런 편지를 끝내 써낼 수 있었다는 사실 그 자체였다. “부인께서 저를 저주하시리라는 것을 압니다.” 그는 이렇게 썼다. “그리고 저는 저주받아 마땅합니다. 이 일로 벌을 받으리라는 것을 알고, 그 벌을 견뎌야 한다는 것도 압니다. 제게 가장 무거운 벌은 이것입니다—다시는 고개를 들고 살 수 없으리라는 것.” 그리고 또 이렇게 말했다. “제 유일한 변명은, 제가 그분을 행복하게 해 드릴 수 없으리라는 확신뿐입니다. 그분은 천사처럼, 순결한 생각과 거룩한 소망과 선하고 높고 고귀한 모든 것에 대한 믿음 속에서 자라났습니다. 저는 평생 낮고 비열하고 천한 것들에 둘러싸여 살아왔습니다. 제가 어떻게 그분과, 또 그분이 어떻게 저와 함께 살 수 있겠습니까? 이제는 그것이 그러하다는 것을 압니다. 그러나 제 잘못은 그분 곁에 있을 때 그것을 알지 못했다는 데 있습니다.” 편지 끝머리에 이르러 그는 이렇게 이어 갔다. “저는 모든 것을 다 말씀드리기로 했습니다. 그러므로 제가 다른 여자와 혼인을 약속했다는 것도 알려 드립니다. 아! 이것을 읽으실 때 부인의 심정이 얼마나 쓰라릴지 저는 눈에 선히 그려 볼 수 있습니다. 그러나 이것을 쓰는 제 심정보다 쓰라리지는 않을 것입니다. 그렇습니다. 저는 이미 저에게 어울리고 저 또한 어울릴 수 있는 사람과 약혼했습니다. 제게 가깝고 소중한 사람이 될 이를 두고 나쁘게 말하기를 부인께서 바라시지는 않겠지요. 다만 그 사람은, 제가 그렇게 함으로써 그 사람의 행복을 모조리 망가뜨리고 말리라는 속마음의 확신 없이 짝을 맺을 수 있는 사람입니다. 릴리언 양은,” 그는 이렇게 썼다. “언제나 제 기도 속에 있을 것입니다. 그리고 그분이 정직한 사람의 사랑 속에서, 자신이 이토록 정직하지 못한 자를 알았다는 사실을 부디 곧 잊게 되기를 바랍니다—어돌퍼스 크로즈비.”\n\n제 손으로 자신에 대해 그런 말들을 써 내려가며 앉아 있을 때, 홀로 놓인 작은 등의 스산한 불빛 아래 그의 낯빛은 대체 어떠했을까? 만일 그가 그 편지를 낮에 사무실에서, 사람들이 제 방을 드나드는 가운데 썼더라면, 자신에 대해 그토록 있는 그대로 쓰지는 못했으리라. 써 놓은 글자는 남는 법이고, 본래 겨냥한 것이 아닌 다른 눈들이 나중에 그것을 읽을지도 모른다는 데 생각이 미쳤을 것이다. 그러나 밤이 깊은 시각에 홀로 앉아, 참된 뉘우침으로 제 죄를 거의 뉘우치다시피 하면서, 그는 누가 그것을 읽든 상관없다고 스스로에게 선언했다. 적어도 그것들은 진실이어야 했다. 이제 그 글은 그것이 보내진 바로 그 사람에게 읽혔고, 딸은 어머니 앞에 서서 제게 내려질 선고를 들으려 하고 있었다.\n\n“한꺼번에 다 말해 주세요.” 릴리는 그렇게 말했다. 그러나 어머니가 무슨 말로 그 아이에게 말해 준단 말인가?\n\n“릴리.” 어머니는 자리에서 일어나며 그렇게 말했고, 두 통의 편지는 긴 의자 위에 남겨 두었다. 딸에게 온 것은 손수건 밑에 감추어져 있었으나, 자기가 읽은 것은 펼친 채로 보이게 두었다. 부인은 딸의 두 손을 제 두 손에 잡고 그 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다. “릴리, 내 딸아!” 그러고는 흐느낌이 터져 나와 이야기를 꺼내지 못했다.\n\n“그이에게서 온 건가요, 엄마? 제가 읽어도 돼요? 그이가 설마—”\n\n“크로즈비 씨한테서 온 거란다.”\n\n“그이가 아픈가요, 엄마? 얼른 말해 주세요. 아프다면 제가 그이에게 가겠어요.”\n\n“아니다, 얘야, 그이는 아프지 않다. 아직은 아니야—아직 읽지 마라. 아, 릴리! 나쁜 소식을 담고 있구나. 아주 나쁜 소식이야.”\n\n“엄마, 그이가 위험한 게 아니라면 저는 읽을 수 있어요. 그이에게 나쁜 일인가요, 저에게만 나쁜 일인가요?”\n\n바로 그때 하인이 문을 두드리더니, 대답도 기다리지 않고 문을 반쯤 열었다.\n\n“실례합니다요, 마님. 버나드 도련님이 아래에 오셔서 마님과 말씀을 나누고 싶다고 하십니다요.”\n\n“버나드가! 벨 아가씨더러 만나 보라고 해라.”\n\n“벨 아가씨도 함께 계십니다요, 마님. 그런데 도련님께서 꼭 마님과 말씀을 나누고 싶다고 하십니다요.”\n\n데일 부인은 릴리를 혼자 둘 수 없다고 느꼈다. 편지를 가지고 나갈 수도 없었고, 편지를 펼쳐 둔 채로 딸을 두고 갈 수도 없었다.\n\n“나는 만날 수 없다.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “무슨 일인지 물어보아라. 지금 당장은 내려갈 수 없다고 전하고.” 그러고서 하인이 갔고, 버나드는 벨에게 전할 말을 남겼다.\n\n“버나드,” 벨은 그에게 말했었다. “너 무슨 일인지 알고 있니? 크로즈비 씨한테 무슨 문제가 생긴 거야?” 그러자 그는 몇 마디로 벨에게 모든 것을 말했고, 숙모가 왜 제게 내려오지 않는지 알아차리고는 큰 집으로 돌아갔다. 그 소식에 거의 넋이 나간 벨은 저도 모르게 식탁 앞에 앉아 두 팔꿈치로 몸을 괴었다.\n\n“이 일이 그 애를 죽이고 말 거야.” 벨은 혼잣말을 했다. “내 릴리, 사랑하는 내 릴리! 정말로 그 애를 죽이고 말 거야!”\n\n그러나 어머니는 아직 딸 곁에 있었고, 이야기는 아직 하지 못한 채였다.\n\n“엄마.” 릴리가 말했다. “그게 무엇이든 저는 당연히 알아야 해요. 저도 이제 짐작이 가기 시작해요. 말하기가 엄마도 괴로우시겠죠. 제가 편지를 읽을까요?”\n\n데일 부인은 딸의 침착함에 놀랐다. 저 아이가 진실을 짐작했을 리 없다. 짐작했다면 저렇게 눈물 한 방울 없이, 꺾이지 않은 용기로 제 앞에 서 있지는 못하리라.\n\n“읽어도 좋다. 하지만 내가 먼저 말해 주어야겠구나. 아, 내 딸, 내 하나뿐인 아이!” 릴리는 이제 침대에 기대어 있었고, 어머니는 그 위로 몸을 굽혀 딸을 어루만지고 있었다.\n\n“그럼 말해 주세요.” 릴리가 말했다. “하지만 저는 그게 무엇인지 알아요. 그이는 저를 떠나 있는 동안 이 일을 곰곰이 생각해 보았고, 우리가 여겨 온 대로 되어서는 안 된다는 것을 깨달은 거예요. 떠나기 전에 제가 그이를 놓아주겠다고 했는데, 이제 그이는 제 말을 받아들이는 편이 낫다는 것을 안 거죠. 그런 거죠, 엄마?” 이 말에 데일 부인은 대답하지 않았으나, 릴리는 어머니의 기색으로 그렇다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n“저에게 직접 쓸 수도 있었을 텐데.” 릴리가 아주 도도하게 말했다. “엄마, 우리 아침 먹으러 내려가요. 그럼 그이는 저에게는 아무것도 보내지 않았나요?”\n\n“쪽지가 한 장 있다. 나더러 읽어 보라고 했다만 나는 뜯지 않았다. 여기 있구나.”\n\n“주세요.” 릴리가 거의 매섭게 말했다. “그이가 저에게 남긴 마지막 말을 제가 갖게 해 주세요.” 그러고서 릴리는 어머니의 손에서 쪽지를 받아 들었다.\n\n“릴리.” 쪽지에는 그렇게 적혀 있었다. “어머님께서 모든 것을 말씀해 주셨겠지요. 이 몇 줄을 읽기 전에 당신은, 자신이 도무지 믿을 수 없는 자를 믿었다는 것을 알게 될 것입니다. 당신이 나를 미워하리라는 것을 압니다.—용서해 달라는 말조차 나는 할 수 없습니다. 그래도 당신이 언젠가는 행복해지기를 기도하게 해 주십시오.—A. C.”\n\n릴리는 여전히 침대에 기댄 채 그 몇 줄을 읽었다. 그러고는 몸을 일으켜 의자 쪽으로 걸어가, 어머니에게 등을 돌리고 앉았다. 데일 부인은 소리 없이 딸을 뒤따라가 의자 등받이 뒤에 섰으나, 감히 말을 걸지 못했다. 릴리는 열린 창에 눈을 붙박은 채, 크로즈비의 쪽지를 손에 쥐고 오 분쯤을 그렇게 앉아 있었다.\n\n“저는 그이를 미워하지 않을 거예요. 그리고 그이를 용서해요.” 마침내 릴리는 목소리를 다잡으려 애쓰며 그렇게 말했고, 그 애씀이 온전히 성공하지는 못했다는 것을 좀처럼 드러내지 않았다. “제가 그이에게 다시 편지를 쓸 수는 없겠지요. 하지만 엄마가 편지를 써서 그렇게 전해 주세요. 이제 아침 먹으러 내려가요.” 그렇게 말하고 릴리는 의자에서 일어났다.\n\n데일 부인은 딸에게 말을 붙이기가 거의 두려웠다. 딸의 침착함이 너무나 완전했고, 태도가 너무나 엄하고 흔들림이 없었다. 가엾이 여기는 마음도 딱히 필요해 보이지 않고 동정조차 구하지 않는 아이에게, 어떻게 가엾이 여기는 마음과 동정을 건네야 할지 알 수 없었다. 게다가 릴리가 한 말을 다 이해할 수도 없었다. 그이를 놓아주겠다고 했다는 것은 무슨 뜻인가? 그렇다면 그가 떠나기 전에 둘 사이에 무슨 다툼이 있었단 말인가? 크로즈비는 편지에서 그런 이야기는 조금도 비치지 않았다. 그러나 데일 부인은 감히 아무것도 묻지 못했다.\n\n“네가 나를 겁나게 하는구나, 릴리.” 부인이 말했다. “네 그 침착함이 나를 겁나게 해.”\n\n“가엾은 엄마!” 그러면서 그 가엾은 딸은 어머니를 껴안으며 실제로 미소까지 지었다. “제 침착함에 겁먹으실 것 없어요. 저는 진실을 잘 알고 있어요. 저는 아주 불행해졌어요—아주요. 제 삶의 가장 밝은 희망들이 다 사라졌고요—그리고 세상 무엇보다 사랑하는 그이를 다시는 볼 수 없겠지요!” 그러고는 마침내 무너져, 어머니의 품에 안겨 울었다.\n\n그때에도 이 모든 일을 저지른 그 사람을 두고 노여운 말은 한마디도 나오지 않았다. 데일 부인은 감히 그를 노여워하는 말을 입에 담을 수 없다고 느꼈고, 가엾은 릴리에게서 노여운 말이 나올 리도 없었다. 게다가 릴리는 아직 그의 잘못을 다 알지 못했다. 그의 편지를 읽지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n“주세요, 엄마.” 마침내 릴리가 말했다. “어차피 언젠가는 해야 할 일이잖아요.”\n\n“지금은 안 된다, 릴리. 내가 다 말해 주었잖니—지금 네가 알아야 할 것은 다 말해 주었다.”\n\n“아니에요, 지금이요, 엄마.” 그러면서 그 달콤한 은방울 같은 목소리가 다시 엄해졌다. “지금 읽고, 그것으로 끝내겠어요.” 그리하여 데일 부인이 편지를 건네주었고, 릴리는 말없이 그것을 읽었다. 어머니는 조금 뒤에 서 있었으나 딸이 읽는 동안 그 모습을 낱낱이 지켜보았다. 릴리는 이제 침대 위로 몸을 뉘어, 한쪽 팔로 몸을 괴고 베개 위에 편지를 놓았다. 눈물이 흘러내려 이따금 읽기를 멈추고 눈을 닦았다. 흐느끼는 소리도 또렷이 들렸다. 그러나 릴리는 흔들림 없이 계속 읽어 내려가, 이미 다른 여자와 혼인을 약속했다고 크로즈비가 적어 놓은 줄에 이르렀다. 그때 어머니는 딸이 문득 멈추는 것을, 그리고 온몸에 가벼운 전율이 지나가는 것을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n“참 빠르기도 하셔라.” 릴리는 거의 속삭이듯 말했다. 그러고는 편지를 마저 읽었다. “그이에게 전해 주세요, 엄마.” 릴리가 말했다. “제가 그이를 용서하고, 그이를 미워하지 않겠다고요. 그렇게 전해 주실 거죠—제 말이라고요. 그러실 거죠?” 그러고서 릴리는 침대에서 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n데일 부인은 딸에게 그런 다짐을 해 주지 않았다. 지금의 심경에서 크로즈비를 향한 감정은 그녀 자신도 도무지 알 수도 헤아릴 수도 없는 것이었다. 만일 그가 눈앞에 있다면 암범처럼 달려들 것만 같았다. 이제껏 살면서 이 남자를 미워하는 것만큼 누군가를 미워해 본 적이 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 살인자였고, 살인자보다 더 나쁜 자였다. 그는 이리처럼 그녀의 작은 우리 안으로 파고들어 그녀의 새끼 양을 물어뜯고, 평생 상하고 온전치 못한 몸으로 남겨 놓았다. 어머니가 어떻게 그런 잘못을 용서할 수 있으며, 용서를 전하는 통로가 되기를 승낙할 수 있단 말인가?\n\n“꼭 해 주셔야 해요, 엄마. 그러지 않으시면 제가 하겠어요. 제가 그이를 사랑한다는 걸 기억해 주세요. 오직 한 사람만을 사랑한다는 게 어떤 것인지 엄마도 아시잖아요. 그이는 저를 아주 불행하게 만들었어요. 얼마나 불행한지 저도 아직 잘 모르겠어요. 하지만 저는 그이를 사랑했고, 지금도 사랑해요. 마음속으로는 그이도 저를 여전히 사랑한다고 믿어요. 그런 것이 있었던 자리에 미움과 용서 없음이 있어서는 안 돼요.”\n\n“그이를 용서할 수 있게 되기를 기도하마.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 그 말을 그대로 써 주셔야 해요. 정말로 꼭 그러셔야 해요, 엄마! ‘그 애가 당신을 용서했으며 당신을 미워하지 않겠다고 전해 달라 합니다.’ 그렇게 약속해 주세요!”\n\n“지금은 약속할 수 없구나, 릴리. 생각해 보고, 내 도리를 다하도록 애써 보마.”\n\n릴리는 이제 앉아서 어머니의 치맛자락을 붙잡고 있었다.\n\n“엄마.” 릴리는 어머니의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다. “이제 엄마는 저에게 아주 잘해 주셔야 해요. 그리고 저도 엄마에게 아주 잘해 드려야죠. 이제 우리는 늘 함께일 거예요. 제가 엄마의 벗이 되고 의논 상대가 되고, 예전보다 더 엄마의 모든 것이 되어 드릴게요. 이제 저는 엄마와 사랑에 빠져야겠어요.” 그러고서 릴리는 다시 미소 지었고, 뺨의 눈물은 거의 말라 있었다.\n\n마침내 두 사람은 아침 식사 방으로 내려갔다. 벨은 그 자리에서 움직이지 않고 있었다. 데일 부인이 먼저 방에 들어섰고, 릴리는 잠시 어머니 뒤에 몸을 숨기며 뒤따랐다. 그러고는 씩씩하게 앞으로 나서서 벨을 두 팔로 안아 제 가슴에 꼭 끌어당겼다.\n\n“언니,” 릴리가 말했다. “그이는 가 버렸어.”\n\n“릴리! 릴리! 릴리!” 벨이 울며 말했다.\n\n“그이는 가 버렸어! 며칠 지나면 그 얘기를 하게 되겠지. 그때는 비참함에 빠져 헤매지 않고 이야기하는 법도 알게 될 거야. 오늘은 더 말하지 말자. 목이 너무 말라, 언니. 차 좀 줘.” 그러고는 아침 식탁에 앉았다.\n\n릴리에게 차가 건네졌고, 릴리는 그것을 마셨다. 그 밖에는 누구도 그 식사를 마음 편히 들었다고 나는 말할 수 없다. 세 사람은 무서운 벼락이 그들 사이에 떨어지지 않았더라도 그러했을 법한 모습으로 앉아 있었고, 크로즈비와 그의 행실에 대해서는 더 이상 한마디도 나오지 않았다. 아침을 먹고 나서 곧 그들은 옆방으로 건너갔고, 릴리는 늘 하던 대로 곧바로 그림 앞에 앉았다. 어머니는 그런 딸을 애타는 눈으로 바라보며 그만두라고 말하고 싶었으나, 딸을 방해하기가 겁이 나 그러지 못했다. 십오 분쯤 릴리는 화판 앞에 앉아 붓이나 연필을 손에 들고 있다가, 이내 일어나 그것을 치웠다.\n\n“아닌 척해 봐야 소용없네요.” 릴리가 말했다. “저는 그림만 망치고 있어요. 하지만 내일은 나아질 거예요. 저는 가서 혼자 누워 있을게요, 엄마.” 그러고는 갔다.\n\n그러고 얼마 지나지 않아 데일 부인은 벨에게서 시아주버니의 전갈을 전해 듣고, 모자를 쓰고 큰 집으로 올라갔다.\n\n“그이가 나에게 무슨 말을 하려는지는 나도 안다.” 부인은 말했다. “그래도 가 보는 편이 낫겠지. 이 일을 두고 서로 이야기를 나누어야 할 테니까.” 그리하여 부인은 잔디밭을 가로질러 큰 집 현관으로 들어섰다. “시아주버님은 서재에 계시냐?” 부인은 하녀 하나에게 그렇게 묻고는, 문을 두드린 뒤 기별 없이 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n지주가 안락의자에서 일어나 부인을 맞으러 나왔다.\n\n“제수씨,” 그가 말했다. “다 알고 계시겠지요.”\n\n“네.” 부인이 말했다. “이걸 읽어 보세요.” 그러고는 크로즈비의 편지를 그에게 건넸다. “사람이 어찌 저리 악할 수 있는지 누가 알았겠어요?”\n\n“그 아이도 들었소?” 지주가 물었다. “견뎌 낼 수 있겠소?”\n\n“놀랍도록요! 그 아이의 힘에 제가 다 놀랐어요. 오히려 그게 무서워요. 반드시 되짚어 무너지는 때가 오리라는 걸 아니까요. 그 아이는 한순간도 그 밑에 주저앉지 않았어요. 저로 말하면, 제 몫의 짐을 견디게 해 주는 것도 그 아이의 힘인 것만 같아요.” 그러고서 부인은 그날 아침에 있었던 일을 지주에게 죄다 이야기했다.\n\n“가엾은 것!” 지주가 말했다. “가엾은 것! 그 아이를 위해 우리가 무얼 해 줄 수 있겠소? 한동안 어디 다녀오는 것이 그 아이에게 좋지 않겠소? 곱고 착하고 사랑스러운 아이인데, 그런 일을 당할 아이가 아니오. 슬픔과 낙심이야 누구에게나 오는 법이지만, 이렇게 이른 나이에 오면 갑절로 무거운 법이지.”\n\n데일 부인은 그가 보이는 동정에 거의 놀랄 지경이었다.\n\n“그럼 그 사람이 받을 벌은 무엇인가요?” 부인이 물었다.\n\n“남자든 여자든 그를 두고 느낄 경멸이지요. 적어도 그 존중이나 경멸이 남에게 의미가 되는 사람들의 경멸 말이오. 나는 다른 벌은 알지 못하오. 릴리의 이름이 법정에 오르는 것을 바라지는 않으시겠지요?”\n\n“물론 그건 아니에요.”\n\n“그리고 버나드가 그에게 결투를 신청하게 두지도 않겠소. 사실 그래 봐야 아무 소용도 없소. 요즘 세상에는 남자가 결투를 하리라고 기대하는 사람이 없으니까.”\n\n“제가 그런 걸 바라리라고 생각하지는 않으시겠지요.”\n\n“그럼 무슨 벌이 있겠소? 나는 알지 못하오. 사람이 저지를 수 있는 악행 가운데는 아무도 벌할 수 없는 것들이 있소. 나는 알지 못하오. 나는 그를 뒤쫓아 런던까지 올라갔지만, 그는 용케 내 앞을 기어서 빠져나갔소. 쥐를 두고 무엇을 할 수 있겠소? 가까이 가지 않는 것 말고?”\n\n데일 부인은 마음속으로, 크로즈비가 온몸의 뼈마디가 다 쑤시도록 매를 맞을 수만 있다면 좋겠다고 느끼고 있었다. 그런 것이 여자가 품을 만한 생각이었는지 나는 잘 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그것이 부인의 생각이었다. 부인은 그가 결투를 하게 되기를 바라지는 않았다. 거기에는 사악한 것이 많고, 부인이 보기에 옳은 것은 하나도 없었다. 그러나 버나드가 그 겁쟁이를 겁쟁이답게 두들겨 준다면 조카를 지금까지보다 더 사랑하게 되리라고 느꼈다. 버나드 역시 제 사촌 누이를 차 버린 사내를 자기가 채찍질해 주기를 세상이 기대할지도 모른다고 생각해 보았고, 몸으로 지는 위험만 놓고 보자면 그 일을 떠맡는 데 못 이길 것도 없었다. 그러나 그런 일은 여러모로 그에게 달갑지 않았다. 클럽에서 소동이 벌어지는 것이 싫었다. 사촌 누이의 이름이 사람들 입에 오르내리지 않기를 무엇보다 바랐다. 지각없는 짓이 될 만한 일은 무엇이든 피하고 싶었다. 사악한 일이 벌어졌고, 그는 크로즈비가 미움받아 마땅한 만큼 크로즈비를 미워할 준비가 얼마든지 되어 있었다. 그러나 제 몫으로 말하자면, 얼마 전까지 제 벗이었던 사내를 자기가 벌해 주기를 세상이 아마도 기대하리라는 생각이 그를 우울하게 만들었다. 게다가 그는 그를 어디서 붙잡아야 할지도, 붙잡은들 어떻게 두들겨야 할지도 알지 못했다. 그는 사촌 누이가 몹시 안쓰러웠고, 크로즈비를 그냥 빠져나가게 두어서는 안 된다고 굳게 느꼈다. 그러나 그가 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가?\n\n“그 아이가 어디 다녀오고 싶어 하지 않겠소?” 지주가 다시 물었다. 무엇이든 너그러이 베풀어 위안을 줄 수 있다면 그러고 싶었던 것이다. 이 순간 그는, 그렇게 해서 조카딸에게 조금이라도 도움이 된다면 평생 연 백 파운드를 떼어 주었을 것이다.\n\n“집에 있는 편이 나을 거예요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “가엾은 것. 한동안은 밖에 나가는 걸 피하고 싶어 할 테니까요.”\n\n“그렇겠구려.” 그러고는 잠시 말이 끊겼다. “내 말해 두겠소, 제수씨. 나는 도무지 이해할 수가 없소. 정말이지 도무지 이해할 수가 없소. 나로서는 그 사람이 내 주머니에서 동전을 집어내는 것을 붙잡은 것만큼이나 기가 막힌 일이오. 내가 젊었을 적에는 신사라는 자리에 있는 사람이 그런 짓을 하리라고는 생각지도 못했소. 감히 그런 짓을 할 엄두를 낼 사람이 있으리라고도 생각지 못했소. 그런데 지금은 사람이 그런 식으로 굴어도 아무 탈이 없는 모양이오. 런던에 그의 친구라는 자가 있어 나를 찾아와서는, 세상에 흔히 있는 일상사인 양 그 일을 이야기합디다. 그래, 들어오너라, 버나드. 그 가엾은 아이도 이제 다 알고 있다.”\n\n버나드는 숙모에게 제가 건넬 수 있는 위로와 동정을 건넸고, 이 이리를 그들의 우리 안에 끌어들인 데 대해 반쯤 말이 되다 만 사과를 했다. “클럽에서는 다들 그를 아주 좋게 여겼습니다.” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“요즘 너희 런던 클럽이란 데는 나도 잘 모른다.” 숙부가 말했다. “저 사람이 이제 그런 짓을 하고서도 그와 어울리는 것이 참아 넘겨진다면, 알고 싶지도 않고.”\n\n“이 일을 알게 될 사람은 여섯 명도 안 될 겁니다.” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“흠!” 지주가 툴툴거렸다. 릴리의 이름이 그 일과 그토록 가까이 얽혀 있으니, 크로즈비의 악행이 널리 이야기되기를 바란다고 말할 수는 없었다. 그러나 그러면서도 크로즈비가 온 세상의 찌푸린 눈살로 벌받지 않아도 된다는 생각은 참아 넘길 수가 없었다. 그가 보기에는 이제부터 크로즈비에게 말을 거는 사람은 누구든 그렇게 함으로써 스스로 제 몸을 더럽히는 것이라 여겨야 마땅했다.\n\n“그 아이에게 내 사랑을 전해 주시오.” 데일 부인이 일어나 작별하려 하자 그가 말했다. “진심으로 내 사랑을 전해 주시오. 늙은 삼촌이 그 아이를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 일이 있다면 알려만 달라고 하시오. 그 아이는 내 집에서 그 사내를 만났소. 그러니 내가 그 아이에게 빚진 것이 많다고 느끼오. 나를 보러 오라고 하시오. 집에서 시름에 잠겨 있는 것보다 나을 게요. 그리고 제수씨”—이 말은 그녀의 귀에 대고 속삭였다—“벨에 대해 내가 한 말도 생각해 보시오.”\n\n데일 부인은 제 집으로 걸어 돌아오면서, 시아주버니의 태도가 이제껏 자기가 알아 온 그 어떤 모습과도 다르다는 것을 스스로 인정했다.\n\n그날 하루 내내 작은 집에서는 크로즈비의 이름이 입에 오르지 않았다. 두 아가씨는 아무도 밖에 나가지 않았고, 벨은 오후의 태반을 소파에 앉아 동생의 허리에 팔을 두른 채로 보냈다. 각자 책을 한 권씩 들고 있었으나, 오간 말이 적었던 만큼이나 읽은 것도 적었다. 크로즈비와 함께 보낸 시간들을, 그의 따뜻한 사랑의 다짐을, 그가 건네고 자기가 받아들인 애무를, 그의 애정 앞에서 거리낌 없이 스스로 인정하던 제 기쁨을 떠올릴 때 릴리의 마음속을 지나간 생각들을 누가 그려 낼 수 있을까? 그때는 그 모든 것이 릴리에게 거룩했다. 그런데 이제 그때 성스러웠던 것들이 그의 잘못으로 거의 부끄러운 것이 되고 말았다. 그러면서도 릴리는 그것을 생각하며 몇 번이고 스스로에게 다짐했다. 나는 그이를 용서하겠다고—아니, 이미 용서했다고. “그리고 그이도 그걸 알게 될 거야.” 릴리는 거의 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n“릴리, 우리 릴리.” 벨이 말했다. “할 수 있거든 잠시라도 그 생각에서 마음을 돌려 보렴.”\n\n“돌려지지가 않아.” 릴리가 말했다. 그날 두 사람 사이에 그 일을 두고 오간 말은 그것이 전부였다.\n\n온 세상이 다 알게 되겠지! 그런 처지에 놓인 아가씨가 마셔야 하는 잔 속에서 가장 쓴 방울 가운데 하나가 바로 이것이 아닐까 싶다. 릴리는 그날 이른 시각에 이미, 응접실 하녀가 자기가 버림받았다는 것을 잘 알고 있음을 알아차렸다. 그 아이의 태도는 동정을 전하려는 것이었으나, 실제로 전해 온 것은 가엾어하는 마음이었다. 릴리는 한순간 화가 났다. 그러나 그럴 수밖에 없다는 생각이 들어, 그 아이에게 미소를 지어 보이고 다정하게 말을 건넸다. 그런들 무슨 상관인가? 하루 이틀이면 온 세상이 다 알 텐데.\n\n이튿날 릴리는 어머니의 권유로 삼촌을 보러 올라갔다.\n\n“얘야.” 그가 말했다. “네 일이 참 안됐구나. 너 때문에 내 가슴이 미어진다.”\n\n“삼촌.” 릴리가 말했다. “마음 쓰지 마세요. 저를 위해 이것 하나만 해 주세요—그 얘기는 하지 말아 주세요. 제 말은, 저한테는요.”\n\n“그래, 그래, 하지 않으마. 내 집 안에 그런 몹쓸 악당이 다 있었다니—”\n\n“삼촌! 삼촌! 그건 안 돼요! 저는 그 어떤 사람한테서든 그이를 헐뜯는 말은 한마디도 듣지 않겠어요—한마디도요! 그것만은 기억해 주세요!” 그렇게 말하는 릴리의 눈이 번뜩였다.\n\n그는 릴리에게 대답하지 않고 그 손을 잡아 꼭 쥐었고, 그러고서 릴리는 그를 떠났다. “데일 집안사람은 언제나 한결같았지!” 그는 집 앞 테라스를 오르내리며 혼잣말을 했다. “언제나 한결같았어!”\n\n# 제31장. 상처 입은 새끼 사슴\n\n거의 두 달이 흘러, 이제 앨링턴에도 크리스마스철이 찾아왔다. 두 집 어느 쪽에서도 흥겨움을 크게 떠들 뜻은 없었으리라고 짐작할 만하다. 릴리 데일이 받은 것과 같은 상처는 빨리 아물 수 있는 것이 아니었고, 온 식구가 자기들 위에 얹힌 무게 때문에 유쾌하게 지내기란 어렵다고 느끼고 있었다. 릴리 자신으로 말하자면, 그 불행을 여자로서 낼 수 있는 온갖 용기로 견뎌 냈다고 해도 좋겠다. 첫 한 주 동안 그녀는 바람 앞에 버티고 선 나무처럼 꼿꼿이 서 있었다. 휘려 들지 않기에 머지않아 산산조각이 나고 마는 그런 나무처럼. 그 한 주 동안 어머니와 언니는 그녀의 침착함과 인내에 오히려 겁을 먹었다. 그녀는 날마다 할 일을 해냈다. 마을을 지나 밖으로 나갔고, 첫 일요일에는 교회의 제자리에 나와 앉았다. 저녁이면 눈물을 삼키며 책을 펴 놓고 앉아 있었다. 그리고 어머니와 언니가 근심 어린 눈으로 자기를 바라보고 있는 것을 알아채면 나무라곤 했다.\n\n“엄마, 그 일은 전부 없었던 일인 셈 쳐요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“아, 얘야! 그럴 수만 있다면 얼마나 좋겠니!”\n\n“속으로야 그럴 수 있어서는 안 되죠.” 릴리가 대답했다. “하지만 겉으로는 그럴 수 있어요. 엄마가 예전보다 저를 더 다정하게 대하시는 게 느껴지는데, 그게 저를 흔들어 놔요. 게으르다고 저를 꾸짖어만 주셔도 저는 금세 나아질 거예요.” 하지만 어머니는, 그 귀여운 딸에게 아무런 슬픔도 닥치지 않았더라면 했을 법한 말투로 딸에게 말할 수가 없었다. 근심 어린 다정한 눈길을 거둘 수가 없었고, 릴리는 그 눈길 때문에 자기가 거의 죽을 만큼 상처 입은 새끼 사슴으로 보이고 있다는 것을 알았다.\n\n첫 주가 끝날 무렵 그녀는 무너졌다. “나 안 일어날래, 언니.” 어느 날 아침 그녀는 거의 짜증을 내듯 말했다. “아파. 그냥 여기 누워서 방해 안 되게 있는 게 나아. 소란 피우지 마. 내가 멍청하고 어리석어서, 그래서 아픈 거야.”\n\n그 말에 데일 부인과 벨은 겁이 나서 서로의 넋 나간 얼굴을 마주 보았다. 사랑이 불행하게 끝난 탓에 가슴이 찢어져 죽어 버린 가여운 처녀들의 이야기가 떠올랐던 것이다. 바람 한 자락만 너무 세게 불어도 불꽃이 꺼져 버리는 작은 밀랍 초처럼 말이다. 그러나 릴리는 실상 그런 가냘픈 초가 아니었다. 휘려 들지 않기에 부러지고 마는 그런 줄기도 아니었다. 그녀는 앓아누운 그 한 주 동안 몰아치는 바람에 몸을 굽혔고, 그러고는 여전히 곧고 우아한 자태로 일어섰다. 그 밝은 불빛은 꺼지지 않은 채였다.\n\n그 뒤로 그녀는 자기가 잃은 것에 대해 어머니에게 더 터놓고 이야기했다. 터놓고, 그리고 자기에게 닥친 불행을 제대로 알아본 채로. 하지만 가슴이 찢어진다느니 하는 생각을 비웃는 듯한 굳센 자신감을 함께 지니고서였다. “저는 견딜 수 있어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “게다가 오래가는 불행 없이 견딜 수 있어요. 물론 저는 늘 그이를 사랑하겠죠. 엄마가 아버지를 잃으셨을 때 느끼셨던 것과 거의 같은 심정일 거예요.”\n\n이 말에 데일 부인은 아무 대답도 할 수 없었다. 크로즈비에 대한 자기 생각을 털어놓고서 그자는 네 사랑을 받을 자격이 없다고 릴리에게 설명해 줄 수는 없었다. 사랑은 값어치를 따라가지 않으며, 훌륭함에 주어지는 것도 아니다. 또한 모질게 대한다고 부서지지도 않고, 매질과 난도질로도 죽지 않는다. 자기를 그토록 모질게 대한 남자를 아직도 사랑한다고 릴리가 선언할 때면 데일 부인은 잠자코 있었다. 둘은 서로를 완전히 이해하고 있었지만, 그 문제에 관해서만은 그 두 사람조차 마음을 자유로이 주고받을 수가 없었다.\n\n“저한테 절대 싫증 내지 않겠다고 약속하셔야 해요, 엄마.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“어미가 제 자식한테 싫증 내는 일은 흔치 않단다. 자식이 제 어미한테 무슨 짓을 하든 말이다.”\n\n“자식이 노처녀가 되고 나면 그건 잘 모르겠는데요. 그리고 저도 제 고집을 부릴 작정이에요, 엄마. 될 수만 있다면 제 방식대로 살 작정이고요. 언니가 시집가고 나면 저는 이 집을 동업으로 여길 거예요. 그러니 시키는 대로만 하지는 않을 거예요.”\n\n“미리 일러 주었으니 미리 대비를 해 두마.”\n\n“바로 그거예요. 엄마를 느닷없이 놀라게 하고 싶지는 않으니까요. 언니가 갈 때까지 한두 해 더는 고분고분할 작정이에요. 하지만 처녀가 평생을 고분고분하기만 하다면 그건 아주 멍청한 짓이겠죠.”\n\n데일 부인은 이 모든 말을 속속들이 알아들었다. 그것은 릴리 쪽에서 하는 선언이나 다름없었다. 자기는 한 번 사랑했고 다시는 사랑할 수 없다는 것, 다른 처녀들이 그러하듯 남편이라는 상을 타 볼까 하는 마음으로 판에 뛰어들었으나 지고 말았으니 다시는 그 판에 끼어들 수 없다는 것 말이다. 릴리로 하여금 어머니에게 그런 말을 하게 만든 것은 바로 그 속마음의 확신이었다. 그러나 데일 부인은 그 확신을 함께 나눌 마음이 조금도 없었다. 세월이 릴리의 상처를 아물게 할 것이며 제 아이도 언젠가는 행복한 혼인의 축복으로 관을 쓰게 되리라고 그녀는 스스로에게 다짐했다. 릴리의 한평생 운명이 이미 정해진 것으로 여겨져야 한다는 그런 계획에 그녀는 마음속으로 결코 동의하지 않았다. 그녀는 구혼자로서의 크로즈비를 애초에 진심으로 좋아한 적이 없었고, 풋내기 시절의 온갖 허물을 머리에 인 존 임스 쪽을 오히려 더 마음에 두었을 사람이었다. 존 임스의 사랑이 행복해지는 일이 아직 일어날 수도 있는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그러는 동안에도 릴리는, 내가 말했듯이, 용기 안에서 굳세어졌고, 남들보다 더 불행해졌으니 그만큼 더 게을러도 된다는 식의 나른한 자기 위안 없이 살아가는 일을 다시 시작했다. 아침저녁으로 그녀는 그를 위해 기도했고, 날마다, 거의 시간마다, 그를 사랑하는 것이 여전히 자기 본분이라고 스스로에게 다짐했다. 그 사랑을 드러낼 힘이라고는 조금도 없는 채로 사랑해야 하는 이 본분은 고된 것이었다. 그래도 그녀는 제 본분을 다하려 했다.\n\n“곧바로 말씀해 주세요, 엄마.” 어느 날 아침 그녀가 말했다. “그이의 혼인 날짜가 정해졌다는 소식을 들으시거든요. 제발 저만 모르게 두지는 마세요.”\n\n“이월이라는구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그래도 날짜는 알려 주세요. 저에게 그날이 여느 날 같아서는 안 되니까요. 하지만 그런 슬픈 얼굴은 하지 마세요, 엄마. 저는 바보짓 같은 건 하지 않을 거예요. 몰래 빠져나가 유령처럼 그 교회에 나타나거나 하지도 않을 거고요.” 그러고는 그 작은 농담을 뱉고 나자 흐느낌이 올라왔고, 그녀는 어머니의 가슴에 얼굴을 묻었다. 이내 다시 얼굴을 들었다. “정말이에요, 엄마. 저는 불행하지 않아요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그 둘째 주가 지나고 나서 데일 부인은 크로즈비에게 정말로 편지를 한 통 썼다.\n\n제 생각에는 [그녀는 이렇게 썼다] 귀하의 편지를 받았음을 알려 드리는 것이 도리에 맞는 일이겠지요. 그 밖에 달리 드릴 말씀이 있는지는 모르겠습니다. 귀하의 처신을 두고 제가 어떻게 생각하는지를 말하는 것은 여자로서 온당한 일이 아니겠으나, 귀하의 양심이 똑같은 말을 들려주리라 믿습니다. 만일 그렇지 않다면 귀하는 정말이지 무디어질 대로 무디어진 사람일 것입니다. 저는 제 아이에게, 그 아이가 보내는 말을 귀하에게 전해 주겠다고 약속했습니다. 그 아이는 자기가 귀하를 용서했으며 귀하를 미워하지 않는다고 전해 달라 합니다. 하느님께서도 귀하를 용서하시고, 귀하가 그분의 사랑을 되찾기를 빕니다.\n\n이 편지에 대해서는 저에게든 제 식구 누구에게든 답장을 보내지 마시기를 부탁드립니다.\n\n지주는 자기가 받은 편지에 아무 답장도 쓰지 않았고, 크로즈비를 당장 응징하기 위한 어떤 조치도 취하지 않았다. 사실 그는 그런 조치란 취할 수 있는 것이 아니라고 잘라 말하면서, 그런 인간은 쥐를 다루듯 다루는 수밖에 없다고 조카에게 설명해 두었던 것이다.\n\n“나는 그자를 다시 볼 일이 없을 게다.” 그가 또 한 번 말했다. “만일 마주친다면 지팡이로 그자의 머리를 갈기는 데 망설이지 않겠지. 하지만 그런 목적으로 그자를 쫓아다닌다면 나 자신을 못나게 여기게 될 거야.”\n\n그러면서도 자기와 자기 식구를 그토록 짓밟은 그 악당이 아무 벌도 받지 않고 빠져나간다는 것은 노인에게 지독한 슬픔이었다. 그는 크로즈비를 용서하지 않았다. 용서라는 생각은 한 번도 그의 머리를 스치지 않았다. 그런 짓을 용서하도록 자기가 이끌릴 수 있다고 여겼다면 그는 스스로를 미워했을 것이다. “거기에는 내가 도무지 이해할 수 없는 만큼의 악당 근성이 있어. 천하고 비열한 근성 말이다.” 그는 조카에게 몇 번이고 되풀이해 말하곤 했다. 그러고는 테라스를 혼자 거닐면서, 버나드가 사촌이 당한 일을 갚아 주려고 무슨 조치를 취할 것인지 마음속으로 헤아려 보곤 했다. “저 애가 옳지.” 그는 혼자 중얼거리곤 했다. “버나드가 아주 옳아. 하지만 내가 젊었을 때라면 나는 그걸 참아 내지 못했을 게다. 그 시절에는 우리를 대접한 그런 식으로 자기를 대접한 놈을 신사가 결투장으로 불러낼 수 있었지. 사람이란 무언가 했다는 느낌만으로도 속이 풀렸던 게야. 요즘 세상은 다른 모양이지.” 세상은 다르다. 그러나 지주는 무언가 나아진 것이 있다고는 마음속으로 결코 인정하지 않았다.\n\n버나드 또한 마음이 몹시 괴로웠다. 요즘 세상에 결투가 가능하기만 했다면 그는 크로즈비와 한 판 붙는 데 아무 이의도 없었을 것이다. 그러나 결투란 이제 가능하지 않다고, 적어도 웃음거리가 되지 않고서는 불가능하다고 그는 믿었다. 그리고 그자와 싸울 수 없다면 달리 무슨 방법으로 그자를 벌한단 말인가? 그런 잘못에 대해서는 세상이 아무 벌도 마련해 두지 않았다는 것이 사실 아닌가? 크로즈비 같은 사내가 한 처녀의 한평생 행복을 밑천 삼아 한두 주 재미를 보고서도 제 몸에는 아무 나쁜 결과도 없이 말끔히 빠져나가는 것이, 바로 그런 사내의 힘 안에 있지 않은가? “나는 그자와 마주칠까 봐 내 클럽에도 못 나가게 될 텐데,” 버나드는 혼자 생각했다. “그자는 못 나갈 일이 없겠지.” 게다가 그의 속에는 이 일이 오히려 크로즈비에게 자랑거리가 되리라는 느낌이 있었다. 릴리 데일 같은 처녀와 연애하는 즐거움을 실컷 챙기고서도 그런 재미에 흔히 따라붙는 대가를 치르지 않았으니, 많은 이들이 그를 두고 크게 부러워할 만하다 여기리라는 것이었다. 그자는 데일 집안사람 모두에게 죄를 지었는데, 그 죄에서 나온 고통은 오로지 데일 집안사람들에게만 떨어지게 되어 있었다. 버나드가 이 일 전체를 꽤나 서글프게 헤아리며 내린 결론은 그러했다. 복수하고 싶으나 어디서 복수를 찾아야 할지 모르는 채로 말이다. 나로서는, 크로즈비의 배신이 크로즈비의 벗들에게 어떻게 비칠 것인가 하는 점에 대해 그가 전적으로 틀렸다고 믿는다. 사람들은 여전히 그런 일을 가볍게 입에 올리며, 사랑에서든 전쟁에서든 무슨 수를 써도 괜찮다고 늘어놓고, 노련한 사기꾼의 행운을 두고 거의 부러워하듯 이야기할 것이다. 그러나 나는 어떤 한 사람의 실제 일을 두고 그런 식으로 생각하는 사내를 여태 만나 본 적이 없다. 자기가 한 짓이 자기에게 어떤 결과를 가져올지에 대해서는 크로즈비 자신의 판단이 버나드 데일의 판단보다 더 정확했다. 그는 그 짓을 아직 하기 전에는, 아직 저지르지 않고 내버려 둘 힘이 자기에게 있는 동안에는 그것을 가벼운 죄로 여겼다. 그러나 그것을 저지른 순간부터 그 일은 제 본모습 그대로 그의 눈앞에 밀어닥쳤다. 그는 자기가 악당이었음을 알았고, 다른 사람들도 자기를 그렇게 여기리라는 것을 알았다. 여자를 그저 장난감으로 여긴다는 평판을 듣던 그의 벗 파울러 프랫조차 그를 그렇게 보았다. 자기가 한 일을 자랑하기는커녕, 그는 훔친 물건 이야기를 꺼리는 사람처럼 자기 혼인에 얽힌 어떤 이야기든 입에 올리기를 두려워했다. 클럽의 사내들이 자기를 곁눈질로 본다는 것을 그는 이미 느끼고 있었다. 그리고 제 살가죽과 뼈에 관해서는 겁쟁이가 아니었으나, 언젠가 지팡이를 단단히 챙겨 든 버나드 데일과 맞닥뜨릴지 모른다는 형체 없는 두려움이 있었다. 크로즈비가 벌을 받지 않고 있다고 여긴 점에서 지주와 그 조카는 틀렸던 것이다.\n\n그리고 겨울이 다가오면서 그는 드 코시 백작 집안이 자기를 바싹 지켜보고 있음을 느꼈다. 그 고귀한 집안 사람들 가운데 몇몇은 이미 진심으로 미워하는 법을 배운 터였다. 아너러블 존이 십일월에 런던으로 올라와 그를 지긋지긋하게 괴롭혔다. 세브라이트에서 만찬을 사라고 우겨 대고, 장차 매부가 될 사람의 방에서 오후 내내 담배를 피워 대고, 장차 매부가 될 사람의 물건을 빌려 갔다. 마침내 크로즈비는 아너러블 존과 사이를 틀어 놓는 편이 현명하겠다고 마음먹었고, 그리하여 실제로 사이를 틀어 버렸다. 그를 제 방에서 내쫓고, 이제 다시는 상대하지 않겠다고 대놓고 말해 버린 것이다.\n\n“나처럼 자네도 그렇게 해야 할 걸세.” 모티머 게이지비가 그에게 말한 적이 있었다. “나는 집안을 생각해서 내키지 않았네만, 레이디 어밀리아가 그래야 한다고 하더군.” 그리하여 크로즈비는 모티머 게이지비의 충고를 따랐다.\n\n그러나 게이지비 부부의 환대는 아너러블 존의 성가신 조름보다도 어쩌면 그를 더 괴롭게 했다. 장차 처형이 될 사람은 그를 가만히 내버려 두지 않기로 작정한 듯했다. 일요일 오후마다 모티머가 그를 데리러 왔고, 그는 제 굳은 뜻을 다해 버티는데도 세인트 존스 우드의 별장으로 가지 않을 수 없다는 것을 깨달았다. 자기 처지의 사정을 스스로도 온전히 따져 볼 수는 없었으나, 그는 며느리발톱이 잘린 수탉 같은, 이빨을 뽑힌 개 같은 기분이었다. 자기가 고분고분해지고 순해지고 있음을 알아차렸다. 자기가 레이디 어밀리아를 두려워하고 있으며 심지어 모티머 게이지비마저 두려워하다시피 하고 있음을 스스로 인정해야 했다. 그들이 자기를 지켜보고 있고 자기가 드나드는 것을 죄다 알고 있다는 것도 알았다. 그들은 그를 어돌퍼스라 부르며 길들였다. 다가오는 이월의 그 날은 그의 귀에 못이 박이도록 울려 댔다. 레이디 어밀리아는 그를 위해 가구를 보러 가겠다고 했고, 침구며 시트 이야기를 몇 시간이고 늘어놓았다. “부엌 세간은 톰킨스에서 장만하는 편이 나을 거요. 물건이 다 좋고, 현금으로 치르면 일 할을 깎아 준다오. 물론 그대야 현금으로 치르겠지요, 안 그렇소!” 그가 릴리 데일을 희생시킨 것이 이것을 위해서였던가? 드 코시라는 고귀한 집안과 손을 잡은 것이 이것을 위해서였던가?\n\n모티머는 그가 런던으로 돌아온 바로 그 순간부터 재산 약정 문제로 그를 물고 늘어졌고, 이미 그를 손발까지 꽁꽁 묶어 놓았다. 그의 생명보험이 들어졌고, 증권은 모티머의 손에 있었다. 그의 얼마 안 되는 재산도 레이디 알렉산드리나의 얼마 안 되는 재산과 함께 묶어 두도록 이미 넘어가 있었다. 마련된 모든 조치를 보자면, 그가 어서 죽어 없어지고 레이디 알렉산드리나에게는 세인트 존스 우드에서 살기에 넉넉한 아담한 수입이 마련되도록 하려는 심산인 듯했다. 그가 제 돈에서 나오는 몫도 그녀의 돈에서 나오는 몫도 쓸 수 없게끔 일이 짜여 있었다. 그 돈은 모티머 게이지비의 알뜰한 손길 아래 보험료로 들어가게 되어 있었다. 그가 혼인한 다음 날 죽어만 준다면 알렉산드리나에게는 정말이지 아주 짭짤한 돈이, 백작의 딸이 받아들이기에 거의 걸맞은 액수가 남을 것이었다. 여섯 달 전이라면 그는 자기와 게이지비 사이에 오갈 수 있는 어떤 문제에 대해서든 게이지비를 제 손가락으로 돌려 세울 수 있다고 여겼을 것이다. 어쩌다 마주치면 게이지비는 그에게 아주 굽실거리며 그를 저보다 윗길인 존재처럼 대했다. 그는 아주 높은 데서 게이지비를 내려다보았다. 그러나 이제는 이 사내의 손아귀에서 자기가 무력한 것처럼 보였다.\n\n그러나 어쩌면 백작부인이야말로 그가 가장 싫어하는 사람이 되었는지도 몰랐다. 그녀는 끊임없이 그에게 짧은 쪽지를 써 보내 수십 가지 심부름을 시켰고, 그를 마치 제 하인처럼 이리저리 부려 먹었다. 게다가 그녀는 심부름보다도 더 고약한 충고로 그를 들볶았다. 알렉산드리나가 어떤 격의 살림을 기대하고 있는지 일러 주었고, 그 같은 자가 그토록 고귀한 집안의 품 안에 받아들여지면서 그 값 매길 수 없는 특권에 대해 아주 비싼 값을 치르지 않기를 바랄 수는 없다고 몇 번이고 일깨웠다. 그녀의 편지는 그에게 역겨운 것이 되어, 그는 그것을 한쪽으로 홱 던져 두고 하루 종일 뜯어보지도 않곤 했다. 그는 혼인하고 나면 곧 백작부인과도 사이를 틀어야겠다고 이미 마음먹고 있었다. 아니, 될 수만 있다면 그 집안 전체와 갈라서겠다고 마음먹었다. 그런데도 그가 이 약혼에 뛰어든 것은 무엇보다 드 코시 집안과 연을 맺음으로써 자기에게 돌아올 이익을 거두어들이려는 속셈에서였던 것이다! 지주와 그 조카는 이 사내가 벌도 받지 않고 빠져나가고 있다고 생각하며 비참해했지만, 그런 괴로움은 겪지 않아도 좋았을 것이다.\n\n그가 크리스마스를 코시 성에서 보내기로 되어 있다는 것은 처음부터 정해진 일이었다. 이 약속에서 몸을 빼내기란 도저히 그의 힘으로 될 일이 아니었다. 다만 그는 머무는 기간을 되도록 짧게 하기로 마음먹었다. 공교롭게도 크리스마스가 월요일이었고, 일반위원회 사무국에서 토요일이란 사실상 근무하지 않는 날, 곧 디에스 논이나 다름없다는 것은 드 코시 집안 사람들도 다 아는 바였다. 그 사흘에 관해서는 그에게 빠져나갈 구멍이 없었다. 그러나 그는 세 분 위원께서 그 사흘을 넘겨 늘리는 문제에 관한 한 쇠붙이 같은 분들이라고 알렉산드리나에게 일러 두었다. “물론 이월에도 다시 자리를 비워야 하잖소.” 그가 말했는데, 그 말투에서는 신음 소리가 거의 들릴 지경이었다. “그러니 지금 월요일을 넘겨 머무는 것은 도저히 될 수가 없소.” 코시 성에 그를 끌어당길 만한 것이 있었더라면 그는 옵티미스트 씨와 이야기해 일주일이나 열흘쯤은 어렵잖게 마련했으리라고 나는 생각한다. “우리끼리만 있게 될 거요.” 백작부인이 그에게 써 보냈다. “그러니 그대도 우리 집안의 방식을 여태 알아 온 것보다 더 깊이 배울 기회를 얻게 되기를 바라오.” 이것은 그에게 쓸개즙처럼 쓴 말이었다. 그러나 이 세상에서 값나가는 물건에는 다 값이 붙어 있는 법이다. 그러니 크로즈비 같은 사내들이 고귀한 집안과 인연 맺기를 바랄 때에는 자기가 사들이는 그 물건에 매겨진 장터 값을 치러야 하는 것이다.\n\n“월요일에는 다들 올라와서 우리와 함께 저녁을 드시오.” 그 전 주 중반쯤 지주가 데일 부인에게 말했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 아무래도 그만두는 게 좋겠어요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “저희는 이대로 지내는 편이 나을지도 모르겠어요.”\n\n이 무렵 지주와 그 제수는 여느 때보다 훨씬 사이가 좋았고, 그는 그녀의 마음을 헤아려 그 대답을 좋게 받아들였다. 그래서 그는 청을 거듭 밀어붙였고, 뜻을 이루었다.\n\n“내 보기엔 제수씨가 틀렸소.” 그가 말했다. “우리가 아주 즐거운 크리스마스를 보내리라고는 나도 생각지 않소. 제수씨와 아이들이야 여기서 푸딩을 먹든 큰 집에서 먹든 즐겁기는 어렵겠지. 하지만 그래도 다 같이 애를 써 보는 편이 우리 모두에게 나을 거요. 그게 옳은 일이오. 나는 그렇게 보오.”\n\n“릴리에게 물어보지요.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그러시오, 그러시오. 그 애에게 내 사랑을 전하고, 그동안 오고 간 온갖 일에도 불구하고 크리스마스는 그 애에게 여전히 기뻐하는 날이어야 한다고 내 말로 일러 주시오. 하인들이 오후를 쉴 수 있도록 세 시쯤에 저녁을 들기로 합시다.”\n\n“당연히 가야죠.” 릴리가 말했다. “안 갈 까닭이 어디 있어요? 우리는 늘 갔잖아요. 그리고 삼촌이 보이스네를 불러 주시면 작년처럼 보이스네 식구 전부와 눈 가리고 술래잡기를 할 거예요.” 그러나 그때만은 보이스네가 불려 오지 않았다.\n\n그러나 릴리는 그 모든 일에 그토록 씩씩한 얼굴을 지어 보였어도 견뎌야 할 것이 많았고, 사실 몹시 괴로워했다. 나의 독자여, 그대가 언젠가 비 오는 날 도랑에 미끄러져 빠져 본 적이 있다면, 곁에 선 사람들의 동정이야말로 그 낭패 가운데 단연 가장 견디기 힘든 대목이 아니었던가? 사람들이 그냥 지나쳐 걸어가고 그대를 쳐다보지만 않는다면 아직 다 괜찮을 수 있으련만, 하고 스스로에게 되뇌지 않았던가? 그러면서도 걸음을 멈추고 그대를 딱하게 여긴 사람들을, 또 어쩌면 그대를 일으켜 주며 흙투성이가 된 그대의 모자를 되찾도록 거들어 준 사람들을 그대는 탓할 수 없다. 그대 자신도 누가 넘어지는 것을 보면 그에게 별일도 없었다는 듯 그냥 지나쳐 갈 수는 없는 것이다. 릴리에게도 그러했다. 앨링턴 사람들은 그녀를 여느 때의 눈으로 볼 수 없었다. 그들은 그녀가 상처 입은 새끼 사슴임을 알기에 다정하게 바라보았고, 그리하여 그 상처의 쓰라림을 덧들였다. 늙은 헌 부인은 그녀를 위로하며, 아마 이렇게 된 편이 오히려 잘된 일일 거라고 말했다. 릴리는 그 일을 두고 어떤 식으로도 거짓말을 하려 들지 않았다. “헌 부인,” 그녀가 말했다. “그 이야기는 저에게 괴로워요.” 헌 부인은 그에 대해 더는 말하지 않았으나, 두 사람이 마주칠 때마다 입 밖에 내지 않은 것들을 눈빛으로 말했다. “릴리 아가씨!” 어느 날 홉킨스가 말했다. “릴리 아가씨!” 그러면서 그녀의 얼굴을 올려다보는 그의 늙은 눈에 눈물이 맺힐 뻔했다. “저는 처음부터 그자가 어떤 인간인지 알고 있었습니다요. 아이고, 저런! 아이고, 저런! 제가 그자를 죽여 버릴 수만 있었어도!” “홉킨스, 어찌 그런 말을 해요?” 릴리가 말했다. “나한테 또 그런 식으로 말하면 삼촌께 이를 거예요.” 그녀는 그에게서 돌아섰다. 그러나 곧바로 다시 돌아서서 그에게 작은 손을 내밀었다. “미안해요.” 그녀가 말했다. “홉킨스가 얼마나 마음을 써 주는지 알아요. 그래서 나는 홉킨스가 좋아요.” 그러고는 가 버렸다. “그래도 내 언젠가는 그자를 쫓아가 그 더러운 목을 부러뜨려 놓고 말 테다.” 홉킨스는 길을 내려가며 혼자 중얼거렸다.\n\n크리스마스를 얼마 앞두고 그녀는 언니와 함께 목사관을 찾아갔다. 벨은 찾아가 있는 동안 보이스네 딸 하나와 함께 그해의 마지막 국화를 보러 방을 나갔다. 그러자 보이스 부인은 그 틈을 타 짤막한 말을 꺼냈다. “릴리 양,” 그녀가 말했다. “내가 한마디도 하지 않으면 아가씨는 나를 매정하다 여기겠지요.” “아니에요, 그러지 않을 거예요.” 릴리는 제 상처를 건드리려 드는 손가락을 피하듯 몸을 움츠리며 거의 쏘아붙이듯 말했다. “아예 입에 올리지 않아야 할 일도 있는 법이니까요.” “그래요, 그래요. 아마 그렇겠지요.” 보이스 부인이 말했다. 그러나 한두 분 동안 그녀는 다른 화제로 옮겨 갈 수가 없어, 앉은 채로 아프도록 다정한 눈길로 릴리를 바라보았다. 그런 눈길 아래에서 릴리가 무엇을 견뎠는지는 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠다. 그러나 그녀는 견뎠고, 그 괴로움 속에서도 잘못이 보이스 부인에게 있지 않음을 스스로 인정했다. 보이스 부인인들 다정한 눈길 말고 달리 어떻게 그녀를 바라볼 수 있었겠는가?\n\n그리하여 릴리가 크리스마스 날 큰 집에 올라가 저녁을 들고, 그로써 자기가 불행이 깊어 세상에서 밀려난 사람으로 여겨질 사람이 아님을 앨링턴 온 동네에 보여 주기로 정해졌다. 그녀가 옳았다는 데에는 아무 의심도 있을 수 없다고 나는 생각한다. 그러나 교회에서 돌아온 뒤 어머니와 언니와 함께 그 작은 다리를 건너 걸어가면서, 그녀는 몸을 돌려 삼촌의 만찬 자리가 아니라 잠자리로 갈 수만 있다면 무엇이든 내주었으리라.\n\n# 제32장. 저민 스트리트의 포킨스\n\n올해 런던의 살진 가축 품평회는 십이월 이십일에 열렸다. 그리고 내가 늘 들어 온 바로는, 드 게스트 경이 내놓은 어떤 수소가 수도의 푸주한들에게서 혈통과 사육과 상태에 있어 있을 수 있는 온갖 훌륭함을 다 이루어 냈다는 판정을 받았다고 한다. 다음 반세기의 푸주한들이 훨씬 더 나은 것을 배워 알게 되리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없으니, 그 게스트윅의 짐승을 방부 처리해 두었다가 그때 내놓는다면 지금 시대의 농사 무지에 대한 비웃음이나 살 것이다. 그러나 드 게스트 경은 제게 바쳐진 찬사를 그대로 받아들이며 일곱째 하늘에 오른 듯한 기쁨에 잠겨 있었다. 그는 제 한평생의 일을 알아볼 줄 알고 저를 귀족의 본보기로 여겨 주는 푸주한과 목축업자와 가축 중개상들에게 둘러싸여 있을 때만큼 행복한 적이 없었다. “저 녀석을 좀 보게.” 그는 우승한 수소를 가리키며 임스에게 말했다. 임스는 관청 일이 끝난 뒤 품평회장에서 제 후원자와 합류하여 가스등 불빛 아래 살아 있는 쇠고기를 구경하고 있던 참이었다. “제 아비를 꼭 닮지 않았나? 램킨이 씨를 붙인 놈일세.”\n\n“램킨 말씀입니까.” 조니가 말했다. 그는 아직 게스트윅의 가축에 대해 그리 많이 알게 되지 못한 터였다.\n\n“그래, 램킨. 우리가 그 소동을 벌였던 그 황소 말일세. 이 녀석은 제 아비의 등판과 앞다리를 그대로 물려받았어. 안 보이나?”\n\n“그렇겠습니다.” 조니는 그렇게 말하며 뚫어져라 들여다보았으나 도무지 보이지 않았다.\n\n“참 이상한 일이야.” 백작이 외쳤다. “그런데 말일세, 저 황소가 그날 이후로는 어찌나 얌전한지,—무엇에 견주어도 얌전하다니까. 아무래도 내 손수건 탓이었던 게지.”\n\n“그러셨을 겁니다.” 조니가 말했다. “—아니면 파리 때문이었는지도 모르지요.”\n\n“파리라니!” 백작이 성난 목소리로 말했다. “저 녀석이 파리에 익숙지 않을 것 같은가? 자, 가세. 만찬을 일곱 시로 시켜 두었는데 지금 여섯 시가 지났네. 내 매부 데일 대령이 서울에 올라와 있어서 우리와 함께 만찬을 드네.” 그러고서 그는 조니의 팔을 붙들고 품평회장을 가로질러 그를 데리고 나갔는데, 가는 길에 제 것만 못한 짐승 여러 마리를 가리키며 눈여겨보게 했다.\n\n그러고서 그들은 포트먼 스퀘어와 그로브너 스퀘어를 지나 피커딜리를 건너 저민 스트리트까지 걸어 내려갔다. 존 임스는 이렇게 거리를 지나가는 동안 백작 한 분이 제 팔에 기대고 있다는 것이 참 묘한 일이라고 스스로 인정했다. 집에서, 제 일상의 삶에서 날마다 어울리는 이들은 크래들과 어밀리아 로퍼, 루펙스 부인과 로퍼 부인이었다. 그 차이는 대단히 컸으나, 그럼에도 그는 백작과 이야기하는 것이 루펙스 부인과 이야기하는 것만큼이나 수월하다는 것을 알았다.\n\n“자네야 물론 앨링턴의 데일 집안 사람들을 알고 있겠지.” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“예, 잘 알고 있습니다.”\n\n“하지만 대령은 만나 본 적이 없을 테지.”\n\n“뵌 적이 없는 듯합니다.”\n\n“좀 별난 사람이야.—제 나름으로야 나무랄 데 없지만 도무지 아무것도 하지 않는다네. 그와 내 누이는 토키에 사는데, 내가 알아낸 바로는 둘 다 무슨 일거리라고 할 만한 것이 하나도 없어. 지금 서울에 올라온 것은 우리 둘이 집안 변호사들을 만나 서류에 서명을 해야 했기 때문인데, 그 사람은 그 여행을 아주 큰 고역으로 여긴다네. 나로 말하자면 그보다 한 살 위지만, 게스트윅에서 날마다 오르내린다 해도 아무렇지 않을 걸세.”\n\n“황소를 돌보시는 덕분이지요.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“이런! 자네 말이 옳네, 조니 도련님. 내 누이와 크로프츠 선생이야 저희 하고 싶은 대로 나를 타이르라지. 하지만 사람이 날마다 여덟아홉 시간을 바깥바람 속에서 지내고 나면, 그 뒤에야 어디 가서 잠을 자든 그리 대수로울 것이 없는 법이야. 여기가 포킨스일세.—참 훌륭한 집이지. 다만 늙은 포킨스가 살아 있을 적만큼은 못하다네. 임스 씨를 침실로 모셔서 손을 씻으시게 해 드리게.”\n\n데일 대령은 얼굴이 제 형과 많이 닮았으나 키가 더 크고 더 여위었으며, 보기에 나이도 더 들어 보였다. 임스가 거실로 들어갔을 때 대령은 혼자 거기 있었고, 스스로 제 소개를 해야 하는 수고를 떠맡아야 했다. 그는 안락의자에서 일어서지는 않은 채 젊은이를 향해 가볍게 고개만 끄덕였다. “임스 씨라고 했던가? 여러 해 전에 게스트윅에서 자네 부친을 알았네.” 그러고 나서 그는 얼굴을 다시 난롯불 쪽으로 돌리고 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n“오늘 오후에는 몹시 추워졌습니다.” 조니가 말을 붙여 보려고 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“런던은 늘 춥지.” 대령이 말했다.\n\n“팔월에 여기 계셔 보셔야 한다면 그런 말씀은 안 하실 겁니다.”\n\n“천만의 말씀.” 대령은 그렇게 말하고는 난롯불에 눈을 붙박은 채 다시 한숨을 쉬었다. 임스는 올랜도 데일이 지극히 무서운 방해를 무릅쓰고 드 게스트 경의 누이와 기어이 달아나 버렸다는 그 씩씩하기 그지없는 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다. 그래서 지금 그 겁 없는 연인을 바라보면서, 그 시절 이후로 대단한 변화가 있었음이 틀림없다고 생각했다. 그 뒤로는 백작이 내려올 때까지 더 오간 말이 없었다.\n\n포킨스의 집은 무슨 일에서나 철저히 구식이었다. 그리하여 만찬이 시작될 때 당대의 포킨스가 몸소 백작의 팔꿈치 뒤에 서서 손수 수프 그릇의 뚜껑을 벗겼다. 드 게스트 경은 제 한 몸에 바쳐지는 시중을 그리 많이 바라는 사람이 아니었으나, 이것이 없었다면 서운해했을 것이다. 실제로 그는 살진 소들에 관해 포킨스에게 정중한 말을 한마디 건넸고, 그로써 자기가 포킨스를 급사 가운데 하나로 잘못 알고 있지 않다는 것을 드러내 보였다. 그러고 나서 포킨스는 술에 관한 백작님의 분부를 받고 물러갔다.\n\n“옛 집을 제법 잘 지켜 가고 있소.” 백작이 제 매부에게 말했다. “삼십 년 전 같지야 않소만, 하기야 그런 것은 죄다 갈수록 더 나빠지기만 하니까.”\n\n“그런 모양이오.” 대령이 말했다.\n\n“늙은 포킨스가 우리 집에 있는 것만 한 포트와인을 내놓던 때가 기억나오.—적어도 그에 가깝기는 했지. 요새는 그런 것을 구할 수가 없다오.”\n\n“나는 포트와인은 마시지 않소.” 대령이 말했다. “만찬 뒤에는 니거스를 조금 드는 것 말고는 무엇이든 좀처럼 입에 대지 않소.”\n\n그의 처남은 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 얼굴을 제 수프 접시 쪽으로 돌리면서 더없이 뜻이 분명한 낯을 지었다. 임스는 그것을 보고 웃음을 참기가 어려웠다. 아홉 시 반이 되어 대령이 방에서 물러가자, 문이 닫히는 것과 함께 백작은 두 손을 번쩍 들어 올리며 한마디만 내뱉었다. “니거스!” 그러자 임스도 용기를 내어 실컷 웃어 젖혔다.\n\n만찬은 몹시 따분했고, 대령이 잠자리에 들기도 전에 조니는 포킨스에서 만찬을 들자는 말에 넘어간 것을 후회하고 있었다. 백작과 함께 만찬에 초대를 받는다는 것은 참으로 굉장한 일일 수도 있었고, 존 임스도 그 덕분에 제 관청에서 위신이 얼마쯤 올라간 것을 그리 싫지 않게 느끼고 있었을 것이다. 그러나 사과 네댓 알과 말린 견과 한 접시가 놓인 식탁에 앉아, 눈을 뜨고 있으려 애쓰는 백작과, 제 동무들이 자든 깨어 있든 도무지 아랑곳하지 않는 듯한 대령을 번갈아 바라보노라니, 그는 자기가 치르고 있는 값이 너무 비싸다는 것을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 로퍼 부인의 차 탁자도 그에게 즐거운 자리는 아니었으나, 서로 나눌 이야깃거리라고는 하나도 없어 보이는 지친 노인 두 사람을 곁에 두고 마주한 포킨스네 마호가니 식탁의 시커먼 칙칙함보다는 그편이 차라리 나았을 것이다. 한두 번쯤 그는 대령에게 말을 붙여 보았다. 대령이 눈을 뜬 채 앉아 난롯불을 바라보고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 돌아온 것은 외마디 대답뿐이었고, 대령이 이야기를 나눌 마음이 없다는 것은 그에게 분명해 보였다. 두 손을 무릎 위에 포개어 쥐고 가만히 앉아 있는 것, 그것이 만찬 뒤의 시간 동안 데일 대령에게는 넉넉한 일거리였다.\n\n그러나 백작은 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알고 있었다. 그와 그의 졸음 사이에 벌어진 저 무서운 싸움, 졸음의 신이 이십 분 남짓 그를 아주 눌러 이겨 버린 그 싸움이 이어지는 동안에도, 그의 양심은 줄곧 손님들을 소홀히 대접하고 있다고 그를 나무라고 있었다. 그는 스스로에게 몹시 화가 나서 정신을 차리고 이야기를 해 보려고 애썼다. 그러나 그의 매부는 그 애씀을 조금도 거들어 주지 않았고, 임스마저도 그를 돕는 데 그리 재치가 있지 못했다. 그러고 나서 이십 분 동안 그는 곤히 잠들었고, 그 끝에 스스로 코 고는 소리를 한 번 내고는 제 소리에 깨어났다. “이런!” 그가 벌떡 일어나 난롯가 깔개에 서면서 말했다. “커피를 좀 들지.” 그 뒤로 그는 더 잠들지 않았다.\n\n“데일.” 그가 말했다. “술을 좀 더 들지 않겠소?”\n\n“더는 됐소.” 대령이 여전히 난롯불을 바라보며 아주 천천히 고개를 저으면서 말했다.\n\n“자, 조니, 잔을 채우게.” 그는 임스 부인이 제 아들을 대개 그 이름으로 부른다는 것을 알고 난 뒤로, 이미 제 젊은 벗을 조니라고 부르는 버릇이 들어 있었다.\n\n“여태 내내 채우고 있었습니다.” 임스가 그렇게 말하면서 유리 술병을 다시 손에 집어 들었다.\n\n“자네가 재미 붙일 것을 찾았다니 다행일세. 내가 보기에는 자네와 데일이 서로 나눌 말이 별로 없었던 것 같아서 말이야. 나는 내내 듣고 있었네.”\n\n“당신은 자고 있었소.” 대령이 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 내가 입을 다물고 있은 데에도 얼마쯤 핑계가 있었던 셈이오.” 백작이 말했다. “그건 그렇고 데일, 그 크로즈비란 자를 어떻게 생각하오?”\n\n임스의 귀가 대번에 곤두섰고, 따분함의 기운은 그에게서 사라져 버렸다.\n\n“그자를 어떻게 생각하느냐고?” 대령이 말했다.\n\n“가죽 속의 뼈란 뼈는 죄다 부러뜨려 놓아야 마땅한 자요.” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“마땅하고말고요.” 임스는 흥분한 나머지 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며, 어른들 앞에서는 어쩌면 어울리지 않을 만큼 다소 큰 목소리로 말했다. “마땅하고말고요, 백작님. 그자는 제 평생에 만나 본 가장 가증스러운 악당입니다. 제가 릴리 데일의 오라비였으면 좋겠습니다.” 그러고 나서 그는 자기가 릴리의 삼촌 앞에서, 또 릴리의 오라비 자리를 대신한다고 볼 만한 버나드 데일의 아버지 앞에서 말하고 있다는 것을 떠올리고 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n대령이 고개를 돌려 놀란 눈으로 젊은이를 바라보았다. “용서하십시오, 어르신.” 임스가 말했다. “하오나 저는 데일 부인과 어르신의 조카딸들을 평생 알고 지내 온 사이입니다.”\n\n“아, 그런가?” 대령이 말했다. “그렇더라도 젊은 아가씨의 이름을 너무 함부로 입에 올리지 않는 편이 나을 것이네. 자네를 조금이라도 나무라는 것은 아니네만, 임스 씨.”\n\n“나무랄 일이 아니고말고요.” 백작이 말했다. “나는 이 사람의 그 마음을 갸륵하게 여기오. 조니, 이 사람아, 내가 언제고 운 사납게 그자를 만나게 된다면 나는 그자에게 내 생각을 말해 줄 것이고, 자네도 그러리라고 믿네.” 이 말을 듣고 존 임스는 백작을 향해 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보이고는, 저에게 등을 돌리고 있는 대령 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다. 그러자 백작도 임스에게 눈을 찡긋해 보였다.\n\n“드 게스트.” 대령이 말했다. “나는 위층으로 올라가야겠소. 나는 늘 내 방에서 애로루트 죽을 조금 든다오.”\n\n“초를 가져오라고 종을 울리겠소.” 주인이 말했다. 그러고서 대령이 가고 그의 뒤로 문이 닫히자, 백작은 두 손을 들어 올리고 그 한마디를 내뱉었다. “니거스!” 그러자 조니는 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 난롯가로 돌아와 대령이 비우고 간 안락의자에 자리를 잡고 앉았다.\n\n“나쁠 것이야 없겠지.” 백작이 말했다. “하지만 나 같으면 니거스를 마시고 싶지도 않고, 침실에 애로루트 죽을 들여놓고 싶지도 않네.”\n\n“해로울 것이야 없겠지요.”\n\n“아무렴, 없고말고. 포킨스가 저 사람을 두고 무어라고 할지 궁금하군. 하기야 호텔에는 별의별 손님이 다 들 테지.”\n\n“급사도 그것을 가져올 때 별로 대수롭게 여기지 않는 눈치던데요.”\n\n“그럼, 그럼. 센나와 하제 소금을 달라고 했더라도 급사는 조금도 놀란 기색을 보이지 않았을 걸세. 그건 그렇고, 자네가 그 가엾은 아가씨 일로 저 사람을 좀 건드렸더군.”\n\n“제가 그랬습니까, 백작님? 그럴 뜻은 아니었습니다.”\n\n“보다시피 저 사람은 버나드 데일의 아버지일세. 그러니 버나드가 그자가 저지른 일에 대해 그자를 벌해야 하지 않겠느냐 하는 것이 문제지. 누군가는 해야 할 일이야. 그자가 무사히 빠져나가는 것은 옳지 않아. 누군가는 크로즈비 씨에게 그자가 제 손으로 저를 얼마나 몹쓸 놈으로 만들어 놓았는지 알려 주어야 하네.”\n\n“저라면 내일이라도 하겠습니다만, 다만 걱정되는 것은—”\n\n“아니, 아니, 아닐세.” 백작이 말했다. “자네는 도무지 그 일에 맞는 사람이 아니야. 자네가 그 일과 무슨 상관인가? 자네는 그저 집안끼리 아는 벗으로 그들을 알고 지냈을 뿐인데, 그것으로는 모자라네.”\n\n“예, 그렇겠지요.” 임스가 서글프게 말했다.\n\n“아마 이대로가 가장 나을지도 모르지.” 백작이 말했다. “그자의 머리통을 후려친다고 무슨 좋은 일이 생길지 나도 모르겠네. 그리고 우리가 그리스도인이어야 하는 것이라면, 아무래도 그리스도인다워야 하는 법이겠지.”\n\n“그자는 어떤 그리스도인 노릇을 했습니까?”\n\n“그건 참말 옳은 말일세. 그리고 내가 버나드라면 온유함에 관한 성경 가르침 따위는 잊어버리기 딱 알맞겠지.”\n\n“제 생각을 말씀드리자면요, 백작님, 저는 그자를 두들겨 패 주는 것이야말로 이 세상에서 가장 그리스도인다운 일이라고 여깁니다. 참말로 그렇습니다. 사람이 시퍼렇게 멍이 들도록 두들겨 맞아야 마땅한 일이라는 것이 세상에는 있습니다.”\n\n“다시는 그런 짓을 하지 못하도록 말인가?”\n\n“바로 그렇습니다. 사람을 목매다는 것이 그리스도인답지 않다고 말할 수도 있겠지요.”\n\n“나는 살인자라면 언제라도 목매달겠네. 양을 훔쳤다고 사람을 목매다는 것이야 옳지 않았지만 말일세.”\n\n“크로즈비 같은 자를 목매다는 편이 훨씬 낫지요.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 나도 그렇게 믿네. 지금 누구든 그 아가씨의 환심을 사고 싶은 사내가 있다면 얼마나 좋은 기회이겠나.”\n\n조니는 대답하기 전에 한두 순간 잠자코 있었다. “저는 그렇게 자신할 수가 없습니다.” 그는 서글프게 말했다. 릴리의 옛 연인을 두들겨 준다고 해서 릴리의 환심을 살 가망은 없다는 생각에 마음이 아픈 듯한 투였다.\n\n“나야 아가씨들에 대해 잘 안다고 내세울 처지는 못 되네만.” 드 게스트 경이 말했다. “그래도 그러리라고 생각하네. 그자가 혼쭐이 났다는 소식, 그리고 그자가 혼쭐이 났다는 것을 온 세상이 알고 있다는 소식만큼 그 아가씨를 기쁘게 할 것은 없으리라고 나는 짐작하네.” 백작은 자기가 아가씨들에 대해 잘 알지 못한다고 밝혔거니와, 그렇게 말한 점에서는 의심할 것 없이 옳았다.\n\n“저도 그렇게 생각한다면.” 임스가 말했다. “당장 내일이라도 그자를 찾아내겠습니다.”\n\n“어째서? 그것이 자네에게 무슨 상관인가?” 그러고 나서 또 한 번 말이 끊겼는데, 그동안 조니는 몹시 겸연쩍은 얼굴을 하고 있었다. “설마 자네가 릴리 데일 양을 사랑하고 있다는 말은 아니겠지?”\n\n“제가 그분을 사랑하고 있는 것인지 어떤지는 잘 모르겠습니다.” 조니는 그렇게 말하면서 얼굴이 새빨개졌다. 그러고 나서 그는 어딘가 무모한 심정으로 제 벗에게 온 진실을 다 털어놓기로 마음을 정했다. 포킨스의 포트와인이 그 결심에 얼마쯤 몫을 했는지도 모른다. “하오나 저는 그분을 위해서라면 불속이든 물속이든 뛰어들 것입니다, 백작님. 저는 그자가 그분을 보기도 여러 해 전부터 그분을 알고 지냈고, 그자가 앞으로 누구를 사랑하게 되든 그보다 훨씬 더 깊이 그분을 사랑해 왔습니다. 그분이 그자를 받아들였다는 말을 들었을 때는 제 목을 그어 버릴까 하는 생각이 절반쯤 들었습니다.—아니면 그자의 목을 긋든지요.”\n\n“어허, 그것참.” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“참으로 우스운 일이라는 것은 저도 압니다.” 조니가 말했다. “그리고 물론 그분이 저를 받아들이는 일은 결코 없었을 테고요.”\n\n“나는 도무지 그렇게 보지 않네.”\n\n“저는 이 세상에 일 실링도 가진 것이 없습니다.”\n\n“아가씨들은 그런 것을 그리 마음에 두지 않네.”\n\n“게다가 소득세 사무국의 서기 아닙니까! 참으로 보잘것없는 것이지요.”\n\n“그 다른 사내도 다른 사무국의 서기에 지나지 않았네.”\n\n게스트윅에 내려가 사는 백작은 시티에 있는 소득세 사무국과 화이트홀에 있는 일반위원회 사무국이 부자와 나사로만큼이나 멀리 떨어져 있고, 도무지 건널 수 없는 구렁으로 갈라져 있다는 것을 알지 못했다.\n\n“예, 그렇습니다.” 조니가 말했다. “하지만 그자의 사무국은 다른 종류의 것이고, 게다가 그자는 그 자신이 멋쟁이였습니다.”\n\n“이런, 나는 도무지 모르겠네.” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“저는 그분이 그런 자를 받아들인 것이 조금도 이상하지 않습니다. 저야 그자를 처음 본 그 순간부터 미워했습니다만, 그렇다고 그분까지 그자를 미워해야 할 까닭은 없지요. 그자에게는 그런 티가 있었으니까요. 그자는 멋쟁이였고, 아가씨들은 그런 것을 좋아합니다. 저는 그분에게 성이 난 적은 한 번도 없습니다. 하지만 그자라면 잡아먹어 버릴 수도 있었습니다.” 그렇게 말하는 그의 낯빛은, 크로즈비가 그 자리에 있었더라면 정말로 그런 짓을 해 보였을 것만 같았다.\n\n“자네를 받아 달라고 그 아가씨에게 청해 본 적은 있는가?” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“없습니다. 그분에게 드릴 빵 한 조각이 없는데 어떻게 청할 수 있었겠습니까?”\n\n“그러면 그 아가씨에게 말한 적도 없겠군.—그러니까 자네가 그 아가씨를 사랑하고 있다는 것이며, 그런 따위 이야기 말일세.”\n\n“이제는 그분도 알고 계십니다.” 조니가 말했다. “요전에 작별 인사를 하러 갔었습니다.—그분이 곧 혼인하시는 줄로 알았을 때였지요. 그때는 말하지 않고는 견딜 수가 없었습니다.”\n\n“하지만 내가 보기에는, 이 사람아, 자네는 크로즈비에게 대단히 고마워해야 할 것 같은데.—그러니까 자네에게 그럴 마음이 있다면 말일세—”\n\n“무슨 말씀이신지 압니다, 백작님. 저는 그자에게 조금도 고맙지 않습니다. 이번 일이 그분을 거의 죽여 놓으리라는 것이 제 생각입니다. 저로 말씀드리자면, 그분이 언젠가 저를 받아 주시리라고 여길 수만 있다면—”\n\n그러고서 그는 다시 입을 다물었고, 백작은 그의 눈에 눈물이 고인 것을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n“이제 얼마쯤 알 것 같군.” 백작이 말했다. “그러니 내가 자네에게 한마디 일러 주겠네. 자네, 내려와서 게스트윅에서 나와 함께 크리스마스를 보내게.”\n\n“아, 백작님!”\n\n“백작님 백작님 하는 것은 그만두고 내가 시키는 대로 하게. 레이디 줄리아가 자네에게 전하는 말을 부탁했는데, 나는 지금까지 그것을 까맣게 잊고 있었네. 누이가 자네가 들판에서 한 일에 대해 제 입으로 직접 고맙다는 말을 하고 싶어 하네.”\n\n“그것은 다 당치 않은 말씀입니다, 백작님.”\n\n“좋네. 그러면 그 말을 누이에게 직접 하게. 그리고 이 말도 내 말을 그대로 믿어도 좋네.—내 누이는 자네 못지않게 크로즈비를 미워하고 있어. 방법만 안다면 자네 말마따나 제 손으로 그자를 ‘두들겨 패 주고’ 싶어 할 걸세. 크리스마스 때 게스트윅으로 내려오게. 그러고 나서 앨링턴으로 건너가 자네 뜻이 무엇인지 그 사람들에게 죄다 툭 터놓고 말하게.”\n\n“지금은 그분에게 한마디도 할 수 없습니다.”\n\n“그러면 지주에게 말하게. 그를 찾아가 자네 뜻이 무엇인지 말하게.—사내답게 고개를 꼿꼿이 들고서 말일세. 내 앞에서 멋쟁이 이야기는 꺼내지 말게. 정직하게 마음먹은 사내가 내가 아는 가장 훌륭한 멋쟁이야. 내가 멋쟁이로 쳐 주는 사람은 그런 사람뿐일세. 늙은 데일에게 가서 나한테서 왔다고 말하게.—게스트윅 저택에서 왔다고 말이야. 그리고 그 사람에게 이렇게 전하게. 냄비가 끓도록 그 밑에 작은 장작을 하나 넣어 준다면 나는 그보다 더 큰 것을 넣겠노라고. 그러면 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알아들을 걸세.”\n\n“아, 그건 안 됩니다, 백작님.”\n\n“하지만 나는 된다고 하겠네.” 그러면서 이제 난롯가 깔개 위에 서 있던 백작은 두 손을 바지 주머니 깊숙이 찔러 넣었다. “나는 그 아가씨를 매우 아끼고 있고, 그 아가씨를 위해서라면 많은 일을 하겠네. 자네가 그쪽으로 연모의 눈길을 던지고 있다는 것을 내가 알기도 전에 내가 그런 말을 했는지 안 했는지 레이디 줄리아에게 물어보게. 그리고 나는 자네에게도 남몰래 정이 가네, 조니 도련님. 이런, 나는 자네 부친을 내가 여태 알았던 그 누구 못지않게 잘 알았네. 그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 그를 망하게 하는 데 내가 거들었다고 나는 믿고 있어. 알다시피 그는 내 땅을 빌려 부쳤고, 그가 제 손으로 제 신세를 망친 것에는 의심할 여지가 없네. 그는 그 일을 다 끝냈을 때에도 짐승에 대해서는 저—저—저 급사보다 더 아는 것이 없었어. 오늘날까지 계속했다 한들 조금도 더 나아지지 않았을 걸세.”\n\n조니는 눈에 눈물이 가득한 채 말없이 앉아 있었다. 제 벗에게 무슨 말을 한단 말인가?\n\n“자네, 나와 함께 내려가세.” 백작이 말을 이었다. “그러면 우리가 이 일을 죄다 바로잡게 되리라는 것을 알게 될 걸세. 지금 당장은 그 아가씨에게 말하지 않는 편이 좋다는 자네 말은 옳다고 보네. 하지만 삼촌에게 죄다 말하고, 그다음에는 어머니에게 말하게. 그리고 무엇보다도, 자네 자신이 모자란 사람이라고는 결코 생각하지 말게. 사람이 그런 생각을 해서는 안 되는 법이야. 내 생각으로는, 세상 사람들은 자네가 스스로를 셈하는 그대로 자네를 받아들이네. 자네가 저 크로즈비란 자처럼 진흙으로 빚어진 사람이라면 끝내는 들통이 나고 말겠지, 그야 의심할 것이 없네. 하지만 나는 자네가 진흙으로 빚어진 사람이라고는 생각하지 않아.”\n\n“그러지 않기를 바랍니다.”\n\n“나도 그러네. 자네도 모레 나와 함께 내려갈 수 있겠지?”\n\n“아무래도 어려울 듯합니다. 저는 휴가를 다 써 버렸습니다.”\n\n“내가 늙은 버플에게 편지를 써서 부탁으로 청해 볼까?”\n\n“아닙니다.” 조니가 말했다. “그것은 제 마음에 내키지 않습니다. 하지만 내일 알아보고 나서 백작님께 알려 드리겠습니다. 아무리 못해도 토요일에 우편 열차로 내려갈 수는 있습니다.”\n\n“그것은 편치 못할 걸세. 될 수 있으면 어떻게든 나와 함께 오도록 하게. 자, 잘 자게, 이 사람아. 그리고 이것을 기억해 두게.—나는 무슨 말을 하면 그 말대로 하는 사람일세. 나는 여태껏 한 번도 내 말을 뒤집은 적이 없다고 자랑해도 좋으리라고 생각하네.”\n\n백작은 그렇게 말하면서 제 손님에게 왼손을 내밀고는, 젊은이의 머리 너머로 다소 거창하게 눈길을 치켜올린 채 오른손으로 제 가슴을 세 번 두드렸다. 그 짤막한 장면을 치러 내는 그를 보며 존 임스는 이분이야말로 어느 모로 보나 백작이라고 느꼈다.\n\n“무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 모르겠습니다, 백작님.”\n\n“아무 말도 하지 말게.—나에게는 한마디도 더 하지 말게. 다만 자네 자신에게 이렇게 말하게. 겁 많은 마음으로는 어여쁜 아가씨를 얻지 못한다고. 잘 자게, 이 사람아, 잘 자게. 나는 내일 밖에서 만찬을 드네만, 자네가 여섯 시쯤에 들러서 알려 주면 되네.”\n\n그러고서 임스는 더는 한마디도 하지 않고 방을 나와 저민 스트리트의 차가운 공기 속으로 걸어 나갔다. 달은 맑고 밝았으며, 그 환한 빛 속의 포도는 귀부인의 손처럼 깨끗해 보였다. 그가 포킨스 호텔에 들어선 뒤로 온 세상이 그에게는 달라져 있었다. 그렇다면 릴리 데일이 아직도 제 아내가 되는 일이 있을 수 있다는 말인가? 그가, 지금 당장이라도, 앨링턴의 지주를 대담하게 찾아가 릴리에 관한 제 뜻이 무엇인지 말할 만한 처지에 있다는 것이 참말일 수 있는가? 그리고 백작의 말을 그대로 곧이듣는 것이 어디까지 옳은 일일까? 릴리 데일과 혼인하기까지는 도무지 믿기 어려울 만큼 큰 재산이 있어야 할 것이었다. 아무리 적게 잡아도 연 이삼백 파운드는 있어야 한다! 백작이 그만한 액수를 그런 목적으로 마련해 주겠다는 뜻으로 말했을 리는 없다! 그럼에도 그는 버턴 크레슨트로 걸어 돌아가면서 게스트윅으로 내려가겠다고, 그리고 백작이 이르신 대로 따르겠다고 마음을 정했다. 릴리 자신에게는 앞으로도 오랜 날 동안 아무 말도 할 수 없으리라고 그는 느꼈다.\n\n“어머, 존, 왜 이렇게 늦었어요!” 그가 대문 열쇠로 문을 열고 들어서는데 어밀리아가 뒤쪽 응접실에서 슬며시 나오며 말했다.\n\n“예, 늦었지요.—아주 늦었습니다.” 존은 그렇게 말하고는 제 초를 집어 들고, 더는 한마디도 없이 계단에서 그녀를 지나쳐 갔다.\n\n# 제33장. 「그때는 오리라」\n\n“젊은 임스가 게스트윅 저택에 묵고 있다는 이야기를 들었소?”\n\n성탄절에 예배가 끝나고 함께 큰 집으로 걸어 올라가면서 지주가 데일 부인에게 건넨 첫마디가 이것이었으니, 조니가 다녀간다는 소식이 그에게 전해졌을 때 얼마쯤 인상을 남겼다는 것이 넉넉히 분명했다.\n\n“게스트윅 저택에요!” 데일 부인이 말했다. “어머나! 벨, 너 그 이야기 들었니? 조니 도련님이 출세를 하셨구나!”\n\n“엄마, 기억 안 나세요?” 벨이 말했다. “그이가 백작님께서 황소 때문에 곤경에 처하셨을 때 도와 드렸잖아요.”\n\n존 임스와 마지막으로 만났던 자리의 모든 대목을 또렷이 기억하고 있던 릴리는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 이런 때에 그가 제 가까이에 와 있다는 생각에 어딘가 마음이 쓰라렸다. 저도 모르는 사이에 그녀는 그가 저를 찾아와 그 모든 말을 해 준 것 때문에 그를 좋아하고 있었다. 그 일이 있은 뒤로 그녀는 전보다 그를 더 높이 치게 되었다. 그러나 이제 형편이 이러한데 그가 굳이 제 앞에 나타나기라도 한다면, 그녀는 스스로 짓밟히고 상처 입었다고 느낄 터였다.\n\n“드 게스트 경이 그만한 작은 호의를 두고 그토록 큰 고마움을 내보일 사람이라고는 생각지 못했소.” 지주가 말했다. “아무튼 나는 내일 거기서 만찬을 들기로 했소.”\n\n“젊은 임스를 만나러요?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그렇소.—특히 젊은 임스를 만나러 말이오. 적어도 나는 꼭 와 달라는 청을 아주 각별히 받았고, 그가 거기 올 거라는 이야기도 들었소.”\n\n“버나드도 가니?”\n\n“저는 안 갑니다.” 버나드가 말했다. “저는 이리로 건너와서 숙모님과 만찬을 들겠어요.”\n\n어렴풋한 생각 하나가 릴리의 마음을 스쳐 지나가, 잠깐 동안이나마 저도 어쩌면 이 일과 무슨 상관이 있을지 모른다는 상상을 하게 했다. 그러나 그 생각은 온 것만큼이나 빠르게 사라지면서 다만 쓰라림만 얼마쯤 남겨 놓았다. 어떤 병은 온몸을 아프게 한다. 그런 병을 앓는 이는 어느 자리를 건드리기만 해도—아니, 건드릴 듯 다가가기만 해도—온몸에 멍이 든 것처럼 고통에 겨워 부르짖는다. 마음의 병도 그와 같다. 가엾은 릴리가 겪는 것과 같은 슬픔은 심장을 어느 자리에서나 쓰라리게 만들어, 앓는 이로 하여금 새 상처를 늘 두려워하게 만든다. 릴리는 제 십자가를 씩씩하게 잘 지고 있었다. 그러나 그것을 지고 있지 않은 사람처럼 걸어갈 힘이 있다고 해서 그 짐이 걸음마다 그녀를 무겁게 누르지 않는 것은 아니었다. 그녀에게 일어나는 일이든 그녀가 있는 자리에서 일어나는 일이든, 어떤 식으로든 제 비참함과 이어지지 않는 것이 없었다. 삼촌이 드 게스트 경의 집으로 존 임스를 만나러 간다. 그러면 물론 거기 모인 남자들은 그녀 이야기를 할 터였고, 그런 이야기는 모두 그녀에게 상처였다.\n\n그날 오후는 밝게 흘러가지 않았다. 하인들이 방에 있는 동안에는 만찬도 여느 만찬과 크게 다르지 않게 지나갔다. 그런 자리에서는 아주 가까운 집안끼리라도 얼마쯤의 위선을 늘 부려야 하는 법이다. 여러 사람이 섞인 만찬 자리에서라면 리처드와 윌리엄이 앞에 있어도 그들이 없을 때 쓸 말과 똑같은 말을 할 수 있다. 그렇게 섞인 사람들은 서로 제 집안의 속마음을 이야기하지 않기 때문이다. 그러나 가까운 사이끼리 모였을 때에는 문이 닫히고 빗장이 걸릴 때까지 얼마쯤 의식하여 말을 아끼게 된다. 이날 같은 모임에서는 그렇게 말을 아끼는 것이 도움이 되어 이로운 효과를 냈다. 그러나 문이 닫히고 빗장이 걸리고 하인들이 물러가고 나서, 그들이 어떻게 함께 즐거울 수 있었겠는가? 그 성탄절에 무슨 흥겨움으로 수염을 흔들며 웃을 수 있었겠는가?\n\n“아버지가 런던에 올라와 계셨습니다.” 버나드가 말했다. “드 게스트 경과 함께 포킨스에 계셨어요.”\n\n“그런데 왜 가서 뵙지 않았니?” 데일 부인이 물었다.\n\n“글쎄요, 저도 모르겠습니다. 아버지도 그걸 바라시는 것 같지 않았고요. 이월에는 토키로 내려갈 생각입니다. 아시다시피 저는 두 주 뒤에는 런던으로 올라가야 합니다, 이제 아주요.” 그러고 나서 그들은 몇 분 동안 다시 모두 말이 없었다. 버나드가 사실대로 털어놓을 수 있었다면, 그는 자기가 런던에 올라가지 않은 것은 크로즈비를 만나게 될 때 그를 어떻게 대해야 할지 아직 알지 못했기 때문이라고 인정했을 것이다. 이 문제에 관한 그의 생각은 가엾은 릴리의 마음에도 어떤 그림자를 드리워, 제 상처가 또 벌어지는 것처럼 느끼게 했다.\n\n“나는 저 아이가 제 직업을 아주 버렸으면 하오.” 지주가 단호하고 느릿하게 말했다. “그리하는 편이 우리 둘 다에게 나으리라고 나는 생각하오.”\n\n“그 나이에, 그것도 그렇게 잘해 오고 있는데 그게 현명한 일일까요?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“나는 현명한 일이라고 생각하오. 저 아이가 내 아들이라면, 런던에 그대로 머물러 있거나 어쩌면 인도로 보내지는 것보다, 이 영지에서 장차 제 소작인이 될 사람들 틈에 살아가는 편이 낫다고들 여길 것이오. 저 아이는 이 자리의 상속인이라는 직업을 하나 가지고 있고, 나는 그것으로 넉넉하다고 생각하오.”\n\n“여기 내려와 살면 저는 한가하기만 한 삶을 살게 될 겁니다.” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“그건 네 탓일 게다. 하지만 네가 내 뜻대로만 해 준다면 네 삶이 한가하지는 않을 게다.” 이 말로 그는 버나드의 혼담을 넌지시 가리킨 것이었으나, 그 일에 대해서는 벨이 있는 자리에서 더 말할 수가 없었다. 벨은 그것을 죄다 알아들었고, 얌전한 낯으로 아무 말 없이 앉아 있었다.—어쩌면 그 얼굴에는 얼마쯤 매서운 데까지 있었는지도 모른다.\n\n“하지만 실은,” 데일 부인이 낮은 목소리로, 무슨 말을 할지 잘 헤아려 두고서 말했다. “버나드는 시아주버니의 아들과 꼭 같지는 않잖아요.”\n\n“어째서 같지 않다는 거요?” 지주가 말했다. “나는 저 아이가 군을 떠나기만 한다면 이 영지를 저 아이 앞으로 물려주겠다는 말까지 했소.”\n\n“시아주버니가 아드님에게 지실 만큼의 빚을 버나드에게 지고 계시지는 않잖아요. 그러니 버나드도 제 아버지에게 질 만큼의 빚을 시아주버니에게 지고 있지는 않은 거예요.”\n\n“내가 저 아이를 억지로 어쩔 수 없다는 뜻이라면, 나도 그쯤은 잘 알고 있소. 돈에 관해서라면 나는 어느 아버지든 외아들에게 마땅히 해 주어야 한다고 여길 만한 것을 죄다 해 주겠다고 했소.”\n\n“제가 은혜를 모른다고 여기지는 말아 주십시오.” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“그렇게 여기지 않는다. 다만 나는 네가 마음을 쓰지 않는다고 여긴다. 하지만 그 일에 대해서는 더 할 말이 없다.—그 일에 대해서는 말이다. 네가 혼인을 하게 된다면—” 그러고서 그는 말을 멈추었으니, 벨이 있는 자리에서는 더 나아갈 수 없다고 느낀 것이었다.\n\n“그 애가 혼인을 하게 된다면,” 데일 부인이 말했다. “그 아내 되는 사람이야 제 집을 갖고 싶어 하는 것도 당연하겠지요.”\n\n“이 집이 저 아이 집이 아니고 무엇이오?” 지주가 성이 나서 말했다. “이 집이 넉넉히 크지 않소? 나야 나 한 사람 쓸 방 하나면 되고, 필요하다면 그것마저도 내놓겠소.”\n\n“그건 당치도 않은 말씀이에요.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“당치 않은 말이 아니오.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“앞으로 이십 년은 앨링턴의 지주로 계실 거예요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “그리고 지주로 계시는 동안에는 이 집의 주인이실 거고요. 적어도 저는 그러기를 바랍니다. 저는 임금이 젊은 사람에게 자리를 넘기고 물러앉는 걸 좋아하지 않아요.”\n\n“크리스토퍼 삼촌이 카를 5세 같은 모습으로 계시면 조금도 어울리지 않으실 것 같은데요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“내가 그리하게 되거든 너를 위해서는 언제나 수도실 한 칸을 남겨 두마, 얘야.” 지주가 그 아프고도 각별한 다정함을 담아 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. 데일 부인 곁에 앉아 있던 릴리는 남몰래 손을 뻗어 어머니의 손을 잡았으니, 그로써 삼촌이 내주겠다는 그 수도실에 들어가 살 뜻이 없다는 것을, 또 제 수도자다운 은거의 길동무로 삼촌을 바라볼 뜻이 없다는 것을 내보인 셈이었다. 그 뒤로 버나드의 앞날에 관해서는 더 이야기되지 않았다.\n\n“헌 부인은 목사관에서 만찬을 드는 모양이지?” 지주가 물었다.\n\n“예, 예배가 끝나고 들어가셨어요.” 벨이 말했다. “보이스 부인과 함께 가시는 걸 봤어요.”\n\n“그분이 저한테 그러시더군요, 겨울에는 어두워진 뒤에 다시는 그 댁에서 만찬을 들지 않겠다고요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “지난번에 거기 가셨을 때, 돌아오시는 길에 아이가 등을 꺼뜨리는 바람에 길을 잃으셨거든요. 사실인즉 보이스 씨가 바래다주지 않아서 화가 나신 거지요.”\n\n“그 부인은 늘 화가 나 있소.” 지주가 말했다. “요새는 나한테 말도 잘 걸지 않는다오. 요전에 졸리프에게 집세를 내면서, 이 돈이 나한테 크게 좋은 일이 되기를 바란다고 하더랍니다. 마치 그것을 받는 내가 짐승이라도 되는 양 여기는 투로 말이오.”\n\n“정말 그렇게 여기시는걸요.” 버나드가 말했다.\n\n“그분은 아주 늙으셨잖아요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“저라면 그 집을 거저 드리겠어요, 삼촌.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“아니다, 얘야. 네가 나라면 그러지 않을 게다. 내가 그리한다면 그건 아주 잘못된 일이지. 헌 부인이 어째서 고기나 옷은 거저 얻지 못하면서 집만은 거저 얻어야 한단 말이냐? 차라리 해마다 그만한 돈을 손에 쥐여 드리는 편이 훨씬 이치에 닿겠지. 그러나 그분은 자선을 받을 처지가 아니니 내가 그리하는 것도 잘못이고,—그분이 그것을 받는 것도 잘못일 게다.”\n\n“그리고 그분은 받지도 않으실 거예요.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“받지 않으시겠지. 하지만 만일 받으신다면, 그 액수가 갑절이 아니라고 투덜대실 것이 틀림없소. 그리고 보이스 씨가 집까지 바래다주었더라면, 걸음이 너무 빠르다고 투덜대셨을 거요.”\n\n“그분은 아주 늙으셨어요.” 벨이 다시 말했다.\n\n“그렇더라도 내 하인들에게 나를 헐뜯는 말을 하지 않을 만큼은 분별이 있어야지. 자기 자신을 좀 더 소중히 여겨야 하고.” 그리고 지주는 그 목소리의 어조로 자기가 그 일을 몹시 마음에 두고 있다는 것을 드러내 보였다.\n\n그 성탄절 저녁은 몹시 길고 몹시 따분했으니, 버나드로 하여금 제 직업을 버리고 앨링턴에서의 삶에 저를 붙들어 매는 것이야말로 어리석기 짝이 없는 짓이라는 생각을 굳히게 만들 지경이었다. 여자들은 남자들보다 길고 따분하고 하는 일 없는 시간에 익숙한 법이다. 그러므로 데일 부인과 그 딸들은 그 지루함을 씩씩하게 견뎌 냈다. 그가 하품을 하고 기지개를 켜고 방을 들락거리는 동안, 그들은 얌전히 앉아 지주가 자잘한 일들을 두고 법을 세우는 것에 귀를 기울였고, 이따금 어느 한 사람의 심사가 유난히 그러도록 부추길 때에만 그의 말을 거슬렀다. “물론 그거야 나보다 자네들이 훨씬 잘 알겠지.” 그가 그렇게 말하면, “천만에요.” 데일 부인이 대답했다. “저는 그 일에 대해 무엇 하나 안다고 내세울 생각이 없어요. 다만—” 이렇게 저녁은 저 혼자 닳아 갔다. 그리고 아홉 시 반에 홀로 남겨졌을 때, 지주는 그날이 자기에게 나쁘게 지나갔다고는 느끼지 않았다. 그것이 그의 삶의 방식이었고, 그는 삶에서 얻은 것보다 더한 것을 바라지도 않았다. 그는 무슨 일이 아주 즐겁기를 기대하지 않았고, 행복하지는 않을지언정 적어도 넉넉히 만족하고 있었다.\n\n“조니 임스가 게스트윅 저택에 있다니, 생각 좀 해 봐!” 집으로 돌아가면서 벨이 말했다.\n\n“거기 있으면 왜 안 되는데?” 릴리가 말했다. “레이디 줄리아가 워낙 심술궂으니까, 나 같으면 내가 가느니 그이가 가는 편이 낫겠어.”\n\n“그런데 크리스토퍼 삼촌더러 각별히 그이를 만나 달라고 청했다지 않니!” 데일 부인이 말했다. “거기에는 무슨 까닭이 있는 게다.” 그러자 릴리는 다시 그 쓰라림이 밀려오는 것을 느꼈고, 그 이야기에 대해 더는 입을 열지 않았다.\n\n우리는 모두 알고 있듯이 거기에는 각별한 까닭이 있었고, 릴리의 쓰라림이 품은 그 알 수 없는 예감도 틀린 것이 아니었다. 임스는 포킨스에서 만찬을 든 다음 날 저녁에 백작을 만나, 토요일 저녁까지는 시내를 떠날 수 없지만 화요일까지는 머물 수 있다고 설명해 두었다. 그는 수요일 열두 시까지 제 관청에 나가 있어야 했는데, 게스트윅에서 이른 기차를 타면 그렇게 할 수 있었다.\n\n“아주 좋네, 조니.” 침실용 촛대를 손에 들고 옷을 갈아입으러 올라가려던 백작이 제 젊은 벗에게 말했다. “그러면 이렇게 하지. 나도 그 일을 생각해 두었네. 화요일에 만찬을 들러 오라고 데일에게 청하겠네. 그리고 그가 오겠다고 하면 내가 몸소 그에게 이 일을 죄다 설명하겠네. 그는 일에 밝은 사람이니 알아들을 걸세. 오지 않겠다고 하면, 그때는 자네가 화요일 아침에 앨링턴으로 건너가서 되도록 그를 찾아보게. 아니면 내가 몸소 찾아가지, 그편이 낫겠군. 지금은 나를 붙들지 말게. 어지간히 늦었으니.”\n\n임스는 그를 붙들려 하지 않고 물러났는데, 이 모든 일이 저를 위해 참으로 놀라운 방식으로 처리되고 있다는 느낌이 들었다. 그리고 그가 앨링턴에 이르렀을 때, 그는 지주가 백작의 초대를 받아들였다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그러자 그는 이제 더는 물러설 길이 없다고 스스로에게 선언했다. 물론 그는 물러설 마음도 없었다. 그의 삶의 하나뿐인 큰 바람은 릴리 데일을 제 사람이라 부르는 것이었다. 그러나 그는 지주가 두려웠다.—지주가 저를 업신여기고 면박을 줄까 봐, 그리고 백작이 제 의뢰인이 그렇게 멸시받고 면박당하는 것을 보고 자기가 잘못 짚었구나 하고 깨닫게 될까 봐 두려웠다. 백작이 만찬 전에 지주를 제 방으로 데려가 잠깐 이야기를 나누기로 되어 있었으니, 조니는 그 두 노인이 함께 나타날 때 응접실에서 제대로 버티고 서 있기가 어려울 것 같다고 느꼈다.\n\n그는 레이디 줄리아와도 아주 잘 지냈다. 그녀는 조금도 젠체하지 않았고 몹시 상냥하게 굴었다. 오라버니가 그녀에게 이야기를 죄다 들려주었으므로, 그녀 역시 오라버니 못지않게 저 끔찍한 크로즈비라는 사내 대신 릴리에게 다른 남편을 마련해 주고 싶어 안달이었다. “그 아이는 벗어난 것만도 아주 운이 좋은 거예요.” 그녀가 오라버니에게 말했다. “참으로 운이 좋았지요.” 백작도 그 말에 동의하면서, 제가 아끼는 조니가 둘 가운데 훨씬 나은 연인이 되리라는 것이 제 생각이라고 말했다. 그러나 레이디 줄리아는 릴리가 순순히 받아들일지에 대해 미심쩍어했다. “하지만 시어도어, 그이는 아직 한동안은 릴리언 데일 양 본인에게 그 이야기를 꺼내서는 안 돼요.”\n\n“그래.” 백작이 말했다. “한 달쯤은 안 되지.”\n\n“여섯 달을 잠자코 있을 수 있다면 그이에게 더 나은 기회가 올 거예요.” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다.\n\n“저런! 그때까지는 다른 누가 벌써 그 아이를 채 갔을 게다.” 백작이 말했다.\n\n이에 대해 레이디 줄리아는 그저 고개를 저었을 뿐이었다.\n\n조니는 성탄절에 예배가 끝난 뒤 어머니에게 건너갔고, 어머니와 누이에게서 큰 대접을 받았다. 그리고 어머니는 백작의 식탁에서 어떻게 처신해야 하는지를 여러 가지로 단단히 일러 주었는데, 구두와 속옷 같은 자잘한 데까지 내려갔다. 그러나 조니는 저택에서 이미, 결국 사람들이 살아가는 방식이란 남들이 여기는 것만큼 그리 다르지 않다는 것을 느끼기 시작한 뒤였다. 레이디 줄리아의 몸가짐이 로퍼 부인의 그것과 똑같지야 않았다. 그러나 그녀도 버턴 크레슨트에서 차를 우리던 것과 거의 같은 방식으로 차를 우렸고, 임스는 은제 달걀 받침에 새겨진 귀족의 관 문장에도 아랑곳없이, 적어도 이튿날 아침에는 손을 떨지 않고 달걀을 먹을 수 있다는 것을 알았다. 일요일에 저택 신도석에 앉았을 때에는 신도들이 죄다 저를 쳐다보고 있다고 여겨져 제자리가 아닌 듯한 기분이 들기는 했다. 그러나 성탄절에는 그것도 넘겼고, 설교가 이어지는 동안 푹신한 구석 자리에 아주 편안히 앉아 거의 잠이 들 지경이었다. 그리고 예배가 끝난 뒤 백작과 함께, 그 귀하신 어른이 죽을 지경에 몰려 기어올랐던 그 문까지 걸어가서 제가 몸을 던져 뚫고 나왔던 산울타리를 살펴볼 때에는, 제 지체 높은 길동무를 두고 그가 어떤 식으로 공중제비를 넘었는지 놀려 대며 아주 스스럼없이 굴었다. 그러나 젊은 사람이 저보다 나이 많고 지체 높은 이에게 스스럼없이 굴 때에는 두 가지 방식이 있다는 것을 늘 기억해 두어야 한다.—기분 좋은 방식과 거슬리는 방식이다. 뒤쪽을 시험해 볼 만한 것이 조니의 천성에 있었더라면 백작은 당장에 등을 곧추세웠을 터이고 그 놀이도 끝났을 것이다. 그러나 그렇게 하는 것은 조니의 천성에 없었고, 그러기에 백작은 그를 좋아했던 것이다.\n\n마침내 화요일 만찬 시각이, 적어도 지주가 저택에 얼굴을 내밀어 달라고 청받은 시각이 되었다. 임스는 제 후원자와 미리 약속해 둔 대로 그 면담이 끝날 때까지는 내려가 얼굴을 보이지 않았다. 두 사람의 의논 자리에 함께 있었던 레이디 줄리아가 지주를 맞기로 했고, 그런 다음 하인이 그에게 백작의 방으로 들어가시라고 청하기로 되어 있었다. 셋이 함께 짜고서 아름다우리만치 풋풋하고 싱그러운 열심으로 계획을 세우고 꾀를 꾸미는 모양은 보기에 사랑스러웠다.\n\n“그 사람은 마음만 먹으면 늙은 막대기처럼 뻣뻣하고 심술궂게 굴 수 있다네.” 백작이 지주를 두고 말했다. “그러니 우리는 그 사람의 결을 거슬러 문질러 대지 않도록 조심해야 하네.”\n\n“저는 내려가서 그분께 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 모르겠습니다.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“그냥 악수만 하시고 아무 말씀도 하지 마세요.” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다.\n\n“내가 그 사람 마음을 누그러뜨릴 만한 포트와인을 좀 내겠네.” 백작이 말했다. “그러고 나서 저녁에 그가 어떤지 보세.”\n\n임스는 지주의 작은 무개마차 바퀴 소리를 듣고 몸을 떨었다. 이런 꾀에 대해서는 아무것도 모르는 지주는 이내 레이디 줄리아와 마주 앉았고, 들어선 지 이 분도 채 되지 않아 백작의 사실로 이끌려 갔다. “그러지요.” 그가 말했다. “그러고말고요.” 그러고서 그는 하인을 따라갔다. 백작은 그가 들어설 때 방 한가운데 서 있었는데, 그 둥글고 발그레한 얼굴은 그대로 좋은 기분의 그림이었다.\n\n“와 주어서 참 반갑소, 데일.” 그가 말했다. “긴히 할 말이 좀 있소.”\n\n마음으로 보나 몸가짐으로 보나 백작만큼 가벼운 사람이 아니었던 데일 씨는 주인이 내미는 손을 잡고 고개를 살짝 숙였으니, 무슨 이야기든 들을 뜻이 있다는 표시였다.\n\n“전에도 말한 것 같소만,” 백작이 말을 이었다. “젊은 존 임스가 여기 내려와 있소. 다만 내일이면 돌아가야 하오. 관청에서 그를 놓아 줄 수가 없다니 말이오. 아주 좋은 친구요.—내가 판단할 수 있는 한으로는 여간 훌륭한 젊은이가 아니오. 나 자신이 그에게 아주 반해 버렸소.”\n\n이에 대해 데일 씨는 별말을 하지 않았다. 그는 자리에 앉아 임스 집안 전체에 대한 제 호의를 두루뭉술한 말로 나타냈다.\n\n“데일, 당신도 알다시피 나는 말재주가 몹시 없는 사람이오. 그러니 지금 하려는 말을 두고 빙빙 돌리지 않겠소. 물론 우리 모두 그 악당 크로즈비 이야기를, 그리고 그자가 당신 조카딸 릴리언을 어떻게 대했는지를 들어 알고 있소.”\n\n“그자는 악당이오.—순전한 악당이오. 그러나 그 이야기는 적게 할수록 좋소. 그런 일로 처녀의 이름을 입에 올리는 것은 좋지 못한 일이오.”\n\n“하지만 데일, 지금 이 자리에서는 내가 그 이야기를 꺼내야만 하겠소. 가엾은 어린것! 나는 그 아이를 위로할 수만 있다면 무슨 일이든 하겠소! 그리고 그 아이를 위로할 무언가가 이루어질 수 있기를 바라오. 크로즈비가 그 아이를 보기 훨씬 전부터 저 젊은이가 그 아이를 사랑하고 있었다는 것을 알고 있소?”\n\n“무어라고요.—존 임스가!”\n\n“그렇소, 존 임스요. 그리고 나는 그를 위해서라도, 당신이 댁에 묵게 했던 그 무뢰한을 만나기 전에 그가 먼저 그 아이의 마음을 얻었더라면 하고 진심으로 바라오.”\n\n“사람이 그런 일을 어찌할 수는 없는 노릇이오, 드 게스트.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“그렇소, 그렇소, 그렇소! 세상에는 그런 사내들이 있고, 한눈에 알아본다는 것은 될 일이 아니오. 그자는 내 조카의 벗이었고, 나는 내 조카에게 잘못이 있다고 말할 생각도 없소. 그러나 나는 바라오.—그저 바랄 뿐이라고 말하는 것이오.—그 아이가 이 젊은이의 마음이 어떠한지를 먼저 알았더라면 하고 말이오.”\n\n“하지만 그 아이가 당신처럼 그를 좋게 여기지는 않았을지도 모르오.”\n\n“그는 여간 잘생긴 젊은이가 아니오. 몸매가 곧고 가슴팍이 넓으며, 착하고 정직한 눈과 젊은이다운 제대로 된 용기를 가졌소. 춤추는 원숭이처럼 젠체하는 법은 배운 적이 없으나, 나는 그 때문에 그가 오히려 더 낫다고 생각하오.”\n\n“하지만 이제 와서는 너무 늦었소, 드 게스트.”\n\n“아니, 아니오. 바로 거기가 요점이오. 너무 늦어서는 안 되오! 악당 하나가 그 아이를 속였다고 해서 그 아이가 제 한평생을 잃어서야 되겠소. 물론 그 아이는 아파할 것이오. 지금 당장이야 새 연인 이야기를 그 아이에게 꺼낸다는 것이 될 일이 아니겠지요. 그러나 데일, 그때는 오리다. 그때는 오리다.—때는 언제나 오는 법이오.”\n\n“당신과 나에게는 온 적이 없소.” 지주가 그 메마른 뺨에 웃음이랄 것도 없는 아주 희미한 웃음을 띠며 말했다. 두 사람의 삶의 이야기는 여기까지 같았다. 각자 사랑했고, 각자 뜻을 이루지 못했으며, 그러고서 각자 평생을 홀로 지냈다.\n\n“아니, 왔소.” 백작이 제가 하는 말에 적잖은 감정을, 낭만이라고까지 할 만한 것을 실어 말했다. “우리는 저마다 제 나름의 방식으로 우리 빛살을 다시 손질했고, 우리 삶도 황량하지만은 않았소. 그러나 그 아이로 말하면—당신과 그 어머니는 언젠가 그 아이가 시집가는 것을 보고 싶어 할 것 아니오.”\n\n“나는 그런 생각을 해 본 적이 없소.”\n\n“하지만 나는 당신이 그 생각을 해 주기를 바라오. 나는 이 친구에게 이롭도록 당신의 마음을 끌고 싶소. 그러자면 나도 당신에게 아주 터놓고 말해야겠소. 당신은 그 아이에게 무언가를 떼어 줄 생각이오?”\n\n“나도 잘 모르겠소, 정말이지.” 지주가 그런 물음에 거의 언짢아하며 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면, 당신이 주든 주지 않든 나는 그에게 무언가를 주겠소.” 백작이 말했다. “내가 그에 관해 이런 물음을 던져도 될 만한 자리에 나 자신을 놓아 둘 작정이 아니었다면, 나는 이 일에 감히 끼어들지 않았을 것이오.” 그러고서 그 귀족은 말하면서 제 키를 온전히 세워 몸을 곧추 폈다. “그런 혼인이 이루어질 수 있다면, 그것은 돈으로 따져 보더라도 당신 조카딸에게 나쁜 혼인이 되지 않을 것이오. 나는 그에게 주는 것이 기쁘겠소. 그러나 그 아이가 내가 주는 것을 함께 나눌 수 있다면 더욱 기쁘겠소.”\n\n“그 아이는 당신에게 크게 고마워해야 마땅하오.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“그 아이가 젊은 임스를 알게 된다면 고마워하리라고 나는 생각하오. 그리하게 될 날이 오기를 바라오. 당신과 내가 두 사람이 함께 행복하게 지내는 것을 보게 되기를, 그리고 그리되도록 내가 거든 것을 두고 당신도 나에게 고마워하게 되기를 바라오. 이제 레이디 줄리아에게로 갈까요?” 백작은 자기가 온전히 성공하지는 못했다는 것을, 제 제안이 얼마쯤 차갑게 받아들여졌다는 것을 느끼고 있었고, 제 가장 좋은 포트와인의 힘을 빌리더라도 그날 더 좋은 일이 이루어지리라는 기대는 크게 품지 않았다.\n\n“잠깐만 기다려 주시오.” 지주가 말했다. “나는 도무지 빨리 말할 수가 없는 일들이 있는데, 이것도 분명 그 가운데 하나인 듯하오. 허락해 주신다면 당신이 한 말을 두고 곰곰 생각해 본 뒤에 다시 만나 뵙겠소.”\n\n“그러시오, 그러시오.”\n\n“그러나 이 일에서 당신이 맡아 준 몫에 대해, 당신의 그 크나큰 너그러움과 어진 마음에 대해 나는 가장 따뜻한 감사를 드리고자 하오.” 그러고서 지주는 깊이 고개를 숙였고, 백작에 앞서 방을 나섰다.\n\n드 게스트 경은 여전히 자기가 성공하지 못했다고 느꼈다. 지주의 성격과 별난 데를 헤아려 보면, 그런 이야기를 처음 꺼낸 자리에서 눈에 띄는 성공을 거두기란 아마도 있을 수 없는 일이었다고 우리는 말해도 좋으리라. 그는 스스로 무게 있는 일에 대해서는 결코 빨리 말할 수 없다고 했으나, 그런 일에 대해서는 빨리 생각할 수가 없다고 했더라면 제 성격을 더 정확히 그려 냈을 것이다. 아무튼 백작은 실망했다. 그러나 지주의 마음속을 읽을 수 있었더라면 그 실망은 덜했을 것이다. 데일 씨는 자기가 잘 대접받고 있다는 것을, 그리고 지금 벌어지고 있는 이 애씀이 제게 딸린 사람들에게 친절을 베풀려는 뜻에서 나온 것임을 넉넉히 알고 있었다. 그러나 감정을 드러내고 감사의 말을 재빨리 내놓는 것은 그의 천성이 아니었다. 그리하여 그는 차갑고 담담한 얼굴로 응접실에 들어섰고, 임스와 레이디 줄리아로 하여금 아무 소득도 없었구나 하고 여기게 만들었다.\n\n“안녕하십니까, 어르신?” 조니가 어딘가 얼떨떨한 태도로, 미리 익혀 둔 대로 하면서도 조금도 침착하지 못하게 그에게 다가서며 말했다.\n\n“잘 있었나, 임스?” 지주가 아주 차가운 목소리로 말했다. 그리고 만찬 준비가 되었다는 알림이 있을 때까지 그 밖에는 아무 말도 오가지 않았다.\n\n“데일, 당신이 포트와인을 마시는 줄은 알고 있소.” 레이디 줄리아가 자리를 뜨자 백작이 말했다. “이게 마음에 들지 않는다고 하면, 나는 당신이 포트와인에 대해 아무것도 모른다고 말하겠소.”\n\n“아! 이건 20년 산이로군.” 지주가 맛을 보며 말했다.\n\n“그야 그렇고말고.” 백작이 말했다. “나는 운이 좋아서 그걸 일찌감치 손에 넣었고, 삼십 년 동안 자리를 옮기지 않았소. 나는 당신처럼 한 모금에 대뜸 알아보는 사람에게 이걸 내놓는 것이 좋소. 그런데 저기 있는 내 벗 조니에게는—저 친구한테는 이게 헛되이 버려지는 셈이지.”\n\n“아닙니다, 백작님, 그렇지 않습니다. 저는 이게 여간 좋은 것이 아니라고 생각합니다.”\n\n“여간 좋지 않다니! 샴페인도 그렇고 생강 맥주도 그렇고 눈깔사탕도 그렇지, 그런 것을 좋아하는 사람들에게는 말일세. 자네, 입안에 절인 오렌지를 반쪽이나 물고서 술맛을 알 수 있다고 나한테 말할 셈인가?”\n\n“저 친구에게도 머지않아 그때가 올 거요.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“20년 산은 저 친구가 우리만큼 나이를 먹었을 때에는 저 친구에게 오지 않을 거요.” 백작이 그렇게 말하면서, 그때가 되면 그 시대에 살아 있을 노인들에게는 60년 산이 지금 제가 아끼는 이 술이 저에게 그러하듯 놀라운 것이 되리라는 것을 잊고 있었다.\n\n그 좋은 술은 얼마쯤 지주의 마음을 누그러뜨렸다. 그러나 당연한 일로 새 혼담에 대해서는 더 아무 말도 나오지 않았다. 다만 백작은 데일 씨가 제 젊은 벗에게 정중하게, 친절하다고 할 만큼 대해 주는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 이따금 런던 생활에 대해 이것저것 묻기도 하고, 소득세 사무국의 일에 대해 무어라 말하기도 했던 것이다.\n\n“일이 고됩니다.” 임스가 말했다. “줄 아래에 이름이 적히기라도 하면 큰 소동이 벌어집니다. 사람을 불러들여서는 마치 은행이라도 턴 것처럼 쳐다보지요. 그러면서도 다섯 시까지 붙들어 두는 것은 아무렇지도 않게 여깁니다.”\n\n“그런데 점심을 먹고 신문을 읽는 데는 시간이 얼마나 나나?” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“십 분도 안 됩니다. 저희는 스무 사람이 신문 한 부를 하루의 절반 동안 나누어 봅니다. 한 사람에 꼭 구 분씩이지요. 그리고 점심으로 말하면, 저희는 잉크에 적신 비스킷 하나를 먹을 뿐입니다.”\n\n“잉크에 적신다니!” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“그런 셈이 되지요. 그걸 우물거리는 동안에도 글을 쓰고 있어야 하니까요.”\n\n“나는 자네 이야기를 죄다 듣고 있네.” 백작이 말했다. “래플 버플 경은 내 오랜 벗이거든.”\n\n“그분은 아직 제 이름도 들어 보신 적이 없을 겁니다.” 조니가 말했다. “한데 그분을 정말 잘 아십니까, 드 게스트 경?”\n\n“삼십 년째 얼굴을 못 보았네만, 알기는 알았지.”\n\n“저희는 그분을 늙은 허플 스커플이라고 부릅니다.”\n\n“허플 스커플이라! 하하하! 그 사람은 늘 허플 스커플이었지. 시끄럽고 젠체하고 머릿속은 텅 빈 친구였네. 하지만 자네 앞에서 이런 말을 해서는 안 되겠지, 젊은이. 자, 응접실로 가세.”\n\n“그래서 그이가 무어라고 하던가요?” 지주가 돌아가자마자 레이디 줄리아가 물었다.\n\n숨길 것이 없었으므로 그 물음은 조니가 있는 자리에서 던져졌다.\n\n“글쎄, 별말은 하지 않았다. 그런데 그 사람에게서 나온 것으로 보면 그것도 좋은 조짐으로 여겨야 하지. 생각해 보고 나를 다시 만나겠다는구나. 조니, 자네는 고개를 들고 있게. 그리고 자네 편에 서 줄 벗이 없지는 않다는 것을 잊지 말게. 겁 많은 마음으로는 결코 아름다운 여인을 얻지 못하는 법일세.”\n\n이튿날 아침 일곱 시에 임스는 돌아가는 길에 올랐고, 열두 시에는 제 책상 앞에 앉아 있었다.—소득세 사무국의 제 상전과 한 약속대로였다.\n\n# 제34장. 격투\n\n존 임스가 열두 시 정각에 제 사무실에 나와 있었다고 나는 앞서 말한 바 있다. 그러나 그가 그곳에 닿기 전에 한 가지 사건이 있었으니, 지금 이야기되고 있는 이 연대기에서 매우 중요한 일이었다—어찌나 중요한지 얼마간 세세하게 그려 두는 것이 반드시 필요할 지경이다.\n\n드 게스트 경은 릴리 데일과 그녀의 지금 처지를 두고 임스와 나눈 여러 차례의 이야기에서 언제나 크로즈비를 가장 격렬한 혐오로 말해 왔다. “그자는 빌어먹을 불한당이야.” 백작은 이렇게 말했고, 말하는 동안 그 동그란 눈에서는 불길이 뿜어져 나왔다. 그런데 백작은 이런 낱말들에 보통 붙는 뜻으로 저주하고 욕설을 늘어놓는 사람이 결코 아니었다. 그가 위에 인용한 것과 같은 표현을 쓸 때에는, 어떤 의미에서 제가 한 말을 그대로 뜻한 것이라고 여겨야 마땅했다. 실제로도 그러하였으니, 크로즈비가 제 처신으로 말미암아 가장 고약한 종류의 불한당 짓에 어울리는 벌로서 내려질 만한 온갖 단죄를 다 받아 마땅하게 되었다는 뜻이었다.\n\n“그자는 목이 부러져야 마땅합니다.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 그건 나도 모르겠네.” 백작이 말했다. “요즘 세상은 하도 얌전해져서 몸에 매질을 가하는 벌은 유행이 지나 버린 모양이야. 다른 벌이 그 자리를 대신하기라도 했다면 나도 그리 개의치 않겠네만. 그런데 내가 보기에는 크로즈비 같은 불한당이 이제는 아예 아무 상처도 입지 않고 빠져나갈 수 있게 된 것 같단 말이지.”\n\n“아직 빠져나가지는 못했습니다.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“괜히 남의 일에 손가락을 들이밀어 바보 꼴이 되지는 말게.” 백작이 말했다. 크로즈비가 저지른 악행을 누군가 격한 방식으로 갚아 주어야 마땅했다면, 그 복수자는 백작의 조카인 버나드 데일이었어야 했다. 백작도 그렇게 느꼈으나, 사정이 이러하고 보니 그런 격렬한 복수는 없는 편이 낫겠다고 생각하는 쪽으로 기울었다. “내가 젊었을 적에는 사정이 달랐지.” 그는 속으로 중얼거렸다. 그러나 임스는 백작의 말투에서 그 말이 그의 생각과 꼭 들어맞지는 않는다는 것을 알아챘고, 크로즈비는 아직 빠져나가지 못했노라고 스스로에게 몇 번이고 되뇌었다.\n\n그는 게스트윅에서 기차에 올랐는데, 제복을 입은 백작의 마부가 배웅을 나와 있었던 터라 일등칸 표를 끊었다. 혼자였더라면 더 싼 칸을 탔을 것이다. 참으로 마음이 약한 짓이 아니었던가? 게다가 좀스럽고 옹졸하지 않은가? 벗이여, 그대는 그 나이에 같은 짓을 하지 않았으리라고 말할 수 있는가? 그의 나이의 곱절이 된 지금이라도 같은 짓을 하지 않으리라고 아주 자신할 수 있는가? 어찌 되었든 조니 임스는 그 어리석은 짓을 했고, 덤으로 제복 입은 마부에게 반 크라운까지 쥐여 주었다.\n\n“머지않아 또 내려오시게 될 겁니다요, 존 나리.” 마부가 말했는데, 임스 씨가 저택에서 아주 스스럼없이 지내도 되는 손님이라는 것을 잘 아는 눈치였다.\n\n그는 객차 안에서 곧 깊이 잠이 들었고, 기차가 바체스터 분기역에 멈춰 설 때까지 깨지 않았다.\n\n“바체스터에서 오는 상행 열차를 기다리는 중입니다, 손님.” 차장이 말했다. “늘 늦지요.” 그러고 나서 그는 다시 잠이 들었다가, 몇 분 뒤 누군가 몹시 서둘러 객차로 들어오는 바람에 잠이 깼다. 마침 그 자리에 있던 노선 관리인들이 본선 승객들을 더 이상 기다리게 해서는 안 되겠다고 마음을 굳힌 참에 지선 열차가 들어온 것이었다. 그리하여 남자와 여자와 짐을 옮기는 일이 몹시 부산하게 이루어졌고, 이제 새 자리를 잡는 이들은 주위를 둘러볼 겨를조차 거의 없었다. 볼과 턱께가 벌겋게 달아오른 늙은 신사 하나가 조니의 객실에 맨 먼저 들어왔는데, 그때까지 조니는 그 칸을 늙은 부인 한 사람과만 함께 쓰고 있었다. 늙은 신사는 서두르게 되었다는 이유로 모두에게 욕을 퍼부으면서도, 객실 안으로 제대로 들어서지는 않고 발판에 선 채 문간을 막고 있었다.\n\n“자, 이제 좀 한가하시거든요, 선생.” 노인 뒤에서 어떤 목소리가 말했고, 그 소리에 임스는 대뜸 흠칫하며 자리에서 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n“나는 조금도 한가하지 않소.” 노인이 말했다. “그리고 내가 아는 한 다리를 부러뜨릴 생각도 없고.”\n\n“천천히 하십시오, 어르신.” 차장이 말했다.\n\n“그럴 참이오.” 노인은 이렇게 말하며 열린 문에서 가장 가까운 구석 자리, 늙은 부인의 맞은편에 앉았다. 그제야 임스는 처음 말한 사람이 크로즈비였다는 것을, 그리고 그가 지금 객차에 오르고 있다는 것을 똑똑히 보았다.\n\n크로즈비는 첫눈에 늙은 신사와 늙은 부인밖에 보지 못했고, 곧장 비어 있는 구석 자리로 갔다. 그는 우산과 세면 가방을 챙기느라 분주했고, 밀치고 서두르는 통에 조금 허둥거리고 있었다. 존 임스가 제 맞은편에 있다는 것을 알아차렸을 때 객차는 이미 움직이고 있었다. 임스는 저도 모르게 다리를 오므려 그와 닿지 않게 하고 있었다. 그는 제 얼굴이 아주 벌게졌다는 것을 느꼈고, 사실을 말하자면 이마에는 땀이 배어 나와 있었다. 그것은 대단한 순간이었다—당장 닥친 곤경으로 보아도 대단하고, 무언가를 할 기회로 보아도 대단한 순간이었다. 원수에게 제 정체가 알아보이는 그 첫 순간에 그는 어떻게 처신할 것이며, 그다음에는 무엇을 할 것인가?\n\n크로즈비 역시 안면 있는 어떤 백작 댁에서 성탄절을 보내고 마찬가지로 제 사무실로 돌아가는 길이었다는 것은 굳이 설명할 필요도 없겠다. 한 가지 점에서는 그가 가엾은 임스보다 훨씬 운이 좋았으니, 사랑하는 여인의 미소로 행복을 누렸기 때문이다. 알렉산드리나와 백작부인은 이제 드 코시 가문이라는 귀한 집안에 딸린 길든 세간붙이를 다루듯 그를 다루며 살랑살랑 그의 둘레를 맴돌았고, 그리하여 그는 그 이름 높은 집안의 안살림 속내에까지 발을 들이게 되었다. 레이디 덤벨로의 시중을 들라고 새로 들였던 하인 둘은 자취를 감추었다. 샴페인이 마르지 않는 시내처럼 흐르던 것도 그쳤다. 레이디 로지나는 칩거에서 나와 그에게 쉴 새 없이 설교를 늘어놓았다. 레이디 마거리타는 그에게 절약에 관한 가르침을 몇 가지 베풀었다. 아너러블 존은 얼마 전에 다툰 일이 있는데도 그에게서 오 파운드를 빌려 갔다. 아너러블 조지는 돌아오는 오월에 누이 집에 와서 묵겠다고 약조했다. 백작은 장인의 특권을 부려 그를 바보라고 불렀다. 레이디 알렉산드리나는 이것도 해야 하고 저것도 해야 한다고 사뭇 쌀쌀한 목소리로 한 번 이상 그에게 일렀다. 그리고 백작부인은 제 입에서 떨어지는 말이라면 무엇이든 따르는 것이 그의 당연한 도리라는 듯이 그에게 분부를 내렸다. 이런 것들이 그의 성탄절 낙이었다. 그리고 이제 그 즐거움을 다 누리고 돌아오는 길에, 그는 철도 객차 안에서 조니 임스와 맞대면하게 된 것이다!\n\n두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤고, 크로즈비는 고개를 살짝 숙여 보였다. 임스는 이에 아무런 아는 체도 하지 않고 상대의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다. 크로즈비는 두 사람이 서로 모르는 사이로 지내기로 되었음을 곧 알아차렸고, 그렇게 되어 아주 다행이라고 여겼다. 그 많은 근심 가운데 존 임스의 적의 따위는 대수롭지 않았다. 우리 벗은 잔뜩 당황한 기색을 보였으나 그는 조금도 당황한 기색을 보이지 않았다. 그는 가방을 열어 책을 한 권 꺼내더니, 맞은편에 앉은 사내를 전혀 모르는 사람인 양 곧 그 책에 깊이 빠져들어 제 공부를 이어 갔다. 그의 마음이 책에서 벗어나 달아나지 않았다고는 말하지 않겠다. 실로 그가 생각하지 않을 수 없는 일들이 많았기 때문이다. 그러나 그 마음이 존 임스에게로 되돌아가지는 않았다. 참으로, 객차가 패딩턴에 닿았을 때에는 그를 거의 잊고 있었고, 가방을 손에 들고 객차에서 내릴 때에는 그 사람 때문에 마음 쓰이는 일이라고는 아주 조금도 없었다.\n\n그러나 임스 쪽은 그렇지 않았다. 여정의 매 순간이 그에게는, 우연이 원수를 제 손 닿는 곳까지 데려다 놓은 지금 무엇을 할 것인가 하는 생각으로 빼곡했다. 그토록 골똘히 생각한 나머지 그는 아주 비참해졌다. 그러면서도 객차가 멈추었을 때까지 마음을 정하지 못했다. 크로즈비가 제 앞에 나타난 뒤로 그의 얼굴은 줄곧 땀에 젖어 있었고, 손발도 좀처럼 제 뜻대로 놀려지지 않았다. 그에게 큰 기회가 찾아왔건만, 그는 제 자신을 하도 믿지 못하여 이 기회를 제대로 쓰지 못하리라는 것을 거의 알고 있었다. 객차 안에서 그는 두 번 세 번 크로즈비의 목으로 달려들 뻔했으나, 저 늙은 부인이 보는 앞에서 그런 짓을 하면 세상과 경찰이 제 편을 들어 주지 않으리라는 생각에 스스로를 억눌렀다.\n\n그러나 크로즈비가 그에게 등을 돌리고 걸어 나가자, 그는 반드시 무언가를 해야만 했다. 그자를 두들겨 주는 것이 상책이라고 그토록 떠들어 놓고서 그냥 놓아 보낼 수는 없었다. 그 밖의 어떤 망신이라도 그것보다는 나을 터였다. 그리하여 원수가 저보다 빠를까 두려워 그는 서둘러 그 뒤를 따라 나갔고, 크로즈비가 몸을 돌려 객차 쪽을 마주 볼 겨를만 겨우 준 채 그에게 덮쳤다. “이 빌어먹을 악당아!” 그는 비명을 지르듯 외쳤다. “이 빌어먹을 악당아!” 그러고는 그의 목을 움켜쥐고 몸째 그에게 달려들었으며, 이글거리는 눈으로 그를 잡아먹을 듯이 노려보았다.\n\n승강장의 사람들은 그리 빽빽하지 않았으나, 이 짧은 연극에 아주 훌륭한 구경꾼 노릇을 할 만큼은 넉넉히 있었다. 크로즈비는 질겁하여 한두 걸음 물러섰는데, 임스가 덮쳐 온 무게 때문에 그 물러섬은 한결 빨라졌다. 그는 원수의 손아귀에서 제 목을 빼내려 애썼으나 그것은 전혀 되지 않았다. 그러나 그 순간만큼은 어떻게든 실제로 한 대도 맞지 않고 넘겼는데, 그 점에서 그가 무사했던 것은 제가 애쓴 덕이라기보다 임스가 서툴렀던 덕이었다. 경찰이 어떻다는 말을 그는 겨우 입 밖에 낼 수 있었고, 물론 곧바로 그 공무원들을 불러오라는 소리가 터져 나왔다. 삼 분쯤 지나 순경 셋이 짐꾼 여섯의 도움을 받아 우리의 가엾은 벗 조니를 붙잡았다. 그러나 그것은 크로즈비의 뜻대로 되기에는 충분히 빠르지 못했다. 불시에 당한 구경꾼들이 두 싸움꾼을 스미스 씨의 책 가판대까지 밀려가도록 내버려 둔 사이, 임스는 신문 더미 한복판에 원수를 넘어뜨렸고, 제 기세가 지나쳤던 탓에 저는 노란 표지 일 실링짜리 소설 무더기 속으로 나동그라졌다. 그러나 넘어지면서도 그는 제 주먹으로 크로즈비의 오른쪽 눈에 한 방을 먹이는 데 성공했다—제대로 들어간 한 방이었다. 그리하여 크로즈비는 어느 모로 보나 두들겨 맞은 셈이 되었다.\n\n“빌—어먹을 악당, 무뢰한, 불한당!” 조니는 순경들에게 끌려 나가면서 남아 있는 목청을 다 짜내어 소리쳤다. “당신들이 알기만 한다면—저놈이 무슨—짓을 했는지.” 그러나 그러는 동안에도 순경들은 그를 단단히 붙들고 있었다.\n\n당연한 일이지만 사람들의 동정은 처음에 크로즈비 쪽으로 쏠렸다. 그는 습격을 당했고, 그 습격은 임스에게서 나왔다. 영국인의 가슴속에는 잘 짜인 질서에 대한 사랑이 하도 굳건해서, 이 사실만으로도 순경 셋과 짐꾼 여섯을 도우러 스무 명의 기사가 나서기에 넉넉했다. 그러니 임스로서는 설령 원했다 한들 달아날 가망이라고는 조금도 없었다. 그러나 그는 달아나기를 원하지 않았다. 다만 한 가지 슬픔이 지금 그를 좀먹고 있었다. 그가 느끼기로 자기는 크로즈비를 덮치기는 했으나 헛되이 덮쳤을 뿐이었다. 기회를 얻고서도 그것을 잘못 써 버린 것이다. 그는 그 다행스러운 한 방을 조금도 의식하지 못했고, 원수의 눈이 이미 부어올라 감겨 버렸다는 것도, 한 시간만 지나면 그 눈이 제 모자만큼이나 새까매지리라는 것도 전혀 알지 못했다.\n\n“저놈은 빌—어먹을 무뢰한이오!” 순경들과 짐꾼들이 그를 이리저리 끌고 다니는 동안 임스가 소리쳐 뱉었다. “당신들은 저놈이 무슨 짓을 했는지 모릅니다.”\n\n“그야 우리는 모르지요.” 고참 순경이 말했다. “하지만 당신이 무슨 짓을 했는지는 압니다. 여보게, 부셔스, 그 신사분은 어디 계신가? 우리와 함께 가시는 편이 좋겠는데.”\n\n크로즈비는 다른 순경 하나와 짐꾼 두엇의 손에 신문 더미 속에서 일으켜 세워졌고, 그를 알아보는 데다 그가 코시 성에서 올라왔다는 것까지 아는 열차 차장의 시중도 받았다. 서너 명의 구경꾼도 그의 둘레에 서 있었고, 당장 거머리를 붙이자고 권하는 자비로운 의사 한 사람도 함께 있었다. 제 뜻대로 할 수 있었다면 그는 조용히 제 갈 길을 갔을 것이고, 임스도 그렇게 하도록 내버려 두었을 것이다. 큰 불행이 그에게 닥쳤으나, 자기를 덮친 사내를 법에 넘긴다고 해서 그 불행을 조금이라도 덜 수 있는 것은 결코 아니었다. 이 일이 되도록 사람들 입에 덜 오르내리게 하는 것이야말로 그가 애써야 할 바였다. 임스를 가두고 벌금을 물리고 치안 판사에게 꾸지람을 듣게 한들 무엇하겠는가? 그런다고 그의 재앙이 조금이라도 가벼워지지는 않을 터였다. 그가 그 공격을 막아 내고 원수를 이길 수 있었더라면, 시퍼렇게 멍든 눈을 얻어맞는 대신 안겨 줄 수 있었더라면, 그야말로 그는 클럽에서 이 일을 웃어넘길 수 있었을 것이고, 애초에 그가 저지른 죄도 무예에서 거둔 승리로 얼마간 덮였을 것이다. 그러나 그런 행운은 그의 것이 아니었다. 그러나 그는 무엇을 할 것인지 그 자리에서 당장 정해야만 했다.\n\n“이자를 저희가 붙잡아 두었습니다, 나리.” 부셔스가 모자에 손을 대며 말했다. 크로즈비가 제법 큰 인물이고, 코시 성에 자주 드나드는 손님이며, 런던 상류 세계에서 명망과 지위가 있는 사람이라는 것이 차장을 통해 알려진 터였다. “지금 패딩턴에 치안 판사들이 나와 앉아 있을 겁니다, 나리—아니면 저희가 갈 때쯤이면 나와 있을 겁니다요.”\n\n그럴 즈음 어떤 대단한 철도 관리가 현장에 나타나 소동의 자초지종을 파악했다—이마에 기관차 여러 대의 무게를 얹고 있는 듯한 근엄한 관리요, 그를 보기만 해도 담배 피우던 자들은 시가를 떨어뜨리고 짐꾼들은 육 펜스짜리 앞에서 주먹을 오므릴 만한 사람이었으며, 턱을 곧추세우고 걸음이 빠르며 챙을 공들여 치켜올린 위엄 있는 모자를 잘 손질해 쓴 대단한 인물이었다. 이 사람이 바로 승강장 감독관으로, 순경들보다도 위에 서는 이였다.\n\n“제 방으로 들어오시지요, 크로즈비 씨.” 그가 말했다. “스터브스, 저 사람을 데리고 들어오게.” 그리하여 크로즈비는 달리 어떤 처신을 할지 마음을 정하기도 전에 감독관의 방 안에 들어와 있었고, 차장과, 조니 임스를 사이에 끼고 데려온 순경 둘이 함께였다.\n\n“이게 다 무슨 일인가?” 감독관은 여전히 모자를 쓴 채 말했다. 제 위엄이 얼마나 그 물건의 매무새에 힘입고 있는지를 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 말하면서 그는 있는 대로 험한 낯빛으로 죄인을 노려보았다. “크로즈비 씨, 우리 승강장에서 이런 난폭한 일을 당하시게 되어 참으로 죄송합니다.”\n\n“당신은 저자가 무슨 짓을 했는지 모릅니다.” 조니가 말했다. “저자는 세상에 살아 있는 가장 빌어먹을 악당이오. 저자는 부수어 놓았단 말이오—” 그러나 그는 거기서 말을 멈추었다. 그 빌어먹을 악당이 아름다운 젊은 아가씨의 마음을 부수어 놓았다고 감독관에게 말하려던 참이었으나, 그런 자리에서 릴리 데일을 더 꼭 집어 입에 올리지는 않겠다고 마음을 고쳐먹었다.\n\n“이 사람이 누구인지 아십니까, 크로즈비 씨?” 감독관이 말했다.\n\n“아, 압니다.” 이미 눈이 시퍼렇게 물들어 가고 있는 크로즈비가 말했다. “소득세 사무국의 서기이고, 이름은 임스요. 이 일은 내게 맡겨 두시는 편이 좋겠소.”\n\n그러나 감독관은 그 자리에서 곧바로 “소득세 사무국—임스”라는 말을 제 수첩에 적었다. “우리 승강장에서 그런 소동이 벌어졌는데 그냥 보아 넘길 수는 없습니다. 나는 이 일을 이사들 앞에 올릴 겁니다. 참으로 망신스러운 일이오, 임스 씨—참으로 망신스러운 일이야.”\n\n그러나 조니는 이때쯤 크로즈비의 눈이 제 아침나절의 수고가 헛되지 않았음을 넉넉히 증명해 주는 꼴이 되어 있다는 것을 알아차렸고, 그에 따라 기운이 솟아오르고 있었다. 이 이야기가 나중에 제게 유리하게 전해지기만 한다면 감독관이든 순경들이든 그는 조금도 개의치 않았다. 크로즈비를 두들겨 주는 것이 그의 목적이었는데, 이제 원수의 얼굴을 바라보며 그는 하늘의 섭리가 제게 어질었노라고 인정했다.\n\n“그건 당신 생각이지요.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“그렇소, 내 생각이오.” 감독관이 말했다. “그리고 나는 이 일을 당신 윗사람들에게 어떻게 알려야 할지도 잘 알고 있소, 젊은이.”\n\n“당신은 이 일을 다 알지 못합니다.” 임스가 말했다. “앞으로도 알게 될 성싶지 않고요. 나는 저 악당을 처음 보는 날 무엇을 할지 이미 마음을 정해 두었고, 이제 그것을 해냈습니다. 기차 안이었더라면 저자는 훨씬 더 험한 꼴을 당했을 텐데, 다만 거기 부인이 한 분 계셨을 뿐이오.”\n\n“크로즈비 씨, 아무래도 이 사람을 치안 판사 앞으로 데려가는 편이 낫겠습니다.”\n\n그러나 이에 크로즈비는 반대했다. 그는 이 일을 어떻게 처리해야 할지 자기가 잘 안다고 감독관에게 장담했으나—실은 바로 그것이 그가 알지 못하는 바였다. 철도 직원 가운데 하나를 시켜 삯마차를 잡아 주고 제 짐을 찾아 주게 할 수 있겠는가? 그는 임스 씨의 무례를 더 겪지 않고 집으로 돌아가기를 몹시 바랐다.\n\n“임스 씨의 무례는 아직 끝나지 않았소, 내가 말해 두지. 온 런던이 이 일을 듣게 될 것이고, 그 까닭도 알게 될 것이오. 당신에게 부끄러움이라는 것이 조금이라도 있다면, 낯을 들고 다니기가 부끄러울 거요.”\n\n가엾은 사내여! 벌이—그것도 넉넉한 벌이—그를 따라잡지 않았다고 누가 말할 수 있겠는가? 당장으로 말하자면 그는 시퍼렇게 멍든 눈을 하고, 소득세 사무국 서기에게 매를 맞았다는 앎을 속에 품은 채 슬그머니 집으로 기어들어야 했다. 그리고 앞날로 말하자면—그는 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시와 혼인해야 할 몸이었다!\n\n그는 승강장으로 다시 나갈 것도 없이 삯마차에 몰래 실려 떠났고, 짐은 부지런한 짐꾼 둘이 그에게 날라다 주었다. 그러나 이 모든 일에도 상한 자존심을 달랠 것은 거의 없었다. 마부에게 마운트 스트리트로 가자고 이르면서 그는 코시 성에서 제 삶에 내디딘 그 한 걸음으로 스스로를 망쳐 놓았다고 느꼈다. 어느 쪽을 돌아보아도 위안이 없었다. “그—놈의 자식!” 그는 삯마차 안에서 거의 소리를 내어 말했다. 그러나 겉으로 낸 목소리로는 임스를 가리켰다 하더라도, 속마음의 저주는 제 자신을 향한 것이었다.\n\n조니는 승강장으로 내려가 제 융단 여행 가방을 찾아가도 좋다는 허락을 받았다. 그런데 젊은 짐꾼 하나가 다가와 그와 벗하듯 굴었다.\n\n“마지막 그 순간에 아주 야무지게 한 대 먹이셨습니다요, 나리. 하지만 저런, 나리, 처음부터 냅다 갈기셨어야지요. 사람 목덜미 깃을 움켜쥐어 봤자 무슨 소용입니까요?”\n\n그때가 열한 시 십오 분이었으나, 그럼에도 임스는 정확히 열두 시에 제 사무실에 나타났다.\n\n# 제35장. 「패자에게 화 있을진저」\n\n크로즈비는 그날 두 가지 약속이 있었다. 하나는 사무실에 나가 제 일을 하는 지극히 당연한 약속이었고, 다른 하나는 이제 그에 못지않게 당연한 일이 되어 가고 있던 약속, 곧 세인트존스우드로 가서 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비와 저녁을 먹는 일이었다. 거울에 비친 제 얼굴을 들여다보니 그 두 약속 가운데 어느 것도 지킬 수 없다는 것이 분명했다. “아이고 저런, 크로즈비 씨.” 하숙집 안주인이 그를 보고는 소리쳤다.\n\n“그래요, 나도 압니다.” 그가 말했다. “사고를 당해서 눈에 멍이 들었소. 여기엔 뭐가 좋겠소?”\n\n“어머나, 사고라니요!” 안주인이 말했다. 그 자국이 다른 사내의 주먹에 생긴 것임을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다. “날고기 한 점이 제일이라고들 합니다요. 하지만 아침 내내 꼬박 대고 있어야 하지요.”\n\n무엇이든 거머리보다는 나았다. 거머리 자국은 오래도록 남아 두고두고 이야깃거리를 만드는 법이니까. 그리하여 크로즈비는 그날 오전의 태반을 날고기를 눈에 대고 앉은 채로 보냈다. 그러나 그렇게 고기를 대고 있는 동안에도 쪽지 두 장은 써야만 했다. 하나는 사무실의 버터웰 씨에게, 다른 하나는 장차 처형이 될 사람에게 보낼 것이었다. 이 재난의 내막을 통째로 감추려 드는 것은 도무지 현명한 짓이 못 된다고 그는 생각했다. 정황의 얼마쯤은 반드시 세상에 알려질 터였기 때문이다. 석탄 통에 걸려 넘어졌다거나 난로 울타리에 부딪혀 얼굴이 찢어졌다고 말했다가는, 사람들은 그가 둘러댔다는 것을 알게 될 것이고, 나아가 그가 둘러댈 만한 무슨 까닭이 있었다는 것까지 알게 될 것이었다. 그래서 그는 어떤 세부에도 제 발이 묶이지 않도록 말을 골라 쪽지를 지었다. 버터웰에게는 사고를 당했다고—아니, 차라리 한바탕 소동을 겪었다고—썼고, 그 통에 얼굴 앞판에 적잖은 손상을 입었다고 썼다. 다음 날에는 남 앞에 내놓을 만한 꼴이 되든 안 되든 사무실에 나갈 작정이었다. 다만 체면을 생각해서 반나절만은 스스로에게 말미를 주는 편이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 그다음 레이디 어밀리아에게도 사고를 당해 조금 다쳤다고 썼다. “대수로운 일은 전혀 아니고 그저 얼굴 모양새에만 관계된 일이라, 하루쯤 집에 들어앉아 있는 편이 낫겠습니다. 일요일에는 꼭 찾아뵙겠습니다. 게이지비더러 일부러 저를 보러 오는 수고는 하지 말라 일러 주십시오. 오늘 이후로는 집에 없을 테니까요.” 게이지비는 마운트 스트리트로 어찌나 자주 찾아오는지, 게다가 게이지비 씨의 사무실이 있는 사우스오들리 스트리트가 마운트 스트리트에서 어찌나 얄궂게 가까운지, 크로즈비는 될 수 있으면 제 몸을 지켜 보자는 심산으로 이 대목을 끼워 넣은 것이었다. 그러고는 자기는 그 누구에게도 집에 없는 것으로 하라고 각별히 일러 두었다. 게이지비가 업무 시간이 끝난 뒤 저를 데리러 와서는 꼼짝 못 하게 붙들어 세인트존스우드로 통째로 끌고 가지나 않을까 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n날고기와 물약과 밤새도록 눈에 얹어 둔 찬물 찜질도, 이튿날 아침 열 시가 되도록 그 흉측한 검푸른 빛깔을 몰아내는 데에는 아무런 효험이 없었다.\n\n“분명히 가라앉기는 했습니다요, 크로즈비 씨. 분명히 가라앉았습니다요.” 하숙집 안주인이 상한 자리를 손가락으로 짚어 보며 말했다. “하지만 그 검은 것이 한순간에 빠지지는 않습니다요, 정말입니다요. 하루만 더 들어앉아 계시면 안 되겠습니까요?”\n\n“하루로 되겠소, 필립스 부인?”\n\n필립스 부인은 그렇게 되리라고 장담할 수는 없었다. “대개는 검은 데에 자잘한 붉은 줄이 죽죽 서고 나서야 빠지지요.” 필립스 부인이 말했다. 멍든 눈에 어찌나 밝던지, 이 여자는 아무래도 권투 선수의 아내였던 모양이다.\n\n“그러면 내일이나 되어야 그렇게 되겠군.” 크로즈비가 괴로운 중에도 짐짓 유쾌한 체하며 말했다.\n\n“사흘째나 되어야지요. 그러고 나서야 차차 저절로 스러집니다요. 거머리가 무슨 소용이 있는 걸 저는 본 적이 없습니다요.”\n\n그는 이튿날도 집에 들어앉아 있었고, 그러고 나서는 멍든 눈이든 무엇이든 그대로 사무실에 나가기로 마음을 정했다. 그날 아침 신문에서 그는 이 일의 전말을 다룬 기사를 보았다. 머지않아 드 C—— 백작의 아리따운 따님을 혼인의 제단으로 이끌게 될 일반위원회 사무국의 C—— 씨가 그레이트웨스턴 철도역 승강장에서 잔악한 인신 공격의 대상이 되었으며, 그로 인해 입은 부상 탓에 방에 들어앉아 지내고 있다는 것이었다. 기사는 이어서, 그 범인이 감히 같은 숙녀분에게 눈길을 들었던 것으로 여겨지며 그 무엄함은 문제의 귀족 가문 구성원 모두에게 경멸로써 응징되었다고 적고 있었다. “그러나 다행스럽게도,” 신문은 이렇게 말했다. “C—— 씨는 충분히 앙갚음을 하여 문제의 젊은이를 어찌나 호되게 후려쳤던지, 그자는 사건 이후로 자리에서 일어나지도 못하고 있다고 한다.”\n\n이것을 읽고 나서 크로즈비는 차라리 당장 모습을 드러내고, 제가 말하지 않더라도 세상이 결국 알아낼 만한 것까지는 사실대로 이야기해 두는 편이 낫겠다고 느꼈다. 그리하여 그 사흘째 되는 날 아침, 아직 불행의 붉은 줄무늬 시기가 채 찾아오지도 않았건만 그는 모자를 쓰고 장갑을 끼고서 마차를 잡아 사무실로 갔다. 사무실 복도를 걸어 사환들의 대기실을 지나 제 방으로 들어가는 일은 여간 거북하지 않았다. 물론 모두가 그를 쳐다보았고, 물론 그는 아무렇지 않은 척해 보려던 시도에 실패했다. “보그스.” 그는 지나가면서 사환 하나에게 말했다. “버터웰 씨가 방에 계신지 좀 보고 오게.” 그러자 아니나 다를까, 몇 분이 지나 버터웰 씨가 그를 찾아왔다.\n\n“이런, 이건 심하군.” 버터웰 씨가 사무국장의 상한 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다. “나 같으면 밖에 나오지 않았을 걸세.”\n\n“물론 거북하지요.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “하지만 견디고 마는 편이 낫습니다. 사람이 하루 이틀 눈에 띄지 않으면 다들 지독한 거짓말을 늘어놓거든요. 좋은 낯을 하고 버티는 게 상책이라고 봅니다.”\n\n“그건 지금 당장 자네가 할 수 있는 일이 아닐 텐데, 안 그런가, 크로즈비?” 그러고서 버터웰 씨는 킥킥 웃었다. “그런데 대체 어쩌다 이리 됐나? 신문에서는 자네가 그 짓을 한 자를 거의 죽여 놓았다고 하던데.”\n\n“신문이란 늘 그렇듯 거짓말입니다. 나는 그자에게 손도 대지 않았소.”\n\n“정말 안 그랬단 말인가? 나 같으면 얼굴에 그만한 걸 한 대 얻어맞고 나서 한 방 쥐어박아 주고 싶었을 텐데.”\n\n“경관들이 오고 어쩌고 했지요. 그런 소동판에서는 솔즈베리 벌판에서처럼 끝까지 붙어 싸우도록 놔두지 않습니다. 그렇다고 내가 그자를 때려눕힐 수 있었다는 말은 아니오. 사람이 제가 이길지 어떨지를 어찌 알겠소?”\n\n“정말이지 어찌 알겠나. 한 번 얻어맞아 보거나, 아니면 때려 보기 전에야. 그런데 그자가 누군가? 그리고 그 귀족 가문에게 경멸을 당했다는 건 또 무슨 소린가?”\n\n“허튼소리에 거짓말이지요. 그자는 드 코시 집안 사람이라고는 하나도 본 적이 없소.”\n\n“내 짐작에 사실은 그 다른 쪽 일 때문이었던 게지, 안 그런가, 크로즈비? 나는 자네가 일을 다 마치기도 전에 무슨 곤경에 빠지고 말 줄 알았네.”\n\n“그게 무엇 때문이었는지, 왜 그자가 그런 짐승 같은 짓을 했는지 나는 모르겠소. 앨링턴의 그 사람들 이야기는 들으셨겠지요?”\n\n“아, 그럼. 들었네.”\n\n“맹세코 나는 그 사람들을 두고 무슨 험한 말을 하려던 게 아니오. 그들은 이 일에 대해 아무것도 몰랐소.”\n\n“하지만 그 젊은 친구는 그들을 알고 있었겠지? 아, 그렇지, 이제 다 알겠네. 자네 자리에 제 발을 들이밀고 싶은 게로군. 그러자고 나선 방식이 나쁘다고는 말 못 하겠네. 그런데 자네는 어쩔 셈인가?”\n\n“아무것도 하지 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“아무것도 안 한다니! 그러면 이상해 보이지 않겠나? 나 같으면 그자를 치안 판사 앞에 세우겠네.”\n\n“보시다시피 버터웰 씨, 나는 그 아가씨의 이름을 지켜야 할 처지요. 내가 못되게 굴었다는 것은 나도 압니다.”\n\n“음, 그렇지. 나도 자네가 그랬다고 생각하네.”\n\n버터웰 씨는 이 말을 목소리에 제법 단호함을 실어 했다. 에둘러 말할 생각도, 제 의견을 조금이라도 감출 생각도 없다는 투였다. 크로즈비는 이번 혼사에 관한 한 스스로를 나무라는 버릇이 들어 있었으나, 남들이 제 입에서 그런 자책을 듣거든 무언가 제 허물을 덜어 주는 말을 해 주기를 몹시 바라고 있었다. 벗이라면 그만한 자잘한 과오는 그리 드문 일도 아니라고, 살다 보면 사람이 제 마음을 저도 모르는 일이 더러 있는 법이라고 한마디쯤 해 주기가 얼마나 쉬운가. 그는 파울러 프랫에게서 그런 너그러움을 바랐으나 헛일이었다. 버터웰은 사람 좋고 무던한 사람이었고, 주위 모든 이와 좋게 지내고 싶어 안달이었으며, 감정이나 도덕의 드높은 가락 따위를 내세우는 법이 결코 없는 자였다. 그런 버터웰조차 그에게 위로의 말 한마디를 해 주지 않았다. 제 죄를 마치 죄가 아닌 양—그저 운 나쁜 실수일 뿐인 양 슬쩍 뭉개 줄 사람을 그는 아무도 얻을 수 없었다. 말하자면 그를 제 것으로 차지해 산 채로 삼켜 버린 드 코시 집안 말고는 아무도.\n\n“이제 와서 어쩔 수 없는 일이지요.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “하지만 그날 아침 나에게 그런 짐승 같은 짓을 한 그자로 말하자면, 그는 자기가 그 아가씨의 치맛자락 뒤에 숨어 안전하다는 것을 알고 있소. 내가 무슨 짓을 하든 그 아가씨의 이름을 입에 올리지 않고서는 안 되니까요.”\n\n“아, 그래, 알겠네.” 버터웰이 말했다. “참으로 딱한 일이야, 딱해. 내가 자네에게 무엇을 해 줄 수 있을지 모르겠군. 오늘 위원회에는 나오려나?”\n\n“예, 물론 나갑니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그의 마음은 점점 쓰라려 오고 있었다. 버터웰의 존경도 다정함도 모조리 사라졌다는 것을—적어도 당분간은 그렇다는 것을 그의 예민한 귀는 알아차렸다. 버터웰은 관직으로는 더 높은 자리에 있으면서도 늘 그를 마치 아랫사람인 자기가 떠받들어야 할 상대인 양 대해 왔다. 그는 사무국에서나 바깥세상에서나 제 지위로 마땅히 주장할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 높은 격을 누려 왔고, 스스로도 그것을 알고 있었다. 그런데 이제 그 자리에서 끌어내려지고 있는 것이다. 이런 일에 버터웰만 한 시금석은 없었다. 그는 세상이 가는 대로 갈 사람이었으나, 세상이 어느 쪽으로 가려 하는지를 거의 직감으로 알아채는 사람이었다. “눈치, 눈치, 눈치.” 퍼트니 별장의 오솔길을 거닐 때면 그가 스스로에게 되뇌곤 하던 말이다. 크로즈비는 이제 사무국장이었고 몇 달 전만 해도 일개 서기에 지나지 않았지만, 그럼에도 버터웰 씨의 본능은 크로즈비가 떨어졌다고 일러 주었다. 그리하여 그는 이 불행에 빠진 사내에게 어떠한 동정도 건네기를 마다했고, 사무국장실을 나서면서 아마 한동안은 다시 이 방을 찾을 일이 없으리라는 것을 스스로 느끼고 있었다.\n\n쓰라림 속에서 크로즈비는 이제부터는 뻔뻔하게 밀고 나가리라 마음먹었다. 멍든 눈 따위 아랑곳하지 않는다는 태도를 될 수 있는 대로 꾸며 위원회에 나갈 것이고, 누구든 제게 무슨 말을 걸어오면 대답할 준비가 되어 있을 것이었다. 클럽에도 나갈 것이며, 저를 조금이라도 업신여길 작정인 자가 있거든 성난 그를 조심하는 편이 좋을 것이었다. 그는 존 임스에게 등을 돌려 덤빌 수는 없었지만, 필요하다면 다른 자들에게 돌아설 수는 있었다. 세상 앞에서 제 지위를 얻어 몇 해 동안이나 지켜 온 그가, 한 번 실수를 저질렀다 해서 대뜸 짓뭉개지도록 스스로를 내버려 둘 수는 없었다. 세상이, 그의 세상이 그와 전쟁을 벌이겠다면 그는 그 싸움에 응할 채비가 되어 있었다. 버터웰로 말하자면—무능한 버터웰, 맹탕인 버터웰, 지난 몇 해 동안 관청 일에 조그만 어려움만 생겨도 저를 찾아왔던 그 버터웰로 말하자면, 제 벗이 되어 주는 것을 굽어살펴 허락해 준 사람에게 그렇게 의리를 저버리는 것이 어떤 일인지 똑똑히 알게 해 주리라. 위원회에서도 저들 모두에게 제가 저들을 경멸한다는 것을, 그리고 제가 저들의 주인 노릇을 할 수 있다는 것을 보여 주리라. 그러고 나서 그는 앞으로의 처신에 관해 몇 가지 다른 결심을 하는 김에, 드 코시 집안 사람들에 관해서도 한두 가지를 결심했다. 자기는 그들의 지극히 미천한 종이 될 생각이 없다는 것을 그들에게 알려 주리라. 제 마음을 사뭇 분명한 말로 털어놓으리라. 그러고서 저들이 이 “결연”을 깨겠다고 한다면 깨든 말든 마음대로 하라지. 자기는 그 일로 가슴이 찢어지지는 않을 테니까. 그렇게 안락의자에 몸을 기대고 이 모든 것을 생각하고 있노라니 한 가지 생각이 그의 머릿속으로 스며들었다. 그것은 실현될 수도 있는 어떤 것의 모습이라기보다는 차라리 공중에 떠 있는 성이었다. 그리고 그 공중누각 속에서 그는 다시 릴리의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 용서를 빌며, 한 번만 더 그 마음에 받아들여 달라고 애원하는 제 모습을 보았다.\n\n“크로즈비 씨가 오늘 나왔습니다.” 버터웰 씨가 옵티미스트 씨에게 말했다.\n\n“아, 그렇소.” 옵티미스트 씨가 아주 근엄하게 말했다. 그도 철도역에서 있었던 소동을 죄다 들어 알고 있었던 것이다.\n\n“얼굴을 아주 흉측한 구경거리로 만들어 놓았더군요.”\n\n“그것참 안된 이야기로군. 그건 참… 참… 참…. 젊은 서기들 가운데 하나였다면 부서의 체면을 깎는 일이라고 일러 주었을 텐데.”\n\n“사람이 눈에 한 대 얻어맞은 걸 어쩌겠습니까. 제 손으로 그런 건 아닐 텐데요.” 피아스코 소령이 말했다.\n\n“그가 제 손으로 그런 게 아니라는 것쯤은 나도 잘 아오.” 옵티미스트 씨가 말을 이었다. “하지만 그만한 자리에 있는 사람이라면 그런 시비판에 휘말리지 말았어야 한다고 나는 정말로 생각하오.”\n\n“할 수만 있었다면 진심으로 그랬겠지요.” 소령이 말했다. “그 사람도 나만큼이나 두들겨 맞는 걸 좋아하지는 않았을 겁니다.”\n\n“나한테 멍든 눈을 안기는 사람은 아무도 없소.” 옵티미스트 씨가 말했다.\n\n“아직까지는 없었지요.” 소령이 말했다.\n\n“앞으로도 영영 없기를 바랍니다.” 버터웰 씨가 말했다. 그러고서 회의 시각이 돌아오자 크로즈비 씨가 위원회실로 들어왔다.\n\n“이 불행한 일을 듣고 우리 모두 몹시 안타까웠소.” 옵티미스트 씨가 아주 근엄하게 말했다.\n\n“저만큼 안타깝지야 않으셨겠지요.” 크로즈비가 웃으며 말했다. “눈에 멍이 들어 권투 선수 같은 몰골로 돌아다니는 건 여간 성가신 일이 아닙니다.”\n\n“게다가 시합에서 이기지도 못한 권투 선수 같은 몰골이지.” 피아스코가 말했다.\n\n“그 점에 무슨 큰 차이가 있는지 저는 모르겠군요.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “아무튼 이 일은 통째로 성가신 노릇이니, 괜찮으시다면 이에 관해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 하지 맙시다.”\n\n옵티미스트 씨는 이에 관해 무언가 더 말하는 것이 제 의무라는 생각을 거의 품고 있었다. 자기가 수석 위원이 아닌가, 그리고 크로즈비 씨는 위원회의 사무국장이 아닌가? 서로의 지위를 생각할 때, 이토록 뚜렷한 온당치 못한 일을 한마디 지적도 없이 넘겨야 옳단 말인가? 버터웰 씨가 사무국장이던 시절에 멍든 눈으로 사무실에 나왔더라면 래플 버플 경이 한마디쯤 하지 않았겠는가? 그는 의장의 온전한 권리를 남김없이 행사하고 싶었다. 그럼에도 사무국장을 바라보고 있노라니 어쩐지 겸연쩍어져서 알맞은 말을 찾을 수가 없었다. “흠, 허, 그럼. 괜찮으시다면 이제 안건으로 들어갑시다.” 그는 이렇게 말했다. 마치 위원회의 통상 업무가 끝나고 나면 사무국장의 멍든 눈으로 되돌아올 권리를 제 몫으로 남겨 두기라도 하는 듯한 투였다. 그러나 위원회의 통상 업무가 다 끝났을 때, 사무국장은 제 눈에 관한 어떤 언급도 더는 없이 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n크로즈비가 제 방으로 돌아와 보니 모티머 게이지비가 거기서 그를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n“여보게.” 게이지비가 말했다. “이건 아주 고약한 일일세.”\n\n“지독하게 고약하지.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “하도 고약해서 누구와도 이 이야기는 하지 않을 작정이네.”\n\n“레이디 어밀리아가 아주 언짢아하고 계시네.” 그는 제 형제자매에게 아내 이야기를 할 때조차 늘 그녀를 레이디 어밀리아라고 불렀다. 백작의 딸을 세례명만으로 부르는 무례를 범하기에는 그는 지나치게 예의 바른 사람이었다. 그 백작의 딸이 제 아내인데도 그러했다. “자네가 많이 다친 게 아닌가 걱정하시네.”\n\n“다치기는 조금도 다치지 않았네. 보다시피 얼굴이 볼썽사납게 되었을 뿐이지.”\n\n“그래서 그런 짓을 한 그자를 자네가 실컷 두들겨 주었다지?”\n\n“아니, 그러지 않았네.” 크로즈비가 몹시 성이 나서 말했다. “나는 그자를 전혀 때리지 않았어. 자네는 신문에서 읽은 것을 죄다 믿는가?”\n\n“아니, 죄다 믿지는 않네. 물론 그자가 레이디 알렉산드리나와의 결연을 넘본다는 대목은 믿지 않았지. 그건 물론 사실이 아니었을 테고.” 게이지비 씨는 그토록 유례없는 무엄함이란 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이라는 것을 목소리의 가락으로 드러내 보였다.\n\n“자네는 아무것도 믿지 말게. 다만 이것만 믿게. 내 눈에 멍이 들었다는 것 말이야.”\n\n“그것만은 확실히 그렇더군. 레이디 어밀리아께서는 자네가 오늘 저녁 우리 집으로 올라오면 한결 편하겠다고 여기시네. 물론 밖에 나다닐 수야 없겠지만, 당신 앞에서라면 개의치 않아도 된다고 아주 너그럽게 말씀하셨네.”\n\n“고맙지만 됐네. 일요일에 가겠네.”\n\n“레이디 알렉산드리나께서도 물론 언니에게서 소식 듣기를 몹시 기다리실 테고, 레이디 어밀리아께서 특별히 나에게 자네를 꼭 오게 하라고 당부하셨네.”\n\n“고맙지만 됐네. 오늘은 안 되겠어.”\n\n“어째서 안 된다는 건가?”\n\n“그저 집에 있는 편이 나을 것 같아서지.”\n\n“집에 있는 편이 어떻게 낫단 말인가? 우리 집에서도 원하는 것은 무엇이든 가질 수 있네. 레이디 어밀리아께서는 개의치 않으실 걸세.”\n\n응접실에 앉아 있는 그의 눈에 대라고 날고기 한 점을 더 내주는 일, 찬물 찜질, 혹은 그 비슷한 자잘한 치료 도구 따위—레이디 어밀리아가 살림하는 사람다운 너그러움으로 개의치 않아 주기를 굽어 허락하시겠다는 것이 바로 그런 것들이었다!\n\n“오늘 저녁에는 부인을 번거롭게 해 드리지 않겠네.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그래도 참말이지 나는 자네가 잘못 생각한다고 보네. 온갖 이야기가 코시 성까지, 백작부인의 귀에까지 흘러 들어갈 걸세. 그러면 무슨 해가 생길지 자네는 모르네. 레이디 어밀리아께서는 당신이 편지를 써서 사정을 설명해 드리는 편이 낫겠다고 여기시지만, 자네에게서 무슨 말이든 듣기 전에는 그러실 수가 없지 않은가.”\n\n“이보게 게이지비. 나는 코시 성까지 어떤 이야기가 흘러 들어가든 지푸라기 하나만큼도 개의치 않네.”\n\n“하지만 백작께서 무슨 말을 들으시고 언짢아하시기라도 하면?”\n\n“언짢으신 것에서야 좋으실 대로 회복하시면 되지.”\n\n“여보게, 그건 함부로 하는 말일세.”\n\n“대체 백작이 나에게 무엇을 할 수 있다고 생각하나? 내가 그분의 따님과 혼인한다고 해서 평생을 드 코시 경을 두려워하며 살 것 같은가? 나는 오늘 알렉산드리나에게 직접 편지를 쓰겠네. 그 언니에게 그렇게 전해 주게. 일요일에는 저녁 식사 시간에 맞춰 올라가겠네. 이 얼굴 꼴 때문에 아예 말도 안 되는 일이 되지만 않는다면 말일세.”\n\n“교회 시간에 맞춰서는 오지 않겠단 말인가?”\n\n“이런 얼굴을 하고 교회에 나가라는 건가?”\n\n그러고서 모티머 게이지비 씨는 돌아갔고, 집에 이르자 아내에게 크로즈비가 아주 뻣뻣하게 나온다고 일렀다. “실은 말입니다, 여보. 그이는 제 자신이 부끄러운 것이지요. 그래서 저렇게 뱃심 좋은 낯을 지어 보이려 애쓰는 것이고요.”\n\n“그 젊은이가 다니는 길목에 제 몸을 내던진 건 참으로 어리석은 짓이었소. 참으로 어리석었지. 일요일에 내가 그렇게 일러 주겠소. 나에게까지 그런 티를 내려 든다면, 그이가 아주 잘못하고 있다는 것을 내가 똑똑히 깨우쳐 주겠소. 이제는 제 처신이 우리 집안 모두에게 중대한 일이라는 것을 명심해야 하오.”\n\n“물론 그래야지요.” 게이지비 씨가 말했다.\n\n일요일이 되자 붉은 줄무늬 시기가 찾아와 있었으나 아직 결코 물러가지는 않은 상태였다. 사무실 사람들은 거의 그 얼굴에 익숙해져 있었다. 그러나 크로즈비는 클럽에 나가겠다던 결심에도 불구하고 아직 다른 데에는 모습을 보이지 않고 있었다. 물론 교회에도 가지 않았고, 다섯 시가 되어서야 세인트존스우드의 그 집에 나타났다. 그 집에서는 일요일이면 늘 다섯 시에 저녁을 먹었는데, 일곱 시에 먹는 것보다 그렇게 하는 편이 안식일을 더 잘 지키는 것이라는 생각이 어느 정도 있었기 때문이다. 안식일을 지키는 것이 일찍 잠자리에 드는 데 있다면, 혹은 그런 관습이 어떤 식으로든 그것을 돕는다면 그들이 옳았다. 요리사에게는 그 어중간하게 이른 저녁 식사가 아마 편리했을 것이다. 오후에 교회에 가지 않을 구실이 되어 주었을 테니 말이다. 하인들과 아이들의 식사가 아침나절에 똑같은 구실이 되어 주었던 것처럼. 이런 자잘한 선행의 시도들—선을 향한 그 성가신 길을 절반쯤, 혹은 이번처럼 사분의 일쯤 걸어가 보는 시도들—은 레이디 어밀리아 같은 점잖은 사람들에게 아주 흔한 일이다. 만일 그녀가 한 시에 식사를 하고 찬 고기를 먹었더라면, 아마 얼마쯤은 칭찬받을 자격이 있다고 느껴 볼 만도 했으리라.\n\n“저런, 저런, 저런. 이거 참 딱하게 되었구려, 어돌퍼스.” 그를 처음 보자마자 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그렇습니다, 딱한 일이지요, 어밀리아.” 그가 말했다. 그는 늘 그녀를 어밀리아라고 불렀는데, 그녀가 그를 어돌퍼스라 불렀기 때문이다. 다만 게이지비 자신은 그 소리를 들을 때마다 결코 썩 유쾌하지 않았다. 레이디 어밀리아는 크로즈비보다 나이가 많았으니 그를 무어라 부르든 그럴 자격이 있었지만, 그로서는 두 사람의 지체가 크게 다르다는 것을 기억했어야 마땅했다. “딱한 일입니다, 어밀리아.” 그가 말했다. “그런데 한 가지만 들어주시겠습니까?”\n\n“무슨 일이오, 어돌퍼스?”\n\n“이 일에 대해 더는 한마디도 하지 말아 주십시오. 멍든 눈은 물론 고약한 것이고, 그 때문에 저도 어지간히 시달렸습니다. 하지만 벗들의 동정에는 그보다 훨씬 더 시달렸습니다. 금요일에 게이지비에게서 온 집안의 위문을 한꺼번에 다 받았으니, 그것이 또 되풀이된다면 저는 그만 드러누워 죽고 말 겁니다.”\n\n“돌퍼스 삼춘, 눈이 아야 해서 죽는 거야?” 집안의 맏이 되는 희망인 드 코시 게이지비가 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 물었다.\n\n“아니란다, 나의 용사님.” 크로즈비가 아이를 두 팔로 안아 올리며 말했다. “눈에 멍이 들었다고 죽지는 않아. 그런 건 별로 해로울 것도 없고, 너도 학교를 마치기 전에 아주 여러 번 멍이 들 거란다. 다만 사람들이 자꾸만 그 이야기를 해 대니까 그렇지.”\n\n“그치만 눈이 미운 아야가 되면 디나 이모가 삼춘 안 좋아할걸.”\n\n“하지만 어돌퍼스.” 레이디 어밀리아가 한바탕 따져 볼 자세를 고쳐 잡으며 말했다. “그 말도 다 좋소만, 나도 그대에게 성가신 일을 만들어 주는 것이 참으로 미안하기는 하오. 그렇지만 이런 일을 두고 아무 말도 하지 않고 그냥 넘어가는 법이 어디 있는지 정말 모르겠구려. 어머니께 편지를 받았소.”\n\n“드 코시 백작부인께서 안녕하시기를 바랍니다.”\n\n“아주 안녕하시오, 고맙소. 하지만 당연한 일이지만 이번 일을 몹시 염려하고 계시오. 신문에 실린 이야기를 읽으셨으니, 집안 고문 변호사로서 모티머가 이 일을 맡아야 할지도 모르겠소.”\n\n“그건 어림도 없는 일입니다.” 어돌퍼스가 말했다.\n\n“나도 그런 조치는 권하지 않겠습니다.” 게이지비가 말했다.\n\n“아마 그렇겠지요. 아마도 그럴 거요. 하지만 모티머, 어머니께서 이런 사정에 처하여 이 일의 사실이 무엇인지 알고 싶어 하시는 것이야 놀랄 일이 아니잖소.”\n\n“전혀 놀랄 일이 아니지요.” 게이지비가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 마지막으로 한 번, 제가 사실을 말씀드리지요. 제가 기차에서 내리는데 평생에 딱 한 번 본 적이 있는 사내가 저에게 달려들었고, 경관들이 오기 전에 저는 얼굴에 한 대 얻어맞았습니다. 이제 다 아신 겁니다.”\n\n바로 그때 저녁 식사가 준비되었다는 전갈이 왔다. “레이디 어밀리아께 팔을 내어 드리겠나?” 남편이 말했다.\n\n“참으로 딱한 일이오.” 레이디 어밀리아가 고개를 살짝 젖히며 말했다. “게다가 내 동생에게 적잖은 속상함을 치르게 할 것 같아 걱정이구려.”\n\n“드 코시의 말에 동의하시는군요. 제 눈이 이렇게 밉게 멍이 들었으니 디나 이모가 저를 좋아하지 않을 거라는 말 말입니다.”\n\n“나는 이게 정말이지 농담할 일이라고 생각하지 않소.” 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다. 그러고서 식사가 이어지는 동안에는 이에 관해 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았다.\n\n식사가 이어지는 동안에는 이에 관해 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았으나, 레이디 어밀리아가 장차 제부가 될 사람의 처신을 그리 탐탁잖게 여긴다는 것은 그 낯빛만 보아도 넉넉히 알 수 있었다. 그녀는 그에게 대단히 후하게 굴어 자꾸만 음식을 권했다. 그러나 그렇게 하면서도 그의 지금 딱한 처지를 잘게잘게 거듭 들먹였다. 그녀는 튀긴 자두 푸딩은 그에게 해롭지 않을 듯하나 식후에 포트와인은 마시지 않기를 권하겠다고 그에게 일렀다. “그건 그렇고 모티머, 클라레를 좀 올려 오게 하는 편이 낫겠소.” 그녀가 한마디 덧붙였다. “어돌퍼스는 몸에 열을 올리는 것은 무엇도 들지 말아야 해요.”\n\n“고맙습니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “저는 게이지비가 주기만 한다면 브랜디에 물을 탄 것을 마시겠습니다.”\n\n“브랜디에 물이라니!” 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다. 크로즈비는 사실 브랜디에 물을 타 마시는 사람이 아니었다. 그러나 레이디 어밀리아의 걱정에 조금만 더 시달렸다가는 맹진을 가져오라고 할 참이었다.\n\n이 일요일 저녁 식탁에서는 안주인이 결코 응접실로 물러가는 법이 없었고, 차도 늘 식사를 한 그 식탁 위로 들여왔다. 그것 또한 한 주의 첫날을 거룩히 지키기 위한 자잘한 한 걸음이었다. 레이디 로지나가 와 있을 때면 그녀는 병들이 치워지자마자 마호가니 위에 놓이는 묵직하고 훌륭한 책 예닐곱 권을 보는 호사를 누렸다. 처음으로 오래 머물렀을 때 그녀는 설교문을 낭독하는 특권을 스스로 따내기까지 했다. 그러나 그럴 때면 레이디 어밀리아도 게이지비 씨도 잠이 들었고—하인까지도 한 번은 그런 낌새를 보였으므로—설교문은 그만두게 되었다. 다만 처제가 와 있는 저녁이면 이 집 주인은 하릴없이 앉아 있거나, 아니면 그 묵직하고 훌륭한 책 가운데 하나에서 위안을 찾는 형벌을 받아야 했다. 그런데 레이디 로지나는 지금 시골에 가 있었으므로 식탁 위는 비어 있었다.\n\n“그러면 나는 어머니께 무어라 말씀드려야 하오?” 그들만 남게 되었을 때 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“제 안부를 각별히 전해 주십시오.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그가 권위에 맞서 반역할 채비를 하고 있다는 것은 남편에게도 아내에게도 아주 분명했다.\n\n한 십 분 동안은 아무 말도 오가지 않았다. 크로즈비는 드 코시를 줄여 딕시라고 부르며 그 사내아이와 놀아 주는 것으로 시간을 보냈다.\n\n“엄마, 삼춘이 나더러 딕시래. 내가 딕시야? 나는 삼춘을 늙다리 크로스라고 부를 거야. 그럼 디나 이모가 삼춘 안 좋아할걸.”\n\n“아이에게 별명을 붙이지 말아 주었으면 좋겠소, 어돌퍼스. 그 아이가 지닌 이름에 흠집을 내고 싶어 하는 것처럼 보이는구려.”\n\n“설마 그이가 그럴 마음이야 먹겠습니까.” 게이지비 씨가 말했다.\n\n“설마 그렇겠습니까.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그 이름은 우리나라 역사에서 아직껏 별명 따위가 되어 욕을 본 적이 없소.” 이 집안의 도도한 따님이 말했다. 그녀는 아마 알지 못했겠지만, 그녀의 아버지는 이따금 그 입에서 나오는 말이 어찌나 기운찼던지 지인들 사이에서는 「욕쟁이 드 코시 경」이라 불려 왔다. “그리고 그런 시도는 무엇이든 내 귀에 거슬리는구려. 나는 내 집안을 제법 소중히 여긴다오, 정말이오, 어돌퍼스. 내 남편도 그렇고.”\n\n“아주 대단히 그렇습니다.” 게이지비 씨가 말했다.\n\n“저도 제 집안을 소중히 여깁니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “그건 우리 모두에게 자연스러운 일이지요. 제 조상 가운데 한 분은 정복왕 윌리엄과 함께 바다를 건너오셨습니다. 아마 왕의 천막에서 요리사를 거들던 사람 가운데 하나였을 겁니다.”\n\n“요리사래!” 어린 드 코시가 말했다.\n\n“그렇단다, 얘야, 요리사였지. 우리 옛 가문들은 대개 그런 식으로 귀족이 된 거란다. 왕의 요리사거나 집사였지. 더러는 그보다 못한 것이기도 했고.”\n\n“그치만 삼춘네 집안은 귀족이 아니잖아?”\n\n“아니지. 그게 어찌 된 일인지 내가 말해 주마. 왕은 제 마음대로 하겠다고 나선 신하 예닐곱을 이 요리사더러 독살하라고 했단다. 그런데 요리사가 말했지. ‘아니 되옵니다, 임금님. 저는 요리사이지 망나니가 아닙니다.’ 그래서 사람들은 그를 설거지방으로 쫓아 보냈고, 다른 하인들은 모두 남작이니 경이니 부르면서 그이만은 그저 쿠키라고 불렀단다. 그 뒤로 그 이름이 차차 바뀌어 크로즈비가 된 거지.”\n\n게이지비 씨는 아연실색했고, 레이디 어밀리아의 얼굴은 아주 어두워졌다. 이 뱀을 품속 가장 깊은 곳에 들여 따뜻하게 데워 주었더니, 그것이 독사가 되어 저희를 물려고 채비하는 것이 뻔하지 않은가? 그날 저녁에는 그 뒤로 오간 이야기가 거의 없었고, 요리사 이야기가 끝나고 얼마 지나지 않아 크로즈비는 자리에서 일어나 제 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n# 제36장. 「보라, 개선하는 영웅이 온다」\n\n존 임스는 정확히 열두 시에 사무국에 닿았지만, 막상 닿고 보니 자기가 발로 서 있는지 머리로 서 있는지도 분간이 되지 않았다. 그날 아침 내내 그는 격렬한 흥분 속에 있었고, 나중에는 어느 정도 개선의 기분에까지 젖어 있었다. 그러나 그 결과가 어찌 될지는 도무지 알 수 없었다. 치안판사 앞에 끌려가 갇히게 될까? 사무국에서 소동이 벌어질까? 크로즈비가 결투를 신청해 올까, 그렇게 되면 권총으로 결투를 치르는 것이 제 의무가 될까? 드 게스트 백작은 무어라 할까—릴리가 당한 일의 원수를 갚는 노릇을 스스로 떠맡지 말라고 특별히 일러 두었던 그 드 게스트 백작이. 데일 집안 사람들은 모두 그의 행동을 두고 무어라 할까? 그리고 무엇보다도, 릴리는 무어라 말하고 어떻게 생각할까? 그럼에도 개선의 기분이 우세했다. 그리고 이제 이만큼 시간이 흐르고 보니, 그는 제 주먹이 크로즈비의 눈으로 들어가던 그 감촉을 흐뭇하게 떠올리기 시작하고 있었다.\n\n사무국에 나간 첫날 그는 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 듣지 못했고, 자기도 누구에게 한마디 하지 않았다. 그가 드 게스트 백작과 성탄절 휴가를 보내러 내려갔다는 것은 그의 방에서도 알려져 있었고, 그래서 그는 그만큼 전보다 얼마쯤 대접을 받았다. 게다가 조니 임스에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려주기 위해 나는 이렇게 설명해 두어야겠다. 그는 소득세 사무국 관리들 사이에서 차츰 제 자리를 잡아 가고 있었다. 그는 제 일을 알았고, 제 능력에 대한 사내다운 자신감을 가지고, 또한 부서의 높으신 어른들이 이따금 찌푸리는 낯빛에 대한 사내다운 무심함을 가지고 그 일을 해냈다. 더구나 그는 인기가 있었다—관청에서의 처신이 다소 급진적인 데다, 높으신 어른들이 낯을 찌푸려도 제 권리를 굽히지 않았기 때문이다. 사실 그는 풋내기 시절에서 벗어나 젊은 사내의 시절로 들어서는 중이었고, 그 시기에 아마 숱한 어리석음과 얼마간의 거짓된 감상을 겪어야 했겠지만, 그의 성품의 조짐을 읽을 줄 아는 이들에게는 그 너머에 참된 사내다움이 있으리라는 좋은 기약이 여전히 보였다.\n\n그 첫날 그는 성탄절의 영광에 대해 여러 질문을 받았으나, 그 일에 대해서는 할 말이 별로 없었다. 실제로 그를 그 지방으로 내려가게 한 하나의 큰 목적과, 휴가가 끝나던 마당의 그 사건이 아니었더라면, 그의 성탄절 방문만큼 평범한 것도 없었을 터였다. 그 두 가지 중 어느 것에 대해서도 그는 터놓고 말할 마음이 없었다. 그러나 크래들과 함께 버턴 크레슨트로 걸어 돌아가면서 그는 크로즈비와의 일을 크래들에게 이야기했다.\n\n“그래서 역에서 그자한테 달려들었단 말인가?” 크래들이 감탄 어린 의심을 담아 물었다.\n\n“그래, 그랬지. 거기서 안 했으면 어디서 했겠나? 하겠다고 말해 두었으니, 그자를 본 김에 한 거야.” 그러고서 시퍼렇게 멍든 눈이며 순경들이며 경감이며 하는 이야기가 죄다 나왔다. “그런데 이제 어떻게 되는 거지?” 우리의 주인공이 물었다.\n\n“뭐, 물론 그자야 친구 손에 맡기겠지. 나도 루펙스와의 그 일 때 피셔에게 그랬으니까. 그리고 정말이지, 조니, 나도 또 한 번 그런 걸 해야 할 판이야. 어젯밤 그자의 행실은 언어도단이었어. 자네 믿겠나—”\n\n“아, 그자는 바보야.”\n\n“그자가 광증이 도졌을 땐 자네도 마주치고 싶지 않을 그런 바보라고, 내 장담하지. 어젯밤엔 꼼짝없이 내 침실에만 앉아 있어야 했네. 로퍼 아줌마가 그러더군, 내가 응접실에 그대로 있으면 자기는 집에 순경을 불러들일 수밖에 없겠다고. 낸들 어쩌겠나? 물론 아줌마더러 불은 지펴 달라고 했지.”\n\n“그러고서 잠자리에 들었고.”\n\n“한참을 기다렸네. 머라이어가 나를 보고 싶어 할 거라고 생각했거든. 마침내 그이가 쪽지를 보내왔지. 머라이어는 어찌나 경솔한지 몰라. 그자가 그이의 필적으로 된 것을 무어라도 찾아냈다면 끔찍했을 거야—정말 끔찍했을 거야. 게다가 제미마가 고자질하지 않으리라고 누가 장담하겠나?”\n\n“그래서 무어라고 썼는데?”\n\n“이런, 그건 발설할 수 없지, 조니 도련님. 무슨 일이 생길까 봐 오늘 아침 사무실에 아주 잘 챙겨 갔다네.”\n\n그러나 임스는 제 모험거리를 잔뜩 짊어지고 있지만 않았더라면 기울였을 만큼, 벗의 모험에 그리 마음을 크게 쓰고 있지 못했다.\n\n“나는 그 크로즈비란 작자가 친구를 내세우는 건 그리 대수롭지 않네만,” 그가 말했다. “경찰 치안판사한테 가는 건 그렇지가 않아.”\n\n“물론 친구 손에 맡길 걸세,” 크래들이 그런 일이라면 겪어 봐서 훤하다는 사람의 태도로 말했다. “그러면 자네야 당연히 내게 오겠지. 관청에 다니는 사람한테는 지독히 성가신 일이고 뭐 그렇긴 하지만 말이야. 그래도 나는 벗을 저버릴 사람이 아니야. 내가 자네 곁에 서 주겠네, 조니, 이 사람아.”\n\n“아, 고맙네,” 임스가 말했다. “그럴 일까지는 없을 것 같은데.”\n\n“친구는 하나 준비해 두어야 하는 법이야, 알겠나.”\n\n“나는 시골에 아는 어른께 편지를 써서 조언을 구할 참이야,” 임스가 말했다. “좀 나이 지긋한 쪽의 친구 말일세.”\n\n“이런, 이 사람아, 하는 일을 조심하게. 자네가 겁쟁이 깃털을 보였다는 소리는 듣지 않도록 해. 명예를 걸고 하는 말인데, 나라면 그런 소리를 듣느니 차라리 무슨 소리를 듣든 나을 거야. 정말이지—무슨 소리든.”\n\n“그건 걱정 없네,” 임스가 목소리에 얼마간 경멸을 담아 말했다. “요즘은 겁쟁이 깃털 따위를 그리 따지지 않아—적어도 결투를 하고 말고 하는 문제로는.”\n\n그 뒤로 크래들은 어떻게든 이야기를 루펙스 부인과 제 자신의 묘한 처지 쪽으로 되돌려 놓았고, 임스는 제 일에 대해 이 길동무에게 더 조언을 구할 마음이 없었으므로 버턴 크레슨트에 이를 때까지 거의 잠자코 듣고만 있었다.\n\n“그 훌륭하신 백작님께서는 안녕하시던가요?” 다들 저녁 식탁에 앉자마자 로퍼 부인이 그에게 말했다.\n\n“그 훌륭하신 백작님께서는 그런대로 안녕하시더군요, 고맙습니다,” 조니가 말했다.\n\n임스의 처지가 드 게스트 백작의 우정을 얻는 영광을 누린 이래 아주 달라졌다는 것은 로퍼네 사람들 모두에게 뻔히 이해되고 있었다. 저녁마다 그가 곁에 앉는 루펙스 부인은—크래들이라는 위험한 이웃으로부터 이를테면 그이를 지켜 준다는 뜻에서 그 자리였는데—그를 눈에 띄게 정중히 대했다. 스프루스 양은 늘 그를 “선생님”이라고 불렀다. 로퍼 부인은 신사들 가운데 그에게 제일 먼저 음식을 덜어 주었고 그의 몫의 기름진 부위와 육즙을 잊지 않았으며, 어밀리아는 전처럼 그의 마음과 애정을 제 것이라고 우겨 댈 수가 없다고 느꼈다. 그렇다고 어밀리아가 싸움을 그만두고 적이 병력을 끌고 물러가도록 내버려 둘 작정이었다고 여겨서는 안 된다. 다만 그이는 마음과 마음의 결합이라면 마땅히 따라야 할 완전한 대등함과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 공대를 그에게 하지 않을 수 없다고 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n“귀족분들과 서로 오가는 사이라는 건 참으로 대단한 특권이지요,” 루펙스 부인이 말했다. “제가 아가씨였을 적에는 아주 가깝게 지내던—”\n\n“당신은 이제 아가씨가 아니니, 그런 얘긴 꺼내지 않는 게 좋을 거요,” 루펙스가 말했다. 루펙스 씨는 무대 배경을 그리고 내려온 뒤 드루리 레인의 그 작은 가게에 들렀던 참이었다.\n\n“여보, 로퍼 부인 댁 손님들 앞에서까지 나한테 짐승처럼 굴 건 없잖아요. 지금 여기서 늘어놓지 않으려는 어떤 감정에 이끌려 내가 당신과 혼인하느라 내 격에 맞는 자리를 떠났다 해도, 온 세상 앞에서 내가 얼마나 후회할 일을 했는지 굳이 가르쳐 줄 건 없잖아요.” 그러고서 루펙스 부인은 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다.\n\n“사내가 밥상머리에서 저런 소리를 듣는 게 유쾌하겠소, 안 그렇소?” 루펙스가 스프루스 양에게 호소하며 말했다. “나는 저런 걸 실컷 겪는데, 내가 저걸 얼마나 좋아하는지 당신은 짐작도 못 할 거요.”\n\n“하느님이 짝지어 주신 것을 사람이 갈라놓지 못할지니라,” 스프루스 양이 말했다. “저야 뭐, 그저 늙은 여자일 뿐이지요.”\n\n이 자그마한 소동이 저녁 식탁에 우울한 기운을 드리웠고, 그 자리에서 임스의 출세담에 대해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 나오지 않았다. 그러나 그날 저녁이 흘러가는 동안 어밀리아는 철도역에서 벌어진 그 충돌 이야기를 듣고, 곧 그 일을 제 목적에 써먹을 수 있겠다고 알아차렸다.\n\n“존,” 그이는 그가 거의 혼자 있는 틈을 붙잡아 제 희생자에게 속삭였다. “이게 무슨 얘기예요? 꼭 말해 주셔야겠어요. 결투를 하실 참이에요?”\n\n“무슨 소리예요,” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“무슨 소리라니요. 그런 일이 벌어지려 한다고 생각하면 제 심정이 어떨지 당신은 모르실 거예요. 하기야 당신은 그렇게도 매정하시니!”\n\n“나는 조금도 매정하지 않고, 결투 같은 것도 하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“그럼 역에서 크로즈비 씨를 때리셨다는 게 정말이에요?”\n\n“정말이에요. 내가 때렸어요.”\n\n“아, 존! 당신이 잘못하셨다는 말이 아니에요. 오히려 저는 그 마음을 훌륭하다고 여겨요. 젊은 남자가 젊은 여자를 속이고, 그 마음을 얻어 놓고서 버리는 것만큼 끔찍한 일은 없지요—더구나 그 아가씨가 분명한 말로, 아니 어쩌면 흰 종이에 검은 글씨로까지 약속을 받아 두었을 때는요.” 존은 자기가 써 준 그 끔찍하고 어리석고 한심한 쪽지를 떠올렸다. “그리고 가엾은 아가씨가 혼인 약속 위반으로 제 권리를 찾을 수 없다면, 어찌해야 좋을지 모르는 거예요. 그렇지 않아요, 존?”\n\n“그런 식으로 제 권리를 찾는 아가씨라면 가질 만한 값어치가 없지요.”\n\n“저는 그렇게 생각하지 않아요. 가엾은 아가씨가 그런 처지에 놓이면 벗들의 도움을 받아야 하는 거예요. 그럼 릴리 데일 양은 그이를 혼인 약속 위반으로 걸지 않겠군요.”\n\n그런 자리에서 릴리의 이름이 입에 오르는 것은 가엾은 임스의 귀에는 신성모독이었다. “나는 알 수 없어요,” 그가 말했다. “당신이 말하는 그 숙녀분의 뜻이 어떠할지는. 그러나 내가 아는 그분의 집안 사람들로 미루어 보면, 그분이 그런 소송으로 스스로를 욕되게 하시리라고는 생각하지 않아요.”\n\n“릴리 데일 양이야 그럴 수 있겠지요—” 어밀리아가 말하다가 머뭇거렸다. 아직은 그를 대놓고 협박하는 것이 좋지 않겠다고 그이는 생각했다—협박 없이도 그를 얻을 가능성이 조금이라도 있는 동안에는. “물론 저는 다 알고 있어요,” 그이가 말을 이었다. “그분이 당신의 L. D.였지요. 제가 그분을 질투한 적이 있었다는 게 아니에요. 당신에게 그분은 어린 시절의 친구들 가운데 하나에 지나지 않았으니까요. 그렇지 않아요, 조니?”\n\n그는 마루를 발로 구르더니 자리에서 벌떡 일어섰다. “어린 시절의 친구니 뭐니 하는 그따위 헛소리는 질색이에요, 그건 당신도 알잖아요. 이러다간 다시는 이 방에 들어오지 않겠다고 나더러 맹세하게 만들 거예요.”\n\n“조니!”\n\n“정말 그럴 거예요. 이 모든 게 신물이 나요. 그리고 저 루펙스 부인으로 말하면—”\n\n“귀족 나리 댁에 다녀와서 배운 게 이런 것이라면, 존, 당신은 집에서 당신 벗들과 지내는 편이 낫겠네요.”\n\n“물론 그러는 게 낫지요—집에서 내 벗들과 지내는 게 훨씬 낫고말고요. 그런데 여기 루펙스 부인이 있으니, 나는 아무래도 저이는 견딜 수가 없어요.” 그러고서 그는 나가 버렸고, 크레슨트를 한 바퀴 돌아 뉴 로드까지, 거의 리전트 파크까지 걸으면서, 릴리 데일을 생각하고 어밀리아 로퍼 앞에서의 제 비겁함을 생각했다.\n\n이튿날 아침나절, 한 시쯤 되었을 때 그는 위원회실 사환의 입을 통해 위원회실로 오라는 전갈을 받았다. “래플 버플 경께서 오시라 하십니다, 임스 씨.”\n\n“나를? 터퍼, 무슨 일로?” 조니가 사환 쪽으로 돌아서며 거의 낭패한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n“저야 알 수 없지요, 임스 씨. 그저 래플 버플 경께서 위원회실로 오시라 하셨습니다.”\n\n관청 생활에서 그런 전갈은 언제나 젊은 사람의 가슴에 두려움을 심는다. 그러면서도 젊은 사람들은 대개 그런 면담에서 별다른 큰 상처 없이 나오며, 자기가 마주쳤던 그 노신사들에 대해 대개는 가벼운 마음의 빈정거림을—요즘 속어로 하자면 “놀려 먹기”를—늘어놓는다. 그렇게 만드는 것은 “왕을 에워싼 그 위엄”이다. 칠면조 수컷은 제 마당에서는 그 자리의 주인이며, 그를 떠올리는 것만으로 두려움이 인다. 아마포 법의를 걸친 주교, 법대에 앉은 판사, 긴 탁자 끝 큰 방의 의장, 혹은 순찰길에서 각등을 든 순경은 저마다에게 허락된 그 위엄의 부속물들로 스스로를 무섭게 만들 수 있다. 그러나 제 집에 있는 순경은 얼마나 초라하며, 낡은 슬리퍼를 신고 저녁 뒤에 잠든 래플 버플 경을 대단하게 여긴 이가 얼마나 적었던가! 이제는 세상을 떠난 지 오래인 어떤 훌륭한 노신사가 모욕당한 위엄의 낯빛으로 두 손을 천천히 맞비비며 천장을 올려다보고 고개를 살짝 저으면서, 마치 내 죄악을 헤아리다 넋을 잃은 듯이 굴 때 내 안에 일던 그 공포를 나는 얼마나 잘 기억하는가! 나는 속이 메스꺼워지고 발목이 부러진 것 같은 느낌이 들었다. 그 치켜뜨는 눈길은 나를 어찌나 무력하게 만들었던지, 나는 어떤 변명도 하지 못한 채 벙어리가 되곤 했다. 나는 그 노인이 제 힘이 어디까지 미치는지를 스스로도 잘 몰랐으리라고 생각한다.\n\n옛날에 어떤 덤벙대는 소년이 왕에게 올리는 편지 묶음을—청원서 따위로, 일이 돌아가는 순서로 보자면 어느 시종 귀족의 부보좌관 손에서 더 나아가지도 못할 것들이었지만—맡았다가, 그것들이 든 행낭을 엉뚱한 곳으로 보낸 일이 있었다. 궁정이 런던에 있는데 윈저로 보냈든지, 윈저에 있는데 세인트 제임스로 보냈든지 했던 것이다. 그는 불려 갔다. 그리고 그 자리의 높으신 어른은 의자에서 일어서며 두 손을 하늘로 치켜들고 두 번 이렇게 외치는 것으로 만족했다. “군주의 행낭을 잘못 보내다니! 군주의 행낭을 잘못 보내다니!” 그 젊은이는 자기가 어떻게 위원회실을 빠져나왔는지 끝내 알지 못했다. 그러나 한동안 그는 아무 기력도 내지 못했고, 여섯 달의 휴가를 얻어 럼주와 당나귀 젖으로 몸을 추스른 뒤에야 일을 다시 잡을 수 있었다. 그 경우에는 군주라는 낱말의 남다른 쓰임이 그 관청의 거물이 미처 헤아려 보지도 못한 힘을 발휘했던 것이다. 이 이야기는 전해 내려오는 것이지만, 나는 그 일이 조지 3세 시대만큼이나 가까운 때에 있었다고 믿는다.\n\n존 임스는 소득세 사무국의 지금 위원장을 두고 아주 자유롭게 웃을 수 있었고, 그를 늙은 허플 스커플이니 뭐니 하고 부를 수 있었다. 그러나 막상 불려 가고 보니, 그 역시 급진적인 기질에도 불구하고 발목 관절에 힘이 조금 빠지는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 전갈을 듣는 순간부터 철도역에서의 그 일 때문에 불려 간다는 것을 알았다. 어쩌면 공공장소에서 주먹을 쓴 서기는 누구든 면직된다는 규정이 있을지도 몰랐다. 여러 죄목에 대해 면직이라는 벌을 지우는 규정이 많이 있었고—그런 규정을 어디선가 본 기억이 나는 것도 같았다. 그러나 그는 일어서서 제 동료들을 한 번 둘러보고는 터퍼를 따라 위원회실로 들어갔다.\n\n“조니가 늙은 스커플스한테 불려 갔군,” 한 서기가 말했다.\n\n“크로즈비와의 그 소동 때문이겠지,” 또 한 사람이 말했다. “그 일로 위원회가 그에게 어쩌지는 못해.”\n\n“못한다고?” 처음 사람이 말했다. “젊은 아우토나이츠는 「사이더 셀러스」에서의 그 소동 때문에 사촌인 콘스턴트 아우토나이츠 경이 할 수 있는 것을 다 해 주었는데도 사직해야 했잖나?”\n\n“하지만 그자는 융통 어음 때문에 아주 거덜이 나 있었지.”\n\n“어쨌든 나라면 한 푼을 준다 해도 임스의 처지가 되고 싶지는 않네. 크로즈비는 위원회 사무국의 사무국장인데, 스커플스가 여기 오기 전에 거기 위원장이었단 말이야. 그러니 둘이야 도둑들처럼 짝짜꿍이지. 그를 내려보내 사과하게 만든다 해도 나는 놀라지 않겠어.”\n\n“조니는 그러지 않을걸,” 다른 이가 말했다.\n\n그러는 동안 존 임스는 그 지엄한 어전에 서 있었다. 래플 버플 경은 아주 큰 방의 긴 탁자 상석에 놓인 커다란 참나무 팔걸이의자에 옥좌에 앉듯 앉아 있었고, 그 곁 탁자 모서리에는 사무국 부서기관 한 사람이 앉아 있었다. 위원회의 또 다른 위원 한 사람도 그 긴 탁자에서 일을 하고 있었으나, 그는 래플 경과 얼마쯤 떨어진 자리에서 서류를 읽고 서명하면서 이 광경에는 조금도 눈길을 주지 않았다. 곁에서 보고 있던 부서기관은 동료의 이런 무관심에 래플 경이 언짢아하는 것을 알아챌 수 있었으나, 그것은 모두 임스에게는 보이지 않는 일이었다.\n\n“임스 씨?” 래플 경이 유난히 거슬리는 목소리로, 이 자리를 위해 큼직한 코에 걸친 금테 안경 너머로 죄인을 바라보며 말했다. “저 사람이 임스 씨인가?”\n\n“예,” 부서기관이 말했다. “이 사람이 임스입니다.”\n\n“아!”—그러고서 잠시 뜸이 있었다. “좀 더 가까이 오게, 임스 씨.” 그러자 조니는 터키 융단 위를 소리 없이 걸어 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n“어디 보자. 2급이지, 그렇지? 아! 임스 씨, 내가 그레이트웨스턴 철도 회사 이사회의 사무국장에게서 편지를 한 통 받았다는 것을 아는가? 거기 적힌 정황이—그 편지에 적힌 대로가 사실이라면—자네의 명예를 아주 크게 떨어뜨리는 것이던데.”\n\n“어제 거기서 소동이 좀 있었습니다, 위원장님.”\n\n“소동이 좀 있었다고! 내가 보기에 자네는 아주 심각한 소동을 일으켰고, 나는 그레이트웨스턴 철도 회사 이사들에게 법이 제 갈 길을 가도록 두어야 한다고 알려야 할 것 같은데.”\n\n“저는 그런 건 조금도 개의치 않습니다, 위원장님,” 임스가 이런 국면에 오히려 얼굴이 조금 밝아지며 말했다.\n\n“개의치 않는다니, 이 사람아!” 래플 경이 말했다—아니, 앞에 선 죄인에게 그 말을 고함쳐 던졌다고 하는 편이 낫겠다. 나는 그가 지나쳤다고, 좀 더 부드러운 어조라면 거두었을 효과를 놓쳤다고 생각하는 편이다. 아마도 그에게는 왕의 편지 행낭에 관한 그 짧은 이야기에서 그토록 효험을 보였던 그 위엄 있는 태도와 극적으로 알맞은 목소리가 얼마쯤 모자랐던 모양이다. 실제로 조니는 흠칫 몸을 떨었지만, 흠칫한 뒤에는 그전보다 오히려 기분이 나아졌다. “개의치 않는다니, 이 사람아, 나라의 형사 법정에 끌려가 공공 승강장에서 저지른 난동으로 중죄인—아니, 차라리 경범죄인으로 벌을 받는 것을 말이야! 개의치 않는다고! 대체 무슨 소린가, 이 사람아?”\n\n“제 말씀은, 치안판사가 그 일로 별말을 하지 않으리라는 것입니다, 위원장님. 그리고 크로즈비 씨도 나서지 않을 거라고 생각합니다.”\n\n“그러나 크로즈비 씨는 나서야 하네, 젊은이. 그가 이 일을 더 따지고 싶어 하지 않는다고 해서 수도의 치안을 해친 난동이 벌을 면할 수 있다고 생각하나? 자네는 아무래도 아주 무지한 젊은이인 것 같군.”\n\n“아마 그렇겠지요,” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“참으로 무지해—참으로 무지하고말고. 그리고 자네는 아는가, 이 사람아, 그처럼 수치스러운 난동으로 경찰 치안판사에게 공공연히 벌을 받는다면 자네를 이 부서에 계속 두어도 좋은가 하는 것이 이 위원회의 위원들 사이에서 문제가 되리라는 것을?”\n\n조니는 다른 위원 쪽을 둘러보았으나, 그 신사는 서류에서 얼굴을 들지 않았다.\n\n“임스 씨는 아주 훌륭한 서기입니다,” 부서기관이 속삭였다. 그러나 그 말은 임스에게도 들리는 목소리였다. “우리에게 있는 젊은이들 가운데 가장 나은 축이지요,” 그가 덧붙였는데, 이 말은 들리지 않는 목소리였다.\n\n“오—아, 알겠네. 그럼 이렇게 말해 두지, 임스 씨. 이번 일이 자네에게 교훈이 되기를 바라네—아주 엄중한 교훈이 말이야.”\n\n부서기관은 의자에 등을 기대어 래플 경의 머리 뒤쪽으로 조금 물러나면서, 다른 위원의 눈길을 붙잡는 데 성공했다. 다른 위원은 얼굴을 거의 돌리지도 않은 채 살짝 웃었고, 그러자 부서기관도 웃었다. 임스가 그것을 보았고, 그도 웃었다.\n\n“자네가 저지른 그 치안 방해에 대해 앞으로 어떤 결과가 더 남아 있을지 나는 아직 말할 수 없네,” 래플 경이 말을 이었다. “이제 가 보게.”\n\n그리고 조니는 위원장의 위엄에 대한 존경심이 조금도 늘지 않은 채로 제자리로 돌아갔다.\n\n이튿날 아침 동료 하나가 아주 신이 나서 그에게 신문의 한 대목을 보여 주었다. 그가 크로즈비에게 어찌나 호되게 얻어맞았는지 그 매질 탓에 지금도 자리에 누워 있어야 한다고 온 세상에 알리는 대목이었다. 그러자 그의 노여움이 일었고, 그는 부서기관이며 수석 서기며 그 밖의 온갖 관청 어른들 앞이라는 것도 아랑곳없이 소득세 사무국의 큰 방을 이리저리 쿵쾅거리며, 언론의 죄악을 성토하고, 그런 뻔뻔한 거짓말이 퍼지도록 두는 나라에 사느니 러시아에 사는 편이 낫겠다는 제 의견을 늘어놓았다.\n\n“그자는 나를 건드리지도 못했네, 피셔. 건드려 보려고나 했는지도 모르겠어. 그러나 명예를 걸고 말하는데, 그자는 나를 건드리지 못했어.”\n\n“그렇지만 조니, 드 코시 백작의 딸에게 수작을 걸다니 자네도 대담했군,” 피셔가 말했다.\n\n“나는 그중 누구도 평생 본 적이 없네.”\n\n“이 친구는 이제 아예 귀족들 사이에서 놀아나는군,” 또 한 사람이 말했다. “자작 아래로는 거들떠보지도 않겠지?”\n\n“그 도둑놈 같은 편집자가 제 신문에 무엇을 싣든 내가 어쩌겠나? 매질이라니! 허플 스커플은 나더러 중죄인이라고 했지만, 그것도 이 작자에 비하면 절반도 고약하지 않아.” 그러고서 조니는 신문을 방 저쪽으로 걷어차 버렸다.\n\n“명예훼손으로 고소하게,” 피셔가 말했다.\n\n“특히 자네가 백작부인의 딸과 혼인하고 싶어 했다고 쓴 대목을 말이야,” 또 다른 서기가 말했다.\n\n“내 평생 이런 추문은 들어 본 적이 없네,” 세 번째 사람이 잘라 말했다. “게다가 그 아가씨가 자네를 거들떠보지도 않았다니.”\n\n그러나 그렇다고 해서, 조니 임스가 저희 가운데서 앞서 나가는 사람이 되어 가고 있으며 저마다 가까이 지내고 싶어 할 만한 사람이라는 것을 사무국 사람들이 못 느낀 것은 아니었다. 그리고 높으신 어른들 사이에서도 철도역의 이 일이 그에게 정말로 해가 되지는 않았다. 크로즈비가 매를 맞아 마땅했다는 것도, 임스가 그를 두들겨 주었다는 것도 다들 알고 있었다. 래플 버플 경이야 경찰 치안판사니 경범죄니 하고 떠들 수 있었겠지만, 소득세 사무국의 온 세상은 임스가 그 일에서 고개를 들고 오른발을 앞세우고 나왔다는 것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n“신문 따위는 마음에 두지 말게,” 생각 깊은 늙은 고참 서기가 그에게 말했다. “매를 맞은 건 그자고 자네는 아니니, 신문쯤은 웃어넘길 여유가 있지 않은가.”\n\n“그럼 편집자에게 편지는 쓰지 말라는 말씀입니까?”\n\n“아니, 아니, 절대 쓰지 말게. 신문에 대고 제 변명을 하겠다고 나서는 건 얼간이뿐이야—제 이름이 좀 오르내리기를 바라는 자가 아니라면 말이지. 자네가 복음처럼 참된 것을 쓴다 해도, 그자들은 그것을 우스개로 만드는 법을 안다네.”\n\n그리하여 조니는 편집자에게 분개한 편지를 쓰겠다는 생각을 접었다. 그러나 이 일의 전말을 드 게스트 백작에게 어떻게든 설명해야 한다고 느꼈다. 그 일은 그가 백작의 집에서 돌아오는 길에 벌어졌고, 이제는 제 모든 일이 그 다정한 벗에게 그토록 큰 관심거리가 되어 있었으므로, 백작이 신문에서 사실이든 거짓이든 그것을 알게 되도록 내버려 두는 것은 도리가 아니라고 생각했다. 그래서 그는 사무국을 나서기 전에 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다.\n\n소득세 사무국, 186-년 12월 29일.\n\n그는 이 귀족에게 첫머리를 어떤 투로 써야 할지를 두고 꽤나 오래 궁리했다. 지금까지 한 번도 편지를 써 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그는 종이 한 장에 “친애하는 백작님께”라고 시작해 보았다가, 너무 대담해 보인다는 생각에 밀쳐 두었다.\n\n백작님께서 제게 그토록 친절히 대해 주셨으니, 제가 게스트윅에서 돌아오던 날 아침 철도역에서 있었던 일을 말씀드려야 마땅하다고 느낍니다. 그 악당 크로즈비가 바체스터 분기역에서 저와 같은 객차에 올라, 런던까지 오는 내내 제 맞은편에 앉아 있었습니다. 저는 그에게 한마디도 하지 않았고 그도 제게 하지 않았습니다. 그러나 그가 패딩턴 역에서 내렸을 때, 저는 그를 그냥 보내서는 안 되겠다고 생각했고, 그래서 저는—제가 바라던 만큼 그를 두들겨 주었다고는 말씀드리지 못하겠습니다. 그러나 해 보기는 했고, 그의 눈에 시퍼런 멍을 하나 안겨 주었습니다. 순경들이 잔뜩 몰려와 저희를 둘러싸는 바람에 저는 제대로 겨룰 틈이 없었습니다. 백작님께서 제가 잘못했다고 여기시리라는 것을 압니다. 어쩌면 정말 잘못이었겠지요. 그러나 그자가 두 시간 동안 제 앞에 앉아, 제가 보기에 스스로를 온 런던에서 제일가는 사내로 여기는 듯한 낯을 하고 있는데, 제가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠습니까?\n\n신문 하나에 끔찍한 대목을 실어 놓았습니다. 제가 어찌나 “매를 맞았는지” 그 뒤로 꼼짝도 못 하고 있다는 것입니다. 그것은 지독한 거짓이며, 그 신문 기사의 나머지도 죄다 마찬가지입니다. 저는 손끝 하나 닿지 않았습니다. 그자는 황소만큼 까다로운 상대가 전혀 아니었고, 모든 것을 아주 조용히 받아들이는 듯했습니다. 다만 그자가 마땅히 받았어야 할 만큼 얻어맞지는 않았다는 것은 인정해야겠습니다.\n\n백작님의 벗이신 R. B. 경께서 오늘 아침 저를 부르시더니 제가 중죄인이라고 하셨습니다. 저는 그 말에는 그리 마음 쓰지 않았습니다. 그분이야 저를 살인자라 부르든 강도라 부르든 마찬가지였을 테니까요. 그러나 제가 백작님을 노엽게 해 드렸다면 그것은 정말로 마음이 쓰일 것입니다. 그렇지만 제가 가장 두려운 것은 다른 어떤 분의 노여움입니다—앨링턴에 계신.\n\n백작님, 크나큰 은혜를 입은, 더없이 진실한 자로 저를 믿어 주시기를 바라며,\n\n“기회만 있으면 그 녀석이 그럴 줄 알았지,” 백작은 편지를 다 읽고 나서 말했다. 그러고는 두 손을 마주 치기도 하고 조끼 주머니에 엄지를 찔러 넣기도 하면서 방 안을 이리저리 걸었다. “그 녀석이 제대로 된 재목으로 만들어졌다는 걸 내가 알았어.” 그러면서 백작은 제가 아끼는 젊은이의 무용에 크게 기뻐했다. “내가 그 자리에 있었더라면 나라도 그렇게 했을 거야. 정말 그랬을 것 같아.” 그러고서 그는 아침 식사 방으로 돌아가 레이디 줄리아에게 이야기했다. “이게 무슨 일인 줄 아나?” 그가 말했다. “조니 임스가 크로즈비를 만나서 아주 지독하게 두들겨 줬다는군.”\n\n“저런!” 레이디 줄리아가 신문과 안경을 내려놓으며 말했다. 그이의 눈빛이 드러낸 것은 그 못된 짓에 대한 기독교인다운 경악과는 도무지 거리가 먼 것이었다.\n\n“정말 그랬다니까. 만나기만 하면 그럴 줄 알았어.”\n\n“때렸다고요! 정말로 때렸어요!”\n\n“두 눈에 시퍼렇게 멍을 들여 레이디 알렉산드리나에게 돌려보냈다는군.”\n\n“두 눈을 다요! 이런 개구쟁이 같으니! 그런데 제 몸은 다치지 않았대요?”\n\n“긁힌 데도 없다는군.”\n\n“그럼 그 사람들이 조니를 어떻게 할까요?”\n\n“아무것도 못 하지. 크로즈비도 무슨 일을 벌일 만큼 바보는 아닐 거야. 그런 수작을 부린 사내는 무법자가 되는 법이야. 누구든 그자에게 손을 대도 벌을 받지 않지. 그자는 낯을 들고 다닐 수가 없어. 제가 한 일에 대해 나서서 물음에 답할 수도 없고. 법이 건드리지 못하면서도 세상의 감정을 너무나 크게 거스르는 죄가 있어서, 그런 것은 누구라도 스스로 나서서 벌하는 노릇을 떠맡아도 되는 거야. 그자는 매를 맞았고, 그 일은 죽을 때까지 그자에게 들러붙어 있을 걸세.”\n\n“다치지 않았기를 바란다고 조니에게 꼭 전해 주세요,” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다. 이 노부인은 그의 무훈을 대놓고 축하해 줄 수는 없었으나, 그다음으로 할 수 있는 일은 했다.\n\n그러나 백작은 제가 옳다고 여긴다는 것을 숨김없이 온전히 밝히면서 그를 축하해 주었다.\n\n“나는,” 그가 말했다. “내가 자네 나이에 같은 처지였다면 나도 그렇게 했기를 바라네. 그리고 그자가 황소보다 다루기 쉬웠다니 참으로 기쁘군. 크로즈비 도련님쯤에는 자네가 누구의 도움도 필요로 하지 않았으리라는 것을 나는 아주 잘 알고 있네. 앨링턴에 있는 그 다른 사람으로 말하자면, 내가 그런 일을 조금이라도 안다면, 그이는 자네를 용서할 걸세.” 그러나 백작이 그런 일을 조금이라도 알았는지는 의문일 수 있다. 그러고서 그는 추신으로 이렇게 덧붙였다. “다음에 내게 편지할 때는—그리고 너무 오래 끌지 말게—편지 첫머리를 ‘친애하는 드 게스트 백작님께’라고 시작하게. 그것이 제대로 된 법식일세.”\n\n# 제37장. 어느 노인의 하소연\n\n“저번에 내가 한 말을 다시 생각해 보았니, 벨?” 어느 날 아침 버나드가 사촌에게 말했다.\n\n“그 일을 생각하다니, 버나드? 내가 왜 그 일을 더 생각해야 하지? 나는 네가 그 일을 잊어버렸기를 바라고 있었어.”\n\n“아니.” 그가 말했다. “나는 그렇게 마음이 가벼운 사람이 아니야. 나는 이런 일을 말 한 마리 사는 일처럼 여길 수는 없어. 말이야 내 주머니 사정에 견주어 너무 비싸다는 것을 알게 되면 서운할 것도 없이 포기할 수 있지만 말이야. 나는 내 마음을 확실히 알기 전까지는 너를 사랑한다는 말을 하지 않았고, 일단 확실히 해 둔 다음에는 조금도 달라질 수가 없어.”\n\n“그러면서도 나더러는 달라지라는 거구나.”\n\n“그래, 물론 그렇지. 네 마음이 지금 비어 있다면, 네가 어떤 남자를 사랑하게 되기 전에 그 마음은 당연히 달라져야 하잖아. 그런 변화라면 바랄 만한 것이지. 하지만 네가 일단 사랑을 하게 되고 나면, 그때는 너를 바꾸기가 쉽지 않을 거야.”\n\n“하지만 나는 사랑한 적이 없는걸.”\n\n“그렇다면 나에게는 바랄 권리가 있는 셈이지. 벨, 나는 여기 있어야 할 것보다 오래 머물러 있었어. 이 이야기를 다시 하지 않고는 너를 두고 떠날 수가 없었기 때문이야. 나는 너에게 끈덕지게 조르는 사람으로 보이고 싶지 않았고—”\n\n“네가 내 말을 그저 믿어만 준다면 좋으련만.”\n\n“믿지 않는 것이 아니야. 나는 네가 나를 사랑할 수밖에 없다고 스스로 우쭐댈 만큼 얼간이나 바보는 아니야. 나는 너를 충분히 믿어. 하지만 그래도 네 마음이 달라지는 일은 있을 수 있잖아.”\n\n“그런 일은 있을 수 없어.”\n\n“삼촌이나 네 어머니가 이 일로 너에게 말씀하셨는지 나는 모르겠어.”\n\n“그런 말씀은 아무 소용도 없을 거야.”\n\n사실 그녀의 어머니는 그녀에게 말을 했었다. 그러나 그런 말은 아무 소용도 없으리라고 한 그녀의 말은 참말이었다. 그녀의 사촌이 제 솜씨로 이 싸움을 이길 수 없다면, 그가 알고 있는 그녀의 성품에 비추어 볼 때 남의 솜씨를 빌려서도 이길 수 없으리라는 것은 그로서도 아주 분명히 알 수 있는 일이었다.\n\n“우리는 모두 몹시 불행해졌어.” 그가 말을 이었다. “가엾은 릴리에게 닥친 이 재앙 때문에 말이야.”\n\n“그리고 릴리가 정말로 사랑했던 남자에게 속았다고 해서, 나는 내가 사랑하지 않는 남자와 혼인해서 셈을 맞추어야 한다는—” 그러고서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. “버나드, 너에게 모질게 들릴 말을 내가 하도록 몰아붙이지는 말아 줘.”\n\n“네가 이미 한 말보다 더 모진 말은 없어. 하지만 벨, 그래도 내 말은 들어 줄 수 있잖아.”\n\n그러고 나서 그는 그녀에게, 데일 집안의 모든 일에 비추어 볼 때 그녀가 자기의 청을 좋게 보아 주려고 애쓰는 것이 얼마나 바람직한 일인지를 이야기했다. 그것은 우리 모두에게 좋은 일이 될 것이며, 특히 지금 삼촌이 그토록 다정하게 마음을 쓰고 있는 릴리에게 좋을 것이라고 그는 말했다. 버나드가 호소한 바로는, 삼촌은 이 혼사를 마음속으로 몹시 바라고 있어서 뜻대로만 되면 무엇을 청하든 다 들어줄 터였다. 그러나 이 일에서 뜻을 이루지 못한다면 그는 언짢아할 만한 까닭이 생겼다고 여길 터였다.\n\n벨은 들어 달라는 부탁을 받았으므로 아주 참을성 있게 들었다. 그러나 사촌의 말이 끝났을 때 그녀의 대답은 아주 짧았다. “삼촌이 무슨 말씀을 하시든, 무슨 생각을 하시든, 무슨 일을 하시든, 이 일은 조금도 달라지지 않아.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 너는 다른 사람들의 행복은 아무것도 생각하지 않겠다는 거구나.”\n\n“다른 사람들의 행복을 아무리 확실하게 지켜 줄 수 있다 해도, 나는 사랑하지 않는 남자와 혼인하지는 않겠어. 적어도 그래서는 안 된다는 것쯤은 알고 있어. 하지만 이 혼인으로 내가 누구의 행복을 지켜 줄 수 있으리라고는 믿지 않아. 네 행복은 분명히 아니고.”\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤 버나드는 제 앞길에 놓인 어려움이 크다는 것을 스스로 인정했다. “내년 가을까지는 떠나 있겠습니다.” 그가 삼촌에게 말했다.\n\n“네가 그 직업을 그만두고 여기 머문다면 그 아이도 그렇게까지 비뚜로 굴지는 않을 게다.”\n\n“그럴 수는 없습니다, 어르신. 그런 요행에 제 평생의 안녕을 걸 수는 없습니다.” 그러자 삼촌은 조카딸에게 그랬듯이 조카에게도 화를 냈다. 홧김에 그는 제수에게 다시 한번 가 보기로 마음먹었고, 어딘가 사리에 닿지 않는 방식으로, 그녀가 어머니로서 지닌 영향력을 다 써서 자기를 돕기를 마다한다면 그녀에게도 몹시 화를 내는 것이 마땅하리라고 작정했다.\n\n“저 아이들이 둘 다 혼인해서 안 될 까닭이 무엇인가?” 그는 혼잣말을 했다. 젊은 임스에 관한 드 게스트 경의 제안은 매우 후한 것이었다. 그때 그가 밝혔듯이 그는 당장에는 제 의견을 말할 수 없었다. 그러나 백작이 한 말을 곰곰이 되새겨 본 끝에, 그는 그런 치유가 조금이라도 가능하기만 하다면 백작이 내놓은 방식으로 집안의 상처를 기꺼이 아물게 하고 싶어 하는 자신을 발견했다. 그러나 그 일을 지금 당장 할 수는 없었다. 때가 오면—그리고 그때가 곧, 어쩌면 날이 화창해지고 저녁이 다시 길어지는 봄쯤에 오리라고 그는 생각했다—그는 그 새 살림을 차려 주는 데 백작과 함께 제 몫을 지는 것이 기꺼울 터였다. 크로즈비에게는 아무것도 주기를 거절했었고, 그렇게 거절한 일로 그의 양심에는 얼마간 뉘우침의 그늘이 드리워져 있었다. 그러나 릴리가 이 다른 남자를 사랑하게 될 수만 있다면 그는 손을 더 크게 열 작정이었다. 그 아이는 사실상 제 딸이나 다름없이 제 몫을 받게 되리라. 그러나 그렇다면, 작은 집 사람들을 위해 그만한 일을 할 뜻이 있는 마당에, 그들도 그 답례로 그를 위해 무언가 해 주어야 하지 않겠는가? 그렇게 생각하며 그는 제수에게 다시 갔다. 둘 사이에 모진 말이 오갈 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 제 뜻을 밝히기로 작정한 것이었다. 제 자신에 대해서라면 그는 자기에게 쏟아지는 모진 말 따위는 그다지 개의치 않았다. 그는 사람들의 말이란 으레 모질고 아프기 마련이라고 여기다시피 했다. 그는 다정하고 부드러운 인사말이 주는 위안 같은 것은 바라지 않았고, 설령 그런 것이 그에게 왔다 한들 아마 고맙게 여기지도 않았으리라. 그는 정원을 거닐고 있던 데일 부인을 붙들어 제 방으로 데려왔다. 그녀의 집에서보다 그곳에서가 더 승산이 있으리라고 느꼈던 것이다. 그녀는 그 방에서 훈계를 듣는 것을 예전부터 싫어했던 터라 이 만남을 피하려고 애썼으나 뜻을 이루지 못했다.\n\n“그래서 그 저택에서 존 임스를 만났소.” 그가 정원에서 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n“아, 그래요? 거기서 그 애는 어떻게 지냈답니까? 가엾은 조니가 백작과 그 누이와 함께 휴가를 보내는 모습은 도무지 그려지지가 않아요. 그 애는 그분들께 어떻게 굴었고, 그분들은 그 애에게 어떻게 하셨답니까?”\n\n“장담하건대 그 애는 거기서 아주 편안해 보였소.”\n\n“정말요? 그렇다면 그 일이 그 애에게 도움이 되면 좋겠어요. 그 애가 참으로 착한 젊은이라는 것은 저도 잘 알아요. 다만 좀 어설플 뿐이지요.”\n\n“나는 그 애가 어설프다는 생각은 조금도 들지 않았소. 제수씨도 곧 알게 될 거요, 그 애가 아주 잘해 나가리라는 것을. 제 아버지가 그랬던 것보다 훨씬 낫게 말이오.”\n\n“정말 그러기를 바라요.” 그러고 나서 데일 부인은 빠져나가려고 애를 썼다. 그러나 지주는 그녀를 사로잡아 제 포로로 삼아서는 집 안으로 이끌었다. “제수씨.” 그녀를 자리에 앉히자마자 그가 말했다. “내 조카와 조카딸 사이의 이 일을 이제는 매듭지어야 할 때요.”\n\n“매듭지을 것이 아무것도 없을까 걱정이에요.”\n\n“그게 무슨 말이오? 제수씨가 그 일을 못마땅해한다는 거요?”\n\n“결코 아니에요. 개인적으로는요. 저도 아주 강하게 찬성해요. 하지만 그것은 이 문제와 아무 상관도 없어요.”\n\n“아니, 상관이 있소. 미안한 말이지만 상관이 있어야 하고 또 있어야 마땅하며, 아주 큰 상관이 있소. 물론 이제 와서 누구든 누구와 혼인하도록 억지로 시킬 수 있다는 말을 하는 것은 아니오.”\n\n“그러시지 않기를 바라요.”\n\n“나는 그래야 한다고 말한 적도 없고 그렇게 생각한 적도 없소. 하지만 잘 자란 처녀라면 제 집안 사람들 모두의 바람이 아주 큰 무게를 지녀야 한다고는 생각하오.”\n\n“벨이 잘 자랐는지는 저도 모르겠어요. 하지만 이런 일에서라면 누구의 바람도 그 애에게는 깃털만 한 무게조차 되지 못할 거예요. 그리고 사실 저는 감히 제 바람을 말로 꺼낼 수조차 없어요. 시아주버니께는 이렇게 말씀드릴 수 있지요. 그 애가 제 사촌을 시아주버니께서 바라시는 대로 여겨 줄 수 있었다면 저는 아주 기뻤으리라고요.”\n\n“그 아이에게 그렇게 말하기가 두렵다는 거요?”\n\n“옳지 않다고 여기는 일을 하기가 두렵다는 거예요. 그런 뜻으로 하신 말씀이라면요.”\n\n“나는 그것이 옳지 않다고 생각하지 않소. 그러니 내가 직접 그 아이에게 말하겠소.”\n\n“그건 마음대로 하세요, 데일 씨. 제가 막을 수는 없으니까요. 그런 일로 그 애를 들볶으시는 것은 잘못이라고 저는 생각할 거예요. 그리고 그 애의 대답이 마음에 흡족하지 않으실 것도 걱정스럽고요. 그 애에게 뜻을 말씀하시겠다면 그렇게 하셔야지요. 물론 저는 잘못하시는 것이라고 생각할 테고요, 그뿐이에요.”\n\n이 말을 할 때 데일 부인의 목소리는 꽤나 매서웠고 낯빛도 그러했다. 그녀는 삼촌이 조카딸에게 제 뜻을 말하는 것을 막을 수는 없었으나, 누가 제 딸의 일에 참견한다는 생각만은 각별히 싫었다. 지주는 자리에서 일어나 방 안을 오가며, 노여움 없이 조리 있게 그녀에게 대답할 수 있도록 마음을 가라앉히려 애썼다.\n\n“이제 가도 될까요?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“가도 되겠느냐고? 물론 가고 싶으면 가도 되오. 내가 내 딸로 여기려고 애쓰는 두 아이의 안녕을 두고 내가 제수씨에게 말하는 것이—다만 그 아이들이 나를 사랑하도록 배운 적이 한 번도 없다는 것만은 나도 알고 있소만—그 아이들의 앞날을 걱정하는 것이 내 쪽의 참견이라고 여긴다면, 물론 가도 좋소.”\n\n“언짢게 해 드릴 말씀을 하려던 것은 아니었어요, 데일 씨.”\n\n“언짢다니! 내가 언짢든 말든 그게 무슨 대수요? 나에게는 자식이 없으니, 조카들과 조카딸들을 건사하는 것이 물론 내 삶의 유일한 일거리요. 그 아이들이 그 답례로 나를 사랑해 주기를 바란다면 나는 늙은 바보지. 그리고 내가 감히 무슨 바람이라도 입에 올릴라치면 나는 참견을 하는 것이고 잘못을 저지르는 것이고! 야박하오. 참으로 야박하오. 그 아이들이 나를 싫어하도록 자라 왔다는 것을 나도 잘 알고 있소. 그런데도 나는 그 아이들에게 내 도리를 다하려고 애쓰고 있단 말이오.”\n\n“데일 씨, 그런 나무람은 들을 까닭이 없는 것이에요. 그 아이들은 데일 씨를 싫어하도록 자라지 않았어요. 저는 그 아이들이 제 삼촌으로서 데일 씨를 사랑하고 또 존경해 왔다고 믿어요. 하지만 그런 사랑과 존경이 그 아이들의 혼처를 마음대로 정할 권리를 드리지는 않아요.”\n\n“누가 그 아이들의 혼처를 마음대로 정하겠다는 거요?”\n\n“어떤 삼촌도, 어떤 부모도 참견해서는 안 된다고 제가 생각하는 일들이 있어요. 그리고 그 모든 일 가운데서도 이것이 으뜸이고요. 그렇게 말씀드린 뒤에도 그 애에게 뜻을 이야기하시겠다면, 물론 그렇게 하실 수 있지요.”\n\n“제수씨가 그 아이를 나에게서 등 돌리게 해 놓은 마당에 그래 봐야 별 소용도 없겠지.”\n\n“데일 씨, 저에게 그런 말씀을 하실 권리는 없어요. 그러시는 것은 아주 부당해요. 제가 제 딸들을 데일 씨께 등 돌리게 만들었다고 여기신다면, 저희가 앨링턴을 아주 떠나 버리는 편이 훨씬 낫겠어요. 저는 제 아이들에게 도리를 다하기 어려운 처지에 놓여 왔어요. 그래도 제 개인적인 바람은 돌아보지 않고 그 도리를 다하려고 애써 왔고요. 하지만 제가 아이들에게 데일 씨를 나쁘게 여기도록 가르쳤다는 말을 들어야 한다면, 아이들을 여기 붙들어 두는 것이야말로 제 잘못이 되리라고 아주 확신해요. 정말이지 저는 그런 말을 저에게 하시는 것을 견딜 수가 없어요.”\n\n이 모든 말을 데일 부인은 단호한 태도로, 그리고 상처를 입었다는 느낌이 배어 나오는 목소리로 했다. 그래서 지주는 그녀가 몹시 진심이라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n“사실이 아니란 말이오?” 그가 스스로를 변호하며 말했다. “그 아이들에 관한 모든 일에서 제수씨가 여태껏 나를 의심의 눈으로 보아 왔다는 것이?”\n\n“아니에요, 사실이 아니에요.” 그러고 나서 그녀는 지주의 마지막 말에 얼마간 진실이 있다고 느끼며 스스로 말을 고쳤다. “의심은 분명히 아니에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “하지만 이야기가 여기까지 온 마당이니, 제 참된 심정이 어떠했는지 밝히겠어요. 세상살이의 일에서 시아주버니는 제 아이들을 위해 많은 일을 해 주실 수 있고, 또 많이 해 주셨어요.”\n\n“그리고 더 해 주고 싶소.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“그러시리라고 저도 믿어요. 하지만 그렇다고 해서 그 아이들의 하나뿐인 살아 있는 부모라는 제 자리를 내놓을 수는 없어요. 그 아이들은 제 자식이지 시아주버니의 자식이 아니니까요. 그리고 설령 제가 마음을 굽혀 시아주버니께서 그 아이들의 후견인이자 타고난 보호자 노릇을 하시도록 허락한다 해도, 그 아이들이 그런 처사에 동의하지 않을 거예요. 그것을 의심이라고 부르실 수는 없어요.”\n\n“시샘이라고는 부를 수 있소.”\n\n“그러면 어머니가 제 아이들의 사랑을 두고 시샘하지 말아야 하나요?”\n\n이러는 내내 지주는 두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 방 안을 오르내리고 있었다. 그리고 데일 부인이 마지막 말을 하고 나서도 그는 한동안 말없이 걸음을 이어 갔다.\n\n“제수씨가 속을 털어놓은 것이 잘된 일인지도 모르겠소.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“저를 나무라시는 그 방식이 그럴 수밖에 없게 만들었지요.”\n\n“나는 제수씨를 나무랄 뜻이 없었고 지금도 그렇지 않소. 하지만 제수씨가 나에게 몹시 야박했고 지금도 야박하다고 나는 생각하오. 참으로 야박하오. 나는 내가 누린 만큼의 넉넉함을 제수씨의 아이들과, 또 제수씨 자신과도 나누려고 애써 왔소. 제수씨의 편안함과 그 아이들의 행복을 더해 주려고 힘써 왔소. 그 아이들의 앞날을 지켜 주고 싶은 마음이 간절하오. 그 아이들을 위해 이것을 받기를 마다했다면 제수씨가 아주 잘못한 것이었겠지. 하지만 그 답례로, 그만한 호의에 으레 따라오기 마련인 애정과 순종만큼은 나에게 아까워하지 않아도 좋았으리라고 나는 생각하오.”\n\n“데일 씨, 저는 그 가운데 무엇도 아까워한 적이 없어요.”\n\n“나는 상처를 받았소. 상처를 받았소.” 그가 말을 이었다. 그리고 그녀는 그가 여느 때와 달리 뜨겁게 말하는 것보다도 그의 아파하는 얼굴빛에 더 놀랐다. “제수씨가 한 말이 줄곧 사실이었다는 것을 나도 알고 있었소. 그러나 그렇게 느껴 오기는 했어도, 제수씨의 입에서 터놓고 나온 그 말에 내가 상처를 받은 것은 인정하오.”\n\n“제 자식은 언제까지나 제 자식이어야 한다고 말했다고 해서요?”\n\n“아, 제수씨는 그보다 더한 말을 했소. 제수씨와 그 아이들이 여기, 내 곁에서 산 지가—이제 몇 해나 되었소?—그 세월 내내 나를 향한 다정한 마음이라고는 자라나지 않았소. 내가 듣지도 보지도 느끼지도 못한다고 생각하오? 내가 바보라서 모른다고 여기는 거요? 제수씨로 말하자면, 남의 눈 때문에 그래야 한다고 스스로를 억누르지 않는다면 이 집에 발도 들이지 않았을 거요. 아마 다 그래야 마땅한 대로겠지. 나에게 자식이 없으니 나는 조카딸들에게 부모의 도리를 지고 있소. 그러나 그 답례로 사랑이든 존중이든 순종이든 그 아이들에게 바랄 권리는 나에게 없소. 제수씨가 바라지도 않는데 내가 여기 붙들어 두고 있다는 것을 나도 알고 있소. 더는 그러지 않으리다.” 그러고서 그는 이제 가도 좋다는 몸짓을 그녀에게 해 보였다.\n\n자리에서 일어서면서 그녀의 마음은 그를 향해 누그러졌다. 요즈음 들어 그는 두 아가씨에게 많은 다정함을 보여 주었다. 여태껏 그에게서 나온 적이 없던, 사랑의 부드러움에 더 가까운 다정함이었다. 릴리의 처지는 그의 준엄함마저 녹여 놓은 듯했고, 그는 말씨와 몸가짐에서 부드러워지려고 애써 왔다. 그리고 지금 그는 그 아이들을 사랑했건만 그 사랑이 헛되었다는 듯이 말하고 있었다. 그가 제수에게 껄끄러운 이웃이었던 것은 틀림없었다. 그가 손을 편 것이 결코 그녀 자신을 위해서였던 적은 없다고 그녀가 늘 느끼게 만들었으니 말이다. 그가 저도 모르는 사이에, 아이들에 대한 그녀의 권위를 허물고 그것을 제가 차지하고 싶은 마음에 움직였던 것도 틀림없었다. 그가 그녀를 제 아우와 혼인한 그날부터 곱지 않은 눈으로 보아 왔던 것도 틀림없었다. 그녀는 그를 알게 된 첫날부터 이 모든 것을 예민하게 느껴 왔고, 작은 집에 살기 시작한 첫 한두 해 동안 그와 정답게 지내 보려고 기울였던 노력이 실패로 돌아간 뒤로는 그 어느 때보다도 예민하게 느껴 왔다. 그러나 그럼에도 불구하고, 이 모든 것에도 불구하고 지금 그녀의 가슴은 그를 위해 피를 흘렸다. 그녀는 아이들에 대한 제 자리를 온전히 지켜 냄으로써 그를 이겼다. 그러나 지금 그가 그 싸움에서 졌노라고 하소연하니 그녀의 가슴은 그를 위해 피를 흘렸다.\n\n“시아주버니.” 그녀가 말했다. 그러면서 그에게 두 손을 내밀었다. “어쩌면 우리는 서로를 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 다정하게 여기지 못했는지도 모르겠어요.”\n\n“나는 애를 썼소.” 노인이 말했다. “나는 애를 썼소—” 그러고는 말을 멈추었다. 북받치는 감정에 막혔거나, 아니면 제 뜻을 나타내는 데 꼭 필요한 말을 찾지 못했거나 한 것이었다.\n\n“우리 한 번만 더 애써 봐요. 둘 다요.”\n\n“뭐라고, 일흔이 다 되어서 다시 시작을 하라고! 아니오, 제수씨. 나에게 다시 시작하는 일 따위는 이제 없소. 이 모든 일이 그 아이들에게는 아무런 차이도 만들지 않을 거요. 내가 여기 있는 한 그 아이들은 그 집을 갖게 될 거요. 혼인을 한다면 내가 할 수 있는 일을 해 주겠소. 버나드가 그 구애에 아주 진지하다고 나는 믿소. 그리고 벨이 그 애의 말에 귀를 기울인다면 그 아이는 앨링턴의 안주인으로서 여전히 이곳에 맞아들여질 거요. 제수씨가 한 말은 아무런 차이도 만들지 않을 거요. 그러나 다시 시작하는 것으로 말하자면, 그것은 그저 있을 수 없는 일이오.”\n\n그 뒤에 데일 부인은 홀로 정원을 지나 집으로 걸어갔다. 그는 저희가 살고 있는 그 집이 제가 살아 있는 동안 그녀에게가 아니라 그녀의 아이들에게 빌려주는 것임을 일부러 못 박아 말했다. 그는 더 따뜻하게 여기겠다는 그녀의 뜻을 딱 잘라 물리쳤다. 그는 두 사람이 서로를 거의 원수처럼 여겨야 한다는 것을 그녀가 알아듣게 만들었다. 다만 그녀는 원수이면서도 여전히 그의 후한 베풂을 누리도록 허락받으리라는 것도, 그리고 그것은 그가 제 조카딸들에게 도리를 다하기로 마음먹었기 때문이라는 것도!\n\n“우리는 이곳을 떠나는 편이 낫겠어.” 응접실 난롯가의 제 안락의자에 앉으며 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n# 제38장. 크로프츠 선생을 부르다\n\n데일 부인이 응접실에 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 한동안 그녀의 마음을 이사 문제에서 떼어 놓는 소식이 전해졌다. “엄마,” 벨이 방으로 들어서며 말했다. “아무래도 제인이 성홍열에 걸린 것 같아요.” 응접실 하녀 제인은 지난 이틀 동안 앓아 왔으나, 여태 대수로운 병이라고는 아무도 의심하지 않았다.\n\n데일 부인은 그 자리에서 벌떡 일어섰다. “누가 그 애 곁에 있느냐?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n벨의 대답으로 보아 그녀와 릴리가 둘 다 그 아이 곁에 있었고, 릴리는 아직도 그 방에 있는 모양이었다. 그 말에 데일 부인은 위층으로 달려 올라갔고, 집 안은 별안간 소란스러워졌다. 한 시간 남짓 지나 마을 의사가 와서, 그 아이의 병은 틀림없이 성홍열이라는 소견을 내놓았다. 데일 부인은 그것으로 만족하지 못했다. 그녀는 여러 해 전부터 그 약제사의 의술이 지닌 평판에 줄곧 반대해 온 터라, 크로프츠 선생을 부르러 게스트윅으로 아이를 하나 보냈고, 두 딸에게는 가엾은 제인을 다시는 찾아가지 말라고 단호히 명령했다. 그녀 자신은 성홍열을 앓은 적이 있으니 마음대로 해도 괜찮았다. 그러고 나서 간병인도 한 사람 들였다.\n\n이 모든 일이 몇 시간 동안 데일 부인의 생각의 물길을 돌려놓았다. 그러나 저녁이 되자 그녀는 그날 아침 나눈 대화의 주제로 되돌아갔고, 세 여자는 잠자리에 들기 전에 그 문제를 두고 함께 공개 작전 회의를 열었다. 크로프츠 선생은 게스트윅에 없는 것으로 밝혀졌고, 대신 그가 이튿날 아침 일찍 앨링턴으로 건너오리라는 전갈이 왔다. 데일 부인은 자기가 아끼는 하녀의 병이 성홍열은 아니라고 거의 마음을 굳혔으나, 그렇다고 해서 딸들을 그 하녀의 머리맡에 얼씬도 못 하게 한 명령을 늦추지는 않았다.\n\n“당장 나가요.” 벨이 말했다. 그녀는 어머니보다도 더 삼촌 쪽의 어떤 지배도 참지 못하는 편이었다. 그들 사이에 오간 그 논의에서 버나드의 구혼 이야기가 통째로 도마 위에 올랐던 것이다. 벨은 사촌의 청혼을 할 수 있는 데까지 오래 저 혼자 간직해 두었으나, 삼촌이 그 문제를 데일 부인에게 들이민 마당에 더 이상 입을 다물고 있기란 불가능했다. “엄마도 제가 그이와 혼인하기를 바라지는 않으시죠?” 어머니가 버나드에 대해 얼마쯤 다정한 기색으로 말하자 벨은 그렇게 말했다. 그에 답하여 데일 부인은 자기에게 그런 바람은 조금도 없다고 격하게 잘라 말했고, 여전히 크로프츠 선생에 대한 믿음을 버리지 않고 있던 릴리도 거의 같은 정도로 열을 올렸다. 그들 모두에게, 삼촌이 자기에게서 받은 금전적 도움을 빌미로 그들 자신의 인생관에 어떤 식으로든 간섭하려 든다는 생각은 유별나게 역겨웠다. 그중에서도 특히 역겨운 것은 그가 그들의 혼인 처분에 대해 의견 따위를 가지려 든다는 점이었다. 그들은 서로에게 이렇게 못 박았다. 어머니만 찬성한다면, 두 사람 중 누가 어떤 혼인을 마음에 두든 삼촌에게는 그것을 반대할 권리가 있을 수 없다고. 가엾은 늙은 지주가 제 자신이 의심의 눈초리로 여겨지고 있다고 말한 것은 옳았다. 그는 정말로 그렇게 여겨지고 있었다. 그 잘못은 분명 그 자신의 것이었다. 어머니의 마음을 얻는 일은 제 수고를 들일 값어치가 없다고 여기면서 딸들의 마음만 얻으려 애썼기 때문이다. 딸들은 그런 시도가 행해지고 있음을 저도 모르게 느꼈고, 그것에 맹렬히 반발했다. 그들이 삼촌의 집에 들어와 살게 되고, 그의 조랑말을 타게 되고, 그의 빵을 얼마간 얻어먹게 된 것은 그들의 잘못이 아니었다. 그들은 그렇게 먹고 그렇게 살았으며, 스스로 고마워하고 있노라고 말해 왔다. 지주는 그 나름으로 좋은 사람이었고 그들도 그 선함을 인정했다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서, 자식으로서 어머니에게 바쳐야 할 그 충심을 한 톨이라도 그에게 옮겨 줄 생각은 없었다. 어머니가 그날 아침 삼촌이 한 말을 설명하며 그 이야기를 들려주자, 그들은 그가 그토록 상심하게 된 것을 안타까워했다. 그러나 어머니를 향해서는, 어머니야말로 죄를 지은 쪽이 아니라 죄를 입은 쪽이라고 힘주어 못 박았다.\n\n“당장 나가요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“말이 쉽지, 하기는 훨씬 어렵단다, 얘야.”\n\n“물론 그렇죠, 엄마. 안 그랬으면 우리가 지금 여기 있지도 않았을 테고요. 제 말은 이런 뜻이에요. 꼭 필요한 첫걸음을 당장 떼자는 거예요. 삼촌은 우리가 여기 머무는 이상 자기에게 우리에 대한 어떤 권리가 생긴다고 여기시는 게 분명해요. 그렇게 생각하시는 게 잘못이라는 말은 아니에요. 어쩌면 그게 당연한 일인지도 몰라요. 어쩌면 그분의 호의를 받는 이상, 우리는 그분께 우리 자신을 굽혀야 하는 건지도 모르고요. 만약 그렇다면, 그거야말로 우리가 나가야 할 결정적인 이유예요.”\n\n“헌 부인처럼 우리도 삼촌께 이 집 집세를 내면 안 될까요?” 릴리가 말했다. “그렇게만 할 수 있다면 엄마는 여기 그냥 계시고 싶으시죠?”\n\n“하지만 그럴 수는 없단다, 릴리. 우리는 이 집보다 작은 집을, 그리고 정원 유지비가 딸리지 않은 집을 골라야 해. 여기 집세를 어지간한 액수만 낸다고 해도 우리에게는 여기서 살아갈 형편이 못 될 거야.”\n\n“마른 빵과 차만 먹고 살아도요?” 릴리가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“하지만 나는 너희가 마른 빵과 차로 연명하기를 바라지 않는단다. 게다가 사실 나부터도 그런 식단에는 진저리가 나고 말 것 같구나.”\n\n“절대 아니실걸요, 엄마.” 릴리가 말했다. “저로 말하자면 양갈비가 그립다는 것을 실토하겠어요. 하지만 엄마는 그런 상스러운 것을 탐내신 적이 없을 것 같아요.”\n\n“어쨌든 여기 남아 있기란 불가능해요.” 벨이 말했다. “크리스토퍼 삼촌은 엄마한테서 집세를 받으려 하지 않으실 테고, 설령 받으신다 해도 그렇게 달라진 뒤에 우리 살림의 나머지를 어떻게 꾸려 가야 할지 알 수가 없어요. 안 돼요. 우리는 이 정든 옛 작은 집을 포기해야 해요.”\n\n“정든 옛집이지요.” 릴리가 말했다. 그렇게 말하면서 그녀가 떠올린 것은 어린 시절의 지난 즐거움들이라기보다, 가을 몇 달 동안 크로즈비가 그들과 함께 있었던 저 요즈음의 정원 풍경이었다.\n\n“따지고 보면 내가 옮기는 것이 옳은 일인지 모르겠구나.” 데일 부인이 미심쩍은 듯 말했다.\n\n“맞아요, 맞고말고요.” 두 딸이 한꺼번에 말했다. “당연히 옳은 일이에요, 엄마. 그건 의심할 여지가 없어요, 엄마. 오두막이든, 하다못해 셋방이든 어디 하나 얻을 수만 있다면, 크리스토퍼 삼촌이 이 일을 어떻게 생각하시는지 알게 된 지금은 여기 남아 있는 것보다 그편이 나아요.”\n\n“그분은 몹시 불행해하실 게다.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n그러나 이 말조차 딸들의 마음을 조금도 움직이지 못했다. 그들은 삼촌이 불행해지는 것이 몹시 안타까웠다. 그들은 애정을 좀 더 드러내 보임으로써 그를 향한 자기들의 감정이 매정한 것이 아님을 알려 드리려 애쓸 참이었다. 그가 말을 걸어온다면, 그를 향한 자기들의 마음이 오로지 다정한 것뿐임을 설명해 드리려 애쓸 참이었다. 그러나 그가 어떤 대가를 기대하고 있고 자기들은 그것을 치를 수 없다고 느끼는 마당에, 앨링턴에 남아 고마움의 짐을 더 무겁게 쌓아 갈 수는 없었다.\n\n“여기 그대로 있는다면 우리는 삼촌의 것을 훔치는 셈이야.” 벨이 잘라 말했다. “그분이 그 거래에서 마땅히 자기 몫이라고 믿는 것을, 알면서도 훔치는 거지.”\n\n그리하여 앨링턴 작은 집을 떠나겠다는 뜻을 삼촌에게 알리기로 그들 사이에서 결정이 났다.\n\n그러자 새 보금자리를 어디로 하느냐는 문제가 나왔다. 데일 부인은 자기 수입이 적어도 임스 부인이 가진 것보다는 낫다는 것을 알고 있었으므로, 게스트윅에 집 한 채는 건사할 형편이 되리라고 짐작할 만한 근거는 충분했다. “우리가 정말 떠난다면, 그렇게 하는 수밖에 없겠지.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 우리는 수전 보이스 대신에 메리 임스와 산책을 다니게 되겠네.” 릴리가 말했다. “따지고 보면 그리 큰 차이도 아니야.”\n\n“그 점에서는 잃는 만큼 얻는 셈이지.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“그리고 가게들이 가까이 있으니 얼마나 좋아.” 릴리가 빈정거리며 말했다.\n\n“다만 우리한테 뭘 살 돈이 도무지 없을 뿐이지.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“그래도 세상 구경은 더 하게 될 거야.” 릴리가 말했다. “레이디 줄리아의 마차가 일주일에 두 번 읍내로 들어오고, 그러펀 댁 아가씨들은 아주 위풍당당하게 마차를 몰고 다니잖아. 통틀어 보면 우리가 얻는 게 많아. 가엾은 옛 정원만 빼면 말이야. 엄마, 저는 홉킨스와 헤어지면 정말 가슴이 찢어질 것 같아요. 그리고 그 사람으로 말하자면, 제가 떠난 뒤에도 그가 다시 고개를 들고 다닌다면 저는 인간이라는 것에 실망하고 말 거예요.”\n\n그러나 실은 그들의 결심에는 슬픔이 아주 많이 서려 있었다. 데일 부인에게는 자기가 딸들을 위해 일을 서투르게 처리하고 있는 것처럼, 제 잘못으로 가난과 불운이 딸들에게 닥치도록 내버려 두는 것처럼 여겨졌다. 릴리가 부려 보이는 그 자잘한 우스개 밑에 얼마나 큰 슬픔의 짐이 그 아이의 가슴에 얹혀 있는지 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다. 그 아이가 인간이라는 것에 실망하고 만다고 말했을 때, 데일 부인은 제 속내를 들키고 말 몸서리를 겉으로 내지 않으려고 가까스로 참았다. 그런데 이제 그녀는 딸들을 그 안락한 보금자리에서, 그 잔디밭과 정원의 호사에서 끌어내어 지방 읍내의 비좁고 칙칙한 한 귀퉁이로 데려가는 데 동의하고 있었다. 시숙과 사이좋게 지내는 데 실패했다는 이유로 말이다. 자기 감정이 상했다는 이유로, 딸들이 앨링턴에서 누리던 그 모든 이점을—그토록 정당한 출처에서 그들에게 온 이점을—포기하는 것이 과연 옳을 수 있을까? 앞으로 겪을 온갖 궁색함 속에서, 자기가 딸들을 옮겨 놓을 새집의 볼품없는 초라함 속에서, 그녀는 제 그릇된 자존심으로 딸들을 그 지경에 데려다 놓았다고 늘 스스로를 탓하게 되지 않을까? 그러면서도 그녀에게는 이제 달리 길이 없는 것처럼 여겨졌다. 이제 와서 딸들에게 모든 일에서 삼촌의 뜻에 따르라고 가르칠 수는 없었다. 지주가 그런 혼인에 마음을 두었다는 이유로 버나드와 혼인하는 편이 좋겠다고 벨에게 납득시킬 수도 없었다. 그녀는 이미 너무 멀리 와 버려서 이제는 되돌아갈 수가 없었다.\n\n“성모영보 축일에는 옮겨야겠죠?” 당장 움직이자는 쪽인 벨이 말했다. “그렇다면 크리스토퍼 삼촌께 곧바로 알리시는 편이 낫지 않을까요?”\n\n“그때까지 집을 구할 수 있을 것 같지 않구나.”\n\n“어디든 들어갈 데는 있어요.” 벨이 말을 이었다. “게스트윅에는 셋방이 얼마든지 있잖아요.” 그러나 셋방이라는 말의 울림은 데일 부인의 귀에 거북했다.\n\n“가야 한다면 당장 나가요.” 릴리가 말했다. “나가는 차례 따위를 따지고 있을 것 없어요.”\n\n“너희 삼촌은 크게 충격을 받으실 게다.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그게 엄마 잘못이라고는 하실 수 없어요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n이리하여 필요한 통고를 지주에게 곧바로 하기로, 그리고 그 오래도록 사랑해 온 집을 영영 떠나기로 그들 사이에 합의가 이루어졌다. 세속의 눈으로 보자면 그것은 크게 굴러떨어지는 일이었다—앨링턴 작은 집에서 게스트윅의 어느 자그마한 거리에 있는 거처로. 앨링턴에서 그들은 고을의 상류 사람들이었고, 사는 곳이라는 사정 덕분에 자기네 지주며 다른 지주들과 같은 반열에 올라 있었다. 그러나 게스트윅에서는 읍내 사람들 축에서도 보잘것없는 축이 될 것이었다. 임스네와 동등한 처지가 되고, 그러펀네에게는 한참 내려다보일 것이었다. 게스트윅 저택에 다시 인사를 가겠다고 감히 나설 수도 없을 터였다. 레이디 줄리아가 게스트윅으로 자기들을 찾아오리라고는 도저히 기대할 수 없을 테니 말이다. 보이스 부인은 틀림없이 그들에게 생색을 내려 들 것이고, 헌 부인이 건네 올 위로의 말은 벌써부터 눈에 선했다. 실로 그런 이사는 그들이 아는 한의 온 세상이 다 듣는 자리에서 실패를 자백하는 것이나 마찬가지였다.\n\n나는 나의 독자들이 이런 고려가 작은 집의 어느 여인에게든 아무래도 좋은 일이었으리라고 짐작하도록 내버려 둘 수 없다. 마음이 굳세고 사색으로 팽팽하게 벼려진 어떤 여인들에게라면 그런 고려쯤은 아무래도 좋은 것이었을지 모른다. 그러나 데일 부인은 그런 천성이 아니었고, 그녀의 딸들도 그러했다. 세상의 좋은 것들은 그들의 눈에도 좋았고, 그들은 벗들 사이에서 기분 좋은 사회적 자리를 차지하는 특권을 소중히 여겼다. 여태 우연이 그들에게 안겨 준 이점들을 현명하게 경멸할 수 있는 사람들은 결코 아니었다. 처지가 바뀌어 상대적으로 가난해지는 것을 기뻐하며 떠날 수 있는 사람들이 아니었던 것이다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서, 이제 버리려 하는 그 호사를 그 값에 사들일 수도 없는 노릇이었다.\n\n“삼촌께 편지를 쓰시는 편이 낫지 않을까요?” 두 딸 가운데 하나가 말했다. 그러나 이에 대해 데일 부인은, 그런 문제를 두고서는 알아듣기 쉽게 편지를 쓸 수 없으니 이튿날 아침 지주를 직접 만나겠다고 반대했다. “아주 끔찍할 게다.” 그녀가 말했다. “하지만 곧 끝날 일이야. 내가 무서운 것은 그분이 그 자리에서 하실 말씀이라기보다, 그 뒤에 만날 때 그분의 얼굴에 어릴 쓰라린 원망이란다.” 그리하여 이튿날 아침 그녀는 다시, 이번에는 부름도 없이, 지주의 서재로 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n“데일 씨,” 그녀는 몸가짐에 얼마쯤 당황한 기색을 띠고 말을 서두르며 제 할 일을 시작했다. “어제 우리가 함께 나눈 이야기를 곰곰이 생각해 보았고, 한시도 미루지 말고 시아주버니께 알려 드려야 한다고 여겨지는 결심에 이르렀습니다.”\n\n지주 역시 두 사람 사이에 오간 일을 생각했고, 그러면서 적잖이 괴로워했다. 그러나 그의 생각에는 모진 기색도 노여움도 없었다. 그의 생각은 언제나 그의 말보다 부드러웠고, 그의 마음은 그가 밖으로 내놓을 수 있는 그 어떤 마음의 표현보다도 물렀다. 그는 아우의 자식들을 사랑하고 또 그들에게서 사랑받기를 바랐다. 그러나 설령 그것이 이루어지지 않더라도 그들에게 잘해 주고 싶었다. 그 면담이 끝난 뒤에 데일 부인에게 화를 낸다는 생각은 그에게 떠오르지도 않았다. 그 대화는 그에게 유쾌하게 흘러가지 않았다. 그러나 애초에 그는 일이 유쾌하게 굴러가리라고 기대하는 사람도 아니었다. 그때 오간 말들 때문에 데일 집안 여자들 가운데 누구에게든 나쁜 일이 닥칠 수 있으리라는 생각은 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다. 그는 작은 집을 그들의 거처이자 보금자리로 여겼으니, 큰 집이 제 것인 것과 꼭 마찬가지였다. 그에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려주자면, 그들이 그 집을 자기가 베푼 은혜로 붙들고 있다는 식의 암시는 그의 생각에서 아주 멀리 떨어져 있었다고 밝혀 두어야 한다. 제 오두막을 실제 값의 절반에 붙들고 있는 헌 부인은 집주인인 그를 두고 거의 날마다 투덜거렸다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 그녀의 집세를 올려야겠다는 생각도, 올리지 않는 것이 각별히 후한 처사라는 생각도 그에게는 떠오르지 않았다. 그에게 이 세상은 늘 투덜거리고 뒤틀리고 언짢은 곳이었다. 그리하여 그는 헌 부인에게서든 제수에게서든 여태 익숙해져 온 것보다 더 나은 무엇을 기대하지 않았다.\n\n“그 결심이 벨과 그 사촌의 혼사와 관계있는 것이라면,” 그가 말했다. “나로서는 아주 기쁘겠소.”\n\n“데일 씨, 그건 있을 수 없는 일입니다. 확신이 없다면 이런 말씀으로 시아주버니를 언짢게 해 드리지도 않겠지요. 하지만 저는 제 아이를 너무나 잘 압니다!”\n\n“그렇다면 그 일은 세월에 맡겨 둡시다, 제수씨.”\n\n“네, 물론이지요. 하지만 아무리 세월이 흘러도 벨의 마음을 바꾸어 놓지는 못할 겁니다. 그렇더라도 그 이야기는 접어 두지요. 그리고 이제, 데일 씨, 다른 말씀을 드려야겠습니다. 저희는 작은 집을 떠나기로 결심했습니다.”\n\n“무엇을 결심했다고?” 지주가 그녀에게로 눈을 똑바로 돌리며 말했다.\n\n“저희는 작은 집을 떠나기로 결심했습니다.”\n\n“작은 집을 떠난다니!” 그가 그녀의 말을 되뇌며 말했다. “대체 어디로 가겠다는 거요?”\n\n“게스트윅으로 갈까 합니다.”\n\n“어째서?”\n\n“아, 그건 참으로 설명하기 어렵습니다. 제가 말씀드리는 그대로 사실만 받아 주시고, 저를 그렇게 이끈 이유는 묻지 않아 주신다면 좋겠습니다만!”\n\n“하지만 그건 있을 수 없는 일이오, 제수씨. 그런 일이라면 나는 제수씨의 이유를 물어야만 하오. 그리고 이 말도 해 두어야겠소. 내 생각으로는, 이유가 정말로 어지간히 강한 것이 아닌 한, 그런 뜻을 밀고 나가는 것은 제수씨가 딸들에 대한 도리를 다하는 일이 못 되오.”\n\n“하지만 그 이유는 아주 강합니다.” 데일 부인이 말했다. 그러고는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“나는 이해할 수가 없소.” 지주가 말했다. “제수씨가 정말로 진심이라고는 도무지 믿어지지가 않소. 거기서 편치 않소?”\n\n“저희 형편에 누릴 자격이 있는 것보다 더 편안하지요.”\n\n“하지만 나는 제수씨가 늘 돈을 아주 알뜰하게 꾸려 간다고 여겼소. 빚을 지는 일도 없지 않소.”\n\n“네, 저는 빚을 지지 않습니다. 딱히 그 때문은 아닙니다. 사실은 이렇습니다, 데일 씨. 저희에게는 집세를 내지 않고 거기서 살 권리가 없습니다. 그렇다고 집세를 낸다면 거기서 살아갈 형편이 못 되고요.”\n\n“집세라니 누가 그런 말을 했소?” 그가 의자에서 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. “누군가 내 등 뒤에서 나를 두고 거짓말을 지껄인 게로군.” 진상의 빛줄기는 아직 그에게 조금도 비쳐 들지 않았다. 제 입으로 한 어떤 말 때문에 제 친척들이 제 집을 떠나야겠다고 여기게 되었다는 생각은 그의 머리에 이르지 않았다. 그는 자기가 그들에게 특별히 너그럽게 굴었다고 여겨 본 적이 한 번도 없었고, 설령 그들을 향한 제 노여움이 세차고 뜨거웠다 한들 그들이 여태 살아온 집을 버리는 것이 사리에 맞는다고는 생각하지 않았을 것이다. “제수씨,” 그가 말했다. “나는 이 일의 밑바닥까지 알아내야겠소. 그 집을 떠난다는 것으로 말하면, 그건 있을 수 없는 일이오. 어디로 간들 지금보다 형편이 나을 수 있소? 아니, 그만하기라도 하겠소? 게스트윅으로 들어가는 것으로 말하면, 아이들에게 거기가 무슨 살 곳이 되겠소? 그런 것은 있을 수 없는 일로 죄다 제쳐 두겠소. 그러나 무엇이 제수씨를 부추겨 그런 소리를 꺼내게 했는지는 알아야겠소. 솔직히 말해 보시오. 누가 내 등 뒤에서 나를 나쁘게 말한 사람이 있소?”\n\n데일 부인은 반대와 나무람에 대비하고 있었다. 그러나 지주의 말에는 단호함이 있었고 그 태도에는 위엄이 서려 있어서, 그녀는 제 처지의 어려움을 여태보다 한결 또렷하게 깨닫게 되었다. 그녀는 제 뜻을 밀고 나갈 힘이 모자라지 않을까 하는 두려움마저 들기 시작했다.\n\n“정말이지 그런 것이 아닙니다, 데일 씨.”\n\n“그럼 대체 무엇이오?”\n\n“말씀드리려 들면 시아주버니께서 언짢아하시고 저를 반박하실 줄 압니다.”\n\n“언짢기야 하겠지, 아마도.”\n\n“그런데도 저로서는 어쩔 도리가 없습니다. 정말로 저는 시아주버니께도 아이들에게도 옳은 일을 하려고 애쓰는 중입니다.”\n\n“나는 아무래도 좋소. 제수씨의 도리는 그 아이들을 생각하는 것이오.”\n\n“물론 그렇지요. 그리고 이 일에서 그 아이들은 저와 진심으로 뜻이 같습니다.”\n\n그런 논거를 끌어댐으로써 데일 부인은 제 약점을 드러냈고, 지주는 그것을 놓치지 않고 파고들었다. “제수씨의 도리는 그 아이들에게 있소.” 그가 말했다. “그러나 그 말은 그 아이들이 당장 제 마음에 가장 드는 대로 무엇이든 하도록 내버려 두는 것이 제수씨의 도리라는 뜻은 아니오. 그 아이들이 무슨 낭만적인 헛소리에 휩쓸려 가는 것이야 나도 이해할 수 있소. 그러나 제수씨마저 그러는 것은 이해할 수가 없소.”\n\n“사실은 이렇습니다, 데일 씨. 시아주버니께서는 제 아이들이 부모에게 바쳐야 할 그런 복종을 시아주버니께 빚지고 있다고 여기시지요. 그리고 그 아이들이 여기 머무르며 나날의 살림 가운데 그토록 큰 몫을 시아주버니 손에서 받는 이상, 그렇게 여기시는 것이 어쩌면 당연한 일인지도 모릅니다. 벨을 둘러싼 이 딱한 일에서도—”\n\n“나는 그런 말은 한 적이 없소.” 지주가 그녀의 말을 가로막으며 말했다.\n\n“네, 그렇게 말씀하시지는 않았지요. 그리고 제가 무슨 불평이라도 하는 것으로 여기지 않으셨으면 합니다. 하지만 저는 그렇다고 느끼고, 아이들도 그렇게 느낍니다. 그래서 저희는 떠나기로 마음을 굳혔습니다.”\n\n데일 부인은 말을 마치면서 자기가 이야기를 잘하지 못했음을 알고 있었다. 그러나 마땅히 이야기되어야 할 방식으로 그것을 이야기하기란 제 힘에 도무지 부치는 일임을 그녀는 스스로 인정한 터였다. 그녀의 주된 목적은 자기가 반드시 그의 집을 떠나리라는 것을 시숙에게 납득시키는 것, 그리고 그에게 되도록 적은 아픔을 주면서 그것을 납득시키는 것이었다. 돌아가서 딸들에게 일이 매듭지어졌다고 말할 수만 있게 제 뜻을 관철할 수 있다면, 그가 자기를 어리석다고 여기는 것쯤은 조금도 개의치 않았다. 그러나 지주는 그 말과 몸가짐으로 보아 그녀에게 그런 호사를 내줄 마음이 없어 보였다.\n\n“내가 여태 들어 본 온갖 소리 가운데,” 그가 말했다. “그것이 가장 사리에 닿지 않는 소리요. 그 말을 간추리면 이런 것이오. 제수씨는 남편의 형에게 딸린 집에서 집세 없이 사는 것이 자존심에 걸린다, 그러니 제 몸과 제 아이들을 몹시 쪼들리는 수입의 큰 고생 속으로 밀어 넣겠다. 제수씨 자신만 걸린 일이라면 나에게는 무슨 말을 할 권리도 없소. 그러나 아이들로 말하면, 제수씨를 아는 사람은 누구나 제수씨가 아주 잘못했다고 여기리라는 것만은 내가 일러 주어야 할 도리라고 생각하오. 그 아이들이 그 집에서 사는 것은 일의 자연스러운 순리요. 그 집은 한 번도 세를 놓은 적이 없소. 내가 아는 한 그 집이 지어진 뒤로 그 집에 집세가 매겨진 적은 한 번도 없었소. 그 집은 언제나 그것을 누릴 가장 큰 권리가 있다고 여겨진 집안 사람에게 주어졌소. 나는 제수씨가 그곳에 발붙이고 선 자리를 내가 여기 선 자리만큼이나 든든한 것으로 여겨 왔소. 나와 제수씨 아이들 사이의 다툼은 나에게 큰 재앙일 것이오. 어쩌면 그 아이들에게는 아무렇지도 않은 일일지 모르지만 말이오. 그러나 설령 그런 다툼이 있다 한들, 그것이 그 아이들이 그 집을 떠날 이유가 되지는 않소. 부디 이 일을 다시 한 번 곰곰이 생각해 주기를 청하오.”\n\n지주는 어떤 자리에서는 위엄 있는 태도를 지어 보일 줄 알았고, 지금이 바로 그러했다. 데일 부인은 자기가 그에게 대답할 수 있는 것이라고는 제 뜻을 그저 되풀이하는 것뿐임을 깨달았다. 아니, 실은 그에게 쓸 만한 대답이라고는 무엇 하나 내놓지 못했다.\n\n“시아주버니께서 제 아이들에게 아주 잘해 주신다는 것은 압니다.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그 이야기는 하지 않겠소.” 그가 대답했다. 그 순간 그가 생각하고 있던 것은 작은 집이 아니었다. 앨링턴의 안주인에게 마땅히 딸릴 그 모든 특권을 맏이에게 온전히 넘겨주고 싶어 했던 마음이었고, 아울러 가엾은 릴리의 부서진 앞날을 자기가 어떤 방편으로 기워 보려 하고 있는지였다. 그 뒤에 오간 말들에는 별다른 뜻이 없었고, 데일 부인은 자기가 실패했다고 느끼며 그 자리를 물러 나왔다. 그녀가 나가자마자 지주는 일어나 두꺼운 외투를 걸치고, 모자와 지팡이를 들고 집 앞으로 나섰다. 그가 밖으로 나간 것은 생각을 좀 더 자유로이 풀어놓기 위해서였고, 상처 입은 사람이 제 상처를 들여다보며 얻는 그 위안에 잠겨 보기 위해서였다. 그는 스스로에게 자기가 참으로 모진 대접을 받고 있다고 되뇌었다. 어찌나 모진 대접인지, 그는 자기 자신과 자기의 속내마저 의심하기 시작할 지경이었다. 어째서 주위 사람들은 그를 그토록 싫어하는 것일까? 어째서 그를 피하고, 그들의 행복을 위해 그가 기울이는 노력을 어깃장 놓는 것일까? 그는 조카에게 아들의 온갖 특권을, 아니 실은 아들의 특권보다 훨씬 더한 것을 내주었다. 그러면서 그 대가로 청한 것이라고는 장차 제 것이 될 그 집에서 눌러살아 달라는 것뿐이었다. 그러나 조카는 거절했다. “그 애는 나와 함께 사는 것을 못 견디는 게지.” 노인은 쓰라린 마음으로 혼잣말을 했다. 그는 앨링턴 가문의 딸들이 여태 제 아버지에게서 받아 온 것보다 더 후하게 조카딸들을 대할 작정이었다. 그런데 그들은 그의 호의를 뿌리치고 그에게서 달아나며, 그에게 신세 지지 않겠노라고 대놓고 말하는 것이었다. 그는 그것을 몹시 쓰라리게 곱씹으며 테라스를 천천히 오르내렸다. 그러나 제 억울함을 들여다보면서도 억울함이 으레 주게 마련인 그 위안을 다 얻지는 못했다. 생각 속에서 그가 탓한 것은 남이라기보다 자기 자신이었기 때문이다. 그는 자기가 미움받도록 생겨 먹은 사람이라고 스스로에게 못 박았고, 자기가 죽어 기억에서 묻혀 버려 남은 데일 집안사람들이 좀 더 행복하게 살 기회를 얻는 편이 좋겠다고 스스로에게 항변했다. 그러고는 이 모든 것을 제 가슴속에서 그렇게 따져 보는 동안 그의 생각은 무척이나 다정했다. 그리하여 그는 억울함을 당하고서도, 자기를 가장 아프게 한 이들에게 가장 애틋했다. 그러나 그런 생각과 감정을 말과 겉으로 드러나는 표시로 밖에 내보이는 일은 도무지 그의 힘에 부치는 것이었다.\n\n이제 한 해가 거의 저물어 가고 있었으나 날씨는 아직 부드럽고 푸근했다. 공기는 차다기보다 눅눅했고, 잔디밭과 들판은 아직도 새로 돋은 풀의 푸른빛을 머금고 있었다. 지주가 테라스를 거닐고 있을 때 홉킨스가 그에게 다가와 모자에 손을 대며, 하루 이틀 사이에 서리가 내리겠다고 말했다.\n\n“그렇겠지.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“저 작은 포도 온실 연관에 미장이를 불러야 합니다요, 나리. 그러기 전에는 거기다 불을 때 봐야 아무 소용이 없습니다요.”\n\n“어느 포도 온실 말인가?” 지주가 퉁명스럽게 말했다.\n\n“아니, 저쪽 정원에 있는 포도 온실 말입니다요, 나리. 따지고 보면 작년에 벌써 했어야 할 일입니다요.” 홉킨스가 이렇게 말한 것은 자기에게 퉁명스럽게 군 주인을 벌주려는 것이었다. 데일 부인네 포도 온실 연관 문제로 말하자면, 그는 지난겨울 내내 주인을 아주 많이 헤아려 주며 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않고 봐주었고, 이제 그 일이 기억되어야 마땅하다고 여겼다. “뭔가 손을 대기 전에는 거기다 불을 때 봐야 제대로 될 리가 없습니다요. 그건 틀림없습니다요.”\n\n“그럼 불을 때지 말게.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n그런데 문제의 그 포도는 유난히 좋다고들 하는 것이었고 작은 집 정원의 자랑거리였다. 그 포도는 늘 촉성으로 길렀는데, 큰 집의 것만큼 이르게 재촉하지는 않았다. 홉킨스는 몹시 어리둥절한 처지가 되었다.\n\n“그러면 절대로 익지 않습니다요, 나리. 한 해가 다 가도록 말입니다요.”\n\n“그럼 안 익은 채로 두게.” 지주가 거닐면서 말했다.\n\n홉킨스로서는 도무지 알 수가 없었다. 지주는 본디 이런 일이라면 어느 것 하나 손 놓고 내버려 두기를 아주 싫어하는 사람이었고, 그중에서도 작은 집에 관련된 일이라면 유난히 더 그러했다. 그리하여 홉킨스는 테라스에 선 채 모자를 들었다 놓으며 머리를 긁적였다. “저 어른들 사이에 뭔가 잘못된 게 있구먼.” 그는 서글프게 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n그러나 지주는 테라스 끝까지 걸어가 집 옆으로 돌아가는 길로 접어들었다가, 걸음을 멈추고 홉킨스를 불렀다.\n\n“그 연관에 필요한 일은 해 두게.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“예, 나리. 잘 알겠습니다요, 나리. 벽돌만 다시 놓으면 됩니다요. 이번 겨울에는 그 밖에 더 필요한 것도 없습니다요.”\n\n“하는 김에 그 자리를 아주 말끔하게 손봐 두게.” 지주가 말했고, 그러고는 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n# 제39장. 크로프츠 선생이 내쫓기다\n\n“여보, 작은 집 소식 들으셨어요?” 데일 부인이 지주를 찾아간 지 이틀인가 사흘 뒤에 보이스 부인이 남편에게 말했다. 한 시였고, 교구 목사는 사목 일을 마치고 아내와 아이들과 함께 식사를 하려고 막 들어온 참이었다.\n\n“무슨 소식 말이오?” 보이스 씨가 말했다. 그는 아무 소식도 듣지 못했던 것이다.\n\n“데일 부인과 아가씨들이 작은 집을 떠난대요. 게스트윅으로 들어가서 산다지 뭐예요.”\n\n“데일 부인이 떠난다니, 말도 안 되는 소리!” 목사가 말했다. “대체 무슨 일로 게스트윅엘 들어간단 말이오? 지금 사는 데서는 집세를 한 실링도 물지 않는데.”\n\n“정말이라니까요, 여보. 제가 방금 헌 부인과 함께 있었는데, 부인이 데일 부인 입으로 직접 들었답니다. 헌 부인 말로는 평생 그렇게 놀라 본 적이 없다는군요. 무슨 다툼이 있었던 거예요. 그건 틀림없어요.”\n\n보이스 씨는 말없이 앉은 채 식사에 앞서 흙 묻은 구두를 벗었다. 제 교구의 사교 생활에 관한 이토록 중대한 소식은 여러 날 동안 그에게 들어온 적이 없었고, 그로서는 그렇게 대뜸 그것을 곧이곧대로 믿을 수가 없었다.\n\n“헌 부인 말로는 데일 부인이 그 이야기를 어찌나 단단하게 하던지, 무엇으로도 마음을 돌릴 수 없겠다 싶은 투였다는군요.”\n\n“그래서 까닭은 말합디까?”\n\n“글쎄요, 딱히 그러지는 않았대요. 하지만 헌 부인은 데일 부인과 지주 사이에 말다툼이 있었던 게로구나 짐작이 가더랍니다. 그것 말고 무슨 일이 있겠어요. 아마 그 크로즈비라는 사내와 관련된 일이겠지요.”\n\n“그 집 사람들은 돈에 몹시 쪼들리게 될 게요.” 보이스 씨가 실내화에 발을 밀어 넣으며 말했다.\n\n“저도 헌 부인한테 꼭 그렇게 말했답니다. 게다가 그 아가씨들은 진짜 절약이라는 걸 해 본 적이 없잖아요. 앞으로 그 애들이 어떻게 될지 저는 모르겠어요.” 그리고 보이스 부인은 제 소중한 벗들을 향한 동정을 그렇게 늘어놓으면서, 그들의 앞날에 놓인 가난을 내다보는 데서 적잖은 위안을 얻었다. 세상일이란 늘 그런 법이고, 보이스 부인이 제 이웃들보다 더 못된 사람인 것도 아니었다.\n\n“때가 되기 전에 저희끼리 화해할 거요, 두고 보시오.” 보이스 씨가 말했다. 그로서는 그런 변고가 주는 흥분이 사실이라기에는 너무 좋은 것이었다.\n\n“그러지 못할 것 같아요.” 보이스 부인이 말했다. “그러지 못할 것 같다니까요. 두 분 다 어찌나 고집이 센지요. 저는 늘 말을 태우고 아가씨들한테 모자며 승마복을 사 주는 게 해로운 일이라고 생각했어요. 그건 마치 그 애들이 지주의 딸인 양 대하는 것이었는데, 그 애들은 지주의 딸이 아니잖아요.”\n\n“그거나 그거나 매한가지였지.”\n\n“하지만 이제 그 차이가 드러난 거지요.” 분별 있는 보이스 부인이 말했다. “저는 소중한 데일 부인이 잘못하고 있다고 여러 번 말했고, 결국 제가 옳았던 것으로 드러난 거예요. 저야 그분들을 찾아뵙고 하는 그런 일에서는 아무것도 달라질 게 없지만요.”\n\n“당연히 달라질 게 없지.”\n\n“그렇다고 차이가 없을 수는 없어요. 그것도 아주 큰 차이가요. 가엾은 릴리한테는 지독한 몰락이 되겠지요, 그 훌륭한 남편감까지 잃은 마당에 말이에요.”\n\n식사가 끝나고 보이스 씨가 다시 제 일터로 나간 뒤에, 보이스 부인과 딸들 사이에서도 같은 이야기가 오갔다. 어머니는 아이들에게 데일 부인이 게스트윅의 아주 초라한 집에 살게 되더라도 여전히 예전과 똑같이 훌륭한 분이고 조금도 다름없는 숙녀라는 것을 가르치려고 몹시 애를 썼다. 그 가르침에서 보이스 집안 아가씨들은 데일 부인이 벨과 릴리와 더불어 세상에서 한 계단 굴러떨어지려 하고 있으며, 따라서 그에 걸맞게 대해야 한다는 것을 똑똑히 배웠다.\n\n이 모든 것으로 미루어, 데일 부인이 지주의 논리에 대답하지는 못했으면서도 그 앞에 굽히지는 않았다는 것을 알 수 있을 것이다. 집으로 돌아오면서 그녀는 거의 패배했다고 느꼈고, 자기 본분이 어느 길에 놓여 있는지 도무지 알 수 없는 여인의 낯빛과 목소리로 딸들에게 이야기했다. 그러나 딸들은 그 자리에서 지주가 어떤 태도를 보였는지 보지 못했고 그가 무슨 말을 했는지도 듣지 못했으므로, 그가 싫어한다고 해서 자기들의 뜻을 접어야 한다는 것을 납득할 수 없었다. 그리하여 딸들은 이야기를 거듭해 어머니를 다시 새로운 결심으로 이끌었고, 이튿날 아침 그녀는 시숙에게 쪽지를 한 장 써 보냈다. 그가 한 말을 모두 곰곰이 생각해 보았노라고 다짐하면서도, 자기는 작은 집을 떠나는 것이 도리라고 여긴다고 다시 한번 못 박은 쪽지였다. 이에 그는 아무 답도 보내지 않았고, 그녀는 이 일을 비밀로 삼지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각해 헌 부인에게 제 뜻을 알렸다.\n\n“제수분께서 우리 곁을 떠나신다니 안타깝습니다.” 바로 그날 오후에 보이스 씨가 지주에게 말했다.\n\n“누가 그럽디까?” 지주가 물었다. 그 말투로 보아 목사가 고른 이야깃거리가 도무지 마음에 들지 않는 것이 분명했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 저는 보이스 부인한테 들었습니다만, 아마 헌 부인이 알려 준 모양입니다.”\n\n“헌 부인이 남의 일에 참견하지 말고 쓸데없는 소문이나 퍼뜨리지 않았으면 좋겠소.”\n\n지주는 더 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 보이스 씨는 자기가 몹시 부당하게 홀대를 당했다고 느꼈다.\n\n크로프츠 선생이 건너와서 그것이 성홍열이 맞다고 확진했다. 시골 약제사들은 대개 사람들이 던지는 의심 때문에 억울한 일을 당하는데, 도시에서 의사가 와 보면 언제나 시골 약제사가 한 말을 그대로 확인해 주기 때문이다.\n\n“성홍열인 것은 의심할 여지가 없습니다.” 의사가 단언했다. “하지만 증세는 다 순조롭습니다.”\n\n그러나 이보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 일이 오고 있었다. 이틀 뒤에 릴리가 몸이 좀 좋지 않다는 것을 스스로 알아차렸다. 그녀는 자기가 환자로 의사의 눈에 들게 될까 두려워 그것을 감추려 했으나 애쓴 보람이 없었고, 이튿날 아침에는 그녀 역시 그 병에 걸렸다는 것이 드러났다. 크로프츠 선생은 모든 것이 그녀에게 유리하다고 말했다. 날씨가 춥고, 집 안에 병이 들어와 있던 터라 모두가 조심하고 있었으며, 게다가 좋은 지시를 곧바로 얻을 수 있는 형편이었다. 의사는 데일 부인에게 마음을 놓으시라고 청했으나, 두 자매를 함께 있게 해서는 안 된다는 것만은 몹시 간절히 당부했다. “벨을 게스트윅으로, 임스 부인 댁으로 보내시면 어떻겠습니까?” 그가 말했다. 그러나 벨은 임스 부인 댁으로 보내지기를 마다했고, 병자가 된 릴리가 옮겨진 어머니의 침실 밖에 붙들어 두는 것조차 몹시 어려웠다.\n\n“이런 말씀을 드려도 된다면,” 릴리의 병이 드러난 지 이틀째 되는 날 그가 벨에게 말했다. “이 집에 남아 계시는 건 잘못하시는 겁니다.”\n\n“어머니가 저렇게 손이 모자라시는데 제가 어머니를 두고 갈 수는 없어요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 아가씨까지 앓아누우시면 어머니 손이 더 모자라게 되지요.” 의사가 간청했다.\n\n“그렇게는 못 해요.” 벨이 대답했다. “게스트윅으로 옮겨진다 해도, 마음이 놓이지 않아서 그 집에서 빠져나올 수 있는 첫 순간에 앨링턴까지 걸어서 돌아오고 말걸요.”\n\n“어머니께서는 아가씨가 없는 편이 더 마음 편하실 것 같습니다만.”\n\n“저는 제가 있는 편이 어머니 마음이 더 편하실 것 같아요. 저는 여자가 병을 피해 달아났다는 이야기는 듣기가 늘 싫어요. 하물며 자매나 딸이 그런다면 견딜 수 없는 일이지요.” 그리하여 벨은 남았다. 동생을 볼 수 있다는 허락은 받지 못했지만, 꼭 필요한 여러 바깥일들을 해 나가면서.\n\n그리하여 온갖 곤경이 한꺼번에 무더기로 작은 집 식구들에게 쏟아져 내렸다. 크로즈비에 관한 그 끔찍한 소식이 온 지 아직 두 달도 채 되지 않았고, 그 소식만으로도 그들을 짓뭉개기에 넉넉하리라고 그때 다들 여겼는데, 이제 그 불행 위에 다른 불행들이 겹쳐 얹힌 것이다. 그것도 하나가 지나가기 무섭게 다음 것이 뒤따라서. 의사의 다정한 예언을 비웃기라도 하듯 릴리는 몹시 앓았고, 며칠 뒤에는 헛소리를 하기에 이르렀다. 그녀는 어머니에게 크로즈비 이야기를 하고는 했는데, 지나간 가을에 그를 이야기하던 그 말투 그대로였다. 그러면서도 정신을 놓은 가운데서도 지금 사는 집을 떠나기로 했다는 것만은 기억하고 있어서, 게스트윅의 셋집이 준비되었느냐고 의사에게 두 번이나 물었다.\n\n크로프츠가 그들의 뜻을 처음 알게 된 것은 그렇게 해서였다. 릴리의 병이 가장 무겁던 그 며칠 동안 그는 날마다 앨링턴으로 건너왔고, 한번은 밤을 꼬박 새우기까지 했다. 이 모든 일에 그는 진찰비를 받으려 하지 않았다. 애초에 데일 부인에게서 진찰비를 받은 적이 한 번도 없었다. “이렇게 자주 오지 않으셨으면 좋겠어요.” 어느 날 저녁, 그가 환자의 방에서 나와 응접실 난롯가에 그녀와 함께 서 있을 때 벨이 그에게 말했다. “저희에게 갚지도 못할 신세를 너무 많이 지우고 계세요.” 그날 릴리는 열의 고비를 넘겼고, 그는 데일 부인에게 이제 위험한 고비는 아닌 것 같다고 말할 수 있었다.\n\n“이제 그리 오래 올 일도 없을 겁니다.” 그가 말했다. “고비는 넘겼으니까요.”\n\n“그 말씀을 듣는 것만으로도 이런 호사가 없네요. 우리는 저 애 목숨을 선생님께 빚진 셈이겠지요. 그렇지만—”\n\n“아, 아닙니다. 성홍열은 이제 예전만큼 무서운 병이 아니에요.”\n\n“그러면 어째서 저 애한테 그렇게까지 시간을 다 바치셨어요? 저희가 선생님께 끼쳤을 손해를 생각하면 겁이 다 나요.”\n\n“저보다 제 말이 더 힘들었을 겁니다.” 의사가 웃으며 말했다. “게스트윅에 제 환자가 그렇게 많지도 않고요.” 그러고 나서 그는 갈 생각을 하기는커녕 자리에 앉았다. “그런데 정말입니까?” 그가 말했다. “다들 이 집을 떠나신다는 게.”\n\n“정말이에요. 릴리를 옮겨도 될 만큼 나으면 삼월 말에 떠날 거예요.”\n\n“릴리는 그보다 훨씬 전에 나을 겁니다. 물론 앞으로도 몇 주 동안은 제 방 밖으로 옮겨서는 안 되겠습니다만.”\n\n“저 애 때문에 발이 묶이지만 않는다면 삼월 말에는 틀림없이 떠날 거예요.” 이제 벨도 자리에 앉았고, 두 사람은 한동안 말없이 불만 바라보고 있었다.\n\n“그런데 왜 그러시는 겁니까, 벨?” 마침내 그가 말했다. “하기야 제게 그런 걸 물을 권리가 있는지는 모르겠습니다만.”\n\n“선생님께는 저희에 대해 무엇이든 물으실 권리가 있어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “삼촌은 아주 친절하세요. 친절한 정도가 아니라 너그러우시지요. 하지만 저희가 여기 사는 것 때문에 어머니 일에 간섭할 권리가 생긴다고 여기시는 것 같아요. 저희는 그게 싫고, 그래서 떠나는 거예요.”\n\n의사는 여전히 난롯가 한쪽에 앉아 있었고, 벨도 여전히 그 맞은편에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 두 사람 사이에 이야기는 그리 술술 풀려 나오지 않았다. “안 좋은 소식이군요.” 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n“어쨌든 저희가 앓을 때 선생님이 그렇게 멀리까지 오실 일은 없어지잖아요.”\n\n“네, 알겠습니다. 여러분이 저와 그만큼 더 가까이 있게 되는 것을 두고 스스로 기뻐하지 않는다면 제가 무례한 사람이라는 말씀이시군요. 하지만 저는 조금도 기쁘지 않습니다. 저는 여러분이 여기 앨링턴 말고 다른 어디에 살고 계신다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없어요. 데일 집안사람들은 게스트윅의 어느 거리에서는 제자리를 잃고 말 겁니다.”\n\n“그것도 데일 집안사람들한테는 가혹한 말씀이네요.”\n\n“가혹하지요. 노블레스 오블리주라는 그 몹시도 폭압적인 법칙에서 뻗어 나온 곁가지 같은 것이니까요. 저는 사정이 아주 절박하지 않은 다음에야 아가씨들이 앨링턴을 떠나서는 안 된다고 생각합니다.”\n\n“그런데 아주 절박한걸요.”\n\n“그렇다면야, 뭐!” 그러고서 그는 다시 말이 없었다.\n\n“어머니가 여기서 행복하지 않으시다는 걸 여태 못 보셨어요?” 또 한 번의 침묵 뒤에 그녀가 말했다. “저도 지금처럼 이 모든 걸 다 알았던 건 아니에요. 하지만 이제는 보여요.”\n\n“저도 보았습니다. 적어도 아가씨가 말씀하시는 것만큼은 보았지요. 어머니께서는 스스로를 억누르는 삶을 사셨습니다. 하지만 그런 억누름이란 삶에서 얼마나 흔히 어쩔 수 없는 일입니까? 저는 어머니께서 그것 때문에 옮기실 분이라고는 생각되지 않는데요.”\n\n“아니에요. 저희 때문이에요. 하지만 선생님 말씀대로 그 억누름이 저희를 불행하게 만들고, 어머니는 그걸 보고 계시니 어쩌지 못하시는 거예요. 삼촌은 돈 문제에서는 어머니께 너그러우세요. 하지만 다른 것들에서는, 마음에 관한 일들에서는 인색하셨다고 저는 생각해요.”\n\n“벨.” 의사가 부르고는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n그녀는 그를 올려다보았으나 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그는 언제나 그녀를 세례명으로 불러 왔고, 두 사람은 서로를 늘 가까운 벗으로 여겨 왔다. 지금 이 순간 그녀는 그것 말고 다른 모든 것을 잊고 있었으며, 그러면서도 거기 앉아 그와 이야기하는 것이 한없이 즐거웠다.\n\n“어쩌면 물어서는 안 될 것을 물으려 합니다. 다만 아가씨를 하도 오래 알고 지낸 터라 누이한테 말하는 것이나 다름없다는 느낌이 들어서요.”\n\n“무엇이든 물으세요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“사촌인 버나드에 관한 겁니다.”\n\n“버나드요!” 벨이 말했다.\n\n이제 땅거미가 내려 있었고, 두 사람은 난롯불 말고는 아무 불빛도 없이 앉아 있었으므로, 사촌의 이름이 나오자 제 얼굴을 뒤덮은 그 빛깔을 그가 알아볼 수 없으리라는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그러나 설령 대낮의 빛이 온 방에 가득했더라도 크로프츠가 그 홍조를 보았을 것 같지는 않다. 그는 눈을 난롯불에 딱 붙박아 두고 있었으니 말이다.\n\n“네, 버나드에 관한 겁니다. 여쭈어도 되는 건지 모르겠습니다만.”\n\n“글쎄요, 저야 뭐라 드릴 말씀이 없네요.” 벨은 그렇게 말했으나, 그 말이 어떤 성질의 것인지 스스로도 의식하지 못했다.\n\n“게스트윅에 소문이 하나 돌았는데, 그와 아가씨가—”\n\n“사실이 아니에요.” 벨이 말했다. “전혀 사실이 아니에요. 그 이야기를 또 들으시거든 아니라고 해 주세요. 사람들은 왜 그런 말을 하는지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 혼사가 되었을 텐데요. 아가씨의 벗들이라면 다들 반겼을 겁니다.”\n\n“무슨 말씀이세요, 크로프츠 선생님? 저는 그 ‘훌륭한 혼사’라는 말이 얼마나 싫은지 몰라요. 그 말 속에는 사람이 입에 담는 그 어떤 말보다도 더 사악한 속물근성이 들어 있어요. 선생님은 제가 사촌과 결혼하기를 바라시는군요. 그저 제가 살 큰 집과 마차가 생기니까요. 선생님이 제 벗이라는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 저는 그런 벗의 정은 싫어요.”\n\n“제 말을 오해하신 것 같군요, 벨. 제 말은 두 분이 서로 사랑하기만 했다면 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 혼사가 되었으리라는 겁니다.”\n\n“아니에요, 저는 오해하지 않았어요. 서로 사랑한다면야 물론 훌륭한 혼사겠지요. 제가 푸주한이나 빵집 주인을 사랑한대도 똑같이 말씀하실 수 있잖아요. 선생님 말씀의 속뜻은, 그 조건이 그를 사랑할 이유가 된다는 거지요.”\n\n“그런 뜻으로 말한 것 같지는 않은데요.”\n\n“그러면 아무 뜻도 없으신 거네요.”\n\n그러고 나서 또 몇 분 동안 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 크로프츠 선생은 돌아가려고 자리에서 일어섰다. “아가씨한테 아주 지독하게 꾸중을 들었군요.” 그가 옅은 웃음을 띠며 말했다. “남의 일에 참견한 값으로 마땅히 들어야 할 꾸중이기는 합니다만—”\n\n“아니에요, 참견하신 게 아니에요.”\n\n“하지만 어쨌든 가기 전에 저를 용서해 주셔야겠습니다.”\n\n“선생님이 죄를 뉘우치시고 그 못된 마음보를 아주 뜯어고치시기 전에는 절대로 용서하지 않을 거예요. 그러다가는 머지않아 그러펀 선생만큼이나 못된 사람이 되고 마실걸요.”\n\n“그럴까요?”\n\n“아, 그래도 용서해 드릴게요. 어쨌든 선생님은 이 세상에서 가장 너그러운 분이니까요.”\n\n“아, 그럼요. 물론 그렇지요. 자,—그럼 안녕히 계십시오.”\n\n“하지만 크로프츠 선생님, 다른 사람들이 선생님보다 그렇게까지 세속적이라고 여기지는 마세요. 선생님이야 돈을 그리 대단하게 여기지 않으시—”\n\n“아니요, 저는 아주 대단하게 여깁니다.”\n\n“그러셨다면 날이면 날마다 여기 거저 오시지는 않았겠지요.”\n\n“저는 돈을 정말로 아주 대단하게 여깁니다. 돈을 벌 기회를 얻지 못해서 가슴이 미어질 뻔한 적도 여러 번 있었어요. 돈은 사람이 가질 수 있는 가장 좋은 벗입니다—”\n\n“어머, 크로프츠 선생님!”\n\n“—사람이 가질 수 있는 가장 좋은 벗이지요, 정직하게 얻은 것이라면 말입니다. 그런 벗이 없어서 한 사내에게 닥치는 슬픔이 어떤 것인지는 여자로서는 좀처럼 헤아릴 수 없을 겁니다.”\n\n“물론 사내라면 제 직업으로 남부럽지 않게 벌어먹고 싶어 하지요. 그리고 선생님은 그렇게 하실 수 있고요.”\n\n“그건 남부럽지 않다는 것을 어떻게 보느냐에 달렸지요.”\n\n“아! 제 기준은 한 번도 그리 높았던 적이 없어요. 저는 늘 돼지우리에서 사는 데 소질 같은 게 있었거든요. 깨끗한 돼지우리 말이에요. 누울 자리에 산뜻한 콩짚이 깔린 그런 데요. 저를 숙녀로 길러 놓은 건 잘못이었지 싶어요. 정말이지 그래요.”\n\n“저는 그렇게 생각하지 않습니다.” 크로프츠 선생이 말했다.\n\n“그건 선생님이 아직 저를 잘 모르셔서 그래요. 저는 하루에 옷을 세 번씩 갈아입는 게 조금도 즐겁지 않아요. 그런데도 자주 그러는 건 그렇게 살도록 배워 온 탓에 그리하게 되니까 그러는 거예요. 하지만 게스트윅으로 가면 그런 걸 다 바꿀 작정이에요. 그리고 선생님이 차를 드시러 오시면, 제가 아침에 입던 그 갈색 원피스를 그대로 입고 있는 걸 보시게 될걸요. 물론 아침 일에 그 갈색 원피스가 더러워지지만 않았다면요. 어머, 크로프츠 선생님! 게스트윅 느릅나무 아래로 말을 타고 돌아가시자면 칠흑같이 어두울 텐데요.”\n\n“어두운 건 상관없습니다.” 그가 말했는데, 아직도 갈 마음이 별로 없어 보였다.\n\n“저는 상관있어요.” 벨이 말했다. “초를 가져오라고 종을 울릴게요.” 그러나 그녀가 초인종 줄로 손을 뻗자 그가 그것을 막았다.\n\n“잠깐만요, 벨. 제가 가기 전에 굳이 초를 켤 것도 없고, 제가 머무는 것을 아까워하실 것도 없습니다. 집에 가 봐야 저 혼자뿐이니 말입니다.”\n\n“머무시는 걸 아까워하다니요!”\n\n“하지만 어쨌든 아까워하시든지, 아니면 저를 아주 반겨 주시든지 하셔야겠습니다.” 그는 하인을 부르지 못하게 막느라 붙잡았던 그녀의 손목을 여태 놓지 않고 있었다.\n\n“무슨 말씀이세요?” 그녀가 말했다. “선생님은 저희한테 오월의 꽃처럼 반가운 손님이라는 걸 아시잖아요. 언제나 반가운 분이셨어요. 더구나 지금처럼 저희가 곤경에 빠졌을 때 이렇게 와 주셨는데— 어쨌든 제가 선생님을 내쫓았다는 말씀만은 결코 못 하실 거예요.”\n\n“결코 못 할까요?” 그러면서도 그는 여전히 그녀의 손목을 붙들고 있었다. 그는 내내 제 의자에 앉은 채였고, 그녀는 그의 앞에, 그와 난롯불 사이에 서 있었다. 그러나 그가 이렇게 붙들고 있는데도 그녀는 그의 말도 그 몸짓도 대수롭게 여기지 않았다. 두 사람은 아주 허물없이 지내 온 사이였고, 릴리는 여전히 크로프츠 선생이 언니의 연인이라며 그녀를 놀리고는 했지만, 그녀는 그런 감정이 둘 사이에 생기는 일은 결코 없으리라고 오래전에 스스로를 가르쳐 두었던 것이다.\n\n“결코 못 할까요, 벨? 만일 나처럼 가난한 사내가, 아가씨가 사촌 버나드처럼 부유한 사내에게 주지 않으려는 그 손을 달라고 한다면 어쩌시겠습니까?”\n\n그녀는 곧바로 팔을 빼고는 아주 재빠르게 깔개를 가로질러 한두 걸음 물러섰다. 그 몸짓은 그가 그녀를 모욕하기라도 했을 때, 또는 어떤 경우에도 그런 말을 할 권리가 없는 사내가 그런 말을 했을 때 나왔음 직한 몸짓이나 다름없었다.\n\n“아, 그렇군요! 그러실 줄 알았습니다.” 그가 말했다. “이제 가도 되겠군요. 내가 내쫓겼다는 것을 알고서 말입니다.”\n\n“그게 무슨 말씀이세요, 크로프츠 선생님? 대체 무슨 말씀을 하고 계세요? 어째서 그런 터무니없는 소리를 하시는 거예요? 저를 화나게 할 수 있는지 시험해 보시려는 거예요?”\n\n“그래요, 터무니없는 소리지요. 제게는 그런 식으로 아가씨한테 말을 건넬 권리가 없고, 더구나 이렇게 의사로서 댁에 와 있는 마당에는 결코 그래서는 안 되는 일이었습니다. 용서하십시오.” 이제 그도 일어서 있었으나, 난롯가 제 자리에서 움직이지는 않았다. “제가 가기 전에 용서해 주시겠습니까?”\n\n“무엇을 용서해 드리라는 말씀이세요?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“감히 아가씨를 사랑한 것을요. 아가씨가 기억할 수 있는 그 오랜 세월만큼이나 오래 아가씨를 사랑해 온 것을요. 다른 무엇보다도 아가씨를 더 사랑한 것을요. 이것만은 용서해 주셔야 합니다. 그런데 그것을 입 밖에 낸 것까지 용서해 주시겠습니까?”\n\n그는 그녀에게 청혼을 한 것이 아니었고, 그녀도 그가 청혼을 하려는 참이라고 여기지 않았다. 그녀 자신도 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 아직 제대로 깨닫지 못했고, 거기에 어떤 대답을 내놓아야 할지 스스로에게 물어본 적도 물론 없었다. 연인이 오해할 여지가 없을 만큼 뚜렷한 모습으로 나타나서, 그가 사랑을 드러내어 말하는 첫마디가 곧 그의 이야기 전부를 들려주는, 그것도 놀랄 겨를조차 주지 않고 들려주는 경우가 있다. 그리고 그것은 대개 그 연인이 오랜 벗이 아닐 때, 알고 지낸 지가 얼마 되지도 않았을 때에 그러하다. 크로즈비와 릴리 데일의 경우가 본디 꼭 그러했다. 크로즈비가 릴리에게 와서 청혼했을 때 그는 조금도 거리낌 없이 아주 침착하게 그 일을 해냈으니, 그것이 기다려지고 있다는 것을 거의 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 릴리도 잠깐 마음이 설레기는 했으나 대답만은 척척 준비되어 있었다. 그녀는 이미 온 마음으로 그 사내를 사랑하고 있었고, 그가 곁에 있는 것을 기뻐했으며, 그의 사내다움이 비추는 볕을 쬐었고, 그의 재치에 즐거워했으며, 제 귀를 그의 목소리 가락에 맞추어 두었던 것이다. 모든 것이 이미 이루어져 있었고 세상도 그것을 기다리고 있었다. 그가 청혼하지 않았더라면 릴리는 몹쓸 대접을 받은 것이 되었으리라. 하기야, 아아, 아아, 그보다 훨씬 더 무거운 몹쓸 대접이 뒷날 그녀를 기다리고 있었지만! 그러나 연인이 제 마음을 연인으로서 알리는 일이 몹시 어려운 경우도 있으니, 그런 경우에는 막상 알린다 해도 자기에게 이로운 대답을 그 자리에서 바랄 수가 없다. 이 어려움이 대개 오랜 벗들에게 가장 무겁게 떨어진다는 것은 그들에게 가혹한 일이다. 크로프츠는 데일 집안과 하도 가까이 지내 온 터라, 그가 두 아가씨 가운데 하나와 혼인하리라고 여긴 사람이 아주 많았다. 데일 부인 자신도 그렇게 생각했고 거의 그러기를 바라기까지 했다. 릴리는 분명 그렇게 생각했고 그러기를 바라기도 했다. 그런 생각과 바람이 얼마쯤 빛이 바래기는 했으나, 그래도 한때 그런 것이 있었다는 사실만은 의사에게 유리하게 작용했어야 마땅했다. 그런데 이제 그가 어떻게든 마음을 털어놓고 보니, 벨은 그에게서 화들짝 물러섰고 그가 진심이라는 것을 믿으려 하지 않았다. 그녀는 아마 이 세상 어느 사내보다도 그를 사랑하고 있었으련만, 막상 그가 그녀에게 사랑을 말하자 도무지 그 말을 알아들을 수가 없었던 것이다.\n\n“무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠어요, 크로프츠 선생님. 정말로 모르겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“저는 아가씨한테 제 아내가 되어 달라고 청할 작정이었습니다. 그저 그뿐이었지요. 하지만 아가씨한테 저를 딱 잘라 거절해야 하는 괴로움까지 지우지는 않겠습니다. 오늘 여기로 말을 달려오면서 그 생각을 했습니다. 요즈음 자주 말을 달리는 동안에도 그 생각 말고는 별로 한 것이 없었지요. 하지만 저는 제게 그럴 권리가 없다고 스스로에게 말했습니다. 저는 아가씨가 사시기에 마땅한 집조차 가지고 있지 않으니까요.”\n\n“크로프츠 선생님, 제가 선생님을 사랑한다면, 제가 선생님과 혼인하고 싶다면—” 그러고서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“하지만 그렇지는 않으시지요?”\n\n“네, 그렇지 않은 것 같아요. 아마 아닐 거예요. 네, 아니에요. 하지만 어쨌든 돈에 관한 생각은 여기에 아무 상관이 없어요.”\n\n“그러면 저는 아가씨가 사랑하실 수 있다는 그 푸주한이나 빵집 주인은 될 수 있을까요?”\n\n“아니요.” 벨이 말했다. 그러고는 더 말을 잇지 못하고 멈추었는데, 그 한마디에 제 대답을 모두 담으려 한 것이 아니라 그다음 말을 어떻게 지어야 할지 몰랐기 때문이었다.\n\n“그러실 줄 알았습니다.” 의사가 말했다.\n\n이 연인의 처신과 구애하는 방식을 굳이 따져 보려는 이들은, 그가 그런 일에 몹시 서툰 사람이었다고 여기지 않을까 나는 걱정한다. 부인네들은 그가 용기가 모자랐다고 할 것이고, 사내들은 그가 재치가 모자랐다고 할 것이다. 그러나 나는 그가 그런 자리에서 사내들이 대개 처신하는 만큼은 처신했으며, 어디까지나 여느 정도는 되는 연인임을 스스로 보여 주었다고 믿는 쪽이다. 세상에는 대담한 연인이 있어서, 새를 쏘아 떨어뜨리듯 제 사랑하는 아가씨를 넘어뜨리고, 혹시라도 제 새를 떨어뜨리지 못하면 제 몸에 온갖 저주를 퍼붓고 제 총이 실성했다고 욕을 하며 세상이 끝나기라도 한 듯한 낯빛으로 둘레를 두리번거린다. 또 소심한 연인이 있어서, 그는 방아쇠를 당기며 눈을 질끈 감고, 반바지를 챙겨 입던 순간부터 적어도 저는 아무것도 잡지 못하리라고 굳게 믿고 있었으므로, 벗들이 큰 소리로 축하를 보내와도 저 아름다운 날개 달린 것을 정말로 제 솜씨로 잡았다는 것을 믿지 못한다. 소심한 사내가 품에 안고 돌아오는 그 아름다운 날개 달린 것은—사냥한 짐승이 뒤섞인 흔한 무더기 속에 파묻히는 일이 없도록 잡동사니 수레에 던져 넣기를 마다하고 안고 오는 그것은—새를 수백 마리씩 잡는 이들에게 그 산더미 같은 사냥물이 주는 것보다 더 값진 것이다.\n\n그러나 크로프츠 선생은 어찌나 눈을 질끈 감고 쏘았던지, 제가 새를 맞혔는지 어떤지 조금도 알지 못했다. 게다가 곁에서 축하해 줄 사람이 하나도 없었으니, 그는 새가 상처 하나 없이 옆 들판으로 날아가 버렸다고 아주 굳게 믿었다. 그의 마지막 에두른 물음에 벨이 준 대답은 ‘아니요’라는 한마디뿐이었는데, ‘아니요’는 연인들에게 편안한 말이 못 된다. 그러나 벨의 그 ‘아니요’ 속에는, 그가 조금이라도 제정신을 붙들고 있었더라면 그 새가 상처 하나 없이 달아나는 것은 아니라는 것을 그에게 가르쳐 주었을 만한 무엇이 들어 있었다.\n\n“이제 가겠습니다.” 그가 말했다. 그러고는 대답을 기다리며 잠시 멈추었으나 아무 대답도 오지 않았다. “그리고 제가 내쫓겼다고 말한 것이 무슨 뜻이었는지는 아시겠지요.”\n\n“아무도— 선생님을 내쫓지 않아요.” 그렇게 말하는 벨은 어느새 흐느낌에 가까운 소리를 내고 있었다.\n\n“어쨌든 갈 때가 되었지요, 그렇지 않습니까? 그리고 벨, 오늘 이 자그마한 소동 때문에 제가 동생분 병상에서 발길을 끊으리라고는 생각지 마십시오. 저는 내일도 올 것이고, 아가씨는 저를 같은 사람이라고 알아보기도 어려우실 겁니다.” 그러고서 그는 어둠 속에서 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n“안녕히 가세요.” 그녀가 제 손을 내주며 말했다. 그는 그녀의 손을 아주 꼭 쥐었으나, 그녀는 그러고 싶었으면서도 마주 힘을 주지 못했다. 그녀의 손은 그의 손안에 가만히 놓인 채 언짢은 기색이라고는 조금도 내비치지 않았으나, 그저 하염없이 가만히 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n“안녕히 계십시오, 더없이 소중한 벗이여.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“안녕히 가세요.” 그녀가 대답했다. 그러고서 그는 가 버렸다.\n\n그녀는 현관문이 그의 뒤에서 닫히는 소리가 들릴 때까지 꼼짝도 하지 않고 있다가, 소리 없이 제 침실로 살며시 올라가 난롯가의 나지막한 흔들의자에 앉았다. 아래층에 차가 준비될 때까지 어머니가 릴리 곁에 남아 있는 것은 이미 굳어진 관례였다. 이렇게 병이 든 날들에는 저녁 식사라 할 만한 것도 일찍 차려 먹었기 때문이다. 그러므로 벨은 아직 반 시간쯤 제 시간이 남아 있다는 것을, 그동안은 앉아서 아무에게도 방해받지 않고 생각에 잠길 수 있다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n그러면 그녀의 첫 생각은 마땅히 무엇이어야 했을까? 이제 와서 보니 자기를 깊이 사랑하는 사내를, 자기 쪽에서도 적어도 더없이 따뜻한 우정만은 늘 품어 온 그 사내를 매정하게 물리쳤다는 것이 아니었을까? 그러나 그녀의 생각은 그런 것이 아니었고, 그 비슷한 것조차 아니었다. 생각은 곧장 지나간 세월로—그녀의 짧은 나이로 헤아리자면 긴 세월로—거슬러 올라가, 그가 자기를 사랑한다고 꿈꾸었고 자기도 그를 사랑한다고 여겼던 그 평온하던 날들에 내려앉았다. 그 뒤로 그녀는 그 날들을 두고 스스로를 얼마나 나무랐으며, 제 생각이 너무 대담했다고 스스로에게 얼마나 가르쳤던가! 그런데 이제 그 모든 것이 한 바퀴 돌아 제자리로 온 것이다. 그녀가 여태 좋아해 본 단 하나의 사내가 그녀를 사랑하고 있었다. 그러다 문득 어느 날의 기억이 떠올랐다. 크로즈비가 자기를 마음에 두고 있다는 생각에 거의 우쭐했던 날, 그것이 정말이기를 거의 바라기까지 했던 날의 기억이었다. 그 생각이 나자 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 발로 마룻바닥을 두 번 굴렀다. “사랑하는 릴리.” 그녀는 속으로 말했다. “가엾은 릴리!” 그러나 그때 그녀로 하여금 동생을 떠올리게 한 감정은, 크로즈비를 맨 처음 마음에 불러들인 그 감정과는 아무 관계도 없었다.\n\n그리고 이 사내는 그 모든 세월 내내 그녀를 사랑해 온 것이다. 값을 매길 수도 없고 견줄 데도 없는 이 사내가. 저쪽 사내가 스스로 거짓된 것임을 드러내 보인 만큼이나 뼛속까지 참된 이 사내가, 저쪽 사내가 스스로 썩은 것임을 드러내 보인 만큼이나 튼튼한 이 사내가 말이다. 그런 생각을 하며 난롯불을 바라보고 앉아 있는 그녀의 얼굴에 미소가 번졌다. 한 사내가 그녀를 사랑했고, 그 사랑은 가질 만한 값어치가 있는 것이었다. 그녀는 자기가 그를 거절한 것인지 받아들인 것인지조차 잘 기억나지 않았다. 앞으로 어떻게 할 것인지도 스스로에게 거의 묻지 않았다. 그 모든 것에 대해서는 여러 가지로 생각해 보아야 할 필요가 있었지만, 그 필요가 지금 당장 그녀를 짓누르지는 않았다. 적어도 지금 이 순간만은 앉아서 마음껏 뿌듯해해도 좋았다. 그리하여 그렇게 뿌듯해하며 앉아 있는데, 늙은 유모가 들어와 어머니가 아래층에서 기다리신다고 일러 주었다.\n\n# 제40장. 혼례 준비\n\n크로즈비 씨가 세상에서 가장 행복한 사내가 되기로 한 날은 마침내 이월 십사일로 정해졌다. 처음에는 더 늦은 날이 거론되었으니, 삼월 첫 주보다는 그편이 낫다 하여 이십칠일이나 이십팔일이 제안되었던 것이다. 그러나 레이디 어밀리아는 그 일요일 저녁 크로즈비가 보인 태도에 겁을 먹었고, 쓸데없이 날을 미루어서는 안 된다고 백작부인에게 일러 두었다. “그이는 그런 짓쯤은 거리끼지 않는 사람이오.” 레이디 어밀리아가 편지 가운데 하나에 그렇게 썼는데, 그것은 그녀에게서 기대할 법한 것보다 오히려 제 신분의 위력을 덜 믿고 있음을 드러낸 셈이었다. 그러나 백작부인도 같은 생각이었고, 그리하여 크로즈비가 장차 장모가 될 이에게서 아주 다정한 서한을 받았을 때—십사일이 이십팔일보다 얼마나 더 편한 날인지 온갖 이유를 늘어놓은 서한이었다—그는 흥정에서 정한 날보다 보름 일찍 행복해지는 것을 마다할 핑곗거리를 지어낼 수가 없었다. 그의 첫 충동은 물러서지 않는 쪽이었으니, 흥정에 든 것 이상을 요구해서는 안 된다는 어떤 마음에서 나온 것이었다. 그러나 다투어 무슨 소용인가? 적어도 지금 다투어 무슨 소용인가? 그는 드 코시 집안의 폭정에서 저를 풀어놓는 일이 지금보다는 일단 혼인하고 난 뒤에 한결 쉬우리라고 믿었다. 레이디 알렉산드리나가 제 사람이 되고 나면 자기가 그녀의 주인 노릇을 할 작정임을 알려 줄 참이었다. 그러기 위해 드 코시 집안과 아주 갈라서야 한다면 그런 갈라섬도 마다하지 않을 것이었다. 지금 당장은, 적어도 이 일에서는 그들에게 양보하기로 했다. 그리하여 이월 십사일이 혼례일로 정해졌다.\n\n일월 둘째 주에 알렉산드리나는 제 물건들을 챙기러 런던으로 올라왔다. 좀 더 고상한 말로 하자면 신부에게 어울리는 혼수 일습을 갖추러 온 것이었다. 그 일을 혼자서, 아니 언니가 곁에서 지켜보며 거들어 주는 것만으로도 제대로 해낼 수는 없었으므로, 드 코시 백작부인도 함께 올라오기로 되어 있었다. 그러나 알렉산드리나가 먼저 와서 백작부인이 도착할 때까지 세인트존스 우드의 언니 집에 머물렀다. 백작부인은 사위의 대접을 받아들이는 아량을 여태 한 번도 베푼 적이 없었고, 언제나 포트먼 스퀘어의 춥고 삭막한 집으로 갔다. 여러 해 동안 드 코시 집안의 런던 저택 노릇을 해 온 집이었으니, 백작부인 같으면 진작에 옥스퍼드 스트리트 저편의 어느 거처와 기꺼이 바꾸었을 것이다. 그러나 백작이 완고했다. 그의 클럽들과 이따금 묵곤 하던 몇몇 하숙이 옥스퍼드 스트리트의 옳은 쪽에 있었으니, 무엇 하러 대대로 살아온 집을 바꾼단 말인가? 그리하여 백작부인은 포트먼 스퀘어로 올라오게 되었고, 이번에는 세인트존스 우드로 오라는 말조차 듣지 못했다.\n\n“우리가 그렇게 하는 편이 낫지 않겠어요?” 게이지비 씨가 아내에게 말했다. 제가 냈던 제안을 다시 꺼내면서 그는 거의 떨다시피 했다.\n\n“그러지 않는 편이 좋겠소, 여보.” 레이디 어밀리아가 대답했다. “어머니가 그리 까다로운 분은 아니지만, 자잘한 것들이 있잖소—”\n\n“아, 그럼요, 물론이지요.” 게이지비 씨가 말했다. 그러고서 이야기는 흐지부지되었다. 그는 존귀한 장모가 수도에 머무는 동안 더없이 기꺼이 대접하고 싶었으나, 그러면서도 그녀가 제 집에 있는 한 그 기간 내내 비참했을 것이었다.\n\n그러나 한 주 동안 알렉산드리나는 게이지비 씨의 지붕 아래 머물렀고, 그동안 크로즈비는 신랑을 앞둔 사내의 온갖 즐거움으로 행복해졌다. 물론 그는 날마다 게이지비네에서 저녁을 들고 저녁나절을 내내 거기서 보내야 하는 것으로 알아 두라는 말을 들었으며, 사정이 그러하니 그러지 않을 구실도 없었다. 실은 요즈음 그의 시간이 달리 어떻게 흘렀겠는가 하면, 지겹도록 더디게 흘렀을 뿐이었다. 제 눈에 관해 대담한 결심을 하고 지난번 싸움의 자국 때문에 사교의 즐거움에서 밀려나지는 않겠다고 마음먹었건만, 그 일이 있은 뒤로 그는 클럽에 그리 자주 드나들지 못했다. 그리고 런던이 다시 제법 북적이기 시작했는데도 그는 예전만큼 나다니지 않는 자신을 발견했다. 다가오는 혼인의 광채는 그의 인기에 별로 보태 준 것이 없는 듯했고, 사실 세상은—그의 세상은—그를 냉랭하게 바라보기 시작하고 있었다. 그러니 날마다 세인트존스 우드로 출근하다시피 하는 일도 예상만큼 지겹게 느껴지지는 않았다.\n\n부부가 살 집은 베이스워터 로드에 면한, 아주 유행하는 집들이 늘어선 줄에 잡아 두었다. 프린세스 로열 크레슨트라 불리는 곳이었다. 집은 아주 새것이었고, 거리는 아직 마무리가 되지 않아 회반죽 냄새가 코를 찌르고 여기저기 건축용 장대와 벽돌 부스러기가 널려 있었다. 그런데도 그곳은 아주 반듯한 동네로 인정받고 있었다. 초승달 모양으로 굽은 그 줄의 한쪽 끝에서는 하이드 파크의 한 귀퉁이가 보였고, 다른 쪽 끝은 참으로 훌륭한 테라스 하우스 줄에 닿아 있었으니, 거기에는 어느 대사가—남아메리카에서 온 대사가—은행가의 고참 서기 몇 사람이, 그리고 왕국의 귀족 하나가 살고 있었다. 베이커 스트리트라는 소리가 얼마나 천하게 들리는지, 피츠로이 스퀘어라는 이름이 점잖은 귀에 얼마나 역겨운지 우리는 안다. 그러나 그 언저리의 집들은 튼튼하고 따뜻하며 크기도 넉넉하다. 프린세스 로열 크레슨트의 집은 분명 튼튼하지 않았으니, 요즘 세상에 튼튼하게 지은 집은 수지가 맞지 않는 까닭이다. 따뜻할 리도 없었으니, 사실대로 말하자면 아직 곳곳이 마무리도 되지 않은 집이었다. 크기로 말하자면, 응접실이야 집 한 층을 통째로 잘라 낸 데다 계단 자리만 한 귀퉁이 도려낸 훌륭한 방이었지만, 다른 데는 몹시 옹색했고, 이 점에서는 그림 속 게루빔을 닮아 저에게 딸린 뒷부분이라는 것이 없었다. “하지만 제 재산이 따로 없는 사람이 모든 걸 다 가질 수는 없는 법이오.” 부엌 계단 밑 골방이 옷 갈아입는 방으로 제게 배정된 것을 두고 크로즈비가 그 집에 이의를 달았을 때 백작부인은 그렇게 말했다.\n\n집 문제가 처음 논의되었을 때 레이디 어밀리아는 세인트존스 우드를 자리로 고르고 싶어 안달했으나, 크로즈비는 여기에 딱 잘라 반대했다.\n\n“세인트존스 우드를 좋아하지 않으시나 보구려.” 레이디 어밀리아가 다소 매섭게 그에게 말했다. 그를 겁주어, 이 동네에 딱히 무슨 원한이 있는 것은 아니라고 밝히게 할 셈이었다. 그러나 크로즈비는 그럴 만큼 무르지 않았다.\n\n“네, 좋아하지 않습니다.” 그가 말했다. “예전부터 늘 싫어했지요. 편견이나 다름없다고 해야겠지요. 하지만 여기서 살아야 한다면 여섯 달 안에 제 목을 그어 버리고 말 것이 틀림없습니다.”\n\n그러자 레이디 어밀리아는 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우며, 제 집이 그토록 그에게 밉살스럽다니 유감이라고 말했다.\n\n“아니, 저런, 아닙니다.” 그가 말했다. “처형께서 사시는 곳으로는 무척 마음에 들고, 여기 오는 것을 무엇보다 즐거워하고 있습니다. 저는 제가 여기서 살 경우에 제게 미칠 영향만을 말한 겁니다.”\n\n레이디 어밀리아는 그 모든 것을 알아들을 만큼 영리했다. 그러나 동생의 이익을 마음에 두고 있었으므로, 세인트존스 우드에 관해서는 그 점을 접어 두고 제부에 대한 다정한 보살핌을 그대로 밀고 나갔다. 크로즈비 자신은 복스홀 다리와 강 가까이에 있는 핌리코의 새 스퀘어 가운데 하나로 가고 싶어 했으니, 주로 그 동네와 레이디 어밀리아가 사는 북쪽 지방 사이에 놓인 어마어마한 거리를 염두에 둔 것이었다. 그러나 여기에는 레이디 알렉산드리나가 완강히 반대했다. 만일 이턴 스퀘어를, 아니면 이턴 스퀘어에서 뻗어 나간 거리 하나라도 손에 넣을 수 있었더라면—벨그레이비아의 치맛자락 끝에라도 기어들 수 있었더라면—신부는 기뻐했을 것이다. 그리고 처음에 그녀는 제게 들어온 제안이 바로 그런 것이라는 생각에 하마터면 속아 넘어갈 뻔했다. 핌리코에 관한 그녀의 지리 지식은 온전하지 못했고, 그리하여 자칫 돌이킬 수 없는 잘못에 빠질 뻔했다. 그러나 어느 벗이 고맙게도 끼어들었다. “세상에, 얘, 그이가 널 에클스턴 스퀘어 너머 어디로도 데려가지 못하게 해요!” 시집간 어느 미더운 벗이 기겁하여 그녀에게 그렇게 외쳤던 것이다. 그렇게 경고를 받고 알렉산드리나는 뜻을 굽히지 않았고, 이제 두 사람의 천막은 프린세스 로열 크레슨트에 쳐질 참이었다. 그 한쪽 끝에서는 하이드 파크가 보이는 곳이다.\n\n가구는 주로 레이디 어밀리아의 감독 아래, 그리고 그녀의 경험에 기대어 주문되었다. 크로즈비는 어차피 자기보다 그녀가 물건을 싸게 살 수 있고 자기는 그런 일에 취미가 없다고 선언해 놓고 마음을 놓아 버렸다. 그러면서도 그는 제가 폭정에 놓이고 복종의 손아귀에 들어가고 있다고 느꼈다. 침대며 의자 이야기에 진심으로 파고들 수가 없었으므로 마침내 그 일을 통째로 드 코시 쪽에 맡겨 버린 것이다. 그리고 여기에는 아직 말하지 않은 또 하나의 이유가 있었다. 가구를 사들이는 돈을 모두 대고 있는 것이 모티머 게이지비 씨였다. 그는 드 코시 집안에 대한, 정직하되 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 열성으로, 크로즈비의 것이라 하여 제 손이 닿는 돈은 한 실링까지 몽땅 레이디 알렉산드리나의 몫으로 묶어 두었다. 그녀를 위해 여기서 긁어모으고 저기서 손을 보며, 마치 제 자식들이 앞으로 먹을 빵이 저의 법률 솜씨가 온전한가에 달리기라도 한 듯이, 새신랑을 혼인 재산 계약이라는 숫돌에 대고 꽁꽁 붙들어 매어 놓았던 것이다. 그러고도 그는 그 대가로 한 푼을 받는 것도 아니었고, 당장이든 나중이든 무슨 이득을 보는 것도 아니었다. 그것은 그의 열성에서, 드 코시 경이 쓰는 관(冠)에 대한 열성에서 나온 것이었다. 그의 생각으로는 백작과 백작에게 딸린 것들은 그만한 열성을 받을 자격이 있었다. 그것은 그가 배우며 자라난 신조였고, 저도 모르게 몸으로 지켜 온 신앙에 가까웠다. 개인으로 보자면 그는 드 코시 경을 싫어했으니, 백작이 그를 함부로 대했기 때문이다. 그는 백작이 인정머리 없고 모질고 못된 사람이라는 것을 알았다. 그러나 백작인 이상 그는 어떤 평민도 게이지비 씨에게서 끌어낼 수 없었을 만큼의 봉사를 받을 자격이 있었다. 이렇게 하여 쓸 수 있는 돈을 모조리 레이디 알렉산드리나의—어쩐지 미리 내다보기라도 한 듯한—과부 살림 몫으로 묶어 놓은 게이지비 씨는, 새집에 가구를 들일 돈을 제가 대고 있었던 것이다. “다 갚을 때까지 해마다 백오십 파운드씩 사 퍼센트 이자를 붙여 내게 갚으면 되네.” 그가 크로즈비에게 말했고, 크로즈비는 끙 소리로 승낙했다. 여태까지 그는 런던에서 돈을 헤프게 쓰며 멋쟁이답게 살아왔으나 누구에게도 빚을 진 적이 없었다. 이제 그는 빚지는 이력을 시작하려는 참이었다. 그러나 관청 서기가 백작의 딸과 혼인할 때에는 무엇이든 제 뜻대로 되기를 바랄 수 없는 법이다.\n\n레이디 어밀리아는 여느 살림살이를 사들였다—침대, 계단 깔개, 세면대, 부엌살림 따위였다. 게이지비는 식탁과 찬장을 헐값에 손에 넣었다. 그러나 응접실에 딸린 것들에 관해서는 레이디 알렉산드리나가 몸소 올라와야 했다. 백작부인이 손을 보태려고 마음먹은 것은 옷차림에 관한 일이었으니, 그 계산서만은 게이지비에게 보내어 그가 치르고 오 퍼센트를 신랑 앞으로 꼬박꼬박 달아 두는 식으로 할 수가 없는 것이었다. 신부의 혼수는 드 코시 쪽 재력으로 마련되어야 했고, 그러니 백작부인 자신이 무대에 나설 수밖에 없었다. “외상은 안 되오, 알아들었소?” 백작이 으르렁댔다. 유난히 흉하게 시커먼 이 하나로 말끝을 갈아붙이듯 딱딱 씹어 대면서였다. “이 일로 외상 다는 것은 두고 볼 수 없소.” 그러면서도 그는 현금을 내놓겠다는 말은 하지 않았다. 그런 사정이니 백작부인 자신이 무대에 나서는 것이 참으로 필요했다. 얼마 전 게이지비 씨가 볼일로 코시 성에 다녀갔을 때, 양장점 계산서도 가구장이와 그릇장수 따위의 계산서에 함께 붙여 두면 되지 않겠느냐는 아리송한 언질이 그에게 건네졌더랬다. 백작부인은 제 나름의 말투로, 요즈음은 그런 일의 유행이 바뀌었으며 정말로 세상 속에서 사는 사람들 사이에서는 그런 처리가 마땅한 것으로 여겨진다고 넌지시 일러 주었다. 그러나 게이지비는 머리가 맑고 정직한 사람이었고, 게다가 백작부인을 잘 알았다. 그는 이번 일에 그런 처리를 할 수 있으리라고 보지 않았다. 그러자 백작부인은 더 이상 그 말을 밀지 않고, 저 자신이 런던으로 올라가야겠다고 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n본드 스트리트의 커다란 양탄자 상점 안에 앉은 레이디 어밀리아와 레이디 알렉산드리나를 보는 것은 즐거운 일이었다. 저희를 시중드는 네 사내에게 이것저것 물어 가며, 머리를 맞대고 소곤거리며, 야드당 이 펜스 더 붙는 것까지 정확히 셈해 가며, 끼칠 수 있는 만큼의 수고는 죄다 끼치면서 앉아 있는 두 사람을 보는 것 말이다. 즐거웠던 것은, 그들이 산더미 같은 양탄자 두루마리 사이에서 큼직한 후프 치마를 솜씨 좋게 다루었기 때문이고, 더할 나위 없이 신이 나 있었기 때문이며, 사내들이 바치는 떠받듦을 저희에게 마땅한 몫으로 여겨 받아들이고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 크로즈비를 보는 것은 그리 즐겁지 않았다. 그는 사무실로 달아나고 싶어 안절부절못했고, 이 일에서 고를 권한이라고는 실상 조금도 주어져 있지 않았으며, 사내들은 그를 아주 군더더기로 여기고 있었다. 부인들은 열 시 반까지 가게에 닿아 크로즈비가 열한 시에—아니면 그보다 조금 지나서—사무실에 들어갈 수 있게 해 주겠다고 약속했더랬다. 그러나 게이지비네 집을 나선 것이 열한 시가 다 되어서였고, 양탄자 사이에서 반 시간으로는 어림도 없으리라는 것이 뻔했다. 몇 마일이고 이어지는 화려한 빛깔이 두 사람 앞에 펼쳐지는 듯했고, 그러다 어느 무늬 하나가 그런대로 쓸 만하다 싶으면 방 하나를 거의 덮을 만큼 앞뒤로 펼쳐졌다. 크로즈비는 그 어마어마한 더미를 끌어 옮기는 사내들이 딱했다. 그러나 레이디 어밀리아는 창고에 있는 천을 한 야드도 빠짐없이 살펴보는 것이 제 의무라도 되는 양 태연히 앉아 있었다. “저 밑에 있는 걸 한 번 더 봐야겠소.” 그러면 사내들은 일에 달려들어 산 하나를 옮겼다. “아니야, 앨리, 저 소용돌이 무늬의 초록은 안 되겠소. 뜨거운 물이라도 엎지르면 당장 날아가 버릴 테니.” 사내는 말할 수 없이 부드러운 미소를 지으며 그 초록만은 어디서도 날아가는 법이 없다고 잘라 말했다. 그러나 레이디 어밀리아는 그에게 아무 눈길도 주지 않았고, 그 산을 옮겨 가며 꺼낸 양탄자는 또 다른 산의 한 자락이 되었다.\n\n“저건 괜찮겠네요.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다. 노란 강물이 굽이굽이 흐르고 그 물줄기마다 파란 꽃이 끝없이 실려 가는, 눈부신 진홍 바탕을 들여다보면서였다. 그렇게 말하며 그녀는 고개를 우아하게 한쪽으로 기울인 채 미심쩍은 눈으로 양탄자를 내려다보았다. 레이디 어밀리아는 튼튼한지 시험이라도 하듯 양산으로 그것을 콕콕 찔러 보고는, 노랑은 때를 잘 탄다는 말을 소곤거렸다. 크로즈비는 시계를 꺼내 보고 끙 소리를 냈다.\n\n“더할 나위 없는 양탄자입니다, 마님. 저희 물건 가운데 가장 새로 나온 것이고요. 바로 지난달에 쿠드글룰치 성의 사우스웨일스 공작부인 댁에 이걸로 사백오십 야드를 깔아 드렸습니다. 그 뒤로는 아무도 가져가지 못했지요, 물건이 들어오지 않았으니까요.” 그러자 레이디 어밀리아는 다시 한 번 그것을 찔러 보고, 이어 일어나서 그 위를 걸어 보았다. 레이디 알렉산드리나는 고개를 조금 더 한쪽으로 기울였다.\n\n“오 실링 삼 펜스요?” 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“아이고, 아닙니다, 마님. 오 실링 칠 펜스입니다. 그러고도 저희 집에 있는 양탄자 가운데 가장 싼 것이고요. 이 빛깔에만 야드당 이 펜스가 더 듭니다. 정말입니다.”\n\n“그럼 에누리는요?” 레이디 어밀리아가 물었다.\n\n“이 점 오 퍼센트입니다, 마님.”\n\n“아이고, 그래서는 안 되오.” 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다. “나는 즉시 치를 때는 언제나 오 퍼센트를 받소—아주 그 자리에서 치른다는 말이오.” 그러자 사내는 그 문제는 주인에게 여쭈어야겠다고 말했다. 이 점 오 퍼센트가 이 집의 규칙이라는 것이었다. 창밖을 내다보고 있던 크로즈비가, 명예를 걸고 말하건대 더는 기다릴 수 없다고 말했다.\n\n“그런데 당신은 이걸 어떻게 보세요, 어돌퍼스?” 알렉산드리나가 물었다.\n\n“무엇을 어떻게 보느냐고요?”\n\n“양탄자 말이에요—이거요, 이거!”\n\n“아—양탄자를 어떻게 보느냐고요? 이 노란 띠들이 다 마음에 들지는 않는 것 같군요. 게다가 너무 붉지 않소? 잔무늬가 든 갈색 같은 것이 나았으리라 싶소만. 하지만 정말이지 나는 별로 상관없소.”\n\n“그럼 그렇지.” 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다. 그러고서 두 부인은 다시 오 분 동안 머리를 맞대었고, 마침내 양탄자가 정해졌다—에누리 문제는 남겨 둔 채로였다. “자, 이제 깔개를 봅시다.” 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다. 그러나 여기서 크로즈비가 반란을 일으켜, 두 사람을 두고 사무실로 가야겠다고 우겼다. “깔개 고르는 데 내가 있어야 할 것도 없잖소.” 그가 말했다. “뭐, 그야 그렇겠지요.” 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다. 그러나 알렉산드리나가 이렇게 제 남자 시종에게 버림받는 것을 달가워하지 않는다는 것만은 분명했다.\n\n의자와 소파를 두고도 옥스퍼드 스트리트에서 똑같은 일이 벌어졌고, 크로즈비는 부엌 계단 밑 골방에서 옷을 갈아입어야 한다 하더라도 어서 살림이 자리 잡히기만을 바라게 되었다. 그는 세인트존스 우드의 그 집안 전체를, 그리고 거기 딸린 거의 모든 것을 미워하는 법을 배워 가고 있었다. 거기서 그는 여태 알지 못하던 집안의 자잘한 절약을 소개받았는데, 그것들은 그에게 역겨웠고, 게다가 그런 절약이 왜 꼭 있어야 하는지까지 유난히 자세하게 설명을 들어야 했다. 그런 절약이 그토록 요긴한 것은 바로 그가 이제 놓이려는 처지의 사람들에게라는 것이었다—넉넉지 못한 살림으로 유행하는 세상 앞에서 겉모양만은 반듯하게 지켜야 하는 사람들 말이다. 정육점 고기를 넉넉히 들이고 빨래삯을 한정 없이 쓰는 것은, 바깥출입이 드물고 나갈 때면 손에 잡히는 아무 삯마차나 탈 수 있는 사람들에게라면 연 천오백 파운드로도 얼마든지 좋을 것이었다. 그러나 레이디 알렉산드리나가 꼭 해야만 하는 일들이 있었고, 그러니 집안의 가장 빈틈없는 절약이 반드시 있어야 했다. 릴리 데일이라면 제 남편의 쇠고기 스테이크와 깨끗한 셔츠를 줄여 가며, 제 것인 양 꾸며 놓은 마차를 굴려야겠다고 했을까? 그런 물음과 그런 종류의 다른 물음들을 크로즈비는 저 혼자 마음속으로 적잖이 던져 보았다.\n\n그런데도 그는 알렉산드리나를 사랑하려고, 아니 저 자신에게 그녀를 사랑한다고 설득하려고 애썼다. 그녀를 드 코시 무리에게서, 특히 게이지비 쪽 가지에게서 떼어 놓을 수만 있다면 그런 버릇은 죄다 고쳐 놓으리라. 삯마차 안에 당당히 앉는 법을, 그리고 넉넉한 손으로 제 식탁을 차리는 법을 가르쳐 주리라. 가르친다니!—서른을 넘긴 나이에, 게다가 그토록 공들여 길든 사람을! 앞으로는 친정 식구를 다시는 만나지 말고, 세인트존스 우드에는 얼씬도 말며, 백작부인이나 레이디 마거리타와 편지도 주고받지 말라고 금할 작정이었단 말인가? 가르치기는 무엇을 가르친단 말인가! 그는 검은 무어인을 씻어 희게 만들 수는 없다는 것을 여태 배우지 못했단 말인가? 설령 저 자신이 그 일에 알맞은 사람이었다 해도 될 수 없는 일이거니와, 실은 그처럼 그 일에 어울리지 않는 사내도 드물었으니 말이다. 그러나 누가 그를 딱하게 여기겠는가? 제 품에 안을 수도 있었던 릴리라면 검은 무어인이 아니었을 것이다.\n\n이윽고 드 코시 백작부인이 런던에 오는 날이 되었고, 알렉산드리나는 포트먼 스퀘어로 옮겨 갔다. 크로즈비에게는 여기에 겉으로나마 위안이 있었으니, 그로써 날마다 서북쪽으로 그 을씨년스러운 걸음을 하지 않아도 되었기 때문이다. 나는 그가 교회 옆의 그 바람 부는 모퉁이를—게이지비네 집으로 가려면 돌아야 하는 그 모퉁이를—진저리 나게 미워했으며, 그를 문간으로 이끌어 주는 등불도, 문 그 자체도 미워했다고 말해 두어야겠다. 그 문은 담벼락에 파묻히다시피 서 있었고, 정원이라고도 앞마당이라고도 하는 곳을 가로지르는 좁다란 통로로 열렸다. 그 마당 양편에는 팔리시 도기처럼 보이도록 칠한 쇠제라늄 화분 두 개와, 받침대 위에 벌거벗은 여인상 하나가 놓여 있었다. 그 문 바로 앞의 포석만큼 런던에서 추운 자리는 없다고 그는 생각했다. 그리고 리처드가 일할 때 걸치던 것을 벗고 위세 부리는 차림으로 갈아입는 동안, 그는 거기서 오 분이고 십 분이고 십오 분이고 붙들려 서 있어야 했다—그의 말로는 그러했으나, 내 진심으로 믿기에는 그 시간이 삼 분을 넘긴 적은 결코 없었을 것이다.\n\n사람들이 그 일에 가장 쉽고 자연스럽게 손이 닿는 이를 시켜 문을 열어 주기만 한다면 얼마나 좋겠는가! 나는 얼마 전 어느 화요일 오후 어느 으리으리한 대문 앞에 몇 분이나 서 있었는데, 참다 못해 부아가 치밀 무렵 예쁘장한 하녀 하나가 나와 문을 열어 주었다. 손이며 얼굴이며 앞치마가 벽난로 살대를 매만진 이야기를 들려주고 있기는 했으나, 어쨌든 예쁘장한 하녀였다. “아이고, 나리.” 그녀가 말했다. “손님 받는 날은 수요일입니다요. 그때 오시면 제복 입은 하인이 나올 텐데요!” 그녀는 앞치마 귀퉁이로 내 명함을 받아 들었고, 제복 입은 하인 못지않게 제 몫을 해냈다. 그러나 그 자그마한 말을 안주인이 엿들었더라면 그녀는 어찌 되었을까?\n\n크로즈비는 세인트존스 우드의 집을 미워했으므로 백작부인이 오는 것이 그에게는 한숨 돌릴 일이었다. 포트먼 스퀘어는 가기가 수월했고, 백작부인의 대접은 게이지비네의 대접만큼 억지로 들이밀리지도 않을 터였다. 처음 찾아갔을 때 그는 집 뒤편으로 난 커다란 가족용 식당으로 안내되었다. 앞쪽 창은 물론 닫혀 있었으니, 이 집 식구들이 런던에 와 있는 것으로 되어 있지 않았기 때문이다. 그는 십오 분쯤 그 방에 남아 있었고, 이윽고 백작부인이 온갖 위엄을 갖추고 그에게로 내려왔다. 아마도 그는 그토록 위엄에 찬 그녀를 여태 본 적이 없었을 것이다. 그녀의 옷은 아주 커다래서, 더 넓은 통로를 요구하기라도 하듯 널찍한 문간을 스치며 지나갔다. 그녀는 놀랍기 그지없는 보닛을 쓰고, 속치마만큼이나 넓게 퍼진 벨벳 망토를 걸치고 있었다. 그녀는 그에게 인사를 건네며 고개를 조금 뒤로 젖혔고, 그는 다정하되 어딘가 얕잡아 보는 생색의 연기 속에 저를 감싸이는 것을 대번에 알아차렸다. 예전에 그는 백작부인을 좋아했으니, 그를 대하는 그녀의 태도가 늘 그를 추어올려 주었기 때문이다. 그녀와 오갈 때면 그는 제가 받는 만큼은 넉넉히 주고 있다고, 그리고 백작부인도 그 사실을 알고 있다고 느낄 수 있었다. 두 사람이 아는 사이가 된 온갖 사정에서 우위는 그의 것이었고, 그래서 그 사귐이 즐거웠던 것이다. 백작부인은 사람 좋고 상냥한 여자였고, 그 신분과 지위 덕에 그녀의 집이 그에게 즐거운 곳이 되었다. 그리하여 그는 제게 있는 만큼의 빛으로 그녀 위에 빛을 비추어 주는 데 동의했던 것이다. 두 사람 사이에 서로 좋게 여길 까닭이 이토록 훨씬 강해진 이제 와서, 어찌하여 그 관계가 뒤집혔단 말인가? 그는 그런 변화가 있다는 것을 알았고, 속으로 쓰디쓴 꾸짖음을 삼키며 이 여자가 저를 손아귀에 넣어 가고 있음을 스스로 인정했다. 백작부인의 벗으로서 그는 그녀의 눈에 대단한 사람이었다—그녀의 자잘한 말과 눈길 하나하나가 그의 힘을 인정해 주었다. 그러나 이제 사위로서 그는 아주 하찮은 사람이 될 참이었다—바로 모티머 게이지비가 그러하듯이!\n\n“어돌퍼스.” 그녀가 그의 두 손을 잡으며 말했다. “이제 그날이 아주 가까워졌구려. 그렇지 않소?”\n\n“정말 가까워졌습니다.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그래요, 아주 가까웠지요. 스스로 행복한 사내라고 느끼기를 바라오.”\n\n“아, 그럼요, 그야 물론이지요.”\n\n“마땅히 그래야 하고말고. 아주 진지하게 하는 말이오만, 그것은 당연한 일이어야 한다는 뜻이오. 그 애는 사내가 아내에게 바랄 만한 모든 것을 갖추었소. 지금 그 애의 신분을 두고 하는 말은 아니오. 물론 그 점에서 그 애가 그대에게 얼마나 큰 이득을 안겨 주는지는 그대도 알고 있겠지만 말이오.”\n\n크로즈비는 제가 제비뽑기에서 상을 뽑았다는 것쯤은 알고 있다는 투로 무어라 웅얼거렸다. 그러나 하도 웅얼거린 탓에 마땅한 감사와 순종의 뜻이 부인의 귀에 가 닿지는 못했다. “그대만큼 운이 좋았던 사내를 나는 알지 못하오.” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “그리고 그대가 그 사실을 훤히 알고 있다는 것을 내 귀한 딸이 알아볼 수 있으면 좋겠소. 내 보기에 그 애가 좀 지쳐 보이더군. 그대가 그 애를 물건 사러 다니는 일에 몸에 좋을 정도보다 더 부려 먹은 게요.”\n\n“많이 다니기는 했지요.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그 애가 그런 일에는 워낙 익어 있지 않으니 말이오! 하기야 일이 이렇게 된 이상, 그 애가 몸소 그런 것들을 챙기지 않을 수 없었소만.”\n\n“오히려 좋아하는 눈치던데요.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그 애는 언제나 제 도리 하는 것을 좋아할 거요. 우리는 지금 마담 밀프랑네에 비단을 좀 보러 가려던 참이오. 그대도 함께 가고 싶다면 어떻소?”\n\n바로 그때 알렉산드리나가 방으로 들어왔는데, 모든 점에서 제 어머니를 작게 줄여 놓은 판본 같아 보였다. 두 사람 다 몸집이 잘 자란 여자로, 훤칠하고 잘생긴 자태에 아름다움이라고 해도 그만인 어떤 기품 같은 것을 지니고 있었다. 백작부인으로 말하자면 그 얼굴은 가까이서 뜯어볼 경우 그럴 만도 하게 나이의 자국을 깊이 드러냈다. 그러나 그녀는 그토록 가까이서 뜯어볼 일이 결코 생기지 않도록 제 얼굴을 잘 다루었고, 그 나이대치고 겉으로 보이는 모양새는 분명 훌륭했다. 그 딸을 두고 좋게 말할 수 있는 것도 그보다 그리 더하지는 않았다.\n\n“어머, 안 돼요, 어머니.” 어머니의 마지막 말을 들은 그녀가 말했다. “이이는 가게에 데려가기로는 세상에서 제일 못한 사람이에요. 좋아하는 것도 없고 싫어하는 것도 없다니까요. 그렇죠, 어돌퍼스?”\n\n“아니요, 있습니다. 싼 것은 죄다 좋아하고 비싼 것은 죄다 싫어하지요.”\n\n“그렇다면 마담 밀프랑네에는 절대로 우리와 같이 못 가요.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다.\n\n“거기서야 저이한테 아무 상관 없을 게다, 얘야.” 백작부인이 말했다. 아마도 얼마 전 게이지비 씨에게 넌지시 건넨 제안을 떠올리고 한 말이었을 것이다.\n\n그날은 크로즈비도 용케 빠져나올 수 있었으니, 저녁을 든 뒤에 포트먼 스퀘어로 다시 오겠다고 약속하는 것으로 그쳤다. “참, 어돌퍼스.” 문 앞에 세워 둔 세낸 마차에 그가 손을 잡아 부인을 태워 줄 때 백작부인이 말했다. “러드게이트 힐의 램버트 상점에 나 대신 좀 가 주었으면 하오. 그 사람이 내 팔찌 하나를 석 달 가까이 쥐고 있소. 그리 해 주오, 착한 사람이니. 찾을 수 있거든 찾아서 오늘 저녁에 가져다주오.”\n\n사무실로 돌아가는 길에 크로즈비는 백작부인의 심부름 따위는 하지 않겠다고 다짐했다. 그녀의 노리개를 찾겠다고 시내까지 터덜터덜 걸어갈 생각은 없었다. 그러나 다섯 시에 사무실을 나서면서 그는 거기에 갔다. 달리 할 일도 없지 않으냐고 스스로에게 변명했고, 지금 형편으로는 장모 될 이의 찌푸린 얼굴보다 웃는 얼굴이 더 편하겠다는 데 생각이 미쳤던 것이다. 그리하여 러드게이트 힐의 램버트 상점에 갔고, 거기서 그 팔찌가 두 달도 더 전에 코시 성으로 내려보내졌다는 말을 들었다.\n\n그 뒤에 그는 세브라이트에 있는 제 클럽에서 저녁을 들었다. 혼자서, 앞에 놓인 셰리 반 파인트를 두고 도무지 흐뭇하다고는 할 수 없는 기분으로 앉아 있었다. 이따금 한 사람씩 다가와 말을 걸었는데, 어떤 이는 몇 마디를, 어떤 이는 또 몇 마디를 건넸고, 두어 사람은 혼인을 두고 축하한다고 했다. 그러나 클럽은 그에게 예전 같은 곳이 아니었다. 그는 난롯가 깔개 한복판에 서서 둘러선 누구에게나 아무렇지 않게 말을 건네지도, 농담을 대령해 놓지도, 그날의 최신 소식을 목청 높여 물어 나르지도 않았다. 사람이 제가 뽐내던 자리에서 굴러떨어졌을 때 그것이 얼마나 쉽게 눈에 띄는지, 그 높이가 아무리 하찮고 그 낙차가 아무리 작다 해도 그러하다. 그렇게 떨어지고서 얼굴에, 목소리에, 걸음걸이에, 팔다리의 온갖 움직임에 그것을 드러내지 않을 사내가 어디 있겠는가? 크로즈비는 제가 떨어졌다는 것을 알았고, 양갈비를 먹는 모양새로 그것을 알고 있음을 드러내 보였다.\n\n여덟 시 반에 그는 다시 포트먼 스퀘어에 가 있었고, 두 부인이 뒤쪽 작은 응접실의 작은 난롯불 앞에 바짝 붙어 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 가구는 죄다 갈색 홀랜드 천으로 덮여 있었고, 그 자리에는 사람이 살지 않는 방이 언제나 자아내는 그 춥고 스산한 느낌이 감돌았다. 크로즈비는 클럽에서 포트먼 스퀘어까지 걸어오는 동안 자못 심각한 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 여태 그가 살아온 그런 삶은 이제 확실히 그에게서 지나가 버렸다. 그는 다시는 클럽의 귀염둥이가 될 수도, 제 힘으로 벌지 않고도 온갖 좋은 것을 받아 챙기는 사람이 될 수도 없었다. 몇 해 동안은 그것이 그의 행운이었으나 이제 그런 행운은 더 이상 그의 것일 수 없었다. 잃어버린 것 대신 손에 넣을 만한 무언가가 그의 손닿는 데에 있기는 한가? 사무실에서라면 아직 이길 수 있을 터였으니, 그런 승리를 거둘 재간이라면 둘레의 누구보다 그가 더 지니고 있었다. 그러나 그런 성공만으로는 그에게 도무지 흡족할 리 없었다. 그러다 그는 저 자신의 집에서라면 아직 행복할 수도 있지 않을까 하고 생각해 보았다—알렉산드리나가 제 어머니에게서 떨어져 나오면 그가 사랑할 만한 아내가 되어 줄 수도 있지 않을까 하고. 사내의 마음을 실패만큼 무르게 하는 것이 없고, 밖에서 만나는 이들에게 시달리는 것만큼 그를 애타게 집이라는 생각으로 돌아서게 하는 것도 없다. 그는 릴리를 버렸으니, 바깥세상이 그에게는 버리기에 너무 눈부시게 보였기 때문이다. 이제 그는 알렉산드리나로 그녀의 자리를 메워 보려 하고 있었으니, 바깥세상이 그에게는 견디기에 너무 모질게 보였기 때문이다. 아아, 아아! 사람이 제 죄를 그리 쉽게 뉘우치고 그 얼룩에서 저를 씻어 희게 할 수는 없는 노릇이다!\n\n그가 방에 들어섰을 때 두 부인은 내가 말한 대로 난롯가에 앉아 있었고, 크로즈비는 백작부인의 심기가 편치 않다는 것을 대번에 알아볼 수 있었다. 실은 혼수 문제를 두고 어머니와 딸 사이에 몇 마디가 오간 참이었으며, 알렉산드리나는 어차피 시집을 갈 바에는 백작의 딸에게 걸맞은 옷가지를 제 것으로 갖추고 가겠노라고 어머니에게 딱 잘라 말해 두었다. 이 점에서 걸맞음이란 귀한 부모 쪽보다는 평민 남편 쪽에 맞추어야 마땅하다고 어머니가 온갖 에두른 말로 설명해 보았으나 헛일이었다. 알렉산드리나는 아주 완강하게 제 권리를 내세웠고, 저의 치레 주문이 받아들여지지 않는다면 처녀인 채로 코시에 돌아가 로지나와 한 패가 될 채비나 하겠다고 백작부인에게 못을 박았다.\n\n“얘야.” 백작부인이 딱한 목소리로 말했다. “내가 네 아버지와 무슨 일을 겪어야 할지 너는 짐작도 못 한다. 게다가 그런 것들이야 나중에 다 장만할 수 있잖으냐.”\n\n“아버지가 저를 그런 식으로 대하실 권리는 없어요. 그리고 손수 돈을 안 주실 거라면 제 몫이라도 얼마쯤 쓰게 해 주셨어야죠.”\n\n“얘야, 그건 게이지비 씨 탓이었단다.”\n\n“누구 탓이든 상관없어요. 제 탓이 아닌 것만은 분명하니까요. 제 혼례복 값을 저이가 치러야 했다는 말을 저이한테서”—“저이”란 어돌퍼스 크로즈비를 가리킨 말이었다—“듣고 싶지는 않아요.”\n\n“그야 물론 아니지, 얘야.”\n\n“그럼요. 그리고 제가 당장 있어야 했던 것들도 그렇고요. 그럴 바에는 차라리 지금 당장 가서 혼례를 미뤄야겠다고 말하겠어요.”\n\n알렉산드리나는 물론 제 뜻을 관철했다. 백작부인이 펠리컨에 견줄 만한 어미의 헌신으로, 백작이 해 봐야 저를 죽이는 것 이상은 못 하리라고 생각했기 때문이다. 그리하여 물건들은 알렉산드리나가 주문하고 싶은 대로 주문되었고, 백작부인은 계산서를 게이지비 씨에게 보내라고 일렀다. 어머니 쪽에서는 크나큰 자기희생이 있었으나, 어머니가 보기에 딸 쪽에서는 아무런 희생도 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 알렉산드리나에게 몹시 심사가 틀어져 있었던 것이다.\n\n크로즈비는 의자를 끌어다 두 사람 사이에 앉아, 아주 싹싹한 말투로 팔찌의 자잘한 사연을 들려주었다. “부인께서 기억을 잘못하신 모양입니다.” 그가 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n“내 기억은 아주 좋소.” 백작부인이 말했다. “아주 좋고말고. 트위치가 그걸 받아 놓고 내게 말하지 않았다면 그건 내 잘못이 아니지요.” 트위치는 부인의 몸종이었다. 크로즈비는 형편이 어떻게 돌아가는지 알아채고 팔찌 이야기는 더 꺼내지 않았다.\n\n일이 분 뒤에 그는 손을 내밀어 알렉산드리나의 손을 잡으려 했다. 이제 한두 주면 혼인할 사이였으니 신부의 어머니가 보는 앞에서라도 그만한 애정의 표시쯤은 허락되어 마땅했을 것이다. 그는 용케 그녀의 손가락을 붙잡기는 했으나, 거기에는 답하여 부드러워지는 기색이라고는 없었다. “이러지 마세요.” 레이디 알렉산드리나가 손을 빼며 말했고, 그 말을 하는 목소리의 결은 그의 귀에 곱게 들리지 않았다. 그 순간 그는 언젠가 저녁 앨링턴의 작은 다리에서 있었던 어떤 장면을, 그리고 자기가 릴리를 어루만질 때의 그 목소리와 그 말과 그 격정을 떠올렸다. “아, 내 사랑, 내 사랑, 내 사랑!”\n\n“얘야.” 백작부인이 말했다. “내가 얼마나 고단한지 저 애들도 알 텐데. 차를 내오기는 할 셈인지 모르겠구나.” 그러자 크로즈비가 종을 울렸고, 다시 의자에 앉으면서 그것을 제 연인에게서 조금 더 멀찍이 물려 놓았다.\n\n이윽고 가정부의 조수가 차를 날라 왔는데, 그 자리를 위해 딱히 몸단장을 한 것 같지도 않아서 크로즈비는 제가 군더더기로구나 싶었다. 그러나 이것은 그의 착각이었다. 그가 이 집안에 받아들여진 이상 그런 자잘한 일은 더 이상 마음 쓸 거리가 아니었던 것이다. 두어 달 전이었다면 백작부인은 그런 하인이 크로즈비 씨 앞에 찻쟁반을 들고 나타난다는 생각만으로도 기절했으리라. 그러나 이제 그녀는 그런 것 따위에는 아주 무심했다. 크로즈비는 이 집안에 받아들여질 참이었고, 그로써 어떤 특권을 누릴 자격을 얻는 동시에 어떤 집안의 허물도 함께 떠안게 되는 것이었다. 데일 부인의 자그마한 살림에는 위세를 부리며 올라서는 일이 없었으나, 그 대신 지저분함으로 굴러떨어지는 일도 결코 없었다. 찻잔을 손에 든 채 앉아 있으면서 크로즈비는 이런 것도 생각했다.\n\n그러나 그는 곧 자리를 떴다. 그가 가려고 일어서자 알렉산드리나도 일어섰고, 그는 인사 삼아 그녀의 광대뼈에 제 코를 대어 보는 것을 허락받았다.\n\n“잘 가오, 어돌퍼스.” 백작부인이 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. “아, 잠깐만. 그대에게 부탁할 일이 하나 있었는데. 아무튼 내일 아침 사무실에 가는 길에 들러 주오.”\n\n# 제41장. 집안의 풍파\n\n크로즈비가 램버트 상점에서 드 코시 백작부인의 팔찌를 찾다가 헛걸음을 하고 있던 바로 그때, 존 임스는 버턴 크레슨트의 로퍼 부인네 대문으로 막 들어서고 있었다.\n\n“어머, 존, 크래들 씨는 어디 계세요?” 그를 맞은 첫마디가 그것이었고, 그 말을 한 사람은 그 거룩하신 어밀리아였다. 그런데 평소 살아가는 방식으로 보자면 어밀리아는 크래들 씨가 어디에 있는지 따위에는 그리 마음을 쓰는 사람이 아니었다.\n\n“크래들이 어디 있느냐고요?” 임스가 그 물음을 되뇌며 말했다. “정말이지 나도 모르겠는데요. 함께 걸어서 관청까지 가기는 했는데, 그 뒤로는 못 봤어요. 우리는 같은 방에 앉아 있지 않으니까요, 알잖아요.”\n\n“존!” 그러고서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“이번엔 또 무슨 일이에요?” 존이 말했다.\n\n“존! 그 여자가 남편을 버리고 달아났어요. 당신 이름이 존 임스인 것만큼이나 틀림없이, 그 어리석은 사람이 그 여자와 함께 달아난 거예요.”\n\n“뭐라고요, 크래들이? 나는 못 믿겠는데요.”\n\n“그 여자는 오늘 오후 두 시에 이 집을 나가서 여태 돌아오지 않았어요.” 그것은 물론 지금으로부터 겨우 네 시간 전의 일이었고, 한낮에 그렇게 집을 비웠다는 것은 유부녀에게 정부와 달아났다는 큰 죄를 씌우기에는 약한 증거일 뿐이었다. 어밀리아도 그것을 느꼈으므로 이렇게 설명을 덧붙였다. “그 사람이 위층 응접실에 있는데, 낙담이라는 것을 그림으로 그려 놓은 꼴이에요.”\n\n“누구요—크래들이요?”\n\n“루펙스 씨요. 술을 좀 마신 것 같기는 한데, 정말이지 몹시 언짢아하고 있어요. 네 시에 여기서 아내를 만나기로 약속이 되어 있었는데, 와 보니 아내가 없었던 거예요. 두 사람 방으로 뛰어 올라갔고, 이제 와서 하는 말이 아내가 제 상자를 부수고 돈을 몽땅 가져갔다는 거예요.”\n\n“하지만 그 사람한테는 돈이라고는 있어 본 적이 없잖아요.”\n\n“그저께 엄마한테 얼마쯤 냈는걸요.”\n\n“그러니까 오늘은 더더욱 돈이 있을 리 없지요.”\n\n“그 여자가 그냥 물건을 사러 나갔다거나 그런 거라면 가져가지 않았을 것들을 가져간 건 분명해요. 내가 방에 올라가서 둘러봤거든요. 그 여자한테 목걸이가 세 개 있었어요. 대단한 것들은 아니었지만, 그걸 다 걸고 갔거나 아니면 주머니에 넣어 갔을 거예요.”\n\n“크래들이 그런 식으로 그 여자와 달아났을 리는 없어요. 바보이기야 하겠지만—”\n\n“어머, 바보 맞지요, 알잖아요. 여자 문제로 그렇게 바보 같은 사람은 본 적이 없어요.”\n\n“하지만 그 사람이 하찮은 장신구 나부랭이를 훔치거나 남편 돈을 가져가는 짓에 한몫 끼지는 않았을 거예요. 정말이지 나는 그 사람이 이 일과 아무 상관도 없다고 생각해요.” 그러고서 임스는 그날 하루의 일들을 되짚어 보았고, 아침 이후로 크래들을 본 적이 없다는 것을 분명히 떠올렸다. 그 관리로 말하면 한낮에 임스의 방으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어와 거기서 빵과 치즈와 맥주를 먹어 치우는 것이 버릇이었다—언젠가 조니가 잉크에 적신 비스킷 부스러기 운운했던 것과는 딴판으로 말이다. 그런데 이 특별한 날에는 그러지 않았던 것이다. “그 친구가 그런 바보짓을 했을 것 같지는 않은데요.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“그런데 했다니까요.” 어밀리아가 말했다. “벌써 저녁 먹을 시각인데 그 사람이 어디 있나요? 그이한테 돈이 남아 있었어요, 조니?”\n\n그렇게 다그침을 받자 임스는 벗이 제게 털어놓은 비밀 하나를 드러내고 말았으니, 다른 어떤 사정으로도 그의 가슴에서 끌어낼 수 없었을 것이었다.\n\n“보름쯤 전에, 분기 지급일 바로 뒤에 그 부인이 그 친구한테서 열두 파운드를 빌렸어요. 게다가 그 전에도 진 빚이 있었고요.”\n\n“어머, 저런 맹추!” 어밀리아가 소리쳤다. “그러면서 두 달째 엄마한테는 한 실링도 안 냈잖아요!”\n\n“그저께 로퍼 부인이 루펙스 씨한테서 받은 게 어쩌면 그 친구 돈이었을지도 모르지요. 그렇다면 로퍼 부인 쪽으로 보자면 결국 같은 이야기 아닌가요.”\n\n“그럼 우리는 이제 어떻게 해야 하죠?” 어밀리아가 제 연인보다 앞서 층계를 오르며 말했다. “아, 존, 당신이 언젠가 나를 그런 식으로 대한다면 나는 어떻게 되나요? 당신이 다른 여자와 달아나 버리면 나는 어떻게 해야 하죠?”\n\n“루펙스 씨는 달아나지 않았는데요.” 임스가 말했다. 문제가 그토록 바싹 제 이야기로 끌려 들어오자 그는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n“하지만 마찬가지지요.” 어밀리아가 말했다. “마음이 갈라지는 거잖아요. 한번 맺어진 마음은 결코 갈라져서는 안 되는 거예요. 안 그래요?” 그러고서 그녀는 응접실 문 앞에 이르는 바로 그 순간 그의 팔에 매달렸다.\n\n“마음이니 화살이니 하는 건 다 헛소리예요.” 조니가 말했다. “내 생각에는 사내란 아예 혼인을 하지 않는 편이 나아요. 안녕하십니까, 루펙스 씨? 무슨 일이라도 있습니까?”\n\n루펙스 씨는 방 한가운데 놓인 의자에 앉아 등받이 너머로 머리를 젖히고 있었다. 그 자세가 어찌나 낙심한 꼴이었던지, 머리가 제 임자가 정해 준 듯한 방향으로 계속 나아갔더라면 목에서 떨어져 나가 마룻바닥으로 굴렀을 것이다. 두 손 또한 의자 뒷다리를 따라 아래로 늘어져 손가락이 거의 바닥에 닿을 지경이었고, 통틀어 그의 몰골은 축 늘어지고 처지고 처량했다. 스프루스 양은 방 한구석에 두 손을 앞으로 모아 무릎 위에 포개어 놓고 앉아 있었고, 로퍼 부인은 이마에 준엄한 덕성의 빛을—겉모양으로 판단하건대 아주 준엄하기 그지없는 덕성의 빛을 띠고 난롯가 깔개 위에 서 있었다. 그리고 그 준엄함이 오직 루펙스 부인만을 겨눈 것도 아니었다. 로퍼 부인은 루펙스 씨에게도 몹시 진력이 나 있던 터라, 그 역시 달아나 준다면—제 셈을 치를 만큼의 재산은 뒤에 남겨 두고 간다면—섭섭할 것이 없었다.\n\n존 임스가 처음 말을 건넸을 때 루펙스 씨는 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다. 다만 뒤통수에 얼마간 경련 같은 움직임이 이는 것이 보였으니, 이 응접실에 새로 들어선 사람이 고통을 한층 더 돋우었음을 알리는 것이었다. 의자도 그의 밑에서 떨렸고, 손가락은 바닥을 향해 더 가까이 뻗어 나가며 부르르 흔들렸다.\n\n“루펙스 씨, 우리는 이제 곧 저녁을 들 참입니다.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다. “임스 씨, 댁의 벗 크래들 씨는 어디 계신가요?”\n\n“정말이지 저도 모르겠습니다.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 나는 아오.” 루펙스가 벌떡 일어나 온 키를 펴고 서면서, 방금까지 제 몸을 받쳐 주던 의자를 넘어뜨리며 말했다. “가정의 행복을 배반한 자! 나는 안단 말이오. 그자가 어디에 있든, 그 거짓된 여자를 제 품에 안고 있소. 그자가 여기 있기만 하다면!” 그리고 그 마지막 말을 뱉으면서 그는 두 손과 두 팔로 어떤 동작을 해 보였으니, 그 딱한 젊은이가 이 자리에 있기만 하다면 갈가리 찢어 반으로 접고 차곡차곡 눌러 담은 다음, 그 잔해를 끝없는 허공 너머 어둠의 왕에게로 부쳐 버리겠다는 뜻인 듯했다. “배반자.” 그 절차를 끝내고서 그가 외쳤다. “거짓된 배반자! 더러운 배반자! 그리고 그 여자도!” 그러고서 이 일의 더 부드러운 쪽을 생각하자 그는 다시 의자에 몸을 던질 채비를 했다. 의자가 바닥에 넘어져 있는 것을 보고 그는 그것을 집어 들어야 했다. 그는 그것을 집어 들었고, 다시 한번 등받이 너머로 머리를 젖히고 손톱을 거의 양탄자에 닿도록 뻗었다.\n\n“제임스,” 로퍼 부인이 어느새 방에 들어와 있던 제 아들에게 말했다. “우리가 저녁을 드는 동안 네가 루펙스 씨 곁에 있어 드리는 게 좋겠구나. 자, 스프루스 양, 이런 일로 언짢으시게 해 드려서 참 죄송해요.”\n\n“저야 상할 것도 없지요.” 스프루스 양이 방을 나설 채비를 하며 말했다. “저야 뭐, 그저 늙은 여자일 뿐이니까요.”\n\n“언짢으시다니!” 루펙스가 다시 의자에서 몸을 일으키며 말했다. 언젠가 제가 셈을 치르기로 되어 있는 그 저녁이 아래층에서 먹히고 있는 동안 위층에 남아 있고 싶은 마음이 딱히 있었던 것도 아니었다. “언짢으시다니! 어느 부인이시든 내 불행 때문에 언짢으셔야 한다는 것은 나에게 깊은 슬픔이오. 스프루스 양으로 말하면, 나는 그 인품을 깊이 우러러보고 있소.”\n\n“저는 마음 쓰지 않으셔도 됩니다. 저야 그저 늙은 여자일 뿐이지요.” 스프루스 양이 말했다.\n\n“그러나, 맹세코, 나는 마음을 쓴단 말이오!” 루펙스가 소리치며 앞으로 달려 나가 스프루스 양의 손을 붙잡았다. “나는 언제나 나이 든 분에게는 마땅히—” 그러나 루펙스 씨가 나이 든 이에게 돌리려 했던 그 각별한 특권이 무엇인지는 로퍼 부인네 하숙집 사람들에게 끝내 알려지지 않았다. 바로 그 순간 방문이 다시 열리며 크래들 씨가 들어섰기 때문이다.\n\n“마침 왔군, 이 사람아, 자네 일은 자네가 해명하게.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n크래들은 대문으로 들어서면서 무슨 소리를 얼마쯤 듣기는 했으나 루펙스가 응접실에 있다는 것까지는 듣지 못한 터라, 그 신사의 얼굴을 보자 흠칫 뒤로 물러섰다. “맹세코 제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데,” 그가 입을 열었다—그러나 그는 말을 더 잇지 못했다. 루펙스는 저가 우러른다던 그 나이 든 부인의 손을 놓아 버리고 순식간에 그에게 달려들었고, 크래들은 그 손아귀 아래에서 사시나무 잎처럼 떨었다—아니, 흔들리는 사시나무 잎이 눈을 감고 입을 벌리고 혀를 늘어뜨린 꼴로 보이는 것이 아니라면, 사시나무 잎과는 딴판이었다.\n\n“이보십시오,” 임스가 벗을 도우러 앞으로 나서며 말했다. “이건 도무지 안 될 일입니다, 루펙스 씨. 술을 드셨군요. 내일 아침까지 기다렸다가 그때 크래들에게 말씀하시는 편이 좋겠습니다.”\n\n“내일 아침이라니, 이 독사 같은 놈.” 루펙스가 제 먹이를 붙든 채 어깨 너머로 임스를 돌아보며 고함쳤다. 누가 독사인지는 분명히 밝혀지지 않았다. “저자가 언제 내 아내를 돌려준단 말이오? 언제 내 명예를 돌려준단 말이오?”\n\n“매—맹—맹세코 제—” 가엾은 크래들 씨가 제 결백을 다짐하고 루펙스 부인에 관한 한 제 깨끗함에 명예를 걸어 보려 한 것은 그 순간에는 헛일이었다. 루펙스는 여전히 제 원수의 크라바트를 붙들고 있었다. 임스가 이제 그의 팔을 붙잡아 움직임을 얼마쯤 막아 놓았으므로 더 심한 공격으로 나아가지는 못했지만 말이다.\n\n“제미마, 제미마, 제미마!” 로퍼 부인이 외쳤다. “순경을 불러 오너라. 순경을 불러 와!” 그러나 어머니보다 침착한 어밀리아가 앞쪽 창문 하나로 달려가려는 제미마를 붙들었다. “그냥 거기 있어.” 어밀리아가 말했다. “한두 번 숨 쉴 참이면 조용해질 테니까.” 그리고 어밀리아의 말이 옳았음은 의심할 여지가 없다. 집안에 소동이 났다고 순경을 부르는 것은 부엌 굴뚝의 검댕에 불이 붙었다고 소방 펌프를 불러들이는 것과 같다. 그런 경우에는 잘만 다스리면 소방 펌프의 도움 없이도 검댕이 저절로 다 타 버리게 할 수 있다. 이번 일에서도 순경은 불려 오지 않았고, 그들이 왔더라면 그 자리의 누구에게도 이롭지 않았으리라고 나는 생각하는 편이다.\n\n“맹—세—코—저는 그 부인에 대해 아무것도 모릅니다.” 임스의 설득으로 루펙스가 마침내 손아귀를 늦추었을 때 크래들이 겨우 뱉어 낸 첫마디가 그것이었다.\n\n루펙스는 비웃는 웃음을 띠고 스프루스 양 쪽으로 돌아섰다. “이자의 말을 들어 보시오—가정의 행복을 해치는 이 원수의 말을—하! 하! 여보시오, 내 아내를 어디로 데려다 놓았는지 말하시오!”\n\n“저한테 잉글랜드 은행을 다 주신대도 저는 모릅니다.” 크래들이 말했다.\n\n“저이는 정말 모르는 게 분명해요.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다. 크래들을 향한 그녀의 의심은 가라앉기 시작하고 있었다. 그러나 의심이 가라앉는 만큼 그에 대한 존경도 줄어들었다. 스프루스 양도, 어밀리아도, 제미마도 마찬가지였다. 그들은 모두 그가 루펙스 부인과 달아났다며 지독한 얼간이라고 여겨 왔는데, 이제는 달아나지 않았다는 이유로 그를 못난 위인이라고 여기기 시작한 것이다. 그 활발한 어리석음을 저질렀더라면 그는 흥미로운 얼간이가 되었을 터였다. 그러나 이제, 그들 모두가 짐작하는 대로 그가 루펙스 부인에 대해 저희보다 더 아는 것이 없다면, 그는 아무런 흥미도 없는 그저 얼간이일 뿐이었다.\n\n“물론 모르지요.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“나도 모르는 것만큼이나요.” 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“저이 얼굴만 봐도 결백하다는 게 드러나지요.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다.\n\n“정말 그렇습니다.” 스프루스 양이 말했다.\n\n루펙스는 저가 의심하는 사내를 그렇게들 두둔하는 동안 이 사람 저 사람에게로 몸을 돌리며, 그런 말이 나올 때마다 고개를 내저었다. “그럼 저자가 모른다면 누가 안단 말이오?” 그가 물었다. “내가 지난 석 달 동안 이 모든 것을 보아 오지 않았소? 평생 가정의 안락에 길들어 온 그런 여자가 저녁 시각에 내 재산과 제 패물을 죄다 챙겨 이런 식으로 나가 버렸는데, 아무도 그녀를 부추기지 않았고 아무도 도와주지 않았다는 것이 말이나 되오? 그것이 나 같은 사람에게 들려줄 이야기요? 그런 소리는 해병한테나 가서 하시오!” 루펙스 씨는 이 말을 하는 동안 방 안을 서성이고 있었고, 말을 마치면서 제 주머니 손수건을 힘껏 바닥에 내던졌다. “나는 무엇을 해야 할지 아오, 로퍼 부인.” 그가 말했다. “어떤 수를 밟아야 할지 안단 말이오. 내일 아침에 이 일을 내 변호사 손에 넘기겠소.” 그러고서 그는 손수건을 집어 들고 식당으로 걸어 내려갔다.\n\n“자네야 물론 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르지?” 임스가 벗에게 한마디 하려고 위층으로 뛰어 올라가, 손을 씻고 있는 그에게 말했다.\n\n“뭐—머라이어 말인가? 그걸 묻는 거라면, 나는 그이가 어디 있는지 모르네.”\n\n“물론 그걸 묻는 거지. 달리 무얼 묻겠나? 그런데 자네는 어째서 그이를 머라이어라고 부르나?”\n\n“잘못이지. 잘못인 줄은 나도 인정하네. 그 말이 저절로 튀어나오는 걸 어쩌겠나.”\n\n“저절로 튀어나온다니! 내 한마디 해 두겠는데, 이 사람아, 자네는 아무것도 아닌 일로 스스로를 곤경에 빠뜨릴 걸세. 그자가 제 물건을 훔쳤다고 자네를 순경 앞에 끌고 갈 거야—”\n\n“하지만, 조니—”\n\n“나도 다 아네. 물론 자네는 아무것도 훔치지 않았고, 물론 훔칠 것도 없었지. 하지만 자네가 계속 그이를 머라이어라고 부르고 다니면 그자가 자네를 걸고넘어질 구실을 잡게 될 걸세. 사내가 남의 아내를 이름으로 부르는 데에는 반드시 까닭이 있는 법이니까.”\n\n“물론 우리는 벗이었지.” 크래들이 말했다. 그는 이렇게 보아 주는 것이 자못 마음에 들었던 것이다.\n\n“그래—자네들은 벗이었지! 그이가 자네 돈을 우려냈고, 그게 처음이자 끝이야. 그런데 이제 와서 자네가 그 우정을 자랑삼아 내보이고 다니면 돈을 더 뜯길 걸세. 자네는 스스로 얼간이 노릇을 하고 있어. 요컨대 그게 전부야.”\n\n“그럼 자네는 그 아가씨한테 무슨 꼴이 되었는가? 나보다 더한 얼간이도 아직 세상에 있는 법이야, 조니 도련님.” 임스는 이 되받아치기에 마땅한 대답이 없었으므로 방을 나와 아래층으로 내려갔다. 크래들도 곧 뒤를 따랐고, 몇 분 뒤에 그들은 모두 로퍼 부인의 후한 식탁에 둘러앉아 함께 저녁을 먹고 있었다.\n\n저녁을 마치자마자 루펙스는 자리를 떴고, 위층에서는 그 부인의 가출을 두고 이야기가 두루 오갔다.\n\n“내가 그 사람이라면 그 여자에 대해서는 한마디도 묻지 않고 그냥 가게 두겠어요.” 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 그러고는 가는 데마다 그 여자가 진 빚이 죄다 따라붙겠지.” 어밀리아의 오라비가 말했다.\n\n“나라면 그 부인보다는 그 부인이 진 빚 쪽을 택하겠는데요.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“제 생각에는 그이가 학대받아 온 여자예요.” 크래들이 말했다. “존경할 수 있고 사랑할 수 있는 남편을, 뭐 그런 것을 두었더라면 그이는 매력 있는 여인이 되었을 겁니다.”\n\n“그이도 그 사람 못지않게 나쁘지요.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그 말씀에는 동의할 수 없습니다, 로퍼 부인.” 그 부인의 투사가 말을 이었다. “아마 저는 여기 있는 누구보다도 그이의 처지를 잘 이해하고 있을 텐데, 그러니—”\n\n“그러니까 바로 그게 크래들 씨가 하시면 안 될 일이라는 거예요.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다. 그리고 이제 이 집의 안주인은 어머니다운 위엄을 잔뜩 담고, 얼마간은 여자다운 준엄함까지 담아 제 속을 털어놓았다. “그건 바로 댁 같은 젊은이가 알아서는 안 될 일이지요. 그런 유부녀가 댁한테 무어고 댁이 그이한테 무업니까. 그이의 처지를 이해한다는 게 댁하고 무슨 상관이에요? 언젠가 댁도 아내를 두게 되면—두기라도 한다면 말이지만—다른 누가 끼어들지 않아도 그때 가서 겪을 골치가 넘칠 만큼 있다는 걸 알게 될 거예요. 그렇다고 루펙스 부인 일로 댁이 어린양처럼 결백하다는 걸 내가 안 믿는다는 소리는 아니고요. 몹쓸 짓을 했느냐 하는 데까지는 말이에요. 하지만 댁은 남의 일에 참견하다가 스스로 이 소동에 말려들었고, 위층에서 저 사람 손에 목이 졸릴 뻔했잖아요. 그리고 저런 식으로 여자한테 반한 척하고 다니는데, 그 여자는 댁의 어머니뻘이나 되고요. 그러니 누가 이상하다 하지 않겠어요? 댁의 어머님이 그 꼴을 보시면 무어라 하시겠어요?”\n\n“하하하!” 크래들이 웃었다.\n\n“웃으셔도 좋지만, 나는 그런 어리석은 짓이 딱 질색이에요. 젊은 남자가 젊은 여자를 사랑하는 것을 보면 나는 그 사람을 존경하지요.” 그러고서 그녀는 조니 임스를 바라보았다. “존경하고말고요—이따금 하지 말아야 할 짓을 한다 해도요. 사내들이란 대개 다 그러지요. 하지만 댁처럼 젊은 남자가, 크래들 씨, 제 몸가짐도 할 줄 모르는 늙은 유부녀 꽁무니를 졸졸 따라다니는 것을, 그것도 그저 그 여자가 그러도록 놔둔다는 까닭 하나로 그러는 것을 보면—어휴!—치마 입힌 낡은 빗자루라도 그만큼은 하겠어요! 그 꼴을 보면 속이 다 뒤집히는데, 그게 사실이에요. 나는 그런 걸 사내답다고 하지 않아요. 사내답지 못하잖아요, 안 그래요, 스프루스 양?”\n\n“저야 물론 아무것도 모릅니다.” 그렇게 물음을 받은 그 부인이 말했다. “하지만 젊은 신사분이라면 제 뜻을 밝힐 때가 올 때까지는 스스로를 지켜야 하는 법이지요—그래도 언짢게 여기지는 마세요, 크래들 씨.”\n\n“유부녀가 제 남편 말고 누구를 꽁무니에 달고 다닐 까닭이 무언지 나는 모르겠어요.” 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“게다가 남편조차 늘 그럴 것은 없지요.” 존 임스가 말했다.\n\n그로부터 한 시간쯤 지났을 때 대문 초인종이 울렸고, 제미마의 비명이 무언가 중대한 순간이 닥쳤음을 모두에게 알렸다. 어밀리아가 벌떡 일어나 문을 열자 아래층 계단에서 여자 옷자락 스치는 소리가 들려왔다. “어이구, 마님, 저희를 이렇게 놀래키시다니요.” 제미마가 말했다. “저희는 다들 마님께서 달아나신 줄로만 알았습니다요.”\n\n“루펙스 부인이에요.” 어밀리아가 말했다. 그리고 두어 분 뒤에 그 학대받았다는 부인이 방에 들어와 있었다.\n\n“저런, 여러분.” 그녀가 쾌활하게 말했다. “저녁을 안 드시고 기다리신 분은 없겠지요.”\n\n“네, 기다리지 않았습니다.” 로퍼 부인이 아주 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n“그런데 내 오슨은 어디 있나요? 집에서 저녁을 안 들었나요? 크래들 씨, 제 숄 좀 받아 주시겠어요? 아니, 그러지 않으시는 편이 낫겠네요. 사람들이 어찌나 말이 많은지요. 안 그래요, 스프루스 양? 임스 씨가 해 주시지요. 그거야 아주 안전하다는 걸 다들 아시니까요. 안 그래요, 어밀리아 양?”\n\n“아주 그렇겠지요.” 어밀리아가 말했다. 그리하여 루펙스 부인은 오늘 이 자리에서는 그쪽에서 제 편을 구할 수 없다는 것을 알았다. 임스가 일어나 숄을 받아 들었고, 루펙스 부인은 말을 이었다.\n\n“그래서 오슨은 집에서 저녁을 안 들었나요? 아마 극장에서 붙들어 두었나 보지요. 하지만 나는 하루 종일 생각했답니다. 그이가 제 새가 날아가 버렸다고 여길 때 얼마나 재미있을까 하고요.”\n\n“집에서 저녁을 들었습니다.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다. “그리고 그리 좋아하는 것 같지도 않더군요. 재미랄 것은 별로 없었다고 제가 장담하지요.”\n\n“어머, 그랬어요, 정말요? 그이는 나를 제 단춧구멍에 매달아 두고 싶어 할 사람이지요. 나는 벗들을 몇 만났어요—여자 벗들이요, 크래들 씨. 그중 둘은 남편을 데리고 있었고요. 그래서 우리끼리 무리를 지어 그냥 햄프턴 코트까지 다녀왔지요. 그래, 우리 나리께서는 또 나가 버리셨다고요? 나 혼자 쏘다닌 값이 그거로군요, 안 그래요, 스프루스 양?”\n\n로퍼 부인은 그날 밤 잠자리에 들면서, 값이 얼마가 들고 성가심이 얼마가 되든 이 부부 하숙인들을 떨쳐 내는 데 더는 시간을 끌지 않겠노라고 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n# 제42장. 릴리의 머리맡\n\n릴리 데일은 체질이 튼튼했고, 회복이 재발이나 지지부진한 쇠약으로 더뎌지는 일도 없었다. 그런데도 열이 물러간 뒤로 여러 날 동안 그녀는 자리에 누워 지내야만 했다. 그동안 크로프츠 선생은 날마다 왔다. 데일 부인이 그러지 말아 달라고 청한 것은 헛일이었다. 이제 그 수고가 반드시 필요한 고비는 지났으니 보수도 받지 않는 그 수고를 더 받을 수는 없다고 스스로 여긴다고, 부인은 꾸밈없는 말로 그에게 일렀다. 그는 자잘한 농담으로만 대답하거나 아예 대답하지 않았다. 그러면서도 그는 날마다, 거의 언제나 같은 시각에, 해가 기울 무렵에 맞추어 왔으니, 그래야 어스름 속에 십오 분쯤 앉아 있다가 어두워진 뒤에 말을 타고 게스트윅으로 돌아갈 수 있었기 때문이다. 이 무렵 벨은 동생의 방에 드나들 수 있게 되었고, 크로프츠 선생을 만날 때면 늘 릴리의 머리맡에서 만났다. 그러나 그가 반쯤만 입 밖에 낸 말로 그녀에게 사랑을 건네고 그녀 또한 반쯤만 입 밖에 낸 말로 그것을 물리친 그날 이후로, 그녀는 그와 단둘이 앉아 있는 일이 없었다. 그 뒤로도 계단에서, 혹은 현관에 서서 그를 혼자 만난 적은 있었으나 예전처럼 그와 남아 이야기를 나누지는 않았고, 그의 사랑에 관한 말은 반이든 온전히든 더는 입 밖에 나오지 않았다.\n\n벨은 지나간 일을 다른 누구에게도 말하지 않았다. 릴리였다면 아마 어머니에게도 언니에게도 그 자리에서 다 털어놓았을 것이다. 그러나 벨에게 벌어진 것과 같은 장면은 릴리에게는 애초에 있을 수 없었을 것이다. 그 일이 그녀에게 어떤 식으로 흘러갔든, 만남이 끝났을 때에는 분명 또렷하게 들려줄 이야기가 남았을 것이다. 그녀라면 자기가 그 사람을 사랑하는지, 사랑할 수 있는지 알았을 것이고, 참되고 알아들을 만한 대답을 그에게 주었을 것이다. 벨은 그러지 못했다. 그녀가 준 대답은 참이라면 알아들을 수 없는 것이었고, 알아들을 만하다면 참이 아니었다. 그런데도 지나간 일을 곱씹으려 물러났을 때, 그녀는 행복하고 흡족했으며 거의 의기양양하기까지 했다. 그녀는 크로프츠 선생에게서 무언가를 더 기대하는지, 그 무언가가 대체 무엇인지 아직 한 번도 자신에게 물어본 적이 없었다—그런데도 그녀는 행복했다!\n\n릴리는 이제 침대에서 깜찍하고 당돌해져서, 앞서 겪은 고통과 속박에 대한 보상으로 회복기 환자에게 허락되는 자잘한 응석을 부렸다. 그녀는 제 저녁상을 두고 몹시 안달하는 척했고, 크로프츠 선생이 아무리 제멋대로 굴어도 며칠 며칠에는 밖에 나가겠노라고 선언했다. “그 사람, 알고 보면 늙은 야만인이야.” 어느 날 저녁 그가 돌아간 뒤에 그녀가 언니에게 말했다. “나머지 사람들이랑 똑같이 못됐어.”\n\n“나머지 사람들이 누구를 말하는 건지는 모르겠구나.” 벨이 말했다. “하지만 어쨌든 그분이 늙지는 않았잖니.”\n\n“내 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알잖아. 그 사람은 그러펀 선생만큼이나 무뚝뚝하고, 누구든 자기가 시키는 대로 해야 한다고 생각해. 물론 언니는 그 사람 편을 들겠지.”\n\n“너도 마땅히 그래야지, 그분이 얼마나 잘해 주셨는지 생각하면.”\n\n“언니만 아니면 누구한테든 그럴 거야. 나는 언니한테 그 사람 흉보는 게 좋거든.”\n\n“릴리, 릴리!”\n\n“정말 그렇다니까. 언니한테서 불꽃 하나 튀기기가 어찌나 어려운지, 부싯돌이 박힌 자리를 한번 찾아내고 나면 두드려 대지 않을 수가 없어. 내가 일요일에 일어나면 안 된다니, 그게 무슨 소리야? 나는 내키면 일어날 거야.”\n\n“엄마가 그러지 말라고 하셔도?”\n\n“아, 하지만 엄마는 그러지 않으실걸. 그 사람이 참견해서 엄마한테 이래라저래라 하지만 않는다면 말이야. 아, 언니, 그 사람이 결혼하면 얼마나 폭군일까!”\n\n“그럴까?”\n\n“그리고 언니가 그 사람 아내라면 얼마나 고분고분할까! 두 사람이 서로 사랑하지 않는다는 건 천만번 아까운 일이야—그러니까, 사랑하지 않는다면 말이지.”\n\n“릴리, 그 얘기는 우리 사이에 약속이 있었던 걸로 아는데.”\n\n“아! 하지만 그건 다른 날들의 일이잖아. 그 약속을 한 뒤로 모든 게 달라졌어—온 세상이 달라졌는걸.” 그렇게 말하는 그녀의 목소리는 가락이 바뀌어 거의 슬프게 들렸다. “나는 이제 무엇이든 내가 말하고 싶은 것을 말해도 되게끔 허락받아야 할 것 같은데.”\n\n“네가 그러고 싶다면 그렇게 하렴, 우리 릴리.”\n\n“언니도 알잖아. 나한테는 이제 다시는 내 것이라고 이야기할 만한 게 없어.”\n\n“아, 얘, 그런 말 하지 마.”\n\n“하지만 사실인걸, 언니. 그런데 왜 말하면 안 돼? 혼자 있을 때, 그 일을 곱씹고—곱씹고, 곱씹고, 또 곱씹는 시간에 내가 그 말을 스스로에게 한 번도 안 한다고 생각해? 언니는—언니는 내가 가끔 그 얘기를 하는 것쯤은 아까워하면 안 돼.”\n\n“나는 너한테 아무것도 아까워하지 않아—다만 언제까지나 그래야만 한다고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없어서 그래.”\n\n“언니 자신한테 물어봐, 언니라면 어떨지. 그런데 나는 가끔 언니가 나를 언니 자신과는 다른 잣대로 재는 것 같다는 생각이 들어.”\n\n“정말 그래. 네가 나보다 얼마나 나은 사람인지 아니까.”\n\n“나는 그 모든 걸 잊을 수 있을 만큼 그렇게 나은 사람이 아니야. 나는 결코 잊지 못할 거야. 그건 또렷하게, 조금도 의심 없이 마음을 정해 두었어.”\n\n“릴리, 릴리, 릴리! 제발 그런 말 하지 마.”\n\n“하지만 나는 말할 거야. 그래도 나는 그렇게 시무룩하고 우울하게 굴지는 않았잖아, 그렇지 않아, 언니? 나도 조금은 칭찬을 받을 만하다고 생각하는데, 정말이지 언니는 나한테 세상에서 제일 작은 특권 하나도 주지 않으려고 해.”\n\n“어떤 특권을 주면 좋겠는데?”\n\n“크로프츠 선생님 이야기를 하는 거.”\n\n“릴리, 너는 고약하고 고약한 폭군이야.” 그러고서 벨은 동생 위로 몸을 굽혀 그녀에게 엎드리며 입을 맞추었고, 저녁의 어스름 속에 제 얼굴을 감추었다. 그 뒤로 벨이 크로프츠 선생에게 아주 무심하지만은 않다는 것이 두 사람 사이에 받아들여진 이야기가 되었다.\n\n“그이가 뭐라고 했는지 들었지, 얘야.” 이튿날 크로프츠 선생이 돌아간 뒤 세 사람이 함께 방에 있을 때 데일 부인이 말했다. 데일 부인은 침대 한쪽에, 벨은 다른 쪽에 서 있었고, 릴리는 두 사람 모두를 나무라고 있는 참이었다. “내일 한두 시간쯤 일어나 있어도 되지만, 방 밖으로는 나가지 않는 편이 좋겠다더구나.”\n\n“그게 무슨 소용이에요, 엄마? 늘 같은 벽지만 쳐다보고 있으려니 너무 지겨워요. 정말 지긋지긋한 벽지예요. 자꾸만 무늬를 세고 또 세게 만들잖아요. 엄마는 어떻게 여기서 사시는지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“나는 익숙해졌으니 그렇지.”\n\n“나는 그런 것에 도무지 익숙해질 수가 없어요. 그저 세고, 세고, 또 세게 될 뿐이죠. 내가 뭘 하고 싶은지 말씀드릴게요. 그리고 그게 제일 좋은 방법이기도 할 거예요.”\n\n“뭘 하고 싶은데?” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“내일 아홉 시에 일어나서 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 교회에 가는 거야. 그러고서 저녁에 크로프츠 선생님이 오시면 내가 학교에 내려가 있다고 말해 주는 거지.”\n\n“그건 그다지 권하고 싶지 않구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그러면 그이가 얼마나 근사하게 놀라겠어요. 그리고 규칙대로라면 마땅히 그래야 할 텐데도 내가 당장 죽지 않는 걸 알면 얼마나 김이 빠지겠어요.”\n\n“아무리 좋게 말해도 배은망덕한 짓이지.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“아니야, 조금도 그렇지 않아. 오기 싫으면 안 와도 되잖아. 그리고 나는 그이가 나를 보러 온다고는 조금도 믿지 않아. 엄마, 그렇게 쳐다보셔도 소용없어요. 하지만 그게 사실인걸요. 그리고 내가 어떻게 할 건지 말씀드릴까요, 나는 다시 아픈 척할 거예요. 안 그러면 그 딱한 양반이 하나뿐인 낙을 빼앗기고 말 테니까요.”\n\n“다 나을 때까지는 저 애가 하고 싶은 말을 다 하게 두는 수밖에 없겠구나.” 데일 부인이 웃으며 말했다. 이제 거의 어두워져 있었으므로, 데일 부인은 벨의 손이 이불 밑으로 기어들어 동생의 손을 잡은 것을 보지 못했다. “사실이에요, 엄마.” 릴리가 말을 이었다. “언니가 아니라고 해 볼 테면 해 보라죠. 그이가 언니를 자기한테 반하게 만들어 주기만 한다면 나를 침대에 붙들어 둔 것쯤은 용서해 줄 텐데.”\n\n“쟤가 흥정을 해 두었어요, 엄마.” 벨이 말했다. “다 나을 때까지는 하고 싶은 말을 다 하기로요.”\n\n“나는 언제나 하고 싶은 말을 다 하기로 했어. 그게 흥정이었고, 나는 그걸 끝까지 지킬 거야.”\n\n다음 일요일에 릴리는 정말로 일어났으나 어머니의 침실을 벗어나지는 않았다. 크로프츠 선생이 들렀을 때 그녀는 환자가 처음으로 자리에서 일어났을 때의 그 반쯤은 위엄 있고 반쯤은 호사스러운 상태로 거기 앉아 있었다. 거기서 그녀는 양고기 구이를 아주 조금 먹고는 한 점도 더 주지 않는다고 어머니를 인색한 늙은이라 불렀고, 거기서 포트와인 반 잔을 마시며 맛이 아주 고약하다고, 의사의 물약보다 곱절은 더 못하다고 우겨 댔다. 그리고 거기서, 일요일이었건만, 독서 대중의 젊은 층과 나이 든 층이 서로 어긋나는 비평을 쏟아 내는 가운데 막 완결된 그 절묘한 새 소설을 읽으며 해가 남은 마지막 한 시간을 마음껏 누렸다.\n\n“그 여자가 그 사람을 받아들인 건 정말 잘한 일이야, 언니.” 빛이 스러지자 그녀가 책을 내려놓고 그 이야기를 칭찬하기 시작하며 말했다.\n\n“그야 당연한 일이지.” 벨이 말했다. “소설에서는 언제나 그게 옳은 일이니까. 그래서 나는 소설이 싫어. 너무 달아.”\n\n“나는 그래서 좋아, 너무 다니까. 설교는 네가 어떤 사람인지가 아니라 어떤 사람이 되어야 하는지를 일러 주는 것이고, 소설은 네가 무엇을 얻게 될지가 아니라 무엇을 얻고 싶은지를 일러 주어야 하는 거야.”\n\n“그렇다면 나는 옛 유파로 돌아가서 여주인공을 정말 여주인공답게 만들겠어. 에든버러에서 런던까지 내내 걸어 올라가다가 도둑 떼를 만나기도 하고, 아니면 부상당한 영웅을 간호하면서 창문 너머로 전투를 그려 보이기도 하는 여주인공 말이야. 우리는 그런 것에 싫증이 났지. 아니면 요즘 글 쓰는 사람들이 그런 걸 못 쓰게 된 거든지. 하지만 우리가 진짜 삶을 담을 거라면, 진짜로 담아야지.”\n\n“아니야, 언니, 아니야!” 릴리가 말했다. “진짜 삶은 때로 너무 아프잖아.” 그러자 언니는 이내 동생의 발치 바닥에 내려앉아 그 손에 입 맞추고 무릎을 어루만지며, 그 상처가 아물게 해 달라고 빌었다.\n\n그날 아침 릴리는 크로프츠 선생이 한 말을 남김없이 들려 달라고 언니를 구슬려 내는 데 성공했었다. 벨은 자기가 한 말도 다 들려줄 셈이었으나, 이야기가 그 대목에 이르자 그녀의 설명은 몹시 궁색해졌다. “나는 아무 말도 안 한 것 같아.” 그녀가 말했다. “하지만 침묵은 언제나 승낙이야. 그이도 그렇게 알 거야.” 릴리가 되받았다. “아니야, 그이는 그렇게 알지 않아. 내 침묵은 아무 승낙도 하지 않았어. 그건 내가 확실해. 그리고 그이도 그렇게 여기지 않았고.” “하지만 언니가 그이를 거절하려던 건 아니었잖아?” “거절하려던 것 같아. 내가 무슨 마음이었는지도 모르겠어. 그러니 그렇다는 낯빛보다 아니라는 낯빛을 하는 편이 더 안전했지. 말로 하지 않았다 해도 낯빛으로는 분명 그랬을 거야.” “하지만 지금이라면 거절하지 않을 거지?” 릴리가 물었다. “모르겠어.” 벨이 말했다. “마음을 정하려면 몇 해는 걸릴 것 같은데, 그이는 다시 묻지 않을 테니까.”\n\n벨은 여전히 동생의 발치에서 그 발을 어루만지며 그 상처가 때가 되면 아물기를 온 마음으로 빌고 있었는데, 그때 데일 부인이 들어와 의사가 날마다 오는 왕진을 왔다고 알렸다. “그럼 나는 나갈게.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“그러면 안 되지.” 릴리가 말했다. “그이는 그냥 아침 문안을 오는 것뿐인데 아무도 달아날 것 없어. 자, 크로프츠 선생님, 시계를 들고 제 앞에 서 계실 것 없어요. 악수하는 것 말고는 제 손에 손대지 못하게 할 테니까요.” 그러고서 그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀었다. “그리고 제 혀에 관해 아실 건 소리로 들으시면 돼요.”\n\n“나는 아가씨 혀에는 조금도 관심이 없어요.”\n\n“그러시겠죠. 그래도 언젠가는 관심이 생기실걸요. 나는 마음만 먹으면 할 말을 다 할 수 있어요, 그렇죠, 엄마?”\n\n“크로프츠 선생님도 이젠 그쯤은 아실 게다, 얘야.”\n\n“모르겠어요. 신사분들이 배우는 데 아주 굼뜬 일들이 있거든요. 어쨌든 앉으세요, 크로프츠 선생님, 편안하고 점잖게 계세요. 이제 여기서 선생님이 더는 주인이 아니라는 걸 아셔야 하니까요. 나는 이제 자리에서 일어났고, 선생님의 치세는 끝났어요.”\n\n“세상의 은혜 갚음이란 게 죄 저렇답니다.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“의사한테 고마워하는 사람이 어디 있어요? 의사는 다른 의사를 이기려고 사람을 고쳐 줄 뿐이에요. 그러고는 그러펀 선생 댁 문 앞을 지나면서 이렇게 말하는 거죠. ‘저것 보라지, 저 아가씨가 당신을 불렀더라면 벌써 죽었거나 아니면 열두 달은 앓아누웠을 거야.’ 그러펀 선생 환자가 죽으면 좋아서 껑충껑충 뛰지 않으세요?”\n\n“물론 뜁니다—장터 한복판에서, 누구나 다 볼 수 있게 말이지요.” 의사가 말했다.\n\n“릴리, 어쩌면 그런 끔찍한 소리를 하니?” 언니가 말했다.\n\n그러고서 의사는 정말로 자리에 앉았고, 그들은 다 함께 난롯가에서 아주 아늑하게, 의술과는 상관없거나 쓰임새로만 반쯤 의술에 걸치는 이야기들을 나누었다. 이야기는 차츰 임스 부인과 존 임스에게로 돌아갔다. 이삼일 전에 크로프츠는 철도역에서 있었던 그 일을 데일 부인에게 이야기해 주었는데, 그때까지 부인은 그 일을 전혀 듣지 못하고 있었다. 젊은 임스가 크로즈비를 엄청나게 두들겨 팼다는 말을—게스트윅에서 사실로 굳어진 그 사건의 소문이 그 다툼의 성질을 그렇게 전하고 있었다—확실히 듣고서, 데일 부인은 얼마간의 갈채를 참아 낼 수가 없었다.\n\n“착한 아이!” 부인은 저도 모르게 말했다. “착한 아이 같으니! 그건 그 아이의 정직한 마음에서 나온 거예요!” 그러고서 부인은 굳이 할 필요도 없었을 당부를 의사에게 각별히 해 두었으니, 릴리 앞에서는 그 일을 한마디도 소곤거리지 말라는 것이었다.\n\n“어제 저택에 갔었는데요.” 의사가 말했다. “백작님이 조니 도련님 이야기밖에는 하시지 않더군요. 세상에 그만한 젊은이가 없다고 하시던걸요.” 그러자 데일 부인이 발로 그를 툭 건드렸으니, 이야기가 조니의 무용담 쪽으로 끌려갈까 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n“정말 기뻐요.” 릴리가 말했다. “나는 사람들이 언젠가는 존을 알아볼 줄 알고 있었어요.”\n\n“레이디 줄리아도 그이를 꼭 그만큼 아끼신답니다.” 의사가 말했다.\n\n“어머나!” 릴리가 말했다. “두 분이 짝을 이루기라도 하면 어쩌죠!”\n\n“릴리, 어쩌면 그렇게 엉뚱하니?”\n\n“어디 보자. 그이는 우리한테 무슨 촌수가 될까? 버나드한테는 분명 이모부가 될 테고, 크리스토퍼 삼촌한테는 반쪽짜리 동서가 되겠지. 이상하지 않겠어?”\n\n“좀 그렇긴 하겠구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그이가 버나드한테 잘해 주면 좋겠어요. 언니도 그렇지? 그이가 소득세 사무국을 그만두게 되나요, 크로프츠 선생님?”\n\n“아직 그렇게 정해졌다는 말은 못 들었어요.” 그렇게 그들은 존 임스 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n“농담은 접어 두고요.” 릴리가 말했다. “나는 드 게스트 백작님이 그이의 손을 잡아 주신 게 정말 기뻐요. 백작이 다른 누구보다 낫다고 생각해서가 아니라, 사람들이 그이에게 무언가 들어 있다는 걸 알아보기 시작했다는 뜻이니까요. 나는 늘 말했어요, 존을 비웃던 사람들이 언젠가는 그이가 고개를 들고 있는 걸 보게 되리라고.” 이 말들은 죄다 데일 부인의 마음속 깊이 가라앉았다. 다가올 어느 날, 제 귀한 딸이 이 새로운 젊은 영웅을 사랑하도록 이끌릴 수만 있다면! 그러나 바로 그 마지막 영웅다운 행동이야말로 그런 사랑의 가능성을 무엇보다 세차게 가로막지 않겠는가!\n\n“이제 그만 가 봐야겠습니다.” 의사가 의자에서 일어나며 말했다. 이때 벨은 방을 나가고 없었으나 데일 부인은 아직 거기 있었다.\n\n“그렇게 서두르실 것 없어요, 오늘 저녁만은요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“오늘 저녁만은 왜죠?”\n\n“오늘이 마지막일 테니까요. 다시 앉으세요, 크로프츠 선생님. 선생님께 드릴 짧은 인사말이 있어요. 아침 내내 준비했으니 말할 기회를 주셔야죠.”\n\n“모레 다시 올 테니 그때 듣지요.”\n\n“하지만 저는 지금 들으셨으면 좋겠어요, 선생님. 내가 처음으로 내 옥좌에 올랐는데 내 말을 따라 주셔야 하는 것 아닌가요? 다정하고 다정하신 크로프츠 선생님, 선생님이 해 주신 그 모든 일에 제가 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할까요?”\n\n“우리 가운데 누가 저분께 제대로 감사할 수 있겠니?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“저는 감사 인사가 질색입니다.” 의사가 말했다. “다정한 눈길 한 번이 그 모든 것보다 값지고, 이 집에서는 그런 눈길을 많이 받았으니까요.”\n\n“어쨌든 저희 마음속 사랑은 선생님 것이랍니다.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“모두에게 하느님의 축복이 있기를!” 그가 떠날 채비를 하며 말했다.\n\n“하지만 저는 아직 인사말을 하지 않았어요.” 릴리가 말했다. “그리고 사실대로 말하면, 엄마, 엄마가 나가 주셔야 해요. 안 그러면 저는 도저히 못 할 거예요. 이렇게 엄마를 내쫓다니 아주 무례하죠? 하지만 삼 분이면 돼요.” 그러자 데일 부인은 무어라 자잘한 농담을 던지며 방을 나갔다. 그러나 나가면서도 마음이 썩 편치는 않았다. 릴리가 크로프츠 선생에게 어떤 말을 하는 것이 좋을지 그 재량에 맡겨 두고 자리를 비켜 준 것이 옳았을까? 여태 부인은 딸들을 의심해 본 적이 없었다—그 분별력조차 의심한 적이 없었다. 그러므로 나가 달라는 말을 들었을 때 나가는 것이 그녀에게는 자연스러운 일이었다. 그러나 아래층으로 내려가면서 부인은 자기가 옳았는지 아닌지 의심스러웠다.\n\n“크로프츠 선생님.” 둘만 남자마자 릴리가 말했다. “여기, 제 곁에 가까이 앉으세요. 여쭤볼 게 있어요. 요전 저녁에 응접실에서 언니와 단둘이 계실 때 언니에게 무슨 말씀을 하셨어요?”\n\n의사는 한참 대답 없이 앉아 있었고, 그를 가까이서 지켜보던 릴리는 난롯불 빛으로 그가 흠칫했음을—그 물음을 받고 거의 몸을 떨었음을 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n“내가 그분에게 무슨 말을 했느냐고요?” 그는 아주 낮은 목소리로 그녀의 말을 되뇌었다. “나를 사랑할 수 있겠느냐고, 내 아내가 되어 주겠느냐고 물었습니다.”\n\n“그래서 언니는 무슨 대답을 했어요?”\n\n“무슨 대답을 했느냐고요? 그냥 나를 거절했습니다.”\n\n“아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요. 언니 말을 믿지 마세요, 크로프츠 선생님. 그런 게 아니었어요—그런 게 아니었을 거예요. 다만 아시다시피, 언니에게서 나온 말이라고는 제가 아무것도 전해 드릴 수 없어요. 언니는 제게 제 속마음을 말한 적이 없으니까요. 하지만 선생님이 정말로 언니를 사랑하신다면, 언니가 선생님을 거절하는 건 미친 짓이에요.”\n\n“나는 그분을 사랑합니다, 릴리 양. 적어도 그건 사실이에요.”\n\n“그러면 다시 가 보세요. 지금은 제 이야기를 하는 거예요. 저는 선생님 같은 형부를 잃을 수는 없어요. 저는 선생님을 너무 아껴서 놓아 드릴 수가 없거든요. 그리고 언니도—언니도 선생님이 사랑받고 싶어 하시는 그대로 선생님을 사랑하는 법을 배우게 될 거예요. 선생님도 언니 성품을 아시잖아요, 얼마나 말이 없는지, 제 이야기 하는 걸 얼마나 꺼리는지. 언니가 제게 털어놓은 건 이것뿐이에요—선생님이 말을 걸어 오셨고 언니는 불시에 놀랐다는 것요. 저희에게 한 번 더 기회가 있을까요? 이런 걸 여쭙는 제가 얼마나 잘못인지 알아요. 하지만 그래도 진실이 가장 낫지 않겠어요?”\n\n“한 번 더 기회라!”\n\n“무슨 말씀이신지 알아요. 그리고 저는 언니가 선생님의 아내가 될 만하다고 생각해요. 정말이지 그렇게 생각해요. 그리고 그렇다면 언니는 아주 훌륭한 사람인 거죠. 제 얘기는 하지 않으실 거죠, 선생님?”\n\n“네, 아가씨 얘기는 하지 않겠습니다.”\n\n“그리고 다시 해 보실 거죠?”\n\n“네, 다시 해 보지요.”\n\n“하느님의 축복이 있기를, 나의 형부! 부디—부디 제 형부가 되어 주세요.” 그러고서 그가 다시 한 번 그녀에게 손을 내밀자 그녀는 그를 향해 고개를 들었고, 그는 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 이마에 입을 맞추었다. “엄마를 들여보내 주세요.” 그가 문을 나설 때 그녀가 한 마지막 말이었다.\n\n“인사말은 다 했니?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“네, 엄마.”\n\n“분별 있는 인사말이었기를 바란다.”\n\n“그랬기를 바라요, 엄마. 그런데 그것 때문에 어찌나 지쳤는지 이제 자야겠어요. 오늘 학교에 내려갔더라면 별로 도움이 못 되었을 것 같지 않으세요?”\n\n그러자 데일 부인은 딸이 무리해서 입었을지 모를 해를 어떻게든 되돌려 보려는 걱정에, 그 작별 인사말에 관해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n크로프츠 선생은 말을 타고 집으로 돌아가면서 사랑을 이룬 사내의 승리감이라고는 거의 누리지 못했다. “그 아가씨 말이 옳을지도 모르지.” 그는 혼자 중얼거렸다. “어쨌든 나는 다시 물어보겠어.” 그런데도 벨이 했던, 그리고 거듭했던 그 “아니오”라는 말이 여전히 그의 귓가에 모질고 결정적으로 울리고 있었다. 어떤 사내들에게는 ‘아니오’ 소리가 귓가에 우르르 쏟아져도 결코 참된 거절로 들리지 않지만, 또 어떤 사내들에게는 그 말이 아무리 나직이 속삭여진 것이라 해도 한번 발음되고 나면 지고의 심판대에서 내려온 돌이킬 수 없는 판결처럼 다가온다.\n\n# 제43장. 「망측해라, 망측해라!」\n\n독자 여러분 가운데 레이디 덤벨로와 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨의 연정을—아니, 연정은 아니다. 그 낱말은 하도 어울리지 않게 갖다 붙인 것이라 어느 독자의 기억도 일깨우지 못할 터이니—그 두 사람의 시시덕거림을 기억하는 이가 있을까? 코시 성에서 벌어졌던 그 시시덕거림은 그 흉악함이 낱낱이 세상의 눈앞에 드러났는데, 만일 세상의 눈이 그것으로 충격을 받았다면 그 눈은 참으로 쉽게도 충격을 받는 눈이라고 고백해 두어야겠다.\n\n그러나 세상의 눈은 정말로 충격을 받았고, 품행에 유난히 까다로운 사람들은 아주 이상한 말들을 했다. 드 코시 백작부인 본인도 참으로 이상한 말을 했으니, 고개를 절레절레 저으며 알쏭달쏭한 말을 흘리는 식이었다. 반면에 레이디 클랜디들럼은 훨씬 더 대놓고 말하며, 레이디 덤벨로가 오월이 되기 전에 달아나 버릴 것이라는 제 의견을 공언했다. 두 사람은 덤벨로 경이 아내를 잃는 것이 그에게 아주 나쁜 일만은 아니리라는 데 뜻을 같이했으나, 가엾은 플랜태저넷 팰리서 이야기가 나오자 몹시 슬프게 고개를 저었다. 그 부인의 신세로 말하자면—코시 성에 머물던 날들 내내 두 사람이 거의 떠받들다시피 했던 그 부인의 신세로 말하자면—그 일로는 도무지 마음을 쓰는 것 같지 않았다.\n\n그리고 팰리서 씨가 조금 경솔했다는 점은 인정해야 한다—경솔했다는 것은, 그가 떠도는 소문을 조금이라도 알고 있었다면 그렇다는 말이다. 코시 성 방문이 끝난 지 얼마 되지 않아 그는 슈롭셔에 있는 레이디 하틀탑의 저택으로 내려갔는데, 덤벨로 부부가 그곳에서 겨울을 날 참이었고, 그는 그곳을 떠나면서 이월에 다시 오겠다는 뜻을 밝혔던 것이다. 하틀탑 사람들이 그를 몹시 붙들었는데—그 붙듦이 유독 힘차게 나온 쪽이 다름 아닌 덤벨로 경이었다. 그러니 하틀탑 사람들은 적어도 그 소문을 듣지 못했다고 보는 것이 이치에 맞다.\n\n플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 크리스마스를 숙부인 옴니엄 공작과 함께 개서럼 성에서 보냈다. 그러니까 크리스마스 전날 저녁 식사 시각에 맞추어 성에 닿았고, 크리스마스 다음 날 아침에 그곳을 떠났다는 말이다. 이는 그의 생활에서 늘 있어 온 관례였고, 옴니엄 가에 매인 소작인이며 식솔이며 따르는 무리는 공작과 그 조카 사이에 건전한 영국식 가정의 정이 이렇게 드러나는 것을 언제나 기뻐하며 바라보았다. 그러나 그런 자리에서 두 사람이 주고받는 말의 양은 대체로 대수롭지 않았다. 공작은 조카에게 오른손을 내밀며 미소를 띠고 이렇게 말하곤 했다—\n\n“그래, 플랜태저넷—무척 바쁘겠지?”\n\n그를 면전에 두고 플랜태저넷이라 부르는 사람은 세상에 공작 하나뿐이었다. 뒤에서 플랜티 팰이라 입에 올리는 자는 수십 명이나 되었지만 말이다. 여태껏 그를 그렇게 부른 살아 있는 사람은 공작 하나뿐이었다. 지금도 여전히 그러하기를, 그리고 그 성질이 위태롭고 그 사정이 온당치 못한 여자 쪽의 예외 따위는 생겨나지 않았기를 바라 두자.\n\n“그래, 플랜태저넷.” 이번에도 공작이 말했다. “무척 바쁘겠지?”\n\n“예, 그렇습니다, 공작 어른.” 팰리서 씨가 말했다. “사람이 한번 멍에를 지고 나면 좀처럼 벗어 버리기 어렵지요.”\n\n공작은 조카가 지난 크리스마스 방문 때도 거의 똑같은 말을 했던 것을 떠올렸다.\n\n“그건 그렇고,” 공작이 말했다. “자네가 가기 전에 한두 마디 할 말이 있네.”\n\n공작 쪽에서 그런 말을 꺼낸 것은 여느 때의 관례에서 크게 벗어난 일이었으나, 조카는 물론 숙부의 분부를 따르겠노라 했다.\n\n“내일 저녁 식사 전에 뵙겠습니다.” 플랜태저넷이 말했다.\n\n“아, 그러게.” 공작이 말했다. “오 분도 붙들지 않겠네.” 그리하여 이튿날 오후 여섯 시에 두 사람은 공작의 사실(私室)에 단둘이 들어앉았다.\n\n“그리 대단한 일은 아닐 걸세.” 공작이 말을 꺼냈다. “허나 사람들이 자네와 레이디 덤벨로를 두고 말들을 하고 있네.”\n\n“저런, 사람들이 참 친절하기도 하군요.” 그러면서 팰리서 씨는 한 가지 사실을 떠올렸다—그것은 분명 사실이었으니, 사람들이 여러 해 동안 제 숙부와 레이디 덤벨로의 시어머니를 두고 말들을 해 왔다는 사실이었다.\n\n“그래, 친절하고말고. 그렇지 않은가? 자네는 하틀베리에서 막 왔다지.” 하틀베리는 슈롭셔에 있는 하틀탑 후작의 저택이었다.\n\n“예, 그렇습니다. 그리고 이월에 또 갈 참입니다.”\n\n“아, 그건 안됐군. 물론 자네 일정에 참견하겠다는 뜻은 아닐세. 내가 그런 짓을 자주 하지 않았다는 것은 자네도 인정하겠지, 무슨 일에서든 말이야.”\n\n“예, 그러신 적 없습니다.” 조카가 그렇게 말하면서, 숙부 쪽에서 그런 참견을 하려 한들 될 리가 없었으리라는 속내의 확신으로 스스로를 달랬다.\n\n“허나 이번 일만은 내게도 좋고, 실은 자네에게도 좋을 성싶네. 자네가 하틀베리에 되도록 적게 머무는 것이 말이야. 자네가 가겠다고 말했으니 물론 가야겠지. 허나 내가 자네라면 하루 이틀 넘게는 머물지 않겠네.”\n\n플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 이 세상에서 가진 모든 것을 숙부에게서 받았다. 숙부의 세력 덕분에 의회에 의석을 두었고, 숙부가 내일이라도 말 한마디로 끊어 버릴 수 있는 연 몇천 파운드의 생활비를 받았다. 그는 숙부의 상속인이었고, 공작위와 한정상속으로 묶인 얼마간의 재산은 숙부가 혼인해 아들을 두지 않는 한 결국 그에게 떨어질 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 공작의 재산 가운데 훨씬 큰 몫은 한정상속에 묶여 있지 않았고, 공작은 앞으로 스무 해 아니 그 이상도 살 법했으며, 비위가 상하면 혼인해 아비가 되는 일도 얼마든지 있을 수 있었다. 플랜태저넷 팰리서가 제 숙부에게 매여 있는 만큼 남에게 매여 있는 사람은 달리 없다고 해도 좋을 것이다. 그리고 상속인을 이만큼 적게 간섭한 아버지나 숙부도 달리 없다고 해도 좋을 것이다. 그럼에도 조카는 제 사생활에 대한 이 언급에 당장 마음이 상했고, 그런 감시 따위에는 따르지 않겠다고 그 자리에서 마음을 정했다.\n\n“얼마나 머무를지는 저도 모르겠습니다.” 그가 말했다. “허나 제 방문이 그런 소문 때문에 이러쿵저러쿵 좌우되리라고는 말씀드릴 수 없군요.”\n\n“그래, 그렇지는 않겠지. 허나 어쩌면 내 부탁 때문에는 좌우될지도 모르지.” 그러면서 공작은 말하는 동안 조금 사나운 낯빛이 되었다.\n\n“아무 근거도 없는 소문을 마음에 두라고 하지는 않으시겠지요.”\n\n“나는 그 근거를 묻고 있는 게 아닐세. 자네 사는 방식에 끼어들 생각도 눈곱만큼 없고.” 이 마지막 말로 공작은, 다른 신사의 아내라면 누구를 데려가든 자네 마음이지만 덤벨로 경의 아내를 데려가고 싶어 한다는 억측을 살 자유만은 없다는 뜻을 조카에게 알린 것이었다. “실은 이런 일일세, 플랜태저넷. 나는 여러 해 그 집안과 가깝게 지내 왔네. 나는 가깝게 지내는 사람이 많지 않고, 아마 앞으로도 늘리지 않을 걸세. 벗이 있으면 그대로 두고 싶단 말이야. 그러니 내가 말한 그런 소문이 돌면 내가 하틀베리에 가기도, 하틀베리 사람들이 이리 오기도 거북해지리라는 것쯤은 자네도 쉽게 알아보겠지.” 공작이 이보다 더 노골적으로 말할 수는 없었을 터이고, 팰리서 씨도 그를 낱낱이 알아들었다. 두 집안 사이의 그런 결연이 둘씩이나 나란히 순조롭게 굴러가리라고는 기대할 수 없는 노릇이었고, 그런 두 번째 결연은 소문만으로도 앞서의 결연이 지닌 즐거움을 해칠 수 있었다.\n\n“그게 다일세.” 공작이 말했다.\n\n“참으로 터무니없는 비방입니다.” 팰리서 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그렇겠지. 그런 비방이란 늘 터무니없는 법이야. 허나 우리가 무얼 할 수 있겠나? 사람들의 혀를 붙들어 맬 수는 없지 않은가.” 그러면서 공작은 이 이야기가 이제 끝난 것으로 여겨져 혼자 있게 되기를 바라는 낯빛이었다.\n\n“허나 무시할 수는 있지요.” 조카가 눈치 없이 말했다.\n\n“자네는 그럴 수 있겠지. 나는 여태 그러지 못했네. 그러면서도 나는 남들의 입방아에 휘둘리는 사람이라는 평판을 얻지는 않았다고 믿네. 자네는 내가 자네에게 큰 것을 요구한다고 여기겠지. 허나 여태 나는 많이 주기만 하고 아무것도 요구한 적이 없다는 것을 잊지 말게. 이 일에서만은 자네가 내 뜻을 들어 주기를 바라네.”\n\n그리하여 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 자기가 협박을 받았음을 알고 방을 나섰다. 공작이 한 말은 이런 뜻이나 다름없었다.—네가 계속 레이디 덤벨로 꽁무니를 따라다닌다면, 내가 주는 연 칠천 파운드를 끊겠다. 다음 실버브리지 선거에서 네가 다시 뽑히는 것을 막겠다. 그리고 유언장을 만들어 매칭과 더 혼스—서리에 있는 아름다운 작은 별장으로, 팰리서 씨가 혼인하게 되면 쓰라고 이미 내주기로 되어 있던 곳이다—와 요크셔의 리틀베리 영지 전부와 저 어마어마한 스코틀랜드 재산을 네게서 떼어 놓겠다. 내 개인 재물과 대부며 주식이며 공채에 넣어 둔 돈에서는 한 실링도, 한 실링어치도 네가 손대지 못하게 하겠다. 그리고 내 짝을 찾을 수 있겠다 싶으면, 너를 그냥 밋밋한 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨로 남겨 두고 어린 사촌 하나를 네 집안의 어른으로 앉힐지도 모른다.\n\n이 협박이 어디까지 미치는지를 팰리서 씨는 낱낱이 알아들었다. 그리고 그것을 곱씹으면서, 자기가 레이디 덤벨로에게 사랑 비슷한 것조차 느껴 본 적이 없음을 스스로 인정했다. 두 사람 사이에 오간 대화 가운데 독자가 이미 본보기로 본 그것보다 더 뜨거운 것은 없었다. 레이디 덤벨로는 그에게 아무것도 아니었다. 그러나 이제—일이 이런 식으로 눈앞에 놓이고 보니, 이미 제 이름과 나란히 엮여 버린 저토록 아름다운 여인에게 사랑을 품는 것이 신사로서 어울리는 일이 아니겠는가? 고해실에서 던진 질문으로 여관 마부에게 말 이빨에 기름칠하는 법을 가르쳐 준 신부 이야기를 우리는 모두 알고 있다. “여태 그런 짓은 해 본 적이 없습니다요.” 여관 마부가 말했다. “그렇지만 한번 해 보지요.” 이번 일에서 공작은 그 신부 노릇을 한 셈이었고, 팰리서 씨는 밤이 다 가기 전에 그 여관 마부만큼이나 고분고분한 제자가 되어 있었다. 협박으로 말하자면, 팰리서 집안 사람이자 플랜태저넷인 그가 그런 것에 마음을 쓰는 일은 어울리지 않았다. 공작은 혼인하지 않을 것이다. 세상 누구보다도 제 얼굴에 침 뱉자고 제 코를 베어 낼 사람이 아니었다. 그 나머지는 팰리서 씨도 운에 맡길 참이었다. 그리하여 그는 이월 초에 하틀베리로 내려갔으니, 레이디 덤벨로에게 각별히 마음을 쓰기로 단단히 작정한 터였다.\n\n하틀베리에 그득한 손님들 가운데서 그는 폴록 경을 만났다. 야위고 병약하고 지쳐 빠져 보이는 사내로, 눈매에는 제 아비의 모진 데가 얼마쯤 있었으나 입가에는 아비의 사나움이라고는 없었다.\n\n“누이가 혼인한다지요?” 팰리서 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그렇소. 그 아비가 자식들을 어떻게 몰아세우는지 떠올려 보면, 그 애들이 무슨 짓을 하든 놀랄 것도 없지요.”\n\n“축하드리려던 참이었소.”\n\n“그러지 마시오.”\n\n“나는 코시에서 그 사람을 만나 보았는데 꽤 마음에 들었소.”\n\n팰리서 씨는 코시에서 크로즈비 씨와 겨우 몇 마디 나누었을 뿐이었으나, 하기야 그는 여느 사교 자리에서도 누구와든 겨우 몇 마디 나누는 것 이상은 좀처럼 하지 않는 사람이었다.\n\n“그랬소?” 폴록 경이 말했다. “가엾은 누이를 봐서라도 그자가 불한당은 아니기를 바라오. 어떤 사내가 우리 아버지에게 그 집 딸을 아내로 달라 청할 마음을 먹는지, 나로서는 도무지 이해할 수가 없소. 우리 어머니는 어떻게 보이시던가요?”\n\n“딱히 언짢은 데는 못 보았소.”\n\n“언젠가는 그 양반이 어머니를 죽이고 말 거요.” 그러고서 그 대화는 끝이 났다.\n\n팰리서 씨 자신도 알아차렸다—그녀를 바라보고 있자니 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다—자기가 다가가면 레이디 덤벨로에게 얼마간의 사교적 활기가 도는 듯하다는 것을. 그녀는 말을 걸어오면 미소를 짓는 버릇이 있었으나, 그 여느 미소는 아무 뜻이 없고 납덩이처럼 무거웠으며, 미소를 받은 사람을 조금이라도 추어올리는 법이 결코 없었다. 아주 많은 여자들이 저에게 건네진 말에 대답하면서 미소를 짓고, 그렇게 하는 이들은 대개 그 미소로 상대를 추어올린다. 그것은 하도 흔한 일이라 아무도 눈여겨보지 않는다. 추어올림은 기분을 좋게 하지만 아무 뜻도 없다. 저도 모르게 받아들이는 인상이란 그저, 그 여자가 제 도리대로 상냥하게 굴었다는 느낌—그 미소가 미치는 만큼, 그러니까 아주 미미한 정도로 상냥했다는 느낌뿐이다. 그러나 그렇게 함으로써 그 여자는 사교계에 제 몫의 작은 이바지를 한 것이다. 그 여자는 같은 저녁에 같은 이바지를 백 번도 할 것이다. 그 여자가 누구를 추어올렸는지는 아무도 알지 못한다. 그 여자 자신도 알지 못한다. 그리고 세상은 그 여자를 상냥한 여자라 부른다. 그러나 레이디 덤벨로는 제 여느 미소에 추어올림을 담지 않았다. 그 미소들은 차갑고 뜻이 없었으며, 각별한 눈길이 따라붙지도 않았고, 어느 한 사람을 향하는 일도 드물었다. 그것들은 방 전체에 주어졌고, 방 전체는 그녀의 대단한 위세를 인정하며 그것으로 넉넉하다고 받아들였다. 그러나 팰리서 씨가 가까이 오면 그녀는 앉은 자리에서 몸을 조금, 아주 조금 틀었고, 잠깐 동안 그의 얼굴에 눈길이 머물도록 두었다. 그러다가 그가 날이 꽤 춥다고 하면, 그녀는 그 말이 옳다고 받으면서 정말로 그를 향해 미소를 지었다. 이 모든 것을 팰리서 씨는 눈여겨보는 법을 스스로 익혔으니, 말 이빨에 기름칠하는 법에 관한 그 가르침을 어리석은 숙부에게서 배운 뒤의 일이었다.\n\n그러나 그럼에도 하틀베리에 머문 첫 주일 동안 그가 그녀에게 한 말 가운데 날씨 이야기보다 더 다정한 것은 하나도 없었다. 그가 몹시 바빴던 것은 사실이다. 그는 답신 연설을 맡기로 되어 있었는데, 이제 곧 의회가 열릴 참이었고 그 연설은 통계에 바탕을 둔 것이었으므로 그는 숫자와 서류에 파묻혀 있었다. 오가는 편지도 밀려 있어서 하루가 그의 볼일에 넉넉한 적이 드물었다. 그는 자기가 바라는 그 가까움이 고된 일과에 가로막혀 있다고 느꼈다. 그럼에도 하틀베리를 떠나기 전에 무언가를 해서 제 마음이 각별하다는 것을 보이려 했다. 그녀에게 무슨 말인가를 해서, 무엇의 비밀을—그의 마음의 비밀이라고 해 두어야 할까?—그녀 앞에 열어 보이려 했다. 날마다 그것이 그의 결심이었다. 그러나 하루하루가 지나가도 아무 말도 나오지 않았다. 덤벨로 경이 전보다 어쩐지 덜 다정한 듯싶었고, 앞을 가로막고 서서 험한 낯을 하는 것 같기도 했다. 그러나 그는 덤벨로 경의 낯빛 따위는 아무래도 좋다고 스스로 다짐했다.\n\n“도시에는 언제 가시오?” 어느 날 저녁 그가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n“아마 사월에요. 그전에는 하틀베리를 떠나지 않을 게 분명해요.”\n\n“아, 그렇겠군요. 사냥 철 때문에 머무르시는군요.”\n\n“예. 덤벨로 경은 삼월 내내 늘 여기 머물러요. 하루 이틀쯤 도시에 다녀올지는 모르지만요.”\n\n“참 편안하시겠소! 나는 목요일에는 런던에 가 있어야 하오, 아시겠지만.”\n\n“의회가 열리니까요?”\n\n“바로 그렇소. 여간 성가신 일이 아니지만, 해야 하는 일이니.”\n\n“사람이 그것을 업으로 삼았다면 해야겠지요.”\n\n“아이고, 그럼요. 꼼짝없이 해야 하는 일이오.” 그러고서 팰리서 씨는 방 안을 둘러보다가 덤벨로 경의 눈이 저에게 박혀 있는 것을 본 듯싶었다. 정말이지 여간 힘든 일이 아니었다. 사실대로 말하자면 그는 어떻게 시작해야 할지를 몰랐다. 그녀에게 무슨 말을 해야 하나? 끝에 가서 다정해질 대화를 어떻게 꺼내야 하나? 그녀는 분명 아주 아름다웠고, 그의 눈에는 마음을 끄는 데가 있어 보일 수도 있었다. 그러나 목숨이 걸렸다 해도 그는 그녀에게 각별한 말을 어떻게 꺼내야 할지 알지 못했다. 레이디 덤벨로 같은 여인과의 정분—플라토닉하고 죄 없으면서도 아주 가까운 사이—이라면 지금처럼 메마른 그의 삶에 우아함을 얼마쯤 더해 줄 것이 틀림없었다. 그는 그 부인이 마음이 있다고 들었다—숙부를 거쳐 그에게 닿은 세상의 소문으로 들었다. 그녀는 분명 그를 좋아하는 듯 보였다. 그런데 어떻게 시작한단 말인가? 통계의 방대한 정확함으로 하원을 깜짝 놀라게 하고 영국 백성을 겁주는 재주는 이미 익혔다. 그러나 어여쁜 여인에게는 무슨 말을 해야 하는가?\n\n“사월에는 꼭 런던에 계시겠지요?”\n\n이것은 또 다른 자리에서였다.\n\n“예, 그럴 것 같아요.”\n\n“칼턴 가든스겠지요.”\n\n“예. 덤벨로 경이 이제 그 집을 세내었어요.”\n\n“아, 정말이오? 아, 아주 좋은 집이지요. 나도 이따금 찾아뵐 수 있으면 좋겠소.”\n\n“물론이지요—다만 그쪽이 몹시 바쁘신 줄은 알아요.”\n\n“토요일과 일요일에는 그렇지 않소.”\n\n“저는 일요일마다 손님을 받아요.” 레이디 덤벨로가 말했다. 팰리서 씨는 여기에 각별히 살가운 데라고는 없다고 느꼈다. 아는 사람들이 죄다 와 있을 때 찾아와도 좋다는 허락이란 대단할 것이 없었다. 그래도 그 자리에서 얻어 낼 수 있는 것으로는 그만하면 다인 듯싶었다. 그가 고개를 드니 덤벨로 경의 눈이 또 저에게 박혀 있었고 덤벨로 경의 이마가 어두웠다. 그는 사람들이 죄다 한데 부대끼며 지내는 시골 저택이 이런 술수를 부리기에 세상에서 가장 좋은 자리인지 의심스러워지기 시작했다. 레이디 덤벨로는 아주 아름다웠고 그는 그녀를 바라보는 것이 좋았다. 그러나 하틀베리의 그 응접실에서는 그녀의 마음을 끌 만한 이야깃거리를 도무지 찾을 수 없었다. 그날 저녁 늦게 그는 설탕 관세에 관해 무언가를 그녀에게 말하고 있는 자신을 발견했고, 그제야 그만두는 편이 낫겠다는 것을 알았다. 남은 날은 하루뿐이었고 그날은 연설을 위해 아무래도 필요했다. 런던에서라면 일이 훨씬 수월할 터이니 그때까지 미루기로 했다. 런던의 붐비는 방들에서는 남모르는 이야기를 나누기가 훨씬 쉬울 것이고, 덤벨로 경이 곁에 버티고 서서 저를 노려보지도 않을 것이었다. 레이디 덤벨로는 설탕에 관한 그의 말을 아주 고맙게 받아들였고, 종가세(從價稅)가 무엇인지 풀이해 달라고까지 했다. 그것은 그가 여태 해 본 어떤 것보다도 진짜 대화에 가까이 다가간 것이었다. 그러나 이야깃거리가 운이 없었고 그의 손에서는 도무지 다정한 쪽으로 돌려세울 수가 없었다. 그리하여 그는 런던의 봄이 이 일을 쉽게 만들어 줄 때까지 제 구애를 미루기로 마음먹었고, 그렇게 하면서 한동안은 무거운 짐을 벗었다고 느꼈다.\n\n“안녕히 계시오, 레이디 덤벨로.” 이튿날 저녁 그가 말했다. “나는 내일 아침 일찍 떠나오.”\n\n“안녕히 가세요, 팰리서 씨.”\n\n그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 더없이 곱게 미소 지었으나, 그를 플랜태저넷이라 부르는 법은 아직 익히지 못한 것이 분명했다. 그는 런던으로 올라가 곧바로 일에 매달렸다. 정확하고 방대한 그 연설은 그에게 적잖은 명예를 안기며 무사히 끝났다—그런 사람들에게 주어지는, 저 조용하고 오래가는 종류의 명예였다. 연설은 점잖고 지루하고 정확했다. 사람들은 그것을 듣거나, 모자로 눈을 덮은 채 앉아 자면서 듣는 척했다. 이튿날 아침 《데일리 주피터》에는 그 연설에 관한 사설이 실렸으나, 다 읽고 나서도 독자는 팰리서 씨를 대단한 재정 전문가로 보아야 할지 어떨지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 팰리서 씨는 돈의 세계에 빛나는 등불이 되고 은행업의 영광이 될지도 몰랐다. 앞날의 재무장관이 될지도 몰랐다. 그러나 다시 또, 이런 일에서 그가 한낱 도깨비불이자 눈먼 길잡이—아주 점잖되 깊이라고는 없어서 옆으로 치워 두어야 할 사람—으로 판명될지도 몰랐다. 그러니 지금 이 시점에 누가 제 신용을 걸고 팰리서 씨가 제 주제를 아는지 모르는지를 분별 있게 단언할 수 있겠는가? 우리는 땅과 사람의 지혜가 줄 수 있는 모든 소식을 신문에서 찾는 것으로 만족하지 않는다. 우리는 저승에서 날마다 한 장씩 소식지가 온다면 요구했을 법한 것을 신문에 요구한다. 그 결과란 물론 이렇다—신문들은 정말로 저승에서 날마다 온 것인 양 굴지만, 그 신탁이란 옛날의 신탁이 그러했듯 몹시 미덥지 못하다는 것이다.\n\n플랜태저넷 팰리서는 그 사설에 만족하면서도, 올버니의 제 거처에 앉아 제 행복에 무언가 다른 것이 모자란다고 느꼈다. 이런 삶도 다 좋기는 했다. 야망은 훌륭한 것이었고, 팰리서 집안 사람이자 앞날의 귀족인 그가 정치를 업으로 삼는 것은 어울리는 일이었다. 그러나 그늘 아래 아마릴리스를 위해 한두 시간쯤 낼 수는 없단 말인가? 이 삶은 너무 고되지 않은가? 레이디 덤벨로가 저에게 미소를 보낸다는 말을 들은 뒤로 그는 분명 그 미소를 두고 제 통계에 이롭지 않을 만큼 생각이 많아졌다. 몸속에 새로운 핏줄 하나가 열려 여태 피가 흐른 적 없는 곳에 피가 흐르는 것만 같았다. 덤벨로가 그녀를 보기 전에 그가 레이디 덤벨로를 보았더라면 그녀와 혼인할 수 있었을까? 아! 그랬더라면, 그러니까 그녀가 그저 그랜틀리 양이거나 형편에 따라 레이디 그리젤다 그랜틀리였더라면, 그녀에게 말을 붙이기가 한결 수월했으리라고 그는 생각했다. 사정이 이러하니 시골에서는 그 일이 여간 어렵지 않았다. 제 계급 사람들이 평생 그런 짓을 한다는 이야기는 익히 들어 왔건만 말이다. 나로 말하자면, 죄인이라 불리는 사람이 실제 죄인보다 훨씬 많다고 믿는다.\n\n그가 그렇게 앉아 있는데 포더길 씨라는 사람이 찾아 들어왔다. 포더길 씨는 그의 숙부의 여느 일들을 거의 도맡아 처리하는 신사로, 제 빵 어느 쪽에 버터가 발려 있는지 아는 영리한 사람이었다. 포더길 씨는 당연히 상속인에게 잘 보이고 싶어 했다. 그러나 주인에게 잘 보이는 것이 그의 밥벌이였고, 그 밥벌이에 무엇이든 끼어들도록 두는 법이 없었다. 이번에 포더길 씨는 아주 공손하여, 앞으로 제 후원자가 될지도 모를 이에게 그 힘찬 연설을 두고 치사를 늘어놓았고, 저승에서 왔거나 오지 않았거나 한 신문들보다 훨씬 더 자신 있게 그의 정치적 앞길을 점쳤다. 포더길 씨는 볼일 한두 가지에 관해 한마디 하러 들른 것이었다. 팰리서 씨의 돈은 모두 포더길 씨의 손을 거쳤고 그의 선거 관련 일도 포더길 씨가 챙겼으므로, 포더길 씨는 꼭 해야 할 말 한두 마디를 하러 곧잘 찾아오곤 했다. 그 일이 끝나면 그는 또 다른 말 한두 마디를 했는데, 그것은 경우에 따라 꼭 해야 할 말일 수도 아닐 수도 있었다.\n\n“팰리서 씨,” 그가 말했다. “혼인할 생각은 안 하시는지 궁금합니다. 무례를 용서해 주시기 바랍니다.”\n\n팰리서 씨는 그를 용서해 줄 마음이 도무지 들지 않았고, 위엄이 상했다는 첫 낌새를 내보이려는 뜻으로 의자에 갑자기 몸을 꼿꼿이 세웠다. 그러나 참으로 묘하게도, 그 자신이 바로 그 순간 혼인을 생각하고 있던 참이었다. 덤벨로 가와의 혼담이 짜이기 전에 그 아름다운 그리젤다를 알았더라면 어찌 되었을까? 그녀와 혼인했을까? 그녀와 혼인했다면 마음이 편했을까? 물론 이제는 혼인할 수 없었다. 그는 레이디 덤벨로에게 마음을 두고 있고, 딱하게도 그 부인에게는 제 남편이 있으니 말이다. 그러나 혼인을 생각하고 있었다고는 해도, 그 이야기가 숙부의 대리인 손에 이렇게 거칠게 제 눈앞으로, 아니 제 무릎 위로 던져지는 것은 달갑지 않았다. 포더길 씨가 위엄이 상했다는 그 첫 낌새를 알아보지 못했을 리 없다. 그는 영리하고 눈치 빠른 사람이었으니까. 그러나 어쩌면 그는 그것을 실은 그다지 마음에 두지 않았는지도 모른다. 어쩌면 그는 무엇보다도 지켜야 할 분부를 받고 온 것인지도 모른다.\n\n“용서해 주시기 바랍니다, 팰리서 씨, 정말이지 그렇습니다. 그렇지만 이런 말씀을 드리는 것은, 팰리서 씨와 공작 각하 사이에 무슨—무슨—무슨 좋은 정이 얼마쯤 식어 가는 것이나 아닌가 하여 반쯤 겁이 나서입니다. 그렇게 불러 두는 편이 낫겠지요. 그런 일이라면 그야말로 끔찍한 일이 될 테니까요.”\n\n“나는 그럴 낌새를 전혀 모르오.”\n\n팰리서 씨는 이 말을 자못 위엄 있게 했다. 그러나 말이 입에서 나오고 나자 자기가 거짓말을 한 것이 아닌가 하는 생각이 들었다.\n\n“예, 아마 없겠지요. 그런 낌새가 없기를 바랍니다. 그렇지만 공작 각하께서는 무슨 일이든 한번 마음에 담으시면 여간 고집스러운 분이 아니십니다—게다가 그분 손아귀에 든 것이 그리도 많고요.”\n\n“그분 손아귀에 내가 든 것은 아니오, 포더길 씨.”\n\n“예, 예, 예. 이 나라에서 사람이 사람을 손아귀에 두는 일은 없지요—그런 식으로는 말입니다. 그렇지만 아시다시피, 팰리서 씨, 그분의 비위를 거스르는 것은 좋을 게 없지 않겠습니까?”\n\n“거스르지 않는 편이 낫겠소, 그야 당연하오. 실은 나는 누구의 비위도 거스르고 싶지 않소.”\n\n“바로 그렇습니다. 그리고 무엇보다도 공작 각하는 안 되지요. 재산 전부를 손에 쥐고 계신 분이니까요. 전부라고 해도 좋습니다. 마음만 내키시면 내일이라도 혼인하실 수 있으니 말입니다. 게다가 그분은 몸이 참 좋으십니다. 그 연세에 그만큼 정정한 분을 저는 어디서도 본 적이 없습니다.”\n\n“그거 참 반가운 이야기요.”\n\n“그러시고말고요, 팰리서 씨. 그렇지만 그분이 언짢아하시기라도 하면 말입니다?”\n\n“나는 참고 견디겠소.”\n\n“예, 그렇지요. 그렇게 하실 겁니다. 그렇지만 그런 일은 피해 두는 편이 값어치가 있지요. 그분 손아귀에 든 것이 그토록 많으니 말입니다.”\n\n“공작께서 지금 그대를 내게 보내신 것이오, 포더길 씨?”\n\n“아니요, 아니요, 아니요—그런 것은 조금도 아닙니다. 그렇지만 얼마 전에 그분이 무심코 흘리신 말씀이 있어서, 저는 그분이 팰리서 씨 일로 아주—아주—아주 마음이 편치는 않으신 모양이라고 짐작했습니다. 그분이 이 재산에 상속인이 태어나는 것을 보고 싶어 하신다는 것은 저도 오래전부터 알고 있었지요. 며칠 전 아침에는—거기에 무슨 뜻이 있었는지는 모르겠습니다만—지금의 조처에 무언가 손을 대려 하시는 듯한 느낌이 들었습니다. 하시지는 않았고, 제 느낌뿐이었을 수도 있고요. 다만 생각해 보십시오, 팰리서 씨, 그분의 말 한마디가 무슨 일을 할 수 있는지! 한번 말씀을 뱉으시면 결코 물리는 법이 없는 분입니다.” 그러고서 그만큼 말한 뒤에 포더길 씨는 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n팰리서 씨는 이 모든 말의 뜻을 아주 잘 알아들었다. 포더길 씨가 그에게 충고를 한 것은—포더길 씨 자신에게는 할 권리가 없는 그런 충고를 한 것은 이번이 처음이 아니었다. 그는 그런 충고를 늘 위엄이 반쯤 상한 낯빛으로 받았으니, 그로써 포더길 씨에게 주제넘은 짓이라는 것을 알리려는 뜻이었다. 그러나 그 충고가 어디서 나온 것인지는 잘 알고 있었다. 그리고 그런 경우마다 그런 충고를 따르지 않겠다고 마음을 정하곤 했으면서도, 어찌 된 셈인지 팰리서 씨의 처신은 대체로 포더길 씨의 충고에 얼추 들어맞곤 했다. 공작의 말 한마디는 참으로 많은 일을 할 수 있었다! 팰리서 씨는 레이디 덤벨로의 일에서만은 제 뜻대로 하리라 마음먹었다. 그러나 그럼에도 공작의 말 한마디가 참으로 많은 일을 할 수 있다는 것은 틀림없는 사실이었다!\n\n그 속내를 아는 우리는 팰리서 씨가 하틀베리를 떠나기까지 그 죄스러운 연정을 어디까지 밀고 나갔는지 알고 있다. 그만큼 잘 알지 못하는 다른 이들은 그가 훨씬 더 멀리까지 성공을 거두었다고 여겼다. 레이디 클랜디들럼은 팰리서 씨가 떠난 직후에 드 코시 백작부인에게 쓴 편지에서, 그 신사가 새벽같이 떠날 참이라는 말을 듣고 아침 식탁에서 레이디 덤벨로가 그와 함께 달아났다는 소식을 듣게 되리라 굳게 믿었노라고 밝혔다. 그 부인의 말투로 보건대, 레이디 덤벨로가 남편의 조상들이 살던 집에 그대로 눌러앉는 바람에 그녀는 기대하던 즐거움을 빼앗긴 것만 같았다. “그렇지만 나는 아주 확신해요.” 레이디 클랜디들럼은 이렇게 썼다. “이 일이 봄을 넘길 수는 없다고 말이에요. 나는 팰리서 씨처럼 홀린 남자를 여태 본 적이 없어요. 그이는 여기 있는 동안 한순간도 그 여자 곁을 떠나지 않았답니다. 레이디 하틀탑 말고는 누구도 그런 짓을 두고 보지 않았을 거예요. 하지만 아시다시피, 오래된 좋은 집안끼리의 우정만큼 즐거운 것은 없지요.”\n\n# 제44장. 앨링턴의 밸런타인 날\n\n릴리는 앓아눕기 전에 어머니에게서 약속 하나를 받아 냈고, 회복기 동안 그것을 자주 들먹이며 그런 약속을 했으니 반드시 지켜야 한다고 어머니에게 일깨웠다. 크로즈비가 혼인하는 날을 릴리에게 알려 주기로 한 약속이었다. 혼례가 이월에 있으리라는 것은 그들 모두가 알게 된 바였다. 그러나 릴리에게는 그것으로 충분하지 않았다. 그녀는 그 날짜를 알아야만 했다.\n\n그리고 그날이 가까워질수록—그사이 릴리는 차츰 기운을 되찾아 의사의 권위에 매인 몸에서 벗어나고 있었다—크로즈비와 알렉산드리나의 혼인은 작은 집에서 훨씬 더 자주 입에 오르내렸다. 그것은 데일 부인이나 벨이 골랐을 법한 이야깃거리가 아니었으나, 릴리가 자꾸 그 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그녀는 으레 거의 익살스러운 투로 말문을 열어, 자기를 이야기책 속의 버림받은 아가씨에 빗대곤 했다. 그러고는 그의 앞날이 자기에게 여전히 대단히 중대한 일이기라도 한 듯 그것을 이야기하기 시작했다. 그러나 그런 이야기를 하는 도중에 그녀는 너무나 자주 무너져 내려, 슬픈 한마디나 서글픈 말투로 제 마음에 얹힌 짐이 얼마나 무거운지를 드러내 보였다. 데일 부인과 벨은 기꺼이 그 이야기를 피했을 테지만, 릴리가 피하도록 놓아두지 않았다. 그들에게는 그녀가 듣는 데서 그 이야기를 하기가 여간 어려운 일이 아니었다. 크로즈비를 헐뜯는 말은 한마디도 허락되지 않았으니—그들 생각에는 어떤 비난의 말로도 그를 넉넉히 나무랄 수 없을 듯싶었는데도 그러했다. 그러면서 그들은 릴리가 지어내기로 마음먹은 그의 행실에 대한 온갖 변명을 듣고 있어야 했고, 그 변명이 얼마나 부질없는지는 감히 짚어 낼 수 없었다.\n\n참으로 그 무렵 릴리는 작은 집에서 여왕처럼 군림했다. 몹쓸 대접과 병이 한꺼번에 그녀의 손에 들어와 그런 권세를 주었기에, 다른 여자들 가운데 누구도 그것을 거스를 수 없었다. 그에 대해 아무 말도 오가지 않았지만, 제인과 요리사까지 포함해 그들 모두는 당분간 릴리가 으뜸이라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그녀는 다정하고 너그럽고 사랑스럽고 용감한 여왕이었으므로 아무도 반역할 마음이 없었다—다만 그 크로즈비 칭찬만은 신하들의 귀에 몹시도 쓰라렸다. 날짜는 머지않아 정해졌고, 그 소식이 앨링턴까지 내려왔다. 이월 십사일에 크로즈비는 행복한 사내가 될 참이었다. 이것을 데일네 사람들이 알게 된 것은 십이일이 되어서였고, 그때라도 그 소식을 릴리에게서 아낄 수만 있었다면 그들은 기꺼이 아꼈을 것이다. 그러나 그럴 수는 없었고, 그날 저녁에 릴리는 그 이야기를 들었다.\n\n그 무렵 벨은 날마다 삼촌을 만나러 갔다. 릴리의 병세를 알려 드린다는 구실을 붙인 걸음이었으나, 어쩌면 그 밑바닥에는 이런 마음이 깔려 있었을 것이다. 식구들이 삼월 말에 작은 집을 떠날 작정이니, 자기들 마음속에 삼촌에 대한 원한 같은 것은 없다는 것을 지주에게 알려 두는 편이 좋겠다는 마음이었다. 이사에 대해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았다—그러니까 그들 쪽에서 그에게 한 말은 없었다. 그러나 일은 진행되고 있었고 그도 그것을 알았다. 크로프츠 선생이 벌써 그들을 대신해 게스트윅의 가구 딸린 작은 집 하나를 놓고 흥정을 하고 있었다. 지주는 그 일로 몹시 서글펐다—참으로 몹시 서글펐다. 홉킨스가 그 이야기를 꺼내자 그는 그 충직한 정원사에게 입을 다물라고 매섭게 일렀으니, 그런 일은 공식으로 알려지기 전에는 앨링턴에 딸린 사람들이 입에 올릴 거리가 아니라는 뜻이었다. 벨이 찾아온 동안에는 그 문제를 한 번도 입에 담지 않았다. 벨은 사촌과 혼인하기를 마다했을 뿐 아니라 그 일에 대한 사리에 맞는 충고에 귀 기울이기조차 거절했으니, 가장 큰 죄인이었다. 그러나 지주는 그 이야기를 그녀와 따질 수 없다고 느꼈다. 자기가 참견하는 것은 허락되지 않는다는 말을 데일 부인에게서 콕 집어 들은 터였기 때문이다. 그리고 또 어쩌면 그는, 벨과 그 이야기를 따져 본들 그런 따짐으로 얻을 것이 별로 없으리라는 것도 알고 있었을 것이다. 그러므로 두 사람의 이야기는 으레 크로즈비에게로 흘렀는데, 큰 집에서 그의 이름이 오르내리는 말투는 릴리 앞에서 취하던 말투와는 아주 딴판이었다.\n\n“그자는 비참한 사내가 될 게다.” 정해진 날짜를 벨에게 일러 주며 지주가 말했다.\n\n“저는 그이가 비참해지기를 바라지는 않아요.” 벨이 말했다. “하지만 그런 짓을 해 놓고 벌을 받지 않으리라고는 도무지 생각할 수가 없어요.”\n\n“그자는 비참한 사내가 될 게다. 그 여자한테서 재산이라고는 한 푼도 받지 못할 텐데, 그 여자는 재산으로 얻을 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 다 바라고 있을 테니까. 게다가 내가 알기로는 그 여자가 그자보다 나이도 위다. 나는 도무지 이해할 수가 없구나. 참말이지, 사내가 어떻게 저렇게 악당이면서 또 저렇게 바보일 수 있는지 나는 이해할 수가 없다. 릴리에게 내 사랑을 전해 다오. 내일이나 모레쯤 내가 그 아이를 보러 가마. 그 아이는 저런 자를 잘 떨쳐 낸 게야. 나는 그것만은 확실히 안다—하기야 그 아이에게 그렇게 말해 봐야 소용없겠지만.”\n\n십사일 아침이 작은 집 사람들에게 찾아왔다. 오래 마음에 두고 헤아려 왔고 또 오래 기억될 그런 특별한 날의 아침이 찾아오듯이 찾아왔다. 그 아침은 매섭고 모진 서리를 데리고 왔다—검고 살을 에는 서리, 물관을 터뜨리고 땅을 화강암처럼 단단하게 얼려 붙이는 그런 서리였다. 릴리는 여왕이기는 했으나 아직 제 방으로 돌아가는 것이 허락되지 않아, 어머니 방의 큰 침대를 차지하고 있었고 어머니는 작은 침대에서 잤다.\n\n“엄마,” 그녀가 말했다. “그이들이 얼마나 추울까!” 어머니가 검은 서리가 내렸다는 것을 알려 준 참이었고, 이것이 그녀가 한 첫마디였다.\n\n“그이들 마음도 차가울까 걱정이구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다. 그런 말을 해서는 안 되는 것이었다. 크로즈비나 그의 신부에게 적의를 품은 것으로 해석될 만한 말은 무엇이든 이 집에서 인정된 규칙을 어기는 짓이었다. 그러나 그 일에 대한 부인의 감정이 너무나 강해서, 스스로를 억누를 수가 없었다.\n\n“왜 그이들 마음이 차가워야 해요? 아, 엄마, 그건 너무 끔찍한 말이에요. 왜 그이들 마음이 차가워야 하죠?”\n\n“그렇지 않기를 바란다.”\n\n“물론 그러시겠죠. 물론 우리 모두 그러기를 바라고요. 어쨌든 그이는 마음이 차가운 사람은 아니었어요. 제 자신을 모른다고 해서 마음이 차가운 사람이 되는 건 아니잖아요. 엄마, 저는 엄마가 그이들의 행복을 빌어 주셨으면 해요.”\n\n데일 부인은 이 말에 대답하기까지 일이 분 동안 잠자코 있었으나, 끝내는 대답을 했다. “나도 그러는 것 같구나.” 부인이 말했다. “나도 그것을 바라는 것 같다.”\n\n“저는 아주 확실히 그래요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n이 무렵 릴리는 아침을 이층에서 먹었으나, 오전 중에 아래층 응접실로 내려가곤 했다.\n\n“아래층에 내려갈 때는 몸을 아주 잘 여미고 가야 해.” 토스트와 차를 담아 올려 온 쟁반 곁에 선 채 벨이 말했다. “추위가 네 식으로 말하면 지독한 지경이거든.”\n\n“나 같으면 신난다고 하겠어.” 릴리가 말했다. “일어나서 밖에 나갈 수만 있다면 말이야. 언니, 그이가 처음 왔던 날 나한테 속어 쓴다고 잔소리했던 거 기억나?”\n\n“내가 그랬니, 우리 아가?”\n\n“기억 안 나? 내가 그이더러 멋쟁이라고 했을 때 말이야. 아, 저런! 정말 그이는 멋쟁이였지. 바로 그게 잘못이었어. 그리고 그건 다 내 탓이야. 나는 처음부터 그걸 보고 있었으니까.”\n\n벨은 잠시 얼굴을 돌리고 발로 바닥을 굴렀다. 그녀의 분노는 어머니의 것보다도 더 억누르기 어려웠다—그리고 지금 그녀는 그것을 억누르는 것이 아니라 감추고 싶어서, 이런 식으로 그것을 흘려보냈다.\n\n“나 알아, 언니. 언니 발이 저렇게 구를 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지 나는 알아. 그러니 그러면 안 돼. 이리 와, 언니, 내가 언니한테 기독교를 가르쳐 줄게. 나 참 대단한 선생이지, 안 그래? 아니, 꼭 그런 뜻으로 한 말은 아니야.”\n\n“나도 누구한테든 그걸 배울 수 있으면 좋겠구나.” 벨이 말했다. “우리가 기독교라 부르는 것이 도무지 될 법하지 않은 처지라는 게 있어.”\n\n“언니 발이 저렇게 구를 때 그건 아주 기독교인답지 못한 발이야. 가만히 두어야 해. 그건 그이에 대한 화를 뜻하잖아. 그이가 너무 늦기 전에 자기가 행복하지 못하리라는 걸—그러니까 그이와 내가 혼인해도 함께 행복하지 못하리라는 걸 알아냈다고 해서 내는 화 말이야.”\n\n“내 발을 너무 꼼꼼히 뜯어보지 말아라, 릴리.”\n\n“하지만 언니 발은 뜯어보는 걸 견뎌야 해. 눈도, 목소리도 그렇고. 그이가 나를 사랑하게 된 건 아주 어리석은 일이었어. 그리고 나도 아주 어리석었지. 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 말하자면 아무 생각도 없이 그이가 나를 사랑하도록 내버려 두었으니까. 나는 그이를 얻은 게 어찌나 자랑스러웠던지, 그이에게 그것을 생각해 볼 틈도 주지 않고 단번에 나를 내주어 버렸어. 한두 주 만에 다 되어 버린 일이야. 그런 약혼이 오래가리라고 누가 바랄 수 있었겠어?”\n\n“왜 못 가? 그건 말도 안 되는 소리야, 릴리. 하지만 그 얘기는 하지 말자.”\n\n“아, 하지만 나는 그 얘기를 하고 싶어. 내가 말한 그대로였어. 그렇다면 언니는 그이를 미워하면 안 돼. 그이는 제 잘못을 알아차렸을 때 정직하게 할 수 있는 단 한 가지 일을 했을 뿐이니까.”\n\n“뭐라고, 한 주도 안 되어 또 약혼을 하는 게?”\n\n“아주 오래된 우정이 있었잖아, 언니. 그건 언니도 기억해야 해. 하지만 나는 그이가 나에게 한 처신을 말한 거지, 그이가 그—에게 한 처신을 말한 게 아니야.” 그러다 그녀는 그 다른 아가씨가 바로 이 순간 자기가 한때 스스로 갖게 되리라 여기며 그토록 자랑스러워하던 그 이름을 지니고 있을지도 모른다는 데 생각이 미쳤다. “언니,” 그녀가 하던 말을 문득 멈추고 말했다. “런던에서는 사람들이 몇 시에 혼인을 해?”\n\n“아, 시간이야 갖가지지—열두 시 전이면 아무 때나. 그이들은 유행을 따를 테니 늦게 할 거야.”\n\n“그럼 언니는 그 여자가 아직 크로즈비 부인이 아니라고 생각해?”\n\n“레이디 알렉산드리나 크로즈비겠지.” 벨이 몸서리를 치며 말했다.\n\n“응, 그렇지. 내가 깜빡했네. 나는 그 여자를 정말 한번 보고 싶어. 그 여자한테 그렇게나 관심이 가. 머리카락이 무슨 빛깔일까. 유노 같은 여자겠지—키가 아주 크고 훤칠하고. 나처럼 들창코는 아닐 게 분명해. 내가 정말로 하고 싶은 게 뭔지 알아? 물론 될 리야 없지만—그이의 첫아이 대모가 되는 거야.”\n\n“아이, 릴리!”\n\n“정말 그러고 싶어. 될 리 없다는 걸 나도 안다고 말하는 게 안 들려? 런던까지 올라가서 그 여자한테 청하겠다는 게 아니야. 그 여자는 대부 대모로 온갖 높으신 어른들을 세우겠지. 나는 그 높으신 분들이 정말 어떤 사람들일까 궁금해.”\n\n“나는 다를 게 없다고 봐. 레이디 줄리아를 보렴.”\n\n“아, 그분은 높으신 분이 아니야. 작위만 있다고 되는 게 아니잖아. 그이가 우리한테 팰리서 씨야말로 그 사람들 가운데 제일가는 높으신 어른이라고 했던 거 기억 안 나? 사람들이 그 부류를 좋아하게 되는 법인가 봐. 그이는 늘 자기가 그런 부류 사이에서 하도 오래 지내서, 그들에게서 제 몸을 떼어 놓기가 아주 어려울 거라고 말하곤 했어. 나는 그런 것에는 도무지 어울리지 못했겠지, 안 그래?”\n\n“네가 그런 것이라고 부르는 그것만큼 내가 업신여기는 건 없어.”\n\n“언니는 그래? 나는 안 그래. 어쨌든 그 사람들이 얼마나 많은 일을 하는지 생각해 봐. 그이가 나한테 그 얘기를 하곤 했어. 나라를 다스리는 일이 죄다 그 사람들 손에 있는데, 그 일로 받는 돈은 아주 적다는 거야.”\n\n“나라로서는 더 안된 일이지.”\n\n“나라는 꽤 잘 굴러가는 것 같은데. 하지만 언니는 급진파야. 내 생각에 언니는 어쩔 수만 있다면 숙녀 노릇도 하지 않을 사람이야.”\n\n“나는 차라리 정직한 여자가 되겠어.”\n\n“언니는 이미 그래—내 사랑하는, 세상에서 제일 사랑하는, 정직한 우리 언니—그리고 내가 아는 가장 어여쁜 숙녀야. 내가 사내였다면, 언니, 언니야말로 내가 떠받들었을 바로 그런 아가씨야.”\n\n“하지만 너는 사내가 아니잖니. 그러니 소용없는 소리야.”\n\n“그래도 언니는 발이 저런 식으로 못된 짓을 하도록 놓아두면 안 돼. 정말이지 안 돼. 누군가 있는 그대로가 다 옳다고 말했는데, 나는 정말로 그 말을 믿어.”\n\n“나는 가끔 있는 그대로가 다 그르다는 쪽으로 마음이 기울어.”\n\n“그건 언니가 급진파라서 그래. 나 이제 일어날까 봐, 언니. 다만 무섭게 추워서 겁이 나.”\n\n“불이 아주 곱게 피워져 있어.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“응, 보여. 하지만 불은 침대처럼 나를 온통 감싸 주지는 않잖아. 그이들이 제단 앞에 서는 바로 그 순간을 알 수 있으면 좋겠어. 아직 열 시 반밖에 안 됐네.”\n\n“벌써 다 끝났다 해도 나는 조금도 놀라지 않겠어.”\n\n“끝났다니! 그 말이 어쩌면 그럴까! 그런 일이 끝나 버리고 나면 온 세상이 달려들어도 도로 물릴 수가 없잖아. 그이가 결국 불행해지면 어떡하지?”\n\n“그건 그이가 제 운에 맡길 일이야.” 벨이 말했다. 그러면서 속으로는 그 운이 아주 나쁜 운이리라 생각했다.\n\n“물론 제 운에 맡길 일이지. 자—이제 일어나야겠어.” 그러고서 그녀는 침대 너머 차가운 세상으로 첫발을 내디뎠다. “우리는 다들 제 운에 맡길 수밖에 없어. 나는 그게 열한 시 반일 거라고 마음을 정했어.”\n\n열한 시 반이 되었을 때 그녀는 응접실 난롯가의 커다란 안락의자에 앉아 있었고, 곁의 작은 탁자에는 소설책 한 권이 놓여 있었다. 그날 아침 그녀는 그 책을 펴 보지 않았고, 눈을 감고 손에 시계를 쥔 채 한참 동안 아주 잠잠히 앉아 있었다.\n\n“엄마,” 마침내 그녀가 말했다. “이제 다 끝났어요, 저는 틀림없다고 생각해요.”\n\n“무엇이 끝났다는 거니, 얘야?”\n\n“그이가 그 아가씨를 아내로 삼았어요. 하느님께서 두 사람을 축복해 주시기를 바라고, 두 사람이 행복하기를 기도해요.” 그녀가 이 말을 할 때 그 말투에는 여느 때 없던 엄숙함이 있어서 데일 부인과 벨을 흠칫하게 했다.\n\n“나도 그러기를 바라마.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “그러니 이제, 릴리, 그 일에서 마음을 돌려 다른 것을 생각하도록 애써 보는 게 좋지 않겠니?”\n\n“하지만 그럴 수가 없는걸요, 엄마. 그렇게 말하기야 쉽지요. 하지만 사람이 제 생각을 골라 가질 수는 없잖아요.”\n\n“애를 쓰기만 하면 대개는 마음먹은 대로 생각을 이끌 수 있단다.”\n\n“하지만 저는 그 애를 쓸 수가 없어요. 정말이지 왜 그래야 하는지도 모르겠고요. 그이를 생각하는 것이 저에게는 자연스러운 일 같고, 그게 그렇게까지 그른 일일 리는 없다고 생각해요. 어떤 사람에게 그토록 깊은 관심을 쏟고 나면, 그 사람을 하루아침에 놓아 버릴 수는 없어요.” 그러고서 다시 침묵이 흘렀고, 얼마 뒤 릴리는 소설책을 집어 들었다. 그녀는 어머니가 말한 그 애를 써 보았으나, 그 애씀은 도무지 헛일이었다. “정말이지, 언니,” 그녀가 말했다. “내가 여태 읽어 보려고 덤빈 것 중에 제일가는 쓰레기야.” 이것은 유난히 배은망덕한 말이었으니, 그 책은 벨이 권한 것이었기 때문이다. “책들이 죄다 어쩜 이렇게 시시해졌을까! 나는 「천로역정」이나 다시 읽을래.”\n\n“「로빈슨 크루소」는 어떠니?” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“아니면 「폴과 비르지니」?” 릴리가 말했다. “그래도 나는 「천로역정」으로 할래. 나는 그 책을 도무지 이해할 수가 없는데, 그래서 오히려 더 좋은 것 같아.”\n\n“나는 이해할 수 없는 책은 질색이야.” 벨이 말했다. “나는 책이 흐르는 물처럼 맑아서 그 뜻이 한눈에 다 들여다보이는 게 좋아.”\n\n“뜻이 얼른 보이고 안 보이고는 읽는 사람에게도 얼마쯤 달린 것 아니겠니?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“물론 읽는 사람이 바보여서는 안 되겠지요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 읽는 사람 중에는 바보가 어찌나 많은지요.” 릴리가 말했다. “그런데도 그이들은 읽어서 무언가를 얻잖아요. 크럼프 부인은 늘 요한계시록을 들여다보고 있어서 거의 외다시피 해요. 그 형상 가운데 하나도 풀이하지는 못할 것 같지만, 진리에 대해 어렴풋하고 안개 같은 생각은 갖고 있죠. 그래서 그걸 좋아하는 거예요—이해하기에는 너무 아름다우니까요. 내가 「천로역정」을 좋아하는 것도 그래서고요.” 그 말끝에 벨이 그 책을 가져다주겠다고 했다.\n\n“아니, 지금은 됐어.” 릴리가 말했다. “언니가 이게 그렇게 훌륭하다고 하니 이걸 마저 읽을래. 나오는 사람들이 늘 성깔을 부리고 미친 사람들처럼 굴기는 하지만. 엄마, 그이들이 신혼여행을 어디로 가는지 아세요?”\n\n“모르겠구나, 얘야.”\n\n“그이는 저한테 호수 지방에 가자는 이야기를 하곤 했어요.” 그러고서 또 한 번 말이 끊겼는데, 그동안 벨은 어머니의 얼굴이 근심으로 흐려지는 것을 보았다. “하지만 이제 그 생각은 더 하지 않을래요.” 릴리가 말을 이었다. “무언가에 마음을 붙들어 매겠어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 의자에서 일어났다. “제가 앓지만 않았더라면 이렇게까지 힘들지는 않았을 것 같아요.”\n\n“물론 그렇지 않았을 게다, 우리 아가.”\n\n“그리고 저는 이제 곧, 당장에 다시 건강해질 거예요. 가만있자, 저더러 칼라일의 「프랑스 혁명사」를 읽으라고 한 이가 있었는데, 이제 시작해 볼까 봐요.” 그 책을 읽으라고 한 사람이 크로즈비라는 것은 벨도 데일 부인도 잘 알고 있었다. “하지만 저쪽 집에서 그걸 내려 올 수 있을 때까지는 미뤄야겠네요.”\n\n“네가 정말 그것을 원한다면 제인더러 가져오라고 하마.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“오후에 언니가 올라갈 때 가져다주면 돼요. 그래 줄 거지, 언니? 그동안 나는 이 물건이나 어떻게 읽어 나가 볼게요.” 그러고서 그녀는 다시 책장에 눈을 붙박은 채 앉아 있었다. “엄마, 이건 말씀드릴게요—이걸로 얼마쯤 위안을 삼으셔도 돼요. 오늘만 지나가면 다른 날을 두고는 법석을 떨지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“아무도 네가 법석을 떤다고 생각하지 않는단다, 릴리.”\n\n“아니에요, 저는 떨고 있어요. 언니, 그게 하필 밸런타인 날에 있다니 이상하지 않아? 날이 날이라서 일부러 그렇게 정한 걸까. 아, 저런. 나는 이날 그이한테서 받게 될 편지를 얼마나 자주 생각했는지 몰라. 내가 자기 밸런타인이라고 적어 보낼 그 편지 말이야. 그래—그이한테는 이제 다른 밸런—타인이—생겼구나.” 여기까지는 또렷한 목소리로 말했으나, 그러고서 그녀는 무너져 내려 발작하듯 흐느끼기 시작했고, 가슴이 미어지기라도 할 듯 어머니 품에 안겨 울었다. 그런데도 그녀의 가슴은 미어지지 않았고, 슬픔에 눌려 넘어가지는 않겠다고 스스로 세운 그 결심에 그녀는 여전히 굳세었다. 그녀 자신이 말했듯, 병으로 몸이 약해지지만 않았더라면 그 일이 그렇게까지 어렵지는 않았을 것이다.\n\n“릴리, 우리 아가. 불쌍한, 몹쓸 대접을 받은 우리 아가.”\n\n“아니에요, 엄마, 저는 그런 사람이 되지 않겠어요.” 그러면서 그녀는 자기를 덮친 발작을 이겨 내려고 애처롭게 몸부림쳤다. “저는 몹쓸 대접을 받은 사람으로 여겨지지 않겠어요. 유난히 몹쓸 대접을 받은 사람으로는요. 하지만 저는 엄마의 아가, 엄마만의 아가예요. 다만 제가 이렇게 바보 같을 때는 저를 가엾어하시는 대신 때리고 쥐어박아 주셨으면 좋겠어요. 사람이 제 스스로 바보짓을 할 때 무르게 대해 주는 건 큰 잘못이에요. 자, 언니, 언니의 그 시시한 책 여기 있어. 나는 이제 더 안 볼래. 이걸 읽어서 이렇게 된 것 같아.” 그러고서 그녀는 책을 저만치 밀어 놓았다.\n\n이 작은 소동이 있은 뒤로 그날 그녀는 크로즈비와 그의 신부에 대해 더는 한마디도 하지 않고, 게스트윅의 새집에 대한 이야기로 말머리를 돌렸다.\n\n“크로프츠 선생 가까이에 있게 되면 아주 마음 든든하겠지, 언니? 안 그래?”\n\n“글쎄.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“우리가 앓을 때 그분이 그렇게 지독하게 먼 길을 오지 않아도 되잖아.”\n\n“그건 그분에게 마음 든든한 일이겠구나.” 벨이 아주 새침하게 말했다.\n\n저녁이 되자 「프랑스 혁명사」 제1권이 마련되었고, 릴리는 칭찬할 만한 끈기로 읽기에 매달렸다. 여덟 시가 되어 어머니가, 여왕이거나 말거나 잠자리에 들어야 한다고 우길 때까지 그러했다.\n\n“있잖아, 나는 왕이 그렇게까지 나쁜 사람이었다는 건 조금도 못 믿겠어.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“나는 믿어.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“아, 그건 언니가 급진파라서 그래. 나는 왕들이 다른 사람들보다 그렇게 훨씬 더 나쁘다고는 결코 믿지 않을 거야. 찰스 1세로 말하자면 그이는 역사를 통틀어 거의 가장 훌륭한 사람이었는걸.”\n\n이것은 오래된 말다툼거리였다. 그러나 이번만은 릴리가 병자라는 까닭으로 제 뜻대로 하도록 허락되었다.\n\n# 제45장. 런던의 밸런타인 날\n\n런던의 이월 십사일도 앨링턴에서와 똑같이 어둡고 춥고 겨울다웠으며, 그 추위는 어쩌면 얼마쯤 더 서글펐다. 그럼에도 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시는, 그날 아침 열한 시에 마차에서 내려 세인트제임스 교회로 걸어 들어갈 때, 혼례의 화려한 차림이 만들어 줄 수 있는 만큼은 눈부시게 보였다.\n\n혼례는 마침내 런던에서 치르기로 정해졌다. 코시 성에서 혼례를 올리는 편이 한결 편했으리라 할 이유는 물론 여럿 있었다. 드 코시 집안 사람들은 모두 시골 본가에 모여 있었으니 비용도 수고도 없이 예식에 참석할 수 있었을 것이다. 게다가 성은 사람이 사는 온기로 따뜻했고, 집안의 딸 하나가 떠나는 자리에 집이라는 정겨움이 우아함을 얹어 주었을 것이다. 딸린 사람들과 하인들도 거기 있었으니, 그렇게 치러진 혼례에서라면 고귀한 인연이 지닌 무르익은 풍요로움 같은 것이 적어도 크로즈비에게는 느껴졌을지도 몰랐다. 그리고 포트먼 스퀘어의 그 집이 몹시 썰렁하다는 것은—거기서 치르는 혼례도 썰렁하리라는 것은, 거기에 무슨 명예와 영광을 붙일 가망도, 《모닝 포스트》의 지면에서 유행의 광채로 울려 퍼지게 할 가망도 없다는 것은—드 코시 백작부인조차 인정할 수밖에 없었을 것이다. 그러나 시골에서 혼례를 치렀다면 백작이 그 자리에 있었을 터였다. 반면 그런 자리에 참석하자고 그가 런던까지 올라올 가망은 없었다.\n\n백작은 요즈음 몹시 무서운 사람이었고, 알렉산드리나는 장차 남편이 될 사람과 이야기가 스스럼없어지자 그를 도깨비라 부르며 말했다. 살아가는 온갖 일에서 도무지 피할 길이 없는 도깨비지만, 교묘한 꾀와 유리한 사정을 조심스레 마련해 두는 것으로 이따금 한 번씩은 비켜 갈 수 있는 도깨비라는 것이었다. 크로즈비는 한두 번 나서서, 앞으로는 그 심술궂은 괴물의 영지에서 아주 멀찍이 떨어져 지낼 수 있으니 자기는 그 도깨비를 딱히 대수롭게 여기지 않는다고 넌지시 말한 적이 있었다.\n\n“우리 새집으로 나를 찾아오지는 않을 테니까요.” 그는 제 사랑에게 그렇게 말했는데, 거기에는 다정함이 조금 묻어 있었다. 그러나 사태를 그렇게 보는 데에 레이디 알렉산드리나는 이의를 달았다. 문제의 도깨비는 그녀의 아버지일 뿐 아니라 고귀한 귀족이기도 했으니, 그녀로서는 앞으로 자기들과 백작 사이의, 나아가 귀족 사회 전반과의 인연이 위태로워질 만한 어떤 처사에도 동의할 수 없었다. 아버지는 틀림없이 도깨비였고, 그 도깨비 노릇으로 가까운 이들에게 아주 무서운 사람이 될 수도 있었다. 그러나 그렇더라도, 아예 어떤 백작과도 가깝지 않은 것보다는 도깨비인 백작과 가까운 편이 낫지 않겠는가? 그리하여 그녀는 크로즈비에게 그 도깨비는 견뎌야 한다고 일러 두었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 그렇더라도 그 행복한 자리에서 그를 떼어 놓을 수 있다는 것은 큰일이었다. 그였다면 아주 끔찍한 말들을 했을 것이다—신랑이 과연 견뎌 낼 수 있었을지 의심스러울 만큼 끔찍한 말들을. 철도역에서 크로즈비가 당한 봉변을 전해 들은 뒤로 그는 사위에게 가해진 그 매질을 두고 악귀처럼 고소해하며 줄곧 떠들어 대고 있었다. 드 코시 백작부인은 크로즈비 편을 들며 이 혼사가 제 딸에게 어울린다고 주장하다가, 남편 앞에서 크로즈비가 유행을 아는 사내라고 감히 말해 버린 적이 있었다. 그래서 백작은 이제 역겨운 웃음을 지어 보이며, 신랑의 유행 감각이 패딩턴에서의 그 자그마한 모험 덕에 나아지기라도 했느냐고 묻곤 하는 것이었다. 이 모든 말이 옮겨지지 않은 크로즈비는 시골에서 혼례를 올리는 편을 더 좋아했을 것이다. 그러나 백작부인과 레이디 알렉산드리나는 더 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n백작은 어떤 지출도 엄히 금해 두었고, 백작부인은 어쩔 수 없이 그 말을 불필요한 비용을 금한 것으로 새겼다. “딸 하나를 시집보내면서 당장 드는 돈이 한 푼도 없다는 것은 아무도 이해 못 할 일이오.” 백작부인이 맏딸에게 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“나라면 정말이지 되도록 적게 쓰겠소.” 레이디 어밀리아가 대답했다. “아시다시피 어머니, 이 일에는 지금 당장 남들 입에 오르내리지 않았으면 하는 사정이 있잖소. 그 아가씨 이야기도 있고—역에서의 그 소동도 있으니 말이오. 나는 정말이지 되도록 조용히 치러야 한다고 생각하오.” 레이디 어밀리아의 분별은 나무랄 데가 없었고, 그 어머니도 그것을 인정했다. 그러나 그렇다고 혼례를 소문나게 조용한 방식으로 치른다면, 그 조용함의 소문 자체가 요란한 영광을 노리는 것만큼이나 위험할 터였다. “하지만 돈은 그만큼 들지 않을 것 아니오.” 어밀리아가 말했다. 그리하여 혼례는 아주 조용히 치르기로 정해졌다.\n\n이에 대해서는 크로즈비도 아주 기꺼이 동의했다. 다만 백작부인이 제 뜻을 그에게 설명한 그 태도만은 마음에 들지 않았다.\n\n“굳이 말할 것도 없지만, 어돌퍼스.” 그녀가 말했다. “나는 이 혼사가 더할 나위 없이 흡족하오. 내 사랑스러운 딸아이가 그대의 아내로서 행복할 수 있다고 여기니, 내가 더 무엇을 바라겠소? 나는 그 아이한테도 어밀리아한테도, 나는 저희를 두고 욕심을 부리는 어미가 아니라고 늘 말해 왔고, 둘 다 제 마음에 드는 대로 하게 두었소.”\n\n“두 분 다 제 마음에 드는 대로 하셨기를 바랍니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“그러기를 바라오. 하지만 그렇더라도—내 말뜻이 통하는지 모르겠구려.”\n\n“잘 알겠습니다, 드 코시 백작부인. 알렉산드리나가 후작의 맏아들과 혼인하는 것이라면, 저와 혼인할 때 필요한 것보다는 교회로 가는 행렬이 더 길었겠지요.”\n\n“참 별나게도 말하는구려, 어돌퍼스.”\n\n“서로 뜻만 통한다면야 아무래도 좋습니다. 정말이지 저는 행렬 같은 것은 조금도 바라지 않는다고 말씀드릴 수 있습니다. 다비와 조앤처럼 알렉산드리나와 팔짱을 끼고 걸어 내려가서, 교회 서기더러 신부를 넘겨 달라고 해도 아주 만족하겠습니다.”\n\n우리는 이렇게 말해도 좋으리라. 그는 제 팔에 아무 짐도 걸치지 않은 채 그 길을 걸어 내려갈 수만 있었다면 훨씬 더 만족했으리라고. 그러나 이제 그런 구원이 있을 가망은 없었다.\n\n레이디 어밀리아와 게이지비 씨는 진작부터 그의 마음속 쓰라림과 그가 후회하고 있다는 사실을 알아차리고 있었고, 게이지비는 아내에게 저 고귀한 처제가 스스로 비참한 삶을 마련하고 있는 것 같다고 넌지시 말한 적이 있었다.\n\n“저 사람도 익숙해지고 나면 조용해지고 행복해질 거요.” 레이디 어밀리아가 대답했다. 아마도 제 경험을 떠올리며 한 말이었을 것이다.\n\n“글쎄요, 여보. 저이는 조용한 사람이 아니에요. 저이 눈에는 여자한테 아주 모질어질 수 있는 무언가가 있어요.”\n\n“이제 와서 무엇을 바꾸기에는 너무 멀리 왔소.” 레이디 어밀리아가 대답했다.\n\n“뭐, 그럴지도 모르지요.”\n\n“그리고 나는 내 동생을 잘 아오. 그 애는 그런 말은 들으려고도 하지 않을 거요. 나는 저 둘이 서로에게 익숙해지고 나면 아주 잘 지내리라고 정말로 생각하오.”\n\n저 나름의 경험이 있는 게이지비 씨는 감히 그만큼까지 바라지는 못했다. 그의 가정이 그에게 흡족했던 것은 그가 셈에 밝은 사람이었고, 셈을 정확히 해 놓은 뒤 그 순이익을 기꺼이 받아들였기 때문이다. 그는 평생 그렇게 해서 성공을 거두어 왔다. 그의 집에서는 아내가 으뜸이었으니—그것은 그 자신도 잘 알고 있었다. 그러나 아내가 아무리 애를 쓴들, 설령 그런 애를 쓰고 싶어 했다 한들, 그로 하여금 수입의 삼분의 이가 넘는 돈을 쓰게 만들 수는 없었을 것이다. 이 점은 아내도 알고 있었고, 그래서 거기에 맞추어 돛을 손보았으며, 그 점에서는 저를 남편에게 맞추어 왔던 것이다. 그러나 그런 지혜와 그런 돛 손질과 그런 맞춤을, 크로즈비 씨와 레이디 알렉산드리나에게서 바랄 여지가 어디 있단 말인가?\n\n“아무튼 이제는 너무 늦었소.” 레이디 어밀리아가 그렇게 말하며 이야기를 맺었다.\n\n그럼에도 막상 마지막 순간이 오자 영광을 노리는 자그마한 시도가 조금은 있었다. 갓 혼인한 부부의 조촐한 만찬 자리가 서대 튀김과 양 다리 고기라는 순전한 소박함에서 시작해, 맑은 수프를 흉내 내는 데까지, 서대가 나간 뒤에 돌려지는 안타깝게도 식어 버린 둥근 완자 요리에까지, 그리고 야심의 마지막 몸부림으로 이웃 과자점에 주문하는 새빨간 젤리와 곱디고운 분홍빛 크림에까지 어떻게 늘어나고 마는지를 모르는 사람이 어디 있겠는가?\n\n“요리사와 세라만 데리고 만찬을 차릴 수는 없어요, 여보.”\n\n이렇게 시작되고, 남편은 자기는 그럴 생각이 없다고 잘라 말한다. “핍스와 다우드니가 이리 와서 양고기 한 점을 먹겠다면야 대환영이고, 아니라면 오지 말라지. 그리고 핍스의 누이도 부르는 게 좋겠군. 당신이 응접실로 들어갈 때 같이 갈 사람이 하나는 있어야 할 테니.”\n\n“나는 혼자 가는 편이 훨씬 좋아요. 그래야 책이라도 읽지요.”—아니면 졸기라도 하지요, 라고 우리는 말해도 좋으리라.\n\n그러나 남편은 그렇게 혼자 있으면 벗 하나 없어 보인다고 설명했고, 그리하여 핍스의 누이가 초대되었다. 그러고 나서 만찬은 마지막 몸부림으로 주문된 그 값비싼 젤리에까지 이르러 버린 것이다. 그저 양 다리 고기 하나였다면 모두에게 훨씬 흥겨웠으리라는 확신이 두 사람 마음속에 있었다. 그 둥근 완자들을 삯꾼이 들고 돌아다니지만 않았더라면, 뜨거운 감자를 곁들인 소박한 양고기를 세라가 핍스 양에게 건네주었더라면, 젊은 다우드니가 말을 걸었을 때 핍스 양이 까닭 없이 뻣뻣하게 새침을 떨지는 않았을 것이다. 훨씬 더 흥겨웠을 것이다. “양고기 좀 더 들게, 핍스. 어느 부위가 좋은가?” 얼마나 정겹게 들리는 말인가! 그러나 우리 모두는 그것이 이루어질 수 없는 일임을 안다. 내 젊은 벗은 그렇게 하려고 했으나, 그의 만찬은 저 혼자 달음질쳐서 식어 빠진 둥근 완자와 과자점의 색색 모양물에까지 이르고 말았다. 크로즈비의 혼례도 그와 같았다.\n\n신부는 격식을 갖추어 마련한 마차를 타고 교회를 떠나야 하고, 마부들은 혼례 장식을 달아야 한다. 그렇게 일이 커졌다. 고귀한 규모로도 아니고 참된 영광의 규모로도 아니어서, 그 시도를 정당화하지도 못했고 그 잔치를 값지게 만들지도 못한 채로 말이다. 잘 만든 리솔이 기분 좋게 나온다면 그것은 좋은 음식이다. 모든 것이 서로 어울리는 성대한 혼례라면 그것은 훌륭한 구경거리다. 성대한 혼례가 없는 세상이라니 당치도 않은 일이다. 그러나 누가 보아도 분수에 맞지 않게, 속으로는 실패하리라는 확신을 품은 채 벌이는 자잘한 발작 같은 시도—그것만은 혼례에서도, 만찬에서도, 삶의 모든 일에서도 마땅히 피해야 한다.\n\n신부 들러리들이 있었고 조찬도 있었다. 마거리타와 로지나 둘 다 이 일을 위해 런던으로 올라왔고, 그들의 사촌 하나인 그레셤 양도 함께 왔으니 아버지가 같은 주에 사는 아가씨였다. 그레셤 씨는 드 코시 경의 누이와 혼인한 사람이었으므로 그의 수고도 함께 청해졌다. 그는 신부를 넘겨주는 일을 맡으러 불려 올라왔으니, 백작이—신문의 한 문단이 밝힌 바로는—대대로 내려오는 옛 원수인 통풍 때문에 코시 성에 갇혀 있었기 때문이다. 네 번째 신부 들러리도 하나 구해졌고, 그리하여 한 무리가 이루어지기는 했으나 요즈음 대개 바람직하다고들 여기는 만큼 큰 무리는 아니었다. 교회 앞에 마차는 서너 대뿐이었지만, 서너 대라도 어디인가. 날씨가 무섭도록 추워서 부인들의 엷은 빛깔 비단옷은 보기만 해도 불편해 보였다. 서리 내린 아침에 엷은 옷을 입고 고와 보이려면 아주 젊어야 하는데, 레이디 알렉산드리나의 혼례에 선 들러리들은 그리 젊지 않았다. 레이디 로지나의 코는 대놓고 빨갰다. 레이디 마거리타는 몹시 겨울다웠고 보아하니 몹시 심사가 틀어져 있었다. 그레셤 양은 굼뜨고 밋밋하고 심심했으며, 네 번째 자리를 채운 아너러블 오플래허티 양은 이토록 절름거리는 공연에 한몫 끼라고 불려 온 것을 알고는 뾰로통해 있었다.\n\n그러나 혼례는 무사히 이루어졌고, 크로즈비는 제 불운을 사내답게 견디어 냈다. 몽고메리 돕스와 파울러 프랫이 둘 다 그의 곁에 서서, 바라건대 그가 온 세상에게서 아주 버림받은 것은 아니라는—손발이 묶인 채 드 코시 집안에 저를 내맡겨 그들이 하고 싶은 대로 모든 일에서 다루어지도록 하지는 않았다는—어떤 믿음을 그에게 주었을 것이다. 그를 그토록 괴롭게 한 것이 바로 그 느낌이었고, 그와 한 짝을 이루는 또 하나의 느낌, 곧 끝내 드 코시 집안에 맞서 반란을 일으키는 데 성공한다 해도 저는 외톨이가 되어 있으리라는 느낌이었다.\n\n“그럼, 나는 갈 걸세.” 파울러 프랫이 몽고메리 돕스에게 말했다. “나는 할 수만 있으면 언제나 벗의 곁을 지키네. 크로즈비는 불한당처럼, 그리고 바보처럼 굴었고, 내가 그렇게 여긴다는 것도 그자가 아네. 하지만 그렇다고 그를 내버릴 까닭은 없다고 보네. 그가 청했으니 나는 갈 걸세.”\n\n“그럼 나도 가야지.” 몽고메리 돕스가 말했다. 파울러 프랫이 하는 대로 하면 안전하리라 여긴 그는, 어쨌거나 크로즈비가 백작의 딸과 혼인하는 것 아니냐고 속으로 되뇌었다.\n\n혼례가 끝난 뒤에는 조찬이 이어졌고, 백작부인이 자못 고귀한 위엄으로 그 자리를 주재했다. 그녀는 교회에는 가지 않았는데, 아마도 교회에서 요통에 걸릴 위험을 무릅쓰지 않아야 잔치에서 기분을 잘 지킬 수 있으리라 여겼기 때문일 것이다. 식탁 아래쪽 끝에는 그녀의 시매부인 그레셤 씨가 앉았으니, 꼭 필요한 축배와 꼭 필요한 축사를 맡기로 한 사람이었다. 아너러블 존도 거기 있었고, 식탁 아래쪽 끝의 제 마땅한 자리에서 밀려났다 하여 제 누이와 새 매부를 두고 온갖 심술궂은 말을 늘어놓았다. 그러나 알렉산드리나는 그 일을 제 오라비에게 맡기지 않겠다고 못 박아 두었더랬다. 아너러블 조지는 백작부인이 제 아내를 부르지 않았으므로 오지 않았다.\n\n“머라이어가 굼뜨고 어쩌고 하는 것은 그럴지도 모르지.” 조지가 말했다. “하지만 그 사람은 내 아내야. 게다가 그 사람한테는 저들한테 없는 것이 있단 말이지. 나를 사랑하거든 내 개도 사랑하라는 말도 있잖나.” 그리하여 그는 코시에 그대로 머물렀는데—내 생각에는 아주 옳은 일이었다.\n\n알렉산드리나는 조찬 전에 떠나고 싶어 했고, 크로즈비로서도 얼마나 일찍 빠져나갈 길이 마련되든 상관하지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 백작부인은 딸에게, 조찬을 기다리지 않겠다면 조찬도 없고 사실상 혼례도 없다고, 그저 밋밋한 혼인이 있을 뿐이라고 일러 두었다. 성대한 잔치가 있었다면 신랑 신부가 먼저 떠나는 것도 아주 좋았으련만, 백작부인은 이번 같은 자리에서는 제물의 몸뚱이가 그 자리에 있어 주는 것 말고는 그 잔치에 무슨 실감을 줄 것이 없다고 느꼈다. 그리하여 크로즈비와 레이디 알렉산드리나 크로즈비는 그레셤 씨의 축사를 들었으니, 그는 이 젊은 부부에게 인간의 처지와는 도무지 어울리지 않을 만큼 큰 행복과 번영을 예언했다. 알게 되어 더없이 기뻤던 저의 젊은 벗 크로즈비는 나라의 떠오르는 기둥 가운데 하나로 널리 알려진 사람이라 했다. 그의 앞날이 의회에 있든 나라의 상근 공직에 바쳐지든, 그 길은 어느 쪽이나 위대하고 고귀하며 번창하리라는 것이었다. 그리고 사랑하는 조카딸로 말하자면, 젊은 여자에게 가장 아름답고 영광스러운 자리를 이제 차지하게 되었으니—이보다 더 잘할 수는 없었으리라 했다. 그 애는 재물보다 재능을 골랐노라고—그레셤 씨는 그렇게 말했다—그러니 그 애는 제게 어울리는 보답을 받게 되리라 했다. 제게 어울리는 보답을 받으리라는 대목만은, 그녀가 무엇을 골랐든 간에 그레셤 씨가 틀림없이 옳았다. 그 점에 관해서라면 나 자신도 조금도 의심하지 않는다. 그 뒤에 크로즈비가 답사를 했는데, 그런 자리에서 열 사람 가운데 아홉이 하는 것보다는 훨씬 나은 연설이었다. 그러고서 일은 끝났다. 다른 연설은 허락되지 않았고, 그때로부터 반 시간이 채 되기 전에 그와 그의 신부는 역마차에 올라 포크스턴으로 가는 철도역으로 실려 갔다. 신혼여행지로 그곳이 골라졌던 것이다. 한때는 포크스턴까지 가는 길을 그저 파리로 가는 첫 구간으로 삼자는 뜻도 있었으나, 파리도 바깥나라 여행도 차츰차츰 접히고 말았다.\n\n“나는 프랑스는 조금도 상관없어요. 우리가 하도 자주 가 보았잖아요.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 나폴리에 데려가 달라고 하고 싶어 했으나, 크로즈비는 그 말이 처음 소곤거려졌을 때 이미 나폴리는 어림도 없다는 것을 그녀에게 알아듣게 해 놓았다. 이제 그는 모든 일에서 돈을 보아야 했다. 앞으로 살아갈 길의 첫걸음부터 한 실링이라도 아낄 수 있는 곳에서는 한 실링을 아껴야 했다. 이런 삶의 방식에는 드 코시 쪽에서도 아무 반대가 없었다. 레이디 어밀리아는 동생에게, 신혼여행이라도 당장 살림을 차리는 것보다 더 들지 않게끔 해야 한다고 설명해 두었다. 물론 하지 않을 수 없는 일들, 본디 값이 나가는 일들이 있었다. 신부는 옷차림이 반듯한 몸종을 데려가야 한다. 포크스턴 호텔의 방은 넓어야 하고 이 층이어야 한다. 머무는 동안 그녀가 쓸 마차도 세내야 한다. 그러나 바깥세상 눈에 띄지 않을 돈이라면 한 실링이라도 아껴야 했다. 아, 적은 밑천으로 부유한 겉모양을 꾸미는 이들의 그 가난에서 우리를 건져 주소서! 회칠한 무덤이 회칠된 그만큼 새하얗게 회칠된 것은 세상에 다시 없으니!\n\n알맞은 자리에 얼마쯤 뇌물을 찔러 넣은 덕에 크로즈비는 저와 아내가 쓸 열차 칸 한 칸을 통째로 얻어 냈다. 그리고 알렉산드리나의 맞은편에 앉아 그 화사한 빛깔의 옷가지들을 빠짐없이 여며 준 뒤에, 그는 저 자신이 여태 한 번도 그녀와 진짜로 단둘이 있어 본 적이 없다는 사실을 떠올렸다. 그는 그녀와 자주 춤을 추었고, 춤곡 사이사이 몇 분씩 그녀와 남겨져 있기도 했다. 사람들로 붐비는 응접실에서 그녀와 시시덕거리기도 했고, 코시 성에서는 릴리언 데일과 약혼한 몸이면서도 그녀와 혼인할 뜻이 있다고 말할 틈을 한 번 낸 적도 있었다. 그러나 릴리와 그랬던 것처럼 몇 시간이고 그녀와 함께 걸어 본 적은 없었다. 그녀에게 정부며 정치며 책 이야기를 해 본 적도 없었고, 그녀가 그에게 시(詩)며 신앙이며 살림살이의 자잘한 의무와 위안을 이야기한 적도 없었다. 그는 레이디 알렉산드리나를 예닐곱 해 전부터 알고 지냈다. 그러나 두 달 사이에 릴리 데일을 알게 된 것처럼 그녀를 알아 본 적은—아마 앞으로도 결코 알지 못하리라—없었다.\n\n그리고 이제 그녀가 제 아내가 되었는데, 그는 그녀에게 무슨 말을 해야 한단 말인가? 두 사람은 남은 평생 동안 서로를 한 몸, 한 살로 만들어 갈 동반을 시작한 것이었다. 서로에게 전부가 되어야 하는 것이었다. 그런데 이 전부가 되는 동반을 그는 어떻게 시작해야 한단 말인가? 사제가 축복과 함께 그 일을 이미 충분히 해 놓아서, 크로즈비 자신이 따로 할 일은 없다는 것인가? 그녀가 저기 그의 맞은편에, 그의 진짜 아내가—그의 뼈 중의 뼈가—앉아 있는데, 그는 그녀에게 무슨 말을 해야 하는가? 그는 제 자리에 몸을 붙이고 앉아, 그녀의 다른 덮개들 위에 이미 덮어 준 진홍색 가장자리를 두른 고운 모피 무릎덮개의 한 자락을 제 무릎에도 끌어 덮으면서, 릴리에게라면 말 붙이기가 얼마나 쉬웠을까 하고 생각했다. 게다가 릴리라면 온 귀와 온 마음과 온 재치를 기울여, 그에게 떠오른 어떤 생각에든 지체 없이 뛰어들 채비가 되어 있었을 것이다. 그 점에서라면 릴리야말로 참으로 아내였을 것이다—제 마음을 날쌘 움직임으로 남편의 마음자리에 옮겨 놓는 아내 말이다. 그가 제 관청 이야기를 꺼냈다면 릴리는 채비가 되어 있었을 텐데, 알렉산드리나는 그의 관직 생활에 관해 여태 단 한 마디도 물은 적이 없었다. 그가 내일의 즐거움을 위한 계획을 갖고 있었다면 릴리는 신이 나서 받아 주었을 텐데, 알렉산드리나는 그런 자잘한 것에는 도무지 마음을 쓰지 않았다.\n\n“편안하시오?” 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n“아, 네, 아주 편안해요, 고마워요. 그런데 참, 내 화장 상자는 어떻게 하셨어요?”\n\n그리고 그 물음만은 그녀도 얼마쯤 열을 내어 물었다.\n\n“당신 밑에 있소. 원하면 발받침으로 써도 되오.”\n\n“어머, 안 돼요. 긁히잖아요. 해나가 갖고 있다가 잃어버릴까 봐 걱정이었어요.” 그러고서 다시 침묵이 흘렀고, 크로즈비는 아내에게 다음으로 무슨 말을 할지 또 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n그런 처지에서 어떻게 해야 하는지에 관해 예로부터 우리에게 주어진 충고를 우리 모두 알고 있거니와, 갓 혼인한 남편만큼 그 충고를 따르는 것이 온전히 정당한 사람이 또 어디 있겠는가? 그리하여 그는 그녀의 손을 잡으려고 제 손을 내밀어 그녀를 제 쪽으로 좀 더 끌어당겼다.\n\n“내 보닛 조심해요.” 그가 입을 맞출 때 열차 칸이 흔들리는 것을 느끼며 그녀가 말했다. 내 생각에 그는 그녀와 그녀의 보닛을 포크스턴에 무사히 내려놓을 때까지 다시는 입을 맞추지 않았다. 릴리였다면 그는 얼마나 자주 입을 맞추었을 것이며, 여정이 끝났을 때 그녀의 보닛은 얼마나 예뻤을 것이며, 보닛을 구부려 놓았다고 그를 나무랄 때 그녀는 또 얼마나 행복해 보였을 것인가! 그러나 알렉산드리나는 제 보닛을 두고 아주 진지했다. 무슨 행복해 보이는 구석이 있기에는 너무나도 진지했다.\n\n그리하여 그는 열차가 터널에 이를 때까지 말없이 앉아 있었다.\n\n“나는 터널이 정말 싫어요.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다.\n\n그는 터널이 저에게 기회를 준다는 그릇된 생각으로 다시 한번 손을 내밀 마음이 반쯤은 있었다. 마음만 먹었다면 그 여정 전체가 하나의 긴 기회였다. 그러나 아내가 터널을 싫어했으므로 그는 손을 도로 거두었다. 릴리의 자그마한 손가락들이라면 그의 손길을 맞을 채비가 되어 있었으련만. 그는 그것을 생각했고, 생각하지 않을 수가 없었다.\n\n그의 세면 가방에는 《타임스》가 들어 있었다. 그녀도 소설책을 하나 갖고 있었다. 신문을 꺼내 읽으면 그녀가 언짢아할까? 거리는 몹시 더디게 줄어드는 듯했고, 포크스턴까지는 아직 한 시간이 더 남아 있었다. 그는 제 《타임스》가 몹시 그리웠으나, 마침내 그녀가 먼저 읽지 않는 한 저도 읽지 않겠다고 마음을 굳혔다. 그녀도 제 소설책을 떠올리고 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 천성이 그보다 참을성이 있었고, 이런 여정에서 무엇을 읽는다는 것은 어쩌면 온당치 못한 일일지도 모르겠다고 생각했다. 그래서 그녀는 남편의 머리 위 그물 선반에 눈길을 둔 채 얌전히 앉아 있었다.\n\n마침내 그는 더 견딜 수 없어, 더없이 다정하고 진지하려는 뜻으로 대화에 뛰어들었다.\n\n“알렉산드리나.” 그가 말했다. 그의 목소리는, 그녀의 귀가 그런 가락을 알아들을 줄만 알았더라면, 부드럽고 진지한 태도에 알맞게 잘 골라져 있었다. “알렉산드리나, 당신과 내가 오늘 내디딘 것은 아주 중요한 걸음이오.”\n\n“네, 정말 그래요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“우리가 서로를 행복하게 해 주는 데 성공하기를 바라오.”\n\n“네, 그러기를 바라요.”\n\n“우리 둘 다 그것을 진지하게 생각하고, 그것이 우리의 으뜸가는 의무임을 잊지 않는다면 그렇게 될 거요.”\n\n“네, 그렇게 되겠지요. 다만 나는 그 집이 너무 춥지만 않았으면 좋겠어요. 지은 지 얼마 안 되었잖아요. 게다가 나는 머리에 감기가 잘 드는데. 어밀리아 언니는 그 집이 아주 추울 거라고 하던데요. 하기야 언니는 처음부터 우리가 거기로 가는 것을 반대했지만요.”\n\n“집은 아무 문제 없을 거요.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 그리고 알렉산드리나는 그렇게 말하는 그의 말투에 무언가 주인 노릇을 하는 기색이 있음을 알아챌 수 있었다.\n\n“나는 그저 어밀리아 언니가 한 말을 전한 것뿐이에요.” 그녀가 대꾸했다.\n\n릴리가 그의 신부였고, 그가 그녀에게 두 사람의 앞날과 서로의 의무를 이야기했더라면, 그녀는 그 이야기에 얼마나 활활 타올랐을 것인가! 그녀는 열차 칸 바닥에 그의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 있는 힘을 다하겠노라고—있는 힘을, 온 힘을 다하겠노라고 약속했을 것이다. 그리고 그 약속을 지키겠다고 얼마나 간절한 속마음의 결심으로 다짐했을 것인가. 그는 이제 그 모든 것을 생각했으나, 그것을 생각해서는 안 된다는 것도 알았다. 그러고서 십오 분쯤 그는 정말로 신문을 꺼내 들었고, 그가 그러는 것을 보자 그녀도 제 소설책을 꺼내 들었다.\n\n그는 신문을 꺼내 들었으나 그날의 정치에 마음을 붙일 수가 없었다. 그는 무서운 잘못을 저지른 것이 아닌가? 제 맞은편에 앉은 저 여자라는 물건이 그의 삶에 무슨 쓸모가 있단 말인가? 크나큰 벌이 그에게 내린 것이 아닌가, 그리고 그는 그 벌을 받아 마땅하지 않은가? 참으로 크나큰 벌이 그에게 내려 있었다. 그가 혼인한 여자가 혼인 생활의 더 높은 의무를 이해할 줄 모르는 여자라는 것만이 아니라, 그 자신은 그것을 이해하는 여자의 값어치를 알아볼 줄 아는 사람이었다는 것이 문제였다. 그는 릴리 데일과라면 행복했을 것이다. 그러므로 레이디 알렉산드리나와의 불행은 그만큼 더 크리라고 우리는 짐작해도 좋다. 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시 같은 여자와 혼인하면 저에게 가장 잘 맞는 물건을 얻게 되는 사내들도 있으니, 모티머 게이지비가 그녀의 언니와 혼인하면서 그러했다. 레이디 덤벨로가 된 그리젤다 그랜틀리 양도, 레이디 알렉산드리나보다 얼마쯤 더 차갑고 얼마쯤 더 영리하기는 했으나 같은 종류였다. 그러나 그녀와 혼인함으로써 덤벨로 경은 저에게 가장 잘 맞는 물건을 얻었다—심술궂은 세상이 그가 그 물건을 지니고 있게만 내버려 둔다면 말이다. 크로즈비의 실패가 그토록 쓰라린 것은 바로 이 점이었으니, 그는 더 나은 길을 보았고 옳다 여겼으면서도 더 나쁜 길로 걷기를 스스로 골랐던 것이다. 코시 성에서 보낸 그 한 주 동안—앨링턴을 두 번째로 다녀온 바로 뒤에 거기서 보낸 그 한 주 동안—그는 저는 좋은 길보다 나쁜 길에 더 맞는 사람이라고 곰곰이 마음을 정했더랬다. 이제 그 길이 그의 앞에 놓여 있었고, 그는 그 길을 걷는 것 말고는 다른 도리가 없었다.\n\n포크스턴에 닿았을 때는 몹시 추웠고, 레이디 알렉산드리나는 저를 위해 역으로 보내진, 자가용처럼 보이는 마차에 올라타며 몸을 떨었다.\n\n“호텔 응접실에는 불이 잘 피워져 있을 거요.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“아, 그랬으면 좋겠어요.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다. “침실에도요.”\n\n젊은 남편은 저 자신이 언짢아진 것을 느꼈으나, 왜 그런지는 잘 알 수 없었다. 그는 언짢았고, 하지 않으면 누구나 알아차렸을 그 자잘한 예의들을 겨우겨우 치러 냈다. 그러나 어쨌든 그는 제 할 일을 했으니, 그녀의 숄과 덮개들을 두루 살피고, 해나에게 상냥하게 말을 건네고, 화장 상자에는 각별히 마음을 썼다.\n\n“저녁은 몇 시에 들고 싶소?” 그녀를 해나와 함께 침실에 두고 나오려 하면서 그가 물었다.\n\n“당신 좋을 때 아무 때나요. 다만 지금 차와 버터 바른 빵을 좀 먹었으면 해요.”\n\n크로즈비는 거실로 가서 차와 버터 바른 빵을 시키고 저녁 식사도 시킨 다음, 앞날의 제 길을 조금 생각해 보려고 벽난로에 등을 대고 섰다.\n\n그는 제 삶이 성공이어야 한다고 오래전에 마음을 정해 둔 사람이었다. 마음먹기에 달린 일이라면 누구나 그렇게 마음을 정할 듯싶지만, 내가 보기에 사람들 대다수는 그런 결심을 하지 않으며, 아주 많은 사람은 성공하지 못하겠다고 결심한다. 그러나 크로즈비는 성공하겠다고 마음을 정했고, 그 뜻을 이루기 위해 많은 것을 해 왔다. 그는 제 이름을 알렸고 어느 만큼의 명성도 얻었다. 그러나 그 명성은, 그 자신도 인정하는 바이지만, 저에게서 떠나가고 있었다. 그는 그 일을 똑바로 마주 보고서, 이제 제 유행은 버려야 한다고 스스로에게 말했다. 그러나 관청은 그에게 남아 있었다. 그는 아직 옵티미스트 씨 위에 군림할 수 있고 버터웰을 고분고분한 종으로 만들 수 있었다. 이제 그것이 그의 삶의 노선이어야 했고, 그 노선에는 충실하려고 애쓸 참이었다. 아내와 가정으로 말하자면—거기서는 아침을, 그리고 어쩌면 저녁을 구하면 될 것이었다. 편안한 안락의자를 하나 두고, 알렉산드리나가 어머니가 되거든 제 아이들을 사랑하려고 애쓸 것이었다. 그러나 무엇보다도 릴리는 결코 생각하지 않을 것이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 서서 반 시간 동안 그녀를 생각했다.\n\n“죄송합니다요, 나리. 마님께서 저녁을 몇 시로 시키셨는지 여쭈어 오라 하십니다요.”\n\n“일곱 시일세, 해나.”\n\n“마님께서 몹시 고단하시다며 저녁때까지 누워 계시겠답니다요.”\n\n“알았네, 해나. 옷 갈아입을 때가 되면 마님 방으로 가겠네. 아래층에서 자네를 잘 돌봐 주고 있는가?”\n\n그러고서 크로즈비는 추운 겨울 저녁의 어스름 속으로 부두를 거닐러 나갔다.\n\n# 제46장. 사무국의 존 임스\n\n크로즈비 씨와 그의 아내는 이월 중순에 포크스턴으로 신혼여행을 떠났고, 삼월 말께 런던으로 돌아왔다. 그 여섯 주 동안 우리 이야기의 관심사에 특별히 중요한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다—바닷가에서 지낸 그 젊은 부부의 행적에 무슨 각별한 흥미가 있다고 여기지 않는 한은 말이다. 그 행적에 관해 내가 말할 수 있는 것이라고는, 그것이 끝났을 때 크로즈비가 무척 기뻐했다는 것뿐이다. 휴가라는 것은 죄다 고된 일이지만, 할 일이 아무것도 없는 휴가야말로 가장 고된 일이다. 삼월 말에 그들은 새집으로 들어갔는데, 레이디 알렉산드리나가 그 집을 그리 춥게 여기지 않았기를 바라 마지않는다.\n\n그동안 릴리는 병에서 차차 온전히 회복해 가고 있었다. 재발도 없었고, 그녀에 대해 새로운 두려움을 자아낼 만한 일도 일어나지 않았다. 그럼에도 크로프츠 선생은 레이디데이에 그녀를 새집으로 옮기는 것은 온당치 않으리라는 의견을 내놓았다. 삼월은 병자에게 너그러운 달이 아니다. 그리하여 데일 부인 쪽에서는 얼마간의 아쉬움을 안고, 벨 쪽에서는 잔뜩 조바심을 내며, 릴리 자신에게서는 적잖이 노골적인 항의를 받으면서, 사월 한 달은 그대로 머물게 해 달라고 지주에게 청하게 되었다. 지주가 이 청을 어떻게 받아들였는지, 그리고 어떤 식으로 의사의 논리에 동의했는지는 한두 장 뒤에 이야기하겠다.\n\n그러는 동안 존 임스는 런던에서 제 앞길을 이어 가고 있었으나, 자신에게도, 스스로 그의 마음이 고른 여왕이라 뽐내는 그 아가씨에게도 이렇다 할 만족을 주지는 못했다. 어밀리아 로퍼 양은 참으로 심사가 사나워져 있었고, 그 심술 속에서 버턴 크레슨트에 무시무시한 분란을 자아낼 만한 놀음을 벌이고 있었다. 그녀는 조니 임스의 코앞에서, 게다가 루펙스 부인의 눈앞에서까지 크래들 씨와 시시덕거리는 데 온 힘을 쏟고 있었다. 이 멍청이 존 임스는 그것이 싫었다. 그는 무엇보다도 어밀리아와 그녀의 요구에서 벗어나기를 간절히 바랐다. 어찌나 간절했던지, 울적한 때면 스스로 런던 생활을 갖가지 비극적인 결말로 끝내겠다고 으르대곤 했다. 군에 들어가겠다. 오스트레일리아로 가겠다. 제 머리통을 쏘아 날려 버리겠다. 어밀리아와 “담판”을 지어, 너는 표독스러운 여자라고 말해 주고 제 증오를 선포하겠다. 앨링턴으로 달려 내려가 절망에 빠져 릴리의 발치에 몸을 던지겠다. 어밀리아는 그의 인생의 골칫덩이였다. 그런데도 그녀가 크래들과 시시덕거리자 그는 그것이 싫었고, 어리석게도 크래들에게 그 말을 꺼낼 만큼 얼간이였다.\n\n“물론 나야 상관없네,” 그가 말했다. “다만 자네가 스스로 바보짓을 하고 있는 것으로 보일 뿐이지.”\n\n“나는 자네가 그 여자를 떼어 내고 싶어 하는 줄 알았는데.”\n\n“그 여자야 나한테 아무것도 아니지. 다만 그게 말이야, 자네도 알다시피—”\n\n“그게 어떻다는 건가?” 크래들이 물었다.\n\n“아니, 내가 저 유부녀와 시시덕거린다면 자네도 좋아하지 않을 것 아닌가. 그뿐이야. 자네 그 여자와 혼인할 셈인가?”\n\n“뭐라고!—어밀리아 말인가?”\n\n“그래, 어밀리아 말일세.”\n\n“내가 알기로는 아니지.”\n\n“그렇다면 나 같으면 그냥 내버려 두겠네. 그 여자는 자네를 놀리고 있을 뿐이야.”\n\n임스의 충고는 옳았을지 모르고, 어밀리아의 행실에 대한 그의 판단도 정확했을지 모른다. 그러나 이 일에서 제 몫에 관한 한 그는 현명하지 못했다. 로퍼 양이 그를 질투하게 만들고 싶어 한 것은 의심할 여지가 없고, 그녀는 그가 자기를 싫어하고 마음이 다른 데 가 있는데도 기어이 그 일을 해냈다. 그는 로퍼 양에게 다정한 말을 건넬 마음이 조금도 없었다. 로퍼 양은 온갖 재주를 다 부려도 일주일에 한 번 그에게서 살가운 말 한마디를 끌어내지 못했다. 그런데도 다른 쪽에서 그녀에게 오가고 그녀에게서 나오는 다정한 말들은 그를 불편하게 했다. 사정이 이러하니, 존 임스가 아직도 제 풋내기 시절의 어리석음 속에서 허우적거리고 있었다는 것을 우리는 인정해야 하지 않겠는가?\n\n이 무렵 루펙스 부부는 나가 달라는 통고를 여러 차례 받고도 여전히 크레슨트에 눌러앉아 있었다. 로퍼 부인은 그들이 제게 진 빚을 죄다 포기하겠다고 늘 말하면서도, 사람이 으레 그러하듯 제 돈에 미련을 버리지 못했다. 그리고 통고를 할 때마다 밀린 값을 내라는 요구가 따라붙었고 대개 그 덕에 얼마쯤은 받아 낼 수 있었으므로, 일은 한 주 한 주 그렇게 흘러갔다. 그리하여 사월 초에도 루펙스 씨 부부는 여전히 로퍼 부인네 하숙인이었다.\n\n임스는 크리스마스에 다녀온 뒤로, 그리고 그에 이어 드 게스트 경과 편지를 주고받은 뒤로 앨링턴에서 아무 소식도 듣지 못했다. 어머니의 편지로는 게스트윅 저택에서 사냥한 고기가 자주 온다는 것을 알았고, 그것으로 백작이 자기를 잊지 않았다는 것은 알 수 있었다. 그러나 릴리에 대해서는 한마디도 듣지 못했다—다만 이제는 널리 퍼진 소문, 곧 작은 집의 데일네가 게스트윅으로 옮겨 갈 참이라는 소문만은 예외였다. 처음 그 소식을 들었을 때 그는 그것을 자기에게 이로운 일로 풀이하여, 앨링턴의 그 위엄에서 벗어난 릴리라면 어쩌면 제 손이 좀 더 쉽게 닿는 데 있으리라 생각했다. 그러나 요즈음에 와서는 그런 희망을 아주 버리고, 저택의 벗이 그 혼사를 이루어 줄 생각을 죄다 접었다고 스스로에게 말하고 있었다. 그가 다녀온 지 벌써 석 달이 지났다. 크로즈비가 제 권리를 내던진 지는 다섯 달이 지났다. 크로즈비 같은 악한쯤이야 다섯 달이면 잊힐 만하지 않은가! 지주를 통해 밟을 수 있는 걸음이 있었다면 석 달이면 충분하고도 남았을 것 아닌가! 앨링턴에 제 희망의 터전이 없다는 것은 그에게 아주 뚜렷해 보였고, 그렇다면 오스트레일리아로 떠나 버리는 편이 옳을 성싶었다. 오스트레일리아로 가겠다, 그러나 그 전에 어밀리아 로퍼의 일에 감히 끼어든 죄로 크래들을 두들겨 패 주겠다. 사월 첫 주 동안 그의 마음은 대체로 그런 상태였다.\n\n그러다가 백작에게서 편지 한 통이 왔고, 그것이 그의 온갖 감정에 대번에 큰 변화를 일으켰다. 오스트레일리아를 한낱 꿈으로 여기게 만들었고, 크래들에게마저 하마터면 좋은 낯을 짓게 할 뻔했다. 백작은 앨링턴에서의 제 벗의 이해관계를 결코 놓아 버리지 않았던 것이다. 게다가 그 이해관계에는 이제, 이 일에서 백작보다 훨씬 더 큰 힘을 지녔다고 보아야 할 원군이 붙어 있었다. 지주가 임스와의 혼사에 동의를 내준 것이다.\n\n백작의 편지는 이러했다.—\n\n게스트윅 저택, 18--년 4월 7일.\n\n다시 편지하라고 내가 일렀는데 자네는 그러지 않았네. 요전 날 자네 어머니를 만났기에 망정이지, 그러지 않았더라면 자네가 죽었는지 살았는지도 내가 알 길이 없었을 걸세. 젊은이란 편지하라는 말을 들었으면 언제나 편지를 해야 하는 법이야.\n\n임스는 여기까지 읽고서 이 꾸중이 제법 억울하게 느껴졌다. 자기가 후원자에게 편지하지 않은 것은 그저 편지 따위로 그를 성가시게 하고 싶지 않아서였음을 알고 있었기 때문이다. “맹세코, 그분이 나한테 진저리를 내실 때까지 매주 편지를 써 드려야지.” 젊은이의 도리에 관해 이런 가르침을 받고 나서 조니는 혼자 이렇게 중얼거렸다.\n\n이제 자네에게 긴 이야기를 하나 해야겠는데, 자네가 여기 내려와 있다면 훨씬 나았을 걸세. 그러면 나도 이 수고를 덜었을 테니 말이야. 하지만 자네를 기다리게 두면 나를 인정머리 없다 여길 테지. 요전 날 우연히 데일 씨를 만났는데, 어떤 젊은 아가씨가 내 어떤 젊은 벗의 말에 귀를 기울일 마음만 먹어 준다면 자기로서는 아주 기쁘겠노라고 하더군. 그래서 내가 그 아가씨의 재산은 어찌할 참이냐고 물었더니, 제가 사는 동안은 해마다 백 파운드를 주고 죽은 뒤에는 사천 파운드를 그 아이 앞으로 앉히겠노라고 하데. 나도 그 젊은이 쪽에 그만큼은 하겠다고 말했네. 다만 연 이백 파운드에 자네 봉급을 보태 봐야 살림을 시작하기에는 넉넉지 않을 터이니, 내 몫은 백오십으로 하겠네. 자네도 머잖아 관청에서 올라갈 테고, 연 오백 파운드면 그럭저럭 꾸려 갈 수 있을 게야. 더구나 자네는 생명 보험에 들 필요도 없으니 말일세. 나 같으면 처음에는 블룸즈버리 스퀘어 근처 어디쯤에 살겠네. 거기서는 집을 거저나 다름없이 얻는다고들 하더군. 따지고 보면 유행이라는 게 무슨 값어치가 있겠나? 가을에는 아내를 데리고 이리로 내려와 사냥이나 좀 하게. 그 사람이 자네를 나무 밑에서 잠들게 두지는 않을 테니, 내 장담하지.\n\n하지만 그 아가씨는 자네가 알아서 챙겨야 하네. 알아 두게, 아무도 그 아이에게 이 일을 말한 사람은 없네. 혹 누가 말했다 해도 나는 모르는 일일세. 지주가 자네 편이라는 것, 그게 전부야. 이번 부활절에 내려올 수는 없겠나? 늙은 버플에게 내 안부와 함께 자네가 필요하다고 전하게. 자네가 원한다면 내가 그에게 편지를 쓰겠네. 한때 그를 알기는 알았지, 썩 좋아한 적은 한 번도 없었다고 해야겠지만. 릴리 양을 만나지 않고서야 일이 될 리 없다는 것은 뻔한 이치 아닌가. 하기야 자네가 편지로 하는 편이 더 좋다면 몰라도, 내게는 그런 게 늘 아주 시시한 놀음으로 보인다네. 내려와서 예로부터 내려온 격식대로 구애를 하는 편이 훨씬 나아. 레이디 줄리아가 자네를 보면 기뻐하리라는 말은 새삼 할 것도 없네. 철도역에서의 그 일이 있은 뒤로 자네는 그 사람이 으뜸으로 아끼는 사람이 되었어. 그 사람은 황소 일보다 그 일을 훨씬 더 많이 입에 올린다네.\n\n자, 이 사람아, 이제 자네도 다 알았을 테니, 내 편지에 곧 답을 하지 않으면 나는 자네를 아주 야속하게 여길 걸세.\n\n자네의 참으로 진실한 벗,\n\n임스가 제 사무 책상에 앉아 이 편지를 다 읽었을 때, 그의 놀라움과 들뜬 마음은 어찌나 컸던지 자기가 어디 있는지도, 무엇을 해야 하는지도 도무지 알 수 없을 지경이었다. 릴리의 삼촌이 이 혼사를 이루자는 데 동의했을 뿐 아니라 조카딸에게 상당한 재산까지 주기로 약속했다는 것이 정말일 수 있을까? 몇 분 동안 조니에게는 제 행복을 가로막던 온갖 걸림돌이 죄다 치워지고, 자기와, 그때껏 감히 꿈꾸어 본 적도 없던 크기의 복락 사이에 아무 장애도 남아 있지 않은 것만 같았다. 그러다가 백작의 후한 마음을 헤아려 보고는 하마터면 울 뻔했다. 그는 생각을 가다듬을 수도, 편지를 쓰려고 손을 가눌 수도 없음을 깨달았다. 살아 있는 사람에게서 그런 금전의 아량을 받는 것이 옳은 일인지 알 수 없었고, 백작의 제안은 물리쳐야 마땅하지 않을까 하는 생각마저 들었다. 지주의 돈이야 받아도 된다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 젊은 여자의 재산이라는 이름으로 오는 것은 무엇이든 어떤 사내라도 받을 수 있는 법이다.\n\n백작의 편지에는 반드시, 그것도 당장 답을 하리라. 답을 쓰기 전에는 사무국을 나서지 않으리라. 제 벗이 그런 종류의 나무람을 다시는 그에게 던질 일이 없도록 하리라. 그러고서 그의 생각은 다시 편지 쓰는 일에서 자기가 당한 부당한 처사로 돌아갔다—지금 벌어진 것과 같은 형편에서 그런 것이 대수라도 되는 양 말이다. 그러나 마침내 그의 생각은 참으로 걸린 문제에 이르렀다. 릴리 데일이 자기를 받아 줄 것인가? 따지고 보면 이 행운이 실제로 이루어지느냐는 온전히 그녀의 마음에 달린 일이었고, 그것을 떠올리자 그의 마음은 몹시 켕겼다. 그 마음에는 젊은 연인이 으레 품는 의심—풋내기 시절의 수줍음에서 오는 두려움과 떨림—만이 아니라, 크로즈비와의 그 일이 여전히 제 앞을 가로막으리라는 생각까지 가득했다. 그는 릴리가 겪은 것을 아마 온전히 이해하지는 못했겠지만, 지난 다섯 달로도 아직 아물지 않은 상처가 있으리라는 짐작은 하고 있었다. 그 상처를 자기가 아물게 하도록 그녀가 허락해 줄 수 있을까? 그것을 생각하자 그는 어찌할 수 없는 수줍음과 제 보잘것없음에 대한 확신에 짓눌려 땅으로 꺼져 드는 듯했다. 릴리언 데일 같은 아가씨가 받아 줄 만한 무엇을 자기가 내놓을 수 있단 말인가?\n\n그날 국왕 정부가 존 임스의 봉급에 걸맞은 대가를 그에게서 받아 내지는 못했으리라고 나는 생각한다. 그러나 얼마 전부터 그가 내어 온 대가가 어찌나 걸맞았던지, 소득세 사무국에서는 그의 승진이 눈앞에 왔다는 말이 돌았고, 그 때문에 크래들이며 피셔며 그와 어깨를 나란히 하던 동무들이 적잖이 시샘을 했다. 그리고 소문이 그에게 돌려준 자리는 관리 사회에서 지상의 완벽한 엘리시움으로 여겨지는 것이었다. 소문에 따르면 그는 수석 위원의 개인 비서가 될 참이었다. 그런 변화가 오면 그는 지금 앉아 있는, 융단도 깔리지 않은 커다란 방에서 벗어나게 될 터였다. 그 방에서 그는 개 두 마리가 한 줄에 묶였을 때 느낄 법한 치욕스러운 결박을 느끼며 다른 사내와 같은 책상을 쓰고 있었다. 이 방은 사무국의 난장판이었다. 열두어 명, 아니 열네 명쯤 되는 사내들이 그 안에 앉아 있었다. 날마다 한 시가 되면 커다란 백랍 술잔들이 들어와, 방에 도무지 점잖다고는 할 수 없는 기색을 입혔다. 이 방의 고참으로 그 방을 제 손아귀에 두고 있다고들 하는 러브 씨는 옛 본을 딴 서기로, 굼뜨고 둔하고 야심이라고는 없으며, 이즐링턴 저쪽 바깥에 살고, 제 사무실 울타리 밖에서는 젊은 동료 누구에게도 알려진 바가 없는 사람이었다. 그가 이 방에 나쁜 기풍을 들여놓았다는 것이 일반의 평이었다. 게다가 이 방의 서기들은 관청의 한 멋쟁이에게, 곧 방금 말한 그 신사보다 나이는 어리되 지위는 훨씬 높은 키싱이라는 이름의 수석 서기에게 걸핏하면 호되게—그것도 아주 눈에 띄게 호되게—야단을 맞곤 했다. 그는 바로 옆 제 방에서 종종걸음을 치며 코를 치켜들고 사무실 슬리퍼를 끌면서 급히 들어오는데, 그 꼴은 볼 때마다 마치 관리 사회 전체가 당장 결딴날 까닭이라도 있는 듯했다. 그러고는 큰 방의 어떤 이들로서는 견디기 쉽지 않은 모진 말들을 사방에 내리쳤다. 그의 머리털은 늘 곧추 세워 빗어 넘겨져 있었고, 두 눈은 늘 아주 크게 열려 있었다. 그리고 그는 대개 커다란 편지 대장을 들고 다니며 손가락으로 어느 자리를 짚어 두었다. 이 책은 그의 힘에 부칠 만큼 무거웠으므로 그는 그것을 이 사람 책상에, 저 사람 책상에 쿵 소리 나게 내려놓곤 했고, 그렇게 오랜 세월 쿵쿵 내려놓은 끝에 스스로를 몹시 미움받는 사람으로 만들어 놓았다. 무슨 해묵은 원한이 있어 그와 러브 씨는 서로 말을 하지 않았다. 그런 까닭에 잘못을 캘 때마다 야단맞는 젊은이는 키싱 씨를 그의 원수에게 떠넘기곤 했다.\n\n“나는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모릅니다,” 러브 씨는 한순간도 책상에서 얼굴을 들지 않은 채 이렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n“나는 반드시 이 문제를 위원회에 올리겠소,” 키싱 씨는 이렇게 대꾸하고는 그 큰 책을 들고 방을 끌 듯이 나가곤 했다.\n\n이따금 키싱 씨는 정말로 그 문제를 위원회에 올렸고, 그러면 그와 러브 씨와 잘못을 저지른 서기 두엇이 그리로 불려 갔다. 그것이 큰일로 번지는 일은 좀처럼 없었다. 잘못한 서기들은 훈계를 들었다. 위원 하나가 러브 씨에게 조용히 한마디 하고, 또 하나가 키싱 씨에게 조용히 한마디 했다. 그러고 나서 저희끼리만 남으면 위원들은 조그만 농지거리를 즐겼다. 아무래도 키싱은 편애로 되는 것 같으니 러브야말로 여전히 만물의 주인이어야 하지 않겠느냐는 것이었다. 그러나 이런 일들은 래플 버플 경이 위원회에 오기 전, 온화하던 시절의 일이었다.\n\n처음에는 이 일에 얼마쯤 재미가 있었다. 그러나 근래에 와서 존 임스는 그것이 시들해졌다. 그는 키싱 씨가 싫었고, 키싱 씨가 늘 그것으로 자기의 무슨 관청 죄악을 잡아내려 드는 그 큰 책도 싫었으며, 키싱과 러브를 서로 물어뜯게 붙여 놓는 그 장난에도 진력이 나 있었다. 부서기관이 처음 그에게 래플 경이 그를 개인 비서로 고를 뜻이 있다고 귀띔했을 때, 그리고 그런 경우에 제 몫으로 돌아올, 융단이 깔리고 가죽 팔걸이의자와 따로 놓인 세면대가 있는 아늑한 작은 방을 떠올렸을 때, 게다가 연 백 파운드가 더 들어오고 그보다 더 나은 승진의 길목에 서게 되리라는 것까지 떠올렸을 때, 그는 뛸 듯이 기뻤다. 그러나 걸리는 것이 몇 가지 있었다. 지금의 개인 비서는—전임 수석 위원의 개인 비서이기도 했던 사람인데—래플 경의 목소리를 견딜 수 없어서 그 엘리시움을 내놓는 참이었다. 래플 경은 개인 비서에게 굽실굽실 시중드는 일을 온당한 정도보다 훨씬 많이 요구한다는 것이었고, 임스도 제가 그 자리에 맞는지 적잖이 의심스러웠다.\n\n“그런데 그분이 왜 저를 고르시는 걸까요?” 그는 부서기관에게 물었었다.\n\n“글쎄, 우리끼리 그 일을 의논해 보았는데, 내 생각에 그분은 이름이 오르내린 다른 누구보다 자네를 마음에 두고 계신 것 같네.”\n\n“하지만 철도역에서의 그 일로 저를 그렇게 호되게 다루셨는걸요.”\n\n“그 뒤에 그 일에 대해 더 들으신 것이 있는 모양이야. 자네 벗인 드 게스트 백작에게서 무슨 전갈이 그분께 갔던 것 같네.”\n\n“아, 그렇군요!” 조니는 백작을 벗으로 둔다는 것이 어떤 것인지 비로소 깨닫기 시작하며 말했다. 그 귀족과 알고 지내게 된 뒤로 그는 사무국에서 백작의 이름을 입에 올리는 일을 애써 피해 왔건만, 그 사실이 거기서 아주 잘 알려져 있고 적잖이 무게 있게 받아들여지고 있다는 낌새를 거의 날마다 받고 있었다.\n\n“하지만 그분은 너무 거칠지 않습니까,” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“그쯤이야 참을 수 있네,” 그의 벗인 부서기관이 말했다. “자네도 알다시피 그분은 짖는 소리가 무는 것보다 사납지. 게다가 연 백 파운드는 탐낼 만한 것이고.” 그때의 임스는 세상만사를 우울하게 보는 쪽으로 기울어 있었고, 그 자리를 제안받더라도 물리칠 마음이었다. 그때는 아직 백작의 편지를 받기 전이었다. 그러나 이제, 그 편지를 펼쳐 놓고—책상 밑 서랍에 넣어 두어 의자에 등을 기대고서도 여전히 읽을 수 있게 해 놓고—앉아 있는 지금은, 세상만사를 사뭇 다른 공기 속에서 바라볼 수 있었다. 우선 릴리언 데일의 남편이라면 융단과 팔걸이의자가 있는 제 방 하나쯤은 있어야 마땅했다. 그리고 그 연 백 파운드가 더 붙으면 그의 수입은 단번에 백작이 어떤 식으로든 조건 삼았던 그 액수에 이를 터였다. 그런데 부활절에 그 휴가를 얻을 수 있을까? 래플 경의 개인 비서가 되기로 승낙한다면 그것을 흥정의 한 조건으로 삼으리라.\n\n바로 이때 큰 방의 문이 열리더니 키싱 씨가 아주 빠른 종종걸음으로 끌 듯이 들어왔다. 그는 끌 듯이 들어와 곧장 존의 책상으로 다가와서는, 그 임자가 그 귀한 편지가 든 서랍을 닫을 겨를도 갖기 전에 장부를 그 위에 쿵 내려놓았다.\n\n“그 서랍에 무엇을 넣어 두었소, 임스 씨?”\n\n“개인 편지입니다, 키싱 씨.”\n\n“오—개인 편지라!” 키싱 씨는 거기 소설책이 숨겨져 있으리라고 굳게 믿으면서도 감히 그 믿음을 입 밖에 내지는 못한 채 말했다. “나는 오늘 아침 내내 이 해군부 편지를 찾았는데, 임스 씨, 당신은 그걸 S 밑에 넣어 두었더군!” 곁에서 키싱 씨의 말투를 듣는 사람이라면 이렇게 드러난 끔찍한 죄악으로 소득세 사무국 전체가 위태로워졌다고 여겼을 것이다.\n\n“서머싯 하우스니까요,” 조니가 변명했다.\n\n“쳇—서머싯 하우스라니! 런던의 관청 절반이—”\n\n“러브 씨에게 물어보시는 게 좋겠습니다,” 임스가 말했다. “그건 다 그분의 특별한 지시대로 한 것이니까요.” 키싱 씨는 러브 씨를 바라보았고, 러브 씨는 제 책상만 뚫어지게 바라보았다. “색인에 관한 것은 러브 씨가 다 아십니다,” 조니가 말을 이었다. “그분이 이 부서의 색인 총감독관이시죠.”\n\n“나는 아니오, 임스 씨,” 존 임스를 제법 좋아하고 키싱 씨는 진심으로 미워하던 러브 씨가 말했다. “하지만 대체로 이 방의 색인은 아주 잘 되어 있다고 나는 믿소. 편지를 찾을 줄 모르는 사람들이 있을 뿐이지.”\n\n“임스 씨,” 키싱 씨가 잘못 쓰인 그 S를 여전히 쓰디쓴 나무람의 손가락으로 짚은 채, 방 전체를 위하고 늙은 러브 씨를 짓뭉개기 위한 일장 연설을 시작했다. “해군부라는 낱말이 S가 아니라 A로 시작한다는 것을 아직도 배워야 한다면, 당신은 이 사무국에 처음 들어오기 전에 익혀 두었어야 할 것들을 아직 많이 배워야 하오. 서머싯 하우스는 부서가 아니오.” 그러고서 그는 방 전체를 향해 돌아서서 그 마지막 말을 되풀이했다. 잘 새겨 두면 아주 쓸모가 있으리라는 듯이. “—부서가 아니오. 재무부는 부서요. 내무부도 부서요. 인도청도 부서요—”\n\n“그건 아닙니다, 키싱 씨,” 방 저쪽 끝에 있던 젊은 서기 하나가 말했다.\n\n“내 말뜻이야 잘 알 것 아니오, 이 사람아. 인도부가 부서란 말이오.”\n\n“청이라는 건 없습니다, 키싱 씨.”\n\n“그건 아무래도 좋소. 하지만 관직에 석 달을—아니 삼 년이라고까지는 않겠소만—있어 본 신사가 어떻게 서머싯 하우스를 부서라고 여길 수 있는지, 나로서는 도무지 이해할 수가 없소. 잘못 배웠다면—”\n\n“그건 다음에 다 알게 되겠지요,” 임스가 말했다. “러브 씨가 그것을 각서로 적어 두실 겁니다.”\n\n“나는 그런 짓은 하지 않겠소,” 러브 씨가 말했다.\n\n“잘못 배웠다면—” 키싱 씨가 다시 말을 꺼내면서 곁눈으로 러브 씨를 훔쳐보았다. 그러나 바로 그때 문이 다시 열리더니 사환이 조니를 참으로 높으신 어른 앞으로 불렀다. “임스 씨, 래플 경께 대령하시랍니다.” 이 말을 듣자 조니는 곧바로 일어나, 키싱 씨와 그 큰 책이 제 책상을 차지하도록 남겨 두고 나갔다. 그 싸움이 어떻게 벌어졌으며 큰 방에서 얼마나 사납게 타올랐는지는 여기 머물러 들어 볼 겨를이 없다. 우리는 우리 주인공을 따라 래플 버플 경 앞으로 가야 하기 때문이다.\n\n“아, 임스—그래,” 젊은이가 들어서자 래플 경이 제 책상에서 얼굴을 들며 말했다. “잠깐만 기다리게, 응?” 그러고서 기사는 제가 하는 일이 조금이라도 미뤄지면 온 나라에 큰 해가 되기라도 할까 두려운 사람처럼 서류에 매달렸다. “아, 임스—음—그래,” 그는 쓰고 있던 서류를 거의 내동댕이치듯 밀쳐 놓으며 다시 말했다. “자네가 이 사무국 일을 제법 잘 안다고들 하더군.”\n\n“얼마쯤은 압니다, 위원장님,” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 그렇지. 얼마쯤은 말이야. 하지만 나한테 오려거든 그 전부를 알아야 하네. 게다가 아주 빈틈없이 해내야 해. 피츠하워드가 나를 떠나는 건 알고 있나?”\n\n“들었습니다, 위원장님.”\n\n“아주 훌륭한 젊은이지, 다만 어쩌면 그리—하지만 그 얘기는 접어 두세. 일이 그에게는 좀 벅찼던 게야. 그래서 사무국으로 돌아가는 걸세. 드 게스트 경이 자네 벗이라던데, 그런가?”\n\n“예, 분명히 제 벗이십니다. 저에게 아주 잘해 주셨지요.”\n\n“아, 그래. 나도 백작을 여러 해 알았지—아주 여러 해 말이야. 한때는 아주 가깝게 지냈고. 혹시 그가 내 이름을 입에 올리는 걸 들은 적이 있는가?”\n\n“예, 들었습니다, 래플 경.”\n\n“한때 우리는 가까웠지. 하지만 그런 건 사그라지는 법 아닌가. 그는 시골 쥐가 되었고 나는 도시 쥐가 되었지. 하하하! 내가 그러더라고 그에게 전해도 좋네. 나한테서 나온 말이라면 그도 싫어하지 않을 게야.”\n\n“아, 그럼요. 조금도 싫어하지 않으실 겁니다,” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“보거든 꼭 전하게. 백작은 내가 늘 크게 존경해 온 사람이야—아주 크게 존경해 온—아니, 흠모해 왔다고 해도 좋겠지. 자, 임스, 피츠하워드의 자리를 맡는 것에 대해 자네는 어떻게 생각하나? 일은 고되다네. 그건 내가 말해 주는 것이 옳겠지. 일은 틀림없이 아주 고될 걸세. 나는 내 앞의 사람들보다 돌아가는 일을 더 많이 떠맡고 있고, 그렇게 해 줄 사람이 필요했기 때문에 사람들이 나를 여기로 보냈다는 것을 자네에게 말해도 나는 꺼릴 것이 없네.” 래플 경의 목소리는 말을 이어 갈수록 점점 더 거슬려졌고, 임스는 피츠하워드가 얼마나 슬기로웠던가를 생각하기 시작했다. “나는 내 도리를 다할 참이고, 내 개인 비서도 제 도리를 다하기를 바랄 걸세. 그러나 임스 씨, 나는 사람을 결코 잊지 않네. 좋은 사람이든 나쁜 사람이든 나는 사람을 결코 잊지 않아. 자네는 늦은 시간을 싫어하지는 않겠지?”\n\n“사무국에 늦게 나오는 것 말씀입니까? 아, 아닙니다, 조금도 싫어하지 않습니다.”\n\n“늦게까지 남는 것 말일세—늦게까지 남는 것. 필요하면 여섯 시 일곱 시까지도 말이야—마차가 진창에 빠지면 바퀴에 어깨를 대는 것 말일세. 그게 내가 평생 해 온 일이야. 사람들은 내가 어떤 사람인지 아주 잘 알고 있었지. 늘 험한 길에는 나를 남겨 두었으니까. 관리 사회에서 시간으로 삯을 쳐 주었더라면 나는 그 안의 누구보다도 많은 수입을 끌어냈으리라고 믿네. 옆방의 그 빈자리를 맡고 보면 그것이 결코 우스개가 아님을 알게 될 걸세. 그것만은 내가 말해 주는 것이 옳겠지.”\n\n“저도 남만큼은 열심히 일할 수 있습니다,” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“그래야지. 그래야지. 그 말을 지키게, 그러면 나도 자네를 지켜 주겠네. 내 오랜 벗 드 게스트의 벗을 곁에 두는 것은 내게 아주 흐뭇한 일일 게야. 그렇게 말하더라고 그에게 전하게. 자, 이제 곧바로 멍에를 메는 것이 좋겠군. 피츠하워드가 저기 있네. 그에게 들어가 보게. 그리고 네 시 반 정각에 내가 자네 둘을 보겠네. 나는 아주 정확한 사람이야, 명심하게—아주 정확하지. 그러니 자네도 정확해야 하네.” 그러고서 래플 경은 혼자 있고 싶어 하는 낯빛을 지었다.\n\n“래플 경, 한 가지 청드릴 것이 있습니다,” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“그게 뭔가?”\n\n“부활절 무렵에 두 주나 세 주쯤 자리를 비우기를 간절히 바라고 있습니다. 열흘쯤 뒤에 떠나고 싶습니다.”\n\n“의회 일이 시작되는 부활절에 세 주를 비운다고! 개인 비서로서는 될 일이 아니지.”\n\n“하지만 아주 중요한 일입니다, 래플 경.”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소릴세, 임스. 도무지 말도 안 돼.”\n\n“저에게는 거의 죽고 사는 일입니다.”\n\n“거의 죽고 사는 일이라. 아니, 대체 무엇을 하려는 겐가?” 그 온갖 위엄과 나라의 중대함을 지녔으면서도 래플 경은 자잘한 사람들의 일에 몹시 궁금해하는 사람이었다.\n\n“글쎄요, 그건 딱히 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다. 저 자신도 아주 확실하지는 않고요.”\n\n“그렇다면 허튼소리는 그만두게. 하필 그맘때에 내 개인 비서를 놓아 준다는 건 있을 수 없는 일이야. 나는 그리 못 하네. 공무가 그것을 허락하지 않아. 자네는 그 철에 휴가를 받을 자격이 없네. 개인 비서는 언제나 가을에 휴가를 얻는 법이야.”\n\n“가을에도 자리를 비우고 싶습니다만—”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소릴세, 임스 씨.”\n\n그러자 존 임스는 이런 위급한 때에는 제 큰 대포를 쏘아 올리는 것이 마땅하다고 생각했다. 그는 이 큰 대포를 쏘는 것이 몹시 싫었지만, 스스로에게 말했듯이 큰 대포가 꼭 필요해지는 때라는 것이 있는 법이었다. “오늘 아침 드 게스트 경에게서 편지를 받았는데, 부활절에 자기에게 와 달라고 몹시 조르십니다. 볼일에 관한 일입니다,” 조니가 덧붙였다. “어려움이 있거든 자기가 위원장님께 편지를 쓰겠노라고 하셨습니다.”\n\n“나한테 편지를 쓴다고,” 래플 경이 말했다. 그는 제 사무실에서 백작에게라도 너무 허물없이 다가오는 것은 달가워하지 않는 사람이었다.\n\n“물론 그렇게 하시라고 제가 말씀드릴 리는 없습니다. 하지만 래플 경, 제가 저 바깥 사무국에 그대로 남아 있다면,” 조니는 고갯짓으로 큰 방 쪽을 가리켰다, “사월을 제 휴가 달로 고를 수 있습니다. 그리고 이 일은 저에게도 백작님께도 아주 중요한 일이니—”\n\n“대체 무슨 일인가?” 래플 경이 말했다.\n\n“그건 아주 사사로운 일입니다,” 존 임스가 말했다.\n\n이에 래플 경은 흥정을 당하고 있다는 느낌에 몹시 심사가 뒤틀렸다. 이 젊은이가 어떤 조건이라야만 제 개인 비서가 되겠다는 것 아닌가! “자, 이제 피츠하워드에게 들어가 보게. 이런 일로 내 하루를 다 잃을 수는 없네.”\n\n“하지만 부활절에는 떠날 수 있게 되는 것입니까?”\n\n“모르겠네. 두고 봄세. 하지만 지금은 거기 서서 말을 늘어놓지 말게.” 그러고서 존 임스는 피츠하워드의 방으로 들어갔고, 그 신사에게서 임명을 축하한다는 인사를 받았다. “하인처럼 시도 때도 없이 종소리에 불려 다니는 걸 자네가 좋아하기를 바라네. 그분은 노상 그 종을 울려 대시거든. 그리고 자네 귀가 먹먹해지도록 고함을 지르실 걸세. 만찬 약속은 죄다 포기해야 해. 할 일이 그리 많지도 않은데 자네를 절대 놓아 주지 않으실 테니까. 아무도 그분을 만찬에 청하지 않는 것 같더군. 그분은 일곱 시까지 여기 있는 걸 좋아하시니까. 그리고 자네는 높으신 분들에 관해 온갖 거짓말을 다 써야 할 걸세. 그리고 이따금 래퍼티를 제 사삿일로 내보내고 나면 자네더러 신발을 가져오라고 하실 게야.” 그런데 래퍼티는 수석 위원의 사환이었다.\n\n그러나 이 짧은 이야기가 자리에서 밀려나 낙담한 개인 비서의 입에서 나온 것임은 잊지 말아야 한다. “사람이 남의 신발을 가져오라는 말을 듣게 되는 것은,” 임스는 속으로 이렇게 말했다. “스스로 그런 일에 알맞은 사람임을 내보였을 때뿐이지.” 그러고서 그는 제 가슴속에서 래플 경의 신발에 관해 조그만 결심을 하나 세웠다.\n\n# 제47장. 새 개인 비서\n\n소득세 사무국, 18--년 4월 8일.\n\n백작님의 편지에 어떻게 답을 드려야 할지 저로서는 참으로 모르겠습니다. 너무나 다정하신—다정하다는 말로도 모자란 편지였습니다. 그리고 진작 글을 올리지 못한 일에 대해서는—편지로 백작님을 번거롭게 해 드리고 싶지 않았다는 것을 말씀드려야겠습니다. 자주 써 보냈더라면 주제넘게 파고드는 것처럼 보였을 테니까요. 정말이지 백작님을 생각하지 않아서 그런 것은 아니었습니다. 그리고 무엇보다 먼저 돈에 관해서—그러니까 백작님의 제안에 관해서 말씀입니다. 저는 이 일에 대해 어리보기처럼 보이지 않으면서 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 정말로 모르겠습니다. 사실을 말씀드리자면 저는 제가 어떻게 해야 옳은지 알지 못하고, 다만 백작님께서 저를 그릇된 길로 이끌지 않으시리라 믿을 수밖에 없습니다. 저에게는 사람이란 제 아버지나 그와 같은 분에게서가 아니라면 돈을 선물로 받아서는 안 된다는 생각이 있습니다. 게다가 백작님께서 말씀하신 액수는 너무나 커서, 차라리 그 말씀을 꺼내지 않으셨더라면 하는 마음이 들 지경입니다. 그토록 너그러이 베푸실 뜻이시라면, 차라리 유언장에 그것을 저에게 남겨 주시는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까?\n\n“그러면 저 녀석은 늘 내가 죽어 가기를 바라게 되겠군.” 드 게스트 경이 누이에게 편지를 소리 내어 읽어 주며 말했다.\n\n“그이가 그런 걸 바랄 리가 없지요.” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다. “게다가 오라버니는 이십오 년은 더 사실 수도 있잖아요.”\n\n“쉰 해라고 하려무나.” 백작이 말했다. 그러고서 그는 편지 읽기를 이어 갔다.\n\n그러나 그 모든 것은 다른 한 사람에게 너무나 크게 달려 있어서, 이야기해 보아야 별 소용이 없습니다. 물론 저는 데일 씨께 몹시 감사드리고 있습니다—정말이지 몹시요—그리고 그분이 조카딸에게 참으로 후하게 처신하고 계시다고 생각합니다. 그러나 그것이 저에게 무슨 도움이 될지는 전혀 별개의 일입니다. 어쨌든 부활절에 초대해 주신 뜻은 반드시 받들겠고, 저에게 가망이 있는지 없는지 알아보겠습니다. 래플 버플 경이 저를 자기 개인 비서로 삼았다는 것을 말씀드려야겠습니다. 그 덕에 연 백 파운드를 더 받게 되었습니다. 그분은 여러 해 전에 백작님과 아주 절친한 사이였다고 하면서, 백작님 이야기 하기를 무척 좋아하시는 듯합니다. 그 모든 것이 무슨 뜻인지는 백작님께서 아실 겁니다. 그분이 백작님께 전해 달라는 말씀을 어찌나 많이 하시던지요. 하지만 백작님께서 그것을 받고 싶어 하시리라고는 생각하지 않습니다. 저는 내일부터 그분께 가야 하는데, 들리는 말로 미루어 보면 어지간히 고생을 하게 될 듯합니다.\n\n“암, 정말 그럴 게다.” 백작이 말했다. “가엾은 녀석!”\n\n“하지만 개인 비서란 할 일이 도무지 없는 자리인 줄 알았는데요.” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다.\n\n“나 같으면 래플 경의 개인 비서 노릇은 하고 싶지 않다. 하지만 그 애는 젊고, 연 백 파운드는 대단한 것이지. 우리가 다들 그 사람을 얼마나 미워했던지. 그 목소리는 금이 간 종소리 같았어. 우리는 늘 누가 저 버플에 머플러라도 좀 씌워 주지 않나 하곤 했지. 그 사람 사무국에서는 그를 허플 스커플이라고 부른다더구나. 가엾은 조니!” 그러고서 그는 편지를 끝까지 읽었다.\n\n저는 그분께 부활절에는 반드시 휴가를 얻어야겠다고 말씀드렸고, 그분은 처음에는 그것이 될 수 없는 일이라고 못 박으셨습니다. 그러나 그 일만큼은 제 뜻을 관철하겠습니다. 총리대신의 개인 비서 자리를 준다 해도 저는 내려가는 일을 그만두지는 않겠습니다. 그러면서도 제가 가 보아야 이룰 것이 없으리라 생각하면, 차라리 가지 않는 편이 나을지도 모르겠다는 마음이 거의 들기도 합니다.\n\n레이디 줄리아께 안부를 전해 주시고, 제가 그분께 얼마나 큰 은혜를 입고 있는지도 말씀드려 주십시오. 백작님께 진 감사의 마음은 이루 다 표현할 길이 없습니다. 부디 저를 믿어 주십시오, 친애하는 드 게스트 백작님, 언제나 지극히 충실한 벗으로부터,\n\n임스가 편지를 다 쓴 것은 늦은 시각이었다. 그는 큰 방에서 떠날 채비를 하며 제 책상과 서류를 후임자에게 넘겨줄 수 있도록 정리해 두던 참이었다. 다섯 시 반쯤에 크래들이 그에게 다가와 함께 걸어서 집으로 가자고 했다.\n\n“아니! 자네 아직 여기 있었나?” 임스가 말했다. “자네는 늘 네 시면 가는 줄 알았는데.” 크래들은 새 개인 비서와 함께 걸어서 집에 가려고 사무실 언저리를 서성이며 남아 있었던 것이다. 그러나 임스는 그것을 바라지 않았다. 그에게는 혼자서 생각해 보고 싶은 것이 많았고, 저 혼자 걷도록 내버려 두어 주기를 바라는 마음이 간절했다.\n\n“그래, 할 일이 좀 있었네. 여보게 조니, 진심으로 축하하네. 정말이야.”\n\n“고맙네, 이 사람아!”\n\n“대단한 일 아닌가, 그렇지 않나. 연 백 파운드가, 그것도 단번에! 게다가 그렇게 아늑한 방을 혼자 쓰게 되고—그 키싱이란 작자는 자네 근처에 얼씬도 못 하게 되지. 그자는 오늘 하루 종일 그렇게 짐승처럼 굴었다네. 하지만 조니, 나는 자네가 언젠가 여느 사람 이상이 되리라는 걸 늘 알고 있었어. 늘 그렇게 말했지.”\n\n“여느 사람 이상이랄 것도 없네. 다만 피츠 말로는 늙은 허플 스커플이 여느 사람 이상으로 고약하게 군다더군.”\n\n“피츠가 무어라 하든 마음에 두지 말게. 죄다 시샘일 뿐이야. 자네가 눈치껏 하면 죄다 자네 뜻대로 될 걸세. 내 보기에 자네는 늘 죄다 자네 뜻대로 하지 않던가. 다 됐나?”\n\n“글쎄—아직 다는 아닐세. 나를 기다리지 말게, 코들.”\n\n“아, 나는 기다리겠네. 기다리는 거야 아무렇지도 않아. 우리 둘 다 늦으면 저녁을 남겨 둘 걸세. 게다가 그게 뭐 대수인가? 자네를 위해서라면 그보다 더한 일도 하겠네.”\n\n“여덟 시까지 일을 더 하다가 양갈비나 시켜다 먹을까 하네.” 조니가 말했다. “게다가—혼자서 들러야 할 데가 있어.”\n\n그러자 크래들은 하마터면 울 뻔했다. 그는 두세 분 동안 잠자코 있으면서 제 감정을 억누르려 애썼고, 마침내 입을 열었을 때에도 그것을 제대로 억누르지는 못한 채였다. “아, 조니.” 그가 말했다. “그게 무슨 뜻인지 나도 아네. 자네는 세상에서 올라서게 되었으니 나를 내던지려는 게지. 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 늘 자네 곁에 붙어 있었네. 안 그런가?”\n\n“스스로 바보가 되지 말게, 코들.”\n\n“아무렴, 나는 그랬네. 그리고 그들이 나를 개인 비서로 삼았더라도 나는 자네에게 여전히 예전 그대로였을 걸세. 자네는 내게서 아무 변화도 찾지 못했을 거야.”\n\n“자네 참 얼간이로군. 내가 시내에서 저녁을 먹고 싶어 한다고 해서 내가 변했다는 건가?”\n\n“죄다 자네가 예전처럼 나와 함께 걸어서 집에 가고 싶어 하지 않기 때문이지. 내가 아무리 얼간이라도 그쯤은 보인다네. 하지만 조니—이제는 자네를 조니라고 불러서는 안 되겠지.”\n\n“그렇게 빌어—먹을—” 그러고서 임스는 일어나 방 안을 이리저리 걸었다. “가세.” 그가 말했다. “남아 있고 싶지도 않고, 어디서 저녁을 먹든 상관없네.” 그러고는 모자와 장갑을 챙겨 부산히 나섰으니, 그가 거리로 나가기 전에 크래들이 그를 따라잡을 겨를도 거의 주지 않았다. “내 말해 두겠는데 말이야, 코들.” 그가 말했다. “그런 식으로 구는 건 죄다 역겨워.”\n\n“하지만 자네라면 어떤 기분이겠나.” 크래들이 훌쩍이며 말했다. 철도역에서의 그 눈부신 승리가 있은 뒤로 그는 제 스스로 매기는 눈으로 보아도 제 벗과 어깨를 나란히 한다고는 도무지 여길 수 없게 되어 있었다. 조니가 크로즈비를 두들겨 팬 것처럼 저도 루펙스를 두들겨 팰 수만 있었더라면, 그때야말로 둘은 대등했을 것이다—한 쌍의 영웅으로서. 그러나 그는 그렇게 하지 못했다. 그는 스스로에게 제가 겁쟁이라고 말한 적은 한 번도 없었으나, 사정이 유독 제게만 야박했다고 여기고 있었다. “하지만 자네라면 어떤 기분이겠나.” 그가 훌쩍이며 말했다. “세상 누구보다도 좋아하는 벗이 자네에게 등을 돌린다면 말일세.”\n\n“나는 자네에게 등을 돌린 적이 없네. 자네를 좀 빨리 걷게 할 수가 없다는 것만 빼면 말이야. 어서 오게, 이 사람아. 그리고 빌어먹을 헛소리 좀 하지 말게. 나는 그런 식으로 구는 건 죄다 질색이야. 사람이 으스대리라고 미리 짐작해서는 안 되는 법일세. 정말 으스댈 때까지 기다려야지. 내가 늙은 스커플스 밑에 한두 달 넘게 붙어 있을 것 같지도 않네. 들리는 말을 죄다 모아 보면 누구든 견딜 수 있는 것이 딱 그만큼이라더군.”\n\n그러자 크래들은 차츰 기분이 좋아지고 다정해져서, 걷는 내내 제가 부릴 줄 아는 온갖 아첨을 다해 임스를 추어올렸다. 그리고 조니는 “그런 식으로 구는 것은 죄다” 질색이라고 스스로 공언하면서도 아첨에는 마음이 열려 있었다. 피츠하워드는 그 타타르 같은 기사 나리를 다루지 못했지만 자네라면 아마 다룰 수 있을 것이라고 크래들이 말하자, 그는 크래들의 말을 믿고 싶은 쪽으로 기울었다. “그리고 그분 신발을 갖다 드리는 일로 말하면,” 크래들이 말했다. “몹시 급한 일이 있다든가 그런 때가 아니고서야 그분이 자네더러 그런 일을 하라고 할 생각은 아예 하지 않으실 걸세.”\n\n“여보게 조니.” 버턴 크레슨트에 잇닿은 거리 하나로 접어들면서 크래들이 말했다. “내가 이 세상에서 가장 하고 싶지 않은 일이 자네 기분을 상하게 하는 것이라는 걸 자네도 알지 않나.”\n\n“알았네, 코들.” 임스는 그렇게 말하고 계속 걸었다. 그의 동행은 걸음을 멈추려는 낌새를 보였는데도 말이다.\n\n“여보게, 자, 만일 내가 어밀리아 로퍼 일로 자네 속을 뒤집어 놓았다면, 다시는 그 사람에게 말을 걸지 않겠다고 약속하겠네.”\n\n“어밀리아 로퍼 따위 저—주나 받으라지.” 임스가 그렇게 말하며 문득 제 걸음을 멈추고 크래들의 걸음까지 멈추게 했다. 그 외침은 낮고 성난 목소리로 터져 나와 지나가던 한둘의 눈길을 끌었다. 조니는 아주 잘못했다—무릇 저주를 입에 담은 것부터가 잘못이고, 사람을 향해 그 저주를 내뱉은 것은 몹시 잘못이며, 여자를 향해—한때 사랑한다고 공언했던 여자를 향해 그것을 퍼부은 것은 특히나 잘못이었다! 그러나 그는 정말로 그렇게 했고, 나는 그 몹쓸 말을 되풀이하지 않고서는 내 이야기를 온전히 들려줄 수 없다.\n\n크래들은 그를 올려다보며 빤히 쳐다보았다. “내 말은 그저.” 크래들이 말했다. “그 일이라면 자네가 바라는 대로 무엇이든 하겠다는 것이었네.”\n\n“그럼 다시는 내 앞에서 그 사람 이름을 입에 올리지 말게. 그리고 그 사람에게 말을 거는 일이라면, 자네만 좋다면 둘 다 얼굴이 새파래지도록 이야기해도 좋아.”\n\n“아—몰랐네. 요전 날에는 자네가 그걸 좋아하지 않는 듯싶던데.”\n\n“요전 날의 나는 바보였네—빌어먹을 바보였어. 하기야 나는 평생을 그러고 살았지. 어밀리아 로퍼라니! 여보게 코들, 오늘 저녁에 그 사람이 자네에게 알랑거리거든—틀림없이 그럴 걸세, 요즘 그 놀음을 노상 벌이고 있는 듯하니—그저 제 마음껏 하게 내버려 두게. 나는 신경 쓰지 말고. 나는 루펙스 부인이나 스프루스 양과 재미나 보겠네.”\n\n“하지만 루펙스 부인 쪽에서 난리가 날 걸세. 어밀리아가 나에게 말을 걸기만 하면 그 사람은 벌써 더할 나위 없이 심통을 부린다네. 자네는 질투하는 여자가 어떤 것인지 모르는 걸세, 조니.” 크래들은 제가 자신 있다고 여기는 자리에 올라선 참이었다. 그리고 그 자리에서라면 그는 누구와 견주어도 제가 대등하다고 느꼈다. 임스는 사내 하나를 두들겨 팼고 저는 그러지 못한 것이 틀림없는 사실이었다. 임스가 개인 비서가 되어 세상에서 아주 높은 자리에 올라선 것도 사실이었다. 그러나 위험하고 알 수 없고 압도적이며 삶을 통째로 휘감는 밀회로 말하자면—그런 사건의 공인된 주인공이 바로 그가 아니던가? 그는 루펙스 부인과 어울리는 축복을 누리느라 주머니로도 편안함으로도 아주 비싼 값을 치렀다. 그러나 그는 제가 너무 비싼 값을 치렀다고는 좀처럼 여기지 않았다. 사람이 겪어 보면 값이 비싸다는 것을 알게 되는 사치가 있는 법이다. 그러나 그렇더라도 그런 것들은 제값을 할 수 있다. 그럼에도 로퍼 부인네 집 층계를 오르면서 그는 제 벗의 뜻을 들어주기로 마음먹었다. 그렇게 하면 그 밀회는 더욱 알 수 없고 더욱 삶을 휘감는 것이 될 터였다. 반면에 더 위험해지지는 않을 것이었으니, 루펙스 씨가 그런 일로 제가 해를 입었다고 여기기는 어려울 것이기 때문이었다.\n\n그날 저녁 식사에는 로퍼 부인네 하숙인이 하나도 빠짐없이 모였다. 루펙스 씨는 그 잔칫상에 좀처럼 끼지 않았으나, 이날만은 자리를 함께했고, 목소리며 몸가짐으로 보아 기분이 아주 좋은 듯했다. 크래들이 응접실에서 여러 사람에게 제 벗에게 떨어진 크나큰 행운을 알린 터라, 조니는 그로써 얼마간 영웅 대접의 과녁이 되어 있었다.\n\n“아이고, 그러셔요!” 로퍼 부인이 말했다. “그 소식을 들으시면 댁의 어머님은 참 행복한 분이 되시겠네요. 하지만 나는 늘 댁이 떨어져도 바른쪽이 위로 오게 떨어질 거라고 말했지요.”\n\n“행실이 고와야 곱다 하는 법이지요.” 스프루스 양이 말했다.\n\n“어머, 임스 씨!” 루펙스 부인이 우아한 열정을 담아 외쳤다. “제 마음 가장 깊은 곳에서 우러나와 기쁨을 빌어요. 참으로 품위 있는 자리로군요.”\n\n“참되고 사심 없는 벗의 손을 받아 주시오.” 루펙스가 말했다. 그리고 조니는 그 손을 정말로 받아 잡았다. 몹시 더럽고 온통 물감으로 얼룩져 있었는데도 말이다.\n\n어밀리아는 한쪽에 떨어져 서서 눈짓 하나로 축하를 전했다—아니, 여러 차례 이어진 눈짓으로 전했다고 하는 편이 나을 것이다. “이제는—이제는 제 사람이 되어 주지 않으시겠어요.” 그 눈짓들은 그렇게 말하고 있었다. “이렇게 부와 번영 속에 뒹굴게 되셨는데요?” 그러고서 아래층으로 내려가기 전에 그녀는 그에게 한마디를 속삭였다. “아, 저는 너무 행복해요, 존—정말 너무 행복해요.”\n\n“귀찮게!” 조니가 그 아가씨의 귀에 넉넉히 닿을 만큼 큰 소리로 말했다. 그러고는 방을 빙 돌아가 스프루스 양에게 제 팔을 내밀었다. 어밀리아는 혼자 층계를 내려가면서 저 사람의 가슴을 쥐어짜 놓고야 말겠다고 스스로에게 다짐했다. 그녀는 지난 며칠 동안 그것을 쥐어짜는 일에 매달려 왔고, 제 성공에 스스로도 놀라던 참이었다. 임스가 저의 크래들을 향한 수작에 발끈했다는 것은 그녀에게 넉넉히 분명한 일이었고, 그러니 그 놀음을 끝까지 밀고 나가야 했다.\n\n“어머, 크래들 씨.” 그의 곁에 자리를 잡으며 그녀가 말했다. “제가 좋아하는 벗은 언제나 한결같은 벗이랍니다. 저는 벼락출세라는 게 싫어요. 그런 게 사람을 우쭐하게 만드는 일이 너무 잦거든요.”\n\n“그래도 나 같으면 한번 겪어 보고 싶은데요.” 크래들이 말했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 제 생각에는 크래들 씨야 그런다고 달라지실 것 같지 않아요. 정말이지 그래요. 그리고 물론 크래들 씨에게도 그때가 오겠지요. 이번 일은 그 백작이라는 분이 해 주신 거예요—황소한테 혼쭐이 났던 그분 말이에요. 우리는 백작 한 분을 알게 된 뒤로 어찌나 대단해졌는지 몰라요.” 그러고서 어밀리아는 고개를 살짝 젖혔다가 짓궂게 미소를 지었는데, 크래들의 눈에는 그 모습이 정말이지 아주 잘 어울려 보였다. 그러나 그는 루펙스 부인이 식탁 건너편에서 저를 바라보고 있는 것을 알아차렸고, 그리하여 신들이 저에게 마련해 준 좋은 것들을 온전히 누리지는 못했다.\n\n부인들이 식당을 떠나자 루펙스와 두 젊은이는 의자를 난롯가로 끌어당기고 저마다 알맞은 양의 술을 한 잔씩 탔다. 임스는 방을 나가려고 조금 애를 써 보았으나, 루펙스가 우정을 어찌나 간곡히 내세우며 남아 달라고 애원하는지, 게다가 으스댄다는 소리를 들을까 하는 두려움에 어찌나 마음이 약해졌는지, 그는 시키는 대로 하고 말았다.\n\n“자, 임스 씨, 그대의 건강을 위하여.” 루펙스가 김이 무럭무럭 나는 물 탄 진 잔을 입으로 들어 올리며 말했다. “그리고 그대가 관직의 번영을 오래오래 누리기를 빌면서.”\n\n“고맙습니다.” 임스가 말했다. “번영이라는 게 어떤 건지야 잘 모르겠습니다만, 아무튼 고맙기는 매한가지입니다.”\n\n“그렇소, 젊은 양반. 그대 나이의 젊은이가 세상에서 올라서기 시작하는 것을 보면 나는 그가 계속 올라가리라는 것을 아오. 자, 나를 보시오, 임스 씨. 크래들 씨, 그대의 건강을 위하여. 그리고 온갖 야박한 마음일랑 이 넘치는 잔 속에 죄다 빠져 죽기를—나를 보시오, 임스 씨. 나는 세상에서 한 번도 올라선 적이 없소. 나는 세상에서 한 번도 좋은 일을 이룬 적이 없고, 앞으로도 없을 것이오.”\n\n“아, 루펙스 씨, 그런 말씀 마십시오.”\n\n“아니, 나는 그렇게 말하오. 나는 늘 악마의 꼬리를 잡아당기며 살아왔는데, 아직껏 그 꼬리조차 제대로 움켜쥐어 본 적이 없소. 그 까닭을 말해 주리다. 나는 젊은 시절에 기회를 한 번도 얻지 못했소. 내가 그대 나이였을 적에 어느 대단한 인물이, 그러니까 별 같은 사람이 제 초상을 그리게 해 주었더라면—이를테면 그대의 벗 래플 경 같은 이 말이오—”\n\n“대단한 별도 다 있군요!” 크래들이 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 그 사람도 세상에 어지간히는 이름이 알려져 있지 않소? 아니면 더비 경이라든가 스퍼전 씨라든가. 내 말뜻을 알 것이오. 젊은 시절에 그런 기회를 얻었더라면 나는 군소 극장에서 평방 야드당 얼마씩 받고 무대 배경이나 그리는 일은 결코 하고 있지 않았을 거요. 그대는 지금 그 기회를 얻었지만, 나는 한 번도 얻지 못했소.”\n\n그러고서 루펙스 씨는 첫 잔의 물 탄 진을 비웠다.\n\n“참으로 야릇한 것이오—인생이라는 것은.” 루펙스가 말을 이었다. 그리고 그는 대놓고 곧장 토디 한 잔을 더 타는 일에 나서지는 않았으나, 차츰차츰, 마치 본능이 시키는 대로인 양 그 일에 필요할 물건들을 만지작거리기 시작했다. “참으로 야릇한 것이지. 자, 젊은 양반들, 잘 새겨 두시오. 나는 인생의 성공이 올바른 처신에 달렸다는 것을 부정하는 게 아니오—물론 그렇지. 그러나 그렇다면, 올바른 처신이 성공에서 나오는 일은 또 얼마나 잦은가! 내가 화가가 되어 보겠다고 발버둥 치던 시절에 어느 대단한 사람이 내 손을 잡아 주었더라면, 그대들 생각에 내가 지금 이런 꼴이 되어 있었겠소? 나도 물 탄 진 못지않게 클라레와 샴페인을 마실 수 있었을 테고, 한때 나를 몹시 좋아했으면서 이제는 거리에서 만나도 말을 걸지 않는 숱한 자들 못지않게 셔츠에 주름 장식을 우아하게 달고 다닐 수 있었을 거요. 나는 기회를 한 번도 얻지 못했소—한 번도.”\n\n“하지만 아직 너무 늦지는 않았습니다, 루펙스 씨.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“아니오, 늦었소, 임스—아니오, 늦었소.” 그리고 이제 루펙스 씨는 진 병을 움켜쥔 참이었다. “이제는 너무 늦었소. 판은 끝났고 승부는 졌소. 재주는 여기 있소. 나는 그것만은 예나 지금이나 확신하오. 나는 내 능력을 의심해 본 적이 한 번도 없소—단 한순간도. 바로 오늘날 제 화판으로 연 천 파운드씩 벌어들이는 자들 가운데는 나만큼 그림에 밝고 참된 눈을 가지지도, 나만큼 좋은 색채 감각을 가지지도 못한 자들이 있소. 이름을 댈 수도 있지만, 대지는 않겠소.”\n\n“그러면 왜 다시 해 보시지 않습니까?” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“내가 사람의 눈을 즐겁게 한 것 가운데 가장 뛰어난 그림을 그린다 한들, 누가 와서 그것을 보아 주겠소? 그것이 정말인지 알아보러 이런 데까지 찾아올 만큼 나를 믿어 줄 사람이 누가 있겠소? 아니오, 임스. 나는 내 처지와 내 버릇을 알고, 내 약점도 아오. 나는 이제 하루 일이 끝날 때 몇 실링이 손에 들어오리라는 것이 확실하지 않으면 하루치 일도 못 하오. 일이 죄다 어긋나 버린 사람은 그렇게 되고 마는 법이오.”\n\n“하지만 무대 배경을 그려서 돈을 잔뜩 번다고 알고 있었는데요.”\n\n“그대가 무엇을 잔뜩이라고 부르는지는 모르겠소, 크래들 씨. 나는 그것을 잔뜩이라고 부르지 않소. 하지만 나는 푸념하는 게 아니오. 내가 누구에게 고마워해야 하는지는 내가 아오. 그리고 혹시라도 내가 제 머리통을 쏘아 날려 버리는 날이 온다 해도 나는 애먼 사람에게 허물을 씌우지는 않을 것이오. 내 충고를 받아들일 생각이 있거든,”—그러고서 그는 임스 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다—“인생에서 너무 일찍 혼인하는 일을 조심하시오.”\n\n“저는 사람이 잘 혼인하기만 한다면 일찍 혼인해야 한다고 생각합니다.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“내 말을 오해하지 마시오.” 루펙스가 말을 이었다. “엘 부인 이야기를 하는 게 아니오. 나는 언제나 내 아내를 아주 매혹적인 여자로 여겨 왔소.”\n\n“옳소, 옳소, 옳소!” 크래들이 식탁을 두드리며 말했다.\n\n“정말 그렇습니다.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 내가 그대에게 혼인을 조심하라고 이르더라도 오해하지는 마시오. 나는 어떤 사람에게도 그이를 헐뜯는 말을 한마디도 한 적이 없고 앞으로도 하지 않을 것이오. 제 아내 편에 서지 않는 사내가 대체 누구 편에 서겠소? 나는 나 자신 말고는 누구도 탓하지 않소. 그러나 이 말만은 하겠소. 나는 기회를 얻지 못했소—나는 기회를 얻지 못했소—기회를 얻지 못했소.” 그리고 그 말을 세 번째로 되뇔 때, 그의 입술은 이미 술잔 가장자리에 붙어 있었다.\n\n바로 이때 식당 문이 열리고 루펙스 부인이 고개를 들이밀었다.\n\n“루펙스.” 그녀가 말했다. “거기서 무얼 하고 계세요?”\n\n“그래, 여보. 지금 이 순간에 무얼 하고 있다고는 못 하겠구려. 이 젊은 양반들에게 조그만 충고를 좀 해 주던 참이오.”\n\n“크래들 씨, 어이가 없군요. 그리고 임스 씨, 임스 씨도 어이가 없어요—그것도 그런 자리에 오르신 분이! 루펙스, 당장 위층으로 올라오세요.” 그러고서 그녀는 방 안으로 들어와 진 병을 확보했다.\n\n“어머, 크래들 씨, 이리로 오세요.” 사내들이 위층에 모습을 드러내자마자 어밀리아가 더없이 활기찬 목소리로 말했다. “반 시간이나 기다렸답니다. 크래들 씨께 낼 수수께끼가 하나 있거든요.” 그러고서 그녀는 그가 제 자리와 벽 사이에 있는 의자로 가도록 길을 내주었다. 크래들은 권해진 자리에 앉으면서 제 앞날이 반쯤은 두려운 낯빛이었으나, 그래도 앉기는 앉았고, 이내 로퍼 양의 크리놀린이 지닌 굳건함과 너비 덕분에 어떤 실제적인 물리적 공격으로부터도 안전해졌다.\n\n“어머나! 이거 참 달라졌군요.” 루펙스 부인이 큰 소리로 말했다.\n\n조니 임스는 가까이 서 있다가 그녀의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. “변화란 때로 참 즐거운 것이지요! 그렇게 생각하지 않으세요? 저는 그렇습니다.”\n\n# 제48장. 응보\n\n크로즈비는 이제 결혼 생활의 잔잔한 현실에 자리를 잡았고, 한두 주 동안 자기에게 들러붙어 있던 미움도 차츰 사그라지고 있다고 여기기 시작했다. 그 미움은 얼마간은 그가 데일 양을 대접한 방식 탓이었지만, 그보다 더 크게는 그가 존 임스에게 두들겨 맞은 일 탓이었다. 그렇다고 그가 예전의 삶의 가락을 조금이라도 되찾았다거나, 되찾으리라 기대했다는 말은 아니다. 그러나 그는 이제 겸연쩍은 기색 없이 클럽을 드나들 수 있었다. 여느 때의 목소리로 이야기할 수 있었고, 사무국에서는 여느 때의 권위로 일을 처리할 수 있었다. 그는 벗들에게, 그 소리를 내는 데서 얼마쯤 즐거움을 느끼며, 레이디 알렉산드리나가 여러분을 뵈면 무척 기뻐할 것이라고 말할 수 있었다. 그리고 만찬을 마친 뒤에는 실내화를 신고 신문을 든 채 제 의자에 편안히 앉아 있을 수 있었다. 편안히 있을 수 있었다 — 적어도 아내에게 편안하다고 말할 수는 있었다.\n\n몹시 따분했다. 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각해 볼 때면 그는 자기가 꾸려 가는 삶이 따분하다는 것을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 성가신 일 없이 클럽에 들어갈 수는 있었지만, 그곳의 누구도 그에게 함께 만찬을 하자고 청할 생각은 하지 않았다. 그가 집에서 식사할 것이라는 게 당연한 일로 여겨졌고, 달리 그러지 않을 빌미가 없는 터라 그는 늘 집에서 식사했다. 이제 제집에 들어앉은 지 세 주가 되었고, 그동안 아내와 함께 신혼 축하 만찬에 몇 번 초대를 받았는데 대개 드 코시 집안의 벗들이 베푼 자리였다. 그런 경우를 빼고는 저녁나절을 제집 밖에서 보내는 법이 없었고, 혼인한 뒤로 아직 한 번도 아내와 떨어져 식사한 적이 없었다. 그는 이 점에서 자기가 처신을 잘하는 것은 스스로 다잡은 결심의 결과라고 되뇌었다. 그러면서도 그는 달리 할 일이 아무것도 남아 있지 않다는 것을 느꼈다. 아무도 그에게 극장에 가자고 청하지 않았다. 아무도 저녁에 잠깐 들르라고 조르지 않았다. 사내들은 어째서 카드 한 판 하지 않느냐고 그에게 묻는 법이 없었다. 그는 대개 사무국을 나선 뒤 세브라이트에 어슬렁어슬렁 들어가 반 시간쯤 방 안을 서성이며 몇몇 사내들과 이야기를 나누었다. 아무도 그에게 무례하지 않았다. 그러나 그는 모든 것이 달라졌음을 알았고, 얼마간 지혜롭게도, 바뀐 처지에 자기를 맞추기로 마음먹었다.\n\n레이디 알렉산드리나 또한 새 생활이 자못 따분하다고 여겼고, 이따금 좀 투덜거리고 싶어 했다. 그녀는 남편에게 자기는 도무지 바깥출입을 못 한다고 말하곤 했고, 그가 함께 걷자고 하면 길거리를 걷는 것은 좋아하지 않는다고 잘라 말하곤 했다. “그럼 대체 어디를 걸어야 한다는 건지 나로서는 잘 모르겠구려.” 언젠가 그가 그렇게 대꾸했다. 그녀는 자기가 승마를 좋아하며 공원이야말로 그런 운동에 아주 알맞은 곳이라는 말은 하지 않았다. 그러나 낯빛이 그렇게 말하고 있었고, 그는 그 뜻을 알아들었다. ‘저 사람을 위해 할 수 있는 일은 다 하겠다.’ 그는 속으로 말했다. ‘하지만 나를 거덜 내지는 않겠다.’ “어밀리아 언니가 마차 나들이를 시켜 주러 온대요.” 또 언젠가 그녀가 말했다. “아, 그거 아주 좋겠구려.” 그가 대답했다. “아뇨, 별로 좋지 않아요.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다. “어밀리아 언니는 늘 물건을 사들이고 장사치들과 값을 깎느라 바쁘거든요. 그래도 한 발짝도 못 나가고 집 안에 갇혀 있는 것보다는 낫겠죠.”\n\n두 사람의 아침 식사는 명목상 아홉 시 반이었다. 실은 언제나 열 시가 다 되어서였는데, 레이디 알렉산드리나가 제 방에서 몸을 빼내기를 힘겨워한 까닭이었다. 열 시 반이 되면 그는 어김없이 집을 나서 사무국으로 갔다. 대개 여섯 시까지는 집에 돌아왔고, 그러고는 만찬 전 한 시간의 태반을 옷 갈아입는 의식으로 보냈다. 적어도 그는 아내와 몇 마디를 나눈 뒤 제 옷방으로 들어가서는, 이것저것 끄집어내고, 손톱을 깎고, 손에 잡히는 종이란 종이는 다 훑어보며 시간을 죽이면서 그 안에 머물렀다. 그는 만찬이 일곱 시 정각에 나오기를 바랐고, 기다리게 되면 슬며시 짜증이 나기 시작했다. 만찬 뒤에는 아내와 함께 포도주를 한 잔 마시고, 다시 혼자서 한 잔을 마셨다. 그 뒤쪽 의식을 치르는 동안 그는 벌겋게 단 석탄을 물끄러미 바라보며 자기가 저지른 일을 생각하곤 했다. 그런 다음 위층으로 올라가 먼저 커피를 한 잔, 이어서 차를 한 잔 마셨다. 신문을 읽고, 책을 한두 권 펼치고, 하품이 나면 얼굴을 가리고, 자기가 이 생활을 좋아하는 척해 보려 애썼다. 그녀에게는 그를 향한 사랑의 몸짓도 말도 없었다. 그녀는 결코 그의 무릎에 앉는 법도, 그를 어루만지는 법도 없었다. 그의 곁에서 살도록 허락받은 것이 자기에게 어떤 행복이 되었다는 기색을 결코 그에게 내보이지 않았다. 두 사람은 서로 사랑한다고 여겼다. 각자가 그렇게 여겼다. 그러나 거기에는 사랑도, 마음의 나눔도, 온기도 없었다. 공기 자체가 차가웠다. 어떤 불로도 그 냉기를 걷어 낼 수 없을 만큼 차가웠다.\n\n레이디 알렉산드리나 대신 릴리 데일이 아내로서 그의 맞은편에 앉아 있었다면 무엇이 달랐을까? 그는 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 삶은 마찬가지였을 것이라고, 자기는 한동안 스스로를 가정생활에 맞지 않는 사람으로 만들어 놓았을 뿐이며 그 부적합을 스스로 고쳐야 한다고 자주 되뇌었다. 그러나 반쯤 소리 내어 굴리는 한 갈래의 생각으로 그렇게 잘라 말하면서도, 또 다른 갈래의 생각으로는 릴리라면 제 밝음으로 집안 전체를 환하게 만들었으리라고, 그녀를 제 화롯가로 데려왔더라면 아침마다 저녁마다 자기 위에 해가 비치고 있었으리라고 스스로에게 잘라 말하기도 했다. 그럼에도 그는 제 본분을 다하려 애썼고, 관청 생활의 흥분만은 아직 자기에게 열려 있다는 것을 떠올렸다. 아침 열한 시부터 오후 다섯 시까지, 그는 여전히 사람들이 자기를 존경으로 바라보고 공손하게 말을 걸지 않을 수 없게 만드는 자리를 지킬 수 있었다. 이 점에서 그는 아내보다 나은 처지였다. 그녀에게는 몸을 붙일 사무국이 없었으니까.\n\n“그럼요.” 그녀가 어밀리아에게 말했다. “다 아주 좋아요. 집이 눅눅한 것도 상관없고요. 그런데 혼자 있는 게 어찌나 지겨운지.”\n\n“사업하는 남자와 혼인한 여자라면 다 그럴 수밖에 없다오.”\n\n“아, 불평하는 건 아녜요. 물론 제가 무슨 일을 하는 건지 알고 한 거니까요. 다들 런던에 올라오면 이렇게까지 따분하지는 않겠지요.”\n\n“크리스마스가 지나고 나면 사교철이라고 우리한테 크게 달라지는 건 없더군요.” 어밀리아가 말했다. “하기야 오월의 런던이 더 흥겨운 건 틀림없지요. 내년에는 여기가 더 마음에 들 게요. 그리고 어쩌면 아기가 생길지도 모르잖소.”\n\n“쳇!” 레이디 알렉산드리나가 내뱉었다. “저는 아기 같은 거 갖고 싶지 않고, 생길 것 같지도 않아요.”\n\n“그래도 늘 할 일이 하나 생기는 거라오.”\n\n레이디 알렉산드리나는 기운찬 기질의 사람은 아니었으나, 자기가 잘못을 저질렀다는 것을 스스로 고백하지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀가 크로즈비와 혼인할 마음이 동한 것은 크로즈비가 사교계의 멋쟁이였기 때문인데, 이제 와서 런던의 사교철이 자기에게 아무 차이도 만들어 주지 않으리라는 말을 듣고 있었다. 그 런던의 사교철이란, 놀이의 만족까지는 주지 못했더라도 여태껏 언제나 그녀에게 모임의 흥분만은 가져다주던 것이었다. 그녀가 혼인할 마음이 동한 것은 또한, 혼인한 여자라면 어머니나 보호자에게 매인 처녀보다 덜 갑갑하게 사교를 누릴 수 있고 혼인한 여자로서 몸가짐이 한결 자유로우리라고 여겨졌기 때문이었다. 그런데 이제 와서는 무언가 할 일이 생기려면 아기가 생길 때까지 기다려야 한다는 말을 듣고 있었다. 코시 성도 이따금 따분하기는 했다. 그러나 코시 성이 이것보다는 나았을 것이다.\n\n아기에 관한 이 자잘한 이야기가 오간 뒤 크로즈비가 집에 돌아오자, 아내는 다음 일요일에 게이지비네에서 저녁을 먹기로 되어 있다고 그에게 알렸다. 이 말을 듣고 그는 짜증스레 고개를 저었다. 그러나 그는 자기에게 불평할 권리가 없다는 것을 알고 있었다. 신혼여행에서 돌아온 뒤로 세인트존스 우드에 끌려간 것이 겨우 한 번뿐이었으니까. 그렇지만 투덜거릴 만한 구석이 한 가지 있기는 했다. “대체 왜 하필 일요일에?”\n\n“어밀리아 언니가 일요일로 청했으니까요. 일요일로 청을 받았는데 월요일에 가겠다고 할 수는 없잖아요.”\n\n“일요일 오후는 지독하지 않소. 몇 시라던가요?”\n\n“다섯 시 반이라고 했어요.”\n\n“맙소사! 저녁 내내 대체 뭘 하란 말이오?”\n\n“어돌퍼스, 제 친정 식구들을 그런 식으로 말하는 건 좋지 않아요.”\n\n“여보, 그건 농담이잖소. 마치 내가 당신이 똑같은 말을 스무 번쯤 하는 걸 듣지 못하기라도 한 것처럼 구는구려. 그 집에 가야 한다고 나보다 훨씬 더 쓰디쓰게 투덜댄 건 당신이오. 내가 당신 언니를 좋아한다는 건 당신도 알 테고, 게이지비도 제 나름으로는 아주 좋은 사람이오. 그렇지만 서너 시간이 지나면 슬슬 그 사람은 그만하면 됐다 싶어지지 않소.”\n\n“여기 있는 것보다 더 따분하기야 하겠어요—” 그러나 레이디 알렉산드리나는 말을 다 맺기 전에 스스로 멈추었다.\n\n“집에서는 어쨌든 언제든 책이라도 읽을 수 있지 않소.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“늘 책만 읽고 있을 수는 없죠. 어쨌든 저는 당신이 가겠다고 말해 두었어요. 굳이 거절하시겠다면 편지를 써서 사정을 설명하셔야 할 거예요.”\n\n일요일이 오자 크로즈비 부부는 물론 세인트존스 우드로 갔고, 그가 그토록 싫어하는 그 문 앞에 다섯 시 반 정각으로 닿았다. 드 코시 집안과의 혼사를 처음 마음에 두었을 때 그가 세운 가장 이른 결심 가운데 하나는 게이지비네에 대해 아주 적대적인 것이었다. 그는 그들을 되도록 적게 보리라 했다. 그 인연에서 몸을 털어 내리라 했다. 그가 맺고자 한 것은 그 집안의 그 갈래가 아니었다. 그런데 이제 일이 흘러가고 보니, 그가 이어져 있는 듯 보이는 것은 그 집안에서 오직 그 갈래뿐이었다. 그는 늘 게이지비네 이야기를 듣고 있었다. 어밀리아와 알렉산드리나는 노상 붙어 지냈다. 그는 이제 일요일 만찬에 그리로 끌려가고 있었고, 앞으로도 자주 그리로 끌려가리라는 것을, 그런 끌려감을 피할 수 없으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 이미 모티머 게이지비에게 빚을 지고 있었고, 제 살림살이가 그 사무 변호사의 손아귀에 들어가되 다시는 빼낼 수 없는 모양새로 들어가 버렸다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그의 집에는 세간이 아주 빈틈없이 갖추어져 있었고 그 값이 모두 치러진 것도 알고 있었다. 그러나 그것을 치른 사람은 그가 아니었다. 한 실링까지 죄다 모티머 게이지비의 손을 거쳐 치러졌다.\n\n“드 코시, 너는 어머니랑 이모를 따라가거라.” 만찬이 끝난 뒤에도 자리를 뜨지 않고 뭉그적거리는 아이에게 그 사무 변호사가 말했다. 그리하여 크로즈비는 아내의 형부와 단둘이 남게 되었다. 그에게 그토록 끔찍한, 세인트존스 우드 연옥의 시간이 바로 이때였다. 처형과는 그래도 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다. 그녀가 백작의 딸이라는 것을 아마도 늘 잊지 않고 있었던 덕이리라. 그러나 게이지비와는 통하는 구석이 하나도 없었다. 게다가 그는 한때 자기를 몹시 깍듯이 대하던 게이지비가 이제 그런 마음을 죄다 잃어버렸음을 느끼고 있었다. 그 사무 변호사가 보기에 크로즈비는 한때 사교계의 멋쟁이였으나, 이제 그런 것은 다 끝난 일이었다. 사무 변호사가 보기에 크로즈비는 이제 그저 관청의 사무국장일 뿐이었다. 자기에게 빚을 진 사내일 뿐이었다. 두 사람은 자매를 아내로 맞았으니, 잘나가는 사무 변호사의 빛이 그다지 잘나가지 못하는 관리의 빛 앞에서 흐려져야 할 까닭이 없었다. 이 모든 것을 두 사내 다 속속들이 알고 있었다.\n\n“코시에서 아주 고약한 소식이 왔네.” 아이가 나가자마자 사무 변호사가 말했다.\n\n“왜, 무슨 일인가?”\n\n“폴록이 혼인을 했다네—그 여자하고 말일세.”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리.”\n\n“했다니까. 노부인께서 어쩔 수 없이 내게 알려 오셨는데, 상심으로 몸져누울 지경이시라네. 그런데 내가 보기엔 그게 최악도 아닐세. 폴록이 진작에 망가질 대로 망가졌다는 건 온 세상이 아는 일이지. 한데 그 사람이 밀린 생활비를 내놓으라고 제 아버지를 걸어 소송을 내겠다는 걸세. 돈을 못 받으면 죄다 법정에서 까발리겠다고 으르고 있어.”\n\n“한데 정말 그 사람한테 갈 돈이 있기는 한가?”\n\n“있지, 있고말고. 이천 파운드쯤 될 걸세. 아마 내가 마련해야 하겠지. 그렇지만 맹세코 그 돈이 어디서 나와야 할지 나도 모르겠네. 정말이지 모르겠어. 이러저러해서 내가 자네를 위해 치른 돈만 해도 천사백 파운드가 넘는다네.”\n\n“천사백 파운드라니!”\n\n“그렇다니까. 보험이며 세간이며, 게다가 혼인 재산 계약서 값으로 우리 사무소에서 나온 청구서까지 있으니 말일세. 그건 아직 치르지 않았지만 매한가지 아닌가. 사내가 거저 장가드는 게 아니라는 걸 내 말해 두겠네.”\n\n“그래도 자네는 담보를 잡아 두었잖은가.”\n\n“아, 그렇지. 담보야 잡아 두었지. 그런데 문제는 당장 쥘 현금일세. 우리 사무소가 코시 영지를 잡고 이미 너무 많이 내주어서 더 나가기를 꺼리거든. 그러니 이 일을 나 혼자 떠맡아야 하는 거지. 결국 그 식구들은 죄다 외국으로 나가야 할 걸세. 그게 끝이야. 백작과 조지 사이에 한바탕 난리가 났다네. 조지가 성질을 못 참고 백작에게 폴록의 혼인은 아버지 탓이라고 쏘아붙였다지. 그 끝에 조지가 제 아내와 함께 쫓겨나고 말았네.”\n\n“그 사람은 제 돈이 있잖은가.”\n\n“그렇지, 그런데 그 돈은 쓰려 들지 않는단 말일세. 여기로 올라온다는데, 우리 주위를 어슬렁거리며 붙어 있게 될 걸세. 나는 여기서 재워 줄 생각이 없고, 자네도 그러지 말라고 권하겠네. 한번 재워 주면 떼어 내지 못할 테니까.”\n\n“나는 그 사람이 세상에서 제일 싫으이.”\n\n“그럼, 못된 위인이지. 존도 마찬가질세. 폴록이 그중 나았는데, 그마저 아주 망해 버렸지. 저희끼리 아주 볼만하게 판을 어질러 놓았어. 안 그런가?”\n\n바로 이 집안을 얻으려고 크로즈비는 릴리 데일을 저버린 것이었다! 그의 하나뿐인 단순한 야심은 백작의 사위가 되는 것이었다. 그것을 이루려고 그는 스스로를 악당으로 만들어야 했다. 그것을 이루는 동안 그는 온갖 더러움과 수치를 다 겪었다. 그는 사랑하지 않는다는 것을 스스로 아는 여자와 혼인했다. 그는 자기가 사랑했고 지금도 사랑하지만, 너무나 깊이 상처를 입힌 나머지 어떤 사정으로도 다시는 말을 붙일 수 없게 된 한 처녀를 거의 시시각각 생각하고 있었다. 맞은편에 앉아, 그런 부류의 사내들이 제가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 훤히 알면서도 짐짓 꾸며 보이는 그 역겨운 걱정스러운 낯빛으로 제 수천 파운드 이야기를 늘어놓고 있는 저 사무 변호사도 그와 비슷한 혼인을 했다. 그러나 그는 자기가 무엇을 하는지 알고 있었다. 그는 제 혼인에서 기대한 것을 다 얻었다. 그런데 크로즈비는 무엇을 얻었는가?\n\n“못돼 먹은 집안일세—아주 못돼 먹은 집안이야.” 그가 쓰디쓴 심정으로 말했다.\n\n“사내들이 그렇지.” 게이지비가 아주 태연하게 말했다.\n\n“흐음.” 크로즈비가 말했다. 제 벗이 그 집안 여자들 또한 마땅히 그래야 할 만한 사람들은 못 된다는 심정을 드러내고 있다는 것이 게이지비에게는 뻔히 보였다. 그러나 그는 조금도 언짢아하지 않았다. 그 흉이 얼마쯤은 제 아내에게까지 붙는다고 볼 수 있는데도 그랬다.\n\n“백작부인께서는 마음씨가 곧으시지.” 게이지비가 말했다. “그렇지만 그분도 고생스러운 세월을 보내셨네—참으로 고생스러운 세월이었어. 나는 백작께서 부인한테 석탄 나르는 인부라도 질겁할 만한 욕설을 퍼붓는 걸 들은 적이 있네. 정말이야. 하지만 백작께서는 머잖아 돌아가실 테고, 그러면 부인께서는 편안해지실 걸세. 부인 앞으로 잡힌 몫이 해마다 삼천 파운드니까.”\n\n백작께서는 머잖아 돌아가실 테고, 그러면 부인께서는 편안해지실 걸세! 이것이 혼인 생활의 한 모습이었다. 크로즈비의 마음이 그 말에 머물러 있는 동안, 그는 들판에서 릴리가 했던 약속을, 자기를 위해 무엇이든 다 하겠다던 그 말을 떠올렸다. 그는 그녀의 입맞춤을, 그녀의 손가락이 닿던 느낌을, 낮고 은빛 같던 웃음소리를, 그녀가 제게 바싹 다가붙을 때 옷자락이 스치던 느낌을 떠올렸다. 그러고 나서 그는 자기 또한 죽어 버리는 편이, 그리하여 알렉산드리나가 편안해지는 편이 낫지 않겠는가 하고 생각했다. 그녀와 그녀의 어머니라면 돈이야 넉넉하니 바덴바덴에서 둘이 아주 편안하게 지낼 수 있으리라!\n\n앨링턴의 지주와 데일 부인과 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트는, 크로즈비에게 아무런 벌도 내리지 않았다는 것 때문에—그 큰 죄를 지은 값으로 아무런 응보도 그를 덮치지 않았다는 것 때문에 마음이 편치 않았고, 지금도 여전히 편치 않았다. 그들이 그 일을 얼마나 모르고 있었던가! 그가 기소되어 열두 달 동안 중노동과 함께 감옥에 갇혔다 한들 그 벌이 이보다 무겁지는 않았을 것이다. 적어도 그런 경우라면 그는 레이디 알렉산드리나로부터는 구원을 받았을 테니 말이다.\n\n“조지 오라버니 부부가 런던에 올라온다는데, 우리한테 한 주쯤 와 있으라고 청하면 안 될까요?” 집으로 돌아가려고 함께 삯마차에 오르자마자 아내가 그에게 말했다.\n\n“안 되오.” 크로즈비가 소리쳤다. “그런 짓은 하지 않을 거요.” 그 이야기는 더 오가지 않았고—집에 닿을 때까지 다른 어떤 이야기도 오가지 않았다. 집에 이르자 알렉산드리나는 머리가 아프다며 곧바로 제 방으로 올라가 버렸다. 크로즈비는 식당의 사그라져 가는 불 앞에 놓인 의자에 몸을 던지고는, 드 코시 집안 사람들과는 아예 인연을 끊어 버리겠다고 마음먹었다. 제 아내는 아내인 이상 자기에게 복종해야 한다. 자기에게 복종하든가—아니면 자기를 떠나 제 갈 길을 가고, 자기는 제 길을 가게 내버려 두든가 해야 한다. 수입은 한 해 천이백 파운드였다. 그중 육백 파운드를 제 몫으로 챙기고 옛 생활로 돌아갈 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까. 물론 예전의 안락을 다 누리지는 못할 것이다—사람들의 옛 존경과 대접도 그럴 것이다. 그러나 클럽 만찬의 호사쯤은 누릴 수 있으리라. 거리낄 것 없는 저녁나절이 제 것이 되고—그 시간을 제 마음대로 보낼 자유도 제 것이 되리라. 그는 아내와 갈라져 사는 사내들을 여럿 알고 있었는데, 그들은 이웃 사람들 못지않게 행복해 보였다. 그러다 그는 이날 저녁 알렉산드리나가 얼마나 못나 보였는지를 떠올렸다. 가짜 보석이 잔뜩 박힌 번쩍이는 싸구려 관을 머리에 얹고, 감기까지 들어 코가 벌게진 꼴이었다. 게다가 혼인한 뒤로 그녀에게는 어딘가 붙박인 듯한 칙칙하고 후줄근한 기운이 내려앉아 있었다. 그녀는 분명 아주 못생겼다! 그는 스스로에게 그렇게 말하고는 잠자리에 들었다. 나로서는 그가 받은 벌이 넉넉히 가혹했다고 여기는 쪽이다.\n\n이튿날 아침에도 아내는 여전히 머리가 아프다고 하여 그는 혼자 아침을 먹었다. 오라버니를 부르자는 그녀의 말에 그가 딱 잘라 안 된다고 한 뒤로 두 사람 사이에는 이야기랄 것이 별로 없었다. “머리가 쪼개질 것 같아요. 당신만 괜찮으시다면 세라더러 차하고 토스트를 제 방으로 올려 보내라고 할게요.”\n\n그는 조금도 개의치 않았고, 혼자서 아침을 먹었는데 여느 때의 그 끼니보다 훨씬 즐거웠다.\n\n이제 제 삶에서 누릴 만족이란 온통 관청 일에서 나와야 한다는 것이 그에게는 분명했다. 세상에는 날마다 마음을 달래 줄 무언가가 없이는 살아가기 힘든 사내들이 있는데, 그도 그런 사내였다. 그는 제 삶 가운데 흐뭇한 눈으로 바라볼 수 있는 대목이 한 쪽이라도 없으면 그 삶을 견디기 어려웠다. 그는 늘 제 일을 좋아했고, 이제는 그 어느 때보다 더 좋아하리라 마음먹었다. 그러나 그러자면 많은 것을 제 뜻대로 해야만 했다. 사무국의 법도로 말하자면, 사무국장인 그가 할 일은 그저 위원들의 지시를 받아 그것이 이행되도록 살피는 것뿐이었고, 그의 전임자는 그 일에만 엄격히 매여 있었다. 그러나 그는 이미 그보다 더 많은 일을 해 왔고, 위원회를 제 손아귀에 쥐다시피 하겠다는 야심을 품고 있었다. 그는 제 일도 그들의 일도 그들보다 자기가 더 잘 안다고, 그러니 조금만 요령껏 다루면 자기가 그들의 주인이 될 수 있으리라고 스스로 흐뭇해했다. 패딩턴 역에서의 그 소동만 없었더라면 정말 그렇게 되었을지도 모른다. 그러나 우리 모두가 알듯이, 마당을 주름잡는 수탉은 언제까지나 주름잡고 있어야만 한다. 한번 날개가 진창에 처발려 낭패를 본 꼴로 비치기라도 하면, 다른 어떤 수탉도 다시는 그를 존중해 주지 않는다. 옵티미스트 씨와 버터웰 씨는 제 사무국장이 몽둥이질을 당했다는 것을 훤히 알고 있었고, 그렇게 당한 사무국장에게 고분고분 따를 수는 없었다.\n\n“아, 그런데 말일세, 크로즈비.” 혼자 아침을 먹은 그날 그가 사무국에 나온 지 얼마 되지 않아 버터웰이 그의 방으로 들어오며 말했다. “자네한테 몇 마디 할 말이 있네.” 그러고서 버터웰은 몸을 돌려 문을 닫았는데, 그때까지 걸쇠가 걸려 있지 않던 문이었다. 크로즈비는 딱히 머리를 굴려 볼 것도 없이 곧바로 앞으로 오갈 이야기가 어떤 것일지를 스스로 짚어 냈다.\n\n“자네도 알다시피—” 버터웰이 말문을 열었다.\n\n“앉으시지요.” 크로즈비가 이렇게 말하며 자기도 자리에 앉았다. 겨루어야 할 일이라면 할 수 있는 한 잘 싸울 참이었다. 그는 철도 승강장에서 보였던 것보다 나은 기개를 여기서 보이려 했다. 버터웰은 정말로 자리에 앉았고, 앉으면서 그 앉는 동작만으로도 제 힘이 얼마쯤 빠져나간다는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 크로즈비를 제 방으로 불렀어야 했다. 사람이 남을 꾸짖으려 할 때에는 언제나 제 쪽 공기의 덕을 보아야 하는 법이다.\n\n“무슨 흠을 잡자는 게 아닐세.” 버터웰이 말문을 열었다.\n\n“흠잡으실 거리가 없기를 바랍니다.” 크로즈비가 말했다.\n\n“아니, 아니지. 그런 게 있다는 말이 아니야. 그렇지만 위원회에서는 우리 생각에—”\n\n“잠깐, 잠깐만요, 버터웰 씨. 언짢은 이야기가 나올 참이라면 위원회에서 나오는 편이 낫겠습니다. 그러면 제가 더 나은 마음으로 받아들이겠지요. 정말 그럴 겁니다.”\n\n“위원회에서 벌어지는 일은 공식적인 것이 될 수밖에 없네.”\n\n“저는 조금도 개의치 않습니다. 오히려 그편이 낫겠습니다.”\n\n“말하자면 이런 걸세—자네가 좀 지나치게 많은 것을 제 손에 쥐고 있다고 우리는 보고 있어. 물론 잘못이라 해도 옳은 쪽으로 기운 잘못이고, 일이 제대로 되기를 바라는 마음에서 나온 것이겠지만 말이야.”\n\n“그럼 제가 하지 않으면 누가 합니까?” 크로즈비가 물었다.\n\n“위원회도 제게 딸린 일쯤은 넉넉히 해낼 수 있네. 이보게 크로즈비, 자네와 나는 오랜 세월 서로 알고 지낸 사이인데 언짢은 말이 오간다면 딱한 노릇이지. 내가 이런 식으로 자네를 찾아온 것은 공식으로 문제가 불거지면 자네에게 언짢을 터이기 때문일세. 옵티미스트는 좀처럼 화를 내는 사람이 아닌데, 어제는 아주 대놓고 화를 냈네. 내 말을 좋게 받아들여서 앞으로는 좀 조용히 가는 편이 좋겠어.”\n\n그러나 크로즈비는 무엇이든 조용히 받아들일 기분이 아니었다. 그는 온몸이 쓰라렸고, 마주치는 사람마다 후려치고 싶은 심사였다. “저는 제 힘이 닿는 데까지 제 본분을 다했습니다, 버터웰 씨.” 그가 말했다. “그리고 잘해 냈다고 믿습니다. 저는 여기서 제가 할 일을 누가 가르쳐 줄 필요가 없을 만큼 잘 알고 있습니다. 제가 제 몫보다 많은 일을 했다면, 그건 다른 사람들이 제 몫보다 적게 했기 때문입니다.” 그가 말하는 동안 그의 이마에는 검은 구름이 끼어 있었고, 그 위원은 사무국장이 몹시 노여워하고 있음을 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n“오! 좋으이.” 버터웰이 의자에서 일어서며 말했다. “사정이 그렇다면 나로서는 위원장에게 말할 수밖에 없고, 그러면 위원장이 위원회에서 제 생각을 자네에게 일러 주겠지. 자네는 어리석은 짓을 하고 있네. 정말 그래. 나로 말하자면 그저 자네에게 좋게 해 주려 했을 뿐일세.” 그러고 나서 버터웰 씨는 자리를 떴다.\n\n그날 오후 크로즈비는 여느 때처럼 두 시와 세 시 사이에 위원회실로 불려 들어갔다. 이는 날마다 있는 일이었으니, 그는 늘 세 위원 가운데 두 사람과 한 시간쯤 마주 앉곤 했다. 그들이 비스킷 한 조각과 셰리 한 잔으로 기운을 차린 뒤였다. 이날도 여느 만큼의 일이 처리되었으나, 그 처리되는 모양새가 크로즈비로 하여금 자기들 모두가 여느 때와 같은 자리에 서 있지 않다는 것을 느끼게 했다. 세 위원이 다 나와 있었다. 위원장은 기분이 좋을 때에는 결코 쓰는 법이 없는 엄숙하고 거드름 섞인 목소리로 지시를 내렸다. 소령은 거의 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 그의 눈에는 흡족해하는 빈정거림이 반짝였다. 위원회의 일이 어긋나게 돌아가고 있었고, 그는 그것이 즐거웠다. 버터웰 씨는 태도가 유난히 깍듯했고 여느 때보다 좀 더 활기찼다. 그날의 정해진 일이 끝나자마자 옵티미스트 씨는 의자 위에서 몸을 뒤척이며 일어섰다가 다시 앉기를 되풀이했다. 그는 제 손 가까이 놓인 서류 뭉치를 안경 너머로 들여다보며 뒤적였다. 그러다 하나를 골라 들고는 안경을 벗고 의자에 등을 기댄 뒤 짧은 연설을 시작했다.\n\n“크로즈비 씨.” 그가 말했다. “우리는 모두 대단히 흡족하게 여기고 있습니다—참으로 대단히 흡족하게 여기고 있어요—귀하가 공직에서 보여 준 열의와 정력을 말입니다.”\n\n“고맙습니다, 위원장님.” 크로즈비가 말했다. “저는 이 공직을 좋아합니다.”\n\n“그렇지요, 그렇지요. 우리 모두 그렇게 느끼고 있습니다. 그런데 우리 생각으로는 귀하가—제가 지나치게 많은 것을 스스로 떠맡고 있다고 말한다면, 아마 우리가 뜻하는 것보다 더한 말을 하는 셈이 되겠습니다만.”\n\n“뜻하시는 것보다 더한 말씀은 하지 마십시오, 옵티미스트 씨.” 그렇게 말하는 크로즈비의 눈은 한순간의 승리로 잠깐 반짝였다. 피아스코 소령의 눈도 그러했다.\n\n“아니, 아니, 아닙니다.” 옵티미스트 씨가 말했다. “귀하처럼 훌륭한 공복에게라면 나는 더한 말보다 차라리 덜한 말을 하겠습니다. 그렇지만 귀하도 내 말뜻을 알아들으시겠지요?”\n\n“아무래도 잘 모르겠습니다, 위원장님. 제가 지나치게 많은 것을 떠맡은 게 아니라면, 제가 하지 말았어야 할 무슨 일을 했다는 말씀입니까?”\n\n“귀하는 먼저 권한을 받았어야 마땅한 여러 경우에 지시부터 내렸습니다. 여기 한 예가 있습니다.” 그러면서 골라 두었던 서류가 곧바로 나왔다.\n\n그것은 사무국장이 성문의 법도로 보아 명백히 잘못한 일이었고, 그는 그 일 자체를 두고는 변명할 수 없었다.\n\n“제가 사무국의 명시된 지시에만 꼭 매여 있기를 바라신다면,” 그가 말했다. “그렇게 하겠습니다. 다만 그것이 불편하게 여겨지시리라 봅니다.”\n\n“그편이 훨씬 나을 겁니다.” 옵티미스트 씨가 말했다.\n\n“좋습니다.” 크로즈비 씨가 말했다. “그렇게 하지요.” 그러고서 그는 방 안의 세 신사에게 제 힘이 닿는 데까지 언짢은 사람이 되어 주기로 그 자리에서 마음먹었다. 그는 아주 언짢은 사람이 될 수 있었다. 그러나 그 언짢음은 그들에게만큼이나 그 자신에게도 언짢은 것이 될 터였다.\n\n이제 그에게는 어느 것 하나 제대로 되어 가지 않았다. 어느 쪽을 보아도 흡족함을 찾을 수 없었다. 그는 집으로 가는 길에 세브라이트에 어슬렁어슬렁 들어갔으나, 그날의 자잘한 일들을 두고 누구에게든 건넬 말을 찾지 못했다. 그는 집으로 갔고, 아내는 자리에서 일어나 있기는 했으나 여전히 머리가 아프다고 하소연했다.\n\n“하루 내내 집 밖에 나가 보지도 못했어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그래서 더 심해진 거예요.”\n\n“걸으려 하지 않는데 어떻게 밖에 나갈 수 있다는 건지 나로서는 잘 모르겠구려.” 그가 대답했다.\n\n그러고 나서 두 사람은 밥상에 마주 앉을 때까지 더는 아무 말도 주고받지 않았다.\n\n앨링턴의 지주가 이 모든 것을 다 알았더라면, 내 생각에는, 크로즈비가 맞닥뜨린 그 벌에 흡족해했을 법도 하다.\n\n# 제49장. 떠날 채비\n\n“엄마, 이 편지 좀 읽어 보세요.”\n\n데일 부인에게 말을 건 것은 그녀의 맏딸이었고, 두 사람은 작은 집 응접실에 단둘이 있었다. 데일 부인은 편지를 받아 아주 꼼꼼히 읽었다. 그러고는 그것을 다시 봉투에 넣어 벨에게 돌려주었다.\n\n“어쨌든 좋은 편지구나. 그리고 내가 보기에는 진실을 적은 것 같다.”\n\n“제 생각엔 진실보다 조금 더 적어 놓은 것 같아요, 엄마. 엄마 말씀대로 잘 쓴 편지이긴 해요. 버나드는 진지할 때면 언제나 글을 잘 쓰니까요. 하지만 그래도—”\n\n“그래도 뭐가 어떻단 말이니, 얘야?”\n\n“거기엔 마음보다 머리가 더 많이 들어 있어요.”\n\n“그렇다면 그 애도 덜 괴로울 게다. 네 마음이 이 일에 아주 굳게 정해져 있다면 말이야.”\n\n“제 마음은 아주 굳게 정해졌어요. 그리고 버나드가 그리 괴로워하리라고는 생각지 않아요. 이 일을 바라지 않았다면 그만한 수고를 들여 그런 편지를 쓰지도 않았겠지요.”\n\n“그 애가 이 일을 바라는 건 분명하다. 그것도 아주 간절하게 말이야. 그리고 크게 실망할 게다.”\n\n“다른 어떤 계획이 제 뜻대로 되지 않았을 때도 꼭 그만큼 실망하겠지요. 그뿐이에요.”\n\n물론 그 편지는 벨의 사촌 버나드가 보낸 것으로, 그녀의 손을 얻으려는 자기 청혼을 두고 그가 펼 수 있는 가장 강력한 호소를 담고 있었다. 버나드 데일은 그런 호소라면 입으로 말하기보다 편지로 밀어붙이는 편이 훨씬 나은 사람이었다. 그는 곰곰이 헤아릴 시간이 조금이라도 주어지는 일이라면 무엇이든 잘 해내는 사람이었으나, 얻고자 하는 것을 청할 때 사람을 웅변으로 몰아가는 그 정념의 힘만은 그에게 없었다. 이번 그의 편지는 길고 조리가 있었다. 그 안에 열렬한 사랑은 거의 없었지만, 듣기 좋은 칭찬은 넉넉히 들어 있었다. 그는 두 사람의 혼인이 두 집안 모두에게 얼마나 이로운 일인지 벨에게 말했다. 떨어져 지낸 탓에 이 일을 향한 자기 마음이 오히려 더 굳세어졌노라고 밝혔다. 앞으로의 처신을 보증할 가장 좋은 담보로 자기가 지나온 삶의 이력을 자랑하는 기색 없이 넌지시 내비쳤다. 이 혼인이 이루어지기만 하면 적어도 숙모가 릴리를 데리고 작은 집에서 나가는 문제만은 더 따질 것이 없어진다고 일러 주었다. 그러고는 마지막으로, 그녀를 향한 자기 애정이 자기 삶을 통째로 사로잡은 정념이라고 말했다. 만약 이 편지가 제삼자에게서 자기 청혼에 유리한 판정을 얻어 낼 목적으로 쓰인 것이었다면, 참으로 훌륭한 편지였을 것이다. 그러나 벨 데일 같은 아가씨의 마음을 흔들 만한 말은 그 안에 한 마디도 없었다.\n\n“다정하게 답장을 쓰렴.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“제가 아는 한 가장 다정하게 쓸게요.” 벨이 말했다. “엄마가 그 편지를 써 주시면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“그건 안 될 말이다. 내가 쓰면 그 애더러 한 번 더 해 보라고 부추기는 꼴밖에 안 될 게다.”\n\n데일 부인은 버나드의 청혼에 가망이 없다는 것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다. 벌써 몇 달 전부터 알고 있었다. 두 사람 사이에 그 문제를 두고 이야기가 오간 적은 없었지만, 크로프츠 선생이 언제든 다시 찾아와 딸의 손을 청하기만 하면 결코 거절당하지 않으리라는 것을 그녀는 확신하고 있었다. 두 사람 가운데 그녀 자신이 더 마음에 들어 한 쪽은 아마 크로프츠 선생이었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 둘 다 좋아했고, 세상의 눈으로 보자면 한쪽은 몹시 변변찮은 혼처인 반면 다른 쪽은 무엇 하나 나무랄 데 없는 혼처라는 사실을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀는 무슨 일이 있어도 딸의 마음을 흔들 말은 한 마디도 하지 않을 작정이었고, 게다가 자기가 무슨 말을 한들 딸의 마음이 흔들리지 않으리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러나 일이 이렇게 되고 만 데 대한 얼마간의 아쉬움만은 스스로에게서 떼어 낼 수가 없었다.\n\n“엄마가 무엇을 바라시는지 저도 알아요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“나에게 바람은 하나뿐이란다, 얘야. 네가 행복한 것 말이다. 부디 하느님께서 릴리와 같은 운명으로부터 너를 지켜 주시기를. 사촌에게 다정하게 답장을 쓰라고 한 것은, 그저 그 애가 정직하게 굴었으니 다정한 답을 받을 만하다고 여긴다는 뜻일 뿐이다.”\n\n“제가 할 수 있는 한 가장 다정하게 쓸게요, 엄마. 하지만 극에 나오는 그 아가씨가 뭐라고 하는지 아시잖아요. ‘아니오’라는 그 말에서 가시를 빼내기가 얼마나 어려운지 말이에요.” 그러고 나서 벨은 한동안 홀로 밖을 거닐다가, 돌아와서는 책상을 꺼내 편지를 썼다. 아주 단호하고 결연한 편지였다. ‘아니오’처럼 날카롭고 쏘아 대는 말에서 가시를 뽑아낸다는 그 재치로 말하자면, 그녀에게는 아무래도 그것이 없었던 듯싶다. “나도 진지하다는 것을 버나드가 알아듣게 하는 편이 낫겠지.” 그녀는 속으로 이렇게 되뇌었고, 그런 마음가짐으로 편지를 썼다. “내가 한 말을 매정하다고는 부디 여기지 말아 줘.” 그녀는 추신에 이렇게 덧붙였다. “버나드가 얼마나 좋은 사람인지 나도 알고, 내가 물리치는 것이 얼마나 값진 것인지도 알아. 그렇지만 이 일에서만은 있는 그대로의 진실을 말하는 것이 내 도리일 거야.”\n\n작은 집에서 게스트윅으로 옮겨 가는 일은 오월 초하루가 되기 전에는 하지 않기로 지주와 데일 부인 사이에 이미 정해져 있었다. 릴리를 삼월에 지금 사는 집에서 데리고 나가는 것은 분별없는 짓이라고 크로프츠 선생이 여긴다는 말을 전해 듣고서, 지주는 자기가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 능변을 다 동원하여 데일 부인이 그 계획을 접도록 설득하려 들었다. 그는 그 집을 늘 자기 것이 아니라 집안의 다른 누군가에게 마땅히 돌아가는 것으로 여겨 왔노라고 말했다. 언제나 그렇게 사람이 들어와 살아 왔고, 앨링턴의 지주가 그 집에서 집세를 받은 일은 여러 해 전부터 없었노라고 했다. “은혜를 베푸는 게 아니오. 그런 것은 조금도 아니오.” 그는 이렇게 말했으나, 그러면서도 어조는 여느 때와 같이 날카롭고 무뚝뚝했다.\n\n“은혜예요, 큰 은혜고 큰 아량이지요.” 데일 부인이 대답했다. “그리고 저는 그것을 받기에 너무 도도했던 적이 한 번도 없어요. 하지만 저희가 게스트윅에서 더 행복하리라 여긴다고 말씀드리는 마당이니, 시아주버니께서도 저희를 보내 주기를 마다하지는 않으시겠지요. 릴리는 이 집에서 큰 타격을 입었고, 벨은 제 일로 시아주버니의 바람을 거스르고 있다고 여기고 있어요. 그토록 고마운 바람을요!”\n\n“그 일이라면 더 말할 것 없소. 그대들이 그대로 여기 남아 있기만 하면 그 모든 것이 아직 바로잡힐 수도 있소.”\n\n그러나 데일 부인은 ‘그 모든 것’이 결코 바로잡힐 수 없음을 알고 있었기에 뜻을 굽히지 않았다. 사실 그녀는 지주의 간청에 굽히고 말았노라고 딸들에게 말할 엄두조차 내지 못했을 것이다. 바로 그 무렵, 바로 그때에 지주는 릴리의 재산 문제를 두고 말하자면 드 게스트 백작과 협상을 벌이는 중이었다. 그리고 자기가 그토록 넉넉한 마음으로 대해 주고 있는 바로 그 순간에 제 살붙이들에게서 그런 식으로 맞섬을 당한다는 것이 그로서는 참으로 야속했다. 그러나 집을 두고 벌인 실랑이에서 그는 릴리에 대해서도, 그 아이의 앞날에 대해서도 한 마디 하지 않았다.\n\n그들은 오월 초하루에 옮겨 가기로 되어 있었고, 사월도 벌써 한 주가 지나 있었다. 방금 말한 데일 부인과의 그 대면이 있고 난 뒤로 지주는 그 일에 대해 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 이 헤어짐이 속상하고 쓰라렸으며, 이번 거절에 드러난 마음씨 탓에 자기가 부당한 대접을 받고 있다고 여겼다. 자기 딴에는 그들에게 도리를 다했고, 실은 도리 이상을 했는데, 이제 그들은 제 손으로 베푼 신세의 무게를 더는 견딜 수 없어서 떠나는 것이라고 그에게 말하고 있었다. 그러나 사실 그는 그들을 이해하지 못했고, 그들 또한 그를 이해하지 못했다. 그는 태도가 거칠었고 이따금 위세를 부렸으니, 자기 자리가 가깝고 다정한 벗의 온갖 특권은 준다 해도 아버지나 남편의 권위까지 주는 것은 아님을 느끼지 못했던 것이다. 버나드의 혼담 문제에서도 그는 마치 벨이 사촌을 거절하기 전에 자기 뜻부터 헤아렸어야 한다는 투로 말했다. 그는 스스로 나서서 데일 부인을 나무랐고, 그로써 두 딸의 마음을 상하게 했으니, 그 아이들은 그때 그것만은 도무지 용서할 수 없다고 여겼던 것이다.\n\n그러나 이 일에 관해서라면 그들도 그보다 딱히 더 흡족한 것은 아니었다. 그리고 이제 때가 닥치고 보니, 자기들이 내건 요구를 스스로 거두어들이지는 못하면서도, 지주를 모질게 대하고 있는 것만 같았다. 그들의 결심이 내려졌을 때, 아니 그 결심이 내려지는 동안에 그는 그들에게 엄격하고 매몰찼다. 그 뒤로 그는 릴리의 불행에 마음이 누그러졌고, 그들이 곁을 떠나고 나면 자기에게 닥칠 외로움을 미리 그려 보면서 또 한 번 누그러졌다. 그가 그들 모두를 위해 최선을 다하고 있는 마당에 그들이 그를 그렇게 대하다니, 그로서는 가혹한 일이었다! 그리고 그들도 그것을 느끼고 있었다. 다만 그가 그들을 돕기 위해 어디까지 나서려 하는지 그 속내는 알지 못했다. 난롯가에 둘러앉아 이사 계획을 짜던 무렵에는 그들의 마음이 그에게 굳게 닫혀 있었고, 그래서 자기들의 독립을 내세우기로 뜻을 모았던 것이다. 그러나 이제 정작 움직일 때가 오고 보니, 그를 향한 서운함이 이미 크게 가라앉아 있음을 그들도 느꼈다. 그 때문에 그들이 하는 모든 일에 어떤 서글픔이 배어들었다. 그러나 그래도 그들은 하던 일을 계속해 나갔다.\n\n이사 채비라는 것이 얼마나 끔찍한지 모르는 사람이 어디 있겠는가. 꾸려 넣어야 할 물건의 가짓수가 얼마나 끝이 없는지, 짐을 싸는 동안이 얼마나 말할 수 없이 불편한지, 그리고 제 살림살이가 그렇게 제자리를 잃고 흐트러져 있는 동안 그 꼴이 얼마나 초라하고 볼품없어 보이는지를 말이다. 요즘 세상 물정에 밝고 그 밝음에 걸맞은 돈까지 지닌 사람들은 이런 재난을 죄다 피해 가는 법을, 그 일을 삯 받고 해 주는 사람들의 손에 맡겨 버리는 법을 익혔다. 그릇은 찬장에 그대로, 책은 서가에 그대로, 포도주는 술광에 그대로, 커튼은 봉에 그대로 걸어 둔 채, 사정을 아는 그 집안은 보름 동안 브라이턴으로 떠난다. 그 기간이 끝날 무렵이면 그릇은 다른 찬장에 편안히 자리를 잡았고, 책은 다른 서가에, 포도주는 다른 술광에, 커튼은 다른 봉에 걸려 있으며, 모든 것이 갖추어져 있다. 그러나 데일 부인과 두 딸은 그런 이사법이라고는 아무것도 알지 못했다. 마을 목수가 제 손으로 짜 준 궤짝 몇 개를 채우는 데 그의 도움을 받는 것, 그것이 제집 하녀 둘 말고 그들이 구할 줄 아는 일손의 전부였다. 물건이란 물건은 하나하나 식구 가운데 누군가의 손을 거쳐야 했다. 그리고 해야 할 일의 크기에 짓눌리는 심정이었던 터라 필요한 것보다 훨씬 일찍 일을 시작해 버렸으니, 일이 진척될수록, 결국 마지막에는 궤짝과 상자 사이에서, 뜯어 헤쳐진 가구들의 온갖 어수선함 속에서 지독하게 따분하고 멍하고 불편한 한 주를 보내야 하리라는 것이 뻔해졌다.\n\n처음에는 릴리가 아무 일도 해서는 안 된다는 엄명이 내려졌다. 그 아이는 환자였으니 떠받들어 주고 조용히 지내게 해야 했다. 그러나 이 엄명은 이내 땅에 떨어졌고, 릴리는 어머니보다도 언니보다도 더 열심히 일했다. 사실 그 아이는 이제 거의 환자가 아니었고, 환자 대접을 받으려 하지도 않았다. 그 아이는 지금으로서는 쉼 없이 몸을 놀리는 것만이 지난 일을 되돌아보는 비참함에서 자기를 건져 줄 수 있음을 스스로 느끼고 있었다. 그리고 그 일이 고될수록 자기에게 더 이로우리라는 생각을 품게 되었다. 책을 끌어내리고, 이불과 천을 개키고, 오래 잊고 있던 자잘한 살림살이를 옛 은신처에서 끄집어내는 동안, 그 아이는 지난날 못지않게 명랑했다. 먼지 낀 폐허 같은 것들에 둘러싸여 뺨을 붉히고 눈웃음을 지으며 선 채로 제 일거리를 두고 이야기를 늘어놓았으니, 어머니는 잠깐씩 그 아이 속이 다 괜찮아졌구나 여기기도 했다. 그러나 또 어떤 때, 반동이 밀려오면 아무것도 괜찮지 않은 것만 같았다. 그 아이는 조용하고 조리 있는 일감을 손에 들고 난롯가에 가만히 앉아 조리 있고 조용하게 이야기를 나눌 수가 없었다. 아직은 그럴 수가 없었다. 그런데도 그 아이는 괜찮았다. 제 가슴속만은 괜찮았다. 그 아이는 제 비참함을 이겨 내겠노라고 스스로에게 선언했었다. 앓는 동안 제 불행이 자기를 죽이지는 못하리라고 스스로에게 선언했던 것처럼. 그리고 그 아이는 그것을 이겨 가는 길에 들어서 있었다. 달콤한 희망이 꺾였다고 해서 자기에게 세상이 끝난 것은 아니라고 그 아이는 스스로에게 일렀다. 상처는 깊고 몹시 쓰라렸으나, 이 환자의 살은 튼튼하고 성했으며 피는 맑았다. 그런 병에 밝은 의사라면 오래도록 그 아이의 증세를 지켜본 끝에 나을 가망이 있다고 진단했을 것이다. 그 아이를 가장 가까이서 지켜본 의사는 어머니였다. 그리고 그녀는 이따금 의심에 몰리기도 했으나, 제 아이가 언젠가는 살아서 제 사랑의 이야기를 아리지 않은 마음으로 떠올릴 수 있으리라고, 날이 갈수록 더 굳센 희망으로 희망했다.\n\n아무도 그 일을 두고 자기에게 말하지 말 것. 그것이 그 아이가 내건 듯이 보이는 단 하나의 조건이었다. 그런 뜻의 부탁을 벗들에게 또박또박 말로 내보낸 것은 아니었으나, 몇 가지 기색으로 그것이 자기 조건임을 드러내 보였다. 그런 뜻의 한마디를 그 아이는 삼촌에게 했었고, 기억하시겠지만 그 한마디는 더없이 고분고분하게 지켜졌다. 크로즈비의 거짓 소식이 그 아이에게 닿고 나서 아주 이내 그 아이는 제 작은 세상으로 나섰다. 먼저 교회로, 그다음에는 마을 사람들 사이로. 자기에게 아무런 짓누르는 무게도 떨어지지 않은 것처럼 처신하리라 마음먹고서. 마을 사람들은 그 모두를 알아들었고, 드러내 놓고 이러쿵저러쿵 늘어놓는 일 없이 그 아이의 말을 듣고 대꾸해 주었다.\n\n“하느님이 복 주시길입쇼.” 우체국장 크럼프 부인이 말했다. 앨링턴에서 성미가 제일 고약하다고들 하는 사람이었다. “릴리 아가씨를 볼 적마다 저는 아가씨야말로 이 근방을 통틀어 젤로 이쁜 아가씨다 싶습니다요.”\n\n“그리고 아주머니는 제일 심술궂은 할머니시고요.” 릴리가 웃으며 우체국장에게 손을 내밀면서 말했다.\n\n“그렇습죠.” 크럼프 부인이 말했다. “그렇고말굽쇼.” 그러고 나서 릴리는 그 오두막에 앉아 그녀의 병치레를 물었다. 헌 부인도 마찬가지였다. 헌 부인은 앞서 이야기한 그 첫 만남 뒤로는 그 아이를 귀여워하고 어루만졌을 뿐, 그 아이의 불행에 대해서는 더 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 릴리가 보이스 부인을 두 번째로 찾아갔을 때—그것도 혼자서 당당히 갔다—그 부인은 위로의 말을 한 마디 더 꺼내려 들었다. “가엾은 우리 릴리, 우리 모두 어찌나 마음이 아팠는지—” 보이스 부인은 릴리 곁에 바싹 붙어 앉아 그 얼굴을 들여다보려 애쓰며 거기까지 말했다. 그러나 릴리는 뺨을 살짝 붉히면서 보이스네 딸들 가운데 하나를 향해 홱 돌아앉아, 보이스 부인의 위로를 잘디잔 조각으로 찢어 버렸다. “미니.” 그 아이는 거의 소녀다운 환희에 차서 아주 큰 소리로 말했다. “어제 타타르가 무슨 짓을 했는지 아니? 내 평생 그렇게 웃어 본 적이 없어.” 그러고는 지주네 아주 못생긴 테리어에 얽힌 우스꽝스러운 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 그 뒤로는 보이스 부인마저 더 손을 대 보려 하지 않았다. 데일 부인과 벨도 그것이 규칙임을, 자기들에게조차 그대로 적용되는 규칙임을 알아차렸다. 릴리는 이따금 그 일을 두고 그들에게 말하곤 했다. 한 번에 한 사람씩, 체념 어린 우울한 한마디 같은 것으로 시작해서, 이윽고 그들 앞에 제 가슴을 활짝 열어 보일 때까지 이야기를 이어 갔다. 그러나 그런 이야기를 그들 쪽에서 먼저 꺼낸 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그런데 이제, 이 분주한 짐 싸기의 나날에는 그 화제가 아예 자취를 감춘 듯했다.\n\n“엄마.” 그 아이가 발판 사다리 맨 윗단에 올라선 채 말했다. 거기서 찬장 속의 유리그릇을 아래로 건네주는 참이었다. “이것들이 정말 우리 것이 맞아요? 몇 개는 이 집에 딸린 물건 같은데요.”\n\n“적어도 저 사발만은 확실하다. 내가 시집오기 전부터 우리 어머니 것이었으니까.”\n\n“어머나, 이걸 깨뜨리기라도 하면 어쩌죠? 저는 아주 귀한 물건을 손에 들 때마다 늘 그걸 내던져서 박살 내고 싶어져요. 어머! 하마터면 정말 갈 뻔했네요, 엄마. 그건 엄마 잘못이에요.”\n\n“조심하지 않으면 이번엔 너 자신이 하마터면 갈 뻔하게 될 게다. 뭐라도 좀 붙잡으렴.”\n\n“어머, 언니, 여기 언니가 삼 년째 없어졌다고 한탄하던 잉크스탠드가 있어.”\n\n“삼 년째 한탄한 적 없거니와, 대체 누가 저기다 올려놓았을까?”\n\n“받아.” 릴리는 이렇게 말하고는 그 병을 융단 더미 위로 던져 내렸다.\n\n이때 현관에서 발소리가 들리더니 지주가 열린 문으로 방에 들어섰다. “다들 일하는 중이구먼.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“네, 일하고 있어요.” 데일 부인이 거의 부끄러워하는 어조로 말했다. “해야 할 일이라면 얼른 해치우는 편이 나으니까요.”\n\n“나야말로 이 일이 아주 비참하오.” 지주가 말했다. “허나 그 얘기를 하러 온 것은 아니오. 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트가 제수씨에게 보낸 쪽지를 가져왔소. 그리고 나도 백작에게서 한 통 받았고. 부활절 다음 주에 우리 모두 그리로 와서 한 주 묵기를 바란다는구려.”\n\n이 아주 느닷없는 제안을 받고서 데일 부인과 두 딸은 모두 제자리에 못 박힌 채 한동안 말을 잃었다. 게스트윅 저택에 가서 한 주를 묵는다니! 온 식구가! 지금껏 저택과 작은 집 사이의 왕래라고는 아주 뜸한 오전 방문이 전부였다. 데일 부인은 그곳에서 만찬을 든 적이 한 번도 없었고, 근래에는 그 방문마저 딸들에게 맡겨 두었다. 한 번은 벨이 삼촌인 지주와 함께 그곳에서 만찬을 들었고, 또 한 번은 릴리가 올랜도 삼촌과 함께 건너갔다. 그것조차도 아주 오래전, 그들이 채 사교계에 나오기도 전의 일이었고, 그들은 그 자리를 아이들이 품는 엄숙한 경외로 대했었다. 그런데 이제 게스트윅의 작고 초라한 살림집으로 옮겨 가는 이 마당에, 저택으로 방문하는 그 일도 그만 흐지부지 접어 두어도 되겠다고 자기들끼리 미리 정해 놓은 참이었다. 임스 부인은 방문하는 법이 없었고, 자기들도 임스 부인의 자리로 내려앉는 중이었으니까. “어쩌면 사냥한 고기를 보내 줄지도 모르지. 그게 더 낫겠다.” 릴리는 그렇게 말했었다. 그런데 이제, 살림이 내려앉는 바로 이 순간에 그들 모두가 저택에 가서 한 주를 묵으라는 청을 받은 것이다! 레이디 줄리아와 한 주를 지내라니! 여왕께서 궁내대신을 내려보내 다들 윈저 성으로 오라 이르셨더라도 첫 충격이 이보다 크지는 않았을 것이다. 삼촌이 들어섰을 때 벨은 개어 놓은 융단 위에 앉아 있었는데, 지금 다시 같은 자리에 주저앉았다. 릴리는 여전히 사다리 꼭대기에 서 있었고, 데일 부인은 그 아래에서 한 손을 릴리의 옷자락에 얹고 있었다. 지주는 제 이야기를 아주 불쑥 꺼냈다. 그러나 그는 할 이야기가 생기면 불쑥 내뱉어 첫 순간에 죄다 털어놓는 것 말고는 더 나은 수를 알지 못하는 사람이었다.\n\n“우리 모두라니요!” 데일 부인이 말했다. “모두라면 몇 사람을 말하는 건가요?” 그러고 나서 그녀는 사다리 아래 제자리에서 움직이지도 않은 채 레이디 줄리아의 쪽지를 펴서 읽었다.\n\n“저도 좀 보여 주세요, 엄마.” 릴리가 말했고, 쪽지는 위로 건네졌다. 데일 부인이 그 일을 잘 헤아려 보았더라면 아마 그 쪽지를 얼마 동안 제 손에 쥐고 있었을 것이다. 그러나 모든 일이 워낙 갑작스러워서 그녀는 그것을 잘 헤아려 보지 못했다.\n\n친애하는 데일 부인께 [편지는 이렇게 적혀 있었다],\n\n이 쪽지는 제 오라버니가 데일 씨께 보내는 편지 안에 함께 넣어 보냅니다. 저희는 부인과 두 따님이 이달 십칠일부터 한 주 동안 저희에게 와 주시기를 각별히 바라고 있습니다. 저희 두 집안의 가까운 인연을 생각하면 지난 여러 해 동안 서로 좀 더 자주 만났어야 마땅했고, 물론 그것은 저희 잘못입니다. 그러나 제 몸가짐을 고치는 데에 너무 늦은 때란 없는 법이지요. 제 고백을 그 안에 담긴 참된 애정의 마음 그대로 받아 주시고, 저희에게 와 주심으로써 부인의 너그러움을 보여 주시기를 바랍니다. 두 따님이 이 집을 즐겁게 여기도록 제가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 하겠습니다. 저는 두 분 모두에게 참으로 큰 정을 품고 있으니까요.\n\n말씀드려 두어야 할 것은, 같은 주에 존 임스도 이곳에 와 있으리라는 것입니다. 제 오라버니가 그를 무척 아끼며 요즘 젊은이 가운데 으뜸이라고 여깁니다. 저 또한 그를 제 영웅 가운데 하나로 여긴다고 고백해야겠습니다.\n\n더없이 진실한 마음을 담아,\n\n사다리에 선 채로 릴리는 그 편지를 아주 주의 깊게 읽었다. 그동안 지주는 아래에 서서 제수와 조카딸에게 한두 마디를 건네고 있었다. 릴리의 얼굴은 창 쪽으로 돌려져 있어서 아무도 볼 수 없었고, 그 아이가 입을 열 때에도 여전히 돌린 채였다. “우리가 간다는 건 말도 안 돼요, 엄마. 그러니까, 우리 모두가 가는 건요.”\n\n“왜 말도 안 된다는 게냐?” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“온 식구가 다요!” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그게 바로 그쪽에서 바라는 바요.” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“저는 한 주 동안 혼자 남는다면 무엇보다 좋겠어요.” 릴리가 말했다. “엄마와 언니가 가신다면요.”\n\n“그건 전혀 안 될 말이다.” 지주가 말했다. “레이디 줄리아는 특별히 네가 그 자리에 끼기를 바라고 있다.”\n\n이 일은 처음부터 끝까지 서투르게 다루어진 셈이었다. 레이디 줄리아의 쪽지에서 존 임스를 언급한 대목이 릴리에게 그 계획의 전모를 단번에 알려 주었고, 그 아이의 눈을 하도 활짝 열어 놓은 터라 데일 집안과 드 게스트 집안의 힘을 죄다 합쳐도 그 아이를 저택으로 끌고 갈 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n“왜 안 될 말이에요?” 릴리가 말했다. “온 식구가 그런 식으로 가는 건 말도 안 되겠지만, 언니한테는 아주 좋을 텐데요.”\n\n“아니, 좋지 않아.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“그 일을 두고 야박하게 굴지 마라, 얘야.” 지주가 벨 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. “레이디 줄리아는 친절을 베풀려는 게다. 하지만, 우리 귀한 것아,” 지주는 다시 릴리 쪽으로 돌아서서, 요즈음의 버릇대로 그 아이에게는 거의 성가시게 느껴질 만큼 다정하게 말을 건넸다. “하지만 우리 귀한 것아, 네가 못 갈 까닭이 무엇이냐? 그런 자리 바꿈은 이제 막 몸이 나아 가는 너에게 이 세상 무엇보다 좋을 게다. 제수씨, 이 아이들에게 가야 마땅하다고 말해 주시오.”\n\n데일 부인은 말없이 서서 그 쪽지를 다시 읽었고, 릴리는 사다리에서 내려왔다. 바닥에 발을 딛자 그 아이는 곧장 삼촌에게 다가가 그 손을 잡고서 자기와 함께 창 하나 쪽으로 돌려세웠고, 그리하여 두 사람은 방을 등지고 서게 되었다. “삼촌.” 그 아이가 말했다. “저에게 화내지 마세요. 저는 갈 수 없어요.” 그러고는 입을 맞추려고 얼굴을 들어 올렸다.\n\n그는 몸을 숙여 입을 맞추고도 그 손은 여전히 놓지 않았다. 그는 그 아이의 얼굴을 들여다보고 거기 있는 것을 죄다 읽었다. 이제 그는 그 아이가 왜 갈 수 없는지를, 아니 그보다도 그 아이 자신이 왜 갈 수 없다고 여기는지를 잘 알고 있었다. “못 가겠느냐, 우리 귀한 것아?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“네, 삼촌. 정말 고마운 일이에요, 정말로 고마운 일이지만 저는 갈 수 없어요. 저는 어디에도 갈 만하지가 못해요.”\n\n“하지만 그런 마음은 이겨 내야지. 애를 좀 써 보아야 한다.”\n\n“애쓰고 있고, 저는 해낼 거예요. 하지만 한꺼번에 다 할 수는 없어요. 어쨌든 그곳에는 갈 수 없어요. 레이디 줄리아에게 제 사랑을 전해 주시고, 그분이 저를 심술궂다 여기지 않게 해 주세요. 어쩌면 언니는 갈지도 모르잖아요.”\n\n벨이 간들 무슨 소용이며, 그 방문의 단 하나뿐인 목적이 이루어질 수 없다면 그 자체로 그토록 성가실 나들이를 위해 제가 불편을 무릅쓴들 또 무슨 소용이겠는가? 백작과 그 누이는 릴리와 임스를 맺어 주겠다는 뚜렷한 뜻을 품고 이 초대를 꾸민 것이었다. 릴리는 그 뜻에 맞서겠다는 결심이 굳은 듯했다. 그렇다면 이 일은 통째로 물거품이 되는 편이 나았다. 그는 몹시 속이 상했으나, 그러면서도 그 아이에게 화가 나지는 않았다. 요즈음 들어 모두가 무슨 일에서든 그에게 맞섰다. 집안을 위해 그가 마련하려 한 것은 죄다 어그러졌다. 그런데도 그는 그런 일을 두고 좀처럼 화를 내지 않았다. 하도 가로막히는 데 익숙해진 터라 성공을 기대하는 일이 거의 없었던 것이다. 릴리에게 두 번째 연인을 마련해 주는 이번 일에서도 그가 제 발로 나선 것은 아니었다. 이웃인 백작이 그에게 청해 왔고, 그는 그 청에 참으로 넉넉한 마음으로 응했다. 그는 열의를 품고, 그것이 이루어지기를 진심으로 바라면서 이 시도에 나서게 되었던 것이다. 그러나 이번에도 제가 꾸민 다른 모든 계획에서와 마찬가지로 그는 곧바로 맞섬과 실패에 부딪혔다.\n\n“자네들끼리 의논하도록 남겨 두고 가겠소.” 그가 말했다. “허나 제수씨, 답장을 보내기 전에 나를 한번 만나 보는 편이 좋겠소. 이따가 올라와 준다면 오후에 랠프가 두 통을 함께 가져가도록 하리다.” 그렇게 말하고서 그는 작은 집을 나와 제 쓸쓸한 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n“릴리야.” 현관문이 닫히자마자 데일 부인이 말했다. “이건 너에게 친절을 베풀려는 마음에서 나온 일이란다. 더없이 다정한 친절 말이다.”\n\n“저도 알아요, 엄마. 그러니 엄마가 레이디 줄리아에게 가셔서 제가 안다고 전해 주세요. 제 사랑도 전해 주셔야 해요. 그리고 정말이지 저는 이제 그분을 사랑해요. 하지만—”\n\n“안 가겠다는 거지, 릴리?” 데일 부인이 애원하듯 말했다.\n\n“네, 엄마. 저는 정말 가지 않을 거예요.” 그러고서 그 아이는 방을 빠져나가 혼자가 되었고, 그 뒤로 한 시간 동안 두 사람 가운데 누구도 감히 그 아이에게 가지 못했다.\n\n# 제50장. 데일 부인이 좋은 일에 감사하다\n\n그날 작은 집에서는 저녁을 일찍 먹었다. 짐 꾸리기가 시작된 뒤로 늘 그래 왔기 때문이다. 저녁을 먹고 나서 데일 부인은 손에 쪽지 한 장을 들고 정원을 가로질러 저쪽 집으로 올라갔다. 그 쪽지에서 그녀는 레이디 줄리아에게 거듭거듭 고마움을 표하면서, 릴리가 앓고 난 지 얼마 되지 않아 이렇게 이른 시일에는 나들이를 할 수 없고 자기도 릴리 곁에 남아 있어야 한다고 적었다. 또 이사 일이 한창이라 자신은 초대에 응할 수 없노라고 설명했다. 그러나 다른 딸아이는 삼촌을 따라 게스트윅 저택에 가게 되어 무척 기뻐할 것이라고 했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 편지를 봉하지 않은 채 지주에게 들고 올라갔다. 그것이 그의 뜻에 맞을지 아닐지 정하게 하려는 것이었다. 그가 벨만 데리고 드 게스트 경의 댁에 가는 것을 내키지 않아 할 수도 있는 일이었다.\n\n“나한테 맡겨 두시오.” 그가 말했다. “제수씨가 마다하지 않는다면 말이오.”\n\n“어머, 그럴 리가요!”\n\n“솔직한 사정을 곧바로 말해 주리다, 제수씨. 나는 이 쪽지를 들고 내가 직접 건너가 백작을 만나 볼 참이오. 그러고 나서 그와 나 사이에 오가는 이야기에 따라 초대를 물리든지 말든지 하겠소. 릴리가 갔더라면 좋았을 것을.”\n\n“아, 그 애는 갈 수가 없었어요.”\n\n“갈 수 있었더라면. 갈 수 있었더라면. 갈 수 있었더라면.” 그가 그 말을 몇 번이고 되뇌는데, 그 목소리에는 간절함이 배어 있어서 데일 부인의 마음은 그를 향한 애틋함으로 가득 찼다.\n\n“실은요,” 데일 부인이 말했다, “그 애는 존 임스를 만나러 거기 갈 수가 없어요.”\n\n“아, 나도 아오.” 지주가 말했다. “그건 나도 이해하오. 하지만 우리가 그 애더러 바라는 것이 바로 그것 아니오. 우리 모두가 좋아하는 정직한 젊은이와 한집에서 일주일을 지내지 못할 까닭이 무어란 말이오.”\n\n“그 애가 그러고 싶어 하지 않을 까닭이 있어요.”\n\n“그렇지, 바로 그거요. 그 까닭이야말로 우리가 할 수만 있다면 그 애를 거기 가도록 구슬려야 할 이유란 말이오. 아무래도 제수씨에게 전부 털어놓는 편이 낫겠소. 드 게스트 경이 그 청년의 손을 잡아 주었고, 그를 장가보내고 싶어 하오. 백작은 그가 평생 넉넉히 지낼 만한 수입을 그 앞으로 떼어 주겠노라 약속했소.”\n\n“참으로 후한 일이네요. 저도 그 말씀을 들으니 기뻐요. 존을 생각하면 말이에요.”\n\n“게다가 사무국에서 그를 승진시켰다오.”\n\n“어머! 그러면 그 사람은 잘될 거예요.”\n\n“아주 잘될 거요. 지금은 그 사무국 우두머리의 개인 비서라오. 그리고 제수씨, 이 혼사가 이루어질 수 있다면 그 애가, 릴리가 빈손으로 가는 일이 없도록, 내가 연 백 파운드를 그 애 앞으로 떼어 주기로 했소. 그 애가 그를 받아들인다면 그 애와 그 애 자식들 앞으로 말이오. 이제 다 아셨소. 말할 생각은 아니었소만, 제수씨도 헤아려 볼 거리는 있어야 할 테니 잘된 일이지. 그 사내는 악당이었소. 이 사람은 정직하오. 그 애가 이 사람을 좋아하도록 배우는 것이 좋지 않겠소? 그 다른 녀석이 우리 사이로 내려오기 전까지는 그 애도 늘 이 사람을 좋아했다고 나는 생각했는데.”\n\n“그 애는 늘 그이를 좋아했지요. 벗으로서요.”\n\n“그보다 나은 연인은 다시 못 얻을 거요.”\n\n데일 부인은 잠자코 앉아 그 모든 것을 곰곰이 생각했다. 지주가 한 말은 한마디 한마디가 다 옳았다. 그것은 더할 나위 없이 바람직하고 이로운 상처의 치유가 될 터였고, 그들 모두에게 득이 되는 일이었으며, 릴리에게는 간절히 바라 마지않을 운명이었다. 그저 그것이 될 수 있는 일이기만 하다면. 데일 부인은 딸이 한두 해 안에 존 임스를 두 번째 연인으로 받아들이게만 된다면 모든 것이 잘되리라고 굳게 믿었다. 그리 되면 크로즈비는 잊히거나 미련 없이 떠올려질 것이고, 릴리는 행복한 가정의 안주인이 될 터였다. 그러나 길을 막는 물리적·물질적 걸림돌이 하나도 없는데도 다다를 수 없는 자리라는 것이 있는 법이다. 어떤 일에서 슬픔이 생겨나게 하는 것은 그 일을 두고 마음이 취하는 관점이다. 심장이 언제나 마음대로 주무를 수 있는 것이고 감정을 다스릴 수 있는 것이라면, 사랑이 맞닥뜨리는 어떤 뒤틀림 때문에 스스로 괴로워하기를 허락할 사람이 어디 있겠는가? 죽음도 슬픔을 낳지 않을 것이고, 배은망덕도 그 가시를 잃을 것이며, 사랑의 배신도 세상살이 형편에 끼치는 손해 말고는 아무 해도 입히지 못할 것이다. 그러나 심장은 마음대로 주무를 수 있는 것이 아니며, 감정 또한 그런 다스림을 받아들이지 않는다.\n\n“그 애로서는 될 수 없는 일이에요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “아무래도 될 수 없는 일 같아요. 너무 이르거든요.”\n\n“여섯 달이오.” 지주가 사정하듯 말했다.\n\n“달이 아니라 해가 걸릴 거예요.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그러다 그 애는 젊음을 몽땅 잃고 말 텐데.”\n\n“그래요, 그 사람이 배신으로 그 일을 다 저질렀지요. 하지만 이미 저질러진 일이고, 이제 와 돌이킬 수는 없어요. 그 애는 아직도 그 사람을 예전에 사랑하던 그대로 애틋하게 사랑하고 있답니다.”\n\n그러자 지주는 숨소리보다 낮게 몇 마디를 중얼거렸다. 크로즈비를 향해 내뱉은 그 험한 말은 그가 하려고 해서 나온 것이라 하기 어려웠으나, 저도 모르게 나온 말이라 해도 무척 온당치 못한 것이었다. 데일 부인은 그 말을 들었으나 그 온당치 못함에도, 그 뜨거움에도 언짢아하지 않았다. “그러니 시아주버니도 아시겠지요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그 애가 도저히 거기 갈 마음을 낼 수 없다는 것을요.” 지주는 주먹으로 탁자를 내리치며 다시 한번 험한 말을 뱉었다. 레이디 알렉산드리나가 얼마나 못 견디게 굴고 있는지 그가 알기만 했더라면 그의 마음이 그토록 사납게 흔들리지는 않았을지도 모른다. 또 크로즈비가 이제 드 코시 집안과 맺은 인연을 어떤 눈으로 보고 있는지 그가 헤아려 알 수 있었더라면, 나는 그가 거기서 얼마간 위로를 받았으리라고 생각한다. 우리를 해친 자들은 대개 저희가 저지른 잘못의 벌을 받는다. 그러나 우리는 우리 원수가 갚아졌다는 사실을 아는 만족을 얼마나 자주 놓치고 마는가! 아무래도 세상 이치는, 해친 자는 벌을 받되 해를 입은 자는 앙갚음하고픈 마음을 채우지 못하도록 짜여 있는 모양이다.\n\n“그러면 시아주버니께서 손수 게스트윅에 가시겠어요?” 데일 부인이 물었다.\n\n“내가 쪽지를 가져가겠소.” 지주가 말했다. “그리고 내일 알려 드리리다. 백작이 하도 후하게 대해 주었으니 할 수 있는 만큼은 마음을 써 드려야 하오. 아무래도 그에게 사실을 죄다 털어놓고, 그가 바라는 대로 가든지 말든지 하는 편이 낫겠소. 가서 무슨 좋을 것이 있을지 모르겠구려. 게스트윅 저택에 가서 내가 무얼 한단 말이오? 우리가 다 함께 거기 갔더라면 어려운 일이 얼마쯤 풀렸을지도 모른다고, 나는 그렇게 생각하기는 했소만.”\n\n데일 부인은 자리를 뜨려고 일어섰으나, 그가 자기네를 위해 애써 준 모든 일에 고마움을 표하는 말 한마디 없이는 갈 수가 없었다. 어려운 일이 풀린다는 말로 그가 무엇을 뜻했는지 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다. 게스트윅에서 다 함께 일주일을 지냈더라면 앨링턴을 떠난다는 생각을 어쩌면 접었을지도 모른다는 뜻이었다. 이제 데일 부인에게는 시아주버니가 그 속셈에 대한 갚음으로 자기 머리 위에 숯불을 쌓아 올리는 것만 같았다. 그녀는 자기가 하고 있는 일이 반쯤 부끄러워졌고, 그가 두 딸에게 베푼 은덕을 생각하면 그의 매몰참쯤은 참았어야 했다고 스스로 거의 인정하다시피 했다. 딸들의 나무람이 두렵지만 않았더라면 그녀는 지금이라도 뜻을 굽혔을 것이다.\n\n“시아주버니의 그 마음씨에 제가 무어라 말씀드려야 할지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“아무 말도 마시오. 내 마음씨가 곱든 사납든 말이오. 그저 있던 자리에 그대로 머물러, 우리 함께 그리스도인답게 살면서 악이 아니라 선을 생각하려 애써 봅시다.” 이는 다정하고 사랑이 담긴 말이었고, 그 말 자체에 너그러움과 참아 주는 마음이 배어 있었다. 그러나 그 말은 거칠고 무뚝뚝한 목소리로 나왔으며, 말하는 이는 그 말을 뱉으면서 침울하게 난롯불을 바라보았다. 사실 지주는 말을 하면서도 제가 한 말이 그토록 뜨거운 것이 반쯤 부끄러웠던 것이다.\n\n“어쨌든 저는 악을 생각하지 않을게요.” 데일 부인이 그에게 손을 내밀며 대답했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 그를 두고 집으로 돌아왔다. 이사 계획을 접고 작은 집에 그대로 머물기에는 너무 늦어 버렸다. 그러나 정원을 가로질러 오면서 그녀는 자기가 하고 있는 일을 뉘우치고 있노라고 스스로에게 거의 고백하다시피 했다.\n\n쌀쌀한 이른 봄의 이 무렵에는 잔디밭에서 응접실 창을 지나 집 안으로 들어가는 길이 아직 열려 있지 않았다. 그래서 채마밭을 돌아 큰길로 나갔다가 거기서 앞문으로 들어오든지, 아니면 뒷문을 지나 부엌을 거쳐 집으로 들어가야 했다. 데일 부인은 이번에 뒤쪽 길을 택했다. 그리고 그녀가 현관으로 들어서는데 릴리가 거실에서 아주 소리 없는 걸음으로 나와 그녀를 붙들어 세웠다. 릴리는 입가에 웃음을 띠고 조심하라는 듯 손가락을 세워 보였는데, 그 모습을 보고 그 애가 제 속에 근심을 안고 있다고 짐작할 사람은 아무도 없었을 것이다. “엄마,” 릴리가 응접실 문을 가리키며 거의 속삭이듯 말했다, “저기 들어가시면 안 돼요. 이쪽 거실로 오세요.”\n\n“누가 왔니? 벨은 어디 있고?” 그러면서 데일 부인은 시키는 대로 거실로 들어갔다. “대체 누가 와 있느냐?” 그녀가 되물었다.\n\n“그이가 와 있어요!”\n\n“그이라니 누구 말이냐?”\n\n“어머, 엄마, 바보처럼 굴지 마세요! 당연히 크로프츠 선생님이시죠. 벌써 한 시간 가까이 계세요. 저 방엔 앉을 것이라고는 둘둘 만 양탄자 뭉치밖에 없는데 어떻게 견디고 계신지 모르겠어요. 방바닥엔 그릇이 널브러져 있고, 언니 꼴은 또 말이 아니고요! 엄마 낡은 체크무늬 앞치마를 두르고 있는데, 그이가 들어왔을 때 언니는 벽난로 연장들을 갈색 종이에 말아 싸고 있었다니까요. 언니가 저런 꼴이었던 적은 여태 한 번도 없었을 거예요. 한 가지만은 분명해요. 그이가 언니 손에 입을 맞출 수는 없다는 거요.”\n\n“바보는 너다, 릴리.”\n\n“그래도 그이가 저 안에 있는 건 틀림없어요. 창으로 빠져나갔거나 굴뚝으로 올라가지 않았다면요.”\n\n“너는 어쩌자고 둘만 두고 나왔느냐?”\n\n“그이가 여기 복도에서 저를 만났는데, 어찌나 심각하게 말을 하던지요. ‘들어오세요, 언니가 부지깽이랑 부집게 싸는 것 좀 보시게요.’ 하고 제가 말했지요. 그랬더니 ‘들어가겠습니다. 하지만 같이 오지는 마세요.’ 하지 뭐예요. 어찌나 심각하던지, 오는 길 내내 그 생각을 하고 왔던 게 틀림없어요.”\n\n“그이가 심각하지 않을 까닭이 무어냐?”\n\n“아니, 그럼요, 당연히 심각해야지요. 그런데 엄마는 안 기뻐요? 저는 너무 기뻐요. 이제 엄마랑 저랑 단둘이 살게 되겠지만, 언니가 우리 아주 가까이에 있게 되잖아요! 제 생각엔 누가 무슨 수를 쓰지 않으면 그이는 저 안에 하염없이 앉아 있을 것 같아요. 기다리면서 엄마 오시나 내다보느라 아주 지쳤다니까요. 어쩌면 그이가 언니를 도와 짐을 싸고 있을지도 몰라요. 우리 들어가 봐도 되지 않을까요? 아니면 그건 심술궂은 짓일까요?”\n\n“릴리야, 무슨 말이든 너무 서둘러 하지 마라. 네가 잘못 짚었을 수도 있잖니. 그리고 잔에서 입술까지 가는 사이에도 흘리는 일이 많은 법이란다.”\n\n“그래요, 엄마, 그렇지요.” 릴리가 어머니의 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. “그건 참말이에요.”\n\n“아이고, 내 새끼, 용서해 다오.” 어머니가 말했다. 그 옛 속담을 하필 이런 때에 쓴 것이 모질다시피 한 일이었음을 문득 떠올렸던 것이다.\n\n“마음 쓰지 마세요.” 릴리가 말했다. “아프지 않아요, 오히려 도움이 되는걸요. 곁에 엄마 말고 아무도 없을 때는 말이에요. 하지만 하느님이 도우시면 여기서는 흘리는 일이 없을 거예요. 언니는 행복해질 거고요. 서둘러 해치운 일과 오래 생각하고 한 일은 그만큼 다른 법이잖아요. 그건 그렇고 우리가 들어가지 않으면 저 둘은 하염없이 저러고 있을 거예요. 자, 엄마, 엄마가 문을 여세요.”\n\n그러자 데일 부인은 문을 열었다. 다만 손잡이를 미리 조금 달그락거려 안에 있는 두 사람이 다가오는 발소리를 알아채도록 해 주었다. 크로프츠는 창으로도 굴뚝으로도 빠져나가지 않았고, 빈 상자 위에 앉아 방 한가운데 있었다. 둘둘 만 양탄자 뭉치 위에 앉은 벨과 마주 보고서였다. 벨은 아직도 동생이 말한 그 체크무늬 앞치마를 두르고 있었다. 그 애 손이 어떤 꼴이었는지는 내가 함부로 말하지 않겠다. 그러나 그 애의 연인이 그 손에서 무슨 흠을 잡았으리라고는 나는 믿지 않는다. “안녕하세요, 선생님?” 데일 부인이 여느 때와 같은 목소리를 내려 애쓰면서, 그의 방문에 무슨 각별한 일이 있는 것은 아니라는 듯이 말했다. “저는 지금 막 큰 집에 다녀오는 길이랍니다.”\n\n“엄마,” 벨이 벌떡 일어서며 말했다, “이제 이이를 선생님이라고 부르시면 안 돼요.”\n\n“안 된다고? 누가 이이한테서 선생을 떼어 가기라도 했다더냐?”\n\n“아이참, 엄마, 아시면서.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“제가 알아요.” 릴리가 선생에게 다가가 입 맞추라고 뺨을 내밀며 말했다. “이분은 제 형부가 되실 분이고, 저는 이 순간부터 형부라고 우길 작정이에요. 저는 형부가 우리를 위해 무슨 일이든 해 주고, 한순간도 제 시간이라 우기지 않기를 바라요.”\n\n“데일 부인,” 선생이 말했다, “어머님께서 허락해 주신다면 그리하겠다고 벨이 승낙했습니다.”\n\n“그거야 의심할 것이 거의 없지요.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“저희가 부유하지는 못할 텐데요—” 선생이 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“저는 부유한 게 싫어요.” 벨이 말했다. “그 이야기를 하는 것조차 싫어요. 그런 걸 생각하는 것부터가 사내답지 못한 일이라고 생각하고요, 여자답지 못한 건 더 말할 것도 없고요.”\n\n“벨은 늘 가난 예찬에 열을 올리는 광신자였답니다.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 저는 광신자가 아니에요. 저는 벌어들인 돈이라면 아주 좋아해요. 방법만 안다면 저도 얼마쯤 벌어 보고 싶은걸요.”\n\n“언니더러 나가서 부인 환자들을 왕진하라고 하세요.” 릴리가 말했다. “미국에서는 그렇게 한대요.”\n\n그러고 나서 그들은 모두 거실로 건너가 난롯가에 둘러앉아, 이미 한 식구라도 된 듯이 이야기를 나누었다. 그 일의 됨됨이를 생각하면, 곧 빼어난 미인으로 인정받고 좋은 집안 태생으로 알려진 젊은 아가씨가 그 자리에서 청혼을 받고 혼인을 허락받은 것이니, 일은 어쩐지 밋밋하게, 평범하다고밖에 할 수 없는 모양새로 흘러갔다. 크로즈비가 청혼했을 때는 얼마나 달랐던가! 그때 릴리는 한동안 꼭대기로 들어 올려졌었다. 위태로울 수도 있었으나 어쨌든 아득히 높은 꼭대기였다. 크로즈비는 얼마나 어여쁜 인사말로 맞아졌던가! 작은 집의 운수가 떠오르는 중이라고 관계된 이들이 모두 얼마나 실감했던가. 물론 얼마쯤 가슴 두근거리기는 했으나 그래도 속으로는 크게 우쭐하면서. 그 일이 얼마나 대단한 사건이었으면 릴리가 벌어진 일 앞에서 저를 잃다시피 하고 놀라워했겠는가! 이번에는 대단한 사건도 없고 놀라움도 없었다. 크로프츠라면 몰라도 그 밖에는 아무도 그리 우쭐해하지 않았다. 그러나 그들은 모두 무척 행복했고, 그 행복 속에 안전이 있음을 확신했다. 그들 가운데 하나가 연인의 배신으로 모질게 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐진 것이 바로 얼마 전 일이건만, 그럼에도 이 연인에게서 배신을 두려워하는 이는 아무도 없었다. 벨은 이미 게스트윅의 소박한 제집으로 데려가지기라도 한 듯이 제 앞날을 확신하고 있었다. 데일 부인은 벌써 그 사람을 제 아들로 여겼고, 난롯가에 둘러앉은 네 사람은 이미 한 식구가 된 것처럼 무리를 이루었다.\n\n그러나 벨은 제 연인 곁에 앉지 않았다. 릴리는 크로즈비를 받아들이고 나서는 그에게 아무리 가까이 있어도 모자란다고 여기는 듯했다. 릴리는 제 사랑을 조금도 부끄러워하지 않았고, 손을 살며시 얹어 쓰다듬거나 그의 팔에 기대거나 그의 얼굴을 들여다보거나 하면서 늘 그것을 드러내 보였다. 마치 그가 곁에 있다는 것을 손에 잡히도록 끊임없이 확인받고 싶어 하는 사람 같았다. 벨은 전혀 그렇지 않았다. 그녀는 사랑하고 사랑받는 것으로 행복했으나, 애정을 겉으로 드러내는 증표 따위는 필요로 하지 않았다. 혼인하기 전에 무슨 급한 일이 생겨 크로프츠가 인도까지 갔다 와야 한다 해도 그 때문에 그녀가 불행해지지는 않았으리라고 나는 생각한다. 일은 정해졌고, 그녀에게는 그것으로 충분했다. 그러나 다른 한편으로 그가 하루빨리 혼인하는 편이 좋겠다고 말했을 때 그녀는 조금도 어려워하지 않았다. 어머니가 새로 집을 얻어 들어가려는 참이니, 그 집이 세 사람이 아니라 두 사람의 살림에 맞게 꾸며지는 편이 나을 수도 있었다. 그리하여 그들은 의자와 탁자, 양탄자와 부엌 이야기를 낭만이라고는 한 톨도 없이, 살림살이답고 실속 있는 투로 주고받았다! 그들이 이제 떠나려는 집 안의 가구는 상당 부분이 지주의 것이었다. 아니, 그들이 늘 말하던 대로 하자면 그 집의 것이었다. 더 오래되고 더 튼튼한 것들, 반세기의 손때를 견디는 세간붙이는 그들이 들어왔을 때 이미 작은 집에 있던 것들이었다. 그러므로 게스트윅의 집에 놓을 가구를 새로 사야 하는 문제가 있었다. 데일 부인처럼 얼마 안 되는 수입을 가진 사람에게는 결코 가볍지 않은 문제였다. 처음 한두 달은 셋방에서 지내고 세간은 어느 인심 좋은 창고에 맡겨 둘 참이었다. 그러한 사정이라면 벨의 혼례를 잘 맞추어 셋방 문제가 그 애의 살림 때문에 조금도 복잡해지지 않게 하는 편이 낫지 않겠는가? 이것이 크로프츠 선생이 내놓은 마지막 제안이었는데, 그가 받은 크나큰 격려가 그리 만든 것임에 틀림없었다.\n\n“그건 도무지 될 수 없는 일이에요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “이제 삼 주밖에 남지 않았는데요. 게다가 집이 이 꼴인데!”\n\n“제임스는 농담하는 거예요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“저는 조금도 농담이 아니었습니다.” 선생이 말했다.\n\n“보이스 씨를 부르러 보내서, 언니를 당장 말 뒷안장에 태워 데려가시지 그래요?” 릴리가 말했다. “형부하고 언니처럼 예스러운 사람들이나 할 법한 일이잖아요. 그건 그렇고 언니, 나는 언니가 이 집에서 시집갈 수 있었더라면 정말 좋았을 텐데.”\n\n“그게 그리 큰 차이가 있을 것 같지는 않은데.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“여름까지만 기다렸으면 잔디밭에서 근사한 잔치를 열었을 텐데. 셋방에서 시집간다니 어쩐지 듣기 흉하잖아. 그렇죠, 엄마?”\n\n“나한테는 조금도 흉하게 들리지 않는걸.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“언니가 시집가면 나는 언니를 늘 평범 부인이라고 부를 테야.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n그러고 나서 그들은 차를 마셨고, 차를 마신 뒤 크로프츠 선생은 말에 올라 게스트윅으로 돌아갔다.\n\n“이제 그이 이야기를 해도 돼요?” 그의 등 뒤로 문이 닫히자마자 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“안 된다.”\n\n“제가 진작부터 몰랐던 것처럼 말씀하시네요! 그리고 엄마가 그렇게 끈덕지게 엄한 얼굴로 저를 꾸짖으시는데 저는 한마디도 대꾸하면 안 됐으니, 그걸 참느라 얼마나 힘들었게요!”\n\n“나는 엄한 얼굴을 한 기억이 없구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“릴리가 잠자코 있었던 기억도 없고요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“어쨌든 이제 다 정해졌고,” 릴리가 말했다, “저는 그저 행복해요. 이보다 더 흡족했던 적은 없어요. 정말이야, 언니!”\n\n“나도 그렇단다.” 어머니가 말했다. “이 좋은 일을 두고 하느님께 감사드린다고 참말로 말할 수 있구나.”\n\n# 제51장. 존 임스가 하지 말았어야 할 일들을 하다\n\n존 임스는 래플 버플 경과의 흥정을 성사시켰다. 그는 사월 말께 보름 동안 휴가를 얻는다는 조건을 분명히 못 박고서 개인 비서 자리를 받아들였다. 이 일을 매듭짓고 나서 그는 러브 씨에게 정 어린 작별을 고했는데, 러브 씨는 그와 헤어지는 것을 정말로 몹시 서운해했다. 그는 큰 방에서 작별의 포터 맥주를 앞에 놓고 이야기를 나누었고, 그 자리에서는 그가 늙은 허플의 발 길이와 너비를 두루 재어 낼 수 있게 되기를 바란다는 덕담이 여러 차례 나왔다. 그러고서 커다란 원장을 두 팔에 안고 복도를 서둘러 지나가는 키싱 씨와 마주치자 마지막으로 그 신사에게 톡 쏘는 농담을 한마디 던졌으며, 그런 다음 피츠하워드가 어쩔 수 없이 내놓아야 했던 그 편안한 팔걸이의자에 제 자리를 잡았다.\n\n“다른 친구들한테는 말하지 말게,” 피츠가 말했다. “하지만 나는 이 판을 통째로 걷어찰 참이야. 우리 아버지는 개인 비서 자리가 아니면 내가 여기 붙어 있는 걸 놔두지 않으실 걸세.”\n\n“아, 자네 아버님은 멋쟁이시지,” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“그건 잘 모르겠네,” 피츠하워드가 말했다. “물론 아버지께 집안 연줄이 제법 많기는 하지. 내 사촌이 다음 선거에서 세인트 벙게이 지역구로 들어오게 되어 있는데, 그러면 나야 여기 남아 있는 것보다 나은 자리를 얻을 수 있으니까.”\n\n“그야 당연한 일이지,” 임스가 말했다. “내 사촌이 세인트 벙게이의 의원이라면 나는 화이트홀 동쪽에 있는 것이라면 무엇도 참고 견디지 않을 걸세.”\n\n“그리고 나도 그럴 뜻은 없네,” 피츠하워드가 말했다. “이 방이야 다 좋지. 하지만 날마다 시티까지 들어와야 하는 건 성가신 일이야. 게다가 하인처럼 종소리에 불려 다니는 걸 좋아할 사람은 없지. 그렇다고 자네더러 이 자리에 정을 붙이지 말라는 소리는 아닐세.”\n\n“나한테야 아주 잘 맞을 걸세,” 임스가 말했다. “나는 여태 그리 까다로운 사람이었던 적이 없으니까.” 그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다. 임스는 그 아름다운 팔걸이의자와 더불어 래플 경의 신발을 가져오라는 말을 들을지도 모르는 위험까지 떠맡았고, 피츠하워드는 제 집안사람 가운데 누군가가 세인트 벙게이 자치구에서 의회로 들어갈 그때까지 큰 방의 빈 책상을 차지하게 되었다.\n\n그러나 임스는 포터를 마시고 피츠하워드를 놀려 먹고 키싱에게 빈정거리기는 했어도, 제 새 팔걸이의자에 앉으면서 자못 진지한 생각에 잠기지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 런던에서 보낸 지금까지의 삶이 스스로 자긍심을 가지고 돌아볼 만한 것이 못 된다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 존경하지 않는 사람들과 어울려 살았다. 그는 게을렀고, 때로는 게으른 것보다 더 나빴다. 그리고 참된 애정이라고는 조금도 느껴 본 적 없는 여자의 거짓 사랑에 발이 묶이도록 자신을 내버려 두었으며, 그 여자에게 속아 넘어가 온갖 어리석은 약속을 내주었고 그것이 아직도 그의 머리 위에 매달려 있었다. 래플 경에게 올릴 편지 뭉치를 앞에 놓고 앉아 있으려니, 그는 버턴 크레슨트의 남자들과 여자들을 거의 소름이 돋는 심정으로 떠올렸다. 크래들을 처음 안 지 이제 세 해쯤 되었는데, 제가 가슴을 나눌 벗으로 고른 자가 얼마나 못난 위인이었는지를 떠올리자 몸이 다 떨렸다. 그는 우리가 그를 위해 만들어 줄 수 있는 그런 변명을 스스로에게는 해 줄 수 없었다. 벗에게서 무엇을 찾아야 하는지 미처 배우기도 전에 사정에 떠밀려 벗을 골라야 했노라고 스스로에게 말할 수가 없었다. 그는 세 해 동안 이 사내와 더없이 가까운 사이로 지내 왔고, 이제야 그 벗의 성품이 어떤 것인지에 눈이 뜨이고 있었다. 크래들은 나이로 치면 그보다 세 살 위였다. “그를 내치지는 않겠다,” 그는 속으로 말했다. “하지만 못난 위인이야.” 그는 루펙스 부부와 스프루스 양과 로퍼 부인도 떠올렸고, 릴리 데일이 그런 사람들 틈에 놓인다면 어찌할지 그려 보려 했다. 그녀가 그런 자리에 놓이는 일은 도무지 있을 수 없었다. 그녀에게 로퍼 부인의 거실에 앉으라고 청하느니 차라리 진 술집 술청에서 한잔하라고 청하는 편이 나을 것이었다. 릴리를 제 사람이라 부르는 그런 복이 운명 속에 마련되어 있다면, 그는 제 살아가는 방식을 통째로 바꾸어야만 했다.\n\n참으로 그의 풋내기 시절은 뱀에게서 낡은 허물이 벗겨져 나가듯 그에게서 벗겨져 나가고 있었다. 어른의 감정 가운데 많은 것과 어른의 지식 가운데 얼마쯤이 그에게 찾아들고 있었고, 그는 앞으로 살아갈 방식이 자기에게 여간 중대한 문제가 아니라는 것을 스스로 깨닫기 시작하고 있었다. 런던에 처음 자리를 잡았을 때에는 그런 생각이 조금도 그에게 다가오지 않았다. 내가 보기에 이 점에서 요즘 세대의 아버지 어머니들은 그토록 애를 태우는 그 젊은이들의 속내를 거의 알지 못한다. 그들은 젊은이들이 도저히 갖추었을 리 없는 것을 갖추었으려니 하고 믿어 주면서, 정작 젊은이들이 지니고 있는 것은 조금도 믿어 주지 않는다! 아직 소년인 그들에게 어른의 분별을—생각에서 우러나는 그 분별을—기대하면서도, 오직 그것만이 끝내 훌륭한 행실을 낳을 수 있는 그 생각의 힘은 도무지 믿어 주지 않는 것이다. 젊은이들은 대체로 생각이 많다—제 윗사람들보다 더 많다. 다만 그 생각의 열매가 아직 거기 있지 않을 뿐이다. 게다가 열아홉이나 스무 살에 런던에 풀려난 젊은이들의 즐길 거리를 위해서는 해 주는 것이 너무나 적다. 대체 어느 어머니가 제 아들이 저녁마다 우중충한 방에 홀로 앉아 맛없는 차를 마시며 좋은 책을 읽으리라고 정말로 기대할 수 있단 말인가? 그런데도 어머니들은 그러기를 기대하는 모양이다—젊음의 무분별을 입에 올리는 바로 그 어머니들이! 오, 해마다 아들들이 세상의 온갖 위태로움 속으로 나아가는 것을 지켜보며 좋은 충고와 플란넬 속옷과 신앙 서적과 치약을 그토록 알뜰히 챙겨 주는 어머니들이여, 즐길 거리를 위해, 춤을 위해, 모임을 위해, 여자들과 어울리는 데서 오는 설렘과 위안을 위해 미리 마련해 주어야 한다는 생각은 한 번도 들지 않는가? 그 설렘을 그대들의 아들은 반드시 가질 것이고, 그것을 그대들이 한 가지 종류로 마련해 주지 않는다면 그들은 틀림없이 저희끼리 다른 종류로 마련하고 말 것이다. 내가 만일 아들들을 세상으로 내보내는 어머니라면, 내 마음에 가장 크게 걸리는 일은 이것이리라—더없이 참한 아가씨들이 가득한 어느 집에 아이들을 들여보내 좋은 자리에서 시시덕거리게 해 줄 수 있을까 하는 것 말이다.\n\n가엾은 존 임스는 참으로 고약한 사람들 틈에서 시시덕거릴 수밖에 없는 자리에 놓여 있었고, 이제는 그것이 그러했다는 것을 온전히 알고 있었다. 게스트윅 저택으로 떠날 날이 이틀밖에 남지 않았는데, 래플 경의 편지를 한 무더기 지어낸 뒤 잠시 숨을 돌리며 앉아 있으려니, 떠나기 전에 로퍼 부인에게 통고를 해 두어야겠다고 마음을 굳혔다. 런던으로 돌아오거든 다시는 버턴 크레슨트에 얼굴을 비치지 않겠다고 말이다. 그는 제 결박을 통째로 끊어 버릴 작정이었고, 그렇게 끊는 데에 무슨 대가가 따른다면 제가 할 수 있는 가장 나은 방식으로 그것을 치를 참이었다. 그는 어밀리아에게 못되게 굴어 왔다고 스스로 인정했다—사실 그 점에서는 실제로 저지른 것보다 더 많은 죄를 고백한 셈이었다. 그러나 어쨌든 이것만은 그에게 분명했다. 릴리 데일에게 감히 말을 꺼내 보기 전에 그쪽 일부터 제대로 매듭지어 놓아야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n이 문제에 대해 확실한 결론에 이르렀을 때, 래플 경의 탁자 위에 늘 놓여 있는 그 작은 손종이 울렸고, 임스는 그 높으신 어른 앞으로 불려 갔다. “아,” 팔걸이의자에 등을 기대고 그동안 쏟아 온 대단한 노고 끝에 기지개를 켜며 래플 경이 말했다. “아, 어디 보자! 자네 모레 런던을 떠난다지.”\n\n“예, 래플 경, 모레입니다.”\n\n“아! 여간 성가신 일이 아니야—참으로 여간 성가신 일이 아니지. 하지만 나는 그런 경우에 결코 내 생각부터 하는 법이 없네. 여태 그래 본 적이 없고, 앞으로도 그럴 성싶지 않아. 그래, 내 오랜 벗 드 게스트한테 내려간다는 게지?”\n\n새 후원자인 래플 경이 백작과의 오랜 벗 사이를 입에 올릴 때마다 임스는 늘 부아가 났고, 그 위원에게 맞장구라고는 조금도 쳐 주지 않았다. “게스트윅으로 내려갑니다,” 그가 말했다.\n\n“아! 그래, 게스트윅 저택으로 말인가? 내가 거기 가 본 적이 있는지는 기억나지 않는군. 아마 가 보았을 테지만, 그런 것들이야 잊어버리게 마련이지.”\n\n“드 게스트 백작께서 그런 말씀을 하시는 건 들어 본 적이 없습니다.”\n\n“아이고, 그야 그럴 테지. 그분 기억이 내 기억보다 나을 까닭이 무엇이겠나? 우리 옛 정을 다시 잇게 되면 내가 얼마나 기쁘겠는지 그분께 전해 주게, 응? 한 해 중 한가한 철에—이를테면 구월이나 시월에—하루 이틀 그분한테 내려가는 것쯤이야 나로서는 아무 일도 아니지. 우리 둘이 다 자네 일에 마음을 쓰고 있다니 제법 공교로운 일 아닌가?”\n\n“꼭 전해 드리겠습니다.”\n\n“꼭 그리하게. 그분은 우리나라에서 가장 철저하게 독립적인 귀족 가운데 한 분이고, 나는 그분을 아주 높이 우러러본다네. 어디 보자. 내가 종을 울리지 않았던가? 무엇이 필요했더라? 아무래도 종을 울린 것 같은데.”\n\n“종을 울리셨습니다.”\n\n“아, 그래, 알겠네. 내가 나가는 참이라 내—래퍼티한테 일러서 내—장화를 가져오라고 해 주겠나?” 그러자 조니는 종을 울렸다—작은 손종이 아니라 다른 종을 말이다. “그리고 내일은 내가 여기 없을 걸세,” 래플 경이 말을 이었다. “내 편지는 스퀘어로 올려 보내 주면 고맙겠네. 그리고 혹시 재무부에서 무엇을 내려보낸다면—아니, 재무장관이라면 직접 쓰실 테고, 그런 경우라면 물론 자네가 그 편지를 곧바로 특별 사환 편에 올려 보내 주게.”\n\n“래퍼티가 왔습니다,” 임스가 말했다. 래플 경의 장화 이야기로는 제 입술을 더럽히지도 않겠다고 작정한 참이었다.\n\n“오, 아, 그래. 래퍼티, 내 장화를 가져오게.”\n\n“더 이르실 말씀 있습니까?” 임스가 물었다.\n\n“아니, 더는 없네. 물론 자네가 떠난 뒤에 모든 것이 반듯하게 남아 있도록 잘 챙겨 두겠지.”\n\n“예, 그럼요. 다 반듯하게 해 놓겠습니다.” 그러고서 임스는 래플 경과 그 장화 사이의 면담 자리에 함께 있지 않으려고 물러났다. “저 친구는 안 되겠어,” 래플 경이 속으로 말했다. “도무지 안 되겠어. 재바르지가 못하고—추진력이 없어. 이 자리에 앉을 만한 위인이 못 돼. 백작이 어쩌자고 저 친구를 그런 식으로 거두어 주었는지 모를 일이야.”\n\n장화를 둘러싼 그 자그마한 일이 있고 얼마 지나지 않아 임스는 사무국을 나서서 혼자 걸어 버턴 크레슨트의 집으로 돌아갔다. 그는 래플 경의 방에서 한 판을 이겼다고 느꼈으나, 그쪽의 승리는 쉬운 것이었다. 이제 그의 손에는 또 다른 싸움이 놓여 있었고, 그가 믿기로 그 싸움에서 승리를 거두기란 훨씬 더 어려울 터였다. 어밀리아 로퍼는 수석 위원보다 훨씬 더 두려워할 만한 사람이었다. 그에게는 적에게 치명상을 입혀야 할 일이 닥칠 경우 기대어 볼 만한 튼튼한 화살이 화살통에 하나 있었다. 지난 한 주 동안 그녀는 크래들에게 힘껏 사랑을 쏟고 있었으니, 옛 연인에게 버림받는 벌쯤은 마땅하다 할 만했다. 그는 될 수 있으면 크래들을 들먹여 그녀의 낯에 내던지지는 않을 작정이었다. 그러나 그로서는 반드시 승리를 거두어야 했고, 최악의 경우가 닥친다면 운명과 전쟁의 우연이 그에게 쥐여 준 무기를 쓸 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그가 들어서니 로퍼 부인이 식당에 있었고, 그는 곧바로 제 일에 착수했다. “로퍼 부인,” 그가 말했다. “저는 모레 런던을 떠납니다.”\n\n“아이참, 그럼요, 임스 씨. 우리도 다 알고 있어요. 드 게스트 백작님의 그 고귀한 저택으로 손님이 되어 가시는 거지요.”\n\n“그 저택이 그리 고귀한지는 잘 모르겠습니다만, 보름 동안 시골로 내려가는 건 맞습니다. 그런데 돌아오면—”\n\n“돌아오시면요, 임스 씨, 그때는 방이 훨씬 더 아늑해져 있는 걸 보시게 되기를 바라요. 그 방이 임스 씨 같은 신사분께 마땅한 방이 못 된다는 건 나도 알고 있고, 얼마 전부터 이런저런 생각을—”\n\n“그런데 로퍼 부인, 저는 여기로 다시 돌아올 뜻이 없습니다. 바로 그 말씀을 드리려던 참이었어요.”\n\n“크레슨트로 아주 안 돌아오신다고요!”\n\n“예, 로퍼 부인. 사람이란 이따금 옮겨 다녀야 하는 법이잖습니까. 그리고 저야말로 오랫동안 참 한결같이 이 집에 붙어 있었지요.”\n\n“그런데 어디로 가시게요, 임스 씨?”\n\n“글쎄요, 아직 딱히 마음을 정하지는 못했습니다. 그러니까 그건 제가 무엇을 하게 되느냐에—시골에 있는 제 벗들이 무어라 하느냐에 달려 있어요. 제가 부인과 다투고 나가는 거라고는 생각지 말아 주십시오, 로퍼 부인.”\n\n“저 루펙스네 사람들이 그렇게 만든 거예요.” 로퍼 부인이 깊은 시름에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n“아닙니다, 정말이지 로퍼 부인, 아무도 그렇게 만들지 않았습니다.”\n\n“아니에요, 그런 거예요. 그리고 임스 씨를 나무랄 생각은 없어요. 그 사람들이 이 집을 임스 씨 같은 참한 젊은 신사분한테 어울리지 않는 집으로 만들어 놓았지요. 나도 진작부터 그걸 느끼고 있었어요. 하지만 나같이 혼자 사는 여자한테는 모진 노릇이잖아요, 안 그래요, 임스 씨?”\n\n“하지만 로퍼 부인, 루펙스네 사람들은 제가 나가는 것과 아무 상관이 없습니다.”\n\n“아니에요, 상관이 있고말고요. 나는 다 알아요. 하지만 내가 무얼 어쩌겠어요, 임스 씨? 지난 여섯 달 동안 주마다 그 사람들한테 나가 달라고 통고를 해 왔어요. 그런데 통고를 하면 할수록 더 안 나가려고 들지요. 순경을 불러다가 집안에 한바탕 소동을 벌이지 않고서야—”\n\n“하지만 저는 루펙스네 사람들을 두고 불평한 적이 없습니다, 로퍼 부인.”\n\n“임스 씨가 아무 까닭도 없이 나가실 리는 없지요. 정말로 혼인을 하시게 된 건 아니지요, 임스 씨?”\n\n“제가 아는 한은 아닙니다.”\n\n“나한테는 말씀하셔도 돼요. 정말이에요. 나는 한마디도 하지 않을 테니까요—누구한테도요. 어밀리아 일은 내 잘못이 아니었어요. 정말로 아니었어요.”\n\n“누가 무슨 잘못이 있었다고 합니까?”\n\n“나도 볼 줄은 알아요, 임스 씨. 물론 내가 끼어들 일은 아니었지요. 그리고 임스 씨가 그 애를 좋아하셨더라면, 그 애는 어느 젊은이가 맞아들인 아내 못지않게 좋은 아내가 되었으리라고 나는 말하겠어요. 그리고 그 애는 몇 푼 안 되는 돈으로도 웬만한 처녀들보다 살림을 훨씬 넉넉하게 꾸릴 줄 알고요. 어미 마음이야 임스 씨도 아실 테지요. 그리고 무슨 일이 되어 갈 참이었다면 내가 그걸 망칠 수야 없잖아요. 안 그래요?”\n\n“하지만 아무 일도 되어 갈 게 없습니다.”\n\n“나도 몇 달째 그 애한테 그렇게 말해 왔어요. 임스 씨를 나무랄 말은 하지 않겠어요. 하지만 젊은 사내들은 아주 신중해야 하는 법이에요. 정말 그래야 하고말고요.” 조니는 이 서글픈 어머니에게 젊은 처녀들 또한 아주 신중해야 마땅하다는 말을 굳이 내비치지 않았으나, 속으로는 그렇게 생각했다. “나는 여러 번 바랐어요, 임스 씨. 그 애가 아예 여기 오지 않았더라면 하고요. 정말이지 그랬어요. 하지만 어미가 무얼 어쩌겠어요? 그 애를 문밖으로 내쫓을 수는 없잖아요.” 그러고서 로퍼 부인은 앞치마를 들어 올려 눈에 갖다 대고 흐느끼기 시작했다.\n\n“제가 무슨 말썽이라도 일으켰다면 정말 죄송합니다.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“임스 씨 잘못이 아니었어요.” 가엾은 여인이 말을 이었다. 눈물을 걷잡을 수 없게 되면서 그 속에서 참된 심정이 저절로 밀려 나오고 여자다운 마음이 그대로 쏟아져 나오고 있었다. “내 잘못도 아니었고요. 하지만 그 애가 어떻게 굴고 있는지 보았을 때 나는 이 일이 어찌 될지 알았어요. 그리고 그 애한테 그렇게 말했지요. 임스 씨가 그 애 같은 사람을 받아들이지 않으리라는 걸 나는 알았어요.”\n\n“정말이지 로퍼 부인, 저는 늘 그분을 크게 아꼈고, 부인도 그랬습니다.”\n\n“하지만 그 애와 혼인하실 마음은 없으셨잖아요. 나는 그 애한테 내내 그렇게 말해 왔고, 그러지 말라고 빌다시피 했어요—거의 무릎을 꿇다시피 했지요. 그런데도 그 애는 내 말을 들으려 하지 않았어요. 도무지 들으려 하지 않았지요. 어찌나 고집이 센지, 곁에 두느니 차라리 멀리 보내 버리는 편이 낫겠다 싶을 지경이에요. 집안일에서야 착실한 처녀지요—정말이에요, 임스 씨. 이 집에 그 애만큼 억척스레 일하는 손도 없고요. 그렇지만 내 말이 무슨 소용이 있어야지요.”\n\n“나쁜 일이 벌어졌다고는 생각하지 않습니다.”\n\n“아니에요, 벌어졌어요. 크게 벌어졌지요. 이 집이 점잖지 못한 집이 되어 버렸으니까요. 제일 나쁜 건 루펙스네 사람들이고요. 스프루스 양만 해도 그래요. 내가 이 집을 맡은 뒤로 아홉 해나 나와 함께 지낸 분인데, 오늘 아침에 나한테 시골로 내려갈 작정이라고 말하더군요. 다 같은 이야기예요. 나는 알아요. 나는 볼 줄 알아요. 이 집은 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 점잖지가 못해요. 그리고 댁의 어머님께서 다 아시게 된다면 나한테 화를 내실 만도 하고요. 나는 점잖게 지내려 했어요, 임스 씨. 정말로 그랬어요.”\n\n“스프루스 양도 다시 생각하실 겁니다.”\n\n“내가 무엇을 겪어 왔는지 임스 씨는 모르세요. 저 사람들 중에 제때 셈을 치르는 이가 하나도 없어요—그분과 임스 씨뿐이지요. 스프루스 양은 잉글랜드 은행이나 다름없었답니다.”\n\n“저야말로 그리 제때 치르지 못한 것 같습니다만, 로퍼 부인.”\n\n“아니에요, 잘 치르셨어요. 언젠가 받을 게 틀림없다면 분기 끝에 한두 파운드쯤 더 오고 덜 오는 거야 나는 마음에 두지 않아요. 푸줏간 사람도—그이도 하숙인이 어떤 사람인지 나만큼이나 잘 안답니다—돈이 정말로 들어올 것 같으면 기다려 주지요. 하지만 돈이 나올 데라고는 어디에도 없는 저 루펙스네 사람들 같은 축은 기다려 주지 않아요. 그리고 크래들 씨도 있고요. 글쎄, 그이가 나한테 스물여덟 파운드를 빚졌다면 믿으시겠어요!”\n\n“스물여덟 파운드라고요!”\n\n“그래요, 임스 씨, 스물여덟 파운드예요! 그이는 바보예요. 그이 돈을 가져간 건 저 루펙스네 사람들이지요. 나는 알아요. 그이는 갚겠다는 말도, 나가겠다는 말도 하지 않고요. 나한테는 결국 그이와 루펙스네 사람들만 남게 되겠지요. 그러고 나면 집달리들이 들이닥쳐 이 집 안의 나무토막 하나까지 죄다 팔아 치울 테고요. 나는 그들한테 안 된다고 하지 않을 거예요.” 그러고서 그녀는 낡은 말총 팔걸이의자에 몸을 던지고 여자다운 설움에 자신을 내맡겼다.\n\n“저는 위층으로 올라가서 저녁 들 채비를 해야겠습니다.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 돌아오시면 정말 나가셔야 하는 건가요?” 로퍼 부인이 말했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 그렇습니다. 아무래도 그래야겠어요. 오늘부터 한 달 전에 미리 통고를 드리는 셈으로 알아 주십사 했던 겁니다. 물론 그 한 달치는 치르고요.”\n\n“나는 덕을 보자는 게 아니에요. 정말 아니고요. 하지만 짐은 두고 가셨으면 좋겠어요. 언제든 원하실 때 가져가시면 되니까요. 임스 씨와 스프루스 양이 둘 다 나간다는 걸 첨펜드가 알게 되면 그이는 당장 돈 내놓으라고 나를 들볶을 테니까요.” 첨펜드는 푸줏간 사람이었다. 그러나 임스는 이 딱한 하소연에 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 앞으로 한두 주 동안 제 헌 장화를 버턴 크레슨트에 그대로 두게 할 수 있을지 없을지는, 오늘 저녁 어밀리아 로퍼가 그를 어떻게 맞이하느냐에 달려 있을 터였다.\n\n그가 거실로 내려가 보니 거기에는 스프루스 양 말고는 아무도 없었다. “날이 좋군요, 스프루스 양.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그렇습니다, 임스 씨. 런던치고는 좋은 날이지요. 그런데 시골 공기가 참 좋다고 생각하지 않으세요?”\n\n“저야 도시가 좋습니다.” 조니가 말했다. 될 수만 있다면 가엾은 로퍼 부인을 위해 좋은 말을 한마디 해 주고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n“임스 씨는 젊은 분이시고, 나는 그저 늙은 여자일 뿐이지요. 그래서 다른 겁니다.” 스프루스 양이 말했다.\n\n“그리 다르지 않습니다.” 예의를 차릴 셈으로 조니가 말했다. “스프루스 양도 저 못지않게 따분한 건 싫으실 텐데요.”\n\n“나는 점잖게 지내는 것이 좋습니다, 임스 씨. 나는 늘 점잖게 지내 왔습니다, 임스 씨.” 노부인은 이 말을 거의 속삭이듯 했고, 문이 열려 다른 귀들이 엿듣고 있지는 않은지 불안한 눈길로 살폈다.\n\n“로퍼 부인도 아주 점잖은 분인걸요.”\n\n“그럼요, 로퍼 부인은 점잖습니다, 임스 씨. 하지만 이 집에는 어떤 사람들이—쉬—쉬—쉿!” 그러고서 노부인은 손가락을 입술에 갖다 댔다. 문이 열리더니 루펙스 부인이 미끄러지듯 방 안으로 들어왔다.\n\n“안녕하세요, 스프루스 양? 참말이지 언제나 제일 먼저 와 계시네요. 젊은 신사분 하나를 독차지할 기회를 잡으시려는 거겠지요. 오늘 시티에는 무슨 소식이 있던가요, 임스 씨? 이제 그런 자리에 계시니 소식이란 소식은 죄다 들으실 테지요.”\n\n“래플 버플 경께서 새 신발을 한 켤레 장만하셨습니다. 확실히 아는 건 아니고, 그분이 그걸 신는 데 걸린 시간을 보고 그렇게 짐작한 겁니다.”\n\n“어머! 이제 저를 놀리시는군요. 남자분들이란 세상에서 조금 올라서기만 하면 늘 그렇답니다. 그때부터는 여자란 농담 한마디 건네는 것 말고는 말을 섞을 값어치도 없다고 여기시지요.”\n\n“저는 래플 버플 경보다야 루펙스 부인과 이야기하는 편이 훨씬 훨씬 좋습니다.”\n\n“그렇게 말씀하시기야 쉽지요. 하지만 우리 여자들은 그런 겉치레 말이 무슨 뜻인지 안답니다—그렇지 않아요, 스프루스 양? 나처럼 혼인한 지 다섯 해 된 여자는—여섯 해라고 해도 되겠네요—젊은 사내들의 관심을 그리 바라지 않아요. 그리고 나는 혼인할 때 젊기는 했지만—나이로야 젊었다는 말이에요—너무 많은 것을 보고 너무 많은 것을 겪은 터라 마음까지 젊을 수는 없었지요.” 이 말을 그녀는 거의 속삭이듯 했다. 그러나 스프루스 양은 그것을 들었고, 버턴 크레슨트가 더는 점잖은 곳이 아니라는 제 믿음을 굳혔다.\n\n“그때 부인이 어떠셨는지야 저는 모릅니다,” 임스가 말했다. “하지만 지금도 무엇을 하기에든 넉넉히 젊으신걸요.”\n\n“임스 씨, 나한테 남은 젊음이라면 죄다 헐값에 팔아 버리겠어요—아주 헐값에요. 다만 이것 하나만 확실할 수 있다면요—”\n\n“무엇이 확실하면 말입니까, 루펙스 부인?”\n\n“내가 사랑한 그 한 사람의 오롯한 애정이지요. 여자의 행복에 필요한 건 그것뿐이에요.”\n\n“그러면 루펙스 씨는—”\n\n“루펙스라니! 아니, 쉿, 그만두세요. 내가 속내를 드러내는 말에 끌려 들어가게 두어서는 안 되는 거였어요. 저기 임스 씨의 벗 크래들 씨가 오시네요. 나는 이따금 궁금하답니다. 저분한테서 대체 무얼 보셨기에 저리 좋아하시는지 말이에요.” 스프루스 양은 그 모든 것을 보았고 그 모든 것을 들었으며, 덜리치에 있는 그 작은 방 두 칸으로 옮겨 가기로 단단히 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n저녁 식사 전에 어밀리아와 임스 사이에는 말이 거의 오가지 않았다. 어밀리아는 여전히 크래들에게 온 마음을 쏟고 있었고, 조니는 그 화살이 필요해질 경우 튼튼한 무기가 되리라는 것을 알아보았다. 로퍼 부인은 식탁의 제자리에 앉아 있었는데, 임스는 그 얼굴에 남은 눈물 자국을 알아볼 수 있었다. 가엾은 여인! 살아가면서 그녀의 처지보다 견디기 힘든 자리도 흔치 않을 것이다! 남들이 치를 수 없는 돈을 노상 조르고, 제가 치를 수 없는 돈으로 노상 졸림을 당하고, 점잖음 그 자체를 바라면서도 그것이 제 형편에 넘치는 사치라는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없게 되고, 돈푼을 바라 점잖지 못한 식구들을 참아 주다가 정작 그 돈푼은 얻지도 못한 채 그 시도 때문에 제가 거덜이 났다고 느끼게 되다니! 해마다 이렇게 주저앉아 스러져 가는 로퍼 부인들이 얼마나 많으며, 그들이 어디로 흘러가는지는 아무도 모른다! 우리는 이따금 거리 모퉁이에서 그들을 보는 듯한 느낌이 든다. 찌그러진 모자를 쓰고 얇은 원피스를 입고 어깨에는 낡은 숄의 너덜너덜한 자락을 걸친 채, 아득히 먼 옛날의 점잖던 시절을 아직 어렴풋이 마음에 담고 있는 듯한 낯빛으로 말이다. 그들은 근심 어린 눈으로 사방을 두리번거린다. 마치 거리에서 또 다른 하숙인들을 찾고 있기라도 하듯이. 그들은 날마다의 빵 한 조각과 멀건 차 한 잔을—섭리가 너그럽다면 진 한 푼어치쯤 타 넣은 그 멀건 차 한 잔을 대체 어디서 얻는 것일까! 로퍼 부인은 이런 형편이 어떤 것인지를 생생하게 알고 있었고, 이제 가엾게도 루펙스네 사람들 덕분에 제가 거기에 이르고 있다고 두려워하고 있었다. 이날 그녀는 말없이 고깃덩이를 썰어, 저를 떠날 좋은 손님들에게도 남아 있을 나쁜 손님들에게도 무심하리만큼 고르게 저민 고기를 돌렸다. 이제 와서 어느 하숙인에게 잘 보이고 어느 하숙인에게 밉보이는 것이 무슨 소용이 있단 말인가? 다들 제 몫의 양고기를 가져가라지—셈을 치를 이들도, 치르지 않을 이들도. 첨펜드가 그날 아침 그녀에게 퍼부은 으름장을 죄다 실행에 옮긴다면, 그녀가 그 집에서 고깃덩이를 썰 일도 이제 얼마 남지 않았을 것이었다.\n\n독자들은 어쩌면 식당 뒤편의 그 작은 방을 기억할지도 모르겠다. 앞선 어느 대목에서 어밀리아와 그 연인 사이에 있었던 면담을 적어 둔 적이 있다. 로퍼 부인네 집안의 모든 면담이 이루어지는 곳이 바로 그 방이었다. 여러 사람이 섞여 사는 집에는 어디든 면담을 위한 방이 따로 있어야 한다. 사내가 제 아내와 단둘이 산다면 마음에 드는 자리에서 면담을 할 수 있다. 아들딸은 다 자란 뒤라 해도 면담실이 꼭 있어야 할 까닭을 좀처럼 만들지 않는다. 다만 딸들이 혼기에 이르고 성정이 자립적이라면 그런 필요가 얼마쯤 느껴질 수는 있다. 그러나 식구가 이보다 얽히게 되면—군식구 젊은이가 하나 들어오거나, 이모가 함께 살게 되거나, 전처 소생의 다 자란 자식들이 집안의 단출함을 어지럽히게 되면—그런 설비가 아주 없어서는 안 될 것이 된다. 그런 방 없이 하숙을 치겠다고 생각할 여자는 없다. 로퍼 부인네에도 이 방이 있었으니, 몹시 작고 우중충하기는 해도 아쉬운 대로 넉넉했다—바로 식당 뒤편, 부엌으로 내려가는 계단 맞은편이었다. 저녁 식사가 끝난 뒤 어밀리아가 이곳으로 불려 내려왔다. 그녀는 막 루펙스 부인과 스프루스 양 사이에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 앞의 사람과는 나가지 않는다고, 뒤의 사람과는 나간다고 한바탕 붙을 채비를 하던 참에 하녀에게 불려 나왔다.\n\n“밀리어 아가씨, 밀리어 아가씨,—쉬—쉬—쉿!” 어밀리아가 돌아보니 크고 붉은 손 하나가 저를 부르고 있었다. “저 아래 와 계십니다요.” 젊은 주인 아가씨가 곁으로 오자마자 제미마가 말했다. “아가씨를 꼭 좀 봬야겠답니다요.”\n\n“둘 중에 누구 말이냐?” 어밀리아가 소곤소곤 물었다.\n\n“왜, 임스 나리시죠, 그럼요. 다른 쪽 나리하고는 무슨 말도 섞지 마셔요, 밀리어 아가씨, 제발 그러지 마셔요. 그 나리는 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없습니다요. 정말이지 없고말고요.”\n\n어밀리아는 층계참에 잠시 멈춰 서서, 이 면담에 응하는 편이 나을지 마다하는 편이 나을지 셈해 보았다. 그녀의 목적은 두 가지였다—아니, 그보다는 그 목적이 성질상 두 갈래였다. 그녀는 당연히 존 임스를 애가 타 못 견디게 만들고 싶었고, 임스의 그 애태움이 아주 빨리 효험을 내지 못할 경우에 대비해 무슨 자잘한 재간이라도 더 부려 크래들만은 확실히 붙들어 두고 싶었다. 그녀는 제미마의 평을 대체로 옳다고 여겼으나, 크래들이 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없다는 데까지는 나아가지 않았다. 될 수만 있다면 임스로 하자. 그러나 임스가 안 될 경우에 대비해 다른 활시위 하나는 쓸 수 있게 남겨 두자. 가엾은 처녀! 이런 결심에 이르기까지 그녀도 괴로움이 없지는 않았다. 그녀에게도 마음이 있었고, 그 마음이 주는 힘이 미치는 데까지 그녀는 존 임스를 사랑했다. 그러나 세상은 그녀에게 모질었다. 이리저리 사정없이 그녀를 후려쳤고, 지금 그러하듯 그녀가 누리는 몇 안 되는 좋은 것들마저 앗아 가겠다고 을러댔다. 처녀가 이런 처지에 놓이면 제 마음을 돌볼 형편이 못 된다. 그녀는 이번에는 임스를 만나지 말까 하고 거의 마음을 굳혔다. 그렇게 거절하면 그가 더 애가 탈지도 모른다고 여겼고, 크래들이 집 안에 있어 그 일을 알게 되리라는 것도 떠올랐던 것이다.\n\n“나리가 저기서 기다리고 계십니다요, 밀리어 아가씨. 어째서 안 내려가십니까요?” 그러면서 제미마는 젊은 주인 아가씨의 팔을 잡아끌었다.\n\n“지금 가잖니.” 어밀리아가 말했다. 그러고서 위엄 있는 걸음걸이로 면담 자리로 내려갔다.\n\n“여기 오셨습니다요, 임스 나리.” 하녀가 말했다. 그러고서 조니는 제 연인과 단둘이 남겨졌다.\n\n“저를 부르셨다지요, 임스 씨.” 그녀는 고개를 살짝 젖히고 얼굴을 그에게서 돌리며 말했다. “위층에 볼일이 있었지만, 그렇게 각별히 부르시는데 내려오지 않는 건 예의가 아닐 듯싶어서요.”\n\n“예, 로퍼 양, 아주 각별히 뵙고 싶었습니다.”\n\n“어머, 저런!” 그녀가 소리쳤다. 그 외침이 그가 늘 하던 대로 세례명을 불러 주지 않은 것을 두고 한 말임을 그는 똑똑히 알아들었다.\n\n“저녁 식사 전에 어머님을 뵙고, 제가 모레 떠난다고 말씀드렸습니다.”\n\n“그거야 우리도 다 알지요—백작님 댁으로 가시는 거잖아요!” 그러고서 또 한 번 고개를 홱 젖혔다.\n\n“그리고 버턴 크레슨트로는 다시 돌아오지 않기로 마음을 정했다는 말씀도 드렸습니다.”\n\n“뭐라고요! 이 집을 아주 나가신다고요!”\n\n“글쎄요, 그렇습니다. 사람이란 이따금 옮겨 다녀야 하는 법이잖습니까.”\n\n“그래서 어디로 가시는데요, 존?”\n\n“그건 아직 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“사실대로 말해 봐요, 존. 혼인을 하시려는 건가요? 그 젊은 여자와—크로즈비 씨가 먹다 남긴 것과 혼인하시려는 거예요? 당장 대답을 들어야겠어요. 그 여자와 혼인하실 참인가요?”\n\n그는 그녀가 무슨 말을 하든 화를 내지 않겠다고 아주 단단히 마음먹고 있었으나, 그녀가 이렇게 “크로즈비가 먹다 남긴 것”을 들먹이며 캐묻자 성미를 다스리기가 여간 어렵지 않았다. “저는,” 그가 말했다. “우리 두 사람 말고 다른 누구의 이야기를 하러 온 게 아닙니다.”\n\n“그런 얼버무림은 나한테 통하지 않아요, 선생님. 마음 내키는 대로 아무 처녀나 그런 식으로 다루어서는 안 되는 거예요—아, 존!” 그러고서 그녀는 그에게 달려들어 입맞춤을 퍼부어야 할지, 달려들어 머리채를 쥐어뜯어야 할지 모르겠다는 얼굴로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n“제가 마땅히 그래야 했던 만큼 처신하지 못한 건 압니다.” 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n“아, 존!” 그러면서 그녀는 고개를 저었다. “그러니까 그 여자와 혼인할 참이라는 말씀을 하시겠다는 거군요?”\n\n“그런 말은 조금도 할 생각이 없습니다. 그저 버턴 크레슨트를 떠난다는 말씀을 드리려는 것뿐이에요.”\n\n“존 임스, 당신한테 무슨 일이 닥칠 것 같은지 나는 모르겠군요! 이것 하나만 대답해 봐요. 내가 당신한테서 약속을—또렷한 약속을 몇 번이고 거듭 받았어요, 안 받았어요?”\n\n“또렷한 약속이라고까지는 잘 모르겠습니다만—”\n\n“어머나, 어머나! 나는 당신이 제 말을 뒤집지 않을 신사인 줄로 알았어요. 정말 그런 줄 알았지요. 나는 당신이 아가씨더러 제가 분수에 넘치는 것을 바라는 게 아님을 밝히려고 제 편지를 꺼내 들게 만드는 일 따위는 결코 하지 않을 줄 알았어요! 모르겠다고요! 그것도 우리 사이에 그 모든 일이 있고 나서! 존 임스!” 그러자 다시금 그에게는 그녀가 곧 달려들 것만 같아 보였다.\n\n“저도 잘 처신하지 못한 걸 안다고 말씀드리지 않습니까. 그 밖에 무슨 말을 더 할 수 있겠습니까?”\n\n“무슨 말을 더 할 수 있느냐고요? 아, 존! 나한테 그런 걸 묻다니! 당신이 사내대장부라면 무슨 말을 더 해야 할지 아주 잘 알 텐데요. 하지만 개인 비서라는 사람들은 죄다 속임수를 타고나는 법이지요. 불똥이 위로 튀는 것이나 마찬가지로 말이에요. 어쨌거나 나는 당신을 경멸해요—정말이지 경멸해요. 나는 당신을 경멸해요.”\n\n“저를 경멸하신다면 지금 당장 악수하고 헤어지는 편이 낫겠군요. 아마 그게 제일 나을 겁니다. 경멸당하는 걸 좋아할 사람이야 물론 없지요. 하지만 때로는 어쩔 수 없는 일이니까요.” 그러고서 그는 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n“그럼 이게 다 끝이라는 거예요?” 그 손을 잡으며 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 그렇습니다. 그런 것 같군요. 저를 경멸한다고 하시니까요.”\n\n“모진 말 한마디를 그런 식으로 물고 늘어져 가엾은 처녀를 몰아세우면 안 되지요—내가 이렇게 괴로움을 겪게 된 마당에는 더더욱요. 한번 생각이나 해 봐요—내가 무엇을 바라고 있었는지 생각해 보라고요!” 그러면서 어밀리아는 울기 시작했고, 곧 그의 품에 쓰러질 것만 같은 낯빛이 되었다.\n\n“사실대로 말하는 편이 낫지요.” 그가 말했다. “안 그렇습니까?”\n\n“하지만 그게 사실이어서는 안 되는 거예요.”\n\n“하지만 사실인걸요. 저는 그렇게 할 수 없습니다. 저도 망치고 당신도 망칠 테고, 우리는 결코 행복해지지 못할 겁니다.”\n\n“나는 행복할 거예요—정말이지 아주 행복할 거예요.” 이 순간 가엾은 처녀의 눈물에는 꾸밈이 없었고 그 말에는 잔꾀가 없었다. 한두 순간 동안 그녀의 마음이—그녀의 참된 마음이 앞설 수 있었던 것이다.\n\n“그럴 수는 없습니다, 어밀리아. 작별 인사를 해 주지 않겠습니까?”\n\n“안녕히 가세요.” 그녀는 그렇게 말하며 그에게 몸을 기댔다.\n\n“부디 행복하시기를 진심으로 빕니다.” 그가 말했다. 그러고서 그녀의 허리에 팔을 두르고 입을 맞추었으니, 그것은 그가 분명 하지 말았어야 할 일이었다.\n\n면담이 끝나자 그는 크레슨트로 빠져나왔다. 그리고 여러 스퀘어를—워번 스퀘어와 러셀 스퀘어와 베드퍼드 스퀘어를—지나 런던 한복판을 향해 걸어 내려가면서, 그는 거의 승리감이라 할 만큼 마음이 들떠 오르는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 제 곤경에서 말끔히 빠져나왔고, 이제 릴리에게 사랑 이야기를 꺼낼 채비가 될 터였다.\n\n# 제52장. 게스트윅 다리로의 첫 방문\n\n존 임스가 게스트윅 저택에 닿았을 때 그를 맨 먼저 맞이한 사람은 레이디 줄리아였다. “임스 씨, 뵙게 되어 얼마나 반가운지 모르겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. 그 뒤로 그녀는 늘 그를 존이라고 불렀고, 머무는 내내 놀라우리만치 친절하게 대해 주었다. 황소 사건이 이런 마음을 얼마쯤 자아냈으리라는 것은 의심할 바 없다. 릴리 데일이 연인으로 받아 주기를 그녀가 바라는 사내였으니 호의를 품게 된 것도 의심할 바 없다. 하지만 나는 레이디 줄리아가 우리 주인공에게 이런 애정을 품게 된 가장 강력한 원인은 그가 크로즈비를 두들겨 팼다는 사실이었다고 생각하는 쪽이다. 부인들은, 특히 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트 같은 조심스러운 노부인들은 평화로운 지론을 지녀야 마땅하고 온갖 종류의 폭력을 규탄해야 마땅하다. 레이디 줄리아라면 임스에게 크로즈비를 폭행하라고 부추긴 사람이 있었다면 누구든 나무랐을 것이다. 그러나 그럼에도 무용의 행적은 여전히 여자의 마음에 소중한 것이어서, 여자란 아무리 나이가 들고 조심스럽다 한들 한바탕 두들겨 패는 것으로 이루어지는 즉결의 정의를 이해하고 또 높이 산다. 레이디 줄리아도 그 일을 두고 말해 보라는 요구를 받았다면 크로즈비 씨를 때린 것은 잘못이라고 임스에게 틀림없이 말했을 것이다. 하지만 일은 이미 저질러졌고, 레이디 줄리아는 존 임스를 무척 좋아하게 되었다.\n\n“올라가 보고 싶으면 비커스가 방을 안내해 드릴 거예요. 그렇지만 밖으로 나가고 싶다면 우리 오라버니가 집 가까이에 있을 테니 만날 수 있을 겁니다. 반 시간도 안 되어 내가 봤거든요.” 하지만 존은 난롯가 안락의자에 앉아 안주인과 이야기하는 것으로 흡족한 듯했다. 그래서 둘 다 자리를 뜨지 않았다.\n\n“그런데 이제 개인 비서가 되었으니, 그 일은 마음에 드나요?”\n\n“일은 그런대로 마음에 듭니다, 레이디 줄리아. 다만 그 사람이 마음에 들지 않습니다. 하지만 이런 말은 하면 안 되겠지요. 오라버님과 그토록 절친한 벗이시니까요.”\n\n“시어도어의 절친한 벗이라니! 래플 버플 경이 말인가요!” 레이디 줄리아는 등을 곧추세우고 정색을 했다. 드 게스트 백작에게 그런 절친한 벗이 있다는 말이 썩 달갑지 않았던 것이다.\n\n“적어도 본인은 하루에 네 번쯤은 저더러 그렇게 말합니다, 레이디 줄리아. 그리고 오는 구월에 여기 꼭 내려오고 싶어 합니다.”\n\n“그 말도 하던가요?”\n\n“정말 그랬습니다. 그가 얼마나 얼간이인지 마님께서는 믿지 못하실 겁니다! 게다가 목소리는 금 간 종소리 같아서, 평생 들어 본 중에 가장 듣기 싫은 목소리입니다. 그리고 늘 조심하고 있어야 합니다. 신사로서는, 그러니까, 그러니까 온당치 못한 일을 시키지나 않을까 해서요. 아시겠지요. 사환이 해야 할 일 말입니다.”\n\n“자기 위엄을 너무 겁내서는 못써요.”\n\n“아뇨, 그러지 않습니다. 드 게스트 경께서 저더러 신발을 가져오라 하시면, 게스트윅까지 뛰어갔다 오면서도 아무렇지 않게 여길 겁니다. 그분이 제 벗이라는 걸 아니까요. 그분에게는 저를 부릴 권리가 있습니다. 하지만 래플 버플 경을 위해 그런 짓을 할 생각은 없습니다.”\n\n“신발을 가져오라니!”\n\n“피츠하워드가 해야 했던 일이 바로 그겁니다. 그도 싫어했고요.”\n\n“피츠하워드 씨는 세인트 벙게이 공작부인의 조카 아닌가요?”\n\n“조카든 사촌이든 뭐 그렇습니다.”\n\n“저런!” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다. “참 끔찍한 사람이군요!” 그리하여 존 임스와 그 마님은 아주 가까운 사이가 되었다.\n\n그날 저택의 만찬 자리에는 백작과 그 누이, 그리고 단 한 사람의 손님뿐이었다. 백작은 들어오면서 아주 따뜻하게 맞아 주었다. 젊은 벗의 등을 툭툭 치고, 빛나지는 않아도 사람 좋은 익살로 농담을 던졌다.\n\n“요새 누구 두들겨 팬 적 있나, 존?”\n\n“입에 올릴 만한 사람은 없습니다.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“한데서 낮잠 자려고 수면 모자는 챙겨 왔나?”\n\n“아니요. 대신 황소 잡을 근사한 몽둥이는 가져왔습니다.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“아! 그건 이제 농담거리가 아닐세, 정말이야.” 백작이 말했다. “그놈을 팔아야 했는데, 그 바람에 내 가슴이 반쯤 무너졌지. 그놈한테 무슨 일이 있었던 건지는 모르겠네만, 그 뒤로 아주 사나워져서 말이야. 짚 마당에서 다벨을 들이받아 넘어뜨렸다네! 아주 고약한 일이었어. 정말이지 아주 고약한 일이었지! 자, 가서 옷을 갈아입게. 그날 자네가 만찬에 내려오던 꼴을 기억하나? 크로프츠가 자네를 빤히 쳐다보던 걸 나는 결코 잊지 못할 걸세. 자, 자네에게는 이십 분밖에 없네. 그런데 런던 친구들은 언제나 한 시간이 필요하지.”\n\n“이제 개인 비서가 되었으니 그만한 대접은 받아야지요.” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다.\n\n“아이고 저런! 그렇지, 그걸 잊었군. 자, 개인 비서 나리, 오늘은 넥타이의 위엄을 뽐내며 뻣뻣하게 굴 것 없네. 여기는 우리밖에 없으니 말이야. 내일 그럴 기회를 주지.”\n\n그러고 나서 조니는 시중드는 하인에게 맡겨졌고, 정확히 이십 분 만에 응접실에 다시 나타났다.\n\n만찬이 끝나고 레이디 줄리아가 자리를 뜨자마자 백작은 다가올 싸움을 위한 제 계획을 설명하기 시작했다. “내가 어떻게 마련해 두었는지 이제 말해 주지.” 그가 말했다. “지주가 내일 여기 온다네. 맏조카딸을 데리고 말이야. 자네의 그 릴리 양의 언니 말일세.”\n\n“뭐라고요, 벨 말입니까?”\n\n“그래, 벨하고. 이름이 벨이라면 말이야. 그 아가씨도 아주 예쁘더군. 어쩌면 둘 중에 그쪽이 더 예쁠지도 모르겠어.”\n\n“그건 보기 나름이지요.”\n\n“바로 그렇지, 조니. 자네는 자네 생각을 지키게. 사나흘 묵으러 오는 거라네. 레이디 줄리아가 데일 부인과 릴리도 청하기는 했네. 내가 그 아가씨를 릴리라고 불러도 될는지 모르겠군?”\n\n“아, 저런! 제가 허락해 드릴 힘이라도 있으면 좋겠습니다.”\n\n“그게 바로 자네가 치러야 할 싸움이지. 그런데 어머니와 동생은 오지 않겠다더군. 레이디 줄리아 말로는 그게 다 잘된 일이라는 거야. 자네가 여기 와 있다는 말을 들었으니 당연히 오지 않는 거라고 말이야. 나는 잘 모르겠네. 내 시절에는 젊은 아가씨들이 제 연인을 만날 수 있는 곳이라면 기꺼이 갔고, 나는 그 때문에 그 아가씨들을 나쁘게 본 적이 한 번도 없네.”\n\n“그것 때문은 아니었습니다.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“레이디 줄리아도 그렇게 말하네. 그리고 그런 일이라면 나는 늘 그 애가 옳다는 걸 알게 되지. 그 애 말로는, 자네가 저쪽으로 건너가는 편이 그 아가씨와 한집에 묵으며 여기서 부딪치는 것보다 승산이 낫다는 거야. 내가 어떤 아가씨에게 구애를 한다면야 물론 그 아가씨를 가까이 두고 싶겠지. 같은 집에 묵으면서 말이야. 나라면 그보다 더 재미난 일이 세상에 없다고 여길 걸세. 무도회도 열 수 있었을 테고, 그런 온갖 것도 말이야. 하지만 오라고 억지로 할 수야 없지 않은가.”\n\n“아, 물론입니다. 그럴 수야 없지요.”\n\n“그리고 레이디 줄리아는 이대로가 제일 낫다고 여기네. 자네는 저쪽으로 건너가야 해. 그래서 할 수 있거든 어머니를 자네 편으로 만들어야지. 내가 보기에 진실은 이런 걸세. 내가 이런 말을 한다고 화내면 안 되네, 알겠나.”\n\n“그럴 리 있겠습니까.”\n\n“그 아가씨는 그 크로즈비란 작자를 좋아했겠지. 그자가 그 아가씨를 그렇게 대접한 마당에 이제는 그리 좋아할 리 없다고 나는 생각하네만, 그자를 좋아했던 것보다 자네를 정말 더 좋아한다고 고백하기는 어려울 걸세. 물론 자네가 그 아가씨한테 듣고 싶은 말이 그것 아닌가.”\n\n“저는 언젠가 제 아내가 되겠노라는 말을 듣고 싶습니다.”\n\n“그리고 그 언젠가에 동의하고 나면, 그때부터는 자네가 정한 날에 동의하라고 몰아붙이기 시작하겠지. 안 그런가, 이 사람아? 내 믿기로는 자네가 그 아가씨의 마음을 돌려놓을 걸세. 가엾은 것! 자네 같은 점잖은 사내가 기꺼이 행복한 여자로 만들어 주겠다는데 어째서 제 가슴을 찢어야 한단 말인가?” 이런 식으로 백작이 임스에게 이야기를 이어 가자, 마침내 임스는 제 앞길에서 어려움이 사라져 가고 있다고 거의 믿어 버릴 지경이 되었다. “보름 뒤에 릴리 데일이 나를 장래의 남편으로 받아들이는 일이 있을 수 있을까?” 잠자리에 들면서 그는 스스로에게 물었다. 그러다 크로즈비가 두 아가씨와 함께 어머니의 집을 찾아왔던 그날이 떠올랐다. 그날 마음이 쓰디쓴 나머지 그는 크로즈비를 언제까지나 제 원수로 여기겠노라고 스스로 맹세했었다. 그 뒤로 세상은 그에게 잘 굴러갔고, 이제 크로즈비에 대해 그리 쓰라린 감정은 남아 있지 않았다. 그 문제는 패딩턴 역 승강장에서 정리되었으니 말이다. 릴리가 이제 그를 받아 준다면 크로즈비와 악수라도 할 수 있을 것 같았다. 그의 생애와 릴리의 생애에 끼어든 그 삽화는 고통스러운 것이었으리라. 하지만 두 연인 가운데 나은 쪽에게 마침내 자기를 맡긴 것이 친절한 운명이었다고 릴리가 믿게 될 수만 있다면, 그는 후회 없이 그 일을 돌아보는 법을 배우게 되리라. “그 사람이 그자를 잊지는 못할까 걱정입니다.” 그가 백작에게 그렇게 말했었다. “잊으려는 시도를 시작하도록 자네가 이끌어 줄 수만 있다면,” 백작이 대답했다. “그 아가씨는 너무나 기꺼이 그자를 잊을 걸세. 물론 처음에는 몹시 쓰라리지. 온 세상이 그 일을 다 알고 있었으니 말이야. 하지만 가엾은 아가씨가 그것 때문에 영영 비참하게 지낼 수야 없지 않은가. 자네는 조금쯤 자신을 갖고 일에 나서게. 그러면 자네 뜻대로 되지 않을까 싶네. 모두가 자네 편이야. 지주도, 그 어머니도, 다들 말일세.” 이런 말들이 귀에 울리는데 어찌 희망을 품고 자신을 갖지 않을 수 있었겠는가? 침실 난롯가에 아늑하게 앉아서 그는 백작의 말대로 되게 하리라고 마음먹었다. 하지만 이튿날 아침 일어나 목욕을 하고 나와 오들오들 떨며 서 있을 때는 같은 자신감이 느껴지지 않았다. “물론 나는 릴리에게 갈 것이고,” 그는 스스로에게 말했다. “꾸밈없이 사실대로 이야기할 것이다. 하지만 릴리가 뭐라고 답할지 나는 안다. 그 사람은 그자를 잊을 수 없노라고 나에게 말하겠지.” 그러자 크로즈비를 향한 그의 감정은 전날 저녁만큼 우호적이지 않았다.\n\n그는 그 첫날에는 작은 집을 찾아가지 않았다. 게스트윅 저택에서 지주와 벨을 먼저 만나는 편이 낫겠다고들 여겼으므로, 데일 부인을 찾아가는 일은 이튿날 아침으로 미루었다.\n\n“가고 싶을 때 가게.” 백작이 말했다. “여기 있는 동안 자네 마음대로 부릴 갈색 승용마가 있네.”\n\n“어머니를 뵈러 가겠습니다.” 존이 말했다. “하지만 오늘은 그 말을 타지 않겠습니다. 내일 빌려주시면 앨링턴까지 타고 가지요.” 그래서 그는 혼자 게스트윅까지 걸어갔다.\n\n그는 자기가 지나가는 땅을 한 뼘까지 훤히 알고 있었다. 어린 시절부터 보아 온 문이며 층계다리며 잔디밭을 하나하나 다 기억하고 있었다. 그리고 이제 옛날에 쏘다니던 곳들을 지나면서, 그는 지난날 헤매고 다닐 적에 마음을 채웠던 생각들을 되돌아보지 않을 수 없었다. 이 책의 앞쪽 어디에선가 내가 이미 말했듯이, 어른이 되어 걷는 산책 가운데 소년 시절의 산책만큼 생각으로 가득 찬 것은 없다. 그는 세상이 자기에게 몹시 모질리라는 것을, 제 노력 말고는 기댈 것이 없다는 것을, 게다가 그 노력이 안타깝게도 대단한 영리함의 뒷받침을 받지는 못하리라는 것을 일찍부터 배워 알고 있었다. 누가 그에게 바보라고 말해 주었는지는 나도 모른다. 하지만 그는 한편으로는 제 겸손함 탓에, 또 한편으로는 틀림없이 어머니의 다소 노골적인 미덥잖아 하는 태도 탓에, 자기가 다른 젊은이들보다 영리하지 못하다고 여기게 되었다. 그가 여느 젊은이들보다 늦은 나이에 이르러서야 영민해졌다는 것은 아마 사실일 것이다. 그는 볕 드는 담장 쪽에서 자라지 않았다. 소득세 사무국의 그 자리가 그의 앞에 굴러 들어오기 전에는 아주 보잘것없는 살길들이 그에게 주어졌다. 아니, 오히려 주어졌다고도 할 수 없었다. 그는 그다지 번창한다고 할 수 없는 어느 상업 학교의 조교사가 되어 보려 했으나, 다행스럽게도 산수가 모자란다는 판정을 받았다. 배절 앤드 피그스킨 상회의 가죽 창고에 들어갈 가망이 얼마간 있기도 했으나, 그 신사분들은 사례금을 요구했고 그런 돈을 치르는 일은 어머니의 힘에 도무지 미치지 못했다. 여러 해 그 집안을 알아 온 어느 시골 사무 변호사에게는, 미망인이 눈물을 흘리며 거의 무릎을 꿇다시피 하여 조니의 손을 잡아 서기로 삼아 달라고 간청했다. 그러나 그 사무 변호사는 조니 임스 도련님이 영리하다는 평판을 얻지 못했다는 것을 알아내고는 그를 마다했다. 게스트윅의 오솔길을 쏘다니는 것만이 유일한 낙이었고 릴리 데일을 기려 제 눈 말고는 어떤 사람의 눈도 본 적 없는 시구를 수백 편이나 지어내던 그 시절, 그 어설프고 소득 없고 아무도 우러러보지 않던 시절에, 그는 스스로를 이 땅 위의 짐덩이나 다름없다고 여기게 되었다. 아무도 그를 원하지 않는 것 같았다. 어머니는 무척 마음을 졸였다. 그러나 그 조바심은 그에게 어서 자기를 떼어 놓고 싶어 하는 끊임없는 바람으로만 비쳤다. 몇 시간이고 그는 공중누각으로 마음을 채우며, 릴리 데일이 언제나 여왕으로 군림하는 놀라운 성공을 꿈꾸었다. 그는 상상 속에서 같은 이야기를 달이 바뀌어도 계속 이어 갔고, 그런 상상 속의 행복에 거의 만족하다시피 했다. 그런 힘을 지니지 못했다면 그의 삶에 무슨 위안이 있었겠는가? 열일곱 살에 공부에서 행복을 찾을 수 있는 젊은이들, 책에 파묻혀 지내며 다른 사람들이 지어낸 것들 사이에서 마음이 편안할 수 있는 젊은이들도 있다. 나중에 학식 있는 사람이 되는 것은 그런 이들이다. 존 임스는 그렇지 않았다. 그는 한 번도 공부에 매달린 적이 없었다. 그 시절 그에게 소설 한 권을 읽는 일은 더디기 짝이 없는 일이었다. 시는 얼마 읽지 않았으나 읽은 것은 죄다 정확히 기억 속에 갈무리해 두었다. 하지만 그는 보기에는 더없이 낭만과 거리가 먼 젊은이였으면서도 제 나름의 로맨스를 스스로 지어냈다. 그리고 그를 가장 잘 안다는 이들도 전혀 알지 못하는 온갖 생각을 품은 채 게스트윅 숲을 헤매고 다녔다. 옛집을 향해 에움길로 걸음을 옮기면서 그는 이 모든 것을 이제 다시 생각했다. 참으로 에움길이었다. 그는 숲을 거슬러 올라, 읍내와는 정반대 쪽으로 뻗어 내려가 작은 물길에 닿는 좁은 길을 따라갔던 것이다. 그 물길 위에는 난간이 달린 나무 인도교가 놓여 있었다. 그는 널빤지 한가운데, 자기가 잘 아는 자리에 서서 난간을 손으로 문질러 몇 인치 너비만큼 그 위에 뒤덮인 이끼를\n\n여전히 LILY 라는 낱말이 있었다. 그 글자들을 새겼을 때 릴리는 거의 어린아이였다. “릴리가 나와 함께 여기 와서 이걸 보여 주게 해 줄까?” 그는 스스로에게 물었다. 그러고는 칼을 꺼내 글자를 새긴 자리를 말끔히 긁어내고, 그러고 나서 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 흐르는 물을 내려다보았다. 세상일이 그에게 얼마나 잘 풀렸던가! 얼마나 잘 풀렸던가! 그런데도 릴리가 그에게 와 주지 않는다면 그 모든 것이 다 무엇이란 말인가? 세상이 그에게 얼마나 잘 굴러갔던가! 그 아가씨의 이름을 새기며 여기 서 있던 그 시절에는 모두가 그를 무거운 짐덩이로 여기는 것 같았고, 그 자신도 스스로를 그렇게 여겼다. 이제 그는 많은 이의 부러움을 사고, 많은 이의 존경을 받고, 세상의 높은 자리에 있는 이들에게 벗으로서 손을 잡히는 사람이 되었다. 옛날에 쏘다니다가 게스트윅 저택 가까이 다가갔을 때, 그래도 늘 멀찍이 떨어져 있기는 했다. 심술궂은 늙은 나리가 덮쳐 와 꾸짖을까 두려웠으므로. 그때 그는 자기와 그 심술궂은 늙은 나리가 언젠가 그토록 허물없는 사이가 되리라고는, 살아 있는 어떤 사람에게보다 그 나리에게 제 속마음을 더 많이 털어놓게 되리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했다. 그런데 일이 그렇게 되었다. 그 심술궂은 늙은 나리는 이제, 릴리 데일이 그를 받아들이든 말든 그 돈은 그의 것이라고 그에게 말해 두었다. “실은 일이 이미 끝났네.” 그 심술궂은 나리가 주머니에서 어떤 서류들을 꺼내며 말했다. “그러니 배당금이 나올 때마다 자네가 받으면 되는 걸세.” 그래서 조니가 항변하자, 사정이 그로서는 항변 말고는 달리 어쩔 도리가 없게 만들었으므로 항변한 것인데, 백작은 무뚝뚝하게 입을 다물라 하면서 런던에 돌아가는 즉시 래플 경의 장화나 가지러 가야 할 처지라고 일러 주었다. 그리하여 이야기는 이내 래플 경 쪽으로 돌아갔고, 두 사람은 그를 두고 아주 흡족하게 비웃었다. “그자가 구월에 여기까지 길을 찾아 내려오거든, 조니 도련님, 아니 다른 어떤 달에라도 그런다면, 내 머리에 광대 모자를 씌워도 좋네. 기억을 못 한다니, 참! 사람이 스스로를 저토록 못난 바보로 만들 수 있다는 게 놀랍지 않은가?” 임스가 다리에 몸을 기대고 있는 동안 이 모든 것이 다시 떠올랐다. 그는 말 한마디 한마디를 다 기억했고, 또 다른 많은 말들도 기억했다. 여러 해 전에 들었던 더 오래된 말들, 제 앞날에 대해 그를 쓸쓸함으로 가득 채우던 말들이었다. 세상으로 나아가는 그의 앞길은 가망이 없으며 빵을 벌어먹는 일은 영영 그의 힘 밖에 있으리라고, 그의 벗들이 입을 모아 예언하는 듯했었다. 그런데 이제 그에게 줄로 재어 준 구역은 아주 즐거운 곳에 떨어졌고, 그는 세상이 떠받들기로 마음먹은 사람들 축에 끼어 있었다. 그런데도 릴리가 그의 행복을 함께 나누어 주지 않는다면 그 모든 것이 다 무엇이란 말인가? 난간에 그 이름을 새겼을 때 릴리를 향한 그의 사랑은 하나의 관념이었다. 그것이 이제 아마도 고통으로 가득 차게 될 현실이 되었다. 만일 그렇다면, 그의 구애의 결말이 그러하다면, 그 옛날 꿈꾸던 나날이 지금 이 나날보다, 성공을 거둔 이 나날보다 오히려 나았던 것이 아닐까?\n\n어머니의 집에 닿았을 때는 한 시였는데, 어머니와 누이가 마음이 뒤숭숭하고 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 것을 보았다. “물론 알고 있겠지, 존.” 첫 인사를 나누자마자 어머니가 말했다. “우리가 오늘 저녁 저택에서 만찬을 하기로 되어 있다는 걸 말이다.” 하지만 그는 그것을 모르고 있었다. 백작도 레이디 줄리아도 그 이야기는 한마디도 하지 않았던 것이다. “물론 우리는 갈 거란다.” 임스 부인이 말했다. “그리고 참 친절도 하시지. 하지만 그런 집에 나가 본 지가 하도 여러 해라서, 존, 가슴이 어찌나 두근거리는지 모르겠구나. 결혼하고 얼마 안 됐을 때 거기서 만찬을 한 적이 있단다. 하지만 그 뒤로는 한 번도 가 본 적이 없어.”\n\n“저는 백작님이 무섭다기보다 레이디 줄리아가 무서워요.” 메리 임스가 말했다.\n\n“세상에서 제일 마음씨 좋은 분이야.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“어머, 사람들은 그분이 아주 심술궂다고 하던데!”\n\n“그건 사람들이 그분을 몰라서 그래. 내가 아는 세상 사람 가운데 누가 제일 마음씨가 고우냐고 묻는다면, 나는 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트라고 대답할 것 같아. 정말 그럴 것 같아.”\n\n“아! 하지만 그건 그분들이 너를 그렇게 아끼시니까 그렇지.” 감탄하는 어머니가 말했다. “네가 그분의 목숨을 구했잖니. 하느님의 섭리 아래서 말이다.”\n\n“그건 다 헛소리예요, 어머니. 크로프츠 선생한테 물어보세요. 그분도 저만큼 그분들을 잘 알아요.”\n\n“크로프츠 선생이 벨 데일과 결혼한대요.” 메리가 말했다. 그러자 이야기는 레이디 줄리아의 훌륭함이며 백작이 자아내는 경외감이라는 화제에서 벗어났다.\n\n“크로프츠가 벨과 결혼한다고!” 임스가 소리쳤다. 자기는 야곱에 맞먹는 한결같음으로 구애해 왔건만 그 의사는 단번에 받아들여지는 행운을 누렸다는 것이 거의 낙담스럽기까지 했던 것이다.\n\n“네.” 메리가 말했다. “그리고 사람들 말로는 벨이 사촌 버나드를 퇴짜 놓았고, 그래서 지주 어른이 그 집을 빼앗아 간다지 뭐예요. 그 사람들이 다 게스트윅으로 온다는 건 오빠도 알잖아요.”\n\n“응, 온다는 건 알아. 하지만 지주 어른이 그 집을 빼앗아 간다는 건 못 믿겠어.”\n\n“그럼 왜 오겠어요? 왜 그렇게 좋은 데를 내놓겠어요?”\n\n“집세도 안 내는데!” 임스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“왜 그 집에서 나오는지는 나도 모르겠지만, 지주 어른이 그들을 내쫓는다는 건 못 믿겠어. 적어도 그런 까닭으로는 아니야.” 지주는 존의 구혼을 편들어 줄 참이었고, 따라서 존도 지주를 위해 싸울 의무가 있었던 것이다.\n\n“아주 엄한 분이시지.” 임스 부인이 말했다. “그리고 사람들 말이, 가엾은 릴리 일이 있고 나서는 그 사람들한테 어느 때보다 더 심술궂게 구신다더구나. 내가 알기로는 릴리 잘못이 아니었는데.”\n\n“가엾은 릴리!” 메리가 말했다. “정말 안됐어요. 내가 그 처지라면 낯을 들고 다닐 수도 없을 거예요. 정말 못 할 거예요.”\n\n“낯을 못 들 게 뭐가 있어?” 존이 성난 어조로 말했다. “부끄러워할 짓을 무얼 했다고? 낯을 들다니, 참! 여자가 다른 여자에게 이따금 품는 그 앙심이라는 걸 나는 도무지 이해할 수가 없어.”\n\n“앙심 같은 건 없어요, 오빠. 그렇게 말하는 건 아주 나빠요.” 메리가 자기를 변호하며 말했다. “하지만 아가씨가 버림받는 건 아주 언짢은 일이잖아요. 그이와 약혼한 사이였다는 걸 온 세상이 다 아는데.”\n\n“그리고 온 세상이 아는 게 또—” 하지만 그는 크로즈비가 그 비열함 때문에 흠씬 두들겨 맞았다는 것도 온 세상이 안다는 말까지 이어 가지는 않았다. 어머니와 누이 앞에서라도 그 이야기를 입에 올리는 것은 그에게 어울리는 일이 아니었다. 온 세상이 그것을 알고 있었다. 그 일에 관해 무엇이든 알고 싶어 하는 온 세상이 말이다. 릴리 데일 자신만 빼고. 패딩턴 철도역에서 있었던 그 일을 아직 아무도 릴리 데일에게 말하지 않았고, 그녀의 벗들과 그의 벗들이 그토록 입이 무거웠던 것은 존에게 다행한 일이었다.\n\n“아, 물론 오빠는 그 사람 편이지요.” 메리가 말했다. “나도 매정한 말을 하려던 건 아니에요. 정말 아니에요. 물론 불행한 일이었죠.”\n\n“나는 그게 그 사람한테 일어날 수 있는 일 가운데 가장 큰 행운이었다고 생각해. 그런 빌—먹을 악당하고 결혼하지 않게 된 게—”\n\n“어머, 존!” 임스 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n“죄송해요, 어머니. 하지만 그런 인간을 빌—먹을 악당이라고 부르는 건 욕설이 아니에요.” 그러고서 그는 그 고약한 낱말에 유난히 힘을 주었다. 그렇게 하면 뜻은 더 보태지고 고약함은 덜어지리라고 여긴 것이다. “하지만 그자 이야기는 더 하지 않겠어요. 나는 그 인간 이름만 들어도 싫어요. 처음 본 순간부터 싫었고, 생김새만 보고도 불한당인 줄 알았어요. 그리고 지주 어른이 그들에게 심술궂게 굴었다는 것도 한마디도 못 믿겠어요. 오히려 심술궂기는커녕 그 반대였다는 걸 나는 알아요. 그래, 벨이 크로프츠 선생과 결혼한다고!”\n\n“그건 세상에 의심할 여지가 없어요.” 메리가 말했다. “그리고 사람들 말로는 버나드 데일이 연대를 따라 외국으로 간대요.”\n\n그러고 나서 존은 어머니와 함께 개인 비서로서의 제 직무며 로퍼 부인 집에서 나올 작정이라는 이야기를 나누었다. “이제 그 집이 너한테는 그리 점잖지 못한 게로구나, 존.” 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“사실을 말씀드리자면 애초부터 그리 점잖은 집이었던 적이 없어요, 어머니. 거기 있던 사람들이—. 하지만 제가 훌륭해졌다고 콧대를 세우는 거라고 여기지는 마세요. 저는 우리가 다 함께 로퍼 부인 댁에서 지내던 것보다 더 잘 살고 싶은 게 아니에요. 다만 그분이 달갑지 않은 사람들을 들여놓았거든요. 거기 루펙스 씨 부부라는 이들이 있어요.” 그러고서 그는 버턴 크레슨트에서의 생활을 얼마간 이야기했으나, 어밀리아 로퍼에 대해서는 별로 말하지 않았다. 어밀리아 로퍼는 한때 그가 두려워했던 것과 달리 게스트윅에 나타나지 않았다. 그러니 제 생애의 그 삽화를 지금 어머니에게 알려야 할 까닭도 없었다.\n\n저택으로 돌아와 보니 데일 씨와 그 조카딸이 와 있었다. 그가 아침 거실로 들어갔을 때 두 사람은 레이디 줄리아와 함께 앉아 있었고, 드 게스트 경은 난롯가에 서서 그들과 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 임스는 그들 틈에 끼어들면서 제 처지가 몹시도 의식되었다. 마치 거기 있는 사람들 모두가 그가 사랑을 고백하려고 일부러 런던에서 내려왔다는 것을 알고 있는 듯했다. 사실 그들은 모두 그 사실을 알고 있었다. 벨도 아무한테서 대놓고 그런 말을 들은 적은 없었으나 다른 사람들만큼이나 그것을 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n“투우사들의 왕자가 오시는군.” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“아닙니다, 백작님. 왕자는 백작님이시죠. 저는 첫 번째 수행자일 뿐입니다.” 말은 짐짓 명랑하게 꾸며 낼 수 있었지만 낯빛은 겸연쩍었고, 지주에게 손을 내밀 때는 애를 써야만 겨우 노인의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라볼 수 있었다.\n\n“자네를 보니 아주 반갑네, 존.” 지주가 말했다. “정말이지 아주 반가워.”\n\n“저도요.” 벨이 말했다. “사무국에서 승진하셨다는 소식을 듣고 얼마나 기뻤는지 몰라요. 엄마도 그러셨고요.”\n\n“데일 부인께서 평안하시기를 바랍니다.” 그가 말했다. “그리고 릴리도요.” 그 이름이 입 밖에 나오기는 했으나 너무나 뚜렷한 애를 쓴 끝에 나온 것이어서 방 안의 모두가 그것을 알아차렸고, 벨이 짤막한 대답을 준비하는 동안 모두 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“제 동생이 몹시 앓았어요, 아시겠지만. 성홍열로요. 하지만 놀랄 만큼 빨리 나아서 이제 거의 다 회복됐어요. 건너오시면 동생이 무척 반가워할 거예요.”\n\n“네, 꼭 건너가겠습니다.” 존이 말했다.\n\n“그럼 이제 방을 안내해 드릴까요, 데일 양?” 레이디 줄리아가 말했다. 그리하여 자리는 파했고, 어색함의 얼음도 깨졌다.\n\n# 제53장. 「홉킨스 가로되」\n\n지주는 조카딸 벨이 크로프츠 선생을 받아들였다는 이야기를 들었고, 그 일에 일종의 묵인 같은 것을 내비치며, 정 그렇게 될 일이라면 크로프츠 선생에게 반대할 말은 없다고 하였다. 그는 이 말을 우울한 목소리로 했는데, 얼굴에는 이제 거의 몸에 밴 그 눌린 슬픔의 빛이 어려 있었다. 그가 이 이야기를 꺼낸 상대는 데일 부인이었다. “달리 되었더라면 좋았으리라는 마음이야 나에게도 있었소.” 그가 말했다. “제수씨도 잘 알다시피 말이오. 나에게는 달리 되기를 바랄 만한 집안의 사정이 있었소. 하지만 그 일에 반대할 말은 없소. 크로프츠 선생이 그 아이의 남편이 된다면 내 집에 반갑게 맞아들이리다.” 이보다 훨씬 나쁜 말을 각오하고 있던 데일 부인은 그의 너그러움에 고마워하기 시작했고, 자기도 딸이 사촌과 혼인하는 편을 보고 싶었노라고 말했다. “하지만 그런 일에서는 결정을 온전히 그 아이한테 맡겨야 하지 않겠어요. 시아주버니도 그렇게 생각하시죠?”\n\n“나는 그 아이한테 반대할 말이 한마디도 없소.” 그가 되풀이했다. 그러고 나서 데일 부인은 그의 곁을 떠나, 삼촌이 그 소식을 받아들인 태도가 그 어른치고는 아주 다정했다고 딸에게 일러 주었다. “네가 그 어른이 가장 아끼는 아이였는데, 이제는 릴리가 그렇게 되겠구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“저는 그런 건 조금도 상관없어요. 아니, 상관은 있지요. 그리고 여러모로 그편이 더 나을 거라고 생각해요. 그런데 못된 쪽인 제가 떠나가고 착한 쪽인 릴리가 엄마와 함께 남을 텐데, 엄마가 이 집을 떠나신다는 게 어쩐지 안타깝지 않아요?”\n\n데일 부인도 그것이 거의 안타까운 일이라고 생각했으나, 이제 와서 그렇게 말할 수는 없었다. “너는 릴리가 남으리라고 보는구나.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“네, 엄마. 저는 릴리가 남을 거라고 확신해요.”\n\n“그 애는 늘 존 임스를 무척 좋아했지. 게다가 그 사람은 아주 잘되고 있고.”\n\n“소용없을 거예요, 엄마. 릴리는 그 사람을 좋아해요. 아주 많이 좋아하죠. 어떤 식으로는 그를 사랑하기까지 해서, 저는 릴리가 그 이름을 입에 올릴 때마다 반드시 속으로는 어릴 적의 생각과 공상을 함께 떠올리리라고 믿어요. 크로즈비 씨보다 먼저 그가 왔더라면 릴리에게는 다 잘되었을 텐데. 하지만 이제는 그럴 수가 없어요. 마음이 허락한다 해도 자존심이 릴리를 막을 거예요. 아이, 참. 제가 여태 해 온 말이 있는데 이런 말을 하다니 저도 아주 못됐지요. 그래도 저는 엄마가 떠나지 않으셨으면 하는 마음이 거의 들어요. 크리스토퍼 삼촌은 예전만큼 모질지 않으신 것 같고, 잘못한 쪽은 저였는데 저는 이렇게 정리가 되었으니—”\n\n“이제는 너무 늦었단다, 얘야.”\n\n“그리고 우리 둘 다 릴리한테 그 얘기를 꺼낼 용기는 없을 거고요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n이튿날 아침 지주는 제수를 불러오게 했다. 사업상의 일로 의논할 필요가 생기면 늘 그렇게 하는 것이 그의 버릇이었다. 이 점은 두 사람 사이에 완전히 이해되어 있었으므로, 그렇게 사람을 보내는 것이 데일 씨 쪽에 예의가 없다는 뜻으로 받아들여지지는 않았다. “제수씨.” 데일 부인이 자리에 앉자마자 그가 말했다. “릴리에게 해 주겠다고 한 것을 벨에게도 꼭 그대로 해 주겠소. 물론 한때는 그보다 더 많은 것을 마음에 두고 있었소. 하지만 그랬더라면 그게 다 버나드의 주머니로 들어갔을 테지. 사정이 이러하니 두 아이 사이에 차별을 두지 않겠소. 저마다 연 백 파운드씩 갖게 될 거요. 그러니까 혼인할 때 말이오. 크로프츠에게 나를 찾아와 이야기하라고 일러 두는 게 좋겠소.”\n\n“시아주버니, 그이는 그런 걸 바라지 않아요. 한 푼도 바라지 않는걸요.”\n\n“그렇다면 그에게는 더 잘된 일이고, 실은 그 아이에게도 더 잘된 일이오. 그 아이가 살림에 보탬이 될 것을 가지고 온다고 해서 그가 제 집에 덜 반갑게 맞아들이지는 않을 테니까.”\n\n“저희는 그런 건 생각해 본 적도 없어요. 아무도요. 말씀이 하도 갑작스러워서 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“아무 말도 마시오. 제수씨가 그 보답으로 무언가 해 주고 싶다면— 하지만 나는 그저 내 도리라고 여기는 바를 하는 것뿐이니, 보답으로 친절을 청할 권리는 없소.”\n\n“하지만 저희가 시아주버니께 무슨 친절을 베풀 수 있겠어요?”\n\n“그 집에 그대로 있으시오.” 이 마지막 말을 하면서 그는 다시 성이 난 사람처럼 말했다. 마치 다시 그들에게 법을 내려놓는 사람처럼, 마치 자기에게 마땅히 돌아와야 하고 그녀가 마땅히 져야 할 의무를 일러 주는 사람처럼 말했다. 그의 목소리는 여느 때처럼 엄했고 얼굴은 여느 때처럼 시었다. 그는 친절을 청하는 것이라고 말했으나, 그토록 위압적인 목소리로 친절을 청한 사람은 세상에 분명 아무도 없었을 것이다. “그 집에 그대로 있으시오.” 그러고 나서 그는 더 할 말이 없다는 듯 제 탁자 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n그러나 데일 부인은 이제야 마침내 그의 마음과 참된 성품을 얼마쯤 이해하기 시작하고 있었다. 그는 무언가를 줄 때에는 다정하고 너그러울 수 있었으나, 청할 때에는 엄하지 않고서는 달리 될 수 없는 사람이었다. 아니, 그는 청할 줄을 몰랐다. 그는 요구할 줄밖에 몰랐다.\n\n“저희는 이미 일을 너무 많이 벌여 놓았어요.” 데일 부인이 사정하기 시작했다.\n\n“됐소, 됐소, 됐소. 그 이야기를 하려던 것은 아니오. 짐이란 싸기보다 푸는 쪽이 쉬운 법이지. 하지만, 어쨌든— 아무것도 아니오. 벨은 오늘 오후 나와 함께 게스트윅 저택으로 갈 거요. 두 시까지 이리로 올려 보내시오. 트렁크는 그라임스가 돌아서 실어다 줄 수 있을 테지.”\n\n“아, 네. 물론이죠.”\n\n“그리고 그 아이가 떠나기 전에 돈 이야기는 하지 마시오. 나는 그러지 않았으면 좋겠소. 무슨 말인지 알 거요. 하지만 크로프츠를 만나거든 나에게 오라고 이르시오. 실은 이 일을 빨리 진행할 요량이면 당장 오는 편이 낫겠소.”\n\n데일 부인이 지주의 마지막 말에 담긴 당부를 어기리라는 것은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 딸들에게 돌아가서 그날 아침 삼촌과 나눈 이야기의 결말을 알려 주지 않는다는 것은 아예 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 연 백 파운드는 의사의 수수한 살림에서 넉넉함과 궁핍함을, 수수한 넉넉함과 견딜 만한 궁핍함을 가르는 차이를 통째로 만들어 낼 것이었다. 물론 그녀는 딸들에게 이야기했고, 벨에게는 그 일을 모르는 척 시치미를 떼야 한다고 일러 두었다.\n\n“저는 당장 고맙다고 말씀드리겠어요.” 벨이 말했다. “그리고 전혀 기대하지 않았지만 받기를 마다할 만큼 자존심이 세지도 않다고 말씀드릴래요.”\n\n“제발 그러지 말아라, 얘야. 지금은 아니야. 너희에게 말한 것만으로도 나는 일종의 약속을 어기는 셈이란다. 다만 나 혼자 담아 두고 있을 수가 없었을 뿐이야. 게다가 그 어른은 마음을 어지럽히는 일이 어찌나 많은지! 지금은 그 일을 입에 담지 않으시지만, 너와 버나드 일로 마음이 반쯤 무너지셨단다.” 그러고 나서 데일 부인은 지주가 방금 어떤 부탁을 했는지, 또 그것을 어떤 태도로 했는지 딸들에게 이야기했다. “그렇게 말씀하시는 목소리에 내 눈에는 눈물이 고였단다. 우리가 아무 일도 벌이지 않았더라면 좋았을걸 하는 마음이 들 지경이야.”\n\n“하지만 엄마.” 릴리가 말했다. “그게 그 어른께 무슨 차이가 있어요? 우리가 곁에 있는 게 그 어른께는 늘 성가신 일이었다는 걸 엄마도 아시잖아요. 그 어른은 정말로 우리를 원한 적이 없어요. 벨 언니가 당신이 아끼는 사람과 혼인하리라고 여기실 때에나 언니가 거기 있는 걸 좋아하셨죠.”\n\n“못되게 굴지 마라, 릴리.”\n\n“못되게 굴려는 건 아니에요. 버나드가 그 어른이 아끼는 사람이면 안 될 게 뭐 있어요? 저도 버나드를 무척 사랑하고, 크리스토퍼 삼촌이 버나드를 그토록 아끼신다는 게 그 어른의 가장 좋은 점이라고 늘 생각했는걸요. 엄마도 아시다시피 저는 그게 소용없는 일이라는 걸 알고 있었어요. 물론 알고 있었죠. 저는 어떤 다른 사람에 관한 일이라면 전부 알고 있었으니까요. 그렇지만 버나드는 그 어른이 아끼는 사람이에요.”\n\n“그 어른은 당신 나름의 방식으로 너희를 다 사랑하신단다.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 그 어른이 엄마를 사랑하시나요? 그게 문제죠.” 릴리가 말했다. “우리한테 하신 일이라면 무엇이든 용서할 수 있었고, 우리한테 하신 말씀이라면 어떤 말이든 참아 넘길 수 있었어요. 그 어른은 우리를 아이로 여기시니까요. 벨 언니에게 연 백 파운드를 주신다고 해서, 여전히 엄마 위에 군림하신다면 엄마가 이 집에서 편안해지지는 않을 거예요. 이웃이 이웃답게 굴면 가까운 이웃살이는 아주 좋은 것이지요. 그렇지만 크리스토퍼 삼촌은 이웃답지 않으셨어요. 그 어른은 엄마에게 시숙만도 못한 사람이 되는 조건으로 우리에게는 삼촌 이상이 되고자 하셨어요. 언니와 저는 그런 조건으로 얻는 애정이라면 가질 값어치가 없다고 늘 여겨 왔어요.”\n\n“우리가 잘못한 것 같다는 생각이 거의 드는구나.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “하지만 정말이지 나는 그 일이 그 어른께 그토록 큰일이 되리라고는 한 번도 생각지 못했단다.”\n\n벨이 떠나고 나자 데일 부인과 릴리는 며칠 전부터 셋이 매달려 온 그 일을 그리 힘차게 이어 갈 마음이 나지 않았다. 처음 짐을 싸기 시작했을 때에는 그 일에 생기와 설렘이 있었으나, 이제는 그것이 지겹고 따분하고 넌더리 나는 일이 되어 있었다. 실은 이미 어지간히 해 놓은 터라 그들이 매달릴 일도 얼마 남아 있지 않았다. 마지막으로 끈을 조이고 잠금쇠를 채우는 일, 그리고 정말로 떠나기 직전이 되어야 마무리할 수 있는 자질구레한 물건들을 마지막으로 그러모으는 일 말고는 없었다. 지주는 짐을 푸는 편이 싸는 편보다 쉬울 거라고 말했었다. 그리하여 데일 부인은 광주리와 궤짝 사이를 거닐면서, 그 모든 물건을 제자리에 도로 갖다 놓는 일이 과연 그토록 고약할까 하고 헤아려 보기 시작했다. 그녀는 이런 생각을 릴리에게는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 릴리 또한 속으로 무슨 생각을 하고 있었든 어머니에게 그런 말을 꺼내지 않았다.\n\n“홉킨스가 누구보다도 우리를 더 아쉬워할 것 같아요.” 그녀가 말했다. “홉킨스는 잔소리할 상대가 없어질 테니까요.”\n\n바로 그때 홉킨스가 응접실 창가에 나타나 이야기를 나누고 싶다는 뜻을 내비쳤다.\n\n“돌아서 들어오세요.” 릴리가 말했다. “창을 열어 두기에는 너무 추워요. 저는 늘 그이를 집 안으로 들이는 게 좋아요. 의자며 탁자 앞에서는 좀 기가 죽거든요. 어쩌면 그이한테 벅찬 건 융단인지도 모르죠. 바깥 자갈길에서는 어찌나 무서운 폭군인지 몰라요. 온실에서는 사람을 아예 짓밟다시피 한다니까요!”\n\n홉킨스가 응접실 문께에 나타났을 때, 그의 태도는 릴리의 분별이 옳았음을 증명해 주는 듯했다. 말투에도 몸가짐에도 위세 부리는 데가 조금도 없었고, 의자와 탁자가 기대할 만한 온갖 경의를 다 표하는 듯이 보였다.\n\n“그러면 정말로 가시는 겁니까요, 마님.” 그가 데일 부인의 발치를 내려다보며 말했다.\n\n데일 부인이 곧바로 대답하지 않자 릴리가 말했다. “그래요, 홉킨스. 이제 며칠 안 남았어요. 게스트윅에서 가끔은 볼 수 있겠지요.”\n\n“흠!” 홉킨스가 말했다. “그러면 정말로 가시는군입쇼! 저는 일이 이 지경까지 오리라고는 생각도 못 했습니다요, 아가씨. 정말이지 못 했습니다요. 그리고 이렇게 되어서도 안 되는 일이고요. 하기야 물론 제가 나설 자리는 아닙지요.”\n\n“사람이란 때로 사는 곳을 옮기기도 하는 법이잖아요, 홉킨스.” 데일 부인이 말했다. 임스가 로퍼 부인에게 제가 떠나는 것을 변명하려 들 때 썼던 것과 똑같은 논거였다.\n\n“예, 마님. 제가 무슨 말을 할 처지는 아닙지요. 하지만 이 말씀만은 드리겠습니다요. 저는 여기 지주 어른 댁 언저리에서 사내로도 아이로도, 그러니까 한평생을 살아왔습니다요. 여기서 태어났으니까요, 데일 부인도 아시다시피 말입니다요. 그런데 제가 이곳에서 보아 온 온갖 나쁜 일 가운데 이번 일이 단연 제일 고약합니다요.”\n\n“아이, 홉킨스!”\n\n“무엇보다 고약합니다요, 마님. 무엇보다 고약합지요! 이 일이 지주 어른을 죽이고 말 겁니다요! 그건 세상에 의심할 여지가 없습니다요. 이 일이 그 노인의 명줄을 끊고 말 겁니다요.”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리예요, 홉킨스.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“예, 아가씨. 말도 안 되는 소리가 아니라고는 하지 않겠습니다요. 다만 두고 보시라는 겁니다요. 버나드 도련님도 떠나 버리셨지요. 그리고 다들 그러는데 그분은 이곳을 그리 아끼신 적이 한 번도 없다더군입쇼. 인도로 가신다고들 합니다요. 게다가 벨 아가씨는 시집을 가시게 되었고요. 그야 물론 다 마땅한 일입지요. 안 될 게 뭐 있겠습니까요? 그러면 릴리 아가씨도 안 될 게 뭐 있겠습니까요?”\n\n“어쩌면 언젠가는 그렇게 되겠지요, 홉킨스.”\n\n“오늘만 한 날이 어디 있겠습니까요, 릴리 아가씨. 그리고 저는 이 말씀을 드리겠습니다요. 그자를 두들겨 패 준 사내라면 제 마음에 꼭 드는 사람일 겁니다요.” 홉킨스가 순간의 격정에 못 이겨 내뱉은 이 말을 릴리는 도무지 알아들을 수 없었고, 그 말을 들으며 몸을 떤 데일 부인은 설명을 청하는 말을 한마디도 하지 않았다. “하지만.” 홉킨스가 말을 이었다. “그야 될 대로 될 일입지요, 릴리 아가씨. 그리고 아가씨는 섭리의 손안에 계시고요. 남들도 다 그렇듯이 말입니다요.”\n\n“그렇고말고요, 홉킨스.”\n\n“그렇지만 마님께서는 어째서 그렇게 떠나려고만 하십니까요? 마님이야 시집가실 것도 아니지 않습니까요. 여기 집이 있고, 마님이 계시고, 지주 어른이 계신데, 어째서 떠나려 하셔야 합니까요? 이렇게 한꺼번에 우수수 떠나 버리는 게 좋은 일일 리가 없습니다요. 그저 모든 걸 다 부수어 흩어 놓는 짓입지요. 마치 아무한테도 무엇 하나 성에 차지 않는다는 듯이 말입니다요. 저는 한 번도 떠나 본 적이 없고, 그런 건 참아 낼 수가 없습니다요.”\n\n“저기, 홉킨스. 이제 다 정해진 일이에요.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “그리고 안타깝지만 되돌릴 수는 없어요.”\n\n“정해졌다굽쇼. 그렇다면 이걸 말씀해 보십쇼. 데일 부인, 그 어른이 거기서 혼자 외따로, 모진 말 한마디 건넬 사람도 없이 지내시리라고 생각하십니까요? 저나 딩글스 말고는 말입니다요. 졸리프는 아무도 없느니만 못한 자입지요. 워낙 저부터가 지독하게 모진 사람이니까요. 그 어른이 그걸 견뎌 내실 리가 없습니다요. 마님이 떠나시면, 데일 부인, 버나드 도련님이 열두 달도 안 되어 지주가 되실 겁니다요. 그러면 그분은 인도에서 돌아오시겠지요, 아마?”\n\n“나는 내 시아주버니가 그런 식으로 받아들이시리라고는 생각하지 않아요, 홉킨스.”\n\n“아이고, 마님, 마님은 그 어른을 모르십니다요. 제가 아는 만큼은 모르시지요. 그 어른의 안팎이며 구석구석 주름살까지 말입니다요. 저는 그 어른을 마흔 해 동안 손대며 길러 온 저 늙은 사과나무들만큼이나 잘 압니다요. 저 늙고 옹이 진 나무들에는 썩은 가지가 수두룩해서, 어떤 사람들은 저것들이 서 있는 땅값어치도 못 한다고 합니다요. 하지만 저는 어디로 수액이 흐르는지 알고, 꽃이 피어나면 어디에 열매가 제일 달게 달릴지도 압니다요. 저 늙은 나무 하나 죽이는 데는 대단한 것도 필요 없습니다요. 그렇지만 잘만 다루면 아직 그 안에 목숨이 남아 있습지요.”\n\n“나야 시아주버니의 목숨이 오래도록 지켜지기를 바라지요.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그러면 떠나지 마십쇼, 마님. 게스트윅의 그 끔찍한 셋집으로는 말입니다요. 데일 집안 같은 분들한테는 그런 데가 끔찍하다는 말씀입니다요. 물론 제가 나설 자리는 아닙지요, 마님. 그리고 저는 그저 온실에서 무엇을 가져가고 싶으신지 여쭈러 올라온 것뿐입니다요.”\n\n“아, 아무것도 필요 없어요, 홉킨스. 고마워요.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그 어른께서 저더러 마님을 위해 제일 좋은 것으로 골라 담아 두라고 하셨습니다요. 그리고 저는 그렇게 할 작정입니다요.” 그러면서 홉킨스는 자기가 지주를 두고 하는 말임을 고갯짓으로 알렸다.\n\n“놓아 둘 자리도 없을 텐데요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“몇 그루쯤은 보내 드려야지요, 아가씨. 마음이라도 좀 밝아지시게 말입니다요. 저는 아가씨들이 거기서 아주 쓸쓸해하실까 봐 걱정입니다요. 그리고 그 의사 선생으로 말하자면, 제대로 된 정원이라 할 만한 건 없고 뒤꼍에 자그마한 자리가 하나 있을 뿐입지요.”\n\n“하지만 이 정든 옛집 것을 빼앗을 수야 없죠.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 그게 무슨 대수겠습니까요? 지주 어른은 하도 비참해지셔서 여기다 양을 풀어 놓아 이곳을 망쳐 놓으시거나, 아니면 정원을 갈아엎게 하실 겁니다요. 안 그러시나 두고 보십쇼. 이곳으로 말하자면, 마님이 떠나시면 이곳은 아주 끝장난 겁니다요. 설마 그 어른이 작은 집을 낯선 사람들에게 세놓으시겠습니까요. 지주 어른은 아무래도 그런 부류가 아니십니다요.”\n\n“아이고!” 홉킨스가 물러가자마자 데일 부인이 탄식했다.\n\n“왜 그러세요, 엄마? 그이는 정 많은 노인이지만, 그이가 하는 말이 정말로 엄마를 괴롭게 할 리는 없잖아요.”\n\n“어떻게 하는 것이 옳은지 알기가 참 어렵구나. 나는 이기적으로 굴 생각이 아니었는데, 어쩐지 내가 세상에서 가장 이기적인 짓을 하고 있는 것만 같아.”\n\n“아니에요, 엄마. 이건 이기적인 것과는 거리가 멀어요. 게다가 이 일을 벌인 건 저희지 엄마가 아니에요.”\n\n“릴리야, 있잖니. 나도 홉킨스가 말한 그 오래된 삶의 방식을 부수어 버린다는 느낌을 똑같이 갖고 있단다. 나는 이곳에서 벗어나면 기쁠 줄 알았는데, 막상 때가 오고 보니 그것이 두렵구나.”\n\n“후회하신다는 말씀이에요?”\n\n데일 부인은 도로 거두어들일 수 없는 말로 스스로를 묶어 버리기가 두려워 딸에게 곧바로 대답하지 않았다. 그러나 마침내 그녀는 말했다. “그래, 릴리. 후회하는 것 같구나. 잘한 일이 아니었던 것 같아.”\n\n“그럼 되돌려요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n그날 게스트윅 저택의 만찬 자리는 그리 흥겹지 않았다. 백작이 손님들을 즐겁게 해 주려고 제 힘닿는 데까지 다 했는데도 그러했다. 그러나 흥이란 것이 이 집에서는 저절로 우러나는 법이 없었으니, 이미 보았다시피 이 집은 그 성질에서 코시 성의 저 또 다른 백작의 집과는 아주 딴판이었다. 드 코시 백작부인은 어쨌거나 사람들로 집을 가득 채우고 맞아들여 대접할 줄을 알았다. 하기야 그렇게 하는 버릇 때문에 집안 살림의 은밀한 자리에서 곤란한 문제들이 일어나기는 했겠지만 말이다. 레이디 줄리아는 그럴 줄을 몰랐다. 그러나 레이디 줄리아는 하인을 더 쓰는 비용을 해명하라는 요구를 받은 적이 한 번도 없었고, 도대체 누가 포도주 상인의 계산서를 치를 것이냐고 한 주에 두 번씩 추궁당한 적도 없었다. 드 게스트 경과 레이디 줄리아 자신들로 말하자면 그편이 가장 나았다고 나는 생각한다. 그러나 어쩌다 찾아드는 손님들에 관해서라면, 이 집이 따분했다는 것을 나는 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 지금 백작의 식탁에 모여 앉은 사람들은 서로 한자리에 있을 때 그리 활기찰 수 있으리라고 기대하기 어려운 이들이었다. 지주는 사교 자리에 그리 나서는 사람이 아니었고, 사람이 가득한 식탁을 즐겁게 해 주는 데에는 익숙하지 않았다. 이날 그는 레이디 줄리아 옆에 앉아 이따금 나라 형편에 대해 몇 마디를 그녀에게 웅얼거렸다. 임스 부인은 거기 있는 모든 사람이 몹시 두려웠고, 그중에서도 제 옆자리에 앉은 백작이 가장 두려웠다. 그녀는 그를 줄곧 “백작님”이라고 불렀는데, 그렇게 부르는 제 목소리에 스스로 놀랄 지경이라는 것이 그 목소리에 다 드러났다. 보이스 씨 부부도 와 있어서, 목사는 레이디 줄리아의 반대편에 앉았고 목사의 아내는 백작의 반대편에 앉았다. 보이스 부인은 자기가 이 자리에 아주 익숙하다는 것을 보이려고 몹시 애를 썼고 아마 누구보다도 말을 많이 했다. 그러나 그렇게 하느라 백작을 어찌나 절묘하게 지루하게 만들었던지, 백작은 이튿날 아침 존 임스에게 그 부인이 그 황소보다 더하다고 말했다. 목사는 저녁을 먹기는 했으나 앞뒤의 감사 기도 사이에는 거의 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 묵직하고 분별 있고 굼뜬 사람으로, 제 자신과 제 능력을 잘 알고 있었다. “쇠고기 조림이 유난히 좋았소.” 집으로 돌아가면서 그가 말했다. “우리는 왜 쇠고기를 저렇게 조려 먹지 못하는 거요?” “우리는 요리사에게 연 예순 파운드를 주지 않으니까요.” 보이스 부인이 말했다. “열여섯 파운드 받는 여자도 예순 파운드 받는 여자만큼 쇠고기를 잘 조릴 수 있소.” 그가 말했다. “다만 지켜보고 챙겨 주기만 하면 되는 거요.” 백작 자신은 일종의 흥겨움을 지닌 사람이었다. 그에게는 마음의 가벼움이 있어서 단둘이 마주 앉은 상대에게는 곧잘 유쾌한 말벗이 되어 주었다. 존 임스는 그를 당대에 가장 발랄한 노인이라고, 소년의 장난기와 흥을 거의 그대로 지닌 노인이라고 여겼다. 그러나 이 기질은 존 임스 앞에서라면 드러났을지언정, 존 임스의 어머니와 누이에다 지주와 앨링턴의 목사와 목사의 아내까지 한꺼번에 즐겁게 해 주기에는 모자랐다. 그리하여 백작은 짐이 겨워 이날만은 제 식탁에서 빛을 내지 못했다. 역시 초대를 받아 이제는 유난히 제 자리가 되어 버린 벨 데일의 옆자리를 차지한 크로프츠 선생은 의심할 바 없이 아주 행복했다. 바라건대 그 젊은 아가씨도 그러했을 것이다. 그러나 두 사람이 좌중의 흥겨움에 보탠 것은 거의 없었다. 존 임스는 제 누이와 목사 사이에 앉았는데 제 자리가 조금도 마음에 들지 않았다. 그 행복한 한 쌍이 맞은편에 앉아 있어서 그는 의사의 복을 고스란히 눈앞에서 보아야 했고, 릴리가 오지 않은 것이 자기에게는 야박한 처사라고 여겼다.\n\n그 자리는 참으로 따분했다. 게스트윅 저택의 만찬이란 다 그러했다. 어떤 집들은 하루하루 굴러가는 모양이 조금도 서글프거나 못마땅하지 않고 삶이 대체로 제법 흥겹게 흘러가는데도 만찬을 베풀 줄은 모른다. 아니, 차라리 이렇게 말하는 편이 낫겠다. 그런 집들은 그런 시도에 홀려 들어가도록 스스로를 내버려 두어서는 결코 안 된다고 말이다. 그런 집의 주인들은 대개 그 사실을 스스로도 잘 알고 있어서, 자기가 베풀기로 마음먹은 그 만찬을 제 벗들이 두려워하는 만큼이나 자기도 두려워한다. 그들은 자기가 손님들에게는 비참한 저녁 한때를 마련해 주고 있고 제 자신에게는 견뎌 내기 힘든 긴 연옥의 시간을 마련해 주고 있다는 것을 안다. 그런데도 그들은 기어이 그 일을 한다. 저 기다란 식탁이며 남아도는 그 모든 잔과 나이프와 포크는, 한 번도 쓰이지 않을 것이라면 대체 무엇 하러 있단 말인가? 이 논거가 이 모든 비참함을 낳는다. 이것과, 이것에 딸린 다른 논거들이 말이다. 이번 경우에는 물론 변명할 거리가 있었다. 지주와 그 조카딸은 특별한 사정이 있어 초대받은 것이니 그 두 사람이 온 것은 나무랄 데가 없었을 것이다. 의사가 거기 보태진 것도 해로울 것이 없었을 터다. 임스 부인과 그 딸에게 보낸 초대도 마음씨 좋은 일이었다. 잘못은 목사와 그 아내에게 있었다. 그들이 거기 있어야 할 필요는 조금도 없었고, 잔치를 벌인다는 구실 말고는 딛고 설 근거도 없었다. 보이스 씨 부부가 만찬을 만들어 놓고 그 자리의 정을 무너뜨렸다. 레이디 줄리아는 쪽지를 내보내자마자 자기가 잘못했다는 것을 알았다.\n\n그날 저녁에는 우리 이야기와 관련이 있는 말이라고는 한마디도 나오지 않았다. 아니, 실은 그 무엇과도 관련이 있는 말이 한마디도 나오지 않았다. 백작이 내세운 목적은 지주와 젊은 임스를 한자리에 맺어 주자는 것이었다. 그러나 사람이란 그런 우울한 자리에서는 결코 맺어지지 않는 법이다. 두 사람이 바싹 붙어 앉아 포트와인을 홀짝인다 해도, 마음과 감정에서는 남극과 북극만큼이나 멀리 떨어져 있다. 임스 부인을 데리러 게스트윅의 삯마차가 오고 목사와 보이스 부인을 데리러 목사네 조랑말 경마차가 왔을 때에는 큰 안도가 느껴졌다. 그러나 남은 사람들의 비참함은 이미 너무 깊이 나아간 터라 그날 저녁에는 어떤 반동도 있을 수 없었다. 지주가 하품을 하고 백작이 하품을 했고, 그러고는 그날 밤은 그것으로 끝이었다.\n\n# 제54장. 게스트윅 다리로의 두 번째 방문\n\n벨은 존 임스가 앨링턴으로 건너오기만 한다면 동생이 그를 무척 반갑게 맞을 것이라고 단언했고, 그는 물론 가겠노라고 대답했다. 말하자면 그만큼은 이야기가 매듭지어진 셈이었으므로, 그는 백작의 만찬이 있은 다음 날 아침 식탁에서 제 방문을 지극히 당연한 일처럼 입에 올릴 수 있었다. “데일, 나와 함께 한 바퀴 돌면서 내가 땅에 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 좀 봐 주셔야겠소.” 백작이 말했다. 그러고는 안장 얹은 말을 내오라 이르자고 했다. 그러나 지주는 걷는 편을 좋아했고, 이런 식으로 두 사람은 아침 식사가 끝난 직후에 치워졌다.\n\n존은 벨을 반 시간쯤 붙들고 단둘이 이야기를 나눌 작정을 품고 있었다. 그러나 레이디 줄리아가 안주인 노릇에 지나치게 열심이었든지, 아니면 그보다 더 그럴듯하게는 벨이 그 만남을 피했든지 했다. 그런 이야기를 나눌 기회는 도무지 오지 않았고, 그는 오전 내내 응접실 언저리를 맴돌았다. “이제 점심때까지 기다리시는 편이 좋겠어요.” 열두 시쯤에 레이디 줄리아가 그에게 말했다. 그러나 그는 이를 마다하고 자리를 떠, 그다음 한 시간 반 동안 이 근방 어딘가에 몸을 숨기고 지냈다. 그동안 그는 말을 타고 갈 것인가 걸어서 갈 것인가를 두고 몹시 궁리했다. 만약 그녀가 그에게 조금이라도 희망을 준다면 그는 육군 원수처럼 의기양양하게 말을 몰아 돌아올 수 있으리라. 그렇게 되면 말은 그에게 더없이 즐거운 것이 되리라. 그러나 만약 그녀가 아무런 희망도 주지 않는다면—만약 그날 아침 아주 매몰차게 거절당하는 것이 그의 운명이라면—그때 말은 그의 슬픔에 참으로 거추장스러운 물건이 되리라. 그런 처지에서 그가 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가, 들판을 가로질러 멀리 헤매 다니다가 쉬고 싶을 때 쉬고 뛰고 싶을 때 뛰는 것 말고는. “게다가 그이는 여느 아가씨들과는 달라.” 그는 속으로 생각했다. “내 구두가 더럽다고 마음 쓸 사람이 아니지.” 그리하여 그는 마침내 걸어가기로 정했다.\n\n“당당하게 부딪쳐 보게, 이 사람아.” 백작이 그에게 말했었다. “세상에, 무서울 게 뭐가 있나? 내 생각에는 여자들이란 가장 많이 조르는 사내에게 가장 많이 주는 법이야. 사내가 쭈뼛거리는 것만큼 여자들 마음을 돌아서게 하는 것도 없네.” 백작 자신은 그 방면의 인생살이에서 어떤 성공의 자취도 보인 적이 없는데 어떻게 그토록 많이 알고 있었는지, 나로서는 말할 준비가 되어 있지 않다. 그러나 임스는 그 충고를 그 자체로 좋은 것이라 받아들이고 그대로 해 보리라 마음먹었다. “결심 따위가 무슨 소용이 있겠느냐마는.” 그는 걸어가면서 속으로 말했다. “막상 그 순간이 오면 나는 그이 앞에서 떨 테고, 그이도 그걸 알아볼 테지. 하지만 그렇다고 그이가 달라지지는 않을 거야.”\n\n그가 그녀를 마지막으로 본 것은 작은 집 뒤편 잔디밭에서였고, 그때는 크로즈비를 향한 그녀의 연정이 가장 뜨거울 무렵이었다. 임스는 가망 없는 제 사랑을 고백하고 싶다는 어리석은 마음에 이끌려 그리로 갔었고, 그녀는 이 세상 무엇보다도 크로즈비 씨를 사랑한다고 대답했다. 물론 그때는 그녀가 그럴 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 그렇다 해도 그에게는 그녀가 그 말을 하던 태도가 잔인하게만 느껴졌다. 그리고 그 역시 잔인했다. 그는 크로즈비가 밉다고 말하며 그를 “그 사내”라 부르고, 이 세상 무슨 사정이 있어도 “그 사내의 집”에는 발을 들이지 않겠노라고 다짐했다. 그러고 나서 그는 그 사내에게 온갖 나쁜 일이 다 닥치기를 바라며 침울하게 걸어 나왔다. 그가 마음속으로 그 사내에게 내리기를 궁리했던 온갖 나쁜 일이 죄다 그 사내에게 떨어졌다는 것은 참으로 기묘한 일이 아닌가. 크로즈비는 제 사랑을 잃었다! 이름조차 입에 올릴 수 없을 만큼 스스로 악당임을 드러내 보였다! 창피스럽게 두들겨 맞았다! 그러나 그의 모습이 아직도 릴리의 마음에 소중하다면 이 모든 것이 무슨 소용이란 말인가? “그때도 나는 그이를 사랑한다고 말했지.” 그는 속으로 말했다. “그럴 자격이 없으면서도. 아무튼 이제는 그렇게 말할 자격이 있어.”\n\n앨링턴에 이르렀을 때 그는 마을을 지나 옆길로 작은 집 정면으로 올라가지 않고, 교회 문 쪽으로 돌아 지주의 테라스를 지나고 큰 집 끄트머리를 끼고 정원을 가로질러 갔다. 여기서 그는 홉킨스와 마주쳤다. “아니, 저건 임스 씨 아니십니까요!” 정원사가 말했다. “존 나리, 이렇게 실례를 무릅써도 될깝쇼!” 그러면서 홉킨스가 몹시 더러운 손을 내밀었고, 임스는 이 새삼스러운 애정의 까닭도 모른 채 물론 그 손을 잡았다.\n\n“작은 집에 잠깐 들르러 가는 길인데, 이 길로 와 볼까 했네.”\n\n“아무렴입죠. 이 길이든 저 길이든 어느 길이든, 존 나리만큼 반가운 분이 어디 있겠습니까요? 부럽습니다요. 세상 어느 사내가 부러운 것보다 나리가 더 부럽습니다요. 제가 그자의 목덜미를 움켜쥘 수만 있었더라면 여느 버러지 다루듯 다뤄 줬을 겁니다요. 정말 그랬을 겁니다요! 그자는 버러지였습니다요! 저는 늘 그리 말했습죠. 늘 미워했습니다요. 정말입니다요, 존 나리, 그자가 그 빌어먹을 공을 툭툭 쳐 대며 진달래 덤불 속으로 처박아 넣기 시작하던 첫 순간부터요. 내년에 필 꽃송이 따위는 있지도 않다는 듯이 말입니다요. 그자는 사람을 사람으로 여기고 쳐다본 적이 한 번도 없었습니다요. 안 그렇습니까요, 존 나리?”\n\n“나도 그 사람을 그리 좋아하지는 않았네, 홉킨스.”\n\n“물론 좋아하실 리가 없습죠. 누가 그자를 좋아했겠습니까요? 그저 그분, 가엾은 아가씨뿐이었습죠. 이제는 나아지실 겁니다요, 존 나리, 훨씬 나아지실 겁니다요. 그자는 성한 연인이 아니었습니다요. 나리 같지가 않았습죠. 말씀 좀 해 주십쇼, 존 나리, 그자를 붙잡았을 때 제대로 갈겨 주셨습니까요? 그러셨다고 들었습니다요. 두 눈이 시퍼렇게 멍들고 얼굴이 온통 피범벅이 되었다고요!” 그러면서 홉킨스는 결코 젊은 사람이 아니었건만 뻣뻣하게 싸움 자세를 잡았다.\n\n임스는 작은 다리를 건너 지나갔다. 그 다리는 쓰일 날이 이제 거의 다 되어 가는 터라 다정한 목수의 손길도 받지 못한 채 빠르게 썩어 가는 듯했다. 그러고는 정원으로 들어서서, 릴리에게 마지막으로 작별을 고했던 그 자리에 잠시 머물렀다. 그는 아직도 그녀가 거기 있기를 바라기라도 하듯 사방을 둘러보았다. 그러나 정원에는 아무도 보이지 않았고 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다. 한 걸음 한 걸음이 그가 찾는 사람에게 그를 데려다 줄수록 그는 제 심부름이 가망 없다는 것을 점점 더 절실히 느꼈다. 그녀는 그를 사랑한 적이 없었는데, 이제 와서 그녀가 자기를 사랑해 주리라고 어찌 감히 바라겠는가? 남들에게 한 약속이 있으니 끝까지 밀고 나가야 한다는 것을 알지 못했더라면 그는 발길을 돌렸을 것이다. 그는 이 일을 하겠노라 말했으니 제 말대로 할 참이었다. 그러나 잘되리라는 희망은 좀처럼 품지 못했다. 이런 마음가짐으로 그는 잔디밭을 가로질러 천천히 올라갔다.\n\n“얘야, 존 임스가 왔구나.” 거실 창으로 그를 맨 먼저 본 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“가지 마세요, 엄마.”\n\n“글쎄다. 아마 내가 자리를 비켜 주는 편이 나을 게다.”\n\n“아니에요, 엄마, 아니에요. 그런다고 무슨 소용이 있겠어요? 아무 소용도 없어요. 저는 그이를 누구 못지않게 좋아해요. 그이를 진심으로 아껴요. 하지만 아무 소용도 없어요. 이리로 들어오시게 해서 아주 다정하게 대해 주세요. 하지만 자리를 뜨고 우리만 남겨 두지는 마세요. 물론 그이가 오리라는 건 알고 있었고, 만나면 저도 무척 반가울 거예요.”\n\n그러고 나서 데일 부인은 다른 방으로 돌아가 응접실 창을 통해 손님을 들였다. “집이 말이 아니게 어수선하구나, 존. 그렇지 않니?”\n\n“그럼 정말로 게스트윅에 가서 사시는 겁니까?”\n\n“글쎄, 그래 보이지? 그런데 비밀 하나를 말해 주마. 다만 비밀이어야 한다. 게스트윅 저택에서는 입에 올리면 안 돼. 벨도 아직 모르는 일이야. 우리는 짐을 죄다 도로 풀고 여기 그대로 살까 하고 반쯤 마음을 굳혔단다.”\n\n임스는 제 목적에만 골똘해 있었고 앞에 놓인 어려운 일에 온통 마음을 빼앗겨 있던 터라, 데일 부인의 소식을 그것이 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼의 관심을 가지고 받아들일 수가 없었다. “짐을 다시 다 푼다고요.” 그가 말했다. “그거 아주 성가시겠는데요. 릴리도 같이 계십니까, 데일 부인?”\n\n“그래, 거실에 있단다. 가서 만나 보렴.” 그리하여 그는 데일 부인을 따라 현관을 지나갔고, 제 사랑 앞에 서게 되었다.\n\n“안녕하세요, 존?” “안녕하세요, 릴리?” 그런 만남이 어떤 식으로 시작되는지는 우리 모두가 안다. 서로가 저마다 다른 방식으로 상대에게 다정하고 살갑고 싶어 했다. 그러나 둘 다 이 첫 인사에 어떻게 다정함을 실어야 할지 알지 못했다. “저택에 묵고 계시다면서요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“네, 거기 묵고 있습니다. 삼촌과 벨이 어제 오후에 왔어요.”\n\n“벨 이야기는 들었니?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아, 네. 메리가 말해 주었습니다. 정말 기쁩니다. 저는 늘 크로프츠 선생을 무척 좋아했어요. 아직 축하 인사는 못 드렸는데, 비밀인지 아닌지 몰라서요. 하지만 어젯밤에 크로프츠도 거기 있었는데, 비밀이라면 그리 조심스레 지킬 생각은 없어 보이더군요.”\n\n“비밀은 아니란다.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “나는 그런 비밀을 좋아하는 편이 아니야.” 그러나 이렇게 말하면서 그녀는 모두에게 알려졌던 크로즈비의 약혼과 그 결말을 떠올렸다.\n\n“곧 하시는 겁니까?” 그가 물었다.\n\n“글쎄, 그래. 우리는 그렇게 생각한단다. 물론 정해진 건 아무것도 없지만.”\n\n“정말 우스웠어요.” 릴리가 말했다. “청혼 한마디 하는 데 아무리 못해도 한두 해가 걸린 제임스가, 그 이튿날 당장 혼인하고 싶어 했다니까요.”\n\n“아니야, 릴리. 그렇게까지는 아니지.”\n\n“그래도 엄마, 거의 그랬잖아요. 그이는 이번 주 안에 다 치를 수 있다고 생각했다니까요. 우리는 그 일로 아주 행복해요, 존! 형부로 그이만큼 마음에 드는 사람은 달리 없을 것 같아요. 당신이 그이를 좋아해 주시니 기뻐요. 정말 기뻐요. 두 분이 늘 벗으로 지내면 좋겠어요.” 여기에는 얼마쯤 다정함이 있었다. 존 자신도 그렇게 인정했다.\n\n“그럼요, 저희는 그럴 겁니다. 그이만 좋다면요. 그러니까, 제가 그이를 만날 일이 생긴다면 말입니다. 그이가 런던에 올라오면 제가 할 수 있는 일은 무엇이든 하겠습니다. 데일 부인, 그이가 런던에 자리를 잡으면 좋지 않겠습니까?”\n\n“아니다, 존. 그건 아주 나쁜 일이지. 그이가 어째서 내게서 딸을 빼앗아 가려 하겠니?”\n\n데일 부인은 맏딸 이야기를 하고 있었다. 그러나 그런 빼앗음에 대한 언급만으로도 존 임스의 얼굴은 붉게 물들었고 머리끝까지 화끈해졌으며, 그는 잠시 말을 잃었다.\n\n“런던에서라면 그이가 더 잘 풀리리라고 생각하시나요?” 남달리 침착한 마음가짐에 힘입어 릴리가 말했다.\n\n어머니에게 자리를 비우지 말아 달라고 청한 것은 분명 릴리의 판단 착오였고, 데일 부인도 곧 그것을 깨달았다. 일은 치러져야 했고, 데일 부인이 억지로 자리를 지키는 따위의 자잘한 예방책으로는 그것을 막을 수 없었다. 데일 부인은 이를 잘 알고 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 존에게도 제 사정을 호소할 기회가 주어져야 마땅하다고 느꼈다. 그런 기회가 그에게 아무 소용이 없을지도 모르나, 그가 원하는 이상 그에게는 마땅히 그럴 권리가 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 데일 부인은 감히 일어나 방을 나가지 못했다. 릴리가 그러지 말아 달라고 청했고, 두 사람 삶의 이 시기에 릴리의 청은 무엇이든 거룩한 것이었다. 그들은 한동안 크로프츠와 그의 혼인 이야기를 계속했고, 그 이야깃거리가 다하자 게스트윅으로 옮겨 갈—이제는 옮겨 가지 않을 듯한—제 일을 두고 이야기했다. “엄마, 우리가 가지 않을 것 같다고 하시는 건 너무 앞서가는 말씀이에요.” 릴리가 말했다. “겨우 어젯밤에 꺼내신 이야기잖아요. 실은 이래요, 존. 홉킨스가 들어와서는 놀라운 웅변을 늘어놓았답니다. 아무도 감히 홉킨스에게 맞설 수 없었어요. 어찌나 애처롭던지 우리를 울릴 뻔했다니까요.”\n\n“저한테도 방금 이야기를 하더군요.” 존이 말했다. “지주 어른 정원을 지나오는 길에요.”\n\n“그이가 너한테는 무슨 말을 하던?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아, 글쎄요. 별말은 없었습니다.” 그러나 이 순간 존은 정원사가 자기에게 한 말을 낱낱이 잘 기억하고 있었다. 그녀도 자기와 크로즈비 사이에 있었던 그 격투를 알고 있을까? 그리고 알고 있다면 그것을 어떻게 여기고 있을까?\n\n그들은 그렇게 한 시간을 함께 앉아 있었고, 임스는 아직 제 목적에 한 치도 다가서지 못했다. 그는 릴리에게 아내가 되어 달라고 청하지 않고서는 작은 집을 떠나지 않겠다고 스스로 맹세했었다. 그러지 않는다면 백작에게 거짓을 저지르는 것이나 다름없을 것 같았다. 드 게스트 경은 그를 위해 제 집을 열어 주었고 데일 집안사람들을 모두 불렀으며, 이 일이 이루어지도록 하기 위해 격식 차린 만찬이라는 잔혹한 제단에 스스로를 제물로 바쳤다. 돈 문제에서 치른, 그보다는 수월하고 가벼운 희생은 말할 것도 없었다. 그런 사정이니 임스는 앞길에 어떤 어려움이 있든 그 일을 하지 않을 만큼 정직하지 못한 사람이 아니었다.\n\n그는 한 시간을 그렇게 앉아 있었건만 데일 부인은 여전히 딸 곁에 남아 있었다. 대담하게 일어나 릴리에게 모자를 쓰고 정원으로 나오자고 청해 볼까? 그런 생각이 들자 그는 일어서서 모자를 집어 들었다. “저는 게스트윅까지 걸어서 돌아가려 합니다.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“이렇게 먼 데까지 우리를 보러 와 주다니 참 고맙구나.”\n\n“저는 늘 걷기를 좋아했습니다.” 그가 말했다. “백작님께서는 말을 타라고 하셨지만, 여기처럼 길을 잘 아는 고장에서는 걷는 편이 좋습니다.”\n\n“가기 전에 포도주라도 한 잔 들려무나.”\n\n“아, 괜찮습니다. 지주 어른 들판을 가로질러 흰 대문께로 큰길에 나설까 합니다. 이제 길이 아주 말랐더군요.”\n\n“그렇겠지.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“릴리, 거기까지만이라도 함께 가 주시지 않겠습니까?” 그 청이 나오자 데일 부인은 거의 애원하듯 딸을 바라보았다. “그래 주세요, 부디.” 그가 말했다. “걷기에 아주 좋은 날입니다.”\n\n그가 말한 그 길은, 릴리가 크로즈비를 데리고 들어가 약혼을 물러 주겠다고 청했던 바로 그 들판을 곧장 가로질렀다. 그 길을 또 다른 연인과 다시 걷는 일이 그녀에게 있을 수 있겠는가? “아니에요, 존.” 그녀가 말했다. “오늘은 그만두겠어요. 좀 피곤하기도 하고, 밖에 나가고 싶지 않아요.”\n\n“나가면 몸에 좋을 텐데.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“저는 몸에 좋은 일 같은 건 받고 싶지 않아요, 엄마. 게다가 혼자 돌아와야 하잖아요.”\n\n“제가 같이 돌아오겠습니다.” 조니가 말했다.\n\n“아, 그렇겠죠. 그러면 저는 또 당신과 함께 가야 하고요. 하지만 존, 정말이지 오늘은 걷고 싶지 않아요.” 그러자 존 임스는 다시 모자를 내려놓았다.\n\n“릴리.” 그가 말했다. 그러고는 멈췄다. 데일 부인은 창가로 걸어가 딸과 손님에게 등을 돌렸다. “릴리, 저는 당신에게 이야기하려고 일부러 여기까지 왔습니다. 실은 당신을 보려고 런던에서 내려온 겁니다.”\n\n“그러셨어요, 존?”\n\n“네, 그랬습니다. 제가 무슨 말을 하려는지 당신도 잘 알고 계시겠지요. 저는 그 사람이 당신을 보기도 전부터 당신을 사랑했습니다. 그리고 이제 그가 가 버렸으니, 예전 어느 때보다도 더 당신을 사랑합니다. 사랑하는 릴리!” 그러고는 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n“아니에요, 존. 안 돼요.” 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“언제까지나 안 되는 겁니까?”\n\n“그것만은 언제까지나 안 돼요. 어떻게 달라질 수가 있겠어요? 제가 다른 사람을 사랑하는데 저를 아내로 맞고 싶으시지는 않겠지요!”\n\n“하지만 그는 갔습니다. 다른 아내를 얻었어요.”\n\n“그이가 변했다고 제가 저를 바꿀 수는 없어요. 저에게 다정하게 굴어 주실 거라면 그것으로 족하게 여겨 주세요.”\n\n“하지만 당신은 저에게 너무 매정하군요!”\n\n“아니에요, 아니에요. 아, 저는 당신에게 얼마나 다정하고 싶은지 몰라요! 존, 여기요. 제 손을 잡으세요. 당신을 아끼고 언제까지나 아낄 벗의 손이에요. 사랑하는 존, 저는 당신을 위해 그것만 빼고 무엇이든, 무엇이든 하겠어요.”\n\n“한 가지밖에 없습니다.” 그가 여전히 그녀의 손을 잡은 채, 그러나 얼굴은 돌린 채로 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 그런 말씀 마세요. 당신이 저보다 더 딱한가요? 저도 그 한 가지를 가질 수 없었어요. 게다가 저는 당신이 이제껏 가 보았던 것보다 제 마음의 소원에 훨씬 가까이 가 있었어요. 저는 그 한 가지를 가질 수 없어요. 하지만 다른 것들이 있다는 것을 저는 알고, 스스로 마음이 부서지도록 내버려 두지는 않겠어요.”\n\n“당신은 저보다 강하군요.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“강한 게 아니라 더 확실할 뿐이에요. 저만큼 확실해지세요. 그러면 당신도 강해질 거예요. 그렇지 않아요, 엄마?”\n\n“그렇지 않았으면 좋으련만—그렇지 않았으면 좋으련만! 네가 저이에게 조금이라도 희망을 줄 수 있다면—”\n\n“엄마!”\n\n“한 해 뒤에 다시 와도 좋다고 말해 주십시오.” 그가 애원했다.\n\n“그렇게 말할 수는 없어요. 다시 오시면 안 돼요. 이런 식으로는요. 전에 정원에서 제가 한 말을 기억하세요? 이 세상 무엇보다도 그이를 사랑한다고 했지요. 그건 지금도 그대로예요. 저는 여전히 이 세상 무엇보다도 그이를 사랑해요. 그러니 제가 어떻게 당신에게 희망을 드릴 수 있겠어요?”\n\n“하지만 언제까지나 그렇지는 않을 겁니다, 릴리.”\n\n“언제까지나라니요! 그 언제까지나가 오면, 그이가 그 여자의 것인 만큼 제 것이 되지 못할 까닭이 어디 있나요? 존, 사랑한다는 것이 무엇인지 아신다면 그 말씀은 더 하지 마세요. 저는 이 일을 두고 누구에게도, 엄마에게조차 해 본 적 없는 이야기를 당신에게 이토록 터놓고 했어요. 제 마음을 당신이 알아주기를 바랐으니까요. 그 일이 있고 나서—그 일이 있고 나서 제가 다른 남자의 사랑을 받아들인다면 저는 제 눈에 스스로 부끄러운 사람이 될 거예요. 저에게는 그이와 혼인한 것이나 거의 다름없어요. 그이를 탓하는 것은 아니에요. 잊지 마세요. 이런 일은 남자에게는 다르니까요.”\n\n그녀는 그의 손을 놓지 않았고, 마지막 말을 하는 동안 늘 앉던 제 의자에 앉아 눈길을 바닥에 두고 있었다. 그녀는 나직한 목소리로 천천히, 거의 힘겹게 말했다. 그러나 그 말은 아주 또렷하게, 맑고 분명한 목소리로 흘러나와 임스와 데일 부인 두 사람의 기억에 한 자도 틀림없이 남게 되었다. 그에게는 그런 선언을 듣고서도 구애를 이어 간다는 것이 있을 수 없는 일로 여겨졌다. 데일 부인에게 그것은 끔찍한 말이었으니, 평생의 과부살이를 이야기하는 것이었고 그녀가 짐작했던 것보다도 더 큰 괴로움을 알리는 것이었다. 릴리가 이제 존 임스에게 한 만큼 어머니에게 말한 적이 없었던 것도, 제 마음을 이토록 또렷이 펼쳐 보이려 한 적이 없었던 것도 사실이었다. “다른 남자의 사랑을 받아들인다면 저는 제 눈에 스스로 부끄러운 사람이 될 거예요!” 끔찍한 말이었으나 알아듣기는 아주 쉬웠다. 데일 부인은 처음부터 임스가 너무 일찍 왔다고, 백작과 지주가 함께 상처를 입은 지 얼마 되지도 않은 그 상처를 너무 빨리 낫게 하려 애쓰고 있다고, 제 딸에게는 추스를 시간이 주어졌어야 했다고 느껴 왔다. 그러나 이제 시도는 이루어졌고, 릴리의 입술에서는 말이 억지로 끌려 나왔으며, 그 말을 한 일을 그녀 자신은 결코 잊지 못할 것이었다.\n\n“그러시리라는 걸 알고 있었습니다.” 존이 말했다.\n\n“아, 그래요. 당신은 알고 있었죠. 당신의 마음이 제 마음을 알아주니까요. 그리고 당신은 저에게 화내지 않을 거예요. 언젠가 한 번 그러셨듯 심술궂고 모진 말을 하지도 않을 거고요. 우리는 서로를 생각하고 서로를 위해 기도해요, 존. 그리고 언제까지나 서로를 아껴요. 만나게 되면 서로 반가워하기로 해요. 어떤 벗도 당신보다 저에게 더 소중하지는 않을 거예요. 당신은 참으로 진실하고 정직한 분이니까요! 당신이 혼인하면 저는 당신의 아내에게, 하느님이 그이에게 얼마나 크나큰 축복을 주셨는지 말해 줄 거예요.”\n\n“그런 일은 절대 하지 마십시오.”\n\n“아니요, 할 거예요. 무슨 뜻으로 하시는 말씀인지는 알아요. 그래도 저는 할 거예요.”\n\n“안녕히 계십시오, 데일 부인.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“잘 가거라, 존. 저 애 마음이 달랐더라면 이 일에 내 온 마음의 축복을 다 주었을 게다. 너를 내 아들처럼 아꼈을 게고, 지금도 너를 아끼마.” 그러고 나서 그녀는 입술을 내밀어 그의 얼굴에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“저도 당신을 아낄 거예요.” 릴리가 다시 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. 그는 그녀도 자기에게 입을 맞춰 줄지 모른다고 생각하기라도 한 듯 애타는 눈길로 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그러고는 그녀의 손을 제 입술에 눌러 대고, 더는 아무런 작별 인사도 하지 않은 채 모자를 집어 들고 방을 나섰다.\n\n“가엾은 사람.” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“저이를 오게 하지 말았어야 했어요.” 릴리가 말했다. “하지만 그분들은 몰라요. 제가 장난감을 하나 잃었으니 착한 마음으로 다른 걸 하나 주면 된다고 생각하는 거예요.” 그로부터 얼마 지나지 않아 릴리는 혼자 자리를 떠 몇 시간이나 홀로 앉아 있었다. 그리고 차 마실 시간에 다시 어머니와 어울렸을 때, 존 임스의 방문에 대해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았다.\n\n그는 앞문으로 나와 교회 묘지를 지나, 릴리에게 함께 걷자고 청했던 그 들판으로 나섰다. 그는 지주의 집을 뒤로 하고 나서야 겨우 방금 있었던 일을 생각하기 시작했다. 묘비들 사이를 지나며 그는 걸음을 멈추고 마치 흥미가 있기라도 한 듯 비문 하나를 읽었다. 종탑 아래 잠시 서서 시계를 올려다보았고, 그런 다음 마치 이쪽 시계로 저쪽 시계를 맞춰 보기라도 하려는 듯 제 회중시계를 꺼냈다. 그는 방금 있었던 장면을 머릿속에서 몰아내려고 저도 모르게 안간힘을 썼고, 오 분에서 십 분쯤은 그렇게 하는 데 성공했다. 그는 래플 경과 그의 편지들에 대해 한두 마디 혼잣말을 했고, 그 기사 어른의 신발을 들고 들어오던 래퍼티의 모습을 떠올리며 속으로 웃었다. 반 마일쯤 갔을 무렵에야 그는 감히 멈춰 서서, 제 인생의 크나큰 목적에서 실패했노라고 스스로에게 말할 수 있었다.\n\n그렇다. 그는 실패했다. 그리고 쓰디쓴 자책과 함께 그는 이제 영영 실패했음을 스스로 인정했다. 그는 슬픔에 잠긴 그녀에게 무례한 사랑을 들이밀었다고 스스로를 나무랐고, 어리석었을 뿐 아니라 너그럽지도 못했다고 자신을 꾸짖었다. 벗인 백작은 늘 익살맞은 투로 그를 개선하는 영웅이라 불러 왔고, 그를 어찌나 구슬려 놓았던지 그는 제 분별을 잃고 이 구애가 잘되리라고 거의 믿기까지 했다. 그런 성공이란 애초에 있을 수 없는 일이었다고 스스로에게 말하는 지금, 그는 자기를 이 지경으로 몰아넣은 백작이 밉기까지 했다. 개선하는 영웅이라니! 저택으로 돌아가 그들 틈에 풀 죽고 비참한 몰골로 어떻게 슬그머니 끼어든단 말인가? 그가 무슨 일로 갔는지는 다들 알고 있었고, 그가 실패했다는 것도 다들 알게 될 터였다. 그 많은 구경꾼의 눈앞에서 그런 일을 떠맡다니 어쩌면 그토록 어리석었을까? 성공을 어찌나 굳게 기대했던지 돌아왔을 때의 개선만 생각하고 더 있음직한 망신은 생각하지 않았던 것이 아닌가? 그는 남들이 자기를 바보로 만들도록 내버려 두었고, 그리하여 스스로를 바보로 만들어 이제 모든 희망과 행복이 끝나 버렸다. 어떻게 하면 당장 이 고장을 벗어나 런던으로 달아날 수 있을까? 어떻게 하면 누구에게도 한마디 더 하지 않고 빠져나갈 수 있을까? 그것이 처음에 그의 마음을 사로잡은 생각이었다.\n\n그는 지주의 땅이 끝나는 곳, 곧 앨링턴 교구가 백작의 집이 서 있는 애벗스 게스트 교구와 갈라지는 데서 큰길을 건넜고, 그들이 황소와 마주쳤던 그 들판 가장자리를 따라 난 잡목숲을 지나 공원 뒤편의 높은 숲으로 되짚어 올라갔다. 아, 그렇다. 말을 타고 나오지 않은 것은 그에게 잘된 일이었다. 지금 같은 처지에서 큰길을 따라 말을 몰아 저택 마구간까지 돌아가는 일은 그로서는 거의 견딜 수 없었을 것이다. 사정이 이러하니 백작의 집에 있는 제자리로 돌아갈 수 있으리라고는 생각되지 않았다. 그 두 노인의 눈앞에서 어떻게 여느 때의 태도를 지키는 척할 수 있단 말인가? 차라리 어머니에게로 가서 거기서 저택에 전갈을 보내고, 그런 다음 런던으로 달아나는 편이 나을 것이었다. 그렇게 생각하면서도 아무런 결심도 내리지 못한 채 그는 숲을 지나 언덕을 내려와 마을 쪽으로 향했고, 이윽고 개울에 놓인 그 작은 다리에 다시 이르렀다. 거기서 그는 걸음을 멈추고 한동안 서서, 그 옛날 제가 새겨 놓은 글자들 위에 널따란 손을 펼쳐 눈에 보이지 않게 가렸다. “나는 참 바보였어. 언제나, 늘 그랬지!” 그는 속으로 말했다.\n\n그가 생각한 것은 방금의 낙담만이 아니라 제 지난 삶 전체였다. 그는 제 풋내기 시절을, 곧 릴리가 크로즈비의 손아귀에 떨어지기 전에 그녀의 마음에 들 만한 사람이 되지 못하게 만들었던, 사내다움을 갖추는 일에서의 그 뒤처짐을 뼈저리게 느꼈다. 이런 생각을 하다가 그는 크로즈비를 다시 만나기만 하면 다시 한 번 두들겨 패 주겠노라고, 정정당당한 매질로 그런 일이 될 수만 있다면 그자를 이 세상 밖으로 내보내 버릴 만큼 두들겨 주겠노라고, 그리하여 제가 그 뒤를 따라가야 한다 해도 개의치 않겠노라고 스스로에게 다짐했다. 그 사내의 불성실함 때문에 두 사람에게—릴리에게도 그 자신에게도—이런 벌이 내린다는 것이 가혹하지 않은가? 그렇게 십오 분쯤 다리 위에 서 있다가 그는 칼을 꺼내, 나무에 깊고 거친 자국을 내며 난간에서 릴리의 이름을 깎아 냈다.\n\n그가 막 일을 끝내고 아직 나뭇조각들이 물살에 실려 떠내려가는 것을 바라보고 있는데, 조용한 발소리가 바로 곁까지 다가왔다. 돌아보니 레이디 줄리아가 다리 위에 있었다. 그녀는 그의 바로 옆에 있었고, 그가 한 일을 이미 보아 버린 뒤였다. “그 애가 당신 마음을 상하게 했나요, 존?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“아, 레이디 줄리아!”\n\n“그 애가 당신 마음을 상하게 했나요?”\n\n“저를 거절했습니다. 이제 다 끝났습니다.”\n\n“그 애가 당신을 거절했을지는 몰라도, 그렇다고 다 끝나야 하는 건 아니에요. 그 이름을 깎아 내다니 안타깝군요, 존. 그 이름을 당신 마음에서도 도려낼 작정인가요?”\n\n“결코 아닙니다. 그럴 수만 있다면 그러겠지만, 저는 결코 그러지 못할 겁니다.”\n\n“큰 보물처럼 간직하세요. 훗날 그것은 당신에게 슬픔이 아니라 기쁨이 될 거예요. 참되게 사랑해 본 일은, 설령 헛되이 사랑한 것이라 해도, 당신이 저만큼 나이가 들었을 때 위로가 될 거예요. 마음이라는 것을 지녀 보았다는 건 대단한 일이니까요.”\n\n“모르겠습니다. 저는 차라리 마음 같은 게 없었으면 좋겠습니다.”\n\n“그리고 존, 저는 이제 그 애의 심정을 알겠어요. 사실 저는 처음부터 당신이 너무 일찍 청한다고 생각했답니다. 하지만 그 애가 당신의 바람을 좋게 여기게 될 날이 아직 올지도 몰라요.”\n\n“아니요, 아닙니다. 결코 그럴 일은 없습니다. 이제야 저는 그이를 알겠습니다.”\n\n“당신이 한결같이 사랑할 수 있다면 아직 그 애를 얻을 수도 있어요. 그 애가 얼마나 어린지 생각해 보세요. 그리고 두 분 다 얼마나 젊은지도요. 두 해 뒤에 다시 오세요. 그리고 그 애를 얻거든, 제가 두 분에게 좋은 늙은이 노릇을 했다고 저에게 말해 주세요.”\n\n“저는 결코 그이를 얻지 못할 겁니다, 레이디 줄리아.” 이 마지막 말을 하는 동안 그의 뺨에는 눈물이 흘러내렸고, 그는 동행 앞에서 드러내 놓고 울고 있었다. 그녀가 슬픔에 잠긴 그를 이렇게 마주친 것은 그에게 잘된 일이었다. 그녀가 제 눈물을 보았다는 것을 알고 나자 그는 그녀에게 제 슬픔의 이야기를 모조리 쏟아 놓을 수 있었다. 그리고 그가 그렇게 하는 동안 그녀는 조용히 그를 집으로 데리고 돌아갔다.\n\n# 제55장. 결국 그리 망측한 일은 아니었다\n\n하틀탑 집안과 옴니엄 집안 사이에 무시무시한 일이 벌어지리라는 예언이 있었다는 것을 아마 독자들은 기억할 것이다. 레이디 덤벨로는 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨가 말을 걸 때마다 미소를 지었다. 팰리서 씨는 정치만으로는 제게 넉넉하지 않으며 행복의 온전한 몫을 채우려면 사랑이 있어야 한다고 스스로에게 고백했다. 덤벨로 경은 근래 들어 공작의 상속인의 훤칠한 모습에 눈길이 닿을 때마다 낯을 찌푸렸다. 그리고 공작 본인이—평소 그 침묵으로 그토록 위엄이 서던 그 권세가가—공작 본인이 입을 열었다. 드 코시 백작부인과 레이디 클랜디들럼은 둘 다 그 일이 이미 완전히 짜여 있다고 철석같이 믿고 있었다. 그러므로 세상이 팰리서 씨와 레이디 덤벨로의 사랑을—그 부정한 사랑을—입에 올리고 있었다고 내가 말한다 해도 나는 조금도 지나칠 것이 없다.\n\n그리고 세상의 그 입방아는 레이디 덤벨로가 태어난 그 점잖은 시골 목사관까지 흘러 내려갔으니, 그곳은 그녀가 이제 제 존재로 빛내고 있는 그 귀한 저택들로 실려 가기 전에 살던 곳이었다. 세상의 입방아는 그 부인의 아버지인 그랜틀리 부주교가 여태 살고 있는 플럼스테드 에피스코피에서도 들렸고, 그 부인의 이모와 외조부가 살고 있는 바체스터의 수석사제관에서도 들렸다. 이 교회의 고장들에 그 소문을 퍼뜨린 것이 누구의 버릇없는 혀였는지는 이제 와서 굳이 말할 것이 없다. 다만 코시 성이 바체스터에서 멀지 않았다는 것과, 드 코시 백작부인이 제 등불을 됫박 아래 감추어 두는 사람이 아니었다는 것은 기억해 둘 만하다.\n\n무서운 소문이었다. 제 딸에 관한 그런 소문이 무섭지 않을 어머니가 어디 있겠는가? 그 소문이 그랜틀리 부인의 귀에 울린 것보다 더 끔찍하게 울릴 어머니의 귀는 없었을 것이다. 딸인 레이디 덤벨로는 온통 세속적인 사람이었는지 몰라도, 그랜틀리 부인은 결코 절반 넘게 세속적인 적이 없었다. 그 성품과 습관과 바람의 한쪽 절반에서 그녀는 그 자체로 선한 것들에—신앙에, 자선에, 정직한 마음의 올곧음에—맺어져 있었다. 살아온 형편이 그녀로 하여금 하느님과 재물을 함께 섬기게 만든 것은 사실이고, 그리하여 제 딸이 후작의 상속인과 혼인한 것을 크게 자랑스러워한 것도 사실이다. 그녀는 제 자식이 귀족의 높은 자리로 올라선 것을 마음껏 누리면서도, 플럼스테드 에피스코피의 일꾼들의 아이들에게 제 손으로 책과 교리문답서를 나누어 주는 일은 그대로 이어 갔다. 그리젤다가 처음 레이디 덤벨로가 되었을 때 어머니는 제 아이가 새 자리가 요구하는 것들을 감당하지 못할까 봐 얼마쯤 겁을 냈다. 그러나 아이는 그것들을 감당하고도 남는다는 것을 스스로 보여 주었고, 아찔한 높이의 성공까지 올라가 어머니에게 큰 영광과 함께 큰 두려움도 함께 가져다주었다. 그녀는 제 그리젤다가 후작의 딸들 가운데서도 대단하다고 생각하면 기뻤다. 그러나 그런 높이에서 떨어지는 일이 있다면—혹시라도 떨어지는 일이 있다면!—그 추락이 얼마나 치명적일까를 곱씹으면 몸이 떨렸다.\n\n그러나 이런 추락은 꿈에도 그려 본 적이 없었다! 그녀는 부주교에게 말하곤 했다—실제로 자주 말했다—그리젤다의 신앙의 원칙은 바깥세상의 일 따위에 흔들리기에는 너무나 굳게 박혀 있다고. 그것은 아마도 플럼스테드 에피스코피의 가르침이 제 딸을 하나의 홈에 단단히 끼워 놓았으므로 하틀베리의 앞날의 가르침을 다 합쳐도 그 끼움을 풀어내지는 못하리라는 그녀의 확신을 드러낸 말이었으리라. 그렇게 자랑을 늘어놓을 때 제 딸이 남편의 집을 뛰쳐나간다는 따위의 생각은 한 번도 그녀에게 떠오른 적이 없었다. 다만 그런 죄와 비슷한 성질의 죄들을—덜 단단히 홈에 끼워진 다른 귀족 부인들이 이따금 빠지곤 하는 죄들을—염두에 두고 한 말이었을 뿐이다. 그리고 그녀의 자랑을 간추리면 이런 것이었다. 곧, 자기가 하느님과 재물을 함께 섬기는 일을 하도 잘 해냈으니 제 자식은 세상의 온갖 것을 누리러 나가도 하늘의 것들을 다치게 할 위험이 없다는 것. 그런데 이 소문이 그녀에게 닥쳤다. 부주교가 쉰 목소리로 소곤거리며 말하기를, 이 일을 살펴보라는 언질을 받았노라고, 그리젤다가 남편을 떠나려 한다는 말이 세상에 파다하다고 했다.\n\n“이 세상 무엇으로도 나는 그 말을 믿지 않을 거예요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 그러나 그 뒤 그녀는 응접실에 혼자 앉아 몸을 떨었다. 이윽고 그녀의 동생인 수석 사제의 부인 애러빈 부인이 목사관으로 건너와, 반쯤 감추는 말로 같은 이야기를 전했다. 그녀는 그것을 주교의 부인인 프라우디 부인에게서 들었다고 했다. “그 여자는 거짓의 아비만큼이나 거짓된 사람이에요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 그러나 그녀는 더욱더 떨었다. 그리고 교구 일을 챙기면서도 제 자식 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수 없었다. 만일 이런 일이 제 아이에게 닥친다면 앞으로 남은 삶은 다 무엇이 되며, 지나온 삶은 또 다 무엇이 되겠는가? 그녀는 그것을 믿지 않으려 했다. 그러면서도 딸이 올라선 그 높이를 떠올리고, 이제 그리젤다가 속한 그 세상에서는 그런 일들이 실제로 있어 왔음을 되새기면 더욱더 떨렸다. 아! 그토록 머리를 높이 쳐들지 않았더라면 그들에게 더 낫지 않았을까! 그리고 그녀는 홀로 이웃 교회 묘지의 무덤들 사이를 걸어 나가, 또 하나의 딸의 주검이 뉘어져 있는 무덤가에 섰다. 그 아이의 운명이 오히려 더 행복했던 것일 수도 있을까.\n\n그 일을 두고 그녀와 부주교 사이에 오간 말은 아주 적었으나, 그래도 무언가 손을 써야 한다는 데에는 둘 사이에 뜻이 모인 듯했다. 그는 런던으로 올라가 딸을 만났다. 그러나 그런 이야기를 꺼낼 엄두는 내지 못했다. 덤벨로 경은 그에게 퉁명스러웠고 도무지 입을 열지 않았다. 사실 부주교와 그랜틀리 부인은 딸의 집이 저희에게 편하지 않다는 것을 진작에 알고 있었고, 저희 계급 안에서는 자존심이 충분히 높은 사람들이었으므로 사위의 손님 대접에 굳이 매달릴 생각이 없었다. 그러나 그는 칼턴 가든스의 그 집에 무언가 온전하지 않은 것이 있음을 알아볼 수 있었다. 덤벨로 경은 아내에게 살갑지 않았고, 사내들의 말보다도 오히려 그 침묵 속에 저에게 닿은 그 소문을 그럴듯하게 만드는 무엇인가가 있었다.\n\n“그 사람이 거기 드나드는 것이 마땅한 정도보다 잦소.” 부주교가 말했다. “그리고 적어도 이것만은 내가 확신하오. 덤벨로가 그것을 좋아하지 않는다는 것 말이오.”\n\n“제가 그 애에게 편지를 쓰겠어요.” 마침내 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “저는 아직도 그 애의 어미예요. 제가 그 애에게 편지를 쓰겠어요. 사람들이 그 애를 두고 무어라 하는지 그 애가 모르고 있는 것일 수도 있으니까요.”\n\n그리고 그랜틀리 부인은 정말로 편지를 썼다.\n\n플럼스테드, 186-년 사월.\n\n때때로 나는 네가 나에게서 너무 멀리 옮겨 가 버려서, 이제는 내가 네 나날의 살림살이에 더 마음을 쓸 권리조차 거의 없는 듯한 느낌이 들어요. 그리고 네가 우리와 같은 지체의 신사와 혼인했더라면 그러했을 것처럼 나에게 조언이나 위로를 구하는 일이란 이제 있을 수 없다는 것도 알고 있어요. 그러나 내 아이가 제 어미를 잊지 않았으리라는 것, 어미의 사랑을 돌아보지 않을 리 없다는 것, 그리고 그 아이가 곤경에 처했다면 내가 사랑하고 아껴 기른 다른 어느 자식에게 말하듯 그 아이에게 말하는 것을 허락해 주리라는 것만은 나도 아주 확신해요. 그런 곤경이 네 가까이에 있다는 내 짐작이 부디 틀린 것이기를 하느님께 빌어요. 만일 그러하다면 이 어미의 노파심을 너그러이 보아 주어요.\n\n한 군데가 아닌 여러 곳에서 우리에게 소문이 닿았어요. 그것이—아! 그리젤다야, 내가 써야 할 것을 감추면서 동시에 밝히자면 어떤 말을 골라야 할지 나는 도무지 모르겠어요. 사람들이 말하기를, 네가 공작의 조카인 팰리서 씨와 가까이 지내며 네 남편이 몹시 언짢아하고 있다는 거예요. 아무래도 내가 다 터놓고 말하는 편이 낫겠지요. 다만 내가 그것을 믿었다고는 생각하지 말아 주어요. 사람들이 말하기를, 네가 팰리서 씨의 보호 아래로 몸을 옮기려 한다는 이야기가 돌고 있다는 거예요. 내 가장 사랑하는 아이야, 내가 어떤 괴로움 속에서 이 말들을 쓰고 있는지—이 말들을 낳은 그 생각을 내 마음에 들이기까지 얼마나 무서운 슬픔에 짓눌려야 했는지 너도 짐작할 수 있으리라 생각해요. 그런 이야기가 바체스터에서 버젓이 오가고 있고, 도시에 가서 너를 만나고 온 네 아버지도 이제 마음을 놓아도 좋다는 말을 나에게 해 주지 못하고 있어요.\n\n네 남편과 사이가 갈라진다면 세상의 눈으로 보아 네가 입을 손해가 어떠할지에 대해서는 나는 한마디도 하지 않겠어요. 그토록 무서운 걸음이 어떤 결과를 낳을지는 내가 짚어 보여 주는 것만큼이나 너도 또렷이 볼 수 있으리라 믿어요. 너는 네 아버지의 가슴을 무너뜨리고 네 어미를 무덤으로 보내게 되겠지요. 그러나 내가 가장 힘주어 말하려는 것은 그것조차 아니에요. 이것이에요—네가 여자로서 저지를 수 있는 가장 나쁜 죄로 네 하느님께 죄를 짓게 되고, 하느님 앞에서 뉘우치는 일이 거의 불가능하며 사람들 앞에서 벗어나는 일은 허락되지 않는 그 치욕의 깊은 구렁으로 스스로를 던지게 되리라는 것 말이에요.\n\n나는 그것을 믿지 않아요, 내 가장 사랑하고 또 사랑하는 아이야—이제 내게 살아 있는 오직 하나뿐인 딸아. 나는 사람들이 내게 한 말을 믿지 않아요. 그러나 어미로서 그 험담을 못 본 체하고 넘길 엄두가 나지 않았어요. 네가 나에게 편지를 써서 그런 일이 아니라고 말해 준다면, 설령 네가 내 의심을 두고 나를 나무란다 하더라도 나는 다시 행복해질 거예요.\n\n언제 어느 때든, 어떤 형편에 놓이든, 내가 지난날과 다름없이 여전히 너를 사랑하는 어미임을 믿어 주어요.\n\n이제 우리는 올버니의 제 거처에 앉아 사랑을 생각하고 있는 팰리서 씨에게로 돌아가 보자. 공작이 그에게 경고했고, 공작의 관리인도 그에게 경고했다. 그리고 그는 독립을 소중히 여기는 마음이 그토록 높았음에도 하마터면 몸을 떨 뻔했다. 해마다 들어오던 그 수천 파운드가 모두 저울 위에 놓였고, 어쩌면 그가 세상 앞에서 차지한 자리가 딛고 선 모든 것이 그러했다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 몸이 떨리면서도 밀고 나가기로 마음먹었다. 통계는 그에게 메말라 가고 있었고 사랑은 아주 달콤했다. 통계도 사랑과 섞을 수만 있다면 예전처럼 얼마든지 황홀해질 수 있으리라고 그는 생각했다. 레이디 덤벨로를 사랑한다는 생각만으로도 그의 삶에는 소금기가 생긴 듯했고, 이제 와서는 그것을 제 손으로 걷어 낼 방도를 알지 못했다. 그가 아직 사랑의 온전한 축복을 그리 많이 누리지 못한 것은 사실이니, 레이디 덤벨로와 나눈 대화라고는 이 책에 옮겨 적힌 것들보다 더 따뜻했던 적이 없었다. 그러나 그의 상상은 부지런히 일하고 있었다. 그리고 이제 레이디 덤벨로가 칼턴 가든스의 제 집에 온전히 자리를 잡았으니, 그는 마땅한 기회가 오는 대로 제 연정을 밝히기로 작정했다. 세상이 저에게 그러기를 기다리고 있다는 것, 그리고 세상이 이미 그의 굼뜬 진행을 두고 얼마쯤 탓하려 든다는 것이 그에게는 충분히 뚜렷했다.\n\n사교철이 시작된 뒤로 그는 칼턴 가든스에 한 번 다녀왔고, 그 부인은 더없이 고운 미소로 그를 맞아 주었다. 그러나 그녀와 단둘이 있었던 것은 반 분뿐이었고, 그 반 분 동안에는 이제 사교철 내내 런던에 머무르시겠지요, 하고 한마디 건넬 겨를밖에 없었다.\n\n“아, 그럼요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “저희는 칠월까지는 떠나지 않아요.” 그도 통계의 사정 때문에 칠월까지는 떠날 수 없었다. 그러니 그의 앞에는 술수를 부리고 제 뜻을 밝히고 앞날의 일을 준비할 온전한 두 달이, 어쩌면 석 달이 놓여 있었다. 어느 날 아침 그가 바로 그날 밤 드 코시 백작부인의 응접실에서—그곳에서 그녀를 만나게 되리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다—레이디 덤벨로에게 첫 다정한 말을 건네리라 마음을 굳혔을 때, 우편으로 편지 한 통이 그에게 왔다. 그는 그 필적을 잘 알았고 그 안에 담겼을 기별도 잘 알았다. 공작의 관리인 포더길 씨에게서 온 것으로, 얼마간의 돈이 그의 거래 은행 계좌에 들어왔음을 알리는 편지였다. 그러나 편지는 거기서 더 나아가, 다음 분기 지급을 하려면 특별한 지시가 있어야 한다는 뜻을 공작이 관리인에게 일러 두었다는 것까지 알렸다. 포더길 씨는 그 이상 아무 말도 하지 않았으나 팰리서 씨는 모두 알아들었다. 그는 심장 둘레로 피가 차갑게 도는 것을 느꼈다. 그러나 그럼에도 그날 밤 드 코시 백작부인과의 약속만은 어기지 않기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그리고 레이디 덤벨로도 같은 날 아침에 제 편지를 받았다. 그녀는 몸단장을 받으며 그것을 읽었는데, 시중드는 하녀들은 그 편지의 무엇인가가 마님을 흔들어 놓았다고 의심할 만한 낌새를 조금도 찾지 못했다. 마님은 좀처럼 흔들리는 법이 없었다. 물론 하녀들에게서 더없이 정성스러운 손길을 받아 내는 데에는 빈틈이 없었지만. 그래도 그녀는 편지를 아주 꼼꼼히 읽었고, 하녀들의 화장 도구 아래 앉아 있는 동안 저에게 전해진 그 소식을 깊이 생각했다. 그녀는 누구에게도 화가 나지 않았고, 누구에게도 고맙지 않았다. 그 일에 얽힌 어느 누구에게도 각별한 사랑을 느끼지 않았다. 그녀의 마음은 “오, 나의 주인이자 남편이여!” 하고도, “오, 나의 연인이여!” 하고도, “오, 나의 어머니, 어린 날의 벗이여!” 하고도 말하지 않았다. 다만 생각할 거리가 제 앞에 놓였다는 것을 알아차렸고, 그래서 생각했다. “덤벨로 경께 내 사랑을 전하고,” 손질이 거의 끝나 갈 무렵 그녀가 말했다. “내가 아침을 드는 동안에 와 주시면 무척 반갑겠다고 전해라.”\n\n“예, 마님.” 그러고서 답이 돌아왔다. “나리께서 마님께 꼭 가시겠답니다.”\n\n“구스타부스,” 조심스레 제 의자에 자리를 잡자마자 그녀가 말했다. “어머니한테서 편지가 왔는데, 당신이 읽어 보시는 게 좋겠어요.” 그러고서 그 문서를 그에게 건넸다. “내가 무슨 짓을 했기에 어머니한테서 이런 의심을 받아야 하는지 모르겠어요. 하지만 어머니는 시골에 사시니 심술궂은 사람들에게 속으신 것이겠지요. 아무튼 당신이 읽어 보시고, 내가 어떻게 해야 할지 말해 주세요.”\n\n이로 미루어 우리는 팰리서 씨가 그 바위에 제 배를 부딪쳐 난파할 기회란 아주 작았으리라는 것, 그리고 그가 제 뜻과는 달리 숙부의 노여움에서 구제되리라는 것을 미리 짚어 볼 수 있다. 덤벨로 경은 편지를 받아 들고 벽난로에 등을 댄 채 서서 아주 천천히 읽었다. 아주 천천히 읽었고, 그의 아내는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 마주 보지는 않으면서도 그가 몹시 붉어지는 것을, 허둥거리며 제 정신을 놓아 버린 것을, 대답이 얼른 나오지 않는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그녀는 지난 석 달 동안 그가 저에게 굴어 온 태도가 예전의 버릇과는 사뭇 달라졌다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 단둘이 있을 때는 말이 거칠어졌고 사람들 속에서는 살피는 정성이 덜해졌다. 그러나 그녀는 그 변화를 알아차렸다는 것을 보이려고 투정을 하거나 한마디를 던진 적이 없었다. 게다가 그녀는 그가 태도를 바꾼 까닭도 알고 있었고, 두루 생각한 끝에 그것을 견디어 눌러 없애기로 마음먹은 터였다. 그녀는 의심받을 만한 일을 한 적도 그런 말을 한 적도 없다고 스스로에게 못 박았으므로, 제 처신을 바꾸지도 않을 것이고 저가 의심받고 있음을 알고 있다는 티도 내지 않을 것이었다. 그러나 이제—어머니의 편지를 손에 쥔 지금—그녀는 저가 그의 질투를 눈치챈 적이 있다는 것을 그에게 알리지 않고도 그를 해명의 자리로 끌어낼 수 있었다. 그녀에게 어머니의 편지는 큰 도움이었다. 그것은 이런 장면을 벌일 구실이 되어 주었고, 제 방식대로 싸움을 치를 수 있게 해 주었다. 팰리서 씨 따위와 눈이 맞아 달아나 저가 얻어 낸 자리를 내던진다니—어림도 없는 소리였다! 그러기에는 그녀가 제 홈에 너무나 단단히 끼워져 있었다! 그런 일로 조금이라도 겁을 낸 그 어머니는 제 딸의 성품이 얼마나 단단한지 모르고 있음을 스스로 드러낸 셈이었다.\n\n“그래서, 구스타부스,” 마침내 그녀가 말했다. “내가 어떤 답장을 써야 할지, 아니면 답장을 하기는 해야 할지 당신이 말해 주셔야겠어요.” 그러나 그는 그때까지도 그녀에게 일러 줄 채비가 되어 있지 않았다. 그래서 그는 편지를 다시 펴서 또 한 번 읽었고, 그녀는 제 손으로 차를 한 잔 따랐다.\n\n“아주 심각한 일이오.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“예, 심각한 일이지요. 어머니한테서 온 그런 편지를 심각하게 여기지 않을 수는 없었어요. 다른 사람한테서 온 것이었다면 당신을 성가시게 해 드렸을지 의문이에요. 하기야 어머니가 나에게 가까운 만큼 당신에게 가까운 누군가한테서 온 것이었다면 또 몰라도요. 사정이 이러하니 당신도 내가 옳게 했다고 느끼지 않을 수 없을 거예요.”\n\n“옳고말고! 아, 그럼, 당신이 옳소—나한테 말한 것은 아주 잘한 일이오. 당신은 나에게 무엇이든 말해야 하오. 빌어먹을 것들 같으니!” 그러나 그가 누구를 저주하려 한 것인지는 밝히지 않았다.\n\n“나는 무엇보다도 당신에게 성가신 일을 끼치는 것이 싫어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “요즘 들어 자잘한 일들을 몇 가지 보아 오기는 했지만—”\n\n“그 사람이 당신한테 무슨 말이라도 한 적이 있소?”\n\n“누구요—팰리서 씨 말인가요? 한마디도 없었어요.”\n\n“이런 투로 넌지시 비친 적도 없단 말이오?”\n\n“한마디도 없었어요. 그랬더라면 그이가 다시는 내 응접실에 들어올 수 없다는 것을 내가 당신께 알려 드렸을 거예요.” 그러고 나서 그는 다시 편지를 읽었다. 아니면 읽는 시늉을 했다.\n\n“당신 어머니는 좋은 뜻으로 하신 거요.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“아, 그럼요, 좋은 뜻으로 하신 거지요. 귀에 들어온 수군거림을 곧이곧대로 믿으신 것이 어리석으셨어요—당신께 이런 성가신 일을 끼치게 만드신 것이 몹시 어리석으셨고요.”\n\n“아, 그거야 나는 성가시지 않소. 맹세코 아니오. 자, 그리젤다, 죄다 털어놓읍시다. 다른 사람들도 이 말을 했고, 나도 마음이 편치 않았소. 이제 당신도 다 알게 되었구려.”\n\n“내가 당신 마음을 편치 않게 했나요?”\n\n“글쎄, 아니오. 당신은 아니오. 내가 사실을 다 털어놓는다고 나를 모질게 몰아세우지는 마오. 바보들과 짐승 같은 것들이 나를 언짢게 하는 말을 수군거렸소. 그것들이야 악마가 데려갈 때까지 수군거리든 말든, 다시는 나를 성가시게 하지 못할 거요. 자, 입을 맞춰 주오, 여보.” 그러고서 그는 정말로 두 팔을 벌려 그녀를 안았다. “당신 어머니께 다정한 편지를 써서 오월에 한 주일쯤 올라오시라고 청하오. 그게 제일 좋겠소. 그러면 그분도 알아들으실 거요. 아이고, 열두 시로군. 잘 있으오.”\n\n레이디 덤벨로는 저가 이겼다는 것을, 그리고 어머니의 편지가 저에게 더없이 값진 물건이었다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그러나 그것은 이미 쓰였으므로 그녀는 다시 읽지 않았다. 그녀는 조용하고 편안하게 아침을 들면서 모자 가게에서 온 프랑스 안내장을 훑어보았다. 그러고서 그런 일에 알맞은 때가 온전히 이르렀을 무렵 글 쓰는 탁자로 가서 어머니의 편지에 답장을 썼다.\n\n사랑하는 엄마께 [라고 그녀는 썼다],\n\n엄마의 편지는 곧바로 덤벨로 경에게 보여 드리는 것이 가장 낫겠다고 생각했어요. 그이는 사람들이란 심술궂게 마련이라고 했고, 그런 이야기가 오가는 것은 어쩔 수 없는 일이라고 여기는 듯했어요. 엄마에 대해서는 조금도 화를 내지 않았고, 다음 달 말께 엄마와 아버지가 한 주일쯤 우리에게 와 계시는 편이 좋겠다고 했어요. 꼭 오세요. 23일에는 제법 큰 만찬 모임이 있어요. 전하께서 오시기로 되어 있는데, 아버지도 그분을 뵙고 싶어 하실 것 같아요. 그 아주 높다란 보닛이 죄다 사라진 것을 보셨어요? 저는 한 번도 그것을 좋아한 적이 없어요. 파리에서 언질을 받은 터라 그것을 눌러 없애려고 힘껏 애써 왔고요. 부디 아무 일도 엄마가 오시는 것을 막지 않기를 바라요.\n\n엄마를 사랑하는 딸,\n\n칼턴 가든스, 수요일.\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 그 편지를 받은 순간부터 제 의심으로 딸에게 못 할 짓을 했음을 알았다. 편지에 적힌 말이나 거기 담긴 뜻을 한마디라도 의심할 생각은 들지 않았다. 그녀가 틀렸던 것이고, 그녀는 그것이 기뻤다. 그러나 그럼에도 그 편지에는 그녀를 성가시게 하고 속을 긁는 무엇인가가 있었는데, 정작 그 성가심의 까닭을 그녀는 스스로에게 설명할 수 없었다. 그녀는 제가 쓴 글에 온 마음을 쏟아부었건만, 제 아이가 쓴 말에서는 마음의 자취라고는 한 조각도 찾을 수 없었다. 그랜틀리 부인이 딸을 기르면서 그토록 훌륭하게 해낸 그 하느님과 재물의 화해에서, 그 기관은 쓸 일이 없었고 그래서 쓰이지 않은 탓에 죽어 버리지는 않았더라도 시들어 버렸던 것이다.\n\n“우리는 거기 가지 않는 게 좋겠어요.” 그랜틀리 부인이 남편에게 말했다.\n\n“아이고, 그럼, 절대로 가지 않지. 당신이 도시에 나갈 일이 정 있거든 내가 방을 얻어 주겠소. 그리고 전하로 말하자면—! 나는 전하를 매우 존경하오만, 덤벨로의 식탁에서 그분을 뵙고 싶은 마음은 조금도 없소.”\n\n그리하여 플럼스테드 에피스코피에 사는 이들에 관한 한 그 일은 그렇게 결말이 났다.\n\n그런데 덤벨로 경은 열두 시에 그토록 부랴부랴 아내의 방을 나선 뒤 어디로 갔을까? 공원도 아니고, 태터솔스도 아니고, 하원의 위원회실도 아니고, 제 클럽의 내닫이창 자리도 아니었다. 그는 곧장 러드게이트 힐의 큰 보석상으로 가서, 아주 귀하고 진기한 놀라운 초록 목걸이 하나를 샀다. 반짝이는 초록 물방울들이 묵직하게 달리고 빛나는 초록 보석이 정갈한 금에 세 줄로 박힌 것으로—무게와 크기로 말하자면 거의 보석 갑옷에 이르는 목걸이였다. 그것은 온갖 전시회에 다 나왔던 물건이었고, 값도 몹시 비싸고 화려했다. 저녁에 레이디 덤벨로가 아직 몸단장을 하고 있을 때 이것이 나리의 사랑과 함께, 다시 믿게 되었다는 표시로 그녀에게 전해졌다. 그리고 레이디 덤벨로는 그 반짝이들을 세면서 제가 패를 잘 놀렸다고 스스로에게 이르며 속으로 승리를 맛보았다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 제 나리와의 온전한 화해가 낳은 그 반짝이들을 세고 있는 동안, 가엾은 플랜태저넷 팰리서는 아무것도 모른 채 여전히 떨고 있었다. 그가 그랜틀리 부인의 편지와 그 부인의 답장과 나리의 선물을 볼 수만 있었더라면! 그러나 그런 봄은 그에게 허락되지 않았고, 그는 제 사륜마차에 실려 드 코시 백작부인의 집으로 갔다. 다가올 사랑에 가슴이 재잘거리고, 다가올 파멸에 몸이 떨렸다. 아무튼 그가 다다른 결론은 이것이었다. 무슨 일이든 해야 한다면 지금 해야 한다는 것. 그는 사랑의 말을 한마디 건넬 것이고, 그 말이 받게 될 응답에 따라 제 앞날을 준비할 것이었다.\n\n그가 도착했을 때 드 코시 백작부인의 방들은 몹시 붐볐다. 그해 사교철의 첫 대혼잡 모임이었고, 온 세상이 포트먼 스퀘어로 모여들어 있었다. 드 코시 백작부인은 제 나리에게 이가 하나도 없기라도 한 듯, 맏아들의 처지가 아주 행복하기라도 한 듯, 드 코시 집안의 이해가 온통 잘 굴러가기라도 한 듯 웃고 있었다. 레이디 마거리타는 그 뒤에서 겉으로는 상냥하고 속으로는 쓰디쓴 얼굴로 있었고, 레이디 로지나도 조금 떨어진 자리에서 춤판이 없다는 이유로 이 세상의 헛됨과 화려함과 화해한 채 있었다. 그리고 이 집의 시집간 딸들도 와서, 의심할 바 없는 태생을 밑천 삼아 제 자리를 지키려 애썼으나 실제 형편이 변변찮은 탓에 얼마쯤 홀대를 받고 있었다. 게이지비도 와 있었다. 백작과 연이 닿았다는 그 사실 하나만으로도 행복했고, 백작의 사위로서 받는 대접에 흐뭇해했다. 그리고 크로즈비도 그 방에 있었다—다시는 백작부인의 시중을 들지 않겠다고, 그 집안의 그 초라함에서 저를 잘라 내겠다고 스스로 맹세해 놓고도 그 자리에 와 있었다. 그러나 그가 그들과 그들의 길을 버린다면 그에게는 그 밖에 무엇이 남는단 말인가? 그리하여 그는 와 있었고, 이제 한구석에 홀로 뾰로통하게 서서 모든 것이 헛되다고 스스로에게 이르고 있었다. 그렇다. 헛된 자에게는 모든 것이 헛될 것이고, 마음이 가난한 자에게는 모든 것이 가난할 것이다.\n\n레이디 덤벨로는 안쪽의 작은 방에 있었는데, 이 집에 처음 닿았을 때 안내되어 앉은 긴 의자에 그대로 앉아 있었고 떠날 때까지 그 자리에 머무를 참이었다. 이따금 아주 지체 높거나 아주 높은 자리에 오른 인물이 그녀 앞에 와서 한마디를 건네면 그녀는 그 높은 인물에게 또 한마디로 답했다. 그러나 아무도 그녀를 붙들고 대화라는 수고를 벌여 볼 엄두를 내지 않았다. 레이디 덤벨로는 대화를 하지 않는다는 것이—이따금 팰리서 씨와 나눌 때가 아니라면—사람들 사이에 통하는 이야기였다.\n\n그녀는 팰리서 씨가 그곳에서 저를 만나기로 되어 있다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그가 백작부인의 초대에 응할 뜻이냐고 몹시 애타게 물어 온 끝에 저도 그러겠노라고 딱 잘라 말해 두었던 것이다. “아마 갈 것 같아요.” 그때 그녀는 그렇게 말했고, 이제 어머니의 편지나 남편이 저에게 한 처사 때문에 제 말을 어기지는 않겠다고 마음을 굳혀 두었다. 팰리서 씨가 “제 몸가짐을 잊는다면”, 그녀는 남편에게 한마디를 건넬 줄 알았던 것처럼 그에게도 한마디를 건넬 줄 알 것이었다. 몸가짐을 잊다니! 그녀는 팰리서 씨가 지난 몇 달 동안 제 몸가짐을 잊기로 마음을 다잡아 왔다는 것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n그는 정말로 그녀에게 다가와, 그녀 위로 우뚝 서서 말로 다 할 수 없는 것들을 눈으로 말했다. 그러나 그 말로 다 할 수 없는 것들이란 워낙 그렇게 눈으로만 말해진 것이어서, 그 부인이 반드시 알은체를 해야 할 정도는 아니었다. 그는 용광로처럼 한숨을 쉬지도 않았고, 그녀의 머리 위 하늘에 해가 둘이라도 뜬 듯이 눈을 크게 뜨지도 않았으며, 제 가슴을 치거나 머리털을 쥐어뜯지도 않았다. 팰리서 씨는 고요함을 즐기며 제자들이 숭고한 쪽으로도 우스꽝스러운 쪽으로도 결코 넘어가지 못하게 하는 학교에서 자란 사람이었다. 그는 말로 다 할 수 없는 것을 한둘쯤 눈으로 말하기는 했으나, 그것을 하도 점잖은 눈으로 해냈으므로 그것을 낱낱이 재고 있던 그 부인도 아직은 팰리서 씨가 “제 몸가짐을 잊었다”고 선언할 수 없었다.\n\n긴 의자에는 그녀 곁에 자리가 있었고, 하틀베리에서는 한두 번 그가 감히 거기 앉아 본 적이 있었다. 그러나 이번에는 그녀의 옷자락을 뻔히 깔고 앉지 않고서는 그럴 수가 없었다. 그럴 마음만 있었다면 그녀는 그보다 더 큰 긴 의자라도 혼자 다 채울 줄 알았을 것이고—또한 자리를 내어 줄 줄도 알았을 것이다. 그리하여 그는 여전히 그녀 위로 선 채였고, 그녀는 그를 보고 미소를 지었다. 그런 미소라니! 그것은 죽음처럼 차가웠고, 아무도 우쭐하게 하지 못했으며, 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 그 뜻 없고 참되지 않은 우아함이 흉하기만 했다. 아! 나는 버릇처럼 웃는 여인의 미소가 어찌나 싫은지! 그것은 팰리서 씨를 몹시 거북하게 만들었다. 그러나 그는 그것을 뜯어보지 않고 밀고 나갔다.\n\n“레이디 덤벨로,” 그가 말했다. 목소리가 아주 낮았다. “여기서 뵙기를 고대하고 있었소.”\n\n“그러셨어요, 팰리서 씨? 예. 제가 오느냐고 물으셨던 것이 기억나요.”\n\n“그랬지요. 흠—레이디 덤벨로!” 그러면서 그는 숭고함도 우스꽝스러움도 다 피하라고 가르친 그 훈육의 바깥 경계를 하마터면 넘을 뻔했다. 그러나 아직은 제 몸가짐을 잊은 것이 아니었으므로 그녀는 다시 미소를 지었다.\n\n“레이디 덤벨로, 우리가 사는 이 세상에서는 이야기를 나눌 한때를 얻기가 참 어렵소.” 그는 그녀가 옷자락을 옮겨 주려니 생각했으나 그녀는 그러지 않았다.\n\n“아, 글쎄요.” 그녀가 말했다. “사람이 그리 많은 말을 하고 싶어 하는 일은 흔치 않은 것 같은데요.”\n\n“아, 그렇지요. 아마 흔치는 않겠지요. 그러나 정말로 하고 싶을 때가 있지 않소! 이렇게 붐비는 방이라니 나는 정말 싫소!” 그러나 하틀베리에 있을 때 그는 런던의 어느 큰 집의 붐비는 응접실만이 제게 알맞은 자리라고 마음을 굳혔던 터였다. “혹시 이런 자리 너머의 무엇인가를 바라신 적은 없으신지 궁금하오.”\n\n“아, 있지요.” 그녀가 말했다. “하지만 저는 모임을 좋아한다고 고백해야겠어요.”\n\n팰리서 씨는 둘레를 돌아보고 아무도 저를 보고 있지 않다고 여겼다. 그는 무엇을 할지 이미 마음을 정해 두었고, 그것을 하기로 작정한 터였다. 그에게는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 사랑을 고백하고 제 둘시네아를 채어 가는 사내들의 그 날램이라고는 조금도 없었다. 그러나 엄숙히 마음먹은 일을 하지 않고 넘긴다면 제 눈에 제가 부끄러워질 만큼의 뱃심은 있었다. 그는 앉아서 하고 싶었으나, 더 나은 수가 없는 터에 자리를 얻지 못했으니 선 채로 하기로 했다.\n\n“그리젤다.” 그가 말했다—그의 어조가 나쁘지 않았다는 것은 인정해야 한다. 그 말은 이끼 위에 내리는 가랑비처럼 부드럽게 그녀의 귀로 스며들었고, 다른 어떤 귀로도 스며들지 않았다. “그리젤다!”\n\n“팰리서 씨!” 그녀가 말했다. 그리고 그녀는 소란을 피우지도 않았고 그저 한 번 그를 흘낏 보았을 뿐이었으나, 그는 제가 잘못했음을 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n“그렇게 불러서는 안 되겠소?”\n\n“물론 안 되지요. 제 사람들이 와 있는지 보아 주십사 청해도 될까요?” 그는 잠시 머뭇거리며 그녀 앞에 서 있었다. “제 마차 말이에요.” 그렇게 이르면서 그녀가 다시 그를 흘낏 보았고, 그러자 그는 그 분부를 따랐다.\n\n그가 돌아왔을 때 그녀는 이미 자리를 떠난 뒤였다. 그러나 그는 계단에서 그녀의 이름이 불리는 것을 들었고, 그녀가 사람들 사이를 우아하게 헤치고 내려가는 뒷머리를 언뜻 보았다. 그는 두 번 다시 그녀에게 사랑을 건네려 하지 않았고, 그리하여 드 코시 백작부인과 프라우디 부인과 레이디 클랜디들럼의 기대를 남김없이 저버렸다.\n\n팰리서 씨의 앞날에 마음을 쓰는 이들에게 이 모험의 마지막 결말을 알려 주고 싶기도 하고, 또 이 자그마한 이야기에서는 그의 일로 다시 돌아올 자리가 없을 터이므로, 나는 아마 조금 앞질러 나아가 그 런던 사교철이 끝나기 전에 운명의 여신이 그를 위해 무엇을 해 주었는지 말해도 좋을 것이다. 그해 봄에 레이디 글렌코라 매클러스키가 세상에 처음 나왔다는 것은 누구나 아는 일이고, 그녀가 고인이 된 아일스 경의 외동딸로서 그 시절 최고의 재산 상속녀였다는 것 또한 그만큼 널리 알려진 일이다. 스카이, 스타파, 멀, 애런, 뷰트의 세습 영지가 케이스니스주와 로스셔와 더불어 작위를 따라 올드리키 후작에게 넘어간 것은 사실이다. 그러나 파이프, 애버딘, 퍼스, 킨카딘셔의 재산은—그 여러 주의 대부분을 차지하는 것으로—러나크의 탄광과 글래스고 시내의 어마어마한 토지와 함께 한정상속이 걸려 있지 않았으므로 레이디 글렌코라에게 갔다. 그녀는 살결이 흰 아가씨였고, 눈은 밝은 파란빛이었으며, 짧고 물결치는 아마빛 머리털은 보기에 몹시 부드러웠다. 레이디 글렌코라는 키가 작았고, 그 발그레하고 동그란 얼굴에는 어쩌면 여인의 아름다움 가운데 가장 높은 우아함이 빠져 있었는지도 모른다. 그러나 그 얼굴에는 늘 미소가 있어 바라보기에 아주 즐거웠고, 춤을 추고 이야기를 하고 제 앞에 놓인 온갖 놀이를 하나도 놓치지 않고 좇는 그 뜨거운 열심은 참으로 사랑스러웠다. 그녀가 타는 말은 더없이 귀여운 것이었고—아! 그녀는 그 말을 어찌나 사랑했는지! 게다가 말만큼이나 귀여운 작은 개도 한 마리 있었다. 어린 날의 벗 서브리나 스콧은—아, 그런 아가씨가 또 있을까! 그리고 사촌인 어린 아일스 경, 후작의 상속인은 어찌나 곱고 아름다운지 그녀는 늘 그 아이를 입맞춤으로 뒤덮어 주었다. 안타깝게도 그 아이는 겨우 여섯 살이었으므로 두 재산이 한데 모일 가망은 거의 없었다.\n\n그러나 레이디 글렌코라는 그토록 사랑스러웠음에도 세상에 처음 나온 이 첫걸음에서부터 벌써 벗들에게 큰 걱정을 끼쳤고, 올드리키 후작을 낙담으로 거의 미치게 만들었다. 도시에는 무섭도록 잘생긴 사내가 하나 있었으니, 누구든 주는 대로 한 푼도 남기지 않고 다 써 버렸고, 브랜디를 몹시 좋아했으며, 뉴마켓에서는 이름이 알려져 있었으되 믿음은 받지 못했고, 온갖 악덕에 깊이 빠져 있다는 말이 돌았으며, 제 아버지조차 말을 섞지 않는 자였다. 그런데 레이디 글렌코라는 그 사내와 춤추는 일에 도무지 싫증을 내지 않았다. 어느 날 아침 그녀는 사촌인 후작에게 눈을 번뜩이며—그 동그란 파란 눈도 번뜩일 줄 알았던 것이다—버고 피츠제럴드는 죄를 지었다기보다 죄를 입은 쪽이라고 말했다. 아이고! 집안 재산의 앞날을 걱정하는 후견인 후작이 그런 형편에서 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n그러나 사교철이 끝나기 전에 후작과 공작은 둘 다 행복한 사람이 되었고, 레이디 글렌코라도 흡족했기를 우리는 바라 마지않는다. 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨가 그녀와 두 번 춤을 추고 제 마음을 털어놓았던 것이다. 그는 후작과 이야기를 나누었는데 그 자리는 더할 나위 없이 흡족했고, 모든 일이 매듭지어졌다. 글렌코라는 틀림없이 그에게, 버고 피츠제럴드에게서 그 밋밋한 금반지를 받았던 일과 그것을 도로 돌려주었던 일을 이야기했을 것이다. 그러나 버고가 그런 보물을 넣어 두는 제 함에 아직도 간직하고 있는 그 물결치는 금빛 머리채 한 타래에 대해서도 그녀가 이야기했을지는 의심스럽다.\n\n“플랜태저넷,” 공작이 여느 때와는 사뭇 다른 따뜻함으로 말했다. “이 일에서도 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 자네는 내가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 보여 주었네. 나는 후작에게 매칭 프라이어리를 영지째로 곧바로 자네에게 넘기겠다고 일러 두었네. 내가 아는 가장 편안한 시골 저택이지. 글렌코라에게는 혼례 선물로 더 혼스를 주겠네.”\n\n그러나 팰리서 씨를 가장 기쁘게 한 것은 포더길 씨의 그 스스럼없고 꾸밈없는 즐거움이었다. 팰리서 가의 상속인이 제 도리를 다했으니, 포더길 씨는 참으로 꾸밈없이 행복한 사람이었다.\n\n# 제56장. 크로즈비 씨가 어떻게 다시 행복한 사람이 되었는가\n\n드 코시 백작부인이 사월 하순에 런던에서 큰 모임을 열었다는 것은 앞 장에서 이야기한 바 있다. 그러니 드 코시 집안의 일이 잘 풀려 가고 있었다고 여길 법도 하다. 그러나 그런 짐작은 사실과 다르지 않을까 나는 걱정한다. 어쨌거나 레이디 알렉산드리나의 일만은 잘 풀려 가고 있지 않았으니, 어머니가 런던에 올라온 첫날 그녀는 제가 겪은 고생을 길게 늘어놓으려고 포트먼 스퀘어로 달려갔던 것이다.\n\n“아, 어머니! 믿지 못하실 거예요. 그이는 저한테 말이라고는 거의 걸지도 않아요.”\n\n“얘야, 사내에게는 그보다 더한 흠도 있는 법이다.”\n\n“저는 온종일 그 집에 혼자 있어요. 도무지 바깥출입을 못 해요. 그이는 저한테 마차 한 대 마련해 주겠다는 말도 없어요. 지난주에 한 번 같이 걷자고 하기는 했는데, 그것도 비가 오는 날이었어요. 저는 그이가 비가 내리기 시작할 때까지 기다렸다는 걸 알아요. 생각 좀 해 보세요, 이번 달에 제가 저녁 나들이를 한 것이 세 번도 안 돼요—어밀리아 언니네 말고는요. 그런데 이제는 삯마차 삯이 너무 비싸다면서 그쪽에도 다시는 안 가겠대요. 그 집이 얼마나 불편한지 어머니는 믿지 못하실 거예요.”\n\n“네가 고른 집인 줄 알았다만, 얘야.”\n\n“보기야 제가 봤죠. 하지만 집이라는 것이 어때야 하는지 제가 알 리 없잖아요. 어밀리아 언니는 좋지 않다고 했는데도 그이가 기어이 그 집으로 하겠다고 했어요. 그이는 어밀리아 언니를 미워해요. 저는 확실히 알아요. 언니와 게이지비 형부를 홀대할 수 있는 말이라면 무엇이든 하니까요. 게이지비 형부도 적어도 그이만큼은 훌륭한 사람인데 말이에요. 어떻게 생각하세요? 그이가 리처드한테 나가라고 통고했어요. 어머니는 그 사람을 보신 적이 없지만 아주 좋은 하인이었어요. 그런데 나가라고 통고해 놓고는 다른 사람을 들일 이야기는 꺼내지도 않아요. 저는 시중들 사람도 없이 그이와 살지는 않겠어요.”\n\n“내 귀한 딸아, 그이를 떠난다는 것 같은 일은 생각도 하지 마라.”\n\n“하지만 저는 생각할 거예요, 어머니. 그 집에서 제 삶이 어떤 것인지 어머니는 모르세요. 그이는 저한테 말을 걸지 않아요—절대로요. 만찬 전 여섯 시 반이면 집에 들어와서, 얼굴만 잠깐 비치고는 제 옷방으로 들어가 버려요. 만찬 때는 늘 잠자코 있고, 만찬이 끝나면 잠이 들어요. 아침은 언제나 아홉 시에 먹고 아홉 시 반에 나가는데, 저는 그이가 열한 시나 되어야 사무국에 닿는다는 걸 알아요. 제가 무엇을 갖고 싶다고 하면 그럴 형편이 못 된다고 해요. 저는 여태 그이가 인색한 사람이라고는 생각해 본 적이 없는데, 이제는 그이가 속으로는 구두쇠임이 틀림없다고 확신해요.”\n\n“낭비꾼인 것보다야 그편이 낫다, 알렉산드리나.”\n\n“그편이 낫다고는 생각하지 않아요. 그이가 저를 지금보다 더 불행하게 만들 수는 없을 거예요. 불행하다는 말로는 모자라요. 아침 아홉 시부터 저녁 여섯 시까지 그런 집에 혼자 갇혀 있으면서 제가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요? 그이가 어떤 사람인지 다들 아니까 아무도 저를 보러 오지 않아요. 솔직히 말씀드릴게요, 어머니. 저는 이것을 견디지 않겠어요. 어머니가 저를 도와주실 수 없다면, 저는 다른 데서 도움을 찾아보겠어요.”\n\n어쨌거나 드 코시 집안의 그 갈래에서 일이 잘 풀려 가고 있지 않았다는 것만은 말해도 좋겠다. 실은 다른 갈래에서도 일이 잘 풀려 가고 있지 않았다. 폴록 경은 혼인을 했는데, 그 평생의 반려를 귀족 사회의 가장 좋은 크림 가운데서 골라 온 것은 아니었다. 그의 어머니가 아들 편을 들어 한마디 거들어 보려 하다가 백작에게 어찌나 심한 욕을 먹었던지, 이제 그런 대접은 더 견딜 수 없노라고 선언하기에 이르렀다. 그녀는 백작이 통풍으로 제 방에 붙들려 있는 틈을 타 그의 명령을 정면으로 거스르고 런던에 올라왔고, 그를 무시하고 제 모임을 열었으니, 제가 등을 돌리고 나면 절망하여 슬그머니 물러갔다고 사람들이 말하지 못하게 하려는 것이었다.\n\n“나는 견뎌 왔다.” 그녀가 마거리타에게 말했다. “영국의 다른 어떤 여자도 못 해 냈을 만큼 오래 견뎠어. 너희 가운데 누구든 시집을 가리라고 여기던 동안은—”\n\n“아이, 그 이야기는 마세요, 어머니.” 마거리타가 목소리에 얼마쯤 경멸을 실어 말했다. 제 어머니까지도 이제 저에게는 아무 가망도 남지 않았다고 넌지시 말하는 것이 그녀는 썩 마땅치 않았다. 그러면서도 정작 그녀 자신은 시집갈 생각일랑 다 접었노라고 어머니에게 자주 말해 온 터였다.\n\n“로지나는 어밀리아네로 가면 된다.” 백작부인이 말을 이었다. “게이지비 씨도 그렇게 되는 것을 아주 흡족하게 여기고, 그 애가 제 몫의 비용은 감당할 만큼 갖도록 챙겨 줄 게다. 나는 너와 내가, 얘야, 바덴바덴으로 가면 어떨까 한다.”\n\n“돈은 어떻게 하고요, 어머니?”\n\n“게이지비 씨가 어떻게든 해내야지. 네 아버지가 무어라고 하든, 나는 돈이 있을 수밖에 없다는 것을 안다. 지금 우리가 사는 방식보다야 비용이 훨씬 적게 들 게다.”\n\n“그러면 아버지는 어떻게 하시고요?”\n\n“나로서는 어쩔 도리가 없다, 얘야. 내가 무엇을 견뎌야 했는지 아무도 모른다. 한 해만 더 그러다가는 내가 죽고 말 게다. 그이의 말버릇은 갈수록 더 험해지고, 나는 날마다 그이가 목발로 나를 후려치지나 않을까 겁이 난다.”\n\n이 모든 사정을 놓고 볼 때 드 코시 집안의 이해가 잘 굴러가고 있었다고는 말할 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 드 코시 백작부인은 바덴바덴으로 가겠다고 마음을 정했을 때, 막내딸을 데리고 갈 생각은 결코 없었다. 그녀는 여러 해를 견뎌 왔는데 이제 알렉산드리나는 여섯 달도 견디지 못한다는 것이었다. 게다가 그 딸이 내세우는 가장 큰 불평이라는 것이 이러했다—제 남편이 말이 없다는 것. 그런 종류의 괴로움을 두고 크게 투덜댈 권리는 어떤 여자에게도 없다고 어머니는 느꼈다. 제 백작이 그런 식으로만 죄를 지었더라면 그녀는 마지막까지 그의 곁에 남아 있는 것으로 만족했으리라!\n\n그러면서도 알렉산드리나의 삶이 그 어머니의 삶만큼이나 고달팠던 것은 아닌지 나로서는 알 수 없다. 그이가 저에게 말을 걸지 않는다고 그녀가 말했을 때, 그것은 사실에서 그리 벗어난 말이 아니었다. 안락함이라고는 없는 그 새집에서 그녀가 보내는 시간은 몹시 길었다—몹시 길고 몹시 지루했다. 혼인이란 그녀가 기대하던 것과는 도무지 딴판이었다. 어머니 곁의 친정에서는 언제나 둘레에 사람이 있었으나, 그들이 늘 그녀 자신이 벗으로 고를 만한 사람들이었던 것은 아니다. 그녀는 혼인을 하고 나면 제 마음에 맞는 사람들을 골라 곁에 둘 수 있으리라고 생각했다. 그런데 막상 보니 찾아오는 사람이 아무도 없었다. 그녀보다 슬기로운 여자였던 언니는 뚜렷한 생각을 품고 혼인 생활을 시작해 그것을 끝까지 밀고 나갔다. 그러나 이 가엾은 사람은 이를테면 모래톱에 얹힌 배처럼 오도 가도 못하는 저를 발견했다. 한번 마음속에 남편에 대한 노여움을 품고 나자—그러기까지 두 사람이 함께 지낸 지 한 주일도 걸리지 않았다—그녀를 그에게로 다시 데려다 놓을 사랑이라고는 남아 있지 않았다. 그가 방에 들어설 때 반가운 낯빛을 지어 보이는 것이, 그리고 적어도 그가 곁에 있음으로 해서 제가 행복하다고 그가 여기게 만드는 것이 제 도리임을 그녀는 알지 못했다. 그녀는 새집에 이르기도 전에 침울해졌고, 그 침울함을 결코 내려놓지 않았다. 그로서도 손닿는 데에 무언가 있어 보이기만 했다면 가정의 안락을 얻으려고 힘껏 싸워 보았을 것이다. 사정이 그러하였으므로 그는 가정의 체면을 지키려 싸웠으니, 그렇게 하는 것이 지금 제게 주어진 처지를 그럭저럭 떠받치는 가장 나은 길이라고 믿었기 때문이다. 그러나 그 일은 갈수록 더 힘겨워졌고, 침울함은 갈수록 더 짙어졌다. 그는 그녀의 불행이 아니라 제 불행을 생각했다. 그녀가 그의 따분함이 아니라 제 따분함을 생각했듯이. “이런 것이 가정의 지복이라면!” 그는 안락의자에 앉아 책 한 권에 정신을 붙들어 매려 애쓰면서 속으로 그렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n“이런 것이 혼인 생활의 행복이라면!” 그녀는 책 읽는 시늉조차 없이 제 찻잔들 뒤에 멍하니 앉은 채 그렇게 생각했다. 사실 그녀는 그와 함께 걸으려 하지 않았으니, 런던의 어느 광장 포도를 빙빙 도는 그런 운동 따위는 마음에 들지 않았던 것이다. 그리고 그는 그녀가 마차 삯으로 빚을 지는 일만은 없게 하겠다고 단단히 마음먹고 있었다. 그는 제 돈을 두고 쩨쩨하게 구는 사람이 아니었다. 구두쇠도 아니었다. 그러나 그는 백작의 딸과 혼인함으로써 스스로 가난한 사람이 되었다는 것을 알아차렸고, 그 위에 빚에 쫓기는 사람까지는 되지 않겠다고 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n신부는 어머니와 언니가 바덴바덴으로 달아날 참이라는 말을 듣자, 저도 그 도피에 함께할 수 있으리라는 희망이 불현듯 솟구쳤다. 그녀는 남편과 갈라설 생각은 아니었다. 적어도 두 사람이 다투었다고 세상이 여길 만한 방식으로 갈라설 생각은 아니었다. 그저 훌쩍 떠나 오래 머물다 오려는 것이었다—아주 오래 머물다 오려는 것이었다. 두 해 전이었다면 어머니와 마거리타와 함께 바덴바덴에 머문다는 것이 그녀에게 그리 매력 있는 일로 보이지 않았을 터이다. 그러나 이제 그녀의 눈에는 그런 삶이 낙원의 삶처럼 보였다. 실로 프린세스 로열 크레슨트에서 보내는 그 시간들의 따분함은 참으로 무거웠던 것이다.\n\n그러나 어떻게 하면 그렇게 되도록 만들 수 있을까? 어머니와 나눈 그 이야기는 모임이 열리기 전날에 있었고, 드 코시 백작부인은 그것을 마거리타에게 질겁한 낯으로 되풀이해 들려주었다.\n\n“그야 그이가 그 애한테 생활비는 대 주겠지요.” 마거리타가 냉랭하게 말했다.\n\n“하지만 얘야, 두 사람이 혼인한 지 겨우 열 주밖에 되지 않았다.”\n\n“혼인했다는 이유로 사람이 아주 비참해져야 할 까닭이 어디 있는지 저는 모르겠어요.” 마거리타가 대답했다. “저는 그 애더러 그이를 떠나라고 부추기고 싶지는 않아요. 그렇지만 그 애 말이 사실이라면 몹시 불편한 노릇임에는 틀림없잖아요.”\n\n크로즈비는 포트먼 스퀘어의 모임에 가는 데 동의하기는 했으나, 그 잔치 자리에서 그리 크게 즐기지는 못했다. 그는 누구에게도 말 한마디 거의 건네지 않은 채 시무룩하게 서 있었다. 그의 삶의 모습은 통틀어 지난 몇 달 사이에 달라져 버린 듯했다. 그가 제 영광을 찾곤 하던 곳이 바로 이런 자리였다. 그런 자리에서 그는 남다른 빛으로 빛났고, 둘레에 무디게 서서 그의 눈부신 앞길을 지켜보던 많은 이의 마음을 시샘으로 물들였다. 그러나 이제 그 방들 안에서 그보다 더 무디고, 더 말이 없고, 덜 대접받는 사람은 없었다. 그런데도 그는 그 귀한 집안의 사위로서 그 자리에 버젓이 자리 잡고 있었다. “꽤 굼뜬 자리로군, 그렇지 않은가?” 게이지비가 여러 번 애를 쓴 끝에 구석에 있는 제 동서에게 다가가는 데 성공하고는 그에게 말했다. 이 말에 크로즈비는 그저 툴툴거리기만 했다. “나로 말하자면,” 게이지비가 말을 이었다. “차라리 집에서 신문이랑 실내화나 끼고 있는 편이 훨씬 낫겠네. 이런 모임이라는 것은 혼인한 사내에게는 맞지 않는 것 같아.” 크로즈비는 다시 툴툴거리고는 다른 구석으로 달아났다.\n\n크로즈비와 그의 아내는 삯마차를 타고 함께 집으로 돌아갔다—둘 다 말이 없었다. 알렉산드리나는 삯마차를 싫어했다. 그러나 그런 탈것으로만, 오직 그런 탈것으로만 다닐 수 있다는 말을 그녀는 진작에 딱 잘라 들은 터였다. 이튿날 아침 그는 아홉 시 정각에 아침 식탁에 앉았으나, 그녀는 그가 사무국으로 떠난 뒤에야 얼굴을 내밀었다. 그러나 그러고 나서 얼마 안 되어 그녀는 어머니와 언니에게로 갔다. 그런데도 그가 제 집에 돌아와 그녀를 보러 들어섰을 때 그녀는 시무룩한 낯으로 응접실에 앉아 있었다. 인사로 받아들일 만한 무슨 말인가를 건네고 그는 물러가려 했으나, 그녀는 저와 이야기를 좀 해 달라는 청으로 그를 붙들어 세웠다.\n\n“좋소.” 그가 말했다. “나는 옷을 갈아입으러 가려던 참이었소. 이제 곧 여섯 시 반이오.”\n\n“오래 붙들지는 않겠어요. 그리고 만찬이 몇 분 늦어진다 해도 대수로울 것 없어요. 어머니와 마거리타 언니가 바덴바덴으로 가신대요.”\n\n“바덴바덴으로 간다고 하오?”\n\n“네. 그리고 거기서 머무를 작정이시래요—꽤 오랫동안요.” 잠시 사이가 있었고, 알렉산드리나는 목을 가다듬고 잔기침을 하며 다음 말을 할 채비를 해야겠다고 여겼다. 그녀는 제 제안을 기어이 꺼낼 작정이었으나, 그것이 처음에 어떻게 받아들여질지가 자못 두려웠다.\n\n“코시 성에 무슨 일이라도 있었소?” 크로즈비가 물었다.\n\n“아니요. 아니, 있기는 해요. 아버지와 어머니 사이에 무슨 말이 오갔나 봐요. 하지만 저도 잘은 몰라요. 그렇지만 그건 지금 문제가 아니에요. 어머니는 떠나시고, 올해 남은 동안은 거기 머무를 작정이시래요.”\n\n“그러면 런던 집은 내놓겠구려.”\n\n“그렇겠지요. 하지만 그건 아버지가 정하실 일이에요. 제가 어머니와 함께 가는 데 반대하세요?”\n\n혼인한 지 열 주밖에 안 된 신부가 물을 만한 질문이라니! 그녀가 새집에서 남편과 함께 지낸 것은 겨우 한 달 남짓인데, 이제 그 집을 떠나게—그리고 그도 떠나게—해 달라고, 그것도 몇 달이 될지 알 수 없는 동안, 어쩌면 영영 그러하겠다고 허락을 청하고 있는 것이었다. 그러나 이 청을 하면서도 그녀는 조금도 마음이 들뜬 기색이 없었다. 그 얼굴에는 슬픔도, 아쉬움도, 희망도 없었다. 마치 제 개인 소유물인 양 꾸며 놓은 마차를 한 주에 두 번 쓰게 해 달라고 청할 때 한때 지어 보였던 그 생기의 절반도 그녀는 지금 띠고 있지 않았다. 그때 크로즈비는 아주 매몰차게 대답했고, 그의 거절이 분명해지자 그녀는 울었다. 그러나 이제 울음이란 없을 참이었다. 그녀는 갈 작정이었다—그가 허락을 준다면 허락을 받아서, 거절한다면 허락 없이라도. 돈 문제야 물론 중요했지만 그것은 게이지비가 처리해 줄 터였다—그가 그런 일이란 일은 다 처리해 주듯이.\n\n“그 사람들과 함께 바덴바덴으로 간다고?” 크로즈비가 말했다. “얼마 동안이나?”\n\n“글쎄요, 어지간히 오래 있지 않으면 아무 소용이 없겠지요.”\n\n“얼마나 오래를 말하는 거요, 알렉산드리나? 정말로 하고 싶은 말을 툭 터놓고 해 보시오. 한 달이오?”\n\n“아, 그보다는 오래요.”\n\n“두 달이오, 아니면 여섯 달이오, 아니면 그 사람들이 거기 머무는 동안 내내요?”\n\n“그건 나중에, 제가 거기 가서 정하면 되잖아요.” 이러는 내내 그녀는 한 번도 그의 얼굴을 들여다보지 않았다. 그는 줄곧 그녀를 뚫어져라 바라보고 있었건만.\n\n“당신 말은,” 그가 말했다. “나에게서 떠나고 싶다는 것이구려.”\n\n“한편으로 보면 떠나는 것이기는 하겠지요.”\n\n“하지만 흔히 쓰는 뜻으로도 그렇지 않소? 몇 달이 될지 기약도 없이 바덴바덴에 가겠다고 말하면서, 당신에게 돌아올 생각이라는 것이 있기는 하오?”\n\n“런던으로 돌아오는 것 말이에요?”\n\n“나에게로 돌아오는 것 말이오—내 집으로—아내로서의 당신 본분으로! 원하는 것이 무엇인지 어째서 대번에 말하지 못하오? 나와 갈라져 살고 싶다는 것 아니오?”\n\n“저는 여기서 행복하지 않아요—이 집에서요.”\n\n“그러면 이 집은 누가 골랐소? 내가 여기로 오고 싶어 했소? 그러나 그것이 문제가 아니오. 당신이 여기서 행복하지 않다면, 다른 어떤 집에서 무엇을 가진들 행복해질 수 있단 말이오?”\n\n“당신이 이 방에 일고여덟 시간씩 혼자 남겨져 찾아오는 사람 하나 없이 있어 본다면 제 말이 무슨 뜻인지 아실 거예요. 게다가 그 시간이 지난 뒤에도 그리 나을 것이 없고요. 당신은 집에 있을 때조차 저한테 말을 걸지 않잖아요.”\n\n“아무도 당신을 찾아오지 않는 것이 내 잘못이오? 사실인즉 이렇소, 알렉산드리나. 당신은 내 수입에 걸맞은 살림살이에 저를 맞출 마음이 없는 거요. 당신은 마차를 타고 공원을 돌 수 없으니 비참한 거요. 나는 당신에게 마차를 마련해 줄 수 없고, 그러려고 애쓰지도 않겠소. 바덴바덴으로 가고 싶으면 가시오—그러니까, 당신 어머니가 당신을 데려갈 뜻이 있다면 말이오.”\n\n“물론 제 비용은 제가 대야겠지요.” 알렉산드리나가 말했다. 그러나 이 말에 그는 그 자리에서 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 허락을 내주자마자 그는 자리에서 일어나 나가려던 참이었고, 그녀의 마지막 말은 문간에서야 그를 붙들었을 뿐이다. 따지고 보면 이것이야말로 그가 꾸밀 수 있는 가장 값싼 방책이 아니겠는가? 셈을 헤아려 보는 동안 그는 옷방에서 벽난로 선반에 팔꿈치를 괴고 서 있었다. 그는 제 아내가 저와 함께 지내며 불행해한다고 나무랐지만, 저 역시 그녀와 함께 지내며 그만큼 불행하지 않았던가? 이렇게 조용히, 남의 눈에 잘 띄지 않게 갈라지는 편이—갈라져서 다시는 함께 살지 않는 편이 낫지 않겠는가? 그에게 다행이었던 것은, 그런 방책의 이점을 망쳐 놓을 자그마한 크로즈비가 생길 낌새가 여태 두 사람에게 없었다는 점이다. 그녀에게 한 해 사백 파운드를 주고, 빚을 갚아 나가라고 게이지비에게 이백 파운드를 더 얹어 준다 해도, 그에게는 런던에서 제 몫으로 즐길 육백 파운드가 남을 터였다. 물론 혼인 전의 그 행복하던 시절처럼 살 수는 없을 것이고, 돈 문제를 떼어 놓고 보더라도 그런 살림살이는 이제 그의 손이 닿는 데에 있지 않았다. 그러나 클럽에 가서 만찬을 들 수는 있었다. 호사스럽게 궐련을 피울 수도 있었다. 온갖 결심에도 불구하고 이제는 거의 견딜 수 없는 것이 되어 버린 그 목석 같은 집에 얽매이지 않아도 될 터였다. 그렇게 그는 셈을 헤아려 보고는, 제 신부가 가 주는 편이 좋겠다는 것을 알아냈다. 그는 집과 세간을 게이지비에게 넘겨주고, 그것을 어떻게 하든 게이지비 마음대로 하게 할 참이었다. 다시 독신이 되어 하숙집에서, 한 해 육백 파운드를 제 몸 하나에 쓰며 산다는 것이 이제 그에게는 어찌나 큰 행복의 전망으로 보였던지, 옷을 갈아입는 동안 그는 거의 마음이 가벼워질 지경이었다. 그는 그녀를 바덴바덴으로 보내 주기로 했다.\n\n만찬 때에는 그 이야기가 나오지 않았고, 하인이 응접실 탁자에 찻그릇을 놓고 물러갈 때까지 그는 그 이야기를 다시 꺼내지 않았다. “원한다면 어머니와 함께 가도 좋소.” 그때 그가 말했다.\n\n“그러는 편이 가장 좋겠어요.” 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“그렇겠지. 어쨌거나 당신 좋을 대로 하시오.”\n\n“그러면 돈은요?”\n\n“그 일은 내가 게이지비에게 말하도록 맡겨 두는 편이 낫겠소.”\n\n“좋아요. 차 좀 드시겠어요?” 그리고 이렇게 모든 일이 끝났다.\n\n이튿날 그녀는 점심을 든 뒤 어머니의 집으로 갔고, 다시는 프린세스 로열 크레슨트로 돌아오지 않았다. 그날 아침 그녀는 제 손으로 꾸리고 싶은 물건들을 꾸렸고, 그 밖에 제 것이라 할 만한 것은 무엇이든 가져오라고 언니들을 집안의 늙은 하인과 함께 그리로 보냈다. “저런, 저런.” 어밀리아가 말했다. “저 물건들을 저 애들한테 갖추어 주느라 내가 얼마나 애를 먹었는데, 그것도 바로 엊그제 일이오. 아무래도 그 애가 떠나는 것은 잘못이라는 생각밖에 안 드는구려.”\n\n“글쎄요.” 마거리타가 말했다. “그 애는 언니만큼 남편 복이 좋지는 못했잖아요. 저는 늘 그 애가 그이를 다루기 어려워하리라고 느꼈어요.”\n\n“하지만 얘, 그 애는 애를 써 보지도 않았소. 대번에 손을 놓아 버린 거요. 모자랐던 것은 다루는 솜씨가 아니오. 사실인즉 알렉산드리나는 시작할 때부터 제가 맞이할 삶이 어떤 것인지에 마음을 다잡지 않았던 거요. 나는 다잡았소. 나는 그것이 모임에 다니고 그런 것들만 하는 삶은 아니라는 것을 알고 있었소. 그렇지만 크로즈비가 모티머와 같은 사람이 아니라는 것만은 인정해야겠구려. 나라면 그이와 함께 살아 나가지는 못했을 것 같소. 저 작은 책들도 마저 꾸려 넣는 편이 좋겠소. 그이는 저런 것에 마음 쓰지 않을 테니.” 그리하여 크로즈비의 집은 허물어져 흩어졌다.\n\n그녀는 그를 다시 보지 못했으니, 그가 포트먼 스퀘어의 집으로 작별 인사를 하러 가지 않았기 때문이다. 쪽지 한 장이 그의 사무국으로 전해져 왔다. “저는 여기 어머니와 함께 있으니 지금 작별 인사를 드려도 되겠지요. 우리는 화요일에 떠나요. 편지를 쓰고 싶으시면 여기 가정부 앞으로 보내시면 돼요. 부디 편안히 지내시고 건강하시기를 바라요. 당신의 다정한 A. C.” 그는 여기에 아무 답장도 하지 않고, 그날 클럽에 가서 만찬을 들었다.\n\n“자네를 통 못 본 지 한참일세.” 몽고메리 돕스가 말했다.\n\n“그렇지. 아내가 어머니와 함께 외국으로 나가는데, 그 사람이 가 있는 동안은 내가 다시 여기로 돌아올 참일세.”\n\n그에게 더 건네진 말은 없었고, 그의 집안일에 대해 물어보는 사람도 아무도 없었다. 이제 그에게는 제 아내나 제 식구의 안부를 물어볼 만큼 가까운 벗이 하나도 없는 듯이 여겨졌다. 그녀는 가 버렸고, 한 달 뒤에 그는 다시 마운트 스트리트에 있는 저를 발견했다—한 해 육백 파운드가 아니라 오백 파운드로 세상살이를 다시 시작하면서. 셈을 맞출 때가 되자 게이지비 씨가 지금 형편으로는 그보다 많은 수입은 그에게 가당치 않다는 것을 밝혀 보였기 때문이다. 백작부인은 사백오십 파운드라는 그런 쥐꼬리만 한 돈으로는 레이디 알렉산드리나를 데려갈 수 없다고 오래도록 버텼다. 게다가 보험료도 계속 물어야 하지 않는가?\n\n그러나 나는 그가 한 해 삼백 파운드만 받고도 제 자유를 받아들이는 데 동의했으리라고 생각한다—그에게 그 안도감은 그토록 컸던 것이다.\n\n# 제57장. 릴리언 데일이 어머니를 이기다\n\n존 임스가 그녀의 딸에게 제 간청을 올리던 그 자리에 데일 부인도 함께 있었으나, 그때 그녀는 이렇다 할 말을 거의 하지 않았다. 그녀의 바람은 온통 그 구혼자 편이었으되 그것을 입 밖에 낼 엄두를 내지 못했고, 그렇다고 방을 나갈 엄두도 내지 못했다. 제삼자가 보는 앞에서 이렇게 억지로 사랑을 고백해야 했던 것은 그에게 가혹한 일이었으나, 그는 그것을 해냈고 제 대답을 받아 들고 돌아갔다. 그러고 나서 일이 끝나자 릴리는 어머니와 한마디도 나누지 않은 채 자리를 떠 버렸고, 두 사람이 다시 함께 있게 되는 것이 자연스러운 저녁 시간이 올 때까지 다시는 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그렇게 홀로 남은 데일 부인은 딸의 손을 두고 새로 들어온 이 청혼 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수가 없었다. 그저 그것이 이루어질 수만 있다면! 그 일이 이루어지는 데에 릴리에게 건네는 제 말이 조금이라도 힘이 될 수만 있다면! 그러면서도 그녀는 여태 한마디를 꺼내는 것조차 두려워하고 있었다.\n\n그것이 몹시 어려운 일임을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그가 너무 이르게 왔다고, 저 또 한 번의 난파가 있은 지 너무 이르게 그런 시도가 이루어졌다고 그녀는 스스로에게 몇 번이고 되뇌었다. 배의 재목이 그토록 모질게 뒤틀린 마당에 어떻게 그 배가 곧바로 다시 바다로 나설 수 있단 말인가? 그럼에도 이제 그 시도가 이루어진 마당에, 이제 임스가 제 청을 말하고 대답을 받아 들고 물러난 마당에, 그녀는 언젠가 이 이야기를 꺼낼 작정이라면 지금 당장 릴리에게 그 이야기를 해야 한다고 느꼈다. 그녀는 제 아이를, 그리고 그 아이의 마음을 다 이해하고 있다고 생각했다. 릴리가 제 마음의 그런 변화를 인정하기까지 견뎌 내야 할 충격이 얼마나 격렬한 것인지도 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그러나 그 일이 이루어질 수만 있다면 릴리는 행복한 여자가 될 것이었다. 일단 이루어지고 나면 그것은 모든 면에서 축복일 터였다. 그리고 그 일이 이루어지지 않는다면 릴리의 삶은 끝까지 텅 비고 외롭고 사랑 없는 것이 되지 않겠는가? 그러나 저녁에 릴리가 여느 때처럼 가볍고 반쯤은 농담 섞인 말을 입에 물고 내려왔을 때, 데일 부인은 여전히 제 할 일에 나설 엄두를 내지 못했다.\n\n“엄마, 짐을 다시 다 풀어야 하고 셋집으로 옮겨 갈 계획은 없던 일이 되었다고, 이제 그렇게 정해진 걸로 여겨도 되겠죠.”\n\n“나는 잘 모르겠구나, 얘야.”\n\n“아이, 저는 알아요—방금 엄마가 하신 말씀을 듣고 나서는요. 사람들이 다 우리를 무슨 거위 떼로 여기겠어요!”\n\n“우리 스스로 우리를 거위라고 여기지만 않는다면, 또 네 삼촌이 우리를 그렇게 여기지만 않는다면, 나는 그런 건 조금도 개의치 않는다.”\n\n“삼촌은 우리를 백조라고 여기실걸요. 삼촌이 그 일을 그토록 진심으로 여기실 줄, 또 우리가 여기 있는 것을 그토록 마음에 두실 줄 제가 조금이라도 알았더라면, 저는 결코 떠나자는 쪽에 표를 던지지 않았을 거예요. 그런데 삼촌은 참 이상하세요. 화를 내셔야 할 자리에서는 다정하시고, 부드럽고 친절하셔야 할 자리에서는 심술궂으시니까요.”\n\n“어쨌거나 삼촌은 우리에게 그분의 정을 믿어도 좋을 이유를 주셨다.”\n\n“우리 딸들한테야 저는 한 번도 의심해 본 적 없어요. 하지만 엄마, 저는 보이스 부인을 마주할 자신이 없어요. 헌 부인과 크럼프 부인도 아주 고약하겠고, 우리가 뜻을 굽혔다는 걸 알면 홉킨스는 무섭게 우리를 몰아세울 거예요. 그래도 보이스 부인이 그중 누구보다 더할 거예요. 그 부인이 어떤 목소리로 축하를 건넬지 짐작이 안 되세요?”\n\n“보이스 부인쯤은 내가 견뎌 낼 것 같구나.”\n\n“아, 그렇겠죠. 가서 그 부인한테 말할 사람은 우리일 테니까요. 저는 엄마가 예전부터 겁쟁이라는 걸 알아요, 엄마. 안 그래요? 그리고 벨 언니는 애인이 있으니 눈 하나 깜짝 안 할 거예요. 보이스 부인쯤은 언니한테 아무것도 아닐 테니까요. 그걸 다 감당해야 할 사람은 저예요. 뭐, 상관없어요. 엄마가 여기서 행복하게 지내겠다고 약속만 해 주시면 저는 남는 쪽에 표를 던질게요. 아, 엄마, 엄마만 행복하시다면 저는 무엇에든 표를 던지겠어요.”\n\n“그러면 너는 행복하겠니?”\n\n“네, 하루 해가 긴 만큼이나 행복할 거예요. 다만 우리가 벨 언니를 영영 못 보게 되리라는 것만은 알아요. 딱 그만한 거리를 두고 살면 사람들은 서로 만나지 않게 되거든요. 오래 머무는 방문을 하기에는 너무 가깝고, 잠깐 들르는 방문을 하기에는 너무 머니까요. 이렇게 하면 어때요. 우리가 각자 절반씩 걸어 나가서 드 게스트 경의 숲 모퉁이에서 만나기로 하는 거예요. 거기에 우리가 걸상 하나 놓아도 된다고 할까요? 작은 오두막을 하나 두고 샌드위치랑 맥주 한 병을 들고 가도 되겠어요. 그런 식으로라면 서로 얼굴을 좀 볼 수 있지 않을까요?”\n\n그리하여 게스트윅으로 옮겨 갈 계획을 접는다는 것이 두 사람 모두에게 굳어진 생각이 되었고, 이 이야기를 두고 그들은 찻상 앞에서 이야기를 이어 갔다. 그러나 그날 저녁 데일 부인은 존 임스에 대해서는 끝내 한마디도 꺼내지 못했다.\n\n그러나 그들은 아직 옛집을 다시 꾸리는 일에 손을 댈 엄두는 내지 못했다. 그러자면 먼저 벨이 돌아와야 했고, 지주의 분명한 동의도 격식을 갖추어 받아 두어야 했다. 데일 부인은 어느 정도 제가 잘못했음을 스스로 인정하고, 제 고집을 부린 것을 용서해 달라고 청해야 했다.\n\n“우리 셋이 베옷을 걸치고 올라가서, 정원에서 홉킨스를 만나면 이마에 재를 뿌리는 게 낫겠어요.” 릴리가 말했다. “그러면 홉킨스는 철 이른 완두 한 접시를 보내 주어서 우리 머리에 숯불을 쌓아 올릴 게 뻔하고요. 딩글스는 꿩을 한 마리 들여보내 주겠죠. 다만 오월에는 꿩이 자라지 않는다는 게 문제지만요.”\n\n“베옷이 그보다 더 언짢은 모양을 하고 나타나지만 않는다면 나는 개의치 않겠다.”\n\n“그건 엄마한테 섬세한 감정이 없어서 그래요. 게다가 크리스토퍼 삼촌의 고마워하시는 꼴이라니!”\n\n“아! 그건 나도 마음에 걸리겠구나.”\n\n“하지만 엄마, 벨 언니가 돌아올 때까지 기다려요. 언니가 정하게 하는 거예요. 언니는 떠날 사람이니까 치우침 없이 판단할 테고요. 삼촌이 집을 새로 칠해 주시겠다고 나서시면—그러실 게 뻔하지만—저는 흙바닥까지 납작 엎드리게 되겠죠.”\n\n그러나 데일 부인은 제 마음에 가장 가까이 있는 그 이야기를 아직 한마디도 꺼내지 못했다. 릴리가 우스갯소리로 그녀를 겁쟁이라고 몰아세웠을 때 그녀의 마음은 곧바로 저 다른 문제로 달려갔고, 그녀는 스스로에게 자기가 겁쟁이라고 말했다. 어찌하여 제 자식에게 충고 한마디 건네는 것을 두려워한단 말인가? 크로즈비의 처사를 두고 저와 릴리 사이에 이렇다 할 말 한마디 오가지 않은 채 그 일을 지나쳐 보낸 것이 무슨 의무를 저버린 일처럼 여겨졌다. 크로즈비가 악당이었으며 그러므로 그 아이의 마음에서 내쳐 마땅하다는 사실을, 딸에게 억지로라도 납득시켰어야 하지 않았을까? 실상 릴리가 존 임스에게 저 약혼 이야기를 두고는 어머니에게조차 한 적 없을 만큼 그에게 터놓고 이야기하고 있다고 말했을 때, 그것은 있는 그대로의 진실이었다. 그날 밤 제 방에 앉아 잠자리에 들기 전에 이것을 곰곰이 생각하며, 데일 부인은 이튿날 아침에는 릴리가 제가 보는 대로 보고 제가 생각하는 대로 생각하도록 애써 보리라 마음먹었다.\n\n그녀는 제 할 일을 시작하기 전에 아침 식사를 그냥 흘려보냈고, 그러고 나서도 곧바로 그 일에 뛰어들지는 않았다. 찻잔이 물려 나가자 릴리는 제 바느질감 앞에 앉았고, 데일 부인은 늘 하던 대로 부엌으로 내려갔다. 그녀가 응접실에 자리를 잡고 앉은 것은 열한 시가 다 되어서였고, 그러고도 그녀는 반짇고리를 앞으로 끌어다 놓고 바늘을 꺼내 들었다.\n\n“벨 언니가 레이디 줄리아하고 잘 지내고 있을까요?” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“잘 지내고 있을 게다, 틀림없이.”\n\n“레이디 줄리아가 언니를 물어뜯지는 않으실 테고, 키 큰 하인들을 보고 놀랐던 것도 이제쯤은 가셨겠죠.”\n\n“그 집에 키 큰 하인이 있는지 없는지 나는 모르겠다만.”\n\n“그럼 키 작은 하인들이라고 하죠—무슨 말인지 아시잖아요. 그 온갖 귀족다운 것들 말이에요. 아무리 놀라지 않겠다고 단단히 마음먹어도 처음에는 사람을 놀라게 하고야 말 거예요, 틀림없이. 그저 경이라는 이유만으로 경을 무서워한다는 건 물론 아주 좀스러운 짓이죠. 그래도 저라면 처음에는 무서웠을 거예요. 그 집에 묵고 있었다면 드 게스트 경한테까지도요.”\n\n“그러면 네가 가지 않은 것이 잘된 일이구나.”\n\n“네, 저도 그렇게 생각해요. 벨 언니는 마음이 더 단단하니까 하루 이틀 지나면 그런 건 이겨 낼 거예요. 그런데 언니는 거기서 도대체 무얼 할까요? 그런 집에서도 양말을 깁기는 하는지 궁금해요.”\n\n“남들 보는 데서 깁지는 않겠지.”\n\n“아주 으리으리한 집에서는 그냥 곧바로 내다 버리겠죠. 저는 그런 걸 자주 생각해 봤어요. 총리대신도 구두를 구둣방에 보내신다고 보세요?”\n\n“제대로 된 구두장이라면 총리대신의 구두쯤은 몸을 낮추어 고쳐 드리기도 하겠지.”\n\n“그러면 고쳐 신기는 하신다고 보시는 거네요? 그런데 그건 누가 시키죠? 저처럼 그분도 조그만 구멍이 나기 시작하는 걸 직접 알아보실까요? 대주교는 한 해에 장갑을 몇 켤레까지 쓰기로 정해 두실까요?”\n\n“그리 엄격하게 정해 두지는 않을 게다.”\n\n“그러면 결국 이렇게 되겠네요. 필요할 때마다 새것을 한 켤레씩 갖는 거요. 그런데 필요하다는 건 무얼 두고 하는 말일까요? 이걸로 일요일을 한 번 더 나겠다고 스스로에게 말하는 일이 있을까요? 언젠가 주교님이 여기 오셨던 게 기억나는데, 엄지손가락 끝에 구멍이 나 있었어요. 저는 견진을 받게 되어 있었는데, 주교님이라면 좀 더 말끔하셔야 하는 것 아닌가 하고 생각했던 게 기억나요.”\n\n“왜 기워 드리겠다고 나서지 않았니?”\n\n“저는 무슨 일이 있어도 그럴 엄두를 못 냈을 거예요.”\n\n이야기는 데일 부인의 계획에 별 도움이 될 성싶지 않은 모양새로 시작되어 버렸다. 릴리는 어떤 이야깃거리에 한번 올라타면 좀처럼 거기서 내려오려 하지 않았고, 마음이 한쪽으로 꼬이면 그것을 다시 풀기가 어려웠다. 그녀는 이제 우리네 가운데 높고 힘 있는 이들의 자잘한 생활 습관을 따져 보는 데 골몰해서, 왕실 아이들도 주머니에 반 페니짜리 동전을 넣고 다닐까요, 아니면 기껏 내려가 봐야 사 펜스짜리 은화까지일까요 하고 어머니에게 묻고 있었다.\n\n“다른 아이들처럼 주머니야 있겠죠.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n그러나 어머니가 갑자기 그녀를 멈춰 세웠다—\n\n“릴리야, 존 임스 이야기를 좀 하고 싶구나.”\n\n“엄마, 저는 지금 당장은 차라리 왕실 이야기를 하겠어요.”\n\n“하지만 얘야, 내가 굽히지 않더라도 용서해 다오. 나는 이 일을 두고 많이 생각해 보았고, 내가 내 의무라고 여기는 일을 하는데 네가 나를 막지는 않으리라고 믿는다.”\n\n“네, 엄마. 막지 않을게요, 물론이죠.”\n\n“크로즈비 씨의 처사가 네게 알려진 뒤로, 나는 네가 듣는 데서 그 이름을 아주 드물게만 입에 올렸다.”\n\n“네, 엄마, 그러셨어요. 그리고 저는 엄마의 그 착하신 마음 때문에 엄마를 그토록 애틋하게 사랑했어요. 제가 엄마의 너그러움을 알아듣지도 알아보지도 못했다고는 생각하지 마세요. 저는 그걸 다 알고 있었고, 제 삶의 하루하루 그것을 생각했고, 말없이 저를 믿어 주신 것을 마음속으로 엄마께 감사드렸어요. 물론 저는 엄마의 마음을 알아요. 엄마는 그이를 나쁜 사람이라고 여기시고, 그이가 한 짓 때문에 그이를 미워하시죠.”\n\n“나는 누구든 기꺼이 미워하고 싶지는 않다, 릴리야.”\n\n“아, 하지만 엄마는 그이를 미워하세요. 제가 엄마였다면 저도 그이를 미워했을 거예요. 하지만 저는 엄마가 아니고, 저는 그이를 사랑해요. 저는 밤마다 아침마다 그이의 행복을 위해, 그리고 그 여자의 행복을 위해 기도해요. 저는 그이를 온전히 용서했고, 그이가 옳게 했다고 생각해요. 제가 나이가 들어서 그래도 잘못이 되지 않을 만해지면, 저는 그이에게 찾아가 그렇게 말해 줄 거예요. 될 수만 있다면 그이가 하는 모든 일과 그이가 거두는 모든 성공을 다 전해 들었으면 좋겠어요. 그러니 엄마와 제가 어떻게 그이 이야기를 할 수 있겠어요? 그건 될 수 없는 일이에요. 엄마도 말이 없으셨고 저도 말이 없었어요—그냥 우리 말없이 있어요.”\n\n“내가 이야기하려는 건 크로즈비 씨에 대한 게 아니다. 하지만 그 사람이 한 것 같은 처사는 온 세상이 나무랄 일이라는 것쯤은 네가 알아 두어야 한다고 나는 생각한다. 네가 그를 용서하는 건 좋으나, 그래도 너는 인정해야—”\n\n“엄마, 저는 아무것도 인정하고 싶지 않아요—그이에 대해서는요. 사람이 따져 볼 수 없는 일이라는 게 있잖아요.” 데일 부인은 지금 이 일이야말로 자기가 따져 볼 수 없는 일이라고 느꼈다. “물론 엄마,” 릴리가 말을 이었다. “저는 어떤 일에서도 엄마를 거스르고 싶지 않아요. 하지만 이 이야기는 우리가 하지 않는 편이 낫겠어요.”\n\n“물론 나는 네 앞날의 행복만을 생각하고 있는 게다.”\n\n“저도 알아요. 하지만 걱정하실 것 없다는 걸 부디 믿어 주세요. 저는 불행해질 생각이 없어요. 아니, 저는 불행하지 않다고 말해도 될 것 같아요. 물론 저도 불행했었죠—아주 많이 불행했어요. 제 마음이 부서질 거라고 정말로 생각했어요. 하지만 그건 다 지나갔고, 저는 이웃 사람들만큼은 행복할 수 있다고 믿어요. 우리가 어렸을 때 엄마가 늘 말씀하셨듯이, 사람은 누구나 저마다 겪을 괴로움이 있는 법이잖아요.”\n\n데일 부인은 자기가 시작을 잘못했다고, 크로즈비의 이름을 아예 입에 올리지 않았더라면 더 잘 나아갈 수 있었으리라고 느꼈다. 그녀는 제가 하고 싶은 말이 무엇인지, 딸 앞에 펼쳐 보이고 싶은 논거가 무엇인지 똑똑히 알고 있었다. 그러나 어떤 말을 써야 할지, 제 생각을 어떻게 말로 옮겨 놓는 것이 가장 나을지는 알지 못했다. 그녀가 한동안 말을 멈추자 릴리는 이야기가 끝난 것처럼 다시 제 바느질을 계속했다. 그러나 이야기는 끝난 것이 아니었다.\n\n“내가 네게 말하고 싶었던 건 존 임스에 대한 것이지 크로즈비 씨에 대한 것이 아니란다.”\n\n“아, 엄마!”\n\n“얘야, 내가 내 의무라고 여기는 일을 하는데 나를 막아서는 안 된다. 나는 그 아이가 네게 한 말과 네가 대답한 말을 다 들었고, 그러니 이 일로 마음이 가득할 수밖에 없지 않으냐. 어찌하여 너는 그토록 요지부동으로 그 아이를 밀어내는 게냐?”\n\n“다른 사람을 사랑하니까요.” 이 말을 그녀는 소리 내어, 흔들림 없이, 거의 고집스러운 어조로, 얼마쯤 대담한 태도로 내뱉었다—그런 말을 입에 올리는 것이 온당치 못하다는 것을 알면서도, 온당치 못할지언정 그 말은 해야만 한다고 마음을 다진 사람처럼.\n\n“하지만 릴리야, 그런 사랑은 그 성질로 보아 그치게 마련이다. 아니, 그보다도 그런 사랑은 네가 그 사람의 아내가 되리라 여기던 때에 느끼던 사랑과 같은 것이 아니란다.”\n\n“같은 거예요. 그 여자가 죽고 그이가 오 년 뒤에 저에게 온다 해도, 저는 그이를 받아들일 거예요. 저는 그이를 받아들이지 않을 수 없다고 여길 거예요.”\n\n“하지만 그 여자는 죽지도 않았고 죽을 성싶지도 않다.”\n\n“그건 아무 상관 없어요. 엄마는 저를 이해하지 못하세요.”\n\n“나는 이해한다고 생각한다. 그리고 너도 나를 이해해 주었으면 한다. 네 처지가 얼마나 어려운지 나도 알고, 네 마음이 어떤지도 안다. 하지만 나는 이것도 안다. 네가 스스로 이치를 따져 보고, 때가 되어 존 임스를 소중한 벗으로 받아들일 수만 있다면—”\n\n“저는 그이를 소중한 벗으로 받아들였는걸요. 왜 아니겠어요? 그이는 소중한 벗이에요. 저는 그이를 진심으로 사랑해요—엄마가 그러시는 것처럼요.”\n\n“내 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알잖니?”\n\n“네, 알아요. 그리고 저는 엄마께 그건 있을 수 없는 일이라고 말씀드리는 거예요.”\n\n“네가 애를 써 보기만 한다면, 이 모든 괴로움도 머지않아 다 잊힐 게다. 네가 한 번만 그 아이를 네 남편이 될 수도 있는 벗으로 여길 수 있게 된다면, 이 모든 것이 달라질 테고—나는 네가 행복해지는 것을 보게 될 텐데!”\n\n“엄마는 이상하게도 저를 떼어 놓지 못해 안달이시네요!”\n\n“그래, 릴리야—그런 식으로 너를 떼어 놓고 싶구나. 네가 그 아이의 약혼한 아내로서 그 아이의 손에 네 손을 얹는 것을 볼 수만 있다면, 나는 이 세상에서 가장 행복한 여자가 될 것 같다.”\n\n“엄마, 저는 그런 식으로는 엄마를 행복하게 해 드릴 수 없어요. 엄마가 제 마음을 정말로 아신다면, 엄마가 말씀하시는 대로 하는 것이 엄마를 몹시 불행하게 만들 거예요. 저는 큰 죄를 짓게 될 테니까요—여자라면 다른 어떤 죄보다도 더 조심해야 할 죄를요. 제 마음속에서 저는 그 다른 사람과 혼인한 몸이에요. 저는 저를 그이에게 내주었고, 그이를 사랑했고, 그이의 사랑을 기뻐했어요. 그이가 저에게 입 맞추면 저도 그이에게 입 맞추었고, 저는 그이의 입맞춤을 애타게 기다렸어요. 저는 그이가 저를 어루만져 주라고 살아 있는 것만 같았어요. 그러는 동안 내내 저는 제가 잘못하고 있다고 느껴 본 적이 없어요—그이가 저에게 전부였으니까요. 저는 그이의 것이었어요. 그것이 달라졌지요—저에게는 크나큰 불행이지만요. 하지만 그것을 없던 일로 돌리거나 잊어버릴 수는 없어요. 저는 그이가 여기 오기 전의 그 계집아이로 돌아갈 수 없어요. 애초에 없었던 일인 양 파묻혀 눈앞에서 치워지기를 마다하는 것들이 있는 법이에요. 저도 엄마와 같아요, 엄마—과부예요. 하지만 엄마에게는 딸이 있고, 저에게는 어머니가 있잖아요. 엄마가 그것으로 만족하시겠다면 저도 만족할게요.” 그러고는 그녀가 일어나 어머니의 목을 끌어안았다.\n\n이제 데일 부인의 논변은 끝났다. 릴리가 마지막으로 내놓은 그런 호소 앞에서 그녀 쪽이 무슨 대꾸를 할 수 있겠는가. 그 뒤로 그녀는 잠자코 있을 수밖에 없다고 스스로에게 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 세월이 흐르면 달라지는 것이 있을지 몰라도 어떤 논리로도 소용이 없을 터였다. 그녀는 딸을 끌어안고 그 위로 눈물을 흘렸다—릴리의 눈은 말라 있었건만. “네 뜻대로 하마.” 데일 부인이 나직이 말했다.\n\n“네, 제 뜻대로요. 제 마음대로 하겠어요. 그래도 되죠? 제가 바라는 건 그것뿐이에요. 엄마 위에 군림하는 폭군이 되어서, 얌전한 어머니답게 무슨 일이든 제가 시키는 대로 하시게 만드는 거요. 그래도 저는 그리 매섭게 시키지는 않을게요. 엄마가 말만 잘 들으시면 저는 아주 자비로울 거예요! 저기 홉킨스가 또 왔네요. 우리를 또 한바탕 연설로 때려눕히고 짓밟으러 온 걸까요.”\n\n홉킨스는 어느 창으로 와야 하는지 잘 알고 있었으니, 지금 이 집에서 사람이 들 만한 방은 하나뿐이었기 때문이다. 그는 식당방으로 올라와 유리창에 코를 거의 납작하게 눌러 붙였다.\n\n“자, 홉킨스,” 릴리가 말했다. “우리 여기 있어요.” 데일 부인은 얼굴을 돌려 버렸으니, 뺨에 아직 눈물이 남아 있는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n“예, 아가씨, 보입니다요. 마님께 여쭐 말씀이 있어서 왔습니다요, 아가씨.”\n\n“돌아서 오세요.” 어머니가 당장 얼굴을 내보이지 않아도 되도록 해 주고 싶어서 릴리가 말했다. “창을 열기에는 너무 추워요. 돌아서 오면 제가 문을 열어 줄게요.”\n\n“너무 춥다니!” 홉킨스가 가면서 중얼거렸다. “게스트윅 셋집에서는 훨씬 더 추운 맛을 보시게 될 텐데.” 그러면서도 그는 부엌 쪽으로 돌아서 왔고, 릴리가 현관에서 그를 맞았다.\n\n“자, 홉킨스, 무슨 일이에요? 엄마가 머리가 아프시대요.”\n\n“머리가 아프시다고요? 제가 마님 머리를 더 아프게 해 드리지는 않겠습니다요. 제 소견으로는 머리 아픈 데에는 신선한 바람만 한 것이 없습니다요. 다만 어떤 분들은 단 일 분도 바람 쐬는 걸 못 견디시지요. 온실 유리창을 날마다 얼마쯤이라도 내려 주지 않으면 화초라고는 하나도 못 얻습니다요—절대로요. 포도도 꼭 그와 같습지요. 제가 돌아가서 마님을 뵙지 못했다고 여쭐깝쇼?”\n\n“들어오고 싶으면 들어와도 돼요. 다만 조용히 해야 해요, 알죠.”\n\n“제가 늘 조용하지 않습니까요, 아가씨? 제가 난동 부리는 걸 누가 들어 본 적이 있습니까요? 실례합니다요, 마님, 나리께서 돌아오셨습니다요.”\n\n“뭐라고요, 게스트윅에서 돌아오셨어요? 벨 아가씨도 데려오셨대요?”\n\n“나리 혼자만 오셨습니다요, 말을 타고 오셨거든입쇼. 그리고 제 짐작으로는 거의 곧바로 도로 가실 것 같습니다요. 그런데 나리께서 마님더러 좀 와 주십사 하십니다요, 데일 마님.”\n\n“아, 그래, 곧 가마.”\n\n“꼭 안부 전해 드리라고 하셨습니다요. 그게 무슨 차이가 있는지야 저는 모릅니다만요.”\n\n“어쨌든 가마, 홉킨스.”\n\n“그러면 머리 아프시다는 말씀은 안 드려도 됩니까요?”\n\n“무슨 말이냐?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아녜요, 아녜요, 아녜요.” 릴리가 말했다. “엄마가 곧 그리로 가실 거예요. 가서 우리 삼촌께 그리 전해 줘요, 착하기도 하지.” 그러면서 그녀는 손을 들어 그를 방 밖으로 밀어냈다.\n\n“머리가 아프기는 무슨, 하나도 안 아프시구먼.” 홉킨스는 뒷마당을 지나 돌아가면서 투덜거렸다. “지체 높은 양반님네들은 어쩌면 저리 거짓말을 잘도 하실까! 내가 밖에 나가 일감들 사이에 있어야 할 판에 머리가 아프다고 했다가는 나한테 무슨 말씀을 하실까? 그런데 가난한 사람은 절대로 거짓말도 못 하지, 술도 못 마시지, 아무것도 못 하지.” 그러고는 제 전할 말을 들고 돌아갔다.\n\n“네 삼촌이 무슨 일로 집에 돌아오셨을까?” 데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그저 가축들이 어떤지 보시고, 돼지들이 다 죽지는 않았는지 살피시려는 거죠. 저는 삼촌이 도대체 어떻게 떠나기는 하셨는지가 더 신기해요.”\n\n“당장 올라가 봐야겠다.”\n\n“네, 그럼요.”\n\n“그런데 집 이야기는 뭐라고 해야 하니?”\n\n“그 일 때문은 아니에요—적어도 저는 아니라고 생각해요. 엄마가 먼저 꺼내시기 전에는 삼촌도 그 이야기를 다시 하지 않으실 것 같아요.”\n\n“그래도 하시면?”\n\n“섭리에 맡기셔야죠. 아까 제가 홉킨스한테 한 것처럼 머리가 몹시 아프다고 하세요. 그저 엄마가 알아듣지 못하시고 저를 저버리셨을 뿐이지만요. 다리까지는 제가 같이 걸어 드릴게요.” 그리하여 두 사람은 함께 잔디밭을 가로질러 갔다.\n\n그러나 릴리는 곧 혼자가 되었고, 어머니가 돌아오기를 기다리며 그대로 산책을 이어 갔다. 자갈길을 빙빙 돌면서 그녀는 어머니에게 했던 말들을 생각했다. 그녀는 자기도 과부라고 선언한 것이었다. “그리고 마땅히 그래야 해.” 그녀는 그 일을 스스로와 따져 보며 말했다. “형편이나 편의나 안락함이 요구하는 대로 이리저리 옮겨 갈 수 있는 것이라면, 마음이란 게 대체 무슨 값어치가 있담? 그이가 나를 여기서 이 팔에 안았을 때”—그리고 이런저런 생각이 머릿속을 스쳐 갈 때 그녀는 두 사람이 서 있던 바로 그 자리를 떠올렸다—“오, 내 사랑!” 그때 그녀는 그의 입맞춤에 답하며 그에게 그렇게 말했었다—“오, 내 사랑, 내 사랑, 내 사랑!” “그이가 나를 여기서 이 팔에 안았을 때, 나는 그이가 내 남편이니 이것이 옳은 일이라고 스스로에게 말했지. 그이는 변했지만 나는 변하지 않았어. 내가 그이를 사랑하지 않게 되었을 수도 있고, 그랬다면 나도 그이에게 그렇게 말했겠지. 나도 그이가 한 대로 했을 거야.” 그러나 여기에 이르자 그녀는 레이디 알렉산드리나를 떠올리며 몸서리를 쳤다. “너무 빨랐어.” 그녀는 여전히 혼잣말로 말했다. “너무, 너무나. 하지만 사내들은 여자와 같지 않으니까.” 그러고는 자기가 어디에 있는지도 거의 잊은 채 열심히 걸음을 옮기며, 날마다 그러하듯 그 모든 것을 되짚어 보았다. 크로즈비를 제 남편이자 주인으로 여기게 되었던 저 파란 많던 몇 달의 자잘한 생각과 말을 하나하나 되새기면서. 그녀는 제 불행을 이겨 냈다고 선언했으나, 비참함에 거의 미칠 것 같은 순간들이 있었다. “나더러 그이를 잊으라고!” 그녀가 말했다. “그것이야말로 영영 잊히지 않을 단 하나의 일인데.”\n\n마침내 그녀는 어머니의 발소리가 지주의 정원을 가로질러 내려오는 것을 듣고 다리 위에 제 자리를 잡았다.\n\n“서라, 가진 걸 내놓아라.” 어머니가 판자다리에 발을 올려놓았을 때 그녀가 말했다. “아니, 내놓을 만한 게 있다면 말이지만요. 뭐가 정해졌어요?”\n\n“집으로 올라가자.” 데일 부인이 말했다. “가서 다 얘기해 주마.”\n\n# 제58장. 작은 집의 운명\n\n데일 부인이 딸에게 집으로 올라오라고 이르며 자기가 가져온 소식 보따리는 거기서 풀겠노라고 말했을 때, 그 목소리의 어조에는 릴리가 짐짓 꾸며 보이던 농기를 대번에 잠재우는 무엇인가가 있었다. 어머니는 꼬박 두 시간을 나가 있었고, 그동안 릴리는 여전히 정원을 빙빙 돌며 걷다가 마침내는 어머니의 발소리를 애타게 기다리게 되었다. 그 긴 두 시간 동안 삼촌과 어머니 사이에 무언가 심상찮은 이야기가 오갔음이 틀림없었다. 데일 부인이 이따금 큰 집으로 불려 가 갖는 면담은 보통 이십 분을 넘지 않았고, 그 결말은 정원을 한두 바퀴 도는 사이에 딸들에게 전해지곤 했다. 그러나 이번만은 데일 부인이 집 안에 들어가 자리를 잡기 전에는 한사코 입을 열려 하지 않았다.\n\n“삼촌이 엄마를 보러 일부러 오신 거예요?”\n\n“그래, 얘야, 그런 것 같더구나. 너도 보고 싶어 하셨단다. 하지만 내가 너와 이야기를 나눈 뒤로 미뤄 달라고 청했지.”\n\n“저를요, 엄마? 무슨 일로요?”\n\n“너에게 입 맞추고 자기를 사랑해 달라 하시려는 거란다. 오직 그것뿐이야. 너를 언짢게 할 말은 한마디도 없으시다.”\n\n“그럼 저도 삼촌께 입 맞추고 사랑해 드릴래요.”\n\n“그래, 내 이야기를 다 듣고 나면 그러고 싶어질 게다. 나는 게스트윅으로 갈 생각을 아주 접겠노라고 그분께 엄숙히 약속드렸다. 그러니 그 일은 끝난 거야.”\n\n“어머, 어머! 그럼 우리 당장 짐을 풀어도 되는 거예요? 사람 일생에 이런 막간극이 다 있네!”\n\n“짐이야 물론 풀어도 되지. 내가 그분께 다짐을 두었으니까. 그리고 그분이 몸소 게스트윅에 들어가서 셋집 일을 정리하실 거란다.”\n\n“홉킨스는 알아요?”\n\n“아직은 모를 게다.”\n\n“보이스 부인도 모르겠네요! 엄마, 저는 이 다음 한 주를 어떻게 견딜지 모르겠어요. 우리 꼴이 얼마나 우스울까요! 우리 이렇게 해요. 그게 제가 누릴 수 있는 유일한 위안일 테니까요. 벨 언니가 집에 돌아오기 전에 얼른 손을 놀려 죄다 제자리에 돌려놓는 거예요. 언니를 깜짝 놀라게 해 주게요.”\n\n“뭐라고! 이틀 만에?”\n\n“왜 못 해요? 홉킨스를 불러다 거들게 할 거예요. 그러면 그이도 그렇게 심통을 부리진 않겠죠. 당장 시작해서 담요랑 침대부터 손댈래요. 그건 저 혼자서도 풀 수 있으니까요.”\n\n“하지만 아직 절반도 이야기하지 않았단다. 게다가 나도 우리 사이에 오간 이야기를 너에게 어떻게 알아듣게 전해야 할지 모르겠구나. 그분은 버나드 일로 몹시 상심해 계셔. 버나드가 외국으로 나가기로 마음을 굳혔고, 몇 해씩 떠나 있게 될지도 모른다는구나.”\n\n“제 직분을 좇아가겠다는 사람을 나무랄 수야 없죠.”\n\n“나무라시는 건 아니었어. 다만 늘그막에 혼자 남겨지게 되었으니 자신에게 참 서글픈 일이라고만 하셨지. 이건 우리가 여기 남는 이야기가 나오기 전이었단다. 사실 그분은 그 일을 다시 청으로 꺼내지는 않겠다고 작정하신 듯했어. 눈빛에서 그게 보였고, 말투에서 알아차렸지. 이어서 너와 벨 이야기를 하시면서, 둘 다 얼마나 사랑하는지 모른다고, 다만 안타깝게도 너희에 대해 품었던 바람이 이루어지지 못했다고 하셨다.”\n\n“아, 하지만 애초에 그런 바람은 품지 말아야 하셨죠.”\n\n“들어 보렴, 얘야. 다 듣고 나면 너도 그분께 화가 나지는 않을 게다. 그분은 이 집이 너희에게 한 번도 즐거운 곳이 되어 주지 못했다는 걸 느끼신다고 하셨어. 그다음에는, 기억이 난다 한들 옮길 수 없는 말들이 이어졌지. 우리 사이가 좀 더 다정하지 못했던 것을 애석해하시며 나에 대해서도 많은 말씀을 하셨다. ‘하지만 내 마음은,’ 하고 그분이 말씀하시더구나. ‘언제나 내 말보다는 따뜻했소.’ 그래서 나는 앉아 있던 자리에서 일어나 그분께 다가가서, 우리가 여기 남겠노라고 말씀드렸다.”\n\n“그랬더니 뭐라고 하셨어요?”\n\n“뭐라고 하셨는지 모르겠구나. 내가 울고 있었다는 것, 그리고 그분이 내게 입을 맞추셨다는 것만 알아. 그분 평생에 처음 있는 일이었지. 그분이 기뻐하셨다는 것도 안다. 이루 말할 수 없이 기뻐하셨어. 조금 지나자 생기가 도셔서 이런저런 일을 해 주마 하고 말씀하시더구나. 네가 말하던 바로 그 페인트칠도 해 주겠다고 약속하셨다.”\n\n“아, 그럼요, 저도 알고 있었어요. 그리고 내일 저녁 식사 전에는 홉킨스가 완두를 들고 나타날 거고, 딩글스는 어깨가 파묻히도록 토끼를 짊어지고 올 거예요. 그리고 보이스 부인은요! 엄마, 삼촌은 보이스 부인 생각은 못 하셨나 봐요. 아니면 순전한 인정으로라도 그 서글픔을 그대로 간직하고 계셨을 텐데.”\n\n“그래, 그 부인 생각은 못 하셨지. 내가 자리를 뜰 때 그분은 조금도 서글퍼 보이지 않으셨으니까. 하지만 아직 절반도 이야기하지 않았다니까.”\n\n“어머나, 엄마. 그것 말고 또 있었어요?”\n\n“게다가 순서도 다 뒤죽박죽으로 말해 버렸구나. 지금 하려는 이야기는 집 문제가 나오기 전에 오간 것이니까 말이다. 버나드 이야기를 하시고 바로 이어서 꺼내셨어. 버나드가 당연히 자기 상속인이 될 거라고 하시더구나.”\n\n“당연히 그렇겠죠.”\n\n“그리고 너희 아가씨들 몫으로 재산에 부담을 지우는 것은 옳지 않다고 여기신다고 하셨다.”\n\n“엄마, 세상에 어떻게 그런—”\n\n“그만, 릴리, 그만하렴. 그리고 될 수 있으면 그분께 마음을 좀 더 너그럽게 가져 보렴.”\n\n“너그러워요. 다만 저는 돈을 탐낸 적도 없는데 내 몫으로 큰돈이 오지 않을 거라는 말을 듣는 게 싫을 뿐이에요. 저는 버나드의 남종이든 여종이든, 그의 소든 나귀든, 그에게 속한 그 무엇이든 부러워한 적이 없어요. 솔직히 말하면 그게 벨 언니 몫이 되는 것조차 바라지 않았어요. 언니한테는 버나드보다 훨씬 더 좋아할 만한 사람이 따로 있다는 걸 저는 잘 알고 있었으니까요.”\n\n“나는 도무지 내 이야기를 끝까지 못 하겠구나.”\n\n“아니에요, 엄마. 끈기 있게 하시면 되죠.”\n\n“요컨대 이런 이야기다. 그분이 벨에게 삼천 파운드를 주셨고, 너에게도 삼천 파운드를 주셨어.”\n\n“그런데 왜 저한테요, 엄마?” 릴리가 말했다. 말하는 동안 두 뺨이 붉게 물들었다. 존 임스에 대해서는 될 수 있는 대로 더는 아무 말도 없어야 했다. 그러나 말을 해야 할 필요가 있든 없든, 어쨌든 오해만은 없게 해 두어야 했다. “그런데 왜 저한테요, 엄마?”\n\n“그분이 내게 설명하시기를, 너희 둘에게 똑같이 해 주는 것이 옳다고 여기신다는구나. 그 돈은 지금 이 순간부터 네 것이란다. 네가 좋다면 머리핀을 사도 되고. 나는 그분이 그만큼 큰 돈을 마음대로 하실 수 있는 줄은 몰랐다.”\n\n“삼천 파운드라니! 마지막으로 저한테 주신 돈은 반 크라운이었고, 저는 삼촌이 어찌나 인색하신가 했는걸요. 그때 저는 특히 십 실링이 필요했거든요. 지금 같아서는 차라리 산뜻한 새 오 파운드짜리 지폐 한 장을 주셨더라면 훨씬 더 좋았을 텐데.”\n\n“그렇게 말씀드려 보렴.”\n\n“안 돼요. 그러면 그것까지 주실 테니까요. 하지만 오 파운드라면 제 마음대로 써도 된다는 기분이 들 거예요. 화장 상자도 사고, 다람쥐가 안에서 돌리며 뛰노는 쳇바퀴도 사고요. 그렇지만 아무도 여자애한테 그렇게 즐길 수 있는 돈은 주지 않죠.”\n\n“저런, 릴리. 이 배은망덕한 것 같으니!”\n\n“아니에요, 저는 아니라고 우길래요. 저는 배은망덕하지 않아요. 저는 아주 고마워하고 있어요. 삼촌 마음이 누그러지셨으니까요. 그리고 우시면서 엄마께 입을 맞추셨으니까요. 저 앞으로 삼촌께 얼마나 잘해 드릴 건데요! 하지만 삼천 파운드를 주신 걸 두고 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지는 도무지 모르겠어요. 그건 아예 제 삶의 테두리 밖에 있는 일이거든요. 다른 세상에 가서야 제게 올 무엇처럼 들리는데, 저는 그걸 아직은 그리 받고 싶지 않아요. 고맙기는 해요. 그런데 안개가 낀 듯 아리송한 고마움이에요. 이 돈 덕분에 제가 언제쯤 새 발모럴 부츠를 한 켤레 얻게 될지 엄마가 말해 주실 수 있어요? 그것만 손에 잡히게 알 수 있다면 제 고마움도 활기를 띨 것 같은데요.”\n\n지주는 게스트윅으로 말을 몰아 돌아가면서, 데일 부인이 묘사했던 그 생기에서 다시 본래의 침울한 기분으로 가라앉았다. 그는 지나온 삶을 오래 생각하며, 자기 마음은 언제나 자기 말보다 따뜻했노라고 제수에게 했던 그 말이 참이라고 스스로에게 다짐했다. 그러나 세상은, 그리고 세상에서 그와 가장 가까운 이들은 모두 늘 그의 마음이 아니라 그의 말로 그를 판단해 왔다. 그들은 그가 마음대로 하거나 바꿀 수 없는 겉모습을 취했을 뿐, 그가 주인이었던 사실은 취하지 않았다. 그는 제 친척 모두에게 잘해 주지 않았던가. 그런데도 그중에 그를 아끼는 이가 하나라도 있었던가? “그들이 남기로 한 것이 차라리 안된 일이지 싶다.” 그는 혼잣말을 했다. “내가 그들을 실망시킬 게 뻔하니까.” 그러면서도 저택에서 벨을 만나자 그는 쾌활하게 말을 걸었고, 게스트윅으로 옮겨 가는 일은 이루어지지 않게 되었노라고 자못 흡족한 기색으로 알렸다.\n\n“정말 기뻐요.” 그녀가 말했다. “저는 오래전부터 그러기를 바랐는걸요.”\n\n“네 어머니도 이제는 그걸 바라지 않는 것 같더구나.”\n\n“당연히 안 바라시죠. 처음부터 다 오해였어요. 저희 가운데 누가 삼촌이 바라시는 대로 다 해 드리지 못하니까, 저희는 그게 낫겠다고 생각했던—” 여기서 벨은 말을 멈췄다. 계속하다가는 곤란한 지경에 빠지겠다 싶었던 것이다.\n\n“그 이야기는 더 하지 말자꾸나.” 지주가 말했다. “그 일은 끝났고, 이렇게 좋게 매듭지어져서 나는 아주 기쁘다. 어제 크로프츠 선생과 이야기를 나눴다.”\n\n“그러셨어요, 삼촌?”\n\n“그래. 혼례 전날에는 그 사람이 와서 나와 함께 묵기로 했다. 다 정해 두었지. 그리고 피로연은 큰 집에서 차리기로 하자. 다만 날짜는 네가 정해야 한다. 나로서는 오월 어느 날쯤이 좋겠구나. 그리고 얘야, 너도 곱게 차려입고 싶을 테지. 여기 얼마간 돈이 있다. 알겠지만 그건 혼례 전에 쓰라고 주는 거다.” 그러고서 그는 어기적어기적 걸어 나갔고, 혼자가 되자마자 다시 서글프고 낙담한 사람이 되었다. 그는 우리가 그 생의 마지막 장에 이르기까지 얼마간의 잔잔한 서글픔과 끊이지 않는 낙담을 미리 점쳐 두어도 좋을 그런 사람이었다.\n\n우리는 존 임스를, 개울을 가로지른 난간에서 릴리의 이름을 지우고 있던 참에 그를 붙든 레이디 줄리아의 손에 맡겨 둔 채 떠나왔었다. 그는 게스트윅에 있는 어머니 집으로 달아났다가 거기서 다시 런던으로 가 버릴 궁리를 하던 중이었다. 저택에는 두 번 다시 얼굴을 내밀지 않을 작정이었던 것이다. 그러나 레이디 줄리아의 발소리를 듣고 그 모습이 코앞에 다가온 것을 보는 순간, 그는 퇴로가 끊겼음을 알았다. 그래서 그는 얌전히 이끌려 집으로 올라갔다. 레이디 줄리아에게만은 그도 이 일을 통째로 털어놓았다. 자기 희망은 끝났고, 행복은 사라졌으며, 가슴은 반쯤 부서졌노라고. 성공했을 경우라면 그녀의 축하 따위에는 별 마음을 두지 않았을지 모르나, 위로와 동정이라면 그는 백작의 집에 머무는 어느 누구에게서보다 그녀에게서 받는 편이 더 견딜 만했다. “오라버님께 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 모르겠습니다.” 그녀가 들어가려던 옆문에 다가가면서 그가 그녀에게 속삭였다.\n\n“제가 오라버니께 말씀드려 둘까요? 그러고 나면 물론 오라버니가 몇 마디 하시겠지만, 두려워하실 것 없어요.”\n\n“그러면 데일 어르신은요?” 조니가 말했다. “다들 그 이야기를 들었을 텐데요. 다들 제가 얼마나 바보짓을 했는지 알게 되겠지요.” 그녀는 백작이 지주에게 말하게 하면 되겠다고 이르고, 아무도 그를 조금이라도 어리석다 여기지 않을 거라고 다짐해 준 뒤, 그를 놓아주어 제 침실로 올라가게 했다. 거기서 그는 크래들에게서 온 편지를 발견했는데, 그가 없는 사이에 배달된 것이었다. 그러나 그 편지의 내용은 다음 장으로 미루는 편이 낫겠다. 그것은 그에게 위안을 줄 만한 것도, 그의 슬픔을 더할 만한 것도 아니었다.\n\n저녁 식사 한 시간쯤 전에 그의 방문을 두드리는 소리가 났고, 들어오라는 말에 백작이 몸소 방 안에 모습을 나타냈다. 그는 평소의 농장 일 차림 그대로였는데, 집으로 다가서던 첫걸음에 레이디 줄리아에게 붙들려, 마음씨 고운 누이에게서 무슨 말을 해야 하는지 단단히 훈련을 받은 뒤 곧장 젊은 벗에게로 온 것이었다. 그러나 나로서는 그가 그 자리에서 한 말이 죄다 누이의 가르침을 고스란히 따른 것이었다고 잘라 말할 준비는 되어 있지 않다.\n\n“그래, 여보게.” 그가 입을 열었다. “그 아가씨가 고집을 부렸다지.”\n\n“예, 백작님. 그러니까, 고집을 부린 건지는 저도 모르겠습니다. 다 끝난 일입니다.”\n\n“그거야 두고 볼 일이지, 조니. 내가 아는 한, 처음 청혼을 받고 그 자리에서 받아들이는 여자는 절반도 되지 않네.”\n\n“저는 다시 청하지 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“아니지, 자네는 다시 청할 걸세. 거절당했다고 그 아가씨에게 화가 났다는 말은 아니겠지.”\n\n“조금도 아닙니다. 화를 낼 권리도 없고요. 저는 그저 이런 바보였던 제 자신에게 화가 날 뿐입니다, 드 게스트 경. 이런 볼일로 여기 내려오기 전에 차라리 죽어 버렸더라면 좋았을 걸 그랬습니다. 이제 와 생각해 보니, 일이 어떻게 될지 진작 알아차렸어야 마땅한 일들이 그렇게나 많았는데 말입니다.”\n\n“나는 조금도 그렇게 보지 않네. 자네 다시 내려오게. 어디 보자, 지금이 오월이지. 구월에 사냥철이 시작되면 오는 걸로 하세. 다른 수로 휴가를 얻어 내지 못하겠거든 늙은 버플까지 함께 오라고 하지 뭐. 다만, 이런, 그자는 우리를 모조리 쏘아 버릴 것 같기는 하네만. 그래도 상관없어. 그건 우리가 어떻게든 해 보겠네. 자네는 구월까지 기운을 잃지 말고 있게. 그러면 그때 우리가 다른 방식으로 싸움을 붙여 보는 거야. 지주가 신부를 위해 조촐한 모임을 열 테고, 우리 릴리 아가씨도 그때는 가야 할 게 아닌가. 자네는 거기서 그 아가씨를 만나는 거지. 그러고 나서 우리는 지주네 땅에서 사냥을 하는 거고. 그렇게 해서 자네들 둘을 붙여 놓겠네. 우리가 못 하나 두고 보게. 원, 세상에! 한 번 거절당했다고! 내 믿기로는, 요즘 여자애들은 한 사내를 여섯 번은 거절해 보고서야 비로소 그 사내를 마음에 두는 법일세.”\n\n“릴리는 조금도 그런 사람이 아닌 것 같습니다.”\n\n“여보게, 조니. 나는 릴리 양을 나쁘게 말할 생각은 털끝만큼도 없네. 나는 그 아가씨를 무척 좋아하고, 내가 아는 아가씨들 가운데 가장 점잖은 축이라고 생각하네. 그 아가씨가 자네 아내가 되면, 허락만 한다면 나는 그 애를 끔찍이 아낄 걸세. 하지만 그 애도 다른 아가씨들과 같은 재료로 빚어졌고, 같은 식으로 행동할 걸세. 자네들 사이의 일이 조금 어긋났으니, 그것이 단박에 바로잡히지는 않아. 이제 그 애는 자네 마음이 어떤지 알았고, 그 일을 두고두고 생각하게 될 걸세. 그러다 마침내는 그 다른 사내보다 자네가 그 애의 생각 속에 더 크게 자리 잡게 되지. 그 애가 저 나이에 그런 거짓된 마음을 지닌 사내와 마주치는 불운을 겪었으니 노처녀가 되고 말 거라는 소리는 내게 하지 말게. 시간이 좀 걸릴 수는 있네. 하지만 자네가 밀고 나가면서 기죽지만 않는다면, 끝에 가서는 다 잘될 걸세. 누구나 원하는 것을 단박에 다 얻지는 못하는 법이야. 자네가 결혼하고 두어 해나 세 해쯤 지나면 내가 이 일로 자네를 얼마나 놀려 먹을지!”\n\n“저는 다시는 그 사람에게 청할 수 없을 것 같습니다. 그리고 설령 청한다 해도 대답은 똑같으리라는 확신이 듭니다. 그 사람이 저에게 아주 분명한 말로—. 아니, 그만두겠습니다. 그 사람의 말을 옮길 수는 없습니다.”\n\n“나는 자네가 그 말을 옮기기를 바라지도 않고, 그 말을 제 값어치 이상으로 새기기를 바라지도 않네. 릴리 데일은 아주 어여쁜 아가씨야. 영리하기도 하겠지, 내 생각에는. 착한 것도 틀림없고. 하지만 그 애의 말이 다른 남자나 여자의 말보다 더 거룩한 것은 아닐세. 지금 자네에게 한 말은 그 애의 진심이겠지, 그야 의심할 바 없어. 하지만 남자든 여자든 마음이란 바뀌기 쉬운 법이고, 더구나 그 바뀜이 제 행복에 도움이 될 때는 더욱 그렇다네.”\n\n“어쨌든 저는 백작님의 온정을 결코 잊지 않겠습니다, 드 게스트 경.”\n\n“그리고 자네에게 하고 싶은 말이 하나 더 있네, 조니. 사내라면 무슨 일로든 기가 꺾여서는 안 되네. 적어도 겉으로, 다른 사람들 눈에 드러나게는 말이야.”\n\n“하지만 그걸 어떻게 막습니까?”\n\n“배짱이 있으면 막아지는 법이지. 자네는 울부짖는 황소도 무서워하지 않았고, 철도역에서 그 사내를 두들겨 팰 때도 무서워하지 않았네. 그런 종류의 배짱은 자네에게 넉넉히 있어. 이제는 다른 종류의 배짱도 있다는 걸 보여 주어야 하네. 자네도 그 소년 이야기를 알겠지. 이리가 겉옷 밑에서 제 살을 물어뜯는데도 울지 않았다는 아이 말일세. 우리 대부분은 어딘가에 저를 물어뜯는 이리를 하나씩 두고 있네. 다만 대개는 옷 밑에서 물어뜯기니 세상이 보지 못할 뿐이야. 그러니 세상이 눈치채지 못하도록 견디는 것이 우리의 도리일세. 제 입으로 나는 비참하다고 떠벌리고 다니는 사내는 비참하기만 한 것이 아니라 경멸스럽기까지 하게 된다네.”\n\n“하지만 이리는 제 옷 밑에서 저를 물어뜯은 게 아닙니다. 다들 알고 있는걸요.”\n\n“그렇다면 아는 사람들에게, 자네가 그런 상처를 겉으로 우는소리 한마디 없이 견뎌 낼 수 있다는 것을 알게 하게. 나는 솔직히 말하네만, 청승맞은 연인에게는 동정이 가지 않아.”\n\n“저는 제가 모두에게 우스운 꼴이 되었다는 걸 압니다. 여기 오지나 말 것을. 백작님께서 저를 만나지나 않으셨더라면 좋았을 것을.”\n\n“그런 말은 말게, 이 사람아. 다만 내 충고를 그 값어치만큼은 받아 두게. 그리고 내가 하는 말을 새겨 두게. 자네의 슬픔에는 나도 마음을 같이하네만, 그것을 겉으로 드러내는 데는 그렇지 않아. 우울한 낯빛이며 가라앉은 목소리며 풀 죽은 걸음걸이 따위에는 말일세. 사내라면 언제나 제 술잔을 비우고 그것을 즐기는 것처럼 보일 줄 알아야 하네. 그러지 못한다면 그는 그럴 수 있었을 때보다 그만큼 덜 사내인 거야. 어떤 사람들이 생각하는 것처럼 그만큼 더 사내인 것이 아니라 말일세. 자, 이제 옷을 갈아입게, 여보게. 그리고 자네에게 아무 일도 없었던 것처럼 저녁 식사 자리로 내려오게.”\n\n백작이 나가자마자 존은 시계를 보았고, 저녁 식사까지 아직 사십 분쯤 남은 것을 알았다. 옷을 갈아입는 데는 십오 분이면 넉넉할 터이니, 안락의자에 앉아 이 모든 일을 곱씹어 볼 시간은 충분했다. 벗이 자기에게 청승맞은 연인에게는 동정이 가지 않는다고 말했을 때 그는 한순간 몹시 화가 났었다. 그것은 심술궂은 말이었다. 그는 그 말을 듣는 순간에도 그렇게 느꼈고, 그 의자에 앉아 있던 삼십 분 내내 계속 그렇게 여겼다. 그러나 그 말은 아마도 백작이 그에게 해 준 어떤 말보다도, 아니 그가 쓸 수 있었을 어떤 다른 말보다도 그에게 더 이로웠을 것이다. “청승맞다니! 나는 청승맞지 않아.” 그는 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다가 곧바로 다시 앉으며 혼잣말을 했다. “나는 그분께 아무 말도 하지 않았어. 내가 말씀드린 것도 아닌데. 어째서 나를 찾아오셨담?” 그러면서도, 그는 속으로 드 게스트 경을 욕하려 애쓰면서도, 드 게스트 경이 옳고 자기가 그르다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 자기가 청승맞았다는 것을 알았고 스스로가 부끄러웠다. 그리고 그 자리에서, 이제부터는 자기에게 아무런 재앙도 일어나지 않은 것처럼 처신하리라 결심했다. “그분 말씀대로 해서 취하도록 술이나 마셔 볼까 보다.” 그러고서 그는 노래를 불러 기운을 내 보려 했다.\n\n그 여인이 나를 위해 어여쁘지 않다면, 내가 무슨 상관이랴, 그 여인이 얼마나—\n\n“하지만 나는 상관이 있는걸. 사내가 시라는 걸 쓰면서 저런 거짓말을 집어넣다니, 무슨 헛소리람! 그자도 상관이 있었다는 건 누구나 아는 일이지. 그자가 무정한 짐승이 아니었다면 말이야.”\n\n그러나 그럼에도 응접실로 내려갈 시간이 되자 그는 벗이 일러 준 대로 애를 썼고, 백작과 레이디 줄리아가 예상했던 것보다는 풀 죽은 낯빛을 덜 하고 방으로 들어섰다. 두 사람 다 거기 있었고, 지주도 함께 있었으며, 벨이 채 일 분도 안 되어 뒤따라 들어왔다.\n\n“오늘 크로프츠를 보지 못했나, 존?” 백작이 말했다.\n\n“못 뵈었습니다. 저는 그분 길목 쪽으로는 어디도 가지 않았으니까요!”\n\n“그분 길목이라니! 내가 보기에 그 사람 길목은 사방 천지가 다 길목일세. 나는 그 사람더러 와서 저녁을 함께 들자고 했는데, 같은 집에서 이틀을 잇달아 저녁을 두 번 먹는 건 온당치 않다고 여기는 모양이더군. 그게 그 사람 지론 아니오, 데일 양?”\n\n“저는 정말 모르겠어요, 드 게스트 경. 어쨌든 제 지론은 아니고요.”\n\n그리하여 그들은 만찬 자리로 갔고, 마지막 남은 기회가 다 지나가기 전에 존 임스는 구운 양고기를 즐기는 시늉쯤은 해낼 수 있었다.\n\n지금 그가 겪고 있는 것과 같은 온갖 재앙에서는 사정이 어떠한지를 주위 사람들이 다 알고 있다는 확신 때문에 그 불운의 고통이 훨씬 커진다는 데에, 나는 의심의 여지가 없다고 본다. 마음이 몹시 따뜻하고 감정이 몹시 깊은 젊은 신사라도 제가 흠모하던 아가씨에게 거절당한 뒤에 훌륭한 만찬을 먹어 치울 수야 물론 있을 것이다. 다만 함께 먹는 이들 가운데 자기가 퇴짜 맞은 것을 아는 사람이 하나도 없다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 있어야 한다. 그러나 바로 그 따뜻하고 감정 깊은 젊은 신사도, 좌중이 모두 자기의 작은 불운을 낱낱이 알고 있음을 안다면, 참으로 식욕이 있는 듯한, 혹은 미식을 즐기는 듯한 낯빛으로 그 의식을 치러 내기가 몹시 어려울 것이다. 대개 그런 처지의 사내는, 우리가 짐작하기로, 저녁을 먹으러 제 클럽으로 가거나 가까운 리치먼드나 햄프턴 코트의 그늘에서 위안을 찾는 법이다. 거기서 그는 말없이 제 처지를 곱씹고, 끝내는 자그마한 뱅어 요리 한 접시와 커틀릿 한 조각과 적당한 양의 셰리 한 파인트를 즐기게 된다. 그는 아마 혼자 극장에 가서, 제 좌석에 앉아 세상의 헛됨을 다소 씁쓸한 비꼼으로 곱씹을 것이다. 그러고서 그는 슬프기는 하되 누그러진 슬픔을 안고 집으로 돌아오며, 열린 창가에서 여송연 연기를 뿜어내며, 팔꿈치 곁에는 아마 브랜디 물 탄 것을 한 잔 놓아 위안 삼으며, 스스로에게 맹세하기를 “아무렴, 한 번 더 부딪쳐 보겠어” 하는 것이다. 혼자 있을 때, 또는 아무것도 모르는 사람들 틈에 있을 때라면 사내는 스스로를 위로할 수 있다. 그러나 존 임스의 처지가 가혹했다는 것만은 인정해야 한다. 그는 릴리 데일에게 구애하라고 그곳에 초대받았고, 지주와 벨은 그 구애 자리에 함께 있어 달라는 청을 받았던 것이다. 만사가 잘 풀렸더라면 그보다 더 좋을 수는 없었으리라. 그는 그날의 주인공이 되었을 테고, 모두가 그를 위해 승리의 노래를 불러 주었으리라. 그러나 만사가 잘 풀리지 않았고, 그는 청승맞지 않게 처신하기가 몹시 어려웠다. 그러나 대체로 그의 애씀이 어지간했던지라 백작은 그의 몸가짐을 대견하게 여겼고, 밤에 헤어질 때 그에게 자네는 훌륭한 사내이며 언젠가는 모든 일이 다 잘될 거라고 말해 주었다.\n\n“그리고 내가 자네에게 모진 말을 했다고 화내지는 말게.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“조금도 화나지 않았습니다.”\n\n“아니, 자네는 화가 났어. 그리고 나는 오히려 자네가 화를 내기를 바랐네. 하지만 앙심을 품은 채 떠나서는 안 되네.”\n\n그는 하루를 더 저택에 머물렀고, 그런 뒤 소득세 사무국의 제 방으로, 래플 경의 작은 종이 내는 귀에 거슬리는 소리와 그보다 훨씬 더 귀에 거슬리는 래플 경의 큰 목소리로 돌아갔다.\n\n# 제59장. 존 임스가 어른이 되다\n\n임스는 런던으로 올라가는 기차간에서 절반쯤 왔을 무렵, 주머니에서 편지 한 통을 꺼내 읽었다. 여정의 앞부분에서는 다른 일들을 생각하고 있었으나, 차츰 그런 다른 일들은 당분간 더 생각하지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 쪽으로 마음을 굳혔고, 그리하여 상념을 흩어 버릴 방편으로 그 편지에 기댄 것이었다. 편지는 크래들에게서 온 것으로, 내용은 이러했다—\n\n소득세 사무국, 186-년 5월 -일.\n\n친애하는 존에게,—내가 자네에게 전해야 할 소식이 자네를 노엽게 하지 않기를 바라며, 이제부터 하려는 이야기 때문에 내가 자네에게 품은 저 크나큰 우정을 저버렸다고 자네가 여기지 않기를 바라네. 내가 자네만큼 그 마음을 높이 치는 사내는—[그리고 사내라는 낱말에는 밑줄이 그어져 있었다]—내가 자네만큼 높이 치는 사내는 이 세상에 없네. 그리고 자네가 여러 자리에서 한 말을 죄 들어 온 터라 자네에게 나를 언짢아할 정당한 까닭은 없으리라 여기네만, 그럼에도 마음의 일이란 한 사람이 다른 사람의 심정을 헤아리기가 여간 어려운 것이 아니고, 숙녀의 애정이 걸린 일에서는 이따금 다툼이 일어나기도 한다는 것을 나도 아네.\n\n임스는 편지를 처음 훑어 내려가다 여기까지 이르렀을 때, 다음에 무엇이 올지 훤히 알았다. “가엾은 코들!” 그는 속으로 말했다. “걸려들었군. 이제 다시는 그 바늘에서 제 몸을 빼내지 못할 거야.”\n\n허나 그것이야 어찌 되었든, 일은 이미 내가 어떻게 고쳐 볼 수 없을 만큼 멀리 가 버렸네. 또한 이 땅의 어떤 유혹으로도 나를 돌려세우지는 못할 걸세. 우정의 요구는 참으로 강한 것이나, 사랑의 요구는 그 위에 있네. 자네와 어밀리아 로퍼 사이에 오간 일은 물론 나도 다 알고 있네. 그 가운데 많은 것은 전에 자네에게서 들었네만, 나머지는 이제 그녀가 제 성품에서 가장 두드러진 특징인 저 맑은 마음의 정직함으로 나에게 털어놓았네. 그녀는 한때 자네에게 마음이 기울었노라고, 그리고 자네의 끈질김에 이끌려 자네가 그녀를 자네의 정혼녀로 여기도록 허락했노라고 고백했네. [그가 쓴 그 우아한 낱말을 상스러운 영어로 옮기면 ‘노리개 계집’이 되는 줄로 그는 아마 여기고 있었으리라.] 허나 이제 자네와 그녀 사이의 일은 죄 끝나야 하네. 어밀리아는 내 사람이 되기로 약속했고—[이 대목에도 밑줄이 그어져 있었다]—나는 그녀를 내 사람으로 만들 작정이네. 이 일로 자네가 겪을지 모를 실망에 대하여 L. D.의 다정한 미소 속에서 위로를 얻기를, 자네의 참된 벗이 간절히 바라네,\n\n추신.—아무래도 자네에게 전부 다 말해 두는 편이 낫겠네. 로퍼 부인이 집안일로 얼마간 곤경에 처해 있네. 집세가 조금 밀렸고, 갚지 못한 계산서도 몇 장 있다네. 저 끔찍한 루펙스네 사람들 때문에 이 지경이 되었다고 그분이 설명하기에, 나는 이 집을 내 손으로 떠맡기로 승낙했고 상인 한둘에게 소액의 어음을 끊어 주었네. 물론 그분이 그것을 갚아 나갈 테지만, 모자랐던 것은 결국 신용이었지. 집은 그분이 계속 꾸려 가겠지만 사실상 주인은 내가 되는 셈이네. 이제 자네가 여기 남아 있기는 곤란하리라 짐작하네만, 우리 사무국 사람들 몇쯤은—이를테면 여섯쯤은—내가 꽤 편안하게 지내도록 해 줄 수 있지 않겠나? 그것이 로퍼 부인의 생각인데, 나로서도 나쁘지 않은 생각이라고 여기네. 우리가 맨 먼저 할 일은 루펙스네 사람들을 몰아내는 것일세. 스프루스 양은 다음 주에 나가네. 그동안 우리는 모두 각자 제 방에서 끼니를 먹고 있어서 루펙스 부부가 먹을 것이 하나도 없다네. 그런데도 저들은 개의치 않는 눈치이고, 여전히 거실과 제일 좋은 침실을 차지하고 있네. 우리는 화요일이 지나면 저들을 문밖으로 잠가 내치고 짐짝을 죄다 선술집으로 보내 버릴 작정이네.\n\n가엾은 크래들! 임스는 좌석에 몸을 기대고 제 벗이 굴러떨어진 불행의 깊이를 헤아려 보다가, 제 처지가 거의 마음에 들기 시작했다. 그 자신이야 물론 몹시 비참한 사내였다. 살아갈 값어치가 있는 일이라고는 이 세상에 단 하나뿐인데 그것을 얻을 수가 없었다. 그는 지난 사흘 동안 기관차 앞에 몸을 던질 생각을 하고 있었고, 아직도 그러지 않으리라고는 아주 장담할 수 없었다. 그럼에도 가엾은 크래들이 제 손으로 골라잡은 자리에 견주면 그의 자리는 신들 틈에 낀 자리였다. 어밀리아 로퍼의 남편이 되는 것만도 모자라, 신부의 재산이랍시고 장차 장모가 될 사람의 빚을 죄다 제 어깨에 짊어지도록 떠밀리다니! 그것도 아주 시원찮은 하숙집의 주인이 되어서 말이다—책임으로 치면 주인이되, 있을 법한 이문으로 치면 결코 주인이 아닌 주인이! 게다가 그 모든 것 위에, 아니 거의 그 모든 것보다 더 고약하게도, 제 인생의 첫머리에서 루펙스 부부를 짐짝처럼 걸머지게 되다니! 참으로 가엾은 크래들이었다!\n\n임스는 런던을 떠나기 전에 하숙집에서 제 물건을 가져오지 않았으므로, 도착하는 즉시 버턴 크레슨트로 마차를 몰기로 마음먹었다. 하룻밤이라도 거기 머물 뜻이 있어서가 아니라, 작별을 고하고 어밀리아에게 축하의 말을 건네고 로퍼 부인과 마지막 셈을 매듭짓기 위해서였다. 앞 장에서 밝혀 두었어야 할 것이 하나 있다. 드 게스트 백작이 그와 헤어지기 전에, 릴리에게서 뜻을 이루지 못한 것이 돈 문제에 관해서는 아무런 차이도 만들지 않으리라고 그에게 말해 두었다는 것이다. 물론 존은 아무것도 바라지 않으며 지금 같은 처지에서는 아무것도 받지 않겠노라고 항변했다. 그러나 백작은 제 뜻을 관철할 줄 아는 사람이었고, 이 일에서도 그렇게 했다. 그러므로 우리의 벗은 런던으로 돌아올 때 재산깨나 있는 사람이었고, 사무국에 닿는 대로 남은 자잘한 셈을 치를 수표를 보내 드리겠노라고 로퍼 부인에게 말할 수 있었다.\n\n그는 한낮에 도착했다—관청 서기들의 여느 방식과는 전혀 다르게 돌아온 것이니, 그들은 대개 제 자리에 얼굴을 보여야 하는 시각보다 반 시간 남짓 앞서 수도에 닿도록 맞추는 법이다. 그러나 그는 기한보다 이틀이나 일찍 돌아왔고, 오월의 런던이 숲이며 들판보다 훨씬 더 마음에 든다는 듯이 시골에서 달아나 온 것이었다. 그렇다고 런던이나 숲과 들판이 그의 귀환에 무슨 힘을 미친 것은 아니었다. 그는 릴리 데일의 발치에 몸을 던지려고 내려갔던 것이고—이제 스스로 인정하듯이 거의 승리를 확신하는 마음으로 내려갔던 것이며—릴리 데일이 그를 제 발치에 두려 하지 않았기에 돌아온 것이었다. “저는 그이를,—크로즈비 그이를,—이 세상 무엇보다도 사랑했어요. 지금도 그대로예요. 저는 아직도 그이를 이 세상 무엇보다도 사랑해요.” 그를 런던으로 몰아낸 것은 이 말이었다. 그런 말을 듣고 쫓겨난 마당에, 사무국에 하루 이틀 일찍 닿든 늦게 닿든 그에게는 대수로울 것이 없었다. 래플 경의 종소리와 래플 경의 목소리가 곁들여진다 해도, 시티의 그 자그마한 방이 이제 그에게는 레이디 줄리아의 응접실보다 더 마음 편한 곳이었다. 그러므로 그는 바로 그날 오후에 래플 경 앞에 나설 것이고, 제 자리를 차지하고 있는 침입자를 몰아낼 참이었다. 그러나 그 전에 먼저 버턴 크레슨트에 들러 로퍼네 사람들에게 작별을 고할 것이었다.\n\n문을 열어 준 것은 충직한 제미마였다. “임스 나리, 임스 나리! 아이고 저런, 아이고 저런!” 하숙집에서 벌어진 온갖 소동에서 늘 그의 편을 들어 온 이 가엾은 처녀는 두 손을 높이 쳐들고, 제가 아끼는 분을 이 집의 운수에서 갈라놓은 팔자를 한탄했다. “다 알고 계시겠지요, 조니 나리?” 조니 나리는 아마 다 알고 있는 것 같다고 대답하고서 이 집 안주인을 찾았다. “예, 그럼요, 댁에 계십니다요. 그 루펙스네 사람들 때문에 좀처럼 밖에 나가지도 못하십니다요. 이거 참 볼만한 판이지요? 만찬도 없고 아무것도 없습니다요! 저 상자들은 스프루스 양 것입니다요. 그분은 이제 곧, 이 참에 떠나신답니다요. 다들 위층 거실에 계신 걸 보시게 될 겁니다요.” 그리하여 그는 위층 거실로 올라갔고, 거기서 어머니와 딸을, 그리고 그들과 함께 보닛과 숄로 몸을 꽁꽁 여민 스프루스 양을 보았다. “그만두세요, 어머니.” 어밀리아가 말하고 있었다. “그렇게 하시는 게 무슨 소용이에요? 가시겠다면 가시게 두세요.”\n\n“하지만 그렇게 오랜 세월을 나하고 함께 지내셨는데,” 로퍼 부인이 흐느끼며 말했다. “나는 늘 이분을 위해 무엇이든 다 해 드렸고요! 안 그래요, 샐리 스프루스?” 임스는 제가 이 집에 두 해나 들어 살았으면서도 저 노처녀의 세례명을 여태 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없다는 것을 문득 깨달았다. 그가 방에 들어섰을 때 그를 맨 먼저 본 사람은 스프루스 양이었다. 아무도 보고 있지 않았더라면 로퍼 부인의 애처로움이 그녀 쪽에서도 얼마쯤 애처로움을 자아냈을지 모르나, 젊은 사내가 눈에 들어오자 그녀는 여느 때의 조용한 상태로 되돌아갔다. “나야 늙은 여자일 뿐이지요,” 그녀가 말했다. “그리고 여기 임스 씨가 돌아오셨습니다.”\n\n“안녕하십니까, 로퍼 부인? 안녕하세요,—어밀리아? 안녕하십니까, 스프루스 양?” 그러고서 그는 그들 모두와 악수를 나누었다.\n\n“어머나 세상에,” 로퍼 부인이 말했다. “깜짝 놀랐잖아요!”\n\n“어머, 임스 씨, 그런 식으로 돌아오시다니 이게 무슨 일이에요.” 어밀리아가 말했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 그럼 어떤 식으로 돌아와야 하나요? 제가 문을 두드리는 소리를 못 들으셨을 뿐이잖아요. 그런데 스프루스 양께서 정말로 이 댁을 떠나시는 겁니까?”\n\n“기막힌 노릇 아니에요, 임스 씨? 열아홉 해를 함께 지냈는데요.—두 집을 다 합치면 그렇지요, 스프루스 양, 정말 그렇고말고요.” 이 대목에서 스프루스 양은 문제의 그 기간이 실은 열여덟 해밖에 되지 않는다는 것을 존 임스에게 납득시키려고 무던히 애를 썼으나, 로퍼 부인은 위세가 당당하여 그것을 허락하지 않았다. “하루를 더 치더라도 열아홉 해예요. 내가 날짜를 모르면 아무도 모르는 거지요. 그리고 이분 성미를 아는 사람은 나 말고는 이 세상에 아무도 없어요. 내가 밤마다 이분 침실로 올라가서 내 손으로 직접 이분께 드린 게—” 그러다가 그녀는 말을 멈추었으니, 밤마다 제 손님에게 베풀어 온 시중이 어떤 성질의 것인지를 젊은 사내 앞에서 밝히기에는 너무나 마음씨 좋은 여자였던 것이다.\n\n“다른 데 가시면 여기만큼 편하시지는 않을 겁니다, 스프루스 양.” 임스가 말했다.\n\n“편하다니요! 당연히 못 하시지요,” 어밀리아가 말했다. “하지만 내가 어머니라면 이 일로 더는 말을 섞지 않겠어요.”\n\n“내가 생각하는 건 돈이 아니라 그 정이에요.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다. “집이 그렇게 허전해질 텐데. 나도 내가 어찌 될지 모르겠어요. 정말 모르겠다고요. 게다가 이제는 일이 다 이렇게 잘 풀렸고 루펙스네 사람들도 화요일이면 나가야 하는데— 내가 말하지요, 샐리. 삯마차 삯은 내가 내겠어요. 그리고 내일 승합마차를 타고 덜리치로 가서 그 일을 죄다 내 주머니를 털어 처리하겠어요. 정말 그렇게 하겠어요. 자, 삯마차가 왔군요. 내가 내려가서 돌려보내지요.”\n\n“그건 제가 하지요.” 임스가 말했다. “대기소에서 부른 것이니 육 펜스면 돼요.” 그가 방을 나서는데 로퍼 부인이 뒤에서 소리쳐 일러 주었다. 그러나 마부는 일 실링을 받았고, 존이 돌아와 보니 제미마가 스프루스 양의 상자들을 다시 그 방으로 나르고 있었다. “가엾은 코들에게는 그만큼 잘된 일이지,” 그는 속으로 말했다. “저 장사에 뛰어든 마당이니 돈을 내줄 사람이 하나라도 있는 편이 좋고말고.”\n\n로퍼 부인이 스프루스 양을 따라 층계를 올라가자 조니는 어밀리아와 단둘이 남았다. “그이가 편지를 보냈겠지요, 다 알아요.” 그녀가 얼굴을 그에게서 조금 돌린 채 말했다. 그녀는 분명 아주 아름다웠으나, 그 낯빛에는 어딘가 굳고 뒤틀리고 거의 뾰로통하다 할 기색이 있어서 얼굴에서 고운 맛을 죄다 앗아 가고 있었다. 그러면서도 그녀는 그에게 뾰로통하게 굴 뜻은 조금도 없었다.\n\n“그렇습니다,” 존이 말했다. “일이 어떻게 되어 가는지 그가 다 알려 주었어요.”\n\n“그래서요?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그래서요?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“하실 말씀이 그게 답니까?”\n\n“허락하신다면 축하를 드리지요.”\n\n“쳇,—축하라니요! 나는 그런 허튼수작이 딱 질색이에요. 그쪽에 아무 감정도 없다면 나도 없어요. 아니, 감정이란 게 무슨 소용인지 도무지 모르겠네요. 그런 게 나한테 무슨 이로움을 준 적도 없고요. L. D.와 혼인하기로 약조했나요?”\n\n“아니요, 그렇지 않습니다.”\n\n“그러면 그 밖에 나한테 하실 말씀은 없고요?”\n\n“없습니다,—그쪽이 행복하기를 바라는 마음 말고는요. 그 밖에 무슨 말을 하겠어요? 그쪽은 내 벗 크래들과 혼인하기로 약조했고, 나는 그것이 행복한 짝이 되리라 생각합니다.”\n\n그녀는 얼굴을 그에게서 더 멀리 돌렸고, 그 낯빛은 한층 더 뾰로통해졌다. 이런 마당에도 그녀에게는 아직 그가 제게 돌아오리라는 희망이 남아 있었던 것일까?\n\n“잘 있어요, 어밀리아.” 그가 그녀에게 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n“이게 이 집에서 보는 마지막 모습이 되겠군요!”\n\n“글쎄요, 그건 잘 모르겠는데요. 허락하신다면 혼인하신 뒤에 찾아뵙지요.”\n\n“그럼요,” 그녀가 말했다. “그래서 이 집에 또 소동이 나고 시끄러워지고 시샘이 일라고요.—저 위층의 못된 여자한테 그랬듯이 말이지요. 내가 알고서야 어림없어요! 존 임스, 나는 당신을 만나지 않았더라면 좋았겠어요. 우리가 처음 만났을 때 둘 다 그 자리에서 쓰러져 죽었더라면 좋았겠어요. 내가 당신을 아꼈던 것처럼 어떤 사내를 아끼게 되리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했어요. 그런 건 죄다 쓰레기고 헛소리고 부질없는 짓이지요. 나도 알아요. 평생 응접실에나 앉아 있을 수 있는 아가씨들한테야 다 좋은 일이겠지만, 여자가 제 앞길을 제 손으로 헤쳐 나가야 할 때는 그런 게 다 부질없는 짓이에요. 게다가 내 앞길처럼 이렇게 험한 길을 말이에요!”\n\n“하지만 이제는 험하지 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“그럴까요? 나는 험할 것 같은데요. 당신이 한번 겪어 봤으면 좋겠어요. 그렇다고 푸념을 하려는 건 아니에요. 나는 일하는 걸 싫다고 여긴 적이 없고, 사람을 겪는 일이라면 누구하고든 견뎌 낼 수 있어요. 나 같은 사람한테 세상이 온통 춤추고 바이올린 켜는 자리일 수는 없지요. 그쯤은 나도 잘 알아요. 하지만—” 그러고서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“그 ‘하지만’이 무엇에 관한 겁니까, 어밀리아?”\n\n“그걸 나한테 묻다니, 당신답네요.” 사실을 말하자면 그는 그것을 묻지 말았어야 했다. “됐어요. 나는 당신하고 말을 섞을 생각이 없어요. 당신이 악한이었다면 나는 바보였고, 그게 더 나쁘지요.”\n\n“하지만 나는 내가 악한이었다고는 생각하지 않는데요.”\n\n“나는 둘 다였어요,” 처녀가 말했다. “게다가 둘 다 아무 보람도 없이 말이에요. 그 말을 들었으면 이제 가셔도 돼요. 내가 어떤 사람인지는 말해 드렸으니, 당신 자신의 이름은 당신이 붙이도록 남겨 두지요. 내 속에 그렇게 바보 같은 구석이 있는 줄은 몰랐어요. 나를 애태우는 건 바로 그거예요. 됐어요, 선생님. 이제 다 지난 일이니, 안녕히 가시라고 인사드리지요.”\n\n존이 헤어지면서 그녀에게 다시 입을 맞출 까닭은 조금도 없었다고 나는 생각하나, 그는 그렇게 했다. 그녀는 그를 뿌리치지 않고 그대로 받았으나, 그 다정한 손길을 뾰로통한 낯으로 견뎌 냈을 뿐이었다. “이게 마지막이에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “안녕히 가세요, 존 임스.”\n\n“잘 있어요, 어밀리아. 그에게 좋은 아내가 되도록 애써 봐요, 그러면 행복해질 겁니다.” 그녀는 이 말에 코를 찡그리며 이루 말할 수 없이 경멸하는 낯빛을 지었다. 그러나 그 뒤로는 더 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 그는 방을 나섰다. 응접실 문간에서 그는 로퍼 부인과 마주쳤고, 그녀와 작별의 말을 나누었다.\n\n“와 주셔서 참 다행이에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “스프루스 양을 붙든 건 바로 임스 씨가 해 주신 그 한마디였어요. 그분은 셈을 꼬박꼬박 치르는 분이거든요! 그래서 크래들 일로 저 애하고 이야기를 다 나누셨군요. 저 애는 그이한테 좋은 아내가 될 거예요. 정말 그럴 거예요.—임스 씨가 저 애를 여겨 주신 것보다 훨씬 더요.”\n\n“저 아가씨가 아주 좋은 아내가 되리라는 건 나도 의심치 않습니다.”\n\n“그러니까요, 임스 씨, 이제 다 지난 일이고 우리는 서로 마음을 알잖아요, 안 그래요? 저 애가 임스 씨한테 마음을 두려고 할 때 나는 참 마음이 편치 않았어요. 정말 그랬어요. 내 딸이니 저 애를 거스를 수도 없었고요.—안 그래요? 하지만 나는 그게 어울리는 일이 아니라는 걸 알고 있었어요. 세상에, 나도 지체 차이쯤은 알지요. 저 애야 그이한테라면 어느 요일에라도 넘치도록 좋은 짝이에요, 임스 씨.”\n\n“정말 그렇지요,—토요일이든 일요일이든 말입니다.” 조니가 무슨 말을 해야 좋을지 딱히 알지 못한 채 말했다.\n\n“그렇고말고요. 그리고 그이가 저 애한테 제 도리를 다한다면 저 애도 그이한테 제 도리에서 어긋나지 않을 거예요. 그리고 임스 씨로 말하면, 이 집에 임스 씨를 모신 것이 나로서는 늘 영광이고 기쁨이었어요. 그러니 언제라도 임스 씨가 아시는 젊은 분들 가운데 누구든 나한테 보내 주실 만한 자리가 있거든 좋은 말씀 한마디 해 주세요. 나는 그분들한테 어머니가 할 도리를 다하겠어요. 정말 그러겠어요. 저 루펙스네 사람들 일로 내가 잘못한 걸 알고 있어요. 하지만 내가 그 값을 치르지 않았나요, 임스 씨? 그리고 처음에는 알아보기가 어려웠잖아요, 안 그래요? 그리고 임스 씨하고 어밀리아 일로 말하면, 임스 씨가 아시는 젊은 분들을 보내 주신다 해도 이제 그런 일은 더 있을 수 없잖아요, 안 그래요? 나도 모든 일이 마땅히 그러했어야 할 대로 되지는 않았다는 걸 알아요.—그러니까 임스 씨에 관해서는요. 하지만 떠나시기 전에 나를 정직한 사람으로 여기신다는 말씀만은 듣고 싶어요. 나는 정직하려고 애써 왔어요. 정말 그랬어요.”\n\n임스는 그녀의 정직함을 자신이 믿어 의심치 않으며, 저 딱한 사람들인 루펙스 부부에 관해서든 다른 일에 관해서든 그녀의 인품을 헐뜯을 생각은 해 본 적이 없다고 그녀를 안심시켰다. “젊은 사람들이 하숙 자리를 두고 저에게 물어 올 것 같지는 않습니다만,” 그가 말했다. “제가 무슨 도움이 될 수 있다면 그렇게 하지요.” 그러고서 그는 그녀에게 작별을 고했고, 충직한 제미마에게 반 소버린을 쥐여 준 다음 버턴 크레슨트에 오래 두고 볼 작별을 고했다. 어밀리아는 제가 혼인한 뒤에 찾아오지 말라고 그에게 일렀고, 그는 그 말을 그대로 받아들이기로 마음을 굳혔다. 그리하여 그는 크레슨트를 떠나 걸어 나왔다. 발에서 먼지를 털어 내지는 않았으나, 그곳의 먼지도 그곳의 때도 다시는 알고 지내지 않으리라 마음을 굳히면서였다. 때는 그가 거기서 실컷 겪었고, 로퍼 부인의 집에 든 사람들이란 그가 제 젊은 시절의 쉼터를 사려 깊게 찾았어야 할 그런 사람들이 아니었다는 것을 이제 그는 알 만한 나이가 되어 있었다. 그러나 그는 그 불길에서 별로 다치지 않고 빠져나왔고, 제 벗이 이미 겪었으며 앞으로 스스로 겪게 될 저 무서운 그을림에 대해 그가 느끼는 바가 거의 없었다는 것을 나는 유감스럽게 여긴다. 그는 이 일을 로퍼 부인이 바라보는 그대로 바라보는 데에 아주 만족하고 있었다. 어밀리아는 조지프 크래들에게라면—어느 요일에라도 넘치도록 좋은 짝이었다. 가엾은 크래들, 이 책장에서는 이 알림 뒤로 다시는 그의 소식을 듣지 못할 것이다! 나는 그가 지은 죄의 크기에 견주어 그에게 매겨진 심판이 모질었다고 생각하지 않을 수 없다. 그는 우리의 벗이자 주인공보다 더 약하고 더 어리석기는 했으나, 내가 아는 한 더 사악하지는 않았다. 그러나 벌이 떨어지는 것은 허영에 차고 어리석은 자들에게이며—그것도 어찌나 촘촘하고 끊임없이 떨어지는지, 생각하는 사람이라면 허영과 어리석음이야말로 모든 죄 가운데 가장 용서받기 어려운 것이 아닐까 하는 데까지 내몰리게 된다. 크래들에 관해서는 이것만 밝혀 두겠다. 그는 정말로 어밀리아와 혼인했고, 얼마간 자랑스러운 마음으로 로퍼 부인네 식탁 끝자리에 이 집 주인의 자리를 차지했으며, 로퍼 부인의 빚을 죄다 제가 떠맡았다. 그 뒤 그의 운명이 어찌 되었는지는 이 책장에서 이야기할 자리가 없다.\n\n크레슨트를 나선 임스는 마차를 몰아 제 사무국으로 갔고, 마침 네 시에 사람들이 그곳을 나서는 참에 그곳에 닿았다. 크래들은 이미 가고 없어서 그날 오후에는 그를 만나지 못했다. 그러나 러브 씨와는 악수를 나눌 기회를 얻었는데, 러브 씨는 관청의 높으신 분에게 마땅한 온갖 웃음 띤 정중함으로 그를 대했다—개인 비서란 아주 높으신 분은 아닐지라도 반쯤은 높으신 분이며, 얼마간의 우러름을 받을 자격이 있는 것이다.—그리고 그는 복도에서 키싱 씨를 스쳐 지났는데, 그는 여느 때처럼 커다란 장부를 옆구리에 끼고 서둘러 가는 중이었다. 키싱 씨는 그렇게 바쁜 와중에도 발을 끌던 걸음을 멈추었다. 그러나 임스는 그를 쳐다보기만 했을 뿐, 고개를 까딱해 아는 체해 주는 영예조차 좀처럼 베풀지 않았다. 그렇다고 키싱 씨가 언짢아한 것은 아니었다. 그는 수석 위원의 개인 비서가 어느 백작의 손님으로 다녀왔다는 것을 알고 있었으니, 그런 사람에게서 고개 까딱 이상의 무엇을 바랄 수 있겠는가? 그러고 나서 존은 래플 경의 지엄한 어전으로 들어갔고, 그 위대한 인물이 피츠하워드가 지켜보는 앞에서 제 신발을 신고 있는 것을 보았다. 피츠하워드는 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그러나 그 신발에 그가 손을 댄 것은 아니었으니, 그는 나중에 존 임스에게 그 사실을 알릴 기회를 얻었다.\n\n래플 경은 온통 웃음과 정중함 그 자체였다. “돌아와서 반갑네, 임스. 정말이지 반가워. 하기야 자네가 없는 동안에도 나와 피츠하워드가 아주 훌륭하게 해내기는 했지. 안 그런가, 피츠하워드?”\n\n“아, 예.” 피츠하워드가 느릿하게 말했다. “임스가 자리를 비운 동안 잠깐이었으니 저야 싫지 않았습니다.”\n\n“자네는 너무 게을러서 이 일을 오래 붙들고 있지 못하지, 나도 알아. 하지만 자네 밥벌이야 다른 데서 버터를 발라 줄 테니 대수로울 것 없고 말이야. 공작부인을 뵙거든 안부 전해 주게.” 그러고서 피츠하워드는 갔다. “그래, 내 정다운 옛 벗께서는 어떠신가?” 래플 경이 물었는데, 마치 살아 있는 온 세상 사람 가운데 드 게스트 경이야말로 제가 가장 깊고 가장 오래된 사랑을 품은 사람이라는 투였다. 그러면서도 그는 존 임스가 그 속을 저만큼이나 훤히 알고 있다는 것을 알고 있었을 것이 틀림없다. 그러나 세상에는 저희가 하는 말을 아무도 믿어 주지 않는다는 것을 알면서도—아니, 그로 인해 저희가 얼마나 큰 미움을 사는지, 저희 허영이 낳는 웃음거리가 얼마나 오래가는지 알면서도—경이니 후작이니 하는 이들을 제 벗이라 부르는 데에서 더없이 생생한 즐거움을 느끼는 사람들이 있다. 그것은 어느 귀족 사회에서든 그 바깥뜰에 퍼져 있는 순한 미친 증세이며, 적잖은 성가심을 안겨 주면서도 미지근한 즐거움밖에 주지 못하는 것이니 너무 매섭게 다룰 일은 아니다.\n\n“그래, 내 정다운 옛 벗께서는 어떠신가?” 임스는 그의 정다운 옛 벗께서 아주 잘 계시고, 레이디 줄리아도 아주 잘 계시며, 그 정다운 옛 저택도 아주 잘 있다고 그를 안심시켰다. 이제 래플 경은 그 “정다운 옛 저택”이 제게도 아주 익숙한 곳인 양 말했다. “사냥감은 잘 있던가? 새들이 많을 조짐이 보이던가?” 래플 경의 궁금증은 자못 간절했고, 거의 허물없다 할 만한 애정을 담아 표현되었다. “그런데 말이지, 임스, 자네 지금은 어디서 지내나?”\n\n“글쎄요, 아직 자리를 잡지 못했습니다. 지금은 그레이트웨스턴 철도 호텔에 있습니다.”\n\n“아주 훌륭한 집이지. 아주. 다만 사교철 내내 거기 묵는다면 비용이 만만찮을 걸세.” 조니는 그곳에서 하룻밤 넘게 묵을 생각이 없었으나, 그 점에 관해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. “그건 그렇고, 내일 우리 집에 와서 만찬을 함께 하는 게 좋겠네. 레이디 버플이 자네를 몹시 알고 싶어 해. 우리 말고도 한두 사람쯤 더 올 걸세. 내 벗 덤벨로에게도 청해 보기는 했네만, 의회에서 무슨 시시한 일이 벌어지고 있어서 그가 빠져나올 수 없다고 하더군.” 조니는 덤벨로 경보다 너그러워서 그 초대를 받아들였다. “레이디 버플은 어떤 분일까?” 사무국을 나서 걸어가면서 그는 속으로 말했다.\n\n그는 아직 어디에 거처를 구해야 할지 모르는 채로 그레이트웨스턴 호텔에 들었고, 우리는 그를 거기 두고 떠나기로 하자. 그는 눈에는 그토록 편안해 보이면서 실상은 그토록 편안하지 못한 그런 식탁 가운데 하나에 앉아 홀로 양갈비를 먹고 있다. 내가 지금 말하는 것은 방금 이름을 댄 그 훌륭한 집을 두고 하는 말이 아니라, 무릇 그런 식탁의 성질 일반을 두고 하는 말이다. 호텔 식당에서 홀로 먹는 양갈비란 어떤 신도 부러워할 잔치가 아니다. 그리고 그 양갈비가 수프와 생선과 작은 접시들과 큰 접시들 따위로 바뀐다면 사정은 나아지기는커녕 더 나빠진다. 저 말총 의자에 홀로 앉아 방 안을 멀거니 바라보며 웨이터들이 수건을 휘두르며 오가는 것을 지켜보고 있자니 무슨 위안이 있겠는가? 영국 사람 말고는 아직 아무도 제 몸을 그런 처지에 두어 볼 생각을 해 본 적이 없다! 그러나 우리는 존 임스를 여기 두고 떠나기로 한다. 그리고 그렇게 하면서 이 말만은 하도록 허락받아야겠으니, 이제야, 바로 이 순간에야 그가 제 어른의 삶으로 들어섰다는 것이다. 여태껏 그는 풋내기였다—말하자면 송아지 티를 여느 송아지들보다 더 늦게까지 삶 속으로 끌고 온 송아지였다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 그가 다른 송아지들보다 못한 황소가 되리라는 조짐이 있었던 것은 아마 아니리라. 이 책장에 적힌 대로의 그의 삶은 그에게 눈부신 성공이라고는 하나도 안겨 주지 못했고, 그가 맡아 온 주인공 노릇에 어울릴 자격을 갖추어 주지도 못했다. 나는 풋내기에게 그토록 큰 자리를 내어 준 것이 내 잘못이며, 크로즈비 씨를 내 화폭 앞자리에 더 두드러지게 내세웠더라면 내 이야기를 더 잘 풀어냈으리라고 느낀다. 어쨌든 그는 아내를 하나 얻지 않았는가—주인공이라면 마땅히 그래야 하듯이 말이다. 그런데 나는 내 가엾은 벗 조니를 혼인의 가망이라고는 하나도 없이 남겨 두어야 하는 것이다.\n\n호텔 식당의 제 외로운 식탁 앞에서 시무룩하게 앉아 있는 동안 그가 제 자신을 두고 생각한 것이 바로 그런 것이었다. 그는 여태껏 자신이 어른이 아니었음을 스스로 인정했다. 그러나 그와 동시에 그는 어떤 결심을 하나 세웠으니, 바라건대 그 결심이 오늘 이날부터 그가 어른의 삶을 시작하는 데에 도움이 되기를 바란다.\n\n# 제60장. 결말\n\n릴리가 큰 집으로 삼촌을 찾아가 홉킨스를 위해 사정한 것은 유월 초의 일이었다—홉킨스에게 큰 집 수석 정원사의 온갖 특권을 되돌려 주십사 하는 사정이었다. 여기에는 좀 우스운 데가 있었으니, 그가 실은 제 특권을 한 번도 내려놓은 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그 다툼이 벌어진 사정은 이러했다.\n\n그 무렵에도, 또 여러 해 전부터도, 홉킨스와 관리인 졸리프 사이에는 골치 아픈 문제가 하나 있었다—마구간 두엄 문제였다. 홉킨스는 누구에게 허락을 구할 것도 없이 농장 마당에서 제게 필요한 만큼 가져갈 권리가 있다고 우겨 왔다. 이에 졸리프는, 만약 그렇다면 홉킨스가 그것을 몽땅 가져가 버릴 것이라고 넌지시 비추었다. “그렇다고 내가 그걸 먹을 수야 없잖소.” 홉킨스는 말했다. 졸리프는 그저 끙 소리만 냈는데, 홉킨스가 생각하기에 그 끙 소리의 뜻은 이러했다. 정원사가 길이 오십 피트에 높이 십오 피트나 되는 두엄 산을 먹을 수는 없다 하더라도—몸으로 직접 먹을 수는 없다 하더라도—그것을 제 몫으로 먹을 것으로 바꿔 놓을 수는 있지 않으냐는 것이었다. 그리하여 큰 반목이 생겼다. 딱한 지주는 당연하다는 듯이 중재를 맡아 달라는 부름을 받았고, 제가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 꾀로 판결을 미루다가 마침내 졸리프에게 떠밀려, 홉킨스는 제게 배정되지 않은 것은 하나도 가져가서는 안 된다고 선언하고 말았다. 주인에게서 그 판결을 전해 들은 홉킨스는 늙은 입술을 깨물고 늙은 발꿈치로 홱 돌아섰을 뿐 말이 없었다. “다른 데서도 다들 그렇게 하네.” 지주가 변명하듯 말했다. “다른 데라굽쇼!” 홉킨스가 코웃음을 쳤다. 자기 같은 정원사를 다른 어디서 찾을 수 있단 말인가? 그 순간부터 그가 그런 명령 따위는 결코 지키지 않겠다고 마음먹었다는 것은 굳이 밝힐 것도 없다. 이튿날 아침 졸리프는 그 명령이 깨졌다고 지주에게 알렸고, 지주는 안달하며 씩씩거렸다. 차라리 졸리프가 그 문제의 두엄 산 밑에 곱게 파묻혀 있었으면 싶었다. “다들 제멋대로 하겠다면요.” 졸리프가 말했다. “아무도 아무한테도 아랑곳하지 않게 될 겁니다요.” 지주는 명령이란 한번 내려진 이상 지켜져야 한다는 것을 알고 있었고, 그리하여 속으로 여러 번 끙끙 앓은 끝에 홉킨스를 상대로 싸움을 벌여야겠다고 마음을 정했다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그는 그 늙은이가 손수 커다란 두엄 손수레를 마당에서 채마밭 쪽으로 밀고 돌아 나오는 것을 보았다. 그런데 여느 때 같으면 홉킨스가 그런 종류의 일을 제 손으로 할 필요는 없었다. 그의 밑에는 장작을 패고 물을 나르고 손수레를 미는 사내가 있었으니—늘 한 사람, 흔히는 두 사람이었다. 지주는 그를 보는 순간 그가 죄를 짓고 있음을 알아차렸고, 가던 길에 멈춰 서라고 명했다.\n\n“홉킨스.” 그가 말했다. “가져가기 전에 필요한 것을 왜 청하지 않았나?” 늙은이는 손수레를 땅에 내려놓고 주인의 얼굴을 올려다보더니, 두 손에 침을 뱉고는 다시 손수레를 잡아 들었다. “홉킨스, 그래서는 안 되네.” 지주가 말했다. “거기 그대로 있게.”\n\n“무엇이 안 된다는 말씀입니까요?” 홉킨스가 말했다. 손수레는 여전히 땅에서 들어 올린 채였으나 아직 나아가지는 않았다.\n\n“내려놓게, 홉킨스.” 그러자 홉킨스는 그것을 내려놓았다. “자네가 내 명령을 대놓고 어기고 있다는 걸 모르나?”\n\n“지주 어른, 제가 이 댁 언저리에서 지낸 것이 이제 일흔 해가 다 되어 갑니다요.”\n\n“백일흔 해가 다 되어 간다 해도 주인이 둘일 수는 없네. 여기 주인은 나고, 나는 끝까지 그럴 작정이야. 그 두엄을 마당으로 도로 가져가게.”\n\n“마당으로 도로 말입니까요?” 홉킨스가 아주 느릿느릿 말했다.\n\n“그래, 마당으로 도로.”\n\n“아니, 저 사람들 다 보는 앞에서 말입니까요?”\n\n“그래, 자네는 저 사람들 다 보는 앞에서 내 말을 어기지 않았나?”\n\n홉킨스는 잠시 말이 없었다. 지주에게서 눈길을 돌린 채, 깊이 생각할 필요가 있다는 듯, 그러나 깊이 생각한 덕분에 마침내 옳은 결론에 이르렀다는 듯 고개를 흔들었다. 그러고 나서 그는 다시 손수레를 잡더니 거의 종종걸음을 치다시피 하여 제 전리품을 채마밭으로 실어 갔다. 그가 내는 속도로는 지주의 힘으로 그를 멈춰 세울 수 없었을 것이고, 데일 씨 또한 제 하인과 몸으로 맞붙고 싶지도 않았다. 그러나 그는 무섭게 성이 나서 그 사내의 뒤에다 대고 소리쳤다. 제 말을 듣지 않는다면 그 불복종한 하인은 두고두고 그 대가를 뉘우치게 되리라고. 홉킨스는 그 자리에 어울리게 종종걸음을 치면서 고개를 흔들었고, 짐을 오이 온상 발치에 부려 놓은 다음 곧바로 주인에게 돌아와 온실 열쇠를 내밀었다.\n\n“나리.” 홉킨스가 모자란 숨으로 낼 수 있는 최선의 목소리로 말했다. “여기 있습니다요—여기 열쇠가 있습니다요. 물론 저는 미리 통고해 주십사 할 것도 없고, 이번 주 삯도 바라지 않습니다요. 밤이 되기 전에 오두막에서 나가겠습니다요. 그리고 구빈원으로 말하자면, 나리께서 한 줄 적어 주시기만 하면 곧바로 들여보내 주겠지요.”\n\n그런데 홉킨스가 삼사백 파운드를 가진 사람이라는 것은 지주도 익히 아는 바였으니, 구빈원 이야기는 지나친 연극이었다고 해 두어야겠다.\n\n“바보 같은 소리 말게.” 지주가 이를 갈다시피 하며 말했다.\n\n“제가 바보짓을 한 건 저도 압니다요.” 홉킨스가 말했다. “그 두엄 일로 말입지요. 제 속에 있는 것이 저한테는 너무 벅찼습니다요. 사람이 제 속에 있는 것을 이기지 못할 지경이 되면, 그저 제 발로 물러나서 죽을 때까지 구빈원에 누워 있는 편이 낫습지요.” 그러고서 그는 다시 열쇠를 내밀었다. 그러나 지주가 열쇠를 받지 않자 홉킨스는 말을 이었다. “아무래도 제가 유리덮개며 그런 것들이나 보아 드리는 게 낫겠습니다요. 데일 어르신께서 사람을 구하실 때까지 말입지요. 저 포도가 다 못쓰게 되면 딱한 일 아니겠습니까요. 이제는 말하자면 죄다 상에 올려도 될 만큼 익었는데 말입니다요. 제가 여태 본 중에 단연 제일 좋은 수확입니다요. 제가 저것들한테 어찌나 마음을 쓰는지 이월 이래로 편히 잠들어 본 밤이 하루도 없습니다요. 이 댁 언저리에는 포도를 아는 사람이 아무도 없고, 가까이 어디에도 데려올 만한 사람이 없습니다요. 백작님 댁 우두머리 정원사는 아주 무식합지요. 하지만 그자가 아무리 잘 안다 한들 물론 여기로 올 수야 없는 노릇이고요. 아무래도 사람을 구하실 때까지는 제가 열쇠를 갖고 있는 게 낫겠습니다요, 데일 어르신.”\n\n그러고서 두 주 동안 정원에는 주인 없는 시절이 이어졌으니, 앨링턴의 연대기에 길이 남을 끔찍한 일이었다. 홉킨스는 그야 제 오두막에 살면서 포도를 더없이 부지런히 돌보았다. 포도를 돌보다 보니 온실들도 제 손으로 맡게 되었다. 그러나 바깥 정원 일에는 손끝 하나 대려 하지 않았고, 삯도 받지 않아 보내온 것을 지주에게 도로 돌려보냈으며, 자기는 쫓겨난 것이라고 누구에게나 우겼다. 그는 늘 무슨 무시무시한 원예 연장을 손에 들고 돌아다녔는데, 그것으로 졸리프를 덮칠 작정이라는 말이 돌았다. 그러나 졸리프는 조심스럽게 그의 앞길을 피해 다녔다.\n\n앨링턴의 작은 집에서 이사하는 일을 그만두기로 데일 부인과 릴리가 정하자마자, 릴리는 그 사실을 홉킨스에게 알렸다.\n\n“아가씨.” 그가 말했다. “제가 아가씨와 마님께 그 몇 마디를 드렸을 때, 저는 아가씨가 사리에 귀를 기울이시리라는 걸 알았습니다요.”\n\n이것은 릴리가 예상한 그대로였다. 홉킨스가 제 주장으로 일을 이겨 냈다는 영예를 제 몫으로 내세우리라는 것은 당연한 일이었다.\n\n“그래요.” 릴리가 말했다. “우리는 남기로 마음을 정했어요. 삼촌이 그러기를 바라시니까요.”\n\n“바라시다니요! 아이고, 아가씨. 바라시는 정도가 아닙지요. 그리고 우리 모두가 바라고 있습니다요. 아니, 자, 이 일의 이치를 좀 보십쇼. 여기 이 집으로 말할 것 같으면—”\n\n“그렇지만 홉킨스, 다 정해진 일이에요. 우리는 남을 거예요. 내가 알고 싶은 건 이거예요. 지금 당장 와서 짐 푸는 걸 도와줄 수 있어요?”\n\n“아니! 바로 오늘 저녁에 말입니까요—”\n\n“그래요, 지금요. 그분들이 게스트윅에서 돌아오기 전에 물건들을 도로 제자리에 놓고 싶어요.”\n\n홉킨스는 머리를 긁적이며 망설였다. 어린애 같다고 여겨질 만한 제안에 넘어가고 싶지는 않았던 것이다. 그러나 그 일 자체가 좋은 일임을 느끼고 마침내 물러섰다. 데일 부인도 그러라고 하면서 릴리의 어리석음을 두고 웃었고, 이렇게 해서 짐은 아주 빠르게 풀렸으며 릴리와 홉킨스 사이는 그동안 아주 가까워졌다. 이 짐을 풀고 다시 자리를 잡는 일이 채 끝나기도 전에 두엄 싸움이 터졌고, 그래서 홉킨스는 “그 두엄 일로” 제 속에 있는 것이 아주 벅차 이길 수 없게 되자 마침내 릴리에게 찾아와, 예순 몇 해 인생의 온갖 무게와 온갖 영광을 그녀의 발치에 내려놓고서 제 일을 바로잡아 달라고 애원했던 것이다. “저는 그것 때문에 죽을 지경입니다요, 아가씨. 정말입니다요. 저것들이 그 아스파라거스를 잘라 내는 꼴을 보면 말입니다요. 그건 자르는 것도 아닙지요. 그냥 마구 파헤치는 겁니다요—될 것 안 될 것 죄다 한꺼번에 말입니다요. 게다가 제가 심으려던 자리가 아닌 데다 모종을 넣어 놓았습니다요. 제가 거기 심으려는 게 아니라는 걸 뻔히 알면서도요. 저는 옆에 서서, 아가씨, 한마디도 하지 않았습니다요. 차라리 죽는 편이 나았지요. 하지만 릴리 아가씨, 그—아가씨도 아시는 그것 때문에 제 속에 있는 것이 저를 이겨 버려서 제가 겪은 고생이 어떠했는지는 아무도 모를 겁니다요—아무도—아무도—아무도.” 그러고서 홉킨스는 돌아서서 울었다.\n\n“삼촌.” 릴리가 그의 의자에 바싹 다가붙으며 말했다. “큰 부탁을 하나 드리고 싶어요.”\n\n“큰 부탁이라. 글쎄, 지금 같아서는 너한테 무엇이든 거절하지는 못할 것 같구나. 백작을 또 한 사람 이 집에 부르자는 건 아니겠지—그렇지?”\n\n“백작을 또 한 사람이라니요!” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 못 들었느냐? 오늘 아침에 벨 아가씨가 여기 와서, 드 게스트 경과 그 누이를 혼례에 불러야 한다고 우겨 대더구나. 벨과 레이디 줄리아 사이에 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있었던 모양이야.”\n\n“당연히 부르셔야죠.”\n\n“당연히 그래야지. 빠져나갈 길이 없구나. 벨이야 제 연인과 웨일스로 떠나 버릴 테니 다 좋겠지. 그런데 그 애들이 가고 나면 나는 백작과 레이디 줄리아를 데리고 무엇을 해야 하느냐? 네가 와서 나를 좀 도와주겠느냐?”\n\n이에 대해 릴리는 물론 와서 돕겠노라고 약속했다. “아니.” 그녀가 말했다. “저는 우리가 다 그날 하루 초대받은 줄 알았는데요. 자, 이제 제 부탁이에요. 삼촌, 딱한 홉킨스를 용서해 주셔야 해요.”\n\n“용서는 무슨 놈의 용서!” 지주가 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 그래도 하셔야 해요. 그이가 얼마나 불행한지 삼촌은 모르실 거예요.”\n\n“나를 용서하지 않는 사람을 내가 어떻게 용서하느냐. 그자는 아무 일도 안 하면서 온 데를 어슬렁거리고 다녀. 게다가 제 삯을 나한테 도로 보내고, 누구라도 죽일 것 같은 낯짝을 하고 다니지. 그게 다 시키는 대로 하지 않으려 해서 생긴 일이야. 그런 사람을 내가 어떻게 용서한단 말이냐?”\n\n“그렇지만 삼촌, 왜 못 하세요?”\n\n“그건 그자가 나를 용서하는 게 될 게다. 그자는 저 오고 싶을 때 언제든 돌아와도 된다는 걸 아주 잘 알아. 게다가 사실을 말하자면 그자는 여태 나간 적도 없어.”\n\n“그렇지만 그이가 아주 불행하잖아요.”\n\n“그자를 더 기쁘게 해 주려면 내가 무엇을 해야 하느냐?”\n\n“그냥 그이 오두막에 내려가셔서 용서한다고 말씀해 주세요.”\n\n“그러면 나한테 따지고 들 게다.”\n\n“아니에요, 그러지 않을 거예요. 지금은 너무 풀이 죽어 있어서 따질 처지가 아니에요.”\n\n“허! 너는 나만큼 그자를 알지 못한다. 세상의 온갖 불행이 다 닥친다 해도 그 사람의 자만심은 꺾이지 않아. 물론 네가 청하니 가기야 가겠다만, 나는 아무한테나 다 굽히라고 생겨난 사람 같구나. 남들 속마음 이야기는 실컷 듣는데, 내 속마음을 그리 많이 헤아려 주는 것 같지는 않으니 말이다.” 그는 마냥 기분이 좋은 상태는 아니었고, 릴리는 괜히 고집을 부렸나 하고 뉘우칠 뻔했다. 그러나 그녀는 끝내 그를 이끌어 정원을 가로질러 오두막까지 데려가는 데 성공했고, 함께 가는 동안 그녀는 언제나—언제나 삼촌을 생각하겠노라고 약속했다. 홉킨스와의 그 장면은 이제 와서 옮길 수가 없다. 얼마 남지 않은 우리의 지면을 너무 많이 잡아먹을 터이기 때문이다. 그 결과는, 미안하지만 고백하건대, 지주에게 서로 용서하기로 하는 조약 이상의 아무런 승리도 되지 못했다. 홉킨스는 스스로를 몹시 나무라며 제 속에 있는 것이 저한테 너무 벅찼노라고 인정했다. 하지만 그가 그토록 부아가 난 사정도 좀 보아 달라는 것이었다! 그는 그 문제에서 혀를 거두지 못했고, 제 죄를 고백하는 만큼이나 제 변명도 실컷 늘어놓았다. 실속 있는 승리는 온전히 그의 것이었으니, 그 뒤로 다시는 아무도 감히 농장 마당에서 벌이는 그의 일에 간섭하지 못했기 때문이다. 그가 주인에게 복종한다는 뜻을 내보인 것이라고는 놀고 지낸 두 주 치 삯을 받겠다고 승낙한 것이 고작이었다.\n\n이 자잘한 사건 덕분에 릴리는 계획이 바뀐 그 문제로 홉킨스에게 시달릴 줄 알았던 것만큼은 시달리지 않았다. 그러나 이 구원은 헌 부인에게도, 크럼프 부인에게도, 무엇보다 보이스 부인에게까지는 미치지 않았다. 그들은 저마다 정도의 차이는 있으나 홉킨스 소동에 관심을 두었다. 그러나 작은 집에 누가 사느냐에 대한 관심은 훨씬 더 컸으니, 헌 부인이 그 집을 바깥만이 아니라 안까지 칠하느냐를 묻고 싶어 잔뜩 부풀어 있는 판에 정원사가 삯을 한사코 마다한다는 이야기로 그녀를 따돌리려 해 봐야 소용이 없었다. “아이고.” 그녀가 말했다. “나도 게스트윅에 셋집이나 보러 가서 내 침대나 몇 개 싸 두어야겠어요.” 릴리는 여기에 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 이것이 자기가 각오했던 벌의 한 부분이라고 느낀 것이다. “저런, 저런.” 크럼프 부인이 두 아가씨에게 말했다. “하기야 그렇지요, 아가씨들이 없으면 우리야 쓸쓸했을 테고, 아가씨들 자리에 더 나쁜 사람이 들어왔을지도 모르지요. 그렇지만 어째서 물건들을 그 큰 상자에 죄다 잡아매어 놓으셨답니까요, 그걸 다시 죄다 끌러 내시려고 말입니다요?”\n\n“우리는 마음을 바꾼 거예요, 크럼프 부인.” 벨이 조금 매섭게 말했다.\n\n“예에, 마음들을 바꾸신 거야 저도 압니다요. 그야 뭐 아가씨들 같은 분들이야 그래도 아무 탈이 없겠지요. 그렇지만 우리 같은 것들이 마음들을 바꿨다가는 소리깨나 듣습지요.”\n\n“그건 우리도 마찬가지인 모양인데요!” 릴리가 말했다. “하지만 신경 쓰지 마세요, 크럼프 부인. 편지나 일찍 올려 보내 주시면 우리도 다투지 않을게요.”\n\n“어이구, 편지요! 편지 따위 염병할. 그런 것이 아예 없었으면 좋겠습니다요. 어제는 여기 웬 사내가 와서 건방을 떨었지 뭡니까요. 어디서 온 것인지도 모르겠고—런던에서 내려온 모양입디다요. 그런데 이것도 잘못됐다, 저것도 잘못됐다, 죄다 잘못됐다 하더니, 나중에는 나를 이 직에서 쫓아내겠다고 하더군입쇼.”\n\n“어머나, 크럼프 부인. 그건 절대로 안 되죠.”\n\n“이 직에서 쫓아낸다굽쇼! 하루에 이 펜스하고 한 파딩을 받는데요. 그래서 제가 그자더러 제 발로나 나가라고, 그 낡은 꾸러미며 물건들이나 죄다 어깨에 지고 가라고 해 주었습니다요. 편지가 다 뭡니까요! 우체국장한테 하루 이 펜스 한 파딩보다 더 못 주겠다면 대체 무슨 볼일이 있다는 말입니까요?” 이렇게 해서, 저를 찾아온 감독관을 향한 크럼프 부인의 노여움이 폭풍처럼 몰아치는 그 그늘에 숨어, 릴리와 벨은 제 머리 위로 쏟아졌을 많은 것을 모면했다. 그러나 보이스 부인은 아직 남아 있었다. 여기에 덧붙여 두거니와, 크럼프 부인의 이야기를 갈 수 있는 데까지 끌고 가 두자면, 그녀는 “이 직에서 쫓겨나지” 않았고 지금도 나라에서 하루 이 펜스 한 파딩을 받고 있다. “고약한 늙은이로군.” 감독관이 제 마차를 모는 사내에게 말했다. “그렇습니다요, 나리. 그 여자에 대해서는 다들 똑같이 말합니다요. 크럼프 부인한테서 재미 보는 사람은 하나도 없습죠.”\n\n벨과 릴리는 보이스 부인네에도 함께 갔다. “그 부인이 아주 밉살스럽게 굴면 나는 언니 혼인 이야기를 기어이 꺼낼 거야.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“나야 조금도 반대하지 않아.” 벨이 말했다. “다만 그 얘기에 무슨 할 말이 있을지 모르겠구나. 의사와 혼인하는 건 아주 평범한 일이잖니.”\n\n“목사와 혼인하는 것보다 조금도 더 평범하지 않은데.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“아니야, 더 평범해. 목사의 혼례는 흔히 아주 성대한 일이 되지. 고을의 유지들 축에 끼어드니까. 그게 그 사람들의 인생 운이야. 의사는 절대 그렇지 못하고, 법률가도 마찬가지야. 법률가는 시골에서 혼인하는 법이 아예 없는 것 같아. 그건 런던에 올라가서 하기로 되어 있나 봐. 하지만 시골 의사의 혼례란 두고두고 이야깃거리가 될 만한 것이 못 돼.”\n\n보이스 부인도 이 문제를 그렇게 보았던 모양이니, 다가오는 혼례를 첫 화젯거리로 고르지 않은 것을 보면 그렇다. 두 아가씨가 자리에 앉자마자 그녀는 곧장 집 이야기로 날아가, 계획이 갑작스레 바뀐 데 대한 자기의 크나큰 놀라움을—놀라움과 아울러 기쁨도—늘어놓기 시작했다. “그편이 훨씬 좋지요, 아시다시피.” 그녀가 말했다. “친척끼리는 일이 화기애애해야 하는 법이니까요.”\n\n“우리는 늘 그럭저럭 화기애애했는걸요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“아, 그럼요. 그건 저도 알아요. 아가씨들이 삼촌하고 함께 있는 걸 보는 게 참 흐뭇하다고 저는 늘 말해 왔는걸요. 그런데 아가씨들이 다 떠나야 한다는 이야기를 들었을 때는—”\n\n“하지만 우리는 떠나야 했던 게 아니에요, 보이스 부인. 엄마가 게스트윅에서 더 편안하시리라고 생각해서 떠나려던 것이었어요. 그리고 이제 떠나지 않는 건 우리 모두가 크럼프 부인 말마따나 ‘마음들을 바꾸었기’ 때문이고요.”\n\n“그런데 집을 칠한다는 게 사실인가요?” 보이스 부인이 물었다.\n\n“사실인 것 같아요.” 릴리가 말했다.\n\n“안팎을 다요?”\n\n“언젠가는 해야 하는 일이잖아요.” 벨이 말했다.\n\n“그럼요, 그렇고말고요. 하지만 지주 어른이 후하시다는 말은 해야겠네요. 아가씨들 집에는 나무로 된 데가 어찌나 많은지. 저야 교회 재산 위원회에서 우리 집도 칠해 주었으면 싶지만, 성직자를 위해서는 아무도 아무것도 해 주지 않지요. 아가씨들이 남게 되어 저는 정말 기뻐요. 제가 보이스 씨한테도 말했지만, 아가씨들 없이 우리가 대체 어떻게 지냈겠어요? 저는 지주 어른이 그 집을 세놓지 않으시겠다고 마음을 굳히셨던 것 같아요.”\n\n“그 집을 세놓으신 적은 여태 한 번도 없는 것 같은데요.”\n\n“그리고 그 안에 아무도 없으면 죄다 허물어져 못쓰게 되지 않겠어요? 어머니께서 게스트윅에 얻어 놓으셨던 셋집에는 삯을 좀 무셔야 했나요?”\n\n“글쎄요, 정말 모르겠어요. 그건 크로프츠 선생이 정리하셨으니 저보다 언니가 더 잘 말해 드릴 수 있을 거예요. 크로프츠 선생은 언니한테 뭐든 다 말씀하시는 것 같으니까요.” 그리하여 화제는 바뀌었고, 보이스 부인은 더 캘 만한 수수께끼가 남아 있다 한들 그날은 그것이 풀리지 않으리라는 것을 알아차리게 되었다.\n\n크로프츠 선생과 벨은 유월 중순쯤에 혼인하기로 정해졌고, 지주는 그날을 위해 제 집을 열어 힘닿는 대로 예식에 격을 더해 주기로 마음먹었다. 드 게스트 경과 레이디 줄리아는 앞서 설명한 대로 레이디 줄리아와 벨 사이의 특별한 주선으로 초대되었다. 대령도 레이디 패니와 함께 그날을 위해 토키에서 올라왔는데, 대령이 제 아버지의 지붕 아래를 찾은 것은 여러 해 만에 처음이었다. 버나드는 아버지를 따라오지 않았다. 그는 아직 외국으로 떠나지 않았으나, 그로서는 그 혼례 자리가 편치 않으리라고 느낄 만한 사정이 있었다. 예식은 보이스 씨가 집전했고, 《카운티 크로니클》이 아주 소상히 적어 둔 대로, 전에 케임브리지 지저스 칼리지에 있었고 지금은 게스트윅 노스게이트 세인트피터 교회의 부목사인 문학 석사 존 조지프 존스 목사가 이를 도왔다. 이 자그마한 광고의 흠이라면 이런 것이었다—그 신문의 독자 가운데 존 조지프 존스 목사를 넘어서까지 참고 읽어 낸 사람이 하나도 없었던 탓에, 벨이 크로프츠 선생과 혼인했다는 사실은 바랐던 만큼 널리 퍼지지 못했다는 것이다.\n\n혼례는 아주 좋게 치러졌다. 지주는 더할 나위 없이 훌륭하게 처신했고, 손님들이 제 집 넓은 방에 와 있는 것을 정말로 기뻐하는 사람처럼 그들을 맞았다. 자기가 이겼다는 것을 잘 알고 있는 홉킨스는 꽃과 푸른 잎을 섞어 옛 방들을 꾸몄는데, 그 정성스러운 손길이 두 아가씨를 여간 기쁘게 하지 않았다. 그리고 이 화환을 엮고 장식을 다는 일이 이어지는 동안 자그마한 마음 한 조각이 드러났으니, 이 마지막 줄들에 적어 두어도 좋으리라. 벨이 잠시 자리를 비운 사이 릴리가 그 늙은이에게 힘을 북돋아 주고 있었다.\n\n“이게 아가씨를 위한 것이었더라면 좋았을 것을, 우리 아가씨!” 그가 말했다. “이게 아가씨를 위한 것이었더라면 좋았을 것을!”\n\n“이대로가 훨씬 좋아요, 홉킨스.” 그녀가 엄숙하게 대답했다.\n\n“그래도 그 사람하고는 아니었습지요.” 그가 말을 이었다. “그 사람하고는 아닙니다요. 저라면 그 사람을 위해서는 나뭇가지 하나 걸어 주지 않았을 겁니다요. 하지만 그 다른 분하고라면요.”\n\n릴리는 더 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 그 사람이 제 둘레에 있는 모든 이의 바람을 말하고 있다는 것을 알았다. 그녀는 더 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 그러고서 벨이 그들에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n그러나 그 혼례에서 릴리만큼 흥겨운 사람은 없었다—그만큼 흥겹고, 그만큼 환하고, 그만큼 혼례다운 사람은 없었다. 그녀가 그 늙은 백작에게 어찌나 살갑게 굴었던지 백작은 제가 그녀와 혼인하겠노라고 선언할 지경이었다. 그날 저녁 그녀를 보고도 그녀의 가까운 지난 일을 모르는 사람이라면, 그녀 자신이 예닐곱 달 아니면 여덟 달 전에 모질게 버림받았으리라고는 짐작도 못 했을 것이다. 그리고 그녀를 아는 사람들은, 그때 그녀가 겪은 일이 그녀를 그토록 세게 때려 이제 그녀에게 회복이란 있을 성싶지 않다는 것을 도무지 그려 볼 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀 자신이 믿기로는 회복이란 없었다 하더라도—그녀는 싸움터에서 오른팔을 잃은 사람과 같았다 하더라도—온 세상이 그 오른팔과 함께 사라져 버린 것은 아니었다. 그녀에게 모진 상처를 입힌 총알이 그녀의 목숨까지 건드리지는 않았고, 그녀는 제가 입은 상처를 말로든 낯빛으로든 하소연하며 세상을 돌아다니는 짓을 떳떳하게 여기지 않았다. “남편을 잃은 아내들도 여전히 먹고 웃는 법이지.” 그녀는 속으로 말했다. “게다가 그이는 그렇게 죽은 것도 아니잖아.” 그리하여 그녀는 행복하게 지내리라 마음먹었고, 나는 여기서 그녀가 그 결심을 지켜 낸 듯 보였을 뿐 아니라 참으로 지켜 냈다고 밝혀 둔다. “선생님은 참 좋으신 분이고, 언니한테 잘해 주시리라는 걸 저는 알아요.” 크로프츠가 막 신부를 데리고 떠나려 할 때 그녀가 그에게 말했다.\n\n“어쨌든 애써 보지요.” 그가 대답했다.\n\n“그리고 저한테도 잘해 주셔야 해요. 선생님은 우리 집안 전체와 혼인하셨다는 걸 잊지 마세요. 그리고 선생님, 그 못된 사람이 제 소설들에서 장모에 대해 하는 말은 한마디도 믿으시면 안 돼요. 그 사람은 아주 큰 해악을 끼쳐서, 잉글랜드의 부인네 절반을 제 딸의 집에서 몰아내 버렸다니까요.”\n\n“그 사람이 데일 부인을 제 집에서 몰아내지는 못할 겁니다.”\n\n“꼭 그러지 못하게 하셔야 해요. 자, 이제 안녕히 가세요.” 그리하여 신부와 신랑은 떠났고, 릴리는 남아서 드 게스트 경에게 살갑게 굴었다.\n\n지친 펜을 손에서 떨구도록 내버려 두기 전에, 또 누구에 대해 한두 마디 해 두어야 할까? 지주는 그 문제로 속으로 많이 씨름한 끝에, 제수가 제게서 마땅히 받았어야 할 다정함을 받지 못했다는 것을 스스로 인정했다. 그는 이것을 인정하면서 지난날의 제 잘못을 힘써 고쳐 보겠노라고 마음먹었고, 그 방면의 그의 노력이 데일 부인에게 곱게 받아들여지지 않을 리는 없으리라고 나는 생각한다. 그러므로 앨링턴의 삶은, 큰 집에서나 작은 집에서나, 머지않아 지난날보다 한결 즐거워지리라고 나는 믿고 싶다. 릴리는 곧 발모럴 부츠를 손에 넣었다. 아니, 적어도 그것을 제 마음대로 살 수 있는 힘이 삼촌의 선물로 제게 넘어왔다는 것을 곧 알게 되었다. 그래서 그녀는 다람쥐 우리까지 사겠다는 말도 했지만, 그녀의 사치가 거기까지 이르렀다는 이야기는 나도 알지 못한다.\n\n드 코시 백작은 코시 성에서 통풍과 심술로 지독히 앓고 있는 채로 우리가 두고 왔다. 그렇다, 정말이지! 그의 말년에 삶은 그에게 편안한 것을 그리 많이 내어 주지 않았다. 아내는 이제 그를 떠나 버렸고, 사위에게 이 세상 무엇을 준다 해도 다시는 돌아가지 않겠노라고 딱 잘라 선언했다—“내가 굶어 죽는 한이 있어도 말이오!” 그녀는 말했다. 이 말로 그녀가 뜻한 바는, 그 때문에 제 마차와 말들을 잃게 된다 하더라도 제 결심을 굳게 지키겠다는 것이었다. 딱한 게이지비 씨가 코시로 내려가 백작과 지독한 담판을 벌였다. 그러나 일은 마침내 정리되었고, 백작부인은 바덴바덴에 반쯤 굶주린 채로 남았다. 그러니까, 마차에 매는 말이 한 필밖에 없었다는 말이다.\n\n크로즈비로 말하자면, 나는 그가 제 관청에서 다시 권세를 되찾았으리라고 믿는 쪽이다. 그는 버터웰 씨의 상관이었고, 옵티미스트 씨와 소령의 상관이기도 했다. 그는 제 일을 알았고 또 해낼 수 있었으니, 아마 나머지 세 사람 누구에 대해서도 그렇게 말하기는 어려웠을 것이다. 이런 사정이니 그가 일을 제 손에 쥐고 다시 이끌어 가게 되는 것은 뻔한 일이었다. 그러나 다른 데서는 그의 별이 예전의 자리를 되찾지 못했다. 그는 거의 날마다 제 클럽에서 저녁을 먹었고, 거기에는 늘 그와 함께 자그마한 무리를 이루는 사람들이 있었다. 그러나 그는 지난날의 크로즈비가—벨그레이비어와 세인트제임스 스트리트에서 알려졌던 그 크로즈비가 아니었다. 그는 제 작은 배를 씩씩하게 깊은 바다로 몰고 나가, 운이 제 곁에 붙어 있는 동안에는 배를 잘도 몰았다. 그러나 그는 뱃사람의 규칙을 잊었고, 성공이 그를 게으르게 만들었다. 그는 측심줄과 추를 쓰지 않았고 앞을 살피지도 않았다. 그리하여 처음 만난 암초가 그의 배를 산산조각 내고 말았다. 그의 아내 레이디 알렉산드리나는 바덴바덴에서 제 어머니와 함께 말 한 필이 끄는 마차에 앉아 있는 모습을 볼 수 있다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-4599"]=[[["# CHAPTER I. THE SQUIRE OF ALLINGTON.", "# 제1장. 앨링턴의 지주"]], [["Of course there was a Great House at Allington.", "물론 앨링턴에는 큰 집이 있었다."], ["How otherwise should there have been a Small House?", "그렇지 않고서야 어떻게 작은 집이 있을 수 있겠는가?"], ["Our story will, as its name imports, have its closest relations with those who lived in the less dignified domicile of the two; but it will have close relations also with the more dignified, and it may be well that I should, in the first instance, say a few words as to the Great House and its owner.", "우리 이야기는 그 제목이 일러 주는 대로 두 집 가운데 덜 위엄 있는 거처에 살던 이들과 가장 가까운 관계를 맺을 것이다. 그러나 더 위엄 있는 쪽과도 밀접한 관계를 맺을 터이니, 우선은 내가 큰 집과 그 주인에 대해 몇 마디 해 두는 편이 좋겠다."]], [["The squires of Allington had been squires of Allington since squires, such as squires are now, were first known in England.", "앨링턴의 지주들은, 오늘날과 같은 지주라는 것이 잉글랜드에 처음 알려진 이래로 줄곧 앨링턴의 지주였다."], ["From father to son, and from uncle to nephew, and, in one instance, from second cousin to second cousin, the sceptre had descended in the family of the Dales; and the acres had remained intact, growing in value and not decreasing in number, though guarded by no entail and protected by no wonderful amount of prudence or wisdom.", "아버지에게서 아들에게로, 삼촌에게서 조카에게로, 그리고 한 번은 육촌에게서 육촌에게로, 그 홀은 데일 집안 안에서 내려왔다. 토지는 온전한 채로 남아 값이 오르기만 했을 뿐 그 수가 줄지 않았는데, 한정상속으로 묶인 것도 아니었고 대단한 신중함이나 지혜가 지켜 준 것도 아니었다."], ["The estate of Dale of Allington had been coterminous with the parish of Allington for some hundreds of years; and though, as I have said, the race of squires had possessed nothing of superhuman discretion, and had perhaps been guided in their walks through life by no very distinct principles, still there had been with them so much of adherence to a sacred law, that no acre of the property had ever been parted from the hands of the existing squire.", "앨링턴 데일 가의 영지는 수백 년 동안 앨링턴 교구와 경계를 같이해 왔다. 그리고 내가 말했듯이 이 지주 일족이 인간을 넘어서는 분별을 지녔던 것도 아니고, 살아가는 길에서 아주 뚜렷한 원칙의 인도를 받았던 것도 아마 아니겠지만, 그럼에도 그들에게는 신성한 법을 지키는 마음이 어찌나 깊었던지 재산의 단 한 에이커도 당대 지주의 손에서 떨어져 나간 적이 없었다."], ["Some futile attempts had been made to increase the territory, as indeed had been done by Kit Dale, the father of Christopher Dale, who will appear as our squire of Allington when the persons of our drama are introduced.", "영토를 늘리려는 헛된 시도가 몇 번 있기는 했으니, 크리스토퍼 데일의 아버지 킷 데일이 실제로 그런 일을 했다. 크리스토퍼 데일은 우리 극의 인물들이 소개될 때 우리의 앨링턴 지주로 등장할 사람이다."], ["Old Kit Dale, who had married money, had bought outlying farms,--a bit of ground here and a bit there,--talking, as he did so, much of political influence and of the good old Tory cause.", "돈 있는 여자와 결혼했던 늙은 킷 데일은 외곽의 농장들을 사들였다. 여기 한 뙈기 저기 한 뙈기 하는 식이었는데, 그러면서 그는 정치적 영향력이니 좋았던 옛 토리당의 대의니 하는 말을 많이 했다."], ["But these farms and bits of ground had gone again before our time.", "그러나 그 농장들과 땅뙈기들은 우리 시대가 오기 전에 이미 다시 나가 버렸다."], ["To them had been attached no religion.", "거기에는 아무런 신앙도 결부되어 있지 않았던 것이다."], ["When old Kit had found himself pressed in that matter of the majority of the Nineteenth Dragoons, in which crack regiment his second son made for himself quite a career, he found it easier to sell than to save--seeing that that which he sold was his own and not the patrimony of the Dales.", "제십구용기병연대의 소령 자리 문제로 늙은 킷이 몰리게 되었을 때 — 그 정예 연대에서 그의 둘째 아들은 제법 출셋길을 닦고 있었다 — 그는 지키기보다 파는 편이 쉽다는 것을 알았다. 파는 물건이 데일 가의 세습 재산이 아니라 제 것이었으니 그럴 만도 했다."], ["At his death the remainder of these purchases had gone.", "그가 죽자 그렇게 사들인 것의 나머지도 없어졌다."], ["Family arrangements required completion, and Christopher Dale required ready money.", "집안일을 정리하자면 마무리가 필요했고, 크리스토퍼 데일에게는 당장 쓸 현금이 필요했다."], ["The outlying farms flew away, as such new purchases had flown before; but the old patrimony of the Dales remained untouched, as it had ever remained.", "외곽의 농장들은 그렇게 새로 사들인 땅이 전에도 그랬듯 훌훌 날아가 버렸다. 그러나 데일 가의 오래된 세습 재산은 여태 그래 왔듯 손 하나 대지 않은 채 남았다."]], [["It had been a religion among them; and seeing that the worship had been carried on without fail, that the vestal fire had never gone down upon the hearth, I should not have said that the Dales had walked their ways without high principle.", "그것은 그들 사이에서 하나의 신앙이었다. 그리고 그 예배가 한 번도 거르는 일 없이 이어졌음을, 화덕의 성화가 한 번도 꺼진 적이 없음을 생각하면, 데일 가 사람들이 높은 원칙 없이 제 길을 걸었다고 나는 말하지 말았어야 했다."], ["To this religion they had all adhered, and the new heir had ever entered in upon his domain without other encumbrances than those with which he himself was then already burdened.", "이 신앙에는 그들 모두가 매달렸고, 새 상속인은 언제나 자기가 그때 이미 짊어지고 있던 것 말고는 다른 부담 없이 제 영지에 들어섰다."], ["And yet there had been no entail.", "그런데도 한정상속은 없었다."], ["The idea of an entail was not in accordance with the peculiarities of the Dale mind.", "한정상속이라는 발상은 데일 가 특유의 기질과 맞지 않았다."], ["It was necessary to the Dale religion that each squire should have the power of wasting the acres of Allington,--and that he should abstain from wasting them.", "데일 가의 신앙에는 지주마다 앨링턴의 토지를 탕진할 권능을 가질 것, 그러면서도 탕진하기를 삼갈 것이 필요했다."], ["I remember to have dined at a house, the whole glory and fortune of which depended on the safety of a glass goblet.", "나는 어느 집에서 저녁을 먹은 일을 기억한다. 그 집의 온 영광과 재산이 유리잔 하나의 무사함에 달려 있었다."], ["We all know the story.", "그 이야기는 우리 모두가 안다."], ["If the luck of Edenhall should be shattered, the doom of the family would be sealed.", "에덴홀의 행운이 깨어지면 그 집안의 운명도 끝장이라는 것이다."], ["Nevertheless I was bidden to drink out of the fatal glass, as were all guests in that house.", "그런데도 그 집의 모든 손님이 그러했듯 나 또한 그 운명의 잔으로 마시라는 권을 받았다."], ["It would not have contented the chivalrous mind of the master to protect his doom by lock and key and padded chest.", "자물쇠와 열쇠와 솜을 댄 궤짝으로 제 운명을 지키는 것은 주인의 기사다운 마음에 흡족하지 않았으리라."], ["And so it was with the Dales of Allington.", "앨링턴의 데일 가도 그러했다."], ["To them an entail would have been a lock and key and a padded chest; but the old chivalry of their house denied to them the use of such protection.", "그들에게 한정상속이란 자물쇠요 열쇠요 솜을 댄 궤짝이었을 텐데, 집안의 오랜 기사도가 그런 보호물을 쓰는 것을 그들에게 허락하지 않았다."]], [["I have spoken something slightingly of the acquirements and doings of the family; and indeed their acquirements had been few and their doings little.", "나는 이 집안의 재주와 행적을 얼마간 대수롭지 않게 말했거니와, 실제로 그들의 재주는 적었고 행적은 보잘것없었다."], ["At Allington, Dale of Allington had always been known as a king.", "앨링턴에서 앨링턴의 데일 어른은 언제나 왕으로 통했다."], ["At Guestwick, the neighbouring market town, he was a great man--to be seen frequently on Saturdays, standing in the market-place, and laying down the law as to barley and oxen among men who knew usually more about barley and oxen than did he.", "이웃한 장터 마을 게스트윅에서 그는 대단한 인물이었다. 토요일이면 장터에 서서 보리와 소에 대해, 보통 자기보다 그것들을 더 잘 아는 사람들 앞에서 법을 늘어놓는 모습을 자주 볼 수 있었다."], ["At Hamersham, the assize town, he was generally in some repute, being a constant grand juror for the county, and a man who paid his way.", "순회재판이 열리는 고을 해머샴에서도 그는 대체로 얼마간 평판이 있었으니, 주의 대배심원을 늘 맡았고 제 빚을 갚고 사는 사람이었기 때문이다."], ["But even at Hamersham the glory of the Dales had, at most periods, begun to pale, for they had seldom been widely conspicuous in the county, and had earned no great reputation by their knowledge of jurisprudence in the grand jury room.", "그러나 해머샴에서조차 데일 가의 영광은 대개의 시기에 이미 빛이 바래기 시작한 터였다. 그들이 주 안에서 널리 두드러진 적이 좀처럼 없었던 데다, 대배심원실에서 법률 지식으로 큰 명성을 얻은 일도 없었기 때문이다."], ["Beyond Hamersham their fame had not spread itself.", "해머샴 너머로는 그들의 이름이 퍼지지 않았다."]], [["They had been men generally built in the same mould, inheriting each from his father the same virtues and the same vices,--men who would have lived, each, as his father had lived before him, had not the new ways of the world gradually drawn away with them, by an invisible magnetism, the upcoming Dale of the day,--not indeed in any case so moving him as to bring him up to the spirit of the age in which he lived, but dragging him forward to a line in advance of that on which his father had trodden. They had been obstinate men; believing much in themselves; just according to their ideas of justice; hard to their tenants--but not known to be hard even by the tenants themselves, for the rules followed had ever been the rules on the Allington estate; imperious to their wives and children, but imperious within bounds, so that no Mrs. Dale had fled from her lord's roof, and no loud scandals had existed between father and sons; exacting in their ideas as to money, expecting that they were to receive much and to give little, and yet not thought to be mean, for they paid their way, and gave money in parish charity and in county charity. They had ever been steady supporters of the Church, graciously receiving into their parish such new vicars as, from time to time, were sent to them from King's College, Cambridge, to which establishment the gift of the living belonged;--but, nevertheless, the Dales had ever carried on some unpronounced warfare against the clergyman, so that the intercourse between the lay family and the clerical had seldom been in all respects pleasant.", "그들은 대체로 같은 틀에서 나온 사람들이었고, 저마다 아버지에게서 같은 미덕과 같은 악덕을 물려받았다. 세상의 새 풍조가 눈에 보이지 않는 자력으로 그때그때의 신진 데일을 조금씩 끌어가지만 않았다면, 저마다 제 아버지가 살았던 그대로 살았을 사람들이었다. 그 힘이 어느 경우에도 그를 당대의 정신에까지 끌어올릴 만큼 움직인 것은 아니었으나, 아버지가 밟았던 자리보다는 한 발 앞선 선까지 그를 끌고 나가기는 했다. 그들은 고집 센 사람들이었다. 스스로를 크게 믿었고, 저희 나름의 정의 관념에 비추어서는 공정했으며, 소작인들에게 모질었으나 정작 소작인들 자신은 그것을 모질다고 여기지 않았으니 지켜지는 규칙이 언제나 앨링턴 영지의 규칙이었기 때문이다. 아내와 자식들에게 오만했으되 그 오만함에는 한도가 있어서, 지아비의 지붕 아래에서 달아난 데일 부인은 하나도 없었고 아버지와 아들 사이에 요란한 추문이 인 적도 없었다. 돈에 관해서는 깐깐하여 많이 받고 적게 주기를 바랐으나 그렇다고 인색하다는 말을 듣지는 않았으니, 제 빚을 갚고 살았고 교구 자선과 주 자선에 돈을 냈기 때문이다. 그들은 언제나 교회를 한결같이 받쳐 왔고, 목사 임명권이 딸린 케임브리지 킹스 칼리지에서 때때로 보내오는 새 교구 목사들을 제 교구에 정중히 맞아들였다. 그럼에도 데일 가는 늘 성직자를 상대로 선포되지 않은 전쟁을 벌였으므로, 속가와 성직자 집안 사이가 모든 면에서 유쾌했던 적은 좀처럼 없었다."]], [["Such had been the Dales of Allington, time out of mind, and such in all respects would have been the Christopher Dale of our time, had he not suffered two accidents in his youth.", "앨링턴의 데일 가는 아득한 옛날부터 그러했고, 우리 시대의 크리스토퍼 데일도 젊은 시절에 두 번의 사고를 겪지만 않았다면 모든 면에서 그러했을 것이다."], ["He had fallen in love with a lady who obstinately refused his hand, and on her account he had remained single; that was his first accident.", "그는 한 여인과 사랑에 빠졌는데 그 여인이 한사코 그의 손을 마다했고, 그 때문에 그는 홀몸으로 남았다. 그것이 첫 번째 사고였다."], ["The second had fallen upon him with reference to his father's assumed wealth.", "두 번째는 아버지의 재산이 넉넉하리라 짐작한 데서 그에게 닥쳤다."], ["He had supposed himself to be richer than other Dales of Allington when coming in upon his property, and had consequently entertained an idea of sitting in Parliament for his county.", "그는 재산을 물려받으며 자기가 앨링턴의 다른 데일들보다 부자라고 생각했고, 그래서 제 주를 대표해 의회에 앉겠다는 생각을 품었다."], ["In order that he might attain this honour he had allowed himself to be talked by the men of Hamersham and Guestwick out of his old family politics, and had declared himself a Liberal.", "그 명예를 얻기 위해 그는 해머샴과 게스트윅 사람들의 말에 넘어가 집안 대대의 정견을 버렸고, 스스로를 자유당원이라 선언하기에 이르렀다."], ["He had never gone to the poll, and, indeed, had never actually stood for the seat.", "그는 투표소까지 간 적이 없고, 실은 그 의석에 실제로 출마한 적조차 없다."], ["But he had come forward as a liberal politician, and had failed; and, although it was well known to all around that Christopher Dale was in heart as thoroughly conservative as any of his forefathers, this accident had made him sour and silent on the subject of politics, and had somewhat estranged him from his brother squires.", "그러나 자유주의 정치가로 나섰다가 실패했다. 그리고 크리스토퍼 데일이 마음속으로는 그 어느 조상 못지않게 철저한 보수주의자임을 주변 사람들이 다 알고 있었음에도, 이 사고는 그를 정치 이야기에 대해 시큰둥하고 말 없는 사람으로 만들었으며 이웃 지주들에게서 그를 얼마간 멀어지게 했다."]], [["In other respects our Christopher Dale was, if anything, superior to the average of the family.", "다른 점에서 보면 우리의 크리스토퍼 데일은 굳이 따지자면 집안의 평균보다 나은 사람이었다."], ["Those whom he did love he loved dearly.", "사랑하는 이는 애틋하게 사랑했다."], ["Those whom he hated he did not ill-use beyond the limits of justice.", "미워하는 이라도 정의의 한도를 넘어서까지 함부로 대하지는 않았다."], ["He was close in small matters of money, and yet in certain family arrangements he was, as we shall see, capable of much liberality.", "자잘한 돈 문제에는 인색했으나, 앞으로 보게 되겠지만 어떤 집안일에서는 크게 너그러울 줄도 알았다."], ["He endeavoured to do his duty in accordance with his lights, and had succeeded in weaning himself from personal indulgences, to which during the early days of his high hopes he had become accustomed.", "그는 제가 아는 빛에 따라 제 도리를 다하려 애썼고, 큰 기대에 부풀었던 젊은 시절에 몸에 밴 개인적인 탐닉에서 스스로를 떼어 놓는 데 성공했다."], ["And in that matter of his unrequited love he had been true throughout.", "그리고 보답받지 못한 그 사랑에서 그는 시종 한결같았다."], ["In his hard, dry, unpleasant way he had loved the woman; and when at last he learned to know that she would not have his love, he had been unable to transfer his heart to another.", "그 딱딱하고 메마르고 유쾌하지 못한 방식으로나마 그는 그 여인을 사랑했으며, 마침내 그 여인이 제 사랑을 받아 주지 않으리라는 것을 알게 되었을 때 그는 마음을 다른 사람에게 옮길 수가 없었다."], ["This had happened just at the period of his father's death, and he had endeavoured to console himself with politics, with what fate we have already seen.", "이 일은 바로 그의 아버지가 죽던 무렵에 일어났고, 그는 정치로 스스로를 달래 보려 했다가 어떤 운명을 맞았는지는 우리가 이미 본 대로다. 우리의 크리스토퍼 데일은 한결같고 올곧으며 결코 거짓되지 않은 사람이었다."], ["A constant, upright, and by no means insincere man was our Christopher Dale,--thin and meagre in his mental attributes, by no means even understanding the fulness of a full man, with power of eye-sight very limited in seeing aught which was above him, but yet worthy of regard in that he had realized a path of duty and did endeavour to walk therein.", "정신의 자질로 보면 얄팍하고 빈약하여 온전한 사람의 그 충만함이라는 것을 도무지 이해하지 못했고, 제 머리 위에 있는 것을 알아보는 눈의 힘은 몹시 좁았다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 존중받을 만했으니, 도리의 길을 알아보았고 그 길로 걸으려 애썼기 때문이다."], ["And, moreover, our Mr. Christopher Dale was a gentleman.", "게다가 우리의 크리스토퍼 데일 씨는 신사였다."]], [["Such in character was the squire of Allington, the only regular inhabitant of the Great House.", "성품으로 보면 그러한 사람이 앨링턴의 지주, 큰 집의 유일한 상주자였다."], ["In person, he was a plain, dry man, with short grizzled hair and thick grizzled eyebrows.", "생김새로 보면 그는 수수하고 메마른 남자로, 희끗한 머리는 짧았고 희끗한 눈썹은 두툼했다."], ["Of beard, he had very little, carrying the smallest possible gray whiskers, which hardly fell below the points of his ears.", "수염은 거의 없어서 될 수 있는 대로 작은 잿빛 구레나룻을 길렀는데 귀 끝 아래로는 좀처럼 내려오지 않았다."], ["His eyes were sharp and expressive, and his nose was straight and well formed,--as was also his chin.", "눈은 날카롭고 표정이 풍부했으며, 코는 곧고 잘생겼고 턱도 그러했다."], ["But the nobility of his face was destroyed by a mean mouth with thin lips; and his forehead, which was high and narrow, though it forbad you to take Mr.", "그러나 그 얼굴의 고귀함은 입술이 얇은 옹색한 입 때문에 망가졌다."], ["Dale for a fool, forbad you also to take him for a man of great parts, or of a wide capacity.", "높고 좁은 이마는 데일 씨를 바보로 여기지 못하게 하는 한편, 그를 크게 뛰어난 사람이나 폭넓은 그릇으로 여기지도 못하게 했다."], ["In height, he was about five feet ten; and at the time of our story was as near to seventy as he was to sixty.", "키는 오 피트 십 인치쯤 되었고, 우리 이야기가 벌어지는 무렵에는 예순보다 일흔에 더 가까웠다."], ["But years had treated him very lightly, and he bore few signs of age.", "그러나 세월은 그를 아주 가볍게 다루어서 그에게는 늙은 티가 별로 없었다."], ["Such in person was Christopher Dale, Esq., the squire of Allington, and owner of some three thousand a year, all of which proceeded from the lands of that parish.", "몸으로 보면 그러한 사람이 앨링턴의 지주이자 연 삼천 파운드가량의 소유자였던 크리스토퍼 데일 님이었으니, 그 수입은 모두 그 교구의 토지에서 나왔다."]], [["And now I will speak of the Great House of Allington.", "이제 앨링턴의 큰 집에 대해 이야기하겠다."], ["After all, it was not very great; nor was it surrounded by much of that exquisite nobility of park appurtenance which graces the habitations of most of our old landed proprietors.", "따지고 보면 그리 크지 않았고, 우리네 오래된 지주들 대부분의 거처를 빛내 주는 그 절묘하게 고귀한 사냥터 부속물에 둘러싸인 것도 아니었다."], ["But the house itself was very graceful.", "그러나 집 자체는 무척 우아했다."], ["It had been built in the days of the early Stuarts, in that style of architecture to which we give the name of the Tudors.", "스튜어트 왕조 초기에, 우리가 튜더 양식이라 이름 붙이는 그 건축 양식으로 지어진 것이었다."], ["On its front it showed three pointed roofs, or gables, as I believe they should be called; and between each gable a thin tall chimney stood, the two chimneys thus raising themselves just above the three peaks I have mentioned.", "정면에는 뾰족한 지붕이 셋, 그러니까 박공이라 불러야 마땅할 것이 셋 보였고, 박공과 박공 사이마다 가늘고 높은 굴뚝이 서 있어서 그 두 굴뚝이 앞서 말한 세 봉우리보다 조금 더 높이 솟아 있었다."], ["I think that the beauty of the house depended much on those two chimneys; on them, and on the mullioned windows with which the front of the house was closely filled.", "나는 이 집의 아름다움이 그 두 굴뚝에 크게 기대고 있었다고 생각한다. 그 굴뚝들과, 집 정면을 빽빽이 채운 멀리언 창들에 말이다."], ["The door, with its jutting porch, was by no means in the centre of the house.", "튀어나온 현관이 딸린 문은 결코 집 한가운데 있지 않았다."], ["As you entered, there was but one window on your right hand, while on your left there were three.", "들어서면 오른편에는 창이 하나뿐인데 왼편에는 셋이 있었다."], ["And over these there was a line of five windows, one taking its place above the porch.", "그리고 그 위로는 창 다섯이 한 줄로 늘어서, 그중 하나가 현관 위에 자리를 잡았다."], ["We all know the beautiful old Tudor window, with its stout stone mullions and its stone transoms, crossing from side to side at a point much nearer to the top than to the bottom.", "튼튼한 돌 멀리언과 돌 트랜섬이 아래쪽보다 훨씬 위쪽에 가까운 지점에서 좌우로 가로지르는 그 아름다운 옛 튜더 창을 우리는 다 안다."], ["Of all windows ever invented it is the sweetest.", "여태 고안된 온갖 창 가운데 그것이 가장 어여쁘다."], ["And here, at Allington, I think their beauty was enhanced by the fact that they were not regular in their shape.", "그리고 여기 앨링턴에서는 그 창들이 모양이 고르지 않다는 사실 덕분에 아름다움이 더해졌다고 나는 생각한다."], ["Some of these windows were long windows, while some of them were high.", "그 창들 가운데 어떤 것은 옆으로 길었고 어떤 것은 위로 높았다."], ["That to the right of the door, and that at the other extremity of the house, were among the former.", "문 오른편의 것과 집 반대편 끝의 것은 앞엣것에 속했다."], ["But the others had been put in without regard to uniformity, a long window here, and a high window there, with a general effect which could hardly have been improved.", "그러나 나머지는 획일성을 아랑곳하지 않고 끼워 넣어져서 여기에 긴 창 하나 저기에 높은 창 하나 하는 식이었는데, 전체 효과는 도저히 더 낫게 하기 어려웠다."], ["Then above, in the three gables, were three other smaller apertures.", "그리고 그 위 세 박공에는 더 작은 구멍이 셋 나 있었다."], ["But these also were mullioned, and the entire frontage of the house was uniform in its style.", "그러나 이것들 또한 멀리언 창이었으므로 집 정면 전체가 한 양식으로 통일되어 있었다."]], [["Round the house there were trim gardens, not very large, but worthy of much note in that they were so trim,--gardens with broad gravel paths, with one walk running in front of the house so broad as to be fitly called a terrace.", "집 둘레에는 잘 손질된 정원이 있었다. 아주 넓지는 않았으나 그토록 잘 손질되었다는 점에서 크게 눈여겨볼 만했다."], ["But this, though in front of the house, was sufficiently removed from it to allow of a coach-road running inside it to the front door.", "널찍한 자갈길이 난 정원이었고, 그중 집 앞으로 난 산책길 하나는 어찌나 넓은지 테라스라 불러도 마땅했다. 그러나 이 길은 집 앞에 있으면서도 집에서 넉넉히 떨어져 있어서 그 안쪽으로 마차길이 현관까지 이어질 수 있었다."], ["The Dales of Allington had always been gardeners, and their garden was perhaps more noted in the county than any other of their properties.", "앨링턴의 데일 가는 대대로 정원을 가꾸는 사람들이었고, 그들의 정원은 아마도 그들의 다른 어떤 재산보다 주 안에서 이름이 나 있었다."], ["But outside the gardens no pretensions had been made to the grandeur of a domain.", "그러나 정원 밖으로는 대영지의 위엄을 부리려는 시도가 없었다."], ["The pastures round the house were but pretty fields, in which timber was abundant.", "집 둘레의 목초지는 그저 어여쁜 들판일 뿐이었고 거기에는 나무가 넉넉했다."], ["There was no deer-park at Allington; and though the Allington woods were well known, they formed no portion of a whole of which the house was a part.", "앨링턴에는 사슴 사냥터가 없었다. 앨링턴 숲이 이름나 있기는 했으나, 그 숲은 집이 한 부분을 이루는 어떤 전체의 일부가 아니었다."], ["They lay away, out of sight, a full mile from the back of the house; but not on that account of less avail for the fitting preservation of foxes.", "숲은 집 뒤편에서 족히 일 마일 떨어진 곳에 눈에 띄지 않게 물러나 있었다. 그렇다고 여우를 알맞게 보존하는 데 쓸모가 덜한 것은 아니었다."]], [["And the house stood much too near the road for purposes of grandeur, had such purposes ever swelled the breast of any of the squires of Allington.", "그리고 그 집은 위엄을 부리기에는 길에서 너무 가까이 서 있었다 — 앨링턴의 지주들 가운데 누구라도 그런 뜻으로 가슴이 부풀었던 적이 있다면 말이다."], ["But I fancy that our ideas of rural grandeur have altered since many of our older country seats were built.", "그러나 시골의 위엄이라는 우리 관념은 우리네 오래된 시골 저택이 많이 지어지던 때 이후로 달라진 것이 아닌가 싶다."], ["To be near the village, so as in some way to afford comfort, protection, and patronage, and perhaps also with some view to the pleasantness of neighbourhood for its own inmates, seemed to be the object of a gentleman when building his house in the old days.", "마을 가까이 있어서 어떤 식으로든 편의와 보호와 후원을 베풀 수 있도록, 또 어쩌면 집 안 사람들 자신을 위해서도 이웃이 있는 편이 즐거우리라는 생각에서, 옛날에는 신사가 집을 지을 때 그것을 목표로 삼았던 듯하다."], ["A solitude in the centre of a wide park is now the only site that can be recognized as eligible.", "이제는 널따란 사냥터 한복판의 호젓함만이 알맞은 자리로 인정받는다."], ["No cottage must be seen, unless the cottage orné of the gardener.", "정원사의 아담한 별채가 아니라면 오두막은 하나도 보여서는 안 된다."], ["The village, if it cannot be abolished, must be got out of sight.", "마을은, 없애 버릴 수 없다면 눈에 띄지 않는 곳으로 치워야 한다."], ["The sound of the church bells is not desirable, and the road on which the profane vulgar travel by their own right must be at a distance.", "교회 종소리는 바람직하지 않고, 속된 무리가 제 권리로 다니는 길은 멀찍이 떨어져 있어야 한다."], ["When some old Dale of Allington built his house, he thought differently.", "앨링턴의 어느 옛 데일이 제 집을 지을 때 그는 다르게 생각했다."], ["There stood the church and there the village, and, pleased with such vicinity, he sat himself down close to his God and to his tenants.", "저기에 교회가 있고 저기에 마을이 있으니, 그런 이웃함이 흐뭇하여 그는 제 하느님과 제 소작인들 곁에 바짝 붙어 앉았던 것이다."]], [["As you pass along the road from Guestwick into the village you see the church near to you on your left hand; but the house is hidden from the road.", "게스트윅에서 마을로 들어오는 길을 따라가면 왼편 가까이에 교회가 보이지만 집은 길에서 가려져 있다."], ["As you approach the church, reaching the gate of it which is not above two hundred yards from the high road, you see the full front of the Great House.", "교회로 다가가 한길에서 이백 야드도 채 안 되는 교회 문에 이르면 큰 집의 정면이 온전히 보인다."], ["Perhaps the best view of it is from the churchyard.", "아마도 그 집을 보는 가장 좋은 자리는 교회 묘지일 것이다."], ["The lane leading up to the church ends in a gate, which is the entrance into Mr. Dale's place.", "교회로 올라가는 좁은 길은 문 하나에서 끝나는데, 그것이 데일 씨 저택으로 들어가는 입구다."], ["There is no lodge there, and the gate generally stands open,--indeed, always does so, unless some need of cattle grazing within requires that it should be closed.", "거기에는 문지기 집이 없고 문은 대개 열려 있다. 아니, 안에서 풀을 뜯는 가축 때문에 닫아 두어야 할 일이 없는 한 늘 열려 있다."], ["But there is an inner gate, leading from the home paddock through the gardens to the house, and another inner gate, some thirty yards farther on, which will take you into the farm-yard.", "그러나 안쪽에 또 문이 있어서 안뜰 목장에서 정원을 지나 집으로 이어지고, 거기서 서른 야드쯤 더 가면 또 다른 안쪽 문이 있어 농장 마당으로 데려간다."], ["Perhaps it is a defect at Allington that the farm-yard is very close to the house.", "아마도 농장 마당이 집과 아주 가까운 것은 앨링턴의 흠일 것이다."], ["But the stables, and the straw-yards, and the unwashed carts, and the lazy lingering cattle of the homestead, are screened off by a row of chestnuts, which, when in its glory of flower, in the early days of May, no other row in England can surpass in beauty.", "그러나 마구간과 짚가리 마당과 씻지 않은 수레와 농가의 굼뜬 소들은 밤나무가 늘어선 줄이 가려 준다. 오월 초순 꽃이 한창 필 때면 잉글랜드의 어느 나무줄도 그 아름다움을 넘어서지 못한다."], ["Had any one told Dale of Allington--this Dale or any former Dale--that his place wanted wood, he would have pointed with mingled pride and disdain to his belt of chestnuts.", "앨링턴의 데일 어른에게 — 지금의 데일이든 앞선 어느 데일이든 — 당신 저택에는 나무가 아쉽다고 말하는 사람이 있었다면, 그는 자랑과 경멸이 뒤섞인 낯으로 제 밤나무 띠를 가리켰을 것이다."]], [["Of the church itself I will say the fewest possible number of words.", "교회 자체에 대해서는 될 수 있는 대로 적은 말을 하겠다."], ["It was a church such as there are, I think, thousands in England--low, incommodious, kept with difficulty in repair, too often pervious to the wet, and yet strangely picturesque, and correct too, according to great rules of architecture.", "잉글랜드에 수천은 있으리라 싶은 그런 교회였다. 낮고 비좁고 손보아 가며 간신히 버티고 너무 자주 비가 새었으나, 이상하게도 그림 같았고 건축의 큰 법도로 보아 반듯하기도 했다."], ["It was built with a nave and aisles, visibly in the form of a cross, though with its arms clipped down to the trunk, with a separate chancel, with a large square short tower, and with a bell-shaped spire, covered with lead and irregular in its proportions.", "신랑과 측랑을 갖추어 눈에 보이게 십자가 모양으로 지었으되 그 팔은 몸통에 붙도록 짧게 잘려 있었고, 따로 떨어진 성단소가 있었으며, 크고 네모지고 나지막한 탑과 납을 입힌 종 모양 첨탑이 있었는데 그 비례는 고르지 않았다. 낮은 현관, 수직 양식의 고딕 창, 지붕이 평평한 측랑, 그리고 이런 교회의 그 의젓한 잿빛 옛 탑을 누가 모르겠는가?"], ["Who does not know the low porch, the perpendicular Gothic window, the flat-roofed aisles, and the noble old gray tower of such a church as this?", "내부로 말하자면 먼지투성이였다. 등받이 높은 흉한 신도석들이 자리를 다 막고 있었다."], ["As regards its interior, it was dusty; it was blocked up with high-backed ugly pews; the gallery in which the children sat at the end of the church, and in which two ancient musicians blew their bassoons, was all awry, and looked as though it would fall; the pulpit was an ugly useless edifice, as high nearly as the roof would allow, and the reading-desk under it hardly permitted the parson to keep his head free from the dangling tassels of the cushion above him.", "교회 끝에서 아이들이 앉고 두 늙은 악사가 바순을 부는 회랑은 온통 비뚤어져서 곧 무너질 것만 같았다. 설교단은 흉하고 쓸모없는 구조물로 지붕이 허락하는 만큼 거의 높았고, 그 아래 성서대에서는 목사가 머리 위 방석에 달랑거리는 술 장식에 머리가 걸리지 않게 두기가 힘들 지경이었다."], ["A clerk also was there beneath him, holding a third position somewhat elevated; and upon the whole things there were not quite as I would have had them.", "그 아래에는 서기도 하나 있어서 얼마간 높은 세 번째 자리를 차지했다. 그리고 대체로 그곳의 물건들은 내가 바랐을 모양과 딱히 같지 않았다."], ["But, nevertheless, the place looked like a church, and I can hardly say so much for all the modern edifices which have been built in my days towards the glory of God.", "그러나 그럼에도 그곳은 교회처럼 보였고, 하느님의 영광을 위해 내 시대에 지어진 근래의 건물들 모두에 대해서는 나는 그만큼도 말하기 어렵다."], ["It looked like a church, and not the less so because in walking up the passage between the pews the visitor trod upon the brass plates which dignified the resting-places of the departed Dales of old.", "그곳은 교회처럼 보였으며, 신도석 사이 통로를 걸어 올라가노라면 찾아온 이가 옛 데일 가 사람들이 잠든 자리를 기리는 놋쇠판을 밟게 된다는 사실 때문에 더욱 그러했다."]], [["Below the church, and between that and the village, stood the vicarage, in such position that the small garden of the vicarage stretched from the churchyard down to the backs of the village cottages.", "교회 아래, 교회와 마을 사이에는 목사관이 서 있었는데, 그 자리가 어떠했는가 하면 목사관의 작은 정원이 교회 묘지에서부터 마을 오두막들 뒤편까지 뻗어 있었다."], ["This was a pleasant residence, newly built within the last thirty years, and creditable to the ideas of comfort entertained by the rich collegiate body from which the vicars of Allington always came.", "지난 삼십 년 안에 새로 지은 쾌적한 집으로, 앨링턴의 교구 목사들이 늘 나오던 그 부유한 대학 단체가 지닌 안락함의 관념에 걸맞은 것이었다."], ["Doubtless we shall in the course of our sojourn at Allington visit the vicarage now and then, but I do not know that any further detailed account of its comforts will be necessary to us.", "우리가 앨링턴에 머무는 동안 이따금 목사관을 찾게 되기야 하겠지만, 그 안락함에 대해 더 자세한 설명이 우리에게 필요하리라고는 생각하지 않는다."]], [["Passing by the lane leading to the vicarage, the church and to the house, the high road descends rapidly to a little brook which runs through the village.", "목사관과 교회와 저택으로 이어지는 좁은 길을 지나면 한길은 마을을 가로질러 흐르는 작은 개울까지 가파르게 내려간다."], ["On the right as you descend you will have seen the \"Red Lion,\" and will have seen no other house conspicuous in any way.", "내려가노라면 오른편에 「붉은 사자」가 보였을 것이고, 그 밖에 이렇다 하게 눈에 띄는 집은 보지 못했을 것이다."], ["At the bottom, close to the brook, is the post-office, kept surely by the crossest old woman in all those parts.", "개울 바로 곁 맨 아래에는 우체국이 있는데, 그 근방을 통틀어 가장 심술궂은 노파가 지키고 있음이 틀림없다."], ["Here the road passes through the water, the accommodation of a narrow wooden bridge having been afforded for those on foot.", "여기서 길은 물을 가로질러 지나가고, 걸어가는 사람들을 위해서는 좁은 나무다리라는 편의가 마련되어 있다."], ["But before passing the stream, you will see a cross street, running to the left, as had run that other lane leading to the house.", "그러나 개울을 건너기 전에 왼편으로 갈라져 나가는 길이 하나 보일 텐데, 저택으로 이어지던 그 다른 좁은 길이 그러했듯이 말이다."], ["Here, as this cross street rises the hill, are the best houses in the village.", "이 가로지르는 길이 언덕을 오르는 곳에 마을에서 가장 좋은 집들이 있다."], ["The baker lives here, and that respectable woman, Mrs. Frummage, who sells ribbons, and toys, and soap, and straw bonnets, with many other things too long to mention.", "빵집 주인이 여기 살고, 리본과 장난감과 비누와 밀짚 보닛을, 그리고 일일이 들자면 너무 긴 다른 여러 물건을 파는 저 점잖은 여인 프러미지 부인도 여기 산다."], ["Here, too, lives an apothecary, whom the veneration of this and neighbouring parishes has raised to the dignity of a doctor.", "여기에는 약제사도 사는데, 이 교구와 이웃 교구들의 우러름이 그를 의사의 지위로 높여 놓았다."], ["And here also, in the smallest but prettiest cottage that can be imagined, lives Mrs. Hearn, the widow of a former vicar, on terms, however, with her neighbour the squire which I regret to say are not as friendly as they should be.", "그리고 여기, 상상할 수 있는 가장 작지만 가장 어여쁜 오두막에는 헌 부인이 사는데, 전임 교구 목사의 미망인으로 이웃인 지주와의 사이는 유감스럽게도 마땅히 그러해야 할 만큼 사이좋지 못하다."], ["Beyond this lady's modest residence, Allington Street, for so the road is called, turns suddenly round towards the church, and at the point of the turn is a pretty low iron railing with a gate, and with a covered way, which leads up to the front door of the house which stands there.", "이 부인의 소박한 거처를 지나면 앨링턴 스트리트가 — 이 길을 그렇게 부른다 — 갑자기 교회 쪽으로 돌아가는데, 그 굽이 지점에는 나지막하고 어여쁜 철제 난간이 문과 함께 있고 지붕 덮인 길이 나 있어 거기 서 있는 집의 현관으로 이어진다."], ["I will only say here, at this fag end of a chapter, that it is the Small House at Allington.", "나는 여기 이 장의 끄트머리에서 그것이 앨링턴의 작은 집이라는 말만 해 두겠다."], ["Allington Street, as I have said, turns short round towards the church at this point, and there ends at a white gate, leading into the churchyard by a second entrance.", "앞서 말했듯 앨링턴 스트리트는 이 지점에서 교회 쪽으로 짧게 돌아 나가고, 흰 문 앞에서 끝나는데 그 문은 두 번째 입구가 되어 교회 묘지로 들어간다."]], [["So much it was needful that I should say of Allington Great House, of the Squire, and of the village.", "앨링턴의 큰 집과 지주와 마을에 대해서는 이만큼 말해 둘 필요가 있었다."], ["Of the Small House, I will speak separately in a further chapter.", "작은 집에 대해서는 뒤에 따로 한 장을 두어 이야기하겠다."]], [["# CHAPTER II. THE TWO PEARLS OF ALLINGTON.", "# 제2장. 앨링턴의 두 진주"]], [["\"But Mr. Crosbie is only a mere clerk.\"", "“하지만 크로즈비 씨는 일개 서기일 뿐이잖아.”"]], [["This sarcastic condemnation was spoken by Miss Lilian Dale to her sister Isabella, and referred to a gentleman with whom we shall have much concern in these pages. I do not say that Mr.", "이 신랄한 단죄를 입에 올린 사람은 릴리언 데일 양이었고, 상대는 그 언니 이저벨라였으며, 문제의 신사는 앞으로 이 지면에서 우리와 적잖은 인연을 맺게 될 사람이다."], ["Crosbie will be our hero, seeing that that part in the drama will be cut up, as it were, into fragments.", "나는 크로즈비 씨가 우리의 주인공이 되리라고는 말하지 않겠다. 이 극에서 그 배역은 말하자면 여러 조각으로 쪼개질 것이기 때문이다."], ["Whatever of the magnificent may be produced will be diluted and apportioned out in very moderate quantities among two or more, probably among three or four, young gentlemen--to none of whom will be vouchsafed the privilege of much heroic action.", "웅장한 무엇인가가 만들어진다 하더라도 그것은 묽게 희석되어 둘 이상의, 아마도 서넛쯤 되는 젊은 신사들에게 아주 알맞은 분량씩 나뉘어 돌아갈 터이고, 그들 가운데 누구에게도 영웅다운 행적을 넉넉히 누릴 특권은 주어지지 않을 것이다."]], [["\"I don't know what you call a mere clerk, Lily. Mr.", "“일개 서기라는 게 무슨 뜻인지 나는 모르겠구나, 릴리."], ["Fanfaron is a mere barrister, and Mr. Boyce is a mere clergyman.\"", "팬퍼런 씨는 일개 법정 변호사고, 보이스 씨는 일개 목사인걸.”"], ["Mr. Boyce was the vicar of Allington, and Mr.", "보이스 씨는 앨링턴의 교구 목사였고, 팬퍼런 씨는 지난번 순회재판 때 앨링턴까지 걸음을 한 법률가였다."], ["Fanfaron was a lawyer who had made his way over to Allington during the last assizes. \"You might as well say that Lord De Guest is a mere earl.\"", "“그런 식이면 드 게스트 경은 일개 백작일 뿐이라고 해도 되겠네.”"]], [["\"So he is--only a mere earl.", "“실제로 그렇잖아 — 일개 백작일 뿐이지."], ["Had he ever done anything except have fat oxen, one wouldn't say so.", "살진 황소를 기르는 것 말고 무언가 해 본 일이 있었다면 그런 말을 하지도 않았을 거야."], ["You know what I mean by a mere clerk?", "내가 일개 서기라고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지 언니도 알잖아."], ["It isn't much in a man to be in a public office, and yet Mr. Crosbie gives himself airs.\"", "관청에 다닌다는 건 사람에게 그리 대단한 일이 못 되는데, 그런데도 크로즈비 씨는 잔뜩 티를 낸단 말이야.”"]], [["\"You don't suppose that Mr. Crosbie is the same as John Eames,\"", "“크로즈비 씨가 존 임스와 같은 사람이라고 생각하는 건 아니겠지.”"], ["said Bell, who, by her tone of voice, did not seem inclined to undervalue the qualifications of Mr.", "벨이 말했다. 그 말투로 보아 벨은 크로즈비 씨의 자질을 낮게 볼 마음이 조금도 없어 보였다."], ["Crosbie. Now John Eames was a young man from Guestwick, who had been appointed to a clerkship in the Income-tax Office, with eighty pounds a year, two years ago.", "존 임스로 말하자면 게스트윅 출신의 젊은이로, 이 년 전에 연봉 팔십 파운드를 받는 소득세 사무국 서기 자리를 얻은 참이었다."]], [["\"Then Johnny Eames is a mere clerk,\"", "“그럼 조니 임스는 일개 서기지.”"], ["said Lily; \"and Mr.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["Crosbie is-- After all, Bell, what is Mr. Crosbie, if he is not a mere clerk?", "“그리고 크로즈비 씨는 — 결국, 언니, 크로즈비 씨가 일개 서기가 아니면 대체 뭐야?"], ["Of course, he is older than John Eames; and, as he has been longer at it, I suppose he has more than eighty pounds a year.\"", "물론 존 임스보다야 나이가 위고, 그 일을 더 오래 해 왔으니 팔십 파운드보다는 많이 받겠지.”"]], [["\"I am not in Mr. Crosbie's confidence.", "“나는 크로즈비 씨의 속사정을 아는 사람이 아니야."], ["He is in the General Committee Office, I know; and, I believe, has pretty nearly the management of the whole of it.", "일반위원회 사무국에 있다는 건 알고, 내 짐작에는 그 일 전체를 거의 도맡아 관리하는 것 같더구나."], ["I have heard Bernard say that he has six or seven young men under him, and that--; but, of course, I don't know what he does at his office.\"", "그분 밑에 젊은 사람이 예닐곱은 있고, 또 — 라고 버나드가 말하는 걸 들은 적이 있어. 하지만 물론 그분이 사무실에서 무슨 일을 하는지 내가 알 리는 없지.”"]], [["\"I'll tell you what he is, Bell; Mr.", "“그럼 그분이 뭔지 내가 말해 줄게, 언니."], ["Crosbie is a swell.\"", "크로즈비 씨는 멋쟁이야.”"], ["And Lilian Dale was right; Mr.", "그리고 릴리언 데일의 말이 옳았다."], ["Crosbie was a swell.", "크로즈비 씨는 멋쟁이였다."]], [["And here I may perhaps best explain who Bernard was, and who was Mr. Crosbie.", "여기서 버나드가 누구이고 크로즈비 씨가 누구인지를 설명해 두는 것이 가장 좋겠다."], ["Captain Bernard Dale was an officer in the corps of Engineers, was the first cousin of the two girls who have been speaking, and was nephew and heir presumptive to the squire.", "버나드 데일 대위는 공병대의 장교였고, 지금까지 이야기를 나눈 두 아가씨의 사촌이었으며, 지주의 조카이자 추정 상속인이었다."], ["His father, Colonel Dale, and his mother, Lady Fanny Dale, were still living at Torquay--an effete, invalid, listless couple, pretty well dead to all the world beyond the region of the Torquay card-tables.", "그의 아버지인 데일 대령과 어머니인 레이디 패니 데일은 아직 토키에 살고 있었는데, 기력이 다하고 병약하고 무기력한 부부로서 토키의 카드 탁자 언저리를 벗어난 세상에 대해서는 거의 죽은 것이나 다름없었다."], ["He it was who had made for himself quite a career in the Nineteenth Dragoons.", "제19 용기병 연대에서 제법 이름을 낸 사람이 바로 그 아버지였다."], ["This he did by eloping with the penniless daughter of that impoverished earl, the Lord De Guest.", "그가 그리한 방법이란, 저 궁핍한 백작 드 게스트 경의 무일푼 딸과 사랑의 도피를 한 것이었다."], ["After the conclusion of that event circumstances had not afforded him the opportunity of making himself conspicuous; and he had gone on declining gradually in the world's esteem--for the world had esteemed him when he first made good his running with the Lady Fanny--till now, in his slippered years, he and his Lady Fanny were unknown except among those Torquay Bath chairs and card-tables.", "그 사건이 끝난 뒤로는 남의 눈에 띌 만한 기회가 그에게 다시 오지 않았고, 그리하여 그는 세상의 존경으로부터 조금씩 미끄러져 내려갔다 — 처음 레이디 패니를 손에 넣었을 때만 해도 세상은 그를 존경했던 것이다 — 마침내 실내화나 끌고 지내는 이 나이에 이르러, 그와 그의 레이디 패니는 토키의 그 환자용 바퀴의자와 카드 탁자 사이가 아니고서는 아무도 모르는 사람이 되어 버렸다."], ["His elder brother was still a hearty man, walking in thick shoes, and constant in his saddle; but the colonel, with nothing beyond his wife's title to keep his body awake, had fallen asleep somewhat prematurely among his slippers.", "그의 형은 아직도 정정하여 두꺼운 구두를 신고 걸어 다니고 늘 말안장에 올랐지만, 대령은 아내의 작위 말고는 제 몸을 깨워 둘 것이 아무것도 없었던 터라 실내화들 사이에서 다소 이르게 잠들어 버린 셈이었다."], ["Of him and of Lady Fanny, Bernard Dale was the only son.", "그와 레이디 패니 사이의 유일한 아들이 버나드 데일이었다."], ["Daughters they had had; some were dead, some married, and one living with them among the card-tables.", "딸들도 있기는 했다. 더러는 죽었고, 더러는 시집갔으며, 하나는 카드 탁자 곁에서 부모와 함께 살고 있었다."], ["Of his parents Bernard had latterly not seen much; not more, that is, than duty and a due attention to the fifth commandment required of him.", "근래에 버나드는 부모를 그리 자주 보지 않았다. 말하자면 자식의 도리와 제5계명에 마땅한 주의를 기울이는 데 필요한 정도 이상은 아니었다."], ["He also was making a career for himself, having obtained a commission in the Engineers, and being known to all his compeers as the nephew of an earl, and as the heir to a property of three thousand a year.", "그 역시 제 앞길을 스스로 닦고 있었으니, 공병대의 임관을 얻어 냈고, 동료들 사이에서는 모두 백작의 조카이자 연 삼천 파운드짜리 재산의 상속인으로 알려져 있었다."], ["And when I say that Bernard Dale was not inclined to throw away any of these advantages, I by no means intend to speak in his dispraise.", "그리고 버나드 데일이 이런 이점들을 어느 하나 내던질 마음이 없었다고 말한다고 해서, 나는 결코 그를 헐뜯을 뜻이 없다."], ["The advantage of being heir to a good property is so manifest--the advantages over and beyond those which are merely fiscal--that no man thinks of throwing them away, or expects another man to do so.", "좋은 재산의 상속인이라는 이점은 너무나 뚜렷해서 — 단순히 금전적인 것을 넘어서는 이점들까지 있어서 — 그것을 내던질 생각을 하는 사람도 없고, 남이 그러기를 기대하는 사람도 없다."], ["Moneys in possession or in expectation do give a set to the head, and a confidence to the voice, and an assurance to the man, which will help him much in his walk in life--if the owner of them will simply use them, and not abuse them.", "손에 쥔 돈이든 장차 들어올 돈이든, 그것은 고개를 반듯이 들게 하고 목소리에 자신을 실어 주며 사람에게 확신을 주어, 세상을 걸어 나가는 데 크게 도움이 된다 — 가진 사람이 그것을 그저 쓰기만 하고 함부로 남용하지만 않는다면 말이다."], ["And for Bernard Dale I will say that he did not often talk of his uncle the earl.", "그리고 버나드 데일에 대해서라면, 그는 백작인 삼촌 이야기를 자주 입에 올리지는 않았다고 말해 두겠다."], ["He was conscious that his uncle was an earl, and that other men knew the fact.", "삼촌이 백작이라는 것도, 다른 사람들이 그 사실을 안다는 것도 그는 의식하고 있었다."], ["He knew that he would not otherwise have been elected at the Beaufort, or at that most aristocratic of little clubs called Sebright's.", "그렇지 않았더라면 보퍼트에도, 또 세브라이트라 불리는 저 가장 귀족적인 작은 클럽에도 뽑혀 들어가지 못했으리라는 것을 그는 알았다."], ["When noble blood was called in question he never alluded specially to his own, but he knew how to speak as one of whom all the world was aware on which side he had been placed by the circumstances of his birth.", "고귀한 혈통이 화제에 오를 때 그는 결코 제 혈통을 콕 집어 말하지 않았지만, 태생의 사정이 자기를 어느 쪽에 놓아 두었는지를 온 세상이 알고 있다는 듯한 투로 말할 줄은 알았다."], ["Thus he used his advantage, and did not abuse it.", "이런 식으로 그는 제 이점을 쓰되 남용하지는 않았다."], ["And in his profession he had been equally fortunate.", "직업에서도 그는 마찬가지로 운이 좋았다."], ["By industry, by a small but wakeful intelligence, and by some aid from patronage, he had got on till he had almost achieved the reputation of talent.", "근면함으로, 크지는 않으나 늘 깨어 있는 지력으로, 그리고 얼마간 후원의 힘을 빌려 그는 거의 재능 있는 사람이라는 평판을 얻는 데까지 나아갔다."], ["His name had become known among scientific experimentalists, not as that of one who had himself invented a cannon or an antidote to a cannon, but as of a man understanding in cannons and well fitted to look at those invented by others; who would honestly test this or that antidote; or, if not honestly, seeing that such thin-minded men can hardly go to the proof of any matter without some pre-judgment in their minds, at any rate with such appearance of honesty that the world might be satisfied.", "그의 이름은 과학 실험가들 사이에 알려지게 되었는데, 대포를 발명했다거나 대포를 막을 방책을 고안했다는 사람으로서가 아니라, 대포에 밝고 남이 발명한 것을 살펴보기에 알맞은 사람으로서였다. 이런저런 방책을 정직하게 시험해 줄 사람으로서, 혹은 정직하지는 않더라도 — 그런 얄팍한 사람들이 무슨 일이든 미리 마음속에 판단을 세워 두지 않고 검증에 임하기란 좀처럼 어려운 노릇이니 — 적어도 세상이 만족할 만큼은 정직해 보이는 사람으로서였다."], ["And in this way Captain Dale was employed much at home, about London; and was not called on to build barracks in Nova Scotia, or to make roads in the Punjaub.", "그리하여 데일 대위는 주로 본국에서, 런던 근방에서 일했고, 노바스코샤에 병영을 짓거나 펀자브에 도로를 놓으라는 부름을 받지 않았다."]], [["He was a small slight man, smaller than his uncle, but in face very like him.", "그는 몸집이 작고 마른 사내였다. 삼촌보다 작았지만 얼굴은 삼촌을 무척 닮았다."], ["He had the same eyes, and nose, and chin, and the same mouth; but his forehead was better,--less high and pointed, and better formed about the brows.", "눈도, 코도, 턱도, 입도 같았다. 다만 이마는 그의 것이 나아서, 덜 높고 덜 뾰족했으며 눈썹 언저리의 생김새가 더 좋았다."], ["And then he wore moustaches, which somewhat hid the thinness of his mouth.", "게다가 그는 콧수염을 길렀는데, 그것이 얇은 입술을 어느 정도 가려 주었다."], ["On the whole, he was not ill-looking; and, as I have said before, he carried with him an air of self-assurance and a confident balance, which in itself gives a grace to a young man.", "대체로 그는 못생긴 편이 아니었고, 앞서 말했듯이 자신에 찬 태도와 흔들림 없는 균형을 몸에 지니고 있었으니, 그것만으로도 젊은 사내에게는 하나의 기품이 된다."]], [["He was staying at the present time in his uncle's house, during the delicious warmth of the summer,--for, as yet, the month of July was not all past; and his intimate friend, Adolphus Crosbie, who was or was not a mere clerk as my readers may choose to form their own opinions on that matter, was a guest in the house with him.", "그는 지금 여름의 그 감미로운 온기 속에서 — 아직 칠월이 다 가지 않았으므로 — 삼촌의 집에 머물고 있었다. 그리고 그의 절친한 벗 어돌퍼스 크로즈비도 함께 그 집의 손님으로 와 있었으니, 그가 일개 서기인지 아닌지는 내 독자들이 그 문제에 대해 각자 판단을 세우면 될 일이다."], ["I am inclined to say that Adolphus Crosbie was not a mere clerk; and I do not think that he would have been so called, even by Lily Dale, had he not given signs to her that he was a \"swell.\"", "나로서는 어돌퍼스 크로즈비가 일개 서기가 아니었다고 말하고 싶은 쪽이다. 그리고 그가 릴리 데일에게 자기가 「멋쟁이」라는 표를 내지 않았더라면, 그 릴리 데일에게조차 그런 말을 듣지 않았으리라고 생각한다."], ["Now a man in becoming a swell,--a swell of such an order as could possibly be known to Lily Dale,--must have ceased to be a mere clerk in that very process.", "사람이 멋쟁이가 되는 과정에서 — 릴리 데일이 알 만한 정도의 멋쟁이라면 — 바로 그 과정에서 이미 일개 서기이기를 그만두게 마련이다."], ["And, moreover, Captain Dale would not have been Damon to any Pythias, of whom it might fairly be said that he was a mere clerk.", "더구나 데일 대위가, 일개 서기라는 말을 들어 마땅한 사내를 상대로 다몬과 피티아스 같은 벗이 되어 줄 리도 없었다."], ["Nor could any mere clerk have got himself in either at the Beaufort or at Sebright's.", "또 일개 서기라면 보퍼트든 세브라이트든 발을 들이지 못했을 것이다."], ["The evidence against that former assertion made by Lily Dale is very strong; but then the evidence as to her latter assertion is as strong.", "릴리 데일이 앞서 내린 단정에 반하는 증거는 이토록 강하다. 그러나 그녀의 뒤엣말을 뒷받침하는 증거 또한 그만큼 강하다."], ["Mr. Crosbie certainly was a swell.", "크로즈비 씨는 분명 멋쟁이였다."], ["It is true that he was a clerk in the General Committee Office.", "그가 일반위원회 사무국의 서기인 것은 사실이다."], ["But then, in the first place, the General Committee Office is situated in Whitehall; whereas poor John Eames was forced to travel daily from his lodgings in Burton Crescent, ever so far beyond Russell Square, to his dingy room in Somerset House.", "그러나 우선 첫째로, 일반위원회 사무국은 화이트홀에 있다. 반면 가엾은 존 임스는 러셀 스퀘어보다도 한참 더 먼 버턴 크레슨트의 하숙에서 서머싯 하우스의 침침한 방까지 날마다 걸어 다녀야 했다."], ["And Adolphus Crosbie, when very young, had been a private secretary, and had afterwards mounted up in his office to some quasi authority and senior-clerkship, bringing him in seven hundred a year, and giving him a status among assistant secretaries and the like, which even in an official point of view was something.", "게다가 어돌퍼스 크로즈비는 아주 젊은 시절에 개인 비서를 지냈고, 그 뒤 사무국에서 승진하여 준하급 결재권과 선임 서기 자리를 얻어 연 칠백 파운드를 받았으며, 차관보들 틈에서 어엿한 지위를 누렸으니 그것만 해도 관청의 눈으로 보아 무언가는 되는 일이었다."], ["But the triumphs of Adolphus Crosbie had been other than these.", "그러나 어돌퍼스 크로즈비의 진짜 개가는 그런 것이 아니었다."], ["Not because he had been intimate with assistant secretaries, and was allowed in Whitehall a room to himself with an arm-chair, would he have been entitled to stand upon the rug at Sebright's and speak while rich men listened,--rich men, and men also who had handles to their names!", "차관보들과 가깝게 지내고 화이트홀에서 안락의자가 놓인 제 방을 하나 받았다고 해서, 세브라이트의 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에 서서 부유한 자들이 — 부유하고 게다가 이름 앞에 붙는 칭호까지 가진 자들이! — 귀를 기울이는 가운데 말할 자격을 얻는 것은 아니다."], ["Adolphus Crosbie had done more than make minutes with discretion on the papers of the General Committee Office.", "어돌퍼스 크로즈비는 일반위원회 사무국의 서류에 분별 있게 기록을 남기는 일 이상을 해냈다."], ["He had set himself down before the gates of the city of fashion, and had taken them by storm; or, perhaps, to speak with more propriety, he had picked the locks and let himself in.", "그는 유행의 도성 성문 앞에 진을 치고 앉아 그것을 급습해 함락시켰다. 아니, 더 정확히 말하자면 자물쇠를 따고 슬쩍 안으로 들어갔다."], ["In his walks of life he was somebody in London.", "자기가 걸어 다니는 세계에서 그는 런던의 어엿한 인물이었다."], ["A man at the West End who did not know who was Adolphus Crosbie knew nothing.", "웨스트 엔드에서 어돌퍼스 크로즈비가 누구인지 모르는 사람은 아무것도 모르는 사람이었다."], ["I do not say that he was the intimate friend of many great men; but even great men acknowledged the acquaintance of Adolphus Crosbie, and he was to be seen in the drawing-rooms, or at any rate on the staircases, of Cabinet Ministers.", "그가 많은 거물들과 절친했다는 말은 아니다. 그러나 거물들조차 어돌퍼스 크로즈비와 아는 사이임을 인정했고, 그는 각료들의 응접실에서, 적어도 그 계단께에서는 반드시 눈에 띄는 사람이었다."]], [["Lilian Dale, dear Lily Dale--for my reader must know that she is to be very dear, and that my story will be nothing to him if he do not love Lily Dale--Lilian Dale had discovered that Mr. Crosbie was a swell.", "릴리언 데일, 사랑스러운 릴리 데일은 — 내 독자는 그녀가 앞으로 아주 사랑스러운 사람이 되리라는 것을, 그리고 그가 릴리 데일을 사랑하지 않는다면 내 이야기는 그에게 아무것도 아니리라는 것을 알아 두어야 한다 — 그 릴리언 데일은 크로즈비 씨가 멋쟁이임을 알아보았던 것이다."], ["But I am bound to say that Mr. Crosbie did not habitually proclaim the fact in any offensive manner; nor in becoming a swell had he become altogether a bad fellow.", "그러나 크로즈비 씨가 그 사실을 남의 비위를 거스르는 식으로 늘 떠벌리고 다니지는 않았다는 말은 해 두어야겠다."], ["It was not to be expected that a man who was petted at Sebright's should carry himself in the Allington drawing-room as would Johnny Eames, who had never been petted by any one but his mother.", "또 멋쟁이가 되면서 아주 못된 사람이 되어 버린 것도 아니었다. 세브라이트에서 떠받들리는 사내가 앨링턴의 응접실에서, 어머니 말고는 누구에게도 떠받들려 본 적 없는 조니 임스처럼 처신하기를 기대할 수는 없는 노릇이다."], ["And this fraction of a hero of ours had other advantages to back him, over and beyond those which fashion had given him.", "그리고 우리의 이 조각난 주인공에게는 유행이 준 것 말고도 뒤를 받쳐 주는 이점이 더 있었다."], ["He was a tall, well-looking man, with pleasant eyes and an expressive mouth,--a man whom you would probably observe in whatever room you might meet him.", "그는 키가 크고 잘생긴 사내로, 눈매가 서글서글하고 입매에는 표정이 있어서, 어느 방에서 마주치든 아마 눈여겨보게 되는 그런 사람이었다."], ["And he knew how to talk, and had in him something which justified talking.", "게다가 그는 말할 줄 알았고, 말할 만한 무언가를 안에 지니고 있었다."], ["He was no butterfly or dandy, who flew about in the world's sun, warmed into prettiness by a sunbeam.", "그는 세상이라는 햇빛 속을 팔랑거리며 햇살 한 줄기에 데워져 예뻐 보이는 나비나 멋쟁이 한량 따위가 아니었다."], ["Crosbie had his opinion on things,--on politics, on religion, on the philanthropic tendencies of the age, and had read something here and there as he formed his opinion.", "크로즈비에게는 정치에 대해, 종교에 대해, 이 시대의 박애적 경향에 대해 제 나름의 견해가 있었고, 그 견해를 세우는 동안 여기저기서 읽은 것도 있었다."], ["Perhaps he might have done better in the world had he not been placed so early in life in that Whitehall public office.", "그가 그토록 이른 나이에 화이트홀의 저 관청에 들어앉지 않았더라면 세상에서 더 나은 일을 이루었을지도 모른다."], ["There was that in him which might have earned better bread for him in an open profession.", "트인 직업에 나섰더라면 제 밥벌이를 더 훌륭히 해냈을 만한 무언가가 그 안에 있었다."]], [["But in that matter of his bread the fate of Adolphus Crosbie had by this time been decided for him, and he had reconciled himself to fate that was now inexorable.", "그러나 그 밥벌이 문제에 관한 한 어돌퍼스 크로즈비의 운명은 이미 정해져 버렸고, 그도 이제는 돌이킬 수 없는 그 운명과 화해한 터였다."], ["Some very slight patrimony, a hundred a year or so, had fallen to his share.", "아주 얼마 안 되는 유산이, 연 백 파운드 남짓이 그의 몫으로 떨어졌다."], ["Beyond that he had his salary from his office, and nothing else; and on his income, thus made up, he had lived as a bachelor in London, enjoying all that London could give him as a man in moderately easy circumstances, and looking forward to no costly luxuries,--such as a wife, a house of his own, or a stable full of horses.", "그 밖에는 사무국에서 받는 봉급뿐 다른 것은 없었다. 그렇게 이루어진 수입으로 그는 런던에서 독신으로 살면서, 그럭저럭 여유 있는 형편의 사내에게 런던이 줄 수 있는 것을 다 누렸고, 아내라든가 제 집이라든가 말이 가득한 마구간 같은 값비싼 사치는 아예 바라보지 않았다."], ["Those which he did enjoy of the good things of the world would, if known to John Eames, have made him appear fabulously rich in the eyes of that brother clerk.", "그가 실제로 누린 세상의 좋은 것들만 해도, 존 임스가 알았더라면 그 동료 서기의 눈에는 그가 놀랍도록 부유한 사람으로 비쳤을 것이다."], ["His lodgings in Mount Street were elegant in their belongings.", "마운트 스트리트의 그의 하숙은 세간이 우아했다."], ["During three months of the season in London he called himself the master of a very neat hack.", "런던의 사교철 석 달 동안 그는 아주 반듯한 삯말 한 필의 주인 노릇을 했다."], ["He was always well dressed, though never over-dressed.", "그는 언제나 옷을 잘 입었으나 결코 지나치게 차려입지 않았다."], ["At his clubs he could live on equal terms with men having ten times his income.", "클럽에서는 수입이 자기의 열 배인 사람들과 대등하게 어울려 지냈다."], ["He was not married.", "그는 결혼하지 않았다."], ["He had acknowledged to himself that he could not marry without money; and he would not marry for money.", "돈 없이는 결혼할 수 없다고 스스로 인정했고, 돈 때문에 결혼하지는 않겠다고 마음먹고 있었다."], ["He had put aside from him, as not within his reach, the comforts of marriage.", "그는 결혼이 주는 안락을 제 손이 닿지 않는 것으로 여겨 옆으로 밀쳐 두었다."], ["But-- We will not, however, at the present moment inquire more curiously into the private life and circumstances of our new friend Adolphus Crosbie.", "그러나 — 아니, 우리의 새 친구 어돌퍼스 크로즈비의 사생활과 형편을 지금 이 자리에서 더 꼬치꼬치 캐고 들지는 않겠다."]], [["After the sentence pronounced against him by Lilian, the two girls remained silent for awhile.", "릴리언이 그에게 내린 선고가 있고 난 뒤, 두 아가씨는 한동안 말이 없었다."], ["Bell was, perhaps, a little angry with her sister.", "벨은 아마 동생에게 조금 화가 나 있었을 것이다."], ["It was not often that she allowed herself to say much in praise of any gentleman; and, now that she had spoken a word or two in favour of Mr. Crosbie, she felt herself to be rebuked by her sister for this unwonted enthusiasm.", "어느 신사에 대해서든 칭찬의 말을 길게 늘어놓도록 스스로를 놓아두는 일이 그녀에게는 흔치 않았는데, 이제 크로즈비 씨를 두둔하는 말을 한두 마디 했다가 그 유별난 열의 때문에 동생에게 핀잔을 들은 기분이었다."], ["Lily was at work on a drawing, and in a minute or two had forgotten all about Mr.", "릴리는 그림을 그리고 있었고, 일이 분 만에 크로즈비 씨 이야기는 죄다 잊어버렸다."], ["Crosbie; but the injury remained on Bell's mind, and prompted her to go back to the subject.", "그러나 벨의 마음에는 앙금이 남아 그녀를 다시 그 화제로 돌아가게 만들었다."], ["\"I don't like those slang words, Lily.\"", "“난 그런 속된 말이 싫어, 릴리.”"]], [["\"What slang words?\"", "“속된 말이라니, 무슨?”"]], [["\"You know what you called Bernard's friend.\"", "“네가 버나드의 친구를 뭐라고 불렀는지 너도 알잖니.”"]], [["\"Oh; a swell.", "“아, 멋쟁이 말이구나."], ["I fancy I do like slang.", "나는 속된 말이 좋은가 봐."], ["I think it's awfully jolly to talk about things being jolly.", "무엇이 신난다고 말하는 건 정말이지 굉장히 신나는 일이거든."], ["Only that I was afraid of your nerves I should have called him stunning.", "언니 신경이 어떨까 걱정만 아니었으면 나는 그분더러 죽여준다고 했을 거야."], ["It's so slow, you know, to use nothing but words out of a dictionary.\"", "사전에 실린 낱말만 쓰고 사는 건 얼마나 굼뜬 노릇이니.”"]], [["\"I don't think it's nice in talking of gentlemen.\"", "“신사분들 이야기를 하면서 쓸 만한 말은 아닌 것 같아.”"]], [["\"Isn't it?", "“점잖지 않다는 거야?"], ["Well, I'd like to be nice--if I knew how.\"", "뭐, 나도 점잖았으면 좋겠어 — 어떻게 하는 건지 알기만 하면.”"]], [["If she knew how!", "어떻게 하는 건지 알기만 하면!"], ["There is no knowing how, for a girl, in that matter.", "그 문제에 관한 한 여자아이가 어떻게 하는지를 알아낼 길이란 없다."], ["If nature and her mother have not done it for her, there is no hope for her on that head.", "천성과 어머니가 미리 그 일을 해 주지 않았다면 그 방면으로는 아무 가망이 없다."], ["I think I may say that nature and her mother had been sufficiently efficacious for Lilian Dale in this respect.", "릴리언 데일의 경우에는 천성과 어머니가 이 점에서 충분히 제 몫을 해 주었다고 나는 말할 수 있을 것 같다."]], [["\"Mr. Crosbie is, at any rate, a gentleman, and knows how to make himself pleasant.", "“어쨌든 크로즈비 씨는 신사고, 사람을 유쾌하게 만드는 법을 아는 분이야."], ["That was all that I meant.", "내 말은 그뿐이었어."], ["Mamma said a great deal more about him than I did.\"", "그분 이야기라면 어머니가 나보다 훨씬 더 많이 하셨는걸.”"]], [["\"Mr. Crosbie is an Apollo; and I always look upon Apollo as the greatest--you know what--that ever lived.", "“크로즈비 씨는 아폴로야."], ["I mustn't say the word, because Apollo was a gentleman.\"", "그리고 나는 아폴로야말로 세상에 났던 이들 가운데 가장 대단한 — 언니도 그게 뭔지 알잖아 — 그것이라고 늘 생각해. 그 낱말은 입에 올리면 안 되겠지, 아폴로는 신사였으니까.”"]], [["At this moment, while the name of the god was still on her lips, the high open window of the drawing-room was darkened, and Bernard entered, followed by Mr. Crosbie.", "바로 그때, 그 신의 이름이 아직 릴리의 입술에 남아 있는 사이에 응접실의 높다란 열린 창이 어두워지더니 버나드가 들어섰고, 그 뒤를 크로즈비 씨가 따랐다."]], [["\"Who is talking about Apollo?\"", "“누가 아폴로 이야기를 하고 있었지?”"], ["said Captain Dale.", "데일 대위가 말했다."]], [["The girls were both stricken dumb.", "두 아가씨는 그만 말문이 막혔다."], ["How would it be with them if Mr. Crosbie had heard himself spoken of in those last words of poor Lily's?", "가엾은 릴리의 그 마지막 말로 자기가 입에 오르내리는 것을 크로즈비 씨가 들었다면 어찌 될 것인가?"], ["This was the rashness of which Bell was ever accusing her sister, and here was the result!", "이것이야말로 벨이 늘 동생을 나무라던 그 경솔함이었고, 여기 그 결과가 있었다!"], ["But, in truth, Bernard had heard nothing more than the name, and Mr. Crosbie, who had been behind him, had heard nothing.", "그러나 사실 버나드는 이름 말고는 아무것도 듣지 못했고, 그의 뒤에 있던 크로즈비 씨는 아무것도 듣지 못했다."]], [["\"'As sweet and musical as bright Apollo's lute, strung with his hair,'\" said Mr.", "““빛나는 아폴로의 비파, 그의 머리카락으로 줄을 맨 그것처럼 달콤하고 아름다운”.”"], ["Crosbie, not meaning much by the quotation, but perceiving that the two girls had been in some way put out and silenced.", "크로즈비 씨가 말했다. 그 인용에 별다른 뜻을 담은 것은 아니었고, 다만 두 아가씨가 무슨 까닭인지 당황하여 입을 다물었다는 것을 알아챈 것이었다."]], [["\"What very bad music it must have made,\"", "“그럼 음악이 무척 형편없었겠네요.”"], ["said Lily; \"unless, indeed, his hair was very different from ours.\"", "릴리가 말했다. “그분 머리카락이 우리 것과 아주 달랐다면 모를까.”"]], [["\"It was all sunbeams,\"", "“온통 햇살이었지.”"], ["suggested Bernard.", "버나드가 거들었다."], ["But by that time Apollo had served his turn, and the ladies welcomed their guests in the proper form.", "그러나 그쯤에서 아폴로는 제 소임을 다했고, 아가씨들은 격식대로 손님들을 맞았다."]], [["\"Mamma is in the garden,\"", "“어머니는 정원에 계세요.”"], ["said Bell, with that hypocritical pretence so common with young ladies when young gentlemen call; as though they were aware that mamma was the object specially sought.", "벨이 말했다. 젊은 신사들이 찾아왔을 때 젊은 아가씨들이 흔히 부리는 그 위선적인 시치미였으니, 마치 그 신사들이 특별히 찾는 대상이 어머니이기라도 한 것처럼 구는 것이었다."]], [["\"Picking peas, with a sun-bonnet on,\"", "“챙 넓은 모자를 쓰고 완두콩을 따고 계세요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Let us by all means go and help her,\" said Mr.", "“그럼 우리도 어서 가서 거들어 드립시다.”"], ["Crosbie; and then they issued out into the garden.", "크로즈비 씨가 말했다. 그리고 그들은 정원으로 나갔다."]], [["The gardens of the Great House of Allington and those of the Small House open on to each other.", "앨링턴 큰 집의 정원과 작은 집의 정원은 서로 통해 있다."], ["A proper boundary of thick laurel hedge, and wide ditch, and of iron spikes guarding the ditch, there is between them; but over the wide ditch there is a foot-bridge, and at the bridge there is a gate which has no key; and for all purposes of enjoyment the gardens of each house are open to the other.", "둘 사이에는 두툼한 월계수 산울타리와 널찍한 도랑, 그리고 그 도랑을 지키는 쇠창살이 제대로 된 경계를 이루고 있다. 그러나 널찍한 도랑 위에는 판자다리가 놓여 있고 다리 어귀에는 열쇠 없는 쪽문이 하나 있어서, 즐기기 위한 용도로는 두 집의 정원이 서로에게 다 열려 있는 셈이다."], ["And the gardens of the Small House are very pretty.", "그리고 작은 집의 정원은 무척 예쁘다."], ["The Small House itself is so near the road that there is nothing between the dining-room windows and the iron rail but a narrow edge rather than border, and a little path made with round fixed cobble stones, not above two feet broad, into which no one but the gardener ever makes his way.", "작은 집은 길에서 워낙 가까워, 식당 창과 쇠 난간 사이에는 화단이라기보다 가장자리라 할 좁은 띠 하나와, 둥근 자갈을 박아 만든 두 자도 안 되는 좁은 오솔길밖에 없다. 그 길로는 정원사 말고는 아무도 들어서지 않는다."], ["The distance from the road to the house is not above five or six feet, and the entrance from the gate is shut in by a covered way.", "길에서 집까지는 대여섯 자밖에 안 되고, 대문에서 들어오는 통로는 지붕 덮인 길로 가려져 있다."], ["But the garden behind the house, on to which the windows from the drawing-room open, is to all the senses as private as though there were no village of Allington, and no road up to the church within a hundred yards of the lawn.", "그러나 응접실 창이 열리는 집 뒤편의 정원은, 앨링턴이라는 마을도 없고 잔디밭에서 백 야드도 안 되는 곳에 교회로 오르는 길도 없는 양, 오감 어디로 보나 호젓하다."], ["The steeple of the church, indeed, can be seen from the lawn, peering, as it were, between the yew-trees which stand in the corner of the churchyard adjoining to Mrs.", "하기야 교회 첨탑은 잔디밭에서 보인다. 데일 부인네 담장에 잇닿은 교회 묘지 모퉁이에 선 주목들 사이로 말하자면 고개를 내밀고 있는 것이다."], ["Dale's wall. But none of the Dale family have any objection to the sight of that steeple.", "그러나 데일 집안사람 가운데 그 첨탑이 눈에 들어오는 것을 싫어하는 이는 아무도 없다."], ["The glory of the Small House at Allington certainly consists in its lawn, which is as smooth, as level, and as much like velvet as grass has ever yet been made to look.", "앨링턴 작은 집의 자랑은 뭐니 뭐니 해도 그 잔디밭에 있으니, 풀로 만들어 낼 수 있는 한 가장 매끄럽고 가장 고르고 가장 벨벳 같은 잔디밭이다."], ["Lily Dale, taking pride in her own lawn, has declared often that it is no good attempting to play croquet up at the Great House.", "릴리 데일은 제 잔디밭을 자랑스러워하여, 큰 집에서 크로케를 해 보아야 아무 소용이 없다고 자주 말해 왔다."], ["The grass, she says, grows in tufts, and nothing that Hopkins, the gardener, can or will do has any effect upon the tufts.", "거기는 풀이 다발다발 뭉쳐 자라는데, 정원사 홉킨스가 무슨 짓을 해도, 또 하려고 들어도 그 뭉치에는 아무 효험이 없다는 것이다."], ["But there are no tufts at the Small House.", "그러나 작은 집에는 그런 뭉치가 없다."], ["As the squire himself has never been very enthusiastic about croquet, the croquet implements have been moved permanently down to the Small House, and croquet there has become quite an institution.", "지주 자신이 크로케에 그리 열을 올린 적이 없었으므로 크로케 도구는 아예 작은 집으로 옮겨졌고, 그리하여 그곳의 크로케는 하나의 제도가 되다시피 했다."]], [["And while I am on the subject of the garden I may also mention Mrs.", "정원 이야기가 나온 김에 데일 부인의 온실도 말해 두어야겠다."], ["Dale's conservatory, as to which Bell was strenuously of opinion that the Great House had nothing to offer equal to it--\"For flowers, of course, I mean,\"", "벨은 이 온실에 관한 한 큰 집에 견줄 만한 것이 없다는 의견을 굽히지 않았다. “물론 꽃 이야기예요.”"], ["she would say, correcting herself; for at the Great House there was a grapery very celebrated.", "그녀는 이렇게 제 말을 바로잡곤 했다. 큰 집에는 아주 이름난 포도 온실이 있었기 때문이다."], ["On this matter the squire would be less tolerant than as regarded the croquet, and would tell his niece that she knew nothing about flowers.", "이 문제에 대해서만은 지주도 크로케 때만큼 너그럽지 못해서, 조카에게 너는 꽃을 하나도 모른다고 말하곤 했다. “모를지도 모르죠, 크리스토퍼 삼촌.”"], ["\"Perhaps not, uncle Christopher,\"", "그녀는 이렇게 말했다."], ["she would say. \"All the same, I like our geraniums best;\"", "“그래도 저는 우리 제라늄이 제일 좋아요.”"], ["for there was a spice of obstinacy about Miss Dale,--as, indeed, there was in all the Dales, male and female, young and old.", "데일 양에게는 고집이라는 양념이 얼마쯤 들어 있었던 것이다. 하기야 남자든 여자든, 젊든 늙든 데일 집안사람이 다 그러했다."]], [["It may be as well to explain that the care of this lawn and of this conservatory, and, indeed, of the entire garden belonging to the Small House, was in the hands of Hopkins, the head gardener to the Great House; and it was so simply for this reason, that Mrs.", "이 잔디밭과 이 온실, 아니 작은 집에 딸린 정원 전체를 돌보는 일이 큰 집의 수석 정원사 홉킨스의 손에 맡겨져 있었다는 것도 밝혀 두는 편이 좋겠다. 그리 된 까닭은 오로지, 데일 부인이 제 정원사를 따로 둘 형편이 못 되었기 때문이다."], ["Dale could not afford to keep a gardener herself. A working lad, at ten shillings a week, who cleaned the knives and shoes, and dug the ground, was the only male attendant on the three ladies.", "주급 십 실링으로 칼과 신발을 닦고 땅을 파는 일꾼 아이 하나가 세 여인을 시중드는 유일한 남자였다."], ["But Hopkins, the head gardener of Allington, who had men under him, was as widely awake to the lawn and the conservatory of the humbler establishment as he was to the grapery, peach-walls, and terraces of the grander one.", "그러나 밑에 사람을 여럿 부리는 앨링턴의 수석 정원사 홉킨스는, 큰 쪽의 포도 온실과 복숭아 담장과 계단식 화단에 그러하듯 이 조촐한 집의 잔디밭과 온실에도 눈을 부릅뜨고 있었다."], ["In his eyes it was all one place.", "그의 눈에는 그것이 다 한 군데였다."], ["The Small House belonged to his master, as indeed did the very furniture within it; and it was lent, not let, to Mrs.", "작은 집은 제 주인의 것이었고, 실은 그 안의 세간까지도 그러했다."], ["Dale. Hopkins, perhaps, did not love Mrs.", "그것은 데일 부인에게 세를 준 것이 아니라 빌려준 것이었다."], ["Dale, seeing that he owed her no duty as one born a Dale.", "홉킨스는 아마 데일 부인을 좋아하지는 않았을 것이다."], ["The two young ladies he did love, and also snubbed in a very peremptory way sometimes. To Mrs.", "데일 집안에 나서 자란 사람이 아니니 자기가 그녀에게 도리를 다할 까닭이 없다고 여긴 것이다."], ["Dale he was coldly civil, always referring to the squire if any direction worthy of special notice as concerning the garden was given to him.", "두 아가씨는 사랑했고, 때로는 아주 딱 잘라 면박을 주기도 했다. 데일 부인에게는 차갑게 예의만 차렸고, 정원에 관하여 특별히 눈여겨볼 만한 지시가 내려오면 언제나 지주에게 되물었다."]], [["All this will serve to explain the terms on which Mrs.", "이만하면 데일 부인이 작은 집에 살게 된 조건이 설명될 것이다."], ["Dale was living at the Small House,--a matter needful of explanation sooner or later.", "언제고 한 번은 설명해 두어야 할 문제였다."], ["Her husband had been the youngest of three brothers, and in many respects the brightest.", "그녀의 남편은 삼 형제 가운데 막내였고, 여러 면에서 가장 총명했다."], ["Early in life he had gone up to London, and there had done well as a land surveyor.", "그는 젊어서 런던으로 올라가 그곳에서 토지 측량사로 잘 풀렸다."], ["He had done so well that Government had employed him, and for some three or four years he had enjoyed a large income, but death had come suddenly on him, while he was only yet ascending the ladder; and, when he died, he had hardly begun to realize the golden prospects which he had seen before him.", "어찌나 잘 풀렸던지 정부가 그를 썼고, 서너 해 동안은 큰 수입을 누렸다. 그러나 아직 사다리를 올라가는 중일 뿐인데 죽음이 불쑥 그를 덮쳤고, 그가 눈을 감을 때 눈앞에 보이던 황금빛 앞날은 아직 손에 잡히기 시작도 하지 않은 참이었다."], ["This had happened some fifteen years before our story commenced, so that the two girls hardly retained any memory of their father.", "이 일은 우리 이야기가 시작되기 열다섯 해쯤 전에 일어났으므로, 두 아가씨에게는 아버지에 대한 기억이랄 것이 거의 남아 있지 않다."], ["For the first five years of her widowhood, Mrs. Dale, who had never been a favourite of the squire's, lived with her two little girls in such modest way as her very limited means allowed.", "과부가 되고 처음 다섯 해 동안 데일 부인은 — 지주의 눈에 든 적이 한 번도 없었던 그녀는 — 두 어린 딸을 데리고 몹시 빠듯한 살림이 허락하는 대로 검소하게 살았다."], ["Old Mrs. Dale, the squire's mother, then occupied the Small House.", "그때 작은 집에는 지주의 어머니인 늙은 데일 부인이 살고 있었다."], ["But when old Mrs. Dale died, the squire offered the place rent-free to his sister-in-law, intimating to her that her daughters would obtain considerable social advantages by living at Allington.", "그러나 늙은 데일 부인이 세상을 뜨자 지주는 형수에게 그 집을 세 없이 쓰라고 내주며, 앨링턴에 살면 따님들이 사교상 적잖은 이득을 보게 되리라고 일러 주었다."], ["She had accepted the offer, and the social advantages had certainly followed. Mrs.", "그녀는 그 제안을 받아들였고, 사교상의 이득은 과연 따라왔다."], ["Dale was poor, her whole income not exceeding three hundred a year, and therefore her own style of living was of necessity very unassuming; but she saw her girls becoming popular in the county, much liked by the families around them, and enjoying nearly all the advantages which would have accrued to them had they been the daughters of Squire Dale of Allington.", "데일 부인은 가난하여 수입을 다 합쳐도 연 삼백 파운드를 넘지 못했고, 따라서 자기 살림살이는 어쩔 수 없이 아주 수수했다. 그러나 그녀는 두 딸이 고을에서 사랑받고, 이웃한 집안들에게 크게 호감을 사며, 앨링턴의 데일 지주의 딸이었더라도 누렸을 만한 이점을 거의 다 누리는 것을 보았다."], ["Under such circumstances it was little to her whether or no she were loved by her brother-in-law, or respected by Hopkins.", "사정이 이러하니, 시아주버니의 사랑을 받는지 못 받는지, 홉킨스에게 존경을 받는지 못 받는지는 그녀에게 대수로운 일이 아니었다."], ["Her own girls loved her, and respected her, and that was pretty much all that she demanded of the world on her own behalf.", "제 딸들이 그녀를 사랑하고 존경했고, 그녀가 제 몫으로 세상에 요구하는 것은 그것이 거의 전부였다."]], [["And uncle Christopher had been very good to the girls in his own obstinate and somewhat ungracious manner.", "그리고 크리스토퍼 삼촌은 제 나름의 고집스럽고 다소 무뚝뚝한 방식으로 두 조카딸에게 무척 잘해 주었다."], ["There were two ponies in the stables of the Great House, which they were allowed to ride, and which, unless on occasions, nobody else did ride.", "큰 집 마구간에는 조랑말 두 필이 있어 두 아가씨가 타도 좋게 되어 있었고, 어쩌다 있는 일을 빼면 다른 사람은 아무도 타지 않았다."], ["I think he might have given the ponies to the girls, but he thought differently.", "내 생각에는 그 조랑말을 아예 아가씨들에게 주어 버릴 수도 있었을 것 같지만, 그는 달리 생각했다."], ["And he contributed to their dresses, sending them home now and again things which he thought necessary, not in the pleasantest way in the world.", "또 그는 옷가지에도 보태 주어, 필요하다 싶은 것을 이따금 집으로 보내 주기는 했으되 세상에서 가장 기분 좋은 방식으로 그러지는 않았다."], ["Money he never gave them, nor did he make them any promises.", "돈은 결코 준 적이 없고, 약속 같은 것도 하지 않았다."], ["But they were Dales, and he loved them; and with Christopher Dale to love once was to love always.", "그러나 그 아이들은 데일이었고, 그는 그 아이들을 사랑했다. 그리고 크리스토퍼 데일로 말하자면 한번 사랑하면 언제까지고 사랑하는 사람이었다."], ["Bell was his chief favourite, sharing with his nephew Bernard the best warmth of his heart.", "벨이 그가 가장 아끼는 아이였고, 조카 버나드와 더불어 그 마음의 가장 따뜻한 자리를 나누어 가졌다."], ["About these two he had his projects, intending that Bell should be the future mistress of the Great House of Allington; as to which project, however, Miss Dale was as yet in very absolute ignorance.", "이 둘을 두고 그에게는 계획이 있었으니, 벨을 장차 앨링턴 큰 집의 안주인으로 앉힐 생각이었다. 그러나 그 계획에 대해 데일 양은 아직 아무것도 모르고 있었다."]], [["We may now, I think, go back to our four friends, as they walked out upon the lawn.", "이제 잔디밭으로 걸어 나간 우리의 네 사람에게로 돌아가도 되겠다."], ["They were understood to be on a mission to assist Mrs.", "그들은 완두콩 따는 데일 부인을 거들러 나선 것으로 되어 있었다."], ["Dale in the picking of the peas; but pleasure intervened in the way of business, and the young people, forgetting the labours of their elder, allowed themselves to be carried away by the fascinations of croquet.", "그러나 즐거움이 일 앞을 가로막았고, 젊은이들은 어른의 수고를 잊은 채 크로케의 매력에 마음을 빼앗겨 버렸다."], ["The iron hoops and the sticks were fixed.", "쇠고리와 말뚝은 이미 박혀 있었다."], ["The mallets and the balls were lying about; and then the party was so nicely made up!", "나무망치와 공은 여기저기 흩어져 있었고, 게다가 편이 어찌나 알맞게 짜였는지!"], ["\"I haven't had a game of croquet yet,\"", "“저는 아직 크로케를 한 판도 못 해 봤습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Crosbie.", "크로즈비 씨가 말했다."], ["It cannot be said that he had lost much time, seeing that he had only arrived before dinner on the preceding day.", "전날 저녁 식사 전에야 도착한 것을 생각하면 그가 시간을 많이 허비했다고는 할 수 없었다."], ["And then the mallets were in their hands in a moment.", "그러고는 이내 모두의 손에 나무망치가 들려 있었다."]], [["\"We'll play sides, of course,\"", "“물론 편을 갈라서 해야지.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"Bernard and I'll play together.\"", "“버나드하고 내가 한편이야.”"], ["But this was not allowed.", "그러나 그것은 허락되지 않았다."], ["Lily was well known to be the queen of the croquet ground; and as Bernard was supposed to be more efficient than his friend, Lily had to take Mr. Crosbie as her partner.", "릴리가 크로케 마당의 여왕이라는 것은 널리 알려진 사실이었고, 버나드가 제 친구보다 솜씨가 낫다고들 여겼으므로 릴리가 크로즈비 씨를 짝으로 데려가야 했다."], ["\"Apollo can't get through the hoops,\"", "“아폴로는 고리를 못 빠져나가더라.”"], ["Lily said afterwards to her sister; \"but then how gracefully he fails to do it!\"", "릴리는 나중에 언니에게 이렇게 말했다. “그런데 못 빠져나가는 모양새가 어쩌면 그렇게 우아한지!”"], ["Lily, however, had been beaten, and may therefore be excused for a little spite against her partner.", "하기야 릴리는 진 편이었으니 제 짝을 두고 얼마쯤 심술을 부린 것도 봐줄 만하다."], ["But it so turned out that before Mr. Crosbie took his final departure from Allington he could get through the hoops; and Lily, though she was still queen of the croquet ground, had to acknowledge a male sovereign in that dominion.", "그러나 크로즈비 씨가 앨링턴을 아주 떠나기 전에 고리를 빠져나갈 수 있게 된 것은 사실이었고, 릴리는 여전히 크로케 마당의 여왕이기는 했으되 그 나라에 남자 군주가 하나 있음을 인정해야 했다."]], [["\"That's not the way we played at--,\"", "“우리가 그, 거기서 하던 방식은 이게 아닌데 —”"], ["said Crosbie, at one point of the game, and then stopped himself.", "크로즈비가 놀이 도중에 이렇게 말하다가 스스로 입을 다물었다."]], [["\"Where was that?\"", "“거기가 어딘데?”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"A place I was at last summer,--in Shropshire.\"", "“지난여름에 내가 가 있던 곳이야 — 슈롭셔에.”"]], [["\"Then they don't play the game, Mr. Crosbie, at the place you were at last summer,--in Shropshire,\"", "“그럼 크로즈비 씨가 지난여름에 가 계셨다는 그곳, 슈롭셔에서는 이 놀이를 할 줄 모르나 보네요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"You mean Lady Hartletop's,\"", "“레이디 하틀탑 댁 말이지.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."], ["Now, the Marchioness of Hartletop was a very great person indeed, and a leader in the fashionable world.", "그런데 하틀탑 후작부인은 참으로 대단한 인물이자 유행 세계의 선두에 선 사람이었다."]], [["\"Oh!", "“어머!"], ["Lady Hartletop's!\"", "레이디 하틀탑 댁이라니!”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"Then I suppose we must give in;\"", "“그럼 우리는 두 손 들 수밖에 없겠네요.”"], ["which little bit of sarcasm was not lost upon Mr. Crosbie, and was put down by him in the tablets of his mind as quite undeserved.", "이 자그마한 빈정거림은 크로즈비 씨의 귀를 그냥 지나치지 않았고, 그는 그것을 부당한 소리라고 마음속 수첩에 적어 두었다."], ["He had endeavoured to avoid any mention of Lady Hartletop and her croquet ground, and her ladyship's name had been forced upon him.", "그는 레이디 하틀탑과 그 댁의 크로케 마당 이야기가 나오지 않게 하려고 애쓴 참인데, 부인의 이름이 억지로 그에게 떠맡겨진 것이었다."], ["Nevertheless, he liked Lily Dale through it all.", "그럼에도 그는 그 모든 일에도 불구하고 릴리 데일이 마음에 들었다."], ["But he thought that he liked Bell the best, though she said little; for Bell was the beauty of the family.", "다만 말수는 적어도 벨이 가장 마음에 든다고 그는 생각했다. 벨이 그 집안의 미인이었기 때문이다."]], [["During the game Bernard remembered that they had especially come over to bid the three ladies to dinner at the house on that day.", "놀이가 한창일 때 버나드는 자기들이 세 부인을 그날 저택 만찬에 청하려고 일부러 건너왔다는 것을 떠올렸다."], ["They had all dined there on the day before, and the girls' uncle had now sent directions to them to come again.", "그들은 바로 전날에도 다 함께 그곳에서 저녁을 먹었는데, 아가씨들의 삼촌이 다시 오라는 전갈을 보낸 것이었다."], ["\"I'll go and ask mamma about it,\"", "“제가 어머니께 여쭤 볼게요.”"], ["said Bell, who was out first.", "벨이 이렇게 말하며 먼저 자리를 떴다."], ["And then she returned, saying, that she and her sister would obey their uncle's behest; but that her mother would prefer to remain at home.", "그러고는 돌아와서, 자기와 동생은 삼촌의 분부를 따르겠지만 어머니는 집에 남아 계시고 싶어 한다고 전했다."], ["\"There are the peas to be eaten, you know,\"", "“먹어야 할 완두콩이 있잖아요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Send them up to the Great House,\"", "“그건 큰 집으로 올려 보내.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"Hopkins would not allow it,\"", "“홉킨스가 가만있지 않을걸.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"He calls that a mixing of things.", "“그건 이것저것 뒤섞는 짓이라고 하거든."], ["Hopkins doesn't like mixings.\"", "홉킨스는 뒤섞는 걸 싫어해.”"], ["And then when the game was over, they sauntered about, out of the small garden into the larger one, and through the shrubberies, and out upon the fields, where they found the still lingering remnants of the haymaking.", "그러고 나서 놀이가 끝나자 그들은 작은 정원에서 큰 정원으로, 관목 숲을 지나 들판으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나갔고, 거기서 아직 남아 있는 건초 일의 끝자락과 마주쳤다."], ["And Lily took a rake, and raked for two minutes; and Mr.", "릴리는 갈퀴를 하나 집어 이 분쯤 건초를 긁었다."], ["Crosbie, making an attempt to pitch the hay into the cart, had to pay half-a-crown for his footing to the haymakers; and Bell sat quiet under a tree, mindful of her complexion; whereupon Mr.", "크로즈비 씨는 건초를 짐수레에 던져 올려 보려다가 건초 일꾼들에게 첫발 값으로 반 크라운을 물어야 했다."], ["Crosbie, finding the hay-pitching not much to his taste, threw himself under the same tree also, quite after the manner of Apollo, as Lily said to her mother late in the evening.", "벨은 살빛을 생각하여 나무 밑에 얌전히 앉아 있었는데, 그러자 건초 던지기가 그리 입에 맞지 않던 크로즈비 씨도 같은 나무 밑으로 몸을 던졌으니, 그야말로 아폴로다운 자세였다고 릴리는 그날 밤 늦게 어머니에게 말했다. 그러자 버나드가 릴리를 건초로 뒤덮었는데, 그로서는 익살로 치면 대단한 솜씨였다."], ["Then Bernard covered Lily with hay, which was a great feat in the jocose way for him; and Lily in returning the compliment, almost smothered Mr. Crosbie,--by accident.", "그리고 릴리는 그 답례를 하다가 크로즈비 씨를 거의 파묻어 버릴 뻔했다 — 실수로 말이다."]], [["\"Oh, Lily,\"", "“어머, 릴리.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I'm sure I beg your pardon, Mr. Crosbie.", "“정말 죄송해요, 크로즈비 씨."], ["It was Bernard's fault.", "버나드 잘못이에요."], ["Bernard, I never will come into a hayfield with you again.\"", "버나드, 다시는 너하고 건초밭에 오지 않을 거야.” 그리하여 그들은 모두 무척 허물없는 사이가 되었다."], ["And so they all became very intimate; while Bell sat quietly under the tree, listening to a word or two now and then as Mr. Crosbie chose to speak them.", "그동안 벨은 나무 밑에 조용히 앉아, 크로즈비 씨가 이따금 골라 하는 한두 마디에 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["There is a kind of enjoyment to be had in society, in which very few words are necessary.", "사람과 어울리는 데는 말이 거의 필요 없는 종류의 즐거움도 있는 법이다. 벨은 동생 릴리만큼 활달하지 않았다."], ["Bell was less vivacious than her sister Lily; and when, an hour after this, she was dressing herself for dinner, she acknowledged that she had passed a pleasant afternoon, though Mr. Crosbie had not said very much.", "그리고 그로부터 한 시간 뒤 만찬을 위해 옷을 갈아입으면서, 그녀는 크로즈비 씨가 그리 많은 말을 하지는 않았어도 즐거운 오후를 보냈다고 스스로 인정했다."]], [["# CHAPTER III. THE WIDOW DALE OF ALLINGTON.", "# 제3장. 앨링턴의 데일 미망인"]], [["As Mrs. Dale, of the Small House, was not a Dale by birth, there can be no necessity for insisting on the fact that none of the Dale peculiarities should be sought for in her character.", "작은 집의 데일 부인은 태생이 데일 집안 사람이 아니었으므로, 그 성품에서 데일 집안 특유의 기질을 찾아서는 안 된다는 사실을 굳이 힘주어 말할 필요는 없을 것이다."], ["These peculiarities were not, perhaps, very conspicuous in her daughters, who had taken more in that respect from their mother than from their father; but a close observer might recognize the girls as Dales.", "그 기질은 딸들에게서도 그다지 두드러지지 않았는데, 그 점에서 두 아이는 아버지보다 어머니를 더 많이 닮았기 때문이다. 그러나 눈이 밝은 사람이라면 이 처녀들에게서 데일 집안 사람을 알아보았을 것이다."], ["They were constant, perhaps obstinate, occasionally a little uncharitable in their judgment, and prone to think that there was a great deal in being a Dale, though not prone to say much about it.", "두 사람은 한결같았고, 어쩌면 고집스러웠으며, 이따금 남을 판단할 때 조금 야박했고, 데일 집안 사람이라는 데에는 대단한 무엇이 있다고 여기는 편이었다. 다만 그것을 입 밖에 내어 말하는 편은 아니었다."], ["But they had also a better pride than this, which had come to them as their mother's heritage.", "그러나 두 사람에게는 이보다 나은 자긍심도 있었으니, 그것은 어머니에게서 물려받은 유산이었다."]], [["Mrs. Dale was certainly a proud woman,--not that there was anything appertaining to herself in which she took a pride.", "데일 부인은 분명 자긍심이 강한 여인이었다."], ["In birth she had been much lower than her husband, seeing that her grandfather had been almost nobody.", "그렇다고 그녀 자신에게 자랑거리로 삼을 만한 무엇이 있어서는 아니었다. 태생으로 보자면 그녀는 남편보다 훨씬 아래였으니, 할아버지 되는 이는 거의 아무것도 아닌 사람이었다."], ["Her fortune had been considerable for her rank in life, and on its proceeds she now mainly depended; but it had not been sufficient to give any of the pride of wealth.", "재산은 그녀의 신분치고는 상당했고 지금도 주로 그 수입에 기대어 살고 있었으나, 부에서 오는 자랑을 조금이라도 갖게 할 만큼은 아니었다. 그리고 그녀는 미인이었다."], ["And she had been a beauty; according to my taste, was still very lovely; but certainly at this time of life, she, a widow of fifteen years'", "내 취향으로 말하자면 지금도 여전히 무척 아름답다. 그러나 이 나이에 이르러, 미망인이 된 지 열다섯 해에 다 자란 딸이 둘이나 있는 그녀가 제 아름다움을 자랑으로 삼는 일은 분명 없었다."], ["standing, with two grown-up daughters, took no pride in her beauty. Nor had she any conscious pride in the fact that she was a lady.", "자신이 숙녀라는 사실에 대해서도 의식적인 자부심 같은 것은 없었다."], ["That she was a lady, inwards and outwards, from the crown of her head to the sole of her feet, in head, in heart, and in mind, a lady by education and a lady by nature, a lady also by birth in spite of that deficiency respecting her grandfather, I hereby state as a fact--meo periculo.", "그녀가 안팎으로, 정수리에서 발바닥까지, 머리로나 마음으로나 정신으로나 숙녀였다는 것, 교육으로도 숙녀요 천성으로도 숙녀였다는 것, 그리고 할아버지에 관한 그 결함에도 불구하고 태생으로도 숙녀였다는 것을, 나는 여기 사실로서 밝혀 둔다. 내가 책임을 지고서(meo periculo) 하는 말이다."], ["And the squire, though he had no special love for her, had recognized this, and in all respects treated her as his equal.", "그리고 지주는, 비록 그녀에게 각별한 애정은 없었으나, 이 점을 인정하고 있었고 모든 면에서 그녀를 자기와 대등한 사람으로 대했다."]], [["But her position was one which required that she should either be very proud or else very humble.", "그러나 그녀의 처지는 아주 자긍심이 높든가 아니면 아주 낮추어야 하든가, 둘 중 하나여야 하는 자리였다."], ["She was poor, and yet her daughters moved in a position which belongs, as a rule, to the daughters of rich men only.", "그녀는 가난했으나 딸들은 흔히 부잣집 딸들에게나 허락되는 자리에서 움직이고 있었다."], ["This they did as nieces of the childless squire of Allington, and as his nieces she felt that they were entitled to accept his countenance and kindness, without loss of self-respect either to her or to them.", "두 아이가 그럴 수 있는 것은 자식 없는 앨링턴 지주의 조카딸이기 때문이었고, 조카딸인 이상 삼촌의 호의와 친절을 받아들이더라도 그녀 자신에게나 아이들에게나 자존심이 상할 일은 없다고 그녀는 여겼다."], ["She would have ill done her duty as a mother to them had she allowed any pride of her own to come between them and such advantage in the world as their uncle might be able to give them.", "자기 자존심 때문에 아이들과, 삼촌이 아이들에게 줄 수 있는 세상에서의 이점 사이를 가로막았다면, 그녀는 어머니로서의 도리를 그르쳤을 것이다."], ["On their behalf she had accepted the loan of the house in which she lived, and the use of many of the appurtenances belonging to her brother-in-law; but on her own account she had accepted nothing.", "아이들을 위하여 그녀는 지금 살고 있는 집을 빌려 쓰는 것을 받아들였고, 시숙에게 딸린 여러 부속물을 쓰는 것도 받아들였다. 그러나 제 몫으로는 아무것도 받지 않았다."], ["Her marriage with Philip Dale had been disliked by his brother the squire, and the squire, while Philip was still living, had continued to show that his feelings in this respect were not to be overcome.", "필립 데일과의 혼인을 그의 형인 지주는 못마땅해했고, 필립이 살아 있는 동안에도 지주는 이 점에 대한 제 감정을 결코 거두지 않으리라는 태도를 계속 보였다."], ["They never had been overcome; and now, though the brother-in-law and sister-in-law had been close neighbours for years, living as one may say almost in the same family, they had never become friends.", "그 감정은 끝내 거두어지지 않았다. 그리하여 이제 시숙과 제수가 여러 해 동안 가까운 이웃으로, 말하자면 거의 한 식구처럼 살아왔건만, 두 사람은 한 번도 벗이 되지 못했다."], ["There had not been a word of quarrel between them.", "둘 사이에 다툼의 말이 오간 적은 없었다."], ["They met constantly.", "두 사람은 끊임없이 만났다."], ["The squire had unconsciously come to entertain a profound respect for his brother's widow.", "지주는 자기도 모르는 사이에 아우의 미망인에게 깊은 존경을 품게 되었다."], ["The widow had acknowledged to herself the truth of the affection shown by the uncle to her daughters.", "미망인은 삼촌이 제 딸들에게 보이는 애정이 참된 것임을 스스로 인정하고 있었다."], ["But yet they had never come together as friends.", "그런데도 두 사람은 끝내 벗으로 어울리지 못했다."], ["Of her own money matters Mrs. Dale had never spoken a word to the squire.", "데일 부인은 제 돈 문제에 대해 지주에게 한마디도 한 적이 없었다."], ["Of his intention respecting the girls the squire had never spoken a word to the mother.", "지주는 아이들에 관한 제 뜻에 대해 어머니에게 한마디도 한 적이 없었다."], ["And in this way they had lived and were living at Allington.", "그런 식으로 두 사람은 앨링턴에서 살아왔고 또 살고 있었다."]], [["The life which Mrs. Dale led was not altogether an easy life,--was not devoid of much painful effort on her part.", "데일 부인이 꾸려 가는 삶은 결코 수월한 삶이 아니었다."], ["The theory of her life one may say was this--that she should bury herself in order that her daughters might live well above ground.", "그녀 편에서 치르는 고통스러운 노력이 없지 않았다. 그 삶의 이치를 말하자면 이러했다."], ["And in order to carry out this theory, it was necessary that she should abstain from all complaint or show of uneasiness before her girls.", "딸들이 땅 위에서 잘 살게 하려고 자신은 땅속에 묻히는 것. 그리고 이 이치를 관철하려면 딸들 앞에서 어떤 불평도, 괴로운 기색도 내비치지 말아야 했다."], ["Their life above ground would not be well if they understood that their mother, in this underground life of hers, was enduring any sacrifice on their behalf.", "어머니가 이 땅속의 삶에서 자기들을 위해 무엇인가를 희생하고 있다는 것을 아이들이 알아차린다면, 아이들의 땅 위의 삶도 온전할 수 없을 터였다."], ["It was needful that they should think that the picking of peas in a sun-bonnet, or long readings by her own fire-side, and solitary hours spent in thinking, were specially to her mind.", "햇빛 가리개 모자를 쓰고 완두를 따는 일이나, 제 난롯가에서 오래 책을 읽는 일이나, 홀로 앉아 생각에 잠기는 시간이야말로 어머니가 각별히 좋아하는 것이라고 아이들이 믿어야 했다."], ["\"Mamma doesn't like going out.\"", "“엄마는 밖에 나가는 걸 좋아하지 않으셔.”"], ["\"I don't think mamma is happy anywhere out of her own drawing-room.\"", "“엄마는 당신 응접실 말고는 어디서도 행복하지 않으신 것 같아.”"], ["I do not say that the girls were taught to say such words, but they were taught to have thoughts which led to such words, and in the early days of their going out into the world used so to speak of their mother.", "나는 아이들이 그런 말을 하도록 배웠다고 말하려는 것이 아니다. 다만 그런 말로 이어질 생각을 하도록 배웠다는 것이며, 세상에 나가기 시작한 초기에는 실제로 어머니를 두고 그렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["But a time came to them before long,--to one first and then to the other, in which they knew that it was not so, and knew also all that their mother had suffered for their sakes.", "그러나 오래지 않아, 한 아이에게 먼저 그리고 그다음에 다른 아이에게, 그렇지 않다는 것을 그리고 어머니가 자기들을 위해 얼마나 견뎌 왔는지를 알게 되는 때가 찾아왔다."]], [["And in truth Mrs. Dale could have been as young in heart as they were.", "그리고 사실 데일 부인도 아이들만큼 마음이 젊을 수 있었다."], ["She, too, could have played croquet, and have coquetted with a haymaker's rake, and have delighted in her pony, ay, and have listened to little nothings from this and that Apollo, had she thought that things had been conformable thereto.", "그녀 역시 크로케를 칠 수 있었고, 건초 갈퀴를 들고 아양을 떨 수도 있었고, 제 조랑말을 귀여워할 수도 있었고, 그렇다, 이런저런 아폴로가 늘어놓는 자잘한 허튼소리에 귀를 기울일 수도 있었다. 사정이 그러기에 알맞다고 여겼더라면 말이다."], ["Women at forty do not become ancient misanthropes, or stern Rhadamanthine moralists, indifferent to the world's pleasures--no, not even though they be widows.", "마흔 살 여자가 늙어 빠진 인간 혐오자가 되거나, 세상의 즐거움에는 아랑곳하지 않는 라다만티스 같은 준엄한 도덕가가 되는 것은 아니다. 아니, 설령 미망인이라 해도 그렇지 않다."], ["There are those who think that such should be the phase of their minds.", "그런 여자의 마음은 마땅히 그러해야 한다고 여기는 이들이 있다."], ["I profess that I do not so think.", "나는 그렇게 여기지 않는다고 분명히 말해 둔다."], ["I would have women, and men also, young as long as they can be young.", "나는 여자도 남자도 젊을 수 있는 동안은 젊기를 바란다."], ["It is not that a woman should call herself in years younger than her father's family Bible will have her to be.", "그렇다고 여자가 제 아버지 집안 성경에 적힌 것보다 제 나이를 적게 부르라는 말은 아니다."], ["Let her who is forty call herself forty; but if she can be young in spirit at forty, let her show that she is so.", "마흔인 사람은 마흔이라고 하라. 다만 마흔에도 마음이 젊을 수 있다면, 젊다는 것을 보여 주게 하라."]], [["I think that Mrs. Dale was wrong.", "나는 데일 부인이 잘못했다고 생각한다."], ["She would have joined that party on the croquet ground, instead of remaining among the pea-sticks in her sun-bonnet, had she done as I would have counselled her.", "내가 권했던 대로 했더라면 그녀는 햇빛 가리개 모자를 쓰고 완두 지지대 사이에 남아 있는 대신 크로케 잔디밭의 그 무리에 끼었을 것이다."], ["Not a word was spoken among the four that she did not hear.", "네 사람 사이에 오간 말 가운데 그녀가 듣지 못한 것은 한마디도 없었다."], ["Those pea-sticks were only removed from the lawn by a low wall and a few shrubs.", "그 완두 지지대는 낮은 담과 관목 몇 그루로 잔디밭과 갈라져 있을 뿐이었다."], ["She listened, not as one suspecting, but simply as one loving.", "그녀는 의심하는 사람으로서가 아니라 그저 사랑하는 사람으로서 귀를 기울였다."], ["The voices of her girls were very dear to her, and the silver ringing tones of Lily's tongue were as sweet to her ears as the music of the gods.", "딸들의 목소리는 그녀에게 더없이 소중했고, 릴리의 은방울 같은 목소리는 신들의 음악처럼 귀에 달았다."], ["She heard all that about Lady Hartletop, and shuddered at Lily's bold sarcasm.", "그녀는 레이디 하틀탑에 관한 이야기를 죄다 들었고, 릴리의 대담한 빈정거림에 몸을 떨었다."], ["And she heard Lily say that mamma would stay at home and eat the peas, and said to herself sadly that that was now her lot in life.", "그리고 엄마는 집에 남아 완두를 드실 거라는 릴리의 말도 들었고, 이제 그것이 제 인생의 몫이로구나 하고 슬프게 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["\"Dear darling girl,--and so it should be!\"", "“사랑스러운 내 딸. 그래, 마땅히 그래야지!”"]], [["It was thus her thoughts ran.", "그녀의 생각은 그렇게 흘렀다."], ["And then, when her ear had traced them, as they passed across the little bridge into the other grounds, she returned across the lawn to the house with her burden on her arm, and sat herself down on the step of the drawing-room window, looking out on the sweet summer flowers and the smooth surface of the grass before her.", "그러다 아이들이 작은 다리를 건너 저쪽 뜰로 넘어가는 것을 귀로 좇고 나서, 그녀는 팔에 짐을 낀 채 잔디밭을 가로질러 집으로 돌아왔고, 응접실 창문 앞 층계에 앉아 눈앞의 어여쁜 여름 꽃들과 매끄러운 잔디밭을 내다보았다."]], [["Had not God done well for her to place her where she was?", "하느님께서 그녀를 지금 이 자리에 두신 것은 잘하신 일이 아니었던가?"], ["Had not her lines been set for her in pleasant places?", "그녀의 몫은 아름다운 곳에 정해진 것이 아니었던가?"], ["Was she not happy in her girls,--her sweet, loving, trusting, trusty children?", "그녀는 제 딸들로 하여, 그 사랑스럽고 다정하고 미더운 아이들로 하여 행복하지 않은가?"], ["As it was to be that her lord, that best half of herself, was to be taken from her in early life, and that the springs of all the lighter pleasures were to be thus stopped for her, had it not been well that in her bereavement so much had been done to soften her lot in life and give it grace and beauty?", "제 주인이자 자신의 더 나은 반쪽이 이른 나이에 그녀에게서 거두어지고 그리하여 가벼운 즐거움의 샘이 죄다 막혀 버리는 것이 정해진 일이었다면, 그 여읨 가운데서도 제 인생의 몫을 이만큼 부드럽게 하고 거기에 품위와 아름다움을 더해 준 것이 이토록 많았으니 다행이 아니었던가?"], ["'Twas so, she argued with herself, and yet she acknowledged to herself that she was not happy.", "그렇지, 하고 그녀는 스스로와 따져 보았고, 그러면서도 자기가 행복하지 않다는 것을 스스로 인정했다."], ["She had resolved, as she herself had said often, to put away childish things, and now she pined for those things which she so put from her.", "그녀는 스스로 곧잘 말해 왔듯 어린아이의 것들을 버리기로 마음먹었건만, 이제 그렇게 제게서 밀어낸 그것들을 그리워하고 있었다."], ["As she sat she could still hear Lily's voice as they went through the shrubbery,--hear it when none but a mother's ears would have distinguished the sound.", "앉아 있는 동안에도 관목숲을 지나가는 릴리의 목소리가 아직 들렸다. 어머니의 귀가 아니고서는 가려낼 수 없을 소리였다."], ["Now that those young men were at the Great House it was natural that her girls should be there too.", "이제 그 젊은이들이 큰 집에 와 있으니 딸들이 그리로 가는 것은 당연했다."], ["The squire would not have had young men to stay with him had there been no ladies to grace his table.", "식탁을 빛내 줄 여자들이 없었다면 지주도 젊은이들을 불러 묵게 하지 않았을 것이다."], ["But for her,--she knew that no one would want her there.", "그러나 그녀 자신은, 거기서 아무도 자기를 바라지 않는다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Now and again she must go, as otherwise her very existence, without going, would be a thing disagreeably noticeable.", "이따금 한 번씩은 가야 했다. 그러지 않으면 가지 않는다는 사실 자체가 거북하게 눈에 띌 터였기 때문이다."], ["But there was no other reason why she should join the party; nor in joining it would she either give or receive pleasure.", "그러나 그 모임에 낄 다른 이유는 없었고, 낀다 한들 즐거움을 주지도 받지도 못할 것이었다."], ["Let her daughters eat from her brother's table and drink of his cup.", "딸들이야 시숙의 식탁에서 먹고 그 잔으로 마시라지."], ["They were made welcome to do so from the heart.", "아이들은 진심으로 환영받고 있었다."], ["For her there was no such welcome as that at the Great House,--nor at any other house, or any other table!", "그녀에게는 큰 집에도 그런 환영이 없었다. 다른 어느 집에도, 다른 어느 식탁에도!"]], [["\"Mamma will stay at home to eat the peas.\"", "“엄마는 집에 남아서 완두를 드실 거야.”"]], [["And then she repeated to herself the words which Lily had spoken, sitting there, leaning with her elbow on her knee, and her head upon her hand.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 머리를 받친 채 거기 앉아, 릴리가 한 그 말을 혼자 되뇌었다."]], [["\"Please, ma'am, cook says, can we have the peas to shell?\"", "“마님, 요리사가 완두를 까도 되겠느냐고 여쭙습니다요.”"], ["and then her reverie was broken.", "그러자 그녀의 몽상이 깨졌다."]], [["Whereupon Mrs. Dale got up and gave over her basket.", "그러자 데일 부인은 일어나 바구니를 넘겨주었다."], ["\"Cook knows that the young ladies are going to dine at the Great House?\"", "“요리사도 아가씨들이 큰 집에서 저녁을 드시는 걸 알고 있지?”"]], [["\"Yes, ma'am.\"", "“네, 마님.”"]], [["\"She needn't mind getting dinner for me.", "“내 저녁은 차릴 것 없다고 해라."], ["I will have tea early.\"", "나는 차를 일찍 마시겠다.”"], ["And so, after all, Mrs. Dale did not perform that special duty appointed for her.", "그리하여 결국 데일 부인은 자기에게 정해진 그 특별한 소임을 끝내 다하지 못했다."]], [["But she soon set herself to work upon another duty.", "그러나 그녀는 곧 다른 소임에 착수했다."], ["When a family of three persons has to live upon an income of three hundred a year, and, nevertheless, makes some pretence of going into society, it has to be very mindful of small details, even though that family may consist only of ladies.", "세 식구가 한 해 삼백 파운드로 살아야 하면서도 그런대로 사교계에 나가는 시늉을 해야 한다면, 설령 그 식구가 여자들뿐이라 해도 자잘한 것들에 몹시 마음을 써야 하는 법이다. 데일 부인은 이를 잘 알고 있었고, 딸들이 단정하고 산뜻하고 어여쁜 차림이기를 바랐으므로 그 일에 긴 시간을 바쳤다."], ["Of this Mrs. Dale was well aware, and as it pleased her that her daughters should be nice and fresh, and pretty in their attire, many a long hour was given up to that care.", "지주는 겨울이면 숄을 보내 주었고, 승마복도 마련해 주었고, 런던에서 드레스감으로 갈색 비단을 내려보내 주기도 했다."], ["The squire would send them shawls in winter, and had given them riding habits, and had sent them down brown silk for dresses from London,--so limited in quantity that the due manufacture of two dresses out of the material had been found to be beyond the art of woman, and the brown silk garments had been a difficulty from that day to this,--the squire having a good memory in such matters, and being anxious to see the fruits of his liberality.", "다만 그 양이 어찌나 빠듯했던지 그 감으로 드레스 두 벌을 제대로 지어내는 일은 여자의 재주로 될 일이 아니었고, 그리하여 그 갈색 비단옷은 그날 이후 지금까지 골칫거리로 남아 있었다. 지주는 그런 일에 기억력이 좋은 데다 제 후한 인심이 맺은 열매를 보고 싶어 안달했기 때문이다."], ["All this was doubtless of assistance, but had the squire given the amount which he so expended in money to his nieces, the benefit would have been greater.", "이 모든 것이 도움이 된 것은 틀림없으나, 지주가 그렇게 쓴 액수를 그냥 돈으로 조카딸들에게 주었더라면 이로움이 더 컸을 것이다."], ["As it was, the girls were always nice and fresh and pretty, they themselves not being idle in that matter; but their tire-woman in chief was their mother.", "어쨌든 두 처녀는 언제나 단정하고 산뜻하고 어여뻤으니, 자기들도 그 일에 게으르지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 그 옷시중의 으뜸은 어머니였다."], ["And now she went up to their room and got out their muslin frocks, and--but, perhaps, I should not tell such tales!--She, however, felt no shame in her work, as she sent for a hot iron, and with her own hands smoothed out the creases, and gave the proper set to the crimp flounces, and fixed a new ribbon where it was wanted, and saw that all was as it should be.", "그리고 이제 그녀는 딸들 방으로 올라가 모슬린 드레스를 꺼냈고, 그리고, 아니 어쩌면 이런 이야기까지 늘어놓아서는 안 될는지도 모르겠다! 그러나 그녀는 제 일을 부끄러워하지 않았으니, 뜨거운 다리미를 가져오게 하여 제 손으로 구김을 펴고, 오글오글한 주름 장식을 제자리에 잡아 주고, 필요한 자리에 새 리본을 달고, 모든 것이 마땅한 대로 되었는지 살폈다."], ["Men think but little how much of this kind is endured that their eyes may be pleased, even though it be but for an hour.", "남자들은 제 눈을 즐겁게 하려고, 그것도 겨우 한 시간을 위하여 이런 종류의 수고가 얼마나 많이 치러지는지 좀처럼 생각하지 않는다."]], [["\"Oh! mamma, how good you are,\"", "“어머, 엄마, 정말 좋으셔라.”"], ["said Bell, as the two girls came in, only just in time to make themselves ready for returning to dinner.", "두 처녀가 들어서며 벨이 말했다. 저녁 식사 자리로 돌아갈 채비를 겨우 할 만한 시각에 맞춰 온 것이었다."]], [["\"Mamma is always good,\"", "“엄마는 언제나 좋으신걸.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"I wish, mamma, I could do the same for you oftener,\"", "“엄마, 나도 더 자주 엄마한테 이렇게 해 드릴 수 있으면 좋겠어요.”"], ["and then she kissed her mother.", "그러고는 어머니에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["But the squire was exact about dinner, so they dressed themselves in haste, and went off again through the garden, their mother accompanying them to the little bridge.", "그러나 지주는 저녁 시각에 엄격했으므로 두 사람은 서둘러 옷을 갈아입고 다시 정원을 지나 나섰고, 어머니는 작은 다리까지 따라 나왔다."]], [["\"Your uncle did not seem vexed at my not coming?\"", "“네 삼촌이 내가 안 간다고 언짢아하시지는 않더냐?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"We have not seen him, mamma,\"", "“삼촌은 뵙지도 못했어요, 엄마.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"We have been ever so far down the fields, and forgot altogether what o'clock it was.\"", "“저 아래 들판까지 한참 갔다 오느라 몇 시인지도 까맣게 잊고 있었지 뭐예요.”"]], [["\"I don't think uncle Christopher was about the place, or we should have met him,\"", "“크리스토퍼 삼촌은 근처에 안 계셨나 봐요. 계셨으면 마주쳤을 텐데.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"But I am vexed with you, mamma.", "“그런데 나는 엄마한테 화가 났어요."], ["Are not you, Bell?", "언니도 그렇지 않아?"], ["It is very bad of you to stay here all alone, and not come.\"", "여기 혼자 남아서 안 오시겠다니 엄마 너무하셔.”"]], [["\"I suppose mamma likes being at home better than up at the Great House,\"", "“엄마는 큰 집보다 집에 계시는 게 더 좋으신가 봐.”"], ["said Bell, very gently; and as she spoke she was holding her mother's hand.", "벨이 아주 부드럽게 말했다. 그렇게 말하면서 벨은 어머니의 손을 잡고 있었다."]], [["\"Well; good-by, dears.", "“자, 잘 다녀오너라, 얘들아."], ["I shall expect you between ten and eleven.", "열 시에서 열한 시 사이에 오겠거니 하고 기다리마."], ["But don't hurry yourselves if anything is going on.\"", "하지만 무슨 일이 있거든 서두르지 말고.”"], ["And so they went, and the widow was again alone.", "그렇게 두 사람은 떠났고 미망인은 다시 혼자가 되었다."], ["The path from the bridge ran straight up towards the back of the Great House, so that for a moment or two she could see them as they tripped on almost in a run.", "다리에서 큰 집 뒤편으로 길이 곧게 뻗어 있어서, 그녀는 아이들이 거의 뛰다시피 사뿐사뿐 걸어가는 모습을 한두 순간 볼 수 있었다."], ["And then she saw their dresses flutter as they turned sharp round, up the terrace steps.", "그러다 두 아이가 홱 방향을 틀어 테라스 계단을 오를 때 옷자락이 펄럭이는 것이 보였다."], ["She would not go beyond the nook among the laurels by which she was surrounded, lest any one should see her as she looked after her girls.", "그녀는 제 몸을 둘러싼 월계수 사이의 그 후미진 자리에서 한 발짝도 더 나가려 하지 않았으니, 딸들의 뒷모습을 지켜보는 것을 누가 볼까 해서였다."], ["But when the last flutter of the pink muslin had been whisked away from her sight, she felt it hard that she might not follow them.", "그러나 분홍 모슬린 자락이 마지막으로 한 번 펄럭이고 시야에서 사라져 버리자, 그녀는 아이들을 따라갈 수 없다는 것이 못내 서러웠다."], ["She stood there, however, without advancing a step.", "그래도 그녀는 한 걸음도 앞으로 나아가지 않고 그 자리에 서 있었다."], ["She would not have Hopkins telling how she watched her daughters as they went from her own home to that of her brother-in-law.", "딸들이 제 집을 나서서 시숙의 집으로 가는 것을 자기가 지켜보더라고 홉킨스가 떠벌리게 할 수는 없었다."], ["It was not within the capacity of Hopkins to understand why she watched them.", "그녀가 왜 지켜보는지를 헤아리는 것은 홉킨스의 능력 밖의 일이었다."]], [["\"Well, girls, you're not much too soon.", "“자, 얘들아, 그리 이른 편은 아니구나."], ["I think your mother might have come with you,\"", "네 어머니도 같이 왔으면 좋았을 것을.”"], ["said uncle Christopher.", "크리스토퍼 삼촌이 말했다."], ["And this was the manner of the man.", "이것이 이 사람의 방식이었다."], ["Had he known his own wishes he must have acknowledged to himself that he was better pleased that Mrs. Dale should stay away.", "제 속마음을 제대로 알았더라면 그는 데일 부인이 오지 않는 편이 자기에게 더 낫다는 것을 스스로 인정해야 했을 것이다."], ["He felt himself more absolutely master and more comfortably at home at his own table without her company than with it.", "그녀가 함께 있을 때보다 없을 때 그는 제 식탁의 주인 노릇을 더 확실히 했고 마음도 더 편했다."], ["And yet he frequently made a grievance of her not coming, and himself believed in that grievance.", "그런데도 그는 그녀가 오지 않는 것을 자주 서운한 일로 삼았고, 스스로도 그 서운함을 믿었다."]], [["\"I think mamma was tired,\"", "“엄마가 피곤하셨나 봐요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Hem.", "“흠."], ["It's not so very far across from one house to the other.", "이 집에서 저 집까지가 그리 먼 것도 아닌데."], ["If I were to shut myself up whenever I'm tired-- But never mind.", "나도 피곤할 때마다 방에 틀어박히기로 한다면. 아니, 됐다."], ["Let's go to dinner.", "저녁이나 먹자꾸나."], ["Mr. Crosbie, will you take my niece Lilian.\"", "크로즈비 씨, 내 조카 릴리언을 모셔 주시겠소.”"], ["And then, offering his own arm to Bell, he walked off to the dining-room.", "그러고는 벨에게 제 팔을 내밀고 식당으로 걸어갔다."]], [["\"If he scolds mamma any more, I'll go away,\"", "“삼촌이 엄마를 또 나무라시면 난 가 버릴 거예요.”"], ["said Lily to her companion; by which it may be seen that they had all become very intimate during the long day that they had passed together.", "릴리가 제 짝에게 말했다. 이로써 그들이 함께 보낸 그 긴 하루 동안 모두가 아주 허물없는 사이가 되었음을 알 수 있다."]], [["Mrs. Dale, after remaining for a moment on the bridge, went in to her tea.", "데일 부인은 다리 위에 잠시 더 머물렀다가 차를 마시러 들어갔다."], ["What succedaneum of mutton chop or broiled ham she had for the roast duck and green peas which were to have been provided for the family dinner we will not particularly inquire.", "식구들 저녁상에 오르기로 되어 있던 오리구이와 풋완두 대신 그녀가 양갈비나 구운 햄 따위로 무엇을 때웠는지는 굳이 캐묻지 말기로 하자."], ["We may, however, imagine that she did not devote herself to her evening repast with any peculiar energy of appetite.", "다만 그녀가 그 저녁 끼니에 유별난 식욕을 쏟지는 않았으리라고 짐작할 수는 있겠다."], ["She took a book with her as she sat herself down,--some novel, probably, for Mrs. Dale was not above novels,--and read a page or two as she sipped her tea.", "그녀는 자리에 앉으면서 책 한 권을 들었고, 아마도 소설이었을 것이다, 데일 부인은 소설을 낮잡아 볼 사람이 아니었으므로, 차를 홀짝이며 한두 쪽을 읽었다."], ["But the book was soon laid on one side, and the tray on which the warm plate had become cold was neglected, and she threw herself back in her own familiar chair, thinking of herself, and of her girls, and thinking also what might have been her lot in life had he lived who had loved her truly during the few years that they had been together.", "그러나 책은 이내 한쪽으로 밀쳐졌고, 데워 두었던 접시가 식어 버린 쟁반은 버려둔 채, 그녀는 익숙한 제 의자에 몸을 기대고 자신에 대해, 딸들에 대해 생각했다. 그리고 함께 지낸 그 몇 해 동안 자기를 진심으로 사랑해 준 그이가 살아 있었더라면 제 인생의 몫이 어떠했을까 하는 생각도 했다."]], [["It is especially the nature of a Dale to be constant in his likings and his dislikings.", "좋아하는 것도 싫어하는 것도 한결같은 것이 데일 집안 사람의 유별난 천성이다."], ["Her husband's affection for her had been unswerving,--so much so that he had quarrelled with his brother because his brother would not express himself in brotherly terms about his wife; but, nevertheless, the two brothers had loved each other always.", "그녀에 대한 남편의 애정은 흔들린 적이 없었다. 어찌나 그러했던지 그는 제 형이 아내를 두고 형제다운 말을 해 주지 않는다 하여 형과 다투기까지 했다."], ["Many years had now gone by since these things had occurred, but still the same feelings remained.", "그럼에도 두 형제는 언제나 서로를 사랑했다. 그 일들이 있은 지 이제 여러 해가 지났으나 감정은 여전히 그대로였다."], ["When she had first come down to Allington she had resolved to win the squire's regard, but she had now long known that any such winning was out of the question; indeed, there was no longer a wish for it.", "앨링턴에 처음 내려왔을 때 그녀는 지주의 호감을 얻겠다고 마음먹었으나, 그런 것을 얻는 일은 이제 가망 없다는 것을 안 지 오래였다. 아니, 이제는 그럴 마음조차 없었다."], ["Mrs. Dale was not one of those soft-hearted women who sometimes thank God that they can love any one.", "데일 부인은 아무나 사랑할 수 있음을 하느님께 감사하는 그런 마음 무른 여자들 축에 들지 않았다."], ["She could once have felt affection for her brother-in-law,--affection, and close, careful, sisterly friendship; but she could not do so now.", "한때는 시숙에게 애정을, 애정과 함께 가깝고 살뜰한 누이 같은 우정을 품을 수도 있었으나 이제는 그럴 수 없었다."], ["He had been cold to her, and had with perseverance rejected her advances.", "그는 그녀에게 차가웠고, 그녀가 다가가는 것을 끈질기게 물리쳤다."], ["That was now seven years since; and during those years Mrs.", "그것이 이제 칠 년 전 일이다."], ["Dale had been, at any rate, as cold to him as he had been to her.", "그리고 그 세월 동안 데일 부인도 적어도 그가 자기에게 그러했던 만큼은 그에게 차가웠다."]], [["But all this was very hard to bear.", "그러나 이 모든 것을 견디기란 몹시 힘든 일이었다."], ["That her daughters should love their uncle was not only reasonable, but in every way desirable.", "딸들이 삼촌을 사랑하는 것은 사리에 맞을 뿐 아니라 모든 면에서 바람직했다."], ["He was not cold to them.", "그는 아이들에게는 차갑지 않았다."], ["To them he was generous and affectionate.", "아이들에게 그는 너그럽고 다정했다."], ["If she were only out of the way, he would have taken them to his house as his own, and they would in all respects have stood before the world as his adopted children.", "그녀만 비켜나 준다면 그는 아이들을 제 자식처럼 제 집으로 데려갔을 것이고, 아이들은 모든 점에서 세상 앞에 그의 양녀로 섰을 것이다."], ["Would it not be better if she were out of the way?", "그녀가 비켜나는 편이 더 낫지 않을까?"]], [["It was only in her most dismal moods that this question would get itself asked within her mind, and then she would recover herself, and answer it stoutly with an indignant protest against her own morbid weakness.", "이런 물음이 마음속에서 고개를 드는 것은 가장 울적한 때뿐이었고, 그러면 그녀는 곧 정신을 차려 제 병적인 나약함을 성내며 물리치고 단호히 답을 내놓았다."], ["It would not be well that she should be away from her girls,--not though their uncle should have been twice a better uncle; not though, by her absence, they might become heiresses of all Allington.", "딸들 곁을 떠나는 것은 옳지 않다. 설령 삼촌이 지금보다 곱절로 나은 삼촌이라 해도, 설령 자기가 없어짐으로써 아이들이 앨링턴 전부의 상속녀가 된다 해도 그렇다."], ["Was it not above everything to them that they should have a mother near them?", "아이들에게 무엇보다 중요한 것은 곁에 어머니가 있다는 것이 아니던가?"], ["And as she asked of herself that morbid question,--wickedly asked it, as she declared to herself,--did she not know that they loved her better than all the world beside, and would prefer her caresses and her care to the guardianship of any uncle, let his house be ever so great?", "그리고 그 병적인 물음을, 스스로 죄스럽게 던지는 것이라고 자인하면서도 그 물음을 던지면서, 그녀는 아이들이 세상 누구보다 자기를 사랑하며 아무리 대단한 집을 가진 삼촌의 보살핌보다도 제 어머니의 쓰다듬음과 보살핌을 더 좋아하리라는 것을 모르지 않았다."], ["As yet they loved her better than all the world beside.", "아직까지는 아이들이 세상 누구보다 자기를 사랑했다."], ["Of other love, should it come, she would not be jealous.", "다른 사랑이 온다 해도 그녀는 시샘하지 않을 것이었다."], ["And if it should come, and should be happy, might there not yet be a bright evening of life for herself?", "그리고 그런 사랑이 오고 또 그것이 행복한 것이라면, 자기에게도 인생의 환한 저녁이 있지 않겠는가?"], ["If they should marry, and if their lords would accept her love, her friendship, and her homage, she might yet escape from the deathlike coldness of that Great House, and be happy in some tiny cottage, from which she might go forth at times among those who would really welcome her.", "아이들이 시집을 가고 그 지아비 되는 이들이 자기의 사랑과 우정과 공경을 받아 준다면, 그녀도 저 큰 집의 주검 같은 냉랭함에서 벗어나 자그마한 오두막에서 행복할 수 있을 것이고, 이따금 자기를 정말로 반겨 줄 사람들 사이로 나갈 수도 있을 것이다."], ["A certain doctor there was, living not very far from Allington, at Guestwick, as to whom she had once thought that he might fill that place of son-in-law,--to be well-beloved.", "앨링턴에서 그리 멀지 않은 게스트윅에 사는 어떤 의사가 있었는데, 그녀는 한때 그이가 그 사위 자리를 채워 줄 만하다고, 참으로 사랑받을 사위가 되리라고 생각한 적이 있었다."], ["Her quiet, beautiful Bell had seemed to like the man; and he had certainly done more than seem to like her.", "조용하고 아름다운 벨이 그 사람을 좋아하는 듯했고, 그 사람 쪽에서는 분명 좋아하는 듯한 정도를 넘어섰다."], ["But now, for some weeks past, this hope, or rather this idea, had faded away. Mrs.", "그러나 이제 몇 주 전부터 이 희망은, 아니 이 짐작은 스러져 가고 있었다."], ["Dale had never questioned her daughter on the matter; she was not a woman prone to put such questions.", "데일 부인은 이 일로 딸에게 캐물은 적이 없었다. 그녀는 그런 물음을 즐겨 던지는 여자가 아니었다."], ["But during the month or two last past, she had seen with regret that Bell looked almost coldly on the man whom her mother favoured.", "그러나 지난 한두 달 사이에 벨이 제 어머니가 마음에 들어 하는 그 사람을 오히려 냉랭하게 대하는 것을 그녀는 안타깝게 지켜보았다."]], [["In thinking of all this the long evening passed away, and at eleven o'clock she heard the coming steps across the garden.", "이런 생각을 하는 사이에 긴 저녁이 지나갔고, 열한 시에 그녀는 정원을 건너오는 발소리를 들었다."], ["The young men had, of course, accompanied the girls home; and as she stepped out from the still open window of her own drawing-room, she saw them all on the centre of the lawn before her.", "젊은이들이 물론 처녀들을 집까지 바래다준 것이었다. 그녀가 아직 열려 있던 제 응접실 창문으로 나서니, 앞쪽 잔디밭 한가운데에 네 사람이 다 모여 있는 것이 보였다."]], [["\"There's mamma,\"", "“엄마다.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"Mamma, Mr. Crosbie wants to play croquet by moonlight.\"", "“엄마, 크로즈비 씨가 달빛 아래서 크로케를 치고 싶으시대요.”"]], [["\"I don't think there is light enough for that,\"", "“그러기엔 빛이 모자랄 것 같은데.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"There is light enough for him,\"", "“저분한테는 빛이 넉넉해요.”"], ["said Lily, \"for he plays quite independently of the hoops; don't you, Mr.", "릴리가 말했다. “저분은 아치문하고는 아무 상관 없이 치시거든요."], ["Crosbie?\"", "안 그래요, 크로즈비 씨?”"]], [["\"There's very pretty croquet light, I should say,\" said Mr.", "“크로케 치기에 아주 좋은 달빛인데요.”"], ["Crosbie, looking up at the bright moon; \"and then it is so stupid going to bed.\"", "크로즈비 씨가 환한 달을 올려다보며 말했다. “게다가 자러 가는 건 너무 시시하지 않습니까.”"]], [["\"Yes, it is stupid going to bed,\"", "“네, 자러 가는 건 시시하죠.”"], ["said Lily; \"but people in the country are stupid, you know.", "릴리가 말했다. “하지만 시골 사람들은 시시하답니다, 아시겠지만요."], ["Billiards, that you can play all night by gas, is much better, isn't it?\"", "가스등을 켜고 밤새도록 칠 수 있는 당구가 훨씬 낫지요, 안 그래요?”"]], [["\"Your arrows fall terribly astray there, Miss Dale, for I never touch a cue; you should talk to your cousin about billiards.\"", "“그 화살은 영 빗나갔습니다, 데일 양. 저는 큐를 잡아 본 적이 없거든요. 당구 이야기는 사촌 되시는 분께 하셔야지요.”"]], [["\"Is Bernard a great billiard player?\"", "“버나드가 당구를 아주 잘 치나요?”"], ["asked Bell.", "벨이 물었다."]], [["\"Well, I do play now and again; about as well as Crosbie does croquet.", "“글쎄, 이따금 치기는 하지. 크로즈비가 크로케 치는 만큼은 되려나."], ["Come, Crosbie, we'll go home and smoke a cigar.\"", "자, 크로즈비, 우린 돌아가서 시가나 한 대 피우세.”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“네.”"], ["said Lily; \"and then, you know, we stupid people can go to bed.", "릴리가 말했다. “그럼 시시한 우리는 자러 가면 되겠네요."], ["Mamma, I wish you had a little smoking-room here for us.", "엄마, 우리한테도 여기 자그마한 흡연실이 하나 있으면 좋겠어요."], ["I don't like being considered stupid.\"", "시시한 사람 취급받는 건 싫거든요.”"], ["And then they parted,--the ladies going into the house, and the two men returning across the lawn.", "그리하여 그들은 헤어졌다. 여자들은 집 안으로 들어가고, 두 남자는 잔디밭을 가로질러 돌아갔다."]], [["\"Lily, my love,\"", "“릴리야.”"], ["said Mrs.", "다들 어머니의 침실에 모였을 때 데일 부인이 말했다."], ["Dale, when they were all together in her bedroom, \"it seems to me that you are very hard upon Mr. Crosbie.\"", "“너 크로즈비 씨한테 너무 모질게 구는 것 같더구나.”"]], [["\"She has been going on like that all the evening,\"", "“저녁 내내 저러고 있었어요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I'm sure we are very good friends,\"", "“우린 아주 좋은 친구인걸요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, very!\"", "“오, 아주 좋고말고!”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Now, Bell, you're jealous; you know you are.\"", "“저것 봐, 언니는 시샘하는 거야."], ["And then, seeing that her sister was in some slight degree vexed, she went up to her and kissed her.", "언니도 알잖아.” 그러고는 언니가 조금 언짢아하는 것을 보고 다가가 입을 맞추었다."], ["\"She shan't be called jealous; shall she, mamma?\"", "“언니를 시샘쟁이라고 부르면 안 되죠. 그렇죠, 엄마?”"]], [["\"I don't think she deserves it,\"", "“그런 소리 들을 애는 아니지.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Now, you don't mean to say that you think I meant anything,\"", "“자, 그럼 내가 무슨 뜻이 있어서 그런 줄 아시는 건 아니죠.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"As if I cared a buttercup about Mr. Crosbie.\"", "“크로즈비 씨 따위를 내가 미나리아재비 한 송이만큼이라도 마음에 두는 것처럼요.”"]], [["\"Or I either, Lily.\"", "“나도 마찬가지야, 릴리.”"]], [["\"Of course you don't.", "“물론 언니도 아니지."], ["But I do care for him very much, mamma.", "하지만 나는 저분이 아주 마음에 들어요, 엄마."], ["He is such a duck of an Apollo.", "그렇게 귀여운 아폴로가 어디 있담."], ["I shall always call him Apollo; Phoebus Apollo!", "나는 앞으로 늘 저분을 아폴로라고 부를 거예요."], ["And when I draw his picture he shall have a mallet in his hand instead of a bow.", "포이보스 아폴로! 그리고 저분 초상을 그릴 때는 활 대신에 크로케 망치를 손에 들려 드릴 테야."], ["Upon my word I am very much obliged to Bernard for bringing him down here; and I do wish he was not going away the day after to-morrow.\"", "정말이지 저분을 여기 데려와 준 버나드에게 나는 아주 고맙게 생각해요. 그리고 저분이 모레 떠나지 않으면 얼마나 좋을까.”"]], [["\"The day after to-morrow!\"", "“모레라고!”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"It was hardly worth coming for two days.\"", "“이틀 있자고 온 보람은 별로 없구나.”"]], [["\"No, it wasn't,--disturbing us all in our quiet little ways just for such a spell as that,--not giving one time even to count his rays.\"", "“그러게 말이에요. 우리 조용한 살림을 그렇게 휘저어 놓고는 겨우 그만큼이라니. 저분 햇살이 몇 가닥이나 되는지 헤아려 볼 틈도 안 주고요.”"]], [["\"But he says he shall perhaps come again,\"", "“하지만 아마 또 오시겠다고 하셨어.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"There is that hope for us,\" said Lily.", "“우리한테 그 희망은 있네.”"], ["\"Uncle Christopher asked him to come down when he gets his long leave of absence.", "릴리가 말했다. “크리스토퍼 삼촌이 긴 휴가를 얻거든 내려오라고 하셨어요."], ["This is only a short sort of leave.", "이번은 그냥 짧은 휴가고요."], ["He is better off than poor Johnny Eames.", "저분은 가엾은 조니 임스보다 형편이 훨씬 낫죠."], ["Johnny Eames only has a month, but Mr. Crosbie has two months just whenever he likes it; and seems to be pretty much his own master all the year round besides.\"", "조니 임스는 한 달밖에 없는데, 크로즈비 씨는 마음 내킬 때 아무 때나 두 달이나 되고, 게다가 일 년 내내 거의 제 마음대로 지내는 것 같으니까요.”"]], [["\"And uncle Christopher asked him to come down for the shooting in September,\"", "“그리고 크리스토퍼 삼촌이 구월에 사냥하러 내려오라고도 하셨어.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"And though he didn't say he'd come I think he meant it,\"", "“오시겠다고 말씀은 안 하셨지만 그럴 뜻이었던 것 같아요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"There is that hope for us, mamma.\"", "“우리한테 그 희망은 있네요, 엄마.”"]], [["\"Then you'll have to draw Apollo with a gun instead of a mallet.\"", "“그럼 너는 아폴로에게 크로케 망치 대신 총을 들려서 그려야겠구나.”"]], [["\"That is the worst of it, mamma.", "“그게 제일 나쁜 점이에요, 엄마."], ["We shan't see much of him or of Bernard either.", "저분도 버나드도 우리는 별로 못 볼 테니까요."], ["They wouldn't let us go out into the woods as beaters, would they?\"", "우리를 몰이꾼으로 숲에 데려가 주지는 않겠죠?”"]], [["\"You'd make too much noise to be of any use.\"", "“네가 하도 시끄럽게 굴 테니 쓸모가 없을 게다.”"]], [["\"Should I?", "“제가요?"], ["I thought the beaters had to shout at the birds.", "몰이꾼은 새들한테 소리를 질러야 하는 줄 알았는데요."], ["I should get very tired of shouting at birds, so I think I'll stay at home and look after my clothes.\"", "새한테 소리 지르는 건 금방 지겨워질 테니, 그럼 저는 집에 남아서 옷이나 챙기는 게 낫겠네요.”"]], [["\"I hope he will come, because uncle Christopher seems to like him so much,\"", "“크리스토퍼 삼촌이 저분을 저렇게 마음에 들어 하시니 나는 저분이 왔으면 좋겠어.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I wonder whether a certain gentleman at Guestwick will like his coming,\"", "“게스트윅의 어떤 신사분은 저분이 오는 걸 좋아하실지 모르겠네.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["And then, as soon as she had spoken the words, she looked at her sister, and saw that she had grieved her.", "그러고는 그 말을 내뱉자마자 언니를 바라보았고, 언니의 마음을 아프게 했다는 것을 알았다."]], [["\"Lily, you let your tongue run too fast,\"", "“릴리야, 너는 혀를 너무 함부로 놀리는구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I didn't mean anything, Bell,\"", "“아무 뜻도 없었어, 언니.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"I beg your pardon.\"", "“미안해.”"]], [["\"It doesn't signify,\"", "“괜찮아.” 벨이 말했다."], ["said Bell. \"Only Lily says things without thinking.\"", "“릴리는 그저 생각 없이 말을 할 뿐이에요.”"], ["And then that conversation came to an end, and nothing more was said among them beyond what appertained to their toilet, and a few last words at parting.", "그러고서 그 이야기는 끝이 났고, 그들 사이에는 몸단장에 관한 것과 헤어지며 나눈 몇 마디 말 외에는 더 오간 것이 없었다."], ["But the two girls occupied the same room, and when their own door was closed upon them, Bell did allude to what had passed with some spirit.", "그러나 두 처녀는 같은 방을 썼고, 제 방문이 닫히자 벨은 아까 있었던 일을 제법 힘주어 다시 꺼냈다."]], [["\"Lily, you promised me,\"", "“릴리, 너 나한테 약속했잖아.” 벨이 말했다."], ["she said, \"that you would not say anything more to me about Dr. Crofts.\"", "“크로프츠 선생 이야기는 다시는 나한테 하지 않겠다고.”"]], [["\"I know I did, and I was very wrong. I beg your pardon, Bell; and I won't do it again,--not if I can help it.\"", "“알아, 내가 그랬지. 내가 아주 잘못했어. 미안해, 언니. 다시는 안 그럴게. 참을 수 있는 한은.”"]], [["\"Not help it, Lily!\"", "“참을 수 있는 한이라니, 릴리!”"]], [["\"But I'm sure I don't know why I shouldn't speak of him,--only not in the way of laughing at you.", "“하지만 정말이지 내가 왜 그분 이야기를 하면 안 되는지 모르겠어. 언니를 놀리는 식만 아니라면 말이야."], ["Of all the men I ever saw in my life I like him best.", "내 평생 본 남자들 가운데 나는 그분이 제일 좋아."], ["And only that I love you better than I love myself I could find it in my heart to grudge you his--\"", "그리고 내가 나 자신보다 언니를 더 사랑하지만 않았다면, 언니한테서 그분의——”"]], [["\"Lily, what did you promise just now?\"", "“릴리, 방금 뭐라고 약속했지?”"]], [["\"Well; after to-night.", "“알았어, 오늘 밤 이후로는."], ["And I don't know why you should turn against him.\"", "그리고 나는 언니가 왜 그분한테 등을 돌리는지 모르겠어.”"]], [["\"I have never turned against him or for him.\"", "“나는 그분한테 등을 돌린 적도, 그분 편으로 돌아선 적도 없어.”"]], [["\"There's no turning about him.", "“그분한테는 돌아설 것도 없어."], ["He'd give his left hand if you'd only smile on him.", "언니가 웃어 주기만 한다면 그분은 왼손이라도 내놓을 거야. 오른손이라도 내놓을걸."], ["Or his right either,--and that's what I should like to see; so now you've heard it.\"", "그리고 나는 그걸 보고 싶어. 자, 이제 들었지.”"]], [["\"You know you are talking nonsense.\"", "“너 지금 허튼소리 하는 거 알잖아.”"]], [["\"So I should like to see it.", "“어쨌든 나는 그걸 보고 싶어."], ["And so would mamma too, I'm sure; though I never heard her say a word about him.", "엄마도 분명 그러실 거야. 엄마가 그분 얘기를 한마디라도 하시는 건 들은 적이 없지만 말이야."], ["In my mind he's the finest fellow I ever saw.", "내 눈에 그분은 내가 본 가장 훌륭한 사람이야."], ["What's Mr. Apollo Crosbie to him?", "아폴로 크로즈비 씨가 그분한테 댈 게 뭐야?"], ["And now, as it makes you unhappy, I'll never say another word about him.\"", "자, 이 얘기가 언니를 언짢게 하니 나는 이제 다시는 그분 얘기를 하지 않을게.”"]], [["As Bell wished her sister good-night with perhaps more than her usual affection, it was evident that Lily's words and eager tone had in some way pleased her, in spite of their opposition to the request which she had made.", "벨이 여느 때보다 더한 애정을 담아 동생에게 잘 자라고 인사하는 것을 보면, 릴리의 말과 그 열띤 어조가, 자기가 한 부탁과는 어긋나는 것이었음에도 어떤 식으로든 벨을 기쁘게 했음이 분명했다."], ["And Lily was aware that it was so.", "그리고 릴리도 그것을 알고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV. MRS. ROPER'S BOARDING-HOUSE.", "# 제4장. 로퍼 부인의 하숙집"]], [["I have said that John Eames had been petted by none but his mother, but I would not have it supposed, on this account, that John Eames had no friends.", "나는 존 임스를 귀여워해 준 사람이 그 어머니 말고는 없었다고 말했지만, 그렇다고 해서 존 임스에게 친구가 없었다고 여겨지기를 바라지는 않는다."], ["There is a class of young men who never get petted, though they may not be the less esteemed, or perhaps loved.", "세상에는 결코 귀여움을 받지 못하는 부류의 젊은이들이 있는데, 그렇다고 그들이 존중을 덜 받거나 어쩌면 사랑을 덜 받는 것도 아니다."], ["They do not come forth to the world as Apollos, nor shine at all, keeping what light they may have for inward purposes.", "그들은 아폴로의 모습으로 세상에 나서지 않으며 도무지 빛나는 일이 없고, 제게 있을지 모를 빛이란 빛은 안으로 쓰려고 간직해 둔다."], ["Such young men are often awkward, ungainly, and not yet formed in their gait; they straggle with their limbs, and are shy; words do not come to them with ease, when words are required, among any but their accustomed associates.", "그런 젊은이들은 흔히 어색하고 볼품없으며 걸음걸이도 아직 자리 잡히지 않았다. 팔다리는 제멋대로 뻗치고 수줍음을 타며, 말이 필요한 자리에서도 늘 어울리던 무리가 아니고서는 말이 쉬 나오지 않는다."], ["Social meetings are periods of penance to them, and any appearance in public will unnerve them.", "사교 모임은 그들에게 고행의 시간이고, 사람들 앞에 나서는 일이라면 무엇이든 그들의 기를 꺾어 놓는다."], ["They go much about alone, and blush when women speak to them.", "그들은 혼자 다니는 일이 많고, 여자가 말을 걸면 얼굴을 붉힌다."], ["In truth, they are not as yet men, whatever the number may be of their years; and, as they are no longer boys, the world has found for them the ungraceful name of hobbledehoy.", "사실 그들은 나이가 몇이든 아직 어른이 아니다. 그러면서도 이미 소년은 아니기에, 세상은 그들에게 풋내기라는 볼품없는 이름을 붙여 주었다."]], [["Such observations, however, as I have been enabled to make on this matter have led me to believe that the hobbledehoy is by no means the least valuable species of the human race.", "그러나 이 문제를 두고 내가 할 수 있었던 관찰에 따르면, 풋내기란 결코 인류 가운데 가장 값어치 없는 부류가 아니라고 나는 믿게 되었다."], ["When I compare the hobbledehoy of one or two and twenty to some finished Apollo of the same age, I regard the former as unripe fruit, and the latter as fruit that is ripe.", "스물한둘 먹은 풋내기를 같은 나이의 다 여문 아폴로와 견주어 볼 때, 나는 앞엣것을 덜 익은 열매로, 뒤엣것을 다 익은 열매로 여긴다. 그러면 이 두 열매를 두고 물음이 하나 생긴다."], ["Then comes the question as to the two fruits.", "어느 쪽이 더 나은 열매인가."], ["Which is the better fruit, that which ripens early--which is, perhaps, favoured with some little forcing apparatus, or which, at least, is backed by the warmth of a southern wall; or that fruit of slower growth, as to which nature works without assistance, on which the sun operates in its own time,--or perhaps never operates if some ungenial shade has been allowed to interpose itself?", "일찍 익는 열매—아마 촉성 온실 장치의 덕을 좀 보았거나, 적어도 남향 담장의 온기를 등에 업은 열매인가. 아니면 더디 자라는 열매, 자연이 아무 도움 없이 손보고 햇볕이 제 때를 골라 내리쬐는 열매—혹은 짓궂은 그늘이 끼어들도록 내버려 두었다면 끝내 햇볕이 들지 않을지도 모르는 그 열매인가?"], ["The world, no doubt, is in favour of the forcing apparatus or of the southern wall.", "세상은 의심할 바 없이 촉성 장치나 남향 담장 편이다."], ["The fruit comes certainly, and at an assured period.", "그 열매는 확실히 열리고, 열리는 때도 정해져 있다."], ["It is spotless, speckless, and of a certain quality by no means despicable.", "흠 하나 없고 티 하나 없으며 결코 얕볼 수 없는 품질을 지닌다."], ["The owner has it when he wants it, and it serves its turn.", "주인은 원할 때 그것을 얻고, 그것은 제 몫을 한다."], ["But, nevertheless, according to my thinking, the fullest flavour of the sun is given to that other fruit,--is given in the sun's own good time, if so be that no ungenial shade has interposed itself.", "그렇지만 그럼에도 불구하고, 내 생각으로는 햇볕의 맛을 가장 그득히 머금는 쪽은 저 다른 열매다. 짓궂은 그늘이 끼어들지만 않았다면, 햇볕이 스스로 고른 좋은 때에 그 맛이 깃드는 것이다."], ["I like the smack of the natural growth, and like it, perhaps, the better because that which has been obtained has been obtained without favour.", "나는 절로 자란 것의 그 맛을 좋아하고, 어쩌면 그렇게 얻은 것이 아무 특혜 없이 얻어졌기에 더 좋아하는지도 모른다."]], [["But the hobbledehoy, though he blushes when women address him, and is uneasy even when he is near them, though he is not master of his limbs in a ball-room, and is hardly master of his tongue at any time, is the most eloquent of beings, and especially eloquent among beautiful women.", "그러나 풋내기는, 여자가 말을 걸면 얼굴을 붉히고 여자 곁에 있기만 해도 불편해하며, 무도회장에서 제 팔다리를 다스리지 못하고 어느 때고 제 혀조차 거의 다스리지 못하지만, 실은 세상에서 가장 말 잘하는 존재이며 특히 아름다운 여인들 앞에서 그러하다."], ["He enjoys all the triumphs of a Don Juan, without any of Don Juan's heartlessness, and is able to conquer in all encounters, through the force of his wit and the sweetness of his voice.", "그는 돈 후안의 승리란 승리는 다 누리면서도 돈 후안의 무정함은 조금도 없고, 재치의 힘과 목소리의 감미로움으로 어떤 겨룸에서든 이길 수 있다."], ["But this eloquence is heard only by his own inner ears, and these triumphs are the triumphs of his imagination.", "다만 그 웅변은 오직 제 안의 귀에만 들리고, 그 승리는 상상 속의 승리일 뿐이다."]], [["The true hobbledehoy is much alone, not being greatly given to social intercourse even with other hobbledehoys--a trait in his character which I think has hardly been sufficiently observed by the world at large.", "참된 풋내기는 홀로 있는 때가 많아서 다른 풋내기들과도 그리 어울리려 들지 않는데, 이는 세상 사람들이 아직 충분히 눈여겨보지 못한 그의 성품 가운데 하나라고 나는 생각한다."], ["He has probably become a hobbledehoy instead of an Apollo, because circumstances have not afforded him much social intercourse; and, therefore, he wanders about in solitude, taking long walks, in which he dreams of those successes which are so far removed from his powers of achievement.", "그가 아폴로가 아니라 풋내기가 된 것은 아마도 사정이 그에게 어울릴 기회를 넉넉히 주지 않았기 때문일 것이다. 그래서 그는 홀로 이리저리 거닐며 먼 길을 걷고, 그 걸음 속에서 제 힘으로는 도저히 이룰 수 없는 온갖 성공을 꿈꾼다."], ["Out in the fields, with his stick in his hand, he is very eloquent, cutting off the heads of the springing summer weeds, as he practises his oratory with energy.", "손에 지팡이를 들고 들판에 나서면 그는 아주 말을 잘하여, 돋아난 여름 잡초의 머리를 쳐 내면서 힘차게 웅변을 익힌다."], ["And thus he feeds an imagination for which those who know him give him but scanty credit, and unconsciously prepares himself for that latter ripening, if only the ungenial shade will some day cease to interpose itself.", "그렇게 그는 그를 아는 이들이 좀처럼 인정해 주지 않는 상상력을 길러 가며, 짓궂은 그늘이 언젠가 끼어들기를 그친다면 마침내 찾아올 그 늦은 무르익음을 저도 모르게 준비하는 것이다."]], [["Such hobbledehoys receive but little petting, unless it be from a mother; and such a hobbledehoy was John Eames when he was sent away from Guestwick to begin his life in the big room of a public office in London.", "그런 풋내기들은 어머니에게서가 아니고서는 귀여움을 거의 받지 못한다. 게스트윅을 떠나 런던의 어느 관청 큰 방에서 제 삶을 시작하도록 보내졌을 때의 존 임스가 바로 그런 풋내기였다."], ["We may say that there was nothing of the young Apollo about him.", "그에게 젊은 아폴로다운 구석이라고는 하나도 없었다고 해도 좋을 것이다."], ["But yet he was not without friends--friends who wished him well, and thought much of his welfare.", "그렇지만 그에게 친구가 없지는 않았다. 그가 잘되기를 바라고 그의 앞날을 깊이 생각해 주는 친구들이 있었다."], ["And he had a younger sister who loved him dearly, who had no idea that he was a hobbledehoy, being somewhat of a hobbledehoya herself. Mrs.", "그리고 그를 몹시 아끼는 여동생이 하나 있었는데, 저 자신이 얼마쯤은 여자 풋내기였던 까닭에 오빠가 풋내기라고는 꿈에도 생각하지 못했다."], ["Eames, their mother, was a widow, living in a small house in Guestwick, whose husband had been throughout his whole life an intimate friend of our squire.", "그들의 어머니인 임스 부인은 미망인으로 게스트윅의 작은 집에 살았고, 그 남편은 평생을 두고 우리 지주의 절친한 벗이었다."], ["He had been a man of many misfortunes, having begun the world almost with affluence, and having ended it in poverty.", "그는 불운이 많은 사람이어서 거의 넉넉하게 세상살이를 시작했으나 가난 속에 그 삶을 마쳤다."], ["He had lived all his days in Guestwick, having at one time occupied a large tract of land, and lost much money in experimental farming; and late in life he had taken a small house on the outskirts of the town, and there had died, some two years previously to the commencement of this story.", "그는 평생을 게스트윅에서 살면서 한때는 넓은 땅을 부치기도 했고 시험 삼아 하는 농사에 많은 돈을 잃었다. 그리고 만년에는 읍내 변두리에 작은 집을 한 채 얻었으며, 이 이야기가 시작되기 두 해쯤 전에 거기서 죽었다."], ["With no other man had Mr. Dale lived on terms so intimate; and when Mr.", "데일 씨가 그토록 가깝게 지낸 사람은 달리 없었다."], ["Eames died Mr. Dale acted as executor under his will, and as guardian to his children.", "임스 씨가 죽자 데일 씨는 그 유언의 집행인이자 그 아이들의 후견인 노릇을 했다."], ["He had, moreover, obtained for John Eames that situation under the Crown which he now held.", "뿐만 아니라 존 임스가 지금 맡고 있는 그 관직 자리도 데일 씨가 얻어 준 것이었다."]], [["And Mrs. Eames had been and still was on very friendly terms with Mrs.", "그리고 임스 부인은 데일 부인과 아주 다정한 사이였고 지금도 그러했다."], ["Dale. The squire had never taken quite kindly to Mrs.", "지주는 임스 부인을 그리 살갑게 대한 적이 없었는데, 그 남편이 이미 마흔을 넘긴 뒤에야 그녀를 만났던 터였다."], ["Eames, whom her husband had not met till he was already past forty years of age. But Mrs.", "그러나 데일 부인은 큰 집에서 베풀어졌음 직한 살가움이 모자란 만큼을 그 가엾고 외로운 여인에게 제 친절로 메워 주었다."], ["Dale had made up by her kindness to the poor forlorn woman for any lack of that cordiality which might have been shown to her from the Great House.", "임스 부인은 가엾고 외로운 여인이었다. 남편이 살아 있던 시절에도 외로웠지만 과부가 된 지금은 몹시 처량했다."], ["Mrs. Eames was a poor forlorn woman--forlorn even during the time of her husband's life, but very wobegone now in her widowhood.", "중요한 일에서는 지주가 그녀에게 친절했다."], ["In matters of importance the squire had been kind to her; arranging for her her little money affairs, advising her about her house and income, also getting for her that appointment for her son.", "그 얼마 안 되는 돈 문제를 정리해 주고, 집과 수입에 관해 조언해 주었으며, 아들의 그 자리도 얻어 주었다."], ["But he snubbed her when he met her, and poor Mrs. Eames held him in great awe.", "그러나 그는 그녀를 만나면 면박을 주었고, 가엾은 임스 부인은 그를 몹시 어려워했다."], ["Mrs. Dale held her brother-in-law in no awe, and sometimes gave to the widow from Guestwick advice quite at variance to that given by the squire.", "데일 부인은 시아주버니를 조금도 어려워하지 않았고, 때로는 게스트윅의 그 과부에게 지주가 한 말과는 사뭇 어긋나는 조언을 해 주기도 했다."], ["In this way there had grown up an intimacy between Bell and Lily and the young Eames, and either of the girls was prepared to declare that Johnny Eames was her own and well-loved friend.", "이런 식으로 벨과 릴리와 젊은 임스 사이에 친밀함이 자라났고, 두 아가씨 모두 조니 임스가 제 소중한 벗이라고 말할 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["Nevertheless, they spoke of him occasionally with some little dash of merriment--as is not unusual with pretty girls who have hobbledehoys among their intimate friends, and who are not themselves unaccustomed to the grace of an Apollo.", "그럼에도 그들은 이따금 그를 두고 조금은 우스개 섞인 투로 이야기했는데, 절친한 벗 가운데 풋내기를 둔 예쁜 아가씨들, 그러면서 아폴로의 우아함에도 낯설지 않은 아가씨들에게는 드문 일이 아니다."]], [["I may as well announce at once that John Eames, when he went up to London, was absolutely and irretrievably in love with Lily Dale.", "이쯤에서 곧바로 밝혀 두는 편이 낫겠다. 존 임스는 런던으로 올라갈 때 릴리 데일에게 완전히, 그리고 돌이킬 수 없이 반해 있었다."], ["He had declared his passion in the most moving language a hundred times; but he had declared it only to himself.", "그는 세상에서 가장 감동적인 말로 제 연정을 백 번도 넘게 고백했다. 다만 그 고백을 들은 것은 저 자신뿐이었다."], ["He had written much poetry about Lily, but he kept his lines safe under double lock and key.", "그는 릴리를 두고 시를 많이 썼지만 그 시구들을 이중 자물쇠로 단단히 잠가 두었다."], ["When he gave the reins to his imagination, he flattered himself that he might win not only her but the world at large also by his verses; but he would have perished rather than exhibit them to human eye.", "상상에 고삐를 놓아 줄 때면 그는 이 시로 그녀뿐 아니라 온 세상까지 얻을 수 있으리라고 우쭐해했다. 그러나 그것을 사람 눈에 내보이느니 차라리 죽는 편을 택했을 것이다."], ["During the last ten weeks of his life at Guestwick, while he was preparing for his career in London, he hung about Allington, walking over frequently and then walking back again; but all in vain.", "게스트윅에서 보낸 마지막 열 주 동안, 런던에서의 일자리를 준비하면서 그는 앨링턴 언저리를 맴돌며 자주 걸어갔다가 다시 걸어 돌아오곤 했다."], ["During these visits he would sit in Mrs.", "그러나 다 헛일이었다."], ["Dale's drawing-room, speaking but little, and addressing himself usually to the mother; but on each occasion, as he started on his long, hot walk, he resolved that he would say something by which Lily might know of his love.", "그렇게 찾아가면 그는 데일 부인의 응접실에 앉아 말은 거의 하지 않고 대개 어머니 쪽에다 말을 붙였다. 하지만 그 길고 더운 길을 나설 때마다 그는 릴리가 제 사랑을 알아차릴 만한 무언가를 말하리라 마음먹었다."], ["When he left for London that something had not been said.", "런던으로 떠날 때까지 그 무언가는 끝내 말해지지 않았다."]], [["He had not dreamed of asking her to be his wife.", "그는 그녀에게 아내가 되어 달라고 청할 생각은 꿈에도 하지 않았다."], ["John Eames was about to begin the world with eighty pounds a year, and an allowance of twenty more from his mother's purse.", "존 임스는 한 해 팔십 파운드에 어머니 지갑에서 나오는 이십 파운드를 더 받으며 세상살이를 시작하려는 참이었다."], ["He was well aware that with such an income he could not establish himself as a married man in London, and he also felt that the man who might be fortunate enough to win Lily for his wife should be prepared to give her every soft luxury that the world could afford.", "그런 수입으로는 런던에서 살림을 차리고 아내를 둔 사내로 자리 잡을 수 없다는 것을 그는 잘 알았고, 또 릴리를 아내로 얻는 행운을 누릴 사내라면 세상이 줄 수 있는 온갖 안락을 그녀에게 베풀 채비가 되어 있어야 한다고 느꼈다."], ["He knew well that he ought not to expect any assurance of Lily's love; but, nevertheless, he thought it possible that he might give her an assurance of his love.", "릴리의 사랑에 대한 어떤 다짐도 바라서는 안 된다는 것을 그는 잘 알았다. 그러나 그럼에도 제 사랑을 그녀에게 다짐해 보일 수는 있으리라고 생각했다."], ["It would probably be in vain.", "아마 헛일이 되겠지만."], ["He had no real hope, unless when he was in one of those poetic moods.", "저 시적인 기분에 잠겨 있을 때가 아니면 그에게 참된 희망은 없었다."], ["He had acknowledged to himself, in some indistinct way, that he was no more than a hobbledehoy, awkward, silent, ungainly, with a face unfinished, as it were, or unripe.", "그는 저 자신이 어색하고 말없고 볼품없으며 얼굴마저 아직 마무리되지 않은, 말하자면 덜 익은 풋내기에 지나지 않는다는 것을 어렴풋하게나마 인정하고 있었다."], ["All this he knew, and knew also that there were Apollos in the world who would be only too ready to carry off Lily in their splendid cars.", "이 모든 것을 그는 알았고, 세상에는 릴리를 저 눈부신 마차에 태워 데려가고 싶어 안달할 아폴로들이 있다는 것도 알았다."], ["But not the less did he make up his mind that having loved her once, it behoved him, as a true man, to love her on to the end.", "그러나 그렇다고 해서, 한번 그녀를 사랑한 이상 참된 사내로서 끝까지 그녀를 사랑하는 것이 도리라는 결심이 흔들리지는 않았다."]], [["One little word he had said to her when they parted, but it had been a word of friendship rather than of love.", "헤어질 때 그는 그녀에게 짤막한 말 한마디를 건넸지만, 그것은 사랑의 말이라기보다 우정의 말이었다."], ["He had strayed out after her on to the lawn, leaving Bell alone in the drawing-room.", "그는 벨을 응접실에 혼자 남겨 둔 채 그녀를 따라 잔디밭으로 나갔다."], ["Perhaps Lily had understood something of the boy's feeling, and had wished to speak kindly to him at parting, or almost more than kindly.", "어쩌면 릴리는 그 소년의 마음을 얼마쯤 알아차리고, 헤어지는 마당에 다정하게, 아니 다정한 것 이상으로 말해 주고 싶었는지도 모른다."], ["There is a silent love which women recognize, and which in some silent way they acknowledge,--giving gracious but silent thanks for the respect which accompanies it.", "여자들이 알아보는 말없는 사랑이 있고, 그들은 어떤 말없는 방식으로 그것을 알은체하며, 그 사랑에 딸린 존중에 대해 곱지만 소리 없는 고마움을 표한다."]], [["\"I have come to say good-by, Lily,\"", "“작별 인사를 하러 왔어요, 릴리.”"], ["said Johnny Eames, following the girl down one of the paths.", "조니 임스가 오솔길 하나를 따라 아가씨의 뒤를 좇으며 말했다."]], [["\"Good-by, John,\"", "“잘 가요, 존.”"], ["said she, turning round.", "그녀가 돌아서며 말했다."], ["\"You know how sorry we are to lose you.", "“우리가 얼마나 서운해하는지 아시죠."], ["But it's a great thing for you to be going up to London.\"", "그래도 런던에 올라가시는 건 대단한 일이에요.”"]], [["\"Well; yes.", "“뭐, 그렇죠."], ["I suppose it is.", "그런 것 같아요."], ["I'd sooner remain here, though.\"", "그래도 저는 여기 남는 편이 더 좋겠는데요.”"]], [["\"What!", "“어머!"], ["stay here, doing nothing!", "여기 남아서 아무 일도 안 하고요?"], ["I am sure you would not.\"", "정말로 그러실 리가 없어요.”"]], [["\"Of course, I should like to do something.", "“물론 무언가 하고는 싶죠."], ["I mean--\"", "제 말은—”"]], [["\"You mean that it is painful to part with old friends; and I'm sure that we all feel that at parting with you.", "“오랜 친구와 헤어지는 게 괴롭다는 말씀이겠죠. 우리도 모두 그렇게 느끼고 있어요."], ["But you'll have a holiday sometimes, and then we shall see you.\"", "그래도 가끔은 휴가가 있을 테고, 그러면 뵐 수 있잖아요.”"]], [["\"Yes; of course, I shall see you then.", "“네, 그럼요, 그때는 뵙겠죠."], ["I think, Lily, I shall care more about seeing you than anybody.\"", "릴리, 제 생각에는 누구보다도 릴리를 보는 게 제일 마음이 쓰일 것 같아요.”"]], [["\"Oh, no, John.", "“어머, 아니에요, 존."], ["There'll be your own mother and sister.\"", "어머니도 계시고 동생도 있잖아요.”"]], [["\"Yes; there'll be mother and Mary, of course.", "“네, 물론 어머니와 메리가 있죠."], ["But I will come over here the very first day,--that is, if you'll care to see me?\"", "하지만 저는 첫날로 곧장 이리 건너올 거예요. 그러니까, 릴리가 저를 보고 싶어 한다면 말이지만요.”"]], [["\"We shall care to see you very much.", "“우리는 아주 많이 보고 싶어 할 거예요."], ["You know that.", "아시잖아요."], ["And--dear John, I do hope you'll be happy.\"", "그리고—존, 부디 행복하시길 바라요.”"]], [["There was a tone in her voice as she spoke which almost upset him; or, I should rather say, which almost put him up upon his legs and made him speak; but its ultimate effect was less powerful.", "그렇게 말할 때 그녀의 목소리에는 그를 거의 무너뜨릴 뻔한 어떤 가락이 있었다. 아니, 차라리 이렇게 말해야겠다."], ["\"Do you?\" said he, as he held her hand for a few happy seconds.", "그를 거의 두 다리로 일어서게 하여 말을 하게 만들 뻔한 가락이었다."], ["\"And I'm sure I hope you'll always be happy.", "그러나 그 끝에 남은 효력은 그만큼 강하지 못했다."], ["Good-by, Lily.\"", "“그래요?”"], ["Then he left her, returning to the house, and she continued her walk, wandering down among the trees in the shrubbery, and not showing herself for the next half hour.", "그가 몇 초 동안 행복하게 그녀의 손을 잡은 채 말했다. “저도 릴리가 언제나 행복하기를 진심으로 바라요."], ["How many girls have some such lover as that,--a lover who says no more to them than Johnny Eames then said to Lily Dale, who never says more than that?", "잘 있어요, 릴리.” 그러고는 그녀를 두고 집 쪽으로 돌아갔고, 그녀는 걸음을 이어 관목 숲의 나무들 사이로 내려가 그 뒤 반 시간 동안 모습을 보이지 않았다."], ["And yet when, in after years, they count over the names of all who have loved them, the name of that awkward youth is never forgotten.", "그때 조니 임스가 릴리 데일에게 한 말보다 더는 말하지 않는 연인, 끝내 그 이상은 결코 말하지 않는 그런 연인을 가진 아가씨가 얼마나 많은가? 그러면서도 훗날 저를 사랑했던 이들의 이름을 하나하나 꼽아 볼 때, 그 어색한 젊은이의 이름만은 결코 잊히지 않는 법이다."]], [["That farewell had been spoken nearly two years since, and Lily Dale was then seventeen.", "그 작별 인사가 오간 지도 벌써 이 년 가까이 되었고, 그때 릴리 데일은 열일곱이었다."], ["Since that time, John Eames had been home once, and during his month's holiday had often visited Allington.", "그 뒤로 존 임스는 한 번 고향에 다녀갔고, 한 달 남짓한 휴가 동안 앨링턴을 자주 찾았다."], ["But he had never improved upon that occasion of which I have told.", "그러나 앞서 이야기한 그 기회보다 더 나아간 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["It had seemed to him that Lily was colder to him than in old days, and he had become, if anything, more shy in his ways with her.", "릴리가 예전보다 자기에게 차가워진 것처럼 여겨졌고, 그는 그녀 앞에서 오히려 전보다 더 수줍어졌다."], ["He was to return to Guestwick again during this autumn; but, to tell honestly the truth in the matter, Lily Dale did not think or care very much for his coming.", "그는 이 가을에 다시 게스트윅에 내려올 참이었다. 그러나 이 일의 진실을 정직하게 말하자면, 릴리 데일은 그가 온다는 것을 그리 마음에 두지도 대수롭게 여기지도 않았다."], ["Girls of nineteen do not care for lovers of one-and-twenty, unless it be when the fruit has had the advantage of some forcing apparatus or southern wall.", "열아홉 먹은 아가씨는 스물한 살 먹은 연인에게 마음을 쓰지 않는 법이다. 그 열매가 촉성 장치나 남향 담장의 덕을 본 것이 아니라면 말이다."]], [["John Eames's love was still as hot as ever, having been sustained on poetry, and kept alive, perhaps, by some close confidence in the ears of a brother clerk; but it is not to be supposed that during these two years he had been a melancholy lover.", "존 임스의 사랑은 여전히 예전만큼이나 뜨거웠다. 시로 떠받쳐지고, 어쩌면 동료 서기의 귀에다 은밀히 털어놓는 고백으로 목숨을 이어 온 사랑이었다."], ["It might, perhaps, have been better for him had his disposition led him to that line of life.", "그렇다고 이 두 해 동안 그가 우수에 잠긴 연인으로 지냈으리라고 생각해서는 안 된다. 아마 그의 기질이 그를 그런 삶 쪽으로 이끌었더라면 그에게 더 나았을지도 모른다."], ["Such, however, had not been the case.", "그러나 사정은 그렇지 않았다."], ["He had already abandoned the flute on which he had learned to sound three sad notes before he left Guestwick, and, after the fifth or sixth Sunday, he had relinquished his solitary walks along the towing-path of the Regent's Park Canal.", "그는 게스트윅을 떠나기 전에 구슬픈 가락 세 개를 낼 줄 알게 되었던 그 플루트를 이미 팽개쳤고, 다섯 번째인가 여섯 번째 일요일이 지난 뒤에는 리전트 파크 운하의 예선로를 따라 홀로 걷던 산책도 그만두었다."], ["To think of one's absent love is very sweet; but it becomes monotonous after a mile or two of a towing-path, and the mind will turn away to Aunt Sally, the Cremorne Gardens, and financial questions.", "곁에 없는 연인을 생각하는 일은 무척 달콤하다. 그러나 예선로를 한두 마일 걷다 보면 그것도 단조로워지고, 마음은 어느새 샐리 아줌마 놀이와 크리몬 정원과 돈 문제 쪽으로 돌아서고 만다."], ["I doubt whether any girl would be satisfied with her lover's mind if she knew the whole of it.", "어떤 아가씨든 제 연인의 마음속을 전부 알게 된다면 그 마음에 만족할 수 있을지 나는 의심스럽다."]], [["\"I say, Caudle, I wonder whether a fellow could get into a club?\"", "“이봐, 코들, 사람이 클럽에 들어갈 수도 있을까?”"]], [["This proposition was made, on one of those Sunday walks, by John Eames to the friend of his bosom, a brother clerk, whose legitimate name was Cradell, and who was therefore called Caudle by his friends.", "이 제안은 그런 일요일 산책 가운데 어느 날, 존 임스가 제 가슴속을 털어놓는 벗이자 동료 서기에게 건넨 것이었다. 그 벗의 본래 이름은 크래들이었고, 그래서 친구들은 그를 코들이라고 불렀다."]], [["\"Get into a club?", "“클럽에 들어간다고?"], ["Fisher in our room belongs to a club.\"", "우리 방 피셔가 클럽 회원이잖아.”"]], [["\"That's only a chess-club.", "“그건 체스 클럽일 뿐이지."], ["I mean a regular club.\"", "나는 제대로 된 클럽 말이야.”"]], [["\"One of the swell ones at the West End?\"", "“웨스트엔드에 있는 멋쟁이 클럽 가운데 하나 말이야?”"], ["said Cradell, almost lost in admiration at the ambition of his friend.", "크래들이 벗의 야심에 거의 넋을 잃고 감탄하며 말했다."]], [["\"I shouldn't want it to be particularly swell.", "“딱히 멋쟁이 클럽일 것까지는 없어."], ["If a man isn't a swell, I don't see what he gets by going among those who are.", "자기가 멋쟁이가 아니라면 멋쟁이들 틈에 끼어 봐야 얻을 게 뭐가 있겠어."], ["But it is so uncommon slow at Mother Roper's.\" Now Mrs.", "하지만 로퍼 아줌마네는 어지간히도 따분하단 말이야.”"], ["Roper was a respectable lady, who kept a boarding-house in Burton Crescent, and to whom Mrs. Eames had been strongly recommended when she was desirous of finding a specially safe domicile for her son.", "그런데 로퍼 부인은 버턴 크레슨트에서 하숙집을 치는 점잖은 부인으로, 임스 부인이 아들을 특별히 안전한 거처에 두고 싶어 했을 때 강력히 추천받은 사람이었다."], ["For the first year of his life in London John Eames had lived alone in lodgings; but that had resulted in discomfort, solitude, and, alas!", "런던 생활 첫해에 존 임스는 셋방에서 혼자 지냈다. 그러나 그 결과는 불편함과 외로움이었고, 아아, 얼마간의 빚까지 생겨 그것이 가엾은 과부에게 무겁게 얹혔다."], ["in some amount of debt, which had come heavily on the poor widow. Now, for the second year, some safer mode of life was necessary.", "이제 둘째 해에는 좀 더 안전한 살림 방식이 필요했다."], ["She had learned that Mrs. Cradell, the widow of a barrister, who had also succeeded in getting her son into the Income-tax Office, had placed him in charge of Mrs.", "임스 부인은 법정 변호사의 미망인인 크래들 부인 역시 아들을 소득세 사무국에 넣는 데 성공했고 그 아들을 로퍼 부인에게 맡겼다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Roper; and she, with many injunctions to that motherly woman, submitted her own boy to the same custody.", "그리하여 그녀도 그 모성 어린 여인에게 이런저런 당부를 잔뜩 하면서 제 아들을 같은 보호 아래 맡겼다."]], [["\"And about going to church?\" Mrs.", "“그리고 교회 가는 일은요?”"], ["Eames had said to Mrs. Roper.", "임스 부인이 로퍼 부인에게 그렇게 말했었다."]], [["\"I don't suppose I can look after that, ma'am,\" Mrs.", "“그것까지는 제가 돌봐 드리지 못할 것 같습니다요, 마님.”"], ["Roper had answered, conscientiously.", "로퍼 부인이 양심껏 대답했었다."], ["\"Young gentlemen choose mostly their own churches.\"", "“젊은 신사분들은 대개 제 교회를 제가 고르시지요.”"]], [["\"But they do go?\"", "“그래도 가기는 하지요?”"], ["asked the mother, very anxious in her heart as to this new life in which her boy was to be left to follow in so many things the guidance of his own lights.", "어머니가 물었다. 아들이 이제 많은 일을 제 판단에 맡겨 따라가야 할 그 새 삶을 두고 그녀의 마음은 몹시 불안했다."]], [["\"They who have been brought up steady do so, mostly.\"", "“착실하게 자란 분들은 대개 그럽니다요.”"]], [["\"He has been brought up steady, Mrs. Roper.", "“그 애는 착실하게 자랐어요, 로퍼 부인."], ["He has, indeed.", "정말 그렇답니다."], ["And you won't give him a latch-key?\"", "그리고 그 애한테 대문 열쇠는 안 주시겠지요?”"]], [["\"Well, they always do ask for it.\"", "“글쎄요, 다들 꼭 달라고들 하기는 합니다요.”"]], [["\"But he won't insist, if you tell him that I had rather that he shouldn't have one.\"", "“하지만 제가 그 애가 그걸 갖지 않기를 바란다고 말씀해 주시면 그 애도 우기지는 않을 거예요.”"]], [["Mrs. Roper promised accordingly, and Johnny Eames was left under her charge.", "로퍼 부인은 그러겠다고 약속했고, 조니 임스는 그녀의 보호 아래 맡겨졌다."], ["He did ask for the latch-key, and Mrs. Roper answered as she was bidden.", "그는 정말로 열쇠를 달라고 했고, 로퍼 부인은 시킨 대로 대답했다."], ["But he asked again, having been sophisticated by the philosophy of Cradell, and then Mrs. Roper handed him the key.", "그러나 그는 크래들의 궤변에 물이 들어 다시 청했고, 그러자 로퍼 부인은 그에게 열쇠를 건네주었다."], ["She was a woman who plumed herself on being as good as her word, not understanding that any one could justly demand from her more than that.", "그녀는 제 말을 지키는 사람이라는 것을 자랑으로 삼는 여인이었고, 누구든 그 이상을 자기에게 요구할 수 있다고는 생각하지 않았다."], ["She gave Johnny Eames the key, as doubtless she had intended to do; for Mrs.", "그녀는 조니 임스에게 열쇠를 주었는데, 처음부터 그럴 작정이었음이 틀림없다."], ["Roper knew the world, and understood that young men without latch-keys would not remain with her.", "로퍼 부인은 세상을 알았고, 열쇠 없는 젊은이들은 제 집에 머물러 있지 않으리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다."]], [["\"I thought you didn't seem to find it so dull since Amelia came home,\"", "“어밀리아가 집에 돌아온 뒤로는 그렇게 따분해 보이지 않던데.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."]], [["\"Amelia!", "“어밀리아라니!"], ["What's Amelia to me?", "어밀리아가 나한테 무슨 상관이야?"], ["I have told you everything, Cradell, and yet you can talk to me about Amelia Roper!\"", "크래들, 내가 너한테 전부 털어놓았는데도 나한테 어밀리아 로퍼 이야기를 할 수 있어?”"]], [["\"Come now, Johnny--\"", "“이봐, 조니—”"], ["He had always been called Johnny, and the name had gone with him to his office.", "그는 늘 조니라고 불렸고, 그 이름은 관청까지 그를 따라왔다."], ["Even Amelia Roper had called him Johnny on more than one occasion before this.", "어밀리아 로퍼조차 이 일이 있기 전에 여러 번 그를 조니라고 불렀다. “요전 날 밤에 너는 세상에 L."], ["\"You were as sweet to her the other night as though there were no such person as L. D.", "D.라는 사람이 있지도 않은 것처럼 그 아가씨한테 살갑게 굴었잖아.”"], ["in existence.\" John Eames turned away and shook his head.", "존 임스는 고개를 돌리고 머리를 내저었다."], ["Nevertheless, the words of his friend were grateful to him.", "그럼에도 벗의 그 말은 그에게 고마웠다."], ["The character of a Don Juan was not unpleasant to his imagination, and he liked to think that he might amuse Amelia Roper with a passing word, though his heart was true to Lilian Dale.", "돈 후안이라는 배역은 그의 상상에 그리 싫지 않았고, 마음은 릴리언 데일에게 참되면서도 지나가는 말 몇 마디로 어밀리아 로퍼를 즐겁게 해 줄 수 있다고 생각하는 것이 좋았다."], ["In truth, however, many more of the passing words had been spoken by the fair Amelia than by him.", "그러나 사실인즉 그 지나가는 말들은 그보다 어여쁜 어밀리아 쪽에서 훨씬 많이 나왔다."]], [["Mrs. Roper had been quite as good as her word when she told Mrs. Eames that her household was composed of herself, of a son who was in an attorney's office, of an ancient maiden cousin, named Miss Spruce, who lodged with her, and of Mr. Cradell. The divine Amelia had not then been living with her, and the nature of the statement which she was making by no means compelled her to inform Mrs. Eames that the young lady would probably return home in the following winter. A Mr. and Mrs. Lupex had also joined the family lately, and Mrs. Roper's house was now supposed to be full.", "로퍼 부인은 제 식구가 자기 자신과, 변호사 사무실에 다니는 아들과, 함께 묵고 있는 스프루스 양이라는 나이 지긋한 노처녀 사촌과, 크래들 씨로 이루어져 있다고 임스 부인에게 말했을 때 어김없이 제 말 그대로였다. 그 거룩하신 어밀리아는 그때 그녀와 함께 살고 있지 않았고, 그녀가 하고 있던 그 이야기의 성격상 그 아가씨가 아마 다음 겨울에 집으로 돌아오리라는 것까지 임스 부인에게 알려야 할 까닭은 조금도 없었다. 루펙스 씨 부부도 근래에 이 식구에 끼어들었고, 이제 로퍼 부인의 집은 다 찬 것으로 여겨졌다."]], [["And it must be acknowledged that Johnny Eames had, in certain unguarded moments, confided to Cradell the secret of a second weaker passion for Amelia.", "그리고 조니 임스가 어느 방심한 순간에 어밀리아를 향한, 그보다 약한 두 번째 연정의 비밀을 크래들에게 털어놓은 적이 있음은 인정해야겠다."], ["\"She is a fine girl,--a deuced fine girl!\"", "“괜찮은 아가씨야, 되게 괜찮은 아가씨라고!”"], ["Johnny Eames had said, using a style of language which he had learned since he left Guestwick and Allington. Mr.", "게스트윅과 앨링턴을 떠난 뒤에 배운 말투를 써서 조니 임스는 그렇게 말했다."], ["Cradell, also, was an admirer of the fair sex; and, alas!", "크래들 씨 또한 여성을 흠모하는 사람이었다."], ["that I should say so, Mrs. Lupex, at the present moment, was the object of his admiration.", "그리고, 아아, 이런 말을 해야 하다니, 지금으로 말하면 그 흠모의 대상은 루펙스 부인이었다."], ["Not that he entertained the slightest idea of wronging Mr.", "그렇다고 그가 루펙스 씨에게 몹쓸 짓을 할 생각을 털끝만큼이라도 품었다는 것은 아니다."], ["Lupex,--a man who was a scene-painter, and knew the world. Mr.", "루펙스 씨는 무대 배경 화가였고 세상 물정을 아는 사람이었다."], ["Cradell admired Mrs. Lupex as a connoisseur, not simply as a man.", "크래들 씨는 그저 사내로서가 아니라 감식가로서 루펙스 부인을 흠모했다."], ["\"By heavens! Johnny, what a figure that woman has!\"", "“이야, 조니, 저 여자 몸매 좀 보라고!”"], ["he said, one morning, as they were walking to their office.", "어느 날 아침 관청으로 걸어가는 길에 그가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; she stands well on her pins.\"", "“그래, 다리 하나는 튼튼하게 잘 섰더군.”"]], [["\"I should think she did.", "“그렇고말고."], ["If I understand anything of form,\"", "내가 몸매라는 걸 조금이라도 안다면,”"], ["said Cradell, \"that woman is nearly perfect.", "크래들이 말했다. “저 여자는 거의 완벽해."], ["What a torso she has!\"", "저 상반신 좀 보라니까!”"]], [["From which expression, and from the fact that Mrs. Lupex depended greatly upon her stays and crinoline for such figure as she succeeded in displaying, it may, perhaps, be understood that Mr. Cradell did not understand much about form.", "이 표현으로 보아, 또 루펙스 부인이 그럭저럭 내보이는 데 성공한 그 몸매라는 것이 코르셋과 크리놀린에 크게 기대고 있었다는 사실로 보아, 크래들 씨가 몸매에 대해 그리 아는 것이 없었음을 아마 짐작할 수 있으리라."]], [["\"It seems to me that her nose isn't quite straight,\"", "“내가 보기엔 코가 그리 반듯하지 않던데.”"], ["said Johnny Eames.", "조니 임스가 말했다."], ["Now, it undoubtedly was the fact that the nose on Mrs. Lupex's face was a little awry.", "그런데 루펙스 부인의 얼굴에 붙은 코가 조금 비뚤어져 있었다는 것은 의심할 여지 없는 사실이었다."], ["It was a long, thin nose, which, as it progressed forward into the air, certainly had a preponderating bias towards the left side.", "그것은 길고 가느다란 코였는데, 앞으로 허공을 향해 뻗어 나가면서 분명히 왼쪽으로 기우는 쪽이 우세했다."]], [["\"I care more for figure than face,\"", "“나는 얼굴보다 몸매를 더 치거든.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."], ["\"But Mrs. Lupex has fine eyes--very fine eyes.\"", "“그래도 루펙스 부인은 눈이 곱잖아, 아주 고운 눈이지.”"]], [["\"And knows how to use them, too,\"", "“게다가 그 눈을 쓸 줄도 알고.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Why shouldn't she?", "“쓸 줄 알면 안 될 게 뭐야?"], ["And then she has lovely hair.\"", "게다가 머리카락도 사랑스럽지.”"]], [["\"Only she never brushes it in the morning.\"", "“다만 아침에는 도무지 빗질을 안 하지.”"]], [["\"Do you know, I like that kind of deshabille,\"", "“있잖아, 나는 그런 흐트러진 차림이 좋더라.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."], ["\"Too much care always betrays itself.\"", "“너무 공을 들이면 늘 티가 나는 법이야.”"]], [["\"But a woman should be tidy.\"", "“그래도 여자는 단정해야지.”"]], [["\"What a word to apply to such a creature as Mrs.", "“루펙스 부인 같은 분한테 단정이라니, 그게 무슨 말이야!"], ["Lupex! I call her a splendid woman.", "나는 그분을 훌륭한 여인이라고 부르겠어."], ["And how well she was got up last night.", "게다가 어젯밤엔 차림새가 얼마나 근사했다고."], ["Do you know, I've an idea that Lupex treats her very badly.", "있잖아, 나는 루펙스가 부인을 아주 못되게 대하는 것 같아."], ["She said a word or two to me yesterday that--,\"", "어제 그분이 나한테 이런 말을 한두 마디 했는데—”"], ["and then he paused.", "그러고는 그가 말을 멈추었다."], ["There are some confidences which a man does not share even with his dearest friend.", "아무리 다정한 벗에게라도 나누지 않는 속내라는 것이 있는 법이다."]], [["\"I rather fancy it's quite the other way,\"", "“내 생각엔 오히려 그 반대인 것 같은데.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"How the other way?\"", "“반대라니 어떻게?”"]], [["\"That Lupex has quite as much as he likes of Mrs. L.", "“루펙스 쪽이 루펙스 부인한테 실컷 시달린다는 말이지."], ["The sound of her voice sometimes makes me shake in my shoes, I know.\"", "나는 가끔 그 목소리만 들려도 신발 속에서 다리가 다 떨린다니까.”"]], [["\"I like a woman with spirit,\"", "“나는 기개 있는 여자가 좋아.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, so do I.", "“아, 나도 그래."], ["But one may have too much of a good thing.", "하지만 좋은 것도 지나치면 탈이지."], ["Amelia did tell me;--only you won't mention it.\"", "어밀리아가 나한테 말해 줬는데, 다만 너 이 말은 옮기지 마.”"]], [["\"Of course, I won't.\"", "“물론 안 옮기지.”"]], [["\"She told me that Lupex sometimes was obliged to run away from her.", "“루펙스가 가끔은 부인한테서 달아나야만 한다더군."], ["He goes down to the theatre, and remains there two or three days at a time.", "극장으로 내려가서 한 번에 이삼 일씩 거기 눌러 있는다는 거야."], ["Then she goes to fetch him, and there is no end of a row in the house.\"", "그러면 부인이 데리러 가고, 그러고 나면 집 안이 온통 난리가 끝이 없다지.”"]], [["\"The fact is, he drinks,\"", "“실은 그자가 술을 마시는 거야.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."], ["\"By George, I pity a woman whose husband drinks--and such a woman as that, too!\"", "“이런, 남편이 술을 마시는 여자는 정말 딱해. 더구나 그런 여인이 말이야!”"]], [["\"Take care, old fellow, or you'll find yourself in a scrape.\"", "“조심해, 이 친구야. 안 그러면 곤경에 빠질 거야.”"]], [["\"I know what I'm at.", "“내가 뭘 하는지는 내가 알아."], ["Lord bless you, I'm not going to lose my head because I see a fine woman.\"", "원 참, 고운 여자 하나 봤다고 정신을 놓을 내가 아니야.”"]], [["\"Or your heart either?\"", "“마음도 놓지 않고?”"]], [["\"Oh, heart!", "“아, 마음이라니!"], ["There's nothing of that kind of thing about me.", "나한테 그런 건 없어."], ["I regard a woman as a picture or a statue.", "나는 여자를 그림이나 조각처럼 보거든."], ["I dare say I shall marry some day, because men do; but I've no idea of losing myself about a woman.\"", "언젠가는 나도 장가를 들겠지, 사내라면 다들 그러니까. 하지만 여자 하나 때문에 나를 잃을 생각은 없어.”"]], [["\"I'd lose myself ten times over for--\"", "“나는 열 번이라도 나를 잃겠어, 그—”"]], [["\"L.", "“L."], ["D.,\"", "D."], ["said Cradell.", "말이지.” 크래들이 말했다."]], [["\"That I would.", "“정말 그러겠어."], ["And yet I know I shall never have her.", "그러면서도 나는 그녀를 결코 얻지 못하리라는 걸 알아."], ["I'm a jolly, laughing sort of fellow; and yet, do you know, Caudle, when that girl marries, it will be all up with me.", "나는 유쾌하게 잘 웃는 축이지. 그런데 말이야, 코들, 그 아가씨가 시집가는 날이면 나는 끝장이야."], ["It will, indeed.\"", "정말로 그래.”"]], [["\"Do you mean that you'll cut your throat?\"", "“네 목이라도 긋겠다는 말이야?”"]], [["\"No; I shan't do that.", "“아니, 그러지는 않아."], ["I shan't do anything of that sort; and yet it will be all up with me.\"", "그런 짓은 하지 않을 거야. 그래도 나는 끝장이야.”"]], [["\"You are going down there in October;--why don't you ask her to have you?\"", "“시월에 거기 내려간다며. 그럼 자기를 받아 달라고 청해 보지 그래?”"]], [["\"With ninety pounds a year!\"", "“한 해 구십 파운드로!”"], ["His grateful country had twice increased his salary at the rate of five pounds each year.", "고마우신 나라가 해마다 오 파운드씩 두 차례 그의 봉급을 올려 주었던 것이다."], ["\"With ninety pounds a year, and twenty allowed me by my mother!\"", "“한 해 구십 파운드에, 어머니가 대 주시는 이십 파운드로!”"]], [["\"She could wait, I suppose.", "“기다려 줄 수도 있잖아."], ["I should ask her, and no mistake.", "나 같으면 두말없이 청하겠어."], ["If one is to love a girl, it's no good one going on in that way!\"", "아가씨를 사랑할 바에야 그런 식으로 끌고 가 봐야 아무 소용 없어!”"]], [["\"It isn't much good, certainly,\"", "“소용이 없기야 없지, 그건 확실해.”"], ["said Johnny Eames.", "조니 임스가 말했다."], ["And then they reached the door of the Income-tax Office, and each went away to his own desk.", "그러고 나서 그들은 소득세 사무국 문 앞에 이르렀고, 저마다 제 책상으로 흩어졌다."]], [["From this little dialogue, it may be imagined that though Mrs. Roper was as good as her word, she was not exactly the woman whom Mrs.", "이 짤막한 대화만 보아도, 로퍼 부인이 제 말을 지키는 사람이기는 했으나 임스 부인이 제 아들의 수호천사로 고르고 싶어 했을 만한 여인은 아니었음을 짐작할 수 있을 것이다."], ["Eames would have wished to select as a protecting angel for her son.", "그러나 내가 보기에 진실은 이렇다. 한 해 사십팔 파운드를 분기마다 나누어 받고 과부의 아들을 지켜 줄 수호천사란 런던에서 그리 쉽사리 구해지지 않는다는 것이다."], ["But the truth I take to be this, that protecting angels for widows'", "로퍼 부인은 같은 처지의 다른 이들보다 나쁠 것이 없었다."], ["sons, at forty-eight pounds a year, paid quarterly, are not to be found very readily in London.", "그녀는 점잖지 못한 하숙인보다 점잖은 하숙인을 훨씬 더 반겼을 것이다."], ["Mrs. Roper was not worse than others of her class.", "원하는 대로 점잖은 하숙인을 구할 수만 있었다면 말이다."], ["She would much have preferred lodgers who were respectable to those who were not so,--if she could only have found respectable lodgers as she wanted them. Mr.", "루펙스 씨 부부는 도무지 그 축에 들지 못했다."], ["and Mrs. Lupex hardly came under that denomination; and when she gave them up her big front bedroom at a hundred a year, she knew she was doing wrong.", "그리고 한 해 백 파운드에 그들에게 앞쪽의 큰 침실을 내주었을 때, 그녀는 자기가 잘못하고 있음을 알고 있었다."], ["And she was troubled, too, about her own daughter Amelia, who was already over thirty years of age.", "그녀는 이미 서른이 넘은 제 딸 어밀리아 때문에도 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["Amelia was a very clever young woman, who had been, if the truth must be told, first young lady at a millinery establishment in Manchester. Mrs.", "어밀리아는 아주 영리한 젊은 여자로, 사실대로 말하자면 맨체스터의 어느 모자 가게에서 수석 점원 아가씨 노릇을 하던 사람이었다."], ["Roper knew that Mrs. Eames and Mrs.", "로퍼 부인은 임스 부인도 크래들 부인도 제 아들이 자기 딸과 어울리기를 바라지 않으리라는 것을 알았다."], ["Cradell would not wish their sons to associate with her daughter. But what could she do?", "그러나 어쩌겠는가?"], ["She could not refuse the shelter of her own house to her own child, and yet her heart misgave her when she saw Amelia flirting with young Eames.", "제 자식에게 제 집의 지붕을 마다할 수도 없는 노릇이고, 그러면서도 어밀리아가 젊은 임스와 시시덕거리는 것을 볼 때면 마음이 켕겼다."]], [["\"I wish, Amelia, you wouldn't have so much to say to that young man.\"", "“어밀리아, 저 젊은이한테 그렇게 말을 많이 붙이지 않았으면 좋겠구나.”"]], [["\"Laws, mother.\"", "“원 참, 엄마도.”"]], [["\"So I do.", "“정말 그렇다니까."], ["If you go on like that, you'll put me out of both my lodgers.\"", "네가 그런 식으로 굴면 하숙인 둘을 다 놓치게 될 게다.”"]], [["\"Go on like what, mother?", "“어떤 식으로 군다는 거예요, 엄마?"], ["If a gentleman speaks to me, I suppose I'm to answer him?", "신사분이 나한테 말을 걸면 대답은 해야 하는 것 아녜요?"], ["I know how to behave myself, I believe.\"", "내 처신쯤은 나도 안다고요.”"], ["And then she gave her head a toss.", "그러고는 고개를 홱 젖혔다."], ["Whereupon her mother was silent; for her mother was afraid of her.", "그러자 어머니는 잠자코 있었다. 어머니가 딸을 무서워했기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER V. ABOUT L. D.", "# 제5장. 「L. D.에 대하여」"]], [["Apollo Crosbie left London for Allington on the 31st of August, intending to stay there four weeks, with the declared intention of recruiting his strength by an absence of two months from official cares, and with no fixed purpose as to his destiny for the last of those two months.", "아폴로 크로즈비는 8월 31일에 런던을 떠나 앨링턴으로 향했다. 그곳에 4주를 머무를 작정이었고, 공무의 시달림에서 두 달간 벗어나 기력을 되찾겠다는 것이 그가 내세운 명분이었으며, 그 두 달 가운데 나머지 한 달을 어디서 보낼지에 대해서는 아직 정해 둔 바가 없었다."], ["Offers of hospitality had been made to him by the dozen.", "그를 맞아들이겠다는 초대는 열두 통도 넘게 들어와 있었다."], ["Lady Hartletop's doors, in Shropshire, were open to him, if he chose to enter them.", "슈롭셔에 있는 레이디 하틀탑의 저택 문은 그가 들어서기만 하면 언제든 열려 있었다."], ["He had been invited by the Countess De Courcy to join her suite at Courcy Castle.", "드 코시 백작부인은 코시 성에서 자기 일행에 합류하라고 그를 청했다."], ["His special friend Montgomerie Dobbs had a place in Scotland, and then there was a yachting party by which he was much wanted.", "각별한 벗인 몽고메리 돕스는 스코틀랜드에 별장을 두고 있었고, 그 밖에 그가 오기를 몹시 바라는 요트 놀이 일행도 있었다."], ["But Mr. Crosbie had as yet knocked himself down to none of these biddings, having before him when he left London no other fixed engagement than that which took him to Allington.", "그러나 크로즈비 씨는 아직 그 어느 부름에도 자신을 낙찰시키지 않은 채였으니, 런던을 떠날 때 그의 앞에 잡혀 있던 확정된 약속이라고는 그를 앨링턴으로 데려가는 그것 하나뿐이었다."], ["On the first of October we shall also find ourselves at Allington in company with Johnny Eames; and Apollo Crosbie will still be there,--by no means to the comfort of our friend from the Income-tax Office.", "10월 1일이 되면 우리 역시 조니 임스와 더불어 앨링턴에 가 있게 될 것이다. 그리고 아폴로 크로즈비는 그때까지도 그곳에 있을 터인데, 소득세 사무국에서 온 우리 벗에게는 결코 마음 편할 일이 아니었다."]], [["Johnny Eames cannot be called unlucky in that matter of his annual holiday, seeing that he was allowed to leave London in October, a month during which few chose to own that they remain in town.", "연차 휴가라는 문제에 관한 한 조니 임스를 운 나쁜 사람이라 부를 수는 없다. 그는 10월에 런던을 떠나도 좋다는 허락을 받았고, 10월이란 시내에 남아 있다고 스스로 인정하려 드는 이가 거의 없는 달이기 때문이다."], ["For myself, I always regard May as the best month for holiday-making; but then no Londoner cares to be absent in May.", "나로 말하자면 휴가를 보내기에 가장 좋은 달은 언제나 5월이라고 여긴다. 하지만 런던 사람이라면 5월에 자리를 비우고 싶어 하는 법이 없다."], ["Young Eames, though he lived in Burton Crescent and had as yet no connection with the West End, had already learned his lesson in this respect.", "젊은 임스는 버턴 크레슨트에 살았고 아직 웨스트엔드와는 아무 연줄도 없었지만, 이 점에 관해서만은 이미 배울 것을 다 배워 두었다."], ["\"Those fellows in the big room want me to take May,\"", "“큰 방에 있는 그 작자들이 나더러 5월에 가라더군,”"], ["he had said to his friend Cradell.", "그는 벗 크래들에게 그렇게 말한 적이 있었다."], ["\"They must think I'm uncommon green.\"", "“나를 어지간히 어수룩한 놈으로 아는 모양이야.”"]], [["\"It's too bad,\"", "“너무하잖아,”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."], ["\"A man shouldn't be asked to take his leave in May.", "“5월에 휴가를 가라고 사람한테 요구할 일이 아니지."], ["I never did, and what's more, I never will.", "나는 한 번도 그런 적 없고, 더구나 앞으로도 절대 그럴 생각이 없어."], ["I'd go to the Board first.\"", "그러느니 위원회에 올라가고 말겠어.”"]], [["Eames had escaped this evil without going to the Board, and had succeeded in obtaining for himself for his own holiday that month of October, which, of all months, is perhaps the most highly esteemed for holiday purposes.", "임스는 위원회까지 올라가지 않고도 이 재난을 피했고, 자기 휴가로 10월을 얻어 내는 데 성공했다. 10월은 온갖 달 가운데서도 휴가를 보내기에 가장 높이 쳐주는 달일 것이다."], ["\"I shall go down by the mail-train to-morrow night,\"", "“내일 밤 우편 열차로 내려가려고요,”"], ["he said to Amelia Roper, on the evening before his departure. At that moment he was sitting alone with Amelia in Mrs.", "그는 떠나기 전날 저녁 어밀리아 로퍼에게 그렇게 말했다."], ["Roper's back drawing-room. In the front room Cradell was talking to Mrs.", "그때 그는 로퍼 부인네 뒤쪽 응접실에서 어밀리아와 단둘이 앉아 있었다."], ["Lupex; but as Miss Spruce was with them, it may be presumed that Mr. Lupex need have had no cause for jealousy.", "앞방에서는 크래들이 루펙스 부인과 이야기를 나누고 있었으나, 스프루스 양이 그들과 함께 있었으니 루펙스 씨로서는 질투할 까닭이 조금도 없었으리라고 짐작해도 좋았다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“그러시겠죠,”"], ["said Amelia; \"I know how great is your haste to get down to that fascinating spot.", "어밀리아가 말했다. “그 매혹적인 고장으로 내려가고 싶어 얼마나 급하신지 잘 알아요."], ["I could not expect that you would lose one single hour in hurrying away from Burton Crescent.\"", "버턴 크레슨트를 떠나느라 서두르시는 마당에 단 한 시간이라도 허비하실 리는 없겠죠.”"]], [["Amelia Roper was a tall, well-grown young woman, with dark hair and dark eyes;--not handsome, for her nose was thick, and the lower part of her face was heavy, but yet not without some feminine attractions.", "어밀리아 로퍼는 키가 크고 몸이 잘 자란 젊은 여자로, 머리와 눈이 검었다. 미인이라고는 할 수 없었으니 코가 두툼했고 얼굴 아랫부분이 무거웠기 때문이다."], ["Her eyes were bright; but then, also, they were mischievous.", "그렇다고 여자다운 매력이 전혀 없지는 않았다. 눈은 반짝였다."], ["She could talk fluently enough; but then, also, she could scold.", "다만 그 눈은 짓궂기도 했다. 말은 제법 거침없이 잘했다."], ["She could assume sometimes the plumage of a dove; but then again she could occasionally ruffle her feathers like an angry kite.", "다만 잔소리도 잘했다. 어떤 때는 비둘기의 깃털을 두른 듯싶다가도, 또 어떤 때는 성난 솔개처럼 깃을 곤두세울 줄도 알았다."], ["I am quite prepared to acknowledge that John Eames should have kept himself clear of Amelia Roper; but then young men so frequently do those things which they should not do!", "존 임스가 어밀리아 로퍼와는 얽히지 말았어야 했다는 것을 나는 기꺼이 인정한다. 하지만 젊은 사내들이란 해서는 안 될 일을 얼마나 자주 저지르고 마는가!"]], [["\"After twelve months up here in London one is glad to get away to one's own friends,\"", "“런던에서 열두 달을 지내고 나면 제 벗들이 있는 곳으로 훌쩍 떠나는 게 반가운 법이죠,”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Your own friends, Mr. Eames!", "“당신 벗들이라고요, 임스 씨!"], ["What sort of friends?", "어떤 벗들인데요?"], ["Do you suppose I don't know?\"", "내가 모를 줄 아세요?”"]], [["\"Well, no.", "“글쎄요, 모르실걸요."], ["I don't think you do know.\"", "아신다고는 생각하지 않는데요.”"]], [["\"L.", "“L."], ["D.!\"", "D.!”"], ["said Amelia, showing that Lily had been spoken of among people who should never have been allowed to hear her name.", "어밀리아가 말했다. 이는 릴리의 이름이 결코 들어서는 안 될 사람들 사이에서 입에 오르내렸다는 뜻이었다."], ["But perhaps, after all, no more than those two initials were known in Burton Crescent.", "하지만 어쩌면 버턴 크레슨트에 알려진 것이라고는 그 머리글자 두 자뿐이었는지도 모른다."], ["From the tone which was now used in naming them, it was sufficiently manifest that Amelia considered herself to be wronged by their very existence.", "지금 그 글자를 부르는 말투로 보아, 어밀리아가 그 두 글자의 존재 자체를 자신에 대한 부당한 처사로 여기고 있다는 것만은 넉넉히 드러났다."]], [["\"L.", "“L."], ["S.", "S."], ["D.,\"", "D.죠,”"], ["said Johnny, attempting the line of a witty, gay young spendthrift.", "조니가 재치 있고 쾌활한 젊은 난봉꾼 흉내를 내며 말했다. “그게 내 사랑입니다."], ["\"That's my love--pounds, shillings, and pence; and a very coy mistress she is.\"", "파운드, 실링, 펜스 말이에요. 게다가 여간 새침한 애인이 아니라니까요.”"]], [["\"Nonsense, sir.", "“실없는 소리 마세요."], ["Don't talk to me in that way.", "나한테 그런 식으로 말하지 마시라고요."], ["As if I didn't know where your heart was.", "내가 당신 마음이 어디 가 있는지 모르는 줄 아세요?"], ["What right had you to speak to me if you had an L.", "시골에 L."], ["D. down in the country?\"", "D.를 두고 있으면서 무슨 자격으로 나한테 말을 걸었나요?”"]], [["It should be here declared on behalf of poor John Eames that he had not ever spoken to Amelia--he had not spoken to her in any such phrase as her words seemed to imply. But then he had written to her a fatal note of which we will speak further before long, and that perhaps was quite as bad,--or worse.", "가엾은 존 임스를 위하여 여기서 한마디 밝혀 두어야겠다. 그는 어밀리아에게 그런 말을 한 적이 없었다. 적어도 그녀의 말이 뜻하는 듯한 그런 투로 말한 적은 없었다. 그러나 그는 그녀에게 치명적인 쪽지 한 장을 써 보낸 일이 있었으니, 그 이야기는 머지않아 더 하게 될 것이다. 그리고 그것은 아마 말을 한 것이나 매한가지로 나빴을 터이다. 아니, 더 나빴을 것이다."]], [["\"Ha, ha, ha!\"", "“하하하!”"], ["laughed Johnny.", "조니가 웃었다."], ["But the laugh was assumed, and not assumed with ease.", "그러나 그 웃음은 꾸며 낸 것이었고, 게다가 편안하게 꾸며 낸 것도 아니었다."]], [["\"Yes, sir; it's a laughing matter to you, I dare say.", "“그래요, 당신한테야 웃을 일이겠죠. 사내가 이런 판에 웃기란 아주 쉬운 일이죠."], ["It is very easy for a man to laugh under such circumstances;--that is to say, if he is perfectly heartless,--if he's got a stone inside his bosom instead of flesh and blood.", "그러니까 하는 말이지만, 마음이라고는 아예 없는 사람이라면 말이에요. 가슴속에 살과 피 대신 돌덩이가 들어앉은 사람이라면요."], ["Some men are made of stone, I know, and are troubled with no feelings.\"", "어떤 사내들은 돌로 만들어져서 아무런 감정에도 시달리지 않는다는 걸 나도 알아요.”"]], [["\"What is it you want me to say?", "“나더러 대체 무슨 말을 하라는 겁니까?"], ["You pretend to know all about it, and it wouldn't be civil in me to contradict you.\"", "다 알고 계신다면서요. 내가 아니라고 대들면 예의가 아니겠죠.”"]], [["\"What is it I want?", "“내가 뭘 바라느냐고요?"], ["You know very well what I want; or rather, I don't want anything.", "내가 뭘 바라는지 잘 아시잖아요. 아니, 그보다 나는 아무것도 바라지 않아요."], ["What is it to me?", "나한테 무슨 상관이겠어요?"], ["It is nothing to me about L.", "L. D.가 나하고 무슨 상관이냐고요."], ["D. You can go down to Allington and do what you like for me.", "앨링턴에 내려가서 좋을 대로 하세요, 나는 아무래도 좋으니까."], ["Only I hate such ways.\"", "다만 나는 그런 수작이 싫을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["\"What ways, Amelia?\"", "“무슨 수작 말입니까, 어밀리아?”"]], [["\"What ways!", "“무슨 수작이냐고요! 자, 들어 봐요, 조니."], ["Now, look here, Johnny: I'm not going to make a fool of myself for any man.", "나는 어떤 사내를 위해서도 바보 노릇 할 생각이 없어요. 석 달 전에 내가 이 집에 돌아왔을 때, 아 정말이지 돌아오지 말걸 그랬어요.”"], ["When I came home here three months ago--and I wish I never had;\"--she paused here a moment, waiting for a word of tenderness; but as the word of tenderness did not come, she went on--\"but when I did come home, I didn't think there was a man in all London could make me care for him,--that I didn't.", "여기서 그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추고 다정한 한마디를 기다렸다. 그러나 다정한 한마디가 나오지 않자 그녀는 말을 이었다."], ["And now you're going away, without so much as hardly saying a word to me.\"", "“하지만 내가 정말 돌아왔을 때, 나는 온 런던을 뒤져도 내가 마음 쓸 만한 사내는 하나도 없을 줄 알았어요. 정말 그렇게 알았다고요."], ["And then she brought out her handkerchief.", "그런데 이제 당신은 떠나면서, 나한테 말 한마디 제대로 건네지도 않는군요.” 그러고서 그녀는 손수건을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"What am I to say, when you keep on scolding me all the time?\"", "“내내 나를 야단만 치시는데 무슨 말을 하란 말입니까?”"]], [["\"Scolding you!--And me too!", "“야단이라고요! 게다가 나까지 그런다고요!"], ["No, Johnny, I ain't scolding you, and don't mean to.", "아니에요, 조니, 나는 야단치는 게 아니고, 그럴 마음도 없어요."], ["If it's to be all over between us, say the word, and I'll take myself away out of the house before you come back again.", "우리 사이가 다 끝난 거라면 그렇다고 한마디만 하세요. 당신이 돌아오기 전에 내가 이 집에서 나가 버릴 테니까."], ["I've had no secrets from you.", "나는 당신한테 숨긴 게 하나도 없어요."], ["I can go back to my business in Manchester, though it is beneath my birth, and not what I've been used to.", "나는 맨체스터의 내 일자리로 돌아가면 그만이에요. 내 출신에 걸맞지 않고 여태 익숙하던 것도 아니지만 말이에요."], ["If L.", "L."], ["D. is more to you than I am, I won't stand in your way.", "D.가 나보다 당신한테 더 중하다면, 나는 당신 앞길을 막지 않겠어요."], ["Only say the word.\"", "그저 한마디만 하세요.”"]], [["L. D.", "L."], ["was more to him than Amelia Roper,--ten times more to him.", "D.는 그에게 어밀리아 로퍼보다 더 중했다. 열 배는 더 중했다."], ["L. D.", "L."], ["would have been everything to him, and Amelia Roper was worse than nothing.", "D.는 그에게 전부였을 것이고, 어밀리아 로퍼는 아무것도 아닌 것만도 못했다."], ["He felt all this at the moment, and struggled hard to collect an amount of courage that would make him free.", "그는 그 순간 이 모든 것을 느꼈고, 자신을 자유롭게 해 줄 만한 용기를 그러모으려고 안간힘을 썼다."]], [["\"Say the word,\"", "“한마디만 하세요.”"], ["said she, rising on her feet before him, \"and all between you and me shall be over.", "그녀가 그의 앞에서 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. “그러면 당신과 나 사이의 모든 일이 끝나는 거예요."], ["I have got your promise, but I'd scorn to take advantage.", "나는 당신의 약속을 받아 두었지만, 그걸 빌미로 삼는 짓은 경멸해요."], ["If Amelia hasn't got your heart, she'd despise to take your hand.", "어밀리아가 당신의 마음을 얻지 못했다면, 당신의 손을 잡는 일도 하찮게 여길 거예요."], ["Only I must have an answer.\"", "다만 나는 대답을 들어야겠어요.”"]], [["It would seem that an easy way of escape was offered to him; but the lady probably knew that the way as offered by her was not easy to such an one as John Eames.", "빠져나갈 손쉬운 길이 그에게 주어진 듯싶었다. 그러나 그 부인은 자기가 내미는 길이 존 임스 같은 사내에게는 결코 손쉽지 않다는 것을 아마 알고 있었을 것이다."]], [["\"Amelia,\"", "“어밀리아,”"], ["he said, still keeping his seat.", "그가 여전히 자리에 앉은 채로 말했다."]], [["\"Well, sir?\"", "“네, 말씀하세요.”"]], [["\"You know I love you.\"", "“내가 당신을 사랑하는 거 알잖아요.”"]], [["\"And about L.", "“그럼 L."], ["D.?\"", "D.는요?”"]], [["\"If you choose to believe all the nonsense that Cradell puts into your head, I can't help it.", "“크래들이 당신 머릿속에 집어넣는 그 헛소리를 다 믿겠다면 나로서도 어쩔 수 없죠."], ["If you like to make yourself jealous about two letters, it isn't my fault.\"", "글자 두 자를 가지고 스스로 질투하겠다면, 그건 내 잘못이 아니에요.”"]], [["\"And you love me?\"", "“그럼 나를 사랑하는 거예요?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Of course I love you.\"", "“물론 당신을 사랑하죠.”"], ["And then, upon hearing these words, Amelia threw herself into his arms.", "그리고 이 말을 듣자마자 어밀리아는 그의 품으로 몸을 던졌다."]], [["As the folding doors between the two rooms were not closed, and as Miss Spruce was sitting in her easy chair immediately opposite to them, it was probable that she saw what passed.", "두 방 사이의 접이문은 닫혀 있지 않았고 스프루스 양은 바로 맞은편 안락의자에 앉아 있었으니, 그녀가 그 광경을 보았을 공산이 컸다."], ["But Miss Spruce was a taciturn old lady, not easily excited to any show of surprise or admiration; and as she had lived with Mrs.", "그러나 스프루스 양은 말수가 적은 노부인이어서 놀라움이나 감탄을 좀처럼 겉으로 드러내는 법이 없었다."], ["Roper for the last twelve years, she was probably well acquainted with her daughter's ways.", "게다가 지난 열두 해 동안 로퍼 부인과 함께 살아왔으니, 그 딸의 수법쯤은 아마 익히 알고 있었을 것이다."]], [["\"You'll be true to me?\"", "“나한테 변치 않을 거죠?”"], ["said Amelia, during the moment of that embrace--\"true to me for ever?\"", "그 포옹의 순간에 어밀리아가 말했다. “나한테 영원히 변치 않을 거죠?”"]], [["\"Oh, yes; that's a matter of course,\"", "“아, 그럼요. 그야 두말할 것도 없죠,”"], ["said Johnny Eames.", "조니 임스가 말했다."], ["And then she liberated him; and the two strolled into the front sitting-room.", "그러자 그녀는 그를 놓아주었고, 두 사람은 앞쪽 거실로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어갔다."]], [["\"I declare, Mr.", "“어머, 임스 씨,”"], ["Eames,\" said Mrs.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다."], ["Lupex, \"I'm glad you've come.", "“오시니 반갑네요."], ["Here's Mr. Cradell does say such queer things.\"", "여기 크래들 씨가 하도 해괴한 말씀을 하셔서요.”"]], [["\"Queer things!\"", "“해괴한 말이라니요!”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."], ["\"Now, Miss Spruce, I appeal to you--Have I said any queer things?\"", "“자, 스프루스 양, 당신께 여쭙겠습니다. 제가 해괴한 말을 한 게 있습니까?”"]], [["\"If you did, sir, I didn't notice them,\"", "“그런 게 있었대도 나는 못 알아들었습니다,”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."]], [["\"I noticed them, then,\"", "“그럼 내가 알아들었죠,”"], ["said Mrs. Lupex.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다."], ["\"An unmarried man like Mr. Cradell has no business to know whether a married lady wears a cap or her own hair--has he, Mr.", "“크래들 씨처럼 장가도 안 든 남자가 유부녀의 머리에 얹힌 게 모자인지 제 머리카락인지 알아야 할 까닭이 어디 있느냐 말이에요."], ["Eames?\"", "안 그래요, 임스 씨?”"]], [["\"I don't think I ever know,\"", "“저는 그런 걸 알아본 적이 없는 것 같은데요,” 조니가 말했다."], ["said Johnny, not intending any sarcasm on Mrs. Lupex.", "루펙스 부인을 빈정댈 뜻은 조금도 없었다."]], [["\"I dare say not, sir,\"", "“그러시겠죠,”"], ["said the lady.", "그 부인이 말했다."], ["\"We all know where your attention is riveted.", "“당신 눈길이 어디에 못 박혀 있는지야 우리 모두 알고 있으니까요."], ["If you were to wear a cap, my dear, somebody would see the difference very soon--wouldn't they, Miss Spruce?\"", "아가씨, 만약 아가씨가 모자를 쓴다면 누군가는 그 차이를 아주 금세 알아볼걸요. 그렇지 않겠어요, 스프루스 양?”"]], [["\"I dare say they would,\"", "“그렇겠지요,”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."]], [["\"If I could look as nice in a cap as you do, Mrs. Lupex, I'd wear one to-morrow,\"", "“제가 부인만큼 모자가 잘 어울린다면 내일이라도 하나 쓰겠어요, 루펙스 부인,”"], ["said Amelia, who did not wish to quarrel with the married lady at the present moment.", "어밀리아가 말했다. 지금 이 자리에서 그 유부녀와 다투고 싶지는 않았던 것이다."], ["There were occasions, however, on which Mrs. Lupex and Miss Roper were by no means so gracious to each other.", "그러나 루펙스 부인과 로퍼 양이 서로에게 결코 그렇게 상냥하지 않은 때도 있었다."]], [["\"Does Lupex like caps?\"", "“루펙스는 모자를 좋아합니까?”"], ["asked Cradell.", "크래들이 물었다."]], [["\"If I wore a plumed helmet on my head, it's my belief he wouldn't know the difference; nor yet if I had got no head at all.", "“내가 깃털 투구를 머리에 얹고 있어도 그이는 차이를 모를 거예요. 아니, 내 머리통이 통째로 없어져도 모를걸요."], ["That's what comes of getting married.", "시집을 가면 그렇게 되는 거랍니다."], ["If you'll take my advice, Miss Roper, you'll stay as you are; even though somebody should break his heart about it.", "내 말을 들으시려거든, 로퍼 양, 지금 그대로 계세요. 누가 그 일로 가슴이 찢어지는 한이 있더라도 말이에요."], ["Wouldn't you, Miss Spruce?\"", "안 그래요, 스프루스 양?”"]], [["\"Oh, as for me, I'm an old woman, you know,\"", "“아이고, 나로 말하면 늙은이 아닙니까,”"], ["said Miss Spruce, which was certainly true.", "스프루스 양이 말했고, 그것은 분명 사실이었다."]], [["\"I don't see what any woman gets by marrying,\"", "“여자가 시집가서 얻는 게 뭔지 나는 도무지 모르겠어요,”"], ["continued Mrs. Lupex.", "루펙스 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["\"But a man gains everything.", "“하지만 남자는 전부를 얻지요."], ["He don't know how to live, unless he's got a woman to help him.\"", "남자란 도와줄 여자가 없으면 사는 법도 모르는 것들이거든요.”"]], [["\"But is love to go for nothing?\"", "“하지만 사랑은 아무것도 아니란 말입니까?”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, love!", "“아이고, 사랑이라니!"], ["I don't believe in love.", "나는 사랑을 안 믿어요."], ["I suppose I thought I loved once, but what did it come to after all?", "한때는 나도 사랑을 한다고 여겼겠지요. 그런데 결국 그게 뭐가 됐나요?"], ["Now, there's Mr.", "자, 여기 임스 씨를 보세요."], ["Eames--we all know he's in love.\"", "저이가 사랑에 빠진 건 우리 모두 알잖아요.”"]], [["\"It comes natural to me, Mrs. Lupex.", "“저는 타고나기를 그렇게 타고났습니다, 루펙스 부인."], ["I was born so,\"", "날 때부터 그랬어요,”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"And there's Miss Roper--one never ought to speak free about a lady, but perhaps she's in love too.\"", "“그리고 여기 로퍼 양도요. 숙녀 이야기를 함부로 입에 올릴 일은 아니지만, 어쩌면 이 아가씨도 사랑에 빠졌을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Speak for yourself, Mrs. Lupex,\"", "“댁 이야기나 하세요, 루펙스 부인,”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"There's no harm in saying that, is there?", "“그렇게 말한다고 해로울 게 있나요?"], ["I'm sure, if you ain't, you're very hard-hearted; for, if ever there was a true lover, I believe you've got one of your own.", "정말이지, 아가씨가 아니라면 아가씨는 어지간히 모진 사람이에요. 세상에 참된 연인이라는 게 있다면 아가씨한테는 아가씨만의 연인이 하나 있으니 말이에요."], ["My!--if there's not Lupex's step on the stair!", "어머나! 저거 계단에 루펙스 발소리 아니에요!"], ["What can bring him home at this hour?", "이 시각에 무슨 일로 집에 들어온담?"], ["If he's been drinking, he'll come home as cross as anything.\"", "술이라도 마셨으면 들어오자마자 어지간히 성을 낼 텐데.”"], ["Then Mr. Lupex entered the room, and the pleasantness of the party was destroyed.", "그러고 나서 루펙스 씨가 방으로 들어섰고, 그 자리의 흥겨움은 깨져 버렸다."]], [["It may be said that neither Mrs. Cradell nor Mrs.", "크래들 부인도 임스 부인도 자기 아들이 어떤 위험에 빠져들지 알았더라면 그 아이들을 버턴 크레슨트에 두지는 않았으리라고 해도 좋을 것이다."], ["Eames would have placed their sons in Burton Crescent if they had known the dangers into which the young men would fall.", "두 젊은이 다 신중하지 못했음은 인정해야겠으나, 둘 다 상대편의 경솔함만은 훤히 보고 있었다."], ["Each, it must be acknowledged, was imprudent; but each clearly saw the imprudence of the other. Not a week before this, Cradell had seriously warned his friend against the arts of Miss Roper.", "이 일이 있기 채 일주일도 전에 크래들은 자기 벗에게 로퍼 양의 술수를 조심하라고 정색하고 경고한 참이었다."], ["\"By George, Johnny, you'll get yourself entangled with that girl.\"", "“이봐 조니, 자네 그 처녀한테 단단히 얽히고 말 거야.”"]], [["\"One always has to go through that sort of thing,\"", "“누구나 한 번쯤은 그런 일을 겪게 마련이지,”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; but those who go through too much of it never get out again.", "“그렇지. 하지만 너무 깊이 겪은 사람은 두 번 다시 못 빠져나와."], ["Where would you be if she got a written promise of marriage from you?\"", "그 여자가 자네한테서 혼인 약속을 글로 받아 두기라도 하면 자네 신세가 어떻게 되겠나?”"]], [["Poor Johnny did not answer this immediately, for in very truth Amelia Roper had such a document in her possession.", "가엾은 조니는 여기에 곧바로 대답하지 못했다. 실은 어밀리아 로퍼가 바로 그런 문서를 손에 쥐고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Where should I be?\" said he.", "“내 신세가 어떻게 되겠느냐고?”"], ["\"Among the breaches of promise, I suppose.\"", "그가 말했다. “혼인 약속 위반 소송 축에 끼겠지 뭐.”"]], [["\"Either that, or else among the victims of matrimony.", "“그렇게 되든지, 아니면 결혼의 희생자 축에 끼든지."], ["My belief of you is, that if you gave such a promise, you'd carry it out.\"", "내가 자네를 보기에는, 자네가 그런 약속을 했다면 자네는 그걸 지킬 사람이야.”"]], [["\"Perhaps I should,\"", "“그럴지도 모르지,”"], ["said Johnny; \"but I don't know.", "조니가 말했다. “하지만 나도 모르겠어."], ["It's a matter of doubt what a man ought to do in such a case.\"", "그런 경우에 사내가 어떻게 해야 옳은지는 아리송한 문제야.”"]], [["\"But there's been nothing of that kind yet?\"", "“하지만 아직 그런 일은 없었단 말이지?”"]], [["\"Oh dear, no!\"", "“저런, 그럼 없고말고!”"]], [["\"If I was you, Johnny, I'd keep away from her.", "“내가 자네라면, 조니, 나는 그 여자를 멀리하겠어."], ["It's very good fun, of course, that sort of thing; but it is so uncommon dangerous!", "물론 그런 일이야 아주 재미가 좋지. 하지만 얼마나 위험한지 몰라!"], ["Where would you be now with such a girl as that for your wife?\"", "그런 처녀를 아내로 두고 나면 자네 신세가 지금 어떻게 되겠나?”"]], [["Such had been the caution given by Cradell to his friend.", "크래들이 자기 벗에게 준 경고란 그런 것이었다."], ["And now, just as he was starting for Allington, Eames returned the compliment.", "그리고 이제 앨링턴으로 떠나려는 참에 임스가 그 답례를 했다."], ["They had gone together to the Great Western station at Paddington, and Johnny tendered his advice as they were walking together up and down the platform.", "두 사람은 함께 패딩턴의 그레이트웨스턴 역까지 갔고, 조니는 나란히 승강장을 오르내리며 자기 충고를 건넸다."]], [["\"I say, Caudle, old boy, you'll find yourself in trouble with that Mrs.", "“이봐 코들, 이 친구야, 자네 그 루펙스 부인 때문에 곤경에 빠질 거야."], ["Lupex, if you don't take care of yourself.\"", "몸조심하지 않으면 말이지.”"]], [["\"But I shall take care of myself.", "“하지만 나는 몸조심할 걸세."], ["There's nothing so safe as a little nonsense with a married woman.", "유부녀와의 실없는 장난만큼 안전한 게 또 어디 있나."], ["Of course, it means nothing, you know, between her and me.\"", "물론 그 여자와 나 사이에 아무 뜻도 없는 일이고 말고.”"]], [["\"I don't suppose it does mean anything.", "“나도 무슨 뜻이 있다고는 생각지 않아."], ["But she's always talking about Lupex being jealous; and if he was to cut up rough, you wouldn't find it pleasant.\"", "하지만 그 여자는 늘 루펙스가 질투한다는 말을 입에 달고 살잖나. 그 사람이 거칠게 나오기라도 하면 자네도 유쾌하진 않을걸.”"]], [["Cradell, however, seemed to think that there was no danger.", "그러나 크래들은 아무 위험도 없다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["His little affair with Mrs. Lupex was quite platonic and safe.", "루펙스 부인과의 그 자그마한 일은 어디까지나 정신적인 것이고 안전했다."], ["As for doing any real harm, his principles, as he assured his friend, were too high.", "정말로 나쁜 짓을 저지르기로 말하면, 그가 벗에게 장담했듯이 그의 지조가 너무 높았다."], ["Mrs. Lupex was a woman of talent, whom no one seemed to understand, and, therefore, he had taken some pleasure in studying her character.", "루펙스 부인은 재능 있는 여인인데 아무도 그녀를 이해하지 못하는 듯싶었고, 그래서 그는 그녀의 성격을 연구하는 데 얼마간 재미를 붙였을 뿐이었다."], ["It was merely a study of character, and nothing more.", "그것은 그저 성격 연구일 뿐 그 이상은 아무것도 아니었다."], ["Then the friends parted, and Eames was carried away by the night mail-train down to Guestwick.", "그러고서 두 벗은 헤어졌고, 임스는 밤 우편 열차에 실려 게스트윅으로 내려갔다."]], [["How his mother was up to receive him at four o'clock in the morning, how her maternal heart was rejoicing at seeing the improvement in his gait, and the manliness of appearance imparted to him by his whiskers, I need not describe at length.", "새벽 네 시에 그의 어머니가 어떻게 그를 맞으려고 일어나 있었는지, 그의 걸음걸이가 나아진 것과 구레나룻이 그에게 얹어 준 사내다운 풍채를 보고 그 어머니의 마음이 어떻게 기뻐 뛰었는지, 나는 길게 늘어놓지 않아도 되리라."], ["Many of the attributes of a hobbledehoy had fallen from him, and even Lily Dale might now probably acknowledge that he was no longer a boy.", "풋내기의 여러 표징이 그에게서 떨어져 나갔으니, 이제는 릴리 데일마저도 그가 더는 소년이 아니라고 아마 인정할 만했다."], ["All which might be regarded as good, if only in putting off childish things he had taken up things which were better than childish.", "이 모든 것을 좋게 여길 수 있을 터인데, 다만 어린아이의 것을 버리면서 그가 집어 든 것들이 어린아이의 것보다 나은 경우에나 그러했다."]], [["On the very first day of his arrival he made his way over to Allington.", "도착한 바로 그 첫날 그는 앨링턴으로 건너갔다."], ["He did not walk on this occasion as he had used to do in the old happy days.", "이번에는 지난날의 행복하던 시절처럼 걸어가지 않았다."], ["He had an idea that it might not be well for him to go into Mrs. Dale's drawing-room with the dust of the road on his boots, and the heat of the day on his brow.", "길바닥 먼지를 장화에 묻히고 한낮의 열기를 이마에 얹은 채 데일 부인의 응접실로 들어서는 것은 좋지 않으리라는 생각이 들었던 것이다."], ["So he borrowed a horse and rode over, taking some pride in a pair of spurs which he had bought in Piccadilly, and in his kid gloves, which were brought out new for the occasion.", "그래서 그는 말을 한 필 빌려 타고 갔는데, 피커딜리에서 사 둔 박차 한 벌과 이 자리를 위해 새로 꺼낸 새끼염소 가죽 장갑이 얼마간 자랑스러웠다."], ["Alas, alas!", "아, 아!"], ["I fear that those two years in London have not improved John Eames; and yet I have to acknowledge that John Eames is one of the heroes of my story.", "런던에서 보낸 그 두 해가 존 임스를 낫게 만들지는 못했구나 싶다. 그러면서도 나는 존 임스가 내 이야기의 주인공 가운데 하나라는 것을 인정해야만 한다."]], [["On entering Mrs. Dale's drawing-room he found Mrs.", "데일 부인의 응접실로 들어서면서 그는 데일 부인과 그 맏딸을 보았다."], ["Dale and her eldest daughter. Lily at the moment was not there, and as he shook hands with the other two, of course, he asked for her.", "그 순간 릴리는 거기 없었고, 그래서 그는 다른 두 사람과 악수를 하면서 당연하게도 릴리는 어디 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["\"She is only in the garden,\"", "“정원에 나가 있을 뿐이에요,”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"She will be here directly.\"", "“곧 올 거예요.”"]], [["\"She has walked across to the Great House with Mr.", "“크로즈비 씨하고 큰 집까지 걸어갔단다,”"], ["Crosbie,\" said Mrs.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["Dale; \"but she is not going to remain.", "“하지만 거기 머물러 있지는 않을 거야."], ["She will be so glad to see you, John!", "자네를 보면 얼마나 반가워하겠니, 존!"], ["We all expected you to-day.\"", "오늘 온다고 우리 모두 기다리고 있었단다.”"]], [["\"Did you?\"", "“그러셨어요?” 조니가 말했다."], ["said Johnny, whose heart had been plunged into cold water at the mention of Mr. Crosbie's name.", "크로즈비 씨의 이름이 나오는 순간 그의 심장은 찬물 속으로 곤두박질쳐 있었다."], ["He had been thinking of Lilian Dale ever since his friend had left him on the railway platform; and, as I beg to assure all ladies who may read my tale, the truth of his love for Lily had moulted no feather through that unholy liaison between him and Miss Roper.", "그는 철도 승강장에서 벗과 헤어진 뒤로 줄곧 릴리언 데일을 생각하고 있었다. 그리고 내 이야기를 읽어 줄 모든 숙녀들께 감히 장담하건대, 릴리를 향한 그의 사랑은 로퍼 양과의 그 불경한 관계로 인해 깃털 하나 빠지지 않은 채였다."], ["I fear that I shall be disbelieved in this; but it was so.", "이 대목에서 내가 믿음을 얻지 못하리라는 것은 나도 안다."], ["His heart was and ever had been true to Lilian, although he had allowed himself to be talked into declarations of affection by such a creature as Amelia Roper.", "그러나 사실이 그러했다. 어밀리아 로퍼 같은 여자에게 넘어가 애정을 고백하는 말까지 흘리고 다녔으면서도, 그의 마음은 지금도 언제나 릴리언에게 참되었다."], ["He had been thinking of his meeting with Lily all the night and throughout the morning, and now he heard that she was walking alone about the gardens with a strange gentleman.", "그는 밤새도록, 그리고 아침 내내 릴리와 만날 일을 생각하고 있었는데, 이제 그녀가 낯선 신사와 단둘이 정원을 거닐고 있다는 말을 들은 것이다."], ["That Mr. Crosbie was very grand and very fashionable he had heard, but he knew no more of him.", "크로즈비 씨가 아주 훌륭하고 아주 멋들어진 사람이라는 이야기는 들은 적이 있었으나, 그 밖에는 그에 관해 아는 것이 없었다."], ["Why should Mr. Crosbie be allowed to walk with Lily Dale?", "어째서 크로즈비 씨가 릴리 데일과 함께 걷도록 허락받는단 말인가?"], ["And why should Mrs. Dale mention the circumstance as though it were quite a thing of course?", "그리고 어째서 데일 부인은 그 일이 지극히 당연한 일이라도 되는 양 입에 올린단 말인가?"], ["Such mystery as there was in this was solved very quickly.", "여기 서린 수수께끼는 아주 빠르게 풀렸다."]], [["\"I'm sure Lily won't object to my telling such a dear friend as you what has happened,\"", "“자네처럼 소중한 벗한테 무슨 일이 있었는지 알려 준다고 릴리가 싫어하지는 않을 게다,”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"She is engaged to be married to Mr. Crosbie.\"", "“그 애가 크로즈비 씨와 혼인하기로 약혼했단다.”"]], [["The water into which Johnny's heart had been plunged now closed over his head and left him speechless.", "조니의 심장이 곤두박질쳤던 그 물이 이제 그의 머리 위까지 차올라 그를 말문이 막힌 채로 남겨 두었다."], ["Lily Dale was engaged to be married to Mr.", "릴리 데일이 크로즈비 씨와 혼인하기로 약혼했다니!"], ["Crosbie! He knew that he should have spoken when he heard the tidings.", "그 소식을 들었을 때 자기가 무슨 말이든 했어야 한다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["He knew that the moments of silence as they passed by told his secret to the two women before him,--that secret which it would now behove him to conceal from all the world.", "침묵의 순간이 흘러갈수록 그것이 앞에 앉은 두 여인에게 자신의 비밀을 일러바치고 있다는 것도, 그리고 그 비밀이란 이제 온 세상에 감추어야 할 것이 되었다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["But yet he could not speak.", "그러나 그런데도 그는 입을 뗄 수가 없었다."]], [["\"We are all very well pleased at the match,\"", "“우리 모두 이 혼사를 아주 기뻐하고 있단다,”"], ["said Mrs.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["Dale, wishing to spare him.", "그를 봐주고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["\"Nothing can be nicer than Mr. Crosbie,\"", "“크로즈비 씨보다 더 좋은 분은 없을 거예요,”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"We have often talked about you, and he will be so happy to know you.\"", "“우리는 자주 당신 이야기를 했어요. 그분도 당신을 알게 되면 아주 기뻐할 거예요.”"]], [["\"He won't know much about me,\"", "“저를 알아 봐야 뭐 알 것도 없을걸요,”"], ["said Johnny; and even in speaking these few senseless words--words which he uttered because it was necessary that he should say something--the tone of his voice was altered.", "조니가 말했다. 이 몇 마디 어이없는 말을 하는 동안에도, 무슨 말이든 해야 했기에 내뱉은 그 말을 하는 동안에도, 그의 목소리는 달라져 있었다."], ["He would have given the world to have been master of himself at this moment, but he felt that he was utterly vanquished.", "이 순간 자신을 다스릴 수만 있다면 그는 온 세상이라도 내주었을 테지만, 자기가 여지없이 무너졌다는 것을 느낄 뿐이었다."]], [["\"There is Lily coming across the lawn,\"", "“저기 릴리가 잔디밭을 가로질러 오는구나,”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Then I'd better go,\" said Eames.", "“그럼 저는 가 보는 게 낫겠습니다,”"], ["\"Don't say anything about it; pray don't.\"", "임스가 말했다. “그 이야기는 아무 말씀도 마세요."], ["And then, without waiting for another word, he escaped out of the drawing-room.", "제발 부탁입니다.” 그러고서 그는 다른 말은 기다리지도 않고 응접실 밖으로 달아났다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI. BEAUTIFUL DAYS.", "# 제6장. 아름다운 나날"]], [["I am well aware that I have not as yet given any description of Bell and Lilian Dale, and equally well aware that the longer the doing so is postponed the greater the difficulty becomes.", "나는 아직 벨과 릴리언 데일이 어떤 처녀들인지 한 번도 그려 보이지 않았음을 잘 알고 있으며, 그 일을 미루면 미룰수록 어려움이 커진다는 것 또한 잘 알고 있다."], ["I wish it could be understood without any description that they were two pretty, fair-haired girls, of whom Bell was the tallest and the prettiest, whereas Lily was almost as pretty as her sister, and perhaps was more attractive.", "아무런 묘사 없이도 이만큼은 이해되었으면 싶다. 두 사람은 머리 빛깔이 밝은 예쁜 처녀들이었고, 그중 벨이 키가 더 크고 더 예뻤으며, 릴리는 언니에 거의 못지않게 예뻤을뿐더러 어쩌면 사람을 끄는 힘은 더 있었다고."]], [["They were fair-haired girls, very like each other, of whom I have before my mind's eye a distinct portrait, which I fear I shall not be able to draw in any such manner as will make it distinct to others.", "두 사람은 머리 빛깔이 밝은 처녀들로 서로 무척 닮았으며, 내 마음의 눈앞에는 그 또렷한 초상이 걸려 있으나, 그것을 남들에게도 또렷하게 보이도록 그려 낼 재간은 아무래도 내게 없을 듯싶다."], ["They were something below the usual height, being slight and slender in all their proportions.", "두 사람은 보통 키보다 조금 작았고, 몸의 모든 선이 가늘고 여렸다."], ["Lily was the shorter of the two, but the difference was so trifling that it was hardly remembered unless the two were together.", "둘 중에서는 릴리가 더 작았으나 그 차이가 하도 미미해서 나란히 서 있지 않고서는 좀처럼 떠오르지 않았다."], ["And when I said that Bell was the prettier, I should, perhaps, have spoken more justly had I simply declared that her features were more regular than her sister's.", "그리고 벨이 더 예뻤다고 내가 말했을 때, 어쩌면 벨의 이목구비가 동생의 것보다 더 반듯했다고만 말하는 편이 더 옳았을지도 모른다."], ["The two girls were very fair, so that the soft tint of colour which relieved the whiteness of their complexion was rather acknowledged than distinctly seen.", "두 처녀는 살결이 무척 희어서, 그 흰빛을 누그러뜨리는 부드러운 혈색은 또렷이 보인다기보다 그저 거기 있다고 인정되는 정도였다."], ["It was there, telling its own tale of health, as its absence would have told a tale of present or coming sickness; and yet nobody could ever talk about the colour in their cheeks.", "그것은 분명 거기 있어서 건강하다는 이야기를 제 나름으로 들려주었으니, 만일 그것이 없었다면 지금의 병이나 다가올 병을 이야기했을 터이다."], ["The hair of the two girls was so alike in hue and texture, that no one, not even their mother, could say that there was a difference.", "그러면서도 그 두 사람의 볼에 도는 빛깔을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵 말할 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 두 처녀의 머리카락은 빛깔과 결이 어찌나 닮았는지, 어머니조차도 다르다고 말할 수 없었다."], ["It was not flaxen hair, and yet it was very light.", "아마빛 머리는 아니었으되 매우 밝았다."], ["Nor did it approach to auburn; and yet there ran through it a golden tint that gave it a distinct brightness of its own.", "적갈색 쪽으로 기운 것도 아니었으나, 그 사이로 금빛이 감돌아 그것만의 또렷한 광채를 주었다."], ["But with Bell it was more plentiful than with Lily, and therefore Lily would always talk of her own scanty locks, and tell how beautiful were those belonging to her sister.", "다만 벨 쪽이 릴리보다 숱이 많아서, 릴리는 늘 제 머리가 성글고 볼품없다고 말하며 언니의 머리가 얼마나 아름다운지를 들려주곤 했다."], ["Nevertheless Lily's head was quite as lovely as her sister's; for its form was perfect, and the simple braids in which they both wore their hair did not require any great exuberance in quantity.", "그러나 릴리의 머리도 언니 못지않게 사랑스러웠다. 두상의 생김이 완벽했고, 두 사람이 다 같이 하고 다니던 수수한 땋은 머리는 숱이 넘치도록 많을 것을 요구하지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Their eyes were brightly blue; but Bell's were long, and soft, and tender, often hardly daring to raise themselves to your face; while those of Lily were rounder, but brighter, and seldom kept by any want of courage from fixing themselves where they pleased.", "두 사람의 눈은 맑은 푸른빛이었다. 다만 벨의 눈은 길고 부드럽고 순해서 상대의 얼굴로 들어 올려질 엄두조차 잘 내지 못했고, 릴리의 눈은 그보다 둥글되 더 반짝였으며, 용기가 모자라서 제가 보고 싶은 곳에 머무르기를 그만두는 일이란 좀처럼 없었다."], ["And Lily's face was perhaps less oval in its form--less perfectly oval--than her sister's.", "그리고 릴리의 얼굴은 언니의 것보다 갸름한 맛이 — 완벽한 달걀형에 가까운 맛이 — 아마 덜했을 것이다."], ["The shape of the forehead was, I think, the same, but with Bell the chin was something more slender and delicate.", "이마의 생김은 내 생각에 같았으나, 벨 쪽이 턱이 조금 더 가늘고 섬세했다."], ["But Bell's chin was unmarked, whereas on her sister's there was a dimple which amply compensated for any other deficiency in its beauty.", "그러나 벨의 턱에는 아무 자국이 없었던 데 반해 동생의 턱에는 보조개가 있어서, 그 아름다움에서 모자란 것이 무엇이든 넉넉히 메우고도 남았다."], ["Bell's teeth were more even than her sister's; but then she showed her teeth more frequently.", "벨의 치아는 동생의 것보다 고르게 났으나, 대신 벨은 이를 더 자주 드러냈다."], ["Her lips were thinner and, as I cannot but think, less expressive.", "입술은 더 얇았고, 내 생각에는 아무래도 표정이 덜 실려 있었다."], ["Her nose was decidedly more regular in its beauty, for Lily's nose was somewhat broader than it should have been.", "코는 그 아름다움이 확실히 더 반듯했으니, 릴리의 코는 마땅한 정도보다 다소 넓적했기 때문이다."], ["It may, therefore, be understood that Bell would be considered the beauty by the family.", "그러니 집안에서 미인으로 꼽히는 쪽은 벨이었으리라고 이해하면 되겠다."]], [["But there was, perhaps, more in the general impression made by these girls, and in the whole tone of their appearance, than in the absolute loveliness of their features or the grace of their figures.", "그러나 이 처녀들이 주는 전체적인 인상과 그 자태 전반의 분위기에는, 이목구비의 절대적인 어여쁨이나 몸매의 우아함에 담긴 것보다 아마 더 많은 것이 담겨 있었다."], ["There was about them a dignity of demeanour devoid of all stiffness or pride, and a maidenly modesty which gave itself no airs.", "두 사람에게는 뻣뻣함이나 오만이라고는 조금도 없는 품위 있는 태도가 있었고, 티를 내지 않는 처녀다운 조심성이 있었다."], ["In them was always apparent that sense of security which women should receive from an unconscious dependence on their own mingled purity and weakness.", "두 사람에게서는, 여자가 제 안에 뒤섞여 있는 순결과 연약함에 저도 모르게 기대는 데서 얻어야 마땅한 그 안심의 기색이 늘 드러나 보였다."], ["These two girls were never afraid of men,--never looked as though they were so afraid.", "이 두 처녀는 남자를 두려워하는 법이 없었다. 두려워하는 것처럼 보이는 법도 없었다."], ["And I may say that they had little cause for that kind of fear to which I allude.", "그리고 내가 말하는 그런 종류의 두려움을 품을 까닭이 두 사람에게는 거의 없었다고 해 두어도 좋겠다."], ["It might be the lot of either of them to be ill-used by a man, but it was hardly possible that either of them should ever be insulted by one.", "둘 중 누구인가가 어떤 남자에게 몹쓸 대접을 받는 일은 있을 수 있었겠으나, 둘 중 누구인가가 남자에게 모욕을 당하는 일은 좀처럼 있을 수 없었다."], ["Lily, as may, perhaps, have been already seen, could be full of play, but in her play she never so carried herself that any one could forget what was due to her.", "릴리는, 어쩌면 이미 보셨을 터이지만, 장난기가 넘칠 수 있는 사람이었다. 그러나 그 장난 속에서도 그녀는 자기가 마땅히 받아야 할 대접이 무엇인지를 누구도 잊어버릴 만한 처신을 결코 하지 않았다."]], [["And now Lily Dale was engaged to be married, and the days of her playfulness were over.", "그리고 이제 릴리 데일은 약혼한 몸이 되었고, 장난스러운 나날은 끝이 났다."], ["It sounds sad, this sentence against her, but I fear that it must be regarded as true.", "그녀에게 내리는 이 선고가 서글프게 들리기는 하나, 나로서는 그것을 참말로 여길 수밖에 없을까 두렵다."], ["And when I think that it is true,--when I see that the sportiveness and kitten-like gambols of girlhood should be over, and generally are over, when a girl has given her troth, it becomes a matter of regret to me that the feminine world should be in such a hurry after matrimony.", "그리고 그것이 참말이라고 생각할 때 — 처녀 시절의 발랄함과 새끼 고양이 같은 재롱이 처녀가 언약을 준 순간 끝나야 마땅하고 또 대개 끝나 버리는 것을 볼 때 — 여자들의 세계가 혼인을 향해 이토록 서두른다는 사실이 내게는 안타깝게 다가온다."], ["I have, however, no remedy to offer for the evil; and, indeed, am aware that the evil, if there be an evil, is not well expressed in the words I have used.", "그러나 나는 그 폐단에 내놓을 처방이 없고, 실은 그 폐단이, 폐단이라는 것이 있다손 치더라도, 내가 쓴 말로는 제대로 표현되지 않았음을 알고 있다."], ["The hurry is not for matrimony, but for love.", "서두르는 대상은 혼인이 아니라 사랑이다."], ["Then, the love once attained, matrimony seizes it for its own, and the evil is accomplished.", "그러고 나면 일단 얻어진 사랑을 혼인이 제 것으로 낚아채고, 그렇게 폐단이 완성되는 것이다."]], [["And Lily Dale was engaged to be married to Adolphus Crosbie,--to Apollo Crosbie, as she still called him, confiding her little joke to his own ears.", "그리고 릴리 데일이 약혼한 상대는 어돌퍼스 크로즈비였다 — 그녀가 여전히 그렇게 부르며 그 작은 농담을 그의 귀에만 들려주던, 아폴로 크로즈비였다."], ["And to her he was an Apollo, as a man who is loved should be to the girl who loves him.", "그리고 그녀에게 그는 정말로 아폴로였으니, 사랑받는 남자란 그를 사랑하는 처녀에게 마땅히 그런 존재여야 하는 법이다."], ["He was handsome, graceful, clever, self-confident, and always cheerful when she asked him to be cheerful.", "그는 잘생기고, 몸가짐이 우아하고, 영리하고, 자신만만했으며, 그녀가 쾌활하기를 청하면 언제나 쾌활했다."], ["But he had also his more serious moments, and could talk to her of serious matters.", "그러면서도 그에게는 좀 더 진지한 순간들이 있어서, 진지한 문제들을 그녀와 이야기할 줄도 알았다."], ["He would read to her, and explain to her things which had hitherto been too hard for her young intelligence.", "그는 그녀에게 책을 읽어 주었고, 그때까지 그녀의 어린 지혜로는 너무 어려웠던 것들을 풀어 설명해 주었다."], ["His voice, too, was pleasant, and well under command.", "목소리 또한 듣기 좋았고 잘 다스려져 있었다."], ["It could be pathetic if pathos were required, or ring with laughter as merry as her own.", "애처로움이 필요하면 애처로울 수 있었고, 그녀 자신의 웃음만큼이나 흥겹게 울릴 수도 있었다."], ["Was not such a man fit to be an Apollo to such a girl, when once the girl had acknowledged to herself that she loved him?", "그러한 처녀가 일단 제 마음속으로 그를 사랑한다고 인정한 마당에, 그러한 남자가 그 처녀의 아폴로가 되기에 마땅하지 않았겠는가?"]], [["She had acknowledged it to herself, and had acknowledged it to him,--as the reader will perhaps say without much delay.", "그녀는 제 마음속으로 그것을 인정했고, 또한 그에게도 인정했다 — 아마 독자들은 그리 오래 끌지도 않고서라고 말하리라."], ["But the courtship had so been carried on that no delay had been needed.", "그러나 구애가 그런 식으로 이어졌으니 오래 끌 필요가 없었던 것이다."], ["All the world had smiled upon it.", "온 세상이 그 일에 미소를 지었다."], ["When Mr. Crosbie had first come among them at Allington, as Bernard's guest, during those few days of his early visit, it had seemed as though Bell had been chiefly noticed by him.", "크로즈비 씨가 처음 앨링턴에서 버나드의 손님으로 그들 사이에 끼어들었던 그 짧은 첫 방문의 며칠 동안에는, 그가 주로 눈여겨보는 쪽이 벨인 듯이 보였다."], ["And Bell in her own quiet way had accepted his admiration, saying nothing of it and thinking but very little.", "그리고 벨은 제 나름의 조용한 방식으로 그의 찬탄을 받아들이며 그것을 입에 올리지도, 그다지 마음에 두지도 않았다."], ["Lily was heart-free at the time, and had ever been so.", "릴리는 그때 마음이 자유로웠고, 여태 늘 그러했다."], ["No first shadow from Love's wing had as yet been thrown across the pure tablets of her bosom.", "사랑의 날개가 드리우는 첫 그림자조차 아직 그 가슴의 깨끗한 서판 위에는 던져진 적이 없었다."], ["With Bell it was not so,--not so in absolute strictness.", "벨은 그렇지 않았다 — 엄밀히 따지자면 그렇지 않았다."], ["Bell's story, too, must be told, but not on this page.", "벨의 이야기 또한 언젠가 해야겠지만, 이 지면에서는 아니다."], ["But before Crosbie had come among them, it was a thing fixed in her mind that such love as she had felt must be overcome and annihilated.", "그러나 크로즈비가 그들 사이에 끼어들기 전에, 자기가 느꼈던 그런 사랑은 이겨 내고 없애 버려야 한다는 것이 그녀의 마음속에 굳게 정해져 있었다."], ["We may say that it had been overcome and annihilated, and that she would have sinned in no way had she listened to vows from this new Apollo.", "그것은 이미 이겨 내고 없애 버린 것이었으니, 그녀가 이 새로운 아폴로의 맹세에 귀를 기울였다 한들 조금도 죄가 되지 않았으리라고 우리는 말할 수 있다."], ["It is almost sad to think that such a man might have had the love of either of such girls, but I fear that I must acknowledge that it was so.", "그런 남자가 그런 처녀들 가운데 어느 쪽의 사랑이든 얻을 수 있었으리라는 것은 생각하기에 거의 서글픈 일이나, 나는 사실이 그러했음을 인정하지 않을 수 없을까 두렵다."], ["Apollo, in the plenitude of his power, soon changed his mind; and before the end of his first visit, had transferred the distant homage which he was then paying from the elder to the younger sister.", "아폴로는 제 힘이 한창일 때 이내 마음을 바꾸었고, 첫 방문이 끝나기도 전에 그때껏 바치던 그 먼발치의 경의를 언니에게서 동생에게로 옮겨 놓았다."], ["He afterwards returned, as the squire's guest, for a longer sojourn among them, and at the end of the first month had already been accepted as Lily's future husband.", "그는 그 뒤 지주의 손님으로 다시 돌아와 그들 사이에서 더 오래 머물렀고, 한 달이 채 지나기 전에 이미 릴리의 장래 남편으로 받아들여져 있었다."]], [["It was beautiful to see how Bell changed in her mood towards Crosbie and towards her sister as soon as she perceived how the affair was going.", "일이 어느 쪽으로 흘러가는지를 알아채자마자 벨이 크로즈비를 대하는 마음과 동생을 대하는 마음을 어떻게 바꾸었는지는 보기에 아름다운 일이었다."], ["She was not long in perceiving it, having caught the first glimpses of the idea on that evening when they both dined at the Great House, leaving their mother alone to eat or to neglect the peas.", "벨이 그것을 알아채기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았으니, 자매가 어머니를 홀로 남겨 두어 완두콩을 먹든 말든 내버려 둔 채 둘이서 큰 집에 저녁을 먹으러 갔던 그 저녁에 이미 그 생각의 첫 자락을 붙들었기 때문이다."], ["For some six or seven weeks Crosbie had been gone, and during that time Bell had been much more open in speaking of him than her sister.", "크로즈비가 떠나 있던 예닐곱 주 남짓 동안 벨은 동생보다 훨씬 거리낌 없이 그의 이야기를 했다."], ["She had been present when Crosbie had bid them good-by, and had listened to his eagerness as he declared to Lily that he should soon be back again at Allington.", "벨은 크로즈비가 작별을 고하던 자리에 함께 있었고, 그가 릴리에게 곧 다시 앨링턴으로 돌아오겠노라고 다짐하던 그 간절함을 들었다."], ["Lily had taken this very quietly, as though it had not belonged at all to herself; but Bell had seen something of the truth, and, believing in Crosbie as an earnest, honest man, had spoken kind words of him, fostering any little aptitude for love which might already have formed itself in Lily's bosom.", "릴리는 그것을 마치 저와는 아무 상관도 없는 일인 양 아주 담담하게 받아들였다. 그러나 벨은 사정의 얼마쯤을 보았고, 크로즈비를 성실하고 정직한 남자라 믿었으므로 그를 두고 좋은 말을 해 주면서, 이미 릴리의 가슴에 자라났을지 모르는 자그마한 사랑의 싹을 무엇이든 북돋아 주었다."]], [["\"But he is such an Apollo, you know,\"", "“하지만 그분은 정말 아폴로잖아.”"], ["Lily had said.", "릴리가 말했었다."]], [["\"He is a gentleman; I can see that.\"", "“그분은 신사야. 그건 나도 알겠어.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes; a man can't be an Apollo unless he's a gentleman.\"", "“그럼, 물론이지. 신사가 아니고서는 아폴로가 될 수 없는걸.”"]], [["\"And he's very clever.\"", "“게다가 아주 영리하시고.”"]], [["\"I suppose he is clever.\"", "“영리하기야 하시겠지.”"], ["There was nothing more said about his being a mere clerk.", "그가 일개 서기일 뿐이라는 말은 더 나오지 않았다."], ["Indeed, Lily had changed her mind on that subject.", "실은 릴리가 그 문제에 관해 마음을 바꾼 것이었다."], ["Johnny Eames was a mere clerk; whereas Crosbie, if he was to be called a clerk at all, was a clerk of some very special denomination.", "조니 임스는 일개 서기였다. 반면에 크로즈비는, 굳이 서기라고 불러야 한다면, 아주 특별한 부류에 드는 서기였다."], ["There may be a great difference between one clerk and another!", "서기와 서기 사이에는 큰 차이가 있을 수 있는 법이다!"], ["A Clerk of the Council and a parish clerk are very different persons.", "추밀원 서기관과 교구 서기는 아주 다른 사람이다."], ["Lily had got some such idea as this into her head as she attempted in her own mind to rescue Mr. Crosbie from the lower orders of the Government service.", "릴리는 크로즈비 씨를 관청 근무의 하급 부류에서 제 마음속으로나마 건져 내려 애쓰다가 그런 식의 생각을 머릿속에 얻게 된 것이었다."]], [["\"I wish he were not coming,\" Mrs.", "“그 사람이 오지 않았으면 싶구나.”"], ["Dale had said to her eldest daughter.", "데일 부인이 큰딸에게 말했었다."]], [["\"I think you are wrong, mamma.\"", "“어머니 생각이 틀리신 것 같아요.”"]], [["\"But if she should become fond of him, and then--\"", "“하지만 그 애가 그 사람을 좋아하게 되고, 그러고 나서 —”"]], [["\"Lily will never become really fond of any man till he shall have given her proper reason.", "“릴리는 상대가 마땅한 이유를 주기 전까지는 어떤 남자도 정말로 좋아하게 되지 않아요."], ["And if he admires her, why should they not come together?\"", "그리고 그분이 릴리를 마음에 두고 있다면, 두 사람이 맺어지지 못할 이유가 무엇이겠어요?”"]], [["\"But she is so young, Bell.\"", "“하지만 그 애는 너무 어리잖니, 벨.”"]], [["\"She is nineteen; and if they were engaged, perhaps, they might wait for a year or so.", "“열아홉이에요. 그리고 약혼을 한다 해도 한 해쯤은 기다릴 수 있겠지요."], ["But it's no good talking in that way, mamma.", "하지만 그런 식으로 말해 봐야 소용없어요, 어머니."], ["If you were to tell Lily not to give him encouragement, she would not speak to him.\"", "어머니가 릴리더러 그분에게 마음을 주는 티를 내지 말라고 하시면, 릴리는 아예 그분과 말을 하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["\"I should not think of interfering.\"", "“간섭할 생각은 없다.”"]], [["\"No, mamma; and therefore it must take its course.", "“그럼요, 어머니. 그러니 일은 흘러가는 대로 두어야지요."], ["For myself, I like Mr. Crosbie very much.\"", "저로 말하자면 크로즈비 씨가 아주 마음에 들어요.”"]], [["\"So do I, my dear.\"", "“나도 그렇단다, 얘야.”"]], [["\"And so does my uncle.", "“삼촌도 그러시고요."], ["I wouldn't have Lily take a lover of my uncle's choosing.\"", "저라면 릴리가 삼촌이 골라 준 사람을 짝으로 맞게 하지는 않겠어요.”"]], [["\"I should hope not.\"", "“그야 그래서는 안 되지.”"]], [["\"But it must be considered a good thing if she happens to choose one of his liking.\"", "“하지만 릴리가 고른 사람이 마침 삼촌 마음에도 드는 사람이라면, 그건 좋은 일로 여겨야겠지요.”"]], [["In this way the matter had been talked over between the mother and her elder daughter. Then Mr.", "이런 식으로 그 문제는 어머니와 큰딸 사이에서 이야기되었다."], ["Crosbie had come; and before the end of the first month his declared admiration for Lily had proved the correctness of her sister's foresight. And during that short courtship all had gone well with the lovers.", "그러고서 크로즈비 씨가 왔고, 첫 달이 다 가기도 전에 릴리를 향한 그의 공공연한 찬탄이 언니의 예감이 옳았음을 증명해 보였다."], ["The squire from the first had declared himself satisfied with the match, informing Mrs.", "그리고 그 짧은 구애 기간 동안 연인들에게는 모든 일이 순조로웠다."], ["Dale, in his cold manner, that Mr. Crosbie was a gentleman with an income sufficient for matrimony.", "지주는 처음부터 이 혼사에 만족한다고 밝히면서, 크로즈비 씨는 혼인을 감당하기에 넉넉한 수입을 지닌 신사라고 그 특유의 차가운 태도로 데일 부인에게 알렸다."]], [["\"It would be close enough in London,\"", "“런던에서라면 빠듯하겠지요.”"], ["Mrs. Dale had said.", "데일 부인이 말했었다."]], [["\"He has more than my brother had when he married,\"", "“내 아우가 혼인할 때 가졌던 것보다는 많소.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"If he will only make her as happy as your brother made me,--while it lasted!\" said Mrs.", "“그이가 저를 행복하게 해 주었던 만큼만 그 애를 행복하게 해 준다면요 — 그것이 이어지는 동안만이라도!”"], ["Dale, as she turned away her face to conceal a tear that was coming.", "데일 부인은 이렇게 말하며 솟아오르는 눈물을 감추려 얼굴을 돌렸다."], ["And then there was nothing more said about it between the squire and his sister-in-law.", "그리고 그 일을 두고 지주와 그 제수 사이에 더 오간 말은 없었다."], ["The squire spoke no word as to assistance in money matters,--did not even suggest that he would lend a hand to the young people at starting, as an uncle in such a position might surely have done.", "지주는 돈 문제의 도움에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그런 처지의 삼촌이라면 응당 그리했음 직하게, 젊은 사람들이 살림을 시작할 때 손을 보태겠다는 뜻조차 비치지 않았다."], ["It may well be conceived that Mrs. Dale herself said nothing on the subject.", "데일 부인 자신도 그 문제에 관해 아무 말도 하지 않았으리라는 것은 넉넉히 짐작할 만하다."], ["And, indeed, it may be conceived, also, that the squire, let his intentions be what they might, would not divulge them to Mrs. Dale.", "그리고 실은, 지주가 속으로 무슨 뜻을 품고 있었든 그것을 데일 부인에게 털어놓지는 않으리라는 것 또한 짐작할 만하다."], ["This was uncomfortable, but the position was one that was well understood between them.", "이는 거북한 일이었으나, 그 처지는 두 사람 사이에 서로 잘 이해되고 있는 것이었다."]], [["Bernard Dale was still at Allington, and had remained there through the period of Crosbie's absence. Whatever words Mrs.", "버나드 데일은 여전히 앨링턴에 있었고, 크로즈비가 자리를 비운 동안 내내 그곳에 머물러 있었다."], ["Dale might choose to speak on the matter would probably be spoken to him; but, then, Bernard could be quite as close as his uncle.", "데일 부인이 그 문제에 관해 굳이 말을 꺼낸다면 아마 그 상대는 버나드였을 것이다. 그러나 버나드도 제 삼촌 못지않게 입이 무거울 수 있는 사람이었다."], ["When Crosbie returned, he and Bernard had, of course, lived much together; and, as was natural, there came to be close discussion between them as to the two girls, when Crosbie allowed it to be understood that his liking for Lily was becoming strong.", "크로즈비가 돌아오자 그와 버나드는 물론 많은 시간을 함께 보냈고, 크로즈비가 릴리를 향한 제 마음이 깊어지고 있음을 은근히 내비치자 자연스럽게 두 처녀를 두고 둘 사이에 속을 터놓는 이야기가 오가게 되었다."]], [["\"You know, I suppose, that my uncle wishes me to marry the elder one,\"", "“자네도 알 테지만, 삼촌은 내가 큰딸과 혼인하기를 바라시네.”"], ["Bernard had said.", "버나드가 말했었다."]], [["\"I have guessed as much.\"", "“그쯤은 짐작하고 있었네.”"]], [["\"And I suppose the match will come off.", "“그리고 그 혼사는 성사되리라고 보네."], ["She's a pretty girl, and as good as gold.\"", "예쁜 처녀인 데다 더없이 착하지.”"]], [["\"Yes, she is.\"", "“그래, 그렇지.”"]], [["\"I don't pretend to be very much in love with her.", "“내가 그 애한테 몹시 반해 있는 척은 하지 않겠네."], ["It's not my way, you know.", "그건 내 방식이 아니니까, 자네도 알겠지만."], ["But, some of these days, I shall ask her to have me, and I suppose it'll all go right.", "하지만 조만간 나를 받아 달라고 청할 참이고, 다 잘 풀리리라 생각하네."], ["The governor has distinctly promised to allow me eight hundred a year off the estate, and to take us in for three months every year if we wish it.", "어르신은 영지에서 해마다 팔백 파운드를 떼어 주고, 우리가 원하면 해마다 석 달씩 집에 들이겠다고 분명히 약속하셨네."], ["I told him simply that I couldn't do it for less, and he agreed with me.\"", "나는 그저 그보다 적어서는 못 하겠다고 말씀드렸고, 어르신도 내 말에 수긍하셨지.”"]], [["\"You and he get on very well together.\"", "“자네와 어르신은 사이가 아주 좋군.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes!", "“그럼!"], ["There's never been any fal-lal between us about love, and duty, and all that.", "우리 사이에 사랑이니 도리니 하는 겉치레 소리가 오간 적은 한 번도 없네."], ["I think we understand each other, and that's everything.", "우리는 서로를 이해하고 있고, 그것이 전부라고 나는 생각해."], ["He knows the comfort of standing well with the heir, and I know the comfort of standing well with the owner.\"", "어르신은 상속인과 사이좋게 지내는 편안함을 아시고, 나는 소유주와 사이좋게 지내는 편안함을 알지.”"], ["It must be admitted, I think, that there was a great deal of sound, common sense about Bernard Dale.", "버나드 데일에게는 건전한 상식이 아주 많았다는 점을 인정해야 하리라고 나는 생각한다."]], [["\"What will he do for the younger sister?\"", "“동생 쪽에는 어르신이 무엇을 해 주실까?”"], ["asked Crosbie; and, as he asked the important question, a close observer might have perceived that there was some slight tremor in his voice.", "크로즈비가 물었다. 그리고 그 중요한 물음을 던질 때, 눈 밝은 사람이었다면 그의 목소리에 희미한 떨림이 있음을 알아챘을 것이다."]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["that's more than I can tell you.", "그건 내가 자네에게 말해 줄 수 있는 범위를 넘네."], ["If I were you, I should ask him.", "내가 자네라면 어르신께 여쭙겠네."], ["The governor is a plain man, and likes plain business.\"", "어르신은 꾸밈없는 분이고, 꾸밈없는 거래를 좋아하시지.”"]], [["\"I suppose you couldn't ask him?\"", "“자네가 여쭤 볼 수는 없겠지?”"]], [["\"No; I don't think I could.", "“그래, 못 할 것 같네."], ["It is my belief that he will not let her go by any means empty-handed.\"", "어르신이 그 애를 결코 빈손으로 보내지는 않으시리라는 것이 내 믿음일세.”"]], [["\"Well, I should suppose not.\"", "“글쎄, 그러시지는 않겠지.”"]], [["\"But remember this, Crosbie,--I can say nothing to you on which you are to depend.", "“하지만 이건 기억해 두게, 크로즈비 — 나는 자네가 기대를 걸 만한 말은 아무것도 해 줄 수 없네."], ["Lily, also, is as good as gold; and, as you seem to be fond of her, I should ask the governor, if I were you, in so many words, what he intends to do.", "릴리도 더없이 착한 아이고, 자네가 그 애를 좋아하는 듯하니, 내가 자네라면 어르신께 어떻게 하실 작정이신지를 에두르지 말고 딱 잘라 여쭙겠네."], ["Of course, it's against my interest, for every shilling he gives Lily will ultimately come out of my pocket.", "물론 그건 내 이익에 반하는 일이지. 어르신이 릴리에게 주시는 한 실링 한 실링이 결국은 내 주머니에서 나가는 것이니까."], ["But I'm not the man to care about that, as you know.\"", "하지만 자네도 알다시피 나는 그런 것을 마음에 두는 사람이 아닐세.”"]], [["What might be Crosbie's knowledge on this subject we will not here inquire; but we may say that it would have mattered very little to him out of whose pocket the money came, so long as it went into his own.", "이 문제에 관해 크로즈비가 무엇을 알고 있었는지는 여기서 캐묻지 않기로 한다. 다만 그 돈이 누구의 주머니에서 나오는지는, 그것이 제 주머니로 들어오기만 한다면, 그에게 거의 아무 상관도 없었으리라는 말은 해 두어도 좋겠다."], ["When he felt quite sure of Lily,--having, in fact, received Lily's permission to speak to her uncle, and Lily's promise that she would herself speak to her mother,--he did tell the squire what was his intention.", "릴리에 대해 완전히 확신이 서자 — 실은 그녀의 삼촌에게 말해도 좋다는 릴리의 허락과, 어머니에게는 자기가 직접 말하겠다는 릴리의 약속을 이미 받아 둔 터였다 — 그는 지주에게 제 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["This he did in an open, manly way, as though he felt that in asking for much he also offered to give much.", "그는 그 일을 드러내 놓고 사내답게 했으니, 많은 것을 청하는 만큼 자기 또한 많은 것을 내놓는 것이라고 느끼는 듯했다."]], [["\"I have nothing to say against it,\"", "“나로서는 반대할 말이 없네.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"And I have your permission to consider myself as engaged to her?\"", "“그러면 제가 그 아가씨와 약혼한 사이라고 여겨도 좋다는 말씀이십니까?”"]], [["\"If you have hers and her mother's.", "“그 애와 그 애 어머니의 허락이 있다면 말이지."], ["Of course you are aware that I have no authority over her.\"", "물론 자네도 알다시피 나에게는 그 애에 대한 권한이 없네.”"]], [["\"She would not marry without your sanction.\"", "“그래도 어르신의 승낙 없이는 혼인하지 않을 것입니다.”"]], [["\"She is very good to think so much of her uncle,\"", "“제 삼촌을 그토록 생각해 주다니 그 애가 참 기특하군.”"], ["said the squire; and his words as he spoke them sounded very cold in Crosbie's ears.", "지주가 말했다. 그리고 그 말이 입에서 나올 때 크로즈비의 귀에는 그 소리가 몹시 차갑게 들렸다."], ["After that Crosbie said nothing about money, having to confess to himself that he was afraid to do so.", "그 뒤로 크로즈비는 돈에 관해 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 그럴 엄두가 나지 않았음을 스스로 인정할 수밖에 없었다."], ["\"And what would be the use?\"", "“게다가 말해 본들 무슨 소용인가?”"], ["said he to himself, wishing to make excuses for what he felt to be weak in his own conduct.", "그는 제 처신에서 약하다고 느껴지는 대목에 변명을 대고 싶은 마음으로 속으로 이렇게 되뇌었다."], ["\"If he should refuse to give her a shilling I could not go back from it now.\"", "“저 어른이 그 애에게 한 실링도 못 주겠다고 하신들, 이제 와서 물러설 수는 없는 노릇 아닌가.”"], ["And then some ideas ran across his mind as to the injustice to which men are subjected in this matter of matrimony.", "그러고 나서 혼인이라는 이 문제에서 남자들이 겪게 되는 부당함에 관한 몇 가지 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["A man has to declare himself before it is fitting that he should make any inquiry about a lady's money; and then, when he has declared himself, any such inquiry is unavailing.", "남자는 여자의 재산에 관해 무엇이든 물어보는 것이 온당해지기도 전에 먼저 제 뜻을 밝혀야 한다. 그리고 일단 제 뜻을 밝히고 나면, 그런 물음은 무엇이든 아무 소용이 없어진다."], ["Which consideration somewhat cooled the ardour of his happiness.", "이런 헤아림이 그의 행복이 지닌 열기를 얼마쯤 식혀 놓았다."], ["Lily Dale was very pretty, very nice, very refreshing in her innocence, her purity, and her quick intelligence.", "릴리 데일은 아주 예뻤고, 아주 고왔고, 그 천진함과 순결함과 재빠른 총명함으로 사람의 마음을 아주 시원하게 해 주었다."], ["No amusement could be more deliciously amusing than that of making love to Lily Dale.", "릴리 데일에게 사랑을 속삭이는 일보다 더 달콤하게 즐거운 놀이는 있을 수 없었다."], ["Her way of flattering her lover without any intention of flattery on her part, had put Crosbie into a seventh heaven.", "아첨할 뜻이라고는 조금도 없이 제 연인을 추어올리는 그녀의 방식은 크로즈비를 하늘 꼭대기에 올려놓았다."], ["In all his experience he had known nothing like it.", "그가 겪어 온 모든 경험 가운데 그런 것은 없었다."], ["\"You may be sure of this,\"", "“이것만은 믿으셔도 좋아요.”"], ["she had said,--\"I shall love you with all my heart and all my strength.\"", "그녀는 이렇게 말했었다. “저는 온 마음과 온 힘을 다해 당신을 사랑할 거예요.”"], ["It was very nice;--but then what were they to live upon?", "그것은 무척 좋았다. 그러나 그렇다면 두 사람은 무엇으로 먹고산단 말인가?"], ["Could it be that he, Adolphus Crosbie, should settle down on the north side of the New Road, as a married man, with eight hundred a year?", "그, 어돌퍼스 크로즈비가 연 팔백 파운드를 가지고 아내를 거느린 사내로서 뉴 로드 북쪽에 자리를 잡는 일이 있을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["If indeed the squire would be as good to Lily as he had promised to be to Bell, then indeed things might be made to arrange themselves.", "만일 지주가 벨에게 해 주겠다고 약속한 만큼을 릴리에게도 해 준다면, 그렇다면 참으로 사정을 그럭저럭 꾸려 나갈 수도 있으리라."]], [["But there was no such drawback on Lily's happiness.", "그러나 릴리의 행복에는 그런 걸림돌이 없었다."], ["Her ideas about money were rather vague, but they were very honest.", "돈에 관한 그녀의 생각은 다소 어렴풋했으나 무척 정직했다."], ["She knew she had none of her own, but supposed it was a husband's duty to find what would be needful.", "그녀는 제게 돈이 조금도 없다는 것을 알았고, 필요한 것을 마련하는 일은 남편의 몫이려니 여겼다."], ["She knew she had none of her own, and was therefore aware that she ought not to expect luxuries in the little household that was to be prepared for her.", "그녀는 제게 돈이 조금도 없다는 것을 알았고, 따라서 자기를 위해 마련될 그 작은 살림에서 호사를 기대해서는 안 된다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["She hoped, for his sake, that her uncle might give some assistance, but was quite prepared to prove that she could be a good poor man's wife.", "그녀는 그를 위해 삼촌이 얼마쯤 도움을 주기를 바랐으나, 가난한 남자의 좋은 아내가 될 수 있음을 증명해 보일 각오도 넉넉히 되어 있었다."], ["In the old colloquies on such matters between her and her sister, she had always declared that some decent income should be considered as indispensable before love could be entertained.", "그런 문제를 두고 언니와 나누던 예전의 이야기에서 그녀는 늘, 사랑을 품어도 좋으려면 그 전에 반듯한 수입이 있어야 하며 그것은 없어서는 안 될 조건이라고 잘라 말하곤 했다."], ["But eight hundred a year had been considered as doing much more than fulfilling this stipulation.", "그러나 연 팔백 파운드라면 그 조건을 채우고도 훨씬 남는 것으로 여겨져 왔다."], ["Bell had had high-flown notions as to the absolute glory of poverty.", "벨은 가난의 절대적인 영광에 관해 드높은 생각을 품고 있었다."], ["She had declared that income should not be considered at all.", "벨은 수입 따위는 아예 셈에 넣어서는 안 된다고 잘라 말했었다."], ["If she had loved a man, she could allow herself to be engaged to him, even though he had no income.", "자기가 어떤 남자를 사랑한다면 그에게 수입이 한 푼 없다 해도 그와 약혼할 수 있다고."], ["Such had been their theories; and as regarded money, Lily was quite contented with the way in which she had carried out her own.", "두 사람의 이론은 그러했다. 그리고 돈에 관해서라면 릴리는 제 이론을 몸소 지켜 낸 그 방식에 아주 만족하고 있었다."]], [["In these beautiful days there was nothing to check her happiness.", "이 아름다운 나날 동안 그녀의 행복을 가로막는 것이라고는 아무것도 없었다."], ["Her mother and sister united in telling her that she had done well,--that she was happy in her choice, and justified in her love.", "어머니와 언니는 한목소리로, 그녀가 잘한 일이라고, 고른 것이 다행이라고, 그 사랑은 마땅한 것이라고 말해 주었다."], ["On that first day, when she told her mother all, she had been made exquisitely blissful by the way in which her tidings had been received.", "어머니에게 모든 것을 털어놓던 그 첫날, 그녀는 제 소식이 받아들여진 그 방식에 더없이 큰 기쁨을 누렸다."]], [["\"Oh!", "“오!"], ["mamma, I must tell you something,\"", "어머니, 드릴 말씀이 있어요.”"], ["she said, coming up to her mother's bedroom, after a long ramble with Mr. Crosbie through those Allington fields.", "크로즈비 씨와 함께 앨링턴의 그 들판을 오래도록 거닐고 돌아온 뒤, 어머니의 침실로 올라가며 그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Is it about Mr. Crosbie?\"", "“크로즈비 씨 이야기니?”"]], [["\"Yes, mamma.\"", "“네, 어머니.”"], ["And then the rest had been said through the medium of warm embraces and happy tears rather than by words.", "그러고 나서 나머지는 말이 아니라 뜨거운 포옹과 행복한 눈물을 통해 이야기되었다."]], [["As she sat in her mother's room, hiding her face on her mother's shoulders, Bell had come, and had knelt at her feet.", "어머니의 방에 앉아 어머니의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻고 있는 사이 벨이 들어와 그녀의 발치에 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["\"Dear Lily,\"", "“우리 릴리.”"], ["she had said, \"I am so glad.\"", "벨이 말했다. “정말 잘됐어.”"], ["And then Lily remembered how she had, as it were, stolen her lover from her sister, and she put her arms round Bell's neck and kissed her.", "그러자 릴리는 자기가 말하자면 언니에게서 그 연인을 훔쳐 낸 셈이라는 것을 떠올리고, 두 팔로 벨의 목을 감고 입을 맞추었다."]], [["\"I knew how it was going to be from the very first,\"", "“나는 처음부터 이렇게 될 줄 알았어.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"Did I not, mamma?\"", "“그렇죠, 어머니?”"]], [["\"I'm sure I didn't,\"", "“난 정말 몰랐는걸.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"I never thought such a thing was possible.\"", "“그런 일이 있을 수 있으리라고는 생각도 못 했어.”"]], [["\"But we did,--mamma and I.\"", "“하지만 우리는 알았어 — 어머니하고 나는.”"]], [["\"Did you?\"", "“그랬어?”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Bell told me that it was to be so,\"", "“벨이 그렇게 될 거라고 나한테 말해 주더구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"But I could hardly bring myself at first to think that he was good enough for my darling.\"", "“하지만 나는 처음에는 그 사람이 내 귀한 딸에게 걸맞을 만큼 훌륭한 사람이라고 좀처럼 생각하기 어려웠단다.”"]], [["\"Oh, mamma!", "“어머, 어머니!"], ["you must not say that.", "그런 말씀 하시면 안 돼요."], ["You must think that he is good enough for anything.\"", "그분은 무엇에든 걸맞을 만큼 훌륭한 분이라고 생각하셔야죠.”"]], [["\"I will think that he is very good.\"", "“아주 좋은 사람이라고 생각하마.”"]], [["\"Who could be better?", "“그분보다 나은 사람이 어디 있겠어요?"], ["And then, when you remember all that he is to give up for my sake!--And what can I do for him in return?", "게다가 그분이 저를 위해 버리시는 것들을 다 떠올려 보면요! 그런데 저는 그 대신 그분께 무얼 해 드릴 수 있을까요?"], ["What have I got to give him?\"", "제가 그분께 드릴 것이 무엇이 있나요?”"]], [["Neither Mrs.", "데일 부인도 벨도 그 문제를 그런 식으로 볼 수는 없었으니, 릴리가 받는 만큼을 고스란히 주고 있다고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["Dale nor Bell could look at the matter in this light, thinking that Lily gave quite as much as she received.", "그러나 두 사람은 다 같이 크로즈비가 나무랄 데 없는 사람이라고 잘라 말했다."], ["But they both declared that Crosbie was perfect, knowing that by such assurances only could they now administer to Lily's happiness; and Lily, between them, was made perfect in her happiness, receiving all manner of encouragement in her love, and being nourished in her passion by the sympathy and approval of her mother and sister.", "이제 릴리의 행복을 돌보아 줄 수 있는 길은 그런 다짐뿐임을 알았기 때문이다. 그리하여 릴리는 두 사람 사이에서 그 행복이 온전해졌고, 제 사랑에 온갖 격려를 받았으며, 어머니와 언니의 공감과 인정으로 그 애틋한 마음을 살찌워 갔다."]], [["And then had come that visit from Johnny Eames.", "그러고 나서 조니 임스의 그 방문이 있었다."], ["As the poor fellow marched out of the room, giving them no time to say farewell, Mrs.", "그 딱한 젊은이가 작별 인사를 할 틈도 주지 않고 방에서 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔을 때, 데일 부인과 벨은 서로 서글프게 바라보았다."], ["Dale and Bell looked at each other sadly; but they were unable to concoct any arrangement, for Lily had run across the lawn, and was already on the ground before the window.", "그러나 두 사람은 아무런 방도도 꾸며 낼 수가 없었으니, 릴리가 잔디밭을 가로질러 달려와 이미 창문 앞에 와 서 있었기 때문이다."]], [["\"As soon as we got to the end of the shrubbery there were uncle Christopher and Bernard close to us; so I told Adolphus he might go on by himself.\"", "“관목 숲 끝에 다다르자마자 크리스토퍼 삼촌하고 버나드가 우리 바로 옆에 있지 뭐예요. 그래서 어돌퍼스더러 혼자 더 가시라고 했어요.”"]], [["\"And who do you think has been here?\"", "“그런데 누가 다녀갔는지 알아?”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["But Mrs. Dale said nothing.", "그러나 데일 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Had time been given to her to use her own judgment, nothing should have been said at that moment as to Johnny's visit.", "제 판단대로 할 겨를이 주어졌더라면 조니의 방문에 관해서는 그 순간 한마디도 나오지 않았어야 했다."]], [["\"Has anybody been here since I went?", "“내가 나간 사이에 누가 왔었어?"], ["Whoever it was didn't stay very long.\"", "누구였든 그리 오래 있지는 않았나 보네.”"]], [["\"Poor Johnny Eames,\"", "“가엾은 조니 임스.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["Then the colour came up into Lily's face, and she bethought herself in a moment that the old friend of her young days had loved her, that he, too, had had hopes as to his love, and that now he had heard tidings which would put an end to such hopes.", "그러자 릴리의 얼굴에 붉은빛이 올라왔고, 그녀는 어린 시절의 그 오랜 벗이 자기를 사랑했다는 것을, 그 또한 제 사랑에 무언가 바라는 바가 있었다는 것을, 그리고 이제 그가 그런 바람에 끝을 낼 소식을 들었다는 것을 한순간에 떠올렸다."], ["She understood it all in a moment, but understood also that it was necessary that she should conceal such understanding.", "그녀는 그 모든 것을 한순간에 알아차렸으나, 그렇게 알아차렸다는 것을 감추어야 한다는 것 또한 알아차렸다."]], [["\"Dear Johnny!\"", "“조니도 참!”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Why did he not wait for me?\"", "“왜 나를 기다려 주지 않았을까?”"]], [["\"We told him you were out,\"", "“네가 나갔다고 일러 주었단다.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"He will be here again before long, no doubt.\"", "“머지않아 또 올 게다, 틀림없이.”"]], [["\"And he knows--?\"", "“그러면 그분도 아는 거예요 —?”"]], [["\"Yes; I thought you would not object to my telling him.\"", "“그래. 내가 말해도 네가 언짢아하지 않으리라고 생각했다.”"]], [["\"No, mamma; of course not.", "“그럼요, 어머니. 물론이죠."], ["And he has gone back to Guestwick?\"", "그러면 그분은 게스트윅으로 돌아갔나요?”"]], [["There was no answer given to this question, nor were there any further words then spoken about Johnny Eames.", "이 물음에는 아무 대답도 주어지지 않았고, 조니 임스에 관해 그때 더 오간 말도 없었다."], ["Each of these women understood exactly how the matter stood, and each knew that the others understood it.", "이 여자들은 저마다 사정이 어떠한지를 정확히 알고 있었고, 저마다 나머지 사람들도 그것을 알고 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["The young man was loved by them all, but not loved with that sort of admiring affection which had been accorded to Mr. Crosbie.", "그 젊은이는 세 사람 모두에게 사랑을 받았으나, 크로즈비 씨에게 주어졌던 그런 종류의 찬탄 어린 애정으로 사랑받는 것은 아니었다."], ["Johnny Eames could not have been accepted as a suitor by their pet.", "조니 임스는 저희 귀염둥이의 구혼자로 받아들여질 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["Mrs. Dale and Bell both felt that.", "데일 부인도 벨도 그것을 느끼고 있었다."], ["And yet they loved him for his love, and for that distant, modest respect which had restrained him from any speech regarding it.", "그러면서도 두 사람은 그의 사랑 때문에, 또 그 사랑에 관해 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 못하게 그를 붙들어 둔 그 멀찍하고 조심스러운 존경 때문에 그를 사랑했다."], ["Poor Johnny!", "가엾은 조니!"], ["But he was young,--hardly as yet out of his hobbledehoyhood,--and he would easily recover this blow, remembering, and perhaps feeling to his advantage, some slight touch of its passing romance.", "그러나 그는 젊었다 — 아직 풋내기 시절을 겨우 벗어났을까 말까 한 나이였다 — 그러니 이 타격도 쉽게 이겨 내고서, 스쳐 지나간 그 낭만의 자그마한 자취를 기억하며 어쩌면 제게 득이 되는 것으로 느끼기까지 하리라."], ["It is thus women think of men who love young and love in vain.", "젊어서 사랑하고 또 헛되이 사랑하는 남자들을 두고 여자들은 이런 식으로 생각하는 법이다."]], [["But Johnny Eames himself, as he rode back to Guestwick, forgetful of his spurs, and with his gloves stuffed into his pocket, thought of the matter very differently.", "그러나 조니 임스 자신은, 박차 따위는 잊어버린 채 장갑을 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고 게스트윅으로 말을 달려 돌아가면서, 그 문제를 아주 다르게 생각하고 있었다."], ["He had never promised to himself any success as to his passion for Lily, and had, indeed, always acknowledged that he could have no hope; but now, that she was actually promised to another man, and as good as married, he was not the less broken-hearted because his former hopes had not been high.", "그는 릴리를 향한 제 연정에 어떤 성공도 스스로에게 약속한 적이 없었고, 실은 아무런 희망도 품을 수 없다고 늘 인정해 왔다. 그러나 이제 그녀가 다른 남자에게 실제로 약속된 몸이 되어 혼인한 것이나 다름없게 되자, 예전의 희망이 드높지 않았다고 해서 그의 가슴이 덜 미어지는 것은 아니었다."], ["He had never dared to speak to Lily of his love, but he was conscious that she knew it, and he did not now dare to stand before her as one convicted of having loved in vain.", "그는 릴리에게 제 사랑을 말할 엄두를 낸 적이 한 번도 없었으나 그녀가 그것을 알고 있다는 것은 의식하고 있었고, 이제 헛되이 사랑한 죄를 선고받은 자로서 그녀 앞에 설 엄두는 나지 않았다."], ["And then, as he rode back, he thought also of his other love, not with many of those pleasant thoughts which Lotharios and Don Juans may be presumed to enjoy when they contemplate their successes.", "그러고 나서 말을 달려 돌아가는 동안 그는 제 또 다른 사랑도 생각했다. 다만 로타리오나 돈 후안 같은 자들이 제 성공을 헤아려 볼 때 누리리라 짐작되는 그 흐뭇한 생각들은 거의 없었다."], ["\"I suppose I shall marry her, and there'll be an end of me,\"", "“나는 그 여자와 혼인하게 되겠지, 그리고 그것으로 내 인생은 끝이겠지.”"], ["he said to himself, as he remembered a short note which he had once written to her in his madness. There had been a little supper at Mrs.", "언젠가 정신이 나가서 그녀에게 써 보낸 짧은 쪽지를 떠올리며 그는 속으로 이렇게 말했다."], ["Roper's, and Mrs. Lupex and Amelia had made the punch.", "로퍼 부인네에서 자그마한 야식 자리가 있었고, 루펙스 부인과 어밀리아가 펀치를 만들었다."], ["After supper, he had been by some accident alone with Amelia in the dining-parlour; and when, warmed by the generous god, he had declared his passion, she had shaken her head mournfully, and had fled from him to some upper region, absolutely refusing his proffered embrace.", "야식이 끝난 뒤 그는 어쩌다 보니 식당에 어밀리아와 단둘이 남게 되었다. 그리고 그 너그러운 신의 기운에 달아올라 제 연정을 고백했을 때, 그녀는 서글프게 고개를 저으며 그에게서 달아나 위층 어딘가로 올라가 버렸고, 그가 내민 포옹을 딱 잘라 마다했다."], ["But on the same night, before his head had found its pillow, a note had come to him, half repentant, half affectionate, half repellent,--\"If, indeed, he would swear to her that his love was honest and manly, then, indeed, she might even yet,--see him through the chink of the doorway with the purport of telling him that he was forgiven.\"", "그러나 바로 그날 밤, 그의 머리가 베개를 찾기도 전에 쪽지 한 장이 그에게 왔으니, 반은 뉘우치고 반은 다정하고 반은 밀쳐 내는 쪽지였다. “만일 참으로 그이가 제 사랑이 정직하고 사내다운 것임을 그녀에게 맹세해 준다면, 그렇다면 참으로 그녀는 그래도 아직은 — 용서받았음을 일러 주려는 뜻으로 문틈 사이로 그이를 만나 볼 수도 있으리라.”"], ["Whereupon, a perfidious pencil being near to his hand, he had written the requisite words.", "그리하여 배신자 같은 연필이 마침 손 가까이 있었으므로 그는 요구되는 그 말을 써 보냈다."], ["\"My only object in life is to call you my own for ever.\"", "“내 삶의 오직 하나뿐인 목적은 그대를 영원히 내 사람이라 부르는 것입니다.”"], ["Amelia had her misgivings whether such a promise, in order that it might be used as legal evidence, should not have been written in ink.", "어밀리아에게는 그런 약속이 법정 증거로 쓰이려면 잉크로 쓰였어야 하지 않았을까 하는 미심쩍음이 있었다. 그것은 괴로운 의심이었다."], ["It was a painful doubt; but nevertheless she was as good as her word, and saw him through the chink, forgiving him for his impetuosity in the parlour with, perhaps, more clemency than a mere pardon required.", "그럼에도 그녀는 제 말을 지켰고 문틈 사이로 그를 만나 주었으며, 식당에서의 그 성급함을 두고 그를 용서하되 그저 용서받는 데 필요한 것보다는 아마 더 너그럽게 용서해 주었다."], ["\"By George!", "“저런!"], ["how well she looked with her hair all loose,\"", "머리를 다 풀어 내리니까 어찌나 예쁘던지.”"], ["he said to himself, as he at last regained his pillow, still warm with the generous god.", "여전히 그 너그러운 신의 기운을 지닌 채 마침내 다시 베개를 찾았을 때 그는 속으로 이렇게 말했었다."], ["But now, as he thought of that night, returning on his road from Allington to Guestwick, those loose, floating locks were remembered by him with no strong feeling as to their charms.", "그러나 이제, 앨링턴에서 게스트윅으로 돌아가는 길 위에서 그날 밤을 떠올리자니, 풀어 내려 하늘거리던 그 머리채는 그 매력에 대한 아무런 강렬한 느낌도 없이 기억될 뿐이었다."], ["And he thought also of Lily Dale, as she was when he had said farewell to her on that day before he first went up to London.", "그리고 그는 릴리 데일도 생각했다 — 처음 런던으로 올라가기 전날 그녀에게 작별을 고하던 그때의 릴리를."], ["\"I shall care more about seeing you than anybody,\"", "“나는 다른 누구보다도 당신을 다시 보는 일을 마음에 두게 될 거예요.”"], ["he had said; and he had often thought of the words since, wondering whether she had understood them as meaning more than an assurance of ordinary friendship.", "그는 그렇게 말했었고, 그 뒤로도 그 말을 자주 떠올리며 그녀가 그것을 여느 우정의 다짐 이상으로 알아들었을까 궁금해하곤 했다."], ["And he remembered well the dress she had then worn.", "그리고 그때 그녀가 입고 있던 옷도 또렷이 기억났다."], ["It was an old brown merino, which he had known before, and which, in truth, had nothing in it to recommend it specially to a lover's notice.", "그것은 전부터 알고 있던 낡은 갈색 메리노 원피스였고, 사실 연인의 눈길을 끌 만한 구석이라고는 조금도 없는 옷이었다."], ["\"Horrid old thing!\"", "“지긋지긋하게 낡아빠진 것!”"], ["had been Lily's own verdict respecting the frock, even before that day.", "그날이 오기 전부터 그 옷을 두고 릴리 자신이 내린 평결은 그러했다."], ["But she had hallowed it in his eyes, and he would have been only too happy to have worn a shred of it near his heart, as a talisman.", "그러나 그녀는 그 옷을 그의 눈에 거룩한 것으로 만들어 놓았으니, 그는 그 자락 한 조각이라도 부적처럼 가슴에 지니고 다닐 수 있었다면 더없이 기뻤을 것이다."], ["How wonderful in its nature is that passion of which men speak when they acknowledge to themselves that they are in love.", "남자들이 스스로 사랑에 빠졌음을 인정할 때 입에 올리는 그 연정이란 그 본성이 얼마나 놀라운 것인가."], ["Of all things, it is, under one condition, the most foul, and under another, the most fair.", "모든 것 가운데 그것은 어떤 조건에서는 가장 더러운 것이고, 또 다른 조건에서는 가장 아름다운 것이다."], ["As that condition is, a man shows himself either as a beast or as a god!", "그 조건이 어떠하냐에 따라 남자는 스스로를 짐승으로도 드러내고 신으로도 드러내는 것이다!"], ["And so we will let poor Johnny Eames ride back to Guestwick, suffering much in that he had loved basely--and suffering much, also, in that he had loved nobly.", "그러니 가엾은 조니 임스로 하여금 게스트윅으로 말을 달려 돌아가게 두자 — 비천하게 사랑한 탓에 많이 괴로워하고, 또한 고결하게 사랑한 탓에 많이 괴로워하면서."]], [["Lily, as she had tripped along through the shrubbery, under her lover's arm, looking up, every other moment, into his face, had espied her uncle and Bernard.", "릴리는 연인의 팔짱을 끼고 두어 걸음마다 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 관목 숲 사이를 가볍게 걸어가다가 삼촌과 버나드를 알아보았다."], ["\"Stop,\"", "“멈춰요.”"], ["she had said, giving him a little pull at the arm; \"I won't go on.", "그녀는 그의 팔을 살짝 잡아당기며 말했다. “저는 더 안 갈래요."], ["Uncle is always teasing me with some old-fashioned wit.", "삼촌은 늘 그 케케묵은 재담으로 저를 놀리시거든요."], ["And I've had quite enough of you to-day, sir.", "그리고 오늘은 당신을 볼 만큼 봤어요, 선생님."], ["Mind you come over to-morrow before you go to your shooting.\"", "내일은 사냥 나가시기 전에 꼭 건너오셔야 해요.”"], ["And so she had left him.", "그러고서 그녀는 그를 두고 떠났다."]], [["We may as well learn here what was the question in dispute between the uncle and cousin, as they were walking there on the broad gravel path behind the Great House.", "큰 집 뒤편의 널찍한 자갈길을 함께 걷던 삼촌과 사촌 사이에서 다투어지던 그 문제가 무엇이었는지는 여기서 알아 두는 편이 좋겠다."], ["\"Bernard,\"", "“버나드.”"], ["the old man had said, \"I wish this matter could be settled between you and Bell.\"", "노인이 말했었다. “이 문제가 너와 벨 사이에서 매듭지어졌으면 싶구나.”"]], [["\"Is there any hurry about it, sir?\"", "“그게 급한 일입니까, 어르신?”"]], [["\"Yes, there is hurry; or, rather, as I hate hurry in all things, I would say that there is ground for despatch.", "“그래, 급하다. 아니, 나는 매사에 서두르는 것을 싫어하니 차라리 서둘러야 할 까닭이 있다고 말해 두자꾸나."], ["Mind, I do not wish to drive you.", "명심해라, 나는 너를 몰아세울 뜻은 없다."], ["If you do not like your cousin, say so.\"", "네 사촌이 마음에 들지 않는다면 그렇다고 말해라.”"]], [["\"But I do like her; only I have a sort of feeling that these things grow best by degrees.", "“마음에 들지 않는 게 아닙니다. 다만 이런 일은 차츰차츰 자라나는 편이 가장 좋다는 느낌이 좀 있을 뿐입니다."], ["I quite share your dislike to being in a hurry.\"", "서두르는 것을 싫어하시는 마음에는 저도 온전히 같은 생각이고요.”"]], [["\"But time enough has been taken now.", "“하지만 이제 시간은 넉넉히 들였다."], ["You see, Bernard, I am going to make a great sacrifice of income on your behalf.\"", "알다시피, 버나드, 나는 너를 위해 수입에서 큰 희생을 치르려는 참이다.”"]], [["\"I am sure I am very grateful.\"", "“정말 감사하게 여기고 있습니다.”"]], [["\"I have no children, and have therefore always regarded you as my own.", "“나는 자식이 없으니 늘 너를 내 자식으로 여겨 왔다."], ["But there is no reason why my brother Philip's daughter should not be as dear to me as my brother Orlando's son.\"", "그러나 내 아우 필립의 딸이 내 아우 올랜도의 아들만큼 내게 귀하지 않을 까닭도 없지 않으냐.”"]], [["\"Of course not, sir; or, rather, his two daughters.\"", "“물론 그렇지요, 어르신. 아니, 딸이 아니라 두 딸이겠지요.”"]], [["\"You may leave that matter to me, Bernard.", "“그 문제는 내게 맡겨 두어라, 버나드."], ["The younger girl is going to marry this friend of yours, and as he has a sufficient income to support a wife, I think that my sister-in-law has good reason to be satisfied by the match.", "작은아이는 네 그 친구와 혼인할 참이고, 그 사람은 아내를 건사하기에 넉넉한 수입이 있으니, 내 제수도 이 혼사에 만족할 만한 충분한 까닭이 있다고 나는 생각한다."], ["She will not be expected to give up any part of her small income, as she must have done had Lily married a poor man.\"", "릴리가 가난한 남자와 혼인했더라면 반드시 그리해야 했을 터인데, 이제는 그 얼마 안 되는 제 수입에서 한 몫을 떼어 놓아야 할 일이 없을 테니 말이다.”"]], [["\"I suppose she could hardly give up much.\"", "“숙모님이 많이 내놓으실 형편도 못 되었겠지요.”"]], [["\"People must be guided by circumstances.", "“사람은 형편에 따라야 하는 법이다."], ["I am not disposed to put myself in the place of a parent to them both.", "나는 그 두 아이 모두에게 아비 노릇을 할 마음은 없다."], ["There is no reason why I should, and I will not encourage false hopes.", "그래야 할 까닭이 없고, 헛된 기대를 부추기지도 않겠다."], ["If I knew that this matter between you and Bell was arranged, I should have reason to feel satisfied with what I was doing.\"", "너와 벨 사이의 이 문제가 매듭지어졌다는 것을 알게 된다면, 나로서도 내가 하려는 일을 두고 흡족해할 까닭이 생기겠지.”"], ["From all which Bernard began to perceive that poor Crosbie's expectations in the matter of money would not probably receive much gratification.", "이 모든 말에서 버나드는, 돈 문제에 관한 가엾은 크로즈비의 기대가 아마 그리 많이 채워지지는 않으리라는 것을 알아차리기 시작했다."], ["But he also perceived--or thought that he perceived--a kind of threat in this warning from his uncle.", "그러나 그는 삼촌의 이 경고에서 일종의 위협 또한 알아차렸다 — 알아차렸다고 생각했다."], ["\"I have promised you eight hundred a year with your wife,\"", "“나는 네 아내와 함께 연 팔백 파운드를 주겠다고 약속했다.”"], ["the warning seemed to say.", "그 경고는 이렇게 말하는 듯했다."], ["\"But if you do not at once accept it, or let me feel that it will be accepted, it may be well for me to change my mind--especially as this other niece is about to be married.", "“그러나 네가 그것을 당장 받아들이지 않거나, 받아들일 것이라고 나를 느끼게 해 주지 않는다면, 내가 마음을 바꾸는 편이 나을지도 모르겠구나 — 더구나 이 또 다른 조카딸이 곧 혼인하려는 참이니 말이다."], ["If I am to give you so large a fortune with Bell, I need do nothing for Lily.", "벨과 함께 그토록 큰 재산을 네게 주어야 한다면 릴리에게는 아무것도 해 줄 필요가 없다."], ["But if you do not choose to take Bell and the fortune, why then--\"", "그러나 네가 벨과 그 재산을 받지 않겠다면, 그렇다면 뭐 —”"], ["And so on.", "그런 식이었다."], ["It was thus that Bernard read his uncle's caution, as they walked together on the broad gravel path.", "널찍한 자갈길을 함께 걸으며 버나드가 삼촌의 그 경계하는 말을 읽어 낸 방식은 그러했다."]], [["\"I have no desire to postpone the matter any longer,\"", "“더는 이 문제를 미룰 생각이 없습니다.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."], ["\"I will propose to Bell at once, if you wish it.\"", "“원하신다면 당장 벨에게 청혼하겠습니다.”"]], [["\"If your mind be quite made up, I cannot see why you should delay it.\"", "“네 마음이 아주 굳게 정해졌다면, 네가 그것을 미룰 까닭이 무엇인지 나는 모르겠구나.”"]], [["And then, having thus arranged that matter, they received their future relative with kind smiles and soft words.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 그 문제를 그렇게 매듭지은 뒤, 앞으로 친척이 될 그 사람을 다정한 미소와 부드러운 말로 맞이했다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII. THE BEGINNING OF TROUBLES.", "# 제7장. 근심의 시작"]], [["Lily, as she parted with her lover in the garden, had required of him to attend upon her the next morning as he went to his shooting, and in obedience to this command he appeared on Mrs.", "릴리는 정원에서 연인과 헤어지면서, 이튿날 아침 사냥을 나가는 길에 자기에게 들러 달라고 요구해 두었다. 그 명령에 순종하여 그는 아침 식사가 끝난 뒤 버나드와 개 두 마리를 데리고 데일 부인의 잔디밭에 나타났다."], ["Dale's lawn after breakfast, accompanied by Bernard and two dogs. The men had guns in their hands, and were got up with all proper sporting appurtenances, but it so turned out that they did not reach the stubble-fields on the farther side of the road until after luncheon.", "두 사내는 손에 총을 들고 사냥에 어울리는 온갖 장비를 빠짐없이 갖추고 있었으나, 결국 길 건너편 그루터기 밭에 이른 것은 점심을 먹고 난 뒤였다."], ["And may it not be fairly doubted whether croquet is not as good as shooting when a man is in love?", "그리고 사내가 사랑에 빠져 있을 때에는 크로케가 사냥 못지않게 좋은 것이 아니냐고 의심해 볼 만하지 않은가?"]], [["It will be said that Bernard Dale was not in love; but they who bring such accusation against him, will bring it falsely.", "버나드 데일은 사랑에 빠져 있지 않았다고들 말할 것이다. 그러나 그런 혐의를 그에게 씌우는 이들은 거짓을 말하는 것이다."], ["He was in love with his cousin Bell according to his manner and fashion.", "그는 그 나름의 방식과 유행대로 사촌 벨을 사랑하고 있었다."], ["It was not his nature to love Bell as John Eames loved Lily; but then neither would his nature bring him into such a trouble as that which the charms of Amelia Roper had brought upon the poor clerk from the Income-tax Office.", "존 임스가 릴리를 사랑하듯 벨을 사랑하는 것은 그의 천성이 아니었다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 그의 천성이 그를, 어밀리아 로퍼의 매력이 소득세 사무국의 가엾은 서기에게 안겨 준 것과 같은 곤경으로 몰아넣지도 않을 터였다."], ["Johnny was susceptible, as the word goes; whereas Captain Dale was a man who had his feelings well under control.", "흔히들 쓰는 말로 조니는 정에 약했다. 반면에 데일 대위는 제 감정을 능히 다스릴 줄 아는 사내였다."], ["He was not one to make a fool of himself about a girl, or to die of a broken heart; but, nevertheless, he would probably love his wife when he got a wife, and would be a careful father to his children.", "그는 여자 하나를 두고 바보짓을 하거나 상심하여 죽을 사람은 아니었다. 그러나 그럼에도 아내를 얻게 되면 아마 제 아내를 사랑할 것이고, 제 자식들에게는 자상한 아버지가 될 사람이었다."]], [["They were very intimate with each other now,--these four.", "이제 이 넷은 서로 무척 허물이 없어졌다."], ["It was Bernard and Adolphus, or sometimes Apollo, and Bell and Lily among them; and Crosbie found it to be pleasant enough.", "그들 사이에서는 버나드, 어돌퍼스, 때로는 아폴로, 그리고 벨과 릴리였고, 크로즈비는 그것이 여간 즐겁지 않았다."], ["A new position of life had come upon him, and one exceeding pleasant; but, nevertheless, there were moments in which cold fits of a melancholy nature came upon him.", "인생의 새로운 국면이 그에게 찾아왔고, 그것은 더할 나위 없이 즐거운 국면이었다. 그러나 그럼에도 우울한 성질의 싸늘한 발작이 그를 덮치는 순간들이 있었다."], ["He was doing the very thing which throughout all the years of his manhood he had declared to himself that he would not do.", "그는 어른이 된 뒤로 줄곧 스스로에게 결코 하지 않겠노라 공언해 온 바로 그 일을 하고 있는 것이었다."], ["According to his plan of life he was to have eschewed marriage, and to have allowed himself to regard it as a possible event only under the circumstances of wealth, rank, and beauty all coming in his way together.", "그가 세운 인생 계획에 따르자면 그는 결혼을 멀리해야 했고, 재산과 지위와 미모가 한꺼번에 제 앞에 굴러들어 오는 경우에 한해서만 그것을 있을 수 있는 일로 여겨야 했다."], ["As he had expected no such glorious prize, he had regarded himself as a man who would reign at the Beaufort and be potent at Sebright's to the end of his chapter.", "그런 찬란한 상품을 기대해 본 적이 없었으므로, 그는 스스로를 제 인생의 마지막 장까지 보퍼트에서 군림하고 세브라이트에서 위세를 떨칠 사내로 여겨 왔다."], ["But now--", "그런데 이제—"]], [["It was the fact that he had fallen from his settled position, vanquished by a silver voice, a pretty wit, and a pair of moderately bright eyes.", "그는 은방울 같은 목소리와 예쁜 재치와 어지간히 반짝이는 두 눈에 정복당해, 제가 굳혀 두었던 자리에서 굴러떨어진 것이었다. 그는 릴리를 매우 좋아했다."], ["He was very fond of Lily, having in truth a stronger capability for falling in love than his friend Captain Dale; but was the sacrifice worth his while?", "실은 벗인 데일 대위보다도 사랑에 빠지는 능력이 강한 사람이었으니까. 그러나 그 희생이 치를 만한 값어치가 있는 것이었을까?"], ["This was the question which he asked himself in those melancholy moments; while he was lying in bed, for instance, awake in the morning, when he was shaving himself, and sometimes also when the squire was prosy after dinner.", "이것이 저 우울한 순간마다 그가 스스로에게 던지는 물음이었다. 이를테면 잠자리에 누워 아침에 눈을 뜨고 있을 때, 면도를 할 때, 그리고 이따금 저녁 식사 뒤 지주의 이야기가 장황해질 때."], ["At such times as these, while he would be listening to Mr. Dale, his self-reproaches would sometimes be very bitter.", "그런 때에 데일 씨의 말을 듣고 앉아 있노라면 그의 자책은 이따금 몹시 쓰라렸다."], ["Why should he undergo this, he, Crosbie of Sebright's, Crosbie of the General Committee Office, Crosbie who would allow no one to bore him between Charing Cross and the far end of Bayswater,--why should he listen to the long-winded stories of such a one as Squire Dale?", "어째서 내가 이런 것을 견뎌야 하는가, 나, 세브라이트의 크로즈비가, 일반위원회 사무국의 크로즈비가, 채링 크로스에서 베이스워터 끝자락에 이르기까지 그 누구도 저를 지루하게 하도록 내버려 두지 않는 크로즈비가— 어째서 데일 지주 같은 사람의 장황한 이야기에 귀를 기울여야 하는가?"], ["If, indeed, the squire intended to be liberal to his niece, then it might be very well.", "만일 지주가 정말로 제 조카딸에게 후하게 굴 작정이라면야 그것도 괜찮을 것이다."], ["But as yet the squire had given no sign of such intention, and Crosbie was angry with himself in that he had not had the courage to ask a question on that subject.", "그러나 지주는 아직 그런 뜻을 내비친 적이 없었고, 크로즈비는 그 문제에 대해 물어볼 용기를 내지 못한 자신에게 화가 났다."]], [["And thus the course of love was not all smooth to our Apollo.", "이리하여 사랑의 길은 우리의 아폴로에게 온통 순탄하기만 한 것은 아니었다."], ["It was still pleasant for him when he was there on the croquet ground, or sitting in Mrs. Dale's drawing-room with all the privileges of an accepted lover.", "크로케 마당에 나가 있을 때나, 받아들여진 연인의 온갖 특권을 누리며 데일 부인의 응접실에 앉아 있을 때는 여전히 그에게 즐거웠다."], ["It was pleasant to him also as he sipped the squire's claret, knowing that his coffee would soon be handed to him by a sweet girl who would have tripped across the two gardens on purpose to perform for him this service.", "지주의 클라레를 홀짝일 때에도 즐거웠으니, 오로지 그 시중을 들려고 두 정원을 총총 가로질러 온 사랑스러운 아가씨가 곧 제 손에 커피를 건네주리라는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["There is nothing pleasanter than all this, although a man when so treated does feel himself to look like a calf at the altar, ready for the knife, with blue ribbons round his horns and neck.", "이보다 더 즐거운 일은 없다. 물론 그런 대접을 받는 사내는 뿔과 목에 푸른 리본을 감고 칼을 기다리며 제단 앞에 선 송아지가 된 것 같은 기분이 들기는 하지만 말이다."], ["Crosbie felt that he was such a calf,--and the more calf-like, in that he had not as yet dared to ask a question about his wife's fortune.", "크로즈비는 자기가 바로 그런 송아지라고 느꼈다. 아내가 될 사람의 재산에 대해 아직 감히 한마디도 묻지 못했다는 점에서 더욱더 송아지 같았다."], ["\"I will have it out of the old fellow this evening,\"", "“오늘 저녁에 그 노인네한테서 기어이 알아내고 말겠다.”"], ["he said to himself, as he buttoned on his dandy shooting gaiters that morning.", "그날 아침 멋쟁이용 사냥 각반의 단추를 채우며 그는 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["\"How nice he looks in them,\"", "“저이는 저걸 신으니 참 근사해 보이지.”"], ["Lily said to her sister afterwards, knowing nothing of the thoughts which had troubled her lover's mind while he was adorning his legs.", "나중에 릴리가 언니에게 말했다. 제 연인이 다리를 치장하는 동안 그의 마음을 어지럽히던 생각 따위는 전혀 알지 못한 채였다."]], [["\"I suppose we shall come back this way,\"", "“돌아올 때도 이 길로 오겠지요.”"], ["Crosbie said, as they prepared to move away on their proper business when lunch was over.", "점심이 끝나고 본래의 볼일을 보러 나서려 하면서 크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, not exactly!\"", "“글쎄, 꼭 그렇지는 않아!”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."], ["\"We shall make our way round by Darvell's farm, and so back by Gruddock's.", "“우리는 다벨 농장 쪽으로 돌아서 그러독 농장을 거쳐 돌아올 거야."], ["Are the girls going to dine up at the Great House to-day?\"", "얘들아, 너희 오늘 큰 집에 저녁 먹으러 올라오니?”"]], [["The girls declared that they were not going to dine up at the Great House,--that they did not intend going to the Great House at all that evening.", "아가씨들은 큰 집에 저녁을 먹으러 올라가지 않겠다고, 오늘 저녁에는 아예 큰 집에 갈 생각이 없다고 잘라 말했다."]], [["\"Then, as you won't have to dress, you might as well meet us at Gruddock's gate, at the back of the farmyard.", "“그럼 옷을 갈아입을 것도 없을 테니, 그러독네 문간, 농장 마당 뒤쪽으로 우리를 마중 나오는 게 어떠냐."], ["We'll be there exactly at half-past five.\"", "우리는 정확히 다섯 시 반에 거기 가 있을 테니.”"]], [["\"That is to say, we're to be there at half-past five, and you'll keep us waiting for three-quarters of an hour,\"", "“그러니까 우리는 다섯 시 반에 거기 가 있고, 오빠들은 우리를 사십오 분쯤 기다리게 하겠다는 말이지.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["Nevertheless the arrangement as proposed was made, and the two ladies were not at all unwilling to make it.", "그렇지만 약속은 제안된 대로 정해졌고, 두 아가씨도 그것이 조금도 싫지 않았다."], ["It is thus that the game is carried on among unsophisticated people who really live in the country.", "정말로 시골에 사는, 때 묻지 않은 사람들 사이에서 이 놀음은 이런 식으로 굴러가는 법이다."], ["The farmyard gate at Farmer Gruddock's has not a fitting sound as a trysting-place in romance, but for people who are in earnest it does as well as any oak in the middle glade of a forest.", "그러독 농부네 농장 마당 문간이란 연애 소설의 밀회 장소로는 걸맞은 울림이 없지만, 진심인 사람들에게는 숲 한가운데 빈터의 어느 참나무 못지않게 훌륭한 구실을 한다. 릴리 데일은 아주 진심이었다."], ["Lily Dale was quite in earnest--and so indeed was Adolphus Crosbie,--only with him the earnest was beginning to take that shade of brown which most earnest things have to wear in this vale of tears.", "어돌퍼스 크로즈비도 실은 진심이었다. 다만 그의 경우에는 그 진심이, 이 눈물의 골짜기에서 진심이라는 것들이 대개 걸치게 마련인 저 갈색빛을 띠기 시작하고 있었다."], ["With Lily it was as yet all rose-coloured.", "릴리에게는 아직 모든 것이 장밋빛이었다."], ["And Bernard Dale was also in earnest.", "그리고 버나드 데일 역시 진심이었다."], ["Throughout this morning he had stood very near to Bell on the lawn, and had thought that his cousin did not receive his little whisperings with any aversion.", "그날 아침 내내 그는 잔디밭에서 벨에게 아주 가까이 서 있었고, 사촌이 제 자잘한 속삭임을 그리 싫어하며 받아들이는 것 같지는 않다고 생각했다. 싫어할 까닭이 무엇이겠는가?"], ["Why should she? Lucky girl that she was, thus to have eight hundred a year pinned to her skirt!", "그리하여 연 팔백 파운드를 치맛자락에 달게 되었으니, 그 아가씨야말로 운 좋은 아가씨가 아닌가!"]], [["\"I say, Dale,\"", "“이보게, 데일.”"], ["Crosbie said, as in the course of their day's work they had come round upon Gruddock's ground, and were preparing to finish off his turnips before they reached the farm-yard gate.", "그날의 볼일을 보는 동안 둘이 그러독의 땅으로 돌아와, 농장 마당 문간에 이르기 전에 그의 순무밭을 마저 훑으려던 참에 크로즈비가 말했다."], ["And now, as Crosbie spoke, they stood leaning on the gate, looking at the turnips while the two dogs squatted on their haunches.", "그리고 이제 크로즈비가 말을 꺼내는 동안, 둘은 문에 몸을 기댄 채 순무밭을 바라보고 있었고 개 두 마리는 뒷다리를 접고 앉아 있었다."], ["Crosbie had been very silent for the last mile or two, and had been making up his mind for this conversation.", "크로즈비는 지난 한두 마일 동안 몹시 말이 없었고, 이 대화를 하려고 마음을 다잡던 참이었다."], ["\"I say, Dale,--your uncle has never said a word to me yet as to Lily's fortune.\"", "“이보게, 데일— 자네 삼촌은 릴리의 재산에 대해 아직 나한테 한마디도 하지 않았네.”"]], [["\"As to Lily's fortune!", "“릴리의 재산이라니!"], ["The question is whether Lily has got a fortune.\"", "릴리에게 재산이 있기나 한가가 문제지.”"]], [["\"He can hardly expect that I am to take her without something.", "“내가 아무것도 없이 그 사람을 데려가리라고 기대하실 리는 없지 않은가."], ["Your uncle is a man of the world and he knows--\"", "자네 삼촌은 세상 물정을 아는 분이고, 그러니 아실 텐데—”"]], [["\"Whether or no my uncle is a man of the world, I will not say; but you are, Crosbie, whether he is or not.", "“우리 삼촌이 세상 물정을 아는 분인지 아닌지는 내가 말하지 않겠네. 하지만 크로즈비, 그분이 그렇든 아니든 자네는 세상 물정을 아는 사람이지."], ["Lily, as you have always known, has nothing of her own.\"", "자네도 늘 알고 있었다시피 릴리에게는 제 몫으로 가진 것이 없어.”"]], [["\"I am not talking of Lily's own.", "“나는 릴리 제 몫을 말하는 게 아닐세."], ["I'm speaking of her uncle.", "그 애 삼촌을 말하는 거야."], ["I have been straightforward with him; and when I became attached to your cousin I declared what I meant at once.\"", "나는 그분께 솔직했네. 자네 사촌에게 마음이 기울었을 때 나는 곧바로 내 뜻을 밝혔다고.”"]], [["\"You should have asked him the question, if you thought there was any room for such a question.\"", "“그럴 여지가 있다고 생각했다면 그때 그분께 물어봤어야지.”"]], [["\"Thought there was any room!", "“여지가 있다고 생각했다면이라니!"], ["Upon my word, you are a cool fellow.\"", "참말이지 자네는 태연한 친구로군.”"]], [["\"Now look here, Crosbie; you may say what you like about my uncle, but you must not say a word against Lily.\"", "“이보게 크로즈비, 우리 삼촌에 대해서는 자네 마음대로 말해도 좋지만, 릴리를 두고는 한마디도 안 되네.”"]], [["\"Who is going to say a word against her?", "“누가 그 사람을 두고 뭐라 한다는 건가?"], ["You can little understand me if you don't know that the protection of her name against evil words is already more my care than it is yours.", "험한 말로부터 그 이름을 지키는 일이 이미 자네보다 내 몫이 되었다는 걸 모른다면 자네는 나를 도무지 모르는 걸세."], ["I regard Lily as my own.\"", "나는 릴리를 내 사람으로 여기고 있어.”"]], [["\"I only meant to say, that any discontent you may feel as to her money, or want of money, you must refer to my uncle, and not to the family at the Small House.\"", "“내 말은 그저, 그 애의 돈이든 돈 없음이든 그것 때문에 불만이 있다면 그건 우리 삼촌한테 따질 일이지 작은 집 식구들한테 따질 일은 아니라는 걸세.”"]], [["\"I am quite well aware of that.\"", "“그쯤은 나도 잘 알고 있네.”"]], [["\"And though you are quite at liberty to say what you like to me about my uncle, I cannot say that I can see that he has been to blame.\"", "“그리고 우리 삼촌에 대해서라면 자네가 나한테 무슨 말을 하든 얼마든지 자유지만, 그분께 잘못이 있다고는 나로서는 도무지 보이지 않는군.”"]], [["\"He should have told me what her prospects are.\"", "“그 애의 앞날이 어떻게 되는 건지 나한테 말해 주셨어야지.”"]], [["\"But if she have got no prospects!", "“앞날이랄 게 없다면 어쩌겠나!"], ["It cannot be an uncle's duty to tell everybody that he does not mean to give his niece a fortune.", "조카딸에게 재산을 줄 뜻이 없다는 것을 만나는 사람마다 붙들고 알릴 의무가 삼촌에게 있을 리는 없지."], ["In point of fact, why should you suppose that he has such an intention?\"", "실은 말이야, 자네는 어째서 그분께 그런 뜻이 있으리라고 생각했나?”"]], [["\"Do you know that he has not?", "“그런 뜻이 없다는 걸 자네가 아는가?"], ["because you once led me to believe that he would give his niece money.\"", "언젠가 자네가 나더러 그분이 조카딸에게 돈을 주시리라고 믿게 만들었지 않나.”"]], [["\"Now, Crosbie, it is necessary that you and I should understand each other in this matter--\"", "“이보게 크로즈비, 이 문제만큼은 자네와 내가 서로 분명히 해 둘 필요가 있네—”"]], [["\"But did you not?\"", "“하지만 자네가 그러지 않았나?”"]], [["\"Listen to me for a moment.", "“잠깐 내 말을 들어 보게."], ["I never said a word to you about my uncle's intentions in any way, until after you had become fully engaged to Lily with the knowledge of us all.", "자네가 우리 모두가 아는 가운데 릴리와 완전히 약혼하기 전까지, 나는 삼촌의 뜻에 대해 자네에게 단 한마디도 한 적이 없네."], ["Then, when my belief on the subject could make no possible difference in your conduct, I told you that I thought my uncle would do something for her.", "그러고 나서, 이 문제에 대한 내 짐작이 자네의 처신을 조금도 바꿔 놓을 수 없게 된 다음에야, 나는 삼촌이 그 애에게 무언가 해 주시리라 생각한다고 말했지."], ["I told you so because I did think so;--and as your friend, I should have told you what I thought in any matter that concerned your interest.\"", "정말로 그렇게 생각했으니까 그리 말한 걸세. 그리고 벗으로서, 자네 이해가 걸린 일이라면 내가 생각하는 바를 자네에게 말해 주는 것이 마땅했으니까.”"]], [["\"And now you have changed your opinion?\"", "“그런데 지금은 생각이 바뀌었다?”"]], [["\"I have changed my opinion; but very probably without sufficient ground.\"", "“생각이 바뀌었네. 하지만 아마도 충분한 근거는 없이 바뀌었겠지.”"]], [["\"That's hard upon me.\"", "“나로서는 가혹한 일이군.”"]], [["\"It may be hard to bear disappointment; but you cannot say that anybody has ill-used you.\"", "“실망을 견디는 일이야 가혹할 수 있지. 하지만 누가 자네를 부당하게 대했다고는 말할 수 없을 걸세.”"]], [["\"And you don't think he will give her anything?\"", "“그럼 자네는 그분이 그 애한테 아무것도 안 주시리라고 보는 건가?”"]], [["\"Nothing that will be of much moment to you.\"", "“자네한테 그리 대수로울 만한 건 아무것도.”"]], [["\"And I'm not to say that that's hard?", "“그런데도 내가 가혹하다고 말해서는 안 된다?"], ["I think it confounded hard.", "나는 이건 지독하게 가혹하다고 보네."], ["Of course I must put off my marriage.\"", "물론 나는 결혼을 미룰 수밖에 없겠지.”"]], [["\"Why do you not speak to my uncle?\"", "“삼촌께 직접 말씀드리지 그러나?”"]], [["\"I shall do so.", "“그럴 참일세."], ["To tell the truth, I think it would have come better from him; but that is a matter of opinion.", "사실을 말하자면 그 말씀이 그분 쪽에서 먼저 나왔어야 옳다고 보네만, 그건 보기 나름이겠지. 나는 내가 어찌 생각하는지 아주 분명히 말씀드릴 걸세."], ["I shall tell him very plainly what I think about it; and if he is angry, why, I suppose I must leave his house; that will be all.\"", "그래서 그분이 노하시면, 뭐, 그 댁에서 나오는 수밖에 없겠지. 그뿐이야.”"]], [["\"Look here, Crosbie; do not begin your conversation with the purpose of angering him.", "“이보게 크로즈비, 처음부터 그분을 노하게 할 작정으로 말을 꺼내지는 말게."], ["He is not a bad-hearted man, but is very obstinate.\"", "마음이 나쁜 분은 아니지만 몹시 완고하시니까.”"]], [["\"I can be quite as obstinate as he is.\"", "“나도 그분 못지않게 완고할 수 있네.”"], ["And, then, without further parley, they went in among the turnips, and each swore against his luck as he missed his birds.", "그러고는 더 실랑이할 것도 없이 둘은 순무밭으로 들어갔고, 새를 놓칠 때마다 각자 제 운수를 두고 욕지거리를 했다."], ["There are certain phases of mind in which a man can neither ride nor shoot, nor play a stroke at billiards, nor remember a card at whist,--and to such a phase of mind had come both Crosbie and Dale after their conversation over the gate.", "사람에게는 말도 못 타고 총도 못 쏘고 당구 한 큐도 제대로 치지 못하고 휘스트에서 패 한 장 기억하지 못하는 어떤 마음 상태라는 것이 있는데, 크로즈비와 데일은 문간에서 대화를 나눈 뒤 바로 그런 마음 상태에 이르러 있었다."]], [["They were not above fifteen minutes late at the trysting-place, but nevertheless, punctual though they had been, the girls were there before them.", "두 사람이 밀회 장소에 늦은 것은 십오 분이 채 못 되었지만, 그렇게 시간을 지켰음에도 아가씨들이 먼저 와 있었다."], ["Of course the first inquiries were made about the game, and of course the gentlemen declared that the birds were scarcer than they had ever been before, that the dogs were wilder, and their luck more excruciatingly bad,--to all which apologies very little attention was paid.", "물론 첫 물음은 사냥거리에 관한 것이었고, 물론 신사들은 자고새가 그 어느 때보다 귀했고 개들은 더 사납게 날뛰었으며 자기들 운은 견딜 수 없으리만치 나빴노라고 늘어놓았다. 그 모든 변명에는 거의 아무도 귀를 기울이지 않았지만."], ["Lily and Bell had not come there to inquire after partridges, and would have forgiven the sportsmen even though no single bird had been killed.", "릴리와 벨은 자고새 소식을 들으러 나온 것이 아니었고, 새를 한 마리도 잡지 못했다 한들 사냥꾼들을 너그러이 용서했을 것이다."], ["But they could not forgive the want of good spirits which was apparent.", "그러나 눈에 훤히 드러나는 저 기운 없음만은 용서할 수가 없었다."]], [["\"I declare I don't know what's the matter with you,\"", "“당신, 대체 왜 그러시는지 도무지 모르겠네요.”"], ["Lily said to her lover.", "릴리가 연인에게 말했다."]], [["\"We have been over fifteen miles of ground, and--\"", "“우리는 십오 마일이 넘는 땅을 걸었고, 게다가—”"]], [["\"I never knew anything so lackadaisical as you gentlemen from London.", "“런던서 오신 신사분들처럼 맥없는 사람들은 처음 봐요."], ["Been over fifteen miles of ground!", "십오 마일이 넘는 땅을 걸었다니!"], ["Why, uncle Christopher would think nothing of that.\"", "크리스토퍼 삼촌 같으면 그쯤은 아무것도 아니라고 하실걸요.”"]], [["\"Uncle Christopher is made of sterner stuff than we are,\"", "“크리스토퍼 삼촌은 우리보다 단단한 재료로 빚어진 분이지요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"They used to be born so sixty or seventy years ago.\"", "“육칠십 년 전에는 사람들이 그렇게 태어나곤 했던 겁니다.”"], ["And then they walked on through Gruddock's fields, and the home paddocks, back to the Great House, where they found the squire standing in the front of the porch.", "그러고 나서 그들은 그러독의 밭과 집 옆 목초지를 지나 큰 집으로 걸어 돌아왔고, 거기서 현관 앞에 서 있는 지주와 마주쳤다."]], [["The walk had not been so pleasant as they had all intended that it should be when they made their arrangements for it.", "산책은 모두가 그 약속을 정할 때 기대했던 만큼 즐겁지 않았다."], ["Crosbie had endeavoured to recover his happy state of mind, but had been unsuccessful; and Lily, fancying that her lover was not all that he should be, had become reserved and silent.", "크로즈비는 행복하던 마음을 되찾으려 애썼으나 뜻대로 되지 않았고, 릴리는 제 연인이 마땅히 그러해야 할 모습이 아니라고 여겨져 말수가 줄고 서먹해졌다."], ["Bernard and Bell had not shared this discomfiture, but then Bernard and Bell were, as a rule, much more given to silence than the other two.", "버나드와 벨은 이 어색함을 함께 겪지 않았으나, 버나드와 벨은 본디 다른 두 사람보다 훨씬 더 말이 없는 편이었다."]], [["\"Uncle,\"", "“삼촌.”"], ["said Lily, \"these men have shot nothing, and you cannot conceive how unhappy they are in consequence.", "릴리가 말했다. “이분들이 아무것도 못 잡아 오셨는데, 그 때문에 얼마나 울적해하시는지 삼촌은 짐작도 못 하실 거예요."], ["It's all the fault of the naughty partridges.\"", "죄다 못된 자고새들 탓이지요.”"]], [["\"There are plenty of partridges if they knew how to get them,\"", "“잡을 줄만 알면 자고새야 얼마든지 있다.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"The dogs are uncommonly wild,\"", "“개들이 유난히 날뛰더군요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"They are not wild with me,\"", "“내가 데리고 나가면 안 날뛰네."], ["said the squire; \"nor yet with Dingles.\"", "딩글스가 데리고 나가도 안 날뛰고.”"], ["Dingles was the squire's gamekeeper.", "지주가 말했다. 딩글스는 지주의 사냥터지기였다."], ["\"The fact is, you young men, nowadays, expect to have dogs trained to do all the work for you.", "“실은 말이야, 요즘 젊은 사람들은 개가 일을 다 해 주도록 훈련되어 있기를 바란단 말이지."], ["It's too much labour for you to walk up to your game.", "제 발로 사냥감까지 걸어가는 건 너무 수고롭다 이거야."], ["You'll be late for dinner, girls, if you don't look sharp.\"", "얘들아, 꾸물대다간 저녁 시간에 늦겠구나.”"]], [["\"We're not coming up this evening, sir,\"", "“저희는 오늘 저녁엔 올라가지 않아요, 삼촌.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"And why not?\"", "“어째서 안 온다는 게냐?”"]], [["\"We're going to stay with mamma.\"", "“어머니 곁에 있으려고요.”"]], [["\"And why will not your mother come with you?", "“네 어미는 왜 너희와 함께 오지 않는 게냐?"], ["I'll be whipped if I can understand it.", "도무지 알 수가 없으니 답답해 죽을 노릇이다."], ["One would have thought that under the present circumstances she would have been glad to see you all as much together as possible.\"", "지금 같은 사정이라면 너희를 되도록 자주 한자리에 모아 놓고 보고 싶어 할 법도 하건만.”"]], [["\"We're together quite enough,\"", "“저희끼리도 충분히 함께 있는걸요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"And as for mamma, I suppose she thinks--\"", "“그리고 어머니로 말하자면, 아마 어머니 생각에는—”"], ["And then she stopped herself, catching the glance of Bell's imploring eye.", "그러고 나서 그 애는 애원하는 듯한 벨의 눈짓을 알아채고 입을 다물었다."], ["She was going to make some indignant excuse for her mother,--some excuse which would be calculated to make her uncle angry.", "어머니를 위해 성난 변명을 늘어놓으려던 참이었으니, 삼촌을 노하게 만들 것이 뻔한 변명이었다."], ["It was her practice to say such sharp words to him, and consequently he did not regard her as warmly as her more silent and more prudent sister.", "이렇게 날 선 말을 삼촌에게 하는 것이 릴리의 버릇이었고, 그래서 삼촌은 더 말수 적고 더 조심스러운 언니만큼 릴리를 따뜻이 여기지 않았다."], ["At the present moment he turned quickly round and went into the house; and then, with a very few words of farewell, the two young men followed him.", "지금도 그는 홱 돌아서서 집 안으로 들어가 버렸고, 그러자 두 젊은이도 아주 짧은 작별 인사만 남기고 그를 따라 들어갔다."], ["The girls went back over the little bridge by themselves, feeling that the afternoon had not gone off altogether well.", "아가씨들은 둘이서 작은 다리를 건너 돌아가면서, 이 오후가 도무지 좋게 끝나지 않았다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["\"You shouldn't provoke him, Lily,\"", "“삼촌 화를 돋우면 안 돼, 릴리.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"And he shouldn't say those things about mamma.", "“삼촌도 어머니에 대해 그런 말씀은 하시면 안 되지."], ["It seems to me that you don't mind what he says.\"", "언니는 삼촌이 무슨 말을 하든 아무렇지도 않은 것 같더라.”"]], [["\"Oh, Lily.\"", "“어머나, 릴리.”"]], [["\"No more you do.", "“언니도 마찬가지야."], ["He makes me so angry that I cannot hold my tongue.", "삼촌은 나를 화나게 만들어서 도저히 입을 다물고 있을 수가 없어."], ["He thinks that because all the place is his, he is to say just what he likes.", "이 땅이 전부 제 것이니 하고 싶은 말은 뭐든 해도 된다고 생각하시는 거야."], ["Why should mamma go up there to please his humours?\"", "어머니가 왜 삼촌 비위 맞추러 저 위까지 올라가야 해?”"]], [["\"You may be sure that mamma will do what she thinks best.", "“어머니는 당신이 가장 좋다고 여기시는 대로 하실 게 틀림없어."], ["She is stronger-minded than uncle Christopher, and does not want any one to help her.", "어머니는 크리스토퍼 삼촌보다 마음이 굳센 분이고, 누구 도움도 바라지 않으셔."], ["But, Lily, you shouldn't speak as though I were careless about mamma.", "하지만 릴리, 내가 어머니를 아끼지 않는다는 식으로 말하면 못써."], ["You didn't mean that, I know.\"", "너도 그런 뜻은 아니었을 테지, 알아.”"]], [["\"Of course I didn't.\"", "“당연히 아니지.”"], ["Then the two girls joined their mother in their own little domain; but we will return to the men at the Great House.", "그러고 나서 두 아가씨는 저희들만의 작은 영지에서 어머니와 합류했다. 그러나 우리는 큰 집의 남자들에게로 돌아가기로 하자."]], [["Crosbie, when he went up to dress for dinner, fell into one of those melancholy fits of which I have spoken.", "크로즈비는 저녁 식사를 위해 옷을 갈아입으러 올라갔다가 내가 앞서 이야기한 저 우울한 발작 가운데 하나에 빠져들었다."], ["Was he absolutely about to destroy all the good that he had done for himself throughout the past years of his hitherto successful life?", "그는 지금껏 성공적이었던 제 인생의 지난 세월 내내 스스로를 위해 이루어 놓은 모든 좋은 것을 기어이 허물어뜨리려는 참인가?"], ["or rather, as he at last put the question to himself more strongly,--was it not the case that he had already destroyed all that success?", "아니, 오히려, 마침내 그가 스스로에게 더 힘주어 물었듯이— 그는 이미 그 성공을 죄다 허물어뜨려 버린 것이 아닌가?"], ["His marriage with Lily, whether it was to be for good or bad, was now a settled thing, and was not regarded as a matter admitting of any doubt.", "릴리와의 결혼은, 그것이 좋은 일이 되든 나쁜 일이 되든, 이제 정해진 일이었고 조금이라도 의심을 품어 볼 여지가 있는 문제로는 여겨지지 않았다."], ["To do the man justice, I must declare that in all these moments of misery he still did the best he could to think of Lily herself as of a great treasure which he had won,--as of a treasure which should, and perhaps would, compensate him for his misery.", "이 사내에게 공정하려면 나는 이렇게 밝혀 두어야겠다. 이 모든 비참한 순간에도 그는 릴리라는 사람 자체를 제가 얻어 낸 큰 보물로— 제 비참함을 보상해 주어야 마땅한, 그리고 어쩌면 보상해 주게 될 보물로 여기려고 여전히 힘껏 애썼다는 것을."], ["But there was the misery very plain.", "그러나 그 비참함은 아주 뚜렷이 거기 있었다."], ["He must give up his clubs, and his fashion, and all that he had hitherto gained, and be content to live a plain, humdrum, domestic life, with eight hundred a year, and a small house, full of babies.", "그는 클럽도, 유행도, 여태 얻어 낸 모든 것도 내려놓고, 연 팔백 파운드에 아기들로 가득한 조그만 집에서 밋밋하고 단조로운 가정생활을 하는 데 만족해야 할 터였다."], ["It was not the kind of Elysium for which he had tutored himself.", "그것은 그가 스스로를 길들여 온 그런 종류의 엘리시움이 아니었다."], ["Lily was very nice, very nice indeed.", "릴리는 아주 참했다, 정말이지 아주 참했다."], ["She was, as he said to himself, \"by odds, the nicest girl that he had ever seen.\"", "스스로에게 말했듯 그 애는 “단연코 여태 본 중에 가장 참한 아가씨”였다."], ["Whatever might now turn up, her happiness should be his first care.", "이제 무슨 일이 벌어지든 그 애의 행복이 그의 첫째 관심사가 될 것이었다."], ["But as for his own,--he began to fear that the compensation would hardly be perfect.", "그러나 제 자신의 행복으로 말하자면— 그는 그 보상이 아무래도 온전하지는 못하리라고 걱정하기 시작했다."], ["\"It is my own doing,\"", "“내가 자초한 일이다.”"], ["he said to himself, intending to be rather noble in the purport of his soliloquy, \"I have trained myself for other things,--very foolishly.", "제 독백의 취지를 자못 고결하게 만들 셈으로 그는 스스로에게 말했다. “나는 다른 것들에 맞추어 나를 길들여 왔지— 아주 어리석게도."], ["Of course I must suffer,--suffer damnably. But she shall never know it.", "물론 나는 괴로워해야 한다— 지독하게 괴로워해야지."], ["Dear, sweet, innocent, pretty little thing!\"", "하지만 그 애는 결코 그것을 알지 못할 것이다."], ["And then he went on about the squire, as to whom he felt himself entitled to be indignant by his own disinterested and manly line of conduct towards the niece.", "사랑스럽고 다정하고 순진한, 어여쁜 것!” 그러고 나서 그는 지주 이야기로 넘어갔는데, 조카딸에게 사심 없고 사내다운 처신을 해 온 제게는 노여워할 자격이 있다고 느꼈던 것이다."], ["\"But I will let him know what I think about it,\" he said.", "“하지만 내가 어떻게 생각하는지는 그분께 알려 드려야겠어.”"], ["\"It's all very well for Dale to say that I have been treated fairly.", "그는 말했다. “내가 공정한 대접을 받았다는 건 데일이야 얼마든지 할 수 있는 말이지."], ["It isn't fair for a man to put forward his niece under false pretences.", "하지만 거짓 구실을 내세워 제 조카딸을 내놓는 건 사내로서 공정한 일이 아니야."], ["Of course I thought that he intended to provide for her.\"", "물론 나는 그분이 그 애의 앞가림을 해 주실 작정이라고 생각했지.”"], ["And then, having made up his mind in a very manly way that he would not desert Lily altogether after having promised to marry her, he endeavoured to find consolation in the reflection that he might, at any rate, allow himself two years' more run as a bachelor in London.", "그러고는, 결혼을 약속해 놓고서 릴리를 아주 저버리지는 않겠다고 자못 사내답게 마음을 정한 뒤에, 그는 적어도 런던에서 총각으로 이태쯤은 더 지낼 수 있으리라는 생각에서 위안을 찾으려 했다."], ["Girls who have to get themselves married without fortunes always know that they will have to wait.", "재산 없이 시집을 가야 하는 아가씨들은 기다려야 하리라는 것을 언제나 알고 있는 법이다."], ["Indeed, Lily had already told him, that as far as she was concerned, she was in no hurry.", "실제로 릴리는 이미 그에게, 자기로서는 조금도 서두를 것 없다고 말해 두었다."], ["He need not, therefore, at once withdraw his name from Sebright's.", "그러니 세브라이트에서 당장 제 이름을 뺄 필요는 없었다."], ["Thus he endeavoured to console himself, still, however, resolving that he would have a little serious conversation with the squire that very evening as to Lily's fortune.", "이렇게 그는 스스로를 달래 보려 했으나, 그러면서도 여전히 바로 그날 저녁 릴리의 재산에 관해 지주와 진지한 이야기를 좀 나누어야겠다고 마음먹고 있었다."]], [["And what was the state of Lily's mind at the same moment, while she, also, was performing some slight toilet changes preparatory to their simple dinner at the Small House?", "그러면 같은 순간 릴리의 마음은 어떠했을까? 그 애 역시 작은 집에서의 소박한 저녁 식사에 앞서 몸단장을 조금 손보고 있던 참이었는데."]], [["\"I didn't behave well to him,\"", "“나는 그이한테 잘하지 못했어.”"], ["she said to herself; \"I never do.", "그 애는 혼잣말을 했다. “나는 늘 그래."], ["I forget how much he is giving up for me; and then, when anything annoys him, I make it worse instead of comforting him.\"", "그이가 나를 위해 얼마나 많은 것을 내주고 있는지는 잊어버리고, 무슨 일로 그이가 언짢아하면 위로해 주기는커녕 더 언짢게 만들지.”"], ["And upon that she made accusation against herself that she did not love him half enough,--that she did not let him see how thoroughly and perfectly she loved him.", "그러고서 그 애는, 자기가 그이를 반만큼도 사랑하지 않는다고— 자기가 그이를 얼마나 온전히 그리고 남김없이 사랑하는지 그이에게 보여 주지 않는다고 스스로를 나무랐다."], ["She had an idea of her own, that as a girl should never show any preference for a man till circumstances should have fully entitled him to such manifestation, so also should she make no drawback on her love, but pour it forth for his benefit with all her strength, when such circumstances had come to exist.", "그 애에게는 나름의 생각이 있었다. 여자는 사정이 그럴 자격을 온전히 그 사람에게 줄 때까지 어떤 남자에게도 마음이 기운 티를 내서는 안 되지만, 그런 사정이 정말로 생기고 나면 사랑을 아끼는 일 없이 온 힘을 다해 그 사람을 위해 쏟아 부어야 한다는 것이었다."], ["But she was ever feeling that she was not acting up to her theory, now that the time for such practice had come.", "그러나 그 애는 그렇게 실행할 때가 온 지금, 자기가 제 이론대로 하고 있지 못하다는 것을 늘 느끼고 있었다."], ["She would unwittingly assume little reserves, and make small pretences of indifference in spite of her own judgment.", "그 애는 저도 모르게 자잘하게 몸을 사렸고, 제 분별과는 어긋나게 아무렇지 않은 척을 조금씩 했다."], ["She had done so on this afternoon, and had left him without giving him her hand to press, without looking up into his face with an assurance of love, and therefore she was angry with herself.", "그날 오후에도 그렇게 하여, 손을 잡아 달라고 내밀지도 않고 사랑을 확인해 주는 눈으로 그이의 얼굴을 올려다보지도 않은 채 그이를 보내 버렸으니, 그래서 그 애는 스스로에게 화가 나 있었다."], ["\"I know I shall teach him to hate me,\"", "“나는 결국 그이가 나를 미워하게 가르치고 말 거야.”"], ["she said out loud to Bell.", "그 애가 벨에게 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["\"That would be very sad,\"", "“그건 아주 슬픈 일이겠구나.”"], ["said Bell; \"but I don't see it.\"", "벨이 말했다. “하지만 나는 그렇게 보이지 않는걸.”"]], [["\"If you were engaged to a man you would be much better to him.", "“언니가 어떤 남자와 약혼을 했다면 언니는 그이한테 훨씬 잘했을 거야."], ["You would not say so much, but what you did say would be all affection.", "언니는 그렇게 말이 많지 않을 테고, 무슨 말을 하든 죄다 애정이 담겨 있겠지."], ["I am always making horrid little speeches, for which I should like to cut out my tongue afterwards.\"", "나는 늘 끔찍한 소리를 조금씩 해 놓고는 나중에 혀를 잘라 버리고 싶어져.”"]], [["\"Whatever sort of speeches they are, I think that he likes them.\"", "“어떤 소리든 간에 그이는 그걸 좋아하는 것 같더라.”"]], [["\"Does he?", "“그럴까?"], ["I'm not all so sure of that, Bell.", "나는 그렇게까지 확신이 서지 않아, 언니."], ["Of course I don't expect that he is to scold me,--not yet, that is.", "물론 그이가 나를 꾸짖으리라고 기대하는 건 아니야— 적어도 아직은."], ["But I know by his eye when he is pleased and when he is displeased.\"", "하지만 그이가 흡족해할 때와 언짢아할 때는 눈만 보면 알 수 있는걸.”"]], [["And then they went down to their dinner.", "그러고 나서 둘은 저녁을 먹으러 내려갔다."]], [["Up at the Great House the three gentlemen met together in apparent good humour.", "큰 집에서는 세 신사가 겉보기에는 기분 좋게 한자리에 모였다."], ["Bernard Dale was a man of an equal temperament, who rarely allowed any feeling, or even any annoyance, to interfere with his usual manner,--a man who could always come to table with a smile, and meet either his friend or his enemy with a properly civil greeting.", "버나드 데일은 마음이 고른 사람이어서, 어떤 감정도, 심지어 짜증조차도 제 평소의 태도를 흐트러뜨리도록 좀처럼 내버려 두지 않았다. 언제나 미소를 띠고 식탁에 나올 수 있고, 벗이든 원수든 알맞게 예의 바른 인사로 맞을 수 있는 사람이었다."], ["Not that he was especially a false man.", "그렇다고 그가 유난히 거짓된 사람이라는 말은 아니다."], ["There was nothing of deceit in his placidity of demeanour.", "그 잔잔한 몸가짐에는 속임수 같은 것이 없었다."], ["It arose from true equanimity; but it was the equanimity of a cold disposition rather than of one well ordered by discipline.", "그것은 참된 평정심에서 나온 것이었으나, 수양으로 잘 다스려진 기질의 평정이라기보다는 차가운 기질의 평정이었다."], ["The squire was aware that he had been unreasonably petulant before dinner, and having taken himself to task in his own way, now entered the dining-room with the courteous greeting of a host.", "지주는 저녁 식사 전에 자기가 까닭 없이 성마르게 굴었음을 알고 있었고, 제 나름의 방식으로 스스로를 나무란 뒤에 이제 주인다운 정중한 인사를 건네며 식당으로 들어섰다."], ["\"I find that your bag was not so bad after all,\"", "“듣자 하니 자네 사냥 자루도 그리 나쁘지는 않았다더군.”"], ["he said, \"and I hope that your appetite is at least as good as your bag.\"", "그가 말했다. “입맛도 적어도 자루만큼은 좋기를 바라네.”"]], [["Crosbie smiled, and made himself pleasant, and said a few flattering words.", "크로즈비는 미소를 짓고 상냥하게 굴며 몇 마디 듣기 좋은 말을 했다."], ["A man who intends to take some very decided step in an hour or two generally contrives to bear himself in the meantime as though the trifles of the world were quite sufficient for him.", "한두 시간 뒤에 아주 단단히 마음먹은 걸음을 내디딜 작정인 사람은 대개 그때까지는 세상의 하찮은 일들만으로도 족하다는 듯이 처신하게 마련이다."], ["So he praised the squire's game; said a good-natured word as to Dingles, and bantered himself as to his own want of skill.", "그리하여 그는 지주의 사냥터를 칭찬하고, 딩글스에 대해서도 너그러운 말을 한마디 하고, 제 서툰 솜씨를 두고는 스스로를 놀렸다."], ["Then all went merry,--not quite as a marriage bell; but still merry enough for a party of three gentlemen.", "그러자 모든 것이 즐거이 흘러갔다— 혼례의 종소리만큼까지는 아니었으나, 그래도 신사 셋의 모임치고는 넉넉히 즐거웠다."]], [["But Crosbie's resolution was fixed; and as soon, therefore, as the old butler was permanently gone, and the wine steadily in transit upon the table, he began his task, not without some apparent abruptness.", "그러나 크로즈비의 결심은 굳어져 있었다. 그래서 늙은 집사가 아주 물러가고 포도주가 식탁 위를 꾸준히 오가게 되자마자 그는 제 볼일을 시작했으니, 얼마간 느닷없어 보이는 것을 면치는 못했다."], ["Having fully considered the matter, he had determined that he would not wait for Bernard Dale's absence.", "곰곰이 따져 본 끝에 그는 버나드 데일이 자리를 비울 때까지 기다리지 않기로 마음을 정했던 것이다."], ["He thought it possible that he might be able to fight his battle better in Bernard's presence than he could do behind his back.", "등 뒤에서보다는 버나드가 있는 앞에서 이 싸움을 더 잘 치를 수 있으리라는 생각이 들었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Squire,\" he began.", "“지주 어른.”"], ["They all called him squire when they were on good terms together, and Crosbie thought it well to begin as though there was nothing amiss between them.", "그가 입을 열었다. 사이가 좋을 때면 그들은 모두 그를 지주라고 불렀고, 크로즈비는 둘 사이에 아무 어긋난 일도 없는 듯이 시작하는 편이 좋겠다고 생각했다."], ["\"Squire, of course I am thinking a good deal at the present moment as to my intended marriage.\"", "“지주 어른, 물론 저는 요즈음 제 결혼에 대해 생각이 많습니다.”"]], [["\"That's natural enough,\"", "“그야 당연한 노릇이지.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, by George!", "“그럼요, 맹세코 그렇습니다!"], ["sir, a man doesn't make a change like that without finding that he has got something to think of.\"", "사내가 그만한 변화를 겪으면서 생각할 거리가 생겼다는 것을 깨닫지 않을 도리는 없지요.”"]], [["\"I suppose not,\"", "“그렇겠지.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"I never was in the way of getting married myself, but I can easily understand that.\"", "“나야 결혼할 처지에 놓여 본 적이 없네만, 그쯤은 쉽게 알 만하이.”"]], [["\"I've been the luckiest fellow in the world in finding such a girl as your niece--\"", "“어르신의 조카딸 같은 아가씨를 만났으니 저는 세상에서 가장 운 좋은 사내입니다—”"], ["Whereupon the squire bowed, intending to make a little courteous declaration that the luck in the matter was on the side of the Dales.", "그러자 지주가 고개를 숙였는데, 이 일에서 운이 좋은 쪽은 데일 집안이라는 뜻으로 조그맣게 예를 차려 말하려던 것이었다."], ["\"I know that,\"", "“저도 압니다.”"], ["continued Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말을 이었다."], ["\"She is exactly everything that a girl ought to be.\"", "“그 사람은 아가씨가 마땅히 갖추어야 할 모든 것을 그대로 갖추었지요.”"]], [["\"She is a good girl,\"", "“착한 아이지.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; I think she is,\"", "“그래, 나도 그렇게 생각하네.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"But it seems to me,\"", "“하지만 제가 보기에는,”"], ["said Crosbie, finding that it was necessary to dash at once headlong into the water, \"that something ought to be said as to my means of supporting her properly.\"", "이제는 단번에 물속으로 뛰어들어야겠다는 생각이 들어 크로즈비가 말했다. “그 사람을 제대로 건사할 제 살림 밑천에 대해서도 무언가 말이 있어야 할 것 같습니다.”"]], [["Then he paused for a moment, expecting that the squire would speak.", "그러고 나서 그는 지주가 말을 받으리라 기대하며 잠시 뜸을 들였다."], ["But the squire sat perfectly still, looking intently at the empty fireplace and saying nothing.", "그러나 지주는 조금도 움직이지 않고 빈 벽난로만 골똘히 바라보며 아무 말도 하지 않았다. “그 사람을 건사한다는 건,”"], ["\"Of supporting her,\"", "크로즈비가 말을 이었다."], ["continued Crosbie, \"with all those comforts to which she has been accustomed.\"", "“그 사람이 여태 익숙해져 있던 온갖 안락과 더불어 그리한다는 뜻입니다.”"]], [["\"She has never been used to expense,\"", "“그 애는 돈 쓰는 데 익숙해져 본 적이 없네.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"Her mother, as you doubtless know, is not a rich woman.\"", "“자네도 익히 알다시피 그 어미는 넉넉한 여인이 아니니까.”"]], [["\"But living here, Lily has had great advantages,--a horse to ride, and all that sort of thing.\"", "“하지만 여기서 지내면서 릴리는 큰 혜택을 누렸지요— 타고 다닐 말이라든가, 그런 것들 말입니다.”"]], [["\"I don't suppose she expects a horse in the park,\"", "“그 애가 공원에서 탈 말까지 바라리라고는 생각지 않네만.”"], ["said the squire, with a very perceptible touch of sarcasm in his voice.", "지주가 말했는데, 목소리에는 비아냥이 아주 뚜렷이 묻어났다."]], [["\"I hope not,\"", "“그야 아니겠지요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"I believe she has had the use of one of the ponies here sometimes, but I hope that has not made her extravagant in her ideas.", "“여기 있는 조랑말 가운데 하나를 이따금 부린 것은 나도 아네만, 그 때문에 그 애 생각이 헤퍼지지는 않았기를 바라네."], ["I did not think that there was anything of that nonsense about either of them.\"", "나는 그 두 아이 어느 쪽에도 그런 실없는 구석이 있다고는 여기지 않았어.”"]], [["\"Nor is there,--as far as I know.\"", "“그런 것은 없습니다— 제가 아는 한은.”"]], [["\"Nothing of the sort,\"", "“전혀 그렇지 않습니다.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"But the long and the short of it is this, sir!\"", "“하지만 이러니저러니 해도 결국은 이것입니다, 어르신!”"], ["and Crosbie, as he spoke, endeavoured to maintain his ordinary voice and usual coolness, but his heightened colour betrayed that he was nervous.", "그리고 크로즈비는 말하면서 여느 때의 목소리와 평소의 침착함을 지키려 애썼으나, 붉어진 낯빛이 그가 떨고 있음을 드러내 주었다."], ["\"Am I to expect any accession of income with my wife?\"", "“제가 아내와 더불어 얼마간의 수입을 기대해도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["\"I have not spoken to my sister-in-law on the subject,\"", "“그 문제로 제수씨와 이야기해 본 적은 없네.” 지주가 말했다."], ["said the squire; \"but I should fear that she cannot do much.\"", "“하지만 그 사람이 해 줄 수 있는 건 없을 것 같으이.”"]], [["\"As a matter of course, I would not take a shilling from her,\"", "“물론 저야 그분께서 주시는 것은 한 실링도 받지 않을 겁니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Then that settles it,\"", "“그렇다면 그것으로 끝난 일이군.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["Crosbie paused a moment, during which his colour became very red.", "크로즈비는 잠시 말을 멈추었고, 그동안 낯빛이 몹시 붉어졌다."], ["He unconsciously took up an apricot and eat it, and then he spoke out.", "그는 저도 모르게 살구 하나를 집어 먹고는 이윽고 대놓고 말했다."], ["\"Of course I was not alluding to Mrs.", "“물론 저는 데일 부인의 수입을 두고 말씀드린 것이 아닙니다."], ["Dale's income; I would not, on any account, disturb her arrangements.", "저는 무슨 일이 있어도 그분의 살림을 흔들 생각이 없습니다."], ["But I wished to learn, sir, whether you intend to do anything for your niece.\"", "다만 제가 알고 싶었던 것은, 어르신께서 조카딸을 위해 무언가 해 주실 뜻이 있으신가 하는 것입니다.”"]], [["\"In the way of giving her a fortune?", "“재산을 물려주는 식으로 말인가?"], ["Nothing at all.", "전혀 없네."], ["I intend to do nothing at all.\"", "나는 아무것도 할 뜻이 없어.”"]], [["\"Then I suppose we understand each other,--at last,\"", "“그렇다면 이제야 서로를 알게 된 셈이군요— 마침내 말입니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"I should have thought that we might have understood each other at first,\"", "“나는 우리가 처음부터 서로를 알고 있었으리라 여겼네만.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"Did I ever make you any promise, or give you any hint that I intended to provide for my niece?", "“내가 자네에게 무슨 약속을 한 적이 있나, 아니면 내 조카딸의 앞가림을 해 줄 뜻이 있다고 귀띔이라도 한 적이 있나?"], ["Have I ever held out to you any such hope?", "내가 자네에게 그런 기대를 품게 한 일이 있는가?"], ["I don't know what you mean by that word 'at last'--unless it be to give offence.\"", "'마침내'라는 그 말을 자네가 무슨 뜻으로 하는지 나는 모르겠네— 사람을 거스르자는 것이 아니라면 말이야.”"]], [["\"I meant the truth, sir;--I meant this--that seeing the manner in which your nieces lived with you, I thought it probable that you would treat them both as though they were your daughters.", "“저는 있는 그대로를 말씀드린 겁니다, 어르신. 제 뜻은 이렇습니다— 조카딸들이 어르신과 더불어 살아가는 모양을 보고서, 저는 어르신께서 두 분을 마치 친딸처럼 대해 주시리라 여겼습니다."], ["Now I find out my mistake;--that is all!\"", "이제 제가 잘못 알았다는 것을 알게 되었지요— 그뿐입니다!”"]], [["\"You have been mistaken,--and without a shadow of excuse for your mistake.\"", "“자네가 잘못 안 걸세— 그리고 그 잘못에는 변명거리가 티끌만큼도 없어.”"]], [["\"Others have been mistaken with me,\"", "“저와 마찬가지로 잘못 안 사람들이 또 있습니다.”"], ["said Crosbie, forgetting, on the spur of the moment, that he had no right to drag the opinion of any other person into the question.", "크로즈비가 말했는데, 그 순간에 문득 잊어버렸던 것이다. 다른 어떤 사람의 생각을 이 문제로 끌어들일 권리가 자기에게는 없다는 것을."]], [["\"What others?\"", "“또 누가 있단 말인가?”"], ["said the squire, with anger; and his mind immediately betook itself to his sister-in-law.", "지주가 성이 나서 말했다. 그리고 그의 마음은 곧바로 제수씨에게로 달려갔다."]], [["\"I do not want to make any mischief,\"", "“저는 아무 말썽도 만들고 싶지 않습니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"If anybody connected with my family has presumed to tell you that I intended to do more for my niece Lilian than I have already done, such person has not only been false, but ungrateful.", "“내 집안과 이어진 누군가가 감히 자네에게, 내가 이미 해 준 것보다 더 많은 것을 조카딸 릴리언에게 해 줄 뜻이 있노라 말했다면, 그런 사람은 거짓될 뿐 아니라 은혜도 모르는 사람일세."], ["I have given to no one any authority to make any promise on behalf of my niece.\"", "나는 그 누구에게도 내 조카딸을 대신해 무슨 약속을 할 권한을 준 적이 없네.”"]], [["\"No such promise has been made.", "“그런 약속은 없었습니다."], ["It was only a suggestion,\"", "그저 그럴 법하다는 말이었지요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["He was not in the least aware to whom the squire was alluding in his anger; but he perceived that his host was angry, and having already reflected that he should not have alluded to the words which Bernard Dale had spoken in his friendship, he resolved to name no one.", "그는 지주가 성이 나서 누구를 두고 말하는 것인지 조금도 알지 못했다. 그러나 주인이 노해 있음을 알아차렸고, 버나드 데일이 우정에서 해 준 말을 끌어대지 말았어야 했다고 이미 뉘우친 터라 아무 이름도 대지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["Bernard, as he sat by listening, knew exactly how the matter stood; but, as he thought, there could be no reason why he should subject himself to his uncle's ill-will, seeing that he had committed no sin.", "곁에 앉아 듣고 있던 버나드는 사정이 어떻게 된 것인지 정확히 알고 있었다. 그러나 그의 생각으로는, 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않은 마당에 굳이 제 발로 삼촌의 미움을 살 까닭이 없었다."]], [["\"No such suggestion should have been made,\"", "“그런 말은 나오지 말았어야 하네.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"No one has had a right to make such a suggestion.", "“누구에게도 그런 말을 할 권리는 없었어."], ["No one has been placed by me in a position to make such a suggestion to you without manifest impropriety.", "나는 그 누구도, 뻔뻔스럽다는 소리를 듣지 않고서 자네에게 그런 말을 할 만한 자리에 앉혀 준 적이 없네. 나는 그 일에 대해 더는 묻지 않겠네."], ["I will ask no further questions about it; but it is quite as well that you should understand at once that I do not consider it to be my duty to give my niece Lilian a fortune on her marriage.", "다만 이것만은 지금 분명히 알아 두는 편이 좋겠지. 나는 내 조카딸 릴리언에게 혼인하면서 재산을 주는 것이 내 의무라고 여기지 않네."], ["I trust that your offer to her was not made under any such delusion.\"", "자네가 그 애에게 청혼한 것이 그런 착각 위에서 한 일이 아니기를 바라네.”"]], [["\"No, sir; it was not,\"", "“아닙니다, 어르신. 그렇지 않았습니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Then I suppose that no great harm has been done.", "“그렇다면 큰 해가 난 일은 없는 셈이군."], ["I am sorry if false hopes have been given to you; but I am sure you will acknowledge that they were not given to you by me.\"", "자네에게 헛된 기대를 품게 한 사람이 있었다면 안된 일이네. 하지만 그것을 품게 한 사람이 나는 아니라는 것쯤은 자네도 인정하리라 믿네.”"]], [["\"I think you have misunderstood me, sir.", "“어르신께서 저를 오해하신 듯합니다."], ["My hopes were never very high; but I thought it right to ascertain your intentions.\"", "제 기대는 결코 크지 않았습니다. 다만 어르신의 뜻을 확실히 알아 두는 것이 도리라고 생각했을 뿐입니다.”"]], [["\"Now you know them.", "“이제 알았겠지."], ["I trust, for the girl's sake, that it will make no difference to her.", "그 아이를 봐서라도 이 일이 그 애에게 아무 차이도 만들지 않기를 바라네."], ["I can hardly believe that she has been to blame in the matter.\"", "이 일에 그 애의 잘못이 있으리라고는 도무지 생각할 수 없으니까.”"]], [["Crosbie hastened at once to exculpate Lily; and then, with more awkward blunders than a man should have made who was so well acquainted with fashionable life as the Apollo of the Beaufort, he proceeded to explain that, as Lily was to have nothing, his own pecuniary arrangements would necessitate some little delay in their marriage.", "크로즈비는 곧바로 서둘러 릴리를 두둔했다. 그러고는, 보퍼트의 아폴로라 할 만큼 사교계 생활에 밝은 사람치고는 지나치게 어설프게 더듬거리면서, 릴리가 아무것도 받지 못하게 된 이상 자기 형편으로는 결혼을 얼마간 미룰 수밖에 없겠다고 설명했다."]], [["\"As far as I myself am concerned,\"", "“내 개인적인 생각을 말하자면,”"], ["said the squire, \"I do not like long engagements.", "지주가 말했다. “나는 긴 약혼을 좋아하지 않네."], ["But I am quite aware that in this matter I have no right to interfere, unless, indeed--\"", "하지만 이 문제에 대해서는 내가 참견할 권리가 없다는 것도 잘 알고 있지. 다만, 정말로—”"], ["and then he stopped himself.", "그러고서 그는 스스로 말을 멈추었다."]], [["\"I suppose it will be well to fix some day; eh, Crosbie?\"", "“날을 하나 잡아 두는 게 좋지 않겠나. 어떤가, 크로즈비?”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"I will discuss that matter with Mrs.", "“그 문제는 데일 부인과 상의하겠네.”"], ["Dale,\" said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"If you and she understand each other,\"", "“자네와 그 사람이 서로 뜻이 맞는다면,”"], ["said the squire, \"that will be sufficient.", "지주가 말했다. “그것으로 족하겠지."], ["Shall we go into the drawing-room now, or out upon the lawn?\"", "이제 응접실로 갈까, 아니면 잔디밭으로 나갈까?”"]], [["That evening, as Crosbie went to bed, he felt that he had not gained the victory in his encounter with the squire.", "그날 저녁 잠자리에 들면서 크로즈비는, 지주와의 겨룸에서 자기가 이기지 못했다고 느꼈다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII. IT CANNOT BE.", "# 제8장. 그럴 수는 없어"]], [["On the following morning at breakfast each of the three gentlemen at the Great House received a little note on pink paper, nominally from Mrs.", "이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서 큰 집의 세 신사는 저마다 분홍빛 종이에 적힌 작은 쪽지를 한 장씩 받았다."], ["Dale, asking them to drink tea at the Small House on that day week. At the bottom of the note which Lily had written for Mr.", "명목상으로는 데일 부인이 보낸 것으로, 그날로부터 일주일 뒤에 작은 집으로 차를 마시러 오라는 초대였다."], ["Crosbie was added: \"Dancing on the lawn, if we can get anybody to stand up.", "릴리가 크로즈비 씨 앞으로 쓴 쪽지의 아래쪽에는 이런 말이 덧붙어 있었다."], ["Of course you must come, whether you like it or not. And Bernard also.", "“나서서 춤출 사람만 구할 수 있다면 잔디밭에서 춤을 출 거예요."], ["Do your possible to talk my uncle into coming.\"", "물론 좋든 싫든 오셔야 해요. 버나드도요."], ["And this note did something towards re-creating good-humour among them at the breakfast-table.", "우리 삼촌도 오시게끔 힘닿는 데까지 구슬려 보세요.” 그리고 이 쪽지는 아침 식탁의 분위기를 다시 누그러뜨리는 데 얼마간 도움이 되었다."], ["It was shown to the squire, and at last he was brought to say that he would perhaps go to Mrs. Dale's little evening-party.", "쪽지는 지주에게도 보여졌고, 마침내 그는 데일 부인의 작은 저녁 모임에 어쩌면 가 볼지도 모르겠다는 말까지 하게 되었다."]], [["It may be well to explain that this promised entertainment had been originated with no special view to the pleasure of Mr. Crosbie, but altogether on behalf of poor Johnny Eames.", "이 약속된 모임이 크로즈비 씨를 즐겁게 해 주려는 뜻에서 나온 것이 결코 아니라, 오로지 가엾은 조니 임스를 위한 것이었다는 점은 밝혀 두는 편이 좋겠다."], ["What was to be done in that matter?", "그 일을 어떻게 하면 좋단 말인가?"], ["This question had been fully discussed between Mrs. Dale and Bell, and they had come to the conclusion that it would be best to ask Johnny over to a little friendly gathering, in which he might be able to meet Lily with some strangers around them.", "이 문제는 데일 부인과 벨 사이에서 충분히 논의되었고, 두 사람은 조니를 조촐한 친목 모임에 불러 낯선 사람들이 여럿 있는 자리에서 릴리와 마주치게 하는 것이 가장 좋겠다는 결론에 이르렀다."], ["In this way his embarrassment might be overcome.", "그렇게 하면 그의 겸연쩍음도 넘어갈 수 있을 터였다."], ["It would never do, as Mrs. Dale said, that he should be suffered to stay away, unnoticed by them.", "데일 부인의 말대로, 그가 저희들에게 아무런 관심도 받지 못한 채 멀찍이 떨어져 있도록 내버려 두어서는 결코 안 될 일이었다."], ["\"When the ice is once broken he won't mind it,\"", "“한번 어색한 고비만 깨지고 나면 그 애도 아무렇지 않을 거예요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["And, therefore, early in the day, a messenger was sent over to Guestwick, who returned with a note from Mrs.", "그리하여 그날 이른 시각에 심부름꾼 하나가 게스트윅으로 보내졌고, 그는 임스 부인의 답장을 들고 돌아왔다."], ["Eames, saying that she would come on the evening in question, with her son and daughter.", "그날 저녁에 아들딸을 데리고 가겠다는 내용이었다."], ["They would keep the fly and get back to Guestwick the same evening.", "삯마차를 붙들어 두었다가 그날 밤 안으로 게스트윅으로 돌아가겠다는 말도 덧붙어 있었다."], ["This was added, as an offer had been made of beds for Mrs. Eames and Mary.", "임스 부인과 메리에게 잠자리를 내어 주겠다는 제안이 있었기에 그렇게 적은 것이었다."]], [["Before the evening of the party another memorable occurrence had taken place at Allington, which must be described, in order that the feelings of the different people on that evening may be understood.", "모임이 열리는 저녁이 오기 전에 앨링턴에서는 또 하나 기억할 만한 일이 벌어졌는데, 그날 저녁에 이 사람 저 사람이 품게 되는 심정을 이해하려면 그 일을 먼저 이야기해 두어야 한다."], ["The squire had given his nephew to understand that he wished to have that matter settled as to his niece Bell; and as Bernard's views were altogether in accordance with the squire's, he resolved to comply with his uncle's wishes.", "지주는 조카에게, 조카딸 벨에 관한 그 문제를 매듭지었으면 한다는 뜻을 알려 둔 터였다. 그리고 버나드의 생각은 지주의 생각과 온전히 맞아떨어졌으므로, 그는 삼촌의 바람대로 따르기로 마음먹었다."], ["The project with him was not a new thing.", "그에게 이 계획은 새삼스러운 것이 아니었다."], ["He did love his cousin quite sufficiently for purposes of matrimony, and was minded that it would be a good thing for him to marry.", "그는 혼인을 목적으로 삼기에 충분할 만큼 사촌을 사랑하고 있었고, 결혼을 하는 편이 자기에게 좋은 일이라고 생각하던 참이었다."], ["He could not marry without money, but this marriage would give him an income without the trouble of intricate settlements, or the interference of lawyers hostile to his own interests.", "돈 없이는 결혼할 수 없었으나, 이 결혼이라면 까다로운 재산 약정으로 골머리를 앓거나 제 이익에 맞서는 변호사들과 부딪치는 일 없이 수입을 얻을 수 있었다."], ["It was possible that he might do better; but then it was possible also that he might do much worse; and, in addition to this, he was fond of his cousin.", "더 나은 자리를 구할 수도 있기는 했다. 그러나 그보다 훨씬 못한 자리를 얻게 될 수도 있는 노릇이었고, 게다가 그는 사촌을 좋아했다."], ["He discussed the matter within himself, very calmly; made some excellent resolutions as to the kind of life which it would behove him to live as a married man; settled on the street in London in which he would have his house, and behaved very prettily to Bell for four or five days running.", "그는 이 문제를 아주 침착하게 혼자 따져 보았고, 아내를 둔 남자로서 마땅히 살아야 할 삶에 대해 훌륭한 결심 몇 가지를 세웠으며, 런던의 어느 거리에 집을 마련할지 정해 두었고, 나흘이고 닷새고 잇달아 벨에게 아주 상냥하게 굴었다."], ["That he did not make love to her, in the ordinary sense of the word, must, I suppose, be taken for granted, seeing that Bell herself did not recognize the fact.", "그가 흔히 말하는 뜻으로의 구애를 한 것이 아니었다는 사실은, 벨 자신이 그것을 눈치채지 못했다는 점으로 미루어 당연한 일로 받아들여야 하리라고 나는 생각한다."], ["She had always liked her cousin, and thought that in these days he was making himself particularly agreeable.", "벨은 늘 사촌을 좋아해 왔고, 요즘 들어 그가 유난히 사근사근하게 군다고 여겼을 뿐이었다."]], [["On the evening before the party the girls were at the Great House, having come up nominally with the intention of discussing the expediency of dancing on the lawn.", "모임 전날 저녁에 두 아가씨는 큰 집에 올라와 있었다. 명목은 잔디밭에서 춤을 추는 것이 마땅한지를 의논하러 왔다는 것이었다."], ["Lily had made up her mind that it was to be so, but Bell had objected that it would be cold and damp, and that the drawing-room would be nicer for dancing.", "릴리는 꼭 그렇게 해야 한다고 마음을 굳혀 놓았으나, 벨은 밖이 춥고 눅눅할 테니 춤을 추기에는 응접실이 나을 것이라고 반대했다."]], [["\"You see we've only got four young gentlemen and one ungrown,\"", "“보다시피 우리한테는 젊은 신사가 넷뿐인 데다 하나는 아직 덜 자랐잖아요.”"], ["said Lily; \"and they will look so stupid standing up all properly in a room, as though we had a regular party.\"", "릴리가 말했다. “그런 사람들이 방 안에서 격식을 차리고 나란히 서 있으면, 무슨 제대로 된 연회라도 되는 것처럼 우스꽝스러워 보일 거예요.”"]], [["\"Thank you for the compliment,\"", "“칭찬 고맙습니다.”"], ["said Crosbie, taking off his straw hat.", "크로즈비가 밀짚모자를 벗으며 말했다."]], [["\"So you will; and we girls will look more stupid still.", "“정말 그럴 거예요. 그리고 우리 여자들은 더 우스꽝스러워 보이겠지요."], ["But out on the lawn it won't look stupid at all.", "하지만 잔디밭에 나가면 조금도 우습지 않아요."], ["Two or three might stand up on the lawn, and it would be jolly enough.\"", "두세 쌍쯤 잔디밭에 나서면 그것으로 충분히 즐거울 거예요.”"]], [["\"I don't quite see it,\"", "“난 잘 모르겠는걸.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, I think I see it,\"", "“아니, 나는 알겠습니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"The unadaptability of the lawn for the purpose of a ball--\"", "“잔디밭이 무도회 자리로는 걸맞지 않다는 점이—”"]], [["\"Nobody is thinking of a ball,\"", "“아무도 무도회 이야기는 안 했어요.”"], ["said Lily, with mock petulance.", "릴리가 짐짓 뾰로통하게 말했다."]], [["\"I'm defending you, and yet you won't let me speak.", "“내가 편을 들어 드리는 참인데도 말을 못 하게 하시는군요."], ["The unadaptability of the lawn for the purposes of a ball will conceal the insufficiency of four men and a boy as a supply of male dancers.", "잔디밭이 무도회 자리로 걸맞지 않다는 점이, 남자 춤꾼이라고는 어른 넷에 아이 하나뿐인 부족함을 가려 줄 겁니다."], ["But, Lily, who is the ungrown gentleman?", "그런데 릴리, 덜 자란 신사라는 게 누구지요?"], ["Is it your old friend Johnny Eames?\"", "옛 친구 조니 임스입니까?”"]], [["Lily's voice became sobered as she answered him.", "릴리는 그에게 대답하면서 목소리가 차분해졌다."]], [["\"Oh, no; I did not mean Mr.", "“아, 아니에요. 임스 씨를 두고 한 말이 아니에요."], ["Eames. He is coming, but I did not mean him.", "그분도 오시지만, 그분 이야기가 아니었어요."], ["Dick Boyce, Mr. Boyce's son, is only sixteen.", "보이스 씨 아들 딕 보이스가 이제 열여섯이거든요."], ["He is the ungrown gentleman.\"", "그 애가 덜 자란 신사예요.”"]], [["\"And who is the fourth adult?\"", "“그럼 네 번째 어른은 누구지요?”"]], [["\"Dr. Crofts, from Guestwick.", "“게스트윅에서 오시는 크로프츠 선생님이요."], ["I do hope you will like him, Adolphus.", "어돌퍼스, 그분을 꼭 좋아해 주셨으면 좋겠어요."], ["We think he is the very perfection of a man.\"", "우리는 그분이야말로 더할 나위 없이 완벽한 남자라고 생각하거든요.”"]], [["\"Then of course I shall hate him; and be very jealous, too!\"", "“그렇다면 나는 당연히 그 사람을 미워하게 되겠군요. 게다가 몹시 질투도 하게 되겠고요!”"]], [["And then that pair went off together, fighting their own little battle on that head, as turtle-doves will sometimes do.", "그러고 나서 그 한 쌍은 그 문제를 두고 저희끼리 작은 싸움을 벌이며 함께 자리를 떴다. 멧비둘기 한 쌍이 이따금 그러하듯이 말이다."], ["They went off, and Bernard was left with Bell standing together over the ha-ha fence which divides the garden at the back of the house from the field.", "두 사람이 떠나고 나자 버나드는 벨과 단둘이 남아, 집 뒤편의 정원과 들판을 갈라놓는 하하 도랑 위에 나란히 서 있게 되었다."]], [["\"Bell,\"", "“벨.”"], ["he said, \"they seem very happy, don't they?\"", "그가 말했다. “저 둘은 무척 행복해 보이는구나, 그렇지?”"]], [["\"And they ought to be happy now, oughtn't they?", "“지금이야 행복해야 마땅하지 않겠니? 가엾은 릴리!"], ["Dear Lily! I hope he will be good to her.", "저 사람이 릴리에게 잘해 주었으면 좋겠어."], ["Do you know, Bernard, though he is your friend, I am very, very anxious about it.", "버나드, 저 사람이 네 친구이긴 하다만, 나는 그 일이 정말이지 몹시 마음에 걸려."], ["It is such a vast trust to put in a man when we do not quite know him.\"", "제대로 알지도 못하는 남자에게 사람을 맡긴다는 건 엄청난 믿음이잖니.”"]], [["\"Yes, it is; but they'll do very well together.", "“그렇지. 하지만 둘은 아주 잘 지낼 거야."], ["Lily will be happy enough.\"", "릴리는 충분히 행복할 거고.”"]], [["\"And he?\"", "“저 사람은?”"]], [["\"I suppose he'll be happy, too.", "“저 친구도 행복할 테지."], ["He'll feel himself a little straightened as to income at first, but that will all come round.\"", "처음에는 수입이 좀 빠듯하다고 느끼겠지만, 그런 건 다 풀리게 마련이야.”"]], [["\"If he is not, she will be wretched.\"", "“저 사람이 행복하지 않으면 릴리는 비참해질 거야.”"]], [["\"They will do very well.", "“둘은 아주 잘 지낼 거라니까."], ["Lily must be prepared to make the money go as far as she can, that's all.\"", "릴리가 돈을 될 수 있는 대로 아껴 쓸 각오만 하면 돼. 그뿐이야.”"]], [["\"Lily won't feel the want of money.", "“릴리는 돈이 아쉬운 줄 모를 애야."], ["It is not that. But if he lets her know that she has made him a poor man, then she will be unhappy.", "그게 문제가 아니라, 저 사람이 자기 때문에 가난해졌다는 걸 릴리가 알게 만들면 그때는 릴리가 불행해진다는 거지."], ["Is he extravagant, Bernard?\"", "저 사람은 헤픈 사람이니, 버나드?”"]], [["But Bernard was anxious to discuss another subject, and therefore would not speak such words of wisdom as to Lily's engagement as might have been expected from him had he been in a different frame of mind.", "그러나 버나드는 다른 이야기를 꺼내고 싶어 안달이 나 있었고, 그래서 릴리의 약혼에 대해 마음이 다른 데 가 있지 않았더라면 응당 그에게서 나왔을 법한 현명한 말들을 늘어놓지 않았다."]], [["\"No, I should say not,\"", "“아니, 그렇지는 않을걸.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"But, Bell--\"", "“그런데 벨—”"]], [["\"I do not know that we could have acted otherwise than we have done, and yet I fear that we have been rash.", "“우리가 한 것 말고 달리 어떻게 할 수 있었을까 싶기는 해. 그래도 우리가 성급했던 게 아닌가 겁이 나."], ["If he makes her unhappy, Bernard, I shall never forgive you.\"", "저 사람이 릴리를 불행하게 만들면, 버나드, 나는 너를 절대로 용서하지 않을 거야.”"]], [["But as she said this she put her hand lovingly upon his arm, as a cousin might do, and spoke in a tone which divested her threat of its acerbity.", "그러나 이렇게 말하면서 벨은 사촌이 그러하듯 다정하게 그의 팔에 손을 얹었고, 그 목소리에는 제 위협에서 가시를 걷어 내는 무엇이 있었다."]], [["\"You must not quarrel with me, Bell, whatever may happen.", "“무슨 일이 있어도 나하고 다투면 안 돼, 벨."], ["I cannot afford to quarrel with you.\"", "나는 너와 다툴 형편이 못 되거든.”"]], [["\"Of course I was not in earnest as to that.\"", "“물론 진심으로 한 말은 아니야.”"]], [["\"You and I must never quarrel, Bell; at least, I hope not.", "“너와 나는 결코 다투는 일이 없어야 해, 벨. 적어도 나는 그러기를 바라."], ["I could bear to quarrel with any one rather than with you.\"", "나는 다른 누구와 다투는 건 견딜 수 있어도 너와 다투는 건 못 견뎌.”"], ["And then, as he spoke, there was something in his voice which gave the girl some slight, indistinct warning of what might be his intention.", "그리고 그가 이렇게 말할 때 그 목소리에는 무언가가 있어서, 그가 무엇을 마음먹고 있는지를 아가씨에게 어렴풋하고도 흐릿하게 일러 주었다."], ["Not that she said to herself at once, that he was going to make her an offer of his hand,--now, on the spot; but she felt that he intended something beyond the tenderness of ordinary cousinly affection.", "그가 지금 이 자리에서 자기에게 청혼을 하려 한다는 생각이 대뜸 들었다는 것은 아니다. 다만 그가 여느 사촌 사이의 다정함을 넘어서는 무언가를 뜻하고 있다는 것을 벨은 느꼈다."]], [["\"I hope we shall never quarrel,\"", "“우리가 다투는 일은 결코 없기를 바라.”"], ["she said.", "벨이 말했다."], ["But as she spoke, her mind was settling itself,--forming its resolution, and coming to a conclusion as to the sort of love which Bernard might, perhaps, expect.", "그러나 이렇게 말하는 동안 벨의 마음은 자리를 잡아 가고 있었다. 결심을 세우면서, 버나드가 어쩌면 바라고 있을지도 모를 사랑이 어떤 것인지에 대해 결론을 내리고 있었던 것이다."], ["And it formed another conclusion; as to the sort of love which might be given in return.", "그리고 그 마음은 또 하나의 결론에 이르렀다. 그 대가로 돌려줄 수 있는 사랑이 어떤 것인지에 대해서."]], [["\"Bell,\"", "“벨.”"], ["he said, \"you and I have always been dear friends.\"", "그가 말했다. “너와 나는 언제나 다정한 벗이었지.”"]], [["\"Yes; always.\"", "“그래, 언제나.”"]], [["\"Why should we not be something more than friends?\"", "“그런데 우리가 벗보다 더한 사이가 되지 못할 것도 없지 않니?”"]], [["To give Captain Dale his due I must declare that his voice was perfectly natural as he asked this question, and that he showed no signs of nervousness, either in his face or limbs.", "데일 대위의 공정을 기하기 위해 나는 이렇게 밝혀 두어야겠다. 그가 이 물음을 던질 때 그의 목소리는 더할 나위 없이 자연스러웠고, 얼굴에도 손발에도 떨리는 기색이라고는 없었다."], ["He had made up his mind to do it on that occasion, and he did it without any signs of outward disturbance.", "그는 그 자리에서 그 일을 하기로 마음을 굳혀 두었고, 겉으로 조금도 흔들리는 기색 없이 그것을 해냈다."], ["He asked his question, and then he waited for his answer.", "그는 물음을 던졌고, 그런 다음 대답을 기다렸다."], ["In this he was rather hard upon his cousin; for, though the question had certainly been asked in language that could not be mistaken, still the matter had not been put forward with all that fulness which a young lady, under such circumstances, has a right to expect.", "이 점에서 그는 사촌에게 다소 가혹했다. 그 물음이 잘못 알아들을 여지가 없는 말로 던져진 것은 분명했으나, 그럼에도 그런 처지에 놓인 젊은 아가씨가 마땅히 기대할 만한 그 온갖 살뜰함을 갖추어 내놓인 것은 아니었기 때문이다."]], [["They had sat down on the turf close to the ha-ha, and they were so near that Bernard was able to put out his hand with the view of taking that of his cousin within his own.", "두 사람은 하하 도랑 바로 곁의 잔디에 앉아 있었는데, 워낙 가까이 있었으므로 버나드는 손을 뻗어 사촌의 손을 제 손 안에 쥘 수 있었다."], ["But she contrived to keep her hands locked together, so that he merely held her gently by the wrist.", "그러나 벨이 용케 두 손을 맞잡은 채로 두었기에 그는 그저 손목을 가만히 잡았을 뿐이었다."]], [["\"I don't quite understand, Bernard,\"", "“무슨 말인지 잘 모르겠어, 버나드.”"], ["she said, after a minute's pause.", "잠깐 사이를 두었다가 벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Shall we be more than cousins?", "“우리가 사촌보다 더한 사이가 되지 않겠느냐는 말이야."], ["Shall we be man and wife?\"", "우리가 부부가 되지 않겠니?”"]], [["Now, at least, she could not say that she did not understand.", "이제는 적어도 무슨 말인지 모르겠다고 할 수가 없었다."], ["If the question was ever asked plainly, Bernard Dale had asked it plainly.", "이런 물음이 여태 분명하게 던져진 적이 있다면, 버나드 데일은 그것을 분명하게 던진 것이었다."], ["Shall we be man and wife?", "우리가 부부가 되지 않겠니?"], ["Few men, I fancy, dare to put it all at once in so abrupt a way, and yet I do not know that the English language affords any better terms for the question.", "내가 보기에 그렇게 대뜸 불쑥 다 털어놓을 만한 배짱을 가진 남자는 드물다. 그러면서도 나는 영어라는 말이 이 물음에 대해 그보다 나은 표현을 마련해 주고 있는지 알지 못한다."]], [["\"Oh, Bernard!", "“어머, 버나드!"], ["you have surprised me.\"", "너 때문에 놀랐어.”"]], [["\"I hope I have not pained you, Bell.", "“너를 아프게 하지는 않았기를 바라, 벨."], ["I have been long thinking of this, but I am well aware that my own manner, even to you, has not been that of a lover.", "나는 오래전부터 이 일을 생각해 왔어. 하지만 내 태도가, 너에게조차도 연인의 태도는 아니었다는 걸 잘 알고 있어."], ["It is not in me to smile and say soft things, as Crosbie can.", "크로즈비처럼 웃음을 흘리며 달콤한 말을 하는 건 내게 없는 재주야."], ["But I do not love you the less on that account.", "그렇다고 해서 너를 덜 사랑하는 건 아니야."], ["I have looked about for a wife, and I have thought that if I could gain you I should be very fortunate.\"", "나는 아내를 구하며 여기저기 둘러보았고, 너를 얻을 수 있다면 내가 아주 운이 좋은 사람이겠구나 생각했어.”"]], [["He did not then say anything about his uncle, and the eight hundred a year; but he fully intended to do so as soon as an opportunity should serve.", "그는 그때 삼촌에 대해서도, 연 팔백 파운드에 대해서도 아무 말을 하지 않았다. 그러나 기회가 닿는 대로 그 말을 할 작정이기는 했다."], ["He was quite of opinion that eight hundred a year and the good-will of a rich uncle were strong grounds for matrimony,--were grounds even for love; and he did not doubt but his cousin would see the matter in the same light.", "연 팔백 파운드와 넉넉한 삼촌의 호의는 혼인의 든든한 근거가 되며, 심지어 사랑의 근거까지도 된다는 것이 그의 확고한 생각이었다. 그리고 사촌도 그 문제를 같은 눈으로 볼 것이라고 그는 믿어 의심치 않았다."]], [["\"You are very good to me--more than good.", "“너는 내게 참 잘해 줘. 잘해 주는 정도가 아니지."], ["Of course I know that.", "물론 나도 그건 알아."], ["But, oh, Bernard!", "하지만, 어머, 버나드!"], ["I did not expect this a bit.\"", "이런 일은 조금도 생각지 못했어.”"]], [["\"But you will answer me, Bell!", "“그래도 대답은 해 줘야지, 벨!"], ["Or if you would like time to think, or to speak to my aunt, perhaps you will answer me to-morrow?\"", "아니면 생각할 시간을 갖고 싶다면, 혹은 숙모님께 여쭙고 싶다면, 내일 대답해 주어도 좋고.”"]], [["\"I think I ought to answer you now.\"", "“지금 대답하는 게 옳을 것 같아.”"]], [["\"Not if it be a refusal, Bell.", "“거절이라면 그러지 마, 벨."], ["Think well of it before you do that.", "그런 대답을 하기 전에 잘 생각해 봐."], ["I should have told you that our uncle wishes this match, and that he will remove any difficulty there might be about money.\"", "미리 말해 두었어야 했는데, 우리 삼촌이 이 혼사를 바라고 계셔. 돈 문제로 걸리는 것이 있다면 무엇이든 삼촌이 없애 주실 거야.”"]], [["\"I do not care for money.\"", "“나는 돈 같은 건 아무래도 좋아.”"]], [["\"But, as you were saying about Lily, one has to be prudent.", "“하지만 네가 릴리를 두고 말했듯이, 사람은 신중해야 하잖니."], ["Now, in our marriage, everything of that kind would be well arranged.", "우리 결혼이라면 그런 종류의 일은 무엇이든 잘 정리될 거야."], ["My uncle has promised me that he would at once allow us--\"", "삼촌이 나에게 약속하시기를, 곧바로 우리에게—”"]], [["\"Stop, Bernard.", "“그만해, 버나드."], ["You must not be led to suppose that any offer made by my uncle would help to purchase-- Indeed, there can be no need for us to talk about money.\"", "우리 삼촌이 무엇을 내놓든 그것으로 나를 살 수 있다고 여기게 되면 곤란해— 아니, 우리가 돈 이야기를 할 까닭은 조금도 없어.”"]], [["\"I wished to let you know the facts of the case, exactly as they are.", "“나는 이 일의 사정을 있는 그대로 네가 알아 두었으면 했을 뿐이야."], ["And as to our uncle, I cannot but think that you would be glad, in such a matter, to have him on your side.\"", "그리고 삼촌 이야기라면, 이런 일에 삼촌이 네 편에 서 계시는 걸 너도 기뻐하리라고 생각할 수밖에 없거든.”"]], [["\"Yes, I should be glad to have him on my side; that is, if I were going-- But my uncle's wishes could not influence my decision.", "“그래, 삼촌이 내 편에 서 계시면 기쁘겠지. 그러니까, 내가 그럴 마음이라면— 하지만 삼촌의 바람이 내 결정을 좌우할 수는 없어."], ["The fact is, Bernard--\"", "실은 말이야, 버나드—”"]], [["\"Well, dearest, what is the fact?\"", "“그래, 얘기해 봐. 실은 뭐라는 거야?”"]], [["\"I have always regarded you rather as a brother than as anything else.\"", "“나는 늘 너를 다른 무엇보다도 오라비처럼 여겨 왔어.”"]], [["\"But that regard may be changed.\"", "“하지만 그런 마음은 바뀔 수도 있잖아.”"]], [["\"No; I think not.", "“아니, 그렇지 않을 거야."], ["Bernard, I will go further and speak on at once.", "버나드, 한 걸음 더 나아가서 지금 다 말할게."], ["It cannot be changed.", "그건 바뀔 수 없어."], ["I know myself well enough to say that with certainty.", "내가 나를 아는 만큼은 아니까 확실히 말할 수 있어."], ["It cannot be changed.\"", "그건 바뀔 수 없어.”"]], [["\"You mean that you cannot love me?\"", "“나를 사랑할 수 없다는 말이니?”"]], [["\"Not as you would have me do.", "“네가 바라는 그런 식으로는 말이야."], ["I do love you very dearly,--very dearly, indeed.", "나는 너를 정말 깊이 사랑해. 정말이지 깊이."], ["I would go to you in any trouble, exactly as I would go to a brother.\"", "무슨 어려운 일이 생기면 나는 오라비를 찾아가듯 꼭 그렇게 너를 찾아갈 거야.”"]], [["\"And must that be all, Bell?\"", "“그것뿐이어야만 하니, 벨?”"]], [["\"Is not that all the sweetest love that can be felt?", "“그거야말로 사람이 느낄 수 있는 사랑 가운데 가장 다정한 것 아닐까?"], ["But you must not think me ungrateful, or proud.", "하지만 나를 은혜도 모르는 사람이라거나 도도한 사람이라고 여기지는 마."], ["I know well that you are--are proposing to do for me much more than I deserve.", "네가 나에게— 내가 받을 자격보다 훨씬 더 큰 것을 해 주려 한다는 걸 나는 잘 알아."], ["Any girl might be proud of such an offer.", "어떤 아가씨라도 그런 청혼이라면 자랑스러워할 거야."], ["But, dear Bernard--\"", "하지만, 버나드—”"]], [["\"Bell, before you give me a final answer, sleep upon this and talk it over with your mother.", "“벨, 마지막 대답을 주기 전에 하룻밤 자면서 생각해 보고 어머니와도 의논해 봐."], ["Of course you were unprepared, and I cannot expect that you should promise me so much without a moment's consideration.\"", "물론 너는 마음의 준비가 없었을 테고, 잠깐도 생각해 보지 않은 채 나에게 그런 큰 약속을 해 주기를 바랄 수는 없지.”"]], [["\"I was unprepared, and therefore I have not answered you as I should have done.", "“마음의 준비가 없었던 건 사실이야. 그래서 마땅히 했어야 할 대답을 못 하고 있었던 거고."], ["But as it has gone so far, I cannot let you leave me in uncertainty.", "하지만 이야기가 여기까지 온 이상 너를 아리송한 채로 보낼 수는 없어."], ["It is not necessary that I should keep you waiting.", "너를 기다리게 할 까닭이 없거든."], ["In this matter I do know my own mind.", "이 일에 대해서는 나도 내 마음을 알아."], ["Dear Bernard, indeed, indeed it cannot be as you have proposed.\"", "버나드, 정말이지, 정말이지 네가 말한 대로는 될 수 없어.”"]], [["She spoke in a low voice, and in a tone that had in it something of almost imploring humility; but, nevertheless, it conveyed to her cousin an assurance that she was in earnest; an assurance also that that earnest would not readily be changed.", "벨은 낮은 목소리로, 거의 애원하는 듯한 겸손함까지 어린 어조로 말했다. 그러나 그럼에도 그 목소리는 벨이 진심이라는 것을, 그리고 그 진심이 쉽사리 바뀌지 않으리라는 것을 사촌에게 일러 주었다."], ["Was she not a Dale? And when did a Dale change his mind?", "벨도 데일 집안사람이 아니던가?"], ["For a while he sat silent by her; and she too, having declared her intention, refrained from further words.", "그리고 데일 집안사람이 언제 제 마음을 바꾼 적이 있던가? 한동안 그는 벨 곁에 말없이 앉아 있었다."], ["For some minutes they thus remained, looking down into the ha-ha.", "벨 또한 제 뜻을 밝혀 놓은 터라 더 말을 보태지 않았다."], ["She still kept her old position, holding her hands clasped together over her knees; but he was now lying on his side, supporting his head upon his arm, with his face indeed turned towards her, but with his eyes fixed upon the grass.", "그렇게 몇 분 동안 두 사람은 하하 도랑 아래를 내려다보며 그대로 있었다. 벨은 여전히 아까 그대로 두 손을 무릎 위에서 맞잡은 자세를 지키고 있었으나, 그는 이제 옆으로 누워 팔을 괴어 머리를 받치고 있었다."], ["During this time, however, he was not idle.", "얼굴은 벨 쪽으로 돌린 채였지만 눈은 풀밭에 못 박혀 있었다."], ["His cousin's answer, though it had grieved him, had not come upon him as a blow stunning him for a moment, and rendering him unfit for instant thought.", "그러나 그 동안에도 그는 놀고 있지 않았다. 사촌의 대답은 그를 슬프게 하기는 했으나, 한순간 그를 멍하게 만들어 당장 아무 생각도 못 하게 하는 그런 타격으로 닥쳐오지는 않았다."], ["He was grieved, more grieved than he had thought he would have been.", "그는 슬펐다. 자기가 슬퍼하리라고 생각했던 것보다 더 슬펐다."], ["The thing that he had wanted moderately, he now wanted the more in that it was denied to him.", "어지간히 갖고 싶었던 것이 이제 거절당하고 나니 그만큼 더 갖고 싶어졌던 것이다."], ["But he was able to perceive the exact truth of his position, and to calculate what might be his chances if he went on with his suit, and what his advantage if he at once abandoned it.", "그러나 그는 제가 놓인 자리의 진상을 정확히 헤아릴 수 있었고, 구애를 밀고 나갈 경우의 승산이 얼마인지, 지금 당장 그만둘 경우의 이득이 무엇인지를 따져 볼 수도 있었다."]], [["\"I do not wish to press you unfairly, Bell; but may I ask if any other preference--\"", "“부당하게 너를 몰아붙일 생각은 없어, 벨. 그래도 물어봐도 될까, 혹시 마음이 가 있는 다른 사람이—”"]], [["\"There is no other preference,\"", "“마음이 가 있는 다른 사람은 없어.”"], ["she answered.", "벨이 대답했다."], ["And then again they were silent for a minute or two.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 다시 한두 분 동안 말이 없었다."]], [["\"My uncle will be much grieved at this,\"", "“우리 삼촌이 이 일로 몹시 상심하실 거야.”"], ["he said at last.", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["\"If that be all,\"", "“그것뿐이라면.”"], ["said Bell, \"I do not think that we need either of us trouble ourselves.", "벨이 말했다. “우리 둘 다 마음 쓸 일은 아니라고 생각해."], ["He can have no right to dispose of our hearts.\"", "삼촌이라고 우리 마음을 마음대로 처분할 권리가 있는 건 아니잖니.”"]], [["\"I understand the taunt, Bell.\"", "“무엇을 빈정대는 건지 알겠어, 벨.”"]], [["\"Dear Bernard, there was no taunt.", "“버나드, 빈정댄 게 아니야."], ["I intended none.\"", "그럴 뜻은 없었어.”"]], [["\"I need not speak of my own grief.", "“내 슬픔에 대해서는 말할 필요도 없겠지."], ["You cannot but know how deep it must be.", "그게 얼마나 깊을지 너도 모를 리 없을 테니까."], ["Why should I have submitted myself to this mortification had not my heart been concerned?", "내 마음이 걸려 있지 않았다면 무엇 하러 이런 창피를 자청했겠니?"], ["But that I will bear, if I must bear it--\"", "하지만 그건 견뎌야 한다면 견딜게—”"], ["And then he paused, looking up at her.", "그러고 나서 그는 말을 멈추고 벨을 올려다보았다."]], [["\"It will soon pass away,\"", "“곧 지나갈 거야.”"], ["she said.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I will accept it at any rate without complaint.", "“어쨌든 나는 군말 없이 받아들이겠어."], ["But as to my uncle's feelings, it is open to me to speak, and to you, I should think, to listen without indifference.", "하지만 삼촌의 심정에 대해서라면 나도 말할 수 있고, 너도 무심하게 흘려듣지는 않으리라고 생각해."], ["He has been kind to us both, and loves us two above any other living beings.", "삼촌은 우리 둘 모두에게 잘해 주셨고, 살아 있는 어느 누구보다도 우리 둘을 사랑하셔."], ["It's not surprising that he should wish to see us married, and it will not be surprising if your refusal should be a great blow to him.\"", "우리가 부부가 되는 걸 보고 싶어 하시는 것도 이상한 일이 아니고, 네 거절이 삼촌께 큰 타격이 된다 해도 이상한 일이 아닐 거야.”"]], [["\"I shall be sorry--very sorry.\"", "“나도 안타까울 거야. 아주 안타깝지.”"]], [["\"I also shall be sorry.", "“나도 안타깝겠지."], ["I am now speaking of him.", "지금은 삼촌 이야기를 하는 거야."], ["He has set his heart upon it; and as he has but few wishes, few desires, so is he the more constant in those which he expresses.", "삼촌은 이 일에 마음을 붙여 두셨어. 그리고 바라는 것도 원하는 것도 몇 안 되는 분이니만큼, 한번 입 밖에 낸 바람에는 그만큼 더 한결같으신 분이지."], ["When he knows this, I fear that we shall find him very stern.\"", "이 일을 아시게 되면 삼촌이 아주 매몰차게 나오실까 봐 걱정이야.”"]], [["\"Then he will be unjust.\"", "“그렇다면 그건 부당한 일이지.”"]], [["\"No; he will not be unjust.", "“아니, 삼촌은 부당하지 않으실 거야."], ["He is always a just man.", "그분은 언제나 공정한 분이니까."], ["But he will be unhappy, and will, I fear, make others unhappy.", "하지만 불행해하실 테고, 그러다 남까지 불행하게 만드실까 봐 나는 겁이 나."], ["Dear Bell, may not this thing remain for a while unsettled?", "벨, 이 일을 얼마 동안만 매듭짓지 않은 채로 두면 안 될까?"], ["You will not find that I take advantage of your goodness.", "내가 네 마음씨 좋은 것을 이용해 먹는 일은 없을 거야."], ["I will not intrude it on you again,--say for a fortnight,--or till Crosbie shall be gone.\"", "다시는 이 일로 너를 성가시게 하지 않을게. 이를테면 보름 동안이라든가— 아니면 크로즈비가 떠날 때까지라든가.”"]], [["\"No, no, no,\"", "“안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Why are you so eager in your noes?", "“어째서 그렇게 기를 쓰고 안 된다고만 하니?"], ["There can be no danger in such delay.", "그렇게 미룬다고 위험할 것도 없잖아."], ["I will not press you,--and you can let my uncle think that you have at least taken time for consideration.\"", "너를 몰아붙이지 않을게. 그리고 너도 적어도 생각할 시간은 가져 보았다고 삼촌이 여기시게 할 수 있고.”"]], [["\"There are things as to which one is bound to answer at once.", "“당장 대답해야만 하는 일도 있는 법이야."], ["If I doubted myself, I would let you persuade me.", "내가 나를 의심스러워한다면 네 설득에 넘어가 주기라도 하겠지."], ["But I do not doubt myself, and I should be wrong to keep you in suspense.", "하지만 나는 나를 의심하지 않아. 그러니 너를 애태우게 두는 건 잘못이야."], ["Dear, dearest Bernard, it cannot be; and as it cannot be, you, as my brother, would bid me say so clearly.", "버나드, 정말 아끼는 버나드, 그럴 수는 없어. 그리고 그럴 수 없는 이상, 네가 내 오라비라면 나더러 그렇게 분명히 말하라고 할 거야."], ["It cannot be.\"", "그럴 수는 없어.”"]], [["As she made this last assurance, they heard the steps of Lily and her lover close to them, and they both felt that it would be well that their intercourse should thus be brought to a close.", "벨이 이 마지막 다짐을 하는 순간, 두 사람은 릴리와 그 연인의 발소리가 가까이 다가오는 것을 들었다. 그리고 둘 다 이쯤에서 이야기가 끝나는 편이 좋겠다고 느꼈다."], ["Neither had known how to get up and leave the place, and yet each had felt that nothing further could then be said.", "어느 쪽도 자리에서 일어나 그곳을 떠날 방도를 찾지 못하고 있었으면서도, 저마다 이제 더 할 말이 없다고 느끼던 참이었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Did you ever see anything so sweet and affectionate and romantic?\"", "“이렇게 다정하고 애틋하고 낭만적인 광경을 본 적 있어?”"], ["said Lily, standing over them and looking at them.", "두 사람 위에 서서 그들을 내려다보며 릴리가 말했다."], ["\"And all the while we have been so practical and worldly.", "“그동안 우리는 어찌나 실속만 차리고 속물스러웠는지."], ["Do you know, Bell, that Adolphus seems to think we can't very well keep pigs in London.", "언니, 그거 알아? 어돌퍼스는 런던에서는 도무지 돼지를 칠 수가 없다고 생각하는 모양이야."], ["It makes me so unhappy.\"", "그래서 내가 어찌나 서운한지.”"]], [["\"It does seem a pity,\"", "“딱하기는 하지요.”"], ["said Crosbie, \"for Lily seems to know all about pigs.\"", "크로즈비가 말했다. “릴리는 돼지에 대해서라면 모르는 게 없는 것 같으니까요.”"]], [["\"Of course I do.", "“당연하지요."], ["I haven't lived in the country all my life for nothing.", "평생을 시골에서 산 게 괜히 그런 줄 아세요."], ["Oh, Bernard, I should so like to see you rolled down into the bottom of the ha-ha.", "아, 버나드, 나는 네가 저 하하 도랑 바닥으로 데굴데굴 굴러떨어지는 걸 정말이지 꼭 한번 보고 싶어."], ["Just remain there, and we'll do it between us.\"", "거기 그대로 있기만 해. 우리 둘이서 힘을 합쳐 밀어 줄 테니까.”"]], [["Whereupon Bernard got up, as did Bell also, and they all went in to tea.", "그리하여 버나드가 일어섰고, 벨도 따라 일어섰으며, 그들은 모두 차를 마시러 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX. MRS. DALE'S LITTLE PARTY.", "# 제9장. 데일 부인의 작은 잔치"]], [["The next day was the day of the party.", "이튿날이 잔치가 열리는 날이었다. 그 저녁에 벨과 사촌 사이에는 더 이상 한마디도 오가지 않았다."], ["Not a word more was said on that evening between Bell and her cousin, at least, not a word more of any peculiar note; and when Crosbie suggested to his friend on the following morning that they should both step down and see how the preparations were getting on at the Small House, Bernard declined.", "적어도 특별히 눈여겨볼 만한 말은 한마디도 없었다. 그리고 이튿날 아침에 크로즈비가 벗에게 둘이 함께 작은 집으로 내려가 준비가 어떻게 되어 가는지 보자고 했을 때, 버나드는 사양했다."]], [["\"You forget, my dear fellow, that I'm not in love as you are,\"", "“이 친구야, 나는 자네처럼 사랑에 빠진 사람이 아니라는 걸 잊었나 보군.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"But I thought you were,\"", "“나는 자네도 그런 줄 알았는데.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"No; not at all as you are.", "“아니야. 적어도 자네처럼은 아닐세."], ["You are an accepted lover, and will be allowed to do anything,--whip the creams, and tune the piano, if you know how.", "자네는 승낙을 받은 구혼자이니 무엇이든 해도 되지. 크림을 젓는 일이든, 할 줄만 안다면 피아노 조율이든."], ["I'm only a half sort of lover, meditating a mariage de convenance to oblige an uncle, and by no means required by the terms of my agreement to undergo a very rigid amount of drill.", "나는 절반짜리 구혼자에 지나지 않아. 삼촌 뜻을 받들어 정략결혼이나 궁리하는 처지이고, 우리가 맺은 약정 어디에도 그렇게까지 혹독한 훈련을 받으라는 조항은 없거든."], ["Your position is just the reverse.\"", "자네 처지는 그 정반대일세.”"], ["In saying all which Captain Dale was no doubt very false; but if falseness can be forgiven to a man in any position, it may be forgiven in that which he then filled.", "이런 말을 하면서 데일 대위는 분명 몹시도 거짓되었다. 그러나 어떤 처지에 놓인 사람에게든 거짓이 용서될 수 있다면, 그 순간 그가 놓여 있던 처지에서는 용서받을 만도 했다."], ["So Crosbie went down to the Small House alone.", "그리하여 크로즈비는 혼자 작은 집으로 내려갔다."]], [["\"Dale wouldn't come,\"", "“데일이 오지 않겠다는군요.”"], ["said he, speaking to the three ladies together, \"I suppose he's keeping himself up for the dance on the lawn.\"", "그가 세 부인에게 한꺼번에 말했다. “잔디밭에서 출 춤을 위해 몸을 아껴 두는 모양입니다.”"]], [["\"I hope he will be here in the evening,\"", "“저녁에는 와 주었으면 좋겠군요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["But Bell said never a word.", "그러나 벨은 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["She had determined, that under the existing circumstances, it would be only fair to her cousin that his offer and her answer to it should be kept secret.", "그녀는 지금 형편에서 사촌의 청혼과 그에 대한 자신의 대답을 비밀로 해 두는 것이 사촌에 대한 도리라고 마음을 정해 두었다."], ["She knew why Bernard did not come across from the Great House with his friend, but she said nothing of her knowledge.", "버나드가 왜 벗과 함께 큰 집에서 건너오지 않았는지 그녀는 알고 있었지만, 아는 바에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Lily looked at her, but looked without speaking; and as for Mrs.", "릴리가 그녀를 쳐다보았으나 말없이 쳐다보기만 했다."], ["Dale, she took no notice of the circumstance.", "데일 부인으로 말하자면 그 일을 전혀 마음에 두지 않았다."], ["Thus they passed the afternoon together without further mention of Bernard Dale; and it may be said, at any rate of Lily and Crosbie, that his presence was not missed.", "그리하여 그들은 버나드 데일의 이야기를 더 꺼내지 않은 채 오후를 함께 보냈다. 그리고 적어도 릴리와 크로즈비에 관한 한, 그가 없는 것이 아쉽지는 않았다고 말해 두어도 좋겠다."]], [["Mrs. Eames, with her son and daughter, were the first to come.", "임스 부인이 아들딸을 데리고 제일 먼저 왔다."], ["\"It is so nice of you to come early,\"", "“일찍 와 주시다니 정말 좋아요.”"], ["said Lily, trying on the spur of the moment to say something which should sound pleasant and happy, but in truth using that form of welcome which to my ears sounds always the most ungracious.", "릴리는 그 자리에서 문득 무언가 상냥하고 즐겁게 들릴 말을 하려다가 그렇게 말했으나, 사실은 내 귀에 늘 가장 무례하게 들리는 그 인사법을 쓴 것이었다."], ["\"Ten minutes before the time named; and, of course, you must have understood that I meant thirty minutes after it!\"", "“말한 시각보다 십 분이나 일찍 오셨네요. 물론 제가 말한 건 그보다 삼십 분 뒤라는 뜻인 줄 아셨어야지요!”"], ["That is my interpretation of the words when I am thanked for coming early.", "일찍 왔다고 고맙다는 인사를 들을 때 내가 그 말을 새기는 방식이 그러하다."], ["But Mrs. Eames was a kind, patient, unexacting woman, who took all civil words as meaning civility.", "그러나 임스 부인은 친절하고 참을성 있고 까다롭지 않은 여인이어서, 예의 바른 말은 모두 예의로 받아들였다."], ["And, indeed, Lily had meant nothing else.", "게다가 사실 릴리에게는 다른 뜻이 조금도 없었다."]], [["\"Yes; we did come early,\" said Mrs.", "“그래요, 우리가 일찍 오기는 했지요.”"], ["Eames, \"because Mary thought she would like to go up into the girls'", "임스 부인이 말했다."], ["room and just settle her hair, you know.\"", "“메리가 아가씨들 방에 올라가서 머리를 좀 매만지고 싶다고 해서요.”"]], [["\"So she shall,\"", "“그러시라고 해야지요.”"], ["said Lily, who had taken Mary by the hand.", "메리의 손을 잡으며 릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"And we knew we shouldn't be in the way.", "“그리고 우리가 방해가 되지 않으리라는 것도 알았고요."], ["Johnny can go out into the garden if there's anything left to be done.\"", "아직 할 일이 남았으면 조니는 정원에 나가 있으면 되니까요.”"]], [["\"He shan't be banished unless he likes it,\"", "“본인이 좋다지 않는 한 내쫓지는 않을 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"If he finds us women too much for his unaided strength--\"", "“혼자 힘으로 감당하기에 여자들이 너무 많다 싶으면 몰라도……”"]], [["John Eames muttered something about being very well as he was, and then got himself into an arm-chair.", "존 임스는 이대로도 아주 좋다는 뜻으로 무언가 우물거리고는 안락의자에 몸을 파묻었다."], ["He had shaken hands with Lily, trying as he did so to pronounce articulately a little speech which he had prepared for the occasion.", "그는 릴리와 악수를 하면서, 이 자리를 위해 준비해 둔 짧은 인사말을 또박또박 발음해 보려고 애를 썼다."], ["\"I have to congratulate you, Lily, and I hope with all my heart that you will be happy.\"", "“축하합니다, 릴리. 진심으로 행복하시기를 바랍니다.”"], ["The words were simple enough, and were not ill-chosen, but the poor young man never got them spoken. The word \"congratulate\"", "말은 아주 단순했고 고른 낱말도 나쁘지 않았지만, 가엾은 젊은이는 끝내 그 말을 입 밖에 내지 못했다."], ["did reach Lily's ears, and she understood it all;--both the kindness of the intended speech and the reason why it could not be spoken.", "“축하”라는 낱말만은 릴리의 귀에 닿았고, 그녀는 모든 것을 알아들었다. 하려 했던 그 인사의 다정함도, 그것을 끝내 말할 수 없었던 까닭도."]], [["\"Thank you, John,\"", "“고마워요, 존.”"], ["she said; \"I hope I shall see so much of you in London.", "그녀가 말했다. “런던에서 자주 뵈었으면 좋겠어요."], ["It will be so nice to have an old Guestwick friend near me.\"", "게스트윅의 오랜 친구가 가까이 있으면 참 좋을 거예요.”"], ["She had her own voice, and the pulses of her heart better under command than had he; but she also felt that the occasion was trying to her.", "그녀는 그보다 목소리도, 심장의 박동도 더 잘 다스리고 있었다. 그러나 그녀 역시 이 자리가 견디기 힘들다고 느꼈다."], ["The man had loved her honestly and truly,--still did love her, paying her the great homage of bitter grief in that he had lost her.", "그 남자는 그녀를 정직하고 진실하게 사랑했으며, 지금도 사랑하고 있었다. 그녀를 잃었다는 쓰디쓴 슬픔으로 그녀에게 큰 경의를 바치고 있었다."], ["Where is the girl who will not sympathize with such love and such grief, if it be shown only because it cannot be concealed, and be declared against the will of him who declares it?", "그러한 사랑과 그러한 슬픔이 오직 감출 수 없어서 드러나고, 드러내는 이의 뜻을 거슬러 스스로 밝혀지는 것이라면, 어느 처녀가 거기에 마음이 움직이지 않겠는가?"]], [["Then came in old Mrs. Hearn, whose cottage was not distant two minutes'", "이윽고 늙은 헌 부인이 들어왔다."], ["walk from the Small House. She always called Mrs.", "그녀의 오두막은 작은 집에서 걸어서 이 분도 걸리지 않는 곳에 있었다."], ["Dale \"my dear,\" and petted the girls as though they had been children.", "그녀는 늘 데일 부인을 “그대”라 불렀고, 아가씨들은 아직도 어린애인 양 귀여워했다."], ["When told of Lily's marriage, she had thrown up her hands with surprise, for she had still left in some corner of her drawers remnants of sugar-plums which she had bought for Lily.", "릴리가 결혼한다는 말을 들었을 때 그녀는 놀라 두 손을 번쩍 들었다. 릴리에게 주려고 사 둔 사탕이 아직도 서랍 어느 구석엔가 남아 있었기 때문이다."], ["\"A London man is he?", "“런던 사람이라고요?"], ["Well, well.", "저런, 저런."], ["I wish he lived in the country.", "시골에 사는 사람이었으면 좋았을 것을."], ["Eight hundred a year, my dear?\"", "연 팔백 파운드라고요, 그대?”"], ["she had said to Mrs. Dale.", "그녀가 데일 부인에게 말했다."], ["\"That sounds nice down here, because we are all so poor.", "“여기서야 듣기 좋은 소리지요, 우리가 다들 가난하니까."], ["But I suppose eight hundred a year isn't very much up in London?\"", "하지만 런던에서 연 팔백 파운드면 그리 큰돈은 아니지 싶은데?”"]], [["\"The squire's coming, I suppose, isn't he?\" said Mrs.", "“지주 어른도 오시겠지요, 안 그래요?”"], ["Hearn, as she seated herself on the sofa close to Mrs. Dale.", "헌 부인이 데일 부인 곁 소파에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, he'll be here by-and-by; unless he changes his mind, you know.", "“네, 곧 오실 거예요. 마음이 바뀌지만 않는다면요."], ["He doesn't stand on ceremony with me.\"", "저한테는 격식을 차리지 않는 분이니까요.”"]], [["\"He change his mind!", "“그 양반이 마음을 바꿔요!"], ["When did you ever know Christopher Dale change his mind?\"", "크리스토퍼 데일이 마음 바꾸는 걸 언제 한 번이라도 본 적이 있어요?”"]], [["\"He is pretty constant, Mrs. Hearn.\"", "“한결같으신 분이지요, 헌 부인.”"]], [["\"If he promised to give a man a penny, he'd give it.", "“누구한테 일 페니를 주기로 했으면 반드시 줄 거예요."], ["But if he promised to take away a pound, he'd take it, though it cost him years to get it.", "하지만 일 파운드를 빼앗기로 했으면 몇 해가 걸리더라도 기어이 빼앗고야 말지요."], ["He's going to turn me out of my cottage, he says.\"", "나를 오두막에서 내쫓겠다고 하더라니까요.”"]], [["\"Nonsense, Mrs. Hearn!\"", "“별말씀을 다 하시네요, 헌 부인!”"]], [["\"Jolliffe came and told me\"--Jolliffe, I should explain, was the bailiff,--\"that if I didn't like it as it was, I might leave it, and that the squire could get double the rent for it.", "“졸리프가 와서 말하기를”—졸리프란 영지 관리인이라는 것을 밝혀 두어야겠다—“지금 있는 그대로가 마음에 들지 않으면 나가도 좋다, 지주 어른은 그 집으로 두 배 세를 받을 수 있다고 하더군요."], ["Now all I asked was that he should do a little painting in the kitchen; and the wood is all as black as his hat.\"", "내가 부탁한 거라고는 부엌에 칠이나 좀 해 달라는 것뿐이었는데. 나무가 온통 그 양반 모자처럼 새까맣거든요.”"]], [["\"I thought it was understood you were to paint inside.\"", "“안쪽 칠은 부인이 하시기로 되어 있는 줄 알았는데요.”"]], [["\"How can I do it, my dear, with a hundred and forty pounds for everything?", "“그대, 모든 걸 백사십 파운드로 꾸리면서 내가 어떻게 그걸 해요?"], ["I must live, you know!", "나도 살아야지요!"], ["And he that has workmen about him every day of the year!", "게다가 그 양반은 일 년 내내 날마다 일꾼들을 데리고 있는 사람이잖아요!"], ["And was that a message to send to me, who have lived in the parish for fifty years?", "그리고 오십 년을 이 교구에서 살아온 나한테 그런 전갈을 보낼 일인가요?"], ["Here he is.\" And Mrs.", "저기 오시는군요.”"], ["Hearn majestically raised herself from her seat as the squire entered the room.", "그러고서 헌 부인은 지주가 방으로 들어서자 자리에서 위엄 있게 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["With him entered Mr. and Mrs.", "그와 함께 목사관에서 보이스 씨 부부가, 아직 다 자라지 못한 도련님 딕 보이스와 열넷, 열다섯 살 난 보이스네 딸 둘을 데리고 들어왔다."], ["Boyce, from the parsonage, with Dick Boyce, the ungrown gentleman, and two girl Boyces, who were fourteen and fifteen years of age. Mrs.", "데일 부인은 이런 자리에서 흔히 그러하듯 넉넉한 인심을 부려, 제인이며 찰스며 플로렌스며 베시는 어째서 오지 않았느냐고 나무라듯 물었다."], ["Dale, with the amount of good-nature usual on such occasions, asked reproachfully why Jane, and Charles, and Florence, and Bessy, did not come,--Boyce being a man who had his quiver full of them,--and Mrs.", "보이스는 화살통을 그런 것들로 가득 채운 사람이었던 것이다."], ["Boyce, giving the usual answer, declared that she already felt that they had come as an avalanche.", "그러자 보이스 부인은 늘 하는 대답으로, 자기들이 이미 눈사태처럼 몰려온 것 같다고 했다."]], [["\"But where are the--the--the young men?\"", "“그런데 그, 그, 그 젊은 분들은 어디 계신가요?”"], ["asked Lily, assuming a look of mock astonishment.", "릴리가 짐짓 놀란 얼굴을 지으며 물었다."]], [["\"They'll be across in two or three hours' time,\"", "“두세 시간쯤 뒤에는 건너올 게다.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"They both dressed for dinner, and, as I thought, made themselves very smart; but for such a grand occasion as this they thought a second dressing necessary.", "“저녁 먹으려고 옷을 갈아입고 내가 보기엔 아주 말쑥해졌는데, 이런 대단한 자리에는 한 번 더 갈아입어야 한다는구나. 안녕하시오, 헌 부인?"], ["How do you do, Mrs.", "몸은 편안하시겠지요."], ["Hearn? I hope you are quite well.", "류머티즘은 이제 없으시고, 응?”"], ["No rheumatism left, eh?\"", "지주는 이 말을 헌 부인의 귀에 대고 아주 큰 소리로 했다."], ["This the squire said very loud into Mrs. Hearn's ear.", "헌 부인은 어쩌면 귀가 조금 어두웠을지도 모른다."], ["Mrs. Hearn was perhaps a little hard of hearing; but it was very little, and she hated to be thought deaf.", "그러나 아주 조금이었고, 그녀는 남들이 자기를 귀머거리로 여기는 것을 몹시 싫어했다."], ["She did not, moreover, like to be thought rheumatic.", "게다가 류머티즘 환자로 여겨지는 것도 좋아하지 않았다."], ["This the squire knew, and therefore his mode of address was not good-natured.", "이것을 지주는 알고 있었으니, 그의 인사법은 그리 인정 있는 것이 아니었다."]], [["\"You needn't make me jump so, Mr. Dale.", "“사람을 그렇게 놀라게 할 것 없어요, 데일 씨."], ["I'm pretty well now, thank ye.", "지금은 그런대로 괜찮아요, 고마워요."], ["I did have a twinge in the spring,--that cottage is so badly built for draughts!", "봄에 한 번 쑤시기는 했지요. 그 오두막이 웃풍이 어찌나 심하게 지어졌는지!"], ["'I wonder you can live in it,' my sister said to me the last time she was over.", "'거기서 어떻게 사는지 모르겠다'고 우리 언니가 지난번 다녀갈 때 그럽디다."], ["I suppose I should be better off over with her at Hamersham, only one doesn't like to move, you know, after living fifty years in one parish.\"", "해머샴으로 언니한테 가 있는 편이 나을지도 모르지요. 다만 한 교구에서 오십 년을 살고 나면 옮기기가 싫어지는 법이라서요.”"]], [["\"You mustn't think of going away from us,\"", "“우리 곁을 떠나실 생각은 마세요.” 보이스 부인이 말했다."], ["Mrs. Boyce said, speaking by no means loud, but slowly and plainly, hoping thereby to flatter the old woman.", "결코 큰 소리는 아니었지만 천천히 또박또박 말했으니, 그렇게 해서 노부인의 비위를 맞추려는 속셈이었다."], ["But the old woman understood it all.", "그러나 노부인은 그 속을 다 알아차렸다."], ["\"She's a sly creature, is Mrs. Boyce,\"", "“보이스 부인은 참 능청스러운 사람이에요.”"], ["Mrs. Hearn said to Mrs.", "저녁이 다 가기 전에 헌 부인은 데일 부인에게 그렇게 말했다."], ["Dale, before the evening was out. There are some old people whom it is very hard to flatter, and with whom it is, nevertheless, almost impossible to live unless you do flatter them.", "세상에는 비위를 맞추기가 몹시 어려우면서도 비위를 맞추지 않고는 도무지 함께 지낼 수 없는 노인들이 있는 법이다."]], [["At last the two heroes came in across the lawn at the drawing-room window; and Lily, as they entered, dropped a low curtsey before them, gently swelling down upon the ground with her light muslin dress, till she looked like some wondrous flower that had bloomed upon the carpet, and putting her two hands, with the backs of her fingers pressed together, on the buckle of her girdle, she said, \"We are waiting upon your honours'", "마침내 두 영웅이 잔디밭을 가로질러 응접실 창으로 들어왔다. 그들이 들어서자 릴리는 그들 앞에서 깊이 무릎을 굽혀 절을 했으니, 얇은 모슬린 옷자락이 바닥으로 사뿐히 부풀어 내려 마치 양탄자 위에 피어난 신기한 꽃송이처럼 보일 지경이었다."], ["kind grace, and feel how much we owe to you for favouring our poor abode.\"", "그러고는 두 손을 손등끼리 맞붙여 허리띠 죔쇠 위에 얹고서 말했다."], ["And then she gently rose up again, smiling, oh, so sweetly, on the man she loved, and the puffings and swellings went out of her muslin.", "“저희는 나리들의 은혜로운 왕림을 기다리고 있었사오며, 누추한 처소를 찾아 주신 은덕이 얼마나 큰지 잘 알고 있사옵니다.” 그런 다음 그녀는 사랑하는 남자를 향해 아, 그토록 달콤하게 미소 지으며 사뿐히 몸을 일으켰고, 모슬린의 부풀음과 물결도 함께 가라앉았다."]], [["I think there is nothing in the world so pretty as the conscious little tricks of love played off by a girl towards the man she loves, when she has made up her mind boldly that all the world may know that she has given herself away to him.", "한 처녀가 사랑하는 남자에게 부리는, 저 스스로도 의식하고 있는 자잘한 사랑의 재롱만큼 세상에 예쁜 것은 없다고 나는 생각한다. 자기를 그에게 내주었다는 것을 온 세상이 알아도 좋다고 당차게 마음을 정한 처녀라면 더욱 그렇다."]], [["I am not sure that Crosbie liked it all as much as he should have done.", "크로즈비가 그 모든 것을 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 좋아했는지는 잘 모르겠다."], ["The bold assurance of her love when they two were alone together he did like.", "둘만 있을 때 그녀가 사랑을 거침없이 내보이는 것은 그도 좋아했다."], ["What man does not like such assurances on such occasions?", "그런 자리에서 그런 확신을 좋아하지 않을 남자가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["But perhaps he would have been better pleased had Lily shown more reticence,--been more secret, as it were, as to her feelings, when others were around them.", "그러나 남들이 곁에 있을 때만은 릴리가 좀 더 말을 아꼈으면, 이를테면 자기 감정을 좀 더 감추어 주었으면 그는 더 흡족했을지도 모른다."], ["It was not that he accused her in his thoughts of any want of delicacy.", "그가 마음속으로 그녀에게 조심성이 없다고 탓했다는 말은 아니다."], ["He read her character too well;--was, if not quite aright in his reading of it, at least too nearly so to admit of his making against her any such accusation as that.", "그는 그녀의 성품을 너무나 잘 읽고 있었다. 설령 그 읽음이 아주 정확하지는 않았다 해도, 적어도 그런 종류의 탓을 그녀에게 돌릴 만큼 어긋나 있지는 않았다."], ["It was the calf-like feeling that was disagreeable to him.", "그에게 거슬린 것은 송아지 같은 느낌이었다."], ["He did not like to be presented, even to the world of Allington, as a victim caught for the sacrifice, and bound with ribbon for the altar.", "그는 비록 앨링턴 세상에 지나지 않는다 해도, 제물로 붙잡혀 제단에 바치려고 리본으로 묶인 짐승처럼 남들 앞에 내보여지는 것이 싫었다."], ["And then there lurked behind it all a feeling that it might be safer that the thing should not be so openly manifested before all the world.", "그리고 그 모든 것 밑바닥에는, 이 일이 온 세상 앞에 그토록 드러나게 밝혀지지 않는 편이 더 안전하리라는 느낌이 도사리고 있었다. 물론 그가 릴리 데일과 약혼했다는 것은 누구나 알고 있었다."], ["Of course, everybody knew that he was engaged to Lily Dale; nor had he, as he said to himself, perhaps too frequently, the slightest idea of breaking from that engagement.", "또 그 자신도, 어쩌면 너무 자주, 스스로에게 되뇌었듯이 그 약혼을 깨뜨릴 생각은 손톱만큼도 없었다. 그러나 혼인은 미루어질 수도 있는 일이었다."], ["But then the marriage might possibly be delayed.", "그는 그 문제를 아직 릴리와 의논하지 않았다."], ["He had not discussed that matter yet with Lily, having, indeed, at the first moment of his gratified love, created some little difficulty for himself by pressing for an early day.", "사실 사랑이 이루어진 첫 순간의 기쁨에 겨워 날을 이르게 잡자고 조른 탓에 스스로 조그마한 어려움을 만들어 놓은 터였다. “저는 당신께 아무것도 마다하지 않을 거예요.”"], ["\"I will refuse you nothing,\"", "그때 그녀는 그에게 말했었다."], ["she had said to him; \"but do not make it too soon.\"", "“하지만 너무 이르게는 하지 말아요.”"], ["He saw, therefore, before him some little embarrassment, and was inclined to wish that Lily would abstain from that manner which seemed to declare to all the world that she was about to be married immediately.", "그러니 그는 앞날에 얼마간의 곤란이 놓여 있음을 보았고, 릴리가 당장이라도 시집을 갈 것처럼 온 세상에 알리는 듯한 저 태도를 삼가 주었으면 하고 바라게 되었다."], ["\"I must speak to her to-morrow,\"", "“내일은 저 사람한테 말해 두어야겠다.”"], ["he said to himself, as he accepted her salute with a mock gravity equal to her own.", "그는 그녀에 못지않게 짐짓 근엄한 태도로 그녀의 인사를 받으며 속으로 그렇게 되뇌었다."]], [["Poor Lily!", "가엾은 릴리!"], ["How little she understood as yet what was passing through his mind.", "그의 마음속을 지나가는 것을 그녀는 아직 얼마나 모르고 있었던가."], ["Had she known his wish she would have wrapped up her love carefully in a napkin, so that no one should have seen it,--no one but he, when he might choose to have the treasure uncovered for his sight.", "그의 바람을 알았더라면 그녀는 자기 사랑을 냅킨에 고이 싸 두어, 아무도 그것을 보지 못하게 했으리라. 오직 그만이, 그가 그 보물을 드러내어 보고 싶을 때에만 보도록 했으리라."], ["And it was all for his sake that she had been thus open in her ways.", "그리고 그녀가 그토록 숨김없이 굴었던 것은 모두 그를 위해서였다."], ["She had seen girls who were half ashamed of their love; but she would never be ashamed of hers or of him.", "그녀는 제 사랑을 반쯤 부끄러워하는 처녀들을 본 적이 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 결코 제 사랑도, 그도 부끄러워하지 않을 작정이었다."], ["She had given herself to him; and now all the world might know it, if all the world cared for such knowledge.", "그녀는 자기를 그에게 주었다. 그러니 온 세상이 그런 것을 알고 싶어 한다면 온 세상이 알아도 좋았다."], ["Why should she be ashamed of that which, to her thinking, was so great an honour to her?", "그녀가 보기에 그토록 큰 명예인 일을 어찌 부끄러워하겠는가?"], ["She had heard of girls who would not speak of their love, arguing to themselves cannily that there may be many a slip between the cup and the lip.", "그녀는 제 사랑을 입에 올리지 않는 처녀들 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다. 잔과 입술 사이에도 흘릴 일이 얼마든지 있다고 야무지게 따져 두는 처녀들 말이다."], ["There could be no need of any such caution with her.", "그녀에게는 그런 조심이 필요할 까닭이 없었다."], ["There could surely be no such slip!", "그런 흘림이 있을 리 없지 않은가!"], ["Should there be such a fall,--should any such fate, either by falseness or misfortune, come upon her,--no such caution could be of service to save her.", "설령 그런 추락이 온다 해도, 거짓 때문이든 불행 때문이든 그런 운명이 그녀에게 닥친다면, 어떤 조심도 그녀를 구하는 데는 소용이 없을 터였다."], ["The cup would have been so shattered in its fall that no further piecing of its parts would be in any way possible.", "잔은 떨어지면서 산산이 부서져, 어떤 조각 맞추기로도 다시 이어 붙일 길이 없을 것이었다."], ["So much as this she did not exactly say to herself; but she felt it all, and went bravely forward,--bold in her love, and careful to hide it from none who chanced to see it.", "이만큼을 그녀가 또박또박 스스로에게 말했던 것은 아니다. 그러나 그녀는 그 모든 것을 느끼고 있었고, 사랑에 당당하게, 어쩌다 보게 되는 그 누구에게도 감추지 않으려 조심하며 씩씩하게 나아갔다."]], [["They had gone through the ceremony with the cake and teacups, and had decided that, at any rate, the first dance or two should be held upon the lawn when the last of the guests arrived.", "그들은 케이크와 찻잔의 예식을 마쳤고, 어쨌든 마지막 손님이 오면 적어도 첫 춤 한두 곡은 잔디밭에서 추기로 정해 두었다."]], [["\"Oh, Adolphus, I am so glad he has come,\"", "“어돌퍼스, 저분이 오셔서 참 기뻐요.” 릴리가 말했다."], ["said Lily.", "“부디 좋아해 주세요.”"], ["\"Do try to like him.\" Of Dr.", "새로 온 사람은 크로프츠 선생이었다."], ["Crofts, who was the new comer, she had sometimes spoken to her lover, but she had never coupled her sister's name with that of the doctor, even in speaking to him.", "그녀는 이따금 연인에게 그의 이야기를 한 적이 있었으나, 그에게 말할 때조차 언니의 이름을 그 의사의 이름과 나란히 놓은 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그럼에도 크로즈비는 어쩐 일인지 이 크로프츠라는 사람이 벨을 사랑했거나, 사랑하고 있거나, 앞으로 사랑하게 되리라는 생각을 품고 있었다."], ["Nevertheless, Crosbie had in some way conceived the idea that this Crofts either had been, or was, or was to be, in love with Bell; and as he was prepared to advocate his friend Dale's claims in that quarter, he was not particularly anxious to welcome the doctor as a thoroughly intimate friend of the family.", "그리고 그는 그 자리에서 벗 데일의 권리를 편들 작정이었으므로, 이 의사를 집안의 아주 가까운 벗으로 반길 마음이 딱히 있지는 않았다. 그는 데일의 청혼도, 벨의 거절도 아직 전혀 몰랐다."], ["He knew nothing as yet of Dale's offer, or of Bell's refusal, but he was prepared for war, if war should be necessary.", "그러나 싸움이 필요하다면 싸울 채비는 되어 있었다. 지금으로서는 지주가 그리 마음에 들지는 않았다."], ["Of the squire, at the present moment, he was not very fond; but if his destiny intended to give him a wife out of this family, he should prefer the owner of Allington and nephew of Lord De Guest as a brother-in-law to a village doctor,--as he took upon himself, in his pride, to call Dr. Crofts.", "그러나 만일 운명이 이 집안에서 자기에게 아내를 줄 작정이라면, 시골 의사보다는—그는 오만하게도 크로프츠 선생을 그렇게 불렀다—앨링턴의 주인이자 드 게스트 경의 조카를 처남으로 삼는 편이 나았다."]], [["\"It is very unfortunate,\"", "“참으로 딱한 일입니다만,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"but I never do like Paragons.\"", "“저는 완벽한 분들을 도무지 좋아해 본 적이 없어서요.”"]], [["\"But you must like this Paragon.", "“하지만 이 완벽한 분은 좋아하셔야 해요."], ["Not that he is a Paragon at all, for he smokes and hunts, and does all manner of wicked things.\"", "사실 조금도 완벽한 분이 아니지만요. 담배도 피우고 사냥도 하고 온갖 못된 짓을 다 하시거든요.”"], ["And then she went forward to welcome her friend.", "그러고서 그녀는 벗을 맞으러 앞으로 나아갔다."]], [["Dr. Crofts was a slight, spare man, about five feet nine in height, with very bright dark eyes, a broad forehead, with dark hair that almost curled, but which did not come so forward over his brow as it should have done for purposes of beauty,--with a thin well-cut nose, and a mouth that would have been perfect had the lips been a little fuller.", "크로프츠 선생은 키가 오 피트 아홉 인치쯤 되는, 가늘고 여윈 사람이었다."], ["The lower part of his face, when seen alone, had in it somewhat of sternness, which, however, was redeemed by the brightness of his eyes.", "아주 반짝이는 검은 눈에 이마가 넓었고, 검은 머리카락은 거의 곱슬거리다시피 했으나 아름다움을 위해서라면 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 이마 앞으로 내려오지는 않았다. 코는 가늘고 선이 반듯했으며, 입술이 조금만 더 도톰했더라면 입매는 나무랄 데가 없었을 것이다."], ["And yet an artist would have declared that the lower features of his face were by far the more handsome.", "얼굴의 아랫부분은 그것만 따로 보면 어딘가 엄한 데가 있었으나, 눈의 광채가 그것을 덜어 주었다. 그러면서도 화가라면 그의 얼굴에서 아랫부분이 훨씬 더 잘생겼다고 말했을 것이다."]], [["Lily went across to him and greeted him heartily, declaring how glad she was to have him there.", "릴리는 그에게 건너가 진심으로 반기며, 그가 와 주어 얼마나 기쁜지 모르겠다고 말했다."], ["\"And I must introduce you to Mr. Crosbie,\"", "“그리고 크로즈비 씨께 소개해 드려야겠어요.”"], ["she said, as though she was determined to carry her point.", "기어이 뜻을 이루고야 말겠다는 듯이 그녀가 말했다."], ["The two men shook hands with each other, coldly, without saying a word, as young men are apt to do when they are brought together in that way.", "두 남자는 한마디도 없이 서먹하게 악수를 나누었다."], ["Then they separated at once, somewhat to the disappointment of Lily.", "젊은 남자들이 그런 식으로 마주 세워지면 으레 그러하듯이."], ["Crosbie stood off by himself, both his eyes turned up towards the ceiling, and looking as though he meant to give himself airs; while Crofts got himself quickly up to the fireplace, making civil little speeches to Mrs.", "그러고는 곧바로 서로 떨어져 버렸으니, 릴리에게는 다소 실망스러운 일이었다. 크로즈비는 저만치 홀로 서서 두 눈을 천장으로 치켜뜬 채 짐짓 거드름을 피우려는 듯한 낯빛이었다."], ["Dale, Mrs. Boyce, and Mrs.", "한편 크로프츠는 얼른 벽난로 쪽으로 가서 데일 부인과 보이스 부인과 헌 부인에게 예의 바른 짧은 인사말들을 건넸다."], ["Hearn. And then at last he made his way round to Bell.", "그러고는 마침내 벨에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["\"I am so glad,\"", "“동생분의 약혼을 축하드리게 되어,”"], ["he said, \"to congratulate you on your sister's engagement.\"", "그가 말했다. “참으로 기쁩니다.”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“네.”"], ["said Bell; \"we knew that you would be glad to hear of her happiness.\"", "벨이 말했다. “그 애가 행복하다는 소식을 들으시면 기뻐하시리라는 걸 알고 있었어요.”"]], [["\"Indeed, I am glad; and thoroughly hope that she may be happy.", "“정말이지 기쁩니다. 그리고 부디 행복하시기를 진심으로 바랍니다."], ["You all like him, do you not?\"", "다들 그분을 좋아하시지요?”"]], [["\"We like him very much.\"", "“저희 모두 아주 좋아해요.”"]], [["\"And I am told that he is well off.", "“그리고 형편도 넉넉한 분이라고 들었습니다. 참으로 운이 좋은 사람이에요."], ["He is a very fortunate man,--very fortunate,--very fortunate.\"", "참으로 운이 좋아요. 참으로 운이 좋습니다.”"]], [["\"Of course we think so,\"", "“물론 저희도 그렇게 생각해요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"Not, however, because he is rich.\"", "“다만 그분이 부자라서 그런 건 아니에요.”"]], [["\"No; not because he is rich.", "“그럼요, 부자라서가 아니지요."], ["But because, being worthy of such happiness, his circumstances should enable him to marry, and to enjoy it.\"", "그런 행복을 누릴 만한 사람인데, 마침 형편이 넉넉해 결혼을 하고 그 행복을 누릴 수 있게 되었으니 그런 거지요.”"]], [["\"Yes, exactly,\"", "“네, 바로 그거예요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"That is just it.\"", "“바로 그거예요.”"], ["Then she sat down, and in sitting down put an end to the conversation.", "그러고서 그녀는 자리에 앉았고, 앉음으로써 이야기를 끝내 버렸다."], ["\"That is just it,\"", "“바로 그거예요.”"], ["she had said.", "그녀는 그렇게 말했다."], ["But as soon as the words were spoken she declared to herself that it was not so, and that Crofts was wrong.", "그러나 말이 입 밖으로 나오자마자 그녀는 그렇지 않다고, 크로프츠가 틀렸다고 속으로 되뇌었다. “우리가 그이를 사랑하는 건,”"], ["\"We love him,\"", "그녀는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["she said to herself, \"not because he is rich enough to marry without anxious thought, but because he dares to marry although he is not rich.\"", "“걱정 없이 결혼할 만큼 넉넉해서가 아니라, 넉넉하지 않은데도 감히 결혼하려 들기 때문이야.”"], ["And then she told herself that she was angry with the doctor.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 자기가 그 의사에게 화가 나 있다고 스스로 인정했다."]], [["After that Dr. Crofts got off towards the door, and stood there by himself, leaning against the wall, with the thumbs of both his hands stuck into the armholes of his waistcoat.", "그 뒤로 크로프츠 선생은 문 쪽으로 물러나 벽에 기댄 채 홀로 서서, 두 손의 엄지를 조끼 겨드랑이 구멍에 찔러 넣고 있었다."], ["People said that he was a shy man.", "사람들은 그를 수줍음이 많은 사람이라고 했다."], ["I suppose he was shy, and yet he was a man that was by no means afraid of doing anything that he had to do.", "아마 수줍음이 많기는 했을 것이다. 그러면서도 그는 해야 할 일이라면 무엇이든 조금도 두려워하지 않는 사람이었다."], ["He could speak before a multitude without being abashed, whether it was a multitude of men or of women.", "그는 여럿 앞에서, 그것이 남자들의 무리든 여자들의 무리든 주눅 들지 않고 말할 수 있었다."], ["He could be very fixed too in his own opinion, and eager, if not violent, in the prosecution of his purpose.", "또 제 의견을 아주 굳게 지킬 줄도 알았고, 뜻한 바를 밀고 나갈 때는 사납지는 않을지언정 열렬했다."], ["But he could not stand and say little words, when he had in truth nothing to say.", "그러나 정작 할 말이 없을 때 서서 자잘한 말을 늘어놓는 것만은 하지 못했다."], ["He could not keep his ground when he felt that he was not using the ground upon which he stood.", "자기가 딛고 선 자리를 제대로 쓰고 있지 않다고 느끼면 그 자리를 지켜 낼 수가 없었다."], ["He had not learned the art of assuming himself to be of importance in whatever place he might find himself.", "어느 자리에 가 있든 스스로를 중요한 사람으로 여기는 재주를 그는 배우지 못했다."], ["It was this art which Crosbie had learned, and by this art that he had flourished.", "크로즈비가 배운 것이 바로 그 재주였고, 그가 번창한 것도 바로 그 재주 덕분이었다."], ["So Crofts retired and leaned against the wall near the door; and Crosbie came forward and shone like an Apollo among all the guests.", "그리하여 크로프츠는 물러나 문 가까운 벽에 기대었고, 크로즈비는 앞으로 나와 손님들 사이에서 아폴로처럼 빛났다."], ["\"How is it that he does it?\"", "“저 사람은 대체 어떻게 저러는 걸까?”"], ["said John Eames to himself, envying the perfect happiness of the London man of fashion.", "존 임스는 유행의 도시 런던 신사의 완벽한 행복을 부러워하며 속으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["At last Lily got the dancers out upon the lawn, and then they managed to go through one quadrille.", "마침내 릴리는 춤출 사람들을 잔디밭으로 끌어내는 데 성공했고, 그들은 카드리유를 한 곡 그럭저럭 마쳤다."], ["But it was found that it did not answer.", "그러나 잘 되지 않는다는 것이 드러났다."], ["The music of the single fiddle which Crosbie had hired from Guestwick was not sufficient for the purpose; and then the grass, though it was perfect for purposes of croquet, was not pleasant to the feet for dancing.", "크로즈비가 게스트윅에서 불러온 바이올린 한 대의 연주로는 아무래도 모자랐다. 게다가 잔디는 크로케에는 더할 나위 없었지만 춤추는 발에는 편하지 않았다."]], [["\"This is very nice,\"", "“참 좋구나.”"], ["said Bernard to his cousin.", "버나드가 사촌에게 말했다."], ["\"I don't know anything that could be nicer; but perhaps--\"", "“이보다 더 좋을 게 있을까 싶어. 하지만 어쩌면……”"]], [["\"I know what you mean,\"", "“무슨 말인지 알아.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"But I shall stay here.", "“하지만 난 여기 있을래."], ["There's no touch of romance about any of you.", "너희들은 하나같이 낭만이라곤 눈곱만큼도 없어."], ["Look at the moon there at the back of the steeple.", "저기 첨탑 뒤에 뜬 달 좀 봐."], ["I don't mean to go in all night.\"", "난 밤새도록 안 들어갈 거야.”"], ["Then she walked off by one of the paths, and her lover went after her.", "그러고서 그녀는 오솔길 하나를 따라 걸어갔고, 그녀의 연인이 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["\"Don't you like the moon?\"", "“달이 좋지 않아요?”"], ["she said, as she took his arm, to which she was now so accustomed that she hardly thought of it as she took it.", "그의 팔을 잡으며 그녀가 말했다. 이제는 하도 익숙해져서 잡으면서도 잡는다는 생각조차 들지 않는 팔이었다."]], [["\"Like the moon?--well; I fancy I like the sun better.", "“달이 좋으냐고요? 글쎄요, 나는 해가 더 좋은 것 같은데요."], ["I don't quite believe in moonlight.", "나는 달빛이라는 걸 그다지 믿지 않아요."], ["I think it does best to talk about when one wants to be sentimental.\"", "감상에 젖고 싶을 때 화제로 삼기에나 가장 알맞지요.”"]], [["\"Ah; that is just what I fear.", "“아, 제가 두려워하는 게 바로 그거예요."], ["That is what I say to Bell when I tell her that her romance will fade as the roses do.", "벨한테도 그렇게 말한답니다. 언니의 낭만도 장미처럼 시들 거라고요."], ["And then I shall have to learn that prose is more serviceable than poetry, and that the mind is better than the heart, and--and that money is better than love.", "그러고 나면 저도 배우게 되겠지요. 산문이 시보다 쓸모 있고, 머리가 가슴보다 낫고, 그리고, 그리고 돈이 사랑보다 낫다는 걸요."], ["It's all coming, I know; and yet I do like the moonlight.\"", "다 오고야 말 일이라는 걸 알아요. 그런데도 저는 달빛이 좋아요.”"]], [["\"And the poetry,--and the love?\"", "“그리고 시도, 사랑도요?”"]], [["\"Yes.", "“네."], ["The poetry much, and the love more.", "시는 많이, 사랑은 더 많이요."], ["To be loved by you is sweeter even than any of my dreams,--is better than all the poetry I have read.\"", "당신에게 사랑받는 건 제가 꾸던 어떤 꿈보다도 달콤해요. 제가 읽은 어떤 시보다도 좋고요.”"]], [["\"Dearest Lily,\"", "“사랑하는 릴리.”"], ["and his unchecked arm stole round her waist.", "그러면서 거리낄 것 없는 그의 팔이 그녀의 허리를 감았다."]], [["\"It is the meaning of the moonlight, and the essence of the poetry,\"", "“그게 바로 달빛의 뜻이고 시의 알맹이예요.”"], ["continued the impassioned girl.", "열에 들뜬 처녀가 말을 이었다."], ["\"I did not know then why I liked such things, but now I know.", "“그때는 내가 왜 그런 것들을 좋아하는지 몰랐지만 이제는 알아요."], ["It was because I longed to be loved.\"", "사랑받기를 애타게 바랐기 때문이었어요.”"]], [["\"And to love.\"", "“그리고 사랑하기를요.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes.", "“아, 네."], ["I would be nothing without that.", "그것이 없다면 저는 아무것도 아니겠지요."], ["But that, you know, is your delight,--or should be.", "하지만 그건 당신의 기쁨이잖아요. 아니, 당신의 기쁨이어야 하고요."], ["The other is mine.", "다른 쪽이 제 몫이지요."], ["And yet it is a delight to love you; to know that I may love you.\"", "그래도 당신을 사랑하는 건 기쁨이에요. 당신을 사랑해도 된다는 걸 아는 것도요.”"]], [["\"You mean that this is the realization of your romance.\"", "“이것이 당신 낭만의 완성이라는 말이군요.”"]], [["\"Yes; but it must not be the end of it, Adolphus. You must like the soft twilight, and the long evenings when we shall be alone; and you must read to me the books I love, and you must not teach me to think that the world is hard, and dry, and cruel,--not yet. I tell Bell so very often; but you must not say so to me.\"", "“네. 하지만 이게 낭만의 끝이 되어서는 안 돼요, 어돌퍼스. 당신도 부드러운 땅거미를 좋아하셔야 하고, 우리 둘만 있게 될 긴 저녁도 좋아하셔야 해요. 제가 좋아하는 책들을 읽어 주셔야 하고요. 그리고 세상이란 딱딱하고 메마르고 잔인한 곳이라고 저에게 가르치시면 안 돼요. 아직은요. 저는 벨에게 그런 말을 곧잘 하지만, 당신은 저에게 그런 말을 하시면 안 돼요.”"]], [["\"It shall not be dry and cruel, if I can prevent it.\"", "“내가 막을 수 있는 한 메마르지도 잔인하지도 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"You understand what I mean, dearest.", "“제 말뜻을 아시잖아요, 여보."], ["I will not think it dry and cruel, even though sorrow should come upon us, if you-- I think you know what I mean.\"", "설령 슬픔이 우리에게 닥친다 해도 저는 세상을 메마르고 잔인하다고 여기지 않을 거예요, 당신만—— 제 말뜻을 아시리라 믿어요.”"]], [["\"If I am good to you.\"", "“내가 당신에게 잘해 준다면 말이지요.”"]], [["\"I am not afraid of that;--I am not the least afraid of that.", "“그건 두렵지 않아요. 조금도 두렵지 않아요."], ["You do not think that I could ever distrust you?", "제가 당신을 못 미더워할 수도 있다고 생각하시는 건 아니겠지요?"], ["But you must not be ashamed to look at the moonlight, and to read poetry, and to--\"", "하지만 당신은 달빛을 바라보는 것도, 시를 읽는 것도, 그리고——”"]], [["\"To talk nonsense, you mean.\"", "“허튼소리를 하는 것도 부끄러워하지 말라는 말이지요.”"]], [["But as he said it, he pressed her closer to his side, and his tone was pleasant to her.", "그러나 그렇게 말하면서 그는 그녀를 제 옆으로 더 바싹 끌어당겼고, 그의 말투는 그녀에게 다정하게 들렸다."]], [["\"I suppose I'm talking nonsense now?\"", "“지금 제가 허튼소리를 하고 있나 보죠?”"], ["she said, pouting.", "그녀가 입을 삐죽이며 말했다."], ["\"You liked me better when I was talking about the pigs; didn't you?\"", "“돼지 이야기를 할 때가 더 좋으셨죠. 안 그래요?”"]], [["\"No; I like you best now.\"", "“아니요. 나는 지금이 가장 좋아요.”"]], [["\"And why didn't you like me then?", "“그러면 그때는 왜 제가 마음에 안 드셨어요?"], ["Did I say anything to offend you?\"", "제가 언짢은 말이라도 했나요?”"]], [["\"I like you best now, because--\"", "“지금이 가장 좋은 건 말이지요——”"]], [["They were standing in the narrow pathway of the gate leading from the bridge into the gardens of the Great House, and the shadow of the thick-spreading laurels was around them.", "두 사람은 다리에서 큰 집 정원으로 이어지는 문의 좁은 오솔길에 서 있었고, 우거진 월계수 그늘이 그들을 둘러싸고 있었다."], ["But the moonlight still pierced brightly through the little avenue, and she, as she looked up to him, could see the form of his face and the loving softness of his eye.", "그러나 달빛은 여전히 그 작은 길로 환하게 스며들었고, 그녀는 그를 올려다보며 그의 얼굴 윤곽과 눈에 어린 사랑의 부드러움을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["\"Because--,\"", "“그건 말이지요——”"], ["said he; and then he stooped over her and pressed her closely, while she put up her lips to his, standing on tip-toe that she might reach to his face.", "그가 말했다. 그러고는 그녀 위로 몸을 숙여 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았고, 그녀는 그의 얼굴에 닿으려고 발끝으로 서서 입술을 그에게 내밀었다."]], [["\"Oh, my love!\"", "“아, 내 사랑!”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"My love!", "“내 사랑!"], ["my love!\"", "내 사랑!”"]], [["As Crosbie walked back to the Great House that night, he made a firm resolution that no consideration of worldly welfare should ever induce him to break his engagement with Lily Dale.", "그날 밤 큰 집으로 돌아가면서 크로즈비는 세상의 어떤 이득도 자기로 하여금 릴리 데일과의 약혼을 깨뜨리게 하지는 못하리라고 굳게 마음먹었다."], ["He went somewhat further also, and determined that he would not put off the marriage for more than six or eight months, or, at the most, ten, if he could possibly get his affairs arranged in that time.", "그는 거기서 한 걸음 더 나아가, 그 안에 자기 일을 정리할 수만 있다면 혼인을 여섯 달이나 여덟 달, 아무리 늦어도 열 달 넘게는 미루지 않겠다고 작정했다."], ["To be sure, he must give up everything,--all the aspirations and ambition of his life; but then, as he declared to himself somewhat mournfully, he was prepared to do that.", "물론 그러자면 모든 것을, 평생의 포부와 야심을 전부 버려야 할 터였다. 그러나 그는 다소 서글프게 스스로에게 밝혔듯이 그렇게 할 각오가 되어 있었다."], ["Such were his resolutions, and, as he thought of them in bed, he came to the conclusion that few men were less selfish than he was.", "그의 결심이 그러했고, 잠자리에서 그 결심을 곱씹으면서 그는 자기만큼 이기적이지 않은 사람도 드물다는 결론에 이르렀다."]], [["\"But what will they say to us for staying away?\"", "“그런데 우리가 자리를 비웠다고 사람들이 뭐라고 할까요?”"], ["said Lily, recovering herself.", "정신을 차리며 릴리가 말했다."], ["\"And I ought to be making the people dance, you know.", "“게다가 저는 사람들을 춤추게 만들어야 하잖아요."], ["Come along, and do make yourself nice.", "어서 가요, 그리고 좀 상냥하게 구세요."], ["Do waltz with Mary Eames;--pray, do.", "메리 임스와 왈츠를 추세요. 부디 그래 주세요."], ["If you don't, I won't speak to you all night!\"", "안 그러시면 밤새 말도 안 걸 거예요!”"]], [["Acting under which threat, Crosbie did, on his return, solicit the honour of that young lady's hand, thereby elating her into a seventh heaven of happiness.", "이 으름장에 따라 크로즈비는 돌아가서 그 젊은 아가씨의 손을 잡는 영광을 청했고, 그로써 그녀를 일곱째 하늘의 기쁨으로 들어 올려 주었다."], ["What could the world afford better than a waltz with such a partner as Adolphus Crosbie?", "어돌퍼스 크로즈비 같은 상대와 추는 왈츠보다 세상이 내줄 수 있는 더 좋은 것이 무엇이겠는가?"], ["And poor Mary Eames could waltz well; though she could not talk much as she danced, and would pant a good deal when she stopped.", "그리고 가엾은 메리 임스는 왈츠를 잘 출 줄 알았다. 다만 춤을 추면서 말은 많이 하지 못했고, 멈추고 나면 숨을 몹시 헐떡였다."], ["She put too much of her energy into the motion, and was too anxious to do the mechanical part of the work in a manner that should be satisfactory to her partner.", "그녀는 몸놀림에 너무 많은 기운을 쏟았고, 이 일의 기계적인 부분을 상대가 만족할 만하게 해내려고 지나치게 애를 썼다."], ["\"Oh!", "“아!"], ["thank you;--it's very nice.", "고맙습니다. 참 좋아요."], ["I shall be able to go on--again directly.\"", "곧, 곧 다시 출 수 있어요.”"], ["Her conversation with Crosbie did not get much beyond that, and yet she felt that she had never done better than on this occasion.", "크로즈비와 나눈 이야기는 그 언저리를 크게 벗어나지 못했지만, 그러면서도 그녀는 이날 밤만큼 잘해 본 적이 없다고 느꼈다."]], [["Though there were, at most, not above five couples of dancers, and though they who did not dance, such as the squire and Mr. Boyce, and a curate from a neighbouring parish, had, in fact, nothing to amuse them, the affair was kept on very merrily for a considerable number of hours.", "춤추는 짝이라야 많아야 다섯 쌍을 넘지 못했고, 지주와 보이스 씨와 이웃 교구에서 온 부목사처럼 춤추지 않는 사람들은 사실 심심풀이가 될 것이라곤 아무것도 없었지만, 그럼에도 잔치는 꽤 여러 시간 동안 아주 흥겹게 이어졌다."], ["Exactly at twelve o'clock there was a little supper, which, no doubt, served to relieve Mrs. Hearn's ennui, and at which Mrs.", "열두 시 정각에는 간단한 야식이 나왔는데, 그것이 헌 부인의 무료함을 덜어 주었을 것은 틀림없고 보이스 부인 역시 그 자리를 즐기는 듯했다."], ["Boyce also seemed to enjoy herself. As to the Mrs.", "이런 자리의 보이스 부인들에 대해서라면 나는 조금도 딱하게 여기지 않는다고 밝혀 둔다."], ["Boyces on such occasions, I profess that I feel no pity. They are generally happy in their children's happiness, or if not, they ought to be.", "그들은 대체로 제 자식들의 즐거움 속에서 즐거우며, 그렇지 않다면 마땅히 그러해야 한다."], ["At any rate, they are simply performing a manifest duty, which duty, in their time, was performed on their behalf.", "어쨌든 그들은 명백한 의무를 다하고 있을 뿐이고, 그 의무는 그들이 젊었을 적에 남들이 그들을 위해 다해 주었던 것이다."], ["But on what account do the Mrs. Hearns betake themselves to such gatherings?", "그러나 헌 부인들은 대체 무슨 까닭으로 그런 모임에 나오는 것일까?"], ["Why did that ancient lady sit there hour after hour yawning, longing for her bed, looking every ten minutes at her watch, while her old bones were stiff and sore, and her old ears pained with the noise?", "어째서 저 늙은 부인은 하품을 하며 침대를 그리워하고 십 분마다 시계를 들여다보면서, 늙은 뼈마디가 뻣뻣하게 쑤시고 늙은 귀가 소음에 시달리는데도 몇 시간이고 그 자리에 앉아 있었을까?"], ["It could hardly have been simply for the sake of the supper.", "설마 야식 하나 때문만은 아니었으리라."], ["After the supper, however, her maid took her across to her cottage, and Mrs. Boyce also then stole away home, and the squire went off with some little parade, suggesting to the young men that they should make no noise in the house as they returned.", "어쨌든 야식이 끝나자 하녀가 그녀를 오두막까지 데려다주었고, 보이스 부인도 그때 슬며시 집으로 돌아갔으며, 지주는 젊은이들에게 돌아올 때 집 안에서 소란을 피우지 말라고 이르면서 자못 요란하게 자리를 떴다."], ["But the poor curate remained, talking a dull word every now and then to Mrs. Dale, and looking on with tantalized eyes at the joys which the world had prepared for others than him.", "그러나 가엾은 부목사는 남아서 이따금 데일 부인에게 시시한 말 한마디씩을 건네며, 자기 아닌 다른 이들을 위해 세상이 마련해 둔 기쁨을 애타는 눈으로 바라보고만 있었다."], ["I must say that I think that public opinion and the bishops together are too hard upon curates in this particular.", "이 대목에서만은 세상의 여론과 주교들이 함께 부목사들에게 너무 모질다고 나는 말하지 않을 수 없다."]], [["In the latter part of the night's delight, when time and practice had made them all happy together, John Eames stood up for the first time to dance with Lily.", "밤의 즐거움이 무르익어 시간과 익숙함이 모두를 함께 즐겁게 만들어 놓았을 무렵, 존 임스는 처음으로 릴리와 춤을 추려고 일어섰다."], ["She had done all she could, short of asking him, to induce him to do her this favour; for she felt that it would be a favour.", "그녀는 대놓고 청하는 것만 빼고는 그가 이 호의를 베풀도록 할 수 있는 일을 다 해 두었다. 그것이 호의이리라고 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["How great had been the desire on his part to ask her, and, at the same time, how great the repugnance, Lily, perhaps, did not quite understand.", "그녀에게 청하고 싶은 마음이 그에게 얼마나 컸는지, 그러면서도 동시에 그 마음을 얼마나 크게 꺼렸는지 릴리는 아마 다 알지는 못했으리라."], ["And yet she understood much of it.", "그래도 그중 많은 것은 알고 있었다."], ["She knew that he was not angry with her.", "그가 자기에게 화가 난 것이 아니라는 것을 그녀는 알았다."], ["She knew that he was suffering from the injured pride of futile love, almost as much as from the futile love itself.", "그가 헛된 사랑 그 자체만큼이나 그 헛된 사랑으로 상한 자존심 때문에 괴로워하고 있다는 것도 알았다."], ["She wished to put him at his ease in this; but she did not quite give him credit for the full sincerity, and the upright, uncontrolled heartiness of his feelings.", "그녀는 그 점에서 그의 마음을 편하게 해 주고 싶었다. 그러나 그의 감정이 얼마나 온전히 진실한지, 얼마나 곧고 걷잡을 수 없이 뜨거운지까지는 그에게 온전히 인정해 주지 못했다."]], [["At length he did come up to her, and though, in truth, she was engaged, she at once accepted his offer.", "마침내 그가 그녀에게 다가왔고, 사실 그녀에게는 이미 약속한 짝이 있었지만 그녀는 그 자리에서 그의 청을 받아들였다."], ["Then she tripped across the room.", "그러고는 방을 가로질러 종종걸음을 쳤다."], ["\"Adolphus,\"", "“어돌퍼스,”"], ["she said, \"I can't dance with you, though I said I would.", "그녀가 말했다. “약속은 했지만 당신과는 춤을 못 추겠어요."], ["John Eames has asked me, and I haven't stood up with him before.", "존 임스가 청했는데 아직 그이와 한 번도 서 본 적이 없거든요."], ["You understand, and you'll be a good boy, won't you?\"", "아시겠지요, 착하게 굴어 주실 거죠?”"]], [["Crosbie, not being in the least jealous, was a good boy, and sat himself down to rest, hidden behind a door.", "조금도 질투 같은 것을 하지 않는 크로즈비는 착하게 굴었고, 문 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 앉아 쉬었다."]], [["For the first few minutes the conversation between Eames and Lily was of a very matter-of-fact kind.", "처음 몇 분 동안 임스와 릴리가 나눈 이야기는 아주 대수롭지 않은 것들이었다."], ["She repeated her wish that she might see him in London, and he said that of course he should come and call.", "그녀는 런던에서 그를 볼 수 있으면 좋겠다는 말을 되풀이했고, 그는 물론 찾아가겠다고 했다."], ["Then there was silence for a little while, and they went through their figure dancing.", "그러고는 잠시 말이 없었고, 두 사람은 춤의 대형을 따라 움직였다."]], [["\"I don't at all know yet when we are to be married,\"", "“저희가 언제 결혼할지는 아직 저도 전혀 몰라요.”"], ["said Lily, as soon as they were again standing together.", "다시 나란히 서게 되자마자 릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"No; I dare say not,\"", "“네, 그렇겠지요.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"But not this year, I suppose.", "“하지만 올해는 아닐 거예요."], ["Indeed, I should say, of course not.\"", "아니, 물론 아니라고 해야겠지요.”"]], [["\"In the spring, perhaps,\"", "“어쩌면 봄쯤이겠지요.”"], ["suggested Eames.", "임스가 말해 보았다."], ["He had an unconscious desire that it might be postponed to some Greek kalends, and yet he did not wish to injure Lily.", "그는 그날이 하염없이 먼 훗날로 미루어졌으면 하는 마음을 저도 모르게 품고 있었으면서도, 릴리를 아프게 하고 싶지는 않았다."]], [["\"The reason I mention it is this, that we should be so very glad if you could be here.", "“제가 그 말씀을 드리는 까닭은 이래요. 그날 여기 와 주실 수 있다면 저희가 정말 기쁠 것 같아서요."], ["We all love you so much, and I should so like to have you here on that day.\"", "저희 모두 존 씨를 무척 좋아하고, 저도 그날 존 씨가 여기 계셨으면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["Why is it that girls so constantly do this,--so frequently ask men who have loved them to be present at their marriages with other men?", "어째서 처녀들은 이런 일을 그토록 자주 하는 것일까? 어째서 자기를 사랑했던 남자에게 다른 남자와 올리는 제 혼인 자리에 와 달라고 그토록 자주 청하는 것일까?"], ["There is no triumph in it.", "거기에는 아무런 우쭐함도 없다."], ["It is done in sheer kindness and affection.", "순전한 친절과 애정에서 하는 일이다."], ["They intend to offer something which shall soften and not aggravate the sorrow that they have caused.", "그들은 자기가 일으킨 슬픔을 덧내지 않고 누그러뜨려 줄 무언가를 내주려는 것이다."], ["\"You can't marry me yourself,\"", "“저와 결혼하실 수는 없지요.”"], ["the lady seems to say.", "아가씨는 그렇게 말하는 듯하다."], ["\"But the next greatest blessing which I can offer you shall be yours,--you shall see me married to somebody else.\"", "“하지만 제가 드릴 수 있는 그다음으로 큰 축복은 당신 것이 될 거예요. 제가 다른 사람과 혼인하는 걸 보실 수 있게요.”"], ["I fully appreciate the intention, but in honest truth, I doubt the eligibility of the proffered entertainment.", "나는 그 뜻을 온전히 헤아리지만, 솔직히 말하자면 그렇게 내놓은 대접이 과연 반가운 것일지는 의심스럽다."]], [["On the present occasion John Eames seemed to be of this opinion, for he did not at once accept the invitation.", "이번 자리에서 존 임스도 이런 생각이었던 모양이니, 그는 그 초대를 선뜻 받아들이지 않았다."]], [["\"Will you not oblige me so far as that?\"", "“그만한 청도 들어주지 않으시겠어요?”"], ["said she softly.", "그녀가 나직이 말했다."]], [["\"I would do anything to oblige you,\"", "“당신을 위해서라면 무엇이든 하겠습니다.”"], ["said he gruffly; \"almost anything.\"", "그가 퉁명스럽게 말했다. “거의 무엇이든지요.”"]], [["\"But not that?\"", "“하지만 그건 못 하시겠고요?”"]], [["\"No; not that.", "“네, 그건 못 합니다."], ["I could not do that.\"", "그것만은 할 수 없어요.”"], ["Then he went off upon his figure, and when they were next both standing together, they remained silent till their turn for dancing had again come.", "그러고서 그는 제 대형을 따라 나아갔고, 다음번에 두 사람이 나란히 서게 되었을 때는 다시 춤출 차례가 돌아올 때까지 말없이 있었다."], ["Why was it, that after that night Lily thought more of John Eames than ever she had thought before;--felt for him, I mean, a higher respect, as for a man who had a will of his own?", "어째서였을까, 그날 밤이 지난 뒤로 릴리가 그 어느 때보다도 존 임스를 마음에 두게 된 것은. 내 말은, 제 뜻을 지닌 남자에게 품는 더 높은 존경을 그에게 느끼게 된 것은."]], [["And in that quadrille Crofts and Bell had been dancing together, and they also had been talking of Lily's marriage.", "그리고 그 카드리유에서는 크로프츠와 벨이 함께 춤을 추고 있었고, 그들 역시 릴리의 혼인 이야기를 하던 참이었다."], ["\"A man may undergo what he likes for himself,\"", "“남자는 제 몫으로 무엇이든 겪어 낼 수 있지요.”"], ["he had said, \"but he has no right to make a woman undergo poverty.\"", "그가 말했다. “하지만 여자에게 가난을 겪게 할 권리는 없습니다.”"]], [["\"Perhaps not,\"", "“그럴지도 모르지요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"That which is no suffering for a man,--which no man should think of for himself,--will make a hell on earth for a woman.\"", "“남자에게는 고생 축에도 들지 않는 것이, 남자라면 제 몫으로는 생각조차 하지 말아야 할 것이, 여자에게는 이 땅 위의 지옥이 되고 맙니다.”"]], [["\"I suppose it would,\"", "“그렇겠지요.”"], ["said Bell, answering him without a sign of feeling in her face or voice.", "벨은 얼굴에도 목소리에도 아무런 감정의 기색 없이 그렇게 대답했다."], ["But she took in every word that he spoke, and disputed their truth inwardly with all the strength of her heart and mind, and with the very vehemence of her soul.", "그러나 그녀는 그가 한 말을 한마디도 빠짐없이 새겨 두었고, 가슴과 머리의 온 힘으로, 영혼의 온 격렬함으로 그 말들이 옳지 않다고 속으로 반박했다."], ["\"As if a woman cannot bear more than a man!\"", "“여자가 남자보다 더 많이 견디지 못한다는 듯이!”"], ["she said to herself, as she walked the length of the room alone, when she had got herself free from the doctor's arm.", "그녀는 의사의 팔에서 풀려나 홀로 방을 가로질러 걸으며 스스로에게 말했다."]], [["# CHAPTER X. MRS. LUPEX AND AMELIA ROPER.", "# 제10장. 루펙스 부인과 어밀리아 로퍼"]], [["I should simply mislead a confiding reader if I were to tell him that Mrs. Lupex was an amiable woman.", "루펙스 부인이 상냥한 여인이었다고 말한다면, 나는 나를 믿어 주는 독자를 그저 그르치는 셈이 될 것이다."], ["Perhaps the fact that she was not amiable is the one great fault that should be laid to her charge; but that fault had spread itself so widely, and had cropped forth in so many different places of her life, like a strong rank plant that will show itself all over a garden, that it may almost be said that it made her odious in every branch of life, and detestable alike to those who knew her little and to those who knew her much.", "어쩌면 그녀가 상냥하지 않았다는 사실이야말로 그녀에게 물어야 할 단 하나의 큰 허물인지도 모른다. 그러나 그 허물은 정원 어디에나 제 모습을 드러내고야 마는 억세고 무성한 잡초처럼 너무나 널리 퍼져 있었고 그녀의 삶의 온갖 자리에서 하도 여러 번 돋아났으므로, 그것이 그녀를 삶의 모든 갈래에서 밉살스러운 사람으로 만들었으며 그녀를 조금 아는 이에게나 많이 아는 이에게나 똑같이 넌더리 나는 사람으로 만들었다고 해도 좋을 지경이었다."], ["If a searcher could have got at the inside spirit of the woman, that searcher would have found that she wished to go right,--that she did make, or at any rate promise to herself that she would make, certain struggles to attain decency and propriety.", "만일 어떤 탐색자가 이 여인의 속마음에까지 다다를 수 있었더라면, 그 탐색자는 그녀가 바르게 살고자 했다는 것을—즉 예절과 품위를 갖추려고 얼마간 애를 썼다는 것을, 적어도 그러겠노라고 스스로에게 약속은 했다는 것을 알아냈으리라."], ["But it was so natural to her to torment those whose misfortune brought them near to her, and especially that wretched man who in an evil day had taken her to his bosom as his wife, that decency fled from her, and propriety would not live in her quarters.", "그러나 불운하게도 제 곁에 오게 된 이들을, 그중에서도 재수 없는 날에 그녀를 아내로 제 품에 받아들인 그 가엾은 사내를 들볶는 일이 그녀에게는 하도 자연스러운 것이어서, 예절은 그녀에게서 달아났고 품위는 그녀가 거처하는 곳에 살려 하지 않았다."]], [["Mrs. Lupex was, as I have already described her, a woman not without some feminine attraction in the eyes of those who like morning negligence and evening finery, and do not object to a long nose somewhat on one side.", "루펙스 부인은, 내가 이미 그려 보인 대로, 아침의 헝클어진 차림과 저녁의 화려한 치장을 좋아하고 한쪽으로 조금 비뚤어진 긴 코를 개의치 않는 사람들의 눈에는 여자다운 매력이 아주 없지는 않은 여인이었다."], ["She was clever in her way, and could say smart things.", "그녀는 그녀 나름으로 영리했고 재치 있는 말을 할 줄 알았다."], ["She could flatter also, though her very flattery had always in it something that was disagreeable.", "아첨도 할 줄 알았으나, 그 아첨에는 언제나 어딘가 거슬리는 데가 있었다."], ["And she must have had some power of will, as otherwise her husband would have escaped from her before the days of which I am writing.", "그리고 그녀에게는 얼마간 의지의 힘도 있었음이 분명하다."], ["Otherwise, also, she could hardly have obtained her footing and kept it in Mrs. Roper's drawing-room.", "그렇지 않았더라면 그녀의 남편은 내가 지금 쓰고 있는 시절이 오기 전에 이미 그녀에게서 달아나 버렸을 것이기 때문이다."], ["For though the hundred pounds a year, either paid, or promised to be paid, was matter with Mrs.", "그렇지 않았더라면 또한 로퍼 부인네 응접실에 발을 붙이고 그 자리를 지켜 내지도 못했을 것이다."], ["Roper of vast consideration, nevertheless the first three months of Mrs. Lupex's sojourn in Burton Crescent were not over before the landlady of that house was most anxiously desirous of getting herself quit of her married boarders.", "실제로 치렀든 치르겠노라 약속만 했든 연 백 파운드라는 돈은 로퍼 부인에게 대단히 중요한 문제였지만, 그럼에도 루펙스 부인이 버턴 크레슨트에 머문 첫 석 달이 채 지나기도 전에 그 집 여주인은 혼인한 그 하숙인 부부를 제 집에서 떨쳐 버리기를 무엇보다도 간절히 바라게 되었으니 말이다."]], [["I shall perhaps best describe a little incident that had occurred in Burton Crescent during the absence of our friend Eames, and the manner in which things were going on in that locality, by giving at length two letters which Johnny received by post at Guestwick on the morning after Mrs.", "우리 벗 임스가 자리를 비운 사이 버턴 크레슨트에서 일어난 자그마한 사건과 그 동네에서 일이 되어 가던 형편을, 나는 아마도 조니가 데일 부인의 잔치가 있은 이튿날 아침 게스트윅에서 우편으로 받은 편지 두 통을 고스란히 옮겨 놓음으로써 가장 잘 그려 보일 수 있을 것이다."], ["Dale's party. One was from his friend Cradell, and the other from the devoted Amelia.", "하나는 그의 벗 크래들에게서 온 것이었고, 다른 하나는 헌신적인 어밀리아에게서 온 것이었다."], ["In this instance I will give that from the gentleman first, presuming that I shall best consult my reader's wishes by keeping the greater delicacy till the last.", "이 경우에 나는 신사 쪽 편지를 먼저 내놓겠으니, 더 섬세한 쪽을 마지막까지 아껴 두는 편이 내 독자의 바람에 가장 잘 들어맞으리라 여기기 때문이다."]], [["Income-tax Office, September, 186--.", "소득세 사무국, 186◯년 9월."]], [["MY DEAR JOHNNY,--We have had a terrible affair in the Crescent; and I really hardly know how to tell you; and yet I must do it, for I want your advice.", "내 소중한 조니에게.—크레슨트에서 끔찍한 일이 벌어졌어. 자네한테 이걸 어떻게 이야기해야 할지 정말 모르겠네."], ["You know the sort of standing that I was on with Mrs.", "그래도 이야기는 해야겠어. 자네 조언이 필요하니까."], ["Lupex, and perhaps you remember what we were saying on the platform at the station.", "내가 루펙스 부인과 어떤 사이로 지내 왔는지는 자네도 알 테고, 역 승강장에서 우리가 나눈 이야기도 어쩌면 기억하고 있을 거야."], ["I have, no doubt, been fond of her society, as I might be of that of any other friend.", "물론 나는 그 부인과 어울리는 게 좋았어. 다른 어떤 벗과 어울리는 것이나 마찬가지로 말이지."], ["I knew, of course, that she was a fine woman; and if her husband chose to be jealous, I couldn't help that.", "그 부인이 잘생긴 여자라는 것쯤이야 나도 알고 있었고, 남편이 굳이 질투를 하겠다면 그거야 내가 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이지."], ["But I never intended anything wrong; and, if it was necessary, couldn't I call you as a witness to prove it?", "하지만 나는 잘못된 일이라고는 조금도 뜻한 적이 없어. 그리고 필요하다면 자네를 증인으로 불러 그걸 증명할 수도 있지 않겠나?"], ["I never spoke a word to her out of Mrs. Roper's drawing-room; and Miss Spruce, or Mrs.", "나는 로퍼 부인네 응접실 밖에서 그 부인에게 말 한마디 건넨 적이 없고, 그 자리에는 늘 스프루스 양이든 로퍼 부인이든 누군가가 함께 있었어."], ["Roper, or somebody has always been there. You know he drinks horribly sometimes, but I do not think he ever gets downright drunk.", "자네도 알다시피 그 사람은 이따금 지독하게 술을 마시지만, 아주 곤드레만드레가 되는 법은 없다고 나는 생각해."], ["Well, he came home last night about nine o'clock after one of these bouts. From what Jemima says [Jemima was Mrs.", "그런데 어젯밤 아홉 시쯤에 그 사람이 그런 술판을 한바탕 벌이고 집에 돌아왔단 말이지."], ["Roper's parlour-maid], I believe he had been at it down at the theatre for three days.", "제미마의 말로는[제미마는 로퍼 부인네 응접실 하녀야] 사흘 내내 극장에서 그러고 있었다는 거야."], ["We hadn't seen him since Tuesday.", "우리는 화요일 이후로 그 사람을 보지 못했고."], ["He went straight into the parlour and sent up Jemima to me, to say that he wanted to see me. Mrs.", "그 사람은 곧장 아래층 거실로 들어가더니 제미마를 나한테 올려보내 나를 보자고 한다는 말을 전하게 했어."], ["Lupex was in the room and heard the girl summon me, and, jumping up, she declared that if there was going to be blood shed she would leave the house.", "루펙스 부인이 방 안에 있다가 하녀가 나를 부르는 소리를 듣고는 벌떡 일어나며, 피를 볼 일이 벌어질 거라면 자기는 이 집을 나가겠다고 선언하더군."], ["There was nobody else in the room but Miss Spruce, and she didn't say a word, but took her candle and went upstairs.", "방에는 그 밖에 스프루스 양밖에 없었는데, 그 노부인은 한마디도 하지 않고 촛대를 들고 위층으로 올라가 버렸어."], ["You must own it looked very uncomfortable.", "이거 참 딱한 꼴이었다는 건 자네도 인정해야 할 거야."], ["What was I to do with a drunken man down in the parlour?", "아래층 거실에 술 취한 사내가 있는데 내가 어쩌란 말인가?"], ["However, she seemed to think I ought to go.", "그런데도 부인은 내가 내려가야 한다고 여기는 눈치였어."], ["\"If he comes up here,\"", "“그이가 여기까지 올라오면,”"], ["said she, \"I shall be the victim.", "하고 부인이 말하더군. “희생되는 건 저예요."], ["You little know of what that man is capable when his wrath has been inflamed by wine!\"", "그 사람이 술기운에 노여움이 타오르면 무슨 짓까지 할 수 있는지 당신은 조금도 모르세요!”"], ["Now, I think you are aware that I am not likely to be very much afraid of any man; but why was I to be got into a row in such a way as this?", "자, 나로 말하자면 어떤 사내를 두고도 그리 겁을 낼 사람이 아니라는 걸 자네도 알 거야. 하지만 어쩌자고 내가 이런 식으로 소동에 말려들어야 한단 말인가?"], ["I hadn't done anything.", "나는 아무 짓도 하지 않았는데."], ["And then, if there was to be a quarrel, and anything was to come of it, as she seemed to expect,--like bloodshed, I mean, or a fight, or if he were to knock me on the head with the poker, where should I be at my office?", "게다가 만일 싸움이 벌어지고 부인이 예상하는 대로 무슨 일이 생긴다면—피를 본다든지, 주먹다짐을 한다든지, 아니면 그 사람이 부지깽이로 내 머리를 후려친다든지 한다면, 나는 사무국에서 어떻게 되겠나?"], ["A man in a public office, as you and I are, can't quarrel like anybody else.", "자네나 나처럼 관청에 몸담은 사람은 아무나처럼 싸움질을 할 수가 없어."], ["It was this that I felt so much at the moment.", "그 순간 내가 그토록 절실하게 느낀 것이 바로 그거였네."], ["\"Go down to him,\"", "“내려가세요,”"], ["said she, \"unless you wish to see me murdered at your feet.\"", "부인이 말했어. “내가 당신 발치에서 살해되는 꼴을 보고 싶지 않으시다면요.”"], ["Fisher says, that if what I say is true, they must have arranged it all between them.", "피셔는 내 말이 사실이라면 그 두 사람이 미리 다 짜 놓은 것이 틀림없다고 하더군."], ["I don't think that; for I do believe that she really is fond of me.", "나는 그렇게 생각하지 않아. 그 부인이 나를 정말 좋아한다고 나는 믿으니까 말이야."], ["And then everybody knows that they never do agree about anything.", "게다가 그 두 사람이 무슨 일에서든 마음이 맞는 법이 없다는 건 누구나 아는 사실이고."], ["But she certainly did implore me to go down to him.", "어쨌든 부인은 나더러 내려가 달라고 간곡히 청했어."], ["Well, I went down; and, as I got to the bottom of the stairs, where I found Jemima, I heard him walking up and down the parlour.", "그래서 나는 내려갔지. 그런데 계단 아래에 이르러 제미마와 마주쳤을 때, 그 사람이 거실 안을 이리저리 왔다 갔다 하는 소리가 들리더군."], ["\"Take care of yourself, Mr.", "“조심하세요, 크래들 씨,”"], ["Cradell,\" said the girl; and I could see by her face that she was in a terrible fright.", "하고 하녀가 말했는데, 얼굴만 봐도 그 애가 몹시 겁에 질려 있는 걸 알 수 있었어."]], [["At that moment I happened to see my hat on the hall table, and it occurred to me that I ought to put myself into the hands of a friend.", "바로 그때 현관 탁자에 놓인 내 모자가 눈에 띄었고, 나 자신을 벗의 손에 맡겨야겠다는 생각이 들었어. 물론 나는 식당에 있는 그 사내가 무섭지는 않았지."], ["Of course, I was not afraid of that man in the dining-room; but should I have been justified in engaging in a struggle, perhaps for dear life, in Mrs.", "하지만 로퍼 부인네 집 안에서, 어쩌면 목숨을 걸고 몸싸움을 벌이는 것이 과연 옳은 일이었겠나? 나는 그 부인의 이해도 생각해야 했네."], ["Roper's house? I was bound to think of her interests.", "그래서 나는 모자를 집어 들고 유유히 대문으로 걸어 나왔어."], ["So I took up my hat, and deliberately walked out of the front door.", "“그 사람한테 전해라,” 나는 제미마에게 말했지."], ["\"Tell him,\"", "“나는 집에 없다고.”"], ["said I to Jemima, \"that I'm not at home.\"", "그러고서 곧장 피셔의 집으로 갔네."], ["And so I went away direct to Fisher's, meaning to send him back to Lupex as my friend; but Fisher was at his chess-club.", "그를 내 입회인으로 삼아 루펙스에게 보낼 작정이었지. 그런데 피셔는 체스 클럽에 가 있더군."]], [["As I thought there was no time to be lost on such an occasion as this, I went down to the club and called him out.", "이런 일에는 지체할 겨를이 없다고 생각했기에 나는 그 클럽까지 찾아가 그를 불러냈어."], ["You know what a cool fellow Fisher is.", "피셔가 얼마나 침착한 친구인지는 자네도 알지."], ["I don't suppose anything would ever excite him.", "무슨 일이 있어도 흥분하는 법이 없는 사람이야."], ["When I told him the story, he said that he would sleep upon it; and I had to walk up and down before the club while he finished his game.", "내가 이야기를 들려주었더니 그는 하룻밤 자면서 생각해 보겠다고 하더군. 그래서 나는 그가 판을 끝낼 때까지 클럽 앞을 오락가락하며 기다려야 했네."], ["Fisher seemed to think that I might go back to Burton Crescent; but, of course, I knew that that would be out of the question.", "피셔는 내가 버턴 크레슨트로 돌아가도 괜찮으리라고 여기는 눈치였지만, 물론 나는 그게 말도 안 되는 소리라는 걸 알고 있었지."], ["So it ended in my going home and sleeping on his sofa, and sending for some of my things in the morning.", "그래서 결국 나는 그의 집으로 가서 소파에서 자고, 아침에 사람을 보내 내 물건 몇 가지를 가져오게 했어."], ["I wanted him to get up and see Lupex before going to the office this morning.", "나는 그가 오늘 아침 출근하기 전에 일어나 루펙스를 만나 주기를 바랐네."], ["But he said it would be better to put it off, and so he will call upon him at the theatre immediately after office hours.", "하지만 그는 미루는 편이 낫겠다고 했고, 그래서 근무 시간이 끝나는 대로 곧 극장으로 그를 찾아가겠다는 거야."]], [["I want you to write to me at once saying what you know about the matter.", "자네가 이 일에 관해 아는 바를 곧바로 편지로 써 보내 주었으면 하네."], ["I ask you, as I don't want to lug in any of the other people at Roper's.", "로퍼네 다른 사람들을 이 일에 끌어들이고 싶지 않아서 자네에게 부탁하는 걸세. 참 난처한 노릇이야."], ["It is very uncomfortable, as I can't exactly leave her at once because of last quarter's money, otherwise I should cut and run; for the house is not the sort of place either for you or me.", "지난 분기 셋돈 때문에 당장 그 집을 떠날 수가 없거든. 안 그랬으면 벌써 짐을 싸서 달아났을 거야."], ["You may take my word for that, Master Johnny.", "그 집은 자네에게도 나에게도 어울리는 곳이 아니야."], ["And I could tell you something, too, about A.", "내 말을 믿어도 좋네, 조니 도련님. 그리고 A."], ["R., only I don't want to make mischief.", "R.에 대해서도 자네한테 해 줄 이야기가 있지만, 공연히 말썽을 일으키고 싶지는 않군."], ["But do you write immediately.", "아무튼 곧바로 편지를 써 주게."], ["And now I think of it, you had better write to Fisher, so that he can show your letter to Lupex,--just saying, that to the best of your belief there had never been anything between her and me but mere friendship; and that, of course, you, as my friend, must have known everything.", "그리고 지금 생각해 보니 피셔에게 편지를 쓰는 편이 낫겠어. 그래야 그가 자네 편지를 루펙스에게 보여 줄 수 있을 테니까—자네가 아는 한 그 부인과 나 사이에는 그저 우정 말고는 아무것도 없었다는 것, 그리고 물론 자네는 내 벗이니 모든 것을 알고 있었을 것이라는 정도만 써 주면 되네."], ["Whether I shall go back to Roper's to-night will depend on what Fisher says after the interview.", "내가 오늘 밤 로퍼네로 돌아갈지 어떨지는 그 면담이 끝난 뒤 피셔가 무어라고 하느냐에 달려 있어."]], [["Good-by, old fellow!", "잘 있게, 이 친구야!"], ["I hope you are enjoying yourself, and that L.", "즐겁게 지내고 있기를 바라고, L."], ["D. is quite well.--Your sincere friend,", "D.도 잘 있기를 바라네.—자네의 진실한 벗,"]], [["John Eames read this letter over twice before he opened that from Amelia.", "존 임스는 어밀리아에게서 온 편지를 뜯기 전에 이 편지를 두 번 읽었다."], ["He had never yet received a letter from Miss Roper; and felt very little of that ardour for its perusal which young men generally experience on the receipt of a first letter from a young lady.", "그는 아직 로퍼 양에게서 편지를 받아 본 적이 없었으나, 젊은 사내가 젊은 아가씨에게서 첫 편지를 받았을 때 대개 느끼는 그 뜨거운 마음은 조금도 느끼지 못했다."], ["The memory of Amelia was at the present moment distasteful to him; and he would have thrown the letter unopened into the fire, had he not felt it might be dangerous to do so.", "그 순간 어밀리아에 대한 기억은 그에게 역겨운 것이었고, 그렇게 하는 것이 위험하리라는 생각만 아니었더라면 그는 편지를 뜯지도 않은 채 불 속에 던져 버렸을 것이다."], ["As regarded his friend Cradell, he could not but feel ashamed of him,--ashamed of him, not for running away from Mr. Lupex, but for excusing his escape on false pretences.", "벗 크래들로 말하자면 그가 부끄럽게 여겨지지 않을 수 없었다—루펙스 씨에게서 달아났다는 것이 부끄러운 것이 아니라, 그 도망을 거짓 구실로 변명한다는 것이 부끄러웠다."]], [["And then, at last, he opened the letter from Amelia.", "그러고서 마침내 그는 어밀리아의 편지를 뜯었다."], ["\"Dearest John,\"", "“더없이 소중한 존”"], ["it began; and as he read the words, he crumpled the paper up between his fingers.", "하고 편지는 시작되었는데, 그 글자를 읽으면서 그는 종이를 손가락 사이에서 구겨 버렸다."], ["It was written in a fair female hand, with sharp points instead of curves to the letters, but still very legible, and looking as though there were a decided purport in every word of it.", "그것은 반듯한 여자 글씨로 쓰여 있었으니, 글자에 둥근 획 대신 뾰족한 모가 서 있었으나 그래도 읽기에는 아주 또렷했고, 한 낱말 한 낱말마다 단단히 정해 둔 속셈이 들어 있는 듯이 보였다."]], [["DEAREST JOHN,--It feels so strange to me to write to you in such language as this.", "더없이 소중한 존에게.—당신에게 이런 말로 편지를 쓰자니 참 이상한 기분이 들어요."], ["And yet you are dearest, and have I not a right to call you so?", "하지만 당신은 정말 더없이 소중한 사람인걸요. 내게 그렇게 부를 권리가 없나요?"], ["And are you not my own, and am not I yours?", "그리고 당신은 내 사람이 아닌가요? 나는 당신 사람이 아니고요?"]], [["Again he crunched the paper up in his hand, and, as he did so, he muttered words which I need not repeat at length.", "그는 다시 종이를 손안에 구겨 쥐었고, 그러면서 여기 길게 옮길 필요는 없는 말을 중얼거렸다."], ["But still he went on with his letter.", "그래도 그는 편지를 계속 읽어 나갔다."]], [["I know that we understand each other perfectly, and when that is the case, heart should be allowed to speak openly to heart.", "우리 둘이 서로를 더없이 잘 알고 있다는 것을 나는 알아요. 그리고 그럴 때에는 마음이 마음에게 거리낌 없이 말하도록 두어야 하지요."], ["Those are my feelings, and I believe that you will find them reciprocal in your own bosom.", "그것이 내 심정이고, 당신의 가슴속에서도 같은 심정을 찾아내리라고 나는 믿어요."], ["Is it not sweet to be loved?", "사랑받는다는 건 달콤한 일이 아닌가요?"], ["I find it so.", "나는 그렇게 느껴요."], ["And, dearest John, let me assure you, with open candour, that there is no room for jealousy in this breast with regard to you.", "그리고 더없이 소중한 존, 터놓고 솔직하게 말씀드리지만, 이 가슴에는 당신을 두고 질투할 자리가 조금도 없답니다. 그러기에는 내가 당신의 명예를, 그리고 내 자신의—매력이라고 말하고 싶지만 그러면 당신이 나를 우쭐댄다고 하시겠죠—그것을 너무나 굳게 믿고 있는걸요."], ["I have too much confidence for that, I can assure you, both in your honour and in my own--I would say charms, only you would call me vain.", "정말이에요. 내가 L."], ["You must not suppose that I meant what I said about L. D.", "D.에 대해 한 말을 진심이었다고 여기시면 안 돼요."], ["Of course, you will be glad to see the friends of your childhood; and it would be far from your Amelia's heart to begrudge you such delightful pleasure.", "물론 당신은 어린 시절의 벗들을 만나 반가우시겠지요. 그런 즐거운 기쁨을 당신에게서 아까워하는 일 따위는 당신의 어밀리아의 마음과는 아주 거리가 먼 일이랍니다."], ["Your friends will, I hope, some day be my friends.", "당신의 벗들이 언젠가는 내 벗들도 되었으면 해요."], ["[Another crunch.] And if there be any one among them, any real L. D.", "[또 한 번 종이가 구겨졌다.] 그리고 그들 가운데 당신이 각별히 좋아하시던 사람이 있다면, 어떤 진짜 L."], ["whom you have specially liked, I will receive her to my heart, specially also.", "D.가 있다면, 나는 그 사람도 각별히 내 마음에 받아들이겠어요."]], [["This assurance on the part of his Amelia was too much for him, and he threw the letter from him, thinking whence he might get relief--whether from suicide or from the colonies; but presently he took it up again, and drained the bitter cup to the bottom.", "자기 어밀리아가 이렇게까지 장담하는 것은 그로서는 견디기 어려운 노릇이어서, 그는 편지를 내던지며 어디에서 구원을 얻을 수 있을까—자살에서 얻을까 아니면 식민지로 달아나 얻을까 하고 생각했다. 그러나 이내 다시 편지를 집어 들고 그 쓴 잔을 바닥까지 들이켰다."]], [["And if I seemed petulant to you before you went away, you must forgive your own Amelia.", "그리고 당신이 떠나시기 전에 내가 당신에게 짜증스럽게 굴었다면, 당신의 어밀리아를 용서해 주셔야 해요."], ["I had nothing before me but misery for the month of your absence.", "당신이 없는 그 한 달 동안 내 앞에 놓인 것이라고는 비참함밖에 없었으니까요."], ["There is no one here congenial to my feelings,--of course not.", "여기에는 내 마음에 맞는 사람이 아무도 없어요—당연한 일이지요."], ["And you would not wish me to be happy in your absence,--would you?", "그리고 당신이 없는 동안 내가 행복하기를 당신도 바라지는 않으시겠죠?"], ["I can assure you, let your wishes be what they may, I never can be happy again unless you are with me.", "정말이지, 당신이 무엇을 바라시든 당신이 곁에 있지 않고서는 나는 두 번 다시 행복해질 수 없어요."], ["Write to me one little line, and tell me that you are grateful to me for my devotion.", "한 줄만이라도 답장을 써 보내 주세요. 그리고 나의 이 헌신에 고마워하고 있다고 말해 주세요."]], [["And now, I must tell you that we have had a sad affair in the house; and I do not think that your friend Mr.", "그리고 이제 알려 드려야 할 것이, 이 집에서 딱한 일이 벌어졌다는 거예요. 나는 당신 벗 크래들 씨가 조금이라도 잘 처신했다고는 생각하지 않아요."], ["Cradell has behaved at all well. You remember how he has been always going on with Mrs.", "그이가 루펙스 부인과 늘 어떻게 지내 왔는지는 당신도 기억하시죠."], ["Lupex. Mother was quite unhappy about it, though she didn't like to say anything.", "어머니는 그 일로 몹시 언짢아하셨지만 무슨 말씀을 하시기는 싫어하셨어요."], ["Of course, when a lady's name is concerned, it is particular.", "물론 부인네 이름이 걸린 일이니 조심스러운 노릇이지요."], ["But Lupex has become dreadful jealous during the last week; and we all knew that something was coming.", "그런데 지난주부터 루펙스가 지독하게 질투를 하기 시작했고, 우리 모두 무슨 일이 벌어지리라는 걸 알고 있었어요."], ["She is an artful woman, but I don't think she meant anything bad--only to drive her husband to desperation.", "그 부인은 술수가 많은 여자이지만 나쁜 뜻이 있었던 것 같지는 않아요—그저 남편을 미치도록 몰아붙이려는 것뿐이었죠."], ["He came here yesterday in one of his tantrums, and wanted to see Cradell; but he got frightened, and took his hat and went off.", "그 사람이 어제 여기에 발작을 일으킨 채 들어와서 크래들을 보자고 했는데, 크래들 씨는 겁을 먹고 모자를 집어 들고 나가 버렸어요."], ["Now, that wasn't quite right.", "그건 옳은 일이 아니었죠."], ["If he was innocent, why didn't he stand his ground and explain the mistake?", "떳떳하다면 왜 자리를 지키고 서서 오해를 풀지 않았을까요?"], ["As mother says, it gives the house such a name.", "어머니 말씀대로 그런 일은 이 집에 나쁜 소문을 안겨 주는 거예요."], ["Lupex swore last night that he'd be off to the Income-tax Office this morning, and have Cradell out before all the commissioners, and clerks, and everybody.", "루펙스는 어젯밤에 오늘 아침 소득세 사무국으로 쳐들어가서 위원들이며 서기들이며 모두가 보는 앞에서 크래들을 끌어내겠다고 큰소리를 쳤어요."], ["If he does that, it will get into the papers, and all London will be full of it.", "정말 그렇게 하면 그 일이 신문에까지 나서 온 런던이 그 이야기로 떠들썩해지겠죠."], ["She would like it, I know; for all she cares for is to be talked about; but only think what it will be for mother's house.", "그 부인이야 그걸 좋아하겠지요, 그 사람이 마음 쓰는 건 오로지 남들 입에 오르내리는 것뿐이니까요. 하지만 어머니 집이 어떻게 되겠는지 좀 생각해 보세요."], ["I wish you were here; for your high prudence and courage would set everything right at once,--at least, I think so.", "당신이 여기 계셨으면 좋겠어요. 당신의 높은 분별과 용기라면 모든 일을 단번에 바로잡아 놓으실 텐데요—적어도 나는 그렇게 생각해요."]], [["I shall count the minutes till I get an answer to this, and shall envy the postman who will have your letter before it will reach me.", "이 편지의 답장을 받을 때까지 나는 시간을 세고 있을 거예요. 그리고 나보다 먼저 당신 편지를 손에 쥘 우체부가 부러울 거예요."], ["Do write at once.", "부디 곧바로 써 주세요."], ["If I do not hear by Monday morning I shall think that something is the matter.", "월요일 아침까지 소식이 없으면 나는 무슨 일이 생겼다고 생각하겠어요."], ["Even though you are among your dear old friends, surely you can find a moment to write to your own Amelia.", "그리운 옛 벗들 사이에 계신다 해도 당신의 어밀리아에게 편지 쓸 짬쯤은 분명 내실 수 있을 거예요."]], [["Mother is very unhappy about this affair of the Lupexes.", "어머니는 이 루펙스네 일로 몹시 마음이 상해 계세요."], ["She says that if you were here to advise her she should not mind it so much.", "당신이 여기 계셔서 조언해 주신다면 이렇게까지 힘들지는 않을 텐데 하고 말씀하세요."], ["It is very hard upon her, for she does strive to make the house respectable and comfortable for everybody.", "어머니께는 정말 가혹한 일이에요. 모두를 위해 이 집을 번듯하고 편안하게 꾸리려고 그토록 애쓰시는데 말이에요."], ["I would send my duty and love to your dear mamma, if I only knew her, as I hope I shall do one day, and to your sister, and to L. D.", "당신의 사랑하는 어머님을 내가 알기만 한다면—언젠가는 알게 되기를 바라지만요—그분께 내 문안과 사랑을 보내 드리고 싶고, 당신 누이동생에게도, 그리고 우리가 서로 어떤 사이인지 그이에게 이야기하실 생각이 있으시다면 L."], ["also, if you like to tell her how we are situated together.", "D.에게도 보내 드리고 싶어요."], ["So, now, no more from your", "그럼 이만, 당신의"]], [["Always affectionate sweetheart,", "언제나 애정 어린 당신의 연인,"]], [["Poor Eames did not feel the least gratified by any part of this fond letter; but the last paragraph of it was the worst.", "가엾은 임스는 이 다정한 편지의 어느 대목에서도 조금도 흐뭇함을 느끼지 못했다. 그러나 그중에서도 마지막 문단이 가장 고약했다."], ["Was it to be endured by him that this woman should send her love to his mother and to his sister, and even to Lily Dale!", "이런 여자가 자기 어머니에게, 자기 누이동생에게, 심지어 릴리 데일에게까지 사랑을 보낸다는 것을 그가 어찌 참을 수 있단 말인가!"], ["He felt that there was a pollution in the very mention of Lily's name by such an one as Amelia Roper.", "어밀리아 로퍼 같은 사람이 릴리의 이름을 입에 올린다는 것만으로도 거기에는 더러움이 묻는 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["And yet Amelia Roper was, as she had assured him,--his own.", "그런데도 어밀리아 로퍼는, 그녀가 그에게 장담한 대로, 그의 사람이었다."], ["Much as he disliked her at the present moment, he did believe that he was--her own.", "지금 이 순간 그녀가 아무리 싫었다 해도 그는 자기가 그녀의 사람이라고 믿고 있었다."], ["He did feel that she had obtained a certain property in him, and that his destiny in life would tie him to her.", "그는 그녀가 자기에 대해 일종의 소유권을 얻어 냈으며 자기의 운명이 자기를 그녀에게 묶어 놓으리라고 느꼈다."], ["He had said very few words of love to her at any time--very few, at least, that were themselves of any moment; but among those few there had undoubtedly been one or two in which he had told her that he loved her.", "그는 지금껏 그녀에게 사랑의 말이라고는 거의 하지 않았다—적어도 그 자체로 무게가 나갈 만한 말은 거의 하지 않았다. 그러나 그 몇 마디 가운데 자기가 그녀를 사랑한다고 말한 것이 한두 번쯤 분명히 있었다."], ["And he had written to her that fatal note!", "게다가 그는 그녀에게 그 치명적인 쪽지를 써 보내지 않았던가!"], ["Upon the whole, would it not be as well for him to go out to the great reservoir behind Guestwick, by which the Hamersham Canal was fed with its waters, and put an end to his miserable existence?", "이러니저러니 해도, 게스트윅 뒤편에 있는 저 큰 저수지로—해머샴 운하에 물을 대 주는 그 저수지로—나가서 이 비참한 목숨을 끝내 버리는 편이 그에게 낫지 않겠는가?"]], [["On that same day he did write a letter to Fisher, and he wrote also to Cradell.", "그날 그는 피셔에게 편지를 썼고, 크래들에게도 편지를 썼다."], ["As to those letters he felt no difficulty.", "그 편지들에 대해서는 아무 어려움도 느끼지 않았다."], ["To Fisher he declared his belief that Cradell was innocent as he was himself as regarded Mrs.", "피셔에게 그는 루펙스 부인에 관한 한 크래들이 자기 자신만큼이나 결백하다고 믿는다고 밝혔다."], ["Lupex. \"I don't think he is the sort of man to make up to a married woman,\"", "“그 친구는 혼인한 여자에게 수작을 걸 만한 부류의 사내가 아닙니다.”"], ["he said, somewhat to Cradell's displeasure, when the letter reached the Income-tax Office; for that gentleman was not averse to the reputation for success in love which the little adventure was, as he thought, calculated to give him among his brother clerks.", "그는 그렇게 썼는데, 그 편지가 소득세 사무국에 이르렀을 때 크래들에게는 다소 언짢은 말이었다. 이 자그마한 모험이 제 딴에는 동료 서기들 사이에서 자기에게 연애에 성공한 사내라는 평판을 안겨 주게 되어 있다고 여겼고, 그 평판이 이 신사에게는 싫지 않았기 때문이다."], ["At the first bursting of the shell, when that desperately jealous man was raging in the parlour, incensed by the fumes both of wine and love, Cradell had felt that the affair was disagreeably painful.", "폭탄이 처음 터졌을 때, 그 지독하게 질투에 사로잡힌 사내가 술기운과 애욕에 함께 달아올라 아래층 거실에서 길길이 뛰던 그때에는 크래들도 이 일이 지긋지긋하게 괴롭다고 느꼈다."], ["But on the morning of the third day--for he had passed two nights on his friend Fisher's sofa--he had begun to be somewhat proud of it, and did not dislike to hear Mrs. Lupex's name in the mouths of the other clerks.", "그러나 사흘째 되는 날 아침—그는 벗 피셔의 소파에서 두 밤을 지냈다—그는 이 일이 다소 자랑스러워지기 시작했고, 다른 서기들의 입에서 루펙스 부인의 이름이 오르내리는 것도 싫지 않게 되었다."], ["When, therefore, Fisher read to him the letter from Guestwick, he hardly was pleased with his friend's tone.", "그러니 피셔가 게스트윅에서 온 편지를 읽어 주었을 때 그는 벗의 말투가 그리 달갑지 않았다."], ["\"Ha, ha, ha,\"", "“하하하,”"], ["said he, laughing.", "그가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"That's just what I wanted him to say.", "“바로 내가 저 친구한테 시키고 싶었던 말이 저거야."], ["Make up to a married woman, indeed.", "혼인한 여자에게 수작이라니, 원."], ["No; I'm the last man in London to do that sort of thing.\"", "아니지. 그런 짓을 할 사람으로 치면 나는 런던에서 맨 꼴찌일걸.”"]], [["\"Upon my word, Caudle, I think you are,\"", "“참말이지, 코들, 나도 자네가 그렇다고 생각하네.”"], ["said Fisher; \"the very last man.\"", "피셔가 말했다. “맨 꼴찌지.”"]], [["And then poor Cradell was not happy.", "그러고 나서 가엾은 크래들은 마음이 편치 않았다. 그날 오후 그는 용감하게 버턴 크레슨트로 가서 거기서 저녁을 먹었다."], ["On that afternoon he boldly went to Burton Crescent, and ate his dinner there. Neither Mr.", "루펙스 씨도 루펙스 부인도 보이지 않았고, 로퍼 부인이 그에게 그들의 이름을 입에 올리지도 않았다."], ["nor Mrs. Lupex were to be seen, nor were their names mentioned to him by Mrs.", "저녁나절이 되어 그는 용기를 내어 스프루스 양에게 두 사람이 어디 있느냐고 물어보았다."], ["Roper. In the course of the evening he did pluck up courage to ask Miss Spruce where they were; but that ancient lady merely shook her head solemnly, and declared that she knew nothing about such goings on--no, not she.", "그러나 그 노부인은 그저 엄숙하게 고개를 저으며 자기는 그런 짓거리에 대해서는 아무것도 모른다고, 아무렴 자기는 모른다고 잘라 말했다."]], [["But what was John Eames to do as to that letter from Amelia Roper?", "그런데 어밀리아 로퍼에게서 온 그 편지를 두고 존 임스는 어떻게 해야 한단 말인가?"], ["He felt that any answer to it would be very dangerous, and yet that he could not safely leave it unanswered.", "그는 어떤 답장이든 매우 위험하리라고 느꼈으나, 그렇다고 답장 없이 내버려 두는 것도 안전하지 않다고 느꼈다."], ["He walked off by himself across Guestwick Common, and through the woods of Guestwick Manor, up by the big avenue of elms in Lord De Guest's park, trying to resolve how he might rescue himself from this scrape.", "그는 혼자서 게스트윅 공유지를 가로질러, 게스트윅 저택의 숲을 지나, 드 게스트 경의 정원에 늘어선 커다란 느릅나무 가로수 길을 따라 걸어 올라가면서 이 곤경에서 어떻게 빠져나갈 수 있을지 궁리해 보았다."], ["Here, over the same ground, he had wandered scores of times in his earlier years, when he knew nothing beyond the innocency of his country home, thinking of Lily Dale, and swearing to himself that she should be his wife.", "이곳 바로 이 땅 위를 그는 젊은 시절에 수십 번도 더 거닐었다. 시골집의 순진함 말고는 아무것도 알지 못하던 그 시절에, 릴리 데일을 생각하며 그녀를 제 아내로 삼겠노라고 스스로에게 맹세하면서."], ["Here he had strung together his rhymes, and fed his ambition with high hopes, building gorgeous castles in the air, in all of which Lilian reigned as a queen; and though in those days he had known himself to be awkward, poor, uncared for by any in the world except his mother and his sister, yet he had been happy in his hopes--happy in his hopes, even though he had never taught himself really to believe that they would be realized.", "이곳에서 그는 시구를 엮었고 드높은 희망으로 제 야심을 길렀으며 허공에 화려한 성을 지어 올렸으니, 그 성마다 릴리언이 여왕으로 군림하고 있었다. 그리고 그 시절 그는 자기가 어설프고 가난하며 어머니와 누이동생 말고는 세상 누구의 눈길도 받지 못한다는 것을 알고 있었으면서도 제 희망 속에서 행복했다—그 희망이 이루어지리라고 정말로 믿도록 스스로를 가르친 적은 한 번도 없었으면서도 그 희망 속에서 행복했던 것이다."], ["But now there was nothing in his hopes or thoughts to make him happy.", "그러나 이제는 그의 희망에도 생각에도 그를 행복하게 해 줄 것이 아무것도 없었다."], ["Everything was black, and wretched, and ruinous.", "모든 것이 캄캄하고 비참하고 무너져 내려 있었다."], ["What would it matter, after all, even if he should marry Amelia Roper, seeing that Lily was to be given to another?", "릴리가 다른 사내에게 주어질 바에야, 그가 어밀리아 로퍼와 혼인한들 결국 무슨 상관이란 말인가?"], ["But then the idea of Amelia as he had seen her that night through the chink in the door came upon his memory, and he confessed to himself that life with such a wife as that would be a living death.", "그러나 그때 그날 밤 문틈으로 보았던 어밀리아의 모습이 기억에 떠올랐고, 그는 그런 아내와 함께하는 삶이란 살아 있는 죽음이 되리라고 스스로 시인했다."]], [["At one moment he thought that he would tell his mother everything, and leave her to write an answer to Amelia's letter.", "어느 순간 그는 어머니에게 모든 것을 털어놓고 어밀리아의 편지에 대한 답장은 어머니께 맡겨 버릴까 하고 생각했다."], ["Should the worst come to the worst, the Ropers could not absolutely destroy him.", "최악의 경우가 닥친다 해도 로퍼네 사람들이 그를 아주 결딴낼 수는 없을 터였다."], ["That they could bring an action against him, and have him locked up for a term of years, and dismissed from his office, and exposed in all the newspapers, he seemed to know.", "그들이 그를 걸어 소송을 낼 수 있다는 것, 그를 몇 해 동안 옥에 가둘 수 있다는 것, 그를 사무국에서 쫓겨나게 하고 온갖 신문에 이름이 나게 할 수 있다는 것쯤은 그도 알고 있는 듯싶었다."], ["That might all, however, be endured, if only the gauntlet could be thrown down for him by some one else.", "그러나 그 모든 것도 누군가 다른 사람이 그를 대신해 장갑을 던져 주기만 한다면 견뎌 낼 수 있을 것 같았다."], ["The one thing which he felt that he could not do was, to write to a girl whom he had professed to love, and tell her that he did not love her.", "그가 도저히 할 수 없다고 느낀 단 한 가지는, 사랑한다고 공언했던 처녀에게 편지를 써서 이제는 사랑하지 않는다고 말하는 일이었다."], ["He knew that he could not himself form such words upon the paper; nor, as he was well aware, could he himself find the courage to tell her to her face that he had changed his mind.", "그런 말을 제 손으로 종이 위에 적을 수는 없다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 또한 그 자신이 잘 알고 있듯이, 마음이 변했노라고 그녀의 얼굴을 마주 보고 말할 용기도 낼 수 없었다."], ["He knew that he must become the victim of his Amelia, unless he could find some friendly knight to do battle in his favour; and then again he thought of his mother.", "자기 대신 싸워 줄 다정한 기사를 찾아내지 못하는 한 자기는 제 어밀리아의 제물이 되고 말리라는 것을 그는 알았다. 그러다가 그는 또다시 어머니를 떠올렸다."]], [["But when he returned home he was as far as ever from any resolve to tell her how he was situated.", "그러나 집에 돌아왔을 때 그는 자기 처지를 어머니에게 알리겠다는 결심에서 여전히 조금도 가까워지지 않은 채였다."], ["I may say that his walk had done him no good, and that he had not made up his mind to anything.", "그 산책은 그에게 아무 도움이 되지 못했고 그는 아무것도 마음을 정하지 못했다고 말해 두어야겠다."], ["He had been building those pernicious castles in the air during more than half the time; not castles in the building of which he could make himself happy, as he had done in the old days, but black castles, with cruel dungeons, into which hardly a ray of light could find its way.", "그는 그 시간의 절반이 넘도록 저 해로운 공중누각을 짓고 있었다. 그것도 옛날처럼 그 성을 지으면서 스스로 행복해질 수 있는 그런 성이 아니라, 잔혹한 지하 감옥이 딸린, 빛 한 줄기 겨우 들까 말까 한 캄캄한 성이었다."], ["In all these edifices his imagination pictured to him Lily as the wife of Mr. Crosbie.", "그 모든 건물 안에서 그의 상상은 릴리를 크로즈비 씨의 아내로 그려 보였다."], ["He accepted that as a fact, and then went to work in his misery, making her as wretched as himself, through the misconduct and harshness of her husband.", "그는 그것을 기정사실로 받아들이고 나서, 제 비참함 속에서 그 남편의 못된 행실과 모진 마음으로 그녀를 저 자신만큼이나 비참하게 만드는 일에 몰두했다."], ["He tried to think, and to resolve what he would do; but there is no task so hard as that of thinking, when the mind has an objection to the matter brought before it.", "그는 생각을 해 보려고, 어떻게 할지 마음을 정해 보려고 애썼다. 그러나 눈앞에 놓인 일에 마음이 도리질을 칠 때, 생각하는 일만큼 어려운 일은 없다."], ["The mind, under such circumstances, is like a horse that is brought to the water, but refuses to drink.", "그럴 때의 마음이란 물가에까지 끌려왔으나 마시기를 마다하는 말과 같다."], ["So Johnny returned to his home, still doubting whether or no he would answer Amelia's letter.", "그리하여 조니는 어밀리아의 편지에 답장을 할지 말지 여전히 망설이면서 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["And if he did not answer it, how would he conduct himself on his return to Burton Crescent?", "그리고 답장을 하지 않는다면, 버턴 크레슨트로 돌아갔을 때 그는 어떻게 처신할 것인가?"]], [["I need hardly say that Miss Roper, in writing her letter, had been aware of all this, and that Johnny's position had been carefully prepared for him by--his affectionate sweetheart.", "로퍼 양이 편지를 쓰면서 이 모든 것을 알고 있었다는 것, 그리고 조니의 처지가 그를 위해—애정 어린 그의 연인에 의해—빈틈없이 마련되어 있었다는 것은 굳이 내가 말할 필요도 없으리라."]], [["# CHAPTER XI. SOCIAL LIFE.", "# 제11장. 사교 생활"]], [["Mr. and Mrs.", "루펙스 씨 부부는 크래들이 로퍼 부인의 후한 밥상으로 돌아오던 바로 그날, 부부다운 더없는 화목 속에서 함께 스위트브레드를 먹었다."], ["Lupex had eaten a sweetbread together in much connubial bliss on that day which had seen Cradell returning to Mrs. Roper's hospitable board.", "두 사람은 극장 근처에서 스위트브레드와 그 밖의 제철 별미 몇 가지를 나란히 먹었고, 온갖 앙금을 쓴 맥주와 물 탄 브랜디로 씻어 내렸다."], ["They had together eaten a sweetbread, with some other delicacies of the season, in the neighbourhood of the theatre, and had washed down all unkindness with bitter beer and brandy-and-water.", "그러나 이 화해에 대해 크래들은 아무것도 들은 바가 없었다."], ["But of this reconciliation Cradell had not heard; and when he saw them come together into the drawing-room, a few minutes after the question he had addressed to Miss Spruce, he was certainly surprised.", "그래서 그가 스프루스 양에게 그 질문을 던지고 몇 분 지나지 않아 두 사람이 나란히 응접실로 들어오는 것을 보았을 때, 그는 분명 놀랐다."]], [["Lupex was not an ill-natured man, nor one naturally savage by disposition.", "루펙스는 심성이 고약한 사람도 아니었고, 천성이 사나운 사람도 아니었다."], ["He was a man fond of sweetbread and little dinners, and one to whom hot brandy-and-water was too dear.", "그는 스위트브레드와 자그마한 만찬을 좋아하는 사람이었고, 뜨겁게 데운 물 탄 브랜디가 너무나 소중한 사람이었다."], ["Had the wife of his bosom been a good helpmate to him, he might have gone through the world, if not respectably, at any rate without open disgrace.", "그의 품에 안긴 아내가 그에게 좋은 반려가 되어 주었더라면, 그는 세상을 살아가되 남의 존경까지는 못 받더라도 적어도 드러내 놓고 망신은 당하지 않은 채 지나갈 수 있었을 것이다."], ["But she was a woman who left a man no solace except that to be found in brandy-and-water.", "그러나 그녀는 사내에게 물 탄 브랜디에서 얻는 것 말고는 아무 위안도 남겨 주지 않는 여자였다."], ["For eight years they had been man and wife; and sometimes--I grieve to say it--he had been driven almost to hope that she would commit a married woman's last sin, and leave him.", "여덟 해 동안 두 사람은 부부로 지냈다. 그리고 때때로—이런 말을 하기가 나는 참으로 애석하지만—그는 그녀가 유부녀로서 지을 수 있는 마지막 죄를 짓고 자기를 떠나 주기를 바라다시피 하는 지경까지 몰리곤 했다."], ["In his misery, any mode of escape would have been welcome to him.", "그 비참함 속에서라면 어떤 탈출구든 그에게는 반가웠으리라."], ["Had his energy been sufficient he would have taken his scene-painting capabilities off to Australia,--or to the farthest shifting of scenes known on the world's stage.", "기력만 넉넉했더라면 그는 무대 배경을 그리는 제 재주를 챙겨 오스트레일리아로—아니, 세상이라는 무대에 알려진 가장 먼 배경 전환의 자리로 떠나 버렸을 것이다."], ["But he was an easy, listless, self-indulgent man; and at any moment, let his misery be as keen as might be, a little dinner, a few soft words, and a glass of brandy-and-water would bring him round.", "그러나 그는 무르고 게으르고 제 몸을 아끼는 사람이었다. 그래서 어느 순간이든, 그의 비참함이 아무리 사무친다 한들, 자그마한 만찬 한 상과 몇 마디 부드러운 말과 물 탄 브랜디 한 잔이면 그를 도로 돌려놓을 수 있었다."], ["The second glass would make him the fondest husband living; but the third would restore to him the memory of all his wrongs, and give him courage against his wife or all the world,--even to the detriment of the furniture around him, should a stray poker chance to meet his hand.", "두 잔째는 그를 세상에서 가장 다정한 남편으로 만들어 주었다. 그러나 석 잔째는 그에게 자기가 당한 온갖 억울함의 기억을 되살려 주고, 아내에게든 온 세상에게든 맞설 용기를 주었다—손끝에 어쩌다 부지깽이라도 걸리는 날이면 주위의 가구까지 결딴내 가면서."], ["All these peculiarities of his character were not, however, known to Cradell; and when our friend saw him enter the drawing-room with his wife on his arm, he was astonished.", "그러나 그의 성품이 지닌 이런 특징들을 크래들은 알지 못했다. 그리하여 우리 벗은 그가 아내를 팔에 낀 채 응접실로 들어서는 것을 보고 깜짝 놀랐던 것이다."]], [["\"Mr. Cradell, your hand,\"", "“크래들 씨, 손을 주시오.”"], ["said Lupex, who had advanced as far as the second glass of brandy-and-water, but had not been allowed to go beyond it.", "루펙스가 말했다. 그는 물 탄 브랜디 두 잔째까지는 나아갔으나 그 이상은 허락받지 못한 참이었다."], ["\"There has been a misunderstanding between us; let it be forgotten.\"", "“우리 사이에 오해가 있었소. 그건 잊읍시다.”"]], [["\"Mr. Cradell, if I know him,\"", "“내가 아는 크래들 씨라면,”"], ["said the lady, \"is too much the gentleman to bear any anger when a gentleman has offered him his hand.\"", "부인이 말했다. “신사가 손을 내미는 마당에 노여움을 품고 있기에는 너무나 신사다운 분이지요.”"]], [["\"Oh, I'm sure,\"", "“아, 그럼요.”"], ["said Cradell, \"I'm quite--indeed, I'm delighted to find there's nothing wrong after all.\"", "크래들이 말했다. “저야 정말—아니, 결국 아무 일도 없었다니 참말 기쁩니다.”"], ["And then he shook hands with both of them; whereupon Miss Spruce got up, curtseyed low, and also shook hands with the husband and wife.", "그러고 나서 그는 두 사람과 차례로 악수했다. 그러자 스프루스 양이 일어나 깊이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는 역시 부부와 악수를 나누었다."]], [["\"You're not a married man, Mr. Cradell,\"", "“크래들 씨는 장가든 사람이 아니니,”"], ["said Lupex, \"and, therefore, you cannot understand the workings of a husband's heart.", "루펙스가 말했다. “남편 된 자의 가슴속에서 무엇이 움직이는지 이해하지 못하실 거요."], ["There have been moments when my regard for that woman has been too much for me.\"", "저 여자를 향한 내 마음이 나로서 감당하기에 너무 벅찼던 순간들이 있었소.”"]], [["\"Now, Lupex, don't,\"", "“자, 루펙스, 그만해요.”"], ["said she, playfully tapping him with an old parasol which she still held.", "그녀가 아직 손에 들고 있던 낡은 양산으로 그를 장난스레 툭툭 치며 말했다."]], [["\"And I do not hesitate to say that my regard for her was too much for me on that night when I sent for you to the dining-room.\"", "“그리고 그날 밤 내가 당신을 식당으로 불러 내렸을 때, 그녀를 향한 내 마음이 나로서 감당하기에 너무 벅찼다는 말도 나는 서슴지 않고 하겠소.”"]], [["\"I'm glad it's all put right now,\"", "“이제 다 잘 풀렸다니 기쁩니다.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."]], [["\"Very glad, indeed,\"", "“참말 다행입니다.”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."]], [["\"And, therefore, we need not say any more about it,\"", "“그러니 그 이야기는 더 할 것 없어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Lupex.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"One word,\"", "“한마디만.”"], ["said Lupex, waving his hand. \"Mr.", "루펙스가 손을 내저으며 말했다."], ["Cradell, I greatly rejoice that you did not obey my summons on that night.", "“크래들 씨, 그날 밤 당신이 내 부름에 응하지 않은 것을 나는 참으로 다행으로 여기오."], ["Had you done so,--I confess it now,--had you done so, blood would have been the consequence.", "만일 응했더라면—이제야 털어놓지만—만일 응했더라면 피를 보고야 말았을 거요."], ["I was mistaken.", "내가 잘못 알았소."], ["I acknowledge my mistake;--but blood would have been the consequence.\"", "내 잘못은 인정하오. 하지만 피를 보고야 말았을 거요.”"]], [["\"Dear, dear, dear,\"", "“저런, 저런, 저런.”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."]], [["\"Miss Spruce,\"", "“스프루스 양,”"], ["continued Lupex, \"there are moments when the heart becomes too strong for a man.\"", "루펙스가 말을 이었다. “사람의 가슴이 그 사람으로서는 감당 못 할 만큼 거세지는 순간이 있는 법이지요.”"]], [["\"I dare say,\"", "“그렇겠지요.”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."]], [["\"Now, Lupex, that will do,\"", "“자, 루펙스, 이제 됐어요.”"], ["said his wife.", "그의 아내가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; that will do. But I think it right to tell Mr.", "“그래, 이제 됐지."], ["Cradell that I am glad he did not come to me. Your friend, Mr.", "하지만 크래들 씨한테는 그가 나한테 오지 않아 다행이라고 말해 두는 것이 도리라고 생각하오."], ["Cradell, did me the honour of calling on me at the theatre yesterday, at half-past four; but I was in the slings then, and could not very well come down to him.", "크래들 씨, 당신 벗이 어제 네 시 반에 극장으로 나를 찾아오는 수고를 해 주셨소. 그런데 그때 나는 밧줄에 매달려 있던 참이라 도무지 내려갈 형편이 못 되었소."], ["I shall be happy to see you both any day at five, and to bury all unkindness with a chop and glass at the Pot and Poker, in Bow-street.\"", "언제든 다섯 시에 두 분을 다 뵈면 반갑겠고, 보 스트리트의 「냄비와 부지깽이」에서 고기 한 점에 술 한 잔으로 지난 앙금은 다 묻어 버립시다.”"]], [["\"I'm sure you're very kind,\"", "“참으로 친절하십니다.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."]], [["\"And Mrs.", "“그리고 루펙스 부인도 우리와 함께할 거요."], ["Lupex will join us.", "「냄비와 부지깽이」"], ["There's a delightful little snuggery upstairs at the Pot and Poker; and if Miss Spruce will condescend to--\"", "이층에는 아주 아늑한 골방이 하나 있소. 그리고 스프루스 양께서도 황송하게 함께해 주신다면—”"]], [["\"Oh, I'm an old woman, sir.\"", "“아이고, 나로 말하면 늙은 여자 아닙니까, 어르신.”"]], [["\"No--no--no,\"", "“아니—아니—아니오.”"], ["said Lupex, \"I deny that.", "루펙스가 말했다."], ["Come, Cradell, what do you say?--just a snug little dinner for four, you know.\"", "“그건 인정 못 하오. 자, 크래들, 어떻소?—넷이서 오붓하게 자그마한 만찬 한 상 말이오.”"]], [["It was, no doubt, pleasant to see Mr. Lupex in his present mood,--much pleasanter than in that other mood of which blood would have been the consequence; but pleasant as he now was, it was, nevertheless, apparent that he was not quite sober.", "지금 이런 기분에 잠긴 루펙스 씨를 보는 일은 분명 유쾌했다—피를 보고야 말았을 그 다른 기분에 잠겼을 때보다야 훨씬 유쾌했다."], ["Cradell, therefore, did not settle the day for the little dinner; but merely remarked that he should be very happy at some future day.", "그러나 그가 지금 유쾌하기는 해도, 그럼에도 그가 아주 말짱한 정신은 아니라는 것이 뻔히 드러났다. 그래서 크래들은 그 자그마한 만찬의 날짜를 정하지 않고, 그저 언젠가 앞날에 기꺼이 그러겠노라고만 말해 두었다."]], [["\"And now, Lupex, suppose you get off to bed,\"", "“자 이제, 루펙스, 그만 자러 가는 게 좋겠어요.”"], ["said his wife.", "그의 아내가 말했다."], ["\"You've had a very trying day, you know.\"", "“오늘 참 고단한 하루였잖아요.”"]], [["\"And you, ducky?\"", "“그럼 당신은, 여보?”"]], [["\"I shall come presently.", "“나도 곧 갈 거예요."], ["Now don't be making a fool of yourself, but get yourself off.", "자, 우스운 꼴 보이지 말고 어서 올라가요."], ["Come--\"", "어서—”"], ["and she stood close up against the open door, waiting for him to pass.", "그러고서 그녀는 열린 문에 바싹 붙어 서서 그가 지나가기를 기다렸다."]], [["\"I rather think I shall remain where I am, and have a glass of something hot,\"", "“나는 아무래도 여기 그대로 있으면서 뜨거운 걸 한 잔 할까 싶은데.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Lupex, do you want to aggravate me again?\"", "“루펙스, 또 나를 돋우고 싶은 거예요?”"], ["said the lady, and she looked at him with a glance of her eye which he thoroughly understood.", "부인이 그렇게 말하며 그를 흘겨보았는데, 그 눈길이 무슨 뜻인지 그는 훤히 알아들었다."], ["He was not in a humour for fighting, nor was he at present desirous of blood; so he resolved to go.", "그는 싸울 기분이 아니었고, 지금은 피를 보고 싶은 마음도 없었다. 그래서 그는 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["But as he went he prepared himself for new battles.", "그러나 그는 가면서 새로운 싸움을 준비했다."], ["\"I shall do something desperate, I am sure; I know I shall,\"", "“나는 무슨 끔찍한 일을 저지르고 말 거야, 틀림없이. 내가 잘 알아.”"], ["he said, as he pulled off his boots.", "그는 장화를 벗으며 그렇게 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Cradell,\"", "“아이고, 크래들 씨,”"], ["said Mrs. Lupex as soon as she had closed the door behind her retreating husband, \"how am I ever to look you in the face again after the events of these last memorable days?\"", "물러가는 남편의 등 뒤로 문을 닫자마자 루펙스 부인이 말했다."], ["And then she seated herself on the sofa, and hid her face in a cambric handkerchief.", "“이 잊지 못할 며칠 동안 일어난 일이 있고서야 내가 무슨 낯으로 당신 얼굴을 다시 본단 말이에요?” 그러고 나서 그녀는 소파에 앉아 고운 삼베 손수건에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["\"As for that,\"", "“그거야,”"], ["said Cradell, \"what does it signify,--among friends like us, you know?\"", "크래들이 말했다. “우리 같은 벗들 사이에 무슨 대수겠습니까?”"]], [["\"But that it should be known at your office,--as of course it is, because of the gentleman that went down to him at the theatre!--I don't think I shall ever survive it.\"", "“하지만 그 일이 당신 관청에까지 알려졌으니—물론 알려졌겠죠, 그 신사분이 극장으로 그이를 찾아 내려갔으니까요!—나는 도무지 견디고 살아남을 것 같지가 않아요.”"]], [["\"You see I was obliged to send somebody, Mrs. Lupex.\"", "“보시다시피 저는 누구든 보내지 않을 수 없었습니다, 루펙스 부인.”"]], [["\"I'm not finding fault, Mr. Cradell.", "“탓하는 게 아니에요, 크래들 씨."], ["I know very well that in my melancholy position I have no right to find fault, and I don't pretend to understand gentlemen's feelings towards each other.", "이 서글픈 처지에 내가 남을 탓할 자격이 없다는 건 나도 잘 알아요. 그리고 신사분들끼리 서로 어떤 마음인지 안다고 내세울 생각도 없어요."], ["But to have had my name mentioned up with yours in that way is-- Oh! Mr.", "하지만 내 이름이 그런 식으로 당신 이름과 나란히 오르내렸다는 게—아아!"], ["Cradell, I don't know how I'm ever to look you in the face again.\"", "크래들 씨, 내가 무슨 낯으로 당신 얼굴을 다시 볼지 모르겠어요.”"], ["And again she buried hers in her pocket-handkerchief.", "그러고서 그녀는 또다시 제 얼굴을 손수건에 묻었다."]], [["\"Handsome is as handsome does,\"", "“고운 것은 고운 행실에 달렸지요.”"], ["said Miss Spruce; and there was that in her tone of voice which seemed to convey much hidden meaning.", "스프루스 양이 말했다. 그 말투에는 감춰 둔 뜻이 잔뜩 실린 듯한 무엇이 있었다."]], [["\"Exactly so, Miss Spruce,\" said Mrs.", "“바로 그래요, 스프루스 양.”"], ["Lupex; \"and that's my only comfort at the present moment.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다. “그게 지금 이 순간 내 유일한 위안이에요."], ["Mr. Cradell is a gentleman who would scorn to take advantage--I'm quite sure of that.\"", "크래들 씨는 남의 약점을 이용하는 짓 따위는 하찮게 여기실 신사분이니까요—그건 내가 잘 알지요.”"], ["And then she did contrive to look at him over the edge of the hand which held the handkerchief.", "그러고서 그녀는 손수건을 쥔 손 너머로 그를 건너다보는 데 용케 성공했다."]], [["\"That I wouldn't, I'm sure,\" said Cradell.", "“그런 짓이야 제가 할 리 없지요, 정말입니다.”"], ["\"That is to say--\"", "크래들이 말했다."], ["And then he paused.", "“그러니까 제 말은—”"], ["He did not wish to get into a scrape about Mrs. Lupex.", "그러고서 그는 말을 멈추었다."], ["He was by no means anxious to encounter her husband in one of his fits of jealousy.", "그는 루펙스 부인 일로 곤경에 빠지고 싶지는 않았다. 질투가 발작한 그녀의 남편과 맞닥뜨리는 일이야 결코 바라지 않았다."], ["But he did like the idea of being talked of as the admirer of a married woman, and he did like the brightness of the lady's eyes.", "그러나 유부녀를 흠모하는 사내로 남의 입에 오르내리는 것은 마음에 들었고, 그 부인의 반짝이는 눈도 마음에 들었다."], ["When the unfortunate moth in his semi-blindness whisks himself and his wings within the flame of the candle, and finds himself mutilated and tortured, he even then will not take the lesson, but returns again and again till he is destroyed.", "반쯤 눈이 먼 가엾은 나방이 촛불의 불꽃 속으로 제 몸과 날개를 던져 넣었다가 몸이 상하고 지져지는 고통을 겪고도, 그러고서도 그 교훈을 받아들이지 못한 채 아주 결딴날 때까지 몇 번이고 되돌아가는 일이 있다."], ["Such a moth was poor Cradell.", "가엾은 크래들이 바로 그런 나방이었다."], ["There was no warmth to be got by him from that flame.", "그 불꽃에서 그가 얻을 온기라고는 없었다."], ["There was no beauty in the light,--not even the false brilliance of unhallowed love.", "그 불빛에는 아름다움이 없었다—떳떳지 못한 사랑의 그 거짓 광채조차 없었다."], ["Injury might come to him,--a pernicious clipping of the wings, which might destroy all power of future flight; injury, and not improbably destruction, if he should persevere.", "그에게 올 것은 상함이었으니—앞으로 날 힘을 아주 없애 버릴, 날개를 못 쓰게 잘라 내는 상함이었다. 고집을 부린다면 상함, 그리고 십중팔구 파멸이었다."], ["But one may say that no single hour of happiness could accrue to him from his intimacy with Mrs.", "그러나 루펙스 부인과의 친분에서 그에게 단 한 시간의 행복도 돌아올 수 없었다고 말해도 좋으리라."], ["Lupex. He felt for her no love.", "그는 그녀에게 아무런 사랑도 느끼지 않았다."], ["He was afraid of her, and, in many respects, disliked her.", "그는 그녀가 두려웠고, 여러 가지로 그녀가 싫었다."], ["But to him, in his moth-like weakness, ignorance, and blindness, it seemed to be a great thing that he should be allowed to fly near the candle.", "그러나 나방 같은 그의 나약함과 무지와 눈멂 속에서는, 촛불 가까이 날아다니도록 허락받는다는 것이 대단한 일로 여겨졌다."], ["Oh! my friends, if you will but think of it, how many of you have been moths, and are now going about ungracefully with wings more or less burnt off, and with bodies sadly scorched!", "아아, 내 벗들이여, 생각해 보기만 한다면, 그대들 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 나방이었으며, 지금 날개가 어지간히 타 버린 채 볼품없이 돌아다니고 있으며, 몸뚱이가 딱하게도 그을려 있는가!"]], [["But before Mr. Cradell could make up his mind whether or no he would take advantage of the present opportunity for another dip into the flame of the candle,--in regard to which proceeding, however, he could not but feel that the presence of Miss Spruce was objectionable,--the door of the room was opened, and Amelia Roper joined the party.", "그러나 크래들 씨가 촛불의 불꽃 속으로 한 번 더 뛰어들 이번 기회를 붙잡을지 말지 마음을 정하기도 전에—하기야 그 일에 관해서는 스프루스 양이 그 자리에 있다는 것이 아무래도 거슬린다는 느낌을 지울 수 없었지만—방문이 열리고 어밀리아 로퍼가 이 자리에 끼어들었다."]], [["\"Oh, indeed; Mrs.", "“어머, 이런."], ["Lupex,\"", "루펙스 부인.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"And Mr. Cradell!\"", "“게다가 크래들 씨까지!”"]], [["\"And Miss Spruce, my dear,\" said Mrs.", "“그리고 스프루스 양도 계세요, 아가씨.”"], ["Lupex, pointing to the ancient lady.", "루펙스 부인이 그 노부인을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["\"I'm only an old woman,\"", "“나는 그저 늙은 여자일 뿐입니다.”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes; I see Miss Spruce,\"", "“그럼요, 스프루스 양 계신 거 보여요.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."], ["\"I was not hinting at anything, I can assure you.\"", "“제가 무슨 뜻을 담아 한 말은 아니었어요, 정말이에요.”"]], [["\"I should think not, my dear,\"", "“그렇고말고요, 아가씨.”"], ["said Mrs. Lupex.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Only I didn't know that you two were quite-- That is, when last I heard about it, I fancied-- But if the quarrel's made up, there's nobody more rejoiced than I am.\"", "“다만 저는 두 분이 아주— 그러니까, 제가 마지막으로 들었을 때는 그런 줄로 알았거든요— 하지만 다툼이 다 풀렸다면 저만큼 기뻐할 사람도 없죠.”"]], [["\"The quarrel is made up,\"", "“다툼은 다 풀렸습니다.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."]], [["\"If Mr. Lupex is satisfied, I'm sure I am,\"", "“루펙스 씨가 흡족하시다면야 저도 그렇고말고요.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Mr. Lupex is satisfied,\"", "“루펙스 씨는 흡족해하세요.”"], ["said Mrs.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다."], ["Lupex; \"and let me tell you, my dear, seeing that you are expecting to get married yourself--\"", "“그리고 아가씨, 내가 한마디 해 두죠. 아가씨도 시집갈 날을 기다리는 처지인 모양이니—”"]], [["\"Mrs. Lupex, I'm not expecting to get married,--not particularly, by any means.\"", "“루펙스 부인, 저는 시집갈 날을 기다리는 처지가 아니에요—적어도 그리 대단히는 아니라고요.”"]], [["\"Oh, I thought you were.", "“어머, 나는 그런 줄 알았죠."], ["And let me tell you, that when you've got a husband of your own, you won't find it so easy to keep everything straight.", "그럼 이 말은 해 두겠어요. 아가씨가 제 남편이라는 걸 갖게 되면, 만사를 곧게 건사하기가 그리 쉽지 않다는 걸 알게 될 거예요."], ["That's the worst of these lodgings; if there is any little thing, everybody knows it.", "하숙이란 게 그래서 못쓰는 거죠. 조그만 일이라도 있으면 온 집안이 다 알아 버리거든요."], ["Don't they, Miss Spruce?\"", "안 그래요, 스프루스 양?”"]], [["\"Lodgings is so much more comfortable than housekeeping,\"", "“하숙이 살림 나는 것보다야 훨씬 편하지요.” 스프루스 양이 말했다."], ["said Miss Spruce, who lived rather in fear of her relatives, the Ropers.", "그녀는 친척인 로퍼네 사람들을 어지간히 어려워하며 살고 있었다."]], [["\"Everybody knows it; does he?\"", "“온 집안이 다 안다고요?”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."], ["\"Why, if a gentleman will come home at night tipsy and threaten to murder another gentleman in the same house; and if a lady--\"", "“아니, 신사라는 사람이 밤중에 술에 취해 돌아와서 한집에 사는 다른 신사를 죽이겠다고 을러대는데야. 그리고 숙녀라는 사람이—”"], ["And then Amelia paused, for she knew that the line-of-battle ship which she was preparing to encounter had within her much power of fighting.", "그러고서 어밀리아는 말을 멈추었다. 자기가 맞서려고 나선 저 전열함이 만만찮은 싸움 실력을 품고 있다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Well, miss,\" said Mrs.", "“자, 아가씨.”"], ["Lupex, getting on her feet, \"and what of the lady?\"", "루펙스 부인이 자리에서 일어서며 말했다. “그래, 숙녀라는 사람이 어쨌다는 거죠?”"]], [["Now we may say that the battle had begun, and that the two ships were pledged by the general laws of courage and naval warfare to maintain the contest till one of them should be absolutely disabled, if not blown up or sunk.", "이제 우리는 싸움이 시작되었다고, 그리고 두 척의 배가 용기와 해전의 일반 법도에 따라 한쪽이 폭파되거나 침몰까지는 아니더라도 아주 못 쓰게 될 때까지 승부를 이어 가기로 서약했다고 말해도 좋다."], ["And at this moment it might be difficult for a bystander to say with which of the combatants rested the better chance of permanent success.", "그리고 이 순간에는 지켜보는 사람으로서 두 싸움꾼 가운데 어느 쪽에 끝내 이길 승산이 더 있는지 말하기가 어려웠을 것이다."], ["Mrs. Lupex had doubtless on her side more matured power, a habit of fighting which had given her infinite skill, a courage which deadened her to the feeling of all wounds while the heat of the battle should last, and a recklessness which made her almost indifferent whether she sank or swam.", "루펙스 부인에게는 의심할 나위 없이 더 무르익은 힘과, 무한한 기량을 안겨 준 싸움의 습관과, 싸움의 열기가 이어지는 동안에는 어떤 상처의 아픔에도 무디게 만들어 주는 담력과, 자기가 가라앉든 뜨든 거의 아랑곳하지 않게 만드는 무모함이 있었다."], ["But then Amelia carried the greater guns, and was able to pour in heavier metal than her enemy could use; and she, too, swam in her own waters.", "그러나 어밀리아 쪽은 더 큰 대포를 싣고 있었고, 적이 쓸 수 있는 것보다 더 무거운 쇳덩이를 퍼부을 수 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 제 앞바다에서 헤엄치고 있었다."], ["Should they absolutely come to grappling and boarding, Amelia would no doubt have the best of it; but Mrs.", "만일 정말로 갈고리를 걸고 배에 올라타는 데까지 간다면 어밀리아가 의심할 것 없이 이길 터였다."], ["Lupex would probably be too crafty to permit such a proceeding as that.", "그러나 루펙스 부인은 그런 일이 벌어지도록 내버려 두기에는 너무 노회할 것이었다."], ["She was, however, ready for the occasion, and greedy for the fight.", "어쨌거나 그녀는 이 판에 채비가 되어 있었고, 싸움에 굶주려 있었다."]], [["\"And what of the lady?\"", "“그래, 숙녀라는 사람이 어쨌다는 거죠?”"], ["said she, in a tone of voice that admitted of no pacific rejoinder.", "그녀가 그렇게 말했는데, 그 말투는 순순한 대꾸 따위는 용납하지 않는 것이었다."]], [["\"A lady, if she is a lady,\"", "“숙녀라면, 정말 숙녀라면 말이죠,”"], ["said Amelia, \"will know how to behave herself.\"", "어밀리아가 말했다. “제 몸가짐을 어떻게 해야 하는지 알 거예요.”"]], [["\"And you're going to teach me, are you, Miss Roper?", "“그래서 아가씨가 나를 가르치겠다는 거예요, 로퍼 양?"], ["I'm sure I'm ever so much obliged to you.", "참으로 고맙기 그지없네요."], ["It's Manchester manners, I suppose, that you prefer?\"", "아가씨가 좋아하는 건 맨체스터 예절인 모양이죠?”"]], [["\"I prefer honest manners, Mrs.", "“저는 정직한 예절이 좋아요, 루펙스 부인. 점잖은 예절이요."], ["Lupex, and decent manners, and manners that won't shock a whole house full of people; and I don't care whether they come from Manchester or London.\"", "온 집안 사람들을 기겁하게 하지 않는 예절 말이에요. 그게 맨체스터에서 왔든 런던에서 왔든 저는 상관 안 해요.”"]], [["\"Milliner's manners, I suppose?\"", "“모자 가게 예절이겠죠?”"]], [["\"I don't care whether they are milliner's manners or theatrical, Mrs.", "“모자 가게 예절이든 극장 예절이든 저는 상관 안 해요, 루펙스 부인."], ["Lupex, as long as they're not downright bad manners--as yours are, Mrs. Lupex.", "아주 못돼먹은 예절만 아니라면요—부인 것처럼 말이에요, 루펙스 부인."], ["And now you've got it.", "자, 이제 들으셨죠."], ["What are you going on for in this way with that young man, till you'll drive your husband into a madhouse with drink and jealousy?\"", "대체 저 젊은 남자하고 이런 식으로 계속 굴어서 뭘 하시려는 거예요? 남편을 술과 질투로 정신 병원에 처넣을 때까지 그러실 참인가요?”"]], [["\"Miss Roper!", "“로퍼 양!"], ["Miss Roper!\"", "로퍼 양!”"], ["said Cradell; \"now really--\"", "크래들이 말했다. “자, 이건 정말—”"]], [["\"Don't mind her, Mr.", "“저런 건 신경 쓰지 마세요, 크래들 씨.”"], ["Cradell,\" said Mrs.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다."], ["Lupex; \"she's not worthy for you to speak to.", "“당신이 말을 섞을 만한 값어치도 없는 아이니까요."], ["And as to that poor fellow Eames, if you've any friendship for him, you'll let him know what she is.", "그리고 저 딱한 임스 씨 말인데, 그이한테 조금이라도 우정이 있거든 저 아이가 어떤 사람인지 알려 주세요."], ["My dear, how's Mr. Juniper, of Grogram's house, at Salford?", "아가씨, 샐퍼드 그로그램 상회의 주니퍼 씨는 잘 계신가요?"], ["I know all about you, and so shall John Eames, too--poor unfortunate fool of a fellow!", "나는 아가씨에 대해 다 알고 있고, 존 임스도 곧 다 알게 될 거예요—가엾고 딱한 바보 같으니!"], ["Telling me of drink and jealousy, indeed!\"", "나더러 술이니 질투니 하고 늘어놓다니, 참!”"]], [["\"Yes, telling you! And now you've, mentioned Mr. Juniper's name, Mr. Eames, and Mr. Cradell too, may know the whole of it. There's been nothing about Mr. Juniper that I'm ashamed of.\"", "“그래요, 늘어놓았어요! 그리고 이제 부인이 주니퍼 씨 이름을 꺼내셨으니, 임스 씨도 크래들 씨도 그 이야기를 몽땅 다 아셔도 좋아요. 주니퍼 씨 일로 제가 부끄러워할 것은 하나도 없으니까요.”"]], [["\"It would be difficult to make you ashamed of anything, I believe.\"", "“아가씨를 무엇에든 부끄럽게 만들기란 어려운 일일 거예요, 내 생각에는.”"]], [["\"But let me tell you this, Mrs.", "“하지만 이 말은 해 두겠어요, 루펙스 부인."], ["Lupex, you're not going to destroy the respectability of this house by your goings on.\"", "부인이 그런 짓거리로 이 집의 체면을 망쳐 놓게 두지는 않을 거예요.”"]], [["\"It was a bad day for me when I let Lupex bring me into it.\"", "“루펙스가 나를 이 집에 데리고 들어온 그날이 나한테는 재수 없는 날이었죠.”"]], [["\"Then pay your bill, and walk out of it,\"", "“그럼 셈을 치르고 나가시죠.”"], ["said Amelia, waving her hand towards the door.", "어밀리아가 문 쪽으로 손을 내저으며 말했다."], ["\"I'll undertake to say there shan't be any notice required.", "“미리 알릴 것도 없다고 제가 책임지고 말씀드리죠."], ["Only you pay mother what you owe, and you're free to go at once.\"", "어머니한테 진 빚만 갚으시면 지금 당장이라도 나가셔도 좋아요.”"]], [["\"I shall go just when I please, and not one hour before.", "“나는 내가 좋을 때 나갈 거예요. 한 시간도 앞당겨서는 안 나가요."], ["Who are you, you gipsy, to speak to me in this way?\"", "이 집시 같은 계집애가 뭐기에 나한테 이런 식으로 말을 하는 거죠?”"]], [["\"And as for going, go you shall, if we have to call in the police to make you.\"", "“그리고 나가는 것으로 말하면, 순경을 불러다 끌어내야 한다 해도 나가시게 만들 거예요.”"]], [["Amelia, as at this period of the fight she stood fronting her foe with her arms akimbo, certainly seemed to have the best of the battle.", "싸움이 이쯤 이르렀을 때 두 손을 허리에 짚고 적을 마주 보고 선 어밀리아는 분명 이 전투에서 이기고 있는 것처럼 보였다."], ["But the bitterness of Mrs. Lupex's tongue had hardly yet produced its greatest results.", "그러나 루펙스 부인의 혀가 지닌 독기는 아직 그 최대의 결과를 내놓지 않은 참이었다."], ["I am inclined to think that the married lady would have silenced her who was single, had the fight been allowed to rage,--always presuming that no resort to grappling-irons took place. But at this moment Mrs.", "나로서는 이 싸움이 계속 타오르도록 내버려 두었더라면 그 유부녀 쪽이 처녀 쪽의 입을 다물게 했으리라고 여기는 편이다—물론 갈고리를 걸고 배에 올라타는 데까지는 가지 않는다는 전제에서 말이다."], ["Roper entered the room, accompanied by her son, and both the combatants for a moment retreated.", "그러나 바로 그때 로퍼 부인이 아들을 데리고 방으로 들어왔고, 두 싸움꾼은 잠시 물러섰다."]], [["\"Amelia, what's all this?\" said Mrs.", "“어밀리아, 이게 다 무슨 일이냐?”"], ["Roper, trying to assume a look of agonized amazement.", "로퍼 부인이 몹시 놀라 괴로워하는 표정을 지어 보이려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["\"Ask Mrs. Lupex,\"", "“루펙스 부인한테 물어보세요.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"And Mrs. Lupex will answer,\"", "“루펙스 부인이 대답하지요.”"], ["said that lady.", "그 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Your daughter has come in here, and attacked me--in such language--before Mr. Cradell, too--\"", "“댁의 따님이 이 방에 들어와서 나한테 대들었어요—그런 말버릇으로—그것도 크래들 씨 앞에서요—”"]], [["\"Why doesn't she pay what she owes, and leave the house?\"", "“저 사람은 왜 진 빚을 갚고 이 집에서 나가지 않는대요?”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Hold your tongue,\"", "“입 다물어.”"], ["said her brother.", "그녀의 오라비가 말했다."], ["\"What she owes is no affair of yours.\"", "“저분이 뭘 빚졌든 네가 상관할 일이 아니야.”"]], [["\"But it's an affair of mine, when I'm insulted by such a creature as that.\"", "“하지만 내가 저런 것한테 모욕을 당했으니 내가 상관할 일이지.”"]], [["\"Creature!\"", "“것이라니!”"], ["said Mrs. Lupex.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I'd like to know which is most like a creature!", "“누가 더 그런 것 같은지 나도 참 알고 싶네요!"], ["But I'll tell you, what it is, Amelia Roper--\"", "하지만 내가 말해 주죠, 어밀리아 로퍼—”"], ["Here, however, her eloquence was stopped, for Amelia had disappeared through the door, having been pushed out of the room by her brother. Whereupon Mrs.", "그러나 여기서 그녀의 웅변은 멎었으니, 어밀리아가 오라비에게 떠밀려 방 밖으로 사라진 뒤였기 때문이다."], ["Lupex, having found a sofa convenient for the service, betook herself to hysterics.", "그러자 루펙스 부인은 쓰기에 알맞은 소파를 하나 찾아내어 발작에 몸을 맡겼다."], ["There for the moment we will leave her, hoping that poor Mrs. Roper was not kept late out of her bed.", "잠시 우리는 그녀를 그렇게 두고 떠나기로 하되, 가엾은 로퍼 부인이 잠자리에 들지 못한 채 너무 늦게까지 붙들려 있지 않았기를 바랄 뿐이다."]], [["\"What a deuce of a mess Eames will make of it if he marries that girl!\"", "“임스가 저 계집애하고 결혼하면 얼마나 지독한 난장판을 만들어 놓을까!”"], ["Such was Cradell's reflection as he betook himself to his own room.", "자기 방으로 물러가면서 크래들이 한 생각은 그런 것이었다."], ["But of his own part in the night's transactions he was rather proud than otherwise, feeling that the married lady's regard for him had been the cause of the battle which had raged.", "그러나 그날 밤의 일에서 자기가 맡은 몫에 대해서는 그는 부끄러워하기는커녕 오히려 자랑스러웠으니, 그 유부녀가 자기를 마음에 두었다는 것이 방금 맹렬히 타오른 저 전투의 원인이었다고 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["So, likewise, did Paris derive much gratification from the ten years' siege of Troy.", "파리스 역시 십 년에 걸친 트로이 공성에서 그와 같이 큰 만족을 얻었던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII. LILIAN DALE BECOMES A BUTTERFLY.", "# 제12장. 릴리언 데일이 나비가 되다"]], [["And now we will go back to Allington.", "이제 우리는 앨링턴으로 돌아가기로 하자."], ["The same morning that brought to John Eames the two letters which were given in the last chapter but one, brought to the Great House, among others, the following epistle for Adolphus Crosbie.", "존 임스에게 지지난 장에 실린 두 통의 편지를 가져다준 바로 그 아침에, 큰 집에도 여러 통의 편지에 섞여 어돌퍼스 크로즈비 앞으로 다음과 같은 서한이 도착했다."], ["It was from a countess, and was written on pink paper, beautifully creamlaid and scented, ornamented with a coronet and certain singularly-entwined initials.", "그것은 어느 백작부인에게서 온 것으로, 분홍빛 종이에 씌어 있었는데, 결이 아름답게 잡힌 데다 향이 배어 있었고, 작위 관 문장과 기묘하게 뒤엉킨 머리글자들로 장식되어 있었다."], ["Altogether, the letter was very fashionable and attractive, and Adolphus Crosbie was by no means sorry to receive it.", "요컨대 그 편지는 대단히 세련되고 마음을 끄는 것이었으며, 어돌퍼스 크로즈비도 그것을 받고 결코 언짢아하지 않았다."]], [["Courcy Castle, September, 186--.", "코시 성, 186--년 9월."]], [["MY DEAR MR.", "친애하는 크로즈비 씨께,--게이지비 부부에게서 당신 소식을 들었소. 그이들이 우리에게 내려와 있는데, 당신이 어느 매력적인 작은 마을에 묻혀 시골살이를 하고 있다더군요."], ["CROSBIE,--We have heard of you from the Gazebees, who have come down to us, and who tell us that you are rusticating at a charming little village, in which, among other attractions, there are wood nymphs and water nymphs, to whom much of your time is devoted.", "그 마을에는 여러 좋은 것들 가운데에서도 숲의 님프와 물의 님프가 있어서, 당신 시간의 태반을 거기에 바치고 있다고요. 그거야말로 당신 취향에 꼭 들어맞는 일이니 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 당신을 방해하고 싶지 않소."], ["As this is just the thing for your taste, I would not for worlds disturb you; but if you should ever tear yourself away from the groves and fountains of Allington, we shall be delighted to welcome you here, though you will find us very unromantic after your late Elysium.", "다만 혹시라도 앨링턴의 숲과 샘에서 몸을 떼어낼 수 있게 되거든, 우리는 여기서 기꺼이 당신을 맞이하겠어요. 하기야 요즈음 당신이 지내던 엘리시온에 견주면 우리 쪽은 아주 낭만이 없다고 여겨지겠지만요."]], [["Lady Dumbello is coming to us, who I know is a favourite of yours.", "레이디 덤벨로가 우리에게 오기로 되어 있어요. 내가 알기로 당신이 아끼는 분이지요."], ["Or is it the other way, and are you a favourite of hers?", "아니면 그 반대인가요, 당신이 그분에게 귀염을 받는 쪽인가요?"], ["I did ask Lady Hartletop, but she cannot get away from the poor marquis, who is, you know, so very infirm.", "레이디 하틀탑에게도 청하기는 했으나, 그분은 가엾은 후작 곁을 떠나지 못한다는군요. 아시다시피 그이가 몹시 쇠약하니 말이오."], ["The duke isn't at Gatherum at present, but, of course, I don't mean that that has anything to do with dear Lady Hartletop's not coming to us.", "공작은 지금 개서럼에 있지 않소만, 물론 그것이 사랑스러운 레이디 하틀탑이 오지 못하는 일과 무슨 상관이 있다는 뜻은 아니라오. 집은 손님으로 가득 차게 될 듯하고, 님프도 아쉽지 않을 거예요."], ["I believe we shall have the house full, and shall not want for nymphs either, though I fear they will not be of the wood and water kind.", "다만 숲이나 물에 사는 종류는 아닐까 걱정이지만요. 마거리타와 알렉산드리나가 특히 당신이 와 주기를 바라요."], ["Margaretta and Alexandrina particularly want you to come, as they say you are so clever at making a houseful of people go off well If you can give us a week before you go back to manage the affairs of the nation, pray do.", "한 집 가득한 사람들을 잘 어우러지게 하는 데 당신만큼 능한 이가 없다고들 하니까요. 나라 살림을 챙기러 돌아가기 전에 우리에게 한 주를 내어줄 수 있거든, 부디 그리해 주오."]], [["Yours very sincerely,", "진심을 담아,"]], [["The Countess De Courcy was a very old friend of Mr.", "드 코시 백작부인은 크로즈비 씨의 아주 오랜 벗이었다."], ["Crosbie's; that is to say, as old friends go in the world in which he had been living.", "물론 그가 살아온 세계에서 오랜 벗이라는 것이 어떤 것인지를 감안하고 하는 말이다."], ["He had known her for the last six or seven years, and had been in the habit of going to all her London balls, and dancing with her daughters everywhere, in a most good-natured and affable way.", "그는 지난 예닐곱 해 동안 그 부인을 알고 지내며, 런던에서 열리는 부인의 무도회란 무도회에는 죄다 가고 어디서든 그 딸들과 춤을 추어 주는 것을 습관으로 삼아 왔다."], ["He had been intimate, from old family relations, with Mr.", "더없이 사람 좋고 싹싹한 태도로였다."], ["Mortimer Gazebee, who, though only an attorney of the more distinguished kind, had married the countess's eldest daughter, and now sat in Parliament for the city of Barchester, near to which Courcy Castle was situated. And, to tell the truth honestly at once, Mr.", "그는 또 집안끼리의 오랜 인연으로 모티머 게이지비 씨와도 친밀하게 지내 왔는데, 게이지비 씨는 좀 더 격이 높은 축에 드는 사무 변호사에 지나지 않으면서도 백작부인의 맏딸과 혼인하였고, 지금은 코시 성이 자리 잡은 인근의 도시 바체스터를 대표하여 의회에 앉아 있었다."], ["Crosbie had been on terms of great friendship with Lady De Courcy's daughters, the Ladies Margaretta and Alexandrina--perhaps especially so with the latter, though I would not have my readers suppose by my saying so that anything more tender than friendship had ever existed between them.", "그리고 이참에 솔직하게 다 털어놓자면, 크로즈비 씨는 드 코시 백작부인의 딸들인 레이디 마거리타, 레이디 알렉산드리나와도 대단히 두터운 사이였다. 어쩌면 특히 뒤엣사람과 그러하였을 터인데, 그렇다고 해서 내 독자들이 내 이 말을 두고 두 사람 사이에 우정보다 더 다정한 무엇이 오간 적이 있으리라 짐작하는 일만은 없기를 바란다."]], [["Crosbie said nothing about the letter on that morning; but during the day, or, perhaps, as he thought over the matter in bed, he made up his mind that he would accept Lady De Courcy's invitation.", "크로즈비는 그날 아침에는 그 편지에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그날 하루가 가는 동안에, 혹은 어쩌면 잠자리에 누워 그 일을 곰곰이 헤아려 보는 동안에, 그는 드 코시 백작부인의 초대를 받아들이기로 마음을 정했다."], ["It was not only that he would be glad to see the Gazebees, or glad to stay in the same house with that great master in the high art of fashionable life, Lady Dumbello, or glad to renew his friendship with the Ladies Margaretta and Alexandrina.", "게이지비 부부를 만나는 것이 반가워서만도 아니고, 사교계 생활이라는 고등 기예의 저 위대한 대가 레이디 덤벨로와 한 지붕 밑에 머무는 것이 반가워서만도 아니며, 레이디 마거리타, 레이디 알렉산드리나와 우정을 새로이 하는 것이 반가워서만도 아니었다."], ["Had he felt that the circumstances of his engagement with Lily made it expedient for him to stay with her till the end of his holidays, he could have thrown over the De Courcys without a struggle.", "릴리와 약혼한 처지의 사정으로 보아 휴가가 끝날 때까지 그 애 곁에 머무는 편이 마땅하다고 느꼈다면, 그는 별다른 갈등 없이 드 코시 집안을 저버릴 수 있었을 것이다."], ["But he told himself that it would be well for him now to tear himself away from Lily; or perhaps he said that it would be well for Lily that he should be torn away.", "그러나 그는 스스로에게, 이제는 릴리에게서 몸을 떼어 놓는 편이 자기에게 좋겠다고 말했다. 아니, 어쩌면 자기가 떼어져 나가는 편이 릴리에게 좋겠다고 말했는지도 모른다."], ["He must not teach her to think that they were to live only in the sunlight of each other's eyes during those months, or perhaps years, which must elapse before their engagement could be carried out.", "약혼이 열매를 맺기까지 흘러가야 할 그 몇 달, 어쩌면 몇 해 동안, 두 사람이 오로지 서로의 눈에서 비쳐 오는 햇빛만으로 살아가는 것이라고 그 애가 여기도록 가르쳐서는 안 되었다."], ["Nor must he allow her to suppose that either he or she were to depend solely upon the other for the amusements and employments of life.", "또 삶의 즐거움과 일거리를 오로지 상대에게서만 구해야 하는 것이라고 그 애가 여기게 두어서도 안 되었다."], ["In this way he argued the matter very sensibly within his own mind, and resolved, without much difficulty, that he would go to Courcy Castle, and bask for a week in the sunlight of the fashion which would be collected there.", "이런 식으로 그는 제 마음속에서 그 문제를 아주 사리에 닿게 따져 보았고, 별로 어렵지 않게 결심을 내렸다. 코시 성으로 가서 그곳에 모여들 화려한 사교계의 햇빛을 한 주 동안 쬐리라고."], ["The quiet humdrum of his own fireside would come upon him soon enough!", "제 집 난롯가의 조용하고 단조로운 나날은 머지않아 실컷 찾아올 터이니!"]], [["\"I think I shall leave you on Wednesday, sir,\"", "“수요일에 떠날까 합니다, 어르신.”"], ["Crosbie said to the squire at breakfast on Sunday morning.", "일요일 아침 식탁에서 크로즈비가 지주에게 말했다."]], [["\"Leave us on Wednesday!\"", "“수요일에 떠난다고!”"], ["said the squire, who had an old-fashioned idea that people who were engaged to marry each other should remain together as long as circumstances could be made to admit of their doing so.", "지주가 말했다. 그는 혼인을 약속한 두 사람이라면 사정이 허락하는 한 되도록 오래 함께 붙어 있어야 한다는 구식 생각을 지니고 있었다."], ["\"Nothing wrong, is there?\"", "“무슨 언짢은 일이라도 있는가?”"]], [["\"O dear, no!", "“아이고, 아닙니다!"], ["But everything must come to an end some day; and as I must make one or two short visits before I get back to town, I might as well go on Wednesday.", "다만 모든 일에는 끝이 있는 법이고, 저도 시내로 돌아가기 전에 한두 군데 짧게 들를 데가 있으니 수요일에 나서는 편이 낫겠지요."], ["Indeed, I have made it as late as I possibly could.\"", "사실 미룰 수 있는 데까지 미룬 것입니다.”"]], [["\"Where do you go from here?\"", "“여기서 어디로 가는데?”"], ["asked Bernard.", "버나드가 물었다."]], [["\"Well, as it happens, only into the next county,--to Courcy Castle.\"", "“글쎄, 마침 바로 옆 주(州)까지밖에 안 가. 코시 성으로.”"], ["And then there was nothing more said about the matter at that breakfast-table.", "그러고는 그 아침 식탁에서 그 일에 관해 더는 아무 말도 오가지 않았다."]], [["It had become their habit to meet together on the Sunday mornings before church, on the lawn belonging to the Small House, and on this day the three gentlemen walked down together, and found Lily and Bell already waiting for them.", "일요일 아침이면 교회에 가기 전에 작은 집의 잔디밭에서 다 함께 모이는 것이 그들의 버릇이 되어 있었다. 이날도 세 신사가 나란히 걸어 내려가 보니 릴리와 벨이 벌써 나와 기다리고 있었다."], ["They generally had some few minutes to spare on those occasions before Mrs. Dale summoned them to pass through the house to church, and such was the case at present.", "그런 자리에서는 대개 데일 부인이 집을 가로질러 교회로 나서자고 부를 때까지 몇 분쯤 여유가 있었고, 이날도 그러하였다."], ["The squire at these times would stand in the middle of the grass-plot, surveying his grounds, and taking stock of the shrubs, and flowers, and fruit-trees round him; for he never forgot that it was all his own, and would thus use this opportunity, as he seldom came down to see the spot on other days.", "지주는 그럴 때면 잔디밭 한복판에 서서 제 땅을 둘러보며 둘레의 관목이며 꽃이며 과실나무를 하나하나 눈으로 헤아리곤 했다. 그는 그것이 전부 제 것임을 한시도 잊은 적이 없었고, 여느 날에는 좀처럼 이곳까지 내려와 보는 일이 없었으므로 이런 기회를 그렇게 썼던 것이다."], ["Mrs. Dale, as she would see him from her own window while she was tying on her bonnet, would feel that she knew what was passing through his mind, and would regret that circumstances had forced her to be beholden to him for such assistance.", "데일 부인은 제 방 창가에서 보닛 끈을 매다가 그 모습을 내다볼 적마다, 시숙의 머릿속에 무엇이 오가는지 다 안다고 느꼈고, 형편에 밀려 그런 도움을 그에게 신세 지게 된 것을 서운해하곤 했다."], ["But, in truth, she did not know all that he thought at such times. \"It is mine,\"", "그러나 실상 부인은 그가 그럴 때 무슨 생각을 다 하는지는 알지 못했다."], ["he would say to himself, as he looked around on the pleasant place.", "“이건 내 것이지.” 그는 그 정겨운 땅을 둘러보며 속으로 말하곤 했다."], ["\"But it is well for me that they should enjoy it.", "“하지만 저 사람들이 이걸 누리는 편이 내게도 좋은 일이야."], ["She is my brother's widow, and she is welcome;--very welcome.\"", "제수는 내 아우의 미망인이니 여기 있어도 좋아. 얼마든지 좋고말고.”"], ["I think that if those two persons had known more than they did of each other's hearts and minds they might have loved each other better.", "나는 이 두 사람이 서로의 마음과 생각을 지금보다 조금만 더 알았더라면 서로를 더 사랑할 수도 있었으리라 생각한다."]], [["And then Crosbie told Lily of his intention.", "그러고 나서 크로즈비는 릴리에게 제 뜻을 알렸다."], ["\"On Wednesday!\"", "“수요일에요!”"], ["she said, turning almost pale with emotion as she heard this news.", "그 소식을 듣자 릴리는 감정이 북받쳐 얼굴빛이 거의 창백해지며 말했다."], ["He had told her abruptly, not thinking, probably, that such tidings would affect her so strongly.", "그는 불쑥 말해 버렸던 것이니, 그런 기별이 그 애를 그토록 세게 흔들어 놓으리라고는 아마 생각하지 못했을 것이다."]], [["\"Well, yes.", "“그래요."], ["I have written to Lady De Courcy and said Wednesday.", "드 코시 백작부인께 편지를 써서 수요일이라고 했어요."], ["It wouldn't do for me exactly to drop everybody, and perhaps--\"", "아무나 다 뿌리치고 지내는 건 나로서는 도리가 아니고, 또 어쩌면—”"]], [["\"Oh, no!", "“아, 아니에요!"], ["And, Adolphus, you don't suppose I begrudge your going.", "그리고 어돌퍼스, 제가 당신이 가시는 걸 아까워한다고는 생각지 마세요."], ["Only it does seem so sudden; does it not?\"", "다만 너무 갑작스러워서요. 그렇지 않아요?”"]], [["\"You see, I've been here over six weeks.\"", "“보다시피 여기 여섯 주가 넘게 있었잖아요.”"]], [["\"Yes; you've been very good.", "“네, 정말 잘해 주셨어요."], ["When I think of it, what a six weeks it has been!", "생각해 보면 그 여섯 주가 어떤 여섯 주였는지!"], ["I wonder whether the difference seems to you as great as it does to me.", "그 차이가 당신께도 제게만큼이나 크게 느껴지는지 궁금해요."], ["I've left off being a grub, and begun to be a butterfly.\"", "저는 애벌레 노릇을 그만두고 나비가 되기 시작했거든요.”"]], [["\"But you mustn't be a butterfly when you're married, Lily.\"", "“하지만 시집을 가면 나비 노릇은 하면 안 돼요, 릴리.”"]], [["\"No; not in that sense.", "“네, 그런 뜻으로 한 말은 아니에요."], ["But I meant that my real position in the world,--that for which I would fain hope that I was created,--opened to me only when I knew you and knew that you loved me.", "제가 말하려던 건, 이 세상에서 제가 놓일 참된 자리가—저는 그러라고 지어진 것이기를 바라는 그 자리가—당신을 알고 당신이 저를 사랑하신다는 걸 알고 나서야 비로소 열렸다는 거예요."], ["But mamma is calling us, and we must go through to church.", "그런데 엄마가 부르시네요, 이제 집을 가로질러 교회로 가야 해요."], ["Going on Wednesday!", "수요일에 가신다니!"], ["There are only three days more, then!\"", "그럼 사흘밖에 안 남았잖아요!”"]], [["\"Yes, just three days,\"", "“그래요, 꼭 사흘이지요.”"], ["he said, as he took her on his arm and passed through the house on to the road.", "그는 이렇게 말하며 릴리를 팔에 끼고 집을 가로질러 길로 나섰다."]], [["\"And when are we to see you again?\"", "“그럼 언제 다시 뵐 수 있어요?”"], ["she asked, as they reached the churchyard.", "교회 묘지에 이르렀을 때 릴리가 물었다."]], [["\"Ah, who is to say that yet?", "“아, 그거야 아직 누가 알겠어요?"], ["We must ask the Chairman of Committees when he will let me go again.\"", "언제 또 놓아 주실지는 위원회 의장께 여쭈어 봐야지요.”"], ["Then there was nothing more said, and they all followed the squire through the little porch and up to the big family-pew in which they all sat.", "그러고는 더 오간 말이 없었고, 모두들 지주를 따라 자그마한 현관을 지나 다들 함께 앉는 큼직한 가족석으로 올라갔다."], ["Here the squire took his place in one special corner which he had occupied ever since his father's death, and from which he read the responses loudly and plainly,--so loudly and plainly, that the parish clerk could by no means equal him, though with emulous voice he still made the attempt.", "여기서 지주는 아버지가 세상을 뜬 뒤로 줄곧 차지해 온 특별한 한쪽 구석에 자리를 잡고서 응답송을 크고 또렷하게 읽었다. 어찌나 크고 또렷한지 교구 서기가 도무지 그를 당해 내지 못했는데, 그래도 서기는 지지 않으려는 목청으로 여전히 애를 썼다."], ["\"T' squire'd like to be squire, and parson, and clerk, and everything; so a would,\"", "“지주 어른께서는 지주도 되고 목사도 되고 서기도 되고 죄다 되고 싶으신 게죠."], ["the poor clerk would say, when complaining of the ill-usage which he suffered.", "암, 그러시고말고요.” 딱한 서기는 제가 겪는 그 몹쓸 꼴을 하소연할 적마다 이렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["If Lily's prayers were interrupted by her new sorrow, I think that her fault in that respect would be forgiven.", "릴리의 기도가 새로 생긴 시름 탓에 자꾸 끊겼다 하더라도, 나는 그 점에서 그 애의 허물은 용서받으리라고 생각한다."], ["Of course she had known that Crosbie was not going to remain at Allington much longer.", "크로즈비가 앨링턴에 그리 오래 더 머물지 않으리라는 것이야 물론 릴리도 알고 있었다."], ["She knew quite as well as he did the exact day on which his leave of absence came to its end, and the hour at which it behoved him to walk into his room at the General Committee Office.", "그의 휴가가 끝나는 날짜도, 그가 일반위원회 사무국의 제 방으로 걸어 들어가야 하는 시각도, 릴리는 그 자신만큼이나 정확히 알고 있었다."], ["She had taught herself to think that he would remain with them up to the end of his vacation, and now she felt as a schoolboy would feel who was told suddenly, a day or two before the time, that the last week of his holidays was to be taken from him.", "다만 그가 휴가가 다 끝날 때까지 자기들 곁에 머물러 있으리라고 제 마음에 새겨 두었던 터라, 지금 릴리는 방학이 끝나기 하루 이틀 전에 갑자기 마지막 한 주를 빼앗기게 되었다는 말을 들은 학생아이와 같은 심정이었다."], ["The grievance would have been slight had she known it from the first; but what schoolboy could stand such a shock, when the loss amounted to two-thirds of his remaining wealth?", "처음부터 알고 있었더라면 그쯤이야 대수롭지 않은 서운함이었으련만, 남은 재산의 삼분의 이를 잃는 셈인데 어느 학생아이가 그런 충격을 견뎌 낼 수 있겠는가?"], ["Lily did not blame her lover.", "릴리는 제 연인을 탓하지 않았다."], ["She did not even think that he ought to stay.", "그가 남아 있어야 한다는 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["She would not allow herself to suppose that he could propose anything that was unkind.", "그가 무엇이든 야박한 일을 하려 든다고는 스스로에게 도무지 여기게 하지 않았다."], ["But she felt her loss, and more than once, as she knelt at her prayers, she wiped a hidden tear from her eyes.", "그러나 잃은 것은 잃은 것이어서, 무릎을 꿇고 기도하는 동안에도 릴리는 한두 번 남몰래 눈물을 훔쳤다."]], [["Crosbie also was thinking of his departure more than he should have done during Mr. Boyce's sermon.", "크로즈비 또한 보이스 씨의 설교가 이어지는 동안 마땅한 정도를 넘어 제가 떠날 일만 생각하고 있었다."], ["\"It's easy listening to him,\" Mrs.", "“그이 말은 듣기가 참 편하지요.”"], ["Hearn used to say of her husband's successor.", "헌 부인은 제 남편의 후임을 두고 늘 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"It don't give one much trouble following him into his arguments.\" Mr.", "“그이 논지를 좇아가느라 애쓸 일이 별로 없으니까요.”"], ["Crosbie perhaps found the difficulty greater than did Mrs. Hearn, and would have devoted his mind more perfectly to the discourse had the argument been deeper.", "크로즈비 씨는 어쩌면 헌 부인보다 그 어려움을 더 크게 느꼈을지도 모르고, 논지가 좀 더 깊었더라면 오히려 그 말씀에 마음을 온전히 바쳤을지도 모른다."], ["It is very hard, that necessity of listening to a man who says nothing.", "아무것도 말하지 않는 사람의 말을 듣고 앉아 있어야 한다는 것은 참으로 고된 노릇이다."], ["On this occasion Crosbie ignored the necessity altogether, and gave up his mind to the consideration of what it might be expedient that he should say to Lily before he went.", "이날 크로즈비는 그 고역을 아예 모른 체하고, 떠나기 전에 릴리에게 무슨 말을 해 두는 것이 좋을지를 궁리하는 데에 마음을 다 내주었다."], ["He remembered well those few words which he had spoken in the first ardour of his love, pleading that an early day might be fixed for their marriage.", "그는 사랑에 처음 불이 붙었을 적에 혼인 날짜를 일찍 잡자고 조르며 했던 그 몇 마디를 또렷이 기억하고 있었다. 그리고 릴리가 얼마나 어여쁘게 제 뜻을 받아 주었는지도 기억하고 있었다."], ["And he remembered, also, how prettily Lily had yielded to him.", "“다만 너무 서두르지는 마세요.”"], ["\"Only do not let it be too soon,\"", "릴리는 그렇게 말했더랬다."], ["she had said. Now he must unsay what he had then said.", "이제 그는 그때 한 말을 도로 물러야 했다."], ["He must plead against his own pleadings, and explain to her that he desired to postpone the marriage rather than to hasten it--a task which, I presume, must always be an unpleasant one for any man engaged to be married.", "제가 편 주장을 제 손으로 꺾어야 했고, 혼인을 앞당기기보다 미루고 싶다는 뜻을 그 애에게 알려야 했다. 짐작건대 혼인을 약속한 남자에게 이보다 더 언짢은 일거리는 없을 것이다."], ["\"I might as well do it at once,\"", "“이왕이면 지금 당장 해치우는 게 낫겠어.”"], ["he said to himself, as he bobbed his head forward into his hands by way of returning thanks for the termination of Mr. Boyce's sermon.", "보이스 씨의 설교가 끝난 데 대한 감사의 표시로 두 손에 이마를 파묻으며 그는 속으로 그렇게 말했다."]], [["As he had only three days left, it was certainly as well that he should do this at once.", "남은 날이 사흘뿐이었으니 곧바로 해치우는 편이 나은 것은 분명했다."], ["Seeing that Lily had no fortune, she could not in justice complain of a prolonged engagement.", "릴리에게 재산이 없는 터에 약혼이 길어진다 한들 그 애가 억울해할 까닭은 없다."], ["That was the argument which he used in his own mind.", "그것이 그가 제 마음속에서 내세운 논리였다."], ["But he as often told himself that she would have very great ground of complaint if she were left for a day unnecessarily in doubt as to this matter.", "그러나 그 못지않게 자주 그는 스스로에게, 이 일을 하루라도 공연히 어정쩡하게 놓아둔다면 그 애가 서운해할 만한 큰 이유가 되리라고 말했다."], ["Why had he rashly spoken those hasty words to her in his love, betraying himself into all manner of scrapes, as a schoolboy might do, or such a one as Johnny Eames?", "사랑에 겨워 어쩌자고 그 성급한 말을 함부로 내뱉어, 학생아이나 조니 임스 같은 자가 저지를 법한 온갖 낭패 속으로 스스로를 밀어 넣었단 말인가?"], ["What an ass he had been not to have remembered himself and to have been collected,--not to have bethought himself on the occasion of all that might be due to Adolphus Crosbie!", "정신을 차리고 마음을 가다듬지 못했다니, 어돌퍼스 크로즈비라는 사람에게 마땅히 돌아가야 할 것이 무엇인지를 그 자리에서 헤아리지 못했다니, 이 얼마나 얼간이 같은 짓인가!"], ["And then the idea came upon him whether he had not altogether made himself an ass in this matter.", "그러다 문득 이 일에서 저 자신을 통째로 얼간이로 만들어 버린 것이 아닌가 하는 생각이 그를 덮쳤다."], ["And as he gave his arm to Lily outside the church-door, he shrugged his shoulders while making that reflection.", "그리고 교회 문을 나서며 릴리에게 팔을 내어 줄 때, 그는 그런 생각을 하면서 어깨를 으쓱했다."], ["\"It is too late now,\"", "“이제 와서는 늦었지.”"], ["he said to himself; and then turned round and made some sweet little loving speech to her.", "그는 속으로 그렇게 말하고는, 돌아서서 그 애에게 다정하고 어여쁜 말을 한마디 건넸다. 어돌퍼스 크로즈비는 영리한 사람이었다."], ["Adolphus Crosbie was a clever man; and he meant also to be a true man,--if only the temptations to falsehood might not be too great for him.", "그리고 그는 진실한 사람이 되고자 하는 뜻도 있었다. 다만 거짓으로 이끄는 유혹이 그가 감당하지 못할 만큼 크지만 않다면 말이다."]], [["\"Lily,\"", "“릴리, 점심 뒤에 들판으로 나가 걸을래요?”"], ["he said to her, \"will you walk in the fields after lunch?\"", "그가 릴리에게 말했다."]], [["Walk in the fields with him!", "그와 함께 들판을 걷다니!"], ["Of course she would.", "물론 걸을 터였다."], ["There were only three days left, and would she not give up to him every moment of her time, if he would accept of all her moments?", "남은 날이 사흘뿐인데, 그가 받아만 준다면 제 시간의 한 순간인들 그에게 내어 주지 않을 리가 있겠는가?"], ["And then they lunched at the Small House, Mrs.", "그러고 나서 그들은 작은 집에서 점심을 먹었다."], ["Dale having promised to join the dinner-party at the squire's table.", "데일 부인은 지주의 식탁에서 열리는 만찬에는 함께하겠노라 약속해 둔 터였다."], ["The squire did not eat any lunch, excusing himself on the plea that lunch in itself was a bad thing.", "지주는 점심에 아무것도 손을 대지 않았는데, 점심이라는 것 자체가 나쁜 것이라는 구실을 댔다."], ["\"He can eat lunch at his own house,\"", "“점심이야 제 집에서 잡수시겠지.”"], ["Mrs. Dale afterwards said to Bell.", "나중에 데일 부인이 벨에게 말했다."], ["\"And I've often seen him take a glass of sherry.\"", "“게다가 셰리주를 한 잔 드시는 것도 여러 번 보았단다.”"], ["While thinking of this, Mrs. Dale made her own dinner.", "이런 생각을 하면서 데일 부인은 제 몫의 저녁을 먹었다."], ["If her brother-in-law would not eat at her board, neither would she eat at his.", "시숙이 제 상에서 먹지 않겠다면 저 역시 그의 상에서 먹지 않을 참이었다."]], [["And then in a few minutes Lily had on her hat, in place of that decorous, church-going bonnet which Crosbie was wont to abuse with a lover's privilege, feeling well assured that he might say what he liked of the bonnet as long as he would praise the hat.", "그러고 몇 분 만에 릴리는 크로즈비가 연인의 특권으로 늘 흉을 보곤 하던 그 얌전한 교회용 보닛 대신 모자를 쓰고 나섰다. 그가 모자를 칭찬해 주기만 한다면 보닛이야 무슨 소리를 하든 좋다고 릴리는 굳게 여기고 있었다."], ["\"Only three days,\"", "“사흘뿐이에요.”"], ["she said, as she walked down with him across the lawn at a quick pace.", "릴리는 그와 함께 잔디밭을 가로질러 빠른 걸음으로 내려가며 말했다."], ["But she said it in a voice which made no complaint,--which seemed to say simply this,--that as the good time was to be so short, they must make the most of it.", "그러나 그 목소리에는 원망이 담겨 있지 않았다. 그저 이렇게 말하는 듯할 뿐이었다—좋은 시간이 그토록 짧을 바에는 그 시간을 한껏 누려야 한다고."], ["And what compliment could be paid to a man so sweet as that?", "그러니 한 남자에게 이보다 더한 찬사가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["What flattery could be more gratifying?", "이보다 더 흐뭇한 아첨이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["All my earthly heaven is with you; and now, for the delight of these immediately present months or so, there are left to me but three days of this heaven!", "이 땅에서 제가 누릴 하늘은 온통 당신과 함께 있는데, 이제 앞으로 몇 달쯤 이어질 이 나날의 기쁨으로는 그 하늘이 사흘밖에 남지 않았다니!"], ["Come, then; I will make the most of what happiness is given to me.", "자, 그렇다면 나는 내게 주어진 행복을 한껏 누리겠다."], ["Crosbie felt it all as she felt it, and recognized the extent of the debt he owed her.", "크로즈비도 릴리가 느끼는 그대로를 느꼈고, 제가 그 애에게 진 빚이 얼마나 큰지도 알아차렸다."], ["\"I'll come down to them for a day at Christmas, though it be only for a day,\"", "“크리스마스에는 하루라도 좋으니 저 사람들한테 내려와야겠어."], ["he said to himself.", "단 하루뿐일지라도.”"], ["Then he reflected that as such was his intention, it might be well for him to open his present conversation with a promise to that effect.", "그는 속으로 그렇게 말했다. 그러고는 이왕 그럴 작정이라면 지금 나눌 이야기를 그 약속으로 여는 편이 좋겠다고 생각했다."]], [["\"Yes, Lily; there are only three days left now.", "“그래요, 릴리, 이제 사흘밖에 안 남았지요."], ["But I wonder whether--I suppose you'll all be at home at Christmas?\"", "그런데 말이오—크리스마스에는 다들 집에 있겠지요?”"]], [["\"At home at Christmas?--of course we shall be at home.", "“크리스마스에 집에 있느냐고요? 물론 집에 있지요."], ["You don't mean to say you'll come to us!\"", "설마 우리한테 오시겠다는 말씀은 아니겠죠!”"]], [["\"Well; I think I will, if you'll have me.\"", "“글쎄, 받아 주기만 한다면 그럴까 하는데요.”"]], [["\"Oh!", "“어머!"], ["that will make such a difference.", "그러면 얼마나 달라지는데요."], ["Let me see.", "어디 보자."], ["That will only be three months.", "그럼 석 달밖에 안 되네요."], ["And to have you here on Christmas Day!", "게다가 크리스마스날에 당신이 여기 계시다니!"], ["I would sooner have you then than on any other day in the year.\"", "저는 한 해의 어느 날보다도 그날 당신이 계셨으면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["\"It will only be for one day, Lily.", "“하루뿐일 거예요, 릴리."], ["I shall come to dinner on Christmas Eve, and must go away the day after.\"", "크리스마스이브 저녁에 맞춰 와서 그 이튿날에는 떠나야 해요.”"]], [["\"But you will come direct to our house!\"", "“그래도 곧장 우리 집으로 오시는 거죠!”"]], [["\"If you can spare me a room.\"", "“나한테 방을 하나 내어 줄 수 있다면요.”"]], [["\"Of course we can.", "“당연히 내어 드리죠."], ["So we could now.", "지금도 내어 드릴 수 있는걸요."], ["Only when you came, you know--\"", "다만 처음 오셨을 때는, 아시다시피—”"], ["Then she looked up into his face and smiled.", "그러고서 릴리는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 웃었다."]], [["\"When I came, I was the squire's friend and your cousin's, rather than yours.", "“내가 왔을 때 나는 그대의 벗이라기보다 지주 어른과 그대 사촌의 벗이었지요."], ["But that's all changed now.\"", "하지만 이제는 다 달라졌어요.”"]], [["\"Yes; you're my friend now,--mine specially.", "“맞아요, 이제 당신은 제 벗이에요. 그것도 저만의 벗이요."], ["I'm to be now and always your own special, dearest friend;--eh, Adolphus?\"", "저는 지금도 앞으로도 늘 당신만의 각별하고 더없이 소중한 벗이 되는 거예요."], ["And then she exacted from him the repetition of the promise which he had so often given her.", "그렇죠, 어돌퍼스?” 그러고서 릴리는 그가 그토록 자주 해 주었던 그 약속을 다시 한번 그의 입으로 말하게 했다."]], [["By this time they had passed through the grounds of the Great House and were in the fields.", "이때쯤 두 사람은 큰 집의 뜰을 지나 들판에 나와 있었다."], ["\"Lily,\"", "“릴리.”"], ["said he, speaking rather suddenly, and making her feel by his manner that something of importance was to be said; \"I want to say a few words to you about,--business.\"", "그는 다소 불쑥 말을 꺼냈고, 그 말투로 무언가 중요한 이야기가 나오리라는 것을 릴리도 느꼈다. “그대에게 몇 마디 해 두고 싶은 게 있는데, 저—실무에 관한 이야기요.”"], ["And he gave a little laugh as he spoke the last word, making her fully understand that he was not quite at his ease.", "그러면서 그는 마지막 낱말을 뱉으며 짧게 웃었으니, 제가 편치 않은 처지라는 것을 릴리도 훤히 알아차릴 만했다."]], [["\"Of course I'll listen.", "“그럼요, 듣고말고요."], ["And, Adolphus, pray don't be afraid about me.", "그리고 어돌퍼스, 제 걱정은 부디 마세요."], ["What I mean is, don't think that I can't bear cares and troubles.", "제 말은, 제가 근심이나 고생을 못 견딘다고 여기지 마시라는 거예요."], ["I can bear anything as long as you love me.", "당신이 저를 사랑해 주시는 한 저는 무엇이든 견딜 수 있어요."], ["I say that because I'm afraid I seemed to complain about your going.", "이런 말을 하는 건, 아까 당신이 떠나신다고 할 때 제가 원망하는 것처럼 보였을까 봐 걱정돼서예요."], ["I didn't mean to.\"", "그럴 뜻은 아니었어요.”"]], [["\"I never thought you complained, dearest.", "“그대가 원망한다고는 한 번도 생각하지 않았어요, 내 사랑."], ["Nothing can be better than you are at all times and in every way.", "언제 어느 모로 보나 그대보다 나은 사람은 있을 수 없지요."], ["A man would be very hard to please if you didn't please him.\"", "그대가 마음에 들지 않는 남자라면 여간 까다로운 사람이 아닐 겁니다.”"]], [["\"If I can only please you--\"", "“제가 당신 마음에만 들 수 있다면—”"]], [["\"You do please me, in everything.", "“그대는 무엇 하나 빠짐없이 내 마음에 들어요."], ["Dear Lily, I think I found an angel when I found you.", "사랑스러운 릴리, 나는 그대를 찾았을 때 천사를 하나 찾은 것이라고 생각해요."], ["But now about this business.", "그건 그렇고 이제 그 실무 이야기인데."], ["Perhaps I'd better tell you everything.\"", "아무래도 전부 다 이야기하는 편이 낫겠소.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, tell me everything.\"", "“네, 그럼요, 전부 다 이야기해 주세요.”"]], [["\"But then you mustn't misunderstand me.", "“하지만 그러자면 그대가 나를 오해해서는 안 돼요."], ["And if I talk about money, you mustn't suppose that it has anything to do with my love for you.\"", "그리고 내가 돈 이야기를 하더라도, 그것이 그대를 향한 내 사랑과 무슨 상관이 있는 것이라고 여겨서는 안 돼요.”"]], [["\"I wish for your sake that I wasn't such a little pauper.\"", "“당신을 봐서라도 제가 이런 쬐그만 빈털터리가 아니었으면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["\"What I mean to say is this, that if I seem to be anxious about money, you must not suppose that that anxiety bears any reference whatever to my affection for you.", "“내가 하려는 말은 이거예요. 내가 돈 문제로 애를 태우는 것처럼 보이더라도, 그 걱정이 그대를 향한 내 애정과 조금이라도 관계가 있다고는 여기지 말라는 거지요."], ["I should love you just the same, and look forward just as much to my happiness in marrying you, whether you were rich or poor.", "그대가 부자든 가난뱅이든 나는 똑같이 그대를 사랑했을 것이고, 그대와 혼인해서 누릴 행복도 똑같이 손꼽아 기다렸을 거예요."], ["You understand that?\"", "알아듣겠어요?”"]], [["She did not quite understand him; but she merely pressed his arm, so as to encourage him to go on.", "릴리는 그의 말을 온전히 알아듣지는 못했다. 그저 그가 말을 이어 가도록 북돋우려고 그의 팔을 꼭 눌렀을 뿐이다."], ["She presumed that he intended to tell her something as to their future mode of life--something which he supposed it might not be pleasant for her to hear, and she was determined to show him that she would receive it pleasantly.", "릴리는 그가 앞으로 두 사람이 어떻게 살아갈지에 관해 무언가를 말하려는 것이라고, 그것도 제가 듣기에 달갑지 않으리라고 그가 여기는 무언가를 말하려는 것이라고 짐작했고, 자기는 그것을 기꺼이 받아들이겠다는 것을 그에게 보여 주리라 마음먹었다."]], [["\"You know,\"", "“그대도 알다시피.”"], ["said he, \"how anxious I have been that our marriage should not be delayed.", "그가 말했다. “나는 우리 혼인이 미뤄지지 않기를 얼마나 애타게 바랐던가요."], ["To me, of course, it must be everything now to call you my own as soon as possible.\"", "물론 나로서는 하루라도 빨리 그대를 내 사람이라 부르는 것이 이제 전부일 수밖에 없지요.”"], ["In answer to which little declaration of love, she merely pressed his arm again, the subject being one on which she had not herself much to say.", "이 자그마한 사랑의 고백에 대해 릴리는 그저 다시 한번 그의 팔을 꼭 눌렀을 뿐이니, 그 대목은 저로서는 딱히 할 말이 많지 않은 이야기였다."]], [["\"Of course I must be very anxious, but I find it not so easy as I expected.\"", "“당연히 나는 몹시 애가 타지요. 그런데 생각만큼 쉽지가 않다는 걸 알게 되었어요.”"]], [["\"You know what I said, Adolphus.", "“제가 뭐라고 했는지 아시잖아요, 어돌퍼스."], ["I said that I thought we had better wait.", "저는 좀 기다리는 편이 낫겠다고 했어요."], ["I'm sure mamma thinks so.", "엄마도 분명 그렇게 생각하실 거예요."], ["And if we can only see you now and then--\"", "그리고 이따금씩만이라도 당신을 뵐 수 있다면—”"]], [["\"That will be a matter of course.", "“그야 당연한 일이지요."], ["But, as I was saying-- Let me see.", "그런데, 아까 하던 말인데— 어디 보자."], ["Yes,--all that waiting will be intolerable to me.", "그렇지, 그렇게 기다리는 일은 내게 견딜 수 없는 노릇일 거예요."], ["It is such a bore for a man when he has made up his mind on such a matter as marriage, not to make the change at once, especially when he is going to take to himself such a little angel as you are,\"", "혼인 같은 문제에 마음을 굳혀 놓고서 당장 그 살림으로 바꾸지 못한다는 건 남자에게 여간 답답한 일이 아니지요. 더구나 그대 같은 작은 천사를 제 사람으로 맞아들이려는 참이라면 말이오.”"], ["and as he spoke these loving words, his arm was again put round her waist; \"but--\"", "이 다정한 말을 하면서 그의 팔은 다시 릴리의 허리를 감았다. “하지만—”"], ["and then he stopped.", "그러고서 그는 말을 멈추었다."], ["He wanted to make her understand that this change of intention on his part was caused by the unexpected misconduct of her uncle.", "그는 제 쪽에서 이렇게 뜻을 바꾸게 된 것이 릴리의 삼촌이 뜻밖에 처신을 잘못한 탓임을 그 애가 알아주기를 바랐다."], ["He desired that she should know exactly how the matter stood; that he had been led to suppose that her uncle would give her some small fortune; that he had been disappointed, and had a right to feel the disappointment keenly; and that in consequence of this blow to his expectations, he must put off his marriage.", "그는 일이 정확히 어떻게 되어 있는지를 그 애가 알아 주기를 바랐다. 자기는 삼촌이 얼마간의 재산을 그 애에게 떼어 주리라 여기게끔 되어 있었고, 그것이 어그러졌으니 그 낭패를 뼈저리게 느낄 만한 까닭이 있으며, 기대가 이렇게 꺾인 이상 혼인을 미룰 수밖에 없다는 것을 말이다."], ["But he wished her also to understand at the same time that this did not in the least mar his love for her; that he did not join her at all in her uncle's fault.", "그러나 그와 동시에 그는 이 일이 그 애를 향한 제 사랑을 조금도 상하게 하지 않는다는 것, 삼촌의 잘못을 두고 그 애까지 싸잡아 나무라는 것은 결코 아니라는 것도 알아 주기를 바랐다."], ["All this he was anxious to convey to her, but he did not know how to get it said in a manner that would not be offensive to her personally, and that should not appear to accuse himself of sordid motives.", "이 모든 것을 그는 그 애에게 전하고 싶었으나, 그 애 자신에게 무람없이 들리지도 않고 제가 돈에 눈이 어두운 것처럼 비치지도 않게 말할 방도를 도무지 찾지 못했다."], ["He had begun by declaring that he would tell her all; but sometimes it is not easy, that task of telling a person everything.", "그는 전부 다 이야기하겠노라고 말문을 열었더랬다. 그러나 어떤 사람에게 전부 다 이야기한다는 그 일은 때로 그리 쉽지가 않다."], ["There are things which will not get themselves told.", "세상에는 제 입으로 말해지기를 한사코 마다하는 것들이 있는 법이다."]], [["\"You mean, dearest,\"", "“그러니까 당신 말씀은,”"], ["said she, \"that you cannot afford to marry at once.\"", "릴리가 말했다. “지금 당장은 혼인할 형편이 못 된다는 거군요.”"]], [["\"Yes; that is it.", "“그래요, 바로 그거예요."], ["I had expected that I should be able, but--\"", "될 줄로 알았는데, 그만—”"]], [["Did any man in love ever yet find himself able to tell the lady whom he loved that he was very much disappointed on discovering that she had got no money?", "사랑에 빠진 남자가 제가 사랑하는 여인에게, 그대에게 돈이 없다는 것을 알고 몹시 낙심했노라고 털어놓을 수 있었던 적이 이제껏 한 번이라도 있었던가?"], ["If so, his courage, I should say, was greater than his love.", "만일 있었다면 그 사람은 사랑보다 용기가 더 컸던 것이라고 나는 말하겠다."], ["Crosbie found himself unable to do it, and thought himself cruelly used because of the difficulty.", "크로즈비는 그리할 수 없음을 깨달았고, 그 어려움 때문에 제가 모진 꼴을 당하고 있다고 여겼다."], ["The delay to which he intended to subject her was occasioned, as he felt, by the squire, and not by himself.", "그가 릴리에게 지우려 하는 그 기다림은 그가 느끼기에 저 자신이 아니라 지주가 만들어 낸 것이었다."], ["He was ready to do his part, if only the squire had been willing to do the part which properly belonged to him.", "지주가 마땅히 제 몫으로 해야 할 일을 해 줄 마음만 있었다면 그도 제 몫을 할 준비가 되어 있었다."], ["The squire would not; and, therefore, neither could he,--not as yet.", "그런데 지주가 하지 않으니 그도 할 수 없다는 것이다—아직은."], ["Justice demanded that all this should be understood; but when he came to the telling of it, he found that the story would not form itself properly.", "이 모든 것이 제대로 알려지는 것이 도리에 맞았다. 그러나 막상 말을 꺼내려 하니 그 이야기가 도무지 반듯하게 모양을 갖추어 주지 않았다."], ["He must let the thing go, and bear the injustice, consoling himself as best he might by the reflection that he at least was behaving well in the matter.", "그는 이 일을 그대로 흘려보내고 그 억울함을 감내하는 수밖에 없었고, 적어도 이 일에서 저만은 훌륭히 처신하고 있다는 생각으로 그럭저럭 스스로를 달랬다."]], [["\"It won't make me unhappy, Adolphus.\"", "“그렇다고 제가 불행해지지는 않아요, 어돌퍼스.”"]], [["\"Will it not?\"", "“그렇지 않아요?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"As regards myself, I own that I cannot bear the delay with so much indifference.\"", "“나로 말하자면, 그 기다림을 그렇게 아무렇지 않게 견딜 수는 없다는 걸 고백하겠어요.”"]], [["\"Nay, my love; but you should not misunderstand me,\"", "“아니에요, 여보. 하지만 저를 오해하시면 안 돼요.”"], ["she said, stopping and facing him on the path in which they were walking.", "릴리는 걸음을 멈추고 걷던 길에서 그를 마주 보고 서서 말했다."], ["\"I suppose I ought to protest, according to the common rules, that I would rather wait.", "“저도 세상 흔한 법도대로라면 차라리 기다리는 편이 좋다고 우기는 시늉을 해야 마땅하겠지요."], ["Young ladies are expected to say so.", "젊은 아가씨란 으레 그렇게 말하는 법이니까요."], ["If you were pressing me to marry at once, I should say so, no doubt.", "만일 당신이 당장 혼인하자고 저를 조르신다면 저도 틀림없이 그렇게 말할 거예요."], ["But now, as it is, I will be more honest.", "하지만 지금 형편이 이러하니 저는 좀 더 솔직해지겠어요."], ["I have only one wish in the world, and that is, to be your wife,--to be able to share everything with you.", "저는 이 세상에 바라는 것이 딱 하나뿐이고, 그건 당신의 아내가 되는 것, 당신과 무엇이든 함께 나눌 수 있게 되는 거예요."], ["The sooner we can be together the better it will be,--at any rate, for me.", "우리가 빨리 함께할수록 그만큼 좋아요. 적어도 제게는요."], ["There; will that satisfy you?\"", "자, 이만하면 마음이 놓이세요?”"]], [["\"My own, own Lily!\"", "“내 것, 오직 내 것인 릴리!”"]], [["\"Yes, your own Lily.", "“네, 당신의 릴리예요."], ["You shall have no cause to doubt me, dearest.", "저를 의심하실 일은 없을 거예요, 여보."], ["But I do not expect that I am to have everything exactly as I want it.", "하지만 저는 무엇이든 제가 바라는 그대로 되기를 기대하지는 않아요."], ["I say again, that I shall not be unhappy in waiting.", "다시 말씀드리지만 저는 기다리는 일로 불행해지지 않아요."], ["How can I be unhappy while I feel certain of your love?", "당신의 사랑을 이렇게 확신하는데 제가 어떻게 불행해지겠어요?"], ["I was disappointed just now when you said that you were going so soon; and I am afraid I showed it.", "아까 당신이 이렇게 빨리 떠나신다고 하셨을 때는 서운했고, 아마 그게 겉으로 드러났을 거예요."], ["But those little things are more unendurable than the big things.\"", "하지만 그런 작은 일들이 큰일보다 오히려 더 견디기 어려운 법이니까요.”"]], [["\"Yes; that's very true.\"", "“그래요, 그건 정말 맞는 말이오.”"]], [["\"But there are three more days, and I mean to enjoy them so much!", "“그래도 사흘이 더 남았고, 저는 그 사흘을 실컷 누릴 작정이에요!"], ["And then you will write to me: and you will come at Christmas.", "그러고 나면 편지를 주실 거고, 크리스마스에 오실 거고요."], ["And next year, when you have your holiday, you will come down to us again; will you not?\"", "그리고 내년에 휴가가 나면 또 우리에게 내려오실 거죠. 그러실 거죠?”"]], [["\"You may be quite sure of that.\"", "“그건 아주 마음 놓아도 좋아요.”"]], [["\"And so the time will go by till it suits you to come and take me.", "“그렇게 시간이 흘러가다 보면 당신이 저를 데려가시기 좋은 때가 오겠지요."], ["I shall not be unhappy.\"", "저는 불행해지지 않아요.”"]], [["\"I, at any rate, shall be impatient.\"", "“나는, 어쨌든, 애가 탈 거예요.”"]], [["\"Ah, men always are impatient.", "“아, 남자들은 늘 애가 타지요."], ["It is one of their privileges, I suppose.", "그것도 남자들 특권 가운데 하나인가 봐요."], ["And I don't think that a man ever has the same positive and complete satisfaction in knowing that he is loved, which a girl feels.", "그리고 저는 남자가 사랑받고 있다는 걸 아는 데에서 여자아이가 느끼는 만큼 또렷하고 온전한 만족을 느끼는 법은 없다고 생각해요."], ["You are my bird that I have shot with my own gun; and the assurance of my success is sufficient for my happiness.\"", "당신은 제가 제 총으로 쏘아 맞힌 새예요. 그리고 저는 제가 맞혔다는 그 확신만으로도 충분히 행복하답니다.”"]], [["\"You have bowled me over, and know that I can't get up again.\"", "“그대는 나를 굴려 넘어뜨려 놓고, 내가 다시 일어나지 못한다는 걸 아는 거로군요.”"]], [["\"I don't know about can't.", "“못 한다는 건 잘 모르겠어요."], ["I would let you up quick enough, if you wished it.\"", "당신이 원하신다면 얼마든지 곧바로 일어나게 해 드릴 텐데요.”"]], [["How he made his loving assurance that he did not wish it, never would or could wish it, the reader will readily understand.", "그가 자기는 그것을 원하지 않으며 앞으로도 결코 원하지 않을 것이고 원할 수도 없다는 다짐을 어떤 사랑스러운 말로 늘어놓았는지는 독자들이 어렵지 않게 짐작할 것이다."], ["And then he considered that he might as well leave all those money questions as they now stood.", "그러고 나서 그는 저 돈 문제일랑 지금 있는 그대로 놓아두는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다."], ["His real object had been to convince her that their joint circumstances did not admit of an immediate marriage; and as to that she completely understood him.", "그의 참뜻은 두 사람의 형편으로는 당장 혼인할 수 없다는 것을 그 애에게 납득시키는 데 있었고, 그 점이라면 그 애가 그를 온전히 알아들었다."], ["Perhaps, during the next three days, some opportunity might arise for explaining the whole matter to Mrs. Dale.", "어쩌면 남은 사흘 사이에 데일 부인에게 이 일을 낱낱이 설명할 기회가 생길지도 모른다."], ["At any rate, he had declared his own purpose honestly, and no one could complain of him.", "어쨌든 그는 제 뜻을 정직하게 밝혔으니 아무도 그를 탓할 수 없을 터였다."]], [["On the following day they all rode over to Guestwick together,--the all consisting of the two girls, with Bernard and Crosbie.", "이튿날 그들은 다 함께 말을 타고 게스트윅으로 건너갔다. 다 함께라 함은 두 아가씨에 버나드와 크로즈비를 더한 것이다."], ["Their object was to pay two visits,--one to their very noble and highly exalted ally, the Lady Julia De Guest; and the other to their much humbler and better known friend, Mrs.", "그들의 볼일은 두 군데를 찾아보는 것이었다. 하나는 지체 높고 더없이 고귀한 인척인 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트를 찾는 일이었고, 다른 하나는 훨씬 수수하고 훨씬 허물없는 벗인 임스 부인을 찾는 일이었다."], ["Eames. As Guestwick Manor lay on their road into the town, they performed the grander ceremony the first.", "게스트윅 저택이 읍내로 들어가는 길목에 있었으므로 그들은 더 거창한 쪽의 예식부터 치렀다."], ["The present Earl De Guest, brother of that Lady Fanny who ran away with Major Dale, was an unmarried nobleman, who devoted himself chiefly to the breeding of cattle.", "데일 소령과 달아나 버린 저 레이디 패니의 오라비인 현 드 게스트 백작은 혼인하지 않은 귀족으로, 주로 소 사육에 몰두하며 지내는 사람이었다."], ["And as he bred very good cattle, taking infinite satisfaction in the employment, devoting all his energies thereto, and abstaining from all prominently evil courses, it should be acknowledged that he was not a bad member of society.", "그리고 그가 아주 좋은 소를 길러 내는 데다 그 일에 무한한 보람을 느끼며 온 힘을 거기에 쏟았고, 남 보기에 흉이 될 만한 못된 짓과는 담을 쌓고 살았으니, 그가 사회의 못난 구성원이 아니었다는 것은 인정해 주어야 마땅하다."], ["He was a thorough-going old Tory, whose proxy was always in the hand of the leader of his party; and who seldom himself went near the metropolis, unless called thither by some occasion of cattle-showing.", "그는 철저한 구식 토리당원이어서 그의 위임장은 늘 제 당 지도자의 손에 들려 있었고, 가축 품평회 같은 일로 불려 가는 일이 아니고서는 좀처럼 몸소 서울로 올라가지 않았다."], ["He was a short, stumpy man, with red cheeks and a round face; who was usually to be seen till dinner-time dressed in a very old shooting coat, with breeches, gaiters, and very thick shoes.", "그는 키가 작고 몸이 뭉툭하며 뺨이 붉고 얼굴이 둥근 사람으로, 저녁 식사 시간 전까지는 으레 아주 낡은 사냥 상의에 반바지와 각반과 몹시 두꺼운 구두 차림으로 눈에 띄었다."], ["He lived generally out of doors, and was almost as great in the preserving of game as in the breeding of oxen.", "그는 거의 바깥에서 살다시피 했고, 사냥감을 기르고 지키는 데에서도 황소를 길러 내는 데 못지않게 뛰어났다."], ["He knew every acre of his own estate, and every tree upon it, as thoroughly as a lady knows the ornaments in her drawing-room.", "그는 제 영지의 땅 한 뙈기와 그 위에 선 나무 한 그루까지, 마치 부인네가 제 응접실의 장식품을 훤히 알듯 속속들이 알고 있었다."], ["There was no gap in a fence of which he did not remember the exact bearings, no path hither or thither as to which he could not tell the why and the wherefore.", "울타리에 벌어진 틈이라면 그 자리를 정확히 기억하지 못하는 것이 없었고, 이리저리 난 오솔길이라면 그것이 어째서 거기 났고 무엇에 쓰이는지 대지 못하는 것이 없었다."], ["He had been in his earlier years a poor man as regarded his income,--very poor, seeing that he was an earl.", "그는 젊은 시절에는 수입으로 보아 가난한 사람이었다. 백작이라는 것을 생각하면 몹시 가난했다."], ["But he was not at present by any means an impoverished man, having been taught a lesson by the miseries of his father and grandfather, and having learned to live within his means.", "그러나 아버지와 할아버지가 겪은 곤경에서 교훈을 얻어 제 분수 안에서 사는 법을 익힌 덕에 지금은 결코 궁색한 사람이 아니었다."], ["Now, as he was going down the vale of years, men said that he was becoming rich, and that he had ready money to spend,--a position in which no Lord De Guest had found himself for many generations back.", "이제 나이의 내리막길을 걷는 그를 두고 사람들은 그가 부자가 되어 가고 있다고, 쓸 수 있는 현금을 쥐고 있다고 말했다. 지난 여러 대에 걸쳐 어떤 드 게스트 경도 누려 보지 못한 처지였다."], ["His father and grandfather had been known as spendthrifts; and now men said that this earl was a miser.", "그의 아버지와 할아버지는 낭비꾼으로 이름났었다. 그런데 이제 사람들은 이 백작을 두고 구두쇠라고 말했다."]], [["There was not much of nobility in his appearance; but they greatly mistook Lord De Guest who conceived that on that account his pride of place was not dear to his soul.", "그의 겉모습에는 귀족다운 구석이 별로 없었다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 제 지위에 대한 자부심이 그의 마음에 소중하지 않으리라 여긴다면 드 게스트 경을 크게 잘못 본 것이다."], ["His peerage dated back to the time of King John, and there were but three lords in England whose patents had been conferred before his own.", "그의 작위는 존 왕 시절까지 거슬러 올라갔고, 잉글랜드를 통틀어 그보다 먼저 특허장을 받은 귀족은 셋뿐이었다."], ["He knew what privileges were due to him on behalf of his blood, and was not disposed to abate one jot of them.", "그는 제 핏줄에 힘입어 제게 돌아오는 특권이 무엇인지를 알고 있었고, 그 가운데 한 오라기도 양보할 마음이 없었다."], ["He was not loud in demanding them.", "그는 그것을 요란하게 내세우지는 않았다."], ["As he went through the world he sent no trumpeters to the right or left, proclaiming that the Earl De Guest was coming.", "세상을 살아가면서 그는 드 게스트 백작께서 납신다고 좌우에 알리는 나팔수를 앞세우는 일이 없었다."], ["When he spread his board for his friends, which he did but on rare occasions, he entertained them simply, with a mild, tedious, old-fashioned courtesy.", "벗들을 위해 상을 차리는 일은 아주 드물었고, 차릴 때에도 그저 온화하고 지루하며 구식인 예의로 소박하게 대접했다."], ["We may say that, if properly treated, the earl never walked over anybody.", "제대로만 대접받는다면 이 백작이 누구를 짓밟고 지나가는 일은 없었다고 말해도 좋을 것이다."], ["But he could, if ill-treated, be grandly indignant; and if attacked, could hold his own against all the world.", "그러나 함부로 대접받으면 그는 당당하게 노여워할 줄 알았고, 공격을 받으면 온 세상을 상대로도 제 자리를 지켜 낼 수 있었다."], ["He knew himself to be every inch an earl, pottering about after his oxen with his muddy gaiters and red cheeks, as much as though he were glittering with stars in courtly royal ceremonies among his peers at Westminster;--ay, more an earl than any of those who use their nobility for pageant purposes.", "진창 묻은 각반에 붉은 뺨을 하고서 제 황소들 뒤꽁무니를 따라다닐 때에도, 그는 웨스트민스터에서 동료 귀족들 사이에 끼여 훈장을 번쩍이며 궁정의 왕실 의식에 참석할 때와 똑같이 제가 뼛속까지 백작임을 알고 있었다. 아니, 제 작위를 볼거리 삼아 쓰는 저들 누구보다도 더 백작이었다."], ["Woe be to him who should mistake that old coat for a badge of rural degradation!", "그 낡은 상의를 시골뜨기로 떨어진 표지로 잘못 아는 자에게 화 있을진저!"], ["Now and again some unlucky wight did make such a mistake, and had to do his penance very uncomfortably.", "이따금 운 없는 작자가 그런 잘못을 저질렀고, 그때마다 아주 거북하게 그 값을 치러야 했다."]], [["With the earl lived a maiden sister, the Lady Julia.", "백작과 함께 미혼의 누이 레이디 줄리아가 살고 있었다."], ["Bernard Dale's father had, in early life, run away with one sister, but no suitor had been fortunate enough to induce the Lady Julia to run with him.", "버나드 데일의 아버지는 젊은 시절 그 자매 가운데 하나와 달아났으나, 레이디 줄리아를 저와 함께 달아나게 할 만큼 운 좋은 구혼자는 없었다."], ["Therefore she still lived, in maiden blessedness, as mistress of Guestwick Manor; and as such had no mean opinion of the high position which destiny had called upon her to fill.", "그리하여 그 여자는 여태 처녀의 복된 몸으로 게스트윅 저택의 안주인 노릇을 하며 살고 있었고, 그런 사람으로서 운명이 저를 불러 앉힌 그 높은 자리에 대해 결코 가볍지 않은 자부심을 품고 있었다."], ["She was a tedious, dull, virtuous old woman, who gave herself infinite credit for having remained all her days in the home of her youth, probably forgetting, in her present advanced years, that her temptations to leave it had not been strong or numerous.", "그 여자는 지루하고 따분하며 흠잡을 데 없이 반듯한 노부인으로, 평생을 제가 나고 자란 집에서 지냈다는 것을 두고 스스로에게 한없는 공을 돌렸는데, 나이가 이만큼 든 지금에 와서는 아마도 그 집을 떠나고 싶게 만들 유혹이 저에게는 그다지 강하지도 잦지도 않았다는 사실을 잊고 있는 듯했다."], ["She generally spoke of her sister Fanny with some little contempt, as though that poor lady had degraded herself in marrying a younger brother.", "그 여자는 제 동생 패니 이야기를 할 때면 대체로 얼마간 업신여기는 투였으니, 마치 그 딱한 여동생이 아우뻘 되는 사내와 혼인하여 스스로 격을 떨어뜨렸다는 듯한 말투였다."], ["She was as proud of her own position as was the earl her brother, but her pride was maintained with more of outward show and less of inward nobility.", "그 여자는 오라비인 백작에 못지않게 제 지위를 자랑스러워했으나, 그 자부심을 지탱하는 데에 겉치레는 더 많았고 속의 기품은 더 적었다."], ["It was hardly enough for her that the world should know that she was a De Guest, and therefore she had assumed little pompous ways and certain airs of condescension which did not make her popular with her neighbours.", "제가 드 게스트 사람이라는 것을 세상이 알아주는 것만으로는 아무래도 모자랐던 모양이어서, 그 여자는 자잘하게 거드름 피우는 버릇과 짐짓 내려다보는 티를 몸에 붙였는데, 그것이 이웃들 사이에서 그 여자를 그리 달갑지 않은 사람으로 만들었다."]], [["The intercourse between Guestwick Manor and Allington was not very frequent or very cordial.", "게스트윅 저택과 앨링턴 사이의 왕래는 그리 잦지도 않았고 그리 살가운 것도 아니었다."], ["Soon after the running away of the Lady Fanny, the two families had agreed to acknowledge their connection with each other, and to let it be known by the world that they were on friendly terms.", "레이디 패니가 달아난 지 얼마 되지 않아 두 집안은 서로 인척임을 인정하고, 사이좋게 지낸다는 것을 세상이 알도록 하자고 합의를 보았다."], ["Either that course was necessary to them, or the other course, of letting it be known that they were enemies.", "두 집안에게는 그 길이 아니면 서로 원수라는 것을 세상에 알리는 다른 길밖에 없었다."], ["Friendship was the less troublesome, and therefore the two families called on each other from time to time, and gave each other dinners about once a year.", "벗이 되는 편이 성가심이 덜했고, 그리하여 두 집안은 이따금 서로를 찾아보고 한 해에 한 번쯤 서로에게 만찬을 베풀었다."], ["The earl regarded the squire as a man who had deserted his politics, and had thereby forfeited the respect due to him as an hereditary land magnate; and the squire was wont to be-little the earl as one who understood nothing of the outer world.", "백작은 지주를 두고 제 정치적 신념을 저버렸으며 그로써 세습 대지주로서 마땅히 받아야 할 존경을 스스로 내던진 사람이라 여겼다. 그리고 지주는 백작을 두고 바깥세상이라고는 아무것도 모르는 사람이라며 깎아내리기 일쑤였다."], ["At Guestwick Manor Bernard was to some extent a favourite.", "게스트윅 저택에서 버나드는 어느 정도 사랑을 받았다."], ["He was actually a relative, having in his veins blood of the De Guests, and was not the less a favourite because he was the heir to Allington, and because the blood of the Dales was older even than that of the noble family to which he was allied.", "그는 실제로 혈족이어서 그 핏줄에 드 게스트의 피가 흐르고 있었고, 앨링턴의 상속인이라는 점, 그리고 데일 가의 피가 그와 이어진 저 귀족 가문의 피보다도 더 오래되었다는 점 때문에도 사랑을 덜 받을 까닭이 없었다."], ["When Bernard should come to be the squire, then indeed there might be cordial relations between Guestwick Manor and Allington; unless, indeed, the earl's heir and the squire's heir should have some fresh cause of ill-will between themselves.", "버나드가 지주가 될 즈음이면 게스트윅 저택과 앨링턴 사이에 참으로 화목한 사이가 열릴지도 모를 일이었다. 다만 백작의 상속인과 지주의 상속인 사이에 또 새로운 앙금이 생기지만 않는다면 말이다."]], [["They found Lady Julia sitting in her drawing-room alone, and introduced to her Mr. Crosbie in due form.", "그들이 가 보니 레이디 줄리아는 응접실에 홀로 앉아 있었고, 그들은 격식을 갖추어 크로즈비 씨를 그 여자에게 소개했다."], ["The fact of Lily's engagement was of course known at the manor, and it was quite understood that her intended husband was now brought over that he might be looked at and approved.", "릴리가 약혼했다는 소식은 물론 저택에도 알려져 있었으므로, 그 신랑감이 이제 사람들 눈에 뵈어 인정을 받으려고 데려와진 것이라는 사정도 훤히 이해되고 있었다."], ["Lady Julia made a very elaborate curtsey, and expressed a hope that her young friend might be made happy in that sphere of life to which it had pleased God to call her.", "레이디 줄리아는 몹시 공들인 무릎절을 하고서, 어린 벗이 하느님께서 부르시어 두신 그 삶의 자리에서 행복해지기를 바란다고 말했다."]], [["\"I hope I shall, Lady Julia,\"", "“그러기를 저도 바라요, 레이디 줄리아.”"], ["said Lily, with a little laugh; \"at any rate I mean to try.\"", "릴리가 가볍게 웃으며 말했다. “아무튼 애는 써 볼 참이에요.”"]], [["\"We all try, my dear, but many of us fail to try with sufficient energy of purpose.", "“우리 모두가 애는 쓴답니다, 아가씨. 다만 우리 가운데 많은 이가 그 뜻에 넉넉한 힘을 싣지 못하고 만답니다."], ["It is only by doing our duty that we can hope to be happy, whether in single life or in married.\"", "홀몸으로 살든 혼인해서 살든, 우리가 행복을 바랄 수 있는 길은 오직 제 본분을 다하는 것뿐이지요.”"]], [["\"Miss Dale means to be a dragon of perfection in the performance of hers,\"", "“데일 양은 제 본분을 다하는 데에서 완벽의 용이 될 작정이랍니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"A dragon!\"", "“용이라니요!”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."], ["\"No; I hope Miss Lily Dale will never become a dragon.\"", "“아니지요. 나는 릴리 데일 양이 결코 용이 되지 않기를 바라요.”"], ["And then she turned to her nephew.", "그러고서 그 여자는 조카에게로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["It may be as well to say at once that she never forgave Mr.", "여기서 미리 말해 두는 편이 좋겠다."], ["Crosbie the freedom of the expression which he had used.", "그 여자는 크로즈비 씨가 함부로 놀린 그 말버릇을 끝내 용서하지 않았다."], ["He had been in the drawing-room of Guestwick Manor for two minutes only, and it did not become him to talk about dragons.", "그는 게스트윅 저택의 응접실에 들어온 지 겨우 이 분밖에 되지 않았으니, 용이 어떻고 하는 소리를 늘어놓는 것은 그의 분수에 맞지 않았다."], ["\"Bernard,\"", "“버나드.”"], ["she said, \"I heard from your mother yesterday.", "그 여자가 말했다. “어제 자네 어머니한테서 소식을 받았네."], ["I am afraid she does not seem to be very strong.\"", "아무래도 그리 튼튼해 보이지는 않으시더군.”"], ["And then there was a little conversation, not very interesting in its nature, between the aunt and the nephew as to the general health of Lady Fanny.", "그러고서 이모와 조카 사이에 레이디 패니의 전반적인 건강에 관한 자잘한 이야기가, 그 내용이 그리 흥미롭지는 않은 이야기가 오갔다."]], [["\"I didn't know my aunt was so unwell,\"", "“큰어머니가 그렇게 편찮으신 줄은 몰랐네요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"She isn't ill,\"", "“앓으시는 건 아니야.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."], ["\"She never is ill; but then she is never well.\"", "“한 번도 앓으신 적은 없지. 그렇다고 성하신 적도 없지만.”"]], [["\"Your aunt,\"", "“큰어머니라.” 레이디 줄리아가 그 낱말을 되뇌며 목소리에 얼마간 빈정거리는 기색을 담는 듯이 말했다."], ["said Lady Julia, seeming to put a touch of sarcasm into the tone of her voice as she repeated the word--\"your aunt has never enjoyed good health since she left this house; but that is a long time ago.\"", "“—그 큰어머니는 이 집을 떠난 뒤로 한 번도 건강해 본 적이 없답니다. 하기야 그것도 아주 오래전 일이지요.”"]], [["\"A very long time,\"", "“아주 오래전이지요.”"], ["said Crosbie, who was not accustomed to be left in his chair silent.", "의자에 앉아 잠자코 있는 데 익숙하지 않은 크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"You, Dale, at any rate, can hardly remember it.\"", "“적어도 자네는, 데일, 그때 일을 기억할 수 없을 테고.”"]], [["\"But I can remember it,\"", "“하지만 나는 기억할 수 있어요.”"], ["said Lady Julia, gathering herself up.", "레이디 줄리아가 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다."], ["\"I can remember when my sister Fanny was recognized as the beauty of the country.", "“내 동생 패니가 이 고장에서 제일가는 미인으로 꼽히던 때를 나는 기억한답니다."], ["It is a dangerous gift, that of beauty.\"", "아름다움이란 위험한 재주지요.”"]], [["\"Very dangerous,\"", "“아주 위험하지요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["Then Lily laughed again, and Lady Julia became more angry than ever.", "그러자 릴리가 또 웃었고, 레이디 줄리아는 그 어느 때보다 노여워졌다."], ["What odious man was this whom her neighbours were going to take into their very bosom! But she had heard of Mr.", "제 이웃들이 제 품속에까지 들이려 하는 이 사내는 대체 얼마나 밉살스러운 자란 말인가!"], ["Crosbie before, and Mr. Crosbie also had heard of her.", "그러나 그 여자는 전에도 크로즈비 씨에 관한 이야기를 들은 적이 있었고, 크로즈비 씨 또한 그 여자에 관한 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다."]], [["\"By-the-by, Lady Julia,\"", "“그런데 말입니다, 레이디 줄리아.”"], ["said he, \"I think I know some very dear friends of yours.\"", "그가 말했다. “제가 마님의 아주 소중한 벗들을 몇 분 아는 듯합니다.”"]], [["\"Very dear friends is a very strong word.", "“아주 소중한 벗이라니, 그건 아주 센 말이로군요."], ["I have not many very dear friends.\"", "내게는 아주 소중한 벗이 그리 많지 않답니다.”"]], [["\"I mean the Gazebees.", "“게이지비 부부 말입니다."], ["I have heard Mortimer Gazebee and Lady Amelia speak of you.\"", "모티머 게이지비와 레이디 어밀리아가 마님 이야기를 하는 걸 들은 적이 있지요.”"]], [["Whereupon Lady Julia confessed that she did know the Gazebees. Mr.", "그 말에 레이디 줄리아는 게이지비 부부를 아는 것이 사실이라고 인정했다."], ["Gazebee, she said, was a man who in early life had wanted many advantages, but still he was a very estimable person.", "게이지비 씨는, 그 여자의 말로는, 젊은 시절에 여러 가지로 갖추지 못한 것이 많았던 사람이지만 그래도 아주 훌륭한 인물이라는 것이었다."], ["He was now in Parliament, and she understood that he was making himself useful.", "그는 지금 의회에 나가 있고, 듣자 하니 쓸모 있는 사람 노릇을 하고 있다고 했다."], ["She had not quite approved of Lady Amelia's marriage at the time, and so she had told her very old friend Lady De Courcy; but-- And then Lady Julia said many words in praise of Mr.", "그 여자는 당시 레이디 어밀리아의 혼인을 썩 마땅히 여기지는 않았고, 그래서 아주 오랜 벗인 드 코시 백작부인에게도 그렇게 말했더랬다는 것이다."], ["Gazebee, which seemed to amount to this; that he was an excellent sort of man, with a full conviction of the too great honour done to him by the earl's daughter who had married him, and a complete consciousness that even that marriage had not put him on a par with his wife's relations, or even with his wife.", "다만— 그러고서 레이디 줄리아는 게이지비 씨를 칭찬하는 말을 많이 늘어놓았는데, 그 말들을 그러모으면 결국 이런 뜻인 듯싶었다. 즉 그가 아주 훌륭한 축의 사람이어서, 백작의 딸이 저와 혼인해 준 것이 제게는 지나치게 큰 영광임을 온전히 확신하고 있으며, 그런 혼인을 하고서도 제가 아내의 친척들과, 아니 제 아내와도 어깨를 나란히 하게 된 것은 아님을 완전히 깨닫고 있는 사람이라는 것이었다."], ["And then it came out that Lady Julia in the course of the next week was going to meet the Gazebees at Courcy Castle.", "그러고 나서 레이디 줄리아가 다음 주중에 코시 성에서 게이지비 부부를 만나기로 되어 있다는 사실이 드러났다."]], [["\"I am delighted to think that I shall have the pleasure of seeing you there,\"", "“거기서 마님을 뵙는 기쁨을 누리게 되겠다고 생각하니 참으로 반갑습니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Indeed!\"", "“그래요!”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."]], [["\"I am going to Courcy on Wednesday.", "“저는 수요일에 코시로 갑니다."], ["That, I fear, will be too early to allow of my being of any service to your ladyship.\"", "그러니 아무래도 너무 일러서 마님을 모시는 데 도움이 되어 드리지는 못하겠군요.”"]], [["Lady Julia drew herself up, and declined the escort which Mr. Crosbie had seemed to offer.", "레이디 줄리아는 몸을 곧추세우고서, 크로즈비 씨가 내미는 듯이 보였던 그 동행을 사양했다."], ["It grieved her to find that Lily Dale's future husband was an intimate friend of her friend's, and it especially grieved her to find that he was now going to that friend's house.", "릴리 데일의 미래의 남편이 제 벗과 가까운 사이라는 것을 알게 된 것이 그 여자로서는 언짢았고, 그가 이제 그 벗의 집으로 간다는 것을 알게 된 것은 더욱 언짢았다."], ["It was a grief to her, and she showed that it was.", "그것은 그 여자에게 언짢은 일이었고, 그 여자는 언짢다는 것을 겉으로 드러냈다."], ["It also grieved Crosbie to find that Lady Julia was to be a fellow guest with himself at Courcy Castle; but he did not show it.", "레이디 줄리아가 코시 성에서 저와 함께 손님으로 묵게 된다는 것은 크로즈비에게도 언짢은 일이었으나, 그는 그것을 겉으로 드러내지 않았다."], ["He expressed nothing but smiles and civil self-congratulation on the matter, pretending that he would have much delight in again meeting Lady Julia; but, in truth, he would have given much could he have invented any manoeuvre by which her ladyship might have been kept at home.", "그는 그 일에 대해 웃음과 점잖은 자축의 말밖에는 내비치지 않았고, 레이디 줄리아를 다시 뵙게 되어 무척 기쁘다는 시늉을 했다. 그러나 실상 그는 그 마님을 집에 붙잡아 둘 무슨 수라도 꾸며 낼 수만 있다면 큰 것이라도 내놓았을 것이다."]], [["\"What a horrid old woman she is,\"", "“정말 지긋지긋한 할머니야.”"], ["said Lily, as they rode back down the avenue.", "그들이 말을 타고 가로수길을 되돌아 내려가며 릴리가 말했다."], ["\"I beg your pardon, Bernard; for, of course, she is your aunt.\"", "“미안해, 버나드. 물론 저분은 네 이모님이시지만.”"]], [["\"Yes; she is my aunt; and though I am not very fond of her, I deny that she is a horrid old woman.", "“그래, 내 이모님이지. 그리고 내가 그분을 그리 좋아하지는 않지만, 그분이 지긋지긋한 할머니라는 건 인정 못 하겠는걸."], ["She never murdered anybody, or robbed anybody, or stole away any other woman's lover.\"", "그분은 누구를 죽인 적도 없고, 누구 것을 빼앗은 적도 없고, 남의 여자의 연인을 가로챈 적도 없어.”"]], [["\"I should think not,\"", "“그야 그렇겠지.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"She says her prayers earnestly, I have no doubt,\"", "“그분이 기도를 정성껏 드린다는 건 의심할 여지가 없지.”"], ["continued Bernard, \"and gives away money to the poor, and would sacrifice to-morrow any desire of her own to her brother's wish.", "버나드가 말을 이었다. “게다가 가난한 이들에게 돈도 나눠 주고, 오라버니가 바라는 일이라면 제 소원쯤은 내일이라도 접어 버릴 분이야."], ["I acknowledge that she is ugly, and pompous, and that, being a woman, she ought not to have such a long black beard on her upper lip.\"", "못생기고 거드름을 피운다는 건 나도 인정하지. 그리고 여자로서 윗입술에 그렇게 길고 검은 수염이 나 있어서는 안 된다는 것도.”"]], [["\"I don't care a bit about her beard,\"", "“나는 그 수염 따위 아무래도 좋아.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"But why did she tell me to do my duty?", "“하지만 왜 나더러 본분을 다하라고 하시는 거야?"], ["I didn't go there to have a sermon preached to me.\"", "나는 설교를 들으러 거기 간 게 아닌데.”"]], [["\"And why did she talk about beauty being dangerous?\"", "“그리고 왜 아름다움이 위험하다는 이야기를 꺼내셨을까?”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"Of course, we all knew what she meant.\"", "“물론 우리야 무슨 뜻인지 다 알았지만.”"]], [["\"I didn't know at all what she meant,\"", "“나는 무슨 뜻인지 전혀 몰랐어.”"], ["said Lily; \"and I don't know now.\"", "릴리가 말했다. “지금도 모르겠고.”"]], [["\"I think she's a charming woman, and I shall be especially civil to her at Lady De Courcy's,\"", "“나는 그분이 매력적인 부인이라고 생각해요. 그러니 드 코시 백작부인 댁에서는 각별히 예의를 갖추어 대할 참이오.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["And in this way, saying hard things of the poor old spinster whom they had left, they made their way into Guestwick, and again dismounted at Mrs. Eames's door.", "이런 식으로, 뒤에 두고 온 그 딱한 노처녀를 두고 모진 말을 주고받으며 그들은 게스트윅으로 들어섰고, 이번에는 임스 부인의 집 문 앞에서 말에서 내렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIII. A VISIT TO GUESTWICK.", "# 제13장. 게스트윅 방문"]], [["As the party from Allington rode up the narrow High-street of Guestwick, and across the market square towards the small, respectable, but very dull row of new houses in which Mrs. Eames lived, the people of Guestwick were all aware that Miss Lily Dale was escorted by her future husband.", "앨링턴에서 온 일행이 게스트윅의 좁은 하이 스트리트를 따라 올라가 장터 광장을 가로질러, 임스 부인이 사는 저 반듯하되 몹시 따분한 새 집들의 짧은 줄을 향해 말을 몰아갈 때, 게스트윅 사람들은 릴리 데일 양이 장래의 남편에게 호위를 받고 있다는 것을 모두 알고 있었다."], ["The opinion that she had been a very fortunate girl was certainly general among the Guestwickians, though it was not always expressed in open or generous terms.", "그녀가 참으로 운 좋은 아가씨라는 의견은 게스트윅 사람들 사이에 분명 두루 퍼져 있었으나, 그것이 늘 드러내 놓고 너그러운 말로 표현되지는 않았다."], ["\"It was a great match for her,\"", "“그 아가씨한테는 대단한 혼처지.”"], ["some said, but shook their heads at the same time, hinting that Mr. Crosbie's life in London was not all that it should be, and suggesting that she might have been more safe had she been content to bestow herself upon some country neighbour of less dangerous pretensions.", "어떤 이들은 그렇게 말하면서도 동시에 고개를 저었고, 크로즈비 씨의 런던 생활이 마땅히 그래야 할 만한 것은 못 된다고 넌지시 비추면서, 위험한 허세가 덜한 시골 이웃 가운데 누구에게 제 몸을 맡기는 것으로 만족했더라면 더 안전했을지도 모른다고 시사했다."], ["Others declared that it was no such great match after all.", "또 어떤 이들은 따지고 보면 그리 대단한 혼처도 아니라고 단언했다."], ["They knew his income to a penny, and believed that the young people would find it very difficult to keep a house in London unless the old squire intended to assist them.", "그들은 그의 수입을 한 푼까지 알고 있었고, 늙은 지주가 도와줄 생각이 아니라면 그 젊은 부부가 런던에서 집 한 채를 건사하기란 몹시 어려우리라고 믿었다."], ["But, nevertheless, Lily was envied as she rode through the town with her handsome lover by her side.", "그러나 그럼에도 릴리는 잘생긴 연인을 곁에 두고 읍내를 지나가는 동안 부러움을 샀다."]], [["And she was very happy.", "그리고 그녀는 매우 행복했다."], ["I will not deny that she had some feeling of triumphant satisfaction in the knowledge that she was envied.", "나는 그녀가 부러움을 사고 있다는 것을 아는 데서 얼마간 의기양양한 만족을 느꼈다는 사실을 부인하지 않겠다."], ["Such a feeling on her part was natural, and is natural to all men and women who are conscious that they have done well in the adjustment of their own affairs.", "그런 감정은 그녀에게 자연스러운 것이었고, 제 앞가림을 잘 매듭지었다고 자각하는 모든 남자와 여자에게 자연스러운 것이다."], ["As she herself had said, he was her bird, the spoil of her own gun, the product of such capacity as she had in her, on which she was to live, and, if possible, to thrive during the remainder of her life.", "그녀 자신이 말했듯이, 그는 그녀의 새였고, 그녀 자신의 총으로 잡은 노획물이었으며, 그녀 안에 있던 역량이 낳은 소산이었다. 그녀는 남은 평생을 그것에 기대어 살아가야 했고, 될 수 있으면 그것으로 번창해야 했다."], ["Lily fully recognized the importance of the thing she was doing, and, in soberest guise, had thought much of this matter of marriage.", "릴리는 자기가 하고 있는 일의 중대함을 충분히 알아보았고, 더없이 진지한 모습으로 이 혼인이라는 문제를 오래 생각해 왔다."], ["But the more she thought of it the more satisfied she was that she was doing well.", "그러나 생각하면 할수록 그녀는 자기가 잘하고 있다는 데 더 만족했다."], ["And yet she knew that there was a risk.", "그러면서도 그녀는 거기에 위험이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He who was now everything to her might die; nay, it was possible that he might be other than she thought him to be; that he might neglect her, desert her, or misuse her.", "이제 그녀에게 전부인 그 사람이 죽을 수도 있었다. 아니, 그가 그녀가 여기는 그런 사람이 아닐 수도 있었고, 그녀를 소홀히 하거나 버리거나 함부로 대할 수도 있었다."], ["But she had resolved to trust in everything, and, having so trusted, she would not provide for herself any possibility of retreat.", "그러나 그녀는 모든 것을 믿기로 마음먹었고, 그렇게 믿은 이상 자신을 위한 어떤 퇴로도 마련해 두지 않으려 했다."], ["Her ship should go out into the middle ocean, beyond all ken of the secure port from which it had sailed; her army should fight its battle with no hope of other safety than that which victory gives.", "그녀의 배는 떠나온 안전한 항구가 보이지도 않는 먼바다 한가운데로 나아가야 했고, 그녀의 군대는 승리가 주는 것 말고는 다른 안전을 바라지 않은 채 싸워야 했다."], ["All the world might know that she loved him if all the world chose to inquire about the matter.", "온 세상이 이 일을 물어 오겠다면 온 세상이 그녀가 그를 사랑한다는 것을 알아도 좋았다."], ["She triumphed in her lover, and did not deny even to herself that she was triumphant.", "그녀는 제 연인을 두고 의기양양했고, 스스로에게조차 자기가 의기양양하다는 것을 부인하지 않았다."]], [["Mrs. Eames was delighted to see them.", "임스 부인은 그들을 보고 기뻐 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."], ["It was so good in Mr.", "크로즈비 씨가 자기처럼 가엾고 외로운 여자를 굳이 찾아와 주다니 참으로 고마운 일이었고, 데일 대위도 참으로 고마웠다."], ["Crosbie to come over and call upon such a poor, forlorn woman as her, and so good in Captain Dale; so good also in the dear girls, who, at the present moment, had so much to make them happy at home at Allington!", "지금 이 순간 앨링턴 집에서 저희를 행복하게 해 줄 일이 그토록 많은 사랑스러운 아가씨들도 참으로 고마웠다!"], ["Little things, accounted as bare civilities by others, were esteemed as great favours by Mrs. Eames.", "남들이 그저 예의치레로 여기는 자잘한 일들이 임스 부인에게는 큰 은혜로 헤아려졌다."]], [["\"And dear Mrs. Dale?", "“그런데 그 고마우신 데일 부인은요?"], ["I hope she was not fatigued when we kept her up the other night so unconscionably late?\"", "요전 밤에 우리가 그렇게 터무니없이 늦게까지 붙들어 두었는데 지치시지는 않았는지요?”"], ["Bell and Lily both assured her that their mother was none the worse for what she had gone through; and then Mrs.", "벨과 릴리는 둘 다 어머니가 그 일로 조금도 상하지 않았다고 그녀를 안심시켰다."], ["Eames got up and left the room, with the declared purpose of looking for John and Mary, but bent, in truth, on the production of some cake and sweet wine which she kept under lock and key in the little parlour.", "그러자 임스 부인은 일어나 방을 나갔는데, 존과 메리를 찾으러 간다고 말은 했으나 실은 작은 응접실에 자물쇠를 채워 간수해 둔 케이크와 단 포도주를 내오려는 속셈이었다."]], [["\"Don't let's stay here very long,\"", "“여기 너무 오래 있지는 맙시다.”"], ["whispered Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 속삭였다."]], [["\"No, not very long,\"", "“네, 아주 오래는 아니고요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"But when you come to see my friends you mustn't be in a hurry, Mr. Crosbie.\"", "“하지만 제 친구들을 보러 오셨으면 서두르시면 안 돼요, 크로즈비 씨.”"]], [["\"He had his turn with Lady Julia,\"", "“이분은 레이디 줄리아하고 벌써 제 차례를 누리셨잖아.”"], ["said Bell, \"and we must have ours now.\"", "벨이 말했다. “이제는 우리 차례여야지.”"]], [["\"At any rate, Mrs. Eames won't tell us to do our duty and to beware of being too beautiful,\"", "“어쨌든 임스 부인은 우리한테 본분을 다하라느니 너무 아름다워지지 않게 조심하라느니 하지는 않으실 거야.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["Mary and John came into the room before their mother returned; then came Mrs.", "메리와 존은 어머니가 돌아오기 전에 방으로 들어왔다."], ["Eames, and a few minutes afterwards the cake and wine arrived.", "그러고 나서 임스 부인이 왔고, 몇 분 뒤에 케이크와 포도주가 도착했다."], ["It certainly was rather dull, as none of the party seemed to be at their ease. The grandeur of Mr.", "그 자리는 분명 다소 따분했으니, 일행 가운데 누구도 편안해 보이지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Crosbie was too great for Mrs. Eames and her daughter, and John was almost silenced by the misery of his position.", "크로즈비 씨의 위풍은 임스 부인과 그 딸에게는 너무 벅찼고, 존은 제 처지의 비참함에 거의 입을 다물고 있었다."], ["He had not yet answered Miss Roper's letter, nor had he even made up his mind whether he would answer it or no.", "그는 아직 로퍼 양의 편지에 답을 하지 않았고, 답을 할 것인지 말 것인지조차 마음을 정하지 못한 터였다."], ["And then the sight of Lily's happiness did not fill him with all that friendly joy which he should perhaps have felt as the friend of her childhood.", "게다가 릴리가 행복해하는 모습은 어린 시절의 벗으로서 마땅히 느꼈어야 할 그 정겨운 기쁨으로 그를 가득 채워 주지 못했다."], ["To tell the truth, he hated Crosbie, and so he had told himself; and had so told his sister also very frequently since the day of the party.", "사실대로 말하자면 그는 크로즈비를 미워했고, 스스로에게 그렇게 말해 왔으며, 그 잔치가 있던 날 이후로 누이에게도 아주 자주 그렇게 말해 왔다."]], [["\"I tell you what it is, Molly,\"", "“내 말 좀 들어 봐, 몰리.”"], ["he had said, \"if there was any way of doing it, I'd fight that man.\"", "그는 그렇게 말했었다. “할 방법만 있다면 나는 저 사람하고 결투를 하겠어.”"]], [["\"What; and make Lily wretched?\"", "“뭐? 그래서 릴리를 비참하게 만들려고?”"]], [["\"She'll never be happy with him.", "“릴리는 저 사람하고는 결코 행복해지지 못해."], ["I'm sure she won't.", "나는 확신해."], ["I don't want to do her any harm, but yet I'd like to fight that man,--if I only knew how to manage it.\"", "릴리한테 해를 끼치고 싶지는 않지만, 그래도 저 사람하고 한판 붙고 싶어—어떻게 해야 하는지만 알면 말이야.”"]], [["And then he bethought himself that if they could both be slaughtered in such an encounter it would be the only fitting termination to the present state of things.", "그러고 나서 그는 만약 그런 맞대결에서 둘 다 죽어 없어질 수 있다면 그것이야말로 지금의 사정에 어울리는 유일한 결말이 되리라고 생각했다."], ["In that way, too, there would be an escape from Amelia, and, at the present moment, he saw none other.", "그렇게 되면 어밀리아에게서 벗어날 길도 생길 터였고, 지금 이 순간 그에게는 그 밖의 다른 길이 보이지 않았다."]], [["When he entered the room he shook hands with all the party from Allington, but, as he told his sister afterwards, his flesh crept when he touched Crosbie.", "그가 방에 들어섰을 때 그는 앨링턴에서 온 일행 모두와 악수를 했으나, 나중에 누이에게 말한 대로 크로즈비를 만졌을 때는 살갗에 소름이 돋았다."], ["Crosbie, as he contemplated the Eames family sitting stiff and ill at ease in their own drawing-room chairs, made up his mind that it would be well that his wife should see as little of John Eames as might be when she came to London;--not that he was in any way jealous of her lover.", "크로즈비는 임스 집안 사람들이 제 집 응접실 의자에 뻣뻣하고 거북하게 앉아 있는 것을 바라보면서, 아내가 런던에 오거든 존 임스를 되도록 적게 보게 하는 편이 좋겠다고 마음먹었다. 아내의 연모자를 조금이라도 질투해서가 아니었다."], ["He had learned everything from Lily,--all, at least, that Lily knew,--and regarded the matter rather as a good joke.", "그는 릴리에게서 모든 것을—적어도 릴리가 아는 모든 것을—들어 알고 있었고, 그 일을 오히려 좋은 우스갯거리로 여겼다."], ["\"Don't see him too often,\"", "“그 사람을 너무 자주 만나지는 마요.”"], ["he had said to her, \"for fear he should make an ass of himself.\"", "그는 그녀에게 그렇게 말했었다. “바보짓을 할까 무서우니.”"], ["Lily had told him everything,--all that she could tell; but yet he did not in the least comprehend that Lily had, in truth, a warm affection for the young man whom he despised.", "릴리는 그에게 모든 것을—말할 수 있는 모든 것을—이야기했었다. 그러나 그는 릴리가 사실은 자기가 업신여기는 그 젊은이에게 따뜻한 애정을 품고 있다는 것을 조금도 깨닫지 못했다."]], [["\"Thank you, no,\"", "“고맙지만 사양하겠습니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"I never do take wine in the middle of the day.\"", "“저는 한낮에는 포도주를 입에 대는 법이 없어서요.”"]], [["\"But a bit of cake?\" And Mrs.", "“그럼 케이크라도 한 조각?”"], ["Eames by her look implored him to do her so much honour.", "그러면서 임스 부인은 눈빛으로 그에게 그만한 영광이라도 베풀어 달라고 간청했다."], ["She implored Captain Dale, also, but they were both inexorable.", "그녀는 데일 대위에게도 간청했으나 두 사람 다 요지부동이었다."], ["I do not know that the two girls were at all more inclined to eat and drink than the two men; but they understood that Mrs.", "나는 그 두 아가씨가 두 남자보다 조금이라도 더 먹고 마실 마음이 있었는지 알지 못한다."], ["Eames would be broken-hearted if no one partook of her delicacies.", "그러나 그들은 아무도 그 진미에 손을 대지 않으면 임스 부인이 가슴 아파하리라는 것을 알았다."], ["The little sacrifices of society are all made by women, as are also the great sacrifices of life.", "사교의 자잘한 희생은 모두 여자들이 치르며, 인생의 큰 희생도 마찬가지다."], ["A man who is good for anything is always ready for his duty, and so is a good woman always ready for a sacrifice.", "무엇에든 쓸모 있는 남자는 언제나 제 본분을 다할 준비가 되어 있고, 착한 여자는 언제나 희생할 준비가 되어 있다."]], [["\"We really must go now,\"", "“이제 정말 가 봐야겠어요.”"], ["said Bell, \"because of the horses.\"", "벨이 말했다. “말들 때문에요.”"], ["And under this excuse they got away.", "그리고 이 핑계로 그들은 자리를 떴다."]], [["\"You will come over before you go back to London, John?\"", "“런던으로 돌아가기 전에 한 번 들르실 거죠, 존?” 릴리가 말했다."], ["said Lily, as he came out with the intention of helping her mount, from which purpose, however, he was forced to recede by the iron will of Mr. Crosbie.", "그는 그녀가 말에 오르는 것을 거들 생각으로 따라 나왔으나, 크로즈비 씨의 무쇠 같은 뜻 앞에서 그 뜻을 접을 수밖에 없었다."]], [["\"Yes, I'll come over again--before I go.", "“네, 다시 들르겠습니다—떠나기 전에요."], ["Good-by.\"", "안녕히 가세요.”"]], [["\"Good-by, John,\" said Bell.", "“안녕히 계세요, 존.”"], ["\"Good-by, Eames,\"", "벨이 말했다."], ["said Captain Dale.", "“잘 있게, 임스.”"], ["Crosbie, as he seated himself in the saddle, made the very slightest sign of recognition, to which his rival would not condescend to pay any attention.", "데일 대위가 말했다. 크로즈비는 안장에 자리를 잡으면서 알은체하는 시늉을 아주 희미하게 지어 보였으나, 그의 연적은 거기에 관심을 기울여 줄 만큼 몸을 낮추려 하지 않았다."], ["\"I'll manage to have a fight with him in some way,\"", "“어떻게든 저 사람하고 한판 붙고 말겠다.”"], ["said Eames to himself as he walked back through the passage of his mother's house.", "임스는 어머니 집의 복도를 걸어 들어가면서 속으로 그렇게 말했다."], ["And Crosbie, as he settled his feet in the stirrups, felt that he disliked the young man more and more.", "그리고 크로즈비는 등자에 발을 고쳐 넣으면서 그 젊은이가 점점 더 싫어지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["It would be monstrous to suppose that there could be aught of jealousy in the feeling; and yet he did dislike him very strongly, and felt almost angry with Lily for asking him to come again to Allington.", "그 감정에 질투라고 할 만한 것이 조금이라도 섞여 있으리라 여긴다면 어처구니없는 일일 터였다. 그럼에도 그는 그가 몹시 싫었고, 그에게 다시 앨링턴으로 오라고 청한 릴리에게 화가 날 지경이었다."], ["\"I must put an end to all that,\"", "“이건 다 끝을 내야겠다.”"], ["he said to himself as he rode silently out of town.", "그는 말없이 읍내를 벗어나 말을 몰면서 속으로 그렇게 말했다."]], [["\"You must not snub my friends, sir,\"", "“제 친구들을 홀대하시면 안 돼요, 선생님.”"], ["said Lily, smiling as she spoke, but yet with something of earnestness in her voice.", "릴리가 말했다. 말하면서 웃고 있었으나 목소리에는 얼마간 진지한 기색이 있었다."], ["They were out of the town by this time, and Crosbie had hardly uttered a word since they had left Mrs. Eames's door.", "이때쯤 그들은 읍내를 벗어나 있었고, 크로즈비는 임스 부인 집 문을 나선 뒤로 거의 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["They were now on the high road, and Bell and Bernard Dale were somewhat in advance of them.", "이제 그들은 큰길에 올라 있었고, 벨과 버나드 데일은 그들보다 조금 앞서 가고 있었다."]], [["\"I never snub anybody,\"", "“나는 아무도 홀대하지 않아요.”"], ["said Crosbie, petulantly; \"that is, unless they have absolutely deserved snubbing.\"", "크로즈비가 짜증스레 말했다. “그러니까, 홀대받아 마땅한 사람이 아니라면 말이오.”"]], [["\"And have I deserved it?", "“그럼 저는 마땅했나요?"], ["Because I seem to have got it,\"", "제가 홀대를 받은 것 같아서요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Nonsense, Lily.", "“말도 안 되는 소리요, 릴리."], ["I never snubbed you yet, and I don't think it likely that I shall begin.", "나는 아직 당신을 홀대한 적이 없고, 앞으로 그럴 성싶지도 않소."], ["But you ought not to accuse me of not being civil to your friends.", "하지만 당신 친구들에게 예의를 차리지 않는다고 나를 몰아세워서는 안 되지요."], ["In the first place I am as civil to them as my nature will allow me to be.", "첫째로 나는 내 천성이 허락하는 만큼 그들에게 예의를 차리고 있소."], ["And, in the second place--\"", "그리고 둘째로는—”"]], [["\"Well; in the second place--?\"", "“그래요, 둘째로는요?”"]], [["\"I am not quite sure that you are very wise to encourage that young man's--friendship just at present.\"", "“나는 지금 이 시점에 당신이 그 젊은이의—우정을 북돋우는 것이 그리 현명한지 잘 모르겠소.”"]], [["\"That means, I suppose, that I am very wrong to do so?\"", "“그건 제가 그러는 게 아주 잘못이라는 말씀이겠지요?”"]], [["\"No, dearest, it does not mean that.", "“아니, 내 사랑, 그런 뜻이 아니오."], ["If I meant so I would tell you so honestly.", "그런 뜻이었다면 정직하게 그렇게 말했을 거요."], ["I mean just what I say.", "나는 딱 말한 그대로를 뜻하오."], ["There can, I suppose, be no doubt that he has filled himself with some kind of romantic attachment for you,--a foolish kind of love which I don't suppose he ever expected to gratify, but the idea of which lends a sort of grace to his life.", "그가 당신에게 어떤 낭만적인 연정 같은 것을 가슴에 가득 채워 두고 있다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없을 거요. 이루어지리라 기대해 본 적도 없을 어리석은 사랑이지만, 그 생각이 그의 삶에 일종의 운치를 더해 주는 것이지."], ["When he meets some young woman fit to be his wife he will forget all about it, but till then he will go about fancying himself a despairing lover.", "아내 삼기에 알맞은 젊은 여자를 만나면 그는 그 일을 죄다 잊을 테지만, 그때까지는 스스로를 절망한 연인이라 여기며 돌아다닐 거요."], ["And then such a young man as John Eames is very apt to talk of his fancies.\"", "그리고 존 임스 같은 젊은이는 제 공상을 입 밖에 내기가 아주 쉽소.”"]], [["\"I don't believe for a moment that he would mention my name to any one.\"", "“그가 제 이름을 누구에게든 입에 올리리라고는 한순간도 믿지 않아요.”"]], [["\"But, Lily, perhaps I may know more of young men than you do.\"", "“하지만 릴리, 아마 내가 당신보다 젊은 남자들을 더 잘 알 거요.”"]], [["\"Yes, of course you do.\"", "“네, 물론 그러시겠죠.”"]], [["\"And I can assure you that they are generally too well inclined to make free with the names of girls whom they think that they like.", "“그리고 장담하건대 그들은 대체로 저희가 좋아한다고 여기는 아가씨들의 이름을 함부로 입에 올리는 데 너무나 익숙하오."], ["You must not be surprised if I am unwilling that any man should make free with your name.\"", "어떤 사내도 당신 이름을 함부로 입에 올리는 것을 내가 원치 않는다고 해서 놀라서는 안 되오.”"]], [["After this Lily was silent for a minute or two.", "그 뒤로 릴리는 일이 분 동안 말이 없었다."], ["She felt that an injustice was being done to her and she was not inclined to put up with it, but she could not quite see where the injustice lay.", "그녀는 자기가 부당한 일을 당하고 있다고 느꼈고 그것을 참고 넘길 마음이 들지 않았으나, 그 부당함이 어디에 있는지 딱 집어 볼 수가 없었다."], ["A great deal was owing from her to Crosbie.", "그녀가 크로즈비에게 빚진 것은 많았다."], ["In very much she was bound to yield to him, and she was anxious to do on his behalf even more than her duty.", "아주 많은 일에서 그녀는 그에게 따라야 했고, 그를 위해서라면 제 본분보다 더한 일도 하고 싶었다."], ["But yet she had a strong conviction that it would not be well that she should give way to him in everything.", "그러면서도 그녀에게는 모든 일에서 그에게 굽히는 것이 좋지 않으리라는 굳은 확신이 있었다."], ["She wished to think as he thought as far as possible, but she could not say that she agreed with him when she knew that she differed from him.", "그녀는 될 수 있는 한 그가 생각하는 대로 생각하고 싶었으나, 그와 다르게 여긴다는 것을 알면서 그와 뜻이 같다고 말할 수는 없었다."], ["John Eames was an old friend whom she could not abandon, and so much at the present time she felt herself obliged to say.", "존 임스는 그녀가 저버릴 수 없는 오랜 벗이었고, 지금으로서는 그만큼은 말해야 한다고 그녀는 느꼈다."]], [["\"But, Adolphus--\"", "“하지만 어돌퍼스—”"]], [["\"Well, dearest?\"", "“왜 그러오, 내 사랑?”"]], [["\"You would not wish me to be unkind to so very old a friend as John Eames?", "“존 임스처럼 아주 오랜 벗에게 제가 매정하게 굴기를 바라시지는 않겠지요?"], ["I have known him all my life, and we have all of us had a very great regard for the whole family.", "저는 그 사람을 평생 알고 지냈고, 우리는 모두 그 집안 전체를 아주 크게 여겨 왔어요."], ["His father was my uncle's most particular friend.\"", "그의 아버지는 삼촌의 각별한 벗이었고요.”"]], [["\"I think, Lily, you must understand what I mean.", "“릴리, 내 말뜻은 알아들었으리라 생각하오."], ["I don't want you to quarrel with any of them, or to be what you call unkind.", "나는 당신이 그 사람들 누구와도 다투기를 바라지 않고, 당신 말대로 매정하게 굴기를 바라지도 않소."], ["But you need not give special and pressing invitations to this young man to come and see you before he goes back to London, and then to come and see you directly you get to London.", "하지만 이 젊은이에게 런던으로 돌아가기 전에 당신을 보러 오라고, 그러고 나서는 당신이 런던에 닿는 즉시 또 보러 오라고 특별히 다잡아 청할 것까지는 없지 않소."], ["You tell me that he has some kind of romantic idea of being in love with you;--of being in despair because you are not in love with him.", "당신은 그가 당신을 사랑한다는 어떤 낭만적인 생각을 품고 있다고, 당신이 자기를 사랑하지 않아서 절망에 빠져 있다고 나에게 말했소."], ["It's all great nonsense, no doubt, but it seems to me that under such circumstances you'd better--just leave him alone.\"", "물론 죄다 터무니없는 소리요. 하지만 그런 사정이라면 내가 보기에 당신은—그냥 그 사람을 내버려 두는 편이 낫겠소.”"]], [["Again Lily was silent.", "릴리는 다시 말이 없었다."], ["These were her three last days, in which it was her intention to be especially happy, but above all things to make him especially happy.", "이 사흘은 그녀의 마지막 사흘이었고, 그동안 그녀는 각별히 행복하려고, 그러나 무엇보다도 그를 각별히 행복하게 해 주려고 마음먹고 있었다."], ["On no account would she say to him sharp words, or encourage in her own heart a feeling of animosity against him, and yet she believed him to be wrong; and so believing could hardly bring herself to bear the injury.", "무슨 일이 있어도 그녀는 그에게 모진 말을 하지 않을 것이고 제 마음속에 그를 향한 미움을 키우지도 않을 것이었다. 그러면서도 그녀는 그가 틀렸다고 여겼고, 그렇게 여기고 있으니 그 부당함을 참아 넘기기가 좀처럼 되지 않았다."], ["Such was her nature, as a Dale.", "데일 집안 사람으로서 그것이 그녀의 천성이었다."], ["And let it be remembered that very many who can devote themselves for great sacrifices, cannot bring themselves to the endurance of little injuries.", "그리고 큰 희생에 자신을 바칠 수 있는 사람들 가운데 아주 많은 이가 작은 부당함은 견디지 못한다는 것을 기억해 두시라."], ["Lily could have given up any gratification for her lover, but she could not allow herself to have been in the wrong, believing herself to have been in the right.", "릴리는 연인을 위해서라면 어떤 즐거움도 내놓을 수 있었으나, 자기가 옳다고 믿으면서 자기가 틀렸었다고 하는 것만은 스스로에게 허락할 수 없었다."]], [["\"I have asked him now, and he must come,\"", "“이제 이미 청했으니 그 사람은 와야 해요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"But do not press him to come any more.\"", "“하지만 더는 오라고 다잡지는 마시오.”"]], [["\"Certainly not, after what you have said, Adolphus.", "“당연히 그러지 않겠어요, 어돌퍼스, 그렇게 말씀하셨으니."], ["If he comes over to Allington, he will see me in mamma's house, to which he has always been made welcome by her.", "그가 앨링턴으로 오면 어머니 집에서 저를 볼 거예요. 어머니는 늘 그를 반겨 맞아 주셨으니까요."], ["Of course I understand perfectly--\"", "물론 저는 잘 알고 있어요—”"]], [["\"You understand what, Lily?\"", "“무엇을 안다는 말이오, 릴리?”"]], [["But she had stopped herself, fearing that she might say that which would be offensive to him if she continued.", "그러나 그녀는 말을 이었다가는 그의 마음을 상하게 할 말을 하게 될까 두려워 스스로 말을 멈추었다."]], [["\"What is it you understand, Lily?\"", "“당신이 안다는 것이 무엇이오, 릴리?”"]], [["\"Do not press me to go on, Adolphus.", "“말을 잇게 다잡지 마세요, 어돌퍼스."], ["As far as I can, I will do all that you want me to do.\"", "제가 할 수 있는 한 당신이 바라시는 것은 다 하겠어요.”"]], [["\"You meant to say that when you find yourself an inmate of my house, as a matter of course you could not ask your own friends to come and see you.", "“내 집에 들어와 살게 되면 당연히 제 친구들을 보러 오라고 청하지 못하리라는 말을 하려던 거겠지."], ["Was that gracious?\"", "그게 고운 마음이오?”"]], [["\"Whatever I may have meant to say, I did not say that.", "“무슨 말을 하려 했든 저는 그렇게 말하지 않았어요."], ["Nor in truth did I mean it.", "사실 그런 뜻도 아니었고요."], ["Pray don't go on about it now.", "제발 이제 그 이야기는 그만하세요."], ["These are to be our last days, you know, and we shouldn't waste them by talking of things that are unpleasant.", "이게 우리의 마지막 날들이잖아요. 그런데 불쾌한 이야기로 그 날들을 허비해서는 안 되지요."], ["After all poor Johnny Eames is nothing to me; nothing, nothing.", "따지고 보면 가엾은 조니 임스는 저에게 아무것도 아니에요. 아무것도, 아무것도."], ["How can any one be anything to me when I think of you?\"", "제가 당신을 생각하는데 누가 저에게 무엇이 될 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["But even this did not bring Crosbie back at once into a pleasant humour.", "그러나 이것으로도 크로즈비는 곧바로 유쾌한 기분으로 돌아오지는 못했다."], ["Had Lily yielded to him and confessed that he was right, he would have made himself at once as pleasant as the sun in May.", "릴리가 그에게 굽히고 그가 옳았다고 인정했더라면 그는 당장 오월의 햇살처럼 환해졌을 것이다."], ["But this she had not done.", "그러나 그녀는 그렇게 하지 않았다."], ["She had simply abstained from her argument because she did not choose to be vexed, and had declared her continued purpose of seeing Eames on his promised visit.", "그녀는 그저 언짢아지고 싶지 않아서 말싸움을 삼갔을 뿐이었고, 임스가 약속한 대로 찾아오면 만나겠다는 뜻은 그대로임을 밝혔던 것이다."], ["Crosbie would have had her acknowledge herself wrong, and would have delighted in the privilege of forgiving her.", "크로즈비는 그녀가 스스로 잘못했다고 인정해 주기를 바랐을 것이고, 그녀를 용서하는 특권을 마음껏 누렸을 것이다."], ["But Lily Dale was one who did not greatly relish forgiveness, or any necessity of being forgiven.", "그러나 릴리 데일은 용서라는 것도, 용서를 받아야 하는 처지라는 것도 그리 반기지 않는 사람이었다."], ["So they rode on, if not in silence, without much joy in their conversation.", "그리하여 두 사람은 말없이는 아니더라도 이야기에 이렇다 할 즐거움 없이 말을 몰아갔다."], ["It was now late on the Monday afternoon, and Crosbie was to go early on the Wednesday morning.", "이제 월요일 오후 늦은 때였고, 크로즈비는 수요일 아침 일찍 떠나기로 되어 있었다."], ["What if these three last days should come to be marred with such terrible drawbacks as these!", "그런데 이 마지막 사흘이 이런 끔찍한 걸림돌들로 얼룩지고 만다면 어찌한단 말인가!"]], [["Bernard Dale had not spoken a word to his cousin of his suit, since they had been interrupted by Crosbie and Lily as they were lying on the bank by the ha-ha.", "버나드 데일은 하하 도랑 옆 둑에 누워 있다가 크로즈비와 릴리에게 방해를 받은 뒤로 사촌에게 제 청혼 이야기를 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["He had danced with her again and again at Mrs. Dale's party, and had seemed to revert to his old modes of conversation without difficulty.", "그는 데일 부인의 잔치에서 그녀와 몇 번이고 거듭 춤을 추었고, 어려움 없이 예전의 이야기 투로 돌아간 듯 보였다."], ["Bell, therefore, had believed the matter to be over, and was thankful to her cousin, declaring within her own bosom that the whole matter should be treated by her as though it had never happened.", "그래서 벨은 그 일이 끝났다고 믿었고 사촌에게 고마워하면서, 그 일 전체를 아예 없었던 일처럼 다루겠다고 제 가슴속으로 다짐했다."], ["To no one,--not even to her mother, would she tell it.", "누구에게도—어머니에게조차 그녀는 말하지 않을 것이었다."], ["To such reticence she bound herself for his sake, feeling that he would be best pleased that it should be so.", "그를 위해 그렇게 입을 다물기로 스스로를 묶었으니, 그가 그렇게 되기를 가장 기뻐하리라 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["But now as they rode on together, far in advance of the other couple, he again returned to the subject.", "그러나 이제 다른 두 사람보다 훨씬 앞서 나란히 말을 몰아가면서 그는 다시 그 이야기로 돌아왔다."]], [["\"Bell,\"", "“벨,”"], ["said he, \"am I to have any hope?\"", "그가 말했다. “나는 조금이라도 희망을 가져도 되는 거야?”"]], [["\"Any hope as to what, Bernard?\"", "“무엇에 대한 희망 말이야, 버나드?”"]], [["\"I hardly know whether a man is bound to take a single answer on such a subject.", "“이런 문제에서 사내가 대답 한 번으로 물러서야 하는 것인지 나는 잘 모르겠어."], ["But this I know, that if a man's heart is concerned, he is not very willing to do so.\"", "하지만 이것만은 알아. 사내의 마음이 걸린 일이라면 그러고 싶은 마음이 별로 들지 않는다는 것 말이야.”"]], [["\"When that answer has been given honestly and truly--\"", "“그 대답이 정직하고 참되게 주어진 것이라면—”"]], [["\"Oh, no doubt.", "“아, 물론이지."], ["I don't at all suppose that you were dishonest or false when you refused to allow me to speak to you.\"", "네가 나한테 말할 기회를 주지 않았을 때 정직하지 못했다거나 거짓이었다고는 조금도 생각하지 않아.”"]], [["\"But, Bernard, I did not refuse to allow you to speak to me.\"", "“하지만 버나드, 나는 너한테 말할 기회를 주지 않은 적이 없어.”"]], [["\"Something very like it.", "“그와 아주 비슷한 일이었지."], ["But, however, I have no doubt you were true enough.", "하지만 어쨌든 네가 충분히 참되었다는 것은 의심하지 않아."], ["But, Bell, why should it be so?", "그런데 벨, 왜 그래야 하지?"], ["If you were in love with any one else I could understand it.\"", "네가 다른 누구를 사랑하고 있다면 나도 이해할 수 있을 텐데.”"]], [["\"I am not in love with any one else.\"", "“나는 다른 누구도 사랑하고 있지 않아.”"]], [["\"Exactly.", "“바로 그거야."], ["And there are so many reasons why you and I should join our fortunes together.\"", "그리고 너와 내가 서로의 앞날과 재산을 하나로 합쳐야 할 이유는 그토록 많고.”"]], [["\"It cannot be a question of fortune, Bernard.\"", "“재산 문제일 수는 없어, 버나드.”"]], [["\"Do listen to me.", "“내 말 좀 들어 줘."], ["Do let me speak, at any rate.", "하다못해 말이라도 하게 해 줘."], ["I presume I may at least suppose that you do not dislike me.\"", "적어도 네가 나를 싫어하지는 않는다고 여겨도 되겠지.”"]], [["\"Oh, no.\"", "“아, 그럴 리가.”"]], [["\"And though you might not be willing to accept any man's hand merely on a question of fortune, surely the fact that our marriage would be in every way suitable as regards money should not set you against it.", "“그리고 네가 그저 재산 문제만으로 어떤 사내의 손을 받아들일 마음이 없다 하더라도, 우리 혼인이 돈 문제에서 모든 면으로 알맞다는 사실이 너를 그 혼인에서 등 돌리게 만들어서는 안 되잖아. 너를 향한 내 사랑에 대해서는 더 말하지 않겠어."], ["Of my own love for you I will not speak further, as I do not doubt that you believe what I say; but should you not question your own feelings very closely before you determine to oppose the wishes of all those who are nearest to you?\"", "내 말을 네가 믿어 주리라는 것을 의심하지 않으니까. 하지만 너와 가장 가까운 사람들 모두의 바람을 거스르기로 마음먹기 전에, 네 마음을 아주 꼼꼼히 캐물어 보아야 하지 않겠어?”"]], [["\"Do you mean mamma, Bernard?\"", "“어머니를 말하는 거야, 버나드?”"]], [["\"Not her especially, though I cannot but think she would like a marriage that would keep all the family together, and would give you an equal claim to the property to that which I have.\"", "“특별히 숙모님을 말하는 건 아니야. 물론 온 집안을 한데 묶어 주고 너에게도 나와 똑같은 몫의 재산 권리를 주는 혼인이라면 숙모님도 좋아하시리라고 생각하지 않을 수 없지만.”"]], [["\"That would not have a feather's-weight with mamma.\"", "“그건 어머니한테는 깃털만 한 무게도 없을 거야.”"]], [["\"Have you asked her?\"", "“여쭤 보기는 했어?”"]], [["\"No, I have mentioned the matter to no one.\"", "“아니, 나는 아무한테도 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않았어.”"]], [["\"Then you cannot know.", "“그럼 알 수 없는 거지."], ["And as to my uncle, I have the means of knowing that it is the great desire of his life.", "그리고 삼촌으로 말하자면, 나는 그것이 삼촌 평생의 큰 소원이라는 것을 알 만한 길이 있어."], ["I must say that I think some consideration for him should induce you to pause before you give a final answer, even though no consideration for me should have any weight with you.\"", "나를 헤아리는 마음은 너에게 아무 무게가 없다 하더라도, 삼촌을 얼마쯤 헤아린다면 마지막 대답을 주기 전에 한 번 멈춰야 마땅하다고 나는 말하지 않을 수 없어.”"]], [["\"I would do more for you than for him,--much more.\"", "“나는 삼촌보다 너를 위해 더 많은 것을 하겠어—훨씬 더.”"]], [["\"Then do this for me.", "“그럼 나를 위해 이것을 해 줘. 내 청혼에 아직 대답을 받지 못했다고 생각하게 해 줘."], ["Allow me to think that I have not yet had an answer to my proposal; give me to this day month, to Christmas; till any time that you like to name, so that I may think that it is not yet settled, and may tell uncle Christopher that such is the case.\"", "오늘로부터 한 달 뒤까지, 크리스마스까지, 네가 대고 싶은 아무 때까지라도 좋아. 그래야 내가 아직 정해지지 않았다고 생각할 수 있고, 크리스토퍼 삼촌한테도 그렇다고 말할 수 있을 테니까.”"]], [["\"Bernard, it would be useless.\"", "“버나드, 그건 부질없는 일이야.”"]], [["\"It would at any rate show him that you are willing to think of it.\"", "“어쨌든 네가 그 일을 생각해 볼 마음은 있다는 걸 삼촌한테 보여 주기는 하겠지.”"]], [["\"But I am not willing to think of it;--not in that way.", "“하지만 나는 그 일을 생각해 볼 마음이 없어—그런 식으로는."], ["I do know my own mind thoroughly, and I should be very wrong if I were to deceive you.\"", "나는 내 마음을 속속들이 알고 있고, 너를 속인다면 그건 아주 나쁜 짓이야.”"]], [["\"And you wish me to give that as your only answer to my uncle?\"", "“그럼 그것을 삼촌께 전할 네 유일한 대답으로 삼기를 바라는 거야?”"]], [["\"To tell the truth, Bernard, I do not much care what you may say to my uncle in this matter.", "“사실대로 말하면, 버나드, 나는 이 일로 네가 삼촌께 무슨 말을 하든 별로 개의치 않아."], ["He can have no right to interfere in the disposal of my hand, and therefore I need not regard his wishes on the subject.", "삼촌한테는 내 혼사를 어떻게 하느냐에 끼어들 권리가 있을 수 없고, 그러니 나는 이 문제에서 삼촌의 바람을 헤아릴 필요가 없어."], ["I will explain to you in one word what my feelings are about it.", "이 일에 대한 내 마음을 한마디로 밝혀 줄게."], ["I would accept no man in opposition to mamma's wishes; but not even for her could I accept any man in opposition to my own.", "나는 어머니의 바람을 거슬러서는 어떤 남자도 받아들이지 않을 거야. 하지만 어머니를 위해서라도 내 마음을 거슬러서는 어떤 남자도 받아들일 수 없어."], ["But as concerns my uncle, I do not feel myself called on to consult him in any way on such a matter.\"", "그러나 삼촌으로 말하자면, 나는 이런 문제에서 어떤 식으로든 삼촌과 의논해야 할 처지라고 여기지 않아.”"]], [["\"And yet he is the head of our family.\"", "“그래도 삼촌은 우리 집안의 어른이잖아.”"]], [["\"I don't care anything about the family,--not in that way.\"", "“나는 집안 같은 건 조금도 개의치 않아—그런 식으로는.”"]], [["\"And he has been very generous to you all.\"", "“그리고 삼촌은 너희 모두에게 아주 너그러우셨고.”"]], [["\"That I deny.", "“그건 아니야."], ["He has not been generous to mamma.", "삼촌은 어머니에게 너그럽지 않았어."], ["He is very hard and ungenerous to mamma.", "삼촌은 어머니에게 아주 매정하고 인색해."], ["He lets her have that house because he is anxious that the Dales should seem to be respectable before the world; and she lives in it, because she thinks it better for us that she should do so.", "삼촌이 어머니에게 그 집을 쓰게 해 주는 건 데일 집안이 세상 앞에 번듯해 보이기를 바라서고, 어머니가 거기 사시는 건 그러는 편이 우리에게 낫다고 여기시기 때문이야."], ["If I had my way, she should leave it to-morrow--or, at any rate, as soon as Lily is married.", "내 뜻대로라면 어머니는 내일이라도 그 집을 나오셔야 해—아니면 적어도 릴리가 시집가는 대로 말이야."], ["I would much sooner go into Guestwick, and live as the Eames do.\"", "나는 차라리 게스트윅으로 들어가서 임스네처럼 사는 편이 훨씬 좋겠어.”"]], [["\"I think you are ungrateful, Bell.\"", "“나는 네가 은혜를 모른다고 생각해, 벨.”"]], [["\"No; I am not ungrateful.", "“아니, 나는 은혜를 모르는 게 아니야."], ["And as to consulting, Bernard,--I should be much more inclined to consult you than him about my marriage.", "그리고 의논으로 말하자면, 버나드—내 혼사에 대해서라면 나는 삼촌보다 너와 의논하는 편이 훨씬 마음이 기울어."], ["If you would let me look on you altogether as a brother, I should think little of promising to marry no one whom you did not approve.\"", "네가 나로 하여금 너를 온전히 오라비로 여기게 해 준다면, 네가 찬성하지 않는 사람과는 아무와도 혼인하지 않겠다고 약속하는 것쯤은 대수롭지 않게 여기겠어.”"]], [["But such an agreement between them would by no means have suited Bernard's views.", "그러나 두 사람 사이의 그런 약조는 버나드가 바라보는 바에 결코 맞지 않았다."], ["He had thought, some four or five weeks back, that he was not personally very anxious for this match.", "그는 너덧 주 전만 해도 이 혼인을 개인적으로 그리 간절히 바라지는 않는다고 생각했다."], ["He had declared to himself that he liked his cousin well enough; that it would be a good thing for him to settle himself; that his uncle was reasonable in his wishes and sufficiently liberal in his offers; and that, therefore, he would marry.", "그는 스스로에게, 사촌이 마음에 든다고, 자리를 잡는 것이 자기에게 좋은 일이라고, 삼촌의 바람은 사리에 맞고 내놓는 조건도 넉넉히 후하다고, 그러니 혼인하겠다고 말해 두었다."], ["It had hardly occurred to him as probable that his cousin would reject so eligible an offer, and had certainly never occurred to him that he would have to suffer anything from such rejection.", "사촌이 그토록 훌륭한 청혼을 물리치리라고는 좀처럼 생각해 본 적이 없었고, 그런 거절 때문에 자기가 무엇인가를 견뎌야 하리라고는 정말이지 생각해 본 적이 없었다."], ["He had entertained none of that feeling of which lovers speak when they declare that they are staking their all upon the hazard of a die.", "연인들이 주사위 한 번 던지기에 제 전부를 건다고 말할 때의 그 감정 같은 것을 그는 조금도 품지 않았다."], ["It had not seemed to him that he was staking anything, as he gently told his tale of languid love, lying on the turf by the ha-ha.", "하하 도랑 옆 잔디밭에 누워 나른한 사랑의 사연을 나직이 늘어놓을 때 그는 자기가 무엇인가를 걸고 있다고 느끼지 않았다."], ["He had not regarded the possibility of disappointment, of sorrow, and of a deeply-vexed mind.", "실망과 슬픔과 깊이 상한 마음의 가능성을 그는 헤아려 보지 않았다."], ["He would have felt but little triumph if accepted, and had not thought that he could be humiliated by any rejection.", "받아들여진다 해도 그는 별로 의기양양하지 않았을 것이고, 거절당한다고 해서 자기가 모욕을 느낄 수 있으리라고는 생각하지도 않았다."], ["In this frame of mind he had gone to his work; but now he found, to his own surprise, that this girl's answer had made him absolutely unhappy.", "그런 마음가짐으로 그는 일에 나섰다. 그러나 이제 그는 이 아가씨의 대답이 자기를 아주 불행하게 만들었다는 것을 스스로도 놀랍게 깨달았다."], ["Having expressed a wish for this thing, the very expression of the wish made him long to possess it.", "이것을 바란다고 입 밖에 낸 이상, 바란다고 말했다는 그 사실 자체가 그것을 손에 넣고 싶게 만들었다."], ["He found, as he rode along silently by her side, that he was capable of more earnestness of desire than he had known himself to possess.", "그는 말없이 그녀 곁에서 말을 몰아가면서, 자기가 스스로 알고 있던 것보다 더 간절한 열망을 품을 수 있는 사람임을 깨달았다."], ["He was at this moment unhappy, disappointed, anxious, distrustful of the future, and more intent on one special toy than he had ever been before, even as a boy.", "그는 이 순간 불행했고, 실망했고, 불안했고, 앞날을 믿지 못했으며, 소년 시절에도 겪어 본 적 없을 만큼 단 하나의 장난감에 마음을 쏟고 있었다."], ["He was vexed, and felt himself to be sore at heart.", "그는 속이 상했고 가슴이 쓰라린 것을 느꼈다."], ["He looked round at her, as she sat silent, quiet, and somewhat sad upon her pony, and declared to himself that she was very beautiful,--that she was a thing to be gained if still there might be the possibility of gaining her.", "그는 조랑말 위에 말없이 조용히, 그리고 얼마쯤 서글프게 앉아 있는 그녀를 돌아보고는, 그녀가 참으로 아름답다고, 아직도 손에 넣을 가망이 있다면 반드시 손에 넣어야 할 것이라고 스스로에게 단언했다."], ["He felt that he really loved her, and yet he was almost angry with himself for so feeling.", "그는 자기가 그녀를 정말로 사랑한다고 느꼈고, 그러면서도 그렇게 느끼는 자신에게 거의 화가 났다."], ["Why had he subjected himself to this numbing weakness?", "왜 그는 스스로를 이런 저리는 나약함에 내맡겼단 말인가?"], ["His love had never given him any pleasure.", "그의 사랑은 그에게 아무런 기쁨도 준 적이 없었다."], ["Indeed he had never hitherto acknowledged it; but now he was driven to do so on finding it to be the source of trouble and pain.", "사실 그는 여태껏 그것을 사랑이라 인정해 본 적조차 없었다. 그런데 이제 그것이 괴로움과 아픔의 근원임을 알게 되자 인정하지 않을 수 없게 된 것이다."], ["I think it is open to us to doubt whether, even yet, Bernard Dale was in love with his cousin; whether he was not rather in love with his own desire.", "나는 여기까지 와서도 버나드 데일이 사촌을 사랑한 것인지, 오히려 제 욕망을 사랑한 것은 아닌지 우리가 의심해 볼 여지가 있다고 생각한다."], ["But against himself he found a verdict that he was in love, and was angry with himself and with all the world.", "그러나 그는 자신을 상대로 자기가 사랑에 빠졌다는 판결을 내렸고, 자신에게도 온 세상에게도 화가 났다."]], [["\"Ah, Bell,\"", "“아, 벨.”"], ["he said, coming close up to her, \"I wish you could understand how I love you.\"", "그가 그녀에게 바싹 다가서며 말했다. “내가 너를 얼마나 사랑하는지 네가 알아줄 수 있으면 좋겠어.”"], ["And, as he spoke, his cousin unconsciously recognized more of affection in his tone, and less of that spirit of bargaining which had seemed to pervade all his former pleas, than she had ever found before.", "그리고 그가 그렇게 말할 때, 사촌은 저도 모르게 그의 말투에서 여태 알던 것보다 더 많은 애정을, 그리고 그가 앞서 내놓은 모든 간청에 배어 있는 듯하던 그 흥정하는 기색은 여태 알던 것보다 덜한 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["\"And do I not love you?", "“나도 너를 사랑하지 않는 게 아니잖아?"], ["Have I not offered to be to you in all respects as a sister?\"", "모든 면에서 누이가 되어 주겠다고 하지 않았어?”"]], [["\"That is nothing.", "“그건 아무것도 아니야."], ["Such an offer to me now is simply laughing at me.", "지금 나한테 그런 말을 하는 건 그냥 나를 비웃는 거야."], ["Bell, I tell you what,--I will not give you up.", "벨, 내 말 들어 봐—나는 너를 포기하지 않겠어."], ["The fact is, you do not know me yet,--not know me as you must know any man before you choose him for your husband.", "사실은 네가 아직 나를 모르는 거야—어떤 사내를 남편으로 고르기 전에 반드시 알아야 하는 만큼은 나를 모르는 거지."], ["You and Lily are not alike in this.", "이 점에서 너와 릴리는 서로 달라."], ["You are cautious, doubtful of yourself, and perhaps, also, somewhat doubtful of others.", "너는 조심스럽고, 너 자신을 미덥지 않아 하고, 어쩌면 남들도 얼마쯤 미덥지 않아 하지."], ["My heart is set upon this, and I shall still try to succeed.\"", "내 마음은 이 일에 걸려 있고, 나는 그래도 이루어 보려고 애쓰겠어.”"]], [["\"Ah, Bernard, do not say that!", "“아, 버나드, 그런 말 하지 마!"], ["Believe me, when I tell you that it can never be.\"", "결코 그럴 수 없다고 말할 때는 내 말을 믿어 줘.”"]], [["\"No; I will not believe you.", "“아니, 나는 네 말을 믿지 않겠어."], ["I will not allow myself to be made utterly wretched.", "나는 나 자신이 아주 비참해지도록 내버려 두지 않겠어."], ["I tell you fairly that I will not believe you.", "솔직히 말하는데 나는 네 말을 믿지 않겠어."], ["I may surely hope if I choose to hope.", "내가 바라기로 마음먹었다면 바라도 되는 것 아니겠어."], ["No, Bell, I will never give you up,--unless, indeed, I should see you become another man's wife.\"", "아니야, 벨, 나는 결코 너를 포기하지 않겠어—네가 다른 남자의 아내가 되는 것을 정말로 보게 되지 않는 한은.”"]], [["As he said this, they all turned in through the squire's gate, and rode up to the yard in which it was their habit to dismount from their horses.", "그가 이 말을 할 때 그들은 모두 지주의 문을 지나 안으로 들어섰고, 늘 말에서 내리곤 하던 마당까지 말을 몰아 올라갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIV. JOHN EAMES TAKES A WALK.", "# 제14장. 존 임스가 산책을 하다"]], [["John Eames watched the party of cavaliers as they rode away from his mother's door, and then started upon a solitary walk, as soon as the noise of the horses' hoofs had passed away out of the street.", "존 임스는 말 탄 일행이 어머니 집 문 앞에서 떠나가는 것을 지켜보다가, 말발굽 소리가 거리 밖으로 사라지자마자 홀로 산책을 나섰다."], ["He was by no means happy in his mind as he did so.", "그렇게 걸어 나서는 그의 마음은 결코 편치 않았다."], ["Indeed, he was overwhelmed with care and trouble, and as he went along very gloomy thoughts passed through his mind.", "아니, 그는 근심과 걱정에 짓눌려 있었고, 길을 가는 동안 아주 우울한 생각들이 머릿속을 지나갔다."], ["Had he not better go to Australia, or Vancouver's Island, or--?", "차라리 오스트레일리아로 가는 편이 낫지 않을까, 아니면 밴쿠버섬으로, 아니면—?"], ["I will not name the places which the poor fellow suggested to himself as possible terminations of the long journeys which he might not improbably be called upon to take.", "이 가엾은 친구가 머지않아 떠나야 할지도 모른다고 여긴 그 긴 여행의 종착지로 스스로 꼽아 본 곳들의 이름을 나는 여기 적지 않겠다."], ["That very day, just before the Dales had come in, he had received a second letter from his darling Amelia, written very closely upon the heels of the first.", "바로 그날, 데일 집안 사람들이 들이닥치기 조금 전에, 그는 사랑하는 어밀리아에게서 두 번째 편지를 받았다. 첫 번째 편지의 뒤를 바짝 좇아 쓰인 편지였다."], ["Why had he not answered her?", "어찌하여 답장을 하지 않았느냐?"], ["Was he ill?", "앓고 있느냐?"], ["Was he untrue?", "마음이 변한 것이냐?"], ["No; she would not believe that, and therefore fell back upon the probability of his illness.", "아니, 그것만은 그녀도 믿지 않겠단다. 그리하여 그가 앓고 있으리라는 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["If it was so, she would rush down to see him.", "만일 그렇다면 그녀는 한달음에 내려와 그를 보겠노라고 했다."], ["Nothing on earth should keep her from the bedside of her betrothed.", "세상 그 무엇도 약혼자의 머리맡에서 그녀를 떼어 놓지는 못하리라고."], ["If she did not get an answer from her beloved John by return of post, she would be down with him at Guestwick by the express train.", "사랑하는 존에게서 다음 우편으로 답장이 오지 않는다면, 그녀는 급행열차를 타고 게스트윅으로 그를 찾아 내려오겠노라고 했다."], ["Here was a position for such a young man as John Eames!", "존 임스 같은 젊은이에게 이 무슨 처지란 말인가!"], ["And of Amelia Roper we may say that she was a young woman who would not give up her game, as long as the least chance remained of her winning it.", "그리고 어밀리아 로퍼로 말하자면, 그녀는 이길 가망이 티끌만큼이라도 남아 있는 한 판을 놓지 않는 젊은 여자였다고 우리는 말해 둘 수 있다."], ["\"I must go somewhere,\"", "“어디로든 가야겠다.”"], ["John said to himself, as he put on his slouched hat and wandered forth through the back streets of Guestwick.", "존은 챙 늘어진 모자를 눌러쓰고 게스트윅의 뒷골목으로 헤매어 나서면서 혼잣말을 했다."], ["What would his mother say when she heard of Amelia Roper?", "어머니가 어밀리아 로퍼 이야기를 들으시면 무어라 하실까?"], ["What would she say when she saw her?", "그녀를 직접 보시면 무어라 하실까?"]], [["He walked away towards the Manor, so that he might roam about the Guestwick woods in solitude.", "그는 게스트윅 숲속을 홀로 거닐 요량으로 저택 쪽을 향해 걸어갔다."], ["There was a path with a stile, leading off from the high road, about half a mile beyond the lodges through which the Dales had ridden up to the house, and by this path John Eames turned in, and went away till he had left the Manor house behind him, and was in the centre of the Guestwick woods.", "데일 집안 사람들이 말을 타고 집으로 올라간 그 문간채에서 반 마일쯤 더 간 곳에, 큰길에서 갈라져 나가는, 층계 달린 오솔길이 하나 있었다. 존 임스는 그 길로 접어들어, 저택을 뒤로 하고 게스트윅 숲 한가운데에 이를 때까지 걸어 들어갔다."], ["He knew the whole ground well, having roamed there ever since he was first allowed to go forth upon his walks alone.", "그는 혼자 산책을 나가도 좋다는 허락을 처음 받은 뒤로 줄곧 그곳을 쏘다녔던 터라 그 일대를 속속들이 알고 있었다."], ["He had thought of Lily Dale by the hour together, as he had lost himself among the oak-trees; but in those former days he had thought of her with some pleasure.", "참나무들 사이에서 저를 잊은 채 몇 시간이고 릴리 데일을 생각한 적도 있었다. 그러나 그 지난날에는 얼마간 즐거운 마음으로 그녀를 생각했다."], ["Now he could only think of her as of one gone from him for ever; and then he had also to think of her whom he had taken to himself in Lily's place.", "이제는 영영 저에게서 떠나가 버린 사람으로만 그녀를 생각할 수 있을 뿐이었다. 그리고 그다음에는, 릴리의 자리에 저 스스로 끌어들인 그 여자까지 생각해야 했다."]], [["Young men, very young men,--men so young that it may be almost a question whether or no they have as yet reached their manhood,--are more inclined to be earnest and thoughtful when alone than they ever are when with others, even though those others be their elders.", "젊은이들, 아주 젊은 사내들—아직 어른이 되었다고 할 수 있을지 없을지조차 의문일 만큼 젊은 사내들은, 남들과 함께 있을 때보다 혼자 있을 때 더 진지해지고 더 생각이 깊어지는 법이다. 그 남들이 저보다 나이 든 사람일 때조차 그러하다."], ["I fancy that, as we grow old ourselves, we are apt to forget that it was so with us; and, forgetting it, we do not believe that it is so with our children.", "나는 우리가 나이를 먹어 가면서 우리에게도 그러했다는 사실을 곧잘 잊어버리고, 잊어버린 나머지 우리 아이들도 그러하리라고는 믿지 않게 되는 것이라고 생각한다."], ["We constantly talk of the thoughtlessness of youth.", "우리는 젊음의 생각 없음을 노상 입에 올린다."], ["I do not know whether we might not more appropriately speak of its thoughtfulness.", "나로서는 오히려 젊음의 생각 많음을 말하는 편이 더 알맞지 않을까 싶다."], ["It is, however, no doubt, true that thought will not at once produce wisdom.", "그러나 생각이 곧바로 지혜를 낳지는 않는다는 것 또한 의심할 바 없는 사실이다."], ["It may almost be a question whether such wisdom as many of us have in our mature years has not come from the dying out of the power of temptation, rather than as the results of thought and resolution.", "우리 가운데 많은 사람이 원숙한 나이에 이르러 지니게 된 지혜라는 것이, 생각과 결심의 열매라기보다는 유혹의 힘이 시들어 버린 데서 온 것은 아닌지 거의 의문스러울 지경이다."], ["Men, full fledged and at their work, are, for the most part, too busy for much thought; but lads, on whom the work of the world has not yet fallen with all its pressure,--they have time for thinking.", "다 자라 제 일에 매인 사내들은 대개 너무 바빠서 깊이 생각할 겨를이 없다. 그러나 세상일의 무게가 아직 온전히 내려앉지 않은 소년들에게는—그들에게는 생각할 시간이 있다."]], [["And thus John Eames was thoughtful.", "그리하여 존 임스는 생각이 많았다."], ["They who knew him best accounted him to be a gay, good-hearted, somewhat reckless young man, open to temptation, but also open to good impressions; as to whom no great success could be predicated, but of whom his friends might fairly hope that he might so live as to bring upon them no disgrace and not much trouble.", "그를 가장 잘 안다는 이들은 그를 쾌활하고 마음씨 착하며 다소 무모한 젊은이, 유혹에도 쉬 넘어가지만 좋은 감화도 쉬 받아들이는 젊은이로 여겼다. 대단한 성공을 점칠 수는 없으나 그래도 벗들에게 망신을 안기지 않고 큰 말썽도 일으키지 않을 만큼은 살아가리라고 어지간히 기대해 볼 만한 사람이라고."], ["But, above all things, they would have called him thoughtless.", "그러나 무엇보다도 그들은 그를 생각 없는 사람이라고 불렀을 것이다."], ["In so calling him, they judged him wrong.", "그렇게 부르는 데서 그들은 그를 잘못 보았다."], ["He was ever thinking,--thinking much of the world as it appeared to him, and of himself as he appeared to the world; and thinking, also, of things beyond the world.", "그는 늘 생각하고 있었다—제 눈에 비친 세상을, 그리고 세상의 눈에 비친 저를 골똘히 생각하고 있었다. 또한 세상 너머의 것들까지 생각하고 있었다."], ["What was to be his fate here and hereafter?", "이승에서, 그리고 저승에서 제 운명은 어찌 될 것인가?"], ["Lily Dale was gone from him, and Amelia Roper was hanging round his neck like a millstone!", "릴리 데일은 저에게서 떠나갔고, 어밀리아 로퍼는 맷돌처럼 목에 매달려 있었다!"], ["What, under such circumstances, was to be his fate here and hereafter?", "그런 처지에서 이승과 저승의 제 운명은 어찌 될 것인가?"]], [["We may say that the difficulties in his way were not as yet very great.", "그의 앞을 가로막은 어려움이 아직 그리 대단한 것은 아니었다고 우리는 말할 수 있다."], ["As to Lily, indeed, he had no room for hope; but, then, his love for Lily had, perhaps, been a sentiment rather than a passion.", "릴리에 관해서라면 그에게 희망의 여지가 없기는 했다. 그러나 릴리를 향한 그의 사랑은 아마도 정념이라기보다는 감상에 가까웠다."], ["Most young men have to go through that disappointment, and are enabled to bear it without much injury to their prospects or happiness.", "젊은이라면 대개 그런 실연을 한 번은 겪게 마련이고, 앞날이나 행복에 큰 손상을 입지 않고 그것을 견뎌 낸다."], ["And in after-life the remembrance of such love is a blessing rather than a curse, enabling the possessor of it to feel that in those early days there was something within him of which he had no cause to be ashamed.", "그리고 훗날에 이르면 그런 사랑의 기억은 저주라기보다 축복이 되어, 그것을 지닌 사람으로 하여금 그 이른 시절에 제 안에 부끄러워할 것 없는 무언가가 있었음을 느끼게 해 준다."], ["I do not pity John Eames much in regard to Lily Dale.", "릴리 데일에 관한 한 나는 존 임스를 그리 딱하게 여기지 않는다."], ["And then, as to Amelia Roper,--had he achieved but a tithe of that lady's experience in the world, or possessed a quarter of her audacity, surely such a difficulty as that need not have stood much in his way!", "그리고 어밀리아 로퍼로 말하자면—만일 그가 그 여자가 세상에서 쌓은 경험의 십분의 일이라도 쌓았거나 그 여자의 대담함의 사분의 일이라도 지녔더라면, 그런 어려움쯤이야 그의 앞길을 그토록 가로막지는 못했으리라!"], ["What could Amelia do to him if he fairly told her that he was not minded to marry her?", "그녀와 결혼할 뜻이 없노라고 그가 딱 잘라 말해 버린다면 어밀리아가 그에게 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["In very truth he had never promised to do so.", "실상 그는 그리하겠다고 약속한 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["He was in no way bound to her, not even by honour.", "그는 어떤 식으로도 그녀에게 매여 있지 않았다. 명예로써조차 매여 있지 않았다."], ["Honour, indeed, with such as her!", "명예라니, 그런 여자를 상대로!"], ["But men are cowards before women until they become tyrants; and are easy dupes, till of a sudden they recognize the fact that it is pleasanter to be the victimizer than the victim,--and as easy.", "그러나 사내란 여자 앞에서 폭군이 되기 전까지는 겁쟁이다. 그리고 희생시키는 편이 희생당하는 편보다 더 즐겁고 또 그만큼 쉽다는 사실을 문득 깨닫기 전까지는 손쉬운 봉이다."], ["There are men, indeed, who never learn the latter lesson.", "물론 그 나중 교훈을 끝내 배우지 못하는 사내들도 있다."]], [["But, though the cause for fear was so slight, poor John Eames was thoroughly afraid.", "그러나 두려워할 까닭이 그토록 하잘것없었건만, 가엾은 존 임스는 철저히 겁에 질려 있었다."], ["Little things which, in connection with so deep a sorrow as his, it is almost ridiculous to mention, added to his embarrassments, and made an escape from them seem to him to be impossible.", "그가 품은 그토록 깊은 슬픔과 나란히 놓고 보자면 입에 올리기조차 우스운 자잘한 일들이 그의 난처함을 보태어, 거기서 빠져나가는 일을 도무지 있을 수 없는 일처럼 보이게 만들었다."], ["He could not return to London without going to Burton Crescent, because his clothes were there, and because he owed to Mrs.", "그는 버턴 크레슨트에 들르지 않고서는 런던으로 돌아갈 수가 없었다."], ["Roper some small sum of money which on his return to London he would not have immediately in his pocket.", "옷가지가 거기 있었고, 또 로퍼 부인에게 얼마간의 돈을 빚지고 있는데 런던에 돌아가는 길로 곧장 주머니에서 꺼내 줄 형편이 못 되었기 때문이다."], ["He must therefore meet Amelia, and he knew that he had not the courage to tell a girl, face to face, that he did not love her, after he had been once induced to say that he did do so.", "그러니 그는 어밀리아를 만나야만 했다. 그리고 그는 한번 사랑한다고 말해 버린 뒤에 처녀의 얼굴을 마주하고 사랑하지 않는다고 말할 배짱이 저에게 없다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["His boldest conception did not go beyond the writing of a letter in which he would renounce her, and removing himself altogether from that quarter of the town in which Burton Crescent was situated.", "그가 낼 수 있는 가장 대담한 생각이라야 그녀를 단념하는 편지를 한 통 쓰고 버턴 크레슨트가 있는 그 동네에서 아주 거처를 옮겨 버리는 데까지가 고작이었다."], ["But then about his clothes, and that debt of his?", "그러나 그러면 옷가지는? 또 그 빚은?"], ["And what if Amelia should in the meantime come down to Guestwick and claim him?", "그리고 만일 그러는 사이에 어밀리아가 게스트윅으로 내려와 저를 제 것이라 주장하고 나선다면?"], ["Could he in his mother's presence declare that she had no right to make such claim?", "어머니가 보시는 앞에서 그녀에게 그런 주장을 할 권리가 없다고 잘라 말할 수 있을까?"], ["The difficulties, in truth, were not very great, but they were too heavy for that poor young clerk from the Income-tax Office.", "어려움이란 것이 사실 그리 대단하지는 않았으나, 소득세 사무국의 그 가엾은 젊은 서기가 감당하기에는 너무나 무거웠다."]], [["You will declare that he must have been a fool and a coward.", "그가 바보에 겁쟁이였음이 분명하다고 여러분은 말하리라."], ["Yet he could read and understand Shakspeare.", "그러나 그는 셰익스피어를 읽고 이해할 줄 알았다."], ["He knew much,--by far too much,--of Byron's poetry by heart.", "바이런의 시라면 많이도—너무나도 많이 외우고 있었다."], ["He was a deep critic, often writing down his criticisms in a lengthy journal which he kept.", "그는 깊이 있는 비평가였고, 저 스스로 적어 나가는 긴 일기에 그 비평을 곧잘 써 두기도 했다."], ["He could write quickly, and with understanding; and I may declare that men at his office had already ascertained that he was no fool.", "그는 글을 빠르게, 또 알아듣게 쓸 줄 알았다. 그리고 그가 결코 바보가 아니라는 것은 그의 사무국 사람들이 이미 알아보았다고 나는 잘라 말할 수 있다."], ["He knew his business, and could do it,--as many men failed to do who were much less foolish before the world.", "그는 제 일을 알았고 해낼 줄도 알았다—세상 눈에는 훨씬 덜 어리석어 보이는 많은 사람이 해내지 못한 그 일을."], ["And as to that matter of cowardice, he would have thought it the greatest blessing in the world to be shut up in a room with Crosbie, having permission to fight with him till one of them should have been brought by stress of battle to give up his claim to Lily Dale.", "그리고 겁쟁이 운운하는 문제로 말하자면, 그는 크로즈비와 한 방에 갇혀 둘 중 하나가 싸움에 지쳐 릴리 데일에 대한 제 권리를 내놓을 때까지 맞붙어도 좋다는 허락을 받는다면 그것을 세상에서 가장 큰 복으로 여겼을 것이다. 임스는 겁쟁이가 아니었다."], ["Eames was no coward. He feared no man on earth.", "그는 세상 어떤 사내도 두려워하지 않았다."], ["But he was terribly afraid of Amelia Roper.", "그러나 어밀리아 로퍼만은 지독히도 두려웠다."]], [["He wandered about through the old Manor woods very ill at ease.", "그는 옛 저택 숲속을 마음 편치 못한 채로 이리저리 헤맸다."], ["The post from Guestwick went out at seven, and he must at once make up his mind whether or no he would write to Amelia on that day.", "게스트윅에서 우편은 일곱 시에 나갔고, 그러니 그날 어밀리아에게 편지를 쓸 것인지 말 것인지 당장 마음을 정해야 했다."], ["He must also make up his mind as to what he would say to her.", "또 그녀에게 무슨 말을 할 것인지도 정해야 했다."], ["He felt that he should at least answer her letter, let his answer be what it might.", "답장이 어떤 것이 되든 적어도 답장은 해야 한다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Should he promise to marry her,--say, in ten or twelve years' time?", "결혼하겠다고 약속할까—이를테면 열 해나 열두 해 뒤에?"], ["Should he tell her that he was a blighted being, unfit for love, and with humility entreat of her that he might be excused?", "자기는 사랑에 걸맞지 않은 시들어 버린 존재라고 말하고 겸손히 용서를 빌어 볼까?"], ["Or should he write to her mother, telling her that Burton Crescent would not suit him any longer, promising her to send the balance on receipt of his next payment, and asking her to send his clothes in a bundle to the Income-tax Office?", "아니면 그 어머니에게 편지를 써서 버턴 크레슨트가 이제 저에게는 맞지 않겠노라 하고, 다음 봉급을 받는 대로 남은 돈을 부치겠다고 약속하고, 옷가지는 보따리에 싸서 소득세 사무국으로 보내 달라고 부탁할까?"], ["Or should he go home to his own mother, and boldly tell it all to her?", "아니면 제 어머니에게 돌아가 다 털어놓아 버릴까?"]], [["He at last resolved that he must write the letter, and as he composed it in his mind he sat himself down beneath an old tree which stood on a spot at which many of the forest tracks met and crossed each other.", "마침내 그는 편지를 써야만 한다고 마음을 정했다. 그리고 머릿속으로 편지를 지어 나가면서, 숲속 오솔길 여럿이 만나 서로 엇갈리는 자리에 서 있는 늙은 나무 한 그루 밑에 앉았다."], ["The letter, as he framed it here, was not a bad letter, if only he could have got it written and posted.", "여기서 그가 엮어 낸 편지는, 종이에 옮겨 적어 부치기만 할 수 있었더라면 결코 나쁜 편지가 아니었다."], ["Every word of it he chose with precision, and in his mind he emphasized every expression which told his mind clearly and justified his purpose.", "그는 낱말 하나하나를 꼼꼼히 골랐고, 제 뜻을 또렷이 전하고 제 목적을 옳게 밝혀 주는 표현마다 머릿속에서 힘주어 눌러 두었다."], ["\"He acknowledged himself to have been wrong in misleading his correspondent, and allowing her to imagine that she possessed his heart.", "“그는 편지를 주고받는 상대를 그릇 이끌어 제 마음을 얻은 줄로 여기게 만든 것이 제 잘못이었음을 인정했다."], ["He had not a heart at her disposal.", "그의 마음은 그녀가 마음대로 할 수 있는 것이 아니었다."], ["He had been weak not to write to her before, having been deterred from doing so by the fear of giving her pain; but now he felt that he was bound in honour to tell her the truth.", "진작 편지하지 않은 것은 그가 약했던 탓이니, 그녀에게 아픔을 줄까 두려워 그리하지 못했던 것이다. 그러나 이제 그는 명예를 걸고 그녀에게 진실을 말해야 한다고 느꼈다."], ["Having so told her, he would not return to Burton Crescent, if it would pain her to see him there.", "그렇게 말한 이상, 저를 그곳에서 보는 것이 그녀에게 괴로운 일이라면 그는 버턴 크레슨트로 돌아가지 않겠다."], ["He would always have a deep regard for her,\"--Oh, Johnny!--\"and would hope anxiously that her welfare in life might be complete.\"", "그는 언제까지나 그녀를 깊이 아끼는 마음을 간직할 것이며”—아, 조니!—“그녀의 앞날이 온전히 복되기를 애타게 바라겠노라고.”"], ["That was the letter, as he wrote it on the tablets of his mind under the tree; but the getting it put on to paper was a task, as he knew, of greater difficulty.", "나무 밑에서 그가 마음의 서판에 적어 넣은 편지는 그러했다. 그러나 그것을 종이에 옮겨 적는 일은, 저도 알다시피 훨씬 더 어려운 일이었다."], ["Then, as he repeated it to himself, he fell asleep.", "그러다가 그것을 속으로 되뇌던 그는 잠이 들었다."]], [["\"Young man,\"", "“이보게, 젊은이.”"], ["said a voice in his ears as he slept.", "그가 잠들어 있는데 귓가에서 어떤 목소리가 말했다."], ["At first the voice spoke as a voice from his dream without waking him, but when it was repeated, he sat up and saw that a stout gentleman was standing over him.", "처음에 그 목소리는 그를 깨우지 않은 채 꿈속의 목소리처럼 들렸으나, 그것이 되풀이되자 그는 몸을 일으켜 앉았고 살진 신사 한 사람이 저를 굽어보고 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["For a moment he did not know where he was, or how he had come there; nor could he recollect, as he saw the trees about him, how long he had been in the wood.", "한순간 그는 저가 어디 있는지, 어떻게 거기 오게 되었는지 알지 못했다. 둘러선 나무들을 보면서도 저가 얼마나 오래 숲속에 있었는지 떠올릴 수 없었다."], ["But he knew the stout gentleman well enough, though he had not seen him for more than two years.", "그러나 두 해가 넘도록 뵌 적이 없었건만 그 살진 신사가 누구인지는 잘 알아보았다. “이보게, 젊은이.”"], ["\"Young man,\"", "목소리가 말했다."], ["said the voice, \"if you want to catch rheumatism, that's the way to do it.", "“류머티즘에 걸리고 싶거든 딱 그렇게 하면 되네."], ["Why, it's young Eames, isn't it?\"", "아니, 임스네 아들 아닌가?”"]], [["\"Yes, my lord,\"", "“네, 백작님.”"], ["said Johnny, raising himself up so that he was now sitting, instead of lying, as he looked up into the earl's rosy face.", "조니가 이렇게 말하며 몸을 추슬러, 누워 있던 자세에서 이제는 앉은 자세가 되어 백작의 발그레한 얼굴을 올려다보았다."]], [["\"I knew your father, and a very good man he was; only he shouldn't have taken to farming.", "“자네 아버님을 내가 알았지, 아주 좋은 분이었네. 다만 농사에 손을 대지 말았어야 했어."], ["People think they can farm without learning the trade, but that's a very great mistake.", "사람들은 배우지 않고도 농사를 지을 수 있는 줄 알지만, 그건 크게 잘못 아는 걸세."], ["I can farm, because I've learned it.", "나는 농사를 지을 줄 아네, 배웠으니까."], ["Don't you think you'd better get up?\"", "그만 일어나는 게 좋지 않겠나?”"], ["Whereupon Johnny raised himself to his feet.", "그 말에 조니는 몸을 일으켜 섰다."], ["\"Not but what you're very welcome to lie there if you like it.", "“뭐, 자네가 좋다면 거기 누워 있어도 얼마든지 괜찮네만."], ["Only, in October, you know--\"", "다만 시월이라는 게 말이야—”"]], [["\"I'm afraid I'm trespassing, my lord,\"", "“함부로 들어와 죄송합니다, 백작님.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."], ["\"I came in off the path, and--\"", "“길에서 벗어나 들어왔다가, 그만—”"]], [["\"You're welcome; you're very welcome.", "“괜찮네, 얼마든지 괜찮아."], ["If you'll come up to the house, I'll give you some luncheon.\"", "집까지 올라오면 점심을 좀 주겠네.”"], ["This hospitable offer, however, Johnny declined, alleging that it was late, and that he was going home to dinner.", "그러나 이 후한 제안을 조니는 거절하며, 시간이 늦었고 저녁을 먹으러 집에 가는 길이라고 둘러댔다."]], [["\"Come along,\"", "“따라오게.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"You can't go any shorter way than by the house.", "“집을 지나는 길보다 가까운 길은 없네."], ["Dear, dear, how well I remember your father.", "저런, 저런, 자네 아버님이 어찌나 또렷이 기억나는지."], ["He was a much cleverer man than I am,--very much; but he didn't know how to send a beast to market any better than a child.", "나보다 훨씬 영리한 분이었지—훨씬 더. 그런데 짐승을 장에 내다 파는 일은 어린애만큼도 몰랐단 말이야."], ["By-the-by, they have put you into a public office, haven't they?\"", "참, 그러고 보니 자네를 관청에 넣어 주었다지, 그렇지 않은가?”"]], [["\"Yes, my lord.\"", "“네, 백작님.”"]], [["\"And a very good thing, too,--a very good thing, indeed.", "“그것도 아주 잘한 일이지—참으로 잘한 일이야."], ["But why were you asleep in the wood?", "그런데 어째서 숲속에서 잠이 들었나?"], ["It isn't warm, you know.", "날이 따뜻하지도 않은데 말일세."], ["I call it rather cold.\"", "나는 도리어 춥다고 하겠네.”"], ["And the earl stopped, and looked at him, scrutinizing him, as though resolved to inquire into so deep a mystery.", "그러고서 백작은 걸음을 멈추고, 그토록 깊은 수수께끼는 기어이 캐어 보고야 말겠다는 듯 그를 찬찬히 뜯어보며 바라보았다."]], [["\"I was taking a walk, and thinking of something, I sat down.\"", "“산책을 하다가, 무언가 생각할 것이 있어서 앉았습니다.”"]], [["\"Leave of absence, I suppose?\"", "“휴가를 받았나 보군?”"]], [["\"Yes, my lord.\"", "“네, 백작님.”"]], [["\"Have you got into trouble?", "“무슨 곤경에 빠진 겐가?"], ["You look as though you were in trouble.", "곤경에 빠진 얼굴인데."], ["Your poor father used to be in trouble.\"", "자네 가엾은 아버님도 곧잘 곤경에 빠지곤 했지.”"]], [["\"I haven't taken to farming,\"", "“저는 농사에 손을 대지는 않았습니다.”"], ["said Johnny, with an attempt at a smile.", "조니가 웃어 보이려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["\"Ha, ha, ha,--quite right.", "“하, 하, 하—옳은 말이야."], ["No, don't take to farming.", "암, 농사에는 손대지 말게."], ["Unless you learn it, you know, you might just as well take to shoemaking;--just the same.", "배우지 않고 할 바에야 구둣방을 차리는 편이 나을 걸세—꼭 마찬가지야."], ["You haven't got into trouble, then; eh?\"", "그러니까 곤경에 빠진 건 아니란 말이지, 응?”"]], [["\"No, my lord, not particularly.\"", "“아닙니다, 백작님, 딱히 그런 건 아닙니다.”"]], [["\"Not particularly!", "“딱히 그런 건 아니라니!"], ["I know very well that young men do get into trouble when they get up to London.", "젊은 사람이 런던에 올라가면 곤경에 빠지게 마련이라는 걸 나도 잘 아네."], ["If you want any--any advice, or that sort of thing, you may come to me; for I knew your father well.", "무엇이든—조언이든 그런 것이든 필요하거든 나를 찾아오게. 자네 아버님을 내가 잘 알았으니 말일세."], ["Do you like shooting?\"", "사냥은 좋아하나?”"]], [["\"I never did shoot anything.\"", "“저는 무얼 쏘아 본 적이 없습니다.”"]], [["\"Well, perhaps better not.", "“그래, 어쩌면 안 하는 편이 낫지."], ["To tell the truth, I'm not very fond of young men who take to shooting without having anything to shoot at.", "사실 나는 쏠 것도 없으면서 총질에 맛을 들이는 젊은이들을 그리 좋아하지 않네."], ["By-the-by, now I think of it, I'll send your mother some game.\"", "참, 그러고 보니 자네 어머님께 사냥한 고기를 좀 보내야겠군.”"], ["It may, however, here be fair to mention that game very often came from Guestwick Manor to Mrs. Eames.", "그러나 여기서 밝혀 두는 것이 온당하겠는데, 게스트윅 저택에서 임스 부인에게 사냥한 고기가 오는 일은 아주 잦았다."], ["\"And look here, cold pheasant for breakfast is the best thing I know of.", "“그리고 이보게, 아침 식사에는 식은 꿩고기만 한 것이 없네."], ["Pheasants at dinner are rubbish,--mere rubbish.", "만찬에 꿩이라니 쓰레기지—순전히 쓰레기야."], ["Here we are at the house.", "자, 집에 다 왔네."], ["Will you come in and have a glass of wine?\"", "들어와서 포도주 한잔하지 않겠나?”"]], [["But this John Eames declined, pleasing the earl better by doing so than he would have done by accepting it.", "그러나 이것도 존 임스는 거절했고, 그리함으로써 받아들였을 때보다 백작을 더 흡족하게 해 주었다."], ["Not that the lord was inhospitable or insincere in his offer, but he preferred that such a one as John Eames should receive his proffered familiarity without too much immediate assurance.", "백작이 손님 대접에 인색하다거나 마음에 없는 말을 한 것은 아니었다. 다만 그는 존 임스 같은 사람이라면 저가 베푼 친근함을 그 자리에서 넙죽 받아들이지는 않기를 바랐던 것이다."], ["He felt that Eames was a little in awe of his companion's rank, and he liked him the better for it.", "백작은 임스가 저와 함께 걷는 이의 지체 앞에서 얼마간 주눅 들어 있음을 알아차렸고, 그 때문에 그가 더 마음에 들었다."], ["He liked him the better for it, and was a man apt to remember his likings.", "그 때문에 그가 더 마음에 들었거니와, 백작은 한번 마음에 든 것을 잘 잊지 않는 사람이었다."], ["\"If you won't come in, good-by,\"", "“들어오지 않겠다면 잘 가게.”"], ["and he gave Johnny his hand.", "그러고서 그는 조니에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["\"Good evening, my lord,\"", "“안녕히 계십시오, 백작님.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"And remember this; it is the deuce of a thing to have rheumatism in your loins.", "“그리고 이것만은 새겨 두게. 허리에 류머티즘이 오면 그야말로 지독한 일일세."], ["I wouldn't go to sleep under a tree, if I were you,--not in October.", "나 같으면 나무 밑에서 잠들지는 않겠네—적어도 시월에는 말이야."], ["But you're always welcome to go anywhere about the place.\"", "하지만 이 근방이라면 어디든 자네는 언제든 와도 좋네.”"]], [["\"Thank you, my lord.\"", "“고맙습니다, 백작님.”"]], [["\"And if you should take to shooting,--but I dare say you won't; and if you come to trouble, and want advice, or that sort of thing, write to me.", "“그리고 혹시라도 사냥에 맛을 들이거든—뭐, 자네는 안 그럴 성싶네만. 그리고 곤경에 빠져 조언이든 그런 것이든 필요하거든 내게 편지하게."], ["I knew your father well.\"", "자네 아버님을 내가 잘 알았네.”"], ["And so they parted, Eames returning on his road towards Guestwick.", "그리하여 두 사람은 헤어졌고, 임스는 게스트윅으로 가는 길로 돌아섰다."]], [["For some reason, which he could not define, he felt better after his interview with the earl.", "무슨 까닭인지는 저도 꼬집어 말할 수 없었으나, 백작과 이야기를 나누고 나서 그는 기분이 나아졌다."], ["There had been something about the fat, good-natured, sensible old man, which had cheered him, in spite of his sorrow.", "그 뚱뚱하고 사람 좋고 지각 있는 노인에게는, 슬픔에 잠긴 그를 그런데도 북돋워 주는 무언가가 있었다."], ["\"Pheasants for dinner are rubbish,--mere rubbish,\"", "“만찬에 꿩이라니 쓰레기지—순전히 쓰레기야.”"], ["he said to himself, over and over again, as he went along the road; and they were the first words which he spoke to his mother, after entering the house.", "그는 길을 가는 내내 몇 번이고 되뇌었다. 그리고 그것이 집에 들어서고 나서 어머니에게 처음으로 한 말이었다."]], [["\"I wish we had some of that sort of rubbish,\"", "“우리한테도 그런 쓰레기가 좀 있었으면 좋겠구나.”"], ["said she.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["\"So you will, to-morrow;\"", "“내일이면 생길 겁니다.”"], ["and then he described to her his interview.", "그러고서 그는 어머니에게 그 만남을 이야기해 드렸다."]], [["\"The earl was, at any rate, quite right about lying upon the ground.", "“땅바닥에 드러누운 일만은 백작님 말씀이 지당하시다."], ["I wonder you can be so foolish.", "어쩌면 그렇게 어리석을 수가 있느냐."], ["And he is right about your poor father too.", "그리고 네 가엾은 아버지에 대해서도 그분 말씀이 옳고."], ["But you have got to change your boots; and we shall be ready for dinner almost immediately.\"", "그건 그렇고 너는 신발을 갈아 신어야겠다. 저녁상은 곧 차려질 게다.”"]], [["But Johnny Eames, before he sat down to dinner, did write his letter to Amelia, and did go out to post it with his own hands,--much to his mother's annoyance.", "그러나 조니 임스는 저녁상에 앉기 전에 어밀리아에게 보낼 편지를 쓰기는 썼고, 제 손으로 부치러 나가기도 했다—어머니에게는 몹시도 못마땅한 일이었다."], ["But the letter would not get itself written in that strong and appropriate language which had come to him as he was roaming through the woods.", "그러나 그 편지는 숲속을 헤맬 때 떠올랐던 그 힘 있고 알맞은 말로는 도무지 쓰이지 않았다."], ["It was a bald letter, and somewhat cowardly withal.", "그것은 맹숭맹숭한 편지였고, 게다가 어딘가 비겁한 편지였다."]], [["DEAR AMELIA [the letter ran],--I have received both of yours; and did not answer the first because I felt that there was a difficulty in expressing what I wish to say; and now it will be better that you should allow the subject to stand over till I am back in town.", "친애하는 어밀리아에게 [편지는 이러했다],—보내신 두 통 다 받았습니다. 첫 번째 편지에 답하지 못한 것은 제가 하고 싶은 말을 표현하기가 어렵다고 느꼈기 때문입니다."], ["I shall be there in ten days from this.", "그리고 이제는 제가 시내로 돌아갈 때까지 그 이야기를 접어 두시는 편이 좋겠습니다."], ["I have been quite well, and am so; but of course am much obliged by your inquiries.", "저는 지금부터 열흘 뒤에 그곳에 가 있을 것입니다. 저는 아주 잘 지내 왔고 지금도 그러합니다."], ["I know you will think this very cold; but when I tell you everything, you will agree with me that it is best.", "물론 물어봐 주신 것은 대단히 고맙게 여깁니다. 이 말이 아주 쌀쌀맞게 들리리라는 것을 압니다."], ["If I were to marry, I know that we should be unhappy, because we should have nothing to live on.", "그러나 제가 모든 것을 다 말씀드리면, 이것이 최선이라는 데 당신도 저와 같은 생각이 되실 것입니다. 만일 제가 결혼을 한다면 우리는 불행해지고 말리라는 것을 저는 압니다."], ["If I have ever said anything to deceive you, I beg your pardon with all my heart;--but perhaps it will be better to let the subject remain till we shall meet again in London.", "먹고살 것이 아무것도 없을 테니까요. 제가 혹 당신을 속이는 말을 한 적이 있다면 온 마음을 다해 용서를 빕니다.—그러나 아무래도 그 이야기는 우리가 런던에서 다시 만날 때까지 접어 두는 편이 좋겠습니다."]], [["Believe me to be Your most sincere friend, And I may say admirer,--[Oh, John Eames!]", "믿어 주십시오 당신의 더없이 진실한 벗이며, 또한 흠모하는 사람이라고 해도 좋을,—[아, 존 임스!]"]], [["# CHAPTER XV. THE LAST DAY.", "# 제15장. 마지막 날"]], [["Last days are wretched days; and so are last moments wretched moments.", "마지막 날이란 비참한 날이다. 그리고 마지막 순간 또한 비참한 순간이다."], ["It is not the fact that the parting is coming which makes these days and moments so wretched, but the feeling that something special is expected from them, which something they always fail to produce.", "이런 날과 순간을 그토록 비참하게 만드는 것은 이별이 다가온다는 사실이 아니라, 그날과 그 순간에서 무언가 특별한 것이 기대된다는 느낌이다. 그런데 그 무언가는 언제나 나오지 않고 만다."], ["Spasmodic periods of pleasure, of affection, or even of study, seldom fail of disappointment when premeditated.", "즐거움이든 애정이든 심지어 공부든, 발작하듯 몰아치는 시간은 미리 계획해 두면 실망으로 끝나지 않는 법이 드물다."], ["When last days are coming, they should be allowed to come and to glide away without special notice or mention.", "마지막 날이 다가올 때에는 특별히 알리거나 입에 올리지 말고 그저 오게 두었다가 그대로 흘러가게 두어야 한다."], ["And as for last moments, there should be none such.", "그리고 마지막 순간으로 말하자면, 그런 것은 아예 없어야 한다."], ["Let them ever be ended, even before their presence has been acknowledged.", "그 존재를 인정하기도 전에 끝나 버리게 두라."]], [["But Lily Dale had not yet been taught these lessons by her world's experience, and she expected that this sweetest cup of which she had ever drank should go on being sweet--sweeter and still sweeter--as long as she could press it to her lips.", "그러나 릴리 데일은 아직 세상 경험으로부터 이런 교훈을 배우지 못했고, 그래서 자기가 지금껏 마셔 본 것 가운데 가장 달콤한 이 잔이 입술에 댈 수 있는 동안은 계속 달콤하기를, 더 달게 더욱더 달게 달기를 기대했다. 그 마지막 몇 방울에 어떻게 앙금이 섞여 들었는지는 우리가 이미 본 바와 같다."], ["How the dregs had come to mix themselves with the last drops we have already seen; and on that same day--on the Monday evening--the bitter task still remained; for Crosbie, as they walked about through the gardens in the evening, found other subjects on which he thought it necessary to give her sundry hints, intended for her edification, which came to her with much of the savour of a lecture.", "그리고 바로 그날, 월요일 저녁에도 쓰디쓴 과업은 여전히 남아 있었다. 저녁에 정원을 거니는 동안 크로즈비가 다른 화제들을 찾아내어, 그녀를 깨우칠 요량으로 이런저런 암시를 주는 것이 필요하다고 여겼기 때문인데, 그 암시는 훈계의 냄새를 잔뜩 풍기며 그녀에게 다가왔다."], ["A girl, when she is thoroughly in love, as surely was the case with Lily, likes to receive hints as to her future life from the man to whom she is devoted; but she would, I think, prefer that such hints should be short, and that the lesson should be implied rather than declared;--that they should, in fact, be hints and not lectures.", "아가씨란, 릴리가 분명히 그러했듯 온전히 사랑에 빠져 있을 때에는, 자기가 몸과 마음을 바친 남자에게서 앞날의 삶에 관한 암시를 받는 것을 좋아한다. 그러나 내 생각으로는, 그런 암시가 짧기를, 그리고 그 가르침이 선언되기보다 넌지시 담기기를 바랄 것이다."], ["Crosbie, who was a man of tact, who understood the world and had been dealing with women for many years, no doubt understood all this as well as we do.", "다시 말해 그것이 훈계가 아니라 암시이기를 바랄 것이다. 크로즈비는 요령 있는 남자였고, 세상을 알았으며, 여러 해 동안 여자들을 상대해 온 사람이었으니, 이 모든 것을 우리만큼이나 잘 알고 있었음에 틀림없다."], ["But he had come to entertain a notion that he was an injured man, that he was giving very much more than was to be given to him, and that therefore he was entitled to take liberties which might not fairly be within the reach of another lover.", "그러나 그는 자기가 손해를 보는 사람이라는 생각, 자기가 받는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이 주고 있다는 생각, 따라서 다른 연인에게라면 정당하게 허용되지 않을 방자함을 자기는 부려도 된다는 생각을 품게 되어 있었다. 내 독자는 이 모든 점에서 그가 너그럽지 못했다고 말할 것이다."], ["My reader will say that in all this he was ungenerous.", "그렇다. 그는 너그럽지 못했다."], ["Well; he was ungenerous.", "나는 그에게서 큰 너그러움을 기대할 만하다고 말한 적이 한 번도 없다고 생각한다."], ["I do not know that I have ever said that much generosity was to be expected from him.", "그에게도 옳고 그름에 관한 몇 가지 원칙은 있었고, 그 인도를 받아 아주 못쓰게 빗나가지는 않으리라고 어쩌면 바라볼 수도 있었다. 그러나 그의 지난 삶은 그를 이기심 없는 사람으로 만들어 줄 만한 종류의 것이 아니었다."], ["He had some principles of right and wrong under the guidance of which it may perhaps be hoped that he will not go utterly astray; but his past life had not been of a nature to make him unselfish.", "그는 너그럽지 못했고, 릴리도 그것을 느꼈다. 다만 그녀는 자기 자신에게조차 그것을 인정하려 들지 않았다."], ["He was ungenerous, and Lily felt it, though she would not acknowledge it even to herself.", "그녀는 그에게 아주 솔직했다. 그를 향한 자기 사랑의 깊이를 털어놓았고, 이제 그가 자기에게 전부라고, 그의 사랑 없는 삶은 자기로서는 불가능하다고 말했다."], ["She had been very open with him,--acknowledging the depth of her love for him; telling him that he was now all in all to her; that life without his love would be impossible to her: and in a certain way he took advantage of these strong avowals, treating her as though she were a creature utterly in his power;--as indeed she was.", "그런데 그는 어떤 면에서 이 강렬한 고백들을 이용하여, 그녀가 온전히 자기 손아귀에 들어 있는 존재인 양 그녀를 대했다. 실제로 그녀는 그러했다."]], [["On that evening he said no more of Johnny Eames, but said much of the difficulty of a man establishing himself with a wife in London, who had nothing but his own moderate income on which to rely.", "그날 저녁 그는 조니 임스에 관해서는 더 말하지 않았으나, 자기 자신의 그리 넉넉지 않은 수입 말고는 기댈 것이 없는 남자가 런던에서 아내를 두고 살림을 차리는 일이 얼마나 어려운지에 대해서는 많은 말을 했다."], ["He did not in so many words tell her that if her friends could make up for her two or three thousand pounds,--that being much less than he had expected when he first made his offer,--this terrible difficulty would be removed; but he said enough to make her understand that the world would call him very imprudent in taking a girl who had nothing.", "그녀의 집안이 그녀 몫으로 이삼천 파운드쯤, 그러니까 그가 처음 청혼했을 때 기대했던 것보다는 훨씬 적은 액수나마 마련해 줄 수 있다면 이 무서운 어려움이 사라질 것이라고 대놓고 말하지는 않았다. 그러나 아무것도 가진 것 없는 아가씨를 데려간다면 세상이 그를 몹시 경솔하다고 부르리라는 것만은 그녀가 알아듣도록 충분히 말했다."], ["And as he spoke of these things, Lily remaining for the most part silent as he did so, it occurred to him that he might talk to her freely of his past life,--more freely than he would have done had he feared that he might lose her by any such disclosures.", "그리고 그가 이런 이야기를 하는 동안 릴리는 대체로 잠자코 있었는데, 그러다 문득 그는 자기 지난 삶에 관해서도 그녀에게 마음 놓고 이야기할 수 있겠다는 생각이 들었다. 그런 고백으로 그녀를 잃을지 모른다는 두려움이 있었다면 결코 그러지 못했을 만큼 마음 놓고 말이다."], ["He had no fear of losing her.", "그는 그녀를 잃을까 두려워하지 않았다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["might it not be possible that he had some such hope!", "어쩌면 그런 희망을 조금 품고 있었던 것은 아닐까!"]], [["He told her that his past life had been expensive; that, though he was not in debt, he had lived up to every shilling that he had, and that he had contracted habits of expenditure which it would be almost impossible for him to lay aside at a day's notice.", "그는 그녀에게 자기 지난 삶이 돈이 많이 드는 삶이었다고 말했다. 빚은 없지만 가진 돈은 한 실링까지 다 쓰며 살아왔고, 하루아침에 내려놓기란 거의 불가능한 씀씀이 습관이 몸에 배었다고 했다."], ["Then he spoke of entanglements, meaning, as he did so, to explain more fully what were their nature,--but not daring to do so when he found that Lily was altogether in the dark as to what he meant.", "그러고 나서 그는 얽힌 일들에 관해 말했는데, 말하면서는 그것이 어떤 성질의 것인지 더 자세히 밝힐 작정이었으나, 릴리가 자기 말뜻을 전혀 알아듣지 못하는 것을 보고는 감히 그러지 못했다."], ["No; he was not a generous man,--a very ungenerous man.", "아니, 그는 너그러운 남자가 아니었다. 지극히 너그럽지 못한 남자였다."], ["And yet, during all this time, he thought that he was guided by principle.", "그런데도 이 모든 시간 내내 그는 자기가 원칙에 이끌려 움직이고 있다고 생각했다."], ["\"It will be best that I should be honest with her,\"", "“그녀에게 솔직한 편이 가장 낫다.”"], ["he said to himself.", "그는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["And then he told himself, scores of times, that when making his offer he had expected, and had a right to expect, that she would not be penniless.", "그러고는 청혼할 때 자기는 그녀가 무일푼이 아니리라 기대했으며 또 그렇게 기대할 권리가 있었다고 수없이 되뇌었다."], ["Under those circumstances he had done the best he could for her--offering her his heart honestly, with a quick readiness to make her his own at the earliest day that she might think possible.", "그런 사정에서 그는 그녀를 위해 최선을 다한 것이었다. 정직하게 자기 마음을 바쳤고, 그녀가 가능하다고 여기는 가장 이른 날에 그녀를 자기 사람으로 삼으려고 선뜻 서둘렀으니 말이다."], ["Had he been more cautious, he need not have fallen into this cruel mistake; but she, at any rate, could not quarrel with him for his imprudence.", "좀 더 조심했더라면 이런 잔인한 실수에 빠지지 않아도 되었을 것이다. 그러나 적어도 그녀만은 그의 경솔함을 두고 그와 다툴 수 없을 터였다."], ["And still he was determined to stand by his engagement and willing to marry her, although, as he the more thought of it, he felt the more strongly that he would thereby ruin his prospects, and thrust beyond his own reach all those good things which he had hoped to win.", "그리고 그는 여전히 약혼을 지키기로 마음먹고 있었고 그녀와 결혼할 뜻도 있었다. 다만 생각하면 할수록, 그렇게 함으로써 자기 앞날을 망치고 얻고자 바라 온 그 모든 좋은 것들을 제 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 밀어내 버리게 되리라는 느낌이 점점 강해질 뿐이었다."], ["As he continued to talk to her he gave himself special credit for his generosity, and felt that he was only doing his duty by her in pointing out to her all the difficulties which lay in the way of their marriage.", "그녀에게 이야기를 계속하는 동안 그는 자기 너그러움에 각별한 공을 돌렸고, 두 사람의 결혼 앞에 놓인 온갖 어려움을 그녀에게 짚어 주는 것이야말로 그녀에 대한 자기 의무를 다하는 일이라고 느꼈다."]], [["At first Lily said some words intended to convey an assurance that she would be the most economical wife that man ever had, but she soon ceased from such promises as these.", "처음에 릴리는 자기가 세상 어느 남자가 가져 본 것보다 알뜰한 아내가 되겠다는 뜻의 말을 몇 마디 했으나, 곧 그런 약속을 그만두었다."], ["Her perceptions were keen, and she discovered that the difficulties of which he was afraid were those which he must overcome before his marriage, not any which might be expected to overwhelm him after it.", "그녀는 눈치가 빨랐고, 그가 두려워하는 어려움이란 결혼한 뒤에 그를 짓누르리라 예상되는 것이 아니라 결혼 전에 그가 넘어야 할 것이라는 사실을 알아차렸다."], ["\"A cheap and nasty ménage would be my aversion,\"", "“싸구려에 지저분한 살림살이는 내가 질색하는 것이오.”"], ["he said to her.", "그가 그녀에게 말했다."], ["\"It is that which I want to avoid,--chiefly for your sake.\"", "“내가 피하고 싶은 것이 바로 그거요. 무엇보다 당신을 위해서 말이오.”"], ["Then she promised him that she would wait patiently for his time--\"even though it should be for seven years,\"", "그러자 그녀는 그의 때가 올 때까지 참을성 있게 기다리겠노라고 약속했다. “설령 칠 년이 걸린다 해도요.”"], ["she said, looking up into his face and trying to find there some sign of approbation.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하며 그의 얼굴을 올려다보고 거기서 무슨 인정의 기색이라도 찾아보려 했다. “그건 말도 안 되는 소리요.”"], ["\"That's nonsense,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said. \"People are not patriarchs now-a-days.", "“요즘 사람들은 족장 시대 사람이 아니오."], ["I suppose we shall have to wait two years.", "아마 이 년은 기다려야 할 거요."], ["And that's a deuce of a bore,--a terrible bore.\"", "그것만 해도 지독한 골칫거리지. 정말 끔찍한 골칫거리요.”"], ["And there was that in the tone of his voice which grated on her feelings, and made her wretched for the moment.", "그리고 그의 목소리에는 그녀의 감정을 긁는 무언가가 있어서, 그 순간 그녀를 비참하게 만들었다."]], [["As he parted with her for the night on her own side of the little bridge which led from one garden to the other, he put his arm round her to embrace her and kiss her, as he had often done at that spot.", "한쪽 정원에서 다른 쪽 정원으로 이어지는 작은 다리의 그녀 쪽 끝에서 밤 인사를 하며 헤어질 때, 그는 그 자리에서 여러 번 그랬듯 그녀를 껴안고 입 맞추려고 팔을 둘렀다."], ["It had become a habit with them to say their evening farewells there, and the secluded little nook amongst the shrubs was inexpressibly dear to Lily.", "거기서 저녁 작별 인사를 하는 것이 두 사람의 버릇이 되어 있었고, 관목 사이의 그 호젓한 구석은 릴리에게 말할 수 없이 소중했다."], ["But on the present occasion she made an effort to avoid his caress.", "그러나 이번만은 그녀가 그의 포옹을 피하려 했다. 그녀는 그에게서 몸을 돌렸다."], ["She turned from him--very slightly, but it was enough, and he felt it.", "아주 조금이었지만 그것으로 충분했고, 그는 그것을 느꼈다. “나한테 화가 났소?”"], ["\"Are you angry with me?\"", "그가 말했다. “아, 아니에요!"], ["he said.", "어돌퍼스."], ["\"Oh, no! Adolphus; how can I be angry with you?\"", "제가 어떻게 당신한테 화를 내겠어요?”"], ["And then she turned to him and gave him her face to kiss almost before he had again asked for it.", "그러고는 그를 향해 돌아서서, 그가 다시 청하기도 전에 얼굴을 내밀어 입 맞추게 했다."], ["\"He shall not at any rate think that I am unkind to him,--and it will not matter now,\"", "“적어도 내가 저이한테 매정하게 굴었다고는 생각하지 않게 해야지. 그리고 이제 와서 그게 무슨 상관이야.”"], ["she said to herself, as she walked slowly across the lawn, in the dark, up to her mother's drawing-room window.", "그녀는 어둠 속에서 잔디밭을 가로질러 어머니의 응접실 창문 쪽으로 천천히 걸어가며 스스로에게 말했다."]], [["\"Well, dearest,\" said Mrs.", "“그래, 우리 딸.”"], ["Dale, who was there alone; \"did the beards wag merry in the Great Hall this evening?\"", "혼자 있던 데일 부인이 말했다. “오늘 저녁 큰 집 홀에서는 수염들이 즐겁게 흔들리더냐?”"], ["That was a joke with them, for neither Crosbie nor Bernard Dale used a razor at his toilet.", "그것은 두 사람 사이의 농담이었으니, 크로즈비도 버나드 데일도 몸단장할 때 면도칼을 쓰지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["\"Not specially merry.", "“딱히 즐겁진 않았어요."], ["And I think it was my fault, for I have a headache.", "그리고 그건 제 탓이에요. 머리가 아파서요."], ["Mamma, I believe I will go at once to bed.\"", "엄마, 저 그냥 바로 자러 갈까 봐요.”"]], [["\"My darling, is there anything wrong?\"", "“얘야, 무슨 안 좋은 일이라도 있니?”"]], [["\"Nothing, mamma.", "“아무것도 아니에요, 엄마. 다만 오늘 말을 아주 오래 탔고요."], ["But we had such a long ride; and then Adolphus is going, and of course we have so much to say.", "그리고 어돌퍼스가 떠나잖아요. 물론 우리도 할 이야기가 많고요."], ["To-morrow will be the last day, for I shall only just see him on Wednesday morning; and as I want to be well, if possible, I'll go to bed.\"", "내일이 마지막 날이에요. 수요일 아침에는 잠깐 얼굴만 볼 테니까요."], ["And so she took her candle and went.", "그러니 될 수 있으면 몸이 괜찮았으면 해서, 자러 갈래요.” 그러고서 그녀는 촛불을 들고 갔다."]], [["When Bell came up, Lily was still awake, but she begged her sister not to disturb her.", "벨이 올라왔을 때 릴리는 아직 깨어 있었지만, 언니에게 자기를 건드리지 말아 달라고 부탁했다."], ["\"Don't talk to me, Bell,\" she said.", "“나한테 말 걸지 마, 언니.”"], ["\"I'm trying to make myself quiet, and I half feel that I should get childish if I went on talking.", "그녀가 말했다. “마음을 가라앉히려는 중인데, 계속 이야기하면 어린애처럼 굴게 될 것 같아."], ["I have almost more to think of than I know how to manage.\"", "감당할 수 있는 것보다 더 많이 생각할 게 있거든.”"], ["And she strove, not altogether unsuccessfully, to speak with a cheery tone, as though the cares which weighed upon her were not unpleasant in their nature.", "그러고서 그녀는 자기를 짓누르는 근심이 성질상 언짢은 것은 아니라는 듯 명랑한 어조로 말하려고 애썼고, 아주 실패하지는 않았다."], ["Then her sister kissed her and left her to her thoughts.", "그러자 언니는 그녀에게 입 맞추고 그녀를 그녀 자신의 생각에 맡겨 두었다."]], [["And she had great matter for thinking; so great, that many hours sounded in her ears from the clock on the stairs before she brought her thoughts to a shape that satisfied herself.", "그리고 그녀에게는 생각할 거리가 컸다. 어찌나 컸던지, 계단의 시계가 여러 시각을 울려 그녀의 귀에 들려온 뒤에야 비로소 자기 마음에 흡족한 모양으로 생각을 정리할 수 있었다."], ["She did so bring them at last, and then she slept.", "마침내 그녀는 그렇게 정리했고, 그러고서 잠이 들었다."], ["She did so bring them, toiling over her work with tears that made her pillow wet, with heart-burning and almost with heart-breaking, with much doubting, and many anxious, eager inquiries within her own bosom as to that which she ought to do, and that which she could endure to do.", "베개를 적시는 눈물과 함께, 가슴이 타고 거의 찢어지는 심정으로, 숱한 의심과 함께, 그리고 자기가 마땅히 해야 할 일과 자기가 견뎌 낼 수 있는 일에 관해 제 가슴속에 던진 많은 불안하고 간절한 물음과 함께, 그 일을 힘겹게 해내고서야 그녀는 생각을 그렇게 정리했다."], ["But at last her resolve was taken, and then she slept.", "그러나 마침내 결심이 섰고, 그러고서 그녀는 잠들었다."]], [["It had been agreed between them that Crosbie should come down to the Small House on the next day after breakfast, and remain there till the time came for riding.", "크로즈비가 이튿날 아침 식사 뒤에 작은 집으로 내려와서 말을 타러 나갈 때까지 거기 머무르기로 두 사람 사이에 정해져 있었다."], ["But Lily determined to alter this arrangement, and accordingly put on her hat immediately after breakfast, and posted herself at the bridge, so as to intercept her lover as he came.", "그러나 릴리는 이 약속을 바꾸기로 마음먹고, 그리하여 아침을 먹자마자 모자를 쓰고 다리에 나가 서서 연인이 오는 길목을 지켰다."], ["He soon appeared with his friend Dale, and she at once told him her purpose.", "그는 곧 친구 데일과 함께 나타났고, 그녀는 대뜸 자기 뜻을 그에게 말했다."]], [["\"I want to have a talk with you, Adolphus, before you go in to mamma; so come with me into the field.\"", "“당신하고 이야기가 하고 싶어요, 어돌퍼스. 엄마한테 들어가시기 전에요. 그러니까 저랑 같이 들판으로 가요.”"]], [["\"All right,\"", "“좋소.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"And Bernard can finish his cigar on the lawn.", "“그리고 버나드는 잔디밭에서 담배나 마저 피우면 돼."], ["Mamma and Bell will join him there.\"", "엄마랑 언니가 그리로 나올 테니까.”"]], [["\"All right,\"", "“좋아.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."], ["So they separated; and Crosbie went away with Lily into the field where they had first learned to know each other in those haymaking days.", "그렇게 그들은 갈라졌고, 크로즈비는 릴리와 함께 들판으로 갔다. 건초를 만들던 그 시절에 두 사람이 처음으로 서로를 알게 된 바로 그 들판이었다."]], [["She did not say much till they were well away from the house; but answered what words he chose to speak,--not knowing very well of what he spoke.", "집에서 멀찍이 벗어날 때까지 그녀는 별말을 하지 않았고, 그가 골라 하는 말에 대답만 했다. 그가 무슨 이야기를 하는지도 잘 모르는 채였다."], ["But when she considered that they had reached the proper spot, she began very abruptly.", "그러나 알맞은 자리에 이르렀다고 여겨지자 그녀는 아주 불쑥 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"Adolphus,\"", "“어돌퍼스.”"], ["she said, \"I have something to say to you,--something to which you must listen very carefully.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “당신한테 할 이야기가 있어요."], ["Then he looked at her, and at once knew that she was in earnest.", "아주 주의 깊게 들어 주셔야 할 이야기예요.” 그러자 그는 그녀를 바라보았고, 곧 그녀가 진심임을 알았다."]], [["\"This is the last day on which I could say it,\"", "“이 이야기를 할 수 있는 건 오늘이 마지막 날이에요.”"], ["she continued; \"and I am very glad that I have not let the last day go by without saying it.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. “그리고 마지막 날을 그냥 흘려보내지 않고 이 말을 하게 되어 참 다행이에요."], ["I should not have known how to put it in a letter.\"", "편지로는 어떻게 써야 할지 몰랐을 거예요.”"]], [["\"What is it, Lily?\"", "“무슨 이야기요, 릴리?”"]], [["\"And I do not know that I can say it properly; but I hope that you will not be hard upon me.", "“그리고 제대로 말할 수 있을지도 모르겠어요. 하지만 저를 모질게 대하지는 말아 주세요."], ["Adolphus, if you wish that all this between us should be over, I will consent.\"", "어돌퍼스, 우리 사이의 이 모든 것을 끝내고 싶으시다면, 저는 그러겠어요.”"]], [["\"Lily!\"", "“릴리!”"]], [["\"I mean what I say.", "“제 말은 진심이에요."], ["If you wish it, I will consent; and when I have said so, proposing it myself, you may be quite sure that I shall never blame you, if you take me at my word.\"", "당신이 원하신다면 저는 그러겠어요. 그리고 제 입으로 먼저 그러자고 한 것이니, 당신이 제 말대로 하신대도 제가 결코 당신을 탓하지 않으리라는 것만은 꼭 믿으셔도 돼요.”"]], [["\"Are you tired of me, Lily?\"", "“내가 싫증이 났소, 릴리?”"]], [["\"No.", "“아니에요."], ["I shall never be tired of you,--never weary with loving you.", "저는 절대 당신에게 싫증 나지 않을 거예요. 당신을 사랑하는 일에 결코 지치지 않을 거예요."], ["I did not wish to say so now; but I will answer your question boldly.", "지금 그런 말을 하려던 건 아니지만, 물으시니 당돌하게 대답할게요."], ["Tired of you!", "당신한테 싫증이라니요!"], ["I fancy that a girl can never grow tired of her lover.", "저는 아가씨가 자기 연인에게 싫증을 낼 수 있으리라고는 생각하지 않아요."], ["But I would sooner die in the struggle than be the cause of your ruin.", "하지만 저는 당신을 망치는 원인이 되느니 차라리 이 싸움 속에서 죽는 편을 택하겠어요."], ["It would be better,--in every way better.\"", "그편이 나아요. 어느 모로 보나 그편이 나아요.”"]], [["\"I have said nothing of being ruined.\"", "“나는 망한다는 말은 한 적이 없소.”"]], [["\"But listen to me.", "“하지만 제 말을 들어 주세요."], ["I should not die if you left me,--not be utterly broken-hearted.", "당신이 저를 떠나신다 해도 저는 죽지 않아요. 아주 가슴이 무너져 버리지도 않을 거예요."], ["Nothing on earth can I ever love as I have loved you.", "이 세상 무엇도 제가 당신을 사랑한 것처럼 사랑할 수는 없겠지요."], ["But I have a God and a Saviour that will be enough for me.", "하지만 제게는 하느님이 계시고 구주가 계시니 그것으로 충분해요."], ["I can turn to them with content, if it be well that you should leave me.", "당신이 저를 떠나시는 게 옳다면, 저는 만족하며 그분들께로 돌아설 수 있어요."], ["I have gone to them, and--\"", "저는 이미 그분들께 갔었고, 그리고….”"], ["But at this moment she could utter no more words.", "그러나 이 대목에서 그녀는 더 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["She had broken down in her effort, losing her voice through the strength of her emotion.", "감정이 북받쳐 목소리를 잃고 말문이 막힌 것이다."], ["As she did not choose that he should see her overcome, she turned from him and walked away across the grass.", "자기가 무너지는 모습을 그에게 보이고 싶지 않았으므로, 그녀는 그에게서 몸을 돌려 풀밭을 가로질러 걸어갔다."]], [["Of course he followed her; but he was not so quick after her, but that time had been given to her to recover herself.", "물론 그는 그녀를 뒤따랐다. 그러나 그리 빠르지 않아서, 그녀에게는 마음을 추스를 시간이 주어졌다."], ["\"It is true,\"", "“정말이에요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"I have the strength of which I tell you.", "“제게는 말씀드린 그런 힘이 있어요."], ["Though I have given myself to you as your wife, I can bear to be divorced from you now,--now.", "저는 제 몸을 아내로 당신께 드렸지만, 지금은, 지금은 당신에게서 갈라지는 것을 견딜 수 있어요."], ["And, my love, though it may sound heartless, I would sooner be so divorced from you, than cling to you as a log that must drag you down under the water, and drown you in trouble and care.", "그리고 내 사랑, 매정하게 들릴지 몰라도, 저는 당신을 물속으로 끌어내려 근심과 걱정에 빠뜨려 익사시키는 통나무처럼 당신에게 매달리느니 차라리 그렇게 갈라지겠어요."], ["I would;--indeed I would.", "그렇게 하겠어요. 정말 그러겠어요."], ["If you go, of course that kind of thing is over for me.", "당신이 가시면 물론 제게 그런 종류의 일은 다 끝나겠지요."], ["But the world has more than that,--much more; and I would make myself happy;--yes, my love, I would be happy.", "하지만 세상에는 그것 말고도 더 많은 것이, 훨씬 더 많은 것이 있어요. 그리고 저는 스스로 행복해지겠어요."], ["You need not fear that.\"", "네, 내 사랑, 저는 행복할 거예요. 그 점은 걱정하지 않으셔도 돼요.”"]], [["\"But, Lily, why is all this said to me here to-day?\"", "“하지만, 릴리, 어째서 이 모든 말을 오늘 여기서 나한테 하는 거요?”"]], [["\"Because it is my duty to say it.", "“그게 제 의무니까요."], ["I understand all your position now, though it is only now.", "저는 이제 당신의 처지를 다 알아요. 이제야 알게 되었지만요."], ["It never flashed on me till yesterday.", "어제까지는 한 번도 생각이 미치지 않았어요."], ["When you proposed to me, you thought that I,--that I had some fortune.\"", "당신이 제게 청혼하셨을 때, 당신은 제게, 제게 재산이 좀 있는 줄 아셨던 거예요.”"]], [["\"Never mind that now, Lily.\"", "“지금 그건 신경 쓰지 마시오, 릴리.”"]], [["\"But you did.", "“하지만 그러셨잖아요."], ["I see it all now.", "이제 다 알겠어요."], ["I ought perhaps to have told you that it was not so.", "어쩌면 제가 그렇지 않다고 당신께 말씀드렸어야 했어요."], ["There has been the mistake, and we are both sufferers.", "착오가 있었고, 우리 둘 다 그 때문에 괴로워하고 있어요."], ["But we need not make the suffering deeper than needs be.", "하지만 그 괴로움을 굳이 더 깊게 만들 필요는 없잖아요."], ["My love, you are free,--from this moment.", "내 사랑, 당신은 자유로워요. 이 순간부터요."], ["And even my heart shall not blame you for accepting your freedom.\"", "그리고 당신이 그 자유를 받아들이신다 해도 제 마음조차 당신을 탓하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["\"And are you afraid of poverty?\"", "“그럼 당신은 가난이 두려운 거요?”"], ["he asked her.", "그가 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["\"I am afraid of poverty for you.", "“저는 당신이 가난해지는 게 두려워요."], ["You and I have lived differently.", "당신과 저는 다르게 살아왔어요."], ["Luxuries, of which I know nothing, have been your daily comforts.", "제가 전혀 알지 못하는 호사가 당신에게는 나날의 편안함이었고요."], ["I tell you I can bear to part with you, but I cannot bear to become the source of your unhappiness.", "말씀드리지만 저는 당신과 헤어지는 건 견딜 수 있어도, 당신을 불행하게 만드는 근원이 되는 것은 견딜 수 없어요. 그래요, 저는 견디겠어요."], ["Yes; I will bear it; and none shall dare in my hearing to speak against you.", "그리고 제가 듣는 데서 아무도 감히 당신을 나쁘게 말하지 못하게 하겠어요. 저는 그 말을 하려고 당신을 여기로 데려왔어요."], ["I have brought you here to say the word; nay, more than that,--to advise you to say it.\"", "아니, 그보다 더한 것을요. 그 말을 하시라고 권하려고요.”"]], [["He stood silent for a moment, during which he held her by the hand.", "그는 잠시 말없이 서서 그녀의 손을 잡고 있었다."], ["She was looking into his face, but he was looking away into the clouds; striving to appear as though he was the master of the occasion.", "그녀는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보고 있었으나 그는 구름 쪽으로 눈길을 돌린 채, 자기가 이 자리의 주인인 것처럼 보이려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["But during those moments his mind was wracked with doubt.", "그러나 그 순간 그의 마음은 의심으로 갈가리 찢기고 있었다."], ["What if he should take her at her word?", "만일 그녀의 말대로 한다면 어떨까?"], ["Some few would say bitter things against him, but such bitter things had been said against many another man without harming him.", "몇몇은 그를 두고 모진 말을 하겠지만, 그런 모진 말은 다른 많은 남자에게도 아무 해를 끼치지 않고 지나가지 않았던가."], ["Would it not be well for both if he should take her at her word?", "그녀의 말대로 하는 편이 두 사람 모두에게 좋지 않을까?"], ["She would recover and love again, as other girls had done; and as for him, he would thus escape from the ruin at which he had been gazing for the last week past.", "그녀는 다른 아가씨들이 그랬듯 회복하여 다시 사랑하게 될 것이다. 그리고 그로 말하자면, 지난 한 주 내내 노려보고 있던 파멸에서 그렇게 벗어날 수 있을 것이다."], ["For it was ruin,--utter ruin.", "그것은 파멸이었으니까."], ["He did love her; so he declared to himself.", "그야말로 완전한 파멸. 그는 그녀를 사랑했다."], ["But was he a man who ought to throw the world away for love?", "적어도 그는 스스로에게 그렇게 단언했다. 그러나 그가 사랑을 위해 세상을 내던져야 할 남자인가?"], ["Such men there were; but was he one of them?", "그런 남자들이 있기는 했다."], ["Could he be happy in that small house, somewhere near the New Road, with five children and horrid misgivings as to the baker's bill?", "그러나 그가 그런 사람 가운데 하나인가? 뉴 로드 근처 어딘가의 그 작은 집에서, 다섯 아이와 빵집 외상값에 대한 끔찍한 걱정을 안고 그가 행복할 수 있을까?"], ["Of all men living, was not he the last that should have allowed himself to fall into such a trap?", "세상 모든 남자 가운데, 그런 덫에 스스로 빠지도록 두어서는 안 될 마지막 사람이 바로 그가 아니었던가?"], ["All this passed through his mind as he turned his face up to the clouds with a look that was intended to be grand and noble.", "웅장하고 고결해 보이려는 표정으로 구름 쪽으로 얼굴을 들고 있는 동안 이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다."]], [["\"Speak to me, Adolphus, and say that it shall be so.\"", "“말씀해 주세요, 어돌퍼스. 그렇게 하자고요.”"]], [["Then his heart misgave him, and he lacked the courage to extricate himself from his trouble; or, as he afterwards said to himself, he had not the heart to do it.", "그러자 그는 겁이 났고, 자기 곤경에서 빠져나올 용기가 없었다. 혹은 나중에 스스로에게 말한 대로, 그렇게 할 만한 매정함이 없었다."], ["\"If I understand you, rightly, Lily, all this comes from no want of love on your own part?\"", "“내가 제대로 알아들었다면, 릴리, 이 모든 말은 당신 쪽에 사랑이 모자라서 나온 것은 아니지요?”"]], [["\"Want of love on my part?", "“제 쪽에 사랑이 모자라다니요?"], ["But you should not ask me that.\"", "그런 건 제게 물으시면 안 되죠.”"]], [["\"Until you tell me that there is such a want, I will agree to no parting.\"", "“그런 것이 모자란다고 당신이 내게 말하기 전에는, 나는 어떤 이별에도 동의하지 않겠소.”"], ["Then he took her hand and put it within his arm.", "그러고서 그는 그녀의 손을 잡아 자기 팔에 끼워 넣었다. “아니오, 릴리."], ["\"No, Lily; whatever may be our cares and troubles, we are bound together,--indissolubly.\"", "우리에게 어떤 근심과 어려움이 있든, 우리는 함께 묶여 있소. 풀 수 없게 말이오.”"]], [["\"Are we?\"", "“그런가요?”"], ["said she; and as she spoke, her voice trembled, and her hand shook.", "그녀가 말했다. 그렇게 말하는 동안 그녀의 목소리가 떨렸고 손도 떨렸다."]], [["\"Much too firmly for any such divorce as that.", "“그런 이별 따위로는 어림도 없이 단단히 묶여 있소. 아니오, 릴리."], ["No, Lily, I claim the right to tell you all my troubles; but I shall not let you go.\"", "나는 내 온갖 근심을 당신에게 말할 권리가 있다고 주장하오. 그러나 당신을 놓아주지는 않겠소.”"]], [["\"But, Adolphus--\"", "“하지만, 어돌퍼스….”"], ["and the hand on his arm was beginning to cling to it again.", "그리고 그의 팔에 얹은 손이 다시 그 팔을 붙잡기 시작했다."]], [["\"Adolphus,\"", "“어돌퍼스는,”"], ["said he, \"has got nothing more to say on that subject.", "그가 말했다. “그 문제에 관해서는 더 할 말이 없소."], ["He exercises the right which he believes to be his own, and chooses to retain the prize which he has won.\"", "그는 자기 것이라 믿는 권리를 행사하며, 자기가 얻어 낸 상을 지키기로 한다오.”"]], [["She was now clinging to him in very truth.", "이제 그녀는 정말로 그에게 매달리고 있었다."], ["\"Oh, my love!\"", "“아, 내 사랑!”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"I do not know how to say it again.", "“어떻게 다시 말해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["It is of you that I am thinking;--of you, of you!\"", "제가 생각하는 건 당신이에요. 당신, 오직 당신이에요!”"]], [["\"I know you are; but you have misunderstood me a little; that's all.\"", "“나도 알고 있소. 다만 당신이 나를 조금 잘못 알아들었을 뿐이오. 그뿐이오.”"]], [["\"Have I?", "“제가요?"], ["Then listen to me again, once more, my heart's own darling, my love, my husband, my lord!", "그럼 다시 한번만 제 말을 들어 주세요. 내 마음의 임자, 내 사랑, 내 남편, 내 주인이여!"], ["If I cannot be to you at once like Ruth, and never cease from coming after you, my thoughts to you shall be like those of Ruth:--if aught but death part thee and me, may God do so to me and more also.\"", "제가 곧바로 룻처럼 되어 당신을 따라나서기를 그치지 못하는 사람이 될 수는 없다 해도, 당신을 향한 제 마음만은 룻의 마음과 같을 거예요. 죽음 외에 무엇이 당신과 저를 갈라놓는다면, 하느님이 제게 벌을 내리시고 또 내리시기를.”"], ["Then she fell upon his breast and wept.", "그러고서 그녀는 그의 가슴에 얼굴을 묻고 울었다."]], [["He still hardly understood the depth of her character.", "그는 여전히 그녀 성품의 깊이를 거의 이해하지 못했다."], ["He was not himself deep enough to comprehend it all.", "그것을 다 헤아릴 만큼 그 자신이 깊지 못했다."], ["But yet he was awed by her great love, and exalted to a certain solemnity of feeling which for the time made him rejoice in his late decision.", "그러나 그래도 그는 그녀의 크나큰 사랑에 경외를 느꼈고, 어떤 엄숙한 감정으로 고양되어 그때만은 얼마 전에 내린 결심을 기뻐하게 되었다."], ["For a few hours he was minded to throw the world behind him, and wear this woman, as such a woman should be worn,--as a comforter to him in all things, and a strong shield against great troubles.", "몇 시간 동안 그는 세상을 뒤로 내던지고, 이런 여자를 마땅히 지녀야 할 방식대로 이 여자를 지니고 살 마음이 되어 있었다. 즉 모든 일에서 자기를 위로해 주는 사람으로, 그리고 큰 어려움을 막아 주는 든든한 방패로 지니고 살 마음 말이다."], ["\"Lily,\"", "“릴리.”"], ["he said, \"my own Lily!\"", "그가 말했다. “나의 릴리!”"]], [["\"Yes, your own, to take when you please, and leave untaken while you please; and as much your own in one way as in the other.\"", "“네, 당신 것이에요. 원하실 때 가지시고, 원하실 때는 가지지 않고 두셔도 되는, 이래도 저래도 똑같이 당신 것이에요.”"], ["Then she looked up again, and essayed to laugh as she did so.", "그러고서 그녀는 다시 올려다보며 웃어 보이려 애썼다."], ["\"You will think I am frantic, but I am so happy.", "“제가 정신이 나갔다고 생각하시겠지만, 저는 너무 행복해요."], ["I don't care about your going now; indeed I don't.", "이제는 당신이 떠나신대도 아무렇지 않아요. 정말 아무렇지 않아요."], ["There; you may go now, this minute, if you like it.\"", "자, 지금 당장이라도 가시고 싶으면 가셔도 돼요.”"], ["And she withdrew her hand from him.", "그러고서 그녀는 그에게서 손을 뺐다."], ["\"I feel so differently from what I have done for the last few days.", "“지난 며칠 동안과는 마음이 아주 달라요."], ["I am so glad you have spoken to me as you did.", "당신이 제게 그렇게 말씀해 주셔서 정말 기뻐요."], ["Of course I ought to bear all those things with you.", "물론 저는 그 모든 것을 당신과 함께 견뎌야지요."], ["But I cannot be unhappy about it now.", "하지만 이제는 그 때문에 불행할 수가 없어요."], ["I wonder if I went to work and made a lot of things, whether that would help?\"", "제가 이것저것 잔뜩 만들어 두면 도움이 될까요?”"]], [["\"A set of shirts for me, for instance?\"", "“이를테면 내 셔츠 한 벌 같은 것 말이오?”"]], [["\"I could do that, at any rate.\"", "“그 정도야 어쨌든 제가 할 수 있죠.”"]], [["\"It may come to that yet, some of these days.\"", "“머지않아 정말 그렇게 될지도 모르오.”"]], [["\"I pray God that it may.\"", "“저는 그렇게 되기를 하느님께 기도해요.”"], ["Then again she was serious, and the tears came once more into her eyes.", "그러고서 그녀는 다시 진지해졌고 두 눈에 또다시 눈물이 고였다."], ["\"I pray God that it may.", "“저는 그렇게 되기를 하느님께 기도해요."], ["To be of use to you,--to work for you,--to do something for you that may have in it some sober, earnest purport of usefulness;--that is what I want above all things.", "당신께 쓸모가 되는 것, 당신을 위해 일하는 것, 무언가 차분하고 진지한 쓸모의 뜻이 담긴 일을 당신을 위해 하는 것, 그게 제가 무엇보다 바라는 거예요."], ["I want to be with you at once that I may be of service to you.", "당신께 도움이 될 수 있도록 어서 당신 곁에 있고 싶어요."], ["Would that you and I were alone together, that I might do everything for you.", "당신과 제가 단둘이 있어서 제가 당신을 위해 모든 걸 할 수 있다면 좋겠어요."], ["I sometimes think that a very poor man's wife is the happiest, because she does do everything.\"", "저는 가끔 아주 가난한 남자의 아내가 제일 행복하지 않을까 생각해요. 정말로 모든 걸 다 하니까요.”"]], [["\"You shall do everything very soon,\"", "“곧 당신이 다 하게 될 거요.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he; and then they sauntered along pleasantly through the morning hours, and when they again appeared at Mrs. Dale's table, Mrs.", "그러고서 두 사람은 오전 내내 즐겁게 거닐었고, 다시 데일 부인의 식탁에 나타났을 때 데일 부인과 벨은 릴리의 밝은 모습에 놀랐다."], ["Dale and Bell were astonished at Lily's brightness. All her old ways had seemed to return to her, and she made her little saucy speeches to Mr.", "그녀의 옛 모습이 죄다 되돌아온 듯했고, 크로즈비 씨가 처음 그녀의 사랑스러움에 홀리던 무렵에 하던 것처럼 그녀는 그에게 앙큼한 말들을 던졌다."], ["Crosbie as she had used to do when he was first becoming fascinated by her sweetness. \"You know that you'll be such a swell when you get to that countess's house that you'll forget all about Allington.\"", "“그 백작부인 댁에 가시면 어찌나 멋쟁이가 되실지, 앨링턴은 죄다 잊어버리실걸요.”"]], [["\"Of course I shall,\"", "“물론 그럴 거요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"And the paper you write upon will be all over coronets,--that is, if ever you do write.", "“그리고 편지지에는 온통 관 무늬가 박혀 있겠지요. 그러니까 편지를 쓰기라도 하신다면요."], ["Perhaps you will to Bernard some day, just to show that you are staying at a castle.\"", "어쩌면 언젠가 버나드에게는 쓰실지도 모르죠. 자기가 성에 묵고 있다는 걸 보여 주려고요.”"]], [["\"You certainly don't deserve that he should write to you,\"", "“네가 그이한테서 편지를 받을 자격이 있다고는 못 하겠구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I don't expect it for a moment,--not till he gets back to London and finds that he has nothing else to do at his office.", "“저도 조금도 기대 안 해요. 런던에 돌아가서 사무실에 달리 할 일이 없다는 걸 알게 되기 전에는요."], ["But I should so like to see how you and Lady Julia get on together.", "하지만 당신하고 레이디 줄리아가 어떻게 지내실지는 정말 보고 싶어요."], ["It was quite clear that she regarded you as an ogre; didn't she, Bell?\"", "그분이 당신을 도깨비처럼 여기는 게 아주 분명했죠. 안 그래, 언니?”"]], [["\"So many people are ogres to Lady Julia,\"", "“레이디 줄리아한테는 도깨비인 사람이 워낙 많으니까.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I believe Lady Julia to be a very good woman,\" said Mrs.", "“나는 레이디 줄리아가 아주 좋은 분이라고 믿는다.”"], ["Dale, \"and I won't have her abused.\"", "데일 부인이 말했다. “그러니 그분 험담은 그만두렴.”"]], [["\"Particularly before poor Bernard, who is her pet nephew,\" said Lily.", "“특히 그분의 귀염둥이 조카인 가엾은 버나드 앞에서는 더 그렇죠.”"], ["\"I dare say Adolphus will become a pet too when she has been a week with him at Courcy Castle.", "릴리가 말했다. “코시 성에서 일주일만 같이 지내면 어돌퍼스도 귀염둥이가 될걸요."], ["Do try and cut Bernard out.\"", "어디 버나드를 밀어내 보세요.”"]], [["From all which Mrs. Dale learned that some care which had sat heavy on Lily's heart was now lightened, if not altogether removed.", "이 모든 것으로부터 데일 부인은 릴리의 마음을 무겁게 짓누르던 어떤 근심이, 완전히 사라지지는 않았더라도 한결 가벼워졌음을 알았다."], ["She had asked no questions of her daughter, but she had perceived during the past few days that Lily was in trouble, and she knew that such trouble had arisen from her engagement.", "그녀는 딸에게 아무것도 묻지 않았지만, 지난 며칠 동안 릴리가 괴로워하고 있음을 알아차렸고, 그 괴로움이 약혼에서 비롯되었다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["She had asked no questions, but of course she had been told what was Mr. Crosbie's income, and had been made to understand that it was not to be considered as amply sufficient for all the wants of matrimony.", "그녀는 아무것도 묻지 않았으나, 물론 크로즈비 씨의 수입이 얼마인지는 들어서 알고 있었고, 그것이 혼인 생활의 온갖 필요를 넉넉히 채울 만한 액수로 여겨지지는 않는다는 것도 알게 되어 있었다."], ["There was little difficulty in guessing what was the source of Lily's care, and as little in now perceiving that something had been said between them by which that care had been relieved.", "릴리의 근심이 어디서 왔는지 짐작하기란 어렵지 않았고, 이제 두 사람 사이에 무슨 이야기가 오가서 그 근심이 덜어졌음을 알아채기도 어렵지 않았다."]], [["After that they all rode, and the afternoon went by pleasantly.", "그러고 나서 다들 말을 타러 나갔고, 오후는 즐겁게 흘러갔다."], ["It was the last day indeed, but Lily had determined that she would not be sad.", "정말 마지막 날이었지만 릴리는 슬퍼하지 않기로 마음먹고 있었다."], ["She had told him that he might go now, and that she would not be discontented at his going.", "이제 가셔도 된다고, 가셔도 서운해하지 않겠다고 그녀는 그에게 말해 두었다."], ["She knew that the morrow would be very blank to her; but she struggled to live up to the spirit of her promise, and she succeeded.", "내일이 자기에게 몹시 텅 빈 날이 되리라는 것을 알았지만, 그녀는 자기 약속의 정신에 걸맞게 살아 보려고 애썼고 또 해냈다."], ["They all dined at the Great House, even Mrs. Dale doing so upon this occasion.", "그들은 모두 큰 집에서 저녁을 먹었는데, 이번만은 데일 부인까지 그리했다."], ["When they had come in from the garden in the evening, Crosbie talked more to Mrs. Dale than he did even to Lily, while Lily sat a little distant, listening with all her ears, sometimes saying a low-toned word, and happy beyond expression in the feeling that her mother and her lover should understand each other.", "저녁에 정원에서 들어왔을 때 크로즈비는 릴리에게보다 데일 부인에게 더 많은 말을 했고, 릴리는 조금 떨어져 앉아 온 귀를 기울이며 이따금 낮은 목소리로 한마디씩 거들었는데, 어머니와 연인이 서로를 알아 가는 것이 말할 수 없이 행복했다."], ["And it must be understood that Crosbie at this time was fully determined to conquer the difficulties of which he had thought so much, and to fix the earliest day which might be possible for his marriage.", "그리고 밝혀 두어야 할 것은, 이때 크로즈비가 그토록 골머리를 앓던 어려움들을 이겨 내고 결혼할 수 있는 가장 이른 날을 정하기로 온전히 마음먹고 있었다는 점이다."], ["The solemnity of that meeting in the field still hung about him, and gave to his present feelings a manliness and a truth of purpose which were too generally wanting to them.", "들판에서 있었던 그 만남의 엄숙함이 아직 그에게 남아 있어서, 그의 지금 감정에 대체로 그에게는 모자랐던 사내다움과 진실한 목적을 부여하고 있었다."], ["If only those feelings would last! But now he talked to Mrs.", "그 감정이 오래가기만 한다면!"], ["Dale about her daughter, and about their future prospects, in a tone which he could not have used had not his mind for the time been true to her.", "그러나 지금 그는 데일 부인에게 그 딸에 관해, 그리고 두 사람의 앞날에 관해, 한동안이나마 그의 마음이 그녀에게 참되지 않았더라면 쓸 수 없었을 어조로 이야기했다. 그는 릴리의 어머니에게 이토록 마음을 터놓고 말한 적이 없었고, 데일 부인 역시 어느 때보다 그에게 어머니의 사랑 같은 것을 느꼈다."], ["He had never spoken so freely to Lily's mother, and at no time had Mrs. Dale felt for him so much of a mother's love.", "그는 얼마간 늦어질 수밖에 없는 사정을 사과하며, 자기 젊은 아내가 제 집의 안락함도 없이 지내는 것을 차마 볼 수 없고, 자기는 지금도 늘 그래 왔듯 빚지는 것이 두렵다고 말했다."], ["He apologized for the necessity of some delay, arguing that he could not endure to see his young wife without the comfort of a home of her own, and that he was now, as he always had been, afraid of incurring debt. Mrs.", "데일 부인은 기다리는 약혼을 싫어했다."], ["Dale disliked waiting engagements,--as do all mothers,--but she could not answer unkindly to such pleading as this.", "모든 어머니가 그렇듯이. 그러나 이런 간청에 매정하게 대답할 수는 없었다."]], [["\"Lily is so very young,\"", "“릴리가 아직 아주 어리니,”"], ["she said, \"that she may well wait for a year or so.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “일 년쯤은 기다려도 괜찮을 거예요.”"]], [["\"For seven years,\"", "“칠 년이라도요.”"], ["said Lily, jumping up and whispering into her mother's ear.", "릴리가 벌떡 일어나 어머니의 귀에 대고 속삭였다."], ["\"I shall hardly be six-and-twenty then, which is not at all too old.\"", "“그때도 스물여섯이 채 안 될 텐데, 조금도 많은 나이가 아니잖아요.”"]], [["And so the evening passed away very pleasantly.", "그리하여 저녁은 아주 즐겁게 지나갔다."]], [["\"God bless you, Adolphus!\" Mrs.", "“하느님의 축복이 함께하기를, 어돌퍼스!”"], ["Dale said to him, as she parted with him at her own door.", "데일 부인이 자기 집 문간에서 그와 헤어지며 말했다."], ["It was the first time that she had called him by his Christian name.", "그를 세례명으로 부른 것은 그때가 처음이었다."], ["\"I hope you understand how much we are trusting to you.\"", "“우리가 자네를 얼마나 믿고 맡기는지 알아주면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["\"I do,--I do,\"", "“압니다."], ["said he, as he pressed her hand.", "잘 압니다.” 그가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다."], ["Then as he walked back alone, he swore to himself, binding himself to the oath with all his heart, that he would be true to those women,--both to the daughter and to the mother; for the solemnity of the morning was still upon him.", "그러고서 혼자 걸어 돌아가면서 그는 그 두 여자에게, 딸에게도 어머니에게도 참되겠노라고 온 마음을 다해 스스로에게 맹세하며 다짐했다. 그날 아침의 엄숙함이 아직 그에게 남아 있었기 때문이다."]], [["He was to start the next morning before eight, Bernard having undertaken to drive him over to the railway at Guestwick.", "그는 이튿날 아침 여덟 시 전에 떠나기로 되어 있었고, 버나드가 게스트윅 철도역까지 그를 태워다 주기로 되어 있었다."], ["The breakfast was on the table shortly after seven; and just as the two men had come down, Lily entered the room, with her hat and shawl.", "아침상은 일곱 시가 조금 지나 차려졌다. 그리고 두 남자가 막 내려왔을 때 릴리가 모자를 쓰고 숄을 두른 채 방으로 들어왔다."], ["\"I said I would be in to pour out your tea,\"", "“차를 따라 드리러 오겠다고 했잖아요.”"], ["said she; and then she sat herself down over against the teapot.", "그녀가 말하고는 찻주전자 맞은편에 자리를 잡고 앉았다."]], [["It was a silent meal, for people do not know what to say in those last minutes.", "말수가 적은 식사였다. 그런 마지막 순간에는 사람들이 무슨 말을 해야 할지 모르는 법이니까."], ["And Bernard, too, was there; proving how true is the adage which says, that two are company, but that three are not.", "게다가 버나드도 거기 있었으니, 둘이면 동무가 되어도 셋이면 그렇지 못하다는 옛말이 얼마나 옳은지 증명해 보이는 셈이었다."], ["I think that Lily was wrong to come up on that last morning; but she would not hear of letting him start without seeing him, when her lover had begged her not to put herself to so much trouble.", "내 생각에 릴리가 그 마지막 아침에 올라온 것은 잘못이었다. 그러나 연인이 그렇게까지 수고하지 말라고 청했는데도 그녀는 그가 떠나는 것을 보지 않고 보낸다는 말을 들으려 하지 않았다."], ["Trouble!", "수고라니!"], ["Would she not have sat up all night to see even the last of the top of his hat?", "그의 모자 꼭대기의 마지막 모습이라도 보기 위해서라면 그녀가 밤을 꼬박 새우지 않았겠는가?"]], [["Then Bernard, muttering something about the horse, went away.", "그러다 버나드가 말에 관해 무어라 중얼거리며 자리를 떴다."], ["\"I have only one minute to speak to you,\"", "“당신한테 말할 시간이 딱 일 분밖에 없네요.”"], ["said she, jumping up, \"and I have been thinking all night of what I had to say.", "그녀가 벌떡 일어나며 말했다. “그리고 무슨 말을 해야 할지 밤새 생각했고요."], ["It is so easy to think, and so hard to speak.\"", "생각하기는 이렇게 쉬운데 말하기는 이렇게 어렵네요.”"]], [["\"My darling, I understand it all.\"", "“내 사랑, 다 알고 있소.”"]], [["\"But you must understand this, that I will never distrust you.", "“하지만 이건 알아주셔야 해요. 저는 절대 당신을 의심하지 않을 거예요."], ["I will never ask you to give me up again, or say that I could be happy without you.", "다시는 저를 놓아 달라고 하지도, 당신 없이도 행복할 수 있다고 말하지도 않을 거예요."], ["I could not live without you; that is, without the knowledge that you are mine.", "저는 당신 없이는 살 수 없어요. 그러니까 당신이 제 사람이라는 걸 알지 못하고는요."], ["But I will never be impatient, never.", "하지만 저는 절대로 조바심 내지 않을 거예요. 절대로요."], ["Pray, pray believe me!", "제발, 제발 믿어 주세요!"], ["Nothing shall make me distrust you.\"", "무슨 일이 있어도 저는 당신을 의심하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Dearest Lily, I will endeavour to give you no cause.\"", "“가장 사랑하는 릴리, 그럴 일이 없도록 애쓰겠소.”"]], [["\"I know you will not; but I specially wanted to tell you that.", "“안 그러실 줄 알아요. 하지만 그 말을 꼭 하고 싶었어요."], ["And you will write,--very soon?\"", "그리고 편지 쓰시겠죠? 아주 곧요?”"]], [["\"Directly I get there.\"", "“도착하는 즉시 쓰겠소.”"]], [["\"And as often as you can.", "“그리고 되도록 자주요."], ["But I won't bother you; only your letters will make me so happy.", "하지만 성가시게 굴진 않을게요. 다만 당신 편지가 저를 정말 행복하게 해 줄 거예요."], ["I shall be so proud when they come to me.", "편지가 오면 얼마나 뿌듯할까요."], ["I shall be afraid of writing too much to you, for fear I should tire you.\"", "저는 당신을 지치게 할까 봐 너무 많이 쓰기가 겁날 거예요.”"]], [["\"You will never do that.\"", "“그럴 일은 없을 거요.”"]], [["\"Shall I not?", "“안 그럴까요?"], ["But you must write first, you know.", "하지만 아시다시피 먼저 쓰셔야 해요."], ["If you could only understand how I shall live upon your letters!", "제가 당신 편지로 어떻게 살아갈지 조금이라도 아신다면!"], ["And now good-by.", "자, 이제 안녕히 가세요."], ["There are the wheels.", "마차 바퀴 소리가 나요."], ["God bless you, my own, my own!\"", "하느님의 축복이 함께하기를, 내 사람, 내 사람!”"], ["And she gave herself up into his arms, as she had given herself up into his heart.", "그러고서 그녀는 자기 마음을 그의 마음에 내맡겼듯이 자기 몸을 그의 두 팔에 내맡겼다."]], [["She stood at the door as the two men got into the gig, and, as it passed down through the gate, she hurried out upon the terrace, from whence she could see it for a few yards down the lane.", "두 남자가 이륜마차에 오르는 동안 그녀는 문간에 서 있었고, 마차가 문을 지나 내려가자 서둘러 테라스로 나갔다. 거기서라면 마차가 오솔길을 얼마쯤 내려가는 모습을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Then she ran from the terrace to the gate, and, hurrying through the gate, made her way into the churchyard, from the farther corner of which she could see the heads of the two men till they had made the turn into the main road beyond the parsonage.", "그러고는 테라스에서 문으로 달려가, 문을 서둘러 빠져나가 교회 묘지로 들어갔다. 그 맨 끝 모퉁이에서라면 두 남자의 머리가 목사관 너머 큰길로 접어들 때까지 보였다."], ["There she remained till the very sound of the wheels no longer reached her ears, stretching her eyes in the direction they had taken.", "그녀는 마차 바퀴 소리마저 더는 귀에 닿지 않을 때까지 거기 서서, 그들이 간 방향으로 눈길을 뻗고 있었다."], ["Then she turned round slowly and made her way out at the churchyard gate, which opened on to the road close to the front door of the Small House.", "그러고서 천천히 몸을 돌려 교회 묘지 문으로 빠져나갔는데, 그 문은 작은 집 정문 바로 옆 길로 나 있었다."]], [["\"I should like to punch his head,\"", "“저 사람 머리통을 한 대 쥐어박아 주고 싶습니다요.”"], ["said Hopkins, the gardener, to himself, as he saw the gig driven away and saw Lily trip after it, that she might see the last of him whom it carried.", "정원사 홉킨스가 이륜마차가 몰려 가는 것을, 그리고 릴리가 그 안에 실려 가는 사람의 마지막 모습이라도 보려고 종종걸음으로 뒤따르는 것을 보며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["\"And I wouldn't think nothing of doing it; no more I wouldn't,\"", "“그러고도 눈 하나 깜짝 안 하지요. 암, 안 하고말고요.”"], ["Hopkins added in his soliloquy.", "홉킨스는 혼잣말로 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["It was generally thought about the place that Miss Lily was Hopkins's favourite; though he showed it chiefly by snubbing her more frequently than he snubbed her sister.", "이 근방에서는 릴리 아가씨가 홉킨스의 귀염둥이라는 말이 대체로 돌았다. 다만 그는 주로 언니보다 그녀에게 더 자주 퉁을 놓는 것으로 그 마음을 드러내곤 했다."]], [["Lily had evidently intended to return home through the front door; but she changed her purpose before she reached the house, and made her way slowly back through the churchyard, and by the gate of the Great House, and by the garden at the back of it, till she crossed the little bridge.", "릴리는 분명히 정문으로 집에 돌아갈 작정이었으나, 집에 이르기 전에 마음을 바꾸어 교회 묘지를 지나, 큰 집 문을 지나, 그 뒤편 정원을 지나 천천히 되돌아가서 마침내 작은 다리를 건넜다."], ["But on the bridge she rested awhile, leaning against the railing as she had often leant with him, and thinking of all that had passed since that July day on which she had first met him.", "그러나 다리 위에서 그녀는 그와 함께 자주 그랬듯 난간에 기대어 잠시 쉬며, 그를 처음 만난 그 칠월의 어느 날 이후로 있었던 모든 일을 생각했다."], ["On no spot had he so often told her of his love as on this, and nowhere had she so eagerly sworn to him that she would be his own dutiful loving wife.", "그가 그녀에게 사랑을 고백한 곳 가운데 이곳만큼 자주 고백한 곳은 없었고, 그녀가 그의 충실하고 사랑하는 아내가 되겠노라고 그토록 간절히 맹세한 곳도 여기 말고는 없었다."]], [["\"And by God's help so I will,\"", "“그리고 하느님이 도우시는 한 그렇게 하겠어.”"], ["she said to herself, as she walked firmly up to the house.", "그녀는 집으로 씩씩하게 걸어 올라가며 스스로에게 말했다."], ["\"He has gone, mamma,\"", "“그이는 갔어요, 엄마.”"], ["she said, as she entered the breakfast-room.", "아침 식사 방으로 들어서며 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"And now we'll go back to our work-a-day ways; it has been all Sunday for me for the last six weeks.\"", "“이제 우리도 다시 일하는 나날로 돌아가요. 지난 여섯 주 동안 저한테는 온통 일요일이었으니까요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XVI. MR. CROSBIE MEETS AN OLD CLERGYMAN ON HIS WAY TO COURCY CASTLE.", "# 제16장. 크로즈비 씨가 코시 성으로 가는 길에 늙은 목사를 만나다"]], [["For the first mile or two of their journey Crosbie and Bernard Dale sat, for the most part, silent in their gig.", "여정의 첫 한두 마일 동안 크로즈비와 버나드 데일은 이륜마차에 앉아 거의 내내 말이 없었다."], ["Lily, as she ran down to the churchyard corner and stood there looking after them with her loving eyes, had not been seen by them.", "릴리가 교회 묘지 모퉁이까지 달려 내려가 거기 서서 사랑에 겨운 눈으로 두 사람의 뒷모습을 지켜보고 있었다는 것을 그들은 알지 못했다."], ["But the spirit of her devotion was still strong upon them both, and they felt that it would not be well to strike at once into any ordinary topic of conversation.", "그러나 릴리의 그 지극한 마음이 뿜어낸 기운은 아직도 두 사람 위에 강하게 남아 있었고, 그래서 그들은 대뜸 여느 이야깃거리로 화제를 돌리는 것이 좋지 않으리라고 느꼈다."], ["And, moreover, we may presume that Crosbie did feel much at thus parting from such a girl as Lily Dale, with whom he had lived in close intercourse for the last six weeks, and whom he loved with all his heart,--with all the heart that he had for such purposes.", "게다가 우리는 이렇게 짐작해도 좋을 것이다. 크로즈비는 릴리 데일 같은 아가씨와, 지난 여섯 주 동안 살뜰히 어울려 지냈고 온 마음을 다해 사랑한 그 아가씨와 이렇게 헤어지는 것이 실로 견디기 어려웠다고 — 그런 데 쓸 마음이 그에게 있는 만큼은 그 전부를 다해서 사랑한 아가씨였다."], ["In those doubts as to his marriage which had troubled him he had never expressed to himself any disapproval of Lily.", "그를 괴롭히던 결혼에 대한 그 의심들 속에서도 그는 릴리를 못마땅해하는 뜻을 스스로에게 한 번도 내비친 적이 없었다."], ["He had not taught himself to think that she was other than he would have her be, that he might thus give himself an excuse for parting from her.", "릴리와 갈라설 구실을 제게 마련해 주려고, 그 아가씨가 자기가 바라는 바와는 다른 사람이라고 여기도록 제 마음을 길들이지도 않았다."], ["Not as yet, at any rate, had he had recourse to that practice, so common with men who wish to free themselves from the bonds with which they have permitted themselves to be bound.", "적어도 아직까지는, 제 발로 묶이기를 허락해 놓고서 그 굴레에서 벗어나고 싶어 하는 사내들이 그토록 흔히 쓰는 그 수법에 기대지 않은 것이다."], ["Lily had been too sweet to his eyes, to his touch, to all his senses for that.", "릴리는 그의 눈에도, 그의 손길에도, 그의 모든 감각에도 그러기에는 너무나 사랑스러웠다."], ["He had enjoyed too keenly the pleasure of being with her, and of hearing her tell him that she loved him, to allow of his being personally tired of her.", "그는 릴리와 함께 있는 즐거움을, 그리고 사랑한다고 말해 주는 릴리의 목소리를 듣는 즐거움을 너무나 절실히 누렸으므로, 그 사람 자체에 싫증이 날 수는 없었다."], ["He had not been so spoilt by his club life but that he had taken exquisite pleasure in all her nice country ways, and soft, kind-hearted, womanly humour.", "그는 클럽 생활에 찌들 대로 찌들지는 않았던 터라, 릴리의 그 점잖은 시골 사람다운 몸가짐과 부드럽고 다정하고 여자다운 익살에서 더없는 기쁨을 맛보았다."], ["He was by no means tired of Lily.", "그는 결코 릴리에게 싫증이 나지 않았다."], ["Better than any of his London pleasures was this pleasure of making love in the green fields to Lily Dale.", "런던의 그 어떤 즐거움보다도, 푸른 들판에서 릴리 데일에게 사랑을 속삭이는 이 즐거움이 나았다."], ["It was the consequences of it that affrighted him.", "그를 겁나게 한 것은 그 뒤에 따라올 결과였다."], ["Babies with their belongings would come; and dull evenings, over a dull fire, or else the pining grief of a disappointed woman.", "아기들이 생기고 아기에게 딸린 온갖 것들이 따라올 터였다. 시들한 난롯불을 앞에 둔 시들한 저녁나절도, 아니면 실망한 여자의 애태우는 슬픔도 따라올 터였다."], ["He would be driven to be careful as to his clothes, because the ordering of a new coat would entail a serious expenditure.", "옷차림에도 조심하지 않을 수 없게 될 것이었으니, 새 외투 한 벌을 맞추는 일이 만만찮은 지출을 뜻하게 될 터이기 때문이었다."], ["He could go no more among countesses and their daughters, because it would be out of the question that his wife should visit at their houses.", "백작부인들과 그 딸들 틈에 드나드는 일도 더는 못 할 것이었으니, 제 아내가 그런 집에 인사를 다닌다는 것은 애당초 될 법하지도 않은 일일 터이기 때문이었다."], ["All the victories that he had ever won must be given up.", "그가 지금까지 거둔 승리란 승리는 죄다 내놓아야 했다."], ["He was thinking of this even while the gig was going round the corner near the parsonage house, and while Lily's eyes were still blessed with some view of his departing back; but he was thinking, also, that moment, that there might be other victory in store for him; that it might be possible for him to learn to like that fireside, even though babies should be there, and a woman opposite to him intent on baby cares.", "이륜마차가 목사관 곁의 모퉁이를 돌아 나가는 동안, 그러니까 릴리의 눈이 아직도 멀어져 가는 그의 등을 조금이나마 볼 수 있는 복을 누리고 있는 동안에도 그는 이런 것들을 생각하고 있었다. 그러나 그는 바로 그 순간 이런 생각도 하고 있었으니, 자기를 기다리는 또 다른 승리가 있을지도 모른다는 것, 아기들이 거기 있고 아기 시중에 여념이 없는 여자가 맞은편에 앉아 있다 한들 그 난롯가를 좋아하는 법을 배울 수도 있으리라는 것이었다."], ["He was struggling as best he knew how; for the solemnity which Lily had imparted to him had not yet vanished from his spirit.", "그는 제가 아는 한 최선을 다해 싸우고 있었다. 릴리가 그에게 나누어 준 그 엄숙한 기운이 아직 그의 마음에서 사라지지 않은 까닭이었다."]], [["\"I hope that, upon the whole, you feel contented with your visit?\"", "“그래도 대체로는, 이번 방문이 마음에 들었겠지?”"], ["said Bernard to him, at last.", "마침내 버나드가 그에게 말했다."]], [["\"Contented?", "“마음에 들었느냐고?"], ["Of course I do.\"", "물론이지.”"]], [["\"That is easily said; and civility to me, perhaps, demands as much.", "“그야 말이야 쉽지. 나한테 인사치레를 하자면 그쯤은 해야 할 테고."], ["But I know that you have, to some extent, been disappointed.\"", "하지만 자네가 어느 정도는 실망했다는 걸 나는 알고 있네.”"]], [["\"Well; yes.", "“음, 그래."], ["I have been disappointed as regards money.", "돈 문제에서는 실망했지."], ["It is of no use denying it.\"", "아니라고 우겨 봐야 소용없는 일이고.”"]], [["\"I should not mention it now, only that I want to know that you exonerate me.\"", "“지금 그 얘기를 꺼낼 생각은 없었네만, 자네가 나한테는 허물을 돌리지 않는다는 걸 알고 싶어서 그러네.”"]], [["\"I have never blamed you;--neither you, nor anybody else; unless, indeed, it has been myself.\"", "“나는 자네를 탓한 적이 없어. 자네도, 다른 누구도. 굳이 탓한 사람이 있다면 나 자신뿐이지.”"]], [["\"You mean that you regret what you've done?\"", "“자네가 한 일을 뉘우친다는 뜻인가?”"]], [["\"No; I don't mean that.", "“아니, 그런 뜻이 아니야."], ["I am too devotedly attached to that dear girl whom we have just left to feel any regret that I have engaged myself to her.", "우리가 방금 두고 온 그 어여쁜 아가씨에게 나는 너무나 마음을 쏟고 있어서, 그 사람과 약혼한 걸 뉘우친다는 마음은 조금도 없네."], ["But I do think that had I managed better with your uncle things might have been different.\"", "다만 자네 삼촌을 내가 좀 더 잘 다루었더라면 사정이 달라졌을지도 모른다는 생각은 드는군.”"]], [["\"I doubt it.", "“나는 그렇게 생각하지 않네."], ["Indeed I know that it is not so; and can assure you that you need not make yourself unhappy on that score.", "아니, 그렇지 않다는 걸 나는 알고 있고, 그 일로 자네가 마음 상해할 것 없다고 장담할 수 있네."], ["I had thought, as you well know, that he would have done something for Lily;--something, though not as much as he always intended to do for Bell.", "자네도 잘 알다시피 나는 삼촌이 릴리를 위해서도 무엇인가 해 주시리라고 생각했었지. 벨을 위해 늘 하려고 마음먹어 온 것만큼은 아니더라도 무엇인가는 말일세."], ["But you may be sure of this; that he had made up his mind as to what he would do.", "하지만 이것만은 믿어도 좋아. 삼촌은 당신이 무엇을 할지 이미 마음을 정해 두고 계셨다는 것."], ["Nothing that you or I could have said would have changed him.\"", "자네나 내가 무슨 말을 했더라도 그분 마음을 바꾸지는 못했을 걸세.”"]], [["\"Well; we won't say anything more about it,\"", "“음, 그 얘기는 이제 그만하세.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["Then they went on again in silence, and arrived at Guestwick in ample time for the train.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 다시 말없이 길을 갔고, 기차 시각에 넉넉히 대어 게스트윅에 닿았다."]], [["\"Let me know as soon as you get to town,\"", "“런던에 닿는 대로 소식 주게.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, of course.", "“아무렴, 그러지."], ["I'll write to you before that.\"", "그 전에도 편지하겠네.”"]], [["And so they parted.", "그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다."], ["As Dale turned and went, Crosbie felt that he liked him less than he had done before; and Bernard, also, as he was driving him, came to the conclusion that Crosbie would not be so good a fellow as a brother-in-law as he had been as a chance friend.", "데일이 돌아서서 가 버릴 때 크로즈비는 전보다 그가 덜 좋아졌다고 느꼈다. 그리고 버나드도 마차를 몰아 그를 데려다 주는 동안, 크로즈비가 우연히 사귄 벗으로서는 좋은 사람이었으나 처남매부 사이로는 그만큼 좋은 사람이 못 되리라는 결론에 이르렀다."], ["\"He'll give us trouble, in some way; and I'm sorry that I brought him down.\"", "“저 친구는 어떤 식으로든 우리에게 골칫거리를 안길 거야. 내가 저 친구를 데려온 것이 유감이군.”"], ["That was Dale's inward conviction in the matter.", "그것이 이 일에 대한 데일의 속마음이었다."]], [["Crosbie's way from Guestwick lay, by railway, to Barchester, the cathedral city lying in the next county, from whence he purposed to have himself conveyed over to Courcy.", "게스트윅을 떠난 크로즈비의 길은 철도를 타고 이웃 주에 있는 대성당 도시 바체스터로 이어졌고, 그는 거기서 마차를 얻어 타고 코시로 건너갈 작정이었다."], ["There had, in truth, been no cause for his very early departure, as he was aware that all arrivals at country houses should take place at some hour not much previous to dinner.", "실은 그렇게 이른 시각에 떠날 까닭이라고는 없었다. 시골 저택을 찾아가는 손님은 누구나 만찬 시각에서 그리 앞서지 않은 때에 닿아야 한다는 것을 그도 알고 있었으니 말이다."], ["He had been determined to be so soon upon the road by a feeling that it would be well for him to get over those last hours.", "그가 그토록 서둘러 길에 나서기로 마음먹은 것은 그 마지막 몇 시간을 얼른 넘겨 버리는 편이 낫겠다는 느낌 때문이었다."], ["Thus he found himself in Barchester at eleven o'clock, with nothing on his hands to do; and, having nothing else to do, he went to church.", "그리하여 그는 열한 시에 바체스터에 이르러 손에 아무 할 일도 없는 처지가 되었고, 달리 할 일이 없으므로 교회에 갔다."], ["There was a full service at the cathedral, and as the verger marshalled him up to one of the empty stalls, a little spare old man was beginning to chant the Litany.", "대성당에서는 온전한 예배가 올려지고 있었고, 성당지기가 그를 이끌어 빈 성가대석 자리 하나에 앉히는 참에 자그마하고 여윈 노인 하나가 연도(連禱)를 선창하기 시작했다."], ["\"I did not mean to fall in for all this,\"", "“여기까지 걸려들 생각은 아니었는데.”"], ["said Crosbie, to himself, as he settled himself with his arms on the cushion.", "크로즈비는 방석 위에 팔을 올려놓고 자리를 잡으며 속으로 중얼거렸다. 그러나 그 노인의 목소리가 지닌 남다른 매력이 곧 그를 사로잡았다."], ["But the peculiar charm of that old man's voice soon attracted him;--a voice that, though tremulous, was yet strong; and he ceased to regret the saint whose honour and glory had occasioned the length of that day's special service.", "떨리면서도 힘이 있는 목소리였다. 그러자 그는 그날의 특별 예배를 그토록 길게 만든 그 성인을 원망하는 마음을 거두었다."]], [["\"And who is the old gentleman who chanted the Litany?\"", "“그런데 연도를 선창하신 저 노신사분은 뉘시오?”"], ["he asked the verger afterwards, as he allowed himself to be shown round the monuments of the cathedral.", "뒤에 성당지기가 그를 이끌고 대성당의 기념비들을 구경시켜 주는 동안 그가 물었다."]], [["\"That's our precentor, sir; Mr.", "“저희 선창자 어른이시지요, 나리."], ["Harding.", "하딩 씨라고 합니다."], ["You must have heard of Mr. Harding.\"", "하딩 씨 말씀은 들어 보셨을 텐데요.”"], ["But Crosbie, with a full apology, confessed his ignorance.", "그러나 크로즈비는 정중히 양해를 구하며 모른다고 털어놓았다."]], [["\"Well, sir; he's pretty well known too, tho'", "“그야, 나리, 저분도 꽤나 이름이 알려진 분입니다."], ["he is so shy like.", "저리 수줍음을 타시기는 해도요."], ["He's father-in-law to our dean, sir; and father-in-law to Archdeacon Grantly also.\"", "저희 수석 사제님의 장인이시고, 그랜틀리 부주교님의 장인이시기도 합니다.”"]], [["\"His daughters have all gone into the profession, then?\"", "“그럼 따님들이 죄다 성직 쪽으로 시집을 갔다는 말이오?”"]], [["\"Why, yes; but Miss Eleanor--for I remember her before she was married at all,--when they lived at the hospital--\"", "“그야 그렇습니다. 하지만 엘리너 아가씨는 — 저는 그분이 시집가시기 훨씬 전부터 기억하고 있습니다, 다들 요양원에 사실 적에 말이지요 —”"]], [["\"At the hospital?\"", "“요양원이라니?”"]], [["\"Hiram's hospital, sir.", "“히램 요양원 말입니다, 나리."], ["He was warden, you know.", "그분이 거기 워든이셨지요."], ["You should go and see the hospital, sir, if you never was there before.", "나리, 여태 가 보신 적이 없으시면 그 요양원을 한번 들러 보셔야 합니다."], ["Well, Miss Eleanor,--that was his youngest,--she married Mr. Bold as her first.", "그래, 엘리너 아가씨는, 그분이 막내따님이신데, 처음에는 볼드 씨에게 시집을 가셨지요."], ["But now she's the dean's lady.\"", "그렇지만 지금은 수석 사제님 부인이십니다.”"]], [["\"Oh; the dean's lady, is she?\"", "“아, 수석 사제 부인이란 말이오?”"]], [["\"Yes, indeed.", "“그렇고말고요."], ["And what do you think, sir? Mr.", "그런데 나리, 이건 또 어떻게 생각하십니까?"], ["Harding might have been dean himself if he'd liked.", "하딩 씨께서는 마음만 잡수셨으면 당신이 수석 사제가 되실 수도 있었습니다."], ["They did offer it to him.\"", "그쪽에서 그 자리를 저분께 내놓았거든요.”"]], [["\"And he refused it?\"", "“그런데 그것을 마다했다고?”"]], [["\"Indeed he did, sir.\"", "“정말로 마다하셨습니다, 나리.”"]], [["\"Nolo decanari.", "“놀로 데카나리라."], ["I never heard of that before.", "그런 말은 여태 들어 본 적이 없군."], ["What made him so modest?\"", "무엇이 그분을 그토록 겸손하게 만들었소?”"]], [["\"Just that, sir; because he is modest.", "“바로 그것입지요, 나리. 겸손한 분이시니까요."], ["He's past his seventy now,--ever so much; but he's just as modest as a young girl.", "이제 연세가 일흔을 넘기셨습니다, 그것도 한참을요. 그런데도 어린 처녀만큼이나 겸손하십니다."], ["A deal more modest than some of them.", "요새 처녀들 가운데 몇몇보다야 훨씬 더 겸손하시고요."], ["To see him and his granddaughter together!\"", "저분과 그 손녀분이 나란히 계신 걸 한번 보시면 압니다!”"]], [["\"And who is his granddaughter?\"", "“그럼 그 손녀란 사람은 누구요?”"]], [["\"Why, Lady Dumbello, as will be the Marchioness of Hartletop.\"", "“아, 레이디 덤벨로시지요. 앞으로 하틀탑 후작부인이 되실 분 말입니다.”"]], [["\"I know Lady Dumbello,\"", "“레이디 덤벨로라면 나도 아는 사람이오.”"], ["said Crosbie; not meaning, however, to boast to the verger of his noble acquaintance.", "크로즈비가 말했다. 그렇다고 성당지기에게 제 고귀한 교분을 뽐낼 뜻이 있었던 것은 아니다."]], [["\"Oh, do you, sir?\"", "“아, 그러십니까, 나리?”"], ["said the man, unconsciously touching his hat at this sign of greatness in the stranger; though in truth he had no love for her ladyship.", "그 사내는 낯선 손님에게서 드러난 이 대단함의 표시에 저도 모르게 모자에 손을 가져다 대며 말했다. 실은 그 자신도 그 마님을 조금도 좋아하지 않았다."], ["\"Perhaps you're going to be one of the party at Courcy Castle.\"", "“아마 코시 성의 손님들 축에 끼시려나 봅니다.”"]], [["\"Well, I believe I am.\"", "“음, 그런 셈이오.”"]], [["\"You'll find her ladyship there before you.", "“가 보시면 그 마님이 나리보다 먼저 와 계실 겁니다."], ["She lunched with her aunt at the deanery as she went through, yesterday; finding it too much trouble to go out to her father's, at Plumstead.", "어제 지나가시는 길에 수석사제관에서 이모님과 점심을 드셨지요. 플럼스테드의 친정아버님 댁까지 나가시기는 너무 번거롭다고 하시면서요."], ["Her father is the archdeacon, you know.", "그 아버님이 부주교님이시랍니다."], ["They do say,--but her ladyship is your friend!\"", "사람들 말로는 — 아 참, 그 마님이 나리와 아시는 사이라고 하셨지요!”"]], [["\"No friend at all; only a very slight acquaintance.", "“아는 사이라고 할 것도 없소. 그저 얼굴이나 겨우 아는 정도지."], ["She's quite as much above my line as she is above her father's.\"", "그 사람은 제 아버지보다 높은 만큼이나 나보다도 높은 데 있는 사람이오.”"]], [["\"Well, she is above them all.", "“아무렴요, 그 마님은 죄다 위에 계시지요."], ["They say she would hardly as much as speak to the old gentleman.\"", "저 노신사분께는 말 한마디 건네시는 법도 거의 없다고들 합니다.”"]], [["\"What, her father?\"", "“뭐라고, 제 아버지한테?”"]], [["\"No, Mr.", "“아니지요, 하딩 씨 말입니다."], ["Harding; he that chanted the Litany just now.", "방금 연도를 선창하신 그분요."], ["There he is, sir, coming out of the deanery.\"", "저기 계시네요, 나리. 수석사제관에서 나오십니다.”"]], [["They were now standing at the door leading out from one of the transepts, and Mr. Harding passed them as they were speaking together.", "두 사람은 그때 익랑 가운데 한 곳에서 밖으로 통하는 문께에 서 있었는데, 그렇게 이야기를 주고받는 사이에 하딩 씨가 그 곁을 지나갔다."], ["He was a little, withered, shambling old man, with bent shoulders, dressed in knee-breeches and long black gaiters, which hung rather loosely about his poor old legs,--rubbing his hands one over the other as he went.", "그는 자그맣고 여위고 발을 끄는 노인으로, 어깨가 굽었고, 반바지에 긴 검정 각반을 차고 있었으며, 그 각반은 가엾은 노인의 다리께에 제법 헐렁하게 늘어져 있었다. 그는 걸어가면서 두 손을 맞잡아 연신 비벼 댔다."], ["And yet he walked quickly; not tottering as he walked, but with an uncertain, doubtful step.", "그러면서도 걸음은 빨랐다. 비틀거리는 걸음은 아니었으나 어딘가 미덥지 못하고 머뭇거리는 걸음이었다."], ["The verger, as Mr. Harding passed, put his hand to his head, and Crosbie also raised his hat.", "하딩 씨가 지나갈 때 성당지기는 손을 머리에 가져다 댔고, 크로즈비도 모자를 들어 보였다."], ["Whereupon Mr. Harding raised his, and bowed, and turned round as though he were about to speak.", "그러자 하딩 씨도 제 모자를 들어 인사하고는, 무슨 말이라도 하려는 듯 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Crosbie felt that he had never seen a face on which traits of human kindness were more plainly written.", "크로즈비는 사람의 다정함이 이보다 더 또렷하게 새겨진 얼굴을 여태 본 적이 없다고 느꼈다."], ["But the old man did not speak.", "그러나 노인은 말을 하지 않았다."], ["He turned his body half round, and then shambled back, as though ashamed of his intention, and passed on.", "그는 몸을 반쯤 돌렸다가, 제 마음이 부끄럽기라도 한 듯 발을 끌며 도로 돌아서서 그대로 지나가 버렸다."]], [["\"He is of that sort that they make the angels of,\"", "“천사를 만든다면 저런 분으로 만들 겁니다.”"], ["said the verger.", "성당지기가 말했다."], ["\"But they can't make many if they want them all as good as he is.", "“하지만 죄다 저분만큼 착한 사람으로 만들려 든다면 많이는 못 만들 테지요."], ["I'm much obliged to you, sir.\"", "대단히 고맙습니다, 나리.”"], ["And he pocketed the half-crown which Crosbie gave him.", "그러고는 크로즈비가 건넨 반 크라운을 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["\"So that's Lady Dumbello's grandfather,\"", "“그러니까 저이가 레이디 덤벨로의 할아버지란 말이지.”"], ["said Crosbie, to himself, as he walked slowly round the close towards the hospital, by the path which the verger had shown him.", "크로즈비는 성당지기가 일러 준 길을 따라 요양원 쪽으로 대성당 구내를 천천히 돌아 걸으며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["He had no great love for Lady Dumbello, who had dared to snub him,--even him.", "그는 레이디 덤벨로를 조금도 좋아하지 않았으니, 그 여자가 감히 그를, 다른 사람도 아닌 그를 쌀쌀맞게 대한 일이 있었던 까닭이다."], ["\"They may make an angel of the old gentleman,\"", "“저 노신사야 천사로 만들 수 있을지 모르지.”"], ["he continued to say; \"but they'll never succeed in that way with the granddaughter.\"", "그는 이어서 중얼거렸다. “하지만 그 손녀를 데리고는 결코 그렇게 되지 않을걸.”"]], [["He sauntered slowly on over a little bridge; and at the gate of the hospital he again came upon Mr.", "그는 작은 다리를 건너 어슬렁어슬렁 천천히 걸어갔다. 그리고 요양원 문간에서 다시 하딩 씨와 마주쳤다."], ["Harding. \"I was going to venture in,\"", "“안을 좀 들여다볼까 하고 실례를 무릅쓸 참이었습니다.”"], ["said he, \"to look at the place.", "그가 말했다."], ["But perhaps I shall be intruding?\"", "“하지만 폐가 되려는지요?”"]], [["\"No, no; by no means,\"", "“아니요, 아니요. 조금도 그렇지 않습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Harding.", "하딩 씨가 말했다."], ["\"Pray come in.", "“어서 들어오세요."], ["I cannot say that I am just at home here.", "내가 여기 주인이라고야 말할 수 없지요."], ["I do not live here,--not now.", "나는 여기 살지 않습니다. 지금은요."], ["But I know the ways of the place well, and can make you welcome.", "그렇지만 이곳 사정은 훤히 알고 있으니 손님을 맞아들일 수는 있어요."], ["That's the warden's house.", "저것이 워든의 사택입니다."], ["Perhaps we won't go in so early in the day, as the lady has a very large family.", "그 댁 부인이 딸린 식구가 아주 많으니, 이렇게 이른 시각에 들어가지는 않는 편이 좋겠군요."], ["An excellent lady, and a dear friend of mine,--as is her husband.\"", "참으로 훌륭한 부인이고 나와는 각별한 벗이에요. 그 남편도 그렇고요.”"]], [["\"And he is warden, you say?\"", "“그러면 그 남편분이 워든이라는 말씀이지요?”"]], [["\"Yes, warden of the hospital.", "“그렇습니다, 요양원의 워든이지요."], ["You see the house, sir.", "저 집이 보이시지요."], ["Very pretty, isn't it?", "참 예쁘지 않습니까?"], ["Very pretty.", "아주 예쁘지요."], ["To my idea it's the prettiest built house I ever saw.\"", "내 생각으로는 여태 본 집 가운데 가장 예쁘게 지은 집입니다.”"]], [["\"I won't go quite so far as that,\"", "“거기까지야 말씀드리기 어렵겠습니다만.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"But you would if you'd lived there twelve years, as I did.", "“나처럼 저기서 열두 해를 살아 보셨다면 그런 말이 나올 겁니다."], ["I lived in that house twelve years, and I don't think there's so sweet a spot on the earth's surface.", "나는 저 집에서 열두 해를 살았는데, 이 땅 위에 저만큼 다정한 자리가 또 있을까 싶어요."], ["Did you ever see such turf as that?\"", "저런 잔디를 어디서 보신 적이 있습니까?”"]], [["\"Very nice indeed,\"", "“참으로 훌륭하군요.”"], ["said Crosbie, who began to make a comparison with Mrs.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["Dale's turf at the Small House, and to determine that the Allington turf was better than that of the hospital.", "그러면서 그는 작은 집에 있는 데일 부인의 잔디와 견주어 보기 시작했고, 앨링턴의 잔디가 이 요양원 잔디보다 낫다고 마음속으로 판정을 내렸다."]], [["\"I had that turf laid down myself.", "“저 잔디는 내가 손수 깔았지요."], ["There were borders there when I first came, with hollyhocks, and those sort of things.", "처음 왔을 적에는 저기에 화단이 있었습니다. 접시꽃이며 그런 것들이 심겨 있었지요."], ["The turf was an improvement.\"", "잔디를 깐 것이 나아진 것이었어요.”"]], [["\"There's no doubt of that, I should say.\"", "“그야 두말할 것도 없겠지요.”"]], [["\"The turf was an improvement, certainly.", "“잔디를 깐 것은 분명 나아진 것이었지요."], ["And I planted those shrubs, too.", "그리고 저 관목들도 내가 심었습니다."], ["There isn't such a Portugal laurel as that in the county.\"", "저만한 포르투갈월계수는 이 주 안에 또 없어요.”"]], [["\"Were you warden here, sir?\"", "“선생께서 여기 워든이셨습니까?”"], ["And Crosbie, as he asked the question, remembered that, in his very young days, he had heard of some newspaper quarrel which had taken place about Hiram's hospital at Barchester.", "그렇게 물으면서 크로즈비는 아주 어릴 적에 바체스터의 히램 요양원을 두고 신문에서 벌어졌던 어떤 다툼 이야기를 들은 적이 있음을 떠올렸다."]], [["\"Yes, sir.", "“그렇습니다."], ["I was warden here for twelve years.", "나는 열두 해 동안 여기 워든이었지요."], ["Dear, dear, dear!", "저런, 저런, 저런!"], ["If they had put any gentleman here that was not on friendly terms with me it would have made me very unhappy,--very.", "만일 나와 사이가 좋지 않은 사람을 여기에 앉혀 놓았더라면 나는 몹시 불행했을 겁니다. 아주 몹시요."], ["But, as it is, I go in and out just as I like; almost as much as I did before they-- But they didn't turn me out.", "그러나 이렇게 되었으니 나는 마음 내키는 대로 드나듭니다. 그들이 나를 — 그렇게 하기 전이나 거의 다름없이 말이지요."], ["There were reasons which made it best that I should resign.\"", "아니, 그들이 나를 내쫓은 것은 아닙니다. 내가 물러나는 편이 가장 낫도록 만든 사정이 있었던 것이지요.”"]], [["\"And you live at the deanery now, Mr. Harding?\"", "“그러면 지금은 수석사제관에서 지내시는군요, 하딩 씨?”"]], [["\"Yes; I live at the deanery now.", "“그렇습니다."], ["But I am not dean, you know.", "나는 지금 수석사제관에서 삽니다."], ["My son-in-law, Dr. Arabin, is the dean.", "그렇다고 내가 수석 사제인 것은 아니에요."], ["I have another daughter married in the neighbourhood, and can truly say that my lines have fallen to me in pleasant places.\"", "내 사위인 애러빈 박사가 수석 사제이지요. 딸 하나는 이 근방으로 시집을 갔으니, 내 몫의 줄이 아름다운 곳에 떨어졌다고 참으로 말할 수 있겠습니다.”"]], [["Then he took Crosbie in among the old men, into all of whose rooms he went.", "그러고 나서 그는 크로즈비를 구호 노인들 틈으로 데리고 들어가 그들의 방을 하나도 빼놓지 않고 돌았다."], ["It was an almshouse for aged men of the city, and before Crosbie had left him Mr. Harding had explained all the circumstances of the hospital, and of the way in which he had left it.", "그곳은 이 도시의 나이 든 남자들을 위한 구빈원이었고, 크로즈비가 그를 떠나기 전에 하딩 씨는 이 요양원의 사정이며 자기가 그곳을 떠나게 된 경위를 죄다 이야기해 주었다."], ["\"I didn't like going, you know; I thought it would break my heart.", "“나는 떠나기가 싫었습니다. 가슴이 미어질 줄만 알았지요."], ["But I could not stay when they said such things as that;--I couldn't stay.", "그렇지만 그런 말들이 오가는 마당에 남아 있을 수는 없었어요. 도저히 남아 있을 수 없었습니다."], ["And, what is more, I should have been wrong to stay.", "게다가 남아 있었더라면 그것이 잘못이었을 겁니다."], ["I see it all now.", "이제는 다 알겠어요."], ["But when I went out under that arch, Mr. Crosbie, leaning on my daughter's arm, I thought that my heart would have broken.\"", "그러나 딸의 팔에 기대어 저 아치 밑을 걸어 나오던 그때에는, 크로즈비 씨, 나는 정말 가슴이 미어지는 줄만 알았습니다.”"], ["And the tears even now ran down the old man's cheeks as he spoke.", "그리고 그렇게 말하는 노인의 뺨으로는 지금도 눈물이 흘러내렸다."]], [["It was a long story, and it need not be repeated here.", "긴 이야기였으니, 여기서 되풀이할 것까지는 없다."], ["And there was no reason why it should have been told to Mr.", "또 그 이야기를 크로즈비 씨에게 들려주어야 할 까닭이라고는 없었다."], ["Crosbie, other than this,--that Mr. Harding was a fond garrulous old man, who loved to indulge his mind in reminiscences of the past.", "굳이 있다면 이것뿐이니, 하딩 씨가 정 많고 말 많은 노인이어서 지난날을 되새기는 데 마음을 맡기기를 좋아했다는 것이다."], ["But this was remarked by Crosbie; that, in telling his story, no word was said by Mr.", "그러나 크로즈비는 이 점을 눈여겨보았다. 그 이야기를 하는 동안 하딩 씨의 입에서는 누구를 헐뜯는 말이 한마디도 나오지 않았다는 것을."], ["Harding injurious to any one.", "그러면서도 그는 해를 입은 사람이었다."], ["And yet he had been injured,--injured very deeply.", "그것도 아주 깊이 해를 입은 사람이었다."], ["\"It was all for the best,\"", "“다 잘된 일이었지요.”"], ["he said at last; \"especially as the happiness has not been denied to me of making myself at home at the old place.", "마침내 그가 말했다. “더구나 옛 자리에서 내 집처럼 지내는 낙까지 내게서 앗아 가지는 않았으니 말입니다."], ["I would take you into the house, which is very comfortable,--very; only it is not always convenient early in the day, where there's a large family.\"", "저 집 안으로 모시고 들어가고 싶습니다만, 참으로 아늑한 집이지요, 아주요. 다만 식구가 많은 집에서는 이른 시각이 늘 편한 것은 아니라서요.”"], ["In hearing which Crosbie was again made to think of his own future home and limited income.", "그 말을 들으며 크로즈비는 다시금 제 앞날의 살림과 빠듯한 수입을 생각하게 되었다."]], [["He had told the old clergyman who he was, and that he was on his way to Courcy.", "그는 이 노목사에게 자기가 누구인지, 또 코시로 가는 길이라는 것을 이미 말해 둔 터였다."], ["\"Where, as I understand, I shall meet a granddaughter of yours.\"", "“거기서 선생의 손녀 되시는 분을 뵙게 되리라고 들었습니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, yes; she is my grandchild.", "“그렇지요, 그렇습니다."], ["She and I have got into different walks of life now, so that I don't see much of her.", "그 애는 내 손녀입니다. 이제는 그 애와 내가 서로 다른 길을 걷게 되어 자주 보지는 못해요."], ["They tell me that she does her duty well in that sphere of life to which it has pleased God to call her.\"", "하느님께서 그 애를 불러 세우신 그 자리에서 제 본분을 잘 지키고 있다고들 하더군요.”"]], [["\"That depends,\"", "“그거야,”"], ["thought Crosbie, \"on what the duties of a viscountess may be supposed to be.\"", "크로즈비는 속으로 생각했다. “자작부인의 본분이라는 것이 무엇이라고들 여기느냐에 달린 문제겠지.”"], ["But he wished his new friend good-by, without saying anything further as to Lady Dumbello, and, at about six o'clock in the evening, had himself driven up under the portico of Courcy Castle.", "그러나 그는 레이디 덤벨로에 대해 더는 아무 말도 하지 않은 채 새로 사귄 벗에게 작별을 고했고, 저녁 여섯 시쯤에 마차를 몰아 코시 성의 현관 지붕 밑으로 들어섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVII. COURCY CASTLE.", "# 제17장. 코시 성"]], [["Courcy Castle was very full.", "코시 성은 손님으로 가득 차 있었다."], ["In the first place, there was a great gathering there of all the Courcy family.", "우선 코시 집안사람들이 죄다 그곳에 모여 있었다. 백작이 와 있었고—물론 백작부인도 있었다."], ["The earl was there,--and the countess, of course.", "해마다 이맘때면 드 코시 백작부인은 늘 집에 있었다."], ["At this period of the year Lady De Courcy was always at home; but the presence of the earl himself had heretofore been by no means so certain.", "그러나 백작 본인이 집에 있는 일은 지금까지 결코 그만큼 확실하지 않았다. 그는 왕실을 드나들며 시종 노릇을 하는 일에, 하일랜드의 모임에, 그리고—틀림없이 꼭 필요했을—런던 사교철의 연장에, 또 독일의 어느 온천장 체류에 몹시 마음을 쏟아 온 사람이었다."], ["He was a man who had been much given to royal visitings and attendances, to parties in the Highlands, to--no doubt necessary--prolongations of the London season, to sojournings at certain German watering-places, convenient, probably, in order that he might study the ways and ceremonies of German Courts,--and to various other absences from home, occasioned by a close pursuit of his own special aims in life; for the Earl De Courcy had been a great courtier.", "그 온천장 체류란 아마도 독일 궁정의 예법과 의식을 연구하기에 편리했을 것이다. 그 밖에도 그는 제 인생의 특별한 목적을 바싹 좇느라 이런저런 일로 집을 비웠다."], ["But of late gout, lumbago, and perhaps also some diminution in his powers of making himself generally agreeable, had reconciled him to domestic duties, and the earl spent much of his time at home.", "드 코시 백작은 대단한 궁정인이었던 것이다. 그러나 근래에는 통풍과 요통이, 그리고 아마도 남들에게 두루 호감을 사는 능력이 얼마간 줄어든 것이 그를 집안의 의무와 화해시켜, 백작은 시간의 많은 부분을 집에서 보내게 되었다."], ["The countess, in former days, had been heard to complain of her lord's frequent absence.", "예전에 백작부인은 남편이 자주 집을 비운다고 불평하곤 했다."], ["But it is hard to please some women,--and now she would not always be satisfied with his presence.", "그러나 어떤 여자들은 좀처럼 만족시키기 어려운 법이어서—이제 그녀는 남편이 집에 있는 것에도 늘 만족하지는 않았다."]], [["And all the sons and daughters were there,--excepting Lord Porlock, the eldest, who never met his father.", "그리고 아들딸도 모두 와 있었다—다만 맏이인 폴록 경만은 빠졌으니, 그는 결코 아버지와 얼굴을 마주하지 않았다."], ["The earl and Lord Porlock were not on terms, and indeed hated each other as only such fathers and such sons can hate.", "백작과 폴록 경은 사이가 틀어져 있었고, 실로 그런 아버지와 그런 아들만이 서로를 미워할 수 있는 방식으로 서로를 미워했다."], ["The Honourable George De Courcy was there with his bride, he having lately performed a manifest duty, in having married a young woman with money.", "아너러블 조지 드 코시는 신부를 데리고 와 있었는데, 그는 얼마 전 돈 있는 젊은 여자와 결혼함으로써 누가 보아도 분명한 의무를 하나 이행한 참이었다."], ["Very young she was not,--having reached some years of her life in advance of thirty; but then, neither was the Honourable George very young; and in this respect the two were not ill-sorted.", "아주 젊다고는 할 수 없었으니—서른을 몇 해 넘긴 나이였다. 그러나 아너러블 조지 역시 아주 젊지는 않았으므로, 그 점에서 두 사람은 어울리지 않는 짝이 아니었다."], ["The lady's money had not been very much,--perhaps thirty thousand pounds or so.", "여자의 돈은 그리 많지 않았다—아마 삼만 파운드쯤 되었을 것이다."], ["But then the Honourable George's money had been absolutely none.", "그러나 아너러블 조지의 돈은 그야말로 한 푼도 없었다."], ["Now he had an income on which he could live, and therefore his father and mother had forgiven him all his sins, and taken him again to their bosom.", "이제 그에게는 그럭저럭 살아갈 만한 수입이 생겼고, 그리하여 아버지와 어머니는 그의 모든 죄를 용서하고 그를 다시 품에 받아들였다."], ["And the marriage was matter of great moment, for the elder scion of the house had not yet taken to himself a wife, and the De Courcy family might have to look to this union for an heir.", "게다가 이 결혼은 대단히 중요한 일이었으니, 이 집안의 맏이가 아직 아내를 맞지 않은 터라 드 코시 집안은 상속자를 이 결합에서 찾아야 할지도 몰랐던 것이다."], ["The lady herself was not beautiful, or clever, or of imposing manners--nor was she of high birth.", "여자 자신은 아름답지도, 영리하지도, 위엄 있는 태도를 갖추지도 않았고—가문이 높지도 않았다."], ["But neither was she ugly, nor unbearably stupid.", "그러나 못생기지도 않았고, 견딜 수 없을 만큼 어리석지도 않았다."], ["Her manners were, at any rate, innocent; and as to her birth,--seeing that, from the first, she was not supposed to have had any,--no disappointment was felt.", "적어도 그 태도는 순박했다. 그리고 가문으로 말하자면—애초부터 그런 것이 있으리라고 아무도 생각하지 않았으므로—실망할 일도 없었다."], ["Her father had been a coal-merchant.", "그 아버지는 석탄 상인이었다."], ["She was always called Mrs. George, and the effort made respecting her by everybody in and about the family was to treat her as though she were a figure of a woman, a large well-dressed resemblance of a being, whom it was necessary for certain purposes that the De Courcys should carry in their train.", "사람들은 늘 그녀를 조지 부인이라 불렀고, 집안 안팎의 모든 사람이 그녀를 두고 기울인 노력이란, 그녀를 여자의 형상, 곧 잘 차려입은 커다란 사람 모양의 물건, 어떤 필요 때문에 드 코시 집안이 행렬에 데리고 다녀야만 하는 물건처럼 대하는 것이었다."], ["Of the Honourable George we may further observe, that, having been a spendthrift all his life, he had now become strictly parsimonious.", "아너러블 조지에 대해서는 이런 말을 더 해 둘 수 있겠다. 그는 평생 낭비꾼으로 살아왔으면서 이제는 지독한 구두쇠가 되었다는 것이다."], ["Having reached the discreet age of forty, he had at last learned that beggary was objectionable; and he, therefore, devoted every energy of his mind to saving shillings and pence wherever pence and shillings might be saved.", "분별이 든다는 마흔 살에 이르러 그는 마침내 빈털터리 신세가 못마땅한 것임을 배웠고, 그리하여 실링과 펜스를 아낄 수 있는 곳이면 어디서든 실링과 펜스를 아끼는 데 온 정신을 쏟았다."], ["When first this turn came upon him both his father and mother were delighted to observe it; but, although it had hardly yet lasted over twelve months, some evil results were beginning to appear.", "이 변화가 처음 그에게 찾아왔을 때에는 아버지도 어머니도 그것을 보고 기뻐했다. 그러나 아직 열두 달이 채 지나지 않았는데도 몇 가지 나쁜 결과가 나타나기 시작했다."], ["Though possessed of an income, he would take no steps towards possessing himself of a house.", "수입이 있으면서도 그는 제 집을 마련할 생각을 조금도 하지 않았다."], ["He hung by the paternal mansion, either in town or country; drank the paternal wines, rode the paternal horses, and had even contrived to obtain his wife's dresses from the maternal milliner.", "그는 도시에서든 시골에서든 아버지의 저택에 눌어붙어 아버지의 포도주를 마시고 아버지의 말을 탔으며, 심지어 아내의 옷까지 어머니의 양장점에서 얻어 내는 재주를 부렸다."], ["In the completion of which little last success, however, some slight family dissent had showed itself.", "다만 이 마지막의 자잘한 성공을 이루는 과정에서는 집안에 약간의 불화가 드러나기도 했다."]], [["The Honourable John, the third son, was also at Courcy.", "셋째 아들인 아너러블 존도 코시에 와 있었다."], ["He had as yet taken to himself no wife, and as he had not hitherto made himself conspicuously useful in any special walk of life his family were beginning to regard him as a burden.", "그는 아직 아내를 맞지 않았고, 어느 특정한 인생의 길에서도 눈에 띄게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되어 본 적이 없었으므로 집안에서는 그를 짐으로 여기기 시작하던 참이었다."], ["Having no income of his own to save, he had not copied his brother's virtue of parsimony; and, to tell the truth plainly, had made himself so generally troublesome to his father, that he had been on more than one occasion threatened with expulsion from the family roof.", "아껴 둘 제 수입이 없었으니 형의 인색이라는 미덕을 본받지도 못했고, 사실대로 툭 터놓고 말하자면 아버지에게 어찌나 두루 성가신 존재가 되었던지 한두 번이 아니게 집에서 쫓아내겠다는 위협을 받은 터였다."], ["But it is not easy to expel a son.", "그러나 아들을 내쫓기란 쉬운 일이 아니다."], ["Human fledglings cannot be driven out of the nest like young birds.", "사람의 새끼는 어린 새처럼 둥지 밖으로 몰아낼 수가 없다."], ["An Honourable John turned adrift into absolute poverty will make himself heard of in the world,--if in no other way, by his ugliness as he starves.", "아너러블 존 하나를 알거지로 만들어 세상에 내던져 보라, 그는 어떻게든 세상에 제 소문을 낼 것이다—달리 방법이 없으면 굶주려 가는 그 흉한 꼴로라도."], ["A thorough-going ne'er-do-well in the upper classes has eminent advantages on his side in the battle which he fights against respectability.", "상류층의 철저한 건달은 체면과 벌이는 싸움에서 제 편에 뛰어난 이점을 여럿 지니고 있다."], ["He can't be sent to Australia against his will.", "그는 제 뜻을 거슬러 오스트레일리아로 보내질 수 없다."], ["He can't be sent to the poor-house without the knowledge of all the world.", "온 세상이 알지 못하게 구빈원으로 보내질 수도 없다."], ["He can't be kept out of tradesmen's shops; nor, without terrible scandal, can he be kept away from the paternal properties.", "상인들의 가게에 못 들어가게 막을 수도 없거니와, 무시무시한 추문 없이는 아버지의 영지에서 떼어 놓을 수도 없다. 백작은 위협하고 으르렁대며 이를 드러냈다."], ["The earl had threatened, and snarled, and shown his teeth; he was an angry man, and a man who could look very angry; with eyes which could almost become red, and a brow that wrinkled itself in perpendicular wrinkles, sometimes very terrible to behold.", "그는 성마른 사람이었고, 몹시 성난 얼굴을 지을 줄 아는 사람이었다. 눈이 거의 붉어질 듯했고 이마에는 세로로 주름이 잡혀, 때로는 보기에 아주 무시무시했다."], ["But he was an inconsistent man, and the Honourable John had learned to measure his father, and in an accurate balance.", "그러나 그는 일관성이 없는 사람이었고, 아너러블 존은 아버지를 재는 법을, 그것도 아주 정확한 저울로 재는 법을 익힌 터였다."]], [["I have mentioned the sons first, because it is to be presumed that they were the elder, seeing that their names were mentioned before those of their sisters in all the peerages.", "내가 아들들을 먼저 말한 것은 그들이 손위였으리라고 짐작되기 때문이다. 어느 귀족 명감을 보아도 그들의 이름이 누이들의 이름보다 앞에 적혀 있으니 말이다."], ["But there were four daughters,--the Ladies Amelia, Rosina, Margaretta, and Alexandrina.", "그러나 딸은 넷이었다—레이디 어밀리아, 레이디 로지나, 레이디 마거리타, 레이디 알렉산드리나."], ["They, we may say, were the flowers of the family, having so lived that they had created none of those family feuds which had been so frequent between their father and their brothers.", "그들은 이를테면 이 집안의 꽃이었으니, 아버지와 오라비들 사이에 그토록 잦았던 집안싸움을 하나도 일으키지 않고 살아왔던 것이다."], ["They were discreet, high-bred women, thinking, perhaps, a little too much of their own position in the world, and somewhat apt to put a wrong value on those advantages which they possessed, and on those which they did not possess.", "그들은 신중하고 교양 있는 여자들이었으나, 아마도 세상에서의 제 자리를 조금 지나치게 의식했고, 제가 가진 이점과 갖지 못한 이점에 그릇된 값을 매기는 버릇이 다소 있었다."], ["The Lady Amelia was already married, having made a substantial if not a brilliant match with Mr.", "레이디 어밀리아는 이미 결혼한 몸으로, 눈부시지는 않아도 실속 있는 혼처를 잡아 모티머 게이지비 씨와 맺어졌다."], ["Mortimer Gazebee, a flourishing solicitor, belonging to a firm which had for many years acted as agents to the De Courcy property.", "게이지비 씨는 잘나가는 사무 변호사로, 여러 해 동안 드 코시 영지의 대리인 노릇을 해 온 회사에 속해 있었다."], ["Mortimer Gazebee was now member of Parliament for Barchester, partly through the influence of his father-in-law.", "모티머 게이지비는 이제 바체스터의 하원 의원이었는데, 그 자리는 얼마간 장인의 힘 덕이었다."], ["That this should be so was a matter of great disgust to the Honourable George, who thought that the seat should have belonged to him. But as Mr.", "이렇게 된 것이 아너러블 조지에게는 몹시 아니꼬운 일이었으니, 그 의석은 마땅히 제 것이 되었어야 한다고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["Gazebee had paid the very heavy expenses of the election out of his own pocket, and as George De Courcy certainly could not have paid them, the justice of his claim may be questionable.", "그러나 게이지비 씨가 선거의 아주 무거운 비용을 제 주머니에서 치렀고 조지 드 코시는 도저히 그 돈을 치를 수 없었을 터이니, 그 요구가 정당한지는 의심해 볼 만하다."], ["Lady Amelia Gazebee was now the happy mother of many babies, whom she was wont to carry with her on her visits to Courcy Castle, and had become an excellent partner to her husband.", "레이디 어밀리아 게이지비는 이제 여러 아기의 행복한 어머니가 되어 코시 성을 찾을 때면 아이들을 데리고 다니는 것이 버릇이었고, 남편에게는 더없이 훌륭한 반려가 되어 있었다."], ["He would perhaps have liked it better if she had not spoken so frequently to him of her own high position as the daughter of an earl, or so frequently to others of her low position as the wife of an attorney.", "다만 그녀가 남편에게는 백작의 딸이라는 제 높은 신분을 그토록 자주 말하지 않고, 남들에게는 변호사의 아내라는 제 낮은 신분을 그토록 자주 말하지 않았더라면 남편은 아마 더 마음이 편했을 것이다."], ["But, on the whole, they did very well together, and Mr. Gazebee had gotten from his marriage quite as much as he expected when he made it.", "그러나 대체로 두 사람은 잘 지냈고, 게이지비 씨는 결혼할 때 기대했던 만큼은 충분히 이 결혼에서 얻어 냈다."]], [["The Lady Rosina was very religious; and I do not know that she was conspicuous in any other way, unless it might be that she somewhat resembled her father in her temper.", "레이디 로지나는 신앙심이 매우 깊었다. 그리고 그 밖의 어떤 점에서 그녀가 두드러졌는지 나는 알지 못하니, 굳이 든다면 성미가 아버지를 얼마간 닮았다는 것쯤이겠다."], ["It was of the Lady Rosina that the servants were afraid, especially with reference to that so-called day of rest which, under her dominion, had become to many of them a day of restless torment.", "하인들이 무서워한 사람은 바로 레이디 로지나였는데, 특히 이른바 안식의 날이라는 그날과 관련해서 그러했다. 그녀의 지배 아래 그날은 하인들 여럿에게 쉴 틈 없는 고문의 날이 되어 있었다."], ["It had not always been so with the Lady Rosina; but her eyes had been opened by the wife of a great church dignitary in the neighbourhood, and she had undergone regeneration.", "레이디 로지나가 늘 그러했던 것은 아니다. 그러나 이웃에 사는 어느 높은 성직자의 부인이 그녀의 눈을 뜨게 해 주었고, 그리하여 그녀는 거듭남을 겪었던 것이다."], ["How great may be the misery inflicted by an energetic, unmarried, healthy woman in that condition,--a woman with no husband, or children, or duties, to distract her from her work--I pray that my readers may never know.", "기운차고 결혼하지 않았으며 몸 성한 여자가 그런 상태에 빠졌을 때—남편도 자식도 의무도 없어 제 일에서 마음을 돌릴 것이 아무것도 없는 여자가—얼마나 큰 괴로움을 끼칠 수 있는지, 내 독자들은 결코 알게 되지 않기를 나는 빈다."]], [["The Lady Margaretta was her mother's favourite, and she was like her mother in all things,--except that her mother had been a beauty.", "레이디 마거리타는 어머니가 가장 아끼는 딸이었고, 모든 점에서 어머니를 닮았다—다만 어머니는 미인이었다는 점만 달랐다."], ["The world called her proud, disdainful, and even insolent; but the world was not aware that in all that she did she was acting in accordance with a principle which had called for much self-abnegation.", "세상은 그녀를 오만하고 도도하며 심지어 무례하다고 했으나, 그녀가 하는 모든 일이 실은 많은 자기 부정을 요구하는 어떤 원칙에 따른 것이라는 사실을 세상은 알지 못했다."], ["She had considered it her duty to be a De Courcy and an earl's daughter at all times; and consequently she had sacrificed to her idea of duty all popularity, adulation, and such admiration as would have been awarded to her as a well-dressed, tall, fashionable, and by no means stupid young woman.", "그녀는 언제 어느 때나 드 코시 집안사람이자 백작의 딸로 있는 것이 제 의무라 여겨 왔고, 그리하여 그 의무의 관념에 인기와 찬사를, 그리고 잘 차려입고 키가 크며 세련되고 결코 어리석지 않은 젊은 여자로서 마땅히 받았을 만한 온갖 흠모를 제물로 바쳤다."], ["To be at all times in something higher than they who were manifestly below her in rank,--that was the effort that she was ever making.", "신분에서 저보다 명백히 아래인 자들보다 어느 때나 무언가 더 높은 데 있는 것—그것이 그녀가 늘 기울이고 있던 노력이었다."], ["But she had been a good daughter, assisting her mother, as best she might, in all family troubles, and never repining at the cold, colourless, unlovely life which had been vouchsafed to her.", "그러나 그녀는 좋은 딸이어서 집안에 어려움이 있을 때마다 힘닿는 대로 어머니를 도왔고, 저에게 주어진 차갑고 빛깔 없고 사랑스럽지 못한 삶을 두고 결코 투덜대지 않았다."]], [["Alexandrina was the beauty of the family, and was in truth the youngest.", "알렉산드리나는 이 집안의 미인이었고, 사실 막내였다."], ["But even she was not very young, and was beginning to make her friends uneasy lest she, too, should let the precious season of hay-harvest run by without due use of her summer's sun.", "그러나 그녀조차도 그리 젊지 않았고, 그래서 이 딸마저 여름 볕을 제대로 쓰지 못한 채 건초 거둘 귀한 철을 흘려보내는 것이 아닌가 하여 벗들이 불안해하기 시작하던 참이었다."], ["She had, perhaps, counted too much on her beauty, which had been beauty according to law rather than beauty according to taste, and had looked, probably, for too bounteous a harvest.", "그녀는 아마도 제 아름다움을 너무 믿었던 듯한데, 그것은 취향에 맞는 아름다움이라기보다 법칙에 맞는 아름다움이었다. 그리고 아마도 너무 넉넉한 수확을 바랐던 듯하다."], ["That her forehead, and nose, and cheeks, and chin were well-formed, no man could deny.", "그 이마와 코와 뺨과 턱이 잘생겼다는 것은 어떤 남자도 부인할 수 없었다."], ["Her hair was soft and plentiful.", "머리칼은 부드럽고 숱이 많았다."], ["Her teeth were good, and her eyes were long and oval.", "이는 고르고 눈은 길쭉한 달걀형이었다."], ["But the fault of her face was this,--that when you left her you could not remember it.", "그러나 그 얼굴의 흠은 이것이었다—헤어지고 나면 그 얼굴이 떠오르지 않는다는 것."], ["After a first acquaintance you could meet her again and not know her.", "한 번 안면을 트고 나서 다시 만나도 알아보지 못할 수 있었다."], ["After many meetings you would fail to carry away with you any portrait of her features.", "여러 번 만난 뒤에도 그 이목구비의 초상 하나를 마음에 담아 오지 못했다."], ["But such as she had been at twenty, such was she now at thirty.", "그러나 스무 살 때 어떠했든, 서른 살인 지금도 꼭 그러했다."], ["Years had not robbed her face of its regularity, or ruffled the smoothness of her too even forehead.", "세월도 그 얼굴에서 반듯함을 앗아 가지 못했고, 너무 매끈한 그 이마를 흐트러뜨리지 못했다."], ["Rumour had declared that on more than one, or perhaps more than two occasions, Lady Alexandrina had been already induced to plight her troth in return for proffered love; but we all know that Rumour, when she takes to such topics, exaggerates the truth, and sets down much in malice.", "레이디 알렉산드리나가 한 번이 아니라 어쩌면 두 번도 넘게, 바쳐진 사랑에 답하여 이미 정혼의 언약을 했다는 소문이 돌았다. 그러나 소문이라는 것이 그런 화제를 다룰 때면 사실을 부풀리고 많은 것을 악의로 적어 넣는다는 것을 우리는 다들 안다."], ["The lady was once engaged, the engagement lasting for two years, and the engagement had been broken off, owing to some money difficulties between the gentlemen of the families.", "이 아가씨는 한 번 약혼한 일이 있었고, 그 약혼은 두 해 동안 이어지다가 양가의 남자들 사이에 돈 문제가 얽히는 바람에 깨졌다."], ["Since that she had become somewhat querulous, and was supposed to be uneasy on that subject of her haymaking.", "그 뒤로 그녀는 다소 투덜대는 사람이 되었고, 제 건초 거두기라는 그 문제로 마음이 편치 않다는 말이 있었다."], ["Her glass and her maid assured her that her sun shone still as brightly as ever; but her spirit was becoming weary with waiting, and she dreaded lest she should become a terror to all, as was her sister Rosina, or an object of interest to none, as was Margaretta.", "거울과 하녀는 그녀의 볕이 여전히 예전만큼 밝게 비친다고 장담해 주었다. 그러나 그 마음은 기다림에 지쳐 가고 있었고, 언니 로지나처럼 모두에게 무서운 사람이 되거나 마거리타처럼 아무의 관심도 끌지 못하는 사람이 될까 두려웠다."], ["It was from her especially that this message had been sent to our friend Crosbie; for, during the last spring in London, she and Crosbie had known each other well.", "우리의 벗 크로즈비에게 그 전갈이 보내진 것은 특히 그녀에게서 비롯된 일이었다. 지난봄 런던에서 그녀와 크로즈비는 서로를 잘 알고 지냈던 것이다."], ["Yes, my gentle readers; it is true, as your heart suggests to you.", "그렇다, 나의 점잖은 독자들이여. 그대들의 마음이 그대들에게 일러 주는 대로다."], ["Under such circumstances Mr. Crosbie should not have gone to Courcy Castle.", "그런 사정이라면 크로즈비 씨는 코시 성에 가지 말았어야 했다."]], [["Such was the family circle of the De Courcys.", "드 코시 집안의 식구들은 이러했다."], ["Among their present guests I need not enumerate many.", "그때 와 있던 손님들 가운데 많은 이를 여기 늘어놓을 필요는 없겠다."], ["First and foremost in all respects was Lady Dumbello, of whose parentage and position a few words were said in the last chapter.", "무엇으로 보나 첫째가고 으뜸가는 이는 레이디 덤벨로였으니, 그 태생과 처지에 대해서는 앞 장에서 몇 마디 적어 두었다."], ["She was a lady still very young, having as yet been little more than two years married.", "그녀는 아직 아주 젊은 부인으로, 결혼한 지 겨우 두 해가 조금 넘었을 뿐이었다."], ["But in those two years her triumphs had been many;--so many, that in the great world her standing already equalled that of her celebrated mother-in-law, the Marchioness of Hartletop, who, for twenty years, had owned no greater potentate than herself in the realms of fashion.", "그러나 그 두 해 동안 그녀의 승리는 여러 번이었으니—어찌나 여러 번이었던지 큰 세상에서 그녀의 지위는 이미 이름 높은 시어머니 하틀탑 후작부인과 맞먹었다. 후작부인으로 말하자면 스무 해 동안 유행의 왕국에서 저보다 더 큰 세력자를 두어 본 적이 없는 사람이었다."], ["But Lady Dumbello was every inch as great as she; and men said, and women also, that the daughter-in-law would soon be the greater.", "그러나 레이디 덤벨로는 한 치도 모자람 없이 그만큼 대단했고, 남자들도 여자들도 머지않아 며느리 쪽이 더 대단해지리라고 말했다."]], [["\"I'll be hanged if I can understand how she does it,\"", "“저 여자가 대체 어떻게 저걸 해내는지 내가 알 수 있다면 목을 매달아도 좋소.”"], ["a certain noble peer had once said to Crosbie, standing at the door of Sebright's, during the latter days of the last season.", "지난 사교철이 저물어 갈 무렵 세브라이트 문간에 서서 어느 귀족이 크로즈비에게 이렇게 말한 적이 있었다."], ["\"She never says anything to any one.", "“아무한테도 아무 말을 안 하잖소."], ["She won't speak ten words a whole night through.\"", "하룻밤 내내 열 마디도 입 밖에 안 낼 거요.”"]], [["\"I don't think she has an idea in her head,\"", "“저 부인 머릿속에 무슨 생각이 하나라도 들어 있는 것 같지는 않군요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Let me tell you that she must be a very clever woman,\"", "“저 여자는 아주 영리한 여자임에 틀림없다는 걸 내 말해 두겠소.”"], ["continued the noble peer.", "귀족이 말을 이었다."], ["\"No fool could do as she does.", "“바보라면 저렇게 못 하지."], ["Remember, she's only a parson's daughter; and as for beauty--\"", "생각해 보시오, 저 여자는 한낱 목사의 딸일 뿐이오. 게다가 미모로 말하자면—”"]], [["\"I don't admire her for one,\"", "“저는 저 부인의 미모를 흠모하는 축이 아닙니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't want to run away with her, if you mean that,\"", "“그 여자를 데리고 달아나고 싶다는 말은 아니오, 그런 뜻이라면.”"], ["said the peer; \"but she is handsome, no doubt.", "귀족이 말했다. “그래도 잘생긴 건 틀림없소."], ["I wonder whether Dumbello likes it.\"", "덤벨로가 이걸 좋아하는지 모르겠군.”"]], [["Dumbello did like it.", "덤벨로는 좋아했다."], ["It satisfied his ambition to be led about as the senior lacquey in his wife's train.", "아내의 행렬에서 우두머리 하인 노릇으로 끌려다니는 것이 그의 야심을 채워 주었다."], ["He believed himself to be a great man because the world fought for his wife's presence; and considered himself to be distinguished even among the eldest sons of marquises, by the greatness reflected from the parson's daughter whom he had married.", "세상이 제 아내와 함께 있으려고 다투는 까닭에 그는 스스로를 대단한 사람이라 믿었고, 제가 아내로 맞은 목사의 딸에게서 비쳐 오는 광채 덕분에 후작의 맏아들들 가운데서도 저는 남다르다고 여겼다."], ["He had now been brought to Courcy Castle, and felt himself proud of his situation because Lady Dumbello had made considerable difficulty in according this week to the Countess De Courcy.", "이제 그는 코시 성에 끌려와 있었는데, 레이디 덤벨로가 이 한 주를 드 코시 백작부인에게 내주기까지 여간 뜸을 들이지 않았던 까닭에 제 처지가 자랑스러웠다."]], [["And Lady Julia De Guest was already there, the sister of the other old earl who lived in the next county.", "그리고 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트가 벌써 와 있었으니, 이웃 주에 사는 또 한 사람의 늙은 백작의 누이였다."], ["She had only arrived on the day before, but had been quick in spreading the news as to Crosbie's engagement.", "그녀는 겨우 하루 전에 도착했으면서도 크로즈비의 약혼 소식은 재빠르게 퍼뜨려 놓은 터였다."], ["\"Engaged to one of the Dales, is he?\"", "“데일 집안의 누구와 약혼했다지요?”"], ["said the countess, with a pretty little smile, which showed plainly that the matter was one of no interest to herself.", "백작부인이 예쁘장하게 살짝 웃으며 말했는데, 그 웃음은 이 일이 저에게는 아무 관심거리도 아님을 훤히 드러냈다."], ["\"Has she got any money?\"", "“돈은 좀 있소?”"]], [["\"Not a shilling, I should think,\"", "“한 푼도 없을 거예요.”"], ["said the Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Pretty, I suppose?\"", "“예쁘기는 한 모양이지요?”"], ["suggested the countess.", "백작부인이 넌지시 물었다."]], [["\"Why, yes; she is pretty--and a nice girl.", "“네, 뭐, 예쁘기는 해요—착한 아가씨고요."], ["I don't know whether her mother and uncle were very wise in encouraging Mr. Crosbie.", "다만 그 어머니와 삼촌이 크로즈비 씨를 그렇게 부추긴 것이 아주 현명한 일이었는지는 모르겠어요."], ["I don't hear that he has anything special to recommend him,--in the way of money I mean.\"", "그 사람이 내세울 만한 것이 특별히 있다는 이야기는 못 들었으니까요—돈으로 말하자면 그렇다는 겁니다.”"]], [["\"I dare say it will come to nothing,\"", "“아마 흐지부지되고 말 거요.”"], ["said the countess, who liked to hear of girls being engaged and then losing their promised husbands.", "백작부인이 말했다. 그녀는 아가씨들이 약혼했다가 약속된 남편을 잃었다는 이야기를 듣기 좋아하는 사람이었다."], ["She did not know that she liked it, but she did; and already had pleasure in anticipating poor Lily's discomfiture.", "스스로는 그것을 좋아하는 줄 몰랐으나 실은 좋아했다. 그리고 벌써 가여운 릴리가 낭패를 볼 일을 미리 그려 보며 즐거워하고 있었다."], ["But not the less was she angry with Crosbie, feeling that he was making his way into her house under false pretences.", "그러나 그러면서도 크로즈비에게는 화가 났으니, 그가 거짓 구실로 제 집에 발을 들이고 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다."]], [["And Alexandrina also was angry when Lady Julia repeated the same tidings in her hearing.", "그리고 알렉산드리나도 레이디 줄리아가 제가 듣는 데서 같은 소식을 되풀이하자 화가 났다."], ["\"I really don't think we care very much about it, Lady Julia,\"", "“저희는 정말 그 일에 별로 관심이 없어요, 레이디 줄리아.”"], ["said she, with a little toss of her head.", "그녀가 고개를 살짝 젖히며 말했다."], ["\"That's three times we've been told of Miss Dale's good fortune.\"", "“데일 양의 그 행운 이야기를 저희가 듣기는 이번이 세 번째예요.”"]], [["\"The Dales are related to you, I think?\"", "“데일 집안은 댁과 친척이지요, 아마?”"], ["said Margaretta.", "마거리타가 말했다."]], [["\"Not at all,\"", "“전혀 아니에요.”"], ["said Lady Julia, bristling up.", "레이디 줄리아가 발끈해서 말했다."], ["\"The lady whom Mr. Crosbie proposes to marry is in no way connected with us.", "“크로즈비 씨가 결혼하겠다는 아가씨는 우리와 아무 연고도 없습니다."], ["Her cousin, who is the heir to the Allington property, is my nephew by his mother.\"", "앨링턴 재산의 상속자인 그 아가씨의 사촌이 어머니 쪽으로 내 조카일 뿐이지요.”"], ["And then the subject was dropped.", "그러고서 그 이야기는 끝났다."]], [["Crosbie, on his arrival, was shown up into his room, told the hour of dinner, and left to his devices.", "크로즈비는 도착하자 제 방으로 안내되어 만찬 시각을 전해 듣고는 알아서 하도록 내버려졌다."], ["He had been at the castle before, and knew the ways of the house.", "그는 전에도 이 성에 와 본 적이 있어 이 집의 사정을 알고 있었다."], ["So he sat himself down to his table, and began a letter to Lily.", "그래서 그는 탁자 앞에 앉아 릴리에게 보낼 편지를 쓰기 시작했다."], ["But he had not proceeded far, not having as yet indeed made up his mind as to the form in which he would commence it, but was sitting idly with the pen in his hand, thinking of Lily, and thinking also how such houses as this in which he now found himself would be soon closed against him, when there came a rap at his door, and before he could answer the Honourable John entered the room.", "그러나 얼마 나아가지 못했으니, 실은 어떤 투로 첫머리를 열지조차 아직 정하지 못한 채 손에 펜을 들고 우두커니 앉아 릴리를 생각하고, 또 지금 제가 와 있는 이런 집들이 머지않아 저에게 문을 닫으리라는 것을 생각하고 있었는데, 그때 문을 두드리는 소리가 나더니 대답할 새도 없이 아너러블 존이 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["\"Well, old fellow,\"", "“여어, 이 사람아.”"], ["said the Honourable John, \"how are you?\"", "아너러블 존이 말했다. “잘 지냈나?”"]], [["Crosbie had been intimate with John De Courcy, but never felt for him either friendship or liking.", "크로즈비는 존 드 코시와 가깝게 지내 왔으나 그에게 우정도 호감도 느껴 본 적이 없었다."], ["Crosbie did not like such men as John De Courcy; but nevertheless, they called each other old fellow, poked each other's ribs, and were very intimate.", "크로즈비는 존 드 코시 같은 자들을 좋아하지 않았다. 그럼에도 두 사람은 서로를 이 사람 저 사람 하고 부르며 서로 옆구리를 찌르고 아주 허물없이 지냈다."]], [["\"Heard you were here,\"", "“여기 와 있다는 소릴 들었지.”"], ["continued the Honourable John; \"so I thought I would come up and look after you.", "아너러블 존이 말을 이었다. “그래서 올라와 얼굴이나 보자 했네."], ["Going to be married, ain't you?\"", "장가간다며, 안 그런가?”"]], [["\"Not that I know of,\"", "“난 그런 줄 모르겠는데.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Come, we know better than that.", "“이보게, 우린 그렇지 않다는 걸 다 아네."], ["The women have been talking about it for the last three days.", "여자들이 사흘째 그 얘기를 하고 있어."], ["I had her name quite pat yesterday, but I've forgot it now.", "어제만 해도 그 아가씨 이름이 입에 척 붙어 있었는데 지금은 잊어버렸군."], ["Hasn't got a tanner; has she?\"", "땡전 한 푼 없다지, 그렇지?”"], ["And the Honourable John had now seated himself upon the table.", "그러면서 아너러블 존은 어느새 탁자 위에 걸터앉아 있었다."]], [["\"You seem to know a great deal more about it than I do.\"", "“나보다 자네가 훨씬 많이 아는 모양이군.”"]], [["\"It is that old woman from Guestwick who told us, then.", "“그렇다면 우리한테 일러 준 건 게스트윅에서 온 그 노파로군."], ["The women will be at you at once, you'll find.", "여자들이 곧장 자네한테 달려들 걸세, 두고 보게."], ["If there's nothing in it, it's what I call a d---- shame.", "아무것도 아닌 일이라면 그건 내가 보기엔 빌어먹을 창피한 노릇이지."], ["Why should they always pull a fellow to pieces in that way?", "왜 늘 남을 그런 식으로 갈가리 찢어 놔야 한단 말인가?"], ["They were going to marry me the other day!\"", "저번에는 나를 장가보내려 들더라니까!”"]], [["\"Were they indeed, though?\"", "“그거 정말인가?”"]], [["\"To Harriet Twistleton.", "“해리엇 트위슬턴한테 말일세."], ["You know Harriet Twistleton?", "해리엇 트위슬턴 알지?"], ["An uncommon fine girl, you know.", "여간 근사한 아가씨가 아니라네."], ["But I wasn't going to be caught like that.", "하지만 난 그런 식으로 잡힐 사람이 아니지."], ["I'm very fond of Harriet,--in my way, you know; but they don't catch an old bird like me with chaff.\"", "나야 해리엇을 무척 좋아해—내 나름대로 말일세. 그렇지만 나 같은 늙은 새는 쭉정이로 잡히지 않는 법이거든.”"]], [["\"I condole with Miss Twistleton for what she has lost.\"", "“트위슬턴 양이 놓친 것을 두고 내가 위로를 보내야겠군.”"]], [["\"I don't know about condoling.", "“위로랄 것까지야 있겠나."], ["But upon my word that getting married is a very slow thing.", "그렇지만 정말이지 결혼이란 건 여간 김빠지는 노릇이 아니야."], ["Have you seen George's wife?\"", "조지 아내는 봤나?”"]], [["Crosbie declared that he had not as yet had that pleasure.", "크로즈비는 아직 그런 기쁨을 누려 보지 못했노라고 밝혔다."]], [["\"She's here now, you know.", "“지금 여기 와 있다네."], ["I wouldn't have taken her, not if she'd had ten times thirty thousand pounds.", "나 같으면 삼만 파운드의 열 곱절을 가졌다 해도 그 여자를 맞지 않았을 걸세."], ["By Jove, no.", "맹세코 그렇지."], ["But he likes it well enough.", "그런데 형은 꽤나 마음에 들어 하더군."], ["Would you believe it now?--he cares for nothing on earth except money.", "자네 믿기나 하겠나?—형은 이 세상에서 돈 말고는 아무것도 마음에 두지 않는다니까."], ["You never saw such a fellow.", "그런 사람은 자네도 본 적이 없을 걸세."], ["But I'll tell you what, his nose will be out of joint yet, for Porlock is going to marry.", "그런데 내 말해 두겠는데, 형의 코는 아직 납작해질 참이야. 폴록이 장가를 든다니 말일세."], ["I heard it from Colepepper, who almost lives with Porlock.", "폴록과 거의 살다시피 하는 콜페퍼한테서 들었네."], ["As soon as Porlock heard that she was in the family way he immediately made up his mind to cut him out.\"", "폴록은 그 여자가 아이를 뱄다는 소릴 듣자마자 당장 형을 제쳐 버리기로 마음먹었다는군.”"]], [["\"That was a great sign of brotherly love,\"", "“형제간의 우애가 참으로 대단하군.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"I knew he'd do it,\"", "“그가 그럴 줄 난 알고 있었지.”"], ["said John; \"and so I told George before he got himself spliced.", "존이 말했다. “그래서 조지가 짝을 맺기 전에 내가 그렇게 말해 주었네."], ["But he would go on.", "그런데도 형은 굳이 밀고 나가더군."], ["If he'd remained as he was for four or five years longer there would have been no danger;--for Porlock, you know, is leading the deuce of a life.", "사오 년만 더 지금 그대로 있었더라면 아무 위험도 없었을 걸세—폴록은, 알다시피, 지독한 생활을 하고 있으니까."], ["I shouldn't wonder if he didn't reform now, and take to singing psalms or something of that sort.\"", "이제 와서 그가 마음을 고쳐먹고 찬송가나 부르고 앉아 있다 한들 난 놀라지 않겠네.”"]], [["\"There's no knowing what a man may come to in this world.\"", "“사람이 이 세상에서 어떻게 될지는 아무도 모르는 법이지.”"]], [["\"By George, no.", "“암, 그렇고말고."], ["But I'll tell you what, they'll find no change in me.", "하지만 내 말해 두겠는데, 나한테서는 아무 변화도 못 볼 걸세."], ["If I marry it will not be with the intention of giving up life.", "내가 장가를 든다 해도 그건 인생을 접겠다는 뜻으로 드는 게 아니야."], ["I say, old fellow, have you got a cigar here?\"", "그런데 이 사람아, 여기 시가 좀 있나?”"]], [["\"What, to smoke up here, do you mean?\"", "“뭐, 여기서 피우겠다는 말인가?”"]], [["\"Yes; why not?", "“그래, 안 될 게 뭔가?"], ["we're ever so far from the women.\"", "여자들한테서 이렇게나 멀리 떨어져 있는데.”"]], [["\"Not whilst I am occupier of this room.", "“내가 이 방을 쓰고 있는 동안은 안 되네."], ["Besides, it's time to dress for dinner.\"", "게다가 만찬 옷을 갈아입을 시각이야.”"]], [["\"Is it?", "“그런가?"], ["So it is, by George!", "정말 그렇군, 이런!"], ["But I mean to have a smoke first, I can tell you.", "그래도 난 먼저 한 대 피우고야 말겠네, 그건 장담하지."], ["So it's all a lie about your being engaged; eh?\"", "그러니까 자네가 약혼했다는 건 죄다 헛소리다, 이 말이지?”"]], [["\"As far as I know, it is,\"", "“내가 아는 한은 그렇다네.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["And then his friend left him.", "그러고서 그의 벗은 그를 두고 나갔다."]], [["What was he to do at once, now, this very day, as to his engagement?", "당장, 오늘 바로 이날, 그는 제 약혼을 두고 어떻게 해야 할 것인가?"], ["He had felt sure that the report of it would be carried to Courcy by Lady Julia De Guest, but he had not settled down upon any resolution as to what he would do in consequence.", "그 소문이 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트의 입을 통해 코시까지 실려 오리라는 것은 그도 확신하고 있었으나, 그에 따라 무엇을 할지에 대해서는 아무런 결심에도 이르지 못한 터였다."], ["It had not occurred to him that he would immediately be charged with the offence, and called upon to plead guilty or not guilty.", "도착하자마자 그 죄목으로 고발당하여 유죄냐 무죄냐를 답하라는 요구를 받으리라고는 생각해 보지 못했다."], ["He had never for a moment meditated any plea of not guilty, but he was aware of an aversion on his part to declare himself as engaged to Lilian Dale.", "무죄를 주장할 마음은 한순간도 품은 적이 없었으나, 릴리언 데일과 약혼했노라고 스스로 밝히는 일이 내키지 않는다는 것은 그도 알고 있었다."], ["It seemed that by doing so he would cut himself off at once from all pleasure at such houses as Courcy Castle; and, as he argued to himself, why should he not enjoy the little remnant of his bachelor life?", "그렇게 밝히면 코시 성 같은 집들에서 누리는 온갖 즐거움에서 당장 잘려 나가는 것만 같았다. 그리고 그가 스스로에게 따져 보았듯, 총각 생활의 이 짤막한 나머지를 즐기지 못할 까닭이 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["As to his denying his engagement to John De Courcy,--that was nothing.", "존 드 코시에게 약혼을 부인한 것으로 말하자면—그것은 아무것도 아니었다."], ["Any one would understand that he would be justified in concealing a fact concerning himself from such a one as he.", "그런 자에게 제 신상의 사실을 감추는 것이야 누구든 정당하다고 여길 터였다."], ["The denial repeated from John's mouth would amount to nothing,--even among John's own sisters.", "존의 입에서 되풀이되는 부인이란 아무 무게도 없을 것이다—존의 친누이들 사이에서조차도."], ["But now it was necessary that Crosbie should make up his mind as to what he would say when questioned by the ladies of the house.", "그러나 이제 크로즈비는 이 집 부인들에게서 질문을 받으면 무어라 말할지 마음을 정해야 했다."], ["If he were to deny the fact to them the denial would be very serious.", "그들에게 사실을 부인한다면 그 부인은 아주 심각한 일이 될 터였다."], ["And, indeed, was it possible that he should make such denial with Lady Julia opposite to him?", "게다가 레이디 줄리아를 맞은편에 두고 그런 부인을 한다는 것이 과연 될 법한 일이겠는가?"]], [["Make such a denial!", "그런 부인을 한다니!"], ["And was it the fact that he could wish to do so,--that he should think of such falsehood, and even meditate on the perpetration of such cowardice?", "그가 그러기를 바랄 수 있다는 것이, 그런 거짓을 생각하고 심지어 그런 비겁한 짓을 저지를 궁리까지 한다는 것이 사실이란 말인가?"], ["He had held that young girl to his heart on that very morning.", "바로 그날 아침에 그는 그 어린 아가씨를 제 가슴에 안았다."], ["He had sworn to her, and had also sworn to himself, that she should have no reason for distrusting him.", "그녀에게 맹세했고, 또 스스로에게도 맹세했다. 그녀가 그를 믿지 못할 까닭을 결코 주지 않겠노라고."], ["He had acknowledged most solemnly to himself that, whether for good or for ill, he was bound to her; and could it be that he was already calculating as to the practicability of disowning her?", "좋은 쪽으로든 나쁜 쪽으로든 자기가 그녀에게 매인 몸이라고 그는 더없이 엄숙하게 스스로 인정한 터였다. 그런데 벌써 그녀를 모른 체할 수 있을지를 셈하고 있다니, 있을 수 있는 일인가?"], ["In doing so must he not have told himself that he was a villain?", "그런 짓을 하면서 스스로에게 나는 악당이라고 말하지 않을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["But in truth he made no such calculation.", "그러나 실은 그는 그런 셈을 하고 있지 않았다."], ["His object was to banish the subject, if it were possible to do so; to think of some answer by which he might create a doubt.", "그의 목적은 가능하기만 하다면 그 화제를 몰아내는 것, 의심을 자아낼 만한 대답을 하나 찾아내는 것이었다."], ["It did not occur to him to tell the countess boldly that there was no truth whatever in the report, and that Miss Dale was nothing to him.", "백작부인에게 대놓고 그 소문에는 아무 근거도 없으며 데일 양은 저와 아무 사이도 아니라고 말할 생각은 그에게 떠오르지 않았다."], ["But might he not skilfully laugh off the subject, even in the presence of Lady Julia?", "그러나 레이디 줄리아가 있는 자리에서라도 그 화제를 솜씨 좋게 웃어넘길 수는 있지 않겠는가?"], ["Men who were engaged did so usually, and why should not he?", "약혼한 남자들이 흔히 그렇게 하는데, 그라고 그러지 못할 까닭이 무엇인가?"], ["It was generally thought that solicitude for the lady's feelings should prevent a man from talking openly of his own engagement.", "아가씨의 마음을 헤아리는 배려 때문에 남자가 제 약혼을 드러내 놓고 말하지 않는 것이 보통이라고들 여겼다."], ["Then he remembered the easy freedom with which his position had been discussed throughout the whole neighbourhood of Allington, and felt for the first time that the Dale family had been almost indelicate in their want of reticence.", "그러다가 그는 앨링턴 근방 온 동네에서 제 처지가 얼마나 스스럼없이 입에 오르내렸던가를 떠올렸고, 데일 집안이 입조심을 하지 않은 것이 거의 무례에 가까웠다는 생각을 난생처음으로 했다."], ["\"I suppose it was done to tie me the faster,\"", "“나를 더 단단히 붙들어 매려고 그런 게로군.”"], ["he said to himself, as he pulled out the ends of his cravat.", "그는 크라바트 끝자락을 잡아당기며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["\"What a fool I was to come here, or indeed to go anywhere, after settling myself as I have done.\"", "“여기 온 것이, 아니 그렇게 내 일을 정해 놓고 어디든 나다니는 것이 얼마나 어리석은 짓이었던가.”"], ["And then he went down into the drawing-room.", "그러고서 그는 응접실로 내려갔다."]], [["It was almost a relief to him when he found that he was not charged with his sin at once.", "당장 제 죄로 추궁당하지 않는다는 것을 알았을 때 그는 거의 마음이 놓였다."], ["He himself had been so full of the subject that he had expected to be attacked at the moment of his entrance.", "그 자신이 그 문제로 머리가 가득 차 있던 터라 들어서는 순간 공격을 받으리라 예상했던 것이다."], ["He was, however, greeted without any allusion to the matter.", "그러나 그는 그 일에 대한 아무런 언급도 없이 맞아들여졌다."], ["The countess, in her own quiet way, shook hands with him as though she had seen him only the day before.", "백작부인은 특유의 조용한 태도로, 마치 바로 어제 만났던 사람인 양 그와 악수했다."], ["The earl, who was seated in his arm-chair, asked some one, out loud, who the stranger was, and then, with two fingers put forth, muttered some apology for a welcome.", "안락의자에 앉아 있던 백작은 누군가에게 저 낯선 자가 누구냐고 큰 소리로 물었고, 그러고는 손가락 두 개를 내밀며 환영이랍시고 무어라 우물거렸다."], ["But Crosbie was quite up to that kind of thing. \"How do, my lord?\"", "그러나 크로즈비는 그런 것쯤은 충분히 감당할 줄 알았다."], ["he said, turning his face away to some one else as he spoke; and then he took no further notice of the master of the house.", "“안녕하십니까, 백작님?” 그는 이렇게 말하면서 얼굴을 돌려 다른 누군가를 보았고, 그 뒤로는 이 집 주인에게 더는 아는 체하지 않았다."], ["\"Not know him, indeed!\"", "“나를 모른다고!”"], ["Crippled though he was by his matrimonial bond, Crosbie felt that, at any rate as yet, he was the earl's equal in social importance.", "혼인의 굴레에 매여 몸이 불편해졌다고는 하나, 크로즈비는 적어도 아직까지는 사교계의 무게에서 저와 백작이 대등하다고 느꼈다."], ["After that, he found himself in the back part of the drawing-room, away from the elder people, standing with Lady Alexandrina, with Miss Gresham, a cousin of the De Courcys, and sundry other of the younger portion of the assembled community.", "그러고 나서 그는 응접실 안쪽, 나이 든 사람들에게서 떨어진 자리에 서 있는 자신을 발견했으니, 레이디 알렉산드리나와 드 코시 집안의 사촌인 그레셤 양, 그리고 그 자리에 모인 젊은 축 몇몇과 함께였다."]], [["\"So you have Lady Dumbello here?\"", "“레이디 덤벨로를 모셔 오셨군요?”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes; the dear creature!\"", "“네, 그럼요. 그 사랑스러운 분!”"], ["said Lady Margaretta.", "레이디 마거리타가 말했다."], ["\"It was so good of her to come, you know.\"", "“와 주신 것만 해도 얼마나 고마운 일인지 몰라요.”"]], [["\"She positively refused the Duchess of St. Bungay,\"", "“세인트 벙게이 공작부인은 딱 잘라 거절하셨답니다.”"], ["said Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."], ["\"I hope you perceive how good we've been to you in getting you to meet her.", "“저희가 그분을 만나게 해 드리려고 크로즈비 씨한테 얼마나 잘해 드린 건지 알아주셔야 해요."], ["People have actually asked to come.\"", "오게 해 달라고 청한 사람들이 실제로 있었으니까요.”"]], [["\"I am grateful; but, in truth, my gratitude has more to do with Courcy Castle and its habitual inmates, than with Lady Dumbello.", "“고맙습니다. 하지만 사실 제 고마움은 레이디 덤벨로보다는 코시 성과 이 성에 늘 머무는 분들과 더 관계가 깊습니다."], ["Is he here?\"", "그분 남편도 와 있습니까?”"]], [["\"Oh, yes! he's in the room somewhere.", "“그럼요, 이 방 어딘가에 계세요."], ["There he is, standing up by Lady Clandidlem.", "저기 계시네요, 레이디 클랜디들럼 곁에 서 계시잖아요."], ["He always stands in that way before dinner.", "만찬 전이면 늘 저렇게 서 계세요."], ["In the evening he sits down much after the same fashion.\"", "저녁에는 거의 같은 모양새로 앉아 계시고요.”"]], [["Crosbie had seen him on first entering the room, and had seen every individual in it.", "크로즈비는 방에 처음 들어설 때 이미 그를 보았고, 방 안의 사람 하나하나를 다 보아 두었다."], ["He knew better than to omit the duty of that scrutinizing glance; but it sounded well in his line not to have observed Lord Dumbello.", "그는 그렇게 훑어보는 눈길의 의무를 빠뜨릴 만큼 어수룩하지 않았다. 그러나 덤벨로 경을 미처 보지 못했다고 말해 두는 편이 그가 걷는 길에서는 듣기에 좋았다."]], [["\"And her ladyship is not down?\"", "“그러면 그 부인께서는 아직 안 내려오셨습니까?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"She is generally last,\"", "“대개 맨 마지막이세요.”"], ["said Lady Margaretta.", "레이디 마거리타가 말했다."]], [["\"And yet she has always three women to dress her,\"", "“그러면서도 옷 입혀 드리는 여자를 늘 셋이나 두고 계신답니다.”"], ["said Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."]], [["\"But when finished, what a success it is!\"", "“그렇지만 다 끝나고 나면 그 얼마나 훌륭한 성공입니까!”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Indeed it is!\"", "“정말 그래요!”"], ["said Margaretta, with energy.", "마거리타가 힘주어 말했다."], ["Then the door was opened, and Lady Dumbello entered the room.", "그때 문이 열리고 레이디 덤벨로가 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["There was immediately a commotion among them all.", "곧 모두가 술렁였다."], ["Even the gouty old lord shuffled up out of his chair, and tried, with a grin, to look sweet and pleasant.", "통풍에 걸린 늙은 백작조차 의자에서 몸을 끌어 올려 이를 드러내 웃으며 상냥하고 다정해 보이려 애썼다."], ["The countess came forward, looking very sweet and pleasant, making little complimentary speeches, to which the viscountess answered simply by a gracious smile.", "백작부인은 앞으로 나서며 아주 상냥하고 다정한 낯빛으로 자잘한 인사치레의 말을 늘어놓았고, 자작부인은 그저 우아한 미소 하나로만 답했다. 레이디 클랜디들럼은 살이 몹시 찌고 몸이 무거운데도 자작 곁을 떠나 일어서서 그 무리에 끼었다."], ["Lady Clandidlem, though she was very fat and heavy, left the viscount, and got up to join the group.", "이름 높은 독일 외교관 포츠뇌프 남작은 가슴 앞에 두 손을 모으고 깊이 허리를 숙였다."], ["Baron Potsneuf, a diplomatic German of great celebrity, crossed his hands upon his breast and made a low bow.", "십오 분째 잠자코 서 있던 아너러블 조지는 부인께서 바깥 공기가 꽤 쌀쌀하다고 느끼셨을 것 같다고 말을 건넸다."], ["The Honourable George, who had stood silent for the last quarter of an hour, suggested to her ladyship that she must have found the air rather cold; and the Ladies Margaretta and Alexandrina fluttered up with little complimentary speeches to their dear Lady Dumbello, hoping this and beseeching that, as though the \"Woman in White\" before them had been the dearest friend of their infancy.", "그리고 레이디 마거리타와 레이디 알렉산드리나는 자잘한 인사치레의 말을 재잘거리며 사랑하는 레이디 덤벨로에게 팔랑팔랑 다가가 이것을 바라고 저것을 간청했으니, 마치 눈앞의 그 「흰옷의 여인」이 어린 시절부터 가장 소중히 여겨 온 벗이기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["She was a woman in white, being dressed in white silk, with white lace over it, and with no other jewels upon her person than diamonds.", "그녀는 정말로 흰옷의 여인이었으니, 흰 비단옷을 입고 그 위에 흰 레이스를 걸쳤으며 몸에 지닌 보석이라고는 다이아몬드뿐이었다."], ["Very beautifully she was dressed; doing infinite credit, no doubt, to those three artists who had, between them, succeeded in turning her out of hand.", "아주 아름답게 차려입어, 그녀를 셋이 힘을 합쳐 빚어내는 데 성공한 그 세 예술가에게 더없는 영예를 안겨 준 것이 틀림없었다."], ["And her face, also, was beautiful, with a certain cold, inexpressive beauty.", "그리고 그 얼굴 또한 아름다웠으니, 차갑고 아무것도 드러내지 않는 어떤 아름다움이었다."], ["She walked up the room very slowly, smiling here and smiling there; but still with very faint smiles, and took the place which her hostess indicated to her.", "그녀는 아주 천천히 방을 걸어 올라오며 여기서 한 번 저기서 한 번 미소 지었으나 그 미소는 여전히 아주 희미했고, 안주인이 가리켜 주는 자리에 가 앉았다."], ["One word she said to the countess and two to the earl.", "백작부인에게 한마디, 백작에게 두 마디를 했다."], ["Beyond that she did not open her lips.", "그 밖에는 입을 열지 않았다."], ["All the homage paid to her she received as though it were clearly her due.", "저에게 바쳐지는 모든 경의를 그녀는 분명히 제 몫인 양 받아들였다."], ["She was not in the least embarrassed, nor did she show herself to be in the slightest degree ashamed of her own silence.", "조금도 어색해하지 않았고, 제 침묵을 조금이라도 부끄러워하는 기색을 보이지도 않았다."], ["She did not look like a fool, nor was she even taken for a fool; but she contributed nothing to society but her cold, hard beauty, her gait, and her dress.", "그녀는 바보처럼 보이지 않았고 바보로 여겨지지도 않았다. 그러나 그녀가 사교계에 내놓은 것이라고는 차갑고 단단한 아름다움과 걸음걸이와 옷차림뿐이었다."], ["We may say that she contributed enough, for society acknowledged itself to be deeply indebted to her.", "그것으로 충분히 내놓은 것이라고 우리는 말해도 좋겠으니, 사교계 스스로가 그녀에게 깊이 빚졌노라고 인정했기 때문이다."]], [["The only person in the room who did not move at Lady Dumbello's entrance was her husband.", "레이디 덤벨로가 들어설 때 방 안에서 유일하게 꿈쩍하지 않은 사람은 그 남편이었다."], ["But he remained unmoved from no want of enthusiasm.", "그러나 그가 움직이지 않은 것은 열의가 없어서가 아니었다."], ["A spark of pleasure actually beamed in his eye as he saw the triumphant entrance of his wife.", "아내가 의기양양하게 들어서는 것을 보았을 때 실로 기쁨의 불꽃 하나가 그의 눈에 반짝였다."], ["He felt that he had made a match that was becoming to him as a great nobleman, and that the world was acknowledging that he had done his duty.", "그는 대귀족인 저에게 걸맞은 혼인을 했으며 세상이 저를 두고 제 할 일을 했다고 인정해 주고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["And yet Lady Dumbello had been simply the daughter of a country parson, of a clergyman who had reached no higher rank than that of an archdeacon.", "그런데도 레이디 덤벨로는 그저 시골 목사의 딸이었을 뿐이니, 부주교보다 높은 자리에 올라 본 적 없는 성직자의 딸이었다."], ["\"How wonderfully well that woman has educated her,\"", "“그 여자가 딸을 참으로 놀랍도록 잘 길러 놓았지.”"], ["the countess said that evening in her dressing-room, to Margaretta.", "그날 저녁 백작부인이 제 화장방에서 마거리타에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["The woman alluded to was Mrs. Grantly, the wife of the parson and mother of Lady Dumbello.", "여기서 그 여자란 그랜틀리 부인, 곧 그 목사의 아내이자 레이디 덤벨로의 어머니를 가리키는 말이었다."]], [["The old earl was very cross because destiny and the table of precedence required him to take out Lady Clandidlem to dinner.", "늙은 백작은 운명과 서열표가 저더러 레이디 클랜디들럼을 만찬석으로 데려가라 요구했으므로 몹시 심사가 뒤틀렸다."], ["He almost insulted her, as she kindly endeavoured to assist him in his infirm step rather than to lean upon him.", "그녀가 그에게 기대기는커녕 성치 못한 그의 걸음을 친절히 거들려 하자 그는 그녀에게 거의 무례한 말을 하다시피 했다."]], [["\"Ugh!\"", "“으!"], ["he said, \"it's a bad arrangement that makes two old people like you and me be sent out together to help each other.\"", "당신하고 나같이 늙은 사람 둘을 서로 부축하라고 함께 내보내다니, 이건 못된 짜임새요.” 그가 말했다."]], [["\"Speak for yourself,\"", "“그건 백작님이나 그러시겠지요.”"], ["said her ladyship, with a laugh.", "부인이 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"I, at any rate, can get about without any assistance,\"--which, indeed, was true enough.", "“저로 말하면 아무 도움 없이도 얼마든지 돌아다닐 수 있답니다.” 그리고 그것은 실로 맞는 말이었다."]], [["\"It's well for you!\"", "“당신은 팔자가 좋구려!”"], ["growled the earl, as he got himself into his seat.", "백작이 제자리에 몸을 앉히며 으르렁거렸다."]], [["And after that he endeavoured to solace his pain by a flirtation with Lady Dumbello on his left.", "그러고 나서 그는 왼쪽에 앉은 레이디 덤벨로와 시시덕거리며 제 고통을 달래 보려 했다."], ["The earl's smiles and the earl's teeth, when he whispered naughty little nothings to pretty young women, were phenomena at which men might marvel.", "백작이 예쁜 젊은 여자들에게 짓궂은 실없는 소리를 속삭일 때 짓는 그 웃음과 그 드러난 이빨은 남자들이 보고도 놀랄 만한 진기한 구경거리였다."], ["Whatever those naughty nothings were on the present occasion, Lady Dumbello took them all with placidity, smiling graciously, but speaking hardly more than monosyllables.", "그 짓궂은 실없는 소리가 이번에는 무엇이었든 간에 레이디 덤벨로는 그것을 죄다 태연히 받아넘기며 우아하게 미소 지었으나, 입에서 나오는 말은 한두 마디 토막말이 고작이었다."]], [["Lady Alexandrina fell to Crosbie's lot, and he felt gratified that it was so.", "레이디 알렉산드리나가 크로즈비의 짝이 되었고, 그는 그렇게 된 것이 흡족했다."], ["It might be necessary for him, as a married man, to give up such acquaintances as the De Courcys, but he should like, if possible, to maintain a friendship with Lady Alexandrina.", "결혼한 몸이 되면 드 코시 집안 같은 사람들과의 교제는 포기해야 할지도 모르나, 될 수만 있다면 레이디 알렉산드리나와의 우정만은 지키고 싶었다."], ["What a friend Lady Alexandrina would be for Lily, if any such friendship were only possible!", "그런 우정이 조금이라도 가능하기만 하다면 레이디 알렉산드리나는 릴리에게 얼마나 좋은 벗이 되어 줄 것인가!"], ["What an advantage would such an alliance confer upon that dear little girl;--for, after all, though the dear little girl's attractions were very great, he could not but admit to himself that she wanted a something,--a way of holding herself and of speaking, which some people call style.", "그런 사귐이 그 사랑스러운 어린 아가씨에게 얼마나 큰 이득을 안겨 줄 것인가—사실 그 사랑스러운 어린 아가씨의 매력이 아무리 크다 해도, 그녀에게 무언가 모자란 것이, 곧 어떤 사람들이 품격이라 부르는 몸가짐과 말씨가 모자란다는 것을 그는 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Lily might certainly learn a great deal from Lady Alexandrina; and it was this conviction, no doubt, which made him so sedulous in pleasing that lady on the present occasion.", "릴리는 분명 레이디 알렉산드리나에게서 많은 것을 배울 수 있으리라. 그리고 이날 저녁 그가 그 부인의 마음에 들려고 그토록 부지런히 애쓴 것도 틀림없이 이런 확신 때문이었다."]], [["And she, as it seemed, was well inclined to be pleased.", "그리고 그녀 쪽도 보아하니 기꺼이 흡족해할 마음이 있었다."], ["She said no word to him during dinner about Lily; and yet she spoke about the Dales, and about Allington, showing that she knew in what quarters he had been staying, and then she alluded to their last parties in London,--those occasions on which, as Crosbie now remembered, the intercourse between them had almost been tender.", "만찬 내내 그녀는 릴리에 대해 그에게 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그러면서도 데일 집안과 앨링턴 이야기를 꺼내어 그가 어느 고장에 머물다 왔는지 알고 있음을 드러내 보였고, 그러고는 런던에서 마지막으로 함께한 모임들을 넌지시 입에 올렸으니, 크로즈비가 이제 와 떠올리기로 그 자리들에서 두 사람 사이는 거의 은근한 데까지 갔던 것이다."], ["It was manifest to him that at any rate she did not wish to quarrel with him.", "적어도 그녀가 그와 다툴 마음이 없다는 것은 그에게 분명했다."], ["It was manifest, also, that she had some little hesitation in speaking to him about his engagement.", "또한 그의 약혼을 두고 그에게 말을 꺼내기를 그녀가 조금 망설이고 있다는 것도 분명했다."], ["He did not for a moment doubt that she was aware of it.", "그녀가 그 일을 알고 있다는 것을 그는 한순간도 의심하지 않았다."], ["And in this way matters went on between them till the ladies left the room.", "그리고 이런 식으로 두 사람 사이에 시간이 흘러 마침내 부인들이 식당을 떠났다."]], [["\"So you're going to be married, too,\"", "“자네도 장가를 든다며?”"], ["said the Honourable George, by whose side Crosbie found himself seated when the ladies were gone.", "부인들이 나가고 나자 크로즈비는 아너러블 조지 곁에 앉아 있는 자신을 발견했는데, 그 조지가 이렇게 말했다."], ["Crosbie was employing himself upon a walnut, and did not find it necessary to make any answer.", "크로즈비는 호두 하나를 까느라 여념이 없었고, 굳이 대답할 필요를 느끼지 못했다."]], [["\"It's the best thing a fellow can do,\"", "“사내가 할 수 있는 일 가운데 그게 제일 낫지.”"], ["continued George; \"that is, if he has been careful to look to the main chance,--if he hasn't been caught napping, you know.", "조지가 말을 이었다. “물론 큰 몫을 챙기는 데 빈틈이 없었다면 말일세—졸다가 덜컥 잡혀 버린 게 아니라면 말이지."], ["It doesn't do for a man to go hanging on by nothing till he finds himself an old man.\"", "아무것도 없이 매달려 지내다가 어느새 늙은이가 되어 있는 건 사내가 할 짓이 아니거든.”"]], [["\"You've feathered your own nest, at any rate.\"", "“어쨌든 자네는 제 둥지를 두둑이 깔아 놓았군.”"]], [["\"Yes; I've got something in the scramble, and I mean to keep it.", "“그럼, 아귀다툼 속에서 나도 뭔가 하나 챙겼고, 그걸 지킬 작정일세."], ["Where will John be when the governor goes off the hooks?", "아버지가 세상을 뜨고 나면 존은 어디 가 있겠나?"], ["Porlock wouldn't give him a bit of bread and cheese and a glass of beer to save his life;--that is to say, not if he wanted it.\"", "폴록은 목숨이 걸린대도 그에게 빵 한 조각에 치즈 한 점, 맥주 한 잔 내주지 않을 걸세—말하자면 정말로 그것이 아쉬운 처지라면 안 준다는 뜻이지.”"]], [["\"I'm told your elder brother is going to be married.\"", "“자네 형이 장가를 든다는 얘길 들었네만.”"]], [["\"You've heard that from John.", "“그건 존한테서 들었겠지."], ["He's spreading that about everywhere to take a rise out of me.", "그놈이 나를 놀려 먹으려고 그 소릴 사방에 퍼뜨리고 다닌다네."], ["I don't believe a word of it.", "난 한마디도 믿지 않아."], ["Porlock never was a marrying man;--and, what's more, from all I hear, I don't think he'll live long.\"", "폴록은 애초에 장가들 위인이 아니었어—게다가 내가 듣는 이야기로 보아 그리 오래 살 것 같지도 않고.”"]], [["In this way Crosbie escaped from his own difficulty; and when he rose from the dinner-table had not as yet been driven to confess anything to his own discredit.", "이런 식으로 크로즈비는 제 곤경에서 벗어났다. 그리고 만찬 식탁에서 일어설 때까지도 그는 제 이름에 흠이 될 만한 것을 무엇 하나 고백하도록 내몰리지 않았다."]], [["But the evening was not yet over.", "그러나 저녁은 아직 끝나지 않았다."], ["When he returned to the drawing-room he endeavoured to avoid any conversation with the countess herself, believing that the attack would more probably come from her than from her daughter.", "응접실로 돌아갔을 때 그는 백작부인과 이야기를 나누는 일만은 피하려 애썼으니, 공격은 딸 쪽보다 어머니 쪽에서 올 공산이 크다고 믿었기 때문이다."], ["He, therefore, got into conversation first with one and then with another of the girls, till at last he found himself again alone with Alexandrina.", "그리하여 그는 아가씨들과 차례로 하나씩 이야기를 나누다가 마침내 다시 알렉산드리나와 단둘이 있는 자신을 발견했다."]], [["\"Mr. Crosbie,\"", "“크로즈비 씨.”"], ["she said, in a low voice, as they were standing together over one of the distant tables, with their backs to the rest of the company, \"I want you to tell me something about Miss Lilian Dale.\"", "두 사람이 저만치 떨어진 탁자 앞에 나머지 사람들에게 등을 돌린 채 나란히 서 있을 때 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “릴리언 데일 양에 대해 뭐라도 좀 얘기해 주셨으면 해요.”"]], [["\"About Miss Lilian Dale!\"", "“릴리언 데일 양에 대해서라니요!”"], ["he said, repeating her words.", "그가 그녀의 말을 되뇌며 말했다."]], [["\"Is she very pretty?\"", "“아주 예쁜가요?”"]], [["\"Yes; she certainly is pretty.\"", "“네, 예쁜 것은 틀림없습니다.”"]], [["\"And very nice, and attractive, and clever,--and all that is delightful?", "“그리고 아주 점잖고, 매력 있고, 영리하고—즐겁지 않은 데라고는 하나도 없고요?"], ["Is she perfect?\"", "완벽한가요?”"]], [["\"She is very attractive,\"", "“아주 매력 있는 아가씨입니다.”"], ["said he; \"but I don't think she's perfect.\"", "그가 말했다. “하지만 완벽하다고는 생각하지 않습니다.”"]], [["\"And what are her faults?\"", "“그러면 그 아가씨의 흠은 무엇이지요?”"]], [["\"That question is hardly fair, is it?", "“그 물음은 공평하지 않군요, 그렇지 않습니까?"], ["Suppose any one were to ask me what were your faults, do you think I should answer the question?\"", "누가 저더러 당신의 흠은 무엇이냐고 묻는다면, 제가 그 물음에 대답할 것 같습니까?”"]], [["\"I am quite sure you would, and make a very long list of them, too.", "“분명히 대답하실 거예요. 그것도 아주 긴 목록을 만들어서요."], ["But as to Miss Dale, you ought to think her perfect.", "하지만 데일 양으로 말하면, 크로즈비 씨는 그분을 완벽하다고 여기셔야 마땅해요."], ["If a gentleman were engaged to me, I should expect him to swear before all the world that I was the very pink of perfection.\"", "어떤 신사가 저와 약혼했다면 저는 그이가 온 세상 앞에서 제가 완벽함의 정수라고 맹세해 주기를 바랄 거예요.”"]], [["\"But supposing the gentleman were not engaged to you?\"", "“그렇지만 그 신사가 당신과 약혼한 사이가 아니라면요?”"]], [["\"That would be a different thing.\"", "“그건 다른 이야기지요.”"]], [["\"I am not engaged to you,\"", "“저는 당신과 약혼한 사이가 아닙니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"Such happiness and such honour are, I fear, very far beyond my reach.", "“그런 행복과 그런 영예는 아무래도 제 손이 닿기에는 너무 먼 곳에 있겠지요."], ["But, nevertheless, I am prepared to testify as to your perfection anywhere.\"", "그렇지만 그럼에도 저는 어디서든 당신의 완벽함을 증언할 준비가 되어 있습니다.”"]], [["\"And what would Miss Dale say?\"", "“그러면 데일 양은 뭐라고 할까요?”"]], [["\"Allow me to assure you that such opinions as I may choose to express of my friends will be my own opinions, and not depend on those of any one else.\"", "“제가 제 벗들에 대해 말하기로 한 의견은 어디까지나 저 자신의 의견이지 다른 누구의 의견에 매인 것이 아님을 분명히 말씀드리겠습니다.”"]], [["\"And you think, then, that you are not bound to be enslaved as yet?", "“그러면 아직은 노예 신세가 될 일이 없다고 생각하시는군요?"], ["How many more months of such freedom are you to enjoy?\"", "그런 자유를 앞으로 몇 달이나 더 누리실 건가요?”"]], [["Crosbie remained silent for a minute before he answered, and then he spoke in a serious voice.", "크로즈비는 대답하기 전에 잠시 잠자코 있다가 진지한 목소리로 말했다. “레이디 알렉산드리나.”"], ["\"Lady Alexandrina,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"I would beg from you a great favour.\"", "“당신께 큰 부탁 하나를 드리고 싶습니다.”"]], [["\"What is the favour, Mr. Crosbie?\"", "“무슨 부탁이지요, 크로즈비 씨?”"]], [["\"I am quite in earnest.", "“저는 아주 진지합니다."], ["Will you be good enough, kind enough, enough my friend, not to connect my name again with that of Miss Dale while I am here?\"", "제가 여기 머무는 동안 제 이름을 데일 양의 이름과 다시는 엮지 말아 주실 만큼 너그럽고, 친절하고, 제 벗이 되어 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["\"Has there been a quarrel?\"", "“다투기라도 하셨나요?”"]], [["\"No; there has been no quarrel.", "“아닙니다. 다툰 일은 없습니다."], ["I cannot explain to you now why I make this request; but to you I will explain it before I go.\"", "제가 왜 이런 부탁을 드리는지 지금은 설명해 드릴 수 없습니다. 그러나 당신께는 떠나기 전에 설명해 드리겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Explain it to me!\"", "“저한테 설명을 해 주시겠다고요!”"]], [["\"I have regarded you as more than an acquaintance,--as a friend.", "“저는 당신을 그저 아는 사이 이상으로—벗으로 여겨 왔습니다."], ["In days now past there were moments when I was almost rash enough to hope that I might have said even more than that.", "이제는 지나간 날들 가운데는 그보다 더한 것을 말해도 좋으리라 바랄 만큼 제가 거의 무모해졌던 순간들도 있었습니다."], ["I confess that I had no warrant for such hopes, but I believe that I may still look on you as a friend?\"", "그런 바람을 품을 근거가 저에게 없었음은 인정합니다. 그러나 지금도 여전히 당신을 벗으로 여겨도 좋다고 저는 믿습니다만?”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, certainly,\"", "“오, 그럼요, 물론이지요.”"], ["said Alexandrina, in a very low voice, and with a certain amount of tenderness in her tone.", "알렉산드리나가 아주 낮은 목소리로, 그 어조에 얼마간 다정함을 담아 말했다."], ["\"I have always regarded you as a friend.\"", "“저는 늘 크로즈비 씨를 벗으로 여겨 왔어요.”"]], [["\"And therefore I venture to make the request.", "“그래서 감히 이런 부탁을 드리는 것입니다."], ["The subject is not one on which I can speak openly, without regret, at the present moment.", "그 일은 지금 이 순간 제가 후회 없이 터놓고 말할 수 있는 화제가 아닙니다."], ["But to you, at least, I promise that I will explain it all before I leave Courcy.\"", "그러나 적어도 당신께만은 코시를 떠나기 전에 모두 설명하겠노라고 약속드립니다.”"]], [["He at any rate succeeded in mystifying Lady Alexandrina.", "어쨌든 그는 레이디 알렉산드리나를 아리송하게 만드는 데 성공했다."], ["\"I don't believe he is engaged a bit,\"", "“난 그이가 약혼했다고는 눈곱만큼도 믿지 않아요.”"], ["she said to Lady Amelia Gazebee that night.", "그날 밤 그녀가 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비에게 말했다."]], [["\"Nonsense, my dear.", "“말도 안 되는 소리라오, 얘야."], ["Lady Julia wouldn't speak of it in that certain way if she didn't know.", "레이디 줄리아가 알지도 못하면서 그렇게 딱 잘라 말할 리가 없지."], ["Of course he doesn't wish to have it talked about.\"", "물론 그 사람이야 그 얘기가 오르내리는 걸 바라지 않겠지만.”"]], [["\"If ever he has been engaged to her, he has broken it off again,\"", "“그이가 그 아가씨와 약혼한 적이 있다 해도 이미 파해 버린 거예요.”"], ["said Lady Alexandrina.", "레이디 알렉산드리나가 말했다."]], [["\"I dare say he will, my dear, if you give him encouragement,\"", "“네가 부추겨 주기만 하면 아마 그렇게 하겠지, 얘야.”"], ["said the married sister, with great sisterly good-nature.", "시집간 언니가 자매다운 큰 호의로 말했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVIII. LILY DALE'S FIRST LOVE-LETTER.", "# 제18장. 릴리 데일의 첫 연애편지"]], [["Crosbie was rather proud of himself when he went to bed.", "크로즈비는 잠자리에 들 때 제법 자기 자신이 자랑스러웠다."], ["He had succeeded in baffling the charge made against him, without saying anything as to which his conscience need condemn him.", "그는 자기에게 겨누어진 추궁을 용케 따돌렸으면서도, 양심이 스스로를 나무랄 만한 말은 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["So, at least, he then told himself.", "적어도 그때 그는 스스로에게 그렇게 일러 두었다."], ["The impression left by what he had said would be that there had been some question of an engagement between him and Lilian Dale, but that nothing at this moment was absolutely fixed.", "그가 한 말이 남길 인상은, 그와 릴리언 데일 사이에 약혼 비슷한 이야기가 오간 적은 있으나 이 순간 확정된 것은 아무것도 없다는 정도이리라."], ["But in the morning his conscience was not quite so clear.", "그러나 아침이 되자 그의 양심은 그리 깨끗하지가 않았다."], ["What would Lily think and say if she knew it all?", "릴리가 그 모든 것을 알게 된다면 무슨 생각을 하고 무슨 말을 할까?"], ["Could he dare to tell her, or to tell any one the real state of his mind?", "그가 감히 그녀에게, 아니 그 누구에게라도 제 마음의 진짜 상태를 털어놓을 수 있을까?"]], [["As he lay in bed, knowing that an hour remained to him before he need encounter the perils of his tub, he felt that he hated Courcy Castle and its inmates.", "침대에 누운 채, 목욕통의 시련과 맞닥뜨리기까지 아직 한 시간이 남았음을 알면서, 그는 코시 성과 그 안에 사는 사람들이 밉다고 느꼈다."], ["Who was there, among them all, that was comparable to Mrs. Dale and her daughters?", "그들 가운데 데일 부인과 그 딸들에 견줄 만한 사람이 누가 있단 말인가?"], ["He detested both George and John.", "그는 조지도 존도 질색이었다."], ["He loathed the earl.", "백작은 진저리가 났다."], ["As to the countess herself, he was perfectly indifferent, regarding her as a woman whom it was well to know, but as one only to be known as the mistress of Courcy Castle and a house in London.", "백작부인으로 말하자면 그는 완전히 무심했으니, 알아 두어서 나쁠 것 없는 여자이되 코시 성과 런던 저택의 안주인으로서만 알아 둘 여자로 여겼다. 딸들로 말하자면, 그는 때때로 그들 모두를 비웃어 왔다."], ["As to the daughters, he had ridiculed them all from time to time--even Alexandrina, whom he now professed to love.", "지금 사랑한다고 공언하는 알렉산드리나까지도. 어쩌면 어떤 식으로든 그녀에게 약한 애정 같은 것이 있었는지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps in some sort of way he had a weak fondness for her;--but it was a fondness that had never touched his heart.", "그러나 그것은 한 번도 그의 가슴에 닿아 본 적 없는 애정이었다. 그는 그 모든 것의 값어치를 정확히 잴 수 있었다."], ["He could measure the whole thing at its worth,--Courcy Castle with its privileges, Lady Dumbello, Lady Clandidlem, and the whole of it.", "온갖 특권을 거느린 코시 성, 레이디 덤벨로, 레이디 클랜디들럼, 그리고 그 전부를. 그는 알고 있었다."], ["He knew that he had been happier on that lawn at Allington, and more contented with himself, than ever he had been even under Lady Hartletop's splendid roof in Shropshire.", "슈롭셔에 있는 레이디 하틀탑의 으리으리한 지붕 아래에서보다도 앨링턴의 그 잔디밭에서 자기가 더 행복했고 스스로에게 더 흡족했다는 것을."], ["Lady Dumbello was satisfied with these things, even in the inmost recesses of her soul; but he was not a male Lady Dumbello.", "레이디 덤벨로는 영혼의 가장 깊은 구석까지도 이런 것들로 만족하는 사람이었다. 그러나 그는 남자 레이디 덤벨로가 아니었다."], ["He knew that there was something better, and that that something was within his reach.", "그는 더 나은 무엇이 있다는 것을, 그리고 그 무엇이 제 손 닿는 곳에 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["But, nevertheless, the air of Courcy was too much for him.", "그러나 그럼에도 코시의 공기는 그에게 너무 벅찼다."], ["In arguing the matter with himself he regarded himself as one infected with a leprosy from which there could be no recovery, and who should, therefore, make his whole life suitable to the circumstances of that leprosy.", "그 문제를 놓고 스스로와 따지는 동안 그는 자신을 두고, 나을 길이 없는 나병에 걸린 사람, 그러니 제 삶 전체를 그 나병의 형편에 맞추어야 할 사람이라고 여겼다."], ["It was of no use for him to tell himself that the Small House at Allington was better than Courcy Castle.", "앨링턴의 작은 집이 코시 성보다 낫다고 스스로에게 일러 봐야 소용이 없었다."], ["Satan knew that heaven was better than hell; but he found himself to be fitter for the latter place.", "사탄도 천국이 지옥보다 낫다는 것쯤은 알았다. 다만 제가 뒤쪽 자리에 더 어울린다는 것을 알았을 뿐이다."], ["Crosbie ridiculed Lady Dumbello, even there among her friends, with all the cutting words that his wit could find; but, nevertheless, the privilege of staying in the same house with her was dear to him.", "크로즈비는 레이디 덤벨로를, 그것도 그녀의 벗들 틈에 앉아서, 제 재치가 찾아낼 수 있는 온갖 신랄한 말로 비웃었다. 그러면서도 그녀와 같은 집에 묵는 특권은 그에게 소중했다."], ["It was the line of life into which he had fallen, and he confessed inwardly that the struggle to extricate himself would be too much for him.", "그것이 그가 떨어져 들어온 삶의 길이었고, 거기서 빠져나오려는 몸부림은 제 힘에 부치리라고 그는 속으로 자인했다."], ["All that had troubled him while he was yet at Allington, but it overwhelmed him almost with dismay beneath the hangings of Courcy Castle.", "그 모든 것이 아직 앨링턴에 있을 때에도 그를 괴롭혔지만, 코시 성의 휘장 아래에서는 거의 낭패감이 될 만큼 그를 짓눌렀다."]], [["Had he not better run from the place at once?", "당장 이곳에서 달아나는 편이 낫지 않을까?"], ["He had almost acknowledged to himself that he repented his engagement with Lilian Dale, but he still was resolved that he would fulfil it.", "그는 릴리언 데일과의 약혼을 후회하고 있다고 거의 스스로 인정했으면서도, 그것을 지키겠다는 결심만은 여전했다."], ["He was bound in honour to marry \"that little girl,\" and he looked sternly up at the drapery over his head, as he assured himself that he was a man of honour.", "그는 명예를 걸고 “그 어린 아가씨”와 결혼할 의무가 있었고, 자신이 명예를 아는 사내라고 스스로 다짐하며 머리 위의 휘장을 준엄하게 올려다보았다."], ["Yes; he would sacrifice himself.", "그렇다, 그는 자신을 희생하리라."], ["As he had been induced to pledge his word, he would not go back from it.", "말을 주도록 이끌려 간 이상, 그 말을 물리지는 않으리라."], ["He was too much of a man for that!", "그는 그러기에는 너무나 사내다웠다!"]], [["But had he not been wrong to refuse the result of Lily's wisdom when she told him in the field that it would be better for them to part?", "그러나 들판에서 릴리가 헤어지는 편이 서로에게 나으리라고 말했을 때, 그 지혜의 결론을 물리친 것은 잘못이 아니었을까?"], ["He did not tell himself that he had refused her offer merely because he had not the courage to accept it on the spur of the moment.", "그 자리에서 그것을 받아들일 용기가 없었기 때문에 그녀의 제안을 물리친 것이라고, 그는 스스로에게 말하지 않았다. 아니다."], ["No. \"He had been too good to the poor girl to take her at her word.\"", "“그는 그 가엾은 아가씨에게 너무나 잘해 주었기에 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받을 수 없었던 것이다.”"], ["It was thus he argued on the matter within his own breast.", "그가 제 가슴속에서 이 문제를 따진 방식이 그러했다."], ["He had been too true to her; and now the effect would be that they would both be unhappy for life!", "그는 그녀에게 너무 진실했다. 그리고 이제 그 결과로 두 사람은 평생 불행해지리라!"], ["He could not live in content with a family upon a small income.", "그는 적은 수입으로 식솔을 거느리고 만족스럽게 살 수 없었다."], ["He was well aware of that.", "그것을 그는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["No one could be harder upon him in that matter than was he himself.", "그 점에 관해서라면 그 자신보다 더 그를 매섭게 몰아붙일 사람은 없었다."], ["But it was too late now to remedy the ill effects of an early education.", "그러나 어린 시절 교육이 남긴 나쁜 결과를 이제 와 고치기에는 너무 늦었다."]], [["It was thus that he debated the matter as he lay in bed,--contradicting one argument by another over and over again; but still in all of them teaching himself to think that this engagement of his was a misfortune.", "침대에 누운 채 그가 이 문제를 따진 방식이 그러했다. 이 논리를 저 논리로 몇 번이고 되받아 뒤엎으면서도, 그 어느 논리로든 결국은 이 약혼이 불운이라고 스스로 믿게끔 가르치면서."], ["Poor Lily!", "가엾은 릴리!"], ["Her last words to him had conveyed an assurance that she would never distrust him.", "그에게 남긴 그녀의 마지막 말은 결코 그를 의심하지 않겠다는 다짐이었다."], ["And she also, as she lay wakeful in her bed on this the first morning of his absence, thought much of their mutual vows.", "그리고 그녀 역시, 그가 떠난 첫 아침 침대에 누워 잠들지 못한 채, 두 사람이 주고받은 맹세를 오래 생각했다."], ["How true she would be to them!", "그녀는 그 맹세에 얼마나 참되려 했던가!"], ["How she would be his wife with all her heart and spirit!", "온 마음과 온 정신을 다해 그의 아내가 되려 했던가! 그를 사랑하기만 하려는 것이 아니었다."], ["It was not only that she would love him;--but in her love she would serve him to her utmost; serve him as regarded this world, and if possible as regarded the next.", "그 사랑 속에서 그녀는 힘 닿는 데까지 그를 섬기려 했다. 이 세상 일에서 그를 섬기고, 할 수만 있다면 저세상 일에서도 그를 섬기려 했다."]], [["\"Bell,\"", "“언니,”"], ["she said, \"I wish you were going to be married too.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “언니도 시집을 갔으면 좋겠어.”"]], [["\"Thank'ye, dear,\"", "“고맙구나, 얘.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"Perhaps I shall some day.\"", "“언젠가는 나도 그리되겠지.”"]], [["\"Ah; but I'm not joking.", "“아, 그런데 나 농담하는 거 아니야."], ["It seems such a serious thing.", "이건 참 엄청난 일 같아."], ["And I can't expect you to talk to me about it now as you would if you were in the same position yourself.", "그리고 언니가 나랑 같은 처지도 아닌데, 지금 나한테 그 얘기를 그때처럼 해 주기를 바랄 수도 없고."], ["Do you think I shall make him happy?\"", "언니는 내가 그이를 행복하게 해 줄 것 같아?”"]], [["\"Yes, I do, certainly.\"", "“그럼, 정말 그럴 것 같구나.”"]], [["\"Happier than he would be with any one else that he might meet?", "“그이가 만날 수 있는 다른 어떤 사람과 있을 때보다도 더?"], ["I dare not think that.", "그렇게까지는 감히 못 믿겠어."], ["I think I could give him up to-morrow, if I could see any one that would suit him better.\"", "그이한테 더 어울릴 사람이 눈에 보이기만 한다면, 나는 내일이라도 그이를 놓아줄 수 있을 것 같아.”"], ["What would Lily have said had she been made acquainted with all the fascinations of Lady Alexandrina De Courcy?", "만일 릴리가 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시의 온갖 매력을 알게 되었더라면 뭐라고 말했을까?"]], [["The countess was very civil to him, saying nothing about his engagement, but still talking to him a good deal about his sojourn at Allington.", "백작부인은 그에게 무척 상냥했다. 약혼에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않으면서도, 앨링턴에 머물렀던 이야기는 꽤 많이 꺼냈다."], ["Crosbie was a pleasant man for ladies in a large house.", "크로즈비는 큰 저택의 부인들에게 반가운 남자였다."], ["Though a sportsman, he was not so keen a sportsman as to be always out with the gamekeepers.", "사냥을 즐기기는 하되 늘 사냥터지기를 따라 나가 있을 만큼 열심인 사냥꾼은 아니었다."], ["Though a politician, he did not sacrifice his mornings to the perusal of blue-books or the preparation of party tactics.", "정치를 하기는 하되 청서(靑書)를 들여다보거나 당의 전술을 짜느라 오전을 바치지는 않았다."], ["Though a reading man, he did not devote himself to study.", "책을 읽기는 하되 연구에 몰두하지는 않았다."], ["Though a horseman, he was not often to be found in the stables.", "말을 타기는 하되 마구간에서 자주 눈에 띄지는 않았다."], ["He could supply conversation when it was wanted, and could take himself out of the way when his presence among the women was not needed.", "이야기가 필요할 때면 이야기를 대 줄 줄 알았고, 부인들 틈에 제가 있을 필요가 없을 때면 슬며시 자리를 비켜 줄 줄도 알았다."], ["Between breakfast and lunch on the day following his arrival he talked a good deal to the countess, and made himself very agreeable.", "도착한 이튿날 아침 식사와 점심 사이에 그는 백작부인과 꽤 오래 이야기를 나누었고, 아주 마음에 드는 사람 노릇을 했다."], ["She continued to ridicule him gently for his prolonged stay among so primitive and rural a tribe of people as the Dales, and he bore her little sarcasm with the utmost good-humour.", "부인은 데일 집안처럼 촌스럽고 시골티 나는 족속들 틈에서 그리 오래 머물렀다고 그를 계속 부드럽게 놀려 댔고, 그는 그 자잘한 빈정거림을 더없이 좋은 낯으로 받아 넘겼다."]], [["\"Six weeks at Allington without a move!", "“꼼짝도 않고 앨링턴에서 여섯 주라니!"], ["Why, Mr. Crosbie, you must have felt yourself to be growing there.\"", "저런, 크로즈비 씨, 거기서 뿌리가 돋는 기분이었겠구려.”"]], [["\"So I did--like an ancient tree.", "“그랬지요. 늙은 나무처럼 말입니다."], ["Indeed, I was so rooted that I could hardly get away.\"", "정말이지 어찌나 뿌리가 박혔는지 좀처럼 빠져나올 수가 없었습니다.”"]], [["\"Was the house full of people all the time?\"", "“그동안 줄곧 그 집에 사람이 가득했소?”"]], [["\"There was nobody there but Bernard Dale, Lady Julia's nephew.\"", "“레이디 줄리아의 조카인 버나드 데일 말고는 아무도 없었습니다.”"]], [["\"Quite a case of Damon and Pythias.", "“그야말로 다몬과 피티아스로군."], ["Fancy your going down to the shades of Allington to enjoy the uninterrupted pleasures of friendship for six weeks.\"", "여섯 주 내내 방해라고는 없는 우정의 즐거움을 누리겠다고 앨링턴의 그늘까지 내려가다니, 생각 좀 해 보구려.”"]], [["\"Friendship and the partridges.\"", "“우정과 자고새지요.”"]], [["\"There was nothing else, then?\"", "“그럼 그 밖에는 아무것도 없었소?”"]], [["\"Indeed there was.", "“아니, 있었습니다."], ["There was a widow with two very nice daughters, living, not exactly in the same house, but on the same grounds.\"", "아주 참한 딸 둘을 둔 미망인이 하나 있었지요. 꼭 같은 집은 아니고, 같은 울안에 살고 있었습니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, indeed.", "“오, 그러셨군."], ["That makes such a difference; doesn't it?", "그러면 사정이 아주 달라지지요, 안 그렇소?"], ["You are not a man to bear much privation on the score of partridges, nor a great deal, I imagine, for friendship.", "그대는 자고새 때문에 궁핍을 견딜 사람도 아니고, 우정 때문이라 해도 그리 많이 견딜 사람은 아닐 성싶소."], ["But when you talk of pretty girls--\"", "하지만 예쁜 아가씨들 이야기가 나온다면—”"]], [["\"It makes a difference, doesn't it?\"", "“사정이 달라지지요, 그렇지 않습니까?”"]], [["\"A very great difference.", "“아주 크게 달라지지요."], ["I think I have heard of that Mrs. Dale before.", "그 데일 부인이라는 사람 이야기는 전에도 들은 것 같소."], ["And so her girls are nice?\"", "그래, 그 딸들이 참하다고?”"]], [["\"Very nice indeed.\"", "“정말이지 아주 참합니다.”"]], [["\"Play croquet, I suppose, and eat syllabub on the lawn?", "“크로케를 하고, 잔디밭에서 실러법을 먹고 그러겠지요?"], ["But, really, didn't you get very tired of it?\"", "그런데 참말이지, 지겨워지지는 않던가요?”"]], [["\"Oh dear, no.", "“저런, 전혀요."], ["I was happy as the day was long.\"", "해가 긴 만큼 내내 행복했습니다.”"]], [["\"Going about with a crook, I suppose?\"", "“양치기 지팡이라도 짚고 돌아다녔겠구려?”"]], [["\"Not exactly a live crook; but doing all that kind of thing.", "“진짜 지팡이까지는 아니었습니다만, 그런 종류의 일은 다 했지요."], ["I learned a great deal about pigs.\"", "돼지에 관해서 아주 많이 배웠습니다.”"]], [["\"Under the guidance of Miss Dale?\"", "“데일 양의 지도 아래서?”"]], [["\"Yes; under the guidance of Miss Dale.\"", "“예, 데일 양의 지도 아래서요.”"]], [["\"I'm sure one is very much obliged to you for tearing yourself away from such charms, and coming to such unromantic people as we are.", "“그런 매력에서 몸을 떼어 내어 우리처럼 낭만이라고는 없는 사람들에게 와 주셨으니 참으로 고마운 일이오."], ["But I fancy men always do that sort of thing once or twice in their lives,--and then they talk of their souvenirs.", "하지만 사내들이란 평생에 한두 번은 꼭 그런 짓을 하는 법이지요. 그러고는 제 추억거리를 이야기하고 말이오."], ["I suppose it won't go beyond a souvenir with you.\"", "그대에게도 추억거리 이상은 되지 않으리라 짐작하오만.”"]], [["This was a direct question, but still admitted of a fencing answer.", "이것은 대놓고 던진 물음이었으나, 그래도 슬쩍 받아넘길 여지는 있었다."], ["\"It has, at any rate, given me one,\"", "“어쨌든 하나는 남겨 주었지요.”"], ["said he, \"which will last me my life!\"", "그가 말했다. “평생 가는 것으로 말입니다!”"]], [["The countess was quite contented.", "백작부인은 아주 흡족했다."], ["That Lady Julia's statement was altogether true she had never for a moment doubted.", "레이디 줄리아의 말이 전부 사실이라는 것을 그녀는 한순간도 의심한 적이 없었다."], ["That Crosbie should become engaged to a young lady in the country, whereas he had shown signs of being in love with her daughter in London, was not at all wonderful.", "크로즈비가 런던에서 제 딸에게 반한 기색을 보였으면서 시골 아가씨와 약혼했다는 것은 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니었다."], ["Nor, in her eyes, did such practice amount to any great sin.", "그녀가 보기에 그런 행실이 큰 죄가 되는 것도 아니었다."], ["Men did so daily, and girls were prepared for their so doing.", "사내들은 날마다 그런 짓을 했고, 아가씨들은 그러려니 하고 대비하는 법이었다."], ["A man in her eyes was not to be regarded as safe from attack because he was engaged.", "그녀가 보기에 사내란 약혼했다고 해서 공격에서 안전하다고 여길 대상이 아니었다."], ["Let the young lady who took upon herself to own him have an eye to that.", "그를 제 것이라 떠맡은 아가씨가 알아서 그 점을 살필 일이었다."], ["When she looked back on the past careers of her own flock, she had to reckon more than one such disappointment for her own daughters.", "제 새끼들이 걸어온 지난 이력을 돌이켜 보면, 딸들이 겪은 그런 낭패가 한 번으로 그치지 않았다."], ["Others besides Alexandrina had been so treated.", "알렉산드리나 말고 다른 딸들도 그런 꼴을 당했다."], ["Lady De Courcy had had her grand hopes respecting her girls, and after them moderate hopes, and again after them bitter disappointments.", "드 코시 백작부인은 딸들을 두고 큰 희망을 품었고, 그다음에는 웬만한 희망을 품었으며, 그다음에는 다시 쓰디쓴 낭패를 맛보았다."], ["Only one had been married, and she was married to an attorney.", "시집간 것은 하나뿐이었고, 그나마 사무 변호사에게 갔다."], ["It was not to be supposed that she would have any very high-toned feelings as to Lily's rights in this matter.", "이 문제에서 릴리의 권리를 두고 그녀가 무슨 고결한 감정을 품으리라고는 짐작할 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["Such a man as Crosbie was certainly no great match for an earl's daughter.", "크로즈비 같은 남자가 백작의 딸에게 대단한 혼처가 아닌 것은 분명했다."], ["Such a marriage, indeed, would, one may say, be but a poor triumph.", "그런 혼인이란 사실 초라한 승리에 지나지 않는다고 해도 좋으리라."], ["When the countess, during the last season in town, had observed how matters were going with Alexandrina, she had cautioned her child, taking her to task for her imprudence.", "지난 사교철에 런던에서 알렉산드리나의 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 눈여겨보았을 때, 백작부인은 딸을 타이르며 그 경솔함을 나무랐다."], ["But the child had been at this work for fourteen years, and was weary of it.", "그러나 그 딸은 이 일에 십사 년을 매달려 온 터라 이제 진력이 나 있었다."], ["Her sisters had been at the work longer, and had almost given it up in despair.", "언니들은 더 오래 매달렸다가 거의 절망 속에 손을 놓아 버렸다."], ["Alexandrina did not tell her parent that her heart was now beyond her control, and that she had devoted herself to Crosbie for ever; but she pouted, saying that she knew very well what she was about, scolding her mother in return, and making Lady De Courcy perceive that the struggle was becoming very weary.", "알렉산드리나는 제 마음이 이제 제 뜻대로 되지 않으며 영원히 크로즈비에게 바쳐졌다는 말을 어머니에게 하지는 않았다. 다만 입을 삐죽이며 제가 무엇을 하는지 아주 잘 알고 있다고 말하고, 도리어 어머니를 나무라며, 이 싸움이 몹시 지겨워지고 있다는 것을 드 코시 백작부인이 알아차리게 만들었다."], ["And then there were other considerations.", "게다가 따져 볼 것이 또 있었다."], ["Mr. Crosbie had not much certainly in his own possession, but he was a man out of whom something might be made by family influence and his own standing.", "크로즈비 씨가 제 손에 쥔 것이 많지 않은 것은 분명하나, 그는 집안의 영향력과 제 지위로 무언가를 만들어 낼 만한 사람이었다."], ["He was not a hopeless, ponderous man, whom no leaven could raise.", "어떤 누룩으로도 부풀릴 수 없는, 가망 없이 둔중한 사내는 아니었다."], ["He was one of whose position in society the countess and her daughters need not be ashamed.", "사교계에서의 그의 자리를 두고 백작부인이나 그 딸들이 부끄러워할 필요는 없는 사람이었다."], ["Lady De Courcy had given no expressed consent to the arrangement, but it had come to be understood between her and her daughter that the scheme was to be entertained as admissible.", "드 코시 백작부인이 이 일에 대놓고 승낙을 준 적은 없었으나, 그 계획을 받아들일 만한 것으로 여긴다는 데에 모녀 사이의 양해는 서 있었다."]], [["Then came these tidings of the little girl down at Allington.", "그러던 차에 저 앨링턴의 어린 아가씨 소식이 들려온 것이었다."], ["She felt no anger against Crosbie.", "그녀는 크로즈비에게 화가 나지 않았다."], ["To be angry on such a subject would be futile, foolish, and almost indecorous.", "그런 일로 화를 내는 것은 부질없고 어리석으며 거의 예의에 어긋나는 짓이었다."], ["It was a part of the game which was as natural to her as fielding is to a cricketer.", "그것은 크리켓 선수에게 수비가 그러하듯 그녀에게는 지극히 자연스러운 경기의 한 부분이었다."], ["One cannot have it all winnings at any game.", "어떤 경기에서도 이기기만 할 수는 없는 법이다."], ["Whether Crosbie should eventually become her own son-in-law or not it came to her naturally, as a part of her duty in life, to bowl down the stumps of that young lady at Allington.", "크로즈비가 끝내 제 사위가 되든 안 되든, 앨링턴의 그 젊은 아가씨의 삼주문을 공으로 쓰러뜨리는 일은 살아가는 도리의 한 부분으로서 자연스럽게 그녀에게 다가왔다."], ["If Miss Dale knew the game well and could protect her own wicket, let her do so.", "데일 양이 경기를 잘 알아서 제 위킷을 지킬 수 있다면, 그렇게 하라지."]], [["She had no doubt as to Crosbie's engagement with Lilian Dale, but she had as little as to his being ashamed of that engagement.", "크로즈비가 릴리언 데일과 약혼했다는 데에 그녀는 조금도 의심이 없었으나, 그가 그 약혼을 부끄러워한다는 데에도 그만큼 의심이 없었다."], ["Had he really cared for Miss Dale he would not have left her to come to Courcy Castle.", "정말로 데일 양을 아꼈다면 그녀를 두고 코시 성으로 오지는 않았으리라."], ["Had he been really resolved to marry her, he would not have warded all questions respecting his engagement with fictitious answers.", "정말로 그녀와 결혼하기로 굳게 마음먹었다면, 약혼에 관한 물음들을 죄다 꾸며 낸 대답으로 막아 내지는 않았으리라."], ["He had amused himself with Lily Dale, and it was to be hoped that the young lady had not thought very seriously about it.", "그는 릴리 데일을 데리고 심심풀이를 한 것이고, 그 젊은 아가씨가 그 일을 그리 심각하게 여기지 않았기를 바랄 따름이었다."], ["That was the most charitable light in which Lady De Courcy was disposed to regard the question.", "드 코시 백작부인이 이 문제를 바라보려는 가장 너그러운 시선이 그러했다."]], [["It behoved Crosbie to write to Lily Dale before dinner.", "크로즈비는 만찬 전에 릴리 데일에게 편지를 써야 했다."], ["He had promised to do so immediately on his arrival, and he was aware that he would be regarded as being already one day beyond his promise.", "도착하는 즉시 쓰겠다고 약속했으니, 이미 약속보다 하루가 늦은 것으로 여겨지리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["Lily had told him that she would live upon his letters, and it was absolutely necessary that he should furnish her with her first meal.", "릴리는 그의 편지를 양식 삼아 살겠노라고 말했었고, 그러니 그가 그 첫 끼니를 차려 주는 일은 반드시 필요했다."], ["So he betook himself to his room in sufficient time before dinner, and got out his pen, ink, and paper.", "그래서 그는 만찬 전에 넉넉히 시간을 두고 제 방으로 물러가 펜과 잉크와 종이를 꺼냈다."]], [["He got out his pen, ink, and paper, and then he found that his difficulties were beginning.", "펜과 잉크와 종이를 꺼내고 나서야 그는 제 고생이 이제부터 시작임을 알았다."], ["I beg that it may be understood that Crosbie was not altogether a villain.", "크로즈비가 아주 악당은 아니었다는 점만은 알아주기를 나는 바란다."], ["He could not sit down and write a letter as coming from his heart, of which as he wrote it he knew the words to be false.", "그는 쓰면서 그 말이 거짓임을 스스로 아는 편지를, 마음에서 우러난 것인 양 앉아서 써 내려갈 수는 없는 사람이었다."], ["He was an ungenerous, worldly, inconstant man, very prone to think well of himself, and to give himself credit for virtues which he did not possess; but he could not be false with premeditated cruelty to a woman he had sworn to love.", "그는 인색하고 세속적이며 변덕스러운 남자였고, 제 자신을 좋게 여기기를 몹시 좋아했으며, 지니지도 않은 미덕을 제 몫으로 셈하기 일쑤였다. 그러나 사랑하겠노라 맹세한 여자에게 미리 작정한 잔인함으로 거짓을 부릴 수는 없었다."], ["He could not write an affectionate, warm-hearted letter to Lily, without bringing himself, at any rate for the time, to feel towards her in an affectionate, warm-hearted way.", "적어도 그동안만이라도 스스로를 다정하고 따뜻한 마음으로 릴리를 향하게 만들지 않고서는, 릴리에게 다정하고 따뜻한 편지를 쓸 수가 없었다."], ["Therefore he now sat himself to work, while his pen yet remained dry in his hand, to remodel his thoughts, which had been turned against Lily and Allington by the craft of Lady De Courcy.", "그리하여 그는 펜이 아직 손안에서 마른 채로 있는 동안, 드 코시 백작부인의 술수로 릴리와 앨링턴에 등을 돌리게 된 제 생각을 다시 빚어내는 일에 앉은 채로 매달렸다."], ["It takes some time before a man can do this.", "사람이 이런 일을 해내려면 시간이 걸린다."], ["He has to struggle with himself in a very uncomfortable way, making efforts which are often unsuccessful.", "아주 불편한 방식으로 제 자신과 싸워야 하고, 그 애씀이 헛수고로 끝나는 일도 잦다."], ["It is sometimes easier to lift a couple of hundredweights than to raise a few thoughts in one's mind which at other moments will come galloping in without a whistle.", "다른 때 같으면 휘파람 한 번 불지 않아도 말달리듯 달려들어 오는 몇 가지 생각을 마음속에서 끌어올리기보다, 차라리 백 근짜리 짐 두 덩이를 들어 올리는 편이 쉬울 때가 있다."]], [["He had just written the date of his letter when a little tap came at his door, and it was opened.", "편지의 날짜를 막 적었을 때 문을 가볍게 두드리는 소리가 나더니 문이 열렸다."]], [["\"I say, Crosbie,\"", "“이보게, 크로즈비.”"], ["said the Honourable John, \"didn't you say something yesterday about a cigar before dinner?\"", "아너러블 존이 말했다. “어제 만찬 전에 시가 한 대 하자는 얘기를 하지 않았나?”"]], [["\"Not a word,\"", "“한마디도 안 했네.”"], ["said Crosbie, in rather an angry tone.", "크로즈비가 제법 성난 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"Then it must have been me,\"", "“그럼 내가 그랬나 보군.”"], ["said John.", "존이 말했다."], ["\"But bring your case with you, and come down to the harness-room, if you won't smoke here.", "“아무튼 여기서 피우지 않겠다면 담배통을 들고 마구실로 내려오게."], ["I've had a regular little snuggery fitted up there; and we can go in and see the fellows making up the horses.\"", "거기다 제대로 된 아늑한 방을 하나 꾸며 놨거든. 들어가서 녀석들이 말 손질하는 것도 구경하고 말이야.”"]], [["Crosbie wished the Honourable John at the mischief.", "크로즈비는 아너러블 존이 어디 지옥에라도 가 있기를 바랐다."]], [["\"I have letters to write,\"", "“편지를 써야 해서.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Besides, I never smoke before dinner.\"", "“게다가 나는 만찬 전에는 담배를 피우지 않네.”"]], [["\"That's nonsense.", "“말도 안 되는 소리."], ["I've smoked hundreds of cigars with you before dinner.", "나는 자네와 만찬 전에 시가를 수백 대는 피웠네."], ["Are you going to turn curmudgeon, too, like George and the rest of them?", "자네까지 조지며 그 패거리처럼 심술궂은 늙은이가 되려는 건가?"], ["I don't know what's coming to the world!", "세상이 어떻게 돌아가려는지 알 수가 없군!"], ["I suppose the fact is, that little girl at Allington won't let you smoke.\"", "실은 앨링턴의 그 어린 아가씨가 자네한테 담배를 못 피우게 하는 게로군.”"]], [["\"The little girl at Allington--\"", "“앨링턴의 그 어린 아가씨는—”"], ["began Crosbie; and then he reflected that it would not be well for him to say anything to his present companion about that little girl.", "크로즈비가 말을 꺼냈다가, 지금 앞에 있는 이 친구에게 그 어린 아가씨에 관해 무슨 말이든 하는 것은 좋지 않으리라는 데 생각이 미쳤다."], ["\"I'll tell you what it is,\"", "“그럼 이렇게 하지.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I really have got letters to write which must go by this post.", "“나는 정말로 이번 우편으로 부쳐야 할 편지가 있어."], ["There's my cigar-case on the dressing-table.\"", "내 시가 통은 화장대 위에 있네.”"]], [["\"I hope it will be long before I'm brought to such a state,\"", "“나는 그런 꼴이 되기까지 한참 걸렸으면 좋겠군.”"], ["said John, taking up the cigars in his hand.", "존이 시가를 손에 집어 들며 말했다."]], [["\"Let me have the case back,\"", "“통은 돌려주게.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"A present from the little girl, I suppose?\"", "“그 어린 아가씨한테 받은 선물인가 보지?”"], ["said John.", "존이 말했다."], ["\"All right, old fellow!", "“알았네, 이 친구야!"], ["you shall have it.\"", "돌려주지.”"]], [["\"There would be a nice brother-in-law for a man,\"", "“사내가 얻기에 참 훌륭한 처남이겠군.”"], ["said Crosbie to himself, as the door closed behind the retreating scion of the De Courcy family.", "물러가는 드 코시 가문의 자제 뒤로 문이 닫히자 크로즈비는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["And then, again, he took up his pen.", "그러고는 다시 펜을 들었다."], ["The letter must be written, and therefore he threw himself upon the table, resolved that the words should come and the paper be filled.", "편지는 써야만 했고, 그래서 그는 말이 나오고 종이가 채워지고야 말리라 마음먹고 탁자 위로 몸을 내던졌다."]], [["Courcy Castle, October, 186--.", "코시 성, 186─년 10월."]], [["DEAREST LILY,--This is the first letter I ever wrote to you, except those little notes when I sent you my compliments discreetly,--and it sounds so odd. You will think that this does not come as soon as it should; but the truth is that after all I only got in here just before dinner yesterday. I stayed ever so long in Barchester, and came across such a queer character. For you must know I went to church, and afterwards fraternized with the clergyman who did the service; such a gentle old soul,--and, singularly enough, he is the grandfather of Lady Dumbello, who is staying here. I wonder what you'd think of Lady Dumbello, or how you'd like to be shut up in the same house with her for a week?", "사랑하는 릴리에게. 이건 내가 그대에게 쓰는 첫 편지예요. 조심스레 안부를 전하던 그 짧은 쪽지들 말고는 말이지요. 그러니 어쩐지 참 이상한 기분이 듭니다. 이 편지가 마땅히 와야 할 때보다 늦었다고 그대는 생각하겠지요. 하지만 사실은 나도 어제 만찬 직전에야 겨우 이곳에 들어왔답니다. 바체스터에 한참을 머물렀고, 거기서 아주 별난 어른을 만났어요. 그러니까 내가 교회에 갔는데, 예배를 집전한 목사와 나중에 스스럼없이 어울리게 되었지요. 어찌나 온화한 노인인지. 그런데 참으로 묘하게도, 그분이 지금 여기 묵고 있는 레이디 덤벨로의 할아버지랍니다. 그대라면 레이디 덤벨로를 어떻게 볼지, 아니 그런 사람과 한집에 일주일이나 갇혀 지내는 걸 어떻게 여길지 궁금하군요."]], [["But with reference to my staying at Barchester, I must tell you the truth now, though I was a gross impostor the day that I went away.", "그런데 바체스터에 머문 일에 관해서는 이제 사실대로 말해야겠어요. 떠나던 날 나는 뻔뻔한 사기꾼이었으니까요."], ["I wanted to avoid a parting on that last morning, and therefore I started much sooner than I need have done.", "나는 그 마지막 아침에 작별하는 일을 피하고 싶었고, 그래서 굳이 그럴 필요가 없는데도 훨씬 일찍 길을 나섰던 겁니다."], ["I know you will be very angry with me; but open confession is good for the soul.", "그대가 몹시 화를 내리라는 걸 압니다. 그러나 숨김없는 고백은 영혼에 이롭다고 하지요."], ["You frustrated all my little plan by your early rising; and as I saw you standing on the terrace, looking after us as we went, I acknowledged that you had been right, and that I was wrong.", "그대가 일찍 일어나는 바람에 내 자잘한 계획은 죄다 어그러졌어요. 그리고 우리가 떠나는 것을 테라스에 서서 바라보는 그대를 보면서, 나는 그대가 옳았고 내가 그르다는 것을 인정했습니다."], ["When the time came, I was very glad to have you with me at the last moment.", "막상 그때가 되고 보니, 마지막 순간에 그대가 곁에 있어 준 것이 나는 몹시 기뻤어요."]], [["My own dearest Lily, you cannot think how different this place is from the two houses at Allington, or how much I prefer the sort of life which belongs to the latter.", "사랑하는 나의 릴리, 이곳이 앨링턴의 두 집과 얼마나 다른지, 그리고 내가 그쪽에 딸린 삶을 얼마나 더 좋아하는지 그대는 짐작도 못 할 겁니다."], ["I know that I have been what the world calls worldly, but you will have to cure me of that.", "내가 세상에서 이르는 대로 세속적인 사람이었다는 걸 압니다. 하지만 그대가 나를 그 병에서 고쳐 주어야 해요."], ["I have questioned myself very much since I left you, and I do not think that I am quite beyond the reach of a cure.", "그대를 떠나온 뒤로 나는 스스로에게 무던히도 캐물었는데, 내가 아주 손쓸 수 없는 지경이라고는 생각지 않습니다."], ["At any rate, I will put myself trustingly into the doctor's hands.", "어쨌든 나는 의사의 손에 나를 믿고 맡기렵니다. 사람이 제 버릇을 고치기가 어렵다는 건 알아요."], ["I know it is hard for a man to change his habits; but I can with truth say this for myself, that I was happy at Allington, enjoying every hour of the day, and that here I am ennuyé by everybody and nearly by everything.", "그래도 나 자신을 위해 이 말만은 참되게 할 수 있습니다. 나는 앨링턴에서 행복했고 하루의 매 시간을 즐겼으나, 여기서는 모든 사람에게, 그리고 거의 모든 것에 ennuyé, 곧 권태를 느낍니다."], ["One of the girls of the house I do like; but as to other people, I can hardly find a companion among them, let alone a friend.", "이 집 딸들 가운데 하나는 내 마음에 듭니다. 그러나 다른 사람들로 말하자면 벗은커녕 말동무 하나 찾기가 어렵군요."], ["However, it would not have done for me to have broken away from all such alliances too suddenly.", "그렇지만 그런 교분을 전부 너무 갑작스레 끊어 버리는 것도 내게는 될 일이 아니었지요."]], [["When I get up to London--and now I really am anxious to get there--I can write to you more at my ease, and more freely than I do here.", "런던으로 올라가면 — 이제는 정말이지 어서 그리로 가고 싶습니다 — 여기서보다 훨씬 마음 편하고 훨씬 자유롭게 그대에게 편지를 쓸 수 있을 거예요."], ["I know that I am hardly myself among these people,--or rather, I am hardly myself as you know me, and as I hope you always will know me.", "이 사람들 틈에서 나는 도무지 내가 아니라는 걸 압니다. 아니, 그대가 아는 나, 그리고 그대가 언제까지나 그렇게 알아 주기를 바라는 그 나는 도무지 아니라고 해야겠지요."], ["But, nevertheless, I am not so overcome by the miasma but what I can tell you how truly I love you.", "그래도 그 독기에 아주 넘어가 버린 것은 아니어서, 내가 그대를 얼마나 참되게 사랑하는지는 말할 수 있습니다."], ["Even though my spirit should be here, which it is not, my heart would be on the Allington lawns.", "설령 내 넋이 이곳에 있다 한들, 실은 그렇지도 않지만, 내 마음만은 앨링턴의 잔디밭에 가 있을 겁니다."], ["That dear lawn and that dear bridge!", "그 정다운 잔디밭과 그 정다운 다리!"]], [["Give my kind love to Bell and your mother.", "벨과 어머님께 내 다정한 안부를 전해 주세요."], ["I feel already that I might almost say my mother.", "나는 벌써 어머님을 내 어머니라고 불러도 되겠다 싶은 기분입니다."], ["And Lily, my darling, write to me at once.", "그리고 릴리, 내 사랑, 당장 답장을 주어요."], ["I expect your letters to me to be longer, and better, and brighter than mine to you.", "그대가 내게 보내는 편지는 내가 그대에게 보내는 것보다 더 길고 더 좋고 더 밝으리라 기대합니다."], ["But I will endeavour to make mine nicer when I get back to town.", "그래도 런던으로 돌아가면 내 편지도 더 나아지도록 애써 보지요."]], [["God bless you.", "하느님의 축복이 그대에게 있기를."], ["Yours, with all my heart,", "온 마음을 다하여, 그대의,"]], [["As he had waxed warm with his writing he had forced himself to be affectionate, and, as he flattered himself, frank and candid.", "글을 써 내려가며 마음이 달아오르자 그는 애써 자신을 다정하게 만들었고, 스스로 흡족해한 대로 솔직하고 담백해졌다."], ["Nevertheless, he was partly conscious that he was preparing for himself a mode of escape in those allusions of his to his own worldliness; if escape should ultimately be necessary.", "그러면서도 그는, 제 세속성을 넌지시 비친 그 대목들 속에 끝내 달아날 길이 필요해질 때를 위한 빠져나갈 구멍을 스스로 마련해 두고 있다는 것을 얼마쯤은 의식하고 있었다. “나는 애썼소.”"], ["\"I have tried,\"", "그때가 되면 그는 이렇게 말하리라."], ["he would then say; \"I have struggled honestly, with my best efforts for success; but I am not good enough for such success.\"", "“나는 성공해 보려고 온 힘을 다해 정직하게 몸부림쳤소. 그러나 나는 그런 성공을 이룰 만큼 훌륭한 사람이 못 되오.”"], ["I do not intend to say that he wrote with a premeditated intention of thus using his words; but as he wrote them he could not keep himself from reflecting that they might be used in that way.", "그가 제 말을 그렇게 써먹으려는 속셈을 미리 품고 편지를 썼다는 말은 아니다. 다만 그 말을 써 내려가면서, 그것이 그런 식으로 쓰일 수도 있겠다는 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수 없었다는 것이다."]], [["He read his letter over, felt satisfied with it, and resolved that he might now free his mind from that consideration for the next forty-eight hours.", "그는 편지를 다시 읽어 보고 흡족해했으며, 이제 앞으로 마흔여덟 시간 동안은 그 생각에서 마음을 놓아도 되겠다고 작정했다."], ["Whatever might be his sins he had done his duty by Lily!", "제 죄가 무엇이든 릴리에 대한 도리는 다한 것이다!"], ["And with this comfortable reflection he deposited his letter in the Courcy Castle letter-box.", "그리고 이 흐뭇한 생각을 안고 그는 편지를 코시 성의 우편함에 넣었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIX. THE SQUIRE MAKES A VISIT TO THE SMALL HOUSE.", "# 제19장. 지주가 작은 집을 찾아가다"]], [["Mrs. Dale acknowledged to herself that she had not much ground for hoping that she should ever find in Crosbie's house much personal happiness for her future life.", "데일 부인은 크로즈비의 집에서 제 앞날의 삶을 위한 개인적인 행복을 많이 찾아내리라고 바랄 만한 근거가 별로 없다는 것을 스스로 인정했다."], ["She did not dislike Mr. Crosbie, nor in any great degree mistrust him; but she had seen enough of him to make her certain that Lily's future home in London could not be a home for her.", "그녀는 크로즈비 씨를 싫어하지도 않았고, 그를 크게 미덥지 않게 여기지도 않았다."], ["He was worldly, or, at least, a man of the world.", "그러나 릴리가 런던에서 꾸릴 앞날의 집이 제게는 집이 될 수 없으리라는 것을 확신할 만큼은 그를 겪어 보았다."], ["He would be anxious to make the most of his income, and his life would be one long struggle, not perhaps for money, but for those things which money only can give.", "그는 세속적인 사람, 적어도 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이었다. 그는 제 수입에서 뽑아낼 수 있는 것을 죄다 뽑아내려 애쓸 터였고, 그의 삶은 어쩌면 돈 그 자체를 위해서는 아닐지언정 오직 돈으로만 얻을 수 있는 것들을 위한 하나의 기나긴 싸움이 될 터였다."], ["There are men to whom eight hundred a year is great wealth, and houses to which it brings all the comforts that life requires.", "연 팔백 파운드가 큰 재산인 사람들이 있고, 그 돈이 삶에 필요한 온갖 안락을 다 가져다주는 집들이 있다."], ["But Crosbie was not such a man, nor would his house be such a house. Mrs.", "그러나 크로즈비는 그런 사람이 아니었고, 그의 집도 그런 집이 되지 않을 터였다."], ["Dale hoped that Lily would be happy with him, and satisfied with his modes of life, and she strove to believe that such would be the case; but as regarded herself she was forced to confess that in such a marriage her child would be much divided from her.", "데일 부인은 릴리가 그와 더불어 행복하기를, 그의 사는 방식에 만족하기를 바랐고, 또 그러하리라고 믿으려 애썼다. 그러나 제 자신에 관해서라면, 그런 혼인으로 제 아이가 저에게서 멀리 갈라져 나가리라는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["That pleasant abode to which she had long looked forward that she might have a welcome there in coming years should be among fields and trees, not in some narrow London street.", "오는 세월에 제가 반갑게 맞아들여지기를 오래도록 고대해 온 그 정다운 거처는 들판과 나무들 사이에 있어야지 런던의 어느 비좁은 거리에 있어서는 안 되는 것이었다."], ["Lily must now become a city lady; but Bell would still be left to her, and it might still be hoped that Bell would find for herself some country home.", "이제 릴리는 도시의 부인이 되어야 한다. 그러나 벨은 여전히 제 곁에 남을 것이고, 벨은 제 몫의 시골 보금자리를 찾아내리라고 아직은 바랄 수 있었다."]], [["Since the day on which Lily had first told her mother of her engagement, Mrs. Dale had found herself talking much more fully and more frequently with Bell than with her younger daughter.", "릴리가 어머니에게 제 약혼을 처음 말한 날 이래로, 데일 부인은 어느새 작은딸보다 벨과 훨씬 더 속속들이, 더 자주 이야기를 나누고 있음을 깨달았다."], ["As long as Crosbie was at Allington this was natural enough.", "크로즈비가 앨링턴에 머무는 동안에는 그것이 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["He and Lily were of course together, while Bell remained with her mother.", "그와 릴리가 함께 있는 것이 당연했고, 그동안 벨은 어머니와 함께 있었으니까."], ["But the same state of things continued even after Crosbie was gone.", "그러나 크로즈비가 떠난 뒤에도 사정은 그대로였다."], ["It was not that there was any coolness or want of affection between the mother and daughter, but that Lily's heart was full of her lover, and that Mrs. Dale, though she had given her cordial consent to the marriage, felt that she had but few points of sympathy with her future son-in-law.", "어머니와 딸 사이에 서먹함이나 애정의 모자람이 있어서가 아니라, 릴리의 마음이 제 연인으로 가득 차 있었기 때문이고, 데일 부인으로 말하자면 그 혼인에 진심으로 동의를 해 주었으면서도 장차 사위가 될 사람과 통하는 데가 거의 없다고 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["She had never said, even to herself, that she disliked him; nay, she had sometimes declared to herself that she was fond of him.", "그녀는 스스로에게조차 그가 싫다고 말한 적이 없었다. 아니, 때로는 그가 마음에 든다고 스스로에게 다짐하기까지 했다."], ["But, in truth, he was not a man after her own heart.", "그러나 참으로 말하자면 그는 그녀의 마음에 맞는 사람이 아니었다."], ["He was not one who could ever be to her as her own son and her own child.", "그는 그녀에게 결코 제 아들이, 제 자식이 되어 줄 수 있는 사람이 아니었다."]], [["But she and Bell would pass hours together talking of Lily's prospects.", "그러나 그녀와 벨은 릴리의 앞날을 이야기하며 몇 시간씩 함께 보내곤 했다."], ["\"It seems so strange to me,\"", "“나는 참 이상하구나.”"], ["said Mrs.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["Dale, \"that she of all girls should have been fancied by such a man as Mr. Crosbie, or that she should have liked him.", "“하필 그 아이가, 그 많은 처녀들 가운데 그 아이가 크로즈비 씨 같은 사람의 눈에 들었다는 것도, 그 아이가 그이를 좋아하게 되었다는 것도 말이다."], ["I cannot imagine Lily living in London.\"", "릴리가 런던에서 사는 모습이 도무지 그려지지 않는구나.”"]], [["\"If he is good and affectionate to her she will be happy wherever he is,\"", "“그이가 그 애한테 착하고 다정하게만 해 준다면 어디서 살든 행복할 거예요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I hope so;--I'm sure I hope so.", "“나도 그러기를 바란다. 정말이지 그러기를 바라."], ["But it seems as though she will be so far separated from us.", "하지만 그 아이가 우리에게서 아주 멀리 갈라져 나가는 것만 같구나."], ["It is not the distance, but the manner of life which makes the separation.", "갈라놓는 것은 거리가 아니라 사는 방식이란다."], ["I hope you'll never be taken so far from me.\"", "너만은 부디 나한테서 그렇게 멀리 데려가지지 않았으면 좋겠구나.”"]], [["\"I don't think I shall allow myself to be taken up to London,\"", "“저는 저를 런던으로 데려가게 놔두지는 않을 것 같아요.”"], ["said Bell, laughing.", "벨이 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"But one can never tell.", "“하지만 사람 일은 모르는 거죠."], ["If I do you must follow us, mamma.\"", "만약 그렇게 되면 엄마도 우리를 따라오셔야 해요.”"]], [["\"I do not want another Mr. Crosbie for you, dear.\"", "“너한테까지 크로즈비 씨 같은 사람이 또 오는 건 바라지 않는다, 얘야.”"]], [["\"But perhaps I may want one for myself.", "“하지만 저는 저대로 그런 사람을 바랄지도 모르잖아요."], ["You need not tremble quite yet, however.", "그래도 아직 떨고 계실 것까지는 없어요."], ["Apollos do not come this road every day.\"", "아폴로가 날마다 이 길로 오는 건 아니니까요.”"]], [["\"Poor Lily!", "“가엾은 릴리!"], ["Do you remember when she first called him Apollo? I do, well.", "그 애가 그이를 처음 아폴로라고 부르던 때가 기억나니?"], ["I remember his coming here the day after Bernard brought him down, and how you were playing on the lawn, while I was in the other garden.", "나는 똑똑히 기억한다. 버나드가 그이를 데리고 내려온 이튿날 그이가 여기 왔던 것도, 내가 저쪽 정원에 있는 동안 너희가 잔디밭에서 놀고 있던 것도 기억나."], ["I little thought then what it would come to.\"", "그때는 이런 일이 되리라고는 꿈에도 생각하지 못했지.”"]], [["\"But, mamma, you don't regret it?\"", "“그렇지만 엄마, 후회하시는 건 아니죠?”"]], [["\"Not if it's to make her happy.", "“그 애를 행복하게 해 줄 일이라면 후회할 게 없지."], ["If she can be happy with him, of course I shall not regret it; not though he were to take her to the world's end away from us.", "그 애가 그이와 행복할 수 있다면야 물론 후회하지 않는다. 그이가 그 애를 우리에게서 땅끝까지 데려간다 해도 말이다."], ["What else have I to look for but that she and you should both be happy?\"", "그 애와 네가 둘 다 행복한 것 말고 내가 무엇을 더 바라겠니?”"]], [["\"Men in London are happy with their wives as well as men in the country.\"", "“런던 남자들도 시골 남자들 못지않게 아내와 행복하게 살아요.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes; of all women I should be the first to acknowledge that.\"", "“그럼, 그렇고말고. 여자들 가운데 그것을 맨 먼저 인정할 사람이 바로 나란다.”"]], [["\"And as to Adolphus himself, I do not know why we should distrust him.\"", "“그리고 어돌퍼스 그이만 놓고 보면 우리가 그이를 못 미더워할 까닭이 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"No, my dear; there is no reason.", "“없지, 얘야. 아무 까닭도 없어."], ["If I did distrust him, I should not have given so ready an assent to the marriage.", "내가 그이를 못 미더워했다면 그 혼인에 그렇게 선뜻 동의하지 않았을 게다."], ["But, nevertheless--\"", "하지만 그래도—”"]], [["\"The truth is, you don't like him, mamma.\"", "“사실은 엄마가 그이를 좋아하지 않으시는 거예요.”"]], [["\"Not so cordially as I hope I may like any man whom you may choose for your husband.\"", "“네가 네 남편으로 고르는 사람이라면 누구든 내가 이만큼은 좋아했으면 하고 바라는데, 그만큼 살갑게는 아니로구나.”"]], [["And Lily, though she said nothing on the subject to Mrs. Dale, felt that her mother was in some degree estranged from her.", "그리고 릴리는, 그 문제에 관해 데일 부인에게 한마디도 하지 않았으나, 어머니가 저에게서 어느 만큼 멀어져 있음을 느꼈다."], ["Crosbie's name was frequently mentioned between them, but in the tone of Mrs.", "두 사람 사이에서 크로즈비의 이름은 자주 오르내렸다."], ["Dale's voice, and in her manner when she spoke of him, there was lacking that enthusiasm and heartiness which real sympathy would have produced.", "그러나 데일 부인의 목소리에도, 그를 이야기할 때의 태도에도, 참된 공감이라면 낳았을 그 열의와 살가움이 없었다."], ["Lily did not analyse her own feelings, or closely make inquiry as to those of her mother, but she perceived that it was not all as she would have wished it to have been.", "릴리는 제 감정을 헤아려 보지도 않았고 어머니의 감정을 꼬치꼬치 캐물어 보지도 않았으나, 모든 것이 제가 바랐을 그대로는 아니라는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["\"I know mamma does not love him,\"", "“엄마는 그이를 사랑하지 않으셔.”"], ["she said to Bell on the evening of the day on which she received Crosbie's first letter.", "크로즈비의 첫 편지를 받은 날 저녁에 릴리가 벨에게 말했다."]], [["\"Not as you do, Lily; but she does love him.\"", "“너만큼은 아니지, 릴리. 하지만 엄마도 그이를 사랑하셔.”"]], [["\"Not as I do!", "“나만큼은 아니라니!"], ["To say that is nonsense, Bell; of course she does not love him as I do.", "그런 말은 부질없어, 언니. 엄마가 나만큼 그이를 사랑하지 않는 거야 당연하지."], ["But the truth is she does not love him at all.", "하지만 사실은 엄마가 그이를 도무지 사랑하지 않으신다는 거야."], ["Do you think I cannot see it?\"", "내가 그걸 못 볼 것 같아?”"]], [["\"I'm afraid that you see too much.\"", "“네가 너무 많은 걸 보는 것 같아 걱정이야.”"]], [["\"She never says a word against him; but if she really liked him she would sometimes say a word in his favour.", "“엄마는 그이를 나쁘게 말한 적이 한 번도 없어. 하지만 정말 그이가 마음에 들었다면 이따금은 그이를 두둔하는 말도 하셨을 거야."], ["I do not think she would ever mention his name unless you or I spoke of him before her.", "언니나 내가 먼저 그이 이야기를 꺼내지 않으면 엄마는 그이 이름을 입에 올리지도 않으실걸."], ["If she did not approve of him, why did she not say so sooner?\"", "그이가 마뜩잖으셨다면 왜 진작 말씀하지 않으셨을까?”"]], [["\"That's hardly fair upon mamma,\"", "“그건 엄마한테 온당한 말이 아니야.”"], ["said Bell, with some earnestness.", "벨이 자못 진지하게 말했다."], ["\"She does not disapprove of him, and she never did.", "“엄마는 그이를 마뜩잖아하지 않으시고, 마뜩잖아하신 적도 없어."], ["You know mamma well enough to be sure that she would not interfere with us in such a matter without very strong reason.", "너도 엄마를 잘 알잖니. 아주 강한 까닭이 없이는 그런 일에 우리한테 참견하실 분이 아니야."], ["As regards Mr. Crosbie, she gave her consent without a moment's hesitation.\"", "크로즈비 씨에 관해서라면 엄마는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 승낙해 주셨어.”"]], [["\"Yes, she did.\"", "“그래, 그러셨지.”"]], [["\"How can you say, then, that she disapproves of him?\"", "“그런데 어떻게 엄마가 그이를 마뜩잖아하신다고 말할 수 있니?”"]], [["\"I didn't mean to find fault with mamma.", "“엄마를 탓하려던 건 아니야."], ["Perhaps it will come all right.\"", "아마 다 잘될 거야.”"]], [["\"It will come all right.\"", "“다 잘될 거야.”"], ["But Bell, though she made this very satisfactory promise, was as well aware as either of the others that the family would be divided when Crosbie should have married Lily and taken her off to London.", "그러나 벨은 이렇게 아주 흡족한 다짐을 해 주면서도 다른 두 사람 못지않게 잘 알고 있었다. 크로즈비가 릴리와 혼인하여 그 애를 런던으로 데려가고 나면 이 집안이 갈라지리라는 것을."]], [["On the following morning Mrs. Dale and Bell were sitting together.", "이튿날 아침 데일 부인과 벨은 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["Lily was above in her own room, either writing to her lover, or reading his letter, or thinking of him, or working for him.", "릴리는 위층 제 방에서 연인에게 편지를 쓰고 있거나, 그의 편지를 읽고 있거나, 그를 생각하고 있거나, 그를 위해 무언가를 짓고 있었다."], ["In some way she was employed on his behalf, and with this object she was alone.", "어떻게든 그녀는 그를 위한 일에 매여 있었고, 그러느라 혼자 있었다."], ["It was now the middle of October, and the fire was lit in Mrs. Dale's drawing-room.", "때는 이제 10월 중순이었고, 데일 부인의 응접실에는 불이 지펴져 있었다."], ["The window which opened upon the lawn was closed, the heavy curtains had been put back in their places, and it had been acknowledged as an unwelcome fact that the last of the summer was over.", "잔디밭으로 열리던 창은 닫혔고, 두꺼운 커튼이 제자리에 다시 드리워졌으며, 여름이 마지막으로 다 갔다는 것이 반갑지 않은 사실로 받아들여져 있었다."], ["This was always a sorrow to Mrs. Dale; but it is one of those sorrows which hardly admit of open expression.", "이것은 데일 부인에게 언제나 하나의 슬픔이었으나, 그것은 좀처럼 입 밖에 내어 말할 수 없는 종류의 슬픔이었다."]], [["\"Bell,\"", "“벨아.”"], ["she said, looking up suddenly; \"there's your uncle at the window.", "그녀가 문득 고개를 들며 말했다. “네 삼촌이 창밖에 와 계신다."], ["Let him in.\"", "들어오시게 해라.”"], ["For now, since the putting up of the curtains, the window had been bolted as well as closed.", "커튼을 드리운 뒤로는 창을 닫아 두었을 뿐 아니라 빗장까지 질러 두었던 것이다."], ["So Bell got up, and opened a passage for the squire's entrance.", "그래서 벨이 일어나 지주가 들어올 길을 열어 주었다."], ["It was not often that he came down in this way, and when he did do so it was generally for some purpose which had been expressed before.", "그가 이렇게 내려오는 일은 흔치 않았고, 내려올 때에는 대개 미리 말해 둔 어떤 볼일이 있어서였다."]], [["\"What! fires already?\"", "“아니, 벌써 불을 지폈소?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I never have fires at the other house in the morning till the first of November.", "“나는 저쪽 집에서는 11월 초하루가 되기 전에는 아침에 불을 피우는 법이 없소."], ["I like to see a spark in the grate after dinner.\"", "나는 저녁 식사 뒤에 벽난로 속에 불티가 이는 걸 보는 게 좋더군.”"]], [["\"I like a fire when I'm cold,\"", "“저는 추우면 불을 지피지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["But this was a subject on which the squire and his sister-in-law had differed before, and as Mr. Dale had some business in hand, he did not now choose to waste his energy in supporting his own views on the question of fires.", "그러나 이것은 지주와 그의 제수가 전에도 서로 뜻을 달리했던 문제였고, 데일 씨는 처리할 볼일이 있었으므로 이번에는 불에 관한 제 지론을 옹호하는 데 기운을 허비하고 싶지 않았다."]], [["\"Bell, my dear,\"", "“벨아, 얘야.”"], ["said he, \"I want to speak to your mother for a minute or two on a matter of business.", "그가 말했다. “네 어머니와 볼일로 잠깐 이야기를 좀 나눠야겠구나."], ["You wouldn't mind leaving us for a little while, would you?\"", "잠시만 자리를 비켜 주겠느냐?”"], ["Whereupon Bell collected up her work and went upstairs to her sister.", "그 말에 벨은 제 바느질감을 챙겨 들고 위층 동생에게로 올라갔다."], ["\"Uncle Christopher is below with mamma,\"", "“크리스토퍼 삼촌이 아래층에서 엄마랑 계셔.”"], ["said she, \"talking about business.", "그녀가 말했다. “볼일 이야기를 하신대."], ["I suppose it is something to do with your marriage.\"", "네 혼사에 관한 일인가 봐.” 그러나 벨의 짐작은 빗나갔다."], ["But Bell was wrong. The squire's visit had no reference to Lily's marriage.", "지주의 방문은 릴리의 혼사와는 아무 상관이 없었다."]], [["Mrs. Dale did not move or speak a word when Bell was gone, though it was evident that the squire paused in order that she might ask some question of him.", "벨이 나가고 나서도 데일 부인은 몸도 움직이지 않고 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["\"Mary,\"", "지주가 그녀 쪽에서 무언가 물어 주기를 바라고 말을 멈춘 것이 뻔했는데도 그랬다."], ["said he, at last, \"I'll tell you what it is that I have come to say to you.\"", "“제수씨.” 마침내 그가 말했다."], ["Whereupon she put the piece of needlework which was in her hands down upon the work-basket before her, and settled herself to listen to him.", "“내가 무슨 말을 하러 왔는지 말하리다.” 그러자 그녀는 손에 들고 있던 바느질감을 앞에 놓인 반짇고리 위에 내려놓고 그의 말을 들을 자세를 갖추었다."]], [["\"I wish to speak to you about Bell.\"", "“벨에 관해 이야기하고 싶소.”"]], [["\"About Bell?\" said Mrs.", "“벨에 관해서요?”"], ["Dale, as though much surprised that he should have anything to say to her respecting her eldest daughter.", "데일 부인이 말했다. 그가 제 맏딸에 관해 무슨 할 말이 있다는 것이 몹시 뜻밖이라는 투였다."]], [["\"Yes, about Bell.", "“그렇소, 벨 말이오."], ["Here's Lily going to be married, and it will be well that Bell should be married too.\"", "릴리가 시집을 가게 되었으니 벨도 시집을 가는 것이 좋을 거요.”"]], [["\"I don't see that at all,\"", "“저는 도무지 그렇게 생각하지 않아요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I am by no means in a hurry to be rid of her.\"", "“그 애를 서둘러 치워 버릴 마음은 조금도 없습니다.”"]], [["\"No, I dare say not.", "“그렇겠지, 그럴 거요."], ["But, of course, you only regard her welfare, and I can truly say that I do the same.", "하지만 물론 제수씨는 그 아이의 앞날만 생각하는 것일 테고, 나 역시 그렇다고 참으로 말할 수 있소."], ["There would be no necessity for hurry as to a marriage for her under ordinary circumstances, but there may be circumstances to make such a thing desirable, and I think that there are.\"", "여느 형편이라면 그 아이의 혼사를 서두를 까닭이 없겠지만, 그런 일을 바랄 만하게 만드는 사정이라는 게 있을 수 있고, 나는 그런 사정이 있다고 생각하오.”"], ["It was evident from the squire's tone and manner that he was very much in earnest; but it was also evident that he found some difficulty in opening out the budget with which he had prepared himself.", "지주의 말투와 태도로 보아 그가 몹시 진지하다는 것이 뚜렷했다. 그러나 그가 채비해 온 보따리를 풀어놓기를 어려워한다는 것 또한 뚜렷했다."], ["He hesitated a little in his voice, and seemed to be almost nervous.", "목소리가 조금 머뭇거렸고, 거의 안절부절못하는 듯도 했다."], ["Mrs. Dale, with some little spice of ill-nature, altogether abstained from assisting him.", "데일 부인은 조금은 심술궂은 마음으로 그를 도와주는 일이라고는 전혀 하지 않았다."], ["She was jealous of interference from him about her girls, and though she was of course bound to listen to him, she did so with a prejudice against and almost with a resolve to oppose anything that he might say.", "그녀는 제 딸들 일에 그가 참견하는 것을 시샘했고, 물론 그의 말을 들어 주어야 할 처지이기는 했으나, 그가 무슨 말을 하든 반대하겠다는 편견을, 거의 작정에 가까운 마음을 품고 들었다."], ["When he had finished his little speech about circumstances, the squire paused again; but Mrs.", "사정이 어떻고 하는 짤막한 말을 마친 뒤 지주는 다시 말을 멈추었다."], ["Dale still sat silent, with her eyes fixed upon his face.", "그러나 데일 부인은 여전히 그의 얼굴에 눈을 붙박은 채 말없이 앉아 있었다."]], [["\"I love your children very dearly,\"", "“나는 제수씨의 아이들을 몹시 사랑하오.”"], ["said he, \"though I believe you hardly give me credit for doing so.\"", "그가 말했다. “제수씨는 내가 그런다는 걸 좀처럼 인정해 주지 않는 것 같지만 말이오.”"]], [["\"I am sure you do,\" said Mrs.", "“그러시다는 걸 잘 알아요.”"], ["Dale, \"and they are both well aware of it.\"", "데일 부인이 말했다. “그 애들 둘 다 그걸 잘 알고 있고요.”"]], [["\"And I am very anxious that they should be comfortably established in life.", "“그리고 나는 그 아이들이 세상에서 편안하게 자리 잡기를 몹시 바라고 있소."], ["I have no children of my own, and those of my two brothers are everything to me.\"", "나에게는 내 자식이 없고, 내 두 아우의 자식들이 나에게는 전부요.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale had always considered it as a matter of course that Bernard should be the squire's heir, and had never felt that her daughters had any claim on that score.", "데일 부인은 버나드가 지주의 상속인이 되는 것을 늘 당연한 일로 여겨 왔고, 제 딸들이 그 몫에 무슨 권리가 있다고 느껴 본 적이 없었다."], ["It was a well-understood thing in the family that the senior male Dale should have all the Dale property and all the Dale money.", "데일 집안의 재산과 데일 집안의 돈은 모두 데일 가의 손위 남자에게 돌아가야 한다는 것이 이 집안에서 다들 잘 알고 있는 일이었다."], ["She fully recognized even the propriety of such an arrangement.", "그녀는 그런 처사가 마땅하다는 것까지도 온전히 인정했다."], ["But it seemed to her that the squire was almost guilty of hypocrisy in naming his nephew and his two nieces together, as though they were the joint heirs of his love.", "그러나 그가 조카 하나와 조카딸 둘을 마치 제 사랑의 공동 상속인이기라도 한 것처럼 나란히 부르는 것은 거의 위선에 가깝게 여겨졌다. 버나드는 그의 양자였고, 그 삼촌이 그런 양자를 들일 권리를 아무도 아까워하지 않았다."], ["Bernard was his adopted son, and no one had begrudged to the uncle the right of making such adoption.", "버나드는 그에게 전부였고, 그의 상속인인 만큼 많은 일에서 그에게 순종할 의무가 있었다."], ["Bernard was everything to him, and as being his heir was bound to obey him in many things.", "그러나 그녀의 딸들은 그에게 여느 삼촌에게 여느 조카딸이 되는 것 이상은 아무것도 아니었다."], ["But her daughters were no more to him than any nieces might be to any uncle.", "그 아이들을 혼인으로 어디에 보내는가는 그가 상관할 일이 아니었다."], ["He had nothing to do with their disposal in marriage; and the mother's spirit was already up in arms and prepared to do battle for her own independence, and for that of her children.", "그리고 어머니의 마음은 벌써 제 몫의 독립을 위해, 또 제 아이들의 독립을 위해 싸울 태세로 곤두서 있었다. “버나드가 좋은 혼처를 만난다면 시아주버니께 위안이 되겠지요.”"], ["\"If Bernard would marry well,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"I have no doubt it would be a comfort to you,\"--meaning to imply thereby that the squire had no right to trouble himself about any other marriage.", "지주에게는 그 밖의 어떤 혼사로도 골머리를 앓을 권리가 없다는 뜻을 담은 말이었다."]], [["\"That's just it,\"", "“바로 그거요.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"It would be a great comfort to me.", "“나에게 큰 위안이 될 거요."], ["And if he and Bell could make up their minds together, it would, I should think, be a great comfort to you also.\"", "그리고 그 아이와 벨이 서로 마음을 맞출 수만 있다면, 내 생각에는 제수씨에게도 큰 위안이 될 거요.”"]], [["\"Bernard and Bell!\"", "“버나드와 벨이라니요!”"], ["exclaimed Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 소리쳤다."], ["No idea of such a union had ever yet come upon her, and now in her surprise she sat silent.", "그런 짝을 지어 볼 생각은 여태 한 번도 그녀에게 떠오른 적이 없었고, 이제 놀라움에 그녀는 말없이 앉아 있었다."], ["She had always liked Bernard Dale, having felt for him more family affection than for any other of the Dale family beyond her own hearth.", "그녀는 버나드 데일을 늘 좋아해 왔고, 제 집 울타리 밖의 데일 집안 사람들 가운데 누구보다도 그에게 집안다운 정을 느껴 왔다."], ["He had been very intimate in her house, having made himself almost as a brother to her girls.", "그는 그녀의 집을 무척 허물없이 드나들었고, 제 딸들에게 거의 오라비나 다름없이 굴어 왔다."], ["But she had never thought of him as a husband for either of them.", "그러나 그를 두 아이 가운데 누구의 남편으로도 생각해 본 적은 한 번도 없었다."]], [["\"Then Bell has not spoken to you about it,\"", "“그러면 벨이 제수씨에게 그 이야기를 하지 않았구려.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"Never a word.\"", "“한마디도 없었어요.”"]], [["\"And you had never thought about it?\"", "“그리고 제수씨도 그런 생각은 한 번도 해 본 적이 없고?”"]], [["\"Certainly not.\"", "“물론 없지요.”"]], [["\"I have thought about it a great deal.", "“나는 그 생각을 아주 많이 했소."], ["For some years I have always been thinking of it.", "몇 해째 늘 그 생각을 해 왔소."], ["I have set my heart upon it, and shall be very unhappy if it cannot be brought about.", "거기에 내 마음을 걸어 두었고, 그것이 이루어지지 않는다면 나는 몹시 불행할 거요."], ["They are both very dear to me,--dearer than anybody else.", "그 둘은 나에게 다 소중하오. 다른 누구보다도 소중하지."], ["If I could see them man and wife, I should not much care then how soon I left the old place to them.\"", "그 둘이 부부가 된 것을 볼 수만 있다면, 그 옛집을 그 아이들에게 아무리 일찍 물려주고 떠나게 된들 나는 크게 개의치 않을 거요.”"]], [["There was a purer touch of feeling in this than the squire had ever before shown in his sister-in-law's presence, and more heartiness than she had given him the credit of possessing.", "이 말에는 지주가 제 제수 앞에서 여태 보인 적이 없는 한결 맑은 감정의 결이 있었고, 그녀가 그에게 있으리라고 쳐 준 것보다 더한 살가움이 있었다."], ["And she could not but acknowledge to herself that her own child was included in this unexpected warmth of love, and that she was bound at any rate to entertain some gratitude for such kindness.", "그리고 그녀는 뜻밖의 이 따뜻한 사랑 안에 제 자식이 들어 있다는 것을, 그런 다정함에 어떻든 얼마간의 고마움은 품어야 마땅하다는 것을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["\"It is good of you to think of her,\"", "“그 애를 그렇게 생각해 주시니 고맙습니다.”"], ["said the mother; \"very good.\"", "어머니가 말했다. “참으로 고마운 일이에요.”"]], [["\"I think a great deal about her,\"", "“나는 그 아이 생각을 많이 하오.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"But that does not much matter now.", "“하지만 지금 그건 그리 중요한 게 아니고."], ["The fact is, that she has declined Bernard's offer.\"", "실은 그 아이가 버나드의 청혼을 물리쳤소.”"]], [["\"Has Bernard offered to her?\"", "“버나드가 그 애한테 청혼을 했나요?”"]], [["\"So he tells me; and she has refused him.", "“그 아이가 나한테 그렇게 말하더군."], ["It may perhaps be natural that she should do so, never having taught herself to look at him in the light of a lover.", "그리고 벨이 그 아이를 물리쳤다고. 여태 그 아이를 연인으로 볼 마음을 먹어 본 적이 없으니 그러는 것이 자연스러운 일인지도 모르지."], ["I don't blame her at all.", "나는 벨을 조금도 나무라지 않소."], ["I am not angry with her.\"", "그 아이한테 화가 난 것도 아니고.”"]], [["\"Angry with her! No.", "“화가 나시다니요!"], ["You can hardly be angry with her for not being in love with her cousin.\"", "그럴 리가요. 사촌을 사랑하지 않는다고 그 애한테 화를 내실 수야 없지요.”"]], [["\"I say that I am not angry with her.", "“나는 그 아이한테 화가 나지 않았다고 말하고 있소."], ["But I think she might undertake to consider the question.", "하지만 그 문제를 한번 헤아려 보겠다고는 할 수 있지 않겠소."], ["You would like such a match, would you not?\"", "제수씨도 그런 혼처라면 좋아할 것 아니오?”"]], [["Mrs. Dale did not at first make any answer, but began to revolve the thing in her mind, and to look at it in various points of view.", "데일 부인은 처음에는 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 그 일을 마음속에서 굴려 보며 이런저런 쪽에서 바라보기 시작했다."], ["There was a great deal in such an arrangement which at the first sight recommended it to her very strongly.", "그런 짜임에는 첫눈에도 그녀의 마음을 아주 강하게 끄는 데가 많았다."], ["All the local circumstances were in its favour.", "이곳의 온갖 형편이 그것을 편들었다."], ["As regarded herself it would promise to her all that she had ever desired.", "제 자신에 관해 말하자면 그것은 그녀가 여태 바라 온 모든 것을 약속해 주었다."], ["It would give her a prospect of seeing very much of Lily; for if Bell were settled at the old family house, Crosbie would naturally be much with his friend.", "그것은 릴리를 아주 자주 볼 수 있는 앞날을 열어 줄 것이었다. 벨이 그 오래된 집안의 저택에 자리를 잡는다면 크로즈비도 제 벗을 찾아 자주 와 있을 것이기 때문이었다."], ["She liked Bernard also; and for a moment or two fancied, as she turned it all over in her mind, that, even yet, if such a marriage were to take place, there might grow up something like true regard between her and the old squire.", "그녀는 버나드도 좋아했다. 그래서 마음속으로 이리저리 굴려 보는 동안 한두 순간은, 그런 혼인이 이루어진다면 이제라도 저와 늙은 지주 사이에 참된 정 비슷한 것이 자라날지도 모른다는 생각까지 들었다."], ["How happy would be her old age in that Small House, if Bell with her children were living so close to her!", "벨이 제 아이들을 데리고 저와 그토록 가까이 살아 준다면 저 작은 집에서의 늘그막이 얼마나 행복하겠는가!"]], [["\"Well?\"", "“어떻소?”"], ["said the squire, who was looking very intently into her face.", "지주가 그녀의 얼굴을 몹시 뚫어지게 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["\"I was thinking,\"", "“생각을 좀 하고 있었어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Do you say that she has already refused him?\"", "“그 애가 벌써 그이를 물리쳤다고 하셨지요?”"]], [["\"I am afraid she has; but then you know--\"", "“아무래도 그런 모양이오. 하지만 제수씨도 알다시피—”"]], [["\"It must of course be left for her to judge.\"", "“그건 물론 그 애가 판단하도록 맡겨 두어야 할 일이지요.”"]], [["\"If you mean that she cannot be made to marry her cousin, of course we all know she can't.\"", "“사촌과 억지로 혼인시킬 수는 없다는 뜻이라면, 그야 물론 우리 모두 그럴 수 없다는 걸 알고 있소.”"]], [["\"I mean rather more than that.\"", "“저는 그것보다 좀 더한 뜻으로 말씀드린 거예요.”"]], [["\"What do you mean, then?\"", "“그러면 무슨 뜻이오?”"]], [["\"That the matter must be left altogether to her own decision; that no persuasion must be used by you or me.", "“그 문제는 온전히 그 애가 결정하도록 맡겨 두어야 한다는 거예요. 시아주버니도 저도 그 애를 구슬려서는 안 된다는 거지요."], ["If he can persuade her, indeed--\"", "그이가 그 애를 구슬릴 수 있다면야 물론—”"]], [["\"Yes, exactly.", "“그렇지, 바로 그거요."], ["He must persuade her.", "그 아이가 벨을 구슬려야지."], ["I quite agree with you that he should have liberty to plead his own cause.", "그 아이가 제 뜻을 펼칠 자리를 얻어야 한다는 데는 나도 아주 동감이오."], ["But look you here, Mary;--she has always been a very good child to you--\"", "하지만 이걸 좀 보시오, 제수씨. 그 아이는 늘 제수씨에게 아주 착한 딸이었소—”"]], [["\"Indeed she has.\"", "“그럼요, 그랬지요.”"]], [["\"And a word from you would go a long way with her,--as it ought.", "“그리고 제수씨의 한마디는 그 아이에게 큰 힘이 될 거요."], ["If she knows that you would like her to marry her cousin, it will make her think it her duty--\"", "마땅히 그래야 하고. 제 어미가 사촌과 혼인하기를 바란다는 걸 알면 그 아이는 그것을 제 도리로 여길 테고—”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["but that is just what I cannot try to make her think.\"", "하지만 바로 그것이 제가 그 애더러 그렇게 생각하도록 해 볼 수 없는 일이에요.”"]], [["\"Will you let me speak, Mary?", "“내 말을 좀 끝까지 하게 해 주겠소, 제수씨?"], ["You take me up and scold me before the words are half out of my mouth.", "내 입에서 말이 반도 나오기 전에 붙잡고 늘어져 나를 나무라는구려."], ["Of course I know that in these days a young lady is not to be compelled into marrying anybody;--not but that, as far as I can see, they did better than they do now when they had not quite so much of their own way.\"", "요즘 세상에 젊은 처녀더러 아무하고나 혼인하라고 억지로 시킬 수 없다는 거야 나도 물론 알고 있소. 하기야 내가 보기에는 처녀들이 지금처럼 제멋대로 하지 못하던 시절이 지금보다 나았지만.”"]], [["\"I never would take upon myself to ask a child to marry any man.\"", "“저는 자식더러 어떤 남자와 혼인하라고 청하는 일은 결코 떠맡지 않겠어요.”"]], [["\"But you may explain to her that it is her duty to give such a proposal much thought before it is absolutely refused.", "“하지만 그런 청혼을 아주 물리쳐 버리기 전에 깊이 생각해 보는 것이 제 도리라는 것쯤은 일러 줄 수 있지 않소."], ["A girl either is in love or she is not.", "처녀란 사랑에 빠져 있거나 아니거나 둘 중 하나요."], ["If she is, she is ready to jump down a man's throat; and that was the case with Lily.\"", "사랑에 빠져 있으면 사내의 목구멍으로라도 뛰어들 채비가 되어 있지. 릴리가 그런 경우였고.”"]], [["\"She never thought of the man till he had proposed to her fully.\"", "“그 애는 그이가 온전히 청혼을 하기 전까지는 그이를 생각해 본 적도 없어요.”"]], [["\"Well, never mind now.", "“뭐, 그건 이제 됐소."], ["But if a girl is not in love, she thinks she is bound to swear and declare that she never will be so.\"", "하지만 처녀가 사랑에 빠져 있지 않으면, 저는 결코 사랑에 빠지지 않으리라고 맹세하고 다짐해야만 하는 줄로 안단 말이오.”"]], [["\"I don't think Bell ever declared anything of the kind.\"", "“벨이 그런 걸 다짐했다고는 생각하지 않아요.”"]], [["\"Yes, she did.", "“아니, 다짐했소."], ["She told Bernard that she didn't love him and couldn't love him,--and, in fact, that she wouldn't think anything more about it.", "그 아이는 버나드한테 그를 사랑하지 않고 사랑할 수도 없다고, 그리고 사실 그 일은 더 생각해 보지도 않겠다고 말했소."], ["Now, Mary, that's what I call being headstrong and positive.", "자, 제수씨, 그런 걸 두고 나는 고집스럽고 딱 잘라 말한다고 하는 거요."], ["I don't want to drive her, and I don't want you to drive her.", "나는 그 아이를 몰아붙일 생각이 없고, 제수씨가 몰아붙이는 것도 바라지 않소."], ["But here is an arrangement which for her will be a very good one; you must admit that.", "하지만 여기 그 아이에게 아주 좋은 자리가 마련되어 있단 말이오. 그건 제수씨도 인정해야지."], ["We all know that she is on excellent terms with Bernard.", "그 아이가 버나드와 더할 나위 없이 잘 지내 온 것은 우리 모두 알고 있소."], ["It isn't as though they had been falling out and hating each other all their lives.", "평생 서로 틀어지고 미워하며 지내 온 사이가 아니란 말이오."], ["She told him that she was very fond of him, and talked nonsense about being his sister, and all that.\"", "그 아이는 버나드한테 그를 아주 좋아한다고 말했고, 누이가 되어 주겠다는 둥 부질없는 소리를 늘어놓았다더군.”"]], [["\"I don't see that it was nonsense at all.\"", "“저는 그게 부질없는 소리라고는 조금도 생각하지 않아요.”"]], [["\"Yes, it was nonsense,--on such an occasion.", "“아니, 부질없는 소리요. 그런 자리에서는 말이오."], ["If a man asks a girl to marry him, he doesn't want her to talk to him about being his sister.", "사내가 처녀한테 혼인해 달라고 청할 때는 누이가 되어 주겠다는 이야기를 듣자는 게 아니오."], ["I think it is nonsense.", "나는 그게 부질없는 소리라고 보오."], ["If she would only consider about it properly she would soon learn to love him.\"", "그 아이가 그 일을 제대로 헤아려 보기만 한다면 곧 그를 사랑하는 법을 배울 텐데.”"]], [["\"That lesson, if it be learned at all, must be learned without any tutor.\"", "“그런 배움이란, 배워지는 것이라면, 어떤 스승도 없이 배워지는 것이지요.”"]], [["\"You won't do anything to help me then?\"", "“그러면 나를 도와주는 일은 아무것도 하지 않겠다는 거요?”"]], [["\"I will, at any rate, do nothing to mar you.", "“적어도 시아주버니를 그르치는 일은 아무것도 하지 않겠어요."], ["And, to tell the truth, I must think over the matter fully before I can decide what I had better say to Bell about it.", "그리고 사실대로 말씀드리자면, 벨에게 이 일을 두고 무슨 말을 하는 것이 나을지 정하기 전에 저도 이 문제를 넉넉히 생각해 봐야겠어요."], ["From her not speaking to me--\"", "그 애가 저한테 말하지 않은 걸 보면—”"]], [["\"I think she ought to have told you.\"", "“그 아이는 제 어미한테 말했어야 하오.”"]], [["\"No, Mr. Dale.", "“아니에요, 시아주버니."], ["Had she accepted him, of course she would have told me.", "그 애가 그이를 받아들였다면 물론 저한테 말했겠지요."], ["Had she thought of doing so she might probably have consulted me.", "받아들일 생각이 있었다면 아마 저와 의논했을 거고요."], ["But if she made up her mind that she must reject him--\"", "하지만 그이를 물리쳐야겠다고 마음을 굳혔다면—”"]], [["\"She oughtn't to have made up her mind.\"", "“마음을 굳히지 말았어야지.”"], ["\"But if she did, it seems natural to me that she should speak of it to no one.", "“하지만 굳혔다면, 아무한테도 그 이야기를 하지 않는 것이 저에게는 자연스러워 보여요."], ["She might probably think that Bernard would be as well pleased that it should not be known.\"", "그 애는 아마 버나드도 그 일이 알려지지 않는 편을 좋아하리라고 생각했을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Psha,--known!--of course it will be known.", "“쳇, 알려진다니! 그야 물론 알려지고말고."], ["As you want time to consider of it, I will say nothing more now.", "생각할 시간이 필요하다니 지금은 더 말하지 않겠소."], ["If she were my daughter, I should have no hesitation in telling her what I thought best for her welfare.\"", "벨이 내 딸이었다면 나는 그 아이의 앞날에 무엇이 가장 좋은지를 일러 주는 데 조금도 망설이지 않았을 거요.”"]], [["\"I have none; though I may have some in making up my mind as to what is best for her welfare.", "“저도 망설이지 않아요. 다만 그 애의 앞날에 무엇이 가장 좋은지 마음을 정하는 데는 얼마간 망설일지도 모르지요."], ["But, Mr. Dale, you may be sure of this; I will speak to her very earnestly of your kindness and love for her.", "하지만 시아주버니, 이것만은 믿으셔도 좋아요."], ["And I wish you would believe that I feel your regard for her very strongly.\"", "저는 그 애에게 시아주버니의 다정함과 사랑을 아주 간곡히 이야기하겠어요. 그리고 그 애를 아껴 주시는 그 마음을 제가 아주 강하게 느끼고 있다는 것도 믿어 주셨으면 해요.”"]], [["In answer to this he merely shook his head, and hummed and hawed.", "이 말에 그는 그저 고개를 저으며 흥흥 헛기침만 했다."], ["\"You would be glad to see them married, as regards yourself?\"", "“제수씨 자신으로 말하자면, 그 아이들이 혼인하는 것을 기꺼워하겠소?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["\"Certainly I would,\"", "“물론 기꺼워하지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I have always liked Bernard, and I believe my girl would be safe with him.", "“저는 버나드를 늘 좋아했고, 제 아이가 그이 곁에서 안전하리라고 믿어요."], ["But then, you see, it's a question on which my own likings or dislikings should not have any bearing.\"", "하지만 아시다시피 이건 제가 좋아하고 싫어하는 것이 조금이라도 좌우해서는 안 되는 문제잖아요.”"]], [["And so they parted, the squire making his way back again through the drawing-room window.", "그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌고, 지주는 응접실 창을 통해 다시 밖으로 나갔다."], ["He was not above half pleased with his interview; but then he was a man for whom half-pleasure almost sufficed.", "그는 이 만남이 절반쯤밖에 흡족하지 않았다. 그러나 그는 절반의 흡족만으로도 거의 넉넉한 사람이었다."], ["He rarely indulged any expectation that people would make themselves agreeable to him.", "그는 사람들이 저에게 살갑게 굴어 주리라고 기대하는 일이 좀처럼 없었다."], ["Mrs. Dale, since she had come to the Small House, had never been a source of satisfaction to him, but he did not on that account regret that he had brought her there.", "데일 부인은 작은 집에 들어온 뒤로 한 번도 그에게 만족의 원천이 되어 준 적이 없었으나, 그렇다고 그녀를 그리로 데려온 것을 후회하지는 않았다."], ["He was a constant man; urgent in carrying out his own plans, but not sanguine in doing so, and by no means apt to expect that all things would go smooth with him.", "그는 한결같은 사람이었다. 제 뜻을 밀고 나가는 데는 끈질겼으나 그러면서도 낙관하지 않았고, 모든 일이 저에게 순조로우리라고 기대하는 사람도 결코 아니었다."], ["He had made up his mind that his nephew and his niece should be married, and should he ultimately fail in this, such failure would probably embitter his future life;--but it was not in the nature of the man to be angry in the meantime, or to fume and scold because he met with opposition.", "그는 제 조카와 조카딸을 혼인시키기로 마음을 굳혔고, 끝내 이 일에 실패한다면 그 실패는 아마 그의 남은 삶을 쓰디쓰게 만들 것이었다. 그러나 그동안 성을 내거나, 반대에 부딪혔다고 씨근거리며 나무라는 것은 그의 됨됨이가 아니었다."], ["He had told Mrs. Dale that he loved Bell dearly.", "그는 데일 부인에게 벨을 몹시 사랑한다고 말했다."], ["So he did, though he seldom spoke to her with much show of special regard, and never was soft and tender with her.", "정말 그러했다. 비록 그가 벨에게 각별한 정을 드러내며 말하는 일은 드물었고, 그녀에게 다정하고 부드러웠던 적은 한 번도 없었지만."], ["But, on the other hand, he did not now love her the less because she opposed his wishes.", "그러나 한편으로 벨이 제 뜻을 거스른다고 해서 이제 와 그녀를 덜 사랑하지도 않았다."], ["He was a constant, undemonstrative man, given rather to brooding than to thinking; harder in his words than in his thoughts, with more of heart than others believed, or than he himself knew; but, above all, he was a man who having once desired a thing would desire it always.", "그는 한결같고 무뚝뚝한 사람이었으며, 생각하기보다는 곱씹는 쪽이었고, 속에 품은 것보다 입에 낸 말이 더 모질었으며, 남들이 믿는 것보다, 아니 제 자신이 아는 것보다 더 많은 마음을 지닌 사람이었다. 그러나 무엇보다도 그는 한번 바란 것은 언제까지나 바라는 사람이었다."]], [["Mrs. Dale, when she was left alone, began to turn over the question in her mind in a much fuller manner than the squire's presence had as yet made possible for her.", "데일 부인은 혼자 남게 되자 지주가 앞에 있을 때에 할 수 있었던 것보다 훨씬 더 넉넉하게 그 문제를 마음속에서 굴려 보기 시작했다."], ["Would not such a marriage as this be for them all the happiest domestic arrangement which circumstances could afford?", "이런 혼인이야말로 형편이 허락할 수 있는 것 가운데 그들 모두에게 가장 행복한 살림의 짜임이 아니겠는가?"], ["Her daughter would have no fortune, but here would be prepared for her all the comforts which fortune can give.", "제 딸은 지참할 재산이 없을 터이나, 재산이 줄 수 있는 온갖 안락이 이미 그 아이를 위해 마련되어 있을 것이었다."], ["She would be received into her uncle's house, not as some penniless, portionless bride whom Bernard might have married and brought home, but as the wife whom of all others Bernard's friends had thought desirable for him.", "그 아이는 버나드가 혼인해서 데려온 한 푼 없고 밑천 없는 신부로서가 아니라, 버나드의 벗들이 누구보다도 그에게 어울린다고 여겨 온 아내로서 삼촌의 집에 맞아들여질 것이었다."], ["And then, as regarded Mrs. Dale herself, there would be nothing in such a marriage which would not be delightful to her.", "그리고 데일 부인 자신에 관해 말하자면, 그런 혼인에는 그녀를 기쁘게 하지 않을 것이라고는 하나도 없었다."], ["It would give a realization to all her dreams of future happiness.", "그것은 앞날의 행복에 관한 그녀의 모든 꿈을 이루어 줄 것이었다."]], [["But, as she said to herself over and over again, all that must go for nothing.", "그러나 몇 번이고 스스로에게 되뇌었듯이, 그 모든 것은 셈에 넣어서는 안 되는 것이었다."], ["It must be for Bell, and for her only, to answer Bernard's question.", "버나드의 물음에 답하는 것은 벨이라야 하고, 오직 벨뿐이라야 했다."], ["In her mind there was something sacred in that idea of love.", "그녀의 마음속에서 사랑이라는 그 관념에는 무언가 거룩한 데가 있었다."], ["She would regard her daughter almost as a castaway if she were to marry any man without absolutely loving him,--loving him as Lily loved her lover, with all her heart and all her strength.", "제 딸이 어떤 남자를 온전히 사랑하지도 않으면서 그와 혼인한다면, 그녀는 그 딸을 거의 몸을 버린 사람처럼 여길 것이었다. 릴리가 제 연인을 사랑하듯 온 마음과 온 힘을 다해 사랑하는 것이 아니라면 말이다."]], [["With such a conviction as this strong upon her, she felt that she could not say much to Bell that would be of any service.", "이런 확신이 이토록 강하게 그녀를 사로잡고 있었으므로, 그녀는 벨에게 무슨 말을 한들 도움이 될 것 같지 않다고 느꼈다."]], [["# CHAPTER XX. DR. CROFTS.", "# 제20장. 크로프츠 선생"]], [["If there was anything in the world as to which Isabella Dale was quite certain, it was this--that she was not in love with Dr. Crofts.", "이저벨라 데일이 이 세상에서 아주 확실하게 알고 있는 것이 하나라도 있었다면, 그것은 바로 이것이었다 — 자기는 크로프츠 선생과 사랑에 빠진 것이 아니라는 것."], ["As to being in love with her cousin Bernard, she had never had occasion to ask herself any question on that head.", "사촌 버나드와 사랑에 빠졌느냐 하는 문제라면, 그 점에 관해서는 스스로에게 물음을 던져 볼 계기조차 가져 본 적이 없었다."], ["She liked him very well, but she had never thought of marrying him; and now, when he made his proposal, she could not bring herself to think of it.", "그를 무척 좋아하기는 했으나 그와 결혼하겠다는 생각은 해 본 적이 없었고, 이제 그가 청혼을 해 온 지금에도 그것을 마음에 담아 볼 수가 없었다."], ["But as regards Dr. Crofts, she had thought of it, and had made up her mind;--in the manner above described.", "그러나 크로프츠 선생에 관해서라면 그녀는 생각을 해 보았고, 마음을 정해 두었다 — 앞서 말한 그런 식으로."]], [["It may be said that she could not have been justified in discussing the matter even within her own bosom, unless authorized to do so by Dr. Crofts himself.", "크로프츠 선생 자신에게서 그럴 만한 권한을 받은 것이 아니라면, 제 가슴속에서라도 그 문제를 따져 보는 일이 그녀에게 온당했을 리 없다고 말할 사람이 있을지도 모른다."], ["Let it then be considered that Dr. Crofts had given her some such authority.", "그렇다면 크로프츠 선생이 그녀에게 그만한 권한을 얼마쯤 주었다고 여겨 두기로 하자."], ["This may be done in more ways than one; and Miss Dale could not have found herself asking herself questions about him, unless there had been fitting occasion for her to do so.", "그런 일이 이루어지는 길은 한 가지만이 아니다. 그리고 그렇게 할 만한 마땅한 계기가 없었더라면, 데일 양이 스스로에게 그에 관한 물음을 던지고 있을 리도 없었을 것이다."]], [["The profession of a medical man in a small provincial town is not often one which gives to its owner in early life a large income.", "작은 지방 도시의 의사라는 직업은 그 임자에게 젊은 시절부터 큰 수입을 안겨 주는 일이 흔치 않다."], ["Perhaps in no career has a man to work harder for what he earns, or to do more work without earning anything.", "아마 어떤 길에서도 사람이 버는 것에 견주어 이토록 고되게 일해야 하거나, 한 푼도 못 벌면서 이토록 많은 일을 해야 하는 경우는 없을 것이다."], ["It has sometimes seemed to me as though the young doctors and the old doctors had agreed to divide between them the different results of their profession,--the young doctors doing all the work and the old doctors taking all the money.", "나에게는 이따금, 젊은 의사와 늙은 의사가 저희 직업의 소출을 서로 나누어 갖기로 합의라도 한 것처럼 보였다 — 젊은 의사는 일을 죄다 하고, 늙은 의사는 돈을 죄다 가져가는 식으로."], ["If this be so it may account for that appearance of premature gravity which is borne by so many of the medical profession.", "만약 그렇다면, 의사라는 직업에 몸담은 이들 상당수가 지니고 있는 저 때 이른 근엄함도 그것으로 설명이 될 것이다."], ["Under such an arrangement a man may be excused for a desire to put away childish things very early in life.", "그런 협정 아래에서라면 사람이 인생의 아주 이른 시기에 어린아이 같은 것들을 치워 버리고 싶어 한대도 나무랄 수 없는 일이다."]], [["Dr. Crofts had now been practising in Guestwick nearly seven years, having settled himself in that town when he was twenty-three years old, and being at this period about thirty.", "크로프츠 선생은 이 무렵 게스트윅에서 개업한 지 거의 칠 년이 되어 가고 있었다."], ["During those seven years his skill and industry had been so fully admitted that he had succeeded in obtaining the medical care of all the paupers in the union, for which work he was paid at the rate of one hundred pounds a year.", "스물세 살에 그 도시에 자리를 잡았으니 지금은 서른쯤 되어 있었다. 그 칠 년 동안 그의 의술과 근면은 워낙 널리 인정을 받아, 구빈 조합에 속한 극빈자 전부의 진료를 맡는 자리를 얻어 내는 데 성공했고, 그 일로 그가 받는 보수는 연 백 파운드였다."], ["He was also assistant-surgeon at a small hospital which was maintained in that town, and held two or three other similar public positions, all of which attested his respectability and general proficiency.", "또한 그 도시에 유지되고 있던 작은 병원의 부속 외과의였으며, 그 밖에도 비슷한 공직을 두세 개 더 맡고 있었으니, 그것들은 모두 그의 반듯함과 두루 능한 솜씨를 입증해 주는 것이었다."], ["They, moreover, thoroughly saved him from any of the dangers of idleness; but, unfortunately, they did not enable him to regard himself as a successful professional man.", "게다가 그것들은 그를 게으름의 위험에서 완전히 지켜 주었다. 그러나 불행하게도, 그것들이 그로 하여금 스스로를 성공한 전문직 인사로 여기게 해 주지는 못했다."], ["Whereas old Dr. Gruffen, of whom but few people spoke well, had made a fortune in Guestwick, and even still drew from the ailments of the town a considerable and hardly yet decreasing income.", "반면에 좋게 말하는 사람이 거의 없던 늙은 그러펀 선생은 게스트윅에서 재산을 모았고, 지금까지도 이 도시의 온갖 병에서 상당한, 그리고 아직 별로 줄지도 않은 수입을 끌어내고 있었다."], ["Now this was hard upon Dr. Crofts--unless there was existing some such well-understood arrangement as that above named.", "자, 이것은 크로프츠 선생에게 가혹한 일이었다 — 앞서 말한 것과 같은 저 널리 이해되는 협정이 실제로 존재하는 것이 아니라면 말이다."]], [["He had been known to the family of the Dales long previous to his settlement at Guestwick, and had been very intimate with them from that time to the present day.", "그는 게스트윅에 자리 잡기 훨씬 전부터 데일 집안과 알고 지냈고, 그때부터 오늘에 이르기까지 그들과 아주 가깝게 지내 왔다."], ["Of all the men, young or old, whom Mrs. Dale counted among her intimate friends, he was the one whom she most trusted and admired.", "데일 부인이 절친한 벗으로 꼽는 남자들 가운데, 젊은이든 늙은이든, 그녀가 가장 신뢰하고 가장 우러르는 사람이 바로 그였다."], ["And he was a man to be trusted by those who knew him well.", "그리고 그는 자기를 잘 아는 사람들에게 신뢰받을 만한 사람이었다."], ["He was not bright and always ready, as was Crosbie, nor had he all the practical worldly good sense of Bernard Dale.", "그는 크로즈비처럼 재기가 번득이고 늘 재바르지는 않았고, 버나드 데일이 지닌 실제적이고 세속적인 분별을 다 갖추지도 못했다."], ["In mental power I doubt whether he was superior to John Eames;--to John Eames, such as he might become when the period of his hobbledehoyhood should have altogether passed away.", "지력으로 말하자면 그가 존 임스보다 나았는지 나는 의심스럽다 — 풋내기 시절이 아주 지나가 버렸을 때의 존 임스 말이다."], ["But Crofts, compared with the other three, as they all were at present, was a man more to be trusted than any of them.", "그러나 크로프츠는, 지금 이 시점의 다른 세 사람과 견주자면, 그 누구보다도 더 믿을 만한 사람이었다."], ["And there was, moreover, about him an occasional dash of humour, without which Mrs. Dale would hardly have regarded him with that thorough liking which she had for him.", "게다가 그에게는 이따금 익살이 한 자락 번뜩이는 데가 있었으니, 그것이 없었더라면 데일 부인이 그를 그토록 진심으로 좋아하지는 않았을 것이다."], ["But it was a quiet humour, apt to show itself when he had but one friend with him, rather than in general society.", "그러나 그것은 조용한 익살이어서, 여럿이 어울린 자리보다는 벗 하나를 앞에 두었을 때 곧잘 드러나곤 했다."], ["Crosbie, on the other hand, would be much more bright among a dozen, than he could with a single companion.", "반면 크로즈비는 열두어 사람 사이에 있을 때가 단둘이 있을 때보다 훨씬 더 빛났다. 버나드 데일은 빛난 적이 없었다."], ["Bernard Dale was never bright; and as for Johnny Eames--; but in this matter of brightness, Johnny Eames had not yet shown to the world what his character might be.", "그리고 조니 임스로 말하자면 — 그러나 이 빛남이라는 문제에 관해서라면, 조니 임스는 아직 제 사람됨이 어떠한지를 세상에 보여 준 바가 없었다."]], [["It was now two years since Crofts had been called upon for medical advice on behalf of his friend Mrs.", "크로프츠가 벗인 데일 부인을 위해 의사로서 불려 간 것은 이제 두 해 전의 일이었다."], ["Dale. She had then been ill for a long period--some two or three months, and Dr.", "그때 그녀는 오랫동안, 두석 달쯤 앓아누워 있었고, 크로프츠 선생은 앨링턴을 자주 드나들었다."], ["Crofts had been frequent in his visits at Allington.", "그 무렵 그는 데일 부인의 두 딸과 아주 가까워졌으며, 특히 큰딸과 그러했다."], ["At that time he became very intimate with Mrs. Dale's daughters, and especially so with the eldest.", "결혼하지 않은 젊은 의사는 어쩌면 젊은 아가씨가 있는 집에서 배제되어야 마땅할지도 모른다."], ["Young unmarried doctors ought perhaps to be excluded from houses in which there are young ladies.", "적어도 나는 많은 슬기로운 부인네들이 그 견해를 아주 굳게 붙들고 있다는 것을 안다."], ["I know, at any rate, that many sage matrons hold very strongly to that opinion, thinking, no doubt, that doctors ought to get themselves married before they venture to begin working for a living.", "의사란 밥벌이를 시작하겠다고 나서기 전에 먼저 장가부터 들어야 한다고 여기는 것이 틀림없다. 데일 부인은 아마 제 딸들을 아직 어린애로만 여겼을 것이다."], ["Mrs. Dale, perhaps, regarded her own girls as still merely children, for Bell, the elder, was then hardly eighteen; or perhaps she held imprudent and heterodox opinions on this subject; or it may be that she selfishly preferred Dr.", "큰딸 벨이 그때 겨우 열여덟이었으니 말이다."], ["Crofts, with all the danger to her children, to Dr. Gruffen, with all the danger to herself.", "아니면 이 문제에 관해 조심성 없고 정통에서 벗어난 견해를 지니고 있었는지도 모른다."], ["But the result was that the young doctor one day informed himself, as he was riding back to Guestwick, that much of his happiness in this world would depend on his being able to marry Mrs.", "아니면 이기적이게도, 제 자신에게 온갖 위험이 따르는 그러펀 선생보다 제 아이들에게 온갖 위험이 따르는 크로프츠 선생을 더 좋아했는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 그 결과는 이러했다."], ["Dale's eldest daughter. At that time his total income amounted to little more than two hundred a year, and he had resolved within his own mind that Dr.", "어느 날 이 젊은 의사는 게스트윅으로 말을 타고 돌아가면서, 이 세상에서 자기가 누릴 행복의 많은 부분이 데일 부인의 큰딸과 결혼할 수 있느냐에 달려 있다는 것을 스스로 깨달았다."], ["Gruffen was esteemed as much the better doctor by the general public opinion of Guestwick, and that Dr. Gruffen's sandy-haired assistant would even have a better chance of success in the town than himself, should it ever come to pass that the doctor was esteemed too old for personal practice.", "그때 그의 수입 전부는 연 이백 파운드가 조금 넘는 정도였고, 게스트윅의 일반 여론은 그러펀 선생을 훨씬 나은 의사로 쳐 준다는 것, 그리고 언젠가 그 의사가 몸소 진료를 보기에는 너무 늙었다는 말을 듣게 되는 날이 온다 해도 그러펀 선생의 모래빛 머리를 한 조수가 자기보다 이 도시에서 성공할 가망이 더 크리라는 것을, 그는 마음속으로 결론지어 두고 있었다."], ["Crofts had no fortune of his own, and he was aware that Miss Dale had none.", "크로프츠에게는 물려받은 재산이 없었고, 데일 양에게도 없다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["Then, under those circumstances, what was he to do?", "그렇다면 그런 형편에서 그가 어찌해야 한단 말인가?"]], [["It is not necessary that we should inquire at any great length into those love passages of the doctor's life which took place three years before the commencement of this narrative.", "이 이야기가 시작되기 삼 년 전에 있었던 이 의사의 연애 대목을 우리가 길게 캐물을 필요는 없다. 그는 벨에게 아무런 고백도 하지 않았다."], ["He made no declaration to Bell; but Bell, young as she was, understood well that he would fain have done so, had not his courage failed him, or rather had not his prudence prevented him. To Mrs.", "그러나 벨은, 그토록 어린 나이였음에도, 용기가 그를 저버리지만 않았더라면, 아니 오히려 신중함이 그를 막지만 않았더라면 그가 기꺼이 그리했으리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Dale he did speak, not openly avowing his love even to her, but hinting at it, and then talking to her of his unsatisfied hopes and professional disappointments.", "데일 부인에게는 그가 말을 하기는 했다. 그녀에게조차 제 사랑을 대놓고 밝히지는 않고 넌지시 비치기만 했으며, 그러고는 이루지 못한 희망과 직업에서 겪은 실망을 그녀에게 이야기했다."], ["\"It is not that I complain of being poor as I am,\"", "“제가 지금처럼 가난한 것을 두고 푸념하는 것은 아닙니다.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he; \"or at any rate, not so poor that my poverty must be any source of discomfort to me; but I could hardly marry with such an income as I have at present.\"", "“적어도 제 가난이 저에게 불편의 씨앗이 될 만큼 그렇게 가난하지는 않습니다. 다만 지금 제가 가진 정도의 수입으로는 도무지 결혼을 할 수가 없지요.”"]], [["\"But it will increase, will it not?\"", "“하지만 수입은 늘어나지 않겠어요?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"It may some day, when I am becoming an old man,\"", "“언젠가는 늘겠지요, 제가 늙은이가 되어 갈 무렵에는.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"But of what use will it be to me then?\"", "“하지만 그때 가서 그게 저에게 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까?”"]], [["Mrs. Dale could not tell him that, as far as her voice in the matter went, he was welcome to woo her daughter and marry her, poor as he was, and doubly poor as they would both be together on such a pittance.", "데일 부인으로서는, 자기 뜻이 미치는 한, 그가 지금처럼 가난하더라도, 또 그런 쥐꼬리만 한 수입으로 둘이 함께라면 갑절로 가난해질 터이지만, 얼마든지 제 딸에게 구애하고 결혼해도 좋다고 그에게 말해 줄 수가 없었다."], ["He had not even mentioned Bell's name, and had he done so she could only have bade him wait and hope.", "그는 벨의 이름을 입에 올리지도 않았고, 설령 올렸다 한들 그녀는 기다리며 희망을 품으라고 이를 수밖에 없었을 것이다."], ["After that he said nothing further to her upon the subject.", "그 뒤로 그는 그 문제에 관해 그녀에게 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["To Bell he spoke no word of overt love; but on an autumn day, when Mrs.", "벨에게는 드러내 놓고 사랑을 말한 적이 한마디도 없었다."], ["Dale was already convalescent, and the repetition of his professional visits had become unnecessary, he got her to walk with him through the half-hidden shrubbery paths, and then told her things which he should never have told her, if he really wished to bind her heart to his.", "그러나 어느 가을날, 데일 부인이 이미 회복기에 접어들어 그의 왕진이 더는 필요하지 않게 되었을 무렵, 그는 벨을 이끌고 반쯤 가려진 관목 숲 오솔길을 함께 걸었고, 그러고는 그녀의 마음을 제 마음에 묶어 두기를 정말로 바랐다면 결코 해서는 안 될 이야기들을 그녀에게 했다."], ["He repeated that story of his income, and explained to her that his poverty was only grievous to him in that it prevented him from thinking of marriage.", "그는 제 수입 이야기를 되풀이했고, 자기가 가난한 것이 서러운 까닭은 오로지 그 때문에 결혼을 생각할 수 없다는 데 있노라고 설명했다."], ["\"I suppose it must,\"", "“그렇겠지요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"I should think it wrong to ask any lady to share such an income as mine,\" said he.", "“제 것과 같은 수입을 어느 부인에게 나누어 가지자고 청하는 건 잘못이라고 생각합니다.”"], ["Whereupon Bell had suggested to him that some ladies had incomes of their own, and that he might in that way get over the difficulty.", "그가 말했다. 그러자 벨은 어떤 부인들은 제 몫의 수입을 가지고 있으니 그런 식으로 어려움을 넘길 수도 있지 않겠느냐고 말해 보았다."], ["\"I should be afraid of myself in marrying a girl with money,\"", "“돈을 가진 아가씨와 결혼한다면 저는 제 자신이 두려울 것 같습니다.”"], ["said he; \"besides, that is altogether out of the question now.\"", "그가 말했다. “게다가 그건 이제 아주 말도 안 되는 이야기지요.”"], ["Of course Bell did not ask him why it was out of the question, and for a time they went on walking in silence.", "물론 벨은 어째서 말도 안 되는 이야기냐고 묻지 않았고, 두 사람은 한동안 말없이 걸었다."], ["\"It is a hard thing to do,\"", "“쉽지 않은 일입니다.”"], ["he then said,--not looking at her, but looking at the gravel on which he stood.", "그가 이윽고 말했다. 그녀를 보는 것이 아니라 제가 딛고 선 자갈을 내려다보면서."], ["\"It is a hard thing to do, but I will determine to think of it no further.", "“쉽지 않은 일이지만, 더는 그것을 생각하지 않기로 마음을 정하겠습니다."], ["I believe a man may be as happy single as he may married,--almost.\"", "사람은 혼자서도 결혼한 만큼 행복할 수 있다고 저는 믿습니다 — 거의는요.”"], ["\"Perhaps more so,\" said Bell.", "“어쩌면 더 행복할지도 모르지요.”"], ["Then the doctor left her, and Bell, as I have said before, made up her mind with great firmness that she was not in love with him.", "벨이 말했다. 그러고 나서 의사는 그녀를 두고 떠났고, 벨은 앞서 내가 말한 대로 자기가 그를 사랑하는 것이 아니라고 아주 단단히 마음을 정했다."], ["I may certainly say that there was nothing in the world as to which she was so certain as she was of this.", "이 세상에서 그녀가 이만큼 확신한 것은 달리 없었다고 나는 분명히 말할 수 있다."]], [["And now, in these days, Dr. Crofts did not come over to Allington very often.", "그리고 요즈음 크로프츠 선생은 앨링턴에 그리 자주 건너오지 않았다."], ["Had any of the family in the Small House been ill, he would have been there of course.", "작은 집 식구 가운데 누가 앓았더라면 물론 그가 거기 있었을 것이다."], ["The squire himself employed the apothecary in the village, or if higher aid was needed, would send for Dr. Gruffen.", "지주는 마을의 약제사를 썼고, 그보다 높은 도움이 필요하면 그러펀 선생을 부르러 보내곤 했다."], ["On the occasion of Mrs. Dale's party, Crofts was there, having been specially invited; but Mrs.", "데일 부인의 모임이 있던 날 크로프츠는 특별히 초대를 받아 그 자리에 있었다."], ["Dale's special invitations to her friends were very few, and the doctor was well aware that he must himself make occasion for going there if he desired to see the inmates of the house.", "그러나 데일 부인이 벗들에게 따로 초대를 보내는 일은 아주 드물었고, 그 집 사람들을 보고 싶으면 스스로 갈 구실을 만들어야 한다는 것을 의사는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["But he very rarely made such occasion, perhaps feeling that he was more in his element at the workhouse and the hospital.", "그러나 그는 그런 구실을 좀처럼 만들지 않았으니, 아마도 구빈원과 병원 쪽이 제게 더 어울리는 자리라고 느꼈기 때문이리라."]], [["Just at this time, however, he made one very great and unexpected step towards success in his profession.", "그러나 바로 이 무렵 그는 제 직업에서 성공을 향해 아주 크고 뜻밖의 한 걸음을 내디뎠다."], ["He was greatly surprised one morning by being summoned to the Manor House to attend upon Lord De Guest.", "어느 날 아침 그는 드 게스트 경을 진찰하러 저택으로 와 달라는 부름을 받고 몹시 놀랐다."], ["The family at the Manor had employed Dr. Gruffen for the last thirty years, and Crofts, when he received the earl's message, could hardly believe the words.", "저택 사람들은 지난 삼십 년 동안 그러펀 선생을 써 왔던 터라, 백작의 전갈을 받은 크로프츠는 그 말을 도무지 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["\"The earl ain't very bad,\"", "“백작님께서 많이 편찮으신 건 아닙니다요.”"], ["said the servant, \"but he would be glad to see you if possible a little before dinner.\"", "하인이 말했다. “다만 되도록 만찬 전에 좀 뵈었으면 하십니다요.”"]], [["\"You're sure he wants to see me?\"", "“그분이 정말로 나를 보자고 하신 게 맞나?”"], ["said Crofts.", "크로프츠가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes; I'm sure enough of that, sir.\"", "“아, 예. 그건 틀림없습니다요, 나리.”"]], [["\"It wasn't Dr. Gruffen?\"", "“그러펀 선생이 아니고?”"]], [["\"No, sir; it wasn't Dr.", "“예, 나리. 그러펀 선생이 아닙니다요."], ["Gruffen. I believe his lordship's had about enough of Dr.", "백작님께서 그러펀 선생한테는 이제 신물이 나신 모양입니다요."], ["Gruffen. The doctor took to chaffing his lordship one day.\"", "그 선생이 하루는 백작님을 주물러 대기 시작하셨거든요.”"]], [["\"Chaffed his lordship;--his hands and feet, and that sort of thing?\"", "“백작님을 주물렀다니 — 손이며 발이며, 뭐 그런 것을 말인가?”"], ["suggested the doctor.", "의사가 넌지시 물었다."]], [["\"Hands and feet!\"", "“손이며 발이라니요!”"], ["said the man.", "하인이 말했다."], ["\"Lord bless you, sir, he poked his fun at him, just as though he was nobody.", "“어이구, 나리, 그 선생은 백작님이 아무것도 아닌 사람인 양 놀려 먹었습니다요."], ["I didn't hear, but Mrs. Connor says that my lord's back was up terribly high.\"", "저야 못 들었습니다만, 코너 부인 말로는 백작님께서 노발대발하셨답니다요.”"], ["And so Dr. Crofts got on his horse and rode up to Guestwick Manor.", "그리하여 크로프츠 선생은 말에 올라 게스트윅 저택으로 올라갔다."]], [["The earl was alone, Lady Julia having already gone to Courcy Castle.", "백작은 혼자였다. 레이디 줄리아는 이미 코시 성으로 떠난 뒤였다."], ["\"How d'ye do, how d'ye do?\"", "“어서 오게, 어서 와.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"I'm not very ill, but I want to get a little advice from you.", "“나는 그리 아프지는 않네만, 자네한테 조언을 좀 얻고 싶어서 말이야."], ["It's quite a trifle, but I thought it well to see somebody.\" Whereupon Dr.", "아주 사소한 일이네만, 그래도 누구한테든 한번 보이는 게 좋겠다 싶었네.”"], ["Crofts of course declared that he was happy to wait upon his lordship.", "이에 크로프츠 선생은 물론 백작님을 모시게 되어 기쁘다고 말했다."]], [["\"I know all about you, you know,\"", "“나는 자네에 대해 다 알고 있네.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"Your grandmother Stoddard was a very old friend of my aunt's.", "“자네 할머니 스토더드 부인은 우리 고모님의 아주 오랜 벗이었지."], ["You don't remember Lady Jemima?\"", "레이디 제미마는 기억나지 않겠지?”"]], [["\"No,\"", "“모릅니다.”"], ["said Crofts.", "크로프츠가 말했다."], ["\"I never had that honour.\"", "“그런 영광은 누려 본 적이 없습니다.”"]], [["\"An excellent old woman, and knew your grandmother Stoddard well.", "“참으로 훌륭한 노부인이셨고, 자네 할머니 스토더드 부인을 잘 아셨네."], ["You see, Gruffen has been attending us for I don't know how many years; but upon my word--\"", "보게, 그러펀이 우리를 봐 준 게 도대체 몇 해나 되는지 나도 모를 지경일세만, 정말이지—”"], ["and then the earl stopped himself.", "그러고서 백작은 말을 멈추었다."]], [["\"It's an ill wind that blows nobody any good,\"", "“누구에게도 이롭지 않은 바람이란 없는 법이지요.”"], ["said Crofts, with a slight laugh.", "크로프츠가 가볍게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"Perhaps it 'll blow me some good, for Gruffen never did me any. The fact is this; I'm very well, you know;--as strong as a horse.\"", "“어쩌면 나한테는 좋은 바람을 불어 줄지도 모르지. 그러펀은 나한테 좋은 일이라곤 해 준 게 없으니 말이야. 사정은 이렇다네. 나는 아주 멀쩡해, 알겠나. 말처럼 튼튼하지.”"]], [["\"You look pretty well.\"", "“안색이 꽤 좋아 보이십니다.”"]], [["\"No man could be better,--not of my age.", "“나보다 나은 사람은 없을 걸세 — 내 나이에는 말이지."], ["I'm sixty, you know.\"", "나는 예순일세, 알다시피.”"]], [["\"You don't look as though you were ailing.\"", "“앓으시는 것처럼은 보이지 않습니다.”"]], [["\"I'm always out in the open air, and that, I take it, is the best thing for a man.\"", "“나는 늘 바깥바람을 쐬고 사네. 그게 사람한테 제일 좋은 것이라고 나는 보네.”"]], [["\"There's nothing like plenty of exercise, certainly.\"", "“운동을 넉넉히 하는 것만 한 게 없지요, 그야 물론입니다.”"]], [["\"And I'm always taking exercise,\"", "“그리고 나는 늘 몸을 움직인다네.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"There isn't a man about the place works much harder than I do.", "“이 근방에서 나보다 더 고되게 일하는 사람은 없어."], ["And, let me tell you, sir, when you undertake to keep six or seven hundred acres of land in your own hand, you must look after it, unless you mean to lose money by it.\"", "그리고 말이지, 자네한테 하는 말이네만, 육칠백 에이커의 땅을 제 손으로 부치겠다고 나서면 그 땅을 잘 살펴야 하네. 손해를 볼 작정이 아니라면 말이야.”"]], [["\"I've always heard that your lordship is a good farmer.\"", "“백작님께서 훌륭한 농사꾼이시라는 말씀은 늘 들어 왔습니다.”"]], [["\"Well, yes; wherever the grass may grow about my place, it doesn't grow under my feet.", "“뭐, 그렇지. 내 땅 어디에 풀이 자란다 해도 내 발밑에서 자라지는 않네."], ["You won't often find me in bed at six o'clock, I can tell you.\"", "여섯 시에 내가 잠자리에 있는 걸 볼 일은 그리 흔치 않을 걸세, 정말이야.”"]], [["After this Dr. Crofts ventured to ask his lordship as to what special physical deficiency his own aid was invoked at the present time.", "이런 이야기가 오간 뒤에야 크로프츠 선생은 대체 몸의 어떤 이상 때문에 이번에 자기 도움을 청하신 것이냐고 백작에게 감히 물어보았다."]], [["\"Ah, I was just coming to that,\"", "“아, 그 이야기를 막 하려던 참이었네.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"They tell me it's a very dangerous practice to go to sleep after dinner.\"", "“만찬을 들고 나서 잠드는 것이 아주 위험한 버릇이라고들 하더군.”"]], [["\"It's not very uncommon at any rate,\"", "“적어도 그리 드문 일은 아닙니다.”"], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["\"I suppose not; but Lady Julia is always at me about it.", "“그렇겠지. 그런데 레이디 줄리아가 그 일로 늘 나를 들볶는다네."], ["And, to tell the truth, I think I sleep almost too sound when I get to my arm-chair in the drawing-room.", "그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 응접실 안락의자에 앉기만 하면 내가 너무 깊이 잠드는 것 같기는 해."], ["Sometimes my sister really can't wake me;--so, at least, she says.\"", "어떤 때는 누이가 나를 도무지 깨우지 못한다더군 — 적어도 저는 그렇게 말하네.”"]], [["\"And how's your appetite at dinner?\"", "“만찬 때 식욕은 어떠십니까?”"]], [["\"Oh, I'm quite right there.", "“아, 그건 아주 멀쩡하네."], ["I never eat any luncheon, you know, and enjoy my dinner thoroughly.", "나는 점심은 아예 안 먹는다는 걸 자네도 알겠지. 그래서 만찬을 아주 흡족하게 즐긴다네."], ["Then I drink three or four glasses of port wine--\"", "그러고 나서 포트와인을 서너 잔 마시는데—”"]], [["\"And feel sleepy afterwards?\"", "“그러고 나면 졸리시고요?”"]], [["\"That's just it,\"", "“바로 그걸세.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["It is not perhaps necessary that we should inquire what was the exact nature of the doctor's advice; but it was, at any rate, given in such a way that the earl said he would be glad to see him again.", "그 의사의 조언이 정확히 어떤 것이었는지까지 우리가 캐물을 필요는 아마 없을 것이다. 다만 그 조언은, 어쨌든, 백작이 그를 또 보고 싶다고 말할 만한 방식으로 주어진 것이었다."]], [["\"And look here, Doctor Crofts, I'm all alone just at present.", "“그리고 이보게, 크로프츠 선생, 내가 지금 아주 혼자일세."], ["Suppose you come over and dine with me to-morrow; then, if I should go to sleep, you know, you'll be able to let me know whether Lady Julia doesn't exaggerate.", "내일 건너와서 나와 함께 만찬을 들지 않겠나? 그러면 혹시 내가 잠이 들더라도 레이디 줄리아가 과장을 하는 것은 아닌지 자네가 알려 줄 수 있을 테니 말이야."], ["Just between ourselves, I don't quite believe all she says about my--my snoring, you know.\"", "우리끼리 이야기네만, 내 — 내 코 고는 소리에 대해 저가 하는 말을 나는 다 믿지는 않는다네.”"]], [["Whether it was that the earl restrained his appetite when at dinner under the doctor's eyes, or whether the mid-day mutton chop which had been ordered for him had the desired effect, or whether the doctor's conversation was more lively than that of the Lady Julia, we will not say; but the earl, on the evening in question, was triumphant.", "만찬 자리에서 의사의 눈길 아래 백작이 식욕을 억눌렀기 때문인지, 아니면 그를 위해 마련하도록 이른 한낮의 양갈비가 바라던 효과를 냈기 때문인지, 아니면 의사의 이야기가 레이디 줄리아의 이야기보다 더 활기찼기 때문인지는 우리가 말하지 않겠다. 어쨌든 그날 저녁 백작은 의기양양했다."], ["As he sat in his easy-chair after dinner he hardly winked above once or twice; and when he had taken the large bowl of tea, which he usually swallowed in a semi-somnolent condition, he was quite lively.", "만찬 뒤 안락의자에 앉아 있는 동안 그는 한두 번밖에 눈을 껌뻑이지 않았고, 여느 때 같으면 반쯤 졸린 채로 삼키던 커다란 사발의 차를 다 마시고 났을 때에도 아주 생기가 돌았다."]], [["\"Ah, yes,\"", "“아, 그렇군.”"], ["he said, jumping up and rubbing his eyes; \"I think I do feel lighter.", "그가 벌떡 일어나 눈을 비비며 말했다. “몸이 한결 가벼워진 것 같네."], ["I enjoy a snooze after dinner; I do indeed; I like it; but then, when one comes to go to bed, one does it in such a sneaking sort of way, as though one were in disgrace!", "나는 만찬 뒤에 한숨 자는 걸 즐기지. 정말 그래."], ["And my sister, she thinks it a crime--literally a sin, to go to sleep in a chair.", "그게 좋아. 하지만 그러고 나서 잠자리에 들 때가 되면, 마치 무슨 죄라도 지은 사람처럼 슬금슬금 기어드는 꼴이 되거든!"], ["Nobody ever caught her napping! By-the-by, Dr.", "그리고 우리 누이는 의자에서 조는 것을 죄악이라고, 말 그대로 죄라고 여긴다네."], ["Crofts, did you know that Mr.", "저가 조는 걸 붙잡은 사람은 아무도 없지!"], ["Crosbie whom Bernard Dale brought down to Allington?", "그건 그렇고, 크로프츠 선생, 버나드 데일이 앨링턴에 데려온 그 크로즈비 씨라는 사람을 자네도 아나?"], ["Lady Julia and he are staying at the same house now.\"", "레이디 줄리아가 지금 그 사람과 같은 집에 묵고 있다네.”"]], [["\"I met him once at Mrs. Dale's.\"", "“데일 부인 댁에서 한 번 만난 적이 있습니다.”"]], [["\"Going to marry one of the girls, isn't he?\"", "“그 집 딸 가운데 하나와 결혼한다지, 아마?”"]], [["Whereupon Dr. Crofts explained that Mr. Crosbie was engaged to Lilian Dale.", "이에 크로프츠 선생은 크로즈비 씨가 릴리언 데일과 약혼했다고 설명해 주었다."]], [["\"Ah, yes; a nice girl, I'm told.", "“아, 그래. 참한 아가씨라고들 하더군."], ["You know all those Dales are connections of ours.", "자네도 알겠지만 그 데일 집안 사람들은 죄다 우리 인척일세."], ["My sister Fanny married their uncle Orlando.", "내 누이 패니가 그 애들의 삼촌 올랜도한테 시집을 갔거든."], ["My brother-in-law doesn't like travelling, and so I don't see very much of him; but of course I'm interested about the family.\"", "내 매부는 여행을 좋아하지 않아서 나도 그를 자주 보지는 못하네만, 그래도 물론 그 집안 일에는 관심이 있지.”"]], [["\"They're very old friends of mine,\"", "“그분들은 저와도 아주 오랜 벗입니다.”"], ["said Crofts.", "크로프츠가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, I daresay.", "“그렇겠지."], ["There are two girls, are there not?\"", "딸이 둘이지, 아마?”"]], [["\"Yes, two.\"", "“예, 둘입니다.”"]], [["\"And Miss Lily is the youngest.", "“그리고 릴리 양이 동생이고."], ["There's nothing about the elder one getting married, is there?\"", "언니 쪽은 결혼 이야기가 없나?”"]], [["\"I've not heard anything of it.\"", "“그런 이야기는 못 들었습니다.”"]], [["\"A very pretty girl she is, too.", "“그 애도 아주 예쁘장한 아가씨지."], ["I remember seeing her at her uncle's last year.", "작년에 제 삼촌 집에서 본 기억이 나네."], ["I shouldn't wonder if she were to marry her cousin Bernard.", "사촌인 버나드와 결혼한다 해도 나는 놀라지 않겠어."], ["He is to have the property, you know; and he's my nephew.\"", "알다시피 그 재산은 버나드가 물려받을 것이고, 그 애는 내 조카이니 말이야.”"]], [["\"I'm not quite sure that it's a good thing for cousins to marry,\"", "“사촌끼리 결혼하는 것이 좋은 일인지 저는 잘 모르겠습니다.”"], ["said Crofts.", "크로프츠가 말했다."]], [["\"They do, you know, very often; and it suits some family arrangements.", "“다들 곧잘 그러지 않나. 그리고 그게 집안 사정에 맞아떨어지기도 하지."], ["I suppose Dale must provide for them, and that would take one off his hands without any trouble.\"", "데일도 그 애들을 어떻게든 건사해 줘야 할 텐데, 그렇게 하면 아무 수고 없이 하나를 제 손에서 덜어 내는 셈이거든.”"]], [["Dr. Crofts didn't exactly see the matter in this light, but he was not anxious to argue it very closely with the earl.", "크로프츠 선생은 이 일을 그런 눈으로 보지는 않았으나, 백작과 그것을 꼬치꼬치 따지고 싶지는 않았다."], ["\"The younger one,\"", "“동생 쪽은”"], ["he said, \"has provided for herself.\"", "하고 그가 말했다. “스스로 건사한 셈이지요.”"]], [["\"What; by getting a husband?", "“뭐라, 남편을 하나 얻어서 말인가?"], ["But I suppose Dale must give her something.", "하지만 데일이 그 애한테 무엇이든 좀 떼 줘야 할 걸세."], ["They're not married yet, you know, and, from what I hear, that fellow may prove a slippery customer.", "아직 혼인한 것도 아니고, 내가 듣기로는 그 친구가 만만찮은 사람일지도 모르거든."], ["He'll not marry her unless old Dale gives her something.", "늙은 데일이 그 애한테 뭘 좀 주지 않으면 그자는 그 애와 결혼하지 않을 걸세."], ["You'll see if he does.", "두고 보게, 정말 그런지."], ["I'm told that he has got another string to his bow at Courcy Castle.\"", "코시 성에 활시위를 하나 더 걸어 두었다는 말을 나는 들었네.”"]], [["Soon after this, Crofts took his horse and rode home, having promised the earl that he would dine with him again before long.", "이윽고 크로프츠는 말을 타고 집으로 돌아갔다. 머지않아 다시 와서 백작과 만찬을 들겠다고 약속한 뒤였다."]], [["\"It'll be a great convenience to me if you'd come about that time,\"", "“그맘때 와 주면 나한테는 아주 좋겠네.” 백작이 말했다."], ["said the earl, \"and as you're a bachelor perhaps you won't mind it.", "“그리고 자네는 총각이니 아마 개의치 않겠지."], ["You'll come on Thursday at seven, will you?", "목요일 일곱 시에 오겠나? 조심해서 가게."], ["Take care of yourself.", "칠흑같이 어두우니 말이야."], ["It's as dark as pitch. John, go and open the first gates for Dr.", "존, 가서 크로프츠 선생께 첫 번째 문을 열어 드려라.”"], ["Crofts.\" And then the earl took himself off to bed.", "그러고 나서 백작은 잠자리로 물러갔다."]], [["Crofts, as he rode home, could not keep his mind from thinking of the two girls at Allington.", "크로프츠는 말을 타고 집으로 돌아오는 내내 앨링턴의 두 아가씨 생각을 마음에서 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다."], ["\"He'll not marry her unless old Dale gives her something.\"", "“늙은 데일이 그 애한테 뭘 좀 주지 않으면 그자는 그 애와 결혼하지 않을 걸세.”"], ["Had it come to that with the world, that a man must be bribed into keeping his engagement with a lady?", "세상이 이 지경에 이르렀단 말인가, 남자가 한 여인과 맺은 약혼을 지키려면 뇌물을 받아야만 한다는 지경에?"], ["Was there no romance left among mankind,--no feeling of chivalry?", "사람들 사이에 낭만이라고는 남아 있지 않단 말인가 — 기사도의 마음이라고는?"], ["\"He's got another string to his bow at Courcy Castle,\"", "“코시 성에 활시위를 하나 더 걸어 두었다지.”"], ["said the earl; and his lordship seemed to be in no degree shocked as he said it.", "백작은 그렇게 말했고, 그리 말하면서도 조금도 놀라는 기색이 아니었다."], ["It was in this tone that men spoke of women now-a-days, and yet he himself had felt such awe of the girl he loved, and such a fear lest he might injure her in her worldly position, that he had not dared to tell her that he loved her.", "요즈음 남자들이 여자에 대해 말하는 투가 이러했다. 그런데도 그 자신은 제가 사랑하는 아가씨에게 그토록 외경을 느꼈고, 세상살이의 처지에서 그녀를 해치게 될까 봐 그토록 두려워한 나머지, 그녀를 사랑한다는 말조차 감히 하지 못했던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXI. JOHN EAMES ENCOUNTERS TWO ADVENTURES, AND DISPLAYS GREAT COURAGE IN BOTH.", "# 제21장. 존 임스가 두 가지 모험을 만나 그 둘 다에서 대단한 용기를 보이다"]], [["Lily thought that her lover's letter was all that it should be.", "릴리는 연인의 편지가 편지로서 갖추어야 할 것을 모두 갖추었다고 생각했다."], ["She was not quite aware what might be the course of post between Courcy and Allington, and had not, therefore, felt very grievously disappointed when the letter did not come on the very first day.", "코시와 앨링턴 사이에 우편이 어떤 길로 오가는지 그녀는 잘 알지 못했고, 그래서 바로 첫날에 편지가 오지 않았을 때에도 그리 몹시 실망하지는 않았다."], ["She had, however, in the course of the morning, walked down to the post-office, in order that she might be sure that it was not remaining there.", "그렇지만 그녀는 그날 오전 중에 우체국까지 걸어 내려갔다. 편지가 거기 남아 있는 것은 아닌지 확인해 두고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["\"Why, miss, they be all delivered; you know that,\"", "“아니 아가씨, 편지는 죄다 배달됐습니다요. 아시면서 그러시네.”"], ["said Mrs. Crump, the post-mistress.", "우체국을 맡아 보는 크럼프 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"But one might be left behind, I thought.\"", "“그래도 하나쯤 남겨질 수 있잖아요.”"]], [["\"John Postman went up to the house this very day, with a newspaper for your mamma.", "“우편배달부 존이 바로 오늘 아가씨 어머님께 신문을 갖다 드리러 댁까지 올라갔습니다요."], ["I can't make letters for people if folks don't write them.\".", "사람들이 편지를 안 쓰는 걸 저더러 만들어 내라 하실 수야 없지 않습니까요.”"]], [["\"But they are left behind sometimes, Mrs.", "“그래도 가끔은 남겨지잖아요, 크럼프 부인."], ["Crump. He wouldn't come up with one letter if he'd got nothing else for anybody in the street.\"", "그이도 온 거리에 다른 사람 것이 하나도 없으면 편지 한 통 때문에 올라오지는 않을 테니까요.”"]], [["\"Indeed but he would then.", "“아니고말고요, 그럴 때도 올라갑니다요."], ["I wouldn't let him leave a letter here no how, nor yet a paper.", "저는 편지 한 통도 여기 두고 가게 놔두지 않습니다요. 신문도 마찬가지고요."], ["It's no good you're coming down here for letters, Miss Lily.", "편지 때문에 여기까지 내려오셔야 아무 소용 없습니다요, 릴리 아가씨."], ["If he don't write to you, I can't make him do it.\"", "그분이 안 쓰시는 걸 제가 쓰게 만들 수는 없으니까요.”"], ["And so poor Lily went home discomforted.", "그리하여 가엾은 릴리는 마음이 언짢은 채로 집에 돌아갔다."]], [["But the letter came on the next morning, and all was right.", "그러나 이튿날 아침에 편지가 왔고, 모든 것이 제자리를 찾았다."], ["According to her judgment it lacked nothing, either in fulness or in affection.", "그녀의 판단으로 그 편지에는 모자란 것이 없었다. 넉넉함으로 보나 애정으로 보나 그러했다."], ["When he told her how he had planned his early departure in order that he might avoid the pain of parting with her on the last moment, she smiled and pressed the paper, and rejoiced inwardly that she had got the better of him as to that manoeuvre.", "마지막 순간에 그녀와 헤어지는 괴로움을 피하려고 일찍 떠날 궁리를 했노라고 그가 털어놓은 대목에서 그녀는 미소를 지으며 종이를 눌러 쥐었고, 그 술수에서 자기가 그를 이겼다는 사실에 속으로 흡족해했다."], ["And then she kissed the words which told her that he had been glad to have her with him at the last moment.", "그러고는 마지막 순간에 그녀가 곁에 있어 주어 기뻤노라고 적힌 낱말들에 입을 맞추었다."], ["When he declared that he had been happier at Allington than he was at Courcy, she believed him thoroughly, and rejoiced that it should be so.", "코시에서보다 앨링턴에서 더 행복했다고 그가 잘라 말했을 때 그녀는 그 말을 고스란히 믿었고, 그러하다는 것이 기뻤다."], ["And when he accused himself of being worldly, she excused him, persuading herself that he was nearly perfect in this respect as in others.", "그리고 그가 스스로를 세속적이라고 나무랐을 때에는 그를 감싸며, 그가 이 점에서도 다른 점에서와 마찬가지로 거의 나무랄 데 없다고 자신을 설득했다."], ["Of course a man living in London, and having to earn his bread out in the world, must be more worldly than a country girl; but the fact of his being able to love such a girl, to choose such a one for his wife,--was not that alone sufficient proof that the world had not enslaved him?", "런던에 살면서 세상에 나가 밥벌이를 해야 하는 남자라면 시골 처녀보다 세속적일 수밖에 없다. 그러나 그런 그가 그런 처녀를 사랑할 수 있다는 것, 그런 처녀를 아내로 고를 수 있다는 것—그것만으로도 세상이 그를 노예로 삼지 못했다는 충분한 증거가 아니겠는가?"], ["\"My heart is on the Allington lawns,\"", "“내 마음은 앨링턴의 잔디밭에 있소.”"], ["he said; and then, as she read the words, she kissed the paper again.", "그가 그렇게 적어 놓았고, 그 낱말들을 읽으면서 그녀는 다시 한번 종이에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["In her eyes, and to her ears, and to her heart, the letter was a beautiful letter.", "그녀의 눈에도, 귀에도, 마음에도 그 편지는 아름다운 편지였다."], ["I believe there is no bliss greater than that which a thorough love-letter gives to a girl who knows that in receiving it she commits no fault,--who can open it before her father and mother with nothing more than the slight blush which the consciousness of her position gives her.", "나는 제대로 된 연애편지 한 통이 처녀에게 주는 것보다 더 큰 기쁨은 없다고 믿는다. 그 편지를 받는 것이 아무 잘못도 아님을 아는 처녀, 제 처지를 의식하는 데서 오는 옅은 홍조 말고는 아무것도 없이 아버지와 어머니 앞에서 그것을 뜯어 볼 수 있는 처녀에게 말이다."], ["And of all love-letters the first must be the sweetest!", "그리고 모든 연애편지 가운데 첫 편지가 가장 달콤할 수밖에 없다!"], ["What a value there is in every word!", "낱말 하나하나에 얼마나 큰 값이 들어 있는가!"], ["How each expression is scanned and turned to the best account!", "표현 하나하나를 얼마나 뜯어보고 얼마나 좋은 쪽으로 새기는가!"], ["With what importance are all those little phrases invested, which too soon become mere phrases, used as a matter of course.", "머지않아 그저 흔한 문구가 되어 으레 쓰이고 말 그 자잘한 말마디들에 얼마나 큰 무게가 실리는가."], ["Crosbie had finished his letter by bidding God bless her; \"And you too,\"", "크로즈비는 하느님의 축복을 빌며 편지를 맺었다. “당신에게도요.”"], ["said Lily, pressing the letter to her bosom.", "릴리는 편지를 가슴에 꼭 누르며 그렇게 말했다."]], [["\"Does he say anything particular?\"", "“뭐 특별한 말이라도 있니?”"], ["asked Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, mamma; it's all very particular.\"", "“네, 엄마. 온통 특별한 말인걸요.”"]], [["\"But there's nothing for the public ear.\"", "“하지만 남들 귀에 들려줄 만한 건 없다는 게로구나.”"]], [["\"He sends his love to you and Bell.\"", "“엄마랑 언니한테 사랑을 보낸대요.”"]], [["\"We are very much obliged to him.\"", "“그것 참 고맙기도 하지.”"]], [["\"So you ought to be.", "“고마워하셔야죠."], ["And he says that he went to church going through Barchester, and that the clergyman was the grandfather of that Lady Dumbello.", "그리고 바체스터를 지나는 길에 교회에 갔는데, 거기 목사님이 그 레이디 덤벨로의 외할아버지였대요."], ["When he got to Courcy Castle Lady Dumbello was there.\"", "그런데 코시 성에 닿아 보니 레이디 덤벨로가 거기 와 있었고요.”"]], [["\"What a singular coincidence!\"", "“참 묘한 우연이로구나!”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I won't tell you a word more about his letter,\"", "“편지 얘기는 이제 한마디도 더 안 할래요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["So she folded it up, and put it in her pocket.", "그러고서 그녀는 편지를 접어 주머니에 넣었다."], ["But as soon as she found herself alone in her own room, she had it out again, and read it over some half-a-dozen times.", "그러나 제 방에 혼자 있게 되자마자 그것을 다시 꺼내어 예닐곱 번은 되풀이해 읽었다."]], [["That was the occupation of her morning;--that, and the manufacture of some very intricate piece of work which was intended for the adornment of Mr.", "그것이 그녀의 오전 일과였다. 그것과, 크로즈비 씨의 몸을 꾸미는 데 쓰일 예정인 아주 공들인 바느질 한 가지가."], ["Crosbie's person. Her hands, however, were very full of work;--or, rather, she intended that they should be full.", "그렇지만 그녀의 손은 일감으로 가득했다."], ["She would take with her to her new home, when she was married, all manner of household gear, the produce of her own industry and economy.", "아니, 가득하게 만들 작정이었다. 혼인해서 새집으로 갈 때 그녀는 제 부지런함과 알뜰함이 낳은 온갖 살림살이를 가지고 갈 참이었다."], ["She had declared that she wanted to do something for her future husband, and she would begin that something at once.", "장차 남편이 될 사람을 위해 무언가를 하고 싶다고 그녀는 이미 선언해 두었고, 그 무언가를 당장 시작할 참이었다."], ["And in this matter she did not belie her promises to herself, or allow her good intentions to evaporate unaccomplished.", "그리고 이 일에서 그녀는 스스로에게 한 약속을 저버리지 않았고, 좋은 뜻이 이루어지지 못한 채 김빠지도록 내버려 두지도 않았다."], ["She soon surrounded herself with harder tasks than those embroidered slippers with which she indulged herself immediately after his departure. And Mrs.", "그가 떠난 직후에 스스로를 달래며 수놓던 그 실내화보다 훨씬 고된 일감들을 그녀는 이내 제 둘레에 쌓아 놓았다."], ["Dale and Bell,--though in their gentle way they laughed at her,--nevertheless they worked with her, sitting sternly to their long tasks, in order that Crosbie's house might not be empty when their darling should go to take her place there as his wife.", "그리고 데일 부인과 벨도—두 사람은 점잖은 방식으로 그녀를 놀리기는 했지만—그래도 그녀와 함께 일했다. 자기들의 귀염둥이가 아내로서 제자리를 차지하러 갈 때 크로즈비의 집이 텅 비어 있지 않도록, 긴 일감을 붙들고 엄숙하게 앉아서."]], [["But it was absolutely necessary that the letter should be answered.", "그러나 그 편지에는 반드시 답장을 해야 했다."], ["It would in her eyes have been a great sin to have let that day's post go without carrying a letter from her to Courcy Castle,--a sin of which she felt no temptation to be guilty.", "그날 우편이 앨링턴에서 코시 성으로 그녀의 편지를 실어 가지 않은 채 지나가게 두는 것은 그녀 눈에는 큰 죄였을 터인데, 그런 죄를 지어 보고 싶은 마음은 조금도 들지 않았다."], ["It was an exquisite pleasure to her to seat herself at her little table, with her neat desk and small appurtenances for epistle-craft, and to feel that she had a letter to write in which she had truly much to say.", "제 자그마한 탁자 앞에 앉아 말끔한 문갑과 편지 쓰는 데 필요한 자잘한 도구들을 앞에 놓고, 정말로 할 말이 많은 편지를 쓸 수 있다고 느끼는 것은 그녀에게 더없는 기쁨이었다."], ["Hitherto her correspondence had been uninteresting and almost weak in its nature.", "여태껏 그녀의 편지 왕래는 재미없고 알맹이도 거의 없다시피 했다."], ["From her mother and sister she had hardly yet been parted; and though she had other friends, she had seldom found herself with very much to tell them by post.", "어머니와 언니에게서는 아직 떨어져 지내 본 적이 거의 없었고, 다른 벗들이 있기는 했으나 우편으로 전할 만한 이야기가 그리 많았던 적도 별로 없었다."], ["What could she communicate to Mary Eames at Guestwick, which should be in itself exciting as she wrote it?", "게스트윅의 메리 임스에게 무슨 소식을 전한들 쓰는 사람 스스로 설렐 만한 것이 있었겠는가?"], ["When she wrote to John Eames, and told \"Dear John\" that mamma hoped to have the pleasure of seeing him to tea at such an hour, the work of writing was of little moment to her, though the note when written became one of the choicest treasures of him to whom it was addressed.", "존 임스에게 편지를 써서 “존에게”라고 적고 엄마가 몇 시에 차를 들러 오시면 좋겠다고 하신다고 전할 때, 그 쪽지가 받는 사람에게는 더없이 귀한 보물이 되었을지언정 그것을 쓰는 일은 그녀에게 대수로울 것이 없었다."]], [["But now the matter was very different.", "그러나 이번에는 사정이 아주 달랐다."], ["When she saw the words \"Dearest Adolphus\" on the paper before her, she was startled with their significance.", "눈앞의 종이에 “사랑하는 어돌퍼스에게”라는 낱말이 적힌 것을 보았을 때 그녀는 그 무게에 스스로 흠칫했다."], ["\"And four months ago I had never even heard of him,\"", "“넉 달 전만 해도 나는 이이의 이름조차 들어 본 적이 없었는데.”"], ["she said to herself, almost with awe.", "그녀는 거의 경외에 가까운 마음으로 혼잣말을 했다."], ["And now he was more to her, and nearer to her, than even was her sister or her mother!", "그런데 이제 그는 언니보다도 어머니보다도 그녀에게 더 큰 사람이고 더 가까운 사람이었다!"], ["She recollected how she had laughed at him behind his back, and called him a swell on the first day of his coming to the Small House, and how, also, she had striven, in her innocent way, to look her best when called upon to go out and walk with the stranger from London.", "그녀는 그가 작은 집에 처음 오던 날 등 뒤에서 그를 두고 웃으며 멋쟁이라 불렀던 일을 떠올렸고, 런던에서 온 낯선 이와 함께 나가 산책하라는 말을 들었을 때 순진하게도 제 딴에는 가장 예뻐 보이려고 애썼던 일도 떠올렸다."], ["He was no longer a stranger now, but her own dearest friend.", "이제 그는 더 이상 낯선 이가 아니라 그녀의 가장 소중한 벗이었다."]], [["She had put down her pen that she might think of all this--by no means for the first time--and then resumed it with a sudden start as though fearing that the postman might be in the village before her letter was finished.", "이 모든 것을 곰곰 생각하려고 그녀는 펜을 내려놓았다가—결코 처음 있는 일은 아니었다—편지를 다 쓰기도 전에 우편배달부가 마을에 들어서지나 않을까 겁이 난 사람처럼 흠칫 놀라 다시 펜을 들었다."], ["\"Dearest Adolphus, I need not tell you how delighted I was when your letter was brought to me this morning.\"", "“사랑하는 어돌퍼스에게, 오늘 아침 당신의 편지를 받아 들었을 때 제가 얼마나 기뻤는지는 굳이 말씀드리지 않아도 되겠지요.”"], ["But I will not repeat the whole of her letter here.", "그러나 그녀의 편지를 여기에 다 옮겨 적지는 않겠다."], ["She had no incident to relate, none even so interesting as that of Mr. Crosbie's encounter with Mr.", "그녀에게는 전할 사건이랄 것이 없었고, 크로즈비 씨가 바체스터에서 하딩 씨를 만난 일만큼 흥미로운 일조차 없었다."], ["Harding at Barchester. She had met no Lady Dumbello, and had no counterpart to Lady Alexandrina, of whom, as a friend, she could say a word in praise.", "그녀는 레이디 덤벨로를 만난 적도 없었고, 벗으로서 한마디 칭찬해 줄 만한 레이디 알렉산드리나에 견줄 사람도 없었다."], ["John Eames's name she did not mention, knowing that John Eames was not a favourite with Mr. Crosbie; nor had she anything to say of John Eames, that had not been already said.", "존 임스가 크로즈비 씨의 마음에 드는 사람이 아님을 알았으므로 그녀는 그 이름을 적지 않았고, 그렇다고 존 임스에 대해 이미 하지 않은 이야기가 남아 있는 것도 아니었다."], ["He had, indeed, promised to come over to Allington; but this visit had not been made when Lily wrote her first letter to Crosbie.", "그가 앨링턴에 건너오겠다고 약속하기는 했으나, 릴리가 크로즈비에게 첫 편지를 쓸 때까지 그 방문은 아직 이루어지지 않고 있었다."], ["It was a sweet, good, honest love-letter, full of assurances of unalterable affection and unlimited confidence, indulging in a little quiet fun as to the grandees of Courcy Castle, and ending with a promise that she would be happy and contented if she might receive his letters constantly, and live with the hope of seeing him at Christmas.", "그것은 달콤하고 착하고 정직한 연애편지였다. 변치 않을 애정과 끝없는 믿음을 다짐하는 말로 가득했고, 코시 성의 높으신 분들을 두고 조용히 조금 놀리기도 했으며, 그의 편지를 늘 받을 수만 있다면, 그리고 성탄절에 그를 만나리라는 희망으로 살 수만 있다면 행복하고 만족하겠노라는 약속으로 끝을 맺었다."]], [["\"I am in time, Mrs. Crump, am I not?\"", "“늦지 않았죠, 크럼프 부인?”"], ["she said, as she walked into the post-office.", "우체국으로 들어서며 그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Of course you be,--for the next half-hour. T'", "“늦기는요."], ["postman--he bain't stirred from t'", "앞으로 반 시간은 넉넉합니다요."], ["ale'us yet. Just put it into t'", "배달부 그 사람은 아직 술집에서 엉덩이도 안 뗐습니다요."], ["box, wull ye?\"", "그냥 통에다 넣으시구려, 예?”"]], [["\"But you won't leave it there?\"", "“그래도 여기 그냥 두시진 않을 거죠?”"]], [["\"Leave it there!", "“그냥 두다니요!"], ["Did you ever hear the like of that?", "별말씀을 다 들어 봅니다요."], ["If you're afeared to put it in, you can take it away; that's all about it, Miss Lily.\"", "넣기가 겁나시면 도로 가져가시구려. 그뿐입니다요, 릴리 아가씨.”"], ["And then Mrs. Crump turned away to her avocations at the washing-tub.", "그러고서 크럼프 부인은 빨래통 앞의 제 볼일로 돌아섰다."], ["Mrs. Crump had a bad temper, but perhaps she had some excuse.", "크럼프 부인은 성미가 고약했으나 아마 그럴 만한 사정이 조금은 있었을 것이다."], ["A separate call was made upon her time with reference to almost every letter brought to her office, and for all this, as she often told her friends in profound disgust, she received as salary no more than \"tuppence farden a day.", "그녀의 우체국에 들어오는 편지 거의 한 통마다 따로 그녀의 시간을 빼앗는 일이 생겼는데, 그 모든 일을 하고도 받는 삯이라고는—그녀가 이웃들에게 넌더리를 내며 자주 하던 말로—“하루에 이 펜스하고 한 파딩”이 고작이었다."], ["It don't find me in shoe-leather; no more it don't.\"", "“그걸로는 신발 가죽값도 안 나옵니다요. 암, 안 나오고말고요.”"], ["As Mrs. Crump was never seen out of her own house, unless it was in church once a month, this latter assertion about her shoe-leather, could hardly have been true.", "그런데 크럼프 부인은 한 달에 한 번 교회에 갈 때 말고는 제 집 밖으로 나서는 것을 아무도 본 적이 없었으니, 신발 가죽에 대한 이 뒤엣말은 도무지 사실일 수가 없었다."]], [["Lily had received another letter, and had answered it before Eames made his promised visit to Allington.", "임스가 약속한 앨링턴 방문을 하기까지 릴리는 또 한 통의 편지를 받았고 답장까지 해 두었다."], ["He, as will be remembered, had also had a correspondence.", "기억하시겠지만 그에게도 오간 편지가 있었다."], ["He had answered Miss Roper's letter, and had since that been living in fear of two things; in a lesser fear of some terrible rejoinder from Amelia, and in a greater fear of a more terrible visit from his lady-love.", "그는 로퍼 양의 편지에 답장을 했고, 그 뒤로 두 가지를 겁내며 지내고 있었다. 어밀리아에게서 무시무시한 반박이 날아올까 하는 것이 작은 겁이었고, 그 연인이 더 무시무시하게 찾아올까 하는 것이 큰 겁이었다."], ["Were she to swoop down in very truth upon his Guestwick home, and declare herself to his mother and sister as his affianced bride, what mode of escape would then be left for him?", "그녀가 정말로 게스트윅의 그의 집에 들이닥쳐 어머니와 누이 앞에서 자기가 그의 약혼녀라고 선언한다면, 그때 그에게 무슨 달아날 길이 남아 있겠는가?"], ["But this she had not yet done, nor had she even answered his cruel missive.", "그러나 그녀는 아직 그러지 않았고, 그의 모진 편지에 답장조차 하지 않고 있었다."]], [["\"What an ass I am to be afraid of her!\"", "“저런 여자를 겁내다니 나도 참 얼간이지!”"], ["he said to himself as he walked along under the elms of Guestwick manor, which overspread the road to Allington.", "앨링턴으로 가는 길 위에 그늘을 드리운 게스트윅 저택의 느릅나무 아래를 걸으며 그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["When he first went over to Allington after his return home, he had mounted himself on horseback, and had gone forth brilliant with spurs, and trusting somewhat to the glories of his dress and gloves.", "집에 돌아온 뒤 처음으로 앨링턴에 건너갔을 때 그는 말에 올라 박차를 번쩍이며 나섰고, 옷과 장갑의 광채에 은근히 기대를 걸었더랬다."], ["But he had then known nothing of Lily's engagement.", "그러나 그때 그는 릴리의 약혼에 대해 아무것도 모르고 있었다."], ["Now he was contented to walk; and as he had taken up his slouched hat and stick in the passage of his mother's house, he had been very indifferent as to his appearance.", "이제 그는 걸어가는 것으로 만족했고, 어머니 집 복도에서 챙 늘어진 모자와 지팡이를 집어 들 때에도 제 겉모양에는 도무지 무심했다."], ["He walked quickly along the road, taking for the first three miles the shade of the Guestwick elms, and keeping his feet on the broad greensward which skirts the outside of the earl's palings.", "그는 길을 따라 빠르게 걸었다. 처음 삼 마일은 게스트윅 느릅나무 그늘을 골라 걸었고, 백작의 울짱 바깥을 따라 뻗은 널찍한 잔디밭에 발을 두었다."], ["\"What an ass I am to be afraid of her!\"", "“저런 여자를 겁내다니 나도 참 얼간이지!”"], ["And as he swung his big stick in his hand, striking a tree here and there, and knocking the stones from his path, he began to question himself in earnest, and to be ashamed of his position in the world.", "그러고서 그는 굵은 지팡이를 휘둘러 여기저기 나무를 치고 길에 놓인 돌을 쳐 내면서 진지하게 스스로를 따져 묻기 시작했고, 세상에서의 제 처지가 부끄러워졌다."], ["\"Nothing on earth shall make me marry her,\"", "“무슨 일이 있어도 그 여자와 혼인하지는 않겠어.”"], ["he said; \"not if they bring a dozen actions against me.", "그는 말했다. “열두 건의 소송을 걸어 온대도 안 해."], ["She knows as well as I do, that I have never intended to marry her.", "내가 그 여자와 혼인할 생각이 한 번도 없었다는 걸 그 여자도 나만큼 잘 알아."], ["It's a cheat from beginning to end.", "처음부터 끝까지 속임수야."], ["If she comes down here, I'll tell her so before my mother.\"", "여기까지 내려온다면 어머니 앞에서 그렇게 말해 주겠어.”"], ["But as the vision of her sudden arrival came before his eyes, he acknowledged to himself that he still held her in great fear.", "그러나 그녀가 불쑥 들이닥치는 모습이 눈앞에 떠오르자 그는 아직도 그녀를 몹시 두려워하고 있음을 스스로 인정했다."], ["He had told her that he loved her.", "그는 그녀를 사랑한다고 말한 적이 있었다."], ["He had written as much as that.", "그만큼은 글로도 적었다."], ["If taxed with so much, he must confess his sin.", "그만큼을 두고 따진다면 그는 제 죄를 고백하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Then, by degrees, his mind turned away from Amelia Roper to Lily Dale, not giving him a prospect much more replete with enjoyment than that other one.", "그러다가 차츰 그의 마음은 어밀리아 로퍼에게서 릴리 데일에게로 옮겨 갔는데, 이쪽이라 해서 저쪽보다 딱히 더 즐거운 앞날을 보여 주지는 않았다."], ["He had said that he would call at Allington before he returned to town, and he was now redeeming his promise.", "그는 런던으로 돌아가기 전에 앨링턴에 들르겠다고 말해 두었고, 이제 그 약속을 지키는 참이었다."], ["But he did not know why he should go there.", "그러나 자기가 왜 그리로 가야 하는지는 알 수 없었다."], ["He felt that he should sit silent and abashed in Mrs. Dale's drawing-room, confessing by his demeanour that secret which it behoved him now to hide from every one.", "데일 부인의 응접실에 말없이 주눅 든 채 앉아, 이제는 누구에게든 감추어야 마땅한 그 비밀을 몸가짐으로 고백하고 말리라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["He could not talk easily before Lily, nor could he speak to her of the only subject which would occupy his thoughts when in her presence.", "그는 릴리 앞에서 편히 이야기할 수도 없었고, 그녀 앞에 있을 때 오로지 머릿속을 채울 그 한 가지 이야깃거리를 그녀에게 꺼낼 수도 없었다."], ["If indeed, he might find her alone-- But, perhaps that might be worse for him than any other condition.", "정말로 그녀를 혼자 만나게 된다면— 그러나 어쩌면 그것이 어떤 경우보다도 그에게 더 나쁠지도 몰랐다."]], [["When he was shown into the drawing-room there was nobody there.", "그가 응접실로 안내되어 들어갔을 때 거기에는 아무도 없었다."], ["\"They were here a minute ago, all three,\"", "“조금 전까지 세 분 다 여기 계셨습니다요.”"], ["said the servant girl.", "하녀가 말했다."], ["\"If you'll walk down the garden, Mr. John, you'll be sure to find some of 'em.\"", "“정원 쪽으로 내려가 보시면 누구든 꼭 만나실 겁니다요, 존 도련님.”"], ["So John Eames, with a little hesitation, walked down the garden.", "그리하여 존 임스는 조금 망설이다가 정원으로 내려갔다."]], [["First of all he went the whole way round the walks, meeting nobody.", "먼저 그는 산책로를 빙 돌았으나 아무도 만나지 못했다."], ["Then he crossed the lawn, returning again to the farther end; and there, emerging from the little path which led from the Great House, he encountered Lily alone.", "그러고는 잔디밭을 가로질러 다시 저쪽 끝으로 돌아왔는데, 거기서 큰 집으로 통하는 좁은 길에서 나오던 릴리와 마주쳤다. 그녀는 혼자였다."], ["\"Oh, John,\"", "“어머, 존.”"], ["she said, \"how d'ye do?", "그녀가 말했다."], ["I'm afraid you did not find anybody in the house.", "“안녕하세요? 집에서 아무도 못 만나셨나 봐요."], ["Mamma and Bell are with Hopkins, away in the large kitchen-garden.\"", "엄마하고 언니는 홉킨스랑 저쪽 큰 채마밭에 가 있어요.”"]], [["\"I've just come over,\" said Eames, \"because I promised.", "“약속을 했으니까 그냥 건너와 봤어요.”"], ["I said I'd come before I went back to London.\"", "임스가 말했다. “런던으로 돌아가기 전에 오겠다고 했잖아요.”"]], [["\"And they'll be very glad to see you, and so am I.", "“다들 무척 반가워하실 거예요. 저도 반갑고요."], ["Shall we go after them into the other grounds?", "저쪽 밭으로 두 분을 찾아가 볼까요?"], ["But perhaps you walked over and are tired.\"", "아, 걸어오셨으면 힘드시겠네요.”"]], [["\"I did walk,\"", "“걷긴 걸었어요.”"], ["said Eames; \"not that I am very tired.\"", "임스가 말했다. “그렇다고 아주 힘든 건 아니고요.”"], ["But in truth he did not wish to go after Mrs.", "그러나 사실 그는 데일 부인을 찾아가고 싶지 않았다."], ["Dale, though he was altogether at a loss as to what he would say to Lily while remaining with her.", "그러면서도 릴리와 함께 남아 무슨 말을 해야 할지는 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못했다."], ["He had fancied that he would like to have some opportunity of speaking to her alone before he went away;--of making some special use of the last interview which he should have with her before she became a married woman.", "떠나기 전에 그녀와 단둘이 이야기할 기회가 있었으면 좋겠다고 그는 마음속으로 그려 보았더랬다. 그녀가 혼인한 여인이 되기 전에 갖게 될 마지막 만남을 무언가 특별하게 써 보고 싶었던 것이다."], ["But now the opportunity was there, and he hardly dared to avail himself of it.", "그런데 이제 그 기회가 눈앞에 있는데도 그것을 붙잡을 엄두가 좀처럼 나지 않았다."]], [["\"You'll stay and dine with us,\"", "“저녁 잡숫고 가세요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"No, I'll not do that, for I especially told my mother that I would be back.\"", "“아니요, 그건 안 되겠어요. 어머니께 꼭 돌아오겠다고 다짐하고 나왔거든요.”"]], [["\"I'm sure it was very good of you to walk so far to see us.", "“이렇게 먼 길을 걸어서 저희를 보러 와 주시다니 정말 고마워요."], ["If you really are not tired, I think we will go to mamma, as she would be very sorry to miss you.\"", "정말로 힘들지 않으시다면 엄마한테 가 보면 좋겠어요. 못 뵈면 무척 서운해하실 테니까요.”"]], [["This she said, remembering at the moment what had been Crosbie's injunctions to her about John Eames.", "그녀가 이렇게 말한 것은 존 임스에 대해 크로즈비가 당부했던 말이 그 순간 떠올랐기 때문이다."], ["But John had resolved that he would say those words which he had come to speak, and that, as Lily was there with him, he would avail himself of the chance which fortune had given him.", "그러나 존은 하려고 온 그 말을 하기로 마음먹은 터였고, 릴리가 이렇게 함께 있으니 운이 그에게 준 기회를 쓰기로 작정한 터였다."]], [["\"I don't think I'll go into the squire's garden,\"", "“지주 어른 정원으로는 들어가고 싶지 않아요.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Uncle Christopher is not there.", "“크리스토퍼 삼촌은 거기 안 계세요."], ["He is about the farm somewhere.\"", "어디 농장 쪽에 계실 거예요.”"]], [["\"If you don't mind, Lily, I think I'll stay here.", "“괜찮으시다면, 릴리, 저는 여기 있을게요."], ["I suppose they'll be back soon.", "곧 돌아오시겠지요."], ["Of course I should like to see them before I go away to London.", "물론 런던으로 가기 전에 두 분도 뵙고 싶어요."], ["But, Lily, I came over now chiefly to see you.", "하지만 릴리, 오늘 제가 건너온 건 무엇보다 릴리를 보러 온 거예요."], ["It was you who asked me to promise.\"", "약속하라고 하신 것도 릴리였잖아요.”"]], [["Had Crosbie been right in those remarks of his?", "크로즈비가 했던 그 말들이 옳았던 것일까?"], ["Had she been imprudent in her little endeavour to be cordially kind to her old friend?", "오랜 벗에게 다정하고 친절하게 대하려 했던 그 자그마한 노력이 그녀의 경솔함이었을까?"], ["\"Shall we go into the drawing-room?\"", "“응접실로 들어갈까요?”"], ["she said, feeling that she would be in some degree safer there than out among the shrubs and paths of the garden.", "그녀가 말했다. 정원의 덤불과 오솔길 사이보다는 거기가 얼마쯤은 더 안전하리라고 느꼈던 것이다."], ["And I think she was right in this.", "그리고 나는 이 점에서 그녀가 옳았다고 생각한다."], ["A man will talk of love out among the lilacs and roses, who would be stricken dumb by the demure propriety of the four walls of a drawing-room.", "응접실 네 벽의 얌전한 예절 앞에서라면 입이 굳어 버렸을 남자도 라일락과 장미 사이에서는 사랑을 이야기하는 법이다."], ["John Eames also had some feeling of this kind, for he determined to remain out in the garden, if he could so manage it.", "존 임스에게도 그와 비슷한 느낌이 있었으니, 그는 될 수만 있다면 정원에 남아 있기로 마음먹었다."]], [["\"I don't want to go in unless you wish it,\"", "“릴리가 원하시는 게 아니라면 저는 들어가고 싶지 않아요.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Indeed, I'd rather stay here.", "“아니, 여기 있는 편이 좋겠어요."], ["So, Lily, you're going to be married?\"", "그래서, 릴리, 혼인을 하신다고요?”"], ["And thus he rushed at once into the middle of his discourse.", "그러고서 그는 대뜸 하려던 이야기의 한복판으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“네.”"], ["said she, \"I believe I am.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “그렇게 될 것 같아요.”"]], [["\"I have not told you yet that I congratulated you.\"", "“축하한다는 말을 아직 못 했네요.”"]], [["\"I have known very well that you did so in your heart.", "“마음으로는 벌써 해 주신 걸 저는 잘 알고 있었어요."], ["I have always been sure that you wished me well.\"", "언제나 제가 잘되기를 바라 주신다는 걸 늘 믿었으니까요.”"]], [["\"Indeed I have.", "“정말 그래요."], ["And if congratulating a person is hoping that she may always be happy, I do congratulate you.", "그리고 누군가를 축하한다는 것이 그 사람이 늘 행복하기를 바라는 것이라면, 저는 정말로 축하해요."], ["But, Lily--\"", "하지만 릴리—”"], ["And then he paused, abashed by the beauty, purity, and woman's grace which had forced him to love her.", "그러고서 그는 말을 멈추었다. 그로 하여금 그녀를 사랑하지 않을 수 없게 만들었던 그 아름다움과 순결함과 여인다운 기품 앞에서 기가 죽은 것이다."]], [["\"I think I understand all that you would say.", "“하시려는 말씀은 다 알 것 같아요."], ["I do not want ordinary words to tell me that I am to count you among my best friends.\"", "제가 존을 가장 좋은 벗으로 헤아리고 있다는 걸 알기 위해 여느 말이 필요하지는 않아요.”"]], [["\"No, Lily; you don't understand all that I would say.", "“아니에요, 릴리. 제가 하려는 말을 다 아시지는 못해요."], ["You have never known how often and how much I have thought of you; how dearly I have loved you.\"", "제가 얼마나 자주, 얼마나 많이 릴리를 생각했는지, 얼마나 애틋하게 사랑했는지 릴리는 한 번도 몰랐어요.”"]], [["\"John, you must not talk of that now.\"", "“존, 지금 그런 말씀은 하시면 안 돼요.”"]], [["\"I cannot go without telling you.", "“말하지 않고는 갈 수가 없어요."], ["When I came over here, and Mrs. Dale told me that you were to be married to that man--\"", "제가 여기 왔을 때, 데일 부인께서 릴리가 그 사람과 혼인하게 됐다고 말씀하셨을 때—”"]], [["\"You must not speak of Mr. Crosbie in that way,\"", "“크로즈비 씨를 그런 식으로 말씀하시면 안 돼요.”"], ["she said, turning upon him almost fiercely.", "그녀가 거의 사납게 그를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["\"I did not mean to say anything disrespectful of him to you.", "“릴리 앞에서 그분을 함부로 말할 뜻은 없었어요."], ["I should hate myself if I were to do so.", "그랬다면 저 자신이 미울 거예요."], ["Of course you like him better than anybody else?\"", "물론 릴리야 다른 누구보다 그분이 좋으시겠지요?”"]], [["\"I love him better than all the world besides.\"", "“세상 누구보다 그분을 사랑해요.”"]], [["\"And so do I love you better than all the world besides.\"", "“그리고 저도 세상 누구보다 릴리를 사랑해요.”"], ["And as he spoke he got up from his seat and stood before her.", "그렇게 말하면서 그는 자리에서 일어나 그녀 앞에 섰다."], ["\"I know how poor I am, and unworthy of you; and only that you are engaged to him, I don't suppose that I should now tell you.", "“제가 얼마나 가난한지, 얼마나 릴리에게 어울리지 않는지 저도 알아요. 그분과 약혼하지만 않으셨다면 지금 이런 말을 하지도 않았을 거예요."], ["Of course you couldn't accept such a one as me.", "물론 릴리가 저 같은 사람을 받아 주실 리는 없지요."], ["But I have loved you ever since you remember; and now that you are going to be his wife, I cannot but tell you that it is so.", "하지만 저는 릴리가 기억하는 그때부터 줄곧 릴리를 사랑해 왔고, 이제 그분의 아내가 되신다고 하니 그렇다는 말을 하지 않을 수가 없어요."], ["You will go and live in London; but as to my seeing you there, it will be impossible.", "릴리는 런던에 가서 사시겠지요. 하지만 제가 거기서 릴리를 뵙는 일은 없을 거예요."], ["I could not go into that man's house.\"", "저는 그 사람 집에는 들어갈 수 없어요.”"]], [["\"Oh, John.\"", "“아, 존.”"]], [["\"No, never; not if you become his wife.", "“아니요, 절대로요. 릴리가 그분의 아내가 되신다면 절대로."], ["I have loved you as well as he does.", "저도 그분만큼 릴리를 사랑했어요."], ["When Mrs. Dale told me of it, I thought I should have fallen.", "데일 부인께 그 말을 들었을 때 저는 쓰러지는 줄 알았어요."], ["I went away without seeing you because I was unable to speak to you.", "릴리에게 말을 할 수가 없어서 뵙지도 않고 가 버렸고요."], ["I made a fool of myself, and have been a fool all along.", "저는 바보짓을 했고, 여태 줄곧 바보였어요."], ["I am foolish now to tell you this, but I cannot help it.\"", "이런 말을 하는 지금도 바보짓이지만 어쩔 수가 없어요.”"]], [["\"You will forget it all when you meet some girl that you can really love.\"", "“정말로 사랑할 수 있는 아가씨를 만나시면 다 잊어버리실 거예요.”"]], [["\"And have I not really loved you? Well, never mind.", "“그럼 제가 릴리를 정말로 사랑한 게 아니었나요?"], ["I have said what I came to say, and I will now go.", "뭐, 됐어요. 하려던 말은 했으니 이제 가겠어요."], ["If it ever happens that we are down in the country together, perhaps I may see you again; but never in London.", "언젠가 시골에서 함께 있게 되면 어쩌면 다시 뵐지도 모르죠. 하지만 런던에서는 절대로요."], ["Good-by, Lily.\"", "잘 있어요, 릴리.”"], ["And he put out his hand to her.", "그러고서 그는 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["\"And won't you stay for mamma?\"", "“엄마 오실 때까지 안 계실 거예요?”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"No.", "“네."], ["Give her my love, and to Bell.", "어머님께, 그리고 벨에게 제 안부를 전해 줘요."], ["They understand all about it.", "두 분은 다 아시니까요."], ["They will know why I have gone.", "제가 왜 갔는지도 아실 거예요."], ["If ever you should want anybody to do anything for you, remember that I will do it, whatever it is.\"", "혹시라도 무슨 일이든 해 줄 사람이 필요하거든, 제가 하겠다는 걸 기억해 주세요. 그게 무엇이든지요.”"], ["And as he paced away from her across the lawn, the special deed in her favour to which his mind was turned,--that one thing which he most longed to do on her behalf,--was an act of corporal chastisement upon Crosbie.", "그리고 그가 잔디밭을 가로질러 그녀에게서 멀어져 갈 때, 그녀를 위한 그 특별한 봉사로 그의 마음이 향한 것—그녀를 위해 그가 무엇보다 하고 싶어 하던 그 한 가지—은 크로즈비의 몸에 매질을 가하는 일이었다."], ["If Crosbie would but ill-treat her,--ill-treat her with some antenuptial barbarity,--and if only he could be called in to avenge her wrongs!", "크로즈비가 그녀를 학대해 주기만 한다면, 혼인 전에 무슨 야만스러운 짓으로 그녀를 학대해 주기만 한다면, 그리고 그 억울함을 갚아 줄 사람으로 자기가 불려 나가기만 한다면!"], ["And as he made his way back along the road towards Guestwick, he built up within his own bosom a castle in the air, for her part in which Lily Dale would by no means have thanked him.", "그리고 게스트윅으로 향한 길을 되짚어 가면서 그는 제 가슴속에 공중누각을 하나 지어 올렸는데, 그 안에서 릴리 데일이 맡은 몫을 알았더라면 그녀는 결코 그에게 고마워하지 않았을 것이다."]], [["Lily when she was left alone burst into tears.", "릴리는 혼자 남게 되자 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["She had certainly said very little to encourage her forlorn suitor, and had so borne herself during the interview that even Crosbie could hardly have been dissatisfied; but now that Eames was gone her heart became very tender towards him.", "그 가엾은 구혼자를 부추길 만한 말은 분명 거의 하지 않았고, 그 만남 동안 몸가짐도 크로즈비조차 도무지 못마땅해할 수 없을 만큼 지켰다. 그러나 임스가 가 버리고 나자 그녀의 마음은 그에게 몹시 애틋해졌다."], ["She felt that she did love him also;--not at all as she loved Crosbie, but still with a love that was tender, soft, and true.", "그녀는 자기도 그를 사랑한다고 느꼈다. 크로즈비를 사랑하는 것과는 조금도 같지 않았으나, 그래도 애틋하고 부드럽고 참된 사랑이었다."], ["If Crosbie could have known all her thoughts at that moment, I doubt whether he would have liked them.", "그 순간 그녀의 생각을 크로즈비가 모두 알 수 있었다면 과연 그것을 좋아했을지 나는 의심스럽다."], ["She burst into tears, and then hurried away into some nook where she could not be seen by her mother and Bell on their return.", "그녀는 울음을 터뜨렸고, 그러고는 어머니와 벨이 돌아왔을 때 눈에 띄지 않을 만한 구석으로 서둘러 숨어 들어갔다."]], [["Eames went on his way, walking very quietly, swinging his stick and kicking through the dust, with his heart full of the scene which had just passed.", "임스는 지팡이를 휘두르고 먼지를 발로 차 올리며 아주 조용히 제 길을 갔는데, 가슴은 방금 지나간 장면으로 가득했다. 그는 스스로에게 화가 났다."], ["He was angry with himself, thinking that he had played his part badly, accusing himself in that he had been rough to her, and selfish in the expression of his love; and he was angry with her because she had declared to him that she loved Crosbie better than all the world besides.", "제 몫을 서투르게 해냈다고, 그녀에게 거칠게 굴었다고, 사랑을 말하면서 제 생각만 했다고 스스로를 나무랐다."], ["He knew that of course she must do so;--that at any rate it was to be expected that such was the case.", "그리고 그녀에게도 화가 났다. 세상 누구보다 크로즈비를 사랑한다고 그녀가 그에게 잘라 말했기 때문이다."], ["Yet, he thought, she might have refrained from saying so to him. \"She chooses to scorn me now,\"", "그녀가 그럴 수밖에 없다는 것을, 적어도 그러하리라 여기는 것이 당연하다는 것을 그도 알고 있었다."], ["he said to himself; \"but the time may come when she will wish that she had scorned him.\"", "그래도, 하고 그는 생각했다. 하필 그 말을 자기에게 하지는 않을 수도 있지 않았는가."], ["That Crosbie was wicked, bad, and selfish, he believed most fully.", "“저 사람은 지금 나를 업신여기기로 한 거야.” 그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["He felt sure that the man would ill-use her and make her wretched.", "“하지만 차라리 그자를 업신여겼더라면 좋았을 것을 하고 바랄 날이 올지도 모르지.”"], ["He had some slight doubt whether he would marry her, and from this doubt he endeavoured to draw a scrap of comfort.", "크로즈비가 못되고 나쁘고 이기적인 사람이라는 것을 그는 굳게 믿고 있었다. 그자가 그녀를 함부로 대하고 그녀를 비참하게 만들리라고 확신했다."], ["If Crosbie would desert her, and if to him might be accorded the privilege of beating the man to death with his fists because of this desertion, then the world would not be quite blank for him.", "그자가 그녀와 혼인이나 할지도 조금은 의심스러웠는데, 그 의심에서 그는 한 조각 위안을 끌어내려 애썼다. 크로즈비가 그녀를 버린다면, 그리고 그 버림 때문에 제 주먹으로 그자를 때려죽일 특권이 자기에게 주어진다면, 그때는 세상이 아주 텅 비지는 않으리라."], ["In all this he was no doubt very cruel to Lily;--but then had not Lily been very cruel to him?", "이 모든 생각에서 그는 릴리에게 몹시 잔인했다. 그러나 릴리도 그에게 몹시 잔인하지 않았던가?"]], [["He was still thinking of these things when he came to the first of the Guestwick pastures.", "그가 아직 이런 것들을 생각하고 있을 때 그는 게스트윅 목초지의 첫머리에 이르렀다."], ["The boundary of the earl's property was very plainly marked, for with it commenced also the shady elms along the roadside, and the broad green margin of turf, grateful equally to those who walked and to those who rode.", "백작 영지의 경계는 아주 뚜렷하게 표시되어 있었으니, 거기서부터 길가에 그늘을 드리우는 느릅나무와, 걷는 사람에게나 말 탄 사람에게나 똑같이 고마운 널찍하고 푸른 잔디 가장자리가 함께 시작되었기 때문이다."], ["Eames had got himself on to the grass, but, in the fulness of his thoughts, was unconscious of the change in his path, when he was startled by a voice in the next field and the loud bellowing of a bull.", "임스는 어느새 잔디 위로 올라서 있었으나 생각에 파묻힌 나머지 제 발밑이 바뀐 줄도 몰랐는데, 그때 옆 밭에서 나는 사람 목소리와 황소가 크게 우는 소리에 화들짝 놀랐다."], ["Lord De Guest's choice cattle he knew were there, and there was one special bull which was esteemed by his lordship as of great value, and regarded as a high favourite.", "드 게스트 경이 아끼는 소 떼가 거기 있다는 것을 그는 알고 있었고, 그중에는 백작이 큰 값어치가 있다고 여기며 유난히 마음에 들어 하는 황소가 한 마리 있었다."], ["The people about the place declared that the beast was vicious, but Lord De Guest had often been heard to boast that it was never vicious with him.", "그 고장 사람들은 그 짐승이 사납다고들 했으나, 드 게스트 경은 저한테는 한 번도 사나웠던 적이 없다고 자랑하는 말을 자주 했다."], ["\"The boys tease him, and the men are almost worse than the boys,\"", "“애들이 저 녀석을 놀려 대고, 어른들은 애들보다 더하지.” 백작은 그렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["said the earl; \"but he'll never hurt any one that has not hurt him.\"", "“하지만 저 녀석은 저를 해치지 않은 사람은 결코 해치지 않네.”"], ["Guided by faith in his own teaching the earl had taught himself to look upon his bull as a large, horned, innocent lamb of the flock.", "제 가르침에 대한 믿음에 이끌려 백작은 제 황소를 크고 뿔 달린, 순한 양 떼의 어린양처럼 여기도록 스스로를 길들여 놓았던 것이다."]], [["As Eames paused on the road, he fancied that he recognized the earl's voice, and it was the voice of one in distress.", "임스가 길에 멈춰 섰을 때 그는 백작의 목소리를 알아들은 듯싶었는데, 그것은 위급에 처한 사람의 목소리였다."], ["Then the bull's roar sounded very plain in his ear, and almost close; upon hearing which he rushed on to the gate, and, without much thinking what he was doing, vaulted over it, and advanced a few steps into the field.", "이어 황소의 울부짖음이 아주 또렷하게, 거의 코앞에서 귀에 울렸다. 그 소리를 듣자 그는 문 쪽으로 달려가 제가 무엇을 하는지 별로 생각도 하지 않은 채 문을 훌쩍 뛰어넘어 밭 안으로 몇 걸음 들어섰다."]], [["\"Halloo!\"", "“여보게!”"], ["shouted the earl.", "백작이 외쳤다."], ["\"There's a man.", "“사람이 있구먼."], ["Come on.\"", "이리 오게.”"], ["And then his continued shoutings hardly formed themselves into intelligible words; but Eames plainly understood that he was invoking assistance under great pressure and stress of circumstances.", "그러고 나서 이어진 그의 외침은 알아들을 만한 말이 되지도 못했다. 그러나 임스는 그가 큰 위급과 압박 속에서 도움을 청하고 있다는 것만은 분명히 알아들었다."], ["The bull was making short runs at his owner, as though determined in each run to have a toss at his lordship; and at each run the earl would retreat quickly for a few paces, but he retreated always facing his enemy, and as the animal got near to him, would make digs at his face with the long spud which he carried in his hand.", "황소는 제 주인에게 짧게짧게 달려들고 있었는데, 달려들 때마다 백작님을 뿔로 들이받고야 말겠다는 기세였다. 그리고 그때마다 백작은 몇 걸음씩 재빨리 물러섰으나, 물러설 때에도 늘 적을 마주 본 채였고, 짐승이 가까이 오면 손에 든 긴 잡초 괭이로 그 낯짝을 찔러 댔다."], ["But in thus making good his retreat he had been unable to keep in a direct line to the gate, and there seemed to be great danger lest the bull should succeed in pressing him up against the hedge.", "그런데 그렇게 물러서느라 그는 문 쪽으로 곧게 길을 잡지 못했고, 자칫 황소가 그를 산울타리에 몰아붙이지나 않을까 하는 큰 위험이 있었다."], ["\"Come on!\"", "“이리 오게!”"], ["shouted the earl, who was fighting his battle manfully, but was by no means anxious to carry off all the laurels of the victory himself.", "백작이 외쳤다. 그는 사내답게 싸우고 있었으나 승리의 월계관을 혼자 다 차지하고 싶은 마음은 조금도 없었다."], ["\"Come on, I say!\"", "“이리 오라니까!”"], ["Then he stopped in his path, shouted into the bull's face, brandished his spud, and threw about his arms, thinking that he might best dismay the beast by the display of these warlike gestures.", "그러고서 그는 걸음을 멈추고 황소의 낯짝에 대고 고함을 지르며 괭이를 휘두르고 두 팔을 내저었다. 이렇게 싸움하는 몸짓을 보여 주는 것이 짐승의 기를 꺾는 데 가장 좋으리라 여긴 것이다."]], [["Johnny Eames ran on gallantly to the peer's assistance, as he would have run to that of any peasant in the land.", "조니 임스는 이 땅의 어느 농사꾼을 도우러 달려갔을 법한 그대로 씩씩하게 그 귀족을 도우러 달려갔다."], ["He was one to whom I should be perhaps wrong to attribute at this period of his life the gift of very high courage.", "그의 삶에서 이 무렵의 그가 아주 높은 용기를 지녔다고 말하는 것은 아마 잘못일 것이다."], ["He feared many things which no man should fear; but he did not fear personal mishap or injury to his own skin and bones.", "그는 사내라면 두려워할 것 없는 것들을 많이도 두려워했다. 그러나 제 몸이 다치거나 제 살과 뼈가 상하는 것은 두려워하지 않았다."], ["When Cradell escaped out of the house in Burton Crescent, making his way through the passage into the outer air, he did so because he feared that Lupex would beat him or kick him, or otherwise ill-use him.", "크래들이 버턴 크레슨트의 집에서 복도를 지나 바깥바람 속으로 빠져나가 달아났을 때, 그것은 루펙스가 자기를 때리거나 걷어차거나 어떻게든 험하게 굴까 봐 겁이 났기 때문이었다."], ["John Eames would also have desired to escape under similar circumstances; but he would have so desired because he could not endure to be looked upon in his difficulties by the people of the house, and because his imagination would have painted the horrors of a policeman dragging him off with a black eye and a torn coat.", "존 임스라도 그런 처지였다면 달아나고 싶어 했을 것이다. 그러나 그가 그러고 싶어 했을 까닭은, 곤경에 빠진 제 꼴을 그 집 사람들이 들여다보는 것을 견딜 수 없어서였고, 순경이 눈에 시퍼렇게 멍이 들고 옷이 찢어진 자기를 끌고 가는 끔찍한 광경을 제 상상이 그려 보였을 것이기 때문이다."], ["There was no one to see him now, and no policeman to take offence.", "지금은 그를 볼 사람도 없었고 트집 잡을 순경도 없었다."], ["Therefore he rushed to the earl's assistance, brandishing his stick, and roaring in emulation of the bull.", "그래서 그는 지팡이를 휘두르고 황소에 질세라 고함을 지르며 백작을 도우러 달려들었다."]], [["When the animal saw with what unfairness he was treated, and that the number of his foes was doubled, while no assistance had lent itself on his side, he stood for a while, disgusted by the injustice of humanity.", "저를 얼마나 부당하게 대하는지, 적의 수는 갑절이 되었는데 제 편은 아무도 거들어 주지 않는다는 것을 그 짐승이 알아차렸을 때, 그는 인간의 불의에 넌더리가 나서 한동안 우두커니 서 있었다."], ["He stopped, and throwing his head up to the heavens, bellowed out his complaint.", "그는 걸음을 멈추더니 머리를 하늘로 쳐들고 제 억울함을 울부짖었다. “가까이 붙지 말게!”"], ["\"Don't come close!\"", "백작이 말했다."], ["said the earl, who was almost out of breath.", "그는 숨이 거의 넘어갈 지경이었다."], ["\"Keep a little apart.", "“조금 떨어져 있게."], ["Ugh!", "어허!"], ["ugh!", "어허!"], ["whoop, whoop!\"", "훠이, 훠이!”"], ["And he threw up his arms manfully, jobbing about with his spud, ever and anon rubbing the perspiration from off his eyebrows with the back of his hand.", "그러고서 그는 사내답게 두 팔을 쳐들고 괭이를 이리저리 내지르면서, 이따금 손등으로 눈썹에 맺힌 땀을 훔쳤다."]], [["As the bull stood pausing, meditating whether under such circumstances flight would not be preferable to gratified passion, Eames made a rush in at him, attempting to hit him on the head.", "황소가 멈춰 선 채 이런 판국에는 성깔을 채우는 것보다 달아나는 편이 낫지 않을까 궁리하고 있을 때, 임스가 그에게 달려들어 머리를 후려치려 했다."], ["The earl, seeing this, advanced a step also, and got his spud almost up to the animal's eye.", "이것을 본 백작도 한 걸음 나아가 괭이를 짐승의 눈앞까지 들이밀었다."], ["But these indignities the beast could not stand.", "그러나 이런 모욕까지 그 짐승이 참아 낼 수는 없었다."], ["He made a charge, bending his head first towards John Eames, and then, with that weak vacillation which is as disgraceful in a bull as in a general, he changed his purpose, and turned his horns upon his other enemy.", "그는 먼저 존 임스 쪽으로 머리를 숙이고 돌진했는데, 그러다가 장군에게 그러하듯 황소에게도 부끄러운 그 우유부단으로 마음을 바꾸어 다른 적에게 뿔을 돌렸다."], ["The consequence was that his steps carried him in between the two, and that the earl and Eames found themselves for a while behind his tail.", "그 바람에 그의 걸음은 두 사람 사이로 들어가게 되었고, 백작과 임스는 잠시 그의 꼬리 뒤에 있게 되었다."]], [["\"Now for the gate,\"", "“이제 문으로.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"Slowly does it; slowly does it; don't run!\"", "“천천히, 천천히요. 뛰지 마시고요!”"], ["said Johnny, assuming in the heat of the moment a tone of counsel which would have been very foreign to him under other circumstances.", "조니가 말했다. 그 순간의 열기에 휩쓸려, 다른 때 같으면 그에게 도무지 어울리지 않았을 훈수하는 말투를 쓰면서."]], [["The earl was not a whit offended.", "백작은 조금도 기분 상해하지 않았다."], ["\"All right,\"", "“알았네.”"], ["said he, taking with a backward motion the direction of the gate.", "그가 이렇게 말하면서 뒷걸음으로 문 쪽 방향을 잡았다."], ["Then as the bull again faced towards him, he jumped from the ground, labouring painfully with arms and legs, and ever keeping his spud well advanced against the foe.", "그러고서 황소가 다시 그를 마주 보자 그는 팔다리를 힘겹게 놀리며 땅에서 펄쩍 뛰었고, 괭이는 늘 적을 향해 잘 내밀어 두었다."], ["Eames, holding his position a little apart from his friend, stooped low and beat the ground with his stick, and as though defying the creature.", "임스는 벗에게서 조금 떨어진 자리를 지키며 몸을 낮추고 지팡이로 땅을 두들겼는데, 마치 그 짐승에게 덤벼 보라는 듯한 시늉이었다."], ["The bull felt himself defied, stood still and roared, and then made another vacillating attack.", "황소는 저를 부추기는 것을 느끼고 우뚝 서서 울부짖더니 다시 또 오락가락하는 공격을 해 왔다."]], [["\"Hold on till we reach the gate,\"", "“문에 닿을 때까지 버티십시오.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"Ugh!", "“어허!"], ["ugh!", "어허!"], ["Whoop!", "훠이!"], ["whoop!\"", "훠이!”"], ["shouted the earl.", "백작이 고함을 질렀다."], ["And so gradually they made good their ground.", "그리하여 두 사람은 차츰 제 자리를 넓혀 갔다."]], [["\"Now get over,\"", "“이제 넘으십시오.”"], ["said Eames, when they had both reached the corner of the field in which the gate stood.", "문이 서 있는 밭 모퉁이에 둘 다 이르렀을 때 임스가 말했다."]], [["\"And what'll you do?\"", "“그럼 자네는 어떻게 하고?”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"I'll go at the hedge to the right.\"", "“저는 오른쪽 산울타리로 넘겠습니다.”"], ["And Johnny as he spoke dashed his stick about, so as to monopolize, for a moment, the attention of the brute.", "그렇게 말하면서 조니는 지팡이를 마구 휘둘러 잠시 그 짐승의 눈길을 혼자 붙들었다."], ["The earl made a spring at the gate, and got well on to the upper rung.", "백작은 문으로 펄쩍 뛰어올라 윗가로대에 제대로 올라섰다."], ["The bull seeing that his prey was going, made a final rush upon the earl and struck the timber furiously with his head, knocking his lordship down on the other side.", "제 먹잇감이 달아나는 것을 본 황소는 마지막으로 백작에게 달려들어 머리로 나무를 사납게 들이받았고, 그 바람에 백작님은 반대편으로 나가떨어졌다."], ["Lord De Guest was already over, but not off the rail; and thus, though he fell, he fell in safety on the sward beyond the gate.", "드 게스트 경은 이미 넘어가 있었으되 가로대에서 발을 뗀 것은 아니었다. 그리하여 그는 넘어지기는 했으나 문 너머 잔디밭에 무사히 떨어졌다."], ["He fell in safety, but utterly exhausted.", "무사히 떨어지기는 했으나 기운은 다 빠져 있었다."], ["Eames, as he had purposed, made a leap almost sideways at a thick hedge which divided the field from one of the Guestwick copses.", "임스는 작정한 대로 밭과 게스트윅 잡목숲 하나를 갈라놓은 두꺼운 산울타리를 향해 거의 옆으로 몸을 날렸다."], ["There was a fairly broad ditch, and on the other side a quickset hedge, which had, however, been weakened and injured by trespassers at this corner, close to the gate.", "꽤 넓은 도랑이 있었고 그 너머에는 산사나무 울타리가 있었는데, 문 가까이의 이 모퉁이만은 함부로 넘나든 사람들 탓에 성기고 상해 있었다."], ["Eames was young and active and jumped well.", "임스는 젊고 날래어 잘 뛰었다."], ["He jumped so well that he carried his body full into the middle of the quickset, and then scrambled through to the other side, not without much injury to his clothes, and some damage also to his hands and face.", "어찌나 잘 뛰었던지 그는 제 몸을 산사나무 울타리 한복판에 통째로 들이밀었고, 그러고 나서 반대편으로 기어 나왔는데, 그 통에 옷은 크게 상하고 손과 얼굴에도 얼마간 상처를 입었다."]], [["The beast, recovering from his shock against the wooden bars, looked wistfully at his last retreating enemy, as he still struggled amidst the bushes.", "나무 빗장에 부딪힌 충격에서 깨어난 그 짐승은 아직 덤불 속에서 허우적대고 있는 마지막 적이 물러가는 것을 아쉬운 듯 바라보았다."], ["He looked at the ditch and at the broken hedge, but he did not understand how weak were the impediments in his way.", "그는 도랑을 보고 성긴 울타리를 보았으나, 제 앞을 막은 것들이 얼마나 허술한지는 알지 못했다."], ["He had knocked his head against the stout timber, which was strong enough to oppose him, but was dismayed by the brambles which he might have trodden under foot without an effort.", "그는 튼튼한 나무에 머리를 부딪혔고 그 나무는 과연 그를 막아 낼 만했다. 그러나 힘들일 것도 없이 발밑에 짓밟아 버릴 수 있었을 가시덤불 앞에서는 기가 꺾이고 말았다."], ["How many of us are like the bull, turning away conquered by opposition which should be as nothing to us, and breaking our feet, and worse still, our hearts, against rocks of adamant.", "우리 가운데 얼마나 많은 사람이 이 황소와 같은가. 우리에게 아무것도 아니어야 할 방해에 지고 돌아서며, 발을, 더 나쁘게는 마음을 금강석 같은 바위에 부딪혀 부수는 것이다."], ["The bull at last made up his mind that he did not dare to face the hedge; so he gave one final roar, and then turning himself round, walked placidly back amidst the herd.", "마침내 황소는 그 울타리를 마주할 엄두가 나지 않는다고 마음을 정하고는, 마지막으로 한 번 울부짖고 몸을 돌려 무리 속으로 유유히 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["Johnny made his way on to the road by a stile that led out of the copse, and was soon standing over the earl, while the blood ran down his cheeks from the scratches.", "조니는 잡목숲에서 나가는 층계 울짱을 넘어 길로 나섰고, 이내 백작 옆에 서 있게 되었는데, 긁힌 자리에서 나온 피가 뺨을 타고 흐르고 있었다."], ["One of the legs of his trowsers had been caught by a stake, and was torn from the hip downward, and his hat was left in the field, the only trophy for the bull.", "바지 한쪽은 말뚝에 걸려 엉덩이께부터 아래로 죽 찢어졌고, 모자는 밭에 남겨져 황소가 얻은 유일한 전리품이 되었다."], ["\"I hope you're not hurt, my lord,\"", "“다치지 않으셨습니까, 백작님.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh dear, no; but I'm terribly out of breath.", "“어허, 다치기는. 그저 숨이 넘어가겠네."], ["Why, you're bleeding all over.", "아니, 자네 온통 피투성이 아닌가."], ["He didn't get at you, did he?\"", "그 녀석이 자네를 받은 건 아니겠지?”"]], [["\"It's only the thorns in the hedge,\"", "“울타리 가시일 뿐입니다.”"], ["said Johnny, passing his hand over his face.", "조니가 손으로 얼굴을 훑으며 말했다."], ["\"But I've lost my hat.\"", "“그런데 모자를 잃어버렸습니다.”"]], [["\"There are plenty more hats,\"", "“모자야 얼마든지 있네.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"I think I'll have a try for it,\"", "“한번 가서 가져와 볼까 합니다.”"], ["said Johnny, with whom the means of getting hats had not been so plentiful as with the earl.", "조니가 말했다. 그에게는 모자를 마련할 방편이 백작만큼 넉넉하지 않았던 것이다."], ["\"He looks quiet now.\"", "“이제 얌전해진 것 같은데요.”"], ["And he moved towards the gate.", "그러고서 그는 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["But Lord De Guest jumped upon his feet, and seized the young man by the collar of his coat.", "그러나 드 게스트 경은 벌떡 일어나 그 젊은이의 웃옷 깃을 붙들었다. “모자를 가지러 간다고!”"], ["\"Go after your hat!\"", "그가 말했다."], ["said he. \"You must be a fool to think of it.", "“그런 생각을 하다니 자네도 어지간한 바보로군."], ["If you're afraid of catching cold, you shall have mine.\"", "감기가 들까 걱정이라면 내 것을 쓰게.”"]], [["\"I'm not the least afraid of catching cold,\"", "“감기는 조금도 걱정되지 않습니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."], ["\"Is he often like that, my lord?\"", "“저 녀석은 자주 저럽니까, 백작님?”"], ["And he made a motion with his head towards the bull.", "그러면서 그는 황소 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다."]], [["\"The gentlest creature alive; he's like a lamb generally--just like a lamb.", "“세상에 저렇게 순한 짐승이 없네. 여느 때는 어린양 같지."], ["Perhaps he saw my red pocket-handkerchief.\"", "꼭 어린양 같아. 아마 내 빨간 손수건을 봤나 보네.”"], ["And Lord De Guest showed his friend that he carried such an article.", "그러고서 드 게스트 경은 자기가 그런 물건을 지니고 있음을 벗에게 보여 주었다."], ["\"But where should I have been if you hadn't come up?\"", "“그건 그렇고, 자네가 오지 않았더라면 나는 어디에 가 있었겠나?”"]], [["\"You'd have got to the gate, my lord.\"", "“문까지는 가셨겠지요, 백작님.”"]], [["\"Yes; with my feet foremost, and four men carrying me.", "“그래, 두 발을 앞으로 뻗고 장정 넷이 떠메고서 말이지."], ["I'm very thirsty.", "목이 몹시 마르군."], ["You don't happen to carry a flask, do you?\"", "자네 혹시 술병 같은 건 가지고 다니지 않겠지?”"]], [["\"No, my lord, I don't.\"", "“예, 백작님, 없습니다.”"]], [["\"Then we'll make the best of our way home, and have a glass of wine there.\"", "“그럼 어서 집으로 가서 포도주나 한 잔 하세.”"], ["And on this occasion his lordship intended that his offer should be accepted.", "그리고 이번만은 백작님도 제 청이 받아들여지게 할 작정이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXII. LORD DE GUEST AT HOME.", "# 제22장. 드 게스트 경의 집에서"]], [["The earl and John Eames, after their escape from the bull, walked up to the Manor House together.", "백작과 존 임스는 황소에게서 벗어난 뒤 함께 저택으로 걸어 올라갔다."], ["\"You can write a note to your mother, and I'll send it by one of the boys,\"", "“어머님께 쪽지를 한 장 써 두게. 그러면 내가 아이들 편에 보내 주겠네.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["This was his lordship's answer when Eames declined to dine at the Manor House, because he would be expected home.", "집에서 저를 기다릴 것이라는 이유로 임스가 저택에서 만찬을 들기를 사양했을 때 백작님이 내놓은 대답이 이것이었다."]], [["\"But I'm so badly off for clothes, my lord,\"", "“하지만 제가 옷차림이 워낙 형편없어서요, 백작님.”"], ["pleaded Johnny.", "조니가 사정하듯 말했다."], ["\"I tore my trowsers in the hedge.\"", "“울타리에서 바지를 찢어 먹었습니다.”"]], [["\"There will be nobody there beside us two and Dr. Crofts.", "“거기엔 우리 둘하고 크로프츠 선생 말고는 아무도 없네."], ["The doctor will forgive you when he hears the story; and as for me, I didn't care if you hadn't a stitch to your back.", "선생이야 이야기를 들으면 자네를 용서할 걸세. 나로 말하자면, 자네가 등에 실오라기 하나 걸치지 않았다 해도 개의치 않았을 걸세."], ["You'll have company back to Guestwick, so come along.\"", "게스트윅으로 돌아갈 때 동행도 생길 테니, 자, 같이 가세.”"]], [["Eames had no further excuse to offer, and therefore did as he was bidden.", "임스는 더 내놓을 핑계가 없었으므로 시키는 대로 했다."], ["He was by no means as much at home with the earl now as during those minutes of the combat.", "그는 지금 백작과 함께 있는 것이 싸움이 벌어지던 그 몇 분 동안만큼 편안하지가 않았다. 차라리 집으로 가고 싶었다."], ["He would rather have gone home, being somewhat ashamed of being seen in his present tattered and bare-headed condition by the servants of the house; and moreover, his mind would sometimes revert to the scene which had taken place in the garden at Allington.", "이렇게 옷이 찢기고 모자도 없는 꼴을 그 집 하인들에게 보이는 것이 어쩐지 부끄러웠던 것이다. 게다가 그의 마음은 이따금 앨링턴의 정원에서 벌어졌던 그 장면으로 되돌아가곤 했다."], ["But he found himself obliged to obey the earl, and so he walked on with him through the woods.", "그러나 그는 백작에게 복종하지 않을 수 없다는 것을 알았고, 그리하여 백작과 함께 숲을 지나 걸어갔다."]], [["The earl did not say very much, being tired and somewhat thoughtful.", "백작은 지치기도 했고 어쩐지 생각에 잠기기도 하여 말을 그리 많이 하지 않았다."], ["In what little he did say he seemed to be specially hurt by the ingratitude of the bull towards himself.", "그 몇 마디 안 되는 말 속에서도 그는 황소가 저에게 보인 배은망덕이 유독 서운한 듯했다."], ["\"I never teased him, or annoyed him in any way.\"", "“나는 저 녀석을 약 올린 적도, 어떤 식으로든 성가시게 군 적도 없단 말이야.”"]], [["\"I suppose they are dangerous beasts?\"", "“그놈들이 원래 위험한 짐승 아닙니까?”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"Not a bit of it, if they're properly treated.", "“제대로 다루기만 하면 조금도 그렇지 않네."], ["It must have been my handkerchief, I suppose.", "아무래도 내 손수건 탓이었나 보이."], ["I remember that I did blow my nose.\"", "내가 코를 풀었던 게 생각나는군.”"]], [["He hardly said a word in the way of thanks to his assistant.", "그는 저를 도운 사람에게 고맙다는 말은 거의 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["\"Where should I have been if you had not come to me?\"", "“자네가 와 주지 않았더라면 나는 지금 어디에 있었겠나?”"], ["he had exclaimed immediately after his deliverance; but having said that he didn't think it necessary to say much more to Eames.", "위기를 벗어난 직후에 그는 그렇게 외쳤다. 그러나 그 말을 하고 나서는 임스에게 더 무슨 말을 해야 한다고 여기지 않았다."], ["But he made himself very pleasant, and by the time he had reached the house his companion was almost glad that he had been forced to dine at the Manor House.", "그래도 그는 아주 유쾌하게 굴었고, 집에 이를 무렵에는 그와 동행한 젊은이도 저택에서 만찬을 들도록 떠밀린 것이 차라리 다행이다 싶어졌다."], ["\"And now we'll have a drink,\"", "“자, 이제 한잔하세.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"I don't know how you feel, but I never was so thirsty in my life.\"", "“자네는 어떤지 모르겠네만, 나는 평생 이렇게 목말라 본 적이 없네.”"]], [["Two servants immediately showed themselves, and evinced some surprise at Johnny's appearance.", "하인 둘이 곧바로 모습을 드러냈고, 조니의 몰골을 보고 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["\"Has the gentleman hurt hisself, my lord?\"", "“저 신사분께서 다치셨습니까요, 백작님?”"], ["asked the butler, looking at the blood upon our friend's face.", "우리 벗의 얼굴에 묻은 피를 보며 집사가 물었다."]], [["\"He has hurt his trowsers the worst, I believe,\"", "“다친 건 바지 쪽이 더 심할 걸세.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"And if he was to put on any of mine they'd be too short and too big, wouldn't they?", "“게다가 저 사람이 내 옷을 입으면 너무 짧고 너무 헐렁하겠지, 안 그런가?"], ["I am sorry you should be so uncomfortable, but you mustn't mind it for once.\"", "이렇게 불편하게 해서 미안하네만, 한 번쯤은 개의치 말게.”"]], [["\"I don't mind it a bit,\"", "“저는 조금도 개의치 않습니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"And I'm sure I don't,\"", "“나도 물론 개의치 않네.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"Mr. Eames is going to dine here, Vickers.\"", "“임스 씨가 여기서 만찬을 드실 걸세, 비커스.”"]], [["\"Yes, my lord.\"", "“예, 백작님.”"]], [["\"And his hat is down in the middle of the nineteen acres.", "“그리고 저 사람 모자가 십구 에이커 밭 한가운데에 떨어져 있네."], ["Let three or four men go for it.\"", "서넛 보내서 찾아오게 하게.”"]], [["\"Three or four men, my lord!\"", "“서넛이나요, 백작님!”"]], [["\"Yes,--three or four men.", "“그래,—서넛일세."], ["There's something gone wrong with that bull.", "저 황소한테 무슨 탈이 났어."], ["And you must get a boy with a pony to take a note into Guestwick, to Mrs.", "그리고 조랑말을 태운 아이를 하나 구해서 게스트윅의 임스 부인께 쪽지를 전하게 해야겠네."], ["Eames. Oh dear, I'm better now,\"", "아이고, 이제 좀 살겠군.”"], ["and he put down the tumbler from which he'd been drinking.", "그러고서 그는 마시고 있던 큰 잔을 내려놓았다."], ["\"Write your note here, and then we'll go and see my pet pheasants before dinner.\"", "“쪽지는 여기서 쓰게. 그러고 나서 만찬 전에 내가 아끼는 꿩들을 보러 가세.”"]], [["Vickers and the footman knew that something had happened of much moment, for the earl was usually very particular about his dinner-table.", "비커스와 하인은 무언가 대단히 중대한 일이 벌어졌음을 알았다. 백작이 평소에 제 만찬 식탁에 관해서는 몹시 까다로웠기 때문이다."], ["He expected every guest who sat there to be dressed in such guise as the fashion of the day demanded; and he himself, though his morning costume was by no means brilliant, never dined, even when alone, without having put himself into a suit of black, with a white cravat, and having exchanged the old silver hunting-watch which he carried during the day tied round his neck by a bit of old ribbon, for a small gold watch, with a chain and seals, which in the evening always dangled over his waistcoat.", "그는 그 자리에 앉는 손님이면 누구든 그날의 유행이 요구하는 차림새를 갖추기를 바랐다. 그리고 그 자신도, 낮 동안의 옷차림이야 결코 화려한 편이 못 되었으나, 혼자 있을 때조차 검은 정장에 흰 크라바트를 갖추어 입지 않고서는 결코 만찬을 들지 않았으며, 낮에는 낡은 리본 끈으로 목에 매달아 지니고 다니던 낡은 은제 사냥용 회중시계를, 저녁이면 늘 조끼 위로 늘어뜨리는 사슬과 인장이 달린 작은 금시계로 바꾸어 차곤 했다."], ["Dr. Gruffen had once been asked to dinner at Guestwick Manor.", "언젠가 그러펀 선생이 게스트윅 저택의 만찬에 초대받은 적이 있었다."], ["\"Just a bachelor's chop,\"", "“그저 총각의 고기 한 점일세.”"], ["said the earl; \"for there's nobody at home but myself.\"", "백작은 그렇게 말했다. “집에 나 말고는 아무도 없으니 말이야.”"], ["Whereupon Dr. Gruffen had come in coloured trowsers,--and had never again been asked to dine at Guestwick Manor.", "그런데 그러펀 선생은 색깔 있는 바지를 입고 왔고,—그 뒤로 다시는 게스트윅 저택의 만찬에 초대받지 못했다."], ["All this Vickers knew well; and now his lordship had brought young Eames home to dine with him with his clothes all hanging about him in a manner which Vickers declared in the servants'", "이 모든 것을 비커스는 잘 알고 있었다. 그런데 지금 백작님이 젊은 임스를 집으로 데려와 함께 만찬을 들자고 하시는데, 그 옷은 비커스가 하인들 방에서 반쯤도 점잖지 못하다고 잘라 말할 만한 꼴로 몸에 걸쳐져 있었다."], ["hall wasn't more than half decent. Therefore, they all knew that something very particular must have happened.", "그러니 그들 모두가 무언가 아주 특별한 일이 일어났음이 틀림없다고 여겼다."], ["\"It's some trouble about the bull, I know,\"", "“황소 때문에 무슨 일이 난 게지, 내가 알아.”"], ["said Vickers;--\"but bless you, the bull couldn't have tore his things in that way!\"", "비커스가 말했다. “—하지만 이보게, 황소가 저 양반 옷을 저런 식으로 찢어 놨을 리는 없잖아!”"]], [["Eames wrote his note, in which he told his mother that he had had an adventure with Lord De Guest, and that his lordship had insisted on bringing him home to dinner.", "임스는 쪽지를 썼는데, 드 게스트 경과 모험을 하나 겪었으며 백작님께서 기어이 자기를 집으로 데려와 만찬을 들게 하셨다고 어머니에게 알렸다."], ["\"I have torn my trowsers all to pieces,\"", "“바지를 온통 갈가리 찢었고,”"], ["he added in a postscript, \"and have lost my hat.", "그는 추신에 이렇게 덧붙였다. “모자를 잃어버렸습니다."], ["Everything else is all right.\"", "그 밖에는 다 괜찮습니다.”"], ["He was not aware that the earl also sent a short note to Mrs. Eames.", "백작 역시 임스 부인에게 짧은 쪽지를 보냈다는 것을 그는 알지 못했다."]], [["DEAR MADAM [ran the earl's note],--", "부인께 [백작의 쪽지는 이러했다],—"]], [["Your son has, under Providence, probably saved my life.", "부인의 아드님이 하늘의 섭리 아래 아마도 제 목숨을 구했습니다."], ["I will leave the story for him to tell.", "그 이야기는 아드님이 직접 하도록 남겨 두겠습니다."], ["He has been good enough to accompany me home, and will return to Guestwick after dinner with Dr. Crofts, who dines here.", "아드님은 고맙게도 저와 함께 집까지 와 주었고, 만찬 뒤에 여기서 만찬을 드는 크로프츠 선생과 함께 게스트윅으로 돌아갈 것입니다."], ["I congratulate you on having a son with so much cool courage and good feeling.", "이토록 침착한 용기와 착한 마음을 지닌 아드님을 두신 것을 축하드립니다."]], [["Your very faithful servant,", "부인의 참으로 충실한 종,"]], [["Guestwick Manor, Thursday, October, 186--.", "게스트윅 저택, 186─년 시월 목요일."]], [["And then they went to see the pheasants.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 꿩을 보러 갔다."], ["\"Now, I'll tell you what,\"", "“자, 내가 자네한테 할 말이 있네.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"I advise you to take to shooting.", "“사냥에 맛을 들여 보라고 권하고 싶네."], ["It's the amusement of a gentleman when a man chances to have the command of game.\"", "어쩌다 사냥거리를 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 처지가 되면, 그건 신사의 놀이일세.”"]], [["\"But I'm always up in London.\"", "“하지만 저는 늘 런던에 올라가 있는걸요.”"]], [["\"No, you're not.", "“아닐세, 그렇지 않네."], ["You're not up in London now.", "지금은 런던에 올라가 있지 않잖나."], ["You always have your holidays.", "자네도 늘 휴가가 있지 않은가."], ["If you choose to try it, I'll see that you have shooting enough while you're here.", "한번 해 볼 마음이 있다면, 여기 있는 동안 쏠 것은 얼마든지 있게 해 주겠네."], ["It's better than going to sleep under the trees.", "나무 밑에서 잠드는 것보다야 낫지."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하하하!"], ["I wonder what made you lay yourself down there.", "대체 무슨 생각으로 거기 드러누워 있었던 겐가."], ["You hadn't been fighting a bull that day?\"", "그날은 황소하고 싸운 것도 아니었을 텐데?”"]], [["\"No, my lord.", "“예, 백작님."], ["I hadn't seen the bull then.\"", "그때는 아직 황소를 보지도 못했습니다.”"]], [["\"Well; you think of what I've been saying.", "“자, 내가 한 말을 잘 생각해 보게."], ["When I say a thing, I mean it.", "나는 말을 하면 그대로 하는 사람일세."], ["You shall have shooting enough, if you have a mind to try it.\"", "한번 해 볼 마음만 있다면 쏠 것은 얼마든지 있을 걸세.”"], ["Then they looked at the pheasants, and pottered about the place till the earl said it was time to dress for dinner.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 꿩을 들여다보았고, 백작이 만찬 옷으로 갈아입을 때가 되었다고 말할 때까지 여기저기를 어슬렁거렸다."], ["\"That's hard upon you, isn't it?\" said he.", "“자네한테는 그게 곤란한 일이겠군, 그렇지?”"], ["\"But, at any rate, you can wash your hands, and get rid of the blood.", "그가 말했다. “하지만 어쨌든 손은 씻고 피는 좀 닦아 낼 수 있겠지."], ["I'll be down in the little drawing-room five minutes before seven, and I suppose I'll find you there.\"", "나는 일곱 시 오 분 전에 작은 응접실로 내려갈 텐데, 자네도 거기 있으리라 생각하네.”"]], [["At five minutes before seven Lord De Guest came into the small drawing-room, and found Johnny seated there, with a book before him.", "일곱 시 오 분 전에 드 게스트 경이 작은 응접실로 들어와 보니, 조니가 책을 한 권 앞에 놓고 앉아 있었다."], ["The earl was a little fussy, and showed by his manner that he was not quite at his ease, as some men do when they have any piece of work on hand which is not customary to them.", "백작은 어쩐지 부산스러웠고, 제게 익숙하지 않은 일거리를 손에 쥔 사람들이 더러 그러하듯이 마음이 썩 편치 않다는 것을 몸짓으로 드러내 보였다."], ["He held something in his hand, and shuffled a little as he made his way up the room.", "그는 손에 무언가를 쥐고 있었고, 방을 가로질러 올라오면서 발을 조금 끌었다."], ["He was dressed, as usual, in black; but his gold chain was not, as usual, dangling over his waistcoat.", "그는 여느 때처럼 검은 옷을 입고 있었으나, 여느 때와 달리 금사슬은 조끼 위로 늘어져 있지 않았다."]], [["\"Eames,\"", "“임스.”"], ["he said, \"I want you to accept a little present from me,--just as a memorial of our affair with the bull.", "그가 말했다. “내가 주는 자그마한 선물을 하나 받아 주었으면 하네,—그저 우리가 황소와 벌인 일을 기억할 물건으로 말이야."], ["It will make you think of it sometimes, when I'm perhaps gone.\"", "훗날 내가 세상을 떠난 뒤에도 이걸 보면 이따금 그 일이 생각날 걸세.”"]], [["\"Oh, my lord--\"", "“아, 백작님—”"]], [["\"It's my own watch, that I have been wearing for some time; but I've got another;--two or three, I believe, somewhere upstairs.", "“내가 얼마 전부터 차고 다니던 내 시계일세. 하지만 다른 게 또 있네;—위층 어딘가에 두세 개는 더 있을 걸세."], ["You mustn't refuse me.", "거절하면 안 되네."], ["I can't bear being refused.", "나는 거절당하는 걸 못 견디는 사람이야."], ["There are two or three little seals, too, which I have worn.", "내가 차던 작은 인장도 두어 개 달려 있네."], ["I have taken off the one with my arms, because that's of no use to you, and it is to me.", "우리 집안 문장이 새겨진 건 떼어 냈네. 그건 자네한테는 아무 쓸모가 없고, 내게는 쓸모가 있으니 말이야."], ["It doesn't want a key, but winds up at the handle, in this way,\"", "이건 열쇠가 필요 없고 이렇게 손잡이를 돌려서 태엽을 감는 걸세.”"], ["and the earl proceeded to explain the nature of the toy.", "그러고서 백작은 이 장난감의 됨됨이를 설명하기 시작했다."]], [["\"My lord, you think too much of what happened to-day,\"", "“백작님, 오늘 있었던 일을 너무 대단하게 여기시는 겁니다.”"], ["said Eames, stammering.", "임스가 더듬거리며 말했다."]], [["\"No, I don't; I think very little about it.", "“아닐세, 그렇지 않네. 나는 그 일은 거의 생각하지도 않네."], ["I know what I think of.", "내가 무슨 생각을 하는지는 내가 알지."], ["Put the watch in your pocket before the doctor comes.", "선생이 오기 전에 시계를 주머니에 넣어 두게."], ["There; I hear his horse.", "저기, 말발굽 소리가 들리는군."], ["Why didn't he drive over, and then he could have taken you back?\"", "왜 마차를 몰고 오지 않았을까, 그랬으면 자네를 태워다 줄 수 있었을 텐데?”"]], [["\"I can walk very well.\"", "“저는 걸어가도 아무렇지 않습니다.”"]], [["\"I'll make that all right.", "“내가 다 알아서 해 줌세."], ["The servant shall ride Crofts' horse, and bring back the little phaeton.", "하인더러 크로프츠의 말을 타고 가서 작은 경마차를 몰고 오게 하지."], ["How d'you do, doctor?", "어서 오시게, 선생."], ["You know Eames, I suppose?", "임스는 알 테지?"], ["You needn't look at him in that way.", "그런 눈으로 볼 것 없네."], ["His leg is not broken; it's only his trowsers.\"", "다리가 부러진 게 아니라 바지가 그런 것뿐이니까.”"], ["And then the earl told the story of the bull.", "그러고 나서 백작은 황소 이야기를 들려주었다."]], [["\"Johnny will become quite a hero in town,\"", "“조니는 런던에 가면 아주 영웅이 되겠습니다.”"], ["said Crofts.", "크로프츠가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; I fear he'll get the most of the credit; and yet I was at it twice as long as he was.", "“그래. 아무래도 공은 저 친구가 거의 다 가져갈 것 같아 걱정일세."], ["I'll tell you what, young men, when I got to that gate I didn't think I'd breath enough left in me to get over it.", "그런데도 내가 저 친구보다 두 배는 오래 그 일에 매달렸단 말이야. 내 말 좀 들어 보게, 젊은이들."], ["It's all very well jumping into a hedge when you're only two-and-twenty; but when a man comes to be sixty he likes to take his time about such things.", "내가 그 문에 이르렀을 때는 그걸 넘어갈 숨이 남아 있지 않다 싶더군. 스물두 살일 때야 산울타리로 뛰어드는 것쯤 아무렇지도 않지."], ["Dinner ready, is it? So am I.", "하지만 예순이 되고 보면 그런 일에는 시간을 좀 들이고 싶어지는 법일세."], ["I quite forgot that mutton chop of yours to-day, doctor.", "만찬 준비가 되었다고? 나도 준비가 됐네."], ["But I suppose a man may eat a good dinner after a fight with a bull?\"", "오늘 자네가 이르던 그 양갈비는 까맣게 잊고 있었네, 선생. 하지만 황소와 한판 붙고 나면 사람이 만찬을 든든히 들어도 되는 것 아니겠나?”"]], [["The evening passed by without any very pleasurable excitement, and I regret to say that the earl went fast to sleep in the drawing-room as soon as he had swallowed his cup of coffee.", "저녁 시간은 그리 즐거울 것도 없이 지나갔고, 유감스럽게도 백작은 커피 한 잔을 들이켜자마자 응접실에서 곤히 잠들어 버렸다고 말해 두어야겠다."], ["During dinner he had been very courteous to both his guests, but towards Eames he had used a good-humoured and almost affectionate familiarity.", "만찬 동안 그는 두 손님 모두에게 아주 정중했으나, 임스에게는 사람 좋고 거의 다정하다 할 만한 허물없음을 보였다."], ["He had quizzed him for having been found asleep under the tree, telling Crofts that he had looked very forlorn,--\"So that I haven't a doubt about his being in love,\"", "그는 임스가 나무 밑에서 잠든 채 발견된 일을 두고 놀려 대며, 크로프츠에게 저 친구가 아주 처량해 보이더라고 말했다,—“그러니 저 친구가 사랑에 빠졌다는 건 나는 의심하지 않네.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작은 그렇게 말했다."], ["And he had asked Johnny to tell the name of the fair one, bringing up the remnants of his half-forgotten classicalities to bear out the joke.", "그러고서 그는 반쯤 잊어버린 옛 고전 지식의 남은 조각들까지 끌어와 농담을 뒷받침하면서, 조니에게 그 어여쁜 이의 이름을 대 보라고 청했다."], ["\"If I am to take more of the severe Falernian,\"", "“내가 저 독한 팔레르눔을 더 들어야 한다면,”"], ["said he, laying his hand on the decanter of port, \"I must know the lady's name.", "그는 포도주 병에 손을 얹으며 말했다. “그 아가씨의 이름을 알아야겠네."], ["Whoever she be, I'm well sure you need not blush for her.", "그이가 누구든 자네가 그이 때문에 낯을 붉힐 까닭이 없다는 건 내가 잘 아네."], ["What!", "뭐라고!"], ["you refuse to tell!", "말하지 않겠다고!"], ["Then I'll drink no more.\"", "그렇다면 나도 더는 마시지 않겠네.”"], ["And so the earl had walked out of the dining-room; but not till he had perceived by his guest's cheeks that the joke had been too true to be pleasant.", "그러고서 백작은 만찬실에서 걸어 나갔다. 그러나 그전에 이미 손님의 뺨을 보고 그 농담이 유쾌하다 하기에는 너무 참말이었다는 것을 알아차린 뒤였다."], ["As he went, however, he leaned with his hand on Eames's shoulder, and the servants looking on saw that the young man was to be a favourite.", "그렇지만 나가면서 그는 임스의 어깨에 손을 얹고 몸을 기댔고, 그것을 지켜보던 하인들은 이 젊은이가 총애를 받게 되리라는 것을 알아보았다."], ["\"He'll make him his heir,\" said Vickers.", "“나리께서 저분을 상속인으로 삼으실 거야.”"], ["\"I shouldn't wonder a bit if he don't make him his heir.\"", "비커스가 말했다."], ["But to this the footman objected, endeavouring to prove to Mr.", "“저분을 상속인으로 삼으신대도 나는 조금도 이상할 게 없어.”"], ["Vickers that, in accordance with the law of the land, his lordship's second cousin, once removed, whom the earl had never seen, but whom he was supposed to hate, must be his heir.", "그러나 이에 하인이 반대하며, 나라의 법에 따르자면 백작님이 한 번도 뵌 적이 없고 미워하신다고들 하는 백작님의 육촌쯤 되는 사람이 상속인이 될 수밖에 없다는 것을 비커스 씨에게 증명해 보이려 들었다."], ["\"A hearl can never choose his own heir, like you or me,\"", "“백작 나리라고 해서 자네나 나처럼 제 상속인을 제 맘대로 고를 수 있는 게 아니라고.”"], ["said the footman, laying down the law.", "하인이 법을 늘어놓으며 말했다."], ["\"Can't he though really, now?", "“정말로 못 하신다고요?"], ["That's very hard on him; isn't it?\"", "그거 백작님께는 참 딱한 일이네요, 안 그래요?”"], ["said the pretty housemaid.", "예쁜 하녀가 말했다."], ["\"Psha,\"", "“쳇.”"], ["said Vickers: \"you know nothing about it.", "비커스가 말했다. “자네는 아무것도 모르는군."], ["My lord could make young Eames his heir to-morrow; that is, the heir of his property.", "백작님은 당장 내일이라도 젊은 임스를 상속인으로 삼으실 수 있어. 그러니까 재산의 상속인 말이야."], ["He couldn't make him a hearl, because that must go to the heirs of his body.", "백작으로 만들어 주실 수는 없지. 그건 몸에서 난 상속인한테 가야 하니까."], ["As to his leaving him the place here, I don't just know how that'd be; and I'm sure Richard don't.\"", "여기 이 저택을 남겨 주시는 문제라면, 그게 어찌 되는지는 나도 잘 모르겠고, 리처드도 모를 게 뻔해.”"]], [["\"But suppose he hasn't got any heirs of his body?\"", "“그렇지만 몸에서 난 상속인이 없으시면 어떡해요?”"], ["asked the pretty housemaid, who was rather fond of putting down Mr. Vickers.", "비커스 씨를 눌러 주기를 꽤 좋아하던 그 예쁜 하녀가 물었다."]], [["\"He must have heirs of his body,\"", "“몸에서 난 상속인은 있게 마련이야.”"], ["said the butler.", "집사가 말했다."], ["\"Everybody has 'em.", "“누구한테나 다 있는 거라고."], ["If a man don't know 'em himself, the law finds 'em out.\"", "제 스스로 모른다 해도 법이 찾아내 주는 법이야.”"], ["And then Mr. Vickers walked away, avoiding further dispute.", "그러고 나서 비커스 씨는 더 다투는 일을 피해 자리를 떴다."]], [["In the meantime, the earl was asleep upstairs, and the two young men from Guestwick did not find that they could amuse themselves with any satisfaction.", "그러는 동안 백작은 위층에서 잠들어 있었고, 게스트윅에서 온 두 젊은이는 도무지 무엇으로도 시간을 즐길 수가 없다는 것을 깨달았다. 저마다 책을 한 권씩 집어 들었다."], ["Each took up a book; but there are times at which a man is quite unable to read, and when a book is only a cover for his idleness or dulness.", "그러나 사람이 도무지 책을 읽을 수 없어 책이란 그저 제 게으름이나 따분함을 가려 주는 덮개에 지나지 않게 되는 때가 있는 법이다."], ["At last, Dr. Crofts suggested, in a whisper, that they might as well begin to think of going home.", "마침내 크로프츠 선생이 속삭이는 소리로, 이제 슬슬 집에 갈 생각을 하는 것이 어떻겠느냐고 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"Eh; yes; what?\"", "“어, 응, 뭐라고?”"], ["said the earl: \"I'm not asleep.\"", "백작이 말했다. “나 안 자네.”"], ["In answer to which the doctor said that he thought he'd go home, if his lordship would let him order his horse.", "이에 의사가 대답하기를, 백작님께서 말을 준비하라 일러 주신다면 이제 집으로 돌아갈까 한다고 했다."], ["But the earl was again fast bound in slumber, and took no further notice of the proposition.", "그러나 백작은 다시 깊은 잠에 단단히 묶여 그 말에는 더 아무런 관심도 보이지 않았다."]], [["\"Perhaps we could get off without waking him,\"", "“깨우지 않고 빠져나갈 수 있지 않을까요?”"], ["suggested Eames, in a whisper.", "임스가 속삭였다."]], [["\"Eh; what?\" said the earl.", "“어, 뭐라고?”"], ["So they both resumed their books, and submitted themselves to their martyrdom for a further period of fifteen minutes.", "백작이 말했다. 그리하여 두 사람은 다시 책을 집어 들고 십오 분을 더 그 고행에 몸을 맡겼다."], ["At the expiration of that time, the footman brought in tea.", "그 시간이 다 지났을 무렵 하인이 차를 들여왔다."]], [["\"Eh, what? tea!\"", "“어, 뭐야?"], ["said the earl.", "차라고!”"], ["\"Yes, we'll have a little tea.", "백작이 말했다. “그래, 차를 좀 들지."], ["I've heard every word you've been saying.\"", "자네들이 한 말은 한마디도 빠짐없이 다 들었네.”"], ["It was that assertion on the part of the earl which always made Lady Julia so angry.", "백작의 이런 주장이야말로 레이디 줄리아를 늘 화나게 만드는 것이었다."], ["\"You cannot have heard what I have been saying, Theodore, because I have said nothing,\"", "“제가 한 말을 들으셨을 리가 없지요, 시어도어. 저는 아무 말도 하지 않았으니까요.”"], ["she would reply.", "그녀는 그렇게 대꾸하곤 했다."], ["\"But I should have heard it if you had,\"", "“하지만 네가 말을 했더라면 나는 들었을 게다.”"], ["the earl would rejoin, snappishly.", "백작은 퉁명스럽게 되받곤 했다."], ["On the present occasion neither Crofts nor Eames contradicted him, and he took his tea and swallowed it while still three parts asleep.", "이날 저녁에는 크로프츠도 임스도 그의 말에 토를 달지 않았고, 그는 아직 사분의 삼쯤 잠든 채로 차를 받아 들이켰다."]], [["\"If you'll allow me, my lord, I think I'll order my horse,\"", "“허락해 주신다면, 백작님, 이제 제 말을 준비하라 이르겠습니다.”"], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; horse--yes--\"", "“그래, 말—그래—”"], ["said the earl, nodding.", "백작이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["\"But what are you to do, Eames, if I ride?\"", "“그런데 내가 말을 타고 가면 자네는 어쩌겠나, 임스?”"], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["\"I'll walk,\"", "“저는 걸어가지요.”"], ["whispered Eames, in his very lowest voice.", "임스가 아주 낮은 목소리로 속삭였다."]], [["\"What--what--what?\"", "“뭐—뭐—뭐라고?”"], ["said the earl, jumping up on his feet.", "백작이 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."], ["\"Oh, ah, yes; going away, are you?", "“아, 그래, 그렇지. 가려는 게로군?"], ["I suppose you might as well, as sit here and see me sleeping.", "하기야 여기 앉아서 내가 자는 걸 지켜보느니 그러는 편이 낫겠지."], ["But, doctor--I didn't snore, did I?\"", "그런데 선생—내가 코를 골지는 않았지?”"]], [["\"Only occasionally.\"", "“가끔은 고셨습니다.”"]], [["\"Not loud, did I?", "“크게는 아니었지?"], ["Come, Eames, did I snore loud?\"", "자, 임스, 내가 크게 골던가?”"]], [["\"Well, my lord, you did snore rather loud two or three times.\"", "“저, 백작님, 두세 번쯤은 꽤 크게 고셨습니다.”"]], [["\"Did I?\"", "“그랬나?”"], ["said the earl, in a voice of great disappointment.", "백작이 몹시 실망한 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"And yet, do you know, I heard every word you said.\"", "“그런데도 말이야, 자네들이 한 말은 한마디도 빠짐없이 다 들었단 말일세.”"]], [["The small phaeton had been already ordered, and the two young men started back to Guestwick together, a servant from the house riding the doctor's horse behind them.", "작은 경마차는 이미 준비되어 있었고, 두 젊은이는 함께 게스트윅으로 돌아가는 길에 올랐다. 저택의 하인 하나가 그 뒤에서 의사의 말을 타고 따라왔다."], ["\"Look here, Eames,\"", "“이보게, 임스.”"], ["said the earl, as they parted on the steps of the hall door.", "현관 계단에서 헤어지며 백작이 말했다."], ["\"You're going back to town the day after to-morrow, you say, so I shan't see you again?\"", "“모레 시내로 돌아간다고 했지. 그러면 다시는 못 보겠군?”"]], [["\"No, my lord,\"", "“예, 백작님.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Look you here, now.", "“자, 잘 들어 두게."], ["I shall be up for the Cattle-show before Christmas.", "나는 크리스마스 전에 가축 품평회 때문에 올라갈 걸세."], ["You must dine with me at my hotel, on the twenty-second of December, Pawkins's, in Jermyn Street; seven o'clock, sharp.", "십이월 이십이일에 내가 묵는 곳에서 나와 함께 만찬을 들어야 하네. 저민 스트리트의 포킨스일세."], ["Mind you do not forget, now.", "일곱 시 정각이야."], ["Put it down in your pocket-book when you get home.", "잊어버리면 안 되네. 집에 가거든 수첩에 적어 두게."], ["Good-by, doctor; good-by.", "잘 가게, 선생, 잘 가게."], ["I see I must stick to that mutton chop in the middle of the day.\"", "아무래도 나는 한낮의 그 양갈비를 지켜야겠어.”"], ["And then they drove off.", "그러고 나서 그들은 마차를 몰고 떠났다."]], [["\"He'll make him his heir for certain,\"", "“틀림없이 저분을 상속인으로 삼으실 거야.”"], ["said Vickers to himself, as he slowly returned to his own quarters.", "비커스는 제 방으로 천천히 돌아가며 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["\"You were returning from Allington, I suppose,\"", "“앨링턴에서 돌아오는 길이었나 보군.”"], ["said Crofts, \"when you came across Lord De Guest and the bull?\"", "크로프츠가 말했다. “드 게스트 경과 황소를 마주쳤을 때 말이야.”"]], [["\"Yes: I just walked over to say good-by to them.\"", "“예. 작별 인사나 하려고 잠깐 건너갔던 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Did you find them all well?\"", "“다들 잘 있던가?”"]], [["\"I only saw one.", "“한 분밖에 못 뵈었습니다."], ["The other two were out.\"", "나머지 두 분은 나가 계셨고요.”"]], [["\"Mrs. Dale, was it?\"", "“데일 부인이었나?”"]], [["\"No; it was Lily.\"", "“아뇨, 릴리였습니다.”"]], [["\"Sitting alone, thinking of her fine London lover, of course?", "“물론 혼자 앉아서 그 근사한 런던 연인을 생각하고 있었겠지?"], ["I suppose we ought to look upon her as a very lucky girl.", "우리야 그 아가씨를 아주 운 좋은 처녀로 보아야 하겠지."], ["I have no doubt she thinks herself so.\"", "저도 스스로 그렇게 여기고 있을 게 틀림없고.”"]], [["\"I'm sure I don't know,\"", "“저야 잘 모르겠습니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"I believe he's a very good young man,\"", "“나는 그가 아주 훌륭한 젊은이라고 믿네.”"], ["said the doctor; \"but I can't say I quite liked his manner.\"", "의사가 말했다. “하지만 그 사람 태도가 썩 마음에 들었다고는 말 못 하겠어.”"]], [["\"I should think not,\"", "“안 그러셨겠지요.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"But then in all probability he did not like mine a bit better, or perhaps yours either.", "“하지만 그쪽도 십중팔구 내 태도가 조금도 더 마음에 들지 않았을 걸세. 자네 태도도 아마 마찬가지였겠지."], ["And if so it's all fair.\"", "그렇다면야 피차 공평한 셈 아닌가.”"]], [["\"I don't see that it's a bit fair. He's a snob,\"", "“저는 그게 조금도 공평하다고 보지 않습니다."], ["said Eames; \"and I don't believe that I am.\"", "그자는 속물이에요.” 임스가 말했다."], ["He had taken a glass or two of the earl's \"severe Falernian,\"", "“그리고 저는 그렇지 않다고 믿습니다.”"], ["and was disposed to a more generous confidence, and perhaps also to stronger language, than might otherwise have been the case.", "그는 백작의 저 “독한 팔레르눔”을 한두 잔 들이켠 터라, 여느 때 같았으면 그러지 않았을 만큼 마음을 넉넉히 털어놓고, 어쩌면 말도 더 거칠게 하고 싶어져 있었다."]], [["\"No; I don't think he is a snob,\" said Crofts.", "“아니, 나는 그가 속물이라고 생각하지 않네.”"], ["\"Had he been so, Mrs.", "크로프츠가 말했다."], ["Dale would have perceived it.\"", "“그랬다면 데일 부인이 알아보셨을 걸세.”"]], [["\"You'll see,\"", "“두고 보세요.”"], ["said Johnny, touching up the earl's horse with energy as he spoke.", "조니가 말하면서 백작의 말에 힘껏 채찍을 대었다."], ["\"You'll see.", "“두고 보시라고요."], ["A man who gives himself airs is a snob; and he gives himself airs.", "거드름을 피우는 사람이 속물이지요. 그런데 그자는 거드름을 피웁니다."], ["And I don't believe he's a straightforward fellow.", "그리고 저는 그자가 솔직한 사람이라고 믿지 않습니다."], ["It was a bad day for us all when he came among them at Allington.\"", "그자가 앨링턴에서 그분들 사이에 끼어든 날은 우리 모두에게 나쁜 날이었어요.”"]], [["\"I can't say that I see that.\"", "“나는 그렇게는 보이지 않는걸.”"]], [["\"I do.", "“저는 그렇습니다."], ["But mind, I haven't spoken a word of this to any one.", "하지만 잘 들어 두세요, 저는 이 이야기를 누구한테도 한마디도 하지 않았습니다."], ["And I don't mean.", "앞으로도 할 생각이 없고요."], ["What would be the good?", "무슨 소용이 있겠습니까?"], ["I suppose she must marry him now?\"", "이제 그 아가씨는 그자와 결혼할 수밖에 없겠지요?”"]], [["\"Of course she must.\"", "“물론 그래야지.”"]], [["\"And be wretched all her life.", "“그리고 평생을 비참하게 살겠지요."], ["Oh-h-h-h!\"", "아—아—아—아!”"], ["and he muttered a deep groan.", "그러고서 그는 깊은 신음을 흘렸다."], ["\"I'll tell you what it is, Crofts.", "“제가 말씀드리지요, 크로프츠."], ["He is going to take the sweetest girl out of this country that ever was in it, and he don't deserve her.\"", "그자는 이 고장에 있었던 아가씨 중에 가장 사랑스러운 아가씨를 데려가려는 겁니다. 그런데 그자는 그 아가씨를 가질 자격이 없어요.”"]], [["\"I don't think she can be compared to her sister,\"", "“나는 그 아가씨가 언니에 견줄 만하다고는 생각하지 않네.”"], ["said Crofts slowly.", "크로프츠가 천천히 말했다."]], [["\"What; not Lily?\"", "“예? 릴리가요?”"], ["said Eames, as though the proposition made by the doctor were one that could not hold water for a minute.", "임스가 말했다. 마치 의사가 내놓은 그 말이 단 일 분도 버틸 수 없는 것이라는 듯이."]], [["\"I have always thought that Bell was the more admired of the two,\"", "“나는 늘 벨 쪽이 둘 중에 더 흠모를 받는다고 생각해 왔네.”"], ["said Crofts.", "크로프츠가 말했다."]], [["\"I'll tell you what,\"", "“제가 말씀드리지요.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."], ["\"I have never yet set my eyes on any human creature whom I thought so beautiful as Lily Dale.", "“저는 여태껏 릴리 데일만큼 아름답다고 여긴 사람을 이 세상에서 본 적이 없습니다."], ["And now that beast is going to marry her!", "그런데 이제 저 짐승 같은 자가 그 아가씨와 결혼한다는 거예요!"], ["I'll tell you what, Crofts; I'll manage to pick a quarrel with him yet.\"", "제가 말씀드리지요, 크로프츠. 저는 기어이 그자와 시비를 붙고야 말 겁니다.”"], ["Whereupon the doctor, seeing the nature of the complaint from which his companion was suffering, said nothing more, either about Lily or about Bell.", "이에 의사는 제 동행이 앓고 있는 병의 성격을 알아차리고, 릴리에 대해서도 벨에 대해서도 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["Soon after this Eames was at his own door, and was received there by his mother and sister with all the enthusiasm due to a hero.", "그러고 얼마 지나지 않아 임스는 제 집 문 앞에 이르렀고, 어머니와 누이는 영웅에게 마땅한 온갖 열광으로 그를 맞았다."], ["\"He has saved the earl's life!\" Mrs.", "“얘가 백작님의 목숨을 구했구나!”"], ["Eames had exclaimed to her daughter on reading Lord De Guest's note.", "드 게스트 경의 쪽지를 읽고서 임스 부인은 딸에게 그렇게 외쳤다."], ["\"Oh, goodness!\"", "“아이고, 세상에!”"], ["and she threw herself back upon the sofa almost in a fainting condition.", "그러고서 그녀는 거의 정신을 잃다시피 하여 소파에 몸을 뒤로 던졌다."]], [["\"Saved Lord De Guest's life!\"", "“드 게스트 경의 목숨을 구했다고요!”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes--under Providence,\" said Mrs.", "“그래—하늘의 섭리 아래서 말이다.”"], ["Eames, as though that latter fact added much to her son's good deed.", "임스 부인이 말했다. 마치 그 뒷말이 제 아들의 갸륵한 일에 무게를 크게 더해 주기라도 하는 듯이."]], [["\"But how did he do it?\"", "“그런데 어떻게 구했대요?”"]], [["\"By cool courage and good feeling--so his lordship says.", "“침착한 용기와 착한 마음으로—백작님 말씀이 그렇구나."], ["But I wonder how he really did do it?\"", "그런데 대체 어떻게 한 걸까?”"]], [["\"Whatever way it was, he's torn all his clothes and lost his hat,\"", "“어떻게 했든 옷은 죄다 찢어 놓고 모자는 잃어버렸대요.”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't care a bit about that,\"", "“나는 그런 건 조금도 아깝지 않다.”"], ["said Mrs. Eames.", "임스 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I wonder whether the earl has any interest at the Income-tax.", "“백작님이 소득세 사무국에 무슨 연줄이라도 있으실까."]], [["\"What a thing it would be if he could get Johnny a step.", "“조니를 한 계단 올려 주실 수 있다면 얼마나 좋은 일이겠니."], ["It would be seventy pounds a year at once.", "당장 연 칠십 파운드가 될 텐데."], ["He was quite right to stay and dine when his lordship asked him.", "백작님이 청하셨을 때 남아서 만찬을 든 건 아주 잘한 일이야."], ["And so Dr. Crofts is there.", "그리고 크로프츠 선생도 거기 계신다니."], ["It couldn't have been anything in the doctoring way, I suppose.\"", "설마 의술과 관계된 일은 아니었겠지.”"]], [["\"No, I should say not; because of what he says of his trowsers.\"", "“네, 아닐 거예요. 바지 이야기를 하는 걸 보면요.”"], ["And so the two ladies were obliged to wait for John's return.", "그리하여 두 부인은 존이 돌아오기를 기다릴 수밖에 없었다."]], [["\"How did you do it, John?\"", "“어떻게 한 게냐, 존?”"], ["said his mother, embracing him, as soon as the door was opened.", "문이 열리자마자 그를 끌어안으며 어머니가 말했다."]], [["\"How did you save the earl's life?\"", "“오빠, 백작님 목숨을 어떻게 구한 거예요?”"], ["said Mary, who was standing behind her mother.", "어머니 뒤에 서 있던 메리가 말했다."]], [["\"Would his lordship really have been killed, if it had not been for you?\"", "“네가 아니었으면 백작님이 정말로 돌아가셨겠니?”"], ["asked Mrs. Eames.", "임스 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"And was he very much hurt?\"", "“그리고 오빠는 많이 다쳤어요?”"], ["asked Mary.", "메리가 물었다."]], [["\"Oh, bother,\"", "“아, 귀찮아요.”"], ["said Johnny, on whom the results of the day's work, together with the earl's Falernian, had made some still remaining impression.", "조니가 말했다. 그날 하루의 일에다 백작의 팔레르눔까지 겹쳐 아직도 얼마간 자취를 남기고 있었던 것이다."], ["On ordinary occasions, Mrs.", "여느 때 같았으면 임스 부인은 아들에게 그런 대답을 듣고 마음이 상했을 것이다."], ["Eames would have felt hurt at being so answered by her son; but at the present moment she regarded him as standing so high in general favour that she took no offence.", "그러나 지금 이 순간에는 아들이 세상의 총애를 한 몸에 받고 서 있다고 여겼으므로 조금도 언짢아하지 않았다. “아이고, 조니야, 어서 이야기해 다오."], ["\"Oh, Johnny, do tell us. Of course we must be very anxious to know it all.\"", "우리야 물론 그 이야기를 전부 알고 싶어 안달일 수밖에 없지 않겠니.”"]], [["\"There's nothing to tell, except that a bull ran at the earl, as I was going by; so I went into the field and helped him, and then he made me stay and dine with him.\"", "“이야기할 게 없어요. 제가 지나가는데 황소가 백작님께 달려들기에 밭으로 들어가서 도와드렸고, 그랬더니 붙들어 두시고 함께 만찬을 들자고 하신 것뿐이에요.”"]], [["\"But his lordship says that you saved his life,\"", "“하지만 백작님은 오빠가 목숨을 구해 드렸다고 하셨잖아요.”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."]], [["\"Under Providence,\"", "“하늘의 섭리 아래서 말이지.”"], ["added their mother.", "어머니가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"At any rate, he has given me a gold watch and chain,\"", "“어쨌든 금시계하고 사슬을 주셨어요.”"], ["said Johnny, drawing the present out of his pocket.", "조니가 주머니에서 선물을 꺼내며 말했다."], ["\"I wanted a watch badly.", "“시계가 몹시 갖고 싶긴 했어요."], ["All the same, I didn't like taking it.\"", "그래도 받는 건 내키지 않았습니다.”"]], [["\"It would have been very wrong to refuse,\"", "“거절했으면 아주 잘못이었을 게다.”"], ["said his mother.", "어머니가 말했다."], ["\"And I am so glad you have been so fortunate.", "“그리고 네가 이렇게 운이 좋았다니 나는 참 기쁘구나."], ["And look here, Johnny: when a friend like that comes in your way, don't turn your back on him.\"", "그리고 얘야, 조니야, 그런 벗이 네 길에 나타나거든 등을 돌리면 안 된다.”"], ["Then, at last, he thawed beneath their kindness, and told them the whole of the story.", "그러자 마침내 그는 두 사람의 다정함에 마음이 풀려 이야기 전부를 들려주었다."], ["I fear that in recounting the earl's efforts with the spud, he hardly spoke of his patron with all that deference which would have been appropriate.", "다만 백작이 괭이를 들고 애쓰던 대목을 옮길 때, 제 후원자를 이야기하는 말투에 마땅히 있어야 할 만한 공경이 그리 담겨 있지 않았던 것 같아 나는 걱정스럽다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIII. MR. PLANTAGENET PALLISER.", "# 제23장. 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨"]], [["A week passed over Mr. Crosbie's head at Courcy Castle without much inconvenience to him from the well-known fact of his matrimonial engagement.", "크로즈비 씨는 혼약을 맺었다는, 이미 널리 알려진 사실 때문에 별다른 불편을 겪는 일도 없이 코시 성에서 한 주를 흘려보냈다."], ["Both George De Courcy and John De Courcy had in their different ways charged him with his offence, and endeavoured to annoy him by recurring to the subject; but he did not care much for the wit or malice of George or John De Courcy.", "조지 드 코시와 존 드 코시는 저마다 제 방식대로 그의 죄를 들추어 대며 그 화제를 자꾸 끄집어내어 그를 성가시게 하려 들었으나, 그는 조지나 존 드 코시의 재치나 심술 따위에는 별로 마음을 쓰지 않았다."], ["The countess had hardly alluded to Lily Dale after those few words which she said on the first day of his visit, and seemed perfectly willing to regard his doings at Allington as the occupation natural to a young man in such a position.", "백작부인은 그가 찾아온 첫날에 했던 그 몇 마디 말고는 릴리 데일에 관해 거의 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 앨링턴에서 그가 한 짓을 그런 처지에 놓인 젊은 남자에게는 지극히 자연스러운 소일거리로 여기려는 듯했다."], ["He had been seduced down to a dull country house, and had, as a matter of course, taken to such amusements as the place afforded.", "그는 따분한 시골집으로 꾀여 내려갔고, 그러니 당연하게도 그곳에서 할 만한 놀이에 손을 댄 것이었다."], ["He had shot the partridges and made love to the young lady, taking those little recreations as compensation for the tedium of the squire's society.", "자고새를 쏘았고 젊은 아가씨에게 사랑을 속삭였으니, 지주와 어울려 지내는 지루함에 대한 보상으로 그런 자잘한 즐길 거리를 취한 것이었다."], ["Perhaps he had gone a little too far with the young lady; but then no one knew better than the countess how difficult it is for a young man to go far enough without going too far.", "어쩌면 그가 그 젊은 아가씨와 조금 지나치게 멀리 나갔는지도 모른다. 그러나 젊은 남자가 지나치게 멀리 가지 않으면서 충분히 멀리 가기가 얼마나 어려운 일인지를 백작부인보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없었다."], ["It was not her business to make herself a censor on a young man's conduct.", "젊은 남자의 처신을 두고 검열관 노릇을 하는 것은 제가 할 일이 아니었다."], ["The blame, no doubt, rested quite as much with Miss Dale as with him.", "잘못은 틀림없이 그 못지않게 데일 양에게도 있었다."], ["She was quite sorry that any young lady should be disappointed; but if girls will be imprudent, and set their caps at men above their mark, they must encounter disappointment.", "어느 젊은 아가씨든 낭패를 보는 것은 안된 일이지만, 처녀들이 굳이 경솔하게 굴며 제 분수에 넘치는 남자에게 눈독을 들인다면 낭패를 겪을 수밖에 없는 법이다."], ["With such language did Lady De Courcy speak of the affair among her daughters, and her daughters altogether agreed with her that it was out of the question that Mr. Crosbie should marry Lily Dale.", "드 코시 백작부인은 딸들 사이에서 그 일을 두고 이런 식으로 말했고, 딸들도 크로즈비 씨가 릴리 데일과 혼인한다는 것은 말도 안 되는 일이라는 데 하나같이 어머니와 뜻을 같이했다."], ["From Alexandrina he encountered during the week none of that raillery which he had expected.", "알렉산드리나에게서는 그 한 주 동안 그가 예상했던 놀림을 조금도 받지 않았다."], ["He had promised to explain to her before he left the castle all the circumstances of his acquaintance with Lily, and she at last showed herself determined to demand the fulfilment of this promise; but, previous to that, she said nothing to manifest either offence or a lessened friendship.", "그는 성을 떠나기 전에 릴리와 알고 지낸 내력을 모두 설명하겠노라 약속해 두었고, 그녀도 마침내 그 약속을 이행하라고 요구할 뜻을 내비쳤다. 그러나 그전까지는 노여움도, 우정이 식었다는 기색도 전혀 내비치지 않았다."], ["And I regret to say, that in the intercourse which had taken place between them, that friendship was by no means less tender that it had been in London.", "그리고 유감스럽게도 나는 이렇게 말해 두어야겠다. 그동안 두 사람 사이에 오간 것으로 보건대 그 우정은 런던에서 그러했던 것보다 결코 덜 살가워지지 않았다고."]], [["\"And when will you tell me what you promised?\"", "“그런데 약속하신 건 언제 이야기해 주실 건가요?”"], ["she asked him one afternoon, speaking in a low voice, as they were standing together at the window of the billiard-room, in that idle half-hour which always occurs before the necessity for dinner preparation has come.", "어느 날 오후, 저녁 식사 준비를 서두를 필요가 아직 생기지 않은 그 한가한 삼십 분 동안, 두 사람이 당구실 창가에 나란히 서 있을 때 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 물었다."], ["She had been riding and was still in her habit, and he had returned from shooting.", "그녀는 말을 타고 나갔다 온 참이라 아직 승마복 차림이었고, 그는 사냥에서 돌아온 참이었다."], ["She knew that she looked more than ordinarily well in her tall straight hat and riding gear, and was wont to hang about the house, walking skilfully with her upheld drapery, during this period of the day.", "그녀는 키 높은 곧은 모자와 승마 차림새를 하고 있을 때 제 모습이 여느 때보다 낫다는 것을 알고 있었고, 하루 중 이맘때면 자락을 손으로 받쳐 든 채 능숙한 걸음으로 집 안을 오가며 서성이는 버릇이 있었다."], ["It was dusk, but not dark, and there was no artificial light in the billiard-room.", "어스름했으나 어둡지는 않았고, 당구실에는 켜 놓은 불이 없었다."], ["There had been some pretence of knocking about the balls, but it had been only pretence.", "공을 쳐 보는 시늉을 얼마간 하기는 했으나 그저 시늉일 뿐이었다."], ["\"Even Diana,\" she had said, \"could not have played billiards in a habit.\"", "“디아나라 한들 승마복 차림으로 당구를 칠 수는 없었을 거예요.”"], ["Then she had put down her mace, and they had stood talking together in the recess of a large bow-window.", "그녀는 그렇게 말했더랬다. 그러고서 밀대를 내려놓았고, 두 사람은 큼직한 퇴창 안쪽에 들어서서 이야기를 나누며 서 있게 된 것이었다."]], [["\"And what did I promise?\"", "“내가 무엇을 약속했지요?”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"You know well enough.", "“잘 아시잖아요."], ["Not that it is a matter of any special interest to me; only, as you undertook to promise, of course my curiosity has been raised.\"", "저한테 특별히 관심 있는 일이라서가 아니라, 굳이 약속을 하겠다고 나서셨으니 당연히 궁금증이 생긴 거지요.”"]], [["\"If it be of no special interest,\"", "“특별히 관심 있는 일이 아니라면,”"], ["said Crosbie, \"you will not object to absolve me from my promise.\"", "크로즈비가 말했다. “저를 그 약속에서 놓아주셔도 괜찮으시겠군요.”"]], [["\"That is just like you,\"", "“꼭 당신다운 말씀이에요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"And how false you men always are.", "“남자들은 어쩌면 그렇게 늘 거짓되지요."], ["You made up your mind to buy my silence on a distasteful subject by pretending to offer me your future confidence; and now you tell me that you do not mean to confide in me.\"", "당신은 언젠가 속내를 털어놓겠다는 시늉을 해서 달갑잖은 화제에 대한 제 침묵을 사려고 마음먹었고, 이제 와서는 저에게 속내를 털어놓을 생각이 없다고 말하는군요.”"]], [["\"You begin by telling me that the matter is one that does not in the least interest you.\"", "“그 일이 조금도 관심 없는 일이라고 먼저 말씀하신 건 당신입니다.”"]], [["\"That is so false again!", "“그것 또한 참 거짓된 말이에요!"], ["You know very well what I meant.", "제 말뜻을 잘 아시면서."], ["Do you remember what you said to me the day you came?", "오시던 날 저에게 하신 말씀을 기억하세요?"], ["and am I not bound to tell you after that, that your marriage with this or that young lady is not matter of special interest to me?", "그런 말을 들은 뒤라면, 당신이 이 아가씨와 혼인하든 저 아가씨와 혼인하든 저에게는 특별한 관심거리가 아니라고 말해 두는 것이 마땅하지 않나요?"], ["Still, as your friend--\"", "그래도 당신 벗으로서는—”"]], [["\"Well, as my friend!\"", "“그래서, 제 벗으로서는!”"]], [["\"I shall be glad to know--.", "“알게 되면 기쁘겠지요—. 하지만 속내를 털어놓아 달라고 조르지는 않겠어요."], ["But I am not going to beg for your confidence; only I tell you this fairly, that no man is so mean in my eyes as a man who fights under false colours.\"", "다만 이것만은 솔직히 말해 두지요. 거짓 깃발을 달고 싸우는 남자만큼 제 눈에 비열해 보이는 사람은 없다는 것을요.”"]], [["\"And am I fighting under false colours?\"", "“그럼 제가 거짓 깃발을 달고 싸우고 있다는 말씀입니까?”"]], [["\"Yes, you are.\"", "“네, 그래요.”"], ["And now, as she spoke, the Lady Alexandrina blushed beneath her hat; and dull as was the remaining light of the evening, Crosbie, looking into her face, saw her heightened colour.", "그리고 이렇게 말하는 동안 레이디 알렉산드리나는 모자 아래로 얼굴을 붉혔다. 저녁의 남은 빛이 흐릿했는데도 크로즈비는 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보다가 짙어진 홍조를 보았다."], ["\"Yes, you are.", "“네, 그래요."], ["A gentleman is fighting under false colours who comes into a house like this, with a public rumour of his being engaged, and then conducts himself as though nothing of the kind existed.", "약혼했다는 소문이 파다한 채로 이런 집에 들어와서는 그런 일이라곤 아예 없다는 듯이 처신하는 신사라면, 그건 거짓 깃발을 달고 싸우는 것이지요."], ["Of course, it is not anything to me specially; but that is fighting under false colours.", "물론 저에게 특별히 무슨 상관이 있는 일은 아니에요. 하지만 그건 거짓 깃발을 달고 싸우는 겁니다."], ["Now, sir, you may redeem the promise you made me when you first came here,--or you may let it alone.\"", "자, 이제 처음 여기 오셨을 때 저에게 하신 약속을 지키시든지—아니면 그냥 내버려 두시든지 하세요.”"]], [["It must be acknowledged that the lady was fighting her battle with much courage, and also with some skill.", "이 부인이 제 싸움을 꽤 용기 있게, 게다가 얼마간 솜씨 있게 치르고 있었다는 것은 인정해야 한다."], ["In three or four days Crosbie would be gone; and this victory, if it were ever to be gained, must be gained in those three or four days.", "사나흘 뒤면 크로즈비는 떠날 것이었다. 그러니 이 승리는, 얻을 수 있는 것이라면, 그 사나흘 안에 얻어 내야만 했다."], ["And if there were to be no victory, then it would be only fair that Crosbie should be punished for his duplicity, and that she should be avenged as far as any revenge might be in her power.", "그리고 승리가 없을 것이라면, 그때는 크로즈비가 제 이중성 때문에 벌을 받고 그녀는 제 힘이 닿는 한 앙갚음을 하는 것이 마땅한 일이었다."], ["Not that she meditated any deep revenge, or was prepared to feel any strong anger.", "그렇다고 그녀가 무슨 깊은 앙갚음을 궁리했다거나 대단한 분노를 느낄 채비가 되어 있었다는 말은 아니다."], ["She liked Crosbie as well as she had ever liked any man.", "그녀는 여태까지 어떤 남자를 좋아해 본 만큼은 크로즈비를 좋아했다."], ["She believed that he liked her also.", "그도 저를 좋아한다고 믿었다."], ["She had no conception of any very strong passion, but conceived that a married life was more pleasant than one of single bliss.", "아주 강렬한 정열이라는 것은 도무지 짐작조차 하지 못했으나, 혼인한 삶이 홀몸의 복락보다는 즐거우리라고 여겼다."], ["She had no doubt that he had promised to make Lily Dale his wife, but so had he previously promised her, or nearly so.", "그가 릴리 데일을 아내로 삼겠다고 약속한 것은 의심하지 않았다. 그러나 그전에 저에게도 그렇게 약속했거나 그와 다름없는 말을 했던 것이다."], ["It was a fair game, and she would win it if she could.", "그것은 공정한 겨루기였고, 할 수만 있다면 그녀는 이길 작정이었다."], ["If she failed, she would show her anger; but she would show it in a mild, weak manner,--turning up her nose at Lily before Crosbie's face, and saying little things against himself behind his back.", "진다면 노여움을 드러낼 터였다. 그러나 온순하고 힘없는 방식으로, 즉 크로즈비의 면전에서 릴리를 두고 코를 치켜올리고 그의 등 뒤에서 그를 헐뜯는 자잘한 말이나 하는 식으로 드러낼 것이었다."], ["Her wrath would not carry her much beyond that.", "그녀의 분노가 그녀를 그보다 더 멀리 데려가지는 않을 터였다."]], [["\"Now, sir, you may redeem the promise you made me when you first came here,--or you may let it alone.\"", "“자, 이제 처음 여기 오셨을 때 저에게 하신 약속을 지키시든지—아니면 그냥 내버려 두시든지 하세요.”"], ["So she spoke, and then she turned her face away from him, gazing out into the darkness.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하고서 그에게서 얼굴을 돌려 어둠 속을 내다보았다."]], [["\"Alexandrina!\"", "“알렉산드리나!”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, sir?", "“네, 무슨 일이시죠?"], ["But you have no right to speak to me in that style.", "하지만 당신은 저에게 그런 투로 말할 권리가 없어요."], ["You know that you have no right to call me by my name in that way!\"", "그런 식으로 제 이름을 부를 권리가 없다는 걸 아시잖아요!”"]], [["\"You mean that you insist upon your title?\"", "“작위를 붙여 부르라고 고집하신다는 말씀입니까?”"]], [["\"All ladies insist on what you call their title, from gentlemen, except under the privilege of greater intimacy than you have the right to claim.", "“여자라면 누구나 신사에게서 당신이 작위라 부르는 그것을 붙여 듣기를 고집한답니다. 당신이 요구할 권리가 있는 것보다 더 가까운 사이라는 특권이 있지 않고서야 말이지요."], ["You did not call Miss Dale by her Christian name till you had obtained permission, I suppose?\"", "당신도 허락을 얻기 전에는 데일 양을 세례명으로 부르지 않으셨겠지요?”"]], [["\"You used to let me call you so.\"", "“전에는 그렇게 부르도록 허락해 주셨는데요.”"]], [["\"Never!", "“한 번도요!"], ["Once or twice, when you have done so, I have not forbidden it, as I should have done.", "당신이 그렇게 부른 적이 한두 번 있었을 때 제가 마땅히 금했어야 할 것을 금하지 않았을 뿐이에요."], ["Very well, sir, as you have nothing to tell me, I will leave you.", "좋아요, 저에게 하실 말씀이 없다니 그만 물러가겠어요."], ["I must confess that I did not think you were such a coward.\"", "당신이 이렇게 겁쟁이일 줄은 몰랐다고 고백해야겠군요.”"], ["And she prepared to go, gathering up the skirts of her habit, and taking up the whip which she had laid on the window-sill.", "그러고서 그녀는 승마복 자락을 그러모으고 창턱에 얹어 두었던 채찍을 집어 들며 갈 채비를 했다."]], [["\"Stay a moment, Alexandrina,\"", "“잠깐만요, 알렉산드리나.” 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"I am not happy, and you should not say words intended to make me more miserable.\"", "“저는 행복하지 않습니다. 저를 더 비참하게 만들려는 말씀은 하지 마십시오.”"]], [["\"And why are you unhappy?\"", "“그럼 왜 불행하신데요?”"]], [["\"Because-- I will tell you instantly, if I may believe that I am telling you only, and not the whole household.\"", "“그건— 이 집안 전체가 아니라 당신에게만 말하는 것이라고 믿어도 된다면 당장 말씀드리지요.”"]], [["\"Of course I shall not talk of it to others.", "“물론 다른 사람들에게 옮기지 않아요."], ["Do you think that I cannot keep a secret?\"", "제가 비밀 하나 지키지 못할 사람으로 보이나요?”"]], [["\"It is because I have promised to marry one woman, and because I love another.", "“한 여자와 혼인하겠다고 약속해 놓고 다른 여자를 사랑하기 때문입니다."], ["I have told you everything now; and if you choose to say again that I am fighting under false colours I will leave the castle before you can see me again.\"", "이제 다 말씀드렸습니다. 그런데도 제가 거짓 깃발을 달고 싸운다고 또 말씀하시겠다면, 저는 당신이 저를 다시 보기 전에 이 성을 떠나겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Mr. Crosbie!\"", "“크로즈비 씨!”"]], [["\"Now you know it all, and may imagine whether or no I am very happy.", "“이제 다 아셨으니 제가 그리 행복한지 어떤지도 짐작하시겠지요."], ["I think you said it was time to dress;--suppose we go?\"", "옷 갈아입을 때가 되었다고 하신 것 같은데요—갈까요?”"], ["And without further speech the two went off to their separate rooms.", "그러고서 더는 말없이 두 사람은 각자의 방으로 갔다."]], [["Crosbie, as soon as he was alone in his chamber, sat himself down in his arm-chair, and went to work striving to make up his mind as to his future conduct.", "크로즈비는 제 방에 혼자 남게 되자마자 안락의자에 앉아 앞으로 어떻게 처신할지 마음을 정하려 애쓰기 시작했다."], ["It must not be supposed that the declaration just made by him had been produced solely by his difficulty at the moment.", "그가 방금 한 고백이 오로지 그 순간의 곤경 때문에 나온 것이라고 여겨서는 안 된다."], ["The atmosphere of Courcy Castle had been at work upon him for the last week past.", "코시 성의 공기가 지난 한 주 내내 그에게 작용해 온 터였다."], ["And every word that he had heard, and every word that he had spoken, had tended to destroy all that was good and true within him, and to foster all that was selfish and false.", "그가 들은 말 하나하나, 그가 한 말 하나하나가 그의 안에 있던 선하고 참된 것을 모조리 허물고 이기적이고 거짓된 것을 모조리 북돋우는 쪽으로 작용했다."], ["He had said to himself a dozen times during that week that he never could be happy with Lily Dale, and that he never could make her happy.", "그는 그 한 주 동안 열두 번도 더 스스로에게 말했다. 자기는 릴리 데일과 결코 행복할 수 없고 그녀를 결코 행복하게 해 줄 수도 없다고."], ["And then he had used the old sophistry in his endeavour to teach himself that it was right to do that which he wished to do.", "그러고는 제가 하고 싶은 일을 하는 것이 옳다고 스스로를 가르치려 애쓰면서 그 해묵은 궤변을 끌어 썼다."], ["Would it not be better for Lily that he should desert her, than marry her against the dictates of his own heart?", "제 마음의 명령을 거슬러 릴리와 혼인하는 것보다는 그녀를 버리는 편이 릴리에게 더 낫지 않겠는가?"], ["And if he really did not love her, would he not be committing a greater crime in marrying her than in deserting her?", "그리고 그가 정말로 그녀를 사랑하지 않는다면, 그녀를 버리는 것보다 그녀와 혼인하는 것이 더 큰 죄를 짓는 일이 되지 않겠는가?"], ["He confessed to himself that he had been very wrong in allowing the outer world to get such a hold upon him that the love of a pure girl like Lily could not suffice for his happiness.", "그는 바깥세상이 저를 그토록 단단히 옭아매도록 내버려 두어 릴리처럼 순결한 처녀의 사랑만으로는 제 행복이 채워지지 않게 만든 것이 아주 잘못이었다고 스스로 인정했다."], ["But there was the fact, and he found himself unable to contend against it.", "그러나 그것은 엄연한 사실이었고, 그는 그것과 맞서 싸울 힘이 제게 없음을 알았다."], ["If by any absolute self-sacrifice he could secure Lily's well-being, he would not hesitate for a moment.", "어떤 절대적인 자기희생으로 릴리의 안녕을 지켜 줄 수 있다면 그는 한순간도 망설이지 않을 것이었다."], ["But would it be well to sacrifice her as well as himself?", "그러나 저와 함께 그녀까지 제물로 바치는 것이 과연 잘하는 일이겠는가?"]], [["He had discussed the matter in this way within his own breast, till he had almost taught himself to believe that it was his duty to break off his engagement with Lily; and he had also almost taught himself to believe that a marriage with a daughter of the house of Courcy would satisfy his ambition and assist him in his battle with the world.", "그는 제 가슴속에서 그 문제를 이런 식으로 따져 온 끝에, 릴리와의 약혼을 깨는 것이 제 의무라고 거의 스스로 믿게 만들어 놓았다. 그리고 코시 가문의 딸과 혼인하는 것이 제 야심을 채워 주고 세상과의 싸움에서 저를 도와주리라는 것도 거의 스스로 믿게 만들어 놓은 터였다."], ["That Lady Alexandrina would accept him he felt certain, if he could only induce her to forgive him for his sin in becoming engaged to Miss Dale.", "데일 양과 약혼한 죄를 용서하도록 그녀를 이끌기만 한다면 레이디 알렉산드리나가 저를 받아들이리라는 것은 확실하다고 느꼈다."], ["How very prone she would be to forgiveness in this matter, he had not divined, having not as yet learned how easily such a woman can forgive such a sin, if the ultimate triumph be accorded to herself.", "다만 그 문제에서 그녀가 얼마나 선뜻 용서할 마음을 품고 있는지까지는 헤아리지 못했으니, 그런 여자가 그런 죄를—최후의 승리가 제게 돌아오기만 한다면—얼마나 쉽게 용서하는지 아직 배우지 못한 까닭이었다."]], [["And there was another reason which operated much with Crosbie, urging him on in his present mood and wishes, though it should have given an exactly opposite impulse to his heart.", "그리고 크로즈비를 크게 움직인 또 하나의 이유가 있어 그를 지금의 심경과 바람 쪽으로 몰아갔으니, 마땅히 그의 마음에 정반대의 충동을 주었어야 할 것이었는데도 그러했다."], ["He had hesitated as to marrying Lily Dale at once, because of the smallness of his income.", "그는 수입이 적다는 이유로 릴리 데일과 당장 혼인하기를 망설였다."], ["Now he had a prospect of considerable increase to that income.", "그런데 이제 그 수입이 상당히 늘어날 전망이 생긴 것이다."], ["One of the commissioners at his office had been promoted to some greater commissionership, and it was understood by everybody that the secretary at the General Committee Office would be the new commissioner.", "그가 다니는 사무국의 위원 하나가 더 높은 위원 자리로 승진했고, 일반위원회 사무국의 사무국장이 새 위원이 되리라는 것은 누구나 알고 있는 일이었다."], ["As to that there was no doubt.", "그 점에는 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["But then the question had arisen as to the place of secretary.", "그러나 그러자 사무국장 자리를 누가 맡느냐 하는 문제가 생겼다."], ["Crosbie had received two or three letters on the subject, and it seemed that the likelihood of his obtaining this step in the world was by no means slight.", "크로즈비는 그 일로 두세 통의 편지를 받은 터였고, 그가 이 출셋길의 한 계단을 얻을 가망은 결코 적지 않아 보였다."], ["It would increase his official income from seven hundred a year to twelve, and would place him altogether above the world.", "그렇게 되면 관직에서 나오는 그의 수입이 연 칠백 파운드에서 천이백 파운드로 늘어나 그를 아주 세상 위로 올려놓을 것이었다."], ["His friend, the present secretary, had written to him, assuring him that no other probable competitor was spoken of as being in the field against him.", "지금 사무국장으로 있는 그의 벗이 편지를 보내, 그와 겨룰 만한 다른 경쟁자가 나섰다는 말은 들리지 않는다고 장담해 주었다."], ["If such good fortune awaited him, would it not smooth any present difficulty which lay in the way of his marriage with Lily Dale?", "그런 행운이 그를 기다리고 있다면, 릴리 데일과의 혼인을 가로막고 있는 지금의 어려움쯤은 말끔히 풀리지 않겠는가?"], ["But, alas, he had not looked at the matter in that light!", "그러나 아아, 그는 그 일을 그런 빛 아래에서 보지 않았다!"], ["Might not the countess help him to this preferment?", "백작부인이 그 승진에 도움을 줄 수 있지 않을까?"], ["And if his destiny intended for him the good things of this world,--secretaryships, commissionerships, chairmanships, and such like, would it not be well that he should struggle on in his upward path by such assistance as good connections might give him?", "그리고 그의 운명이 그에게 이 세상의 좋은 것들—사무국장 자리며 위원 자리며 의장 자리며 그런 것들을 마련해 두었다면, 좋은 연줄이 줄 수 있는 도움을 받아 그 오르막길을 밀고 올라가는 것이 잘하는 일이 아니겠는가?"]], [["He sat thinking over it all in his own room on that evening.", "그날 저녁 그는 제 방에 앉아 이 모든 것을 곰곰 생각했다."], ["He had written twice to Lily since his arrival at Courcy Castle.", "코시 성에 온 뒤로 그는 릴리에게 두 번 편지를 썼다."], ["His first letter has been given.", "첫 번째 편지는 앞서 옮겨 두었다."], ["His second was written much in the same tone; though Lily, as she had read it, had unconsciously felt somewhat less satisfied than she had been with the first.", "두 번째 편지도 거의 같은 투로 쓰였다. 다만 릴리는 그것을 읽으면서 자기도 모르게 첫 번째 편지만큼 흡족하지가 않았다."], ["Expressions of love were not wanting, but they were vague and without heartiness.", "사랑의 말이 모자라지는 않았으나 어렴풋했고 진심이 담기지 않았다."], ["They savoured of insincerity, though there was nothing in the words themselves to convict them.", "말 자체에서 죄를 잡아낼 만한 것은 없었어도 어딘가 진실하지 못한 냄새가 났다."], ["Few liars can lie with the full roundness and self-sufficiency of truth; and Crosbie, bad as he was, had not yet become bad enough to reach that perfection.", "진실이 지니는 그 온전한 원만함과 자족을 갖추고 거짓말을 할 수 있는 거짓말쟁이는 드물다. 크로즈비도 나쁜 사람이기는 했으나 아직 그런 완벽함에 이를 만큼 나빠지지는 않았던 것이다."], ["He had said nothing to Lily of the hopes of promotion which had been opened to him; but he had again spoken of his own worldliness--acknowledging that he received an unsatisfying satisfaction from the pomps and vanities of Courcy Castle.", "그는 저에게 열린 승진의 희망에 관해서는 릴리에게 한마디도 하지 않았다. 대신 제 세속됨을 또 한 번 이야기했으니, 코시 성의 화려함과 허영에서 흡족하지 못한 흡족을 얻고 있음을 인정한 것이다."], ["In fact he was paving the way for that which he had almost resolved that he would do, now he had told Lady Alexandrina that he loved her; and he was obliged to confess to himself that the die was cast.", "실은 이제 레이디 알렉산드리나에게 사랑한다고 말해 버린 마당에 저지르기로 거의 마음먹은 그 일을 위해 미리 길을 닦고 있었던 것이다. 그리고 그는 주사위가 던져졌음을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["As he thought of all this, there was not wanting to him some of the satisfaction of an escape.", "이 모든 것을 생각하는 동안 그에게 도망쳐 나온 자의 만족 같은 것이 아주 없지는 않았다."], ["Soon after making that declaration of love at Allington he had begun to feel that in making it he had cut his throat.", "앨링턴에서 그 사랑 고백을 하고 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 그렇게 함으로써 제 목을 그어 버렸다고 느끼기 시작했더랬다."], ["He had endeavoured to persuade himself that he could live comfortably with his throat cut in that way; and as long as Lily was with him he would believe that he could do so; but as soon as he was again alone he would again accuse himself of suicide.", "그렇게 목이 그어진 채로도 그럭저럭 편안히 살 수 있으리라고 스스로를 설득해 보려 애썼고, 릴리가 곁에 있는 동안에는 그렇게 할 수 있다고 믿었다. 그러나 다시 혼자가 되기만 하면 또다시 스스로를 자살자라고 몰아세웠다."], ["This was his frame of mind even while he was yet at Allington, and his ideas on the subject had become stronger during his sojourn at Courcy.", "이것이 아직 앨링턴에 있을 때부터의 그의 마음가짐이었고, 코시에 머무는 동안 그 생각은 더 굳어졌다."], ["But the self-immolation had not been completed, and he now began to think that he could save himself.", "그러나 제 몸을 제단에 바치는 일은 아직 다 끝나지 않았고, 이제 그는 저를 구할 수 있으리라 생각하기 시작했다."], ["I need hardly say that this was not all triumph to him.", "이것이 그에게 온통 개가만은 아니었다는 말은 굳이 할 것도 없겠다."], ["Even had there been no material difficulty as to his desertion of Lily,--no uncle, cousin, and mother whose anger he must face,--no vision of a pale face, more eloquent of wrong in its silence than even uncle, cousin, and mother, with their indignant storm of words,--he was not altogether heartless.", "릴리를 저버리는 데 아무런 실질적인 어려움이 없다 하더라도—맞서야 할 노여운 삼촌과 사촌과 어머니가 없다 하더라도, 삼촌과 사촌과 어머니가 성난 말의 소나기를 퍼붓는 것보다도 그 침묵으로 잘못을 더 웅변하는 창백한 얼굴이 눈앞에 떠오르지 않는다 하더라도—그는 아주 매정한 사람은 아니었다."], ["How should he tell all this to the girl who had loved him so well; who had so loved him, that, as he himself felt, her love would fashion all her future life either for weal or for woe?", "저를 그토록 잘 사랑해 준 처녀에게, 저 자신도 느끼다시피 그 사랑이 앞으로의 삶 전체를 좋은 쪽으로든 나쁜 쪽으로든 빚어 놓을 만큼 그렇게 저를 사랑해 준 처녀에게 이 모든 것을 어떻게 말한단 말인가?"], ["\"I am unworthy of her, and will tell her so,\" he said to himself.", "“나는 그녀에게 어울리지 않는 사람이고, 그렇다고 말해 주겠다.”"], ["How many a false hound of a man has endeavoured to salve his own conscience by such mock humility?", "그는 스스로에게 그렇게 말했다. 사내라는 이름의 거짓된 사냥개들이 그런 짐짓 꾸민 겸손으로 제 양심을 달래려 든 적이 얼마나 많았던가?"], ["But he acknowledged at this moment, as he rose from his seat to dress himself, that the die was cast, and that it was open to him now to say what he pleased to Lady Alexandrina.", "그러나 옷을 갈아입으려고 자리에서 일어서면서 그는 이제 주사위가 던져졌음을, 그리고 이제 레이디 알렉산드리나에게 제 마음대로 무슨 말이든 할 수 있게 되었음을 인정했다."], ["\"Others have gone through the same fire before,\"", "“전에도 같은 불길을 지나온 사람들이 있지.”"], ["he said to himself, as he walked downstairs, \"and have come out scatheless.\"", "계단을 내려가면서 그는 스스로에게 말했다. “그러고도 멀쩡히 나왔고 말이야.”"], ["And then he recalled to himself the names of various men of high repute in the world who were supposed to have committed in their younger days some such little mistake as that into which he had been betrayed.", "그러고서 그는 젊은 시절에 저와 같은 자잘한 잘못을 저질렀으리라 여겨지는, 세상에서 이름 높은 여러 사람의 이름을 떠올려 보았다."]], [["In passing through the hall he overtook Lady Julia De Guest, and was in time to open for her the door of the drawing-room.", "현관홀을 지나다가 그는 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트를 따라잡았고, 마침 때맞추어 응접실 문을 열어 주었다."], ["He then remembered that she had come into the billiard-room at one side, and had gone out at the other, while he was standing with Alexandrina at the window.", "그제야 그는 저와 알렉산드리나가 창가에 서 있는 동안 그녀가 당구실 한쪽 문으로 들어왔다가 다른 쪽 문으로 나갔다는 것이 떠올랐다."], ["He had not, however, then thought much of Lady Julia; and as he now stood for her to pass by him through the door-way, he made to her some indifferent remark.", "그러나 그때는 레이디 줄리아를 그리 대수롭게 여기지 않았다. 그리고 이제 문간에 서서 그녀가 제 옆을 지나가기를 기다리며 그는 무어라 심상한 말을 건넸다."]], [["But Lady Julia was on some subjects a stern woman, and not without a certain amount of courage.", "그러나 레이디 줄리아는 어떤 문제에서는 엄정한 여자였고 얼마간의 용기도 갖추고 있었다."], ["In the last week she had seen what had been going on, and had become more and more angry.", "지난 한 주 동안 그녀는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지를 보았고 점점 더 화가 났다."], ["Though she had disowned any family connection with Lily Dale, nevertheless she now felt for her sympathy and almost affection.", "릴리 데일과는 아무 친척 관계도 없다고 잘라 말한 적이 있으면서도, 이제 그녀는 그 처녀에게 동정을, 아니 거의 애정을 느끼고 있었다."], ["Nearly every day she had repeated stiffly to the countess some incident of Crosbie's courtship and engagement to Miss Dale,--speaking of it as with absolute knowledge, as a thing settled at all points.", "거의 날마다 그녀는 크로즈비가 데일 양에게 구애하고 약혼한 일의 이런저런 대목을 백작부인에게 딱딱하게 되풀이해 말했다. 마치 낱낱이 다 알고 있다는 듯이, 무엇 하나 빠짐없이 다 정해진 일이라는 듯이 말이다."], ["This she had done to the countess alone, in the presence of the countess and Alexandrina, and also before all the female guests of the castle.", "백작부인과 단둘이 있을 때도 그렇게 했고, 백작부인과 알렉산드리나가 함께 있는 자리에서도, 성에 묵는 부인 손님들이 다 모인 앞에서도 그렇게 했다."], ["But what she had said was received simply with an incredulous smile.", "그러나 그녀가 한 말은 그저 못 믿겠다는 웃음으로 받아넘겨졌다."], ["\"Dear me!", "“어머나!"], ["Lady Julia,\"", "레이디 줄리아.”"], ["the countess had replied at last, \"I shall begin to think you are in love with Mr.", "마침내 백작부인이 대꾸했다. “나는 그대가 크로즈비 씨를 사랑하고 있다고 여기게 될 것 같소."], ["Crosbie yourself; you harp so constantly on this affair of his.", "그이의 그 일을 어찌 그리 노상 되뇌시오."], ["One would think that young ladies in your part of the world must find it very difficult to get husbands, seeing that the success of one young lady is trumpeted so loudly.\"", "처녀 하나가 거둔 성공을 그토록 요란하게 나발 불어 대는 걸 보면, 그대가 사는 고장에서는 아가씨들이 남편감 구하기가 여간 어렵지 않은가 보다고들 여기겠구려.”"], ["For the moment, Lady Julia was silenced; but it was not easy to silence her altogether when she had a subject for speech near her heart.", "그 순간에는 레이디 줄리아도 입을 다물었다. 그러나 마음에 가까운 이야깃거리가 있을 때 그녀를 아주 잠잠하게 만들기란 쉬운 일이 아니었다."]], [["Almost all the Courcy world were assembled in the drawing-room as she now walked into the room with Crosbie at her heels.", "크로즈비를 뒤에 달고 그녀가 응접실로 걸어 들어갔을 때 코시의 온 세계가 거의 다 그 방에 모여 있었다."], ["When she found herself near the crowd she turned round, and addressed him in a voice more audible than that generally required for purposes of drawing-room conversation.", "사람들 무리 가까이에 이르자 그녀는 몸을 돌려, 응접실에서 이야기를 나눌 때 흔히 필요한 것보다 훨씬 잘 들리는 목소리로 그에게 말을 건넸다. “크로즈비 씨.”"], ["\"Mr. Crosbie,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"have you heard lately from our dear friend, Lily Dale?\"", "“우리의 소중한 벗 릴리 데일에게서 요즘 소식을 들으셨나요?”"], ["And she looked him full in the face, in a manner more significant, probably, than even she had intended it to be.", "그러고서 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았는데, 그 태도는 아마 그녀 자신이 뜻했던 것보다도 더 의미심장했다."], ["There was, at once, a general hush in the room, and all eyes were turned upon her and upon him.", "방 안은 대번에 물을 끼얹은 듯 조용해졌고 모든 눈이 그녀와 그에게 쏠렸다."]], [["Crosbie instantly made an effort to bear the attack gallantly, but he felt that he could not quite command his colour, or prevent a sudden drop of perspiration from showing itself upon his brow.", "크로즈비는 그 공격을 의젓하게 받아넘기려고 곧바로 애를 썼으나, 낯빛까지 제 뜻대로 다스릴 수는 없고 이마에 갑자기 돋는 땀방울이 드러나는 것도 막을 수 없다고 느꼈다."], ["\"I had a letter from Allington yesterday,\"", "“어제 앨링턴에서 편지를 받았습니다.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I suppose you have heard of your brother's encounter with the bull?\"", "“댁의 오라버님께서 황소와 맞닥뜨리신 일은 들으셨겠지요?”"]], [["\"The bull!\"", "“황소라니요!”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."], ["And it was instantly manifest to all that her attack had been foiled and her flank turned.", "그리고 그녀의 공격이 막히고 옆구리가 뚫렸다는 것이 대번에 모두에게 뚜렷해졌다."]], [["\"Good gracious!", "“세상에!"], ["Lady Julia, how very odd you are!\"", "레이디 줄리아, 어쩌면 그리 별나시오!”"], ["said the countess.", "백작부인이 말했다."]], [["\"But what about the bull?\"", "“한데 그 황소 얘기는 뭔가?”"], ["asked the Honourable George.", "아너러블 조지가 물었다."]], [["\"It seems that the earl was knocked down in the middle of one of his own fields.\"", "“백작께서 당신 밭 한복판에서 나가떨어지셨다는 모양입니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, dear!\"", "“어머나!”"], ["exclaimed Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 외쳤다."], ["And sundry other exclamations were made by all the assembled ladies.", "그리고 모여 있던 부인들이 저마다 이런저런 감탄을 터뜨렸다."]], [["\"But he wasn't hurt,\" said Crosbie.", "“하지만 다치시지는 않았습니다.”"], ["\"A young man named Eames seems to have fallen from the sky and carried off the earl on his back.\"", "크로즈비가 말했다. “임스라는 젊은이가 하늘에서 뚝 떨어져 백작을 등에 업고 데려간 모양이더군요.”"]], [["\"Ha, ha, ha, ha!\"", "“하, 하, 하, 하!”"], ["growled the other earl, as he heard of the discomfiture of his brother peer.", "다른 백작이 제 동료 귀족의 낭패를 전해 듣고 으르렁대듯 웃었다."]], [["Lady Julia, who had received her own letters that day from Guestwick, knew that nothing of importance had happened to her brother; but she felt that she was foiled for that time.", "그날 게스트윅에서 제 앞으로 온 편지를 받아 본 레이디 줄리아는 오라비에게 대단한 일이라곤 아무것도 일어나지 않았음을 알고 있었다. 그러나 이번만은 저의 공격이 막혔음을 느꼈다."]], [["\"I hope that there has not really been any accident,\"", "“정말로 무슨 사고가 있었던 것은 아니기를 바랍니다.”"], ["said Mr. Gazebee, with a voice of great solicitude.", "게이지비 씨가 몹시 걱정스러운 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"My brother was quite well last night, thank you,\" said she.", "“제 오라비는 어젯밤까지 아주 잘 있었습니다, 고맙습니다.”"], ["And then the little groups again formed themselves, and Lady Julia was left alone on the corner of a sofa.", "그녀가 말했다. 그러고 나서 작은 무리들이 다시 만들어졌고, 레이디 줄리아는 소파 한쪽 구석에 혼자 남겨졌다."]], [["\"Was that all an invention of yours, sir?\"", "“그건 다 지어내신 거예요?”"], ["said Alexandrina to Crosbie.", "알렉산드리나가 크로즈비에게 말했다."]], [["\"Not quite.", "“꼭 그런 건 아닙니다."], ["I did get a letter this morning from my friend Bernard Dale,--that old harridan's nephew; and Lord De Guest has been worried by some of his animals.", "오늘 아침에 제 벗 버나드 데일에게서—저 늙은 마귀할멈의 조카지요—편지를 받은 건 사실이고, 드 게스트 경이 기르는 짐승 가운데 하나 때문에 시달린 것도 사실입니다."], ["I wish I had told her that his stupid old neck had been broken.\"", "그 미련한 늙은 목이 부러졌다고 말해 줄걸 그랬군요.”"]], [["\"Fie, Mr. Crosbie!\"", "“저런, 크로즈비 씨!”"]], [["\"What business has she to interfere with me?\"", "“저 여자가 무슨 상관이라고 내 일에 끼어든답니까?”"]], [["\"But I mean to ask the same question that she asked, and you won't put me off with a cock-and-bull story like that.\"", "“하지만 저는 저분이 한 그 질문을 똑같이 물을 작정이에요. 그런 소 나오는 허튼소리로 저를 따돌리진 못하실 거예요.”"], ["But then, as she was going to ask the question, dinner was announced.", "그러나 그녀가 그 질문을 하려는 참에 만찬이 알려졌다."]], [["\"And is it true that De Guest has been tossed by a bull?\"", "“그래, 드 게스트가 황소에게 받혔다는 게 정말이오?”"], ["said the earl, as soon as the ladies were gone.", "부인들이 물러가자마자 백작이 말했다."], ["He had spoken nothing during dinner except what words he had muttered into the ear of Lady Dumbello.", "그는 만찬 내내 레이디 덤벨로의 귀에 대고 우물거린 몇 마디 말고는 아무 말도 하지 않은 터였다."], ["It was seldom that conversation had many charms for him in his own house; but there was a savour of pleasantry in the idea of Lord De Guest having been tossed, by which even he was tickled.", "제 집에서 이야기라는 것이 그에게 큰 재미를 주는 일은 드물었다. 그러나 드 게스트 경이 뿔에 받혀 나가떨어졌다는 생각에는 익살스러운 맛이 있어서 그마저도 간지러웠던 것이다."]], [["\"Only knocked down, I believe,\"", "“그저 나가떨어지기만 하신 모양입니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Ha, ha, ha!\"", "“하, 하, 하!”"], ["growled the earl; then he filled his glass, and allowed some one else to pass the bottle.", "백작이 으르렁대듯 웃었다. 그러고서 제 잔을 채우고 술병을 다른 사람에게 넘겼다."], ["Poor man!", "가엾은 사람!"], ["There was not much left to him now in the world which did amuse him.", "이제 이 세상에는 그를 즐겁게 해 주는 것이 별로 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["\"I don't see anything to laugh at,\"", "“나는 웃을 일이 도무지 보이지 않소.”"], ["said Plantagenet Palliser, who was sitting at the earl's right hand, opposite to Lord Dumbello.", "백작의 오른편, 덤벨로 경의 맞은편에 앉아 있던 플랜태저넷 팰리서가 말했다."]], [["\"Don't you?\"", "“안 보이시오?”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"Ha, ha, ha!\"", "“하, 하, 하!”"]], [["\"I'll be shot if I do.", "“정말이지 하나도 모르겠소."], ["From all I hear De Guest is an uncommon good farmer.", "내가 듣기로 드 게스트는 보기 드물게 훌륭한 농사꾼이오."], ["And I don't see the joke of tossing a farmer merely because he's a nobleman also. Do you?\"", "농사짓는 사람이 귀족이기도 하다는 이유만으로 뿔에 받혔다는 것이 어디가 우스운지 나는 모르겠소."], ["and he turned round to Mr.", "그쪽은 아시겠소?”"], ["Gazebee, who was sitting on the other side. The earl was an earl, and was also Mr.", "그러면서 그는 반대쪽에 앉아 있던 게이지비 씨에게로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Gazebee's father-in-law. Mr.", "백작은 백작이었고, 게이지비 씨의 장인이기도 했다."], ["Plantagenet Palliser was the heir to a dukedom. Therefore, Mr.", "플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 공작 자리의 상속인이었다."], ["Gazebee merely simpered, and did not answer the question put to him. Mr.", "그러니 게이지비 씨는 그저 배시시 웃기만 할 뿐 저에게 던져진 질문에 답하지 않았다."], ["Palliser said nothing more about it, nor did the earl; and then the joke died away.", "팰리서 씨도 그 일에 관해 더는 말하지 않았고 백작도 그러했다. 그리하여 그 농담은 사그라들었다."]], [["Mr. Plantagenet Palliser was the Duke of Omnium's heir--heir to that nobleman's title and to his enormous wealth; and, therefore, was a man of mark in the world.", "플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 옴니엄 공작의 상속인이었다."], ["He sat in the House of Commons, of course.", "그 귀인의 작위와 그 막대한 재산을 물려받을 사람이었으니, 따라서 세상에서 이름난 인물이었다."], ["He was about five-and-twenty years of age, and was, as yet, unmarried.", "물론 하원 의원이었다. 나이는 스물다섯쯤이었고 아직 혼인하지 않았다."], ["He did not hunt or shoot or keep a yacht, and had been heard to say that he had never put a foot upon a race-course in his life.", "사냥개를 몰지도 총을 쏘지도 요트를 두지도 않았고, 평생 경마장에 발 한 번 들여놓은 적이 없다고 말하는 것을 들은 사람도 있었다. 옷차림은 몹시 수수해서 옷의 빛깔도 모양도 결코 바꾸는 법이 없었다."], ["He dressed very quietly, never changing the colour or form of his garments; and in society was quiet, reserved, and very often silent.", "사교 자리에서는 조용하고 삼가는 편이었으며 아주 자주 말이 없었다. 키가 크고 호리호리했으며 인물도 나쁘지 않았다."], ["He was tall, slight, and not ill-looking; but more than this cannot be said for his personal appearance--except, indeed, this, that no one could mistake him for other than a gentleman.", "그러나 그의 겉모습에 대해서는 그 이상 말할 것이 없다—다만 이것만은 덧붙여야겠다. 누구도 그를 신사가 아닌 다른 무엇으로 잘못 볼 수는 없었다는 것이다."], ["With his uncle, the duke, he was on good terms--that is to say, they had never quarrelled.", "숙부인 공작과는 사이가 좋았다. 다시 말해 두 사람은 한 번도 다툰 적이 없었다."], ["A very liberal allowance had been made to the nephew; but the two relatives had no tastes in common, and did not often meet.", "조카에게는 아주 후한 생활비가 주어져 있었다. 그러나 이 두 친척은 취향이 도무지 겹치는 데가 없어 자주 만나지 않았다."], ["Once a year Mr. Palliser visited the duke at his great country seat for two or three days, and usually dined with him two or three times during the season in London.", "팰리서 씨는 해마다 한 번 공작의 큰 시골 저택을 이삼 일 찾았고, 런던 사교철에는 대개 두세 번 공작과 저녁을 들었다."], ["Mr. Palliser sat for a borough which was absolutely under the duke's command; but had accepted his seat under the distinct understanding that he was to take whatever part in politics might seem good to himself.", "팰리서 씨는 전적으로 공작의 손아귀에 있는 자치구를 대표해 의석을 얻었으나, 정치에서는 제 눈에 옳아 보이는 편을 마음대로 들겠다는 분명한 양해 아래 그 자리를 받아들였다."], ["Under these well-understood arrangements, the duke and his heir showed to the world quite a pattern of a happy family.", "이렇게 서로 잘 알고 있는 약속 덕분에 공작과 그 상속인은 더할 나위 없이 화목한 집안의 본보기를 세상에 보여 주었다."], ["\"So different to the earl and Lord Porlock!\"", "“백작과 폴록 경하고는 어쩌면 그리 다른지!”"], ["the people of West Barsetshire used to say.", "서부 바셋주 사람들은 그렇게들 말하곤 했다."], ["For the estates, both of the duke and of the earl, were situated in the western division of that county.", "공작의 영지도 백작의 영지도 다 그 주의 서부에 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Mr. Palliser was chiefly known to the world as a rising politician.", "팰리서 씨는 세상에 주로 떠오르는 정치인으로 알려져 있었다."], ["We may say that he had everything at his command, in the way of pleasure, that the world could offer him.", "즐길 거리라면 이 세상이 사람에게 줄 수 있는 것을 모조리 손안에 쥐고 있었다고 말해도 좋을 것이다."], ["He had wealth, position, power, and the certainty of attaining the highest rank among, perhaps, the most brilliant nobility of the world.", "재산이 있었고, 지위가 있었고, 권세가 있었으며, 아마도 이 세상에서 가장 빛나는 귀족 사회 안에서 가장 높은 신분에 오르리라는 것이 확실했다."], ["He was courted by all who could get near enough to court him.", "가까이 다가가 비위를 맞출 수 있는 사람이라면 누구나 그의 비위를 맞추었다."], ["It is hardly too much to say that he might have selected a bride from all that was most beautiful and best among English women.", "영국 여인 가운데 가장 아름답고 가장 훌륭한 사람들 중에서 신부를 골라잡을 수 있었으리라고 해도 지나친 말은 아닐 것이다."], ["If he would have bought race-horses, and have expended thousands on the turf, he would have gratified his uncle by doing so.", "경주마를 사들이고 경마에 수천 파운드를 쏟아부었다면 숙부를 흡족하게 해 주었을 것이다."], ["He might have been the master of hounds, or the slaughterer of hecatombs of birds.", "사냥개 무리의 주인이 될 수도 있었고, 새를 백 마리씩 잡아 죽이는 사람이 될 수도 있었다."], ["But to none of these things would he devote himself.", "그러나 그런 것에는 하나도 몸을 바치지 않았다."], ["He had chosen to be a politician, and in that pursuit he laboured with a zeal and perseverance which would have made his fortune at any profession or in any trade.", "그는 정치인이 되기를 택했고, 그 길에서 어떤 직업에서든 어떤 장사에서든 한밑천을 잡았을 만한 열성과 끈기로 애를 썼다."], ["He was constant in committee-rooms up to the very middle of August.", "팔월 한복판이 될 때까지도 그는 위원회실을 지켰다."], ["He was rarely absent from any debate of importance, and never from any important division.", "중요한 토론에는 좀처럼 빠지지 않았고 중요한 표결에는 한 번도 빠지지 않았다."], ["Though he seldom spoke, he was always ready to speak if his purpose required it.", "말은 드물게 했으나, 필요하다면 언제든 말할 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["No man gave him credit for any great genius--few even considered that he could become either an orator or a mighty statesman.", "그에게 대단한 천재성이 있다고 인정해 주는 사람은 없었다. 그가 웅변가나 위대한 정치가가 되리라고 여기는 사람도 거의 없었다."], ["But the world said that he was a rising man, and old Nestor of the Cabinet looked on him as one who would be able, at some far future day, to come among them as a younger brother.", "그러나 세상은 그를 떠오르는 인물이라고 말했고, 내각의 늙은 네스토르들은 그를 언젠가 먼 훗날 아우뻘로 저희 틈에 끼어들 수 있는 사람으로 여겼다."], ["Hitherto he had declined such inferior offices as had been offered to him, biding his time carefully; and he was as yet tied hand and neck to no party, though known to be liberal in all his political tendencies.", "여태까지 그는 저에게 주어진 낮은 자리들은 사양하며 때를 조심스레 기다렸다. 그리고 정치적 성향은 온통 자유주의 쪽이라고 알려져 있었으나 아직 어느 당파에도 손발이 묶여 있지 않았다."], ["He was a great reader--not taking up a book here, and another there, as chance brought books before him, but working through an enormous course of books, getting up the great subject of the world's history--filling himself full of facts--though perhaps not destined to acquire the power of using those facts otherwise than as precedents.", "그는 책을 많이 읽는 사람이었다—우연히 눈앞에 오는 대로 이 책 한 권 저 책 한 권 집어 드는 식이 아니라, 어마어마한 분량의 책을 차례차례 헤쳐 나가며 세계사라는 큰 주제를 익히고 제 안을 사실로 가득 채우는 식이었다—다만 그 사실들을 선례로 쓰는 것 말고 달리 부릴 힘까지 얻을 운명은 아니었는지도 모른다."], ["He strove also diligently to become a linguist--not without success, as far as a competent understanding of various languages.", "또한 여러 나라 말에 능해지려고 부지런히 애썼고, 여러 언어를 그럭저럭 알아듣는 정도까지는 성과를 거두었다."], ["He was a thin-minded, plodding, respectable man, willing to devote all his youth to work, in order that in old age he might be allowed to sit among the Councillors of the State.", "그는 생각의 폭이 좁고 우직하게 일하는 점잖은 사람이었으니, 늙어서 나라의 중신들 틈에 앉는 것이 허락되기만 한다면 제 젊음을 통째로 일에 바칠 각오가 되어 있는 사람이었다."]], [["Hitherto his name had not been coupled by the world with that of any woman whom he had been supposed to admire; but latterly it had been observed that he had often been seen in the same room with Lady Dumbello.", "여태까지 그의 이름이 그가 사모한다고 여겨지는 어떤 여자의 이름과 나란히 오르내린 적은 없었다. 그런데 근래 들어 그가 레이디 덤벨로와 같은 방에 있는 모습이 자주 눈에 띈다는 말이 돌기 시작했다."], ["It had hardly amounted to more than this; but when it was remembered how undemonstrative were the two persons concerned--how little disposed was either of them to any strong display of feeling--even this was thought matter to be mentioned.", "그 이상은 거의 아무것도 아니었다. 그러나 이 두 사람이 얼마나 내색을 하지 않는 사람들인지—어느 쪽이든 감정을 강하게 드러내는 것을 얼마나 싫어하는지를 떠올려 보면, 그 정도만 해도 입에 올릴 만한 일이라고 여겨졌던 것이다."], ["He certainly would speak to her from time to time almost with an air of interest; and Lady Dumbello, when she saw that he was in the room, would be observed to raise her head with some little show of life, and to look round as though there were something there on which it might be worth her while to allow her eyes to rest.", "그는 이따금 거의 관심 있는 기색을 띠고 그녀에게 말을 걸곤 했다. 그리고 레이디 덤벨로는 그가 방 안에 있는 것을 보면 얼마간 생기 있는 낌새로 고개를 들고, 마치 눈길을 얹어 둘 만한 무엇이 저기 있기라도 한 듯이 둘러보는 모습이 눈에 띄곤 했다."], ["When such innuendoes were abroad, no one would probably make more of them than Lady De Courcy.", "그런 뒷말이 떠돌 때 그것을 드 코시 백작부인보다 더 크게 부풀릴 사람은 아마 없었을 것이다."], ["Many, when they heard that Mr. Palliser was to be at the castle, had expressed their surprise at her success in that quarter.", "팰리서 씨가 성에 온다는 말을 듣고 그쪽 방면에서 거둔 그녀의 성공에 놀라움을 나타낸 사람이 많았다."], ["Others, when they learned that Lady Dumbello had consented to become her guest, had also wondered greatly.", "레이디 덤벨로가 그녀의 손님이 되기로 했다는 것을 알고 크게 놀란 사람들도 있었다."], ["But when it was ascertained that the two were to be there together, her good-natured friends had acknowledged that she was a very clever woman. To have either Mr.", "그러나 그 둘이 함께 그곳에 온다는 것이 밝혀지자 마음씨 좋은 벗들은 그녀가 참으로 영리한 여자라고 인정했다."], ["Palliser or Lady Dumbello would have been a feather in her cap; but to succeed in getting both, by enabling each to know that the other would be there, was indeed a triumph.", "팰리서 씨든 레이디 덤벨로든 하나만 모셔 와도 모자에 깃털 하나를 꽂는 셈이었을 것이다. 그러나 서로 상대가 온다는 것을 알려 주는 방식으로 둘 다 잡아채는 데 성공한 것은 참으로 개가였다."], ["As regards Lady Dumbello, however, the bargain was not fairly carried out; for, after all, Mr.", "다만 레이디 덤벨로 쪽에서 보자면 그 거래는 온전히 이행되지 않은 셈이었다."], ["Palliser came to Courcy Castle only for two nights and a day, and during the whole of that day he was closeted with sundry large blue-books.", "결국 팰리서 씨는 이틀 밤과 하루만 묵으러 코시 성에 왔고, 그 하루 동안에는 온종일 이런저런 큼직한 청서에 파묻혀 방에 틀어박혀 있었으니 말이다."], ["As for Lady De Courcy, she did not care how he might be employed.", "드 코시 백작부인으로 말하자면 그가 무엇에 매달려 있든 상관하지 않았다."], ["Blue-books and Lady Dumbello were all the same to her.", "그녀에게는 청서든 레이디 덤벨로든 매한가지였다."], ["Mr. Palliser had been at Courcy Castle, and neither enemy nor friend could deny the fact.", "팰리서 씨가 코시 성에 와 있었다는 것, 이는 적도 벗도 부인할 수 없는 사실이었으니까."]], [["This was his second evening; and as he had promised to meet his constituents at Silverbridge at one P.M.", "이날이 그가 묵는 두 번째 저녁이었다."], ["on the following day, with the view of explaining to them his own conduct and the political position of the world in general; and as he was not to return from Silverbridge to Courcy, Lady Dumbello, if she made any way at all, must take advantage of the short gleam of sunshine which the present hour afforded her.", "그리고 그는 이튿날 낮 한 시에 실버브리지에서 저를 뽑아 준 사람들을 만나 제 처신과 세상 전반의 정치 형편을 설명해 주기로 약속해 둔 터였고, 실버브리지에서 코시로 돌아오지도 않을 것이었으므로, 레이디 덤벨로가 조금이라도 앞으로 나아갈 작정이라면 지금 이 시간이 그녀에게 내어 주는 그 짧은 볕뉘를 붙잡는 수밖에 없었다."], ["No one, however, could say that she showed any active disposition to monopolize Mr. Palliser's attention.", "그러나 팰리서 씨의 관심을 독차지하려는 적극적인 기색을 그녀가 내비쳤다고 말할 사람은 아무도 없었을 것이다."], ["When he sauntered into the drawing-room she was sitting, alone, in a large, low chair, made without arms, so as to admit the full expansion of her dress, but hollowed and round at the back, so as to afford her the support that was necessary to her.", "그가 어슬렁어슬렁 응접실로 들어섰을 때 그녀는 혼자 앉아 있었다. 옷자락을 활짝 펼칠 수 있도록 팔걸이 없이 만든 낮고 널찍한 의자였는데, 등받이 쪽은 옴폭하고 둥글게 되어 있어 그녀에게 꼭 필요한 받침이 되어 주었다."], ["She had barely spoken three words since she had left the dining-room, but the time had not passed heavily with her.", "식당을 나온 뒤로 그녀가 입 밖에 낸 말은 세 마디도 되지 않았으나, 그렇다고 시간이 지루하게 흐른 것은 아니었다."], ["Lady Julia had again attacked the countess about Lily Dale and Mr. Crosbie, and Alexandrina, driven almost to rage, had stalked off to the farther end of the room, not concealing her special concern in the matter.", "레이디 줄리아가 릴리 데일과 크로즈비 씨를 두고 백작부인에게 또 한 번 덤벼들었고, 알렉산드리나는 화가 치밀다 못해 이 일에 저만이 각별히 마음을 쓰고 있다는 것을 감추지도 않은 채 방 저쪽 끝으로 성큼성큼 가 버린 참이었다."]], [["\"How I do wish they were married and done with,\"", "“그 사람들이 어서 혼인해서 다 끝나 버렸으면 좋겠소.”"], ["said the countess; \"and then we should hear no more about them.\"", "백작부인이 말했다. “그러면 그 얘기를 더는 안 들어도 될 것 아니오.”"]], [["All of which Lady Dumbello heard and understood; and in all of it she took a certain interest.", "이 모든 것을 레이디 덤벨로는 다 듣고 다 알아들었으며, 그 모두에 얼마간 관심을 두었다."], ["She remembered such things, learning thereby who was who, and regulating her own conduct by what she learned.", "그녀는 그런 것들을 기억해 두었다가 누가 어떤 사람인지를 익혔고, 그렇게 익힌 것에 맞추어 제 처신을 다스렸다."], ["She was by no means idle at this or at other such times, going through, we may say, a considerable amount of really hard work in her manner of working.", "이런 때나 그 밖의 비슷한 자리에서 그녀는 결코 놀고 있는 것이 아니었으니, 제 나름의 일하는 방식으로 꽤나 고된 일을 적잖이 해내고 있었다고 말해도 좋을 것이다."], ["There she had sat speechless, unless when acknowledging by a low word of assent some expression of flattery from those around her.", "그녀는 그 자리에 말없이 앉아 있었고, 다만 곁에 있는 사람들이 늘어놓는 아첨에 나직한 한마디로 맞장구를 쳐 줄 때만 입을 열었다."], ["Then the door opened, and when Mr.", "그러다 문이 열렸다."], ["Palliser entered she raised her head, and the faintest possible gleam of satisfaction might have been discerned upon her features.", "팰리서 씨가 들어서자 그녀는 고개를 들었고, 그 얼굴에서 더할 나위 없이 희미한 만족의 빛을 알아볼 수 있었을 것이다."], ["But she made no attempt to speak to him; and when, as he stood at the table, he took up a book and remained thus standing for a quarter of an hour, she neither showed nor felt any impatience.", "그러나 그녀는 그에게 말을 걸려는 시늉조차 하지 않았다. 그가 탁자 곁에 서서 책 한 권을 집어 들고는 그대로 십오 분이나 서 있었을 때도, 그녀는 조바심을 내보이지도 않았고 느끼지도 않았다."], ["After that Lord Dumbello came in, and he stood at the table without a book.", "그 뒤에 덤벨로 경이 들어와 책도 없이 탁자 곁에 섰다."], ["Even then Lady Dumbello felt no impatience.", "그때도 레이디 덤벨로는 조바심을 내지 않았다."]], [["Plantagenet Palliser skimmed through his little book, and probably learned something.", "플랜태저넷 팰리서는 그 작은 책을 대충 훑어보았고, 아마 무언가를 배웠을 것이다."], ["When he put it down he sipped a cup of tea, and remarked to Lady De Courcy that he believed it was only twelve miles to Silverbridge.", "책을 내려놓고 그는 차를 한 모금 홀짝이더니 드 코시 백작부인에게 실버브리지까지가 십이 마일밖에 안 되는 줄로 안다고 말했다."]], [["\"I wish it was a hundred and twelve,\"", "“백열두 마일이면 좋겠소.”"], ["said the countess.", "백작부인이 말했다."]], [["\"In that case I should be forced to start to-night,\"", "“그렇다면 저는 오늘 밤에 떠나야 할 것입니다.”"], ["said Mr. Palliser.", "팰리서 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Then I wish it was a thousand and twelve,\"", "“그럼 천십이 마일이면 좋겠구려.”"], ["said Lady De Courcy.", "드 코시 백작부인이 말했다."]], [["\"In that case I should not have come at all,\"", "“그렇다면 저는 아예 오지도 않았을 것입니다.”"], ["said Mr. Palliser.", "팰리서 씨가 말했다."], ["He did not mean to be uncivil, and had only stated a fact.", "무례하게 굴 뜻은 없었고, 그저 사실을 말했을 뿐이었다."]], [["\"The young men are becoming absolute bears,\"", "“요즘 젊은 남자들은 아주 곰이 다 되었구나.”"], ["said the countess to her daughter Margaretta.", "백작부인이 딸 마거리타에게 말했다."]], [["He had been in the room nearly an hour when he did at last find himself standing close to Lady Dumbello: close to her, and without any other very near neighbour.", "방에 들어온 지 거의 한 시간이 지나서야 그는 마침내 레이디 덤벨로 가까이에 서 있는 저를 발견했다. 그녀 곁에, 그것도 아주 가까이에 다른 사람이라고는 아무도 없이."]], [["\"I should hardly have expected to find you here,\"", "“여기서 뵈리라고는 좀처럼 생각하지 못했소.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Nor I you,\"", "“저도 그랬어요.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["\"Though, for the matter of that, we are both near our own homes.\"", "“하기야 그 점을 따지자면 우리 둘 다 제집 가까이에 와 있는 셈이오만.”"]], [["\"I am not near mine.\"", "“저는 제집 가까이가 아니에요.”"]], [["\"I meant Plumstead; your father's place.\"", "“플럼스테드를 말한 것이오. 그대 아버님 댁 말이오.”"]], [["\"Yes; that was my home once.\"", "“네, 한때는 그곳이 제집이었지요.”"]], [["\"I wish I could show you my uncle's place.", "“숙부님 댁을 보여 드릴 수 있으면 좋겠소."], ["The castle is very fine, and he has some good pictures.\"", "그 성은 아주 훌륭하고, 좋은 그림도 몇 점 있소.”"]], [["\"So I have heard.\"", "“그렇다고 들었어요.”"]], [["\"Do you stay here long?\"", "“여기 오래 머무시오?”"]], [["\"Oh, no.", "“아니에요."], ["I go to Cheshire the day after to-morrow.", "모레 체셔로 갑니다."], ["Lord Dumbello is always there when the hunting begins.\"", "덤벨로 경은 사냥철이 시작되면 늘 그곳에 있거든요.”"]], [["\"Ah, yes; of course.", "“아, 그렇지요. 물론 그렇겠소."], ["What a happy fellow he is; never any work to do!", "참 팔자 좋은 사람이오. 할 일이라고는 도무지 없으니!"], ["His constituents never trouble him, I suppose?\"", "그를 뽑아 준 사람들이 그를 성가시게 하는 일은 없겠지요?”"]], [["\"I don't think they ever do, much.\"", "“그런 일은 별로 없는 것 같아요.”"]], [["After that Mr. Palliser sauntered away again, and Lady Dumbello passed the rest of the evening in silence.", "그러고서 팰리서 씨는 다시 어슬렁어슬렁 자리를 떴고, 레이디 덤벨로는 그날 저녁의 남은 시간을 잠자코 보냈다."], ["It is to be hoped that they both were rewarded by that ten minutes of sympathetic intercourse for the inconvenience which they had suffered in coming to Courcy Castle.", "두 사람 다 코시 성까지 오느라 겪은 불편을 그 십 분 동안의 마음 맞는 교제로 보상받았기를 바랄 따름이다."]], [["But that which seems so innocent to us had been looked on in a different light by the stern moralists of that house.", "그러나 우리 눈에 그토록 죄 없어 보이는 그 일을 그 집안의 준엄한 도덕가들은 다른 빛 아래에서 바라보았다."]], [["\"By Jove!\"", "“이거 참!”"], ["said the Honourable George to his cousin, Mr.", "아너러블 조지가 사촌인 그레셤 씨에게 말했다."], ["Gresham, \"I wonder how Dumbello likes it.\"", "“덤벨로가 저걸 어떻게 여길지 궁금하군.”"]], [["\"It seems to me that Dumbello takes it very easily.\"", "“내가 보기엔 덤벨로가 아주 태평하게 받아넘기는 것 같은데.”"]], [["\"There are some men who will take anything easily,\"", "“무슨 일이든 태평하게 받아넘기는 사내들이 있지.”"], ["said George, who, since his own marriage, had learned to have a holy horror of such wicked things.", "조지가 말했다. 제가 혼인한 뒤로 그런 못된 일이라면 거룩한 진저리를 치는 법을 배운 사람이었다."]], [["\"She's beginning to come out a little,\"", "“저 여자도 이제 슬슬 나오기 시작하는구려.”"], ["said Lady Clandidlem to Lady De Courcy, when the two old women found themselves together over a fire in some back sitting-room.", "뒤쪽 어느 거실 난롯가에 두 늙은 여자가 마주 앉게 되었을 때, 레이디 클랜디들럼이 드 코시 백작부인에게 말했다."], ["\"Still waters always run deep, you know.\"", "“잔잔한 물이 늘 깊은 법 아니오.”"]], [["\"I shouldn't at all wonder if she were to go off with him,\"", "“저 여자가 저이와 달아난다 해도 나는 조금도 놀라지 않겠소.”"], ["said Lady De Courcy.", "드 코시 백작부인이 말했다."]], [["\"He'll never be such a fool as that,\"", "“저이가 그런 바보짓을 할 리야 있겠소.”"], ["said Lady Clandidlem.", "레이디 클랜디들럼이 말했다."]], [["\"I believe men will be fools enough for anything,\"", "“남자들은 무슨 짓이든 저지를 만큼 어리석다고 나는 믿소.”"], ["said Lady De Courcy.", "드 코시 백작부인이 말했다."], ["\"But, of course, if he did, it would come to nothing afterwards.", "“하지만 물론 그이가 그런다 한들 나중에는 아무 일도 아닌 것이 되고 말 거요."], ["I know one who would not be sorry.", "섭섭해하지 않을 사람을 나는 하나 알고 있소."], ["If ever a man was tired of a woman, Lord Dumbello is tired of her.\"", "사내가 여자에게 물리는 일이 있다면, 덤벨로 경은 그 여자에게 물릴 대로 물렸다오.”"]], [["But in this, as in almost everything else, the wicked old woman spoke scandal.", "그러나 이 일에서도 다른 거의 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 그 못된 늙은 여자는 헛소문을 옮긴 것이었다."], ["Lord Dumbello was still proud of his wife, and as fond of her as a man can be of a woman whose fondness depends upon mere pride.", "덤벨로 경은 여전히 제 아내를 자랑스러워했고, 그 애정이 오로지 자부심에만 기대고 있는 남자가 여자를 아낄 수 있는 만큼은 그녀를 아꼈다."]], [["There had not been much that was dangerous in the conversation between Mr.", "팰리서 씨와 레이디 덤벨로 사이에 오간 이야기에는 위험한 구석이 별로 없었다."], ["Palliser and Lady Dumbello, but I cannot say the same as to that which was going on at the same moment between Crosbie and Lady Alexandrina.", "그러나 바로 그 시각에 크로즈비와 레이디 알렉산드리나 사이에서 벌어지고 있던 이야기를 두고는 그렇게 말할 수가 없다."], ["She, as I have said, walked away in almost open dudgeon when Lady Julia recommenced her attack about poor Lily, nor did she return to the general circle during the evening.", "앞서 말했다시피 그녀는 레이디 줄리아가 가엾은 릴리 이야기로 다시 공격을 시작하자 거의 대놓고 골이 나서 자리를 떴고, 그날 저녁 내내 사람들 무리로 돌아오지 않았다."], ["There were two large drawing-rooms at Courcy Castle, joined together by a narrow link of a room, which might have been called a passage, had it not been lighted by two windows coming down to the floor, carpeted as were the drawing-rooms, and warmed with a separate fireplace.", "코시 성에는 큰 응접실이 둘 있었고, 그 둘을 좁다란 방 하나가 고리처럼 이어 주고 있었다. 바닥까지 내려오는 창이 둘 나 있어 밝고, 응접실과 마찬가지로 양탄자가 깔려 있으며, 벽난로가 따로 있어 데워지지 않았더라면 그저 복도라 불렸을 방이었다."], ["Hither she betook herself, and was soon followed by her married sister Amelia.", "그녀는 그곳으로 물러갔고, 얼마 지나지 않아 혼인한 언니 어밀리아가 뒤따라왔다."]], [["\"That woman almost drives me mad,\"", "“저 여자 때문에 미쳐 버릴 지경이야.”"], ["said Alexandrina, as they stood together with their toes upon the fender.", "두 사람이 발끝을 난로 울에 얹고 나란히 선 채로 알렉산드리나가 말했다."]], [["\"But, my dear, you of all people should not allow yourself to be driven mad on such a subject.\"", "“하지만 얘, 다른 사람은 몰라도 네가 그런 일로 미쳐서야 되겠니.”"]], [["\"That's all very well, Amelia.\"", "“말이야 그렇지, 어밀리아 언니.”"]], [["\"The question is this, my dear,--what does Mr. Crosbie mean to do?\"", "“문제는 이거야, 얘—크로즈비 씨가 어쩔 작정이냐는 것.”"]], [["\"How should I know?\"", "“내가 그걸 어떻게 알아?”"]], [["\"If you don't know, it will be safer to suppose that he is going to marry this girl; and in that case--\"", "“네가 모르겠거든 그이가 그 처녀와 혼인할 거라고 치는 편이 안전해. 그리고 그렇다면—”"]], [["\"Well, what in that case?", "“그렇다면 뭐?"], ["Are you going to be another Lady Julia?", "언니도 또 하나의 레이디 줄리아가 될 참이야?"], ["What do I care about the girl?\"", "내가 그 처녀를 무슨 상관한다고?”"]], [["\"I don't suppose you care much about the girl; and if you care as little about Mr.", "“네가 그 처녀를 상관하지 않는 건 알아. 그리고 크로즈비 씨도 그만큼 상관없다면 그것으로 끝이지."], ["Crosbie, there's an end of it; only in that case, Alexandrina--\"", "다만 그렇다면, 알렉산드리나—”"]], [["\"Well, what in that case?\"", "“그렇다면 뭐?”"]], [["\"You know I don't want to preach to you.", "“너한테 설교하고 싶지 않다는 건 알잖니."], ["Can't you tell me at once whether you really like him?", "그이가 정말로 마음에 드는지 아닌지 그냥 대번에 말해 줄 수는 없어?"], ["You and I have always been good friends.\"", "우리 둘은 늘 사이가 좋았잖니.”"], ["And the married sister put her arm affectionately round the waist of her who wished to be married.", "그러고서 혼인한 언니는 혼인하고 싶어 하는 동생의 허리를 다정하게 팔로 감쌌다."]], [["\"I like him well enough.\"", "“그이가 마음에 들어, 충분히.”"]], [["\"And has he made any declaration to you?\"", "“그럼 그이가 너한테 무슨 고백이라도 했니?”"]], [["\"In a sort of a way he has.", "“어떻게 보면 했지."], ["Hark, here he is!\"", "쉿, 저기 오네!”"], ["And Crosbie, coming in from the larger room, joined the sisters at the fireplace.", "그러고서 더 큰 방에서 건너온 크로즈비가 난롯가의 두 자매와 어울렸다."]], [["\"We were driven away by the clack of Lady Julia's tongue,\"", "“레이디 줄리아의 혀가 딸그락대는 통에 쫓겨 왔다오.”"], ["said the elder.", "언니가 말했다."]], [["\"I never met such a woman,\"", "“저런 여자는 처음 만나 봅니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"There cannot well be many like her,\"", "“저런 여자가 여럿 있을 것 같지는 않네요.”"], ["said Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."], ["And after that they all stood silent for a minute or two.", "그러고서 세 사람은 일이 분 동안 말없이 서 있었다."], ["Lady Amelia Gazebee was considering whether or no she would do well to go and leave the two together.", "레이디 어밀리아 게이지비는 두 사람을 남겨 두고 자리를 뜨는 편이 좋을지 어떨지 궁리하고 있었다."], ["If it were intended that Mr. Crosbie should marry her sister, it would certainly be well to give him an opportunity of expressing such a wish on his own part.", "크로즈비 씨가 제 동생과 혼인하기로 되어 있는 것이라면, 그가 제 뜻을 스스로 말로 나타낼 기회를 주는 것이 분명 좋을 터였다."], ["But if Alexandrina was simply making a fool of herself, then it would be well for her to stay.", "그러나 알렉산드리나가 그저 바보짓을 하고 있는 것이라면 그때는 남아 있는 편이 좋을 것이었다."], ["\"I suppose she would rather I should go,\"", "“내가 가 주기를 바라겠지.”"], ["said the elder sister to herself; and then, obeying the rule which should guide all our actions from one to another, she went back and joined the crowd.", "언니는 속으로 그렇게 말했다. 그러고는 사람과 사람 사이의 모든 행동을 이끌어야 마땅한 그 계율에 따라 되돌아가 사람들 무리에 끼었다."]], [["\"Will you come on into the other room?\"", "“저쪽 방으로 가시겠습니까?”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"I think we are very well here,\"", "“여기도 아주 좋은 것 같은데요.”"], ["Alexandrina replied.", "알렉산드리나가 대답했다."]], [["\"But I wish to speak to you,--particularly,\"", "“하지만 드릴 말씀이 있습니다—긴히요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"And cannot you speak here?\"", "“여기서는 못 하시나요?”"]], [["\"No. They will be passing backwards and forwards.\"", "“못 합니다."], ["Lady Alexandrina said nothing further, but led the way into the other large room.", "사람들이 이리로 저리로 오갈 테니까요.” 레이디 알렉산드리나는 더는 아무 말도 하지 않고 앞장서서 다른 큰 응접실로 갔다."], ["That also was lighted, and there were in it four or five persons.", "그곳에도 불이 켜져 있었고 네댓 사람이 있었다."], ["Lady Rosina was reading a work on the Millennium, with a light to herself in one corner.", "레이디 로지나는 한쪽 구석에서 저 혼자 등불을 켜 놓고 천년왕국에 관한 책을 읽고 있었다."], ["Her brother John was asleep in an arm-chair, and a young gentleman and lady were playing chess.", "그녀의 오라비 존은 안락의자에서 잠들어 있었고, 젊은 신사와 아가씨가 체스를 두고 있었다."], ["There was, however, ample room for Crosbie and Alexandrina to take up a position apart.", "그러나 크로즈비와 알렉산드리나가 따로 떨어져 자리를 잡을 만한 데는 넉넉했다."]], [["\"And now, Mr. Crosbie, what have you got to say to me?", "“자, 크로즈비 씨, 저에게 하실 말씀이 무엇인가요?"], ["But, first, I mean to repeat Lady Julia's question, as I told you that I should do.--When did you hear last from Miss Dale?\"", "하지만 먼저, 그러겠다고 말씀드린 대로 레이디 줄리아가 한 그 질문을 그대로 되풀이하겠어요.—데일 양에게서 마지막으로 소식을 들으신 것이 언제죠?”"]], [["\"It is cruel in you to ask me such a question, after what I have already told you.", "“이미 말씀드린 것이 있는데도 그런 질문을 하시다니 잔인하십니다."], ["You know that I have given to Miss Dale a promise of marriage.\"", "제가 데일 양에게 혼인 약속을 했다는 것을 아시지 않습니까.”"]], [["\"Very well, sir.", "“그렇군요."], ["I don't see why you should bring me in here to tell me anything that is so publicly known as that.", "그렇게 온 세상이 다 아는 이야기를 하려고 굳이 저를 여기까지 데려오실 까닭이 무엇인지 모르겠네요."], ["With such a herald as Lady Julia it was quite unnecessary.\"", "레이디 줄리아 같은 전령이 있는데 그럴 필요는 조금도 없었잖아요.”"]], [["\"If you can only answer me in that tone I will make an end of it at once.", "“그런 말투로밖에 대답해 주실 수 없다면 저는 이 일을 당장 끝내겠습니다."], ["When I told you of my engagement, I told you also that another woman possessed my heart.", "제 약혼 이야기를 했을 때 저는 다른 여자가 제 마음을 차지하고 있다는 말도 함께 드렸습니다."], ["Am I wrong to suppose that you knew to whom I alluded?\"", "제가 누구를 가리킨 것인지 당신이 아셨으리라고 여기는 것이 잘못입니까?”"]], [["\"Indeed, I did not, Mr. Crosbie.", "“정말이지 몰랐어요, 크로즈비 씨."], ["I am no conjuror, and I have not scrutinized you so closely as your friend Lady Julia.\"", "저는 요술쟁이가 아니고, 당신 벗인 레이디 줄리아만큼 당신을 샅샅이 뜯어본 것도 아니니까요.”"]], [["\"It is you that I love.", "“제가 사랑하는 사람은 당신입니다."], ["I am sure I need hardly say so now.\"", "이제 와서 굳이 말할 것도 없겠지요.”"]], [["\"Hardly, indeed,--considering that you are engaged to Miss Dale.\"", "“정말이지 그렇겠네요—당신이 데일 양과 약혼했다는 것을 생각하면요.”"]], [["\"As to that I have, of course, to own that I have behaved foolishly;--worse than foolishly, if you choose to say so.", "“그 점에 대해서는 물론 제가 어리석게 굴었다고 인정해야겠지요—당신이 그렇게 말하고 싶으시다면 어리석은 것보다 더한 짓이었다고요."], ["You cannot condemn me more absolutely than I condemn myself.", "제가 저를 단죄하는 것보다 더 모질게 저를 단죄하실 수는 없을 겁니다."], ["But I have made up my mind as to one thing.", "그러나 한 가지만은 마음을 정했습니다."], ["I will not marry where I do not love.\"", "저는 사랑하지 않는 곳에서는 혼인하지 않겠습니다.”"], ["Oh, if Lily could have heard him as he then spoke!", "아, 그때 그가 그렇게 말하는 것을 릴리가 들을 수 있었더라면!"], ["\"It would be impossible for me to speak in terms too high of Miss Dale; but I am quite sure that I could not make her happy as her husband.\"", "“데일 양을 아무리 높여 말한들 지나치지 않을 것입니다. 그러나 제가 그 사람의 남편이 되어 그 사람을 행복하게 해 줄 수는 없으리라는 것만은 아주 확실합니다.”"]], [["\"Why did you not think of that before you asked her?\"", "“청혼하시기 전에는 왜 그 생각을 못 하셨나요?”"], ["said Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."], ["But there was very little of condemnation in her tone.", "그러나 그 말투에는 나무라는 기색이 거의 없었다."]], [["\"I ought to have done so; but it is hardly for you to blame me with severity.", "“그랬어야 했지요. 하지만 당신이 저를 모질게 나무랄 처지는 아니지 않습니까."], ["Had you, when we were last together in London--had you been less--\"", "우리가 지난번 런던에서 함께 있었을 때 당신이—당신이 조금만 덜—”"]], [["\"Less what?\"", "“덜 무엇이요?”"]], [["\"Less defiant,\"", "“덜 도도하셨더라면,”"], ["said Crosbie, \"all this might perhaps have been avoided.\"", "크로즈비가 말했다. “이 모든 일을 어쩌면 피할 수 있었을 겁니다.”"]], [["Lady Alexandrina could not remember that she had been defiant; but, however, she let that pass.", "레이디 알렉산드리나는 제가 도도하게 굴었던 기억이 나지 않았다. 그러나 어쨌든 그것은 그냥 넘겼다."], ["\"Oh, yes; of course it was my fault.\"", "“아, 그렇지요. 물론 제 잘못이었겠지요.”"]], [["\"I went down there to Allington with my heart ill at ease, and now I have fallen into this trouble.", "“저는 마음이 편치 않은 채로 앨링턴에 내려갔고, 그러다 이 곤경에 빠진 것입니다."], ["I tell you all as it has happened.", "있었던 그대로 다 말씀드립니다."], ["It is impossible that I should marry Miss Dale.", "제가 데일 양과 혼인한다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일입니다."], ["It would be wicked in me to do so, seeing that my heart belongs altogether to another.", "제 마음이 온통 다른 사람의 것인데 그렇게 한다면 그것은 몹쓸 짓이 될 테지요."], ["I have told you who is that other; and now may I hope for an answer?\"", "그 다른 사람이 누구인지는 말씀드렸습니다. 이제 대답을 바라도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["\"An answer to what?\"", "“무엇에 대한 대답이요?”"]], [["\"Alexandrina, will you be my wife?\"", "“알렉산드리나, 제 아내가 되어 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["If it had been her object to bring him to a point-blank declaration and proposition of marriage, she had certainly achieved her object now.", "그를 에두르지 않은 고백과 혼인 신청에까지 끌어내는 것이 그녀의 목적이었다면, 이제 그 목적은 분명히 이룬 셈이었다."], ["And she had that trust in her own power of management and in her mother's, that she did not fear that in accepting him she would incur the risk of being served as he was serving Lily Dale.", "그리고 그녀는 제 수완과 제 어머니의 수완을 어찌나 믿었던지, 그를 받아들였다가 그가 릴리 데일에게 하고 있는 그 짓을 저도 당하게 될 위험을 겁내지 않았다."], ["She knew her own position and his too well for that.", "그러기에는 제 처지도 그의 처지도 너무 잘 알고 있었다."], ["If she accepted him she would in due course of time become his wife,--let Miss Dale and all her friends say what they might to the contrary.", "그를 받아들인다면 때가 되어 그의 아내가 될 것이었다—데일 양이며 그 벗들이 무어라 반대해 떠들어 대든 말이다."], ["As to that head she had no fear.", "그 점에서는 두려울 것이 없었다."], ["But nevertheless she did not accept him at once.", "그런데도 그녀는 그를 대뜸 받아들이지는 않았다."], ["Though she wished for the prize, her woman's nature hindered her from taking it when it was offered to her.", "그 전리품을 바라면서도, 여자로 태어난 천성이 그것을 내미는 대로 냉큼 받아 쥐는 것을 가로막았다."]], [["\"How long is it, Mr.", "“크로즈비 씨,”"], ["Crosbie,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"since you put the same question to Miss Dale?\"", "“똑같은 질문을 데일 양에게 하신 지는 얼마나 되었죠?”"]], [["\"I have told you everything, Alexandrina,--as I promised that I would do.", "“저는 당신에게 모든 것을 말씀드렸습니다, 알렉산드리나—그러겠다고 약속한 대로요."], ["If you intend to punish me for doing so--\"", "그렇게 한 것을 두고 저를 벌하실 작정이라면—”"]], [["\"And I might ask another question.", "“그리고 저는 또 하나 물어볼 수도 있겠지요."], ["How long will it be before you put the same question to some other girl?\"", "똑같은 질문을 또 다른 아가씨에게 하시기까지는 얼마나 걸릴까요?”"]], [["He turned round as though to walk away from her in anger; but when he had gone half the distance to the door he returned.", "그는 성이 나서 그녀에게서 걸어 나가려는 듯이 몸을 돌렸다. 그러나 문까지 절반쯤 갔을 때 되돌아왔다."]], [["\"By heaven!\"", "“맹세코,”"], ["he said, and he spoke somewhat roughly, too, \"I'll have an answer.", "그가 말했는데, 말투도 얼마간 거칠었다. “저는 대답을 듣고야 말겠습니다."], ["You at any rate have nothing with which to reproach me.", "적어도 당신에게는 저를 나무랄 거리가 없습니다."], ["All that I have done wrong, I have done through you, or on your behalf.", "제가 저지른 잘못은 모두 당신 때문에, 그렇지 않으면 당신을 위해 저지른 것입니다."], ["You have heard my proposal.", "제 청혼을 들으셨습니다."], ["Do you intend to accept it?\"", "받아들이실 작정입니까?”"]], [["\"I declare you startle me.", "“정말이지 깜짝 놀랐네요."], ["If you demanded my money or my life, you could not be more imperious.\"", "제 돈이나 목숨을 내놓으라 하신다 해도 이보다 더 위압적일 수는 없겠어요.”"]], [["\"Certainly not more resolute in my determination.\"", "“제 결심이 이보다 더 굳을 수는 없다는 것만은 분명합니다.”"]], [["\"And if I decline the honour?\"", "“제가 그 영광을 사양한다면요?”"]], [["\"I shall think you the most fickle of your sex.\"", "“당신을 여자들 가운데 가장 변덕스러운 사람이라 여기겠지요.”"]], [["\"And if I were to accept it?\"", "“그럼 제가 받아들인다면요?”"]], [["\"I would swear that you were the best, the dearest, and the sweetest of women.\"", "“당신이 여자들 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 사랑스럽고 가장 다정한 사람이라고 맹세하겠지요.”"]], [["\"I would rather have your good opinion than your bad, certainly,\"", "“나쁘게 여기시는 것보다야 좋게 여기시는 편이 낫겠지요, 물론.”"], ["said Lady Alexandrina.", "레이디 알렉산드리나가 말했다."], ["And then it was understood by both of them that that affair was settled.", "그러고서 두 사람 다 그 일이 정해졌다는 것을 알아들었다."], ["Whenever she was called on in future to speak of Lily, she always called her, \"that poor Miss Dale;\"", "앞으로 릴리 이야기를 해야 할 일이 있을 때면 그녀는 언제나 그녀를 ‘그 가엾은 데일 양’이라고 불렀다."], ["but she never again spoke a word of reproach to her future lord about that little adventure.", "그러나 그 자잘한 사건을 두고 제 미래의 주인에게 나무라는 말을 두 번 다시 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["\"I shall tell mamma, to-night,\"", "“오늘 밤에 어머니께 말씀드릴게요.”"], ["she said to him, as she bade him good-night in some sequestered nook to which they had betaken themselves.", "두 사람이 물러가 있던 어느 후미진 구석에서 그에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하며 그녀가 말했다."], ["Lady Julia's eye was again on them as they came out from the sequestered nook, but Alexandrina no longer cared for Lady Julia.", "그 후미진 구석에서 두 사람이 나올 때 레이디 줄리아의 눈이 또 그들에게 쏠려 있었으나, 알렉산드리나는 이제 레이디 줄리아 따위는 아랑곳하지 않았다."]], [["\"George, I cannot quite understand about that Mr. Palliser.", "“조지, 그 팰리서 씨라는 분은 도무지 모르겠어요."], ["Isn't he to be a duke, and oughtn't he to be a lord now?\"", "공작이 될 사람 아닌가요? 그렇다면 지금도 경이어야 하는 것 아니에요?”"], ["This question was asked by Mrs. George De Courcy of her husband, when they found themselves together in the seclusion of the nuptial chamber.", "조지 드 코시 부인이 부부의 침실이라는 은밀한 곳에 남편과 단둘이 있게 되었을 때 던진 질문이었다."]], [["\"Yes; he'll be Duke of Omnium when the old fellow dies.", "“그래, 그 늙은이가 죽으면 옴니엄 공작이 되지."], ["I think he's one of the slowest fellows I ever came across.", "내가 여태 만나 본 사람 중에 제일 굼뜬 축에 드는 것 같더군."], ["He'll take deuced good care of the property, though.\"", "하기야 재산 하나는 지독히도 잘 건사하겠지만.”"]], [["\"But, George, do explain it to me.", "“하지만 조지, 좀 설명해 줘요."], ["It is so stupid not to understand, and I am afraid of opening my mouth for fear of blundering.\"", "모르고 있으려니 어찌나 바보 같은지, 실수할까 무서워서 입도 못 떼겠어요.”"]], [["\"Then keep your mouth shut, my dear.", "“그럼 입을 다물고 있어, 여보."], ["You'll learn all those sort of things in time, and nobody notices it if you don't say anything.\"", "그런 건 차차 다 알게 될 테고, 아무 말도 안 하면 아무도 눈치채지 못하니까.”"]], [["\"Yes, but, George;--I don't like to sit silent all the night.", "“그건 그렇지만, 조지—밤새 잠자코 앉아 있는 건 싫어요."], ["I'd sooner be up here with a novel if I can't speak about anything.\"", "아무 얘기도 못 할 바에는 차라리 여기 올라와서 소설이나 읽는 편이 낫겠어요.”"]], [["\"Look at Lady Dumbello.", "“레이디 덤벨로를 봐."], ["She doesn't want to be always talking.\"", "그 사람은 노상 떠들고 싶어 하지 않잖아.”"]], [["\"Lady Dumbello is very different from me.", "“레이디 덤벨로는 나하고 아주 다르지요. 그건 그렇고 좀 말해 줘요."], ["But do tell me, who is Mr. Palliser?\"", "팰리서 씨는 대체 누구예요?”"]], [["\"He's the duke's nephew.", "“공작의 조카야."], ["If he were the duke's son, he would be the Marquis of Silverbridge.\"", "공작의 아들이었다면 실버브리지 후작이 되었겠지.”"]], [["\"And will he be plain Mister till his uncle dies?\"", "“그럼 숙부가 죽을 때까지는 그냥 밋밋한 씨로 불리는 거예요?”"]], [["\"Yes, a very plain Mister.\"", "“그래, 아주 밋밋한 씨지.”"]], [["\"What a pity for him.", "“그이한테는 참 안됐네요."], ["But, George,--if I have a baby, and if he should be a boy, and if--\"", "그런데 조지—내가 아기를 낳고, 그 아기가 아들이고, 그리고 만약—”"]], [["\"Oh, nonsense; it will be time enough to talk of that when he comes.", "“아, 무슨 소리야. 그런 얘기는 그 녀석이 나오고 나서 해도 늦지 않아."], ["I'm going to sleep.\"", "난 잘 테야.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXIV. A MOTHER-IN-LAW AND A FATHER-IN-LAW.", "# 제24장. 장모와 장인"]], [["On the following morning Mr. Plantagenet Palliser was off upon his political mission before breakfast;--either that, or else some private comfort was afforded to him in guise of solitary rolls and coffee.", "이튿날 아침 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 아침 식사도 하기 전에 정치 임무를 띠고 떠났다."], ["The public breakfast at Courcy Castle was going on at eleven o'clock, and at that hour Mr.", "그게 아니라면, 혼자 먹는 롤빵과 커피라는 모양새로 그에게 어떤 사사로운 위안이 주어졌든가."], ["Palliser was already closeted with the Mayor of Silverbridge.", "코시 성의 공식 아침 식사는 열한 시에 차려졌고, 그 시각에 팰리서 씨는 이미 실버브리지 시장과 단둘이 마주 앉아 밀담을 나누고 있었다."]], [["\"I must get off by the 3.45 train,\"", "“나는 3시 45분 기차로 떠나야 하오.”"], ["said Mr. Palliser.", "팰리서 씨가 말했다."], ["\"Who is there to speak after me?\"", "“내 뒤에 연설할 사람은 누가 있소?”"]], [["\"Well, I shall say a few words; and Growdy,--he'll expect them to listen to him.", "“글쎄요, 제가 몇 마디 하겠습니다. 그리고 그라우디가—그 사람은 사람들이 제 말을 들어 주기를 바랄 겁니다."], ["Growdy has always stood very firm by his grace, Mr. Palliser.\"", "그라우디는 늘 공작 각하 편에 굳건히 서 온 사람입니다, 팰리서 씨.”"]], [["\"Mind we are in the room sharp at one.", "“한 시 정각에는 회장에 들어가도록 신경 쓰시오."], ["And you can have a fly, for me to get away to the station, ready in the yard.", "그리고 내가 역으로 갈 삯마차를 마당에 대기시켜 두시오."], ["I won't go a moment before I can help. I shall be just an hour and a half myself.", "어쩔 수 없이 떠나야 할 때가 되기 전에는 한시도 먼저 자리를 뜨지 않겠소."], ["No, thank you, I never take any wine in the morning.\"", "내가 맡을 몫은 딱 한 시간 반이오. 아니, 고맙지만 됐소."], ["And I may here state that Mr.", "나는 아침에는 술을 입에 대지 않소.”"], ["Palliser did get away by the 3.45 train, leaving Mr. Growdy still talking on the platform.", "그리고 여기서 나는, 팰리서 씨가 정말로 3시 45분 기차로 떠났고 그라우디 씨는 승강장에서 여전히 이야기를 늘어놓고 있었다는 사실을 밝혀 두어야겠다."], ["Constituents must be treated with respect; but time has become so scarce now-a-days that that respect has to be meted out by the quarter of an hour with parsimonious care.", "유권자들은 존중을 받아야 마땅하다. 그러나 요즈음은 시간이 하도 귀해져서 그 존중이라는 것도 십오 분 단위로 인색하게 아껴 가며 나누어 주어야 하는 것이다."]], [["In the meantime there was more leisure at Courcy Castle.", "그러는 동안 코시 성에는 한결 여유가 있었다."], ["Neither the countess nor Lady Alexandrina came down to breakfast, but their absence gave rise to no special remark.", "백작부인도 레이디 알렉산드리나도 아침 식사에 내려오지 않았지만, 두 사람이 보이지 않는다고 해서 특별히 말이 나오지는 않았다."], ["Breakfast at the castle was a morning meal at which people showed themselves, or did not show themselves, as it pleased them.", "성의 아침 식사란 사람들이 내키는 대로 얼굴을 내밀기도 하고 내밀지 않기도 하는 아침 끼니였다."], ["Lady Julia was there looking very glum, and Crosbie was sitting next to his future sister-in-law Margaretta, who already had placed herself on terms of close affection with him.", "레이디 줄리아는 몹시 시무룩한 얼굴로 그 자리에 있었고, 크로즈비는 장차 처형이 될 마거리타 옆에 앉아 있었는데, 그녀는 벌써부터 그와 각별히 다정한 사이가 되어 있었다."], ["As he finished his tea she whispered into his ear, \"Mr.", "그가 차를 다 마시자 그녀가 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였다."], ["Crosbie, if you could spare half an hour, mamma would so like to see you in her own room.\"", "“크로즈비 씨, 반 시간만 내실 수 있다면 어머니께서 자기 방에서 당신을 뵙고 싶어 하세요.”"], ["Crosbie declared that he would be delighted to wait upon her, and did in truth feel some gratitude in being welcomed as a son-in-law into the house.", "크로즈비는 기꺼이 찾아뵙겠다고 말했고, 사위로서 이 집에 맞아들여지는 데 대해 실제로 얼마간 고마움을 느끼기도 했다."], ["And yet he felt also that he was being caught, and that in ascending into the private domains of the countess he would be setting the seal upon his own captivity.", "그러면서도 그는 자기가 붙잡히고 있다는 것을, 그리고 백작부인의 사사로운 영역으로 올라가는 것이 곧 제 감금에 스스로 봉인을 찍는 일이라는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Nevertheless, he went with a smiling face and a light step, Lady Margaretta ushering him the way. \"Mamma,\"", "그럼에도 그는 미소 띤 얼굴로 가벼운 걸음을 옮겼고, 레이디 마거리타가 앞서 길을 안내했다."], ["said she; \"I have brought Mr.", "“어머니.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["Crosbie up to you.", "“크로즈비 씨를 모시고 왔어요."], ["I did not know that you were here, Alexandrina, or I should have warned him.\"", "알렉산드리나, 네가 여기 있는 줄은 몰랐구나. 알았으면 이분께 미리 일러 두었을 텐데.”"]], [["The countess and her youngest daughter had been breakfasting together in the elder lady's sitting-room, and were now seated in a very graceful and well-arranged deshabille.", "백작부인과 그 막내딸은 어머니 쪽 거실에서 함께 아침을 먹고 있었고, 이제는 아주 우아하고 매무새를 잘 다듬은 실내복 차림으로 앉아 있었다."], ["The tea-cups out of which they had been drinking were made of some elegant porcelain, the teapot and cream-jug were of chased silver and as delicate in their way.", "두 사람이 마시던 찻잔은 고급 자기로 만든 것이었고, 찻주전자와 크림 단지는 돋을새김을 한 은제로 그 나름대로 섬세했다."], ["The remnant of food consisted of morsels of French roll which had not even been allowed to crumble themselves in a disorderly fashion, and of infinitesimal pats of butter.", "먹다 남은 것이라고는 부스러기 하나 흐트러뜨리는 일조차 허락받지 못한 프랑스식 롤빵 몇 조각과, 티끌만 한 버터 덩이 몇 개뿐이었다."], ["If the morning meal of the two ladies had been as unsubstantial as the appearance of the fragments indicated, it must be presumed that they intended to lunch early.", "두 부인의 아침 끼니가 그 남은 조각들이 말해 주는 것만큼이나 부실한 것이었다면, 두 사람은 점심을 일찍 들 작정이었다고 보아야 할 것이다."], ["The countess herself was arrayed in an elaborate morning wrapper of figured silk, but the simple Alexandrina wore a plain white muslin peignoir, fastened with pink ribbon.", "백작부인은 무늬 있는 비단으로 공들여 지은 아침 실내복을 걸치고 있었으나, 소박한 알렉산드리나는 분홍 리본으로 여민 밋밋한 흰 모슬린 실내 가운을 입고 있었다."], ["Her hair, which she usually carried in long rolls, now hung loose over her shoulders, and certainly added something to her stock of female charms.", "평소에는 길게 말아 올리고 다니던 머리채가 지금은 어깨 위로 풀어 내려져 있었는데, 그것이 그녀가 지닌 여성적 매력에 무언가를 확실히 보태 주고 있었다."], ["The countess got up as Crosbie entered and greeted him with an open hand; but Alexandrina kept her seat, and merely nodded at him a little welcome.", "크로즈비가 들어서자 백작부인은 자리에서 일어나 손을 내밀며 그를 맞았다. 그러나 알렉산드리나는 앉은 자리를 지킨 채 그저 고개를 조금 끄덕여 인사를 대신했다."], ["\"I must run down again,\"", "“저는 다시 내려가 봐야겠어요.”"], ["said Margaretta, \"or I shall have left Amelia with all the cares of the house upon her.\"", "마거리타가 말했다. “안 그러면 어밀리아 언니한테 집안일을 전부 떠맡기게 될 테니까요.”"]], [["\"Alexandrina has told me all about it,\"", "“알렉산드리나가 나한테 다 이야기했어요.”"], ["said the countess, with her sweetest smile; \"and I have given her my approval.", "백작부인이 더없이 상냥한 미소를 지으며 말했다. “그리고 나는 그 애한테 승낙을 해 주었지요."], ["I really do think you will suit each other very well.\"", "정말이지 두 사람은 서로 아주 잘 어울릴 것 같군요.”"]], [["\"I am very much obliged to you,\"", "“대단히 감사합니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"I'm sure at any rate of this,--that she will suit me very well.\"", "“적어도 이것만은 확실합니다. 따님은 저한테 아주 잘 어울릴 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Yes; I think she will.", "“그럼요, 나도 그러리라 생각해요."], ["She is a good sensible girl.\"", "착하고 분별 있는 아이지요.”"]], [["\"Psha, mamma; pray don't go on in that Goody Twoshoes sort of way.\"", "“쳇, 어머니, 제발 그런 굿디 투슈즈 같은 투로 말씀하지 마세요.”"]], [["\"So you are, my dear.", "“너는 정말 그렇단다, 얘야."], ["If you were not it would not be well for you to do as you are going to do.", "그렇지 않았다면 네가 지금 하려는 일을 하는 것이 좋을 리 없지."], ["If you were giddy and harum-scarum, and devoted to rank and wealth and that sort of thing, it would not be well for you to marry a commoner without fortune.", "네가 경박하고 덜렁대는 아이였다면, 그리고 지위니 재산이니 하는 것에 마음을 온통 빼앗긴 아이였다면, 재산 없는 평민과 결혼하는 것이 너한테 좋을 리 없을 테고."], ["I'm sure Mr. Crosbie will excuse me for saying so much as that.\"", "크로즈비 씨도 내가 이만큼 말하는 것쯤은 너그러이 봐주시리라 믿어요.”"]], [["\"Of course I know,\"", "“저야 물론 압니다.”"], ["said Crosbie, \"that I had no right to look so high.\"", "크로즈비가 말했다. “제가 그토록 높은 곳을 넘볼 처지가 아니었다는 것을요.”"]], [["\"Well; we'll say nothing more about it,\"", "“자, 그 이야기는 이제 그만합시다.”"], ["said the countess.", "백작부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Pray don't,\"", "“제발 그만하세요.”"], ["said Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."], ["\"It sounds so like a sermon.\"", "“꼭 설교 같잖아요.”"]], [["\"Sit down, Mr. Crosbie,\"", "“앉으세요, 크로즈비 씨.”"], ["said the countess, \"and let us have a little conversation.", "백작부인이 말했다. “우리 이야기나 좀 나눕시다."], ["She shall sit by you, if you like it.", "원하신다면 저 애를 당신 곁에 앉히지요."], ["Nonsense, Alexandrina,--if he asks it!\"", "무슨 소리냐, 알렉산드리나. 이분이 청하시는데!”"]], [["\"Don't, mamma;--I mean to remain where I am.\"", "“그만두세요, 어머니. 저는 여기 그대로 있겠어요.”"]], [["\"Very well, my dear;--then remain where you are.", "“좋아, 얘야. 그럼 그 자리에 그대로 있으렴."], ["She is a wilful girl, Mr.", "고집이 센 아이예요, 크로즈비 씨."], ["Crosbie; as you will say when you hear that she has told me all that you told her last night.\"", "어젯밤에 당신이 저 애한테 한 말을 저 애가 나한테 죄다 옮겼다는 걸 들으시면 당신도 그렇게 말씀하실 거예요.”"], ["Upon hearing this, he changed colour a little, but said nothing.", "이 말을 듣고 그는 안색이 조금 변했으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["\"She has told me,\"", "“저 애가 나한테 이야기하더군요.”"], ["continued the countess, \"about that young lady at Allington.", "백작부인이 말을 이었다. “앨링턴의 그 젊은 아가씨에 대해서요."], ["Upon my word, I'm afraid you have been very naughty.\"", "정말이지, 당신은 아주 짓궂게 굴었던 모양이에요.”"]], [["\"I have been foolish, Lady De Courcy.\"", "“제가 어리석었습니다, 드 코시 백작부인.”"]], [["\"Of course; I did not mean anything worse than that.", "“물론이지요. 나도 그보다 더 나쁜 뜻으로 말한 건 아니에요."], ["Yes, you have been foolish;--amusing yourself in a thoughtless way, you know, and, perhaps, a little piqued because a certain lady was not to be won so easily as your Royal Highness wished.", "그래요, 당신은 어리석었어요. 아무 생각 없이 심심풀이를 한 거지요."], ["Well, now, all that must be settled, you know, as quickly as possible.", "그리고 어쩌면, 어떤 아가씨가 전하께서 바라시는 만큼 쉽게 넘어오지 않아서 자존심이 조금 상했던 것일 테고요. 자, 이제 그 일은 되도록 빨리 정리되어야 해요."], ["I don't want to ask any indiscreet questions; but if the young lady has really been left with any idea that you meant anything, don't you think you should undeceive her at once?\"", "나는 경솔한 질문을 하고 싶지는 않아요. 하지만 그 아가씨가 정말로 당신에게 무슨 뜻이 있었던 것으로 알고 남아 있다면, 당장 그 오해를 풀어 주어야 하지 않겠어요?”"]], [["\"Of course he will, mamma.\"", "“물론 그러실 거예요, 어머니.”"]], [["\"Of course you will; and it will be a great comfort to Alexandrina to know that the matter is arranged.", "“물론 그러시겠지요. 그리고 그 일이 정리되었다는 걸 알면 알렉산드리나한테도 큰 위안이 될 거예요."], ["You hear what Lady Julia is saying almost every hour of her life.", "레이디 줄리아가 하루에도 몇 번씩 무슨 말을 하고 다니는지 당신도 들으셨겠지요."], ["Now, of course, Alexandrina does not care what an old maid like Lady Julia may say; but it will be better for all parties that the rumour should be put a stop to.", "물론 알렉산드리나야 레이디 줄리아 같은 노처녀가 무슨 말을 하든 개의치 않아요. 하지만 그 소문이 그치는 편이 모두에게 나을 거예요."], ["If the earl were to hear it, he might, you know--\"", "백작이 그 이야기를 듣기라도 하면, 그이는 말이지요—”"], ["And the countess shook her head, thinking that she could thus best indicate what the earl might do, if he were to take it into his head to do anything.", "그러고 나서 백작부인은 고개를 내저었는데, 백작이 무슨 일이든 벌이기로 마음을 먹었을 때 어떻게 나올 수 있는지를 그렇게 하는 것이 가장 잘 내비치는 길이라고 여겼던 것이다."]], [["Crosbie could not bring himself to hold any very confidential intercourse with the countess about Lily; but he gave a muttered assurance that he should, as a matter of course, make known the truth to Miss Dale with as little delay as possible.", "크로즈비는 릴리에 관해 백작부인과 마음을 터놓고 이야기할 마음이 도무지 나지 않았다. 그러나 그는 물론 되도록 지체 없이 데일 양에게 진실을 알리겠다고 웅얼거리듯 다짐했다."], ["He could not say exactly when he would write, nor whether he would write to her or to her mother; but the thing should be done immediately on his return to town.", "언제 편지를 쓰겠다고도, 그녀에게 쓸지 그녀의 어머니에게 쓸지도 정확히 말할 수는 없었지만, 그 일만은 런던에 돌아가는 즉시 처리하겠다고 했다."]], [["\"If it will make the matter easier, I will write to Mrs. Dale,\"", "“그러는 편이 일을 쉽게 만든다면, 내가 데일 부인에게 편지를 쓰지요.”"], ["said the countess.", "백작부인이 말했다."], ["But to this scheme Mr. Crosbie objected very strongly.", "그러나 이 계획에는 크로즈비 씨가 아주 강하게 반대했다."]], [["And then a few words were said about the earl.", "그러고 나서 백작에 관해 몇 마디가 오갔다."], ["\"I will tell him this afternoon,\"", "“오늘 오후에 내가 그이한테 말하겠어요.”"], ["said the countess; \"and then you can see him to-morrow morning.", "백작부인이 말했다. “그러면 당신은 내일 아침에 그이를 만나면 돼요."], ["I don't suppose he will say very much, you know; and perhaps he may think,--you won't mind my saying it, I'm sure,--that Alexandrina might have done better.", "그이가 별말은 하지 않을 거예요. 그리고 어쩌면—내가 이런 말을 해도 언짢아하지 않으시리라 믿어요—알렉산드리나가 더 나은 자리를 골랐을 수도 있었다고 생각할지도 모르지요."], ["But I don't believe that he'll raise any strong objection.", "하지만 그이가 크게 반대하리라고는 믿지 않아요."], ["There will be something about settlements, and that sort of thing, of course.\"", "물론 혼인 재산 계약이니 뭐니 하는 이야기는 좀 있을 거예요.”"], ["Then the countess went away, and Alexandrina was left with her lover for half an hour.", "그런 다음 백작부인은 자리를 떴고, 알렉산드리나는 반 시간 동안 연인과 함께 남겨졌다."], ["When the half-hour was over, he felt that he would have given all that he had in the world to have back the last four-and-twenty hours of his existence.", "그 반 시간이 지났을 때, 그는 제 삶의 지난 스물네 시간을 되돌릴 수만 있다면 이 세상에서 가진 것을 전부 내놓아도 좋겠다고 느꼈다. 그러나 그에게는 아무런 희망이 없었다."], ["But he had no hope. To jilt Lily Dale would, no doubt, be within his power, but he knew that he could not jilt Lady Alexandrina De Courcy.", "릴리 데일을 차 버리는 일이야 틀림없이 그의 힘으로 할 수 있는 일이었겠지만, 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시를 차 버릴 수는 없다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."]], [["On the next morning at twelve o'clock he had his interview with the father, and a very unpleasant interview it was.", "이튿날 아침 열두 시에 그는 아버지 되는 이와 면담을 가졌는데, 그것은 대단히 불쾌한 면담이었다."], ["He was ushered into the earl's room, and found the great peer standing on the rug, with his back to the fire, and his hands in his breeches pockets.", "그는 백작의 방으로 안내되었고, 벽난로를 등진 채 난롯가 깔개 위에 서서 두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣고 있는 그 대단한 귀족과 마주쳤다."]], [["\"So you mean to marry my daughter?\"", "“그래, 자네가 내 딸과 결혼하겠다고?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I'm not very well, as you see; I seldom am.\"", "“보다시피 나는 몸이 좋지 않네. 늘 그렇긴 하네만.”"]], [["These last words were spoken in answer to Crosbie's greeting.", "이 마지막 말은 크로즈비의 인사에 대한 대답이었다."], ["Crosbie had held out his hand to the earl, and had carried his point so far that the earl had been forced to take one of his own out of his pocket, and give it to his proposed son-in-law.", "크로즈비가 백작에게 손을 내밀었고 그 수를 관철한 덕에, 백작은 어쩔 수 없이 주머니에서 한쪽 손을 꺼내어 사위가 되겠다는 사람에게 내어 주어야 했던 것이다."]], [["\"If your lordship has no objection.", "“백작님께서 반대하지 않으신다면 말입니다."], ["I have, at any rate, her permission to ask for yours.\"", "어쨌든 저는 따님의 허락을 받고서 백작님의 허락을 청하는 것입니다.”"]], [["\"I believe you have not any fortune, have you?", "“자네한테는 재산이 없다지, 그렇지 않은가?"], ["She's got none; of course you know that?\"", "그 애도 없네. 물론 그건 알고 있겠지?”"]], [["\"I have a few thousand pounds, and I believe she has as much.\"", "“저에게 몇천 파운드가 있고, 따님께도 그만큼 있는 줄로 압니다.”"]], [["\"About as much as will buy bread to keep the two of you from starving.", "“둘이 굶어 죽지 않을 만큼 빵이나 사면 딱 맞겠군."], ["It's nothing to me.", "나야 알 바 아니네."], ["You can marry her if you like; only, look here, I'll have no nonsense.", "원한다면 그 애와 결혼하게. 다만, 이보게, 허튼짓은 용납하지 않겠네."], ["I've had an old woman in with me this morning,--one of those that are here in the house,--telling me some story about some other girl that you have made a fool of.", "오늘 아침에 어떤 늙은 여자가 나를 찾아왔네—이 집에 묵고 있는 사람들 가운데 하나지—자네가 어떤 다른 아가씨를 우롱했다는 이야기를 늘어놓더군."], ["It's nothing to me how much of that sort of thing you may have done, so that you do none of it here.", "자네가 그런 짓을 얼마나 했든 나와는 상관없네. 여기서만 그러지 않으면 되네."], ["But,--if you play any prank of that kind with me, you'll find that you've made a mistake.\"", "그러나—나한테 그런 수작을 부렸다가는 자네가 잘못 짚었다는 것을 알게 될 걸세.”"]], [["Crosbie hardly made any answer to this, but got himself out of the room as quickly as he could.", "크로즈비는 이 말에 이렇다 할 대답을 거의 하지 못한 채, 되도록 빨리 그 방에서 빠져나왔다."]], [["\"You'd better talk to Gazebee about the trifle of money you've got,\"", "“자네가 가진 그 푼돈 이야기는 게이지비와 하는 편이 좋을 걸세.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["Then he dismissed the subject from his mind, and no doubt imagined that he had fully done his duty by his daughter.", "그러고 나서 그는 그 문제를 머릿속에서 치워 버렸고, 딸에 대한 제 도리를 남김없이 다했다고 여겼음이 틀림없다."]], [["On the day after this, Crosbie was to go.", "그 이튿날 크로즈비는 떠나기로 되어 있었다."], ["On the last afternoon, shortly before dinner, he was waylaid by Lady Julia, who had passed the day in preparing traps to catch him.", "마지막 날 오후, 만찬을 얼마 앞두고 그는 레이디 줄리아에게 길목을 붙잡혔는데, 그녀는 그날 하루를 그를 붙잡을 덫을 놓는 데 다 쓴 참이었다."]], [["\"Mr. Crosbie,\"", "“크로즈비 씨.”"], ["she said, \"let me have one word with you.", "그녀가 말했다. “한마디만 합시다."], ["Is this true?\"", "이게 사실인가요?”"]], [["\"Lady Julia,\"", "“레이디 줄리아.”"], ["he said, \"I really do not know why you should inquire into my private affairs.\"", "그가 말했다. “어째서 제 사사로운 일을 캐물으시는지 저는 정말 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, sir, you do know; you know very well.", "“아니요, 선생, 알고 계세요. 아주 잘 알고 계시죠."], ["That poor young lady who has no father and no brother, is my neighbour, and her friends are my friends.", "아버지도 오라비도 없는 그 가엾은 아가씨는 내 이웃이고, 그 아이의 벗들은 내 벗들이에요."], ["She is a friend of my own, and being an old woman, I have a right to speak for her.", "그 아이는 나 자신의 벗이기도 하고, 나는 늙은 여자이니 그 아이를 대신해 말할 자격이 있어요."], ["If this is true, Mr. Crosbie, you are treating her like a villain.\"", "이게 사실이라면, 크로즈비 씨, 당신은 그 아이를 악당처럼 대하고 있는 거예요.”"]], [["\"Lady Julia, I really must decline to discuss the matter with you.\"", "“레이디 줄리아, 저는 그 문제를 부인과 논하는 것을 정말이지 사양하겠습니다.”"]], [["\"I'll tell everybody what a villain you are; I will, indeed;--a villain and a poor weak silly fool.", "“당신이 얼마나 악당인지 온 세상에 다 말하고 다니겠어요. 정말로 그러겠어요."], ["She was too good for you; that's what she was.\"", "악당이자, 가엾고 나약한 얼간이라고요. 그 아이는 당신한테는 과분한 사람이었어요."], ["Crosbie, as Lady Julia was addressing to him the last words, hurried upstairs away from her, but her ladyship, standing on a landing-place, spoke up loudly, so that no word should be lost on her retreating enemy.", "그 아이는 그런 사람이었다고요.” 레이디 줄리아가 그 마지막 말을 그에게 퍼붓고 있는 동안 크로즈비는 그녀를 피해 서둘러 위층으로 올라갔으나, 부인은 층계참에 버티고 서서 목소리를 크게 높였으니, 달아나는 적이 한 마디도 놓치지 않게 하려는 것이었다."]], [["\"We positively must get rid of that woman,\"", "“저 여자는 기어이 내보내야겠다.”"], ["the countess, who heard it all, said to Margaretta.", "그 소리를 죄다 들은 백작부인이 마거리타에게 말했다."], ["\"She is disturbing the house and disgracing herself every day.\"", "“날마다 집안을 어지럽히고 제 낯을 깎고 있으니 말이다.”"]], [["\"She went to papa this morning, mamma.\"", "“오늘 아침에는 아버지께 갔었어요, 어머니.”"]], [["\"She did not get much by that move,\"", "“그런다고 별 소득은 없었을 게다.”"], ["said the countess.", "백작부인이 말했다."]], [["On the following morning Crosbie returned to town, but just before he left the castle he received a third letter from Lily Dale.", "그 이튿날 아침 크로즈비는 런던으로 돌아갔는데, 성을 떠나기 바로 직전에 그는 릴리 데일에게서 세 번째 편지를 받았다."], ["\"I have been rather disappointed at not hearing this morning,\"", "“오늘 아침에 소식이 없어서 좀 서운했어요.”"], ["said Lily, \"for I thought the postman would have brought me a letter.", "릴리는 이렇게 적었다. “우편배달부가 편지를 가져다줄 줄 알았거든요."], ["But I know you'll be a better boy when you get back to London, and I won't scold you.", "하지만 런던에 돌아가시면 더 착한 아이가 되실 걸 아니까 꾸짖지 않을래요."], ["Scold you, indeed!", "꾸짖다니요! 아니에요."], ["No; I'll never scold you, not though I shouldn't hear for a month.\"", "한 달 동안 소식이 없더라도 절대로 꾸짖지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["He would have given all that he had in the world, three times told, if he could have blotted out that visit to Courcy Castle from the past facts of his existence.", "코시 성으로의 그 방문을 제 삶의 지나간 사실들에서 지워 버릴 수만 있다면, 그는 이 세상에서 가진 것을 전부, 세 곱절로 내놓았을 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXV. ADOLPHUS CROSBIE SPENDS AN EVENING AT HIS CLUB.", "# 제25장. 어돌퍼스 크로즈비가 클럽에서 저녁을 보내다"]], [["Crosbie, as he was being driven from the castle to the nearest station, in a dog-cart hired from the hotel, could not keep himself from thinking of that other morning, not yet a fortnight past, on which he had left Allington; and as he thought of it he knew that he was a villain.", "크로즈비는 호텔에서 빌린 이륜 짐마차에 실려 성에서 가장 가까운 역으로 가는 동안, 아직 보름도 지나지 않은 그 다른 아침을, 곧 그가 앨링턴을 떠나던 아침을 도무지 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그것을 생각하는 동안 그는 자기가 악당임을 알았다."], ["On this morning Alexandrina had not come out from the house to watch his departure, and catch the last glance of his receding figure.", "이날 아침 알렉산드리나는 그가 떠나는 것을 지켜보려고, 멀어져 가는 그의 뒷모습을 마지막으로 한 번 담아 두려고 집에서 나오지 않았다."], ["As he had not started very early she had sat with him at the breakfast-table; but others also had sat there, and when he got up to go, she did no more than smile softly and give him her hand.", "그가 아주 이른 시각에 출발한 것은 아니었으므로 그녀가 아침 식탁에 그와 함께 앉아 있기는 했다. 그러나 그 자리에는 다른 사람들도 앉아 있었고, 그가 일어나 떠나려 할 때 그녀는 부드럽게 미소 지으며 손을 내미는 것 이상은 하지 않았다."], ["It had been already settled that he was to spend his Christmas at Courcy; as it had been also settled that he was to spend it at Allington.", "그가 성탄절을 코시에서 보내기로 하는 것은 이미 정해진 일이었다. 그가 그것을 앨링턴에서 보내기로 정해져 있었던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로."]], [["Lady Amelia was, of all the family, the most affectionate to him, and perhaps of them all she was the one whose affection was worth the most.", "레이디 어밀리아는 그 집안 사람들 가운데 그에게 가장 다정했고, 어쩌면 그들 모두 가운데 그 애정이 가장 값진 사람이었을 것이다."], ["She was not a woman endowed with a very high mind or with very noble feelings.", "그녀는 아주 고결한 정신이나 아주 고매한 감정을 타고난 여자가 아니었다."], ["She had begun life trusting to the nobility of her blood for everything, and declaring somewhat loudly among her friends that her father's rank and her mother's birth imposed on her the duty of standing closely by her own order.", "그녀는 무슨 일에나 제 핏줄의 고귀함에 기대는 것으로 인생을 시작했고, 아버지의 지위와 어머니의 가문이 제 계급 곁을 굳게 지킬 의무를 자신에게 지운다고 벗들 사이에서 제법 소리 높여 떠들어 왔다."], ["Nevertheless, at the age of thirty-three she had married her father's man of business, under circumstances which were not altogether creditable to her.", "그럼에도 서른셋의 나이에 그녀는 아버지의 사무 대리인과 결혼했으니, 그 정황이 그녀에게 도무지 명예로운 것은 아니었다."], ["But she had done her duty in her new sphere of life with some constancy and a fixed purpose; and now that her sister was going to marry, as she had done, a man much below herself in social standing, she was prepared to do her duty as a sister and a sister-in-law.", "그러나 그녀는 새로 놓인 처지에서 제 의무를 얼마간의 한결같음과 확고한 목적을 가지고 다해 왔다. 그리고 이제 제 동생이 저와 마찬가지로 사회적 지위가 저보다 훨씬 낮은 남자와 결혼하려 하니, 그녀는 언니로서 또 처형으로서 제 의무를 다할 채비가 되어 있었다."]], [["\"We shall be up in town in November, and of course you'll come to us at once.", "“우리는 십일월에 런던으로 올라갈 텐데, 물론 그때는 곧장 우리 집으로 와야 하오."], ["Albert Villa, you know, in Hamilton Terrace, St. John's Wood.", "세인트존스 우드, 해밀턴 테라스의 앨버트 빌라라오."], ["We dine at seven, and on Sundays at two; and you'll always find a place.", "만찬은 일곱 시고, 일요일에는 두 시요. 언제라도 자리는 있을 거요."], ["Mind you come to us, and make yourself quite at home.", "꼭 우리 집에 오시오, 그리고 제집처럼 편히 지내구려."], ["I do so hope you and Mortimer will get on well together.\"", "크로즈비 씨와 모티머가 서로 잘 지내면 얼마나 좋겠소.”"]], [["\"I'm sure we shall,\"", "“분명 그럴 겁니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["But he had had higher hopes in marrying into this noble family than that of becoming intimate with Mortimer Gazebee.", "그러나 그가 이 귀족 집안과 혼인하면서 품었던 희망은 모티머 게이지비와 가까워지는 것보다는 높은 것이었다."], ["What those hopes were he could hardly define to himself now that he had brought himself so near to the fruition of them.", "그 희망이 무엇이었는지는, 이제 그것을 손에 넣기 직전까지 스스로를 몰아 놓고 보니 그 자신도 좀처럼 규정할 수가 없었다."], ["Lady De Courcy had certainly promised to write to her first cousin who was Under-Secretary of State for India, with reference to that secretaryship at the General Committee Office; but Crosbie, when he came to weigh in his mind what good might result to him from this, was disposed to think that his chance of obtaining the promotion would be quite as good without the interest of the Under-Secretary of State for India as with it.", "드 코시 백작부인이 일반위원회 사무국의 그 사무국장 자리와 관련해 인도 담당 정무차관인 사촌에게 편지를 써 주겠다고 약속한 것은 분명했다. 그러나 크로즈비는 이 일로 자기에게 어떤 이득이 돌아올지 마음속으로 저울질해 보고는, 그 승진을 얻을 가망이란 인도 담당 정무차관의 후원이 있으나 없으나 매한가지겠다는 쪽으로 기울었다."], ["Now that he belonged, as we may say, to this noble family, he could hardly discern what were the advantages which he had expected from this alliance.", "말하자면 이제 자신이 이 귀족 집안에 속하게 되고 보니, 그는 이 결연에서 기대했던 이점이 무엇이었는지 좀처럼 분간할 수가 없었다."], ["He had said to himself that it would be much to have a countess for a mother-in-law; but now, even already, although the possession to which he had looked was not yet garnered, he was beginning to tell himself that the thing was not worth possessing.", "백작부인을 장모로 두는 것은 대단한 일이라고 그는 스스로에게 말해 왔다. 그러나 이제, 벌써부터, 눈독 들였던 그 소유물을 아직 거두어들이지도 않았는데 그는 그것이 가질 만한 물건이 못 된다고 스스로에게 말하기 시작하고 있었다."]], [["As he sat in the train, with a newspaper in his hand, he went on acknowledging to himself that he was a villain.", "기차에 앉아 신문을 손에 들고서도 그는 자기가 악당이라고 계속 자인했다."], ["Lady Julia had spoken the truth to him on the stairs at Courcy, and so he confessed over and over again.", "레이디 줄리아는 코시의 계단에서 그에게 진실을 말했던 것이고, 그렇게 그는 몇 번이고 되풀이해 고백했다."], ["But he was chiefly angry with himself for this,--that he had been a villain without gaining anything by his villany; that he had been a villain, and was to lose so much by his villany.", "그러나 그가 자신에게 가장 화가 난 것은 이것 때문이었다. 곧 악당 짓으로 얻은 것이라곤 아무것도 없이 악당이 되었다는 것, 악당이 되고서도 그 악당 짓으로 그토록 많은 것을 잃게 되었다는 것."], ["He made comparison between Lily and Alexandrina, and owned to himself, over and over again, that Lily would make the best wife that a man could take to his bosom.", "그는 릴리와 알렉산드리나를 견주어 보았고, 릴리야말로 사내가 제 품에 안을 수 있는 가장 좋은 아내가 되리라고 몇 번이고 스스로 인정했다."], ["As to Alexandrina, he knew the thinness of her character.", "알렉산드리나로 말하자면, 그는 그 사람됨이 얼마나 얄팍한지 알고 있었다."], ["She would stick by him, no doubt; and in a circuitous, discontented, unhappy way, would probably be true to her duties as a wife and mother.", "물론 그녀는 그의 곁을 지킬 것이다. 그리고 빙 둘러 가는, 불평 많고 즐겁지 않은 방식으로나마 아내와 어머니로서의 제 의무에는 아마 충실할 것이다."], ["She would be nearly such another as Lady Amelia Gazebee.", "그녀는 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비와 거의 똑같은 또 하나의 사람이 될 것이다."], ["But was that a prize sufficiently rich to make him contented with his own prowess and skill in winning it?", "그러나 그것이 제 솜씨와 수완으로 얻어 낸 것을 스스로 흡족해할 만큼 값진 상이던가?"], ["And was that a prize sufficiently rich to justify him to himself for his terrible villany?", "그리고 그것이 제 끔찍한 악당 짓을 스스로에게 정당화할 만큼 값진 상이던가?"], ["Lily Dale he had loved; and he now declared to himself that he could have continued to love her through his whole life.", "릴리 데일을 그는 사랑했다. 그리고 이제 그는 평생토록 그녀를 계속 사랑할 수 있었으리라고 스스로에게 단언했다."], ["But what was there for any man to love in Alexandrina De Courcy?", "그러나 알렉산드리나 드 코시에게는 사내가 사랑할 만한 것이 대체 무엇이 있단 말인가?"]], [["While resolving, during his first four or five days at the castle, that he would throw Lily Dale overboard, he had contrived to quiet his conscience by inward allusions to sundry heroes of romance.", "성에서 지낸 첫 나흘이나 닷새 동안 릴리 데일을 내던지기로 마음을 정하는 사이, 그는 소설 속 여러 영웅들을 마음속으로 끌어대며 제 양심을 달래 왔다."], ["He had thought of Lothario, Don Juan, and of Lovelace; and had told himself that the world had ever been full of such heroes.", "그는 로타리오와 돈 후안과 러블레이스를 떠올렸고, 세상에는 언제나 그런 영웅들이 가득했다고 스스로에게 말했다."], ["And the world, too, had treated such heroes well; not punishing them at all as villains, but caressing them rather, and calling them curled darlings.", "게다가 세상은 그런 영웅들을 잘도 대접해 왔다. 악당이라며 벌하기는커녕 오히려 어루만졌고, 곱슬머리 총아라 불러 주었다."], ["Why should not he be a curled darling as well as another?", "어찌하여 자기라고 남들처럼 곱슬머리 총아가 되지 못하겠는가?"], ["Ladies had ever been fond of the Don Juan character, and Don Juan had generally been popular with men also.", "부인들은 언제나 돈 후안 같은 인물을 좋아했고, 돈 후안은 대체로 사내들 사이에서도 인기가 있었다."], ["And then he named to himself a dozen modern Lotharios,--men who were holding their heads well above water, although it was known that they had played this lady false, and brought that other one to death's door, or perhaps even to death itself.", "그러고 나서 그는 요즘 세상의 로타리오를 열두어 명이나 손꼽아 보았다. 이 여자를 속이고 저 여자를 죽음의 문턱까지, 어쩌면 죽음 그 자체에까지 몰고 갔다는 것이 알려져 있는데도 물 위로 머리를 잘도 내밀고 다니는 사내들이었다."], ["War and love were alike, and the world was prepared to forgive any guile to militants in either camp.", "전쟁과 사랑은 매한가지였고, 세상은 어느 진영의 싸움꾼에게든 어떤 술책이라도 용서할 채비가 되어 있었다."]], [["But now that he had done the deed he found himself forced to look at it from quite another point of view.", "그러나 이제 그 일을 저지르고 나자 그는 전혀 다른 자리에서 그것을 바라보지 않을 수 없게 되었다."], ["Suddenly that character of Lothario showed itself to him in a different light, and one in which it did not please him to look at it as belonging to himself.", "문득 그 로타리오라는 인물이 다른 빛깔로 그에게 비쳤고, 그 빛깔 속에서 그것을 제 것으로 여기며 바라보는 일은 조금도 즐겁지 않았다."], ["He began to feel that it would be almost impossible for him to write that letter to Lily, which it was absolutely necessary that he should write.", "그는 릴리에게 그 편지를, 반드시 써야만 하는 그 편지를 쓴다는 것이 자기로서는 거의 불가능하리라고 느끼기 시작했다."], ["He was in a position in which his mind would almost turn itself to thoughts of self-destruction as the only means of escape.", "그는 벗어날 유일한 길로 마음이 저절로 제 목숨을 끊는 생각 쪽으로 기울 지경의 처지에 놓여 있었다."], ["A fortnight ago he was a happy man, having everything before him that a man ought to want; and now--now that he was the accepted son-in-law of an earl, and the confident expectant of high promotion--he was the most miserable, degraded wretch in the world!", "보름 전만 해도 그는 사내가 바랄 만한 것을 다 앞에 두고 있는 행복한 사람이었다. 그런데 이제—이제 백작의 사위로 받아들여지고 높은 승진을 자신 있게 기다리는 처지가 된 지금—그는 세상에서 가장 비참하고 타락한 가련한 자였다!"]], [["He changed his clothes at his lodgings in Mount Street and went down to his club to dinner.", "그는 마운트 스트리트의 하숙에서 옷을 갈아입고 클럽으로 만찬을 하러 내려갔다."], ["He could, at any rate, do nothing that night.", "어차피 그날 밤에는 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["His letter to Allington must, no doubt, be written at once; but, as he could not send it before the next night's post, he was not forced to set to work upon it that evening.", "앨링턴으로 보낼 편지는 물론 당장 써야 했다. 그러나 그것을 이튿날 밤 우편보다 먼저 부칠 수는 없었으니 그날 저녁에 굳이 붙들고 앉아야 할 까닭은 없었다."], ["As he walked along Piccadilly on his way to St.", "피커딜리를 지나 세인트제임스 스퀘어로 가는 길에 그는 이런 생각이 들었다."], ["James's Square, it occurred to him that it might be well to write a short line to Lily, telling her nothing of the truth,--a note written as though his engagement with her was still unbroken, but yet written with care, saying nothing about that engagement, so as to give him a little time.", "릴리에게 진실은 하나도 알리지 않은 짤막한 쪽지를 써 보내는 편이 좋겠다는 것이었다. 그녀와의 약혼이 아직 깨지지 않은 듯이 쓰되, 그 약혼에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않도록 조심해서 쓴 쪽지, 그리하여 자기에게 시간을 조금 벌어 줄 쪽지였다."], ["Then he thought that he would telegraph to Bernard and tell everything to him.", "그러다가 그는 버나드에게 전보를 쳐서 그에게 모든 것을 털어놓을까 하는 생각도 했다."], ["Bernard would, of course, be prepared to avenge his cousin in some way, but for such vengeance Crosbie felt that he should care little.", "버나드야 물론 어떤 식으로든 제 사촌의 원수를 갚으려 들 테지만, 그런 복수 따위는 크로즈비도 별로 마음에 두지 않을 것 같았다."], ["Lady Julia had told him that Lily was without father or brother, thereby accusing him of the basest cowardice.", "레이디 줄리아는 릴리에게 아버지도 오라비도 없다고 그에게 말했고, 그로써 그를 더없이 비열한 겁쟁이라고 몰아세웠던 것이다."], ["\"I wish she had a dozen brothers,\"", "“그 애한테 오라비가 열둘쯤 있었으면 좋았을 텐데.”"], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["But he hardly knew why he expressed such a wish.", "그러나 그가 어째서 그런 바람을 입에 올렸는지는 그 자신도 잘 알지 못했다."]], [["He returned to London on the last day of October, and he found the streets at the West End nearly deserted.", "그는 시월의 마지막 날에 런던으로 돌아왔고, 웨스트엔드의 거리들이 거의 텅 비어 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He thought, therefore, that he should be quite alone at his club, but as he entered the dinner room he saw one of his oldest and most intimate friends standing before the fire.", "그래서 그는 클럽에도 저 혼자뿐이려니 생각했는데, 만찬실로 들어서다가 가장 오래되고 가장 가까운 벗 하나가 벽난로 앞에 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Fowler Pratt was the man who had first brought him into Sebright's, and had given him almost his earliest start on his successful career in life.", "파울러 프랫은 그를 세브라이트에 처음 데려다 놓은 사람이었고, 세상에서의 성공적인 이력에 첫걸음이라 할 것을 거의 마련해 준 사람이었다."], ["Since that time he and his friend Fowler Pratt had lived in close communion, though Pratt had always held a certain ascendancy in their friendship.", "그때 이후로 그와 그의 벗 파울러 프랫은 가깝게 지내 왔다. 다만 그 우정에서 언제나 우위를 쥔 쪽은 프랫이었다."], ["He was in age a few years senior to Crosbie, and was in truth a man of better parts.", "그는 나이로 크로즈비보다 몇 살 위였고, 사실 재능도 더 나은 사람이었다."], ["But he was less ambitious, less desirous of shining in the world, and much less popular with men in general.", "그러나 그는 야심이 덜했고, 세상에서 빛나고자 하는 마음이 덜했으며, 사람들 사이에서의 인기는 훨씬 덜했다."], ["He was possessed of a moderate private fortune on which he lived in a quiet, modest manner, and was unmarried, not likely to marry, inoffensive, useless, and prudent.", "그는 얼마간의 개인 재산을 가지고 조용하고 수수하게 살았으며, 미혼이었고, 결혼할 성싶지도 않았고, 남에게 해를 끼치지 않았고, 쓸모가 없었고, 신중했다."], ["For the first few years of Crosbie's life in London he had lived very much with his friend Pratt, and had been accustomed to depend much on his friend's counsel; but latterly, since he had himself become somewhat noticeable, he had found more pleasure in the society of such men as Dale, who were not his superiors either in age or wisdom.", "런던 생활 처음 몇 해 동안 크로즈비는 벗 프랫과 아주 많이 어울렸고 벗의 조언에 많이 기대는 데 익숙했다. 그러나 요즈음에는 저 자신이 제법 눈에 띄는 사람이 되고부터 데일 같은, 나이로나 지혜로나 저보다 나을 것이 없는 사내들과 어울리는 데서 더 즐거움을 찾았다."], ["But there had been no coolness between him and Pratt, and now they met with perfect cordiality.", "그러나 그와 프랫 사이가 서먹해진 것은 아니었으므로, 이제 두 사람은 더할 나위 없이 반갑게 만났다."]], [["\"I thought you were down in Barsetshire,\"", "“자네 바셋주에 내려가 있는 줄 알았는데.”"], ["said Pratt.", "프랫이 말했다."]], [["\"And I thought you were in Switzerland.\"", "“나는 자네가 스위스에 있는 줄 알았고.”"]], [["\"I have been in Switzerland,\"", "“스위스에 있었지.”"], ["said Pratt.", "프랫이 말했다."]], [["\"And I have been in Barsetshire,\"", "“나는 바셋주에 있었고.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["Then they ordered their dinner together.", "그러고서 두 사람은 함께 만찬을 시켰다."]], [["\"And so you're going to be married?\"", "“그래서, 자네 결혼한다지?”"], ["said Pratt, when the waiter had carried away the cheese.", "급사가 치즈를 물려 간 뒤에 프랫이 말했다."]], [["\"Who told you that?\"", "“누가 그러던가?”"]], [["\"Well, but you are?", "“아니, 하지만 하기는 하는 거지?"], ["Never mind who told me, if I was told the truth.\"", "누가 말했든 무슨 상관인가, 들은 게 사실이라면.”"]], [["\"But if it be not true?\"", "“하지만 사실이 아니라면?”"]], [["\"I have heard it for the last month,\"", "“지난 한 달 내내 들어 왔네.” 프랫이 말했다."], ["said Pratt, \"and it has been spoken of as a thing certain; and it is true; is it not?\"", "“게다가 다들 확실한 일처럼 이야기하더군. 그러니 사실이지, 아닌가?”"]], [["\"I believe it is,\"", "“그런 것 같네.”"], ["said Crosbie, slowly.", "크로즈비가 느릿하게 말했다."]], [["\"Why, what on earth is the matter with you, that you speak of it in that way?", "“아니, 대체 무슨 일이 있길래 그런 투로 말하는 건가?"], ["Am I to congratulate you, or am I not?", "축하를 해 줘야 하는 건가, 아닌가?"], ["The lady, I'm told, is a cousin of Dale's.\"", "듣자니 그 아가씨가 데일의 사촌이라던데.”"]], [["Crosbie had turned his chair from the table round to the fire, and said nothing in answer to this.", "크로즈비는 식탁에서 의자를 돌려 벽난로 쪽으로 향했고, 이 말에는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["He sat with his glass of sherry in his hand, looking at the coals, and thinking whether it would not be well that he should tell the whole story to Pratt.", "그는 셰리주 잔을 손에 든 채 앉아서 석탄불을 들여다보며, 프랫에게 이야기를 전부 털어놓는 편이 낫지 않을까 생각하고 있었다."], ["No one could give him better advice; and no one, as far as he knew his friend, would be less shocked at the telling of such a story.", "그보다 더 나은 조언을 해 줄 사람은 없었다. 또 그가 제 벗을 아는 한, 그런 이야기를 들려주었을 때 그보다 덜 놀랄 사람도 없었다."], ["Pratt had no romance about women, and had never pretended to very high sentiments.", "프랫은 여자에 대해 낭만이라곤 없었고, 아주 고상한 감정을 지닌 척한 적도 없었다."]], [["\"Come up into the smoking-room and I'll tell you all about it,\"", "“흡연실로 올라가세. 죄다 이야기해 줄 테니.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["So they went off together, and, as the smoking-room was untenanted, Crosbie was able to tell his story.", "그리하여 두 사람은 함께 올라갔고, 흡연실이 비어 있었으므로 크로즈비는 제 이야기를 할 수 있었다."]], [["He found it very hard to tell;--much harder than he had beforehand fancied.", "털어놓기는 아주 어려웠다. 미리 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 어려웠다."], ["\"I have got into terrible trouble,\"", "“내가 지독한 곤경에 빠졌네.”"], ["he began by saying.", "그는 이렇게 말문을 열었다."], ["Then he told how he had fallen suddenly in love with Lily, how he had been rash and imprudent, how nice she was--\"infinitely too good for such a man as I am,\"", "그러고는 자기가 어떻게 릴리에게 느닷없이 반해 버렸는지, 자기가 얼마나 성급하고 경솔했는지, 그녀가 얼마나 좋은 사람인지—“나 같은 사내에게는 한없이 과분하지.”"], ["he said;--how she had accepted him, and then how he had repented.", "그는 말했다—그녀가 어떻게 자기를 받아 주었는지, 그러고 나서 자기가 어떻게 후회했는지를 이야기했다."], ["\"I should have told you beforehand,\"", "“미리 말해 두었어야 했는데,”"], ["he then said, \"that I was already half engaged to Lady Alexandrina De Courcy.\"", "그는 이어서 말했다. “나는 이미 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시와 반쯤 약혼한 사이였네.”"], ["The reader, however, will understand that this half-engagement was a fiction.", "그러나 독자는 이 반쯤의 약혼이 꾸며 낸 이야기라는 것을 이해할 것이다."]], [["\"And now you mean that you are altogether engaged to her?\"", "“그런데 이제는 그 여자와 아주 약혼했다는 말인가?”"]], [["\"Exactly so.\"", "“바로 그렇네.”"]], [["\"And that Miss Dale must be told that, on second thoughts, you have changed your mind?\"", "“그리고 데일 양에게는 자네가 다시 생각해 보고 마음을 바꾸었다고 알려야 한다는 말이고?”"]], [["\"I know that I have behaved very badly,\"", "“내가 아주 못되게 굴었다는 건 알고 있네.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Indeed you have,\"", "“정말 그랬지.”"], ["said his friend.", "그의 벗이 말했다."]], [["\"It is one of those troubles in which a man finds himself involved almost before he knows where he is.\"", "“사내가 저 있는 자리를 알아차리기도 전에 벌써 휘말려 버리는 그런 곤경이 있는 법 아닌가.”"]], [["\"Well; I can't look at it exactly in that light.", "“글쎄, 나는 도무지 그런 식으로는 볼 수가 없네."], ["A man may amuse himself with a girl, and I can understand his disappointing her and not offering to marry her,--though even that sort of thing isn't much to my taste.", "사내가 아가씨와 재미 삼아 어울릴 수는 있지. 그러다 그 아가씨를 실망시키고 청혼하지 않는 것도 나로선 이해할 수 있어."], ["But, by George, to make an offer of marriage to such a girl as that in September, to live for a month in her family as her affianced husband, and then coolly go away to another house in October, and make an offer to another girl of higher rank--\"", "물론 그런 짓조차 내 취향에는 그리 맞지 않네만. 하지만 이거 참, 구월에 그런 아가씨에게 청혼을 하고, 한 달을 그 집안에서 약혼한 남편으로 지내 놓고서, 그러고는 시월에 태연히 다른 집으로 건너가 신분이 더 높은 다른 아가씨에게 청혼을 한다니—”"]], [["\"You know very well that that has had nothing to do with it.\"", "“그 일과는 아무 상관이 없다는 걸 자네도 잘 알잖나.”"]], [["\"It looks very like it.", "“꼭 그런 것처럼 보이는걸."], ["And how are you going to communicate these tidings to Miss Dale?\"", "그래서 이 소식을 데일 양에게 어떻게 전할 셈인가?”"]], [["\"I don't know,\"", "“모르겠네.”"], ["said Crosbie, who was beginning to be very sore.", "크로즈비가 말했다. 그는 점점 견디기가 괴로워지고 있었다."]], [["\"And you have quite made up your mind that you'll stick to the earl's daughter?\"", "“그러면 백작의 딸 쪽을 붙들겠다고 아주 마음을 굳힌 건가?”"]], [["The idea of jilting Alexandrina instead of Lily had never as yet presented itself to Crosbie, and now, as he thought of it, he could not perceive that it was feasible.", "릴리 대신 알렉산드리나를 차 버린다는 생각은 여태 크로즈비의 머리에 떠오른 적이 없었고, 이제 그것을 생각해 보아도 그는 그것이 될 법한 일이라고는 여겨지지 않았다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“그래.” 그는 말했다."], ["he said, \"I shall marry Lady Alexandrina;--that is, if I do not cut the whole concern, and my own throat into the bargain.\"", "“나는 레이디 알렉산드리나와 결혼하겠네. 이 판을 통째로 걷어차고 덤으로 내 목까지 긋지 않는다면 말이지.”"]], [["\"If I were in your shoes I think I should cut the whole concern.", "“내가 자네 처지라면 이 판을 통째로 걷어찰 것 같네."], ["I could not stand it.", "나라면 견디지 못할 걸세."], ["What do you mean to say to Miss Dale's uncle?\"", "데일 양의 삼촌에게는 뭐라고 할 셈인가?”"]], [["\"I don't care a ---- for Miss Dale's uncle,\"", "“데일 양의 삼촌 따위는 ——만큼도 상관없네.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"If he were to walk in at that door this moment, I would tell him the whole story, without--\"", "“그 사람이 지금 이 순간 저 문으로 걸어 들어온다 해도 나는 이야기를 죄다 들려줄 걸세, 조금도—”"]], [["As he was yet speaking, one of the club servants opened the door of the smoking-room, and seeing Crosbie seated in a lounging-chair near the fire, went up to him with a gentleman's card.", "그가 아직 말하고 있는 사이에 클럽의 하인 하나가 흡연실 문을 열었고, 크로즈비가 벽난로 가까운 안락의자에 앉아 있는 것을 보고는 신사의 명함 한 장을 들고 그에게 다가갔다."], ["Crosbie took the card and read the name.", "크로즈비는 명함을 받아 이름을 읽었다."], ["\"Mr. Dale, Allington.\"", "“앨링턴, 데일 씨.”"]], [["\"The gentleman is in the waiting-room,\"", "“그 신사분은 대기실에 계십니다.”"], ["said the servant.", "하인이 말했다."]], [["Crosbie for the moment was struck dumb.", "크로즈비는 그 순간 입이 딱 막혔다."], ["He had declared that very moment that he should feel no personal disinclination to meet Mr. Dale, and now that gentleman was within the walls of the club, waiting to see him!", "바로 그 순간에 그는 데일 씨를 만나는 것이 개인적으로 조금도 꺼려지지 않는다고 큰소리를 쳤는데, 이제 그 신사가 클럽 담장 안에 들어와 그를 만나려고 기다리고 있는 것이었다!"]], [["\"Who's that?\"", "“누군가?”"], ["asked Pratt.", "프랫이 물었다."], ["And then Crosbie handed him the card.", "그러자 크로즈비가 명함을 그에게 건넸다."], ["\"Whew-w-w-hew,\"", "“휘이—이—이—유.”"], ["whistled Pratt.", "프랫이 휘파람을 불었다."]], [["\"Did you tell the gentleman I was here?\"", "“그 신사분께 내가 여기 있다고 말씀드렸나?”"], ["asked Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 물었다."]], [["\"I said I thought you were upstairs, sir.\"", "“위층에 계신 듯하다고 말씀드렸습니다, 나리.”"]], [["\"That will do,\"", "“그만하면 됐네.”"], ["said Pratt.", "프랫이 말했다."], ["\"The gentleman will no doubt wait for a minute.\"", "“그 신사분은 분명 잠시 기다려 주실 걸세.”"], ["And then the servant went out of the room.", "그러고서 하인은 방을 나갔다."], ["\"Now, Crosbie, you must make up your mind.", "“자, 크로즈비, 자네는 마음을 정해야 하네."], ["By one of these women and all her friends you will ever be regarded as a rascal, and they of course will look out to punish you with such punishment as may come to their hands.", "저 두 여자 가운데 하나와 그 여자의 벗들 모두에게 자네는 두고두고 악당으로 여겨질 테고, 그 사람들은 물론 제 손이 닿는 대로 벌을 주려고 벼를 걸세."], ["You must now choose which shall be the sufferer.\"", "이제 자네는 어느 쪽을 그 고통받는 사람으로 삼을지 골라야 하네.”"]], [["The man was a coward at heart.", "이 사내는 속으로 겁쟁이였다."], ["The reflection that he might, even now, at this moment, meet the old squire on pleasant terms,--or at any rate not on terms of defiance, pleaded more strongly in Lily's favour than had any other argument since Crosbie had first made up his mind to abandon her.", "지금 이 순간에도 늙은 지주를 좋은 낯으로, 적어도 서로 등을 돌리지 않는 낯으로 만날 수 있을지 모른다는 생각은, 크로즈비가 릴리를 저버리기로 처음 마음먹은 뒤로 나온 그 어떤 논거보다도 릴리 쪽에 강하게 힘을 실어 주었다. 그는 몸이 험한 꼴을 당할까 두려운 것이 아니었다."], ["He did not fear personal ill-usage;--he was not afraid lest he should be kicked or beaten; but he did not dare to face the just anger of the angry man.", "걷어차이거나 두들겨 맞을까 봐 겁내는 것이 아니었다. 다만 그는 그 노한 사람의 정당한 노여움을 마주 볼 엄두가 나지 않았다."]], [["\"If I were you,\"", "“내가 자네라면,”"], ["said Pratt, \"I would not go down to that man at the present moment for a trifle.\"", "프랫이 말했다. “아무리 하찮은 일이 걸렸다 해도 지금 저 사람에게 내려가지는 않겠네.”"]], [["\"But what can I do?\"", "“하지만 내가 어떻게 하면 좋겠나?”"]], [["\"Shirk away out of the club.", "“클럽에서 슬그머니 빠져나가게."], ["Only if you do that it seems to me that you'll have to go on shirking for the rest of your life.\"", "다만 그렇게 하면 자네는 남은 평생을 슬그머니 빠져나가며 살아야 할 것 같네만.”"]], [["\"Pratt, I must say that I expected something more like friendship from you.\"", "“프랫, 나는 자네에게 좀 더 우정다운 것을 기대했다고 말해야겠네.”"]], [["\"What can I do for you?", "“내가 자네를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있겠나?"], ["There are positions in which it is impossible to help a man.", "사내를 도울 길이 없는 처지라는 게 있는 법일세."], ["I tell you plainly that you have behaved very badly.", "나는 자네에게 대놓고 말하네만, 자네는 아주 못되게 굴었어."], ["I do not see that I can help you.\"", "내가 자네를 도울 수 있으리라고는 보이지 않네.”"]], [["\"Would you see him?\"", "“자네가 그 사람을 만나 주겠나?”"]], [["\"Certainly not, if I am to be expected to take your part.\"", "“자네 편을 들라는 뜻이라면, 그건 못 하겠네.”"]], [["\"Take any part you like,--only tell him the truth.\"", "“어느 편을 들든 좋으니—그저 사실을 말해 주게.”"]], [["\"And what is the truth?\"", "“그런데 사실이 무엇인가?”"]], [["\"I was part engaged to that other girl before; and then, when I came to think of it, I knew that I was not fit to marry Miss Dale.", "“나는 전부터 그 다른 아가씨와 반쯤 약혼한 사이였네. 그러다가 곰곰 생각해 보니 내가 데일 양과 결혼할 만한 사람이 못 된다는 걸 알게 된 걸세."], ["I know I have behaved badly; but, Pratt, thousands have done the same thing before.\"", "내가 못되게 굴었다는 건 알아. 하지만, 프랫, 전에도 수천 명이 똑같은 짓을 했네.”"]], [["\"I can only say that I have not been so unfortunate as to reckon any of those thousands among my friends.\"", "“나로서는 그 수천 명 가운데 하나라도 내 벗으로 꼽는 불운은 겪지 않았다고 말할 수밖에 없군.”"]], [["\"You mean to tell me, then, that you are going to turn your back on me?\"", "“그러니까 자네는 나에게 등을 돌리겠다는 말인가?”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"I haven't said anything of the kind.", "“나는 그런 말은 한 적 없네."], ["I certainly won't undertake to defend you, for I don't see that your conduct admits of defence.", "물론 자네를 변호하는 일은 맡지 않겠어. 자네가 한 짓은 변호할 여지가 있어 보이지 않으니까."], ["I will see this gentleman if you wish it, and tell him anything that you may desire me to tell him.\"", "자네가 원한다면 그 신사분을 만나서, 자네가 전해 달라는 것은 무엇이든 전해 주겠네.”"]], [["At this moment the servant returned with a note for Crosbie.", "그때 하인이 크로즈비에게 보내는 쪽지를 하나 가지고 돌아왔다."], ["Mr. Dale had called for paper and envelope, and sent up to him the following missive:--\"Do you intend to come down to me?", "데일 씨가 종이와 봉투를 달라고 하여 그에게 다음과 같은 전갈을 올려 보낸 것이었다.—“내게 내려올 생각인가?"], ["I know that you are in the house.\"", "자네가 이 안에 있다는 것은 알고 있네.”"], ["\"For heaven's sake go to him,\"", "“제발 저 사람에게 가 주게.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"He is well aware that I was deceived about his niece,--that I thought he was to give her some fortune.", "“저 사람도 내가 조카딸 일로 속았다는 걸, 그가 그 애에게 얼마쯤 재산을 줄 줄로 내가 알았다는 걸 잘 알고 있네."], ["He knows all about that, and that when I learned from him that she was to have nothing--\"", "그 일은 죄다 알고 있어. 그리고 그 애가 아무것도 받지 못한다는 걸 내가 그 사람에게서 듣고 나서—”"]], [["\"Upon my word, Crosbie, I wish you could find another messenger.\"", "“이거 참, 크로즈비, 자네가 다른 심부름꾼을 찾을 수 있으면 좋겠네.”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["you do not understand,\"", "자네는 이해하지 못하는군.”"], ["said Crosbie in his agony.", "크로즈비가 괴로움에 차서 말했다."], ["\"You think that I am inventing this plea about her fortune now.", "“자네는 내가 지금 와서 그 애 재산 이야기를 핑계로 지어낸다고 여기지."], ["It isn't so.", "그렇지 않네."], ["He will understand.", "그 사람은 알아들을 걸세."], ["We have talked all this over before, and he knew how terribly I was disappointed.", "우리는 전에 이 이야기를 다 나누었고, 그 사람은 내가 얼마나 끔찍하게 실망했는지 알고 있었네."], ["Shall I wait for you here, or will you come to my lodgings?", "여기서 자네를 기다릴까, 아니면 자네가 내 하숙으로 오겠나?"], ["Or I will go down to the Beaufort, and will wait for you there.\"", "아니면 내가 보퍼트로 내려가서 거기서 자네를 기다리겠네.”"], ["And it was finally arranged that he should get himself out of this club and wait at the other for Pratt's report of the interview.", "그리하여 마침내 그가 이 클럽에서 빠져나가 다른 클럽에서 프랫의 면담 보고를 기다리기로 정해졌다."]], [["\"Do you go down first,\"", "“자네가 먼저 내려가게.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes: I had better,\"", "“그래, 그러는 편이 낫겠지.”"], ["said Pratt.", "프랫이 말했다."], ["\"Otherwise you may be seen. Mr.", "“안 그러면 자네가 눈에 띌 테니."], ["Dale would have his eye upon you, and there would be a row in the house.\"", "데일 씨가 자네를 노려보고 있을 테고, 그러면 클럽 안에서 소동이 벌어질 걸세.”"], ["There was a smile of sarcasm on Pratt's face as he spoke which angered Crosbie even in his misery, and made him long to tell his friend that he would not trouble him with this mission,--that he would manage his own affairs himself; but he was weakened and mentally humiliated by the sense of his own rascality, and had already lost the power of asserting himself, and of maintaining his ascendancy.", "그가 이렇게 말할 때 프랫의 얼굴에는 비웃음의 미소가 떠올라 있었고, 그것이 비참한 처지에서도 크로즈비를 화나게 하여, 이런 심부름으로 벗을 성가시게 하지 않겠다고, 제 일은 제가 알아서 하겠다고 말하고 싶게 만들었다. 그러나 그는 제 악당 짓을 자각한 탓에 기운이 빠지고 마음이 꺾여 있었고, 저를 내세우고 우위를 지킬 힘은 이미 잃은 뒤였다."], ["He was beginning to recognize the fact that he had done that for which he must endure to be kicked, to be kicked morally if not materially; and that it was no longer possible for him to hold his head up without shame.", "그는 자기가 걷어차이는 것을 견뎌야 마땅한 짓을, 몸으로는 아니더라도 마음으로는 걷어차여야 마땅한 짓을 저질렀다는 것을, 그리하여 이제는 부끄러움 없이 머리를 들 수 없게 되었다는 것을 깨닫기 시작하고 있었다."]], [["Pratt took Mr. Dale's note in his hand and went down into the stranger's room.", "프랫은 데일 씨의 쪽지를 손에 들고 손님 응접실로 내려갔다."], ["There he found the squire standing, so that he could see through the open door of the room to the foot of the stairs down which Crosbie must descend before he could leave the club.", "거기서 그는 지주가 서 있는 것을 보았는데, 방문이 열려 있어 크로즈비가 클럽을 나가려면 반드시 내려와야 하는 계단 아래쪽까지 내다보이는 자리였다. 사절은 첫 번째 방책으로 문을 닫았다."], ["As a measure of first precaution the ambassador closed the door; then he bowed to Mr. Dale, and asked him if he would take a chair.", "그러고서 데일 씨에게 고개를 숙여 인사하고 자리에 앉으시겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["\"I wanted to see Mr. Crosbie,\"", "“나는 크로즈비 씨를 보러 왔소.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"I have your note to that gentleman in my hand,\"", "“그 신사분께 보내신 쪽지를 제가 들고 있습니다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"He has thought it better that you should have this interview with me;--and under all the circumstances perhaps it is better.\"", "“그는 이 면담을 저와 하시는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다. 모든 정황을 보아 아마 그편이 나을 것입니다.”"]], [["\"Is he such a coward that he dare not see me?\"", "“그자는 나를 만날 엄두도 못 낼 만큼 겁쟁이란 말이오?”"]], [["\"There are some actions, Mr. Dale, that will make a coward of any man.", "“데일 씨, 어떤 사람이라도 겁쟁이로 만들어 버리는 행실이라는 게 있습니다."], ["My friend Crosbie is, I take it, brave enough in the ordinary sense of the word, but he has injured you.\"", "제 벗 크로즈비는 흔히 말하는 뜻으로는 제법 담대한 사람이라고 봅니다만, 그는 어르신께 해를 끼쳤습니다.”"]], [["\"It is all true, then?\"", "“그러면 다 사실이란 말이오?”"]], [["\"Yes, Mr.", "“그렇습니다, 데일 씨."], ["Dale; I fear it is all true.\"", "유감이지만 다 사실입니다.”"]], [["\"And you call that man your friend!", "“그런데 당신은 그런 자를 벗이라 부르는구려!"], ["Mr.--; I don't know what your name is.\"", "그—, 성함이 어떻게 되는지 모르겠소만.”"]], [["\"Pratt;--Fowler Pratt.", "“프랫입니다."], ["I have known Crosbie for fourteen years,--ever since he was a boy; and it is not my way, Mr.", "파울러 프랫입니다. 저는 크로즈비를 열네 해째, 그가 소년이던 시절부터 알아 왔습니다."], ["Dale, to throw over an old friend under any circumstances.\"", "그리고 데일 씨, 어떤 정황에서든 오랜 벗을 내던지는 것은 제 방식이 아닙니다.”"]], [["\"Not if he committed a murder.\"", "“살인을 저질렀더라도 말이오?”"]], [["\"No; not though he committed a murder.\"", "“예. 살인을 저질렀더라도 그렇습니다.”"]], [["\"If what I hear is true, this man is worse than a murderer.\"", "“내가 들은 것이 사실이라면, 이 사내는 살인자보다 더 나쁘오.”"]], [["\"Of course, Mr.", "“물론 데일 씨께서 무엇을 들으셨는지는 제가 알 수 없습니다."], ["Dale, I cannot know what you have heard. I believe that Mr.", "저는 크로즈비 씨가 어르신의 조카딸 되시는 데일 양에게 아주 못되게 굴었다고 봅니다."], ["Crosbie has behaved very badly to your niece, Miss Dale; I believe that he was engaged to marry her, or, at any rate, that some such proposition had been made.\"", "저는 그가 그분과 결혼하기로 약혼했다고, 아니 적어도 그런 제의 비슷한 것이 오갔다고 봅니다.”"]], [["\"Proposition!", "“제의라니!"], ["Why, sir, it was a thing so completely understood that everybody knew it in the county.", "여보시오, 그건 온 고을 사람이 다 알 만큼 완전히 통해 있던 일이오."], ["It was so positively fixed that there was no secret about it.", "어찌나 확실하게 정해진 일이었던지 감출 것도 없었소."], ["Upon my honour, Mr. Pratt, I can't as yet understand it.", "명예를 걸고 말하는데, 프랫 씨, 나는 아직까지도 이 일을 이해할 수가 없소."], ["If I remember right, it's not a fortnight since he left my house at Allington,--not a fortnight.", "내 기억이 맞다면, 그가 앨링턴의 내 집을 떠난 지 보름도 되지 않았소—보름도 되지 않았단 말이오."], ["And that poor girl was with him on the morning of his going as his betrothed bride.", "그리고 그 가엾은 아이는 그가 떠나던 날 아침에 약혼한 신부로서 그와 함께 있었소."], ["Not a fortnight since!", "보름도 되지 않았소!"], ["And now I've had a letter from an old family friend telling me that he is going to marry one of Lord De Courcy's daughters!", "그런데 이제 집안의 오랜 벗한테서 편지를 받아 보니 그가 드 코시 백작의 딸들 가운데 하나와 결혼한다지 않소!"], ["I went instantly off to Courcy, and found that he had started for London.", "나는 곧장 코시로 달려갔고, 그가 런던으로 떠났다는 것을 알았소."], ["Now, I have followed him here; and you tell me it's all true.\"", "그래서 여기까지 그를 쫓아왔는데, 당신은 그게 다 사실이라고 하는구려.”"]], [["\"I am afraid it is, Mr.", "“유감이지만 사실입니다, 데일 씨."], ["Dale; too true.\"", "너무나 사실입니다.”"]], [["\"I don't understand it; I don't, indeed.", "“나는 이해할 수 없소. 정말이지 이해할 수 없소."], ["I cannot bring myself to believe that the man who was sitting the other day at my table should be so great a scoundrel.", "얼마 전까지 내 식탁에 앉아 있던 사람이 그토록 큰 악당일 수 있다는 것을 나는 도무지 믿을 수가 없소."], ["Did he mean it all the time that he was there?\"", "그는 거기 있는 내내 이럴 작정이었던 거요?”"]], [["\"No; certainly not.", "“아닙니다. 분명 그렇지 않습니다."], ["Lady Alexandrina De Courcy was, I believe, an old friend of his;--with whom, perhaps, he had had some lover's quarrel.", "레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시는 제가 알기로 그의 오랜 벗이었습니다. 어쩌면 그 사이에 연인들끼리의 다툼 같은 것이 있었겠지요."], ["On his going to Courcy they made it up; and this is the result.\"", "코시로 가서 두 사람이 그것을 풀었고, 그래서 이런 결과가 된 것입니다.”"]], [["\"And that is to be sufficient for my poor girl?\"", "“그러면 그것으로 내 가엾은 아이한테는 충분하다는 거요?”"]], [["\"You will, of course, understand that I am not defending Mr. Crosbie.", "“물론 제가 크로즈비 씨를 변호하는 것이 아니라는 점은 이해해 주시겠지요."], ["The whole affair is very sad,--very sad, indeed.", "이 일은 전부 아주 딱한 일입니다. 참으로 딱한 일이지요."], ["I can only say, in his excuse, that he is not the first man who has behaved badly to a lady.\"", "그를 위해 변명 삼아 드릴 수 있는 말이라고는, 숙녀에게 못되게 군 사내가 그가 처음은 아니라는 것뿐입니다.”"]], [["\"And that is his message to me, is it?", "“그러니까 그것이 나에게 보내는 그자의 전갈이란 말이오?"], ["And that is what I am to tell my niece?", "그리고 그것이 내가 조카딸한테 전해야 할 말이고?"], ["You have been deceived by a scoundrel.", "너는 악당한테 속았다. 하지만 그게 어쨌다는 거냐?"], ["But what then? You are not the first!", "네가 처음은 아니다!"], ["Mr. Pratt, I give you my word as a gentleman, I do not understand it.", "프랫 씨, 나는 신사로서 내 말을 걸고 말하는데, 나는 이 일을 이해할 수가 없소."], ["I have lived a good deal out of the world, and am, therefore, perhaps, more astonished than I ought to be.\"", "나는 세상 물정과 꽤 동떨어져 살아왔으니, 아마 그래서 마땅한 것보다 더 놀라는 것인지도 모르겠소.”"]], [["\"Mr. Dale, I feel for you--\"", "“데일 씨, 저도 안타깝게—”"]], [["\"Feel for me!", "“안타깝다니!"], ["What is to become of my girl?", "내 아이는 어찌 되란 말이오?"], ["And do you suppose that I will let this other marriage go on; that I will not tell the De Courcys, and all the world at large, what sort of a man this is;--that I will not get at him to punish him?", "그리고 당신은 내가 이 다른 결혼을 그냥 두고 볼 성싶소? 내가 드 코시 집안에도, 온 세상에도 이 사내가 어떤 자인지 알리지 않을 성싶소?"], ["Does he think that I will put up with this?\"", "내가 그를 붙들어 벌하지 않을 성싶소? 그자는 내가 이 일을 참고 넘기리라 여기는 거요?”"]], [["\"I do not know what he thinks; I must only beg that you will not mix me up in the matter--as though I were a participator in his offence.\"", "“그가 무슨 생각인지는 저도 모릅니다. 다만 제가 마치 그의 잘못에 한몫 낀 사람인 양 이 일에 저를 끌어넣지는 말아 주시기를 부탁드릴 뿐입니다.”"]], [["\"Will you tell him from me that I desire to see him?\"", "“내가 그를 만나고 싶어 한다고 내 말을 그에게 전해 주겠소?”"]], [["\"I do not think that that would do any good.\"", "“그것이 무슨 도움이 되리라고는 생각지 않습니다.”"]], [["\"Never mind, sir; you have brought me his message; will you have the goodness now to take back mine to him?\"", "“상관없소. 당신은 나에게 그의 전갈을 가져왔소. 이제 내 전갈도 그에게 가져다주는 수고를 해 주겠소?”"]], [["\"Do you mean at once--this evening,--now?\"", "“지금 당장, 오늘 저녁에, 지금 말씀입니까?”"]], [["\"Yes, at once--this evening,--now;--this minute.\"", "“그렇소, 당장, 오늘 저녁에, 지금—바로 이 순간에 말이오.”"]], [["\"Ah; he has left the club; he is not here now; he went when I came to you.\"", "“아, 그는 클럽을 떠났습니다. 지금은 여기에 없습니다. 제가 어르신께 오는 사이에 갔습니다.”"]], [["\"Then he is a coward as well as a scoundrel.\"", "“그렇다면 그는 악당인 데다 겁쟁이이기도 하구려.”"], ["In answer to which assertion, Mr. Fowler Pratt merely shrugged his shoulders.", "이 단언에 대해 파울러 프랫 씨는 그저 어깨를 으쓱해 보였을 뿐이었다."]], [["\"He is a coward as well as a scoundrel.", "“그는 악당인 데다 겁쟁이이기도 하오."], ["Will you have the kindness to tell your friend from me that he is a coward and a scoundrel,--and a liar, sir.\"", "당신 벗에게 내 말을 전해 주는 친절을 베풀어 주겠소? 그가 겁쟁이이고 악당이며—또한 거짓말쟁이라고 말이오.”"]], [["\"If it be so, Miss Dale is well quit of her engagement.\"", "“그렇다면 데일 양은 그 약혼에서 벗어난 것이 다행입니다.”"]], [["\"That is your consolation, is it?", "“그게 당신의 위로란 말이오?"], ["That may be all very well now-a-days; but when I was a young man, I would sooner have burnt out my tongue than have spoken in such a way on such a subject.", "요즘 세상에는 그런 말이 통할지도 모르겠소만, 내가 젊었을 적에는 그런 문제를 두고 그런 식으로 말하느니 차라리 내 혀를 태워 버렸을 거요."], ["I would, indeed.", "정말 그랬을 거요."], ["Good-night, Mr. Pratt.", "안녕히 계시오, 프랫 씨."], ["Pray make your friend understand that he has not yet seen the last of the Dales; although, as you hint, the ladies of that family will no doubt have learned that he is not fit to associate with them.\"", "부디 당신 벗에게, 그가 데일 집안 사람들을 아직 다 본 것이 아니라는 걸 일러 주시오. 하기야 당신이 넌지시 말한 대로, 그 집안 여자들이야 그가 저희와 어울릴 만한 사람이 못 된다는 걸 이제 알게 되었겠지만 말이오.”"], ["Then, taking up his hat, the squire made his way out of the club.", "그러고서 지주는 모자를 집어 들고 클럽 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["\"I would not have done it,\"", "“나라면 그런 짓은 하지 않았을 텐데.”"], ["said Pratt to himself, \"for all the beauty, and all the wealth, and all the rank that ever were owned by a woman.\"", "프랫이 혼자 중얼거렸다. “여자가 지녔던 그 어떤 아름다움과, 그 어떤 재물과, 그 어떤 지위를 다 준다 해도.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXVI. LORD DE COURCY IN THE BOSOM OF HIS FAMILY.", "# 제26장. 제 가족 품에 안긴 드 코시 백작"]], [["Lady Julia De Guest had not during her life written many letters to Mr. Dale of Allington, nor had she ever been very fond of him.", "레이디 줄리아 드 게스트는 평생 앨링턴의 데일 씨에게 편지를 그리 많이 쓴 적이 없었고, 그를 그리 좋아한 적도 없었다."], ["But when she felt certain how things were going at Courcy, or rather, as we may say, how they had already gone, she took pen in hand, and sat herself to work, doing, as she conceived, her duty by her neighbour.", "그러나 코시에서 일이 어떻게 돌아가고 있는지—아니, 우리로서는 이렇게 말해도 좋겠으니, 일이 이미 어떻게 되어 버렸는지—를 확실히 알게 되자, 그녀는 펜을 손에 들고 자리에 앉아, 스스로 생각하기에 이웃에 대한 제 도리를 다하는 일에 착수했다."]], [["MY DEAR MR. DALE [she said],", "친애하는 데일 씨께 [그녀는 이렇게 썼다],"]], [["I believe I need make no secret of having known that your niece Lilian is engaged to Mr. Crosbie, of London.", "댁의 조카 릴리언 양이 런던의 크로즈비 씨와 약혼했다는 것을 제가 알고 있었다는 사실은 굳이 숨길 것이 없다고 봅니다."], ["I think it proper to warn you that if this be true Mr. Crosbie is behaving himself in a very improper manner here.", "만일 그것이 사실이라면, 크로즈비 씨가 이곳에서 매우 온당치 못한 처신을 하고 있음을 알려 드리는 것이 마땅하다고 생각합니다."], ["I am not a person who concerns myself much in the affairs of other people; and under ordinary circumstances, the conduct of Mr.", "저는 남의 일에 그리 참견하는 사람이 아니며, 여느 때 같으면 크로즈비 씨의 행실 따위는 저와 아무 상관도 없을 것입니다—아니, 실로 아무 상관도 없는 것만도 못할 것입니다."], ["Crosbie would be nothing to me,--or, indeed, less than nothing; but I do to you as I would wish that others should do unto me.", "그러나 저는 남들이 저에게 해 주었으면 하는 대로 댁에게 하는 것입니다."], ["I believe it is only too true that Mr. Crosbie has proposed to Lady Alexandrina De Courcy, and been accepted by her.", "크로즈비 씨가 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시에게 청혼했고 그녀가 그것을 받아들였다는 것은 너무도 틀림없는 사실이라고 저는 믿습니다."], ["I think you will believe that I would not say this without warrant, and if there be anything in it, it may be well, for the poor young lady's sake, that you should put yourself in the way of learning the truth.", "제가 근거 없이 이런 말을 할 사람이 아님은 댁도 믿어 주시리라 생각합니다. 그리고 만일 이 일에 조금이라도 사실이 있다면, 그 가엾은 아가씨를 위해서라도 댁이 몸소 나서서 진실을 알아보시는 편이 좋을 것입니다."]], [["Believe me to be yours sincerely,", "진심을 담은 그대의 벗임을 믿어 주시기 바라며,"]], [["Courcy Castle, Thursday.", "코시 성, 목요일."]], [["The squire had never been very fond of any of the De Guest family, and had, perhaps, liked Lady Julia the least of them all.", "지주는 드 게스트 집안의 누구도 그리 좋아한 적이 없었고, 그중에서도 레이디 줄리아를 아마 가장 덜 좋아했을 것이다."], ["He was wont to call her a meddling old woman,--remembering her bitterness and pride in those now long bygone days in which the gallant major had run off with Lady Fanny.", "그는 그녀를 참견 좋아하는 늙은 여자라 부르곤 했으니, 이제는 아득히 지나간 그 시절, 저 늠름한 소령이 레이디 패니를 데리고 달아났을 때 그녀가 보였던 독설과 오만을 잊지 않고 있었던 것이다."], ["When he first received this letter, he did not, on the first reading of it, believe a word of its contents.", "이 편지를 처음 받아 들었을 때 그는 한 번 읽고 나서도 그 내용을 한마디도 믿지 않았다."], ["\"Cross-grained old harridan,\"", "“심술궂은 늙은 마귀할멈 같으니라고.”"], ["he said out loud to his nephew.", "그는 조카에게 소리 내어 말했다."], ["\"Look what that aunt of yours has written to me.\"", "“네 이모라는 사람이 나한테 뭐라고 써 보냈는지 좀 봐라.”"], ["Bernard read the letter twice, and as he did so his face became hard and angry.", "버나드는 그 편지를 두 번 읽었고, 읽어 가는 동안 그의 얼굴은 굳어지며 성난 빛을 띠었다."]], [["\"You don't mean to say you believe it?\"", "“설마 저 말을 믿는다는 건 아니겠지?”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't think it will be safe to disregard it.\"", "“무시해도 괜찮을 일은 아닌 듯합니다.”"]], [["\"What!", "“뭐라고!"], ["you think it possible that your friend is doing as she says?\"", "네 친구가 저 여자 말대로 하고 있을 수도 있다고 보는 게냐?”"]], [["\"It is certainly possible.", "“그럴 가능성은 분명히 있습니다."], ["He was angry when he found that Lily had no fortune.\"", "릴리에게 재산이 한 푼도 없다는 걸 알았을 때 그 사람은 화를 냈으니까요.”"]], [["\"Heavens, Bernard!", "“세상에, 버나드!"], ["And you can speak of it in that way?\"", "그런 일을 그런 투로 말할 수가 있느냐?”"]], [["\"I don't say that it is true; but I think we should look to it.", "“사실이라는 말씀은 아닙니다. 다만 살펴보아야 한다는 것입니다."], ["I will go to Courcy Castle and learn the truth.\"", "제가 코시 성에 가서 진상을 알아보겠습니다.”"]], [["The squire at last decided that he would go.", "지주는 마침내 제가 가겠다고 마음을 정했다."], ["He went to Courcy Castle, and found that Crosbie had started two hours before his arrival.", "그는 코시 성으로 갔고, 크로즈비가 제 도착보다 두 시간 앞서 떠났다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["He asked for Lady Julia, and learned from her that Crosbie had actually left the house as the betrothed husband of Lady Alexandrina.", "그는 레이디 줄리아를 찾았고, 크로즈비가 실제로 레이디 알렉산드리나의 약혼한 남편으로서 그 집을 떠났다는 것을 그녀에게서 들었다."]], [["\"The countess, I am sure, will not contradict it, if you will see her,\"", "“백작부인을 만나 보시면, 그분도 분명 이 말을 부인하지는 못할 겁니다.”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."], ["But this the squire was unwilling to do.", "그러나 지주는 그러고 싶지 않았다."], ["He would not proclaim the wretched condition of his niece more loudly than was necessary, and therefore he started on his pursuit of Crosbie.", "그는 조카딸의 비참한 처지를 필요 이상으로 요란하게 떠벌리고 싶지 않았고, 그리하여 크로즈비를 뒤쫓아 나섰다."], ["What was his success on that evening we have already learned.", "그날 저녁 그 추적이 어떤 성과를 거두었는지는 우리가 이미 본 대로다."]], [["Both Lady Alexandrina and her mother heard of Mr. Dale's arrival at the castle, but nothing was said between them on the subject.", "레이디 알렉산드리나도 그 어머니도 데일 씨가 성에 왔다는 소식을 들었으나, 두 사람 사이에 그 일에 관한 이야기는 한마디도 오가지 않았다."], ["Lady Amelia Gazebee heard of it also, and she ventured to discuss the matter with her sister.", "레이디 어밀리아 게이지비도 그 소식을 들었고, 그녀는 큰맘 먹고 동생과 그 문제를 이야기해 보았다."]], [["\"You don't know exactly how far it went, do you?\"", "“일이 정확히 어디까지 갔는지는 모르지 않소?”"]], [["\"No; yes;--not exactly, that is,\"", "“몰라요. 아니, 알아요—그러니까, 정확히는 아니고요.”"], ["said Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."]], [["\"I suppose he did say something about marriage to the girl?\"", "“그이가 그 아가씨한테 혼인 이야기 비슷한 것은 했겠구려?”"]], [["\"Yes, I'm afraid he did.\"", "“네, 아마 그랬을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Dear, dear!", "“저런, 저런!"], ["It's very unfortunate.", "참으로 딱한 일이오."], ["What sort of people are those Dales?", "그 데일 집안은 어떤 사람들이오?"], ["I suppose he talked to you about them.\"", "그이가 그 집안 이야기를 했을 것 아니오.”"]], [["\"No, he didn't; not very much.", "“아니요, 하지 않았어요. 그리 많이는요."], ["I daresay she is an artful, sly thing!", "아마 교활하고 능청스러운 계집애일 거예요."], ["It's a great pity men should go on in such a way.\"", "사내들이 그런 식으로 굴다니 참 딱한 노릇이지요.”"]], [["\"Yes, it is,\" said Lady Amelia.", "“그래요, 딱한 노릇이오.”"], ["\"And I do suppose that in this case the blame has been more with him than with her.", "레이디 어밀리아가 말했다. “그리고 이번 일은 그 아가씨보다 그이 쪽에 허물이 더 많다고 나는 생각하오."], ["It's only right I should tell you that.\"", "그 말만은 내가 해 두는 것이 옳겠지.”"]], [["\"But what can I do?\"", "“하지만 제가 어쩌란 말이에요?”"]], [["\"I don't say you can do anything; but it's as well you should know.\"", "“무얼 할 수 있다는 말이 아니오. 다만 알고는 있는 편이 좋다는 것이오.”"]], [["\"But I don't know, and you don't know; and I can't see that there is any use talking about it now.", "“하지만 저도 모르고 언니도 모르잖아요. 이제 와서 그 이야기를 해 봐야 무슨 소용이 있는지 모르겠어요."], ["I knew him a long while before she did, and if she has allowed him to make a fool of her, it isn't my fault.\"", "저는 그 아가씨보다 훨씬 전부터 그이를 알았어요. 그 아가씨가 그이한테 놀아난 것이라면 그건 제 잘못이 아니에요.”"]], [["\"Nobody says it is, my dear.\"", "“아무도 그렇다고는 하지 않소, 앨리.”"]], [["\"But you seem to preach to me about it.", "“그런데 언니는 그 일로 저한테 설교를 하시는 것 같잖아요."], ["What can I do for the girl?", "제가 그 아가씨한테 무엇을 해 줄 수 있어요?"], ["The fact is, he don't care for her a bit, and never did.\"", "사실 그이는 그 아가씨를 손톱만큼도 마음에 두지 않아요. 처음부터 그랬고요.”"]], [["\"Then he shouldn't have told her that he did.\"", "“그렇다면 마음에 둔다고 그 아가씨한테 말하지는 말았어야 하오.”"]], [["\"That's all very well, Amelia; but people don't always do exactly all that they ought to do.", "“그거야 다 옳은 말이에요, 언니. 하지만 사람이 늘 마땅히 해야 할 일만 딱 그대로 하고 사는 건 아니잖아요."], ["I suppose Mr. Crosbie isn't the first man that has proposed to two ladies.", "크로즈비 씨가 두 아가씨한테 청혼한 첫 번째 남자도 아닐 테고요."], ["I dare say it was wrong, but I can't help it.", "잘못된 일이라는 건 저도 알아요. 그렇지만 저로서는 어쩔 수가 없어요."], ["As to Mr. Dale coming here with a tale of his niece's wrongs, I think it very absurd,--very absurd indeed.", "데일 씨가 제 조카딸이 억울한 일을 당했다는 이야기를 들고 여기까지 온 것으로 말하자면, 저는 그게 아주 우스꽝스러운 짓이라고 생각해요—정말이지 아주 우스꽝스러워요."], ["It makes it look as though there had been a scheme to catch Mr. Crosbie, and it's my belief that there was such a scheme.\"", "그건 마치 크로즈비 씨를 낚으려는 계략이 있었던 것처럼 보이게 하는데, 저는 정말 그런 계략이 있었다고 믿어요.”"]], [["\"I only hope that there'll be no quarrel.\"", "“나는 그저 싸움이나 나지 않기를 바랄 뿐이오.”"]], [["\"Men don't fight duels now-a-days, Amelia.\"", "“요새는 사내들이 결투 같은 건 하지 않아요, 언니.”"]], [["\"But do you remember what Frank Gresham did to Mr. Moffat when he behaved so badly to poor Augusta?\"", "“하지만 모팻 씨가 가엾은 오거스타에게 그렇게 못되게 굴었을 때 프랭크 그레셤이 그이한테 어떻게 했는지 기억하지 않소?”"]], [["\"Mr. Crosbie isn't afraid of that kind of thing.", "“크로즈비 씨는 그런 것쯤 두려워하지 않아요."], ["And I always thought that Frank was very wrong,--very wrong indeed.", "그리고 저는 늘 프랭크가 아주 잘못했다고 생각했어요—정말이지 아주 잘못했지요."], ["What's the good of two men beating each other in the street?\"", "사내 둘이 길거리에서 서로 때린다고 무슨 좋은 일이 있어요?”"]], [["\"Well; I'm sure I hope there'll be no quarrel.", "“글쎄, 나야 싸움이 나지 않기를 진심으로 바라오."], ["But I own I don't like the look of it.", "그렇지만 솔직히 말해 나는 이 일의 모양새가 마음에 들지 않소."], ["You see the uncle must have known all about it, and have consented to the marriage, or he would not have come here.\"", "보시오, 그 삼촌이라는 사람은 죄다 알고 있었고 그 혼인에 동의했던 것이 분명하오. 그렇지 않았다면 여기까지 오지도 않았을 테니까.”"]], [["\"I don't see that it can make any difference to me, Amelia.\"", "“그게 저한테 무슨 상관이 있는지 모르겠어요, 언니.”"]], [["\"No, my dear, I don't see that it can.", "“그렇겠지, 앨리. 나도 상관이 있으리라고는 생각지 않소."], ["We shall be up in town soon, and I will see as much as possible of Mr. Crosbie.", "우리는 곧 런던으로 올라갈 테고, 나도 크로즈비 씨를 되도록 자주 볼 작정이오."], ["The marriage, I hope, will take place soon.\"", "혼례가 어서 치러지면 좋겠구려.”"]], [["\"He talks of February.\"", "“그이는 2월이라고 해요.”"]], [["\"Don't put it off, Alley, whatever you do.", "“무슨 일이 있어도 미루지는 마오, 앨리."], ["There are so many slips, you know, in these things.\"", "알다시피 이런 일에는 어그러질 구석이 참 많으니까.”"]], [["\"I'm not a bit afraid of that,\"", "“저는 그런 건 조금도 겁나지 않아요.”"], ["said Alexandrina, sticking up her head.", "알렉산드리나가 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들며 말했다."]], [["\"I daresay not; and you may be sure that we will keep an eye on him.", "“그렇겠지. 그리고 우리가 그이를 눈여겨보고 있으리라는 것만은 믿어도 좋소."], ["Mortimer will get him up to dine with us as often as possible, and as his leave of absence is all over, he can't get out of town.", "모티머가 되도록 자주 그이를 불러 우리와 저녁을 들게 할 것이고, 휴가도 다 써 버렸으니 그이는 런던을 벗어나지도 못할 거요."], ["He's to be here at Christmas, isn't he?\"", "크리스마스에 여기 오기로 되어 있지 않소?”"]], [["\"Of course he is.\"", "“물론이지요.”"]], [["\"Mind you keep him to that.", "“꼭 그러도록 붙들어 두어요."], ["And as to these Dales, I would be very careful, if I were you, not to say anything unkind of them to any one.", "그리고 그 데일 집안 사람들 이야기인데, 내가 앨리라면 누구한테도 그이들을 두고 모진 말은 하지 않도록 아주 조심하겠소."], ["It sounds badly in your position.\"", "지금 앨리 처지에서는 남 듣기에 좋지 않으니까.”"], ["And with this last piece of advice Lady Amelia Gazebee allowed the subject to drop.", "그리고 이 마지막 충고를 끝으로 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비는 그 이야기가 흐지부지되도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["On that day Lady Julia returned to her own home.", "그날 레이디 줄리아는 제 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["Her adieux to the whole family at Courcy Castle were very cold, but about Mr. Crosbie and his lady-love at Allington she said no further word to any of them.", "코시 성의 온 식구에게 건넨 작별 인사는 몹시 쌀쌀했으나, 크로즈비 씨와 앨링턴에 있는 그의 연인에 대해서는 누구에게도 더 이상 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["Alexandrina did not show herself at all on the occasion, and indeed had not spoken to her enemy since that evening on which she had felt herself constrained to retreat from the drawing-room.", "알렉산드리나는 그 자리에 아예 얼굴을 내밀지 않았고, 실로 응접실에서 물러나지 않을 수 없다고 느꼈던 그날 저녁 이후로 제 원수와 말을 섞은 적이 없었다."]], [["\"Good-by,\"", "“안녕히 가세요.”"], ["said the countess.", "백작부인이 말했다."], ["\"You have been so good to come, and we have enjoyed it so much.\"", "“와 주셔서 얼마나 좋았는지 몰라요. 저희도 아주 즐거웠답니다.”"]], [["\"I thank you very much.", "“대단히 고맙습니다."], ["Good morning,\"", "안녕히 계십시오.”"], ["said Lady Julia, with a stately courtesy.", "레이디 줄리아가 위엄 있게 몸을 숙여 인사하며 말했다."]], [["\"Pray remember me to your brother.", "“오라버님께 안부 전해 주세요."], ["I wish we could have seen him; I hope he has not been hurt by the--the bull.\"", "그분도 뵈었더라면 좋았을 텐데요. 그—그 황소 때문에 다치신 데는 없기를 바라요.”"], ["And then Lady Julia went her way.", "그러고서 레이디 줄리아는 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["\"What a fool I have been to have that woman in the house,\"", "“저런 여자를 집에 들이다니 내가 어리석었지.”"], ["said the countess, before the door was closed behind her guest's back.", "손님의 등 뒤로 문이 채 닫히기도 전에 백작부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Indeed you have,\"", "“그렇고말고요.”"], ["said Lady Julia, screaming back through the passage.", "레이디 줄리아가 복도 저편에서 소리쳐 되받았다."], ["Then there was a long silence, then a suppressed titter, and after that a loud laugh.", "그러고는 긴 침묵이 흘렀고, 이어 참다 못한 킥킥거림이, 그다음에는 요란한 웃음소리가 터졌다."]], [["\"Oh, mamma, what shall we do?\"", "“어머, 어머니, 이를 어째요?”"], ["said Lady Amelia.", "레이디 어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Do!\"", "“어쩌긴!”"], ["said Margaretta; \"why should we do anything?", "마거리타가 말했다. “우리가 뭘 어쩐단 말이에요?"], ["She has heard the truth for once in her life.\"", "저 사람이 평생 처음으로 진실을 들은 것뿐인데.”"]], [["\"Dear Lady Dumbello, what will you think of us?\"", "“어쩌면 좋아요, 레이디 덤벨로."], ["said the countess, turning round to another guest, who was also just about to depart.", "저희를 어떻게 보시겠어요?” 백작부인이 마침 떠나려던 또 다른 손님 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["\"Did any one ever know such a woman before?\"", "“저런 여자를 여태 본 사람이 있을까요?”"]], [["\"I think she's very nice,\"", "“아주 점잖은 분 같던데요.”"], ["said Lady Dumbello, smiling.", "레이디 덤벨로가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"I can't quite agree with you there,\"", "“그 점에서는 저는 좀 동의하기 어렵네요.”"], ["said Lady Clandidlem.", "레이디 클랜디들럼이 말했다."], ["\"But I do believe she means to do her best.", "“하지만 저 사람이 나름대로 최선을 다하려 한다는 것만은 믿어요."], ["She is very charitable, and all that sort of thing.\"", "자선심도 많고, 뭐 그런 것들 말이지요.”"]], [["\"I'm sure I don't know,\" said Rosina.", "“글쎄, 저는 잘 모르겠어요.”"], ["\"I asked her for a subscription to the mission for putting down the Papists in the west of Ireland, and she refused me point-blank.\"", "로지나가 말했다. “아일랜드 서부의 교황파를 눌러 없애자는 선교회에 낼 기부금을 청했더니 저한테 딱 잘라 거절하던걸요.”"]], [["\"Now, my dear, if you're quite ready,\"", "“자, 여보, 준비가 다 되었으면.”"], ["said Lord Dumbello, coming into the room.", "덤벨로 경이 방으로 들어서며 말했다. 그리하여 또 한 무리가 떠나갔다."], ["Then there was another departure; but on this occasion the countess waited till the doors were shut, and the retreating footsteps were no longer heard.", "그러나 이번에는 백작부인도 문이 다 닫히고 물러가는 발소리가 더는 들리지 않을 때까지 기다렸다. “혹시 눈치채셨어요,”"], ["\"Have you observed,\"", "그녀가 레이디 클랜디들럼에게 말했다."], ["said she to Lady Clandidlem, \"that she has not held her head up since Mr. Palliser went away?\"", "“저 부인이 팰리서 씨가 떠난 뒤로 도무지 고개를 들지 못한다는 걸?”"]], [["\"Indeed I have,\"", "“암요, 눈치채고말고요.”"], ["said Lady Clandidlem.", "레이디 클랜디들럼이 말했다."], ["\"As for poor Dumbello, he's the blindest creature I ever saw in my life.\"", "“가엾은 덤벨로로 말하자면, 내 평생 본 가운데 가장 눈먼 사람이지요.”"]], [["\"We shall hear of something before next May,\"", "“내년 오월이 오기 전에 무슨 소식이 들릴 거예요.”"], ["said Lady De Courcy, shaking her head; \"but for all that she'll never be Duchess of Omnium.\"", "레이디 드 코시가 고개를 저으며 말했다. “하지만 그 모든 일에도 저 부인은 결코 옴니엄 공작부인은 되지 못할 거예요.”"]], [["\"I wonder what your mamma will say of me when I go away to-morrow,\"", "“내일 내가 떠나고 나면 댁의 어머님은 나를 두고 뭐라고 하실까요.”"], ["said Lady Clandidlem to Margaretta, as they walked across the hall together.", "레이디 클랜디들럼이 마거리타와 함께 홀을 가로질러 걸으며 말했다."]], [["\"She won't say that you are going to run away with any gentleman,\"", "“어느 신사분과 달아날 참이라는 말씀만은 안 하실 거예요.”"], ["said Margaretta.", "마거리타가 말했다."]], [["\"At any rate not with the earl,\"", "“어쨌든 백작님과는 아니겠지요.”"], ["said Lady Clandidlem.", "레이디 클랜디들럼이 말했다."], ["\"Ha, ha, ha!", "“하하하!"], ["Well, we are all very good-natured, are we not?", "그래요, 우리 모두 참으로 마음씨가 곱지 않나요?"], ["The best is that it means nothing.\"", "무엇보다 좋은 건 그게 다 아무 뜻도 없다는 거고요.”"]], [["Thus by degrees all the guests went, and the family of the De Courcys was left to the bliss of their own domestic circle.", "그리하여 손님들은 차츰 다 떠나고, 드 코시 집안사람들은 저희끼리의 단란한 가정의 행복 속에 남겨졌다."], ["This, we may presume, was not without its charms, seeing that there were so many feelings in common between the mother and her children.", "어머니와 자식들 사이에 통하는 마음이 그토록 많았던 것을 보면, 이 행복에 나름의 매력이 없지는 않았으리라고 우리는 짐작해도 좋겠다."], ["There were drawbacks to it, no doubt, arising perhaps chiefly from the earl's bodily infirmities.", "물론 흠도 있었으니, 그것은 아마도 주로 백작의 병약한 몸에서 비롯한 것이었다."], ["\"When your father speaks to me,\" said Mrs.", "“아버님이 저한테 말을 거시면요,”"], ["George to her husband, \"he puts me in such a shiver that I cannot open my mouth to answer him.\"", "조지 부인이 남편에게 말했다. “어찌나 몸이 떨리는지 대답하려고 입을 열 수조차 없어요.”"]], [["\"You should stand up to him,\"", "“당신이 맞서야 하오.”"], ["said George.", "조지가 말했다."], ["\"He can't hurt you, you know.", "“아버지는 당신을 어쩌지 못하오."], ["Your money's your own; and if I'm ever to be the heir, it won't be by his doing.\"", "당신 돈은 당신 것이고, 내가 언젠가 상속자가 된다 한들 그건 아버지가 해 주셔서가 아닐 테니까.”"]], [["\"But he gnashes his teeth at me.\"", "“하지만 저한테 이를 가시는걸요.”"]], [["\"You shouldn't care for that, if he don't bite.", "“물지만 않는다면 상관할 것 없소. 나한테도 늘 이를 갈곤 했지."], ["He used to gnash them at me; and when I had to ask him for money I didn't like it; but now I don't mind him a bit.", "돈을 달라고 해야 할 때는 나도 그게 싫었소. 그렇지만 이제는 조금도 개의치 않소."], ["He threw the peerage at me one day, but it didn't go within a yard of my head.\"", "언젠가는 귀족 명감을 나한테 집어던지기도 했는데, 내 머리에서 한 자도 못 미쳐 떨어지더군.”"]], [["\"If he throws anything at me, George, I shall drop upon the spot.\"", "“저한테 무얼 집어던지시면요, 조지, 저는 그 자리에서 쓰러지고 말 거예요.”"]], [["But the countess had a worse time with the earl than any of her children.", "그러나 백작에게서 겪는 고초로 말하자면 백작부인이 어느 자식보다도 심했다."], ["It was necessary that she should see him daily, and necessary also that she should say much that he did not like to hear, and make many petitions that caused him to gnash his teeth.", "그녀는 날마다 그를 보아야 했고, 그가 듣기 싫어하는 말을 숱하게 해야 했으며, 그로 하여금 이를 갈게 만드는 청을 여러 가지로 넣어야 했다."], ["The earl was one of those men who could not endure to live otherwise than expensively, and yet was made miserable by every recurring expense.", "백작은 사치스럽게 사는 것 말고는 견디지 못하면서도 새로 드는 비용 하나하나에 비참해지는 그런 부류의 사내였다."], ["He ought to have known by this time that butchers, and bakers, and corn-chandlers, and coal-merchants will not supply their goods for nothing; and yet it always seemed as though he had expected that at this special period they would do so.", "푸주한이며 빵집이며 곡물상이며 석탄상이 제 물건을 거저 대 주지는 않는다는 것쯤은 이제 알 만도 하련만, 그런데도 그는 늘 이 특별한 시기만은 그들이 그렇게 해 주리라 기대하기라도 한 듯이 굴었다. 그가 돈에 쪼들리는 사람인 것은 틀림없었고, 뉴마켓이나 홈부르크에서 벌인 투기도 운이 따라 주지 않았다."], ["He was an embarrassed man, no doubt, and had not been fortunate in his speculations at Newmarket or Homburg; but, nevertheless, he had still the means of living without daily torment; and it must be supposed that his self-imposed sufferings, with regard to money, rose rather from his disposition than his necessities.", "그럼에도 그에게는 날마다 이런 고문을 겪지 않고도 살아갈 만한 재력이 아직 남아 있었다. 그러니 돈에 관해 그가 스스로에게 지운 이 괴로움은 그의 형편에서라기보다 그의 성미에서 나온 것이라고 보아야 한다."], ["His wife never knew whether he were really ruined, or simply pretending it.", "그의 아내는 그가 정말로 파산한 것인지 그저 그런 척하는 것인지 끝내 알지 못했다."], ["She had now become so used to her position in this respect, that she did not allow fiscal considerations to mar her happiness.", "그녀는 이 점에서 제 처지에 하도 익어 버린 나머지, 돈 문제 따위가 제 행복을 망치도록 내버려 두지 않았다."], ["Food and clothing had always come to her,--including velvet gowns, new trinkets, and a man-cook,--and she presumed that they would continue to come.", "먹을 것과 입을 것은 늘 그녀에게 왔고—벨벳 가운이며 새 장신구며 남자 요리사까지 딸려서 왔고—앞으로도 계속 오리라고 그녀는 짐작했다."], ["But that daily conference with her husband was almost too much for her.", "그러나 남편과 날마다 갖는 그 회견만은 그녀에게 거의 견딜 수 없는 일이었다."], ["She struggled to avoid it; and, as far as the ways and means were concerned, would have allowed them to arrange themselves, if he would only have permitted it.", "그녀는 그것을 피하려고 몸부림쳤으며, 살림 비용에 관한 한 그가 허락만 해 준다면 그것들이 저희끼리 알아서 정리되도록 내버려 두었을 것이다."], ["But he insisted on seeing her daily in his own sitting-room; and she had acknowledged to her favourite daughter, Margaretta, that those half-hours would soon be the death of her.", "그러나 그는 날마다 제 거실에서 그녀를 보아야 한다고 고집했다. 그리고 그녀는 가장 아끼는 딸 마거리타에게, 그 반 시간이 머지않아 제 목숨을 앗아 가고 말 것이라고 털어놓은 터였다."], ["\"I sometimes feel,\"", "“나는 이따금,”"], ["she said, \"that I am going mad before I can get out.\"", "그녀는 이렇게 말했다. “거기서 빠져나오기도 전에 미쳐 버릴 것 같은 기분이 든단다.”"], ["And she reproached herself, probably without reason, in that she had brought much of this upon herself.", "그리고 그녀는, 아마도 까닭 없이, 이 일의 많은 부분을 제가 스스로 불러들였다며 자책했다."], ["In former days the earl had been constantly away from home, and the countess had complained.", "지난날 백작은 늘 집을 비웠고, 백작부인은 그것을 두고 불평했다."], ["Like many other women she had not known when she was well off.", "다른 많은 여자들처럼 그녀도 제가 얼마나 편한 처지인지를 알지 못했던 것이다."], ["She had complained, urging upon her lord that he should devote more of his time to his own hearth.", "그녀는 불평했고, 제 집 화롯가에 좀 더 많은 시간을 바치라고 남편을 다그쳤다."], ["It is probable that her ladyship's remonstrances had been less efficacious than the state of his own health in producing that domestic constancy which he now practised; but it is certain that she looked back with bitter regret to the happy days when she was deserted, jealous, and querulous.", "지금 그가 실천하고 있는 저 가정에의 충실함을 낳은 것은 부인의 항의라기보다 그 자신의 건강 상태였을 가능성이 크다. 그러나 그녀가 버림받고 질투에 차서 푸념하던 저 행복한 나날을 이제 쓰디쓴 회한으로 돌아본 것만은 분명하다."], ["\"Don't you wish we could get Sir Omicron to order him to the German Spas?\" she had said to Margaretta.", "“오미크론 경을 시켜 저이를 독일 온천장으로 보내라는 명을 내리게 할 수 있으면 얼마나 좋겠니?”"], ["Now Sir Omicron was the great London physician, and might, no doubt, do much in that way.", "그녀는 마거리타에게 이렇게 말한 적이 있었다. 그런데 오미크론 경은 런던의 대단한 명의였으니, 아마도 그런 방면에서 큰 힘을 쓸 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["But no such happy order had as yet been given; and, as far as the family could foresee, paterfamilias intended to pass the winter with them at Courcy.", "그러나 그런 행복한 명령은 아직 내려지지 않았다. 그리고 식구들이 내다볼 수 있는 한, 이 집 가장은 코시에서 저희와 함께 겨울을 날 작정이었다."], ["The guests, as I have said, were all gone, and none but the family were in the house when her ladyship waited upon her lord one morning at twelve o'clock, a few days after Mr. Dale's visit to the castle.", "손님들은 내가 말한 대로 모두 떠났고, 데일 씨가 성에 다녀간 지 며칠 뒤 어느 날 아침 열두 시에 부인이 제 주인을 뵈러 갔을 때 집 안에는 식구들밖에 없었다."], ["He always breakfasted alone, and after breakfast found in a French novel and a cigar what solace those innocent recreations were still able to afford him.", "그는 늘 혼자 아침을 들었고, 아침을 든 뒤에는 프랑스 소설과 시가 속에서, 그 무해한 소일거리들이 아직 그에게 줄 수 있는 만큼의 위안을 찾았다."], ["When the novel no longer excited him and when he was saturated with smoke, he would send for his wife.", "소설이 더는 그를 자극하지 못하고 연기에 흠뻑 절고 나면 그는 아내를 부르러 사람을 보냈다. 그다음에는 시종이 그의 옷을 입혔다."], ["After that, his valet would dress him.", "“마님이 저보다 더 심하게 당하십니다요.”"], ["\"She gets it worse than I do,\"", "그 사내는 하인 방에서 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["the man declared in the servants'", "“게다가 훨씬 더 괴로워하시지요."], ["hall; \"and minds it a deal more. I can give warning, and she can't.\"", "저야 그만두겠다고 통고라도 할 수 있지만 마님은 그러지도 못하시니까요.”"]], [["\"Better?", "“나아졌느냐고?"], ["No, I ain't better,\"", "아니, 나아지긴 뭐가 나아졌소.”"], ["the husband said, in answer to his wife's inquiries.", "아내가 안부를 묻자 남편은 이렇게 대답했다."], ["\"I never shall be better while you keep that cook in the kitchen.\"", "“당신이 저 요리사를 부엌에 두고 있는 한 나는 결코 나아지지 않을 거요.”"]], [["\"But where are we to get another if we send him away?\"", "“하지만 그이를 내보내면 다른 사람을 어디서 구해요?”"]], [["\"It's not my business to find cooks.", "“요리사 구하는 건 내 일이 아니오."], ["I don't know where you're to get one.", "어디서 구할지는 나도 모르오."], ["It's my belief you won't have a cook at all before long.", "내 짐작으로는 머지않아 요리사고 뭐고 하나도 없게 될 거요."], ["It seems you have got two extra men into the house without telling me.\"", "게다가 나한테 말도 없이 하인을 둘이나 더 들였다더군.”"]], [["\"We must have servants, you know, when there is company.", "“손님이 계실 때는 하인이 있어야 하잖아요."], ["It wouldn't do to have Lady Dumbello here, and no one to wait on her.\"", "레이디 덤벨로를 모셔 놓고 시중들 사람이 하나도 없으면 될 일이 아니지요.”"]], [["\"Who asked Lady Dumbello?", "“레이디 덤벨로는 누가 불렀소?"], ["I didn't.\"", "나는 아니오.”"]], [["\"I'm sure, my dear, you liked having her here.\"", "“여보, 당신도 그분이 와 계신 걸 좋아하셨잖아요.”"]], [["\"D---- Lady Dumbello!\" and then there was a pause.", "“빌어먹을 레이디 덤벨로 같으니!”"], ["The countess had no objection whatsoever to the above proposition, and was rejoiced that that question of the servants was allowed to slip aside, through the aid of her ladyship.", "그러고는 잠시 말이 끊겼다. 백작부인은 위의 명제에 조금도 이의가 없었고, 부인의 도움을 빌려 하인 문제가 슬며시 옆으로 밀려난 것을 기뻐했다."]], [["\"Look at that letter from Porlock,\"", "“폴록에게서 온 저 편지나 보시오.”"], ["said the earl; and he pushed over to the unhappy mother a letter from her eldest son.", "백작이 말하며, 그 불행한 어머니 앞으로 맏아들의 편지를 밀어 주었다."], ["Of all her children he was the one she loved the best; but him she was never allowed to see under her own roof.", "자식들 가운데 그녀가 가장 사랑하는 것이 그였으나, 그를 제 지붕 아래에서 만나는 것만은 결코 허락되지 않았다."], ["\"I sometimes think that he is the greatest rascal with whom I ever had occasion to concern myself,\"", "“나는 이따금 저 애가 내가 상대해 본 가운데 가장 몹쓸 놈이라는 생각이 드오.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["She took the letter and read it.", "그녀는 편지를 받아 들고 읽었다."], ["The epistle was certainly not one which a father could receive with pleasure from his son; but the disagreeable nature of its contents was the fault rather of the parent than of the child.", "그 서한은 아버지가 아들에게서 기꺼이 받아 볼 만한 것이 결코 아니었다. 그러나 그 내용이 불쾌한 것은 자식의 잘못이라기보다 어버이의 잘못이었다."], ["The writer intimated that certain money due to him had not been paid with necessary punctuality, and that unless he received it, he should instruct his lawyer to take some authorized legal proceedings.", "글쓴이는 제 앞으로 지불되어야 할 어떤 돈이 제때에 지불되지 않았음을 알리면서, 그것을 받지 못한다면 변호사에게 일러 법이 허락하는 절차를 밟게 하겠다고 통고하고 있었다."], ["Lord De Courcy had raised certain moneys on the family property, which he could not have raised without the co-operation of his heir, and had bound himself, in return for that co-operation, to pay a certain fixed income to his eldest son.", "드 코시 백작은 집안 재산을 담보로 얼마간의 돈을 끌어다 쓴 일이 있었는데, 그것은 상속자의 협조 없이는 끌어다 쓸 수 없는 것이었다. 그리하여 그는 그 협조의 대가로 맏아들에게 일정한 액수의 수입을 지불하기로 스스로 묶여 있었다."], ["This he regarded as an allowance from himself; but Lord Porlock regarded it as his own, by lawful claim.", "이것을 그는 제가 주는 용돈으로 여겼으나, 폴록 경은 법으로 제 것이 된 제 몫으로 여겼다."], ["The son had not worded his letter with any affectionate phraseology.", "아들은 편지에 다정한 말이라고는 한마디도 쓰지 않았다."], ["\"Lord Porlock begs to inform Lord De Courcy--\"", "“폴록 경은 드 코시 경에게 알리는 바—”"], ["Such had been the commencement.", "서두가 그런 식이었다."]], [["\"I suppose he must have his money; else how can he live?\"", "“그 애도 제 돈은 받아야겠지요. 안 그러면 어떻게 살아요?”"], ["said the countess, trembling.", "백작부인이 떨면서 말했다."]], [["\"Live!\"", "“살아!”"], ["shouted the earl.", "백작이 고함쳤다."], ["\"And so you think it proper that he should write such a letter as that to his father!\"", "“그러니까 당신은 저 애가 제 아비한테 저따위 편지를 쓰는 것이 온당하다고 여긴다는 말이오!”"]], [["\"It is all very unfortunate,\"", "“모두 참으로 딱한 일이에요.”"], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["\"I don't know where the money's to come from.", "“그 돈이 대체 어디서 나온단 말이오."], ["As for him, if he were starving, it would serve him right.", "그 애로 말하자면, 굶어 죽는다 해도 싸지."], ["He's a disgrace to the name and the family.", "이름에도 가문에도 수치일 뿐이오."], ["From all I hear, he won't live long.\"", "내가 듣기로는 오래 살지도 못할 거라더군.”"]], [["\"Oh, De Courcy, don't talk of it in that way!\"", "“오, 드 코시, 그런 식으로 말하지 말아요!”"]], [["\"What way am I to talk of it?", "“그럼 어떤 식으로 말하란 말이오?"], ["If I say that he's my greatest comfort, and living as becomes a nobleman, and is a fine healthy man of his age, with a good wife and a lot of legitimate children, will that make you believe it?", "그 애가 내 가장 큰 위안이고, 귀족답게 살고 있고, 제 나이에 걸맞게 몸이 튼튼하고, 착한 아내와 적자를 여럿 두었다고 내가 말하면, 당신이 그걸 믿기라도 하겠소?"], ["Women are such fools.", "여자들이란 참으로 바보들이오."], ["Nothing that I say will make him worse than he is.\"", "내가 무슨 말을 한들 그 애가 지금보다 더 나빠지지는 않소.”"]], [["\"But he may reform.\"", "“그래도 마음을 고쳐먹을지 몰라요.”"]], [["\"Reform!", "“고쳐먹어!"], ["He's over forty, and when I last saw him he looked nearly sixty.", "그 애는 마흔이 넘었고, 내가 마지막으로 보았을 때는 예순이 다 되어 보였소."], ["There;--you may answer his letter; I won't.\"", "자—당신이 답장을 쓰시오. 나는 안 쓰겠소.”"]], [["\"And about the money?\"", "“그럼 돈은요?”"]], [["\"Why doesn't he write to Gazebee about his dirty money?", "“그 더러운 돈 이야기라면 왜 게이지비한테 쓰지 않는 거요?"], ["Why does he trouble me?", "왜 나를 성가시게 하지?"], ["I haven't got his money.", "그 애 돈은 나한테 없소."], ["Ask Gazebee about his money.", "돈 이야기는 게이지비한테 물어보시오."], ["I won't trouble myself about it.\"", "나는 그 일로 신경 쓰지 않겠소.”"], ["Then there was another pause, during which the countess folded the letter, and put it in her pocket.", "그러고서 또 한 번 말이 끊겼고, 그동안 백작부인은 편지를 접어 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["\"How long is George going to remain here with that woman?\"", "“조지는 저 여자를 데리고 언제까지 여기 있을 작정이오?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["\"I'm sure she is very harmless,\"", "“그 애는 정말 아무 해도 끼치지 않아요.”"], ["pleaded the countess.", "백작부인이 사정하듯 말했다."]], [["\"I always think when I see her that I'm sitting down to dinner with my own housemaid.", "“그 여자를 볼 때마다 나는 내 집 하녀와 마주 앉아 만찬을 드는 기분이오."], ["I never saw such a woman.", "그런 여자는 난생처음 보오."], ["How he can put up with it!", "저 애는 어떻게 그걸 견딘단 말인가!"], ["But I don't suppose he cares for anything.\"", "하기야 저 애는 아무것도 마음에 두지 않는 인간이지.”"]], [["\"It has made him very steady.\"", "“그 덕에 조지가 아주 착실해졌어요.”"]], [["\"Steady!\"", "“착실하다니!”"]], [["\"And as she will be confined before long it may be as well that she should remain here.", "“게다가 머지않아 해산할 몸이니 여기 머무는 편이 나을지도 몰라요."], ["If Porlock doesn't marry, you know--\"", "폴록이 결혼하지 않으면, 아시다시피—”"]], [["\"And so he means to live here altogether, does he?", "“그래, 아주 눌러살 작정이란 말이지?"], ["I'll tell you what it is,--I won't have it.", "내 분명히 말해 두겠소—나는 용납 못 하오."], ["He's better able to keep a house over his own head and his wife's than I am to do it for them, and so you may tell them.", "제 머리와 제 아내 머리 위에 지붕을 얹는 일이라면, 내가 저것들을 위해 해 주는 것보다 저 애가 제 힘으로 하는 편이 훨씬 낫소. 그렇게 전하시오."], ["I won't have it.", "나는 용납 못 하오."], ["D'ye hear?\"", "알아들었소?”"], ["Then there was another short pause.", "그러고서 또 한 번 짧은 사이가 있었다."], ["\"D'ye hear?\"", "“알아들었소?”"], ["he shouted at her.", "그가 아내에게 고함쳤다."]], [["\"Yes; of course I hear.", "“네, 물론 알아들어요."], ["I was only thinking you wouldn't wish me to turn them out, just as her confinement is coming on.\"", "다만 해산이 코앞인데 그 애들을 내쫓기를 당신이 바라지는 않으시리라 생각했을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["\"I know what that means.", "“그게 무슨 뜻인지 나는 아오."], ["Then they'd never go.", "그러면 저것들은 영영 안 나갈 거요."], ["I won't have it; and if you don't tell them I will.\"", "나는 용납 못 하오. 당신이 말하지 않으면 내가 하겠소.”"], ["In answer to this Lady De Courcy promised that she would tell them, thinking perhaps that the earl's mode of telling might not be beneficial in that particular epoch which was now coming in the life of Mrs. George.", "이에 드 코시 백작부인은 제가 말하겠노라 약속했으니, 아마도 백작이 말을 전하는 방식이 조지 부인의 삶에 이제 막 닥쳐오는 저 특별한 시기에는 이로울 것이 없으리라고 여겼기 때문일 것이다."]], [["\"Did you know,\"", "“당신 알고 있었소,”"], ["said he, breaking out on a new subject, \"that a man had been here named Dale, calling on somebody in this house?\"", "그가 느닷없이 새로운 화제를 꺼내며 말했다. “데일이라는 자가 여기 와서 이 집 누군가를 찾았다는 것을?”"], ["In answer to which the countess acknowledged that she had known it.", "이에 백작부인은 알고 있었노라고 시인했다."]], [["\"Then why did you keep it from me?\"", "“그런데 왜 그것을 나한테 숨겼소?”"], ["And that gnashing of the teeth took place which was so specially objectionable to Mrs. George.", "그러고는 조지 부인이 그토록 질색하는 저 이 가는 소리가 났다."]], [["\"It was a matter of no moment.", "“대수로운 일이 아니었어요."], ["He came to see Lady Julia De Guest.\"", "레이디 줄리아 드 게스트를 만나러 온 것이니까요.”"]], [["\"Yes; but he came about that man Crosbie.\"", "“그렇겠지. 하지만 크로즈비라는 그자 일로 온 것 아니오.”"]], [["\"I suppose he did.\"", "“그랬겠지요.”"]], [["\"Why have you let that girl be such a fool?", "“어쩌자고 그 애를 그런 바보로 놔두었소?"], ["You'll find he'll play her some knave's trick.\"", "그자가 그 애한테 무슨 사기꾼 짓을 할 게요, 두고 보시오.”"]], [["\"Oh dear, no.\"", "“어머, 그럴 리가요.”"]], [["\"And why should she want to marry such a man as that?\"", "“그리고 대체 왜 그 애가 그따위 사내와 결혼하고 싶어 한단 말이오?”"]], [["\"He's quite a gentleman, you know, and very much thought of in the world.", "“그이는 어엿한 신사고, 세상에서 아주 좋은 평을 듣고 있어요."], ["It won't be at all bad for her, poor thing.", "그 애한테 조금도 나쁜 혼처가 아니에요, 가엾은 것."], ["It is so very hard for a girl to get married now-a-days without money.\"", "요새는 돈 없는 처녀가 시집가기가 여간 어려운 게 아니잖아요.”"]], [["\"And so they're to take up with anybody.", "“그래서 아무하고나 붙어도 된다 이 말이오."], ["As far as I can see, this is a worse affair than that of Amelia.\"", "내가 보기엔 어밀리아 때보다 더 나쁜 일이오.”"]], [["\"Amelia has done very well, my dear.\"", "“어밀리아는 아주 잘했어요, 여보.”"]], [["\"Oh, if you call it doing well for your girls, I don't.", "“오, 당신이 그걸 딸들이 잘한 일이라 부른다면 나는 아니오."], ["I call it doing uncommon badly; about as bad as they well can do.", "나는 여간 못한 일이 아니라고 하겠소. 할 수 있는 한 가장 못한 짓이지."], ["But it's your affair.", "하지만 그건 당신 일이오."], ["I have never meddled with them, and don't intend to do it now.\"", "나는 여태 그 애들 일에 참견한 적이 없고, 이제 와서 참견할 생각도 없소.”"]], [["\"I really think she'll be happy, and she is devotedly attached to the young man.\"", "“저는 정말 그 애가 행복할 거라고 생각해요. 그리고 그 애는 그 젊은이에게 헌신적으로 마음을 바치고 있어요.”"]], [["\"Devotedly attached to the young man!\"", "“그 젊은이에게 헌신적으로 마음을 바치고 있다니!”"], ["The tone and manner in which the earl repeated these words were such as to warrant an opinion that his lordship might have done very well on the stage had his attention been called to that profession.", "백작이 이 말을 되뇌는 어조와 태도는, 만일 그가 무대라는 직업에 마음을 두었더라면 그 방면에서 크게 성공했으리라는 견해를 넉넉히 뒷받침할 만한 것이었다."], ["\"It makes me sick to hear people talk in that way.", "“사람들이 그런 식으로 지껄이는 것을 들으면 나는 속이 뒤집히오."], ["She wants to get married, and she's a fool for her pains;--I can't help that; only remember that I'll have no nonsense here about that other girl.", "그 애는 시집을 가고 싶은 것이고, 그 수고를 자청하는 걸 보면 바보요—그건 나도 어쩔 수 없소. 다만 그 다른 아가씨 일로 여기서 무슨 헛소리가 오가는 것만은 내가 결코 두고 보지 않겠다는 것을 기억해 두시오."], ["If he gives me trouble of that sort, by ----, I'll be the death of him.", "그자가 그런 종류의 말썽을 나한테 일으킨다면, ——에 걸고, 내가 그자를 죽여 놓고 말겠소."], ["When is the marriage to be?\"", "혼례는 언제요?”"]], [["\"They talk of February.\"", "“2월이라고들 해요.”"]], [["\"I won't have any tomfoolery and expense.", "“나는 어떤 법석도 낭비도 용납 못 하오."], ["If she chooses to marry a clerk in an office, she shall marry him as clerks are married.\"", "그 애가 관청 서기와 결혼하겠다면, 서기들이 결혼하는 식으로 시집가야 할 거요.”"]], [["\"He'll be the secretary before that, De Courcy.\"", "“그전에 사무국장이 될 거예요, 드 코시.”"]], [["\"What difference does that make?", "“그게 무슨 차이가 있소?"], ["Secretary, indeed!", "사무국장이라니, 참!"], ["What sort of men do you suppose secretaries are?", "사무국장이라는 자들이 대체 어떤 인간들인 줄 아시오?"], ["A beggar that came from nobody knows where!", "어디서 굴러왔는지도 모를 거지 아니오!"], ["I won't have any tomfoolery;--d'ye hear?\"", "나는 어떤 법석도 용납 못 하오—알아들었소?”"], ["Whereupon the countess said that she did hear, and soon afterwards managed to escape.", "이에 백작부인은 알아들었다고 말했고, 얼마 지나지 않아 겨우 빠져나올 수 있었다. 그다음은 시종의 차례였다."], ["The valet then took his turn; and repeated, after his hour of service, that \"Old Nick\" in his tantrums had been more like the Prince of Darkness than ever.", "그리고 그는 제 근무 시간이 끝난 뒤에, “마왕 영감”이 성깔을 부리는 품이 그 어느 때보다도 암흑의 왕을 빼닮았더라고 되뇌었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVII. \"ON MY HONOUR, I DO NOT UNDERSTAND IT.\"", "# 제27장. 「명예를 걸고, 나는 이해할 수가 없다」"]], [["In the meantime Lady Alexandrina endeavoured to realize to herself all the advantages and disadvantages of her own position.", "그러는 동안 레이디 알렉산드리나는 제 처지의 이로운 점과 이롭지 못한 점을 남김없이 헤아려 보려 애썼다."], ["She was not possessed of strong affections, nor of depth of character, nor of high purpose; but she was no fool, nor was she devoid of principle.", "그녀에게는 강한 애정도, 깊이 있는 성품도, 높은 뜻도 없었다. 그러나 바보는 아니었고, 원칙이 없는 사람도 아니었다."], ["She had asked herself many times whether her present life was so happy as to make her think that a permanent continuance in it would suffice for her desires, and she had always replied to herself that she would fain change to some other life if it were possible.", "지금의 삶이 이대로 내내 이어져도 제 바람에 넉넉하겠다 싶을 만큼 행복한 것인지를 그녀는 여러 번 스스로에게 물어 왔고, 그때마다 할 수만 있다면 다른 삶으로 기꺼이 바꾸겠노라고 스스로에게 대답해 왔다."], ["She had also questioned herself as to her rank, of which she was quite sufficiently proud, and had told herself that she could not degrade herself in the world without a heavy pang.", "또한 그녀는 스스로 넉넉히 자랑스러워하는 제 신분에 대해서도 자문해 보았고, 세상에서 제 몸을 낮추자면 무거운 쓰라림 없이는 안 되리라고 스스로에게 말했다."], ["But she had at last taught herself to believe that she had more to gain by becoming the wife of such a man as Crosbie than by remaining as an unmarried daughter of her father's house.", "그러나 마침내 그녀는, 아버지 집안의 시집 안 간 딸로 남아 있기보다 크로즈비 같은 남자의 아내가 되는 편이 얻을 것이 더 많다고 스스로를 가르쳐 믿게 만들었다."], ["There was much in her sister Amelia's position which she did not envy, but there was less to envy in that of her sister Rosina. The Gazebee house in St.", "언니 어밀리아의 처지에는 부럽지 않은 구석이 많았으나, 언니 로지나의 처지에는 부러울 것이 그보다도 적었다."], ["John's Wood Road was not so magnificent as Courcy Castle; but then it was less dull, less embittered by torment, and was moreover her sister's own.", "세인트 존스 우드 로드의 게이지비 집은 코시 성만큼 웅장하지 않았다. 그러나 그 대신 덜 따분했고, 괴로움에 덜 시달렸으며, 게다가 언니 제 것이었다."]], [["\"Very many do marry commoners,\"", "“평민과 결혼하는 사람이야 아주 많잖아요.”"], ["she had said to Margaretta.", "그녀는 마거리타에게 그렇게 말했었다."]], [["\"Oh, yes, of course.", "“아, 그럼요, 물론이죠."], ["It makes a difference, you know, when a man has a fortune.\"", "하지만 남자한테 재산이 있으면 사정이 다르잖아요.”"]], [["Of course it did make a difference.", "물론 사정이 달랐다."], ["Crosbie had no fortune, was not even so rich as Mr. Gazebee, could keep no carriage, and would have no country house.", "크로즈비에게는 재산이 없었고, 게이지비 씨만큼 넉넉하지도 못했으며, 마차를 둘 수도 없었고, 시골 저택을 갖게 될 일도 없었다."], ["But then he was a man of fashion, was more thought of in the world than Mr. Gazebee, might probably rise in his own profession,--and was at any rate thoroughly presentable.", "그러나 그는 유행을 아는 사람이었고, 세상에서 게이지비 씨보다 더 높이 쳐주는 사람이었으며, 아마도 제 직업에서 출세할 수도 있을 터였고—어쨌든 어디에 내놓아도 손색이 없었다."], ["She would have preferred a gentleman with £5,000 a year; but then as no gentleman with £5,000 a year came that way, would she not be happier with Mr.", "그녀로서야 연 5,000파운드를 가진 신사가 더 좋았을 것이다."], ["Crosbie than she would be with no husband at all? She was not very much in love with Mr.", "그러나 연 5,000파운드를 가진 신사가 그쪽 길로 오지 않는 바에야, 남편이 아예 없는 것보다는 크로즈비 씨와 함께하는 편이 더 행복하지 않겠는가?"], ["Crosbie, but she thought that she could live with him comfortably, and that on the whole it would be a good thing to be married.", "그녀는 크로즈비 씨를 그리 깊이 사랑하지는 않았지만, 그와 함께라면 편안히 살 수 있으리라 여겼고, 대체로 보아 결혼을 한다는 것은 좋은 일이라고 생각했다."]], [["And she made certain resolves as to the manner in which she would do her duty by her husband.", "그리고 그녀는 남편에게 제 도리를 어떤 식으로 다할 것인지에 대해 몇 가지를 마음에 정해 두었다."], ["Her sister Amelia was paramount in her own house, ruling indeed with a moderate, endurable dominion, and ruling much to her husband's advantage.", "언니 어밀리아는 제 집에서 으뜸가는 사람이었으니, 온건하고 견딜 만한 정도의 지배를 펴며 다스렸고, 그 다스림이 남편에게 크게 이롭기까지 했다."], ["Alexandrina feared that she would not be allowed to rule, but she could at any rate try.", "알렉산드리나는 저에게는 다스리는 일이 허락되지 않으리라 짐작했지만, 어쨌든 해 보기는 할 참이었다."], ["She would do all in her power to make him comfortable, and would be specially careful not to irritate him by any insistence on her own higher rank.", "그녀는 그를 편안하게 해 주는 데 힘닿는 대로 다 하겠다고, 그리고 특히 제 신분이 더 높다는 것을 내세워 그를 성나게 하는 일이 없도록 조심하겠다고 마음먹었다."], ["She would be very meek in this respect; and if children should come she would be as painstaking about them as though her own father had been merely a clergyman or a lawyer.", "그 점에 대해서는 아주 얌전하게 굴 작정이었다. 그리고 아이들이 생긴다면, 마치 제 아버지가 일개 목사나 일개 변호사이기라도 했던 것처럼 아이들 일에 정성을 쏟으리라 했다."], ["She thought also much about poor Lilian Dale, asking herself sundry questions, with an idea of being high-principled as to her duty in that respect.", "그녀는 또 가엾은 릴리언 데일에 대해서도 많이 생각하며, 그 일에 관한 제 도리에서 높은 원칙을 지키겠다는 마음으로 스스로에게 이런저런 물음을 던져 보았다."], ["Was she wrong in taking Mr. Crosbie away from Lilian Dale?", "크로즈비 씨를 릴리언 데일에게서 빼앗은 것이 잘못인가?"], ["In answer to these questions she was able to assure herself comfortably that she was not wrong.", "이 물음들에 대해 그녀는 제가 잘못한 것이 아니라고 마음 편히 스스로를 안심시킬 수 있었다."], ["Mr. Crosbie would not, under any circumstances, marry Lilian Dale.", "크로즈비 씨는 어떤 사정에서도 릴리언 데일과 결혼하지 않을 것이다."], ["He had told her so more than once, and that in a solemn way.", "그가 그녀에게 그렇게 말한 것이 한 번이 아니었고, 그것도 엄숙한 태도로 말했던 것이다."], ["She could therefore be doing no harm to Lilian Dale.", "그러니 그녀가 릴리언 데일에게 해를 끼치는 일일 리 없었다."], ["If she entertained any inner feeling that Crosbie's fault in jilting Lilian Dale was less than it would have been had she herself not been an earl's daughter,--that her own rank did in some degree extenuate her lover's falseness,--she did not express it in words even to herself.", "만일 그녀가 마음속 어딘가에서, 릴리언 데일을 저버린 크로즈비의 잘못이 제가 백작의 딸이 아니었더라면 더 컸으리라는 느낌을—제 신분이 어느 정도는 제 연인의 신의 없음을 덜어 준다는 느낌을 품고 있었다 하더라도, 그녀는 그것을 스스로에게조차 말로 옮기지는 않았다."]], [["She did not get very much sympathy from her own family.", "그녀는 제 집안 사람들에게서 그리 큰 공감을 얻지 못했다."], ["\"I'm afraid he does not think much of his religious duties.", "“그이가 신앙의 의무를 대수롭게 여기지 않는 것 같아 걱정이에요."], ["I'm told that young men of that sort seldom do,\"", "그런 부류의 젊은 남자들은 좀처럼 그러지 않는다더군요.”"], ["said Rosina.", "로지나가 말했다."], ["\"I don't say you're wrong,\"", "“네가 틀렸다는 말은 아니야.”"], ["said Margaretta.", "마거리타가 말했다."], ["\"By no means.", "“결코 아니고말고."], ["Indeed I think less of it now than I did when Amelia did the same thing.", "사실 나는 어밀리아 언니가 같은 일을 했을 때보다 지금 이 일을 덜 대수롭게 여긴단다."], ["I shouldn't do it myself, that's all.\"", "다만 나라면 하지 않겠다는 것뿐이야.”"], ["Her father told her that he supposed she knew her own mind.", "아버지는 그녀에게 네가 네 마음을 알고 있는 모양이지 하고 말했다."], ["Her mother, who endeavoured to comfort and in some sort to congratulate her, nevertheless, harped constantly on the fact that she was marrying a man without rank and without a fortune.", "어머니는 그녀를 위로하고 어떻게든 축하해 주려 애쓰면서도, 신분도 재산도 없는 남자와 결혼하는 것이라는 사실을 끊임없이 되뇌었다."], ["Her congratulations were apologetic, and her comfortings took the guise of consolation.", "그 축하는 변명 같았고, 그 위로는 딱하게 여기는 말투를 띠었다."], ["\"Of course you won't be rich, my dear; but I really think you'll do very well.", "“물론 너는 넉넉하게 살지는 못하겠지, 얘야. 하지만 나는 네가 아주 잘하는 거라고 정말로 생각한다."], ["Mr. Crosbie may be received anywhere, and you never need be ashamed of him.\"", "크로즈비 씨는 어디에서든 받아들여질 사람이고, 너는 그이를 부끄러워할 일이 결코 없을 게다.”"], ["By which the countess implied that her elder married daughter was occasionally called on to be ashamed of her husband.", "이 말로 백작부인은 제 맏딸이 이따금 남편을 부끄러워해야 할 처지에 놓인다는 뜻을 내비쳤다."], ["\"I wish he could keep a carriage for you, but perhaps that will come some day.\"", "“그이가 너한테 마차 한 대쯤 마련해 줄 수 있으면 좋으련만. 하지만 어쩌면 언젠가는 그리되겠지.”"], ["Upon the whole Alexandrina did not repent, and stoutly told her father that she did know her own mind.", "대체로 보아 알렉산드리나는 후회하지 않았고, 아버지에게 저는 제 마음을 알고 있노라고 당차게 말했다."]], [["During all this time Lily Dale was as yet perfect in her happiness.", "그러는 내내 릴리 데일은 아직 온전한 행복 속에 있었다."], ["That delay of a day or two in the receipt of the expected letter from her lover had not disquieted her.", "연인에게서 오리라던 편지가 하루 이틀 늦어진 일도 그녀의 마음을 어지럽히지는 않았다."], ["She had promised him that she would not distrust him, and she was firmly minded to keep her promises.", "그녀는 그를 의심하지 않겠노라 약속했고, 제 약속을 지키겠다고 굳게 마음먹고 있었다."], ["Indeed no idea of breaking it came to her at this time.", "실로 이때까지 그 약속을 깨겠다는 생각은 그녀에게 떠오르지도 않았다."], ["She was disappointed when the postman would come and bring no letter for her,--disappointed, as is the husbandman when the longed-for rain does not come to refresh the parched earth; but she was in no degree angry.", "우편배달부가 와서 제게 올 편지를 가져오지 않을 때면 그녀는 실망했다—목마른 땅을 적셔 줄 그토록 기다리던 비가 오지 않을 때 농부가 실망하듯이. 그러나 그녀는 조금도 화가 나지는 않았다."], ["\"He will explain it,\"", "“그이가 설명해 줄 거야.”"], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["And she assured Bell that men never recognized the hunger and thirst after letters which women feel when away from those whom they love.", "그러고는 벨에게, 사랑하는 이와 떨어져 있을 때 여자가 느끼는 편지에 대한 주림과 목마름을 남자들은 결코 알지 못한다고 잘라 말했다."]], [["Then they heard at the Small House that the squire had gone away from Allington.", "그러던 참에 작은 집에서는 지주가 앨링턴을 떠났다는 소식을 들었다."], ["During the last few days Bernard had not been much with them, and now they heard the news, not through their cousin, but from Hopkins.", "지난 며칠 동안 버나드는 그들과 그리 자주 어울리지 않았고, 그래서 이번 소식도 사촌을 통해서가 아니라 홉킨스에게서 들은 것이었다."], ["\"I really can't undertake to say, Miss Bell, where the master's gone to.", "“나리께서 어디로 가셨는지는 제가 정말이지 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다요, 벨 아가씨."], ["It's not likely the master'd tell me where he was going to; not unless it was about seeds, or the likes of that.\"", "나리께서 어디 가신다고 저한테 일러 주실 리가 있습니까요. 씨앗이나 그런 것에 관한 일이라면 몰라도요.”"]], [["\"He has gone very suddenly,\"", "“아주 갑작스럽게 떠나셨네요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Well, miss, I've nothing to say to that.", "“글쎄올시다, 아가씨, 그 일이야 제가 드릴 말씀이 없습니다요."], ["And why shouldn't he go sudden if he likes?", "그리고 나리께서 그러고 싶으시면 갑작스럽게 가시면 어떻습니까요?"], ["I only know he had his gig, and went to the station.", "저야 나리께서 이륜마차를 타고 역으로 가셨다는 것밖에 모릅니다요."], ["If you was to bury me alive I couldn't tell you more.\"", "저를 산 채로 파묻는다 해도 그보다 더는 말씀드릴 게 없습니다요.”"]], [["\"I should like to try,\"", "“한번 해 보고 싶네.”"], ["said Lily as they walked away.", "두 사람이 그 자리를 떠나며 릴리가 말했다."], ["\"He is such a cross old thing.", "“저이는 어쩜 저렇게 심술궂은 늙은이일까."], ["I wonder whether Bernard has gone with my uncle.\"", "버나드가 삼촌하고 같이 갔을까?”"], ["And then they thought no more about it.", "그러고서 그들은 그 일을 더 생각하지 않았다."]], [["On the day after that Bernard came down to the Small House, but he said nothing by way of accounting for the squire's absence.", "그 이튿날 버나드가 작은 집으로 내려왔으나, 지주가 자리를 비운 까닭에 대해서는 아무 설명도 하지 않았다."], ["\"He is in London, I know,\"", "“런던에 계셔, 그건 알아.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"I hope he'll call on Mr. Crosbie,\"", "“크로즈비 씨를 찾아가 주셨으면 좋겠어.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["But on this subject Bernard said not a word.", "그러나 이 이야기에 대해 버나드는 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["He did ask Lily whether she had heard from Adolphus, in answer to which she replied, with as indifferent a voice as she could assume, that she had not had a letter that morning.", "그는 어돌퍼스에게서 소식이 있었느냐고 릴리에게 묻기는 했는데, 그 물음에 그녀는 되도록 아무렇지도 않은 목소리를 지어내며 그날 아침에는 편지를 받지 못했다고 대답했다."]], [["\"I shall be angry with him if he's not a good correspondent,\"", "“편지를 잘 안 쓰는 사람이라면 내가 그이한테 화를 낼 테다.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale, when she and Lily were alone together.", "데일 부인이 릴리와 단둘이 있게 되었을 때 말했다."]], [["\"No, mamma, you mustn't be angry with him.", "“아니에요, 엄마, 그이한테 화내시면 안 돼요."], ["I won't let you be angry with him.", "화내시게 두지 않을 거예요."], ["Please to remember he's my lover and not yours.\"", "그이는 엄마 연인이 아니라 제 연인이라는 걸 기억해 주세요.”"]], [["\"But I can see you when you watch for the postman.\"", "“하지만 네가 우편배달부를 기다리고 있는 게 눈에 보이는걸.”"]], [["\"I won't watch for the postman any more if it makes you have bad thoughts about him.", "“엄마가 그이를 나쁘게 생각하시게 된다면 저는 이제 우편배달부를 기다리지 않을래요."], ["Yes, they are bad thoughts.", "네, 그건 나쁜 생각이에요."], ["I won't have you think that he doesn't do everything that is right.\"", "그이가 옳은 일을 다 하지 않는다고 엄마가 생각하시게 두지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["On the next morning the postman brought a letter, or rather a note, and Lily at once saw that it was from Crosbie.", "이튿날 아침 우편배달부가 편지를, 아니 쪽지 한 장을 가져왔고, 릴리는 그것이 크로즈비에게서 온 것임을 대번에 알아보았다."], ["She had contrived to intercept it near the back door, at which the postman called, so that her mother should not watch her watchings, nor see her disappointment if none should come.", "그녀는 어머니가 자기가 기다리는 모습을 지켜보지 못하도록, 그리고 아무것도 오지 않았을 때 자기가 실망하는 것을 보지 못하도록, 우편배달부가 들르는 뒷문 가까이에서 그것을 가로챌 궁리를 해 두었던 터였다."], ["\"Thank you, Jane,\"", "“고마워, 제인.”"], ["she said, very calmly, when the eager, kindly girl ran to her with the little missive; and she walked off to some solitude, trying to hide her impatience.", "마음씨 곱고 신이 난 하녀가 그 작은 편지를 들고 달려왔을 때 그녀는 아주 차분하게 말했다. 그러고는 조바심을 감추려 애쓰며 아무도 없는 곳으로 걸어갔다."], ["The note had seemed so small that it amazed her; but when she opened it the contents amazed her more.", "쪽지가 어찌나 작아 보이던지 그녀는 놀랐다. 그러나 그것을 펴 보았을 때 그 내용은 그녀를 더욱 놀라게 했다."], ["There was neither beginning nor end.", "첫머리도 끝맺음도 없었다."], ["There was no appellation of love, and no signature.", "사랑을 담은 부름말도 없었고, 서명도 없었다."], ["It contained but two lines.", "거기에는 두 줄뿐이었다."], ["\"I will write to you at length to-morrow.", "“내일 길게 쓰겠소."], ["This is my first day in London, and I have been so driven about that I cannot write.\"", "오늘이 런던에서의 첫날인데, 하도 이리저리 끌려다녀서 도무지 쓸 수가 없구려.”"], ["That was all, and it was scrawled on half a sheet of note-paper.", "그것이 전부였고, 그나마 편지지 반 장에 갈겨쓴 것이었다."], ["Why, at any rate, had he not called her his dearest Lily?", "아무리 그렇더라도, 어째서 그는 그녀를 사랑하는 릴리라고 불러 주지 않았을까?"], ["Why had he not assured her that he was ever her own?", "어째서 자기는 언제까지나 그대의 것이라고 다짐해 주지 않았을까?"], ["Such expressions, meaning so much, may be conveyed in a glance of the pen.", "그토록 많은 것을 담은 그런 말들은 펜을 한 번 놀리는 사이에도 전할 수 있는 것을."], ["\"Ah,\"", "“아,”"], ["she said, \"if he knew how I hunger and thirst after his love!\"", "그녀가 말했다. “내가 그이의 사랑을 얼마나 주리고 목말라하는지 그이가 알아준다면!”"]], [["She had but a moment left to her before she must join her mother and sister, and she used that moment in remembering her promise.", "어머니와 언니에게 돌아가기까지 그녀에게 남은 시간은 잠깐뿐이었고, 그녀는 그 잠깐을 제 약속을 되새기는 데 썼다."], ["\"I know it is all right,\"", "“다 괜찮은 거야.”"], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["\"He does not think of these things as I do.", "“그이는 이런 일들을 나처럼 생각하지 않는 것뿐이야."], ["He had to write at the last moment,--as he was leaving his office.\"", "마지막 순간에, 사무실을 나서면서 써야 했던 거지.”"], ["And then with a quiet, smiling face, she walked into the breakfast-parlour.", "그러고서 그녀는 조용히 미소 띤 얼굴로 아침 식사하는 방으로 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["\"What does he say, Lily?\"", "“뭐라고 썼어, 릴리?”"], ["asked Bell.", "벨이 물었다."]], [["\"What would you give to know?\"", "“알면 뭘 줄 건데?”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"I wouldn't give twopence for the whole of it,\"", "“그거 전부라도 나는 이 펜스도 안 내겠어.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"When you get anybody to write to you letters, I wonder whether you'll show them to everybody?\"", "“언니도 누군가한테서 편지를 받게 되면, 그때는 그걸 아무한테나 다 보여 줄 참이야?”"]], [["\"But if there's any special London news, I suppose we might hear it,\"", "“그래도 런던의 무슨 특별한 소식이 있다면 우리도 들을 수 있지 않겠니.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"But suppose there's no special London news, mamma.", "“하지만 특별한 런던 소식이 없다면요, 엄마."], ["The poor man had only been in town one day, you know: and there never is any news at this time of the year.\"", "그 딱한 사람은 런던에 온 지 하루밖에 안 됐잖아요. 게다가 이맘때에는 원래 아무 소식도 없는걸요.”"]], [["\"Had he seen uncle Christopher?\"", "“크리스토퍼 삼촌은 만났다더냐?”"]], [["\"I don't think he had; but he doesn't say.", "“못 만난 것 같아요. 하지만 그런 말은 없어요."], ["We shall get all the news from him when he comes.", "삼촌이 오시면 소식은 다 삼촌한테서 듣게 될 거예요."], ["He cares much more about London news than Adolphus does.\"", "삼촌이 어돌퍼스보다 런던 소식에 훨씬 더 관심이 많으시니까요.”"], ["And then there was no more said about the letter.", "그러고 나서 그 편지에 대해서는 더 이야기가 없었다."]], [["But Lily had read her two former letters over and over again at the breakfast-table; and though she had not read them aloud, she had repeated many words out of them, and had so annotated upon them that her mother, who had heard her, could have almost re-written them.", "그러나 릴리는 앞서 온 편지 두 통은 아침 식탁에서 읽고 또 읽었었다. 소리 내어 읽지는 않았어도 거기서 여러 구절을 되뇌었고, 하도 이런저런 풀이를 곁들여 놓았기에, 그것을 들은 어머니는 그 편지를 거의 그대로 다시 써 낼 수도 있을 지경이었다."], ["Now, she did not even show the paper; and then her absence, during which she had read the letter, had hardly exceeded a minute or two.", "그런데 이번에는 종이를 내보이지조차 않았다. 게다가 편지를 읽느라 자리를 비운 시간도 일이 분을 넘지 않았다."], ["All this Mrs. Dale observed, and she knew that her daughter had been again disappointed.", "데일 부인은 이 모든 것을 눈여겨보았고, 딸이 또 실망했다는 것을 알았다."]], [["In fact that day Lily was very serious, but she did not appear to be unhappy.", "실은 그날 릴리는 아주 진지했으나 불행해 보이지는 않았다."], ["Early after breakfast Bell went over to the parsonage, and Mrs. Dale and her youngest daughter sat together over their work.", "아침을 먹고 얼마 되지 않아 벨이 목사관으로 건너갔고, 데일 부인과 막내딸은 일감을 앞에 두고 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["\"Mamma,\"", "“엄마,”"], ["she said, \"I hope you and I are not to be divided when I go to live in London.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “제가 런던에 가서 살게 되더라도 엄마와 제가 갈라지지는 않았으면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["\"We shall never be divided in heart, my love.\"", "“마음이야 우리가 결코 갈라지지 않을 게다, 내 사랑.”"]], [["\"Ah, but that will not be enough for happiness, though perhaps enough to prevent absolute unhappiness.", "“아, 하지만 그것만으로는 행복하기에 넉넉하지 않아요. 아주 불행해지는 것을 막기에는 넉넉할지 몰라도요."], ["I shall want to see you, touch you, and pet you as I do now.\"", "저는 지금처럼 엄마를 보고, 만지고, 어루만지고 싶을 거예요.”"], ["And she came and knelt on the cushion at her mother's feet.", "그러고서 그녀는 다가와 어머니 발치의 방석에 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["\"You will have some one else to caress and pet,--perhaps many others.\"", "“너에게는 어루만지고 쓰다듬을 다른 사람이 생길 게다. 어쩌면 여럿이 생기겠지.”"]], [["\"Do you mean to say that you are going to throw me off, mamma?\"", "“그럼 엄마는 저를 내치시겠다는 말씀이에요?”"]], [["\"God forbid, my darling.", "“천만에, 우리 딸."], ["It is not mothers that throw off their children.", "제 자식을 내치는 어미는 없단다."], ["What shall I have left when you and Bell are gone from me?\"", "너와 벨이 내게서 떠나고 나면 나한테 무엇이 남겠니?”"]], [["\"But we will never be gone.", "“하지만 우리는 결코 떠나지 않을 거예요."], ["That's what I mean.", "제 말이 그거예요."], ["We are to be just the same to you always, even though we are married.", "우리는 결혼을 하더라도 엄마에게는 언제까지나 똑같아야 해요."], ["I must have my right to be here as much as I have it now; and, in return, you shall have your right to be there.", "저는 지금만큼이나 여기 있을 권리를 가져야 하고, 그 대신 엄마는 저쪽에 계실 권리를 가지셔야 해요."], ["His house must be a home to you,--not a cold place which you may visit now and again, with your best clothes on.", "그이의 집이 엄마에게 한 집이어야 해요. 나들이옷을 차려입고 이따금 한 번씩 찾아가는 서먹한 곳이 아니라요."], ["You know what I mean, when I say that we must not be divided.\"", "우리가 갈라져서는 안 된다고 할 때 제가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 아시겠죠.”"]], [["\"But Lily--\"", "“하지만 릴리—”"]], [["\"Well, mamma?\"", "“네, 엄마?”"]], [["\"I have no doubt we shall be happy together,--you and I.\"", "“우리 둘이야 함께 행복하겠지. 너와 나는 말이다.”"]], [["\"But you were going to say more than that.\"", "“하지만 그보다 더 하실 말씀이 있으셨잖아요.”"]], [["\"Only this,--that your house will be his house, and will be full without me.", "“이것뿐이란다. 네 집은 그이의 집이 될 테고, 내가 없어도 가득 차 있을 게다."], ["A daughter's marriage is always a painful parting.\"", "딸을 시집보내는 일은 언제나 괴로운 이별이지.”"]], [["\"Is it, mamma?\"", "“그런가요, 엄마?”"]], [["\"Not that I would have it otherwise than it is.", "“그렇다고 지금과 달라지기를 바라는 것은 아니란다."], ["Do not think that I would wish to keep you at home with me.", "내가 너를 집에 붙들어 두고 싶어 한다고는 생각하지 마라."], ["Of course you will both marry and leave me.", "물론 너희 둘 다 결혼해서 나를 떠나겠지."], ["I hope that he to whom you are going to devote yourself may be spared to love you and protect you.\"", "네가 몸과 마음을 바치려는 그 사람이 오래 살아남아 너를 사랑하고 지켜 주기를 바랄 뿐이다.”"], ["Then the widow's heart became too full, and she put away her child from her that she might hide her face.", "그러고 나자 미망인의 가슴이 벅차올라, 그녀는 얼굴을 감추려고 아이를 제게서 떼어 놓았다."]], [["\"Mamma, mamma, I wish I was not going from you.\"", "“엄마, 엄마, 저는 엄마 곁을 떠나지 않았으면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["\"No, Lily; do not say that.", "“아니다, 릴리. 그런 말은 하지 마라."], ["I should not be contented with life if I did not see both my girls married.", "내 두 딸이 시집가는 것을 보지 못한다면 나는 이 삶에 만족하지 못할 게다."], ["I think that it is the only lot which can give to a woman perfect content and satisfaction.", "나는 그것이야말로 여자에게 온전한 만족과 흡족함을 줄 수 있는 하나뿐인 몫이라고 생각한단다."], ["I would have you both married.", "나는 너희 둘 다 결혼하기를 바란다."], ["I should be the most selfish being alive if I wished otherwise.\"", "그렇지 않기를 바란다면 나는 세상에서 가장 이기적인 사람일 게다.”"]], [["\"Bell will settle herself near you, and then you will see more of her and love her better than you do me.\"", "“벨 언니는 엄마 가까이에 자리를 잡을 테고, 그러면 엄마는 저보다 언니를 더 자주 보고 더 아끼시게 되겠죠.”"]], [["\"I shall not love her better.\"", "“벨을 더 아끼지는 않을 게다.”"]], [["\"I wish she would marry some London man, and then you would come with us, and be near to us.", "“언니가 런던 사람하고 결혼하면 좋겠어요. 그러면 엄마도 우리와 함께 오셔서 우리 가까이 계실 텐데."], ["Do you know, mamma, I sometimes think you don't like this place here.\"", "있잖아요, 엄마, 저는 가끔 엄마가 이곳을 좋아하지 않으신다는 생각이 들어요.”"]], [["\"Your uncle has been very kind to give it to us.\"", "“너희 삼촌이 이 집을 우리에게 주신 건 참으로 고마운 일이었단다.”"]], [["\"I know he has; and we have been very happy here.", "“저도 알아요. 그리고 우리는 여기서 아주 행복했고요."], ["But if Bell should leave you--\"", "하지만 벨 언니가 엄마 곁을 떠난다면—”"]], [["\"Then should I go also.", "“그러면 나도 떠나야지."], ["Your uncle has been very kind, but I sometimes feel that his kindness is a burden which I should not be strong enough to bear solely on my own shoulders. And what should keep me here, then?\"", "너희 삼촌은 참으로 고마운 분이지만, 나는 이따금 그 고마움이 내 어깨만으로는 짊어질 힘이 모자란 짐이라는 느낌이 든단다."], ["Mrs. Dale as she said this felt that the \"here\"", "그리고 그때가 되면 무엇이 나를 여기 붙들어 두겠니?”"], ["of which she spoke extended beyond the limits of the home which she held through the charity of her brother-in-law.", "데일 부인은 이렇게 말하면서, 자기가 말한 ‘여기’가 시숙의 자비로 얻어 지내는 이 집의 울타리 너머까지 뻗어 있음을 느꼈다."], ["Might not all the world, as far as she was concerned in it, be contained in that \"here\"?", "자기가 몸담은 한에서의 온 세상이 그 ‘여기’ 안에 담겨 있는 것이 아니겠는가?"], ["How was she to live if both her children should be taken away from her?", "두 아이를 다 빼앗기고 나면 자기는 어떻게 살아야 하는가?"], ["She had already realized the fact that Crosbie's house could never be a home to her,--never even a temporary home.", "그녀는 크로즈비의 집이 자기에게 결코 한 집이 될 수 없으리라는 것을, 잠시 머무는 집조차 될 수 없으리라는 것을 이미 깨닫고 있었다."], ["Her visits there must be of that full-dressed nature to which Lily had alluded.", "그 집을 찾아가는 일은 릴리가 빗대어 말한 그 나들이옷 차림의 방문일 수밖에 없었다."], ["It was impossible that she could explain this to Lily.", "이것을 릴리에게 설명하기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["She would not prophesy that the hero of her girl's heart would be inhospitable to his wife's mother; but such had been her reading of Crosbie's character.", "딸의 마음속 영웅이 아내의 어머니에게 매정하리라고 미리 점칠 생각은 없었다. 그러나 그녀가 읽어 낸 크로즈비의 사람됨이 그러했다."], ["Alas, alas, as matters were to go, his hospitality or inhospitality would be matter of small moment to them.", "아아, 슬프도다, 일이 앞으로 흘러갈 모양대로라면 그의 후한 대접이든 매정함이든 그들에게는 대수롭지 않은 일이 될 터였다."]], [["Again in the afternoon the two sisters were together, and Lily was still more serious than her wont.", "오후에 다시 두 자매가 함께 있게 되었는데, 릴리는 여느 때보다도 더 진지했다."], ["It might almost have been gathered from her manner that this marriage of hers was about to take place at once, and that she was preparing to leave her home. \"Bell,\"", "그 태도만 보아서는 그녀의 결혼이 당장이라도 치러질 참이고 그녀가 집을 떠날 채비를 하고 있다고 여겨질 정도였다."], ["she said, \"I wonder why Dr.", "“언니,” 그녀가 말했다."], ["Crofts never comes to see us now?\"", "“크로프츠 선생은 요즘 왜 통 우리를 보러 오지 않을까?”"]], [["\"It isn't a month since he was here, at our party.\"", "“여기 오신 지 한 달도 안 됐어. 우리 모임 때 오셨잖아.”"]], [["\"A month!", "“한 달이라니!"], ["But there was a time when he made some pretext for being here every other day.\"", "하지만 이틀에 한 번꼴로 무슨 핑계를 만들어서라도 여기 오시던 때가 있었잖아.”"]], [["\"Yes, when mamma was ill.\"", "“그래, 엄마가 편찮으셨을 때지.”"]], [["\"Ay, and since mamma was well, too.", "“그리고 엄마가 나으신 뒤에도 그랬어."], ["But I suppose I must not break the promise you made me give you.", "하지만 언니가 나한테 하게 만든 그 약속을 내가 깨서는 안 되겠지."], ["He's not to be talked about even yet, is he?\"", "아직도 그분 이야기는 하면 안 되는 거야?”"]], [["\"I didn't say he was not to be talked about.", "“이야기하면 안 된다고는 하지 않았어."], ["You know what I meant, Lily; and what I meant then, I mean now.\"", "내가 무슨 뜻으로 그랬는지 너도 알잖니, 릴리. 그때 내 뜻이 지금도 내 뜻이고.”"]], [["\"And how long will it be before you mean something else?", "“그러면 언니 뜻이 달라지려면 얼마나 더 걸릴까?"], ["I do hope it will come some day,--I do indeed.\"", "언젠가는 그렇게 되면 좋겠어. 정말 그러면 좋겠어.”"]], [["\"It never will, Lily.", "“결코 그렇게 되지 않을 거야, 릴리."], ["I once fancied that I cared for Dr. Crofts, but it was only fancy.", "나도 한때는 크로프츠 선생을 마음에 두고 있다고 여겼지만, 그건 그저 여겨 본 것뿐이었어."], ["I know it, because--\"", "나는 그걸 알아. 왜냐하면—”"], ["She was going to explain that her knowledge on that point was assured to her, because since that day she had felt that she might have learned to love another man.", "그녀는 그 점에 대한 자기 앎이 어째서 확실한지를 설명하려던 참이었다. 그날 이후로 자기가 다른 남자를 사랑하게 될 수도 있었겠다고 느꼈기 때문이라고."], ["But that other man had been Mr. Crosbie, and so she stopped herself.", "그러나 그 다른 남자란 크로즈비 씨였으므로, 그녀는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["\"I wish he would come and ask you himself.\"", "“그분이 와서 언니한테 직접 물어봐 주면 좋겠어.”"]], [["\"He will never do so.", "“그분은 결코 그러지 않을 거야."], ["He would never ask such a question without encouragement, and I shall give him none.", "부추김이 없으면 그런 물음은 결코 던지지 않을 분이고, 나는 부추길 마음이 없어."], ["Nor will he ever think of marrying till he can do so without,--without what he thinks to be imprudence as regards money.", "그리고 돈 문제에서 경솔하다고 스스로 여기지 않을 만큼 되기 전에는 결혼을 생각조차 하지 않으실 거야."], ["He has courage enough to be poor himself without unhappiness, but he has not courage to endure poverty with a wife.", "그분은 혼자서 가난하게 지내면서도 불행해하지 않을 용기는 있지만, 아내를 데리고 가난을 견딜 용기는 없어."], ["I know well what his feelings are.\"", "나는 그분 마음을 잘 알아.”"]], [["\"Well, we shall see,\"", "“그럼 두고 보자.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"I shouldn't wonder if you were married first now, Bell.", "“이제 언니가 먼저 결혼한다 해도 나는 놀라지 않을 거야, 벨 언니."], ["For my part I'm quite prepared to wait for three years.\"", "내 쪽은 삼 년이라도 기다릴 각오가 아주 되어 있으니까.”"]], [["Late on that evening the squire returned to Allington, Bernard having driven over to meet him at the station.", "그날 저녁 늦게 지주가 앨링턴으로 돌아왔고, 버나드가 이륜마차를 몰아 역으로 그를 맞으러 나갔다."], ["He had telegraphed to his nephew that he would be back by a late train, and no more than this had been heard from him since he went.", "그는 늦은 기차로 돌아가겠다고 조카에게 전보를 쳤는데, 떠난 뒤로 그에게서 들려온 소식이라고는 그것뿐이었다."], ["On that day Bernard had seen none of the ladies at the Small House.", "그날 버나드는 작은 집의 여인들을 아무도 만나지 않았다."], ["With Bell at the present moment it was impossible that he should be on easy terms.", "지금 형편에서 벨과 허물없이 지내기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["He could not meet her alone without recurring to the one special subject of interest between them, and as to that he did not choose to speak without much forethought.", "그녀를 단둘이 만나면 두 사람 사이의 그 한 가지 관심사로 되돌아가지 않을 수 없었고, 그 일에 대해서는 깊이 헤아려 보지 않고 말할 마음이 없었다."], ["He had not known himself, when he had gone about his wooing so lightly, thinking it a slight thing, whether or no he might be accepted.", "청혼을 그토록 가볍게, 대수롭지 않은 일로 여기며 밀고 나갔을 때만 해도, 받아들여지든 말든 그는 제 마음을 알지 못했다."], ["Now it was no longer a slight thing to him.", "이제 그것은 그에게 더 이상 대수롭지 않은 일이 아니었다."], ["I do not know that it was love that made him so eager; not good, honest, downright love.", "그를 그토록 조바심 나게 한 것이 사랑이었는지 나는 알지 못한다. 착하고 정직하고 온전한 사랑 말이다."], ["But he had set his heart upon the object, and with the wilfulness of a Dale was determined that it should be his.", "그러나 그는 그 목표에 마음을 붙박아 두었고, 데일 집안사람다운 고집으로 그것이 제 것이 되어야 한다고 굳게 마음먹고 있었다."], ["He had no remotest idea of giving up his cousin, but he had at last persuaded himself that she was not to be won without some toil, and perhaps also some delay.", "사촌을 포기할 생각은 털끝만큼도 없었으나, 그녀를 얻자면 얼마간의 수고가, 어쩌면 얼마간의 기다림도 있어야 하리라고 마침내 스스로를 납득시켜 두고 있었다."]], [["Nor had he been in a humour to talk either to Mrs. Dale or to Lily.", "그는 데일 부인에게도 릴리에게도 말을 붙일 기분이 아니었다."], ["He feared that Lady Julia's news was true,--that at any rate there might be in it something of truth; and while thus in doubt he could not go down to the Small House.", "그는 레이디 줄리아의 소식이 사실일까 봐, 적어도 거기에 얼마쯤은 사실이 섞여 있을까 봐 두려웠다. 그렇게 미심쩍어하는 동안에는 작은 집으로 내려갈 수가 없었다."], ["So he hung about the place by himself, with a cigar in his mouth, fearing that something evil was going to happen, and when the message came for him, almost shuddered as he seated himself in the gig.", "그래서 그는 입에 시가을 문 채 혼자서 그 언저리를 서성거리며 무슨 나쁜 일이 벌어지려나 보다 하고 두려워했고, 자기를 부르는 전갈이 왔을 때는 이륜마차에 올라앉으며 하마터면 몸서리를 칠 뻔했다."], ["What would it become him to do in this emergency if Crosbie had truly been guilty of the villany with which Lady Julia had charged him?", "레이디 줄리아가 크로즈비에게 씌운 그 몹쓸 짓을 그가 정말로 저질렀다면, 이 다급한 처지에서 자기는 어떻게 하는 것이 마땅한가?"], ["Thirty years ago he would have called the man out, and shot at him till one of them was hit.", "삼십 년 전이었다면 그는 그 사람을 결투에 불러내어 둘 중 하나가 총알을 맞을 때까지 쏘았을 것이다."], ["Now-a-days it was hardly possible for a man to do that; and yet what would the world say of him if he allowed such an injury as this to pass without vengeance?", "요즈음에는 사람이 그렇게 하기란 거의 불가능했다. 그런데도 이런 모욕을 앙갚음 없이 넘긴다면 세상은 그를 두고 무어라 할 것인가?"]], [["His uncle, as he came forth from the station with his travelling-bag in his hand, was stern, gloomy, and silent.", "삼촌은 여행 가방을 손에 들고 역에서 걸어 나오는데, 엄하고 침울하고 말이 없었다."], ["He came out and took his place in the gig almost without speaking.", "그는 나와서 거의 한마디도 없이 이륜마차에 자리를 잡았다."], ["There were strangers about, and therefore his nephew at first could ask no question, but as the gig turned the corner out of the station-house yard he demanded the news.", "둘레에 낯선 사람들이 있었으므로 조카는 처음에는 아무것도 물을 수 없었으나, 이륜마차가 역사 앞마당을 벗어나 모퉁이를 도는 참에 소식을 물었다."]], [["\"What have you heard?\"", "“무엇을 들으셨습니까?”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["But even then the squire did not answer at once.", "그러나 그때에도 지주는 곧바로 대답하지 않았다."], ["He shook his head, and turned away his face, as though he did not choose to be interrogated.", "그는 고개를 저었고, 캐물음을 받고 싶지 않다는 듯 얼굴을 돌렸다."]], [["\"Have you seen him, sir?\"", "“그를 만나셨습니까, 어르신?”"], ["asked Bernard.", "버나드가 물었다."]], [["\"No, he has not dared to see me.\"", "“아니다. 그자는 감히 나를 만나지 못하더구나.”"]], [["\"Then it is true?\"", "“그러면 사실이군요?”"]], [["\"True?--yes, it is all true.", "“사실이냐고? 그래, 죄다 사실이다."], ["Why did you bring the scoundrel here?", "어쩌자고 너는 그 악당을 여기 데려왔느냐?"], ["It has been your fault.\"", "이건 네 잘못이야.”"]], [["\"No, sir; I must contradict that.", "“아닙니다, 어르신. 그 말씀에는 반박해야겠습니다."], ["I did not know him for a scoundrel.\"", "저는 그가 악당인 줄 몰랐습니다.”"]], [["\"But it was your duty to have known him before you brought him here among them.", "“하지만 그를 여기 저 아이들 사이로 데려오기 전에 그가 어떤 사람인지 알아 두는 것이 네 도리였다. 가엾은 것!"], ["Poor girl! how is she to be told?\"", "그 아이에게 이 일을 어떻게 알린단 말이냐?”"]], [["\"Then she does not know it?\"", "“그럼 그 아이는 아직 모른단 말씀입니까?”"]], [["\"I fear not.", "“모르는 듯싶어 걱정이구나."], ["Have you seen them?\"", "너는 그 애들을 보았느냐?”"]], [["\"I saw them yesterday, and she did not know it then; she may have heard it to-day.\"", "“어제 보았습니다만, 그때는 몰랐습니다. 오늘 들었을지도 모르지요.”"]], [["\"I don't think so.", "“그러지는 않았을 게다."], ["I believe he has been too great a coward to write to her.", "내 생각에 그자는 겁이 너무 많아서 그 아이에게 편지조차 못 썼을 거다."], ["A coward indeed!", "겁쟁이라니, 참으로 겁쟁이지!"], ["How can any man find the courage to write such a letter as that?\"", "어떤 사내가 그런 편지를 쓸 용기를 낼 수 있단 말이냐?”"]], [["By degrees the squire told his tale.", "지주는 차츰차츰 제 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["How he had gone to Lady Julia, had made his way to London, had tracked Crosbie to his club, and had there learned the whole truth from Crosbie's friend, Fowler Pratt, we already know.", "그가 레이디 줄리아를 찾아갔던 일, 런던으로 올라갔던 일, 크로즈비를 그의 클럽까지 뒤쫓아 갔던 일, 그리고 거기서 크로즈비의 벗 파울러 프랫에게서 진상을 죄다 알게 된 일은 우리가 이미 아는 바다."], ["\"The coward escaped me while I was talking to the man he sent down,\"", "“그자가 내려보낸 사람과 내가 이야기하는 동안에 그 겁쟁이는 나를 피해 달아났다.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"It was a concerted plan, and I think he was right.", "“미리 짜 둔 계략이었지. 그리고 나는 그자가 옳았다고 본다."], ["I should have brained him in the hall of the club.\"", "나는 클럽 현관에서 그자의 머리통을 부숴 놓았을 테니까.”"], ["On the following morning Pratt had called upon him at his inn with Crosbie's apology.", "이튿날 아침에 프랫이 크로즈비의 사과문을 들고 그가 묵는 여관으로 찾아왔었다."], ["\"His apology!\"", "“사과문이라니!”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"I have it in my pocket.", "“그게 내 주머니에 있다."], ["Poor reptile; wretched worm of a man!", "가련한 파충류 같으니, 벌레만도 못한 가엾은 인간!"], ["I cannot understand it.", "나는 이 일을 이해할 수가 없다."], ["On my honour, Bernard, I do not understand it.", "명예를 걸고 말하는데, 버나드, 나는 이 일을 이해할 수가 없다."], ["I think men are changed since I knew much of them.", "내가 사람들을 잘 알던 시절 이후로 사람이 달라진 모양이다."], ["It would have been impossible for me to write such a letter as that.\"", "나로서는 그런 편지를 쓴다는 것이 있을 수 없는 일이었을 게다.”"], ["He went on telling how Pratt had brought him this letter, and had stated that Crosbie declined an interview.", "그는 프랫이 이 편지를 가져왔고 크로즈비가 만나기를 마다한다고 전했다는 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["\"The gentleman had the goodness to assure me that no good could come from such a meeting. 'You mean,'", "“그 신사분께서는 친절하게도 그런 만남에서는 좋은 것이 나올 수 없다고 나를 안심시켜 주더구나."], ["I answered, 'that I cannot touch pitch and not be defiled!' He acknowledged that the man was pitch.", "‘그러니까 자네 말은,’ 내가 대답했지, ‘내가 역청을 만지고서 더럽혀지지 않을 수는 없다는 뜻이로군!’ 그자도 그 사람이 역청이라는 것은 인정하더라."], ["Indeed, he could not say a word for his friend.\"", "참으로, 제 벗을 두고 한마디도 변명하지 못했으니까.”"]], [["\"I know Pratt.", "“저는 프랫을 압니다."], ["He is a gentleman.", "그는 신사입니다."], ["I am sure he would not excuse him.\"", "그가 그자를 두둔했을 리는 없다고 저는 확신합니다.”"]], [["\"Excuse him!", "“두둔하다니!"], ["How could any one excuse him?", "누가 그자를 두둔할 수 있겠느냐?"], ["Words could not be found to excuse him.\"", "그자를 두둔할 말이란 찾아낼 수 없을 게다.”"], ["And then he sat silent for some half mile.", "그러고서 그는 반 마일쯤 되는 거리를 말없이 앉아 있었다."], ["\"On my honour, Bernard, I can hardly yet bring myself to believe it.", "“명예를 걸고 말하는데, 버나드, 나는 아직도 이 일을 좀처럼 믿을 수가 없구나."], ["It is so new to me.", "나에게는 너무나 낯선 일이다."], ["It makes me feel that the world is changed, and that it is no longer worth a man's while to live in it.\"", "세상이 달라졌다는 느낌, 이제는 사람이 이 세상에서 살아갈 값어치가 없다는 느낌이 든다.”"]], [["\"And he is engaged to this other girl?\"", "“그러면 그자가 그 다른 아가씨와 약혼했다는 말씀입니까?”"]], [["\"Oh, yes; with the full consent of the family.", "“오, 그렇다. 그 집안이 죄다 찬성한 채로 말이다."], ["It is all arranged, and the settlements, no doubt, in the lawyer's hands by this time.", "다 정해졌고, 혼인 재산 계약서도 이맘때면 벌써 변호사 손에 넘어가 있겠지."], ["He must have gone away from here determined to throw her over.", "그자는 여기를 떠날 때 이미 그 아이를 저버리기로 작정하고 갔음이 틀림없다."], ["Indeed, I don't suppose he ever meant to marry her.", "사실 나는 그자가 애초에 그 아이와 결혼할 마음을 품기나 했는지 모르겠다."], ["He was just passing away his time here in the country.\"", "그자는 그저 시골에서 한때를 흘려보내고 있었던 게야.”"]], [["\"He meant it up to the time of his leaving.\"", "“떠나실 때까지는 그럴 마음이 있었습니다.”"]], [["\"I don't think it.", "“나는 그렇게 생각하지 않는다."], ["Had he found me able and willing to give her a fortune he might, perhaps, have married her.", "내가 그 아이에게 재산을 줄 수 있고 또 줄 마음도 있다는 것을 알았더라면 그자는 어쩌면 그 아이와 결혼했을지도 모르지."], ["But I don't think he meant it for a moment after I told him that she would have nothing.", "하지만 그 아이에게 아무것도 없을 거라고 내가 말한 뒤로는 그자가 한순간도 그럴 마음이 없었다고 나는 생각한다."], ["Well, here we are.", "자, 다 왔구나."], ["I may truly say that I never before came back to my own house with so sore a heart.\"", "내 집으로 돌아오면서 이토록 마음이 쓰라렸던 적은 정말이지 여태 없었다.”"]], [["They sat silently over their supper, the squire showing more open sorrow than might have been expected from his character.", "두 사람은 말없이 저녁을 먹었는데, 지주는 그 성품에서 짐작할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 드러내 놓고 슬퍼했다. “아침에 그들에게 무어라고 말한단 말이냐?”"], ["\"What am I to say to them in the morning?\"", "그는 몇 번이고 되뇌었다."], ["he repeated over and over again.", "“그 일을 내가 어떻게 한단 말이냐?"], ["\"How am I to do it? And if I tell the mother, how is she to tell her child?\"", "그리고 어미에게 말한들, 어미가 제 아이에게 그것을 어떻게 말한단 말이냐?”"]], [["\"Do you think that he has given no intimation of his purpose?\"", "“그자가 제 뜻을 조금도 내비치지 않았다고 보십니까?”"]], [["\"As far as I can tell, none.", "“내가 아는 한에서는 조금도 없다."], ["That man Pratt knew that he had not done so yesterday afternoon.", "그 프랫이라는 사람도 어제 오후까지는 그자가 그러지 않았다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["I asked him what were the intentions of his blackguard friend, and he said that he did not know--that Crosbie would probably have written to me.", "나는 그자의 그 불한당 같은 벗이 어쩔 작정인지 물었는데, 그는 모르겠다고, 크로즈비가 아마 내게 편지를 썼을 거라고 하더구나."], ["Then he brought me this letter.", "그러고 나서 그가 내게 이 편지를 가져왔다."], ["There it is,\"", "자, 여기 있다.”"], ["and the squire threw the letter over the table; \"read it and let me have it back.", "그러면서 지주는 그 편지를 탁자 너머로 던졌다. “읽어 보고 나한테 도로 다오."], ["He thinks probably that the trouble is now over as far as he is concerned.\"", "그자는 아마 이제 제 몫의 성가신 일은 끝났다고 여기고 있겠지.”"]], [["It was a vile letter to have written--not because the language was bad, or the mode of expression unfeeling, or the facts falsely stated--but because the thing to be told was in itself so vile.", "그것은 써서는 안 될 더러운 편지였다. 말씨가 나빠서도, 표현하는 방식이 매정해서도, 사실을 거짓으로 적어서도 아니라, 알려야 할 그 일 자체가 그토록 더러웠기 때문이다."], ["There are deeds which will not bear a gloss--sins as to which the perpetrator cannot speak otherwise than as a reptile; circumstances which change a man and put upon him the worthlessness of vermin.", "아무리 겉을 발라도 가려지지 않는 짓들이 있다. 저지른 자가 파충류로서밖에는 달리 말할 수 없는 죄들이 있고, 사람을 바꾸어 놓고 그에게 해충 같은 하찮음을 씌워 버리는 사정들이 있는 것이다."], ["Crosbie had struggled hard to write it, going home to do it after his last interview on that night with Pratt.", "크로즈비는 그것을 쓰느라 몹시 애를 먹었다. 그날 밤 프랫과 마지막으로 만난 뒤에 그것을 쓰려고 집으로 갔던 것이다."], ["But he had sat moodily in his chair at his lodgings, unable to take the pen in his hand.", "그러나 그는 하숙집 의자에 시무룩하게 앉은 채 펜을 손에 쥐지 못했다."], ["Pratt was to come to him at his office on the following morning, and he went to bed resolving that he would write it at his desk.", "프랫이 이튿날 아침에 그의 사무실로 찾아오기로 되어 있었으므로, 그는 책상에서 그것을 쓰리라 마음먹고 잠자리에 들었다."], ["On the next day Pratt was there before a word of it had been written.", "이튿날 프랫이 왔을 때에도 그것은 한 글자조차 쓰여 있지 않았다."]], [["\"I can't stand this kind of thing,\"", "“나는 이런 짓은 못 참겠네.”"], ["said Pratt.", "프랫이 말했다."], ["\"If you mean me to take it, you must write it at once.\"", "“내가 그것을 가져가기를 바란다면 자네는 지금 당장 써야 하네.”"], ["Then, with inward groaning, Crosbie sat himself at his table, and the words at last were forthcoming.", "그러자 크로즈비는 속으로 끙끙 앓으면서 제 탁자 앞에 앉았고, 마침내 말들이 나왔다."], ["Such words as they were!", "그런 꼴의 말들이!"], ["\"I know that I can have no excuse to make to you--or to her.", "“저로서는 어르신께—아니, 그분께도 드릴 변명이 있을 수 없다는 것을 압니다."], ["But, circumstanced as I now am, the truth is the best.", "그러나 지금 제가 처한 사정에서는 진실이 가장 낫습니다."], ["I feel that I should not make Miss Dale happy; and, therefore, as an honest man, I think I best do my duty by relinquishing the honour which she and you had proposed for me.\"", "저는 제가 데일 양을 행복하게 해 드리지 못하리라고 느낍니다. 그러므로 정직한 사람으로서, 그분과 어르신께서 제게 베풀고자 하신 그 영예를 내려놓는 것이 제 도리를 가장 잘 다하는 길이라고 생각합니다.”"], ["There was more of it, but we all know of what words such letters are composed, and how men write when they feel themselves constrained to write as reptiles.", "그 뒤로도 더 있었으나, 그런 편지들이 어떤 말로 이루어지는지, 그리고 사람이 스스로 파충류로서 쓸 수밖에 없다고 느낄 때 어떻게 쓰는지는 우리 모두가 아는 바다."]], [["\"As an honest man!\"", "“정직한 사람으로서라니!”"], ["repeated the squire.", "지주가 되뇌었다."], ["\"On my honour, Bernard, as a gentleman, I do not understand it.", "“명예를 걸고, 신사로서 말하는데, 버나드, 나는 이 일을 이해할 수가 없다."], ["I cannot believe it possible that the man who wrote that letter was sitting the other day as a guest at my table.\"", "저 편지를 쓴 사내가 바로 며칠 전에 내 식탁에 손님으로 앉아 있었다니, 나로서는 도무지 믿을 수가 없구나.”"]], [["\"What are we to do to him?\"", "“우리가 그자를 어떻게 해야 합니까?”"], ["said Bernard, after a while.", "얼마쯤 있다가 버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"Treat him as you would a rat.", "“쥐를 다루듯 다루어라."], ["Throw your stick at him, if he comes under your feet; but beware, above all things, that he does not get into your house.", "발밑으로 기어들거든 지팡이를 던지고. 그러나 무엇보다도 그자가 네 집 안으로 들어오지 못하게 조심해라."], ["That is too late for us now.\"", "우리에게는 이미 너무 늦은 일이다만.”"]], [["\"There must be more than that, uncle.\"", "“그것보다는 더 있어야 합니다, 삼촌.”"]], [["\"I don't know what more.", "“나도 더 무엇이 있는지 모르겠다."], ["There are deeds for committing which a man is doubly damned, because he has screened himself from overt punishment by the nature of his own villany.", "저지르고 나면 갑절로 저주받는 짓들이 있는 법이다. 제 악행의 성질 그 자체 덕분에 드러내 놓고 받는 벌은 면해 버리니까 말이다."], ["We have to remember Lily's name, and do what may best tend to her comfort.", "우리는 릴리의 이름을 생각해야 하고, 그 아이가 편안해지는 데 가장 도움이 될 일을 해야 한다."], ["Poor girl!", "가엾은 것!"], ["poor girl!\"", "가엾은 것!”"]], [["Then they were silent, till the squire rose and took his bed candle.", "그러고서 두 사람은 말이 없었고, 이윽고 지주가 일어나 제 침실 촛대를 집어 들었다."], ["\"Bernard,\"", "“버나드,”"], ["he said, \"let my sister-in-law know early to-morrow that I will see her here, if she will be good enough to come to me after breakfast.", "그가 말했다. “내일 아침 일찍 제수한테, 아침 식사 뒤에 여기로 와 주면 고맙겠으니 내가 여기서 만나겠다고 알려 다오."], ["Do not have anything else said at the Small House.", "작은 집에서 그 밖의 말은 하지 마라."], ["It may be that he has written to-day.\"", "어쩌면 그자가 오늘 편지를 썼을지도 모르니.”"]], [["Then the squire went to bed, and Bernard sat over the dining-room fire, meditating on it all.", "그러고서 지주는 잠자리에 들었고, 버나드는 식당 난롯가에 앉아 이 모든 일을 곰곰 생각했다."], ["How would the world expect that he should behave to Crosbie?", "세상은 그가 크로즈비에게 어떻게 처신하기를 바랄 것인가?"], ["and what should he do when he met Crosbie at the club?", "그리고 클럽에서 크로즈비와 마주치면 자기는 어떻게 해야 하는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER XXVIII. THE BOARD.", "# 제28장. 위원회"]], [["Crosbie, as we already know, went to his office in Whitehall on the morning after his escape from Sebright's, at which establishment he left the Squire of Allington in conference with Fowler Pratt.", "크로즈비는, 우리가 이미 아는 대로, 세브라이트에서 빠져나온 이튿날 아침 화이트홀의 제 사무국으로 갔다. 그는 그 클럽에 앨링턴의 지주를 파울러 프랫과 이야기하도록 남겨 두고 나왔던 것이다."], ["He had seen Fowler Pratt again that same night, and the course of the story will have shown what took place at that interview.", "그날 밤 그는 파울러 프랫을 다시 만났고, 그 자리에서 무슨 일이 오갔는지는 이야기의 흐름이 이미 보여 주었을 것이다."]], [["He went early to his office, knowing that he had before him the work of writing two letters, neither of which would run very glibly from his pen.", "그는 일찍 사무국으로 나갔다. 편지 두 통을 써야 한다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문인데, 어느 쪽도 그의 펜에서 술술 흘러나올 편지는 아니었다."], ["One was to be his missive to the squire, to be delivered by his friend; the other, that fatal epistle to poor Lily, which, as the day passed away, he found himself utterly unable to accomplish.", "하나는 벗의 손에 들려 지주에게 전할 서한이었고, 다른 하나는 가엾은 릴리에게 보낼 그 끔찍한 편지였는데, 하루가 다 저물도록 그는 도무지 그것을 써낼 수가 없었다."], ["The letter to the squire he did write, under certain threats; and, as we have seen, was considered to have degraded himself to the vermin rank of humanity by the meanness of his production.", "지주에게 보내는 편지는 어떤 위협 아래 쓰기는 썼다. 그리고 우리가 본 대로, 그 글의 비루함 때문에 그는 인간 가운데 버러지의 등급으로 스스로를 떨어뜨렸다는 평을 들었다."]], [["But on reaching his office he found that other cares awaited him,--cares which he would have taken much delight in bearing, had the state of his mind enabled him to take delight in anything.", "그러나 사무국에 이르러 보니 다른 근심거리들이 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그의 마음이 무엇에서든 기쁨을 느낄 수 있는 상태이기만 했더라면 짊어지기에 몹시 즐거웠을 근심거리였다."], ["On entering the lobby of his office, at ten o'clock, he became aware that he was received by the messengers assembled there with almost more than their usual deference.", "열 시에 사무국 현관으로 들어서면서 그는 거기 모여 있던 사환들이 여느 때보다도 더한 공손함으로 자기를 맞이한다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["He was always a great man at the General Committee Office; but there are shades of greatness and shades of deference, which, though quite beyond the powers of definition, nevertheless manifest themselves clearly to the experienced ear and eye.", "그는 일반위원회 사무국에서 늘 대단한 사람이었다. 그러나 대단함에도 등급이 있고 공손함에도 등급이 있어서, 도무지 말로 규정할 수는 없으되 겪어 본 귀와 눈에는 뚜렷이 드러나는 법이다."], ["He walked through to his own apartment, and there found two official letters addressed to him lying on his table.", "그는 곧장 제 방으로 걸어 들어갔고, 거기 탁자 위에 제 앞으로 온 공문 두 통이 놓여 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The first which came to hand, though official, was small, and marked private, and it was addressed in the handwriting of his old friend, Butterwell, the outgoing secretary.", "먼저 손에 잡힌 것은 공문이기는 하되 작았고 사사로운 편지라는 표시가 붙어 있었으며, 겉봉의 글씨는 오랜 벗이자 물러나는 사무국장인 버터웰의 것이었다."], ["\"I shall see you in the morning, nearly as soon as you get this,\"", "“이 글을 받아 보는 것과 거의 동시에 아침에 만나게 되겠지만,”"], ["said the semi-official note; \"but I must be the first to congratulate you on the acquisition of my old shoes.", "그 반쯤 공적인 쪽지는 이렇게 말하고 있었다. “그래도 내 헌 신발을 물려받게 된 것을 축하하는 사람은 내가 첫 번째여야겠네."], ["They will be very easy in the wearing to you, though they pinched my corns a little at first.", "처음에는 내 티눈을 조금 죄었네만, 자네가 신으면 아주 편할 걸세. 밑창은 새로 대야 할 테고 뒤축도 좀 닳았을 게야."], ["I dare say they want new soling, and perhaps they are a little down at heels; but you will find some excellent cobbler to make them all right, and will give them a grace in the wearing which they have sadly lacked since they came into my possession.", "하지만 자네라면 훌륭한 구둣방을 찾아 다 손보게 하겠지. 그리고 그 신발이 내 손에 들어온 뒤로 애석하게도 없었던 품위를, 자네가 신음으로써 얹어 줄 걸세."], ["I wish you much joy with them,\" &c., &c.", "그 신발로 큰 기쁨을 누리기 바라네,”"], ["He then opened the larger official letter, but that had now but little interest for him.", "등등, 등등. 그러고 나서 그는 더 큰 공문을 뜯었으나, 그것은 이제 그에게 거의 흥미가 없었다."], ["He could have made a copy of the contents without seeing them.", "보지 않고도 그 내용을 그대로 베껴 쓸 수 있을 정도였다."], ["The Board of Commissioners had had great pleasure in promoting him to the office of secretary, vacated by the promotion of Mr.", "위원들의 위원회는 버터웰 씨가 그들 자신의 위원회 자리로 올라가면서 비게 된 사무국장직에 그를 승진시키게 되어 대단히 기쁘다는 것이었다."], ["Butterwell to a seat at their own Board; and then the letter was signed by Mr. Butterwell himself.", "그리고 그 편지에는 버터웰 씨 자신의 서명이 있었다."]], [["How delightful to him would have been this welcome on his return to his office had his heart in other respects been free from care!", "다른 일들에서 그의 마음이 근심으로부터 자유로웠더라면, 사무국으로 돌아온 그를 맞이한 이 환대가 얼마나 기꺼웠으랴!"], ["And as he thought of this, he remembered all Lily's charms.", "그리고 그것을 생각하다가 그는 릴리의 온갖 사랑스러움을 떠올렸다."], ["He told himself how much she excelled the noble scion of the De Courcy stock, with whom he was now destined to mate himself; how the bride he had rejected excelled the one he had chosen in grace, beauty, faith, freshness, and all feminine virtues.", "이제 제 짝으로 삼을 운명이 된 드 코시 가문의 고귀한 자손보다 그 아이가 얼마나 나은지를, 그가 버린 신부가 그가 고른 신부보다 우아함에서, 아름다움에서, 신실함에서, 싱그러움에서, 그리고 여인의 온갖 덕에서 얼마나 나은지를 그는 스스로에게 일러 주었다."], ["If he could only wipe out the last fortnight from the facts of his existence!", "지난 두 주만 제 삶의 사실에서 지워 버릴 수 있다면!"], ["But fortnights such as those are not to be wiped out,--not even with many sorrowful years of tedious scrubbing.", "그러나 그런 두 주는 지워지지 않는 법이다. 여러 해를 슬픔 속에서 지겹도록 문질러 대도 지워지지 않는다."]], [["And at this moment it seemed to him as though all those impediments which had frightened him when he had thought of marrying Lily Dale were withdrawn.", "그리고 이 순간 그에게는, 릴리 데일과 혼인하는 일을 생각할 때 그를 겁먹게 했던 온갖 장애물이 죄다 걷힌 것처럼 보였다."], ["That which would have been terrible with seven or eight hundred a year, would have been made delightful with twelve or thirteen.", "연 칠팔백 파운드로는 끔찍했을 일이 천이삼백 파운드로는 즐거운 일이 되었을 터였다."], ["Why had his fate been so unkind to him?", "어찌하여 운명은 그에게 그토록 모질었을까?"], ["Why had not this promotion come to him but one fortnight earlier?", "어찌하여 이 승진이 꼭 두 주만 일찍 오지 않았을까?"], ["Why had it not been declared before he had made his visit to that terrible castle?", "어찌하여 그가 그 무시무시한 성을 찾아가기 전에 발표되지 않았을까?"], ["He even said to himself that if he had positively known the fact before Pratt had seen Mr.", "그는 심지어 스스로에게 이렇게까지 말했다."], ["Dale, he would have sent a different message to the squire, and would have braved the anger of all the race of the De Courcys.", "프랫이 데일 씨를 만나기 전에 이 사실을 확실히 알았더라면 자기는 지주에게 다른 전갈을 보냈을 것이고, 드 코시 일족 전부의 노여움도 맞받았으리라고."], ["But in that he lied to himself, and he knew that he did so.", "그러나 그 대목에서 그는 스스로에게 거짓말을 하고 있었고, 자기가 그런다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["An earl, in his imagination, was hedged by so strong a divinity, that his treason towards Alexandrina could do no more than peep at what it would.", "그의 상상 속에서 백작이란 어찌나 굳센 신성으로 둘려 있는지, 알렉산드리나에게 반역할 마음이 있어도 기껏 넘겨다보는 것이 고작이었다."], ["It had been considered but little by him, when the project first offered itself to his mind, to jilt the niece of a small rural squire; but it was not in him to jilt the daughter of a countess.", "처음 그 계획이 머릿속에 떠올랐을 때 시골 소지주의 조카딸을 차 버리는 일은 그에게 대수롭지 않게 여겨졌다. 그러나 백작부인의 딸을 차 버리는 일은 그로서는 할 수 없는 짓이었다."]], [["That house full of babies in St. John's Wood appeared to him now under a very different guise from that which it wore as he sat in his room at Courcy Castle on the evening of his arrival there.", "세인트존스우드의 아이들로 가득한 그 집이 이제 그에게는, 코시 성에 닿던 날 저녁 제 방에 앉아 그려 보던 것과는 아주 다른 모습으로 보였다."], ["Then such an establishment had to him the flavour of a graveyard.", "그때 그런 살림은 그에게 무덤 냄새를 풍겼다."], ["It was as though he were going to bury himself alive.", "산 채로 제 몸을 파묻는 일 같았다."], ["Now that it was out of his reach, he thought of it as a paradise upon earth.", "이제 그것이 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 가 버리니, 그는 그것을 지상의 낙원으로 여겼다."], ["And then he considered what sort of a paradise Lady Alexandrina would make for him.", "그러고는 레이디 알렉산드리나가 어떤 낙원을 만들어 줄 것인지를 헤아려 보았다."], ["It was astonishing how ugly was the Lady Alexandrina, how old, how graceless, how destitute of all pleasant charm, seen through the spectacles which he wore at the present moment.", "지금 이 순간 그가 쓰고 있는 안경으로 내다보니, 레이디 알렉산드리나는 놀랍도록 못생겼고, 놀랍도록 늙었으며, 놀랍도록 볼품없었고, 사람을 끄는 매력이라고는 놀랍도록 없었다."]], [["During his first hour at the office he did nothing.", "사무국에서의 첫 한 시간 동안 그는 아무 일도 하지 않았다."], ["One or two of the younger clerks came in and congratulated him with much heartiness.", "젊은 서기 한둘이 들어와 아주 진심으로 축하해 주었다."], ["He was popular at his office, and they had got a step by his promotion.", "그는 사무국에서 인기가 있었고, 그의 승진으로 그들도 한 계단씩 올라선 참이었다."], ["Then he met one or two of the elder clerks, and was congratulated with much less heartiness.", "그다음에는 나이 든 서기 한둘을 만났는데, 그쪽의 축하에는 진심이 훨씬 덜했다."], ["\"I suppose it's all right,\"", "“다 옳은 일이겠지요.”"], ["said one bluff old gentleman.", "무뚝뚝한 노신사 하나가 말했다."], ["\"My time is gone by, I know.", "“내 때는 지나갔다는 걸 나도 압니다."], ["I married too early to be able to wear a good coat when I was young, and I never was acquainted with any lords or lords' families.\"", "나는 너무 일찍 혼인하는 바람에 젊었을 적에 좋은 외투 한 벌 걸칠 수가 없었고, 경이니 경의 집안이니 하는 이들과는 사귈 일이 없었지요.”"], ["The sting of this was the sharper because Crosbie had begun to feel how absolutely useless to him had been all that high interest and noble connection which he had formed.", "이 말의 가시가 더욱 따갑게 박힌 것은, 자기가 맺어 놓은 그 높은 연줄과 고귀한 인연이 실은 자기에게 얼마나 쓸모없었는지를 크로즈비가 막 깨닫기 시작한 참이었기 때문이다."], ["He had really been promoted because he knew more about his work than any of the other men, and Lady De Courcy's influential relation at the India Board had not yet even had time to write a note upon the subject.", "그가 승진한 것은 사실 다른 어느 누구보다 제 일을 잘 알았기 때문이었고, 인도청에 있다는 드 코시 백작부인의 세력 있는 친척은 아직 그 일로 쪽지 한 장 쓸 겨를조차 없었던 것이다."]], [["At eleven Mr. Butterwell came into Crosbie's room, and the new secretary was forced to clothe himself in smiles.", "열한 시에 버터웰 씨가 크로즈비의 방으로 들어왔고, 새 사무국장은 억지로 웃음을 지어 걸쳐야 했다."], ["Mr. Butterwell was a pleasant, handsome man of about fifty, who had never yet set the Thames on fire, and had never attempted to do so.", "버터웰 씨는 쉰 줄의 유쾌하고 잘생긴 사람으로, 이제껏 세상을 놀라게 할 만한 일이라고는 해 본 적이 없었고 해 보려 한 적도 없었다."], ["He was perhaps a little more civil to great men and a little more patronizing to those below him than he would have been had he been perfect.", "그는 아마 완벽한 사람이었을 때보다 지체 높은 이들에게 조금 더 굽실거렸고 아랫사람들에게는 조금 더 생색을 냈을 것이다."], ["But there was something frank and English even in his mode of bowing before the mighty ones, and to those who were not mighty he was rather too civil than either stern or supercilious.", "그러나 힘 있는 자들 앞에서 허리를 굽히는 그 태도에조차 어딘가 솔직하고 영국인다운 데가 있었고, 힘없는 이들에게는 매몰차거나 거만하기보다는 차라리 지나치게 공손했다."], ["He knew that he was not very clever, but he knew also how to use those who were clever.", "그는 제가 그리 영리하지 못하다는 것을 알았고, 영리한 자들을 부리는 법도 알았다."], ["He seldom made any mistake, and was very scrupulous not to tread on men's corns.", "그는 좀처럼 실수를 하지 않았고, 남의 티눈을 밟지 않으려고 무척 조심했다."], ["Though he had no enemies, yet he had a friend or two; and we may therefore say of Mr.", "원수는 하나도 없으면서 벗은 한둘 있었다."], ["Butterwell that he had walked his path in life discreetly.", "그러니 버터웰 씨로 말하자면 인생의 길을 슬기롭게 걸어왔다고 해도 좋겠다."], ["At the age of thirty-five he had married a lady with some little fortune, and now he lived a pleasant, easy, smiling life in a villa at Putney.", "서른다섯에 그는 재산을 조금 지닌 부인과 혼인했고, 지금은 퍼트니의 별장에서 유쾌하고 편안하고 웃음 띤 삶을 살고 있었다."], ["When Mr. Butterwell heard, as he often did hear, of the difficulty which an English gentleman has of earning his bread in his own country, he was wont to look back on his own career with some complacency.", "영국 신사가 제 나라에서 밥벌이하기가 얼마나 어려운가 하는 이야기를 들을 때면 — 그는 그런 이야기를 자주 들었다 — 버터웰 씨는 제가 걸어온 길을 얼마간 흐뭇하게 돌아보곤 했다."], ["He knew that he had not given the world much; yet he had received largely, and no one had begrudged it to him.", "그는 자기가 세상에 그리 많이 주지 않았음을 알았다. 그런데도 받기는 넉넉히 받았고, 아무도 그것을 아까워하지 않았다."], ["\"Tact,\" Mr.", "“요령이지.”"], ["Butterwell used to say to himself, as he walked along the paths of his Putney villa.", "버터웰 씨는 퍼트니 별장의 오솔길을 거닐며 스스로에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["\"Tact.", "“요령."], ["Tact.", "요령."], ["Tact.\"", "요령.”"]], [["\"Crosbie,\"", "“크로즈비.”"], ["he said, as he entered the room cheerily, \"I congratulate you with all my heart.", "그는 쾌활하게 방으로 들어서며 말했다. “진심으로 축하하네."], ["I do, indeed.", "정말일세. 자네는 인생의 이른 나이에 한 계단을 올랐고, 그럴 자격이 충분하네."], ["You have got the step early in life, and you deserve it thoroughly;--much better than I did when I was appointed to the same office.\"", "나보다 훨씬 낫지. 나는 같은 자리에 임명됐을 때 자네만 못했으니.”"]], [["\"Oh, no,\"", "“원, 별말씀을.”"], ["said Crosbie, gloomily.", "크로즈비가 침울하게 말했다."]], [["\"But I say, Oh, yes.", "“하지만 나는 별말이 아니라고 말하겠네."], ["We are deuced lucky to have such a man, and so I told the commissioners.\"", "우리는 이런 사람을 얻어 지독하게 운이 좋은 거야. 위원들에게도 그렇게 말해 두었네.”"]], [["\"I'm sure I'm very much obliged to you.\"", "“정말 뭐라 감사드려야 할지 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["\"I've known it all along,--before you left even.", "“나는 진작부터 알고 있었네. 자네가 떠나기도 전부터 말이야."], ["Sir Raffle Buffle had told me he was to go to the Income-tax Office.", "래플 버플 경이 소득세 사무국으로 간다고 내게 말했거든."], ["The chair is two thousand there, you know; and I had been promised the first seat at the Board.\"", "거기 의장 자리는 이천 파운드일세, 자네도 알겠지만. 그리고 나는 위원회의 첫 자리를 약속받아 두었고.”"]], [["\"Ah;--I wish I'd known,\"", "“아, 미리 알았더라면 좋았을걸.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"You are much better as you are,\"", "“모르는 편이 훨씬 낫네.”"], ["said Butterwell.", "버터웰이 말했다."], ["\"There's no pleasure like a surprise!", "“뜻밖의 일만 한 즐거움이 어디 있나!"], ["Besides, one knows a thing of that kind, and yet doesn't know it.", "게다가 그런 일이란 알면서도 또 모르는 것이거든."], ["I don't mind saying now that I knew it,--swearing that I knew it,--but I wouldn't have said so to a living being the day before yesterday.", "이제야 알고 있었다고 말하는 거고, 알고 있었노라 맹세도 하겠네만, 그저께만 해도 살아 있는 누구에게도 그런 말은 하지 않았을 걸세."], ["There are such slips between the cups and the lips.", "잔과 입술 사이에 그런 미끄러짐이 얼마나 많은가."], ["Suppose Sir Raffle had not gone to the Income-tax!\"", "래플 경이 소득세 사무국으로 가지 않았다고 생각해 보게!”"]], [["\"Exactly so,\"", "“그렇지요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"But it's all right now.", "“하지만 이제 다 됐네."], ["Indeed I sat at the Board yesterday, though I signed the letter afterwards.", "실은 내가 어제 위원회에 앉았지. 편지에 서명은 그 뒤에 했네만."], ["I'm not sure that I don't lose more than I gain.\"", "얻은 것보다 잃은 것이 더 많은 게 아닌가 싶기도 하이.”"]], [["\"What!", "“무슨 말씀입니까!"], ["with three hundred a year more and less work?\"", "연봉이 삼백 파운드 더 늘고 일은 줄었는데요?”"]], [["\"Ah, but look at the interest of the thing.", "“아, 하지만 일의 재미를 생각해 보게."], ["The secretary sees everything and knows everything.", "사무국장은 모든 것을 보고 모든 것을 알지."], ["But I'm getting old, and, as you say, the lighter work will suit me.", "그래도 나는 늙어 가고 있고, 자네 말대로 가벼운 일이 내게 맞을 걸세."], ["By the by, will you come down to Putney to-morrow?", "그건 그렇고, 내일 퍼트니로 내려오지 않겠나?"], ["Mrs. Butterwell will be delighted to see the new secretary.", "버터웰 부인이 새 사무국장을 보면 아주 기뻐할 걸세."], ["There's nobody in town now, so you can have no ground for refusing.\"", "지금은 런던에 아무도 없으니 거절할 핑계도 없을 테고.”"]], [["But Mr. Crosbie did find some ground for refusing.", "그러나 크로즈비 씨는 거절할 핑계를 기어이 찾아냈다."], ["It would have been impossible for him to have sat and smiled at Mrs. Butterwell's table in his present frame of mind.", "지금 같은 마음으로는 버터웰 부인의 식탁에 앉아 웃고 있기란 도무지 불가능한 일이었다."], ["In a mysterious, half-explanatory manner, he let Mr. Butterwell know that private affairs of importance made it absolutely necessary that he should remain that evening in town.", "그는 알 듯 말 듯 하게, 반쯤만 털어놓는 투로, 중요한 사사로운 일이 있어 그날 저녁에는 반드시 시내에 남아 있어야 한다고 버터웰 씨에게 알렸다."], ["\"And indeed,\" as he said, \"he was not his own master just at present.\"", "그리고 그가 말한 대로 “요즘은 제 몸이 제 것이 아니라서요.”"]], [["\"By the by,--of course not.", "“아, 그렇지, 그렇고말고."], ["I had quite forgotten to congratulate you on that head.", "그 일을 축하하는 것을 깜빡 잊었구먼."], ["So you're going to be married?", "그래, 혼인을 한다지?"], ["Well; I'm very glad, and hope you'll be as lucky as I have been.\"", "그것참 반가운 일일세. 자네도 나만큼 운이 좋기를 바라네.”"]], [["\"Thank you,\"", "“고맙습니다.”"], ["said Crosbie, again rather gloomily.", "크로즈비가 다시 얼마간 침울하게 말했다."]], [["\"A young lady from near Guestwick, isn't it; or somewhere in those parts?\"", "“게스트윅 가까이 사는 아가씨라던가? 아니면 그쯤 어디라던가?”"]], [["\"N--no,\"", "“아, 아닙니다.”"], ["stammered Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 더듬거렸다."], ["\"The lady comes from Barsetshire.\"", "“그 아가씨는 바셋주 사람입니다.”"]], [["\"Why, I heard the name.", "“그것참, 나는 이름까지 들었는데."], ["Isn't she a Bell, or Tait, or Ball, or some such name as that?\"", "벨이라던가, 테이트라던가, 볼이라던가, 그런 이름 아닌가?”"]], [["\"No,\"", "“아닙니다.”"], ["said Crosbie, assuming what boldness he could command.", "크로즈비가 낼 수 있는 대로 대담함을 그러모아 말했다."], ["\"Her name is De Courcy.\"", "“드 코시라는 이름입니다.”"]], [["\"One of the earl's daughters?\"", "“백작의 따님들 가운데 한 분인가?”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“그렇습니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, I beg your pardon.", "“이런, 미안하네."], ["I'd heard wrong.", "내가 잘못 들었군."], ["You're going to be allied to a very noble family, and I am heartily glad to hear of your success in life.\"", "자네는 아주 고귀한 집안과 인연을 맺게 되는 거로군. 자네가 인생에서 잘되어 간다니 진심으로 기쁘이.”"], ["Then Butterwell shook him very cordially by the hand,--having offered him no such special testimony of approval when under the belief that he was going to marry a Bell, a Tait, or a Ball.", "그러고서 버터웰은 그의 손을 아주 다정하게 흔들었다. 그가 벨이나 테이트나 볼과 혼인한다고 믿고 있었을 때에는 그런 각별한 찬동의 표시를 내놓지 않았던 것이다."], ["All the same, Mr. Butterwell began to think that there was something wrong.", "그러면서도 버터웰 씨는 무언가 잘못되었다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["He had heard from an indubitable source that Crosbie had engaged himself to a niece of a squire with whom he had been staying near Guestwick,--a girl without any money; and Mr.", "그는 아주 미덥고도 확실한 데서 이런 이야기를 들은 터였다. 크로즈비가 게스트윅 가까이 머무는 동안 그곳 지주의 조카딸과 약혼했다는 것, 그 아가씨는 돈이라고는 없다는 것."], ["Butterwell, in his wisdom, had thought his friend Crosbie to be rather a fool for his pains.", "그래서 버터웰 씨는 제 지혜를 다해, 벗 크로즈비가 사서 고생하는 얼간이라고 여겨 왔던 것이다."], ["But now he was going to marry one of the De Courcys! Mr.", "그런데 이제 드 코시 집안의 딸과 혼인한다니!"], ["Butterwell was rather at his wits' ends.", "버터웰 씨는 아주 어리둥절해졌다."]], [["\"Well; we shall be sitting at two, you know, and of course you'll come to us.", "“자, 우리는 두 시에 자리에 앉을 걸세, 알겠지. 물론 자네도 우리에게 와야 하고."], ["If you're at leisure before that I'll make over what papers I have to you.", "그 전에 틈이 나거든 내가 가진 서류를 자네에게 넘겨주겠네."], ["I've not been a Lord Eldon in my office, and they won't break your back.\"", "나는 이 자리에서 엘던 경 노릇을 하지는 않았으니, 그 서류들이 자네 허리를 부러뜨리지는 않을 걸세.”"]], [["Immediately after that Fowler Pratt had been shown into Crosbie's room, and Crosbie had written the letter to the squire under Pratt's eye.", "그 직후에 파울러 프랫이 크로즈비의 방으로 안내되어 들어왔고, 크로즈비는 프랫이 지켜보는 앞에서 지주에게 보내는 편지를 썼다."]], [["He could take no joy in his promotion.", "그는 제 승진에서 아무 기쁨도 얻을 수 없었다."], ["When Pratt left him he tried to lighten his heart.", "프랫이 떠난 뒤 그는 마음을 가볍게 해 보려 애썼다."], ["He endeavoured to throw Lily and her wrongs behind him, and fix his thoughts on his advancing successes in life; but he could not do it.", "릴리와 그 아이가 당한 부당함을 등 뒤로 던져 버리고 인생에서 나아가는 성공에 생각을 붙들어 매려 했지만, 그렇게 되지 않았다."], ["A self-imposed trouble will not allow itself to be banished.", "제 손으로 불러들인 괴로움은 쫓겨나 주지 않는 법이다."], ["If a man lose a thousand pounds by a friend's fault, or by a turn in the wheel of fortune, he can, if he be a man, put his grief down and trample it under foot; he can exorcise the spirit of his grievance, and bid the evil one depart from out of his house.", "어떤 사람이 벗의 잘못으로, 혹은 운수의 수레바퀴가 돌아간 탓으로 천 파운드를 잃었다면, 그가 사내라면 그 슬픔을 내려놓고 발밑에 밟아 버릴 수 있다. 그는 제 억울함의 혼령을 쫓아내고 악한 것더러 제 집에서 나가라 명할 수 있다."], ["But such exorcism is not to be used when the sorrow has come from a man's own folly and sin;--especially not if it has come from his own selfishness.", "그러나 슬픔이 제 어리석음과 제 죄에서 온 것일 때에는 그런 축귀가 통하지 않는다. 더구나 제 이기심에서 온 것이라면 더욱 그렇다."], ["Such are the cases which make men drink; which drive them on to the avoidance of all thought; which create gamblers and reckless prodigals; which are the promoters of suicide.", "사람을 술로 몰아가는 것이 바로 그런 경우요, 사람을 아무 생각도 하지 않도록 내모는 것이 그런 경우요, 노름꾼과 앞뒤 없는 탕아를 만들어 내는 것, 자살을 부추기는 것이 그런 경우다."], ["How could he avoid writing this letter to Lily?", "릴리에게 이 편지를 쓰는 일을 어떻게 피할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["He might blow his brains out, and so let there be an end of it all.", "제 머리를 쏘아 버리면 그것으로 죄다 끝이 나기는 하겠지."], ["It was to such reflections that he came, when he sat himself down endeavouring to reap satisfaction from his promotion.", "제 승진에서 만족을 거두어 보려고 자리에 앉았을 때 그가 이른 생각이란 이런 것들이었다."]], [["But Crosbie was not a man to commit suicide.", "그러나 크로즈비는 스스로 목숨을 끊을 사람이 아니었다."], ["In giving him his due I must protest that he was too good for that.", "그에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려주자면, 나는 그가 그러기에는 너무 괜찮은 사람이었다고 말해 두어야겠다."], ["He knew too well that a pistol-bullet could not be the be-all and the end-all here, and there was too much manliness in him for so cowardly an escape.", "그는 권총알 한 발이 이 세상에서 모든 것의 처음이자 끝이 될 수는 없다는 것을 너무나 잘 알았고, 그토록 비겁한 도피를 하기에는 그 안에 사내다움이 너무 많았다."], ["The burden must be borne.", "짐은 짊어져야 한다."], ["But how was he to bear it?", "그러나 어떻게 짊어질 것인가?"], ["There he sat till it was two o'clock, neglecting Mr. Butterwell and his office papers, and not stirring from his seat till a messenger summoned him before the Board.", "그는 두 시가 될 때까지 거기 앉아 버터웰 씨도 사무국 서류도 내버려 둔 채, 사환이 와서 그를 위원회 앞으로 부를 때까지 제자리에서 꼼짝하지 않았다."], ["The Board, as he entered the room, was not such a Board as the public may, perhaps, imagine such Boards to be.", "그가 방으로 들어섰을 때의 그 위원회는, 세상 사람들이 아마 그런 위원회를 어떠려니 상상하는 것과는 사뭇 다른 위원회였다."], ["There was a round table, with a few pens lying about, and a comfortable leathern arm-chair at the side of it, farthest from the door.", "둥근 탁자가 하나 있었고 그 위에 펜 몇 자루가 아무렇게나 놓여 있었으며, 문에서 가장 먼 쪽 곁에는 편안한 가죽 안락의자가 하나 있었다."], ["Sir Raffle Buffle was leaving his late colleagues, and was standing with his back to the fire-place, talking very loudly.", "래플 버플 경은 옛 동료들과 작별하는 참이어서 벽난로에 등을 대고 서서 아주 큰 소리로 떠들고 있었다."], ["Sir Raffle was a great bully, and the Board was uncommonly glad to be rid of him; but as this was to be his last appearance at the Committee Office, they submitted to his voice meekly.", "래플 경은 지독한 폭군이었고 위원회는 그를 치워 버리게 되어 여간 기쁘지 않았다. 그러나 이번이 그가 위원회 사무국에 모습을 보이는 마지막 자리인지라 다들 그 목소리를 얌전히 받아 주고 있었다."], ["Mr. Butterwell was standing close to him, essaying to laugh mildly at Sir Raffle's jokes.", "버터웰 씨는 그의 곁에 바짝 붙어 서서 래플 경의 우스개에 부드럽게 웃어 주려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["A little man, hardly more than five feet high, with small but honest-looking eyes, and close-cut hair, was standing behind the arm-chair, rubbing his hands together, and longing for the departure of Sir Raffle, in order that he might sit down. This was Mr.", "키가 오 척을 겨우 넘을까 말까 한 자그마한 사람이, 작지만 정직해 보이는 눈에 머리를 짧게 깎고서, 안락의자 뒤에 서서 두 손을 비비며 래플 경이 어서 떠나 주기를, 그래야 자기가 앉을 수 있으니 애타게 바라고 있었다."], ["Optimist, the new chairman, in praise of whose appointment the Daily Jupiter had been so loud, declaring that the present Minister was showing himself superior to all Ministers who had ever gone before him, in giving promotion solely on the score of merit.", "이 사람이 새 의장인 옵티미스트 씨였다. 《데일리 주피터》가 그의 임명을 두고 그토록 목청껏 칭송하면서, 지금의 총리대신은 오로지 공적만을 잣대로 승진을 시킨다는 점에서 앞서 간 어떤 총리대신보다도 뛰어남을 스스로 보여 주고 있다고 선언했던 바로 그 사람이다."], ["The Daily Jupiter, a fortnight since, had published a very eloquent article, strongly advocating the claims of Mr. Optimist, and was naturally pleased to find that its advice had been taken.", "《데일리 주피터》는 두 주 전에 옵티미스트 씨의 자격을 힘껏 내세우는 매우 유창한 논설을 실었고, 제 충고가 받아들여진 것을 보고 흐뭇해한 것도 당연한 일이었다."], ["Has not an obedient Minister a right to the praise of those powers which he obeys?", "순종하는 대신이라면 제가 순종하는 그 권세로부터 칭찬을 받을 권리가 있지 않겠는가?"]], [["Mr. Optimist was, in truth, an industrious little gentleman, very well connected, who had served the public all his life, and who was, at any rate, honest in his dealings.", "옵티미스트 씨는 사실 부지런한 자그마한 신사로, 집안이 아주 좋았고, 평생 공직에서 나라를 섬겨 왔으며, 적어도 일 처리에 있어서는 정직한 사람이었다."], ["Nor was he a bully, such as his predecessor.", "또 제 앞사람처럼 폭군도 아니었다."], ["It might, however, be a question whether he carried guns enough for the command in which he was now to be employed.", "다만 이제 맡게 된 자리를 감당할 만한 대포를 넉넉히 싣고 있느냐 하는 것은 물음거리일 수 있었다."], ["There was but one other member of the Board, Major Fiasco by name, a discontented, broken-hearted, silent man, who had been sent to the General Committee Office some few years before because he was not wanted anywhere else.", "위원회에는 그 밖에 위원이 딱 한 사람 더 있었으니, 피아스코 소령이라는 이였다. 불평 많고 가슴이 무너진 채 말이 없는 사람으로, 몇 해 전에 달리 그를 원하는 데가 없다는 이유로 일반위원회 사무국에 보내진 이였다."], ["He was a man who had intended to do great things when he entered public life, and had possessed the talent and energy for things moderately great.", "그는 공직에 발을 들일 때 큰일을 해내겠다고 마음먹었던 사람이었고, 웬만큼 큰일을 해낼 만한 재주와 기운도 지니고 있었다."], ["He had also possessed to a certain extent the ear of those high in office; but, in some way, matters had not gone well with him, and in running his course he had gone on the wrong side of the post.", "높은 자리에 있는 이들의 귀도 어느 정도까지는 얻고 있었다. 그러나 어쩌다 보니 일이 그에게 잘 풀리지 않았고, 제 길을 달리다가 기둥을 잘못된 쪽으로 돌고 말았다."], ["He was still in the prime of life, and yet all men knew that Major Fiasco had nothing further to expect from the public or from the Government.", "그는 아직 한창때였으나, 피아스코 소령이 이제 세상으로부터든 정부로부터든 더 기대할 것이 없다는 것은 누구나 아는 일이었다."], ["Indeed, there were not wanting those who said that Major Fiasco was already in receipt of a liberal income, for which he gave no work in return; that he merely filled a chair for four hours a day four or five days a week, signing his name to certain forms and documents, reading, or pretending to read, certain papers, but, in truth, doing no good.", "실로, 피아스코 소령은 아무 일도 해 주지 않으면서 후한 봉급만 받고 있다고, 그저 하루 네 시간씩 한 주에 나흘이나 닷새 의자를 차지하고 앉아 이런저런 서식과 문서에 제 이름을 적어 넣고, 이런저런 서류를 읽거나 읽는 시늉을 할 뿐 실은 아무 쓸모 있는 일도 하지 않는다고 말하는 이들도 없지 않았다."], ["Major Fiasco, on the other hand, considered himself to be a deeply injured individual, and he spent his life in brooding over his wrongs.", "반면에 피아스코 소령 자신은 스스로를 깊이 상처 입은 사람으로 여겨, 제가 당한 부당함을 곱씹으며 나날을 보냈다."], ["He believed now in nothing and in nobody.", "이제 그는 아무것도, 아무도 믿지 않았다."], ["He had begun public life striving to be honest, and he now regarded all around him as dishonest.", "그는 정직하려 애쓰며 공직 생활을 시작했으나, 이제는 제 둘레의 모든 사람을 부정직하다고 여겼다."], ["He had no satisfaction in any man other than that which he found when some event would show to him that this or that other compeer of his own had proved himself to be self-interested, false, or fraudulent.", "그는 어떤 사람에게서도 흡족함을 느끼지 못했고, 다만 어떤 일이 벌어져 제 또래의 이 아무개나 저 아무개가 잇속만 차리는 자요 거짓된 자요 사기꾼임이 드러날 때에만 흡족해했다."], ["\"Don't tell me, Butterwell,\"", "“나한테 그런 소리 마시게, 버터웰.”"], ["he would say--for with Mr.", "그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["Butterwell he maintained some semi-official intimacy, and he would take that gentleman by the button-hole, holding him close.", "버터웰 씨와는 얼마간 반쯤 공적인 친분을 이어 오던 터라, 그는 그 신사의 단춧구멍을 붙들고 놓아 주지 않으며 말하곤 했다. “나한테 그런 소리 마시게."], ["\"Don't tell me. I know what men are.", "나는 사람이라는 게 어떤지 알아."], ["I've seen the world.", "세상을 다 봤네."], ["I've been looking at things with my eyes open.", "눈을 뜨고 세상일을 지켜봐 왔단 말일세."], ["I knew what he was doing.\"", "그자가 무슨 짓을 하고 있었는지 나는 알고 있었네.”"], ["And then he would tell of the sly deed of some official known well to them both, not denouncing it by any means, but affecting to take it for granted that the man in question was a rogue.", "그러고는 둘 다 잘 아는 어떤 관리의 교활한 짓거리를 일러바치되, 결코 그것을 성토하는 것은 아니고, 다만 문제의 그 사람이 악당이라는 것을 당연한 사실인 양 여기는 투로 말했다."], ["Butterwell would shrug his shoulders, and laugh gently, and say that, upon his word, he didn't think the world so bad as Fiasco made it out to be.", "버터웰은 어깨를 으쓱하고 부드럽게 웃으며, 맹세코 자기는 세상이 피아스코가 말하는 것만큼 나쁘다고는 생각하지 않는다고 대답하곤 했다."]], [["Nor did he; for Butterwell believed in many things.", "실제로 그는 그렇게 생각하지 않았다."], ["He believed in his Putney villa on this earth, and he believed also that he might achieve some sort of Putney villa in the world beyond without undergoing present martyrdom.", "버터웰은 여러 가지를 믿는 사람이었기 때문이다. 그는 이 땅에 있는 제 퍼트니 별장을 믿었고, 지금 순교를 겪지 않고도 저세상에서 어떤 종류의 퍼트니 별장을 얻을 수 있으리라는 것 또한 믿었다."], ["His Putney villa first, with all its attendant comforts, and then his duty to the public afterwards. It was thus that Mr.", "먼저 제 퍼트니 별장과 거기 딸린 온갖 안락함이요, 나라에 대한 의무는 그다음이었다."], ["Butterwell regulated his conduct; and as he was solicitous that the villa should be as comfortable a home to his wife as to himself, and that it should be specially comfortable to his friends, I do not think that we need quarrel with his creed.", "버터웰 씨는 이렇게 제 처신을 다스렸다. 그리고 그는 그 별장이 저 자신에게만큼 아내에게도 편안한 집이 되기를, 또 벗들에게는 각별히 편안한 집이 되기를 마음 써 왔으니, 우리가 그의 신조를 두고 다툴 것까지는 없지 싶다."]], [["Mr. Optimist believed in everything, but especially he believed in the Prime Minister, in the Daily Jupiter, in the General Committee Office, and in himself.", "옵티미스트 씨는 모든 것을 믿었으나, 그중에서도 특히 총리대신을 믿었고, 《데일리 주피터》를 믿었고, 일반위원회 사무국을 믿었고, 저 자신을 믿었다."], ["He had long thought that everything was nearly right; but now that he himself was chairman at the General Committee Office, he was quite sure that everything must be right.", "그는 오래전부터 모든 것이 거의 옳게 되어 있다고 생각해 왔다. 그런데 이제 자기가 몸소 일반위원회 사무국의 의장이 되었으니, 모든 것이 반드시 옳게 되어 있으리라고 그는 아주 확신했다."], ["In Sir Raffle Buffle, indeed, he had never believed; and now it was, perhaps, the greatest joy of his life that he should never again be called upon to hear the tones of that terrible knight's hated voice.", "실로 래플 버플 경만은 한 번도 믿어 본 적이 없었다. 그러니 그 무시무시한 기사의 미운 목소리를 다시는 들을 일이 없으리라는 것이야말로 아마 그의 생애에서 가장 큰 기쁨이었을 것이다."]], [["Seeing who were the components of the new Board, it may be presumed that Crosbie would look forward to enjoying a not uninfluential position in his office.", "새 위원회를 이루는 사람들이 누구인지를 보면, 크로즈비가 제 사무국에서 적잖이 영향력 있는 자리를 누리게 되리라고 기대했으리라 짐작할 만하다."], ["There were, indeed, some among the clerks who did not hesitate to say that the new secretary would have it pretty nearly all his own way.", "실제로 서기들 가운데에는 새 사무국장이 거의 모든 것을 제 뜻대로 하게 되리라고 서슴없이 말하는 이들도 있었다."], ["As for \"Old Opt,\" there would be, they said, no difficulty about him.", "늙은 옵트로 말하자면 아무 어려움도 없으리라는 것이었다."], ["Only tell him that such and such a decision was his own, and he would be sure to believe the teller.", "이러이러한 결정이 당신 자신의 결정이라고 일러 주기만 하면 그는 틀림없이 그 말을 믿어 준다는 것이다."], ["Butterwell was not fond of work, and had been accustomed to lean upon Crosbie for many years.", "버터웰은 일을 좋아하지 않았고, 여러 해 동안 크로즈비에게 기대는 데 익숙해져 있었다."], ["As for Fiasco, he would be cynical in words, but wholly indifferent in deed.", "피아스코로 말하자면 말로는 빈정댈지언정 행동으로는 전혀 아랑곳하지 않을 것이었다."], ["If the whole office were made to go to the mischief, Fiasco, in his own grim way, would enjoy the confusion.", "사무국 전체가 결딴이 난다 해도 피아스코는 제 특유의 음침한 방식으로 그 난장판을 즐길 사람이었다."]], [["\"Wish you joy, Crosbie,\"", "“축하하네, 크로즈비.”"], ["said Sir Raffle, standing up on the rug, waiting for the new secretary to go up to him and shake hands.", "래플 경이 난로 앞 깔개를 밟고 서서, 새 사무국장이 제게로 다가와 손을 잡아 주기를 기다리며 말했다."], ["But Sir Raffle was going, and the new secretary did not indulge him.", "그러나 래플 경은 떠나는 사람이었고, 새 사무국장은 그 청을 들어주지 않았다."]], [["\"Thank ye, Sir Raffle,\"", "“고맙습니다, 래플 경.”"], ["said Crosbie, without going near the rug.", "크로즈비는 깔개 쪽으로는 가지도 않은 채 말했다."]], [["\"Mr. Crosbie, I congratulate you most sincerely,\"", "“크로즈비 씨, 진심으로 축하드립니다.”"], ["said Mr. Optimist.", "옵티미스트 씨가 말했다."], ["\"Your promotion has been the result altogether of your own merit.", "“당신의 승진은 오로지 당신 자신의 공적에서 비롯된 결과입니다."], ["You have been selected for the high office which you are now called upon to fill solely because it has been thought that you are the most fit man to perform the onerous duties attached to it.", "당신이 이제 맡게 된 높은 자리에 뽑힌 것은, 오직 거기 딸린 무거운 직무를 수행하기에 당신이 가장 적합한 사람이라고 여겨졌기 때문입니다."], ["Hum--h-m--ha.", "흠, 으흠, 어험."], ["As regards my share in the recommendation which we found ourselves bound to submit to the Treasury, I must say that I never felt less hesitation in my life, and I believe I may declare as much as regards the other members of the Board.\"", "우리가 재무부에 올려야 한다고 여긴 그 추천에서 제가 맡은 몫으로 말하자면, 저는 평생 그때만큼 망설임이 적었던 적이 없다고 말해야겠습니다. 그리고 위원회의 다른 위원들에 대해서도 그만큼 말해도 좋으리라 믿습니다.”"], ["And Mr. Optimist looked around him for approving words.", "그러고서 옵티미스트 씨는 찬동하는 말을 구하며 둘레를 둘러보았다."], ["He had come forward from his standing ground behind his chair to welcome Crosbie, and had shaken his hand cordially.", "그는 크로즈비를 맞이하려고 제 의자 뒤에 서 있던 자리에서 앞으로 나와 다정하게 손을 잡아 주었다."], ["Fiasco also had risen from his seat, and had assured Crosbie in a whisper that he had feathered his nest uncommon well.", "피아스코 또한 제자리에서 일어나 제 둥지를 여간 잘 틀어 놓지 않았다고 크로즈비에게 속삭였다."], ["Then he had sat down again.", "그러고는 다시 앉았다."]], [["\"Indeed you may, as far as I am concerned,\"", "“제게 관한 한 얼마든지 그렇게 말씀하셔도 좋습니다.”"], ["said Butterwell.", "버터웰이 말했다."]], [["\"I told the Chancellor of the Exchequer,\"", "“내가 재무장관에게 말했지.”"], ["said Sir Raffle, speaking very loud and with much authority, \"that unless he had some first-rate man to send from elsewhere I could name a fitting candidate. 'Sir Raffle,'", "래플 경이 아주 큰 소리로, 대단한 권위를 실어 말했다."], ["he said, 'I mean to keep it in the office, and therefore shall be glad of your opinion.'", "“다른 데서 데려올 일류 인재가 따로 없다면 내가 알맞은 후보를 하나 대겠노라고."], ["'In that case, Mr.", "‘래플 경,’ 그가 말하더군."], ["Chancellor,' said I, 'Mr.", "‘나는 이 자리를 사무국 안에서 채울 생각이오."], ["Crosbie must be the man.' 'Mr.", "그러니 당신의 의견을 듣는다면 반갑겠소.’ ‘그렇다면, 장관 각하,’ 내가 말했지."], ["Crosbie shall be the man,' said the Chancellor.", "‘크로즈비 씨가 그 사람이어야 합니다.’ ‘크로즈비 씨가 그 사람이 될 것이오.’ 장관이 말했네."], ["And Mr. Crosbie is the man.\"", "그리고 크로즈비 씨가 그 사람이 되었지.”"]], [["\"Your friend Sark spoke to Lord Brock about it,\" said Fiasco.", "“당신 친구 사크가 브록 경에게 그 이야기를 했다더군요.”"], ["Now the Earl of Sark was a young nobleman of much influence at the present moment, and Lord Brock was the Prime Minister.", "피아스코가 말했다. 사크 백작은 요즘 세력이 대단한 젊은 귀족이었고, 브록 경은 총리대신이었다."], ["\"You should thank Lord Sark.\"", "“사크 경께 고마워하셔야겠소.”"]], [["\"Had as much to do with it as if my footman had spoken,\"", "“내 하인가 말한 것만큼이나 상관이 있었겠구먼.”"], ["said Sir Raffle.", "래플 경이 말했다."]], [["\"I am very much obliged to the Board for their good opinion,\"", "“위원회께서 저를 좋게 보아 주신 데 대해 대단히 감사드립니다.”"], ["said Crosbie, gravely.", "크로즈비가 정색하고 말했다."], ["\"I am obliged to Lord Sark as well,--and also to your footman, Sir Raffle, if, as you seem to say, he has interested himself in my favour.\"", "“사크 경께도 감사드립니다. 그리고 래플 경, 말씀하신 것처럼 그가 저를 위해 애써 주었다면 경의 하인에게도 감사드립니다.”"]], [["\"I didn't say anything of the kind,\"", "“나는 그런 말을 한 적이 없네.”"], ["said Sir Raffle.", "래플 경이 말했다."], ["\"I thought it right to make you understand that it was my opinion, given, of course, officially, which prevailed with the Chancellor of the Exchequer.", "“재무장관을 움직인 것이 물론 공적으로 개진된 내 의견이었다는 점을 자네가 알아 두는 것이 옳다고 여겼을 뿐이야."], ["Well, gentlemen, as I shall be wanted in the city, I will say good morning to you.", "자, 여러분, 나는 시내에서 부르는 데가 있으니 이만 작별 인사를 하겠소."], ["Is my carriage ready, Boggs?\"", "보그스, 내 마차는 준비됐나?”"], ["Upon which the attendant messenger opened the door, and the great Sir Raffle Buffle took his final departure from the scene of his former labours.", "그 말에 시중드는 사환이 문을 열었고, 위대한 래플 버플 경은 제가 예전에 수고하던 무대에서 마지막으로 퇴장했다."]], [["\"As to the duties of your new office\"--and Mr.", "“그러면 새 자리의 직무에 대해서 말인데”"], ["Optimist continued his speech, taking no other notice of the departure of his enemy than what was indicated by an increased brightness of his eye and a more satisfactory tone of voice--\"you will find yourself quite familiar with them.\"", "— 옵티미스트 씨는 제 원수가 떠난 것을 두고, 눈빛이 한결 밝아지고 목소리가 한결 흡족해진 것 말고는 아무런 내색도 하지 않은 채 하던 말을 이었다 — “당신은 그 일에 아주 익숙하다는 것을 알게 될 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Indeed he will,\"", "“그야 물론이지요.”"], ["said Butterwell.", "버터웰이 말했다."]], [["\"And I am quite sure that you will perform them with equal credit to yourself, satisfaction to the department, and advantage to the public.", "“그리고 당신이 그 직무를 수행하여 스스로에게는 명예를, 부서에는 만족을, 나라에는 이로움을 똑같이 안겨 주리라고 나는 아주 확신합니다."], ["We shall always be glad to have your opinion on any subject of importance that may come before us; and as regards the internal discipline of the office, we feel that we may leave it safely in your hands.", "우리 앞에 놓이는 중요한 일이라면 언제든 당신의 의견을 들으면 기쁘겠습니다. 그리고 사무국 내부의 기강에 관해서는 안심하고 당신 손에 맡겨도 되겠다고 느낍니다."], ["In any matter of importance you will, of course, consult us, and I feel very confident that we shall go on together with great comfort and with mutual confidence.\"", "중요한 일이 생기면 물론 우리와 의논하시겠지요. 나는 우리가 큰 편안함 속에서, 서로 믿으며 함께 잘해 나가리라고 아주 자신합니다.”"], ["Then Mr. Optimist looked at his brother commissioners, sat down in his arm-chair, and taking in his hands some papers before him, began the routine business of the day.", "그러고서 옵티미스트 씨는 동료 위원들을 한 번 바라보고는 제 안락의자에 앉아, 앞에 놓인 서류를 손에 집어 들고 그날의 일상 업무를 시작했다."]], [["It was nearly five o'clock when, on this special occasion, the secretary returned from the board-room to his own office.", "이 특별한 날에 사무국장이 위원회실에서 제 방으로 돌아온 것은 거의 다섯 시가 다 되어서였다."], ["Not for a moment had the weight been off his shoulders while Sir Raffle had been bragging or Mr. Optimist making his speech.", "래플 경이 큰소리를 치는 동안에도, 옵티미스트 씨가 연설을 하는 동안에도, 그 무게는 잠시도 그의 어깨에서 내려오지 않았다."], ["He had been thinking, not of them, but of Lily Dale; and though they had not discovered his thoughts, they had perceived that he was hardly like himself.", "그는 그들이 아니라 릴리 데일을 생각하고 있었던 것이다. 그리고 그들은 그의 생각을 알아내지는 못했으되, 그가 어쩐지 평소의 그답지 않다는 것은 알아차렸다."]], [["\"I never saw a man so little elated by good fortune in my life,\"", "“좋은 일을 만나고도 저렇게 신이 나지 않는 사람은 내 평생 처음 보오.”"], ["said Mr. Optimist.", "옵티미스트 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Ah, he's got something on his mind,\" said Butterwell.", "“아, 저 사람 마음에 무언가 걸리는 게 있소.”"], ["\"He's going to be married, I believe.\"", "버터웰이 말했다. “혼인을 한다지 아마.”"]], [["\"If that's the case, it's no wonder he shouldn't be elated,\"", "“그렇다면야 신이 나지 않는 것도 이상할 게 없지요.”"], ["said Major Fiasco, who was himself a bachelor.", "저 자신이 독신인 피아스코 소령이 말했다."]], [["When in his own room again, Crosbie at once seized on a sheet of note-paper, as though by hurrying himself on with it he could get that letter to Allington written.", "제 방에 돌아오자 크로즈비는 곧바로 편지지 한 장을 붙들었다. 서둘러 밀어붙이면 앨링턴으로 보낼 그 편지를 써낼 수 있기라도 하다는 듯이."], ["But though the paper was before him, and the pen in his hand, the letter did not, would not, get itself written.", "그러나 종이가 앞에 놓이고 펜이 손에 들려 있어도 편지는 써지지 않았고, 써지려 하지도 않았다."], ["With what words was he to begin it?", "무슨 말로 시작한단 말인가?"], ["To whom should it be written?", "누구 앞으로 써야 하는가?"], ["How was he to declare himself the villain which he had made himself?", "제가 스스로를 만들어 놓은 그 악당임을 어떻게 밝힌단 말인가?"], ["The letters from his office were taken away every night shortly after six, and at six o'clock he had not written a word.", "사무국의 편지는 밤마다 여섯 시가 조금 지나면 걷어 가는데, 여섯 시가 되도록 그는 한 글자도 쓰지 못했다."], ["\"I will do it at home to-night,\"", "“오늘 밤 집에서 쓰자.”"], ["he said to himself, and then, tearing off a scrap of paper, he scratched those few lines which Lily received, and which she had declined to communicate to her mother or sister.", "그는 스스로에게 이렇게 말하고는, 종이 한 귀퉁이를 찢어 릴리가 받았던 그 몇 줄을 갈겨썼다. 릴리가 어머니에게도 언니에게도 보이기를 마다한 그 쪽지였다."], ["Crosbie, as he wrote them, conceived that they would in some way prepare the poor girl for the coming blow,--that they would, at any rate, make her know that all was not right; but in so supposing he had not counted on the constancy of her nature, nor had he thought of the promise which she had given him that nothing should make her doubt him.", "크로즈비는 그것을 쓰면서, 그 몇 줄이 어떻게든 그 가엾은 아가씨로 하여금 다가올 타격을 미리 채비하게 해 주리라고, 적어도 무언가 잘못되었음을 알게는 해 주리라고 여겼다. 그러나 그렇게 여기면서 그는 그 아이의 한결같은 성품을 셈에 넣지 않았고, 무슨 일이 있어도 자기를 의심하지 않겠다던 그 아이의 약속도 떠올리지 못했다."], ["He wrote the scrap, and then taking his hat walked off through the gloom of the November evening up Charing Cross and St. Martin's Lane, towards the Seven Dials and Bloomsbury, into regions of the town with which he had no business, and which he never frequented.", "그는 그 쪽지를 써 놓고 모자를 집어 들고는, 십일월 저녁의 어스름을 헤치고 채링 크로스와 세인트 마틴스 레인을 거슬러 세븐 다이얼스와 블룸즈버리 쪽으로, 제게는 볼일도 없고 한 번도 드나든 적 없는 동네로 걸어 나갔다."], ["He hardly knew where he went or wherefore. How was he to escape from the weight of the burden which was now crushing him?", "자기가 어디로 가는지도, 무엇 하러 가는지도 그는 거의 알지 못했다."], ["It seemed to him as though he would change his position with thankfulness for that of the junior clerk in his office, if only that junior clerk had upon his mind no such betrayal of trust as that of which he was guilty.", "지금 저를 짓뭉개고 있는 이 짐의 무게에서 어떻게 벗어난단 말인가? 제 사무국의 말단 서기와 처지를 바꾸라 해도 그 말단 서기에게 제가 저지른 것 같은 믿음의 배신만 없다면 고맙게 바꾸겠다 싶었다."]], [["At half-past seven he found himself at Sebright's, and there he dined.", "일곱 시 반에 그는 세브라이트에 이르렀고, 거기서 저녁을 먹었다."], ["A man will dine, even though his heart be breaking.", "사람은 가슴이 미어져도 저녁은 먹는 법이다."], ["Then he got into a cab, and had himself taken home to Mount Street.", "그러고서 그는 삯마차를 잡아타고 마운트 스트리트의 제 집으로 갔다."], ["During his walk he had sworn to himself that he would not go to bed that night till the letter was written and posted.", "걷는 동안 그는 그날 밤 편지를 써서 부치기 전에는 잠자리에 들지 않겠노라고 스스로에게 맹세했다."], ["It was twelve before the first words were marked on the paper, and yet he kept his oath.", "첫 낱말들이 종이에 적힌 것은 열두 시가 지나서였으나, 그래도 그는 제 맹세를 지켰다."], ["Between two and three, in the cold moonlight, he crawled out and deposited his letter in the nearest post-office.", "두 시와 세 시 사이, 차가운 달빛 속에서 그는 기어 나가 가장 가까운 우체국에 제 편지를 넣었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIX. JOHN EAMES RETURNS TO BURTON CRESCENT.", "# 제29장. 존 임스가 버턴 크레슨트로 돌아오다"]], [["John Eames and Crosbie returned to town on the same day.", "존 임스와 크로즈비는 같은 날 런던으로 돌아왔다."], ["It will be remembered how Eames had assisted Lord De Guest in the matter of the bull, and how great had been the earl's gratitude on the occasion.", "임스가 황소 사건에서 드 게스트 백작을 도운 일과, 그때 백작이 얼마나 크게 고마워했는지는 다들 기억할 것이다."], ["The memory of this, and the strong encouragement which he received from his mother and sister for having made such a friend by his gallantry, lent some slight satisfaction to his last hours at home.", "그 일에 대한 기억과, 그런 용감한 행동으로 그런 벗을 얻었다고 어머니와 누이가 보내 준 열렬한 칭찬은 집에서 보내는 마지막 시간에 얼마간의 흐뭇함을 안겨 주었다."], ["But his two misfortunes were too serious to allow of anything like real happiness.", "그러나 그의 두 가지 불행은 참된 행복이라 할 만한 것을 조금이라도 허락하기에는 너무나 심각했다."], ["He was leaving Lily behind him, engaged to be married to a man whom he hated, and he was returning to Burton Crescent, where he would have to face Amelia Roper,--Amelia either in her rage or in her love.", "그는 자기가 미워하는 사내와 혼인하기로 약혼한 릴리를 뒤에 남겨 두고 떠나는 참이었고, 어밀리아 로퍼를 마주해야 하는 버턴 크레슨트로 돌아가는 참이었다—성이 난 어밀리아든, 사랑에 빠진 어밀리아든."], ["The prospect of Amelia in her rage was very terrible to him; but his greatest fear was of Amelia in her love.", "성이 난 어밀리아를 생각하면 몹시 무서웠다. 그러나 그가 가장 두려워한 것은 사랑에 빠진 어밀리아였다."], ["He had in his letter declined matrimony; but what if she talked down all his objections, and carried him off to church in spite of himself!", "그는 편지에서 혼인을 사양했다. 그러나 그녀가 그의 반대를 죄다 말로 눌러 버리고, 그의 뜻과는 아랑곳없이 그를 교회로 끌고 가 버린다면 어쩐단 말인가!"]], [["When he reached London and got into a cab with his portmanteau, he could hardly fetch up courage to bid the man drive him to Burton Crescent.", "런던에 닿아 큰 여행 가방을 들고 삯마차에 올랐을 때, 그는 버턴 크레슨트로 가 달라고 마부에게 이르는 것조차 좀처럼 용기를 낼 수 없었다."], ["\"I might as well go to an hotel for the night,\"", "“오늘 밤은 그냥 여관에서 자는 편이 나을지도 모르지.”"], ["he said to himself, \"and then I can learn how things are going on from Cradell at the office.\"", "그는 혼잣말을 했다. “그러고 나서 사무국에서 크래들한테 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 들으면 될 테고.”"], ["Nevertheless, he did give the direction to Burton Crescent, and when it was once given felt ashamed to change it.", "그런데도 그는 버턴 크레슨트라고 일러 주었고, 한번 그렇게 말하고 나자 바꾸기가 부끄러웠다."], ["But, as he was driven up to the well-known door, his heart was so low within him that he might almost be said to have lost it.", "그러나 그 낯익은 문 앞으로 마차가 다다랐을 때 그의 심장은 어찌나 내려앉았던지 아예 잃어버렸다고 해도 좋을 지경이었다."], ["When the cabman demanded whether he should knock, he could not answer; and when the maid-servant at the door greeted him, he almost ran away.", "마부가 문을 두드릴까요 하고 물었을 때 그는 대답을 하지 못했고, 문간에서 하녀가 그를 반겼을 때는 하마터면 달아날 뻔했다."]], [["\"Who's at home?\"", "“누구누구 계신가?”"], ["said he, asking the question in a very low voice.", "그는 아주 낮은 목소리로 그렇게 물었다."]], [["\"There's missus,\"", "“마님이 계시고요,” 하녀가 말했다."], ["said the girl, \"and Miss Spruce, and Mrs. Lupex.", "“스프루스 양이랑 루펙스 부인도 계십니다요."], ["He's away somewhere, in his tantrums again; and there's Mr.--\"", "그 양반은 또 심통이 나서 어디론가 가 버렸고요. 그리고 그—”"]], [["\"Is Miss Roper here?\"", "“로퍼 양은 있나?”"], ["he said, still whispering.", "그가 여전히 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes! Miss Mealyer's here,\"", "“아이참, 그럼요!"], ["said the girl, speaking in a cruelly loud voice.", "밀리어 아가씨도 계시지요.” 하녀가 잔인하리만큼 큰 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"She was in the dining-room just now, putting out the table.", "“방금까지 식당에서 상을 보고 계셨는걸요."], ["Miss Mealyer!\"", "밀리어 아가씨!”"], ["And the girl, as she called out the name, opened the dining-room door.", "그러고서 하녀는 그 이름을 소리쳐 부르며 식당 문을 열어젖혔다."], ["Johnny Eames felt that his knees were too weak to support him.", "조니 임스는 제 무릎이 몸을 버티기에는 너무 약해졌다고 느꼈다."]], [["But Miss Mealyer was not in the dining-room.", "그러나 밀리어 아가씨는 식당에 없었다."], ["She had perceived the advancing cab of her sworn adorer, and had thought it expedient to retreat from her domestic duties, and fortify herself among her brushes and ribbons.", "그녀는 제게 맹세를 바친 흠모자의 마차가 다가오는 것을 알아차리고, 집안일에서 물러나 제 솔과 리본 틈에서 몸을 단장하는 편이 상책이라고 여겼던 것이다."], ["Had it been possible that she should know how very weak and cowardly was the enemy against whom she was called upon to put herself in action, she might probably have fought her battle somewhat differently, and have achieved a speedy victory, at the cost of an energetic shot or two.", "자기가 나서서 맞서야 할 적이 얼마나 약하고 얼마나 겁이 많은 사내인지 알 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 아마 싸움을 사뭇 다르게 치러 힘찬 한두 방으로 값싸게 속전속결의 승리를 거두었을 것이다."], ["But she did not know.", "그러나 그녀는 알지 못했다."], ["She thought it probable that she might obtain power over him and manage him; but it did not occur to her that his legs were so weak beneath him that she might almost blow him over with a breath.", "그녀는 그를 손아귀에 넣고 마음대로 부릴 수 있을지도 모른다고 여기기는 했다. 그러나 그의 다리가 어찌나 후들거리는지 숨 한 번 불면 넘어뜨릴 수 있으리라는 데까지는 생각이 미치지 못했다."], ["None but the worst and most heartless of women know the extent of their own power over men;--as none but the worst and most heartless of men know the extent of their power over women.", "여자들 가운데 가장 못되고 가장 매정한 이들만이 남자에 대한 제 힘이 어디까지 미치는지를 안다. 남자들 가운데 가장 못되고 가장 매정한 이들만이 여자에 대한 제 힘이 어디까지 미치는지를 아는 것과 마찬가지다."], ["Amelia Roper was not a good specimen of the female sex, but there were worse women than her.", "어밀리아 로퍼가 여성이라는 성의 훌륭한 본보기는 아니었으나, 그녀보다 못한 여자도 있었다."]], [["\"She ain't there, Mr.", "“거기엔 안 계십니다요, 임스 나리."], ["Eames; but you'll see her in the drawen-room,\"", "그래도 응접실에 가시면 뵐 수 있을 겁니다요.”"], ["said the girl.", "하녀가 말했다."], ["\"And it's she'll be glad to see you back again, Mr. Eames.\"", "“그리고 나리께서 돌아오신 걸 제일 반가워하실 분이 바로 그 아가씨지요, 임스 나리.”"], ["But he scrupulously passed the door of the upstairs sitting-room, not even looking within it, and contrived to get himself into his own chamber without having encountered anybody.", "그러나 그는 위층 거실 문 앞을 조심스레 지나쳐 안을 들여다보지도 않았고, 아무와도 마주치지 않은 채 용케 제 방으로 들어섰다."], ["\"Here's yer 'ot water, Mr. Eames,\"", "“더운물 여기 있습니다요, 임스 나리.”"], ["said the girl, coming up to him after an interval of half-an-hour; \"and dinner'll be on the table in ten minutes.", "반 시간쯤 지나 그에게 올라온 하녀가 말했다. “그리고 십 분 뒤면 저녁상이 차려집니다요."], ["Mr. Cradell is come in, and so is missus's son.\"", "크래들 씨도 들어오셨고, 마님 아드님도 오셨어요.”"]], [["It was still open to him to go out and dine at some eating-house in the Strand.", "그에게는 아직 밖으로 나가 스트랜드의 어느 음식점에서 저녁을 먹을 길이 열려 있었다."], ["He could start out, leaving word that he was engaged, and so postpone the evil hour.", "선약이 있다는 말만 남기고 나섰다가 그 고약한 시각을 뒤로 미룰 수도 있었다."], ["He had almost made up his mind to do so, and certainly would have done it, had not the sitting-room door opened as he was on the landing-place.", "그는 그렇게 하기로 거의 마음을 굳혔고, 그가 층계참에 서 있을 때 거실 문이 열리지만 않았더라면 틀림없이 그렇게 했을 것이다."], ["The door opened, and he found himself confronting the assembled company.", "문이 열렸고, 그는 모여 있던 사람들과 얼굴을 맞대고 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["First came Cradell, and leaning on his arm, I regret to say, was Mrs. Lupex,--Egyptia conjux!", "맨 앞에 크래들이 나왔고, 애석하게도 그 팔에 매달려 있는 것은 루펙스 부인이었다—아이깁티아 콘육스!"], ["Then there came Miss Spruce with young Roper; Amelia and her mother brought up the rear together.", "그다음에 스프루스 양이 젊은 로퍼와 함께 나왔고, 어밀리아와 그 어머니가 나란히 뒤를 따랐다."], ["There was no longer question of flight now; and poor Eames, before he knew what he was doing, was carried down into the dining-room with the rest of the company.", "이제 달아날 여지는 없었다. 가엾은 임스는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 깨닫기도 전에 나머지 사람들과 함께 식당으로 떠밀려 내려갔다."], ["They were all glad to see him, and welcomed him back warmly, but he was so much beside himself that he could not ascertain whether Amelia's voice was joined with the others.", "다들 그를 보고 반가워하며 따뜻이 맞아 주었으나, 그는 넋이 나간 나머지 어밀리아의 목소리가 그 속에 섞여 있었는지조차 가려낼 수 없었다. 자기가 로퍼 부인과 루펙스 부인 사이에 앉아 있다는 사실을 알아차렸을 때 그는 이미 식탁 앞에 앉아 수프 접시를 앞에 두고 있었다."], ["He was already seated at table, and had before him a plate of soup, before he recognized the fact that he was sitting between Mrs. Roper and Mrs.", "뒤의 부인은 방에 들어서면서 크래들 씨에게서 제 몸을 떼어 놓았던 것이다."], ["Lupex. The latter lady had separated herself from Mr.", "“여러 사정을 생각하면 우리는 떨어져 앉는 편이 나을 것 같아요.”"], ["Cradell as she entered the room.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Under all the circumstances perhaps it will be better for us to be apart,\" she said.", "“여자란 아무리 몸을 사려도 지나치지 않으니까요."], ["\"A lady can't make herself too safe; can she, Mrs. Roper?", "안 그래요, 로퍼 부인?"], ["There's no danger between you and me, is there, Mr. Eames,--specially when Miss Amelia is opposite?\"", "당신과 나 사이야 위험할 게 없지요, 그렇지 않아요, 임스 씨—더구나 어밀리아 양이 맞은편에 앉아 있는데야?”"], ["The last words, however, were intended to be whispered into his ear.", "그러나 마지막 말은 그의 귀에 대고 속삭인다고 한 것이었다."]], [["But Johnny made no answer to her; contenting himself for the moment with wiping the perspiration from his brow.", "그러나 조니는 그녀에게 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 그저 이마의 땀을 닦는 것으로 그 순간을 넘겼다."], ["There was Amelia opposite to him, looking at him--the very Amelia to whom he had written, declining the honour of marrying her.", "맞은편에 어밀리아가 앉아 그를 바라보고 있었다—그와 혼인하는 영광을 사양하겠노라고 그가 편지를 써 보낸 바로 그 어밀리아가."], ["Of what her mood towards him might be, he could form no judgment from her looks.", "그를 대하는 그녀의 심사가 어떤지는 그 낯빛으로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었다."], ["Her face was simply stern and impassive, and she seemed inclined to eat her dinner in silence. A slight smile of derision had passed across her face as she heard Mrs.", "그녀의 얼굴은 그저 엄하고 무표정했으며, 그녀는 말없이 저녁을 먹을 작정인 듯했다."], ["Lupex whisper, and it might have been discerned that her nose, at the same time, became somewhat elevated; but she said not a word.", "루펙스 부인이 속삭이는 소리를 들었을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 얼핏 비웃음이 스쳤고, 동시에 그녀의 코끝이 조금 치켜 올라갔다는 것도 알아볼 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"I hope you've enjoyed yourself, Mr. Eames, among the vernal beauties of the country,\"", "“시골의 봄다운 아름다움 속에서 즐겁게 지내셨기를 바라요, 임스 씨.”"], ["said Mrs. Lupex.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Very much, thank you,\"", "“아주 즐거웠습니다, 고맙습니다.”"], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["\"There's nothing like the country at this autumnal season of the year.", "“한 해 중에 이 가을철만큼 시골이 좋은 때도 없지요."], ["As for myself, I've never been accustomed to remain in London after the breaking up of the beau monde.", "나로 말하자면 상류 사교계가 흩어진 뒤에 런던에 남아 있어 본 적이 한 번도 없어요."], ["We've usually been to Broadstairs, which is a very charming place, with most elegant society, but now--\"", "우리는 대개 브로드스테어스로 가곤 했는데, 아주 매력적인 곳인 데다 사람들도 더없이 우아하답니다. 그런데 이제는—”"], ["and she shook her head, by which all the company knew that she intended to allude to the sins of Mr. Lupex.", "그러고서 그녀가 고개를 저었으므로, 좌중은 모두 그녀가 루펙스 씨의 죄상을 넌지시 가리키려 한다는 것을 알았다."]], [["\"I'd never wish to sleep out of London for my part,\"", "“나 같으면 런던을 벗어나서 잠을 자고 싶은 마음은 없어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Roper.", "로퍼 부인이 말했다."], ["\"When a woman's got a house over her head, I don't think her mind's ever easy out of it.\"", "“여자란 제 머리 위에 제집 지붕이 있으면, 그 밖에 나가서는 결코 마음이 편하지 않은 법이거든요.”"]], [["She had not intended any reflection on Mrs. Lupex for not having a house of her own, but that lady immediately bristled up.", "그녀는 루펙스 부인이 제집이 없다는 것을 빗대려던 것이 아니었으나, 그 부인은 대뜸 발끈했다."], ["\"That's just what the snails say, Mrs. Roper.", "“그건 딱 달팽이가 하는 소리네요, 로퍼 부인."], ["And as for having a house of one's own, it's a very good thing, no doubt, sometimes; but that's according to circumstances.", "그리고 제집을 갖는 일로 말하자면, 그야 물론 때로는 아주 좋은 일이지요. 하지만 그것도 사정에 따라 다른 법이에요."], ["It has suited me lately to live in lodgings, but there's no knowing whether I mayn't fall lower than that yet, and have--\"", "나야 근래에 셋방살이가 형편에 맞았지만, 앞으로 그보다 더 떨어져서 셋방을 갖게 되지 말란 법도 없지요—”"], ["but here she stopped herself, and looking over at Mr. Cradell nodded her head.", "그러나 그녀는 여기서 말을 멈추고 크래들 씨 쪽을 건너다보며 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["\"And have to let them,\"", "“그리고 그 방을 세놓아야 하게 되겠지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Roper.", "로퍼 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I hope you'll be more lucky with your lodgers than I have been with some of mine.", "“나보다는 하숙인 복이 좋으시길 바라요. 나는 몇 사람한테 어지간히도 시달렸으니까요."], ["Jemima, hand the potatoes to Miss Spruce.", "제미마, 스프루스 양께 감자를 갖다 드려라."], ["Miss Spruce, do let me send you a little more gravy?", "스프루스 양, 고기 국물을 좀 더 드리게 해 주세요."], ["There's plenty here, really.\" Mrs.", "여기 정말로 넉넉하답니다.”"], ["Roper was probably thinking of Mr. Todgers.", "로퍼 부인은 아마도 토저스 씨를 떠올리고 있었을 것이다."]], [["\"I hope I shall,\"", "“나도 그러기를 바라요.”"], ["said Mrs. Lupex.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다."], ["\"But, as I was saying, Broadstairs is delightful.", "“하지만 아까 말씀드린 대로 브로드스테어스는 아주 좋아요."], ["Were you ever at Broadstairs, Mr. Cradell?\"", "브로드스테어스에 가 보신 적 있으세요, 크래들 씨?”"]], [["\"Never, Mrs. Lupex.", "“한 번도 없습니다, 루펙스 부인."], ["I generally go abroad in my leave.", "저는 휴가 때면 대개 외국으로 나갑니다."], ["One sees more of the world, you know.", "아시다시피 세상을 더 넓게 볼 수 있으니까요."], ["I was at Dieppe last June, and found that very delightful--though rather lonely.", "지난 유월에는 디에프에 있었는데 아주 즐거웠지요. 다만 좀 쓸쓸하긴 했습니다."], ["I shall go to Ostend this year; only December is so late for Ostend.", "올해는 오스텐드로 갈 참입니다. 다만 오스텐드에 십이월은 너무 늦은 철이지요."], ["It was a deuced shame my getting December, wasn't it, Johnny?\"", "내가 십이월에 걸린 건 지독하게 억울한 노릇이었어, 안 그런가, 조니?”"]], [["\"Yes, it was,\"", "“그래, 그랬지.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."], ["\"I managed better.\"", "“나는 더 잘 챙겼고.”"]], [["\"And what have you been doing, Mr. Eames?\"", "“그러면 임스 씨는 무얼 하고 계셨나요?”"], ["said Mrs. Lupex, with one of her sweetest smiles.", "루펙스 부인이 더없이 상냥한 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["\"Whatever it may have been, you've not been false to the cause of beauty, I'm sure.\"", "“무엇을 하셨든 미인을 받드는 대의를 저버리지는 않으셨을 테지요, 나는 그렇게 믿어요.”"], ["And she looked over to Amelia with a knowing smile.", "그러고서 그녀는 뜻을 담은 미소를 지으며 어밀리아 쪽을 건너다보았다."], ["But Amelia was engaged upon her plate, and went on with her dinner without turning her eyes either on Mrs. Lupex or on John Eames.", "그러나 어밀리아는 제 접시에 열중해 있었고, 루펙스 부인에게도 존 임스에게도 눈길을 돌리지 않은 채 저녁을 계속 먹었다."]], [["\"I haven't done anything particular,\"", "“딱히 한 일은 아무것도 없습니다.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."], ["\"I've just been staying with my mother.\"", "“그저 어머니와 함께 지내다 왔지요.”"]], [["\"We've been very social here, haven't we, Miss Amelia?\"", "“여기서는 우리끼리 아주 정답게 지냈지요, 안 그래요, 어밀리아 양?”"], ["continued Mrs. Lupex.", "루펙스 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["\"Only now and then a cloud comes across the heavens, and the lights at the banquet are darkened.\"", "“다만 이따금 하늘에 구름이 끼고 잔칫상의 불빛이 어두워질 뿐이지요.”"], ["Then she put her handkerchief up to her eyes, sobbing deeply, and they all knew that she was again alluding to the sins of her husband.", "그러고서 그녀는 손수건을 눈으로 가져가 깊이 흐느꼈고, 좌중은 모두 그녀가 또 제 남편의 죄상을 넌지시 가리키고 있음을 알았다."]], [["As soon as dinner was over the ladies with young Mr. Roper retired, and Eames and Cradell were left to take their wine over the dining-room fire,--or their glass of gin and water, as it might be.", "저녁 식사가 끝나자마자 부인들은 젊은 로퍼 씨와 함께 물러갔고, 임스와 크래들만 남아 식당 난롯가에서 포도주를—혹은 형편에 따라 진에 물을 탄 잔을—들게 되었다."], ["\"Well, Caudle, old fellow,\"", "“이보게 코들, 이 친구야.”"], ["said one.", "한 사람이 말했다."], ["\"Well, Johnny, my boy,\"", "“이보게 조니, 이 사람아.”"], ["said the other.", "다른 사람이 말했다."], ["\"What's the news at the office?\"", "“사무국에 무슨 소식은 없나?”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"Muggeridge has been playing the very mischief.\"", "“머거리지가 아주 못된 짓을 하고 있어.”"], ["Muggeridge was the second clerk in Cradell's room.", "머거리지는 크래들의 방 차석 서기였다."], ["\"We're going to put him into Coventry and not speak to him except officially.", "“우리는 그자를 따돌려서 공무가 아니면 말도 걸지 않기로 했네."], ["But to tell you the truth, my hands have been so full here at home, that I haven't thought much about the office.", "그렇지만 사실대로 말하자면 여기 집안일로 손이 어찌나 바빴는지 사무국 일은 별로 생각할 겨를도 없었어."], ["What am I to do about that woman?\"", "그 여자를 어떻게 하면 좋겠나?”"]], [["\"Do about her?", "“어떻게 하다니?"], ["How do about her?\"", "뭘 어떻게 한단 말인가?”"]], [["\"Yes; what am I to do about her?", "“그러니까, 그 여자를 어떻게 해야 하느냐 말일세."], ["How am I to manage with her?", "그 부인과 어떻게 꾸려 가야 하나?"], ["There's Lupex off again in one of his fits of jealousy.\"", "루펙스가 또 질투 발작이 나서 나가 버렸단 말이지.”"]], [["\"But it's not your fault, I suppose?\"", "“하지만 자네 잘못은 아니잖나?”"]], [["\"Well; I can't just say.", "“글쎄, 그건 잘라 말하기 어렵네. 나는 그 부인이 좋아."], ["I am fond of her, and that's the long and the short of it; deuced fond of her.\"", "길게 말할 것도 짧게 말할 것도 없이 그게 전부야. 지독하게 좋단 말이지.”"]], [["\"But, my dear Caudle, you know she's that man's wife.\"", "“하지만 이보게 코들, 그 부인은 그 사내의 아내가 아닌가.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, I know all about it.", "“아, 그럼, 나도 다 알고 있네."], ["I'm not going to defend myself.", "나를 두둔할 생각은 없어."], ["It's wrong, I know,--pleasant, but wrong.", "잘못된 일이지, 알고 있네—즐겁기는 하지만 잘못된 일이야."], ["But what's a fellow to do?", "그렇지만 사내가 어쩌겠나?"], ["I suppose in strict morality I ought to leave the lodgings.", "엄격한 도덕으로 따지자면 나는 이 하숙을 떠나야 마땅하겠지."], ["But, by George, I don't see why a man's to be turned out in that way.", "하지만 이런 참, 사내가 그런 식으로 쫓겨나야 할 까닭이 어디 있나."], ["And then I couldn't make a clean score with old mother Roper.", "게다가 로퍼 아줌마한테 셈을 깨끗이 치를 형편도 못 되고."], ["But I say, old fellow, who gave you the gold chain?\"", "그런데 이보게, 그 금줄은 누가 준 건가?”"]], [["\"Well; it was an old family friend at Guestwick; or rather, I should say, a man who said he knew my father.\"", "“음, 게스트윅의 오랜 집안 벗이지. 아니, 좀 더 정확히 말하자면 우리 아버지를 알았다고 하는 사람이야.”"]], [["\"And he gave you that because he knew your governor!", "“자네 아버님을 알았다고 그런 걸 주었단 말인가!"], ["Is there a watch to it?\"", "시계도 딸렸나?”"]], [["\"Yes, there's a watch.", "“그래, 시계도 있네."], ["It wasn't exactly that.", "그런데 꼭 그런 사연은 아니었어."], ["There was some trouble about a bull.", "황소 때문에 소동이 좀 있었거든."], ["To tell the truth, it was Lord De Guest; the queerest fellow, Caudle, you ever met in your life; but such a trump.", "사실대로 말하면 드 게스트 백작이야. 자네가 평생 만나 본 중에 제일 별난 양반이지, 코들."], ["I've got to go and dine with him at Christmas.\"", "그렇지만 참 대단한 분이야. 성탄절에 그 어른 댁에 가서 저녁을 먹기로 되어 있네.”"], ["And then the old story of the bull was told.", "그러고서 황소에 얽힌 그 옛이야기를 다 들려주었다."]], [["\"I wish I could find a lord in a field with a bull,\"", "“나도 들판에서 황소한테 쫓기는 귀족 나리 하나 만났으면 좋겠군.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."], ["We may, however, be permitted to doubt whether Mr. Cradell would have earned a watch even if he had had his wish.", "그러나 크래들 씨가 그 소원을 이루었다 한들 과연 시계를 벌어들였을지는 우리가 의심해 보아도 좋으리라."]], [["\"You see,\"", "“알다시피 말이야.”"], ["continued Cradell, reverting to the subject on which he most delighted to talk, \"I'm not responsible for that man's ill-conduct.\"", "크래들이 자기가 이야기하기를 가장 즐기는 화제로 되돌아가며 말을 이었다. “그 사내가 못되게 구는 것까지 내가 책임질 일은 아니잖나.”"]], [["\"Does anybody say you are?\"", "“누가 자네더러 책임지라고 하던가?”"]], [["\"No; nobody says so.", "“아니, 그렇게 말하는 사람이야 없지."], ["But people seem to think so.", "하지만 사람들이 그렇게 여기는 것 같단 말이야."], ["When he is by I hardly speak to her.", "그 사내가 곁에 있을 때는 나도 그 부인에게 말을 거의 걸지 않아."], ["She is thoughtless and giddy, as women are, and takes my arm, and that kind of thing, you know.", "그 부인이야 여자들이 다 그렇듯 생각이 짧고 들떠 있어서 내 팔짱을 낀다든지 그런 짓을 하지, 자네도 알잖나."], ["It makes him mad with rage, but upon my honour I don't think she means any harm.\"", "그러면 그자는 분해서 미쳐 날뛰지. 하지만 내 명예를 걸고 말하는데, 나는 그 부인에게 나쁜 뜻이 있다고는 생각하지 않네.”"]], [["\"I don't suppose she does,\"", "“나도 그렇지 않다고 보네.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"Well; she may or she mayn't.", "“글쎄, 그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있지."], ["I hope with all my heart she doesn't.\"", "나는 아니기를 진심으로 바라네.”"]], [["\"And where is he now?\"", "“그런데 그 사람은 지금 어디 있나?”"]], [["\"This is between ourselves, you know; but she went to find him this afternoon.", "“이건 우리끼리만 아는 얘긴데, 그 부인이 오늘 오후에 그 사내를 찾으러 갔네."], ["Unless he gives her money she can't stay here, nor, for the matter of that, will she be able to go away.", "그자가 돈을 내주지 않으면 그 부인은 여기 머물 수도 없고, 하기야 그렇다고 떠날 수도 없는 형편이야."], ["If I mention something to you, you won't tell any one?\"", "내가 한 가지 이야기하면 자네 아무한테도 말하지 않겠나?”"]], [["\"Of course I won't.\"", "“물론 말하지 않겠네.”"]], [["\"I wouldn't have it known to any one for the world.", "“이건 세상 누구에게도 알려지면 안 되네."], ["I've lent her seven pounds ten.", "내가 그 부인에게 칠 파운드 십 실링을 빌려주었어."], ["It's that which makes me so short with mother Roper.\"", "로퍼 아줌마한테 셈이 그렇게 밀린 것도 그것 때문일세.”"]], [["\"Then I think you're a fool for your pains.\"", "“그렇다면 자네는 애만 쓰고 바보짓을 한 걸세.”"]], [["\"Ah, that's so like you.", "“아, 딱 자네다운 소리로군."], ["I always said you'd no feeling of real romance.", "나는 늘 자네한테는 참된 낭만이라는 감정이 없다고 말해 왔지."], ["If I cared for a woman I'd give her the coat off my back.\"", "나 같으면 마음에 둔 여자한테는 등에 걸친 웃옷이라도 벗어 줄 걸세.”"]], [["\"I'd do better than that,\"", "“나라면 그보다 더한 것도 하겠네.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."], ["\"I'd give her the heart out of my body.", "“내 몸에서 심장을 꺼내 주겠어."], ["I'd be chopped up alive for a girl I loved; but it shouldn't be for another man's wife.\"", "사랑하는 아가씨를 위해서라면 산 채로 토막이 나도 좋아. 하지만 남의 아내를 위해서라면 안 될 말이지.”"]], [["\"That's a matter of taste.", "“그거야 취향 문제지."], ["But she's been to Lupex to-day at that house he goes to in Drury Lane.", "아무튼 그 부인이 오늘 드루리 레인에 있는, 루펙스가 드나드는 그 집으로 그자를 찾아갔어."], ["She had a terrible scene there.", "거기서 지독한 소동이 벌어졌지."], ["He was going to commit suicide in the middle of the street, and she declares that it all comes from jealousy.", "그자는 길 한복판에서 목숨을 끊겠다고 나섰고, 부인은 그게 전부 질투에서 나온 일이라고 잘라 말하더군."], ["Think what a time I have of it--standing always, as one may say, on gunpowder.", "내가 어떤 세월을 보내고 있는지 좀 생각해 보게—말하자면 늘 화약 더미 위에 서 있는 셈이야."], ["He may turn up here any moment, you know.", "그자가 언제 여기 들이닥칠지 모르는 노릇이지."], ["But, upon my word, for the life of me I cannot desert her.", "하지만 정말이지, 죽었다 깨어나도 나는 그 부인을 저버릴 수가 없네."], ["If I were to turn my back on her she wouldn't have a friend in the world.", "내가 그 부인에게 등을 돌린다면 그 부인한테는 이 세상에 벗이라고는 하나도 남지 않을 테니까. 그런데 L."], ["And how's L. D.?", "D.는 어떻게 되었나?"], ["I'll tell you what it is--you'll have some trouble with the divine Amelia.\"", "내 한마디 해 두겠는데—자네는 그 거룩하신 어밀리아 때문에 곤경을 좀 치를 걸세.”"]], [["\"Shall I?\"", "“내가 그럴까?”"]], [["\"By Jove, you will.", "“맹세코 그럴 걸세."], ["But how's L. D.", "그런데 그동안 L."], ["all this time?\"", "D.는 어떻게 되었나?”"]], [["\"L. D.", "“L."], ["is engaged to be married to a man named Adolphus Crosbie,\"", "D.는 어돌퍼스 크로즈비라는 사내와 혼인하기로 약혼했네.”"], ["said poor Johnny, slowly.", "가엾은 조니가 천천히 말했다."], ["\"If you please, we will not say any more about her.\"", "“미안하지만 그 사람 이야기는 더 하지 마세.”"]], [["\"Whew--w--w!", "“휘이—이—이!"], ["That's what makes you so down in the mouth!", "그래서 자네가 그렇게 풀이 죽어 있었군!"], ["L. D.", "L."], ["going to marry Crosbie!", "D.가 크로즈비와 혼인하다니!"], ["Why, that's the man who is to be the new secretary at the General Committee Office.", "아니, 그 사람이 바로 일반위원회 사무국의 새 사무국장이 될 사람 아닌가."], ["Old Huffle Scuffle, who was their chair, has come to us, you know.", "알다시피 거기 의장으로 있던 늙은 허플 스커플이 우리 쪽으로 왔거든. G."], ["There's been a general move at the G. C., and this Crosbie has got to be secretary.", "C.에 인사가 크게 돌았고, 그 크로즈비가 사무국장 자리를 차지하게 된 거야."], ["He's a lucky chap, isn't he?\"", "운 좋은 친구지, 안 그런가?”"]], [["\"I don't know anything about his luck.", "“그자의 운수 따위야 나는 아무것도 모르네."], ["He's one of those fellows that make me hate them the first time I look at them.", "그자는 처음 눈에 들어오는 순간부터 미워지는 그런 부류야."], ["I've a sort of a feeling that I shall live to kick him some day.\"", "언젠가 내가 살아서 그자를 걷어차게 되리라는 느낌 같은 게 있어.”"]], [["\"That's the time, is it?", "“사정이 그렇게 되었단 말이지?"], ["Then I suppose Amelia will have it all her own way now.\"", "그럼 이제 어밀리아가 제 뜻대로 다 하겠는걸.”"]], [["\"I'll tell you what, Caudle.", "“내 한마디 해 두겠는데, 코들."], ["I'd sooner get up through the trap-door, and throw myself off the roof into the area, than marry Amelia Roper.\"", "나는 어밀리아 로퍼와 혼인하느니 차라리 다락문으로 기어 올라가 지붕에서 지하 앞마당으로 몸을 던지겠네.”"]], [["\"Have you and she had any conversation since you came back?\"", "“돌아온 뒤로 그 아가씨와 무슨 이야기라도 나누었나?”"]], [["\"Not a word.\"", "“한마디도 안 했네.”"]], [["\"Then I tell you fairly you've got trouble before you.", "“그렇다면 솔직히 말해 두는데 자네 앞에 곤경이 놓여 있어."], ["Amelia and Maria--Mrs. Lupex, I mean--are as thick as thieves just at present, and they have been talking you over.", "어밀리아와 머라이어가—루펙스 부인 말일세—요즘 도둑들처럼 짝짜꿍이 맞아서 자네 이야기를 해 왔거든."], ["Maria--that is, Mrs. Lupex--lets it all out to me.", "머라이어가—그러니까 루펙스 부인이 나한테 죄다 흘려 준다네."], ["You'll have to mind where you are, old fellow.\"", "자네 발밑을 조심해야 할 걸세, 이 친구야.”"]], [["Eames was not inclined to discuss the matter any further, so he finished his toddy in silence.", "임스는 그 문제를 더 이야기하고 싶지 않았으므로 말없이 제 토디를 마저 비웠다."], ["Cradell, however, who felt that there was something in his affairs of which he had reason to be proud, soon returned to the story of his own very extraordinary position.", "그러나 크래들은 제 형편 가운데 자랑할 만한 구석이 있다고 여기던 터라 이내 제 자신의 몹시 별난 처지에 얽힌 이야기로 돌아갔다."], ["\"By Jove, I don't know that a man was ever so circumstanced,\"", "“맹세코 사내가 이런 처지에 놓인 적이 여태 있었나 싶네.” 그가 말했다."], ["he said. \"She looks to me to protect her, and yet what can I do?\"", "“그 부인은 나더러 지켜 달라고 기대는데, 그러면서 내가 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가?”"]], [["At last Cradell got up, and declared that he must go to the ladies.", "마침내 크래들이 일어서더니 부인들에게 가 보아야겠다고 잘라 말했다."], ["\"She's so nervous, that unless she has some one to countenance her she becomes unwell.\"", "“그 부인은 어찌나 예민한지, 곁에서 힘이 되어 주는 사람이 없으면 몸이 상하고 만다네.”"]], [["Eames declared his purpose of going to the divan, or to the theatre, or to take a walk in the streets.", "임스는 끽연실이든 극장이든 아니면 거리를 좀 걷든 하겠노라고 잘라 말했다."], ["The smiles of beauty had no longer charms for him in Burton Crescent.", "버턴 크레슨트에서는 미인의 미소가 그에게 더는 아무런 매력이 없었다."]], [["\"They'll expect you to take a cup of tea the first night,\"", "“돌아온 첫날 밤이니 다들 자네가 차 한 잔은 들 거라고 기다릴 걸세.”"], ["said Cradell; but Eames declared that they might expect it.", "크래들이 말했다. 그러나 임스는 기다리려면 기다리라지 하고 잘라 말했다."]], [["\"I'm in no humour for it,\"", "“그럴 기분이 아닐세.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I'll tell you what, Cradell, I shall leave this place, and take rooms for myself somewhere.", "“내 한마디 해 두겠는데, 크래들, 나는 이 집을 떠나 어디든 따로 방을 얻을 참이야."], ["I'll never go into a lodging-house again.\"", "다시는 하숙집에 들어가지 않겠네.”"]], [["As he so spoke, he was standing at the dining-room door; but he was not allowed to escape in this easy way.", "그렇게 말할 때 그는 식당 문 앞에 서 있었다. 그러나 그렇게 쉽게 빠져나가도록 놓아주지는 않았다."], ["Jemima, as he went out into the passage, was there with a three-cornered note in her hand.", "그가 복도로 나서자 제미마가 세모꼴로 접은 쪽지를 손에 들고 거기 서 있었다."], ["\"From Miss Mealyer,\" she said.", "“밀리어 아가씨한테서 온 겁니다요.”"], ["\"Miss Mealyer is in the back parlour all by herself.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “밀리어 아가씨는 뒤쪽 응접실에 혼자 계십니다요.”"]], [["Poor Johnny took the note, and read it by the lamp over the front door.", "가엾은 조니는 쪽지를 받아 들고 현관문 위에 걸린 등불에 비추어 읽었다."]], [["\"Are you not going to speak to me on the day of your return?", "“돌아오신 날에 저에게 말도 걸지 않으실 건가요?"], ["It cannot be that you will leave the house without seeing me for a moment.", "잠깐이라도 저를 보지 않고 이 집을 나서시다니 그럴 리는 없겠지요."], ["I am in the back parlour.\"", "저는 뒤쪽 응접실에 있어요.”"]], [["When he had read these words, he paused in the passage, with his hat on.", "그 말을 다 읽고 나서 그는 모자를 쓴 채 복도에 우두커니 서 있었다."], ["Jemima, who could not understand why any young man should hesitate as to seeing his lady-love in the back parlour alone, whispered to him again, in her audible way, \"Miss Mealyer is there, sir; and all the rest on 'em's upstairs!\"", "제미마는 젊은 사내가 뒤쪽 응접실에서 제 연인을 단둘이 만나는 일을 두고 왜 망설이는지 도무지 알 수 없었으므로, 남들도 다 들리는 그 목소리로 다시 그에게 속삭였다. “밀리어 아가씨가 저기 계십니다요, 나리."], ["So compelled, Eames put down his hat, and walked with slow steps into the back parlour.", "나머지 사람들은 죄다 위층에 있고요!” 그렇게 떠밀린 임스는 모자를 내려놓고 느린 걸음으로 뒤쪽 응접실로 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["How was it to be with the enemy?", "적은 어떤 모습으로 나올 것인가?"], ["Was he to encounter Amelia in anger, or Amelia in love?", "그가 맞닥뜨릴 것은 성이 난 어밀리아인가, 사랑에 빠진 어밀리아인가?"], ["She had seemed to be stern and defiant when he had ventured to steal a look at her across the dining-table, and now he expected that she would turn upon him with loud threatenings and protestations as to her wrongs.", "식탁 너머로 슬쩍 눈길을 던져 보았을 때 그녀는 엄하고 도전적으로 보였고, 그래서 그는 이제 그녀가 제 억울함을 두고 큰 소리로 을러대고 따지고 들리라 예상했다."], ["But it was not so.", "그러나 그렇지 않았다."], ["When he entered the room she was standing with her back to him, leaning on the mantel-piece, and at the first moment she did not essay to speak.", "그가 방으로 들어섰을 때 그녀는 그에게 등을 돌린 채 벽난로 선반에 기대어 서 있었고, 처음 한동안은 입을 열려고도 하지 않았다."], ["He walked into the middle of the room and stood there, waiting for her to begin.", "그는 방 한가운데로 걸어 들어가 거기 선 채 그녀가 시작하기를 기다렸다."]], [["\"Shut the door!\"", "“문 닫으세요!”"], ["she said, looking over her shoulder.", "그녀가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 말했다."], ["\"I suppose you don't want the girl to hear all you've got to say to me!\"", "“저한테 하실 말씀을 하녀가 죄다 듣는 건 바라지 않으실 테니까요!”"]], [["Then he shut the door; but still Amelia stood with her back to him, leaning upon the mantel-piece.", "그래서 그는 문을 닫았다. 그러나 어밀리아는 여전히 그에게 등을 돌린 채 벽난로 선반에 기대어 서 있었다."]], [["It did not seem that he had much to say, for he remained perfectly silent.", "그에게는 할 말이 그리 많지 않은 듯했다. 그는 여전히 완전히 잠자코 있었으니까."]], [["\"Well!\"", "“어머!”"], ["said Amelia, after a long pause, and she then again looked over her shoulder.", "한참 만에 어밀리아가 그렇게 말하고는 다시 어깨 너머로 돌아보았다."], ["\"Well, Mr. Eames!\"", "“어머, 임스 씨!”"]], [["\"Jemima gave me your note, and so I've come,\"", "“제미마가 쪽지를 주기에 이렇게 온 겁니다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"And is this the way we meet!\"", "“그래, 우리가 만나는 방식이 이런 건가요!”"], ["she exclaimed, turning suddenly upon him, and throwing her long black hair back over her shoulders.", "그녀가 갑자기 그를 향해 돌아서며, 길고 검은 머리채를 어깨 뒤로 넘기면서 외쳤다. 그녀에게 얼마간의 아름다움이 있는 것만은 분명했다."], ["There certainly was some beauty about her.", "눈은 크고 반짝였으며 어깨선도 고왔다."], ["Her eyes were large and bright, and her shoulders were well turned.", "화가가 유디트를 그리는 데 모델로 삼기에는 알맞았을 것이다."], ["She might have done as an artist's model for a Judith, but I doubt whether any man, looking well into her face, could think that she would do well as a wife.", "그러나 그녀의 얼굴을 찬찬히 들여다본 사내라면 그녀가 아내 노릇을 잘하리라고 생각할 수 있었을지 나는 의심스럽다."], ["\"Oh, John, is it to be thus, after love such as ours?\"", "“아, 존, 우리 같은 사랑을 나누고서도 이래야 하나요?”"], ["And she clasped her hands together, and stood before him.", "그러고서 그녀는 두 손을 마주 잡고 그의 앞에 섰다."]], [["\"I don't know what you mean,\"", "“무슨 말씀인지 모르겠는데요.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"If you are engaged to marry L.", "“L. D.와 혼인하기로 약혼하셨다면 지금 당장 그렇다고 말씀하세요."], ["D., tell me so at once. Be a man, and speak out, sir.\"", "사내답게 툭 털어놓으시라고요, 임스 씨.”"]], [["\"No,\"", "“아닙니다.”"], ["said Eames; \"I am not engaged to marry the lady to whom you allude.\"", "임스가 말했다. “나는 그쪽이 말하는 그 아가씨와 혼인하기로 약혼하지 않았어요.”"]], [["\"On your honour?\"", "“명예를 걸고요?”"]], [["\"I won't have her spoken about.", "“그 사람 이야기는 입에 올리지 마세요."], ["I'm not going to marry her, and that's enough.\"", "나는 그 사람과 혼인하지 않을 거고, 그러면 된 거 아닙니까.”"]], [["\"Do you think that I wish to speak of her? What can L.", "“내가 그 사람 이야기를 하고 싶어 한다고 생각하세요?"], ["D. be to me as long as she is nothing to you?", "그 사람이 당신한테 아무것도 아닌 이상, L."], ["Oh, Johnny, why did you write me that heartless letter?\"", "D.가 나한테 무엇이겠어요? 아, 조니, 왜 나한테 그런 매정한 편지를 쓰셨나요?”"], ["Then she leaned upon his shoulder--or attempted to do so.", "그러고서 그녀는 그의 어깨에 기댔다—아니, 기대려고 했다."]], [["I cannot say that Eames shook her off, seeing that he lacked the courage to do so; but he shuffled his shoulder about so that the support was uneasy to her, and she was driven to stand erect again.", "임스가 그녀를 뿌리쳤다고는 말할 수 없다. 그에게는 그럴 용기가 없었으니까."], ["\"Why did you write that cruel letter?\"", "그러나 그가 어깨를 이리저리 들썩거리는 바람에 기대기가 불편해진 그녀는 하는 수 없이 다시 몸을 곧추세워야 했다. “왜 그런 모진 편지를 쓰셨어요?”"], ["she said again.", "그녀가 다시 말했다."]], [["\"Because I thought it best, Amelia.", "“그게 제일 낫다고 생각했으니까요, 어밀리아."], ["What's a man to do with ninety pounds a year, you know?\"", "한 해 구십 파운드로 사내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["\"But your mother allows you twenty.\"", "“하지만 어머님이 이십 파운드를 대 주시잖아요.”"]], [["\"And what's a man to do with a hundred and ten?\"", "“그러면 백십 파운드로 사내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["\"Rising five pounds every year,\"", "“해마다 오 파운드씩 오르고요.”"], ["said the well-informed Amelia.", "사정에 밝은 어밀리아가 말했다."], ["\"Of course we should live here, with mamma, and you would just go on paying her as you do now.", "“물론 우리는 여기서 엄마와 함께 살 거고, 당신은 지금처럼 엄마한테 하숙비를 내면 그만이지요."], ["If your heart was right, Johnny, you wouldn't think so much about money.", "마음이 제자리에 있다면, 조니, 돈 생각을 그렇게까지 하지는 않을 거예요."], ["If you loved me--as you said you did--\"", "당신 말대로 정말 나를 사랑한다면—”"], ["Then a little sob came, and the words were stopped.", "그러고서 작은 흐느낌이 새어 나오며 말이 끊겼다."], ["The words were stopped, but she was again upon his shoulder.", "말은 끊겼으나 그녀는 다시 그의 어깨에 얹혀 있었다."], ["What was he to do?", "그가 어쩌겠는가?"], ["In truth, his only wish was to escape, and yet his arm, quite in opposition to his own desires, found its way round her waist.", "사실 그의 유일한 바람은 달아나는 것이었는데도, 그의 팔은 제 뜻과는 아주 딴판으로 그녀의 허리를 감아 들어가고 말았다."], ["In such a combat a woman has so many points in her favour!", "그런 싸움에서는 여자 쪽에 유리한 점이 어찌나 많은지!"], ["\"Oh, Johnny,\"", "“아, 조니.”"], ["she said again, as soon as she felt the pressure of his arm.", "그의 팔에 힘이 들어가는 것을 느끼자마자 그녀가 다시 말했다."], ["\"Gracious, what a beautiful watch you've got,\"", "“어머나, 참 고운 시계를 가지셨네요.”"], ["and she took the trinket out of his pocket.", "그러고서 그녀는 그의 주머니에서 그 패물을 꺼냈다."], ["\"Did you buy that?\"", "“이거 사신 거예요?”"]], [["\"No; it was given to me.\"", "“아니요, 받은 겁니다.”"]], [["\"John Eames, did L.", "“존 임스, L."], ["D. give it you?\"", "D.가 준 건가요?”"]], [["\"No, no, no,\"", "“아니, 아니, 아닙니다.”"], ["he shouted, stamping on the floor as he spoke.", "그가 말하면서 발로 바닥을 구르며 소리쳤다."]], [["\"Oh, I beg your pardon,\"", "“어머, 미안해요.”"], ["said Amelia, quelled for the moment by his energy.", "어밀리아가 그 기세에 잠시 눌려 말했다."], ["\"Perhaps it was your mother.\"", "“어머님이 주셨나 보죠.”"]], [["\"No; it was a man.", "“아니요, 어떤 남자가 준 겁니다."], ["Never mind about the watch now.\"", "이제 시계 이야기는 그만하세요.”"]], [["\"I wouldn't mind anything, Johnny, if you would tell me that you loved me again.", "“나를 다시 사랑한다고만 말해 주시면 나는 아무래도 좋아요, 조니."], ["Perhaps I oughtn't to ask you, and it isn't becoming in a lady; but how can I help it, when you know you've got my heart.", "이런 걸 물으면 안 되는지도 모르고, 숙녀답지 못한 일인지도 모르지요. 하지만 내 마음을 당신이 가지고 있는 걸 아는데 내가 어쩌겠어요."], ["Come upstairs and have tea with us now, won't you?\"", "이제 위층으로 올라가서 우리와 차 한 잔 드세요, 네?”"]], [["What was he to do?", "그가 어쩌겠는가?"], ["He said that he would go up and have tea; and as he led her to the door he put down his face and kissed her.", "그는 올라가서 차를 들겠다고 말했다. 그리고 그녀를 문으로 이끌면서 얼굴을 숙여 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["Oh, Johnny Eames!", "아, 조니 임스!"], ["But then a woman in such a contest has so many points in her favour.", "그러나 그런 겨루기에서는 여자 쪽에 유리한 점이 참으로 많은 법이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXX. IS IT FROM HIM?", "# 제30장. 「그이에게서 온 건가요?」"]], [["I have already declared that Crosbie wrote and posted the fatal letter to Allington, and we must now follow it down to that place.", "크로즈비가 앨링턴으로 그 치명적인 편지를 써서 부쳤다는 것은 내가 이미 말해 두었으니, 이제 우리는 그 편지를 따라 그곳까지 내려가 보아야 한다."], ["On the morning following the squire's return to his own house, Mrs. Crump, the post-mistress at Allington, received a parcel by post directed to herself.", "지주가 제 집으로 돌아온 이튿날 아침, 앨링턴의 우체국장 크럼프 부인은 자기 앞으로 온 소포를 우편으로 받았다."], ["She opened it, and found an enclosure addressed to Mrs. Dale, with a written request that she would herself deliver it into that lady's own hand at once.", "부인이 그것을 뜯어 보니 안에는 데일 부인 앞으로 된 편지 한 통이 들어 있었고, 그것을 즉시 그 부인의 손에 직접 건네 달라는 부탁이 적혀 있었다."], ["This was Crosbie's letter.", "이것이 크로즈비의 편지였다."]], [["\"It's from Miss Lily's gentleman,\" said Mrs.", "“릴리 아가씨의 그 신사분한테서 온 것입니다요.”"], ["Crump, looking at the handwriting.", "크럼프 부인은 글씨를 들여다보며 말했다."], ["\"There's something up, or he wouldn't be writing to her mamma in this way.\"", "“무슨 일이 난 게 틀림없습니다요. 안 그러고서야 그 어머니께 이런 식으로 편지를 쓸 리가 없지요.”"], ["But Mrs. Crump lost no time in putting on her bonnet, and trudging up with the letter to the Small House.", "그러면서도 크럼프 부인은 지체 없이 모자를 눌러쓰고 편지를 든 채 작은 집까지 터덕터덕 걸어 올라갔다."], ["\"I must see the missus herself,\" said Mrs.", "“마님을 직접 뵈어야겠습니다요.”"], ["Crump. Whereupon Mrs.", "크럼프 부인이 말했다."], ["Dale was called downstairs into the hall, and there received the packet.", "그리하여 데일 부인이 아래층 현관으로 불려 내려와 그 꾸러미를 받았다."], ["Lily was in the breakfast-parlour, and had seen the post-mistress arrive;--had seen also that she carried a letter in her hand.", "릴리는 아침 식사 방에 있다가 우체국장이 오는 것을 보았고, 손에 편지를 들고 있는 것도 보았다."], ["For a moment she had thought that it was for her, and imagined that the old woman had brought it herself from simple good-nature.", "잠깐 동안 릴리는 그것이 제게 온 것이라고, 그 노파가 그저 마음이 좋아 손수 가져다준 것이라고 생각했다."], ["But Lily, when she heard her mother mentioned, instantly withdrew and shut the parlour door.", "그러나 어머니를 찾는다는 말을 듣자 릴리는 곧바로 물러나 방문을 닫았다."], ["Her heart misgave her that something was wrong, but she hardly tried to think what it might be.", "무언가 잘못되었다는 예감에 가슴이 내려앉았으나, 그것이 무엇일지 굳이 헤아려 보려 하지는 않았다."], ["After all, the regular postman might bring the letter she herself expected.", "어차피 제가 기다리는 편지는 늘 오던 우편배달부가 가져올 수도 있는 것이었다."], ["Bell was not yet downstairs, and she stood alone over the tea-cups on the breakfast-table, feeling that there was something for her to fear.", "벨은 아직 내려오지 않았고, 릴리는 아침 식탁의 찻잔들 앞에 혼자 서서 자기가 두려워해야 할 무언가가 있다고 느꼈다."], ["Her mother did not come at once into the room, but, after a pause of a moment or two, went again upstairs.", "어머니는 곧바로 방으로 들어오지 않고, 잠깐 사이를 두었다가 다시 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["So she remained, either standing against the table, or at the window, or seated in one of the two arm-chairs, for a space of ten minutes, when Bell entered the room.", "그리하여 릴리는 식탁에 기대섰다가, 창가에 갔다가, 안락의자 두 개 가운데 하나에 앉았다가 하면서 십 분쯤을 그렇게 있었고, 그때 벨이 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["\"Isn't mamma down yet?\"", "“엄마는 아직 안 내려오셨니?”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Bell,\"", "“언니,”"], ["said Lily, \"something has happened.", "릴리가 말했다. “무슨 일이 생겼어."], ["Mamma has got a letter.\"", "엄마한테 편지가 왔어.”"]], [["\"Happened!", "“생겼다니!"], ["What has happened?", "무슨 일이 생겼다는 거야?"], ["Is anybody ill?", "누가 아프기라도 해?"], ["Who is the letter from?\"", "편지는 누구한테서 온 건데?”"], ["And Bell was going to return through the door in search of her mother.", "그러면서 벨은 어머니를 찾으러 문으로 도로 나가려 했다."]], [["\"Stop, Bell,\"", "“잠깐, 언니.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"Do not go to her yet.", "“아직 엄마한테 가지 마."], ["I think it's from--Adolphus.\"", "그거, 어돌퍼스한테서 온 것 같아.”"]], [["\"Oh, Lily, what do you mean?\"", "“아, 릴리, 그게 무슨 소리야?”"]], [["\"I don't know, dear.", "“나도 몰라, 언니."], ["We'll wait a little longer.", "조금만 더 기다려 보자."], ["Don't look like that, Bell.\"", "그런 얼굴 하지 마, 언니.”"], ["And Lily strove to appear calm, and strove almost successfully.", "그러면서 릴리는 침착해 보이려 애썼고, 거의 성공했다."]], [["\"You have frightened me so,\"", "“네가 나를 이렇게 겁먹게 만들잖니.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I am frightened myself.", "“나도 겁이 나."], ["He only sent me one line yesterday, and now he has sent nothing.", "어제는 그이가 겨우 한 줄 보냈는데, 오늘은 아무것도 안 보냈어."], ["If some misfortune should have happened to him!", "그이한테 무슨 변고라도 생긴 거라면!"], ["Mrs. Crump brought down the letter herself to mamma, and that is so odd, you know.\"", "크럼프 부인이 편지를 손수 엄마한테 가져왔다는 것도 참 이상하잖아.”"]], [["\"Are you sure it was from him?\"", "“정말 그이한테서 온 거야?”"]], [["\"No; I have not spoken to her.", "“아니, 엄마하고 얘기해 본 건 아니야."], ["I will go up to her now.", "지금 올라가 볼게."], ["Don't you come, Bell.", "언니는 오지 마."], ["Oh! Bell, do not look so unhappy.\"", "아, 언니, 그렇게 슬픈 얼굴 하지 마.”"], ["She then went over and kissed her sister, and after that, with very gentle steps, made her way up to her mother's room.", "그러고서 릴리는 언니에게 다가가 입을 맞추고는, 아주 조용한 걸음으로 어머니 방까지 올라갔다."], ["\"Mamma, may I come in?\"", "“엄마, 들어가도 돼요?”"], ["she said.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh!", "“아!"], ["my child!\"", "내 딸아!”"]], [["\"I know it is from him, mamma.", "“그이한테서 온 거죠, 엄마."], ["Tell me all at once.\"", "한꺼번에 다 말해 주세요.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale had read the letter.", "데일 부인은 편지를 이미 읽은 뒤였다."], ["With quick, glancing eyes, she had made herself mistress of its whole contents, and was already aware of the nature and extent of the sorrow which had come upon them.", "빠르게 훑는 눈길로 그 내용 전부를 제 것으로 만들었고, 자기들에게 닥친 슬픔이 어떤 것이며 얼마나 큰 것인지 이미 알고 있었다."], ["It was a sorrow that admitted of no hope.", "그것은 희망이라고는 허락하지 않는 슬픔이었다."], ["The man who had written that letter could never return again; nor if he should return could he be welcomed back to them.", "그런 편지를 쓴 남자는 결코 다시 돌아올 수 없었다. 설령 돌아온다 한들 그를 다시 반겨 맞아들일 수는 없었다."], ["The blow had fallen, and it was to be borne.", "벼락은 이미 떨어졌고, 이제 견뎌 내는 수밖에 없었다."], ["Inside the letter to herself had been a very small note addressed to Lily.", "제 앞으로 온 편지 속에는 릴리 앞으로 된 아주 작은 쪽지가 한 장 들어 있었다."], ["\"Give her the enclosed,\"", "“동봉한 것을 따님에게 주십시오.”"], ["Crosbie had said in his letter, \"if you do not now think it wrong to do so.", "크로즈비는 편지에 그렇게 적었다. “그렇게 하는 것이 잘못이라고 여기지 않으신다면 말입니다."], ["I have left it open, that you may read it.\" Mrs.", "부인께서 읽어 보실 수 있도록 봉하지 않고 두었습니다.”"], ["Dale, however, had not yet read it, and she now concealed it beneath her handkerchief.", "그러나 데일 부인은 아직 그것을 읽지 않았고, 지금은 손수건 밑에 감추어 두었다."]], [["I will not repeat at length Crosbie's letter to Mrs. Dale.", "크로즈비가 데일 부인에게 보낸 편지를 여기에 길게 옮기지는 않겠다."], ["It covered four sides of letter-paper, and was such a letter that any man who wrote it must have felt himself to be a rascal.", "그것은 편지지 넉 장을 채웠고, 그런 편지를 쓴 사람이라면 누구든 스스로를 악당이라고 느끼지 않을 수 없는 그런 편지였다. 우리는 그가 그것을 쓰기 어려워했다는 것을 보았다."], ["We saw that he had difficulty in writing it, but the miracle was, that any man could have found it possible to write it.", "그러나 놀라운 일은, 사람이 그런 편지를 끝내 써낼 수 있었다는 사실 그 자체였다. “부인께서 저를 저주하시리라는 것을 압니다.”"], ["\"I know you will curse me,\"", "그는 이렇게 썼다."], ["said he; \"and I deserve to be cursed.", "“그리고 저는 저주받아 마땅합니다."], ["I know that I shall be punished for this, and I must bear my punishment.", "이 일로 벌을 받으리라는 것을 알고, 그 벌을 견뎌야 한다는 것도 압니다."], ["My worst punishment will be this,--that I never more shall hold up my head again.\"", "제게 가장 무거운 벌은 이것입니다—다시는 고개를 들고 살 수 없으리라는 것.”"], ["And then, again, he said:--\"My only excuse is my conviction that I should never make her happy.", "그리고 또 이렇게 말했다. “제 유일한 변명은, 제가 그분을 행복하게 해 드릴 수 없으리라는 확신뿐입니다."], ["She has been brought up as an angel, with pure thoughts, with holy hopes, with a belief in all that is good, and high, and noble.", "그분은 천사처럼, 순결한 생각과 거룩한 소망과 선하고 높고 고귀한 모든 것에 대한 믿음 속에서 자라났습니다. 저는 평생 낮고 비열하고 천한 것들에 둘러싸여 살아왔습니다."], ["I have been surrounded through my whole life by things low, and mean, and ignoble.", "제가 어떻게 그분과, 또 그분이 어떻게 저와 함께 살 수 있겠습니까?"], ["How could I live with her, or she with me?", "이제는 그것이 그러하다는 것을 압니다."], ["I know now that this is so; but my fault has been that I did not know it when I was there with her.", "그러나 제 잘못은 그분 곁에 있을 때 그것을 알지 못했다는 데 있습니다.”"], ["I choose to tell you all,\"", "편지 끝머리에 이르러 그는 이렇게 이어 갔다."], ["he continued, towards the end of the letter, \"and therefore I let you know that I have engaged myself to marry another woman.", "“저는 모든 것을 다 말씀드리기로 했습니다. 그러므로 제가 다른 여자와 혼인을 약속했다는 것도 알려 드립니다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["I can foresee how bitter will be your feelings when you read this: but they will not be so bitter as mine while I write it.", "이것을 읽으실 때 부인의 심정이 얼마나 쓰라릴지 저는 눈에 선히 그려 볼 수 있습니다. 그러나 이것을 쓰는 제 심정보다 쓰라리지는 않을 것입니다."], ["Yes; I am already engaged to one who will suit me, and whom I may suit.", "그렇습니다. 저는 이미 저에게 어울리고 저 또한 어울릴 수 있는 사람과 약혼했습니다."], ["You will not expect me to speak ill of her who is to be near and dear to me.", "제게 가깝고 소중한 사람이 될 이를 두고 나쁘게 말하기를 부인께서 바라시지는 않겠지요."], ["But she is one with whom I may mate myself without an inward conviction that I shall destroy all her happiness by doing so.", "다만 그 사람은, 제가 그렇게 함으로써 그 사람의 행복을 모조리 망가뜨리고 말리라는 속마음의 확신 없이 짝을 맺을 수 있는 사람입니다. 릴리언 양은,”"], ["Lilian,\"", "그는 이렇게 썼다."], ["he said, \"shall always have my prayers; and I trust that she may soon forget, in the love of an honest man, that she ever knew one so dishonest as--Adolphus Crosbie.\"", "“언제나 제 기도 속에 있을 것입니다. 그리고 그분이 정직한 사람의 사랑 속에서, 자신이 이토록 정직하지 못한 자를 알았다는 사실을 부디 곧 잊게 되기를 바랍니다—어돌퍼스 크로즈비.”"]], [["Of what like must have been his countenance as he sat writing such words of himself under the ghastly light of his own small, solitary lamp?", "제 손으로 자신에 대해 그런 말들을 써 내려가며 앉아 있을 때, 홀로 놓인 작은 등의 스산한 불빛 아래 그의 낯빛은 대체 어떠했을까?"], ["Had he written his letter at his office, in the day-time, with men coming in and out of his room, he could hardly have written of himself so plainly.", "만일 그가 그 편지를 낮에 사무실에서, 사람들이 제 방을 드나드는 가운데 썼더라면, 자신에 대해 그토록 있는 그대로 쓰지는 못했으리라."], ["He would have bethought himself that the written words might remain, and be read hereafter by other eyes than those for which they were intended.", "써 놓은 글자는 남는 법이고, 본래 겨냥한 것이 아닌 다른 눈들이 나중에 그것을 읽을지도 모른다는 데 생각이 미쳤을 것이다."], ["But, as he sat alone, during the small hours of the night, almost repenting of his sin with true repentance, he declared to himself that he did not care who might read them.", "그러나 밤이 깊은 시각에 홀로 앉아, 참된 뉘우침으로 제 죄를 거의 뉘우치다시피 하면서, 그는 누가 그것을 읽든 상관없다고 스스로에게 선언했다."], ["They should, at any rate, be true.", "적어도 그것들은 진실이어야 했다."], ["Now they had been read by her to whom they had been addressed, and the daughter was standing before the mother to hear her doom.", "이제 그 글은 그것이 보내진 바로 그 사람에게 읽혔고, 딸은 어머니 앞에 서서 제게 내려질 선고를 들으려 하고 있었다."]], [["\"Tell me all at once,\"", "“한꺼번에 다 말해 주세요.”"], ["Lily had said; but in what words was her mother to tell her?", "릴리는 그렇게 말했다. 그러나 어머니가 무슨 말로 그 아이에게 말해 준단 말인가?"]], [["\"Lily,\"", "“릴리.”"], ["she said, rising from her seat, and leaving the two letters on the couch; that addressed to the daughter was hidden beneath a handkerchief, but that which she had read she left open and in sight.", "어머니는 자리에서 일어나며 그렇게 말했고, 두 통의 편지는 긴 의자 위에 남겨 두었다. 딸에게 온 것은 손수건 밑에 감추어져 있었으나, 자기가 읽은 것은 펼친 채로 보이게 두었다."], ["She took both the girl's hands in hers as she looked into her face, and spoke to her.", "부인은 딸의 두 손을 제 두 손에 잡고 그 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["\"Lily, my child!\"", "“릴리, 내 딸아!”"], ["Then she burst into sobs, and was unable to tell her tale.", "그러고는 흐느낌이 터져 나와 이야기를 꺼내지 못했다."]], [["\"Is it from him, mamma?", "“그이에게서 온 건가요, 엄마?"], ["May I read it?", "제가 읽어도 돼요?"], ["He cannot be--\"", "그이가 설마—”"]], [["\"It is from Mr. Crosbie.\"", "“크로즈비 씨한테서 온 거란다.”"]], [["\"Is he ill, mamma?", "“그이가 아픈가요, 엄마?"], ["Tell me at once.", "얼른 말해 주세요."], ["If he is ill I will go to him.\"", "아프다면 제가 그이에게 가겠어요.”"]], [["\"No, my darling, he is not ill.", "“아니다, 얘야, 그이는 아프지 않다."], ["Not yet;--do not read it yet.", "아직은 아니야—아직 읽지 마라."], ["Oh, Lily!", "아, 릴리!"], ["It brings bad news; very bad news.\"", "나쁜 소식을 담고 있구나. 아주 나쁜 소식이야.”"]], [["\"Mamma, if he is not in danger, I can read it.", "“엄마, 그이가 위험한 게 아니라면 저는 읽을 수 있어요."], ["Is it bad to him, or only bad to me?\"", "그이에게 나쁜 일인가요, 저에게만 나쁜 일인가요?”"]], [["At this moment the servant knocked, and not waiting for an answer half opened the door.", "바로 그때 하인이 문을 두드리더니, 대답도 기다리지 않고 문을 반쯤 열었다."]], [["\"If you please, ma'am, Mr.", "“실례합니다요, 마님."], ["Bernard is below, and wants to speak to you.\"", "버나드 도련님이 아래에 오셔서 마님과 말씀을 나누고 싶다고 하십니다요.”"]], [["\"Mr. Bernard!", "“버나드가!"], ["ask Miss Bell to see him.\"", "벨 아가씨더러 만나 보라고 해라.”"]], [["\"Miss Bell is with him, ma'am, but he says that he specially wants to speak to you.\"", "“벨 아가씨도 함께 계십니다요, 마님. 그런데 도련님께서 꼭 마님과 말씀을 나누고 싶다고 하십니다요.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale felt that she could not leave Lily alone.", "데일 부인은 릴리를 혼자 둘 수 없다고 느꼈다."], ["She could not take the letter away, nor could she leave her child with the letter open.", "편지를 가지고 나갈 수도 없었고, 편지를 펼쳐 둔 채로 딸을 두고 갈 수도 없었다."]], [["\"I cannot see him,\"", "“나는 만날 수 없다.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Ask him what it is.", "“무슨 일인지 물어보아라."], ["Tell him I cannot come down just at present.\"", "지금 당장은 내려갈 수 없다고 전하고.”"], ["And then the servant went, and Bernard left his message with Bell.", "그러고서 하인이 갔고, 버나드는 벨에게 전할 말을 남겼다."]], [["\"Bernard,\"", "“버나드,”"], ["she had said, \"do you know of anything?", "벨은 그에게 말했었다. “너 무슨 일인지 알고 있니?"], ["Is there anything wrong about Mr. Crosbie?\"", "크로즈비 씨한테 무슨 문제가 생긴 거야?”"], ["Then, in a few words, he told her all, and understanding why his aunt had not come down to him, he went back to the Great House.", "그러자 그는 몇 마디로 벨에게 모든 것을 말했고, 숙모가 왜 제게 내려오지 않는지 알아차리고는 큰 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["Bell, almost stupefied by the tidings, seated herself at the table unconsciously, leaning upon her elbows.", "그 소식에 거의 넋이 나간 벨은 저도 모르게 식탁 앞에 앉아 두 팔꿈치로 몸을 괴었다."]], [["\"It will kill her,\"", "“이 일이 그 애를 죽이고 말 거야.”"], ["she said to herself.", "벨은 혼잣말을 했다."], ["\"My Lily, my darling Lily!", "“내 릴리, 사랑하는 내 릴리!"], ["It will surely kill her!\"", "정말로 그 애를 죽이고 말 거야!”"]], [["But the mother was still with the daughter, and the story was still untold.", "그러나 어머니는 아직 딸 곁에 있었고, 이야기는 아직 하지 못한 채였다."]], [["\"Mamma,\"", "“엄마.”"], ["said Lily, \"whatever it is, I must, of course, be made to know it.", "릴리가 말했다. “그게 무엇이든 저는 당연히 알아야 해요."], ["I begin to guess the truth.", "저도 이제 짐작이 가기 시작해요."], ["It will pain you to say it.", "말하기가 엄마도 괴로우시겠죠."], ["Shall I read the letter?\"", "제가 편지를 읽을까요?”"]], [["Mrs. Dale was astonished at her calmness.", "데일 부인은 딸의 침착함에 놀랐다."], ["It could not be that she had guessed the truth, or she would not stand like that, with tearless eyes and unquelled courage before her.", "저 아이가 진실을 짐작했을 리 없다. 짐작했다면 저렇게 눈물 한 방울 없이, 꺾이지 않은 용기로 제 앞에 서 있지는 못하리라."]], [["\"You shall read it, but I ought to tell you first.", "“읽어도 좋다. 하지만 내가 먼저 말해 주어야겠구나."], ["Oh, my child, my own one!\"", "아, 내 딸, 내 하나뿐인 아이!”"], ["Lily was now leaning against the bed, and her mother was standing over her, caressing her.", "릴리는 이제 침대에 기대어 있었고, 어머니는 그 위로 몸을 굽혀 딸을 어루만지고 있었다."]], [["\"Then tell me,\"", "“그럼 말해 주세요.”"], ["said she.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"But I know what it is.", "“하지만 저는 그게 무엇인지 알아요."], ["He has thought it all over while away from me, and he finds that it must not be as we have supposed.", "그이는 저를 떠나 있는 동안 이 일을 곰곰이 생각해 보았고, 우리가 여겨 온 대로 되어서는 안 된다는 것을 깨달은 거예요."], ["Before he went I offered to release him, and now he knows that he had better accept my offer.", "떠나기 전에 제가 그이를 놓아주겠다고 했는데, 이제 그이는 제 말을 받아들이는 편이 낫다는 것을 안 거죠."], ["Is it so, mamma?\"", "그런 거죠, 엄마?”"], ["In answer to this Mrs. Dale did not speak, but Lily understood from her signs that it was so.", "이 말에 데일 부인은 대답하지 않았으나, 릴리는 어머니의 기색으로 그렇다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["\"He might have written it to me, myself,\"", "“저에게 직접 쓸 수도 있었을 텐데.”"], ["said Lily, very proudly.", "릴리가 아주 도도하게 말했다."], ["\"Mamma, we will go down to breakfast.", "“엄마, 우리 아침 먹으러 내려가요."], ["He has sent nothing to me, then?\"", "그럼 그이는 저에게는 아무것도 보내지 않았나요?”"]], [["\"There is a note.", "“쪽지가 한 장 있다."], ["He bids me read it, but I have not opened it.", "나더러 읽어 보라고 했다만 나는 뜯지 않았다."], ["It is here.\"", "여기 있구나.”"]], [["\"Give it me,\"", "“주세요.”"], ["said Lily, almost sternly.", "릴리가 거의 매섭게 말했다."], ["\"Let me have his last words to me;\"", "“그이가 저에게 남긴 마지막 말을 제가 갖게 해 주세요.”"], ["and she took the note from her mother's hands.", "그러고서 릴리는 어머니의 손에서 쪽지를 받아 들었다."]], [["\"Lily,\"", "“릴리.”"], ["said the note, \"your mother will have told you all.", "쪽지에는 그렇게 적혀 있었다. “어머님께서 모든 것을 말씀해 주셨겠지요."], ["Before you read these few words you will know that you have trusted one who was quite untrustworthy.", "이 몇 줄을 읽기 전에 당신은, 자신이 도무지 믿을 수 없는 자를 믿었다는 것을 알게 될 것입니다."], ["I know that you will hate me.--I cannot even ask you to forgive me.", "당신이 나를 미워하리라는 것을 압니다.—용서해 달라는 말조차 나는 할 수 없습니다."], ["You will let me pray that you may yet be happy.--A.", "그래도 당신이 언젠가는 행복해지기를 기도하게 해 주십시오.—A."], ["C.\"", "C.”"]], [["She read these few words, still leaning against the bed.", "릴리는 여전히 침대에 기댄 채 그 몇 줄을 읽었다."], ["Then she got up, and walking to a chair, seated herself with her back to her mother.", "그러고는 몸을 일으켜 의자 쪽으로 걸어가, 어머니에게 등을 돌리고 앉았다."], ["Mrs. Dale moving silently after her stood over the back of the chair, not daring to speak to her.", "데일 부인은 소리 없이 딸을 뒤따라가 의자 등받이 뒤에 섰으나, 감히 말을 걸지 못했다."], ["So she sat for some five minutes, with her eyes fixed upon the open window, and with Crosbie's note in her hand.", "릴리는 열린 창에 눈을 붙박은 채, 크로즈비의 쪽지를 손에 쥐고 오 분쯤을 그렇게 앉아 있었다."]], [["\"I will not hate him, and I do forgive him,\"", "“저는 그이를 미워하지 않을 거예요."], ["she said at last, struggling to command her voice, and hardly showing that she could not altogether succeed in her attempt.", "그리고 그이를 용서해요.” 마침내 릴리는 목소리를 다잡으려 애쓰며 그렇게 말했고, 그 애씀이 온전히 성공하지는 못했다는 것을 좀처럼 드러내지 않았다."], ["\"I may not write to him again, but you shall write and tell him so.", "“제가 그이에게 다시 편지를 쓸 수는 없겠지요. 하지만 엄마가 편지를 써서 그렇게 전해 주세요."], ["Now we will go down to breakfast.\"", "이제 아침 먹으러 내려가요.”"], ["And so saying, she got up from her chair.", "그렇게 말하고 릴리는 의자에서 일어났다."]], [["Mrs. Dale almost feared to speak to her, her composure was so complete, and her manner so stern and fixed.", "데일 부인은 딸에게 말을 붙이기가 거의 두려웠다."], ["She hardly knew how to offer pity and sympathy, seeing that pity seemed to be so little necessary, and that even sympathy was not demanded.", "딸의 침착함이 너무나 완전했고, 태도가 너무나 엄하고 흔들림이 없었다. 가엾이 여기는 마음도 딱히 필요해 보이지 않고 동정조차 구하지 않는 아이에게, 어떻게 가엾이 여기는 마음과 동정을 건네야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["And she could not understand all that Lily had said.", "게다가 릴리가 한 말을 다 이해할 수도 없었다."], ["What had she meant by the offer to release him?", "그이를 놓아주겠다고 했다는 것은 무슨 뜻인가?"], ["Had there, then, been some quarrel between them before he went?", "그렇다면 그가 떠나기 전에 둘 사이에 무슨 다툼이 있었단 말인가?"], ["Crosbie had made no such allusion in his letter.", "크로즈비는 편지에서 그런 이야기는 조금도 비치지 않았다."], ["But Mrs. Dale did not dare to ask any questions.", "그러나 데일 부인은 감히 아무것도 묻지 못했다."]], [["\"You frighten me, Lily,\"", "“네가 나를 겁나게 하는구나, 릴리.”"], ["she said.", "부인이 말했다."], ["\"Your very calmness frightens me.\"", "“네 그 침착함이 나를 겁나게 해.”"]], [["\"Dear mamma!\"", "“가엾은 엄마!”"], ["and the poor girl absolutely smiled as she embraced her mother.", "그러면서 그 가엾은 딸은 어머니를 껴안으며 실제로 미소까지 지었다."], ["\"You need not be frightened by my calmness.", "“제 침착함에 겁먹으실 것 없어요."], ["I know the truth well.", "저는 진실을 잘 알고 있어요."], ["I have been very unfortunate;--very.", "저는 아주 불행해졌어요—아주요."], ["The brightest hopes of my life are all gone;--and I shall never again see him whom I love beyond all the world!\"", "제 삶의 가장 밝은 희망들이 다 사라졌고요—그리고 세상 무엇보다 사랑하는 그이를 다시는 볼 수 없겠지요!”"], ["Then at last she broke down, and wept in her mother's arms.", "그러고는 마침내 무너져, 어머니의 품에 안겨 울었다."]], [["There was not a word of anger spoken then against him who had done all this. Mrs.", "그때에도 이 모든 일을 저지른 그 사람을 두고 노여운 말은 한마디도 나오지 않았다."], ["Dale felt that she did not dare to speak in anger against him, and words of anger were not likely to come from poor Lily.", "데일 부인은 감히 그를 노여워하는 말을 입에 담을 수 없다고 느꼈고, 가엾은 릴리에게서 노여운 말이 나올 리도 없었다."], ["She, indeed, hitherto did not know the whole of his offence, for she had not read his letter.", "게다가 릴리는 아직 그의 잘못을 다 알지 못했다. 그의 편지를 읽지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["\"Give it me, mamma,\"", "“주세요, 엄마.”"], ["she said at last.", "마침내 릴리가 말했다."], ["\"It has to be done sooner or later.\"", "“어차피 언젠가는 해야 할 일이잖아요.”"]], [["\"Not now, Lily.", "“지금은 안 된다, 릴리."], ["I have told you all,--all that you need know at present.\"", "내가 다 말해 주었잖니—지금 네가 알아야 할 것은 다 말해 주었다.”"]], [["\"Yes; now, mamma,\"", "“아니에요, 지금이요, 엄마.”"], ["and again that sweet silvery voice became stern.", "그러면서 그 달콤한 은방울 같은 목소리가 다시 엄해졌다."], ["\"I will read it now, and there shall be an end.\"", "“지금 읽고, 그것으로 끝내겠어요.”"], ["Whereupon Mrs. Dale gave her the letter and she read it in silence.", "그리하여 데일 부인이 편지를 건네주었고, 릴리는 말없이 그것을 읽었다."], ["Her mother, though standing somewhat behind her, watched her narrowly as she did so.", "어머니는 조금 뒤에 서 있었으나 딸이 읽는 동안 그 모습을 낱낱이 지켜보았다."], ["She was now lying over upon the bed, and the letter was on the pillow, as she propped herself upon her arm.", "릴리는 이제 침대 위로 몸을 뉘어, 한쪽 팔로 몸을 괴고 베개 위에 편지를 놓았다."], ["Her tears were running, and ever and again she would stop to dry her eyes.", "눈물이 흘러내려 이따금 읽기를 멈추고 눈을 닦았다."], ["Her sobs, too, were very audible, but she went on steadily with her reading till she came to the line on which Crosbie told that he had already engaged himself to another woman.", "흐느끼는 소리도 또렷이 들렸다. 그러나 릴리는 흔들림 없이 계속 읽어 내려가, 이미 다른 여자와 혼인을 약속했다고 크로즈비가 적어 놓은 줄에 이르렀다."], ["Then her mother could see that she paused suddenly, and that a shudder slightly convulsed all her limbs.", "그때 어머니는 딸이 문득 멈추는 것을, 그리고 온몸에 가벼운 전율이 지나가는 것을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["\"He has been very quick,\"", "“참 빠르기도 하셔라.”"], ["she said, almost in a whisper; and then she finished the letter.", "릴리는 거의 속삭이듯 말했다. 그러고는 편지를 마저 읽었다."], ["\"Tell him, mamma,\"", "“그이에게 전해 주세요, 엄마.”"], ["she said, \"that I do forgive him, and I will not hate him.", "릴리가 말했다. “제가 그이를 용서하고, 그이를 미워하지 않겠다고요."], ["You will tell him that,--from me; will you not?\"", "그렇게 전해 주실 거죠—제 말이라고요. 그러실 거죠?”"], ["And then she raised herself from the bed.", "그러고서 릴리는 침대에서 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["Mrs. Dale would give her no such assurance.", "데일 부인은 딸에게 그런 다짐을 해 주지 않았다."], ["In her present mood her feelings against Crosbie were of a nature which she herself hardly could understand or analyze.", "지금의 심경에서 크로즈비를 향한 감정은 그녀 자신도 도무지 알 수도 헤아릴 수도 없는 것이었다."], ["She felt that if he were present she could almost fly at him as would a tigress.", "만일 그가 눈앞에 있다면 암범처럼 달려들 것만 같았다."], ["She had never hated before as she now hated this man.", "이제껏 살면서 이 남자를 미워하는 것만큼 누군가를 미워해 본 적이 없었다."], ["He was to her a murderer, and worse than a murderer.", "그는 그녀에게 살인자였고, 살인자보다 더 나쁜 자였다."], ["He had made his way like a wolf into her little fold, and torn her ewe-lamb and left her maimed and mutilated for life.", "그는 이리처럼 그녀의 작은 우리 안으로 파고들어 그녀의 새끼 양을 물어뜯고, 평생 상하고 온전치 못한 몸으로 남겨 놓았다."], ["How could a mother forgive such an offence as that, or consent to be the medium through which forgiveness should be expressed?", "어머니가 어떻게 그런 잘못을 용서할 수 있으며, 용서를 전하는 통로가 되기를 승낙할 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["\"You must, mamma; or, if you do not, I shall do so.", "“꼭 해 주셔야 해요, 엄마. 그러지 않으시면 제가 하겠어요."], ["Remember that I love him.", "제가 그이를 사랑한다는 걸 기억해 주세요."], ["You know what it is to have loved one single man.", "오직 한 사람만을 사랑한다는 게 어떤 것인지 엄마도 아시잖아요."], ["He has made me very unhappy; I hardly know yet how unhappy.", "그이는 저를 아주 불행하게 만들었어요. 얼마나 불행한지 저도 아직 잘 모르겠어요."], ["But I have loved him, and do love him.", "하지만 저는 그이를 사랑했고, 지금도 사랑해요."], ["I believe, in my heart, that he still loves me.", "마음속으로는 그이도 저를 여전히 사랑한다고 믿어요."], ["Where this has been there must not be hatred and unforgiveness.\"", "그런 것이 있었던 자리에 미움과 용서 없음이 있어서는 안 돼요.”"]], [["\"I will pray that I may become able to forgive him,\"", "“그이를 용서할 수 있게 되기를 기도하마.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"But you must write to him those words.", "“하지만 그 말을 그대로 써 주셔야 해요."], ["Indeed you must, mamma!", "정말로 꼭 그러셔야 해요, 엄마!"], ["'She bids me tell you that she has forgiven you, and will not hate you.' Promise me that!\"", "‘그 애가 당신을 용서했으며 당신을 미워하지 않겠다고 전해 달라 합니다.’ 그렇게 약속해 주세요!”"]], [["\"I can make no promise now, Lily.", "“지금은 약속할 수 없구나, 릴리."], ["I will think about it, and endeavour to do my duty.\"", "생각해 보고, 내 도리를 다하도록 애써 보마.”"]], [["Lily was now seated, and was holding the skirt of her mother's dress.", "릴리는 이제 앉아서 어머니의 치맛자락을 붙잡고 있었다."]], [["\"Mamma,\"", "“엄마.”"], ["she said, looking up into her mother's face, \"you must be very good to me now; and I must be very good to you.", "릴리는 어머니의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다. “이제 엄마는 저에게 아주 잘해 주셔야 해요."], ["We shall be always together now.", "그리고 저도 엄마에게 아주 잘해 드려야죠."], ["I must be your friend and counsellor; and be everything to you, more than ever.", "이제 우리는 늘 함께일 거예요. 제가 엄마의 벗이 되고 의논 상대가 되고, 예전보다 더 엄마의 모든 것이 되어 드릴게요."], ["I must fall in love with you now;\"", "이제 저는 엄마와 사랑에 빠져야겠어요.”"], ["and she smiled again, and the tears were almost dry upon her cheeks.", "그러고서 릴리는 다시 미소 지었고, 뺨의 눈물은 거의 말라 있었다."]], [["At last they went down to the breakfast-room, from which Bell had not moved.", "마침내 두 사람은 아침 식사 방으로 내려갔다. 벨은 그 자리에서 움직이지 않고 있었다."], ["Mrs. Dale entered the room first, and Lily followed, hiding herself for a moment behind her mother.", "데일 부인이 먼저 방에 들어섰고, 릴리는 잠시 어머니 뒤에 몸을 숨기며 뒤따랐다."], ["Then she came forward boldly, and taking Bell in her arms, clasped her close to her bosom.", "그러고는 씩씩하게 앞으로 나서서 벨을 두 팔로 안아 제 가슴에 꼭 끌어당겼다."]], [["\"Bell,\"", "“언니,”"], ["she said, \"he has gone.\"", "릴리가 말했다. “그이는 가 버렸어.”"]], [["\"Lily!", "“릴리!"], ["Lily!", "릴리!"], ["Lily!\"", "릴리!”"], ["said Bell, weeping.", "벨이 울며 말했다."]], [["\"He has gone!", "“그이는 가 버렸어!"], ["We shall talk it over in a few days, and shall know how to do so without losing ourselves in misery.", "며칠 지나면 그 얘기를 하게 되겠지. 그때는 비참함에 빠져 헤매지 않고 이야기하는 법도 알게 될 거야."], ["To-day we will say no more about it.", "오늘은 더 말하지 말자."], ["I am so thirsty, Bell; do give me my tea;\"", "목이 너무 말라, 언니. 차 좀 줘.”"], ["and she sat herself down at the breakfast-table.", "그러고는 아침 식탁에 앉았다."]], [["Lily's tea was given to her, and she drank it.", "릴리에게 차가 건네졌고, 릴리는 그것을 마셨다."], ["Beyond that I cannot say that any of them partook with much heartiness of the meal.", "그 밖에는 누구도 그 식사를 마음 편히 들었다고 나는 말할 수 없다."], ["They sat there, as they would have sat if no terrible thunderbolt had fallen among them, and no word further was spoken about Crosbie and his conduct.", "세 사람은 무서운 벼락이 그들 사이에 떨어지지 않았더라도 그러했을 법한 모습으로 앉아 있었고, 크로즈비와 그의 행실에 대해서는 더 이상 한마디도 나오지 않았다."], ["Immediately after breakfast they went into the other room, and Lily, as was her wont, sat herself immediately down to her drawing.", "아침을 먹고 나서 곧 그들은 옆방으로 건너갔고, 릴리는 늘 하던 대로 곧바로 그림 앞에 앉았다."], ["Her mother looked at her with wistful eyes, longing to bid her spare herself, but she shrank from interfering with her.", "어머니는 그런 딸을 애타는 눈으로 바라보며 그만두라고 말하고 싶었으나, 딸을 방해하기가 겁이 나 그러지 못했다."], ["For a quarter of an hour Lily sat over her board, with her brush or pencil in her hand, and then she rose up and put it away.", "십오 분쯤 릴리는 화판 앞에 앉아 붓이나 연필을 손에 들고 있다가, 이내 일어나 그것을 치웠다."]], [["\"It is no good pretending,\"", "“아닌 척해 봐야 소용없네요.”"], ["she said.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"I am only spoiling the things; but I will be better to-morrow.", "“저는 그림만 망치고 있어요. 하지만 내일은 나아질 거예요."], ["I'll go away and lie down by myself, mamma.\"", "저는 가서 혼자 누워 있을게요, 엄마.”"], ["And so she went.", "그러고는 갔다."]], [["Soon after this Mrs. Dale took her bonnet and went up to the Great House, having received her brother-in-law's message from Bell.", "그러고 얼마 지나지 않아 데일 부인은 벨에게서 시아주버니의 전갈을 전해 듣고, 모자를 쓰고 큰 집으로 올라갔다."]], [["\"I know what he has to tell me,\"", "“그이가 나에게 무슨 말을 하려는지는 나도 안다.”"], ["she said; \"but I might as well go.", "부인은 말했다. “그래도 가 보는 편이 낫겠지."], ["It will be necessary that we should speak to each other about it.\"", "이 일을 두고 서로 이야기를 나누어야 할 테니까.”"], ["So she walked across the lawn, and up into the hall of the Great House.", "그리하여 부인은 잔디밭을 가로질러 큰 집 현관으로 들어섰다."], ["\"Is my brother in the book-room?\"", "“시아주버님은 서재에 계시냐?”"], ["she said to one of the maids; and then knocking at the door, went in unannounced.", "부인은 하녀 하나에게 그렇게 묻고는, 문을 두드린 뒤 기별 없이 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["The squire rose from his arm-chair, and came forward to meet her.", "지주가 안락의자에서 일어나 부인을 맞으러 나왔다."]], [["\"Mary,\"", "“제수씨,”"], ["he said, \"I believe you know it all.\"", "그가 말했다. “다 알고 계시겠지요.”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“네.”"], ["she said.", "부인이 말했다."], ["\"You can read that,\"", "“이걸 읽어 보세요.”"], ["and she handed him Crosbie's letter.", "그러고는 크로즈비의 편지를 그에게 건넸다."], ["\"How was one to know that any man could be so wicked as that?\"", "“사람이 어찌 저리 악할 수 있는지 누가 알았겠어요?”"]], [["\"And she has heard it?\"", "“그 아이도 들었소?”"], ["asked the squire.", "지주가 물었다."], ["\"Is she able to bear it?\"", "“견뎌 낼 수 있겠소?”"]], [["\"Wonderfully!", "“놀랍도록요!"], ["She has amazed me by her strength.", "그 아이의 힘에 제가 다 놀랐어요."], ["It frightens me; for I know that a relapse must come.", "오히려 그게 무서워요. 반드시 되짚어 무너지는 때가 오리라는 걸 아니까요."], ["She has never sunk for a moment beneath it.", "그 아이는 한순간도 그 밑에 주저앉지 않았어요."], ["For myself, I feel as though it were her strength that enables me to bear my share of it.\"", "저로 말하면, 제 몫의 짐을 견디게 해 주는 것도 그 아이의 힘인 것만 같아요.”"], ["And then she described to the squire all that had taken place that morning.", "그러고서 부인은 그날 아침에 있었던 일을 지주에게 죄다 이야기했다."]], [["\"Poor child!\"", "“가엾은 것!”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"Poor child!", "“가엾은 것!"], ["What can we do for her?", "그 아이를 위해 우리가 무얼 해 줄 수 있겠소?"], ["Would it be good for her to go away for a time?", "한동안 어디 다녀오는 것이 그 아이에게 좋지 않겠소?"], ["She is a sweet, good, lovely girl, and has deserved better than that.", "곱고 착하고 사랑스러운 아이인데, 그런 일을 당할 아이가 아니오."], ["Sorrow and disappointment come to us all; but they are doubly heavy when they come so early.\"", "슬픔과 낙심이야 누구에게나 오는 법이지만, 이렇게 이른 나이에 오면 갑절로 무거운 법이지.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale was almost surprised at the amount of sympathy which he showed.", "데일 부인은 그가 보이는 동정에 거의 놀랄 지경이었다."]], [["\"And what is to be his punishment?\"", "“그럼 그 사람이 받을 벌은 무엇인가요?”"], ["she asked.", "부인이 물었다."]], [["\"The scorn which men and women will feel for him; those, at least, whose esteem or scorn are matters of concern to any one.", "“남자든 여자든 그를 두고 느낄 경멸이지요. 적어도 그 존중이나 경멸이 남에게 의미가 되는 사람들의 경멸 말이오."], ["I know no other punishment.", "나는 다른 벌은 알지 못하오."], ["You would not have Lily's name brought before a tribunal of law?\"", "릴리의 이름이 법정에 오르는 것을 바라지는 않으시겠지요?”"]], [["\"Certainly not that.\"", "“물론 그건 아니에요.”"]], [["\"And I will not have Bernard calling him out.", "“그리고 버나드가 그에게 결투를 신청하게 두지도 않겠소."], ["Indeed, it would be for nothing; for in these days a man is not expected to fight duels.\"", "사실 그래 봐야 아무 소용도 없소. 요즘 세상에는 남자가 결투를 하리라고 기대하는 사람이 없으니까.”"]], [["\"You cannot think that I would wish that.\"", "“제가 그런 걸 바라리라고 생각하지는 않으시겠지요.”"]], [["\"What punishment is there, then?", "“그럼 무슨 벌이 있겠소?"], ["I know of none.", "나는 알지 못하오."], ["There are evils which a man may do, and no one can punish him.", "사람이 저지를 수 있는 악행 가운데는 아무도 벌할 수 없는 것들이 있소."], ["I know of nothing.", "나는 알지 못하오."], ["I went up to London after him, but he contrived to crawl out of my way.", "나는 그를 뒤쫓아 런던까지 올라갔지만, 그는 용케 내 앞을 기어서 빠져나갔소."], ["What can you do to a rat but keep clear of him?\"", "쥐를 두고 무엇을 할 수 있겠소? 가까이 가지 않는 것 말고?”"]], [["Mrs. Dale had felt in her heart that it would be well if Crosbie could be beaten till all his bones were sore.", "데일 부인은 마음속으로, 크로즈비가 온몸의 뼈마디가 다 쑤시도록 매를 맞을 수만 있다면 좋겠다고 느끼고 있었다."], ["I hardly know whether such should have been a woman's thought, but it was hers.", "그런 것이 여자가 품을 만한 생각이었는지 나는 잘 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그것이 부인의 생각이었다."], ["She had no wish that he should be made to fight a duel.", "부인은 그가 결투를 하게 되기를 바라지는 않았다."], ["In that there would have been much that was wicked, and in her estimation nothing that was just.", "거기에는 사악한 것이 많고, 부인이 보기에 옳은 것은 하나도 없었다."], ["But she felt that if Bernard would thrash the coward for his cowardice she would love her nephew better than ever she had loved him.", "그러나 버나드가 그 겁쟁이를 겁쟁이답게 두들겨 준다면 조카를 지금까지보다 더 사랑하게 되리라고 느꼈다."], ["Bernard also had considered it probable that he might be expected to horsewhip the man who had jilted his cousin, and, as regarded the absolute bodily risk, he would not have felt any insuperable objection to undertake the task.", "버나드 역시 제 사촌 누이를 차 버린 사내를 자기가 채찍질해 주기를 세상이 기대할지도 모른다고 생각해 보았고, 몸으로 지는 위험만 놓고 보자면 그 일을 떠맡는 데 못 이길 것도 없었다."], ["But such a piece of work was disagreeable to him in many ways.", "그러나 그런 일은 여러모로 그에게 달갑지 않았다."], ["He hated the idea of a row at his club.", "클럽에서 소동이 벌어지는 것이 싫었다."], ["He was most desirous that his cousin's name should not be made public.", "사촌 누이의 이름이 사람들 입에 오르내리지 않기를 무엇보다 바랐다."], ["He wished to avoid anything that might be impolitic.", "지각없는 짓이 될 만한 일은 무엇이든 피하고 싶었다."], ["A wicked thing had been done, and he was quite ready to hate Crosbie as Crosbie ought to be hated; but as regarded himself, it made him unhappy to think that the world might probably expect him to punish the man who had so lately been his friend.", "사악한 일이 벌어졌고, 그는 크로즈비가 미움받아 마땅한 만큼 크로즈비를 미워할 준비가 얼마든지 되어 있었다. 그러나 제 몫으로 말하자면, 얼마 전까지 제 벗이었던 사내를 자기가 벌해 주기를 세상이 아마도 기대하리라는 생각이 그를 우울하게 만들었다."], ["And then he did not know where to catch him, or how to thrash him when caught.", "게다가 그는 그를 어디서 붙잡아야 할지도, 붙잡은들 어떻게 두들겨야 할지도 알지 못했다."], ["He was very sorry for his cousin, and felt strongly that Crosbie should not be allowed to escape.", "그는 사촌 누이가 몹시 안쓰러웠고, 크로즈비를 그냥 빠져나가게 두어서는 안 된다고 굳게 느꼈다."], ["But what was he to do?", "그러나 그가 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["\"Would she like to go anywhere?\"", "“그 아이가 어디 다녀오고 싶어 하지 않겠소?”"], ["said the squire again, anxious, if he could, to afford solace by some act of generosity.", "지주가 다시 물었다. 무엇이든 너그러이 베풀어 위안을 줄 수 있다면 그러고 싶었던 것이다."], ["At this moment he would have settled a hundred a year for life upon his niece if by so doing he could have done her any good.", "이 순간 그는, 그렇게 해서 조카딸에게 조금이라도 도움이 된다면 평생 연 백 파운드를 떼어 주었을 것이다."]], [["\"She will be better at home,\"", "“집에 있는 편이 나을 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Poor thing.", "“가엾은 것."], ["For a while she will wish to avoid going out.\"", "한동안은 밖에 나가는 걸 피하고 싶어 할 테니까요.”"]], [["\"I suppose so;\"", "“그렇겠구려.”"], ["and then there was a pause.", "그러고는 잠시 말이 끊겼다."], ["\"I'll tell you what, Mary; I don't understand it.", "“내 말해 두겠소, 제수씨. 나는 도무지 이해할 수가 없소."], ["On my honour I don't understand it.", "정말이지 도무지 이해할 수가 없소."], ["It is to me as wonderful as though I had caught the man picking my pence out of my pocket.", "나로서는 그 사람이 내 주머니에서 동전을 집어내는 것을 붙잡은 것만큼이나 기가 막힌 일이오."], ["I don't think any man in the position of a gentleman would have done such a thing when I was young.", "내가 젊었을 적에는 신사라는 자리에 있는 사람이 그런 짓을 하리라고는 생각지도 못했소."], ["I don't think any man would have dared to do it.", "감히 그런 짓을 할 엄두를 낼 사람이 있으리라고도 생각지 못했소."], ["But now it seems that a man may act in that way and no harm come to him.", "그런데 지금은 사람이 그런 식으로 굴어도 아무 탈이 없는 모양이오."], ["He had a friend in London who came to me and talked about it as though it were some ordinary, everyday transaction of life.", "런던에 그의 친구라는 자가 있어 나를 찾아와서는, 세상에 흔히 있는 일상사인 양 그 일을 이야기합디다."], ["Yes; you may come in, Bernard.", "그래, 들어오너라, 버나드."], ["The poor child knows it all now.\"", "그 가엾은 아이도 이제 다 알고 있다.”"]], [["Bernard offered to his aunt what of solace and sympathy he had to offer, and made some sort of half-expressed apology for having introduced this wolf into their flock.", "버나드는 숙모에게 제가 건넬 수 있는 위로와 동정을 건넸고, 이 이리를 그들의 우리 안에 끌어들인 데 대해 반쯤 말이 되다 만 사과를 했다."], ["\"We always thought very much of him at his club,\"", "“클럽에서는 다들 그를 아주 좋게 여겼습니다.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't know much about your London clubs now-a-days,\"", "“요즘 너희 런던 클럽이란 데는 나도 잘 모른다.”"], ["said his uncle, \"nor do I wish to do so if the society of that man can be endured after what he has now done.\"", "숙부가 말했다. “저 사람이 이제 그런 짓을 하고서도 그와 어울리는 것이 참아 넘겨진다면, 알고 싶지도 않고.”"]], [["\"I don't suppose half-a-dozen men will ever know anything about it,\"", "“이 일을 알게 될 사람은 여섯 명도 안 될 겁니다.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"Umph!\"", "“흠!”"], ["ejaculated the squire.", "지주가 툴툴거렸다."], ["He could not say that he wished Crosbie's villany to be widely discussed, seeing that Lily's name was so closely connected with it.", "릴리의 이름이 그 일과 그토록 가까이 얽혀 있으니, 크로즈비의 악행이 널리 이야기되기를 바란다고 말할 수는 없었다."], ["But yet he could not support the idea that Crosbie should not be punished by the frown of the world at large.", "그러나 그러면서도 크로즈비가 온 세상의 찌푸린 눈살로 벌받지 않아도 된다는 생각은 참아 넘길 수가 없었다."], ["It seemed to him that from this time forward any man speaking to Crosbie should be held to have disgraced himself by so doing.", "그가 보기에는 이제부터 크로즈비에게 말을 거는 사람은 누구든 그렇게 함으로써 스스로 제 몸을 더럽히는 것이라 여겨야 마땅했다."]], [["\"Give her my best love,\" he said, as Mrs.", "“그 아이에게 내 사랑을 전해 주시오.”"], ["Dale got up to take her leave; \"my very best love.", "데일 부인이 일어나 작별하려 하자 그가 말했다. “진심으로 내 사랑을 전해 주시오."], ["If her old uncle can do anything for her she has only to let me know.", "늙은 삼촌이 그 아이를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 일이 있다면 알려만 달라고 하시오."], ["She met the man in my house, and I feel that I owe her much.", "그 아이는 내 집에서 그 사내를 만났소. 그러니 내가 그 아이에게 빚진 것이 많다고 느끼오."], ["Bid her come and see me.", "나를 보러 오라고 하시오."], ["It will be better for her than moping at home.", "집에서 시름에 잠겨 있는 것보다 나을 게요."], ["And Mary\"--this he said to her, whispering into her ear--\"think of what I said to you about Bell.\"", "그리고 제수씨”—이 말은 그녀의 귀에 대고 속삭였다—“벨에 대해 내가 한 말도 생각해 보시오.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale, as she walked back to her own house, acknowledged to herself that her brother-in-law's manner was different to her from anything that she had hitherto known of him.", "데일 부인은 제 집으로 걸어 돌아오면서, 시아주버니의 태도가 이제껏 자기가 알아 온 그 어떤 모습과도 다르다는 것을 스스로 인정했다."]], [["During the whole of that day Crosbie's name was not mentioned at the Small House.", "그날 하루 내내 작은 집에서는 크로즈비의 이름이 입에 오르지 않았다."], ["Neither of the girls stirred out, and Bell spent the greater part of the afternoon sitting, with her arm round her sister's waist, upon the sofa.", "두 아가씨는 아무도 밖에 나가지 않았고, 벨은 오후의 태반을 소파에 앉아 동생의 허리에 팔을 두른 채로 보냈다."], ["Each of them had a book; but though there was little spoken, there was as little read.", "각자 책을 한 권씩 들고 있었으나, 오간 말이 적었던 만큼이나 읽은 것도 적었다."], ["Who can describe the thoughts that were passing through Lily's mind as she remembered the hours which she had passed with Crosbie, of his warm assurances of love, of his accepted caresses, of her uncontrolled and acknowledged joy in his affection?", "크로즈비와 함께 보낸 시간들을, 그의 따뜻한 사랑의 다짐을, 그가 건네고 자기가 받아들인 애무를, 그의 애정 앞에서 거리낌 없이 스스로 인정하던 제 기쁨을 떠올릴 때 릴리의 마음속을 지나간 생각들을 누가 그려 낼 수 있을까?"], ["It had all been holy to her then; and now those things which were then sacred had been made almost disgraceful by his fault.", "그때는 그 모든 것이 릴리에게 거룩했다. 그런데 이제 그때 성스러웠던 것들이 그의 잘못으로 거의 부끄러운 것이 되고 말았다."], ["And yet as she thought of this she declared to herself over and over again that she would forgive him;--nay, that she had forgiven him.", "그러면서도 릴리는 그것을 생각하며 몇 번이고 스스로에게 다짐했다. 나는 그이를 용서하겠다고—아니, 이미 용서했다고."], ["\"And he shall know it, too,\"", "“그리고 그이도 그걸 알게 될 거야.”"], ["she said, speaking almost out loud.", "릴리는 거의 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["\"Lily, dear Lily,\"", "“릴리, 우리 릴리.”"], ["said Bell, \"turn your thoughts away from it for a while, if you can.\"", "벨이 말했다. “할 수 있거든 잠시라도 그 생각에서 마음을 돌려 보렴.”"]], [["\"They won't go away,\"", "“돌려지지가 않아.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["And that was all that was said between them on the subject.", "그날 두 사람 사이에 그 일을 두고 오간 말은 그것이 전부였다."]], [["Everybody would know it!", "온 세상이 다 알게 되겠지!"], ["I doubt whether that must not be one of the bitterest drops in the cup which a girl in such circumstances is made to drain.", "그런 처지에 놓인 아가씨가 마셔야 하는 잔 속에서 가장 쓴 방울 가운데 하나가 바로 이것이 아닐까 싶다."], ["Lily perceived early in the day that the parlour-maid well knew that she had been jilted.", "릴리는 그날 이른 시각에 이미, 응접실 하녀가 자기가 버림받았다는 것을 잘 알고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["The girl's manner was intended to convey sympathy; but it did convey pity; and Lily for a moment felt angry.", "그 아이의 태도는 동정을 전하려는 것이었으나, 실제로 전해 온 것은 가엾어하는 마음이었다. 릴리는 한순간 화가 났다."], ["But she remembered that it must be so, and smiled upon the girl, and spoke kindly to her.", "그러나 그럴 수밖에 없다는 생각이 들어, 그 아이에게 미소를 지어 보이고 다정하게 말을 건넸다."], ["What mattered it?", "그런들 무슨 상관인가?"], ["All the world would know it in a day or two.", "하루 이틀이면 온 세상이 다 알 텐데."]], [["On the following day she went up, by her mother's advice, to see her uncle.", "이튿날 릴리는 어머니의 권유로 삼촌을 보러 올라갔다."]], [["\"My child,\"", "“얘야.”"], ["said he, \"I am sorry for you.", "그가 말했다. “네 일이 참 안됐구나."], ["My heart bleeds for you.\"", "너 때문에 내 가슴이 미어진다.”"]], [["\"Uncle,\"", "“삼촌.”"], ["she said, \"do not mind it.", "릴리가 말했다. “마음 쓰지 마세요."], ["Only do this for me,--do not talk about it,--I mean to me.\"", "저를 위해 이것 하나만 해 주세요—그 얘기는 하지 말아 주세요. 제 말은, 저한테는요.”"]], [["\"No, no; I will not.", "“그래, 그래, 하지 않으마."], ["That there should ever have been in my house so great a rascal--\"", "내 집 안에 그런 몹쓸 악당이 다 있었다니—”"]], [["\"Uncle!", "“삼촌!"], ["uncle!", "삼촌!"], ["I will not have that!", "그건 안 돼요!"], ["I will not listen to a word against him from any human being,--not a word!", "저는 그 어떤 사람한테서든 그이를 헐뜯는 말은 한마디도 듣지 않겠어요—한마디도요!"], ["Remember that!\"", "그것만은 기억해 주세요!”"], ["And her eyes flashed as she spoke.", "그렇게 말하는 릴리의 눈이 번뜩였다."]], [["He did not answer her, but took her hand and pressed it, and then she left him.", "그는 릴리에게 대답하지 않고 그 손을 잡아 꼭 쥐었고, 그러고서 릴리는 그를 떠났다."], ["\"The Dales were ever constant!\"", "“데일 집안사람은 언제나 한결같았지!”"], ["he said to himself, as he walked up and down the terrace before his house.", "그는 집 앞 테라스를 오르내리며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["\"Ever constant!\"", "“언제나 한결같았어!”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXI. THE WOUNDED FAWN.", "# 제31장. 상처 입은 새끼 사슴"]], [["Nearly two months passed away, and it was now Christmas time at Allington.", "거의 두 달이 흘러, 이제 앨링턴에도 크리스마스철이 찾아왔다."], ["It may be presumed that there was no intention at either house that the mirth should be very loud.", "두 집 어느 쪽에서도 흥겨움을 크게 떠들 뜻은 없었으리라고 짐작할 만하다."], ["Such a wound as that received by Lily Dale was one from which recovery could not be quick, and it was felt by all the family that a weight was upon them which made gaiety impracticable.", "릴리 데일이 받은 것과 같은 상처는 빨리 아물 수 있는 것이 아니었고, 온 식구가 자기들 위에 얹힌 무게 때문에 유쾌하게 지내기란 어렵다고 느끼고 있었다."], ["As for Lily herself it may be said that she bore her misfortune with all a woman's courage.", "릴리 자신으로 말하자면, 그 불행을 여자로서 낼 수 있는 온갖 용기로 견뎌 냈다고 해도 좋겠다."], ["For the first week she stood up as a tree that stands against the wind, which is soon to be shivered to pieces because it will not bend.", "첫 한 주 동안 그녀는 바람 앞에 버티고 선 나무처럼 꼿꼿이 서 있었다. 휘려 들지 않기에 머지않아 산산조각이 나고 마는 그런 나무처럼."], ["During that week her mother and sister were frightened by her calmness and endurance.", "그 한 주 동안 어머니와 언니는 그녀의 침착함과 인내에 오히려 겁을 먹었다."], ["She would perform her daily task.", "그녀는 날마다 할 일을 해냈다."], ["She would go out through the village, and appear at her place in church on the first Sunday.", "마을을 지나 밖으로 나갔고, 첫 일요일에는 교회의 제자리에 나와 앉았다."], ["She would sit over her book of an evening, keeping back her tears; and would chide her mother and sister when she found that they were regarding her with earnest anxiety.", "저녁이면 눈물을 삼키며 책을 펴 놓고 앉아 있었다. 그리고 어머니와 언니가 근심 어린 눈으로 자기를 바라보고 있는 것을 알아채면 나무라곤 했다."]], [["\"Mamma, let it all be as though it had never been,\"", "“엄마, 그 일은 전부 없었던 일인 셈 쳐요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Ah, dear!", "“아, 얘야!"], ["if that were but possible!\"", "그럴 수만 있다면 얼마나 좋겠니!”"]], [["\"God forbid that it should be possible inwardly,\"", "“속으로야 그럴 수 있어서는 안 되죠.”"], ["Lily replied, \"but it is possible outwardly.", "릴리가 대답했다. “하지만 겉으로는 그럴 수 있어요."], ["I feel that you are more tender to me than you used to be, and that upsets me.", "엄마가 예전보다 저를 더 다정하게 대하시는 게 느껴지는데, 그게 저를 흔들어 놔요."], ["If you would only scold me because I am idle, I should soon be better.\"", "게으르다고 저를 꾸짖어만 주셔도 저는 금세 나아질 거예요.”"], ["But her mother could not speak to her as she perhaps might have spoken had no grief fallen upon her pet.", "하지만 어머니는, 그 귀여운 딸에게 아무런 슬픔도 닥치지 않았더라면 했을 법한 말투로 딸에게 말할 수가 없었다."], ["She could not cease from those anxious tender glances which made Lily know that she was looked on as a fawn wounded almost to death.", "근심 어린 다정한 눈길을 거둘 수가 없었고, 릴리는 그 눈길 때문에 자기가 거의 죽을 만큼 상처 입은 새끼 사슴으로 보이고 있다는 것을 알았다."]], [["At the end of the first week she gave way.", "첫 주가 끝날 무렵 그녀는 무너졌다."], ["\"I won't get up, Bell,\"", "“나 안 일어날래, 언니.”"], ["she said one morning, almost petulantly.", "어느 날 아침 그녀는 거의 짜증을 내듯 말했다."], ["\"I am ill;--I had better lie here out of the way.", "“아파. 그냥 여기 누워서 방해 안 되게 있는 게 나아."], ["Don't make a fuss about it.", "소란 피우지 마."], ["I'm stupid and foolish, and that makes me ill.\"", "내가 멍청하고 어리석어서, 그래서 아픈 거야.”"]], [["Thereupon Mrs. Dale and Bell were frightened, and looked into each other's blank faces, remembering stories of poor broken-hearted girls who had died because their loves had been unfortunate,--as small wax tapers whose lights are quenched if a breath of wind blows upon them too strongly.", "그 말에 데일 부인과 벨은 겁이 나서 서로의 넋 나간 얼굴을 마주 보았다."], ["But then Lily was in truth no such slight taper as that.", "사랑이 불행하게 끝난 탓에 가슴이 찢어져 죽어 버린 가여운 처녀들의 이야기가 떠올랐던 것이다. 바람 한 자락만 너무 세게 불어도 불꽃이 꺼져 버리는 작은 밀랍 초처럼 말이다."], ["Nor was she the stem that must be broken because it will not bend.", "그러나 릴리는 실상 그런 가냘픈 초가 아니었다. 휘려 들지 않기에 부러지고 마는 그런 줄기도 아니었다."], ["She bent herself to the blast during that week of illness, and then arose with her form still straight and graceful, and with her bright light unquenched.", "그녀는 앓아누운 그 한 주 동안 몰아치는 바람에 몸을 굽혔고, 그러고는 여전히 곧고 우아한 자태로 일어섰다. 그 밝은 불빛은 꺼지지 않은 채였다."]], [["After that she would talk more openly to her mother about her loss,--openly and with a true appreciation of the misfortune which had befallen her; but with an assurance of strength which seemed to ridicule the idea of a broken heart.", "그 뒤로 그녀는 자기가 잃은 것에 대해 어머니에게 더 터놓고 이야기했다. 터놓고, 그리고 자기에게 닥친 불행을 제대로 알아본 채로."], ["\"I know that I can bear it,\"", "하지만 가슴이 찢어진다느니 하는 생각을 비웃는 듯한 굳센 자신감을 함께 지니고서였다. “저는 견딜 수 있어요.”"], ["she said, \"and that I can bear it without lasting unhappiness.", "그녀가 말했다. “게다가 오래가는 불행 없이 견딜 수 있어요."], ["Of course I shall always love him, and must feel almost as you felt when you lost my father.\"", "물론 저는 늘 그이를 사랑하겠죠. 엄마가 아버지를 잃으셨을 때 느끼셨던 것과 거의 같은 심정일 거예요.”"]], [["In answer to this Mrs. Dale could say nothing.", "이 말에 데일 부인은 아무 대답도 할 수 없었다."], ["She could not speak out her thoughts about Crosbie, and explain to Lily that he was unworthy of her love.", "크로즈비에 대한 자기 생각을 털어놓고서 그자는 네 사랑을 받을 자격이 없다고 릴리에게 설명해 줄 수는 없었다."], ["Love does not follow worth, and is not given to excellence;--nor is it destroyed by ill-usage, nor killed by blows and mutilation.", "사랑은 값어치를 따라가지 않으며, 훌륭함에 주어지는 것도 아니다. 또한 모질게 대한다고 부서지지도 않고, 매질과 난도질로도 죽지 않는다."], ["When Lily declared that she still loved the man who had so ill-used her, Mrs. Dale would be silent.", "자기를 그토록 모질게 대한 남자를 아직도 사랑한다고 릴리가 선언할 때면 데일 부인은 잠자코 있었다."], ["Each perfectly understood the other, but on that matter even they could not interchange their thoughts with freedom.", "둘은 서로를 완전히 이해하고 있었지만, 그 문제에 관해서만은 그 두 사람조차 마음을 자유로이 주고받을 수가 없었다."]], [["\"You must promise never to be tired of me, mamma,\"", "“저한테 절대 싫증 내지 않겠다고 약속하셔야 해요, 엄마.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Mothers do not often get tired of their children, whatever the children may do of their mothers.\"", "“어미가 제 자식한테 싫증 내는 일은 흔치 않단다. 자식이 제 어미한테 무슨 짓을 하든 말이다.”"]], [["\"I'm not so sure of that when the children turn out old maids.", "“자식이 노처녀가 되고 나면 그건 잘 모르겠는데요."], ["And I mean to have a will of my own, too, mamma; and a way also, if it be possible.", "그리고 저도 제 고집을 부릴 작정이에요, 엄마. 될 수만 있다면 제 방식대로 살 작정이고요."], ["When Bell is married I shall consider it a partnership, and I shan't do what I'm told any longer.\"", "언니가 시집가고 나면 저는 이 집을 동업으로 여길 거예요. 그러니 시키는 대로만 하지는 않을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Forewarned will be forearmed.\"", "“미리 일러 주었으니 미리 대비를 해 두마.”"]], [["\"Exactly;--and I don't want to take you by surprise.", "“바로 그거예요. 엄마를 느닷없이 놀라게 하고 싶지는 않으니까요."], ["For a year or two longer, till Bell is gone, I mean to be dutiful; but it would be very stupid for a girl to be dutiful all her life.\"", "언니가 갈 때까지 한두 해 더는 고분고분할 작정이에요. 하지만 처녀가 평생을 고분고분하기만 하다면 그건 아주 멍청한 짓이겠죠.”"]], [["All of which Mrs. Dale understood thoroughly.", "데일 부인은 이 모든 말을 속속들이 알아들었다."], ["It amounted to an assertion on Lily's part that she had loved once and could never love again; that she had played her game, hoping, as other girls hope, that she might win the prize of a husband; but that, having lost, she could never play the game again.", "그것은 릴리 쪽에서 하는 선언이나 다름없었다. 자기는 한 번 사랑했고 다시는 사랑할 수 없다는 것, 다른 처녀들이 그러하듯 남편이라는 상을 타 볼까 하는 마음으로 판에 뛰어들었으나 지고 말았으니 다시는 그 판에 끼어들 수 없다는 것 말이다."], ["It was that inward conviction on Lily's part which made her say such words to her mother.", "릴리로 하여금 어머니에게 그런 말을 하게 만든 것은 바로 그 속마음의 확신이었다."], ["But Mrs. Dale would by no means allow herself to share this conviction.", "그러나 데일 부인은 그 확신을 함께 나눌 마음이 조금도 없었다."], ["She declared to herself that time would cure Lily's wound, and that her child might yet be crowned by the bliss of a happy marriage.", "세월이 릴리의 상처를 아물게 할 것이며 제 아이도 언젠가는 행복한 혼인의 축복으로 관을 쓰게 되리라고 그녀는 스스로에게 다짐했다."], ["She would not in her heart consent to that plan in accordance with which Lily's destiny in life was to be regarded as already fixed.", "릴리의 한평생 운명이 이미 정해진 것으로 여겨져야 한다는 그런 계획에 그녀는 마음속으로 결코 동의하지 않았다."], ["She had never really liked Crosbie as a suitor, and would herself have preferred John Eames, with all the faults of his hobbledehoyhood on his head.", "그녀는 구혼자로서의 크로즈비를 애초에 진심으로 좋아한 적이 없었고, 풋내기 시절의 온갖 허물을 머리에 인 존 임스 쪽을 오히려 더 마음에 두었을 사람이었다."], ["It might yet come to pass that John Eames' love might be made happy.", "존 임스의 사랑이 행복해지는 일이 아직 일어날 수도 있는 것이었다."]], [["But in the meantime Lily, as I have said, had become strong in her courage, and recommenced the work of living with no lackadaisical self-assurance that because she had been made more unhappy than others, therefore she should allow herself to be more idle.", "그러나 그러는 동안에도 릴리는, 내가 말했듯이, 용기 안에서 굳세어졌고, 남들보다 더 불행해졌으니 그만큼 더 게을러도 된다는 식의 나른한 자기 위안 없이 살아가는 일을 다시 시작했다."], ["Morning and night she prayed for him, and daily, almost hour by hour, she assured herself that it was still her duty to love him.", "아침저녁으로 그녀는 그를 위해 기도했고, 날마다, 거의 시간마다, 그를 사랑하는 것이 여전히 자기 본분이라고 스스로에게 다짐했다."], ["It was hard, this duty of loving, without any power of expressing such love.", "그 사랑을 드러낼 힘이라고는 조금도 없는 채로 사랑해야 하는 이 본분은 고된 것이었다."], ["But still she would do her duty.", "그래도 그녀는 제 본분을 다하려 했다."]], [["\"Tell me at once, mamma,\"", "“곧바로 말씀해 주세요, 엄마.”"], ["she said one morning, \"when you hear that the day is fixed for his marriage.", "어느 날 아침 그녀가 말했다. “그이의 혼인 날짜가 정해졌다는 소식을 들으시거든요."], ["Pray don't keep me in the dark.\"", "제발 저만 모르게 두지는 마세요.”"]], [["\"It is to be in February,\"", "“이월이라는구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"But let me know the day.", "“그래도 날짜는 알려 주세요."], ["It must not be to me like ordinary days.", "저에게 그날이 여느 날 같아서는 안 되니까요."], ["But do not look unhappy, mamma; I am not going to make a fool of myself.", "하지만 그런 슬픈 얼굴은 하지 마세요, 엄마. 저는 바보짓 같은 건 하지 않을 거예요."], ["I shan't steal off and appear in the church like a ghost.\"", "몰래 빠져나가 유령처럼 그 교회에 나타나거나 하지도 않을 거고요.”"], ["And then, having uttered her little joke, a sob came, and she hid her face on her mother's bosom.", "그러고는 그 작은 농담을 뱉고 나자 흐느낌이 올라왔고, 그녀는 어머니의 가슴에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["In a moment she raised it again.", "이내 다시 얼굴을 들었다."], ["\"Believe me, mamma, that I am not unhappy,\"", "“정말이에요, 엄마. 저는 불행하지 않아요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["After the expiration of that second week Mrs. Dale did write a letter to Crosbie:", "그 둘째 주가 지나고 나서 데일 부인은 크로즈비에게 정말로 편지를 한 통 썼다."]], [["I suppose [she said] it is right that I should acknowledge the receipt of your letter.", "제 생각에는 [그녀는 이렇게 썼다] 귀하의 편지를 받았음을 알려 드리는 것이 도리에 맞는 일이겠지요."], ["I do not know that I have aught else to say to you.", "그 밖에 달리 드릴 말씀이 있는지는 모르겠습니다."], ["It would not become me as a woman to say what I think of your conduct, but I believe that your conscience will tell you the same things.", "귀하의 처신을 두고 제가 어떻게 생각하는지를 말하는 것은 여자로서 온당한 일이 아니겠으나, 귀하의 양심이 똑같은 말을 들려주리라 믿습니다."], ["If it do not, you must, indeed, be hardened.", "만일 그렇지 않다면 귀하는 정말이지 무디어질 대로 무디어진 사람일 것입니다."], ["I have promised my child that I will send to you a message from her.", "저는 제 아이에게, 그 아이가 보내는 말을 귀하에게 전해 주겠다고 약속했습니다."], ["She bids me tell you that she has forgiven you, and that she does not hate you.", "그 아이는 자기가 귀하를 용서했으며 귀하를 미워하지 않는다고 전해 달라 합니다."], ["May God also forgive you, and may you recover his love.", "하느님께서도 귀하를 용서하시고, 귀하가 그분의 사랑을 되찾기를 빕니다."]], [["I beg that no rejoinder may be made to this letter, either to myself or to any of my family.", "이 편지에 대해서는 저에게든 제 식구 누구에게든 답장을 보내지 마시기를 부탁드립니다."]], [["The squire wrote no answer to the letter which he had received, nor did he take any steps towards the immediate punishment of Crosbie.", "지주는 자기가 받은 편지에 아무 답장도 쓰지 않았고, 크로즈비를 당장 응징하기 위한 어떤 조치도 취하지 않았다."], ["Indeed he had declared that no such steps could be taken, explaining to his nephew that such a man could be served only as one serves a rat.", "사실 그는 그런 조치란 취할 수 있는 것이 아니라고 잘라 말하면서, 그런 인간은 쥐를 다루듯 다루는 수밖에 없다고 조카에게 설명해 두었던 것이다."]], [["\"I shall never see him,\"", "“나는 그자를 다시 볼 일이 없을 게다.” 그가 또 한 번 말했다."], ["he said once again; \"if I did, I should not scruple to hit him on the head with my stick; but I should think ill of myself to go after him with such an object.\"", "“만일 마주친다면 지팡이로 그자의 머리를 갈기는 데 망설이지 않겠지. 하지만 그런 목적으로 그자를 쫓아다닌다면 나 자신을 못나게 여기게 될 거야.”"]], [["And yet it was a terrible sorrow to the old man that the scoundrel who had so injured him and his should escape scot-free.", "그러면서도 자기와 자기 식구를 그토록 짓밟은 그 악당이 아무 벌도 받지 않고 빠져나간다는 것은 노인에게 지독한 슬픔이었다."], ["He had not forgiven Crosbie.", "그는 크로즈비를 용서하지 않았다."], ["No idea of forgiveness had ever crossed his mind.", "용서라는 생각은 한 번도 그의 머리를 스치지 않았다."], ["He would have hated himself had he thought it possible that he could be induced to forgive such an injury.", "그런 짓을 용서하도록 자기가 이끌릴 수 있다고 여겼다면 그는 스스로를 미워했을 것이다."], ["\"There is an amount of rascality in it,--of low meanness, which I do not understand,\"", "“거기에는 내가 도무지 이해할 수 없는 만큼의 악당 근성이 있어. 천하고 비열한 근성 말이다.”"], ["he would say over and over again to his nephew.", "그는 조카에게 몇 번이고 되풀이해 말하곤 했다."], ["And then as he would walk alone on the terrace he would speculate within his own mind whether Bernard would take any steps towards avenging his cousin's injury.", "그러고는 테라스를 혼자 거닐면서, 버나드가 사촌이 당한 일을 갚아 주려고 무슨 조치를 취할 것인지 마음속으로 헤아려 보곤 했다."], ["\"He is right,\"", "“저 애가 옳지.”"], ["he would say to himself; \"Bernard is quite right.", "그는 혼자 중얼거리곤 했다. “버나드가 아주 옳아."], ["But when I was young I could not have stood it.", "하지만 내가 젊었을 때라면 나는 그걸 참아 내지 못했을 게다."], ["In those days a gentleman might have a fellow out who had treated him as he has treated us.", "그 시절에는 우리를 대접한 그런 식으로 자기를 대접한 놈을 신사가 결투장으로 불러낼 수 있었지."], ["A man was satisfied in feeling that he had done something.", "사람이란 무언가 했다는 느낌만으로도 속이 풀렸던 게야."], ["I suppose the world is different now-a-days.\"", "요즘 세상은 다른 모양이지.” 세상은 다르다."], ["The world is different; but the squire by no means acknowledged in his heart that there had been any improvement.", "그러나 지주는 무언가 나아진 것이 있다고는 마음속으로 결코 인정하지 않았다."]], [["Bernard also was greatly troubled in his mind.", "버나드 또한 마음이 몹시 괴로웠다."], ["He would have had no objection to fight a duel with Crosbie, had duels in these days been possible.", "요즘 세상에 결투가 가능하기만 했다면 그는 크로즈비와 한 판 붙는 데 아무 이의도 없었을 것이다."], ["But he believed them to be no longer possible,--at any rate without ridicule.", "그러나 결투란 이제 가능하지 않다고, 적어도 웃음거리가 되지 않고서는 불가능하다고 그는 믿었다."], ["And if he could not fight the man, in what other way was he to punish him?", "그리고 그자와 싸울 수 없다면 달리 무슨 방법으로 그자를 벌한단 말인가?"], ["Was it not the fact that for such a fault the world afforded no punishment?", "그런 잘못에 대해서는 세상이 아무 벌도 마련해 두지 않았다는 것이 사실 아닌가?"], ["Was it not in the power of a man like Crosbie to amuse himself for a week or two at the expense of a girl's happiness for life, and then to escape absolutely without any ill effects to himself?", "크로즈비 같은 사내가 한 처녀의 한평생 행복을 밑천 삼아 한두 주 재미를 보고서도 제 몸에는 아무 나쁜 결과도 없이 말끔히 빠져나가는 것이, 바로 그런 사내의 힘 안에 있지 않은가?"], ["\"I shall be barred out of my club lest I should meet him,\"", "“나는 그자와 마주칠까 봐 내 클럽에도 못 나가게 될 텐데,”"], ["Bernard said to himself, \"but he will not be barred out.\"", "버나드는 혼자 생각했다. “그자는 못 나갈 일이 없겠지.”"], ["Moreover, there was a feeling within him that the matter would be one of triumph to Crosbie rather than otherwise.", "게다가 그의 속에는 이 일이 오히려 크로즈비에게 자랑거리가 되리라는 느낌이 있었다."], ["In having secured for himself the pleasure of his courtship with such a girl as Lily Dale, without encountering the penalty usually consequent upon such amusement, he would be held by many as having merited much admiration.", "릴리 데일 같은 처녀와 연애하는 즐거움을 실컷 챙기고서도 그런 재미에 흔히 따라붙는 대가를 치르지 않았으니, 많은 이들이 그를 두고 크게 부러워할 만하다 여기리라는 것이었다."], ["He had sinned against all the Dales, and yet the suffering arising from his sin was to fall upon the Dales exclusively.", "그자는 데일 집안사람 모두에게 죄를 지었는데, 그 죄에서 나온 고통은 오로지 데일 집안사람들에게만 떨어지게 되어 있었다."], ["Such was Bernard's reasoning, as he speculated on the whole affair, sadly enough,--wishing to be avenged, but not knowing where to look for vengeance.", "버나드가 이 일 전체를 꽤나 서글프게 헤아리며 내린 결론은 그러했다. 복수하고 싶으나 어디서 복수를 찾아야 할지 모르는 채로 말이다."], ["For myself I believe him to have been altogether wrong as to the light in which he supposed that Crosbie's falsehood would be regarded by Crosbie's friends.", "나로서는, 크로즈비의 배신이 크로즈비의 벗들에게 어떻게 비칠 것인가 하는 점에 대해 그가 전적으로 틀렸다고 믿는다."], ["Men will still talk of such things lightly, professing that all is fair in love as it is in war, and speaking almost with envy of the good fortunes of a practised deceiver.", "사람들은 여전히 그런 일을 가볍게 입에 올리며, 사랑에서든 전쟁에서든 무슨 수를 써도 괜찮다고 늘어놓고, 노련한 사기꾼의 행운을 두고 거의 부러워하듯 이야기할 것이다."], ["But I have never come across the man who thought in this way with reference to an individual case.", "그러나 나는 어떤 한 사람의 실제 일을 두고 그런 식으로 생각하는 사내를 여태 만나 본 적이 없다."], ["Crosbie's own judgment as to the consequences to himself of what he had done was more correct than that formed by Bernard Dale.", "자기가 한 짓이 자기에게 어떤 결과를 가져올지에 대해서는 크로즈비 자신의 판단이 버나드 데일의 판단보다 더 정확했다."], ["He had regarded the act as venial as long as it was still to do,--while it was still within his power to leave it undone; but from the moment of its accomplishment it had forced itself upon his own view in its proper light.", "그는 그 짓을 아직 하기 전에는, 아직 저지르지 않고 내버려 둘 힘이 자기에게 있는 동안에는 그것을 가벼운 죄로 여겼다. 그러나 그것을 저지른 순간부터 그 일은 제 본모습 그대로 그의 눈앞에 밀어닥쳤다."], ["He knew that he had been a scoundrel, and he knew that other men would so think of him.", "그는 자기가 악당이었음을 알았고, 다른 사람들도 자기를 그렇게 여기리라는 것을 알았다."], ["His friend Fowler Pratt, who had the reputation of looking at women simply as toys, had so regarded him.", "여자를 그저 장난감으로 여긴다는 평판을 듣던 그의 벗 파울러 프랫조차 그를 그렇게 보았다."], ["Instead of boasting of what he had done, he was as afraid of alluding to any matter connected with his marriage as a man is of talking of the articles which he has stolen.", "자기가 한 일을 자랑하기는커녕, 그는 훔친 물건 이야기를 꺼리는 사람처럼 자기 혼인에 얽힌 어떤 이야기든 입에 올리기를 두려워했다."], ["He had already felt that men at his club looked askance at him; and, though he was no coward as regarded his own skin and bones, he had an undefined fear lest some day he might encounter Bernard Dale purposely armed with a stick.", "클럽의 사내들이 자기를 곁눈질로 본다는 것을 그는 이미 느끼고 있었다. 그리고 제 살가죽과 뼈에 관해서는 겁쟁이가 아니었으나, 언젠가 지팡이를 단단히 챙겨 든 버나드 데일과 맞닥뜨릴지 모른다는 형체 없는 두려움이 있었다."], ["The squire and his nephew were wrong in supposing that Crosbie was unpunished.", "크로즈비가 벌을 받지 않고 있다고 여긴 점에서 지주와 그 조카는 틀렸던 것이다."]], [["And as the winter came on he felt that he was closely watched by the noble family of De Courcy.", "그리고 겨울이 다가오면서 그는 드 코시 백작 집안이 자기를 바싹 지켜보고 있음을 느꼈다. 그 고귀한 집안 사람들 가운데 몇몇은 이미 진심으로 미워하는 법을 배운 터였다."], ["Some of that noble family he had already learned to hate cordially.", "아너러블 존이 십일월에 런던으로 올라와 그를 지긋지긋하게 괴롭혔다. 세브라이트에서 만찬을 사라고 우겨 대고, 장차 매부가 될 사람의 방에서 오후 내내 담배를 피워 대고, 장차 매부가 될 사람의 물건을 빌려 갔다."], ["The Honourable John came up to town in November, and persecuted him vilely;--insisted on having dinners given to him at Sebright's, of smoking throughout the whole afternoon in his future brother-in-law's rooms, and on borrowing his future brother-in-law's possessions; till at last Crosbie determined that it would be wise to quarrel with the Honourable John,--and he quarrelled with him accordingly, turning him out of his rooms, and telling him in so many words that he would have no more to do with him.", "마침내 크로즈비는 아너러블 존과 사이를 틀어 놓는 편이 현명하겠다고 마음먹었고, 그리하여 실제로 사이를 틀어 버렸다. 그를 제 방에서 내쫓고, 이제 다시는 상대하지 않겠다고 대놓고 말해 버린 것이다."]], [["\"You'll have to do it, as I did,\"", "“나처럼 자네도 그렇게 해야 할 걸세.”"], ["Mortimer Gazebee had said to him; \"I didn't like it because of the family, but Lady Amelia told me that it must be so.\"", "모티머 게이지비가 그에게 말한 적이 있었다. “나는 집안을 생각해서 내키지 않았네만, 레이디 어밀리아가 그래야 한다고 하더군.”"], ["Whereupon Crosbie took the advice of Mortimer Gazebee.", "그리하여 크로즈비는 모티머 게이지비의 충고를 따랐다."]], [["But the hospitality of the Gazebees was perhaps more distressing to him than even the importunities of the Honourable John.", "그러나 게이지비 부부의 환대는 아너러블 존의 성가신 조름보다도 어쩌면 그를 더 괴롭게 했다."], ["It seemed as though his future sister-in-law was determined not to leave him alone.", "장차 처형이 될 사람은 그를 가만히 내버려 두지 않기로 작정한 듯했다."], ["Mortimer was sent to fetch him up for the Sunday afternoons, and he found that he was constrained to go to the villa in St. John's Wood, even in opposition to his own most strenuous will.", "일요일 오후마다 모티머가 그를 데리러 왔고, 그는 제 굳은 뜻을 다해 버티는데도 세인트 존스 우드의 별장으로 가지 않을 수 없다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["He could not quite analyze the circumstances of his own position, but he felt as though he were a cock with his spurs cut off,--as a dog with his teeth drawn.", "자기 처지의 사정을 스스로도 온전히 따져 볼 수는 없었으나, 그는 며느리발톱이 잘린 수탉 같은, 이빨을 뽑힌 개 같은 기분이었다."], ["He found himself becoming humble and meek.", "자기가 고분고분해지고 순해지고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["He had to acknowledge to himself that he was afraid of Lady Amelia, and almost even afraid of Mortimer Gazebee.", "자기가 레이디 어밀리아를 두려워하고 있으며 심지어 모티머 게이지비마저 두려워하다시피 하고 있음을 스스로 인정해야 했다."], ["He was aware that they watched him, and knew all his goings out and comings in.", "그들이 자기를 지켜보고 있고 자기가 드나드는 것을 죄다 알고 있다는 것도 알았다."], ["They called him Adolphus, and made him tame.", "그들은 그를 어돌퍼스라 부르며 길들였다."], ["That coming evil day in February was dinned into his ears.", "다가오는 이월의 그 날은 그의 귀에 못이 박이도록 울려 댔다."], ["Lady Amelia would go and look at furniture for him, and talked by the hour about bedding and sheets.", "레이디 어밀리아는 그를 위해 가구를 보러 가겠다고 했고, 침구며 시트 이야기를 몇 시간이고 늘어놓았다."], ["\"You had better get your kitchen things at Tomkins'.", "“부엌 세간은 톰킨스에서 장만하는 편이 나을 거요."], ["They're all good, and he'll give you ten per cent.", "물건이 다 좋고, 현금으로 치르면 일 할을 깎아 준다오."], ["off if you pay him ready money,--which of course you will, you know!\"", "물론 그대야 현금으로 치르겠지요, 안 그렇소!”"], ["Was it for this that he had sacrificed Lily Dale?--for this that he had allied himself with the noble house of De Courcy?", "그가 릴리 데일을 희생시킨 것이 이것을 위해서였던가? 드 코시라는 고귀한 집안과 손을 잡은 것이 이것을 위해서였던가?"]], [["Mortimer had been at him about the settlements from the very first moment of his return to London, and had already bound him up hand and foot.", "모티머는 그가 런던으로 돌아온 바로 그 순간부터 재산 약정 문제로 그를 물고 늘어졌고, 이미 그를 손발까지 꽁꽁 묶어 놓았다."], ["His life was insured, and the policy was in Mortimer's hands.", "그의 생명보험이 들어졌고, 증권은 모티머의 손에 있었다."], ["His own little bit of money had been already handed over to be tied up with Lady Alexandrina's little bit.", "그의 얼마 안 되는 재산도 레이디 알렉산드리나의 얼마 안 되는 재산과 함께 묶어 두도록 이미 넘어가 있었다."], ["It seemed to him that in all the arrangements made the intention was that he should die off speedily, and that Lady Alexandrina should be provided with a decent little income, sufficient for St. John's Wood.", "마련된 모든 조치를 보자면, 그가 어서 죽어 없어지고 레이디 알렉산드리나에게는 세인트 존스 우드에서 살기에 넉넉한 아담한 수입이 마련되도록 하려는 심산인 듯했다."], ["Things were to be so settled that he could not even spend the proceeds of his own money, or of hers.", "그가 제 돈에서 나오는 몫도 그녀의 돈에서 나오는 몫도 쓸 수 없게끔 일이 짜여 있었다."], ["They were to go, under the fostering hand of Mortimer Gazebee, in paying insurances.", "그 돈은 모티머 게이지비의 알뜰한 손길 아래 보험료로 들어가게 되어 있었다."], ["If he would only die the day after his marriage, there would really be a very nice sum of money for Alexandrina, almost worthy of the acceptance of an earl's daughter.", "그가 혼인한 다음 날 죽어만 준다면 알렉산드리나에게는 정말이지 아주 짭짤한 돈이, 백작의 딸이 받아들이기에 거의 걸맞은 액수가 남을 것이었다."], ["Six months ago he would have considered himself able to turn Mortimer Gazebee round his finger on any subject that could be introduced between them.", "여섯 달 전이라면 그는 자기와 게이지비 사이에 오갈 수 있는 어떤 문제에 대해서든 게이지비를 제 손가락으로 돌려 세울 수 있다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["When they chanced to meet Gazebee had been quite humble to him, treating him almost as a superior being.", "어쩌다 마주치면 게이지비는 그에게 아주 굽실거리며 그를 저보다 윗길인 존재처럼 대했다."], ["He had looked down on Gazebee from a very great height.", "그는 아주 높은 데서 게이지비를 내려다보았다."], ["But now it seemed as though he were powerless in this man's hands.", "그러나 이제는 이 사내의 손아귀에서 자기가 무력한 것처럼 보였다."]], [["But perhaps the countess had become his greatest aversion.", "그러나 어쩌면 백작부인이야말로 그가 가장 싫어하는 사람이 되었는지도 몰랐다."], ["She was perpetually writing to him little notes in which she gave him multitudes of commissions, sending him about as though he had been her servant.", "그녀는 끊임없이 그에게 짧은 쪽지를 써 보내 수십 가지 심부름을 시켰고, 그를 마치 제 하인처럼 이리저리 부려 먹었다."], ["And she pestered him with advice which was even worse than her commissions, telling him of the style of life in which Alexandrina would expect to live, and warning him very frequently that such an one as he could not expect to be admitted within the bosom of so noble a family without paying very dearly for that inestimable privilege.", "게다가 그녀는 심부름보다도 더 고약한 충고로 그를 들볶았다. 알렉산드리나가 어떤 격의 살림을 기대하고 있는지 일러 주었고, 그 같은 자가 그토록 고귀한 집안의 품 안에 받아들여지면서 그 값 매길 수 없는 특권에 대해 아주 비싼 값을 치르지 않기를 바랄 수는 없다고 몇 번이고 일깨웠다."], ["Her letters had become odious to him, and he would chuck them on one side, leaving them for the whole day unopened.", "그녀의 편지는 그에게 역겨운 것이 되어, 그는 그것을 한쪽으로 홱 던져 두고 하루 종일 뜯어보지도 않곤 했다."], ["He had already made up his mind that he would quarrel with the countess also, very shortly after his marriage; indeed, that he would separate himself from the whole family if it were possible.", "그는 혼인하고 나면 곧 백작부인과도 사이를 틀어야겠다고 이미 마음먹고 있었다. 아니, 될 수만 있다면 그 집안 전체와 갈라서겠다고 마음먹었다."], ["And yet he had entered into this engagement mainly with the view of reaping those advantages which would accrue to him from being allied to the De Courcys!", "그런데도 그가 이 약혼에 뛰어든 것은 무엇보다 드 코시 집안과 연을 맺음으로써 자기에게 돌아올 이익을 거두어들이려는 속셈에서였던 것이다!"], ["The squire and his nephew were wretched in thinking that this man was escaping without punishment, but they might have spared themselves that misery.", "지주와 그 조카는 이 사내가 벌도 받지 않고 빠져나가고 있다고 생각하며 비참해했지만, 그런 괴로움은 겪지 않아도 좋았을 것이다."]], [["It had been understood from the first that he was to spend his Christmas at Courcy Castle.", "그가 크리스마스를 코시 성에서 보내기로 되어 있다는 것은 처음부터 정해진 일이었다."], ["From this undertaking it was quite out of his power to enfranchise himself: but he resolved that his visit should be as short as possible.", "이 약속에서 몸을 빼내기란 도저히 그의 힘으로 될 일이 아니었다. 다만 그는 머무는 기간을 되도록 짧게 하기로 마음먹었다."], ["Christmas Day unfortunately came on a Monday, and it was known to the De Courcy world that Saturday was almost a dies non at the General Committee Office.", "공교롭게도 크리스마스가 월요일이었고, 일반위원회 사무국에서 토요일이란 사실상 근무하지 않는 날, 곧 디에스 논이나 다름없다는 것은 드 코시 집안 사람들도 다 아는 바였다."], ["As to those three days there was no escape for him; but he made Alexandrina understand that the three Commissioners were men of iron as to any extension of those three days.", "그 사흘에 관해서는 그에게 빠져나갈 구멍이 없었다. 그러나 그는 세 분 위원께서 그 사흘을 넘겨 늘리는 문제에 관한 한 쇠붙이 같은 분들이라고 알렉산드리나에게 일러 두었다."], ["\"I must be absent again in February, of course,\"", "“물론 이월에도 다시 자리를 비워야 하잖소.”"], ["he said, almost making his wail audible in the words he used, \"and therefore it is quite impossible that I should stay now beyond the Monday.\"", "그가 말했는데, 그 말투에서는 신음 소리가 거의 들릴 지경이었다. “그러니 지금 월요일을 넘겨 머무는 것은 도저히 될 수가 없소.”"], ["Had there been attractions for him at Courcy Castle I think he might have arranged with Mr. Optimist for a week or ten days.", "코시 성에 그를 끌어당길 만한 것이 있었더라면 그는 옵티미스트 씨와 이야기해 일주일이나 열흘쯤은 어렵잖게 마련했으리라고 나는 생각한다."], ["\"We shall be all alone,\"", "“우리끼리만 있게 될 거요.”"], ["the countess wrote to him, \"and I hope you will have an opportunity of learning more of our ways than you have ever really been able to do as yet.\"", "백작부인이 그에게 써 보냈다. “그러니 그대도 우리 집안의 방식을 여태 알아 온 것보다 더 깊이 배울 기회를 얻게 되기를 바라오.”"], ["This was bitter as gall to him.", "이것은 그에게 쓸개즙처럼 쓴 말이었다."], ["But in this world all valuable commodities have their price; and when men such as Crosbie aspire to obtain for themselves an alliance with noble families, they must pay the market price for the article which they purchase.", "그러나 이 세상에서 값나가는 물건에는 다 값이 붙어 있는 법이다. 그러니 크로즈비 같은 사내들이 고귀한 집안과 인연 맺기를 바랄 때에는 자기가 사들이는 그 물건에 매겨진 장터 값을 치러야 하는 것이다."]], [["\"You'll all come up and dine with us on Monday,\" the squire said to Mrs.", "“월요일에는 다들 올라와서 우리와 함께 저녁을 드시오.”"], ["Dale, about the middle of the previous week.", "그 전 주 중반쯤 지주가 데일 부인에게 말했다."]], [["\"Well, I think not,\" said Mrs.", "“글쎄요, 아무래도 그만두는 게 좋겠어요.”"], ["Dale; \"we are better, perhaps, as we are.\"", "데일 부인이 말했다. “저희는 이대로 지내는 편이 나을지도 모르겠어요.”"]], [["At this moment the squire and his sister-in-law were on much more friendly terms than had been usual with them, and he took her reply in good part, understanding her feeling.", "이 무렵 지주와 그 제수는 여느 때보다 훨씬 사이가 좋았고, 그는 그녀의 마음을 헤아려 그 대답을 좋게 받아들였다."], ["Therefore, he pressed his request, and succeeded.", "그래서 그는 청을 거듭 밀어붙였고, 뜻을 이루었다."]], [["\"I think you're wrong,\"", "“내 보기엔 제수씨가 틀렸소.”"], ["he said; \"I don't suppose that we shall have a very merry Christmas.", "그가 말했다. “우리가 아주 즐거운 크리스마스를 보내리라고는 나도 생각지 않소."], ["You and the girls will hardly have that whether you eat your pudding here or at the Great House.", "제수씨와 아이들이야 여기서 푸딩을 먹든 큰 집에서 먹든 즐겁기는 어렵겠지."], ["But it will be better for us all to make the attempt.", "하지만 그래도 다 같이 애를 써 보는 편이 우리 모두에게 나을 거요."], ["It's the right thing to do.", "그게 옳은 일이오."], ["That's the way I look at it.\"", "나는 그렇게 보오.”"]], [["\"I'll ask Lily,\"", "“릴리에게 물어보지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Do, do.", "“그러시오, 그러시오."], ["Give her my love, and tell her from me that, in spite of all that has come and gone, Christmas Day should still be to her a day of rejoicing.", "그 애에게 내 사랑을 전하고, 그동안 오고 간 온갖 일에도 불구하고 크리스마스는 그 애에게 여전히 기뻐하는 날이어야 한다고 내 말로 일러 주시오."], ["We'll dine about three, so that the servants can have the afternoon.\"", "하인들이 오후를 쉴 수 있도록 세 시쯤에 저녁을 들기로 합시다.”"]], [["\"Of course we'll go,\"", "“당연히 가야죠.” 릴리가 말했다."], ["said Lily; \"why not?", "“안 갈 까닭이 어디 있어요?"], ["We always do.", "우리는 늘 갔잖아요."], ["And we'll have blind-man's-buff with all the Boyces, as we had last year, if uncle will ask them up.\"", "그리고 삼촌이 보이스네를 불러 주시면 작년처럼 보이스네 식구 전부와 눈 가리고 술래잡기를 할 거예요.”"], ["But the Boyces were not asked up for that occasion.", "그러나 그때만은 보이스네가 불려 오지 않았다."]], [["But Lily, though she put on it all so brave a face, had much to suffer, and did in truth suffer greatly.", "그러나 릴리는 그 모든 일에 그토록 씩씩한 얼굴을 지어 보였어도 견뎌야 할 것이 많았고, 사실 몹시 괴로워했다."], ["If you, my reader, ever chanced to slip into the gutter on a wet day, did you not find that the sympathy of the bystanders was by far the severest part of your misfortune?", "나의 독자여, 그대가 언젠가 비 오는 날 도랑에 미끄러져 빠져 본 적이 있다면, 곁에 선 사람들의 동정이야말로 그 낭패 가운데 단연 가장 견디기 힘든 대목이 아니었던가?"], ["Did you not declare to yourself that all might yet be well, if the people would only walk on and not look at you?", "사람들이 그냥 지나쳐 걸어가고 그대를 쳐다보지만 않는다면 아직 다 괜찮을 수 있으련만, 하고 스스로에게 되뇌지 않았던가?"], ["And yet you cannot blame those who stood and pitied you; or, perhaps, essayed to rub you down, and assist you in the recovery of your bedaubed hat.", "그러면서도 걸음을 멈추고 그대를 딱하게 여긴 사람들을, 또 어쩌면 그대를 일으켜 주며 흙투성이가 된 그대의 모자를 되찾도록 거들어 준 사람들을 그대는 탓할 수 없다."], ["You, yourself, if you see a man fall, cannot walk by as though nothing uncommon had happened to him.", "그대 자신도 누가 넘어지는 것을 보면 그에게 별일도 없었다는 듯 그냥 지나쳐 갈 수는 없는 것이다."], ["It was so with Lily.", "릴리에게도 그러했다."], ["The people of Allington could not regard her with their ordinary eyes.", "앨링턴 사람들은 그녀를 여느 때의 눈으로 볼 수 없었다."], ["They would look at her tenderly, knowing that she was a wounded fawn, and thus they aggravated the soreness of her wound. Old Mrs.", "그들은 그녀가 상처 입은 새끼 사슴임을 알기에 다정하게 바라보았고, 그리하여 그 상처의 쓰라림을 덧들였다."], ["Hearn condoled with her, telling her that very likely she would be better off as she was.", "늙은 헌 부인은 그녀를 위로하며, 아마 이렇게 된 편이 오히려 잘된 일일 거라고 말했다."], ["Lily would not lie about it in any way.", "릴리는 그 일을 두고 어떤 식으로도 거짓말을 하려 들지 않았다. “헌 부인,”"], ["\"Mrs. Hearn,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"the subject is painful to me.\" Mrs.", "“그 이야기는 저에게 괴로워요.”"], ["Hearn said no more about it, but on every meeting between them she looked the things she did not say.", "헌 부인은 그에 대해 더는 말하지 않았으나, 두 사람이 마주칠 때마다 입 밖에 내지 않은 것들을 눈빛으로 말했다. “릴리 아가씨!”"], ["\"Miss Lily!\"", "어느 날 홉킨스가 말했다. “릴리 아가씨!”"], ["said Hopkins, one day, \"Miss Lily!\"--and as he looked up into her face a tear had almost formed itself in his old eye--\"I knew what he was from the first.", "그러면서 그녀의 얼굴을 올려다보는 그의 늙은 눈에 눈물이 맺힐 뻔했다. “저는 처음부터 그자가 어떤 인간인지 알고 있었습니다요."], ["Oh, dear!", "아이고, 저런!"], ["oh, dear!", "아이고, 저런!"], ["if I could have had him killed!\"", "제가 그자를 죽여 버릴 수만 있었어도!”"], ["\"Hopkins, how dare you?\" said Lily.", "“홉킨스, 어찌 그런 말을 해요?”"], ["\"If you speak to me again in such a way, I will tell my uncle.\"", "릴리가 말했다. “나한테 또 그런 식으로 말하면 삼촌께 이를 거예요.”"], ["She turned away from him; but immediately turned back again, and put out her little hand to him.", "그녀는 그에게서 돌아섰다. 그러나 곧바로 다시 돌아서서 그에게 작은 손을 내밀었다."], ["\"I beg your pardon,\"", "“미안해요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"I know how kind you are, and I love you for it.\"", "“홉킨스가 얼마나 마음을 써 주는지 알아요. 그래서 나는 홉킨스가 좋아요.”"], ["And then she went away.", "그러고는 가 버렸다."], ["\"I'll go after him yet, and break the dirty neck of him,\"", "“그래도 내 언젠가는 그자를 쫓아가 그 더러운 목을 부러뜨려 놓고 말 테다.”"], ["said Hopkins to himself, as he walked down the path.", "홉킨스는 길을 내려가며 혼자 중얼거렸다."]], [["Shortly before Christmas Day she called with her sister at the vicarage.", "크리스마스를 얼마 앞두고 그녀는 언니와 함께 목사관을 찾아갔다."], ["Bell, in the course of the visit, left the room with one of the Boyce girls, to look at the last chrysanthemums of the year. Then Mrs.", "벨은 찾아가 있는 동안 보이스네 딸 하나와 함께 그해의 마지막 국화를 보러 방을 나갔다."], ["Boyce took advantage of the occasion to make her little speech.", "그러자 보이스 부인은 그 틈을 타 짤막한 말을 꺼냈다. “릴리 양,”"], ["\"My dear Lily,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"you will think me cold if I do not say one word to you.\"", "“내가 한마디도 하지 않으면 아가씨는 나를 매정하다 여기겠지요.”"], ["\"No, I shall not,\"", "“아니에요, 그러지 않을 거예요.”"], ["said Lily, almost sharply, shrinking from the finger that threatened to touch her sore.", "릴리는 제 상처를 건드리려 드는 손가락을 피하듯 몸을 움츠리며 거의 쏘아붙이듯 말했다."], ["\"There are things which should never be talked about.\"", "“아예 입에 올리지 않아야 할 일도 있는 법이니까요.”"], ["\"Well, well; perhaps so,\"", "“그래요, 그래요. 아마 그렇겠지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Boyce.", "보이스 부인이 말했다."], ["But for a minute or two she was unable to fall back upon any other topic, and sat looking at Lily with painful tenderness.", "그러나 한두 분 동안 그녀는 다른 화제로 옮겨 갈 수가 없어, 앉은 채로 아프도록 다정한 눈길로 릴리를 바라보았다. 그런 눈길 아래에서 릴리가 무엇을 견뎠는지는 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠다."], ["I need hardly say what were Lily's sufferings under such a gaze; but she bore it, acknowledging to herself in her misery that the fault did not lie with Mrs. Boyce.", "그러나 그녀는 견뎠고, 그 괴로움 속에서도 잘못이 보이스 부인에게 있지 않음을 스스로 인정했다."], ["How could Mrs. Boyce have looked at her otherwise than tenderly?", "보이스 부인인들 다정한 눈길 말고 달리 어떻게 그녀를 바라볼 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["It was settled, then, that Lily was to dine up at the Great House on Christmas Day, and thus show to the Allington world that she was not to be regarded as a person shut out from the world by the depth of her misfortune.", "그리하여 릴리가 크리스마스 날 큰 집에 올라가 저녁을 들고, 그로써 자기가 불행이 깊어 세상에서 밀려난 사람으로 여겨질 사람이 아님을 앨링턴 온 동네에 보여 주기로 정해졌다."], ["That she was right there can, I think, be no doubt; but as she walked across the little bridge, with her mother and sister, after returning from church, she would have given much to be able to have turned round, and have gone to bed instead of to her uncle's dinner.", "그녀가 옳았다는 데에는 아무 의심도 있을 수 없다고 나는 생각한다. 그러나 교회에서 돌아온 뒤 어머니와 언니와 함께 그 작은 다리를 건너 걸어가면서, 그녀는 몸을 돌려 삼촌의 만찬 자리가 아니라 잠자리로 갈 수만 있다면 무엇이든 내주었으리라."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXII. PAWKINS'S IN JERMYN STREET.", "# 제32장. 저민 스트리트의 포킨스"]], [["The show of fat beasts in London took place this year on the twentieth day of December, and I have always understood that a certain bullock exhibited by Lord De Guest was declared by the metropolitan butchers to have realized all the possible excellences of breeding, feeding, and condition.", "올해 런던의 살진 가축 품평회는 십이월 이십일에 열렸다. 그리고 내가 늘 들어 온 바로는, 드 게스트 경이 내놓은 어떤 수소가 수도의 푸주한들에게서 혈통과 사육과 상태에 있어 있을 수 있는 온갖 훌륭함을 다 이루어 냈다는 판정을 받았다고 한다."], ["No doubt the butchers of the next half-century will have learned much better, and the Guestwick beast, could it be embalmed and then produced, would excite only ridicule at the agricultural ignorance of the present age; but Lord De Guest took the praise that was offered to him, and found himself in a seventh heaven of delight.", "다음 반세기의 푸주한들이 훨씬 더 나은 것을 배워 알게 되리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없으니, 그 게스트윅의 짐승을 방부 처리해 두었다가 그때 내놓는다면 지금 시대의 농사 무지에 대한 비웃음이나 살 것이다. 그러나 드 게스트 경은 제게 바쳐진 찬사를 그대로 받아들이며 일곱째 하늘에 오른 듯한 기쁨에 잠겨 있었다."], ["He was never so happy as when surrounded by butchers, graziers, and salesmen who were able to appreciate the work of his life, and who regarded him as a model nobleman.", "그는 제 한평생의 일을 알아볼 줄 알고 저를 귀족의 본보기로 여겨 주는 푸주한과 목축업자와 가축 중개상들에게 둘러싸여 있을 때만큼 행복한 적이 없었다."], ["\"Look at that fellow,\"", "“저 녀석을 좀 보게.”"], ["he said to Eames, pointing to the prize bullock.", "그는 우승한 수소를 가리키며 임스에게 말했다."], ["Eames had joined his patron at the show after his office hours, looking on upon the living beef by gaslight.", "임스는 관청 일이 끝난 뒤 품평회장에서 제 후원자와 합류하여 가스등 불빛 아래 살아 있는 쇠고기를 구경하고 있던 참이었다."], ["\"Isn't he like his sire?", "“제 아비를 꼭 닮지 않았나?"], ["He was got by Lambkin, you know.\"", "램킨이 씨를 붙인 놈일세.”"]], [["\"Lambkin,\"", "“램킨 말씀입니까.”"], ["said Johnny, who had not as yet been able to learn much about the Guestwick stock.", "조니가 말했다. 그는 아직 게스트윅의 가축에 대해 그리 많이 알게 되지 못한 터였다."]], [["\"Yes, Lambkin.", "“그래, 램킨."], ["The bull that we had the trouble with.", "우리가 그 소동을 벌였던 그 황소 말일세."], ["He has just got his sire's back and fore-quarters.", "이 녀석은 제 아비의 등판과 앞다리를 그대로 물려받았어."], ["Don't you see?\"", "안 보이나?”"]], [["\"I daresay,\"", "“그렇겠습니다.”"], ["said Johnny, who looked very hard, but could not see.", "조니는 그렇게 말하며 뚫어져라 들여다보았으나 도무지 보이지 않았다."]], [["\"It's very odd,\"", "“참 이상한 일이야.”"], ["exclaimed the earl, \"but do you know, that bull has been as quiet since that day,--as quiet as--as anything.", "백작이 외쳤다. “그런데 말일세, 저 황소가 그날 이후로는 어찌나 얌전한지,—무엇에 견주어도 얌전하다니까."], ["I think it must have been my pocket-handkerchief.\"", "아무래도 내 손수건 탓이었던 게지.”"]], [["\"I daresay it was,\"", "“그러셨을 겁니다.”"], ["said Johnny;--\"or perhaps the flies.\"", "조니가 말했다. “—아니면 파리 때문이었는지도 모르지요.”"]], [["\"Flies!\"", "“파리라니!”"], ["said the earl, angrily.", "백작이 성난 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"Do you suppose he isn't used to flies?", "“저 녀석이 파리에 익숙지 않을 것 같은가?"], ["Come away.", "자, 가세."], ["I ordered dinner at seven, and it's past six now.", "만찬을 일곱 시로 시켜 두었는데 지금 여섯 시가 지났네."], ["My brother-in-law, Colonel Dale, is up in town, and he dines with us.\"", "내 매부 데일 대령이 서울에 올라와 있어서 우리와 함께 만찬을 드네.”"], ["So he took Johnny's arm, and led him off through the show, calling his attention as he went to several beasts which were inferior to his own.", "그러고서 그는 조니의 팔을 붙들고 품평회장을 가로질러 그를 데리고 나갔는데, 가는 길에 제 것만 못한 짐승 여러 마리를 가리키며 눈여겨보게 했다."]], [["And then they walked down through Portman Square and Grosvenor Square, and across Piccadilly to Jermyn Street.", "그러고서 그들은 포트먼 스퀘어와 그로브너 스퀘어를 지나 피커딜리를 건너 저민 스트리트까지 걸어 내려갔다."], ["John Eames acknowledged to himself that it was odd that he should have an earl leaning on his arm as he passed along through the streets. At home, in his own life, his daily companions were Cradell and Amelia Roper, Mrs.", "존 임스는 이렇게 거리를 지나가는 동안 백작 한 분이 제 팔에 기대고 있다는 것이 참 묘한 일이라고 스스로 인정했다."], ["Lupex and Mrs. Roper.", "집에서, 제 일상의 삶에서 날마다 어울리는 이들은 크래들과 어밀리아 로퍼, 루펙스 부인과 로퍼 부인이었다."], ["The difference was very great, and yet he found it quite as easy to talk to the earl as to Mrs. Lupex.", "그 차이는 대단히 컸으나, 그럼에도 그는 백작과 이야기하는 것이 루펙스 부인과 이야기하는 것만큼이나 수월하다는 것을 알았다."]], [["\"You know the Dales down at Allington, of course,\"", "“자네야 물론 앨링턴의 데일 집안 사람들을 알고 있겠지.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes, I know them.\"", "“예, 잘 알고 있습니다.”"]], [["\"But, perhaps, you never met the colonel.\"", "“하지만 대령은 만나 본 적이 없을 테지.”"]], [["\"I don't think I ever did.\"", "“뵌 적이 없는 듯합니다.”"]], [["\"He's a queer sort of fellow;--very well in his way, but he never does anything.", "“좀 별난 사람이야.—제 나름으로야 나무랄 데 없지만 도무지 아무것도 하지 않는다네."], ["He and my sister live at Torquay, and as far as I can find out, they neither of them have any occupation of any sort.", "그와 내 누이는 토키에 사는데, 내가 알아낸 바로는 둘 다 무슨 일거리라고 할 만한 것이 하나도 없어."], ["He's come up to town now because we both had to meet our family lawyers and sign some papers, but he looks on the journey as a great hardship.", "지금 서울에 올라온 것은 우리 둘이 집안 변호사들을 만나 서류에 서명을 해야 했기 때문인데, 그 사람은 그 여행을 아주 큰 고역으로 여긴다네."], ["As for me, I'm a year older than he is, but I wouldn't mind going up and down from Guestwick every day.\"", "나로 말하자면 그보다 한 살 위지만, 게스트윅에서 날마다 오르내린다 해도 아무렇지 않을 걸세.”"]], [["\"It's looking after the bull that does it,\"", "“황소를 돌보시는 덕분이지요.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"By George!", "“이런!"], ["you're right, Master Johnny.", "자네 말이 옳네, 조니 도련님."], ["My sister and Crofts may tell me what they like, but when a man's out in the open air for eight or nine hours every day, it doesn't much matter where he goes to sleep after that.", "내 누이와 크로프츠 선생이야 저희 하고 싶은 대로 나를 타이르라지. 하지만 사람이 날마다 여덟아홉 시간을 바깥바람 속에서 지내고 나면, 그 뒤에야 어디 가서 잠을 자든 그리 대수로울 것이 없는 법이야."], ["This is Pawkins's,--capital good house, but not so good as it used to be while old Pawkins was alive.", "여기가 포킨스일세.—참 훌륭한 집이지. 다만 늙은 포킨스가 살아 있을 적만큼은 못하다네."], ["Show Mr. Eames up into a bedroom to wash his hands.\"", "임스 씨를 침실로 모셔서 손을 씻으시게 해 드리게.”"]], [["Colonel Dale was much like his brother in face, but was taller, even thinner, and apparently older.", "데일 대령은 얼굴이 제 형과 많이 닮았으나 키가 더 크고 더 여위었으며, 보기에 나이도 더 들어 보였다."], ["When Eames went into the sitting-room, the colonel was there alone, and had to take upon himself the trouble of introducing himself.", "임스가 거실로 들어갔을 때 대령은 혼자 거기 있었고, 스스로 제 소개를 해야 하는 수고를 떠맡아야 했다."], ["He did not get up from his arm-chair, but nodded gently at the young man.", "그는 안락의자에서 일어서지는 않은 채 젊은이를 향해 가볍게 고개만 끄덕였다."], ["\"Mr. Eames, I believe?", "“임스 씨라고 했던가?"], ["I knew your father at Guestwick, a great many years ago;\"", "여러 해 전에 게스트윅에서 자네 부친을 알았네.”"], ["then he turned his face back towards the fire and sighed.", "그러고 나서 그는 얼굴을 다시 난롯불 쪽으로 돌리고 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["\"It's got very cold this afternoon,\"", "“오늘 오후에는 몹시 추워졌습니다.”"], ["said Johnny, trying to make conversation.", "조니가 말을 붙여 보려고 그렇게 말했다."]], [["\"It's always cold in London,\"", "“런던은 늘 춥지.”"], ["said the colonel.", "대령이 말했다."]], [["\"If you had to be here in August you wouldn't say so.\"", "“팔월에 여기 계셔 보셔야 한다면 그런 말씀은 안 하실 겁니다.”"]], [["\"God forbid,\"", "“천만의 말씀.”"], ["said the colonel, and he sighed again, with his eyes fixed upon the fire.", "대령은 그렇게 말하고는 난롯불에 눈을 붙박은 채 다시 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["Eames had heard of the very gallant way in which Orlando Dale had persisted in running away with Lord De Guest's sister, in opposition to very terrible obstacles, and as he now looked at the intrepid lover, he thought that there must have been a great change since those days.", "임스는 올랜도 데일이 지극히 무서운 방해를 무릅쓰고 드 게스트 경의 누이와 기어이 달아나 버렸다는 그 씩씩하기 그지없는 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다. 그래서 지금 그 겁 없는 연인을 바라보면서, 그 시절 이후로 대단한 변화가 있었음이 틀림없다고 생각했다."], ["After that nothing more was said till the earl came down.", "그 뒤로는 백작이 내려올 때까지 더 오간 말이 없었다."]], [["Pawkins's house was thoroughly old-fashioned in all things, and the Pawkins of that day himself stood behind the earl's elbow when the dinner began, and himself removed the cover from the soup tureen.", "포킨스의 집은 무슨 일에서나 철저히 구식이었다. 그리하여 만찬이 시작될 때 당대의 포킨스가 몸소 백작의 팔꿈치 뒤에 서서 손수 수프 그릇의 뚜껑을 벗겼다."], ["Lord De Guest did not require much personal attention, but he would have felt annoyed if this hadn't been done.", "드 게스트 경은 제 한 몸에 바쳐지는 시중을 그리 많이 바라는 사람이 아니었으나, 이것이 없었다면 서운해했을 것이다."], ["As it was he had a civil word to say to Pawkins about the fat cattle, thereby showing that he did not mistake Pawkins for one of the waiters.", "실제로 그는 살진 소들에 관해 포킨스에게 정중한 말을 한마디 건넸고, 그로써 자기가 포킨스를 급사 가운데 하나로 잘못 알고 있지 않다는 것을 드러내 보였다."], ["Pawkins then took his lordship's orders about the wine and retired.", "그러고 나서 포킨스는 술에 관한 백작님의 분부를 받고 물러갔다."]], [["\"He keeps up the old house pretty well,\"", "“옛 집을 제법 잘 지켜 가고 있소.”"], ["said the earl to his brother-in-law.", "백작이 제 매부에게 말했다."], ["\"It isn't like what it was thirty years ago, but then everything of that sort has got worse and worse.\"", "“삼십 년 전 같지야 않소만, 하기야 그런 것은 죄다 갈수록 더 나빠지기만 하니까.”"]], [["\"I suppose it has,\"", "“그런 모양이오.”"], ["said the colonel.", "대령이 말했다."]], [["\"I remember when old Pawkins had as good a glass of port as I've got at home,--or nearly.", "“늙은 포킨스가 우리 집에 있는 것만 한 포트와인을 내놓던 때가 기억나오.—적어도 그에 가깝기는 했지."], ["They can't get it now, you know.\"", "요새는 그런 것을 구할 수가 없다오.”"]], [["\"I never drink port,\"", "“나는 포트와인은 마시지 않소.”"], ["said the colonel.", "대령이 말했다."], ["\"I seldom take anything after dinner, except a little negus.\"", "“만찬 뒤에는 니거스를 조금 드는 것 말고는 무엇이든 좀처럼 입에 대지 않소.”"]], [["His brother-in-law said nothing, but made a most eloquent grimace as he turned his face towards his soup-plate.", "그의 처남은 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 얼굴을 제 수프 접시 쪽으로 돌리면서 더없이 뜻이 분명한 낯을 지었다."], ["Eames saw it, and could hardly refrain from laughing.", "임스는 그것을 보고 웃음을 참기가 어려웠다."], ["When, at half-past nine o'clock, the colonel retired from the room, the earl, as the door was closed, threw up his hands, and uttered the one word \"negus!\"", "아홉 시 반이 되어 대령이 방에서 물러가자, 문이 닫히는 것과 함께 백작은 두 손을 번쩍 들어 올리며 한마디만 내뱉었다. “니거스!”"], ["Then Eames took heart of grace and had his laughter out.", "그러자 임스도 용기를 내어 실컷 웃어 젖혔다."]], [["The dinner was very dull, and before the colonel went to bed Johnny regretted that he had been induced to dine at Pawkins's.", "만찬은 몹시 따분했고, 대령이 잠자리에 들기도 전에 조니는 포킨스에서 만찬을 들자는 말에 넘어간 것을 후회하고 있었다."], ["It might be a very fine thing to be asked to dinner with an earl, and John Eames had perhaps received at his office some little accession of dignity from the circumstance, of which he had been not unpleasantly aware; but, as he sat at the table, on which there were four or five apples and a plate of dried nuts, looking at the earl, as he endeavoured to keep his eyes open, and at the colonel, to whom it seemed absolutely a matter of indifference whether his companions were asleep or awake, he confessed to himself that the price he was paying was almost too dear.", "백작과 함께 만찬에 초대를 받는다는 것은 참으로 굉장한 일일 수도 있었고, 존 임스도 그 덕분에 제 관청에서 위신이 얼마쯤 올라간 것을 그리 싫지 않게 느끼고 있었을 것이다. 그러나 사과 네댓 알과 말린 견과 한 접시가 놓인 식탁에 앉아, 눈을 뜨고 있으려 애쓰는 백작과, 제 동무들이 자든 깨어 있든 도무지 아랑곳하지 않는 듯한 대령을 번갈아 바라보노라니, 그는 자기가 치르고 있는 값이 너무 비싸다는 것을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Mrs. Roper's tea-table was not pleasant to him, but even that would have been preferable to the black dinginess of Pawkins's mahogany, with the company of two tired old men, with whom he seemed to have no mutual subject of conversation.", "로퍼 부인의 차 탁자도 그에게 즐거운 자리는 아니었으나, 서로 나눌 이야깃거리라고는 하나도 없어 보이는 지친 노인 두 사람을 곁에 두고 마주한 포킨스네 마호가니 식탁의 시커먼 칙칙함보다는 그편이 차라리 나았을 것이다."], ["Once or twice he tried a word with the colonel, for the colonel sat with his eyes open looking at the fire.", "한두 번쯤 그는 대령에게 말을 붙여 보았다. 대령이 눈을 뜬 채 앉아 난롯불을 바라보고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But he was answered with monosyllables, and it was evident to him that the colonel did not wish to talk.", "그러나 돌아온 것은 외마디 대답뿐이었고, 대령이 이야기를 나눌 마음이 없다는 것은 그에게 분명해 보였다."], ["To sit still, with his hands closed over each other on his lap, was work enough for Colonel Dale during his after-dinner hours.", "두 손을 무릎 위에 포개어 쥐고 가만히 앉아 있는 것, 그것이 만찬 뒤의 시간 동안 데일 대령에게는 넉넉한 일거리였다."]], [["But the earl knew what was going on.", "그러나 백작은 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알고 있었다."], ["During that terrible conflict between him and his slumber, in which the drowsy god fairly vanquished him for some twenty minutes, his conscience was always accusing him of treating his guests badly.", "그와 그의 졸음 사이에 벌어진 저 무서운 싸움, 졸음의 신이 이십 분 남짓 그를 아주 눌러 이겨 버린 그 싸움이 이어지는 동안에도, 그의 양심은 줄곧 손님들을 소홀히 대접하고 있다고 그를 나무라고 있었다."], ["He was very angry with himself, and tried to arouse himself and talk.", "그는 스스로에게 몹시 화가 나서 정신을 차리고 이야기를 해 보려고 애썼다."], ["But his brother-in-law would not help him in his efforts; and even Eames was not bright in rendering him assistance.", "그러나 그의 매부는 그 애씀을 조금도 거들어 주지 않았고, 임스마저도 그를 돕는 데 그리 재치가 있지 못했다."], ["Then for twenty minutes he slept soundly, and at the end of that he woke himself with one of his own snorts.", "그러고 나서 이십 분 동안 그는 곤히 잠들었고, 그 끝에 스스로 코 고는 소리를 한 번 내고는 제 소리에 깨어났다."], ["\"By George!\"", "“이런!”"], ["he said, jumping up and standing on the rug, \"we'll have some coffee;\"", "그가 벌떡 일어나 난롯가 깔개에 서면서 말했다. “커피를 좀 들지.”"], ["and after that he did not sleep any more.", "그 뒤로 그는 더 잠들지 않았다."]], [["\"Dale,\"", "“데일.”"], ["said he, \"won't you take some more wine?\"", "그가 말했다. “술을 좀 더 들지 않겠소?”"]], [["\"Nothing more,\"", "“더는 됐소.”"], ["said the colonel, still looking at the fire, and shaking his head very slowly.", "대령이 여전히 난롯불을 바라보며 아주 천천히 고개를 저으면서 말했다."]], [["\"Come, Johnny, fill your glass.\"", "“자, 조니, 잔을 채우게.”"], ["He had already got into the way of calling his young friend Johnny, having found that Mrs. Eames generally spoke of her son by that name.", "그는 임스 부인이 제 아들을 대개 그 이름으로 부른다는 것을 알고 난 뒤로, 이미 제 젊은 벗을 조니라고 부르는 버릇이 들어 있었다."]], [["\"I have been filling my glass all the time,\"", "“여태 내내 채우고 있었습니다.”"], ["said Eames, taking the decanter again in his hand as he spoke.", "임스가 그렇게 말하면서 유리 술병을 다시 손에 집어 들었다."]], [["\"I'm glad you've found something to amuse you, for it has seemed to me that you and Dale haven't had much to say to each other.", "“자네가 재미 붙일 것을 찾았다니 다행일세. 내가 보기에는 자네와 데일이 서로 나눌 말이 별로 없었던 것 같아서 말이야."], ["I've been listening all the time.\"", "나는 내내 듣고 있었네.”"]], [["\"You've been asleep,\"", "“당신은 자고 있었소.”"], ["said the colonel.", "대령이 말했다."]], [["\"Then there's been some excuse for my holding my tongue,\"", "“그렇다면 내가 입을 다물고 있은 데에도 얼마쯤 핑계가 있었던 셈이오.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"By-the-by, Dale, what do you think of that fellow Crosbie?\"", "“그건 그렇고 데일, 그 크로즈비란 자를 어떻게 생각하오?”"]], [["Eames' ears were instantly on the alert, and the spirit of dullness vanished from him.", "임스의 귀가 대번에 곤두섰고, 따분함의 기운은 그에게서 사라져 버렸다."]], [["\"Think of him?\"", "“그자를 어떻게 생각하느냐고?”"], ["said the colonel.", "대령이 말했다."]], [["\"He ought to have every bone in his skin broken,\"", "“가죽 속의 뼈란 뼈는 죄다 부러뜨려 놓아야 마땅한 자요.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"So he ought,\"", "“마땅하고말고요.”"], ["said Eames, getting up from his chair in his eagerness, and speaking in a tone somewhat louder than was perhaps becoming in the presence of his seniors.", "임스는 흥분한 나머지 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며, 어른들 앞에서는 어쩌면 어울리지 않을 만큼 다소 큰 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"So he ought, my lord.", "“마땅하고말고요, 백작님."], ["He is the most abominable rascal that ever I met in my life.", "그자는 제 평생에 만나 본 가장 가증스러운 악당입니다."], ["I wish I was Lily Dale's brother.\"", "제가 릴리 데일의 오라비였으면 좋겠습니다.”"], ["Then he sat down again, remembering that he was speaking in the presence of Lily's uncle, and of the father of Bernard Dale, who might be supposed to occupy the place of Lily's brother.", "그러고 나서 그는 자기가 릴리의 삼촌 앞에서, 또 릴리의 오라비 자리를 대신한다고 볼 만한 버나드 데일의 아버지 앞에서 말하고 있다는 것을 떠올리고 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["The colonel turned his head round, and looked at the young man with surprise.", "대령이 고개를 돌려 놀란 눈으로 젊은이를 바라보았다. “용서하십시오, 어르신.”"], ["\"I beg your pardon, sir,\"", "임스가 말했다."], ["said Eames, \"but I have known Mrs. Dale and your nieces all my life.\"", "“하오나 저는 데일 부인과 어르신의 조카딸들을 평생 알고 지내 온 사이입니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, have you?\"", "“아, 그런가?”"], ["said the colonel.", "대령이 말했다."], ["\"Nevertheless it is, perhaps, as well not to make too free with a young lady's name.", "“그렇더라도 젊은 아가씨의 이름을 너무 함부로 입에 올리지 않는 편이 나을 것이네."], ["Not that I blame you in the least, Mr. Eames.\"", "자네를 조금이라도 나무라는 것은 아니네만, 임스 씨.”"]], [["\"I should think not,\"", "“나무랄 일이 아니고말고요.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"I honour him for his feeling.", "“나는 이 사람의 그 마음을 갸륵하게 여기오."], ["Johnny, my boy, if ever I am unfortunate enough to meet that man, I shall tell him my mind, and I believe you will do the same.\"", "조니, 이 사람아, 내가 언제고 운 사납게 그자를 만나게 된다면 나는 그자에게 내 생각을 말해 줄 것이고, 자네도 그러리라고 믿네.”"], ["On hearing this John Eames winked at the earl, and made a motion with his head towards the colonel, whose back was turned to him.", "이 말을 듣고 존 임스는 백작을 향해 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보이고는, 저에게 등을 돌리고 있는 대령 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다."], ["And then the earl winked back at Eames.", "그러자 백작도 임스에게 눈을 찡긋해 보였다."]], [["\"De Guest,\"", "“드 게스트.” 대령이 말했다."], ["said the colonel, \"I think I'll go upstairs; I always have a little arrowroot in my own room.\"", "“나는 위층으로 올라가야겠소. 나는 늘 내 방에서 애로루트 죽을 조금 든다오.”"]], [["\"I'll ring the bell for a candle,\"", "“초를 가져오라고 종을 울리겠소.”"], ["said the host.", "주인이 말했다."], ["Then the colonel went, and as the door was closed behind him, the earl raised his two hands and uttered that single word, \"negus!\"", "그러고서 대령이 가고 그의 뒤로 문이 닫히자, 백작은 두 손을 들어 올리고 그 한마디를 내뱉었다. “니거스!”"], ["Whereupon Johnny burst out laughing, and coming round to the fire, sat himself down in the arm-chair which the colonel had left.", "그러자 조니는 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 난롯가로 돌아와 대령이 비우고 간 안락의자에 자리를 잡고 앉았다."]], [["\"I've no doubt it's all right,\"", "“나쁠 것이야 없겠지.”"], ["said the earl; \"but I shouldn't like to drink negus myself, nor yet to have arrowroot up in my bedroom.\"", "백작이 말했다. “하지만 나 같으면 니거스를 마시고 싶지도 않고, 침실에 애로루트 죽을 들여놓고 싶지도 않네.”"]], [["\"I don't suppose there's any harm in it.\"", "“해로울 것이야 없겠지요.”"]], [["\"Oh dear, no; I wonder what Pawkins says about him.", "“아무렴, 없고말고. 포킨스가 저 사람을 두고 무어라고 할지 궁금하군."], ["But I suppose they have them of all sorts in an hotel.\"", "하기야 호텔에는 별의별 손님이 다 들 테지.”"]], [["\"The waiter didn't seem to think much of it when he brought it.\"", "“급사도 그것을 가져올 때 별로 대수롭게 여기지 않는 눈치던데요.”"]], [["\"No, no.", "“그럼, 그럼."], ["If he'd asked for senna and salts, the waiter wouldn't have showed any surprise.", "센나와 하제 소금을 달라고 했더라도 급사는 조금도 놀란 기색을 보이지 않았을 걸세."], ["By-the-by, you touched him up about that poor girl.\"", "그건 그렇고, 자네가 그 가엾은 아가씨 일로 저 사람을 좀 건드렸더군.”"]], [["\"Did I, my lord?", "“제가 그랬습니까, 백작님?"], ["I didn't mean it.\"", "그럴 뜻은 아니었습니다.”"]], [["\"You see he's Bernard Dale's father, and the question is, whether Bernard shouldn't punish the fellow for what he has done.", "“보다시피 저 사람은 버나드 데일의 아버지일세. 그러니 버나드가 그자가 저지른 일에 대해 그자를 벌해야 하지 않겠느냐 하는 것이 문제지."], ["Somebody ought to do it.", "누군가는 해야 할 일이야."], ["It isn't right that he should escape.", "그자가 무사히 빠져나가는 것은 옳지 않아."], ["Somebody ought to let Mr. Crosbie know what a scoundrel he has made himself.\"", "누군가는 크로즈비 씨에게 그자가 제 손으로 저를 얼마나 몹쓸 놈으로 만들어 놓았는지 알려 주어야 하네.”"]], [["\"I'd do it to-morrow, only I'm afraid--\"", "“저라면 내일이라도 하겠습니다만, 다만 걱정되는 것은—”"]], [["\"No, no, no,\"", "“아니, 아니, 아닐세.”"], ["said the earl; \"you are not the right person at all.", "백작이 말했다. “자네는 도무지 그 일에 맞는 사람이 아니야."], ["What have you got to do with it?", "자네가 그 일과 무슨 상관인가?"], ["You've merely known them as family friends, but that's not enough.\"", "자네는 그저 집안끼리 아는 벗으로 그들을 알고 지냈을 뿐인데, 그것으로는 모자라네.”"]], [["\"No, I suppose not,\"", "“예, 그렇겠지요.”"], ["said Eames, sadly.", "임스가 서글프게 말했다."]], [["\"Perhaps it's best as it is,\"", "“아마 이대로가 가장 나을지도 모르지.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"I don't know that any good would be got by knocking him over the head.", "“그자의 머리통을 후려친다고 무슨 좋은 일이 생길지 나도 모르겠네."], ["And if we are to be Christians, I suppose we ought to be Christians.\"", "그리고 우리가 그리스도인이어야 하는 것이라면, 아무래도 그리스도인다워야 하는 법이겠지.”"]], [["\"What sort of a Christian has he been?\"", "“그자는 어떤 그리스도인 노릇을 했습니까?”"]], [["\"That's true enough; and if I was Bernard, I should be very apt to forget my Bible lessons about meekness.\"", "“그건 참말 옳은 말일세. 그리고 내가 버나드라면 온유함에 관한 성경 가르침 따위는 잊어버리기 딱 알맞겠지.”"]], [["\"Do you know, my lord, I should think it the most Christian thing in the world to pitch into him; I should, indeed.", "“제 생각을 말씀드리자면요, 백작님, 저는 그자를 두들겨 패 주는 것이야말로 이 세상에서 가장 그리스도인다운 일이라고 여깁니다. 참말로 그렇습니다."], ["There are some things for which a man ought to be beaten black and blue.\"", "사람이 시퍼렇게 멍이 들도록 두들겨 맞아야 마땅한 일이라는 것이 세상에는 있습니다.”"]], [["\"So that he shouldn't do them again?\"", "“다시는 그런 짓을 하지 못하도록 말인가?”"]], [["\"Exactly.", "“바로 그렇습니다."], ["You might say it isn't Christian to hang a man.\"", "사람을 목매다는 것이 그리스도인답지 않다고 말할 수도 있겠지요.”"]], [["\"I'd always hang a murderer.", "“나는 살인자라면 언제라도 목매달겠네."], ["It wasn't right to hang men for stealing sheep.\"", "양을 훔쳤다고 사람을 목매다는 것이야 옳지 않았지만 말일세.”"]], [["\"Much better hang such a fellow as Crosbie,\"", "“크로즈비 같은 자를 목매다는 편이 훨씬 낫지요.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, I believe so.", "“글쎄, 나도 그렇게 믿네."], ["If any fellow wanted now to curry favour with the young lady, what an opportunity he'd have.\"", "지금 누구든 그 아가씨의 환심을 사고 싶은 사내가 있다면 얼마나 좋은 기회이겠나.”"]], [["Johnny remained silent for a moment or two before he answered.", "조니는 대답하기 전에 한두 순간 잠자코 있었다. “저는 그렇게 자신할 수가 없습니다.”"], ["\"I'm not so sure of that,\"", "그는 서글프게 말했다."], ["he said, mournfully, as though grieving at the thought that there was no chance of currying favour with Lily by thrashing her late lover.", "릴리의 옛 연인을 두들겨 준다고 해서 릴리의 환심을 살 가망은 없다는 생각에 마음이 아픈 듯한 투였다."]], [["\"I don't pretend to know much about girls,\"", "“나야 아가씨들에 대해 잘 안다고 내세울 처지는 못 되네만.”"], ["said Lord De Guest; \"but I should think it would be so.", "드 게스트 경이 말했다. “그래도 그러리라고 생각하네."], ["I should fancy that nothing would please her so much as hearing that he had caught it, and that all the world knew that he'd caught it.\"", "그자가 혼쭐이 났다는 소식, 그리고 그자가 혼쭐이 났다는 것을 온 세상이 알고 있다는 소식만큼 그 아가씨를 기쁘게 할 것은 없으리라고 나는 짐작하네.”"], ["The earl had declared that he didn't know much about girls, and in so saying, he was no doubt right.", "백작은 자기가 아가씨들에 대해 잘 알지 못한다고 밝혔거니와, 그렇게 말한 점에서는 의심할 것 없이 옳았다."]], [["\"If I thought so,\"", "“저도 그렇게 생각한다면.”"], ["said Eames, \"I'd find him out to-morrow.\"", "임스가 말했다. “당장 내일이라도 그자를 찾아내겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Why so?", "“어째서?"], ["what difference does it make to you?\"", "그것이 자네에게 무슨 상관인가?”"], ["Then there was another pause, during which Johnny looked very sheepish.", "그러고 나서 또 한 번 말이 끊겼는데, 그동안 조니는 몹시 겸연쩍은 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["\"You don't mean to say that you're in love with Miss Lily Dale?\"", "“설마 자네가 릴리 데일 양을 사랑하고 있다는 말은 아니겠지?”"]], [["\"I don't know much about being in love with her,\"", "“제가 그분을 사랑하고 있는 것인지 어떤지는 잘 모르겠습니다.”"], ["said Johnny, turning very red as he spoke.", "조니는 그렇게 말하면서 얼굴이 새빨개졌다."], ["And then he made up his mind, in a wild sort of way, to tell all the truth to his friend.", "그러고 나서 그는 어딘가 무모한 심정으로 제 벗에게 온 진실을 다 털어놓기로 마음을 정했다."], ["Pawkins's port wine may, perhaps, have had something to do with the resolution.", "포킨스의 포트와인이 그 결심에 얼마쯤 몫을 했는지도 모른다."], ["\"But I'd go through fire and water for her, my lord.", "“하오나 저는 그분을 위해서라면 불속이든 물속이든 뛰어들 것입니다, 백작님."], ["I knew her years before he had ever seen her, and have loved her a great deal better than he will ever love any one.", "저는 그자가 그분을 보기도 여러 해 전부터 그분을 알고 지냈고, 그자가 앞으로 누구를 사랑하게 되든 그보다 훨씬 더 깊이 그분을 사랑해 왔습니다."], ["When I heard that she had accepted him, I had half a mind to cut my own throat,--or else his.\"", "그분이 그자를 받아들였다는 말을 들었을 때는 제 목을 그어 버릴까 하는 생각이 절반쯤 들었습니다.—아니면 그자의 목을 긋든지요.”"]], [["\"Highty tighty,\"", "“어허, 그것참.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"It's very ridiculous, I know,\"", "“참으로 우스운 일이라는 것은 저도 압니다.”"], ["said Johnny, \"and of course she would never have accepted me.\"", "조니가 말했다. “그리고 물론 그분이 저를 받아들이는 일은 결코 없었을 테고요.”"]], [["\"I don't see that at all.\"", "“나는 도무지 그렇게 보지 않네.”"]], [["\"I haven't a shilling in the world.\"", "“저는 이 세상에 일 실링도 가진 것이 없습니다.”"]], [["\"Girls don't care much for that.\"", "“아가씨들은 그런 것을 그리 마음에 두지 않네.”"]], [["\"And then a clerk in the Income-tax Office!", "“게다가 소득세 사무국의 서기 아닙니까!"], ["It's such a poor thing.\"", "참으로 보잘것없는 것이지요.”"]], [["\"The other fellow was only a clerk in another office.\"", "“그 다른 사내도 다른 사무국의 서기에 지나지 않았네.”"]], [["The earl living down at Guestwick did not understand that the Income-tax Office in the city, and the General Committee Office at Whitehall, were as far apart as Dives and Lazarus, and separated by as impassable a gulf.", "게스트윅에 내려가 사는 백작은 시티에 있는 소득세 사무국과 화이트홀에 있는 일반위원회 사무국이 부자와 나사로만큼이나 멀리 떨어져 있고, 도무지 건널 수 없는 구렁으로 갈라져 있다는 것을 알지 못했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes,\"", "“예, 그렇습니다.”"], ["said Johnny; \"but his office is another kind of thing, and then he was a swell himself.\"", "조니가 말했다. “하지만 그자의 사무국은 다른 종류의 것이고, 게다가 그자는 그 자신이 멋쟁이였습니다.”"]], [["\"By George, I don't see it,\"", "“이런, 나는 도무지 모르겠네.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"I don't wonder a bit at her accepting a fellow like that.", "“저는 그분이 그런 자를 받아들인 것이 조금도 이상하지 않습니다."], ["I hated him the first moment I saw him; but that's no reason she should hate him.", "저야 그자를 처음 본 그 순간부터 미워했습니다만, 그렇다고 그분까지 그자를 미워해야 할 까닭은 없지요."], ["He had that sort of manner, you know.", "그자에게는 그런 티가 있었으니까요."], ["He was a swell, and girls like that kind of thing.", "그자는 멋쟁이였고, 아가씨들은 그런 것을 좋아합니다."], ["I never felt angry with her, but I could have eaten him.\"", "저는 그분에게 성이 난 적은 한 번도 없습니다. 하지만 그자라면 잡아먹어 버릴 수도 있었습니다.”"], ["As he spoke he looked as though he would have made some such attempt had Crosbie been present.", "그렇게 말하는 그의 낯빛은, 크로즈비가 그 자리에 있었더라면 정말로 그런 짓을 해 보였을 것만 같았다."]], [["\"Did you ever ask her to have you?\"", "“자네를 받아 달라고 그 아가씨에게 청해 본 적은 있는가?”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"No; how could I ask her, when I hadn't bread to give her?\"", "“없습니다. 그분에게 드릴 빵 한 조각이 없는데 어떻게 청할 수 있었겠습니까?”"]], [["\"And you never told her--that you were in love with her, I mean, and all that kind of thing.\"", "“그러면 그 아가씨에게 말한 적도 없겠군.—그러니까 자네가 그 아가씨를 사랑하고 있다는 것이며, 그런 따위 이야기 말일세.”"]], [["\"She knows it now,\"", "“이제는 그분도 알고 계십니다.”"], ["said Johnny; \"I went to say good-by to her the other day,--when I thought she was going to be married.", "조니가 말했다. “요전에 작별 인사를 하러 갔었습니다.—그분이 곧 혼인하시는 줄로 알았을 때였지요."], ["I could not help telling her then.\"", "그때는 말하지 않고는 견딜 수가 없었습니다.”"]], [["\"But it seems to me, my dear fellow, that you ought to be very much obliged to Crosbie;--that is to say, if you've a mind to--\"", "“하지만 내가 보기에는, 이 사람아, 자네는 크로즈비에게 대단히 고마워해야 할 것 같은데.—그러니까 자네에게 그럴 마음이 있다면 말일세—”"]], [["\"I know what you mean, my lord.", "“무슨 말씀이신지 압니다, 백작님."], ["I am not a bit obliged to him.", "저는 그자에게 조금도 고맙지 않습니다."], ["It's my belief that all this will about kill her.", "이번 일이 그분을 거의 죽여 놓으리라는 것이 제 생각입니다."], ["As to myself, if I thought she'd ever have me--\"", "저로 말씀드리자면, 그분이 언젠가 저를 받아 주시리라고 여길 수만 있다면—”"]], [["Then he was again silent, and the earl could see that the tears were in his eyes.", "그러고서 그는 다시 입을 다물었고, 백작은 그의 눈에 눈물이 고인 것을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["\"I think I begin to understand it,\"", "“이제 얼마쯤 알 것 같군.”"], ["said the earl, \"and I'll give you a bit of advice.", "백작이 말했다. “그러니 내가 자네에게 한마디 일러 주겠네."], ["You come down and spend your Christmas with me at Guestwick.\"", "자네, 내려와서 게스트윅에서 나와 함께 크리스마스를 보내게.”"]], [["\"Oh, my lord!\"", "“아, 백작님!”"]], [["\"Never mind my-lording me, but do as I tell you.", "“백작님 백작님 하는 것은 그만두고 내가 시키는 대로 하게."], ["Lady Julia sent you a message, though I forgot all about it till now.", "레이디 줄리아가 자네에게 전하는 말을 부탁했는데, 나는 지금까지 그것을 까맣게 잊고 있었네."], ["She wants to thank you herself for what you did in the field.\"", "누이가 자네가 들판에서 한 일에 대해 제 입으로 직접 고맙다는 말을 하고 싶어 하네.”"]], [["\"That's all nonsense, my lord.\"", "“그것은 다 당치 않은 말씀입니다, 백작님.”"]], [["\"Very well; you can tell her so.", "“좋네. 그러면 그 말을 누이에게 직접 하게."], ["You may take my word for this, too,--my sister hates Crosbie quite as much as you do.", "그리고 이 말도 내 말을 그대로 믿어도 좋네.—내 누이는 자네 못지않게 크로즈비를 미워하고 있어."], ["I think she'd 'pitch into him,' as you call it, herself, if she knew how.", "방법만 안다면 자네 말마따나 제 손으로 그자를 ‘두들겨 패 주고’ 싶어 할 걸세."], ["You come down to Guestwick for the Christmas, and then go over to Allington and tell them all plainly what you mean.\"", "크리스마스 때 게스트윅으로 내려오게. 그러고 나서 앨링턴으로 건너가 자네 뜻이 무엇인지 그 사람들에게 죄다 툭 터놓고 말하게.”"]], [["\"I couldn't say a word to her now.\"", "“지금은 그분에게 한마디도 할 수 없습니다.”"]], [["\"Say it to the squire, then.", "“그러면 지주에게 말하게."], ["Go to him, and tell him what you mean,--holding your head up like a man.", "그를 찾아가 자네 뜻이 무엇인지 말하게.—사내답게 고개를 꼿꼿이 들고서 말일세."], ["Don't talk to me about swells.", "내 앞에서 멋쟁이 이야기는 꺼내지 말게."], ["The man who means honestly is the best swell I know.", "정직하게 마음먹은 사내가 내가 아는 가장 훌륭한 멋쟁이야."], ["He's the only swell I recognize.", "내가 멋쟁이로 쳐 주는 사람은 그런 사람뿐일세."], ["Go to old Dale, and say you come from me,--from Guestwick Manor.", "늙은 데일에게 가서 나한테서 왔다고 말하게.—게스트윅 저택에서 왔다고 말이야."], ["Tell him that if he'll put a little stick under the pot to make it boil, I'll put a bigger one.", "그리고 그 사람에게 이렇게 전하게. 냄비가 끓도록 그 밑에 작은 장작을 하나 넣어 준다면 나는 그보다 더 큰 것을 넣겠노라고."], ["He'll understand what that means.\"", "그러면 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알아들을 걸세.”"]], [["\"Oh, no, my lord.\"", "“아, 그건 안 됩니다, 백작님.”"]], [["\"But I say, oh, yes;\"", "“하지만 나는 된다고 하겠네.”"], ["and the earl, who was now standing on the rug before the fire, dug his hands deep down into his trousers'", "그러면서 이제 난롯가 깔개 위에 서 있던 백작은 두 손을 바지 주머니 깊숙이 찔러 넣었다."], ["pockets. \"I'm very fond of that girl, and would do much for her.", "“나는 그 아가씨를 매우 아끼고 있고, 그 아가씨를 위해서라면 많은 일을 하겠네."], ["You ask Lady Julia if I didn't say so to her before I ever knew of your casting a sheep's-eye that way.", "자네가 그쪽으로 연모의 눈길을 던지고 있다는 것을 내가 알기도 전에 내가 그런 말을 했는지 안 했는지 레이디 줄리아에게 물어보게."], ["And I've a sneaking kindness for you too, Master Johnny.", "그리고 나는 자네에게도 남몰래 정이 가네, 조니 도련님."], ["Lord bless you, I knew your father as well as I ever knew any man; and to tell the truth, I believe I helped to ruin him.", "이런, 나는 자네 부친을 내가 여태 알았던 그 누구 못지않게 잘 알았네. 그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 그를 망하게 하는 데 내가 거들었다고 나는 믿고 있어."], ["He held land of me, you know, and there can't be any doubt that he did ruin himself.", "알다시피 그는 내 땅을 빌려 부쳤고, 그가 제 손으로 제 신세를 망친 것에는 의심할 여지가 없네."], ["He knew no more about a beast when he'd done, than--than--than that waiter.", "그는 그 일을 다 끝냈을 때에도 짐승에 대해서는 저—저—저 급사보다 더 아는 것이 없었어."], ["If he'd gone on to this day he wouldn't have been any wiser.\"", "오늘날까지 계속했다 한들 조금도 더 나아지지 않았을 걸세.”"]], [["Johnny sat silent, with his eyes full of tears.", "조니는 눈에 눈물이 가득한 채 말없이 앉아 있었다."], ["What was he to say to his friend?", "제 벗에게 무슨 말을 한단 말인가?"]], [["\"You come down with me,\"", "“자네, 나와 함께 내려가세.”"], ["continued the earl, \"and you'll find we'll make it all straight.", "백작이 말을 이었다. “그러면 우리가 이 일을 죄다 바로잡게 되리라는 것을 알게 될 걸세."], ["I daresay you're right about not speaking to the girl just at present.", "지금 당장은 그 아가씨에게 말하지 않는 편이 좋다는 자네 말은 옳다고 보네."], ["But tell everything to the uncle, and then to the mother.", "하지만 삼촌에게 죄다 말하고, 그다음에는 어머니에게 말하게."], ["And, above all things, never think that you're not good enough yourself.", "그리고 무엇보다도, 자네 자신이 모자란 사람이라고는 결코 생각하지 말게."], ["A man should never think that.", "사람이 그런 생각을 해서는 안 되는 법이야."], ["My belief is that in life people will take you very much at your own reckoning.", "내 생각으로는, 세상 사람들은 자네가 스스로를 셈하는 그대로 자네를 받아들이네."], ["If you are made of dirt, like that fellow Crosbie, you'll be found out at last, no doubt.", "자네가 저 크로즈비란 자처럼 진흙으로 빚어진 사람이라면 끝내는 들통이 나고 말겠지, 그야 의심할 것이 없네."], ["But then I don't think you are made of dirt.\"", "하지만 나는 자네가 진흙으로 빚어진 사람이라고는 생각하지 않아.”"]], [["\"I hope not.\"", "“그러지 않기를 바랍니다.”"]], [["\"And so do I.", "“나도 그러네."], ["You can come down, I suppose, with me the day after to-morrow?\"", "자네도 모레 나와 함께 내려갈 수 있겠지?”"]], [["\"I'm afraid not.", "“아무래도 어려울 듯합니다."], ["I have had all my leave.\"", "저는 휴가를 다 써 버렸습니다.”"]], [["\"Shall I write to old Buffle, and ask it as a favour?\"", "“내가 늙은 버플에게 편지를 써서 부탁으로 청해 볼까?”"]], [["\"No,\"", "“아닙니다.”"], ["said Johnny; \"I shouldn't like that.", "조니가 말했다. “그것은 제 마음에 내키지 않습니다."], ["But I'll see to-morrow, and then I'll let you know.", "하지만 내일 알아보고 나서 백작님께 알려 드리겠습니다."], ["I can go down by the mail train on Saturday, at any rate.\"", "아무리 못해도 토요일에 우편 열차로 내려갈 수는 있습니다.”"]], [["\"That won't be comfortable.", "“그것은 편치 못할 걸세."], ["See and come with me if you can.", "될 수 있으면 어떻게든 나와 함께 오도록 하게."], ["Now, good-night, my dear fellow, and remember this,--when I say a thing I mean it.", "자, 잘 자게, 이 사람아. 그리고 이것을 기억해 두게.—나는 무슨 말을 하면 그 말대로 하는 사람일세."], ["I think I may boast that I never yet went back from my word.\"", "나는 여태껏 한 번도 내 말을 뒤집은 적이 없다고 자랑해도 좋으리라고 생각하네.”"]], [["The earl as he spoke gave his left hand to his guest, and looking somewhat grandly up over the young man's head, he tapped his own breast thrice with his right hand.", "백작은 그렇게 말하면서 제 손님에게 왼손을 내밀고는, 젊은이의 머리 너머로 다소 거창하게 눈길을 치켜올린 채 오른손으로 제 가슴을 세 번 두드렸다."], ["As he went through the little scene, John Eames felt that he was every inch an earl.", "그 짤막한 장면을 치러 내는 그를 보며 존 임스는 이분이야말로 어느 모로 보나 백작이라고 느꼈다."]], [["\"I don't know what to say to you, my lord.\"", "“무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 모르겠습니다, 백작님.”"]], [["\"Say nothing,--not a word more to me.", "“아무 말도 하지 말게.—나에게는 한마디도 더 하지 말게."], ["But say to yourself that faint heart never won fair lady.", "다만 자네 자신에게 이렇게 말하게. 겁 많은 마음으로는 어여쁜 아가씨를 얻지 못한다고."], ["Good-night, my dear boy, good-night.", "잘 자게, 이 사람아, 잘 자게."], ["I dine out to-morrow, but you can call and let me know at about six.\"", "나는 내일 밖에서 만찬을 드네만, 자네가 여섯 시쯤에 들러서 알려 주면 되네.”"]], [["Eames then left the room without another word, and walked out into the cold air of Jermyn Street.", "그러고서 임스는 더는 한마디도 하지 않고 방을 나와 저민 스트리트의 차가운 공기 속으로 걸어 나갔다."], ["The moon was clear and bright, and the pavement in the shining light seemed to be as clean as a lady's hand.", "달은 맑고 밝았으며, 그 환한 빛 속의 포도는 귀부인의 손처럼 깨끗해 보였다."], ["All the world was altered to him since he had entered Pawkins's Hotel.", "그가 포킨스 호텔에 들어선 뒤로 온 세상이 그에게는 달라져 있었다."], ["Was it then possible that Lily Dale might even yet become his wife?", "그렇다면 릴리 데일이 아직도 제 아내가 되는 일이 있을 수 있다는 말인가?"], ["Could it be true that he, even now, was in a position to go boldly to the Squire of Allington, and tell him what were his views with reference to Lily?", "그가, 지금 당장이라도, 앨링턴의 지주를 대담하게 찾아가 릴리에 관한 제 뜻이 무엇인지 말할 만한 처지에 있다는 것이 참말일 수 있는가?"], ["And how far would he be justified in taking the earl at his word?", "그리고 백작의 말을 그대로 곧이듣는 것이 어디까지 옳은 일일까?"], ["Some incredible amount of wealth would be required before he could marry Lily Dale.", "릴리 데일과 혼인하기까지는 도무지 믿기 어려울 만큼 큰 재산이 있어야 할 것이었다."], ["Two or three hundred pounds a year at the very least!", "아무리 적게 잡아도 연 이삼백 파운드는 있어야 한다!"], ["The earl could not mean him to understand that any such sum as that would be made up with such an object!", "백작이 그만한 액수를 그런 목적으로 마련해 주겠다는 뜻으로 말했을 리는 없다!"], ["Nevertheless he resolved as he walked home to Burton Crescent that he would go down to Guestwick, and that he would obey the earl's behest.", "그럼에도 그는 버턴 크레슨트로 걸어 돌아가면서 게스트윅으로 내려가겠다고, 그리고 백작이 이르신 대로 따르겠다고 마음을 정했다."], ["As regarded Lily herself he felt that nothing could be said to her for many a long day as yet.", "릴리 자신에게는 앞으로도 오랜 날 동안 아무 말도 할 수 없으리라고 그는 느꼈다."]], [["\"Oh, John, how late you are!\"", "“어머, 존, 왜 이렇게 늦었어요!”"], ["said Amelia, slipping out from the back parlour as he let himself in with his latch-key.", "그가 대문 열쇠로 문을 열고 들어서는데 어밀리아가 뒤쪽 응접실에서 슬며시 나오며 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, I am;--very late,\"", "“예, 늦었지요.—아주 늦었습니다.”"], ["said John, taking his candle, and passing her by on the stairs without another word.", "존은 그렇게 말하고는 제 초를 집어 들고, 더는 한마디도 없이 계단에서 그녀를 지나쳐 갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIII. \"THE TIME WILL COME.\"", "# 제33장. 「그때는 오리라」"]], [["\"Did you hear that young Eames is staying at Guestwick Manor?\"", "“젊은 임스가 게스트윅 저택에 묵고 있다는 이야기를 들었소?”"]], [["As these were the first words which the squire spoke to Mrs. Dale as they walked together up to the Great House, after church, on Christmas Day, it was clear enough that the tidings of Johnny's visit, when told to him, had made some impression.", "성탄절에 예배가 끝나고 함께 큰 집으로 걸어 올라가면서 지주가 데일 부인에게 건넨 첫마디가 이것이었으니, 조니가 다녀간다는 소식이 그에게 전해졌을 때 얼마쯤 인상을 남겼다는 것이 넉넉히 분명했다."]], [["\"At Guestwick Manor!\"", "“게스트윅 저택에요!”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"Dear me!", "“어머나!"], ["Do you hear that, Bell?", "벨, 너 그 이야기 들었니?"], ["There's promotion for Master Johnny!\"", "조니 도련님이 출세를 하셨구나!”"]], [["\"Don't you remember, mamma,\"", "“엄마, 기억 안 나세요?”"], ["said Bell, \"that he helped his lordship in his trouble with the bull?\"", "벨이 말했다. “그이가 백작님께서 황소 때문에 곤경에 처하셨을 때 도와 드렸잖아요.”"]], [["Lily, who remembered accurately all the passages of her last interview with John Eames, said nothing, but felt, in some sort, sore at the idea that he should be so near her at such a time.", "존 임스와 마지막으로 만났던 자리의 모든 대목을 또렷이 기억하고 있던 릴리는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 이런 때에 그가 제 가까이에 와 있다는 생각에 어딘가 마음이 쓰라렸다."], ["In some unconscious way she had liked him for coming to her and saying all that he did say.", "저도 모르는 사이에 그녀는 그가 저를 찾아와 그 모든 말을 해 준 것 때문에 그를 좋아하고 있었다."], ["She valued him more highly after that scene than she did before.", "그 일이 있은 뒤로 그녀는 전보다 그를 더 높이 치게 되었다."], ["But now, she would feel herself injured and hurt if he ever made his way into her presence under circumstances as they existed.", "그러나 이제 형편이 이러한데 그가 굳이 제 앞에 나타나기라도 한다면, 그녀는 스스로 짓밟히고 상처 입었다고 느낄 터였다."]], [["\"I should not have thought that Lord De Guest was the man to show so much gratitude for so slight a favour,\"", "“드 게스트 경이 그만한 작은 호의를 두고 그토록 큰 고마움을 내보일 사람이라고는 생각지 못했소.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"However, I'm going to dine there to-morrow.\"", "“아무튼 나는 내일 거기서 만찬을 들기로 했소.”"]], [["\"To meet young Eames?\"", "“젊은 임스를 만나러요?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Yes,--especially to meet young Eames.", "“그렇소.—특히 젊은 임스를 만나러 말이오."], ["At least, I've been very specially asked to come, and I've been told that he is to be there.\"", "적어도 나는 꼭 와 달라는 청을 아주 각별히 받았고, 그가 거기 올 거라는 이야기도 들었소.”"]], [["\"And is Bernard going?\"", "“버나드도 가니?”"]], [["\"Indeed I'm not,\"", "“저는 안 갑니다.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."], ["\"I shall come over and dine with you.\"", "“저는 이리로 건너와서 숙모님과 만찬을 들겠어요.”"]], [["A half-formed idea flitted across Lily's mind, teaching her to imagine for a moment that she might possibly be concerned in this arrangement.", "어렴풋한 생각 하나가 릴리의 마음을 스쳐 지나가, 잠깐 동안이나마 저도 어쩌면 이 일과 무슨 상관이 있을지 모른다는 상상을 하게 했다."], ["But the thought vanished as quickly as it came, merely leaving some soreness behind it.", "그러나 그 생각은 온 것만큼이나 빠르게 사라지면서 다만 쓰라림만 얼마쯤 남겨 놓았다."], ["There are certain maladies which make the whole body sore.", "어떤 병은 온몸을 아프게 한다."], ["The patient, let him be touched on any point,--let him even be nearly touched,--will roar with agony as though his whole body had been bruised.", "그런 병을 앓는 이는 어느 자리를 건드리기만 해도—아니, 건드릴 듯 다가가기만 해도—온몸에 멍이 든 것처럼 고통에 겨워 부르짖는다."], ["So it is also with maladies of the mind.", "마음의 병도 그와 같다."], ["Sorrows such as that of poor Lily's leave the heart sore at every point, and compel the sufferer to be ever in fear of new wounds.", "가엾은 릴리가 겪는 것과 같은 슬픔은 심장을 어느 자리에서나 쓰라리게 만들어, 앓는 이로 하여금 새 상처를 늘 두려워하게 만든다."], ["Lily bore her cross bravely and well; but not the less did it weigh heavily upon her at every turn because she had the strength to walk as though she did not bear it.", "릴리는 제 십자가를 씩씩하게 잘 지고 있었다. 그러나 그것을 지고 있지 않은 사람처럼 걸어갈 힘이 있다고 해서 그 짐이 걸음마다 그녀를 무겁게 누르지 않는 것은 아니었다."], ["Nothing happened to her, or in her presence, that did not in some way connect itself with her misery.", "그녀에게 일어나는 일이든 그녀가 있는 자리에서 일어나는 일이든, 어떤 식으로든 제 비참함과 이어지지 않는 것이 없었다."], ["Her uncle was going over to meet John Eames at Lord De Guest's.", "삼촌이 드 게스트 경의 집으로 존 임스를 만나러 간다."], ["Of course the men there would talk about her, and all such talking was an injury to her.", "그러면 물론 거기 모인 남자들은 그녀 이야기를 할 터였고, 그런 이야기는 모두 그녀에게 상처였다."]], [["The afternoon of that day did not pass away brightly.", "그날 오후는 밝게 흘러가지 않았다."], ["As long as the servants were in the room the dinner went on much as other dinners.", "하인들이 방에 있는 동안에는 만찬도 여느 만찬과 크게 다르지 않게 지나갔다."], ["At such times a certain amount of hypocrisy must always be practised in closely domestic circles.", "그런 자리에서는 아주 가까운 집안끼리라도 얼마쯤의 위선을 늘 부려야 하는 법이다."], ["At mixed dinner-parties people can talk before Richard and William the same words that they would use if Richard and William were not there.", "여러 사람이 섞인 만찬 자리에서라면 리처드와 윌리엄이 앞에 있어도 그들이 없을 때 쓸 말과 똑같은 말을 할 수 있다."], ["People so mixed do not talk together their inward home thoughts.", "그렇게 섞인 사람들은 서로 제 집안의 속마음을 이야기하지 않기 때문이다."], ["But when close friends are together, a little conscious reticence is practised till the door is tiled.", "그러나 가까운 사이끼리 모였을 때에는 문이 닫히고 빗장이 걸릴 때까지 얼마쯤 의식하여 말을 아끼게 된다."], ["At such a meeting as this that conscious reticence was of service, and created an effect which was salutary.", "이날 같은 모임에서는 그렇게 말을 아끼는 것이 도움이 되어 이로운 효과를 냈다."], ["When the door was tiled, and when the servants were gone, how could they be merry together?", "그러나 문이 닫히고 빗장이 걸리고 하인들이 물러가고 나서, 그들이 어떻게 함께 즐거울 수 있었겠는가?"], ["By what mirth should the beards be made to wag on that Christmas Day?", "그 성탄절에 무슨 흥겨움으로 수염을 흔들며 웃을 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["\"My father has been up in town,\"", "“아버지가 런던에 올라와 계셨습니다.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."], ["\"He was with Lord De Guest at Pawkins's.\"", "“드 게스트 경과 함께 포킨스에 계셨어요.”"]], [["\"Why didn't you go and see him?\"", "“그런데 왜 가서 뵙지 않았니?”"], ["asked Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"Well, I don't know.", "“글쎄요, 저도 모르겠습니다."], ["He did not seem to wish it.", "아버지도 그걸 바라시는 것 같지 않았고요."], ["I shall go down to Torquay in February.", "이월에는 토키로 내려갈 생각입니다."], ["I must be up in London, you know, in a fortnight, for good.\"", "아시다시피 저는 두 주 뒤에는 런던으로 올라가야 합니다, 이제 아주요.”"], ["Then they were all silent again for a few minutes.", "그러고 나서 그들은 몇 분 동안 다시 모두 말이 없었다."], ["If Bernard could have owned the truth, he would have acknowledged that he had not gone up to London, because he did not yet know how to treat Crosbie when he should meet him.", "버나드가 사실대로 털어놓을 수 있었다면, 그는 자기가 런던에 올라가지 않은 것은 크로즈비를 만나게 될 때 그를 어떻게 대해야 할지 아직 알지 못했기 때문이라고 인정했을 것이다."], ["His thoughts on this matter threw some sort of shadow across poor Lily's mind, making her feel that her wound was again opened.", "이 문제에 관한 그의 생각은 가엾은 릴리의 마음에도 어떤 그림자를 드리워, 제 상처가 또 벌어지는 것처럼 느끼게 했다."]], [["\"I want him to give up his profession altogether,\"", "“나는 저 아이가 제 직업을 아주 버렸으면 하오.”"], ["said the squire, speaking firmly and slowly.", "지주가 단호하고 느릿하게 말했다."], ["\"It would be better, I think, for both of us that he should do so.\"", "“그리하는 편이 우리 둘 다에게 나으리라고 나는 생각하오.”"]], [["\"Would it be wise at his time of life,\" said Mrs.", "“그 나이에, 그것도 그렇게 잘해 오고 있는데 그게 현명한 일일까요?”"], ["Dale, \"and when he has been doing so well?\"", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I think it would be wise.", "“나는 현명한 일이라고 생각하오."], ["If he were my son it would be thought better that he should live here upon the property, among the people who are to become his tenants, than remain up in London, or perhaps be sent to India.", "저 아이가 내 아들이라면, 런던에 그대로 머물러 있거나 어쩌면 인도로 보내지는 것보다, 이 영지에서 장차 제 소작인이 될 사람들 틈에 살아가는 편이 낫다고들 여길 것이오."], ["He has one profession as the heir of this place, and that, I think, should be enough.\"", "저 아이는 이 자리의 상속인이라는 직업을 하나 가지고 있고, 나는 그것으로 넉넉하다고 생각하오.”"]], [["\"I should have but an idle life of it down here,\"", "“여기 내려와 살면 저는 한가하기만 한 삶을 살게 될 겁니다.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"That would be your own fault.", "“그건 네 탓일 게다."], ["But if you did as I would have you, your life would not be idle.\"", "하지만 네가 내 뜻대로만 해 준다면 네 삶이 한가하지는 않을 게다.”"], ["In this he was alluding to Bernard's proposed marriage, but as to that nothing further could be said in Bell's presence.", "이 말로 그는 버나드의 혼담을 넌지시 가리킨 것이었으나, 그 일에 대해서는 벨이 있는 자리에서 더 말할 수가 없었다."], ["Bell understood it all, and sat quite silent, with demure countenance;--perhaps even with something of sternness in her face.", "벨은 그것을 죄다 알아들었고, 얌전한 낯으로 아무 말 없이 앉아 있었다.—어쩌면 그 얼굴에는 얼마쯤 매서운 데까지 있었는지도 모른다."]], [["\"But the fact is,\" said Mrs.", "“하지만 실은,”"], ["Dale, speaking in a low tone, and having well considered what she was about to say, \"that Bernard is not exactly the same as your son.\"", "데일 부인이 낮은 목소리로, 무슨 말을 할지 잘 헤아려 두고서 말했다. “버나드는 시아주버니의 아들과 꼭 같지는 않잖아요.”"]], [["\"Why not?\" said the squire.", "“어째서 같지 않다는 거요?”"], ["\"I have even offered to settle the property on him if he will leave the service.\"", "지주가 말했다. “나는 저 아이가 군을 떠나기만 한다면 이 영지를 저 아이 앞으로 물려주겠다는 말까지 했소.”"]], [["\"You do not owe him so much as you would owe your son; and, therefore, he does not owe you as much as he would owe his father.\"", "“시아주버니가 아드님에게 지실 만큼의 빚을 버나드에게 지고 계시지는 않잖아요. 그러니 버나드도 제 아버지에게 질 만큼의 빚을 시아주버니에게 지고 있지는 않은 거예요.”"]], [["\"If you mean that I cannot constrain him, I know that well enough.", "“내가 저 아이를 억지로 어쩔 수 없다는 뜻이라면, 나도 그쯤은 잘 알고 있소."], ["As regards money, I have offered to do for him quite as much as any father would feel called upon to do for an only son.\"", "돈에 관해서라면 나는 어느 아버지든 외아들에게 마땅히 해 주어야 한다고 여길 만한 것을 죄다 해 주겠다고 했소.”"]], [["\"I hope you don't think me ungrateful,\"", "“제가 은혜를 모른다고 여기지는 말아 주십시오.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"No, I do not; but I think you unmindful.", "“그렇게 여기지 않는다. 다만 나는 네가 마음을 쓰지 않는다고 여긴다."], ["I have nothing more to say about it, however;--not about that.", "하지만 그 일에 대해서는 더 할 말이 없다.—그 일에 대해서는 말이다."], ["If you should marry--\"", "네가 혼인을 하게 된다면—”"], ["And then he stopped himself, feeling that he could not go on in Bell's presence.", "그러고서 그는 말을 멈추었으니, 벨이 있는 자리에서는 더 나아갈 수 없다고 느낀 것이었다."]], [["\"If he should marry,\" said Mrs.", "“그 애가 혼인을 하게 된다면,”"], ["Dale, \"it may well be that his wife would like a house of her own.\"", "데일 부인이 말했다. “그 아내 되는 사람이야 제 집을 갖고 싶어 하는 것도 당연하겠지요.”"]], [["\"Wouldn't she have this house?\"", "“이 집이 저 아이 집이 아니고 무엇이오?”"], ["said the squire, angrily.", "지주가 성이 나서 말했다."], ["\"Isn't it big enough?", "“이 집이 넉넉히 크지 않소?"], ["I only want one room for myself, and I'd give up that if it were necessary.\"", "나야 나 한 사람 쓸 방 하나면 되고, 필요하다면 그것마저도 내놓겠소.”"]], [["\"That's nonsense,\"", "“그건 당치도 않은 말씀이에요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"It isn't nonsense,\"", "“당치 않은 말이 아니오.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"You'll be squire of Allington for the next twenty years,\"", "“앞으로 이십 년은 앨링턴의 지주로 계실 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"And as long as you are the squire, you'll be master of this house; at least, I hope so.", "“그리고 지주로 계시는 동안에는 이 집의 주인이실 거고요. 적어도 저는 그러기를 바랍니다."], ["I don't approve of monarchs abdicating in favour of young people.\"", "저는 임금이 젊은 사람에게 자리를 넘기고 물러앉는 걸 좋아하지 않아요.”"]], [["\"I don't think uncle Christopher would look at all well like Charles the Fifth,\"", "“크리스토퍼 삼촌이 카를 5세 같은 모습으로 계시면 조금도 어울리지 않으실 것 같은데요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"I would always keep a cell for you, my darling, if I did,\"", "“내가 그리하게 되거든 너를 위해서는 언제나 수도실 한 칸을 남겨 두마, 얘야.”"], ["said the squire, regarding her with that painful, special tenderness.", "지주가 그 아프고도 각별한 다정함을 담아 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["Lily, who was sitting next to Mrs. Dale, put her hand out secretly and got hold of her mother's, thereby indicating that she did not intend to occupy the cell offered to her by her uncle; or to look to him as the companion of her monastic seclusion.", "데일 부인 곁에 앉아 있던 릴리는 남몰래 손을 뻗어 어머니의 손을 잡았으니, 그로써 삼촌이 내주겠다는 그 수도실에 들어가 살 뜻이 없다는 것을, 또 제 수도자다운 은거의 길동무로 삼촌을 바라볼 뜻이 없다는 것을 내보인 셈이었다."], ["After that there was nothing more then said as to Bernard's prospects.", "그 뒤로 버나드의 앞날에 관해서는 더 이야기되지 않았다."]], [["\"Mrs. Hearn is dining at the vicarage, I suppose?\"", "“헌 부인은 목사관에서 만찬을 드는 모양이지?”"], ["asked the squire.", "지주가 물었다."]], [["\"Yes; she went in after church,\"", "“예, 예배가 끝나고 들어가셨어요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"I saw her go with Mrs. Boyce.\"", "“보이스 부인과 함께 가시는 걸 봤어요.”"]], [["\"She told me she never would dine with them again after dark in winter,\"", "“그분이 저한테 그러시더군요, 겨울에는 어두워진 뒤에 다시는 그 댁에서 만찬을 들지 않겠다고요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"The last time she was there, the boy let the lamp blow out as she was going home, and she lost her way.", "“지난번에 거기 가셨을 때, 돌아오시는 길에 아이가 등을 꺼뜨리는 바람에 길을 잃으셨거든요."], ["The truth was, she was angry because Mr. Boyce didn't go with her.\"", "사실인즉 보이스 씨가 바래다주지 않아서 화가 나신 거지요.”"]], [["\"She's always angry,\"", "“그 부인은 늘 화가 나 있소.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"She hardly speaks to me now.", "“요새는 나한테 말도 잘 걸지 않는다오."], ["When she paid her rent the other day to Jolliffe, she said she hoped it would do me much good; as though she thought me a brute for taking it.\"", "요전에 졸리프에게 집세를 내면서, 이 돈이 나한테 크게 좋은 일이 되기를 바란다고 하더랍니다. 마치 그것을 받는 내가 짐승이라도 되는 양 여기는 투로 말이오.”"]], [["\"So she does,\"", "“정말 그렇게 여기시는걸요.”"], ["said Bernard.", "버나드가 말했다."]], [["\"She's very old, you know,\"", "“그분은 아주 늙으셨잖아요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I'd give her the house for nothing, if I were you, uncle,\"", "“저라면 그 집을 거저 드리겠어요, 삼촌.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"No, my dear; if you were me you would not.", "“아니다, 얘야. 네가 나라면 그러지 않을 게다."], ["I should be very wrong to do so. Why should Mrs.", "내가 그리한다면 그건 아주 잘못된 일이지."], ["Hearn have her house for nothing, any more than her meat or her clothes?", "헌 부인이 어째서 고기나 옷은 거저 얻지 못하면서 집만은 거저 얻어야 한단 말이냐?"], ["It would be much more reasonable were I to give her so much money into her hand yearly; but it would be wrong in me to do so, seeing that she is not an object of charity;--and it would be wrong in her to take it.\"", "차라리 해마다 그만한 돈을 손에 쥐여 드리는 편이 훨씬 이치에 닿겠지. 그러나 그분은 자선을 받을 처지가 아니니 내가 그리하는 것도 잘못이고,—그분이 그것을 받는 것도 잘못일 게다.”"]], [["\"And she wouldn't take it,\"", "“그리고 그분은 받지도 않으실 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I don't think she would.", "“받지 않으시겠지."], ["But if she did, I'm sure she would grumble because it wasn't double the amount.", "하지만 만일 받으신다면, 그 액수가 갑절이 아니라고 투덜대실 것이 틀림없소."], ["And if Mr. Boyce had gone home with her, she would have grumbled because he walked too fast.\"", "그리고 보이스 씨가 집까지 바래다주었더라면, 걸음이 너무 빠르다고 투덜대셨을 거요.”"]], [["\"She is very old,\"", "“그분은 아주 늙으셨어요.”"], ["said Bell, again.", "벨이 다시 말했다."]], [["\"But, nevertheless, she ought to know better than to speak disparagingly of me to my servants.", "“그렇더라도 내 하인들에게 나를 헐뜯는 말을 하지 않을 만큼은 분별이 있어야지."], ["She should have more respect for herself.\"", "자기 자신을 좀 더 소중히 여겨야 하고.”"], ["And the squire showed by the tone of his voice that he thought very much about it.", "그리고 지주는 그 목소리의 어조로 자기가 그 일을 몹시 마음에 두고 있다는 것을 드러내 보였다."]], [["It was very long and very dull that Christmas evening, making Bernard feel strongly that he would be very foolish to give up his profession, and tie himself down to a life at Allington.", "그 성탄절 저녁은 몹시 길고 몹시 따분했으니, 버나드로 하여금 제 직업을 버리고 앨링턴에서의 삶에 저를 붙들어 매는 것이야말로 어리석기 짝이 없는 짓이라는 생각을 굳히게 만들 지경이었다."], ["Women are more accustomed than men to long, dull, unemployed hours; and, therefore, Mrs.", "여자들은 남자들보다 길고 따분하고 하는 일 없는 시간에 익숙한 법이다."], ["Dale and her daughters bore the tedium courageously.", "그러므로 데일 부인과 그 딸들은 그 지루함을 씩씩하게 견뎌 냈다."], ["While he yawned, stretched himself, and went in and out of the room, they sat demurely, listening as the squire laid down the law on small matters, and contradicting him occasionally when the spirit of either of them prompted her specially to do so.", "그가 하품을 하고 기지개를 켜고 방을 들락거리는 동안, 그들은 얌전히 앉아 지주가 자잘한 일들을 두고 법을 세우는 것에 귀를 기울였고, 이따금 어느 한 사람의 심사가 유난히 그러도록 부추길 때에만 그의 말을 거슬렀다."], ["\"Of course you know much better than I do,\"", "“물론 그거야 나보다 자네들이 훨씬 잘 알겠지.”"], ["he would say. \"Not at all,\"", "그가 그렇게 말하면, “천만에요.”"], ["Mrs. Dale would answer.", "데일 부인이 대답했다."], ["\"I don't pretend to know anything about it.", "“저는 그 일에 대해 무엇 하나 안다고 내세울 생각이 없어요."], ["But--\"", "다만—”"], ["So the evening wore itself away; and when the squire was left alone at half-past nine, he did not feel that the day had passed badly with him.", "이렇게 저녁은 저 혼자 닳아 갔다. 그리고 아홉 시 반에 홀로 남겨졌을 때, 지주는 그날이 자기에게 나쁘게 지나갔다고는 느끼지 않았다."], ["That was his style of life, and he expected no more from it than he got.", "그것이 그의 삶의 방식이었고, 그는 삶에서 얻은 것보다 더한 것을 바라지도 않았다."], ["He did not look to find things very pleasant, and, if not happy, he was, at any rate, contented.", "그는 무슨 일이 아주 즐겁기를 기대하지 않았고, 행복하지는 않을지언정 적어도 넉넉히 만족하고 있었다."]], [["\"Only think of Johnny Eames being at Guestwick Manor!\"", "“조니 임스가 게스트윅 저택에 있다니, 생각 좀 해 봐!”"], ["said Bell, as they were going home.", "집으로 돌아가면서 벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I don't see why he shouldn't be there,\"", "“거기 있으면 왜 안 되는데?” 릴리가 말했다."], ["said Lily. \"I would rather it should be he than I, because Lady Julia is so grumpy.\"", "“레이디 줄리아가 워낙 심술궂으니까, 나 같으면 내가 가느니 그이가 가는 편이 낫겠어.”"]], [["\"But asking your uncle Christopher especially to meet him!\"", "“그런데 크리스토퍼 삼촌더러 각별히 그이를 만나 달라고 청했다지 않니!”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"There must be some reason for it.\"", "“거기에는 무슨 까닭이 있는 게다.”"], ["Then Lily felt the soreness come upon her again, and spoke no further upon the subject.", "그러자 릴리는 다시 그 쓰라림이 밀려오는 것을 느꼈고, 그 이야기에 대해 더는 입을 열지 않았다."]], [["We all know that there was a special reason, and that Lily's soreness was not false in its mysterious forebodings.", "우리는 모두 알고 있듯이 거기에는 각별한 까닭이 있었고, 릴리의 쓰라림이 품은 그 알 수 없는 예감도 틀린 것이 아니었다."], ["Eames, on the evening after his dinner at Pawkins's, had seen the earl, and explained to him that he could not leave town till the Saturday evening; but that he could remain over the Tuesday.", "임스는 포킨스에서 만찬을 든 다음 날 저녁에 백작을 만나, 토요일 저녁까지는 시내를 떠날 수 없지만 화요일까지는 머물 수 있다고 설명해 두었다."], ["He must be at his office by twelve on Wednesday, and could manage to do that by an early train from Guestwick.", "그는 수요일 열두 시까지 제 관청에 나가 있어야 했는데, 게스트윅에서 이른 기차를 타면 그렇게 할 수 있었다."]], [["\"Very well, Johnny,\"", "“아주 좋네, 조니.”"], ["said the earl, talking to his young friend with the bedroom candle in his hand, as he was going up to dress.", "침실용 촛대를 손에 들고 옷을 갈아입으러 올라가려던 백작이 제 젊은 벗에게 말했다."], ["\"Then I'll tell you what; I've been thinking of it.", "“그러면 이렇게 하지. 나도 그 일을 생각해 두었네."], ["I'll ask Dale to come over to dinner on Tuesday; and if he'll come, I'll explain the whole matter to him myself.", "화요일에 만찬을 들러 오라고 데일에게 청하겠네. 그리고 그가 오겠다고 하면 내가 몸소 그에게 이 일을 죄다 설명하겠네."], ["He's a man of business, and he'll understand.", "그는 일에 밝은 사람이니 알아들을 걸세."], ["If he won't come, why then you must go over to Allington, and find him, if you can, on the Tuesday morning; or I'll go to him myself, which will be better.", "오지 않겠다고 하면, 그때는 자네가 화요일 아침에 앨링턴으로 건너가서 되도록 그를 찾아보게. 아니면 내가 몸소 찾아가지, 그편이 낫겠군."], ["You mustn't keep me now, as I am ever so much too late.\"", "지금은 나를 붙들지 말게. 어지간히 늦었으니.”"]], [["Eames did not attempt to keep him, but went away feeling that the whole matter was being arranged for him in a very wonderful way.", "임스는 그를 붙들려 하지 않고 물러났는데, 이 모든 일이 저를 위해 참으로 놀라운 방식으로 처리되고 있다는 느낌이 들었다."], ["And when he got to Allington he found that the squire had accepted the earl's invitation.", "그리고 그가 앨링턴에 이르렀을 때, 그는 지주가 백작의 초대를 받아들였다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Then he declared to himself that there was no longer any possibility of retractation for him.", "그러자 그는 이제 더는 물러설 길이 없다고 스스로에게 선언했다."], ["Of course he did not wish to retract.", "물론 그는 물러설 마음도 없었다."], ["The one great longing of his life was to call Lily Dale his own.", "그의 삶의 하나뿐인 큰 바람은 릴리 데일을 제 사람이라 부르는 것이었다."], ["But he felt afraid of the squire,--that the squire would despise him and snub him, and that the earl would perceive that he had made a mistake when he saw how his client was scorned and snubbed.", "그러나 그는 지주가 두려웠다.—지주가 저를 업신여기고 면박을 줄까 봐, 그리고 백작이 제 의뢰인이 그렇게 멸시받고 면박당하는 것을 보고 자기가 잘못 짚었구나 하고 깨닫게 될까 봐 두려웠다."], ["It was arranged that the earl was to take the squire into his own room for a few minutes before dinner, and Johnny felt that he would be hardly able to stand his ground in the drawing-room when the two old men should make their appearance together.", "백작이 만찬 전에 지주를 제 방으로 데려가 잠깐 이야기를 나누기로 되어 있었으니, 조니는 그 두 노인이 함께 나타날 때 응접실에서 제대로 버티고 서 있기가 어려울 것 같다고 느꼈다."]], [["He got on very well with Lady Julia, who gave herself no airs, and made herself very civil.", "그는 레이디 줄리아와도 아주 잘 지냈다. 그녀는 조금도 젠체하지 않았고 몹시 상냥하게 굴었다."], ["Her brother had told her the whole story, and she felt as anxious as he did to provide Lily with another husband in place of that horrible man Crosbie.", "오라버니가 그녀에게 이야기를 죄다 들려주었으므로, 그녀 역시 오라버니 못지않게 저 끔찍한 크로즈비라는 사내 대신 릴리에게 다른 남편을 마련해 주고 싶어 안달이었다."], ["\"She has been very fortunate in her escape,\"", "“그 아이는 벗어난 것만도 아주 운이 좋은 거예요.”"], ["she said to her brother; \"very fortunate.\"", "그녀가 오라버니에게 말했다. “참으로 운이 좋았지요.”"], ["The earl agreed with this, saying that in his opinion his own favourite Johnny would make much the nicer lover of the two.", "백작도 그 말에 동의하면서, 제가 아끼는 조니가 둘 가운데 훨씬 나은 연인이 되리라는 것이 제 생각이라고 말했다."], ["But Lady Julia had her doubts as to Lily's acquiescence.", "그러나 레이디 줄리아는 릴리가 순순히 받아들일지에 대해 미심쩍어했다."], ["\"But, Theodore, he must not speak to Miss Lilian Dale herself about it yet a while.\"", "“하지만 시어도어, 그이는 아직 한동안은 릴리언 데일 양 본인에게 그 이야기를 꺼내서는 안 돼요.”"]], [["\"No,\"", "“그래.”"], ["said the earl; \"not for a month or so.\"", "백작이 말했다. “한 달쯤은 안 되지.”"]], [["\"He will have a better chance if he can remain silent for six months,\"", "“여섯 달을 잠자코 있을 수 있다면 그이에게 더 나은 기회가 올 거예요.”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Bless my soul!", "“저런!"], ["somebody else will have picked her up before that,\"", "그때까지는 다른 누가 벌써 그 아이를 채 갔을 게다.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["In answer to this Lady Julia merely shook her head.", "이에 대해 레이디 줄리아는 그저 고개를 저었을 뿐이었다."]], [["Johnny went over to his mother on Christmas Day after church, and was received by her and by his sister with great honour.", "조니는 성탄절에 예배가 끝난 뒤 어머니에게 건너갔고, 어머니와 누이에게서 큰 대접을 받았다."], ["And she gave him many injunctions as to his behaviour at the earl's table, even descending to small details about his boots and linen.", "그리고 어머니는 백작의 식탁에서 어떻게 처신해야 하는지를 여러 가지로 단단히 일러 주었는데, 구두와 속옷 같은 자잘한 데까지 내려갔다."], ["But Johnny had already begun to feel at the Manor that, after all, people are not so very different in their ways of life as they are supposed to be.", "그러나 조니는 저택에서 이미, 결국 사람들이 살아가는 방식이란 남들이 여기는 것만큼 그리 다르지 않다는 것을 느끼기 시작한 뒤였다."], ["Lady Julia's manners were certainly not quite those of Mrs.", "레이디 줄리아의 몸가짐이 로퍼 부인의 그것과 똑같지야 않았다."], ["Roper; but she made the tea very much in the way in which it was made at Burton Crescent, and Eames found that he could eat his egg, at any rate on the second morning, without any tremor in his hand, in spite of the coronet on the silver egg-cup.", "그러나 그녀도 버턴 크레슨트에서 차를 우리던 것과 거의 같은 방식으로 차를 우렸고, 임스는 은제 달걀 받침에 새겨진 귀족의 관 문장에도 아랑곳없이, 적어도 이튿날 아침에는 손을 떨지 않고 달걀을 먹을 수 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["He did feel himself to be rather out of his place in the Manor pew on the Sunday, conceiving that all the congregation was looking at him; but he got over this on Christmas Day, and sat quite comfortably in his soft corner during the sermon, almost going to sleep.", "일요일에 저택 신도석에 앉았을 때에는 신도들이 죄다 저를 쳐다보고 있다고 여겨져 제자리가 아닌 듯한 기분이 들기는 했다. 그러나 성탄절에는 그것도 넘겼고, 설교가 이어지는 동안 푹신한 구석 자리에 아주 편안히 앉아 거의 잠이 들 지경이었다."], ["And when he walked with the earl after church to the gate over which the noble peer had climbed in his agony, and inspected the hedge through which he had thrown himself, he was quite at home with his little jokes, bantering his august companion as to the mode of his somersault.", "그리고 예배가 끝난 뒤 백작과 함께, 그 귀하신 어른이 죽을 지경에 몰려 기어올랐던 그 문까지 걸어가서 제가 몸을 던져 뚫고 나왔던 산울타리를 살펴볼 때에는, 제 지체 높은 길동무를 두고 그가 어떤 식으로 공중제비를 넘었는지 놀려 대며 아주 스스럼없이 굴었다."], ["But be it always remembered that there are two modes in which a young man may be free and easy with his elder and superior,--the mode pleasant and the mode offensive.", "그러나 젊은 사람이 저보다 나이 많고 지체 높은 이에게 스스럼없이 굴 때에는 두 가지 방식이 있다는 것을 늘 기억해 두어야 한다.—기분 좋은 방식과 거슬리는 방식이다."], ["Had it been in Johnny's nature to try the latter, the earl's back would soon have been up at once, and the play would have been over.", "뒤쪽을 시험해 볼 만한 것이 조니의 천성에 있었더라면 백작은 당장에 등을 곧추세웠을 터이고 그 놀이도 끝났을 것이다."], ["But it was not in Johnny's nature to do so, and therefore it was that the earl liked him.", "그러나 그렇게 하는 것은 조니의 천성에 없었고, 그러기에 백작은 그를 좋아했던 것이다."]], [["At last came the hour of dinner on Tuesday, or at least the hour at which the squire had been asked to show himself at the Manor House.", "마침내 화요일 만찬 시각이, 적어도 지주가 저택에 얼굴을 내밀어 달라고 청받은 시각이 되었다."], ["Eames, as by agreement with his patron, did not come down so as to show himself till after the interview.", "임스는 제 후원자와 미리 약속해 둔 대로 그 면담이 끝날 때까지는 내려가 얼굴을 보이지 않았다."], ["Lady Julia, who had been present at their discussions, had agreed to receive the squire; and then a servant was to ask him to step into the earl's own room.", "두 사람의 의논 자리에 함께 있었던 레이디 줄리아가 지주를 맞기로 했고, 그런 다음 하인이 그에게 백작의 방으로 들어가시라고 청하기로 되어 있었다."], ["It was pretty to see the way in which the three conspired together, planning and plotting with an eagerness that was beautifully green and fresh.", "셋이 함께 짜고서 아름다우리만치 풋풋하고 싱그러운 열심으로 계획을 세우고 꾀를 꾸미는 모양은 보기에 사랑스러웠다."]], [["\"He can be as cross as an old stick when he likes it,\"", "“그 사람은 마음만 먹으면 늙은 막대기처럼 뻣뻣하고 심술궂게 굴 수 있다네.”"], ["said the earl, speaking of the squire; \"and we must take care not to rub him the wrong way.\"", "백작이 지주를 두고 말했다. “그러니 우리는 그 사람의 결을 거슬러 문질러 대지 않도록 조심해야 하네.”"]], [["\"I shan't know what to say to him when I come down,\"", "“저는 내려가서 그분께 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 모르겠습니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Just shake hands with him and don't say anything,\"", "“그냥 악수만 하시고 아무 말씀도 하지 마세요.”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."]], [["\"I'll give him some port wine that ought to soften his heart,\"", "“내가 그 사람 마음을 누그러뜨릴 만한 포트와인을 좀 내겠네.”"], ["said the earl, \"and then we'll see how he is in the evening.\"", "백작이 말했다. “그러고 나서 저녁에 그가 어떤지 보세.”"]], [["Eames heard the wheels of the squire's little open carriage and trembled.", "임스는 지주의 작은 무개마차 바퀴 소리를 듣고 몸을 떨었다."], ["The squire, unconscious of all schemes, soon found himself with Lady Julia, and within two minutes of his entrance was walked off to the earl's private room.", "이런 꾀에 대해서는 아무것도 모르는 지주는 이내 레이디 줄리아와 마주 앉았고, 들어선 지 이 분도 채 되지 않아 백작의 사실로 이끌려 갔다. “그러지요.”"], ["\"Certainly,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"certainly;\"", "“그러고말고요.”"], ["and followed the man-servant.", "그러고서 그는 하인을 따라갔다."], ["The earl, as he entered, was standing in the middle of the room, and his round rosy face was a picture of good-humour.", "백작은 그가 들어설 때 방 한가운데 서 있었는데, 그 둥글고 발그레한 얼굴은 그대로 좋은 기분의 그림이었다."]], [["\"I'm very glad you've come, Dale,\" said he.", "“와 주어서 참 반갑소, 데일.”"], ["\"I've something I want to say to you.\"", "그가 말했다. “긴히 할 말이 좀 있소.”"]], [["Mr. Dale, who neither in heart nor in manner was so light a man as the earl, took the proffered hand of his host, and bowed his head slightly, signifying that he was willing to listen to anything.", "마음으로 보나 몸가짐으로 보나 백작만큼 가벼운 사람이 아니었던 데일 씨는 주인이 내미는 손을 잡고 고개를 살짝 숙였으니, 무슨 이야기든 들을 뜻이 있다는 표시였다."]], [["\"I think I told you,\"", "“전에도 말한 것 같소만,” 백작이 말을 이었다."], ["continued the earl, \"that young John Eames is down here; but he goes back to-morrow, as they can't spare him at his office.", "“젊은 존 임스가 여기 내려와 있소. 다만 내일이면 돌아가야 하오."], ["He's a very good fellow,--as far as I am able to judge, an uncommonly good young man.", "관청에서 그를 놓아 줄 수가 없다니 말이오. 아주 좋은 친구요.—내가 판단할 수 있는 한으로는 여간 훌륭한 젊은이가 아니오."], ["I've taken a great fancy to him myself.\"", "나 자신이 그에게 아주 반해 버렸소.”"]], [["In answer to this Mr. Dale did not say much.", "이에 대해 데일 씨는 별말을 하지 않았다."], ["He sat down, and in some general terms expressed his good-will towards all the Eames family.", "그는 자리에 앉아 임스 집안 전체에 대한 제 호의를 두루뭉술한 말로 나타냈다."]], [["\"As you know, Dale, I'm a very bad hand at talking, and therefore I won't beat about the bush in what I've got to say at present.", "“데일, 당신도 알다시피 나는 말재주가 몹시 없는 사람이오. 그러니 지금 하려는 말을 두고 빙빙 돌리지 않겠소."], ["Of course we've all heard of that scoundrel Crosbie, and the way he has treated your niece Lilian.\"", "물론 우리 모두 그 악당 크로즈비 이야기를, 그리고 그자가 당신 조카딸 릴리언을 어떻게 대했는지를 들어 알고 있소.”"]], [["\"He is a scoundrel,--an unmixed scoundrel.", "“그자는 악당이오.—순전한 악당이오."], ["But the less we say about that the better.", "그러나 그 이야기는 적게 할수록 좋소."], ["It is ill mentioning a girl's name in such a matter as that.\"", "그런 일로 처녀의 이름을 입에 올리는 것은 좋지 못한 일이오.”"]], [["\"But, my dear Dale, I must mention it at the present moment.", "“하지만 데일, 지금 이 자리에서는 내가 그 이야기를 꺼내야만 하겠소."], ["Dear young child, I would do anything to comfort her!", "가엾은 어린것! 나는 그 아이를 위로할 수만 있다면 무슨 일이든 하겠소!"], ["And I hope that something may be done to comfort her.", "그리고 그 아이를 위로할 무언가가 이루어질 수 있기를 바라오."], ["Do you know that that young man was in love with her long before Crosbie ever saw her?\"", "크로즈비가 그 아이를 보기 훨씬 전부터 저 젊은이가 그 아이를 사랑하고 있었다는 것을 알고 있소?”"]], [["\"What;--John Eames!\"", "“무어라고요.—존 임스가!”"]], [["\"Yes, John Eames.", "“그렇소, 존 임스요."], ["And I wish heartily for his sake that he had won her regard before she had met that rascal whom you had to stay down at your house.\"", "그리고 나는 그를 위해서라도, 당신이 댁에 묵게 했던 그 무뢰한을 만나기 전에 그가 먼저 그 아이의 마음을 얻었더라면 하고 진심으로 바라오.”"]], [["\"A man cannot help these things, De Guest,\"", "“사람이 그런 일을 어찌할 수는 없는 노릇이오, 드 게스트.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"No, no, no!", "“그렇소, 그렇소, 그렇소!"], ["There are such men about the world, and it is impossible to know them at a glance.", "세상에는 그런 사내들이 있고, 한눈에 알아본다는 것은 될 일이 아니오."], ["He was my nephew's friend, and I am not going to say that my nephew was in fault.", "그자는 내 조카의 벗이었고, 나는 내 조카에게 잘못이 있다고 말할 생각도 없소."], ["But I wish,--I only say that I wish,--she had first known what are this young man's feelings towards her.\"", "그러나 나는 바라오.—그저 바랄 뿐이라고 말하는 것이오.—그 아이가 이 젊은이의 마음이 어떠한지를 먼저 알았더라면 하고 말이오.”"]], [["\"But she might not have thought of him as you do.\"", "“하지만 그 아이가 당신처럼 그를 좋게 여기지는 않았을지도 모르오.”"]], [["\"He is an uncommonly good-looking young fellow; straight made, broad in the chest, with a good, honest eye, and a young man's proper courage.", "“그는 여간 잘생긴 젊은이가 아니오. 몸매가 곧고 가슴팍이 넓으며, 착하고 정직한 눈과 젊은이다운 제대로 된 용기를 가졌소."], ["He has never been taught to give himself airs like a dancing monkey; but I think he's all the better for that.\"", "춤추는 원숭이처럼 젠체하는 법은 배운 적이 없으나, 나는 그 때문에 그가 오히려 더 낫다고 생각하오.”"]], [["\"But it's too late now, De Guest.\"", "“하지만 이제 와서는 너무 늦었소, 드 게스트.”"]], [["\"No, no; that's just where it is.", "“아니, 아니오. 바로 거기가 요점이오."], ["It mustn't be too late!", "너무 늦어서는 안 되오!"], ["That child is not to lose her whole life because a villain has played her false.", "악당 하나가 그 아이를 속였다고 해서 그 아이가 제 한평생을 잃어서야 되겠소."], ["Of course she'll suffer.", "물론 그 아이는 아파할 것이오."], ["Just at present it wouldn't do, I suppose, to talk to her about a new sweetheart.", "지금 당장이야 새 연인 이야기를 그 아이에게 꺼낸다는 것이 될 일이 아니겠지요."], ["But, Dale, the time will come; the time will come;--the time always does come.\"", "그러나 데일, 그때는 오리다. 그때는 오리다.—때는 언제나 오는 법이오.”"]], [["\"It has never come to you and me,\"", "“당신과 나에게는 온 적이 없소.”"], ["said the squire, with the slightest possible smile on his dry cheeks.", "지주가 그 메마른 뺨에 웃음이랄 것도 없는 아주 희미한 웃음을 띠며 말했다."], ["The story of their lives had been so far the same; each had loved, and each had been disappointed, and then each had remained single through life.", "두 사람의 삶의 이야기는 여기까지 같았다. 각자 사랑했고, 각자 뜻을 이루지 못했으며, 그러고서 각자 평생을 홀로 지냈다."]], [["\"Yes, it has,\"", "“아니, 왔소.”"], ["said the earl, with no slight touch of feeling and even of romance in what he said.", "백작이 제가 하는 말에 적잖은 감정을, 낭만이라고까지 할 만한 것을 실어 말했다."], ["\"We have retricked our beams in our own ways, and our lives have not been desolate.", "“우리는 저마다 제 나름의 방식으로 우리 빛살을 다시 손질했고, 우리 삶도 황량하지만은 않았소."], ["But for her,--you and her mother will look forward to see her married some day.\"", "그러나 그 아이로 말하면—당신과 그 어머니는 언젠가 그 아이가 시집가는 것을 보고 싶어 할 것 아니오.”"]], [["\"I have not thought about it.\"", "“나는 그런 생각을 해 본 적이 없소.”"]], [["\"But I want you to think about it.", "“하지만 나는 당신이 그 생각을 해 주기를 바라오."], ["I want to interest you in this fellow's favour; and in doing so, I mean to be very open with you.", "나는 이 친구에게 이롭도록 당신의 마음을 끌고 싶소. 그러자면 나도 당신에게 아주 터놓고 말해야겠소."], ["I suppose you'll give her something?\"", "당신은 그 아이에게 무언가를 떼어 줄 생각이오?”"]], [["\"I don't know, I'm sure,\"", "“나도 잘 모르겠소, 정말이지.”"], ["said the squire, almost offended at an inquiry of such a nature.", "지주가 그런 물음에 거의 언짢아하며 말했다."]], [["\"Well, then, whether you do or not, I'll give him something,\"", "“그렇다면, 당신이 주든 주지 않든 나는 그에게 무언가를 주겠소.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"I shouldn't have ventured to meddle in the matter had I not intended to put myself in such a position with reference to him as would justify me in asking the question.\"", "“내가 그에 관해 이런 물음을 던져도 될 만한 자리에 나 자신을 놓아 둘 작정이 아니었다면, 나는 이 일에 감히 끼어들지 않았을 것이오.”"], ["And the peer as he spoke drew himself up to his full height.", "그러고서 그 귀족은 말하면서 제 키를 온전히 세워 몸을 곧추 폈다."], ["\"If such a match can be made, it shall not be a bad marriage for your niece in a pecuniary point of view.", "“그런 혼인이 이루어질 수 있다면, 그것은 돈으로 따져 보더라도 당신 조카딸에게 나쁜 혼인이 되지 않을 것이오."], ["I shall have pleasure in giving to him; but I shall have more pleasure if she can share what I give.\"", "나는 그에게 주는 것이 기쁘겠소. 그러나 그 아이가 내가 주는 것을 함께 나눌 수 있다면 더욱 기쁘겠소.”"]], [["\"She ought to be very much obliged to you,\"", "“그 아이는 당신에게 크게 고마워해야 마땅하오.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"I think she would be if she knew young Eames.", "“그 아이가 젊은 임스를 알게 된다면 고마워하리라고 나는 생각하오."], ["I hope the day may come when she will be so.", "그리하게 될 날이 오기를 바라오."], ["I hope that you and I may see them happy together, and that you too may thank me for having assisted in making them so.", "당신과 내가 두 사람이 함께 행복하게 지내는 것을 보게 되기를, 그리고 그리되도록 내가 거든 것을 두고 당신도 나에게 고마워하게 되기를 바라오."], ["Shall we go in to Lady Julia now?\"", "이제 레이디 줄리아에게로 갈까요?”"], ["The earl had felt that he had not quite succeeded; that his offer had been accepted somewhat coldly, and had not much hope that further good could be done on that day, even with the help of his best port wine.", "백작은 자기가 온전히 성공하지는 못했다는 것을, 제 제안이 얼마쯤 차갑게 받아들여졌다는 것을 느끼고 있었고, 제 가장 좋은 포트와인의 힘을 빌리더라도 그날 더 좋은 일이 이루어지리라는 기대는 크게 품지 않았다."]], [["\"Half a moment,\" said the squire.", "“잠깐만 기다려 주시오.”"], ["\"There are matters as to which I never find myself able to speak quickly, and this certainly seems to be one of them.", "지주가 말했다. “나는 도무지 빨리 말할 수가 없는 일들이 있는데, 이것도 분명 그 가운데 하나인 듯하오."], ["If you will allow me I will think over what you have said, and then see you again.\"", "허락해 주신다면 당신이 한 말을 두고 곰곰 생각해 본 뒤에 다시 만나 뵙겠소.”"]], [["\"Certainly, certainly.\"", "“그러시오, 그러시오.”"]], [["\"But for your own part in the matter, for your great generosity and kind heart, I beg to offer you my warmest thanks.\"", "“그러나 이 일에서 당신이 맡아 준 몫에 대해, 당신의 그 크나큰 너그러움과 어진 마음에 대해 나는 가장 따뜻한 감사를 드리고자 하오.”"], ["Then the squire bowed low, and preceded the earl out of the room.", "그러고서 지주는 깊이 고개를 숙였고, 백작에 앞서 방을 나섰다."]], [["Lord De Guest still felt that he had not succeeded.", "드 게스트 경은 여전히 자기가 성공하지 못했다고 느꼈다."], ["We may probably say, looking at the squire's character and peculiarities, that no marked success was probable at the first opening-out of such a subject.", "지주의 성격과 별난 데를 헤아려 보면, 그런 이야기를 처음 꺼낸 자리에서 눈에 띄는 성공을 거두기란 아마도 있을 수 없는 일이었다고 우리는 말해도 좋으리라."], ["He had said of himself that he was never able to speak quickly in matters of moment; but he would more correctly have described his own character had he declared that he could not think of them quickly.", "그는 스스로 무게 있는 일에 대해서는 결코 빨리 말할 수 없다고 했으나, 그런 일에 대해서는 빨리 생각할 수가 없다고 했더라면 제 성격을 더 정확히 그려 냈을 것이다. 아무튼 백작은 실망했다."], ["As it was, the earl was disappointed; but had he been able to read the squire's mind, his disappointment would have been less strong. Mr.", "그러나 지주의 마음속을 읽을 수 있었더라면 그 실망은 덜했을 것이다."], ["Dale knew well enough that he was being treated well, and that the effort being made was intended with kindness to those belonging to him; but it was not in his nature to be demonstrative and quick at expressions of gratitude.", "데일 씨는 자기가 잘 대접받고 있다는 것을, 그리고 지금 벌어지고 있는 이 애씀이 제게 딸린 사람들에게 친절을 베풀려는 뜻에서 나온 것임을 넉넉히 알고 있었다. 그러나 감정을 드러내고 감사의 말을 재빨리 내놓는 것은 그의 천성이 아니었다."], ["So he entered the drawing-room with a cold, placid face, leading Eames, and Lady Julia also, to suppose that no good had been done.", "그리하여 그는 차갑고 담담한 얼굴로 응접실에 들어섰고, 임스와 레이디 줄리아로 하여금 아무 소득도 없었구나 하고 여기게 만들었다."]], [["\"How do you do, sir?\"", "“안녕하십니까, 어르신?”"], ["said Johnny, walking up to him in a wild sort of manner,--going through a premeditated lesson, but doing it without any presence of mind.", "조니가 어딘가 얼떨떨한 태도로, 미리 익혀 둔 대로 하면서도 조금도 침착하지 못하게 그에게 다가서며 말했다."]], [["\"How do you do, Eames?\"", "“잘 있었나, 임스?”"], ["said the squire, speaking with a very cold voice.", "지주가 아주 차가운 목소리로 말했다."], ["And then there was nothing further said till the dinner was announced.", "그리고 만찬 준비가 되었다는 알림이 있을 때까지 그 밖에는 아무 말도 오가지 않았다."]], [["\"Dale, I know you drink port,\"", "“데일, 당신이 포트와인을 마시는 줄은 알고 있소.”"], ["said the earl when Lady Julia left them.", "레이디 줄리아가 자리를 뜨자 백작이 말했다."], ["\"If you say you don't like that, I shall say you know nothing about it.\"", "“이게 마음에 들지 않는다고 하면, 나는 당신이 포트와인에 대해 아무것도 모른다고 말하겠소.”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["that's the '20,\"", "이건 20년 산이로군.”"], ["said the squire, tasting it.", "지주가 맛을 보며 말했다."]], [["\"I should rather think it is,\"", "“그야 그렇고말고.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"I was lucky enough to get it early, and it hasn't been moved for thirty years.", "“나는 운이 좋아서 그걸 일찌감치 손에 넣었고, 삼십 년 동안 자리를 옮기지 않았소."], ["I like to give it to a man who knows it, as you do, at the first glance.", "나는 당신처럼 한 모금에 대뜸 알아보는 사람에게 이걸 내놓는 것이 좋소."], ["Now there's my friend Johnny there; it's thrown away upon him.\"", "그런데 저기 있는 내 벗 조니에게는—저 친구한테는 이게 헛되이 버려지는 셈이지.”"]], [["\"No, my lord, it is not.", "“아닙니다, 백작님, 그렇지 않습니다."], ["I think it's uncommonly nice.\"", "저는 이게 여간 좋은 것이 아니라고 생각합니다.”"]], [["\"Uncommonly nice!", "“여간 좋지 않다니!"], ["So is champagne, or ginger-beer, or lollipops,--for those who like them.", "샴페인도 그렇고 생강 맥주도 그렇고 눈깔사탕도 그렇지, 그런 것을 좋아하는 사람들에게는 말일세."], ["Do you mean to tell me you can taste wine with half a pickled orange in your mouth?\"", "자네, 입안에 절인 오렌지를 반쪽이나 물고서 술맛을 알 수 있다고 나한테 말할 셈인가?”"]], [["\"It'll come to him soon enough,\"", "“저 친구에게도 머지않아 그때가 올 거요.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"Twenty port won't come to him when he is as old as we are,\"", "“20년 산은 저 친구가 우리만큼 나이를 먹었을 때에는 저 친구에게 오지 않을 거요.”"], ["said the earl, forgetting that by that time sixty port will be as wonderful to the then living seniors of the age as was his own pet vintage to him.", "백작이 그렇게 말하면서, 그때가 되면 그 시대에 살아 있을 노인들에게는 60년 산이 지금 제가 아끼는 이 술이 저에게 그러하듯 놀라운 것이 되리라는 것을 잊고 있었다."]], [["The good wine did in some sort soften the squire; but, as a matter of course, nothing further was said as to the new matrimonial scheme.", "그 좋은 술은 얼마쯤 지주의 마음을 누그러뜨렸다."], ["The earl did observe, however, that Mr.", "그러나 당연한 일로 새 혼담에 대해서는 더 아무 말도 나오지 않았다."], ["Dale was civil, and even kind, to his own young friend, asking a question here and there as to his life in London, and saying something about the work at the Income-tax Office.", "다만 백작은 데일 씨가 제 젊은 벗에게 정중하게, 친절하다고 할 만큼 대해 주는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 이따금 런던 생활에 대해 이것저것 묻기도 하고, 소득세 사무국의 일에 대해 무어라 말하기도 했던 것이다."]], [["\"It is hard work,\"", "“일이 고됩니다.” 임스가 말했다."], ["said Eames.", "“줄 아래에 이름이 적히기라도 하면 큰 소동이 벌어집니다."], ["\"If you're under the line, they make a great row about it, send for you, and look at you as though you'd been robbing the bank; but they think nothing of keeping you till five.\"", "사람을 불러들여서는 마치 은행이라도 턴 것처럼 쳐다보지요. 그러면서도 다섯 시까지 붙들어 두는 것은 아무렇지도 않게 여깁니다.”"]], [["\"But how long do you have for lunch and reading the papers?\"", "“그런데 점심을 먹고 신문을 읽는 데는 시간이 얼마나 나나?”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"Not ten minutes.", "“십 분도 안 됩니다."], ["We take a paper among twenty of us for half the day.", "저희는 스무 사람이 신문 한 부를 하루의 절반 동안 나누어 봅니다."], ["That's exactly nine minutes to each; and as for lunch, we only have a biscuit dipped in ink.\"", "한 사람에 꼭 구 분씩이지요. 그리고 점심으로 말하면, 저희는 잉크에 적신 비스킷 하나를 먹을 뿐입니다.”"]], [["\"Dipped in ink!\"", "“잉크에 적신다니!”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"It comes to that, for you have to be writing while you munch it.\"", "“그런 셈이 되지요. 그걸 우물거리는 동안에도 글을 쓰고 있어야 하니까요.”"]], [["\"I hear all about you,\"", "“나는 자네 이야기를 죄다 듣고 있네.”"], ["said the earl; \"Sir Raffle Buffle is an old crony of mine.\"", "백작이 말했다. “래플 버플 경은 내 오랜 벗이거든.”"]], [["\"I don't suppose he ever heard my name as yet,\"", "“그분은 아직 제 이름도 들어 보신 적이 없을 겁니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."], ["\"But do you really know him well, Lord De Guest?\"", "“한데 그분을 정말 잘 아십니까, 드 게스트 경?”"]], [["\"Haven't seen him these thirty years; but I did know him.\"", "“삼십 년째 얼굴을 못 보았네만, 알기는 알았지.”"]], [["\"We call him old Huffle Scuffle.\"", "“저희는 그분을 늙은 허플 스커플이라고 부릅니다.”"]], [["\"Huffle Scuffle!", "“허플 스커플이라!"], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하하하!"], ["He always was Huffle Scuffle; a noisy, pretentious, empty-headed fellow.", "그 사람은 늘 허플 스커플이었지. 시끄럽고 젠체하고 머릿속은 텅 빈 친구였네."], ["But I oughtn't to say so before you, young man.", "하지만 자네 앞에서 이런 말을 해서는 안 되겠지, 젊은이."], ["Come, we'll go into the drawing-room.\"", "자, 응접실로 가세.”"]], [["\"And what did he say?\"", "“그래서 그이가 무어라고 하던가요?”"], ["asked Lady Julia, as soon as the squire was gone.", "지주가 돌아가자마자 레이디 줄리아가 물었다."]], [["There was no attempt at concealment, and the question was asked in Johnny's presence.", "숨길 것이 없었으므로 그 물음은 조니가 있는 자리에서 던져졌다."]], [["\"Well, he did not say much.", "“글쎄, 별말은 하지 않았다."], ["And coming from him, that ought to be taken as a good sign.", "그런데 그 사람에게서 나온 것으로 보면 그것도 좋은 조짐으로 여겨야 하지."], ["He is to think of it, and let me see him again.", "생각해 보고 나를 다시 만나겠다는구나."], ["You hold your head up, Johnny, and remember that you shan't want a friend on your side.", "조니, 자네는 고개를 들고 있게. 그리고 자네 편에 서 줄 벗이 없지는 않다는 것을 잊지 말게."], ["Faint heart never won fair lady.\"", "겁 많은 마음으로는 결코 아름다운 여인을 얻지 못하는 법일세.”"]], [["At seven o'clock on the following morning Eames started on his return journey, and was at his desk at twelve o'clock,--as per agreement with his taskmaster at the Income-tax Office.", "이튿날 아침 일곱 시에 임스는 돌아가는 길에 올랐고, 열두 시에는 제 책상 앞에 앉아 있었다.—소득세 사무국의 제 상전과 한 약속대로였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIV. THE COMBAT.", "# 제34장. 격투"]], [["I have said that John Eames was at his office punctually at twelve; but an incident had happened before his arrival there very important in the annals which are now being told,--so important that it is essentially necessary that it should be described with some minuteness of detail.", "존 임스가 열두 시 정각에 제 사무실에 나와 있었다고 나는 앞서 말한 바 있다. 그러나 그가 그곳에 닿기 전에 한 가지 사건이 있었으니, 지금 이야기되고 있는 이 연대기에서 매우 중요한 일이었다—어찌나 중요한지 얼마간 세세하게 그려 두는 것이 반드시 필요할 지경이다."]], [["Lord De Guest, in the various conversations which he had had with Eames as to Lily Dale and her present position, had always spoken of Crosbie with the most vehement abhorrence.", "드 게스트 경은 릴리 데일과 그녀의 지금 처지를 두고 임스와 나눈 여러 차례의 이야기에서 언제나 크로즈비를 가장 격렬한 혐오로 말해 왔다."], ["\"He is a damned blackguard,\"", "“그자는 빌어먹을 불한당이야.”"], ["said the earl, and the fire had come out of his round eyes as he spoke.", "백작은 이렇게 말했고, 말하는 동안 그 동그란 눈에서는 불길이 뿜어져 나왔다."], ["Now the earl was by no means given to cursing and swearing, in the sense which is ordinarily applied to these words.", "그런데 백작은 이런 낱말들에 보통 붙는 뜻으로 저주하고 욕설을 늘어놓는 사람이 결코 아니었다."], ["When he made use of such a phrase as that quoted above, it was to be presumed that he in some sort meant what he said; and so he did, and had intended to signify that Crosbie by his conduct had merited all such condemnation as was the fitting punishment for blackguardism of the worst description.", "그가 위에 인용한 것과 같은 표현을 쓸 때에는, 어떤 의미에서 제가 한 말을 그대로 뜻한 것이라고 여겨야 마땅했다. 실제로도 그러하였으니, 크로즈비가 제 처신으로 말미암아 가장 고약한 종류의 불한당 짓에 어울리는 벌로서 내려질 만한 온갖 단죄를 다 받아 마땅하게 되었다는 뜻이었다."]], [["\"He ought to have his neck broken,\"", "“그자는 목이 부러져야 마땅합니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't know about that,\"", "“글쎄, 그건 나도 모르겠네.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"The present times have become so pretty behaved that corporal punishment seems to have gone out of fashion.", "“요즘 세상은 하도 얌전해져서 몸에 매질을 가하는 벌은 유행이 지나 버린 모양이야."], ["I shouldn't care so much about that, if any other punishment had taken its place.", "다른 벌이 그 자리를 대신하기라도 했다면 나도 그리 개의치 않겠네만."], ["But it seems to me that a blackguard such as Crosbie can escape now altogether unscathed.\"", "그런데 내가 보기에는 크로즈비 같은 불한당이 이제는 아예 아무 상처도 입지 않고 빠져나갈 수 있게 된 것 같단 말이지.”"]], [["\"He hasn't escaped yet,\"", "“아직 빠져나가지는 못했습니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Don't you go and put your finger in the pie and make a fool of yourself,\"", "“괜히 남의 일에 손가락을 들이밀어 바보 꼴이 되지는 말게.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["If it had behoved any one to resent in any violent fashion the evil done by Crosbie, Bernard Dale, the earl's nephew, should have been the avenger.", "크로즈비가 저지른 악행을 누군가 격한 방식으로 갚아 주어야 마땅했다면, 그 복수자는 백작의 조카인 버나드 데일이었어야 했다."], ["This the earl felt, but under these circumstances he was disposed to think that there should be no such violent vengeance.", "백작도 그렇게 느꼈으나, 사정이 이러하고 보니 그런 격렬한 복수는 없는 편이 낫겠다고 생각하는 쪽으로 기울었다."], ["\"Things were different when I was young,\"", "“내가 젊었을 적에는 사정이 달랐지.”"], ["he said to himself.", "그는 속으로 중얼거렸다."], ["But Eames gathered from the earl's tone that the earl's words were not strictly in accordance with his thoughts, and he declared to himself over and over again that Crosbie had not yet escaped.", "그러나 임스는 백작의 말투에서 그 말이 그의 생각과 꼭 들어맞지는 않는다는 것을 알아챘고, 크로즈비는 아직 빠져나가지 못했노라고 스스로에게 몇 번이고 되뇌었다."]], [["He got into the train at Guestwick, taking a first-class ticket, because the earl's groom in livery was in attendance upon him.", "그는 게스트윅에서 기차에 올랐는데, 제복을 입은 백작의 마부가 배웅을 나와 있었던 터라 일등칸 표를 끊었다."], ["Had he been alone he would have gone in a cheaper carriage.", "혼자였더라면 더 싼 칸을 탔을 것이다."], ["Very weak in him, was it not?", "참으로 마음이 약한 짓이 아니었던가?"], ["little also, and mean?", "게다가 좀스럽고 옹졸하지 않은가?"], ["My friend, can you say that you would not have done the same at his age?", "벗이여, 그대는 그 나이에 같은 짓을 하지 않았으리라고 말할 수 있는가?"], ["Are you quite sure that you would not do the same now that you are double his age?", "그의 나이의 곱절이 된 지금이라도 같은 짓을 하지 않으리라고 아주 자신할 수 있는가?"], ["Be that as it may, Johnny Eames did that foolish thing, and gave the groom in livery half-a-crown into the bargain.", "어찌 되었든 조니 임스는 그 어리석은 짓을 했고, 덤으로 제복 입은 마부에게 반 크라운까지 쥐여 주었다."]], [["\"We shall have you down again soon, Mr. John,\"", "“머지않아 또 내려오시게 될 겁니다요, 존 나리.”"], ["said the groom, who seemed to understand that Mr. Eames was to be made quite at home at the manor.", "마부가 말했는데, 임스 씨가 저택에서 아주 스스럼없이 지내도 되는 손님이라는 것을 잘 아는 눈치였다."]], [["He went fast to sleep in the carriage, and did not awake till the train was stopped at the Barchester Junction.", "그는 객차 안에서 곧 깊이 잠이 들었고, 기차가 바체스터 분기역에 멈춰 설 때까지 깨지 않았다."]], [["\"Waiting for the up-train from Barchester, sir,\"", "“바체스터에서 오는 상행 열차를 기다리는 중입니다, 손님.”"], ["said the guard.", "차장이 말했다."], ["\"They're always late.\"", "“늘 늦지요.”"], ["Then he went to sleep again, and was aroused in a few minutes by some one entering the carriage in a great hurry.", "그러고 나서 그는 다시 잠이 들었다가, 몇 분 뒤 누군가 몹시 서둘러 객차로 들어오는 바람에 잠이 깼다."], ["The branch train had come in, just as the guardians of the line then present had made up their minds that the passengers on the main line should not be kept waiting any longer.", "마침 그 자리에 있던 노선 관리인들이 본선 승객들을 더 이상 기다리게 해서는 안 되겠다고 마음을 굳힌 참에 지선 열차가 들어온 것이었다."], ["The transfer of men, women, and luggage was therefore made in great haste, and they who were now taking their new seats had hardly time to look about them.", "그리하여 남자와 여자와 짐을 옮기는 일이 몹시 부산하게 이루어졌고, 이제 새 자리를 잡는 이들은 주위를 둘러볼 겨를조차 거의 없었다."], ["An old gentleman, very red about the gills, first came into Johnny's carriage, which up to that moment he had shared with an old lady.", "볼과 턱께가 벌겋게 달아오른 늙은 신사 하나가 조니의 객실에 맨 먼저 들어왔는데, 그때까지 조니는 그 칸을 늙은 부인 한 사람과만 함께 쓰고 있었다."], ["The old gentleman was abusing everybody, because he was hurried, and would not take himself well into the compartment, but stuck in the doorway, standing on the step.", "늙은 신사는 서두르게 되었다는 이유로 모두에게 욕을 퍼부으면서도, 객실 안으로 제대로 들어서지는 않고 발판에 선 채 문간을 막고 있었다."]], [["\"Now, sir, when you're quite at leisure,\"", "“자, 이제 좀 한가하시거든요, 선생.”"], ["said a voice behind the old man, which instantly made Eames start up in his seat.", "노인 뒤에서 어떤 목소리가 말했고, 그 소리에 임스는 대뜸 흠칫하며 자리에서 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["\"I'm not at all at leisure,\"", "“나는 조금도 한가하지 않소.”"], ["said the old man; \"and I'm not going to break my legs if I know it.\"", "노인이 말했다. “그리고 내가 아는 한 다리를 부러뜨릴 생각도 없고.”"]], [["\"Take your time, sir,\"", "“천천히 하십시오, 어르신.”"], ["said the guard.", "차장이 말했다."]], [["\"So I mean,\"", "“그럴 참이오.”"], ["said the old man, seating himself in the corner nearest to the open door, opposite to the old lady.", "노인은 이렇게 말하며 열린 문에서 가장 가까운 구석 자리, 늙은 부인의 맞은편에 앉았다."], ["Then Eames saw plainly that it was Crosbie who had first spoken, and that he was getting into the carriage.", "그제야 임스는 처음 말한 사람이 크로즈비였다는 것을, 그리고 그가 지금 객차에 오르고 있다는 것을 똑똑히 보았다."]], [["Crosbie at the first glance saw no one but the old gentleman and the old lady, and he immediately made for the unoccupied corner seat.", "크로즈비는 첫눈에 늙은 신사와 늙은 부인밖에 보지 못했고, 곧장 비어 있는 구석 자리로 갔다."], ["He was busy with his umbrella and his dressing-bag, and a little flustered by the pushing and hurrying.", "그는 우산과 세면 가방을 챙기느라 분주했고, 밀치고 서두르는 통에 조금 허둥거리고 있었다."], ["The carriage was actually in motion before he perceived that John Eames was opposite to him: Eames had, instinctively, drawn up his legs so as not to touch him.", "존 임스가 제 맞은편에 있다는 것을 알아차렸을 때 객차는 이미 움직이고 있었다. 임스는 저도 모르게 다리를 오므려 그와 닿지 않게 하고 있었다."], ["He felt that he had become very red in the face, and to tell the truth, the perspiration had broken out upon his brow.", "그는 제 얼굴이 아주 벌게졌다는 것을 느꼈고, 사실을 말하자면 이마에는 땀이 배어 나와 있었다."], ["It was a great occasion,--great in its imminent trouble, and great in its opportunity for action.", "그것은 대단한 순간이었다—당장 닥친 곤경으로 보아도 대단하고, 무언가를 할 기회로 보아도 대단한 순간이었다."], ["How was he to carry himself at the first moment of his recognition by his enemy, and what was he to do afterwards?", "원수에게 제 정체가 알아보이는 그 첫 순간에 그는 어떻게 처신할 것이며, 그다음에는 무엇을 할 것인가?"]], [["It need hardly be explained that Crosbie had also been spending his Christmas with a certain earl of his acquaintance, and that he too was returning to his office.", "크로즈비 역시 안면 있는 어떤 백작 댁에서 성탄절을 보내고 마찬가지로 제 사무실로 돌아가는 길이었다는 것은 굳이 설명할 필요도 없겠다."], ["In one respect he had been much more fortunate than poor Eames, for he had been made happy with the smiles of his lady love.", "한 가지 점에서는 그가 가엾은 임스보다 훨씬 운이 좋았으니, 사랑하는 여인의 미소로 행복을 누렸기 때문이다."], ["Alexandrina and the countess had fluttered about him softly, treating him as a tame chattel, now belonging to the noble house of De Courcy, and in this way he had been initiated into the inner domesticities of that illustrious family.", "알렉산드리나와 백작부인은 이제 드 코시 가문이라는 귀한 집안에 딸린 길든 세간붙이를 다루듯 그를 다루며 살랑살랑 그의 둘레를 맴돌았고, 그리하여 그는 그 이름 높은 집안의 안살림 속내에까지 발을 들이게 되었다."], ["The two extra men-servants, hired to wait upon Lady Dumbello, had vanished.", "레이디 덤벨로의 시중을 들라고 새로 들였던 하인 둘은 자취를 감추었다."], ["The champagne had ceased to flow in a perennial stream.", "샴페인이 마르지 않는 시내처럼 흐르던 것도 그쳤다."], ["Lady Rosina had come out from her solitude, and had preached at him constantly.", "레이디 로지나는 칩거에서 나와 그에게 쉴 새 없이 설교를 늘어놓았다."], ["Lady Margaretta had given him some lessons in economy.", "레이디 마거리타는 그에게 절약에 관한 가르침을 몇 가지 베풀었다."], ["The Honourable John, in spite of a late quarrel, had borrowed five pounds from him.", "아너러블 존은 얼마 전에 다툰 일이 있는데도 그에게서 오 파운드를 빌려 갔다."], ["The Honourable George had engaged to come and stay with his sister during the next May.", "아너러블 조지는 돌아오는 오월에 누이 집에 와서 묵겠다고 약조했다."], ["The earl had used a father-in-law's privilege, and had called him a fool.", "백작은 장인의 특권을 부려 그를 바보라고 불렀다."], ["Lady Alexandrina had told him more than once, in rather a tart voice, that this must be done, and that that must be done; and the countess had given him her orders as though it was his duty, in the course of nature, to obey every word that fell from her.", "레이디 알렉산드리나는 이것도 해야 하고 저것도 해야 한다고 사뭇 쌀쌀한 목소리로 한 번 이상 그에게 일렀다. 그리고 백작부인은 제 입에서 떨어지는 말이라면 무엇이든 따르는 것이 그의 당연한 도리라는 듯이 그에게 분부를 내렸다."], ["Such had been his Christmas delights; and now, as he returned back from the enjoyment of them, he found himself confronted in the railway carriage with Johnny Eames!", "이런 것들이 그의 성탄절 낙이었다. 그리고 이제 그 즐거움을 다 누리고 돌아오는 길에, 그는 철도 객차 안에서 조니 임스와 맞대면하게 된 것이다!"]], [["The eyes of the two met, and Crosbie made a slight inclination of his head.", "두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤고, 크로즈비는 고개를 살짝 숙여 보였다."], ["To this Eames gave no acknowledgment whatever, but looked straight into the other's face.", "임스는 이에 아무런 아는 체도 하지 않고 상대의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다."], ["Crosbie immediately saw that they were not to know each other, and was well contented that it should be so.", "크로즈비는 두 사람이 서로 모르는 사이로 지내기로 되었음을 곧 알아차렸고, 그렇게 되어 아주 다행이라고 여겼다."], ["Among all his many troubles, the enmity of John Eames did not go for much.", "그 많은 근심 가운데 존 임스의 적의 따위는 대수롭지 않았다."], ["He showed no appearance of being disconcerted, though our friend had shown much.", "우리 벗은 잔뜩 당황한 기색을 보였으나 그는 조금도 당황한 기색을 보이지 않았다."], ["He opened his bag, and taking out a book was soon deeply engaged in it, pursuing his studies as though the man opposite was quite unknown to him.", "그는 가방을 열어 책을 한 권 꺼내더니, 맞은편에 앉은 사내를 전혀 모르는 사람인 양 곧 그 책에 깊이 빠져들어 제 공부를 이어 갔다."], ["I will not say that his mind did not run away from his book, for indeed there were many things of which he found it impossible not to think; but it did not revert to John Eames.", "그의 마음이 책에서 벗어나 달아나지 않았다고는 말하지 않겠다. 실로 그가 생각하지 않을 수 없는 일들이 많았기 때문이다."], ["Indeed, when the carriages reached Paddington, he had in truth all but forgotten him; and as he stepped out of the carriage, with his bag in his hand, was quite free from any remotest trouble on his account.", "그러나 그 마음이 존 임스에게로 되돌아가지는 않았다. 참으로, 객차가 패딩턴에 닿았을 때에는 그를 거의 잊고 있었고, 가방을 손에 들고 객차에서 내릴 때에는 그 사람 때문에 마음 쓰이는 일이라고는 아주 조금도 없었다."]], [["But it had not been so with Eames himself.", "그러나 임스 쪽은 그렇지 않았다."], ["Every moment of the journey had for him been crowded with thought as to what he would do now that chance had brought his enemy within his reach.", "여정의 매 순간이 그에게는, 우연이 원수를 제 손 닿는 곳까지 데려다 놓은 지금 무엇을 할 것인가 하는 생각으로 빼곡했다."], ["He had been made quite wretched by the intensity of his thinking; and yet, when the carriages stopped, he had not made up his mind.", "그토록 골똘히 생각한 나머지 그는 아주 비참해졌다. 그러면서도 객차가 멈추었을 때까지 마음을 정하지 못했다."], ["His face had been covered with perspiration ever since Crosbie had come across him, and his limbs had hardly been under his own command.", "크로즈비가 제 앞에 나타난 뒤로 그의 얼굴은 줄곧 땀에 젖어 있었고, 손발도 좀처럼 제 뜻대로 놀려지지 않았다."], ["Here had come to him a great opportunity, and he felt so little confidence in himself that he almost knew that he would not use it properly.", "그에게 큰 기회가 찾아왔건만, 그는 제 자신을 하도 믿지 못하여 이 기회를 제대로 쓰지 못하리라는 것을 거의 알고 있었다."], ["Twice and thrice he had almost flown at Crosbie's throat in the carriage, but he was restrained by an idea that the world and the police would be against him if he did such a thing in the presence of that old lady.", "객차 안에서 그는 두 번 세 번 크로즈비의 목으로 달려들 뻔했으나, 저 늙은 부인이 보는 앞에서 그런 짓을 하면 세상과 경찰이 제 편을 들어 주지 않으리라는 생각에 스스로를 억눌렀다."]], [["But when Crosbie turned his back upon him, and walked out, it was absolutely necessary that he should do something.", "그러나 크로즈비가 그에게 등을 돌리고 걸어 나가자, 그는 반드시 무언가를 해야만 했다."], ["He was not going to let the man escape, after all that he had said as to the expediency of thrashing him.", "그자를 두들겨 주는 것이 상책이라고 그토록 떠들어 놓고서 그냥 놓아 보낼 수는 없었다."], ["Any other disgrace would be preferable to that.", "그 밖의 어떤 망신이라도 그것보다는 나을 터였다."], ["Fearing, therefore, lest his enemy should be too quick for him, he hurried out after him, and only just gave Crosbie time to turn round and face the carriages before he was upon him.", "그리하여 원수가 저보다 빠를까 두려워 그는 서둘러 그 뒤를 따라 나갔고, 크로즈비가 몸을 돌려 객차 쪽을 마주 볼 겨를만 겨우 준 채 그에게 덮쳤다. “이 빌어먹을 악당아!”"], ["\"You confounded scoundrel!\" he screamed out.", "그는 비명을 지르듯 외쳤다."], ["\"You confounded scoundrel!\"", "“이 빌어먹을 악당아!”"], ["and seized him by the throat, throwing himself upon him, and almost devouring him by the fury of his eyes.", "그러고는 그의 목을 움켜쥐고 몸째 그에게 달려들었으며, 이글거리는 눈으로 그를 잡아먹을 듯이 노려보았다."]], [["The crowd upon the platform was not very dense, but there were quite enough of people to make a very respectable audience for this little play.", "승강장의 사람들은 그리 빽빽하지 않았으나, 이 짧은 연극에 아주 훌륭한 구경꾼 노릇을 할 만큼은 넉넉히 있었다."], ["Crosbie, in his dismay, retreated a step or two, and his retreat was much accelerated by the weight of Eames's attack.", "크로즈비는 질겁하여 한두 걸음 물러섰는데, 임스가 덮쳐 온 무게 때문에 그 물러섬은 한결 빨라졌다."], ["He endeavoured to free his throat from his foe's grasp; but in that he failed entirely.", "그는 원수의 손아귀에서 제 목을 빼내려 애썼으나 그것은 전혀 되지 않았다."], ["For the minute, however, he did manage to escape any positive blow, owing his safety in that respect rather to Eames's awkwardness than to his own efforts.", "그러나 그 순간만큼은 어떻게든 실제로 한 대도 맞지 않고 넘겼는데, 그 점에서 그가 무사했던 것은 제가 애쓴 덕이라기보다 임스가 서툴렀던 덕이었다. 경찰이 어떻다는 말을 그는 겨우 입 밖에 낼 수 있었고, 물론 곧바로 그 공무원들을 불러오라는 소리가 터져 나왔다."], ["Something about the police he was just able to utter, and there was, as a matter of course, an immediate call for a supply of those functionaries.", "삼 분쯤 지나 순경 셋이 짐꾼 여섯의 도움을 받아 우리의 가엾은 벗 조니를 붙잡았다. 그러나 그것은 크로즈비의 뜻대로 되기에는 충분히 빠르지 못했다."], ["In about three minutes three policemen, assisted by six porters, had captured our poor friend Johnny; but this had not been done quick enough for Crosbie's purposes. The bystanders, taken by surprise, had allowed the combatants to fall back upon Mr.", "불시에 당한 구경꾼들이 두 싸움꾼을 스미스 씨의 책 가판대까지 밀려가도록 내버려 둔 사이, 임스는 신문 더미 한복판에 원수를 넘어뜨렸고, 제 기세가 지나쳤던 탓에 저는 노란 표지 일 실링짜리 소설 무더기 속으로 나동그라졌다."], ["Smith's book-stall, and there Eames laid his foe prostrate among the newspapers, falling himself into the yellow shilling-novel depot by the over fury of his own energy; but as he fell, he contrived to lodge one blow with his fist in Crosbie's right eye,--one telling blow; and Crosbie had, to all intents and purposes, been thrashed.", "그러나 넘어지면서도 그는 제 주먹으로 크로즈비의 오른쪽 눈에 한 방을 먹이는 데 성공했다—제대로 들어간 한 방이었다. 그리하여 크로즈비는 어느 모로 보나 두들겨 맞은 셈이 되었다."]], [["\"Con--founded scoundrel, rascal, blackguard!\"", "“빌—어먹을 악당, 무뢰한, 불한당!”"], ["shouted Johnny, with what remnants of voice were left to him, as the police dragged him off.", "조니는 순경들에게 끌려 나가면서 남아 있는 목청을 다 짜내어 소리쳤다."], ["\"If you only knew--what he's--done.\"", "“당신들이 알기만 한다면—저놈이 무슨—짓을 했는지.”"], ["But in the meantime the policemen held him fast.", "그러나 그러는 동안에도 순경들은 그를 단단히 붙들고 있었다."]], [["As a matter of course the first burst of public sympathy went with Crosbie.", "당연한 일이지만 사람들의 동정은 처음에 크로즈비 쪽으로 쏠렸다."], ["He had been assaulted, and the assault had come from Eames.", "그는 습격을 당했고, 그 습격은 임스에게서 나왔다."], ["In the British bosom there is so firm a love of well-constituted order, that these facts alone were sufficient to bring twenty knights to the assistance of the three policemen and the six porters; so that for Eames, even had he desired it, there was no possible chance of escape.", "영국인의 가슴속에는 잘 짜인 질서에 대한 사랑이 하도 굳건해서, 이 사실만으로도 순경 셋과 짐꾼 여섯을 도우러 스무 명의 기사가 나서기에 넉넉했다. 그러니 임스로서는 설령 원했다 한들 달아날 가망이라고는 조금도 없었다."], ["But he did not desire it.", "그러나 그는 달아나기를 원하지 않았다."], ["One only sorrow consumed him at present.", "다만 한 가지 슬픔이 지금 그를 좀먹고 있었다."], ["He had, as he felt, attacked Crosbie, but had attacked him in vain.", "그가 느끼기로 자기는 크로즈비를 덮치기는 했으나 헛되이 덮쳤을 뿐이었다."], ["He had had his opportunity, and had misused it.", "기회를 얻고서도 그것을 잘못 써 버린 것이다."], ["He was perfectly unconscious of that happy blow, and was in absolute ignorance of the great fact that his enemy's eye was already swollen and closed, and that in another hour it would be as black as his hat.", "그는 그 다행스러운 한 방을 조금도 의식하지 못했고, 원수의 눈이 이미 부어올라 감겨 버렸다는 것도, 한 시간만 지나면 그 눈이 제 모자만큼이나 새까매지리라는 것도 전혀 알지 못했다."]], [["\"He is a con--founded rascal!\"", "“저놈은 빌—어먹을 무뢰한이오!”"], ["ejaculated Eames, as the policemen and porters hauled him about.", "순경들과 짐꾼들이 그를 이리저리 끌고 다니는 동안 임스가 소리쳐 뱉었다."], ["\"You don't know what he's done.\"", "“당신들은 저놈이 무슨 짓을 했는지 모릅니다.”"]], [["\"No, we don't,\"", "“그야 우리는 모르지요.”"], ["said the senior constable; \"but we know what you have done.", "고참 순경이 말했다. “하지만 당신이 무슨 짓을 했는지는 압니다."], ["I say, Bushers, where's that gentleman?", "여보게, 부셔스, 그 신사분은 어디 계신가?"], ["He'd better come along with us.\"", "우리와 함께 가시는 편이 좋겠는데.”"]], [["Crosbie had been picked up from among the newspapers by another policeman and two or three other porters, and was attended also by the guard of the train, who knew him, and knew that he had come up from Courcy Castle.", "크로즈비는 다른 순경 하나와 짐꾼 두엇의 손에 신문 더미 속에서 일으켜 세워졌고, 그를 알아보는 데다 그가 코시 성에서 올라왔다는 것까지 아는 열차 차장의 시중도 받았다."], ["Three or four hangers-on were standing also around him, together with a benevolent medical man who was proposing to him an immediate application of leeches.", "서너 명의 구경꾼도 그의 둘레에 서 있었고, 당장 거머리를 붙이자고 권하는 자비로운 의사 한 사람도 함께 있었다."], ["If he could have done as he wished, he would have gone his way quietly, allowing Eames to do the same.", "제 뜻대로 할 수 있었다면 그는 조용히 제 갈 길을 갔을 것이고, 임스도 그렇게 하도록 내버려 두었을 것이다."], ["A great evil had befallen him, but he could in no way mitigate that evil by taking the law of the man who had attacked him.", "큰 불행이 그에게 닥쳤으나, 자기를 덮친 사내를 법에 넘긴다고 해서 그 불행을 조금이라도 덜 수 있는 것은 결코 아니었다."], ["To have the thing as little talked about as possible should be his endeavour.", "이 일이 되도록 사람들 입에 덜 오르내리게 하는 것이야말로 그가 애써야 할 바였다."], ["What though he should have Eames locked up and fined, and scolded by a police magistrate?", "임스를 가두고 벌금을 물리고 치안 판사에게 꾸지람을 듣게 한들 무엇하겠는가?"], ["That would not in any degree lessen his calamity.", "그런다고 그의 재앙이 조금이라도 가벼워지지는 않을 터였다."], ["If he could have parried the attack, and got the better of his foe; if he could have administered the black eye instead of receiving it, then indeed he could have laughed the matter off at his club, and his original crime would have been somewhat glozed over by his success in arms.", "그가 그 공격을 막아 내고 원수를 이길 수 있었더라면, 시퍼렇게 멍든 눈을 얻어맞는 대신 안겨 줄 수 있었더라면, 그야말로 그는 클럽에서 이 일을 웃어넘길 수 있었을 것이고, 애초에 그가 저지른 죄도 무예에서 거둔 승리로 얼마간 덮였을 것이다."], ["But such good fortune had not been his.", "그러나 그런 행운은 그의 것이 아니었다."], ["He was forced, however, on the moment to decide as to what he would do.", "그러나 그는 무엇을 할 것인지 그 자리에서 당장 정해야만 했다."]], [["\"We've got him here in custody, sir,\"", "“이자를 저희가 붙잡아 두었습니다, 나리.”"], ["said Bushers, touching his hat.", "부셔스가 모자에 손을 대며 말했다."], ["It had become known from the guard that Crosbie was somewhat of a big man, a frequent guest at Courcy Castle, and of repute and station in the higher regions of the Metropolitan world.", "크로즈비가 제법 큰 인물이고, 코시 성에 자주 드나드는 손님이며, 런던 상류 세계에서 명망과 지위가 있는 사람이라는 것이 차장을 통해 알려진 터였다."], ["\"The magistrates will be sitting at Paddington, now, sir--or will be by the time we get there.\"", "“지금 패딩턴에 치안 판사들이 나와 앉아 있을 겁니다, 나리—아니면 저희가 갈 때쯤이면 나와 있을 겁니다요.”"]], [["By this time some mighty railway authority had come upon the scene and made himself cognizant of the facts of the row,--a stern official who seemed to carry the weight of many engines on his brow; one at the very sight of whom smokers would drop their cigars, and porters close their fists against sixpences; a great man with an erect chin, a quick step, and a well-brushed hat powerful with an elaborately upturned brim.", "그럴 즈음 어떤 대단한 철도 관리가 현장에 나타나 소동의 자초지종을 파악했다—이마에 기관차 여러 대의 무게를 얹고 있는 듯한 근엄한 관리요, 그를 보기만 해도 담배 피우던 자들은 시가를 떨어뜨리고 짐꾼들은 육 펜스짜리 앞에서 주먹을 오므릴 만한 사람이었으며, 턱을 곧추세우고 걸음이 빠르며 챙을 공들여 치켜올린 위엄 있는 모자를 잘 손질해 쓴 대단한 인물이었다."], ["This was the platform-superintendent, dominant even over the policemen.", "이 사람이 바로 승강장 감독관으로, 순경들보다도 위에 서는 이였다."]], [["\"Step into my room, Mr.", "“제 방으로 들어오시지요, 크로즈비 씨.”"], ["Crosbie,\" he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Stubbs, bring that man in with you.\"", "“스터브스, 저 사람을 데리고 들어오게.”"], ["And then, before Crosbie had been able to make up his mind as to any other line of conduct, he found himself in the superintendent's room, accompanied by the guard, and by the two policemen who conducted Johnny Eames between them.", "그리하여 크로즈비는 달리 어떤 처신을 할지 마음을 정하기도 전에 감독관의 방 안에 들어와 있었고, 차장과, 조니 임스를 사이에 끼고 데려온 순경 둘이 함께였다."]], [["\"What's all this?\"", "“이게 다 무슨 일인가?” 감독관은 여전히 모자를 쓴 채 말했다."], ["said the superintendent, still keeping on his hat, for he was aware how much of the excellence of his personal dignity was owing to the arrangement of that article; and as he spoke he frowned upon the culprit with his utmost severity.", "제 위엄이 얼마나 그 물건의 매무새에 힘입고 있는지를 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 말하면서 그는 있는 대로 험한 낯빛으로 죄인을 노려보았다."], ["\"Mr. Crosbie, I am very sorry that you should have been exposed to such brutality on our platform.\"", "“크로즈비 씨, 우리 승강장에서 이런 난폭한 일을 당하시게 되어 참으로 죄송합니다.”"]], [["\"You don't know what he has done,\"", "“당신은 저자가 무슨 짓을 했는지 모릅니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."], ["\"He is the most confounded scoundrel living. He has broken--\"", "“저자는 세상에 살아 있는 가장 빌어먹을 악당이오."], ["But then he stopped himself.", "저자는 부수어 놓았단 말이오—”"], ["He was going to tell the superintendent that the confounded scoundrel had broken a beautiful young lady's heart; but he bethought himself that he would not allude more specially to Lily Dale in that hearing.", "그러나 그는 거기서 말을 멈추었다. 그 빌어먹을 악당이 아름다운 젊은 아가씨의 마음을 부수어 놓았다고 감독관에게 말하려던 참이었으나, 그런 자리에서 릴리 데일을 더 꼭 집어 입에 올리지는 않겠다고 마음을 고쳐먹었다."]], [["\"Do you know who he is, Mr. Crosbie?\"", "“이 사람이 누구인지 아십니까, 크로즈비 씨?”"], ["said the superintendent.", "감독관이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes,\"", "“아, 압니다.”"], ["said Crosbie, whose eye was already becoming blue.", "이미 눈이 시퍼렇게 물들어 가고 있는 크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"He is a clerk in the Income-tax Office, and his name is Eames.", "“소득세 사무국의 서기이고, 이름은 임스요."], ["I believe you had better leave him to me.\"", "이 일은 내게 맡겨 두시는 편이 좋겠소.”"]], [["But the superintendent at once wrote down the words \"Income-tax Office--Eames,\" on his tablet.", "그러나 감독관은 그 자리에서 곧바로 “소득세 사무국—임스”라는 말을 제 수첩에 적었다."], ["\"We can't allow a row like that to take place on our platform and not notice it.", "“우리 승강장에서 그런 소동이 벌어졌는데 그냥 보아 넘길 수는 없습니다."], ["I shall bring it before the directors.", "나는 이 일을 이사들 앞에 올릴 겁니다."], ["It's a most disgraceful affair, Mr. Eames--most disgraceful.\"", "참으로 망신스러운 일이오, 임스 씨—참으로 망신스러운 일이야.”"]], [["But Johnny by this time had perceived that Crosbie's eye was in a state which proved satisfactorily that his morning's work had not been thrown away, and his spirits were rising accordingly.", "그러나 조니는 이때쯤 크로즈비의 눈이 제 아침나절의 수고가 헛되지 않았음을 넉넉히 증명해 주는 꼴이 되어 있다는 것을 알아차렸고, 그에 따라 기운이 솟아오르고 있었다."], ["He did not care two straws for the superintendent or even for the policemen, if only the story could be made to tell well for himself hereafter.", "이 이야기가 나중에 제게 유리하게 전해지기만 한다면 감독관이든 순경들이든 그는 조금도 개의치 않았다."], ["It was his object to have thrashed Crosbie, and now, as he looked at his enemy's face, he acknowledged that Providence had been good to him.", "크로즈비를 두들겨 주는 것이 그의 목적이었는데, 이제 원수의 얼굴을 바라보며 그는 하늘의 섭리가 제게 어질었노라고 인정했다."]], [["\"That's your opinion,\"", "“그건 당신 생각이지요.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, sir, it is,\"", "“그렇소, 내 생각이오.”"], ["said the superintendent; \"and I shall know how to represent the matter to your superiors, young man.\"", "감독관이 말했다. “그리고 나는 이 일을 당신 윗사람들에게 어떻게 알려야 할지도 잘 알고 있소, 젊은이.”"]], [["\"You don't know all about it,\"", "“당신은 이 일을 다 알지 못합니다.”"], ["said Eames; \"and I don't suppose you ever will.", "임스가 말했다. “앞으로도 알게 될 성싶지 않고요."], ["I had made up my mind what I'd do the first time I saw that scoundrel there; and now I've done it.", "나는 저 악당을 처음 보는 날 무엇을 할지 이미 마음을 정해 두었고, 이제 그것을 해냈습니다."], ["He'd have got much worse in the railway carriage, only there was a lady there.\"", "기차 안이었더라면 저자는 훨씬 더 험한 꼴을 당했을 텐데, 다만 거기 부인이 한 분 계셨을 뿐이오.”"]], [["\"Mr. Crosbie, I really think we had better take him before the magistrates.\"", "“크로즈비 씨, 아무래도 이 사람을 치안 판사 앞으로 데려가는 편이 낫겠습니다.”"]], [["To this, however, Crosbie objected.", "그러나 이에 크로즈비는 반대했다."], ["He assured the superintendent that he would himself know how to deal with the matter--which, however, was exactly what he did not know.", "그는 이 일을 어떻게 처리해야 할지 자기가 잘 안다고 감독관에게 장담했으나—실은 바로 그것이 그가 알지 못하는 바였다."], ["Would the superintendent allow one of the railway servants to get a cab for him, and to find his luggage?", "철도 직원 가운데 하나를 시켜 삯마차를 잡아 주고 제 짐을 찾아 주게 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["He was very anxious to get home without being subjected to any more of Mr. Eames's insolence.", "그는 임스 씨의 무례를 더 겪지 않고 집으로 돌아가기를 몹시 바랐다."]], [["\"You haven't done with Mr. Eames's insolence yet, I can tell you.", "“임스 씨의 무례는 아직 끝나지 않았소, 내가 말해 두지."], ["All London shall hear of it, and shall know why.", "온 런던이 이 일을 듣게 될 것이고, 그 까닭도 알게 될 것이오."], ["If you have any shame in you, you shall be ashamed to show your face.\"", "당신에게 부끄러움이라는 것이 조금이라도 있다면, 낯을 들고 다니기가 부끄러울 거요.”"]], [["Unfortunate man!", "가엾은 사내여!"], ["Who can say that punishment--adequate punishment--had not overtaken him?", "벌이—그것도 넉넉한 벌이—그를 따라잡지 않았다고 누가 말할 수 있겠는가?"], ["For the present, he had to sneak home with a black eye, with the knowledge inside him that he had been whipped by a clerk in the Income-tax Office; and for the future--he was bound over to marry Lady Alexandrina De Courcy!", "당장으로 말하자면 그는 시퍼렇게 멍든 눈을 하고, 소득세 사무국 서기에게 매를 맞았다는 앎을 속에 품은 채 슬그머니 집으로 기어들어야 했다. 그리고 앞날로 말하자면—그는 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시와 혼인해야 할 몸이었다!"]], [["He got himself smuggled off in a cab, without being forced to go again upon the platform--his luggage being brought to him by two assiduous porters.", "그는 승강장으로 다시 나갈 것도 없이 삯마차에 몰래 실려 떠났고, 짐은 부지런한 짐꾼 둘이 그에게 날라다 주었다."], ["But in all this there was very little balm for his hurt pride.", "그러나 이 모든 일에도 상한 자존심을 달랠 것은 거의 없었다."], ["As he ordered the cabman to drive to Mount Street, he felt that he had ruined himself by that step in life which he had taken at Courcy Castle.", "마부에게 마운트 스트리트로 가자고 이르면서 그는 코시 성에서 제 삶에 내디딘 그 한 걸음으로 스스로를 망쳐 놓았다고 느꼈다."], ["Whichever way he looked he had no comfort.", "어느 쪽을 돌아보아도 위안이 없었다."], ["\"D---- the fellow!\"", "“그—놈의 자식!”"], ["he said, almost out loud in the cab; but though he did with his outward voice allude to Eames, the curse in his inner thoughts was uttered against himself.", "그는 삯마차 안에서 거의 소리를 내어 말했다. 그러나 겉으로 낸 목소리로는 임스를 가리켰다 하더라도, 속마음의 저주는 제 자신을 향한 것이었다."]], [["Johnny was allowed to make his way down to the platform, and there find his own carpet-bag.", "조니는 승강장으로 내려가 제 융단 여행 가방을 찾아가도 좋다는 허락을 받았다."], ["One young porter, however, came up and fraternized with him.", "그런데 젊은 짐꾼 하나가 다가와 그와 벗하듯 굴었다."]], [["\"You guve it him tidy just at that last moment, sir.", "“마지막 그 순간에 아주 야무지게 한 대 먹이셨습니다요, 나리."], ["But, laws, sir, you should have let out at him at fust.", "하지만 저런, 나리, 처음부터 냅다 갈기셨어야지요."], ["What's the use of clawing a man's neck-collar?\"", "사람 목덜미 깃을 움켜쥐어 봤자 무슨 소용입니까요?”"]], [["It was then a quarter past eleven, but, nevertheless, Eames appeared at his office precisely at twelve.", "그때가 열한 시 십오 분이었으나, 그럼에도 임스는 정확히 열두 시에 제 사무실에 나타났다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXV. VÆ VICTIS.", "# 제35장. 「패자에게 화 있을진저」"]], [["Crosbie had two engagements for that day; one being his natural engagement to do his work at his office, and the other an engagement, which was now very often becoming as natural, to dine at St.", "크로즈비는 그날 두 가지 약속이 있었다. 하나는 사무실에 나가 제 일을 하는 지극히 당연한 약속이었고, 다른 하나는 이제 그에 못지않게 당연한 일이 되어 가고 있던 약속, 곧 세인트존스우드로 가서 레이디 어밀리아 게이지비와 저녁을 먹는 일이었다."], ["John's Wood with Lady Amelia Gazebee. It was manifest to him when he looked at himself in the glass that he could keep neither of these engagements.", "거울에 비친 제 얼굴을 들여다보니 그 두 약속 가운데 어느 것도 지킬 수 없다는 것이 분명했다."], ["\"Oh, laws, Mr. Crosbie,\"", "“아이고 저런, 크로즈비 씨.”"], ["the woman of the house exclaimed when she saw him.", "하숙집 안주인이 그를 보고는 소리쳤다."]], [["\"Yes, I know,\" said he.", "“그래요, 나도 압니다.”"], ["\"I've had an accident and got a black eye.", "그가 말했다. “사고를 당해서 눈에 멍이 들었소."], ["What's a good thing for it?\"", "여기엔 뭐가 좋겠소?”"]], [["\"Oh! an accident!\"", "“어머나, 사고라니요!”"], ["said the woman, who knew well that that mark had been made by another man's fist.", "안주인이 말했다. 그 자국이 다른 사내의 주먹에 생긴 것임을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["\"They do say that a bit of raw beef is about the best thing.", "“날고기 한 점이 제일이라고들 합니다요."], ["But then it must be held on constant all the morning.\"", "하지만 아침 내내 꼬박 대고 있어야 하지요.”"]], [["Anything would be better than leeches, which tell long-enduring tales, and therefore Crosbie sat through the greater part of the morning holding the raw beef to his eye.", "무엇이든 거머리보다는 나았다. 거머리 자국은 오래도록 남아 두고두고 이야깃거리를 만드는 법이니까."], ["But it was necessary that he should write two notes as he held it, one to Mr.", "그리하여 크로즈비는 그날 오전의 태반을 날고기를 눈에 대고 앉은 채로 보냈다."], ["Butterwell at his office, and the other to his future sister-in-law.", "그러나 그렇게 고기를 대고 있는 동안에도 쪽지 두 장은 써야만 했다."], ["He felt that it would hardly be wise to attempt any entire concealment of the nature of his catastrophe, as some of the circumstances would assuredly become known.", "하나는 사무실의 버터웰 씨에게, 다른 하나는 장차 처형이 될 사람에게 보낼 것이었다. 이 재난의 내막을 통째로 감추려 드는 것은 도무지 현명한 짓이 못 된다고 그는 생각했다."], ["If he said that he had fallen over the coal-scuttle, or on to the fender, thereby cutting his face, people would learn that he had fibbed, and would learn also that he had had some reason for fibbing.", "정황의 얼마쯤은 반드시 세상에 알려질 터였기 때문이다. 석탄 통에 걸려 넘어졌다거나 난로 울타리에 부딪혀 얼굴이 찢어졌다고 말했다가는, 사람들은 그가 둘러댔다는 것을 알게 될 것이고, 나아가 그가 둘러댈 만한 무슨 까닭이 있었다는 것까지 알게 될 것이었다."], ["Therefore he constructed his notes with a phraseology that bound him to no details.", "그래서 그는 어떤 세부에도 제 발이 묶이지 않도록 말을 골라 쪽지를 지었다."], ["To Butterwell he said that he had had an accident--or rather a row--and that he had come out of it with considerable damage to his frontispiece.", "버터웰에게는 사고를 당했다고—아니, 차라리 한바탕 소동을 겪었다고—썼고, 그 통에 얼굴 앞판에 적잖은 손상을 입었다고 썼다."], ["He intended to be at the office on the next day, whether able to appear decently there or not.", "다음 날에는 남 앞에 내놓을 만한 꼴이 되든 안 되든 사무실에 나갈 작정이었다."], ["But for the sake of decency he thought it well to give himself that one half-day's chance.", "다만 체면을 생각해서 반나절만은 스스로에게 말미를 주는 편이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 그다음 레이디 어밀리아에게도 사고를 당해 조금 다쳤다고 썼다."], ["Then to the Lady Amelia he also said that he had had an accident, and had been a little hurt.", "“대수로운 일은 전혀 아니고 그저 얼굴 모양새에만 관계된 일이라, 하루쯤 집에 들어앉아 있는 편이 낫겠습니다."], ["\"It is nothing at all serious, and affects only my appearance, so that I had better remain in for a day.", "일요일에는 꼭 찾아뵙겠습니다. 게이지비더러 일부러 저를 보러 오는 수고는 하지 말라 일러 주십시오."], ["I shall certainly be with you on Sunday.", "오늘 이후로는 집에 없을 테니까요.”"], ["Don't let Gazebee trouble himself to come to me, as I shan't be at home after to-day.\" Gazebee did trouble himself to come to Mount Street so often, and South Audley Street, in which was Mr.", "게이지비는 마운트 스트리트로 어찌나 자주 찾아오는지, 게다가 게이지비 씨의 사무실이 있는 사우스오들리 스트리트가 마운트 스트리트에서 어찌나 얄궂게 가까운지, 크로즈비는 될 수 있으면 제 몸을 지켜 보자는 심산으로 이 대목을 끼워 넣은 것이었다."], ["Gazebee's office, was so disagreeably near to Mount Street, that Crosbie inserted this in order to protect himself if possible.", "그러고는 자기는 그 누구에게도 집에 없는 것으로 하라고 각별히 일러 두었다."], ["Then he gave special orders that he was to be at home to no one, fearing that Gazebee would call for him after the hours of business--to make him safe and carry him off bodily to St. John's Wood.", "게이지비가 업무 시간이 끝난 뒤 저를 데리러 와서는 꼼짝 못 하게 붙들어 세인트존스우드로 통째로 끌고 가지나 않을까 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["The beefsteak and the dose of physic and the cold-water application which was kept upon it all night was not efficacious in dispelling that horrid, black-blue colour by ten o'clock on the following morning.", "날고기와 물약과 밤새도록 눈에 얹어 둔 찬물 찜질도, 이튿날 아침 열 시가 되도록 그 흉측한 검푸른 빛깔을 몰아내는 데에는 아무런 효험이 없었다."]], [["\"It certainly have gone down, Mr.", "“분명히 가라앉기는 했습니다요, 크로즈비 씨."], ["Crosbie; it certainly have,\"", "분명히 가라앉았습니다요.”"], ["said the mistress of the lodgings, touching the part affected with her finger.", "하숙집 안주인이 상한 자리를 손가락으로 짚어 보며 말했다."], ["\"But the black won't go out of them all in a minute; it won't indeed.", "“하지만 그 검은 것이 한순간에 빠지지는 않습니다요, 정말입니다요."], ["Couldn't you just stay in one more day?\"", "하루만 더 들어앉아 계시면 안 되겠습니까요?”"]], [["\"But will one day do it, Mrs. Phillips?\"", "“하루로 되겠소, 필립스 부인?”"]], [["Mrs. Phillips couldn't take upon herself to say that it would.", "필립스 부인은 그렇게 되리라고 장담할 수는 없었다."], ["\"They mostly come with little red streaks across the black before they goes away,\" said Mrs.", "“대개는 검은 데에 자잘한 붉은 줄이 죽죽 서고 나서야 빠지지요.”"], ["Phillips, who would seem to have been the wife of a prize-fighter, so well was she acquainted with black eyes.", "필립스 부인이 말했다. 멍든 눈에 어찌나 밝던지, 이 여자는 아무래도 권투 선수의 아내였던 모양이다."]], [["\"And that won't be till to-morrow,\"", "“그러면 내일이나 되어야 그렇게 되겠군.”"], ["said Crosbie, affecting to be mirthful in his agony.", "크로즈비가 괴로운 중에도 짐짓 유쾌한 체하며 말했다."]], [["\"Not till the third day;--and then they wears themselves out, gradual.", "“사흘째나 되어야지요. 그러고 나서야 차차 저절로 스러집니다요."], ["I never knew leeches do any good.\"", "거머리가 무슨 소용이 있는 걸 저는 본 적이 없습니다요.”"]], [["He stayed at home the second day, and then resolved that he would go to his office, black eye and all.", "그는 이튿날도 집에 들어앉아 있었고, 그러고 나서는 멍든 눈이든 무엇이든 그대로 사무실에 나가기로 마음을 정했다."], ["In that morning's newspaper he saw an account of the whole transaction, saying how Mr.", "그날 아침 신문에서 그는 이 일의 전말을 다룬 기사를 보았다."], ["C---- of the office of General Committees, who was soon about to lead to the hymeneal altar the beautiful daughter of the Earl De C----, had been made the subject of a brutal personal attack on the platform of the Great Western Railway Station, and how he was confined to his room from the injuries which he had received.", "머지않아 드 C—— 백작의 아리따운 따님을 혼인의 제단으로 이끌게 될 일반위원회 사무국의 C—— 씨가 그레이트웨스턴 철도역 승강장에서 잔악한 인신 공격의 대상이 되었으며, 그로 인해 입은 부상 탓에 방에 들어앉아 지내고 있다는 것이었다."], ["The paragraph went on to state that the delinquent had, as it was believed, dared to raise his eyes to the same lady, and that his audacity had been treated with scorn by every member of the noble family in question.", "기사는 이어서, 그 범인이 감히 같은 숙녀분에게 눈길을 들었던 것으로 여겨지며 그 무엄함은 문제의 귀족 가문 구성원 모두에게 경멸로써 응징되었다고 적고 있었다."], ["\"It was, however, satisfactory to know,\"", "“그러나 다행스럽게도,”"], ["so said the newspaper, \"that Mr.", "신문은 이렇게 말했다."], ["C---- had amply avenged himself, and had so flogged the young man in question, that he had been unable to stir from his bed since the occurrence.\"", "“C—— 씨는 충분히 앙갚음을 하여 문제의 젊은이를 어찌나 호되게 후려쳤던지, 그자는 사건 이후로 자리에서 일어나지도 못하고 있다고 한다.”"]], [["On reading this Crosbie felt that it would be better that he should show himself at once, and tell as much of the truth as the world would be likely to ascertain at last without his telling.", "이것을 읽고 나서 크로즈비는 차라리 당장 모습을 드러내고, 제가 말하지 않더라도 세상이 결국 알아낼 만한 것까지는 사실대로 이야기해 두는 편이 낫겠다고 느꼈다."], ["So on that third morning he put on his hat and gloves, and had himself taken to his office, though the red-streaky period of his misfortune had hardly even yet come upon him.", "그리하여 그 사흘째 되는 날 아침, 아직 불행의 붉은 줄무늬 시기가 채 찾아오지도 않았건만 그는 모자를 쓰고 장갑을 끼고서 마차를 잡아 사무실로 갔다."], ["The task of walking along the office passage, through the messengers' lobby, and into his room, was very disagreeable.", "사무실 복도를 걸어 사환들의 대기실을 지나 제 방으로 들어가는 일은 여간 거북하지 않았다."], ["Of course everybody looked at him, and of course he failed in his attempt to appear as though he did not mind it. \"Boggs,\"", "물론 모두가 그를 쳐다보았고, 물론 그는 아무렇지 않은 척해 보려던 시도에 실패했다."], ["he said to one of the men as he passed by, \"just see if Mr.", "“보그스.” 그는 지나가면서 사환 하나에게 말했다."], ["Butterwell is in his room,\" and then, as he expected, Mr.", "“버터웰 씨가 방에 계신지 좀 보고 오게.”"], ["Butterwell came to him after the expiration of a few minutes.", "그러자 아니나 다를까, 몇 분이 지나 버터웰 씨가 그를 찾아왔다."]], [["\"Upon my word, that is serious,\"", "“이런, 이건 심하군.”"], ["said Mr. Butterwell, looking into the secretary's damaged face.", "버터웰 씨가 사무국장의 상한 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["\"I don't think I would have come out if I had been you.\"", "“나 같으면 밖에 나오지 않았을 걸세.”"]], [["\"Of course it's disagreeable,\"", "“물론 거북하지요.”"], ["said Crosbie; \"but it's better to put up with it.", "크로즈비가 말했다. “하지만 견디고 마는 편이 낫습니다."], ["Fellows do tell such horrid lies if a man isn't seen for a day or two.", "사람이 하루 이틀 눈에 띄지 않으면 다들 지독한 거짓말을 늘어놓거든요."], ["I believe it's best to put a good face upon it.\"", "좋은 낯을 하고 버티는 게 상책이라고 봅니다.”"]], [["\"That's more than you can do just at present, eh, Crosbie?\"", "“그건 지금 당장 자네가 할 수 있는 일이 아닐 텐데, 안 그런가, 크로즈비?”"], ["And then Mr. Butterwell tittered.", "그러고서 버터웰 씨는 킥킥 웃었다."], ["\"But how on earth did it happen?", "“그런데 대체 어쩌다 이리 됐나?"], ["The paper says that you pretty well killed the fellow who did it.\"", "신문에서는 자네가 그 짓을 한 자를 거의 죽여 놓았다고 하던데.”"]], [["\"The paper lies, as papers always do.", "“신문이란 늘 그렇듯 거짓말입니다."], ["I didn't touch him at all.\"", "나는 그자에게 손도 대지 않았소.”"]], [["\"Didn't you, though?", "“정말 안 그랬단 말인가?"], ["I should like to have had a poke at him after getting such a tap in the face as that.\"", "나 같으면 얼굴에 그만한 걸 한 대 얻어맞고 나서 한 방 쥐어박아 주고 싶었을 텐데.”"]], [["\"The policemen came, and all that sort of thing.", "“경관들이 오고 어쩌고 했지요."], ["One isn't allowed to fight it out in a row of that kind as one would have to do on Salisbury heath.", "그런 소동판에서는 솔즈베리 벌판에서처럼 끝까지 붙어 싸우도록 놔두지 않습니다."], ["Not that I mean to say that I could lick the fellow.", "그렇다고 내가 그자를 때려눕힐 수 있었다는 말은 아니오."], ["How's a man to know whether he can or not?\"", "사람이 제가 이길지 어떨지를 어찌 알겠소?”"]], [["\"How, indeed, unless he gets a licking,--or gives it?", "“정말이지 어찌 알겠나. 한 번 얻어맞아 보거나, 아니면 때려 보기 전에야."], ["But who was he, and what's this about his having been scorned by the noble family?\"", "그런데 그자가 누군가? 그리고 그 귀족 가문에게 경멸을 당했다는 건 또 무슨 소린가?”"]], [["\"Trash and lies, of course.", "“허튼소리에 거짓말이지요."], ["He had never seen any of the De Courcy people.\"", "그자는 드 코시 집안 사람이라고는 하나도 본 적이 없소.”"]], [["\"I suppose the truth is, it was about that other--eh, Crosbie?", "“내 짐작에 사실은 그 다른 쪽 일 때문이었던 게지, 안 그런가, 크로즈비?"], ["I knew you'd find yourself in some trouble before you'd done.\"", "나는 자네가 일을 다 마치기도 전에 무슨 곤경에 빠지고 말 줄 알았네.”"]], [["\"I don't know what it was about, or why he should have made such a brute of himself.", "“그게 무엇 때문이었는지, 왜 그자가 그런 짐승 같은 짓을 했는지 나는 모르겠소."], ["You have heard about those people at Allington?\"", "앨링턴의 그 사람들 이야기는 들으셨겠지요?”"]], [["\"Oh, yes; I have heard about them.\"", "“아, 그럼. 들었네.”"]], [["\"God knows, I didn't mean to say anything against them.", "“맹세코 나는 그 사람들을 두고 무슨 험한 말을 하려던 게 아니오."], ["They knew nothing about it.\"", "그들은 이 일에 대해 아무것도 몰랐소.”"]], [["\"But the young fellow knew them?", "“하지만 그 젊은 친구는 그들을 알고 있었겠지?"], ["Ah, yes, I see all about it.", "아, 그렇지, 이제 다 알겠네."], ["He wants to step into your shoes.", "자네 자리에 제 발을 들이밀고 싶은 게로군."], ["I can't say that he sets about it in a bad way.", "그러자고 나선 방식이 나쁘다고는 말 못 하겠네."], ["But what do you mean to do?\"", "그런데 자네는 어쩔 셈인가?”"]], [["\"Nothing.\"", "“아무것도 하지 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Nothing! Won't that look queer?", "“아무것도 안 한다니!"], ["I think I should have him before the magistrates.\"", "그러면 이상해 보이지 않겠나? 나 같으면 그자를 치안 판사 앞에 세우겠네.”"]], [["\"You see, Butterwell, I am bound to spare that girl's name.", "“보시다시피 버터웰 씨, 나는 그 아가씨의 이름을 지켜야 할 처지요."], ["I know I have behaved badly.\"", "내가 못되게 굴었다는 것은 나도 압니다.”"]], [["\"Well, yes; I fear you have.\"", "“음, 그렇지. 나도 자네가 그랬다고 생각하네.”"]], [["Mr. Butterwell said this with some considerable amount of decision in his voice, as though he did not intend to mince matters, or in any way to hide his opinion.", "버터웰 씨는 이 말을 목소리에 제법 단호함을 실어 했다."], ["Crosbie had got into a way of condemning himself in this matter of his marriage, but was very anxious that others, on hearing such condemnation from him, should say something in the way of palliating his fault.", "에둘러 말할 생각도, 제 의견을 조금이라도 감출 생각도 없다는 투였다. 크로즈비는 이번 혼사에 관한 한 스스로를 나무라는 버릇이 들어 있었으나, 남들이 제 입에서 그런 자책을 듣거든 무언가 제 허물을 덜어 주는 말을 해 주기를 몹시 바라고 있었다."], ["It would be so easy for a friend to remark that such little peccadilloes were not altogether uncommon, and that it would sometimes happen in life that people did not know their own minds.", "벗이라면 그만한 자잘한 과오는 그리 드문 일도 아니라고, 살다 보면 사람이 제 마음을 저도 모르는 일이 더러 있는 법이라고 한마디쯤 해 주기가 얼마나 쉬운가."], ["He had hoped for some such benevolence from Fowler Pratt, but had hoped in vain.", "그는 파울러 프랫에게서 그런 너그러움을 바랐으나 헛일이었다."], ["Butterwell was a good-natured, easy man, anxious to stand well with all about him, never pretending to any very high tone of feeling or of morals; and yet Butterwell would say no word of comfort to him.", "버터웰은 사람 좋고 무던한 사람이었고, 주위 모든 이와 좋게 지내고 싶어 안달이었으며, 감정이나 도덕의 드높은 가락 따위를 내세우는 법이 결코 없는 자였다. 그런 버터웰조차 그에게 위로의 말 한마디를 해 주지 않았다."], ["He could get no one to slur over his sin for him, as though it were no sin,--only an unfortunate mistake; no one but the De Courcys, who had, as it were, taken possession of him and swallowed him alive.", "제 죄를 마치 죄가 아닌 양—그저 운 나쁜 실수일 뿐인 양 슬쩍 뭉개 줄 사람을 그는 아무도 얻을 수 없었다. 말하자면 그를 제 것으로 차지해 산 채로 삼켜 버린 드 코시 집안 말고는 아무도."]], [["\"It can't be helped now,\"", "“이제 와서 어쩔 수 없는 일이지요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"But as for that fellow who made such a brutal attack on me the other morning, he knows that he is safe behind her petticoats.", "“하지만 그날 아침 나에게 그런 짐승 같은 짓을 한 그자로 말하자면, 그는 자기가 그 아가씨의 치맛자락 뒤에 숨어 안전하다는 것을 알고 있소."], ["I can do nothing which would not make some mention of her name necessary.\"", "내가 무슨 짓을 하든 그 아가씨의 이름을 입에 올리지 않고서는 안 되니까요.”"]], [["\"Ah, yes; I see,\"", "“아, 그래, 알겠네.”"], ["said Butterwell.", "버터웰이 말했다."], ["\"It's very unfortunate; very.", "“참으로 딱한 일이야, 딱해."], ["I don't know that I can do anything for you.", "내가 자네에게 무엇을 해 줄 수 있을지 모르겠군."], ["Will you come before the Board to-day?\"", "오늘 위원회에는 나오려나?”"]], [["\"Yes; of course I shall,\"", "“예, 물론 나갑니다.”"], ["said Crosbie, who was becoming very sore.", "크로즈비가 말했다. 그의 마음은 점점 쓰라려 오고 있었다."], ["His sharp ear had told him that all Butterwell's respect and cordiality were gone,--at any rate for the time.", "버터웰의 존경도 다정함도 모조리 사라졌다는 것을—적어도 당분간은 그렇다는 것을 그의 예민한 귀는 알아차렸다."], ["Butterwell, though holding the higher official rank, had always been accustomed to treat him as though he, the inferior, were to be courted.", "버터웰은 관직으로는 더 높은 자리에 있으면서도 늘 그를 마치 아랫사람인 자기가 떠받들어야 할 상대인 양 대해 왔다."], ["He had possessed, and had known himself to possess, in his office as well as in the outside world, a sort of rank much higher than that which from his position he could claim legitimately.", "그는 사무국에서나 바깥세상에서나 제 지위로 마땅히 주장할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 높은 격을 누려 왔고, 스스로도 그것을 알고 있었다. 그런데 이제 그 자리에서 끌어내려지고 있는 것이다."], ["Now he was being deposed. There could be no better touchstone in such a matter than Butterwell.", "이런 일에 버터웰만 한 시금석은 없었다."], ["He would go as the world went, but he would perceive almost intuitively how the world intended to go. \"Tact, tact, tact,\"", "그는 세상이 가는 대로 갈 사람이었으나, 세상이 어느 쪽으로 가려 하는지를 거의 직감으로 알아채는 사람이었다."], ["as he was in the habit of saying to himself when walking along the paths of his Putney villa.", "“눈치, 눈치, 눈치.” 퍼트니 별장의 오솔길을 거닐 때면 그가 스스로에게 되뇌곤 하던 말이다."], ["Crosbie was now secretary, whereas a few months before he had been simply a clerk; but, nevertheless, Mr. Butterwell's instinct told him that Crosbie had fallen.", "크로즈비는 이제 사무국장이었고 몇 달 전만 해도 일개 서기에 지나지 않았지만, 그럼에도 버터웰 씨의 본능은 크로즈비가 떨어졌다고 일러 주었다."], ["Therefore he declined to offer any sympathy to the man in his misfortune, and felt aware, as he left the secretary's room, that it might probably be some time before he visited it again.", "그리하여 그는 이 불행에 빠진 사내에게 어떠한 동정도 건네기를 마다했고, 사무국장실을 나서면서 아마 한동안은 다시 이 방을 찾을 일이 없으리라는 것을 스스로 느끼고 있었다."]], [["Crosbie resolved in his soreness that henceforth he would brazen it out.", "쓰라림 속에서 크로즈비는 이제부터는 뻔뻔하게 밀고 나가리라 마음먹었다."], ["He would go to the Board, with as much indifference as to his black eye as he was able to assume, and if any one said aught to him he would be ready with his answer.", "멍든 눈 따위 아랑곳하지 않는다는 태도를 될 수 있는 대로 꾸며 위원회에 나갈 것이고, 누구든 제게 무슨 말을 걸어오면 대답할 준비가 되어 있을 것이었다."], ["He would go to his club, and let him who intended to show him any slight beware of him in his wrath.", "클럽에도 나갈 것이며, 저를 조금이라도 업신여길 작정인 자가 있거든 성난 그를 조심하는 편이 좋을 것이었다."], ["He could not turn upon John Eames, but he could turn upon others if it were necessary.", "그는 존 임스에게 등을 돌려 덤빌 수는 없었지만, 필요하다면 다른 자들에게 돌아설 수는 있었다."], ["He had not gained for himself a position before the world, and held it now for some years, to allow himself to be crushed at once because he had made a mistake.", "세상 앞에서 제 지위를 얻어 몇 해 동안이나 지켜 온 그가, 한 번 실수를 저질렀다 해서 대뜸 짓뭉개지도록 스스로를 내버려 둘 수는 없었다."], ["If the world, his world, chose to go to war with him, he would be ready for the fight.", "세상이, 그의 세상이 그와 전쟁을 벌이겠다면 그는 그 싸움에 응할 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["As for Butterwell,--Butterwell the incompetent, Butterwell the vapid,--for Butterwell, who in every little official difficulty had for years past come to him, he would let Butterwell know what it was to be thus disloyal to one who had condescended to be his friend.", "버터웰로 말하자면—무능한 버터웰, 맹탕인 버터웰, 지난 몇 해 동안 관청 일에 조그만 어려움만 생겨도 저를 찾아왔던 그 버터웰로 말하자면, 제 벗이 되어 주는 것을 굽어살펴 허락해 준 사람에게 그렇게 의리를 저버리는 것이 어떤 일인지 똑똑히 알게 해 주리라."], ["He would show them all at the Board that he scorned them, and could be their master.", "위원회에서도 저들 모두에게 제가 저들을 경멸한다는 것을, 그리고 제가 저들의 주인 노릇을 할 수 있다는 것을 보여 주리라."], ["Then, too, as he was making some other resolves as to his future conduct, he made one or two resolutions respecting the De Courcy people.", "그러고 나서 그는 앞으로의 처신에 관해 몇 가지 다른 결심을 하는 김에, 드 코시 집안 사람들에 관해서도 한두 가지를 결심했다."], ["He would make it known to them that he was not going to be their very humble servant.", "자기는 그들의 지극히 미천한 종이 될 생각이 없다는 것을 그들에게 알려 주리라. 제 마음을 사뭇 분명한 말로 털어놓으리라."], ["He would speak out his mind with considerable plainness; and if upon that they should choose to break off this \"alliance,\"", "그러고서 저들이 이 “결연”을 깨겠다고 한다면 깨든 말든 마음대로 하라지. 자기는 그 일로 가슴이 찢어지지는 않을 테니까."], ["they might do so; he would not break his heart.", "그렇게 안락의자에 몸을 기대고 이 모든 것을 생각하고 있노라니 한 가지 생각이 그의 머릿속으로 스며들었다."], ["And as he leaned back in his arm-chair, thinking of all this, an idea made its way into his brain,--a floating castle in the air, rather than the image of a thing that might by possibility be realized; and in this castle in the air he saw himself kneeling again at Lily's feet, asking her pardon, and begging that he might once more be taken to her heart.", "그것은 실현될 수도 있는 어떤 것의 모습이라기보다는 차라리 공중에 떠 있는 성이었다. 그리고 그 공중누각 속에서 그는 다시 릴리의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 용서를 빌며, 한 번만 더 그 마음에 받아들여 달라고 애원하는 제 모습을 보았다."]], [["\"Mr. Crosbie is here to-day,\" said Mr. Butterwell to Mr. Optimist.", "“크로즈비 씨가 오늘 나왔습니다.” 버터웰 씨가 옵티미스트 씨에게 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, indeed,\" said Mr.", "“아, 그렇소.”"], ["Optimist, very gravely; for he had heard all about the row at the railway station.", "옵티미스트 씨가 아주 근엄하게 말했다. 그도 철도역에서 있었던 소동을 죄다 들어 알고 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"They've made a monstrous show of him.\"", "“얼굴을 아주 흉측한 구경거리로 만들어 놓았더군요.”"]], [["\"I am very sorry to hear it. It's so--so--so-- If it were one of the younger clerks, you know, we should tell him that it was discreditable to the department.\"", "“그것참 안된 이야기로군. 그건 참… 참… 참…. 젊은 서기들 가운데 하나였다면 부서의 체면을 깎는 일이라고 일러 주었을 텐데.”"]], [["\"If a man gets a blow in the eye, he can't help it, you know.", "“사람이 눈에 한 대 얻어맞은 걸 어쩌겠습니까."], ["He didn't do it himself, I suppose,\"", "제 손으로 그런 건 아닐 텐데요.”"], ["said Major Fiasco.", "피아스코 소령이 말했다."]], [["\"I am well aware that he didn't do it himself,\" continued Mr.", "“그가 제 손으로 그런 게 아니라는 것쯤은 나도 잘 아오.”"], ["Optimist; \"but I really think that, in his position, he should have kept himself out of any such encounter.\"", "옵티미스트 씨가 말을 이었다. “하지만 그만한 자리에 있는 사람이라면 그런 시비판에 휘말리지 말았어야 한다고 나는 정말로 생각하오.”"]], [["\"He would have done so if he could, with all his heart,\"", "“할 수만 있었다면 진심으로 그랬겠지요.”"], ["said the major.", "소령이 말했다."], ["\"I don't suppose he liked being thrashed any better than I should.\"", "“그 사람도 나만큼이나 두들겨 맞는 걸 좋아하지는 않았을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Nobody gives me a black eye,\"", "“나한테 멍든 눈을 안기는 사람은 아무도 없소.”"], ["said Mr. Optimist.", "옵티미스트 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Nobody has as yet,\"", "“아직까지는 없었지요.”"], ["said the major.", "소령이 말했다."]], [["\"I hope they never will,\"", "“앞으로도 영영 없기를 바랍니다.”"], ["said Mr. Butterwell.", "버터웰 씨가 말했다."], ["Then, the hour for their meeting having come round, Mr. Crosbie came into the Board-room.", "그러고서 회의 시각이 돌아오자 크로즈비 씨가 위원회실로 들어왔다."]], [["\"We have been very sorry to hear of this misfortune,\"", "“이 불행한 일을 듣고 우리 모두 몹시 안타까웠소.”"], ["said Mr. Optimist, very gravely.", "옵티미스트 씨가 아주 근엄하게 말했다."]], [["\"Not half so sorry as I have been,\"", "“저만큼 안타깝지야 않으셨겠지요.”"], ["said Crosbie, with a laugh.", "크로즈비가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"It's an uncommon nuisance to have a black eye, and to go about looking like a prize-fighter.\"", "“눈에 멍이 들어 권투 선수 같은 몰골로 돌아다니는 건 여간 성가신 일이 아닙니다.”"]], [["\"And like a prize-fighter that didn't win his battle, too,\"", "“게다가 시합에서 이기지도 못한 권투 선수 같은 몰골이지.”"], ["said Fiasco.", "피아스코가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't know that there's much difference as to that,\"", "“그 점에 무슨 큰 차이가 있는지 저는 모르겠군요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"But the whole thing is a nuisance, and, if you please, we won't say anything more about it.\"", "“아무튼 이 일은 통째로 성가신 노릇이니, 괜찮으시다면 이에 관해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 하지 맙시다.”"]], [["Mr. Optimist almost entertained an opinion that it was his duty to say something more about it.", "옵티미스트 씨는 이에 관해 무언가 더 말하는 것이 제 의무라는 생각을 거의 품고 있었다."], ["Was not he the chief Commissioner, and was not Mr. Crosbie secretary to the Board?", "자기가 수석 위원이 아닌가, 그리고 크로즈비 씨는 위원회의 사무국장이 아닌가?"], ["Ought he, looking at their respective positions, to pass over without a word of notice such a manifest impropriety as this?", "서로의 지위를 생각할 때, 이토록 뚜렷한 온당치 못한 일을 한마디 지적도 없이 넘겨야 옳단 말인가?"], ["Would not Sir Raffle Buffle have said something had Mr. Butterwell, when secretary, come to the office with a black eye?", "버터웰 씨가 사무국장이던 시절에 멍든 눈으로 사무실에 나왔더라면 래플 버플 경이 한마디쯤 하지 않았겠는가?"], ["He wished to exercise all the full rights of a chairman; but, nevertheless, as he looked at the secretary he felt embarrassed, and was unable to find the proper words.", "그는 의장의 온전한 권리를 남김없이 행사하고 싶었다. 그럼에도 사무국장을 바라보고 있노라니 어쩐지 겸연쩍어져서 알맞은 말을 찾을 수가 없었다."], ["\"H--m, ha, well; we'll go to business now, if you please,\"", "“흠, 허, 그럼. 괜찮으시다면 이제 안건으로 들어갑시다.”"], ["he said, as though reserving to himself the right of returning to the secretary's black eye when the more usual business of the Board should be completed.", "그는 이렇게 말했다. 마치 위원회의 통상 업무가 끝나고 나면 사무국장의 멍든 눈으로 되돌아올 권리를 제 몫으로 남겨 두기라도 하는 듯한 투였다."], ["But when the more usual business of the Board had been completed, the secretary left the room without any further reference to his eye.", "그러나 위원회의 통상 업무가 다 끝났을 때, 사무국장은 제 눈에 관한 어떤 언급도 더는 없이 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["Crosbie, when he got back to his own apartment, found Mortimer Gazebee waiting there for him.", "크로즈비가 제 방으로 돌아와 보니 모티머 게이지비가 거기서 그를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["\"My dear fellow,\"", "“여보게.”"], ["said Gazebee, \"this is a very nasty affair.\"", "게이지비가 말했다. “이건 아주 고약한 일일세.”"]], [["\"Uncommonly nasty,\"", "“지독하게 고약하지.”"], ["said Crosbie; \"so nasty that I don't mean to talk about it to anybody.\"", "크로즈비가 말했다. “하도 고약해서 누구와도 이 이야기는 하지 않을 작정이네.”"]], [["\"Lady Amelia is quite unhappy.\"", "“레이디 어밀리아가 아주 언짢아하고 계시네.”"], ["He always called her Lady Amelia, even when speaking of her to his own brothers and sisters.", "그는 제 형제자매에게 아내 이야기를 할 때조차 늘 그녀를 레이디 어밀리아라고 불렀다."], ["He was too well behaved to take the liberty of calling an earl's daughter by her plain Christian name, even though that earl's daughter was his own wife.", "백작의 딸을 세례명만으로 부르는 무례를 범하기에는 그는 지나치게 예의 바른 사람이었다. 그 백작의 딸이 제 아내인데도 그러했다."], ["\"She fears that you have been a good deal hurt.\"", "“자네가 많이 다친 게 아닌가 걱정하시네.”"]], [["\"Not at all hurt; but disfigured, as you see.\"", "“다치기는 조금도 다치지 않았네. 보다시피 얼굴이 볼썽사납게 되었을 뿐이지.”"]], [["\"And so you beat the fellow well that did it?\"", "“그래서 그런 짓을 한 그자를 자네가 실컷 두들겨 주었다지?”"]], [["\"No, I didn't,\" said Crosbie, very angrily.", "“아니, 그러지 않았네.”"], ["\"I didn't beat him at all.", "크로즈비가 몹시 성이 나서 말했다."], ["You don't believe everything you read in the newspapers, do you?\"", "“나는 그자를 전혀 때리지 않았어. 자네는 신문에서 읽은 것을 죄다 믿는가?”"]], [["\"No, I don't believe everything.", "“아니, 죄다 믿지는 않네."], ["Of course I didn't believe about his having aspired to an alliance with Lady Alexandrina.", "물론 그자가 레이디 알렉산드리나와의 결연을 넘본다는 대목은 믿지 않았지."], ["That was untrue, of course.\" Mr.", "그건 물론 사실이 아니었을 테고.”"], ["Gazebee showed by the tone of his voice that imprudence so unparalleled as that was quite incredible.", "게이지비 씨는 그토록 유례없는 무엄함이란 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이라는 것을 목소리의 가락으로 드러내 보였다."]], [["\"You shouldn't believe anything; except this,--that I have got a black eye.\"", "“자네는 아무것도 믿지 말게. 다만 이것만 믿게. 내 눈에 멍이 들었다는 것 말이야.”"]], [["\"You certainly have got that.", "“그것만은 확실히 그렇더군."], ["Lady Amelia thinks you would be more comfortable if you would come up to us this evening.", "레이디 어밀리아께서는 자네가 오늘 저녁 우리 집으로 올라오면 한결 편하겠다고 여기시네."], ["You can't go out, of course; but Lady Amelia said, very good-naturedly, that you need not mind with her.\"", "물론 밖에 나다닐 수야 없겠지만, 당신 앞에서라면 개의치 않아도 된다고 아주 너그럽게 말씀하셨네.”"]], [["\"Thank you, no; I'll come on Sunday.\"", "“고맙지만 됐네. 일요일에 가겠네.”"]], [["\"Of course Lady Alexandrina will be very anxious to hear from her sister; and Lady Amelia begged me very particularly to press you to come.\"", "“레이디 알렉산드리나께서도 물론 언니에게서 소식 듣기를 몹시 기다리실 테고, 레이디 어밀리아께서 특별히 나에게 자네를 꼭 오게 하라고 당부하셨네.”"]], [["\"Thank you, no; not to-day.\"", "“고맙지만 됐네. 오늘은 안 되겠어.”"]], [["\"Why not?\"", "“어째서 안 된다는 건가?”"]], [["\"Oh, simply because I shall be better at home.\"", "“그저 집에 있는 편이 나을 것 같아서지.”"]], [["\"How can you be better at home?", "“집에 있는 편이 어떻게 낫단 말인가?"], ["You can have anything that you want.", "우리 집에서도 원하는 것은 무엇이든 가질 수 있네."], ["Lady Amelia won't mind, you know.\"", "레이디 어밀리아께서는 개의치 않으실 걸세.”"]], [["Another beefsteak to his eye, as he sat in the drawing-room, a cold-water bandage, or any little medical appliance of that sort;--these were the things which Lady Amelia would, in her domestic good nature, condescend not to mind!", "응접실에 앉아 있는 그의 눈에 대라고 날고기 한 점을 더 내주는 일, 찬물 찜질, 혹은 그 비슷한 자잘한 치료 도구 따위—레이디 어밀리아가 살림하는 사람다운 너그러움으로 개의치 않아 주기를 굽어 허락하시겠다는 것이 바로 그런 것들이었다!"]], [["\"I won't trouble her this evening,\"", "“오늘 저녁에는 부인을 번거롭게 해 드리지 않겠네.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, upon my word, I think you're wrong.", "“그래도 참말이지 나는 자네가 잘못 생각한다고 보네."], ["All manner of stories will get down to Courcy Castle, and to the countess's ears; and you don't know what harm may come of it.", "온갖 이야기가 코시 성까지, 백작부인의 귀에까지 흘러 들어갈 걸세. 그러면 무슨 해가 생길지 자네는 모르네."], ["Lady Amelia thinks she had better write and explain it; but she can't do so till she has heard something about it from you.\"", "레이디 어밀리아께서는 당신이 편지를 써서 사정을 설명해 드리는 편이 낫겠다고 여기시지만, 자네에게서 무슨 말이든 듣기 전에는 그러실 수가 없지 않은가.”"]], [["\"Look here, Gazebee.", "“이보게 게이지비."], ["I don't care one straw what story finds its way down to Courcy Castle.\"", "나는 코시 성까지 어떤 이야기가 흘러 들어가든 지푸라기 하나만큼도 개의치 않네.”"]], [["\"But if the earl were to hear anything, and be offended?\"", "“하지만 백작께서 무슨 말을 들으시고 언짢아하시기라도 하면?”"]], [["\"He may recover from his offence as he best likes.\"", "“언짢으신 것에서야 좋으실 대로 회복하시면 되지.”"]], [["\"My dear fellow; that's talking wildly, you know.\"", "“여보게, 그건 함부로 하는 말일세.”"]], [["\"What on earth do you suppose the earl can do to me?", "“대체 백작이 나에게 무엇을 할 수 있다고 생각하나?"], ["Do you think I'm going to live in fear of Lord De Courcy all my life, because I'm going to marry his daughter?", "내가 그분의 따님과 혼인한다고 해서 평생을 드 코시 경을 두려워하며 살 것 같은가? 나는 오늘 알렉산드리나에게 직접 편지를 쓰겠네."], ["I shall write to Alexandrina myself to-day, and you can tell her sister so.", "그 언니에게 그렇게 전해 주게. 일요일에는 저녁 식사 시간에 맞춰 올라가겠네."], ["I'll be up to dinner on Sunday, unless my face makes it altogether out of the question.\"", "이 얼굴 꼴 때문에 아예 말도 안 되는 일이 되지만 않는다면 말일세.”"]], [["\"And you won't come in time for church?\"", "“교회 시간에 맞춰서는 오지 않겠단 말인가?”"]], [["\"Would you have me go to church with such a face as this?\"", "“이런 얼굴을 하고 교회에 나가라는 건가?”"]], [["Then Mr.", "그러고서 모티머 게이지비 씨는 돌아갔고, 집에 이르자 아내에게 크로즈비가 아주 뻣뻣하게 나온다고 일렀다."], ["Mortimer Gazebee went, and when he got home he told his wife that Crosbie was taking things with a high hand.", "“실은 말입니다, 여보."], ["\"The fact is, my dear, that he's ashamed of himself, and therefore tries to put a bold face upon it.\"", "그이는 제 자신이 부끄러운 것이지요. 그래서 저렇게 뱃심 좋은 낯을 지어 보이려 애쓰는 것이고요.”"]], [["\"It was very foolish of him throwing himself in the way of that young man,--very; and so I shall tell him on Sunday.", "“그 젊은이가 다니는 길목에 제 몸을 내던진 건 참으로 어리석은 짓이었소. 참으로 어리석었지."], ["If he chooses to give himself airs to me, I shall make him understand that he is very wrong.", "일요일에 내가 그렇게 일러 주겠소. 나에게까지 그런 티를 내려 든다면, 그이가 아주 잘못하고 있다는 것을 내가 똑똑히 깨우쳐 주겠소."], ["He should remember now that the way in which he conducts himself is a matter of moment to all our family.\"", "이제는 제 처신이 우리 집안 모두에게 중대한 일이라는 것을 명심해야 하오.”"]], [["\"Of course he should,\"", "“물론 그래야지요.”"], ["said Mr. Gazebee.", "게이지비 씨가 말했다."]], [["When the Sunday came the red-streaky period had arrived, but had by no means as yet passed away.", "일요일이 되자 붉은 줄무늬 시기가 찾아와 있었으나 아직 결코 물러가지는 않은 상태였다."], ["The men at the office had almost become used to it; but Crosbie, in spite of his determination to go down to the club, had not yet shown himself elsewhere.", "사무실 사람들은 거의 그 얼굴에 익숙해져 있었다. 그러나 크로즈비는 클럽에 나가겠다던 결심에도 불구하고 아직 다른 데에는 모습을 보이지 않고 있었다."], ["Of course he did not go to church, but at five he made his appearance at the house in St.", "물론 교회에도 가지 않았고, 다섯 시가 되어서야 세인트존스우드의 그 집에 나타났다."], ["John's Wood. They always dined at five on Sundays, having some idea that by doing so they kept the Sabbath better than they would have done had they dined at seven.", "그 집에서는 일요일이면 늘 다섯 시에 저녁을 먹었는데, 일곱 시에 먹는 것보다 그렇게 하는 편이 안식일을 더 잘 지키는 것이라는 생각이 어느 정도 있었기 때문이다."], ["If keeping the Sabbath consists in going to bed early, or is in any way assisted by such a practice, they were right.", "안식일을 지키는 것이 일찍 잠자리에 드는 데 있다면, 혹은 그런 관습이 어떤 식으로든 그것을 돕는다면 그들이 옳았다."], ["To the cook that semi-early dinner might perhaps be convenient, as it gave her an excuse for not going to church in the afternoon, as the servants'", "요리사에게는 그 어중간하게 이른 저녁 식사가 아마 편리했을 것이다. 오후에 교회에 가지 않을 구실이 되어 주었을 테니 말이다."], ["and children's dinner gave her a similar excuse in the morning.", "하인들과 아이들의 식사가 아침나절에 똑같은 구실이 되어 주었던 것처럼."], ["Such little attempts at goodness,--proceeding half the way, or perhaps, as in this instance, one quarter of the way, on the disagreeable path towards goodness,--are very common with respectable people, such as Lady Amelia.", "이런 자잘한 선행의 시도들—선을 향한 그 성가신 길을 절반쯤, 혹은 이번처럼 사분의 일쯤 걸어가 보는 시도들—은 레이디 어밀리아 같은 점잖은 사람들에게 아주 흔한 일이다."], ["If she would have dined at one o'clock, and have eaten cold meat, one perhaps might have felt that she was entitled to some praise.", "만일 그녀가 한 시에 식사를 하고 찬 고기를 먹었더라면, 아마 얼마쯤은 칭찬받을 자격이 있다고 느껴 볼 만도 했으리라."]], [["\"Dear, dear, dear; this is very sad, isn't it, Adolphus?\"", "“저런, 저런, 저런. 이거 참 딱하게 되었구려, 어돌퍼스.”"], ["she said on first seeing him.", "그를 처음 보자마자 그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, it is sad, Amelia,\"", "“그렇습니다, 딱한 일이지요, 어밀리아.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["He always called her Amelia, because she called him Adolphus; but Gazebee himself was never quite pleased when he heard it.", "그는 늘 그녀를 어밀리아라고 불렀는데, 그녀가 그를 어돌퍼스라 불렀기 때문이다. 다만 게이지비 자신은 그 소리를 들을 때마다 결코 썩 유쾌하지 않았다."], ["Lady Amelia was older than Crosbie, and entitled to call him anything she liked; but he should have remembered the great difference in their rank.", "레이디 어밀리아는 크로즈비보다 나이가 많았으니 그를 무어라 부르든 그럴 자격이 있었지만, 그로서는 두 사람의 지체가 크게 다르다는 것을 기억했어야 마땅했다."], ["\"It is sad, Amelia,\"", "“딱한 일입니다, 어밀리아.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"But will you oblige me in one thing?\"", "“그런데 한 가지만 들어주시겠습니까?”"]], [["\"What thing, Adolphus?\"", "“무슨 일이오, 어돌퍼스?”"]], [["\"Not to say a word more about it.", "“이 일에 대해 더는 한마디도 하지 말아 주십시오."], ["The black eye is a bad thing, no doubt, and has troubled me much; but the sympathy of my friends has troubled me a great deal more.", "멍든 눈은 물론 고약한 것이고, 그 때문에 저도 어지간히 시달렸습니다. 하지만 벗들의 동정에는 그보다 훨씬 더 시달렸습니다."], ["I had all the family commiseration from Gazebee on Friday, and if it is repeated again, I shall lie down and die.\"", "금요일에 게이지비에게서 온 집안의 위문을 한꺼번에 다 받았으니, 그것이 또 되풀이된다면 저는 그만 드러누워 죽고 말 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Shall 'oo die, uncle Dolphus, 'cause 'oo've got a bad eye?\"", "“돌퍼스 삼춘, 눈이 아야 해서 죽는 거야?”"], ["asked De Courcy Gazebee, the eldest hope of the family, looking up into his face.", "집안의 맏이 되는 희망인 드 코시 게이지비가 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 물었다."]], [["\"No, my hero,\"", "“아니란다, 나의 용사님.”"], ["said Crosbie, taking the boy up into his arms, \"not because I've got a black eye.", "크로즈비가 아이를 두 팔로 안아 올리며 말했다. “눈에 멍이 들었다고 죽지는 않아."], ["There isn't very much harm in that, and you'll have a great many before you leave school.", "그런 건 별로 해로울 것도 없고, 너도 학교를 마치기 전에 아주 여러 번 멍이 들 거란다."], ["But because the people will go on talking about it.\"", "다만 사람들이 자꾸만 그 이야기를 해 대니까 그렇지.”"]], [["\"But aunt Dina on't like 'oo, if oo've got an ugly bad eye.\"", "“그치만 눈이 미운 아야가 되면 디나 이모가 삼춘 안 좋아할걸.”"]], [["\"But, Adolphus,\"", "“하지만 어돌퍼스.”"], ["said Lady Amelia, settling herself for an argument, \"that's all very well, you know--and I'm sure I'm very sorry to cause you any annoyance,--but really one doesn't know how to pass over such a thing without speaking of it.", "레이디 어밀리아가 한바탕 따져 볼 자세를 고쳐 잡으며 말했다. “그 말도 다 좋소만, 나도 그대에게 성가신 일을 만들어 주는 것이 참으로 미안하기는 하오."], ["I have had a letter from mamma.\"", "그렇지만 이런 일을 두고 아무 말도 하지 않고 그냥 넘어가는 법이 어디 있는지 정말 모르겠구려. 어머니께 편지를 받았소.”"]], [["\"I hope Lady De Courcy is quite well.\"", "“드 코시 백작부인께서 안녕하시기를 바랍니다.”"]], [["\"Quite well, thank you.", "“아주 안녕하시오, 고맙소."], ["But as a matter of course she is very anxious about this affair.", "하지만 당연한 일이지만 이번 일을 몹시 염려하고 계시오."], ["She had read what has been said in the newspapers, and it may be necessary that Mortimer should take it up, as the family solicitor.\"", "신문에 실린 이야기를 읽으셨으니, 집안 고문 변호사로서 모티머가 이 일을 맡아야 할지도 모르겠소.”"]], [["\"Quite out of the question,\"", "“그건 어림도 없는 일입니다.”"], ["said Adolphus.", "어돌퍼스가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't think I should advise any such step as that,\"", "“나도 그런 조치는 권하지 않겠습니다.”"], ["said Gazebee.", "게이지비가 말했다."]], [["\"Perhaps not; very likely not.", "“아마 그렇겠지요. 아마도 그럴 거요."], ["But you cannot be surprised, Mortimer, that my mother under such circumstances should wish to know what are the facts of the case.\"", "하지만 모티머, 어머니께서 이런 사정에 처하여 이 일의 사실이 무엇인지 알고 싶어 하시는 것이야 놀랄 일이 아니잖소.”"]], [["\"Not at all surprised,\"", "“전혀 놀랄 일이 아니지요.”"], ["said Gazebee.", "게이지비가 말했다."]], [["\"Then once for all, I'll tell you the facts.", "“그러면 마지막으로 한 번, 제가 사실을 말씀드리지요."], ["As I got out of the train a man I'd seen once before in my life made an attack upon me, and before the police came up, I got a blow in the face.", "제가 기차에서 내리는데 평생에 딱 한 번 본 적이 있는 사내가 저에게 달려들었고, 경관들이 오기 전에 저는 얼굴에 한 대 얻어맞았습니다."], ["Now you know all about it.\"", "이제 다 아신 겁니다.”"]], [["At that moment dinner was announced.", "바로 그때 저녁 식사가 준비되었다는 전갈이 왔다."], ["\"Will you give Lady Amelia your arm?\"", "“레이디 어밀리아께 팔을 내어 드리겠나?”"], ["said the husband.", "남편이 말했다."]], [["\"It's a very sad occurrence,\"", "“참으로 딱한 일이오.”"], ["said Lady Amelia with a slight toss of her head, \"and, I'm afraid, will cost my sister a great deal of vexation.\"", "레이디 어밀리아가 고개를 살짝 젖히며 말했다. “게다가 내 동생에게 적잖은 속상함을 치르게 할 것 같아 걱정이구려.”"]], [["\"You agree with De Courcy, do you, that aunt Dina won't like me with an ugly black eye?\"", "“드 코시의 말에 동의하시는군요. 제 눈이 이렇게 밉게 멍이 들었으니 디나 이모가 저를 좋아하지 않을 거라는 말 말입니다.”"]], [["\"I really don't think it's a joking matter,\"", "“나는 이게 정말이지 농담할 일이라고 생각하지 않소.”"], ["said the Lady Amelia.", "레이디 어밀리아가 말했다."], ["And then there was nothing more said about it during the dinner.", "그러고서 식사가 이어지는 동안에는 이에 관해 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았다."]], [["There was nothing more said about it during the dinner, but it was plain enough from Lady Amelia's countenance that she was not very well pleased with her future brother-in-law's conduct.", "식사가 이어지는 동안에는 이에 관해 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았으나, 레이디 어밀리아가 장차 제부가 될 사람의 처신을 그리 탐탁잖게 여긴다는 것은 그 낯빛만 보아도 넉넉히 알 수 있었다."], ["She was very hospitable to him, pressing him to eat; but even in doing that she made repeated little references to his present unfortunate state.", "그녀는 그에게 대단히 후하게 굴어 자꾸만 음식을 권했다. 그러나 그렇게 하면서도 그의 지금 딱한 처지를 잘게잘게 거듭 들먹였다."], ["She told him that she did not think fried plum-pudding would be bad for him, but that she would recommend him not to drink port-wine after dinner.", "그녀는 튀긴 자두 푸딩은 그에게 해롭지 않을 듯하나 식후에 포트와인은 마시지 않기를 권하겠다고 그에게 일렀다. “그건 그렇고 모티머, 클라레를 좀 올려 오게 하는 편이 낫겠소.”"], ["\"By-the-by, Mortimer, you'd better have some claret up,\"", "그녀가 한마디 덧붙였다."], ["she remarked. \"Adolphus shouldn't take anything that is heating.\"", "“어돌퍼스는 몸에 열을 올리는 것은 무엇도 들지 말아야 해요.”"]], [["\"Thank you,\"", "“고맙습니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"I'll have some brandy-and-water, if Gazebee will give it me.\"", "“저는 게이지비가 주기만 한다면 브랜디에 물을 탄 것을 마시겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Brandy-and-water!\"", "“브랜디에 물이라니!”"], ["said Lady Amelia.", "레이디 어밀리아가 말했다."], ["Crosbie in truth was not given to the drinking of brandy-and-water; but he was prepared to call for raw gin, if he were driven much further by Lady Amelia's solicitude.", "크로즈비는 사실 브랜디에 물을 타 마시는 사람이 아니었다. 그러나 레이디 어밀리아의 걱정에 조금만 더 시달렸다가는 맹진을 가져오라고 할 참이었다."]], [["At these Sunday dinners the mistress of the house never went away into the drawing-room, and the tea was always brought into them at the table on which they had dined.", "이 일요일 저녁 식탁에서는 안주인이 결코 응접실로 물러가는 법이 없었고, 차도 늘 식사를 한 그 식탁 위로 들여왔다."], ["It was another little step towards keeping holy the first day of the week.", "그것 또한 한 주의 첫날을 거룩히 지키기 위한 자잘한 한 걸음이었다."], ["When Lady Rosina was there, she was indulged with the sight of six or seven solid good books which were laid upon the mahogany as soon as the bottles were taken off it.", "레이디 로지나가 와 있을 때면 그녀는 병들이 치워지자마자 마호가니 위에 놓이는 묵직하고 훌륭한 책 예닐곱 권을 보는 호사를 누렸다."], ["At her first prolonged visit she had obtained for herself the privilege of reading a sermon; but as on such occasions both Lady Amelia and Mr.", "처음으로 오래 머물렀을 때 그녀는 설교문을 낭독하는 특권을 스스로 따내기까지 했다."], ["Gazebee would go to sleep,--and as the footman had also once shown a tendency that way,--the sermon had been abandoned.", "그러나 그럴 때면 레이디 어밀리아도 게이지비 씨도 잠이 들었고—하인까지도 한 번은 그런 낌새를 보였으므로—설교문은 그만두게 되었다."], ["But the master of the house, on these evenings, when his sister-in-law was present, was doomed to sit in idleness, or else to find solace in one of the solid good books.", "다만 처제가 와 있는 저녁이면 이 집 주인은 하릴없이 앉아 있거나, 아니면 그 묵직하고 훌륭한 책 가운데 하나에서 위안을 찾는 형벌을 받아야 했다."], ["But Lady Rosina just now was in the country, and therefore the table was left unfurnished.", "그런데 레이디 로지나는 지금 시골에 가 있었으므로 식탁 위는 비어 있었다."]], [["\"And what am I to say to my mother?\"", "“그러면 나는 어머니께 무어라 말씀드려야 하오?”"], ["said Lady Amelia, when they were alone.", "그들만 남게 되었을 때 레이디 어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Give her my kindest regards,\" said Crosbie.", "“제 안부를 각별히 전해 주십시오.”"], ["It was quite clear, both to the husband and to the wife, that he was preparing himself for rebellion against authority.", "크로즈비가 말했다. 그가 권위에 맞서 반역할 채비를 하고 있다는 것은 남편에게도 아내에게도 아주 분명했다."]], [["For some ten minutes there was nothing said.", "한 십 분 동안은 아무 말도 오가지 않았다."], ["Crosbie amused himself by playing with the boy whom he called Dicksey, by way of a nickname for De Courcy.", "크로즈비는 드 코시를 줄여 딕시라고 부르며 그 사내아이와 놀아 주는 것으로 시간을 보냈다."]], [["\"Mamma, he calls me Dicksey.", "“엄마, 삼춘이 나더러 딕시래."], ["Am I Dicksey?", "내가 딕시야?"], ["I'll call 'oo old Cross, and then aunt Dina 'on't like 'oo.\"", "나는 삼춘을 늙다리 크로스라고 부를 거야. 그럼 디나 이모가 삼춘 안 좋아할걸.”"]], [["\"I wish you would not call the child nicknames, Adolphus.", "“아이에게 별명을 붙이지 말아 주었으면 좋겠소, 어돌퍼스."], ["It seems as though you would wish to cast a slur upon the one which he bears.\"", "그 아이가 지닌 이름에 흠집을 내고 싶어 하는 것처럼 보이는구려.”"]], [["\"I should hardly think that he would feel disposed to do that,\"", "“설마 그이가 그럴 마음이야 먹겠습니까.”"], ["said Mr. Gazebee.", "게이지비 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Hardly, indeed,\"", "“설마 그렇겠습니까.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"It has never yet been disgraced in the annals of our country by being made into a nickname,\"", "“그 이름은 우리나라 역사에서 아직껏 별명 따위가 되어 욕을 본 적이 없소.”"], ["said the proud daughter of the house.", "이 집안의 도도한 따님이 말했다."], ["She was probably unaware that among many of his associates her father had been called Lord De Curse'ye, from the occasional energy of his language.", "그녀는 아마 알지 못했겠지만, 그녀의 아버지는 이따금 그 입에서 나오는 말이 어찌나 기운찼던지 지인들 사이에서는 「욕쟁이 드 코시 경」이라 불려 왔다."], ["\"And any such attempt is painful in my ears.", "“그리고 그런 시도는 무엇이든 내 귀에 거슬리는구려."], ["I think something of my family, I can assure you, Adolphus, and so does my husband.\"", "나는 내 집안을 제법 소중히 여긴다오, 정말이오, 어돌퍼스. 내 남편도 그렇고.”"]], [["\"A very great deal,\"", "“아주 대단히 그렇습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Gazebee.", "게이지비 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"So do I of mine,\"", "“저도 제 집안을 소중히 여깁니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"That's natural to all of us.", "“그건 우리 모두에게 자연스러운 일이지요."], ["One of my ancestors came over with William the Conqueror.", "제 조상 가운데 한 분은 정복왕 윌리엄과 함께 바다를 건너오셨습니다."], ["I think he was one of the assistant cooks in the king's tent.\"", "아마 왕의 천막에서 요리사를 거들던 사람 가운데 하나였을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"A cook!\"", "“요리사래!”"], ["said young De Courcy.", "어린 드 코시가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, my boy, a cook.", "“그렇단다, 얘야, 요리사였지."], ["That was the way most of our old families were made noble.", "우리 옛 가문들은 대개 그런 식으로 귀족이 된 거란다."], ["They were cooks, or butlers to the kings--or sometimes something worse.\"", "왕의 요리사거나 집사였지. 더러는 그보다 못한 것이기도 했고.”"]], [["\"But your family isn't noble?\"", "“그치만 삼춘네 집안은 귀족이 아니잖아?”"]], [["\"No--I'll tell you how that was.", "“아니지. 그게 어찌 된 일인지 내가 말해 주마."], ["The king wanted this cook to poison half-a-dozen of his officers who wished to have a way of their own; but the cook said, 'No, my Lord King; I am a cook, not an executioner.'", "왕은 제 마음대로 하겠다고 나선 신하 예닐곱을 이 요리사더러 독살하라고 했단다. 그런데 요리사가 말했지."], ["So they sent him into the scullery, and when they called all the other servants barons and lords, they only called him Cookey.", "‘아니 되옵니다, 임금님. 저는 요리사이지 망나니가 아닙니다.’ 그래서 사람들은 그를 설거지방으로 쫓아 보냈고, 다른 하인들은 모두 남작이니 경이니 부르면서 그이만은 그저 쿠키라고 불렀단다."], ["They've changed the name to Crosbie since that, by degrees.\"", "그 뒤로 그 이름이 차차 바뀌어 크로즈비가 된 거지.”"]], [["Mr. Gazebee was awestruck, and the face of the Lady Amelia became very dark.", "게이지비 씨는 아연실색했고, 레이디 어밀리아의 얼굴은 아주 어두워졌다."], ["Was it not evident that this snake, when taken into their innermost bosoms that they might there warm him, was becoming an adder, and preparing to sting them?", "이 뱀을 품속 가장 깊은 곳에 들여 따뜻하게 데워 주었더니, 그것이 독사가 되어 저희를 물려고 채비하는 것이 뻔하지 않은가?"], ["There was very little more conversation that evening, and soon after the story of the cook, Crosbie got up and went away to his own home.", "그날 저녁에는 그 뒤로 오간 이야기가 거의 없었고, 요리사 이야기가 끝나고 얼마 지나지 않아 크로즈비는 자리에서 일어나 제 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVI. \"SEE, THE CONQUERING HERO COMES.\"", "# 제36장. 「보라, 개선하는 영웅이 온다」"]], [["John Eames had reached his office precisely at twelve o'clock, but when he did so he hardly knew whether he was standing on his heels or his head.", "존 임스는 정확히 열두 시에 사무국에 닿았지만, 막상 닿고 보니 자기가 발로 서 있는지 머리로 서 있는지도 분간이 되지 않았다."], ["The whole morning had been to him one of intense excitement, and latterly, to a certain extent, one of triumph.", "그날 아침 내내 그는 격렬한 흥분 속에 있었고, 나중에는 어느 정도 개선의 기분에까지 젖어 있었다."], ["But he did not at all know what might be the results.", "그러나 그 결과가 어찌 될지는 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["Would he be taken before a magistrate and locked up?", "치안판사 앞에 끌려가 갇히게 될까?"], ["Would there be a row at the office?", "사무국에서 소동이 벌어질까?"], ["Would Crosbie call him out, and, if so, would it be incumbent on him to fight a duel with pistols?", "크로즈비가 결투를 신청해 올까, 그렇게 되면 권총으로 결투를 치르는 것이 제 의무가 될까?"], ["What would Lord De Guest say--Lord De Guest, who had specially warned him not to take upon himself the duty of avenging Lily's wrongs?", "드 게스트 백작은 무어라 할까—릴리가 당한 일의 원수를 갚는 노릇을 스스로 떠맡지 말라고 특별히 일러 두었던 그 드 게스트 백작이."], ["What would all the Dale family say of his conduct?", "데일 집안 사람들은 모두 그의 행동을 두고 무어라 할까?"], ["And, above all, what would Lily say and think?", "그리고 무엇보다도, 릴리는 무어라 말하고 어떻게 생각할까?"], ["Nevertheless, the feeling of triumph was predominant; and now, at this interval of time, he was beginning to remember with pleasure the sensation of his fist as it went into Crosbie's eye.", "그럼에도 개선의 기분이 우세했다. 그리고 이제 이만큼 시간이 흐르고 보니, 그는 제 주먹이 크로즈비의 눈으로 들어가던 그 감촉을 흐뭇하게 떠올리기 시작하고 있었다."]], [["During his first day at the office he heard nothing about the affair, nor did he say a word of it to any one.", "사무국에 나간 첫날 그는 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 듣지 못했고, 자기도 누구에게 한마디 하지 않았다."], ["It was known in his room that he had gone down to spend his Christmas holiday with Lord De Guest, and he was treated with some increased consideration accordingly.", "그가 드 게스트 백작과 성탄절 휴가를 보내러 내려갔다는 것은 그의 방에서도 알려져 있었고, 그래서 그는 그만큼 전보다 얼마쯤 대접을 받았다."], ["And, moreover, I must explain, in order that I may give Johnny Eames his due, he was gradually acquiring for himself a good footing among the Income-tax officials.", "게다가 조니 임스에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려주기 위해 나는 이렇게 설명해 두어야겠다. 그는 소득세 사무국 관리들 사이에서 차츰 제 자리를 잡아 가고 있었다."], ["He knew his work, and did it with some manly confidence in his own powers, and also with some manly indifference to the occasional frowns of the mighty men of the department.", "그는 제 일을 알았고, 제 능력에 대한 사내다운 자신감을 가지고, 또한 부서의 높으신 어른들이 이따금 찌푸리는 낯빛에 대한 사내다운 무심함을 가지고 그 일을 해냈다."], ["He was, moreover, popular--being somewhat of a radical in his official demeanour, and holding by his own rights, even though mighty men should frown.", "더구나 그는 인기가 있었다—관청에서의 처신이 다소 급진적인 데다, 높으신 어른들이 낯을 찌푸려도 제 권리를 굽히지 않았기 때문이다."], ["In truth, he was emerging from his hobbledehoyhood and entering upon his young manhood, having probably to go through much folly and some false sentiment in that period of his existence, but still with fair promise of true manliness beyond, to those who were able to read the signs of his character.", "사실 그는 풋내기 시절에서 벗어나 젊은 사내의 시절로 들어서는 중이었고, 그 시기에 아마 숱한 어리석음과 얼마간의 거짓된 감상을 겪어야 했겠지만, 그의 성품의 조짐을 읽을 줄 아는 이들에게는 그 너머에 참된 사내다움이 있으리라는 좋은 기약이 여전히 보였다."]], [["Many questions on that first day were asked him about the glories of his Christmas, but he had very little to say on the subject.", "그 첫날 그는 성탄절의 영광에 대해 여러 질문을 받았으나, 그 일에 대해서는 할 말이 별로 없었다."], ["Indeed nothing could have been much more commonplace than his Christmas visit, had it not been for the one great object which had taken him down to that part of the country, and for the circumstance with which his holiday had been ended.", "실제로 그를 그 지방으로 내려가게 한 하나의 큰 목적과, 휴가가 끝나던 마당의 그 사건이 아니었더라면, 그의 성탄절 방문만큼 평범한 것도 없었을 터였다. 그 두 가지 중 어느 것에 대해서도 그는 터놓고 말할 마음이 없었다."], ["On neither of these subjects was he disposed to speak openly; but as he walked home to Burton Crescent with Cradell, he did tell him of the affair with Crosbie.", "그러나 크래들과 함께 버턴 크레슨트로 걸어 돌아가면서 그는 크로즈비와의 일을 크래들에게 이야기했다."]], [["\"And you went in at him on the station?\"", "“그래서 역에서 그자한테 달려들었단 말인가?”"], ["asked Cradell, with admiring doubt.", "크래들이 감탄 어린 의심을 담아 물었다."]], [["\"Yes, I did.", "“그래, 그랬지."], ["If I didn't do it there, where was I to do it?", "거기서 안 했으면 어디서 했겠나?"], ["I'd said I would, and therefore when I saw him I did it.\"", "하겠다고 말해 두었으니, 그자를 본 김에 한 거야.”"], ["Then the whole affair was told as to the black eye, the police, and the superintendent.", "그러고서 시퍼렇게 멍든 눈이며 순경들이며 경감이며 하는 이야기가 죄다 나왔다."], ["\"And what's to come next?\"", "“그런데 이제 어떻게 되는 거지?”"], ["asked our hero.", "우리의 주인공이 물었다."]], [["\"Well, he'll put it in the hands of a friend, of course; as I did with Fisher in that affair with Lupex.", "“뭐, 물론 그자야 친구 손에 맡기겠지. 나도 루펙스와의 그 일 때 피셔에게 그랬으니까."], ["And, upon my word, Johnny, I shall have to do something of the kind again.", "그리고 정말이지, 조니, 나도 또 한 번 그런 걸 해야 할 판이야."], ["His conduct last night was outrageous; would you believe it--\"", "어젯밤 그자의 행실은 언어도단이었어. 자네 믿겠나—”"]], [["\"Oh, he's a fool.\"", "“아, 그자는 바보야.”"]], [["\"He's a fool you wouldn't like to meet when he's in one of his mad fits, I can tell you that.", "“그자가 광증이 도졌을 땐 자네도 마주치고 싶지 않을 그런 바보라고, 내 장담하지."], ["I absolutely had to sit up in my own bedroom all last night.", "어젯밤엔 꼼짝없이 내 침실에만 앉아 있어야 했네."], ["Mother Roper told me that if I remained in the drawing-room she would feel herself obliged to have a policeman in the house.", "로퍼 아줌마가 그러더군, 내가 응접실에 그대로 있으면 자기는 집에 순경을 불러들일 수밖에 없겠다고."], ["What could I do, you know?", "낸들 어쩌겠나?"], ["I made her have a fire for me, of course.\"", "물론 아줌마더러 불은 지펴 달라고 했지.”"]], [["\"And then you went to bed.\"", "“그러고서 잠자리에 들었고.”"]], [["\"I waited ever so long, because I thought that Maria would want to see me.", "“한참을 기다렸네. 머라이어가 나를 보고 싶어 할 거라고 생각했거든."], ["At last she sent me a note.", "마침내 그이가 쪽지를 보내왔지."], ["Maria is so imprudent, you know.", "머라이어는 어찌나 경솔한지 몰라."], ["If he had found anything in her writing, it would have been terrible, you know,--quite terrible.", "그자가 그이의 필적으로 된 것을 무어라도 찾아냈다면 끔찍했을 거야—정말 끔찍했을 거야."], ["And who can say whether Jemima mayn't tell?\"", "게다가 제미마가 고자질하지 않으리라고 누가 장담하겠나?”"]], [["\"And what did she say?\"", "“그래서 무어라고 썼는데?”"]], [["\"Come; that's tellings, Master Johnny.", "“이런, 그건 발설할 수 없지, 조니 도련님."], ["I took very good care to take it with me to the office this morning, for fear of accidents.\"", "무슨 일이 생길까 봐 오늘 아침 사무실에 아주 잘 챙겨 갔다네.”"]], [["But Eames was not so widely awake to the importance of his friend's adventures as he might have been had he not been weighted with adventures of his own.", "그러나 임스는 제 모험거리를 잔뜩 짊어지고 있지만 않았더라면 기울였을 만큼, 벗의 모험에 그리 마음을 크게 쓰고 있지 못했다."]], [["\"I shouldn't care so much,\"", "“나는 그 크로즈비란 작자가 친구를 내세우는 건 그리 대수롭지 않네만,”"], ["said he, \"about that fellow Crosbie going to a friend, as I should about his going to a police magistrate.\"", "그가 말했다. “경찰 치안판사한테 가는 건 그렇지가 않아.”"]], [["\"He'll put it in a friend's hands, of course,\"", "“물론 친구 손에 맡길 걸세,”"], ["said Cradell, with the air of a man who from experience was well up in such matters.", "크래들이 그런 일이라면 겪어 봐서 훤하다는 사람의 태도로 말했다."], ["\"And I suppose you'll naturally come to me.", "“그러면 자네야 당연히 내게 오겠지."], ["It's a deuced bore to a man in a public office, and all that kind of thing, of course.", "관청에 다니는 사람한테는 지독히 성가신 일이고 뭐 그렇긴 하지만 말이야."], ["But I'm not the man to desert my friend.", "그래도 나는 벗을 저버릴 사람이 아니야."], ["I'll stand by you, Johnny, my boy.\"", "내가 자네 곁에 서 주겠네, 조니, 이 사람아.”"]], [["\"Oh, thank you,\"", "“아, 고맙네,”"], ["said Eames, \"I don't think that I shall want that.\"", "임스가 말했다. “그럴 일까지는 없을 것 같은데.”"]], [["\"You must be ready with a friend, you know.\"", "“친구는 하나 준비해 두어야 하는 법이야, 알겠나.”"]], [["\"I should write down to a man I know in the country, and ask his advice,\"", "“나는 시골에 아는 어른께 편지를 써서 조언을 구할 참이야,” 임스가 말했다."], ["said Eames; \"an older sort of friend, you know.\"", "“좀 나이 지긋한 쪽의 친구 말일세.”"]], [["\"By Jove, old fellow, take care what you're about.", "“이런, 이 사람아, 하는 일을 조심하게."], ["Don't let them say of you that you show the white feather.", "자네가 겁쟁이 깃털을 보였다는 소리는 듣지 않도록 해."], ["Upon my honour, I'd sooner have anything said of me than that.", "명예를 걸고 하는 말인데, 나라면 그런 소리를 듣느니 차라리 무슨 소리를 듣든 나을 거야."], ["I would, indeed,--anything.\"", "정말이지—무슨 소리든.”"]], [["\"I'm not afraid of that,\"", "“그건 걱정 없네,”"], ["said Eames, with a touch of scorn in his voice.", "임스가 목소리에 얼마간 경멸을 담아 말했다."], ["\"There isn't much thought about white feathers now-a-days,--not in the way of fighting duels.\"", "“요즘은 겁쟁이 깃털 따위를 그리 따지지 않아—적어도 결투를 하고 말고 하는 문제로는.”"]], [["After that, Cradell managed to carry back the conversation to Mrs. Lupex and his own peculiar position, and as Eames did not care to ask from his companion further advice in his own matters, he listened nearly in silence till they reached Burton Crescent.", "그 뒤로 크래들은 어떻게든 이야기를 루펙스 부인과 제 자신의 묘한 처지 쪽으로 되돌려 놓았고, 임스는 제 일에 대해 이 길동무에게 더 조언을 구할 마음이 없었으므로 버턴 크레슨트에 이를 때까지 거의 잠자코 듣고만 있었다."]], [["\"I hope you found the noble earl well,\" said Mrs.", "“그 훌륭하신 백작님께서는 안녕하시던가요?”"], ["Roper to him, as soon as they were all seated at dinner.", "다들 저녁 식탁에 앉자마자 로퍼 부인이 그에게 말했다."]], [["\"I found the noble earl pretty well, thank you,\"", "“그 훌륭하신 백작님께서는 그런대로 안녕하시더군요, 고맙습니다,”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["It had become plainly understood by all the Roperites that Eames's position was quite altered since he had been honoured with the friendship of Lord De Guest. Mrs.", "임스의 처지가 드 게스트 백작의 우정을 얻는 영광을 누린 이래 아주 달라졌다는 것은 로퍼네 사람들 모두에게 뻔히 이해되고 있었다."], ["Lupex, next to whom he always sat at dinner, with a view to protecting her as it were from the dangerous neighbourhood of Cradell, treated him with a marked courtesy.", "저녁마다 그가 곁에 앉는 루펙스 부인은—크래들이라는 위험한 이웃으로부터 이를테면 그이를 지켜 준다는 뜻에서 그 자리였는데—그를 눈에 띄게 정중히 대했다."], ["Miss Spruce always called him \"sir.\" Mrs.", "스프루스 양은 늘 그를 “선생님”이라고 불렀다."], ["Roper helped him the first of the gentlemen, and was mindful about his fat and gravy, and Amelia felt less able than she was before to insist upon the possession of his heart and affections.", "로퍼 부인은 신사들 가운데 그에게 제일 먼저 음식을 덜어 주었고 그의 몫의 기름진 부위와 육즙을 잊지 않았으며, 어밀리아는 전처럼 그의 마음과 애정을 제 것이라고 우겨 댈 수가 없다고 느꼈다."], ["It must not be supposed that Amelia intended to abandon the fight, and allow the enemy to walk off with his forces; but she felt herself constrained to treat him with a deference that was hardly compatible with the perfect equality which should attend any union of hearts.", "그렇다고 어밀리아가 싸움을 그만두고 적이 병력을 끌고 물러가도록 내버려 둘 작정이었다고 여겨서는 안 된다. 다만 그이는 마음과 마음의 결합이라면 마땅히 따라야 할 완전한 대등함과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 공대를 그에게 하지 않을 수 없다고 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["\"It is such a privilege to be on visiting terms with the nobility,\"", "“귀족분들과 서로 오가는 사이라는 건 참으로 대단한 특권이지요,”"], ["said Mrs. Lupex.", "루펙스 부인이 말했다."], ["\"When I was a girl, I used to be very intimate--\"", "“제가 아가씨였을 적에는 아주 가깝게 지내던—”"]], [["\"You ain't a girl any longer, and so you'd better not talk about it,\"", "“당신은 이제 아가씨가 아니니, 그런 얘긴 꺼내지 않는 게 좋을 거요,”"], ["said Lupex. Mr.", "루펙스가 말했다."], ["Lupex had been at that little shop in Drury Lane after he came down from his scene-painting.", "루펙스 씨는 무대 배경을 그리고 내려온 뒤 드루리 레인의 그 작은 가게에 들렀던 참이었다."]], [["\"My dear, you needn't be a brute to me before all Mrs. Roper's company.", "“여보, 로퍼 부인 댁 손님들 앞에서까지 나한테 짐승처럼 굴 건 없잖아요."], ["If, led away by feelings which I will not now describe, I left my proper circles in marrying you, you need not before all the world teach me how much I have to regret.\" And Mrs.", "지금 여기서 늘어놓지 않으려는 어떤 감정에 이끌려 내가 당신과 혼인하느라 내 격에 맞는 자리를 떠났다 해도, 온 세상 앞에서 내가 얼마나 후회할 일을 했는지 굳이 가르쳐 줄 건 없잖아요.”"], ["Lupex, putting down her knife and fork, applied her handkerchief to her eyes.", "그러고서 루펙스 부인은 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다."]], [["\"That's pleasant for a man over his meals, isn't it?\"", "“사내가 밥상머리에서 저런 소리를 듣는 게 유쾌하겠소, 안 그렇소?”"], ["said Lupex, appealing to Miss Spruce.", "루펙스가 스프루스 양에게 호소하며 말했다."], ["\"I have plenty of that kind of thing, and you can't think how I like it.\"", "“나는 저런 걸 실컷 겪는데, 내가 저걸 얼마나 좋아하는지 당신은 짐작도 못 할 거요.”"]], [["\"Them whom God has joined together, let no man put asunder,\"", "“하느님이 짝지어 주신 것을 사람이 갈라놓지 못할지니라,”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."], ["\"As for me myself, I'm only an old woman.\"", "“저야 뭐, 그저 늙은 여자일 뿐이지요.”"]], [["This little ebullition threw a gloom over the dinner-table, and nothing more was said on the occasion as to the glories of Eames's career.", "이 자그마한 소동이 저녁 식탁에 우울한 기운을 드리웠고, 그 자리에서 임스의 출세담에 대해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 나오지 않았다."], ["But, in the course of the evening, Amelia heard of the encounter which had taken place at the railway station, and at once perceived that she might use the occasion for her own purposes.", "그러나 그날 저녁이 흘러가는 동안 어밀리아는 철도역에서 벌어진 그 충돌 이야기를 듣고, 곧 그 일을 제 목적에 써먹을 수 있겠다고 알아차렸다."]], [["\"John,\"", "“존,”"], ["she whispered to her victim, finding an opportunity for coming upon him when almost alone, \"what is this I hear?", "그이는 그가 거의 혼자 있는 틈을 붙잡아 제 희생자에게 속삭였다. “이게 무슨 얘기예요?"], ["I insist upon knowing.", "꼭 말해 주셔야겠어요."], ["Are you going to fight a duel?\"", "결투를 하실 참이에요?”"]], [["\"Nonsense,\"", "“무슨 소리예요,”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"But it is not nonsense.", "“무슨 소리라니요."], ["You don't know what my feelings will be, if I think that such a thing is going to happen.", "그런 일이 벌어지려 한다고 생각하면 제 심정이 어떨지 당신은 모르실 거예요."], ["But then you are so hard-hearted!\"", "하기야 당신은 그렇게도 매정하시니!”"]], [["\"I ain't hard-hearted a bit, and I'm not going to fight a duel.\"", "“나는 조금도 매정하지 않고, 결투 같은 것도 하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["\"But is it true that you beat Mr. Crosbie at the station?\"", "“그럼 역에서 크로즈비 씨를 때리셨다는 게 정말이에요?”"]], [["\"It is true.", "“정말이에요."], ["I did beat him.\"", "내가 때렸어요.”"]], [["\"Oh, John!", "“아, 존!"], ["not that I mean to say you were wrong, and indeed I honour you for the feeling.", "당신이 잘못하셨다는 말이 아니에요. 오히려 저는 그 마음을 훌륭하다고 여겨요."], ["There can be nothing so dreadful as a young man's deceiving a young woman and leaving her after he has won her heart--particularly when she has had his promise in plain words, or, perhaps, even in black and white.\"", "젊은 남자가 젊은 여자를 속이고, 그 마음을 얻어 놓고서 버리는 것만큼 끔찍한 일은 없지요—더구나 그 아가씨가 분명한 말로, 아니 어쩌면 흰 종이에 검은 글씨로까지 약속을 받아 두었을 때는요.”"], ["John thought of that horrid, foolish, wretched note which he had written.", "존은 자기가 써 준 그 끔찍하고 어리석고 한심한 쪽지를 떠올렸다."], ["\"And a poor girl, if she can't right herself by a breach of promise, doesn't know what to do.", "“그리고 가엾은 아가씨가 혼인 약속 위반으로 제 권리를 찾을 수 없다면, 어찌해야 좋을지 모르는 거예요."], ["Does she, John?\"", "그렇지 않아요, 존?”"]], [["\"A girl who'd right herself that way wouldn't be worth having.\"", "“그런 식으로 제 권리를 찾는 아가씨라면 가질 만한 값어치가 없지요.”"]], [["\"I don't know about that.", "“저는 그렇게 생각하지 않아요."], ["When a poor girl is in such a position, she has to be aided by her friends.", "가엾은 아가씨가 그런 처지에 놓이면 벗들의 도움을 받아야 하는 거예요."], ["I suppose, then, Miss Lily Dale won't bring a breach of promise against him.\"", "그럼 릴리 데일 양은 그이를 혼인 약속 위반으로 걸지 않겠군요.”"]], [["This mention of Lily's name in such a place was sacrilege in the ears of poor Eames.", "그런 자리에서 릴리의 이름이 입에 오르는 것은 가엾은 임스의 귀에는 신성모독이었다."], ["\"I cannot tell,\"", "“나는 알 수 없어요,”"], ["said he, \"what may be the intention of the lady of whom you speak.", "그가 말했다. “당신이 말하는 그 숙녀분의 뜻이 어떠할지는."], ["But from what I know of her friends, I should not think that she will be disgraced by such a proceeding.\"", "그러나 내가 아는 그분의 집안 사람들로 미루어 보면, 그분이 그런 소송으로 스스로를 욕되게 하시리라고는 생각하지 않아요.”"]], [["\"That may be all very well for Miss Lily Dale--\"", "“릴리 데일 양이야 그럴 수 있겠지요—”"], ["Amelia said, and then she hesitated.", "어밀리아가 말하다가 머뭇거렸다."], ["It would not be well, she thought, absolutely to threaten him as yet,--not as long as there was any possibility that he might be won without a threat.", "아직은 그를 대놓고 협박하는 것이 좋지 않겠다고 그이는 생각했다—협박 없이도 그를 얻을 가능성이 조금이라도 있는 동안에는."], ["\"Of course I know all about it,\"", "“물론 저는 다 알고 있어요,”"], ["she continued.", "그이가 말을 이었다."], ["\"She was your L.", "“그분이 당신의 L."], ["D., you know.", "D.였지요."], ["Not that I was ever jealous of her.", "제가 그분을 질투한 적이 있었다는 게 아니에요."], ["To you she was no more than one of childhood's friends.", "당신에게 그분은 어린 시절의 친구들 가운데 하나에 지나지 않았으니까요."], ["Was she, Johnny?\"", "그렇지 않아요, 조니?”"]], [["He stamped his foot upon the floor, and then jumped up from his seat.", "그는 마루를 발로 구르더니 자리에서 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["\"I hate all that sort of twaddle about childhood's friends, and you know I do.", "“어린 시절의 친구니 뭐니 하는 그따위 헛소리는 질색이에요, 그건 당신도 알잖아요."], ["You'll make me swear that I'll never come into this room again.\"", "이러다간 다시는 이 방에 들어오지 않겠다고 나더러 맹세하게 만들 거예요.”"]], [["\"Johnny!\"", "“조니!”"]], [["\"So I will.", "“정말 그럴 거예요."], ["The whole thing makes me sick.", "이 모든 게 신물이 나요."], ["And as for that Mrs. Lupex--\"", "그리고 저 루펙스 부인으로 말하면—”"]], [["\"If this is what you learn, John, by going to a lord's house, I think you had better stay at home with your own friends.\"", "“귀족 나리 댁에 다녀와서 배운 게 이런 것이라면, 존, 당신은 집에서 당신 벗들과 지내는 편이 낫겠네요.”"]], [["\"Of course I had;--much better stay at home with my own friends.", "“물론 그러는 게 낫지요—집에서 내 벗들과 지내는 게 훨씬 낫고말고요."], ["Here's Mrs. Lupex, and at any rate I can't stand her.\"", "그런데 여기 루펙스 부인이 있으니, 나는 아무래도 저이는 견딜 수가 없어요.”"], ["So he went off, and walked round the Crescent, and down to the New Road, and almost into the Regent's Park, thinking of Lily Dale and of his own cowardice with Amelia Roper.", "그러고서 그는 나가 버렸고, 크레슨트를 한 바퀴 돌아 뉴 로드까지, 거의 리전트 파크까지 걸으면서, 릴리 데일을 생각하고 어밀리아 로퍼 앞에서의 제 비겁함을 생각했다."]], [["On the following morning he received a message, at about one o'clock, by the mouth of the Board-room messenger, informing him that his presence was required in the Board-room.", "이튿날 아침나절, 한 시쯤 되었을 때 그는 위원회실 사환의 입을 통해 위원회실로 오라는 전갈을 받았다."], ["\"Sir Raffle Buffle has desired your presence, Mr. Eames.\"", "“래플 버플 경께서 오시라 하십니다, 임스 씨.”"]], [["\"My presence, Tupper!", "“나를? 터퍼, 무슨 일로?”"], ["what for?\" said Johnny, turning upon the messenger almost with dismay.", "조니가 사환 쪽으로 돌아서며 거의 낭패한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["\"Indeed I can't say, Mr.", "“저야 알 수 없지요, 임스 씨."], ["Eames; but Sir Raffle Buffle has desired your presence in the Board-room.\"", "그저 래플 버플 경께서 위원회실로 오시라 하셨습니다.”"]], [["Such a message as that in official life always strikes awe into the heart of a young man.", "관청 생활에서 그런 전갈은 언제나 젊은 사람의 가슴에 두려움을 심는다."], ["And yet, young men generally come forth from such interviews without having received any serious damage, and generally talk about the old gentlemen whom they have encountered with a good deal of light-spirited sarcasm,--or chaff, as it is called in the slang phraseology of the day.", "그러면서도 젊은 사람들은 대개 그런 면담에서 별다른 큰 상처 없이 나오며, 자기가 마주쳤던 그 노신사들에 대해 대개는 가벼운 마음의 빈정거림을—요즘 속어로 하자면 “놀려 먹기”를—늘어놓는다."], ["It is that same \"majesty which doth hedge a king\" that does it.", "그렇게 만드는 것은 “왕을 에워싼 그 위엄”이다."], ["The turkey-cock in his own farmyard is master of the occasion, and the thought of him creates fear.", "칠면조 수컷은 제 마당에서는 그 자리의 주인이며, 그를 떠올리는 것만으로 두려움이 인다."], ["A bishop in his lawn, a judge on the bench, a chairman in the big room at the end of a long table, or a policeman with his bull's-eye lamp upon his beat, can all make themselves terrible by means of those appanages of majesty which have been vouchsafed to them.", "아마포 법의를 걸친 주교, 법대에 앉은 판사, 긴 탁자 끝 큰 방의 의장, 혹은 순찰길에서 각등을 든 순경은 저마다에게 허락된 그 위엄의 부속물들로 스스로를 무섭게 만들 수 있다."], ["But how mean is the policeman in his own home, and how few thought much of Sir Raffle Buffle as he sat asleep after dinner in his old slippers!", "그러나 제 집에 있는 순경은 얼마나 초라하며, 낡은 슬리퍼를 신고 저녁 뒤에 잠든 래플 버플 경을 대단하게 여긴 이가 얼마나 적었던가!"], ["How well can I remember the terror created within me by the air of outraged dignity with which a certain fine old gentleman, now long since gone, could rub his hands slowly, one on the other, and look up to the ceiling, slightly shaking his head, as though lost in the contemplation of my iniquities!", "이제는 세상을 떠난 지 오래인 어떤 훌륭한 노신사가 모욕당한 위엄의 낯빛으로 두 손을 천천히 맞비비며 천장을 올려다보고 고개를 살짝 저으면서, 마치 내 죄악을 헤아리다 넋을 잃은 듯이 굴 때 내 안에 일던 그 공포를 나는 얼마나 잘 기억하는가!"], ["I would become sick in my stomach, and feel as though my ankles had been broken.", "나는 속이 메스꺼워지고 발목이 부러진 것 같은 느낌이 들었다."], ["That upward turn of the eye unmanned me so completely that I was speechless as regarded any defence.", "그 치켜뜨는 눈길은 나를 어찌나 무력하게 만들었던지, 나는 어떤 변명도 하지 못한 채 벙어리가 되곤 했다."], ["I think that that old man could hardly have known the extent of his own power.", "나는 그 노인이 제 힘이 어디까지 미치는지를 스스로도 잘 몰랐으리라고 생각한다."]], [["Once upon a time a careless lad, having the charge of a bundle of letters addressed to the King,--petitions and such like, which in the course of business would not get beyond the hands of some lord-in-waiting's deputy assistant,--sent the bag which contained them to the wrong place; to Windsor, perhaps, if the Court were in London; or to St.", "옛날에 어떤 덤벙대는 소년이 왕에게 올리는 편지 묶음을—청원서 따위로, 일이 돌아가는 순서로 보자면 어느 시종 귀족의 부보좌관 손에서 더 나아가지도 못할 것들이었지만—맡았다가, 그것들이 든 행낭을 엉뚱한 곳으로 보낸 일이 있었다. 궁정이 런던에 있는데 윈저로 보냈든지, 윈저에 있는데 세인트 제임스로 보냈든지 했던 것이다."], ["James's, if it were at Windsor.", "그는 불려 갔다."], ["He was summoned; and the great man of the occasion contented himself with holding his hands up to the heavens as he stood up from his chair, and exclaiming twice, \"Mis-sent the Monarch's pouch!", "그리고 그 자리의 높으신 어른은 의자에서 일어서며 두 손을 하늘로 치켜들고 두 번 이렇게 외치는 것으로 만족했다."], ["Mis-sent the Monarch's pouch!\"", "“군주의 행낭을 잘못 보내다니!"], ["That young man never knew how he escaped from the Board-room; but for a time he was deprived of all power of exertion, and could not resume his work till he had had six months'", "군주의 행낭을 잘못 보내다니!” 그 젊은이는 자기가 어떻게 위원회실을 빠져나왔는지 끝내 알지 못했다."], ["leave of absence, and been brought round upon rum and asses' milk.", "그러나 한동안 그는 아무 기력도 내지 못했고, 여섯 달의 휴가를 얻어 럼주와 당나귀 젖으로 몸을 추스른 뒤에야 일을 다시 잡을 수 있었다."], ["In that instance the peculiar use of the word Monarch had a power which the official magnate had never contemplated.", "그 경우에는 군주라는 낱말의 남다른 쓰임이 그 관청의 거물이 미처 헤아려 보지도 못한 힘을 발휘했던 것이다."], ["The story is traditional; but I believe that the circumstance happened as lately as in the days of George the Third.", "이 이야기는 전해 내려오는 것이지만, 나는 그 일이 조지 3세 시대만큼이나 가까운 때에 있었다고 믿는다."]], [["John Eames could laugh at the present chairman of the Income-tax Office with great freedom, and call him old Huffle Scuffle, and the like; but now that he was sent for, he also, in spite of his radical propensities, felt a little weak about his ankle joints.", "존 임스는 소득세 사무국의 지금 위원장을 두고 아주 자유롭게 웃을 수 있었고, 그를 늙은 허플 스커플이니 뭐니 하고 부를 수 있었다. 그러나 막상 불려 가고 보니, 그 역시 급진적인 기질에도 불구하고 발목 관절에 힘이 조금 빠지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He knew, from the first hearing of the message, that he was wanted with reference to that affair at the railway station.", "그는 전갈을 듣는 순간부터 철도역에서의 그 일 때문에 불려 간다는 것을 알았다."], ["Perhaps there might be a rule that any clerk should be dismissed who used his fists in any public place.", "어쩌면 공공장소에서 주먹을 쓴 서기는 누구든 면직된다는 규정이 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["There were many rules entailing the punishment of dismissal for many offences,--and he began to think that he did remember something of such a regulation.", "여러 죄목에 대해 면직이라는 벌을 지우는 규정이 많이 있었고—그런 규정을 어디선가 본 기억이 나는 것도 같았다."], ["However, he got up, looked once around him upon his friends, and then followed Tupper into the Board-room.", "그러나 그는 일어서서 제 동료들을 한 번 둘러보고는 터퍼를 따라 위원회실로 들어갔다."]], [["\"There's Johnny been sent for by old Scuffles,\"", "“조니가 늙은 스커플스한테 불려 갔군,”"], ["said one clerk.", "한 서기가 말했다."]], [["\"That's about his row with Crosbie,\"", "“크로즈비와의 그 소동 때문이겠지,”"], ["said another.", "또 한 사람이 말했다."], ["\"The Board can't do anything to him for that.\"", "“그 일로 위원회가 그에게 어쩌지는 못해.”"]], [["\"Can't it?\"", "“못한다고?”"], ["said the first.", "처음 사람이 말했다."], ["\"Didn't young Outonites have to resign because of that row at the Cider Cellars, though his cousin, Sir Constant Outonites, did all that he could for him?\"", "“젊은 아우토나이츠는 「사이더 셀러스」에서의 그 소동 때문에 사촌인 콘스턴트 아우토나이츠 경이 할 수 있는 것을 다 해 주었는데도 사직해야 했잖나?”"]], [["\"But he was regularly up the spout with accommodation bills.\"", "“하지만 그자는 융통 어음 때문에 아주 거덜이 나 있었지.”"]], [["\"I tell you that I wouldn't be in Eames's shoes for a trifle.", "“어쨌든 나라면 한 푼을 준다 해도 임스의 처지가 되고 싶지는 않네."], ["Crosbie is secretary at the Committee Office, where Scuffles was chairman before he came here; and of course they're as thick as thieves.", "크로즈비는 위원회 사무국의 사무국장인데, 스커플스가 여기 오기 전에 거기 위원장이었단 말이야. 그러니 둘이야 도둑들처럼 짝짜꿍이지."], ["I shouldn't wonder if they didn't make him go down and apologize.\"", "그를 내려보내 사과하게 만든다 해도 나는 놀라지 않겠어.”"]], [["\"Johnny won't do that,\"", "“조니는 그러지 않을걸,”"], ["said the other.", "다른 이가 말했다."]], [["In the meantime John Eames was standing in the august presence.", "그러는 동안 존 임스는 그 지엄한 어전에 서 있었다."], ["Sir Raffle Buffle was throned in his great oak arm-chair at the head of a long table in a very large room; and by him, at the corner of the table, was seated one of the assistant secretaries of the office.", "래플 버플 경은 아주 큰 방의 긴 탁자 상석에 놓인 커다란 참나무 팔걸이의자에 옥좌에 앉듯 앉아 있었고, 그 곁 탁자 모서리에는 사무국 부서기관 한 사람이 앉아 있었다."], ["Another member of the Board was also at work upon the long table; but he was reading and signing papers at some distance from Sir Raffle, and paid no heed whatever to the scene.", "위원회의 또 다른 위원 한 사람도 그 긴 탁자에서 일을 하고 있었으나, 그는 래플 경과 얼마쯤 떨어진 자리에서 서류를 읽고 서명하면서 이 광경에는 조금도 눈길을 주지 않았다."], ["The assistant secretary, looking on, could see that Sir Raffle was annoyed by this want of attention on the part of his colleague, but all this was lost upon Eames.", "곁에서 보고 있던 부서기관은 동료의 이런 무관심에 래플 경이 언짢아하는 것을 알아챌 수 있었으나, 그것은 모두 임스에게는 보이지 않는 일이었다."]], [["\"Mr. Eames?\"", "“임스 씨?”"], ["said Sir Raffle, speaking with a peculiarly harsh voice, and looking at the culprit through a pair of gold-rimmed glasses, which he perched for the occasion upon his big nose.", "래플 경이 유난히 거슬리는 목소리로, 이 자리를 위해 큼직한 코에 걸친 금테 안경 너머로 죄인을 바라보며 말했다."], ["\"Isn't that Mr. Eames?\"", "“저 사람이 임스 씨인가?”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“예,”"], ["said the assistant secretary, \"this is Eames.\"", "부서기관이 말했다. “이 사람이 임스입니다.”"]], [["\"Ah!\"--and then there was a pause.", "“아!”—그러고서 잠시 뜸이 있었다."], ["\"Come a little nearer, Mr. Eames, will you?\"", "“좀 더 가까이 오게, 임스 씨.”"], ["and Johnny drew nearer, advancing noiselessly over the Turkey carpet.", "그러자 조니는 터키 융단 위를 소리 없이 걸어 앞으로 나아갔다."]], [["\"Let me see; in the second class, isn't he?", "“어디 보자. 2급이지, 그렇지?"], ["Ah! Do you know, Mr.", "아!"], ["Eames, that I have received a letter from the secretary to the Directors of the Great Western Railway Company, detailing circumstances which,--if truly stated in that letter,--redound very much to your discredit?\"", "임스 씨, 내가 그레이트웨스턴 철도 회사 이사회의 사무국장에게서 편지를 한 통 받았다는 것을 아는가? 거기 적힌 정황이—그 편지에 적힌 대로가 사실이라면—자네의 명예를 아주 크게 떨어뜨리는 것이던데.”"]], [["\"I did get into a row there yesterday, sir.\"", "“어제 거기서 소동이 좀 있었습니다, 위원장님.”"]], [["\"Got into a row!", "“소동이 좀 있었다고!"], ["It seems to me that you have got into a very serious row, and that I must tell the Directors of the Great Western Railway Company that the law must be allowed to take its course.\"", "내가 보기에 자네는 아주 심각한 소동을 일으켰고, 나는 그레이트웨스턴 철도 회사 이사들에게 법이 제 갈 길을 가도록 두어야 한다고 알려야 할 것 같은데.”"]], [["\"I shan't mind that, sir, in the least,\"", "“저는 그런 건 조금도 개의치 않습니다, 위원장님,”"], ["said Eames, brightening up a little under this view of the case.", "임스가 이런 국면에 오히려 얼굴이 조금 밝아지며 말했다."]], [["\"Not mind that, sir!\"", "“개의치 않는다니, 이 사람아!”"], ["said Sir Raffle--or rather, he shouted out the words at the offender before him.", "래플 경이 말했다—아니, 앞에 선 죄인에게 그 말을 고함쳐 던졌다고 하는 편이 낫겠다."], ["I am inclined to think that he overdid it, missing the effect which a milder tone might have attained.", "나는 그가 지나쳤다고, 좀 더 부드러운 어조라면 거두었을 효과를 놓쳤다고 생각하는 편이다."], ["Perhaps there was lacking to him some of that majesty of demeanour and dramatic propriety of voice which had been so efficacious in the little story as to the King's bag of letters.", "아마도 그에게는 왕의 편지 행낭에 관한 그 짧은 이야기에서 그토록 효험을 보였던 그 위엄 있는 태도와 극적으로 알맞은 목소리가 얼마쯤 모자랐던 모양이다."], ["As it was, Johnny gave a slight jump, but after his jump he felt better than he had been before.", "실제로 조니는 흠칫 몸을 떨었지만, 흠칫한 뒤에는 그전보다 오히려 기분이 나아졌다."], ["\"Not mind, sir, being dragged before the criminal tribunals of your country, and being punished as a felon,--or rather as a misdemeanour,--for an outrage committed on a public platform!", "“개의치 않는다니, 이 사람아, 나라의 형사 법정에 끌려가 공공 승강장에서 저지른 난동으로 중죄인—아니, 차라리 경범죄인으로 벌을 받는 것을 말이야! 개의치 않는다고!"], ["Not mind it! What do you mean, sir?\"", "대체 무슨 소린가, 이 사람아?”"]], [["\"I mean, that I don't think the magistrate would say very much about it, sir.", "“제 말씀은, 치안판사가 그 일로 별말을 하지 않으리라는 것입니다, 위원장님."], ["And I don't think Mr. Crosbie would come forward.\"", "그리고 크로즈비 씨도 나서지 않을 거라고 생각합니다.”"]], [["\"But Mr. Crosbie must come forward, young man.", "“그러나 크로즈비 씨는 나서야 하네, 젊은이."], ["Do you suppose that an outrage against the peace of the Metropolis is to go unpunished because he may not wish to pursue the matter?", "그가 이 일을 더 따지고 싶어 하지 않는다고 해서 수도의 치안을 해친 난동이 벌을 면할 수 있다고 생각하나?"], ["I'm afraid you must be very ignorant, young man.\"", "자네는 아무래도 아주 무지한 젊은이인 것 같군.”"]], [["\"Perhaps I am,\"", "“아마 그렇겠지요,”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Very ignorant indeed,--very ignorant indeed.", "“참으로 무지해—참으로 무지하고말고."], ["And are you aware, sir, that it would become a question with the Commissioners of this Board whether you could be retained in the service of this department if you were publicly punished by a police magistrate for such a disgraceful outrage as that?\"", "그리고 자네는 아는가, 이 사람아, 그처럼 수치스러운 난동으로 경찰 치안판사에게 공공연히 벌을 받는다면 자네를 이 부서에 계속 두어도 좋은가 하는 것이 이 위원회의 위원들 사이에서 문제가 되리라는 것을?”"]], [["Johnny looked round at the other Commissioner, but that gentleman did not raise his face from his papers.", "조니는 다른 위원 쪽을 둘러보았으나, 그 신사는 서류에서 얼굴을 들지 않았다."]], [["\"Mr. Eames is a very good clerk,\"", "“임스 씨는 아주 훌륭한 서기입니다,”"], ["whispered the assistant secretary, but in a voice which made his words audible to Eames; \"one of the best young men we have,\"", "부서기관이 속삭였다. 그러나 그 말은 임스에게도 들리는 목소리였다."], ["he added, in a voice which was not audible.", "“우리에게 있는 젊은이들 가운데 가장 나은 축이지요,” 그가 덧붙였는데, 이 말은 들리지 않는 목소리였다."]], [["\"Oh,--ah; very well.", "“오—아, 알겠네."], ["Now, I'll tell you what, Mr.", "그럼 이렇게 말해 두지, 임스 씨."], ["Eames, I hope this will be a lesson to you,--a very serious lesson.\"", "이번 일이 자네에게 교훈이 되기를 바라네—아주 엄중한 교훈이 말이야.”"]], [["The assistant secretary, leaning back in his chair so as to be a little behind the head of Sir Raffle, did manage to catch the eye of the other Commissioner.", "부서기관은 의자에 등을 기대어 래플 경의 머리 뒤쪽으로 조금 물러나면서, 다른 위원의 눈길을 붙잡는 데 성공했다."], ["The other Commissioner, barely looking round, smiled a little, and then the assistant secretary smiled also.", "다른 위원은 얼굴을 거의 돌리지도 않은 채 살짝 웃었고, 그러자 부서기관도 웃었다."], ["Eames saw this, and he smiled too.", "임스가 그것을 보았고, 그도 웃었다."]], [["\"Whether any ulterior consequences may still await the breach of the peace of which you have been guilty, I am not yet prepared to say,\"", "“자네가 저지른 그 치안 방해에 대해 앞으로 어떤 결과가 더 남아 있을지 나는 아직 말할 수 없네,”"], ["continued Sir Raffle.", "래플 경이 말을 이었다."], ["\"You may go now.\"", "“이제 가 보게.”"]], [["And Johnny returned to his own place, with no increased reverence for the dignity of the chairman.", "그리고 조니는 위원장의 위엄에 대한 존경심이 조금도 늘지 않은 채로 제자리로 돌아갔다."]], [["On the following morning one of his colleagues showed him with great glee the passage in the newspaper which informed the world that he had been so desperately beaten by Crosbie that he was obliged to keep his bed at this present time in consequence of the flogging that he had received.", "이튿날 아침 동료 하나가 아주 신이 나서 그에게 신문의 한 대목을 보여 주었다. 그가 크로즈비에게 어찌나 호되게 얻어맞았는지 그 매질 탓에 지금도 자리에 누워 있어야 한다고 온 세상에 알리는 대목이었다."], ["Then his anger was aroused, and he bounced about the big room of the Income-tax Office, regardless of assistant secretaries, head clerks, and all other official grandees whatsoever, denouncing the iniquities of the public press, and declaring his opinion that it would be better to live in Russia than in a country which allowed such audacious falsehoods to be propagated.", "그러자 그의 노여움이 일었고, 그는 부서기관이며 수석 서기며 그 밖의 온갖 관청 어른들 앞이라는 것도 아랑곳없이 소득세 사무국의 큰 방을 이리저리 쿵쾅거리며, 언론의 죄악을 성토하고, 그런 뻔뻔한 거짓말이 퍼지도록 두는 나라에 사느니 러시아에 사는 편이 낫겠다는 제 의견을 늘어놓았다."]], [["\"He never touched me, Fisher; I don't think he ever tried; but, upon my honour, he never touched me.\"", "“그자는 나를 건드리지도 못했네, 피셔. 건드려 보려고나 했는지도 모르겠어. 그러나 명예를 걸고 말하는데, 그자는 나를 건드리지 못했어.”"]], [["\"But, Johnny, it was bold in you to make up to Lord De Courcy's daughter,\"", "“그렇지만 조니, 드 코시 백작의 딸에게 수작을 걸다니 자네도 대담했군,”"], ["said Fisher.", "피셔가 말했다."]], [["\"I never saw one of them in my life.\"", "“나는 그중 누구도 평생 본 적이 없네.”"]], [["\"He's going it altogether among the aristocracy, now,\"", "“이 친구는 이제 아예 귀족들 사이에서 놀아나는군,”"], ["said another; \"I suppose you wouldn't look at anybody under a viscount?\"", "또 한 사람이 말했다. “자작 아래로는 거들떠보지도 않겠지?”"]], [["\"Can I help what that thief of an editor puts into his paper?", "“그 도둑놈 같은 편집자가 제 신문에 무엇을 싣든 내가 어쩌겠나?"], ["Flogged!", "매질이라니!"], ["Huffle Scuffle told me I was a felon, but that wasn't half so bad as this fellow;\"", "허플 스커플은 나더러 중죄인이라고 했지만, 그것도 이 작자에 비하면 절반도 고약하지 않아.”"], ["and Johnny kicked the newspaper across the room.", "그러고서 조니는 신문을 방 저쪽으로 걷어차 버렸다."]], [["\"Indict him for a libel,\"", "“명예훼손으로 고소하게,”"], ["said Fisher.", "피셔가 말했다."]], [["\"Particularly for saying you wanted to marry a countess's daughter,\"", "“특히 자네가 백작부인의 딸과 혼인하고 싶어 했다고 쓴 대목을 말이야,”"], ["said another clerk.", "또 다른 서기가 말했다."]], [["\"I never heard such a scandal in my life,\"", "“내 평생 이런 추문은 들어 본 적이 없네,”"], ["declared a third; \"and then to say that the girl wouldn't look at you.\"", "세 번째 사람이 잘라 말했다. “게다가 그 아가씨가 자네를 거들떠보지도 않았다니.”"]], [["But not the less was it felt by all in the office that Johnny Eames was becoming a leading man among them, and that he was one with whom each of them would be pleased to be intimate.", "그러나 그렇다고 해서, 조니 임스가 저희 가운데서 앞서 나가는 사람이 되어 가고 있으며 저마다 가까이 지내고 싶어 할 만한 사람이라는 것을 사무국 사람들이 못 느낀 것은 아니었다."], ["And even among the grandees this affair of the railway station did him no real harm.", "그리고 높으신 어른들 사이에서도 철도역의 이 일이 그에게 정말로 해가 되지는 않았다."], ["It was known that Crosbie had deserved to be thrashed, and known that Eames had thrashed him.", "크로즈비가 매를 맞아 마땅했다는 것도, 임스가 그를 두들겨 주었다는 것도 다들 알고 있었다."], ["It was all very well for Sir Raffle Buffle to talk of police magistrates and misdemeanours, but all the world at the Income-tax Office knew very well that Eames had come out from that affair with his head upright, and his right foot foremost.", "래플 버플 경이야 경찰 치안판사니 경범죄니 하고 떠들 수 있었겠지만, 소득세 사무국의 온 세상은 임스가 그 일에서 고개를 들고 오른발을 앞세우고 나왔다는 것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["\"Never mind about the newspaper,\"", "“신문 따위는 마음에 두지 말게,”"], ["a thoughtful old senior clerk said to him.", "생각 깊은 늙은 고참 서기가 그에게 말했다."], ["\"As he did get the licking and you didn't, you can afford to laugh at the newspaper.\"", "“매를 맞은 건 그자고 자네는 아니니, 신문쯤은 웃어넘길 여유가 있지 않은가.”"]], [["\"And you wouldn't write to the editor?\"", "“그럼 편집자에게 편지는 쓰지 말라는 말씀입니까?”"]], [["\"No, no; certainly not.", "“아니, 아니, 절대 쓰지 말게."], ["No one thinks of defending himself to a newspaper except an ass;--unless it be some fellow who wants to have his name puffed.", "신문에 대고 제 변명을 하겠다고 나서는 건 얼간이뿐이야—제 이름이 좀 오르내리기를 바라는 자가 아니라면 말이지."], ["You may write what's as true as the gospel, but they'll know how to make fun of it.\"", "자네가 복음처럼 참된 것을 쓴다 해도, 그자들은 그것을 우스개로 만드는 법을 안다네.”"]], [["Johnny therefore gave up his idea of an indignant letter to the editor, but he felt that he was bound to give some explanation of the whole matter to Lord De Guest.", "그리하여 조니는 편집자에게 분개한 편지를 쓰겠다는 생각을 접었다. 그러나 이 일의 전말을 드 게스트 백작에게 어떻게든 설명해야 한다고 느꼈다."], ["The affair had happened as he was coming from the earl's house, and all his own concerns had now been made so much a matter of interest to his kind friend, that he thought that he could not with propriety leave the earl to learn from the newspapers either the facts or the falsehoods.", "그 일은 그가 백작의 집에서 돌아오는 길에 벌어졌고, 이제는 제 모든 일이 그 다정한 벗에게 그토록 큰 관심거리가 되어 있었으므로, 백작이 신문에서 사실이든 거짓이든 그것을 알게 되도록 내버려 두는 것은 도리가 아니라고 생각했다."], ["And, therefore, before he left his office he wrote the following letter:--", "그래서 그는 사무국을 나서기 전에 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다."]], [["Income-tax Office, December 29, 186--.", "소득세 사무국, 186-년 12월 29일."]], [["He thought a good deal about the style in which he ought to address the peer, never having hitherto written to him.", "그는 이 귀족에게 첫머리를 어떤 투로 써야 할지를 두고 꽤나 오래 궁리했다. 지금까지 한 번도 편지를 써 본 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["He began, \"My dear Lord,\" on one sheet of paper, and then put it aside, thinking that it looked over-bold.", "그는 종이 한 장에 “친애하는 백작님께”라고 시작해 보았다가, 너무 대담해 보인다는 생각에 밀쳐 두었다."]], [["As you have been so very kind to me, I feel that I ought to tell you what happened the other morning at the railway station, as I was coming back from Guestwick.", "백작님께서 제게 그토록 친절히 대해 주셨으니, 제가 게스트윅에서 돌아오던 날 아침 철도역에서 있었던 일을 말씀드려야 마땅하다고 느낍니다."], ["That scoundrel Crosbie got into the same carriage with me at the Barchester Junction, and sat opposite to me all the way up to London.", "그 악당 크로즈비가 바체스터 분기역에서 저와 같은 객차에 올라, 런던까지 오는 내내 제 맞은편에 앉아 있었습니다. 저는 그에게 한마디도 하지 않았고 그도 제게 하지 않았습니다."], ["I did not speak a word to him, or he to me; but when he got out at the Paddington Station, I thought I ought not to let him go away, so I-- I can't say that I thrashed him as I wished to do; but I made an attempt, and I did give him a black eye.", "그러나 그가 패딩턴 역에서 내렸을 때, 저는 그를 그냥 보내서는 안 되겠다고 생각했고, 그래서 저는—제가 바라던 만큼 그를 두들겨 주었다고는 말씀드리지 못하겠습니다. 그러나 해 보기는 했고, 그의 눈에 시퍼런 멍을 하나 안겨 주었습니다."], ["A whole quantity of policemen got round us, and I hadn't a fair chance.", "순경들이 잔뜩 몰려와 저희를 둘러싸는 바람에 저는 제대로 겨룰 틈이 없었습니다. 백작님께서 제가 잘못했다고 여기시리라는 것을 압니다."], ["I know you will think that I was wrong, and perhaps I was; but what could I do when he sat opposite to me there for two hours, looking as though he thought himself the finest fellow in all London?", "어쩌면 정말 잘못이었겠지요. 그러나 그자가 두 시간 동안 제 앞에 앉아, 제가 보기에 스스로를 온 런던에서 제일가는 사내로 여기는 듯한 낯을 하고 있는데, 제가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠습니까?"]], [["They've put a horrible paragraph into one of the newspapers, saying that I got so \"flogged\"", "신문 하나에 끔찍한 대목을 실어 놓았습니다. 제가 어찌나 “매를 맞았는지”"], ["that I haven't been able to stir since.", "그 뒤로 꼼짝도 못 하고 있다는 것입니다."], ["It is an atrocious falsehood, as is all the rest of the newspaper account.", "그것은 지독한 거짓이며, 그 신문 기사의 나머지도 죄다 마찬가지입니다."], ["I was not touched.", "저는 손끝 하나 닿지 않았습니다."], ["He was not nearly so bad a customer as the bull, and seemed to take it all very quietly.", "그자는 황소만큼 까다로운 상대가 전혀 아니었고, 모든 것을 아주 조용히 받아들이는 듯했습니다."], ["I must acknowledge, though, that he didn't get such a beating as he deserved.", "다만 그자가 마땅히 받았어야 할 만큼 얻어맞지는 않았다는 것은 인정해야겠습니다."]], [["Your friend Sir R.", "백작님의 벗이신 R."], ["B.", "B."], ["sent for me this morning, and told me I was a felon.", "경께서 오늘 아침 저를 부르시더니 제가 중죄인이라고 하셨습니다. 저는 그 말에는 그리 마음 쓰지 않았습니다."], ["I didn't seem to care much for that, for he might as well have called me a murderer or a burglar; but I shall care very much indeed if I have made you angry with me.", "그분이야 저를 살인자라 부르든 강도라 부르든 마찬가지였을 테니까요. 그러나 제가 백작님을 노엽게 해 드렸다면 그것은 정말로 마음이 쓰일 것입니다."], ["But what I most fear is the anger of some one else,--at Allington.", "그렇지만 제가 가장 두려운 것은 다른 어떤 분의 노여움입니다—앨링턴에 계신."]], [["Believe me to be, my Lord, Yours very much obliged and most sincerely,", "백작님, 크나큰 은혜를 입은, 더없이 진실한 자로 저를 믿어 주시기를 바라며,"]], [["\"I knew he'd do it if ever he got the opportunity,\"", "“기회만 있으면 그 녀석이 그럴 줄 알았지,”"], ["said the earl when he had read his letter; and he walked about his room striking his hands together, and then thrusting his thumbs into his waistcoat-pockets.", "백작은 편지를 다 읽고 나서 말했다. 그러고는 두 손을 마주 치기도 하고 조끼 주머니에 엄지를 찔러 넣기도 하면서 방 안을 이리저리 걸었다."], ["\"I knew he was made of the right stuff,\"", "“그 녀석이 제대로 된 재목으로 만들어졌다는 걸 내가 알았어.”"], ["and the earl rejoiced greatly in the prowess of his favourite.", "그러면서 백작은 제가 아끼는 젊은이의 무용에 크게 기뻐했다."], ["\"I'd have done it myself if I'd seen him.", "“내가 그 자리에 있었더라면 나라도 그렇게 했을 거야."], ["I do believe I would.\"", "정말 그랬을 것 같아.”"], ["Then he went back to the breakfast-room and told Lady Julia.", "그러고서 그는 아침 식사 방으로 돌아가 레이디 줄리아에게 이야기했다."], ["\"What do you think?\"", "“이게 무슨 일인 줄 아나?”"], ["said he; \"Johnny Eames has come across Crosbie, and given him a desperate beating.\"", "그가 말했다. “조니 임스가 크로즈비를 만나서 아주 지독하게 두들겨 줬다는군.”"]], [["\"No!\"", "“저런!”"], ["said Lady Julia, putting down her newspaper and spectacles, and expressing by the light of her eyes anything but Christian horror at the wickedness of the deed.", "레이디 줄리아가 신문과 안경을 내려놓으며 말했다. 그이의 눈빛이 드러낸 것은 그 못된 짓에 대한 기독교인다운 경악과는 도무지 거리가 먼 것이었다."]], [["\"But he has, though.", "“정말 그랬다니까."], ["I knew he would if he saw him.\"", "만나기만 하면 그럴 줄 알았어.”"]], [["\"Beaten him!", "“때렸다고요!"], ["Actually beaten him!\"", "정말로 때렸어요!”"]], [["\"Sent him home to Lady Alexandrina with two black eyes.\"", "“두 눈에 시퍼렇게 멍을 들여 레이디 알렉산드리나에게 돌려보냈다는군.”"]], [["\"Two black eyes!", "“두 눈을 다요!"], ["What a young pickle!", "이런 개구쟁이 같으니!"], ["But did he get hurt himself?\"", "그런데 제 몸은 다치지 않았대요?”"]], [["\"Not a scratch, he says.\"", "“긁힌 데도 없다는군.”"]], [["\"And what'll they do to him?\"", "“그럼 그 사람들이 조니를 어떻게 할까요?”"]], [["\"Nothing. Crosbie won't be fool enough to do anything.", "“아무것도 못 하지."], ["A man becomes an outlaw when he plays such a game as he has played.", "크로즈비도 무슨 일을 벌일 만큼 바보는 아닐 거야. 그런 수작을 부린 사내는 무법자가 되는 법이야."], ["Anybody's hand may be raised against him with impunity.", "누구든 그자에게 손을 대도 벌을 받지 않지."], ["He can't show his face, you know.", "그자는 낯을 들고 다닐 수가 없어."], ["He can't come forward and answer questions as to what he has done.", "제가 한 일에 대해 나서서 물음에 답할 수도 없고."], ["There are offences which the law can't touch, but which outrage public feeling so strongly that any one may take upon himself the duty of punishing them.", "법이 건드리지 못하면서도 세상의 감정을 너무나 크게 거스르는 죄가 있어서, 그런 것은 누구라도 스스로 나서서 벌하는 노릇을 떠맡아도 되는 거야."], ["He has been thrashed, and that will stick to him till he dies.\"", "그자는 매를 맞았고, 그 일은 죽을 때까지 그자에게 들러붙어 있을 걸세.”"]], [["\"Do tell Johnny from me that I hope he didn't get hurt,\"", "“다치지 않았기를 바란다고 조니에게 꼭 전해 주세요,”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."], ["The old lady could not absolutely congratulate him on his feat of arms, but she did the next thing to it.", "이 노부인은 그의 무훈을 대놓고 축하해 줄 수는 없었으나, 그다음으로 할 수 있는 일은 했다."]], [["But the earl did congratulate him, with a full open assurance of his approval.", "그러나 백작은 제가 옳다고 여긴다는 것을 숨김없이 온전히 밝히면서 그를 축하해 주었다."]], [["\"I hope,\"", "“나는,”"], ["he said, \"I should have done the same at your age, under similar circumstances, and I'm very glad that he proved less difficult than the bull.", "그가 말했다. “내가 자네 나이에 같은 처지였다면 나도 그렇게 했기를 바라네."], ["I'm quite sure you didn't want any one to help you with Master Crosbie.", "그리고 그자가 황소보다 다루기 쉬웠다니 참으로 기쁘군. 크로즈비 도련님쯤에는 자네가 누구의 도움도 필요로 하지 않았으리라는 것을 나는 아주 잘 알고 있네."], ["As for that other person at Allington, if I understand such matters at all, I think she will forgive you.\"", "앨링턴에 있는 그 다른 사람으로 말하자면, 내가 그런 일을 조금이라도 안다면, 그이는 자네를 용서할 걸세.”"], ["It may, however, be a question whether the earl did understand such matters at all.", "그러나 백작이 그런 일을 조금이라도 알았는지는 의문일 수 있다. 그러고서 그는 추신으로 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["And then he added, in a postscript: \"When you write to me again,--and don't be long first, begin your letter, 'My dear Lord De Guest,'--that is the proper way.\"", "“다음에 내게 편지할 때는—그리고 너무 오래 끌지 말게—편지 첫머리를 ‘친애하는 드 게스트 백작님께’라고 시작하게. 그것이 제대로 된 법식일세.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVII. AN OLD MAN'S COMPLAINT.", "# 제37장. 어느 노인의 하소연"]], [["\"Have you been thinking again of what I was saying to you, Bell?\"", "“저번에 내가 한 말을 다시 생각해 보았니, 벨?”"], ["Bernard said to his cousin one morning.", "어느 날 아침 버나드가 사촌에게 말했다."]], [["\"Thinking of it, Bernard?", "“그 일을 생각하다니, 버나드?"], ["Why should I think more of it?", "내가 왜 그 일을 더 생각해야 하지?"], ["I had hoped that you had forgotten it yourself.\"", "나는 네가 그 일을 잊어버렸기를 바라고 있었어.”"]], [["\"No,\"", "“아니.”"], ["he said; \"I am not so easy-hearted as that.", "그가 말했다. “나는 그렇게 마음이 가벼운 사람이 아니야."], ["I cannot look on such a thing as I would the purchase of a horse, which I could give up without sorrow if I found that the animal was too costly for my purse.", "나는 이런 일을 말 한 마리 사는 일처럼 여길 수는 없어. 말이야 내 주머니 사정에 견주어 너무 비싸다는 것을 알게 되면 서운할 것도 없이 포기할 수 있지만 말이야."], ["I did not tell you that I loved you till I was sure of myself, and having made myself sure I cannot change at all.\"", "나는 내 마음을 확실히 알기 전까지는 너를 사랑한다는 말을 하지 않았고, 일단 확실히 해 둔 다음에는 조금도 달라질 수가 없어.”"]], [["\"And yet you would have me change.\"", "“그러면서도 나더러는 달라지라는 거구나.”"]], [["\"Yes, of course I would.", "“그래, 물론 그렇지."], ["If your heart be free now, it must of course be changed before you come to love any man.", "네 마음이 지금 비어 있다면, 네가 어떤 남자를 사랑하게 되기 전에 그 마음은 당연히 달라져야 하잖아."], ["Such change as that is to be looked for.", "그런 변화라면 바랄 만한 것이지."], ["But when you have loved, then it will not be easy to change you.\"", "하지만 네가 일단 사랑을 하게 되고 나면, 그때는 너를 바꾸기가 쉽지 않을 거야.”"]], [["\"But I have not.\"", "“하지만 나는 사랑한 적이 없는걸.”"]], [["\"Then I have a right to hope.", "“그렇다면 나에게는 바랄 권리가 있는 셈이지."], ["I have been hanging on here, Bell, longer than I ought to have done, because I could not bring myself to leave you without speaking of this again.", "벨, 나는 여기 있어야 할 것보다 오래 머물러 있었어. 이 이야기를 다시 하지 않고는 너를 두고 떠날 수가 없었기 때문이야."], ["I did not wish to seem to you to be importunate--\"", "나는 너에게 끈덕지게 조르는 사람으로 보이고 싶지 않았고—”"]], [["\"If you could only believe me in what I say.\"", "“네가 내 말을 그저 믿어만 준다면 좋으련만.”"]], [["\"It is not that I do not believe.", "“믿지 않는 것이 아니야."], ["I am not a puppy or a fool, to flatter myself that you must be in love with me.", "나는 네가 나를 사랑할 수밖에 없다고 스스로 우쭐댈 만큼 얼간이나 바보는 아니야."], ["I believe you well enough.", "나는 너를 충분히 믿어."], ["But still it is possible that your mind may alter.\"", "하지만 그래도 네 마음이 달라지는 일은 있을 수 있잖아.”"]], [["\"It is impossible.\"", "“그런 일은 있을 수 없어.”"]], [["\"I do not know whether my uncle or your mother have spoken to you about this.\"", "“삼촌이나 네 어머니가 이 일로 너에게 말씀하셨는지 나는 모르겠어.”"]], [["\"Such speaking would have no effect.\"", "“그런 말씀은 아무 소용도 없을 거야.”"]], [["In fact, her mother had spoken to her, but she truly said that such speaking would have no effect.", "사실 그녀의 어머니는 그녀에게 말을 했었다. 그러나 그런 말은 아무 소용도 없으리라고 한 그녀의 말은 참말이었다."], ["If her cousin could not win the battle by his own skill, he might have been quite sure, looking at her character as it was known to him, that he would not be able to win it by the skill of others.", "그녀의 사촌이 제 솜씨로 이 싸움을 이길 수 없다면, 그가 알고 있는 그녀의 성품에 비추어 볼 때 남의 솜씨를 빌려서도 이길 수 없으리라는 것은 그로서도 아주 분명히 알 수 있는 일이었다."]], [["\"We have all been made very unhappy,\"", "“우리는 모두 몹시 불행해졌어.”"], ["he went on to say, \"by this calamity which has fallen on poor Lily.\"", "그가 말을 이었다. “가엾은 릴리에게 닥친 이 재앙 때문에 말이야.”"]], [["\"And because she has been deceived by the man she did love, I am to make matters square by marrying a man I--\"", "“그리고 릴리가 정말로 사랑했던 남자에게 속았다고 해서, 나는 내가 사랑하지 않는 남자와 혼인해서 셈을 맞추어야 한다는—”"], ["and then she paused.", "그러고서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다."], ["\"Dear Bernard, you should not drive me to say words which will sound harsh to you.\"", "“버나드, 너에게 모질게 들릴 말을 내가 하도록 몰아붙이지는 말아 줘.”"]], [["\"No words can be harsher than those which you have already spoken.", "“네가 이미 한 말보다 더 모진 말은 없어."], ["But, Bell, at any rate, you may listen to me.\"", "하지만 벨, 그래도 내 말은 들어 줄 수 있잖아.”"]], [["Then he told her how desirable it was with reference to all the concerns of the Dale family that she should endeavour to look favourably on his proposition.", "그러고 나서 그는 그녀에게, 데일 집안의 모든 일에 비추어 볼 때 그녀가 자기의 청을 좋게 보아 주려고 애쓰는 것이 얼마나 바람직한 일인지를 이야기했다."], ["It would be good for them all, he said, especially for Lily, as to whom, at the present moment, their uncle felt so kindly.", "그것은 우리 모두에게 좋은 일이 될 것이며, 특히 지금 삼촌이 그토록 다정하게 마음을 쓰고 있는 릴리에게 좋을 것이라고 그는 말했다."], ["He, as Bernard pleaded, was so anxious at heart for this marriage, that he would do anything that was asked of him if he were gratified.", "버나드가 호소한 바로는, 삼촌은 이 혼사를 마음속으로 몹시 바라고 있어서 뜻대로만 되면 무엇을 청하든 다 들어줄 터였다."], ["But if he were not gratified in this, he would feel that he had ground for displeasure.", "그러나 이 일에서 뜻을 이루지 못한다면 그는 언짢아할 만한 까닭이 생겼다고 여길 터였다."]], [["Bell, as she had been desired to listen, did listen very patiently.", "벨은 들어 달라는 부탁을 받았으므로 아주 참을성 있게 들었다."], ["But when her cousin had finished, her answer was very short.", "그러나 사촌의 말이 끝났을 때 그녀의 대답은 아주 짧았다."], ["\"Nothing that my uncle can say, or think, or do, can make any difference in this,\"", "“삼촌이 무슨 말씀을 하시든, 무슨 생각을 하시든, 무슨 일을 하시든, 이 일은 조금도 달라지지 않아.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"You will think nothing, then, of the happiness of others.\"", "“그러면 너는 다른 사람들의 행복은 아무것도 생각하지 않겠다는 거구나.”"]], [["\"I would not marry a man I did not love, to ensure any amount of happiness to others;--at least I know I ought not to do so.", "“다른 사람들의 행복을 아무리 확실하게 지켜 줄 수 있다 해도, 나는 사랑하지 않는 남자와 혼인하지는 않겠어. 적어도 그래서는 안 된다는 것쯤은 알고 있어."], ["But I do not believe I should ensure any one's happiness by this marriage.", "하지만 이 혼인으로 내가 누구의 행복을 지켜 줄 수 있으리라고는 믿지 않아."], ["Certainly not yours.\"", "네 행복은 분명히 아니고.”"]], [["After this Bernard had acknowledged to himself that the difficulties in his way were great.", "이 일이 있은 뒤 버나드는 제 앞길에 놓인 어려움이 크다는 것을 스스로 인정했다."], ["\"I will go away till next autumn,\"", "“내년 가을까지는 떠나 있겠습니다.”"], ["he said to his uncle.", "그가 삼촌에게 말했다."]], [["\"If you would give up your profession and remain here, she would not be so perverse.\"", "“네가 그 직업을 그만두고 여기 머문다면 그 아이도 그렇게까지 비뚜로 굴지는 않을 게다.”"]], [["\"I cannot do that, sir.", "“그럴 수는 없습니다, 어르신."], ["I cannot risk the well-being of my life on such a chance.\"", "그런 요행에 제 평생의 안녕을 걸 수는 없습니다.”"], ["Then his uncle had been angry with him, as well as with his niece.", "그러자 삼촌은 조카딸에게 그랬듯이 조카에게도 화를 냈다."], ["In his anger he determined that he would go again to his sister-in-law, and, after some unreasonable fashion, he resolved that it would become him to be very angry with her also, if she declined to assist him with all her influence as a mother.", "홧김에 그는 제수에게 다시 한번 가 보기로 마음먹었고, 어딘가 사리에 닿지 않는 방식으로, 그녀가 어머니로서 지닌 영향력을 다 써서 자기를 돕기를 마다한다면 그녀에게도 몹시 화를 내는 것이 마땅하리라고 작정했다."]], [["\"Why should they not both marry?\"", "“저 아이들이 둘 다 혼인해서 안 될 까닭이 무엇인가?”"], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["Lord De Guest's offer as to young Eames had been very generous.", "젊은 임스에 관한 드 게스트 경의 제안은 매우 후한 것이었다."], ["As he had then declared, he had not been able to express his own opinion at once; but on thinking over what the earl had said, he had found himself very willing to heal the family wound in the manner proposed, if any such healing might be possible.", "그때 그가 밝혔듯이 그는 당장에는 제 의견을 말할 수 없었다. 그러나 백작이 한 말을 곰곰이 되새겨 본 끝에, 그는 그런 치유가 조금이라도 가능하기만 하다면 백작이 내놓은 방식으로 집안의 상처를 기꺼이 아물게 하고 싶어 하는 자신을 발견했다."], ["That, however, could not be done quite as yet.", "그러나 그 일을 지금 당장 할 수는 없었다."], ["When the time should come, and he thought it might come soon,--perhaps in the spring, when the days should be fine and the evenings again long,--he would be willing to take his share with the earl in establishing that new household.", "때가 오면—그리고 그때가 곧, 어쩌면 날이 화창해지고 저녁이 다시 길어지는 봄쯤에 오리라고 그는 생각했다—그는 그 새 살림을 차려 주는 데 백작과 함께 제 몫을 지는 것이 기꺼울 터였다."], ["To Crosbie he had refused to give anything, and there was upon his conscience a shade of remorse in that he had so refused.", "크로즈비에게는 아무것도 주기를 거절했었고, 그렇게 거절한 일로 그의 양심에는 얼마간 뉘우침의 그늘이 드리워져 있었다."], ["But if Lily could be brought to love this other man, he would be more open-handed.", "그러나 릴리가 이 다른 남자를 사랑하게 될 수만 있다면 그는 손을 더 크게 열 작정이었다."], ["She should have her share as though she was in fact his daughter.", "그 아이는 사실상 제 딸이나 다름없이 제 몫을 받게 되리라."], ["But then, if he intended to do so much for them at the Small House, should not they in return do something also for him?", "그러나 그렇다면, 작은 집 사람들을 위해 그만한 일을 할 뜻이 있는 마당에, 그들도 그 답례로 그를 위해 무언가 해 주어야 하지 않겠는가?"], ["So thinking, he went again to his sister-in-law, determined to explain his views, even though it might be at the risk of some hard words between them.", "그렇게 생각하며 그는 제수에게 다시 갔다. 둘 사이에 모진 말이 오갈 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 제 뜻을 밝히기로 작정한 것이었다."], ["As regarded himself, he did not much care for hard words spoken to him.", "제 자신에 대해서라면 그는 자기에게 쏟아지는 모진 말 따위는 그다지 개의치 않았다."], ["He almost expected that people's words should be hard and painful.", "그는 사람들의 말이란 으레 모질고 아프기 마련이라고 여기다시피 했다."], ["He did not look for the comfort of affectionate soft greetings, and perhaps would not have appreciated them had they come to him. He caught Mrs.", "그는 다정하고 부드러운 인사말이 주는 위안 같은 것은 바라지 않았고, 설령 그런 것이 그에게 왔다 한들 아마 고맙게 여기지도 않았으리라."], ["Dale walking in the garden, and brought her into his own room, feeling that he had a better chance there than in her own house.", "그는 정원을 거닐고 있던 데일 부인을 붙들어 제 방으로 데려왔다. 그녀의 집에서보다 그곳에서가 더 승산이 있으리라고 느꼈던 것이다."], ["She, with an old dislike to being lectured in that room, had endeavoured to avoid the interview, but had failed.", "그녀는 그 방에서 훈계를 듣는 것을 예전부터 싫어했던 터라 이 만남을 피하려고 애썼으나 뜻을 이루지 못했다."]], [["\"So I met John Eames at the manor,\"", "“그래서 그 저택에서 존 임스를 만났소.”"], ["he had said to her in the garden.", "그가 정원에서 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["\"Ah, yes; and how did he get on there?", "“아, 그래요? 거기서 그 애는 어떻게 지냈답니까?"], ["I cannot conceive poor Johnny keeping holiday with the earl and his sister.", "가엾은 조니가 백작과 그 누이와 함께 휴가를 보내는 모습은 도무지 그려지지가 않아요."], ["How did he behave to them, and how did they behave to him?\"", "그 애는 그분들께 어떻게 굴었고, 그분들은 그 애에게 어떻게 하셨답니까?”"]], [["\"I can assure you he was very much at home there.\"", "“장담하건대 그 애는 거기서 아주 편안해 보였소.”"]], [["\"Was he, indeed?", "“정말요?"], ["Well, I hope it will do him good.", "그렇다면 그 일이 그 애에게 도움이 되면 좋겠어요."], ["He is, I'm sure, a very good young man; only rather awkward.\"", "그 애가 참으로 착한 젊은이라는 것은 저도 잘 알아요. 다만 좀 어설플 뿐이지요.”"]], [["\"I didn't think him awkward at all.", "“나는 그 애가 어설프다는 생각은 조금도 들지 않았소."], ["You'll find, Mary, that he'll do very well;--a great deal better than his father did.\"", "제수씨도 곧 알게 될 거요, 그 애가 아주 잘해 나가리라는 것을. 제 아버지가 그랬던 것보다 훨씬 낫게 말이오.”"]], [["\"I'm sure I hope he may.\" After that Mrs.", "“정말 그러기를 바라요.”"], ["Dale made her attempt to escape; but the squire had taken her prisoner, and led her captive into the house.", "그러고 나서 데일 부인은 빠져나가려고 애를 썼다. 그러나 지주는 그녀를 사로잡아 제 포로로 삼아서는 집 안으로 이끌었다."], ["\"Mary,\"", "“제수씨.”"], ["he said, as soon as he had induced her to sit down, \"it is time that this should be settled between my nephew and niece.\"", "그녀를 자리에 앉히자마자 그가 말했다. “내 조카와 조카딸 사이의 이 일을 이제는 매듭지어야 할 때요.”"]], [["\"I am afraid there will be nothing to settle.\"", "“매듭지을 것이 아무것도 없을까 걱정이에요.”"]], [["\"What do you mean;--that you disapprove of it?\"", "“그게 무슨 말이오? 제수씨가 그 일을 못마땅해한다는 거요?”"]], [["\"By no means,--personally.", "“결코 아니에요. 개인적으로는요."], ["I should approve of it very strongly.", "저도 아주 강하게 찬성해요."], ["But that has nothing to do with the question.\"", "하지만 그것은 이 문제와 아무 상관도 없어요.”"]], [["\"Yes, it has.", "“아니, 상관이 있소."], ["I beg your pardon, but it must have, and should have a great deal to do with it.", "미안한 말이지만 상관이 있어야 하고 또 있어야 마땅하며, 아주 큰 상관이 있소."], ["Of course, I am not saying that anybody should now ever be compelled to marry anybody.\"", "물론 이제 와서 누구든 누구와 혼인하도록 억지로 시킬 수 있다는 말을 하는 것은 아니오.”"]], [["\"I hope not.\"", "“그러시지 않기를 바라요.”"]], [["\"I never said that they ought, and never thought so.", "“나는 그래야 한다고 말한 적도 없고 그렇게 생각한 적도 없소."], ["But I do think that the wishes of all her family should have very great weight with a girl that has been well brought up.\"", "하지만 잘 자란 처녀라면 제 집안 사람들 모두의 바람이 아주 큰 무게를 지녀야 한다고는 생각하오.”"]], [["\"I don't know whether Bell has been well brought up; but in such a matter as this nobody's wishes would weigh a feather with her; and, indeed, I could not take upon myself even to express a wish. To you I can say that I should have been very happy if she could have regarded her cousin as you wish her to do.\"", "“벨이 잘 자랐는지는 저도 모르겠어요. 하지만 이런 일에서라면 누구의 바람도 그 애에게는 깃털만 한 무게조차 되지 못할 거예요. 그리고 사실 저는 감히 제 바람을 말로 꺼낼 수조차 없어요. 시아주버니께는 이렇게 말씀드릴 수 있지요. 그 애가 제 사촌을 시아주버니께서 바라시는 대로 여겨 줄 수 있었다면 저는 아주 기뻤으리라고요.”"]], [["\"You mean that you are afraid to tell her so?\"", "“그 아이에게 그렇게 말하기가 두렵다는 거요?”"]], [["\"I am afraid to do what I think is wrong, if you mean that.\"", "“옳지 않다고 여기는 일을 하기가 두렵다는 거예요. 그런 뜻으로 하신 말씀이라면요.”"]], [["\"I don't think it would be wrong, and therefore I shall speak to her myself.\"", "“나는 그것이 옳지 않다고 생각하지 않소. 그러니 내가 직접 그 아이에게 말하겠소.”"]], [["\"You must do as you like about that, Mr.", "“그건 마음대로 하세요, 데일 씨."], ["Dale; I can't prevent you.", "제가 막을 수는 없으니까요."], ["I shall think you wrong to harass her on such a matter, and I fear also that her answer will not be satisfactory to you.", "그런 일로 그 애를 들볶으시는 것은 잘못이라고 저는 생각할 거예요. 그리고 그 애의 대답이 마음에 흡족하지 않으실 것도 걱정스럽고요."], ["If you choose to tell her your opinion, you must do so.", "그 애에게 뜻을 말씀하시겠다면 그렇게 하셔야지요."], ["Of course I shall think you wrong, that's all.\"", "물론 저는 잘못하시는 것이라고 생각할 테고요, 그뿐이에요.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale's voice as she said this was stern enough, and so was her countenance.", "이 말을 할 때 데일 부인의 목소리는 꽤나 매서웠고 낯빛도 그러했다."], ["She could not forbid the uncle to speak his mind to his niece, but she especially disliked the idea of any interference with her daughter.", "그녀는 삼촌이 조카딸에게 제 뜻을 말하는 것을 막을 수는 없었으나, 누가 제 딸의 일에 참견한다는 생각만은 각별히 싫었다."], ["The squire got up and walked about the room, trying to compose himself that he might answer her rationally, but without anger.", "지주는 자리에서 일어나 방 안을 오가며, 노여움 없이 조리 있게 그녀에게 대답할 수 있도록 마음을 가라앉히려 애썼다."]], [["\"May I go now?\"", "“이제 가도 될까요?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"May you go?", "“가도 되겠느냐고?"], ["Of course you may go if you like it.", "물론 가고 싶으면 가도 되오."], ["If you think that I am intruding upon you in speaking to you of the welfare of your two girls, whom I endeavour to regard as my own daughters,--except in this, that I know they have never been taught to love me,--if you think that it is an interference on my part to show anxiety for their welfare, of course you may go.\"", "내가 내 딸로 여기려고 애쓰는 두 아이의 안녕을 두고 내가 제수씨에게 말하는 것이—다만 그 아이들이 나를 사랑하도록 배운 적이 한 번도 없다는 것만은 나도 알고 있소만—그 아이들의 앞날을 걱정하는 것이 내 쪽의 참견이라고 여긴다면, 물론 가도 좋소.”"]], [["\"I did not mean to say anything to hurt you, Mr. Dale.\"", "“언짢게 해 드릴 말씀을 하려던 것은 아니었어요, 데일 씨.”"]], [["\"Hurt me!", "“언짢다니!"], ["What does it signify whether I am hurt or not?", "내가 언짢든 말든 그게 무슨 대수요?"], ["I have no children of my own, and of course my only business in life is to provide for my nephews and nieces.", "나에게는 자식이 없으니, 조카들과 조카딸들을 건사하는 것이 물론 내 삶의 유일한 일거리요."], ["I am an old fool if I expect that they are to love me in return, and if I venture to express a wish I am interfering and doing wrong!", "그 아이들이 그 답례로 나를 사랑해 주기를 바란다면 나는 늙은 바보지. 그리고 내가 감히 무슨 바람이라도 입에 올릴라치면 나는 참견을 하는 것이고 잘못을 저지르는 것이고!"], ["It is hard,--very hard.", "야박하오. 참으로 야박하오."], ["I know well that they have been brought up to dislike me, and yet I am endeavouring to do my duty by them.\"", "그 아이들이 나를 싫어하도록 자라 왔다는 것을 나도 잘 알고 있소. 그런데도 나는 그 아이들에게 내 도리를 다하려고 애쓰고 있단 말이오.”"]], [["\"Mr. Dale, that accusation has not been deserved.", "“데일 씨, 그런 나무람은 들을 까닭이 없는 것이에요."], ["They have not been brought up to dislike you.", "그 아이들은 데일 씨를 싫어하도록 자라지 않았어요."], ["I believe that they have both loved and respected you as their uncle; but such love and respect will not give you a right to dispose of their hands.\"", "저는 그 아이들이 제 삼촌으로서 데일 씨를 사랑하고 또 존경해 왔다고 믿어요. 하지만 그런 사랑과 존경이 그 아이들의 혼처를 마음대로 정할 권리를 드리지는 않아요.”"]], [["\"Who wants to dispose of their hands?\"", "“누가 그 아이들의 혼처를 마음대로 정하겠다는 거요?”"]], [["\"There are some things in which I think no uncle,--no parent,--should interfere, and of all such things this is the chief.", "“어떤 삼촌도, 어떤 부모도 참견해서는 안 된다고 제가 생각하는 일들이 있어요. 그리고 그 모든 일 가운데서도 이것이 으뜸이고요."], ["If after that you may choose to tell her your wishes, of course you can do so.\"", "그렇게 말씀드린 뒤에도 그 애에게 뜻을 이야기하시겠다면, 물론 그렇게 하실 수 있지요.”"]], [["\"It will not be much good after you have set her against me.\"", "“제수씨가 그 아이를 나에게서 등 돌리게 해 놓은 마당에 그래 봐야 별 소용도 없겠지.”"]], [["\"Mr. Dale, you have no right to say such things to me, and you are very unjust in doing so.", "“데일 씨, 저에게 그런 말씀을 하실 권리는 없어요."], ["If you think that I have set my girls against you, it will be much better that we should leave Allington altogether.", "그러시는 것은 아주 부당해요. 제가 제 딸들을 데일 씨께 등 돌리게 만들었다고 여기신다면, 저희가 앨링턴을 아주 떠나 버리는 편이 훨씬 낫겠어요."], ["I have been placed in circumstances which have made it difficult for me to do my duty to my children; but I have endeavoured to do it, not regarding my own personal wishes.", "저는 제 아이들에게 도리를 다하기 어려운 처지에 놓여 왔어요. 그래도 제 개인적인 바람은 돌아보지 않고 그 도리를 다하려고 애써 왔고요."], ["I am quite sure, however, that it would be wrong in me to keep them here, if I am to be told by you that I have taught them to regard you unfavourably.", "하지만 제가 아이들에게 데일 씨를 나쁘게 여기도록 가르쳤다는 말을 들어야 한다면, 아이들을 여기 붙들어 두는 것이야말로 제 잘못이 되리라고 아주 확신해요."], ["Indeed, I cannot suffer such a thing to be said to me.\"", "정말이지 저는 그런 말을 저에게 하시는 것을 견딜 수가 없어요.”"]], [["All this Mrs.", "이 모든 말을 데일 부인은 단호한 태도로, 그리고 상처를 입었다는 느낌이 배어 나오는 목소리로 했다."], ["Dale said with an air of decision, and with a voice expressing a sense of injury received, which made the squire feel that she was very much in earnest.", "그래서 지주는 그녀가 몹시 진심이라는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["\"Is it not true,\"", "“사실이 아니란 말이오?”"], ["he said, defending himself, \"that in all that relates to the girls you have ever regarded me with suspicion?\"", "그가 스스로를 변호하며 말했다. “그 아이들에 관한 모든 일에서 제수씨가 여태껏 나를 의심의 눈으로 보아 왔다는 것이?”"]], [["\"No, it is not true.\"", "“아니에요, 사실이 아니에요.”"], ["And then she corrected herself, feeling that there was something of truth in the squire's last assertion.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 지주의 마지막 말에 얼마간 진실이 있다고 느끼며 스스로 말을 고쳤다."], ["\"Certainly not with suspicion,\"", "“의심은 분명히 아니에요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"But as this matter has gone so far, I will explain what my real feelings have been.", "“하지만 이야기가 여기까지 온 마당이니, 제 참된 심정이 어떠했는지 밝히겠어요."], ["In worldly matters you can do much for my girls, and have done much.\"", "세상살이의 일에서 시아주버니는 제 아이들을 위해 많은 일을 해 주실 수 있고, 또 많이 해 주셨어요.”"]], [["\"And wish to do more,\"", "“그리고 더 해 주고 싶소.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"I am sure you do.", "“그러시리라고 저도 믿어요."], ["But I cannot on that account give up my place as their only living parent.", "하지만 그렇다고 해서 그 아이들의 하나뿐인 살아 있는 부모라는 제 자리를 내놓을 수는 없어요."], ["They are my children, and not yours.", "그 아이들은 제 자식이지 시아주버니의 자식이 아니니까요."], ["And even could I bring myself to allow you to act as their guardian and natural protector, they would not consent to such an arrangement.", "그리고 설령 제가 마음을 굽혀 시아주버니께서 그 아이들의 후견인이자 타고난 보호자 노릇을 하시도록 허락한다 해도, 그 아이들이 그런 처사에 동의하지 않을 거예요."], ["You cannot call that suspicion.\"", "그것을 의심이라고 부르실 수는 없어요.”"]], [["\"I can call it jealousy.\"", "“시샘이라고는 부를 수 있소.”"]], [["\"And should not a mother be jealous of her children's love?\"", "“그러면 어머니가 제 아이들의 사랑을 두고 시샘하지 말아야 하나요?”"]], [["During all this time the squire was walking up and down the room with his hands in his trousers pockets.", "이러는 내내 지주는 두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 방 안을 오르내리고 있었다."], ["And when Mrs. Dale had last spoken, he continued his walk for some time in silence.", "그리고 데일 부인이 마지막 말을 하고 나서도 그는 한동안 말없이 걸음을 이어 갔다."]], [["\"Perhaps it is well that you should have spoken out,\"", "“제수씨가 속을 털어놓은 것이 잘된 일인지도 모르겠소.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"The manner in which you accused me made it necessary.\"", "“저를 나무라시는 그 방식이 그럴 수밖에 없게 만들었지요.”"]], [["\"I did not intend to accuse you, and I do not do so now; but I think that you have been, and that you are, very hard to me,--very hard indeed.", "“나는 제수씨를 나무랄 뜻이 없었고 지금도 그렇지 않소. 하지만 제수씨가 나에게 몹시 야박했고 지금도 야박하다고 나는 생각하오."], ["I have endeavoured to make your children, and yourself also, sharers with me in such prosperity as has been mine.", "참으로 야박하오. 나는 내가 누린 만큼의 넉넉함을 제수씨의 아이들과, 또 제수씨 자신과도 나누려고 애써 왔소."], ["I have striven to add to your comfort and to their happiness.", "제수씨의 편안함과 그 아이들의 행복을 더해 주려고 힘써 왔소."], ["I am most anxious to secure their future welfare.", "그 아이들의 앞날을 지켜 주고 싶은 마음이 간절하오."], ["You would have been very wrong had you declined to accept this on their behalf; but I think that in return for it you need not have begrudged me the affection and obedience which generally follows from such good offices.\"", "그 아이들을 위해 이것을 받기를 마다했다면 제수씨가 아주 잘못한 것이었겠지. 하지만 그 답례로, 그만한 호의에 으레 따라오기 마련인 애정과 순종만큼은 나에게 아까워하지 않아도 좋았으리라고 나는 생각하오.”"]], [["\"Mr. Dale, I have begrudged you nothing of this.\"", "“데일 씨, 저는 그 가운데 무엇도 아까워한 적이 없어요.”"]], [["\"I am hurt;--I am hurt,\"", "“나는 상처를 받았소."], ["he continued.", "상처를 받았소.”"], ["And she was surprised by his look of pain even more than by the unaccustomed warmth of his words.", "그가 말을 이었다. 그리고 그녀는 그가 여느 때와 달리 뜨겁게 말하는 것보다도 그의 아파하는 얼굴빛에 더 놀랐다."], ["\"What you have said has, I have known, been the case all along.", "“제수씨가 한 말이 줄곧 사실이었다는 것을 나도 알고 있었소."], ["But though I had felt it to be so, I own that I am hurt by your open words.\"", "그러나 그렇게 느껴 오기는 했어도, 제수씨의 입에서 터놓고 나온 그 말에 내가 상처를 받은 것은 인정하오.”"]], [["\"Because I have said that my own children must ever be my own?\"", "“제 자식은 언제까지나 제 자식이어야 한다고 말했다고 해서요?”"]], [["\"Ah, you have said more than that.", "“아, 제수씨는 그보다 더한 말을 했소."], ["You and the girls have been living here, close to me, for--how many years is it now?--and during all those years there has grown up for me no kindly feeling.", "제수씨와 그 아이들이 여기, 내 곁에서 산 지가—이제 몇 해나 되었소?—그 세월 내내 나를 향한 다정한 마음이라고는 자라나지 않았소."], ["Do you think that I cannot hear, and see, and feel?", "내가 듣지도 보지도 느끼지도 못한다고 생각하오?"], ["Do you suppose that I am a fool and do not know?", "내가 바보라서 모른다고 여기는 거요?"], ["As for yourself you would never enter this house if you did not feel yourself constrained to do so for the sake of appearances.", "제수씨로 말하자면, 남의 눈 때문에 그래야 한다고 스스로를 억누르지 않는다면 이 집에 발도 들이지 않았을 거요."], ["I suppose it is all as it should be.", "아마 다 그래야 마땅한 대로겠지."], ["Having no children of my own, I owe the duty of a parent to my nieces; but I have no right to expect from them in return either love, regard, or obedience.", "나에게 자식이 없으니 나는 조카딸들에게 부모의 도리를 지고 있소. 그러나 그 답례로 사랑이든 존중이든 순종이든 그 아이들에게 바랄 권리는 나에게 없소."], ["I know I am keeping you here against your will, Mary.", "제수씨가 바라지도 않는데 내가 여기 붙들어 두고 있다는 것을 나도 알고 있소."], ["I won't do so any longer.\"", "더는 그러지 않으리다.”"], ["And he made a sign to her that she was to depart.", "그러고서 그는 이제 가도 좋다는 몸짓을 그녀에게 해 보였다."]], [["As she rose from her seat her heart was softened towards him.", "자리에서 일어서면서 그녀의 마음은 그를 향해 누그러졌다."], ["In these latter days he had shown much kindness to the girls,--a kindness that was more akin to the gentleness of love than had ever come from him before.", "요즈음 들어 그는 두 아가씨에게 많은 다정함을 보여 주었다. 여태껏 그에게서 나온 적이 없던, 사랑의 부드러움에 더 가까운 다정함이었다."], ["Lily's fate had seemed to melt even his sternness, and he had striven to be tender in his words and ways.", "릴리의 처지는 그의 준엄함마저 녹여 놓은 듯했고, 그는 말씨와 몸가짐에서 부드러워지려고 애써 왔다."], ["And now he spoke as though he had loved the girls, and had loved them in vain.", "그리고 지금 그는 그 아이들을 사랑했건만 그 사랑이 헛되었다는 듯이 말하고 있었다."], ["Doubtless he had been a disagreeable neighbour to his sister-in-law, making her feel that it was never for her personally that he had opened his hand.", "그가 제수에게 껄끄러운 이웃이었던 것은 틀림없었다. 그가 손을 편 것이 결코 그녀 자신을 위해서였던 적은 없다고 그녀가 늘 느끼게 만들었으니 말이다."], ["Doubtless he had been moved by an unconscious desire to undermine and take upon himself her authority with her own children.", "그가 저도 모르는 사이에, 아이들에 대한 그녀의 권위를 허물고 그것을 제가 차지하고 싶은 마음에 움직였던 것도 틀림없었다."], ["Doubtless he had looked askance at her from the first day of her marriage with his brother.", "그가 그녀를 제 아우와 혼인한 그날부터 곱지 않은 눈으로 보아 왔던 것도 틀림없었다."], ["She had been keenly alive to all this since she had first known him, and more keenly alive to it than ever since the failure of those efforts she had made to live with him on terms of affection, made during the first year or two of her residence at the Small House.", "그녀는 그를 알게 된 첫날부터 이 모든 것을 예민하게 느껴 왔고, 작은 집에 살기 시작한 첫 한두 해 동안 그와 정답게 지내 보려고 기울였던 노력이 실패로 돌아간 뒤로는 그 어느 때보다도 예민하게 느껴 왔다. 그러나 그럼에도 불구하고, 이 모든 것에도 불구하고 지금 그녀의 가슴은 그를 위해 피를 흘렸다."], ["But, nevertheless, in spite of all, her heart bled for him now.", "그녀는 아이들에 대한 제 자리를 온전히 지켜 냄으로써 그를 이겼다."], ["She had gained her victory over him, having fully held her own position with her children; but now that he complained that he had been beaten in the struggle, her heart bled for him.", "그러나 지금 그가 그 싸움에서 졌노라고 하소연하니 그녀의 가슴은 그를 위해 피를 흘렸다."]], [["\"My brother,\"", "“시아주버니.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, and as she spoke she offered him her hands, \"it may be that we have not thought as kindly of each other as we should have done.\"", "그러면서 그에게 두 손을 내밀었다. “어쩌면 우리는 서로를 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 다정하게 여기지 못했는지도 모르겠어요.”"]], [["\"I have endeavoured,\"", "“나는 애를 썼소.”"], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."], ["\"I have endeavoured--\"", "“나는 애를 썼소—”"], ["And then he stopped, either hindered by some excess of emotion, or unable to find the words which were necessary for the expression of his meaning.", "그러고는 말을 멈추었다. 북받치는 감정에 막혔거나, 아니면 제 뜻을 나타내는 데 꼭 필요한 말을 찾지 못했거나 한 것이었다."]], [["\"Let us endeavour once again,--both of us.\"", "“우리 한 번만 더 애써 봐요. 둘 다요.”"]], [["\"What, begin again at near seventy!", "“뭐라고, 일흔이 다 되어서 다시 시작을 하라고!"], ["No, Mary, there is no more beginning again for me.", "아니오, 제수씨. 나에게 다시 시작하는 일 따위는 이제 없소."], ["All this shall make no difference to the girls.", "이 모든 일이 그 아이들에게는 아무런 차이도 만들지 않을 거요."], ["As long as I am here they shall have the house.", "내가 여기 있는 한 그 아이들은 그 집을 갖게 될 거요."], ["If they marry, I will do for them what I can.", "혼인을 한다면 내가 할 수 있는 일을 해 주겠소."], ["I believe Bernard is much in earnest in his suit, and if Bell will listen to him, she shall still be welcomed here as mistress of Allington.", "버나드가 그 구애에 아주 진지하다고 나는 믿소. 그리고 벨이 그 애의 말에 귀를 기울인다면 그 아이는 앨링턴의 안주인으로서 여전히 이곳에 맞아들여질 거요."], ["What you have said shall make no difference;--but as to beginning again, it is simply impossible.\"", "제수씨가 한 말은 아무런 차이도 만들지 않을 거요. 그러나 다시 시작하는 것으로 말하자면, 그것은 그저 있을 수 없는 일이오.”"]], [["After that Mrs. Dale walked home through the garden by herself.", "그 뒤에 데일 부인은 홀로 정원을 지나 집으로 걸어갔다."], ["He had studiously told her that that house in which they lived should be lent, not to her, but to her children, during his lifetime.", "그는 저희가 살고 있는 그 집이 제가 살아 있는 동안 그녀에게가 아니라 그녀의 아이들에게 빌려주는 것임을 일부러 못 박아 말했다."], ["He had positively declined the offer of her warmer regard.", "그는 더 따뜻하게 여기겠다는 그녀의 뜻을 딱 잘라 물리쳤다."], ["He had made her understand that they were to look on each other almost as enemies; but that she, enemy as she was, should still be allowed the use of his munificence, because he chose to do his duty by his nieces!", "그는 두 사람이 서로를 거의 원수처럼 여겨야 한다는 것을 그녀가 알아듣게 만들었다. 다만 그녀는 원수이면서도 여전히 그의 후한 베풂을 누리도록 허락받으리라는 것도, 그리고 그것은 그가 제 조카딸들에게 도리를 다하기로 마음먹었기 때문이라는 것도!"]], [["\"It will be better for us that we shall leave it,\"", "“우리는 이곳을 떠나는 편이 낫겠어.”"], ["she said to herself as she seated herself in her own arm-chair over the drawing-room fire.", "응접실 난롯가의 제 안락의자에 앉으며 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVIII. DOCTOR CROFTS IS CALLED IN.", "# 제38장. 크로프츠 선생을 부르다"]], [["Mrs. Dale had not sat long in her drawing-room before tidings were brought to her which for a while drew her mind away from that question of her removal.", "데일 부인이 응접실에 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 한동안 그녀의 마음을 이사 문제에서 떼어 놓는 소식이 전해졌다."], ["\"Mamma,\"", "“엄마,”"], ["said Bell, entering the room, \"I really do believe that Jane has got scarlatina.\"", "벨이 방으로 들어서며 말했다. “아무래도 제인이 성홍열에 걸린 것 같아요.”"], ["Jane, the parlour-maid, had been ailing for the last two days, but nothing serious had hitherto been suspected.", "응접실 하녀 제인은 지난 이틀 동안 앓아 왔으나, 여태 대수로운 병이라고는 아무도 의심하지 않았다."]], [["Mrs. Dale instantly jumped up.", "데일 부인은 그 자리에서 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["\"Who is with her?\"", "“누가 그 애 곁에 있느냐?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["It appeared from Bell's answer that both she and Lily had been with the girl, and that Lily was still in the room.", "벨의 대답으로 보아 그녀와 릴리가 둘 다 그 아이 곁에 있었고, 릴리는 아직도 그 방에 있는 모양이었다."], ["Whereupon Mrs. Dale ran upstairs, and there was on the sudden a commotion in the house.", "그 말에 데일 부인은 위층으로 달려 올라갔고, 집 안은 별안간 소란스러워졌다."], ["In an hour or so the village doctor was there, and he expressed an opinion that the girl's ailment was certainly scarlatina. Mrs.", "한 시간 남짓 지나 마을 의사가 와서, 그 아이의 병은 틀림없이 성홍열이라는 소견을 내놓았다."], ["Dale, not satisfied with this, sent off a boy to Guestwick for Dr.", "데일 부인은 그것으로 만족하지 못했다."], ["Crofts, having herself maintained an opposition of many years' standing against the medical reputation of the apothecary, and gave a positive order to the two girls not to visit poor Jane again.", "그녀는 여러 해 전부터 그 약제사의 의술이 지닌 평판에 줄곧 반대해 온 터라, 크로프츠 선생을 부르러 게스트윅으로 아이를 하나 보냈고, 두 딸에게는 가엾은 제인을 다시는 찾아가지 말라고 단호히 명령했다."], ["She herself had had scarlatina, and might do as she pleased.", "그녀 자신은 성홍열을 앓은 적이 있으니 마음대로 해도 괜찮았다."], ["Then, too, a nurse was hired.", "그러고 나서 간병인도 한 사람 들였다."]], [["All this changed for a few hours the current of Mrs.", "이 모든 일이 몇 시간 동안 데일 부인의 생각의 물길을 돌려놓았다."], ["Dale's thoughts: but in the evening she went back to the subject of her morning conversation, and before the three ladies went to bed, they held together an open council of war upon the subject. Dr.", "그러나 저녁이 되자 그녀는 그날 아침 나눈 대화의 주제로 되돌아갔고, 세 여자는 잠자리에 들기 전에 그 문제를 두고 함께 공개 작전 회의를 열었다."], ["Crofts had been found to be away from Guestwick, and word had been sent on his behalf that he would be over at Allington early on the following morning.", "크로프츠 선생은 게스트윅에 없는 것으로 밝혀졌고, 대신 그가 이튿날 아침 일찍 앨링턴으로 건너오리라는 전갈이 왔다."], ["Mrs. Dale had almost made up her mind that the malady of her favourite maid was not scarlatina, but had not on that account relaxed her order as to the absence of her daughters from the maid's bedside.", "데일 부인은 자기가 아끼는 하녀의 병이 성홍열은 아니라고 거의 마음을 굳혔으나, 그렇다고 해서 딸들을 그 하녀의 머리맡에 얼씬도 못 하게 한 명령을 늦추지는 않았다."]], [["\"Let us go at once,\"", "“당장 나가요.”"], ["said Bell, who was even more opposed to any domination on the part of her uncle than was her mother.", "벨이 말했다. 그녀는 어머니보다도 더 삼촌 쪽의 어떤 지배도 참지 못하는 편이었다."], ["In the discussion which had been taking place between them the whole matter of Bernard's courtship had come upon the carpet.", "그들 사이에 오간 그 논의에서 버나드의 구혼 이야기가 통째로 도마 위에 올랐던 것이다."], ["Bell had kept her cousin's offer to herself as long as she had been able to do so; but since her uncle had pressed the subject upon Mrs. Dale, it was impossible for Bell to remain silent any longer.", "벨은 사촌의 청혼을 할 수 있는 데까지 오래 저 혼자 간직해 두었으나, 삼촌이 그 문제를 데일 부인에게 들이민 마당에 더 이상 입을 다물고 있기란 불가능했다."], ["\"You do not want me to marry him, mamma; do you?\"", "“엄마도 제가 그이와 혼인하기를 바라지는 않으시죠?”"], ["she had said, when her mother had spoken with some show of kindness towards Bernard. In answer to this, Mrs.", "어머니가 버나드에 대해 얼마쯤 다정한 기색으로 말하자 벨은 그렇게 말했다."], ["Dale had protested vehemently that she had no such wish, and Lily, who still held to her belief in Dr. Crofts, was almost equally animated.", "그에 답하여 데일 부인은 자기에게 그런 바람은 조금도 없다고 격하게 잘라 말했고, 여전히 크로프츠 선생에 대한 믿음을 버리지 않고 있던 릴리도 거의 같은 정도로 열을 올렸다."], ["To them all, the idea that their uncle should in any way interfere in their own views of life, on the strength of the pecuniary assistance which they had received from him, was peculiarly distasteful.", "그들 모두에게, 삼촌이 자기에게서 받은 금전적 도움을 빌미로 그들 자신의 인생관에 어떤 식으로든 간섭하려 든다는 생각은 유별나게 역겨웠다."], ["But it was especially distasteful that he should presume to have even an opinion as to their disposition in marriage.", "그중에서도 특히 역겨운 것은 그가 그들의 혼인 처분에 대해 의견 따위를 가지려 든다는 점이었다. 그들은 서로에게 이렇게 못 박았다."], ["They declared to each other that their uncle could have no right to object to any marriage which either of them might contemplate as long as their mother should approve of it.", "어머니만 찬성한다면, 두 사람 중 누가 어떤 혼인을 마음에 두든 삼촌에게는 그것을 반대할 권리가 있을 수 없다고. 가엾은 늙은 지주가 제 자신이 의심의 눈초리로 여겨지고 있다고 말한 것은 옳았다."], ["The poor old squire had been right in saying that he was regarded with suspicion.", "그는 정말로 그렇게 여겨지고 있었다. 그 잘못은 분명 그 자신의 것이었다."], ["He was so regarded. The fault had certainly been his own, in having endeavoured to win the daughters without thinking it worth his while to win the mother.", "어머니의 마음을 얻는 일은 제 수고를 들일 값어치가 없다고 여기면서 딸들의 마음만 얻으려 애썼기 때문이다."], ["The girls had unconsciously felt that the attempt was made, and had vigorously rebelled against it.", "딸들은 그런 시도가 행해지고 있음을 저도 모르게 느꼈고, 그것에 맹렬히 반발했다."], ["It had not been their fault that they had been brought to live in their uncle's house, and made to ride on his ponies, and to eat partially of his bread.", "그들이 삼촌의 집에 들어와 살게 되고, 그의 조랑말을 타게 되고, 그의 빵을 얼마간 얻어먹게 된 것은 그들의 잘못이 아니었다."], ["They had so eaten, and so lived, and declared themselves to be grateful.", "그들은 그렇게 먹고 그렇게 살았으며, 스스로 고마워하고 있노라고 말해 왔다."], ["The squire was good in his way, and they recognized his goodness; but not on that account would they transfer to him one jot of the allegiance which as children they owed to their mother.", "지주는 그 나름으로 좋은 사람이었고 그들도 그 선함을 인정했다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서, 자식으로서 어머니에게 바쳐야 할 그 충심을 한 톨이라도 그에게 옮겨 줄 생각은 없었다."], ["When she told them her tale, explaining to them the words which their uncle had spoken that morning, they expressed their regret that he should be so grieved; but they were strong in assurances to their mother that she had been sinned against, and was not sinning.", "어머니가 그날 아침 삼촌이 한 말을 설명하며 그 이야기를 들려주자, 그들은 그가 그토록 상심하게 된 것을 안타까워했다. 그러나 어머니를 향해서는, 어머니야말로 죄를 지은 쪽이 아니라 죄를 입은 쪽이라고 힘주어 못 박았다."]], [["\"Let us go at once,\"", "“당장 나가요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"It is much easier said than done, my dear.\"", "“말이 쉽지, 하기는 훨씬 어렵단다, 얘야.”"]], [["\"Of course it is, mamma; else we shouldn't be here now.", "“물론 그렇죠, 엄마. 안 그랬으면 우리가 지금 여기 있지도 않았을 테고요."], ["What I mean is this,--let us take some necessary first step at once.", "제 말은 이런 뜻이에요. 꼭 필요한 첫걸음을 당장 떼자는 거예요."], ["It is clear that my uncle thinks that our remaining here should give him some right over us.", "삼촌은 우리가 여기 머무는 이상 자기에게 우리에 대한 어떤 권리가 생긴다고 여기시는 게 분명해요."], ["I do not say that he is wrong to think so.", "그렇게 생각하시는 게 잘못이라는 말은 아니에요."], ["Perhaps it is natural.", "어쩌면 그게 당연한 일인지도 몰라요."], ["Perhaps, in accepting his kindness, we ought to submit ourselves to him.", "어쩌면 그분의 호의를 받는 이상, 우리는 그분께 우리 자신을 굽혀야 하는 건지도 모르고요."], ["If that be so, it is a conclusive reason for our going.\"", "만약 그렇다면, 그거야말로 우리가 나가야 할 결정적인 이유예요.”"]], [["\"Could we not pay him rent for the house,\" said Lily, \"as Mrs.", "“헌 부인처럼 우리도 삼촌께 이 집 집세를 내면 안 될까요?”"], ["Hearn does?", "릴리가 말했다."], ["You would like to remain here, mamma, if you could do that?\"", "“그렇게만 할 수 있다면 엄마는 여기 그냥 계시고 싶으시죠?”"]], [["\"But we could not do that, Lily.", "“하지만 그럴 수는 없단다, 릴리."], ["We must choose for ourselves a smaller house than this, and one that is not burdened with the expense of a garden.", "우리는 이 집보다 작은 집을, 그리고 정원 유지비가 딸리지 않은 집을 골라야 해."], ["Even if we paid but a moderate rent for this place, we should not have the means of living here.\"", "여기 집세를 어지간한 액수만 낸다고 해도 우리에게는 여기서 살아갈 형편이 못 될 거야.”"]], [["\"Not if we lived on toast and tea?\"", "“마른 빵과 차만 먹고 살아도요?”"], ["said Lily, laughing.", "릴리가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["\"But I should hardly wish you to live upon toast and tea; and indeed I fancy that I should get tired of such a diet myself.\"", "“하지만 나는 너희가 마른 빵과 차로 연명하기를 바라지 않는단다. 게다가 사실 나부터도 그런 식단에는 진저리가 나고 말 것 같구나.”"]], [["\"Never, mamma,\"", "“절대 아니실걸요, 엄마.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"As for me, I confess to a longing after mutton chops; but I don't think you would ever want such vulgar things.\"", "“저로 말하자면 양갈비가 그립다는 것을 실토하겠어요. 하지만 엄마는 그런 상스러운 것을 탐내신 적이 없을 것 같아요.”"]], [["\"At any rate, it would be impossible to remain here,\"", "“어쨌든 여기 남아 있기란 불가능해요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"Uncle Christopher would not take rent from mamma; and even if he did, we should not know how to go on with our other arrangements after such a change.", "“크리스토퍼 삼촌은 엄마한테서 집세를 받으려 하지 않으실 테고, 설령 받으신다 해도 그렇게 달라진 뒤에 우리 살림의 나머지를 어떻게 꾸려 가야 할지 알 수가 없어요. 안 돼요."], ["No; we must give up the dear old Small House.\"", "우리는 이 정든 옛 작은 집을 포기해야 해요.”"]], [["\"It is a dear old house,\"", "“정든 옛집이지요.”"], ["said Lily, thinking, as she spoke, more of those late scenes in the garden, when Crosbie had been with them in the autumn months, than of any of the former joys of her childhood.", "릴리가 말했다. 그렇게 말하면서 그녀가 떠올린 것은 어린 시절의 지난 즐거움들이라기보다, 가을 몇 달 동안 크로즈비가 그들과 함께 있었던 저 요즈음의 정원 풍경이었다."]], [["\"After all, I do not know that I should be right to move,\"", "“따지고 보면 내가 옮기는 것이 옳은 일인지 모르겠구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale, doubtingly.", "데일 부인이 미심쩍은 듯 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, yes,\"", "“맞아요, 맞고말고요.”"], ["said both the girls at once.", "두 딸이 한꺼번에 말했다."], ["\"Of course you will be right, mamma; there cannot be a doubt about it, mamma.", "“당연히 옳은 일이에요, 엄마. 그건 의심할 여지가 없어요, 엄마."], ["If we can get any cottage, or even lodgings, that would be better than remaining here, now that we know what uncle Christopher thinks of it.\"", "오두막이든, 하다못해 셋방이든 어디 하나 얻을 수만 있다면, 크리스토퍼 삼촌이 이 일을 어떻게 생각하시는지 알게 된 지금은 여기 남아 있는 것보다 그편이 나아요.”"]], [["\"It will make him very unhappy,\"", "“그분은 몹시 불행해하실 게다.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["But even this argument did not in the least move the girls.", "그러나 이 말조차 딸들의 마음을 조금도 움직이지 못했다."], ["They were very sorry that their uncle should be unhappy.", "그들은 삼촌이 불행해지는 것이 몹시 안타까웠다."], ["They would endeavour to show him by some increased show of affection that their feelings towards him were not unkind.", "그들은 애정을 좀 더 드러내 보임으로써 그를 향한 자기들의 감정이 매정한 것이 아님을 알려 드리려 애쓸 참이었다."], ["Should he speak to them they would endeavour to explain to him that their thoughts towards him were altogether affectionate.", "그가 말을 걸어온다면, 그를 향한 자기들의 마음이 오로지 다정한 것뿐임을 설명해 드리려 애쓸 참이었다."], ["But they could not remain at Allington increasing their load of gratitude, seeing that he expected a certain payment which they did not feel themselves able to render.", "그러나 그가 어떤 대가를 기대하고 있고 자기들은 그것을 치를 수 없다고 느끼는 마당에, 앨링턴에 남아 고마움의 짐을 더 무겁게 쌓아 갈 수는 없었다."]], [["\"We should be robbing him, if we stayed here,\"", "“여기 그대로 있는다면 우리는 삼촌의 것을 훔치는 셈이야.”"], ["Bell declared;--\"wilfully robbing him of what he believes to be his just share of the bargain.\"", "벨이 잘라 말했다. “그분이 그 거래에서 마땅히 자기 몫이라고 믿는 것을, 알면서도 훔치는 거지.”"]], [["So it was settled among them that notice should be given to their uncle of their intention to quit the Small House of Allington.", "그리하여 앨링턴 작은 집을 떠나겠다는 뜻을 삼촌에게 알리기로 그들 사이에서 결정이 났다."]], [["And then came the question as to their new home. Mrs.", "그러자 새 보금자리를 어디로 하느냐는 문제가 나왔다."], ["Dale was aware that her income was at any rate better than that possessed by Mrs. Eames, and therefore she had fair ground for presuming that she could afford to keep a house at Guestwick.", "데일 부인은 자기 수입이 적어도 임스 부인이 가진 것보다는 낫다는 것을 알고 있었으므로, 게스트윅에 집 한 채는 건사할 형편이 되리라고 짐작할 만한 근거는 충분했다."], ["\"If we do go away, that is what we must do,\"", "“우리가 정말 떠난다면, 그렇게 하는 수밖에 없겠지.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"And we shall have to walk out with Mary Eames, instead of Susan Boyce,\"", "“그럼 우리는 수전 보이스 대신에 메리 임스와 산책을 다니게 되겠네.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"It won't make so much difference after all.\"", "“따지고 보면 그리 큰 차이도 아니야.”"]], [["\"In that respect we shall gain as much as we lose,\"", "“그 점에서는 잃는 만큼 얻는 셈이지.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"And then it will be so nice to have the shops,\"", "“그리고 가게들이 가까이 있으니 얼마나 좋아.”"], ["said Lily, ironically.", "릴리가 빈정거리며 말했다."]], [["\"Only we shall never have any money to buy anything,\"", "“다만 우리한테 뭘 살 돈이 도무지 없을 뿐이지.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"But we shall see more of the world,\"", "“그래도 세상 구경은 더 하게 될 거야.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"Lady Julia's carriage comes into town twice a week, and the Miss Gruffens drive about in great style.", "“레이디 줄리아의 마차가 일주일에 두 번 읍내로 들어오고, 그러펀 댁 아가씨들은 아주 위풍당당하게 마차를 몰고 다니잖아."], ["Upon the whole, we shall gain a great deal; only for the poor old garden.", "통틀어 보면 우리가 얻는 게 많아. 가엾은 옛 정원만 빼면 말이야."], ["Mamma, I do think I shall break my heart at parting with Hopkins; and as to him, I shall be disappointed in mankind if he ever holds his head up again after I am gone.\"", "엄마, 저는 홉킨스와 헤어지면 정말 가슴이 찢어질 것 같아요. 그리고 그 사람으로 말하자면, 제가 떠난 뒤에도 그가 다시 고개를 들고 다닌다면 저는 인간이라는 것에 실망하고 말 거예요.”"]], [["But in truth there was very much of sadness in their resolution, and to Mrs.", "그러나 실은 그들의 결심에는 슬픔이 아주 많이 서려 있었다."], ["Dale it seemed as though she were managing matters badly for her daughters, and allowing poverty and misfortune to come upon them through her own fault.", "데일 부인에게는 자기가 딸들을 위해 일을 서투르게 처리하고 있는 것처럼, 제 잘못으로 가난과 불운이 딸들에게 닥치도록 내버려 두는 것처럼 여겨졌다."], ["She well knew how great a load of sorrow was lying on Lily's heart, hidden beneath those little attempts at pleasantry which she made.", "릴리가 부려 보이는 그 자잘한 우스개 밑에 얼마나 큰 슬픔의 짐이 그 아이의 가슴에 얹혀 있는지 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["When she spoke of being disappointed in mankind, Mrs. Dale could hardly repress an outward shudder that would betray her thoughts.", "그 아이가 인간이라는 것에 실망하고 만다고 말했을 때, 데일 부인은 제 속내를 들키고 말 몸서리를 겉으로 내지 않으려고 가까스로 참았다."], ["And now she was consenting to take them forth from their comfortable home, from the luxury of their lawns and gardens, and to bring them to some small dingy corner of a provincial town,--because she had failed to make herself happy with her brother-in-law.", "그런데 이제 그녀는 딸들을 그 안락한 보금자리에서, 그 잔디밭과 정원의 호사에서 끌어내어 지방 읍내의 비좁고 칙칙한 한 귀퉁이로 데려가는 데 동의하고 있었다. 시숙과 사이좋게 지내는 데 실패했다는 이유로 말이다."], ["Could she be right to give up all the advantages which they enjoyed at Allington,--advantages which had come to them from so legitimate a source,--because her own feelings had been wounded?", "자기 감정이 상했다는 이유로, 딸들이 앨링턴에서 누리던 그 모든 이점을—그토록 정당한 출처에서 그들에게 온 이점을—포기하는 것이 과연 옳을 수 있을까?"], ["In all their future want of comfort, in the comfortless dowdiness of the new home to which she would remove them, would she not always blame herself for having brought them to that by her own false pride?", "앞으로 겪을 온갖 궁색함 속에서, 자기가 딸들을 옮겨 놓을 새집의 볼품없는 초라함 속에서, 그녀는 제 그릇된 자존심으로 딸들을 그 지경에 데려다 놓았다고 늘 스스로를 탓하게 되지 않을까?"], ["And yet it seemed to her that she now had no alternative.", "그러면서도 그녀에게는 이제 달리 길이 없는 것처럼 여겨졌다."], ["She could not now teach her daughters to obey their uncle's wishes in all things.", "이제 와서 딸들에게 모든 일에서 삼촌의 뜻에 따르라고 가르칠 수는 없었다."], ["She could not make Bell understand that it would be well that she should marry Bernard because the squire had set his heart on such a marriage.", "지주가 그런 혼인에 마음을 두었다는 이유로 버나드와 혼인하는 편이 좋겠다고 벨에게 납득시킬 수도 없었다."], ["She had gone so far that she could not now go back.", "그녀는 이미 너무 멀리 와 버려서 이제는 되돌아갈 수가 없었다."]], [["\"I suppose we must move at Lady-day?\"", "“성모영보 축일에는 옮겨야겠죠?”"], ["said Bell, who was in favour of instant action.", "당장 움직이자는 쪽인 벨이 말했다."], ["\"If so, had you not better let uncle Christopher know at once?\"", "“그렇다면 크리스토퍼 삼촌께 곧바로 알리시는 편이 낫지 않을까요?”"]], [["\"I don't think that we can find a house by that time.\"", "“그때까지 집을 구할 수 있을 것 같지 않구나.”"]], [["\"We can get in somewhere,\"", "“어디든 들어갈 데는 있어요.”"], ["continued Bell.", "벨이 말을 이었다."], ["\"There are plenty of lodgings in Guestwick, you know.\"", "“게스트윅에는 셋방이 얼마든지 있잖아요.”"], ["But the sound of the word lodgings was uncomfortable in Mrs. Dale's ears.", "그러나 셋방이라는 말의 울림은 데일 부인의 귀에 거북했다."]], [["\"If we are to go, let us go at once,\"", "“가야 한다면 당장 나가요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"We need not stand much upon the order of our going.\"", "“나가는 차례 따위를 따지고 있을 것 없어요.”"]], [["\"Your uncle will be very much shocked,\"", "“너희 삼촌은 크게 충격을 받으실 게다.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"He cannot say that it is your fault,\"", "“그게 엄마 잘못이라고는 하실 수 없어요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["It was thus agreed between them that the necessary information should be at once given to the squire, and that the old, well-loved house should be left for ever.", "이리하여 필요한 통고를 지주에게 곧바로 하기로, 그리고 그 오래도록 사랑해 온 집을 영영 떠나기로 그들 사이에 합의가 이루어졌다."], ["It would be a great fall in a worldly point of view,--from the Allington Small House to an abode in some little street of Guestwick.", "세속의 눈으로 보자면 그것은 크게 굴러떨어지는 일이었다—앨링턴 작은 집에서 게스트윅의 어느 자그마한 거리에 있는 거처로."], ["At Allington they had been county people,--raised to a level with their own squire and other squires by the circumstance of their residence; but at Guestwick they would be small even among the people of the town.", "앨링턴에서 그들은 고을의 상류 사람들이었고, 사는 곳이라는 사정 덕분에 자기네 지주며 다른 지주들과 같은 반열에 올라 있었다. 그러나 게스트윅에서는 읍내 사람들 축에서도 보잘것없는 축이 될 것이었다."], ["They would be on an equality with the Eameses, and much looked down upon by the Gruffens.", "임스네와 동등한 처지가 되고, 그러펀네에게는 한참 내려다보일 것이었다."], ["They would hardly dare to call any more at Guestwick Manor, seeing that they certainly could not expect Lady Julia to call upon them at Guestwick.", "게스트윅 저택에 다시 인사를 가겠다고 감히 나설 수도 없을 터였다. 레이디 줄리아가 게스트윅으로 자기들을 찾아오리라고는 도저히 기대할 수 없을 테니 말이다."], ["Mrs. Boyce no doubt would patronize them, and they could already anticipate the condolence which would be offered to them by Mrs.", "보이스 부인은 틀림없이 그들에게 생색을 내려 들 것이고, 헌 부인이 건네 올 위로의 말은 벌써부터 눈에 선했다."], ["Hearn. Indeed such a movement on their part would be tantamount to a confession of failure in the full hearing of so much of the world as was known to them.", "실로 그런 이사는 그들이 아는 한의 온 세상이 다 듣는 자리에서 실패를 자백하는 것이나 마찬가지였다."]], [["I must not allow my readers to suppose that these considerations were a matter of indifference to any of the ladies at the Small House.", "나는 나의 독자들이 이런 고려가 작은 집의 어느 여인에게든 아무래도 좋은 일이었으리라고 짐작하도록 내버려 둘 수 없다."], ["To some women of strong mind, of highly-strung philosophic tendencies, such considerations might have been indifferent.", "마음이 굳세고 사색으로 팽팽하게 벼려진 어떤 여인들에게라면 그런 고려쯤은 아무래도 좋은 것이었을지 모른다."], ["But Mrs. Dale was not of this nature, nor were her daughters.", "그러나 데일 부인은 그런 천성이 아니었고, 그녀의 딸들도 그러했다."], ["The good things of the world were good in their eyes, and they valued the privilege of a pleasant social footing among their friends.", "세상의 좋은 것들은 그들의 눈에도 좋았고, 그들은 벗들 사이에서 기분 좋은 사회적 자리를 차지하는 특권을 소중히 여겼다."], ["They were by no means capable of a wise contempt of the advantages which chance had hitherto given to them.", "여태 우연이 그들에게 안겨 준 이점들을 현명하게 경멸할 수 있는 사람들은 결코 아니었다."], ["They could not go forth rejoicing in the comparative poverty of their altered condition.", "처지가 바뀌어 상대적으로 가난해지는 것을 기뻐하며 떠날 수 있는 사람들이 아니었던 것이다."], ["But then, neither could they purchase those luxuries which they were about to abandon at the price which was asked for them.", "그러나 그렇다고 해서, 이제 버리려 하는 그 호사를 그 값에 사들일 수도 없는 노릇이었다."]], [["\"Had you not better write to my uncle?\"", "“삼촌께 편지를 쓰시는 편이 낫지 않을까요?”"], ["said one of the girls. But to this Mrs.", "두 딸 가운데 하나가 말했다."], ["Dale objected that she could not make a letter on such a subject clearly intelligible, and that therefore she would see the squire on the following morning.", "그러나 이에 대해 데일 부인은, 그런 문제를 두고서는 알아듣기 쉽게 편지를 쓸 수 없으니 이튿날 아침 지주를 직접 만나겠다고 반대했다."], ["\"It will be very dreadful,\"", "“아주 끔찍할 게다.”"], ["she said, \"but it will soon be over.", "그녀가 말했다. “하지만 곧 끝날 일이야."], ["It is not what he will say at the moment that I fear so much, as the bitter reproaches of his face when I shall meet him afterwards.\"", "내가 무서운 것은 그분이 그 자리에서 하실 말씀이라기보다, 그 뒤에 만날 때 그분의 얼굴에 어릴 쓰라린 원망이란다.”"], ["So, on the following morning, she again made her way, and now without invitation, to the squire's study.", "그리하여 이튿날 아침 그녀는 다시, 이번에는 부름도 없이, 지주의 서재로 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["\"Mr. Dale,\"", "“데일 씨,”"], ["she began, starting upon her work with some confusion in her manner, and hurry in her speech, \"I have been thinking over what we were saying together yesterday, and I have come to a resolution which I know I ought to make known to you without a moment's delay.\"", "그녀는 몸가짐에 얼마쯤 당황한 기색을 띠고 말을 서두르며 제 할 일을 시작했다. “어제 우리가 함께 나눈 이야기를 곰곰이 생각해 보았고, 한시도 미루지 말고 시아주버니께 알려 드려야 한다고 여겨지는 결심에 이르렀습니다.”"]], [["The squire also had thought of what had passed between them, and had suffered much as he had done so; but he had thought of it without acerbity or anger.", "지주 역시 두 사람 사이에 오간 일을 생각했고, 그러면서 적잖이 괴로워했다. 그러나 그의 생각에는 모진 기색도 노여움도 없었다."], ["His thoughts were ever gentler than his words, and his heart softer than any exponent of his heart that he was able to put forth.", "그의 생각은 언제나 그의 말보다 부드러웠고, 그의 마음은 그가 밖으로 내놓을 수 있는 그 어떤 마음의 표현보다도 물렀다."], ["He wished to love his brother's children, and to be loved by them; but even failing that, he wished to do good to them.", "그는 아우의 자식들을 사랑하고 또 그들에게서 사랑받기를 바랐다. 그러나 설령 그것이 이루어지지 않더라도 그들에게 잘해 주고 싶었다."], ["It had not occurred to him to be angry with Mrs. Dale after that interview was over.", "그 면담이 끝난 뒤에 데일 부인에게 화를 낸다는 생각은 그에게 떠오르지도 않았다."], ["The conversation had not gone pleasantly with him; but then he hardly expected that things would go pleasantly.", "그 대화는 그에게 유쾌하게 흘러가지 않았다. 그러나 애초에 그는 일이 유쾌하게 굴러가리라고 기대하는 사람도 아니었다."], ["No idea had occurred to him that evil could come upon any of the Dale ladies from the words which had then been spoken.", "그때 오간 말들 때문에 데일 집안 여자들 가운데 누구에게든 나쁜 일이 닥칠 수 있으리라는 생각은 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다."], ["He regarded the Small House as their abode and home as surely as the Great House was his own.", "그는 작은 집을 그들의 거처이자 보금자리로 여겼으니, 큰 집이 제 것인 것과 꼭 마찬가지였다."], ["In giving him his due, it must be declared that any allusion to their holding these as a benefit done to them by him had been very far from his thoughts. Mrs.", "그에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려주자면, 그들이 그 집을 자기가 베푼 은혜로 붙들고 있다는 식의 암시는 그의 생각에서 아주 멀리 떨어져 있었다고 밝혀 두어야 한다."], ["Hearn, who held her cottage at half its real value, grumbled almost daily at him as her landlord; but it never occurred to him that therefore he should raise her rent, or that in not doing so he was acting with special munificence.", "제 오두막을 실제 값의 절반에 붙들고 있는 헌 부인은 집주인인 그를 두고 거의 날마다 투덜거렸다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 그녀의 집세를 올려야겠다는 생각도, 올리지 않는 것이 각별히 후한 처사라는 생각도 그에게는 떠오르지 않았다."], ["It had ever been to him a grumbling, cross-grained, unpleasant world; and he did not expect from Mrs.", "그에게 이 세상은 늘 투덜거리고 뒤틀리고 언짢은 곳이었다."], ["Hearn, or from his sister-in-law, anything better than that to which he had ever been used.", "그리하여 그는 헌 부인에게서든 제수에게서든 여태 익숙해져 온 것보다 더 나은 무엇을 기대하지 않았다."]], [["\"It will make me very happy,\"", "“그 결심이 벨과 그 사촌의 혼사와 관계있는 것이라면,”"], ["said he, \"if it has any bearing on Bell's marriage with her cousin.\"", "그가 말했다. “나로서는 아주 기쁘겠소.”"]], [["\"Mr. Dale, that is out of the question.", "“데일 씨, 그건 있을 수 없는 일입니다."], ["I would not vex you by saying so if I were not certain of it; but I know my child so well!\"", "확신이 없다면 이런 말씀으로 시아주버니를 언짢게 해 드리지도 않겠지요. 하지만 저는 제 아이를 너무나 잘 압니다!”"]], [["\"Then we must leave it to time, Mary.\"", "“그렇다면 그 일은 세월에 맡겨 둡시다, 제수씨.”"]], [["\"Yes, of course; but no time will suffice to make Bell change her mind.", "“네, 물론이지요. 하지만 아무리 세월이 흘러도 벨의 마음을 바꾸어 놓지는 못할 겁니다."], ["We will, however, leave the subject. And now, Mr.", "그렇더라도 그 이야기는 접어 두지요."], ["Dale, I have to tell you of something else;--we have resolved to leave the Small House.\"", "그리고 이제, 데일 씨, 다른 말씀을 드려야겠습니다. 저희는 작은 집을 떠나기로 결심했습니다.”"]], [["\"Resolved on what?\"", "“무엇을 결심했다고?”"], ["said the squire, turning his eyes full upon her.", "지주가 그녀에게로 눈을 똑바로 돌리며 말했다."]], [["\"We have resolved to leave the Small House.\"", "“저희는 작은 집을 떠나기로 결심했습니다.”"]], [["\"Leave the Small House!\"", "“작은 집을 떠난다니!”"], ["he said, repeating her words; \"and where on earth do you mean to go?\"", "그가 그녀의 말을 되뇌며 말했다. “대체 어디로 가겠다는 거요?”"]], [["\"We think we shall go into Guestwick.\"", "“게스트윅으로 갈까 합니다.”"]], [["\"And why?\"", "“어째서?”"]], [["\"Ah, that is so hard to explain.", "“아, 그건 참으로 설명하기 어렵습니다."], ["If you would only accept the fact as I tell it to you, and not ask for the reasons which have guided me!\"", "제가 말씀드리는 그대로 사실만 받아 주시고, 저를 그렇게 이끈 이유는 묻지 않아 주신다면 좋겠습니다만!”"]], [["\"But that is out of the question, Mary.", "“하지만 그건 있을 수 없는 일이오, 제수씨. 그런 일이라면 나는 제수씨의 이유를 물어야만 하오."], ["In such a matter as that I must ask your reasons; and I must tell you also that, in my opinion, you will not be doing your duty to your daughters in carrying out such an intention, unless your reasons are very strong indeed.\"", "그리고 이 말도 해 두어야겠소. 내 생각으로는, 이유가 정말로 어지간히 강한 것이 아닌 한, 그런 뜻을 밀고 나가는 것은 제수씨가 딸들에 대한 도리를 다하는 일이 못 되오.”"]], [["\"But they are very strong,\"", "“하지만 그 이유는 아주 강합니다.”"], ["said Mrs.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["Dale; and then she paused.", "그러고는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["\"I cannot understand it,\"", "“나는 이해할 수가 없소.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"I cannot bring myself to believe that you are really in earnest.", "“제수씨가 정말로 진심이라고는 도무지 믿어지지가 않소."], ["Are you not comfortable there?\"", "거기서 편치 않소?”"]], [["\"More comfortable than we have any right to be with our means.\"", "“저희 형편에 누릴 자격이 있는 것보다 더 편안하지요.”"]], [["\"But I thought you always did very nicely with your money.", "“하지만 나는 제수씨가 늘 돈을 아주 알뜰하게 꾸려 간다고 여겼소."], ["You never get into debt.\"", "빚을 지는 일도 없지 않소.”"]], [["\"No; I never get into debt.", "“네, 저는 빚을 지지 않습니다."], ["It is not that, exactly.", "딱히 그 때문은 아닙니다."], ["The fact is, Mr.", "사실은 이렇습니다, 데일 씨."], ["Dale, we have no right to live there without paying rent; but we could not afford to live there if we did pay rent.\"", "저희에게는 집세를 내지 않고 거기서 살 권리가 없습니다. 그렇다고 집세를 낸다면 거기서 살아갈 형편이 못 되고요.”"]], [["\"Who has talked about rent?\"", "“집세라니 누가 그런 말을 했소?”"], ["he said, jumping up from his chair.", "그가 의자에서 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."], ["\"Some one has been speaking falsehoods of me behind my back.\"", "“누군가 내 등 뒤에서 나를 두고 거짓말을 지껄인 게로군.”"], ["No gleam of the real truth had yet come to him.", "진상의 빛줄기는 아직 그에게 조금도 비쳐 들지 않았다."], ["No idea had reached his mind that his relatives thought it necessary to leave his house in consequence of any word that he himself had spoken.", "제 입으로 한 어떤 말 때문에 제 친척들이 제 집을 떠나야겠다고 여기게 되었다는 생각은 그의 머리에 이르지 않았다."], ["He had never considered himself to have been in any special way generous to them, and would not have thought it reasonable that they should abandon the house in which they had been living, even if his anger against them had been strong and hot.", "그는 자기가 그들에게 특별히 너그럽게 굴었다고 여겨 본 적이 한 번도 없었고, 설령 그들을 향한 제 노여움이 세차고 뜨거웠다 한들 그들이 여태 살아온 집을 버리는 것이 사리에 맞는다고는 생각하지 않았을 것이다."], ["\"Mary,\"", "“제수씨,”"], ["he said, \"I must insist upon getting to the bottom of this.", "그가 말했다. “나는 이 일의 밑바닥까지 알아내야겠소."], ["As for your leaving the house, it is out of the question.", "그 집을 떠난다는 것으로 말하면, 그건 있을 수 없는 일이오."], ["Where can you be better off, or so well?", "어디로 간들 지금보다 형편이 나을 수 있소?"], ["As to going into Guestwick, what sort of life would there be for the girls?", "아니, 그만하기라도 하겠소? 게스트윅으로 들어가는 것으로 말하면, 아이들에게 거기가 무슨 살 곳이 되겠소?"], ["I put all that aside as out of the question; but I must know what has induced you to make such a proposition.", "그런 것은 있을 수 없는 일로 죄다 제쳐 두겠소. 그러나 무엇이 제수씨를 부추겨 그런 소리를 꺼내게 했는지는 알아야겠소."], ["Tell me honestly,--has any one spoken evil of me behind my back?\"", "솔직히 말해 보시오. 누가 내 등 뒤에서 나를 나쁘게 말한 사람이 있소?”"]], [["Mrs. Dale had been prepared for opposition and for reproach; but there was a decision about the squire's words, and an air of masterdom in his manner, which made her recognize more fully than she had yet done the difficulty of her position.", "데일 부인은 반대와 나무람에 대비하고 있었다."], ["She almost began to fear that she would lack power to carry out her purpose.", "그러나 지주의 말에는 단호함이 있었고 그 태도에는 위엄이 서려 있어서, 그녀는 제 처지의 어려움을 여태보다 한결 또렷하게 깨닫게 되었다. 그녀는 제 뜻을 밀고 나갈 힘이 모자라지 않을까 하는 두려움마저 들기 시작했다."]], [["\"Indeed, it is not so, Mr. Dale.\"", "“정말이지 그런 것이 아닙니다, 데일 씨.”"]], [["\"Then what is it?\"", "“그럼 대체 무엇이오?”"]], [["\"I know that if I attempt to tell you, you will be vexed, and will contradict me.\"", "“말씀드리려 들면 시아주버니께서 언짢아하시고 저를 반박하실 줄 압니다.”"]], [["\"Vexed I shall be, probably.\"", "“언짢기야 하겠지, 아마도.”"]], [["\"And yet I cannot help it.", "“그런데도 저로서는 어쩔 도리가 없습니다."], ["Indeed, I am endeavouring to do what is right by you and by the children.\"", "정말로 저는 시아주버니께도 아이들에게도 옳은 일을 하려고 애쓰는 중입니다.”"]], [["\"Never mind me; your duty is to think of them.\"", "“나는 아무래도 좋소. 제수씨의 도리는 그 아이들을 생각하는 것이오.”"]], [["\"Of course it is; and in doing this they most cordially agree with me.\"", "“물론 그렇지요. 그리고 이 일에서 그 아이들은 저와 진심으로 뜻이 같습니다.”"]], [["In using such argument as that, Mrs. Dale showed her weakness, and the squire was not slow to take advantage of it.", "그런 논거를 끌어댐으로써 데일 부인은 제 약점을 드러냈고, 지주는 그것을 놓치지 않고 파고들었다."], ["\"Your duty is to them,\"", "“제수씨의 도리는 그 아이들에게 있소.”"], ["he said; \"but I do not mean by that that your duty is to let them act in any way that may best please them for the moment.", "그가 말했다. “그러나 그 말은 그 아이들이 당장 제 마음에 가장 드는 대로 무엇이든 하도록 내버려 두는 것이 제수씨의 도리라는 뜻은 아니오."], ["I can understand that they should be run away with by some romantic nonsense, but I cannot understand it of you.\"", "그 아이들이 무슨 낭만적인 헛소리에 휩쓸려 가는 것이야 나도 이해할 수 있소. 그러나 제수씨마저 그러는 것은 이해할 수가 없소.”"]], [["\"The truth is this, Mr. Dale.", "“사실은 이렇습니다, 데일 씨."], ["You think that my children owe to you that sort of obedience which is due to a parent, and as long as they remain here, accepting from your hands so large a part of their daily support, it is perhaps natural that you should think so.", "시아주버니께서는 제 아이들이 부모에게 바쳐야 할 그런 복종을 시아주버니께 빚지고 있다고 여기시지요. 그리고 그 아이들이 여기 머무르며 나날의 살림 가운데 그토록 큰 몫을 시아주버니 손에서 받는 이상, 그렇게 여기시는 것이 어쩌면 당연한 일인지도 모릅니다."], ["In this unhappy affair about Bell--\"", "벨을 둘러싼 이 딱한 일에서도—”"]], [["\"I have never said anything of the kind,\"", "“나는 그런 말은 한 적이 없소.”"], ["said the squire, interrupting her.", "지주가 그녀의 말을 가로막으며 말했다."]], [["\"No; you have not said so.", "“네, 그렇게 말씀하시지는 않았지요."], ["And I do not wish you to think that I make any complaint.", "그리고 제가 무슨 불평이라도 하는 것으로 여기지 않으셨으면 합니다."], ["But I feel that it is so, and they feel it.", "하지만 저는 그렇다고 느끼고, 아이들도 그렇게 느낍니다."], ["And, therefore, we have made up our minds to go away.\"", "그래서 저희는 떠나기로 마음을 굳혔습니다.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale, as she finished, was aware that she had not told her story well, but she had acknowledged to herself that it was quite out of her power to tell it as it should be told.", "데일 부인은 말을 마치면서 자기가 이야기를 잘하지 못했음을 알고 있었다."], ["Her main object was to make her brother-in-law understand that she certainly would leave his house, and to make him understand this with as little pain to himself as possible.", "그러나 마땅히 이야기되어야 할 방식으로 그것을 이야기하기란 제 힘에 도무지 부치는 일임을 그녀는 스스로 인정한 터였다. 그녀의 주된 목적은 자기가 반드시 그의 집을 떠나리라는 것을 시숙에게 납득시키는 것, 그리고 그에게 되도록 적은 아픔을 주면서 그것을 납득시키는 것이었다."], ["She did not in the least mind his thinking her foolish, if only she could so carry her point as to be able to tell her daughters on her return that the matter was settled.", "돌아가서 딸들에게 일이 매듭지어졌다고 말할 수만 있게 제 뜻을 관철할 수 있다면, 그가 자기를 어리석다고 여기는 것쯤은 조금도 개의치 않았다."], ["But the squire, from his words and manners, seemed indisposed to give her this privilege.", "그러나 지주는 그 말과 몸가짐으로 보아 그녀에게 그런 호사를 내줄 마음이 없어 보였다."]], [["\"Of all the propositions which I ever heard,\"", "“내가 여태 들어 본 온갖 소리 가운데,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"it is the most unreasonable.", "“그것이 가장 사리에 닿지 않는 소리요."], ["It amounts to this, that you are too proud to live rent-free in a house which belongs to your husband's brother, and therefore you intend to subject yourself and your children to the great discomfort of a very straitened income.", "그 말을 간추리면 이런 것이오. 제수씨는 남편의 형에게 딸린 집에서 집세 없이 사는 것이 자존심에 걸린다, 그러니 제 몸과 제 아이들을 몹시 쪼들리는 수입의 큰 고생 속으로 밀어 넣겠다."], ["If you yourself only were concerned I should have no right to say anything; but I think myself bound to tell you that, as regards the girls, everybody that knows you will think you to have been very wrong.", "제수씨 자신만 걸린 일이라면 나에게는 무슨 말을 할 권리도 없소. 그러나 아이들로 말하면, 제수씨를 아는 사람은 누구나 제수씨가 아주 잘못했다고 여기리라는 것만은 내가 일러 주어야 할 도리라고 생각하오."], ["It is in the natural course of things that they should live in that house.", "그 아이들이 그 집에서 사는 것은 일의 자연스러운 순리요."], ["The place has never been let.", "그 집은 한 번도 세를 놓은 적이 없소."], ["As far as I know, no rent has ever been paid for the house since it was built.", "내가 아는 한 그 집이 지어진 뒤로 그 집에 집세가 매겨진 적은 한 번도 없었소."], ["It has always been given to some member of the family, who has been considered as having the best right to it.", "그 집은 언제나 그것을 누릴 가장 큰 권리가 있다고 여겨진 집안 사람에게 주어졌소."], ["I have considered your footing there as firm as my own here.", "나는 제수씨가 그곳에 발붙이고 선 자리를 내가 여기 선 자리만큼이나 든든한 것으로 여겨 왔소."], ["A quarrel between me and your children would be to me a great calamity, though, perhaps, they might be indifferent to it.", "나와 제수씨 아이들 사이의 다툼은 나에게 큰 재앙일 것이오. 어쩌면 그 아이들에게는 아무렇지도 않은 일일지 모르지만 말이오."], ["But if there were such a quarrel it would afford no reason for their leaving that house.", "그러나 설령 그런 다툼이 있다 한들, 그것이 그 아이들이 그 집을 떠날 이유가 되지는 않소."], ["Let me beg you to think over the matter again.\"", "부디 이 일을 다시 한 번 곰곰이 생각해 주기를 청하오.”"]], [["The squire could assume an air of authority on certain occasions, and he had done so now. Mrs.", "지주는 어떤 자리에서는 위엄 있는 태도를 지어 보일 줄 알았고, 지금이 바로 그러했다."], ["Dale found that she could only answer him by a simple repetition of her own intention; and, indeed, failed in making him any serviceable answer whatsoever.", "데일 부인은 자기가 그에게 대답할 수 있는 것이라고는 제 뜻을 그저 되풀이하는 것뿐임을 깨달았다. 아니, 실은 그에게 쓸 만한 대답이라고는 무엇 하나 내놓지 못했다."]], [["\"I know that you are very good to my girls,\"", "“시아주버니께서 제 아이들에게 아주 잘해 주신다는 것은 압니다.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"I will say nothing about that,\"", "“그 이야기는 하지 않겠소.” 그가 대답했다."], ["he answered; not thinking at that moment of the Small House, but of the full possession which he had desired to give to the elder of all the privileges which should belong to the mistress of Allington,--thinking also of the means by which he was hoping to repair poor Lily's shattered fortunes.", "그 순간 그가 생각하고 있던 것은 작은 집이 아니었다. 앨링턴의 안주인에게 마땅히 딸릴 그 모든 특권을 맏이에게 온전히 넘겨주고 싶어 했던 마음이었고, 아울러 가엾은 릴리의 부서진 앞날을 자기가 어떤 방편으로 기워 보려 하고 있는지였다."], ["What words were further said had no great significance, and Mrs. Dale got herself away, feeling that she had failed.", "그 뒤에 오간 말들에는 별다른 뜻이 없었고, 데일 부인은 자기가 실패했다고 느끼며 그 자리를 물러 나왔다."], ["As soon as she was gone the squire arose, and putting on his great-coat, went forth with his hat and stick to the front of the house.", "그녀가 나가자마자 지주는 일어나 두꺼운 외투를 걸치고, 모자와 지팡이를 들고 집 앞으로 나섰다."], ["He went out in order that his thoughts might be more free, and that he might indulge in that solace which an injured man finds in contemplating his injury.", "그가 밖으로 나간 것은 생각을 좀 더 자유로이 풀어놓기 위해서였고, 상처 입은 사람이 제 상처를 들여다보며 얻는 그 위안에 잠겨 보기 위해서였다."], ["He declared to himself that he was very hardly used,--so hardly used, that he almost began to doubt himself and his own motives.", "그는 스스로에게 자기가 참으로 모진 대접을 받고 있다고 되뇌었다. 어찌나 모진 대접인지, 그는 자기 자신과 자기의 속내마저 의심하기 시작할 지경이었다."], ["Why was it that the people around him disliked him so strongly,--avoided him and thwarted him in the efforts which he made for their welfare?", "어째서 주위 사람들은 그를 그토록 싫어하는 것일까? 어째서 그를 피하고, 그들의 행복을 위해 그가 기울이는 노력을 어깃장 놓는 것일까?"], ["He offered to his nephew all the privileges of a son,--much more indeed than the privileges of a son,--merely asking in return that he would consent to live permanently in the house which was to be his own.", "그는 조카에게 아들의 온갖 특권을, 아니 실은 아들의 특권보다 훨씬 더한 것을 내주었다. 그러면서 그 대가로 청한 것이라고는 장차 제 것이 될 그 집에서 눌러살아 달라는 것뿐이었다."], ["But his nephew refused.", "그러나 조카는 거절했다."], ["\"He cannot bear to live with me,\"", "“그 애는 나와 함께 사는 것을 못 견디는 게지.”"], ["said the old man to himself sorely.", "노인은 쓰라린 마음으로 혼잣말을 했다."], ["He was prepared to treat his nieces with more generosity than the daughters of the House of Allington had usually received from their fathers; and they repelled his kindness, running away from him, and telling him openly that they would not be beholden to him.", "그는 앨링턴 가문의 딸들이 여태 제 아버지에게서 받아 온 것보다 더 후하게 조카딸들을 대할 작정이었다. 그런데 그들은 그의 호의를 뿌리치고 그에게서 달아나며, 그에게 신세 지지 않겠노라고 대놓고 말하는 것이었다."], ["He walked slowly up and down the terrace, thinking of this very bitterly.", "그는 그것을 몹시 쓰라리게 곱씹으며 테라스를 천천히 오르내렸다."], ["He did not find in the contemplation of his grievance all that solace which a grievance usually gives, because he accused himself in his thoughts rather than others.", "그러나 제 억울함을 들여다보면서도 억울함이 으레 주게 마련인 그 위안을 다 얻지는 못했다. 생각 속에서 그가 탓한 것은 남이라기보다 자기 자신이었기 때문이다."], ["He declared to himself that he was made to be hated, and protested to himself that it would be well that he should die and be buried out of memory, so that the remaining Dales might have a better chance of living happily; and then as he thus discussed all this within his own bosom, his thoughts were very tender, and though he was aggrieved, he was most affectionate to those who had most injured him.", "그는 자기가 미움받도록 생겨 먹은 사람이라고 스스로에게 못 박았고, 자기가 죽어 기억에서 묻혀 버려 남은 데일 집안사람들이 좀 더 행복하게 살 기회를 얻는 편이 좋겠다고 스스로에게 항변했다. 그러고는 이 모든 것을 제 가슴속에서 그렇게 따져 보는 동안 그의 생각은 무척이나 다정했다."], ["But it was absolutely beyond his power to reproduce outwardly, with words and outward signs, such thoughts and feelings.", "그리하여 그는 억울함을 당하고서도, 자기를 가장 아프게 한 이들에게 가장 애틋했다. 그러나 그런 생각과 감정을 말과 겉으로 드러나는 표시로 밖에 내보이는 일은 도무지 그의 힘에 부치는 것이었다."]], [["It was now very nearly the end of the year, but the weather was still soft and open.", "이제 한 해가 거의 저물어 가고 있었으나 날씨는 아직 부드럽고 푸근했다."], ["The air was damp rather than cold, and the lawns and fields still retained the green tints of new vegetation.", "공기는 차다기보다 눅눅했고, 잔디밭과 들판은 아직도 새로 돋은 풀의 푸른빛을 머금고 있었다."], ["As the squire was walking on the terrace Hopkins came up to him, and touching his hat, remarked that they should have frost in a day or two.", "지주가 테라스를 거닐고 있을 때 홉킨스가 그에게 다가와 모자에 손을 대며, 하루 이틀 사이에 서리가 내리겠다고 말했다."]], [["\"I suppose we shall,\"", "“그렇겠지.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"We must have the mason to the flues of that little grape-house, sir, before I can do any good with a fire there.\"", "“저 작은 포도 온실 연관에 미장이를 불러야 합니다요, 나리. 그러기 전에는 거기다 불을 때 봐야 아무 소용이 없습니다요.”"]], [["\"Which grape-house?\"", "“어느 포도 온실 말인가?”"], ["said the squire, crossly.", "지주가 퉁명스럽게 말했다."]], [["\"Why, the grape-house in the other garden, sir.", "“아니, 저쪽 정원에 있는 포도 온실 말입니다요, 나리."], ["It ought to have been done last year by rights.\"", "따지고 보면 작년에 벌써 했어야 할 일입니다요.”"], ["This Hopkins said to punish his master for being cross to him.", "홉킨스가 이렇게 말한 것은 자기에게 퉁명스럽게 군 주인을 벌주려는 것이었다."], ["On that matter of the flues of Mrs. Dale's grape-house he had, with much consideration, spared his master during the last winter, and he felt that this ought to be remembered now.", "데일 부인네 포도 온실 연관 문제로 말하자면, 그는 지난겨울 내내 주인을 아주 많이 헤아려 주며 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않고 봐주었고, 이제 그 일이 기억되어야 마땅하다고 여겼다."], ["\"I can't put any fire in it, not to do any real good, till something's done.", "“뭔가 손을 대기 전에는 거기다 불을 때 봐야 제대로 될 리가 없습니다요."], ["That's sure.\"", "그건 틀림없습니다요.”"]], [["\"Then don't put any fire in it,\"", "“그럼 불을 때지 말게.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["Now the grapes in question were supposed to be peculiarly fine, and were the glory of the garden of the Small House.", "그런데 문제의 그 포도는 유난히 좋다고들 하는 것이었고 작은 집 정원의 자랑거리였다."], ["They were always forced, though not forced so early as those at the Great House, and Hopkins was in a state of great confusion.", "그 포도는 늘 촉성으로 길렀는데, 큰 집의 것만큼 이르게 재촉하지는 않았다. 홉킨스는 몹시 어리둥절한 처지가 되었다."]], [["\"They'll never ripen, sir; not the whole year through.\"", "“그러면 절대로 익지 않습니다요, 나리. 한 해가 다 가도록 말입니다요.”"]], [["\"Then let them be unripe,\"", "“그럼 안 익은 채로 두게.”"], ["said the squire, walking about.", "지주가 거닐면서 말했다."]], [["Hopkins did not at all understand it.", "홉킨스로서는 도무지 알 수가 없었다."], ["The squire in his natural course was very unwilling to neglect any such matter as this, but would be specially unwilling to neglect anything touching the Small House.", "지주는 본디 이런 일이라면 어느 것 하나 손 놓고 내버려 두기를 아주 싫어하는 사람이었고, 그중에서도 작은 집에 관련된 일이라면 유난히 더 그러했다."], ["So Hopkins stood on the terrace, raising his hat and scratching his head.", "그리하여 홉킨스는 테라스에 선 채 모자를 들었다 놓으며 머리를 긁적였다."], ["\"There's something wrong amongst them,\"", "“저 어른들 사이에 뭔가 잘못된 게 있구먼.”"], ["said he to himself, sorrowfully.", "그는 서글프게 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["But when the squire had walked to the end of the terrace and had turned upon the path which led round the side of the house, he stopped and called to Hopkins.", "그러나 지주는 테라스 끝까지 걸어가 집 옆으로 돌아가는 길로 접어들었다가, 걸음을 멈추고 홉킨스를 불렀다."]], [["\"Have what is needful done to the flue,\"", "“그 연관에 필요한 일은 해 두게.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, sir; very well, sir.", "“예, 나리. 잘 알겠습니다요, 나리."], ["It'll only be re-setting the bricks.", "벽돌만 다시 놓으면 됩니다요."], ["Nothing more ain't needful, just this winter.\"", "이번 겨울에는 그 밖에 더 필요한 것도 없습니다요.”"]], [["\"Have the place put in perfect order while you're about it,\"", "“하는 김에 그 자리를 아주 말끔하게 손봐 두게.”"], ["said the squire, and then he walked away.", "지주가 말했고, 그러고는 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIX. DR. CROFTS IS TURNED OUT.", "# 제39장. 크로프츠 선생이 내쫓기다"]], [["\"Have you heard the news, my dear, from the Small House?\" said Mrs.", "“여보, 작은 집 소식 들으셨어요?”"], ["Boyce to her husband, some two or three days after Mrs. Dale's visit to the squire.", "데일 부인이 지주를 찾아간 지 이틀인가 사흘 뒤에 보이스 부인이 남편에게 말했다."], ["It was one o'clock, and the parish pastor had come in from his ministrations to dine with his wife and children.", "한 시였고, 교구 목사는 사목 일을 마치고 아내와 아이들과 함께 식사를 하려고 막 들어온 참이었다."]], [["\"What news?\"", "“무슨 소식 말이오?”"], ["said Mr.", "보이스 씨가 말했다."], ["Boyce, for he had heard none.", "그는 아무 소식도 듣지 못했던 것이다."]], [["\"Mrs.", "“데일 부인과 아가씨들이 작은 집을 떠난대요."], ["Dale and the girls are going to leave the Small House; they're going into Guestwick to live.\"", "게스트윅으로 들어가서 산다지 뭐예요.”"]], [["\"Mrs. Dale going away; nonsense!\"", "“데일 부인이 떠난다니, 말도 안 되는 소리!”"], ["said the vicar.", "목사가 말했다."], ["\"What on earth should take her into Guestwick?", "“대체 무슨 일로 게스트윅엘 들어간단 말이오?"], ["She doesn't pay a shilling of rent where she is.\"", "지금 사는 데서는 집세를 한 실링도 물지 않는데.”"]], [["\"I can assure you it's true, my dear. I was with Mrs.", "“정말이라니까요, 여보."], ["Hearn just now, and she had it direct from Mrs. Dale's own lips.", "제가 방금 헌 부인과 함께 있었는데, 부인이 데일 부인 입으로 직접 들었답니다."], ["Mrs. Hearn said she'd never been taken so much aback in her whole life.", "헌 부인 말로는 평생 그렇게 놀라 본 적이 없다는군요."], ["There's been some quarrel, you may be sure of that.\"", "무슨 다툼이 있었던 거예요. 그건 틀림없어요.”"]], [["Mr. Boyce sat silent, pulling off his dirty shoes preparatory to his dinner.", "보이스 씨는 말없이 앉은 채 식사에 앞서 흙 묻은 구두를 벗었다."], ["Tidings so important, as touching the social life of his parish, had not come to him for many a day, and he could hardly bring himself to credit them at so short a notice.", "제 교구의 사교 생활에 관한 이토록 중대한 소식은 여러 날 동안 그에게 들어온 적이 없었고, 그로서는 그렇게 대뜸 그것을 곧이곧대로 믿을 수가 없었다."]], [["\"Mrs. Hearn says that Mrs. Dale spoke ever so firmly about it, as though determined that nothing should change her.\"", "“헌 부인 말로는 데일 부인이 그 이야기를 어찌나 단단하게 하던지, 무엇으로도 마음을 돌릴 수 없겠다 싶은 투였다는군요.”"]], [["\"And did she say why?\"", "“그래서 까닭은 말합디까?”"]], [["\"Well, not exactly. But Mrs.", "“글쎄요, 딱히 그러지는 않았대요."], ["Hearn said she could understand there had been words between her and the squire.", "하지만 헌 부인은 데일 부인과 지주 사이에 말다툼이 있었던 게로구나 짐작이 가더랍니다."], ["It couldn't be anything else, you know.", "그것 말고 무슨 일이 있겠어요."], ["Probably it had something to do with that man Crosbie.\"", "아마 그 크로즈비라는 사내와 관련된 일이겠지요.”"]], [["\"They'll be very pushed about money,\" said Mr.", "“그 집 사람들은 돈에 몹시 쪼들리게 될 게요.”"], ["Boyce, thrusting his feet into his slippers.", "보이스 씨가 실내화에 발을 밀어 넣으며 말했다."]], [["\"That's just what I said to Mrs. Hearn.", "“저도 헌 부인한테 꼭 그렇게 말했답니다."], ["And those girls have never been used to anything like real economy.", "게다가 그 아가씨들은 진짜 절약이라는 걸 해 본 적이 없잖아요."], ["What's to become of them I don't know;\" and Mrs.", "앞으로 그 애들이 어떻게 될지 저는 모르겠어요.”"], ["Boyce, as she expressed her sympathy for her dear friends, received considerable comfort from the prospect of their future poverty.", "그리고 보이스 부인은 제 소중한 벗들을 향한 동정을 그렇게 늘어놓으면서, 그들의 앞날에 놓인 가난을 내다보는 데서 적잖은 위안을 얻었다."], ["It always is so, and Mrs. Boyce was not worse than her neighbours.", "세상일이란 늘 그런 법이고, 보이스 부인이 제 이웃들보다 더 못된 사람인 것도 아니었다."]], [["\"You'll find they'll make it up before the time comes,\" said Mr.", "“때가 되기 전에 저희끼리 화해할 거요, 두고 보시오.”"], ["Boyce, to whom the excitement of such a change in affairs was almost too good to be true.", "보이스 씨가 말했다. 그로서는 그런 변고가 주는 흥분이 사실이라기에는 너무 좋은 것이었다."]], [["\"I am afraid not,\" said Mrs.", "“그러지 못할 것 같아요.”"], ["Boyce; \"I'm afraid not.", "보이스 부인이 말했다."], ["They are both so determined.", "“그러지 못할 것 같다니까요."], ["I always thought that riding and giving the girls hats and habits was injurious.", "두 분 다 어찌나 고집이 센지요. 저는 늘 말을 태우고 아가씨들한테 모자며 승마복을 사 주는 게 해로운 일이라고 생각했어요."], ["It was treating them as though they were the squire's daughters, and they were not the squire's daughters.\"", "그건 마치 그 애들이 지주의 딸인 양 대하는 것이었는데, 그 애들은 지주의 딸이 아니잖아요.”"]], [["\"It was almost the same thing.\"", "“그거나 그거나 매한가지였지.”"]], [["\"But now we see the difference,\"", "“하지만 이제 그 차이가 드러난 거지요.”"], ["said the judicious Mrs. Boyce.", "분별 있는 보이스 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I often said that dear Mrs. Dale was wrong, and it turns out that I was right.", "“저는 소중한 데일 부인이 잘못하고 있다고 여러 번 말했고, 결국 제가 옳았던 것으로 드러난 거예요."], ["It will make no difference to me, as regards calling on them and that sort of thing.\"", "저야 그분들을 찾아뵙고 하는 그런 일에서는 아무것도 달라질 게 없지만요.”"]], [["\"Of course it won't.\"", "“당연히 달라질 게 없지.”"]], [["\"Not but what there must be a difference, and a very great difference too.", "“그렇다고 차이가 없을 수는 없어요. 그것도 아주 큰 차이가요."], ["It will be a terrible come down for poor Lily, with the loss of her fine husband and all.\"", "가엾은 릴리한테는 지독한 몰락이 되겠지요, 그 훌륭한 남편감까지 잃은 마당에 말이에요.”"]], [["After dinner, when Mr. Boyce had again gone forth upon his labours, the same subject was discussed between Mrs.", "식사가 끝나고 보이스 씨가 다시 제 일터로 나간 뒤에, 보이스 부인과 딸들 사이에서도 같은 이야기가 오갔다."], ["Boyce and her daughters, and the mother was very careful to teach her children that Mrs. Dale would be just as good a person as ever she had been, and quite as much a lady, even though she should live in a very dingy house at Guestwick; from which lesson the Boyce girls learned plainly that Mrs.", "어머니는 아이들에게 데일 부인이 게스트윅의 아주 초라한 집에 살게 되더라도 여전히 예전과 똑같이 훌륭한 분이고 조금도 다름없는 숙녀라는 것을 가르치려고 몹시 애를 썼다."], ["Dale, with Bell and Lily, were about to have a fall in the world, and that they were to be treated accordingly.", "그 가르침에서 보이스 집안 아가씨들은 데일 부인이 벨과 릴리와 더불어 세상에서 한 계단 굴러떨어지려 하고 있으며, 따라서 그에 걸맞게 대해야 한다는 것을 똑똑히 배웠다."]], [["From all this, it will be discovered that Mrs. Dale had not given way to the squire's arguments, although she had found herself unable to answer them.", "이 모든 것으로 미루어, 데일 부인이 지주의 논리에 대답하지는 못했으면서도 그 앞에 굽히지는 않았다는 것을 알 수 있을 것이다."], ["As she had returned home she had felt herself to be almost vanquished, and had spoken to the girls with the air and tone of a woman who hardly knew in which course lay the line of her duty.", "집으로 돌아오면서 그녀는 거의 패배했다고 느꼈고, 자기 본분이 어느 길에 놓여 있는지 도무지 알 수 없는 여인의 낯빛과 목소리로 딸들에게 이야기했다."], ["But they had not seen the squire's manner on the occasion, nor heard his words, and they could not understand that their own purpose should be abandoned because he did not like it.", "그러나 딸들은 그 자리에서 지주가 어떤 태도를 보였는지 보지 못했고 그가 무슨 말을 했는지도 듣지 못했으므로, 그가 싫어한다고 해서 자기들의 뜻을 접어야 한다는 것을 납득할 수 없었다."], ["So they talked their mother into fresh resolves, and on the following morning she wrote a note to her brother-in-law, assuring him that she had thought much of all that he had said, but again declaring that she regarded herself as bound in duty to leave the Small House.", "그리하여 딸들은 이야기를 거듭해 어머니를 다시 새로운 결심으로 이끌었고, 이튿날 아침 그녀는 시숙에게 쪽지를 한 장 써 보냈다. 그가 한 말을 모두 곰곰이 생각해 보았노라고 다짐하면서도, 자기는 작은 집을 떠나는 것이 도리라고 여긴다고 다시 한번 못 박은 쪽지였다."], ["To this he had returned no answer, and she had communicated her intention to Mrs. Hearn, thinking it better that there should be no secret in the matter.", "이에 그는 아무 답도 보내지 않았고, 그녀는 이 일을 비밀로 삼지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각해 헌 부인에게 제 뜻을 알렸다."]], [["\"I am sorry to hear that your sister-in-law is going to leave us,\"", "“제수분께서 우리 곁을 떠나신다니 안타깝습니다.”"], ["Mr. Boyce said to the squire that same afternoon.", "바로 그날 오후에 보이스 씨가 지주에게 말했다."]], [["\"Who told you that?\"", "“누가 그럽디까?”"], ["asked the squire, showing by his tone that he by no means liked the topic of conversation which the parson had chosen.", "지주가 물었다. 그 말투로 보아 목사가 고른 이야깃거리가 도무지 마음에 들지 않는 것이 분명했다."]], [["\"Well, I had it from Mrs. Boyce, and I think Mrs. Hearn told her.\"", "“글쎄요, 저는 보이스 부인한테 들었습니다만, 아마 헌 부인이 알려 준 모양입니다.”"]], [["\"I wish Mrs. Hearn would mind her own business, and not spread idle reports.\"", "“헌 부인이 남의 일에 참견하지 말고 쓸데없는 소문이나 퍼뜨리지 않았으면 좋겠소.”"]], [["The squire said nothing more, and Mr. Boyce felt that he had been very unjustly snubbed.", "지주는 더 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 보이스 씨는 자기가 몹시 부당하게 홀대를 당했다고 느꼈다."]], [["Dr. Crofts had come over and pronounced as a fact that it was scarlatina.", "크로프츠 선생이 건너와서 그것이 성홍열이 맞다고 확진했다."], ["Village apothecaries are generally wronged by the doubts which are thrown upon them, for the town doctors when they come always confirm what the village apothecaries have said.", "시골 약제사들은 대개 사람들이 던지는 의심 때문에 억울한 일을 당하는데, 도시에서 의사가 와 보면 언제나 시골 약제사가 한 말을 그대로 확인해 주기 때문이다."]], [["\"There can be no doubt as to its being scarlatina,\"", "“성홍열인 것은 의심할 여지가 없습니다.”"], ["the doctor declared; \"but the symptoms are all favourable.\"", "의사가 단언했다. “하지만 증세는 다 순조롭습니다.”"]], [["There was, however, much worse coming than this.", "그러나 이보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 일이 오고 있었다."], ["Two days afterwards Lily found herself to be rather unwell.", "이틀 뒤에 릴리가 몸이 좀 좋지 않다는 것을 스스로 알아차렸다."], ["She endeavoured to keep it to herself, fearing that she should be brought under the doctor's notice as a patient; but her efforts were unavailing, and on the following morning it was known that she had also taken the disease.", "그녀는 자기가 환자로 의사의 눈에 들게 될까 두려워 그것을 감추려 했으나 애쓴 보람이 없었고, 이튿날 아침에는 그녀 역시 그 병에 걸렸다는 것이 드러났다."], ["Dr. Crofts declared that everything was in her favour.", "크로프츠 선생은 모든 것이 그녀에게 유리하다고 말했다."], ["The weather was cold. The presence of the malady in the house had caused them all to be careful, and, moreover, good advice was at hand at once.", "날씨가 춥고, 집 안에 병이 들어와 있던 터라 모두가 조심하고 있었으며, 게다가 좋은 지시를 곧바로 얻을 수 있는 형편이었다."], ["The doctor begged Mrs. Dale not to be uneasy, but he was very eager in begging that the two sisters might not be allowed to be together.", "의사는 데일 부인에게 마음을 놓으시라고 청했으나, 두 자매를 함께 있게 해서는 안 된다는 것만은 몹시 간절히 당부했다."], ["\"Could you not send Bell into Guestwick,--to Mrs.", "“벨을 게스트윅으로, 임스 부인 댁으로 보내시면 어떻겠습니까?”"], ["Eames's?\" said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["But Bell did not choose to be sent to Mrs. Eames's, and was with great difficulty kept out of her mother's bedroom, to which Lily as an invalid was transferred.", "그러나 벨은 임스 부인 댁으로 보내지기를 마다했고, 병자가 된 릴리가 옮겨진 어머니의 침실 밖에 붙들어 두는 것조차 몹시 어려웠다."]], [["\"If you will allow me to say so,\"", "“이런 말씀을 드려도 된다면,”"], ["he said to Bell, on the second day after Lily's complaint had declared itself, \"you are wrong to stay here in the house.\"", "릴리의 병이 드러난 지 이틀째 되는 날 그가 벨에게 말했다. “이 집에 남아 계시는 건 잘못하시는 겁니다.”"]], [["\"I certainly shall not leave mamma, when she has got so much upon her hands,\"", "“어머니가 저렇게 손이 모자라시는데 제가 어머니를 두고 갈 수는 없어요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"But if you should be taken ill she would have more on her hands,\"", "“하지만 아가씨까지 앓아누우시면 어머니 손이 더 모자라게 되지요.”"], ["pleaded the doctor.", "의사가 간청했다."]], [["\"I could not do it,\"", "“그렇게는 못 해요.”"], ["Bell replied.", "벨이 대답했다."], ["\"If I were taken over to Guestwick, I should be so uneasy that I should walk back to Allington the first moment that I could escape from the house.\"", "“게스트윅으로 옮겨진다 해도, 마음이 놓이지 않아서 그 집에서 빠져나올 수 있는 첫 순간에 앨링턴까지 걸어서 돌아오고 말걸요.”"]], [["\"I think your mother would be more comfortable without you.\"", "“어머니께서는 아가씨가 없는 편이 더 마음 편하실 것 같습니다만.”"]], [["\"And I think she would be more comfortable with me.", "“저는 제가 있는 편이 어머니 마음이 더 편하실 것 같아요."], ["I don't ever like to hear of a woman running away from illness; but when a sister or a daughter does so, it is intolerable.\"", "저는 여자가 병을 피해 달아났다는 이야기는 듣기가 늘 싫어요. 하물며 자매나 딸이 그런다면 견딜 수 없는 일이지요.”"], ["So Bell remained, without permission indeed to see her sister, but performing various outside administrations which were much needed.", "그리하여 벨은 남았다. 동생을 볼 수 있다는 허락은 받지 못했지만, 꼭 필요한 여러 바깥일들을 해 나가면서."]], [["And thus all manner of trouble came upon the inhabitants of the Small House, falling upon them as it were in a heap together.", "그리하여 온갖 곤경이 한꺼번에 무더기로 작은 집 식구들에게 쏟아져 내렸다."], ["It was as yet barely two months since those terrible tidings had come respecting Crosbie; tidings which, it was felt at the time, would of themselves be sufficient to crush them; and now to that misfortune other misfortunes had been added,--one quick upon the heels of another.", "크로즈비에 관한 그 끔찍한 소식이 온 지 아직 두 달도 채 되지 않았고, 그 소식만으로도 그들을 짓뭉개기에 넉넉하리라고 그때 다들 여겼는데, 이제 그 불행 위에 다른 불행들이 겹쳐 얹힌 것이다. 그것도 하나가 지나가기 무섭게 다음 것이 뒤따라서."], ["In the teeth of the doctor's kind prophecy Lily became very ill, and after a few days was delirious.", "의사의 다정한 예언을 비웃기라도 하듯 릴리는 몹시 앓았고, 며칠 뒤에는 헛소리를 하기에 이르렀다."], ["She would talk to her mother about Crosbie, speaking of him as she used to speak in the autumn that was passed.", "그녀는 어머니에게 크로즈비 이야기를 하고는 했는데, 지나간 가을에 그를 이야기하던 그 말투 그대로였다."], ["But even in her madness she remembered that they had resolved to leave their present home; and she asked the doctor twice whether their lodgings in Guestwick were ready for them.", "그러면서도 정신을 놓은 가운데서도 지금 사는 집을 떠나기로 했다는 것만은 기억하고 있어서, 게스트윅의 셋집이 준비되었느냐고 의사에게 두 번이나 물었다."]], [["It was thus that Crofts first heard of their intention.", "크로프츠가 그들의 뜻을 처음 알게 된 것은 그렇게 해서였다."], ["Now, in these days of Lily's worst illness, he came daily over to Allington, remaining there, on one occasion, the whole night.", "릴리의 병이 가장 무겁던 그 며칠 동안 그는 날마다 앨링턴으로 건너왔고, 한번은 밤을 꼬박 새우기까지 했다. 이 모든 일에 그는 진찰비를 받으려 하지 않았다."], ["For all this he would take no fee;--nor had he ever taken a fee from Mrs.", "애초에 데일 부인에게서 진찰비를 받은 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["Dale. \"I wish you would not come so often,\"", "“이렇게 자주 오지 않으셨으면 좋겠어요.”"], ["Bell said to him one evening, as he stood with her at the drawing-room fire, after he had left the patient's room; \"you are overloading us with obligations.\"", "어느 날 저녁, 그가 환자의 방에서 나와 응접실 난롯가에 그녀와 함께 서 있을 때 벨이 그에게 말했다. “저희에게 갚지도 못할 신세를 너무 많이 지우고 계세요.”"], ["On that day Lily was over the worst of the fever, and he had been able to tell Mrs. Dale that he did not think that she was now in danger.", "그날 릴리는 열의 고비를 넘겼고, 그는 데일 부인에게 이제 위험한 고비는 아닌 것 같다고 말할 수 있었다."]], [["\"It will not be necessary much longer,\"", "“이제 그리 오래 올 일도 없을 겁니다.”"], ["he said; \"the worst of it is over.\"", "그가 말했다. “고비는 넘겼으니까요.”"]], [["\"It is such a luxury to hear you say so.", "“그 말씀을 듣는 것만으로도 이런 호사가 없네요."], ["I suppose we shall owe her life to you; but nevertheless--\"", "우리는 저 애 목숨을 선생님께 빚진 셈이겠지요. 그렇지만—”"]], [["\"Oh, no; scarlatina is not such a terrible thing now as it used to be.\"", "“아, 아닙니다. 성홍열은 이제 예전만큼 무서운 병이 아니에요.”"]], [["\"Then why should you have devoted your time to her as you have done?", "“그러면 어째서 저 애한테 그렇게까지 시간을 다 바치셨어요?"], ["It frightens me when I think of the injury we must have done you.\"", "저희가 선생님께 끼쳤을 손해를 생각하면 겁이 다 나요.”"]], [["\"My horse has felt it more than I have,\"", "“저보다 제 말이 더 힘들었을 겁니다.”"], ["said the doctor, laughing.", "의사가 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"My patients at Guestwick are not so very numerous.\"", "“게스트윅에 제 환자가 그렇게 많지도 않고요.”"], ["Then, instead of going, he sat himself down.", "그러고 나서 그는 갈 생각을 하기는커녕 자리에 앉았다."], ["\"And it is really true,\"", "“그런데 정말입니까?”"], ["he said, \"that you are all going to leave this house?\"", "그가 말했다. “다들 이 집을 떠나신다는 게.”"]], [["\"Quite true.", "“정말이에요."], ["We shall do so at the end of March, if Lily is well enough to be moved.\"", "릴리를 옮겨도 될 만큼 나으면 삼월 말에 떠날 거예요.”"]], [["\"Lily will be well long before that, I hope; not, indeed, that she ought to be moved out of her own rooms for many weeks to come yet.\"", "“릴리는 그보다 훨씬 전에 나을 겁니다. 물론 앞으로도 몇 주 동안은 제 방 밖으로 옮겨서는 안 되겠습니다만.”"]], [["\"Unless we are stopped by her we shall certainly go at the end of March.\"", "“저 애 때문에 발이 묶이지만 않는다면 삼월 말에는 틀림없이 떠날 거예요.”"], ["Bell now had also sat down, and they both remained for some time looking at the fire in silence.", "이제 벨도 자리에 앉았고, 두 사람은 한동안 말없이 불만 바라보고 있었다."]], [["\"And why is it, Bell?\"", "“그런데 왜 그러시는 겁니까, 벨?”"], ["he said, at last.", "마침내 그가 말했다."], ["\"But I don't know whether I have a right to ask.\"", "“하기야 제게 그런 걸 물을 권리가 있는지는 모르겠습니다만.”"]], [["\"You have a right to ask any question about us,\"", "“선생님께는 저희에 대해 무엇이든 물으실 권리가 있어요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"My uncle is very kind.", "“삼촌은 아주 친절하세요."], ["He is more than kind; he is generous.", "친절한 정도가 아니라 너그러우시지요."], ["But he seems to think that our living here gives him a right to interfere with mamma.", "하지만 저희가 여기 사는 것 때문에 어머니 일에 간섭할 권리가 생긴다고 여기시는 것 같아요."], ["We don't like that, and, therefore, we are going.\"", "저희는 그게 싫고, 그래서 떠나는 거예요.”"]], [["The doctor still sat on one side of the fire, and Bell still sat opposite to him; but the conversation did not form itself very freely between them.", "의사는 여전히 난롯가 한쪽에 앉아 있었고, 벨도 여전히 그 맞은편에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 두 사람 사이에 이야기는 그리 술술 풀려 나오지 않았다."], ["\"It is bad news,\"", "“안 좋은 소식이군요.”"], ["he said, at last.", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["\"At any rate, when we are ill you will not have so far to come and see us.\"", "“어쨌든 저희가 앓을 때 선생님이 그렇게 멀리까지 오실 일은 없어지잖아요.”"]], [["\"Yes, I understand.", "“네, 알겠습니다."], ["That means that I am ungracious not to congratulate myself on having you all so much nearer to me; but I do not in the least.", "여러분이 저와 그만큼 더 가까이 있게 되는 것을 두고 스스로 기뻐하지 않는다면 제가 무례한 사람이라는 말씀이시군요. 하지만 저는 조금도 기쁘지 않습니다."], ["I cannot bear to think of you as living anywhere but here at Allington.", "저는 여러분이 여기 앨링턴 말고 다른 어디에 살고 계신다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["Dales will be out of their place in a street at Guestwick.\"", "데일 집안사람들은 게스트윅의 어느 거리에서는 제자리를 잃고 말 겁니다.”"]], [["\"That's hard upon the Dales, too.\"", "“그것도 데일 집안사람들한테는 가혹한 말씀이네요.”"]], [["\"It is hard upon them.", "“가혹하지요."], ["It's a sort of offshoot from that very tyrannical law of noblesse oblige.", "노블레스 오블리주라는 그 몹시도 폭압적인 법칙에서 뻗어 나온 곁가지 같은 것이니까요."], ["I don't think you ought to go away from Allington, unless the circumstances are very imperative.\"", "저는 사정이 아주 절박하지 않은 다음에야 아가씨들이 앨링턴을 떠나서는 안 된다고 생각합니다.”"]], [["\"But they are very imperative.\"", "“그런데 아주 절박한걸요.”"]], [["\"In that case, indeed!\"", "“그렇다면야, 뭐!”"], ["And then again he fell into silence.", "그러고서 그는 다시 말이 없었다."]], [["\"Have you never seen that mamma is not happy here?\"", "“어머니가 여기서 행복하지 않으시다는 걸 여태 못 보셨어요?”"], ["she said, after another pause.", "또 한 번의 침묵 뒤에 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"For myself, I never quite understood it all before as I do now; but now I see it.\"", "“저도 지금처럼 이 모든 걸 다 알았던 건 아니에요. 하지만 이제는 보여요.”"]], [["\"And I have seen it;--have seen at least what you mean.", "“저도 보았습니다. 적어도 아가씨가 말씀하시는 것만큼은 보았지요."], ["She has led a life of restraint; but then, how frequently is such restraint the necessity of a life?", "어머니께서는 스스로를 억누르는 삶을 사셨습니다. 하지만 그런 억누름이란 삶에서 얼마나 흔히 어쩔 수 없는 일입니까?"], ["I hardly think that your mother would move on that account.\"", "저는 어머니께서 그것 때문에 옮기실 분이라고는 생각되지 않는데요.”"]], [["\"No. It is on our account.", "“아니에요."], ["But this restraint, as you call it, makes us unhappy, and she is governed by seeing that.", "저희 때문이에요. 하지만 선생님 말씀대로 그 억누름이 저희를 불행하게 만들고, 어머니는 그걸 보고 계시니 어쩌지 못하시는 거예요."], ["My uncle is generous to her as regards money; but in other things,--in matters of feeling,--I think he has been ungenerous.\"", "삼촌은 돈 문제에서는 어머니께 너그러우세요. 하지만 다른 것들에서는, 마음에 관한 일들에서는 인색하셨다고 저는 생각해요.”"]], [["\"Bell,\"", "“벨.”"], ["said the doctor; and then he paused.", "의사가 부르고는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["She looked up at him, but made no answer.", "그녀는 그를 올려다보았으나 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["He had always called her by her Christian name, and they two had ever regarded each other as close friends.", "그는 언제나 그녀를 세례명으로 불러 왔고, 두 사람은 서로를 늘 가까운 벗으로 여겨 왔다."], ["At the present moment she had forgotten all else besides this, and yet she had infinite pleasure in sitting there and talking to him.", "지금 이 순간 그녀는 그것 말고 다른 모든 것을 잊고 있었으며, 그러면서도 거기 앉아 그와 이야기하는 것이 한없이 즐거웠다."]], [["\"I am going to ask you a question which perhaps I ought not to ask, only that I have known you so long that I almost feel that I am speaking to a sister.\"", "“어쩌면 물어서는 안 될 것을 물으려 합니다. 다만 아가씨를 하도 오래 알고 지낸 터라 누이한테 말하는 것이나 다름없다는 느낌이 들어서요.”"]], [["\"You may ask me what you please,\"", "“무엇이든 물으세요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"It is about your cousin Bernard.\"", "“사촌인 버나드에 관한 겁니다.”"]], [["\"About Bernard!\"", "“버나드요!”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["It was now dusk; and as they were sitting without other light than that of the fire, she knew that he could not discern the colour which covered her face as her cousin's name was mentioned.", "이제 땅거미가 내려 있었고, 두 사람은 난롯불 말고는 아무 불빛도 없이 앉아 있었으므로, 사촌의 이름이 나오자 제 얼굴을 뒤덮은 그 빛깔을 그가 알아볼 수 없으리라는 것을 그녀는 알았다."], ["But, had the light of day pervaded the whole room, I doubt whether Crofts would have seen that blush, for he kept his eyes firmly fixed upon the fire.", "그러나 설령 대낮의 빛이 온 방에 가득했더라도 크로프츠가 그 홍조를 보았을 것 같지는 않다. 그는 눈을 난롯불에 딱 붙박아 두고 있었으니 말이다."]], [["\"Yes, about Bernard.", "“네, 버나드에 관한 겁니다."], ["I don't know whether I ought to ask you.\"", "여쭈어도 되는 건지 모르겠습니다만.”"]], [["\"I'm sure I can't say,\"", "“글쎄요, 저야 뭐라 드릴 말씀이 없네요.”"], ["said Bell, speaking words of the nature of which she was not conscious.", "벨은 그렇게 말했으나, 그 말이 어떤 성질의 것인지 스스로도 의식하지 못했다."]], [["\"There has been a rumour in Guestwick that he and you--\"", "“게스트윅에 소문이 하나 돌았는데, 그와 아가씨가—”"]], [["\"It is untrue,\"", "“사실이 아니에요.”"], ["said Bell; \"quite untrue.", "벨이 말했다. “전혀 사실이 아니에요."], ["If you hear it repeated, you should contradict it.", "그 이야기를 또 들으시거든 아니라고 해 주세요."], ["I wonder why people should say such things.\"", "사람들은 왜 그런 말을 하는지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["\"It would have been an excellent marriage;--all your friends must have approved it.\"", "“더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 혼사가 되었을 텐데요. 아가씨의 벗들이라면 다들 반겼을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"What do you mean, Dr. Crofts?", "“무슨 말씀이세요, 크로프츠 선생님?"], ["How I do hate those words, 'an excellent marriage.'", "저는 그 ‘훌륭한 혼사’라는 말이 얼마나 싫은지 몰라요."], ["In them is contained more of wicked worldliness than any other words that one ever hears spoken.", "그 말 속에는 사람이 입에 담는 그 어떤 말보다도 더 사악한 속물근성이 들어 있어요."], ["You want me to marry my cousin simply because I should have a great house to live in, and a coach.", "선생님은 제가 사촌과 결혼하기를 바라시는군요. 그저 제가 살 큰 집과 마차가 생기니까요."], ["I know that you are my friend; but I hate such friendship as that.\"", "선생님이 제 벗이라는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 저는 그런 벗의 정은 싫어요.”"]], [["\"I think you misunderstand me, Bell.", "“제 말을 오해하신 것 같군요, 벨."], ["I mean that it would have been an excellent marriage, provided you had both loved each other.\"", "제 말은 두 분이 서로 사랑하기만 했다면 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 혼사가 되었으리라는 겁니다.”"]], [["\"No, I don't misunderstand you.", "“아니에요, 저는 오해하지 않았어요."], ["Of course it would be an excellent marriage, if we loved each other.", "서로 사랑한다면야 물론 훌륭한 혼사겠지요."], ["You might say the same if I loved the butcher or the baker.", "제가 푸주한이나 빵집 주인을 사랑한대도 똑같이 말씀하실 수 있잖아요."], ["What you mean is, that it makes a reason for loving him.\"", "선생님 말씀의 속뜻은, 그 조건이 그를 사랑할 이유가 된다는 거지요.”"]], [["\"I don't think I did mean that.\"", "“그런 뜻으로 말한 것 같지는 않은데요.”"]], [["\"Then you mean nothing.\"", "“그러면 아무 뜻도 없으신 거네요.”"]], [["After that, there were again some minutes of silence during which Dr. Crofts got up to go away.", "그러고 나서 또 몇 분 동안 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 크로프츠 선생은 돌아가려고 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["\"You have scolded me very dreadfully,\"", "“아가씨한테 아주 지독하게 꾸중을 들었군요.”"], ["he said, with a slight smile, \"and I believe I have deserved it for interfering--\"", "그가 옅은 웃음을 띠며 말했다. “남의 일에 참견한 값으로 마땅히 들어야 할 꾸중이기는 합니다만—”"]], [["\"No; not at all for interfering.\"", "“아니에요, 참견하신 게 아니에요.”"]], [["\"But at any rate you must forgive me before I go.\"", "“하지만 어쨌든 가기 전에 저를 용서해 주셔야겠습니다.”"]], [["\"I won't forgive you at all, unless you repent of your sins, and alter altogether the wickedness of your mind.", "“선생님이 죄를 뉘우치시고 그 못된 마음보를 아주 뜯어고치시기 전에는 절대로 용서하지 않을 거예요."], ["You will become very soon as bad as Dr. Gruffen.\"", "그러다가는 머지않아 그러펀 선생만큼이나 못된 사람이 되고 마실걸요.”"]], [["\"Shall I?\"", "“그럴까요?”"]], [["\"Oh, but I will forgive you; for after all, you are the most generous man in the world.\"", "“아, 그래도 용서해 드릴게요. 어쨌든 선생님은 이 세상에서 가장 너그러운 분이니까요.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes; of course I am.", "“아, 그럼요. 물론 그렇지요."], ["Well,--good-by.\"", "자,—그럼 안녕히 계십시오.”"]], [["\"But, Dr. Crofts, you should not suppose others to be so much more worldly than yourself.", "“하지만 크로프츠 선생님, 다른 사람들이 선생님보다 그렇게까지 세속적이라고 여기지는 마세요."], ["You do not care for money so very much--\"", "선생님이야 돈을 그리 대단하게 여기지 않으시—”"]], [["\"But I do care very much.\"", "“아니요, 저는 아주 대단하게 여깁니다.”"]], [["\"If you did, you would not come here for nothing day after day.\"", "“그러셨다면 날이면 날마다 여기 거저 오시지는 않았겠지요.”"]], [["\"I do care for money very much.", "“저는 돈을 정말로 아주 대단하게 여깁니다."], ["I have sometimes nearly broken my heart because I could not get opportunities of earning it.", "돈을 벌 기회를 얻지 못해서 가슴이 미어질 뻔한 적도 여러 번 있었어요."], ["It is the best friend that a man can have--\"", "돈은 사람이 가질 수 있는 가장 좋은 벗입니다—”"]], [["\"Oh, Dr. Crofts!\"", "“어머, 크로프츠 선생님!”"]], [["\"--the best friend that a man can have, if it be honestly come by.", "“—사람이 가질 수 있는 가장 좋은 벗이지요, 정직하게 얻은 것이라면 말입니다."], ["A woman can hardly realize the sorrow which may fall upon a man from the want of such a friend.\"", "그런 벗이 없어서 한 사내에게 닥치는 슬픔이 어떤 것인지는 여자로서는 좀처럼 헤아릴 수 없을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Of course a man likes to earn a decent living by his profession; and you can do that.\"", "“물론 사내라면 제 직업으로 남부럽지 않게 벌어먹고 싶어 하지요. 그리고 선생님은 그렇게 하실 수 있고요.”"]], [["\"That depends upon one's ideas of decency.\"", "“그건 남부럽지 않다는 것을 어떻게 보느냐에 달렸지요.”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["mine never ran very high.", "제 기준은 한 번도 그리 높았던 적이 없어요."], ["I've always had a sort of aptitude for living in a pigsty;--a clean pigsty, you know, with nice fresh bean straw to lie upon.", "저는 늘 돼지우리에서 사는 데 소질 같은 게 있었거든요. 깨끗한 돼지우리 말이에요."], ["I think it was a mistake when they made a lady of me.", "누울 자리에 산뜻한 콩짚이 깔린 그런 데요. 저를 숙녀로 길러 놓은 건 잘못이었지 싶어요."], ["I do, indeed.\"", "정말이지 그래요.”"]], [["\"I do not,\" said Dr.", "“저는 그렇게 생각하지 않습니다.”"], ["Crofts.", "크로프츠 선생이 말했다."]], [["\"That's because you don't quite know me yet.", "“그건 선생님이 아직 저를 잘 모르셔서 그래요."], ["I've not the slightest pleasure in putting on three different dresses a day.", "저는 하루에 옷을 세 번씩 갈아입는 게 조금도 즐겁지 않아요."], ["I do it very often because it comes to me to do it, from the way in which we have been taught to live.", "그런데도 자주 그러는 건 그렇게 살도록 배워 온 탓에 그리하게 되니까 그러는 거예요. 하지만 게스트윅으로 가면 그런 걸 다 바꿀 작정이에요."], ["But when we get to Guestwick I mean to change all that; and if you come in to tea, you'll see me in the same brown frock that I wear in the morning,--unless, indeed, the morning work makes the brown frock dirty.", "그리고 선생님이 차를 드시러 오시면, 제가 아침에 입던 그 갈색 원피스를 그대로 입고 있는 걸 보시게 될걸요. 물론 아침 일에 그 갈색 원피스가 더러워지지만 않았다면요."], ["Oh, Dr. Crofts!", "어머, 크로프츠 선생님!"], ["you'll have it pitch-dark riding home under the Guestwick elms.\"", "게스트윅 느릅나무 아래로 말을 타고 돌아가시자면 칠흑같이 어두울 텐데요.”"]], [["\"I don't mind the dark,\"", "“어두운 건 상관없습니다.”"], ["he said; and it seemed as though he hardly intended to go even yet.", "그가 말했는데, 아직도 갈 마음이 별로 없어 보였다."]], [["\"But I do,\"", "“저는 상관있어요.”"], ["said Bell, \"and I shall ring for candles.\"", "벨이 말했다. “초를 가져오라고 종을 울릴게요.”"], ["But he stopped her as she put her hand out to the bell-pull.", "그러나 그녀가 초인종 줄로 손을 뻗자 그가 그것을 막았다."]], [["\"Stop a moment, Bell.", "“잠깐만요, 벨."], ["You need hardly have the candles before I go, and you need not begrudge my staying either, seeing that I shall be all alone at home.\"", "제가 가기 전에 굳이 초를 켤 것도 없고, 제가 머무는 것을 아까워하실 것도 없습니다. 집에 가 봐야 저 혼자뿐이니 말입니다.”"]], [["\"Begrudge your staying!\"", "“머무시는 걸 아까워하다니요!”"]], [["\"But, however, you shall begrudge it, or else make me very welcome.\"", "“하지만 어쨌든 아까워하시든지, 아니면 저를 아주 반겨 주시든지 하셔야겠습니다.”"], ["He still held her by the wrist, which he had caught as he prevented her from summoning the servant.", "그는 하인을 부르지 못하게 막느라 붙잡았던 그녀의 손목을 여태 놓지 않고 있었다."]], [["\"What do you mean?\"", "“무슨 말씀이세요?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"You know you are welcome to us as flowers in May.", "“선생님은 저희한테 오월의 꽃처럼 반가운 손님이라는 걸 아시잖아요."], ["You always were welcome; but now, when you have come to us in our trouble-- At any rate, you shall never say that I turn you out.\"", "언제나 반가운 분이셨어요. 더구나 지금처럼 저희가 곤경에 빠졌을 때 이렇게 와 주셨는데— 어쨌든 제가 선생님을 내쫓았다는 말씀만은 결코 못 하실 거예요.”"]], [["\"Shall I never say so?\"", "“결코 못 할까요?”"], ["And still he held her by the wrist.", "그러면서도 그는 여전히 그녀의 손목을 붙들고 있었다."], ["He had kept his chair throughout, but she was standing before him,--between him and the fire.", "그는 내내 제 의자에 앉은 채였고, 그녀는 그의 앞에, 그와 난롯불 사이에 서 있었다."], ["But she, though he held her in this way, thought little of his words, or of his action.", "그러나 그가 이렇게 붙들고 있는데도 그녀는 그의 말도 그 몸짓도 대수롭게 여기지 않았다."], ["They had known each other with great intimacy, and though Lily would still laugh at her, saying that Dr. Crofts was her lover, she had long since taught herself that no such feeling as that would ever exist between them.", "두 사람은 아주 허물없이 지내 온 사이였고, 릴리는 여전히 크로프츠 선생이 언니의 연인이라며 그녀를 놀리고는 했지만, 그녀는 그런 감정이 둘 사이에 생기는 일은 결코 없으리라고 오래전에 스스로를 가르쳐 두었던 것이다."]], [["\"Shall I never say so, Bell?", "“결코 못 할까요, 벨?"], ["What if so poor a man as I ask for the hand that you will not give to so rich a man as your cousin Bernard?\"", "만일 나처럼 가난한 사내가, 아가씨가 사촌 버나드처럼 부유한 사내에게 주지 않으려는 그 손을 달라고 한다면 어쩌시겠습니까?”"]], [["She instantly withdrew her arm and moved back very quickly a step or two across the rug.", "그녀는 곧바로 팔을 빼고는 아주 재빠르게 깔개를 가로질러 한두 걸음 물러섰다."], ["She did it almost with the motion which she might have used had he insulted her; or had a man spoken such words who would not, under any circumstances, have a right to speak them.", "그 몸짓은 그가 그녀를 모욕하기라도 했을 때, 또는 어떤 경우에도 그런 말을 할 권리가 없는 사내가 그런 말을 했을 때 나왔음 직한 몸짓이나 다름없었다."]], [["\"Ah, yes!", "“아, 그렇군요!"], ["I thought it would be so,\"", "그러실 줄 알았습니다.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I may go now, and may know that I have been turned out.\"", "“이제 가도 되겠군요. 내가 내쫓겼다는 것을 알고서 말입니다.”"]], [["\"What is it you mean, Dr. Crofts?", "“그게 무슨 말씀이세요, 크로프츠 선생님?"], ["What is it you are saying?", "대체 무슨 말씀을 하고 계세요?"], ["Why do you talk that nonsense, trying to see if you can provoke me?\"", "어째서 그런 터무니없는 소리를 하시는 거예요? 저를 화나게 할 수 있는지 시험해 보시려는 거예요?”"]], [["\"Yes; it is nonsense.", "“그래요, 터무니없는 소리지요."], ["I have no right to address you in that way, and certainly should not have done it now that I am in your house in the way of my profession.", "제게는 그런 식으로 아가씨한테 말을 건넬 권리가 없고, 더구나 이렇게 의사로서 댁에 와 있는 마당에는 결코 그래서는 안 되는 일이었습니다."], ["I beg your pardon.\"", "용서하십시오.”"], ["Now he also was standing, but he had not moved from his side of the fireplace.", "이제 그도 일어서 있었으나, 난롯가 제 자리에서 움직이지는 않았다."], ["\"Are you going to forgive me before I go?\"", "“제가 가기 전에 용서해 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["\"Forgive you for what?\"", "“무엇을 용서해 드리라는 말씀이세요?”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"For daring to love you; for having loved you almost as long as you can remember; for loving you better than all beside. This alone you should forgive; but will you forgive me for having told it?\"", "“감히 아가씨를 사랑한 것을요. 아가씨가 기억할 수 있는 그 오랜 세월만큼이나 오래 아가씨를 사랑해 온 것을요. 다른 무엇보다도 아가씨를 더 사랑한 것을요. 이것만은 용서해 주셔야 합니다. 그런데 그것을 입 밖에 낸 것까지 용서해 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["He had made her no offer, nor did she expect that he was about to make one.", "그는 그녀에게 청혼을 한 것이 아니었고, 그녀도 그가 청혼을 하려는 참이라고 여기지 않았다."], ["She herself had hardly yet realized the meaning of his words, and she certainly had asked herself no question as to the answer which she should give to them.", "그녀 자신도 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 아직 제대로 깨닫지 못했고, 거기에 어떤 대답을 내놓아야 할지 스스로에게 물어본 적도 물론 없었다."], ["There are cases in which lovers present themselves in so unmistakeable a guise, that the first word of open love uttered by them tells their whole story, and tells it without the possibility of a surprise.", "연인이 오해할 여지가 없을 만큼 뚜렷한 모습으로 나타나서, 그가 사랑을 드러내어 말하는 첫마디가 곧 그의 이야기 전부를 들려주는, 그것도 놀랄 겨를조차 주지 않고 들려주는 경우가 있다."], ["And it is generally so when the lover has not been an old friend, when even his acquaintance has been of modern date.", "그리고 그것은 대개 그 연인이 오랜 벗이 아닐 때, 알고 지낸 지가 얼마 되지도 않았을 때에 그러하다."], ["It had been so essentially in the case of Crosbie and Lily Dale.", "크로즈비와 릴리 데일의 경우가 본디 꼭 그러했다."], ["When Crosbie came to Lily and made his offer, he did it with perfect ease and thorough self-possession, for he almost knew that it was expected.", "크로즈비가 릴리에게 와서 청혼했을 때 그는 조금도 거리낌 없이 아주 침착하게 그 일을 해냈으니, 그것이 기다려지고 있다는 것을 거의 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["And Lily, though she had been flurried for a moment, had her answer pat enough.", "그리고 릴리도 잠깐 마음이 설레기는 했으나 대답만은 척척 준비되어 있었다."], ["She already loved the man with all her heart, delighted in his presence, basked in the sunshine of his manliness, rejoiced in his wit, and had tuned her ears to the tone of his voice.", "그녀는 이미 온 마음으로 그 사내를 사랑하고 있었고, 그가 곁에 있는 것을 기뻐했으며, 그의 사내다움이 비추는 볕을 쬐었고, 그의 재치에 즐거워했으며, 제 귀를 그의 목소리 가락에 맞추어 두었던 것이다."], ["It had all been done, and the world expected it.", "모든 것이 이미 이루어져 있었고 세상도 그것을 기다리고 있었다."], ["Had he not made his offer, Lily would have been ill-treated;--though, alas, alas, there was future ill-treatment, so much heavier, in store for her!", "그가 청혼하지 않았더라면 릴리는 몹쓸 대접을 받은 것이 되었으리라. 하기야, 아아, 아아, 그보다 훨씬 더 무거운 몹쓸 대접이 뒷날 그녀를 기다리고 있었지만!"], ["But there are other cases in which a lover cannot make himself known as such without great difficulty, and when he does do so, cannot hope for an immediate answer in his favour.", "그러나 연인이 제 마음을 연인으로서 알리는 일이 몹시 어려운 경우도 있으니, 그런 경우에는 막상 알린다 해도 자기에게 이로운 대답을 그 자리에서 바랄 수가 없다."], ["It is hard upon old friends that this difficulty should usually fall the heaviest upon them.", "이 어려움이 대개 오랜 벗들에게 가장 무겁게 떨어진다는 것은 그들에게 가혹한 일이다."], ["Crofts had been so intimate with the Dale family that very many persons had thought it probable that he would marry one of the girls.", "크로프츠는 데일 집안과 하도 가까이 지내 온 터라, 그가 두 아가씨 가운데 하나와 혼인하리라고 여긴 사람이 아주 많았다."], ["Mrs. Dale herself had thought so, and had almost hoped it.", "데일 부인 자신도 그렇게 생각했고 거의 그러기를 바라기까지 했다."], ["Lily had certainly done both.", "릴리는 분명 그렇게 생각했고 그러기를 바라기도 했다."], ["These thoughts and hopes had somewhat faded away, but yet their former existence should have been in the doctor's favour.", "그런 생각과 바람이 얼마쯤 빛이 바래기는 했으나, 그래도 한때 그런 것이 있었다는 사실만은 의사에게 유리하게 작용했어야 마땅했다."], ["But now, when he had in some way spoken out, Bell started back from him and would not believe that he was in earnest.", "그런데 이제 그가 어떻게든 마음을 털어놓고 보니, 벨은 그에게서 화들짝 물러섰고 그가 진심이라는 것을 믿으려 하지 않았다."], ["She probably loved him better than any man in the world, and yet, when he spoke to her of love, she could not bring herself to understand him.", "그녀는 아마 이 세상 어느 사내보다도 그를 사랑하고 있었으련만, 막상 그가 그녀에게 사랑을 말하자 도무지 그 말을 알아들을 수가 없었던 것이다."]], [["\"I don't know what you mean, Dr.", "“무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠어요, 크로프츠 선생님."], ["Crofts; indeed I do not,\"", "정말로 모르겠어요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"I had meant to ask you to be my wife; simply that.", "“저는 아가씨한테 제 아내가 되어 달라고 청할 작정이었습니다."], ["But you shall not have the pain of making me a positive refusal.", "그저 그뿐이었지요. 하지만 아가씨한테 저를 딱 잘라 거절해야 하는 괴로움까지 지우지는 않겠습니다."], ["As I rode here to-day I thought of it.", "오늘 여기로 말을 달려오면서 그 생각을 했습니다."], ["During my frequent rides of late I have thought of little else.", "요즈음 자주 말을 달리는 동안에도 그 생각 말고는 별로 한 것이 없었지요."], ["But I told myself that I had no right to do it.", "하지만 저는 제게 그럴 권리가 없다고 스스로에게 말했습니다."], ["I have not even a house in which it would be fit that you should live.\"", "저는 아가씨가 사시기에 마땅한 집조차 가지고 있지 않으니까요.”"]], [["\"Dr. Crofts, if I loved you,--if I wished to marry you--\"", "“크로프츠 선생님, 제가 선생님을 사랑한다면, 제가 선생님과 혼인하고 싶다면—”"], ["and then she stopped herself.", "그러고서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["\"But you do not?\"", "“하지만 그렇지는 않으시지요?”"]], [["\"No; I think not.", "“네, 그렇지 않은 것 같아요."], ["I suppose not. No.", "아마 아닐 거예요."], ["But in any way no consideration about money has anything to do with it.\"", "네, 아니에요. 하지만 어쨌든 돈에 관한 생각은 여기에 아무 상관이 없어요.”"]], [["\"But I am not that butcher or that baker whom you could love?\"", "“그러면 저는 아가씨가 사랑하실 수 있다는 그 푸주한이나 빵집 주인은 될 수 있을까요?”"]], [["\"No,\"", "“아니요.”"], ["said Bell; and then she stopped herself from further speech, not as intending to convey all her answer in that one word, but as not knowing how to fashion any further words.", "벨이 말했다. 그러고는 더 말을 잇지 못하고 멈추었는데, 그 한마디에 제 대답을 모두 담으려 한 것이 아니라 그다음 말을 어떻게 지어야 할지 몰랐기 때문이었다."]], [["\"I knew it would be so,\"", "“그러실 줄 알았습니다.”"], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["It will, I fear, be thought by those who condescend to criticize this lover's conduct and his mode of carrying on his suit, that he was very unfit for such work.", "이 연인의 처신과 구애하는 방식을 굳이 따져 보려는 이들은, 그가 그런 일에 몹시 서툰 사람이었다고 여기지 않을까 나는 걱정한다."], ["Ladies will say that he wanted courage, and men will say that he wanted wit.", "부인네들은 그가 용기가 모자랐다고 할 것이고, 사내들은 그가 재치가 모자랐다고 할 것이다."], ["I am inclined, however, to believe that he behaved as well as men generally do behave on such occasions, and that he showed himself to be a good average lover.", "그러나 나는 그가 그런 자리에서 사내들이 대개 처신하는 만큼은 처신했으며, 어디까지나 여느 정도는 되는 연인임을 스스로 보여 주었다고 믿는 쪽이다."], ["There is your bold lover, who knocks his lady-love over as he does a bird, and who would anathematize himself all over, and swear that his gun was distraught, and look about as though he thought the world was coming to an end, if he missed to knock over his bird.", "세상에는 대담한 연인이 있어서, 새를 쏘아 떨어뜨리듯 제 사랑하는 아가씨를 넘어뜨리고, 혹시라도 제 새를 떨어뜨리지 못하면 제 몸에 온갖 저주를 퍼붓고 제 총이 실성했다고 욕을 하며 세상이 끝나기라도 한 듯한 낯빛으로 둘레를 두리번거린다."], ["And there is your timid lover, who winks his eyes when he fires, who has felt certain from the moment in which he buttoned on his knickerbockers that he at any rate would kill nothing, and who, when he hears the loud congratulations of his friends, cannot believe that he really did bag that beautiful winged thing by his own prowess.", "또 소심한 연인이 있어서, 그는 방아쇠를 당기며 눈을 질끈 감고, 반바지를 챙겨 입던 순간부터 적어도 저는 아무것도 잡지 못하리라고 굳게 믿고 있었으므로, 벗들이 큰 소리로 축하를 보내와도 저 아름다운 날개 달린 것을 정말로 제 솜씨로 잡았다는 것을 믿지 못한다."], ["The beautiful winged thing which the timid man carries home in his bosom, declining to have it thrown into a miscellaneous cart, so that it may never be lost in a common crowd of game, is better to him than are the slaughtered hecatombs to those who kill their birds by the hundred.", "소심한 사내가 품에 안고 돌아오는 그 아름다운 날개 달린 것은—사냥한 짐승이 뒤섞인 흔한 무더기 속에 파묻히는 일이 없도록 잡동사니 수레에 던져 넣기를 마다하고 안고 오는 그것은—새를 수백 마리씩 잡는 이들에게 그 산더미 같은 사냥물이 주는 것보다 더 값진 것이다."]], [["But Dr. Crofts had so winked his eye, that he was not in the least aware whether he had winged his bird or no.", "그러나 크로프츠 선생은 어찌나 눈을 질끈 감고 쏘았던지, 제가 새를 맞혔는지 어떤지 조금도 알지 못했다."], ["Indeed, having no one at hand to congratulate him, he was quite sure that the bird had flown away uninjured into the next field. \"No\"", "게다가 곁에서 축하해 줄 사람이 하나도 없었으니, 그는 새가 상처 하나 없이 옆 들판으로 날아가 버렸다고 아주 굳게 믿었다."], ["was the only word which Bell had given in answer to his last sidelong question, and No is not a comfortable word to lovers.", "그의 마지막 에두른 물음에 벨이 준 대답은 ‘아니요’라는 한마디뿐이었는데, ‘아니요’는 연인들에게 편안한 말이 못 된다."], ["But there had been that in Bell's No which might have taught him that the bird was not escaping without a wound, if he had still had any of his wits about him.", "그러나 벨의 그 ‘아니요’ 속에는, 그가 조금이라도 제정신을 붙들고 있었더라면 그 새가 상처 하나 없이 달아나는 것은 아니라는 것을 그에게 가르쳐 주었을 만한 무엇이 들어 있었다."]], [["\"Now I will go,\"", "“이제 가겠습니다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["Then he paused for an answer, but none came.", "그러고는 대답을 기다리며 잠시 멈추었으나 아무 대답도 오지 않았다."], ["\"And you will understand what I meant when I spoke of being turned out.\"", "“그리고 제가 내쫓겼다고 말한 것이 무슨 뜻이었는지는 아시겠지요.”"]], [["\"Nobody--turns you out.\"", "“아무도— 선생님을 내쫓지 않아요.”"], ["And Bell, as she spoke, had almost descended to a sob.", "그렇게 말하는 벨은 어느새 흐느낌에 가까운 소리를 내고 있었다."]], [["\"It is time, at any rate, that I should go; is it not?", "“어쨌든 갈 때가 되었지요, 그렇지 않습니까?"], ["And, Bell, don't suppose that this little scene will keep me away from your sister's bedside.", "그리고 벨, 오늘 이 자그마한 소동 때문에 제가 동생분 병상에서 발길을 끊으리라고는 생각지 마십시오."], ["I shall be here to-morrow, and you will find that you will hardly know me again for the same person.\"", "저는 내일도 올 것이고, 아가씨는 저를 같은 사람이라고 알아보기도 어려우실 겁니다.”"], ["Then in the dark he put out his hand to her.", "그러고서 그는 어둠 속에서 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["\"Good-by,\"", "“안녕히 가세요.”"], ["she said, giving him her hand.", "그녀가 제 손을 내주며 말했다."], ["He pressed hers very closely, but she, though she wished to do so, could not bring herself to return the pressure.", "그는 그녀의 손을 아주 꼭 쥐었으나, 그녀는 그러고 싶었으면서도 마주 힘을 주지 못했다."], ["Her hand remained passive in his, showing no sign of offence; but it was absolutely passive.", "그녀의 손은 그의 손안에 가만히 놓인 채 언짢은 기색이라고는 조금도 내비치지 않았으나, 그저 하염없이 가만히 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["\"Good-by, dearest friend,\"", "“안녕히 계십시오, 더없이 소중한 벗이여.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Good-by,\"", "“안녕히 가세요.”"], ["she answered,--and then he was gone.", "그녀가 대답했다. 그러고서 그는 가 버렸다."]], [["She waited quite still till she heard the front-door close after him, and then she crept silently up to her own bedroom, and sat herself down in a low rocking-chair over the fire.", "그녀는 현관문이 그의 뒤에서 닫히는 소리가 들릴 때까지 꼼짝도 하지 않고 있다가, 소리 없이 제 침실로 살며시 올라가 난롯가의 나지막한 흔들의자에 앉았다."], ["It was in accordance with a custom already established that her mother should remain with Lily till the tea was ready downstairs; for in these days of illness such dinners as were provided were eaten early.", "아래층에 차가 준비될 때까지 어머니가 릴리 곁에 남아 있는 것은 이미 굳어진 관례였다. 이렇게 병이 든 날들에는 저녁 식사라 할 만한 것도 일찍 차려 먹었기 때문이다."], ["Bell, therefore, knew that she had still some half-hour of her own, during which she might sit and think undisturbed.", "그러므로 벨은 아직 반 시간쯤 제 시간이 남아 있다는 것을, 그동안은 앉아서 아무에게도 방해받지 않고 생각에 잠길 수 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["And what naturally should have been her first thoughts?--that she had ruthlessly refused a man who, as she now knew, loved her well, and for whom she had always felt at any rate the warmest friendship?", "그러면 그녀의 첫 생각은 마땅히 무엇이어야 했을까? 이제 와서 보니 자기를 깊이 사랑하는 사내를, 자기 쪽에서도 적어도 더없이 따뜻한 우정만은 늘 품어 온 그 사내를 매정하게 물리쳤다는 것이 아니었을까?"], ["Such were not her thoughts, nor were they in any way akin to this.", "그러나 그녀의 생각은 그런 것이 아니었고, 그 비슷한 것조차 아니었다."], ["They ran back instantly to years gone by,--over long years, as her few years were counted,--and settled themselves on certain halcyon days, in which she had dreamed that he had loved her, and had fancied that she had loved him.", "생각은 곧장 지나간 세월로—그녀의 짧은 나이로 헤아리자면 긴 세월로—거슬러 올라가, 그가 자기를 사랑한다고 꿈꾸었고 자기도 그를 사랑한다고 여겼던 그 평온하던 날들에 내려앉았다. 그 뒤로 그녀는 그 날들을 두고 스스로를 얼마나 나무랐으며, 제 생각이 너무 대담했다고 스스로에게 얼마나 가르쳤던가!"], ["How she had schooled herself for those days since that, and taught herself to know that her thoughts had been over-bold!", "그런데 이제 그 모든 것이 한 바퀴 돌아 제자리로 온 것이다. 그녀가 여태 좋아해 본 단 하나의 사내가 그녀를 사랑하고 있었다."], ["And now it had all come round. The only man that she had ever liked had loved her.", "그러다 문득 어느 날의 기억이 떠올랐다."], ["Then there came to her a memory of a certain day, in which she had been almost proud to think that Crosbie had admired her, in which she had almost hoped that it might be so; and as she thought of this she blushed, and struck her foot twice upon the floor.", "크로즈비가 자기를 마음에 두고 있다는 생각에 거의 우쭐했던 날, 그것이 정말이기를 거의 바라기까지 했던 날의 기억이었다. 그 생각이 나자 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 발로 마룻바닥을 두 번 굴렀다."], ["\"Dear Lily,\"", "“사랑하는 릴리.”"], ["she said to herself--\"poor Lily!\"", "그녀는 속으로 말했다. “가엾은 릴리!”"], ["But the feeling which induced her then to think of her sister had had no relation to that which had first brought Crosbie into her mind.", "그러나 그때 그녀로 하여금 동생을 떠올리게 한 감정은, 크로즈비를 맨 처음 마음에 불러들인 그 감정과는 아무 관계도 없었다."]], [["And this man had loved her through it all,--this priceless, peerless man,--this man who was as true to the backbone as that other man had shown himself to be false; who was as sound as the other man had proved himself to be rotten.", "그리고 이 사내는 그 모든 세월 내내 그녀를 사랑해 온 것이다. 값을 매길 수도 없고 견줄 데도 없는 이 사내가."], ["A smile came across her face as she sat looking at the fire, thinking of this.", "저쪽 사내가 스스로 거짓된 것임을 드러내 보인 만큼이나 뼛속까지 참된 이 사내가, 저쪽 사내가 스스로 썩은 것임을 드러내 보인 만큼이나 튼튼한 이 사내가 말이다."], ["A man had loved her, whose love was worth possessing.", "그런 생각을 하며 난롯불을 바라보고 앉아 있는 그녀의 얼굴에 미소가 번졌다."], ["She hardly remembered whether or no she had refused him or accepted him.", "한 사내가 그녀를 사랑했고, 그 사랑은 가질 만한 값어치가 있는 것이었다."], ["She hardly asked herself what she would do.", "그녀는 자기가 그를 거절한 것인지 받아들인 것인지조차 잘 기억나지 않았다."], ["As to all that it was necessary that she should have many thoughts, but the necessity did not press upon her quite immediately.", "앞으로 어떻게 할 것인지도 스스로에게 거의 묻지 않았다. 그 모든 것에 대해서는 여러 가지로 생각해 보아야 할 필요가 있었지만, 그 필요가 지금 당장 그녀를 짓누르지는 않았다."], ["For the present, at any rate, she might sit and triumph;--and thus triumphant she sat there till the old nurse came in and told her that her mother was waiting for her below.", "적어도 지금 이 순간만은 앉아서 마음껏 뿌듯해해도 좋았다. 그리하여 그렇게 뿌듯해하며 앉아 있는데, 늙은 유모가 들어와 어머니가 아래층에서 기다리신다고 일러 주었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XL. PREPARATIONS FOR THE WEDDING.", "# 제40장. 혼례 준비"]], [["The fourteenth of February was finally settled as the day on which Mr. Crosbie was to be made the happiest of men.", "크로즈비 씨가 세상에서 가장 행복한 사내가 되기로 한 날은 마침내 이월 십사일로 정해졌다."], ["A later day had been at first named, the twenty-seventh or twenty-eighth having been suggested as an improvement over the first week in March; but Lady Amelia had been frightened by Crosbie's behaviour on that Sunday evening, and had made the countess understand that there should be no unnecessary delay.", "처음에는 더 늦은 날이 거론되었으니, 삼월 첫 주보다는 그편이 낫다 하여 이십칠일이나 이십팔일이 제안되었던 것이다. 그러나 레이디 어밀리아는 그 일요일 저녁 크로즈비가 보인 태도에 겁을 먹었고, 쓸데없이 날을 미루어서는 안 된다고 백작부인에게 일러 두었다."], ["\"He doesn't scruple at that kind of thing,\"", "“그이는 그런 짓쯤은 거리끼지 않는 사람이오.”"], ["Lady Amelia had said in one of her letters, showing perhaps less trust in the potency of her own rank than might have been expected from her.", "레이디 어밀리아가 편지 가운데 하나에 그렇게 썼는데, 그것은 그녀에게서 기대할 법한 것보다 오히려 제 신분의 위력을 덜 믿고 있음을 드러낸 셈이었다."], ["The countess, however, had agreed with her, and when Crosbie received from his mother-in-law a very affectionate epistle, setting forth all the reasons which would make the fourteenth so much more convenient a day than the twenty-eighth, he was unable to invent an excuse for not being made happy a fortnight earlier than the time named in the bargain.", "그러나 백작부인도 같은 생각이었고, 그리하여 크로즈비가 장차 장모가 될 이에게서 아주 다정한 서한을 받았을 때—십사일이 이십팔일보다 얼마나 더 편한 날인지 온갖 이유를 늘어놓은 서한이었다—그는 흥정에서 정한 날보다 보름 일찍 행복해지는 것을 마다할 핑곗거리를 지어낼 수가 없었다."], ["His first impulse had been against yielding, arising from some feeling which made him think that more than the bargain ought not to be exacted.", "그의 첫 충동은 물러서지 않는 쪽이었으니, 흥정에 든 것 이상을 요구해서는 안 된다는 어떤 마음에서 나온 것이었다."], ["But what was the use to him of quarrelling?", "그러나 다투어 무슨 소용인가?"], ["What the use, at least, of quarrelling just then?", "적어도 지금 다투어 무슨 소용인가?"], ["He believed that he could more easily enfranchise himself from the De Courcy tyranny when he should be once married than he could do now.", "그는 드 코시 집안의 폭정에서 저를 풀어놓는 일이 지금보다는 일단 혼인하고 난 뒤에 한결 쉬우리라고 믿었다."], ["When Lady Alexandrina should be his own he would let her know that he intended to be her master.", "레이디 알렉산드리나가 제 사람이 되고 나면 자기가 그녀의 주인 노릇을 할 작정임을 알려 줄 참이었다."], ["If in doing so it would be necessary that he should divide himself altogether from the De Courcys, such division should be made.", "그러기 위해 드 코시 집안과 아주 갈라서야 한다면 그런 갈라섬도 마다하지 않을 것이었다."], ["At the present moment he would yield to them, at any rate in this matter.", "지금 당장은, 적어도 이 일에서는 그들에게 양보하기로 했다."], ["And so the fourteenth of February was fixed for the marriage.", "그리하여 이월 십사일이 혼례일로 정해졌다."]], [["In the second week in January Alexandrina came up to look after her things; or, in more noble language, to fit herself with becoming bridal appanages.", "일월 둘째 주에 알렉산드리나는 제 물건들을 챙기러 런던으로 올라왔다. 좀 더 고상한 말로 하자면 신부에게 어울리는 혼수 일습을 갖추러 온 것이었다."], ["As she could not properly do all this work alone, or even under the surveillance and with the assistance of a sister, Lady De Courcy was to come up also.", "그 일을 혼자서, 아니 언니가 곁에서 지켜보며 거들어 주는 것만으로도 제대로 해낼 수는 없었으므로, 드 코시 백작부인도 함께 올라오기로 되어 있었다. 그러나 알렉산드리나가 먼저 와서 백작부인이 도착할 때까지 세인트존스 우드의 언니 집에 머물렀다."], ["But Alexandrina came first, remaining with her sister in St.", "백작부인은 사위의 대접을 받아들이는 아량을 여태 한 번도 베푼 적이 없었고, 언제나 포트먼 스퀘어의 춥고 삭막한 집으로 갔다."], ["John's Wood till the countess should arrive.", "여러 해 동안 드 코시 집안의 런던 저택 노릇을 해 온 집이었으니, 백작부인 같으면 진작에 옥스퍼드 스트리트 저편의 어느 거처와 기꺼이 바꾸었을 것이다."], ["The countess had never yet condescended to accept of her son-in-law's hospitality, but always went to the cold, comfortless house in Portman Square,--the house which had been the De Courcy town family mansion for many years, and which the countess would long since have willingly exchanged for some abode on the other side of Oxford Street; but the earl had been obdurate; his clubs and certain lodgings which he had occasionally been wont to occupy, were on the right side of Oxford Street; why should he change his old family residence?", "그러나 백작이 완고했다. 그의 클럽들과 이따금 묵곤 하던 몇몇 하숙이 옥스퍼드 스트리트의 옳은 쪽에 있었으니, 무엇 하러 대대로 살아온 집을 바꾼단 말인가?"], ["So the countess was coming up to Portman Square, not having been even asked on this occasion to St. John's Wood.", "그리하여 백작부인은 포트먼 스퀘어로 올라오게 되었고, 이번에는 세인트존스 우드로 오라는 말조차 듣지 못했다."]], [["\"Don't you think we'd better,\" Mr.", "“우리가 그렇게 하는 편이 낫지 않겠어요?”"], ["Gazebee had said to his wife, almost trembling at the renewal of his own proposition.", "게이지비 씨가 아내에게 말했다. 제가 냈던 제안을 다시 꺼내면서 그는 거의 떨다시피 했다."]], [["\"I think not, my dear,\"", "“그러지 않는 편이 좋겠소, 여보.”"], ["Lady Amelia had answered.", "레이디 어밀리아가 대답했다."], ["\"Mamma is not very particular; but there are little things, you know--\"", "“어머니가 그리 까다로운 분은 아니지만, 자잘한 것들이 있잖소—”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, of course,\" said Mr.", "“아, 그럼요, 물론이지요.”"], ["Gazebee; and then the conversation had been dropped.", "게이지비 씨가 말했다. 그러고서 이야기는 흐지부지되었다."], ["He would most willingly have entertained his august mother-in-law during her visit to the metropolis, and yet her presence in his house would have made him miserable as long as she remained there.", "그는 존귀한 장모가 수도에 머무는 동안 더없이 기꺼이 대접하고 싶었으나, 그러면서도 그녀가 제 집에 있는 한 그 기간 내내 비참했을 것이었다."]], [["But for a week Alexandrina sojourned under Mr. Gazebee's roof, during which time Crosbie was made happy with all the delights of an expectant bridegroom.", "그러나 한 주 동안 알렉산드리나는 게이지비 씨의 지붕 아래 머물렀고, 그동안 크로즈비는 신랑을 앞둔 사내의 온갖 즐거움으로 행복해졌다."], ["Of course he was given to understand that he was to dine at the Gazebees' every day, and spend all his evenings there; and, under the circumstances, he had no excuse for not doing so.", "물론 그는 날마다 게이지비네에서 저녁을 들고 저녁나절을 내내 거기서 보내야 하는 것으로 알아 두라는 말을 들었으며, 사정이 그러하니 그러지 않을 구실도 없었다."], ["Indeed, at the present moment, his hours would otherwise have hung heavily enough upon his hands.", "실은 요즈음 그의 시간이 달리 어떻게 흘렀겠는가 하면, 지겹도록 더디게 흘렀을 뿐이었다."], ["In spite of his bold resolution with reference to his eye, and his intention not to be debarred from the pleasures of society by the marks of the late combat, he had not, since that occurrence, frequented his club very closely; and though London was now again becoming fairly full, he did not find himself going out so much as had been his wont.", "제 눈에 관해 대담한 결심을 하고 지난번 싸움의 자국 때문에 사교의 즐거움에서 밀려나지는 않겠다고 마음먹었건만, 그 일이 있은 뒤로 그는 클럽에 그리 자주 드나들지 못했다. 그리고 런던이 다시 제법 북적이기 시작했는데도 그는 예전만큼 나다니지 않는 자신을 발견했다."], ["The brilliance of his coming marriage did not seem to have added much to his popularity; in fact, the world,--his world,--was beginning to look coldly at him.", "다가오는 혼인의 광채는 그의 인기에 별로 보태 준 것이 없는 듯했고, 사실 세상은—그의 세상은—그를 냉랭하게 바라보기 시작하고 있었다."], ["Therefore that daily attendance at St. John's Wood was not felt to be so irksome as might have been expected.", "그러니 날마다 세인트존스 우드로 출근하다시피 하는 일도 예상만큼 지겹게 느껴지지는 않았다."]], [["A residence had been taken for the couple in a very fashionable row of buildings abutting upon the Bayswater Road, called Princess Royal Crescent.", "부부가 살 집은 베이스워터 로드에 면한, 아주 유행하는 집들이 늘어선 줄에 잡아 두었다. 프린세스 로열 크레슨트라 불리는 곳이었다."], ["The house was quite new, and the street being unfinished had about it a strong smell of mortar, and a general aspect of builders'", "집은 아주 새것이었고, 거리는 아직 마무리가 되지 않아 회반죽 냄새가 코를 찌르고 여기저기 건축용 장대와 벽돌 부스러기가 널려 있었다."], ["poles and brickbats; but nevertheless, it was acknowledged to be a quite correct locality.", "그런데도 그곳은 아주 반듯한 동네로 인정받고 있었다."], ["From one end of the crescent a corner of Hyde Park could be seen, and the other abutted on a very handsome terrace indeed, in which lived an ambassador,--from South America,--a few bankers' senior clerks, and a peer of the realm.", "초승달 모양으로 굽은 그 줄의 한쪽 끝에서는 하이드 파크의 한 귀퉁이가 보였고, 다른 쪽 끝은 참으로 훌륭한 테라스 하우스 줄에 닿아 있었으니, 거기에는 어느 대사가—남아메리카에서 온 대사가—은행가의 고참 서기 몇 사람이, 그리고 왕국의 귀족 하나가 살고 있었다."], ["We know how vile is the sound of Baker Street, and how absolutely foul to the polite ear is the name of Fitzroy Square.", "베이커 스트리트라는 소리가 얼마나 천하게 들리는지, 피츠로이 스퀘어라는 이름이 점잖은 귀에 얼마나 역겨운지 우리는 안다."], ["The houses, however, in those purlieus are substantial, warm, and of good size.", "그러나 그 언저리의 집들은 튼튼하고 따뜻하며 크기도 넉넉하다."], ["The house in Princess Royal Crescent was certainly not substantial, for in these days substantially-built houses do not pay.", "프린세스 로열 크레슨트의 집은 분명 튼튼하지 않았으니, 요즘 세상에 튼튼하게 지은 집은 수지가 맞지 않는 까닭이다."], ["It could hardly have been warm, for, to speak the truth, it was even yet not finished throughout; and as for the size, though the drawing-room was a noble apartment, consisting of a section of the whole house, with a corner cut out for the staircase, it was very much cramped in its other parts, and was made like a cherub, in this respect, that it had no rear belonging to it.", "따뜻할 리도 없었으니, 사실대로 말하자면 아직 곳곳이 마무리도 되지 않은 집이었다. 크기로 말하자면, 응접실이야 집 한 층을 통째로 잘라 낸 데다 계단 자리만 한 귀퉁이 도려낸 훌륭한 방이었지만, 다른 데는 몹시 옹색했고, 이 점에서는 그림 속 게루빔을 닮아 저에게 딸린 뒷부분이라는 것이 없었다."], ["\"But if you have no private fortune of your own, you cannot have everything,\"", "“하지만 제 재산이 따로 없는 사람이 모든 걸 다 가질 수는 없는 법이오.”"], ["as the countess observed when Crosbie objected to the house because a closet under the kitchen-stairs was to be assigned to him as his own dressing-room.", "부엌 계단 밑 골방이 옷 갈아입는 방으로 제게 배정된 것을 두고 크로즈비가 그 집에 이의를 달았을 때 백작부인은 그렇게 말했다."]], [["When the question of the house was first debated, Lady Amelia had been anxious that St. John's Wood should be selected as the site, but to this Crosbie had positively objected.", "집 문제가 처음 논의되었을 때 레이디 어밀리아는 세인트존스 우드를 자리로 고르고 싶어 안달했으나, 크로즈비는 여기에 딱 잘라 반대했다."]], [["\"I think you don't like St. John's Wood,\"", "“세인트존스 우드를 좋아하지 않으시나 보구려.”"], ["Lady Amelia had said to him somewhat sternly, thinking to awe him into a declaration that he entertained no general enmity to the neighbourhood.", "레이디 어밀리아가 다소 매섭게 그에게 말했다. 그를 겁주어, 이 동네에 딱히 무슨 원한이 있는 것은 아니라고 밝히게 할 셈이었다."], ["But Crosbie was not weak enough for this.", "그러나 크로즈비는 그럴 만큼 무르지 않았다."]], [["\"No; I do not,\" he said.", "“네, 좋아하지 않습니다.”"], ["\"I have always disliked it.", "그가 말했다. “예전부터 늘 싫어했지요."], ["It amounts to a prejudice, I daresay.", "편견이나 다름없다고 해야겠지요."], ["But if I were made to live here I am convinced I should cut my throat in the first six months.\"", "하지만 여기서 살아야 한다면 여섯 달 안에 제 목을 그어 버리고 말 것이 틀림없습니다.”"]], [["Lady Amelia had then drawn herself up, declaring her sorrow that her house should be so hateful to him.", "그러자 레이디 어밀리아는 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우며, 제 집이 그토록 그에게 밉살스럽다니 유감이라고 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, dear, no,\" said he.", "“아니, 저런, 아닙니다.”"], ["\"I like it very much for you, and enjoy coming here of all things.", "그가 말했다. “처형께서 사시는 곳으로는 무척 마음에 들고, 여기 오는 것을 무엇보다 즐거워하고 있습니다."], ["I speak only of the effect which living here myself would have upon me.\"", "저는 제가 여기서 살 경우에 제게 미칠 영향만을 말한 겁니다.”"]], [["Lady Amelia was quite clever enough to understand it all; but she had her sister's interest at heart, and therefore persevered in her affectionate solicitude for her brother-in-law, giving up that point as to St.", "레이디 어밀리아는 그 모든 것을 알아들을 만큼 영리했다. 그러나 동생의 이익을 마음에 두고 있었으므로, 세인트존스 우드에 관해서는 그 점을 접어 두고 제부에 대한 다정한 보살핌을 그대로 밀고 나갔다."], ["John's Wood. Crosbie himself had wished to go to one of the new Pimlico squares down near Vauxhall Bridge and the river, actuated chiefly by consideration of the enormous distance lying between that locality and the northern region in which Lady Amelia lived; but to this Lady Alexandrina had objected strongly.", "크로즈비 자신은 복스홀 다리와 강 가까이에 있는 핌리코의 새 스퀘어 가운데 하나로 가고 싶어 했으니, 주로 그 동네와 레이디 어밀리아가 사는 북쪽 지방 사이에 놓인 어마어마한 거리를 염두에 둔 것이었다."], ["If, indeed, they could have achieved Eaton Square, or a street leading out of Eaton Square,--if they could have crept on to the hem of the skirt of Belgravia,--the bride would have been delighted.", "그러나 여기에는 레이디 알렉산드리나가 완강히 반대했다. 만일 이턴 스퀘어를, 아니면 이턴 스퀘어에서 뻗어 나간 거리 하나라도 손에 넣을 수 있었더라면—벨그레이비아의 치맛자락 끝에라도 기어들 수 있었더라면—신부는 기뻐했을 것이다."], ["And at first she was very nearly being taken in with the idea that such was the proposal made to her.", "그리고 처음에 그녀는 제게 들어온 제안이 바로 그런 것이라는 생각에 하마터면 속아 넘어갈 뻔했다."], ["Her geographical knowledge of Pimlico had not been perfect, and she had nearly fallen into a fatal error.", "핌리코에 관한 그녀의 지리 지식은 온전하지 못했고, 그리하여 자칫 돌이킬 수 없는 잘못에 빠질 뻔했다."], ["But a friend had kindly intervened.", "그러나 어느 벗이 고맙게도 끼어들었다."], ["\"For heaven's sake, my dear, don't let him take you anywhere beyond Eccleston Square!\"", "“세상에, 얘, 그이가 널 에클스턴 스퀘어 너머 어디로도 데려가지 못하게 해요!”"], ["had been exclaimed to her in dismay by a faithful married friend.", "시집간 어느 미더운 벗이 기겁하여 그녀에게 그렇게 외쳤던 것이다."], ["Thus warned, Alexandrina had been firm, and now their tent was to be pitched in Princess Royal Crescent, from one end of which the Hyde Park may be seen.", "그렇게 경고를 받고 알렉산드리나는 뜻을 굽히지 않았고, 이제 두 사람의 천막은 프린세스 로열 크레슨트에 쳐질 참이었다. 그 한쪽 끝에서는 하이드 파크가 보이는 곳이다."]], [["The furniture had been ordered chiefly under the inspection, and by the experience, of the Lady Amelia.", "가구는 주로 레이디 어밀리아의 감독 아래, 그리고 그녀의 경험에 기대어 주문되었다."], ["Crosbie had satisfied himself by declaring that she at any rate could get the things cheaper than he could buy them, and that he had no taste for such employment.", "크로즈비는 어차피 자기보다 그녀가 물건을 싸게 살 수 있고 자기는 그런 일에 취미가 없다고 선언해 놓고 마음을 놓아 버렸다."], ["Nevertheless, he had felt that he was being made subject to tyranny and brought under the thumb of subjection.", "그러면서도 그는 제가 폭정에 놓이고 복종의 손아귀에 들어가고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["He could not go cordially into this matter of beds and chairs, and, therefore, at last deputed the whole matter to the De Courcy faction.", "침대며 의자 이야기에 진심으로 파고들 수가 없었으므로 마침내 그 일을 통째로 드 코시 쪽에 맡겨 버린 것이다."], ["And for this there was another reason, not hitherto mentioned. Mr.", "그리고 여기에는 아직 말하지 않은 또 하나의 이유가 있었다."], ["Mortimer Gazebee was finding the money with which all the furniture was being bought.", "가구를 사들이는 돈을 모두 대고 있는 것이 모티머 게이지비 씨였다."], ["He, with an honest but almost unintelligible zeal for the De Courcy family, had tied up every shilling on which he could lay his hand as belonging to Crosbie, in the interest of Lady Alexandrina.", "그는 드 코시 집안에 대한, 정직하되 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 열성으로, 크로즈비의 것이라 하여 제 손이 닿는 돈은 한 실링까지 몽땅 레이디 알렉산드리나의 몫으로 묶어 두었다."], ["He had gone to work for her, scraping here and arranging there, strapping the new husband down upon the grindstone of his matrimonial settlement, as though the future bread of his, Gazebee's, own children were dependent on the validity of his legal workmanship.", "그녀를 위해 여기서 긁어모으고 저기서 손을 보며, 마치 제 자식들이 앞으로 먹을 빵이 저의 법률 솜씨가 온전한가에 달리기라도 한 듯이, 새신랑을 혼인 재산 계약이라는 숫돌에 대고 꽁꽁 붙들어 매어 놓았던 것이다."], ["And for this he was not to receive a penny, or gain any advantage, immediate or ulterior.", "그러고도 그는 그 대가로 한 푼을 받는 것도 아니었고, 당장이든 나중이든 무슨 이득을 보는 것도 아니었다."], ["It came from his zeal,--his zeal for the coronet which Lord De Courcy wore.", "그것은 그의 열성에서, 드 코시 경이 쓰는 관(冠)에 대한 열성에서 나온 것이었다."], ["According to his mind an earl and an earl's belongings were entitled to such zeal.", "그의 생각으로는 백작과 백작에게 딸린 것들은 그만한 열성을 받을 자격이 있었다."], ["It was the theory in which he had been educated, and amounted to a worship which, unconsciously, he practised.", "그것은 그가 배우며 자라난 신조였고, 저도 모르게 몸으로 지켜 온 신앙에 가까웠다."], ["Personally, he disliked Lord De Courcy, who ill-treated him.", "개인으로 보자면 그는 드 코시 경을 싫어했으니, 백작이 그를 함부로 대했기 때문이다."], ["He knew that the earl was a heartless, cruel, bad man.", "그는 백작이 인정머리 없고 모질고 못된 사람이라는 것을 알았다."], ["But as an earl he was entitled to an amount of service which no commoner could have commanded from Mr. Gazebee.", "그러나 백작인 이상 그는 어떤 평민도 게이지비 씨에게서 끌어낼 수 없었을 만큼의 봉사를 받을 자격이 있었다."], ["Mr. Gazebee, having thus tied up all the available funds in favour of Lady Alexandrina's seemingly expected widowhood, was himself providing the money with which the new house was to be furnished.", "이렇게 하여 쓸 수 있는 돈을 모조리 레이디 알렉산드리나의—어쩐지 미리 내다보기라도 한 듯한—과부 살림 몫으로 묶어 놓은 게이지비 씨는, 새집에 가구를 들일 돈을 제가 대고 있었던 것이다."], ["\"You can pay me a hundred and fifty a year with four per cent. till it is liquidated,\"", "“다 갚을 때까지 해마다 백오십 파운드씩 사 퍼센트 이자를 붙여 내게 갚으면 되네.”"], ["he had said to Crosbie; and Crosbie had assented with a grunt.", "그가 크로즈비에게 말했고, 크로즈비는 끙 소리로 승낙했다."], ["Hitherto, though he had lived in London expensively, and as a man of fashion, he had never owed any one anything.", "여태까지 그는 런던에서 돈을 헤프게 쓰며 멋쟁이답게 살아왔으나 누구에게도 빚을 진 적이 없었다."], ["He was now to begin that career of owing.", "이제 그는 빚지는 이력을 시작하려는 참이었다."], ["But when a clerk in a public office marries an earl's daughter, he cannot expect to have everything his own way.", "그러나 관청 서기가 백작의 딸과 혼인할 때에는 무엇이든 제 뜻대로 되기를 바랄 수 없는 법이다."]], [["Lady Amelia had bought the ordinary furniture--the beds, the stair-carpets, the washing-stands, and the kitchen things.", "레이디 어밀리아는 여느 살림살이를 사들였다—침대, 계단 깔개, 세면대, 부엌살림 따위였다."], ["Gazebee had got a bargain of the dinner-table and sideboard.", "게이지비는 식탁과 찬장을 헐값에 손에 넣었다."], ["But Lady Alexandrina herself was to come up with reference to the appurtenances of the drawing-room.", "그러나 응접실에 딸린 것들에 관해서는 레이디 알렉산드리나가 몸소 올라와야 했다."], ["It was with reference to matters of costume that the countess intended to lend her assistance--matters of costume as to which the bill could not be sent in to Gazebee, and be paid for by him with five per cent. duly charged against the bridegroom.", "백작부인이 손을 보태려고 마음먹은 것은 옷차림에 관한 일이었으니, 그 계산서만은 게이지비에게 보내어 그가 치르고 오 퍼센트를 신랑 앞으로 꼬박꼬박 달아 두는 식으로 할 수가 없는 것이었다."], ["The bridal trousseau must be produced by De Courcy's means, and, therefore, it was necessary that the countess herself should come upon the scene.", "신부의 혼수는 드 코시 쪽 재력으로 마련되어야 했고, 그러니 백작부인 자신이 무대에 나설 수밖에 없었다. “외상은 안 되오, 알아들었소?”"], ["\"I will have no bills, d'ye hear?\"", "백작이 으르렁댔다."], ["snarled the earl, gnashing and snapping upon his words with one specially ugly black tooth.", "유난히 흉하게 시커먼 이 하나로 말끝을 갈아붙이듯 딱딱 씹어 대면서였다."], ["\"I won't have any bills about this affair.\"", "“이 일로 외상 다는 것은 두고 볼 수 없소.”"], ["And yet he made no offer of ready money.", "그러면서도 그는 현금을 내놓겠다는 말은 하지 않았다."], ["It was very necessary under such circumstances that the countess herself should come upon the scene.", "그런 사정이니 백작부인 자신이 무대에 나서는 것이 참으로 필요했다."], ["An ambiguous hint had been conveyed to Mr. Gazebee, during a visit of business which he had lately made to Courcy Castle, that the milliner's bills might as well be pinned on to those of the furniture-makers, the crockery-mongers, and the like.", "얼마 전 게이지비 씨가 볼일로 코시 성에 다녀갔을 때, 양장점 계산서도 가구장이와 그릇장수 따위의 계산서에 함께 붙여 두면 되지 않겠느냐는 아리송한 언질이 그에게 건네졌더랬다."], ["The countess, putting it in her own way, had gently suggested that the fashion of the thing had changed lately, and that such an arrangement was considered to be the proper thing among people who lived really in the world.", "백작부인은 제 나름의 말투로, 요즈음은 그런 일의 유행이 바뀌었으며 정말로 세상 속에서 사는 사람들 사이에서는 그런 처리가 마땅한 것으로 여겨진다고 넌지시 일러 주었다."], ["But Gazebee was a clear-headed, honest man; and he knew the countess.", "그러나 게이지비는 머리가 맑고 정직한 사람이었고, 게다가 백작부인을 잘 알았다."], ["He did not think that such an arrangement could be made on the present occasion.", "그는 이번 일에 그런 처리를 할 수 있으리라고 보지 않았다."], ["Whereupon the countess pushed her suggestion no further, but made up her mind that she must come up to London herself.", "그러자 백작부인은 더 이상 그 말을 밀지 않고, 저 자신이 런던으로 올라가야겠다고 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["It was pleasant to see the Ladies Amelia and Alexandrina, as they sat within a vast emporium of carpets in Bond Street, asking questions of the four men who were waiting upon them, putting their heads together and whispering, calculating accurately as to extra twopences a yard, and occasioning as much trouble as it was possible for them to give.", "본드 스트리트의 커다란 양탄자 상점 안에 앉은 레이디 어밀리아와 레이디 알렉산드리나를 보는 것은 즐거운 일이었다. 저희를 시중드는 네 사내에게 이것저것 물어 가며, 머리를 맞대고 소곤거리며, 야드당 이 펜스 더 붙는 것까지 정확히 셈해 가며, 끼칠 수 있는 만큼의 수고는 죄다 끼치면서 앉아 있는 두 사람을 보는 것 말이다."], ["It was pleasant because they managed their large hoops cleverly among the huge rolls of carpets, because they were enjoying themselves thoroughly, and taking to themselves the homage of the men as clearly their due.", "즐거웠던 것은, 그들이 산더미 같은 양탄자 두루마리 사이에서 큼직한 후프 치마를 솜씨 좋게 다루었기 때문이고, 더할 나위 없이 신이 나 있었기 때문이며, 사내들이 바치는 떠받듦을 저희에게 마땅한 몫으로 여겨 받아들이고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But it was not so pleasant to look at Crosbie, who was fidgeting to get away to his office, to whom no power of choosing in the matter was really given, and whom the men regarded as being altogether supernumerary.", "그러나 크로즈비를 보는 것은 그리 즐겁지 않았다. 그는 사무실로 달아나고 싶어 안절부절못했고, 이 일에서 고를 권한이라고는 실상 조금도 주어져 있지 않았으며, 사내들은 그를 아주 군더더기로 여기고 있었다."], ["The ladies had promised to be at the shop by half-past ten, so that Crosbie should reach his office at eleven--or a little after.", "부인들은 열 시 반까지 가게에 닿아 크로즈비가 열한 시에—아니면 그보다 조금 지나서—사무실에 들어갈 수 있게 해 주겠다고 약속했더랬다."], ["But it was nearly eleven before they left the Gazebee residence, and it was very evident that half-an-hour among the carpets would be by no means sufficient.", "그러나 게이지비네 집을 나선 것이 열한 시가 다 되어서였고, 양탄자 사이에서 반 시간으로는 어림도 없으리라는 것이 뻔했다."], ["It seemed as though miles upon miles of gorgeous colouring were unrolled before them; and then when any pattern was regarded as at all practicable, it was unrolled backwards and forwards till a room was nearly covered by it.", "몇 마일이고 이어지는 화려한 빛깔이 두 사람 앞에 펼쳐지는 듯했고, 그러다 어느 무늬 하나가 그런대로 쓸 만하다 싶으면 방 하나를 거의 덮을 만큼 앞뒤로 펼쳐졌다."], ["Crosbie felt for the men who were hauling about the huge heaps of material; but Lady Amelia sat as composed as though it were her duty to inspect every yard of stuff in the warehouse.", "크로즈비는 그 어마어마한 더미를 끌어 옮기는 사내들이 딱했다. 그러나 레이디 어밀리아는 창고에 있는 천을 한 야드도 빠짐없이 살펴보는 것이 제 의무라도 되는 양 태연히 앉아 있었다."], ["\"I think we'll look at that one at the bottom again.\"", "“저 밑에 있는 걸 한 번 더 봐야겠소.”"], ["Then the men went to work and removed a mountain.", "그러면 사내들은 일에 달려들어 산 하나를 옮겼다."], ["\"No, my dear, that green in the scroll-work won't do.", "“아니야, 앨리, 저 소용돌이 무늬의 초록은 안 되겠소."], ["It would fly directly, if any hot water were spilt.\"", "뜨거운 물이라도 엎지르면 당장 날아가 버릴 테니.”"], ["The man smiling ineffably, declared that that particular green never flew anywhere.", "사내는 말할 수 없이 부드러운 미소를 지으며 그 초록만은 어디서도 날아가는 법이 없다고 잘라 말했다."], ["But Lady Amelia paid no attention to him, and the carpet for which the mountain had been removed became part of another mountain.", "그러나 레이디 어밀리아는 그에게 아무 눈길도 주지 않았고, 그 산을 옮겨 가며 꺼낸 양탄자는 또 다른 산의 한 자락이 되었다."]], [["\"That might do,\"", "“저건 괜찮겠네요.”"], ["said Alexandrina, gazing upon a magnificent crimson ground through which rivers of yellow meandered, carrying with them in their streams an infinity of blue flowers.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다. 노란 강물이 굽이굽이 흐르고 그 물줄기마다 파란 꽃이 끝없이 실려 가는, 눈부신 진홍 바탕을 들여다보면서였다."], ["And as she spoke she held her head gracefully on one side, and looked down upon the carpet doubtingly.", "그렇게 말하며 그녀는 고개를 우아하게 한쪽으로 기울인 채 미심쩍은 눈으로 양탄자를 내려다보았다."], ["Lady Amelia poked it with her parasol as though to test its durability, and whispered something about yellows showing the dirt.", "레이디 어밀리아는 튼튼한지 시험이라도 하듯 양산으로 그것을 콕콕 찔러 보고는, 노랑은 때를 잘 탄다는 말을 소곤거렸다."], ["Crosbie took out his watch and groaned.", "크로즈비는 시계를 꺼내 보고 끙 소리를 냈다."]], [["\"It's a superb carpet, my lady, and about the newest thing we have.", "“더할 나위 없는 양탄자입니다, 마님. 저희 물건 가운데 가장 새로 나온 것이고요."], ["We put down four hundred and fifty yards of it for the Duchess of South Wales, at Cwddglwlch Castle, only last month.", "바로 지난달에 쿠드글룰치 성의 사우스웨일스 공작부인 댁에 이걸로 사백오십 야드를 깔아 드렸습니다."], ["Nobody has had it since, for it has not been in stock.\"", "그 뒤로는 아무도 가져가지 못했지요, 물건이 들어오지 않았으니까요.”"], ["Whereupon Lady Amelia again poked it, and then got up and walked upon it.", "그러자 레이디 어밀리아는 다시 한 번 그것을 찔러 보고, 이어 일어나서 그 위를 걸어 보았다."], ["Lady Alexandrina held her head a little more on one side.", "레이디 알렉산드리나는 고개를 조금 더 한쪽으로 기울였다."]], [["\"Five and three?\"", "“오 실링 삼 펜스요?”"], ["said Lady Amelia.", "레이디 어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, no, my lady; five and seven; and the cheapest carpet we have in the house. There is twopence a yard more in the colour; there is, indeed.\"", "“아이고, 아닙니다, 마님. 오 실링 칠 펜스입니다. 그러고도 저희 집에 있는 양탄자 가운데 가장 싼 것이고요. 이 빛깔에만 야드당 이 펜스가 더 듭니다. 정말입니다.”"]], [["\"And the discount?\"", "“그럼 에누리는요?”"], ["asked Lady Amelia.", "레이디 어밀리아가 물었다."]], [["\"Two and a half, my lady.\"", "“이 점 오 퍼센트입니다, 마님.”"]], [["\"Oh dear, no,\" said Lady Amelia.", "“아이고, 그래서는 안 되오.”"], ["\"I always have five per cent.", "레이디 어밀리아가 말했다."], ["for immediate payment--quite immediate, you know.\"", "“나는 즉시 치를 때는 언제나 오 퍼센트를 받소—아주 그 자리에서 치른다는 말이오.”"], ["Upon which the man declared the question must be referred to his master.", "그러자 사내는 그 문제는 주인에게 여쭈어야겠다고 말했다."], ["Two and a half was the rule of the house.", "이 점 오 퍼센트가 이 집의 규칙이라는 것이었다."], ["Crosbie, who had been looking out of the window, said that upon his honour he couldn't wait any longer.", "창밖을 내다보고 있던 크로즈비가, 명예를 걸고 말하건대 더는 기다릴 수 없다고 말했다."]], [["\"And what do you think of it, Adolphus?\"", "“그런데 당신은 이걸 어떻게 보세요, 어돌퍼스?”"], ["asked Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 물었다."]], [["\"Think of what?\"", "“무엇을 어떻게 보느냐고요?”"]], [["\"Of the carpet--this one, you know!\"", "“양탄자 말이에요—이거요, 이거!”"]], [["\"Oh--what do I think of the carpet?", "“아—양탄자를 어떻게 보느냐고요?"], ["I don't think I quite like all these yellow bands; and isn't it too red?", "이 노란 띠들이 다 마음에 들지는 않는 것 같군요."], ["I should have thought something brown with a small pattern would have been better.", "게다가 너무 붉지 않소? 잔무늬가 든 갈색 같은 것이 나았으리라 싶소만."], ["But, upon my word, I don't much care.\"", "하지만 정말이지 나는 별로 상관없소.”"]], [["\"Of course he doesn't,\"", "“그럼 그렇지.”"], ["said Lady Amelia.", "레이디 어밀리아가 말했다."], ["Then the two ladies put their heads together for another five minutes, and the carpet was chosen--subject to that question of the discount.", "그러고서 두 부인은 다시 오 분 동안 머리를 맞대었고, 마침내 양탄자가 정해졌다—에누리 문제는 남겨 둔 채로였다."], ["\"And now about the rug,\"", "“자, 이제 깔개를 봅시다.”"], ["said Lady Amelia.", "레이디 어밀리아가 말했다."], ["But here Crosbie rebelled, and insisted that he must leave them and go to his office.", "그러나 여기서 크로즈비가 반란을 일으켜, 두 사람을 두고 사무실로 가야겠다고 우겼다."], ["\"You can't want me about the rug,\"", "“깔개 고르는 데 내가 있어야 할 것도 없잖소.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Well, perhaps not,\"", "“뭐, 그야 그렇겠지요.”"], ["said Lady Amelia.", "레이디 어밀리아가 말했다."], ["But it was manifest that Alexandrina did not approve of being thus left by her male attendant.", "그러나 알렉산드리나가 이렇게 제 남자 시종에게 버림받는 것을 달가워하지 않는다는 것만은 분명했다."]], [["The same thing happened in Oxford Street with reference to the chairs and sofas, and Crosbie began to wish that he were settled, even though he should have to dress himself in the closet below the kitchen-stairs.", "의자와 소파를 두고도 옥스퍼드 스트리트에서 똑같은 일이 벌어졌고, 크로즈비는 부엌 계단 밑 골방에서 옷을 갈아입어야 한다 하더라도 어서 살림이 자리 잡히기만을 바라게 되었다."], ["He was learning to hate the whole household in St. John's Wood, and almost all that belonged to it.", "그는 세인트존스 우드의 그 집안 전체를, 그리고 거기 딸린 거의 모든 것을 미워하는 법을 배워 가고 있었다."], ["He was introduced there to little family economies of which hitherto he had known nothing, and which were disgusting to him, and the necessity for which was especially explained to him.", "거기서 그는 여태 알지 못하던 집안의 자잘한 절약을 소개받았는데, 그것들은 그에게 역겨웠고, 게다가 그런 절약이 왜 꼭 있어야 하는지까지 유난히 자세하게 설명을 들어야 했다."], ["It was to men placed as he was about to place himself that these economies were so vitally essential--to men who with limited means had to maintain a decorous outward face towards the fashionable world.", "그런 절약이 그토록 요긴한 것은 바로 그가 이제 놓이려는 처지의 사람들에게라는 것이었다—넉넉지 못한 살림으로 유행하는 세상 앞에서 겉모양만은 반듯하게 지켜야 하는 사람들 말이다."], ["Ample supplies of butchers' meat and unlimited washing-bills might be very well upon fifteen hundred a year to those who went out but seldom, and who could use the first cab that came to hand when they did go out.", "정육점 고기를 넉넉히 들이고 빨래삯을 한정 없이 쓰는 것은, 바깥출입이 드물고 나갈 때면 손에 잡히는 아무 삯마차나 탈 수 있는 사람들에게라면 연 천오백 파운드로도 얼마든지 좋을 것이었다."], ["But there were certain things that Lady Alexandrina must do, and therefore the strictest household economy became necessary.", "그러나 레이디 알렉산드리나가 꼭 해야만 하는 일들이 있었고, 그러니 집안의 가장 빈틈없는 절약이 반드시 있어야 했다."], ["Would Lily Dale have required the use of a carriage, got up to look as though it were private, at the expense of her husband's beefsteaks and clean shirts?", "릴리 데일이라면 제 남편의 쇠고기 스테이크와 깨끗한 셔츠를 줄여 가며, 제 것인 양 꾸며 놓은 마차를 굴려야겠다고 했을까?"], ["That question and others of that nature were asked by Crosbie within his own mind, not unfrequently.", "그런 물음과 그런 종류의 다른 물음들을 크로즈비는 저 혼자 마음속으로 적잖이 던져 보았다."]], [["But, nevertheless, he tried to love Alexandrina, or rather to persuade himself that he loved her.", "그런데도 그는 알렉산드리나를 사랑하려고, 아니 저 자신에게 그녀를 사랑한다고 설득하려고 애썼다."], ["If he could only get her away from the De Courcy faction, and especially from the Gazebee branch of it, he would break her of all that.", "그녀를 드 코시 무리에게서, 특히 게이지비 쪽 가지에게서 떼어 놓을 수만 있다면 그런 버릇은 죄다 고쳐 놓으리라."], ["He would teach her to sit triumphantly in a street cab, and to cater for her table with a plentiful hand.", "삯마차 안에 당당히 앉는 법을, 그리고 넉넉한 손으로 제 식탁을 차리는 법을 가르쳐 주리라."], ["Teach her!--at some age over thirty; and with such careful training as she had already received!", "가르친다니!—서른을 넘긴 나이에, 게다가 그토록 공들여 길든 사람을!"], ["Did he intend to forbid her ever again to see her relations, ever to go to St. John's Wood, or to correspond with the countess and Lady Margaretta?", "앞으로는 친정 식구를 다시는 만나지 말고, 세인트존스 우드에는 얼씬도 말며, 백작부인이나 레이디 마거리타와 편지도 주고받지 말라고 금할 작정이었단 말인가?"], ["Teach her, indeed!", "가르치기는 무엇을 가르친단 말인가!"], ["Had he yet to learn that he could not wash a blackamoor white?--that he could not have done so even had he himself been well adapted for the attempt, whereas he was in truth nearly as ill adapted as a man might be?", "그는 검은 무어인을 씻어 희게 만들 수는 없다는 것을 여태 배우지 못했단 말인가? 설령 저 자신이 그 일에 알맞은 사람이었다 해도 될 수 없는 일이거니와, 실은 그처럼 그 일에 어울리지 않는 사내도 드물었으니 말이다."], ["But who could pity him?", "그러나 누가 그를 딱하게 여기겠는가?"], ["Lily, whom he might have had in his bosom, would have been no blackamoor.", "제 품에 안을 수도 있었던 릴리라면 검은 무어인이 아니었을 것이다."]], [["Then came the time of Lady De Courcy's visit to town, and Alexandrina moved herself off to Portman Square.", "이윽고 드 코시 백작부인이 런던에 오는 날이 되었고, 알렉산드리나는 포트먼 스퀘어로 옮겨 갔다."], ["There was some apparent comfort in this to Crosbie, for he would thereby be saved from those daily dreary journeys up to the north-west.", "크로즈비에게는 여기에 겉으로나마 위안이 있었으니, 그로써 날마다 서북쪽으로 그 을씨년스러운 걸음을 하지 않아도 되었기 때문이다."], ["I may say that he positively hated that windy corner near the church, round which he had to walk in getting to the Gazebee residence, and that he hated the lamp which guided him to the door, and the very door itself.", "나는 그가 교회 옆의 그 바람 부는 모퉁이를—게이지비네 집으로 가려면 돌아야 하는 그 모퉁이를—진저리 나게 미워했으며, 그를 문간으로 이끌어 주는 등불도, 문 그 자체도 미워했다고 말해 두어야겠다."], ["This door stood buried as it were in a wall, and opened on to a narrow passage which ran across a so-called garden, or front yard, containing on each side two iron receptacles for geraniums, painted to look like Palissy ware, and a naked female on a pedestal.", "그 문은 담벼락에 파묻히다시피 서 있었고, 정원이라고도 앞마당이라고도 하는 곳을 가로지르는 좁다란 통로로 열렸다. 그 마당 양편에는 팔리시 도기처럼 보이도록 칠한 쇠제라늄 화분 두 개와, 받침대 위에 벌거벗은 여인상 하나가 놓여 있었다."], ["No spot in London was, as he thought, so cold as the bit of pavement immediately in front of that door.", "그 문 바로 앞의 포석만큼 런던에서 추운 자리는 없다고 그는 생각했다."], ["And there he would be kept five, ten, fifteen minutes, as he declared--though I believe in my heart that the time never exceeded three,--while Richard was putting off the trappings of his work and putting on the trappings of his grandeur.", "그리고 리처드가 일할 때 걸치던 것을 벗고 위세 부리는 차림으로 갈아입는 동안, 그는 거기서 오 분이고 십 분이고 십오 분이고 붙들려 서 있어야 했다—그의 말로는 그러했으나, 내 진심으로 믿기에는 그 시간이 삼 분을 넘긴 적은 결코 없었을 것이다."]], [["If people would only have their doors opened to you by such assistance as may come most easily and naturally to the work!", "사람들이 그 일에 가장 쉽고 자연스럽게 손이 닿는 이를 시켜 문을 열어 주기만 한다면 얼마나 좋겠는가!"], ["I stood lately for some minutes on a Tuesday afternoon at a gallant portal, and as I waxed impatient a pretty maiden came and opened it.", "나는 얼마 전 어느 화요일 오후 어느 으리으리한 대문 앞에 몇 분이나 서 있었는데, 참다 못해 부아가 치밀 무렵 예쁘장한 하녀 하나가 나와 문을 열어 주었다."], ["She was a pretty maiden, though her hands and face and apron told tales of the fire-grates. \"Laws, sir,\"", "손이며 얼굴이며 앞치마가 벽난로 살대를 매만진 이야기를 들려주고 있기는 했으나, 어쨌든 예쁘장한 하녀였다."], ["she said, \"the visitors'", "“아이고, 나리.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["day is Wednesday; and if you would come then, there would be the man in livery!\"", "“손님 받는 날은 수요일입니다요. 그때 오시면 제복 입은 하인이 나올 텐데요!”"], ["She took my card with the corner of her apron, and did just as well as the man in livery; but what would have happened to her had her little speech been overheard by her mistress?", "그녀는 앞치마 귀퉁이로 내 명함을 받아 들었고, 제복 입은 하인 못지않게 제 몫을 해냈다. 그러나 그 자그마한 말을 안주인이 엿들었더라면 그녀는 어찌 되었을까?"]], [["Crosbie hated the house in St. John's Wood, and therefore the coming of the countess was a relief to him.", "크로즈비는 세인트존스 우드의 집을 미워했으므로 백작부인이 오는 것이 그에게는 한숨 돌릴 일이었다."], ["Portman Square was easily to be reached, and the hospitalities of the countess would not be pressed upon him so strongly as those of the Gazebees.", "포트먼 스퀘어는 가기가 수월했고, 백작부인의 대접은 게이지비네의 대접만큼 억지로 들이밀리지도 않을 터였다."], ["When he first called he was shown into the great family dining-room, which looked out towards the back of the house.", "처음 찾아갔을 때 그는 집 뒤편으로 난 커다란 가족용 식당으로 안내되었다."], ["The front windows were, of course, closed, as the family was not supposed to be in London.", "앞쪽 창은 물론 닫혀 있었으니, 이 집 식구들이 런던에 와 있는 것으로 되어 있지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Here he remained in the room for some quarter of an hour, and then the countess descended upon him in all her grandeur.", "그는 십오 분쯤 그 방에 남아 있었고, 이윽고 백작부인이 온갖 위엄을 갖추고 그에게로 내려왔다."], ["Perhaps he had never before seen her so grand.", "아마도 그는 그토록 위엄에 찬 그녀를 여태 본 적이 없었을 것이다."], ["Her dress was very large, and rustled through the broad doorway, as if demanding even a broader passage.", "그녀의 옷은 아주 커다래서, 더 넓은 통로를 요구하기라도 하듯 널찍한 문간을 스치며 지나갔다."], ["She had on a wonder of a bonnet, and a velvet mantle that was nearly as expansive as her petticoats.", "그녀는 놀랍기 그지없는 보닛을 쓰고, 속치마만큼이나 넓게 퍼진 벨벳 망토를 걸치고 있었다."], ["She threw her head a little back as she accosted him, and he instantly perceived that he was enveloped in the fumes of an affectionate but somewhat contemptuous patronage.", "그녀는 그에게 인사를 건네며 고개를 조금 뒤로 젖혔고, 그는 다정하되 어딘가 얕잡아 보는 생색의 연기 속에 저를 감싸이는 것을 대번에 알아차렸다."], ["In old days he had liked the countess, because her manner to him had always been flattering.", "예전에 그는 백작부인을 좋아했으니, 그를 대하는 그녀의 태도가 늘 그를 추어올려 주었기 때문이다."], ["In his intercourse with her he had been able to feel that he gave quite as much as he got, and that the countess was aware of the fact.", "그녀와 오갈 때면 그는 제가 받는 만큼은 넉넉히 주고 있다고, 그리고 백작부인도 그 사실을 알고 있다고 느낄 수 있었다."], ["In all the circumstances of their acquaintance the ascendancy had been with him, and therefore the acquaintance had been a pleasant one.", "두 사람이 아는 사이가 된 온갖 사정에서 우위는 그의 것이었고, 그래서 그 사귐이 즐거웠던 것이다."], ["The countess had been a good-natured, agreeable woman, whose rank and position had made her house pleasant to him; and therefore he had consented to shine upon her with such light as he had to give.", "백작부인은 사람 좋고 상냥한 여자였고, 그 신분과 지위 덕에 그녀의 집이 그에게 즐거운 곳이 되었다. 그리하여 그는 제게 있는 만큼의 빛으로 그녀 위에 빛을 비추어 주는 데 동의했던 것이다."], ["Why was it that the matter was reversed, now that there was so much stronger a cause for good feeling between them?", "두 사람 사이에 서로 좋게 여길 까닭이 이토록 훨씬 강해진 이제 와서, 어찌하여 그 관계가 뒤집혔단 말인가?"], ["He knew that there was such change, and with bitter internal upbraidings he acknowledged to himself that this woman was getting the mastery over him.", "그는 그런 변화가 있다는 것을 알았고, 속으로 쓰디쓴 꾸짖음을 삼키며 이 여자가 저를 손아귀에 넣어 가고 있음을 스스로 인정했다."], ["As the friend of the countess he had been a great man in her eyes;--in all her little words and looks she had acknowledged his power; but now, as her son-in-law, he was to become a very little man,--such as was Mortimer Gazebee!", "백작부인의 벗으로서 그는 그녀의 눈에 대단한 사람이었다—그녀의 자잘한 말과 눈길 하나하나가 그의 힘을 인정해 주었다. 그러나 이제 사위로서 그는 아주 하찮은 사람이 될 참이었다—바로 모티머 게이지비가 그러하듯이!"]], [["\"My dear Adolphus,\"", "“어돌퍼스.” 그녀가 그의 두 손을 잡으며 말했다."], ["she said, taking both his hands, \"the day is coming very near now; is it not?\"", "“이제 그날이 아주 가까워졌구려. 그렇지 않소?”"]], [["\"Very near, indeed,\"", "“정말 가까워졌습니다.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, it is very near.", "“그래요, 아주 가까웠지요."], ["I hope you feel yourself a happy man.\"", "스스로 행복한 사내라고 느끼기를 바라오.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, that's of course.\"", "“아, 그럼요, 그야 물론이지요.”"]], [["\"It ought to be.", "“마땅히 그래야 하고말고."], ["Speaking very seriously, I mean that it ought to be a matter of course.", "아주 진지하게 하는 말이오만, 그것은 당연한 일이어야 한다는 뜻이오."], ["She is everything that a man should desire in a wife.", "그 애는 사내가 아내에게 바랄 만한 모든 것을 갖추었소."], ["I am not alluding now to her rank, though of course you feel what a great advantage she gives you in this respect.\"", "지금 그 애의 신분을 두고 하는 말은 아니오. 물론 그 점에서 그 애가 그대에게 얼마나 큰 이득을 안겨 주는지는 그대도 알고 있겠지만 말이오.”"]], [["Crosbie muttered something as to his consciousness of having drawn a prize in the lottery; but he so muttered it as not to convey to the lady's ears a proper sense of his dependent gratitude.", "크로즈비는 제가 제비뽑기에서 상을 뽑았다는 것쯤은 알고 있다는 투로 무어라 웅얼거렸다. 그러나 하도 웅얼거린 탓에 마땅한 감사와 순종의 뜻이 부인의 귀에 가 닿지는 못했다."], ["\"I know of no man more fortunate than you have been,\"", "“그대만큼 운이 좋았던 사내를 나는 알지 못하오.”"], ["she continued; \"and I hope that my dear girl will find that you are fully aware that it is so.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. “그리고 그대가 그 사실을 훤히 알고 있다는 것을 내 귀한 딸이 알아볼 수 있으면 좋겠소."], ["I think that she is looking rather fagged.", "내 보기에 그 애가 좀 지쳐 보이더군."], ["You have allowed her to do more than was good for her in the way of shopping.\"", "그대가 그 애를 물건 사러 다니는 일에 몸에 좋을 정도보다 더 부려 먹은 게요.”"]], [["\"She has done a good deal, certainly,\"", "“많이 다니기는 했지요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"She is so little used to anything of that kind!", "“그 애가 그런 일에는 워낙 익어 있지 않으니 말이오!"], ["But of course, as things have turned out, it was necessary that she should see to these things herself.\"", "하기야 일이 이렇게 된 이상, 그 애가 몸소 그런 것들을 챙기지 않을 수 없었소만.”"]], [["\"I rather think she liked it,\"", "“오히려 좋아하는 눈치던데요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"I believe she will always like doing her duty.", "“그 애는 언제나 제 도리 하는 것을 좋아할 거요."], ["We are just going now to Madame Millefranc's, to see some silks;--perhaps you would wish to go with us?\"", "우리는 지금 마담 밀프랑네에 비단을 좀 보러 가려던 참이오. 그대도 함께 가고 싶다면 어떻소?”"]], [["Just at this moment Alexandrina came into the room, and looked as though she were in all respects a smaller edition of her mother.", "바로 그때 알렉산드리나가 방으로 들어왔는데, 모든 점에서 제 어머니를 작게 줄여 놓은 판본 같아 보였다."], ["They were both well-grown women, with handsome, large figures, and a certain air about them which answered almost for beauty.", "두 사람 다 몸집이 잘 자란 여자로, 훤칠하고 잘생긴 자태에 아름다움이라고 해도 그만인 어떤 기품 같은 것을 지니고 있었다."], ["As to the countess, her face, on close inspection, bore, as it was entitled to do, deep signs of age; but she so managed her face that any such close inspection was never made; and her general appearance for her time of life was certainly good.", "백작부인으로 말하자면 그 얼굴은 가까이서 뜯어볼 경우 그럴 만도 하게 나이의 자국을 깊이 드러냈다. 그러나 그녀는 그토록 가까이서 뜯어볼 일이 결코 생기지 않도록 제 얼굴을 잘 다루었고, 그 나이대치고 겉으로 보이는 모양새는 분명 훌륭했다."], ["Very little more than this could be said in favour of her daughter.", "그 딸을 두고 좋게 말할 수 있는 것도 그보다 그리 더하지는 않았다."]], [["\"Oh dear, no, mamma,\"", "“어머, 안 돼요, 어머니.”"], ["she said, having heard her mother's last words.", "어머니의 마지막 말을 들은 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"He's the worst person in a shop in the world.", "“이이는 가게에 데려가기로는 세상에서 제일 못한 사람이에요."], ["He likes nothing, and dislikes nothing.", "좋아하는 것도 없고 싫어하는 것도 없다니까요."], ["Do you, Adolphus?\"", "그렇죠, 어돌퍼스?”"]], [["\"Indeed I do.", "“아니요, 있습니다."], ["I like all the cheap things, and dislike all the dear things.\"", "싼 것은 죄다 좋아하고 비싼 것은 죄다 싫어하지요.”"]], [["\"Then you certainly shall not go with us to Madame Millefranc's,\"", "“그렇다면 마담 밀프랑네에는 절대로 우리와 같이 못 가요.”"], ["said Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."]], [["\"It would not matter to him there, you know, my dear,\"", "“거기서야 저이한테 아무 상관 없을 게다, 얘야.” 백작부인이 말했다."], ["said the countess, thinking perhaps of the suggestion she had lately made to Mr. Gazebee.", "아마도 얼마 전 게이지비 씨에게 넌지시 건넨 제안을 떠올리고 한 말이었을 것이다."]], [["On this occasion Crosbie managed to escape, simply promising to return to Portman Square in the evening after dinner.", "그날은 크로즈비도 용케 빠져나올 수 있었으니, 저녁을 든 뒤에 포트먼 스퀘어로 다시 오겠다고 약속하는 것으로 그쳤다."], ["\"By-the-by, Adolphus,\"", "“참, 어돌퍼스.”"], ["said the countess, as he handed her into the hired carriage which stood at the door, \"I wish you would go to Lambert's, on Ludgate Hill, for me.", "문 앞에 세워 둔 세낸 마차에 그가 손을 잡아 부인을 태워 줄 때 백작부인이 말했다. “러드게이트 힐의 램버트 상점에 나 대신 좀 가 주었으면 하오."], ["He has had a bracelet of mine for nearly three months.", "그 사람이 내 팔찌 하나를 석 달 가까이 쥐고 있소."], ["Do, there's a good creature.", "그리 해 주오, 착한 사람이니."], ["Get it if you can, and bring it up this evening.\"", "찾을 수 있거든 찾아서 오늘 저녁에 가져다주오.”"]], [["Crosbie, as he made his way back to his office, swore that he would not do the bidding of the countess.", "사무실로 돌아가는 길에 크로즈비는 백작부인의 심부름 따위는 하지 않겠다고 다짐했다."], ["He would not trudge off into the city after her trinkets.", "그녀의 노리개를 찾겠다고 시내까지 터덜터덜 걸어갈 생각은 없었다."], ["But at five o'clock, when he left his office, he did go there.", "그러나 다섯 시에 사무실을 나서면서 그는 거기에 갔다."], ["He apologized to himself by saying that he had nothing else to do, and bethought himself that at the present moment his lady mother-in-law's smiles might be more convenient than her frowns.", "달리 할 일도 없지 않으냐고 스스로에게 변명했고, 지금 형편으로는 장모 될 이의 찌푸린 얼굴보다 웃는 얼굴이 더 편하겠다는 데 생각이 미쳤던 것이다."], ["So he went to Lambert's, on Ludgate Hill, and there learned that the bracelet had been sent down to Courcy Castle full two months since.", "그리하여 러드게이트 힐의 램버트 상점에 갔고, 거기서 그 팔찌가 두 달도 더 전에 코시 성으로 내려보내졌다는 말을 들었다."]], [["After that he dined at his club, at Sebright's.", "그 뒤에 그는 세브라이트에 있는 제 클럽에서 저녁을 들었다."], ["He dined alone, sitting by no means in bliss with his half-pint of sherry on the table before him.", "혼자서, 앞에 놓인 셰리 반 파인트를 두고 도무지 흐뭇하다고는 할 수 없는 기분으로 앉아 있었다."], ["A man now and then came up and spoke to him, one a few words, and another a few, and two or three congratulated him as to his marriage; but the club was not the same thing to him as it had formerly been.", "이따금 한 사람씩 다가와 말을 걸었는데, 어떤 이는 몇 마디를, 어떤 이는 또 몇 마디를 건넸고, 두어 사람은 혼인을 두고 축하한다고 했다. 그러나 클럽은 그에게 예전 같은 곳이 아니었다."], ["He did not stand in the centre of the rug, speaking indifferently to all or any around him, ready with his joke, and loudly on the alert with the last news of the day.", "그는 난롯가 깔개 한복판에 서서 둘러선 누구에게나 아무렇지 않게 말을 건네지도, 농담을 대령해 놓지도, 그날의 최신 소식을 목청 높여 물어 나르지도 않았다."], ["How easy it is to be seen when any man has fallen from his pride of place, though the altitude was ever so small, and the fall ever so slight.", "사람이 제가 뽐내던 자리에서 굴러떨어졌을 때 그것이 얼마나 쉽게 눈에 띄는지, 그 높이가 아무리 하찮고 그 낙차가 아무리 작다 해도 그러하다."], ["Where is the man who can endure such a fall without showing it in his face, in his voice, in his step, and in every motion of every limb?", "그렇게 떨어지고서 얼굴에, 목소리에, 걸음걸이에, 팔다리의 온갖 움직임에 그것을 드러내지 않을 사내가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["Crosbie knew that he had fallen, and showed that he knew it by the manner in which he ate his mutton chop.", "크로즈비는 제가 떨어졌다는 것을 알았고, 양갈비를 먹는 모양새로 그것을 알고 있음을 드러내 보였다."]], [["At half-past eight he was again in Portman Square, and found the two ladies crowding over a small fire in a small back drawing-room.", "여덟 시 반에 그는 다시 포트먼 스퀘어에 가 있었고, 두 부인이 뒤쪽 작은 응접실의 작은 난롯불 앞에 바짝 붙어 앉아 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The furniture was all covered with brown holland, and the place had about it that cold comfortless feeling which uninhabited rooms always produce.", "가구는 죄다 갈색 홀랜드 천으로 덮여 있었고, 그 자리에는 사람이 살지 않는 방이 언제나 자아내는 그 춥고 스산한 느낌이 감돌았다."], ["Crosbie, as he had walked from the club up to Portman Square, had indulged in some serious thoughts.", "크로즈비는 클럽에서 포트먼 스퀘어까지 걸어오는 동안 자못 심각한 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["The kind of life which he had hitherto led had certainly passed away from him.", "여태 그가 살아온 그런 삶은 이제 확실히 그에게서 지나가 버렸다."], ["He could never again be the pet of a club, or indulged as one to whom all good things were to be given without any labour at earning them on his own part.", "그는 다시는 클럽의 귀염둥이가 될 수도, 제 힘으로 벌지 않고도 온갖 좋은 것을 받아 챙기는 사람이 될 수도 없었다."], ["Such for some years had been his good fortune, but such could be his good fortune no longer.", "몇 해 동안은 그것이 그의 행운이었으나 이제 그런 행운은 더 이상 그의 것일 수 없었다."], ["Was there anything within his reach which he might take in lieu of that which he had lost?", "잃어버린 것 대신 손에 넣을 만한 무언가가 그의 손닿는 데에 있기는 한가?"], ["He might still be victorious at his office, having more capacity for such victory than others around him.", "사무실에서라면 아직 이길 수 있을 터였으니, 그런 승리를 거둘 재간이라면 둘레의 누구보다 그가 더 지니고 있었다."], ["But such success alone would hardly suffice for him.", "그러나 그런 성공만으로는 그에게 도무지 흡족할 리 없었다."], ["Then he considered whether he might not even yet be happy in his own home,--whether Alexandrina, when separated from her mother, might not become such a wife as he could love.", "그러다 그는 저 자신의 집에서라면 아직 행복할 수도 있지 않을까 하고 생각해 보았다—알렉산드리나가 제 어머니에게서 떨어져 나오면 그가 사랑할 만한 아내가 되어 줄 수도 있지 않을까 하고."], ["Nothing softens a man's feelings so much as failure, or makes him turn so anxiously to an idea of home as buffetings from those he meets abroad.", "사내의 마음을 실패만큼 무르게 하는 것이 없고, 밖에서 만나는 이들에게 시달리는 것만큼 그를 애타게 집이라는 생각으로 돌아서게 하는 것도 없다."], ["He had abandoned Lily because his outer world had seemed to him too bright to be deserted.", "그는 릴리를 버렸으니, 바깥세상이 그에게는 버리기에 너무 눈부시게 보였기 때문이다."], ["He would endeavour to supply her place with Alexandrina, because his outer world had seemed to him too harsh to be supported.", "이제 그는 알렉산드리나로 그녀의 자리를 메워 보려 하고 있었으니, 바깥세상이 그에게는 견디기에 너무 모질게 보였기 때문이다."], ["Alas! alas!", "아아, 아아!"], ["a man cannot so easily repent of his sins, and wash himself white from their stains!", "사람이 제 죄를 그리 쉽게 뉘우치고 그 얼룩에서 저를 씻어 희게 할 수는 없는 노릇이다!"]], [["When he entered the room the two ladies were sitting over the fire, as I have stated, and Crosbie could immediately perceive that the spirit of the countess was not serene.", "그가 방에 들어섰을 때 두 부인은 내가 말한 대로 난롯가에 앉아 있었고, 크로즈비는 백작부인의 심기가 편치 않다는 것을 대번에 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["In fact there had been a few words between the mother and child on that matter of the trousseau, and Alexandrina had plainly told her mother that if she were to be married at all she would be married with such garments belonging to her as were fitting for an earl's daughter.", "실은 혼수 문제를 두고 어머니와 딸 사이에 몇 마디가 오간 참이었으며, 알렉산드리나는 어차피 시집을 갈 바에는 백작의 딸에게 걸맞은 옷가지를 제 것으로 갖추고 가겠노라고 어머니에게 딱 잘라 말해 두었다."], ["It was in vain that her mother had explained with many circumlocutional phrases, that the fitness in this respect should be accommodated rather to the plebeian husband than to the noble parent.", "이 점에서 걸맞음이란 귀한 부모 쪽보다는 평민 남편 쪽에 맞추어야 마땅하다고 어머니가 온갖 에두른 말로 설명해 보았으나 헛일이었다."], ["Alexandrina had been very firm, and had insisted on her rights, giving the countess to understand that if her orders for finery were not complied with, she would return as a spinster to Courcy, and prepare herself for partnership with Rosina.", "알렉산드리나는 아주 완강하게 제 권리를 내세웠고, 저의 치레 주문이 받아들여지지 않는다면 처녀인 채로 코시에 돌아가 로지나와 한 패가 될 채비나 하겠다고 백작부인에게 못을 박았다."]], [["\"My dear,\"", "“얘야.”"], ["said the countess, piteously, \"you can have no idea of what I shall have to go through with your father.", "백작부인이 딱한 목소리로 말했다. “내가 네 아버지와 무슨 일을 겪어야 할지 너는 짐작도 못 한다."], ["And, of course, you could get all these things afterwards.\"", "게다가 그런 것들이야 나중에 다 장만할 수 있잖으냐.”"]], [["\"Papa has no right to treat me in such a way.", "“아버지가 저를 그런 식으로 대하실 권리는 없어요."], ["And if he would not give me any money himself, he should have let me have some of my own.\"", "그리고 손수 돈을 안 주실 거라면 제 몫이라도 얼마쯤 쓰게 해 주셨어야죠.”"]], [["\"Ah, my dear, that was Mr. Gazebee's fault.\"", "“얘야, 그건 게이지비 씨 탓이었단다.”"]], [["\"I don't care whose fault it was.", "“누구 탓이든 상관없어요."], ["It certainly was not mine.", "제 탓이 아닌 것만은 분명하니까요."], ["I won't have him to tell me\"--\"him\" was intended to signify Adolphus Crosbie--\"that he had to pay for my wedding-clothes.\"", "제 혼례복 값을 저이가 치러야 했다는 말을 저이한테서”—“저이”란 어돌퍼스 크로즈비를 가리킨 말이었다—“듣고 싶지는 않아요.”"]], [["\"Of course not that, my dear.\"", "“그야 물론 아니지, 얘야.”"]], [["\"No; nor yet for the things which I wanted immediately.", "“그럼요. 그리고 제가 당장 있어야 했던 것들도 그렇고요."], ["I'd much rather go and tell him at once that the marriage must be put off.\"", "그럴 바에는 차라리 지금 당장 가서 혼례를 미뤄야겠다고 말하겠어요.”"]], [["Alexandrina of course carried her point, the countess reflecting with a maternal devotion equal almost to that of the pelican, that the earl could not do more than kill her.", "알렉산드리나는 물론 제 뜻을 관철했다. 백작부인이 펠리컨에 견줄 만한 어미의 헌신으로, 백작이 해 봐야 저를 죽이는 것 이상은 못 하리라고 생각했기 때문이다."], ["So the things were ordered as Alexandrina chose to order them, and the countess desired that the bills might be sent in to Mr. Gazebee.", "그리하여 물건들은 알렉산드리나가 주문하고 싶은 대로 주문되었고, 백작부인은 계산서를 게이지비 씨에게 보내라고 일렀다."], ["Much self-devotion had been displayed by the mother, but the mother thought that none had been displayed by the daughter, and therefore she had been very cross with Alexandrina.", "어머니 쪽에서는 크나큰 자기희생이 있었으나, 어머니가 보기에 딸 쪽에서는 아무런 희생도 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 알렉산드리나에게 몹시 심사가 틀어져 있었던 것이다."]], [["Crosbie, taking a chair, sat himself between them, and in a very good-humoured tone explained the little affair of the bracelet.", "크로즈비는 의자를 끌어다 두 사람 사이에 앉아, 아주 싹싹한 말투로 팔찌의 자잘한 사연을 들려주었다."], ["\"Your ladyship's memory must have played you false,\"", "“부인께서 기억을 잘못하신 모양입니다.”"], ["said he, with a smile.", "그가 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["\"My memory is very good,\"", "“내 기억은 아주 좋소.”"], ["said the countess; \"very good indeed.", "백작부인이 말했다. “아주 좋고말고."], ["If Twitch got it, and didn't tell me, that was not my fault.\"", "트위치가 그걸 받아 놓고 내게 말하지 않았다면 그건 내 잘못이 아니지요.”"], ["Twitch was her ladyship's lady's-maid.", "트위치는 부인의 몸종이었다."], ["Crosbie, seeing how the land lay, said nothing more about the bracelet.", "크로즈비는 형편이 어떻게 돌아가는지 알아채고 팔찌 이야기는 더 꺼내지 않았다."]], [["After a minute or two he put out his hand to take that of Alexandrina.", "일이 분 뒤에 그는 손을 내밀어 알렉산드리나의 손을 잡으려 했다."], ["They were to be married now in a week or two, and such a sign of love might have been allowed to him, even in the presence of the bride's mother.", "이제 한두 주면 혼인할 사이였으니 신부의 어머니가 보는 앞에서라도 그만한 애정의 표시쯤은 허락되어 마땅했을 것이다."], ["He did succeed in getting hold of her fingers, but found in them none of the softness of a response. \"Don't,\"", "그는 용케 그녀의 손가락을 붙잡기는 했으나, 거기에는 답하여 부드러워지는 기색이라고는 없었다."], ["said Lady Alexandrina, withdrawing her hand; and the tone of her voice as she spoke the word was not sweet to his ears.", "“이러지 마세요.” 레이디 알렉산드리나가 손을 빼며 말했고, 그 말을 하는 목소리의 결은 그의 귀에 곱게 들리지 않았다."], ["He remembered at the moment a certain scene which took place one evening at the little bridge at Allington, and Lily's voice, and Lily's words, and Lily's passion, as he caressed her: \"Oh, my love, my love, my love!\"", "그 순간 그는 언젠가 저녁 앨링턴의 작은 다리에서 있었던 어떤 장면을, 그리고 자기가 릴리를 어루만질 때의 그 목소리와 그 말과 그 격정을 떠올렸다. “아, 내 사랑, 내 사랑, 내 사랑!”"]], [["\"My dear,\"", "“얘야.”"], ["said the countess, \"they know how tired I am.", "백작부인이 말했다. “내가 얼마나 고단한지 저 애들도 알 텐데."], ["I wonder whether they are going to give us any tea.\"", "차를 내오기는 할 셈인지 모르겠구나.”"], ["Whereupon Crosbie rang the bell, and, on resuming his chair, moved it a little farther away from his lady-love.", "그러자 크로즈비가 종을 울렸고, 다시 의자에 앉으면서 그것을 제 연인에게서 조금 더 멀찍이 물려 놓았다."]], [["Presently the tea was brought to them by the housekeeper's assistant, who did not appear to have made herself very smart for the occasion, and Crosbie thought that he was de trop.", "이윽고 가정부의 조수가 차를 날라 왔는데, 그 자리를 위해 딱히 몸단장을 한 것 같지도 않아서 크로즈비는 제가 군더더기로구나 싶었다."], ["This, however, was a mistake on his part.", "그러나 이것은 그의 착각이었다."], ["As he had been admitted into the family, such little matters were no longer subject of care.", "그가 이 집안에 받아들여진 이상 그런 자잘한 일은 더 이상 마음 쓸 거리가 아니었던 것이다."], ["Two or three months since, the countess would have fainted at the idea of such a domestic appearing with a tea-tray before Mr. Crosbie.", "두어 달 전이었다면 백작부인은 그런 하인이 크로즈비 씨 앞에 찻쟁반을 들고 나타난다는 생각만으로도 기절했으리라."], ["Now, however, she was utterly indifferent to any such consideration.", "그러나 이제 그녀는 그런 것 따위에는 아주 무심했다."], ["Crosbie was to be admitted into the family, thereby becoming entitled to certain privileges,--and thereby also becoming subject to certain domestic drawbacks.", "크로즈비는 이 집안에 받아들여질 참이었고, 그로써 어떤 특권을 누릴 자격을 얻는 동시에 어떤 집안의 허물도 함께 떠안게 되는 것이었다."], ["In Mrs. Dale's little household there had been no rising to grandeur; but then, also, there had never been any bathos of dirt.", "데일 부인의 자그마한 살림에는 위세를 부리며 올라서는 일이 없었으나, 그 대신 지저분함으로 굴러떨어지는 일도 결코 없었다."], ["Of this also Crosbie thought as he sat with his tea in his hand.", "찻잔을 손에 든 채 앉아 있으면서 크로즈비는 이런 것도 생각했다."]], [["He soon, however, got himself away.", "그러나 그는 곧 자리를 떴다."], ["When he rose to go Alexandrina also rose, and he was permitted to press his nose against her cheekbone by way of a salute.", "그가 가려고 일어서자 알렉산드리나도 일어섰고, 그는 인사 삼아 그녀의 광대뼈에 제 코를 대어 보는 것을 허락받았다."]], [["\"Good-night, Adolphus,\"", "“잘 가오, 어돌퍼스.”"], ["said the countess, putting out her hand to him.", "백작부인이 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["\"But stop a minute; I know there is something I want you to do for me.", "“아, 잠깐만. 그대에게 부탁할 일이 하나 있었는데."], ["But you will look in as you go to your office to-morrow morning.\"", "아무튼 내일 아침 사무실에 가는 길에 들러 주오.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLI. DOMESTIC TROUBLES.", "# 제41장. 집안의 풍파"]], [["When Crosbie was making his ineffectual inquiry after Lady De Courcy's bracelet at Lambert's, John Eames was in the act of entering Mrs. Roper's front door in Burton Crescent.", "크로즈비가 램버트 상점에서 드 코시 백작부인의 팔찌를 찾다가 헛걸음을 하고 있던 바로 그때, 존 임스는 버턴 크레슨트의 로퍼 부인네 대문으로 막 들어서고 있었다."]], [["\"Oh, John, where's Mr. Cradell?\"", "“어머, 존, 크래들 씨는 어디 계세요?”"], ["were the first words which greeted him, and they were spoken by the divine Amelia.", "그를 맞은 첫마디가 그것이었고, 그 말을 한 사람은 그 거룩하신 어밀리아였다."], ["Now, in her usual practice of life, Amelia did not interest herself much as to the whereabouts of Mr. Cradell.", "그런데 평소 살아가는 방식으로 보자면 어밀리아는 크래들 씨가 어디에 있는지 따위에는 그리 마음을 쓰는 사람이 아니었다."]], [["\"Where's Cradell?\"", "“크래들이 어디 있느냐고요?”"], ["said Eames, repeating the question.", "임스가 그 물음을 되뇌며 말했다."], ["\"Upon my word, I don't know.", "“정말이지 나도 모르겠는데요."], ["I walked to the office with him, but I haven't seen him since.", "함께 걸어서 관청까지 가기는 했는데, 그 뒤로는 못 봤어요."], ["We don't sit in the same room, you know.\"", "우리는 같은 방에 앉아 있지 않으니까요, 알잖아요.”"]], [["\"John!\"", "“존!”"], ["and then she stopped.", "그러고서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["\"What's up now?\"", "“이번엔 또 무슨 일이에요?”"], ["said John.", "존이 말했다."]], [["\"John!", "“존!"], ["That woman's off and left her husband.", "그 여자가 남편을 버리고 달아났어요."], ["As sure as your name's John Eames, that foolish fellow has gone off with her.\"", "당신 이름이 존 임스인 것만큼이나 틀림없이, 그 어리석은 사람이 그 여자와 함께 달아난 거예요.”"]], [["\"What, Cradell?", "“뭐라고요, 크래들이?"], ["I don't believe it.\"", "나는 못 믿겠는데요.”"]], [["\"She went out of this house at two o'clock in the afternoon, and has never been back since.\"", "“그 여자는 오늘 오후 두 시에 이 집을 나가서 여태 돌아오지 않았어요.”"], ["That, certainly, was only four hours from the present time, and such an absence from home in the middle of the day was but weak evidence on which to charge a married woman with the great sin of running off with a lover.", "그것은 물론 지금으로부터 겨우 네 시간 전의 일이었고, 한낮에 그렇게 집을 비웠다는 것은 유부녀에게 정부와 달아났다는 큰 죄를 씌우기에는 약한 증거일 뿐이었다."], ["This Amelia felt, and therefore she went on to explain.", "어밀리아도 그것을 느꼈으므로 이렇게 설명을 덧붙였다."], ["\"He's there upstairs in the drawing-room, the very picture of disconsolateness.\"", "“그 사람이 위층 응접실에 있는데, 낙담이라는 것을 그림으로 그려 놓은 꼴이에요.”"]], [["\"Who,--Cradell?\"", "“누구요—크래들이요?”"]], [["\"Lupex is.", "“루펙스 씨요."], ["He's been drinking a little, I'm afraid; but he's very unhappy, indeed.", "술을 좀 마신 것 같기는 한데, 정말이지 몹시 언짢아하고 있어요."], ["He had an appointment to meet his wife here at four o'clock, and when he came he found her gone.", "네 시에 여기서 아내를 만나기로 약속이 되어 있었는데, 와 보니 아내가 없었던 거예요."], ["He rushed up into their room, and now he says she has broken open a box he had and taken off all his money.\"", "두 사람 방으로 뛰어 올라갔고, 이제 와서 하는 말이 아내가 제 상자를 부수고 돈을 몽땅 가져갔다는 거예요.”"]], [["\"But he never had any money.\"", "“하지만 그 사람한테는 돈이라고는 있어 본 적이 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"He paid mother some the day before yesterday.\"", "“그저께 엄마한테 얼마쯤 냈는걸요.”"]], [["\"That's just the reason he shouldn't have any to-day.\"", "“그러니까 오늘은 더더욱 돈이 있을 리 없지요.”"]], [["\"She certainly has taken things she wouldn't have taken if she'd merely gone out shopping or anything like that, for I've been up in the room and looked about it.", "“그 여자가 그냥 물건을 사러 나갔다거나 그런 거라면 가져가지 않았을 것들을 가져간 건 분명해요. 내가 방에 올라가서 둘러봤거든요."], ["She'd three necklaces.", "그 여자한테 목걸이가 세 개 있었어요."], ["They weren't much account; but she must have them all on, or else have got them in her pocket.\"", "대단한 것들은 아니었지만, 그걸 다 걸고 갔거나 아니면 주머니에 넣어 갔을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Cradell has never gone off with her in that way.", "“크래들이 그런 식으로 그 여자와 달아났을 리는 없어요."], ["He may be a fool--\"", "바보이기야 하겠지만—”"]], [["\"Oh, he is, you know.", "“어머, 바보 맞지요, 알잖아요."], ["I've never seen such a fool about a woman as he has been.\"", "여자 문제로 그렇게 바보 같은 사람은 본 적이 없어요.”"]], [["\"But he wouldn't be a party to stealing a lot of trumpery trinkets, or taking her husband's money.", "“하지만 그 사람이 하찮은 장신구 나부랭이를 훔치거나 남편 돈을 가져가는 짓에 한몫 끼지는 않았을 거예요."], ["Indeed, I don't think he has anything to do with it.\"", "정말이지 나는 그 사람이 이 일과 아무 상관도 없다고 생각해요.”"], ["Then Eames thought over the circumstances of the day, and remembered that he had certainly not seen Cradell since the morning.", "그러고서 임스는 그날 하루의 일들을 되짚어 보았고, 아침 이후로 크래들을 본 적이 없다는 것을 분명히 떠올렸다."], ["It was that public servant's practice to saunter into Eames's room in the middle of the day, and there consume bread and cheese and beer,--in spite of an assertion which Johnny had once made as to crumbs of biscuit bathed in ink.", "그 관리로 말하면 한낮에 임스의 방으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어와 거기서 빵과 치즈와 맥주를 먹어 치우는 것이 버릇이었다—언젠가 조니가 잉크에 적신 비스킷 부스러기 운운했던 것과는 딴판으로 말이다."], ["But on this special day he had not done so.", "그런데 이 특별한 날에는 그러지 않았던 것이다."], ["\"I can't think he has been such a fool as that,\"", "“그 친구가 그런 바보짓을 했을 것 같지는 않은데요.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"But he has,\"", "“그런데 했다니까요.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."], ["\"It's dinner-time now, and where is he?", "“벌써 저녁 먹을 시각인데 그 사람이 어디 있나요?"], ["Had he any money left, Johnny?\"", "그이한테 돈이 남아 있었어요, 조니?”"]], [["So interrogated, Eames disclosed a secret confided to him by his friend which no other circumstances would have succeeded in dragging from his breast.", "그렇게 다그침을 받자 임스는 벗이 제게 털어놓은 비밀 하나를 드러내고 말았으니, 다른 어떤 사정으로도 그의 가슴에서 끌어낼 수 없었을 것이었다."]], [["\"She borrowed twelve pounds from him about a fortnight since, immediately after quarter-day.", "“보름쯤 전에, 분기 지급일 바로 뒤에 그 부인이 그 친구한테서 열두 파운드를 빌렸어요."], ["And she owed him money, too, before that.\"", "게다가 그 전에도 진 빚이 있었고요.”"]], [["\"Oh, what a soft!\"", "“어머, 저런 맹추!”"], ["exclaimed Amelia; \"and he hasn't paid mother a shilling for the last two months!\"", "어밀리아가 소리쳤다. “그러면서 두 달째 엄마한테는 한 실링도 안 냈잖아요!”"]], [["\"It was his money, perhaps, that Mrs. Roper got from Lupex the day before yesterday.", "“그저께 로퍼 부인이 루펙스 씨한테서 받은 게 어쩌면 그 친구 돈이었을지도 모르지요."], ["If so, it comes to the same thing as far as she is concerned, you know.\"", "그렇다면 로퍼 부인 쪽으로 보자면 결국 같은 이야기 아닌가요.”"]], [["\"And what are we to do now?\"", "“그럼 우리는 이제 어떻게 해야 하죠?”"], ["said Amelia, as she went before her lover upstairs.", "어밀리아가 제 연인보다 앞서 층계를 오르며 말했다."], ["\"Oh, John, what will become of me if ever you serve me in that way?", "“아, 존, 당신이 언젠가 나를 그런 식으로 대한다면 나는 어떻게 되나요?"], ["What should I do if you were to go off with another lady?\"", "당신이 다른 여자와 달아나 버리면 나는 어떻게 해야 하죠?”"]], [["\"Lupex hasn't gone off,\"", "“루펙스 씨는 달아나지 않았는데요.”"], ["said Eames, who hardly knew what to say when the matter was brought before him with so closely personal a reference.", "임스가 말했다. 문제가 그토록 바싹 제 이야기로 끌려 들어오자 그는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["\"But it's the same thing,\"", "“하지만 마찬가지지요.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."], ["\"Hearts is divided.", "“마음이 갈라지는 거잖아요."], ["Hearts that have been joined together ought never to be divided; ought they?\"", "한번 맺어진 마음은 결코 갈라져서는 안 되는 거예요. 안 그래요?”"], ["And then she hung upon his arm just as they got to the drawing-room door.", "그러고서 그녀는 응접실 문 앞에 이르는 바로 그 순간 그의 팔에 매달렸다."]], [["\"Hearts and darts are all my eye,\"", "“마음이니 화살이니 하는 건 다 헛소리예요.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."], ["\"My belief is that a man had better never marry at all.", "“내 생각에는 사내란 아예 혼인을 하지 않는 편이 나아요."], ["How d'you do, Mr. Lupex?", "안녕하십니까, 루펙스 씨?"], ["Is anything the matter?\"", "무슨 일이라도 있습니까?”"]], [["Mr. Lupex was seated on a chair in the middle of the room, and was leaning with his head over the back of it.", "루펙스 씨는 방 한가운데 놓인 의자에 앉아 등받이 너머로 머리를 젖히고 있었다."], ["So despondent was he in his attitude that his head would have fallen off and rolled on to the floor, had it followed the course which its owner seemed to intend that it should take. His hands hung down also along the back legs of the chair, till his fingers almost touched the ground, and altogether his appearance was pendent, drooping, and wobegone.", "그 자세가 어찌나 낙심한 꼴이었던지, 머리가 제 임자가 정해 준 듯한 방향으로 계속 나아갔더라면 목에서 떨어져 나가 마룻바닥으로 굴렀을 것이다."], ["Miss Spruce was seated in one corner of the room, with her hands folded in her lap before her, and Mrs. Roper was standing on the rug with a look of severe virtue on her brow,--of virtue which, to judge by its appearance, was very severe.", "두 손 또한 의자 뒷다리를 따라 아래로 늘어져 손가락이 거의 바닥에 닿을 지경이었고, 통틀어 그의 몰골은 축 늘어지고 처지고 처량했다."], ["Nor was its severity intended to be exercised solely against Mrs. Lupex.", "스프루스 양은 방 한구석에 두 손을 앞으로 모아 무릎 위에 포개어 놓고 앉아 있었고, 로퍼 부인은 이마에 준엄한 덕성의 빛을—겉모양으로 판단하건대 아주 준엄하기 그지없는 덕성의 빛을 띠고 난롯가 깔개 위에 서 있었다."], ["Mrs. Roper was becoming very tired of Mr.", "그리고 그 준엄함이 오직 루펙스 부인만을 겨눈 것도 아니었다."], ["Lupex also, and would not have been unhappy if he also had run away,--leaving behind him so much of his property as would have paid his bill.", "로퍼 부인은 루펙스 씨에게도 몹시 진력이 나 있던 터라, 그 역시 달아나 준다면—제 셈을 치를 만큼의 재산은 뒤에 남겨 두고 간다면—섭섭할 것이 없었다."]], [["Mr. Lupex did not stir when first addressed by John Eames, but a certain convulsive movement was to be seen on the back of his head, indicating that this new arrival in the drawing-room had produced a fresh accession of agony.", "존 임스가 처음 말을 건넸을 때 루펙스 씨는 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다."], ["The chair, too, quivered under him, and his fingers stretched themselves nearer to the ground and shook themselves.", "다만 뒤통수에 얼마간 경련 같은 움직임이 이는 것이 보였으니, 이 응접실에 새로 들어선 사람이 고통을 한층 더 돋우었음을 알리는 것이었다. 의자도 그의 밑에서 떨렸고, 손가락은 바닥을 향해 더 가까이 뻗어 나가며 부르르 흔들렸다."]], [["\"Mr. Lupex, we're going to dinner immediately,\" said Mrs. Roper. \"Mr. Eames, where is your friend, Mr. Cradell?\"", "“루펙스 씨, 우리는 이제 곧 저녁을 들 참입니다.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다. “임스 씨, 댁의 벗 크래들 씨는 어디 계신가요?”"]], [["\"Upon my word I don't know,\"", "“정말이지 저도 모르겠습니다.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"But I know,\"", "“하지만 나는 아오.”"], ["said Lupex, jumping up and standing at his full height, while he knocked down the chair which had lately supported him.", "루펙스가 벌떡 일어나 온 키를 펴고 서면서, 방금까지 제 몸을 받쳐 주던 의자를 넘어뜨리며 말했다. “가정의 행복을 배반한 자!"], ["\"The traitor to domestic bliss!", "나는 안단 말이오."], ["I know. And wherever he is, he has that false woman in his arms.", "그자가 어디에 있든, 그 거짓된 여자를 제 품에 안고 있소."], ["Would he were here!\"", "그자가 여기 있기만 하다면!”"], ["And as he expressed the last wish he went through a motion with his hands and arms which seemed intended to signify that if that unfortunate young man were in the company he would pull him in pieces and double him up, and pack him close, and then despatch his remains off, through infinite space, to the Prince of Darkness.", "그리고 그 마지막 말을 뱉으면서 그는 두 손과 두 팔로 어떤 동작을 해 보였으니, 그 딱한 젊은이가 이 자리에 있기만 하다면 갈가리 찢어 반으로 접고 차곡차곡 눌러 담은 다음, 그 잔해를 끝없는 허공 너머 어둠의 왕에게로 부쳐 버리겠다는 뜻인 듯했다."], ["\"Traitor,\"", "“배반자.”"], ["he exclaimed, as he finished the process.", "그 절차를 끝내고서 그가 외쳤다. “거짓된 배반자!"], ["\"False traitor!", "더러운 배반자!"], ["Foul traitor! And she too!\"", "그리고 그 여자도!”"], ["Then, as he thought of this softer side of the subject, he prepared himself to relapse again on to the chair.", "그러고서 이 일의 더 부드러운 쪽을 생각하자 그는 다시 의자에 몸을 던질 채비를 했다."], ["Finding it on the ground he had to pick it up.", "의자가 바닥에 넘어져 있는 것을 보고 그는 그것을 집어 들어야 했다."], ["He did pick it up, and once more flung away his head over the back of it, and stretched his finger-nails almost down to the carpet.", "그는 그것을 집어 들었고, 다시 한번 등받이 너머로 머리를 젖히고 손톱을 거의 양탄자에 닿도록 뻗었다."]], [["\"James,\" said Mrs.", "“제임스,”"], ["Roper to her son, who was now in the room, \"I think you'd better stay with Mr.", "로퍼 부인이 어느새 방에 들어와 있던 제 아들에게 말했다. “우리가 저녁을 드는 동안 네가 루펙스 씨 곁에 있어 드리는 게 좋겠구나."], ["Lupex while we are at dinner. Come, Miss Spruce, I'm very sorry that you should be annoyed by this kind of thing.\"", "자, 스프루스 양, 이런 일로 언짢으시게 해 드려서 참 죄송해요.”"]], [["\"It don't hurt me,\"", "“저야 상할 것도 없지요.”"], ["said Miss Spruce, preparing to leave the room.", "스프루스 양이 방을 나설 채비를 하며 말했다."], ["\"I'm only an old woman.\"", "“저야 뭐, 그저 늙은 여자일 뿐이니까요.”"]], [["\"Annoyed!\"", "“언짢으시다니!”"], ["said Lupex, raising himself again from his chair, not perhaps altogether disposed to remain upstairs while the dinner, for which it was intended that he should some day pay, was being eaten below.", "루펙스가 다시 의자에서 몸을 일으키며 말했다. 언젠가 제가 셈을 치르기로 되어 있는 그 저녁이 아래층에서 먹히고 있는 동안 위층에 남아 있고 싶은 마음이 딱히 있었던 것도 아니었다."], ["\"Annoyed!", "“언짢으시다니!"], ["It is a profound sorrow to me that any lady should be annoyed by my misfortunes.", "어느 부인이시든 내 불행 때문에 언짢으셔야 한다는 것은 나에게 깊은 슬픔이오."], ["As regards Miss Spruce, I look upon her character with profound veneration.\"", "스프루스 양으로 말하면, 나는 그 인품을 깊이 우러러보고 있소.”"]], [["\"You needn't mind me; I'm only an old woman,\"", "“저는 마음 쓰지 않으셔도 됩니다. 저야 그저 늙은 여자일 뿐이지요.”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."]], [["\"But, by heavens, I do mind!\"", "“그러나, 맹세코, 나는 마음을 쓴단 말이오!”"], ["exclaimed Lupex; and hurrying forward he seized Miss Spruce by the hand.", "루펙스가 소리치며 앞으로 달려 나가 스프루스 양의 손을 붙잡았다."], ["\"I shall always regard age as entitled--\"", "“나는 언제나 나이 든 분에게는 마땅히—”"], ["But the special privileges which Mr. Lupex would have accorded to age were never made known to the inhabitants of Mrs.", "그러나 루펙스 씨가 나이 든 이에게 돌리려 했던 그 각별한 특권이 무엇인지는 로퍼 부인네 하숙집 사람들에게 끝내 알려지지 않았다."], ["Roper's boarding-house, for the door of the room was again opened at this moment, and Mr. Cradell entered.", "바로 그 순간 방문이 다시 열리며 크래들 씨가 들어섰기 때문이다."]], [["\"Here you are, old fellow, to answer for yourself,\"", "“마침 왔군, 이 사람아, 자네 일은 자네가 해명하게.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["Cradell, who had heard something as he came in at the front door, but had not heard that Lupex was in the drawing-room, made a slight start backwards when he saw that gentleman's face.", "크래들은 대문으로 들어서면서 무슨 소리를 얼마쯤 듣기는 했으나 루펙스가 응접실에 있다는 것까지는 듣지 못한 터라, 그 신사의 얼굴을 보자 흠칫 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["\"Upon my word and honour,\"", "“맹세코 제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데,”"], ["he began;--but he was able to carry his speech no further.", "그가 입을 열었다—그러나 그는 말을 더 잇지 못했다."], ["Lupex, dropping the hand of the elderly lady whom he reverenced, was upon him in an instant, and Cradell was shaking beneath his grasp like an aspen leaf,--or rather not like an aspen leaf, unless an aspen leaf when shaken is to be seen with its eyes shut, its mouth open, and its tongue hanging out.", "루펙스는 저가 우러른다던 그 나이 든 부인의 손을 놓아 버리고 순식간에 그에게 달려들었고, 크래들은 그 손아귀 아래에서 사시나무 잎처럼 떨었다—아니, 흔들리는 사시나무 잎이 눈을 감고 입을 벌리고 혀를 늘어뜨린 꼴로 보이는 것이 아니라면, 사시나무 잎과는 딴판이었다."]], [["\"Come, I say,\"", "“이보십시오,”"], ["said Eames, stepping forward to his friend's assistance; \"this won't do at all, Mr.", "임스가 벗을 도우러 앞으로 나서며 말했다. “이건 도무지 안 될 일입니다, 루펙스 씨."], ["Lupex. You've been drinking.", "술을 드셨군요."], ["You'd better wait till to-morrow morning, and speak to Cradell then.\"", "내일 아침까지 기다렸다가 그때 크래들에게 말씀하시는 편이 좋겠습니다.”"]], [["\"To-morrow morning, viper,\"", "“내일 아침이라니, 이 독사 같은 놈.”"], ["shouted Lupex, still holding his prey, but looking back at Eames over his shoulder.", "루펙스가 제 먹이를 붙든 채 어깨 너머로 임스를 돌아보며 고함쳤다."], ["Who the viper was had not been clearly indicated.", "누가 독사인지는 분명히 밝혀지지 않았다."], ["\"When will he restore to me my wife?", "“저자가 언제 내 아내를 돌려준단 말이오?"], ["When will he restore to me my honour?\"", "언제 내 명예를 돌려준단 말이오?”"]], [["\"Upon-on-on-on my--\" It was for the moment in vain that poor Mr.", "“매—맹—맹세코 제—”"], ["Cradell endeavoured to asseverate his innocence, and to stake his honour upon his own purity as regarded Mrs. Lupex.", "가엾은 크래들 씨가 제 결백을 다짐하고 루펙스 부인에 관한 한 제 깨끗함에 명예를 걸어 보려 한 것은 그 순간에는 헛일이었다."], ["Lupex still held to his enemy's cravat, though Eames had now got him by the arm, and so far impeded his movements as to hinder him from proceeding to any graver attack.", "루펙스는 여전히 제 원수의 크라바트를 붙들고 있었다. 임스가 이제 그의 팔을 붙잡아 움직임을 얼마쯤 막아 놓았으므로 더 심한 공격으로 나아가지는 못했지만 말이다."]], [["\"Jemima, Jemima, Jemima!\"", "“제미마, 제미마, 제미마!”"], ["shouted Mrs. Roper.", "로퍼 부인이 외쳤다."], ["\"Run for the police; run for the police!\"", "“순경을 불러 오너라. 순경을 불러 와!”"], ["But Amelia, who had more presence of mind than her mother, stopped Jemima as she was making to one of the front windows.", "그러나 어머니보다 침착한 어밀리아가 앞쪽 창문 하나로 달려가려는 제미마를 붙들었다. “그냥 거기 있어.”"], ["\"Keep where you are,\"", "어밀리아가 말했다."], ["said Amelia. \"They'll come quiet in a minute or two.\"", "“한두 번 숨 쉴 참이면 조용해질 테니까.”"], ["And Amelia no doubt was right.", "그리고 어밀리아의 말이 옳았음은 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["Calling for the police when there is a row in the house is like summoning the water-engines when the soot is on fire in the kitchen chimney.", "집안에 소동이 났다고 순경을 부르는 것은 부엌 굴뚝의 검댕에 불이 붙었다고 소방 펌프를 불러들이는 것과 같다."], ["In such cases good management will allow the soot to burn itself out, without aid from the water-engines.", "그런 경우에는 잘만 다스리면 소방 펌프의 도움 없이도 검댕이 저절로 다 타 버리게 할 수 있다."], ["In the present instance the police were not called in, and I am inclined to think that their presence would not have been advantageous to any of the party.", "이번 일에서도 순경은 불려 오지 않았고, 그들이 왔더라면 그 자리의 누구에게도 이롭지 않았으리라고 나는 생각하는 편이다."]], [["\"Upon-my-honour--I know nothing about her,\"", "“맹—세—코—저는 그 부인에 대해 아무것도 모릅니다.”"], ["were the first words which Cradell was able to articulate, when Lupex, under Eames's persuasion, at last relaxed his hold.", "임스의 설득으로 루펙스가 마침내 손아귀를 늦추었을 때 크래들이 겨우 뱉어 낸 첫마디가 그것이었다."]], [["Lupex turned round to Miss Spruce with a sardonic grin.", "루펙스는 비웃는 웃음을 띠고 스프루스 양 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["\"You hear his words,--this enemy to domestic bliss,--Ha, ha!", "“이자의 말을 들어 보시오—가정의 행복을 해치는 이 원수의 말을—하! 하!"], ["man, tell me whither you have conveyed my wife!\"", "여보시오, 내 아내를 어디로 데려다 놓았는지 말하시오!”"]], [["\"If you were to give me the Bank of England I don't know,\"", "“저한테 잉글랜드 은행을 다 주신대도 저는 모릅니다.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."]], [["\"And I'm sure he does not know,\" said Mrs.", "“저이는 정말 모르는 게 분명해요.”"], ["Roper, whose suspicions against Cradell were beginning to subside.", "로퍼 부인이 말했다. 크래들을 향한 그녀의 의심은 가라앉기 시작하고 있었다."], ["But as her suspicions subsided, her respect for him decreased.", "그러나 의심이 가라앉는 만큼 그에 대한 존경도 줄어들었다."], ["Such was the case also with Miss Spruce, and with Amelia, and with Jemima.", "스프루스 양도, 어밀리아도, 제미마도 마찬가지였다."], ["They had all thought him to be a great fool for running away with Mrs. Lupex, but now they were beginning to think him a poor creature because he had not done so.", "그들은 모두 그가 루펙스 부인과 달아났다며 지독한 얼간이라고 여겨 왔는데, 이제는 달아나지 않았다는 이유로 그를 못난 위인이라고 여기기 시작한 것이다."], ["Had he committed that active folly he would have been an interesting fool.", "그 활발한 어리석음을 저질렀더라면 그는 흥미로운 얼간이가 되었을 터였다."], ["But now, if, as they all suspected, he knew no more about Mrs. Lupex than they did, he would be a fool without any special interest whatever.", "그러나 이제, 그들 모두가 짐작하는 대로 그가 루펙스 부인에 대해 저희보다 더 아는 것이 없다면, 그는 아무런 흥미도 없는 그저 얼간이일 뿐이었다."]], [["\"Of course he doesn't,\"", "“물론 모르지요.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"No more than I do,\"", "“나도 모르는 것만큼이나요.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"His very looks show him innocent,\"", "“저이 얼굴만 봐도 결백하다는 게 드러나지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Roper.", "로퍼 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Indeed they do,\"", "“정말 그렇습니다.”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."]], [["Lupex turned from one to the other as they thus defended the man whom he suspected, and shook his head at each assertion that was made.", "루펙스는 저가 의심하는 사내를 그렇게들 두둔하는 동안 이 사람 저 사람에게로 몸을 돌리며, 그런 말이 나올 때마다 고개를 내저었다."], ["\"And if he doesn't know who does?\"", "“그럼 저자가 모른다면 누가 안단 말이오?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["\"Haven't I seen it all for the last three months?", "“내가 지난 석 달 동안 이 모든 것을 보아 오지 않았소?"], ["Is it reasonable to suppose that a creature such as she, used to domestic comforts all her life, should have gone off in this way, at dinner-time, taking with her my property and all her jewels, and that nobody should have instigated her; nobody assisted her!", "평생 가정의 안락에 길들어 온 그런 여자가 저녁 시각에 내 재산과 제 패물을 죄다 챙겨 이런 식으로 나가 버렸는데, 아무도 그녀를 부추기지 않았고 아무도 도와주지 않았다는 것이 말이나 되오?"], ["Is that a story to tell to such a man as me!", "그것이 나 같은 사람에게 들려줄 이야기요?"], ["You may tell it to the marines!\" Mr.", "그런 소리는 해병한테나 가서 하시오!”"], ["Lupex, as he made this speech, was walking about the room, and as he finished it he threw his pocket-handkerchief with violence on to the floor.", "루펙스 씨는 이 말을 하는 동안 방 안을 서성이고 있었고, 말을 마치면서 제 주머니 손수건을 힘껏 바닥에 내던졌다. “나는 무엇을 해야 할지 아오, 로퍼 부인.”"], ["\"I know what to do, Mrs. Roper,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said. \"I know what steps to take.", "“어떤 수를 밟아야 할지 안단 말이오."], ["I shall put the affair into the hands of my lawyer to-morrow morning.\"", "내일 아침에 이 일을 내 변호사 손에 넘기겠소.”"], ["Then he picked up his handkerchief and walked down into the dining-room.", "그러고서 그는 손수건을 집어 들고 식당으로 걸어 내려갔다."]], [["\"Of course you know nothing about it?\"", "“자네야 물론 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르지?”"], ["said Eames to his friend, having run upstairs for the purpose of saying a word to him while he washed his hands.", "임스가 벗에게 한마디 하려고 위층으로 뛰어 올라가, 손을 씻고 있는 그에게 말했다."]], [["\"What,--about Maria?", "“뭐—머라이어 말인가?"], ["I don't know where she is, if you mean that.\"", "그걸 묻는 거라면, 나는 그이가 어디 있는지 모르네.”"]], [["\"Of course I mean that.", "“물론 그걸 묻는 거지."], ["What else should I mean?", "달리 무얼 묻겠나?"], ["And what makes you call her Maria?\"", "그런데 자네는 어째서 그이를 머라이어라고 부르나?”"]], [["\"It is wrong.", "“잘못이지."], ["I admit it's wrong.", "잘못인 줄은 나도 인정하네."], ["The word will come out, you know.\"", "그 말이 저절로 튀어나오는 걸 어쩌겠나.”"]], [["\"Will come out!", "“저절로 튀어나온다니!"], ["I'll tell you what it is, old fellow, you'll get yourself into a mess, and all for nothing.", "내 한마디 해 두겠는데, 이 사람아, 자네는 아무것도 아닌 일로 스스로를 곤경에 빠뜨릴 걸세."], ["That fellow will have you up before the police for stealing his things--\"", "그자가 제 물건을 훔쳤다고 자네를 순경 앞에 끌고 갈 거야—”"]], [["\"But, Johnny--\"", "“하지만, 조니—”"]], [["\"I know all about it.", "“나도 다 아네."], ["Of course you have not stolen them, and of course there was nothing to steal.", "물론 자네는 아무것도 훔치지 않았고, 물론 훔칠 것도 없었지."], ["But if you go on calling her Maria you'll find that he'll have a pull on you.", "하지만 자네가 계속 그이를 머라이어라고 부르고 다니면 그자가 자네를 걸고넘어질 구실을 잡게 될 걸세."], ["Men don't call other men's wives names for nothing.\"", "사내가 남의 아내를 이름으로 부르는 데에는 반드시 까닭이 있는 법이니까.”"]], [["\"Of course we've been friends,\"", "“물론 우리는 벗이었지.” 크래들이 말했다."], ["said Cradell, who rather liked this view of the matter.", "그는 이렇게 보아 주는 것이 자못 마음에 들었던 것이다."]], [["\"Yes,--you have been friends!", "“그래—자네들은 벗이었지!"], ["She's diddled you out of your money, and that's the beginning and the end of it.", "그이가 자네 돈을 우려냈고, 그게 처음이자 끝이야."], ["And now, if you go on showing off your friendship, you'll be done out of more money.", "그런데 이제 와서 자네가 그 우정을 자랑삼아 내보이고 다니면 돈을 더 뜯길 걸세."], ["You're making an ass of yourself.", "자네는 스스로 얼간이 노릇을 하고 있어."], ["That's the long and the short of it.\"", "요컨대 그게 전부야.”"]], [["\"And what have you made of yourself with that girl?", "“그럼 자네는 그 아가씨한테 무슨 꼴이 되었는가?"], ["There are worse asses than I am yet, Master Johnny.\"", "나보다 더한 얼간이도 아직 세상에 있는 법이야, 조니 도련님.”"], ["Eames, as he had no answer ready to this counter attack, left the room and went downstairs.", "임스는 이 되받아치기에 마땅한 대답이 없었으므로 방을 나와 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["Cradell soon followed him, and in a few minutes they were all eating their dinner together at Mrs. Roper's hospitable table.", "크래들도 곧 뒤를 따랐고, 몇 분 뒤에 그들은 모두 로퍼 부인의 후한 식탁에 둘러앉아 함께 저녁을 먹고 있었다."]], [["Immediately after dinner Lupex took himself away, and the conversation upstairs became general on the subject of the lady's departure.", "저녁을 마치자마자 루펙스는 자리를 떴고, 위층에서는 그 부인의 가출을 두고 이야기가 두루 오갔다."]], [["\"If I was him I'd never ask a question about her, but let her go,\"", "“내가 그 사람이라면 그 여자에 대해서는 한마디도 묻지 않고 그냥 가게 두겠어요.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; and then have all her bills following you, wherever you went,\"", "“그래, 그러고는 가는 데마다 그 여자가 진 빚이 죄다 따라붙겠지.”"], ["said Amelia's brother.", "어밀리아의 오라비가 말했다."]], [["\"I'd sooner have her bills than herself,\"", "“나라면 그 부인보다는 그 부인이 진 빚 쪽을 택하겠는데요.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"My belief is, that she's been an ill-used woman,\"", "“제 생각에는 그이가 학대받아 온 여자예요.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."], ["\"If she had a husband that she could respect and have loved, and all that sort of thing, she would have been a charming woman.\"", "“존경할 수 있고 사랑할 수 있는 남편을, 뭐 그런 것을 두었더라면 그이는 매력 있는 여인이 되었을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"She's every bit as bad as he is,\"", "“그이도 그 사람 못지않게 나쁘지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Roper.", "로퍼 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I can't agree with you, Mrs. Roper,\"", "“그 말씀에는 동의할 수 없습니다, 로퍼 부인.”"], ["continued the lady's champion.", "그 부인의 투사가 말을 이었다."], ["\"Perhaps I ought to understand her position better than any one here, and--\"", "“아마 저는 여기 있는 누구보다도 그이의 처지를 잘 이해하고 있을 텐데, 그러니—”"]], [["\"Then that's just what you ought not to do, Mr. Cradell,\"", "“그러니까 바로 그게 크래들 씨가 하시면 안 될 일이라는 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Roper.", "로퍼 부인이 말했다."], ["And now the lady of the house spoke out her mind with much maternal dignity and with some feminine severity.", "그리고 이제 이 집의 안주인은 어머니다운 위엄을 잔뜩 담고, 얼마간은 여자다운 준엄함까지 담아 제 속을 털어놓았다."], ["\"That's just what a young man like you has no business to know.", "“그건 바로 댁 같은 젊은이가 알아서는 안 될 일이지요."], ["What's a married woman like that to you, or you to her; or what have you to do with understanding her position?", "그런 유부녀가 댁한테 무어고 댁이 그이한테 무업니까. 그이의 처지를 이해한다는 게 댁하고 무슨 상관이에요?"], ["When you've a wife of your own, if ever you do have one, you'll find you'll have trouble enough then without anybody else interfering with you.", "언젠가 댁도 아내를 두게 되면—두기라도 한다면 말이지만—다른 누가 끼어들지 않아도 그때 가서 겪을 골치가 넘칠 만큼 있다는 걸 알게 될 거예요."], ["Not but what I believe you're innocent as a lamb about Mrs.", "그렇다고 루펙스 부인 일로 댁이 어린양처럼 결백하다는 걸 내가 안 믿는다는 소리는 아니고요."], ["Lupex; that is, as far as any harm goes.", "몹쓸 짓을 했느냐 하는 데까지는 말이에요."], ["But you've got yourself into all this trouble by meddling, and was like enough to get yourself choked upstairs by that man.", "하지만 댁은 남의 일에 참견하다가 스스로 이 소동에 말려들었고, 위층에서 저 사람 손에 목이 졸릴 뻔했잖아요."], ["And who's to wonder when you go on pretending to be in love with a woman in that way, and she old enough to be your mother?", "그리고 저런 식으로 여자한테 반한 척하고 다니는데, 그 여자는 댁의 어머니뻘이나 되고요. 그러니 누가 이상하다 하지 않겠어요?"], ["What would your mamma say if she saw you at it?\"", "댁의 어머님이 그 꼴을 보시면 무어라 하시겠어요?”"]], [["\"Ha, ha, ha!\"", "“하하하!”"], ["laughed Cradell.", "크래들이 웃었다."]], [["\"It's all very well your laughing, but I hate such folly.", "“웃으셔도 좋지만, 나는 그런 어리석은 짓이 딱 질색이에요."], ["If I see a young man in love with a young woman, I respect him for it;\"", "젊은 남자가 젊은 여자를 사랑하는 것을 보면 나는 그 사람을 존경하지요.”"], ["and then she looked at Johnny Eames.", "그러고서 그녀는 조니 임스를 바라보았다."], ["\"I respect him for it,--even though he may now and then do things as he shouldn't.", "“존경하고말고요—이따금 하지 말아야 할 짓을 한다 해도요."], ["They most of 'em does that.", "사내들이란 대개 다 그러지요."], ["But to see a young man like you, Mr. Cradell, dangling after an old married woman, who doesn't know how to behave herself; and all just because she lets him to do it;--ugh!--an old broomstick with a petticoat on would do just as well!", "하지만 댁처럼 젊은 남자가, 크래들 씨, 제 몸가짐도 할 줄 모르는 늙은 유부녀 꽁무니를 졸졸 따라다니는 것을, 그것도 그저 그 여자가 그러도록 놔둔다는 까닭 하나로 그러는 것을 보면—어휴!—치마 입힌 낡은 빗자루라도 그만큼은 하겠어요!"], ["It makes me sick to see it, and that's the truth of it.", "그 꼴을 보면 속이 다 뒤집히는데, 그게 사실이에요."], ["I don't call it manly; and it ain't manly, is it, Miss Spruce?\"", "나는 그런 걸 사내답다고 하지 않아요. 사내답지 못하잖아요, 안 그래요, 스프루스 양?”"]], [["\"Of course I know nothing about it,\"", "“저야 물론 아무것도 모릅니다.”"], ["said the lady to whom the appeal was thus made.", "그렇게 물음을 받은 그 부인이 말했다."], ["\"But a young gentleman should keep himself to himself till the time comes for him to speak out,--begging your pardon all the same, Mr. Cradell.\"", "“하지만 젊은 신사분이라면 제 뜻을 밝힐 때가 올 때까지는 스스로를 지켜야 하는 법이지요—그래도 언짢게 여기지는 마세요, 크래들 씨.”"]], [["\"I don't see what a married woman should want with any one after her but her own husband,\"", "“유부녀가 제 남편 말고 누구를 꽁무니에 달고 다닐 까닭이 무언지 나는 모르겠어요.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"And perhaps not always that,\"", "“게다가 남편조차 늘 그럴 것은 없지요.”"], ["said John Eames.", "존 임스가 말했다."]], [["It was about an hour after this when the front-door bell was rung, and a scream from Jemima announced to them all that some critical moment had arrived.", "그로부터 한 시간쯤 지났을 때 대문 초인종이 울렸고, 제미마의 비명이 무언가 중대한 순간이 닥쳤음을 모두에게 알렸다."], ["Amelia, jumping up, opened the door, and then the rustle of a woman's dress was heard on the lower stairs.", "어밀리아가 벌떡 일어나 문을 열자 아래층 계단에서 여자 옷자락 스치는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["\"Oh, laws, ma'am, you have given us sich a turn,\"", "“어이구, 마님, 저희를 이렇게 놀래키시다니요.”"], ["said Jemima.", "제미마가 말했다."], ["\"We all thought you was run away.\"", "“저희는 다들 마님께서 달아나신 줄로만 알았습니다요.”"]], [["\"It's Mrs. Lupex,\"", "“루펙스 부인이에요.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."], ["And in two minutes more that ill-used lady was in the room.", "그리고 두어 분 뒤에 그 학대받았다는 부인이 방에 들어와 있었다."]], [["\"Well, my dears,\"", "“저런, 여러분.”"], ["said she, gaily, \"I hope nobody has waited dinner.\"", "그녀가 쾌활하게 말했다. “저녁을 안 드시고 기다리신 분은 없겠지요.”"]], [["\"No; we didn't wait dinner,\"", "“네, 기다리지 않았습니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Roper, very gravely.", "로퍼 부인이 아주 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["\"And where's my Orson?", "“그런데 내 오슨은 어디 있나요?"], ["Didn't he dine at home? Mr.", "집에서 저녁을 안 들었나요?"], ["Cradell, will you oblige me by taking my shawl?", "크래들 씨, 제 숄 좀 받아 주시겠어요? 아니, 그러지 않으시는 편이 낫겠네요."], ["But perhaps you had better not.", "사람들이 어찌나 말이 많은지요."], ["People are so censorious; ain't they, Miss Spruce?", "안 그래요, 스프루스 양? 임스 씨가 해 주시지요."], ["Mr. Eames shall do it; and everybody knows that that will be quite safe.", "그거야 아주 안전하다는 걸 다들 아시니까요."], ["Won't it, Miss Amelia?\"", "안 그래요, 어밀리아 양?”"]], [["\"Quite, I should think,\"", "“아주 그렇겠지요.”"], ["said Amelia. And Mrs.", "어밀리아가 말했다."], ["Lupex knew that she was not to look for an ally in that quarter on the present occasion.", "그리하여 루펙스 부인은 오늘 이 자리에서는 그쪽에서 제 편을 구할 수 없다는 것을 알았다."], ["Eames got up to take the shawl, and Mrs. Lupex went on.", "임스가 일어나 숄을 받아 들었고, 루펙스 부인은 말을 이었다."]], [["\"And didn't Orson dine at home?", "“그래서 오슨은 집에서 저녁을 안 들었나요?"], ["Perhaps they kept him down at the theatre.", "아마 극장에서 붙들어 두었나 보지요."], ["But I've been thinking all day what fun it would be when he thought his bird was flown.\"", "하지만 나는 하루 종일 생각했답니다. 그이가 제 새가 날아가 버렸다고 여길 때 얼마나 재미있을까 하고요.”"]], [["\"He did dine at home,\"", "“집에서 저녁을 들었습니다.”"], ["said Mrs.", "로퍼 부인이 말했다."], ["Roper; \"and he didn't seem to like it.", "“그리고 그리 좋아하는 것 같지도 않더군요."], ["There wasn't much fun, I can assure you.\"", "재미랄 것은 별로 없었다고 제가 장담하지요.”"]], [["\"Ah, wasn't there, though?", "“어머, 그랬어요, 정말요?"], ["I believe that man would like to have me tied to his button-hole.", "그이는 나를 제 단춧구멍에 매달아 두고 싶어 할 사람이지요."], ["I came across a few friends,--lady friends, Mr.", "나는 벗들을 몇 만났어요—여자 벗들이요, 크래들 씨."], ["Cradell, though two of them had their husbands; so we made a party, and just went down to Hampton Court.", "그중 둘은 남편을 데리고 있었고요. 그래서 우리끼리 무리를 지어 그냥 햄프턴 코트까지 다녀왔지요."], ["So my gentleman has gone again, has he?", "그래, 우리 나리께서는 또 나가 버리셨다고요?"], ["That's what I get for gadding about myself, isn't it, Miss Spruce?\"", "나 혼자 쏘다닌 값이 그거로군요, 안 그래요, 스프루스 양?”"]], [["Mrs. Roper, as she went to bed that night, made up her mind that, whatever might be the cost and trouble of doing so, she would lose no further time in getting rid of her married guests.", "로퍼 부인은 그날 밤 잠자리에 들면서, 값이 얼마가 들고 성가심이 얼마가 되든 이 부부 하숙인들을 떨쳐 내는 데 더는 시간을 끌지 않겠노라고 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLII. LILY'S BEDSIDE.", "# 제42장. 릴리의 머리맡"]], [["Lily Dale's constitution was good, and her recovery was retarded by no relapse or lingering debility; but, nevertheless, she was forced to keep her bed for many days after the fever had left her.", "릴리 데일은 체질이 튼튼했고, 회복이 재발이나 지지부진한 쇠약으로 더뎌지는 일도 없었다. 그런데도 열이 물러간 뒤로 여러 날 동안 그녀는 자리에 누워 지내야만 했다."], ["During all this period Dr.", "그동안 크로프츠 선생은 날마다 왔다."], ["Crofts came every day. It was in vain that Mrs.", "데일 부인이 그러지 말아 달라고 청한 것은 헛일이었다."], ["Dale begged him not to do so; telling him in simple words that she felt herself bound not to accept from him all this continuation of his unremunerated labours now that the absolute necessity for them was over.", "이제 그 수고가 반드시 필요한 고비는 지났으니 보수도 받지 않는 그 수고를 더 받을 수는 없다고 스스로 여긴다고, 부인은 꾸밈없는 말로 그에게 일렀다. 그는 자잘한 농담으로만 대답하거나 아예 대답하지 않았다."], ["He answered her only by little jokes, or did not answer her at all; but still he came daily, almost always at the same hour, just as the day was waning, so that he could sit for a quarter of an hour in the dusk, and then ride home to Guestwick in the dark.", "그러면서도 그는 날마다, 거의 언제나 같은 시각에, 해가 기울 무렵에 맞추어 왔으니, 그래야 어스름 속에 십오 분쯤 앉아 있다가 어두워진 뒤에 말을 타고 게스트윅으로 돌아갈 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["At this time Bell had been admitted into her sister's room, and she would always meet Dr.", "이 무렵 벨은 동생의 방에 드나들 수 있게 되었고, 크로프츠 선생을 만날 때면 늘 릴리의 머리맡에서 만났다."], ["Crofts at Lily's bedside; but she never sat with him alone, since the day on which he had offered her his love with half-articulated words, and she had declined it with words also half-articulated.", "그러나 그가 반쯤만 입 밖에 낸 말로 그녀에게 사랑을 건네고 그녀 또한 반쯤만 입 밖에 낸 말로 그것을 물리친 그날 이후로, 그녀는 그와 단둘이 앉아 있는 일이 없었다."], ["She had seen him alone since that, on the stairs, or standing in the hall, but she had not remained with him, talking to him after her old fashion, and no further word of his love had been spoken in speech either half or wholly articulate.", "그 뒤로도 계단에서, 혹은 현관에 서서 그를 혼자 만난 적은 있었으나 예전처럼 그와 남아 이야기를 나누지는 않았고, 그의 사랑에 관한 말은 반이든 온전히든 더는 입 밖에 나오지 않았다."]], [["Nor had Bell spoken of what had passed to any one else.", "벨은 지나간 일을 다른 누구에게도 말하지 않았다."], ["Lily would probably have told both her mother and sister instantly; but then no such scene as that which had taken place with Bell would have been possible with Lily.", "릴리였다면 아마 어머니에게도 언니에게도 그 자리에서 다 털어놓았을 것이다. 그러나 벨에게 벌어진 것과 같은 장면은 릴리에게는 애초에 있을 수 없었을 것이다."], ["In whatever way the matter might have gone with her, there would certainly have been some clear tale to tell when the interview was over.", "그 일이 그녀에게 어떤 식으로 흘러갔든, 만남이 끝났을 때에는 분명 또렷하게 들려줄 이야기가 남았을 것이다."], ["She would have known whether or no she loved the man, or could love him, and would have given him some true and intelligible answer.", "그녀라면 자기가 그 사람을 사랑하는지, 사랑할 수 있는지 알았을 것이고, 참되고 알아들을 만한 대답을 그에게 주었을 것이다."], ["Bell had not done so, but had given him an answer which, if true, was not intelligible, and if intelligible was not true.", "벨은 그러지 못했다. 그녀가 준 대답은 참이라면 알아들을 수 없는 것이었고, 알아들을 만하다면 참이 아니었다."], ["And yet, when she had gone away to think over what had passed, she had been happy and satisfied, and almost triumphant.", "그런데도 지나간 일을 곱씹으려 물러났을 때, 그녀는 행복하고 흡족했으며 거의 의기양양하기까지 했다."], ["She had never yet asked herself whether she expected anything further from Dr. Crofts, nor what that something further might be,--and yet she was happy!", "그녀는 크로프츠 선생에게서 무언가를 더 기대하는지, 그 무언가가 대체 무엇인지 아직 한 번도 자신에게 물어본 적이 없었다—그런데도 그녀는 행복했다!"]], [["Lily had now become pert and saucy in her bed, taking upon herself the little airs which are allowed to a convalescent invalid as compensation for previous suffering and restraint.", "릴리는 이제 침대에서 깜찍하고 당돌해져서, 앞서 겪은 고통과 속박에 대한 보상으로 회복기 환자에게 허락되는 자잘한 응석을 부렸다."], ["She pretended to much anxiety on the subject of her dinner, and declared that she would go out on such or such a day, let Dr.", "그녀는 제 저녁상을 두고 몹시 안달하는 척했고, 크로프츠 선생이 아무리 제멋대로 굴어도 며칠 며칠에는 밖에 나가겠노라고 선언했다."], ["Crofts be as imperious as he might. \"He's an old savage, after all,\"", "“그 사람, 알고 보면 늙은 야만인이야.”"], ["she said to her sister, one evening, after he was gone, \"and just as bad as the rest of them.\"", "어느 날 저녁 그가 돌아간 뒤에 그녀가 언니에게 말했다. “나머지 사람들이랑 똑같이 못됐어.”"]], [["\"I do not know who the rest of them are,\"", "“나머지 사람들이 누구를 말하는 건지는 모르겠구나.”"], ["said Bell, \"but at any rate he's not very old.\"", "벨이 말했다. “하지만 어쨌든 그분이 늙지는 않았잖니.”"]], [["\"You know what I mean.", "“내 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알잖아."], ["He's just as grumpy as Dr. Gruffen, and thinks everybody is to do what he tells them.", "그 사람은 그러펀 선생만큼이나 무뚝뚝하고, 누구든 자기가 시키는 대로 해야 한다고 생각해."], ["Of course, you take his part.\"", "물론 언니는 그 사람 편을 들겠지.”"]], [["\"And of course you ought, seeing how good he has been.\"", "“너도 마땅히 그래야지, 그분이 얼마나 잘해 주셨는지 생각하면.”"]], [["\"And of course I should, to anybody but you.", "“언니만 아니면 누구한테든 그럴 거야."], ["I do like to abuse him to you.\"", "나는 언니한테 그 사람 흉보는 게 좋거든.”"]], [["\"Lily, Lily!\"", "“릴리, 릴리!”"]], [["\"So I do.", "“정말 그렇다니까."], ["It's so hard to knock any fire out of you, that when one does find the place where the flint lies, one can't help hammering at it.", "언니한테서 불꽃 하나 튀기기가 어찌나 어려운지, 부싯돌이 박힌 자리를 한번 찾아내고 나면 두드려 대지 않을 수가 없어."], ["What did he mean by saying that I shouldn't get up on Sunday?", "내가 일요일에 일어나면 안 된다니, 그게 무슨 소리야?"], ["Of course I shall get up if I like it.\"", "나는 내키면 일어날 거야.”"]], [["\"Not if mamma asks you not?\"", "“엄마가 그러지 말라고 하셔도?”"]], [["\"Oh, but she won't, unless he interferes and dictates to her.", "“아, 하지만 엄마는 그러지 않으실걸. 그 사람이 참견해서 엄마한테 이래라저래라 하지만 않는다면 말이야."], ["Oh, Bell, what a tyrant he would be if he were married!\"", "아, 언니, 그 사람이 결혼하면 얼마나 폭군일까!”"]], [["\"Would he?\"", "“그럴까?”"]], [["\"And how submissive you would be, if you were his wife!", "“그리고 언니가 그 사람 아내라면 얼마나 고분고분할까!"], ["It's a thousand pities that you are not in love with each other;--that is, if you are not.\"", "두 사람이 서로 사랑하지 않는다는 건 천만번 아까운 일이야—그러니까, 사랑하지 않는다면 말이지.”"]], [["\"Lily, I thought that there was a promise between us about that.\"", "“릴리, 그 얘기는 우리 사이에 약속이 있었던 걸로 아는데.”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["but that was in other days.", "하지만 그건 다른 날들의 일이잖아."], ["Things are all altered since that promise was given,--all the world has been altered.\"", "그 약속을 한 뒤로 모든 게 달라졌어—온 세상이 달라졌는걸.”"], ["And as she said this the tone of her voice was changed, and it had become almost sad.", "그렇게 말하는 그녀의 목소리는 가락이 바뀌어 거의 슬프게 들렸다."], ["\"I feel as though I ought to be allowed now to speak about anything I please.\"", "“나는 이제 무엇이든 내가 말하고 싶은 것을 말해도 되게끔 허락받아야 할 것 같은데.”"]], [["\"You shall, if it pleases you, my pet.\"", "“네가 그러고 싶다면 그렇게 하렴, 우리 릴리.”"]], [["\"You see how it is, Bell; I can never again have anything of my own to talk about.\"", "“언니도 알잖아. 나한테는 이제 다시는 내 것이라고 이야기할 만한 게 없어.”"]], [["\"Oh, my darling, do not say that.\"", "“아, 얘, 그런 말 하지 마.”"]], [["\"But it is so, Bell; and why not say it?", "“하지만 사실인걸, 언니. 그런데 왜 말하면 안 돼?"], ["Do you think I never say it to myself in the hours when I am all alone, thinking over it--thinking, thinking, thinking.", "혼자 있을 때, 그 일을 곱씹고—곱씹고, 곱씹고, 또 곱씹는 시간에 내가 그 말을 스스로에게 한 번도 안 한다고 생각해?"], ["You must not,--you must not grudge to let me talk of it sometimes.\"", "언니는—언니는 내가 가끔 그 얘기를 하는 것쯤은 아까워하면 안 돼.”"]], [["\"I will not grudge you anything;--only I cannot believe that it must be so always.\"", "“나는 너한테 아무것도 아까워하지 않아—다만 언제까지나 그래야만 한다고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없어서 그래.”"]], [["\"Ask yourself, Bell, how it would be with you.", "“언니 자신한테 물어봐, 언니라면 어떨지."], ["But I sometimes fancy that you measure me differently from yourself.\"", "그런데 나는 가끔 언니가 나를 언니 자신과는 다른 잣대로 재는 것 같다는 생각이 들어.”"]], [["\"Indeed I do, for I know how much better you are.\"", "“정말 그래. 네가 나보다 얼마나 나은 사람인지 아니까.”"]], [["\"I am not so much better as to be ever able to forget all that.", "“나는 그 모든 걸 잊을 수 있을 만큼 그렇게 나은 사람이 아니야."], ["I know I never shall do so.", "나는 결코 잊지 못할 거야."], ["I have made up my mind about it clearly and with an absolute certainty.\"", "그건 또렷하게, 조금도 의심 없이 마음을 정해 두었어.”"]], [["\"Lily, Lily, Lily!", "“릴리, 릴리, 릴리!"], ["pray do not say so.\"", "제발 그런 말 하지 마.”"]], [["\"But I do say it.", "“하지만 나는 말할 거야."], ["And yet I have not been very mopish and melancholy; have I, Bell?", "그래도 나는 그렇게 시무룩하고 우울하게 굴지는 않았잖아, 그렇지 않아, 언니?"], ["I do think I deserve some little credit, and yet, I declare, you won't allow me the least privilege in the world.\"", "나도 조금은 칭찬을 받을 만하다고 생각하는데, 정말이지 언니는 나한테 세상에서 제일 작은 특권 하나도 주지 않으려고 해.”"]], [["\"What privilege would you wish me to give you?\"", "“어떤 특권을 주면 좋겠는데?”"]], [["\"To talk about Dr. Crofts.\"", "“크로프츠 선생님 이야기를 하는 거.”"]], [["\"Lily, you are a wicked, wicked tyrant.\"", "“릴리, 너는 고약하고 고약한 폭군이야.”"], ["And Bell leaned over her, and fell upon her, and kissed her, hiding her own face in the gloom of the evening.", "그러고서 벨은 동생 위로 몸을 굽혀 그녀에게 엎드리며 입을 맞추었고, 저녁의 어스름 속에 제 얼굴을 감추었다."], ["After that it came to be an accepted understanding between them that Bell was not altogether indifferent to Dr. Crofts.", "그 뒤로 벨이 크로프츠 선생에게 아주 무심하지만은 않다는 것이 두 사람 사이에 받아들여진 이야기가 되었다."]], [["\"You heard what he said, my darling,\" Mrs.", "“그이가 뭐라고 했는지 들었지, 얘야.”"], ["Dale said the next day, as the three were in the room together after Dr. Crofts was gone.", "이튿날 크로프츠 선생이 돌아간 뒤 세 사람이 함께 방에 있을 때 데일 부인이 말했다."], ["Mrs. Dale was standing on one side of the bed, and Bell on the other, while Lily was scolding them both.", "데일 부인은 침대 한쪽에, 벨은 다른 쪽에 서 있었고, 릴리는 두 사람 모두를 나무라고 있는 참이었다."], ["\"You can get up for an hour or two to-morrow, but he thinks you had better not go out of the room.\"", "“내일 한두 시간쯤 일어나 있어도 되지만, 방 밖으로는 나가지 않는 편이 좋겠다더구나.”"]], [["\"What would be the good of that, mamma?", "“그게 무슨 소용이에요, 엄마?"], ["I am so tired of looking always at the same paper.", "늘 같은 벽지만 쳐다보고 있으려니 너무 지겨워요."], ["It is such a tiresome paper.", "정말 지긋지긋한 벽지예요."], ["It makes one count the pattern over and over again.", "자꾸만 무늬를 세고 또 세게 만들잖아요."], ["I wonder how you ever can live here.\"", "엄마는 어떻게 여기서 사시는지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["\"I've got used to it, you see.\"", "“나는 익숙해졌으니 그렇지.”"]], [["\"I never can get used to that sort of thing; but go on counting, and counting, and counting.", "“나는 그런 것에 도무지 익숙해질 수가 없어요. 그저 세고, 세고, 또 세게 될 뿐이죠."], ["I'll tell you what I should like; and I'm sure it would be the best thing, too.\"", "내가 뭘 하고 싶은지 말씀드릴게요. 그리고 그게 제일 좋은 방법이기도 할 거예요.”"]], [["\"And what would you like?\"", "“뭘 하고 싶은데?”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Just to get up at nine o'clock to-morrow, and go to church as though nothing had happened.", "“내일 아홉 시에 일어나서 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 교회에 가는 거야."], ["Then, when Dr. Crofts came in the evening, you would tell him I was down at the school.\"", "그러고서 저녁에 크로프츠 선생님이 오시면 내가 학교에 내려가 있다고 말해 주는 거지.”"]], [["\"I wouldn't quite advise that,\"", "“그건 그다지 권하고 싶지 않구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"It would give him such a delightful start.", "“그러면 그이가 얼마나 근사하게 놀라겠어요."], ["And when he found I didn't die immediately, as of course I ought to do according to rule, he would be so disgusted.\"", "그리고 규칙대로라면 마땅히 그래야 할 텐데도 내가 당장 죽지 않는 걸 알면 얼마나 김이 빠지겠어요.”"]], [["\"It would be very ungrateful, to say the least of it,\"", "“아무리 좋게 말해도 배은망덕한 짓이지.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"No, it wouldn't, a bit.", "“아니야, 조금도 그렇지 않아."], ["He needn't come, unless he likes it.", "오기 싫으면 안 와도 되잖아."], ["And I don't believe he comes to see me at all.", "그리고 나는 그이가 나를 보러 온다고는 조금도 믿지 않아."], ["It's all very well, mamma, your looking in that way; but I'm sure it's true.", "엄마, 그렇게 쳐다보셔도 소용없어요. 하지만 그게 사실인걸요."], ["And I'll tell you what I'll do, I'll pretend to be bad again, otherwise the poor man will be robbed of his only happiness.\"", "그리고 내가 어떻게 할 건지 말씀드릴까요, 나는 다시 아픈 척할 거예요. 안 그러면 그 딱한 양반이 하나뿐인 낙을 빼앗기고 말 테니까요.”"]], [["\"I suppose we must allow her to say what she likes till she gets well,\"", "“다 나을 때까지는 저 애가 하고 싶은 말을 다 하게 두는 수밖에 없겠구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale, laughing.", "데일 부인이 웃으며 말했다."], ["It was now nearly dark, and Mrs. Dale did not see that Bell's hand had crept under the bed-clothes, and taken hold of that of her sister.", "이제 거의 어두워져 있었으므로, 데일 부인은 벨의 손이 이불 밑으로 기어들어 동생의 손을 잡은 것을 보지 못했다."], ["\"It's true, mamma,\"", "“사실이에요, 엄마.”"], ["continued Lily, \"and I defy her to deny it.", "릴리가 말을 이었다. “언니가 아니라고 해 볼 테면 해 보라죠."], ["I would forgive him for keeping me in bed if he would only make her fall in love with him.\"", "그이가 언니를 자기한테 반하게 만들어 주기만 한다면 나를 침대에 붙들어 둔 것쯤은 용서해 줄 텐데.”"]], [["\"She has made a bargain, mamma,\"", "“쟤가 흥정을 해 두었어요, 엄마.”"], ["said Bell, \"that she is to say whatever she likes till she gets well.\"", "벨이 말했다. “다 나을 때까지는 하고 싶은 말을 다 하기로요.”"]], [["\"I am to say whatever I like always; that was the bargain, and I mean to stand to it.\"", "“나는 언제나 하고 싶은 말을 다 하기로 했어. 그게 흥정이었고, 나는 그걸 끝까지 지킬 거야.”"]], [["On the following Sunday Lily did get up, but did not leave her mother's bedroom.", "다음 일요일에 릴리는 정말로 일어났으나 어머니의 침실을 벗어나지는 않았다."], ["There she was, seated in that half-dignified and half-luxurious state which belongs to the first getting up of an invalid, when Dr. Crofts called.", "크로프츠 선생이 들렀을 때 그녀는 환자가 처음으로 자리에서 일어났을 때의 그 반쯤은 위엄 있고 반쯤은 호사스러운 상태로 거기 앉아 있었다."], ["There she had eaten her tiny bit of roast mutton, and had called her mother a stingy old creature, because she would not permit another morsel; and there she had drunk her half glass of port wine, pretending that it was very bad, and twice worse than the doctor's physic; and there, Sunday though it was, she had fully enjoyed the last hour of daylight, reading that exquisite new novel which had just completed itself, amidst the jarring criticisms of the youth and age of the reading public.", "거기서 그녀는 양고기 구이를 아주 조금 먹고는 한 점도 더 주지 않는다고 어머니를 인색한 늙은이라 불렀고, 거기서 포트와인 반 잔을 마시며 맛이 아주 고약하다고, 의사의 물약보다 곱절은 더 못하다고 우겨 댔다. 그리고 거기서, 일요일이었건만, 독서 대중의 젊은 층과 나이 든 층이 서로 어긋나는 비평을 쏟아 내는 가운데 막 완결된 그 절묘한 새 소설을 읽으며 해가 남은 마지막 한 시간을 마음껏 누렸다."]], [["\"I am quite sure she was right in accepting him, Bell,\"", "“그 여자가 그 사람을 받아들인 건 정말 잘한 일이야, 언니.”"], ["she said, putting down the book as the light was fading, and beginning to praise the story.", "빛이 스러지자 그녀가 책을 내려놓고 그 이야기를 칭찬하기 시작하며 말했다."]], [["\"It was a matter of course,\"", "“그야 당연한 일이지.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"It always is right in the novels.", "“소설에서는 언제나 그게 옳은 일이니까."], ["That's why I don't like them.", "그래서 나는 소설이 싫어."], ["They are too sweet.\"", "너무 달아.”"]], [["\"That's why I do like them, because they are so sweet.", "“나는 그래서 좋아, 너무 다니까."], ["A sermon is not to tell you what you are, but what you ought to be, and a novel should tell you not what you are to get, but what you'd like to get.\"", "설교는 네가 어떤 사람인지가 아니라 어떤 사람이 되어야 하는지를 일러 주는 것이고, 소설은 네가 무엇을 얻게 될지가 아니라 무엇을 얻고 싶은지를 일러 주어야 하는 거야.”"]], [["\"If so, then, I'd go back to the old school, and have the heroine really a heroine, walking all the way up from Edinburgh to London, and falling among thieves; or else nursing a wounded hero, and describing the battle from the window.", "“그렇다면 나는 옛 유파로 돌아가서 여주인공을 정말 여주인공답게 만들겠어. 에든버러에서 런던까지 내내 걸어 올라가다가 도둑 떼를 만나기도 하고, 아니면 부상당한 영웅을 간호하면서 창문 너머로 전투를 그려 보이기도 하는 여주인공 말이야."], ["We've got tired of that; or else the people who write can't do it now-a-days.", "우리는 그런 것에 싫증이 났지. 아니면 요즘 글 쓰는 사람들이 그런 걸 못 쓰게 된 거든지."], ["But if we are to have real life, let it be real.\"", "하지만 우리가 진짜 삶을 담을 거라면, 진짜로 담아야지.”"]], [["\"No, Bell, no!\" said Lily.", "“아니야, 언니, 아니야!”"], ["\"Real life sometimes is so painful.\"", "릴리가 말했다. “진짜 삶은 때로 너무 아프잖아.”"], ["Then her sister, in a moment, was down on the floor at her feet, kissing her hand and caressing her knees, and praying that the wound might be healed.", "그러자 언니는 이내 동생의 발치 바닥에 내려앉아 그 손에 입 맞추고 무릎을 어루만지며, 그 상처가 아물게 해 달라고 빌었다."]], [["On that morning Lily had succeeded in inducing her sister to tell her all that had been said by Dr.", "그날 아침 릴리는 크로프츠 선생이 한 말을 남김없이 들려 달라고 언니를 구슬려 내는 데 성공했었다."], ["Crofts. All that had been said by herself also, Bell had intended to tell; but when she came to this part of the story, her account was very lame.", "벨은 자기가 한 말도 다 들려줄 셈이었으나, 이야기가 그 대목에 이르자 그녀의 설명은 몹시 궁색해졌다."], ["\"I don't think I said anything,\"", "“나는 아무 말도 안 한 것 같아.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"But silence always gives consent.", "“하지만 침묵은 언제나 승낙이야."], ["He'll know that,\"", "그이도 그렇게 알 거야.”"], ["Lily had rejoined.", "릴리가 되받았다."], ["\"No, he will not; my silence didn't give any consent; I'm sure of that.", "“아니야, 그이는 그렇게 알지 않아. 내 침묵은 아무 승낙도 하지 않았어."], ["And he didn't think that it did.\"", "그건 내가 확실해. 그리고 그이도 그렇게 여기지 않았고.”"], ["\"But you didn't mean to refuse him?\"", "“하지만 언니가 그이를 거절하려던 건 아니었잖아?”"], ["\"I think I did.", "“거절하려던 것 같아."], ["I don't think I knew what I meant; and it was safer, therefore, to look no, than to look yes.", "내가 무슨 마음이었는지도 모르겠어. 그러니 그렇다는 낯빛보다 아니라는 낯빛을 하는 편이 더 안전했지."], ["If I didn't say it, I'm sure I looked it.\"", "말로 하지 않았다 해도 낯빛으로는 분명 그랬을 거야.”"], ["\"But you wouldn't refuse him now?\"", "“하지만 지금이라면 거절하지 않을 거지?”"], ["asked Lily.", "릴리가 물었다."], ["\"I don't know,\"", "“모르겠어.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"It seems as though I should want years to make up my mind; and he won't ask me again.\"", "“마음을 정하려면 몇 해는 걸릴 것 같은데, 그이는 다시 묻지 않을 테니까.”"]], [["Bell was still at her sister's feet, caressing them, and praying with all her heart that that wound might be healed in due time, when Mrs. Dale came in and announced the doctor's daily visit.", "벨은 여전히 동생의 발치에서 그 발을 어루만지며 그 상처가 때가 되면 아물기를 온 마음으로 빌고 있었는데, 그때 데일 부인이 들어와 의사가 날마다 오는 왕진을 왔다고 알렸다."], ["\"Then I'll go,\"", "“그럼 나는 나갈게.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Indeed you won't,\"", "“그러면 안 되지.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"He's coming simply to make a morning call, and nobody need run away. Now, Dr.", "“그이는 그냥 아침 문안을 오는 것뿐인데 아무도 달아날 것 없어."], ["Crofts, you need not come and stand over me with your watch, for I won't let you touch my hand except to shake hands with me;\"", "자, 크로프츠 선생님, 시계를 들고 제 앞에 서 계실 것 없어요. 악수하는 것 말고는 제 손에 손대지 못하게 할 테니까요.”"], ["and then she held her hand out to him.", "그러고서 그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["\"And all you'll know of my tongue you'll learn from the sound.\"", "“그리고 제 혀에 관해 아실 건 소리로 들으시면 돼요.”"]], [["\"I don't care in the least for your tongue.\"", "“나는 아가씨 혀에는 조금도 관심이 없어요.”"]], [["\"I dare say not, and yet you may some of these days.", "“그러시겠죠. 그래도 언젠가는 관심이 생기실걸요."], ["I can speak out, if I like it; can't I, mamma?\"", "나는 마음만 먹으면 할 말을 다 할 수 있어요, 그렇죠, 엄마?”"]], [["\"I should think Dr. Crofts knows that by this time, my dear.\"", "“크로프츠 선생님도 이젠 그쯤은 아실 게다, 얘야.”"]], [["\"I don't know.", "“모르겠어요."], ["There are some things gentlemen are very slow to learn. But you must sit down, Dr.", "신사분들이 배우는 데 아주 굼뜬 일들이 있거든요."], ["Crofts, and make yourself comfortable and polite; for you must understand that you are not master here any longer.", "어쨌든 앉으세요, 크로프츠 선생님, 편안하고 점잖게 계세요. 이제 여기서 선생님이 더는 주인이 아니라는 걸 아셔야 하니까요."], ["I am out of bed now, and your reign is over.\"", "나는 이제 자리에서 일어났고, 선생님의 치세는 끝났어요.”"]], [["\"That's the gratitude of the world, all through,\"", "“세상의 은혜 갚음이란 게 죄 저렇답니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Who is ever grateful to a doctor?", "“의사한테 고마워하는 사람이 어디 있어요?"], ["He only cures you that he may triumph over some other doctor, and declare, as he goes by Dr.", "의사는 다른 의사를 이기려고 사람을 고쳐 줄 뿐이에요."], ["Gruffen's door, 'There, had she called you in, she'd have been dead before now; or else would have been ill for twelve months.'", "그러고는 그러펀 선생 댁 문 앞을 지나면서 이렇게 말하는 거죠."], ["Don't you jump for joy when Dr. Gruffen's patients die?\"", "‘저것 보라지, 저 아가씨가 당신을 불렀더라면 벌써 죽었거나 아니면 열두 달은 앓아누웠을 거야.’ 그러펀 선생 환자가 죽으면 좋아서 껑충껑충 뛰지 않으세요?”"]], [["\"Of course I do--out in the market-place, so that everybody shall see me,\"", "“물론 뜁니다—장터 한복판에서, 누구나 다 볼 수 있게 말이지요.”"], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["\"Lily, how can you say such shocking things?\"", "“릴리, 어쩌면 그런 끔찍한 소리를 하니?”"], ["said her sister.", "언니가 말했다."]], [["Then the doctor did sit down, and they were all very cosy together over the fire, talking about things which were not medical, or only half medical in their appliance.", "그러고서 의사는 정말로 자리에 앉았고, 그들은 다 함께 난롯가에서 아주 아늑하게, 의술과는 상관없거나 쓰임새로만 반쯤 의술에 걸치는 이야기들을 나누었다."], ["By degrees the conversation came round to Mrs. Eames and to John Eames.", "이야기는 차츰 임스 부인과 존 임스에게로 돌아갔다."], ["Two or three days since, Crofts had told Mrs. Dale of that affair at the railway station, of which up to that time she had heard nothing.", "이삼일 전에 크로프츠는 철도역에서 있었던 그 일을 데일 부인에게 이야기해 주었는데, 그때까지 부인은 그 일을 전혀 듣지 못하고 있었다."], ["Mrs. Dale, when she was assured that young Eames had given Crosbie a tremendous thrashing--the tidings of the affair which had got themselves substantiated at Guestwick so described the nature of the encounter--could not withhold some meed of applause.", "젊은 임스가 크로즈비를 엄청나게 두들겨 팼다는 말을—게스트윅에서 사실로 굳어진 그 사건의 소문이 그 다툼의 성질을 그렇게 전하고 있었다—확실히 듣고서, 데일 부인은 얼마간의 갈채를 참아 낼 수가 없었다."]], [["\"Dear boy!\"", "“착한 아이!”"], ["she said, almost involuntarily.", "부인은 저도 모르게 말했다. “착한 아이 같으니!"], ["\"Dear boy! it came from the honestness of his heart!\"", "그건 그 아이의 정직한 마음에서 나온 거예요!”"], ["And then she gave special injunctions to the doctor--injunctions which were surely unnecessary--that no word of the matter should be whispered before Lily.", "그러고서 부인은 굳이 할 필요도 없었을 당부를 의사에게 각별히 해 두었으니, 릴리 앞에서는 그 일을 한마디도 소곤거리지 말라는 것이었다."]], [["\"I was at the manor, yesterday,\"", "“어제 저택에 갔었는데요.”"], ["said the doctor, \"and the earl would talk about nothing but Master Johnny.", "의사가 말했다. “백작님이 조니 도련님 이야기밖에는 하시지 않더군요."], ["He says he's the finest fellow going.\" Whereupon Mrs.", "세상에 그만한 젊은이가 없다고 하시던걸요.”"], ["Dale touched him with her foot, fearing that the conversation might be led away in the direction of Johnny's prowess.", "그러자 데일 부인이 발로 그를 툭 건드렸으니, 이야기가 조니의 무용담 쪽으로 끌려갈까 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["\"I am so glad,\"", "“정말 기뻐요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"I always knew that they'd find John out at last.\"", "“나는 사람들이 언젠가는 존을 알아볼 줄 알고 있었어요.”"]], [["\"And Lady Julia is just as fond of him,\"", "“레이디 줄리아도 그이를 꼭 그만큼 아끼신답니다.”"], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["\"Dear me!\"", "“어머나!”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"Suppose they were to make up a match!\"", "“두 분이 짝을 이루기라도 하면 어쩌죠!”"]], [["\"Lily, how can you be so absurd?\"", "“릴리, 어쩌면 그렇게 엉뚱하니?”"]], [["\"Let me see; what relation would he be to us?", "“어디 보자. 그이는 우리한테 무슨 촌수가 될까?"], ["He would certainly be Bernard's uncle, and uncle Christopher's half brother-in-law.", "버나드한테는 분명 이모부가 될 테고, 크리스토퍼 삼촌한테는 반쪽짜리 동서가 되겠지."], ["Wouldn't it be odd?\"", "이상하지 않겠어?”"]], [["\"It would rather,\"", "“좀 그렇긴 하겠구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I hope he'll be civil to Bernard.", "“그이가 버나드한테 잘해 주면 좋겠어요."], ["Don't you, Bell?", "언니도 그렇지?"], ["Is he to give up the Income-tax Office, Dr. Crofts?\"", "그이가 소득세 사무국을 그만두게 되나요, 크로프츠 선생님?”"]], [["\"I didn't hear that that was settled yet.\"", "“아직 그렇게 정해졌다는 말은 못 들었어요.”"], ["And so they went on talking about John Eames.", "그렇게 그들은 존 임스 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["\"Joking apart,\"", "“농담은 접어 두고요.”"], ["said Lily, \"I am very glad that Lord De Guest has taken him by the hand.", "릴리가 말했다. “나는 드 게스트 백작님이 그이의 손을 잡아 주신 게 정말 기뻐요."], ["Not that I think an earl is better than anybody else, but because it shows that people are beginning to understand that he has got something in him.", "백작이 다른 누구보다 낫다고 생각해서가 아니라, 사람들이 그이에게 무언가 들어 있다는 걸 알아보기 시작했다는 뜻이니까요."], ["I always said that they who laughed at John would see him hold up his head yet.\"", "나는 늘 말했어요, 존을 비웃던 사람들이 언젠가는 그이가 고개를 들고 있는 걸 보게 되리라고.”"], ["All which words sank deep into Mrs. Dale's mind.", "이 말들은 죄다 데일 부인의 마음속 깊이 가라앉았다."], ["If only, in some coming time, her pet might be taught to love this new young hero!", "다가올 어느 날, 제 귀한 딸이 이 새로운 젊은 영웅을 사랑하도록 이끌릴 수만 있다면!"], ["But then would not that last heroic deed of his militate most strongly against any possibility of such love!", "그러나 바로 그 마지막 영웅다운 행동이야말로 그런 사랑의 가능성을 무엇보다 세차게 가로막지 않겠는가!"]], [["\"And now I may as well be going,\"", "“이제 그만 가 봐야겠습니다.”"], ["said the doctor, rising from his chair.", "의사가 의자에서 일어나며 말했다."], ["At this time Bell had left the room, but Mrs. Dale was still there.", "이때 벨은 방을 나가고 없었으나 데일 부인은 아직 거기 있었다."]], [["\"You need not be in such a hurry, especially this evening,\"", "“그렇게 서두르실 것 없어요, 오늘 저녁만은요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Why especially this evening?\"", "“오늘 저녁만은 왜죠?”"]], [["\"Because it will be the last. Sit down again, Dr.", "“오늘이 마지막일 테니까요."], ["Crofts. I've got a little speech to make to you.", "다시 앉으세요, 크로프츠 선생님."], ["I've been preparing it all the morning, and you must give me an opportunity of speaking it.\"", "선생님께 드릴 짧은 인사말이 있어요. 아침 내내 준비했으니 말할 기회를 주셔야죠.”"]], [["\"I'll come the day after to-morrow, and I'll hear it then.\"", "“모레 다시 올 테니 그때 듣지요.”"]], [["\"But I choose, sir, that you should hear it now.", "“하지만 저는 지금 들으셨으면 좋겠어요, 선생님."], ["Am I not to be obeyed when I first get up on to my own throne?", "내가 처음으로 내 옥좌에 올랐는데 내 말을 따라 주셔야 하는 것 아닌가요?"], ["Dear, dear Dr. Crofts, how am I to thank you for all that you have done?\"", "다정하고 다정하신 크로프츠 선생님, 선생님이 해 주신 그 모든 일에 제가 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할까요?”"]], [["\"How are any of us to thank him?\"", "“우리 가운데 누가 저분께 제대로 감사할 수 있겠니?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I hate thanks,\" said the doctor.", "“저는 감사 인사가 질색입니다.”"], ["\"One kind glance of the eye is worth them all, and I've had many such in this house.\"", "의사가 말했다. “다정한 눈길 한 번이 그 모든 것보다 값지고, 이 집에서는 그런 눈길을 많이 받았으니까요.”"]], [["\"You have our hearts' love, at any rate,\"", "“어쨌든 저희 마음속 사랑은 선생님 것이랍니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"God bless you all!\"", "“모두에게 하느님의 축복이 있기를!”"], ["said he, as he prepared to go.", "그가 떠날 채비를 하며 말했다."]], [["\"But I haven't made my speech yet,\" said Lily.", "“하지만 저는 아직 인사말을 하지 않았어요.”"], ["\"And to tell the truth, mamma, you must go away, or I shall never be able to make it.", "릴리가 말했다. “그리고 사실대로 말하면, 엄마, 엄마가 나가 주셔야 해요."], ["It's very improper, is it not, turning you out, but it shall only take three minutes.\"", "안 그러면 저는 도저히 못 할 거예요. 이렇게 엄마를 내쫓다니 아주 무례하죠?"], ["Then Mrs.", "하지만 삼 분이면 돼요.”"], ["Dale, with some little joking word, left the room; but, as she left it, her mind was hardly at ease.", "그러자 데일 부인은 무어라 자잘한 농담을 던지며 방을 나갔다. 그러나 나가면서도 마음이 썩 편치는 않았다."], ["Ought she to have gone, leaving it to Lily's discretion to say what words she might think fit to Dr. Crofts?", "릴리가 크로프츠 선생에게 어떤 말을 하는 것이 좋을지 그 재량에 맡겨 두고 자리를 비켜 준 것이 옳았을까?"], ["Hitherto she had never doubted her daughters--not even their discretion; and therefore it had been natural to her to go when she was bidden.", "여태 부인은 딸들을 의심해 본 적이 없었다—그 분별력조차 의심한 적이 없었다. 그러므로 나가 달라는 말을 들었을 때 나가는 것이 그녀에게는 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["But as she went downstairs she had her doubts whether she was right or no.", "그러나 아래층으로 내려가면서 부인은 자기가 옳았는지 아닌지 의심스러웠다."]], [["\"Dr. Crofts,\"", "“크로프츠 선생님.”"], ["said Lily, as soon as they were alone.", "둘만 남자마자 릴리가 말했다."], ["\"Sit down there, close to me.", "“여기, 제 곁에 가까이 앉으세요."], ["I want to ask you a question.", "여쭤볼 게 있어요."], ["What was it you said to Bell when you were alone with her the other evening in the parlour?\"", "요전 저녁에 응접실에서 언니와 단둘이 계실 때 언니에게 무슨 말씀을 하셨어요?”"]], [["The doctor sat for a moment without answering, and Lily, who was watching him closely, could see by the light of the fire that he had been startled--had almost shuddered as the question was asked him.", "의사는 한참 대답 없이 앉아 있었고, 그를 가까이서 지켜보던 릴리는 난롯불 빛으로 그가 흠칫했음을—그 물음을 받고 거의 몸을 떨었음을 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["\"What did I say to her?\"", "“내가 그분에게 무슨 말을 했느냐고요?”"], ["and he repeated her words in a very low voice.", "그는 아주 낮은 목소리로 그녀의 말을 되뇌었다."], ["\"I asked her if she could love me, and be my wife.\"", "“나를 사랑할 수 있겠느냐고, 내 아내가 되어 주겠느냐고 물었습니다.”"]], [["\"And what answer did she make to you?\"", "“그래서 언니는 무슨 대답을 했어요?”"]], [["\"What answer did she make?", "“무슨 대답을 했느냐고요?"], ["She simply refused me.\"", "그냥 나를 거절했습니다.”"]], [["\"No, no, no; don't believe her, Dr.", "“아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요. 언니 말을 믿지 마세요, 크로프츠 선생님."], ["Crofts. It was not so;--I think it was not so.", "그런 게 아니었어요—그런 게 아니었을 거예요."], ["Mind you, I can say nothing as coming from her.", "다만 아시다시피, 언니에게서 나온 말이라고는 제가 아무것도 전해 드릴 수 없어요."], ["She has not told me her own mind.", "언니는 제게 제 속마음을 말한 적이 없으니까요."], ["But if you really love her, she will be mad to refuse you.\"", "하지만 선생님이 정말로 언니를 사랑하신다면, 언니가 선생님을 거절하는 건 미친 짓이에요.”"]], [["\"I do love her, Lily; that at any rate is true.\"", "“나는 그분을 사랑합니다, 릴리 양. 적어도 그건 사실이에요.”"]], [["\"Then go to her again.", "“그러면 다시 가 보세요."], ["I am speaking for myself now.", "지금은 제 이야기를 하는 거예요."], ["I cannot afford to lose such a brother as you would be.", "저는 선생님 같은 형부를 잃을 수는 없어요."], ["I love you so dearly that I cannot spare you.", "저는 선생님을 너무 아껴서 놓아 드릴 수가 없거든요."], ["And she,--I think she'll learn to love you as you would wish to be loved.", "그리고 언니도—언니도 선생님이 사랑받고 싶어 하시는 그대로 선생님을 사랑하는 법을 배우게 될 거예요."], ["You know her nature, how silent she is, and averse to talk about herself.", "선생님도 언니 성품을 아시잖아요, 얼마나 말이 없는지, 제 이야기 하는 걸 얼마나 꺼리는지."], ["She has confessed nothing to me but this,--that you spoke to her and took her by surprise.", "언니가 제게 털어놓은 건 이것뿐이에요—선생님이 말을 걸어 오셨고 언니는 불시에 놀랐다는 것요."], ["Are we to have another chance?", "저희에게 한 번 더 기회가 있을까요?"], ["I know how wrong I am to ask such a question.", "이런 걸 여쭙는 제가 얼마나 잘못인지 알아요."], ["But, after all, is not the truth the best?\"", "하지만 그래도 진실이 가장 낫지 않겠어요?”"]], [["\"Another chance!\"", "“한 번 더 기회라!”"]], [["\"I know what you mean, and I think she is worthy to be your wife.", "“무슨 말씀이신지 알아요. 그리고 저는 언니가 선생님의 아내가 될 만하다고 생각해요."], ["I do, indeed; and if so, she must be very worthy.", "정말이지 그렇게 생각해요. 그리고 그렇다면 언니는 아주 훌륭한 사람인 거죠."], ["You won't tell of me, will you now, doctor?\"", "제 얘기는 하지 않으실 거죠, 선생님?”"]], [["\"No; I won't tell of you.\"", "“네, 아가씨 얘기는 하지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["\"And you'll try again?\"", "“그리고 다시 해 보실 거죠?”"]], [["\"Yes; I'll try again.\"", "“네, 다시 해 보지요.”"]], [["\"God bless you, my brother!", "“하느님의 축복이 있기를, 나의 형부!"], ["I hope,--I hope you'll be my brother.\"", "부디—부디 제 형부가 되어 주세요.”"], ["Then, as he put out his hand to her once more, she raised her head towards him, and he, stooping down, kissed her forehead.", "그러고서 그가 다시 한 번 그녀에게 손을 내밀자 그녀는 그를 향해 고개를 들었고, 그는 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 이마에 입을 맞추었다."], ["\"Make mamma come to me,\"", "“엄마를 들여보내 주세요.”"], ["were the last words she spoke as he went out at the door.", "그가 문을 나설 때 그녀가 한 마지막 말이었다."]], [["\"So you've made your speech,\"", "“인사말은 다 했니?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, mamma.\"", "“네, 엄마.”"]], [["\"I hope it was a discreet speech.\"", "“분별 있는 인사말이었기를 바란다.”"]], [["\"I hope it was, mamma.", "“그랬기를 바라요, 엄마."], ["But it has made me so tired, and I believe I'll go to bed.", "그런데 그것 때문에 어찌나 지쳤는지 이제 자야겠어요."], ["Do you know I don't think I should have done much good down at the school to-day?\"", "오늘 학교에 내려갔더라면 별로 도움이 못 되었을 것 같지 않으세요?”"]], [["Then Mrs. Dale, in her anxiety to repair what injury might have been done to her daughter by over-exertion, omitted any further mention of the farewell speech.", "그러자 데일 부인은 딸이 무리해서 입었을지 모를 해를 어떻게든 되돌려 보려는 걱정에, 그 작별 인사말에 관해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["Dr. Crofts as he rode home enjoyed but little of the triumph of a successful lover.", "크로프츠 선생은 말을 타고 집으로 돌아가면서 사랑을 이룬 사내의 승리감이라고는 거의 누리지 못했다."], ["\"It may be that she's right,\"", "“그 아가씨 말이 옳을지도 모르지.”"], ["he said to himself; \"and, at any rate, I'll ask again.\"", "그는 혼자 중얼거렸다. “어쨌든 나는 다시 물어보겠어.”"], ["Nevertheless, that \"No\" which Bell had spoken, and had repeated, still sounded in his ears harsh and conclusive.", "그런데도 벨이 했던, 그리고 거듭했던 그 “아니오”라는 말이 여전히 그의 귓가에 모질고 결정적으로 울리고 있었다."], ["There are men to whom a peal of noes rattling about their ears never takes the sound of a true denial, and others to whom the word once pronounced, be it whispered ever so softly, comes as though it were an unchangeable verdict from the supreme judgment-seat.", "어떤 사내들에게는 ‘아니오’ 소리가 귓가에 우르르 쏟아져도 결코 참된 거절로 들리지 않지만, 또 어떤 사내들에게는 그 말이 아무리 나직이 속삭여진 것이라 해도 한번 발음되고 나면 지고의 심판대에서 내려온 돌이킬 수 없는 판결처럼 다가온다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIII. FIE, FIE!", "# 제43장. 「망측해라, 망측해라!」"]], [["Will any reader remember the loves,--no, not the loves; that word is so decidedly ill-applied as to be incapable of awakening the remembrance of any reader; but the flirtations--of Lady Dumbello and Mr.", "독자 여러분 가운데 레이디 덤벨로와 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨의 연정을—아니, 연정은 아니다. 그 낱말은 하도 어울리지 않게 갖다 붙인 것이라 어느 독자의 기억도 일깨우지 못할 터이니—그 두 사람의 시시덕거림을 기억하는 이가 있을까?"], ["Plantagenet Palliser? Those flirtations, as they had been carried on at Courcy Castle, were laid bare in all their enormities to the eye of the public, and it must be confessed that if the eye of the public was shocked, that eye must be shocked very easily.", "코시 성에서 벌어졌던 그 시시덕거림은 그 흉악함이 낱낱이 세상의 눈앞에 드러났는데, 만일 세상의 눈이 그것으로 충격을 받았다면 그 눈은 참으로 쉽게도 충격을 받는 눈이라고 고백해 두어야겠다."]], [["But the eye of the public was shocked, and people who were particular as to their morals said very strange things.", "그러나 세상의 눈은 정말로 충격을 받았고, 품행에 유난히 까다로운 사람들은 아주 이상한 말들을 했다."], ["Lady De Courcy herself said very strange things indeed, shaking her head, and dropping mysterious words; whereas Lady Clandidlem spoke much more openly, declaring her opinion that Lady Dumbello would be off before May.", "드 코시 백작부인 본인도 참으로 이상한 말을 했으니, 고개를 절레절레 저으며 알쏭달쏭한 말을 흘리는 식이었다. 반면에 레이디 클랜디들럼은 훨씬 더 대놓고 말하며, 레이디 덤벨로가 오월이 되기 전에 달아나 버릴 것이라는 제 의견을 공언했다."], ["They both agreed that it would not be altogether bad for Lord Dumbello that he should lose his wife, but shook their heads very sadly when they spoke of poor Plantagenet Palliser.", "두 사람은 덤벨로 경이 아내를 잃는 것이 그에게 아주 나쁜 일만은 아니리라는 데 뜻을 같이했으나, 가엾은 플랜태저넷 팰리서 이야기가 나오자 몹시 슬프게 고개를 저었다."], ["As to the lady's fate, that lady whom they had both almost worshipped during the days at Courcy Castle,--they did not seem to trouble themselves about that.", "그 부인의 신세로 말하자면—코시 성에 머물던 날들 내내 두 사람이 거의 떠받들다시피 했던 그 부인의 신세로 말하자면—그 일로는 도무지 마음을 쓰는 것 같지 않았다."]], [["And it must be admitted that Mr. Palliser had been a little imprudent,--imprudent, that is, if he knew anything about the rumours afloat,--seeing that soon after his visit at Courcy Castle he had gone down to Lady Hartletop's place in Shropshire, at which the Dumbellos intended to spend the winter, and on leaving it had expressed his intention of returning in February.", "그리고 팰리서 씨가 조금 경솔했다는 점은 인정해야 한다—경솔했다는 것은, 그가 떠도는 소문을 조금이라도 알고 있었다면 그렇다는 말이다."], ["The Hartletop people had pressed him very much,--the pressure having come with peculiar force from Lord Dumbello.", "코시 성 방문이 끝난 지 얼마 되지 않아 그는 슈롭셔에 있는 레이디 하틀탑의 저택으로 내려갔는데, 덤벨로 부부가 그곳에서 겨울을 날 참이었고, 그는 그곳을 떠나면서 이월에 다시 오겠다는 뜻을 밝혔던 것이다."], ["Therefore it is reasonable to suppose that the Hartletop people had at any rate not heard of the rumour.", "하틀탑 사람들이 그를 몹시 붙들었는데—그 붙듦이 유독 힘차게 나온 쪽이 다름 아닌 덤벨로 경이었다. 그러니 하틀탑 사람들은 적어도 그 소문을 듣지 못했다고 보는 것이 이치에 맞다."]], [["Mr. Plantagenet Palliser spent his Christmas with his uncle, the Duke of Omnium, at Gatherum Castle.", "플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 크리스마스를 숙부인 옴니엄 공작과 함께 개서럼 성에서 보냈다."], ["That is to say, he reached the castle in time for dinner on Christmas eve, and left it on the morning after Christmas day.", "그러니까 크리스마스 전날 저녁 식사 시각에 맞추어 성에 닿았고, 크리스마스 다음 날 아침에 그곳을 떠났다는 말이다."], ["This was in accordance with the usual practice of his life, and the tenants, dependants, and followers of the Omnium interest were always delighted to see this manifestation of a healthy English domestic family feeling between the duke and his nephew.", "이는 그의 생활에서 늘 있어 온 관례였고, 옴니엄 가에 매인 소작인이며 식솔이며 따르는 무리는 공작과 그 조카 사이에 건전한 영국식 가정의 정이 이렇게 드러나는 것을 언제나 기뻐하며 바라보았다."], ["But the amount of intercourse on such occasions between them was generally trifling.", "그러나 그런 자리에서 두 사람이 주고받는 말의 양은 대체로 대수롭지 않았다."], ["The duke would smile as he put out his right hand to his nephew, and say,--", "공작은 조카에게 오른손을 내밀며 미소를 띠고 이렇게 말하곤 했다—"]], [["\"Well, Plantagenet,--very busy, I suppose?\"", "“그래, 플랜태저넷—무척 바쁘겠지?”"]], [["The duke was the only living being who called him Plantagenet to his face, though there were some scores of men who talked of Planty Pal behind his back.", "그를 면전에 두고 플랜태저넷이라 부르는 사람은 세상에 공작 하나뿐이었다. 뒤에서 플랜티 팰이라 입에 올리는 자는 수십 명이나 되었지만 말이다."], ["The duke had been the only living being so to call him.", "여태껏 그를 그렇게 부른 살아 있는 사람은 공작 하나뿐이었다."], ["Let us hope that it still was so, and that there had arisen no feminine exception, dangerous in its nature and improper in its circumstances.", "지금도 여전히 그러하기를, 그리고 그 성질이 위태롭고 그 사정이 온당치 못한 여자 쪽의 예외 따위는 생겨나지 않았기를 바라 두자."]], [["\"Well, Plantagenet,\"", "“그래, 플랜태저넷.”"], ["said the duke, on the present occasion, \"very busy, I suppose?\"", "이번에도 공작이 말했다. “무척 바쁘겠지?”"]], [["\"Yes, indeed, duke,\"", "“예, 그렇습니다, 공작 어른.”"], ["said Mr. Palliser.", "팰리서 씨가 말했다."], ["\"When a man gets the harness on him he does not easily get quit of it.\"", "“사람이 한번 멍에를 지고 나면 좀처럼 벗어 버리기 어렵지요.”"]], [["The duke remembered that his nephew had made almost the same remark at his last Christmas visit.", "공작은 조카가 지난 크리스마스 방문 때도 거의 똑같은 말을 했던 것을 떠올렸다."]], [["\"By-the-by,\"", "“그건 그렇고,”"], ["said the duke, \"I want to say a word or two to you before you go.\"", "공작이 말했다. “자네가 가기 전에 한두 마디 할 말이 있네.”"]], [["Such a proposition on the duke's part was a great departure from his usual practice, but the nephew of course undertook to obey his uncle's behests.", "공작 쪽에서 그런 말을 꺼낸 것은 여느 때의 관례에서 크게 벗어난 일이었으나, 조카는 물론 숙부의 분부를 따르겠노라 했다."]], [["\"I'll see you before dinner to-morrow,\"", "“내일 저녁 식사 전에 뵙겠습니다.”"], ["said Plantagenet.", "플랜태저넷이 말했다."]], [["\"Ah, do,\" said the duke.", "“아, 그러게.”"], ["\"I'll not keep you five minutes.\"", "공작이 말했다. “오 분도 붙들지 않겠네.”"], ["And at six o'clock on the following afternoon the two were closeted together in the duke's private room.", "그리하여 이튿날 오후 여섯 시에 두 사람은 공작의 사실(私室)에 단둘이 들어앉았다."]], [["\"I don't suppose there is much in it,\"", "“그리 대단한 일은 아닐 걸세.”"], ["began the duke, \"but people are talking about you and Lady Dumbello.\"", "공작이 말을 꺼냈다. “허나 사람들이 자네와 레이디 덤벨로를 두고 말들을 하고 있네.”"]], [["\"Upon my word, people are very kind.\" And Mr.", "“저런, 사람들이 참 친절하기도 하군요.”"], ["Palliser bethought himself of the fact,--for it certainly was a fact,--that people for a great many years had talked about his uncle and Lady Dumbello's mother-in-law.", "그러면서 팰리서 씨는 한 가지 사실을 떠올렸다—그것은 분명 사실이었으니, 사람들이 여러 해 동안 제 숙부와 레이디 덤벨로의 시어머니를 두고 말들을 해 왔다는 사실이었다."]], [["\"Yes; kind enough; are they not?", "“그래, 친절하고말고."], ["You've just come from Hartlebury, I believe.\"", "그렇지 않은가? 자네는 하틀베리에서 막 왔다지.”"], ["Hartlebury was the Marquis of Hartletop's seat in Shropshire.", "하틀베리는 슈롭셔에 있는 하틀탑 후작의 저택이었다."]], [["\"Yes, I have.", "“예, 그렇습니다."], ["And I'm going there again in February.\"", "그리고 이월에 또 갈 참입니다.”"]], [["\"Ah, I'm sorry for that.", "“아, 그건 안됐군."], ["Not that I mean, of course, to interfere with your arrangements.", "물론 자네 일정에 참견하겠다는 뜻은 아닐세."], ["You will acknowledge that I have not often done so, in any matter whatever.\"", "내가 그런 짓을 자주 하지 않았다는 것은 자네도 인정하겠지, 무슨 일에서든 말이야.”"]], [["\"No; you have not,\"", "“예, 그러신 적 없습니다.”"], ["said the nephew, comforting himself with an inward assurance that no such interference on his uncle's part could have been possible.", "조카가 그렇게 말하면서, 숙부 쪽에서 그런 참견을 하려 한들 될 리가 없었으리라는 속내의 확신으로 스스로를 달랬다."]], [["\"But in this instance it would suit me, and I really think it would suit you too, that you should be as little at Hartlebury as possible.", "“허나 이번 일만은 내게도 좋고, 실은 자네에게도 좋을 성싶네. 자네가 하틀베리에 되도록 적게 머무는 것이 말이야."], ["You have said you would go there, and of course you will go.", "자네가 가겠다고 말했으니 물론 가야겠지."], ["But if I were you, I would not stay above a day or two.\"", "허나 내가 자네라면 하루 이틀 넘게는 머물지 않겠네.”"]], [["Mr. Plantagenet Palliser received everything he had in the world from his uncle.", "플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 이 세상에서 가진 모든 것을 숙부에게서 받았다."], ["He sat in Parliament through his uncle's interest, and received an allowance of ever so many thousand a year which his uncle could stop to-morrow by his mere word.", "숙부의 세력 덕분에 의회에 의석을 두었고, 숙부가 내일이라도 말 한마디로 끊어 버릴 수 있는 연 몇천 파운드의 생활비를 받았다."], ["He was his uncle's heir, and the dukedom, with certain entailed properties, must ultimately fall to him, unless his uncle should marry and have a son.", "그는 숙부의 상속인이었고, 공작위와 한정상속으로 묶인 얼마간의 재산은 숙부가 혼인해 아들을 두지 않는 한 결국 그에게 떨어질 수밖에 없었다."], ["But by far the greater portion of the duke's property was unentailed; the duke might probably live for the next twenty years or more; and it was quite possible that, if offended, he might marry and become a father.", "그러나 공작의 재산 가운데 훨씬 큰 몫은 한정상속에 묶여 있지 않았고, 공작은 앞으로 스무 해 아니 그 이상도 살 법했으며, 비위가 상하면 혼인해 아비가 되는 일도 얼마든지 있을 수 있었다."], ["It may be said that no man could well be more dependent on another than Plantagenet Palliser was upon his uncle; and it may be said also that no father or uncle ever troubled his heir with less interference.", "플랜태저넷 팰리서가 제 숙부에게 매여 있는 만큼 남에게 매여 있는 사람은 달리 없다고 해도 좋을 것이다. 그리고 상속인을 이만큼 적게 간섭한 아버지나 숙부도 달리 없다고 해도 좋을 것이다."], ["Nevertheless, the nephew immediately felt himself aggrieved by this allusion to his private life, and resolved at once that he would not submit to such surveillance.", "그럼에도 조카는 제 사생활에 대한 이 언급에 당장 마음이 상했고, 그런 감시 따위에는 따르지 않겠다고 그 자리에서 마음을 정했다."]], [["\"I don't know how long I shall stay,\"", "“얼마나 머무를지는 저도 모르겠습니다.”"], ["said he; \"but I cannot say that my visit will be influenced one way or the other by such a rumour as that.\"", "그가 말했다. “허나 제 방문이 그런 소문 때문에 이러쿵저러쿵 좌우되리라고는 말씀드릴 수 없군요.”"]], [["\"No; probably not.", "“그래, 그렇지는 않겠지."], ["But it may perhaps be influenced by my request.\"", "허나 어쩌면 내 부탁 때문에는 좌우될지도 모르지.”"], ["And the duke, as he spoke, looked a little savage.", "그러면서 공작은 말하는 동안 조금 사나운 낯빛이 되었다."]], [["\"You wouldn't ask me to regard a report that has no foundation.\"", "“아무 근거도 없는 소문을 마음에 두라고 하지는 않으시겠지요.”"]], [["\"I am not asking about its foundation.", "“나는 그 근거를 묻고 있는 게 아닐세."], ["Nor do I in the least wish to interfere with your manner in life.\"", "자네 사는 방식에 끼어들 생각도 눈곱만큼 없고.”"], ["By which last observation the duke intended his nephew to understand that he was quite at liberty to take away any other gentleman's wife, but that he was not at liberty to give occasion even for a surmise that he wanted to take Lord Dumbello's wife.", "이 마지막 말로 공작은, 다른 신사의 아내라면 누구를 데려가든 자네 마음이지만 덤벨로 경의 아내를 데려가고 싶어 한다는 억측을 살 자유만은 없다는 뜻을 조카에게 알린 것이었다."], ["\"The fact is this, Plantagenet.", "“실은 이런 일일세, 플랜태저넷."], ["I have for many years been intimate with that family.", "나는 여러 해 그 집안과 가깝게 지내 왔네."], ["I have not many intimacies, and shall probably never increase them.", "나는 가깝게 지내는 사람이 많지 않고, 아마 앞으로도 늘리지 않을 걸세."], ["Such friends as I have, I wish to keep, and you will easily perceive that any such report as that which I have mentioned, might make it unpleasant for me to go to Hartlebury, or for the Hartlebury people to come here.\"", "벗이 있으면 그대로 두고 싶단 말이야. 그러니 내가 말한 그런 소문이 돌면 내가 하틀베리에 가기도, 하틀베리 사람들이 이리 오기도 거북해지리라는 것쯤은 자네도 쉽게 알아보겠지.”"], ["The duke certainly could not have spoken plainer, and Mr. Palliser understood him thoroughly.", "공작이 이보다 더 노골적으로 말할 수는 없었을 터이고, 팰리서 씨도 그를 낱낱이 알아들었다."], ["Two such alliances between the two families could not be expected to run pleasantly together, and even the rumour of any such second alliance might interfere with the pleasantness of the former one.", "두 집안 사이의 그런 결연이 둘씩이나 나란히 순조롭게 굴러가리라고는 기대할 수 없는 노릇이었고, 그런 두 번째 결연은 소문만으로도 앞서의 결연이 지닌 즐거움을 해칠 수 있었다."]], [["\"That's all,\"", "“그게 다일세.”"], ["said the duke.", "공작이 말했다."]], [["\"It's a most absurd slander,\"", "“참으로 터무니없는 비방입니다.”"], ["said Mr. Palliser.", "팰리서 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"I dare say.", "“그렇겠지."], ["Those slanders always are absurd; but what can we do?", "그런 비방이란 늘 터무니없는 법이야."], ["We can't tie up people's tongues.\"", "허나 우리가 무얼 할 수 있겠나?"], ["And the duke looked as though he wished to have the subject considered as finished, and to be left alone.", "사람들의 혀를 붙들어 맬 수는 없지 않은가.” 그러면서 공작은 이 이야기가 이제 끝난 것으로 여겨져 혼자 있게 되기를 바라는 낯빛이었다."]], [["\"But we can disregard them,\"", "“허나 무시할 수는 있지요.”"], ["said the nephew, indiscreetly.", "조카가 눈치 없이 말했다."]], [["\"You may. I have never been able to do so.", "“자네는 그럴 수 있겠지."], ["And yet, I believe, I have not earned for myself the reputation of being subject to the voices of men.", "나는 여태 그러지 못했네. 그러면서도 나는 남들의 입방아에 휘둘리는 사람이라는 평판을 얻지는 않았다고 믿네."], ["You think that I am asking much of you; but you should remember that hitherto I have given much and have asked nothing.", "자네는 내가 자네에게 큰 것을 요구한다고 여기겠지. 허나 여태 나는 많이 주기만 하고 아무것도 요구한 적이 없다는 것을 잊지 말게."], ["I expect you to oblige me in this matter.\"", "이 일에서만은 자네가 내 뜻을 들어 주기를 바라네.”"]], [["Then Mr. Plantagenet Palliser left the room, knowing that he had been threatened.", "그리하여 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨는 자기가 협박을 받았음을 알고 방을 나섰다."], ["What the duke had said amounted to this.--If you go on dangling after Lady Dumbello, I'll stop the seven thousand a year which I give you.", "공작이 한 말은 이런 뜻이나 다름없었다.—네가 계속 레이디 덤벨로 꽁무니를 따라다닌다면, 내가 주는 연 칠천 파운드를 끊겠다. 다음 실버브리지 선거에서 네가 다시 뽑히는 것을 막겠다."], ["I'll oppose your next return at Silverbridge, and I'll make a will and leave away from you Matching and The Horns,--a beautiful little place in Surrey, the use of which had been already offered to Mr. Palliser in the event of his marriage; all the Littlebury estate in Yorkshire, and the enormous Scotch property.", "그리고 유언장을 만들어 매칭과 더 혼스—서리에 있는 아름다운 작은 별장으로, 팰리서 씨가 혼인하게 되면 쓰라고 이미 내주기로 되어 있던 곳이다—와 요크셔의 리틀베리 영지 전부와 저 어마어마한 스코틀랜드 재산을 네게서 떼어 놓겠다."], ["Of my personal goods, and money invested in loans, shares, and funds, you shall never touch a shilling, or the value of a shilling.", "내 개인 재물과 대부며 주식이며 공채에 넣어 둔 돈에서는 한 실링도, 한 실링어치도 네가 손대지 못하게 하겠다."], ["And, if I find that I can suit myself, it may be that I'll leave you plain Mr. Plantagenet Palliser, with a little first cousin for the head of your family.", "그리고 내 짝을 찾을 수 있겠다 싶으면, 너를 그냥 밋밋한 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨로 남겨 두고 어린 사촌 하나를 네 집안의 어른으로 앉힐지도 모른다."]], [["The full amount of this threat Mr.", "이 협박이 어디까지 미치는지를 팰리서 씨는 낱낱이 알아들었다."], ["Palliser understood, and, as he thought of it, he acknowledged to himself that he had never felt for Lady Dumbello anything like love.", "그리고 그것을 곱씹으면서, 자기가 레이디 덤벨로에게 사랑 비슷한 것조차 느껴 본 적이 없음을 스스로 인정했다."], ["No conversation between them had ever been warmer than that of which the reader has seen a sample.", "두 사람 사이에 오간 대화 가운데 독자가 이미 본보기로 본 그것보다 더 뜨거운 것은 없었다."], ["Lady Dumbello had been nothing to him.", "레이디 덤벨로는 그에게 아무것도 아니었다."], ["But now,--now that the matter had been put before him in this way, might it not become him, as a gentleman, to fall in love with so very beautiful a woman, whose name had already been linked with his own?", "그러나 이제—일이 이런 식으로 눈앞에 놓이고 보니, 이미 제 이름과 나란히 엮여 버린 저토록 아름다운 여인에게 사랑을 품는 것이 신사로서 어울리는 일이 아니겠는가?"], ["We all know that story of the priest, who, by his question in the confessional, taught the ostler to grease the horses'", "고해실에서 던진 질문으로 여관 마부에게 말 이빨에 기름칠하는 법을 가르쳐 준 신부 이야기를 우리는 모두 알고 있다. “여태 그런 짓은 해 본 적이 없습니다요.”"], ["teeth. \"I never did yet,\"", "여관 마부가 말했다."], ["said the ostler, \"but I'll have a try at it.\"", "“그렇지만 한번 해 보지요.”"], ["In this case, the duke had acted the part of the priest, and Mr. Palliser, before the night was over, had almost become as ready a pupil as the ostler.", "이번 일에서 공작은 그 신부 노릇을 한 셈이었고, 팰리서 씨는 밤이 다 가기 전에 그 여관 마부만큼이나 고분고분한 제자가 되어 있었다."], ["As to the threat, it would ill become him, as a Palliser and a Plantagenet, to regard it.", "협박으로 말하자면, 팰리서 집안 사람이자 플랜태저넷인 그가 그런 것에 마음을 쓰는 일은 어울리지 않았다."], ["The duke would not marry.", "공작은 혼인하지 않을 것이다."], ["Of all men in the world he was the least likely to spite his own face by cutting off his own nose; and, for the rest of it, Mr.", "세상 누구보다도 제 얼굴에 침 뱉자고 제 코를 베어 낼 사람이 아니었다. 그 나머지는 팰리서 씨도 운에 맡길 참이었다."], ["Palliser would take his chance. Therefore he went down to Hartlebury early in February, having fully determined to be very particular in his attentions to Lady Dumbello.", "그리하여 그는 이월 초에 하틀베리로 내려갔으니, 레이디 덤벨로에게 각별히 마음을 쓰기로 단단히 작정한 터였다."]], [["Among a houseful of people at Hartlebury, he found Lord Porlock, a slight, sickly, worn-out looking man, who had something about his eye of his father's hardness, but nothing in his mouth of his father's ferocity.", "하틀베리에 그득한 손님들 가운데서 그는 폴록 경을 만났다. 야위고 병약하고 지쳐 빠져 보이는 사내로, 눈매에는 제 아비의 모진 데가 얼마쯤 있었으나 입가에는 아비의 사나움이라고는 없었다."]], [["\"So your sister is going to be married?\"", "“누이가 혼인한다지요?”"], ["said Mr. Palliser.", "팰리서 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes.", "“그렇소."], ["One has no right to be surprised at anything they do, when one remembers the life their father leads them.\"", "그 아비가 자식들을 어떻게 몰아세우는지 떠올려 보면, 그 애들이 무슨 짓을 하든 놀랄 것도 없지요.”"]], [["\"I was going to congratulate you.\"", "“축하드리려던 참이었소.”"]], [["\"Don't do that.\"", "“그러지 마시오.”"]], [["\"I met him at Courcy, and rather liked him.\"", "“나는 코시에서 그 사람을 만나 보았는데 꽤 마음에 들었소.”"]], [["Mr. Palliser had barely spoken to Mr. Crosbie at Courcy, but then in the usual course of his social life he seldom did more than barely speak to anybody.", "팰리서 씨는 코시에서 크로즈비 씨와 겨우 몇 마디 나누었을 뿐이었으나, 하기야 그는 여느 사교 자리에서도 누구와든 겨우 몇 마디 나누는 것 이상은 좀처럼 하지 않는 사람이었다."]], [["\"Did you?\"", "“그랬소?”"], ["said Lord Porlock.", "폴록 경이 말했다."], ["\"For the poor girl's sake I hope he's not a ruffian.", "“가엾은 누이를 봐서라도 그자가 불한당은 아니기를 바라오."], ["How any man should propose to my father to marry a daughter out of his house, is more than I can understand.", "어떤 사내가 우리 아버지에게 그 집 딸을 아내로 달라 청할 마음을 먹는지, 나로서는 도무지 이해할 수가 없소."], ["How was my mother looking?\"", "우리 어머니는 어떻게 보이시던가요?”"]], [["\"I didn't see anything amiss about her.\"", "“딱히 언짢은 데는 못 보았소.”"]], [["\"I expect that he'll murder her some day.\"", "“언젠가는 그 양반이 어머니를 죽이고 말 거요.”"], ["Then that conversation came to an end.", "그러고서 그 대화는 끝이 났다."]], [["Mr. Palliser himself perceived--as he looked at her he could not but perceive--that a certain amount of social energy seemed to enliven Lady Dumbello when he approached her.", "팰리서 씨 자신도 알아차렸다—그녀를 바라보고 있자니 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다—자기가 다가가면 레이디 덤벨로에게 얼마간의 사교적 활기가 도는 듯하다는 것을."], ["She was given to smile when addressed, but her usual smile was meaningless, almost leaden, and never in any degree flattering to the person to whom it was accorded.", "그녀는 말을 걸어오면 미소를 짓는 버릇이 있었으나, 그 여느 미소는 아무 뜻이 없고 납덩이처럼 무거웠으며, 미소를 받은 사람을 조금이라도 추어올리는 법이 결코 없었다."], ["Very many women smile as they answer the words which are spoken to them, and most who do so flatter by their smile.", "아주 많은 여자들이 저에게 건네진 말에 대답하면서 미소를 짓고, 그렇게 하는 이들은 대개 그 미소로 상대를 추어올린다."], ["The thing is so common that no one thinks of it.", "그것은 하도 흔한 일이라 아무도 눈여겨보지 않는다."], ["The flattering pleases, but means nothing.", "추어올림은 기분을 좋게 하지만 아무 뜻도 없다."], ["The impression unconsciously taken simply conveys a feeling that the woman has made herself agreeable, as it was her duty to do,--agreeable, as far as that smile went, in some very infinitesimal degree.", "저도 모르게 받아들이는 인상이란 그저, 그 여자가 제 도리대로 상냥하게 굴었다는 느낌—그 미소가 미치는 만큼, 그러니까 아주 미미한 정도로 상냥했다는 느낌뿐이다. 그러나 그렇게 함으로써 그 여자는 사교계에 제 몫의 작은 이바지를 한 것이다."], ["But she has thereby made her little contribution to society.", "그 여자는 같은 저녁에 같은 이바지를 백 번도 할 것이다."], ["She will make the same contribution a hundred times in the same evening.", "그 여자가 누구를 추어올렸는지는 아무도 알지 못한다."], ["No one knows that she has flattered anybody; she does not know it herself; and the world calls her an agreeable woman.", "그 여자 자신도 알지 못한다. 그리고 세상은 그 여자를 상냥한 여자라 부른다."], ["But Lady Dumbello put no flattery into her customary smiles.", "그러나 레이디 덤벨로는 제 여느 미소에 추어올림을 담지 않았다."], ["They were cold, unmeaning, accompanied by no special glance of the eye, and seldom addressed to the individual.", "그 미소들은 차갑고 뜻이 없었으며, 각별한 눈길이 따라붙지도 않았고, 어느 한 사람을 향하는 일도 드물었다."], ["They were given to the room at large; and the room at large, acknowledging her great pretensions, accepted them as sufficient.", "그것들은 방 전체에 주어졌고, 방 전체는 그녀의 대단한 위세를 인정하며 그것으로 넉넉하다고 받아들였다."], ["But when Mr. Palliser came near to her she would turn herself slightly, ever so slightly, on her seat, and would allow her eyes to rest for a moment upon his face.", "그러나 팰리서 씨가 가까이 오면 그녀는 앉은 자리에서 몸을 조금, 아주 조금 틀었고, 잠깐 동안 그의 얼굴에 눈길이 머물도록 두었다."], ["Then when he remarked that it had been rather cold, she would smile actually upon him as she acknowledged the truth of his observation. All this Mr.", "그러다가 그가 날이 꽤 춥다고 하면, 그녀는 그 말이 옳다고 받으면서 정말로 그를 향해 미소를 지었다."], ["Palliser taught himself to observe, having been instructed by his foolish uncle in that lesson as to the greasing of the horses' teeth.", "이 모든 것을 팰리서 씨는 눈여겨보는 법을 스스로 익혔으니, 말 이빨에 기름칠하는 법에 관한 그 가르침을 어리석은 숙부에게서 배운 뒤의 일이었다."]], [["But, nevertheless, during the first week of his stay at Hartlebury, he did not say a word to her more tender than his observation about the weather.", "그러나 그럼에도 하틀베리에 머문 첫 주일 동안 그가 그녀에게 한 말 가운데 날씨 이야기보다 더 다정한 것은 하나도 없었다."], ["It is true that he was very busy.", "그가 몹시 바빴던 것은 사실이다."], ["He had undertaken to speak upon the address, and as Parliament was now about to be opened, and as his speech was to be based upon statistics, he was full of figures and papers.", "그는 답신 연설을 맡기로 되어 있었는데, 이제 곧 의회가 열릴 참이었고 그 연설은 통계에 바탕을 둔 것이었으므로 그는 숫자와 서류에 파묻혀 있었다."], ["His correspondence was pressing, and the day was seldom long enough for his purposes.", "오가는 편지도 밀려 있어서 하루가 그의 볼일에 넉넉한 적이 드물었다."], ["He felt that the intimacy to which he aspired was hindered by the laborious routine of his life; but nevertheless he would do something before he left Hartlebury, to show the special nature of his regard.", "그는 자기가 바라는 그 가까움이 고된 일과에 가로막혀 있다고 느꼈다. 그럼에도 하틀베리를 떠나기 전에 무언가를 해서 제 마음이 각별하다는 것을 보이려 했다."], ["He would say something to her, that should open to her view the secret of--shall we say his heart?", "그녀에게 무슨 말인가를 해서, 무엇의 비밀을—그의 마음의 비밀이라고 해 두어야 할까?—그녀 앞에 열어 보이려 했다."], ["Such was his resolve, day after day.", "날마다 그것이 그의 결심이었다."], ["And yet day after day went by, and nothing was said.", "그러나 하루하루가 지나가도 아무 말도 나오지 않았다."], ["He fancied that Lord Dumbello was somewhat less friendly in his manner than he had been, that he put himself in the way and looked cross; but, as he declared to himself, he cared very little for Lord Dumbello's looks.", "덤벨로 경이 전보다 어쩐지 덜 다정한 듯싶었고, 앞을 가로막고 서서 험한 낯을 하는 것 같기도 했다. 그러나 그는 덤벨로 경의 낯빛 따위는 아무래도 좋다고 스스로 다짐했다."]], [["\"When do you go to town?\"", "“도시에는 언제 가시오?”"], ["he said to her one evening.", "어느 날 저녁 그가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["\"Probably in April.", "“아마 사월에요."], ["We certainly shall not leave Hartlebury before that.\"", "그전에는 하틀베리를 떠나지 않을 게 분명해요.”"]], [["\"Ah, yes.", "“아, 그렇겠군요."], ["You stay for the hunting.\"", "사냥 철 때문에 머무르시는군요.”"]], [["\"Yes; Lord Dumbello always remains here through March.", "“예. 덤벨로 경은 삼월 내내 늘 여기 머물러요."], ["He may run up to town for a day or two.\"", "하루 이틀쯤 도시에 다녀올지는 모르지만요.”"]], [["\"How comfortable!", "“참 편안하시겠소!"], ["I must be in London on Thursday, you know.\"", "나는 목요일에는 런던에 가 있어야 하오, 아시겠지만.”"]], [["\"When Parliament meets, I suppose?\"", "“의회가 열리니까요?”"]], [["\"Exactly.", "“바로 그렇소."], ["It is such a bore; but one has to do it.\"", "여간 성가신 일이 아니지만, 해야 하는 일이니.”"]], [["\"When a man makes a business of it, I suppose he must.\"", "“사람이 그것을 업으로 삼았다면 해야겠지요.”"]], [["\"Oh, dear, yes; it's quite imperative.\"", "“아이고, 그럼요. 꼼짝없이 해야 하는 일이오.”"], ["Then Mr. Palliser looked round the room, and thought he saw Lord Dumbello's eye fixed upon him.", "그러고서 팰리서 씨는 방 안을 둘러보다가 덤벨로 경의 눈이 저에게 박혀 있는 것을 본 듯싶었다."], ["It was really very hard work.", "정말이지 여간 힘든 일이 아니었다."], ["If the truth must be told, he did not know how to begin.", "사실대로 말하자면 그는 어떻게 시작해야 할지를 몰랐다."], ["What was he to say to her?", "그녀에게 무슨 말을 해야 하나?"], ["How was he to commence a conversation that should end by being tender?", "끝에 가서 다정해질 대화를 어떻게 꺼내야 하나?"], ["She was very handsome certainly, and for him she could look interesting; but for his very life he did not know how to begin to say anything special to her.", "그녀는 분명 아주 아름다웠고, 그의 눈에는 마음을 끄는 데가 있어 보일 수도 있었다. 그러나 목숨이 걸렸다 해도 그는 그녀에게 각별한 말을 어떻게 꺼내야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["A liaison with such a woman as Lady Dumbello,--platonic, innocent, but nevertheless very intimate,--would certainly lend a grace to his life, which, under its present circumstances, was rather dry.", "레이디 덤벨로 같은 여인과의 정분—플라토닉하고 죄 없으면서도 아주 가까운 사이—이라면 지금처럼 메마른 그의 삶에 우아함을 얼마쯤 더해 줄 것이 틀림없었다."], ["He was told,--told by public rumour which had reached him through his uncle,--that the lady was willing.", "그는 그 부인이 마음이 있다고 들었다—숙부를 거쳐 그에게 닿은 세상의 소문으로 들었다."], ["She certainly looked as though she liked him; but how was he to begin?", "그녀는 분명 그를 좋아하는 듯 보였다. 그런데 어떻게 시작한단 말인가?"], ["The art of startling the House of Commons and frightening the British public by the voluminous accuracy of his statistics he had already learned; but what was he to say to a pretty woman?", "통계의 방대한 정확함으로 하원을 깜짝 놀라게 하고 영국 백성을 겁주는 재주는 이미 익혔다. 그러나 어여쁜 여인에게는 무슨 말을 해야 하는가?"]], [["\"You'll be sure to be in London in April?\"", "“사월에는 꼭 런던에 계시겠지요?”"]], [["This was on another occasion.", "이것은 또 다른 자리에서였다."]], [["\"Oh, yes; I think so.\"", "“예, 그럴 것 같아요.”"]], [["\"In Carlton Gardens, I suppose.\"", "“칼턴 가든스겠지요.”"]], [["\"Yes; Lord Dumbello has got a lease of the house now.\"", "“예. 덤벨로 경이 이제 그 집을 세내었어요.”"]], [["\"Has he, indeed?", "“아, 정말이오?"], ["Ah, it's an excellent house.", "아, 아주 좋은 집이지요."], ["I hope I shall be allowed to call there sometimes.\"", "나도 이따금 찾아뵐 수 있으면 좋겠소.”"]], [["\"Certainly,--only I know you must be so busy.\"", "“물론이지요—다만 그쪽이 몹시 바쁘신 줄은 알아요.”"]], [["\"Not on Saturdays and Sundays.\"", "“토요일과 일요일에는 그렇지 않소.”"]], [["\"I always receive on Sundays,\"", "“저는 일요일마다 손님을 받아요.”"], ["said Lady Dumbello. Mr.", "레이디 덤벨로가 말했다."], ["Palliser felt that there was nothing peculiarly gracious in this.", "팰리서 씨는 여기에 각별히 살가운 데라고는 없다고 느꼈다."], ["A permission to call when all her other acquaintances would be there, was not much; but still, perhaps, it was as much as he could expect to obtain on that occasion.", "아는 사람들이 죄다 와 있을 때 찾아와도 좋다는 허락이란 대단할 것이 없었다. 그래도 그 자리에서 얻어 낼 수 있는 것으로는 그만하면 다인 듯싶었다."], ["He looked up and saw that Lord Dumbello's eyes were again upon him, and that Lord Dumbello's brow was black.", "그가 고개를 드니 덤벨로 경의 눈이 또 저에게 박혀 있었고 덤벨로 경의 이마가 어두웠다."], ["He began to doubt whether a country house, where all the people were thrown together, was the best place in the world for such manoeuvring.", "그는 사람들이 죄다 한데 부대끼며 지내는 시골 저택이 이런 술수를 부리기에 세상에서 가장 좋은 자리인지 의심스러워지기 시작했다."], ["Lady Dumbello was very handsome, and he liked to look at her, but he could not find any subject on which to interest her in that drawing-room at Hartlebury.", "레이디 덤벨로는 아주 아름다웠고 그는 그녀를 바라보는 것이 좋았다. 그러나 하틀베리의 그 응접실에서는 그녀의 마음을 끌 만한 이야깃거리를 도무지 찾을 수 없었다."], ["Later in the evening he found himself saying something to her about the sugar duties, and then he knew that he had better give it up.", "그날 저녁 늦게 그는 설탕 관세에 관해 무언가를 그녀에게 말하고 있는 자신을 발견했고, 그제야 그만두는 편이 낫겠다는 것을 알았다."], ["He had only one day more, and that was required imperatively for his speech.", "남은 날은 하루뿐이었고 그날은 연설을 위해 아무래도 필요했다."], ["The matter would go much easier in London, and he would postpone it till then.", "런던에서라면 일이 훨씬 수월할 터이니 그때까지 미루기로 했다."], ["In the crowded rooms of London private conversation would be much easier, and Lord Dumbello wouldn't stand over and look at him.", "런던의 붐비는 방들에서는 남모르는 이야기를 나누기가 훨씬 쉬울 것이고, 덤벨로 경이 곁에 버티고 서서 저를 노려보지도 않을 것이었다."], ["Lady Dumbello had taken his remarks about the sugar very kindly, and had asked for a definition of an ad valorem duty.", "레이디 덤벨로는 설탕에 관한 그의 말을 아주 고맙게 받아들였고, 종가세(從價稅)가 무엇인지 풀이해 달라고까지 했다. 그것은 그가 여태 해 본 어떤 것보다도 진짜 대화에 가까이 다가간 것이었다."], ["It was a nearer approach to a real conversation than he had ever before made; but the subject had been unlucky, and could not, in his hands, be brought round to anything tender; so he resolved to postpone his gallantry till the London spring should make it easy, and felt as he did so, that he was relieved for the time from a heavy weight.", "그러나 이야깃거리가 운이 없었고 그의 손에서는 도무지 다정한 쪽으로 돌려세울 수가 없었다. 그리하여 그는 런던의 봄이 이 일을 쉽게 만들어 줄 때까지 제 구애를 미루기로 마음먹었고, 그렇게 하면서 한동안은 무거운 짐을 벗었다고 느꼈다."]], [["\"Good-by, Lady Dumbello,\"", "“안녕히 계시오, 레이디 덤벨로.”"], ["he said, on the next evening.", "이튿날 저녁 그가 말했다."], ["\"I start early to-morrow morning.\"", "“나는 내일 아침 일찍 떠나오.”"]], [["\"Good-by, Mr. Palliser.\"", "“안녕히 가세요, 팰리서 씨.”"]], [["As she spoke she smiled ever so sweetly, but she certainly had not learned to call him Plantagenet as yet.", "그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 더없이 곱게 미소 지었으나, 그를 플랜태저넷이라 부르는 법은 아직 익히지 못한 것이 분명했다."], ["He went up to London and immediately got himself to work.", "그는 런던으로 올라가 곧바로 일에 매달렸다."], ["The accurate and voluminous speech came off with considerable credit to himself,--credit of that quiet, enduring kind which is accorded to such men.", "정확하고 방대한 그 연설은 그에게 적잖은 명예를 안기며 무사히 끝났다—그런 사람들에게 주어지는, 저 조용하고 오래가는 종류의 명예였다."], ["The speech was respectable, dull, and correct.", "연설은 점잖고 지루하고 정확했다."], ["Men listened to it, or sat with their hats over their eyes, asleep, pretending to do so; and the Daily Jupiter in the morning had a leading article about it, which, however, left the reader at its close altogether in doubt whether Mr.", "사람들은 그것을 듣거나, 모자로 눈을 덮은 채 앉아 자면서 듣는 척했다. 이튿날 아침 《데일리 주피터》에는 그 연설에 관한 사설이 실렸으나, 다 읽고 나서도 독자는 팰리서 씨를 대단한 재정 전문가로 보아야 할지 어떨지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["Palliser might be supposed to be a great financial pundit or no. Mr.", "팰리서 씨는 돈의 세계에 빛나는 등불이 되고 은행업의 영광이 될지도 몰랐다."], ["Palliser might become a shining light to the moneyed world, and a glory to the banking interests; he might be a future Chancellor of the Exchequer.", "앞날의 재무장관이 될지도 몰랐다."], ["But then again, it might turn out that, in these affairs, he was a mere ignis fatuus, a blind guide,--a man to be laid aside as very respectable, but of no depth.", "그러나 다시 또, 이런 일에서 그가 한낱 도깨비불이자 눈먼 길잡이—아주 점잖되 깊이라고는 없어서 옆으로 치워 두어야 할 사람—으로 판명될지도 몰랐다."], ["Who, then, at the present time, could judiciously risk his credit by declaring whether Mr. Palliser understood his subject or did not understand it?", "그러니 지금 이 시점에 누가 제 신용을 걸고 팰리서 씨가 제 주제를 아는지 모르는지를 분별 있게 단언할 수 있겠는가?"], ["We are not content in looking to our newspapers for all the information that earth and human intellect can afford; but we demand from them what we might demand if a daily sheet could come to us from the world of spirits.", "우리는 땅과 사람의 지혜가 줄 수 있는 모든 소식을 신문에서 찾는 것으로 만족하지 않는다. 우리는 저승에서 날마다 한 장씩 소식지가 온다면 요구했을 법한 것을 신문에 요구한다."], ["The result, of course, is this,--that the papers do pretend that they have come daily from the world of spirits; but the oracles are very doubtful, as were those of old.", "그 결과란 물론 이렇다—신문들은 정말로 저승에서 날마다 온 것인 양 굴지만, 그 신탁이란 옛날의 신탁이 그러했듯 몹시 미덥지 못하다는 것이다."]], [["Plantagenet Palliser, though he was contented with this article, felt, as he sat in his chambers in the Albany, that something else was wanting to his happiness.", "플랜태저넷 팰리서는 그 사설에 만족하면서도, 올버니의 제 거처에 앉아 제 행복에 무언가 다른 것이 모자란다고 느꼈다."], ["This sort of life was all very well.", "이런 삶도 다 좋기는 했다."], ["Ambition was a grand thing, and it became him, as a Palliser and a future peer, to make politics his profession.", "야망은 훌륭한 것이었고, 팰리서 집안 사람이자 앞날의 귀족인 그가 정치를 업으로 삼는 것은 어울리는 일이었다."], ["But might he not spare an hour or two for Amaryllis in the shade?", "그러나 그늘 아래 아마릴리스를 위해 한두 시간쯤 낼 수는 없단 말인가?"], ["Was it not hard, this life of his?", "이 삶은 너무 고되지 않은가?"], ["Since he had been told that Lady Dumbello smiled upon him, he had certainly thought more about her smiles than had been good for his statistics.", "레이디 덤벨로가 저에게 미소를 보낸다는 말을 들은 뒤로 그는 분명 그 미소를 두고 제 통계에 이롭지 않을 만큼 생각이 많아졌다."], ["It seemed as though a new vein in his body had been brought into use, and that blood was running where blood had never run before.", "몸속에 새로운 핏줄 하나가 열려 여태 피가 흐른 적 없는 곳에 피가 흐르는 것만 같았다."], ["If he had seen Lady Dumbello before Dumbello had seen her, might he not have married her?", "덤벨로가 그녀를 보기 전에 그가 레이디 덤벨로를 보았더라면 그녀와 혼인할 수 있었을까?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["in such case as that, had she been simply Miss Grantly, or Lady Griselda Grantly, as the case might have been, he thought he might have been able to speak to her with more ease.", "그랬더라면, 그러니까 그녀가 그저 그랜틀리 양이거나 형편에 따라 레이디 그리젤다 그랜틀리였더라면, 그녀에게 말을 붙이기가 한결 수월했으리라고 그는 생각했다."], ["As it was, he certainly had found the task difficult, down in the country, though he had heard of men of his class doing the same sort of thing all his life.", "사정이 이러하니 시골에서는 그 일이 여간 어렵지 않았다. 제 계급 사람들이 평생 그런 짓을 한다는 이야기는 익히 들어 왔건만 말이다."], ["For my own part, I believe that the reputed sinners are much more numerous than the sinners.", "나로 말하자면, 죄인이라 불리는 사람이 실제 죄인보다 훨씬 많다고 믿는다."]], [["As he sat there, a certain Mr. Fothergill came in upon him.", "그가 그렇게 앉아 있는데 포더길 씨라는 사람이 찾아 들어왔다."], ["Mr. Fothergill was a gentleman who managed most of his uncle's ordinary affairs,--a clever fellow, who knew on which side his bread was buttered.", "포더길 씨는 그의 숙부의 여느 일들을 거의 도맡아 처리하는 신사로, 제 빵 어느 쪽에 버터가 발려 있는지 아는 영리한 사람이었다."], ["Mr. Fothergill was naturally anxious to stand well with the heir; but to stand well with the owner was his business in life, and with that business he never allowed anything to interfere.", "포더길 씨는 당연히 상속인에게 잘 보이고 싶어 했다."], ["On this occasion Mr. Fothergill was very civil, complimenting his future possible patron on his very powerful speech, and predicting for him political power with much more certainty than the newspapers which had, or had not, come from the world of spirits.", "그러나 주인에게 잘 보이는 것이 그의 밥벌이였고, 그 밥벌이에 무엇이든 끼어들도록 두는 법이 없었다."], ["Mr. Fothergill had come in to say a word or two about some matter of business.", "이번에 포더길 씨는 아주 공손하여, 앞으로 제 후원자가 될지도 모를 이에게 그 힘찬 연설을 두고 치사를 늘어놓았고, 저승에서 왔거나 오지 않았거나 한 신문들보다 훨씬 더 자신 있게 그의 정치적 앞길을 점쳤다."], ["As all Mr. Palliser's money passed through Mr.", "포더길 씨는 볼일 한두 가지에 관해 한마디 하러 들른 것이었다."], ["Fothergill's hands, and as his electioneering interests were managed by Mr. Fothergill, Mr.", "팰리서 씨의 돈은 모두 포더길 씨의 손을 거쳤고 그의 선거 관련 일도 포더길 씨가 챙겼으므로, 포더길 씨는 꼭 해야 할 말 한두 마디를 하러 곧잘 찾아오곤 했다."], ["Fothergill not unfrequently called to say a necessary word or two. When this was done he said another word or two, which might be necessary or not, as the case might be.", "그 일이 끝나면 그는 또 다른 말 한두 마디를 했는데, 그것은 경우에 따라 꼭 해야 할 말일 수도 아닐 수도 있었다."]], [["\"Mr. Palliser,\"", "“팰리서 씨,”"], ["said he, \"I wonder you don't think of marrying.", "그가 말했다. “혼인할 생각은 안 하시는지 궁금합니다."], ["I hope you'll excuse me.\"", "무례를 용서해 주시기 바랍니다.”"]], [["Mr. Palliser was by no means sure that he would excuse him, and sat himself suddenly upright in his chair in a manner that was intended to exhibit a first symptom of outraged dignity.", "팰리서 씨는 그를 용서해 줄 마음이 도무지 들지 않았고, 위엄이 상했다는 첫 낌새를 내보이려는 뜻으로 의자에 갑자기 몸을 꼿꼿이 세웠다."], ["But, singularly enough, he had himself been thinking of marriage at that moment.", "그러나 참으로 묘하게도, 그 자신이 바로 그 순간 혼인을 생각하고 있던 참이었다."], ["How would it have been with him had he known the beautiful Griselda before the Dumbello alliance had been arranged?", "덤벨로 가와의 혼담이 짜이기 전에 그 아름다운 그리젤다를 알았더라면 어찌 되었을까?"], ["Would he have married her?", "그녀와 혼인했을까?"], ["Would he have been comfortable if he had married her?", "그녀와 혼인했다면 마음이 편했을까? 물론 이제는 혼인할 수 없었다."], ["Of course he could not marry now, seeing that he was in love with Lady Dumbello, and that the lady in question, unfortunately, had a husband of her own; but though he had been thinking of marrying, he did not like to have the subject thus roughly thrust before his eyes, and, as it were, into his very lap by his uncle's agent.", "그는 레이디 덤벨로에게 마음을 두고 있고, 딱하게도 그 부인에게는 제 남편이 있으니 말이다. 그러나 혼인을 생각하고 있었다고는 해도, 그 이야기가 숙부의 대리인 손에 이렇게 거칠게 제 눈앞으로, 아니 제 무릎 위로 던져지는 것은 달갑지 않았다."], ["Mr. Fothergill, no doubt, saw the first symptom of outraged dignity, for he was a clever, sharp man.", "포더길 씨가 위엄이 상했다는 그 첫 낌새를 알아보지 못했을 리 없다."], ["But, perhaps, he did not in truth much regard it.", "그는 영리하고 눈치 빠른 사람이었으니까."], ["Perhaps he had received instructions which he was bound to regard above all other matters.", "그러나 어쩌면 그는 그것을 실은 그다지 마음에 두지 않았는지도 모른다. 어쩌면 그는 무엇보다도 지켜야 할 분부를 받고 온 것인지도 모른다."]], [["\"I hope you'll excuse me, Mr.", "“용서해 주시기 바랍니다, 팰리서 씨, 정말이지 그렇습니다."], ["Palliser, I do, indeed; but I say it because I am half afraid of some,--some,--some diminution of good feeling, perhaps, I had better call it, between you and your uncle.", "그렇지만 이런 말씀을 드리는 것은, 팰리서 씨와 공작 각하 사이에 무슨—무슨—무슨 좋은 정이 얼마쯤 식어 가는 것이나 아닌가 하여 반쯤 겁이 나서입니다. 그렇게 불러 두는 편이 낫겠지요."], ["Anything of that kind would be such a monstrous pity.\"", "그런 일이라면 그야말로 끔찍한 일이 될 테니까요.”"]], [["\"I am not aware of any such probability.\"", "“나는 그럴 낌새를 전혀 모르오.”"]], [["This Mr.", "팰리서 씨는 이 말을 자못 위엄 있게 했다."], ["Palliser said with considerable dignity; but when the words were spoken he bethought himself whether he had not told a fib.", "그러나 말이 입에서 나오고 나자 자기가 거짓말을 한 것이 아닌가 하는 생각이 들었다."]], [["\"No; perhaps not.", "“예, 아마 없겠지요."], ["I trust there is no such probability.", "그런 낌새가 없기를 바랍니다."], ["But the duke is a very determined man if he takes anything into his head;--and then he has so much in his power.\"", "그렇지만 공작 각하께서는 무슨 일이든 한번 마음에 담으시면 여간 고집스러운 분이 아니십니다—게다가 그분 손아귀에 든 것이 그리도 많고요.”"]], [["\"He has not me in his power, Mr. Fothergill.\"", "“그분 손아귀에 내가 든 것은 아니오, 포더길 씨.”"]], [["\"No, no, no.", "“예, 예, 예."], ["One man does not have another in his power in this country,--not in that way; but then you know, Mr.", "이 나라에서 사람이 사람을 손아귀에 두는 일은 없지요—그런 식으로는 말입니다."], ["Palliser, it would hardly do to offend him; would it?\"", "그렇지만 아시다시피, 팰리서 씨, 그분의 비위를 거스르는 것은 좋을 게 없지 않겠습니까?”"]], [["\"I would rather not offend him, as is natural.", "“거스르지 않는 편이 낫겠소, 그야 당연하오."], ["Indeed, I do not wish to offend any one.\"", "실은 나는 누구의 비위도 거스르고 싶지 않소.”"]], [["\"Exactly so; and least of all the duke, who has the whole property in his own hands.", "“바로 그렇습니다. 그리고 무엇보다도 공작 각하는 안 되지요."], ["We may say the whole, for he can marry to-morrow if he pleases.", "재산 전부를 손에 쥐고 계신 분이니까요. 전부라고 해도 좋습니다."], ["And then his life is so good.", "마음만 내키시면 내일이라도 혼인하실 수 있으니 말입니다."], ["I don't know a stouter man of his age, anywhere.\"", "게다가 그분은 몸이 참 좋으십니다. 그 연세에 그만큼 정정한 분을 저는 어디서도 본 적이 없습니다.”"]], [["\"I'm very glad to hear it.\"", "“그거 참 반가운 이야기요.”"]], [["\"I'm sure you are, Mr. Palliser.", "“그러시고말고요, 팰리서 씨."], ["But if he were to take offence, you know?\"", "그렇지만 그분이 언짢아하시기라도 하면 말입니다?”"]], [["\"I should put up with it.\"", "“나는 참고 견디겠소.”"]], [["\"Yes, exactly; that's what you would do.", "“예, 그렇지요. 그렇게 하실 겁니다."], ["But it would be worth while to avoid it, seeing how much he has in his power.\"", "그렇지만 그런 일은 피해 두는 편이 값어치가 있지요. 그분 손아귀에 든 것이 그토록 많으니 말입니다.”"]], [["\"Has the duke sent you to me now, Mr. Fothergill?\"", "“공작께서 지금 그대를 내게 보내신 것이오, 포더길 씨?”"]], [["\"No, no, no,--nothing of the sort.", "“아니요, 아니요, 아니요—그런 것은 조금도 아닙니다."], ["But he dropped words the other day which made me fancy that he was not quite,--quite,--quite at ease about you.", "그렇지만 얼마 전에 그분이 무심코 흘리신 말씀이 있어서, 저는 그분이 팰리서 씨 일로 아주—아주—아주 마음이 편치는 않으신 모양이라고 짐작했습니다."], ["I have long known that he would be very glad indeed to see an heir born to the property.", "그분이 이 재산에 상속인이 태어나는 것을 보고 싶어 하신다는 것은 저도 오래전부터 알고 있었지요."], ["The other morning,--I don't know whether there was anything in it,--but I fancied he was going to make some change in the present arrangements.", "며칠 전 아침에는—거기에 무슨 뜻이 있었는지는 모르겠습니다만—지금의 조처에 무언가 손을 대려 하시는 듯한 느낌이 들었습니다."], ["He did not do it, and it might have been fancy.", "하시지는 않았고, 제 느낌뿐이었을 수도 있고요."], ["Only think, Mr. Palliser, what one word of his might do!", "다만 생각해 보십시오, 팰리서 씨, 그분의 말 한마디가 무슨 일을 할 수 있는지!"], ["If he says a word, he never goes back from it.\"", "한번 말씀을 뱉으시면 결코 물리는 법이 없는 분입니다.”"], ["Then, having said so much, Mr. Fothergill went his way.", "그러고서 그만큼 말한 뒤에 포더길 씨는 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["Mr. Palliser understood the meaning of all this very well.", "팰리서 씨는 이 모든 말의 뜻을 아주 잘 알아들었다."], ["It was not the first occasion on which Mr. Fothergill had given him advice,--advice such as Mr.", "포더길 씨가 그에게 충고를 한 것은—포더길 씨 자신에게는 할 권리가 없는 그런 충고를 한 것은 이번이 처음이 아니었다."], ["Fothergill himself had no right to give him. He always received such counsel with an air of half-injured dignity, intending thereby to explain to Mr.", "그는 그런 충고를 늘 위엄이 반쯤 상한 낯빛으로 받았으니, 그로써 포더길 씨에게 주제넘은 짓이라는 것을 알리려는 뜻이었다."], ["Fothergill that he was intruding.", "그러나 그 충고가 어디서 나온 것인지는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["But he knew well whence the advice came; and though, in all such cases, he had made up his mind not to follow such counsel, it had generally come to pass that Mr. Palliser's conduct had more or less accurately conformed itself to Mr.", "그리고 그런 경우마다 그런 충고를 따르지 않겠다고 마음을 정하곤 했으면서도, 어찌 된 셈인지 팰리서 씨의 처신은 대체로 포더길 씨의 충고에 얼추 들어맞곤 했다."], ["Fothergill's advice. A word from the duke might certainly do a great deal!", "공작의 말 한마디는 참으로 많은 일을 할 수 있었다!"], ["Mr. Palliser resolved that in that affair of Lady Dumbello he would follow his own devices.", "팰리서 씨는 레이디 덤벨로의 일에서만은 제 뜻대로 하리라 마음먹었다."], ["But, nevertheless, it was undoubtedly true that a word from the duke might do a great deal!", "그러나 그럼에도 공작의 말 한마디가 참으로 많은 일을 할 수 있다는 것은 틀림없는 사실이었다!"]], [["We, who are in the secret, know how far Mr. Palliser had already progressed in his iniquitous passion before he left Hartlebury.", "그 속내를 아는 우리는 팰리서 씨가 하틀베리를 떠나기까지 그 죄스러운 연정을 어디까지 밀고 나갔는지 알고 있다."], ["Others, who were perhaps not so well informed, gave him credit for a much more advanced success.", "그만큼 잘 알지 못하는 다른 이들은 그가 훨씬 더 멀리까지 성공을 거두었다고 여겼다."], ["Lady Clandidlem, in her letter to Lady De Courcy, written immediately after the departure of Mr. Palliser, declared that, having heard of that gentleman's intended matutinal departure, she had confidently expected to learn at the breakfast-table that Lady Dumbello had flown with him.", "레이디 클랜디들럼은 팰리서 씨가 떠난 직후에 드 코시 백작부인에게 쓴 편지에서, 그 신사가 새벽같이 떠날 참이라는 말을 듣고 아침 식탁에서 레이디 덤벨로가 그와 함께 달아났다는 소식을 듣게 되리라 굳게 믿었노라고 밝혔다."], ["From the tone of her ladyship's language, it seemed as though she had been robbed of an anticipated pleasure by Lady Dumbello's prolonged sojourn in the halls of her husband's ancestors.", "그 부인의 말투로 보건대, 레이디 덤벨로가 남편의 조상들이 살던 집에 그대로 눌러앉는 바람에 그녀는 기대하던 즐거움을 빼앗긴 것만 같았다. “그렇지만 나는 아주 확신해요.”"], ["\"I feel, however, quite convinced,\"", "레이디 클랜디들럼은 이렇게 썼다."], ["said Lady Clandidlem, \"that it cannot go on longer than the spring.", "“이 일이 봄을 넘길 수는 없다고 말이에요."], ["I never yet saw a man so infatuated as Mr. Palliser.", "나는 팰리서 씨처럼 홀린 남자를 여태 본 적이 없어요."], ["He did not leave her for one moment all the time he was here.", "그이는 여기 있는 동안 한순간도 그 여자 곁을 떠나지 않았답니다."], ["No one but Lady Hartletop would have permitted it.", "레이디 하틀탑 말고는 누구도 그런 짓을 두고 보지 않았을 거예요."], ["But, you know, there is nothing so pleasant as good old family friendships.\"", "하지만 아시다시피, 오래된 좋은 집안끼리의 우정만큼 즐거운 것은 없지요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLIV. VALENTINE'S DAY AT ALLINGTON.", "# 제44장. 앨링턴의 밸런타인 날"]], [["Lily had exacted a promise from her mother before her illness, and during the period of her convalescence often referred to it, reminding her mother that that promise had been made, and must be kept.", "릴리는 앓아눕기 전에 어머니에게서 약속 하나를 받아 냈고, 회복기 동안 그것을 자주 들먹이며 그런 약속을 했으니 반드시 지켜야 한다고 어머니에게 일깨웠다."], ["Lily was to be told the day on which Crosbie was to be married.", "크로즈비가 혼인하는 날을 릴리에게 알려 주기로 한 약속이었다."], ["It had come to the knowledge of them all that the marriage was to take place in February.", "혼례가 이월에 있으리라는 것은 그들 모두가 알게 된 바였다."], ["But this was not sufficient for Lily.", "그러나 릴리에게는 그것으로 충분하지 않았다."], ["She must know the day.", "그녀는 그 날짜를 알아야만 했다."]], [["And as the time drew nearer,--Lily becoming stronger the while, and less subject to medical authority,--the marriage of Crosbie and Alexandrina was spoken of much more frequently at the Small House.", "그리고 그날이 가까워질수록—그사이 릴리는 차츰 기운을 되찾아 의사의 권위에 매인 몸에서 벗어나고 있었다—크로즈비와 알렉산드리나의 혼인은 작은 집에서 훨씬 더 자주 입에 오르내렸다."], ["It was not a subject which Mrs. Dale or Bell would have chosen for conversation; but Lily would refer to it.", "그것은 데일 부인이나 벨이 골랐을 법한 이야깃거리가 아니었으나, 릴리가 자꾸 그 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["She would begin by doing so almost in a drolling strain, alluding to herself as a forlorn damsel in a play-book; and then she would go on to speak of his interests as a matter which was still of great moment to her.", "그녀는 으레 거의 익살스러운 투로 말문을 열어, 자기를 이야기책 속의 버림받은 아가씨에 빗대곤 했다. 그러고는 그의 앞날이 자기에게 여전히 대단히 중대한 일이기라도 한 듯 그것을 이야기하기 시작했다."], ["But in the course of such talking she would too often break down, showing by some sad word or melancholy tone how great was the burden on her heart. Mrs.", "그러나 그런 이야기를 하는 도중에 그녀는 너무나 자주 무너져 내려, 슬픈 한마디나 서글픈 말투로 제 마음에 얹힌 짐이 얼마나 무거운지를 드러내 보였다."], ["Dale and Bell would willingly have avoided the subject, but Lily would not have it avoided.", "데일 부인과 벨은 기꺼이 그 이야기를 피했을 테지만, 릴리가 피하도록 놓아두지 않았다."], ["For them it was a very difficult matter on which to speak in her hearing.", "그들에게는 그녀가 듣는 데서 그 이야기를 하기가 여간 어려운 일이 아니었다."], ["It was not permitted to them to say a word of abuse against Crosbie, as to whom they thought that no word of condemnation could be sufficiently severe; and they were forced to listen to such excuses for his conduct as Lily chose to manufacture, never daring to point out how vain those excuses were.", "크로즈비를 헐뜯는 말은 한마디도 허락되지 않았으니—그들 생각에는 어떤 비난의 말로도 그를 넉넉히 나무랄 수 없을 듯싶었는데도 그러했다. 그러면서 그들은 릴리가 지어내기로 마음먹은 그의 행실에 대한 온갖 변명을 듣고 있어야 했고, 그 변명이 얼마나 부질없는지는 감히 짚어 낼 수 없었다."]], [["Indeed, in those days Lily reigned as a queen at the Small House.", "참으로 그 무렵 릴리는 작은 집에서 여왕처럼 군림했다."], ["Ill-usage and illness together falling into her hands had given her such power, that none of the other women were able to withstand it.", "몹쓸 대접과 병이 한꺼번에 그녀의 손에 들어와 그런 권세를 주었기에, 다른 여자들 가운데 누구도 그것을 거스를 수 없었다."], ["Nothing was said about it; but it was understood by them all, Jane and the cook included, that Lily was for the time paramount.", "그에 대해 아무 말도 오가지 않았지만, 제인과 요리사까지 포함해 그들 모두는 당분간 릴리가 으뜸이라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["She was a dear, gracious, loving, brave queen, and no one was anxious to rebel;--only that those praises of Crosbie were so very bitter in the ears of her subjects.", "그녀는 다정하고 너그럽고 사랑스럽고 용감한 여왕이었으므로 아무도 반역할 마음이 없었다—다만 그 크로즈비 칭찬만은 신하들의 귀에 몹시도 쓰라렸다."], ["The day was named soon enough, and the tidings came down to Allington.", "날짜는 머지않아 정해졌고, 그 소식이 앨링턴까지 내려왔다."], ["On the fourteenth of February, Crosbie was to be made a happy man.", "이월 십사일에 크로즈비는 행복한 사내가 될 참이었다."], ["This was not known to the Dales till the twelfth, and they would willingly have spared the knowledge then, had it been possible to spare it.", "이것을 데일네 사람들이 알게 된 것은 십이일이 되어서였고, 그때라도 그 소식을 릴리에게서 아낄 수만 있었다면 그들은 기꺼이 아꼈을 것이다."], ["But it was not so, and on that evening Lily was told.", "그러나 그럴 수는 없었고, 그날 저녁에 릴리는 그 이야기를 들었다."]], [["During these days, Bell used to see her uncle daily.", "그 무렵 벨은 날마다 삼촌을 만나러 갔다."], ["Her visits were made with the pretence of taking to him information as to Lily's health; but there was perhaps at the bottom of them a feeling that, as the family intended to leave the Small House at the end of March, it would be well to let the squire know that there was no enmity in their hearts against him.", "릴리의 병세를 알려 드린다는 구실을 붙인 걸음이었으나, 어쩌면 그 밑바닥에는 이런 마음이 깔려 있었을 것이다. 식구들이 삼월 말에 작은 집을 떠날 작정이니, 자기들 마음속에 삼촌에 대한 원한 같은 것은 없다는 것을 지주에게 알려 두는 편이 좋겠다는 마음이었다."], ["Nothing more had been said about their moving,--nothing, that is, from them to him.", "이사에 대해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았다—그러니까 그들 쪽에서 그에게 한 말은 없었다."], ["But the matter was going on, and he knew it. Dr.", "그러나 일은 진행되고 있었고 그도 그것을 알았다."], ["Crofts was already in treaty on their behalf for a small furnished house at Guestwick.", "크로프츠 선생이 벌써 그들을 대신해 게스트윅의 가구 딸린 작은 집 하나를 놓고 흥정을 하고 있었다."], ["The squire was very sad about it,--very sad indeed.", "지주는 그 일로 몹시 서글펐다—참으로 몹시 서글펐다."], ["When Hopkins spoke to him on the subject, he sharply desired that faithful gardener to hold his tongue, giving it to be understood that such things were not to be made matter of talk by the Allington dependants till they had been officially announced.", "홉킨스가 그 이야기를 꺼내자 그는 그 충직한 정원사에게 입을 다물라고 매섭게 일렀으니, 그런 일은 공식으로 알려지기 전에는 앨링턴에 딸린 사람들이 입에 올릴 거리가 아니라는 뜻이었다."], ["With Bell during these visits he never alluded to the matter.", "벨이 찾아온 동안에는 그 문제를 한 번도 입에 담지 않았다."], ["She was the chief sinner, in that she had refused to marry her cousin, and had declined even to listen to rational counsel upon the matter.", "벨은 사촌과 혼인하기를 마다했을 뿐 아니라 그 일에 대한 사리에 맞는 충고에 귀 기울이기조차 거절했으니, 가장 큰 죄인이었다."], ["But the squire felt that he could not discuss the subject with her, seeing that he had been specially informed by Mrs.", "그러나 지주는 그 이야기를 그녀와 따질 수 없다고 느꼈다. 자기가 참견하는 것은 허락되지 않는다는 말을 데일 부인에게서 콕 집어 들은 터였기 때문이다."], ["Dale that his interference would not be permitted; and then he was perhaps aware that if he did discuss the subject with Bell, he would not gain much by such discussion.", "그리고 또 어쩌면 그는, 벨과 그 이야기를 따져 본들 그런 따짐으로 얻을 것이 별로 없으리라는 것도 알고 있었을 것이다."], ["Their conversation, therefore, generally fell upon Crosbie, and the tone in which he was mentioned in the Great House was very different from that assumed in Lily's presence.", "그러므로 두 사람의 이야기는 으레 크로즈비에게로 흘렀는데, 큰 집에서 그의 이름이 오르내리는 말투는 릴리 앞에서 취하던 말투와는 아주 딴판이었다."]], [["\"He'll be a wretched man,\"", "“그자는 비참한 사내가 될 게다.”"], ["said the squire, when he told Bell of the day that had been fixed.", "정해진 날짜를 벨에게 일러 주며 지주가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't want him to be wretched,\"", "“저는 그이가 비참해지기를 바라지는 않아요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"But I can hardly think that he can act as he has done without being punished.\"", "“하지만 그런 짓을 해 놓고 벌을 받지 않으리라고는 도무지 생각할 수가 없어요.”"]], [["\"He will be a wretched man.", "“그자는 비참한 사내가 될 게다."], ["He gets no fortune with her, and she will expect everything that fortune can give.", "그 여자한테서 재산이라고는 한 푼도 받지 못할 텐데, 그 여자는 재산으로 얻을 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 다 바라고 있을 테니까."], ["I believe, too, that she is older than he is.", "게다가 내가 알기로는 그 여자가 그자보다 나이도 위다."], ["I cannot understand it.", "나는 도무지 이해할 수가 없구나."], ["Upon my word, I cannot understand how a man can be such a knave and such a fool.", "참말이지, 사내가 어떻게 저렇게 악당이면서 또 저렇게 바보일 수 있는지 나는 이해할 수가 없다."], ["Give my love to Lily.", "릴리에게 내 사랑을 전해 다오."], ["I'll see her to-morrow or the next day.", "내일이나 모레쯤 내가 그 아이를 보러 가마."], ["She's well rid of him; I'm sure of that;--though I suppose it would not do to tell her so.\"", "그 아이는 저런 자를 잘 떨쳐 낸 게야. 나는 그것만은 확실히 안다—하기야 그 아이에게 그렇게 말해 봐야 소용없겠지만.”"]], [["The morning of the fourteenth came upon them at the Small House, as comes the morning of those special days which have been long considered, and which are to be long remembered.", "십사일 아침이 작은 집 사람들에게 찾아왔다. 오래 마음에 두고 헤아려 왔고 또 오래 기억될 그런 특별한 날의 아침이 찾아오듯이 찾아왔다."], ["It brought with it a hard, bitter frost,--a black, biting frost,--such a frost as breaks the water-pipes, and binds the ground to the hardness of granite.", "그 아침은 매섭고 모진 서리를 데리고 왔다—검고 살을 에는 서리, 물관을 터뜨리고 땅을 화강암처럼 단단하게 얼려 붙이는 그런 서리였다."], ["Lily, queen as she was, had not yet been allowed to go back to her own chamber, but occupied the larger bed in her mother's room, her mother sleeping on a smaller one.", "릴리는 여왕이기는 했으나 아직 제 방으로 돌아가는 것이 허락되지 않아, 어머니 방의 큰 침대를 차지하고 있었고 어머니는 작은 침대에서 잤다."]], [["\"Mamma,\"", "“엄마,”"], ["she said, \"how cold they'll be!\"", "그녀가 말했다. “그이들이 얼마나 추울까!”"], ["Her mother had announced to her the fact of the black frost, and these were the first words she spoke.", "어머니가 검은 서리가 내렸다는 것을 알려 준 참이었고, 이것이 그녀가 한 첫마디였다."]], [["\"I fear their hearts will be cold also,\"", "“그이들 마음도 차가울까 걱정이구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["She ought not to have said so.", "그런 말을 해서는 안 되는 것이었다."], ["She was transgressing the acknowledged rule of the house in saying any word that could be construed as being inimical to Crosbie or his bride.", "크로즈비나 그의 신부에게 적의를 품은 것으로 해석될 만한 말은 무엇이든 이 집에서 인정된 규칙을 어기는 짓이었다."], ["But her feeling on the matter was too strong, and she could not restrain herself.", "그러나 그 일에 대한 부인의 감정이 너무나 강해서, 스스로를 억누를 수가 없었다."]], [["\"Why should their hearts be cold?", "“왜 그이들 마음이 차가워야 해요?"], ["Oh, mamma, that is a terrible thing to say.", "아, 엄마, 그건 너무 끔찍한 말이에요."], ["Why should their hearts be cold?\"", "왜 그이들 마음이 차가워야 하죠?”"]], [["\"I hope it may not be so.\"", "“그렇지 않기를 바란다.”"]], [["\"Of course you do; of course we all hope it.", "“물론 그러시겠죠. 물론 우리 모두 그러기를 바라고요."], ["He was not cold-hearted, at any rate.", "어쨌든 그이는 마음이 차가운 사람은 아니었어요."], ["A man is not cold-hearted, because he does not know himself.", "제 자신을 모른다고 해서 마음이 차가운 사람이 되는 건 아니잖아요."], ["Mamma, I want you to wish for their happiness.\"", "엄마, 저는 엄마가 그이들의 행복을 빌어 주셨으면 해요.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale was silent for a minute or two before she answered this, but then she did answer it.", "데일 부인은 이 말에 대답하기까지 일이 분 동안 잠자코 있었으나, 끝내는 대답을 했다."], ["\"I think I do,\"", "“나도 그러는 것 같구나.”"], ["said she.", "부인이 말했다."], ["\"I think I do wish for it.\"", "“나도 그것을 바라는 것 같다.”"]], [["\"I am very sure that I do,\"", "“저는 아주 확실히 그래요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["At this time Lily had her breakfast upstairs, but went down into the drawing-room in the course of the morning.", "이 무렵 릴리는 아침을 이층에서 먹었으나, 오전 중에 아래층 응접실로 내려가곤 했다."]], [["\"You must be very careful in wrapping yourself as you go downstairs,\"", "“아래층에 내려갈 때는 몸을 아주 잘 여미고 가야 해.”"], ["said Bell, who stood by the tray on which she had brought up the toast and tea.", "토스트와 차를 담아 올려 온 쟁반 곁에 선 채 벨이 말했다."], ["\"The cold is what you would call awful.\"", "“추위가 네 식으로 말하면 지독한 지경이거든.”"]], [["\"I should call it jolly,\"", "“나 같으면 신난다고 하겠어.”"], ["said Lily, \"if I could get up and go out.", "릴리가 말했다. “일어나서 밖에 나갈 수만 있다면 말이야."], ["Do you remember lecturing me about talking slang the day that he first came?\"", "언니, 그이가 처음 왔던 날 나한테 속어 쓴다고 잔소리했던 거 기억나?”"]], [["\"Did I, my pet?\"", "“내가 그랬니, 우리 아가?”"]], [["\"Don't you remember, when I called him a swell?", "“기억 안 나? 내가 그이더러 멋쟁이라고 했을 때 말이야."], ["Ah, dear!", "아, 저런!"], ["so he was.", "정말 그이는 멋쟁이였지. 바로 그게 잘못이었어."], ["That was the mistake, and it was all my own fault, as I had seen it from the first.\"", "그리고 그건 다 내 탓이야. 나는 처음부터 그걸 보고 있었으니까.”"]], [["Bell for a moment turned her face away, and beat with her foot against the ground.", "벨은 잠시 얼굴을 돌리고 발로 바닥을 굴렀다."], ["Her anger was more difficult of restraint than was even her mother's,--and now, not restraining it, but wishing to hide it, she gave it vent in this way.", "그녀의 분노는 어머니의 것보다도 더 억누르기 어려웠다—그리고 지금 그녀는 그것을 억누르는 것이 아니라 감추고 싶어서, 이런 식으로 그것을 흘려보냈다."]], [["\"I understand, Bell.", "“나 알아, 언니."], ["I know what your foot means when it goes in that way; and you shan't do it.", "언니 발이 저렇게 구를 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지 나는 알아. 그러니 그러면 안 돼."], ["Come here, Bell, and let me teach you Christianity.", "이리 와, 언니, 내가 언니한테 기독교를 가르쳐 줄게."], ["I'm a fine sort of teacher, am I not?", "나 참 대단한 선생이지, 안 그래?"], ["And I did not quite mean that.\"", "아니, 꼭 그런 뜻으로 한 말은 아니야.”"]], [["\"I wish I could learn it from some one,\" said Bell.", "“나도 누구한테든 그걸 배울 수 있으면 좋겠구나.”"], ["\"There are circumstances in which what we call Christianity seems to me to be hardly possible.\"", "벨이 말했다. “우리가 기독교라 부르는 것이 도무지 될 법하지 않은 처지라는 게 있어.”"]], [["\"When your foot goes in that way it is a very unchristian foot, and you ought to keep it still.", "“언니 발이 저렇게 구를 때 그건 아주 기독교인답지 못한 발이야. 가만히 두어야 해."], ["It means anger against him, because he discovered before it was too late that he would not be happy,--that is, that he and I would not be happy together if we were married.\"", "그건 그이에 대한 화를 뜻하잖아. 그이가 너무 늦기 전에 자기가 행복하지 못하리라는 걸—그러니까 그이와 내가 혼인해도 함께 행복하지 못하리라는 걸 알아냈다고 해서 내는 화 말이야.”"]], [["\"Don't scrutinize my foot too closely, Lily.\"", "“내 발을 너무 꼼꼼히 뜯어보지 말아라, 릴리.”"]], [["\"But your foot must bear scrutiny, and your eyes, and your voice.", "“하지만 언니 발은 뜯어보는 걸 견뎌야 해. 눈도, 목소리도 그렇고."], ["He was very foolish to fall in love with me.", "그이가 나를 사랑하게 된 건 아주 어리석은 일이었어."], ["And so was I very foolish to let him love me, at a moment's notice,--without a thought as it were.", "그리고 나도 아주 어리석었지. 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 말하자면 아무 생각도 없이 그이가 나를 사랑하도록 내버려 두었으니까."], ["I was so proud of having him, that I gave myself up to him all at once, without giving him a chance of thinking of it.", "나는 그이를 얻은 게 어찌나 자랑스러웠던지, 그이에게 그것을 생각해 볼 틈도 주지 않고 단번에 나를 내주어 버렸어."], ["In a week or two it was done.", "한두 주 만에 다 되어 버린 일이야."], ["Who could expect that such an engagement should be lasting?\"", "그런 약혼이 오래가리라고 누가 바랄 수 있었겠어?”"]], [["\"And why not?", "“왜 못 가?"], ["That is nonsense, Lily.", "그건 말도 안 되는 소리야, 릴리."], ["But we will not talk about it.\"", "하지만 그 얘기는 하지 말자.”"]], [["\"Ah, but I want to talk about it.", "“아, 하지만 나는 그 얘기를 하고 싶어. 내가 말한 그대로였어."], ["It was as I have said, and if so, you shouldn't hate him because he did the only thing which he honestly could do when he found out his mistake.\"", "그렇다면 언니는 그이를 미워하면 안 돼. 그이는 제 잘못을 알아차렸을 때 정직하게 할 수 있는 단 한 가지 일을 했을 뿐이니까.”"]], [["\"What; become engaged again within a week!\"", "“뭐라고, 한 주도 안 되어 또 약혼을 하는 게?”"]], [["\"There had been a very old friendship, Bell; you must remember that.", "“아주 오래된 우정이 있었잖아, 언니. 그건 언니도 기억해야 해."], ["But I was speaking of his conduct to me, and not of his conduct to--\"", "하지만 나는 그이가 나에게 한 처신을 말한 거지, 그이가 그—에게 한 처신을 말한 게 아니야.”"], ["And then she remembered that that other lady might at this very moment possess the name which she had once been so proud to think that she would bear herself.", "그러다 그녀는 그 다른 아가씨가 바로 이 순간 자기가 한때 스스로 갖게 되리라 여기며 그토록 자랑스러워하던 그 이름을 지니고 있을지도 모른다는 데 생각이 미쳤다."], ["\"Bell,\"", "“언니,”"], ["she said, stopping her other speech suddenly, \"at what o'clock do people get married in London?\"", "그녀가 하던 말을 문득 멈추고 말했다. “런던에서는 사람들이 몇 시에 혼인을 해?”"]], [["\"Oh, at all manner of hours,--any time before twelve.", "“아, 시간이야 갖가지지—열두 시 전이면 아무 때나."], ["They will be fashionable, and will be married late.\"", "그이들은 유행을 따를 테니 늦게 할 거야.”"]], [["\"You don't think she's Mrs. Crosbie yet, then?\"", "“그럼 언니는 그 여자가 아직 크로즈비 부인이 아니라고 생각해?”"]], [["\"Lady Alexandrina Crosbie,\"", "“레이디 알렉산드리나 크로즈비겠지.”"], ["said Bell, shuddering.", "벨이 몸서리를 치며 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, of course; I forgot.", "“응, 그렇지."], ["I should so like to see her.", "내가 깜빡했네."], ["I feel such an interest about her.", "나는 그 여자를 정말 한번 보고 싶어."], ["I wonder what coloured hair she has.", "그 여자한테 그렇게나 관심이 가."], ["I suppose she is a sort of Juno of a woman,--very tall and handsome.", "머리카락이 무슨 빛깔일까. 유노 같은 여자겠지—키가 아주 크고 훤칠하고."], ["I'm sure she has not got a pug-nose like me.", "나처럼 들창코는 아닐 게 분명해."], ["Do you know what I should really like, only of course it's not possible;--to be godmother to his first child.\"", "내가 정말로 하고 싶은 게 뭔지 알아? 물론 될 리야 없지만—그이의 첫아이 대모가 되는 거야.”"]], [["\"Oh, Lily!\"", "“아이, 릴리!”"]], [["\"I should.", "“정말 그러고 싶어."], ["Don't you hear me say that I know it's not possible?", "될 리 없다는 걸 나도 안다고 말하는 게 안 들려?"], ["I'm not going up to London to ask her.", "런던까지 올라가서 그 여자한테 청하겠다는 게 아니야."], ["She'll have all manner of grandees for her godfathers and godmothers.", "그 여자는 대부 대모로 온갖 높으신 어른들을 세우겠지."], ["I wonder what those grand people are really like.\"", "나는 그 높으신 분들이 정말 어떤 사람들일까 궁금해.”"]], [["\"I don't think there's any difference.", "“나는 다를 게 없다고 봐."], ["Look at Lady Julia.\"", "레이디 줄리아를 보렴.”"]], [["\"Oh, she's not a grand person.", "“아, 그분은 높으신 분이 아니야."], ["It isn't merely having a title.", "작위만 있다고 되는 게 아니잖아."], ["Don't you remember that he told us that Mr. Palliser is about the grandest grandee of them all.", "그이가 우리한테 팰리서 씨야말로 그 사람들 가운데 제일가는 높으신 어른이라고 했던 거 기억 안 나?"], ["I suppose people do learn to like them.", "사람들이 그 부류를 좋아하게 되는 법인가 봐."], ["He always used to say that he had been so long among people of that sort, that it would be very difficult for him to divide himself off from them.", "그이는 늘 자기가 그런 부류 사이에서 하도 오래 지내서, 그들에게서 제 몸을 떼어 놓기가 아주 어려울 거라고 말하곤 했어."], ["I should never have done for that kind of thing; should I?\"", "나는 그런 것에는 도무지 어울리지 못했겠지, 안 그래?”"]], [["\"There is nothing I despise so much as what you call that kind of thing.\"", "“네가 그런 것이라고 부르는 그것만큼 내가 업신여기는 건 없어.”"]], [["\"Do you?", "“언니는 그래?"], ["I don't.", "나는 안 그래."], ["After all, think how much work they do.", "어쨌든 그 사람들이 얼마나 많은 일을 하는지 생각해 봐."], ["He used to tell me of that.", "그이가 나한테 그 얘기를 하곤 했어."], ["They have all the governing in their hands, and get very little money for doing it.\"", "나라를 다스리는 일이 죄다 그 사람들 손에 있는데, 그 일로 받는 돈은 아주 적다는 거야.”"]], [["\"Worse luck for the country.\"", "“나라로서는 더 안된 일이지.”"]], [["\"The country seems to do pretty well.", "“나라는 꽤 잘 굴러가는 것 같은데."], ["But you're a radical, Bell.", "하지만 언니는 급진파야."], ["My belief is, you wouldn't be a lady if you could help it.\"", "내 생각에 언니는 어쩔 수만 있다면 숙녀 노릇도 하지 않을 사람이야.”"]], [["\"I'd sooner be an honest woman.\"", "“나는 차라리 정직한 여자가 되겠어.”"]], [["\"And so you are,--my own dear, dearest, honest Bell,--and the fairest lady that I know.", "“언니는 이미 그래—내 사랑하는, 세상에서 제일 사랑하는, 정직한 우리 언니—그리고 내가 아는 가장 어여쁜 숙녀야."], ["If I were a man, Bell, you are just the girl that I should worship.\"", "내가 사내였다면, 언니, 언니야말로 내가 떠받들었을 바로 그런 아가씨야.”"]], [["\"But you are not a man; so it's no good.\"", "“하지만 너는 사내가 아니잖니. 그러니 소용없는 소리야.”"]], [["\"But you mustn't let your foot go astray in that way; you mustn't, indeed.", "“그래도 언니는 발이 저런 식으로 못된 짓을 하도록 놓아두면 안 돼. 정말이지 안 돼."], ["Somebody said, that whatever is, is right, and I declare I believe it.\"", "누군가 있는 그대로가 다 옳다고 말했는데, 나는 정말로 그 말을 믿어.”"]], [["\"I'm sometimes inclined to think, that whatever is, is wrong.\"", "“나는 가끔 있는 그대로가 다 그르다는 쪽으로 마음이 기울어.”"]], [["\"That's because you're a radical.", "“그건 언니가 급진파라서 그래."], ["I think I'll get up now, Bell; only it's so frightfully cold that I'm afraid.\"", "나 이제 일어날까 봐, 언니. 다만 무섭게 추워서 겁이 나.”"]], [["\"There's a beautiful fire,\"", "“불이 아주 곱게 피워져 있어.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; I see.", "“응, 보여."], ["But the fire won't go all around me, like the bed does.", "하지만 불은 침대처럼 나를 온통 감싸 주지는 않잖아."], ["I wish I could know the very moment when they're at the altar.", "그이들이 제단 앞에 서는 바로 그 순간을 알 수 있으면 좋겠어."], ["It's only half-past ten yet.\"", "아직 열 시 반밖에 안 됐네.”"]], [["\"I shouldn't be at all surprised if it's over.\"", "“벌써 다 끝났다 해도 나는 조금도 놀라지 않겠어.”"]], [["\"Over!", "“끝났다니!"], ["What a word that is!", "그 말이 어쩌면 그럴까!"], ["A thing like that is over, and then all the world cannot put it back again.", "그런 일이 끝나 버리고 나면 온 세상이 달려들어도 도로 물릴 수가 없잖아."], ["What if he should be unhappy after all?\"", "그이가 결국 불행해지면 어떡하지?”"]], [["\"He must take his chance,\"", "“그건 그이가 제 운에 맡길 일이야.”"], ["said Bell, thinking within her own mind that that chance would be a very bad one.", "벨이 말했다. 그러면서 속으로는 그 운이 아주 나쁜 운이리라 생각했다."]], [["\"Of course he must take his chance.", "“물론 제 운에 맡길 일이지."], ["Well,--I'll get up now.\"", "자—이제 일어나야겠어.”"], ["And then she took her first step out into the cold world beyond her bed.", "그러고서 그녀는 침대 너머 차가운 세상으로 첫발을 내디뎠다."], ["\"We must all take our chance.", "“우리는 다들 제 운에 맡길 수밖에 없어."], ["I have made up my mind that it will be at half-past eleven.\"", "나는 그게 열한 시 반일 거라고 마음을 정했어.”"]], [["When half-past eleven came, she was seated in a large easy chair over the drawing-room fire, with a little table by her side, on which a novel was lying.", "열한 시 반이 되었을 때 그녀는 응접실 난롯가의 커다란 안락의자에 앉아 있었고, 곁의 작은 탁자에는 소설책 한 권이 놓여 있었다."], ["She had not opened her book that morning, and had been sitting for some time perfectly silent, with her eyes closed, and her watch in her hand.", "그날 아침 그녀는 그 책을 펴 보지 않았고, 눈을 감고 손에 시계를 쥔 채 한참 동안 아주 잠잠히 앉아 있었다."]], [["\"Mamma,\"", "“엄마,”"], ["she said at last, \"it is over now, I'm sure.\"", "마침내 그녀가 말했다. “이제 다 끝났어요, 저는 틀림없다고 생각해요.”"]], [["\"What is over, my dear?\"", "“무엇이 끝났다는 거니, 얘야?”"]], [["\"He has made that lady his wife.", "“그이가 그 아가씨를 아내로 삼았어요."], ["I hope God will bless them, and I pray that they may be happy.\"", "하느님께서 두 사람을 축복해 주시기를 바라고, 두 사람이 행복하기를 기도해요.”"], ["As she spoke these words, there was an unwonted solemnity in her tone which startled Mrs. Dale and Bell.", "그녀가 이 말을 할 때 그 말투에는 여느 때 없던 엄숙함이 있어서 데일 부인과 벨을 흠칫하게 했다."]], [["\"I also will hope so,\"", "“나도 그러기를 바라마.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"And now, Lily, will it not be well that you should turn your mind away from the subject, and endeavour to think of other things?\"", "“그러니 이제, 릴리, 그 일에서 마음을 돌려 다른 것을 생각하도록 애써 보는 게 좋지 않겠니?”"]], [["\"But I can't, mamma.", "“하지만 그럴 수가 없는걸요, 엄마."], ["It is so easy to say that; but people can't choose their own thoughts.\"", "그렇게 말하기야 쉽지요. 하지만 사람이 제 생각을 골라 가질 수는 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"They can usually direct them as they will, if they make the effort.\"", "“애를 쓰기만 하면 대개는 마음먹은 대로 생각을 이끌 수 있단다.”"]], [["\"But I can't make the effort.", "“하지만 저는 그 애를 쓸 수가 없어요."], ["Indeed, I don't know why I should.", "정말이지 왜 그래야 하는지도 모르겠고요."], ["It seems natural to me to think about him, and I don't suppose it can be very wrong.", "그이를 생각하는 것이 저에게는 자연스러운 일 같고, 그게 그렇게까지 그른 일일 리는 없다고 생각해요."], ["When you have had so deep an interest in a person, you can't drop him all of a sudden.\"", "어떤 사람에게 그토록 깊은 관심을 쏟고 나면, 그 사람을 하루아침에 놓아 버릴 수는 없어요.”"], ["Then there was again silence, and after a while Lily took up her novel.", "그러고서 다시 침묵이 흘렀고, 얼마 뒤 릴리는 소설책을 집어 들었다."], ["She made that effort of which her mother had spoken, but she made it altogether in vain.", "그녀는 어머니가 말한 그 애를 써 보았으나, 그 애씀은 도무지 헛일이었다. “정말이지, 언니,”"], ["\"I declare, Bell,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"it's the greatest rubbish I ever attempted to read.\"", "“내가 여태 읽어 보려고 덤빈 것 중에 제일가는 쓰레기야.”"], ["This was specially ungrateful, because Bell had recommended the book.", "이것은 유난히 배은망덕한 말이었으니, 그 책은 벨이 권한 것이었기 때문이다."], ["\"All the books have got to be so stupid!", "“책들이 죄다 어쩜 이렇게 시시해졌을까!"], ["I think I'll read Pilgrim's Progress again.\"", "나는 「천로역정」이나 다시 읽을래.”"]], [["\"What do you say to Robinson Crusoe?\"", "“「로빈슨 크루소」는 어떠니?”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Or Paul and Virginia?\"", "“아니면 「폴과 비르지니」?”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"But I believe I'll have Pilgrim's Progress.", "“그래도 나는 「천로역정」으로 할래."], ["I never can understand it, but I rather think that makes it nicer.\"", "나는 그 책을 도무지 이해할 수가 없는데, 그래서 오히려 더 좋은 것 같아.”"]], [["\"I hate books I can't understand,\"", "“나는 이해할 수 없는 책은 질색이야.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"I like a book to be clear as running water, so that the whole meaning may be seen at once.\"", "“나는 책이 흐르는 물처럼 맑아서 그 뜻이 한눈에 다 들여다보이는 게 좋아.”"]], [["\"The quick seeing of the meaning must depend a little on the reader, must it not?\"", "“뜻이 얼른 보이고 안 보이고는 읽는 사람에게도 얼마쯤 달린 것 아니겠니?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"The reader mustn't be a fool, of course,\"", "“물론 읽는 사람이 바보여서는 안 되겠지요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"But then so many readers are fools,\" said Lily.", "“하지만 읽는 사람 중에는 바보가 어찌나 많은지요.”"], ["\"And yet they get something out of their reading.", "릴리가 말했다. “그런데도 그이들은 읽어서 무언가를 얻잖아요."], ["Mrs. Crump is always poring over the Revelations, and nearly knows them by heart.", "크럼프 부인은 늘 요한계시록을 들여다보고 있어서 거의 외다시피 해요."], ["I don't think she could interpret a single image, but she has a hazy, misty idea of the truth.", "그 형상 가운데 하나도 풀이하지는 못할 것 같지만, 진리에 대해 어렴풋하고 안개 같은 생각은 갖고 있죠."], ["That's why she likes it,--because it's too beautiful to be understood; and that's why I like Pilgrim's Progress.\"", "그래서 그걸 좋아하는 거예요—이해하기에는 너무 아름다우니까요. 내가 「천로역정」을 좋아하는 것도 그래서고요.”"], ["After which Bell offered to get the book in question.", "그 말끝에 벨이 그 책을 가져다주겠다고 했다."]], [["\"No, not now,\" said Lily.", "“아니, 지금은 됐어.”"], ["\"I'll go on with this, as you say it's so grand.", "릴리가 말했다. “언니가 이게 그렇게 훌륭하다고 하니 이걸 마저 읽을래."], ["The personages are always in their tantrums, and go on as though they were mad.", "나오는 사람들이 늘 성깔을 부리고 미친 사람들처럼 굴기는 하지만."], ["Mamma, do you know where they're going for the honeymoon?\"", "엄마, 그이들이 신혼여행을 어디로 가는지 아세요?”"]], [["\"No, my dear.\"", "“모르겠구나, 얘야.”"]], [["\"He used to talk to me about going to the lakes.\"", "“그이는 저한테 호수 지방에 가자는 이야기를 하곤 했어요.”"], ["And then there was another pause, during which Bell observed that her mother's face became clouded with anxiety.", "그러고서 또 한 번 말이 끊겼는데, 그동안 벨은 어머니의 얼굴이 근심으로 흐려지는 것을 보았다."], ["\"But I won't think of it any more,\"", "“하지만 이제 그 생각은 더 하지 않을래요.”"], ["continued Lily; \"I will fix my mind to something.\"", "릴리가 말을 이었다. “무언가에 마음을 붙들어 매겠어요.”"], ["And then she got up from her chair.", "그러고서 그녀는 의자에서 일어났다."], ["\"I don't think it would have been so difficult if I had not been ill.\"", "“제가 앓지만 않았더라면 이렇게까지 힘들지는 않았을 것 같아요.”"]], [["\"Of course it would not, my darling.\"", "“물론 그렇지 않았을 게다, 우리 아가.”"]], [["\"And I'm going to be well again now, immediately.", "“그리고 저는 이제 곧, 당장에 다시 건강해질 거예요."], ["Let me see: I was told to read Carlyle's History of the French Revolution, and I think I'll begin now.\"", "가만있자, 저더러 칼라일의 「프랑스 혁명사」를 읽으라고 한 이가 있었는데, 이제 시작해 볼까 봐요.”"], ["It was Crosbie who had told her to read the book, as both Bell and Mrs. Dale were well aware.", "그 책을 읽으라고 한 사람이 크로즈비라는 것은 벨도 데일 부인도 잘 알고 있었다."], ["\"But I must put it off till I can get it down from the other house.\"", "“하지만 저쪽 집에서 그걸 내려 올 수 있을 때까지는 미뤄야겠네요.”"]], [["\"Jane shall fetch it, if you really want it,\"", "“네가 정말 그것을 원한다면 제인더러 가져오라고 하마.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Bell shall get it, when she goes up in the afternoon; will you, Bell?", "“오후에 언니가 올라갈 때 가져다주면 돼요. 그래 줄 거지, 언니?"], ["And I'll try to get on with this stuff in the meantime.\"", "그동안 나는 이 물건이나 어떻게 읽어 나가 볼게요.”"], ["Then again she sat with her eyes fixed upon the pages of the book.", "그러고서 그녀는 다시 책장에 눈을 붙박은 채 앉아 있었다."], ["\"I'll tell you what, mamma,--you may have some comfort in this: that when to-day's gone by, I shan't make a fuss about any other day.\"", "“엄마, 이건 말씀드릴게요—이걸로 얼마쯤 위안을 삼으셔도 돼요. 오늘만 지나가면 다른 날을 두고는 법석을 떨지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Nobody thinks that you are making a fuss, Lily.\"", "“아무도 네가 법석을 떤다고 생각하지 않는단다, 릴리.”"]], [["\"Yes, but I am.", "“아니에요, 저는 떨고 있어요."], ["Isn't it odd, Bell, that it should take place on Valentine's day?", "언니, 그게 하필 밸런타인 날에 있다니 이상하지 않아?"], ["I wonder whether it was so settled on purpose, because of the day.", "날이 날이라서 일부러 그렇게 정한 걸까. 아, 저런."], ["Oh, dear, I used to think so often of the letter that I should get from him on this day, when he would tell me that I was his valentine.", "나는 이날 그이한테서 받게 될 편지를 얼마나 자주 생각했는지 몰라. 내가 자기 밸런타인이라고 적어 보낼 그 편지 말이야."], ["Well; he's got another--valen--tine--now.\"", "그래—그이한테는 이제 다른 밸런—타인이—생겼구나.”"], ["So much she said with articulate voice, and then she broke down, bursting out into convulsive sobs, and crying in her mother's arms as though she would break her heart.", "여기까지는 또렷한 목소리로 말했으나, 그러고서 그녀는 무너져 내려 발작하듯 흐느끼기 시작했고, 가슴이 미어지기라도 할 듯 어머니 품에 안겨 울었다."], ["And yet her heart was not broken, and she was still strong in that resolve which she had made, that her grief should not overpower her.", "그런데도 그녀의 가슴은 미어지지 않았고, 슬픔에 눌려 넘어가지는 않겠다고 스스로 세운 그 결심에 그녀는 여전히 굳세었다."], ["As she had herself said, the thing would not have been so difficult, had she not been weakened by illness.", "그녀 자신이 말했듯, 병으로 몸이 약해지지만 않았더라면 그 일이 그렇게까지 어렵지는 않았을 것이다."]], [["\"Lily, my darling; my poor, ill-used darling.\"", "“릴리, 우리 아가. 불쌍한, 몹쓸 대접을 받은 우리 아가.”"]], [["\"No, mamma, I won't be that.\"", "“아니에요, 엄마, 저는 그런 사람이 되지 않겠어요.”"], ["And she struggled grievously to get the better of the hysterical attack which had overpowered her.", "그러면서 그녀는 자기를 덮친 발작을 이겨 내려고 애처롭게 몸부림쳤다. “저는 몹쓸 대접을 받은 사람으로 여겨지지 않겠어요."], ["\"I won't be regarded as ill-used; not as specially ill-used.", "유난히 몹쓸 대접을 받은 사람으로는요."], ["But I am your darling, your own darling.", "하지만 저는 엄마의 아가, 엄마만의 아가예요."], ["Only I wish you'd beat me and thump me when I'm such a fool, instead of pitying me.", "다만 제가 이렇게 바보 같을 때는 저를 가엾어하시는 대신 때리고 쥐어박아 주셨으면 좋겠어요."], ["It's a great mistake being soft to people when they make fools of themselves.", "사람이 제 스스로 바보짓을 할 때 무르게 대해 주는 건 큰 잘못이에요."], ["There, Bell; there's your stupid book, and I won't have any more of it.", "자, 언니, 언니의 그 시시한 책 여기 있어. 나는 이제 더 안 볼래."], ["I believe it was that that did it.\"", "이걸 읽어서 이렇게 된 것 같아.”"], ["And she pushed the book away from her.", "그러고서 그녀는 책을 저만치 밀어 놓았다."]], [["After this little scene she said no further word about Crosbie and his bride on that day, but turned the conversation towards the prospect of their new house at Guestwick.", "이 작은 소동이 있은 뒤로 그날 그녀는 크로즈비와 그의 신부에 대해 더는 한마디도 하지 않고, 게스트윅의 새집에 대한 이야기로 말머리를 돌렸다."]], [["\"It will be a great comfort to be nearer Dr.", "“크로프츠 선생 가까이에 있게 되면 아주 마음 든든하겠지, 언니?"], ["Crofts; won't it, Bell?\"", "안 그래?”"]], [["\"I don't know,\"", "“글쎄.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Because if we are ill, he won't have such a terrible distance to come.\"", "“우리가 앓을 때 그분이 그렇게 지독하게 먼 길을 오지 않아도 되잖아.”"]], [["\"That will be a comfort for him, I should think,\"", "“그건 그분에게 마음 든든한 일이겠구나.”"], ["said Bell, very demurely.", "벨이 아주 새침하게 말했다."]], [["In the evening the first volume of the French Revolution had been procured, and Lily stuck to her reading with laudable perseverance; till at eight her mother insisted on her going to bed, queen as she was.", "저녁이 되자 「프랑스 혁명사」 제1권이 마련되었고, 릴리는 칭찬할 만한 끈기로 읽기에 매달렸다. 여덟 시가 되어 어머니가, 여왕이거나 말거나 잠자리에 들어야 한다고 우길 때까지 그러했다."]], [["\"I don't believe a bit, you know, that the king was such a bad man as that,\"", "“있잖아, 나는 왕이 그렇게까지 나쁜 사람이었다는 건 조금도 못 믿겠어.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"I do,\"", "“나는 믿어.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Ah, that's because you're a radical.", "“아, 그건 언니가 급진파라서 그래."], ["I never will believe that kings are so much worse than other people.", "나는 왕들이 다른 사람들보다 그렇게 훨씬 더 나쁘다고는 결코 믿지 않을 거야."], ["As for Charles the First, he was about the best man in history.\"", "찰스 1세로 말하자면 그이는 역사를 통틀어 거의 가장 훌륭한 사람이었는걸.”"]], [["This was an old subject of dispute; but Lily on the present occasion was allowed her own way,--as being an invalid.", "이것은 오래된 말다툼거리였다. 그러나 이번만은 릴리가 병자라는 까닭으로 제 뜻대로 하도록 허락되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLV. VALENTINE'S DAY IN LONDON.", "# 제45장. 런던의 밸런타인 날"]], [["The fourteenth of February in London was quite as black, and cold, and as wintersome as it was at Allington, and was, perhaps, somewhat more melancholy in its coldness.", "런던의 이월 십사일도 앨링턴에서와 똑같이 어둡고 춥고 겨울다웠으며, 그 추위는 어쩌면 얼마쯤 더 서글펐다."], ["Nevertheless Lady Alexandrina De Courcy looked as bright as bridal finery could make her, when she got out of her carriage and walked into St. James's church at eleven o'clock on that morning.", "그럼에도 레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시는, 그날 아침 열한 시에 마차에서 내려 세인트제임스 교회로 걸어 들어갈 때, 혼례의 화려한 차림이 만들어 줄 수 있는 만큼은 눈부시게 보였다."]], [["It had been finally arranged that the marriage should take place in London.", "혼례는 마침내 런던에서 치르기로 정해졌다."], ["There were certainly many reasons which would have made a marriage from Courcy Castle more convenient.", "코시 성에서 혼례를 올리는 편이 한결 편했으리라 할 이유는 물론 여럿 있었다."], ["The De Courcy family were all assembled at their country family residence, and could therefore have been present at the ceremony without cost or trouble.", "드 코시 집안 사람들은 모두 시골 본가에 모여 있었으니 비용도 수고도 없이 예식에 참석할 수 있었을 것이다."], ["The castle too was warm with the warmth of life, and the pleasantness of home would have lent a grace to the departure of one of the daughters of the house.", "게다가 성은 사람이 사는 온기로 따뜻했고, 집안의 딸 하나가 떠나는 자리에 집이라는 정겨움이 우아함을 얹어 주었을 것이다."], ["The retainers and servants were there, and something of the rich mellowness of a noble alliance might have been felt, at any rate by Crosbie, at a marriage so celebrated.", "딸린 사람들과 하인들도 거기 있었으니, 그렇게 치러진 혼례에서라면 고귀한 인연이 지닌 무르익은 풍요로움 같은 것이 적어도 크로즈비에게는 느껴졌을지도 몰랐다."], ["And it must have been acknowledged, even by Lady De Courcy, that the house in Portman Square was very cold--that a marriage from thence would be cold,--that there could be no hope of attaching to it any honour and glory, or of making it resound with fashionable éclat in the columns of the Morning Post.", "그리고 포트먼 스퀘어의 그 집이 몹시 썰렁하다는 것은—거기서 치르는 혼례도 썰렁하리라는 것은, 거기에 무슨 명예와 영광을 붙일 가망도, 《모닝 포스트》의 지면에서 유행의 광채로 울려 퍼지게 할 가망도 없다는 것은—드 코시 백작부인조차 인정할 수밖에 없었을 것이다."], ["But then, had they been married in the country, the earl would have been there; whereas there was no probability of his travelling up to London for the purpose of being present on such an occasion.", "그러나 시골에서 혼례를 치렀다면 백작이 그 자리에 있었을 터였다. 반면 그런 자리에 참석하자고 그가 런던까지 올라올 가망은 없었다."]], [["The earl was very terrible in these days, and Alexandrina, as she became confidential in her communications with her future husband, spoke of him as of an ogre, who could not by any means be avoided in all the concerns of life, but whom one might shun now and again by some subtle device and careful arrangement of favourable circumstances.", "백작은 요즈음 몹시 무서운 사람이었고, 알렉산드리나는 장차 남편이 될 사람과 이야기가 스스럼없어지자 그를 도깨비라 부르며 말했다. 살아가는 온갖 일에서 도무지 피할 길이 없는 도깨비지만, 교묘한 꾀와 유리한 사정을 조심스레 마련해 두는 것으로 이따금 한 번씩은 비켜 갈 수 있는 도깨비라는 것이었다."], ["Crosbie had more than once taken upon himself to hint that he did not specially regard the ogre, seeing that for the future he could keep himself altogether apart from the malicious monster's dominions.", "크로즈비는 한두 번 나서서, 앞으로는 그 심술궂은 괴물의 영지에서 아주 멀찍이 떨어져 지낼 수 있으니 자기는 그 도깨비를 딱히 대수롭게 여기지 않는다고 넌지시 말한 적이 있었다."]], [["\"He will not come to me in our new home,\"", "“우리 새집으로 나를 찾아오지는 않을 테니까요.”"], ["he had said to his love, with some little touch of affection.", "그는 제 사랑에게 그렇게 말했는데, 거기에는 다정함이 조금 묻어 있었다."], ["But to this view of the case Lady Alexandrina had demurred.", "그러나 사태를 그렇게 보는 데에 레이디 알렉산드리나는 이의를 달았다."], ["The ogre in question was not only her parent, but was also a noble peer, and she could not agree to any arrangement by which their future connection with the earl, and with nobility in general, might be endangered.", "문제의 도깨비는 그녀의 아버지일 뿐 아니라 고귀한 귀족이기도 했으니, 그녀로서는 앞으로 자기들과 백작 사이의, 나아가 귀족 사회 전반과의 인연이 위태로워질 만한 어떤 처사에도 동의할 수 없었다."], ["Her parent, doubtless, was an ogre, and in his ogreship could make himself very terrible to those near him; but then might it not be better for them to be near to an earl who was an ogre, than not to be near to any earl at all?", "아버지는 틀림없이 도깨비였고, 그 도깨비 노릇으로 가까운 이들에게 아주 무서운 사람이 될 수도 있었다. 그러나 그렇더라도, 아예 어떤 백작과도 가깝지 않은 것보다는 도깨비인 백작과 가까운 편이 낫지 않겠는가?"], ["She had therefore signified to Crosbie that the ogre must be endured.", "그리하여 그녀는 크로즈비에게 그 도깨비는 견뎌야 한다고 일러 두었던 것이다."]], [["But, nevertheless, it was a great thing to be rid of him on that happy occasion.", "그러나 그렇더라도 그 행복한 자리에서 그를 떼어 놓을 수 있다는 것은 큰일이었다."], ["He would have said very dreadful things,--things so dreadful that there might have been a question whether the bridegroom could have borne them.", "그였다면 아주 끔찍한 말들을 했을 것이다—신랑이 과연 견뎌 낼 수 있었을지 의심스러울 만큼 끔찍한 말들을."], ["Since he had heard of Crosbie's accident at the railway station, he had constantly talked with fiendish glee of the beating which had been administered to his son-in-law.", "철도역에서 크로즈비가 당한 봉변을 전해 들은 뒤로 그는 사위에게 가해진 그 매질을 두고 악귀처럼 고소해하며 줄곧 떠들어 대고 있었다."], ["Lady De Courcy in taking Crosbie's part, and maintaining that the match was fitting for her daughter, had ventured to declare before her husband that Crosbie was a man of fashion, and the earl would now ask, with a loathsome grin, whether the bridegroom's fashion had been improved by his little adventure at Paddington.", "드 코시 백작부인은 크로즈비 편을 들며 이 혼사가 제 딸에게 어울린다고 주장하다가, 남편 앞에서 크로즈비가 유행을 아는 사내라고 감히 말해 버린 적이 있었다. 그래서 백작은 이제 역겨운 웃음을 지어 보이며, 신랑의 유행 감각이 패딩턴에서의 그 자그마한 모험 덕에 나아지기라도 했느냐고 묻곤 하는 것이었다."], ["Crosbie, to whom all this was not repeated, would have preferred a wedding in the country.", "이 모든 말이 옮겨지지 않은 크로즈비는 시골에서 혼례를 올리는 편을 더 좋아했을 것이다."], ["But the countess and Lady Alexandrina knew better.", "그러나 백작부인과 레이디 알렉산드리나는 더 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["The earl had strictly interdicted any expenditure, and the countess had of necessity construed this as forbidding any unnecessary expense.", "백작은 어떤 지출도 엄히 금해 두었고, 백작부인은 어쩔 수 없이 그 말을 불필요한 비용을 금한 것으로 새겼다."], ["\"To marry a girl without any immediate cost was a thing which nobody could understand,\"", "“딸 하나를 시집보내면서 당장 드는 돈이 한 푼도 없다는 것은 아무도 이해 못 할 일이오.”"], ["as the countess remarked to her eldest daughter.", "백작부인이 맏딸에게 그렇게 말했다."]], [["\"I would really spend as little as possible,\"", "“나라면 정말이지 되도록 적게 쓰겠소.”"], ["Lady Amelia had answered.", "레이디 어밀리아가 대답했다."], ["\"You see, mamma, there are circumstances about it which one doesn't wish to have talked about just at present.", "“아시다시피 어머니, 이 일에는 지금 당장 남들 입에 오르내리지 않았으면 하는 사정이 있잖소."], ["There's the story of that girl,--and then that fracas at the station.", "그 아가씨 이야기도 있고—역에서의 그 소동도 있으니 말이오."], ["I really think it ought to be as quiet as possible.\"", "나는 정말이지 되도록 조용히 치러야 한다고 생각하오.”"], ["The good sense of Lady Amelia was not to be disputed, as her mother acknowledged.", "레이디 어밀리아의 분별은 나무랄 데가 없었고, 그 어머니도 그것을 인정했다."], ["But then if the marriage were managed in any notoriously quiet way, the very notoriety of that quiet would be as dangerous as an attempt at loud glory.", "그러나 그렇다고 혼례를 소문나게 조용한 방식으로 치른다면, 그 조용함의 소문 자체가 요란한 영광을 노리는 것만큼이나 위험할 터였다."], ["\"But it won't cost as much,\" said Amelia.", "“하지만 돈은 그만큼 들지 않을 것 아니오.”"], ["And thus it had been resolved that the wedding should be very quiet.", "어밀리아가 말했다. 그리하여 혼례는 아주 조용히 치르기로 정해졌다."]], [["To this Crosbie had assented very willingly, though he had not relished the manner in which the countess had explained to him her views.", "이에 대해서는 크로즈비도 아주 기꺼이 동의했다. 다만 백작부인이 제 뜻을 그에게 설명한 그 태도만은 마음에 들지 않았다."]], [["\"I need not tell you, Adolphus,\"", "“굳이 말할 것도 없지만, 어돌퍼스.”"], ["she had said, \"how thoroughly satisfied I am with this marriage.", "그녀가 말했다. “나는 이 혼사가 더할 나위 없이 흡족하오."], ["My dear girl feels that she can be happy as your wife, and what more can I want?", "내 사랑스러운 딸아이가 그대의 아내로서 행복할 수 있다고 여기니, 내가 더 무엇을 바라겠소?"], ["I declared to her and to Amelia that I was not ambitious, for their sakes, and have allowed them both to please themselves.\"", "나는 그 아이한테도 어밀리아한테도, 나는 저희를 두고 욕심을 부리는 어미가 아니라고 늘 말해 왔고, 둘 다 제 마음에 드는 대로 하게 두었소.”"]], [["\"I hope they have pleased themselves,\"", "“두 분 다 제 마음에 드는 대로 하셨기를 바랍니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"I trust so; but nevertheless,--I don't know whether I make myself understood?\"", "“그러기를 바라오. 하지만 그렇더라도—내 말뜻이 통하는지 모르겠구려.”"]], [["\"Quite so, Lady De Courcy.", "“잘 알겠습니다, 드 코시 백작부인."], ["If Alexandrina were going to marry the eldest son of a marquis, you would have a longer procession to church than will be necessary when she marries me.\"", "알렉산드리나가 후작의 맏아들과 혼인하는 것이라면, 저와 혼인할 때 필요한 것보다는 교회로 가는 행렬이 더 길었겠지요.”"]], [["\"You put it in such an odd way, Adolphus.\"", "“참 별나게도 말하는구려, 어돌퍼스.”"]], [["\"It's all right so long as we understand each other.", "“서로 뜻만 통한다면야 아무래도 좋습니다."], ["I can assure you I don't want any procession at all.", "정말이지 저는 행렬 같은 것은 조금도 바라지 않는다고 말씀드릴 수 있습니다."], ["I should be quite contented to go down with Alexandrina, arm in arm, like Darby and Joan, and let the clerk give her away.\"", "다비와 조앤처럼 알렉산드리나와 팔짱을 끼고 걸어 내려가서, 교회 서기더러 신부를 넘겨 달라고 해도 아주 만족하겠습니다.”"]], [["We may say that he would have been much better contented could he have been allowed to go down the street without any encumbrance on his arm.", "우리는 이렇게 말해도 좋으리라. 그는 제 팔에 아무 짐도 걸치지 않은 채 그 길을 걸어 내려갈 수만 있었다면 훨씬 더 만족했으리라고."], ["But there was no possibility now for such deliverance as that.", "그러나 이제 그런 구원이 있을 가망은 없었다."]], [["Both Lady Amelia and Mr. Gazebee had long since discovered the bitterness of his heart and the fact of his repentance, and Gazebee had ventured to suggest to his wife that his noble sister-in-law was preparing for herself a life of misery.", "레이디 어밀리아와 게이지비 씨는 진작부터 그의 마음속 쓰라림과 그가 후회하고 있다는 사실을 알아차리고 있었고, 게이지비는 아내에게 저 고귀한 처제가 스스로 비참한 삶을 마련하고 있는 것 같다고 넌지시 말한 적이 있었다."]], [["\"He'll become quiet and happy when he's used to it,\"", "“저 사람도 익숙해지고 나면 조용해지고 행복해질 거요.”"], ["Lady Amelia had replied, thinking, perhaps, of her own experiences.", "레이디 어밀리아가 대답했다. 아마도 제 경험을 떠올리며 한 말이었을 것이다."]], [["\"I don't know, my dear; he's not a quiet man.", "“글쎄요, 여보. 저이는 조용한 사람이 아니에요."], ["There's something in his eye which tells me that he could be very hard to a woman.\"", "저이 눈에는 여자한테 아주 모질어질 수 있는 무언가가 있어요.”"]], [["\"It has gone too far now for any change,\"", "“이제 와서 무엇을 바꾸기에는 너무 멀리 왔소.”"], ["Lady Amelia had answered.", "레이디 어밀리아가 대답했다."]], [["\"Well; perhaps it has.\"", "“뭐, 그럴지도 모르지요.”"]], [["\"And I know my sister so well; she would not hear of it.", "“그리고 나는 내 동생을 잘 아오. 그 애는 그런 말은 들으려고도 하지 않을 거요."], ["I really think they will do very well when they become used to each other.\"", "나는 저 둘이 서로에게 익숙해지고 나면 아주 잘 지내리라고 정말로 생각하오.”"]], [["Mr. Gazebee, who also had had his own experiences, hardly dared to hope so much.", "저 나름의 경험이 있는 게이지비 씨는 감히 그만큼까지 바라지는 못했다."], ["His home had been satisfactory to him, because he had been a calculating man, and having made his calculation correctly was willing to take the net result.", "그의 가정이 그에게 흡족했던 것은 그가 셈에 밝은 사람이었고, 셈을 정확히 해 놓은 뒤 그 순이익을 기꺼이 받아들였기 때문이다."], ["He had done so all his life with success.", "그는 평생 그렇게 해서 성공을 거두어 왔다."], ["In his house his wife was paramount,--as he very well knew.", "그의 집에서는 아내가 으뜸이었으니—그것은 그 자신도 잘 알고 있었다."], ["But no effort on his wife's part, had she wished to make such effort, could have forced him to spend more than two-thirds of his income.", "그러나 아내가 아무리 애를 쓴들, 설령 그런 애를 쓰고 싶어 했다 한들, 그로 하여금 수입의 삼분의 이가 넘는 돈을 쓰게 만들 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["Of this she also was aware, and had trimmed her sails accordingly, likening herself to him in this respect.", "이 점은 아내도 알고 있었고, 그래서 거기에 맞추어 돛을 손보았으며, 그 점에서는 저를 남편에게 맞추어 왔던 것이다."], ["But of such wisdom, and such trimmings, and such adaptability, what likelihood was there with Mr. Crosbie and Lady Alexandrina?", "그러나 그런 지혜와 그런 돛 손질과 그런 맞춤을, 크로즈비 씨와 레이디 알렉산드리나에게서 바랄 여지가 어디 있단 말인가?"]], [["\"At any rate, it is too late now,\"", "“아무튼 이제는 너무 늦었소.”"], ["said Lady Amelia, thus concluding the conversation.", "레이디 어밀리아가 그렇게 말하며 이야기를 맺었다."]], [["But nevertheless, when the last moment came, there was some little attempt at glory.", "그럼에도 막상 마지막 순간이 오자 영광을 노리는 자그마한 시도가 조금은 있었다."], ["Who does not know the way in which a lately married couple's little dinner-party stretches itself out from the pure simplicity of a fried sole and a leg of mutton to the attempt at clear soup, the unfortunately cold dish of round balls which is handed about after the sole, and the brightly red jelly, and beautifully pink cream, which are ordered, in the last agony of ambition, from the next pastrycook's shop?", "갓 혼인한 부부의 조촐한 만찬 자리가 서대 튀김과 양 다리 고기라는 순전한 소박함에서 시작해, 맑은 수프를 흉내 내는 데까지, 서대가 나간 뒤에 돌려지는 안타깝게도 식어 버린 둥근 완자 요리에까지, 그리고 야심의 마지막 몸부림으로 이웃 과자점에 주문하는 새빨간 젤리와 곱디고운 분홍빛 크림에까지 어떻게 늘어나고 마는지를 모르는 사람이 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["\"We cannot give a dinner, my dear, with only cook and Sarah.\"", "“요리사와 세라만 데리고 만찬을 차릴 수는 없어요, 여보.”"]], [["It has thus begun, and the husband has declared that he has no such idea.", "이렇게 시작되고, 남편은 자기는 그럴 생각이 없다고 잘라 말한다."], ["\"If Phipps and Dowdney can come here and eat a bit of mutton, they are very welcome; if not, let them stay away.", "“핍스와 다우드니가 이리 와서 양고기 한 점을 먹겠다면야 대환영이고, 아니라면 오지 말라지."], ["And you might as well ask Phipps's sister; just to have some one to go with you into the drawing-room.\"", "그리고 핍스의 누이도 부르는 게 좋겠군. 당신이 응접실로 들어갈 때 같이 갈 사람이 하나는 있어야 할 테니.”"]], [["\"I'd much rather go alone, because then I can read,\"--or sleep, we may say.", "“나는 혼자 가는 편이 훨씬 좋아요. 그래야 책이라도 읽지요.”—아니면 졸기라도 하지요, 라고 우리는 말해도 좋으리라."]], [["But her husband has explained that she would look friendless in this solitary state, and therefore Phipps's sister has been asked.", "그러나 남편은 그렇게 혼자 있으면 벗 하나 없어 보인다고 설명했고, 그리하여 핍스의 누이가 초대되었다."], ["Then the dinner has progressed down to those costly jellies which have been ordered in a last agony.", "그러고 나서 만찬은 마지막 몸부림으로 주문된 그 값비싼 젤리에까지 이르러 버린 것이다."], ["There has been a conviction on the minds of both of them that the simple leg of mutton would have been more jolly for them all.", "그저 양 다리 고기 하나였다면 모두에게 훨씬 흥겨웠으리라는 확신이 두 사람 마음속에 있었다."], ["Had those round balls not been carried about by a hired man; had simple mutton with hot potatoes been handed to Miss Phipps by Sarah, Miss Phipps would not have simpered with such unmeaning stiffness when young Dowdney spoke to her.", "그 둥근 완자들을 삯꾼이 들고 돌아다니지만 않았더라면, 뜨거운 감자를 곁들인 소박한 양고기를 세라가 핍스 양에게 건네주었더라면, 젊은 다우드니가 말을 걸었을 때 핍스 양이 까닭 없이 뻣뻣하게 새침을 떨지는 않았을 것이다. 훨씬 더 흥겨웠을 것이다."], ["They would have been much more jolly.", "“양고기 좀 더 들게, 핍스."], ["\"Have a bit more mutton, Phipps; and where do you like it?\"", "어느 부위가 좋은가?” 얼마나 정겹게 들리는 말인가!"], ["How pleasant it sounds! But we all know that it is impossible.", "그러나 우리 모두는 그것이 이루어질 수 없는 일임을 안다."], ["My young friend had intended this, but his dinner had run itself away to cold round balls and coloured forms from the pastrycook.", "내 젊은 벗은 그렇게 하려고 했으나, 그의 만찬은 저 혼자 달음질쳐서 식어 빠진 둥근 완자와 과자점의 색색 모양물에까지 이르고 말았다."], ["And so it was with the Crosbie marriage.", "크로즈비의 혼례도 그와 같았다."]], [["The bride must leave the church in a properly appointed carriage, and the postboys must have wedding favours.", "신부는 격식을 갖추어 마련한 마차를 타고 교회를 떠나야 하고, 마부들은 혼례 장식을 달아야 한다. 그렇게 일이 커졌다."], ["So the thing grew; not into noble proportions, not into proportions of true glory, justifying the attempt and making good the gala.", "고귀한 규모로도 아니고 참된 영광의 규모로도 아니어서, 그 시도를 정당화하지도 못했고 그 잔치를 값지게 만들지도 못한 채로 말이다."], ["A well-cooked rissole, brought pleasantly to you, is good eating.", "잘 만든 리솔이 기분 좋게 나온다면 그것은 좋은 음식이다."], ["A gala marriage, when everything is in keeping, is excellent sport.", "모든 것이 서로 어울리는 성대한 혼례라면 그것은 훌륭한 구경거리다."], ["Heaven forbid that we should have no gala marriages.", "성대한 혼례가 없는 세상이라니 당치도 않은 일이다."], ["But the small spasmodic attempt, made in opposition to manifest propriety, made with an inner conviction of failure,--that surely should be avoided in marriages, in dinners, and in all affairs of life.", "그러나 누가 보아도 분수에 맞지 않게, 속으로는 실패하리라는 확신을 품은 채 벌이는 자잘한 발작 같은 시도—그것만은 혼례에서도, 만찬에서도, 삶의 모든 일에서도 마땅히 피해야 한다."]], [["There were bridesmaids and there was a breakfast.", "신부 들러리들이 있었고 조찬도 있었다."], ["Both Margaretta and Rosina came up to London for the occasion, as did also a first cousin of theirs, one Miss Gresham, a lady whose father lived in the same county. Mr.", "마거리타와 로지나 둘 다 이 일을 위해 런던으로 올라왔고, 그들의 사촌 하나인 그레셤 양도 함께 왔으니 아버지가 같은 주에 사는 아가씨였다."], ["Gresham had married a sister of Lord De Courcy's, and his services were also called into requisition.", "그레셤 씨는 드 코시 경의 누이와 혼인한 사람이었으므로 그의 수고도 함께 청해졌다."], ["He was brought up to give away the bride, because the earl,--as the paragraph in the newspaper declared,--was confined at Courcy Castle by his old hereditary enemy, the gout.", "그는 신부를 넘겨주는 일을 맡으러 불려 올라왔으니, 백작이—신문의 한 문단이 밝힌 바로는—대대로 내려오는 옛 원수인 통풍 때문에 코시 성에 갇혀 있었기 때문이다."], ["A fourth bridesmaid also was procured, and thus there was a bevy, though not so large a bevy as is now generally thought to be desirable.", "네 번째 신부 들러리도 하나 구해졌고, 그리하여 한 무리가 이루어지기는 했으나 요즈음 대개 바람직하다고들 여기는 만큼 큰 무리는 아니었다."], ["There were only three or four carriages at the church, but even three or four were something.", "교회 앞에 마차는 서너 대뿐이었지만, 서너 대라도 어디인가."], ["The weather was so frightfully cold that the light-coloured silks of the ladies carried with them a show of discomfort.", "날씨가 무섭도록 추워서 부인들의 엷은 빛깔 비단옷은 보기만 해도 불편해 보였다."], ["Girls should be very young to look nice in light dresses on a frosty morning, and the bridesmaids at Lady Alexandrina's wedding were not very young.", "서리 내린 아침에 엷은 옷을 입고 고와 보이려면 아주 젊어야 하는데, 레이디 알렉산드리나의 혼례에 선 들러리들은 그리 젊지 않았다."], ["Lady Rosina's nose was decidedly red.", "레이디 로지나의 코는 대놓고 빨갰다."], ["Lady Margaretta was very wintry, and apparently very cross.", "레이디 마거리타는 몹시 겨울다웠고 보아하니 몹시 심사가 틀어져 있었다."], ["Miss Gresham was dull, tame, and insipid; and the Honourable Miss O'Flaherty, who filled the fourth place, was sulky at finding that she had been invited to take a share in so very lame a performance.", "그레셤 양은 굼뜨고 밋밋하고 심심했으며, 네 번째 자리를 채운 아너러블 오플래허티 양은 이토록 절름거리는 공연에 한몫 끼라고 불려 온 것을 알고는 뾰로통해 있었다."]], [["But the marriage was made good, and Crosbie bore up against his misfortunes like a man.", "그러나 혼례는 무사히 이루어졌고, 크로즈비는 제 불운을 사내답게 견디어 냈다."], ["Montgomerie Dobbs and Fowler Pratt both stood by him, giving him, let us hope, some assurance that he was not absolutely deserted by all the world,--that he had not given himself up, bound hand and foot, to the De Courcys, to be dealt with in all matters as they might please.", "몽고메리 돕스와 파울러 프랫이 둘 다 그의 곁에 서서, 바라건대 그가 온 세상에게서 아주 버림받은 것은 아니라는—손발이 묶인 채 드 코시 집안에 저를 내맡겨 그들이 하고 싶은 대로 모든 일에서 다루어지도록 하지는 않았다는—어떤 믿음을 그에게 주었을 것이다."], ["It was that feeling which had been so grievous to him,--and that other feeling, cognate to it, that if he should ultimately succeed in rebelling against the De Courcys, he would find himself a solitary man.", "그를 그토록 괴롭게 한 것이 바로 그 느낌이었고, 그와 한 짝을 이루는 또 하나의 느낌, 곧 끝내 드 코시 집안에 맞서 반란을 일으키는 데 성공한다 해도 저는 외톨이가 되어 있으리라는 느낌이었다."]], [["\"Yes; I shall go,\"", "“그럼, 나는 갈 걸세.”"], ["Fowler Pratt had said to Montgomerie Dobbs.", "파울러 프랫이 몽고메리 돕스에게 말했다."], ["\"I always stick to a fellow if I can.", "“나는 할 수만 있으면 언제나 벗의 곁을 지키네."], ["Crosbie has behaved like a blackguard, and like a fool also; and he knows that I think so.", "크로즈비는 불한당처럼, 그리고 바보처럼 굴었고, 내가 그렇게 여긴다는 것도 그자가 아네."], ["But I don't see why I should drop him on that account.", "하지만 그렇다고 그를 내버릴 까닭은 없다고 보네."], ["I shall go as he has asked me.\"", "그가 청했으니 나는 갈 걸세.”"]], [["\"So shall I,\"", "“그럼 나도 가야지.”"], ["said Montgomerie Dobbs, who considered that he would be safe in doing whatever Fowler Pratt did, and who remarked to himself that after all Crosbie was marrying the daughter of an earl.", "몽고메리 돕스가 말했다. 파울러 프랫이 하는 대로 하면 안전하리라 여긴 그는, 어쨌거나 크로즈비가 백작의 딸과 혼인하는 것 아니냐고 속으로 되뇌었다."]], [["Then, after the marriage, came the breakfast, at which the countess presided with much noble magnificence.", "혼례가 끝난 뒤에는 조찬이 이어졌고, 백작부인이 자못 고귀한 위엄으로 그 자리를 주재했다."], ["She had not gone to church, thinking, no doubt, that she would be better able to maintain her good humour at the feast, if she did not subject herself to the chance of lumbago in the church.", "그녀는 교회에는 가지 않았는데, 아마도 교회에서 요통에 걸릴 위험을 무릅쓰지 않아야 잔치에서 기분을 잘 지킬 수 있으리라 여겼기 때문일 것이다."], ["At the foot of the table sat Mr. Gresham, her brother-in-law, who had undertaken to give the necessary toast and make the necessary speech.", "식탁 아래쪽 끝에는 그녀의 시매부인 그레셤 씨가 앉았으니, 꼭 필요한 축배와 꼭 필요한 축사를 맡기로 한 사람이었다."], ["The Honourable John was there, saying all manner of ill-natured things about his sister and new brother-in-law, because he had been excluded from his proper position at the foot of the table.", "아너러블 존도 거기 있었고, 식탁 아래쪽 끝의 제 마땅한 자리에서 밀려났다 하여 제 누이와 새 매부를 두고 온갖 심술궂은 말을 늘어놓았다."], ["But Alexandrina had declared that she would not have the matter entrusted to her brother.", "그러나 알렉산드리나는 그 일을 제 오라비에게 맡기지 않겠다고 못 박아 두었더랬다."], ["The Honourable George would not come, because the countess had not asked his wife.", "아너러블 조지는 백작부인이 제 아내를 부르지 않았으므로 오지 않았다."]], [["\"Maria may be slow, and all that sort of thing,\"", "“머라이어가 굼뜨고 어쩌고 하는 것은 그럴지도 모르지.”"], ["George had said; \"but she is my wife.", "조지가 말했다. “하지만 그 사람은 내 아내야."], ["And she had got what they haven't.", "게다가 그 사람한테는 저들한테 없는 것이 있단 말이지."], ["Love me, love my dog, you know.\"", "나를 사랑하거든 내 개도 사랑하라는 말도 있잖나.”"], ["So he had stayed down at Courcy,--very properly as I think.", "그리하여 그는 코시에 그대로 머물렀는데—내 생각에는 아주 옳은 일이었다."]], [["Alexandrina had wished to go away before breakfast, and Crosbie would not have cared how early an escape had been provided for him; but the countess had told her daughter that if she would not wait for the breakfast, there should be no breakfast at all, and in fact no wedding; nothing but a simple marriage.", "알렉산드리나는 조찬 전에 떠나고 싶어 했고, 크로즈비로서도 얼마나 일찍 빠져나갈 길이 마련되든 상관하지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 백작부인은 딸에게, 조찬을 기다리지 않겠다면 조찬도 없고 사실상 혼례도 없다고, 그저 밋밋한 혼인이 있을 뿐이라고 일러 두었다."], ["Had there been a grand party, that going away of the bride and bridegroom might be very well; but the countess felt that on such an occasion as this nothing but the presence of the body of the sacrifice could give any reality to the festivity.", "성대한 잔치가 있었다면 신랑 신부가 먼저 떠나는 것도 아주 좋았으련만, 백작부인은 이번 같은 자리에서는 제물의 몸뚱이가 그 자리에 있어 주는 것 말고는 그 잔치에 무슨 실감을 줄 것이 없다고 느꼈다."], ["So Crosbie and Lady Alexandrina Crosbie heard Mr. Gresham's speech, in which he prophesied for the young couple an amount of happiness and prosperity almost greater than is compatible with the circumstances of humanity.", "그리하여 크로즈비와 레이디 알렉산드리나 크로즈비는 그레셤 씨의 축사를 들었으니, 그는 이 젊은 부부에게 인간의 처지와는 도무지 어울리지 않을 만큼 큰 행복과 번영을 예언했다."], ["His young friend Crosbie, whose acquaintance he had been delighted to make, was well known as one of the rising pillars of the State.", "알게 되어 더없이 기뻤던 저의 젊은 벗 크로즈비는 나라의 떠오르는 기둥 가운데 하나로 널리 알려진 사람이라 했다."], ["Whether his future career might be parliamentary, or devoted to the permanent Civil Service of the country, it would be alike great, noble, and prosperous.", "그의 앞날이 의회에 있든 나라의 상근 공직에 바쳐지든, 그 길은 어느 쪽이나 위대하고 고귀하며 번창하리라는 것이었다."], ["As to his dear niece, who was now filling that position in life which was most beautiful and glorious for a young woman,--she could not have done better.", "그리고 사랑하는 조카딸로 말하자면, 젊은 여자에게 가장 아름답고 영광스러운 자리를 이제 차지하게 되었으니—이보다 더 잘할 수는 없었으리라 했다."], ["She had preferred genius to wealth,--so said Mr. Gresham,--and she would find her fitting reward.", "그 애는 재물보다 재능을 골랐노라고—그레셤 씨는 그렇게 말했다—그러니 그 애는 제게 어울리는 보답을 받게 되리라 했다."], ["As to her finding her fitting reward, whatever her preferences may have been, there Mr. Gresham was no doubt quite right.", "제게 어울리는 보답을 받으리라는 대목만은, 그녀가 무엇을 골랐든 간에 그레셤 씨가 틀림없이 옳았다."], ["On that head I myself have no doubt whatever.", "그 점에 관해서라면 나 자신도 조금도 의심하지 않는다."], ["After that Crosbie returned thanks, making a much better speech than nine men do out of ten on such occasions, and then the thing was over.", "그 뒤에 크로즈비가 답사를 했는데, 그런 자리에서 열 사람 가운데 아홉이 하는 것보다는 훨씬 나은 연설이었다."], ["No other speaking was allowed, and within half an hour from that time, he and his bride were in the post-chaise, being carried away to the Folkestone railway station; for that place had been chosen as the scene of their honeymoon.", "그러고서 일은 끝났다. 다른 연설은 허락되지 않았고, 그때로부터 반 시간이 채 되기 전에 그와 그의 신부는 역마차에 올라 포크스턴으로 가는 철도역으로 실려 갔다."], ["It had been at one time intended that the journey to Folkestone should be made simply as the first stage to Paris, but Paris and all foreign travelling had been given up by degrees.", "신혼여행지로 그곳이 골라졌던 것이다. 한때는 포크스턴까지 가는 길을 그저 파리로 가는 첫 구간으로 삼자는 뜻도 있었으나, 파리도 바깥나라 여행도 차츰차츰 접히고 말았다."]], [["\"I don't care a bit about France,--we have been there so often,\"", "“나는 프랑스는 조금도 상관없어요. 우리가 하도 자주 가 보았잖아요.”"], ["Alexandrina said.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."]], [["She had wished to be taken to Naples, but Crosbie had made her understand at the first whispering of the word, that Naples was quite out of the question.", "그녀는 나폴리에 데려가 달라고 하고 싶어 했으나, 크로즈비는 그 말이 처음 소곤거려졌을 때 이미 나폴리는 어림도 없다는 것을 그녀에게 알아듣게 해 놓았다."], ["He must look now in all things to money.", "이제 그는 모든 일에서 돈을 보아야 했다."], ["From the very first outset of his career he must save a shilling wherever a shilling could be saved.", "앞으로 살아갈 길의 첫걸음부터 한 실링이라도 아낄 수 있는 곳에서는 한 실링을 아껴야 했다."], ["To this view of life no opposition was made by the De Courcy interest.", "이런 삶의 방식에는 드 코시 쪽에서도 아무 반대가 없었다."], ["Lady Amelia had explained to her sister that they ought so to do their honeymooning that it should not cost more than if they began keeping house at once.", "레이디 어밀리아는 동생에게, 신혼여행이라도 당장 살림을 차리는 것보다 더 들지 않게끔 해야 한다고 설명해 두었다."], ["Certain things must be done which, no doubt, were costly in their nature.", "물론 하지 않을 수 없는 일들, 본디 값이 나가는 일들이 있었다."], ["The bride must take with her a well-dressed lady's-maid.", "신부는 옷차림이 반듯한 몸종을 데려가야 한다."], ["The rooms at the Folkestone hotel must be large, and on the first floor.", "포크스턴 호텔의 방은 넓어야 하고 이 층이어야 한다."], ["A carriage must be hired for her use while she remained; but every shilling must be saved the spending of which would not make itself apparent to the outer world.", "머무는 동안 그녀가 쓸 마차도 세내야 한다. 그러나 바깥세상 눈에 띄지 않을 돈이라면 한 실링이라도 아껴야 했다."], ["Oh, deliver us from the poverty of those who, with small means, affect a show of wealth!", "아, 적은 밑천으로 부유한 겉모양을 꾸미는 이들의 그 가난에서 우리를 건져 주소서!"], ["There is no whitening equal to that of sepulchres whited as they are whited!", "회칠한 무덤이 회칠된 그만큼 새하얗게 회칠된 것은 세상에 다시 없으니!"]], [["By the proper administration of a slight bribe Crosbie secured for himself and his wife a compartment in the railway carriage to themselves.", "알맞은 자리에 얼마쯤 뇌물을 찔러 넣은 덕에 크로즈비는 저와 아내가 쓸 열차 칸 한 칸을 통째로 얻어 냈다."], ["And as he seated himself opposite to Alexandrina, having properly tucked her up with all her bright-coloured trappings, he remembered that he had never in truth been alone with her before.", "그리고 알렉산드리나의 맞은편에 앉아 그 화사한 빛깔의 옷가지들을 빠짐없이 여며 준 뒤에, 그는 저 자신이 여태 한 번도 그녀와 진짜로 단둘이 있어 본 적이 없다는 사실을 떠올렸다."], ["He had danced with her frequently, and been left with her for a few minutes between the figures.", "그는 그녀와 자주 춤을 추었고, 춤곡 사이사이 몇 분씩 그녀와 남겨져 있기도 했다."], ["He had flirted with her in crowded drawing-rooms, and had once found a moment at Courcy Castle to tell her that he was willing to marry her in spite of his engagement with Lilian Dale.", "사람들로 붐비는 응접실에서 그녀와 시시덕거리기도 했고, 코시 성에서는 릴리언 데일과 약혼한 몸이면서도 그녀와 혼인할 뜻이 있다고 말할 틈을 한 번 낸 적도 있었다."], ["But he had never walked with her for hours together as he had walked with Lily.", "그러나 릴리와 그랬던 것처럼 몇 시간이고 그녀와 함께 걸어 본 적은 없었다."], ["He had never talked to her about government, and politics, and books, nor had she talked to him of poetry, of religion, and of the little duties and comforts of life.", "그녀에게 정부며 정치며 책 이야기를 해 본 적도 없었고, 그녀가 그에게 시(詩)며 신앙이며 살림살이의 자잘한 의무와 위안을 이야기한 적도 없었다."], ["He had known the Lady Alexandrina for the last six or seven years; but he had never known her,--perhaps never would know her,--as he had learned to know Lily Dale within the space of two months.", "그는 레이디 알렉산드리나를 예닐곱 해 전부터 알고 지냈다. 그러나 두 달 사이에 릴리 데일을 알게 된 것처럼 그녀를 알아 본 적은—아마 앞으로도 결코 알지 못하리라—없었다."]], [["And now that she was his wife, what was he to say to her?", "그리고 이제 그녀가 제 아내가 되었는데, 그는 그녀에게 무슨 말을 해야 한단 말인가?"], ["They two had commenced a partnership which was to make of them for the remaining term of their lives one body and one flesh.", "두 사람은 남은 평생 동안 서로를 한 몸, 한 살로 만들어 갈 동반을 시작한 것이었다."], ["They were to be all-in-all to each other.", "서로에게 전부가 되어야 하는 것이었다."], ["But how was he to begin this all-in-all partnership?", "그런데 이 전부가 되는 동반을 그는 어떻게 시작해야 한단 말인가?"], ["Had the priest, with his blessing, done it so sufficiently that no other doing on Crosbie's own part was necessary?", "사제가 축복과 함께 그 일을 이미 충분히 해 놓아서, 크로즈비 자신이 따로 할 일은 없다는 것인가?"], ["There she was, opposite to him, his very actual wife,--bone of his bone; and what was he to say to her?", "그녀가 저기 그의 맞은편에, 그의 진짜 아내가—그의 뼈 중의 뼈가—앉아 있는데, 그는 그녀에게 무슨 말을 해야 하는가?"], ["As he settled himself on his seat, taking over his own knees a part of a fine fur rug trimmed with scarlet, with which he had covered her other mufflings, he bethought himself how much easier it would have been to talk to Lily.", "그는 제 자리에 몸을 붙이고 앉아, 그녀의 다른 덮개들 위에 이미 덮어 준 진홍색 가장자리를 두른 고운 모피 무릎덮개의 한 자락을 제 무릎에도 끌어 덮으면서, 릴리에게라면 말 붙이기가 얼마나 쉬웠을까 하고 생각했다."], ["And Lily would have been ready with all her ears, and all her mind, and all her wit, to enter quickly upon whatever thoughts had occurred to him.", "게다가 릴리라면 온 귀와 온 마음과 온 재치를 기울여, 그에게 떠오른 어떤 생각에든 지체 없이 뛰어들 채비가 되어 있었을 것이다."], ["In that respect Lily would have been a wife indeed,--a wife that would have transferred herself with quick mental activity into her husband's mental sphere.", "그 점에서라면 릴리야말로 참으로 아내였을 것이다—제 마음을 날쌘 움직임으로 남편의 마음자리에 옮겨 놓는 아내 말이다."], ["Had he begun about his office Lily would have been ready for him, but Alexandrina had never yet asked him a single question about his official life.", "그가 제 관청 이야기를 꺼냈다면 릴리는 채비가 되어 있었을 텐데, 알렉산드리나는 그의 관직 생활에 관해 여태 단 한 마디도 물은 적이 없었다."], ["Had he been prepared with a plan for to-morrow's happiness Lily would have taken it up eagerly, but Alexandrina never cared for such trifles.", "그가 내일의 즐거움을 위한 계획을 갖고 있었다면 릴리는 신이 나서 받아 주었을 텐데, 알렉산드리나는 그런 자잘한 것에는 도무지 마음을 쓰지 않았다."]], [["\"Are you quite comfortable?\"", "“편안하시오?”"], ["he said, at last.", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes, quite, thank you.", "“아, 네, 아주 편안해요, 고마워요."], ["By-the-by, what did you do with my dressing-case?\"", "그런데 참, 내 화장 상자는 어떻게 하셨어요?”"]], [["And that question she did ask with some energy.", "그리고 그 물음만은 그녀도 얼마쯤 열을 내어 물었다."]], [["\"It is under you.", "“당신 밑에 있소."], ["You can have it as foot-stool if you like it.\"", "원하면 발받침으로 써도 되오.”"]], [["\"Oh, no; I should scratch it.", "“어머, 안 돼요. 긁히잖아요."], ["I was afraid that if Hannah had it, it might be lost.\"", "해나가 갖고 있다가 잃어버릴까 봐 걱정이었어요.”"], ["Then again there was silence, and Crosbie again considered as to what he would next say to his wife.", "그러고서 다시 침묵이 흘렀고, 크로즈비는 아내에게 다음으로 무슨 말을 할지 또 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["We all know the advice given us of old as to what we should do under such circumstances; and who can be so thoroughly justified in following that advice as a newly-married husband?", "그런 처지에서 어떻게 해야 하는지에 관해 예로부터 우리에게 주어진 충고를 우리 모두 알고 있거니와, 갓 혼인한 남편만큼 그 충고를 따르는 것이 온전히 정당한 사람이 또 어디 있겠는가?"], ["So he put out his hand for hers and drew her closer to him.", "그리하여 그는 그녀의 손을 잡으려고 제 손을 내밀어 그녀를 제 쪽으로 좀 더 끌어당겼다."]], [["\"Take care of my bonnet,\"", "“내 보닛 조심해요.”"], ["she said, as she felt the motion of the railway carriage when he kissed her.", "그가 입을 맞출 때 열차 칸이 흔들리는 것을 느끼며 그녀가 말했다."], ["I don't think he kissed her again till he had landed her and her bonnet safely at Folkestone.", "내 생각에 그는 그녀와 그녀의 보닛을 포크스턴에 무사히 내려놓을 때까지 다시는 입을 맞추지 않았다."], ["How often would he have kissed Lily, and how pretty would her bonnet have been when she reached the end of her journey, and how delightfully happy would she have looked when she scolded him for bending it!", "릴리였다면 그는 얼마나 자주 입을 맞추었을 것이며, 여정이 끝났을 때 그녀의 보닛은 얼마나 예뻤을 것이며, 보닛을 구부려 놓았다고 그를 나무랄 때 그녀는 또 얼마나 행복해 보였을 것인가!"], ["But Alexandrina was quite in earnest about her bonnet; by far too much in earnest for any appearance of happiness.", "그러나 알렉산드리나는 제 보닛을 두고 아주 진지했다. 무슨 행복해 보이는 구석이 있기에는 너무나도 진지했다."]], [["So he sat without speaking, till the train came to the tunnel.", "그리하여 그는 열차가 터널에 이를 때까지 말없이 앉아 있었다."]], [["\"I do so hate tunnels,\"", "“나는 터널이 정말 싫어요.”"], ["said Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."]], [["He had half intended to put out his hand again, under some mistaken idea that the tunnel afforded him an opportunity.", "그는 터널이 저에게 기회를 준다는 그릇된 생각으로 다시 한번 손을 내밀 마음이 반쯤은 있었다."], ["The whole journey was one long opportunity, had he desired it; but his wife hated tunnels, and so he drew his hand back again.", "마음만 먹었다면 그 여정 전체가 하나의 긴 기회였다. 그러나 아내가 터널을 싫어했으므로 그는 손을 도로 거두었다."], ["Lily's little fingers would have been ready for his touch.", "릴리의 자그마한 손가락들이라면 그의 손길을 맞을 채비가 되어 있었으련만."], ["He thought of this, and could not help thinking of it.", "그는 그것을 생각했고, 생각하지 않을 수가 없었다."]], [["He had The Times newspaper in his dressing-bag.", "그의 세면 가방에는 《타임스》가 들어 있었다."], ["She also had a novel with her.", "그녀도 소설책을 하나 갖고 있었다."], ["Would she be offended if he took out the paper and read it?", "신문을 꺼내 읽으면 그녀가 언짢아할까?"], ["The miles seemed to pass by very slowly, and there was still another hour down to Folkestone.", "거리는 몹시 더디게 줄어드는 듯했고, 포크스턴까지는 아직 한 시간이 더 남아 있었다."], ["He longed for his Times, but resolved at last that he would not read unless she read first.", "그는 제 《타임스》가 몹시 그리웠으나, 마침내 그녀가 먼저 읽지 않는 한 저도 읽지 않겠다고 마음을 굳혔다."], ["She also had remembered her novel; but by nature she was more patient than he, and she thought that on such a journey any reading might perhaps be almost improper.", "그녀도 제 소설책을 떠올리고 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 천성이 그보다 참을성이 있었고, 이런 여정에서 무엇을 읽는다는 것은 어쩌면 온당치 못한 일일지도 모르겠다고 생각했다."], ["So she sat tranquilly, with her eyes fixed on the netting over her husband's head.", "그래서 그녀는 남편의 머리 위 그물 선반에 눈길을 둔 채 얌전히 앉아 있었다."]], [["At last he could stand it no longer, and he dashed off into a conversation, intended to be most affectionate and serious.", "마침내 그는 더 견딜 수 없어, 더없이 다정하고 진지하려는 뜻으로 대화에 뛰어들었다."]], [["\"Alexandrina,\"", "“알렉산드리나.”"], ["he said, and his voice was well-tuned for the tender serious manner, had her ears been alive to such tuning.", "그가 말했다. 그의 목소리는, 그녀의 귀가 그런 가락을 알아들을 줄만 알았더라면, 부드럽고 진지한 태도에 알맞게 잘 골라져 있었다."], ["\"Alexandrina, this is a very important step that you and I have taken to-day.\"", "“알렉산드리나, 당신과 내가 오늘 내디딘 것은 아주 중요한 걸음이오.”"]], [["\"Yes; it is, indeed,\"", "“네, 정말 그래요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"I trust we shall succeed in making each other happy.\"", "“우리가 서로를 행복하게 해 주는 데 성공하기를 바라오.”"]], [["\"Yes; I hope we shall.\"", "“네, 그러기를 바라요.”"]], [["\"If we both think seriously of it, and remember that that is our chief duty, we shall do so.\"", "“우리 둘 다 그것을 진지하게 생각하고, 그것이 우리의 으뜸가는 의무임을 잊지 않는다면 그렇게 될 거요.”"]], [["\"Yes, I suppose we shall.", "“네, 그렇게 되겠지요."], ["I only hope we shan't find the house very cold.", "다만 나는 그 집이 너무 춥지만 않았으면 좋겠어요."], ["It is so new, and I am so subject to colds in my head.", "지은 지 얼마 안 되었잖아요. 게다가 나는 머리에 감기가 잘 드는데."], ["Amelia says we shall find it very cold; but then she was always against our going there.\"", "어밀리아 언니는 그 집이 아주 추울 거라고 하던데요. 하기야 언니는 처음부터 우리가 거기로 가는 것을 반대했지만요.”"]], [["\"The house will do very well,\"", "“집은 아무 문제 없을 거요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["And Alexandrina could perceive that there was something of the master in his tone as he spoke.", "그리고 알렉산드리나는 그렇게 말하는 그의 말투에 무언가 주인 노릇을 하는 기색이 있음을 알아챌 수 있었다."]], [["\"I am only telling you what Amelia said,\"", "“나는 그저 어밀리아 언니가 한 말을 전한 것뿐이에요.”"], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대꾸했다."]], [["Had Lily been his bride, and had he spoken to her of their future life and mutual duties, how she would have kindled to the theme!", "릴리가 그의 신부였고, 그가 그녀에게 두 사람의 앞날과 서로의 의무를 이야기했더라면, 그녀는 그 이야기에 얼마나 활활 타올랐을 것인가!"], ["She would have knelt at his feet on the floor of the carriage, and, looking up into his face, would have promised him to do her best,--her best,--her very best.", "그녀는 열차 칸 바닥에 그의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 있는 힘을 다하겠노라고—있는 힘을, 온 힘을 다하겠노라고 약속했을 것이다."], ["And with what an eagerness of inward resolution would she have determined to keep her promise.", "그리고 그 약속을 지키겠다고 얼마나 간절한 속마음의 결심으로 다짐했을 것인가."], ["He thought of all this now, but he knew that he ought not to think of it.", "그는 이제 그 모든 것을 생각했으나, 그것을 생각해서는 안 된다는 것도 알았다."], ["Then, for some quarter of an hour, he did take out his newspaper, and she, when she saw him do so, did take out her novel.", "그러고서 십오 분쯤 그는 정말로 신문을 꺼내 들었고, 그가 그러는 것을 보자 그녀도 제 소설책을 꺼내 들었다."]], [["He took out his newspaper, but he could not fix his mind upon the politics of the day.", "그는 신문을 꺼내 들었으나 그날의 정치에 마음을 붙일 수가 없었다."], ["Had he not made a terrible mistake?", "그는 무서운 잘못을 저지른 것이 아닌가?"], ["Of what use to him in life would be that thing of a woman that sat opposite to him?", "제 맞은편에 앉은 저 여자라는 물건이 그의 삶에 무슨 쓸모가 있단 말인가?"], ["Had not a great punishment come upon him, and had he not deserved the punishment?", "크나큰 벌이 그에게 내린 것이 아닌가, 그리고 그는 그 벌을 받아 마땅하지 않은가?"], ["In truth, a great punishment had come upon him.", "참으로 크나큰 벌이 그에게 내려 있었다."], ["It was not only that he had married a woman incapable of understanding the higher duties of married life, but that he himself would have been capable of appreciating the value of a woman who did understand them.", "그가 혼인한 여자가 혼인 생활의 더 높은 의무를 이해할 줄 모르는 여자라는 것만이 아니라, 그 자신은 그것을 이해하는 여자의 값어치를 알아볼 줄 아는 사람이었다는 것이 문제였다."], ["He would have been happy with Lily Dale; and therefore we may surmise that his unhappiness with Lady Alexandrina would be the greater.", "그는 릴리 데일과라면 행복했을 것이다. 그러므로 레이디 알렉산드리나와의 불행은 그만큼 더 크리라고 우리는 짐작해도 좋다."], ["There are men who, in marrying such as Lady Alexandrina De Courcy, would get the article best suited to them, as Mortimer Gazebee had done in marrying her sister.", "레이디 알렉산드리나 드 코시 같은 여자와 혼인하면 저에게 가장 잘 맞는 물건을 얻게 되는 사내들도 있으니, 모티머 게이지비가 그녀의 언니와 혼인하면서 그러했다."], ["Miss Griselda Grantly, who had become Lady Dumbello, though somewhat colder and somewhat cleverer than Lady Alexandrina, had been of the same sort.", "레이디 덤벨로가 된 그리젤다 그랜틀리 양도, 레이디 알렉산드리나보다 얼마쯤 더 차갑고 얼마쯤 더 영리하기는 했으나 같은 종류였다."], ["But in marrying her, Lord Dumbello had got the article best suited to him;--if only the ill-natured world would allow him to keep the article.", "그러나 그녀와 혼인함으로써 덤벨로 경은 저에게 가장 잘 맞는 물건을 얻었다—심술궂은 세상이 그가 그 물건을 지니고 있게만 내버려 둔다면 말이다."], ["It was in this that Crosbie's failure had been so grievous,--that he had seen and approved the better course, but had chosen for himself to walk in that which was worse.", "크로즈비의 실패가 그토록 쓰라린 것은 바로 이 점이었으니, 그는 더 나은 길을 보았고 옳다 여겼으면서도 더 나쁜 길로 걷기를 스스로 골랐던 것이다."], ["During that week at Courcy Castle,--the week which he passed there immediately after his second visit to Allington,--he had deliberately made up his mind that he was more fit for the bad course than for the good one.", "코시 성에서 보낸 그 한 주 동안—앨링턴을 두 번째로 다녀온 바로 뒤에 거기서 보낸 그 한 주 동안—그는 저는 좋은 길보다 나쁜 길에 더 맞는 사람이라고 곰곰이 마음을 정했더랬다."], ["The course was now before him, and he had no choice but to walk in it.", "이제 그 길이 그의 앞에 놓여 있었고, 그는 그 길을 걷는 것 말고는 다른 도리가 없었다."]], [["It was very cold when they got to Folkestone, and Lady Alexandrina shivered as she stepped into the private-looking carriage which had been sent to the station for her use.", "포크스턴에 닿았을 때는 몹시 추웠고, 레이디 알렉산드리나는 저를 위해 역으로 보내진, 자가용처럼 보이는 마차에 올라타며 몸을 떨었다."]], [["\"We shall find a good fire in the parlour at the hotel,\"", "“호텔 응접실에는 불이 잘 피워져 있을 거요.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, I hope so,\"", "“아, 그랬으면 좋겠어요.”"], ["said Alexandrina, \"and in the bedroom too.\"", "알렉산드리나가 말했다. “침실에도요.”"]], [["The young husband felt himself to be offended, but he hardly knew why.", "젊은 남편은 저 자신이 언짢아진 것을 느꼈으나, 왜 그런지는 잘 알 수 없었다."], ["He felt himself to be offended, and with difficulty induced himself to go through all those little ceremonies the absence of which would have been remarked by everybody.", "그는 언짢았고, 하지 않으면 누구나 알아차렸을 그 자잘한 예의들을 겨우겨우 치러 냈다."], ["He did his work, however, seeing to all her shawls and wrappings, speaking with good-nature to Hannah, and paying special attention to the dressing-case.", "그러나 어쨌든 그는 제 할 일을 했으니, 그녀의 숄과 덮개들을 두루 살피고, 해나에게 상냥하게 말을 건네고, 화장 상자에는 각별히 마음을 썼다."]], [["\"What time would you like to dine?\"", "“저녁은 몇 시에 들고 싶소?”"], ["he asked, as he prepared to leave her alone with Hannah in the bedroom.", "그녀를 해나와 함께 침실에 두고 나오려 하면서 그가 물었다."]], [["\"Whenever you please; only I should like some tea and bread-and-butter presently.\"", "“당신 좋을 때 아무 때나요. 다만 지금 차와 버터 바른 빵을 좀 먹었으면 해요.”"]], [["Crosbie went into the sitting-room, ordered the tea and bread-and-butter, ordered also the dinner, and then stood himself up with his back to the fire, in order that he might think a little of his future career.", "크로즈비는 거실로 가서 차와 버터 바른 빵을 시키고 저녁 식사도 시킨 다음, 앞날의 제 길을 조금 생각해 보려고 벽난로에 등을 대고 섰다."]], [["He was a man who had long since resolved that his life should be a success.", "그는 제 삶이 성공이어야 한다고 오래전에 마음을 정해 둔 사람이었다."], ["It would seem that all men would so resolve, if the matter were simply one of resolution. But the majority of men, as I take it, make no such resolution, and very many men resolve that they will be unsuccessful.", "마음먹기에 달린 일이라면 누구나 그렇게 마음을 정할 듯싶지만, 내가 보기에 사람들 대다수는 그런 결심을 하지 않으며, 아주 많은 사람은 성공하지 못하겠다고 결심한다."], ["Crosbie, however, had resolved on success, and had done much towards carrying out his purpose.", "그러나 크로즈비는 성공하겠다고 마음을 정했고, 그 뜻을 이루기 위해 많은 것을 해 왔다."], ["He had made a name for himself, and had acquired a certain fame.", "그는 제 이름을 알렸고 어느 만큼의 명성도 얻었다."], ["That, however, was, as he acknowledged to himself, departing from him.", "그러나 그 명성은, 그 자신도 인정하는 바이지만, 저에게서 떠나가고 있었다."], ["He looked the matter straight in the face, and told himself that his fashion must be abandoned; but the office remained to him.", "그는 그 일을 똑바로 마주 보고서, 이제 제 유행은 버려야 한다고 스스로에게 말했다. 그러나 관청은 그에게 남아 있었다."], ["He might still rule over Mr. Optimist, and make a subservient slave of Butterwell.", "그는 아직 옵티미스트 씨 위에 군림할 수 있고 버터웰을 고분고분한 종으로 만들 수 있었다."], ["That must be his line in life now, and to that line he would endeavour to be true.", "이제 그것이 그의 삶의 노선이어야 했고, 그 노선에는 충실하려고 애쓸 참이었다."], ["As to his wife and his home,--he would look to them for his breakfast, and perhaps his dinner.", "아내와 가정으로 말하자면—거기서는 아침을, 그리고 어쩌면 저녁을 구하면 될 것이었다."], ["He would have a comfortable arm-chair, and if Alexandrina should become a mother, he would endeavour to love his children; but above all things he would never think of Lily.", "편안한 안락의자를 하나 두고, 알렉산드리나가 어머니가 되거든 제 아이들을 사랑하려고 애쓸 것이었다. 그러나 무엇보다도 릴리는 결코 생각하지 않을 것이었다."], ["After that he stood and thought of her for half an hour.", "그러고 나서 그는 서서 반 시간 동안 그녀를 생각했다."]], [["\"If you please, sir, my lady wants to know at what time you have ordered dinner.\"", "“죄송합니다요, 나리. 마님께서 저녁을 몇 시로 시키셨는지 여쭈어 오라 하십니다요.”"]], [["\"At seven, Hannah.\"", "“일곱 시일세, 해나.”"]], [["\"My lady says she is very tired, and will lie down till dinner-time.\"", "“마님께서 몹시 고단하시다며 저녁때까지 누워 계시겠답니다요.”"]], [["\"Very well, Hannah.", "“알았네, 해나."], ["I will go into her room when it is time to dress.", "옷 갈아입을 때가 되면 마님 방으로 가겠네."], ["I hope they are making you comfortable downstairs?\"", "아래층에서 자네를 잘 돌봐 주고 있는가?”"]], [["Then Crosbie strolled out on the pier in the dusk of the cold winter evening.", "그러고서 크로즈비는 추운 겨울 저녁의 어스름 속으로 부두를 거닐러 나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVI. JOHN EAMES AT HIS OFFICE.", "# 제46장. 사무국의 존 임스"]], [["Mr. Crosbie and his wife went upon their honeymoon tour to Folkestone in the middle of February, and returned to London about the end of March.", "크로즈비 씨와 그의 아내는 이월 중순에 포크스턴으로 신혼여행을 떠났고, 삼월 말께 런던으로 돌아왔다."], ["Nothing of special moment to the interests of our story occurred during those six weeks, unless the proceedings of the young married couple by the sea-side may be thought to have any special interest.", "그 여섯 주 동안 우리 이야기의 관심사에 특별히 중요한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다—바닷가에서 지낸 그 젊은 부부의 행적에 무슨 각별한 흥미가 있다고 여기지 않는 한은 말이다."], ["With regard to those proceedings I can only say that Crosbie was very glad when they were brought to a close.", "그 행적에 관해 내가 말할 수 있는 것이라고는, 그것이 끝났을 때 크로즈비가 무척 기뻐했다는 것뿐이다."], ["All holiday-making is hard work, but holiday-making with nothing to do is the hardest work of all.", "휴가라는 것은 죄다 고된 일이지만, 할 일이 아무것도 없는 휴가야말로 가장 고된 일이다."], ["At the end of March they went into their new house, and we will hope that Lady Alexandrina did not find it very cold.", "삼월 말에 그들은 새집으로 들어갔는데, 레이디 알렉산드리나가 그 집을 그리 춥게 여기지 않았기를 바라 마지않는다."]], [["During this time Lily's recovery from her illness was being completed.", "그동안 릴리는 병에서 차차 온전히 회복해 가고 있었다."], ["She had no relapse, nor did anything occur to create a new fear on her account.", "재발도 없었고, 그녀에 대해 새로운 두려움을 자아낼 만한 일도 일어나지 않았다."], ["But, nevertheless, Dr. Crofts gave it as his opinion that it would be inexpedient to move her into a fresh house at Lady-day.", "그럼에도 크로프츠 선생은 레이디데이에 그녀를 새집으로 옮기는 것은 온당치 않으리라는 의견을 내놓았다."], ["March is not a kindly month for invalids; and therefore with some regret on the part of Mrs.", "삼월은 병자에게 너그러운 달이 아니다."], ["Dale, with much impatience on that of Bell, and with considerable outspoken remonstrance from Lily herself, the squire was requested to let them remain through the month of April.", "그리하여 데일 부인 쪽에서는 얼마간의 아쉬움을 안고, 벨 쪽에서는 잔뜩 조바심을 내며, 릴리 자신에게서는 적잖이 노골적인 항의를 받으면서, 사월 한 달은 그대로 머물게 해 달라고 지주에게 청하게 되었다."], ["How the squire received this request, and in what way he assented to the doctor's reasoning, will be told in the course of a chapter or two.", "지주가 이 청을 어떻게 받아들였는지, 그리고 어떤 식으로 의사의 논리에 동의했는지는 한두 장 뒤에 이야기하겠다."]], [["In the meantime John Eames had continued his career in London without much immediate satisfaction to himself, or to the lady who boasted to be his heart's chosen queen.", "그러는 동안 존 임스는 런던에서 제 앞길을 이어 가고 있었으나, 자신에게도, 스스로 그의 마음이 고른 여왕이라 뽐내는 그 아가씨에게도 이렇다 할 만족을 주지는 못했다."], ["Miss Amelia Roper, indeed, was becoming very cross, and in her ill-temper was playing a game that was tending to create a frightful amount of hot water in Burton Crescent.", "어밀리아 로퍼 양은 참으로 심사가 사나워져 있었고, 그 심술 속에서 버턴 크레슨트에 무시무시한 분란을 자아낼 만한 놀음을 벌이고 있었다."], ["She was devoting herself to a flirtation with Mr. Cradell, not only under the immediate eyes of Johnny Eames, but also under those of Mrs.", "그녀는 조니 임스의 코앞에서, 게다가 루펙스 부인의 눈앞에서까지 크래들 씨와 시시덕거리는 데 온 힘을 쏟고 있었다."], ["Lupex. John Eames, the blockhead, did not like it.", "이 멍청이 존 임스는 그것이 싫었다."], ["He was above all things anxious to get rid of Amelia and her claims; so anxious, that on certain moody occasions he would threaten himself with diverse tragical terminations to his career in London.", "그는 무엇보다도 어밀리아와 그녀의 요구에서 벗어나기를 간절히 바랐다. 어찌나 간절했던지, 울적한 때면 스스로 런던 생활을 갖가지 비극적인 결말로 끝내겠다고 으르대곤 했다."], ["He would enlist.", "군에 들어가겠다."], ["He would go to Australia.", "오스트레일리아로 가겠다."], ["He would blow out his brains.", "제 머리통을 쏘아 날려 버리겠다."], ["He would have \"an explanation\" with Amelia, tell her that she was a vixen, and proclaim his hatred.", "어밀리아와 “담판”을 지어, 너는 표독스러운 여자라고 말해 주고 제 증오를 선포하겠다."], ["He would rush down to Allington and throw himself in despair at Lily's feet.", "앨링턴으로 달려 내려가 절망에 빠져 릴리의 발치에 몸을 던지겠다."], ["Amelia was the bugbear of his life.", "어밀리아는 그의 인생의 골칫덩이였다."], ["Nevertheless, when she flirted with Cradell, he did not like it, and was ass enough to speak to Cradell about it.", "그런데도 그녀가 크래들과 시시덕거리자 그는 그것이 싫었고, 어리석게도 크래들에게 그 말을 꺼낼 만큼 얼간이였다."]], [["\"Of course I don't care,\"", "“물론 나야 상관없네,”"], ["he said, \"only it seems to me that you are making a fool of yourself.\"", "그가 말했다. “다만 자네가 스스로 바보짓을 하고 있는 것으로 보일 뿐이지.”"]], [["\"I thought you wanted to get rid of her.\"", "“나는 자네가 그 여자를 떼어 내고 싶어 하는 줄 알았는데.”"]], [["\"She's nothing on earth to me; only it does, you know--\"", "“그 여자야 나한테 아무것도 아니지. 다만 그게 말이야, 자네도 알다시피—”"]], [["\"Does do what?\"", "“그게 어떻다는 건가?”"], ["asked Cradell.", "크래들이 물었다."]], [["\"Why, if I was to be fal-lalling with that married woman, you wouldn't like it.", "“아니, 내가 저 유부녀와 시시덕거린다면 자네도 좋아하지 않을 것 아닌가."], ["That's all about it.", "그뿐이야."], ["Do you mean to marry her?\"", "자네 그 여자와 혼인할 셈인가?”"]], [["\"What!--Amelia?\"", "“뭐라고!—어밀리아 말인가?”"]], [["\"Yes; Amelia.\"", "“그래, 어밀리아 말일세.”"]], [["\"Not if I know it.\"", "“내가 알기로는 아니지.”"]], [["\"Then if I were you I would leave her alone.", "“그렇다면 나 같으면 그냥 내버려 두겠네."], ["She's only making a fool of you.\"", "그 여자는 자네를 놀리고 있을 뿐이야.”"]], [["Eames's advice may have been good, and the view taken by him of Amelia's proceedings may have been correct; but as regarded his own part in the affair, he was not wise.", "임스의 충고는 옳았을지 모르고, 어밀리아의 행실에 대한 그의 판단도 정확했을지 모른다. 그러나 이 일에서 제 몫에 관한 한 그는 현명하지 못했다."], ["Miss Roper, no doubt, wished to make him jealous; and she succeeded in the teeth of his aversion to her and of his love elsewhere.", "로퍼 양이 그를 질투하게 만들고 싶어 한 것은 의심할 여지가 없고, 그녀는 그가 자기를 싫어하고 마음이 다른 데 가 있는데도 기어이 그 일을 해냈다."], ["He had no desire to say soft things to Miss Roper.", "그는 로퍼 양에게 다정한 말을 건넬 마음이 조금도 없었다."], ["Miss Roper, with all her skill, could not extract a word pleasantly soft from him once a week.", "로퍼 양은 온갖 재주를 다 부려도 일주일에 한 번 그에게서 살가운 말 한마디를 끌어내지 못했다."], ["But, nevertheless, soft words to her and from her in another quarter made him uneasy.", "그런데도 다른 쪽에서 그녀에게 오가고 그녀에게서 나오는 다정한 말들은 그를 불편하게 했다."], ["Such being the case, must we not acknowledge that John Eames was still floundering in the ignorance of his hobbledehoyhood?", "사정이 이러하니, 존 임스가 아직도 제 풋내기 시절의 어리석음 속에서 허우적거리고 있었다는 것을 우리는 인정해야 하지 않겠는가?"]], [["The Lupexes at this time still held their ground in the Crescent, although repeated warnings to go had been given them.", "이 무렵 루펙스 부부는 나가 달라는 통고를 여러 차례 받고도 여전히 크레슨트에 눌러앉아 있었다."], ["Mrs. Roper, though she constantly spoke of sacrificing all that they owed her, still hankered, with a natural hankering, after her money.", "로퍼 부인은 그들이 제게 진 빚을 죄다 포기하겠다고 늘 말하면서도, 사람이 으레 그러하듯 제 돈에 미련을 버리지 못했다."], ["And as each warning was accompanied by a demand for payment, and usually produced some slight subsidy on account, the thing went on from week to week; and at the beginning of April Mr. and Mrs.", "그리고 통고를 할 때마다 밀린 값을 내라는 요구가 따라붙었고 대개 그 덕에 얼마쯤은 받아 낼 수 있었으므로, 일은 한 주 한 주 그렇게 흘러갔다."], ["Lupex were still boarders at Mrs. Roper's house.", "그리하여 사월 초에도 루펙스 씨 부부는 여전히 로퍼 부인네 하숙인이었다."]], [["Eames had heard nothing from Allington since the time of his Christmas visit, and his subsequent correspondence with Lord De Guest.", "임스는 크리스마스에 다녀온 뒤로, 그리고 그에 이어 드 게스트 경과 편지를 주고받은 뒤로 앨링턴에서 아무 소식도 듣지 못했다."], ["In his letters from his mother he was told that game came frequently from Guestwick Manor, and in this way he knew that he was not forgotten by the earl.", "어머니의 편지로는 게스트윅 저택에서 사냥한 고기가 자주 온다는 것을 알았고, 그것으로 백작이 자기를 잊지 않았다는 것은 알 수 있었다."], ["But of Lily he had heard not a word,--except, indeed, the rumour, which had now become general, that the Dales from the Small House were about to move themselves into Guestwick.", "그러나 릴리에 대해서는 한마디도 듣지 못했다—다만 이제는 널리 퍼진 소문, 곧 작은 집의 데일네가 게스트윅으로 옮겨 갈 참이라는 소문만은 예외였다."], ["When first he learned this he construed the tidings as favourable to himself, thinking that Lily, removed from the grandeur of Allington, might possibly be more easily within his reach; but, latterly, he had given up any such hope as that, and was telling himself that his friend at the Manor had abandoned all idea of making up the marriage.", "처음 그 소식을 들었을 때 그는 그것을 자기에게 이로운 일로 풀이하여, 앨링턴의 그 위엄에서 벗어난 릴리라면 어쩌면 제 손이 좀 더 쉽게 닿는 데 있으리라 생각했다. 그러나 요즈음에 와서는 그런 희망을 아주 버리고, 저택의 벗이 그 혼사를 이루어 줄 생각을 죄다 접었다고 스스로에게 말하고 있었다."], ["Three months had already elapsed since his visit.", "그가 다녀온 지 벌써 석 달이 지났다."], ["Five months had passed since Crosbie had surrendered his claim.", "크로즈비가 제 권리를 내던진 지는 다섯 달이 지났다."], ["Surely such a knave as Crosbie might be forgotten in five months!", "크로즈비 같은 악한쯤이야 다섯 달이면 잊힐 만하지 않은가!"], ["If any steps could have been taken through the squire, surely three months would have sufficed for them!", "지주를 통해 밟을 수 있는 걸음이 있었다면 석 달이면 충분하고도 남았을 것 아닌가!"], ["It was very manifest to him that there was no ground of hope for him at Allington, and it would certainly be well for him to go off to Australia.", "앨링턴에 제 희망의 터전이 없다는 것은 그에게 아주 뚜렷해 보였고, 그렇다면 오스트레일리아로 떠나 버리는 편이 옳을 성싶었다."], ["He would go to Australia, but he would thrash Cradell first for having dared to interfere with Amelia Roper.", "오스트레일리아로 가겠다, 그러나 그 전에 어밀리아 로퍼의 일에 감히 끼어든 죄로 크래들을 두들겨 패 주겠다."], ["That, generally, was the state of his mind during the first week in April.", "사월 첫 주 동안 그의 마음은 대체로 그런 상태였다."]], [["Then there came to him a letter from the earl which instantly effected a great change in all his feelings; which taught him to regard Australia as a dream, and almost put him into a good humour with Cradell.", "그러다가 백작에게서 편지 한 통이 왔고, 그것이 그의 온갖 감정에 대번에 큰 변화를 일으켰다. 오스트레일리아를 한낱 꿈으로 여기게 만들었고, 크래들에게마저 하마터면 좋은 낯을 짓게 할 뻔했다."], ["The earl had by no means lost sight of his friend's interests at Allington; and, moreover, those interests were now backed by an ally who in this matter must be regarded as much more powerful than the earl.", "백작은 앨링턴에서의 제 벗의 이해관계를 결코 놓아 버리지 않았던 것이다. 게다가 그 이해관계에는 이제, 이 일에서 백작보다 훨씬 더 큰 힘을 지녔다고 보아야 할 원군이 붙어 있었다."], ["The squire had given in his consent to the Eames alliance.", "지주가 임스와의 혼사에 동의를 내준 것이다."]], [["The earl's letter was as follows:--", "백작의 편지는 이러했다.—"]], [["Guestwick Manor, April 7, 18--.", "게스트윅 저택, 18--년 4월 7일."]], [["I told you to write to me again, and you haven't done it.", "다시 편지하라고 내가 일렀는데 자네는 그러지 않았네."], ["I saw your mother the other day, or else you might have been dead for anything I knew.", "요전 날 자네 어머니를 만났기에 망정이지, 그러지 않았더라면 자네가 죽었는지 살았는지도 내가 알 길이 없었을 걸세."], ["A young man always ought to write letters when he is told to do so.", "젊은이란 편지하라는 말을 들었으면 언제나 편지를 해야 하는 법이야."]], [["Eames, when he had got so far, felt himself rather aggrieved by this rebuke, knowing that he had abstained from writing to his patron simply from an unwillingness to intrude upon him with his letters.", "임스는 여기까지 읽고서 이 꾸중이 제법 억울하게 느껴졌다. 자기가 후원자에게 편지하지 않은 것은 그저 편지 따위로 그를 성가시게 하고 싶지 않아서였음을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["\"By Jove, I'll write to him every week of his life, till he's sick of me,\"", "“맹세코, 그분이 나한테 진저리를 내실 때까지 매주 편지를 써 드려야지.”"], ["Johnny said to himself when he found himself thus instructed as to a young man's duties.", "젊은이의 도리에 관해 이런 가르침을 받고 나서 조니는 혼자 이렇게 중얼거렸다."]], [["And now I have got to tell you a long story, and I should like it much better if you were down here, so that I might save myself the trouble; but you would think me ill-natured if I were to keep you waiting. I happened to meet Mr.", "이제 자네에게 긴 이야기를 하나 해야겠는데, 자네가 여기 내려와 있다면 훨씬 나았을 걸세."], ["Dale the other day, and he said that he should be very glad if a certain young lady would make up her mind to listen to a certain young friend of mine.", "그러면 나도 이 수고를 덜었을 테니 말이야. 하지만 자네를 기다리게 두면 나를 인정머리 없다 여길 테지."], ["So I asked him what he meant to do about the young lady's fortune, and he declared himself willing to give her a hundred a year during his life, and to settle four thousand pounds upon her after his death.", "요전 날 우연히 데일 씨를 만났는데, 어떤 젊은 아가씨가 내 어떤 젊은 벗의 말에 귀를 기울일 마음만 먹어 준다면 자기로서는 아주 기쁘겠노라고 하더군. 그래서 내가 그 아가씨의 재산은 어찌할 참이냐고 물었더니, 제가 사는 동안은 해마다 백 파운드를 주고 죽은 뒤에는 사천 파운드를 그 아이 앞으로 앉히겠노라고 하데."], ["I said that I would do as much on my part by the young man; but as two hundred a year, with your salary, would hardly give you enough to begin with, I'll make mine a hundred and fifty.", "나도 그 젊은이 쪽에 그만큼은 하겠다고 말했네. 다만 연 이백 파운드에 자네 봉급을 보태 봐야 살림을 시작하기에는 넉넉지 않을 터이니, 내 몫은 백오십으로 하겠네."], ["You'll be getting up in your office soon, and with five hundred a year you ought to be able to get along; especially as you need not insure your life.", "자네도 머잖아 관청에서 올라갈 테고, 연 오백 파운드면 그럭저럭 꾸려 갈 수 있을 게야. 더구나 자네는 생명 보험에 들 필요도 없으니 말일세."], ["I should live somewhere near Bloomsbury Square at first, because I'm told you can get a house for nothing.", "나 같으면 처음에는 블룸즈버리 스퀘어 근처 어디쯤에 살겠네. 거기서는 집을 거저나 다름없이 얻는다고들 하더군."], ["After all, what's fashion worth?", "따지고 보면 유행이라는 게 무슨 값어치가 있겠나?"], ["You can bring your wife down here in the autumn, and have some shooting.", "가을에는 아내를 데리고 이리로 내려와 사냥이나 좀 하게."], ["She won't let you go to sleep under the trees, I'll be bound.", "그 사람이 자네를 나무 밑에서 잠들게 두지는 않을 테니, 내 장담하지."]], [["But you must look after the young lady.", "하지만 그 아가씨는 자네가 알아서 챙겨야 하네."], ["You will understand that no one has said a word to her about it; or, if they have, I don't know it.", "알아 두게, 아무도 그 아이에게 이 일을 말한 사람은 없네. 혹 누가 말했다 해도 나는 모르는 일일세."], ["You'll find the squire on your side, that's all.", "지주가 자네 편이라는 것, 그게 전부야."], ["Couldn't you manage to come down this Easter?", "이번 부활절에 내려올 수는 없겠나?"], ["Tell old Buffle, with my compliments, that I want you.", "늙은 버플에게 내 안부와 함께 자네가 필요하다고 전하게."], ["I'll write to him if you like it.", "자네가 원한다면 내가 그에게 편지를 쓰겠네."], ["I did know him at one time, though I can't say I was ever very fond of him.", "한때 그를 알기는 알았지, 썩 좋아한 적은 한 번도 없었다고 해야겠지만."], ["It stands to reason that you can't get on with Miss Lily without seeing her; unless, indeed, you like better to write to her, which always seems to me to be very poor sort of fun.", "릴리 양을 만나지 않고서야 일이 될 리 없다는 것은 뻔한 이치 아닌가. 하기야 자네가 편지로 하는 편이 더 좋다면 몰라도, 내게는 그런 게 늘 아주 시시한 놀음으로 보인다네."], ["You'd much better come down, and go a-wooing in the regular old-fashioned way.", "내려와서 예로부터 내려온 격식대로 구애를 하는 편이 훨씬 나아."], ["I need not tell you that Lady Julia will be delighted to see you.", "레이디 줄리아가 자네를 보면 기뻐하리라는 말은 새삼 할 것도 없네."], ["You are a prime favourite with her since that affair at the railway station.", "철도역에서의 그 일이 있은 뒤로 자네는 그 사람이 으뜸으로 아끼는 사람이 되었어."], ["She thinks a great deal more about that than she does about the bull.", "그 사람은 황소 일보다 그 일을 훨씬 더 많이 입에 올린다네."]], [["Now, my dear fellow, you know all about it, and I shall take it very much amiss of you if you don't answer my letter soon.", "자, 이 사람아, 이제 자네도 다 알았을 테니, 내 편지에 곧 답을 하지 않으면 나는 자네를 아주 야속하게 여길 걸세."]], [["Your very sincere friend,", "자네의 참으로 진실한 벗,"]], [["When Eames had finished this letter, sitting at his office-desk, his surprise and elation were so great that he hardly knew where he was or what he ought to do.", "임스가 제 사무 책상에 앉아 이 편지를 다 읽었을 때, 그의 놀라움과 들뜬 마음은 어찌나 컸던지 자기가 어디 있는지도, 무엇을 해야 하는지도 도무지 알 수 없을 지경이었다."], ["Could it be the truth that Lily's uncle had not only consented that the match should be made, but that he had also promised to give his niece a considerable fortune?", "릴리의 삼촌이 이 혼사를 이루자는 데 동의했을 뿐 아니라 조카딸에게 상당한 재산까지 주기로 약속했다는 것이 정말일 수 있을까?"], ["For a few minutes it seemed to Johnny as though all obstacles to his happiness were removed, and that there was no impediment between him and an amount of bliss of which he had hitherto hardly dared to dream.", "몇 분 동안 조니에게는 제 행복을 가로막던 온갖 걸림돌이 죄다 치워지고, 자기와, 그때껏 감히 꿈꾸어 본 적도 없던 크기의 복락 사이에 아무 장애도 남아 있지 않은 것만 같았다."], ["Then, when he considered the earl's munificence, he almost cried.", "그러다가 백작의 후한 마음을 헤아려 보고는 하마터면 울 뻔했다."], ["He found that he could not compose his mind to think, or even his hand to write.", "그는 생각을 가다듬을 수도, 편지를 쓰려고 손을 가눌 수도 없음을 깨달았다."], ["He did not know whether it would be right in him to accept such pecuniary liberality from any living man, and almost thought that he should feel himself bound to reject the earl's offer.", "살아 있는 사람에게서 그런 금전의 아량을 받는 것이 옳은 일인지 알 수 없었고, 백작의 제안은 물리쳐야 마땅하지 않을까 하는 생각마저 들었다."], ["As to the squire's money, that he knew he might accept.", "지주의 돈이야 받아도 된다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["All that comes in the shape of a young woman's fortune may be taken by any man.", "젊은 여자의 재산이라는 이름으로 오는 것은 무엇이든 어떤 사내라도 받을 수 있는 법이다."]], [["He would certainly answer the earl's letter, and that at once.", "백작의 편지에는 반드시, 그것도 당장 답을 하리라."], ["He would not leave the office till he had done so.", "답을 쓰기 전에는 사무국을 나서지 않으리라."], ["His friend should have cause to bring no further charge against him of that kind.", "제 벗이 그런 종류의 나무람을 다시는 그에게 던질 일이 없도록 하리라."], ["And then again he reverted to the injustice which had been done to him in the matter of letter-writing--as if that consideration were of moment in such a state of circumstances as was now existing.", "그러고서 그의 생각은 다시 편지 쓰는 일에서 자기가 당한 부당한 처사로 돌아갔다—지금 벌어진 것과 같은 형편에서 그런 것이 대수라도 되는 양 말이다."], ["But at last his thoughts brought themselves to the real question at issue.", "그러나 마침내 그의 생각은 참으로 걸린 문제에 이르렀다."], ["Would Lily Dale accept him?", "릴리 데일이 자기를 받아 줄 것인가?"], ["After all, the realization of his good fortune depended altogether upon her feelings; and, as he remembered this, his mind misgave him sorely.", "따지고 보면 이 행운이 실제로 이루어지느냐는 온전히 그녀의 마음에 달린 일이었고, 그것을 떠올리자 그의 마음은 몹시 켕겼다."], ["It was filled not only with a young lover's ordinary doubts,--with the fear and trembling incidental to the bashfulness of hobbledehoyhood--but with an idea that that affair with Crosbie would still stand in his way.", "그 마음에는 젊은 연인이 으레 품는 의심—풋내기 시절의 수줍음에서 오는 두려움과 떨림—만이 아니라, 크로즈비와의 그 일이 여전히 제 앞을 가로막으리라는 생각까지 가득했다."], ["He did not, perhaps, rightly understand all that Lily had suffered, but he conceived it to be probable that there had been wounds which even the last five months might not yet have cured.", "그는 릴리가 겪은 것을 아마 온전히 이해하지는 못했겠지만, 지난 다섯 달로도 아직 아물지 않은 상처가 있으리라는 짐작은 하고 있었다."], ["Could it be that she would allow him to cure these wounds?", "그 상처를 자기가 아물게 하도록 그녀가 허락해 줄 수 있을까?"], ["As he thought of this he felt almost crushed to the earth by an indomitable bashfulness and conviction of his own unworthiness.", "그것을 생각하자 그는 어찌할 수 없는 수줍음과 제 보잘것없음에 대한 확신에 짓눌려 땅으로 꺼져 드는 듯했다."], ["What had he to offer worthy of the acceptance of such a girl as Lilian Dale?", "릴리언 데일 같은 아가씨가 받아 줄 만한 무엇을 자기가 내놓을 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["I fear that the Crown did not get out of John Eames an adequate return for his salary on that day.", "그날 국왕 정부가 존 임스의 봉급에 걸맞은 대가를 그에게서 받아 내지는 못했으리라고 나는 생각한다."], ["So adequate, however, had been the return given by him for some time past, that promotion was supposed throughout the Income-tax Office to be coming in his way, much to the jealousy of Cradell, Fisher, and others, his immediate compeers and cronies.", "그러나 얼마 전부터 그가 내어 온 대가가 어찌나 걸맞았던지, 소득세 사무국에서는 그의 승진이 눈앞에 왔다는 말이 돌았고, 그 때문에 크래들이며 피셔며 그와 어깨를 나란히 하던 동무들이 적잖이 시샘을 했다."], ["And the place assigned to him by rumour was one which was generally regarded as a perfect Elysium upon earth in the Civil Service world.", "그리고 소문이 그에게 돌려준 자리는 관리 사회에서 지상의 완벽한 엘리시움으로 여겨지는 것이었다. 소문에 따르면 그는 수석 위원의 개인 비서가 될 참이었다."], ["He was, so rumour said, to become private secretary to the First Commissioner.", "그런 변화가 오면 그는 지금 앉아 있는, 융단도 깔리지 않은 커다란 방에서 벗어나게 될 터였다."], ["He would be removed by such a change as this from the large uncarpeted room in which he at present sat; occupying the same desk with another man to whom he had felt himself to be ignominiously bound, as dogs must feel when they are coupled.", "그 방에서 그는 개 두 마리가 한 줄에 묶였을 때 느낄 법한 치욕스러운 결박을 느끼며 다른 사내와 같은 책상을 쓰고 있었다. 이 방은 사무국의 난장판이었다."], ["This room had been the bear-garden of the office. Twelve or fourteen men sat in it.", "열두어 명, 아니 열네 명쯤 되는 사내들이 그 안에 앉아 있었다."], ["Large pewter pots were brought into it daily at one o'clock, giving it an air that was not aristocratic.", "날마다 한 시가 되면 커다란 백랍 술잔들이 들어와, 방에 도무지 점잖다고는 할 수 없는 기색을 입혔다."], ["The senior of the room, one Mr. Love, who was presumed to have it under his immediate dominion, was a clerk of the ancient stamp, dull, heavy, unambitious, living out on the farther side of Islington, and unknown beyond the limits of his office to any of his younger brethren.", "이 방의 고참으로 그 방을 제 손아귀에 두고 있다고들 하는 러브 씨는 옛 본을 딴 서기로, 굼뜨고 둔하고 야심이라고는 없으며, 이즐링턴 저쪽 바깥에 살고, 제 사무실 울타리 밖에서는 젊은 동료 누구에게도 알려진 바가 없는 사람이었다."], ["He was generally regarded as having given a bad tone to the room.", "그가 이 방에 나쁜 기풍을 들여놓았다는 것이 일반의 평이었다."], ["And then the clerks in this room would not unfrequently be blown up,--with very palpable blowings up,--by an official swell, a certain chief clerk, named Kissing, much higher in standing though younger in age than the gentleman of whom we have before spoken.", "게다가 이 방의 서기들은 관청의 한 멋쟁이에게, 곧 방금 말한 그 신사보다 나이는 어리되 지위는 훨씬 높은 키싱이라는 이름의 수석 서기에게 걸핏하면 호되게—그것도 아주 눈에 띄게 호되게—야단을 맞곤 했다."], ["He would hurry in, out of his own neighbouring chamber, with quick step and nose in the air, shuffling in his office slippers, looking on each occasion as though there were some cause to fear that the whole Civil Service were coming to an abrupt termination, and would lay about him with hard words, which some of those in the big room did not find it very easy to bear.", "그는 바로 옆 제 방에서 종종걸음을 치며 코를 치켜들고 사무실 슬리퍼를 끌면서 급히 들어오는데, 그 꼴은 볼 때마다 마치 관리 사회 전체가 당장 결딴날 까닭이라도 있는 듯했다. 그러고는 큰 방의 어떤 이들로서는 견디기 쉽지 않은 모진 말들을 사방에 내리쳤다."], ["His hair was always brushed straight up, his eyes were always very wide open,--and he usually carried a big letter-book with him, keeping in it a certain place with his finger.", "그의 머리털은 늘 곧추 세워 빗어 넘겨져 있었고, 두 눈은 늘 아주 크게 열려 있었다. 그리고 그는 대개 커다란 편지 대장을 들고 다니며 손가락으로 어느 자리를 짚어 두었다."], ["This book was almost too much for his strength, and he would flop it down, now on this man's desk and now on that man's, and in a long career of such floppings had made himself to be very much hated.", "이 책은 그의 힘에 부칠 만큼 무거웠으므로 그는 그것을 이 사람 책상에, 저 사람 책상에 쿵 소리 나게 내려놓곤 했고, 그렇게 오랜 세월 쿵쿵 내려놓은 끝에 스스로를 몹시 미움받는 사람으로 만들어 놓았다."], ["On the score of some old grudge he and Mr.", "무슨 해묵은 원한이 있어 그와 러브 씨는 서로 말을 하지 않았다."], ["Love did not speak to each other; and for this reason, on all occasions of fault-finding, the blown-up young man would refer Mr. Kissing to his enemy.", "그런 까닭에 잘못을 캘 때마다 야단맞는 젊은이는 키싱 씨를 그의 원수에게 떠넘기곤 했다."]], [["\"I know nothing about it,\" Mr.", "“나는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모릅니다,”"], ["Love would say, not lifting his face from his desk for a moment.", "러브 씨는 한순간도 책상에서 얼굴을 들지 않은 채 이렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["\"I shall certainly lay the matter before the Board,\"", "“나는 반드시 이 문제를 위원회에 올리겠소,”"], ["Mr. Kissing would reply, and would then shuffle out of the room with the big book.", "키싱 씨는 이렇게 대꾸하고는 그 큰 책을 들고 방을 끌 듯이 나가곤 했다."]], [["Sometimes Mr. Kissing would lay the matter before the Board, and then he, and Mr.", "이따금 키싱 씨는 정말로 그 문제를 위원회에 올렸고, 그러면 그와 러브 씨와 잘못을 저지른 서기 두엇이 그리로 불려 갔다."], ["Love, and two or three delinquent clerks would be summoned thither.", "그것이 큰일로 번지는 일은 좀처럼 없었다."], ["It seldom led to much.", "잘못한 서기들은 훈계를 들었다."], ["The delinquent clerks would be cautioned. One Commissioner would say a word in private to Mr.", "위원 하나가 러브 씨에게 조용히 한마디 하고, 또 하나가 키싱 씨에게 조용히 한마디 했다."], ["Love, and another a word in private to Mr. Kissing.", "그러고 나서 저희끼리만 남으면 위원들은 조그만 농지거리를 즐겼다."], ["Then, when left alone, the Commissioners would have their little jokes, saying that Kissing, they feared, went by favour; and that Love should still be lord of all.", "아무래도 키싱은 편애로 되는 것 같으니 러브야말로 여전히 만물의 주인이어야 하지 않겠느냐는 것이었다."], ["But these things were done in the mild days, before Sir Raffle Buffle came to the Board.", "그러나 이런 일들은 래플 버플 경이 위원회에 오기 전, 온화하던 시절의 일이었다."]], [["There had been some fun in this at first; but of late John Eames had become tired of it.", "처음에는 이 일에 얼마쯤 재미가 있었다."], ["He disliked Mr. Kissing, and the big book out of which Mr.", "그러나 근래에 와서 존 임스는 그것이 시들해졌다."], ["Kissing was always endeavouring to convict him of some official sin, and had got tired of that joke of setting Kissing and Love by the ears together.", "그는 키싱 씨가 싫었고, 키싱 씨가 늘 그것으로 자기의 무슨 관청 죄악을 잡아내려 드는 그 큰 책도 싫었으며, 키싱과 러브를 서로 물어뜯게 붙여 놓는 그 장난에도 진력이 나 있었다."], ["When the Assistant Secretary first suggested to him that Sir Raffle had an idea of selecting him as private secretary, and when he remembered the cosy little room, all carpeted, with a leathern arm-chair and a separate washing-stand, which in such case would be devoted to his use, and remembered also that he would be put into receipt of an additional hundred a year, and would stand in the way of still better promotion, he was overjoyed.", "부서기관이 처음 그에게 래플 경이 그를 개인 비서로 고를 뜻이 있다고 귀띔했을 때, 그리고 그런 경우에 제 몫으로 돌아올, 융단이 깔리고 가죽 팔걸이의자와 따로 놓인 세면대가 있는 아늑한 작은 방을 떠올렸을 때, 게다가 연 백 파운드가 더 들어오고 그보다 더 나은 승진의 길목에 서게 되리라는 것까지 떠올렸을 때, 그는 뛸 듯이 기뻤다."], ["But there were certain drawbacks.", "그러나 걸리는 것이 몇 가지 있었다."], ["The present private secretary,--who had been private secretary also to the late First Commissioner,--was giving up his Elysium because he could not endure the tones of Sir Raffle's voice.", "지금의 개인 비서는—전임 수석 위원의 개인 비서이기도 했던 사람인데—래플 경의 목소리를 견딜 수 없어서 그 엘리시움을 내놓는 참이었다."], ["It was understood that Sir Raffle required rather more of a private secretary, in the way of obsequious attendance, than was desirable, and Eames almost doubted his own fitness for the place.", "래플 경은 개인 비서에게 굽실굽실 시중드는 일을 온당한 정도보다 훨씬 많이 요구한다는 것이었고, 임스도 제가 그 자리에 맞는지 적잖이 의심스러웠다."]], [["\"And why should he choose me?\"", "“그런데 그분이 왜 저를 고르시는 걸까요?”"], ["he had asked the Assistant Secretary.", "그는 부서기관에게 물었었다."]], [["\"Well, we have talked it over together, and I think that he prefers you to any other that has been named.\"", "“글쎄, 우리끼리 그 일을 의논해 보았는데, 내 생각에 그분은 이름이 오르내린 다른 누구보다 자네를 마음에 두고 계신 것 같네.”"]], [["\"But he was so very hard upon me about the affair at the railway station.\"", "“하지만 철도역에서의 그 일로 저를 그렇게 호되게 다루셨는걸요.”"]], [["\"I think he has heard more about that since; I think that some message has reached him from your friend, Earl De Guest.\"", "“그 뒤에 그 일에 대해 더 들으신 것이 있는 모양이야. 자네 벗인 드 게스트 백작에게서 무슨 전갈이 그분께 갔던 것 같네.”"]], [["\"Oh, indeed!\"", "“아, 그렇군요!”"], ["said Johnny, beginning to comprehend what it was to have an earl for his friend.", "조니는 백작을 벗으로 둔다는 것이 어떤 것인지 비로소 깨닫기 시작하며 말했다."], ["Since his acquaintance with the nobleman had commenced, he had studiously avoided all mention of the earl's name at his office; and yet he received almost daily intimation that the fact was well known there, and not a little considered.", "그 귀족과 알고 지내게 된 뒤로 그는 사무국에서 백작의 이름을 입에 올리는 일을 애써 피해 왔건만, 그 사실이 거기서 아주 잘 알려져 있고 적잖이 무게 있게 받아들여지고 있다는 낌새를 거의 날마다 받고 있었다."]], [["\"But he is so very rough,\"", "“하지만 그분은 너무 거칠지 않습니까,”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"You can put up with that,\"", "“그쯤이야 참을 수 있네,”"], ["said his friend the Assistant Secretary.", "그의 벗인 부서기관이 말했다."], ["\"His bark is worse than his bite, as you know; and then a hundred a year is worth having.\"", "“자네도 알다시피 그분은 짖는 소리가 무는 것보다 사납지. 게다가 연 백 파운드는 탐낼 만한 것이고.”"], ["Eames was at that moment inclined to take a gloomy view of life in general, and was disposed to refuse the place, should it be offered to him.", "그때의 임스는 세상만사를 우울하게 보는 쪽으로 기울어 있었고, 그 자리를 제안받더라도 물리칠 마음이었다."], ["He had not then received the earl's letter; but now, as he sat with that letter open before him, lying in the drawer beneath his desk so that he could still read it as he leaned back in his chair, he was enabled to look at things in general through a different atmosphere.", "그때는 아직 백작의 편지를 받기 전이었다. 그러나 이제, 그 편지를 펼쳐 놓고—책상 밑 서랍에 넣어 두어 의자에 등을 기대고서도 여전히 읽을 수 있게 해 놓고—앉아 있는 지금은, 세상만사를 사뭇 다른 공기 속에서 바라볼 수 있었다."], ["In the first place, Lilian Dale's husband ought to have a room to himself, with a carpet and an arm-chair; and then that additional hundred a year would raise his income at once to the sum as to which the earl had made some sort of stipulation.", "우선 릴리언 데일의 남편이라면 융단과 팔걸이의자가 있는 제 방 하나쯤은 있어야 마땅했다. 그리고 그 연 백 파운드가 더 붙으면 그의 수입은 단번에 백작이 어떤 식으로든 조건 삼았던 그 액수에 이를 터였다."], ["But could he get that leave of absence at Easter?", "그런데 부활절에 그 휴가를 얻을 수 있을까?"], ["If he consented to be Sir Raffle's private secretary, he would make that a part of the bargain.", "래플 경의 개인 비서가 되기로 승낙한다면 그것을 흥정의 한 조건으로 삼으리라."]], [["At this moment the door of the big room was opened, and Mr. Kissing shuffled in with very quick little steps.", "바로 이때 큰 방의 문이 열리더니 키싱 씨가 아주 빠른 종종걸음으로 끌 듯이 들어왔다."], ["He shuffled in, and coming direct up to John's desk, flopped his ledger down upon it before its owner had had time to close the drawer which contained the precious letter.", "그는 끌 듯이 들어와 곧장 존의 책상으로 다가와서는, 그 임자가 그 귀한 편지가 든 서랍을 닫을 겨를도 갖기 전에 장부를 그 위에 쿵 내려놓았다."]], [["\"What have you got in that drawer, Mr. Eames?\"", "“그 서랍에 무엇을 넣어 두었소, 임스 씨?”"]], [["\"A private letter, Mr. Kissing.\"", "“개인 편지입니다, 키싱 씨.”"]], [["\"Oh;--a private letter!\"", "“오—개인 편지라!”"], ["said Mr. Kissing, feeling strongly convinced there was a novel hidden there, but not daring to express his belief.", "키싱 씨는 거기 소설책이 숨겨져 있으리라고 굳게 믿으면서도 감히 그 믿음을 입 밖에 내지는 못한 채 말했다."], ["\"I have been half the morning, Mr. Eames, looking for this letter to the Admiralty, and you've put it under S!\"", "“나는 오늘 아침 내내 이 해군부 편지를 찾았는데, 임스 씨, 당신은 그걸 S 밑에 넣어 두었더군!”"], ["A bystander listening to Mr. Kissing's tone would have been led to believe that the whole Income-tax Office was jeopardized by the terrible iniquity thus disclosed.", "곁에서 키싱 씨의 말투를 듣는 사람이라면 이렇게 드러난 끔찍한 죄악으로 소득세 사무국 전체가 위태로워졌다고 여겼을 것이다."]], [["\"Somerset House,\"", "“서머싯 하우스니까요,”"], ["pleaded Johnny.", "조니가 변명했다."]], [["\"Psha;--Somerset House!", "“쳇—서머싯 하우스라니!"], ["Half the offices in London--\"", "런던의 관청 절반이—”"]], [["\"You'd better ask Mr. Love,\"", "“러브 씨에게 물어보시는 게 좋겠습니다,”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."], ["\"It's all done under his special instructions.\" Mr.", "“그건 다 그분의 특별한 지시대로 한 것이니까요.”"], ["Kissing looked at Mr. Love, and Mr.", "키싱 씨는 러브 씨를 바라보았고, 러브 씨는 제 책상만 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["Love looked steadfastly at his desk. \"Mr.", "“색인에 관한 것은 러브 씨가 다 아십니다,”"], ["Love knows all about the indexing,\" continued Johnny.", "조니가 말을 이었다."], ["\"He's index master general to the department.\"", "“그분이 이 부서의 색인 총감독관이시죠.”"]], [["\"No, I'm not, Mr. Eames,\"", "“나는 아니오, 임스 씨,”"], ["said Mr. Love, who rather liked John Eames, and hated Mr.", "존 임스를 제법 좋아하고 키싱 씨는 진심으로 미워하던 러브 씨가 말했다."], ["Kissing with his whole heart. \"But I believe the indexes, on the whole, are very well done in this room.", "“하지만 대체로 이 방의 색인은 아주 잘 되어 있다고 나는 믿소."], ["Some people don't know how to find letters.\"", "편지를 찾을 줄 모르는 사람들이 있을 뿐이지.”"]], [["\"Mr. Eames,\"", "“임스 씨,”"], ["began Mr. Kissing, still pointing with a finger of bitter reproach to the misused S, and beginning an oration which was intended for the benefit of the whole room, and for the annihilation of old Mr.", "키싱 씨가 잘못 쓰인 그 S를 여전히 쓰디쓴 나무람의 손가락으로 짚은 채, 방 전체를 위하고 늙은 러브 씨를 짓뭉개기 위한 일장 연설을 시작했다."], ["Love, \"if you have yet to learn that the word Admiralty begins with A and not with S, you have much to learn which should have been acquired before you first came into this office.", "“해군부라는 낱말이 S가 아니라 A로 시작한다는 것을 아직도 배워야 한다면, 당신은 이 사무국에 처음 들어오기 전에 익혀 두었어야 할 것들을 아직 많이 배워야 하오. 서머싯 하우스는 부서가 아니오.”"], ["Somerset House is not a department.\"", "그러고서 그는 방 전체를 향해 돌아서서 그 마지막 말을 되풀이했다. 잘 새겨 두면 아주 쓸모가 있으리라는 듯이."], ["Then he turned round to the room at large, and repeated the last words, as though they might become very useful if taken well to heart--\"Is not a department.", "“—부서가 아니오. 재무부는 부서요."], ["The Treasury is a department; the Home Office is a department; the India Board is a department--\"", "내무부도 부서요. 인도청도 부서요—”"]], [["\"No, Mr. Kissing, it isn't,\"", "“그건 아닙니다, 키싱 씨,”"], ["said a young clerk from the other end of the room.", "방 저쪽 끝에 있던 젊은 서기 하나가 말했다."]], [["\"You know very well what I mean, sir.", "“내 말뜻이야 잘 알 것 아니오, 이 사람아."], ["The India Office is a department.\"", "인도부가 부서란 말이오.”"]], [["\"There's no Board, sir.\"", "“청이라는 건 없습니다, 키싱 씨.”"]], [["\"Never mind; but how any gentleman who has been in the service three months,--not to say three years,--can suppose Somerset House to be a department, is beyond my comprehension.", "“그건 아무래도 좋소. 하지만 관직에 석 달을—아니 삼 년이라고까지는 않겠소만—있어 본 신사가 어떻게 서머싯 하우스를 부서라고 여길 수 있는지, 나로서는 도무지 이해할 수가 없소."], ["If you have been improperly instructed--\"", "잘못 배웠다면—”"]], [["\"We shall know all about it another time,\"", "“그건 다음에 다 알게 되겠지요,”"], ["said Eames. \"Mr.", "임스가 말했다."], ["Love will make a memorandum of it.\"", "“러브 씨가 그것을 각서로 적어 두실 겁니다.”"]], [["\"I shan't do anything of the kind,\"", "“나는 그런 짓은 하지 않겠소,”"], ["said Mr. Love.", "러브 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"If you have been wrongly instructed,--\"", "“잘못 배웠다면—”"], ["Mr. Kissing began again, stealing a glance at Mr.", "키싱 씨가 다시 말을 꺼내면서 곁눈으로 러브 씨를 훔쳐보았다."], ["Love as he did so; but at this moment the door was again opened, and a messenger summoned Johnny to the presence of the really great man.", "그러나 바로 그때 문이 다시 열리더니 사환이 조니를 참으로 높으신 어른 앞으로 불렀다."], ["\"Mr. Eames, to wait upon Sir Raffle.\"", "“임스 씨, 래플 경께 대령하시랍니다.”"], ["Upon hearing this Johnny immediately started, and left Mr. Kissing and the big book in possession of his desk.", "이 말을 듣자 조니는 곧바로 일어나, 키싱 씨와 그 큰 책이 제 책상을 차지하도록 남겨 두고 나갔다."], ["How the battle was waged, and how it raged in the large room, we cannot stop to hear, as it is necessary that we should follow our hero into the presence of Sir Raffle Buffle.", "그 싸움이 어떻게 벌어졌으며 큰 방에서 얼마나 사납게 타올랐는지는 여기 머물러 들어 볼 겨를이 없다. 우리는 우리 주인공을 따라 래플 버플 경 앞으로 가야 하기 때문이다."]], [["\"Ah, Eames,--yes,\"", "“아, 임스—그래,”"], ["said Sir Raffle, looking up from his desk when the young man entered; \"just wait half a minute, will you?\"", "젊은이가 들어서자 래플 경이 제 책상에서 얼굴을 들며 말했다. “잠깐만 기다리게, 응?”"], ["And the knight went to work at his papers, as though fearing that any delay in what he was doing might be very prejudicial to the nation at large.", "그러고서 기사는 제가 하는 일이 조금이라도 미뤄지면 온 나라에 큰 해가 되기라도 할까 두려운 사람처럼 서류에 매달렸다."], ["\"Ah, Eames,--well,--yes,\"", "“아, 임스—음—그래,”"], ["he said again, as he pushed away from him, almost with a jerk, the papers on which he had been writing.", "그는 쓰고 있던 서류를 거의 내동댕이치듯 밀쳐 놓으며 다시 말했다."], ["\"They tell me that you know the business of this office pretty well.\"", "“자네가 이 사무국 일을 제법 잘 안다고들 하더군.”"]], [["\"Some of it, sir,\"", "“얼마쯤은 압니다, 위원장님,”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, yes; some of it.", "“그래, 그렇지."], ["But you'll have to understand the whole of it if you come to me.", "얼마쯤은 말이야. 하지만 나한테 오려거든 그 전부를 알아야 하네."], ["And you must be very sharp about it too.", "게다가 아주 빈틈없이 해내야 해."], ["You know that FitzHoward is leaving me?\"", "피츠하워드가 나를 떠나는 건 알고 있나?”"]], [["\"I have heard of it, sir.\"", "“들었습니다, 위원장님.”"]], [["\"A very excellent young man, though perhaps not-- But we won't mind that.", "“아주 훌륭한 젊은이지, 다만 어쩌면 그리—하지만 그 얘기는 접어 두세."], ["The work is a little too much for him, and he's going back into the office.", "일이 그에게는 좀 벅찼던 게야. 그래서 사무국으로 돌아가는 걸세."], ["I believe Lord De Guest is a friend of yours; isn't he?\"", "드 게스트 경이 자네 벗이라던데, 그런가?”"]], [["\"Yes; he is a friend of mine, certainly.", "“예, 분명히 제 벗이십니다."], ["He's been very kind to me.\"", "저에게 아주 잘해 주셨지요.”"]], [["\"Ah, well.", "“아, 그래."], ["I've known the earl for many years,--for very many years; and intimately at one time.", "나도 백작을 여러 해 알았지—아주 여러 해 말이야. 한때는 아주 가깝게 지냈고."], ["Perhaps you may have heard him mention my name?\"", "혹시 그가 내 이름을 입에 올리는 걸 들은 적이 있는가?”"]], [["\"Yes, I have, Sir Raffle.\"", "“예, 들었습니다, 래플 경.”"]], [["\"We were intimate once, but those things go off, you know.", "“한때 우리는 가까웠지. 하지만 그런 건 사그라지는 법 아닌가."], ["He's been the country mouse and I've been the town mouse.", "그는 시골 쥐가 되었고 나는 도시 쥐가 되었지."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하하하!"], ["You may tell him that I say so.", "내가 그러더라고 그에게 전해도 좋네."], ["He won't mind that coming from me.\"", "나한테서 나온 말이라면 그도 싫어하지 않을 게야.”"]], [["\"Oh, no; not at all,\"", "“아, 그럼요. 조금도 싫어하지 않으실 겁니다,”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"Mind you tell him when you see him.", "“보거든 꼭 전하게."], ["The earl is a man for whom I've always had a great respect,--a very great respect,--I may say regard.", "백작은 내가 늘 크게 존경해 온 사람이야—아주 크게 존경해 온—아니, 흠모해 왔다고 해도 좋겠지."], ["And now, Eames, what do you say to taking FitzHoward's place?", "자, 임스, 피츠하워드의 자리를 맡는 것에 대해 자네는 어떻게 생각하나?"], ["The work is hard.", "일은 고되다네."], ["It is fair that I should tell you that.", "그건 내가 말해 주는 것이 옳겠지."], ["The work will, no doubt, be very hard.", "일은 틀림없이 아주 고될 걸세."], ["I take a greater share of what's going than my predecessors have done; and I don't mind telling you that I have been sent here, because a man was wanted who would do that.\"", "나는 내 앞의 사람들보다 돌아가는 일을 더 많이 떠맡고 있고, 그렇게 해 줄 사람이 필요했기 때문에 사람들이 나를 여기로 보냈다는 것을 자네에게 말해도 나는 꺼릴 것이 없네.”"], ["The voice of Sir Raffle, as he continued, became more and more harsh, and Eames began to think how wise FitzHoward had been.", "래플 경의 목소리는 말을 이어 갈수록 점점 더 거슬려졌고, 임스는 피츠하워드가 얼마나 슬기로웠던가를 생각하기 시작했다."], ["\"I mean to do my duty, and I shall expect that my private secretary will do his.", "“나는 내 도리를 다할 참이고, 내 개인 비서도 제 도리를 다하기를 바랄 걸세."], ["But, Mr. Eames, I never forget a man.", "그러나 임스 씨, 나는 사람을 결코 잊지 않네."], ["Whether he be good or bad, I never forget a man.", "좋은 사람이든 나쁜 사람이든 나는 사람을 결코 잊지 않아."], ["You don't dislike late hours, I suppose.\"", "자네는 늦은 시간을 싫어하지는 않겠지?”"]], [["\"Coming late to the office, you mean?", "“사무국에 늦게 나오는 것 말씀입니까?"], ["Oh, no, not in the least.\"", "아, 아닙니다, 조금도 싫어하지 않습니다.”"]], [["\"Staying late,--staying late.", "“늦게까지 남는 것 말일세—늦게까지 남는 것."], ["Six or seven o'clock if necessary,--putting your shoulder to the wheel when the coach gets into the mud.", "필요하면 여섯 시 일곱 시까지도 말이야—마차가 진창에 빠지면 바퀴에 어깨를 대는 것 말일세."], ["That's what I've been doing all my life.", "그게 내가 평생 해 온 일이야."], ["They've known what I am very well.", "사람들은 내가 어떤 사람인지 아주 잘 알고 있었지."], ["They've always kept me for the heavy roads.", "늘 험한 길에는 나를 남겨 두었으니까."], ["If they paid, in the Civil Service, by the hour, I believe I should have drawn a larger income than any man in it.", "관리 사회에서 시간으로 삯을 쳐 주었더라면 나는 그 안의 누구보다도 많은 수입을 끌어냈으리라고 믿네."], ["If you take the vacant chair in the next room you'll find it's no joke.", "옆방의 그 빈자리를 맡고 보면 그것이 결코 우스개가 아님을 알게 될 걸세."], ["It's only fair that I should tell you that.\"", "그것만은 내가 말해 주는 것이 옳겠지.”"]], [["\"I can work as hard as any man,\"", "“저도 남만큼은 열심히 일할 수 있습니다,”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"That's right.", "“그래야지."], ["That's right.", "그래야지."], ["Stick to that and I'll stick to you.", "그 말을 지키게, 그러면 나도 자네를 지켜 주겠네."], ["It will be a great gratification to me to have by me a friend of my old friend De Guest.", "내 오랜 벗 드 게스트의 벗을 곁에 두는 것은 내게 아주 흐뭇한 일일 게야."], ["Tell him I say so.", "그렇게 말하더라고 그에게 전하게."], ["And now you may as well get into harness at once.", "자, 이제 곧바로 멍에를 메는 것이 좋겠군."], ["FitzHoward is there.", "피츠하워드가 저기 있네."], ["You can go in to him, and at half-past four exactly I'll see you both.", "그에게 들어가 보게. 그리고 네 시 반 정각에 내가 자네 둘을 보겠네."], ["I'm very exact, mind,--very;--and therefore you must be exact.\"", "나는 아주 정확한 사람이야, 명심하게—아주 정확하지. 그러니 자네도 정확해야 하네.”"], ["Then Sir Raffle looked as though he desired to be left alone.", "그러고서 래플 경은 혼자 있고 싶어 하는 낯빛을 지었다."]], [["\"Sir Raffle, there's one favour I want to ask of you,\"", "“래플 경, 한 가지 청드릴 것이 있습니다,”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"And what's that?\"", "“그게 뭔가?”"]], [["\"I am most anxious to be absent for a fortnight or three weeks, just at Easter.", "“부활절 무렵에 두 주나 세 주쯤 자리를 비우기를 간절히 바라고 있습니다."], ["I shall want to go in about ten days.\"", "열흘쯤 뒤에 떠나고 싶습니다.”"]], [["\"Absent for three weeks at Easter, when the parliamentary work is beginning!", "“의회 일이 시작되는 부활절에 세 주를 비운다고!"], ["That won't do for a private secretary.\"", "개인 비서로서는 될 일이 아니지.”"]], [["\"But it's very important, Sir Raffle.\"", "“하지만 아주 중요한 일입니다, 래플 경.”"]], [["\"Out of the question, Eames; quite out of the question.\"", "“말도 안 되는 소릴세, 임스. 도무지 말도 안 돼.”"]], [["\"It's almost life and death to me.\"", "“저에게는 거의 죽고 사는 일입니다.”"]], [["\"Almost life and death.", "“거의 죽고 사는 일이라."], ["Why, what are you going to do?\"", "아니, 대체 무엇을 하려는 겐가?”"], ["With all his grandeur and national importance, Sir Raffle would be very curious as to little people.", "그 온갖 위엄과 나라의 중대함을 지녔으면서도 래플 경은 자잘한 사람들의 일에 몹시 궁금해하는 사람이었다."]], [["\"Well, I can't exactly tell you, and I'm not quite sure myself.\"", "“글쎄요, 그건 딱히 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다. 저 자신도 아주 확실하지는 않고요.”"]], [["\"Then don't talk nonsense.", "“그렇다면 허튼소리는 그만두게."], ["It's impossible that I should spare my private secretary just at that time of the year.", "하필 그맘때에 내 개인 비서를 놓아 준다는 건 있을 수 없는 일이야."], ["I couldn't do it.", "나는 그리 못 하네."], ["The service won't admit of it.", "공무가 그것을 허락하지 않아."], ["You're not entitled to leave at that season.", "자네는 그 철에 휴가를 받을 자격이 없네."], ["Private secretaries always take their leave in the autumn.\"", "개인 비서는 언제나 가을에 휴가를 얻는 법이야.”"]], [["\"I should like to be absent in the autumn too, but--\"", "“가을에도 자리를 비우고 싶습니다만—”"]], [["\"It's out of the question, Mr. Eames.\"", "“말도 안 되는 소릴세, 임스 씨.”"]], [["Then John Eames reflected that it behoved him in such an emergency to fire off his big gun.", "그러자 존 임스는 이런 위급한 때에는 제 큰 대포를 쏘아 올리는 것이 마땅하다고 생각했다."], ["He had a great dislike to firing this big gun, but, as he said to himself, there are occasions which make a big gun very necessary.", "그는 이 큰 대포를 쏘는 것이 몹시 싫었지만, 스스로에게 말했듯이 큰 대포가 꼭 필요해지는 때라는 것이 있는 법이었다."], ["\"I got a letter from Lord De Guest this morning, pressing me very much to go to him at Easter.", "“오늘 아침 드 게스트 경에게서 편지를 받았는데, 부활절에 자기에게 와 달라고 몹시 조르십니다."], ["It's about business,\"", "볼일에 관한 일입니다,”"], ["added Johnny.", "조니가 덧붙였다."], ["\"If there was any difficulty, he said, he should write to you.\"", "“어려움이 있거든 자기가 위원장님께 편지를 쓰겠노라고 하셨습니다.”"]], [["\"Write to me,\"", "“나한테 편지를 쓴다고,”"], ["said Sir Raffle, who did not like to be approached too familiarly in his office, even by an earl.", "래플 경이 말했다. 그는 제 사무실에서 백작에게라도 너무 허물없이 다가오는 것은 달가워하지 않는 사람이었다."]], [["\"Of course I shouldn't tell him to do that.", "“물론 그렇게 하시라고 제가 말씀드릴 리는 없습니다."], ["But, Sir Raffle, if I remained out there, in the office,\"", "하지만 래플 경, 제가 저 바깥 사무국에 그대로 남아 있다면,”"], ["and Johnny pointed towards the big room with his head, \"I could choose April for my month.", "조니는 고갯짓으로 큰 방 쪽을 가리켰다, “사월을 제 휴가 달로 고를 수 있습니다."], ["And as the matter is so important to me, and to the earl--\"", "그리고 이 일은 저에게도 백작님께도 아주 중요한 일이니—”"]], [["\"What can it be?\"", "“대체 무슨 일인가?”"], ["said Sir Raffle.", "래플 경이 말했다."]], [["\"It's quite private,\"", "“그건 아주 사사로운 일입니다,”"], ["said John Eames.", "존 임스가 말했다."]], [["Hereupon Sir Raffle became very petulant, feeling that a bargain was being made with him.", "이에 래플 경은 흥정을 당하고 있다는 느낌에 몹시 심사가 뒤틀렸다."], ["This young man would only consent to become his private secretary upon certain terms!", "이 젊은이가 어떤 조건이라야만 제 개인 비서가 되겠다는 것 아닌가!"], ["\"Well, go in to FitzHoward now.", "“자, 이제 피츠하워드에게 들어가 보게."], ["I can't lose all my day in this way.\"", "이런 일로 내 하루를 다 잃을 수는 없네.”"]], [["\"But I shall be able to get away at Easter?\"", "“하지만 부활절에는 떠날 수 있게 되는 것입니까?”"]], [["\"I don't know.", "“모르겠네."], ["We shall see about it.", "두고 봄세."], ["But don't stand talking there now.\"", "하지만 지금은 거기 서서 말을 늘어놓지 말게.”"], ["Then John Eames went into FitzHoward's room, and received that gentleman's congratulations on his appointment.", "그러고서 존 임스는 피츠하워드의 방으로 들어갔고, 그 신사에게서 임명을 축하한다는 인사를 받았다."], ["\"I hope you like being rung for, like a servant, every minute, for he's always ringing that bell.", "“하인처럼 시도 때도 없이 종소리에 불려 다니는 걸 자네가 좋아하기를 바라네. 그분은 노상 그 종을 울려 대시거든."], ["And he'll roar at you till you're deaf.", "그리고 자네 귀가 먹먹해지도록 고함을 지르실 걸세."], ["You must give up all dinner engagements, for though there is not much to do, he'll never let you go.", "만찬 약속은 죄다 포기해야 해. 할 일이 그리 많지도 않은데 자네를 절대 놓아 주지 않으실 테니까."], ["I don't think anybody ever asks him out to dinner, for he likes being here till seven.", "아무도 그분을 만찬에 청하지 않는 것 같더군. 그분은 일곱 시까지 여기 있는 걸 좋아하시니까."], ["And you'll have to write all manner of lies about big people.", "그리고 자네는 높으신 분들에 관해 온갖 거짓말을 다 써야 할 걸세."], ["And, sometimes, when he has sent Rafferty out about his private business, he'll ask you to bring him his shoes.\"", "그리고 이따금 래퍼티를 제 사삿일로 내보내고 나면 자네더러 신발을 가져오라고 하실 게야.”"], ["Now Rafferty was the First Commissioner's messenger.", "그런데 래퍼티는 수석 위원의 사환이었다."]], [["It must be remembered, however, that this little account was given by an outgoing and discomfited private secretary.", "그러나 이 짧은 이야기가 자리에서 밀려나 낙담한 개인 비서의 입에서 나온 것임은 잊지 말아야 한다."], ["\"A man is not asked to bring another man his shoes,\"", "“사람이 남의 신발을 가져오라는 말을 듣게 되는 것은,”"], ["said Eames to himself, \"until he shows himself fit for that sort of business.\"", "임스는 속으로 이렇게 말했다. “스스로 그런 일에 알맞은 사람임을 내보였을 때뿐이지.”"], ["Then he made within his own breast a little resolution about Sir Raffle's shoes.", "그러고서 그는 제 가슴속에서 래플 경의 신발에 관해 조그만 결심을 하나 세웠다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVII. THE NEW PRIVATE SECRETARY.", "# 제47장. 새 개인 비서"]], [["Income-tax Office, April 8, 18--.", "소득세 사무국, 18--년 4월 8일."]], [["I hardly know how to answer your letter, it is so very kind--more than kind.", "백작님의 편지에 어떻게 답을 드려야 할지 저로서는 참으로 모르겠습니다. 너무나 다정하신—다정하다는 말로도 모자란 편지였습니다."], ["And about not writing before,--I must explain that I have not liked to trouble you with letters.", "그리고 진작 글을 올리지 못한 일에 대해서는—편지로 백작님을 번거롭게 해 드리고 싶지 않았다는 것을 말씀드려야겠습니다."], ["I should have seemed to be encroaching if I had written much.", "자주 써 보냈더라면 주제넘게 파고드는 것처럼 보였을 테니까요."], ["Indeed it didn't come from not thinking about you.", "정말이지 백작님을 생각하지 않아서 그런 것은 아니었습니다."], ["And first of all, about the money,--as to your offer, I mean.", "그리고 무엇보다 먼저 돈에 관해서—그러니까 백작님의 제안에 관해서 말씀입니다."], ["I really feel that I do not know what I ought to say to you about it, without appearing to be a simpleton.", "저는 이 일에 대해 어리보기처럼 보이지 않으면서 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 정말로 모르겠습니다."], ["The truth is, I don't know what I ought to do, and can only trust to you not to put me wrong.", "사실을 말씀드리자면 저는 제가 어떻게 해야 옳은지 알지 못하고, 다만 백작님께서 저를 그릇된 길로 이끌지 않으시리라 믿을 수밖에 없습니다."], ["I have an idea that a man ought not to accept a present of money, unless from his father, or somebody like that.", "저에게는 사람이란 제 아버지나 그와 같은 분에게서가 아니라면 돈을 선물로 받아서는 안 된다는 생각이 있습니다."], ["And the sum you mention is so very large that it makes me wish you had not named it.", "게다가 백작님께서 말씀하신 액수는 너무나 커서, 차라리 그 말씀을 꺼내지 않으셨더라면 하는 마음이 들 지경입니다."], ["If you choose to be so generous, would it not be better that you should leave it me in your will?", "그토록 너그러이 베푸실 뜻이시라면, 차라리 유언장에 그것을 저에게 남겨 주시는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까?"]], [["\"So that he might always want me to be dying,\"", "“그러면 저 녀석은 늘 내가 죽어 가기를 바라게 되겠군.”"], ["said Lord De Guest, as he read the letter out loud to his sister.", "드 게스트 경이 누이에게 편지를 소리 내어 읽어 주며 말했다."]], [["\"I'm sure he wouldn't want that,\"", "“그이가 그런 걸 바랄 리가 없지요.”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."], ["\"But you may live for twenty-five years, you know.\"", "“게다가 오라버니는 이십오 년은 더 사실 수도 있잖아요.”"]], [["\"Say fifty,\" said the earl.", "“쉰 해라고 하려무나.”"], ["And then he continued the reading of his letter.", "백작이 말했다. 그러고서 그는 편지 읽기를 이어 갔다."]], [["But all that depends so much upon another person, that it is hardly worth while talking about it.", "그러나 그 모든 것은 다른 한 사람에게 너무나 크게 달려 있어서, 이야기해 보아야 별 소용이 없습니다."], ["Of course I am very much obliged to Mr. Dale,--very much indeed,--and I think that he is behaving very handsomely to his niece.", "물론 저는 데일 씨께 몹시 감사드리고 있습니다—정말이지 몹시요—그리고 그분이 조카딸에게 참으로 후하게 처신하고 계시다고 생각합니다."], ["But whether it will do me any good, that is quite another thing.", "그러나 그것이 저에게 무슨 도움이 될지는 전혀 별개의 일입니다."], ["However, I shall certainly accept your kind invitation for Easter, and find out whether I have a chance or not.", "어쨌든 부활절에 초대해 주신 뜻은 반드시 받들겠고, 저에게 가망이 있는지 없는지 알아보겠습니다."], ["I must tell you that Sir Raffle Buffle has made me his private secretary, by which I get a hundred a year.", "래플 버플 경이 저를 자기 개인 비서로 삼았다는 것을 말씀드려야겠습니다. 그 덕에 연 백 파운드를 더 받게 되었습니다."], ["He says he was a great crony of yours many years ago, and seems to like talking about you very much.", "그분은 여러 해 전에 백작님과 아주 절친한 사이였다고 하면서, 백작님 이야기 하기를 무척 좋아하시는 듯합니다."], ["You will understand what all that means.", "그 모든 것이 무슨 뜻인지는 백작님께서 아실 겁니다."], ["He has sent you ever so many messages, but I don't suppose you will care to get them.", "그분이 백작님께 전해 달라는 말씀을 어찌나 많이 하시던지요. 하지만 백작님께서 그것을 받고 싶어 하시리라고는 생각하지 않습니다."], ["I am to go to him to-morrow, and from all I hear I shall have a hard time of it.", "저는 내일부터 그분께 가야 하는데, 들리는 말로 미루어 보면 어지간히 고생을 하게 될 듯합니다."]], [["\"By George, he will,\"", "“암, 정말 그럴 게다.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"Poor fellow!\"", "“가엾은 녀석!”"]], [["\"But I thought a private secretary never had anything to do,\"", "“하지만 개인 비서란 할 일이 도무지 없는 자리인 줄 알았는데요.”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."]], [["\"I shouldn't like to be private secretary to Sir Raffle, myself.", "“나 같으면 래플 경의 개인 비서 노릇은 하고 싶지 않다."], ["But he's young, and a hundred a year is a great thing.", "하지만 그 애는 젊고, 연 백 파운드는 대단한 것이지."], ["How we all of us used to hate that man.", "우리가 다들 그 사람을 얼마나 미워했던지."], ["His voice sounded like a bell with a crack in it.", "그 목소리는 금이 간 종소리 같았어."], ["We always used to be asking for some one to muffle the Buffle.", "우리는 늘 누가 저 버플에 머플러라도 좀 씌워 주지 않나 하곤 했지."], ["They call him Huffle Scuffle at his office.", "그 사람 사무국에서는 그를 허플 스커플이라고 부른다더구나."], ["Poor Johnny!\"", "가엾은 조니!”"], ["Then he finished the letter:--", "그러고서 그는 편지를 끝까지 읽었다."]], [["I told him that I must have leave of absence at Easter, and he at first declared that it was impossible.", "저는 그분께 부활절에는 반드시 휴가를 얻어야겠다고 말씀드렸고, 그분은 처음에는 그것이 될 수 없는 일이라고 못 박으셨습니다."], ["But I shall carry my point about that.", "그러나 그 일만큼은 제 뜻을 관철하겠습니다."], ["I would not stay away to be made private secretary to the Prime Minister; and yet I almost feel that I might as well stay away for any good that I shall do.", "총리대신의 개인 비서 자리를 준다 해도 저는 내려가는 일을 그만두지는 않겠습니다. 그러면서도 제가 가 보아야 이룰 것이 없으리라 생각하면, 차라리 가지 않는 편이 나을지도 모르겠다는 마음이 거의 들기도 합니다."]], [["Give my kind regards to Lady Julia, and tell her how very much obliged to her I am.", "레이디 줄리아께 안부를 전해 주시고, 제가 그분께 얼마나 큰 은혜를 입고 있는지도 말씀드려 주십시오."], ["I cannot express the gratitude which I owe to you.", "백작님께 진 감사의 마음은 이루 다 표현할 길이 없습니다."], ["But pray believe me, my dear Lord De Guest, always very faithfully yours,", "부디 저를 믿어 주십시오, 친애하는 드 게스트 백작님, 언제나 지극히 충실한 벗으로부터,"]], [["It was late before Eames had finished his letter.", "임스가 편지를 다 쓴 것은 늦은 시각이었다."], ["He had been making himself ready for his exodus from the big room, and preparing his desk and papers for his successor.", "그는 큰 방에서 떠날 채비를 하며 제 책상과 서류를 후임자에게 넘겨줄 수 있도록 정리해 두던 참이었다."], ["About half-past five Cradell came up to him, and suggested that they should walk home together.", "다섯 시 반쯤에 크래들이 그에게 다가와 함께 걸어서 집으로 가자고 했다."]], [["\"What!", "“아니!"], ["you here still?\"", "자네 아직 여기 있었나?”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."], ["\"I thought you always went at four.\"", "“자네는 늘 네 시면 가는 줄 알았는데.”"], ["Cradell had remained, hanging about the office, in order that he might walk home with the new private secretary.", "크래들은 새 개인 비서와 함께 걸어서 집에 가려고 사무실 언저리를 서성이며 남아 있었던 것이다."], ["But Eames did not desire this.", "그러나 임스는 그것을 바라지 않았다."], ["He had much of which he desired to think alone, and would fain have been allowed to walk by himself.", "그에게는 혼자서 생각해 보고 싶은 것이 많았고, 저 혼자 걷도록 내버려 두어 주기를 바라는 마음이 간절했다."]], [["\"Yes; I had things to do.", "“그래, 할 일이 좀 있었네."], ["I say, Johnny, I congratulate you most heartily; I do, indeed.\"", "여보게 조니, 진심으로 축하하네. 정말이야.”"]], [["\"Thank you, old fellow!\"", "“고맙네, 이 사람아!”"]], [["\"It is such a grand thing, you know.", "“대단한 일 아닌가, 그렇지 않나."], ["A hundred a year and all at once!", "연 백 파운드가, 그것도 단번에!"], ["And then such a snug room to yourself,--and that fellow, Kissing, never can come near you.", "게다가 그렇게 아늑한 방을 혼자 쓰게 되고—그 키싱이란 작자는 자네 근처에 얼씬도 못 하게 되지."], ["He has been making himself such a beast all day.", "그자는 오늘 하루 종일 그렇게 짐승처럼 굴었다네."], ["But, Johnny, I always knew you'd come to something more than common.", "하지만 조니, 나는 자네가 언젠가 여느 사람 이상이 되리라는 걸 늘 알고 있었어."], ["I always said so.\"", "늘 그렇게 말했지.”"]], [["\"There's nothing uncommon about this; except that Fitz says that old Huffle Scuffle makes himself uncommon nasty.\"", "“여느 사람 이상이랄 것도 없네. 다만 피츠 말로는 늙은 허플 스커플이 여느 사람 이상으로 고약하게 군다더군.”"]], [["\"Never mind what Fitz says.", "“피츠가 무어라 하든 마음에 두지 말게."], ["It's all jealousy.", "죄다 시샘일 뿐이야."], ["You'll have it all your own way, if you look sharp.", "자네가 눈치껏 하면 죄다 자네 뜻대로 될 걸세."], ["I think you always do have it all your own way.", "내 보기에 자네는 늘 죄다 자네 뜻대로 하지 않던가."], ["Are you nearly ready?\"", "다 됐나?”"]], [["\"Well,--not quite.", "“글쎄—아직 다는 아닐세."], ["Don't wait for me, Caudle.\"", "나를 기다리지 말게, 코들.”"]], [["\"Oh, I'll wait.", "“아, 나는 기다리겠네."], ["I don't mind waiting.", "기다리는 거야 아무렇지도 않아."], ["They'll keep dinner for us if we both stay.", "우리 둘 다 늦으면 저녁을 남겨 둘 걸세."], ["Besides, what matters?", "게다가 그게 뭐 대수인가?"], ["I'd do more than that for you.\"", "자네를 위해서라면 그보다 더한 일도 하겠네.”"]], [["\"I have some idea of working on till eight, and having a chop sent in,\"", "“여덟 시까지 일을 더 하다가 양갈비나 시켜다 먹을까 하네.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."], ["\"Besides--I've got somewhere to call, by myself.\"", "“게다가—혼자서 들러야 할 데가 있어.”"]], [["Then Cradell almost cried.", "그러자 크래들은 하마터면 울 뻔했다."], ["He remained silent for two or three minutes, striving to master his emotion; and at last, when he did speak, had hardly succeeded in doing so.", "그는 두세 분 동안 잠자코 있으면서 제 감정을 억누르려 애썼고, 마침내 입을 열었을 때에도 그것을 제대로 억누르지는 못한 채였다. “아, 조니.”"], ["\"Oh, Johnny,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"I know what that means.", "“그게 무슨 뜻인지 나도 아네."], ["You are going to throw me over because you are getting up in the world.", "자네는 세상에서 올라서게 되었으니 나를 내던지려는 게지."], ["I have always stuck to you, through everything; haven't I?\"", "나는 무슨 일이 있어도 늘 자네 곁에 붙어 있었네. 안 그런가?”"]], [["\"Don't make yourself a fool, Caudle.\"", "“스스로 바보가 되지 말게, 코들.”"]], [["\"Well; so I have.", "“아무렴, 나는 그랬네."], ["And if they had made me private secretary, I should have been just the same to you as ever.", "그리고 그들이 나를 개인 비서로 삼았더라도 나는 자네에게 여전히 예전 그대로였을 걸세."], ["You'd have found no change in me.\"", "자네는 내게서 아무 변화도 찾지 못했을 거야.”"]], [["\"What a goose you are.", "“자네 참 얼간이로군."], ["Do you say I'm changed, because I want to dine in the city?\"", "내가 시내에서 저녁을 먹고 싶어 한다고 해서 내가 변했다는 건가?”"]], [["\"It's all because you don't want to walk home with me, as we used to do.", "“죄다 자네가 예전처럼 나와 함께 걸어서 집에 가고 싶어 하지 않기 때문이지."], ["I'm not such a goose but what I can see.", "내가 아무리 얼간이라도 그쯤은 보인다네."], ["But, Johnny-- I suppose I mustn't call you Johnny, now.\"", "하지만 조니—이제는 자네를 조니라고 불러서는 안 되겠지.”"]], [["\"Don't be such a--con-founded--\"", "“그렇게 빌어—먹을—”"], ["Then Eames got up, and walked about the room.", "그러고서 임스는 일어나 방 안을 이리저리 걸었다."], ["\"Come along,\"", "“가세.”"], ["said he, \"I don't care about staying, and don't mind where I dine.\"", "그가 말했다. “남아 있고 싶지도 않고, 어디서 저녁을 먹든 상관없네.”"], ["And he bustled away with his hat and gloves, hardly giving Cradell time to catch him before he got out into the streets.", "그러고는 모자와 장갑을 챙겨 부산히 나섰으니, 그가 거리로 나가기 전에 크래들이 그를 따라잡을 겨를도 거의 주지 않았다."], ["\"I tell you what it is, Caudle,\"", "“내 말해 두겠는데 말이야, 코들.”"], ["said he, \"all that kind of thing is disgusting.\"", "그가 말했다. “그런 식으로 구는 건 죄다 역겨워.”"]], [["\"But how would you feel,\"", "“하지만 자네라면 어떤 기분이겠나.”"], ["whimpered Cradell, who had never succeeded in putting himself quite on a par with his friend, even in his own estimation, since that glorious victory at the railway station.", "크래들이 훌쩍이며 말했다. 철도역에서의 그 눈부신 승리가 있은 뒤로 그는 제 스스로 매기는 눈으로 보아도 제 벗과 어깨를 나란히 한다고는 도무지 여길 수 없게 되어 있었다."], ["If he could only have thrashed Lupex as Johnny had thrashed Crosbie; then indeed they might have been equal,--a pair of heroes.", "조니가 크로즈비를 두들겨 팬 것처럼 저도 루펙스를 두들겨 팰 수만 있었더라면, 그때야말로 둘은 대등했을 것이다—한 쌍의 영웅으로서."], ["But he had not done so.", "그러나 그는 그렇게 하지 못했다."], ["He had never told himself that he was a coward, but he considered that circumstances had been specially unkind to him.", "그는 스스로에게 제가 겁쟁이라고 말한 적은 한 번도 없었으나, 사정이 유독 제게만 야박했다고 여기고 있었다."], ["\"But how would you feel,\"", "“하지만 자네라면 어떤 기분이겠나.”"], ["he whimpered, \"if the friend whom you liked better than anybody else in the world, turned his back upon you?\"", "그가 훌쩍이며 말했다. “세상 누구보다도 좋아하는 벗이 자네에게 등을 돌린다면 말일세.”"]], [["\"I haven't turned my back upon you; except that I can't get you to walk fast enough.", "“나는 자네에게 등을 돌린 적이 없네. 자네를 좀 빨리 걷게 할 수가 없다는 것만 빼면 말이야."], ["Come along, old fellow, and don't talk confounded nonsense.", "어서 오게, 이 사람아. 그리고 빌어먹을 헛소리 좀 하지 말게."], ["I hate all that kind of thing.", "나는 그런 식으로 구는 건 죄다 질색이야."], ["You never ought to suppose that a man will give himself airs, but wait till he does.", "사람이 으스대리라고 미리 짐작해서는 안 되는 법일세. 정말 으스댈 때까지 기다려야지."], ["I don't believe I shall remain with old Scuffles above a month or two.", "내가 늙은 스커플스 밑에 한두 달 넘게 붙어 있을 것 같지도 않네."], ["From all that I can hear that's as much as any one can bear.\"", "들리는 말을 죄다 모아 보면 누구든 견딜 수 있는 것이 딱 그만큼이라더군.”"]], [["Then Cradell by degrees became happy and cordial, and during the whole walk flattered Eames with all the flattery of which he was master.", "그러자 크래들은 차츰 기분이 좋아지고 다정해져서, 걷는 내내 제가 부릴 줄 아는 온갖 아첨을 다해 임스를 추어올렸다."], ["And Johnny, though he did profess himself to be averse to \"all that kind of thing,\"", "그리고 조니는 “그런 식으로 구는 것은 죄다” 질색이라고 스스로 공언하면서도 아첨에는 마음이 열려 있었다."], ["was nevertheless open to flattery. When Cradell told him that though FitzHoward could not manage the Tartar knight, he might probably do so; he was inclined to believe what Cradell said.", "피츠하워드는 그 타타르 같은 기사 나리를 다루지 못했지만 자네라면 아마 다룰 수 있을 것이라고 크래들이 말하자, 그는 크래들의 말을 믿고 싶은 쪽으로 기울었다."], ["\"And as to getting him his shoes,\"", "“그리고 그분 신발을 갖다 드리는 일로 말하면,”"], ["said Cradell, \"I don't suppose he'd ever think of asking you to do such a thing, unless he was in a very great hurry, or something of that kind.\"", "크래들이 말했다. “몹시 급한 일이 있다든가 그런 때가 아니고서야 그분이 자네더러 그런 일을 하라고 할 생각은 아예 하지 않으실 걸세.”"]], [["\"Look here, Johnny,\"", "“여보게 조니.”"], ["said Cradell, as they got into one of the streets bordering on Burton Crescent, \"you know the last thing in the world I should like to do would be to offend you.\"", "버턴 크레슨트에 잇닿은 거리 하나로 접어들면서 크래들이 말했다. “내가 이 세상에서 가장 하고 싶지 않은 일이 자네 기분을 상하게 하는 것이라는 걸 자네도 알지 않나.”"]], [["\"All right, Caudle,\"", "“알았네, 코들.”"], ["said Eames, going on, whereas his companion had shown a tendency towards stopping.", "임스는 그렇게 말하고 계속 걸었다. 그의 동행은 걸음을 멈추려는 낌새를 보였는데도 말이다."]], [["\"Look here, now; if I have vexed you about Amelia Roper, I'll make you a promise never to speak to her again.\"", "“여보게, 자, 만일 내가 어밀리아 로퍼 일로 자네 속을 뒤집어 놓았다면, 다시는 그 사람에게 말을 걸지 않겠다고 약속하겠네.”"]], [["\"D---- Amelia Roper,\"", "“어밀리아 로퍼 따위 저—주나 받으라지.”"], ["said Eames, suddenly stopping himself and stopping Cradell as well.", "임스가 그렇게 말하며 문득 제 걸음을 멈추고 크래들의 걸음까지 멈추게 했다."], ["The exclamation was made in a deep angry voice which attracted the notice of one or two who were passing.", "그 외침은 낮고 성난 목소리로 터져 나와 지나가던 한둘의 눈길을 끌었다."], ["Johnny was very wrong,--wrong to utter any curse;--very wrong to ejaculate that curse against a human being; and especially wrong to fulminate it against a woman--a woman whom he had professed to love!", "조니는 아주 잘못했다—무릇 저주를 입에 담은 것부터가 잘못이고, 사람을 향해 그 저주를 내뱉은 것은 몹시 잘못이며, 여자를 향해—한때 사랑한다고 공언했던 여자를 향해 그것을 퍼부은 것은 특히나 잘못이었다!"], ["But he did do so, and I cannot tell my story thoroughly without repeating the wicked word.", "그러나 그는 정말로 그렇게 했고, 나는 그 몹쓸 말을 되풀이하지 않고서는 내 이야기를 온전히 들려줄 수 없다."]], [["Cradell looked up at him and stared.", "크래들은 그를 올려다보며 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["\"I only meant to say,\"", "“내 말은 그저.” 크래들이 말했다."], ["said Cradell, \"I'll do anything you like in the matter.\"", "“그 일이라면 자네가 바라는 대로 무엇이든 하겠다는 것이었네.”"]], [["\"Then never mention her name to me again.", "“그럼 다시는 내 앞에서 그 사람 이름을 입에 올리지 말게."], ["And as to talking to her, you may talk to her till you're both blue in the face, if you please.\"", "그리고 그 사람에게 말을 거는 일이라면, 자네만 좋다면 둘 다 얼굴이 새파래지도록 이야기해도 좋아.”"]], [["\"Oh;--I didn't know.", "“아—몰랐네."], ["You didn't seem to like it the other day.\"", "요전 날에는 자네가 그걸 좋아하지 않는 듯싶던데.”"]], [["\"I was a fool the other day,--a confounded fool.", "“요전 날의 나는 바보였네—빌어먹을 바보였어."], ["And so I have been all my life.", "하기야 나는 평생을 그러고 살았지."], ["Amelia Roper!", "어밀리아 로퍼라니!"], ["Look here, Caudle; if she makes up to you this evening, as I've no doubt she will, for she seems to be playing that game constantly now, just let her have her fling.", "여보게 코들, 오늘 저녁에 그 사람이 자네에게 알랑거리거든—틀림없이 그럴 걸세, 요즘 그 놀음을 노상 벌이고 있는 듯하니—그저 제 마음껏 하게 내버려 두게. 나는 신경 쓰지 말고."], ["Never mind me; I'll amuse myself with Mrs. Lupex, or Miss Spruce.\"", "나는 루펙스 부인이나 스프루스 양과 재미나 보겠네.”"]], [["\"But there'll be the deuce to pay with Mrs.", "“하지만 루펙스 부인 쪽에서 난리가 날 걸세."], ["Lupex. She's as cross as possible already whenever Amelia speaks to me.", "어밀리아가 나에게 말을 걸기만 하면 그 사람은 벌써 더할 나위 없이 심통을 부린다네."], ["You don't know what a jealous woman is, Johnny.\"", "자네는 질투하는 여자가 어떤 것인지 모르는 걸세, 조니.”"], ["Cradell had got upon what he considered to be his high ground.", "크래들은 제가 자신 있다고 여기는 자리에 올라선 참이었다."], ["And on that he felt himself equal to any man.", "그리고 그 자리에서라면 그는 누구와 견주어도 제가 대등하다고 느꼈다."], ["It was no doubt true that Eames had thrashed a man, and that he had not; it was true also that Eames had risen to very high place in the social world, having become a private secretary; but for a dangerous, mysterious, overwhelming, life-enveloping intrigue--was not he the acknowledged hero of such an affair?", "임스는 사내 하나를 두들겨 팼고 저는 그러지 못한 것이 틀림없는 사실이었다. 임스가 개인 비서가 되어 세상에서 아주 높은 자리에 올라선 것도 사실이었다."], ["He had paid very dearly, both in pocket and in comfort, for the blessing of Mrs.", "그러나 위험하고 알 수 없고 압도적이며 삶을 통째로 휘감는 밀회로 말하자면—그런 사건의 공인된 주인공이 바로 그가 아니던가?"], ["Lupex's society; but he hardly considered that he had paid too dearly.", "그는 루펙스 부인과 어울리는 축복을 누리느라 주머니로도 편안함으로도 아주 비싼 값을 치렀다."], ["There are certain luxuries which a man will find to be expensive; but, for all that, they may be worth their price.", "그러나 그는 제가 너무 비싼 값을 치렀다고는 좀처럼 여기지 않았다. 사람이 겪어 보면 값이 비싸다는 것을 알게 되는 사치가 있는 법이다."], ["Nevertheless as he went up the steps of Mrs.", "그러나 그렇더라도 그런 것들은 제값을 할 수 있다."], ["Roper's house he made up his mind that he would oblige his friend.", "그럼에도 로퍼 부인네 집 층계를 오르면서 그는 제 벗의 뜻을 들어주기로 마음먹었다."], ["The intrigue might in that way become more mysterious, and more life-enveloping; whereas it would not become more dangerous, seeing that Mr.", "그렇게 하면 그 밀회는 더욱 알 수 없고 더욱 삶을 휘감는 것이 될 터였다."], ["Lupex could hardly find himself to be aggrieved by such a proceeding.", "반면에 더 위험해지지는 않을 것이었으니, 루펙스 씨가 그런 일로 제가 해를 입었다고 여기기는 어려울 것이기 때문이었다."]], [["The whole number of Mrs. Roper's boarders were assembled at dinner that day.", "그날 저녁 식사에는 로퍼 부인네 하숙인이 하나도 빠짐없이 모였다."], ["Mr. Lupex seldom joined that festive board, but on this occasion he was present, appearing from his voice and manner to be in high good-humour.", "루펙스 씨는 그 잔칫상에 좀처럼 끼지 않았으나, 이날만은 자리를 함께했고, 목소리며 몸가짐으로 보아 기분이 아주 좋은 듯했다."], ["Cradell had communicated to the company in the drawing-room the great good fortune which had fallen upon his friend, and Johnny had thereby become the mark of a certain amount of hero-worship.", "크래들이 응접실에서 여러 사람에게 제 벗에게 떨어진 크나큰 행운을 알린 터라, 조니는 그로써 얼마간 영웅 대접의 과녁이 되어 있었다."]], [["\"Oh, indeed!\"", "“아이고, 그러셔요!”"], ["said Mrs. Roper.", "로퍼 부인이 말했다."], ["\"An 'appy woman your mother will be when she hears it.", "“그 소식을 들으시면 댁의 어머님은 참 행복한 분이 되시겠네요."], ["But I always said you'd come down right side uppermost.\"", "하지만 나는 늘 댁이 떨어져도 바른쪽이 위로 오게 떨어질 거라고 말했지요.”"]], [["\"Handsome is as handsome does,\"", "“행실이 고와야 곱다 하는 법이지요.”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Eames!\"", "“어머, 임스 씨!”"], ["exclaimed Mrs.", "루펙스 부인이 우아한 열정을 담아 외쳤다."], ["Lupex, with graceful enthusiasm, \"I wish you joy from the very depth of my heart.", "“제 마음 가장 깊은 곳에서 우러나와 기쁨을 빌어요."], ["It is such an elegant appointment.\"", "참으로 품위 있는 자리로군요.”"]], [["\"Accept the hand of a true and disinterested friend,\"", "“참되고 사심 없는 벗의 손을 받아 주시오.”"], ["said Lupex.", "루펙스가 말했다."], ["And Johnny did accept the hand, though it was very dirty and stained all over with paint.", "그리고 조니는 그 손을 정말로 받아 잡았다. 몹시 더럽고 온통 물감으로 얼룩져 있었는데도 말이다."]], [["Amelia stood apart and conveyed her congratulations by a glance,--or, I might better say, by a series of glances.", "어밀리아는 한쪽에 떨어져 서서 눈짓 하나로 축하를 전했다—아니, 여러 차례 이어진 눈짓으로 전했다고 하는 편이 나을 것이다."], ["\"And now,--now will you not be mine,\"", "“이제는—이제는 제 사람이 되어 주지 않으시겠어요.”"], ["the glances said; \"now that you are rolling in wealth and prosperity?\"", "그 눈짓들은 그렇게 말하고 있었다. “이렇게 부와 번영 속에 뒹굴게 되셨는데요?”"], ["And then before they went downstairs she did whisper one word to him.", "그러고서 아래층으로 내려가기 전에 그녀는 그에게 한마디를 속삭였다."], ["\"Oh, I am so happy, John;--so very happy.\"", "“아, 저는 너무 행복해요, 존—정말 너무 행복해요.”"]], [["\"Bother!\"", "“귀찮게!”"], ["said Johnny, in a tone quite loud enough to reach the lady's ear.", "조니가 그 아가씨의 귀에 넉넉히 닿을 만큼 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["Then making his way round the room, he gave his arm to Miss Spruce.", "그러고는 방을 빙 돌아가 스프루스 양에게 제 팔을 내밀었다."], ["Amelia, as she walked downstairs alone, declared to herself that she would wring his heart.", "어밀리아는 혼자 층계를 내려가면서 저 사람의 가슴을 쥐어짜 놓고야 말겠다고 스스로에게 다짐했다."], ["She had been employed in wringing it for some days past, and had been astonished at her own success.", "그녀는 지난 며칠 동안 그것을 쥐어짜는 일에 매달려 왔고, 제 성공에 스스로도 놀라던 참이었다."], ["It had been clear enough to her that Eames had been piqued by her overtures to Cradell, and she had therefore to play out that game.", "임스가 저의 크래들을 향한 수작에 발끈했다는 것은 그녀에게 넉넉히 분명한 일이었고, 그러니 그 놀음을 끝까지 밀고 나가야 했다."]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Cradell,\"", "“어머, 크래들 씨.”"], ["she said, as she took her seat next to him.", "그의 곁에 자리를 잡으며 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"The friends I like are the friends that remain always the same.", "“제가 좋아하는 벗은 언제나 한결같은 벗이랍니다."], ["I hate your sudden rises.", "저는 벼락출세라는 게 싫어요."], ["They do so often make a man upsetting.\"", "그런 게 사람을 우쭐하게 만드는 일이 너무 잦거든요.”"]], [["\"I should like to try, myself, all the same,\"", "“그래도 나 같으면 한번 겪어 보고 싶은데요.”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."]], [["\"Well, I don't think it would make any difference in you; I don't indeed.", "“글쎄요, 제 생각에는 크래들 씨야 그런다고 달라지실 것 같지 않아요. 정말이지 그래요."], ["And of course your time will come too.", "그리고 물론 크래들 씨에게도 그때가 오겠지요."], ["It's that earl as has done it,--he that was worried by the bull.", "이번 일은 그 백작이라는 분이 해 주신 거예요—황소한테 혼쭐이 났던 그분 말이에요."], ["Since we have known an earl we have been so mighty fine.\"", "우리는 백작 한 분을 알게 된 뒤로 어찌나 대단해졌는지 몰라요.”"], ["And Amelia gave her head a little toss, and then smiled archly, in a manner which, to Cradell's eyes, was really very becoming.", "그러고서 어밀리아는 고개를 살짝 젖혔다가 짓궂게 미소를 지었는데, 크래들의 눈에는 그 모습이 정말이지 아주 잘 어울려 보였다."], ["But he saw that Mrs. Lupex was looking at him from the other side of the table, and he could not quite enjoy the goods which the gods had provided for him.", "그러나 그는 루펙스 부인이 식탁 건너편에서 저를 바라보고 있는 것을 알아차렸고, 그리하여 신들이 저에게 마련해 준 좋은 것들을 온전히 누리지는 못했다."]], [["When the ladies left the dining-room Lupex and the two young men drew their chairs near the fire, and each prepared for himself a moderate potation.", "부인들이 식당을 떠나자 루펙스와 두 젊은이는 의자를 난롯가로 끌어당기고 저마다 알맞은 양의 술을 한 잔씩 탔다."], ["Eames made a little attempt at leaving the room, but he was implored by Lupex with such earnest protestations of friendship to remain, and was so weakly fearful of being charged with giving himself airs, that he did as he was desired.", "임스는 방을 나가려고 조금 애를 써 보았으나, 루펙스가 우정을 어찌나 간곡히 내세우며 남아 달라고 애원하는지, 게다가 으스댄다는 소리를 들을까 하는 두려움에 어찌나 마음이 약해졌는지, 그는 시키는 대로 하고 말았다."]], [["\"And here, Mr. Eames, is to your very good health,\"", "“자, 임스 씨, 그대의 건강을 위하여.”"], ["said Lupex, raising to his mouth a steaming goblet of gin-and-water, \"and wishing you many years to enjoy your official prosperity.\"", "루펙스가 김이 무럭무럭 나는 물 탄 진 잔을 입으로 들어 올리며 말했다. “그리고 그대가 관직의 번영을 오래오래 누리기를 빌면서.”"]], [["\"Thank ye,\"", "“고맙습니다.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."], ["\"I don't know much about the prosperity, but I'm just as much obliged.\"", "“번영이라는 게 어떤 건지야 잘 모르겠습니다만, 아무튼 고맙기는 매한가지입니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, sir; when I see a young man of your age beginning to rise in the world, I know he'll go on.", "“그렇소, 젊은 양반. 그대 나이의 젊은이가 세상에서 올라서기 시작하는 것을 보면 나는 그가 계속 올라가리라는 것을 아오."], ["Now look at me, Mr.", "자, 나를 보시오, 임스 씨."], ["Eames. Mr.", "크래들 씨, 그대의 건강을 위하여."], ["Cradell, here's your very good health, and may all unkindness be drowned in the flowing bowl-- Look at me, Mr.", "그리고 온갖 야박한 마음일랑 이 넘치는 잔 속에 죄다 빠져 죽기를—나를 보시오, 임스 씨. 나는 세상에서 한 번도 올라선 적이 없소."], ["Eames. I've never risen in the world; I've never done any good in the world, and never shall.\"", "나는 세상에서 한 번도 좋은 일을 이룬 적이 없고, 앞으로도 없을 것이오.”"]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Lupex, don't say that.\"", "“아, 루펙스 씨, 그런 말씀 마십시오.”"]], [["\"Ah, but I do say it.", "“아니, 나는 그렇게 말하오."], ["I've always been pulling the devil by the tail, and never yet got as much as a good hold on to that.", "나는 늘 악마의 꼬리를 잡아당기며 살아왔는데, 아직껏 그 꼬리조차 제대로 움켜쥐어 본 적이 없소."], ["And I'll tell you why; I never got a chance when I was young.", "그 까닭을 말해 주리다. 나는 젊은 시절에 기회를 한 번도 얻지 못했소."], ["If I could have got any big fellow, a star, you know, to let me paint his portrait when I was your age,--such a one, let us say, as your friend Sir Raffle--\"", "내가 그대 나이였을 적에 어느 대단한 인물이, 그러니까 별 같은 사람이 제 초상을 그리게 해 주었더라면—이를테면 그대의 벗 래플 경 같은 이 말이오—”"]], [["\"What a star!\"", "“대단한 별도 다 있군요!”"], ["said Cradell.", "크래들이 말했다."]], [["\"Well, I suppose he's pretty much known in the world, isn't he?", "“글쎄, 그 사람도 세상에 어지간히는 이름이 알려져 있지 않소?"], ["Or Lord Derby, or Mr. Spurgeon.", "아니면 더비 경이라든가 스퍼전 씨라든가."], ["You know what I mean.", "내 말뜻을 알 것이오."], ["If I'd got such a chance as that when I was young, I should never have been doing jobs of scene-painting at the minor theatres at so much a square yard.", "젊은 시절에 그런 기회를 얻었더라면 나는 군소 극장에서 평방 야드당 얼마씩 받고 무대 배경이나 그리는 일은 결코 하고 있지 않았을 거요."], ["You've got the chance now, but I never had it.\"", "그대는 지금 그 기회를 얻었지만, 나는 한 번도 얻지 못했소.”"]], [["Whereupon Mr. Lupex finished his first measure of gin-and-water.", "그러고서 루펙스 씨는 첫 잔의 물 탄 진을 비웠다."]], [["\"It's a very queer thing,--life is,\"", "“참으로 야릇한 것이오—인생이라는 것은.”"], ["continued Lupex; and, though he did not at once go to work boldly at the mixing of another glass of toddy, he began gradually, and as if by instinct, to finger the things which would be necessary for that operation.", "루펙스가 말을 이었다. 그리고 그는 대놓고 곧장 토디 한 잔을 더 타는 일에 나서지는 않았으나, 차츰차츰, 마치 본능이 시키는 대로인 양 그 일에 필요할 물건들을 만지작거리기 시작했다."], ["\"A very queer thing.", "“참으로 야릇한 것이지."], ["Now, remember, young gentlemen, I'm not denying that success in life will depend upon good conduct;--of course it does; but, then, how often good conduct comes from success!", "자, 젊은 양반들, 잘 새겨 두시오. 나는 인생의 성공이 올바른 처신에 달렸다는 것을 부정하는 게 아니오—물론 그렇지."], ["Should I have been what I am now, do you suppose, if some big fellow had taken me by the hand when I was struggling to make an artist of myself?", "그러나 그렇다면, 올바른 처신이 성공에서 나오는 일은 또 얼마나 잦은가! 내가 화가가 되어 보겠다고 발버둥 치던 시절에 어느 대단한 사람이 내 손을 잡아 주었더라면, 그대들 생각에 내가 지금 이런 꼴이 되어 있었겠소?"], ["I could have drunk claret and champagne just as well as gin-and-water, and worn ruffles to my shirt as gracefully as many a fellow who used to be very fond of me, and now won't speak to me if he meets me in the streets.", "나도 물 탄 진 못지않게 클라레와 샴페인을 마실 수 있었을 테고, 한때 나를 몹시 좋아했으면서 이제는 거리에서 만나도 말을 걸지 않는 숱한 자들 못지않게 셔츠에 주름 장식을 우아하게 달고 다닐 수 있었을 거요."], ["I never got a chance,--never.\"", "나는 기회를 한 번도 얻지 못했소—한 번도.”"]], [["\"But it's not too late yet, Mr. Lupex,\"", "“하지만 아직 너무 늦지는 않았습니다, 루펙스 씨.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, it is, Eames,--yes, it is.\"", "“아니오, 늦었소, 임스—아니오, 늦었소.”"], ["And now Mr. Lupex had grasped the gin-bottle.", "그리고 이제 루펙스 씨는 진 병을 움켜쥔 참이었다."], ["\"It's too late now.", "“이제는 너무 늦었소."], ["The game's over, and the match is lost.", "판은 끝났고 승부는 졌소."], ["The talent is here.", "재주는 여기 있소."], ["I'm as sure of that now as ever I was.", "나는 그것만은 예나 지금이나 확신하오."], ["I've never doubted my own ability,--never for a moment.", "나는 내 능력을 의심해 본 적이 한 번도 없소—단 한순간도."], ["There are men this very day making a thousand a year off their easels who haven't so good and true an eye in drawing as I have, or so good a feeling in colours.", "바로 오늘날 제 화판으로 연 천 파운드씩 벌어들이는 자들 가운데는 나만큼 그림에 밝고 참된 눈을 가지지도, 나만큼 좋은 색채 감각을 가지지도 못한 자들이 있소."], ["I could name them; only I won't.\"", "이름을 댈 수도 있지만, 대지는 않겠소.”"]], [["\"And why shouldn't you try again?\"", "“그러면 왜 다시 해 보시지 않습니까?”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"If I were to paint the finest piece that ever delighted the eye of man, who would come and look at it?", "“내가 사람의 눈을 즐겁게 한 것 가운데 가장 뛰어난 그림을 그린다 한들, 누가 와서 그것을 보아 주겠소?"], ["Who would have enough belief in me to come as far as this place and see if it were true?", "그것이 정말인지 알아보러 이런 데까지 찾아올 만큼 나를 믿어 줄 사람이 누가 있겠소?"], ["No, Eames; I know my own position and my own ways, and I know my own weakness.", "아니오, 임스. 나는 내 처지와 내 버릇을 알고, 내 약점도 아오."], ["I couldn't do a day's work now, unless I were certain of getting a certain number of shillings at the end of it.", "나는 이제 하루 일이 끝날 때 몇 실링이 손에 들어오리라는 것이 확실하지 않으면 하루치 일도 못 하오."], ["That's what a man comes to when things have gone against him.\"", "일이 죄다 어긋나 버린 사람은 그렇게 되고 마는 법이오.”"]], [["\"But I thought men got lots of money by scene-painting?\"", "“하지만 무대 배경을 그려서 돈을 잔뜩 번다고 알고 있었는데요.”"]], [["\"I don't know what you may call lots, Mr.", "“그대가 무엇을 잔뜩이라고 부르는지는 모르겠소, 크래들 씨."], ["Cradell; I don't call it lots.", "나는 그것을 잔뜩이라고 부르지 않소."], ["But I'm not complaining.", "하지만 나는 푸념하는 게 아니오."], ["I know who I have to thank; and if ever I blow my own brains out I shan't be putting the blame on the wrong shoulders.", "내가 누구에게 고마워해야 하는지는 내가 아오. 그리고 혹시라도 내가 제 머리통을 쏘아 날려 버리는 날이 온다 해도 나는 애먼 사람에게 허물을 씌우지는 않을 것이오."], ["If you'll take my advice,\"--and now he turned round to Eames,--\"you'll beware of marrying too soon in life.\"", "내 충고를 받아들일 생각이 있거든,”—그러고서 그는 임스 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다—“인생에서 너무 일찍 혼인하는 일을 조심하시오.”"]], [["\"I think a man should marry early, if he marries well,\"", "“저는 사람이 잘 혼인하기만 한다면 일찍 혼인해야 한다고 생각합니다.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"Don't misunderstand me,\"", "“내 말을 오해하지 마시오.”"], ["continued Lupex.", "루펙스가 말을 이었다."], ["\"It isn't about Mrs. L.", "“엘 부인 이야기를 하는 게 아니오."], ["I'm speaking. I've always regarded my wife as a very fascinating woman.\"", "나는 언제나 내 아내를 아주 매혹적인 여자로 여겨 왔소.”"]], [["\"Hear, hear, hear!\"", "“옳소, 옳소, 옳소!”"], ["said Cradell, thumping the table.", "크래들이 식탁을 두드리며 말했다."]], [["\"Indeed she is,\"", "“정말 그렇습니다.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"And when I caution you against marrying, don't you misunderstand me.", "“그리고 내가 그대에게 혼인을 조심하라고 이르더라도 오해하지는 마시오."], ["I've never said a word against her to any man, and never will.", "나는 어떤 사람에게도 그이를 헐뜯는 말을 한마디도 한 적이 없고 앞으로도 하지 않을 것이오."], ["If a man don't stand by his wife, whom will he stand by?", "제 아내 편에 서지 않는 사내가 대체 누구 편에 서겠소?"], ["I blame no one but myself.", "나는 나 자신 말고는 누구도 탓하지 않소."], ["But I do say this; I never had a chance;--I never had a chance;--never had a chance.\"", "그러나 이 말만은 하겠소. 나는 기회를 얻지 못했소—나는 기회를 얻지 못했소—기회를 얻지 못했소.”"], ["And as he repeated the words for the third time, his lips were already fixed to the rim of his tumbler.", "그리고 그 말을 세 번째로 되뇔 때, 그의 입술은 이미 술잔 가장자리에 붙어 있었다."]], [["At this moment the door of the dining-room was opened, and Mrs. Lupex put in her head.", "바로 이때 식당 문이 열리고 루펙스 부인이 고개를 들이밀었다."]], [["\"Lupex,\"", "“루펙스.”"], ["she said, \"what are you doing?\"", "그녀가 말했다. “거기서 무얼 하고 계세요?”"]], [["\"Yes, my dear.", "“그래, 여보."], ["I can't say I'm doing anything at the present moment.", "지금 이 순간에 무얼 하고 있다고는 못 하겠구려."], ["I was giving a little advice to these young gentlemen.\"", "이 젊은 양반들에게 조그만 충고를 좀 해 주던 참이오.”"]], [["\"Mr. Cradell, I wonder at you.", "“크래들 씨, 어이가 없군요."], ["And, Mr. Eames, I wonder at you, too,--in your position!", "그리고 임스 씨, 임스 씨도 어이가 없어요—그것도 그런 자리에 오르신 분이!"], ["Lupex, come upstairs at once.\"", "루펙스, 당장 위층으로 올라오세요.”"], ["She then stepped into the room and secured the gin-bottle.", "그러고서 그녀는 방 안으로 들어와 진 병을 확보했다."]], [["\"Oh, Mr. Cradell, do come here,\"", "“어머, 크래들 씨, 이리로 오세요.”"], ["said Amelia, in her liveliest tone, as soon as the men made their appearance above.", "사내들이 위층에 모습을 드러내자마자 어밀리아가 더없이 활기찬 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"I've been waiting for you this half-hour.", "“반 시간이나 기다렸답니다."], ["I've got such a puzzle for you.\"", "크래들 씨께 낼 수수께끼가 하나 있거든요.”"], ["And she made way for him to a chair which was between herself and the wall.", "그러고서 그녀는 그가 제 자리와 벽 사이에 있는 의자로 가도록 길을 내주었다."], ["Cradell looked half afraid of his fortunes as he took the proffered seat; but he did take it, and was soon secured from any positive physical attack by the strength and breadth of Miss Roper's crinoline.", "크래들은 권해진 자리에 앉으면서 제 앞날이 반쯤은 두려운 낯빛이었으나, 그래도 앉기는 앉았고, 이내 로퍼 양의 크리놀린이 지닌 굳건함과 너비 덕분에 어떤 실제적인 물리적 공격으로부터도 안전해졌다."]], [["\"Dear me!", "“어머나!"], ["Here's a change,\"", "이거 참 달라졌군요.”"], ["said Mrs. Lupex, out loud.", "루펙스 부인이 큰 소리로 말했다."]], [["Johnny Eames was standing close, and whispered into her ear, \"Changes are so pleasant sometimes!", "조니 임스는 가까이 서 있다가 그녀의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. “변화란 때로 참 즐거운 것이지요!"], ["Don't you think so?", "그렇게 생각하지 않으세요?"], ["I do.\"", "저는 그렇습니다.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLVIII. NEMESIS.", "# 제48장. 응보"]], [["Crosbie had now settled down to the calm realities of married life, and was beginning to think that the odium was dying away which for a week or two had attached itself to him, partly on account of his usage of Miss Dale, but more strongly in consequence of the thrashing which he had received from John Eames.", "크로즈비는 이제 결혼 생활의 잔잔한 현실에 자리를 잡았고, 한두 주 동안 자기에게 들러붙어 있던 미움도 차츰 사그라지고 있다고 여기기 시작했다. 그 미움은 얼마간은 그가 데일 양을 대접한 방식 탓이었지만, 그보다 더 크게는 그가 존 임스에게 두들겨 맞은 일 탓이었다."], ["Not that he had in any way recovered his former tone of life, or that he ever hoped to do so.", "그렇다고 그가 예전의 삶의 가락을 조금이라도 되찾았다거나, 되찾으리라 기대했다는 말은 아니다."], ["But he was able to go in and out of his club without embarrassment.", "그러나 그는 이제 겸연쩍은 기색 없이 클럽을 드나들 수 있었다."], ["He could talk with his wonted voice, and act with his wonted authority at his office.", "여느 때의 목소리로 이야기할 수 있었고, 사무국에서는 여느 때의 권위로 일을 처리할 수 있었다."], ["He could tell his friends, with some little degree of pleasure in the sound, that Lady Alexandrina would be very happy to see them.", "그는 벗들에게, 그 소리를 내는 데서 얼마쯤 즐거움을 느끼며, 레이디 알렉산드리나가 여러분을 뵈면 무척 기뻐할 것이라고 말할 수 있었다."], ["And he could make himself comfortable in his own chair after dinner, with his slippers and his newspaper.", "그리고 만찬을 마친 뒤에는 실내화를 신고 신문을 든 채 제 의자에 편안히 앉아 있을 수 있었다."], ["He could make himself comfortable, or at any rate could tell his wife that he did so.", "편안히 있을 수 있었다 — 적어도 아내에게 편안하다고 말할 수는 있었다."]], [["It was very dull.", "몹시 따분했다."], ["He was obliged to acknowledge to himself, when he thought over the subject, that the life which he was leading was dull.", "그 문제를 곰곰이 생각해 볼 때면 그는 자기가 꾸려 가는 삶이 따분하다는 것을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Though he could go into his club without annoyance, nobody there ever thought of asking him to join them at dinner.", "성가신 일 없이 클럽에 들어갈 수는 있었지만, 그곳의 누구도 그에게 함께 만찬을 하자고 청할 생각은 하지 않았다."], ["It was taken for granted that he was going to dine at home; and in the absence of any provocation to the contrary, he always did dine at home.", "그가 집에서 식사할 것이라는 게 당연한 일로 여겨졌고, 달리 그러지 않을 빌미가 없는 터라 그는 늘 집에서 식사했다."], ["He had now been in his house for three weeks, and had been asked with his wife to a few bridal dinner-parties, given chiefly by friends of the De Courcy family.", "이제 제집에 들어앉은 지 세 주가 되었고, 그동안 아내와 함께 신혼 축하 만찬에 몇 번 초대를 받았는데 대개 드 코시 집안의 벗들이 베푼 자리였다."], ["Except on such occasions he never passed an evening out of his own house, and had not yet, since his marriage, dined once away from his wife.", "그런 경우를 빼고는 저녁나절을 제집 밖에서 보내는 법이 없었고, 혼인한 뒤로 아직 한 번도 아내와 떨어져 식사한 적이 없었다."], ["He told himself that his good conduct in this respect was the result of his own resolution; but, nevertheless, he felt that there was nothing else left for him to do.", "그는 이 점에서 자기가 처신을 잘하는 것은 스스로 다잡은 결심의 결과라고 되뇌었다. 그러면서도 그는 달리 할 일이 아무것도 남아 있지 않다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Nobody asked him to go to the theatre.", "아무도 그에게 극장에 가자고 청하지 않았다."], ["Nobody begged him to drop in of an evening.", "아무도 저녁에 잠깐 들르라고 조르지 않았다."], ["Men never asked him why he did not play a rubber.", "사내들은 어째서 카드 한 판 하지 않느냐고 그에게 묻는 법이 없었다."], ["He would generally saunter into Sebright's after he left his office, and lounge about the room for half an hour, talking to a few men.", "그는 대개 사무국을 나선 뒤 세브라이트에 어슬렁어슬렁 들어가 반 시간쯤 방 안을 서성이며 몇몇 사내들과 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Nobody was uncivil to him.", "아무도 그에게 무례하지 않았다."], ["But he knew that the whole thing was changed, and he resolved, with some wisdom, to accommodate himself to his altered circumstances.", "그러나 그는 모든 것이 달라졌음을 알았고, 얼마간 지혜롭게도, 바뀐 처지에 자기를 맞추기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Lady Alexandrina also found her new life rather dull, and was sometimes inclined to be a little querulous.", "레이디 알렉산드리나 또한 새 생활이 자못 따분하다고 여겼고, 이따금 좀 투덜거리고 싶어 했다."], ["She would tell her husband that she never got out, and would declare, when he offered to walk with her, that she did not care for walking in the streets.", "그녀는 남편에게 자기는 도무지 바깥출입을 못 한다고 말하곤 했고, 그가 함께 걷자고 하면 길거리를 걷는 것은 좋아하지 않는다고 잘라 말하곤 했다."], ["\"I don't exactly see, then, where you are to walk,\"", "“그럼 대체 어디를 걸어야 한다는 건지 나로서는 잘 모르겠구려.”"], ["he once replied.", "언젠가 그가 그렇게 대꾸했다."], ["She did not tell him that she was fond of riding, and that the Park was a very fitting place for such exercise; but she looked it, and he understood her.", "그녀는 자기가 승마를 좋아하며 공원이야말로 그런 운동에 아주 알맞은 곳이라는 말은 하지 않았다. 그러나 낯빛이 그렇게 말하고 있었고, 그는 그 뜻을 알아들었다."], ["\"I'll do all I can for her,\" he said to himself; \"but I'll not ruin myself.\"", "‘저 사람을 위해 할 수 있는 일은 다 하겠다.’ 그는 속으로 말했다."], ["\"Amelia is coming to take me for a drive,\"", "‘하지만 나를 거덜 내지는 않겠다.’ “어밀리아 언니가 마차 나들이를 시켜 주러 온대요.”"], ["she said another time.", "또 언젠가 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Ah, that'll be very nice,\"", "“아, 그거 아주 좋겠구려.”"], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["\"No; it won't be very nice,\"", "“아뇨, 별로 좋지 않아요.”"], ["said Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."], ["\"Amelia is always shopping and bargaining with the tradespeople.", "“어밀리아 언니는 늘 물건을 사들이고 장사치들과 값을 깎느라 바쁘거든요."], ["But it will be better than being kept in the house without ever stirring out.\"", "그래도 한 발짝도 못 나가고 집 안에 갇혀 있는 것보다는 낫겠죠.”"]], [["They breakfasted nominally at half-past nine; in truth, it was always nearly ten, as Lady Alexandrina found it difficult to get herself out of her room.", "두 사람의 아침 식사는 명목상 아홉 시 반이었다. 실은 언제나 열 시가 다 되어서였는데, 레이디 알렉산드리나가 제 방에서 몸을 빼내기를 힘겨워한 까닭이었다."], ["At half-past ten punctually he left his house for his office.", "열 시 반이 되면 그는 어김없이 집을 나서 사무국으로 갔다."], ["He usually got home by six, and then spent the greatest part of the hour before dinner in the ceremony of dressing.", "대개 여섯 시까지는 집에 돌아왔고, 그러고는 만찬 전 한 시간의 태반을 옷 갈아입는 의식으로 보냈다."], ["He went, at least, into his dressing-room, after speaking a few words to his wife, and there remained, pulling things about, clipping his nails, looking over any paper that came in his way, and killing the time.", "적어도 그는 아내와 몇 마디를 나눈 뒤 제 옷방으로 들어가서는, 이것저것 끄집어내고, 손톱을 깎고, 손에 잡히는 종이란 종이는 다 훑어보며 시간을 죽이면서 그 안에 머물렀다."], ["He expected his dinner punctually at seven, and began to feel a little cross if he were kept waiting.", "그는 만찬이 일곱 시 정각에 나오기를 바랐고, 기다리게 되면 슬며시 짜증이 나기 시작했다."], ["After dinner, he drank one glass of wine in company with his wife, and one other by himself, during which latter ceremony he would stare at the hot coals, and think of the thing he had done.", "만찬 뒤에는 아내와 함께 포도주를 한 잔 마시고, 다시 혼자서 한 잔을 마셨다. 그 뒤쪽 의식을 치르는 동안 그는 벌겋게 단 석탄을 물끄러미 바라보며 자기가 저지른 일을 생각하곤 했다."], ["Then he would go upstairs, and have, first a cup of coffee, and then a cup of tea.", "그런 다음 위층으로 올라가 먼저 커피를 한 잔, 이어서 차를 한 잔 마셨다."], ["He would read his newspaper, open a book or two, hide his face when he yawned, and try to make believe that he liked it.", "신문을 읽고, 책을 한두 권 펼치고, 하품이 나면 얼굴을 가리고, 자기가 이 생활을 좋아하는 척해 보려 애썼다."], ["She had no signs or words of love for him.", "그녀에게는 그를 향한 사랑의 몸짓도 말도 없었다."], ["She never sat on his knee, or caressed him.", "그녀는 결코 그의 무릎에 앉는 법도, 그를 어루만지는 법도 없었다."], ["She never showed him that any happiness had come to her in being allowed to live close to him.", "그의 곁에서 살도록 허락받은 것이 자기에게 어떤 행복이 되었다는 기색을 결코 그에게 내보이지 않았다. 두 사람은 서로 사랑한다고 여겼다."], ["They thought that they loved each other:--each thought so; but there was no love, no sympathy, no warmth.", "각자가 그렇게 여겼다. 그러나 거기에는 사랑도, 마음의 나눔도, 온기도 없었다."], ["The very atmosphere was cold;--so cold that no fire could remove the chill.", "공기 자체가 차가웠다. 어떤 불로도 그 냉기를 걷어 낼 수 없을 만큼 차가웠다."]], [["In what way would it have been different had Lily Dale sat opposite to him there as his wife, instead of Lady Alexandrina?", "레이디 알렉산드리나 대신 릴리 데일이 아내로서 그의 맞은편에 앉아 있었다면 무엇이 달랐을까?"], ["He told himself frequently that either with one or with the other life would have been the same; that he had made himself for a while unfit for domestic life, and that he must cure himself of that unfitness.", "그는 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 삶은 마찬가지였을 것이라고, 자기는 한동안 스스로를 가정생활에 맞지 않는 사람으로 만들어 놓았을 뿐이며 그 부적합을 스스로 고쳐야 한다고 자주 되뇌었다."], ["But though he declared this to himself in one set of half-spoken thoughts, he would also declare to himself in another set, that Lily would have made the whole house bright with her brightness; that had he brought her home to his hearth, there would have been a sun shining on him every morning and every evening.", "그러나 반쯤 소리 내어 굴리는 한 갈래의 생각으로 그렇게 잘라 말하면서도, 또 다른 갈래의 생각으로는 릴리라면 제 밝음으로 집안 전체를 환하게 만들었으리라고, 그녀를 제 화롯가로 데려왔더라면 아침마다 저녁마다 자기 위에 해가 비치고 있었으리라고 스스로에게 잘라 말하기도 했다."], ["But, nevertheless, he strove to do his duty, and remembered that the excitement of official life was still open to him.", "그럼에도 그는 제 본분을 다하려 애썼고, 관청 생활의 흥분만은 아직 자기에게 열려 있다는 것을 떠올렸다."], ["From eleven in the morning till five in the afternoon he could still hold a position which made it necessary that men should regard him with respect, and speak to him with deference.", "아침 열한 시부터 오후 다섯 시까지, 그는 여전히 사람들이 자기를 존경으로 바라보고 공손하게 말을 걸지 않을 수 없게 만드는 자리를 지킬 수 있었다."], ["In this respect he was better off than his wife, for she had no office to which she could betake herself.", "이 점에서 그는 아내보다 나은 처지였다. 그녀에게는 몸을 붙일 사무국이 없었으니까."]], [["\"Yes,\" she said to Amelia, \"it is all very nice, and I don't mind the house being damp; but I get so tired of being alone.\"", "“그럼요.” 그녀가 어밀리아에게 말했다. “다 아주 좋아요. 집이 눅눅한 것도 상관없고요. 그런데 혼자 있는 게 어찌나 지겨운지.”"]], [["\"That must be the case with women who are married to men of business.\"", "“사업하는 남자와 혼인한 여자라면 다 그럴 수밖에 없다오.”"]], [["\"Oh, I don't complain.", "“아, 불평하는 건 아녜요."], ["Of course I knew what I was about.", "물론 제가 무슨 일을 하는 건지 알고 한 거니까요."], ["I suppose it won't be so very dull when everybody is up in London.\"", "다들 런던에 올라오면 이렇게까지 따분하지는 않겠지요.”"]], [["\"I don't find the season makes much difference to us after Christmas,\"", "“크리스마스가 지나고 나면 사교철이라고 우리한테 크게 달라지는 건 없더군요.”"], ["said Amelia; \"but no doubt London is gayer in May.", "어밀리아가 말했다. “하기야 오월의 런던이 더 흥겨운 건 틀림없지요."], ["You'll find you'll like it better next year; and perhaps you'll have a baby, you know.\"", "내년에는 여기가 더 마음에 들 게요. 그리고 어쩌면 아기가 생길지도 모르잖소.”"]], [["\"Psha!\"", "“쳇!”"], ["ejaculated Lady Alexandrina; \"I don't want a baby, and don't suppose I shall have one.\"", "레이디 알렉산드리나가 내뱉었다. “저는 아기 같은 거 갖고 싶지 않고, 생길 것 같지도 않아요.”"]], [["\"It's always something to do, you know.\"", "“그래도 늘 할 일이 하나 생기는 거라오.”"]], [["Lady Alexandrina, though she was not of an energetic temperament, could not but confess to herself that she had made a mistake.", "레이디 알렉산드리나는 기운찬 기질의 사람은 아니었으나, 자기가 잘못을 저질렀다는 것을 스스로 고백하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She had been tempted to marry Crosbie because Crosbie was a man of fashion, and now she was told that the London season would make no difference to her;--the London season which had hitherto always brought to her the excitement of parties, if it had not given her the satisfaction of amusement.", "그녀가 크로즈비와 혼인할 마음이 동한 것은 크로즈비가 사교계의 멋쟁이였기 때문인데, 이제 와서 런던의 사교철이 자기에게 아무 차이도 만들어 주지 않으리라는 말을 듣고 있었다. 그 런던의 사교철이란, 놀이의 만족까지는 주지 못했더라도 여태껏 언제나 그녀에게 모임의 흥분만은 가져다주던 것이었다."], ["She had been tempted to marry because it appeared to her that a married woman could enjoy society with less restraint than a girl who was subject to her mother or her chaperon; that she would have more freedom of action as a married woman; and now she was told that she must wait for a baby before she could have anything to do.", "그녀가 혼인할 마음이 동한 것은 또한, 혼인한 여자라면 어머니나 보호자에게 매인 처녀보다 덜 갑갑하게 사교를 누릴 수 있고 혼인한 여자로서 몸가짐이 한결 자유로우리라고 여겨졌기 때문이었다. 그런데 이제 와서는 무언가 할 일이 생기려면 아기가 생길 때까지 기다려야 한다는 말을 듣고 있었다."], ["Courcy Castle was sometimes dull, but Courcy Castle would have been better than this.", "코시 성도 이따금 따분하기는 했다. 그러나 코시 성이 이것보다는 나았을 것이다."]], [["When Crosbie returned home after this little conversation about the baby, he was told by his wife that they were to dine with the Gazebees on the next Sunday.", "아기에 관한 이 자잘한 이야기가 오간 뒤 크로즈비가 집에 돌아오자, 아내는 다음 일요일에 게이지비네에서 저녁을 먹기로 되어 있다고 그에게 알렸다."], ["On hearing this he shook his head with vexation.", "이 말을 듣고 그는 짜증스레 고개를 저었다."], ["He knew, however, that he had no right to make complaint, as he had been only taken to St.", "그러나 그는 자기에게 불평할 권리가 없다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["John's Wood once since they had come home from their marriage trip.", "신혼여행에서 돌아온 뒤로 세인트존스 우드에 끌려간 것이 겨우 한 번뿐이었으니까."], ["There was, however, one point as to which he could grumble.", "그렇지만 투덜거릴 만한 구석이 한 가지 있기는 했다."], ["\"Why, on earth, on Sunday?\"", "“대체 왜 하필 일요일에?”"]], [["\"Because Amelia asked me for Sunday.", "“어밀리아 언니가 일요일로 청했으니까요."], ["If you are asked for Sunday, you cannot say you'll go on Monday.\"", "일요일로 청을 받았는데 월요일에 가겠다고 할 수는 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"It is so terrible on a Sunday afternoon.", "“일요일 오후는 지독하지 않소."], ["At what hour?\"", "몇 시라던가요?”"]], [["\"She said half-past five.\"", "“다섯 시 반이라고 했어요.”"]], [["\"Heavens and earth!", "“맙소사!"], ["What are we to do all the evening?\"", "저녁 내내 대체 뭘 하란 말이오?”"]], [["\"It is not kind of you, Adolphus, to speak in that way of my relations.\"", "“어돌퍼스, 제 친정 식구들을 그런 식으로 말하는 건 좋지 않아요.”"]], [["\"Come, my love, that's a joke; as if I hadn't heard you say the same thing twenty times.", "“여보, 그건 농담이잖소. 마치 내가 당신이 똑같은 말을 스무 번쯤 하는 걸 듣지 못하기라도 한 것처럼 구는구려."], ["You've complained of having to go up there much more bitterly than I ever did.", "그 집에 가야 한다고 나보다 훨씬 더 쓰디쓰게 투덜댄 건 당신이오."], ["You know I like your sister, and, in his way, Gazebee is a very good fellow; but after three or four hours, one begins to have had enough of him.\"", "내가 당신 언니를 좋아한다는 건 당신도 알 테고, 게이지비도 제 나름으로는 아주 좋은 사람이오. 그렇지만 서너 시간이 지나면 슬슬 그 사람은 그만하면 됐다 싶어지지 않소.”"]], [["\"It can't be much duller than it is--;\"", "“여기 있는 것보다 더 따분하기야 하겠어요—”"], ["but Lady Alexandrina stopped herself before she finished her speech.", "그러나 레이디 알렉산드리나는 말을 다 맺기 전에 스스로 멈추었다."]], [["\"One can always read at home, at any rate,\"", "“집에서는 어쨌든 언제든 책이라도 읽을 수 있지 않소.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"One can't always be reading.", "“늘 책만 읽고 있을 수는 없죠."], ["However, I have said you would go.", "어쨌든 저는 당신이 가겠다고 말해 두었어요."], ["If you choose to refuse, you must write and explain.\"", "굳이 거절하시겠다면 편지를 써서 사정을 설명하셔야 할 거예요.”"]], [["When the Sunday came the Crosbies of course did go to St. John's Wood, arriving punctually at that door which he so hated at half-past five.", "일요일이 오자 크로즈비 부부는 물론 세인트존스 우드로 갔고, 그가 그토록 싫어하는 그 문 앞에 다섯 시 반 정각으로 닿았다."], ["One of the earliest resolutions which he made when he first contemplated the De Courcy match, was altogether hostile to the Gazebees.", "드 코시 집안과의 혼사를 처음 마음에 두었을 때 그가 세운 가장 이른 결심 가운데 하나는 게이지비네에 대해 아주 적대적인 것이었다."], ["He would see but very little of them.", "그는 그들을 되도록 적게 보리라 했다."], ["He would shake himself free of that connexion.", "그 인연에서 몸을 털어 내리라 했다."], ["It was not with that branch of the family that he desired an alliance.", "그가 맺고자 한 것은 그 집안의 그 갈래가 아니었다."], ["But now, as things had gone, that was the only branch of the family with which he seemed to be allied.", "그런데 이제 일이 흘러가고 보니, 그가 이어져 있는 듯 보이는 것은 그 집안에서 오직 그 갈래뿐이었다."], ["He was always hearing of the Gazebees.", "그는 늘 게이지비네 이야기를 듣고 있었다."], ["Amelia and Alexandrina were constantly together.", "어밀리아와 알렉산드리나는 노상 붙어 지냈다."], ["He was now dragged there to a Sunday dinner; and he knew that he should often be dragged there,--that he could not avoid such draggings.", "그는 이제 일요일 만찬에 그리로 끌려가고 있었고, 앞으로도 자주 그리로 끌려가리라는 것을, 그런 끌려감을 피할 수 없으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He already owed money to Mortimer Gazebee, and was aware that his affairs had been allowed to fall into that lawyer's hands in such a way that he could not take them out again.", "그는 이미 모티머 게이지비에게 빚을 지고 있었고, 제 살림살이가 그 사무 변호사의 손아귀에 들어가되 다시는 빼낼 수 없는 모양새로 들어가 버렸다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그의 집에는 세간이 아주 빈틈없이 갖추어져 있었고 그 값이 모두 치러진 것도 알고 있었다."], ["His house was very thoroughly furnished, and he knew that the bills had been paid; but he had not paid them; every shilling had been paid through Mortimer Gazebee.", "그러나 그것을 치른 사람은 그가 아니었다. 한 실링까지 죄다 모티머 게이지비의 손을 거쳐 치러졌다."]], [["\"Go with your mother and aunt, De Courcy,\"", "“드 코시, 너는 어머니랑 이모를 따라가거라.”"], ["the attorney said to the lingering child after dinner; and then Crosbie was left alone with his wife's brother-in-law.", "만찬이 끝난 뒤에도 자리를 뜨지 않고 뭉그적거리는 아이에게 그 사무 변호사가 말했다. 그리하여 크로즈비는 아내의 형부와 단둘이 남게 되었다."], ["This was the period of the St. John's Wood purgatory which was so dreadful to him.", "그에게 그토록 끔찍한, 세인트존스 우드 연옥의 시간이 바로 이때였다."], ["With his sister-in-law he could talk, remembering perhaps always that she was an earl's daughter.", "처형과는 그래도 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다. 그녀가 백작의 딸이라는 것을 아마도 늘 잊지 않고 있었던 덕이리라."], ["But with Gazebee he had nothing in common.", "그러나 게이지비와는 통하는 구석이 하나도 없었다."], ["And he felt that Gazebee, who had once treated him with great deference, had now lost all such feeling.", "게다가 그는 한때 자기를 몹시 깍듯이 대하던 게이지비가 이제 그런 마음을 죄다 잃어버렸음을 느끼고 있었다."], ["Crosbie had once been a man of fashion in the estimation of the attorney, but that was all over.", "그 사무 변호사가 보기에 크로즈비는 한때 사교계의 멋쟁이였으나, 이제 그런 것은 다 끝난 일이었다."], ["Crosbie, in the attorney's estimation, was now simply the secretary of a public office,--a man who owed him money.", "사무 변호사가 보기에 크로즈비는 이제 그저 관청의 사무국장일 뿐이었다. 자기에게 빚을 진 사내일 뿐이었다."], ["The two had married sisters, and there was no reason why the light of the prosperous attorney should pale before that of the civil servant, who was not very prosperous.", "두 사람은 자매를 아내로 맞았으니, 잘나가는 사무 변호사의 빛이 그다지 잘나가지 못하는 관리의 빛 앞에서 흐려져야 할 까닭이 없었다."], ["All this was understood thoroughly by both the men.", "이 모든 것을 두 사내 다 속속들이 알고 있었다."]], [["\"There's terrible bad news from Courcy,\"", "“코시에서 아주 고약한 소식이 왔네.”"], ["said the attorney, as soon as the boy was gone.", "아이가 나가자마자 사무 변호사가 말했다."]], [["\"Why; what's the matter?\"", "“왜, 무슨 일인가?”"]], [["\"Porlock has married--that woman, you know.\"", "“폴록이 혼인을 했다네—그 여자하고 말일세.”"]], [["\"Nonsense.\"", "“말도 안 되는 소리.”"]], [["\"He has.", "“했다니까."], ["The old lady has been obliged to tell me, and she's nearly broken-hearted about it.", "노부인께서 어쩔 수 없이 내게 알려 오셨는데, 상심으로 몸져누울 지경이시라네."], ["But that's not the worst of it to my mind.", "그런데 내가 보기엔 그게 최악도 아닐세."], ["All the world knows that Porlock had gone to the mischief.", "폴록이 진작에 망가질 대로 망가졌다는 건 온 세상이 아는 일이지."], ["But he is going to bring an action against his father for some arrears of his allowance, and he threatens to have everything out in court, if he doesn't get his money.\"", "한데 그 사람이 밀린 생활비를 내놓으라고 제 아버지를 걸어 소송을 내겠다는 걸세. 돈을 못 받으면 죄다 법정에서 까발리겠다고 으르고 있어.”"]], [["\"But is there money due to him?\"", "“한데 정말 그 사람한테 갈 돈이 있기는 한가?”"]], [["\"Yes, there is.", "“있지, 있고말고."], ["A couple of thousand pounds or so.", "이천 파운드쯤 될 걸세."], ["I suppose I shall have to find it.", "아마 내가 마련해야 하겠지."], ["But, upon my honour, I don't know where it's to come from; I don't, indeed.", "그렇지만 맹세코 그 돈이 어디서 나와야 할지 나도 모르겠네. 정말이지 모르겠어."], ["In one way or another, I've paid over fourteen hundred pounds for you.\"", "이러저러해서 내가 자네를 위해 치른 돈만 해도 천사백 파운드가 넘는다네.”"]], [["\"Fourteen hundred pounds!\"", "“천사백 파운드라니!”"]], [["\"Yes, indeed;--what with the insurance and the furniture, and the bill from our house for the settlements.", "“그렇다니까. 보험이며 세간이며, 게다가 혼인 재산 계약서 값으로 우리 사무소에서 나온 청구서까지 있으니 말일세."], ["That's not paid yet, but it's the same thing.", "그건 아직 치르지 않았지만 매한가지 아닌가."], ["A man doesn't get married for nothing, I can tell you.\"", "사내가 거저 장가드는 게 아니라는 걸 내 말해 두겠네.”"]], [["\"But you've got security.\"", "“그래도 자네는 담보를 잡아 두었잖은가.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes; I've got security.", "“아, 그렇지. 담보야 잡아 두었지."], ["But the thing is the ready money.", "그런데 문제는 당장 쥘 현금일세."], ["Our house has advanced so much on the Courcy property, that they don't like going any further; and therefore it is that I have to do this myself.", "우리 사무소가 코시 영지를 잡고 이미 너무 많이 내주어서 더 나가기를 꺼리거든. 그러니 이 일을 나 혼자 떠맡아야 하는 거지."], ["They'll all have to go abroad,--that'll be the end of it.", "결국 그 식구들은 죄다 외국으로 나가야 할 걸세."], ["There's been such a scene between the earl and George.", "그게 끝이야. 백작과 조지 사이에 한바탕 난리가 났다네."], ["George lost his temper and told the earl that Porlock's marriage was his fault.", "조지가 성질을 못 참고 백작에게 폴록의 혼인은 아버지 탓이라고 쏘아붙였다지."], ["It has ended in George with his wife being turned out.\"", "그 끝에 조지가 제 아내와 함께 쫓겨나고 말았네.”"]], [["\"He has money of his own.\"", "“그 사람은 제 돈이 있잖은가.”"]], [["\"Yes, but he won't spend it.", "“그렇지, 그런데 그 돈은 쓰려 들지 않는단 말일세."], ["He's coming up here, and we shall find him hanging about us.", "여기로 올라온다는데, 우리 주위를 어슬렁거리며 붙어 있게 될 걸세."], ["I don't mean to give him a bed here, and I advise you not to do so either.", "나는 여기서 재워 줄 생각이 없고, 자네도 그러지 말라고 권하겠네."], ["You'll not get rid of him if you do.\"", "한번 재워 주면 떼어 내지 못할 테니까.”"]], [["\"I have the greatest possible dislike to him.\"", "“나는 그 사람이 세상에서 제일 싫으이.”"]], [["\"Yes; he's a bad fellow.", "“그럼, 못된 위인이지."], ["So is John.", "존도 마찬가질세."], ["Porlock was the best, but he's gone altogether to ruin.", "폴록이 그중 나았는데, 그마저 아주 망해 버렸지."], ["They've made a nice mess of it between them; haven't they?\"", "저희끼리 아주 볼만하게 판을 어질러 놓았어. 안 그런가?”"]], [["This was the family for whose sake Crosbie had jilted Lily Dale!", "바로 이 집안을 얻으려고 크로즈비는 릴리 데일을 저버린 것이었다!"], ["His single and simple ambition had been that of being an earl's son-in-law.", "그의 하나뿐인 단순한 야심은 백작의 사위가 되는 것이었다."], ["To achieve that it had been necessary that he should make himself a villain.", "그것을 이루려고 그는 스스로를 악당으로 만들어야 했다."], ["In achieving it he had gone through all manner of dirt and disgrace.", "그것을 이루는 동안 그는 온갖 더러움과 수치를 다 겪었다."], ["He had married a woman whom he knew he did not love.", "그는 사랑하지 않는다는 것을 스스로 아는 여자와 혼인했다."], ["He was thinking almost hourly of a girl whom he had loved, whom he did love, but whom he had so injured, that, under no circumstances, could he be allowed to speak to her again.", "그는 자기가 사랑했고 지금도 사랑하지만, 너무나 깊이 상처를 입힌 나머지 어떤 사정으로도 다시는 말을 붙일 수 없게 된 한 처녀를 거의 시시각각 생각하고 있었다."], ["The attorney there--who sat opposite to him, talking about his thousands of pounds with that disgusting assumed solicitude which such men put on, when they know very well what they are doing--had made a similar marriage.", "맞은편에 앉아, 그런 부류의 사내들이 제가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 훤히 알면서도 짐짓 꾸며 보이는 그 역겨운 걱정스러운 낯빛으로 제 수천 파운드 이야기를 늘어놓고 있는 저 사무 변호사도 그와 비슷한 혼인을 했다."], ["But he had known what he was about.", "그러나 그는 자기가 무엇을 하는지 알고 있었다."], ["He had got from his marriage all that he had expected.", "그는 제 혼인에서 기대한 것을 다 얻었다."], ["But what had Crosbie got?", "그런데 크로즈비는 무엇을 얻었는가?"]], [["\"They're a bad set,--a bad set,\"", "“못돼 먹은 집안일세—아주 못돼 먹은 집안이야.”"], ["said he in his bitterness.", "그가 쓰디쓴 심정으로 말했다."]], [["\"The men are,\"", "“사내들이 그렇지.”"], ["said Gazebee, very comfortably.", "게이지비가 아주 태연하게 말했다."]], [["\"H--m,\"", "“흐음.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다. 제 벗이 그 집안 여자들 또한 마땅히 그래야 할 만한 사람들은 못 된다는 심정을 드러내고 있다는 것이 게이지비에게는 뻔히 보였다."], ["It was manifest to Gazebee that his friend was expressing a feeling that the women, also, were not all that they should be, but he took no offence, though some portion of the censure might thereby be supposed to attach to his own wife.", "그러나 그는 조금도 언짢아하지 않았다. 그 흉이 얼마쯤은 제 아내에게까지 붙는다고 볼 수 있는데도 그랬다."]], [["\"The countess means well,\"", "“백작부인께서는 마음씨가 곧으시지.”"], ["said Gazebee.", "게이지비가 말했다."], ["\"But she's had a hard life of it,--a very hard life.", "“그렇지만 그분도 고생스러운 세월을 보내셨네—참으로 고생스러운 세월이었어."], ["I've heard him call her names that would frighten a coal-heaver.", "나는 백작께서 부인한테 석탄 나르는 인부라도 질겁할 만한 욕설을 퍼붓는 걸 들은 적이 있네."], ["I have, indeed.", "정말이야."], ["But he'll die soon, and then she'll be comfortable.", "하지만 백작께서는 머잖아 돌아가실 테고, 그러면 부인께서는 편안해지실 걸세."], ["She has three thousand a year jointure.\"", "부인 앞으로 잡힌 몫이 해마다 삼천 파운드니까.”"]], [["He'll die soon, and then she'll be comfortable!", "백작께서는 머잖아 돌아가실 테고, 그러면 부인께서는 편안해지실 걸세!"], ["That was one phase of married life.", "이것이 혼인 생활의 한 모습이었다."], ["As Crosbie's mind dwelt upon the words, he remembered Lily's promise made in the fields, that she would do everything for him.", "크로즈비의 마음이 그 말에 머물러 있는 동안, 그는 들판에서 릴리가 했던 약속을, 자기를 위해 무엇이든 다 하겠다던 그 말을 떠올렸다."], ["He remembered her kisses; the touch of her fingers; the low silvery laughing voice; the feel of her dress as she would press close to him.", "그는 그녀의 입맞춤을, 그녀의 손가락이 닿던 느낌을, 낮고 은빛 같던 웃음소리를, 그녀가 제게 바싹 다가붙을 때 옷자락이 스치던 느낌을 떠올렸다."], ["After that he reflected whether it would not be well that he too should die, so that Alexandrina might be comfortable.", "그러고 나서 그는 자기 또한 죽어 버리는 편이, 그리하여 알렉산드리나가 편안해지는 편이 낫지 않겠는가 하고 생각했다."], ["She and her mother might be very comfortable together, with plenty of money, at Baden-Baden!", "그녀와 그녀의 어머니라면 돈이야 넉넉하니 바덴바덴에서 둘이 아주 편안하게 지낼 수 있으리라!"]], [["The squire at Allington, and Mrs. Dale, and Lady Julia De Guest, had been, and still were, uneasy in their minds because no punishment had fallen upon Crosbie,--no vengeance had overtaken him in consequence of his great sin.", "앨링턴의 지주와 데일 부인과 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트는, 크로즈비에게 아무런 벌도 내리지 않았다는 것 때문에—그 큰 죄를 지은 값으로 아무런 응보도 그를 덮치지 않았다는 것 때문에 마음이 편치 않았고, 지금도 여전히 편치 않았다."], ["How little did they know about it!", "그들이 그 일을 얼마나 모르고 있었던가!"], ["Could he have been prosecuted and put into prison, with hard labour, for twelve months, the punishment would not have been heavier.", "그가 기소되어 열두 달 동안 중노동과 함께 감옥에 갇혔다 한들 그 벌이 이보다 무겁지는 않았을 것이다."], ["He would, in that case, at any rate, have been saved from Lady Alexandrina.", "적어도 그런 경우라면 그는 레이디 알렉산드리나로부터는 구원을 받았을 테니 말이다."]], [["\"George and his wife are coming up to town; couldn't we ask them to come to us for a week or so?\"", "“조지 오라버니 부부가 런던에 올라온다는데, 우리한테 한 주쯤 와 있으라고 청하면 안 될까요?”"], ["said his wife to him, as soon as they were in the fly together, going home.", "집으로 돌아가려고 함께 삯마차에 오르자마자 아내가 그에게 말했다."]], [["\"No,\"", "“안 되오.”"], ["shouted Crosbie; \"we will do no such thing.\"", "크로즈비가 소리쳤다. “그런 짓은 하지 않을 거요.”"], ["There was not another word said on the subject,--nor on any other subject till they got home.", "그 이야기는 더 오가지 않았고—집에 닿을 때까지 다른 어떤 이야기도 오가지 않았다."], ["When they reached their house Alexandrina had a headache, and went up to her room immediately.", "집에 이르자 알렉산드리나는 머리가 아프다며 곧바로 제 방으로 올라가 버렸다."], ["Crosbie threw himself into a chair before the remains of a fire in the dining-room, and resolved that he would cut the whole De Courcy family altogether.", "크로즈비는 식당의 사그라져 가는 불 앞에 놓인 의자에 몸을 던지고는, 드 코시 집안 사람들과는 아예 인연을 끊어 버리겠다고 마음먹었다."], ["His wife, as his wife, should obey him.", "제 아내는 아내인 이상 자기에게 복종해야 한다."], ["She should obey him--or else leave him and go her way by herself, leaving him to go his way.", "자기에게 복종하든가—아니면 자기를 떠나 제 갈 길을 가고, 자기는 제 길을 가게 내버려 두든가 해야 한다."], ["There was an income of twelve hundred a year.", "수입은 한 해 천이백 파운드였다."], ["Would it not be a fine thing for him if he could keep six hundred for himself and return to his old manner of life.", "그중 육백 파운드를 제 몫으로 챙기고 옛 생활로 돌아갈 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까."], ["All his old comforts of course he would not have,--nor the old esteem and regard of men.", "물론 예전의 안락을 다 누리지는 못할 것이다—사람들의 옛 존경과 대접도 그럴 것이다."], ["But the luxury of a club dinner he might enjoy.", "그러나 클럽 만찬의 호사쯤은 누릴 수 있으리라."], ["Unembarrassed evenings might be his,--with liberty to him to pass them as he pleased.", "거리낄 것 없는 저녁나절이 제 것이 되고—그 시간을 제 마음대로 보낼 자유도 제 것이 되리라."], ["He knew many men who were separated from their wives, and who seemed to be as happy as their neighbours.", "그는 아내와 갈라져 사는 사내들을 여럿 알고 있었는데, 그들은 이웃 사람들 못지않게 행복해 보였다."], ["And then he remembered how ugly Alexandrina had been this evening, wearing a great tinsel coronet full of false stones, with a cold in her head which had reddened her nose.", "그러다 그는 이날 저녁 알렉산드리나가 얼마나 못나 보였는지를 떠올렸다. 가짜 보석이 잔뜩 박힌 번쩍이는 싸구려 관을 머리에 얹고, 감기까지 들어 코가 벌게진 꼴이었다."], ["There had, too, fallen upon her in these her married days a certain fixed dreary dowdiness.", "게다가 혼인한 뒤로 그녀에게는 어딘가 붙박인 듯한 칙칙하고 후줄근한 기운이 내려앉아 있었다."], ["She certainly was very plain!", "그녀는 분명 아주 못생겼다!"], ["So he said to himself, and then he went to bed.", "그는 스스로에게 그렇게 말하고는 잠자리에 들었다."], ["I myself am inclined to think that his punishment was sufficiently severe.", "나로서는 그가 받은 벌이 넉넉히 가혹했다고 여기는 쪽이다."]], [["The next morning his wife still complained of headache, so that he breakfasted alone.", "이튿날 아침에도 아내는 여전히 머리가 아프다고 하여 그는 혼자 아침을 먹었다."], ["Since that positive refusal which he had given to her proposition for inviting her brother, there had not been much conversation between them.", "오라버니를 부르자는 그녀의 말에 그가 딱 잘라 안 된다고 한 뒤로 두 사람 사이에는 이야기랄 것이 별로 없었다. “머리가 쪼개질 것 같아요."], ["\"My head is splitting, and Sarah shall bring some tea and toast up to me, if you will not mind it.\"", "당신만 괜찮으시다면 세라더러 차하고 토스트를 제 방으로 올려 보내라고 할게요.”"]], [["He did not mind it in the least, and ate his breakfast by himself, with more enjoyment than usually attended that meal.", "그는 조금도 개의치 않았고, 혼자서 아침을 먹었는데 여느 때의 그 끼니보다 훨씬 즐거웠다."]], [["It was clear to him that all the present satisfaction of his life must come to him from his office work.", "이제 제 삶에서 누릴 만족이란 온통 관청 일에서 나와야 한다는 것이 그에게는 분명했다."], ["There are men who find it difficult to live without some source of daily comfort, and he was such a man.", "세상에는 날마다 마음을 달래 줄 무언가가 없이는 살아가기 힘든 사내들이 있는데, 그도 그런 사내였다."], ["He could hardly endure his life unless there were some page in it on which he could look with gratified eyes.", "그는 제 삶 가운데 흐뭇한 눈으로 바라볼 수 있는 대목이 한 쪽이라도 없으면 그 삶을 견디기 어려웠다."], ["He had always liked his work, and he now determined that he would like it better than ever.", "그는 늘 제 일을 좋아했고, 이제는 그 어느 때보다 더 좋아하리라 마음먹었다."], ["But in order that he might do so it was necessary that he should have much of his own way.", "그러나 그러자면 많은 것을 제 뜻대로 해야만 했다."], ["According to the theory of his office, it was incumbent on him as Secretary simply to take the orders of the Commissioners, and see that they were executed; and to such work as this his predecessor had strictly confined himself.", "사무국의 법도로 말하자면, 사무국장인 그가 할 일은 그저 위원들의 지시를 받아 그것이 이행되도록 살피는 것뿐이었고, 그의 전임자는 그 일에만 엄격히 매여 있었다."], ["But he had already done more than this, and had conceived the ambition of holding the Board almost under his thumb.", "그러나 그는 이미 그보다 더 많은 일을 해 왔고, 위원회를 제 손아귀에 쥐다시피 하겠다는 야심을 품고 있었다."], ["He flattered himself that he knew his own work and theirs better than they knew either, and that by a little management he might be their master.", "그는 제 일도 그들의 일도 그들보다 자기가 더 잘 안다고, 그러니 조금만 요령껏 다루면 자기가 그들의 주인이 될 수 있으리라고 스스로 흐뭇해했다."], ["It is not impossible that such might have been the case had there been no fracas at the Paddington station; but, as we all know, the dominant cock of the farmyard must be ever dominant.", "패딩턴 역에서의 그 소동만 없었더라면 정말 그렇게 되었을지도 모른다. 그러나 우리 모두가 알듯이, 마당을 주름잡는 수탉은 언제까지나 주름잡고 있어야만 한다."], ["When he shall once have had his wings so smeared with mud as to give him even the appearance of adversity, no other cock will ever respect him again. Mr.", "한번 날개가 진창에 처발려 낭패를 본 꼴로 비치기라도 하면, 다른 어떤 수탉도 다시는 그를 존중해 주지 않는다."], ["Optimist and Mr. Butterwell knew very well that their secretary had been cudgelled, and they could not submit themselves to a secretary who had been so treated.", "옵티미스트 씨와 버터웰 씨는 제 사무국장이 몽둥이질을 당했다는 것을 훤히 알고 있었고, 그렇게 당한 사무국장에게 고분고분 따를 수는 없었다."]], [["\"Oh, by-the-by, Crosbie,\"", "“아, 그런데 말일세, 크로즈비.”"], ["said Butterwell, coming into his room, soon after his arrival at his office on that day of his solitary breakfast, \"I want to say just a few words to you.\"", "혼자 아침을 먹은 그날 그가 사무국에 나온 지 얼마 되지 않아 버터웰이 그의 방으로 들어오며 말했다. “자네한테 몇 마디 할 말이 있네.”"], ["And Butterwell turned round and closed the door, the lock of which had not previously been fastened.", "그러고서 버터웰은 몸을 돌려 문을 닫았는데, 그때까지 걸쇠가 걸려 있지 않던 문이었다."], ["Crosbie, without much thinking, immediately foretold himself the nature of the coming conversation.", "크로즈비는 딱히 머리를 굴려 볼 것도 없이 곧바로 앞으로 오갈 이야기가 어떤 것일지를 스스로 짚어 냈다."]], [["\"Do you know--\"", "“자네도 알다시피—”"], ["said Butterwell, beginning.", "버터웰이 말문을 열었다."]], [["\"Sit down, won't you?\"", "“앉으시지요.”"], ["said Crosbie, seating himself as he spoke.", "크로즈비가 이렇게 말하며 자기도 자리에 앉았다."], ["If there was to be a contest, he would make the best fight he could.", "겨루어야 할 일이라면 할 수 있는 한 잘 싸울 참이었다."], ["He would show a better spirit here than he had done on the railway platform.", "그는 철도 승강장에서 보였던 것보다 나은 기개를 여기서 보이려 했다."], ["Butterwell did sit down, and felt as he did so, that the very motion of sitting took away some of his power.", "버터웰은 정말로 자리에 앉았고, 앉으면서 그 앉는 동작만으로도 제 힘이 얼마쯤 빠져나간다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He ought to have sent for Crosbie into his own room.", "그는 크로즈비를 제 방으로 불렀어야 했다."], ["A man, when he wishes to reprimand another, should always have the benefit of his own atmosphere.", "사람이 남을 꾸짖으려 할 때에는 언제나 제 쪽 공기의 덕을 보아야 하는 법이다."]], [["\"I don't want to find any fault,\"", "“무슨 흠을 잡자는 게 아닐세.”"], ["Butterwell began.", "버터웰이 말문을 열었다."]], [["\"I hope you have not any cause,\"", "“흠잡으실 거리가 없기를 바랍니다.”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."]], [["\"No, no; I don't say that I have.", "“아니, 아니지. 그런 게 있다는 말이 아니야."], ["But we think at the Board--\"", "그렇지만 위원회에서는 우리 생각에—”"]], [["\"Stop, stop, Butterwell.", "“잠깐, 잠깐만요, 버터웰 씨."], ["If anything unpleasant is coming, it had better come from the Board.", "언짢은 이야기가 나올 참이라면 위원회에서 나오는 편이 낫겠습니다."], ["I should take it in better spirit; I should, indeed.\"", "그러면 제가 더 나은 마음으로 받아들이겠지요. 정말 그럴 겁니다.”"]], [["\"What takes place at the Board must be official.\"", "“위원회에서 벌어지는 일은 공식적인 것이 될 수밖에 없네.”"]], [["\"I shall not mind that in the least.", "“저는 조금도 개의치 않습니다."], ["I should rather like it than otherwise.\"", "오히려 그편이 낫겠습니다.”"]], [["\"It simply amounts to this,--that we think you are taking a little too much on yourself.", "“말하자면 이런 걸세—자네가 좀 지나치게 많은 것을 제 손에 쥐고 있다고 우리는 보고 있어."], ["No doubt, it's a fault on the right side, and arises from your wishing to have the work well done.\"", "물론 잘못이라 해도 옳은 쪽으로 기운 잘못이고, 일이 제대로 되기를 바라는 마음에서 나온 것이겠지만 말이야.”"]], [["\"And if I don't do it, who will?\"", "“그럼 제가 하지 않으면 누가 합니까?”"], ["asked Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 물었다."]], [["\"The Board is very well able to get through all that appertains to it.", "“위원회도 제게 딸린 일쯤은 넉넉히 해낼 수 있네."], ["Come, Crosbie, you and I have known each other a great many years, and it would be a pity that we should have any words.", "이보게 크로즈비, 자네와 나는 오랜 세월 서로 알고 지낸 사이인데 언짢은 말이 오간다면 딱한 노릇이지."], ["I have come to you in this way because it would be disagreeable to you to have any question raised officially.", "내가 이런 식으로 자네를 찾아온 것은 공식으로 문제가 불거지면 자네에게 언짢을 터이기 때문일세."], ["Optimist isn't given to being very angry, but he was downright angry yesterday.", "옵티미스트는 좀처럼 화를 내는 사람이 아닌데, 어제는 아주 대놓고 화를 냈네."], ["You had better take what I say in good part, and go along a little quieter.\"", "내 말을 좋게 받아들여서 앞으로는 좀 조용히 가는 편이 좋겠어.”"]], [["But Crosbie was not in a humour to take anything quietly.", "그러나 크로즈비는 무엇이든 조용히 받아들일 기분이 아니었다."], ["He was sore all over, and prone to hit out at everybody that he met.", "그는 온몸이 쓰라렸고, 마주치는 사람마다 후려치고 싶은 심사였다."], ["\"I have done my duty to the best of my ability, Mr.", "“저는 제 힘이 닿는 데까지 제 본분을 다했습니다, 버터웰 씨.”"], ["Butterwell,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"and I believe I have done it well.", "“그리고 잘해 냈다고 믿습니다."], ["I believe I know my duty here as well as any one can teach me.", "저는 여기서 제가 할 일을 누가 가르쳐 줄 필요가 없을 만큼 잘 알고 있습니다."], ["If I have done more than my share of work, it is because other people have done less than theirs.\"", "제가 제 몫보다 많은 일을 했다면, 그건 다른 사람들이 제 몫보다 적게 했기 때문입니다.”"], ["As he spoke, there was a black cloud upon his brow, and the Commissioner could perceive that the Secretary was very wrathful.", "그가 말하는 동안 그의 이마에는 검은 구름이 끼어 있었고, 그 위원은 사무국장이 몹시 노여워하고 있음을 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["\"Oh!", "“오!"], ["very well,\"", "좋으이.”"], ["said Butterwell, rising from his chair.", "버터웰이 의자에서 일어서며 말했다."], ["\"I can only, under such circumstances, speak to the Chairman, and he will tell you what he thinks at the Board.", "“사정이 그렇다면 나로서는 위원장에게 말할 수밖에 없고, 그러면 위원장이 위원회에서 제 생각을 자네에게 일러 주겠지."], ["I think you're foolish; I do, indeed.", "자네는 어리석은 짓을 하고 있네. 정말 그래."], ["As for myself, I have only meant to act kindly by you.\"", "나로 말하자면 그저 자네에게 좋게 해 주려 했을 뿐일세.”"], ["After that, Mr. Butterwell took himself off.", "그러고 나서 버터웰 씨는 자리를 떴다."]], [["On the same afternoon, Crosbie was summoned into the Board-room in the usual way, between two and three.", "그날 오후 크로즈비는 여느 때처럼 두 시와 세 시 사이에 위원회실로 불려 들어갔다."], ["This was a daily occurrence, as he always sat for about an hour with two out of the three Commissioners, after they had fortified themselves with a biscuit and a glass of sherry.", "이는 날마다 있는 일이었으니, 그는 늘 세 위원 가운데 두 사람과 한 시간쯤 마주 앉곤 했다. 그들이 비스킷 한 조각과 셰리 한 잔으로 기운을 차린 뒤였다."], ["On the present occasion, the usual amount of business was transacted, but it was done in a manner which made Crosbie feel that they did not all stand together on their usual footing.", "이날도 여느 만큼의 일이 처리되었으나, 그 처리되는 모양새가 크로즈비로 하여금 자기들 모두가 여느 때와 같은 자리에 서 있지 않다는 것을 느끼게 했다."], ["The three Commissioners were all there.", "세 위원이 다 나와 있었다."], ["The Chairman gave his directions in a solemn, pompous voice, which was by no means usual to him when he was in good humour.", "위원장은 기분이 좋을 때에는 결코 쓰는 법이 없는 엄숙하고 거드름 섞인 목소리로 지시를 내렸다."], ["The Major said little or nothing; but there was a gleam of satisfied sarcasm in his eye.", "소령은 거의 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 그의 눈에는 흡족해하는 빈정거림이 반짝였다."], ["Things were going wrong at the Board, and he was pleased. Mr.", "위원회의 일이 어긋나게 돌아가고 있었고, 그는 그것이 즐거웠다."], ["Butterwell was exceedingly civil in his demeanour, and rather more than ordinarily brisk.", "버터웰 씨는 태도가 유난히 깍듯했고 여느 때보다 좀 더 활기찼다."], ["As soon as the regular work of the day was over, Mr. Optimist shuffled about on his chair, rising from his seat, and then sitting down again.", "그날의 정해진 일이 끝나자마자 옵티미스트 씨는 의자 위에서 몸을 뒤척이며 일어섰다가 다시 앉기를 되풀이했다."], ["He looked through a lot of papers close to his hand, peering at them over his spectacles.", "그는 제 손 가까이 놓인 서류 뭉치를 안경 너머로 들여다보며 뒤적였다."], ["Then he selected one, took off his spectacles, leaned back in his chair, and began his little speech.", "그러다 하나를 골라 들고는 안경을 벗고 의자에 등을 기댄 뒤 짧은 연설을 시작했다."]], [["\"Mr. Crosbie,\"", "“크로즈비 씨.”"], ["he said, \"we are all very much gratified,--very much gratified, indeed,--by your zeal and energy in the service.\"", "그가 말했다. “우리는 모두 대단히 흡족하게 여기고 있습니다—참으로 대단히 흡족하게 여기고 있어요—귀하가 공직에서 보여 준 열의와 정력을 말입니다.”"]], [["\"Thank you, sir,\"", "“고맙습니다, 위원장님.”"], ["said Crosbie; \"I am fond of the service.\"", "크로즈비가 말했다. “저는 이 공직을 좋아합니다.”"]], [["\"Exactly, exactly; we all feel that.", "“그렇지요, 그렇지요. 우리 모두 그렇게 느끼고 있습니다."], ["But we think that you,--if I were to say take too much upon yourself, I should say, perhaps, more than we mean.\"", "그런데 우리 생각으로는 귀하가—제가 지나치게 많은 것을 스스로 떠맡고 있다고 말한다면, 아마 우리가 뜻하는 것보다 더한 말을 하는 셈이 되겠습니다만.”"]], [["\"Don't say more than you mean, Mr. Optimist.\"", "“뜻하시는 것보다 더한 말씀은 하지 마십시오, 옵티미스트 씨.”"], ["Crosbie's eyes, as he spoke, gleamed slightly with his momentary triumph; as did also those of Major Fiasco.", "그렇게 말하는 크로즈비의 눈은 한순간의 승리로 잠깐 반짝였다. 피아스코 소령의 눈도 그러했다."]], [["\"No, no, no,\" said Mr.", "“아니, 아니, 아닙니다.”"], ["Optimist; \"I would say rather less than more to so very good a public servant as yourself.", "옵티미스트 씨가 말했다. “귀하처럼 훌륭한 공복에게라면 나는 더한 말보다 차라리 덜한 말을 하겠습니다."], ["But you, doubtless, understand me?\"", "그렇지만 귀하도 내 말뜻을 알아들으시겠지요?”"]], [["\"I don't think I do quite, sir.", "“아무래도 잘 모르겠습니다, 위원장님."], ["If I have not taken too much on me, what is it that I have done that I ought not to have done?\"", "제가 지나치게 많은 것을 떠맡은 게 아니라면, 제가 하지 말았어야 할 무슨 일을 했다는 말씀입니까?”"]], [["\"You have given directions in many cases for which you ought first to have received authority.", "“귀하는 먼저 권한을 받았어야 마땅한 여러 경우에 지시부터 내렸습니다."], ["Here is an instance,\"", "여기 한 예가 있습니다.”"], ["and the selected paper was at once brought out.", "그러면서 골라 두었던 서류가 곧바로 나왔다."]], [["It was a matter in which the Secretary had been manifestly wrong according to written law, and he could not defend it on its own merits.", "그것은 사무국장이 성문의 법도로 보아 명백히 잘못한 일이었고, 그는 그 일 자체를 두고는 변명할 수 없었다."]], [["\"If you wish me,\"", "“제가 사무국의 명시된 지시에만 꼭 매여 있기를 바라신다면,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"to confine myself exactly to the positive instructions of the office, I will do so; but I think you will find it inconvenient.\"", "“그렇게 하겠습니다. 다만 그것이 불편하게 여겨지시리라 봅니다.”"]], [["\"It will be far the best,\"", "“그편이 훨씬 나을 겁니다.”"], ["said Mr. Optimist.", "옵티미스트 씨가 말했다."]], [["\"Very well,\"", "“좋습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Crosbie, \"it shall be done.\"", "크로즈비 씨가 말했다."], ["And he at once determined to make himself as unpleasant to the three gentlemen in the room as he might find it within his power to do.", "“그렇게 하지요.” 그러고서 그는 방 안의 세 신사에게 제 힘이 닿는 데까지 언짢은 사람이 되어 주기로 그 자리에서 마음먹었다."], ["He could make himself very unpleasant, but the unpleasantness would be as much to him as to them.", "그는 아주 언짢은 사람이 될 수 있었다. 그러나 그 언짢음은 그들에게만큼이나 그 자신에게도 언짢은 것이 될 터였다."]], [["Nothing would now go right with him.", "이제 그에게는 어느 것 하나 제대로 되어 가지 않았다."], ["He could look in no direction for satisfaction.", "어느 쪽을 보아도 흡족함을 찾을 수 없었다."], ["He sauntered into Sebright's, as he went home, but he could not find words to speak to any one about the little matters of the day.", "그는 집으로 가는 길에 세브라이트에 어슬렁어슬렁 들어갔으나, 그날의 자잘한 일들을 두고 누구에게든 건넬 말을 찾지 못했다."], ["He went home, and his wife, though she was up, complained still of her headache.", "그는 집으로 갔고, 아내는 자리에서 일어나 있기는 했으나 여전히 머리가 아프다고 하소연했다."]], [["\"I haven't been out of the house all day,\"", "“하루 내내 집 밖에 나가 보지도 못했어요.”"], ["she said, \"and that has made it worse.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “그래서 더 심해진 거예요.”"]], [["\"I don't know how you are to get out if you won't walk,\"", "“걸으려 하지 않는데 어떻게 밖에 나갈 수 있다는 건지 나로서는 잘 모르겠구려.”"], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["Then there was no more said between them till they sat down to their meal.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 밥상에 마주 앉을 때까지 더는 아무 말도 주고받지 않았다."]], [["Had the squire at Allington known all, he might, I think, have been satisfied with the punishment which Crosbie had encountered.", "앨링턴의 지주가 이 모든 것을 다 알았더라면, 내 생각에는, 크로즈비가 맞닥뜨린 그 벌에 흡족해했을 법도 하다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIX. PREPARATIONS FOR GOING.", "# 제49장. 떠날 채비"]], [["\"Mamma, read that letter.\"", "“엄마, 이 편지 좀 읽어 보세요.”"]], [["It was Mrs. Dale's eldest daughter who spoke to her, and they were alone together in the parlour at the Small House.", "데일 부인에게 말을 건 것은 그녀의 맏딸이었고, 두 사람은 작은 집 응접실에 단둘이 있었다."], ["Mrs. Dale took the letter and read it very carefully.", "데일 부인은 편지를 받아 아주 꼼꼼히 읽었다."], ["She then put it back into its envelope and returned it to Bell.", "그러고는 그것을 다시 봉투에 넣어 벨에게 돌려주었다."]], [["\"It is, at any rate, a good letter, and, as I believe, tells the truth.\"", "“어쨌든 좋은 편지구나. 그리고 내가 보기에는 진실을 적은 것 같다.”"]], [["\"I think it tells a little more than the truth, mamma.", "“제 생각엔 진실보다 조금 더 적어 놓은 것 같아요, 엄마."], ["As you say, it is a well-written letter.", "엄마 말씀대로 잘 쓴 편지이긴 해요."], ["He always writes well when he is in earnest.", "버나드는 진지할 때면 언제나 글을 잘 쓰니까요."], ["But yet--\"", "하지만 그래도—”"]], [["\"Yet what, my dear?\"", "“그래도 뭐가 어떻단 말이니, 얘야?”"]], [["\"There is more head than heart in it.\"", "“거기엔 마음보다 머리가 더 많이 들어 있어요.”"]], [["\"If so, he will suffer the less; that is, if you are quite resolved in the matter.\"", "“그렇다면 그 애도 덜 괴로울 게다. 네 마음이 이 일에 아주 굳게 정해져 있다면 말이야.”"]], [["\"I am quite resolved, and I do not think he will suffer much.", "“제 마음은 아주 굳게 정해졌어요. 그리고 버나드가 그리 괴로워하리라고는 생각지 않아요."], ["He would not, I suppose, have taken the trouble to write like that, if he did not wish this thing.\"", "이 일을 바라지 않았다면 그만한 수고를 들여 그런 편지를 쓰지도 않았겠지요.”"]], [["\"I am quite sure that he does wish it, most earnestly; and that he will be greatly disappointed.\"", "“그 애가 이 일을 바라는 건 분명하다. 그것도 아주 간절하게 말이야. 그리고 크게 실망할 게다.”"]], [["\"As he would be if any other scheme did not turn out to his satisfaction; that is all.\"", "“다른 어떤 계획이 제 뜻대로 되지 않았을 때도 꼭 그만큼 실망하겠지요. 그뿐이에요.”"]], [["The letter, of course, was from Bell's cousin Bernard, and containing the strongest plea he was able to make in favour of his suit for her hand.", "물론 그 편지는 벨의 사촌 버나드가 보낸 것으로, 그녀의 손을 얻으려는 자기 청혼을 두고 그가 펼 수 있는 가장 강력한 호소를 담고 있었다. 버나드 데일은 그런 호소라면 입으로 말하기보다 편지로 밀어붙이는 편이 훨씬 나은 사람이었다."], ["Bernard Dale was better able to press such a plea by letter than by spoken words.", "그는 곰곰이 헤아릴 시간이 조금이라도 주어지는 일이라면 무엇이든 잘 해내는 사람이었으나, 얻고자 하는 것을 청할 때 사람을 웅변으로 몰아가는 그 정념의 힘만은 그에게 없었다. 이번 그의 편지는 길고 조리가 있었다."], ["He was a man capable of doing anything well in the doing of which a little time for consideration might be given to him; but he had not in him that power of passion which will force a man to eloquence in asking for that which he desires to obtain.", "그 안에 열렬한 사랑은 거의 없었지만, 듣기 좋은 칭찬은 넉넉히 들어 있었다. 그는 두 사람의 혼인이 두 집안 모두에게 얼마나 이로운 일인지 벨에게 말했다."], ["His letter on this occasion was long, and well argued.", "떨어져 지낸 탓에 이 일을 향한 자기 마음이 오히려 더 굳세어졌노라고 밝혔다."], ["If there was little in it of passionate love, there was much of pleasant flattery.", "앞으로의 처신을 보증할 가장 좋은 담보로 자기가 지나온 삶의 이력을 자랑하는 기색 없이 넌지시 내비쳤다."], ["He told Bell how advantageous to both their families their marriage would be; he declared to her that his own feeling in the matter had been rendered stronger by absence; he alluded without boasting to his past career of life as her best guarantee for his future conduct; he explained to her that if this marriage could be arranged there need then, at any rate, be no further question as to his aunt removing with Lily from the Small House; and then he told her that his affection for herself was the absorbing passion of his existence.", "이 혼인이 이루어지기만 하면 적어도 숙모가 릴리를 데리고 작은 집에서 나가는 문제만은 더 따질 것이 없어진다고 일러 주었다. 그러고는 마지막으로, 그녀를 향한 자기 애정이 자기 삶을 통째로 사로잡은 정념이라고 말했다."], ["Had the letter been written with the view of obtaining from a third person a favourable verdict as to his suit, it would have been a very good letter indeed; but there was not a word in it that could stir the heart of such a girl as Bell Dale.", "만약 이 편지가 제삼자에게서 자기 청혼에 유리한 판정을 얻어 낼 목적으로 쓰인 것이었다면, 참으로 훌륭한 편지였을 것이다. 그러나 벨 데일 같은 아가씨의 마음을 흔들 만한 말은 그 안에 한 마디도 없었다."]], [["\"Answer him kindly,\"", "“다정하게 답장을 쓰렴.”"], ["Mrs. Dale said.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"As kindly as I know how,\"", "“제가 아는 한 가장 다정하게 쓸게요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"I wish you would write the letter, mamma.\"", "“엄마가 그 편지를 써 주시면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["\"I fear that would not do.", "“그건 안 될 말이다."], ["What I should say would only tempt him to try again.\"", "내가 쓰면 그 애더러 한 번 더 해 보라고 부추기는 꼴밖에 안 될 게다.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale knew very well,--had known for some months past,--that Bernard's suit was hopeless.", "데일 부인은 버나드의 청혼에 가망이 없다는 것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다."], ["She felt certain, although the matter had not been discussed between them, that whenever Dr.", "벌써 몇 달 전부터 알고 있었다."], ["Crofts might choose to come again and ask for her daughter's hand he would not be refused. Of the two men she probably liked Dr.", "두 사람 사이에 그 문제를 두고 이야기가 오간 적은 없었지만, 크로프츠 선생이 언제든 다시 찾아와 딸의 손을 청하기만 하면 결코 거절당하지 않으리라는 것을 그녀는 확신하고 있었다."], ["Crofts the best; but she liked them both, and she could not but remember that the one, in a worldly point of view, would be a very poor match, whereas the other would, in all respects, be excellent.", "두 사람 가운데 그녀 자신이 더 마음에 들어 한 쪽은 아마 크로프츠 선생이었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 둘 다 좋아했고, 세상의 눈으로 보자면 한쪽은 몹시 변변찮은 혼처인 반면 다른 쪽은 무엇 하나 나무랄 데 없는 혼처라는 사실을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She would not, on any account, say a word to influence her daughter, and knew, moreover, that no word which she could say would influence her; but she could not divest herself of some regret that it should be so.", "그녀는 무슨 일이 있어도 딸의 마음을 흔들 말은 한 마디도 하지 않을 작정이었고, 게다가 자기가 무슨 말을 한들 딸의 마음이 흔들리지 않으리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러나 일이 이렇게 되고 만 데 대한 얼마간의 아쉬움만은 스스로에게서 떼어 낼 수가 없었다."]], [["\"I know what you would wish, mamma,\"", "“엄마가 무엇을 바라시는지 저도 알아요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I have but one wish, dearest, and that is for your happiness.", "“나에게 바람은 하나뿐이란다, 얘야. 네가 행복한 것 말이다."], ["May God preserve you from any such fate as Lily's.", "부디 하느님께서 릴리와 같은 운명으로부터 너를 지켜 주시기를."], ["When I tell you to write kindly to your cousin, I simply mean that I think him to have deserved a kind reply by his honesty.\"", "사촌에게 다정하게 답장을 쓰라고 한 것은, 그저 그 애가 정직하게 굴었으니 다정한 답을 받을 만하다고 여긴다는 뜻일 뿐이다.”"]], [["\"It shall be as kind as I can make it, mamma; but you know what the lady says in the play,--how hard it is to take the sting from that word 'no.'\"", "“제가 할 수 있는 한 가장 다정하게 쓸게요, 엄마. 하지만 극에 나오는 그 아가씨가 뭐라고 하는지 아시잖아요."], ["Then Bell walked out alone for a while, and on her return got her desk and wrote her letter.", "‘아니오’라는 그 말에서 가시를 빼내기가 얼마나 어려운지 말이에요.” 그러고 나서 벨은 한동안 홀로 밖을 거닐다가, 돌아와서는 책상을 꺼내 편지를 썼다."], ["It was very firm and decisive.", "아주 단호하고 결연한 편지였다."], ["As for that wit which should pluck the sting \"from such a sharp and waspish word as 'no,'\" I fear she had it not.", "‘아니오’처럼 날카롭고 쏘아 대는 말에서 가시를 뽑아낸다는 그 재치로 말하자면, 그녀에게는 아무래도 그것이 없었던 듯싶다."], ["\"It will be better to make him understand that I, also, am in earnest,\"", "“나도 진지하다는 것을 버나드가 알아듣게 하는 편이 낫겠지.”"], ["she said to herself; and in this frame of mind she wrote her letter.", "그녀는 속으로 이렇게 되뇌었고, 그런 마음가짐으로 편지를 썼다."], ["\"Pray do not allow yourself to think that what I have said is unfriendly,\"", "“내가 한 말을 매정하다고는 부디 여기지 말아 줘.”"], ["she added, in a postscript.", "그녀는 추신에 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["\"I know how good you are, and I know the great value of what I refuse; but in this matter it must be my duty to tell you the simple truth.\"", "“버나드가 얼마나 좋은 사람인지 나도 알고, 내가 물리치는 것이 얼마나 값진 것인지도 알아. 그렇지만 이 일에서만은 있는 그대로의 진실을 말하는 것이 내 도리일 거야.”"]], [["It had been decided between the squire and Mrs. Dale that the removal from the Small House to Guestwick was not to take place till the first of May.", "작은 집에서 게스트윅으로 옮겨 가는 일은 오월 초하루가 되기 전에는 하지 않기로 지주와 데일 부인 사이에 이미 정해져 있었다."], ["When he had been made to understand that Dr. Crofts had thought it injudicious that Lily should be taken out of their present house in March, he had used all the eloquence of which he was master to induce Mrs.", "릴리를 삼월에 지금 사는 집에서 데리고 나가는 것은 분별없는 짓이라고 크로프츠 선생이 여긴다는 말을 전해 듣고서, 지주는 자기가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 능변을 다 동원하여 데일 부인이 그 계획을 접도록 설득하려 들었다."], ["Dale to consent to abandon her project.", "그는 그 집을 늘 자기 것이 아니라 집안의 다른 누군가에게 마땅히 돌아가는 것으로 여겨 왔노라고 말했다."], ["He had told her that he had always considered that house as belonging, of right, to some other of the family than himself; that it had always been so inhabited, and that no squire of Allington had for years past taken rent for it.", "언제나 그렇게 사람이 들어와 살아 왔고, 앨링턴의 지주가 그 집에서 집세를 받은 일은 여러 해 전부터 없었노라고 했다. “은혜를 베푸는 게 아니오."], ["\"There is no favour conferred,--none at all,\"", "그런 것은 조금도 아니오.”"], ["he had said; but speaking nevertheless in his usual sharp, ungenial tone.", "그는 이렇게 말했으나, 그러면서도 어조는 여느 때와 같이 날카롭고 무뚝뚝했다."]], [["\"There is a favour, a great favour, and great generosity,\"", "“은혜예요, 큰 은혜고 큰 아량이지요.”"], ["Mrs. Dale had replied.", "데일 부인이 대답했다."], ["\"And I have never been too proud to accept it; but when I tell you that we think we shall be happier at Guestwick, you will not refuse to let us go.", "“그리고 저는 그것을 받기에 너무 도도했던 적이 한 번도 없어요. 하지만 저희가 게스트윅에서 더 행복하리라 여긴다고 말씀드리는 마당이니, 시아주버니께서도 저희를 보내 주기를 마다하지는 않으시겠지요."], ["Lily has had a great blow in that house, and Bell feels that she is running counter to your wishes on her behalf,--wishes that are so very kind!\"", "릴리는 이 집에서 큰 타격을 입었고, 벨은 제 일로 시아주버니의 바람을 거스르고 있다고 여기고 있어요. 그토록 고마운 바람을요!”"]], [["\"No more need be said about that.", "“그 일이라면 더 말할 것 없소."], ["All that may come right yet, if you will remain where you are.\"", "그대들이 그대로 여기 남아 있기만 하면 그 모든 것이 아직 바로잡힐 수도 있소.”"]], [["But Mrs. Dale knew that \"all that\"", "그러나 데일 부인은 ‘그 모든 것’이 결코 바로잡힐 수 없음을 알고 있었기에 뜻을 굽히지 않았다."], ["could never come right, and persisted. Indeed, she would hardly have dared to tell her girls that she had yielded to the squire's entreaties.", "사실 그녀는 지주의 간청에 굽히고 말았노라고 딸들에게 말할 엄두조차 내지 못했을 것이다."], ["It was just then, at that very time, that the squire was, as it were, in treaty with the earl about Lily's fortune; and he did feel it hard that he should be opposed in such a way by his own relatives at the moment when he was behaving towards them with so much generosity.", "바로 그 무렵, 바로 그때에 지주는 릴리의 재산 문제를 두고 말하자면 드 게스트 백작과 협상을 벌이는 중이었다. 그리고 자기가 그토록 넉넉한 마음으로 대해 주고 있는 바로 그 순간에 제 살붙이들에게서 그런 식으로 맞섬을 당한다는 것이 그로서는 참으로 야속했다."], ["But in his arguments about the house he said nothing of Lily, or her future prospects.", "그러나 집을 두고 벌인 실랑이에서 그는 릴리에 대해서도, 그 아이의 앞날에 대해서도 한 마디 하지 않았다."]], [["They were to move on the first of May, and one week of April was already past.", "그들은 오월 초하루에 옮겨 가기로 되어 있었고, 사월도 벌써 한 주가 지나 있었다."], ["The squire had said nothing further on the matter after the interview with Mrs. Dale to which allusion has just been made.", "방금 말한 데일 부인과의 그 대면이 있고 난 뒤로 지주는 그 일에 대해 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He was vexed and sore at the separation, thinking that he was ill-used by the feeling which was displayed by this refusal.", "그는 이 헤어짐이 속상하고 쓰라렸으며, 이번 거절에 드러난 마음씨 탓에 자기가 부당한 대접을 받고 있다고 여겼다."], ["He had done his duty by them, as he thought; indeed more than his duty, and now they told him that they were leaving him because they could no longer bear the weight of an obligation conferred by his hands.", "자기 딴에는 그들에게 도리를 다했고, 실은 도리 이상을 했는데, 이제 그들은 제 손으로 베푼 신세의 무게를 더는 견딜 수 없어서 떠나는 것이라고 그에게 말하고 있었다."], ["But in truth he did not understand them; nor did they understand him.", "그러나 사실 그는 그들을 이해하지 못했고, 그들 또한 그를 이해하지 못했다."], ["He had been hard in his manner, and had occasionally domineered, not feeling that his position, though it gave him all the privileges of a near and a dear friend, did not give him the authority of a father or a husband.", "그는 태도가 거칠었고 이따금 위세를 부렸으니, 자기 자리가 가깝고 다정한 벗의 온갖 특권은 준다 해도 아버지나 남편의 권위까지 주는 것은 아님을 느끼지 못했던 것이다."], ["In that matter of Bernard's proposed marriage he had spoken as though Bell should have considered his wishes before she refused her cousin.", "버나드의 혼담 문제에서도 그는 마치 벨이 사촌을 거절하기 전에 자기 뜻부터 헤아렸어야 한다는 투로 말했다."], ["He had taken upon himself to scold Mrs. Dale, and had thereby given offence to the girls, which they at the time had found it utterly impossible to forgive.", "그는 스스로 나서서 데일 부인을 나무랐고, 그로써 두 딸의 마음을 상하게 했으니, 그 아이들은 그때 그것만은 도무지 용서할 수 없다고 여겼던 것이다."]], [["But they were hardly better satisfied in the matter than was he; and now that the time had come, though they could not bring themselves to go back from their demand, almost felt that they were treating the squire with cruelty.", "그러나 이 일에 관해서라면 그들도 그보다 딱히 더 흡족한 것은 아니었다. 그리고 이제 때가 닥치고 보니, 자기들이 내건 요구를 스스로 거두어들이지는 못하면서도, 지주를 모질게 대하고 있는 것만 같았다."], ["When their decision had been made,--while it had been making,--he had been stern and hard to them.", "그들의 결심이 내려졌을 때, 아니 그 결심이 내려지는 동안에 그는 그들에게 엄격하고 매몰찼다."], ["Since that he had been softened by Lily's misfortune, and softened also by the anticipated loneliness which would come upon him when they should be gone from his side.", "그 뒤로 그는 릴리의 불행에 마음이 누그러졌고, 그들이 곁을 떠나고 나면 자기에게 닥칠 외로움을 미리 그려 보면서 또 한 번 누그러졌다."], ["It was hard upon him that they should so treat him when he was doing his best for them all!", "그가 그들 모두를 위해 최선을 다하고 있는 마당에 그들이 그를 그렇게 대하다니, 그로서는 가혹한 일이었다!"], ["And they also felt this, though they did not know the extent to which he was anxious to go in serving them.", "그리고 그들도 그것을 느끼고 있었다. 다만 그가 그들을 돕기 위해 어디까지 나서려 하는지 그 속내는 알지 못했다."], ["When they had sat round the fire planning the scheme of their removal, their hearts had been hardened against him, and they had resolved to assert their independence.", "난롯가에 둘러앉아 이사 계획을 짜던 무렵에는 그들의 마음이 그에게 굳게 닫혀 있었고, 그래서 자기들의 독립을 내세우기로 뜻을 모았던 것이다."], ["But now, when the time for action had come, they felt that their grievances against him had already been in a great measure assuaged.", "그러나 이제 정작 움직일 때가 오고 보니, 그를 향한 서운함이 이미 크게 가라앉아 있음을 그들도 느꼈다."], ["This tinged all that they did with a certain sadness; but still they continued their work.", "그 때문에 그들이 하는 모든 일에 어떤 서글픔이 배어들었다. 그러나 그래도 그들은 하던 일을 계속해 나갔다."]], [["Who does not know how terrible are those preparations for house-moving;--how infinite in number are the articles which must be packed, how inexpressibly uncomfortable is the period of packing, and how poor and tawdry is the aspect of one's belongings while they are thus in a state of dislocation?", "이사 채비라는 것이 얼마나 끔찍한지 모르는 사람이 어디 있겠는가. 꾸려 넣어야 할 물건의 가짓수가 얼마나 끝이 없는지, 짐을 싸는 동안이 얼마나 말할 수 없이 불편한지, 그리고 제 살림살이가 그렇게 제자리를 잃고 흐트러져 있는 동안 그 꼴이 얼마나 초라하고 볼품없어 보이는지를 말이다."], ["Now-a-days people who understand the world, and have money commensurate with their understanding, have learned the way of shunning all these disasters, and of leaving the work to the hands of persons paid for doing it.", "요즘 세상 물정에 밝고 그 밝음에 걸맞은 돈까지 지닌 사람들은 이런 재난을 죄다 피해 가는 법을, 그 일을 삯 받고 해 주는 사람들의 손에 맡겨 버리는 법을 익혔다."], ["The crockery is left in the cupboards, the books on the shelves, the wine in the bins, the curtains on their poles, and the family that is understanding goes for a fortnight to Brighton.", "그릇은 찬장에 그대로, 책은 서가에 그대로, 포도주는 술광에 그대로, 커튼은 봉에 그대로 걸어 둔 채, 사정을 아는 그 집안은 보름 동안 브라이턴으로 떠난다."], ["At the end of that time the crockery is comfortably settled in other cupboards, the books on other shelves, the wine in other bins, the curtains are hung on other poles, and all is arranged. But Mrs.", "그 기간이 끝날 무렵이면 그릇은 다른 찬장에 편안히 자리를 잡았고, 책은 다른 서가에, 포도주는 다른 술광에, 커튼은 다른 봉에 걸려 있으며, 모든 것이 갖추어져 있다."], ["Dale and her daughters understood nothing of such a method of moving as this.", "그러나 데일 부인과 두 딸은 그런 이사법이라고는 아무것도 알지 못했다."], ["The assistance of the village carpenter in filling certain cases that he had made was all that they knew how to obtain beyond that of their own two servants.", "마을 목수가 제 손으로 짜 준 궤짝 몇 개를 채우는 데 그의 도움을 받는 것, 그것이 제집 하녀 둘 말고 그들이 구할 줄 아는 일손의 전부였다."], ["Every article had to pass through the hands of some one of the family; and as they felt almost overwhelmed by the extent of the work to be done, they began it much sooner than was necessary, so that it became evident as they advanced in their work, that they would have to pass a dreadfully dull, stupid, uncomfortable week at last, among their boxes and cases, in all the confusion of dismantled furniture.", "물건이란 물건은 하나하나 식구 가운데 누군가의 손을 거쳐야 했다. 그리고 해야 할 일의 크기에 짓눌리는 심정이었던 터라 필요한 것보다 훨씬 일찍 일을 시작해 버렸으니, 일이 진척될수록, 결국 마지막에는 궤짝과 상자 사이에서, 뜯어 헤쳐진 가구들의 온갖 어수선함 속에서 지독하게 따분하고 멍하고 불편한 한 주를 보내야 하리라는 것이 뻔해졌다."]], [["At first an edict had gone forth that Lily was to do nothing.", "처음에는 릴리가 아무 일도 해서는 안 된다는 엄명이 내려졌다."], ["She was an invalid, and was to be petted and kept quiet.", "그 아이는 환자였으니 떠받들어 주고 조용히 지내게 해야 했다."], ["But this edict soon fell to the ground, and Lily worked harder than either her mother or her sister.", "그러나 이 엄명은 이내 땅에 떨어졌고, 릴리는 어머니보다도 언니보다도 더 열심히 일했다."], ["In truth she was hardly an invalid any longer, and would not submit to an invalid's treatment.", "사실 그 아이는 이제 거의 환자가 아니었고, 환자 대접을 받으려 하지도 않았다."], ["She felt herself that for the present constant occupation could alone save her from the misery of looking back,--and she had conceived an idea that the harder that occupation was, the better it would be for her.", "그 아이는 지금으로서는 쉼 없이 몸을 놀리는 것만이 지난 일을 되돌아보는 비참함에서 자기를 건져 줄 수 있음을 스스로 느끼고 있었다. 그리고 그 일이 고될수록 자기에게 더 이로우리라는 생각을 품게 되었다."], ["While pulling down the books, and folding the linen, and turning out from their old hiding-places the small long-forgotten properties of the household, she would be as gay as ever she had been in old times.", "책을 끌어내리고, 이불과 천을 개키고, 오래 잊고 있던 자잘한 살림살이를 옛 은신처에서 끄집어내는 동안, 그 아이는 지난날 못지않게 명랑했다."], ["She would talk over her work, standing with flushed cheek and laughing eyes among the dusty ruins around her, till for a moment her mother would think that all was well within her.", "먼지 낀 폐허 같은 것들에 둘러싸여 뺨을 붉히고 눈웃음을 지으며 선 채로 제 일거리를 두고 이야기를 늘어놓았으니, 어머니는 잠깐씩 그 아이 속이 다 괜찮아졌구나 여기기도 했다."], ["But then at other moments, when the reaction came, it would seem as though nothing were well.", "그러나 또 어떤 때, 반동이 밀려오면 아무것도 괜찮지 않은 것만 같았다."], ["She could not sit quietly over the fire, with quiet rational work in her hands, and chat in a rational quiet way.", "그 아이는 조용하고 조리 있는 일감을 손에 들고 난롯가에 가만히 앉아 조리 있고 조용하게 이야기를 나눌 수가 없었다."], ["Not as yet could she do so.", "아직은 그럴 수가 없었다."], ["Nevertheless it was well with her,--within her own bosom.", "그런데도 그 아이는 괜찮았다. 제 가슴속만은 괜찮았다."], ["She had declared to herself that she would conquer her misery,--as she had also declared to herself during her illness that her misfortune should not kill her,--and she was in the way to conquer it.", "그 아이는 제 비참함을 이겨 내겠노라고 스스로에게 선언했었다. 앓는 동안 제 불행이 자기를 죽이지는 못하리라고 스스로에게 선언했던 것처럼."], ["She told herself that the world was not over for her because her sweet hopes had been frustrated.", "그리고 그 아이는 그것을 이겨 가는 길에 들어서 있었다."], ["The wound had been deep and very sore, but the flesh of the patient had been sound and healthy, and her blood pure.", "달콤한 희망이 꺾였다고 해서 자기에게 세상이 끝난 것은 아니라고 그 아이는 스스로에게 일렀다."], ["A physician having knowledge in such cases would have declared, after long watching of her symptoms, that a cure was probable.", "상처는 깊고 몹시 쓰라렸으나, 이 환자의 살은 튼튼하고 성했으며 피는 맑았다. 그런 병에 밝은 의사라면 오래도록 그 아이의 증세를 지켜본 끝에 나을 가망이 있다고 진단했을 것이다."], ["Her mother was the physician who watched her with the closest eyes; and she, though she was sometimes driven to doubt, did hope, with stronger hope from day to day, that her child might live to remember the story of her love without abiding agony.", "그 아이를 가장 가까이서 지켜본 의사는 어머니였다. 그리고 그녀는 이따금 의심에 몰리기도 했으나, 제 아이가 언젠가는 살아서 제 사랑의 이야기를 아리지 않은 마음으로 떠올릴 수 있으리라고, 날이 갈수록 더 굳센 희망으로 희망했다."]], [["That nobody should talk to her about it,--that had been the one stipulation which she had seemed to make, not sending forth a request to that effect among her friends in so many words, but showing by certain signs that such was her stipulation.", "아무도 그 일을 두고 자기에게 말하지 말 것. 그것이 그 아이가 내건 듯이 보이는 단 하나의 조건이었다."], ["A word to that effect she had spoken to her uncle,--as may be remembered, which word had been regarded with the closest obedience.", "그런 뜻의 부탁을 벗들에게 또박또박 말로 내보낸 것은 아니었으나, 몇 가지 기색으로 그것이 자기 조건임을 드러내 보였다. 그런 뜻의 한마디를 그 아이는 삼촌에게 했었고, 기억하시겠지만 그 한마디는 더없이 고분고분하게 지켜졌다."], ["She had gone out into her little world very soon after the news of Crosbie's falsehood had reached her,--first to church and then among the people of the village, resolving to carry herself as though no crushing weight had fallen upon her.", "크로즈비의 거짓 소식이 그 아이에게 닿고 나서 아주 이내 그 아이는 제 작은 세상으로 나섰다. 먼저 교회로, 그다음에는 마을 사람들 사이로."], ["The village people had understood it all, listening to her and answering her without the proffer of any outspoken parley.", "자기에게 아무런 짓누르는 무게도 떨어지지 않은 것처럼 처신하리라 마음먹고서. 마을 사람들은 그 모두를 알아들었고, 드러내 놓고 이러쿵저러쿵 늘어놓는 일 없이 그 아이의 말을 듣고 대꾸해 주었다."]], [["\"Lord bless 'ee,\" said Mrs.", "“하느님이 복 주시길입쇼.”"], ["Crump, the postmistress,--and Mrs.", "우체국장 크럼프 부인이 말했다."], ["Crump was supposed to have the sourest temper in Allington,--\"whenever I look at thee, Miss Lily, I thinks that surely thee is the beautifulest young 'ooman in all these parts.\"", "앨링턴에서 성미가 제일 고약하다고들 하는 사람이었다. “릴리 아가씨를 볼 적마다 저는 아가씨야말로 이 근방을 통틀어 젤로 이쁜 아가씨다 싶습니다요.”"]], [["\"And you are the crossest old woman,\"", "“그리고 아주머니는 제일 심술궂은 할머니시고요.”"], ["said Lily, laughing, and giving her hand to the postmistress.", "릴리가 웃으며 우체국장에게 손을 내밀면서 말했다."]], [["\"So I be,\"", "“그렇습죠.”"], ["said Mrs. Crump.", "크럼프 부인이 말했다."], ["\"So I be.\"", "“그렇고말굽쇼.”"], ["Then Lily sat down in the cottage and asked after her ailments. With Mrs.", "그러고 나서 릴리는 그 오두막에 앉아 그녀의 병치레를 물었다."], ["Hearn it was the same.", "헌 부인도 마찬가지였다."], ["Mrs. Hearn, after that first meeting which has been already mentioned, petted and caressed her, but spoke no further word of her misfortune.", "헌 부인은 앞서 이야기한 그 첫 만남 뒤로는 그 아이를 귀여워하고 어루만졌을 뿐, 그 아이의 불행에 대해서는 더 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["When Lily called a second time upon Mrs. Boyce, which she did boldly by herself, that lady did begin one other word of commiseration.", "릴리가 보이스 부인을 두 번째로 찾아갔을 때—그것도 혼자서 당당히 갔다—그 부인은 위로의 말을 한 마디 더 꺼내려 들었다."], ["\"My dearest Lily, we have all been made so unhappy--\" So far Mrs.", "“가엾은 우리 릴리, 우리 모두 어찌나 마음이 아팠는지—”"], ["Boyce got, sitting close to Lily and striving to look into her face; but Lily, with a slightly heightened colour, turned sharp round upon one of the Boyce girls, tearing Mrs.", "보이스 부인은 릴리 곁에 바싹 붙어 앉아 그 얼굴을 들여다보려 애쓰며 거기까지 말했다. 그러나 릴리는 뺨을 살짝 붉히면서 보이스네 딸들 가운데 하나를 향해 홱 돌아앉아, 보이스 부인의 위로를 잘디잔 조각으로 찢어 버렸다."], ["Boyce's commiseration into the smallest shreds. \"Minnie,\"", "“미니.”"], ["she said, speaking quite loud, almost with girlish ecstasy, \"what do you think Tartar did yesterday?", "그 아이는 거의 소녀다운 환희에 차서 아주 큰 소리로 말했다. “어제 타타르가 무슨 짓을 했는지 아니?"], ["I never laughed so much in my life.\"", "내 평생 그렇게 웃어 본 적이 없어.”"], ["Then she told a ludicrous story about a very ugly terrier which belonged to the squire.", "그러고는 지주네 아주 못생긴 테리어에 얽힌 우스꽝스러운 이야기를 늘어놓았다."], ["After that even Mrs. Boyce made no further attempt.", "그 뒤로는 보이스 부인마저 더 손을 대 보려 하지 않았다."], ["Mrs. Dale and Bell both understood that such was to be the rule--the rule even to them.", "데일 부인과 벨도 그것이 규칙임을, 자기들에게조차 그대로 적용되는 규칙임을 알아차렸다."], ["Lily would speak to them occasionally on the matter,--to one of them at a time, beginning with some almost single word of melancholy resignation, and then would go on till she opened her very bosom before them; but no such conversation was ever begun by them.", "릴리는 이따금 그 일을 두고 그들에게 말하곤 했다. 한 번에 한 사람씩, 체념 어린 우울한 한마디 같은 것으로 시작해서, 이윽고 그들 앞에 제 가슴을 활짝 열어 보일 때까지 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["But now, in these busy days of the packing, that topic seemed to have been banished altogether.", "그러나 그런 이야기를 그들 쪽에서 먼저 꺼낸 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그런데 이제, 이 분주한 짐 싸기의 나날에는 그 화제가 아예 자취를 감춘 듯했다."]], [["\"Mamma,\"", "“엄마.”"], ["she said, standing on the top rung of a house-ladder, from which position she was handing down glass out of a cupboard, \"are you sure that these things are ours?", "그 아이가 발판 사다리 맨 윗단에 올라선 채 말했다. 거기서 찬장 속의 유리그릇을 아래로 건네주는 참이었다."], ["I think some of them belong to the house.\"", "“이것들이 정말 우리 것이 맞아요? 몇 개는 이 집에 딸린 물건 같은데요.”"]], [["\"I'm sure about that bowl at any rate, because it was my mother's before I was married.\"", "“적어도 저 사발만은 확실하다. 내가 시집오기 전부터 우리 어머니 것이었으니까.”"]], [["\"Oh, dear, what should I do if I were to break it?", "“어머나, 이걸 깨뜨리기라도 하면 어쩌죠?"], ["Whenever I handle anything very precious I always feel inclined to throw it down and smash it.", "저는 아주 귀한 물건을 손에 들 때마다 늘 그걸 내던져서 박살 내고 싶어져요."], ["Oh!", "어머!"], ["it was as nearly gone as possible, mamma; but that was your fault.\"", "하마터면 정말 갈 뻔했네요, 엄마. 그건 엄마 잘못이에요.”"]], [["\"If you don't take care you'll be nearly gone yourself.", "“조심하지 않으면 이번엔 너 자신이 하마터면 갈 뻔하게 될 게다."], ["Do take hold of something.\"", "뭐라도 좀 붙잡으렴.”"]], [["\"Oh, Bell, here's the inkstand for which you've been moaning for three years.\"", "“어머, 언니, 여기 언니가 삼 년째 없어졌다고 한탄하던 잉크스탠드가 있어.”"]], [["\"I haven't been moaning for three years; but who could have put it up there?\"", "“삼 년째 한탄한 적 없거니와, 대체 누가 저기다 올려놓았을까?”"]], [["\"Catch it,\"", "“받아.”"], ["said Lily; and she threw the bottle down on to a pile of carpets.", "릴리는 이렇게 말하고는 그 병을 융단 더미 위로 던져 내렸다."]], [["At this moment a step was heard in the hall, and the squire entered through the open door of the room.", "이때 현관에서 발소리가 들리더니 지주가 열린 문으로 방에 들어섰다. “다들 일하는 중이구먼.”"], ["\"So you're all at work,\" said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, we're at work,\"", "“네, 일하고 있어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale, almost with a tone of shame.", "데일 부인이 거의 부끄러워하는 어조로 말했다."], ["\"If it is to be done it is as well that it should be got over.\"", "“해야 할 일이라면 얼른 해치우는 편이 나으니까요.”"]], [["\"It makes me wretched enough,\"", "“나야말로 이 일이 아주 비참하오.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"But I didn't come to talk about that.", "“허나 그 얘기를 하러 온 것은 아니오."], ["I've brought you a note from Lady Julia De Guest, and I've had one from the earl.", "레이디 줄리아 드 게스트가 제수씨에게 보낸 쪽지를 가져왔소. 그리고 나도 백작에게서 한 통 받았고."], ["They want us all to go there and stay the week after Easter.\"", "부활절 다음 주에 우리 모두 그리로 와서 한 주 묵기를 바란다는구려.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale and the girls, when this very sudden proposition was made to them, all remained fixed in their places, and, for a moment, were speechless.", "이 아주 느닷없는 제안을 받고서 데일 부인과 두 딸은 모두 제자리에 못 박힌 채 한동안 말을 잃었다."], ["Go and stay a week at Guestwick Manor!", "게스트윅 저택에 가서 한 주를 묵는다니!"], ["The whole family!", "온 식구가!"], ["Hitherto the intercourse between the Manor and the Small House had been confined to morning calls, very far between.", "지금껏 저택과 작은 집 사이의 왕래라고는 아주 뜸한 오전 방문이 전부였다."], ["Mrs. Dale had never dined there, and had latterly even deputed the calling to her daughters.", "데일 부인은 그곳에서 만찬을 든 적이 한 번도 없었고, 근래에는 그 방문마저 딸들에게 맡겨 두었다."], ["Once Bell had dined there with her uncle, the squire, and once Lily had gone over with her uncle Orlando.", "한 번은 벨이 삼촌인 지주와 함께 그곳에서 만찬을 들었고, 또 한 번은 릴리가 올랜도 삼촌과 함께 건너갔다."], ["Even this had been long ago, before they were quite brought out, and they had regarded the occasion with the solemn awe of children.", "그것조차도 아주 오래전, 그들이 채 사교계에 나오기도 전의 일이었고, 그들은 그 자리를 아이들이 품는 엄숙한 경외로 대했었다."], ["Now, at this time of their flitting into some small mean dwelling at Guestwick, they had previously settled among themselves that that affair of calling at the Manor might be allowed to drop. Mrs.", "그런데 이제 게스트윅의 작고 초라한 살림집으로 옮겨 가는 이 마당에, 저택으로 방문하는 그 일도 그만 흐지부지 접어 두어도 되겠다고 자기들끼리 미리 정해 놓은 참이었다."], ["Eames never called, and they were descending to the level of Mrs. Eames.", "임스 부인은 방문하는 법이 없었고, 자기들도 임스 부인의 자리로 내려앉는 중이었으니까."], ["\"Perhaps we shall get game sent to us, and that will be better,\"", "“어쩌면 사냥한 고기를 보내 줄지도 모르지."], ["Lily had said.", "그게 더 낫겠다.”"], ["And now, at this very moment of their descent in life, they were all asked to go and stay a week at the Manor!", "릴리는 그렇게 말했었다. 그런데 이제, 살림이 내려앉는 바로 이 순간에 그들 모두가 저택에 가서 한 주를 묵으라는 청을 받은 것이다!"], ["Stay a week with Lady Julia!", "레이디 줄리아와 한 주를 지내라니!"], ["Had the Queen sent the Lord Chamberlain down to bid them all go to Windsor Castle it could hardly have startled them more at the first blow.", "여왕께서 궁내대신을 내려보내 다들 윈저 성으로 오라 이르셨더라도 첫 충격이 이보다 크지는 않았을 것이다."], ["Bell had been seated on the folded carpet when her uncle had entered, and now had again sat herself in the same place.", "삼촌이 들어섰을 때 벨은 개어 놓은 융단 위에 앉아 있었는데, 지금 다시 같은 자리에 주저앉았다."], ["Lily was still standing at the top of the ladder, and Mrs. Dale was at the foot with one hand on Lily's dress.", "릴리는 여전히 사다리 꼭대기에 서 있었고, 데일 부인은 그 아래에서 한 손을 릴리의 옷자락에 얹고 있었다."], ["The squire had told his story very abruptly, but he was a man who, having a story to tell, knew nothing better than to tell it out abruptly, letting out everything at the first moment.", "지주는 제 이야기를 아주 불쑥 꺼냈다. 그러나 그는 할 이야기가 생기면 불쑥 내뱉어 첫 순간에 죄다 털어놓는 것 말고는 더 나은 수를 알지 못하는 사람이었다."]], [["\"Wants us all!\"", "“우리 모두라니요!”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"How many does the all mean?\"", "“모두라면 몇 사람을 말하는 건가요?”"], ["Then she opened Lady Julia's note and read it, not moving from her position at the foot of the ladder.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 사다리 아래 제자리에서 움직이지도 않은 채 레이디 줄리아의 쪽지를 펴서 읽었다."]], [["\"Do let me see, mamma,\"", "“저도 좀 보여 주세요, 엄마.”"], ["said Lily; and then the note was handed up to her. Had Mrs.", "릴리가 말했고, 쪽지는 위로 건네졌다."], ["Dale well considered the matter she might probably have kept the note to herself for a while, but the whole thing was so sudden that she had not considered the matter well.", "데일 부인이 그 일을 잘 헤아려 보았더라면 아마 그 쪽지를 얼마 동안 제 손에 쥐고 있었을 것이다. 그러나 모든 일이 워낙 갑작스러워서 그녀는 그것을 잘 헤아려 보지 못했다."]], [["MY DEAR MRS. DALE [the letter ran],", "친애하는 데일 부인께 [편지는 이렇게 적혀 있었다],"]], [["I send this inside a note from my brother to Mr. Dale.", "이 쪽지는 제 오라버니가 데일 씨께 보내는 편지 안에 함께 넣어 보냅니다."], ["We particularly want you and your two girls to come to us for a week from the seventeenth of this month.", "저희는 부인과 두 따님이 이달 십칠일부터 한 주 동안 저희에게 와 주시기를 각별히 바라고 있습니다."], ["Considering our near connection we ought to have seen more of each other than we have done for years past, and of course it has been our fault.", "저희 두 집안의 가까운 인연을 생각하면 지난 여러 해 동안 서로 좀 더 자주 만났어야 마땅했고, 물론 그것은 저희 잘못입니다."], ["But it is never too late to amend one's ways; and I hope you will receive my confession in the true spirit of affection in which it is intended, and that you will show your goodness by coming to us.", "그러나 제 몸가짐을 고치는 데에 너무 늦은 때란 없는 법이지요. 제 고백을 그 안에 담긴 참된 애정의 마음 그대로 받아 주시고, 저희에게 와 주심으로써 부인의 너그러움을 보여 주시기를 바랍니다."], ["I will do all I can to make the house pleasant to your girls, for both of whom I have much real regard.", "두 따님이 이 집을 즐겁게 여기도록 제가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 하겠습니다. 저는 두 분 모두에게 참으로 큰 정을 품고 있으니까요."]], [["I should tell you that John Eames will be here for the same week.", "말씀드려 두어야 할 것은, 같은 주에 존 임스도 이곳에 와 있으리라는 것입니다."], ["My brother is very fond of him, and thinks him the best young man of the day.", "제 오라버니가 그를 무척 아끼며 요즘 젊은이 가운데 으뜸이라고 여깁니다."], ["He is one of my heroes, too, I must confess.", "저 또한 그를 제 영웅 가운데 하나로 여긴다고 고백해야겠습니다."]], [["Very sincerely yours,", "더없이 진실한 마음을 담아,"]], [["Lily, standing on the ladder, read the letter very attentively.", "사다리에 선 채로 릴리는 그 편지를 아주 주의 깊게 읽었다."], ["The squire meanwhile stood below speaking a word or two to his sister-in-law and niece.", "그동안 지주는 아래에 서서 제수와 조카딸에게 한두 마디를 건네고 있었다."], ["No one could see Lily's face, as it was turned away towards the window, and it was still averted when she spoke.", "릴리의 얼굴은 창 쪽으로 돌려져 있어서 아무도 볼 수 없었고, 그 아이가 입을 열 때에도 여전히 돌린 채였다."], ["\"It is out of the question that we should go, mamma;--that is, all of us.\"", "“우리가 간다는 건 말도 안 돼요, 엄마. 그러니까, 우리 모두가 가는 건요.”"]], [["\"Why out of the question?\"", "“왜 말도 안 된다는 게냐?”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"A whole family!\"", "“온 식구가 다요!”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"That is just what they want,\"", "“그게 바로 그쪽에서 바라는 바요.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"I should like of all things to be left alone for a week,\"", "“저는 한 주 동안 혼자 남는다면 무엇보다 좋겠어요.”"], ["said Lily, \"if mamma and Bell would go.\"", "릴리가 말했다. “엄마와 언니가 가신다면요.”"]], [["\"That wouldn't do at all,\"", "“그건 전혀 안 될 말이다.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"Lady Julia specially wants you to be one of the party.\"", "“레이디 줄리아는 특별히 네가 그 자리에 끼기를 바라고 있다.”"]], [["The thing had been badly managed altogether.", "이 일은 처음부터 끝까지 서투르게 다루어진 셈이었다."], ["The reference in Lady Julia's note to John Eames had explained to Lily the whole scheme at once, and had so opened her eyes that all the combined influence of the Dale and De Guest families could not have dragged her over to the Manor.", "레이디 줄리아의 쪽지에서 존 임스를 언급한 대목이 릴리에게 그 계획의 전모를 단번에 알려 주었고, 그 아이의 눈을 하도 활짝 열어 놓은 터라 데일 집안과 드 게스트 집안의 힘을 죄다 합쳐도 그 아이를 저택으로 끌고 갈 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["\"Why not do?\" said Lily.", "“왜 안 될 말이에요?”"], ["\"It would be out of the question a whole family going in that way, but it would be very nice for Bell.\"", "릴리가 말했다. “온 식구가 그런 식으로 가는 건 말도 안 되겠지만, 언니한테는 아주 좋을 텐데요.”"]], [["\"No, it would not,\"", "“아니, 좋지 않아.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Don't be ungenerous about it, my dear,\"", "“그 일을 두고 야박하게 굴지 마라, 얘야.”"], ["said the squire, turning to Bell; \"Lady Julia means to be kind.", "지주가 벨 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. “레이디 줄리아는 친절을 베풀려는 게다."], ["But, my darling,\"", "하지만, 우리 귀한 것아,”"], ["and the squire turned again towards Lily, addressing her, as was his wont in these days, with an affection that was almost vexatious to her; \"but, my darling, why should you not go?", "지주는 다시 릴리 쪽으로 돌아서서, 요즈음의 버릇대로 그 아이에게는 거의 성가시게 느껴질 만큼 다정하게 말을 건넸다. “하지만 우리 귀한 것아, 네가 못 갈 까닭이 무엇이냐?"], ["A change of scene like that will do you all the good in the world, just when you are getting well.", "그런 자리 바꿈은 이제 막 몸이 나아 가는 너에게 이 세상 무엇보다 좋을 게다."], ["Mary, tell the girls that they ought to go.\"", "제수씨, 이 아이들에게 가야 마땅하다고 말해 주시오.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale stood silent, again reading the note, and Lily came down from the ladder.", "데일 부인은 말없이 서서 그 쪽지를 다시 읽었고, 릴리는 사다리에서 내려왔다."], ["When she reached the floor she went directly up to her uncle, and taking his hand turned him round with herself towards one of the windows, so that they stood with their backs to the room.", "바닥에 발을 딛자 그 아이는 곧장 삼촌에게 다가가 그 손을 잡고서 자기와 함께 창 하나 쪽으로 돌려세웠고, 그리하여 두 사람은 방을 등지고 서게 되었다."], ["\"Uncle,\"", "“삼촌.”"], ["she said, \"do not be angry with me.", "그 아이가 말했다. “저에게 화내지 마세요."], ["I can't go;\"", "저는 갈 수 없어요.”"], ["and then she put up her face to kiss him.", "그러고는 입을 맞추려고 얼굴을 들어 올렸다."]], [["He stooped and kissed her and still held her hand.", "그는 몸을 숙여 입을 맞추고도 그 손은 여전히 놓지 않았다."], ["He looked into her face and read it all.", "그는 그 아이의 얼굴을 들여다보고 거기 있는 것을 죄다 읽었다."], ["He knew well, now, why she could not go; or, rather, why she herself thought that she could not go.", "이제 그는 그 아이가 왜 갈 수 없는지를, 아니 그보다도 그 아이 자신이 왜 갈 수 없다고 여기는지를 잘 알고 있었다."], ["\"Cannot you, my darling?\"", "“못 가겠느냐, 우리 귀한 것아?”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"No, uncle.", "“네, 삼촌."], ["It is very kind,--very kind; but I cannot go.", "정말 고마운 일이에요, 정말로 고마운 일이지만 저는 갈 수 없어요."], ["I am not fit to go anywhere.\"", "저는 어디에도 갈 만하지가 못해요.”"]], [["\"But you should get over that feeling.", "“하지만 그런 마음은 이겨 내야지."], ["You should make a struggle.\"", "애를 좀 써 보아야 한다.”"]], [["\"I am struggling, and I shall succeed; but I cannot do it all at once.", "“애쓰고 있고, 저는 해낼 거예요. 하지만 한꺼번에 다 할 수는 없어요."], ["At any rate I could not go there.", "어쨌든 그곳에는 갈 수 없어요."], ["You must give my love to Lady Julia, and not let her think me cross.", "레이디 줄리아에게 제 사랑을 전해 주시고, 그분이 저를 심술궂다 여기지 않게 해 주세요."], ["Perhaps Bell will go.\"", "어쩌면 언니는 갈지도 모르잖아요.”"]], [["What would be the good of Bell's going--or the good of his putting himself out of the way, by a visit which would of itself be so tiresome to him, if the one object of the visit could not be carried out?", "벨이 간들 무슨 소용이며, 그 방문의 단 하나뿐인 목적이 이루어질 수 없다면 그 자체로 그토록 성가실 나들이를 위해 제가 불편을 무릅쓴들 또 무슨 소용이겠는가?"], ["The earl and his sister had planned the invitation with the express intention of bringing Lily and Eames together.", "백작과 그 누이는 릴리와 임스를 맺어 주겠다는 뚜렷한 뜻을 품고 이 초대를 꾸민 것이었다. 릴리는 그 뜻에 맞서겠다는 결심이 굳은 듯했다."], ["It seemed that Lily was firm in her determination to resist this intention; and, if so, it would be better that the whole thing should fall to the ground.", "그렇다면 이 일은 통째로 물거품이 되는 편이 나았다. 그는 몹시 속이 상했으나, 그러면서도 그 아이에게 화가 나지는 않았다."], ["He was very vexed, and yet he was not angry with her.", "요즈음 들어 모두가 무슨 일에서든 그에게 맞섰다."], ["Everybody lately had opposed him in everything.", "집안을 위해 그가 마련하려 한 것은 죄다 어그러졌다."], ["All his intended family arrangements had gone wrong.", "그런데도 그는 그런 일을 두고 좀처럼 화를 내지 않았다."], ["But yet he was seldom angry respecting them. He was so accustomed to be thwarted that he hardly expected success.", "하도 가로막히는 데 익숙해진 터라 성공을 기대하는 일이 거의 없었던 것이다."], ["In this matter of providing Lily with a second lover, he had not come forward of his own accord.", "릴리에게 두 번째 연인을 마련해 주는 이번 일에서도 그가 제 발로 나선 것은 아니었다."], ["He had been appealed to by his neighbour the earl, and had certainly answered the appeal with much generosity.", "이웃인 백작이 그에게 청해 왔고, 그는 그 청에 참으로 넉넉한 마음으로 응했다."], ["He had been induced to make the attempt with eagerness, and a true desire for its accomplishment; but in this, as in all his own schemes, he was met at once by opposition and failure.", "그는 열의를 품고, 그것이 이루어지기를 진심으로 바라면서 이 시도에 나서게 되었던 것이다. 그러나 이번에도 제가 꾸민 다른 모든 계획에서와 마찬가지로 그는 곧바로 맞섬과 실패에 부딪혔다."]], [["\"I will leave you to talk it over among yourselves,\"", "“자네들끼리 의논하도록 남겨 두고 가겠소.” 그가 말했다."], ["he said. \"But, Mary, you had better see me before you send your answer.", "“허나 제수씨, 답장을 보내기 전에 나를 한번 만나 보는 편이 좋겠소."], ["If you will come up by-and-by, Ralph shall take the two notes over together in the afternoon.\"", "이따가 올라와 준다면 오후에 랠프가 두 통을 함께 가져가도록 하리다.”"], ["So saying, he left the Small House, and went back to his own solitary home.", "그렇게 말하고서 그는 작은 집을 나와 제 쓸쓸한 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["\"Lily, dear,\"", "“릴리야.”"], ["said Mrs.", "현관문이 닫히자마자 데일 부인이 말했다."], ["Dale, as soon as the front door had been closed, \"this is meant for kindness to you,--for most affectionate kindness.\"", "“이건 너에게 친절을 베풀려는 마음에서 나온 일이란다. 더없이 다정한 친절 말이다.”"]], [["\"I know it, mamma; and you must go to Lady Julia, and must tell her that I know it.", "“저도 알아요, 엄마. 그러니 엄마가 레이디 줄리아에게 가셔서 제가 안다고 전해 주세요."], ["You must give her my love.", "제 사랑도 전해 주셔야 해요."], ["And, indeed, I do love her now.", "그리고 정말이지 저는 이제 그분을 사랑해요."], ["But--\"", "하지만—”"]], [["\"You won't go, Lily?\"", "“안 가겠다는 거지, 릴리?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale, beseechingly.", "데일 부인이 애원하듯 말했다."]], [["\"No, mamma; certainly I will not go.\"", "“네, 엄마. 저는 정말 가지 않을 거예요.”"], ["Then she escaped out of the room by herself, and for the next hour neither of them dared to go to her.", "그러고서 그 아이는 방을 빠져나가 혼자가 되었고, 그 뒤로 한 시간 동안 두 사람 가운데 누구도 감히 그 아이에게 가지 못했다."]], [["# CHAPTER L. MRS. DALE IS THANKFUL FOR A GOOD THING.", "# 제50장. 데일 부인이 좋은 일에 감사하다"]], [["On that day they dined early at the Small House, as they had been in the habit of doing since the packing had commenced.", "그날 작은 집에서는 저녁을 일찍 먹었다. 짐 꾸리기가 시작된 뒤로 늘 그래 왔기 때문이다."], ["And after dinner Mrs. Dale went through the gardens, up to the other house, with a written note in her hand.", "저녁을 먹고 나서 데일 부인은 손에 쪽지 한 장을 들고 정원을 가로질러 저쪽 집으로 올라갔다."], ["In that note she had told Lady Julia, with many protestations of gratitude, that Lily was unable to go out so soon after her illness, and that she herself was obliged to stay with Lily.", "그 쪽지에서 그녀는 레이디 줄리아에게 거듭거듭 고마움을 표하면서, 릴리가 앓고 난 지 얼마 되지 않아 이렇게 이른 시일에는 나들이를 할 수 없고 자기도 릴리 곁에 남아 있어야 한다고 적었다."], ["She explained also, that the business of moving was in hand, and that, therefore, she could not herself accept the invitation.", "또 이사 일이 한창이라 자신은 초대에 응할 수 없노라고 설명했다."], ["But her other daughter, she said, would be very happy to accompany her uncle to Guestwick Manor.", "그러나 다른 딸아이는 삼촌을 따라 게스트윅 저택에 가게 되어 무척 기뻐할 것이라고 했다."], ["Then, without closing her letter, she took it up to the squire in order that it might be decided whether it would or would not suit his views.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 편지를 봉하지 않은 채 지주에게 들고 올라갔다. 그것이 그의 뜻에 맞을지 아닐지 정하게 하려는 것이었다."], ["It might well be that he would not care to go to Lord De Guest's with Bell alone.", "그가 벨만 데리고 드 게스트 경의 댁에 가는 것을 내키지 않아 할 수도 있는 일이었다."]], [["\"Leave it with me,\"", "“나한테 맡겨 두시오.”"], ["he said; \"that is, if you do not object.\"", "그가 말했다. “제수씨가 마다하지 않는다면 말이오.”"]], [["\"Oh dear, no!\"", "“어머, 그럴 리가요!”"]], [["\"I'll tell you the plain truth at once, Mary.", "“솔직한 사정을 곧바로 말해 주리다, 제수씨."], ["I shall go over myself with it, and see the earl.", "나는 이 쪽지를 들고 내가 직접 건너가 백작을 만나 볼 참이오."], ["Then I will decline it or not, according to what passes between me and him.", "그러고 나서 그와 나 사이에 오가는 이야기에 따라 초대를 물리든지 말든지 하겠소."], ["I wish Lily would have gone.\"", "릴리가 갔더라면 좋았을 것을.”"]], [["\"Ah! she could not.\"", "“아, 그 애는 갈 수가 없었어요.”"]], [["\"I wish she could.", "“갈 수 있었더라면."], ["I wish she could.", "갈 수 있었더라면."], ["I wish she could.\"", "갈 수 있었더라면.”"], ["As he repeated the words over and over again, there was an eagerness in his voice that filled Mrs. Dale's heart with tenderness towards him.", "그가 그 말을 몇 번이고 되뇌는데, 그 목소리에는 간절함이 배어 있어서 데일 부인의 마음은 그를 향한 애틋함으로 가득 찼다."]], [["\"The truth is,\"", "“실은요,”"], ["said Mrs. Dale, \"she could not go there to meet John Eames.\"", "데일 부인이 말했다, “그 애는 존 임스를 만나러 거기 갈 수가 없어요.”"]], [["\"Oh, I know,\"", "“아, 나도 아오.”"], ["said the squire: \"I understand it.", "지주가 말했다. “그건 나도 이해하오."], ["But that is just what we want her to do.", "하지만 우리가 그 애더러 바라는 것이 바로 그것 아니오."], ["Why should she not spend a week in the same house with an honest young man whom we all like.\"", "우리 모두가 좋아하는 정직한 젊은이와 한집에서 일주일을 지내지 못할 까닭이 무어란 말이오.”"]], [["\"There are reasons why she would not wish it.\"", "“그 애가 그러고 싶어 하지 않을 까닭이 있어요.”"]], [["\"Ah, exactly; the very reasons which should make us induce her to go there if we can.", "“그렇지, 바로 그거요. 그 까닭이야말로 우리가 할 수만 있다면 그 애를 거기 가도록 구슬려야 할 이유란 말이오."], ["Perhaps I had better tell you all.", "아무래도 제수씨에게 전부 털어놓는 편이 낫겠소."], ["Lord De Guest has taken him by the hand, and wishes him to marry.", "드 게스트 경이 그 청년의 손을 잡아 주었고, 그를 장가보내고 싶어 하오."], ["He has promised to settle on him an income which will make him comfortable for life.\"", "백작은 그가 평생 넉넉히 지낼 만한 수입을 그 앞으로 떼어 주겠노라 약속했소.”"]], [["\"That is very generous; and I am delighted to hear it,--for John's sake.\"", "“참으로 후한 일이네요. 저도 그 말씀을 들으니 기뻐요. 존을 생각하면 말이에요.”"]], [["\"And they have promoted him at his office.\"", "“게다가 사무국에서 그를 승진시켰다오.”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“어머!"], ["then he will do well.\"", "그러면 그 사람은 잘될 거예요.”"]], [["\"He will do very well.", "“아주 잘될 거요."], ["He is private secretary now to their head man.", "지금은 그 사무국 우두머리의 개인 비서라오."], ["And, Mary, so that she, Lily, should not be empty-handed if this marriage can be arranged, I have undertaken to settle a hundred a year on her,--on her and her children, if she will accept him.", "그리고 제수씨, 이 혼사가 이루어질 수 있다면 그 애가, 릴리가 빈손으로 가는 일이 없도록, 내가 연 백 파운드를 그 애 앞으로 떼어 주기로 했소. 그 애가 그를 받아들인다면 그 애와 그 애 자식들 앞으로 말이오."], ["Now you know it all.", "이제 다 아셨소."], ["I did not mean to tell you; but it is as well that you should have the means of judging.", "말할 생각은 아니었소만, 제수씨도 헤아려 볼 거리는 있어야 할 테니 잘된 일이지."], ["That other man was a villain.", "그 사내는 악당이었소."], ["This man is honest.", "이 사람은 정직하오."], ["Would it not be well that she should learn to like him?", "그 애가 이 사람을 좋아하도록 배우는 것이 좋지 않겠소?"], ["She always did like him, I thought, before that other fellow came down here among us.\"", "그 다른 녀석이 우리 사이로 내려오기 전까지는 그 애도 늘 이 사람을 좋아했다고 나는 생각했는데.”"]], [["\"She has always liked him--as a friend.\"", "“그 애는 늘 그이를 좋아했지요. 벗으로서요.”"]], [["\"She will never get a better lover.\"", "“그보다 나은 연인은 다시 못 얻을 거요.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale sat silent, thinking over it all.", "데일 부인은 잠자코 앉아 그 모든 것을 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["Every word that the squire said was true.", "지주가 한 말은 한마디 한마디가 다 옳았다."], ["It would be a healing of wounds most desirable and salutary; an arrangement advantageous to them all; a destiny for Lily most devoutly to be desired,--if only it were possible.", "그것은 더할 나위 없이 바람직하고 이로운 상처의 치유가 될 터였고, 그들 모두에게 득이 되는 일이었으며, 릴리에게는 간절히 바라 마지않을 운명이었다. 그저 그것이 될 수 있는 일이기만 하다면."], ["Mrs. Dale firmly believed that if her daughter could be made to accept John Eames as her second lover in a year or two all would be well.", "데일 부인은 딸이 한두 해 안에 존 임스를 두 번째 연인으로 받아들이게만 된다면 모든 것이 잘되리라고 굳게 믿었다."], ["Crosbie would then be forgotten or thought of without regret, and Lily would become the mistress of a happy home.", "그리 되면 크로즈비는 잊히거나 미련 없이 떠올려질 것이고, 릴리는 행복한 가정의 안주인이 될 터였다."], ["But there are positions which cannot be reached, though there be no physical or material objection in the way.", "그러나 길을 막는 물리적·물질적 걸림돌이 하나도 없는데도 다다를 수 없는 자리라는 것이 있는 법이다."], ["It is the view which the mind takes of a thing which creates the sorrow that arises from it.", "어떤 일에서 슬픔이 생겨나게 하는 것은 그 일을 두고 마음이 취하는 관점이다."], ["If the heart were always malleable and the feelings could be controlled, who would permit himself to be tormented by any of the reverses which affection meets?", "심장이 언제나 마음대로 주무를 수 있는 것이고 감정을 다스릴 수 있는 것이라면, 사랑이 맞닥뜨리는 어떤 뒤틀림 때문에 스스로 괴로워하기를 허락할 사람이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["Death would create no sorrow; ingratitude would lose its sting; and the betrayal of love would do no injury beyond that which it might entail upon worldly circumstances.", "죽음도 슬픔을 낳지 않을 것이고, 배은망덕도 그 가시를 잃을 것이며, 사랑의 배신도 세상살이 형편에 끼치는 손해 말고는 아무 해도 입히지 못할 것이다."], ["But the heart is not malleable; nor will the feelings admit of such control.", "그러나 심장은 마음대로 주무를 수 있는 것이 아니며, 감정 또한 그런 다스림을 받아들이지 않는다."]], [["\"It is not possible for her,\"", "“그 애로서는 될 수 없는 일이에요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I fear it is not possible.", "“아무래도 될 수 없는 일 같아요."], ["It is too soon.\"", "너무 이르거든요.”"]], [["\"Six months,\"", "“여섯 달이오.”"], ["pleaded the squire.", "지주가 사정하듯 말했다."]], [["\"It will take years,--not months,\"", "“달이 아니라 해가 걸릴 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"And she will lose all her youth.\"", "“그러다 그 애는 젊음을 몽땅 잃고 말 텐데.”"]], [["\"Yes; he has done all that by his treachery.", "“그래요, 그 사람이 배신으로 그 일을 다 저질렀지요."], ["But it is done, and we cannot now go back.", "하지만 이미 저질러진 일이고, 이제 와 돌이킬 수는 없어요."], ["She loves him yet as dearly as she ever loved him.\"", "그 애는 아직도 그 사람을 예전에 사랑하던 그대로 애틋하게 사랑하고 있답니다.”"]], [["Then the squire muttered certain words below his breath,--ejaculations against Crosbie, which were hardly voluntary; but even as involuntary ejaculations were very improper.", "그러자 지주는 숨소리보다 낮게 몇 마디를 중얼거렸다. 크로즈비를 향해 내뱉은 그 험한 말은 그가 하려고 해서 나온 것이라 하기 어려웠으나, 저도 모르게 나온 말이라 해도 무척 온당치 못한 것이었다."], ["Mrs. Dale heard them, and was not offended either by their impropriety or their warmth.", "데일 부인은 그 말을 들었으나 그 온당치 못함에도, 그 뜨거움에도 언짢아하지 않았다."], ["\"But you can understand,\"", "“그러니 시아주버니도 아시겠지요.”"], ["she said, \"that she cannot bring herself to go there.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “그 애가 도저히 거기 갈 마음을 낼 수 없다는 것을요.”"], ["The squire struck the table with his fist, and repeated his ejaculations.", "지주는 주먹으로 탁자를 내리치며 다시 한번 험한 말을 뱉었다."], ["If he could only have known how very disagreeable Lady Alexandrina was making herself, his spirit might, perhaps, have been less vehemently disturbed.", "레이디 알렉산드리나가 얼마나 못 견디게 굴고 있는지 그가 알기만 했더라면 그의 마음이 그토록 사납게 흔들리지는 않았을지도 모른다."], ["If, also, he could have perceived and understood the light in which an alliance with the De Courcy family was now regarded by Crosbie, I think that he would have received some consolation from that consideration.", "또 크로즈비가 이제 드 코시 집안과 맺은 인연을 어떤 눈으로 보고 있는지 그가 헤아려 알 수 있었더라면, 나는 그가 거기서 얼마간 위로를 받았으리라고 생각한다."], ["Those who offend us are generally punished for the offence they give; but we so frequently miss the satisfaction of knowing that we are avenged!", "우리를 해친 자들은 대개 저희가 저지른 잘못의 벌을 받는다. 그러나 우리는 우리 원수가 갚아졌다는 사실을 아는 만족을 얼마나 자주 놓치고 마는가!"], ["It is arranged, apparently, that the injurer shall be punished, but that the person injured shall not gratify his desire for vengeance.", "아무래도 세상 이치는, 해친 자는 벌을 받되 해를 입은 자는 앙갚음하고픈 마음을 채우지 못하도록 짜여 있는 모양이다."]], [["\"And will you go to Guestwick yourself?\"", "“그러면 시아주버니께서 손수 게스트윅에 가시겠어요?”"], ["asked Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"I will take the note,\"", "“내가 쪽지를 가져가겠소.”"], ["said the squire, \"and will let you know to-morrow.", "지주가 말했다. “그리고 내일 알려 드리리다."], ["The earl has behaved so kindly that every possible consideration is due to him.", "백작이 하도 후하게 대해 주었으니 할 수 있는 만큼은 마음을 써 드려야 하오."], ["I had better tell him the whole truth, and go or stay, as he may wish.", "아무래도 그에게 사실을 죄다 털어놓고, 그가 바라는 대로 가든지 말든지 하는 편이 낫겠소."], ["I don't see the good of going.", "가서 무슨 좋을 것이 있을지 모르겠구려."], ["What am I to do at Guestwick Manor?", "게스트윅 저택에 가서 내가 무얼 한단 말이오?"], ["I did think that if we had all been there it might have cured some difficulties.\"", "우리가 다 함께 거기 갔더라면 어려운 일이 얼마쯤 풀렸을지도 모른다고, 나는 그렇게 생각하기는 했소만.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale got up to leave him, but she could not go without saying some word of gratitude for all that he had attempted to do for them.", "데일 부인은 자리를 뜨려고 일어섰으나, 그가 자기네를 위해 애써 준 모든 일에 고마움을 표하는 말 한마디 없이는 갈 수가 없었다."], ["She well knew what he meant by the curing of difficulties.", "어려운 일이 풀린다는 말로 그가 무엇을 뜻했는지 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["He had intended to signify that had they lived together for a week at Guestwick the idea of flitting from Allington might possibly have been abandoned.", "게스트윅에서 다 함께 일주일을 지냈더라면 앨링턴을 떠난다는 생각을 어쩌면 접었을지도 모른다는 뜻이었다."], ["It seemed now to Mrs. Dale as though her brother-in-law were heaping coals of fire on her head in return for that intention.", "이제 데일 부인에게는 시아주버니가 그 속셈에 대한 갚음으로 자기 머리 위에 숯불을 쌓아 올리는 것만 같았다."], ["She felt half-ashamed of what she was doing, almost acknowledging to herself that she should have borne with his sternness in return for the benefits he had done to her daughters.", "그녀는 자기가 하고 있는 일이 반쯤 부끄러워졌고, 그가 두 딸에게 베푼 은덕을 생각하면 그의 매몰참쯤은 참았어야 했다고 스스로 거의 인정하다시피 했다."], ["Had she not feared their reproaches she would, even now, have given way.", "딸들의 나무람이 두렵지만 않았더라면 그녀는 지금이라도 뜻을 굽혔을 것이다."]], [["\"I do not know what I ought to say to you for your kindness.\"", "“시아주버니의 그 마음씨에 제가 무어라 말씀드려야 할지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["\"Say nothing,--either for my kindness or unkindness; but stay where you are, and let us live like Christians together, striving to think good and not evil.\"", "“아무 말도 마시오. 내 마음씨가 곱든 사납든 말이오."], ["These were kind, loving words, showing in themselves a spirit of love and forbearance; but they were spoken in a harsh, unsympathizing voice, and the speaker, as he uttered them, looked gloomily at the fire.", "그저 있던 자리에 그대로 머물러, 우리 함께 그리스도인답게 살면서 악이 아니라 선을 생각하려 애써 봅시다.” 이는 다정하고 사랑이 담긴 말이었고, 그 말 자체에 너그러움과 참아 주는 마음이 배어 있었다."], ["In truth the squire, as he spoke, was half-ashamed of the warmth of what he said.", "그러나 그 말은 거칠고 무뚝뚝한 목소리로 나왔으며, 말하는 이는 그 말을 뱉으면서 침울하게 난롯불을 바라보았다. 사실 지주는 말을 하면서도 제가 한 말이 그토록 뜨거운 것이 반쯤 부끄러웠던 것이다."]], [["\"At any rate I will not think evil,\"", "“어쨌든 저는 악을 생각하지 않을게요.”"], ["Mrs. Dale answered, giving him her hand.", "데일 부인이 그에게 손을 내밀며 대답했다."], ["After that she left him, and returned home.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 그를 두고 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["It was too late for her to abandon her project of moving and remain at the Small House; but as she went across the garden she almost confessed to herself that she repented of what she was doing.", "이사 계획을 접고 작은 집에 그대로 머물기에는 너무 늦어 버렸다. 그러나 정원을 가로질러 오면서 그녀는 자기가 하고 있는 일을 뉘우치고 있노라고 스스로에게 거의 고백하다시피 했다."]], [["In these days of the cold early spring, the way from the lawn into the house, through the drawing-room window, was not as yet open, and it was necessary to go round by the kitchen-garden on to the road, and thence in by the front door; or else to pass through the back door, and into the house by the kitchen.", "쌀쌀한 이른 봄의 이 무렵에는 잔디밭에서 응접실 창을 지나 집 안으로 들어가는 길이 아직 열려 있지 않았다. 그래서 채마밭을 돌아 큰길로 나갔다가 거기서 앞문으로 들어오든지, 아니면 뒷문을 지나 부엌을 거쳐 집으로 들어가야 했다."], ["This latter mode of entrance Mrs.", "데일 부인은 이번에 뒤쪽 길을 택했다."], ["Dale now adopted; and as she made her way into the hall Lily came upon her, with very silent steps, out from the parlour, and arrested her progress.", "그리고 그녀가 현관으로 들어서는데 릴리가 거실에서 아주 소리 없는 걸음으로 나와 그녀를 붙들어 세웠다."], ["There was a smile upon Lily's face as she lifted up her finger as if in caution, and no one looking at her would have supposed that she was herself in trouble.", "릴리는 입가에 웃음을 띠고 조심하라는 듯 손가락을 세워 보였는데, 그 모습을 보고 그 애가 제 속에 근심을 안고 있다고 짐작할 사람은 아무도 없었을 것이다."], ["\"Mamma,\"", "“엄마,”"], ["she said, pointing to the drawing-room door, and speaking almost in a whisper, \"you must not go in there; come into the parlour.\"", "릴리가 응접실 문을 가리키며 거의 속삭이듯 말했다, “저기 들어가시면 안 돼요. 이쪽 거실로 오세요.”"]], [["\"Who's there?", "“누가 왔니?"], ["Where's Bell?\" and Mrs.", "벨은 어디 있고?”"], ["Dale went into the parlour as she was bidden.", "그러면서 데일 부인은 시키는 대로 거실로 들어갔다."], ["\"But who is there?\"", "“대체 누가 와 있느냐?”"], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되물었다."]], [["\"He's there!\"", "“그이가 와 있어요!”"]], [["\"Who is he?\"", "“그이라니 누구 말이냐?”"]], [["\"Oh, mamma, don't be a goose! Dr.", "“어머, 엄마, 바보처럼 굴지 마세요!"], ["Crofts is there, of course.", "당연히 크로프츠 선생님이시죠."], ["He's been nearly an hour.", "벌써 한 시간 가까이 계세요."], ["I wonder how he is managing, for there is nothing on earth to sit upon but the old lump of a carpet.", "저 방엔 앉을 것이라고는 둘둘 만 양탄자 뭉치밖에 없는데 어떻게 견디고 계신지 모르겠어요."], ["The room is strewed about with crockery, and Bell is such a figure!", "방바닥엔 그릇이 널브러져 있고, 언니 꼴은 또 말이 아니고요!"], ["She has got on your old checked apron, and when he came in she was rolling up the fire-irons in brown paper.", "엄마 낡은 체크무늬 앞치마를 두르고 있는데, 그이가 들어왔을 때 언니는 벽난로 연장들을 갈색 종이에 말아 싸고 있었다니까요."], ["I don't suppose she was ever in such a mess before.", "언니가 저런 꼴이었던 적은 여태 한 번도 없었을 거예요."], ["There's one thing certain,--he can't kiss her hand.\"", "한 가지만은 분명해요. 그이가 언니 손에 입을 맞출 수는 없다는 거요.”"]], [["\"It's you are the goose, Lily.\"", "“바보는 너다, 릴리.”"]], [["\"But he's in there certainly, unless he has gone out through the window, or up the chimney.\"", "“그래도 그이가 저 안에 있는 건 틀림없어요. 창으로 빠져나갔거나 굴뚝으로 올라가지 않았다면요.”"]], [["\"What made you leave them?\"", "“너는 어쩌자고 둘만 두고 나왔느냐?”"]], [["\"He met me here, in the passage, and spoke to me ever so seriously.", "“그이가 여기 복도에서 저를 만났는데, 어찌나 심각하게 말을 하던지요."], ["'Come in,' I said, 'and see Bell packing the pokers and tongs.'", "‘들어오세요, 언니가 부지깽이랑 부집게 싸는 것 좀 보시게요.’ 하고 제가 말했지요."], ["'I will go in,'", "그랬더니 ‘들어가겠습니다."], ["he said, 'but don't come with me.'", "하지만 같이 오지는 마세요.’ 하지 뭐예요."], ["He was ever so serious, and I'm sure he had been thinking of it all the way along.\"", "어찌나 심각하던지, 오는 길 내내 그 생각을 하고 왔던 게 틀림없어요.”"]], [["\"And why should he not be serious?\"", "“그이가 심각하지 않을 까닭이 무어냐?”"]], [["\"Oh, no, of course he ought to be serious; but are you not glad, mamma?", "“아니, 그럼요, 당연히 심각해야지요. 그런데 엄마는 안 기뻐요?"], ["I am so glad.", "저는 너무 기뻐요."], ["We shall live alone together, you and I; but she will be so close to us!", "이제 엄마랑 저랑 단둘이 살게 되겠지만, 언니가 우리 아주 가까이에 있게 되잖아요!"], ["My belief is that he'll stay there for ever unless somebody does something.", "제 생각엔 누가 무슨 수를 쓰지 않으면 그이는 저 안에 하염없이 앉아 있을 것 같아요."], ["I have been so tired of waiting and looking out for you.", "기다리면서 엄마 오시나 내다보느라 아주 지쳤다니까요."], ["Perhaps he's helping her to pack the things.", "어쩌면 그이가 언니를 도와 짐을 싸고 있을지도 몰라요."], ["Don't you think we might go in; or would it be ill-natured?\"", "우리 들어가 봐도 되지 않을까요? 아니면 그건 심술궂은 짓일까요?”"]], [["\"Lily, don't be in too great a hurry to say anything.", "“릴리야, 무슨 말이든 너무 서둘러 하지 마라."], ["You may be mistaken, you know; and there's many a slip between the cup and the lip.\"", "네가 잘못 짚었을 수도 있잖니. 그리고 잔에서 입술까지 가는 사이에도 흘리는 일이 많은 법이란다.”"]], [["\"Yes, mamma, there is,\"", "“그래요, 엄마, 그렇지요.”"], ["said Lily, putting her hand inside her mother's arm, \"that's true enough.\"", "릴리가 어머니의 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. “그건 참말이에요.”"]], [["\"Oh, my darling, forgive me,\"", "“아이고, 내 새끼, 용서해 다오.”"], ["said the mother, suddenly remembering that the use of the old proverb at the present moment had been almost cruel.", "어머니가 말했다. 그 옛 속담을 하필 이런 때에 쓴 것이 모질다시피 한 일이었음을 문득 떠올렸던 것이다."]], [["\"Do not mind it,\"", "“마음 쓰지 마세요.”"], ["said Lily, \"it does not hurt me, it does me good; that is to say, when there is nobody by except yourself.", "릴리가 말했다. “아프지 않아요, 오히려 도움이 되는걸요."], ["But, with God's help, there shall be no slip here, and she shall be happy.", "곁에 엄마 말고 아무도 없을 때는 말이에요. 하지만 하느님이 도우시면 여기서는 흘리는 일이 없을 거예요."], ["It is all the difference between one thing done in a hurry, and another done with much thinking.", "언니는 행복해질 거고요. 서둘러 해치운 일과 오래 생각하고 한 일은 그만큼 다른 법이잖아요."], ["But they'll remain there for ever if we don't go in.", "그건 그렇고 우리가 들어가지 않으면 저 둘은 하염없이 저러고 있을 거예요."], ["Come, mamma, you open the door.\"", "자, 엄마, 엄마가 문을 여세요.”"]], [["Then Mrs. Dale did open the door, giving some little premonitory notice with the handle, so that the couple inside might be warned of approaching footsteps.", "그러자 데일 부인은 문을 열었다."], ["Crofts had not escaped, either through the window or up the chimney, but was seated in the middle of the room on an empty box, just opposite to Bell, who was seated upon the lump of carpeting.", "다만 손잡이를 미리 조금 달그락거려 안에 있는 두 사람이 다가오는 발소리를 알아채도록 해 주었다. 크로프츠는 창으로도 굴뚝으로도 빠져나가지 않았고, 빈 상자 위에 앉아 방 한가운데 있었다."], ["Bell still wore the checked apron as described by her sister.", "둘둘 만 양탄자 뭉치 위에 앉은 벨과 마주 보고서였다. 벨은 아직도 동생이 말한 그 체크무늬 앞치마를 두르고 있었다."], ["What might have been the state of her hands I will not pretend to say; but I do not believe that her lover had found anything amiss with them.", "그 애 손이 어떤 꼴이었는지는 내가 함부로 말하지 않겠다. 그러나 그 애의 연인이 그 손에서 무슨 흠을 잡았으리라고는 나는 믿지 않는다."], ["\"How do you do, doctor?\"", "“안녕하세요, 선생님?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale, striving to use her accustomed voice, and to look as though there were nothing of special importance in his visit.", "데일 부인이 여느 때와 같은 목소리를 내려 애쓰면서, 그의 방문에 무슨 각별한 일이 있는 것은 아니라는 듯이 말했다."], ["\"I have just come down from the Great House.\"", "“저는 지금 막 큰 집에 다녀오는 길이랍니다.”"]], [["\"Mamma,\"", "“엄마,”"], ["said Bell, jumping up, \"you must not call him doctor any more.\"", "벨이 벌떡 일어서며 말했다, “이제 이이를 선생님이라고 부르시면 안 돼요.”"]], [["\"Must I not?", "“안 된다고?"], ["Has any one undoctored him?\"", "누가 이이한테서 선생을 떼어 가기라도 했다더냐?”"]], [["\"Oh, mamma, you understand,\"", "“아이참, 엄마, 아시면서.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I understand,\"", "“제가 알아요.”"], ["said Lily, going up to the doctor, and giving him her cheek to kiss, \"he is to be my brother, and I mean to claim him as such from this moment.", "릴리가 선생에게 다가가 입 맞추라고 뺨을 내밀며 말했다. “이분은 제 형부가 되실 분이고, 저는 이 순간부터 형부라고 우길 작정이에요."], ["I expect him to do everything for us, and not to call a moment of his time his own.\"", "저는 형부가 우리를 위해 무슨 일이든 해 주고, 한순간도 제 시간이라 우기지 않기를 바라요.”"]], [["\"Mrs. Dale,\"", "“데일 부인,”"], ["said the doctor, \"Bell has consented that it shall be so, if you will consent.\"", "선생이 말했다, “어머님께서 허락해 주신다면 그리하겠다고 벨이 승낙했습니다.”"]], [["\"There is but little doubt of that,\"", "“그거야 의심할 것이 거의 없지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"We shall not be rich--\"", "“저희가 부유하지는 못할 텐데요—”"], ["began the doctor.", "선생이 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"I hate to be rich,\"", "“저는 부유한 게 싫어요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"I hate even to talk about it.", "“그 이야기를 하는 것조차 싫어요."], ["I don't think it quite manly even to think about it; and I'm sure it isn't womanly.\"", "그런 걸 생각하는 것부터가 사내답지 못한 일이라고 생각하고요, 여자답지 못한 건 더 말할 것도 없고요.”"]], [["\"Bell was always a fanatic in praise of poverty,\"", "“벨은 늘 가난 예찬에 열을 올리는 광신자였답니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"No; I'm no fanatic.", "“아니에요, 저는 광신자가 아니에요."], ["I'm very fond of money earned.", "저는 벌어들인 돈이라면 아주 좋아해요."], ["I would like to earn some myself if I knew how.\"", "방법만 안다면 저도 얼마쯤 벌어 보고 싶은걸요.”"]], [["\"Let her go out and visit the lady patients,\"", "“언니더러 나가서 부인 환자들을 왕진하라고 하세요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"They do in America.\"", "“미국에서는 그렇게 한대요.”"]], [["Then they all went into the parlour and sat round the fire talking as though they were already one family.", "그러고 나서 그들은 모두 거실로 건너가 난롯가에 둘러앉아, 이미 한 식구라도 된 듯이 이야기를 나누었다."], ["The proceeding, considering the nature of it,--that a young lady, acknowledged to be of great beauty and known to be of good birth, had on the occasion been asked and given in marriage,--was carried on after a somewhat humdrum fashion, and in a manner that must be called commonplace.", "그 일의 됨됨이를 생각하면, 곧 빼어난 미인으로 인정받고 좋은 집안 태생으로 알려진 젊은 아가씨가 그 자리에서 청혼을 받고 혼인을 허락받은 것이니, 일은 어쩐지 밋밋하게, 평범하다고밖에 할 수 없는 모양새로 흘러갔다."], ["How different had it been when Crosbie had made his offer!", "크로즈비가 청혼했을 때는 얼마나 달랐던가!"], ["Lily for the time had been raised to a pinnacle,--a pinnacle which might be dangerous, but which was, at any rate, lofty.", "그때 릴리는 한동안 꼭대기로 들어 올려졌었다. 위태로울 수도 있었으나 어쨌든 아득히 높은 꼭대기였다."], ["With what a pretty speech had Crosbie been greeted!", "크로즈비는 얼마나 어여쁜 인사말로 맞아졌던가!"], ["How it had been felt by all concerned that the fortunes of the Small House were in the ascendant,--felt, indeed, with some trepidation, but still with much inward triumph.", "작은 집의 운수가 떠오르는 중이라고 관계된 이들이 모두 얼마나 실감했던가. 물론 얼마쯤 가슴 두근거리기는 했으나 그래도 속으로는 크게 우쭐하면서."], ["How great had been the occasion, forcing Lily almost to lose herself in wonderment at what had occurred!", "그 일이 얼마나 대단한 사건이었으면 릴리가 벌어진 일 앞에서 저를 잃다시피 하고 놀라워했겠는가!"], ["There was no great occasion now, and no wonderment.", "이번에는 대단한 사건도 없고 놀라움도 없었다."], ["No one, unless it was Crofts, felt very triumphant.", "크로프츠라면 몰라도 그 밖에는 아무도 그리 우쭐해하지 않았다."], ["But they were all very happy, and were sure that there was safety in their happiness.", "그러나 그들은 모두 무척 행복했고, 그 행복 속에 안전이 있음을 확신했다."], ["It was but the other day that one of them had been thrown rudely to the ground through the treachery of a lover, but yet none of them feared treachery from this lover.", "그들 가운데 하나가 연인의 배신으로 모질게 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐진 것이 바로 얼마 전 일이건만, 그럼에도 이 연인에게서 배신을 두려워하는 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["Bell was as sure of her lot in life as though she were already being taken home to her modest house in Guestwick.", "벨은 이미 게스트윅의 소박한 제집으로 데려가지기라도 한 듯이 제 앞날을 확신하고 있었다."], ["Mrs. Dale already looked upon the man as her son, and the party of four as they sat round the fire grouped themselves as though they already formed one family.", "데일 부인은 벌써 그 사람을 제 아들로 여겼고, 난롯가에 둘러앉은 네 사람은 이미 한 식구가 된 것처럼 무리를 이루었다."]], [["But Bell was not seated next to her lover.", "그러나 벨은 제 연인 곁에 앉지 않았다."], ["Lily, when she had once accepted Crosbie, seemed to think that she could never be too near to him.", "릴리는 크로즈비를 받아들이고 나서는 그에게 아무리 가까이 있어도 모자란다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["She had been in no wise ashamed of her love, and had shown it constantly by some little caressing motion of her hand, leaning on his arm, looking into his face, as though she were continually desirous of some palpable assurance of his presence.", "릴리는 제 사랑을 조금도 부끄러워하지 않았고, 손을 살며시 얹어 쓰다듬거나 그의 팔에 기대거나 그의 얼굴을 들여다보거나 하면서 늘 그것을 드러내 보였다. 마치 그가 곁에 있다는 것을 손에 잡히도록 끊임없이 확인받고 싶어 하는 사람 같았다."], ["It was not so at all with Bell.", "벨은 전혀 그렇지 않았다."], ["She was happy in loving and in being loved, but she required no overt testimonies of affection.", "그녀는 사랑하고 사랑받는 것으로 행복했으나, 애정을 겉으로 드러내는 증표 따위는 필요로 하지 않았다."], ["I do not think it would have made her unhappy if some sudden need had required that Crofts should go to India and back before they were married.", "혼인하기 전에 무슨 급한 일이 생겨 크로프츠가 인도까지 갔다 와야 한다 해도 그 때문에 그녀가 불행해지지는 않았으리라고 나는 생각한다."], ["The thing was settled, and that was enough for her.", "일은 정해졌고, 그녀에게는 그것으로 충분했다."], ["But, on the other hand, when he spoke of the expediency of an immediate marriage, she raised no difficulty.", "그러나 다른 한편으로 그가 하루빨리 혼인하는 편이 좋겠다고 말했을 때 그녀는 조금도 어려워하지 않았다."], ["As her mother was about to go into a new residence, it might be as well that that residence should be fitted to the wants of two persons instead of three.", "어머니가 새로 집을 얻어 들어가려는 참이니, 그 집이 세 사람이 아니라 두 사람의 살림에 맞게 꾸며지는 편이 나을 수도 있었다."], ["So they talked about chairs and tables, carpets and kitchens, in a most unromantic, homely, useful manner!", "그리하여 그들은 의자와 탁자, 양탄자와 부엌 이야기를 낭만이라고는 한 톨도 없이, 살림살이답고 실속 있는 투로 주고받았다!"], ["A considerable portion of the furniture in the house they were now about to leave belonged to the squire,--or to the house rather, as they were in the habit of saying.", "그들이 이제 떠나려는 집 안의 가구는 상당 부분이 지주의 것이었다. 아니, 그들이 늘 말하던 대로 하자면 그 집의 것이었다."], ["The older and more solid things,--articles of household stuff that stand the wear of half a century,--had been in the Small House when they came to it.", "더 오래되고 더 튼튼한 것들, 반세기의 손때를 견디는 세간붙이는 그들이 들어왔을 때 이미 작은 집에 있던 것들이었다."], ["There was, therefore, a question of buying new furniture for a house in Guestwick,--a question not devoid of importance to the possessor of so moderate an income as that owned by Mrs.", "그러므로 게스트윅의 집에 놓을 가구를 새로 사야 하는 문제가 있었다. 데일 부인처럼 얼마 안 되는 수입을 가진 사람에게는 결코 가볍지 않은 문제였다."], ["Dale. In the first month or two they were to live in lodgings, and their goods were to be stored in some friendly warehouse.", "처음 한두 달은 셋방에서 지내고 세간은 어느 인심 좋은 창고에 맡겨 둘 참이었다."], ["Under such circumstances would it not be well that Bell's marriage should be so arranged that the lodging question might not be in any degree complicated by her necessities?", "그러한 사정이라면 벨의 혼례를 잘 맞추어 셋방 문제가 그 애의 살림 때문에 조금도 복잡해지지 않게 하는 편이 낫지 않겠는가?"], ["This was the last suggestion made by Dr. Crofts, induced no doubt by the great encouragement he had received.", "이것이 크로프츠 선생이 내놓은 마지막 제안이었는데, 그가 받은 크나큰 격려가 그리 만든 것임에 틀림없었다."]], [["\"That would be hardly possible,\"", "“그건 도무지 될 수 없는 일이에요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"It only wants three weeks;--and with the house in such a condition!\"", "“이제 삼 주밖에 남지 않았는데요. 게다가 집이 이 꼴인데!”"]], [["\"James is joking,\"", "“제임스는 농담하는 거예요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I was not joking at all,\"", "“저는 조금도 농담이 아니었습니다.”"], ["said the doctor.", "선생이 말했다."]], [["\"Why not send for Mr. Boyce, and carry her off at once on a pillion behind you?\"", "“보이스 씨를 부르러 보내서, 언니를 당장 말 뒷안장에 태워 데려가시지 그래요?”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"It's just the sort of thing for primitive people to do, like you and Bell.", "“형부하고 언니처럼 예스러운 사람들이나 할 법한 일이잖아요."], ["All the same, Bell, I do wish you could have been married from this house.\"", "그건 그렇고 언니, 나는 언니가 이 집에서 시집갈 수 있었더라면 정말 좋았을 텐데.”"]], [["\"I don't think it will make much difference,\"", "“그게 그리 큰 차이가 있을 것 같지는 않은데.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Only if you would have waited till summer we would have had such a nice party on the lawn.", "“여름까지만 기다렸으면 잔디밭에서 근사한 잔치를 열었을 텐데."], ["It sounds so ugly, being married from lodgings; doesn't it, mamma?\"", "셋방에서 시집간다니 어쩐지 듣기 흉하잖아. 그렇죠, 엄마?”"]], [["\"It doesn't sound at all ugly to me,\"", "“나한테는 조금도 흉하게 들리지 않는걸.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"I shall always call you Dame Commonplace when you're married,\"", "“언니가 시집가면 나는 언니를 늘 평범 부인이라고 부를 테야.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["Then they had tea, and after tea Dr. Crofts got on his horse and rode back to Guestwick.", "그러고 나서 그들은 차를 마셨고, 차를 마신 뒤 크로프츠 선생은 말에 올라 게스트윅으로 돌아갔다."]], [["\"Now may I talk about him?\"", "“이제 그이 이야기를 해도 돼요?”"], ["said Lily, as soon as the door was closed behind his back.", "그의 등 뒤로 문이 닫히자마자 릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"No; you may not.\"", "“안 된다.”"]], [["\"As if I hadn't known it all along!", "“제가 진작부터 몰랐던 것처럼 말씀하시네요!"], ["And wasn't it hard to bear that you should have scolded me with such pertinacious austerity, and that I wasn't to say a word in answer!\"", "그리고 엄마가 그렇게 끈덕지게 엄한 얼굴로 저를 꾸짖으시는데 저는 한마디도 대꾸하면 안 됐으니, 그걸 참느라 얼마나 힘들었게요!”"]], [["\"I don't remember the austerity,\"", "“나는 엄한 얼굴을 한 기억이 없구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Nor yet Lily's silence,\"", "“릴리가 잠자코 있었던 기억도 없고요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"But it's all settled now,\"", "“어쨌든 이제 다 정해졌고,”"], ["said Lily, \"and I'm downright happy.", "릴리가 말했다, “저는 그저 행복해요."], ["I never felt more satisfaction,--never, Bell!\"", "이보다 더 흡족했던 적은 없어요. 정말이야, 언니!”"]], [["\"Nor did I,\"", "“나도 그렇단다.”"], ["said her mother; \"I may truly say that I thank God for this good thing.\"", "어머니가 말했다. “이 좋은 일을 두고 하느님께 감사드린다고 참말로 말할 수 있구나.”"]], [["# CHAPTER LI. JOHN EAMES DOES THINGS WHICH HE OUGHT NOT TO HAVE DONE.", "# 제51장. 존 임스가 하지 말았어야 할 일들을 하다"]], [["John Eames succeeded in making his bargain with Sir Raffle Buffle.", "존 임스는 래플 버플 경과의 흥정을 성사시켰다."], ["He accepted the private secretaryship on the plainly expressed condition that he was to have leave of absence for a fortnight towards the end of April. Having arranged this he took an affectionate leave of Mr.", "그는 사월 말께 보름 동안 휴가를 얻는다는 조건을 분명히 못 박고서 개인 비서 자리를 받아들였다."], ["Love, who was really much affected at parting with him, discussed valedictory pots of porter in the big room, over which many wishes were expressed that he might be enabled to compass the length and breadth of old Huffle's feet, uttered a last cutting joke at Mr.", "이 일을 매듭짓고 나서 그는 러브 씨에게 정 어린 작별을 고했는데, 러브 씨는 그와 헤어지는 것을 정말로 몹시 서운해했다. 그는 큰 방에서 작별의 포터 맥주를 앞에 놓고 이야기를 나누었고, 그 자리에서는 그가 늙은 허플의 발 길이와 너비를 두루 재어 낼 수 있게 되기를 바란다는 덕담이 여러 차례 나왔다."], ["Kissing as he met that gentleman hurrying through the passages with an enormous ledger in his hands, and then took his place in the comfortable arm-chair which FitzHoward had been forced to relinquish.", "그러고서 커다란 원장을 두 팔에 안고 복도를 서둘러 지나가는 키싱 씨와 마주치자 마지막으로 그 신사에게 톡 쏘는 농담을 한마디 던졌으며, 그런 다음 피츠하워드가 어쩔 수 없이 내놓아야 했던 그 편안한 팔걸이의자에 제 자리를 잡았다."]], [["\"Don't tell any of the fellows,\"", "“다른 친구들한테는 말하지 말게,”"], ["said Fitz, \"but I'm going to cut the concern altogether.", "피츠가 말했다. “하지만 나는 이 판을 통째로 걷어찰 참이야."], ["My governor wouldn't let me stop here in any other place than that of private secretary.\"", "우리 아버지는 개인 비서 자리가 아니면 내가 여기 붙어 있는 걸 놔두지 않으실 걸세.”"]], [["\"Ah, your governor is a swell,\"", "“아, 자네 아버님은 멋쟁이시지,”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"I don't know about that,\"", "“그건 잘 모르겠네,”"], ["said FitzHoward.", "피츠하워드가 말했다."], ["\"Of course he has a good deal of family interest.", "“물론 아버지께 집안 연줄이 제법 많기는 하지."], ["My cousin is to come in for St. Bungay at the next election, and then I can do better than remain here.\"", "내 사촌이 다음 선거에서 세인트 벙게이 지역구로 들어오게 되어 있는데, 그러면 나야 여기 남아 있는 것보다 나은 자리를 얻을 수 있으니까.”"]], [["\"That's a matter of course,\"", "“그야 당연한 일이지,”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."], ["\"If my cousin were Member for St. Bungay, I'd never stand anything east of Whitehall.\"", "“내 사촌이 세인트 벙게이의 의원이라면 나는 화이트홀 동쪽에 있는 것이라면 무엇도 참고 견디지 않을 걸세.”"]], [["\"And I don't mean,\"", "“그리고 나도 그럴 뜻은 없네,”"], ["said FitzHoward.", "피츠하워드가 말했다."], ["\"This room, you know, is all very nice; but it is a bore coming into the City every day.", "“이 방이야 다 좋지. 하지만 날마다 시티까지 들어와야 하는 건 성가신 일이야."], ["And then one doesn't like to be rung for like a servant.", "게다가 하인처럼 종소리에 불려 다니는 걸 좋아할 사람은 없지."], ["Not that I mean to put you out of conceit with it.\"", "그렇다고 자네더러 이 자리에 정을 붙이지 말라는 소리는 아닐세.”"]], [["\"It will do very well for me,\"", "“나한테야 아주 잘 맞을 걸세,”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."], ["\"I never was very particular.\"", "“나는 여태 그리 까다로운 사람이었던 적이 없으니까.” 그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다."], ["And so they parted, Eames assuming the beautiful arm-chair and the peril of being asked to carry Sir Raffle's shoes, while FitzHoward took the vacant desk in the big room till such time as some member of his family should come into Parliament for the borough of St. Bungay.", "임스는 그 아름다운 팔걸이의자와 더불어 래플 경의 신발을 가져오라는 말을 들을지도 모르는 위험까지 떠맡았고, 피츠하워드는 제 집안사람 가운데 누군가가 세인트 벙게이 자치구에서 의회로 들어갈 그때까지 큰 방의 빈 책상을 차지하게 되었다."]], [["But Eames, though he drank the porter, and quizzed FitzHoward, and gibed at Kissing, did not seat himself in his new arm-chair without some serious thoughts.", "그러나 임스는 포터를 마시고 피츠하워드를 놀려 먹고 키싱에게 빈정거리기는 했어도, 제 새 팔걸이의자에 앉으면서 자못 진지한 생각에 잠기지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He was aware that his career in London had not hitherto been one on which he could look back with self-respect.", "그는 런던에서 보낸 지금까지의 삶이 스스로 자긍심을 가지고 돌아볼 만한 것이 못 된다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 존경하지 않는 사람들과 어울려 살았다."], ["He had lived with friends whom he did not esteem; he had been idle, and sometimes worse than idle; and he had allowed himself to be hampered by the pretended love of a woman for whom he had never felt any true affection, and by whom he had been cozened out of various foolish promises which even yet were hanging over his head.", "그는 게을렀고, 때로는 게으른 것보다 더 나빴다. 그리고 참된 애정이라고는 조금도 느껴 본 적 없는 여자의 거짓 사랑에 발이 묶이도록 자신을 내버려 두었으며, 그 여자에게 속아 넘어가 온갖 어리석은 약속을 내주었고 그것이 아직도 그의 머리 위에 매달려 있었다."], ["As he sat with Sir Raffle's notes before him, he thought almost with horror of the men and women in Burton Crescent.", "래플 경에게 올릴 편지 뭉치를 앞에 놓고 앉아 있으려니, 그는 버턴 크레슨트의 남자들과 여자들을 거의 소름이 돋는 심정으로 떠올렸다."], ["It was now about three years since he had first known Cradell, and he shuddered as he remembered how very poor a creature was he whom he had chosen for his bosom friend.", "크래들을 처음 안 지 이제 세 해쯤 되었는데, 제가 가슴을 나눌 벗으로 고른 자가 얼마나 못난 위인이었는지를 떠올리자 몸이 다 떨렸다."], ["He could not make for himself those excuses which we can make for him.", "그는 우리가 그를 위해 만들어 줄 수 있는 그런 변명을 스스로에게는 해 줄 수 없었다."], ["He could not tell himself that he had been driven by circumstances to choose a friend, before he had learned to know what were the requisites for which he should look.", "벗에게서 무엇을 찾아야 하는지 미처 배우기도 전에 사정에 떠밀려 벗을 골라야 했노라고 스스로에게 말할 수가 없었다."], ["He had lived on terms of closest intimacy with this man for three years, and now his eyes were opening themselves to the nature of his friend's character.", "그는 세 해 동안 이 사내와 더없이 가까운 사이로 지내 왔고, 이제야 그 벗의 성품이 어떤 것인지에 눈이 뜨이고 있었다."], ["Cradell was in age three years his senior.", "크래들은 나이로 치면 그보다 세 살 위였다."], ["\"I won't drop him,\"", "“그를 내치지는 않겠다,”"], ["he said to himself; \"but he is a poor creature.\"", "그는 속으로 말했다. “하지만 못난 위인이야.”"], ["He thought, too, of the Lupexes, of Miss Spruce, and of Mrs. Roper, and tried to imagine what Lily Dale would do if she found herself among such people.", "그는 루펙스 부부와 스프루스 양과 로퍼 부인도 떠올렸고, 릴리 데일이 그런 사람들 틈에 놓인다면 어찌할지 그려 보려 했다."], ["It would be impossible that she should ever so find herself.", "그녀가 그런 자리에 놓이는 일은 도무지 있을 수 없었다."], ["He might as well ask her to drink at the bar of a gin-shop as to sit down in Mrs. Roper's drawing-room.", "그녀에게 로퍼 부인의 거실에 앉으라고 청하느니 차라리 진 술집 술청에서 한잔하라고 청하는 편이 나을 것이었다."], ["If destiny had in store for him such good fortune as that of calling Lily his own, it was necessary that he should altogether alter his mode of life.", "릴리를 제 사람이라 부르는 그런 복이 운명 속에 마련되어 있다면, 그는 제 살아가는 방식을 통째로 바꾸어야만 했다."]], [["In truth his hobbledehoyhood was dropping off from him, as its old skin drops from a snake.", "참으로 그의 풋내기 시절은 뱀에게서 낡은 허물이 벗겨져 나가듯 그에게서 벗겨져 나가고 있었다."], ["Much of the feeling and something of the knowledge of manhood was coming on him, and he was beginning to recognize to himself that the future manner of his life must be to him a matter of very serious concern.", "어른의 감정 가운데 많은 것과 어른의 지식 가운데 얼마쯤이 그에게 찾아들고 있었고, 그는 앞으로 살아갈 방식이 자기에게 여간 중대한 문제가 아니라는 것을 스스로 깨닫기 시작하고 있었다."], ["No such thought had come near him when he first established himself in London.", "런던에 처음 자리를 잡았을 때에는 그런 생각이 조금도 그에게 다가오지 않았다."], ["It seems to me that in this respect the fathers and mothers of the present generation understand but little of the inward nature of the young men for whom they are so anxious.", "내가 보기에 이 점에서 요즘 세대의 아버지 어머니들은 그토록 애를 태우는 그 젊은이들의 속내를 거의 알지 못한다."], ["They give them credit for so much that it is impossible they should have, and then deny them credit for so much that they possess!", "그들은 젊은이들이 도저히 갖추었을 리 없는 것을 갖추었으려니 하고 믿어 주면서, 정작 젊은이들이 지니고 있는 것은 조금도 믿어 주지 않는다!"], ["They expect from them when boys the discretion of men,--that discretion which comes from thinking; but will not give them credit for any of that power of thought which alone can ultimately produce good conduct.", "아직 소년인 그들에게 어른의 분별을—생각에서 우러나는 그 분별을—기대하면서도, 오직 그것만이 끝내 훌륭한 행실을 낳을 수 있는 그 생각의 힘은 도무지 믿어 주지 않는 것이다."], ["Young men are generally thoughtful,--more thoughtful than their seniors; but the fruit of their thought is not as yet there.", "젊은이들은 대체로 생각이 많다—제 윗사람들보다 더 많다. 다만 그 생각의 열매가 아직 거기 있지 않을 뿐이다."], ["And then so little is done for the amusement of lads who are turned loose into London at nineteen or twenty.", "게다가 열아홉이나 스무 살에 런던에 풀려난 젊은이들의 즐길 거리를 위해서는 해 주는 것이 너무나 적다."], ["Can it be that any mother really expects her son to sit alone evening after evening in a dingy room drinking bad tea, and reading good books?", "대체 어느 어머니가 제 아들이 저녁마다 우중충한 방에 홀로 앉아 맛없는 차를 마시며 좋은 책을 읽으리라고 정말로 기대할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["And yet it seems that mothers do so expect,--the very mothers who talk about the thoughtlessness of youth!", "그런데도 어머니들은 그러기를 기대하는 모양이다—젊음의 무분별을 입에 올리는 바로 그 어머니들이!"], ["O ye mothers who from year to year see your sons launched forth upon the perils of the world, and who are so careful with your good advice, with under flannel shirting, with books of devotion and tooth-powder, does it never occur to you that provision should be made for amusement, for dancing, for parties, for the excitement and comfort of women's society?", "오, 해마다 아들들이 세상의 온갖 위태로움 속으로 나아가는 것을 지켜보며 좋은 충고와 플란넬 속옷과 신앙 서적과 치약을 그토록 알뜰히 챙겨 주는 어머니들이여, 즐길 거리를 위해, 춤을 위해, 모임을 위해, 여자들과 어울리는 데서 오는 설렘과 위안을 위해 미리 마련해 주어야 한다는 생각은 한 번도 들지 않는가?"], ["That excitement your sons will have, and if it be not provided by you of one kind, will certainly be provided by themselves of another kind.", "그 설렘을 그대들의 아들은 반드시 가질 것이고, 그것을 그대들이 한 가지 종류로 마련해 주지 않는다면 그들은 틀림없이 저희끼리 다른 종류로 마련하고 말 것이다."], ["If I were a mother sending lads out into the world, the matter most in my mind would be this,--to what houses full of nicest girls could I get them admission, so that they might do their flirting in good company.", "내가 만일 아들들을 세상으로 내보내는 어머니라면, 내 마음에 가장 크게 걸리는 일은 이것이리라—더없이 참한 아가씨들이 가득한 어느 집에 아이들을 들여보내 좋은 자리에서 시시덕거리게 해 줄 수 있을까 하는 것 말이다."]], [["Poor John Eames had been so placed that he had been driven to do his flirting in very bad company, and he was now fully aware that it had been so.", "가엾은 존 임스는 참으로 고약한 사람들 틈에서 시시덕거릴 수밖에 없는 자리에 놓여 있었고, 이제는 그것이 그러했다는 것을 온전히 알고 있었다."], ["It wanted but two days to his departure for Guestwick Manor, and as he sat breathing a while after the manufacture of a large batch of Sir Raffle's notes, he made up his mind that he would give Mrs.", "게스트윅 저택으로 떠날 날이 이틀밖에 남지 않았는데, 래플 경의 편지를 한 무더기 지어낸 뒤 잠시 숨을 돌리며 앉아 있으려니, 떠나기 전에 로퍼 부인에게 통고를 해 두어야겠다고 마음을 굳혔다. 런던으로 돌아오거든 다시는 버턴 크레슨트에 얼굴을 비치지 않겠다고 말이다."], ["Roper notice before he started, that on his return to London he would be seen no more in Burton Crescent.", "그는 제 결박을 통째로 끊어 버릴 작정이었고, 그렇게 끊는 데에 무슨 대가가 따른다면 제가 할 수 있는 가장 나은 방식으로 그것을 치를 참이었다."], ["He would break his bonds altogether asunder, and if there should be any penalty for such breaking he would pay it in what best manner he might be able.", "그는 어밀리아에게 못되게 굴어 왔다고 스스로 인정했다—사실 그 점에서는 실제로 저지른 것보다 더 많은 죄를 고백한 셈이었다."], ["He acknowledged to himself that he had been behaving badly to Amelia, confessing, indeed, more sin in that respect than he had in truth committed; but this, at any rate, was clear to him, that he must put himself on a proper footing in that quarter before he could venture to speak to Lily Dale.", "그러나 어쨌든 이것만은 그에게 분명했다. 릴리 데일에게 감히 말을 꺼내 보기 전에 그쪽 일부터 제대로 매듭지어 놓아야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["As he came to a definite conclusion on this subject the little handbell which always stood on Sir Raffle's table was sounded, and Eames was called into the presence of the great man. \"Ah,\"", "이 문제에 대해 확실한 결론에 이르렀을 때, 래플 경의 탁자 위에 늘 놓여 있는 그 작은 손종이 울렸고, 임스는 그 높으신 어른 앞으로 불려 갔다."], ["said Sir Raffle, leaning back in his arm-chair, and stretching himself after the great exertions which he had been making--\"Ah, let me see!", "“아,” 팔걸이의자에 등을 기대고 그동안 쏟아 온 대단한 노고 끝에 기지개를 켜며 래플 경이 말했다."], ["You are going out of town the day after to-morrow.\"", "“아, 어디 보자! 자네 모레 런던을 떠난다지.”"]], [["\"Yes, Sir Raffle, the day after to-morrow.\"", "“예, 래플 경, 모레입니다.”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["it's a great annoyance,--a very great annoyance.", "여간 성가신 일이 아니야—참으로 여간 성가신 일이 아니지."], ["But on such occasions I never think of myself.", "하지만 나는 그런 경우에 결코 내 생각부터 하는 법이 없네."], ["I never have done so, and don't suppose I ever shall.", "여태 그래 본 적이 없고, 앞으로도 그럴 성싶지 않아."], ["So you're going down to my old friend De Guest?\"", "그래, 내 오랜 벗 드 게스트한테 내려간다는 게지?”"]], [["Eames was always angered when his new patron Sir Raffle talked of his old friendship with the earl, and never gave the Commissioner any encouragement.", "새 후원자인 래플 경이 백작과의 오랜 벗 사이를 입에 올릴 때마다 임스는 늘 부아가 났고, 그 위원에게 맞장구라고는 조금도 쳐 주지 않았다."], ["\"I am going down to Guestwick,\"", "“게스트윅으로 내려갑니다,”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["yes; to Guestwick Manor?", "그래, 게스트윅 저택으로 말인가?"], ["I don't remember that I was ever there.", "내가 거기 가 본 적이 있는지는 기억나지 않는군."], ["I daresay I may have been, but one forgets those things.\"", "아마 가 보았을 테지만, 그런 것들이야 잊어버리게 마련이지.”"]], [["\"I never heard Lord De Guest speak of it.\"", "“드 게스트 백작께서 그런 말씀을 하시는 건 들어 본 적이 없습니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, dear, no.", "“아이고, 그야 그럴 테지."], ["Why should his memory be better than mine?", "그분 기억이 내 기억보다 나을 까닭이 무엇이겠나?"], ["Tell him, will you, how very glad I shall be to renew our old intimacy.", "우리 옛 정을 다시 잇게 되면 내가 얼마나 기쁘겠는지 그분께 전해 주게, 응?"], ["I should think nothing of running down to him for a day or two in the dull time of the year,--say in September or October.", "한 해 중 한가한 철에—이를테면 구월이나 시월에—하루 이틀 그분한테 내려가는 것쯤이야 나로서는 아무 일도 아니지."], ["It's rather a coincidence our both being interested about you,--isn't it?\"", "우리 둘이 다 자네 일에 마음을 쓰고 있다니 제법 공교로운 일 아닌가?”"]], [["\"I'll be sure to tell him.\"", "“꼭 전해 드리겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Mind you do.", "“꼭 그리하게."], ["He's one of our most thoroughly independent noblemen, and I respect him very highly.", "그분은 우리나라에서 가장 철저하게 독립적인 귀족 가운데 한 분이고, 나는 그분을 아주 높이 우러러본다네."], ["Let me see; didn't I ring my bell?", "어디 보자. 내가 종을 울리지 않았던가?"], ["What was it I wanted?", "무엇이 필요했더라?"], ["I think I rang my bell.\"", "아무래도 종을 울린 것 같은데.”"]], [["\"You did ring your bell.\"", "“종을 울리셨습니다.”"]], [["\"Ah, yes; I know.", "“아, 그래, 알겠네."], ["I am going away, and I wanted my--would you tell Rafferty to bring me--my boots?\"", "내가 나가는 참이라 내—래퍼티한테 일러서 내—장화를 가져오라고 해 주겠나?”"], ["Whereupon Johnny rang the bell--not the little handbell, but the other bell.", "그러자 조니는 종을 울렸다—작은 손종이 아니라 다른 종을 말이다."], ["\"And I shan't be here to-morrow,\"", "“그리고 내일은 내가 여기 없을 걸세,”"], ["continued Sir Raffle.", "래플 경이 말을 이었다."], ["\"I'll thank you to send my letters up to the square; and if they should send down from the Treasury;--but the Chancellor would write, and in that case you'll send up his letter at once by a special messenger, of course.\"", "“내 편지는 스퀘어로 올려 보내 주면 고맙겠네. 그리고 혹시 재무부에서 무엇을 내려보낸다면—아니, 재무장관이라면 직접 쓰실 테고, 그런 경우라면 물론 자네가 그 편지를 곧바로 특별 사환 편에 올려 보내 주게.”"]], [["\"Here's Rafferty,\"", "“래퍼티가 왔습니다,”"], ["said Eames, determined that he would not even sully his lips with speaking of Sir Raffle's boots.", "임스가 말했다. 래플 경의 장화 이야기로는 제 입술을 더럽히지도 않겠다고 작정한 참이었다."]], [["\"Oh, ah, yes; Rafferty, bring me my boots.\"", "“오, 아, 그래. 래퍼티, 내 장화를 가져오게.”"]], [["\"Anything else to say?\"", "“더 이르실 말씀 있습니까?”"], ["asked Eames.", "임스가 물었다."]], [["\"No, nothing else.", "“아니, 더는 없네."], ["Of course you'll be careful to leave everything straight behind you.\"", "물론 자네가 떠난 뒤에 모든 것이 반듯하게 남아 있도록 잘 챙겨 두겠지.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes; I'll leave it all straight.\"", "“예, 그럼요. 다 반듯하게 해 놓겠습니다.”"], ["Then Eames withdrew, so that he might not be present at the interview between Sir Raffle and his boots.", "그러고서 임스는 래플 경과 그 장화 사이의 면담 자리에 함께 있지 않으려고 물러났다."], ["\"He'll not do,\"", "“저 친구는 안 되겠어,”"], ["said Sir Raffle to himself.", "래플 경이 속으로 말했다."], ["\"He'll never do.", "“도무지 안 되겠어."], ["He's not quick enough,--has no go in him.", "재바르지가 못하고—추진력이 없어."], ["He's not man enough for the place.", "이 자리에 앉을 만한 위인이 못 돼."], ["I wonder why the earl has taken him by the hand in that way.\"", "백작이 어쩌자고 저 친구를 그런 식으로 거두어 주었는지 모를 일이야.”"]], [["Soon after the little episode of the boots Eames left his office, and walked home alone to Burton Crescent.", "장화를 둘러싼 그 자그마한 일이 있고 얼마 지나지 않아 임스는 사무국을 나서서 혼자 걸어 버턴 크레슨트의 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["He felt that he had gained a victory in Sir Raffle's room, but the victory there had been easy.", "그는 래플 경의 방에서 한 판을 이겼다고 느꼈으나, 그쪽의 승리는 쉬운 것이었다."], ["Now he had another battle on his hands, in which, as he believed, the achievement of victory would be much more difficult.", "이제 그의 손에는 또 다른 싸움이 놓여 있었고, 그가 믿기로 그 싸움에서 승리를 거두기란 훨씬 더 어려울 터였다."], ["Amelia Roper was a person much more to be feared than the Chief Commissioner.", "어밀리아 로퍼는 수석 위원보다 훨씬 더 두려워할 만한 사람이었다."], ["He had one strong arrow in his quiver on which he would depend, if there should come to him the necessity of giving his enemy a death-wound.", "그에게는 적에게 치명상을 입혀야 할 일이 닥칠 경우 기대어 볼 만한 튼튼한 화살이 화살통에 하나 있었다."], ["During the last week she had been making powerful love to Cradell, so as to justify the punishment of desertion from a former lover.", "지난 한 주 동안 그녀는 크래들에게 힘껏 사랑을 쏟고 있었으니, 옛 연인에게 버림받는 벌쯤은 마땅하다 할 만했다."], ["He would not throw Cradell in her teeth if he could help it; but it was incumbent on him to gain a victory, and if the worst should come to the worst, he must use such weapons as destiny and the chance of war had given him.", "그는 될 수 있으면 크래들을 들먹여 그녀의 낯에 내던지지는 않을 작정이었다. 그러나 그로서는 반드시 승리를 거두어야 했고, 최악의 경우가 닥친다면 운명과 전쟁의 우연이 그에게 쥐여 준 무기를 쓸 수밖에 없었다."]], [["He found Mrs. Roper in the dining-room as he entered, and immediately began his work.", "그가 들어서니 로퍼 부인이 식당에 있었고, 그는 곧바로 제 일에 착수했다."], ["\"Mrs. Roper,\"", "“로퍼 부인,”"], ["he said, \"I'm going out of town the day after to-morrow.\"", "그가 말했다. “저는 모레 런던을 떠납니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, Mr. Eames, we know that.", "“아이참, 그럼요, 임스 씨."], ["You're going as a visitor to the noble mansion of the Earl De Guest.\"", "우리도 다 알고 있어요. 드 게스트 백작님의 그 고귀한 저택으로 손님이 되어 가시는 거지요.”"]], [["\"I don't know about the mansion being very noble, but I'm going down into the country for a fortnight.", "“그 저택이 그리 고귀한지는 잘 모르겠습니다만, 보름 동안 시골로 내려가는 건 맞습니다."], ["When I come back--\"", "그런데 돌아오면—”"]], [["\"When you come back, Mr. Eames, I hope you'll find your room a deal more comfortable.", "“돌아오시면요, 임스 씨, 그때는 방이 훨씬 더 아늑해져 있는 걸 보시게 되기를 바라요."], ["I know it isn't quite what it should be for a gentleman like you, and I've been thinking for some time past--\"", "그 방이 임스 씨 같은 신사분께 마땅한 방이 못 된다는 건 나도 알고 있고, 얼마 전부터 이런저런 생각을—”"]], [["\"But, Mrs. Roper, I don't mean to come back here any more.", "“그런데 로퍼 부인, 저는 여기로 다시 돌아올 뜻이 없습니다."], ["It's just that that I want to say to you.\"", "바로 그 말씀을 드리려던 참이었어요.”"]], [["\"Not come back to the crescent!\"", "“크레슨트로 아주 안 돌아오신다고요!”"]], [["\"No, Mrs. Roper.", "“예, 로퍼 부인."], ["A fellow must move sometimes, you know; and I'm sure I've been very constant to you for a long time.\"", "사람이란 이따금 옮겨 다녀야 하는 법이잖습니까. 그리고 저야말로 오랫동안 참 한결같이 이 집에 붙어 있었지요.”"]], [["\"But where are you going, Mr. Eames?\"", "“그런데 어디로 가시게요, 임스 씨?”"]], [["\"Well; I haven't just made up my mind as yet.", "“글쎄요, 아직 딱히 마음을 정하지는 못했습니다."], ["That is, it will depend on what I may do,--on what friends of mine may say down in the country.", "그러니까 그건 제가 무엇을 하게 되느냐에—시골에 있는 제 벗들이 무어라 하느냐에 달려 있어요."], ["You'll not think I'm quarrelling with you, Mrs. Roper.\"", "제가 부인과 다투고 나가는 거라고는 생각지 말아 주십시오, 로퍼 부인.”"]], [["\"It's them Lupexes as have done it,\"", "“저 루펙스네 사람들이 그렇게 만든 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Roper, in her deep distress.", "로퍼 부인이 깊은 시름에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["\"No, indeed, Mrs. Roper, nobody has done it.\"", "“아닙니다, 정말이지 로퍼 부인, 아무도 그렇게 만들지 않았습니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, it is; and I'm not going to blame you, Mr.", "“아니에요, 그런 거예요."], ["Eames.", "그리고 임스 씨를 나무랄 생각은 없어요."], ["They've made the house unfit for any decent young gentleman like you.", "그 사람들이 이 집을 임스 씨 같은 참한 젊은 신사분한테 어울리지 않는 집으로 만들어 놓았지요. 나도 진작부터 그걸 느끼고 있었어요."], ["I've been feeling that all along; but it's hard upon a lone woman like me, isn't it, Mr. Eames?\"", "하지만 나같이 혼자 사는 여자한테는 모진 노릇이잖아요, 안 그래요, 임스 씨?”"]], [["\"But, Mrs. Roper, the Lupexes have had nothing to do with my going.\"", "“하지만 로퍼 부인, 루펙스네 사람들은 제가 나가는 것과 아무 상관이 없습니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, they have; I understand it all.", "“아니에요, 상관이 있고말고요. 나는 다 알아요."], ["But what could I do, Mr. Eames?", "하지만 내가 무얼 어쩌겠어요, 임스 씨?"], ["I've been giving them warning every week for the last six months; but the more I give them warning, the more they won't go.", "지난 여섯 달 동안 주마다 그 사람들한테 나가 달라고 통고를 해 왔어요. 그런데 통고를 하면 할수록 더 안 나가려고 들지요."], ["Unless I were to send for a policeman, and have a row in the house--\"", "순경을 불러다가 집안에 한바탕 소동을 벌이지 않고서야—”"]], [["\"But I haven't complained of the Lupexes, Mrs. Roper.\"", "“하지만 저는 루펙스네 사람들을 두고 불평한 적이 없습니다, 로퍼 부인.”"]], [["\"You wouldn't be quitting without any reason, Mr. Eames.", "“임스 씨가 아무 까닭도 없이 나가실 리는 없지요."], ["You are not going to be married in earnest, are you, Mr. Eames?\"", "정말로 혼인을 하시게 된 건 아니지요, 임스 씨?”"]], [["\"Not that I know of.\"", "“제가 아는 한은 아닙니다.”"]], [["\"You may tell me; you may, indeed.", "“나한테는 말씀하셔도 돼요. 정말이에요."], ["I won't say a word,--not to anybody.", "나는 한마디도 하지 않을 테니까요—누구한테도요."], ["It hasn't been my fault about Amelia.", "어밀리아 일은 내 잘못이 아니었어요."], ["It hasn't really.\"", "정말로 아니었어요.”"]], [["\"Who says there's been any fault?\"", "“누가 무슨 잘못이 있었다고 합니까?”"]], [["\"I can see, Mr. Eames.", "“나도 볼 줄은 알아요, 임스 씨."], ["Of course it didn't do for me to interfere.", "물론 내가 끼어들 일은 아니었지요. 그리고 임스 씨가 그 애를 좋아하셨더라면, 그 애는 어느 젊은이가 맞아들인 아내 못지않게 좋은 아내가 되었으리라고 나는 말하겠어요."], ["And if you had liked her, I will say I believe she'd have made as good a wife as any young man ever took; and she can make a few pounds go farther than most girls.", "그리고 그 애는 몇 푼 안 되는 돈으로도 웬만한 처녀들보다 살림을 훨씬 넉넉하게 꾸릴 줄 알고요. 어미 마음이야 임스 씨도 아실 테지요."], ["You can understand a mother's feelings; and if there was to be anything, I couldn't spoil it; could I, now?\"", "그리고 무슨 일이 되어 갈 참이었다면 내가 그걸 망칠 수야 없잖아요. 안 그래요?”"]], [["\"But there isn't to be anything.\"", "“하지만 아무 일도 되어 갈 게 없습니다.”"]], [["\"So I've told her for months past.", "“나도 몇 달째 그 애한테 그렇게 말해 왔어요."], ["I'm not going to say anything to blame you; but young men ought to be very particular; indeed they ought.\"", "임스 씨를 나무랄 말은 하지 않겠어요. 하지만 젊은 사내들은 아주 신중해야 하는 법이에요."], ["Johnny did not choose to hint to the disconsolate mother that it also behoved young women to be very particular, but he thought it.", "정말 그래야 하고말고요.” 조니는 이 서글픈 어머니에게 젊은 처녀들 또한 아주 신중해야 마땅하다는 말을 굳이 내비치지 않았으나, 속으로는 그렇게 생각했다."], ["\"I've wished many a time, Mr.", "“나는 여러 번 바랐어요, 임스 씨."], ["Eames, that she had never come here; indeed I have.", "그 애가 아예 여기 오지 않았더라면 하고요. 정말이지 그랬어요."], ["But what's a mother to do?", "하지만 어미가 무얼 어쩌겠어요?"], ["I couldn't put her outside the door.\"", "그 애를 문밖으로 내쫓을 수는 없잖아요.”"], ["Then Mrs. Roper raised her apron up to her eyes, and began to sob.", "그러고서 로퍼 부인은 앞치마를 들어 올려 눈에 갖다 대고 흐느끼기 시작했다."]], [["\"I'm very sorry if I've made any mischief,\"", "“제가 무슨 말썽이라도 일으켰다면 정말 죄송합니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"It hasn't been your fault,\"", "“임스 씨 잘못이 아니었어요.”"], ["continued the poor woman, from whom, as her tears became uncontrollable, her true feelings forced themselves and the real outpouring of her feminine nature.", "가엾은 여인이 말을 이었다. 눈물을 걷잡을 수 없게 되면서 그 속에서 참된 심정이 저절로 밀려 나오고 여자다운 마음이 그대로 쏟아져 나오고 있었다."], ["\"Nor it hasn't been my fault.", "“내 잘못도 아니었고요."], ["But I knew what it would come to when I saw how she was going on; and I told her so.", "하지만 그 애가 어떻게 굴고 있는지 보았을 때 나는 이 일이 어찌 될지 알았어요. 그리고 그 애한테 그렇게 말했지요."], ["I knew you wouldn't put up with the likes of her.\"", "임스 씨가 그 애 같은 사람을 받아들이지 않으리라는 걸 나는 알았어요.”"]], [["\"Indeed, Mrs. Roper, I've always had a great regard for her, and for you too.\"", "“정말이지 로퍼 부인, 저는 늘 그분을 크게 아꼈고, 부인도 그랬습니다.”"]], [["\"But you weren't going to marry her.", "“하지만 그 애와 혼인하실 마음은 없으셨잖아요."], ["I've told her so all along, and I've begged her not to do it,--almost on my knees I have; but she wouldn't be said by me.", "나는 그 애한테 내내 그렇게 말해 왔고, 그러지 말라고 빌다시피 했어요—거의 무릎을 꿇다시피 했지요. 그런데도 그 애는 내 말을 들으려 하지 않았어요."], ["She never would.", "도무지 들으려 하지 않았지요."], ["She's always been that wilful that I'd sooner have her away from me than with me.", "어찌나 고집이 센지, 곁에 두느니 차라리 멀리 보내 버리는 편이 낫겠다 싶을 지경이에요."], ["Though she's a good young woman in the house,--she is, indeed, Mr.", "집안일에서야 착실한 처녀지요—정말이에요, 임스 씨."], ["Eames;--and there isn't a pair of hands in it that works so hard; but it was no use my talking.\"", "이 집에 그 애만큼 억척스레 일하는 손도 없고요. 그렇지만 내 말이 무슨 소용이 있어야지요.”"]], [["\"I don't think any harm has been done.\"", "“나쁜 일이 벌어졌다고는 생각하지 않습니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, there has; great harm.", "“아니에요, 벌어졌어요. 크게 벌어졌지요."], ["It has made the place not respectable.", "이 집이 점잖지 못한 집이 되어 버렸으니까요."], ["It's the Lupexes is the worst.", "제일 나쁜 건 루펙스네 사람들이고요."], ["There's Miss Spruce, who has been with me for nine years,--ever since I've had the house,--she's been telling me this morning that she means to go into the country.", "스프루스 양만 해도 그래요. 내가 이 집을 맡은 뒤로 아홉 해나 나와 함께 지낸 분인데, 오늘 아침에 나한테 시골로 내려갈 작정이라고 말하더군요."], ["It's all the same thing.", "다 같은 이야기예요."], ["I understand it.", "나는 알아요."], ["I can see it.", "나는 볼 줄 알아요."], ["The house isn't respectable, as it should be; and your mamma, if she were to know all, would have a right to be angry with me.", "이 집은 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 점잖지가 못해요. 그리고 댁의 어머님께서 다 아시게 된다면 나한테 화를 내실 만도 하고요."], ["I did mean to be respectable, Mr.", "나는 점잖게 지내려 했어요, 임스 씨."], ["Eames; I did indeed.\"", "정말로 그랬어요.”"]], [["\"Miss Spruce will think better of it.\"", "“스프루스 양도 다시 생각하실 겁니다.”"]], [["\"You don't know what I've had to go through.", "“내가 무엇을 겪어 왔는지 임스 씨는 모르세요."], ["There's none of them pays, not regular,--only she and you.", "저 사람들 중에 제때 셈을 치르는 이가 하나도 없어요—그분과 임스 씨뿐이지요."], ["She's been like the Bank of England, has Miss Spruce.\"", "스프루스 양은 잉글랜드 은행이나 다름없었답니다.”"]], [["\"I'm afraid I've not been very regular, Mrs. Roper.\"", "“저야말로 그리 제때 치르지 못한 것 같습니다만, 로퍼 부인.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, you have.", "“아니에요, 잘 치르셨어요."], ["I don't think of a pound or two more or less at the end of a quarter, if I'm sure to have it some day.", "언젠가 받을 게 틀림없다면 분기 끝에 한두 파운드쯤 더 오고 덜 오는 거야 나는 마음에 두지 않아요."], ["The butcher,--he understands one's lodgers just as well as I do,--if the money's really coming, he'll wait; but he won't wait for such as them Lupexes, whose money's nowhere.", "푸줏간 사람도—그이도 하숙인이 어떤 사람인지 나만큼이나 잘 안답니다—돈이 정말로 들어올 것 같으면 기다려 주지요. 하지만 돈이 나올 데라고는 어디에도 없는 저 루펙스네 사람들 같은 축은 기다려 주지 않아요."], ["And there's Cradell; would you believe it, that fellow owes me eight and twenty pounds!\"", "그리고 크래들 씨도 있고요. 글쎄, 그이가 나한테 스물여덟 파운드를 빚졌다면 믿으시겠어요!”"]], [["\"Eight and twenty pounds!\"", "“스물여덟 파운드라고요!”"]], [["\"Yes, Mr. Eames, eight and twenty pounds!", "“그래요, 임스 씨, 스물여덟 파운드예요!"], ["He's a fool.", "그이는 바보예요."], ["It's them Lupexes as have had his money.", "그이 돈을 가져간 건 저 루펙스네 사람들이지요."], ["I know it.", "나는 알아요."], ["He don't talk of paying, and going away.", "그이는 갚겠다는 말도, 나가겠다는 말도 하지 않고요."], ["I shall be just left with him and the Lupexes on my hands; and then the bailiffs may come and sell every stick about the place.", "나한테는 결국 그이와 루펙스네 사람들만 남게 되겠지요. 그러고 나면 집달리들이 들이닥쳐 이 집 안의 나무토막 하나까지 죄다 팔아 치울 테고요."], ["I won't say nay to them.\"", "나는 그들한테 안 된다고 하지 않을 거예요.”"], ["Then she threw herself into the old horsehair arm-chair, and gave way to her womanly sorrow.", "그러고서 그녀는 낡은 말총 팔걸이의자에 몸을 던지고 여자다운 설움에 자신을 내맡겼다."]], [["\"I think I'll go upstairs, and get ready for dinner,\"", "“저는 위층으로 올라가서 저녁 들 채비를 해야겠습니다.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"And you must go away when you come back?\"", "“그럼 돌아오시면 정말 나가셔야 하는 건가요?”"], ["said Mrs. Roper.", "로퍼 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Well, yes, I'm afraid I must.", "“글쎄요, 그렇습니다. 아무래도 그래야겠어요."], ["I meant you to have a month's warning from to-day.", "오늘부터 한 달 전에 미리 통고를 드리는 셈으로 알아 주십사 했던 겁니다."], ["Of course I shall pay for the month.\"", "물론 그 한 달치는 치르고요.”"]], [["\"I don't want to take any advantage; indeed, I don't.", "“나는 덕을 보자는 게 아니에요. 정말 아니고요."], ["But I do hope you'll leave your things.", "하지만 짐은 두고 가셨으면 좋겠어요."], ["You can have them whenever you like.", "언제든 원하실 때 가져가시면 되니까요."], ["If Chumpend knows that you and Miss Spruce are both going, of course he'll be down upon me for his money.\"", "임스 씨와 스프루스 양이 둘 다 나간다는 걸 첨펜드가 알게 되면 그이는 당장 돈 내놓으라고 나를 들볶을 테니까요.”"], ["Chumpend was the butcher.", "첨펜드는 푸줏간 사람이었다."], ["But Eames made no answer to this piteous plea.", "그러나 임스는 이 딱한 하소연에 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["Whether or no he could allow his old boots to remain in Burton Crescent for the next week or two, must depend on the manner in which he might be received by Amelia Roper this evening.", "앞으로 한두 주 동안 제 헌 장화를 버턴 크레슨트에 그대로 두게 할 수 있을지 없을지는, 오늘 저녁 어밀리아 로퍼가 그를 어떻게 맞이하느냐에 달려 있을 터였다."]], [["When he came down to the drawing-room, there was no one there but Miss Spruce.", "그가 거실로 내려가 보니 거기에는 스프루스 양 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["\"A fine day, Miss Spruce,\"", "“날이 좋군요, 스프루스 양.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, Mr.", "“그렇습니다, 임스 씨."], ["Eames, it is a fine day for London; but don't you think the country air is very nice?\"", "런던치고는 좋은 날이지요. 그런데 시골 공기가 참 좋다고 생각하지 않으세요?”"]], [["\"Give me the town,\"", "“저야 도시가 좋습니다.” 조니가 말했다."], ["said Johnny, wishing to say a good word for poor Mrs. Roper, if it were possible.", "될 수만 있다면 가엾은 로퍼 부인을 위해 좋은 말을 한마디 해 주고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["\"You're a young man, Mr. Eames; but I'm only an old woman.", "“임스 씨는 젊은 분이시고, 나는 그저 늙은 여자일 뿐이지요."], ["That makes a difference,\"", "그래서 다른 겁니다.”"], ["said Miss Spruce.", "스프루스 양이 말했다."]], [["\"Not much,\"", "“그리 다르지 않습니다.”"], ["said Johnny, meaning to be civil.", "예의를 차릴 셈으로 조니가 말했다."], ["\"You don't like to be dull any more than I do.\"", "“스프루스 양도 저 못지않게 따분한 건 싫으실 텐데요.”"]], [["\"I like to be respectable, Mr. Eames.", "“나는 점잖게 지내는 것이 좋습니다, 임스 씨."], ["I always have been respectable, Mr. Eames.\"", "나는 늘 점잖게 지내 왔습니다, 임스 씨.”"], ["This the old woman said almost in a whisper, looking anxiously to see that the door had not been opened to other listening ears.", "노부인은 이 말을 거의 속삭이듯 했고, 문이 열려 다른 귀들이 엿듣고 있지는 않은지 불안한 눈길로 살폈다."]], [["\"I'm sure Mrs. Roper is very respectable.\"", "“로퍼 부인도 아주 점잖은 분인걸요.”"]], [["\"Yes; Mrs. Roper is respectable, Mr.", "“그럼요, 로퍼 부인은 점잖습니다, 임스 씨."], ["Eames; but there are some here that-- Hush-sh-sh!\"", "하지만 이 집에는 어떤 사람들이—쉬—쉬—쉿!”"], ["And the old lady put her finger up to her lips.", "그러고서 노부인은 손가락을 입술에 갖다 댔다."], ["The door opened and Mrs. Lupex swam into the room.", "문이 열리더니 루펙스 부인이 미끄러지듯 방 안으로 들어왔다."]], [["\"How d'ye do, Miss Spruce?", "“안녕하세요, 스프루스 양?"], ["I declare you're always first.", "참말이지 언제나 제일 먼저 와 계시네요."], ["It's to get a chance of having one of the young gentlemen to yourself, I believe.", "젊은 신사분 하나를 독차지할 기회를 잡으시려는 거겠지요."], ["What's the news in the city to-day, Mr. Eames?", "오늘 시티에는 무슨 소식이 있던가요, 임스 씨?"], ["In your position now of course you hear all the news.\"", "이제 그런 자리에 계시니 소식이란 소식은 죄다 들으실 테지요.”"]], [["\"Sir Raffle Buffle has got a new pair of shoes.", "“래플 버플 경께서 새 신발을 한 켤레 장만하셨습니다."], ["I don't know that for certain, but I guess it from the time it took him to put them on.\"", "확실히 아는 건 아니고, 그분이 그걸 신는 데 걸린 시간을 보고 그렇게 짐작한 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“어머!"], ["now you're quizzing.", "이제 저를 놀리시는군요."], ["That's always the way with you gentlemen when you get a little up in the world.", "남자분들이란 세상에서 조금 올라서기만 하면 늘 그렇답니다."], ["You don't think women are worth talking to then, unless just for a joke or so.\"", "그때부터는 여자란 농담 한마디 건네는 것 말고는 말을 섞을 값어치도 없다고 여기시지요.”"]], [["\"I'd a great deal sooner talk to you, Mrs. Lupex, than I would to Sir Raffle Buffle.\"", "“저는 래플 버플 경보다야 루펙스 부인과 이야기하는 편이 훨씬 훨씬 좋습니다.”"]], [["\"It's all very well for you to say that.", "“그렇게 말씀하시기야 쉽지요."], ["But we women know what such compliments as those mean;--don't we, Miss Spruce?", "하지만 우리 여자들은 그런 겉치레 말이 무슨 뜻인지 안답니다—그렇지 않아요, 스프루스 양?"], ["A woman that's been married five years as I have--or I may say six,--doesn't expect much attention from young men.", "나처럼 혼인한 지 다섯 해 된 여자는—여섯 해라고 해도 되겠네요—젊은 사내들의 관심을 그리 바라지 않아요."], ["And though I was young when I married--young in years, that is,--I'd seen too much and gone through too much to be young in heart.\"", "그리고 나는 혼인할 때 젊기는 했지만—나이로야 젊었다는 말이에요—너무 많은 것을 보고 너무 많은 것을 겪은 터라 마음까지 젊을 수는 없었지요.”"], ["This she said almost in a whisper; but Miss Spruce heard it, and was confirmed in her belief that Burton Crescent was no longer respectable.", "이 말을 그녀는 거의 속삭이듯 했다. 그러나 스프루스 양은 그것을 들었고, 버턴 크레슨트가 더는 점잖은 곳이 아니라는 제 믿음을 굳혔다."]], [["\"I don't know what you were then, Mrs. Lupex,\"", "“그때 부인이 어떠셨는지야 저는 모릅니다,”"], ["said Eames; \"but you're young enough now for anything.\"", "임스가 말했다. “하지만 지금도 무엇을 하기에든 넉넉히 젊으신걸요.”"]], [["\"Mr.", "“임스 씨, 나한테 남은 젊음이라면 죄다 헐값에 팔아 버리겠어요—아주 헐값에요."], ["Eames, I'd sell all that remains of my youth at a cheap rate,--at a very cheap rate, if I could only be sure of--\"", "다만 이것 하나만 확실할 수 있다면요—”"]], [["\"Sure of what, Mrs. Lupex?\"", "“무엇이 확실하면 말입니까, 루펙스 부인?”"]], [["\"The undivided affection of the one person that I loved.", "“내가 사랑한 그 한 사람의 오롯한 애정이지요."], ["That is all that is necessary to a woman's happiness.\"", "여자의 행복에 필요한 건 그것뿐이에요.”"]], [["\"And isn't Lupex--\"", "“그러면 루펙스 씨는—”"]], [["\"Lupex!", "“루펙스라니!"], ["But, hush, never mind.", "아니, 쉿, 그만두세요."], ["I should not have allowed myself to be betrayed into an expression of feeling.", "내가 속내를 드러내는 말에 끌려 들어가게 두어서는 안 되는 거였어요."], ["Here's your friend Mr. Cradell.", "저기 임스 씨의 벗 크래들 씨가 오시네요."], ["Do you know I sometimes wonder what you find in that man to be so fond of him.\"", "나는 이따금 궁금하답니다. 저분한테서 대체 무얼 보셨기에 저리 좋아하시는지 말이에요.”"], ["Miss Spruce saw it all, and heard it all, and positively resolved upon moving herself to those two small rooms at Dulwich.", "스프루스 양은 그 모든 것을 보았고 그 모든 것을 들었으며, 덜리치에 있는 그 작은 방 두 칸으로 옮겨 가기로 단단히 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["Hardly a word was exchanged between Amelia and Eames before dinner.", "저녁 식사 전에 어밀리아와 임스 사이에는 말이 거의 오가지 않았다."], ["Amelia still devoted herself to Cradell, and Johnny saw that that arrow, if it should be needed, would be a strong weapon. Mrs.", "어밀리아는 여전히 크래들에게 온 마음을 쏟고 있었고, 조니는 그 화살이 필요해질 경우 튼튼한 무기가 되리라는 것을 알아보았다."], ["Roper they found seated at her place at the dining-table, and Eames could perceive the traces of her tears.", "로퍼 부인은 식탁의 제자리에 앉아 있었는데, 임스는 그 얼굴에 남은 눈물 자국을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["Poor woman!", "가엾은 여인!"], ["Few positions in life could be harder to bear than hers!", "살아가면서 그녀의 처지보다 견디기 힘든 자리도 흔치 않을 것이다!"], ["To be ever tugging at others for money that they could not pay; to be ever tugged at for money which she could not pay; to desire respectability for its own sake, but to be driven to confess that it was a luxury beyond her means; to put up with disreputable belongings for the sake of lucre, and then not to get the lucre, but be driven to feel that she was ruined by the attempt!", "남들이 치를 수 없는 돈을 노상 조르고, 제가 치를 수 없는 돈으로 노상 졸림을 당하고, 점잖음 그 자체를 바라면서도 그것이 제 형편에 넘치는 사치라는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없게 되고, 돈푼을 바라 점잖지 못한 식구들을 참아 주다가 정작 그 돈푼은 얻지도 못한 채 그 시도 때문에 제가 거덜이 났다고 느끼게 되다니!"], ["How many Mrs. Ropers there are who from year to year sink down and fall away, and no one knows whither they betake themselves!", "해마다 이렇게 주저앉아 스러져 가는 로퍼 부인들이 얼마나 많으며, 그들이 어디로 흘러가는지는 아무도 모른다!"], ["One fancies that one sees them from time to time at the corners of the streets in battered bonnets and thin gowns, with the tattered remnants of old shawls upon their shoulders, still looking as though they had within them a faint remembrance of long-distant respectability.", "우리는 이따금 거리 모퉁이에서 그들을 보는 듯한 느낌이 든다. 찌그러진 모자를 쓰고 얇은 원피스를 입고 어깨에는 낡은 숄의 너덜너덜한 자락을 걸친 채, 아득히 먼 옛날의 점잖던 시절을 아직 어렴풋이 마음에 담고 있는 듯한 낯빛으로 말이다."], ["With anxious eyes they peer about, as though searching in the streets for other lodgers.", "그들은 근심 어린 눈으로 사방을 두리번거린다. 마치 거리에서 또 다른 하숙인들을 찾고 있기라도 하듯이."], ["Where do they get their daily morsels of bread, and their poor cups of thin tea,--their cups of thin tea, with perhaps a pennyworth of gin added to it, if Providence be good!", "그들은 날마다의 빵 한 조각과 멀건 차 한 잔을—섭리가 너그럽다면 진 한 푼어치쯤 타 넣은 그 멀건 차 한 잔을 대체 어디서 얻는 것일까!"], ["Of this state of things Mrs. Roper had a lively appreciation, and now, poor woman, she feared that she was reaching it, by the aid of the Lupexes.", "로퍼 부인은 이런 형편이 어떤 것인지를 생생하게 알고 있었고, 이제 가엾게도 루펙스네 사람들 덕분에 제가 거기에 이르고 있다고 두려워하고 있었다."], ["On the present occasion she carved her joint of meat in silence, and sent out her slices to the good guests that would leave her, and to the bad guests that would remain, with apathetic impartiality.", "이날 그녀는 말없이 고깃덩이를 썰어, 저를 떠날 좋은 손님들에게도 남아 있을 나쁜 손님들에게도 무심하리만큼 고르게 저민 고기를 돌렸다."], ["What was the use now of doing favour to one lodger or disfavour to another?", "이제 와서 어느 하숙인에게 잘 보이고 어느 하숙인에게 밉보이는 것이 무슨 소용이 있단 말인가?"], ["Let them take their mutton,--they who would pay for it and they who would not.", "다들 제 몫의 양고기를 가져가라지—셈을 치를 이들도, 치르지 않을 이들도."], ["She would not have the carving of many more joints in that house if Chumpend acted up to all the threats which he had uttered to her that morning.", "첨펜드가 그날 아침 그녀에게 퍼부은 으름장을 죄다 실행에 옮긴다면, 그녀가 그 집에서 고깃덩이를 썰 일도 이제 얼마 남지 않았을 것이었다."]], [["The reader may, perhaps, remember the little back room behind the dining parlour.", "독자들은 어쩌면 식당 뒤편의 그 작은 방을 기억할지도 모르겠다."], ["A description was given in some former pages of an interview which was held between Amelia and her lover.", "앞선 어느 대목에서 어밀리아와 그 연인 사이에 있었던 면담을 적어 둔 적이 있다."], ["It was in that room that all the interviews of Mrs. Roper's establishment had their existence.", "로퍼 부인네 집안의 모든 면담이 이루어지는 곳이 바로 그 방이었다."], ["A special room for interviews is necessary in all households of a mixed nature.", "여러 사람이 섞여 사는 집에는 어디든 면담을 위한 방이 따로 있어야 한다."], ["If a man lives alone with his wife, he can have his interviews where he pleases.", "사내가 제 아내와 단둘이 산다면 마음에 드는 자리에서 면담을 할 수 있다."], ["Sons and daughters, even when they are grown up, hardly create the necessity of an interview-chamber, though some such need may be felt if the daughters are marriageable and independent in their natures.", "아들딸은 다 자란 뒤라 해도 면담실이 꼭 있어야 할 까닭을 좀처럼 만들지 않는다. 다만 딸들이 혼기에 이르고 성정이 자립적이라면 그런 필요가 얼마쯤 느껴질 수는 있다."], ["But when the family becomes more complicated than this, if an extra young man be introduced, or an aunt comes into residence, or grown up children by a former wife interfere with the domestic simplicity, then such accommodation becomes quite indispensable.", "그러나 식구가 이보다 얽히게 되면—군식구 젊은이가 하나 들어오거나, 이모가 함께 살게 되거나, 전처 소생의 다 자란 자식들이 집안의 단출함을 어지럽히게 되면—그런 설비가 아주 없어서는 안 될 것이 된다."], ["No woman would think of taking in lodgers without such a room; and this room there was at Mrs.", "그런 방 없이 하숙을 치겠다고 생각할 여자는 없다."], ["Roper's, very small and dingy, but still sufficient,--just behind the dining parlour and opposite to the kitchen stairs.", "로퍼 부인네에도 이 방이 있었으니, 몹시 작고 우중충하기는 해도 아쉬운 대로 넉넉했다—바로 식당 뒤편, 부엌으로 내려가는 계단 맞은편이었다."], ["Hither, after dinner, Amelia was summoned. She had just seated herself between Mrs.", "저녁 식사가 끝난 뒤 어밀리아가 이곳으로 불려 내려왔다."], ["Lupex and Miss Spruce, ready to do battle with the former because she would stay, and with the latter because she would go, when she was called out by the servant girl.", "그녀는 막 루펙스 부인과 스프루스 양 사이에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 앞의 사람과는 나가지 않는다고, 뒤의 사람과는 나간다고 한바탕 붙을 채비를 하던 참에 하녀에게 불려 나왔다."]], [["\"Miss Mealyer, Miss Mealyer,--sh--sh--sh!\"", "“밀리어 아가씨, 밀리어 아가씨,—쉬—쉬—쉿!”"], ["And Amelia, looking round, saw a large red hand beckoning to her.", "어밀리아가 돌아보니 크고 붉은 손 하나가 저를 부르고 있었다."], ["\"He's down there,\"", "“저 아래 와 계십니다요.”"], ["said Jemima, as soon as her young mistress had joined her, \"and wants to see you most partic'lar.\"", "젊은 주인 아가씨가 곁으로 오자마자 제미마가 말했다. “아가씨를 꼭 좀 봬야겠답니다요.”"]], [["\"Which of 'em?\"", "“둘 중에 누구 말이냐?”"], ["asked Amelia, in a whisper.", "어밀리아가 소곤소곤 물었다."]], [["\"Why, Mr.", "“왜, 임스 나리시죠, 그럼요."], ["Heames, to be sure.", "다른 쪽 나리하고는 무슨 말도 섞지 마셔요, 밀리어 아가씨, 제발 그러지 마셔요."], ["Don't you go and have anythink to say to the other one, Miss Mealyer, pray don't; he ain't no good; he ain't indeed.\"", "그 나리는 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없습니다요. 정말이지 없고말고요.”"]], [["Amelia stood still for a moment on the landing, calculating whether it would be well for her to have the interview, or well to decline it.", "어밀리아는 층계참에 잠시 멈춰 서서, 이 면담에 응하는 편이 나을지 마다하는 편이 나을지 셈해 보았다."], ["Her objects were two;--or, rather, her object was in its nature twofold.", "그녀의 목적은 두 가지였다—아니, 그보다는 그 목적이 성질상 두 갈래였다."], ["She was, naturally, anxious to drive John Eames to desperation; and anxious also, by some slight added artifice, to make sure of Cradell if Eames's desperation did not have a very speedy effect.", "그녀는 당연히 존 임스를 애가 타 못 견디게 만들고 싶었고, 임스의 그 애태움이 아주 빨리 효험을 내지 못할 경우에 대비해 무슨 자잘한 재간이라도 더 부려 크래들만은 확실히 붙들어 두고 싶었다."], ["She agreed with Jemima's criticism in the main, but she did not go quite so far as to think that Cradell was no good at all.", "그녀는 제미마의 평을 대체로 옳다고 여겼으나, 크래들이 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없다는 데까지는 나아가지 않았다."], ["Let it be Eames, if Eames were possible; but let the other string be kept for use if Eames were not possible.", "될 수만 있다면 임스로 하자. 그러나 임스가 안 될 경우에 대비해 다른 활시위 하나는 쓸 수 있게 남겨 두자."], ["Poor girl!", "가엾은 처녀!"], ["in coming to this resolve she had not done so without agony.", "이런 결심에 이르기까지 그녀도 괴로움이 없지는 않았다."], ["She had a heart, and with such power as it gave her, she loved John Eames.", "그녀에게도 마음이 있었고, 그 마음이 주는 힘이 미치는 데까지 그녀는 존 임스를 사랑했다."], ["But the world had been hard to her; knocking her about hither and thither unmercifully; threatening, as it now threatened, to take from her what few good things she enjoyed.", "그러나 세상은 그녀에게 모질었다. 이리저리 사정없이 그녀를 후려쳤고, 지금 그러하듯 그녀가 누리는 몇 안 되는 좋은 것들마저 앗아 가겠다고 을러댔다."], ["When a girl is so circumstanced she cannot afford to attend to her heart.", "처녀가 이런 처지에 놓이면 제 마음을 돌볼 형편이 못 된다."], ["She almost resolved not to see Eames on the present occasion, thinking that he might be made the more desperate by such refusal, and remembering also that Cradell was in the house and would know of it.", "그녀는 이번에는 임스를 만나지 말까 하고 거의 마음을 굳혔다. 그렇게 거절하면 그가 더 애가 탈지도 모른다고 여겼고, 크래들이 집 안에 있어 그 일을 알게 되리라는 것도 떠올랐던 것이다."]], [["\"He's there a-waiting, Miss Mealyer.", "“나리가 저기서 기다리고 계십니다요, 밀리어 아가씨."], ["Why don't yer come down?\"", "어째서 안 내려가십니까요?”"], ["and Jemima plucked her young mistress by the arm.", "그러면서 제미마는 젊은 주인 아가씨의 팔을 잡아끌었다."]], [["\"I am coming,\"", "“지금 가잖니.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."], ["And with dignified steps she descended to the interview.", "그러고서 위엄 있는 걸음걸이로 면담 자리로 내려갔다."]], [["\"Here she is, Mr. Heames,\"", "“여기 오셨습니다요, 임스 나리.”"], ["said the girl.", "하녀가 말했다."], ["And then Johnny found himself alone with his lady-love.", "그러고서 조니는 제 연인과 단둘이 남겨졌다."]], [["\"You have sent for me, Mr. Eames,\"", "“저를 부르셨다지요, 임스 씨.”"], ["she said, giving her head a little toss, and turning her face away from him.", "그녀는 고개를 살짝 젖히고 얼굴을 그에게서 돌리며 말했다."], ["\"I was engaged upstairs, but I thought it uncivil not to come down to you as you sent for me so special.\"", "“위층에 볼일이 있었지만, 그렇게 각별히 부르시는데 내려오지 않는 건 예의가 아닐 듯싶어서요.”"]], [["\"Yes, Miss Roper, I did want to see you very particularly.\"", "“예, 로퍼 양, 아주 각별히 뵙고 싶었습니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, dear!\"", "“어머, 저런!”"], ["she exclaimed, and he understood fully that the exclamation referred to his having omitted the customary use of her Christian name.", "그녀가 소리쳤다. 그 외침이 그가 늘 하던 대로 세례명을 불러 주지 않은 것을 두고 한 말임을 그는 똑똑히 알아들었다."]], [["\"I saw your mother before dinner, and I told her that I am going away the day after to-morrow.\"", "“저녁 식사 전에 어머님을 뵙고, 제가 모레 떠난다고 말씀드렸습니다.”"]], [["\"We all know about that;--to the earl's, of course!\"", "“그거야 우리도 다 알지요—백작님 댁으로 가시는 거잖아요!”"], ["And then there was another chuck of her head.", "그러고서 또 한 번 고개를 홱 젖혔다."]], [["\"And I told her also that I had made up my mind not to come back to Burton Crescent.\"", "“그리고 버턴 크레슨트로는 다시 돌아오지 않기로 마음을 정했다는 말씀도 드렸습니다.”"]], [["\"What!", "“뭐라고요!"], ["leave the house altogether!\"", "이 집을 아주 나가신다고요!”"]], [["\"Well; yes.", "“글쎄요, 그렇습니다."], ["A fellow must make a change sometimes, you know.\"", "사람이란 이따금 옮겨 다녀야 하는 법이잖습니까.”"]], [["\"And where are you going, John?\"", "“그래서 어디로 가시는데요, 존?”"]], [["\"That I don't know as yet.\"", "“그건 아직 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["\"Tell me the truth, John; are you going to be married?", "“사실대로 말해 봐요, 존. 혼인을 하시려는 건가요?"], ["Are you--going--to marry--that young woman,--Mr. Crosbie's leavings?", "그 젊은 여자와—크로즈비 씨가 먹다 남긴 것과 혼인하시려는 거예요?"], ["I demand to have an answer at once.", "당장 대답을 들어야겠어요."], ["Are you going to marry her?\"", "그 여자와 혼인하실 참인가요?”"]], [["He had determined very resolutely that nothing she might say should make him angry, but when she thus questioned him about \"Crosbie's leavings\" he found it very difficult to keep his temper.", "그는 그녀가 무슨 말을 하든 화를 내지 않겠다고 아주 단단히 마음먹고 있었으나, 그녀가 이렇게 “크로즈비가 먹다 남긴 것”을 들먹이며 캐묻자 성미를 다스리기가 여간 어렵지 않았다."], ["\"I have not come,\"", "“저는,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, \"to speak to you about any one but ourselves.\"", "“우리 두 사람 말고 다른 누구의 이야기를 하러 온 게 아닙니다.”"]], [["\"That put-off won't do with me, sir.", "“그런 얼버무림은 나한테 통하지 않아요, 선생님."], ["You are not to treat any girl you may please in that sort of way;--oh, John!\"", "마음 내키는 대로 아무 처녀나 그런 식으로 다루어서는 안 되는 거예요—아, 존!”"], ["Then she looked at him as though she did not know whether to fly at him and cover him with kisses, or to fly at him and tear his hair.", "그러고서 그녀는 그에게 달려들어 입맞춤을 퍼부어야 할지, 달려들어 머리채를 쥐어뜯어야 할지 모르겠다는 얼굴로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["\"I know I haven't behaved quite as I should have done,\"", "“제가 마땅히 그래야 했던 만큼 처신하지 못한 건 압니다.”"], ["he began.", "그가 입을 열었다."]], [["\"Oh, John!\"", "“아, 존!”"], ["and she shook her head.", "그러면서 그녀는 고개를 저었다."], ["\"You mean, then, to tell me that you are going to marry her?\"", "“그러니까 그 여자와 혼인할 참이라는 말씀을 하시겠다는 거군요?”"]], [["\"I mean to say nothing of the kind.", "“그런 말은 조금도 할 생각이 없습니다."], ["I only mean to say that I am going away from Burton Crescent.\"", "그저 버턴 크레슨트를 떠난다는 말씀을 드리려는 것뿐이에요.”"]], [["\"John Eames, I wonder what you think will come to you!", "“존 임스, 당신한테 무슨 일이 닥칠 것 같은지 나는 모르겠군요!"], ["Will you answer me this; have I had a promise from you,--a distinct promise, over and over again, or have I not?\"", "이것 하나만 대답해 봐요. 내가 당신한테서 약속을—또렷한 약속을 몇 번이고 거듭 받았어요, 안 받았어요?”"]], [["\"I don't know about a distinct promise--\"", "“또렷한 약속이라고까지는 잘 모르겠습니다만—”"]], [["\"Well, well!", "“어머나, 어머나!"], ["I did think that you was a gentleman that would not go back from your word.", "나는 당신이 제 말을 뒤집지 않을 신사인 줄로 알았어요."], ["I did think that.", "정말 그런 줄 알았지요."], ["I did think that you would never put a young lady to the necessity of bringing forward her own letters to prove that she is not expecting more than she has a right!", "나는 당신이 아가씨더러 제가 분수에 넘치는 것을 바라는 게 아님을 밝히려고 제 편지를 꺼내 들게 만드는 일 따위는 결코 하지 않을 줄 알았어요!"], ["You don't know!", "모르겠다고요!"], ["And that, after all that has been between us!", "그것도 우리 사이에 그 모든 일이 있고 나서!"], ["John Eames!\"", "존 임스!”"], ["And again it seemed to him as though she were about to fly.", "그러자 다시금 그에게는 그녀가 곧 달려들 것만 같아 보였다."]], [["\"I tell you that I know I haven't behaved well.", "“저도 잘 처신하지 못한 걸 안다고 말씀드리지 않습니까."], ["What more can I say?\"", "그 밖에 무슨 말을 더 할 수 있겠습니까?”"]], [["\"What more can you say?", "“무슨 말을 더 할 수 있느냐고요?"], ["Oh, John!", "아, 존!"], ["to ask me such a question!", "나한테 그런 걸 묻다니!"], ["If you were a man you would know very well what more to say.", "당신이 사내대장부라면 무슨 말을 더 해야 할지 아주 잘 알 텐데요."], ["But all you private secretaries are given to deceit, as the sparks fly upwards.", "하지만 개인 비서라는 사람들은 죄다 속임수를 타고나는 법이지요. 불똥이 위로 튀는 것이나 마찬가지로 말이에요."], ["However, I despise you,--I do, indeed.", "어쨌거나 나는 당신을 경멸해요—정말이지 경멸해요."], ["I despise you.\"", "나는 당신을 경멸해요.”"]], [["\"If you despise me, we might as well shake hands and part at once.", "“저를 경멸하신다면 지금 당장 악수하고 헤어지는 편이 낫겠군요."], ["I daresay that will be best.", "아마 그게 제일 나을 겁니다."], ["One doesn't like to be despised, of course; but sometimes one can't help it.\"", "경멸당하는 걸 좋아할 사람이야 물론 없지요. 하지만 때로는 어쩔 수 없는 일이니까요.”"], ["And then he put out his hand to her.", "그러고서 그는 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["\"And is this to be the end of all?\"", "“그럼 이게 다 끝이라는 거예요?”"], ["she said, taking it.", "그 손을 잡으며 그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, yes; I suppose so.", "“글쎄요, 그렇습니다. 그런 것 같군요."], ["You say I'm despised.\"", "저를 경멸한다고 하시니까요.”"]], [["\"You shouldn't take up a poor girl in that way for a sharp word,--not when she is suffering as I am made to suffer.", "“모진 말 한마디를 그런 식으로 물고 늘어져 가엾은 처녀를 몰아세우면 안 되지요—내가 이렇게 괴로움을 겪게 된 마당에는 더더욱요."], ["If you only think of it,--think what I have been expecting!\"", "한번 생각이나 해 봐요—내가 무엇을 바라고 있었는지 생각해 보라고요!”"], ["And now Amelia began to cry, and to look as though she were going to fall into his arms.", "그러면서 어밀리아는 울기 시작했고, 곧 그의 품에 쓰러질 것만 같은 낯빛이 되었다."]], [["\"It is better to tell the truth,\"", "“사실대로 말하는 편이 낫지요.” 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"isn't it?\"", "“안 그렇습니까?”"]], [["\"But it shouldn't be the truth.\"", "“하지만 그게 사실이어서는 안 되는 거예요.”"]], [["\"But it is the truth.", "“하지만 사실인걸요."], ["I couldn't do it.", "저는 그렇게 할 수 없습니다."], ["I should ruin myself and you too, and we should never be happy.\"", "저도 망치고 당신도 망칠 테고, 우리는 결코 행복해지지 못할 겁니다.”"]], [["\"I should be happy,--very happy indeed.\"", "“나는 행복할 거예요—정말이지 아주 행복할 거예요.”"], ["At this moment the poor girl's tears were unaffected, and her words were not artful.", "이 순간 가엾은 처녀의 눈물에는 꾸밈이 없었고 그 말에는 잔꾀가 없었다."], ["For a minute or two her heart,--her actual heart,--was allowed to prevail.", "한두 순간 동안 그녀의 마음이—그녀의 참된 마음이 앞설 수 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"It cannot be, Amelia.", "“그럴 수는 없습니다, 어밀리아."], ["Will you not say good-by?\"", "작별 인사를 해 주지 않겠습니까?”"]], [["\"Good-by,\"", "“안녕히 가세요.”"], ["she said, leaning against him as she spoke.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하며 그에게 몸을 기댔다."]], [["\"I do so hope you will be happy,\"", "“부디 행복하시기를 진심으로 빕니다.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["And then, putting his arm round her waist, he kissed her; which he certainly ought not to have done.", "그러고서 그녀의 허리에 팔을 두르고 입을 맞추었으니, 그것은 그가 분명 하지 말았어야 할 일이었다."]], [["When the interview was over, he escaped out into the crescent, and as he walked down through the squares,--Woburn Square, and Russell Square, and Bedford Square,--towards the heart of London, he felt himself elated almost to a state of triumph.", "면담이 끝나자 그는 크레슨트로 빠져나왔다. 그리고 여러 스퀘어를—워번 스퀘어와 러셀 스퀘어와 베드퍼드 스퀘어를—지나 런던 한복판을 향해 걸어 내려가면서, 그는 거의 승리감이라 할 만큼 마음이 들떠 오르는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He had got himself well out of his difficulties, and now he would be ready for his love-tale to Lily.", "그는 제 곤경에서 말끔히 빠져나왔고, 이제 릴리에게 사랑 이야기를 꺼낼 채비가 될 터였다."]], [["# CHAPTER LII. THE FIRST VISIT TO THE GUESTWICK BRIDGE.", "# 제52장. 게스트윅 다리로의 첫 방문"]], [["When John Eames arrived at Guestwick Manor, he was first welcomed by Lady Julia. \"My dear Mr.", "존 임스가 게스트윅 저택에 닿았을 때 그를 맨 먼저 맞이한 사람은 레이디 줄리아였다."], ["Eames,\" she said, \"I cannot tell you how glad we are to see you.\"", "“임스 씨, 뵙게 되어 얼마나 반가운지 모르겠어요.”"], ["After that she always called him John, and treated him throughout his visit with wonderful kindness.", "그녀가 말했다. 그 뒤로 그녀는 늘 그를 존이라고 불렀고, 머무는 내내 놀라우리만치 친절하게 대해 주었다."], ["No doubt that affair of the bull had in some measure produced this feeling; no doubt, also, she was well disposed to the man who she hoped might be accepted as a lover by Lily Dale.", "황소 사건이 이런 마음을 얼마쯤 자아냈으리라는 것은 의심할 바 없다. 릴리 데일이 연인으로 받아 주기를 그녀가 바라는 사내였으니 호의를 품게 된 것도 의심할 바 없다."], ["But I am inclined to think that the fact of his having beaten Crosbie had been the most potential cause of this affection for our hero on the part of Lady Julia.", "하지만 나는 레이디 줄리아가 우리 주인공에게 이런 애정을 품게 된 가장 강력한 원인은 그가 크로즈비를 두들겨 팼다는 사실이었다고 생각하는 쪽이다."], ["Ladies,--especially discreet old ladies, such as Lady Julia De Guest,--are bound to entertain pacific theories, and to condemn all manner of violence.", "부인들은, 특히 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트 같은 조심스러운 노부인들은 평화로운 지론을 지녀야 마땅하고 온갖 종류의 폭력을 규탄해야 마땅하다."], ["Lady Julia would have blamed any one who might have advised Eames to commit an assault upon Crosbie.", "레이디 줄리아라면 임스에게 크로즈비를 폭행하라고 부추긴 사람이 있었다면 누구든 나무랐을 것이다."], ["But, nevertheless, deeds of prowess are still dear to the female heart, and a woman, be she ever so old and discreet, understands and appreciates the summary justice which may be done by means of a thrashing.", "그러나 그럼에도 무용의 행적은 여전히 여자의 마음에 소중한 것이어서, 여자란 아무리 나이가 들고 조심스럽다 한들 한바탕 두들겨 패는 것으로 이루어지는 즉결의 정의를 이해하고 또 높이 산다."], ["Lady Julia, had she been called upon to talk of it, would undoubtedly have told Eames that he had committed a fault in striking Mr.", "레이디 줄리아도 그 일을 두고 말해 보라는 요구를 받았다면 크로즈비 씨를 때린 것은 잘못이라고 임스에게 틀림없이 말했을 것이다."], ["Crosbie; but the deed had been done, and Lady Julia became very fond of John Eames.", "하지만 일은 이미 저질러졌고, 레이디 줄리아는 존 임스를 무척 좋아하게 되었다."]], [["\"Vickers shall show you your room, if you like to go upstairs; but you'll find my brother close about the house if you choose to go out; I saw him not half an hour since.\" But John seemed to be well satisfied to sit in the arm-chair over the fire, and talk to his hostess; so neither of them moved.", "“올라가 보고 싶으면 비커스가 방을 안내해 드릴 거예요. 그렇지만 밖으로 나가고 싶다면 우리 오라버니가 집 가까이에 있을 테니 만날 수 있을 겁니다. 반 시간도 안 되어 내가 봤거든요.” 하지만 존은 난롯가 안락의자에 앉아 안주인과 이야기하는 것으로 흡족한 듯했다. 그래서 둘 다 자리를 뜨지 않았다."]], [["\"And now that you're a private secretary, how do you like it?\"", "“그런데 이제 개인 비서가 되었으니, 그 일은 마음에 드나요?”"]], [["\"I like the work well enough; only I don't like the man, Lady Julia.", "“일은 그런대로 마음에 듭니다, 레이디 줄리아. 다만 그 사람이 마음에 들지 않습니다."], ["But I shouldn't say so, because he is such an intimate friend of your brother's.\"", "하지만 이런 말은 하면 안 되겠지요. 오라버님과 그토록 절친한 벗이시니까요.”"]], [["\"An intimate friend of Theodore's!--Sir Raffle Buffle!\"", "“시어도어의 절친한 벗이라니! 래플 버플 경이 말인가요!”"], ["Lady Julia stiffened her back and put on a serious face, not being exactly pleased at being told that the Earl De Guest had any such intimate friend.", "레이디 줄리아는 등을 곧추세우고 정색을 했다. 드 게스트 백작에게 그런 절친한 벗이 있다는 말이 썩 달갑지 않았던 것이다."]], [["\"At any rate he tells me so about four times a day, Lady Julia.", "“적어도 본인은 하루에 네 번쯤은 저더러 그렇게 말합니다, 레이디 줄리아."], ["And he particularly wants to come down here next September.\"", "그리고 오는 구월에 여기 꼭 내려오고 싶어 합니다.”"]], [["\"Did he tell you that, too?\"", "“그 말도 하던가요?”"]], [["\"Indeed he did.", "“정말 그랬습니다."], ["You can't believe what a goose he is!", "그가 얼마나 얼간이인지 마님께서는 믿지 못하실 겁니다!"], ["Then his voice sounds like a cracked bell; it's the most disagreeable voice you ever heard in your life.", "게다가 목소리는 금 간 종소리 같아서, 평생 들어 본 중에 가장 듣기 싫은 목소리입니다."], ["And one has always to be on one's guard lest he should make one do something that is--is--that isn't quite the thing for a gentleman.", "그리고 늘 조심하고 있어야 합니다. 신사로서는, 그러니까, 그러니까 온당치 못한 일을 시키지나 않을까 해서요."], ["You understand;--what the messenger ought to do.\"", "아시겠지요. 사환이 해야 할 일 말입니다.”"]], [["\"You shouldn't be too much afraid of your own dignity.\"", "“자기 위엄을 너무 겁내서는 못써요.”"]], [["\"No, I'm not.", "“아뇨, 그러지 않습니다."], ["If Lord De Guest were to ask me to fetch him his shoes, I'd run to Guestwick and back for them and think nothing of it,--just because I know he's my friend.", "드 게스트 경께서 저더러 신발을 가져오라 하시면, 게스트윅까지 뛰어갔다 오면서도 아무렇지 않게 여길 겁니다. 그분이 제 벗이라는 걸 아니까요."], ["He'd have a right to send me.", "그분에게는 저를 부릴 권리가 있습니다."], ["But I'm not going to do such things as that for Sir Raffle Buffle.\"", "하지만 래플 버플 경을 위해 그런 짓을 할 생각은 없습니다.”"]], [["\"Fetch him his shoes!\"", "“신발을 가져오라니!”"]], [["\"That's what FitzHoward had to do, and he didn't like it.\"", "“피츠하워드가 해야 했던 일이 바로 그겁니다. 그도 싫어했고요.”"]], [["\"Isn't Mr. FitzHoward nephew to the Duchess of St. Bungay?\"", "“피츠하워드 씨는 세인트 벙게이 공작부인의 조카 아닌가요?”"]], [["\"Nephew, or cousin, or something.\"", "“조카든 사촌이든 뭐 그렇습니다.”"]], [["\"Dear me!\"", "“저런!”"], ["said Lady Julia, \"what a horrible man!\"", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다. “참 끔찍한 사람이군요!”"], ["And in this way John Eames and her ladyship became very intimate.", "그리하여 존 임스와 그 마님은 아주 가까운 사이가 되었다."]], [["There was no one at dinner at the Manor that day but the earl and his sister and their single guest.", "그날 저택의 만찬 자리에는 백작과 그 누이, 그리고 단 한 사람의 손님뿐이었다."], ["The earl when he came in was very warm in his welcome, slapping his young friend on the back, and poking jokes at him with a good-humoured if not brilliant pleasantry.", "백작은 들어오면서 아주 따뜻하게 맞아 주었다. 젊은 벗의 등을 툭툭 치고, 빛나지는 않아도 사람 좋은 익살로 농담을 던졌다."]], [["\"Thrashed anybody lately, John?\"", "“요새 누구 두들겨 팬 적 있나, 존?”"]], [["\"Nobody to speak of,\"", "“입에 올릴 만한 사람은 없습니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Brought your nightcap down for your out-o'-doors nap?\"", "“한데서 낮잠 자려고 수면 모자는 챙겨 왔나?”"]], [["\"No; but I've got a grand stick for the bull,\"", "“아니요. 대신 황소 잡을 근사한 몽둥이는 가져왔습니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["that's no joke now, I can tell you,\"", "그건 이제 농담거리가 아닐세, 정말이야.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"We had to sell him, and it half broke my heart.", "“그놈을 팔아야 했는데, 그 바람에 내 가슴이 반쯤 무너졌지."], ["We don't know what had come to him, but he became quite unruly after that;--knocked Darvell down in the straw-yard!", "그놈한테 무슨 일이 있었던 건지는 모르겠네만, 그 뒤로 아주 사나워져서 말이야. 짚 마당에서 다벨을 들이받아 넘어뜨렸다네!"], ["It was a very bad business,--a very bad business, indeed!", "아주 고약한 일이었어. 정말이지 아주 고약한 일이었지!"], ["Come, go and dress.", "자, 가서 옷을 갈아입게."], ["Do you remember how you came down to dinner that day?", "그날 자네가 만찬에 내려오던 꼴을 기억하나?"], ["I shall never forget how Crofts stared at you.", "크로프츠가 자네를 빤히 쳐다보던 걸 나는 결코 잊지 못할 걸세."], ["Come, you've only got twenty minutes, and you London fellows always want an hour.\"", "자, 자네에게는 이십 분밖에 없네. 그런데 런던 친구들은 언제나 한 시간이 필요하지.”"]], [["\"He's entitled to some consideration now he's a private secretary,\"", "“이제 개인 비서가 되었으니 그만한 대접은 받아야지요.”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Bless us all!", "“아이고 저런!"], ["yes; I forgot that. Come, Mr.", "그렇지, 그걸 잊었군."], ["Private Secretary, don't stand on the grandeur of your neck-tie to-day, as there's nobody here but ourselves.", "자, 개인 비서 나리, 오늘은 넥타이의 위엄을 뽐내며 뻣뻣하게 굴 것 없네. 여기는 우리밖에 없으니 말이야."], ["You shall have an opportunity to-morrow.\"", "내일 그럴 기회를 주지.”"]], [["Then Johnny was handed over to the groom of the chambers, and exactly in twenty minutes he re-appeared in the drawing-room.", "그러고 나서 조니는 시중드는 하인에게 맡겨졌고, 정확히 이십 분 만에 응접실에 다시 나타났다."]], [["As soon as Lady Julia had left them after dinner, the earl began to explain his plan for the coming campaign.", "만찬이 끝나고 레이디 줄리아가 자리를 뜨자마자 백작은 다가올 싸움을 위한 제 계획을 설명하기 시작했다. “내가 어떻게 마련해 두었는지 이제 말해 주지.”"], ["\"I'll tell you now what I have arranged,\"", "그가 말했다. “지주가 내일 여기 온다네."], ["said he.", "맏조카딸을 데리고 말이야."], ["\"The squire is to be here to-morrow with his eldest niece,--your Miss Lily's sister, you know.\"", "자네의 그 릴리 양의 언니 말일세.”"]], [["\"What, Bell?\"", "“뭐라고요, 벨 말입니까?”"]], [["\"Yes, with Bell, if her name is Bell.", "“그래, 벨하고. 이름이 벨이라면 말이야."], ["She's a very pretty girl, too.", "그 아가씨도 아주 예쁘더군."], ["I don't know whether she's not the prettiest of the two, after all.\"", "어쩌면 둘 중에 그쪽이 더 예쁠지도 모르겠어.”"]], [["\"That's a matter of opinion.\"", "“그건 보기 나름이지요.”"]], [["\"Just so, Johnny; and do you stick to your own.", "“바로 그렇지, 조니. 자네는 자네 생각을 지키게."], ["They're coming here for three or four days.", "사나흘 묵으러 오는 거라네."], ["Lady Julia did ask Mrs. Dale and Lily.", "레이디 줄리아가 데일 부인과 릴리도 청하기는 했네."], ["I wonder whether you'll let me call her Lily?\"", "내가 그 아가씨를 릴리라고 불러도 될는지 모르겠군?”"]], [["\"Oh, dear!", "“아, 저런!"], ["I wish I might have the power of letting you.\"", "제가 허락해 드릴 힘이라도 있으면 좋겠습니다.”"]], [["\"That's just the battle that you've got to fight.", "“그게 바로 자네가 치러야 할 싸움이지."], ["But the mother and the younger sister wouldn't come.", "그런데 어머니와 동생은 오지 않겠다더군."], ["Lady Julia says it's all right;--that, as a matter of course, she wouldn't come when she heard you were to be here.", "레이디 줄리아 말로는 그게 다 잘된 일이라는 거야. 자네가 여기 와 있다는 말을 들었으니 당연히 오지 않는 거라고 말이야."], ["I don't quite understand it.", "나는 잘 모르겠네."], ["In my days the young girls were ready enough to go where they knew they'd meet their lovers, and I never thought any the worse of them for it.\"", "내 시절에는 젊은 아가씨들이 제 연인을 만날 수 있는 곳이라면 기꺼이 갔고, 나는 그 때문에 그 아가씨들을 나쁘게 본 적이 한 번도 없네.”"]], [["\"It wasn't because of that,\"", "“그것 때문은 아니었습니다.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"That's what Lady Julia says, and I always find her to be right in things of that sort.", "“레이디 줄리아도 그렇게 말하네. 그리고 그런 일이라면 나는 늘 그 애가 옳다는 걸 알게 되지."], ["And she says you'll have a better chance in going over there than you would here, if she were in the same house with you.", "그 애 말로는, 자네가 저쪽으로 건너가는 편이 그 아가씨와 한집에 묵으며 여기서 부딪치는 것보다 승산이 낫다는 거야."], ["If I was going to make love to a girl, of course I'd sooner have her close to me,--staying in the same house.", "내가 어떤 아가씨에게 구애를 한다면야 물론 그 아가씨를 가까이 두고 싶겠지. 같은 집에 묵으면서 말이야."], ["I should think it the best fun in the world.", "나라면 그보다 더 재미난 일이 세상에 없다고 여길 걸세."], ["And we might have had a dance, and all that kind of thing.", "무도회도 열 수 있었을 테고, 그런 온갖 것도 말이야."], ["But I couldn't make her come, you know.\"", "하지만 오라고 억지로 할 수야 없지 않은가.”"]], [["\"Oh, no; of course not.\"", "“아, 물론입니다. 그럴 수야 없지요.”"]], [["\"And Lady Julia thinks that it's best as it is.", "“그리고 레이디 줄리아는 이대로가 제일 낫다고 여기네."], ["You must go over, you know, and get the mother on your side, if you can.", "자네는 저쪽으로 건너가야 해. 그래서 할 수 있거든 어머니를 자네 편으로 만들어야지."], ["I take it, the truth is this;--you mustn't be angry with me, you know, for saying it.\"", "내가 보기에 진실은 이런 걸세. 내가 이런 말을 한다고 화내면 안 되네, 알겠나.”"]], [["\"You may be sure of that.\"", "“그럴 리 있겠습니까.”"]], [["\"I suppose she was fond of that fellow, Crosbie.", "“그 아가씨는 그 크로즈비란 작자를 좋아했겠지."], ["She can't be very fond of him now, I should think, after the way he has treated her; but she'll find a difficulty in making her confession that she really likes you better than she ever liked him.", "그자가 그 아가씨를 그렇게 대접한 마당에 이제는 그리 좋아할 리 없다고 나는 생각하네만, 그자를 좋아했던 것보다 자네를 정말 더 좋아한다고 고백하기는 어려울 걸세."], ["Of course that's what you'll want her to say.\"", "물론 자네가 그 아가씨한테 듣고 싶은 말이 그것 아닌가.”"]], [["\"I want her to say that she'll be my wife,--some day.\"", "“저는 언젠가 제 아내가 되겠노라는 말을 듣고 싶습니다.”"]], [["\"And when she has agreed to the some day, then you'll begin to press her to agree to your day;--eh, sir?", "“그리고 그 언젠가에 동의하고 나면, 그때부터는 자네가 정한 날에 동의하라고 몰아붙이기 시작하겠지. 안 그런가, 이 사람아?"], ["My belief is you'll bring her round.", "내 믿기로는 자네가 그 아가씨의 마음을 돌려놓을 걸세."], ["Poor girl!", "가엾은 것!"], ["why should she break her heart when a decent fellow like you will only be too glad to make her a happy woman?\"", "자네 같은 점잖은 사내가 기꺼이 행복한 여자로 만들어 주겠다는데 어째서 제 가슴을 찢어야 한단 말인가?”"], ["And in this way the earl talked to Eames till the latter almost believed that the difficulties were vanishing from out of his path. \"Could it be possible,\"", "이런 식으로 백작이 임스에게 이야기를 이어 가자, 마침내 임스는 제 앞길에서 어려움이 사라져 가고 있다고 거의 믿어 버릴 지경이 되었다."], ["he asked himself, as he went to bed, \"that in a fortnight's time Lily Dale should have accepted him as her future husband?\"", "“보름 뒤에 릴리 데일이 나를 장래의 남편으로 받아들이는 일이 있을 수 있을까?” 잠자리에 들면서 그는 스스로에게 물었다."], ["Then he remembered that day on which Crosbie, with the two girls, had called at his mother's house, when in the bitterness of his heart, he had sworn to himself that he would always regard Crosbie as his enemy.", "그러다 크로즈비가 두 아가씨와 함께 어머니의 집을 찾아왔던 그날이 떠올랐다. 그날 마음이 쓰디쓴 나머지 그는 크로즈비를 언제까지나 제 원수로 여기겠노라고 스스로 맹세했었다."], ["Since then the world had gone well with him; and he had no longer any very bitter feeling against Crosbie.", "그 뒤로 세상은 그에게 잘 굴러갔고, 이제 크로즈비에 대해 그리 쓰라린 감정은 남아 있지 않았다."], ["That matter had been arranged on the platform of the Paddington Station.", "그 문제는 패딩턴 역 승강장에서 정리되었으니 말이다."], ["He felt that if Lily would now accept him he could almost shake hands with Crosbie.", "릴리가 이제 그를 받아 준다면 크로즈비와 악수라도 할 수 있을 것 같았다."], ["The episode in his life and in Lily's would have been painful; but he would learn to look back upon that without regret, if Lily could be taught to believe that a kind fate had at last given her to the better of her two lovers.", "그의 생애와 릴리의 생애에 끼어든 그 삽화는 고통스러운 것이었으리라. 하지만 두 연인 가운데 나은 쪽에게 마침내 자기를 맡긴 것이 친절한 운명이었다고 릴리가 믿게 될 수만 있다면, 그는 후회 없이 그 일을 돌아보는 법을 배우게 되리라."], ["\"I'm afraid she won't bring herself to forget him,\"", "“그 사람이 그자를 잊지는 못할까 걱정입니다.”"], ["he had said to the earl.", "그가 백작에게 그렇게 말했었다."], ["\"She'll only be too happy to forget him,\"", "“잊으려는 시도를 시작하도록 자네가 이끌어 줄 수만 있다면,” 백작이 대답했다."], ["the earl had answered, \"if you can induce her to begin the attempt.", "“그 아가씨는 너무나 기꺼이 그자를 잊을 걸세. 물론 처음에는 몹시 쓰라리지."], ["Of course it is very bitter at first;--all the world knew about it; but, poor girl, she is not to be wretched for ever, because of that.", "온 세상이 그 일을 다 알고 있었으니 말이야. 하지만 가엾은 아가씨가 그것 때문에 영영 비참하게 지낼 수야 없지 않은가."], ["Do you go about your work with some little confidence, and I doubt not but what you'll have your way.", "자네는 조금쯤 자신을 갖고 일에 나서게. 그러면 자네 뜻대로 되지 않을까 싶네."], ["You have everybody in your favour,--the squire, her mother, and all.\"", "모두가 자네 편이야. 지주도, 그 어머니도, 다들 말일세.”"], ["While such words as these were in his ears how could he fail to hope and to be confident?", "이런 말들이 귀에 울리는데 어찌 희망을 품고 자신을 갖지 않을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["While he was sitting cozily over his bedroom fire he resolved that it should be as the earl had said.", "침실 난롯가에 아늑하게 앉아서 그는 백작의 말대로 되게 하리라고 마음먹었다."], ["But when he got up on the following morning, and stood shivering as he came out of his bath, he could not feel the same confidence.", "하지만 이튿날 아침 일어나 목욕을 하고 나와 오들오들 떨며 서 있을 때는 같은 자신감이 느껴지지 않았다. “물론 나는 릴리에게 갈 것이고,”"], ["\"Of course I shall go to her,\"", "그는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["he said to himself, \"and make a plain story of it.", "“꾸밈없이 사실대로 이야기할 것이다."], ["But I know what her answer will be.", "하지만 릴리가 뭐라고 답할지 나는 안다."], ["She will tell me that she cannot forget him.\"", "그 사람은 그자를 잊을 수 없노라고 나에게 말하겠지.”"], ["Then his feelings towards Crosbie were not so friendly as they had been on the previous evening.", "그러자 크로즈비를 향한 그의 감정은 전날 저녁만큼 우호적이지 않았다."]], [["He did not visit the Small House on that, his first day.", "그는 그 첫날에는 작은 집을 찾아가지 않았다."], ["It had been thought better that he should first meet the squire and Bell at Guestwick Manor, so he postponed his visit to Mrs. Dale till the next morning.", "게스트윅 저택에서 지주와 벨을 먼저 만나는 편이 낫겠다고들 여겼으므로, 데일 부인을 찾아가는 일은 이튿날 아침으로 미루었다."]], [["\"Go when you like,\"", "“가고 싶을 때 가게.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."], ["\"There's the brown cob for you to do what you like with him while you are here.\"", "“여기 있는 동안 자네 마음대로 부릴 갈색 승용마가 있네.”"]], [["\"I'll go and see my mother,\"", "“어머니를 뵈러 가겠습니다.”"], ["said John; \"but I won't take the cob to-day.", "존이 말했다. “하지만 오늘은 그 말을 타지 않겠습니다."], ["If you'll let me have him to-morrow, I'll ride to Allington.\"", "내일 빌려주시면 앨링턴까지 타고 가지요.”"], ["So he walked off to Guestwick by himself.", "그래서 그는 혼자 게스트윅까지 걸어갔다."]], [["He knew well every yard of the ground over which he went, remembering every gate and stile and greensward from the time of his early boyhood.", "그는 자기가 지나가는 땅을 한 뼘까지 훤히 알고 있었다. 어린 시절부터 보아 온 문이며 층계다리며 잔디밭을 하나하나 다 기억하고 있었다."], ["And now as he went along through his old haunts, he could not but look back and think of the thoughts which had filled his mind in his earlier wanderings.", "그리고 이제 옛날에 쏘다니던 곳들을 지나면서, 그는 지난날 헤매고 다닐 적에 마음을 채웠던 생각들을 되돌아보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["As I have said before, in some of these pages, no walks taken by the man are so crowded with thought as those taken by the boy.", "이 책의 앞쪽 어디에선가 내가 이미 말했듯이, 어른이 되어 걷는 산책 가운데 소년 시절의 산책만큼 생각으로 가득 찬 것은 없다."], ["He had been early taught to understand that the world to him would be very hard; that he had nothing to look to but his own exertions, and that those exertions would not, unfortunately, be backed by any great cleverness of his own.", "그는 세상이 자기에게 몹시 모질리라는 것을, 제 노력 말고는 기댈 것이 없다는 것을, 게다가 그 노력이 안타깝게도 대단한 영리함의 뒷받침을 받지는 못하리라는 것을 일찍부터 배워 알고 있었다."], ["I do not know that anybody had told him that he was a fool; but he had come to understand, partly through his own modesty, and partly, no doubt, through the somewhat obtrusive diffidence of his mother, that he was less sharp than other lads.", "누가 그에게 바보라고 말해 주었는지는 나도 모른다. 하지만 그는 한편으로는 제 겸손함 탓에, 또 한편으로는 틀림없이 어머니의 다소 노골적인 미덥잖아 하는 태도 탓에, 자기가 다른 젊은이들보다 영리하지 못하다고 여기게 되었다."], ["It is probably true that he had come to his sharpness later in life than is the case with many young men.", "그가 여느 젊은이들보다 늦은 나이에 이르러서야 영민해졌다는 것은 아마 사실일 것이다."], ["He had not grown on the sunny side of the wall.", "그는 볕 드는 담장 쪽에서 자라지 않았다."], ["Before that situation in the Income-tax Office had fallen in his way, very humble modes of life had offered themselves,--or, rather, had not offered themselves for his acceptance.", "소득세 사무국의 그 자리가 그의 앞에 굴러 들어오기 전에는 아주 보잘것없는 살길들이 그에게 주어졌다. 아니, 오히려 주어졌다고도 할 수 없었다."], ["He had endeavoured to become an usher at a commercial seminary, not supposed to be in a very thriving condition; but he had been, luckily, found deficient in his arithmetic.", "그는 그다지 번창한다고 할 수 없는 어느 상업 학교의 조교사가 되어 보려 했으나, 다행스럽게도 산수가 모자란다는 판정을 받았다."], ["There had been some chance of his going into the leather-warehouse of Messrs. Basil and Pigskin, but those gentlemen had required a premium, and any payment of that kind had been quite out of his mother's power.", "배절 앤드 피그스킨 상회의 가죽 창고에 들어갈 가망이 얼마간 있기도 했으나, 그 신사분들은 사례금을 요구했고 그런 돈을 치르는 일은 어머니의 힘에 도무지 미치지 못했다."], ["A country attorney, who had known the family for years, had been humbly solicited, the widow almost kneeling before him with tears, to take Johnny by the hand and make a clerk of him; but the attorney had discovered that Master Johnny Eames was not supposed to be sharp, and would have none of him.", "여러 해 그 집안을 알아 온 어느 시골 사무 변호사에게는, 미망인이 눈물을 흘리며 거의 무릎을 꿇다시피 하여 조니의 손을 잡아 서기로 삼아 달라고 간청했다. 그러나 그 사무 변호사는 조니 임스 도련님이 영리하다는 평판을 얻지 못했다는 것을 알아내고는 그를 마다했다."], ["During those days, those gawky, gainless, un-admired days, in which he had wandered about the lanes of Guestwick as his only amusement, and had composed hundreds of rhymes in honour of Lily Dale which no human eye but his own had ever seen, he had come to regard himself as almost a burden upon the earth.", "게스트윅의 오솔길을 쏘다니는 것만이 유일한 낙이었고 릴리 데일을 기려 제 눈 말고는 어떤 사람의 눈도 본 적 없는 시구를 수백 편이나 지어내던 그 시절, 그 어설프고 소득 없고 아무도 우러러보지 않던 시절에, 그는 스스로를 이 땅 위의 짐덩이나 다름없다고 여기게 되었다. 아무도 그를 원하지 않는 것 같았다."], ["Nobody seemed to want him.", "어머니는 무척 마음을 졸였다."], ["His own mother was very anxious; but her anxiety seemed to him to indicate a continual desire to get rid of him.", "그러나 그 조바심은 그에게 어서 자기를 떼어 놓고 싶어 하는 끊임없는 바람으로만 비쳤다."], ["For hours upon hours he would fill his mind with castles in the air, dreaming of wonderful successes in the midst of which Lily Dale always reigned as a queen.", "몇 시간이고 그는 공중누각으로 마음을 채우며, 릴리 데일이 언제나 여왕으로 군림하는 놀라운 성공을 꿈꾸었다."], ["He would carry on the same story in his imagination from month to month, almost contenting himself with such ideal happiness.", "그는 상상 속에서 같은 이야기를 달이 바뀌어도 계속 이어 갔고, 그런 상상 속의 행복에 거의 만족하다시피 했다."], ["Had it not been for the possession of that power, what comfort could there have been to him in his life?", "그런 힘을 지니지 못했다면 그의 삶에 무슨 위안이 있었겠는가?"], ["There are lads of seventeen who can find happiness in study, who can busy themselves in books and be at their ease among the creations of other minds.", "열일곱 살에 공부에서 행복을 찾을 수 있는 젊은이들, 책에 파묻혀 지내며 다른 사람들이 지어낸 것들 사이에서 마음이 편안할 수 있는 젊은이들도 있다. 나중에 학식 있는 사람이 되는 것은 그런 이들이다."], ["These are they who afterwards become well-informed men.", "존 임스는 그렇지 않았다. 그는 한 번도 공부에 매달린 적이 없었다."], ["It was not so with John Eames. He had never been studious.", "그 시절 그에게 소설 한 권을 읽는 일은 더디기 짝이 없는 일이었다."], ["The perusal of a novel was to him in those days a slow affair; and of poetry he read but little, storing up accurately in his memory all that he did read.", "시는 얼마 읽지 않았으나 읽은 것은 죄다 정확히 기억 속에 갈무리해 두었다. 하지만 그는 보기에는 더없이 낭만과 거리가 먼 젊은이였으면서도 제 나름의 로맨스를 스스로 지어냈다."], ["But he created for himself his own romance, though to the eye a most unromantic youth; and he wandered through the Guestwick woods with many thoughts of which they who knew him best knew nothing.", "그리고 그를 가장 잘 안다는 이들도 전혀 알지 못하는 온갖 생각을 품은 채 게스트윅 숲을 헤매고 다녔다. 옛집을 향해 에움길로 걸음을 옮기면서 그는 이 모든 것을 이제 다시 생각했다."], ["All this he thought of now as, with devious steps, he made his way towards his old home;--with very devious steps, for he went backwards through the woods by a narrow path which led right away from the town down to a little water-course, over which stood a wooden foot-bridge with a rail.", "참으로 에움길이었다. 그는 숲을 거슬러 올라, 읍내와는 정반대 쪽으로 뻗어 내려가 작은 물길에 닿는 좁은 길을 따라갔던 것이다."], ["He stood on the centre of the plank, at a spot which he knew well, and rubbing his hand upon the rail, cleansed it for the space of a few inches of the vegetable growth", "그 물길 위에는 난간이 달린 나무 인도교가 놓여 있었다. 그는 널빤지 한가운데, 자기가 잘 아는 자리에 서서 난간을 손으로 문질러 몇 인치 너비만큼 그 위에 뒤덮인 이끼를"]], [["was still the word LILY.", "여전히 LILY 라는 낱말이 있었다."], ["When he cut those letters she had been almost a child.", "그 글자들을 새겼을 때 릴리는 거의 어린아이였다."], ["\"I wonder whether she will come here with me and let me show it to her,\"", "“릴리가 나와 함께 여기 와서 이걸 보여 주게 해 줄까?”"], ["he said to himself.", "그는 스스로에게 물었다."], ["Then he took out his knife and cleared the cuttings of the letters, and having done so, leaned upon the rail, and looked down upon the running water.", "그러고는 칼을 꺼내 글자를 새긴 자리를 말끔히 긁어내고, 그러고 나서 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 흐르는 물을 내려다보았다."], ["How well things in the world had gone for him!", "세상일이 그에게 얼마나 잘 풀렸던가! 얼마나 잘 풀렸던가!"], ["How well! And yet what would it all be if Lily would not come to him?", "그런데도 릴리가 그에게 와 주지 않는다면 그 모든 것이 다 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["How well the world had gone for him!", "세상이 그에게 얼마나 잘 굴러갔던가!"], ["In those days when he stood there carving the girl's name everybody had seemed to regard him as a heavy burden, and he had so regarded himself.", "그 아가씨의 이름을 새기며 여기 서 있던 그 시절에는 모두가 그를 무거운 짐덩이로 여기는 것 같았고, 그 자신도 스스로를 그렇게 여겼다."], ["Now he was envied by many, respected by many, taken by the hand as a friend by those high in the world's esteem.", "이제 그는 많은 이의 부러움을 사고, 많은 이의 존경을 받고, 세상의 높은 자리에 있는 이들에게 벗으로서 손을 잡히는 사람이 되었다. 옛날에 쏘다니다가 게스트윅 저택 가까이 다가갔을 때, 그래도 늘 멀찍이 떨어져 있기는 했다."], ["When he had come near the Guestwick Mansion in his old walks,--always, however, keeping at a great distance lest the grumpy old lord should be down upon him and scold him,--he had little dreamed that he and the grumpy old lord would ever be together on such familiar terms, that he would tell to that lord more of his private thoughts than to any other living being; yet it had come to that.", "심술궂은 늙은 나리가 덮쳐 와 꾸짖을까 두려웠으므로. 그때 그는 자기와 그 심술궂은 늙은 나리가 언젠가 그토록 허물없는 사이가 되리라고는, 살아 있는 어떤 사람에게보다 그 나리에게 제 속마음을 더 많이 털어놓게 되리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했다."], ["The grumpy old lord had now told him that that gift of money was to be his whether Lily Dale accepted him or no.", "그런데 일이 그렇게 되었다. 그 심술궂은 늙은 나리는 이제, 릴리 데일이 그를 받아들이든 말든 그 돈은 그의 것이라고 그에게 말해 두었다."], ["\"Indeed, the thing's done,\"", "“실은 일이 이미 끝났네.”"], ["said the grumpy lord, pulling out from his pocket certain papers, \"and you've got to receive the dividends as they become due.\"", "그 심술궂은 나리가 주머니에서 어떤 서류들을 꺼내며 말했다. “그러니 배당금이 나올 때마다 자네가 받으면 되는 걸세.”"], ["Then, when Johnny had expostulated,--as, indeed, the circumstances had left him no alternative but to expostulate,--the earl had roughly bade him hold his tongue, telling him that he would have to fetch Sir Raffle's boots directly he got back to London.", "그래서 조니가 항변하자, 사정이 그로서는 항변 말고는 달리 어쩔 도리가 없게 만들었으므로 항변한 것인데, 백작은 무뚝뚝하게 입을 다물라 하면서 런던에 돌아가는 즉시 래플 경의 장화나 가지러 가야 할 처지라고 일러 주었다."], ["So the conversation had quickly turned itself away to Sir Raffle, whom they had both ridiculed with much satisfaction.", "그리하여 이야기는 이내 래플 경 쪽으로 돌아갔고, 두 사람은 그를 두고 아주 흡족하게 비웃었다."], ["\"If he finds his way down here in September, Master Johnny, or in any other month either, you may fit my head with a foolscap.", "“그자가 구월에 여기까지 길을 찾아 내려오거든, 조니 도련님, 아니 다른 어떤 달에라도 그런다면, 내 머리에 광대 모자를 씌워도 좋네."], ["Not remember, indeed!", "기억을 못 한다니, 참!"], ["Is it not wonderful that any man should make himself so mean a fool?\"", "사람이 스스로를 저토록 못난 바보로 만들 수 있다는 게 놀랍지 않은가?”"], ["All this was thought over again, as Eames leaned upon the bridge.", "임스가 다리에 몸을 기대고 있는 동안 이 모든 것이 다시 떠올랐다."], ["He remembered every word, and remembered many other words,--earlier words, spoken years ago, filling him with desolation as to the prospects of his life.", "그는 말 한마디 한마디를 다 기억했고, 또 다른 많은 말들도 기억했다. 여러 해 전에 들었던 더 오래된 말들, 제 앞날에 대해 그를 쓸쓸함으로 가득 채우던 말들이었다."], ["It had seemed that his friends had united in prophesying that the outlook into the world for him was hopeless, and that the earning of bread must be for ever beyond his power.", "세상으로 나아가는 그의 앞길은 가망이 없으며 빵을 벌어먹는 일은 영영 그의 힘 밖에 있으리라고, 그의 벗들이 입을 모아 예언하는 듯했었다."], ["And now his lines had fallen to him in very pleasant places, and he was among those whom the world had determined to caress.", "그런데 이제 그에게 줄로 재어 준 구역은 아주 즐거운 곳에 떨어졌고, 그는 세상이 떠받들기로 마음먹은 사람들 축에 끼어 있었다."], ["And yet, what would it all be if Lily would not share his happiness?", "그런데도 릴리가 그의 행복을 함께 나누어 주지 않는다면 그 모든 것이 다 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["When he had carved that name on the rail, his love for Lily had been an idea.", "난간에 그 이름을 새겼을 때 릴리를 향한 그의 사랑은 하나의 관념이었다."], ["It had now become a reality which might probably be full of pain.", "그것이 이제 아마도 고통으로 가득 차게 될 현실이 되었다."], ["If it were so,--if such should be the result of his wooing,--would not those old dreamy days have been better than these--the days of his success?", "만일 그렇다면, 그의 구애의 결말이 그러하다면, 그 옛날 꿈꾸던 나날이 지금 이 나날보다, 성공을 거둔 이 나날보다 오히려 나았던 것이 아닐까?"]], [["It was one o'clock by the time that he reached his mother's house, and he found her and his sister in a troubled and embarrassed state.", "어머니의 집에 닿았을 때는 한 시였는데, 어머니와 누이가 마음이 뒤숭숭하고 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 것을 보았다."], ["\"Of course you know, John,\"", "“물론 알고 있겠지, 존.”"], ["said his mother, as soon as their first embraces were over, \"that we are going to dine at the Manor this evening?\"", "첫 인사를 나누자마자 어머니가 말했다. “우리가 오늘 저녁 저택에서 만찬을 하기로 되어 있다는 걸 말이다.”"], ["But he did not know it, neither the earl nor Lady Julia having said anything on the subject.", "하지만 그는 그것을 모르고 있었다. 백작도 레이디 줄리아도 그 이야기는 한마디도 하지 않았던 것이다."], ["\"Of course we are going,\" said Mrs.", "“물론 우리는 갈 거란다.”"], ["Eames, \"and it was so very kind.", "임스 부인이 말했다. “그리고 참 친절도 하시지."], ["But I've never been out to such a house for so many years, John, and I do feel in such a twitter.", "하지만 그런 집에 나가 본 지가 하도 여러 해라서, 존, 가슴이 어찌나 두근거리는지 모르겠구나."], ["I dined there once, soon after we were married; but I never have been there since that.\"", "결혼하고 얼마 안 됐을 때 거기서 만찬을 한 적이 있단다. 하지만 그 뒤로는 한 번도 가 본 적이 없어.”"]], [["\"It's not the earl I mind, but Lady Julia,\"", "“저는 백작님이 무섭다기보다 레이디 줄리아가 무서워요.”"], ["said Mary Eames.", "메리 임스가 말했다."]], [["\"She's the most good-natured woman in the world,\"", "“세상에서 제일 마음씨 좋은 분이야.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, dear; people say she is so cross!\"", "“어머, 사람들은 그분이 아주 심술궂다고 하던데!”"]], [["\"That's because people don't know her.", "“그건 사람들이 그분을 몰라서 그래."], ["If I was asked who is the kindest-hearted woman I know in the world, I think I should say Lady Julia De Guest.", "내가 아는 세상 사람 가운데 누가 제일 마음씨가 고우냐고 묻는다면, 나는 레이디 줄리아 드 게스트라고 대답할 것 같아."], ["I think I should.\"", "정말 그럴 것 같아.”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["but then they're so fond of you,\"", "하지만 그건 그분들이 너를 그렇게 아끼시니까 그렇지.”"], ["said the admiring mother.", "감탄하는 어머니가 말했다."], ["\"You saved his lordship's life,--under Providence.\"", "“네가 그분의 목숨을 구했잖니. 하느님의 섭리 아래서 말이다.”"]], [["\"That's all bosh, mother.", "“그건 다 헛소리예요, 어머니."], ["You ask Dr. Crofts.", "크로프츠 선생한테 물어보세요."], ["He knows them as well as I do.\"", "그분도 저만큼 그분들을 잘 알아요.”"]], [["\"Dr. Crofts is going to marry Bell Dale,\"", "“크로프츠 선생이 벨 데일과 결혼한대요.”"], ["said Mary; and then the conversation was turned from the subject of Lady Julia's perfections, and the awe inspired by the earl.", "메리가 말했다. 그러자 이야기는 레이디 줄리아의 훌륭함이며 백작이 자아내는 경외감이라는 화제에서 벗어났다."]], [["\"Crofts going to marry Bell!\"", "“크로프츠가 벨과 결혼한다고!”"], ["exclaimed Eames, thinking almost with dismay of the doctor's luck in thus getting himself accepted all at once, while he had been suing with the constancy almost of a Jacob.", "임스가 소리쳤다. 자기는 야곱에 맞먹는 한결같음으로 구애해 왔건만 그 의사는 단번에 받아들여지는 행운을 누렸다는 것이 거의 낙담스럽기까지 했던 것이다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“네.”"], ["said Mary; \"and they say that she has refused her cousin Bernard, and that, therefore, the squire is taking away the house from them.", "메리가 말했다. “그리고 사람들 말로는 벨이 사촌 버나드를 퇴짜 놓았고, 그래서 지주 어른이 그 집을 빼앗아 간다지 뭐예요."], ["You know they're all coming into Guestwick.\"", "그 사람들이 다 게스트윅으로 온다는 건 오빠도 알잖아요.”"]], [["\"Yes, I know they are.", "“응, 온다는 건 알아."], ["But I don't believe that the squire is taking away the house.\"", "하지만 지주 어른이 그 집을 빼앗아 간다는 건 못 믿겠어.”"]], [["\"Why should they come then?", "“그럼 왜 오겠어요?"], ["Why should they give up such a charming place as that?\"", "왜 그렇게 좋은 데를 내놓겠어요?”"]], [["\"Rent-free!\"", "“집세도 안 내는데!”"], ["said Mrs. Eames.", "임스 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I don't know why they should come away, but I can't believe the squire is turning them out; at any rate not for that reason.\"", "“왜 그 집에서 나오는지는 나도 모르겠지만, 지주 어른이 그들을 내쫓는다는 건 못 믿겠어. 적어도 그런 까닭으로는 아니야.”"], ["The squire was prepared to advocate John's suit, and therefore John was bound to do battle on the squire's behalf.", "지주는 존의 구혼을 편들어 줄 참이었고, 따라서 존도 지주를 위해 싸울 의무가 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"He is a very stern man,\" said Mrs.", "“아주 엄한 분이시지.”"], ["Eames, \"and they say that since that affair of poor Lily's he has been more cross than ever with them.", "임스 부인이 말했다. “그리고 사람들 말이, 가엾은 릴리 일이 있고 나서는 그 사람들한테 어느 때보다 더 심술궂게 구신다더구나."], ["As far as I know, it was not Lily's fault.\"", "내가 알기로는 릴리 잘못이 아니었는데.”"]], [["\"Poor Lily!\"", "“가엾은 릴리!”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["\"I do pity her.", "“정말 안됐어요."], ["If I was her I should hardly know how to show my face; I shouldn't, indeed.\"", "내가 그 처지라면 낯을 들고 다닐 수도 없을 거예요. 정말 못 할 거예요.”"]], [["\"And why shouldn't she show her face?\"", "“낯을 못 들 게 뭐가 있어?”"], ["said John, in an angry tone.", "존이 성난 어조로 말했다."], ["\"What has she done to be ashamed of?", "“부끄러워할 짓을 무얼 했다고?"], ["Show her face indeed!", "낯을 들다니, 참!"], ["I cannot understand the spite which one woman will sometimes have to another.\"", "여자가 다른 여자에게 이따금 품는 그 앙심이라는 걸 나는 도무지 이해할 수가 없어.”"]], [["\"There is no spite, John; and it's very wrong of you to say so,\"", "“앙심 같은 건 없어요, 오빠. 그렇게 말하는 건 아주 나빠요.”"], ["said Mary, defending herself.", "메리가 자기를 변호하며 말했다."], ["\"But it is a very unpleasant thing for a girl to be jilted.", "“하지만 아가씨가 버림받는 건 아주 언짢은 일이잖아요."], ["All the world knows that she was engaged to him.\"", "그이와 약혼한 사이였다는 걸 온 세상이 다 아는데.”"]], [["\"And all the world knows--\"", "“그리고 온 세상이 아는 게 또—”"], ["But he would not proceed to declare that all the world knew also that Crosbie had been well thrashed for his baseness.", "하지만 그는 크로즈비가 그 비열함 때문에 흠씬 두들겨 맞았다는 것도 온 세상이 안다는 말까지 이어 가지는 않았다."], ["It would not become him to mention that even before his mother and sister.", "어머니와 누이 앞에서라도 그 이야기를 입에 올리는 것은 그에게 어울리는 일이 아니었다."], ["All the world did know it; all the world that cared to know anything of the matter;--except Lily Dale herself.", "온 세상이 그것을 알고 있었다. 그 일에 관해 무엇이든 알고 싶어 하는 온 세상이 말이다."], ["Nobody had ever yet told Lily Dale of that occurrence at the Paddington Railway Station, and it was well for John that her friends and his had been so discreet.", "릴리 데일 자신만 빼고. 패딩턴 철도역에서 있었던 그 일을 아직 아무도 릴리 데일에게 말하지 않았고, 그녀의 벗들과 그의 벗들이 그토록 입이 무거웠던 것은 존에게 다행한 일이었다."]], [["\"Oh, of course you are her champion,\"", "“아, 물론 오빠는 그 사람 편이지요.”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["\"And I didn't mean to say anything unkind.", "“나도 매정한 말을 하려던 건 아니에요."], ["Indeed I didn't.", "정말 아니에요."], ["Of course it was a misfortune.\"", "물론 불행한 일이었죠.”"]], [["\"I think it was the best piece of good fortune that could have happened to her, not to marry a d---- scoundrel like--\"", "“나는 그게 그 사람한테 일어날 수 있는 일 가운데 가장 큰 행운이었다고 생각해. 그런 빌—먹을 악당하고 결혼하지 않게 된 게—”"]], [["\"Oh, John!\"", "“어머, 존!”"], ["exclaimed Mrs. Eames.", "임스 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["\"I beg your pardon, mother.", "“죄송해요, 어머니."], ["But it isn't swearing to call such a man as that a d---- scoundrel.\"", "하지만 그런 인간을 빌—먹을 악당이라고 부르는 건 욕설이 아니에요.”"], ["And he particularly emphasized the naughty word, thinking that thereby he would add to its import, and take away from its naughtiness.", "그러고서 그는 그 고약한 낱말에 유난히 힘을 주었다. 그렇게 하면 뜻은 더 보태지고 고약함은 덜어지리라고 여긴 것이다."], ["\"But we won't talk any more about him.", "“하지만 그자 이야기는 더 하지 않겠어요."], ["I hate the man's very name.", "나는 그 인간 이름만 들어도 싫어요."], ["I hated him the first moment that I saw him, and knew that he was a blackguard from his look.", "처음 본 순간부터 싫었고, 생김새만 보고도 불한당인 줄 알았어요."], ["And I don't believe a word about the squire having been cross to them.", "그리고 지주 어른이 그들에게 심술궂게 굴었다는 것도 한마디도 못 믿겠어요."], ["Indeed I know he has been the reverse of cross.", "오히려 심술궂기는커녕 그 반대였다는 걸 나는 알아요."], ["So Bell is going to marry Dr. Crofts!\"", "그래, 벨이 크로프츠 선생과 결혼한다고!”"]], [["\"There is no doubt on earth about that,\"", "“그건 세상에 의심할 여지가 없어요.”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["\"And they say that Bernard Dale is going abroad with his regiment.\"", "“그리고 사람들 말로는 버나드 데일이 연대를 따라 외국으로 간대요.”"]], [["Then John discussed with his mother his duties as private secretary, and his intention of leaving Mrs.", "그러고 나서 존은 어머니와 함께 개인 비서로서의 제 직무며 로퍼 부인 집에서 나올 작정이라는 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Roper's house. \"I suppose it isn't nice enough for you now, John,\"", "“이제 그 집이 너한테는 그리 점잖지 못한 게로구나, 존.”"], ["said his mother.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["\"It never was very nice, mother, to tell you the truth.", "“사실을 말씀드리자면 애초부터 그리 점잖은 집이었던 적이 없어요, 어머니."], ["There were people there--.", "거기 있던 사람들이—."], ["But you mustn't think I am turning up my nose because I'm getting grand.", "하지만 제가 훌륭해졌다고 콧대를 세우는 거라고 여기지는 마세요."], ["I don't want to live any better than we all lived at Mrs.", "저는 우리가 다 함께 로퍼 부인 댁에서 지내던 것보다 더 잘 살고 싶은 게 아니에요."], ["Roper's; but she took in persons that were not agreeable.", "다만 그분이 달갑지 않은 사람들을 들여놓았거든요."], ["There is a Mr. and Mrs.", "거기 루펙스 씨 부부라는 이들이 있어요.”"], ["Lupex there.\" Then he described something of their life in Burton Crescent, but did not say much about Amelia Roper.", "그러고서 그는 버턴 크레슨트에서의 생활을 얼마간 이야기했으나, 어밀리아 로퍼에 대해서는 별로 말하지 않았다."], ["Amelia Roper had not made her appearance in Guestwick, as he had once feared that she would do; and therefore it did not need that he should at present make known to his mother that episode in his life.", "어밀리아 로퍼는 한때 그가 두려워했던 것과 달리 게스트윅에 나타나지 않았다. 그러니 제 생애의 그 삽화를 지금 어머니에게 알려야 할 까닭도 없었다."]], [["When he got back to the Manor House he found that Mr.", "저택으로 돌아와 보니 데일 씨와 그 조카딸이 와 있었다."], ["Dale and his niece had arrived. They were both sitting with Lady Julia when he went into the morning room, and Lord De Guest was standing over the fire talking to them.", "그가 아침 거실로 들어갔을 때 두 사람은 레이디 줄리아와 함께 앉아 있었고, 드 게스트 경은 난롯가에 서서 그들과 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Eames as he came among them felt terribly conscious of his position, as though all there were aware that he had been brought down from London on purpose to make a declaration of love;--as, indeed, all of them were aware of that fact.", "임스는 그들 틈에 끼어들면서 제 처지가 몹시도 의식되었다. 마치 거기 있는 사람들 모두가 그가 사랑을 고백하려고 일부러 런던에서 내려왔다는 것을 알고 있는 듯했다."], ["Bell, though no one had told her so in direct words, was as sure of it as the others.", "사실 그들은 모두 그 사실을 알고 있었다. 벨도 아무한테서 대놓고 그런 말을 들은 적은 없었으나 다른 사람들만큼이나 그것을 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["\"Here comes the prince of matadores,\"", "“투우사들의 왕자가 오시는군.”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"No, my lord; you're the prince.", "“아닙니다, 백작님."], ["I'm only your first follower.\"", "왕자는 백작님이시죠."], ["Though he could contrive that his words should be gay, his looks were sheepish, and when he gave his hand to the squire it was only by a struggle that he could bring himself to look straight into the old man's face.", "저는 첫 번째 수행자일 뿐입니다.” 말은 짐짓 명랑하게 꾸며 낼 수 있었지만 낯빛은 겸연쩍었고, 지주에게 손을 내밀 때는 애를 써야만 겨우 노인의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라볼 수 있었다."]], [["\"I'm very glad to see you, John,\"", "“자네를 보니 아주 반갑네, 존.”"], ["said the squire, \"very glad indeed.\"", "지주가 말했다. “정말이지 아주 반가워.”"]], [["\"And so am I,\"", "“저도요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."], ["\"I have been so happy to hear that you have been promoted at your office, and so is mamma.\"", "“사무국에서 승진하셨다는 소식을 듣고 얼마나 기뻤는지 몰라요. 엄마도 그러셨고요.”"]], [["\"I hope Mrs. Dale is quite well,\"", "“데일 부인께서 평안하시기를 바랍니다.”"], ["said he;--\"and Lily.\"", "그가 말했다. “그리고 릴리도요.”"], ["The word had been pronounced, but it had been done with so manifest an effort that all in the room were conscious of it, and paused as Bell prepared her little answer.", "그 이름이 입 밖에 나오기는 했으나 너무나 뚜렷한 애를 쓴 끝에 나온 것이어서 방 안의 모두가 그것을 알아차렸고, 벨이 짤막한 대답을 준비하는 동안 모두 말을 멈추었다."]], [["\"My sister has been very ill, you know,--with scarlatina.", "“제 동생이 몹시 앓았어요, 아시겠지만. 성홍열로요."], ["But she has recovered with wonderful quickness, and is nearly well again now.", "하지만 놀랄 만큼 빨리 나아서 이제 거의 다 회복됐어요."], ["She will be so glad to see you if you will go over.\"", "건너오시면 동생이 무척 반가워할 거예요.”"]], [["\"Yes; I shall certainly go over,\"", "“네, 꼭 건너가겠습니다.”"], ["said John.", "존이 말했다."]], [["\"And now shall I show you your room, Miss Dale?\"", "“그럼 이제 방을 안내해 드릴까요, 데일 양?”"], ["said Lady Julia.", "레이디 줄리아가 말했다."], ["And so the party was broken up, and the ice had been broken.", "그리하여 자리는 파했고, 어색함의 얼음도 깨졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER LIII. LOQUITUR HOPKINS.", "# 제53장. 「홉킨스 가로되」"]], [["The squire had been told that his niece Bell had accepted Dr. Crofts, and he had signified a sort of acquiescence in the arrangement, saying that if it were to be so, he had nothing to say against Dr.", "지주는 조카딸 벨이 크로프츠 선생을 받아들였다는 이야기를 들었고, 그 일에 일종의 묵인 같은 것을 내비치며, 정 그렇게 될 일이라면 크로프츠 선생에게 반대할 말은 없다고 하였다."], ["Crofts. He spoke this in a melancholy tone of voice, wearing on his face that look of subdued sorrow which was now almost habitual to him.", "그는 이 말을 우울한 목소리로 했는데, 얼굴에는 이제 거의 몸에 밴 그 눌린 슬픔의 빛이 어려 있었다."], ["It was to Mrs. Dale that he spoke on the subject.", "그가 이 이야기를 꺼낸 상대는 데일 부인이었다."], ["\"I could have wished that it might have been otherwise,\"", "“달리 되었더라면 좋았으리라는 마음이야 나에게도 있었소.” 그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"as you are well aware.", "“제수씨도 잘 알다시피 말이오."], ["I had family reasons for wishing that it might be otherwise.", "나에게는 달리 되기를 바랄 만한 집안의 사정이 있었소."], ["But I have nothing to say against it.", "하지만 그 일에 반대할 말은 없소."], ["Dr. Crofts, as her husband, shall be welcome to my house.\"", "크로프츠 선생이 그 아이의 남편이 된다면 내 집에 반갑게 맞아들이리다.”"], ["Mrs. Dale, who had expected much worse than this, began to thank him for his kindness, and to say that she also would have preferred to see her daughter married to her cousin.", "이보다 훨씬 나쁜 말을 각오하고 있던 데일 부인은 그의 너그러움에 고마워하기 시작했고, 자기도 딸이 사촌과 혼인하는 편을 보고 싶었노라고 말했다."], ["\"But in such a matter the decision should be left entirely to the girl.", "“하지만 그런 일에서는 결정을 온전히 그 아이한테 맡겨야 하지 않겠어요."], ["Don't you think so?\"", "시아주버니도 그렇게 생각하시죠?”"]], [["\"I have not a word to say against her,\"", "“나는 그 아이한테 반대할 말이 한마디도 없소.”"], ["he repeated.", "그가 되풀이했다."], ["Then Mrs. Dale left him, and told her daughter that her uncle's manner of receiving the news had been, for him, very gracious.", "그러고 나서 데일 부인은 그의 곁을 떠나, 삼촌이 그 소식을 받아들인 태도가 그 어른치고는 아주 다정했다고 딸에게 일러 주었다."], ["\"You were his favourite, but Lily will be so now,\"", "“네가 그 어른이 가장 아끼는 아이였는데, 이제는 릴리가 그렇게 되겠구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I don't care a bit about that;--or, rather, I do care, and think it will be in every way better.", "“저는 그런 건 조금도 상관없어요. 아니, 상관은 있지요."], ["But as I, who am the naughty one, will go away, and as Lily, who is the good one, will remain with you, doesn't it almost seem a pity that you should be leaving the house?\"", "그리고 여러모로 그편이 더 나을 거라고 생각해요. 그런데 못된 쪽인 제가 떠나가고 착한 쪽인 릴리가 엄마와 함께 남을 텐데, 엄마가 이 집을 떠나신다는 게 어쩐지 안타깝지 않아요?”"]], [["Mrs. Dale thought it was almost a pity, but she could not say so now.", "데일 부인도 그것이 거의 안타까운 일이라고 생각했으나, 이제 와서 그렇게 말할 수는 없었다."], ["\"You think Lily will remain,\"", "“너는 릴리가 남으리라고 보는구나.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, mamma; I feel sure she will.\"", "“네, 엄마. 저는 릴리가 남을 거라고 확신해요.”"]], [["\"She was always very fond of John Eames;--and he is doing so well.\"", "“그 애는 늘 존 임스를 무척 좋아했지. 게다가 그 사람은 아주 잘되고 있고.”"]], [["\"It will be of no use, mamma.", "“소용없을 거예요, 엄마."], ["She is fond of him,--very fond.", "릴리는 그 사람을 좋아해요."], ["In a sort of a way she loves him--so well, that I feel sure she never mentions his name without some inward reference to her old childish thoughts and fancies.", "아주 많이 좋아하죠. 어떤 식으로는 그를 사랑하기까지 해서, 저는 릴리가 그 이름을 입에 올릴 때마다 반드시 속으로는 어릴 적의 생각과 공상을 함께 떠올리리라고 믿어요."], ["If he had come before Mr. Crosbie it would have all been well with her.", "크로즈비 씨보다 먼저 그가 왔더라면 릴리에게는 다 잘되었을 텐데."], ["But she cannot do it now.", "하지만 이제는 그럴 수가 없어요."], ["Her pride would prevent her, even if her heart permitted it.", "마음이 허락한다 해도 자존심이 릴리를 막을 거예요. 아이, 참."], ["Oh! dear; it's very wrong of me to say so, after all that I have said before; but I almost wish you were not going.", "제가 여태 해 온 말이 있는데 이런 말을 하다니 저도 아주 못됐지요."], ["Uncle Christopher seems to be less hard than he used to be; and as I was the sinner, and as I am disposed of--\"", "그래도 저는 엄마가 떠나지 않으셨으면 하는 마음이 거의 들어요. 크리스토퍼 삼촌은 예전만큼 모질지 않으신 것 같고, 잘못한 쪽은 저였는데 저는 이렇게 정리가 되었으니—”"]], [["\"It is too late now, my dear.\"", "“이제는 너무 늦었단다, 얘야.”"]], [["\"And we should neither of us have the courage to mention it to Lily,\"", "“그리고 우리 둘 다 릴리한테 그 얘기를 꺼낼 용기는 없을 거고요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["On the following morning the squire sent for his sister-in-law, as it was his wont to do when necessity came for any discussion on matters of business.", "이튿날 아침 지주는 제수를 불러오게 했다. 사업상의 일로 의논할 필요가 생기면 늘 그렇게 하는 것이 그의 버릇이었다."], ["This was perfectly understood between them, and such sending was not taken as indicating any lack of courtesy on the part of Mr.", "이 점은 두 사람 사이에 완전히 이해되어 있었으므로, 그렇게 사람을 보내는 것이 데일 씨 쪽에 예의가 없다는 뜻으로 받아들여지지는 않았다."], ["Dale. \"Mary,\"", "“제수씨.”"], ["he said, as soon as Mrs.", "데일 부인이 자리에 앉자마자 그가 말했다."], ["Dale was seated, \"I shall do for Bell exactly what I have proposed to do for Lily.", "“릴리에게 해 주겠다고 한 것을 벨에게도 꼭 그대로 해 주겠소."], ["I had intended more than that once, of course.", "물론 한때는 그보다 더 많은 것을 마음에 두고 있었소."], ["But then it would all have gone into Bernard's pocket; as it is, I shall make no difference between them.", "하지만 그랬더라면 그게 다 버나드의 주머니로 들어갔을 테지. 사정이 이러하니 두 아이 사이에 차별을 두지 않겠소."], ["They shall each have a hundred a year,--that is, when they marry.", "저마다 연 백 파운드씩 갖게 될 거요. 그러니까 혼인할 때 말이오."], ["You had better tell Crofts to speak to me.\"", "크로프츠에게 나를 찾아와 이야기하라고 일러 두는 게 좋겠소.”"]], [["\"Mr. Dale, he doesn't expect it.", "“시아주버니, 그이는 그런 걸 바라지 않아요."], ["He does not expect a penny.\"", "한 푼도 바라지 않는걸요.”"]], [["\"So much the better for him; and, indeed, so much the better for her.", "“그렇다면 그에게는 더 잘된 일이고, 실은 그 아이에게도 더 잘된 일이오."], ["He won't make her the less welcome to his home because she brings some assistance to it.\"", "그 아이가 살림에 보탬이 될 것을 가지고 온다고 해서 그가 제 집에 덜 반갑게 맞아들이지는 않을 테니까.”"]], [["\"We have never thought of it,--any of us.", "“저희는 그런 건 생각해 본 적도 없어요."], ["The offer has come so suddenly that I don't know what I ought to say.\"", "아무도요. 말씀이 하도 갑작스러워서 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["\"Say--nothing.", "“아무 말도 마시오."], ["If you choose to make me a return for it--; but I am only doing what I conceive to be my duty, and have no right to ask for a kindness in return.\"", "제수씨가 그 보답으로 무언가 해 주고 싶다면— 하지만 나는 그저 내 도리라고 여기는 바를 하는 것뿐이니, 보답으로 친절을 청할 권리는 없소.”"]], [["\"But what kindness can we show you, Mr. Dale?\"", "“하지만 저희가 시아주버니께 무슨 친절을 베풀 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["\"Remain in that house.\"", "“그 집에 그대로 있으시오.”"], ["In saying these last words he spoke as though he were again angry,--as though he were again laying down the law to them,--as though he were telling her of a duty which was due to him and incumbent on her.", "이 마지막 말을 하면서 그는 다시 성이 난 사람처럼 말했다. 마치 다시 그들에게 법을 내려놓는 사람처럼, 마치 자기에게 마땅히 돌아와야 하고 그녀가 마땅히 져야 할 의무를 일러 주는 사람처럼 말했다."], ["His voice was as stern and his face as acid as ever.", "그의 목소리는 여느 때처럼 엄했고 얼굴은 여느 때처럼 시었다."], ["He said that he was asking for a kindness; but surely no man ever asked for kindness in a voice so peremptory.", "그는 친절을 청하는 것이라고 말했으나, 그토록 위압적인 목소리로 친절을 청한 사람은 세상에 분명 아무도 없었을 것이다."], ["\"Remain in that house.\"", "“그 집에 그대로 있으시오.”"], ["Then he turned himself in towards his table as though he had no more to say.", "그러고 나서 그는 더 할 말이 없다는 듯 제 탁자 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["But Mrs. Dale was beginning, now at last, to understand something of his mind and real character.", "그러나 데일 부인은 이제야 마침내 그의 마음과 참된 성품을 얼마쯤 이해하기 시작하고 있었다."], ["He could be affectionate and forbearing in his giving; but when asking, he could not be otherwise than stern.", "그는 무언가를 줄 때에는 다정하고 너그러울 수 있었으나, 청할 때에는 엄하지 않고서는 달리 될 수 없는 사람이었다."], ["Indeed, he could not ask; he could only demand.", "아니, 그는 청할 줄을 몰랐다. 그는 요구할 줄밖에 몰랐다."]], [["\"We have done so much now,\" Mrs.", "“저희는 이미 일을 너무 많이 벌여 놓았어요.”"], ["Dale began to plead.", "데일 부인이 사정하기 시작했다."]], [["\"Well, well, well.", "“됐소, 됐소, 됐소."], ["I did not mean to speak about that.", "그 이야기를 하려던 것은 아니오."], ["Things are unpacked easier than they are packed.", "짐이란 싸기보다 푸는 쪽이 쉬운 법이지."], ["But, however-- Never mind.", "하지만, 어쨌든— 아무것도 아니오."], ["Bell is to go with me this afternoon to Guestwick Manor.", "벨은 오늘 오후 나와 함께 게스트윅 저택으로 갈 거요."], ["Let her be up here at two.", "두 시까지 이리로 올려 보내시오."], ["Grimes can bring her box round, I suppose.\"", "트렁크는 그라임스가 돌아서 실어다 줄 수 있을 테지.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes: of course.\"", "“아, 네. 물론이죠.”"]], [["\"And don't be talking to her about money before she starts.", "“그리고 그 아이가 떠나기 전에 돈 이야기는 하지 마시오."], ["I had rather you didn't;--you understand.", "나는 그러지 않았으면 좋겠소. 무슨 말인지 알 거요."], ["But when you see Crofts, tell him to come to me.", "하지만 크로프츠를 만나거든 나에게 오라고 이르시오."], ["Indeed, he'd better come at once, if this thing is to go on quickly.\"", "실은 이 일을 빨리 진행할 요량이면 당장 오는 편이 낫겠소.”"]], [["It may easily be understood that Mrs. Dale would disobey the injunctions contained in the squire's last words.", "데일 부인이 지주의 마지막 말에 담긴 당부를 어기리라는 것은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다."], ["It was quite out of the question that she should return to her daughters and not tell them the result of her morning's interview with their uncle.", "딸들에게 돌아가서 그날 아침 삼촌과 나눈 이야기의 결말을 알려 주지 않는다는 것은 아예 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["A hundred a year in the doctor's modest household would make all the difference between plenty and want, between modest plenty and endurable want.", "연 백 파운드는 의사의 수수한 살림에서 넉넉함과 궁핍함을, 수수한 넉넉함과 견딜 만한 궁핍함을 가르는 차이를 통째로 만들어 낼 것이었다."], ["Of course she told them, giving Bell to understand that she must dissemble so far as to pretend ignorance of the affair.", "물론 그녀는 딸들에게 이야기했고, 벨에게는 그 일을 모르는 척 시치미를 떼야 한다고 일러 두었다."]], [["\"I shall thank him at once,\"", "“저는 당장 고맙다고 말씀드리겠어요.”"], ["said Bell; \"and tell him that I did not at all expect it, but am not too proud to accept it.\"", "벨이 말했다. “그리고 전혀 기대하지 않았지만 받기를 마다할 만큼 자존심이 세지도 않다고 말씀드릴래요.”"]], [["\"Pray don't, my dear; not just now.", "“제발 그러지 말아라, 얘야. 지금은 아니야."], ["I am breaking a sort of promise in telling you at all,--only I could not keep it to myself.", "너희에게 말한 것만으로도 나는 일종의 약속을 어기는 셈이란다. 다만 나 혼자 담아 두고 있을 수가 없었을 뿐이야."], ["And he has so many things to worry him!", "게다가 그 어른은 마음을 어지럽히는 일이 어찌나 많은지!"], ["Though he says nothing about it now, he has half broken his heart about you and Bernard.\" Then, too, Mrs.", "지금은 그 일을 입에 담지 않으시지만, 너와 버나드 일로 마음이 반쯤 무너지셨단다.”"], ["Dale told the girls what request the squire had just made, and the manner in which he had made it.", "그러고 나서 데일 부인은 지주가 방금 어떤 부탁을 했는지, 또 그것을 어떤 태도로 했는지 딸들에게 이야기했다."], ["\"The tone of his voice as he spoke brought tears into my eyes.", "“그렇게 말씀하시는 목소리에 내 눈에는 눈물이 고였단다."], ["I almost wish we had not done anything.\"", "우리가 아무 일도 벌이지 않았더라면 좋았을걸 하는 마음이 들 지경이야.”"]], [["\"But, mamma,\"", "“하지만 엄마.”"], ["said Lily, \"what difference can it make to him?", "릴리가 말했다. “그게 그 어른께 무슨 차이가 있어요?"], ["You know that our presence near him was always a trouble to him.", "우리가 곁에 있는 게 그 어른께는 늘 성가신 일이었다는 걸 엄마도 아시잖아요."], ["He never really wanted us.", "그 어른은 정말로 우리를 원한 적이 없어요."], ["He liked to have Bell there when he thought that Bell would marry his pet.\"", "벨 언니가 당신이 아끼는 사람과 혼인하리라고 여기실 때에나 언니가 거기 있는 걸 좋아하셨죠.”"]], [["\"Don't be unkind, Lily.\"", "“못되게 굴지 마라, 릴리.”"]], [["\"I don't mean to be unkind.", "“못되게 굴려는 건 아니에요."], ["Why shouldn't Bernard be his pet?", "버나드가 그 어른이 아끼는 사람이면 안 될 게 뭐 있어요?"], ["I love Bernard dearly, and always thought it the best point in uncle Christopher that he was so fond of him.", "저도 버나드를 무척 사랑하고, 크리스토퍼 삼촌이 버나드를 그토록 아끼신다는 게 그 어른의 가장 좋은 점이라고 늘 생각했는걸요."], ["I knew, you know, that it was no use.", "엄마도 아시다시피 저는 그게 소용없는 일이라는 걸 알고 있었어요."], ["Of course I knew it, as I understood all about--somebody else.", "물론 알고 있었죠. 저는 어떤 다른 사람에 관한 일이라면 전부 알고 있었으니까요."], ["But Bernard is his pet.\"", "그렇지만 버나드는 그 어른이 아끼는 사람이에요.”"]], [["\"He's fond of you all, in his own way,\"", "“그 어른은 당신 나름의 방식으로 너희를 다 사랑하신단다.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"But is he fond of you?--that's the question,\"", "“하지만 그 어른이 엄마를 사랑하시나요? 그게 문제죠.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"We could have forgiven him anything done to us, and have put up with any words he might have spoken to us, because he regards us as children.", "“우리한테 하신 일이라면 무엇이든 용서할 수 있었고, 우리한테 하신 말씀이라면 어떤 말이든 참아 넘길 수 있었어요. 그 어른은 우리를 아이로 여기시니까요."], ["His giving a hundred a year to Bell won't make you comfortable in this house if he still domineers over you.", "벨 언니에게 연 백 파운드를 주신다고 해서, 여전히 엄마 위에 군림하신다면 엄마가 이 집에서 편안해지지는 않을 거예요."], ["If a neighbour be neighbourly, near neighbourhood is very nice.", "이웃이 이웃답게 굴면 가까운 이웃살이는 아주 좋은 것이지요."], ["But uncle Christopher has not been neighbourly.", "그렇지만 크리스토퍼 삼촌은 이웃답지 않으셨어요."], ["He has wanted to be more than an uncle to us, on condition that he might be less than a brother to you.", "그 어른은 엄마에게 시숙만도 못한 사람이 되는 조건으로 우리에게는 삼촌 이상이 되고자 하셨어요."], ["Bell and I have always felt that his regard on such terms was not worth having.\"", "언니와 저는 그런 조건으로 얻는 애정이라면 가질 값어치가 없다고 늘 여겨 왔어요.”"]], [["\"I almost feel that we have been wrong,\" said Mrs.", "“우리가 잘못한 것 같다는 생각이 거의 드는구나.”"], ["Dale; \"but in truth I never thought that the matter would be to him one of so much moment.\"", "데일 부인이 말했다. “하지만 정말이지 나는 그 일이 그 어른께 그토록 큰일이 되리라고는 한 번도 생각지 못했단다.”"]], [["When Bell had gone, Mrs. Dale and Lily were not disposed to continue with much energy the occupation on which they had all been employed for some days past.", "벨이 떠나고 나자 데일 부인과 릴리는 며칠 전부터 셋이 매달려 온 그 일을 그리 힘차게 이어 갈 마음이 나지 않았다."], ["There had been life and excitement in the work when they had first commenced their packing, but now it was grown wearisome, dull, and distasteful.", "처음 짐을 싸기 시작했을 때에는 그 일에 생기와 설렘이 있었으나, 이제는 그것이 지겹고 따분하고 넌더리 나는 일이 되어 있었다."], ["Indeed so much of it was done that but little was left to employ them, except those final strappings and fastenings, and that last collection of odds and ends which could not be accomplished till they were absolutely on the point of starting.", "실은 이미 어지간히 해 놓은 터라 그들이 매달릴 일도 얼마 남아 있지 않았다. 마지막으로 끈을 조이고 잠금쇠를 채우는 일, 그리고 정말로 떠나기 직전이 되어야 마무리할 수 있는 자질구레한 물건들을 마지막으로 그러모으는 일 말고는 없었다."], ["The squire had said that unpacking would be easier than packing, and Mrs.", "지주는 짐을 푸는 편이 싸는 편보다 쉬울 거라고 말했었다."], ["Dale, as she wandered about among the hampers and cases, began to consider whether the task of restoring all the things to their old places would be very disagreeable.", "그리하여 데일 부인은 광주리와 궤짝 사이를 거닐면서, 그 모든 물건을 제자리에 도로 갖다 놓는 일이 과연 그토록 고약할까 하고 헤아려 보기 시작했다."], ["She said nothing of this to Lily, and Lily herself, whatever might be her thoughts, made no such suggestion to her mother.", "그녀는 이런 생각을 릴리에게는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 릴리 또한 속으로 무슨 생각을 하고 있었든 어머니에게 그런 말을 꺼내지 않았다."]], [["\"I think Hopkins will miss us more than any one else,\"", "“홉킨스가 누구보다도 우리를 더 아쉬워할 것 같아요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Hopkins will have no one to scold.\"", "“홉킨스는 잔소리할 상대가 없어질 테니까요.”"]], [["Just at that moment Hopkins appeared at the parlour window, and signified his desire for a conference.", "바로 그때 홉킨스가 응접실 창가에 나타나 이야기를 나누고 싶다는 뜻을 내비쳤다."]], [["\"You must come round,\"", "“돌아서 들어오세요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"It's too cold for the window to be opened.", "“창을 열어 두기에는 너무 추워요. 저는 늘 그이를 집 안으로 들이는 게 좋아요."], ["I always like to get him into the house, because he feels himself a little abashed by the chairs and tables; or, perhaps, it is the carpet that is too much for him.", "의자며 탁자 앞에서는 좀 기가 죽거든요. 어쩌면 그이한테 벅찬 건 융단인지도 모르죠."], ["Out on the gravel-walks he is such a terrible tyrant, and in the greenhouse he almost tramples upon one!\"", "바깥 자갈길에서는 어찌나 무서운 폭군인지 몰라요. 온실에서는 사람을 아예 짓밟다시피 한다니까요!”"]], [["Hopkins, when he did appear at the parlour door, seemed by his manner to justify Lily's discretion.", "홉킨스가 응접실 문께에 나타났을 때, 그의 태도는 릴리의 분별이 옳았음을 증명해 주는 듯했다."], ["He was not at all masterful in his tone or bearing, and seemed to pay to the chairs and tables all the deference which they could have expected.", "말투에도 몸가짐에도 위세 부리는 데가 조금도 없었고, 의자와 탁자가 기대할 만한 온갖 경의를 다 표하는 듯이 보였다."]], [["\"So you be going in earnest, ma'am,\"", "“그러면 정말로 가시는 겁니까요, 마님.”"], ["he said, looking down at Mrs. Dale's feet.", "그가 데일 부인의 발치를 내려다보며 말했다."]], [["As Mrs.", "데일 부인이 곧바로 대답하지 않자 릴리가 말했다."], ["Dale did not answer him at once, Lily spoke:--\"Yes, Hopkins, we are going in a very few days, now.", "“그래요, 홉킨스. 이제 며칠 안 남았어요."], ["We shall see you sometimes, I hope, over at Guestwick.\"", "게스트윅에서 가끔은 볼 수 있겠지요.”"]], [["\"Humph!\"", "“흠!” 홉킨스가 말했다."], ["said Hopkins.", "“그러면 정말로 가시는군입쇼!"], ["\"So you be really going!", "저는 일이 이 지경까지 오리라고는 생각도 못 했습니다요, 아가씨. 정말이지 못 했습니다요."], ["I didn't think it'd ever come to that, miss; I didn't indeed,--and no more it oughtn't; but of course it isn't for me to speak.\"", "그리고 이렇게 되어서도 안 되는 일이고요. 하기야 물론 제가 나설 자리는 아닙지요.”"]], [["\"People must change their residence sometimes, you know,\"", "“사람이란 때로 사는 곳을 옮기기도 하는 법이잖아요, 홉킨스.”"], ["said Mrs.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["Dale, using the same argument by which Eames had endeavoured to excuse his departure to Mrs. Roper.", "임스가 로퍼 부인에게 제가 떠나는 것을 변명하려 들 때 썼던 것과 똑같은 논거였다."]], [["\"Well, ma'am; it ain't for me to say anything.", "“예, 마님. 제가 무슨 말을 할 처지는 아닙지요."], ["But this I will say, I've lived here about t'", "하지만 이 말씀만은 드리겠습니다요."], ["squire's place, man and boy, jist all my life, seeing I was born here, as you knows, Mrs.", "저는 여기 지주 어른 댁 언저리에서 사내로도 아이로도, 그러니까 한평생을 살아왔습니다요."], ["Dale; and of all the bad things I ever see come about the place, this is a sight the worst.\"", "여기서 태어났으니까요, 데일 부인도 아시다시피 말입니다요. 그런데 제가 이곳에서 보아 온 온갖 나쁜 일 가운데 이번 일이 단연 제일 고약합니다요.”"]], [["\"Oh, Hopkins!\"", "“아이, 홉킨스!”"]], [["\"The worst of all, ma'am; the worst of all!", "“무엇보다 고약합니다요, 마님. 무엇보다 고약합지요!"], ["It'll just kill t' squire!", "이 일이 지주 어른을 죽이고 말 겁니다요!"], ["There's ne'ery doubt in the world about that.", "그건 세상에 의심할 여지가 없습니다요."], ["It'll be the very death of t' old man.\"", "이 일이 그 노인의 명줄을 끊고 말 겁니다요.”"]], [["\"That's nonsense, Hopkins,\"", "“말도 안 되는 소리예요, 홉킨스.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Very well, miss.", "“예, 아가씨."], ["I don't say but what it is nonsense; only you'll see.", "말도 안 되는 소리가 아니라고는 하지 않겠습니다요. 다만 두고 보시라는 겁니다요."], ["There's Mr.", "버나드 도련님도 떠나 버리셨지요."], ["Bernard,--he's gone away; and by all accounts he never did care very much for the place.", "그리고 다들 그러는데 그분은 이곳을 그리 아끼신 적이 한 번도 없다더군입쇼. 인도로 가신다고들 합니다요."], ["They all say he's a-going to the Hingies.", "게다가 벨 아가씨는 시집을 가시게 되었고요."], ["And Miss Bell is going to be married,--which is all proper, in course; why shouldn't she?", "그야 물론 다 마땅한 일입지요. 안 될 게 뭐 있겠습니까요?"], ["And why shouldn't you, too, Miss Lily?\"", "그러면 릴리 아가씨도 안 될 게 뭐 있겠습니까요?”"]], [["\"Perhaps I shall, some day, Hopkins.\"", "“어쩌면 언젠가는 그렇게 되겠지요, 홉킨스.”"]], [["\"There's no day like the present, Miss Lily.", "“오늘만 한 날이 어디 있겠습니까요, 릴리 아가씨. 그리고 저는 이 말씀을 드리겠습니다요."], ["And I do say this, that the man as pitched into him would be the man for my money.\"", "그자를 두들겨 패 준 사내라면 제 마음에 꼭 드는 사람일 겁니다요.” 홉킨스가 순간의 격정에 못 이겨 내뱉은 이 말을 릴리는 도무지 알아들을 수 없었고, 그 말을 들으며 몸을 떤 데일 부인은 설명을 청하는 말을 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["This, which Hopkins spoke in the excitement of the moment, was perfectly unintelligible to Lily, and Mrs.", "“하지만.” 홉킨스가 말을 이었다."], ["Dale, who shuddered as she heard him, said not a word to call for any explanation.", "“그야 될 대로 될 일입지요, 릴리 아가씨. 그리고 아가씨는 섭리의 손안에 계시고요."], ["\"But,\" continued Hopkins, \"that's all as it may be, Miss Lily, and you be in the hands of Providence,--as is others.\"", "남들도 다 그렇듯이 말입니다요.”"]], [["\"Exactly so, Hopkins.\"", "“그렇고말고요, 홉킨스.”"]], [["\"But why should your mamma be all for going away?", "“그렇지만 마님께서는 어째서 그렇게 떠나려고만 하십니까요?"], ["She ain't going to marry no one.", "마님이야 시집가실 것도 아니지 않습니까요."], ["Here's the house, and there's she, and there's t' squire; and why should she be for going away?", "여기 집이 있고, 마님이 계시고, 지주 어른이 계신데, 어째서 떠나려 하셔야 합니까요?"], ["So much going away all at once can't be for any good.", "이렇게 한꺼번에 우수수 떠나 버리는 게 좋은 일일 리가 없습니다요."], ["It's just a breaking up of everything, as though nothing wasn't good enough for nobody.", "그저 모든 걸 다 부수어 흩어 놓는 짓입지요. 마치 아무한테도 무엇 하나 성에 차지 않는다는 듯이 말입니다요."], ["I never went away, and I can't abide it.\"", "저는 한 번도 떠나 본 적이 없고, 그런 건 참아 낼 수가 없습니다요.”"]], [["\"Well, Hopkins; it's settled now,\"", "“저기, 홉킨스. 이제 다 정해진 일이에요.”"], ["said Mrs.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["Dale, \"and I'm afraid it can't be unsettled.\"", "“그리고 안타깝지만 되돌릴 수는 없어요.”"]], [["\"Settled;--well.", "“정해졌다굽쇼."], ["Tell me this: do you expect, Mrs.", "그렇다면 이걸 말씀해 보십쇼."], ["Dale, that he's to live there all alone by hisself without any one to say a cross word to,--unless it be me or Dingles; for Jolliffe's worse than nobody, he's so mortial cross hisself.", "데일 부인, 그 어른이 거기서 혼자 외따로, 모진 말 한마디 건넬 사람도 없이 지내시리라고 생각하십니까요? 저나 딩글스 말고는 말입니다요."], ["Of course he can't stand it.", "졸리프는 아무도 없느니만 못한 자입지요."], ["If you goes away, Mrs.", "워낙 저부터가 지독하게 모진 사람이니까요."], ["Dale, Mister Bernard, he'll be squire in less than twelve months.", "그 어른이 그걸 견뎌 내실 리가 없습니다요. 마님이 떠나시면, 데일 부인, 버나드 도련님이 열두 달도 안 되어 지주가 되실 겁니다요."], ["He'll come back from the Hingies, then, I suppose?\"", "그러면 그분은 인도에서 돌아오시겠지요, 아마?”"]], [["\"I don't think my brother-in-law will take it in that way, Hopkins.\"", "“나는 내 시아주버니가 그런 식으로 받아들이시리라고는 생각하지 않아요, 홉킨스.”"]], [["\"Ah, ma'am, you don't know him,--not as I knows him;--all the ins and outs and crinks and crannies of him.", "“아이고, 마님, 마님은 그 어른을 모르십니다요. 제가 아는 만큼은 모르시지요."], ["I knows him as I does the old apple-trees that I've been a-handling for forty year.", "그 어른의 안팎이며 구석구석 주름살까지 말입니다요. 저는 그 어른을 마흔 해 동안 손대며 길러 온 저 늙은 사과나무들만큼이나 잘 압니다요."], ["There's a deal of bad wood about them old cankered trees, and some folk say they ain't worth the ground they stand on; but I know where the sap runs, and when the fruit-blossom shows itself I know where the fruit will be the sweetest.", "저 늙고 옹이 진 나무들에는 썩은 가지가 수두룩해서, 어떤 사람들은 저것들이 서 있는 땅값어치도 못 한다고 합니다요. 하지만 저는 어디로 수액이 흐르는지 알고, 꽃이 피어나면 어디에 열매가 제일 달게 달릴지도 압니다요."], ["It don't take much to kill one of them old trees,--but there's life in 'm yet if they be well handled.\"", "저 늙은 나무 하나 죽이는 데는 대단한 것도 필요 없습니다요. 그렇지만 잘만 다루면 아직 그 안에 목숨이 남아 있습지요.”"]], [["\"I'm sure I hope my brother's life may be long spared to him,\"", "“나야 시아주버니의 목숨이 오래도록 지켜지기를 바라지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Then don't be taking yourself away, ma'am, into them gashly lodgings at Guestwick.", "“그러면 떠나지 마십쇼, 마님. 게스트윅의 그 끔찍한 셋집으로는 말입니다요."], ["I says they are gashly for the likes of a Dale.", "데일 집안 같은 분들한테는 그런 데가 끔찍하다는 말씀입니다요."], ["It is not for me to speak, ma'am, of course.", "물론 제가 나설 자리는 아닙지요, 마님."], ["And I only came up now just to know what things you'd like with you out of the greenhouse.\"", "그리고 저는 그저 온실에서 무엇을 가져가고 싶으신지 여쭈러 올라온 것뿐입니다요.”"]], [["\"Oh, nothing, Hopkins, thank you,\"", "“아, 아무것도 필요 없어요, 홉킨스. 고마워요.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"He told me to put up for you the best I could pick, and I means to do it;\"", "“그 어른께서 저더러 마님을 위해 제일 좋은 것으로 골라 담아 두라고 하셨습니다요."], ["and Hopkins, as he spoke, indicated by a motion of his head that he was making reference to the squire.", "그리고 저는 그렇게 할 작정입니다요.” 그러면서 홉킨스는 자기가 지주를 두고 하는 말임을 고갯짓으로 알렸다."]], [["\"We shan't have any place for them,\"", "“놓아 둘 자리도 없을 텐데요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"I must send a few, miss, just to cheer you up a bit.", "“몇 그루쯤은 보내 드려야지요, 아가씨. 마음이라도 좀 밝아지시게 말입니다요."], ["I fear you'll be very dolesome there.", "저는 아가씨들이 거기서 아주 쓸쓸해하실까 봐 걱정입니다요."], ["And the doctor,--he ain't got what you can call a regular garden, but there is a bit of a place behind.\"", "그리고 그 의사 선생으로 말하자면, 제대로 된 정원이라 할 만한 건 없고 뒤꼍에 자그마한 자리가 하나 있을 뿐입지요.”"]], [["\"But we wouldn't rob the dear old place,\"", "“하지만 이 정든 옛집 것을 빼앗을 수야 없죠.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"For the matter of that what does it signify?", "“그러면 그게 무슨 대수겠습니까요?"], ["T' squire'll be that wretched he'll turn sheep in here to destroy the place, or he'll have the garden ploughed.", "지주 어른은 하도 비참해지셔서 여기다 양을 풀어 놓아 이곳을 망쳐 놓으시거나, 아니면 정원을 갈아엎게 하실 겁니다요."], ["You see if he don't.", "안 그러시나 두고 보십쇼."], ["As for the place, the place is clean done for, if you leave it.", "이곳으로 말하자면, 마님이 떠나시면 이곳은 아주 끝장난 겁니다요."], ["You don't suppose he'll go and let the Small House to strangers.", "설마 그 어른이 작은 집을 낯선 사람들에게 세놓으시겠습니까요."], ["T' squire ain't one of that sort any ways.\"", "지주 어른은 아무래도 그런 부류가 아니십니다요.”"]], [["\"Ah me!\" exclaimed Mrs.", "“아이고!”"], ["Dale, as soon as Hopkins had taken himself off.", "홉킨스가 물러가자마자 데일 부인이 탄식했다."]], [["\"What is it, mamma?", "“왜 그러세요, 엄마?"], ["He's a dear old man, but surely what he says cannot make you really unhappy.\"", "그이는 정 많은 노인이지만, 그이가 하는 말이 정말로 엄마를 괴롭게 할 리는 없잖아요.”"]], [["\"It is so hard to know what one ought to do.", "“어떻게 하는 것이 옳은지 알기가 참 어렵구나."], ["I did not mean to be selfish, but it seems to me as though I were doing the most selfish thing in the world.\"", "나는 이기적으로 굴 생각이 아니었는데, 어쩐지 내가 세상에서 가장 이기적인 짓을 하고 있는 것만 같아.”"]], [["\"Nay, mamma; it has been anything but selfish.", "“아니에요, 엄마. 이건 이기적인 것과는 거리가 멀어요."], ["Besides, it is we that have done it; not you.\"", "게다가 이 일을 벌인 건 저희지 엄마가 아니에요.”"]], [["\"Do you know, Lily, that I also have that feeling as to breaking up one's old mode of life of which Hopkins spoke.", "“릴리야, 있잖니. 나도 홉킨스가 말한 그 오래된 삶의 방식을 부수어 버린다는 느낌을 똑같이 갖고 있단다."], ["I thought that I should be glad to escape from this place, but now that the time has come I dread it.\"", "나는 이곳에서 벗어나면 기쁠 줄 알았는데, 막상 때가 오고 보니 그것이 두렵구나.”"]], [["\"Do you mean that you repent?\"", "“후회하신다는 말씀이에요?”"]], [["Mrs. Dale did not answer her daughter at once, fearing to commit herself by words which could not be retracted.", "데일 부인은 도로 거두어들일 수 없는 말로 스스로를 묶어 버리기가 두려워 딸에게 곧바로 대답하지 않았다."], ["But at last she said, \"Yes, Lily; I think I do repent.", "그러나 마침내 그녀는 말했다. “그래, 릴리."], ["I think that it has not been well done.\"", "후회하는 것 같구나. 잘한 일이 아니었던 것 같아.”"]], [["\"Then let it be undone,\"", "“그럼 되돌려요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["The dinner-party at Guestwick Manor on that day was not very bright, and yet the earl had done all in his power to make his guests happy.", "그날 게스트윅 저택의 만찬 자리는 그리 흥겹지 않았다. 백작이 손님들을 즐겁게 해 주려고 제 힘닿는 데까지 다 했는데도 그러했다."], ["But gaiety did not come naturally to his house, which, as will have been seen, was an abode very unlike in its nature to that of the other earl at Courcy Castle.", "그러나 흥이란 것이 이 집에서는 저절로 우러나는 법이 없었으니, 이미 보았다시피 이 집은 그 성질에서 코시 성의 저 또 다른 백작의 집과는 아주 딴판이었다."], ["Lady De Courcy at any rate understood how to receive and entertain a house full of people, though the practice of doing so might give rise to difficult questions in the privacy of her domestic relations.", "드 코시 백작부인은 어쨌거나 사람들로 집을 가득 채우고 맞아들여 대접할 줄을 알았다. 하기야 그렇게 하는 버릇 때문에 집안 살림의 은밀한 자리에서 곤란한 문제들이 일어나기는 했겠지만 말이다."], ["Lady Julia did not understand it; but then Lady Julia was never called upon to answer for the expense of extra servants, nor was she asked about twice a week who the ---- was to pay the wine-merchant's bill?", "레이디 줄리아는 그럴 줄을 몰랐다. 그러나 레이디 줄리아는 하인을 더 쓰는 비용을 해명하라는 요구를 받은 적이 한 번도 없었고, 도대체 누가 포도주 상인의 계산서를 치를 것이냐고 한 주에 두 번씩 추궁당한 적도 없었다."], ["As regards Lord De Guest and the Lady Julia themselves, I think they had the best of it; but I am bound to admit, with reference to chance guests, that the house was dull.", "드 게스트 경과 레이디 줄리아 자신들로 말하자면 그편이 가장 나았다고 나는 생각한다. 그러나 어쩌다 찾아드는 손님들에 관해서라면, 이 집이 따분했다는 것을 나는 인정하지 않을 수 없다."], ["The people who were now gathered at the earl's table could hardly have been expected to be very sprightly when in company with each other.", "지금 백작의 식탁에 모여 앉은 사람들은 서로 한자리에 있을 때 그리 활기찰 수 있으리라고 기대하기 어려운 이들이었다."], ["The squire was not a man much given to general society, and was unused to amuse a table full of people.", "지주는 사교 자리에 그리 나서는 사람이 아니었고, 사람이 가득한 식탁을 즐겁게 해 주는 데에는 익숙하지 않았다."], ["On the present occasion he sat next to Lady Julia, and from time to time muttered a few words to her about the state of the country.", "이날 그는 레이디 줄리아 옆에 앉아 이따금 나라 형편에 대해 몇 마디를 그녀에게 웅얼거렸다. 임스 부인은 거기 있는 모든 사람이 몹시 두려웠고, 그중에서도 제 옆자리에 앉은 백작이 가장 두려웠다."], ["Mrs. Eames was terribly afraid of everybody there, and especially of the earl, next to whom she sat, and whom she continually called \"my lord,\"", "그녀는 그를 줄곧 “백작님”이라고 불렀는데, 그렇게 부르는 제 목소리에 스스로 놀랄 지경이라는 것이 그 목소리에 다 드러났다."], ["showing by her voice as she did so that she was almost alarmed by the sound of her own voice. Mr.", "보이스 씨 부부도 와 있어서, 목사는 레이디 줄리아의 반대편에 앉았고 목사의 아내는 백작의 반대편에 앉았다."], ["and Mrs. Boyce were there, the parson sitting on the other side of Lady Julia, and the parson's wife on the other side of the earl.", "보이스 부인은 자기가 이 자리에 아주 익숙하다는 것을 보이려고 몹시 애를 썼고 아마 누구보다도 말을 많이 했다."], ["Mrs. Boyce was very studious to show that she was quite at home, and talked perhaps more than any one else; but in doing so she bored the earl most exquisitely, so that he told John Eames the next morning that she was worse than the bull.", "그러나 그렇게 하느라 백작을 어찌나 절묘하게 지루하게 만들었던지, 백작은 이튿날 아침 존 임스에게 그 부인이 그 황소보다 더하다고 말했다."], ["The parson ate his dinner, but said little or nothing between the two graces.", "목사는 저녁을 먹기는 했으나 앞뒤의 감사 기도 사이에는 거의 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He was a heavy, sensible, slow man, who knew himself and his own powers.", "그는 묵직하고 분별 있고 굼뜬 사람으로, 제 자신과 제 능력을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["\"Uncommon good stewed beef,\"", "“쇠고기 조림이 유난히 좋았소.”"], ["he said, as he went home; \"why can't we have our beef stewed like that?\"", "집으로 돌아가면서 그가 말했다. “우리는 왜 쇠고기를 저렇게 조려 먹지 못하는 거요?”"], ["\"Because we don't pay our cook sixty pounds a year,\"", "“우리는 요리사에게 연 예순 파운드를 주지 않으니까요.”"], ["said Mrs. Boyce.", "보이스 부인이 말했다."], ["\"A woman with sixteen pounds can stew beef as well as a woman with sixty,\"", "“열여섯 파운드 받는 여자도 예순 파운드 받는 여자만큼 쇠고기를 잘 조릴 수 있소.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he; \"she only wants looking after.\"", "“다만 지켜보고 챙겨 주기만 하면 되는 거요.”"], ["The earl himself was possessed of a sort of gaiety.", "백작 자신은 일종의 흥겨움을 지닌 사람이었다."], ["There was about him a lightness of spirit which often made him an agreeable companion to one single person.", "그에게는 마음의 가벼움이 있어서 단둘이 마주 앉은 상대에게는 곧잘 유쾌한 말벗이 되어 주었다."], ["John Eames conceived him to be the most sprightly old man of his day,--an old man with the fun and frolic almost of a boy.", "존 임스는 그를 당대에 가장 발랄한 노인이라고, 소년의 장난기와 흥을 거의 그대로 지닌 노인이라고 여겼다."], ["But this spirit, though it would show itself before John Eames, was not up to the entertainment of John Eames's mother and sister, together with the squire, the parson, and the parson's wife of Allington.", "그러나 이 기질은 존 임스 앞에서라면 드러났을지언정, 존 임스의 어머니와 누이에다 지주와 앨링턴의 목사와 목사의 아내까지 한꺼번에 즐겁게 해 주기에는 모자랐다."], ["So that the earl was overweighted and did not shine on this occasion at his own dinner-table. Dr.", "그리하여 백작은 짐이 겨워 이날만은 제 식탁에서 빛을 내지 못했다."], ["Crofts, who had also been invited, and who had secured the place which was now peculiarly his own, next to Bell Dale, was no doubt happy enough; as, let us hope, was the young lady also; but they added very little to the general hilarity of the company.", "역시 초대를 받아 이제는 유난히 제 자리가 되어 버린 벨 데일의 옆자리를 차지한 크로프츠 선생은 의심할 바 없이 아주 행복했다. 바라건대 그 젊은 아가씨도 그러했을 것이다."], ["John Eames was seated between his own sister and the parson, and did not at all enjoy his position.", "그러나 두 사람이 좌중의 흥겨움에 보탠 것은 거의 없었다. 존 임스는 제 누이와 목사 사이에 앉았는데 제 자리가 조금도 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["He had a full view of the doctor's felicity, as the happy pair sat opposite to him, and conceived himself to be hardly treated by Lily's absence.", "그 행복한 한 쌍이 맞은편에 앉아 있어서 그는 의사의 복을 고스란히 눈앞에서 보아야 했고, 릴리가 오지 않은 것이 자기에게는 야박한 처사라고 여겼다."]], [["The party was certainly very dull, as were all such dinners at Guestwick Manor.", "그 자리는 참으로 따분했다. 게스트윅 저택의 만찬이란 다 그러했다."], ["There are houses, which, in their every-day course, are not conducted by any means in a sad or unsatisfactory manner,--in which life, as a rule, runs along merrily enough; but which cannot give a dinner-party; or, I might rather say, should never allow themselves to be allured into the attempt.", "어떤 집들은 하루하루 굴러가는 모양이 조금도 서글프거나 못마땅하지 않고 삶이 대체로 제법 흥겹게 흘러가는데도 만찬을 베풀 줄은 모른다. 아니, 차라리 이렇게 말하는 편이 낫겠다."], ["The owners of such houses are generally themselves quite aware of the fact, and dread the dinner which they resolved to give quite as much as it is dreaded by their friends.", "그런 집들은 그런 시도에 홀려 들어가도록 스스로를 내버려 두어서는 결코 안 된다고 말이다. 그런 집의 주인들은 대개 그 사실을 스스로도 잘 알고 있어서, 자기가 베풀기로 마음먹은 그 만찬을 제 벗들이 두려워하는 만큼이나 자기도 두려워한다."], ["They know that they prepare for their guests an evening of misery, and for themselves certain long hours of purgatory which are hardly to be endured.", "그들은 자기가 손님들에게는 비참한 저녁 한때를 마련해 주고 있고 제 자신에게는 견뎌 내기 힘든 긴 연옥의 시간을 마련해 주고 있다는 것을 안다. 그런데도 그들은 기어이 그 일을 한다."], ["But they will do it. Why that long table, and all those supernumerary glasses and knives and forks, if they are never to be used?", "저 기다란 식탁이며 남아도는 그 모든 잔과 나이프와 포크는, 한 번도 쓰이지 않을 것이라면 대체 무엇 하러 있단 말인가?"], ["That argument produces all this misery; that and others cognate to it.", "이 논거가 이 모든 비참함을 낳는다. 이것과, 이것에 딸린 다른 논거들이 말이다."], ["On the present occasion, no doubt, there were excuses to be made.", "이번 경우에는 물론 변명할 거리가 있었다."], ["The squire and his niece had been invited on special cause, and their presence would have been well enough.", "지주와 그 조카딸은 특별한 사정이 있어 초대받은 것이니 그 두 사람이 온 것은 나무랄 데가 없었을 것이다."], ["The doctor added in would have done no harm.", "의사가 거기 보태진 것도 해로울 것이 없었을 터다."], ["It was good-natured, too, that invitation given to Mrs. Eames and her daughter.", "임스 부인과 그 딸에게 보낸 초대도 마음씨 좋은 일이었다."], ["The error lay in the parson and his wife.", "잘못은 목사와 그 아내에게 있었다."], ["There was no necessity for their being there, nor had they any ground on which to stand, except the party-giving ground. Mr.", "그들이 거기 있어야 할 필요는 조금도 없었고, 잔치를 벌인다는 구실 말고는 딛고 설 근거도 없었다."], ["and Mrs. Boyce made the dinner-party, and destroyed the social circle.", "보이스 씨 부부가 만찬을 만들어 놓고 그 자리의 정을 무너뜨렸다."], ["Lady Julia knew that she had been wrong as soon as she had sent out the note.", "레이디 줄리아는 쪽지를 내보내자마자 자기가 잘못했다는 것을 알았다."]], [["Nothing was said on that evening which has any bearing on our story.", "그날 저녁에는 우리 이야기와 관련이 있는 말이라고는 한마디도 나오지 않았다."], ["Nothing, indeed, was said which had any bearing on anything.", "아니, 실은 그 무엇과도 관련이 있는 말이 한마디도 나오지 않았다."], ["The earl's professed object had been to bring the squire and young Eames together; but people are never brought together on such melancholy occasions.", "백작이 내세운 목적은 지주와 젊은 임스를 한자리에 맺어 주자는 것이었다. 그러나 사람이란 그런 우울한 자리에서는 결코 맺어지지 않는 법이다."], ["Though they sip their port in close contiguity, they are poles asunder in their minds and feelings.", "두 사람이 바싹 붙어 앉아 포트와인을 홀짝인다 해도, 마음과 감정에서는 남극과 북극만큼이나 멀리 떨어져 있다."], ["When the Guestwick fly came for Mrs. Eames, and the parson's pony phaeton came for him and Mrs.", "임스 부인을 데리러 게스트윅의 삯마차가 오고 목사와 보이스 부인을 데리러 목사네 조랑말 경마차가 왔을 때에는 큰 안도가 느껴졌다."], ["Boyce, a great relief was felt; but the misery of those who were left had gone too far to allow of any reaction on that evening.", "그러나 남은 사람들의 비참함은 이미 너무 깊이 나아간 터라 그날 저녁에는 어떤 반동도 있을 수 없었다."], ["The squire yawned, and the earl yawned, and then there was an end of it for that night.", "지주가 하품을 하고 백작이 하품을 했고, 그러고는 그날 밤은 그것으로 끝이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LIV. THE SECOND VISIT TO THE GUESTWICK BRIDGE.", "# 제54장. 게스트윅 다리로의 두 번째 방문"]], [["Bell had declared that her sister would be very happy to see John Eames if he would go over to Allington, and he had replied that of course he would go there.", "벨은 존 임스가 앨링턴으로 건너오기만 한다면 동생이 그를 무척 반갑게 맞을 것이라고 단언했고, 그는 물론 가겠노라고 대답했다."], ["So much having been, as it were, settled, he was able to speak of his visit as a matter of course at the breakfast-table, on the morning after the earl's dinner-party.", "말하자면 그만큼은 이야기가 매듭지어진 셈이었으므로, 그는 백작의 만찬이 있은 다음 날 아침 식탁에서 제 방문을 지극히 당연한 일처럼 입에 올릴 수 있었다."], ["\"I must get you to come round with me, Dale, and see what I am doing to the land,\"", "“데일, 나와 함께 한 바퀴 돌면서 내가 땅에 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 좀 봐 주셔야겠소.”"], ["the earl said.", "백작이 말했다."], ["And then he proposed to order saddle-horses.", "그러고는 안장 얹은 말을 내오라 이르자고 했다."], ["But the squire preferred walking, and in this way they were disposed of soon after breakfast.", "그러나 지주는 걷는 편을 좋아했고, 이런 식으로 두 사람은 아침 식사가 끝난 직후에 치워졌다."]], [["John had it in his mind to get Bell to himself for half an hour, and hold a conference with her; but it either happened that Lady Julia was too keen in her duties as a hostess, or else, as was more possible, Bell avoided the meeting.", "존은 벨을 반 시간쯤 붙들고 단둘이 이야기를 나눌 작정을 품고 있었다. 그러나 레이디 줄리아가 안주인 노릇에 지나치게 열심이었든지, 아니면 그보다 더 그럴듯하게는 벨이 그 만남을 피했든지 했다."], ["No opportunity for such an interview offered itself, though he hung about the drawing-room all the morning.", "그런 이야기를 나눌 기회는 도무지 오지 않았고, 그는 오전 내내 응접실 언저리를 맴돌았다."], ["\"You had better wait for luncheon, now,\"", "“이제 점심때까지 기다리시는 편이 좋겠어요.”"], ["Lady Julia said to him about twelve.", "열두 시쯤에 레이디 줄리아가 그에게 말했다."], ["But this he declined; and taking himself away hid himself about the place for the next hour and a half.", "그러나 그는 이를 마다하고 자리를 떠, 그다음 한 시간 반 동안 이 근방 어딘가에 몸을 숨기고 지냈다."], ["During this time he considered much whether it would be better for him to ride or walk.", "그동안 그는 말을 타고 갈 것인가 걸어서 갈 것인가를 두고 몹시 궁리했다."], ["If she should give him any hope, he could ride back triumphant as a field-marshal.", "만약 그녀가 그에게 조금이라도 희망을 준다면 그는 육군 원수처럼 의기양양하게 말을 몰아 돌아올 수 있으리라."], ["Then the horse would be delightful to him.", "그렇게 되면 말은 그에게 더없이 즐거운 것이 되리라."], ["But if she should give him no hope,--if it should be his destiny to be rejected utterly on that morning,--then the horse would be terribly in the way of his sorrow.", "그러나 만약 그녀가 아무런 희망도 주지 않는다면—만약 그날 아침 아주 매몰차게 거절당하는 것이 그의 운명이라면—그때 말은 그의 슬픔에 참으로 거추장스러운 물건이 되리라."], ["Under such circumstances what could he do but roam wide about across the fields, resting when he might choose to rest, and running when it might suit him to run.", "그런 처지에서 그가 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가, 들판을 가로질러 멀리 헤매 다니다가 쉬고 싶을 때 쉬고 뛰고 싶을 때 뛰는 것 말고는."], ["\"And she is not like other girls,\"", "“게다가 그이는 여느 아가씨들과는 달라.”"], ["he thought to himself.", "그는 속으로 생각했다."], ["\"She won't care for my boots being dirty.\"", "“내 구두가 더럽다고 마음 쓸 사람이 아니지.”"], ["So at last he elected to walk.", "그리하여 그는 마침내 걸어가기로 정했다."]], [["\"Stand up to her boldly, man,\"", "“당당하게 부딪쳐 보게, 이 사람아.”"], ["the earl had said to him.", "백작이 그에게 말했었다."], ["\"By George, what is there to be afraid of?", "“세상에, 무서울 게 뭐가 있나?"], ["It's my belief they'll give most to those who ask for most.", "내 생각에는 여자들이란 가장 많이 조르는 사내에게 가장 많이 주는 법이야."], ["There's nothing sets 'em against a man like being sheepish.\"", "사내가 쭈뼛거리는 것만큼 여자들 마음을 돌아서게 하는 것도 없네.”"], ["How the earl knew so much, seeing that he had not himself given signs of any success in that walk of life, I am not prepared to say.", "백작 자신은 그 방면의 인생살이에서 어떤 성공의 자취도 보인 적이 없는데 어떻게 그토록 많이 알고 있었는지, 나로서는 말할 준비가 되어 있지 않다."], ["But Eames took his advice as being in itself good, and resolved to act upon it.", "그러나 임스는 그 충고를 그 자체로 좋은 것이라 받아들이고 그대로 해 보리라 마음먹었다."], ["\"Not that any resolution will be of any use,\"", "“결심 따위가 무슨 소용이 있겠느냐마는.”"], ["he said to himself, as he walked along.", "그는 걸어가면서 속으로 말했다."], ["\"When the moment comes I know that I shall tremble before her, and I know that she'll see it; but I don't think it will make any difference in her.\"", "“막상 그 순간이 오면 나는 그이 앞에서 떨 테고, 그이도 그걸 알아볼 테지. 하지만 그렇다고 그이가 달라지지는 않을 거야.”"]], [["He had last seen her on the lawn behind the Small House, just at that time when her passion for Crosbie was at the strongest.", "그가 그녀를 마지막으로 본 것은 작은 집 뒤편 잔디밭에서였고, 그때는 크로즈비를 향한 그녀의 연정이 가장 뜨거울 무렵이었다."], ["Eames had gone thither impelled by a foolish desire to declare to her his hopeless love, and she had answered him by telling him that she loved Mr.", "임스는 가망 없는 제 사랑을 고백하고 싶다는 어리석은 마음에 이끌려 그리로 갔었고, 그녀는 이 세상 무엇보다도 크로즈비 씨를 사랑한다고 대답했다."], ["Crosbie better than all the world besides.", "물론 그때는 그녀가 그럴 수밖에 없었다."], ["Of course she had done so, at that time; but, nevertheless, her manner of telling him had seemed to him to be cruel.", "그러나 그렇다 해도 그에게는 그녀가 그 말을 하던 태도가 잔인하게만 느껴졌다."], ["And he also had been cruel.", "그리고 그 역시 잔인했다."], ["He had told her that he hated Crosbie,--calling him \"that man,\" and assuring her that no earthly consideration should induce him to go into \"that man's house.\"", "그는 크로즈비가 밉다고 말하며 그를 “그 사내”라 부르고, 이 세상 무슨 사정이 있어도 “그 사내의 집”에는 발을 들이지 않겠노라고 다짐했다."], ["Then he had walked away moodily wishing him all manner of evil.", "그러고 나서 그는 그 사내에게 온갖 나쁜 일이 다 닥치기를 바라며 침울하게 걸어 나왔다."], ["Was it not singular that all the evil things which he, in his mind, had meditated for the man, had fallen upon him.", "그가 마음속으로 그 사내에게 내리기를 궁리했던 온갖 나쁜 일이 죄다 그 사내에게 떨어졌다는 것은 참으로 기묘한 일이 아닌가."], ["Crosbie had lost his love!", "크로즈비는 제 사랑을 잃었다!"], ["He had so proved himself to be a villain that his name might not be so much as mentioned!", "이름조차 입에 올릴 수 없을 만큼 스스로 악당임을 드러내 보였다!"], ["He had been ignominiously thrashed!", "창피스럽게 두들겨 맞았다!"], ["But what good would all this be if his image were still dear to Lily's heart?", "그러나 그의 모습이 아직도 릴리의 마음에 소중하다면 이 모든 것이 무슨 소용이란 말인가?"], ["\"I told her that I loved her then,\"", "“그때도 나는 그이를 사랑한다고 말했지.”"], ["he said to himself, \"though I had no right to do so.", "그는 속으로 말했다. “그럴 자격이 없으면서도."], ["At any rate I have a right to tell her now.\"", "아무튼 이제는 그렇게 말할 자격이 있어.”"]], [["When he reached Allington he did not go in through the village and up to the front of the Small House by the cross street, but turned by the church gate and passed over the squire's terrace, and by the end of the Great House through the garden.", "앨링턴에 이르렀을 때 그는 마을을 지나 옆길로 작은 집 정면으로 올라가지 않고, 교회 문 쪽으로 돌아 지주의 테라스를 지나고 큰 집 끄트머리를 끼고 정원을 가로질러 갔다."], ["Here he encountered Hopkins.", "여기서 그는 홉킨스와 마주쳤다."], ["\"Why, if that b'aint Mr. Eames!\"", "“아니, 저건 임스 씨 아니십니까요!”"], ["said the gardener.", "정원사가 말했다."], ["\"Mr. John, may I make so bold!\"", "“존 나리, 이렇게 실례를 무릅써도 될깝쇼!”"], ["and Hopkins held out a very dirty hand, which Eames of course took, unconscious of the cause of this new affection.", "그러면서 홉킨스가 몹시 더러운 손을 내밀었고, 임스는 이 새삼스러운 애정의 까닭도 모른 채 물론 그 손을 잡았다."]], [["\"I'm just going to call at the Small House, and I thought I'd come this way.\"", "“작은 집에 잠깐 들르러 가는 길인데, 이 길로 와 볼까 했네.”"]], [["\"To be sure; this way, or that way, or any way, who's so welcome, Mr.", "“아무렴입죠. 이 길이든 저 길이든 어느 길이든, 존 나리만큼 반가운 분이 어디 있겠습니까요?"], ["John?", "부럽습니다요."], ["I envies you; I envies you more than I envies any man.", "세상 어느 사내가 부러운 것보다 나리가 더 부럽습니다요."], ["If I could a got him by the scuff of the neck, I'd a treated him jist like any wermin;--I would, indeed!", "제가 그자의 목덜미를 움켜쥘 수만 있었더라면 여느 버러지 다루듯 다뤄 줬을 겁니다요."], ["He was wermin!", "정말 그랬을 겁니다요!"], ["I ollays said it.", "그자는 버러지였습니다요!"], ["I hated him ollays; I did indeed, Mr.", "저는 늘 그리 말했습죠. 늘 미워했습니다요."], ["John, from the first moment when he used to be nigging away at them foutry balls, knocking them in among the rhododendrons, as though there weren't no flower blossoms for next year.", "정말입니다요, 존 나리, 그자가 그 빌어먹을 공을 툭툭 쳐 대며 진달래 덤불 속으로 처박아 넣기 시작하던 첫 순간부터요. 내년에 필 꽃송이 따위는 있지도 않다는 듯이 말입니다요."], ["He never looked at one as though one were a Christian; did he, Mr.", "그자는 사람을 사람으로 여기고 쳐다본 적이 한 번도 없었습니다요."], ["John?\"", "안 그렇습니까요, 존 나리?”"]], [["\"I wasn't very fond of him myself, Hopkins.\"", "“나도 그 사람을 그리 좋아하지는 않았네, 홉킨스.”"]], [["\"Of course you weren't very fond of him.", "“물론 좋아하실 리가 없습죠. 누가 그자를 좋아했겠습니까요?"], ["Who was?--only she, poor young lady.", "그저 그분, 가엾은 아가씨뿐이었습죠."], ["She'll be better now, Mr. John, a deal better.", "이제는 나아지실 겁니다요, 존 나리, 훨씬 나아지실 겁니다요."], ["He wasn't a wholesome lover,--not like you are.", "그자는 성한 연인이 아니었습니다요. 나리 같지가 않았습죠."], ["Tell me, Mr. John, did you give it him well when you got him?", "말씀 좀 해 주십쇼, 존 나리, 그자를 붙잡았을 때 제대로 갈겨 주셨습니까요?"], ["I heard you did;--two black eyes, and all his face one mash of gore!\"", "그러셨다고 들었습니다요. 두 눈이 시퍼렇게 멍들고 얼굴이 온통 피범벅이 되었다고요!”"], ["And Hopkins, who was by no means a young man, stiffly put himself into a fighting attitude.", "그러면서 홉킨스는 결코 젊은 사람이 아니었건만 뻣뻣하게 싸움 자세를 잡았다."]], [["Eames passed on over the little bridge, which seemed to be in a state of fast decay, unattended to by any friendly carpenter, now that the days of its use were so nearly at an end; and on into the garden, lingering on the spot where he had last said farewell to Lily.", "임스는 작은 다리를 건너 지나갔다. 그 다리는 쓰일 날이 이제 거의 다 되어 가는 터라 다정한 목수의 손길도 받지 못한 채 빠르게 썩어 가는 듯했다."], ["He looked about as though he expected still to find her there; but there was no one to be seen in the garden, and no sound to be heard.", "그러고는 정원으로 들어서서, 릴리에게 마지막으로 작별을 고했던 그 자리에 잠시 머물렀다. 그는 아직도 그녀가 거기 있기를 바라기라도 하듯 사방을 둘러보았다."], ["As every step brought him nearer to her whom he was seeking, he became more and more conscious of the hopelessness of his errand.", "그러나 정원에는 아무도 보이지 않았고 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다. 한 걸음 한 걸음이 그가 찾는 사람에게 그를 데려다 줄수록 그는 제 심부름이 가망 없다는 것을 점점 더 절실히 느꼈다."], ["Him she had never loved, and why should he venture to hope that she would love him now?", "그녀는 그를 사랑한 적이 없었는데, 이제 와서 그녀가 자기를 사랑해 주리라고 어찌 감히 바라겠는가?"], ["He would have turned back had he not been aware that his promise to others required that he should persevere.", "남들에게 한 약속이 있으니 끝까지 밀고 나가야 한다는 것을 알지 못했더라면 그는 발길을 돌렸을 것이다."], ["He had said that he would do this thing, and he would be as good as his word.", "그는 이 일을 하겠노라 말했으니 제 말대로 할 참이었다."], ["But he hardly ventured to hope that he might be successful.", "그러나 잘되리라는 희망은 좀처럼 품지 못했다."], ["In this frame of mind he slowly made his way up across the lawn.", "이런 마음가짐으로 그는 잔디밭을 가로질러 천천히 올라갔다."]], [["\"My dear, there is John Eames,\"", "“얘야, 존 임스가 왔구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale, who had first seen him from the parlour window.", "거실 창으로 그를 맨 먼저 본 데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Don't go, mamma.\"", "“가지 마세요, 엄마.”"]], [["\"I don't know; perhaps it will be better that I should.\"", "“글쎄다. 아마 내가 자리를 비켜 주는 편이 나을 게다.”"]], [["\"No, mamma, no; what good can it do?", "“아니에요, 엄마, 아니에요. 그런다고 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?"], ["It can do no good.", "아무 소용도 없어요."], ["I like him as well as I can like any one.", "저는 그이를 누구 못지않게 좋아해요."], ["I love him dearly.", "그이를 진심으로 아껴요."], ["But it can do no good.", "하지만 아무 소용도 없어요."], ["Let him come in here, and be very kind to him; but do not go away and leave us.", "이리로 들어오시게 해서 아주 다정하게 대해 주세요. 하지만 자리를 뜨고 우리만 남겨 두지는 마세요."], ["Of course I knew he would come, and I shall be very glad to see him.\"", "물론 그이가 오리라는 건 알고 있었고, 만나면 저도 무척 반가울 거예요.”"]], [["Then Mrs. Dale went round to the other room, and admitted her visitor through the window of the drawing-room.", "그러고 나서 데일 부인은 다른 방으로 돌아가 응접실 창을 통해 손님을 들였다."], ["\"We are in terrible confusion, John, are we not?\"", "“집이 말이 아니게 어수선하구나, 존. 그렇지 않니?”"]], [["\"And so you are really going to live in Guestwick?\"", "“그럼 정말로 게스트윅에 가서 사시는 겁니까?”"]], [["\"Well, it looks like it, does it not? But, to tell you a secret,--only it must be a secret; you must not mention it at Guestwick Manor; even Bell does not know;--we have half made up our minds to unpack all our things and stay where we are.\"", "“글쎄, 그래 보이지? 그런데 비밀 하나를 말해 주마. 다만 비밀이어야 한다. 게스트윅 저택에서는 입에 올리면 안 돼. 벨도 아직 모르는 일이야. 우리는 짐을 죄다 도로 풀고 여기 그대로 살까 하고 반쯤 마음을 굳혔단다.”"]], [["Eames was so intent on his own purpose, and so fully occupied with the difficulty of the task before him, that he could hardly receive Mrs. Dale's tidings with all the interest which they deserved.", "임스는 제 목적에만 골똘해 있었고 앞에 놓인 어려운 일에 온통 마음을 빼앗겨 있던 터라, 데일 부인의 소식을 그것이 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼의 관심을 가지고 받아들일 수가 없었다."], ["\"Unpack them all again,\"", "“짐을 다시 다 푼다고요.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"That will be very troublesome.", "“그거 아주 성가시겠는데요."], ["Is Lily with you, Mrs. Dale?\"", "릴리도 같이 계십니까, 데일 부인?”"]], [["\"Yes, she is in the parlour.", "“그래, 거실에 있단다."], ["Come and see her.\"", "가서 만나 보렴.”"], ["So he followed Mrs. Dale through the hall, and found himself in the presence of his love.", "그리하여 그는 데일 부인을 따라 현관을 지나갔고, 제 사랑 앞에 서게 되었다."]], [["\"How do you do, John?\"", "“안녕하세요, 존?”"], ["\"How do you do, Lily?\"", "“안녕하세요, 릴리?”"], ["We all know the way in which such meetings are commenced.", "그런 만남이 어떤 식으로 시작되는지는 우리 모두가 안다."], ["Each longed to be tender and affectionate to the other,--each in a different way; but neither knew how to throw any tenderness into this first greeting.", "서로가 저마다 다른 방식으로 상대에게 다정하고 살갑고 싶어 했다. 그러나 둘 다 이 첫 인사에 어떻게 다정함을 실어야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["\"So you're staying at the Manor House,\"", "“저택에 묵고 계시다면서요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; I'm staying there.", "“네, 거기 묵고 있습니다."], ["Your uncle and Bell came yesterday afternoon.\"", "삼촌과 벨이 어제 오후에 왔어요.”"]], [["\"Have you heard about Bell?\"", "“벨 이야기는 들었니?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes; Mary told me.", "“아, 네. 메리가 말해 주었습니다."], ["I'm so glad of it.", "정말 기쁩니다."], ["I always liked Dr. Crofts very much.", "저는 늘 크로프츠 선생을 무척 좋아했어요."], ["I have not congratulated her, because I didn't know whether it was a secret.", "아직 축하 인사는 못 드렸는데, 비밀인지 아닌지 몰라서요."], ["But Crofts was there last night, and if it is a secret he didn't seem to be very careful about keeping it.\"", "하지만 어젯밤에 크로프츠도 거기 있었는데, 비밀이라면 그리 조심스레 지킬 생각은 없어 보이더군요.”"]], [["\"It is no secret,\"", "“비밀은 아니란다.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I don't know that I am fond of such secrets.\"", "“나는 그런 비밀을 좋아하는 편이 아니야.”"], ["But as she said this, she thought of Crosbie's engagement, which had been told to every one, and of its consequences.", "그러나 이렇게 말하면서 그녀는 모두에게 알려졌던 크로즈비의 약혼과 그 결말을 떠올렸다."]], [["\"Is it to be soon?\"", "“곧 하시는 겁니까?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["\"Well, yes; we think so.", "“글쎄, 그래. 우리는 그렇게 생각한단다."], ["Of course nothing is settled.\"", "물론 정해진 건 아무것도 없지만.”"]], [["\"It was such fun,\"", "“정말 우스웠어요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"James, who took, at any rate, a year or two to make his proposal, wanted to be married the next day afterwards.\"", "“청혼 한마디 하는 데 아무리 못해도 한두 해가 걸린 제임스가, 그 이튿날 당장 혼인하고 싶어 했다니까요.”"]], [["\"No, Lily; not quite that.\"", "“아니야, 릴리. 그렇게까지는 아니지.”"]], [["\"Well, mamma, it was very nearly that.", "“그래도 엄마, 거의 그랬잖아요."], ["He thought it could all be done this week.", "그이는 이번 주 안에 다 치를 수 있다고 생각했다니까요."], ["It has made us so happy, John!", "우리는 그 일로 아주 행복해요, 존!"], ["I don't know anybody I should so much like for a brother.", "형부로 그이만큼 마음에 드는 사람은 달리 없을 것 같아요."], ["I'm very glad you like him;--very glad.", "당신이 그이를 좋아해 주시니 기뻐요. 정말 기뻐요."], ["I hope you'll be friends always.\"", "두 분이 늘 벗으로 지내면 좋겠어요.”"], ["There was some little tenderness in this,--as John acknowledged to himself.", "여기에는 얼마쯤 다정함이 있었다. 존 자신도 그렇게 인정했다."]], [["\"I'm sure we shall,--if he likes it.", "“그럼요, 저희는 그럴 겁니다. 그이만 좋다면요."], ["That is, if I ever happen to see him.", "그러니까, 제가 그이를 만날 일이 생긴다면 말입니다."], ["I'll do anything for him I can if he ever comes up to London.", "그이가 런던에 올라오면 제가 할 수 있는 일은 무엇이든 하겠습니다."], ["Wouldn't it be a good thing, Mrs. Dale, if he settled himself in London?\"", "데일 부인, 그이가 런던에 자리를 잡으면 좋지 않겠습니까?”"]], [["\"No, John; it would be a very bad thing.", "“아니다, 존. 그건 아주 나쁜 일이지."], ["Why should he wish to rob me of my daughter?\"", "그이가 어째서 내게서 딸을 빼앗아 가려 하겠니?”"]], [["Mrs.", "데일 부인은 맏딸 이야기를 하고 있었다."], ["Dale was speaking of her eldest daughter; but the very allusion to any such robbery covered John Eames's face with a blush, made him hot up to the roots of his hair, and for the moment silenced him.", "그러나 그런 빼앗음에 대한 언급만으로도 존 임스의 얼굴은 붉게 물들었고 머리끝까지 화끈해졌으며, 그는 잠시 말을 잃었다."]], [["\"You think he would have a better career in London?\"", "“런던에서라면 그이가 더 잘 풀리리라고 생각하시나요?”"], ["said Lily, speaking under the influence of her superior presence of mind.", "남달리 침착한 마음가짐에 힘입어 릴리가 말했다."]], [["She had certainly shown defective judgment in desiring her mother not to leave them alone; and of this Mrs.", "어머니에게 자리를 비우지 말아 달라고 청한 것은 분명 릴리의 판단 착오였고, 데일 부인도 곧 그것을 깨달았다."], ["Dale soon felt herself aware. The thing had to be done, and no little precautionary measure, such as this of Mrs.", "일은 치러져야 했고, 데일 부인이 억지로 자리를 지키는 따위의 자잘한 예방책으로는 그것을 막을 수 없었다."], ["Dale's enforced presence, would prevent it.", "데일 부인은 이를 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Of this Mrs. Dale was well aware; and she felt, moreover, that John was entitled to an opportunity of pleading his own cause.", "게다가 그녀는 존에게도 제 사정을 호소할 기회가 주어져야 마땅하다고 느꼈다."], ["It might be that such opportunity would avail him nothing, but not the less should he have it of right, seeing that he desired it.", "그런 기회가 그에게 아무 소용이 없을지도 모르나, 그가 원하는 이상 그에게는 마땅히 그럴 권리가 있었다."], ["But yet Mrs. Dale did not dare to get up and leave the room.", "그러나 그럼에도 데일 부인은 감히 일어나 방을 나가지 못했다."], ["Lily had asked her not to do so, and at the present period of their lives all Lily's requests were sacred.", "릴리가 그러지 말아 달라고 청했고, 두 사람 삶의 이 시기에 릴리의 청은 무엇이든 거룩한 것이었다."], ["They continued for some time to talk of Crofts and his marriage; and when that subject was finished, they discussed their own probable,--or, as it seemed now, improbable,--removal to Guestwick.", "그들은 한동안 크로프츠와 그의 혼인 이야기를 계속했고, 그 이야깃거리가 다하자 게스트윅으로 옮겨 갈—이제는 옮겨 가지 않을 듯한—제 일을 두고 이야기했다."], ["\"It's going too far, mamma,\" said Lily, \"to say that you think we shall not go.", "“엄마, 우리가 가지 않을 것 같다고 하시는 건 너무 앞서가는 말씀이에요.”"], ["It was only last night that you suggested it.", "릴리가 말했다. “겨우 어젯밤에 꺼내신 이야기잖아요."], ["The truth is, John, that Hopkins came in and discoursed with the most wonderful eloquence.", "실은 이래요, 존. 홉킨스가 들어와서는 놀라운 웅변을 늘어놓았답니다."], ["Nobody dared to oppose Hopkins.", "아무도 감히 홉킨스에게 맞설 수 없었어요."], ["He made us almost cry; he was so pathetic.\"", "어찌나 애처롭던지 우리를 울릴 뻔했다니까요.”"]], [["\"He has just been talking to me, too,\"", "“저한테도 방금 이야기를 하더군요.”"], ["said John, \"as I came through the squire's garden.\"", "존이 말했다. “지주 어른 정원을 지나오는 길에요.”"]], [["\"And what has he been saying to you?\"", "“그이가 너한테는 무슨 말을 하던?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, I don't know; not much.\"", "“아, 글쎄요. 별말은 없었습니다.”"], ["John, however, remembered well, at this moment, all that the gardener had said to him.", "그러나 이 순간 존은 정원사가 자기에게 한 말을 낱낱이 잘 기억하고 있었다."], ["Did she know of that encounter between him and Crosbie?", "그녀도 자기와 크로즈비 사이에 있었던 그 격투를 알고 있을까?"], ["and if she did know of it, in what light did she regard it?", "그리고 알고 있다면 그것을 어떻게 여기고 있을까?"]], [["They had sat thus for an hour together, and Eames was not as yet an inch nearer to his object.", "그들은 그렇게 한 시간을 함께 앉아 있었고, 임스는 아직 제 목적에 한 치도 다가서지 못했다."], ["He had sworn to himself that he would not leave the Small House without asking Lily to be his wife.", "그는 릴리에게 아내가 되어 달라고 청하지 않고서는 작은 집을 떠나지 않겠다고 스스로 맹세했었다."], ["It seemed to him as though he would be guilty of falsehood towards the earl if he did so.", "그러지 않는다면 백작에게 거짓을 저지르는 것이나 다름없을 것 같았다."], ["Lord De Guest had opened his house to him, and had asked all the Dales there, and had offered himself up as a sacrifice at the cruel shrine of a serious dinner-party, to say nothing of that easier and lighter sacrifice which he had made in a pecuniary point of view, in order that this thing might be done.", "드 게스트 경은 그를 위해 제 집을 열어 주었고 데일 집안사람들을 모두 불렀으며, 이 일이 이루어지도록 하기 위해 격식 차린 만찬이라는 잔혹한 제단에 스스로를 제물로 바쳤다. 돈 문제에서 치른, 그보다는 수월하고 가벼운 희생은 말할 것도 없었다."], ["Under such circumstances Eames was too honest a man not to do it, let the difficulties in his way be what they might.", "그런 사정이니 임스는 앞길에 어떤 어려움이 있든 그 일을 하지 않을 만큼 정직하지 못한 사람이 아니었다."]], [["He had sat there for an hour, and Mrs. Dale still remained with her daughter.", "그는 한 시간을 그렇게 앉아 있었건만 데일 부인은 여전히 딸 곁에 남아 있었다."], ["Should he get up boldly and ask Lily to put on her bonnet and come out into the garden?", "대담하게 일어나 릴리에게 모자를 쓰고 정원으로 나오자고 청해 볼까?"], ["As the thought struck him, he rose and grasped at his hat.", "그런 생각이 들자 그는 일어서서 모자를 집어 들었다."], ["\"I am going to walk back to Guestwick,\"", "“저는 게스트윅까지 걸어서 돌아가려 합니다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"It was very good of you to come so far to see us.\"", "“이렇게 먼 데까지 우리를 보러 와 주다니 참 고맙구나.”"]], [["\"I was always fond of walking,\"", "“저는 늘 걷기를 좋아했습니다.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"The earl wanted me to ride, but I prefer being on foot when I know the country, as I do here.\"", "“백작님께서는 말을 타라고 하셨지만, 여기처럼 길을 잘 아는 고장에서는 걷는 편이 좋습니다.”"]], [["\"Have a glass of wine before you go.\"", "“가기 전에 포도주라도 한 잔 들려무나.”"]], [["\"Oh, dear, no.", "“아, 괜찮습니다."], ["I think I'll go back through the squire's fields, and out on the road at the white gate.", "지주 어른 들판을 가로질러 흰 대문께로 큰길에 나설까 합니다."], ["The path is quite dry now.\"", "이제 길이 아주 말랐더군요.”"]], [["\"I dare say it is,\"", "“그렇겠지.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Lily, I wonder whether you would come as far as that with me.\"", "“릴리, 거기까지만이라도 함께 가 주시지 않겠습니까?”"], ["As the request was made Mrs. Dale looked at her daughter almost beseechingly.", "그 청이 나오자 데일 부인은 거의 애원하듯 딸을 바라보았다."], ["\"Do, pray do,\"", "“그래 주세요, 부디.”"], ["said he; \"it is a beautiful day for walking.\"", "그가 말했다. “걷기에 아주 좋은 날입니다.”"]], [["The path proposed lay right across the field into which Lily had taken Crosbie when she made her offer to let him off from his engagement.", "그가 말한 그 길은, 릴리가 크로즈비를 데리고 들어가 약혼을 물러 주겠다고 청했던 바로 그 들판을 곧장 가로질렀다."], ["Could it be possible that she should ever walk there again with another lover?", "그 길을 또 다른 연인과 다시 걷는 일이 그녀에게 있을 수 있겠는가? “아니에요, 존.”"], ["\"No, John,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said; \"not to-day, I think.", "“오늘은 그만두겠어요."], ["I am almost tired, and I had rather not go out.\"", "좀 피곤하기도 하고, 밖에 나가고 싶지 않아요.”"]], [["\"It would do you good,\"", "“나가면 몸에 좋을 텐데.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"I don't want to be done good to, mamma.", "“저는 몸에 좋은 일 같은 건 받고 싶지 않아요, 엄마."], ["Besides, I should have to come back by myself.\"", "게다가 혼자 돌아와야 하잖아요.”"]], [["\"I'll come back with you,\"", "“제가 같이 돌아오겠습니다.”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes; and then I should have to go again with you.", "“아, 그렇겠죠. 그러면 저는 또 당신과 함께 가야 하고요."], ["But, John, really I don't wish to walk to-day.\"", "하지만 존, 정말이지 오늘은 걷고 싶지 않아요.”"], ["Whereupon John Eames again put down his hat.", "그러자 존 임스는 다시 모자를 내려놓았다."]], [["\"Lily,\"", "“릴리.”"], ["said he; and then he stopped.", "그가 말했다. 그러고는 멈췄다."], ["Mrs. Dale walked away to the window, turning her back upon her daughter and visitor.", "데일 부인은 창가로 걸어가 딸과 손님에게 등을 돌렸다."], ["\"Lily, I have come over here on purpose to speak to you.", "“릴리, 저는 당신에게 이야기하려고 일부러 여기까지 왔습니다."], ["Indeed, I have come down from London only that I might see you.\"", "실은 당신을 보려고 런던에서 내려온 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Have you, John?\"", "“그러셨어요, 존?”"]], [["\"Yes, I have.", "“네, 그랬습니다."], ["You know well all that I have got to tell you.", "제가 무슨 말을 하려는지 당신도 잘 알고 계시겠지요."], ["I loved you before he ever saw you; and now that he has gone, I love you better than I ever did.", "저는 그 사람이 당신을 보기도 전부터 당신을 사랑했습니다. 그리고 이제 그가 가 버렸으니, 예전 어느 때보다도 더 당신을 사랑합니다."], ["Dear Lily!\"", "사랑하는 릴리!”"], ["and he put out his hand to her.", "그러고는 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["\"No, John; no,\"", "“아니에요, 존. 안 돼요.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["\"Must it be always no?\"", "“언제까지나 안 되는 겁니까?”"]], [["\"Always no to that.", "“그것만은 언제까지나 안 돼요."], ["How can it be otherwise?", "어떻게 달라질 수가 있겠어요?"], ["You would not have me marry you while I love another!\"", "제가 다른 사람을 사랑하는데 저를 아내로 맞고 싶으시지는 않겠지요!”"]], [["\"But he is gone.", "“하지만 그는 갔습니다."], ["He has taken another wife.\"", "다른 아내를 얻었어요.”"]], [["\"I cannot change myself because he is changed.", "“그이가 변했다고 제가 저를 바꿀 수는 없어요."], ["If you are kind to me you will let that be enough.\"", "저에게 다정하게 굴어 주실 거라면 그것으로 족하게 여겨 주세요.”"]], [["\"But you are so unkind to me!\"", "“하지만 당신은 저에게 너무 매정하군요!”"]], [["\"No, no; oh, I would wish to be so kind to you!", "“아니에요, 아니에요. 아, 저는 당신에게 얼마나 다정하고 싶은지 몰라요!"], ["John, here; take my hand.", "존, 여기요. 제 손을 잡으세요."], ["It is the hand of a friend who loves you, and will always love you.", "당신을 아끼고 언제까지나 아낄 벗의 손이에요."], ["Dear John, I will do anything,--everything for you but that.\"", "사랑하는 존, 저는 당신을 위해 그것만 빼고 무엇이든, 무엇이든 하겠어요.”"]], [["\"There is only one thing,\"", "“한 가지밖에 없습니다.”"], ["said he, still holding her by the hand, but with his face turned from her.", "그가 여전히 그녀의 손을 잡은 채, 그러나 얼굴은 돌린 채로 말했다."]], [["\"Nay; do not say so.", "“아니에요, 그런 말씀 마세요."], ["Are you worse off than I am?", "당신이 저보다 더 딱한가요?"], ["I could not have that one thing, and I was nearer to my heart's longings than you have ever been.", "저도 그 한 가지를 가질 수 없었어요. 게다가 저는 당신이 이제껏 가 보았던 것보다 제 마음의 소원에 훨씬 가까이 가 있었어요."], ["I cannot have that one thing; but I know that there are other things, and I will not allow myself to be broken-hearted.\"", "저는 그 한 가지를 가질 수 없어요. 하지만 다른 것들이 있다는 것을 저는 알고, 스스로 마음이 부서지도록 내버려 두지는 않겠어요.”"]], [["\"You are stronger than I am,\"", "“당신은 저보다 강하군요.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Not stronger, but more certain.", "“강한 게 아니라 더 확실할 뿐이에요."], ["Make yourself as sure as I am, and you, too, will be strong.", "저만큼 확실해지세요. 그러면 당신도 강해질 거예요."], ["Is it not so, mamma?\"", "그렇지 않아요, 엄마?”"]], [["\"I wish it could be otherwise;--I wish it could be otherwise!", "“그렇지 않았으면 좋으련만—그렇지 않았으면 좋으련만!"], ["If you can give him any hope--\"", "네가 저이에게 조금이라도 희망을 줄 수 있다면—”"]], [["\"Mamma!\"", "“엄마!”"]], [["\"Tell me that I may come again,--in a year,\"", "“한 해 뒤에 다시 와도 좋다고 말해 주십시오.”"], ["he pleaded.", "그가 애원했다."]], [["\"I cannot tell you so.", "“그렇게 말할 수는 없어요."], ["You may not come again,--not in this way.", "다시 오시면 안 돼요. 이런 식으로는요."], ["Do you remember what I told you before, in the garden; that I loved him better than all the world besides?", "전에 정원에서 제가 한 말을 기억하세요? 이 세상 무엇보다도 그이를 사랑한다고 했지요."], ["It is still the same.", "그건 지금도 그대로예요."], ["I still love him better than all the world.", "저는 여전히 이 세상 무엇보다도 그이를 사랑해요."], ["How, then, can I give you any hope?\"", "그러니 제가 어떻게 당신에게 희망을 드릴 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["\"But it will not be so for ever, Lily.\"", "“하지만 언제까지나 그렇지는 않을 겁니다, 릴리.”"]], [["\"For ever!", "“언제까지나라니요!"], ["Why should he not be mine as well as hers when that for ever comes?", "그 언제까지나가 오면, 그이가 그 여자의 것인 만큼 제 것이 되지 못할 까닭이 어디 있나요?"], ["John, if you understand what it is to love, you will say nothing more of it.", "존, 사랑한다는 것이 무엇인지 아신다면 그 말씀은 더 하지 마세요."], ["I have spoken to you more openly about this than I have ever done to anybody, even to mamma, because I have wished to make you understand my feelings.", "저는 이 일을 두고 누구에게도, 엄마에게조차 해 본 적 없는 이야기를 당신에게 이토록 터놓고 했어요. 제 마음을 당신이 알아주기를 바랐으니까요."], ["I should be disgraced in my own eyes if I admitted the love of another man, after--after--.", "그 일이 있고 나서—그 일이 있고 나서 제가 다른 남자의 사랑을 받아들인다면 저는 제 눈에 스스로 부끄러운 사람이 될 거예요."], ["It is to me almost as though I had married him.", "저에게는 그이와 혼인한 것이나 거의 다름없어요."], ["I am not blaming him, remember.", "그이를 탓하는 것은 아니에요."], ["These things are different with a man.\"", "잊지 마세요. 이런 일은 남자에게는 다르니까요.”"]], [["She had not dropped his hand, and as she made her last speech was sitting in her old chair with her eyes fixed upon the ground.", "그녀는 그의 손을 놓지 않았고, 마지막 말을 하는 동안 늘 앉던 제 의자에 앉아 눈길을 바닥에 두고 있었다."], ["She spoke in a low voice, slowly, almost with difficulty; but still the words came very clearly, with a clear, distinct voice which caused them to be remembered with accuracy, both by Eames and Mrs.", "그녀는 나직한 목소리로 천천히, 거의 힘겹게 말했다. 그러나 그 말은 아주 또렷하게, 맑고 분명한 목소리로 흘러나와 임스와 데일 부인 두 사람의 기억에 한 자도 틀림없이 남게 되었다."], ["Dale. To him it seemed to be impossible that he should continue his suit after such a declaration.", "그에게는 그런 선언을 듣고서도 구애를 이어 간다는 것이 있을 수 없는 일로 여겨졌다."], ["To Mrs. Dale they were terrible words, speaking of a perpetual widowhood, and telling of an amount of suffering greater even than that which she had anticipated.", "데일 부인에게 그것은 끔찍한 말이었으니, 평생의 과부살이를 이야기하는 것이었고 그녀가 짐작했던 것보다도 더 큰 괴로움을 알리는 것이었다."], ["It was true that Lily had never said so much to her as she had now said to John Eames, or had attempted to make so clear an exposition of her own feelings.", "릴리가 이제 존 임스에게 한 만큼 어머니에게 말한 적이 없었던 것도, 제 마음을 이토록 또렷이 펼쳐 보이려 한 적이 없었던 것도 사실이었다."], ["\"I should be disgraced in my own eyes if I admitted the love of another man!\"", "“다른 남자의 사랑을 받아들인다면 저는 제 눈에 스스로 부끄러운 사람이 될 거예요!”"], ["They were terrible words, but very easy to be understood.", "끔찍한 말이었으나 알아듣기는 아주 쉬웠다."], ["Mrs. Dale had felt, from the first, that Eames was coming too soon, that the earl and the squire together were making an effort to cure the wound too quickly after its infliction; that time should have been given to her girl to recover.", "데일 부인은 처음부터 임스가 너무 일찍 왔다고, 백작과 지주가 함께 상처를 입은 지 얼마 되지도 않은 그 상처를 너무 빨리 낫게 하려 애쓰고 있다고, 제 딸에게는 추스를 시간이 주어졌어야 했다고 느껴 왔다."], ["But now the attempt had been made, and words had been forced from Lily's lips, the speaking of which would never be forgotten by herself.", "그러나 이제 시도는 이루어졌고, 릴리의 입술에서는 말이 억지로 끌려 나왔으며, 그 말을 한 일을 그녀 자신은 결코 잊지 못할 것이었다."]], [["\"I knew that it would be so,\"", "“그러시리라는 걸 알고 있었습니다.”"], ["said John.", "존이 말했다."]], [["\"Ah, yes; you know it, because your heart understands my heart.", "“아, 그래요. 당신은 알고 있었죠."], ["And you will not be angry with me, and say naughty, cruel words, as you did once before.", "당신의 마음이 제 마음을 알아주니까요. 그리고 당신은 저에게 화내지 않을 거예요."], ["We will think of each other, John, and pray for each other; and will always love one another.", "언젠가 한 번 그러셨듯 심술궂고 모진 말을 하지도 않을 거고요. 우리는 서로를 생각하고 서로를 위해 기도해요, 존."], ["When we do meet let us be glad to see each other.", "그리고 언제까지나 서로를 아껴요. 만나게 되면 서로 반가워하기로 해요."], ["No other friend shall ever be dearer to me than you are.", "어떤 벗도 당신보다 저에게 더 소중하지는 않을 거예요."], ["You are so true and honest!", "당신은 참으로 진실하고 정직한 분이니까요!"], ["When you marry I will tell your wife what an infinite blessing God has given her.\"", "당신이 혼인하면 저는 당신의 아내에게, 하느님이 그이에게 얼마나 크나큰 축복을 주셨는지 말해 줄 거예요.”"]], [["\"You shall never do that.\"", "“그런 일은 절대 하지 마십시오.”"]], [["\"Yes, I will.", "“아니요, 할 거예요."], ["I understand what you mean; but yet I will.\"", "무슨 뜻으로 하시는 말씀인지는 알아요. 그래도 저는 할 거예요.”"]], [["\"Good-by, Mrs. Dale,\"", "“안녕히 계십시오, 데일 부인.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Good-by, John.", "“잘 가거라, 존."], ["If it could have been otherwise with her, you should have had all my best wishes in the matter.", "저 애 마음이 달랐더라면 이 일에 내 온 마음의 축복을 다 주었을 게다."], ["I would have loved you dearly as my son; and I will love you now.\"", "너를 내 아들처럼 아꼈을 게고, 지금도 너를 아끼마.”"], ["Then she put up her lips and kissed his face.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 입술을 내밀어 그의 얼굴에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["\"And so will I love you,\"", "“저도 당신을 아낄 거예요.”"], ["said Lily, giving him her hand again.", "릴리가 다시 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["He looked longingly into her face as though he had thought it possible that she also might kiss him: then he pressed her hand to his lips, and without speaking any further farewell, took up his hat and left the room.", "그는 그녀도 자기에게 입을 맞춰 줄지 모른다고 생각하기라도 한 듯 애타는 눈길로 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그러고는 그녀의 손을 제 입술에 눌러 대고, 더는 아무런 작별 인사도 하지 않은 채 모자를 집어 들고 방을 나섰다."]], [["\"Poor fellow!\"", "“가엾은 사람.”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"They should not have let him come,\"", "“저이를 오게 하지 말았어야 했어요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"But they don't understand.", "“하지만 그분들은 몰라요."], ["They think that I have lost a toy, and they mean to be good-natured, and to give me another.\"", "제가 장난감을 하나 잃었으니 착한 마음으로 다른 걸 하나 주면 된다고 생각하는 거예요.”"], ["Very shortly after that Lily went away by herself, and sat alone for hours; and when she joined her mother again at tea-time, nothing further was said of John Eames's visit.", "그로부터 얼마 지나지 않아 릴리는 혼자 자리를 떠 몇 시간이나 홀로 앉아 있었다. 그리고 차 마실 시간에 다시 어머니와 어울렸을 때, 존 임스의 방문에 대해서는 더 이상 아무 말도 오가지 않았다."]], [["He made his way out by the front door, and through the churchyard, and in this way on to the field through which he had asked Lily to walk with him.", "그는 앞문으로 나와 교회 묘지를 지나, 릴리에게 함께 걷자고 청했던 그 들판으로 나섰다."], ["He hardly began to think of what had passed till he had left the squire's house behind him.", "그는 지주의 집을 뒤로 하고 나서야 겨우 방금 있었던 일을 생각하기 시작했다."], ["As he made his way through the tombstones he paused and read one, as though it interested him.", "묘비들 사이를 지나며 그는 걸음을 멈추고 마치 흥미가 있기라도 한 듯 비문 하나를 읽었다."], ["He stood a moment under the tower looking up at the clock, and then pulled out his own watch, as though to verify the one by the other.", "종탑 아래 잠시 서서 시계를 올려다보았고, 그런 다음 마치 이쪽 시계로 저쪽 시계를 맞춰 보기라도 하려는 듯 제 회중시계를 꺼냈다."], ["He made, unconsciously, a struggle to drive away from his thoughts the facts of the late scene, and for some five or ten minutes he succeeded.", "그는 방금 있었던 장면을 머릿속에서 몰아내려고 저도 모르게 안간힘을 썼고, 오 분에서 십 분쯤은 그렇게 하는 데 성공했다."], ["He said to himself a word or two about Sir Raffle and his letters, and laughed inwardly as he remembered the figure of Rafferty bringing in the knight's shoes.", "그는 래플 경과 그의 편지들에 대해 한두 마디 혼잣말을 했고, 그 기사 어른의 신발을 들고 들어오던 래퍼티의 모습을 떠올리며 속으로 웃었다."], ["He had gone some half mile upon his way before he ventured to stand still and tell himself that he had failed in the great object of his life.", "반 마일쯤 갔을 무렵에야 그는 감히 멈춰 서서, 제 인생의 크나큰 목적에서 실패했노라고 스스로에게 말할 수 있었다."]], [["Yes; he had failed: and he acknowledged to himself, with bitter reproaches, that he had failed, now and for ever.", "그렇다. 그는 실패했다."], ["He told himself that he had obtruded upon her in her sorrow with an unmannerly love, and rebuked himself as having been not only foolish but ungenerous.", "그리고 쓰디쓴 자책과 함께 그는 이제 영영 실패했음을 스스로 인정했다. 그는 슬픔에 잠긴 그녀에게 무례한 사랑을 들이밀었다고 스스로를 나무랐고, 어리석었을 뿐 아니라 너그럽지도 못했다고 자신을 꾸짖었다."], ["His friend the earl had been wont, in his waggish way, to call him the conquering hero, and had so talked him out of his common sense as to have made him almost think that he would be successful in his suit.", "벗인 백작은 늘 익살맞은 투로 그를 개선하는 영웅이라 불러 왔고, 그를 어찌나 구슬려 놓았던지 그는 제 분별을 잃고 이 구애가 잘되리라고 거의 믿기까지 했다."], ["Now, as he told himself that any such success must have been impossible, he almost hated the earl for having brought him to this condition.", "그런 성공이란 애초에 있을 수 없는 일이었다고 스스로에게 말하는 지금, 그는 자기를 이 지경으로 몰아넣은 백작이 밉기까지 했다."], ["A conquering hero, indeed!", "개선하는 영웅이라니!"], ["How should he manage to sneak back among them all at the Manor House, crestfallen and abject in his misery?", "저택으로 돌아가 그들 틈에 풀 죽고 비참한 몰골로 어떻게 슬그머니 끼어든단 말인가?"], ["Everybody knew the errand on which he had gone, and everybody must know of his failure.", "그가 무슨 일로 갔는지는 다들 알고 있었고, 그가 실패했다는 것도 다들 알게 될 터였다."], ["How could he have been such a fool as to undertake such a task under the eyes of so many lookers-on?", "그 많은 구경꾼의 눈앞에서 그런 일을 떠맡다니 어쩌면 그토록 어리석었을까?"], ["Was it not the case that he had so fondly expected success, as to think only of his triumph in returning, and not of his more probable disgrace?", "성공을 어찌나 굳게 기대했던지 돌아왔을 때의 개선만 생각하고 더 있음직한 망신은 생각하지 않았던 것이 아닌가?"], ["He had allowed others to make a fool of him, and had so made a fool of himself that now all hope and happiness were over for him.", "그는 남들이 자기를 바보로 만들도록 내버려 두었고, 그리하여 스스로를 바보로 만들어 이제 모든 희망과 행복이 끝나 버렸다."], ["How could he escape at once out of the country,--back to London?", "어떻게 하면 당장 이 고장을 벗어나 런던으로 달아날 수 있을까?"], ["How could he get away without saying a word further to any one?", "어떻게 하면 누구에게도 한마디 더 하지 않고 빠져나갈 수 있을까?"], ["That was the thought that at first occupied his mind.", "그것이 처음에 그의 마음을 사로잡은 생각이었다."]], [["He crossed the road at the end of the squire's property, where the parish of Allington divides itself from that of Abbot's Guest in which the earl's house stands, and made his way back along the copse which skirted the field in which they had encountered the bull, into the high woods which were at the back of the park.", "그는 지주의 땅이 끝나는 곳, 곧 앨링턴 교구가 백작의 집이 서 있는 애벗스 게스트 교구와 갈라지는 데서 큰길을 건넜고, 그들이 황소와 마주쳤던 그 들판 가장자리를 따라 난 잡목숲을 지나 공원 뒤편의 높은 숲으로 되짚어 올라갔다."], ["Ah, yes; it had been well for him that he had not come out on horseback.", "아, 그렇다. 말을 타고 나오지 않은 것은 그에게 잘된 일이었다."], ["That ride home along the high road and up to the Manor House stables would, under his present circumstances, have been almost impossible to him.", "지금 같은 처지에서 큰길을 따라 말을 몰아 저택 마구간까지 돌아가는 일은 그로서는 거의 견딜 수 없었을 것이다."], ["As it was, he did not think it possible that he should return to his place in the earl's house.", "사정이 이러하니 백작의 집에 있는 제자리로 돌아갈 수 있으리라고는 생각되지 않았다."], ["How could he pretend to maintain his ordinary demeanour under the eyes of those two old men?", "그 두 노인의 눈앞에서 어떻게 여느 때의 태도를 지키는 척할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["It would be better for him to get home to his mother,--to send a message from thence to the Manor, and then to escape back to London.", "차라리 어머니에게로 가서 거기서 저택에 전갈을 보내고, 그런 다음 런던으로 달아나는 편이 나을 것이었다."], ["So thinking, but with no resolution made, he went on through the woods, and down from the hill back towards the town till he again came to the little bridge over the brook.", "그렇게 생각하면서도 아무런 결심도 내리지 못한 채 그는 숲을 지나 언덕을 내려와 마을 쪽으로 향했고, 이윽고 개울에 놓인 그 작은 다리에 다시 이르렀다."], ["There he stopped and stood a while with his broad hand spread over the letters which he had cut in those early days, so as to hide them from his sight.", "거기서 그는 걸음을 멈추고 한동안 서서, 그 옛날 제가 새겨 놓은 글자들 위에 널따란 손을 펼쳐 눈에 보이지 않게 가렸다."], ["\"What an ass I have been,--always and ever!\"", "“나는 참 바보였어. 언제나, 늘 그랬지!”"], ["he said to himself.", "그는 속으로 말했다."]], [["It was not only of his late disappointment that he was thinking, but of his whole past life.", "그가 생각한 것은 방금의 낙담만이 아니라 제 지난 삶 전체였다."], ["He was conscious of his hobbledehoyhood,--of that backwardness on his part in assuming manhood which had rendered him incapable of making himself acceptable to Lily before she had fallen into the clutches of Crosbie.", "그는 제 풋내기 시절을, 곧 릴리가 크로즈비의 손아귀에 떨어지기 전에 그녀의 마음에 들 만한 사람이 되지 못하게 만들었던, 사내다움을 갖추는 일에서의 그 뒤처짐을 뼈저리게 느꼈다."], ["As he thought of this he declared to himself that if he could meet Crosbie again he would again thrash him,--that he would so belabour him as to send him out of the world, if such sending might possibly be done by fair beating, regardless whether he himself might be called upon to follow him.", "이런 생각을 하다가 그는 크로즈비를 다시 만나기만 하면 다시 한 번 두들겨 패 주겠노라고, 정정당당한 매질로 그런 일이 될 수만 있다면 그자를 이 세상 밖으로 내보내 버릴 만큼 두들겨 주겠노라고, 그리하여 제가 그 뒤를 따라가야 한다 해도 개의치 않겠노라고 스스로에게 다짐했다."], ["Was it not hard that for the two of them,--for Lily and for him also,--there should be such punishment because of the insincerity of that man?", "그 사내의 불성실함 때문에 두 사람에게—릴리에게도 그 자신에게도—이런 벌이 내린다는 것이 가혹하지 않은가?"], ["When he had thus stood upon the bridge for some quarter of an hour, he took out his knife, and, with deep, rough gashes in the wood, cut out Lily's name from the rail.", "그렇게 십오 분쯤 다리 위에 서 있다가 그는 칼을 꺼내, 나무에 깊고 거친 자국을 내며 난간에서 릴리의 이름을 깎아 냈다."]], [["He had hardly finished, and was still looking at the chips as they were being carried away by the stream, when a gentle step came close up to him, and turning round, he saw that Lady Julia was on the bridge.", "그가 막 일을 끝내고 아직 나뭇조각들이 물살에 실려 떠내려가는 것을 바라보고 있는데, 조용한 발소리가 바로 곁까지 다가왔다. 돌아보니 레이디 줄리아가 다리 위에 있었다."], ["She was close to him, and had already seen his handiwork.", "그녀는 그의 바로 옆에 있었고, 그가 한 일을 이미 보아 버린 뒤였다."], ["\"Has she offended you, John?\"", "“그 애가 당신 마음을 상하게 했나요, 존?”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, Lady Julia!\"", "“아, 레이디 줄리아!”"]], [["\"Has she offended you?\"", "“그 애가 당신 마음을 상하게 했나요?”"]], [["\"She has refused me, and it is all over.\"", "“저를 거절했습니다. 이제 다 끝났습니다.”"]], [["\"It may be that she has refused you, and that yet it need not be all over.", "“그 애가 당신을 거절했을지는 몰라도, 그렇다고 다 끝나야 하는 건 아니에요."], ["I am sorry that you have cut out the name, John.", "그 이름을 깎아 내다니 안타깝군요, 존."], ["Do you mean to cut it out from your heart?\"", "그 이름을 당신 마음에서도 도려낼 작정인가요?”"]], [["\"Never.", "“결코 아닙니다."], ["I would if I could, but I never shall.\"", "그럴 수만 있다면 그러겠지만, 저는 결코 그러지 못할 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Keep to it as to a great treasure.", "“큰 보물처럼 간직하세요."], ["It will be a joy to you in after years, and not a sorrow.", "훗날 그것은 당신에게 슬픔이 아니라 기쁨이 될 거예요."], ["To have loved truly, even though you shall have loved in vain, will be a consolation when you are as old as I am.", "참되게 사랑해 본 일은, 설령 헛되이 사랑한 것이라 해도, 당신이 저만큼 나이가 들었을 때 위로가 될 거예요."], ["It is something to have had a heart.\"", "마음이라는 것을 지녀 보았다는 건 대단한 일이니까요.”"]], [["\"I don't know.", "“모르겠습니다."], ["I wish that I had none.\"", "저는 차라리 마음 같은 게 없었으면 좋겠습니다.”"]], [["\"And, John;--I can understand her feeling now; and indeed, I thought all through that you were asking her too soon; but the time may yet come when she will think better of your wishes.\"", "“그리고 존, 저는 이제 그 애의 심정을 알겠어요. 사실 저는 처음부터 당신이 너무 일찍 청한다고 생각했답니다. 하지만 그 애가 당신의 바람을 좋게 여기게 될 날이 아직 올지도 몰라요.”"]], [["\"No, no; never.", "“아니요, 아닙니다."], ["I begin to know her now.\"", "결코 그럴 일은 없습니다. 이제야 저는 그이를 알겠습니다.”"]], [["\"If you can be constant in your love you may win her yet.", "“당신이 한결같이 사랑할 수 있다면 아직 그 애를 얻을 수도 있어요."], ["Remember how young she is; and how young you both are.", "그 애가 얼마나 어린지 생각해 보세요. 그리고 두 분 다 얼마나 젊은지도요."], ["Come again in two years'", "두 해 뒤에 다시 오세요."], ["time, and then, when you have won her, you shall tell me that I have been a good old woman to you both.\"", "그리고 그 애를 얻거든, 제가 두 분에게 좋은 늙은이 노릇을 했다고 저에게 말해 주세요.”"]], [["\"I shall never win her, Lady Julia.\"", "“저는 결코 그이를 얻지 못할 겁니다, 레이디 줄리아.”"], ["As he spoke these last words the tears were running down his cheeks, and he was weeping openly in presence of his companion.", "이 마지막 말을 하는 동안 그의 뺨에는 눈물이 흘러내렸고, 그는 동행 앞에서 드러내 놓고 울고 있었다."], ["It was well for him that she had come upon him in his sorrow.", "그녀가 슬픔에 잠긴 그를 이렇게 마주친 것은 그에게 잘된 일이었다."], ["When he once knew that she had seen his tears, he could pour out to her the whole story of his grief; and as he did so she led him back quietly to the house.", "그녀가 제 눈물을 보았다는 것을 알고 나자 그는 그녀에게 제 슬픔의 이야기를 모조리 쏟아 놓을 수 있었다. 그리고 그가 그렇게 하는 동안 그녀는 조용히 그를 집으로 데리고 돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER LV. NOT VERY FIE FIE AFTER ALL.", "# 제55장. 결국 그리 망측한 일은 아니었다"]], [["It will perhaps be remembered that terrible things had been foretold as about to happen between the Hartletop and Omnium families.", "하틀탑 집안과 옴니엄 집안 사이에 무시무시한 일이 벌어지리라는 예언이 있었다는 것을 아마 독자들은 기억할 것이다."], ["Lady Dumbello had smiled whenever Mr. Plantagenet Palliser had spoken to her.", "레이디 덤벨로는 플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨가 말을 걸 때마다 미소를 지었다."], ["Mr. Palliser had confessed to himself that politics were not enough for him, and that Love was necessary to make up the full complement of his happiness.", "팰리서 씨는 정치만으로는 제게 넉넉하지 않으며 행복의 온전한 몫을 채우려면 사랑이 있어야 한다고 스스로에게 고백했다."], ["Lord Dumbello had frowned latterly when his eyes fell on the tall figure of the duke's heir; and the duke himself,--that potentate, generally so mighty in his silence,--the duke himself had spoken.", "덤벨로 경은 근래 들어 공작의 상속인의 훤칠한 모습에 눈길이 닿을 때마다 낯을 찌푸렸다. 그리고 공작 본인이—평소 그 침묵으로 그토록 위엄이 서던 그 권세가가—공작 본인이 입을 열었다."], ["Lady De Courcy and Lady Clandidlem were, both of them, absolutely certain that the thing had been fully arranged.", "드 코시 백작부인과 레이디 클랜디들럼은 둘 다 그 일이 이미 완전히 짜여 있다고 철석같이 믿고 있었다."], ["I am, therefore, perfectly justified in stating that the world was talking about the loves,--the illicit loves,--of Mr. Palliser and Lady Dumbello.", "그러므로 세상이 팰리서 씨와 레이디 덤벨로의 사랑을—그 부정한 사랑을—입에 올리고 있었다고 내가 말한다 해도 나는 조금도 지나칠 것이 없다."]], [["And the talking of the world found its way down to that respectable country parsonage in which Lady Dumbello had been born, and from which she had been taken away to those noble halls which she now graced by her presence.", "그리고 세상의 그 입방아는 레이디 덤벨로가 태어난 그 점잖은 시골 목사관까지 흘러 내려갔으니, 그곳은 그녀가 이제 제 존재로 빛내고 있는 그 귀한 저택들로 실려 가기 전에 살던 곳이었다."], ["The talking of the world was heard at Plumstead Episcopi, where still lived Archdeacon Grantly, the lady's father; and was heard also at the deanery of Barchester, where lived the lady's aunt and grandfather.", "세상의 입방아는 그 부인의 아버지인 그랜틀리 부주교가 여태 살고 있는 플럼스테드 에피스코피에서도 들렸고, 그 부인의 이모와 외조부가 살고 있는 바체스터의 수석사제관에서도 들렸다."], ["By whose ill-mannered tongue the rumour was spread in these ecclesiastical regions it boots not now to tell.", "이 교회의 고장들에 그 소문을 퍼뜨린 것이 누구의 버릇없는 혀였는지는 이제 와서 굳이 말할 것이 없다."], ["But it may be remembered that Courcy Castle was not far from Barchester, and that Lady De Courcy was not given to hide her lights under a bushel.", "다만 코시 성이 바체스터에서 멀지 않았다는 것과, 드 코시 백작부인이 제 등불을 됫박 아래 감추어 두는 사람이 아니었다는 것은 기억해 둘 만하다."]], [["It was a terrible rumour.", "무서운 소문이었다."], ["To what mother must not such a rumour respecting her daughter be very terrible?", "제 딸에 관한 그런 소문이 무섭지 않을 어머니가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["In no mother's ears could it have sounded more frightfully than it did in those of Mrs. Grantly.", "그 소문이 그랜틀리 부인의 귀에 울린 것보다 더 끔찍하게 울릴 어머니의 귀는 없었을 것이다."], ["Lady Dumbello, the daughter, might be altogether worldly; but Mrs. Grantly had never been more than half worldly.", "딸인 레이디 덤벨로는 온통 세속적인 사람이었는지 몰라도, 그랜틀리 부인은 결코 절반 넘게 세속적인 적이 없었다."], ["In one moiety of her character, her habits, and her desires, she had been wedded to things good in themselves,--to religion, to charity, and to honest-hearted uprightness.", "그 성품과 습관과 바람의 한쪽 절반에서 그녀는 그 자체로 선한 것들에—신앙에, 자선에, 정직한 마음의 올곧음에—맺어져 있었다."], ["It is true that the circumstances of her life had induced her to serve both God and Mammon, and that, therefore, she had gloried greatly in the marriage of her daughter with the heir of a marquis.", "살아온 형편이 그녀로 하여금 하느님과 재물을 함께 섬기게 만든 것은 사실이고, 그리하여 제 딸이 후작의 상속인과 혼인한 것을 크게 자랑스러워한 것도 사실이다."], ["She had revelled in the aristocratic elevation of her child, though she continued to dispense books and catechisms with her own hands to the children of the labourers of Plumstead Episcopi.", "그녀는 제 자식이 귀족의 높은 자리로 올라선 것을 마음껏 누리면서도, 플럼스테드 에피스코피의 일꾼들의 아이들에게 제 손으로 책과 교리문답서를 나누어 주는 일은 그대로 이어 갔다."], ["When Griselda first became Lady Dumbello the mother feared somewhat lest her child should find herself unequal to the exigencies of her new position.", "그리젤다가 처음 레이디 덤벨로가 되었을 때 어머니는 제 아이가 새 자리가 요구하는 것들을 감당하지 못할까 봐 얼마쯤 겁을 냈다."], ["But the child had proved herself more than equal to them, and had mounted up to a dizzy height of success, which brought to the mother great glory and great fear also.", "그러나 아이는 그것들을 감당하고도 남는다는 것을 스스로 보여 주었고, 아찔한 높이의 성공까지 올라가 어머니에게 큰 영광과 함께 큰 두려움도 함께 가져다주었다."], ["She delighted to think that her Griselda was great even among the daughters of marquises; but she trembled as she reflected how deadly would be the fall from such a height--should there ever be a fall!", "그녀는 제 그리젤다가 후작의 딸들 가운데서도 대단하다고 생각하면 기뻤다. 그러나 그런 높이에서 떨어지는 일이 있다면—혹시라도 떨어지는 일이 있다면!—그 추락이 얼마나 치명적일까를 곱씹으면 몸이 떨렸다."]], [["But she had never dreamed of such a fall as this!", "그러나 이런 추락은 꿈에도 그려 본 적이 없었다!"], ["She would have said,--indeed, she often had said,--to the archdeacon that Griselda's religious principles were too firmly fixed to be moved by outward worldly matters; signifying, it may be, her conviction that that teaching of Plumstead Episcopi had so fastened her daughter into a groove, that all the future teaching of Hartlebury would not suffice to undo the fastenings.", "그녀는 부주교에게 말하곤 했다—실제로 자주 말했다—그리젤다의 신앙의 원칙은 바깥세상의 일 따위에 흔들리기에는 너무나 굳게 박혀 있다고. 그것은 아마도 플럼스테드 에피스코피의 가르침이 제 딸을 하나의 홈에 단단히 끼워 놓았으므로 하틀베리의 앞날의 가르침을 다 합쳐도 그 끼움을 풀어내지는 못하리라는 그녀의 확신을 드러낸 말이었으리라."], ["When she had thus boasted no such idea as that of her daughter running from her husband's house had ever come upon her; but she had alluded to vices of a nature kindred to that vice,--to vices into which other aristocratic ladies sometimes fell, who had been less firmly grooved; and her boastings had amounted to this,--that she herself had so successfully served God and Mammon together, that her child might go forth and enjoy all worldly things without risk of damage to things heavenly.", "그렇게 자랑을 늘어놓을 때 제 딸이 남편의 집을 뛰쳐나간다는 따위의 생각은 한 번도 그녀에게 떠오른 적이 없었다. 다만 그런 죄와 비슷한 성질의 죄들을—덜 단단히 홈에 끼워진 다른 귀족 부인들이 이따금 빠지곤 하는 죄들을—염두에 두고 한 말이었을 뿐이다."], ["Then came upon her this rumour.", "그리고 그녀의 자랑을 간추리면 이런 것이었다. 곧, 자기가 하느님과 재물을 함께 섬기는 일을 하도 잘 해냈으니 제 자식은 세상의 온갖 것을 누리러 나가도 하늘의 것들을 다치게 할 위험이 없다는 것."], ["The archdeacon told her in a hoarse whisper that he had been recommended to look to it, that it was current through the world that Griselda was about to leave her husband.", "그런데 이 소문이 그녀에게 닥쳤다. 부주교가 쉰 목소리로 소곤거리며 말하기를, 이 일을 살펴보라는 언질을 받았노라고, 그리젤다가 남편을 떠나려 한다는 말이 세상에 파다하다고 했다."]], [["\"Nothing on earth shall make me believe it,\"", "“이 세상 무엇으로도 나는 그 말을 믿지 않을 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["But she sat alone in her drawing-room afterwards and trembled.", "그러나 그 뒤 그녀는 응접실에 혼자 앉아 몸을 떨었다."], ["Then came her sister, Mrs. Arabin, the dean's wife, over to the parsonage, and in half-hidden words told the same story.", "이윽고 그녀의 동생인 수석 사제의 부인 애러빈 부인이 목사관으로 건너와, 반쯤 감추는 말로 같은 이야기를 전했다."], ["She had heard it from Mrs. Proudie, the bishop's wife.", "그녀는 그것을 주교의 부인인 프라우디 부인에게서 들었다고 했다."], ["\"That woman is as false as the father of falsehoods,\"", "“그 여자는 거짓의 아비만큼이나 거짓된 사람이에요.”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["But she trembled the more; and as she prepared her parish work, could think of nothing but her child.", "그러나 그녀는 더욱더 떨었다. 그리고 교구 일을 챙기면서도 제 자식 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수 없었다."], ["What would be all her life to come, what would have been all that was past of her life, if this thing should happen to her?", "만일 이런 일이 제 아이에게 닥친다면 앞으로 남은 삶은 다 무엇이 되며, 지나온 삶은 또 다 무엇이 되겠는가?"], ["She would not believe it; but yet she trembled the more as she thought of her daughter's exaltation, and remembered that such things had been done in that world to which Griselda now belonged.", "그녀는 그것을 믿지 않으려 했다. 그러면서도 딸이 올라선 그 높이를 떠올리고, 이제 그리젤다가 속한 그 세상에서는 그런 일들이 실제로 있어 왔음을 되새기면 더욱더 떨렸다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["would it not have been better for them if they had not raised their heads so high!", "그토록 머리를 높이 쳐들지 않았더라면 그들에게 더 낫지 않았을까!"], ["And she walked out alone among the tombs of the neighbouring churchyard, and stood over the grave in which had been laid the body of her other daughter.", "그리고 그녀는 홀로 이웃 교회 묘지의 무덤들 사이를 걸어 나가, 또 하나의 딸의 주검이 뉘어져 있는 무덤가에 섰다."], ["Could be it that the fate of that one had been the happier.", "그 아이의 운명이 오히려 더 행복했던 것일 수도 있을까."]], [["Very few words were spoken on the subject between her and the archdeacon, and yet it seemed agreed among them that something should be done.", "그 일을 두고 그녀와 부주교 사이에 오간 말은 아주 적었으나, 그래도 무언가 손을 써야 한다는 데에는 둘 사이에 뜻이 모인 듯했다."], ["He went up to London, and saw his daughter,--not daring, however, to mention such a subject.", "그는 런던으로 올라가 딸을 만났다. 그러나 그런 이야기를 꺼낼 엄두는 내지 못했다."], ["Lord Dumbello was cross with him, and very uncommunicative.", "덤벨로 경은 그에게 퉁명스러웠고 도무지 입을 열지 않았다."], ["Indeed both the archdeacon and Mrs. Grantly had found that their daughter's house was not comfortable to them, and as they were sufficiently proud among their own class they had not cared to press themselves on the hospitality of their son-in-law.", "사실 부주교와 그랜틀리 부인은 딸의 집이 저희에게 편하지 않다는 것을 진작에 알고 있었고, 저희 계급 안에서는 자존심이 충분히 높은 사람들이었으므로 사위의 손님 대접에 굳이 매달릴 생각이 없었다."], ["But he had been able to perceive that all was not right in the house in Carlton Gardens.", "그러나 그는 칼턴 가든스의 그 집에 무언가 온전하지 않은 것이 있음을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["Lord Dumbello was not gracious with his wife, and there was something in the silence, rather than in the speech, of men, which seemed to justify the report which had reached him.", "덤벨로 경은 아내에게 살갑지 않았고, 사내들의 말보다도 오히려 그 침묵 속에 저에게 닿은 그 소문을 그럴듯하게 만드는 무엇인가가 있었다."]], [["\"He is there oftener than he should be,\"", "“그 사람이 거기 드나드는 것이 마땅한 정도보다 잦소.”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."], ["\"And I am sure of this, at least, that Dumbello does not like it.\"", "“그리고 적어도 이것만은 내가 확신하오. 덤벨로가 그것을 좋아하지 않는다는 것 말이오.”"]], [["\"I will write to her,\"", "“제가 그 애에게 편지를 쓰겠어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly at last.", "마침내 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["\"I am still her mother;--I will write to her.", "“저는 아직도 그 애의 어미예요. 제가 그 애에게 편지를 쓰겠어요."], ["It may be that she does not know what people say of her.\"", "사람들이 그 애를 두고 무어라 하는지 그 애가 모르고 있는 것일 수도 있으니까요.”"]], [["And Mrs. Grantly did write.", "그리고 그랜틀리 부인은 정말로 편지를 썼다."]], [["Plumstead, April, 186--.", "플럼스테드, 186-년 사월."]], [["It seems sometimes that you have been moved so far away from me that I have hardly a right to concern myself more in the affairs of your daily life, and I know that it is impossible that you should refer to me for advice or sympathy, as you would have done had you married some gentleman of our own standing.", "때때로 나는 네가 나에게서 너무 멀리 옮겨 가 버려서, 이제는 내가 네 나날의 살림살이에 더 마음을 쓸 권리조차 거의 없는 듯한 느낌이 들어요. 그리고 네가 우리와 같은 지체의 신사와 혼인했더라면 그러했을 것처럼 나에게 조언이나 위로를 구하는 일이란 이제 있을 수 없다는 것도 알고 있어요."], ["But I am quite sure that my child does not forget her mother, or fail to look back upon her mother's love; and that she will allow me to speak to her if she be in trouble, as I would to any other child whom I had loved and cherished.", "그러나 내 아이가 제 어미를 잊지 않았으리라는 것, 어미의 사랑을 돌아보지 않을 리 없다는 것, 그리고 그 아이가 곤경에 처했다면 내가 사랑하고 아껴 기른 다른 어느 자식에게 말하듯 그 아이에게 말하는 것을 허락해 주리라는 것만은 나도 아주 확신해요."], ["I pray God that I may be wrong in supposing that such trouble is near you.", "그런 곤경이 네 가까이에 있다는 내 짐작이 부디 틀린 것이기를 하느님께 빌어요."], ["If I am so you will forgive me my solicitude.", "만일 그러하다면 이 어미의 노파심을 너그러이 보아 주어요."]], [["Rumours have reached us from more than one quarter that--Oh!", "한 군데가 아닌 여러 곳에서 우리에게 소문이 닿았어요. 그것이—아!"], ["Griselda, I hardly know in what words to conceal and yet to declare that which I have to write.", "그리젤다야, 내가 써야 할 것을 감추면서 동시에 밝히자면 어떤 말을 골라야 할지 나는 도무지 모르겠어요."], ["They say that you are intimate with Mr. Palliser, the nephew of the duke, and that your husband is much offended.", "사람들이 말하기를, 네가 공작의 조카인 팰리서 씨와 가까이 지내며 네 남편이 몹시 언짢아하고 있다는 거예요."], ["Perhaps I had better tell you all, openly, cautioning you not to suppose that I have believed it.", "아무래도 내가 다 터놓고 말하는 편이 낫겠지요. 다만 내가 그것을 믿었다고는 생각하지 말아 주어요."], ["They say that it is thought that you are going to put yourself under Mr. Palliser's protection.", "사람들이 말하기를, 네가 팰리서 씨의 보호 아래로 몸을 옮기려 한다는 이야기가 돌고 있다는 거예요."], ["My dearest child, I think you can imagine with what an agony I write these words,--with what terrible grief I must have been oppressed before I could have allowed myself to entertain the thoughts which have produced them.", "내 가장 사랑하는 아이야, 내가 어떤 괴로움 속에서 이 말들을 쓰고 있는지—이 말들을 낳은 그 생각을 내 마음에 들이기까지 얼마나 무서운 슬픔에 짓눌려야 했는지 너도 짐작할 수 있으리라 생각해요."], ["Such things are said openly in Barchester, and your father, who has been in town and has seen you, feels himself unable to tell me that my mind may be at rest.", "그런 이야기가 바체스터에서 버젓이 오가고 있고, 도시에 가서 너를 만나고 온 네 아버지도 이제 마음을 놓아도 좋다는 말을 나에게 해 주지 못하고 있어요."]], [["I will not say to you a word as to the injury in a worldly point of view which would come to you from any rupture with your husband.", "네 남편과 사이가 갈라진다면 세상의 눈으로 보아 네가 입을 손해가 어떠할지에 대해서는 나는 한마디도 하지 않겠어요."], ["I believe that you can see what would be the effect of so terrible a step quite as plainly as I can show it you.", "그토록 무서운 걸음이 어떤 결과를 낳을지는 내가 짚어 보여 주는 것만큼이나 너도 또렷이 볼 수 있으리라 믿어요."], ["You would break the heart of your father, and send your mother to her grave;--but it is not even on that that I may most insist.", "너는 네 아버지의 가슴을 무너뜨리고 네 어미를 무덤으로 보내게 되겠지요. 그러나 내가 가장 힘주어 말하려는 것은 그것조차 아니에요."], ["It is this,--that you would offend your God by the worst sin that a woman can commit, and cast yourself into a depth of infamy in which repentance before God is almost impossible, and from which escape before man is not permitted.", "이것이에요—네가 여자로서 저지를 수 있는 가장 나쁜 죄로 네 하느님께 죄를 짓게 되고, 하느님 앞에서 뉘우치는 일이 거의 불가능하며 사람들 앞에서 벗어나는 일은 허락되지 않는 그 치욕의 깊은 구렁으로 스스로를 던지게 되리라는 것 말이에요."]], [["I do not believe it, my dearest, dearest child,--my only living daughter; I do not believe what they have said to me.", "나는 그것을 믿지 않아요, 내 가장 사랑하고 또 사랑하는 아이야—이제 내게 살아 있는 오직 하나뿐인 딸아. 나는 사람들이 내게 한 말을 믿지 않아요."], ["But as a mother I have not dared to leave the slander unnoticed.", "그러나 어미로서 그 험담을 못 본 체하고 넘길 엄두가 나지 않았어요."], ["If you will write to me and say that it is not so, you will make me happy again, even though you should rebuke me for my suspicion.", "네가 나에게 편지를 써서 그런 일이 아니라고 말해 준다면, 설령 네가 내 의심을 두고 나를 나무란다 하더라도 나는 다시 행복해질 거예요."]], [["Believe that at all times, and under all circumstances, I am still your loving mother, as I was in other days.", "언제 어느 때든, 어떤 형편에 놓이든, 내가 지난날과 다름없이 여전히 너를 사랑하는 어미임을 믿어 주어요."]], [["We will now go back to Mr. Palliser as he sat in his chambers at the Albany, thinking of his love.", "이제 우리는 올버니의 제 거처에 앉아 사랑을 생각하고 있는 팰리서 씨에게로 돌아가 보자."], ["The duke had cautioned him, and the duke's agent had cautioned him; and he, in spite of his high feeling of independence, had almost been made to tremble.", "공작이 그에게 경고했고, 공작의 관리인도 그에게 경고했다. 그리고 그는 독립을 소중히 여기는 마음이 그토록 높았음에도 하마터면 몸을 떨 뻔했다."], ["All his thousands a year were in the balance, and perhaps everything on which depended his position before the world.", "해마다 들어오던 그 수천 파운드가 모두 저울 위에 놓였고, 어쩌면 그가 세상 앞에서 차지한 자리가 딛고 선 모든 것이 그러했다."], ["But, nevertheless, though he did tremble, he resolved to persevere.", "그러나 그럼에도 그는 몸이 떨리면서도 밀고 나가기로 마음먹었다."], ["Statistics were becoming dry to him, and love was very sweet.", "통계는 그에게 메말라 가고 있었고 사랑은 아주 달콤했다."], ["Statistics, he thought, might be made as enchanting as ever, if only they could be mingled with love.", "통계도 사랑과 섞을 수만 있다면 예전처럼 얼마든지 황홀해질 수 있으리라고 그는 생각했다."], ["The mere idea of loving Lady Dumbello had seemed to give a salt to his life of which he did not now know how to rob himself.", "레이디 덤벨로를 사랑한다는 생각만으로도 그의 삶에는 소금기가 생긴 듯했고, 이제 와서는 그것을 제 손으로 걷어 낼 방도를 알지 못했다."], ["It is true that he had not as yet enjoyed many of the absolute blessings of love, seeing that his conversations with Lady Dumbello had never been warmer than those which have been repeated in these pages; but his imagination had been at work; and now that Lady Dumbello was fully established at her house in Carlton Gardens, he was determined to declare his passion on the first convenient opportunity.", "그가 아직 사랑의 온전한 축복을 그리 많이 누리지 못한 것은 사실이니, 레이디 덤벨로와 나눈 대화라고는 이 책에 옮겨 적힌 것들보다 더 따뜻했던 적이 없었다. 그러나 그의 상상은 부지런히 일하고 있었다."], ["It was sufficiently manifest to him that the world expected him to do so, and that the world was already a little disposed to find fault with the slowness of his proceedings.", "그리고 이제 레이디 덤벨로가 칼턴 가든스의 제 집에 온전히 자리를 잡았으니, 그는 마땅한 기회가 오는 대로 제 연정을 밝히기로 작정했다. 세상이 저에게 그러기를 기다리고 있다는 것, 그리고 세상이 이미 그의 굼뜬 진행을 두고 얼마쯤 탓하려 든다는 것이 그에게는 충분히 뚜렷했다."]], [["He had been once at Carlton Gardens since the season had commenced, and the lady had favoured him with her sweetest smile.", "사교철이 시작된 뒤로 그는 칼턴 가든스에 한 번 다녀왔고, 그 부인은 더없이 고운 미소로 그를 맞아 주었다."], ["But he had only been half a minute alone with her, and during that half-minute had only time to remark that he supposed she would now remain in London for the season.", "그러나 그녀와 단둘이 있었던 것은 반 분뿐이었고, 그 반 분 동안에는 이제 사교철 내내 런던에 머무르시겠지요, 하고 한마디 건넬 겨를밖에 없었다."]], [["\"Oh, yes,\"", "“아, 그럼요.”"], ["she had answered, \"we shall not leave till July.\"", "그녀가 대답했다. “저희는 칠월까지는 떠나지 않아요.”"], ["Nor could he leave till July, because of the exigencies of his statistics.", "그도 통계의 사정 때문에 칠월까지는 떠날 수 없었다."], ["He therefore had before him two, if not three, clear months in which to manoeuvre, to declare his purposes, and prepare for the future events of his life.", "그러니 그의 앞에는 술수를 부리고 제 뜻을 밝히고 앞날의 일을 준비할 온전한 두 달이, 어쩌면 석 달이 놓여 있었다."], ["As he resolved on a certain morning that he would say his first tender word to Lady Dumbello that very night, in the drawing-room of Lady De Courcy, where he knew that he should meet her, a letter came to him by the post.", "어느 날 아침 그가 바로 그날 밤 드 코시 백작부인의 응접실에서—그곳에서 그녀를 만나게 되리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다—레이디 덤벨로에게 첫 다정한 말을 건네리라 마음을 굳혔을 때, 우편으로 편지 한 통이 그에게 왔다."], ["He well knew the hand and the intimation which it would contain.", "그는 그 필적을 잘 알았고 그 안에 담겼을 기별도 잘 알았다."], ["It was from the duke's agent, Mr. Fothergill, and informed him that a certain sum of money had been placed to his credit at his banker's.", "공작의 관리인 포더길 씨에게서 온 것으로, 얼마간의 돈이 그의 거래 은행 계좌에 들어왔음을 알리는 편지였다."], ["But the letter went further, and informed him also that the duke had given his agent to understand that special instructions would be necessary before the next quarterly payment could be made. Mr.", "그러나 편지는 거기서 더 나아가, 다음 분기 지급을 하려면 특별한 지시가 있어야 한다는 뜻을 공작이 관리인에게 일러 두었다는 것까지 알렸다."], ["Fothergill said nothing further, but Mr. Palliser understood it all.", "포더길 씨는 그 이상 아무 말도 하지 않았으나 팰리서 씨는 모두 알아들었다."], ["He felt his blood run cold round his heart; but, nevertheless, he determined that he would not break his word to Lady De Courcy that night.", "그는 심장 둘레로 피가 차갑게 도는 것을 느꼈다. 그러나 그럼에도 그날 밤 드 코시 백작부인과의 약속만은 어기지 않기로 마음먹었다."]], [["And Lady Dumbello received her letter also on the same morning.", "그리고 레이디 덤벨로도 같은 날 아침에 제 편지를 받았다."], ["She was being dressed as she read it, and the maidens who attended her found no cause to suspect that anything in the letter had excited her ladyship.", "그녀는 몸단장을 받으며 그것을 읽었는데, 시중드는 하녀들은 그 편지의 무엇인가가 마님을 흔들어 놓았다고 의심할 만한 낌새를 조금도 찾지 못했다."], ["Her ladyship was not often excited, though she was vigilant in exacting from them their utmost cares.", "마님은 좀처럼 흔들리는 법이 없었다. 물론 하녀들에게서 더없이 정성스러운 손길을 받아 내는 데에는 빈틈이 없었지만."], ["She read her letter, however, very carefully, and as she sat beneath the toilet implements of her maidens thought deeply of the tidings which had been brought to her.", "그래도 그녀는 편지를 아주 꼼꼼히 읽었고, 하녀들의 화장 도구 아래 앉아 있는 동안 저에게 전해진 그 소식을 깊이 생각했다."], ["She was angry with no one;--she was thankful to no one.", "그녀는 누구에게도 화가 나지 않았고, 누구에게도 고맙지 않았다."], ["She felt no special love for any person concerned in the matter.", "그 일에 얽힌 어느 누구에게도 각별한 사랑을 느끼지 않았다."], ["Her heart did not say, \"Oh, my lord and husband!\"", "그녀의 마음은 “오, 나의 주인이자 남편이여!”"], ["or, \"Oh, my lover!\"", "하고도, “오, 나의 연인이여!”"], ["or, \"Oh, my mother, the friend of my childhood!\"", "하고도, “오, 나의 어머니, 어린 날의 벗이여!”"], ["But she became aware that matter for thought had been brought before her, and she did think.", "하고도 말하지 않았다. 다만 생각할 거리가 제 앞에 놓였다는 것을 알아차렸고, 그래서 생각했다."], ["\"Send my love to Lord Dumbello,\"", "“덤벨로 경께 내 사랑을 전하고,”"], ["she said, when the operations were nearly completed, \"and tell him that I shall be so glad to see him if he will come to me while I am at breakfast.\"", "손질이 거의 끝나 갈 무렵 그녀가 말했다. “내가 아침을 드는 동안에 와 주시면 무척 반갑겠다고 전해라.”"]], [["\"Yes, my lady.\"", "“예, 마님.”"], ["And then the message came back: \"His lordship would be with her ladyship certainly.\"", "그러고서 답이 돌아왔다. “나리께서 마님께 꼭 가시겠답니다.”"]], [["\"Gustavus,\"", "“구스타부스,”"], ["she said, as soon as she had seated herself discreetly in her chair, \"I have had a letter from my mother, which you had better read;\"", "조심스레 제 의자에 자리를 잡자마자 그녀가 말했다. “어머니한테서 편지가 왔는데, 당신이 읽어 보시는 게 좋겠어요.”"], ["and she handed to him the document.", "그러고서 그 문서를 그에게 건넸다."], ["\"I do not know what I have done to deserve such suspicions from her; but she lives in the country, and has probably been deceived by ill-natured people.", "“내가 무슨 짓을 했기에 어머니한테서 이런 의심을 받아야 하는지 모르겠어요. 하지만 어머니는 시골에 사시니 심술궂은 사람들에게 속으신 것이겠지요."], ["At any rate you must read it, and tell me what I should do.\"", "아무튼 당신이 읽어 보시고, 내가 어떻게 해야 할지 말해 주세요.”"]], [["We may predicate from this that Mr. Palliser's chance of being able to shipwreck himself upon that rock was but small, and that he would, in spite of himself, be saved from his uncle's anger.", "이로 미루어 우리는 팰리서 씨가 그 바위에 제 배를 부딪쳐 난파할 기회란 아주 작았으리라는 것, 그리고 그가 제 뜻과는 달리 숙부의 노여움에서 구제되리라는 것을 미리 짚어 볼 수 있다."], ["Lord Dumbello took the letter and read it very slowly, standing, as he did so, with his back to the fire.", "덤벨로 경은 편지를 받아 들고 벽난로에 등을 댄 채 서서 아주 천천히 읽었다."], ["He read it very slowly, and his wife, though she never turned her face directly upon his, could perceive that he became very red, that he was fluttered and put beyond himself, and that his answer was not ready.", "아주 천천히 읽었고, 그의 아내는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 마주 보지는 않으면서도 그가 몹시 붉어지는 것을, 허둥거리며 제 정신을 놓아 버린 것을, 대답이 얼른 나오지 않는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그녀는 지난 석 달 동안 그가 저에게 굴어 온 태도가 예전의 버릇과는 사뭇 달라졌다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["She was well aware that his conduct to her during the last three months had been much altered from his former usages; that he had been rougher with her in his speech when alone, and less courteous in his attention when in society; but she had made no complaint or spoken a word to show him that she had marked the change.", "단둘이 있을 때는 말이 거칠어졌고 사람들 속에서는 살피는 정성이 덜해졌다. 그러나 그녀는 그 변화를 알아차렸다는 것을 보이려고 투정을 하거나 한마디를 던진 적이 없었다."], ["She had known, moreover, the cause of his altered manner, and having considered much, had resolved that she would live it down.", "게다가 그녀는 그가 태도를 바꾼 까닭도 알고 있었고, 두루 생각한 끝에 그것을 견디어 눌러 없애기로 마음먹은 터였다."], ["She had declared to herself that she had done no deed and spoken no word that justified suspicion, and therefore she would make no change in her ways, or show herself to be conscious that she was suspected.", "그녀는 의심받을 만한 일을 한 적도 그런 말을 한 적도 없다고 스스로에게 못 박았으므로, 제 처신을 바꾸지도 않을 것이고 저가 의심받고 있음을 알고 있다는 티도 내지 않을 것이었다."], ["But now,--having her mother's letter in her hand,--she could bring him to an explanation without making him aware that she had ever thought that he had been jealous of her.", "그러나 이제—어머니의 편지를 손에 쥔 지금—그녀는 저가 그의 질투를 눈치챈 적이 있다는 것을 그에게 알리지 않고도 그를 해명의 자리로 끌어낼 수 있었다."], ["To her, her mother's letter was a great assistance.", "그녀에게 어머니의 편지는 큰 도움이었다."], ["It justified a scene like this, and enabled her to fight her battle after her own fashion.", "그것은 이런 장면을 벌일 구실이 되어 주었고, 제 방식대로 싸움을 치를 수 있게 해 주었다."], ["As for eloping with any Mr. Palliser, and giving up the position which she had won;--no, indeed!", "팰리서 씨 따위와 눈이 맞아 달아나 저가 얻어 낸 자리를 내던진다니—어림도 없는 소리였다!"], ["She had been fastened in her grooves too well for that!", "그러기에는 그녀가 제 홈에 너무나 단단히 끼워져 있었다!"], ["Her mother, in entertaining any fear on such a subject, had shown herself to be ignorant of the solidity of her daughter's character.", "그런 일로 조금이라도 겁을 낸 그 어머니는 제 딸의 성품이 얼마나 단단한지 모르고 있음을 스스로 드러낸 셈이었다."]], [["\"Well, Gustavus,\"", "“그래서, 구스타부스,”"], ["she said at last.", "마침내 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"You must say what answer I shall make, or whether I shall make any answer.\"", "“내가 어떤 답장을 써야 할지, 아니면 답장을 하기는 해야 할지 당신이 말해 주셔야겠어요.”"], ["But he was not even yet ready to instruct her.", "그러나 그는 그때까지도 그녀에게 일러 줄 채비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["So he unfolded the letter and read it again, and she poured out for herself a cup of tea.", "그래서 그는 편지를 다시 펴서 또 한 번 읽었고, 그녀는 제 손으로 차를 한 잔 따랐다."]], [["\"It's a very serious matter,\"", "“아주 심각한 일이오.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, it is serious; I could not but think such a letter from my mother to be serious.", "“예, 심각한 일이지요. 어머니한테서 온 그런 편지를 심각하게 여기지 않을 수는 없었어요."], ["Had it come from any one else I doubt whether I should have troubled you; unless, indeed, it had been from any as near to you as she is to me.", "다른 사람한테서 온 것이었다면 당신을 성가시게 해 드렸을지 의문이에요. 하기야 어머니가 나에게 가까운 만큼 당신에게 가까운 누군가한테서 온 것이었다면 또 몰라도요."], ["As it is, you cannot but feel that I am right.\"", "사정이 이러하니 당신도 내가 옳게 했다고 느끼지 않을 수 없을 거예요.”"]], [["\"Right!", "“옳고말고!"], ["Oh, yes, you are right,--quite right to tell me; you should tell me everything.", "아, 그럼, 당신이 옳소—나한테 말한 것은 아주 잘한 일이오. 당신은 나에게 무엇이든 말해야 하오."], ["D---- them!\"", "빌어먹을 것들 같으니!”"], ["But whom he meant to condemn he did not explain.", "그러나 그가 누구를 저주하려 한 것인지는 밝히지 않았다."]], [["\"I am above all things averse to cause you trouble,\"", "“나는 무엇보다도 당신에게 성가신 일을 끼치는 것이 싫어요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"I have seen some little things of late--\"", "“요즘 들어 자잘한 일들을 몇 가지 보아 오기는 했지만—”"]], [["\"Has he ever said anything to you?\"", "“그 사람이 당신한테 무슨 말이라도 한 적이 있소?”"]], [["\"Who,--Mr. Palliser?", "“누구요—팰리서 씨 말인가요?"], ["Never a word.\"", "한마디도 없었어요.”"]], [["\"He has hinted at nothing of this kind?\"", "“이런 투로 넌지시 비친 적도 없단 말이오?”"]], [["\"Never a word.", "“한마디도 없었어요."], ["Had he done so, I must have made you understand that he could not have been allowed again into my drawing-room.\"", "그랬더라면 그이가 다시는 내 응접실에 들어올 수 없다는 것을 내가 당신께 알려 드렸을 거예요.”"], ["Then again he read the letter, or pretended to do so.", "그러고 나서 그는 다시 편지를 읽었다. 아니면 읽는 시늉을 했다."]], [["\"Your mother means well,\"", "“당신 어머니는 좋은 뜻으로 하신 거요.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes, she means well.", "“아, 그럼요, 좋은 뜻으로 하신 거지요."], ["She has been foolish to believe the tittle-tattle that has reached her,--very foolish to oblige me to give you this annoyance.\"", "귀에 들어온 수군거림을 곧이곧대로 믿으신 것이 어리석으셨어요—당신께 이런 성가신 일을 끼치게 만드신 것이 몹시 어리석으셨고요.”"]], [["\"Oh, as for that, I'm not annoyed.", "“아, 그거야 나는 성가시지 않소."], ["By Jove, no.", "맹세코 아니오."], ["Come, Griselda, let us have it all out; other people have said this, and I have been unhappy.", "자, 그리젤다, 죄다 털어놓읍시다. 다른 사람들도 이 말을 했고, 나도 마음이 편치 않았소."], ["Now, you know it all.\"", "이제 당신도 다 알게 되었구려.”"]], [["\"Have I made you unhappy?\"", "“내가 당신 마음을 편치 않게 했나요?”"]], [["\"Well, no; not you.", "“글쎄, 아니오."], ["Don't be hard upon me when I tell you the whole truth.", "당신은 아니오. 내가 사실을 다 털어놓는다고 나를 모질게 몰아세우지는 마오."], ["Fools and brutes have whispered things that have vexed me.", "바보들과 짐승 같은 것들이 나를 언짢게 하는 말을 수군거렸소."], ["They may whisper till the devil fetches them, but they shan't annoy me again.", "그것들이야 악마가 데려갈 때까지 수군거리든 말든, 다시는 나를 성가시게 하지 못할 거요."], ["Give me a kiss, my girl.\"", "자, 입을 맞춰 주오, 여보.”"], ["And he absolutely put out his arms and embraced her.", "그러고서 그는 정말로 두 팔을 벌려 그녀를 안았다."], ["\"Write a good-natured letter to your mother, and ask her to come up for a week in May.", "“당신 어머니께 다정한 편지를 써서 오월에 한 주일쯤 올라오시라고 청하오."], ["That'll be the best thing; and then she'll understand.", "그게 제일 좋겠소. 그러면 그분도 알아들으실 거요."], ["By Jove, it's twelve o'clock.", "아이고, 열두 시로군."], ["Good-by.\"", "잘 있으오.”"]], [["Lady Dumbello was well aware that she had triumphed, and that her mother's letter had been invaluable to her.", "레이디 덤벨로는 저가 이겼다는 것을, 그리고 어머니의 편지가 저에게 더없이 값진 물건이었다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["But it had been used, and therefore she did not read it again.", "그러나 그것은 이미 쓰였으므로 그녀는 다시 읽지 않았다."], ["She ate her breakfast in quiet comfort, looking over a milliner's French circular as she did so; and then, when the time for such an operation had fully come, she got to her writing-table and answered her mother's letter.", "그녀는 조용하고 편안하게 아침을 들면서 모자 가게에서 온 프랑스 안내장을 훑어보았다. 그러고서 그런 일에 알맞은 때가 온전히 이르렀을 무렵 글 쓰는 탁자로 가서 어머니의 편지에 답장을 썼다."]], [["DEAR MAMMA [she said],", "사랑하는 엄마께 [라고 그녀는 썼다],"]], [["I thought it best to show your letter at once to Lord Dumbello.", "엄마의 편지는 곧바로 덤벨로 경에게 보여 드리는 것이 가장 낫겠다고 생각했어요."], ["He said that people would be ill-natured, and seemed to think that the telling of such stories could not be helped.", "그이는 사람들이란 심술궂게 마련이라고 했고, 그런 이야기가 오가는 것은 어쩔 수 없는 일이라고 여기는 듯했어요."], ["As regards you, he was not a bit angry, but said that you and papa had better come to us for a week about the end of next month.", "엄마에 대해서는 조금도 화를 내지 않았고, 다음 달 말께 엄마와 아버지가 한 주일쯤 우리에게 와 계시는 편이 좋겠다고 했어요."], ["Do come.", "꼭 오세요."], ["We are to have rather a large dinner-party on the 23rd.", "23일에는 제법 큰 만찬 모임이 있어요."], ["His Royal Highness is coming, and I think papa would like to meet him.", "전하께서 오시기로 되어 있는데, 아버지도 그분을 뵙고 싶어 하실 것 같아요. 그 아주 높다란 보닛이 죄다 사라진 것을 보셨어요?"], ["Have you observed that those very high bonnets have all gone out: I never liked them; and as I had got a hint from Paris, I have been doing my best to put them down.", "저는 한 번도 그것을 좋아한 적이 없어요. 파리에서 언질을 받은 터라 그것을 눌러 없애려고 힘껏 애써 왔고요."], ["I do hope nothing will prevent your coming.", "부디 아무 일도 엄마가 오시는 것을 막지 않기를 바라요."]], [["Your affectionate daughter,", "엄마를 사랑하는 딸,"]], [["Carlton Gardens, Wednesday.", "칼턴 가든스, 수요일."]], [["Mrs. Grantly was aware, from the moment in which she received the letter, that she had wronged her daughter by her suspicions.", "그랜틀리 부인은 그 편지를 받은 순간부터 제 의심으로 딸에게 못 할 짓을 했음을 알았다."], ["It did not occur to her to disbelieve a word that was said in the letter, or an inference that was implied.", "편지에 적힌 말이나 거기 담긴 뜻을 한마디라도 의심할 생각은 들지 않았다."], ["She had been wrong, and rejoiced that it was so.", "그녀가 틀렸던 것이고, 그녀는 그것이 기뻤다."], ["But nevertheless there was that in the letter which annoyed and irritated her, though she could not explain to herself the cause of her annoyance.", "그러나 그럼에도 그 편지에는 그녀를 성가시게 하고 속을 긁는 무엇인가가 있었는데, 정작 그 성가심의 까닭을 그녀는 스스로에게 설명할 수 없었다."], ["She had thrown all her heart into that which she had written, but in the words which her child had written not a vestige of heart was to be found.", "그녀는 제가 쓴 글에 온 마음을 쏟아부었건만, 제 아이가 쓴 말에서는 마음의 자취라고는 한 조각도 찾을 수 없었다."], ["In that reconciling of God and Mammon which Mrs. Grantly had carried on so successfully in the education of her daughter, the organ had not been required, and had become withered, if not defunct, through want of use.", "그랜틀리 부인이 딸을 기르면서 그토록 훌륭하게 해낸 그 하느님과 재물의 화해에서, 그 기관은 쓸 일이 없었고 그래서 쓰이지 않은 탓에 죽어 버리지는 않았더라도 시들어 버렸던 것이다."]], [["\"We will not go there, I think,\" said Mrs.", "“우리는 거기 가지 않는 게 좋겠어요.”"], ["Grantly, speaking to her husband.", "그랜틀리 부인이 남편에게 말했다."]], [["\"Oh dear, no; certainly not.", "“아이고, 그럼, 절대로 가지 않지."], ["If you want to go to town at all, I will take rooms for you.", "당신이 도시에 나갈 일이 정 있거든 내가 방을 얻어 주겠소."], ["And as for his Royal Highness--!", "그리고 전하로 말하자면—!"], ["I have a great respect for his Royal Highness, but I do not in the least desire to meet him at Dumbello's table.\"", "나는 전하를 매우 존경하오만, 덤벨로의 식탁에서 그분을 뵙고 싶은 마음은 조금도 없소.”"]], [["And so that matter was settled, as regarded the inhabitants of Plumstead Episcopi.", "그리하여 플럼스테드 에피스코피에 사는 이들에 관한 한 그 일은 그렇게 결말이 났다."]], [["And whither did Lord Dumbello betake himself when he left his wife's room in so great a hurry at twelve o'clock?", "그런데 덤벨로 경은 열두 시에 그토록 부랴부랴 아내의 방을 나선 뒤 어디로 갔을까?"], ["Not to the Park, nor to Tattersall's, nor to a Committee-room of the House of Commons, nor yet to the bow-window of his club.", "공원도 아니고, 태터솔스도 아니고, 하원의 위원회실도 아니고, 제 클럽의 내닫이창 자리도 아니었다."], ["But he went straight to a great jeweller's in Ludgate-hill, and there purchased a wonderful green necklace, very rare and curious, heavy with green sparkling drops, with three rows of shining green stones embedded in chaste gold,--a necklace amounting almost to a jewelled cuirass in weight and extent.", "그는 곧장 러드게이트 힐의 큰 보석상으로 가서, 아주 귀하고 진기한 놀라운 초록 목걸이 하나를 샀다. 반짝이는 초록 물방울들이 묵직하게 달리고 빛나는 초록 보석이 정갈한 금에 세 줄로 박힌 것으로—무게와 크기로 말하자면 거의 보석 갑옷에 이르는 목걸이였다."], ["It had been in all the exhibitions, and was very costly and magnificent.", "그것은 온갖 전시회에 다 나왔던 물건이었고, 값도 몹시 비싸고 화려했다."], ["While Lady Dumbello was still dressing in the evening this was brought to her with her lord's love, as his token of renewed confidence; and Lady Dumbello, as she counted the sparkles, triumphed inwardly, telling herself that she had played her cards well.", "저녁에 레이디 덤벨로가 아직 몸단장을 하고 있을 때 이것이 나리의 사랑과 함께, 다시 믿게 되었다는 표시로 그녀에게 전해졌다. 그리고 레이디 덤벨로는 그 반짝이들을 세면서 제가 패를 잘 놀렸다고 스스로에게 이르며 속으로 승리를 맛보았다."]], [["But while she counted the sparkles produced by her full reconciliation with her lord, poor Plantagenet Palliser was still trembling in his ignorance.", "그러나 그녀가 제 나리와의 온전한 화해가 낳은 그 반짝이들을 세고 있는 동안, 가엾은 플랜태저넷 팰리서는 아무것도 모른 채 여전히 떨고 있었다."], ["If only he could have been allowed to see Mrs. Grantly's letter, and the lady's answer, and the lord's present!", "그가 그랜틀리 부인의 편지와 그 부인의 답장과 나리의 선물을 볼 수만 있었더라면!"], ["But no such seeing was vouchsafed to him, and he was carried off in his brougham to Lady De Courcy's house, twittering with expectant love, and trembling with expectant ruin.", "그러나 그런 봄은 그에게 허락되지 않았고, 그는 제 사륜마차에 실려 드 코시 백작부인의 집으로 갔다. 다가올 사랑에 가슴이 재잘거리고, 다가올 파멸에 몸이 떨렸다."], ["To this conclusion he had come at any rate, that if anything was to be done, it should be done now.", "아무튼 그가 다다른 결론은 이것이었다. 무슨 일이든 해야 한다면 지금 해야 한다는 것."], ["He would speak a word of love, and prepare his future in accordance with the acceptance it might receive.", "그는 사랑의 말을 한마디 건넬 것이고, 그 말이 받게 될 응답에 따라 제 앞날을 준비할 것이었다."]], [["Lady De Courcy's rooms were very crowded when he arrived there.", "그가 도착했을 때 드 코시 백작부인의 방들은 몹시 붐볐다."], ["It was the first great crushing party of the season, and all the world had been collected into Portman Square.", "그해 사교철의 첫 대혼잡 모임이었고, 온 세상이 포트먼 스퀘어로 모여들어 있었다."], ["Lady De Courcy was smiling as though her lord had no teeth, as though her eldest son's condition was quite happy, and all things were going well with the De Courcy interests.", "드 코시 백작부인은 제 나리에게 이가 하나도 없기라도 한 듯, 맏아들의 처지가 아주 행복하기라도 한 듯, 드 코시 집안의 이해가 온통 잘 굴러가기라도 한 듯 웃고 있었다."], ["Lady Margaretta was there behind her, bland without and bitter within; and Lady Rosina also, at some further distance, reconciled to this world's vanity and finery because there was to be no dancing.", "레이디 마거리타는 그 뒤에서 겉으로는 상냥하고 속으로는 쓰디쓴 얼굴로 있었고, 레이디 로지나도 조금 떨어진 자리에서 춤판이 없다는 이유로 이 세상의 헛됨과 화려함과 화해한 채 있었다."], ["And the married daughters of the house were there also, striving to maintain their positions on the strength of their undoubted birth, but subjected to some snubbing by the lowness of their absolute circumstances.", "그리고 이 집의 시집간 딸들도 와서, 의심할 바 없는 태생을 밑천 삼아 제 자리를 지키려 애썼으나 실제 형편이 변변찮은 탓에 얼마쯤 홀대를 받고 있었다."], ["Gazebee was there, happy in the absolute fact of his connection with an earl, and blessed with the consideration that was extended to him as an earl's son-in-law.", "게이지비도 와 있었다. 백작과 연이 닿았다는 그 사실 하나만으로도 행복했고, 백작의 사위로서 받는 대접에 흐뭇해했다."], ["And Crosbie, also, was in the rooms,--was present there, though he had sworn to himself that he would no longer dance attendance on the countess, and that he would sever himself away from the wretchedness of the family.", "그리고 크로즈비도 그 방에 있었다—다시는 백작부인의 시중을 들지 않겠다고, 그 집안의 그 초라함에서 저를 잘라 내겠다고 스스로 맹세해 놓고도 그 자리에 와 있었다."], ["But if he gave up them and their ways, what else would then be left to him?", "그러나 그가 그들과 그들의 길을 버린다면 그에게는 그 밖에 무엇이 남는단 말인가?"], ["He had come, therefore, and now stood alone, sullen, in a corner, telling himself that all was vanity.", "그리하여 그는 와 있었고, 이제 한구석에 홀로 뾰로통하게 서서 모든 것이 헛되다고 스스로에게 이르고 있었다. 그렇다."], ["Yes; to the vain all will be vanity; and to the poor of heart all will be poor.", "헛된 자에게는 모든 것이 헛될 것이고, 마음이 가난한 자에게는 모든 것이 가난할 것이다."]], [["Lady Dumbello was there in a small inner room, seated on a couch to which she had been brought on her first arrival at the house, and on which she would remain till she departed.", "레이디 덤벨로는 안쪽의 작은 방에 있었는데, 이 집에 처음 닿았을 때 안내되어 앉은 긴 의자에 그대로 앉아 있었고 떠날 때까지 그 자리에 머무를 참이었다."], ["From time to time some very noble or very elevated personage would come before her and say a word, and she would answer that elevated personage with another word; but nobody had attempted with her the task of conversation.", "이따금 아주 지체 높거나 아주 높은 자리에 오른 인물이 그녀 앞에 와서 한마디를 건네면 그녀는 그 높은 인물에게 또 한마디로 답했다. 그러나 아무도 그녀를 붙들고 대화라는 수고를 벌여 볼 엄두를 내지 않았다."], ["It was understood that Lady Dumbello did not converse,--unless it were occasionally with Mr. Palliser.", "레이디 덤벨로는 대화를 하지 않는다는 것이—이따금 팰리서 씨와 나눌 때가 아니라면—사람들 사이에 통하는 이야기였다."]], [["She knew well that Mr. Palliser was to meet her there.", "그녀는 팰리서 씨가 그곳에서 저를 만나기로 되어 있다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["He had told her expressly that he should do so, having inquired, with much solicitude, whether she intended to obey the invitation of the countess.", "그가 백작부인의 초대에 응할 뜻이냐고 몹시 애타게 물어 온 끝에 저도 그러겠노라고 딱 잘라 말해 두었던 것이다."], ["\"I shall probably be there,\"", "“아마 갈 것 같아요.”"], ["she had said, and now had determined that her mother's letter and her husband's conduct to her should not cause her to break her word. Should Mr.", "그때 그녀는 그렇게 말했고, 이제 어머니의 편지나 남편이 저에게 한 처사 때문에 제 말을 어기지는 않겠다고 마음을 굳혀 두었다."], ["Palliser \"forget\" himself, she would know how to say a word to him as she had known how to say a word to her husband.", "팰리서 씨가 “제 몸가짐을 잊는다면”, 그녀는 남편에게 한마디를 건넬 줄 알았던 것처럼 그에게도 한마디를 건넬 줄 알 것이었다."], ["Forget himself!", "몸가짐을 잊다니!"], ["She was very sure that Mr. Palliser had been making up his mind to forget himself for some months past.", "그녀는 팰리서 씨가 지난 몇 달 동안 제 몸가짐을 잊기로 마음을 다잡아 왔다는 것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["He did come to her, and stood over her, looking unutterable things.", "그는 정말로 그녀에게 다가와, 그녀 위로 우뚝 서서 말로 다 할 수 없는 것들을 눈으로 말했다."], ["His unutterable things, however, were so looked, that they did not absolutely demand notice from the lady.", "그러나 그 말로 다 할 수 없는 것들이란 워낙 그렇게 눈으로만 말해진 것이어서, 그 부인이 반드시 알은체를 해야 할 정도는 아니었다."], ["He did not sigh like a furnace, nor open his eyes upon her as though there were two suns in the firmament above her head, nor did he beat his breast or tear his hair.", "그는 용광로처럼 한숨을 쉬지도 않았고, 그녀의 머리 위 하늘에 해가 둘이라도 뜬 듯이 눈을 크게 뜨지도 않았으며, 제 가슴을 치거나 머리털을 쥐어뜯지도 않았다."], ["Mr. Palliser had been brought up in a school which delights in tranquillity, and never allows its pupils to commit themselves either to the sublime or to the ridiculous.", "팰리서 씨는 고요함을 즐기며 제자들이 숭고한 쪽으로도 우스꽝스러운 쪽으로도 결코 넘어가지 못하게 하는 학교에서 자란 사람이었다."], ["He did look an unutterable thing or two; but he did it with so decorous an eye, that the lady, who was measuring it all with great accuracy, could not, as yet, declare that Mr. Palliser had \"forgotten himself.\"", "그는 말로 다 할 수 없는 것을 한둘쯤 눈으로 말하기는 했으나, 그것을 하도 점잖은 눈으로 해냈으므로 그것을 낱낱이 재고 있던 그 부인도 아직은 팰리서 씨가 “제 몸가짐을 잊었다”고 선언할 수 없었다."]], [["There was room by her on the couch, and once or twice, at Hartlebury, he had ventured so to seat himself.", "긴 의자에는 그녀 곁에 자리가 있었고, 하틀베리에서는 한두 번 그가 감히 거기 앉아 본 적이 있었다."], ["On the present occasion, however, he could not do so without placing himself manifestly on her dress.", "그러나 이번에는 그녀의 옷자락을 뻔히 깔고 앉지 않고서는 그럴 수가 없었다."], ["She would have known how to fill a larger couch even than that,--as she would have known, also, how to make room,--had it been her mind to do so.", "그럴 마음만 있었다면 그녀는 그보다 더 큰 긴 의자라도 혼자 다 채울 줄 알았을 것이고—또한 자리를 내어 줄 줄도 알았을 것이다."], ["So he stood still over her, and she smiled at him.", "그리하여 그는 여전히 그녀 위로 선 채였고, 그녀는 그를 보고 미소를 지었다."], ["Such a smile!", "그런 미소라니!"], ["It was cold as death, flattering no one, saying nothing, hideous in its unmeaning, unreal grace.", "그것은 죽음처럼 차가웠고, 아무도 우쭐하게 하지 못했으며, 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 그 뜻 없고 참되지 않은 우아함이 흉하기만 했다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["how I hate the smile of a woman who smiles by rote! It made Mr.", "나는 버릇처럼 웃는 여인의 미소가 어찌나 싫은지!"], ["Palliser feel very uncomfortable;--but he did not analyze it, and persevered.", "그것은 팰리서 씨를 몹시 거북하게 만들었다. 그러나 그는 그것을 뜯어보지 않고 밀고 나갔다."]], [["\"Lady Dumbello,\"", "“레이디 덤벨로,” 그가 말했다."], ["he said, and his voice was very low, \"I have been looking forward to meeting you here.\"", "목소리가 아주 낮았다. “여기서 뵙기를 고대하고 있었소.”"]], [["\"Have you, Mr. Palliser?", "“그러셨어요, 팰리서 씨?"], ["Yes; I remember that you asked me whether I was coming.\"", "예. 제가 오느냐고 물으셨던 것이 기억나요.”"]], [["\"I did.", "“그랬지요."], ["Hm--Lady Dumbello!\"", "흠—레이디 덤벨로!”"], ["and he almost trenched upon the outside verge of that schooling which had taught him to avoid both the sublime and the ridiculous.", "그러면서 그는 숭고함도 우스꽝스러움도 다 피하라고 가르친 그 훈육의 바깥 경계를 하마터면 넘을 뻔했다."], ["But he had not forgotten himself as yet, and so she smiled again.", "그러나 아직은 제 몸가짐을 잊은 것이 아니었으므로 그녀는 다시 미소를 지었다."]], [["\"Lady Dumbello, in this world in which we live, it is so hard to get a moment in which we can speak.\"", "“레이디 덤벨로, 우리가 사는 이 세상에서는 이야기를 나눌 한때를 얻기가 참 어렵소.”"], ["He had thought that she would move her dress, but she did not.", "그는 그녀가 옷자락을 옮겨 주려니 생각했으나 그녀는 그러지 않았다."]], [["\"Oh, I don't know,\"", "“아, 글쎄요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said; \"one doesn't often want to say very much, I think.\"", "“사람이 그리 많은 말을 하고 싶어 하는 일은 흔치 않은 것 같은데요.”"]], [["\"Ah, no; not often, perhaps.", "“아, 그렇지요."], ["But when one does want!", "아마 흔치는 않겠지요."], ["How I do hate these crowded rooms!\"", "그러나 정말로 하고 싶을 때가 있지 않소!"], ["Yet, when he had been at Hartlebury he had resolved that the only ground for him would be the crowded drawing-room of some large London house.", "이렇게 붐비는 방이라니 나는 정말 싫소!” 그러나 하틀베리에 있을 때 그는 런던의 어느 큰 집의 붐비는 응접실만이 제게 알맞은 자리라고 마음을 굳혔던 터였다."], ["\"I wonder whether you ever desire anything beyond them?\"", "“혹시 이런 자리 너머의 무엇인가를 바라신 적은 없으신지 궁금하오.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes,\"", "“아, 있지요.”"], ["said she; \"but I confess that I am fond of parties.\"", "그녀가 말했다. “하지만 저는 모임을 좋아한다고 고백해야겠어요.”"]], [["Mr. Palliser looked round and thought that he saw that he was unobserved.", "팰리서 씨는 둘레를 돌아보고 아무도 저를 보고 있지 않다고 여겼다."], ["He had made up his mind as to what he would do, and he was determined to do it.", "그는 무엇을 할지 이미 마음을 정해 두었고, 그것을 하기로 작정한 터였다."], ["He had in him none of that readiness which enables some men to make love and carry off their Dulcineas at a moment's notice, but he had that pluck which would have made himself disgraceful in his own eyes if he omitted to do that as to the doing of which he had made a solemn resolution.", "그에게는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 사랑을 고백하고 제 둘시네아를 채어 가는 사내들의 그 날램이라고는 조금도 없었다. 그러나 엄숙히 마음먹은 일을 하지 않고 넘긴다면 제 눈에 제가 부끄러워질 만큼의 뱃심은 있었다."], ["He would have preferred to do it sitting, but, faute de mieux, seeing that a seat was denied to him, he would do it standing.", "그는 앉아서 하고 싶었으나, 더 나은 수가 없는 터에 자리를 얻지 못했으니 선 채로 하기로 했다."]], [["\"Griselda,\"", "“그리젤다.”"], ["he said,--and it must be admitted that his tone was not bad.", "그가 말했다—그의 어조가 나쁘지 않았다는 것은 인정해야 한다."], ["The word sank softly into her ear, like small rain upon moss, and it sank into no other ear.", "그 말은 이끼 위에 내리는 가랑비처럼 부드럽게 그녀의 귀로 스며들었고, 다른 어떤 귀로도 스며들지 않았다."], ["\"Griselda!\"", "“그리젤다!”"]], [["\"Mr. Palliser!\"", "“팰리서 씨!”"], ["said she;--and though she made no scene, though she merely glanced upon him once, he could see that he was wrong.", "그녀가 말했다. 그리고 그녀는 소란을 피우지도 않았고 그저 한 번 그를 흘낏 보았을 뿐이었으나, 그는 제가 잘못했음을 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["\"May I not call you so?\"", "“그렇게 불러서는 안 되겠소?”"]], [["\"Certainly not.", "“물론 안 되지요."], ["Shall I ask you to see if my people are there?\"", "제 사람들이 와 있는지 보아 주십사 청해도 될까요?”"], ["He stood a moment before her hesitating.", "그는 잠시 머뭇거리며 그녀 앞에 서 있었다."], ["\"My carriage, I mean.\"", "“제 마차 말이에요.”"], ["As she gave the command she glanced at him again, and then he obeyed her orders.", "그렇게 이르면서 그녀가 다시 그를 흘낏 보았고, 그러자 그는 그 분부를 따랐다."]], [["When he returned she had left her seat; but he heard her name announced on the stairs, and caught a glance of the back of her head as she made her way gracefully down through the crowd.", "그가 돌아왔을 때 그녀는 이미 자리를 떠난 뒤였다. 그러나 그는 계단에서 그녀의 이름이 불리는 것을 들었고, 그녀가 사람들 사이를 우아하게 헤치고 내려가는 뒷머리를 언뜻 보았다."], ["He never attempted to make love to her again, utterly disappointing the hopes of Lady De Courcy, Mrs. Proudie, and Lady Clandidlem.", "그는 두 번 다시 그녀에게 사랑을 건네려 하지 않았고, 그리하여 드 코시 백작부인과 프라우디 부인과 레이디 클랜디들럼의 기대를 남김없이 저버렸다."]], [["As I would wish those who are interested in Mr. Palliser's fortunes to know the ultimate result of this adventure, and as we shall not have space to return to his affairs in this little history, I may, perhaps, be allowed to press somewhat forward, and tell what Fortune did for him before the close of that London season.", "팰리서 씨의 앞날에 마음을 쓰는 이들에게 이 모험의 마지막 결말을 알려 주고 싶기도 하고, 또 이 자그마한 이야기에서는 그의 일로 다시 돌아올 자리가 없을 터이므로, 나는 아마 조금 앞질러 나아가 그 런던 사교철이 끝나기 전에 운명의 여신이 그를 위해 무엇을 해 주었는지 말해도 좋을 것이다."], ["Everybody knows that in that spring Lady Glencora MacCluskie was brought out before the world, and it is equally well known that she, as the only child of the late Lord of the Isles, was the great heiress of the day.", "그해 봄에 레이디 글렌코라 매클러스키가 세상에 처음 나왔다는 것은 누구나 아는 일이고, 그녀가 고인이 된 아일스 경의 외동딸로서 그 시절 최고의 재산 상속녀였다는 것 또한 그만큼 널리 알려진 일이다."], ["It is true that the hereditary possession of Skye, Staffa, Mull, Arran, and Bute went, with the title, to the Marquis of Auldreekie, together with the counties of Caithness and Ross-shire.", "스카이, 스타파, 멀, 애런, 뷰트의 세습 영지가 케이스니스주와 로스셔와 더불어 작위를 따라 올드리키 후작에게 넘어간 것은 사실이다."], ["But the property in Fife, Aberdeen, Perth, and Kincardine-shire, comprising the greater part of those counties, and the coal-mines in Lanark, as well as the enormous estate within the city of Glasgow, were unentailed, and went to the Lady Glencora.", "그러나 파이프, 애버딘, 퍼스, 킨카딘셔의 재산은—그 여러 주의 대부분을 차지하는 것으로—러나크의 탄광과 글래스고 시내의 어마어마한 토지와 함께 한정상속이 걸려 있지 않았으므로 레이디 글렌코라에게 갔다."], ["She was a fair girl, with bright blue eyes and short wavy flaxen hair, very soft to the eye.", "그녀는 살결이 흰 아가씨였고, 눈은 밝은 파란빛이었으며, 짧고 물결치는 아마빛 머리털은 보기에 몹시 부드러웠다."], ["The Lady Glencora was small in stature, and her happy round face lacked, perhaps, the highest grace of female beauty.", "레이디 글렌코라는 키가 작았고, 그 발그레하고 동그란 얼굴에는 어쩌면 여인의 아름다움 가운데 가장 높은 우아함이 빠져 있었는지도 모른다."], ["But there was ever a smile upon it, at which it was very pleasant to look; and the intense interest with which she would dance, and talk, and follow up every amusement that was offered her, was very charming.", "그러나 그 얼굴에는 늘 미소가 있어 바라보기에 아주 즐거웠고, 춤을 추고 이야기를 하고 제 앞에 놓인 온갖 놀이를 하나도 놓치지 않고 좇는 그 뜨거운 열심은 참으로 사랑스러웠다."], ["The horse she rode was the dearest love--oh!", "그녀가 타는 말은 더없이 귀여운 것이었고—아!"], ["she loved him so dearly!", "그녀는 그 말을 어찌나 사랑했는지!"], ["And she had a little dog that was almost as dear as the horse.", "게다가 말만큼이나 귀여운 작은 개도 한 마리 있었다."], ["The friend of her youth, Sabrina Scott, was--oh, such a girl!", "어린 날의 벗 서브리나 스콧은—아, 그런 아가씨가 또 있을까!"], ["And her cousin, the little Lord of the Isles, the heir of the marquis, was so gracious and beautiful that she was always covering him with kisses.", "그리고 사촌인 어린 아일스 경, 후작의 상속인은 어찌나 곱고 아름다운지 그녀는 늘 그 아이를 입맞춤으로 뒤덮어 주었다."], ["Unfortunately he was only six, so that there was hardly a possibility that the properties should be brought together.", "안타깝게도 그 아이는 겨우 여섯 살이었으므로 두 재산이 한데 모일 가망은 거의 없었다."]], [["But Lady Glencora, though she was so charming, had even in this, her first outset upon the world, given great uneasiness to her friends, and caused the Marquis of Auldreekie to be almost wild with dismay.", "그러나 레이디 글렌코라는 그토록 사랑스러웠음에도 세상에 처음 나온 이 첫걸음에서부터 벌써 벗들에게 큰 걱정을 끼쳤고, 올드리키 후작을 낙담으로 거의 미치게 만들었다."], ["There was a terribly handsome man about town, who had spent every shilling that anybody would give him, who was very fond of brandy, who was known, but not trusted, at Newmarket, who was said to be deep in every vice, whose father would not speak to him;--and with him the Lady Glencora was never tired of dancing.", "도시에는 무섭도록 잘생긴 사내가 하나 있었으니, 누구든 주는 대로 한 푼도 남기지 않고 다 써 버렸고, 브랜디를 몹시 좋아했으며, 뉴마켓에서는 이름이 알려져 있었으되 믿음은 받지 못했고, 온갖 악덕에 깊이 빠져 있다는 말이 돌았으며, 제 아버지조차 말을 섞지 않는 자였다. 그런데 레이디 글렌코라는 그 사내와 춤추는 일에 도무지 싫증을 내지 않았다."], ["One morning she had told her cousin the marquis, with a flashing eye,--for the round blue eye could flash,--that Burgo Fitzgerald was more sinned against than sinning.", "어느 날 아침 그녀는 사촌인 후작에게 눈을 번뜩이며—그 동그란 파란 눈도 번뜩일 줄 알았던 것이다—버고 피츠제럴드는 죄를 지었다기보다 죄를 입은 쪽이라고 말했다."], ["Ah me!", "아이고!"], ["what was a guardian marquis, anxious for the fate of the family property, to do under such circumstances as that?", "집안 재산의 앞날을 걱정하는 후견인 후작이 그런 형편에서 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["But before the end of the season the marquis and the duke were both happy men, and we will hope that the Lady Glencora also was satisfied.", "그러나 사교철이 끝나기 전에 후작과 공작은 둘 다 행복한 사람이 되었고, 레이디 글렌코라도 흡족했기를 우리는 바라 마지않는다."], ["Mr. Plantagenet Palliser had danced with her twice, and had spoken his mind.", "플랜태저넷 팰리서 씨가 그녀와 두 번 춤을 추고 제 마음을 털어놓았던 것이다."], ["He had an interview with the marquis, which was pre-eminently satisfactory, and everything was settled.", "그는 후작과 이야기를 나누었는데 그 자리는 더할 나위 없이 흡족했고, 모든 일이 매듭지어졌다."], ["Glencora no doubt told him how she had accepted that plain gold ring from Burgo Fitzgerald, and how she had restored it; but I doubt whether she ever told him of that wavy lock of golden hair which Burgo still keeps in his receptacle for such treasures.", "글렌코라는 틀림없이 그에게, 버고 피츠제럴드에게서 그 밋밋한 금반지를 받았던 일과 그것을 도로 돌려주었던 일을 이야기했을 것이다. 그러나 버고가 그런 보물을 넣어 두는 제 함에 아직도 간직하고 있는 그 물결치는 금빛 머리채 한 타래에 대해서도 그녀가 이야기했을지는 의심스럽다."]], [["\"Plantagenet,\"", "“플랜태저넷,”"], ["said the duke, with quite unaccustomed warmth, \"in this, as in all things, you have shown yourself to be everything that I could desire.", "공작이 여느 때와는 사뭇 다른 따뜻함으로 말했다. “이 일에서도 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 자네는 내가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 보여 주었네."], ["I have told the marquis that Matching Priory, with the whole estate, should be given over to you at once.", "나는 후작에게 매칭 프라이어리를 영지째로 곧바로 자네에게 넘기겠다고 일러 두었네."], ["It is the most comfortable country-house I know.", "내가 아는 가장 편안한 시골 저택이지."], ["Glencora shall have The Horns as her wedding present.\"", "글렌코라에게는 혼례 선물로 더 혼스를 주겠네.”"]], [["But the genial, frank delight of Mr. Fothergill pleased Mr. Palliser the most. The heir of the Pallisers had done his duty, and Mr. Fothergill was unfeignedly a happy man.", "그러나 팰리서 씨를 가장 기쁘게 한 것은 포더길 씨의 그 스스럼없고 꾸밈없는 즐거움이었다. 팰리서 가의 상속인이 제 도리를 다했으니, 포더길 씨는 참으로 꾸밈없이 행복한 사람이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LVI. SHOWING HOW MR. CROSBIE BECAME AGAIN A HAPPY MAN.", "# 제56장. 크로즈비 씨가 어떻게 다시 행복한 사람이 되었는가"]], [["It has been told in the last chapter how Lady De Courcy gave a great party in London in the latter days of April, and it may therefore be thought that things were going well with the De Courcys; but I fear the inference would be untrue.", "드 코시 백작부인이 사월 하순에 런던에서 큰 모임을 열었다는 것은 앞 장에서 이야기한 바 있다. 그러니 드 코시 집안의 일이 잘 풀려 가고 있었다고 여길 법도 하다."], ["At any rate, things were not going well with Lady Alexandrina, for she, on her mother's first arrival in town, had rushed to Portman-square with a long tale of her sufferings.", "그러나 그런 짐작은 사실과 다르지 않을까 나는 걱정한다. 어쨌거나 레이디 알렉산드리나의 일만은 잘 풀려 가고 있지 않았으니, 어머니가 런던에 올라온 첫날 그녀는 제가 겪은 고생을 길게 늘어놓으려고 포트먼 스퀘어로 달려갔던 것이다."]], [["\"Oh, mamma!", "“아, 어머니!"], ["you would not believe it; but he hardly ever speaks to me.\"", "믿지 못하실 거예요. 그이는 저한테 말이라고는 거의 걸지도 않아요.”"]], [["\"My dear, there are worse faults in a man than that.\"", "“얘야, 사내에게는 그보다 더한 흠도 있는 법이다.”"]], [["\"I am alone there all the day.", "“저는 온종일 그 집에 혼자 있어요."], ["I never get out.", "도무지 바깥출입을 못 해요."], ["He never offers to get me a carriage.", "그이는 저한테 마차 한 대 마련해 주겠다는 말도 없어요."], ["He asked me to walk with him once last week, when it was raining.", "지난주에 한 번 같이 걷자고 하기는 했는데, 그것도 비가 오는 날이었어요."], ["I saw that he waited till the rain began.", "저는 그이가 비가 내리기 시작할 때까지 기다렸다는 걸 알아요."], ["Only think, I have not been out three evenings this month,--except to Amelia's; and now he says he won't go there any more, because a fly is so expensive.", "생각 좀 해 보세요, 이번 달에 제가 저녁 나들이를 한 것이 세 번도 안 돼요—어밀리아 언니네 말고는요. 그런데 이제는 삯마차 삯이 너무 비싸다면서 그쪽에도 다시는 안 가겠대요."], ["You can't believe how uncomfortable the house is.\"", "그 집이 얼마나 불편한지 어머니는 믿지 못하실 거예요.”"]], [["\"I thought you chose it, my dear.\"", "“네가 고른 집인 줄 알았다만, 얘야.”"]], [["\"I looked at it, but, of course, I didn't know what a house ought to be.", "“보기야 제가 봤죠. 하지만 집이라는 것이 어때야 하는지 제가 알 리 없잖아요."], ["Amelia said it wasn't nice, but he would have it.", "어밀리아 언니는 좋지 않다고 했는데도 그이가 기어이 그 집으로 하겠다고 했어요. 그이는 어밀리아 언니를 미워해요."], ["He hates Amelia.", "저는 확실히 알아요."], ["I'm sure of that, for he says everything he can to snub her and Mr. Gazebee.", "언니와 게이지비 형부를 홀대할 수 있는 말이라면 무엇이든 하니까요."], ["Mr. Gazebee is as good as he, at any rate.", "게이지비 형부도 적어도 그이만큼은 훌륭한 사람인데 말이에요."], ["What do you think?", "어떻게 생각하세요?"], ["He has given Richard warning to go.", "그이가 리처드한테 나가라고 통고했어요."], ["You never saw him, but he was a very good servant.", "어머니는 그 사람을 보신 적이 없지만 아주 좋은 하인이었어요."], ["He has given him warning, and he is not talking of getting another man.", "그런데 나가라고 통고해 놓고는 다른 사람을 들일 이야기는 꺼내지도 않아요."], ["I won't live with him without somebody to wait upon me.\"", "저는 시중들 사람도 없이 그이와 살지는 않겠어요.”"]], [["\"My dearest girl, do not think of such a thing as leaving him.\"", "“내 귀한 딸아, 그이를 떠난다는 것 같은 일은 생각도 하지 마라.”"]], [["\"But I will think of it, mamma.", "“하지만 저는 생각할 거예요, 어머니."], ["You do not know what my life is in that house.", "그 집에서 제 삶이 어떤 것인지 어머니는 모르세요."], ["He never speaks to me,--never.", "그이는 저한테 말을 걸지 않아요—절대로요."], ["He comes home before dinner at half-past six, and when he has just shown himself he goes to his dressing-room.", "만찬 전 여섯 시 반이면 집에 들어와서, 얼굴만 잠깐 비치고는 제 옷방으로 들어가 버려요."], ["He is always silent at dinner-time, and after dinner he goes to sleep.", "만찬 때는 늘 잠자코 있고, 만찬이 끝나면 잠이 들어요."], ["He breakfasts always at nine, and goes away at half-past nine, though I know he does not get to his office till eleven.", "아침은 언제나 아홉 시에 먹고 아홉 시 반에 나가는데, 저는 그이가 열한 시나 되어야 사무국에 닿는다는 걸 알아요."], ["If I want anything, he says that it cannot be afforded.", "제가 무엇을 갖고 싶다고 하면 그럴 형편이 못 된다고 해요."], ["I never thought before that he was stingy, but I am sure now that he must be a miser at heart.\"", "저는 여태 그이가 인색한 사람이라고는 생각해 본 적이 없는데, 이제는 그이가 속으로는 구두쇠임이 틀림없다고 확신해요.”"]], [["\"It is better so than a spendthrift, Alexandrina.\"", "“낭비꾼인 것보다야 그편이 낫다, 알렉산드리나.”"]], [["\"I don't know that it is better.", "“그편이 낫다고는 생각하지 않아요."], ["He could not make me more unhappy than I am.", "그이가 저를 지금보다 더 불행하게 만들 수는 없을 거예요."], ["Unhappy is no word for it.", "불행하다는 말로는 모자라요."], ["What can I do, shut up in such a house as that by myself from nine o'clock in the morning till six in the evening?", "아침 아홉 시부터 저녁 여섯 시까지 그런 집에 혼자 갇혀 있으면서 제가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Everybody knows what he is, so that nobody will come to see me.", "그이가 어떤 사람인지 다들 아니까 아무도 저를 보러 오지 않아요."], ["I tell you fairly, mamma, I will not stand it.", "솔직히 말씀드릴게요, 어머니. 저는 이것을 견디지 않겠어요."], ["If you cannot help me, I will look for help elsewhere.\"", "어머니가 저를 도와주실 수 없다면, 저는 다른 데서 도움을 찾아보겠어요.”"]], [["It may, at any rate, be said that things were not going well with that branch of the De Courcy family.", "어쨌거나 드 코시 집안의 그 갈래에서 일이 잘 풀려 가고 있지 않았다는 것만은 말해도 좋겠다."], ["Nor, indeed, was it going well with some other branches.", "실은 다른 갈래에서도 일이 잘 풀려 가고 있지 않았다."], ["Lord Porlock had married, not having selected his partner for life from the choicest cream of the aristocratic circles, and his mother, while endeavouring to say a word in his favour, had been so abused by the earl that she had been driven to declare that she could no longer endure such usage.", "폴록 경은 혼인을 했는데, 그 평생의 반려를 귀족 사회의 가장 좋은 크림 가운데서 골라 온 것은 아니었다. 그의 어머니가 아들 편을 들어 한마디 거들어 보려 하다가 백작에게 어찌나 심한 욕을 먹었던지, 이제 그런 대접은 더 견딜 수 없노라고 선언하기에 이르렀다."], ["She had come up to London in direct opposition to his commands, while he was fastened to his room by gout; and had given her party in defiance of him, so that people should not say, when her back was turned, that she had slunk away in despair.", "그녀는 백작이 통풍으로 제 방에 붙들려 있는 틈을 타 그의 명령을 정면으로 거스르고 런던에 올라왔고, 그를 무시하고 제 모임을 열었으니, 제가 등을 돌리고 나면 절망하여 슬그머니 물러갔다고 사람들이 말하지 못하게 하려는 것이었다."]], [["\"I have borne it,\"", "“나는 견뎌 왔다.”"], ["she said to Margaretta, \"longer than any other woman in England would have done.", "그녀가 마거리타에게 말했다. “영국의 다른 어떤 여자도 못 해 냈을 만큼 오래 견뎠어."], ["While I thought that any of you would marry--\"", "너희 가운데 누구든 시집을 가리라고 여기던 동안은—”"]], [["\"Oh, don't talk of that, mamma,\"", "“아이, 그 이야기는 마세요, 어머니.”"], ["said Margaretta, putting a little scorn into her voice.", "마거리타가 목소리에 얼마쯤 경멸을 실어 말했다."], ["She had not been quite pleased that even her mother should intimate that all her chance was over, and yet she herself had often told her mother that she had given up all thought of marrying.", "제 어머니까지도 이제 저에게는 아무 가망도 남지 않았다고 넌지시 말하는 것이 그녀는 썩 마땅치 않았다. 그러면서도 정작 그녀 자신은 시집갈 생각일랑 다 접었노라고 어머니에게 자주 말해 온 터였다."]], [["\"Rosina will go to Amelia's,\"", "“로지나는 어밀리아네로 가면 된다.”"], ["the countess continued; \"Mr.", "백작부인이 말을 이었다."], ["Gazebee is quite satisfied that it should be so, and he will take care that she shall have enough to cover her own expenses.", "“게이지비 씨도 그렇게 되는 것을 아주 흡족하게 여기고, 그 애가 제 몫의 비용은 감당할 만큼 갖도록 챙겨 줄 게다."], ["I propose that you and I, dear, shall go to Baden-Baden.\"", "나는 너와 내가, 얘야, 바덴바덴으로 가면 어떨까 한다.”"]], [["\"And about money, mamma?\"", "“돈은 어떻게 하고요, 어머니?”"]], [["\"Mr. Gazebee must manage it.", "“게이지비 씨가 어떻게든 해내야지."], ["In spite of all that your father says, I know that there must be money.", "네 아버지가 무어라고 하든, 나는 돈이 있을 수밖에 없다는 것을 안다."], ["The expense will be much less so than in our present way.\"", "지금 우리가 사는 방식보다야 비용이 훨씬 적게 들 게다.”"]], [["\"And what will papa do himself?\"", "“그러면 아버지는 어떻게 하시고요?”"]], [["\"I cannot help it, my dear.", "“나로서는 어쩔 도리가 없다, 얘야."], ["No one knows what I have had to bear.", "내가 무엇을 견뎌야 했는지 아무도 모른다."], ["Another year of it would kill me.", "한 해만 더 그러다가는 내가 죽고 말 게다."], ["His language has become worse and worse, and I fear every day that he is going to strike me with his crutch.\"", "그이의 말버릇은 갈수록 더 험해지고, 나는 날마다 그이가 목발로 나를 후려치지나 않을까 겁이 난다.”"]], [["Under all these circumstances it cannot be said that the De Courcy interests were prospering.", "이 모든 사정을 놓고 볼 때 드 코시 집안의 이해가 잘 굴러가고 있었다고는 말할 수 없었다."]], [["But Lady De Courcy, when she had made up her mind to go to Baden-Baden, had by no means intended to take her youngest daughter with her.", "그러나 드 코시 백작부인은 바덴바덴으로 가겠다고 마음을 정했을 때, 막내딸을 데리고 갈 생각은 결코 없었다."], ["She had endured for years, and now Alexandrina was unable to endure for six months.", "그녀는 여러 해를 견뎌 왔는데 이제 알렉산드리나는 여섯 달도 견디지 못한다는 것이었다."], ["Her chief grievance, moreover, was this,--that her husband was silent.", "게다가 그 딸이 내세우는 가장 큰 불평이라는 것이 이러했다—제 남편이 말이 없다는 것."], ["The mother felt that no woman had a right to complain much of any such sorrow as that.", "그런 종류의 괴로움을 두고 크게 투덜댈 권리는 어떤 여자에게도 없다고 어머니는 느꼈다."], ["If her earl had sinned only in that way, she would have been content to have remained by him till the last!", "제 백작이 그런 식으로만 죄를 지었더라면 그녀는 마지막까지 그의 곁에 남아 있는 것으로 만족했으리라!"]], [["And yet I do not know whether Alexandrina's life was not quite as hard as that of her mother.", "그러면서도 알렉산드리나의 삶이 그 어머니의 삶만큼이나 고달팠던 것은 아닌지 나로서는 알 수 없다."], ["She barely exceeded the truth when she said that he never spoke to her.", "그이가 저에게 말을 걸지 않는다고 그녀가 말했을 때, 그것은 사실에서 그리 벗어난 말이 아니었다."], ["The hours with her in her new comfortless house were very long,--very long and very tedious.", "안락함이라고는 없는 그 새집에서 그녀가 보내는 시간은 몹시 길었다—몹시 길고 몹시 지루했다."], ["Marriage with her had by no means been the thing that she had expected.", "혼인이란 그녀가 기대하던 것과는 도무지 딴판이었다."], ["At home, with her mother, there had always been people around her, but they had not always been such as she herself would have chosen for her companions.", "어머니 곁의 친정에서는 언제나 둘레에 사람이 있었으나, 그들이 늘 그녀 자신이 벗으로 고를 만한 사람들이었던 것은 아니다."], ["She had thought that, when married, she could choose and have those about her who were congenial to her; but she found that none came to her.", "그녀는 혼인을 하고 나면 제 마음에 맞는 사람들을 골라 곁에 둘 수 있으리라고 생각했다. 그런데 막상 보니 찾아오는 사람이 아무도 없었다."], ["Her sister, who was a wiser woman than she, had begun her married life with a definite idea, and had carried it out; but this poor creature found herself, as it were, stranded.", "그녀보다 슬기로운 여자였던 언니는 뚜렷한 생각을 품고 혼인 생활을 시작해 그것을 끝까지 밀고 나갔다. 그러나 이 가엾은 사람은 이를테면 모래톱에 얹힌 배처럼 오도 가도 못하는 저를 발견했다."], ["When once she had conceived it in her heart to feel anger against her husband,--and she had done so before they had been a week together,--there was no love to bring her back to him again.", "한번 마음속에 남편에 대한 노여움을 품고 나자—그러기까지 두 사람이 함께 지낸 지 한 주일도 걸리지 않았다—그녀를 그에게로 다시 데려다 놓을 사랑이라고는 남아 있지 않았다."], ["She did not know that it behoved her to look pleased when he entered the room, and to make him at any rate think that his presence gave her happiness.", "그가 방에 들어설 때 반가운 낯빛을 지어 보이는 것이, 그리고 적어도 그가 곁에 있음으로 해서 제가 행복하다고 그가 여기게 만드는 것이 제 도리임을 그녀는 알지 못했다."], ["She became gloomy before she reached her new house, and never laid her gloom aside.", "그녀는 새집에 이르기도 전에 침울해졌고, 그 침울함을 결코 내려놓지 않았다."], ["He would have made a struggle for some domestic comfort, had any seemed to be within his reach.", "그로서도 손닿는 데에 무언가 있어 보이기만 했다면 가정의 안락을 얻으려고 힘껏 싸워 보았을 것이다."], ["As it was, he struggled for domestic propriety, believing that he might so best bolster up his present lot in life.", "사정이 그러하였으므로 그는 가정의 체면을 지키려 싸웠으니, 그렇게 하는 것이 지금 제게 주어진 처지를 그럭저럭 떠받치는 가장 나은 길이라고 믿었기 때문이다."], ["But the task became harder and harder to him, and the gloom became denser and more dense.", "그러나 그 일은 갈수록 더 힘겨워졌고, 침울함은 갈수록 더 짙어졌다."], ["He did not think of her unhappiness, but of his own; as she did not think of his tedium, but of hers.", "그는 그녀의 불행이 아니라 제 불행을 생각했다. 그녀가 그의 따분함이 아니라 제 따분함을 생각했듯이."], ["\"If this be domestic felicity!\"", "“이런 것이 가정의 지복이라면!”"], ["he would say to himself, as he sat in his arm-chair, striving to fix his attention upon a book.", "그는 안락의자에 앉아 책 한 권에 정신을 붙들어 매려 애쓰면서 속으로 그렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["\"If this be the happiness of married life!\"", "“이런 것이 혼인 생활의 행복이라면!”"], ["she thought, as she remained listless, without even the pretence of a book, behind her teacups.", "그녀는 책 읽는 시늉조차 없이 제 찻잔들 뒤에 멍하니 앉은 채 그렇게 생각했다."], ["In truth she would not walk with him, not caring for such exercise round the pavement of a London square; and he had resolutely determined that she should not run into debt for carriage hire.", "사실 그녀는 그와 함께 걸으려 하지 않았으니, 런던의 어느 광장 포도를 빙빙 도는 그런 운동 따위는 마음에 들지 않았던 것이다. 그리고 그는 그녀가 마차 삯으로 빚을 지는 일만은 없게 하겠다고 단단히 마음먹고 있었다."], ["He was not a curmudgeon with his money; he was no miser.", "그는 제 돈을 두고 쩨쩨하게 구는 사람이 아니었다."], ["But he had found that in marrying an earl's daughter he had made himself a poor man, and he was resolved that he would not also be an embarrassed man.", "구두쇠도 아니었다. 그러나 그는 백작의 딸과 혼인함으로써 스스로 가난한 사람이 되었다는 것을 알아차렸고, 그 위에 빚에 쫓기는 사람까지는 되지 않겠다고 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["When the bride heard that her mother and sister were about to escape to Baden-Baden, there rushed upon her a sudden hope that she might be able to accompany the flight.", "신부는 어머니와 언니가 바덴바덴으로 달아날 참이라는 말을 듣자, 저도 그 도피에 함께할 수 있으리라는 희망이 불현듯 솟구쳤다."], ["She would not be parted from her husband, or at least not so parted that the world should suppose that they had quarrelled.", "그녀는 남편과 갈라설 생각은 아니었다. 적어도 두 사람이 다투었다고 세상이 여길 만한 방식으로 갈라설 생각은 아니었다."], ["She would simply go away and make a long visit,--a very long visit.", "그저 훌쩍 떠나 오래 머물다 오려는 것이었다—아주 오래 머물다 오려는 것이었다."], ["Two years ago a sojourn with her mother and Margaretta at Baden-Baden would not have offered to her much that was attractive; but now, in her eyes, such a life seemed to be a life in Paradise.", "두 해 전이었다면 어머니와 마거리타와 함께 바덴바덴에 머문다는 것이 그녀에게 그리 매력 있는 일로 보이지 않았을 터이다. 그러나 이제 그녀의 눈에는 그런 삶이 낙원의 삶처럼 보였다."], ["In truth, the tedium of those hours in Princess Royal Crescent had been very heavy.", "실로 프린세스 로열 크레슨트에서 보내는 그 시간들의 따분함은 참으로 무거웠던 것이다."]], [["But how could she contrive that it should be so?", "그러나 어떻게 하면 그렇게 되도록 만들 수 있을까?"], ["That conversation with her mother had taken place on the day preceding the party, and Lady De Courcy had repeated it with dismay to Margaretta.", "어머니와 나눈 그 이야기는 모임이 열리기 전날에 있었고, 드 코시 백작부인은 그것을 마거리타에게 질겁한 낯으로 되풀이해 들려주었다."]], [["\"Of course he would allow her an income,\"", "“그야 그이가 그 애한테 생활비는 대 주겠지요.”"], ["Margaretta had coolly said.", "마거리타가 냉랭하게 말했다."]], [["\"But, my dear, they have been married only ten weeks.\"", "“하지만 얘야, 두 사람이 혼인한 지 겨우 열 주밖에 되지 않았다.”"]], [["\"I don't see why people are to be made absolutely wretched because they are married,\"", "“혼인했다는 이유로 사람이 아주 비참해져야 할 까닭이 어디 있는지 저는 모르겠어요.”"], ["Margaretta answered.", "마거리타가 대답했다."], ["\"I don't want to persuade her to leave him, but if what she says is true, it must be very uncomfortable.\"", "“저는 그 애더러 그이를 떠나라고 부추기고 싶지는 않아요. 그렇지만 그 애 말이 사실이라면 몹시 불편한 노릇임에는 틀림없잖아요.”"]], [["Crosbie had consented to go to the party in Portman-square, but had not greatly enjoyed himself on that festive occasion.", "크로즈비는 포트먼 스퀘어의 모임에 가는 데 동의하기는 했으나, 그 잔치 자리에서 그리 크게 즐기지는 못했다."], ["He had stood about moodily, speaking hardly a word to any one.", "그는 누구에게도 말 한마디 거의 건네지 않은 채 시무룩하게 서 있었다."], ["His whole aspect of life seemed to have been altered during the last few months.", "그의 삶의 모습은 통틀어 지난 몇 달 사이에 달라져 버린 듯했다."], ["It was here, in such spots as this that he had been used to find his glory.", "그가 제 영광을 찾곤 하던 곳이 바로 이런 자리였다."], ["On such occasions he had shone with peculiar light, making envious the hearts of many who watched the brilliance of his career as they stood around in dull quiescence.", "그런 자리에서 그는 남다른 빛으로 빛났고, 둘레에 무디게 서서 그의 눈부신 앞길을 지켜보던 많은 이의 마음을 시샘으로 물들였다."], ["But now no one in those rooms had been more dull, more silent, or less courted than he; and yet he was established there as the son-in-law of that noble house.", "그러나 이제 그 방들 안에서 그보다 더 무디고, 더 말이 없고, 덜 대접받는 사람은 없었다. 그런데도 그는 그 귀한 집안의 사위로서 그 자리에 버젓이 자리 잡고 있었다."], ["\"Rather slow work; isn't it?\"", "“꽤 굼뜬 자리로군, 그렇지 않은가?”"], ["Gazebee had said to him, having, after many efforts, succeeded in reaching his brother-in-law in a corner.", "게이지비가 여러 번 애를 쓴 끝에 구석에 있는 제 동서에게 다가가는 데 성공하고는 그에게 말했다."], ["In answer to this Crosbie had only grunted.", "이 말에 크로즈비는 그저 툴툴거리기만 했다."], ["\"As for myself,\"", "“나로 말하자면,”"], ["continued Gazebee, \"I would a deal sooner be at home with my paper and slippers.", "게이지비가 말을 이었다. “차라리 집에서 신문이랑 실내화나 끼고 있는 편이 훨씬 낫겠네."], ["It seems to me these sort of gatherings don't suit married men.\"", "이런 모임이라는 것은 혼인한 사내에게는 맞지 않는 것 같아.”"], ["Crosbie had again grunted, and had then escaped into another corner.", "크로즈비는 다시 툴툴거리고는 다른 구석으로 달아났다."]], [["Crosbie and his wife went home together in a cab,--speechless both of them.", "크로즈비와 그의 아내는 삯마차를 타고 함께 집으로 돌아갔다—둘 다 말이 없었다."], ["Alexandrina hated cabs,--but she had been plainly told that in such vehicles, and in such vehicles only, could she be allowed to travel.", "알렉산드리나는 삯마차를 싫어했다. 그러나 그런 탈것으로만, 오직 그런 탈것으로만 다닐 수 있다는 말을 그녀는 진작에 딱 잘라 들은 터였다."], ["On the following morning he was at the breakfast-table punctually by nine, but she did not make her appearance till after he had gone to his office.", "이튿날 아침 그는 아홉 시 정각에 아침 식탁에 앉았으나, 그녀는 그가 사무국으로 떠난 뒤에야 얼굴을 내밀었다."], ["Soon after that, however, she was away to her mother and her sister; but she was seated grimly in her drawing-room when he came in to see her, on his return to his house.", "그러나 그러고 나서 얼마 안 되어 그녀는 어머니와 언니에게로 갔다. 그런데도 그가 제 집에 돌아와 그녀를 보러 들어섰을 때 그녀는 시무룩한 낯으로 응접실에 앉아 있었다."], ["Having said some word which might be taken for a greeting, he was about to retire; but she stopped him with a request that he would speak to her.", "인사로 받아들일 만한 무슨 말인가를 건네고 그는 물러가려 했으나, 그녀는 저와 이야기를 좀 해 달라는 청으로 그를 붙들어 세웠다."]], [["\"Certainly,\"", "“좋소.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"I was only going to dress.", "“나는 옷을 갈아입으러 가려던 참이었소."], ["It is nearly the half-hour.\"", "이제 곧 여섯 시 반이오.”"]], [["\"I won't keep you very long, and if dinner is a few minutes late it won't signify.", "“오래 붙들지는 않겠어요. 그리고 만찬이 몇 분 늦어진다 해도 대수로울 것 없어요."], ["Mamma and Margaretta are going to Baden-Baden.\"", "어머니와 마거리타 언니가 바덴바덴으로 가신대요.”"]], [["\"To Baden-Baden, are they?\"", "“바덴바덴으로 간다고 하오?”"]], [["\"Yes; and they intend to remain there--for a considerable time.\"", "“네. 그리고 거기서 머무를 작정이시래요—꽤 오랫동안요.”"], ["There was a little pause, and Alexandrina found it necessary to clear her voice and to prepare herself for further speech by a little cough.", "잠시 사이가 있었고, 알렉산드리나는 목을 가다듬고 잔기침을 하며 다음 말을 할 채비를 해야겠다고 여겼다."], ["She was determined to make her proposition, but was rather afraid of the manner in which it might be first received.", "그녀는 제 제안을 기어이 꺼낼 작정이었으나, 그것이 처음에 어떻게 받아들여질지가 자못 두려웠다."]], [["\"Has anything happened at Courcy Castle?\"", "“코시 성에 무슨 일이라도 있었소?”"], ["Crosbie asked.", "크로즈비가 물었다."]], [["\"No; that is, yes; there may have been some words between papa and mamma; but I don't quite know.", "“아니요. 아니, 있기는 해요."], ["That, however, does not matter now.", "아버지와 어머니 사이에 무슨 말이 오갔나 봐요. 하지만 저도 잘은 몰라요."], ["Mamma is going, and purposes to remain there for the rest of the year.\"", "그렇지만 그건 지금 문제가 아니에요. 어머니는 떠나시고, 올해 남은 동안은 거기 머무를 작정이시래요.”"]], [["\"And the house in town will be given up.\"", "“그러면 런던 집은 내놓겠구려.”"]], [["\"I suppose so, but that will be as papa chooses.", "“그렇겠지요. 하지만 그건 아버지가 정하실 일이에요."], ["Have you any objection to my going with mamma?\"", "제가 어머니와 함께 가는 데 반대하세요?”"]], [["What a question to be asked by a bride of ten weeks' standing!", "혼인한 지 열 주밖에 안 된 신부가 물을 만한 질문이라니!"], ["She had hardly been above a month with her husband in her new house, and she was now asking permission to leave it, and to leave him also, for an indefinite number of months--perhaps for ever.", "그녀가 새집에서 남편과 함께 지낸 것은 겨우 한 달 남짓인데, 이제 그 집을 떠나게—그리고 그도 떠나게—해 달라고, 그것도 몇 달이 될지 알 수 없는 동안, 어쩌면 영영 그러하겠다고 허락을 청하고 있는 것이었다."], ["But she showed no excitement as she made her request.", "그러나 이 청을 하면서도 그녀는 조금도 마음이 들뜬 기색이 없었다."], ["There was neither sorrow, nor regret, nor hope in her face.", "그 얼굴에는 슬픔도, 아쉬움도, 희망도 없었다."], ["She had not put on half the animation which she had once assumed in asking for the use, twice a week, of a carriage done up to look as though it were her own private possession.", "마치 제 개인 소유물인 양 꾸며 놓은 마차를 한 주에 두 번 쓰게 해 달라고 청할 때 한때 지어 보였던 그 생기의 절반도 그녀는 지금 띠고 있지 않았다."], ["Crosbie had then answered her with great sternness, and she had wept when his refusal was made certain to her.", "그때 크로즈비는 아주 매몰차게 대답했고, 그의 거절이 분명해지자 그녀는 울었다."], ["But there was to be no weeping now.", "그러나 이제 울음이란 없을 참이었다."], ["She meant to go,--with his permission if he would accord it, and without it if he should refuse it.", "그녀는 갈 작정이었다—그가 허락을 준다면 허락을 받아서, 거절한다면 허락 없이라도."], ["The question of money was no doubt important, but Gazebee should manage that,--as he managed all those things.", "돈 문제야 물론 중요했지만 그것은 게이지비가 처리해 줄 터였다—그가 그런 일이란 일은 다 처리해 주듯이."]], [["\"Going with them to Baden-Baden?\"", "“그 사람들과 함께 바덴바덴으로 간다고?”"], ["said Crosbie.", "크로즈비가 말했다."], ["\"For how long?\"", "“얼마 동안이나?”"]], [["\"Well; it would be no use unless it were for some time.\"", "“글쎄요, 어지간히 오래 있지 않으면 아무 소용이 없겠지요.”"]], [["\"For how long a time do you mean, Alexandrina?", "“얼마나 오래를 말하는 거요, 알렉산드리나?"], ["Speak out what you really have to say.", "정말로 하고 싶은 말을 툭 터놓고 해 보시오."], ["For a month?\"", "한 달이오?”"]], [["\"Oh, more than that.\"", "“아, 그보다는 오래요.”"]], [["\"For two months, or six, or as long as they may stay there?\"", "“두 달이오, 아니면 여섯 달이오, 아니면 그 사람들이 거기 머무는 동안 내내요?”"]], [["\"We could settle that afterwards, when I am there.\"", "“그건 나중에, 제가 거기 가서 정하면 되잖아요.”"], ["During all this time she did not once look into his face, though he was looking hard at her throughout.", "이러는 내내 그녀는 한 번도 그의 얼굴을 들여다보지 않았다. 그는 줄곧 그녀를 뚫어져라 바라보고 있었건만."]], [["\"You mean,\"", "“당신 말은,”"], ["said he, \"that you wish to go away from me.\"", "그가 말했다. “나에게서 떠나고 싶다는 것이구려.”"]], [["\"In one sense it would be going away, certainly.\"", "“한편으로 보면 떠나는 것이기는 하겠지요.”"]], [["\"But in the ordinary sense? is it not so?", "“하지만 흔히 쓰는 뜻으로도 그렇지 않소?"], ["When you talk of going to Baden-Baden for an unlimited number of months, have you any idea of coming back again?\"", "몇 달이 될지 기약도 없이 바덴바덴에 가겠다고 말하면서, 당신에게 돌아올 생각이라는 것이 있기는 하오?”"]], [["\"Back to London, you mean?\"", "“런던으로 돌아오는 것 말이에요?”"]], [["\"Back to me,--to my house,--to your duties as a wife!", "“나에게로 돌아오는 것 말이오—내 집으로—아내로서의 당신 본분으로!"], ["Why cannot you say at once what it is you want?", "원하는 것이 무엇인지 어째서 대번에 말하지 못하오?"], ["You wish to be separated from me?\"", "나와 갈라져 살고 싶다는 것 아니오?”"]], [["\"I am not happy here,--in this house.\"", "“저는 여기서 행복하지 않아요—이 집에서요.”"]], [["\"And who chose the house?", "“그러면 이 집은 누가 골랐소?"], ["Did I want to come here?", "내가 여기로 오고 싶어 했소?"], ["But it is not that.", "그러나 그것이 문제가 아니오."], ["If you are not happy here, what could you have in any other house to make you happy?\"", "당신이 여기서 행복하지 않다면, 다른 어떤 집에서 무엇을 가진들 행복해질 수 있단 말이오?”"]], [["\"If you were left alone in this room for seven or eight hours at a time, without a soul to come to you, you would know what I mean.", "“당신이 이 방에 일고여덟 시간씩 혼자 남겨져 찾아오는 사람 하나 없이 있어 본다면 제 말이 무슨 뜻인지 아실 거예요."], ["And even after that, it is not much better.", "게다가 그 시간이 지난 뒤에도 그리 나을 것이 없고요."], ["You never speak to me when you are here.\"", "당신은 집에 있을 때조차 저한테 말을 걸지 않잖아요.”"]], [["\"Is it my fault that nobody comes to you?", "“아무도 당신을 찾아오지 않는 것이 내 잘못이오?"], ["The fact is, Alexandrina, that you will not reconcile yourself to the manner of life which is suitable to my income.", "사실인즉 이렇소, 알렉산드리나. 당신은 내 수입에 걸맞은 살림살이에 저를 맞출 마음이 없는 거요."], ["You are wretched because you cannot have yourself driven round the Park.", "당신은 마차를 타고 공원을 돌 수 없으니 비참한 거요."], ["I cannot find you a carriage, and will not attempt to do so.", "나는 당신에게 마차를 마련해 줄 수 없고, 그러려고 애쓰지도 않겠소."], ["You may go to Baden-Baden, if you please;--that is, if your mother is willing to take you.\"", "바덴바덴으로 가고 싶으면 가시오—그러니까, 당신 어머니가 당신을 데려갈 뜻이 있다면 말이오.”"]], [["\"Of course I must pay my own expenses,\"", "“물론 제 비용은 제가 대야겠지요.”"], ["said Alexandrina.", "알렉산드리나가 말했다."], ["But to this he made no answer on the moment.", "그러나 이 말에 그는 그 자리에서 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["As soon as he had given his permission he had risen from his seat and was going, and her last words only caught him in the doorway.", "허락을 내주자마자 그는 자리에서 일어나 나가려던 참이었고, 그녀의 마지막 말은 문간에서야 그를 붙들었을 뿐이다."], ["After all, would not this be the cheapest arrangement that he could make?", "따지고 보면 이것이야말로 그가 꾸밀 수 있는 가장 값싼 방책이 아니겠는가?"], ["As he went through his calculations he stood up with his elbow on the mantel-piece in his dressing-room.", "셈을 헤아려 보는 동안 그는 옷방에서 벽난로 선반에 팔꿈치를 괴고 서 있었다."], ["He had scolded his wife because she had been unhappy with him; but had he not been quite as unhappy with her?", "그는 제 아내가 저와 함께 지내며 불행해한다고 나무랐지만, 저 역시 그녀와 함께 지내며 그만큼 불행하지 않았던가?"], ["Would it not be better that they should part in this quiet, half-unnoticed way;--that they should part and never again come together?", "이렇게 조용히, 남의 눈에 잘 띄지 않게 갈라지는 편이—갈라져서 다시는 함께 살지 않는 편이 낫지 않겠는가?"], ["He was lucky in this, that hitherto had come upon them no prospect of any little Crosbie to mar the advantages of such an arrangement.", "그에게 다행이었던 것은, 그런 방책의 이점을 망쳐 놓을 자그마한 크로즈비가 생길 낌새가 여태 두 사람에게 없었다는 점이다."], ["If he gave her four hundred a year, and allowed Gazebee two more towards the paying off of encumbrances, he would still have six on which to enjoy himself in London.", "그녀에게 한 해 사백 파운드를 주고, 빚을 갚아 나가라고 게이지비에게 이백 파운드를 더 얹어 준다 해도, 그에게는 런던에서 제 몫으로 즐길 육백 파운드가 남을 터였다."], ["Of course he could not live as he had lived in those happy days before his marriage, nor, independently of the cost, would such a mode of life be within his reach.", "물론 혼인 전의 그 행복하던 시절처럼 살 수는 없을 것이고, 돈 문제를 떼어 놓고 보더라도 그런 살림살이는 이제 그의 손이 닿는 데에 있지 않았다. 그러나 클럽에 가서 만찬을 들 수는 있었다."], ["But he might go to his club for his dinners; he might smoke his cigar in luxury; he would not be bound to that wooden home which, in spite of all his resolutions, had become almost unendurable to him.", "호사스럽게 궐련을 피울 수도 있었다. 온갖 결심에도 불구하고 이제는 거의 견딜 수 없는 것이 되어 버린 그 목석 같은 집에 얽매이지 않아도 될 터였다."], ["So he made his calculations, and found that it would be well that his bride should go.", "그렇게 그는 셈을 헤아려 보고는, 제 신부가 가 주는 편이 좋겠다는 것을 알아냈다."], ["He would give over his house and furniture to Gazebee, allowing Gazebee to do as he would about that.", "그는 집과 세간을 게이지비에게 넘겨주고, 그것을 어떻게 하든 게이지비 마음대로 하게 할 참이었다."], ["To be once more a bachelor, in lodgings, with six hundred a year to spend on himself, seemed to him now such a prospect of happiness that he almost became light-hearted as he dressed himself.", "다시 독신이 되어 하숙집에서, 한 해 육백 파운드를 제 몸 하나에 쓰며 산다는 것이 이제 그에게는 어찌나 큰 행복의 전망으로 보였던지, 옷을 갈아입는 동안 그는 거의 마음이 가벼워질 지경이었다."], ["He would let her go to Baden-Baden.", "그는 그녀를 바덴바덴으로 보내 주기로 했다."]], [["There was nothing said about it at dinner, nor did he mention the subject again till the servant had left the tea-things on the drawing-room table.", "만찬 때에는 그 이야기가 나오지 않았고, 하인이 응접실 탁자에 찻그릇을 놓고 물러갈 때까지 그는 그 이야기를 다시 꺼내지 않았다."], ["\"You can go with your mother if you like it,\"", "“원한다면 어머니와 함께 가도 좋소.”"], ["he then said.", "그때 그가 말했다."]], [["\"I think it will be best,\"", "“그러는 편이 가장 좋겠어요.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["\"Perhaps it will.", "“그렇겠지."], ["At any rate you shall suit yourself.\"", "어쨌거나 당신 좋을 대로 하시오.”"]], [["\"And about money?\"", "“그러면 돈은요?”"]], [["\"You had better leave me to speak to Gazebee about that.\"", "“그 일은 내가 게이지비에게 말하도록 맡겨 두는 편이 낫겠소.”"]], [["\"Very well.", "“좋아요."], ["Will you have some tea?\"", "차 좀 드시겠어요?”"], ["And then the whole thing was finished.", "그리고 이렇게 모든 일이 끝났다."]], [["On the next day she went after lunch to her mother's house, and never came back again to Princess Royal Crescent.", "이튿날 그녀는 점심을 든 뒤 어머니의 집으로 갔고, 다시는 프린세스 로열 크레슨트로 돌아오지 않았다."], ["During that morning she packed up those things which she cared to pack herself, and sent her sisters there, with an old family servant, to bring away whatever else might be supposed to belong to her.", "그날 아침 그녀는 제 손으로 꾸리고 싶은 물건들을 꾸렸고, 그 밖에 제 것이라 할 만한 것은 무엇이든 가져오라고 언니들을 집안의 늙은 하인과 함께 그리로 보냈다. “저런, 저런.”"], ["\"Dear, dear,\"", "어밀리아가 말했다."], ["said Amelia, \"what trouble I had in getting these things together for them, and only the other day.", "“저 물건들을 저 애들한테 갖추어 주느라 내가 얼마나 애를 먹었는데, 그것도 바로 엊그제 일이오."], ["I can't but think she's wrong to go away.\"", "아무래도 그 애가 떠나는 것은 잘못이라는 생각밖에 안 드는구려.”"]], [["\"I don't know,\"", "“글쎄요.”"], ["said Margaretta.", "마거리타가 말했다."], ["\"She has not been so lucky as you have in the man she has married.", "“그 애는 언니만큼 남편 복이 좋지는 못했잖아요."], ["I always felt that she would find it difficult to manage him.\"", "저는 늘 그 애가 그이를 다루기 어려워하리라고 느꼈어요.”"]], [["\"But, my dear, she has not tried.", "“하지만 얘, 그 애는 애를 써 보지도 않았소."], ["She has given up at once.", "대번에 손을 놓아 버린 거요."], ["It isn't management that was wanting.", "모자랐던 것은 다루는 솜씨가 아니오."], ["The fact is that when Alexandrina began she didn't make up her mind to the kind of thing she was coming to.", "사실인즉 알렉산드리나는 시작할 때부터 제가 맞이할 삶이 어떤 것인지에 마음을 다잡지 않았던 거요. 나는 다잡았소."], ["I did. I knew it wasn't to be all party-going and that sort of thing.", "나는 그것이 모임에 다니고 그런 것들만 하는 삶은 아니라는 것을 알고 있었소."], ["But I must own that Crosbie isn't the same sort of man as Mortimer.", "그렇지만 크로즈비가 모티머와 같은 사람이 아니라는 것만은 인정해야겠구려."], ["I don't think I could have gone on with him.", "나라면 그이와 함께 살아 나가지는 못했을 것 같소."], ["You might as well have those small books put up; he won't care about them.\"", "저 작은 책들도 마저 꾸려 넣는 편이 좋겠소. 그이는 저런 것에 마음 쓰지 않을 테니.”"], ["And in this way Crosbie's house was dismantled.", "그리하여 크로즈비의 집은 허물어져 흩어졌다."]], [["She saw him no more, for he made no farewell visit to the house in Portman Square.", "그녀는 그를 다시 보지 못했으니, 그가 포트먼 스퀘어의 집으로 작별 인사를 하러 가지 않았기 때문이다."], ["A note had been brought to him at his office: \"I am here with mamma, and may as well say good-by now.", "쪽지 한 장이 그의 사무국으로 전해져 왔다. “저는 여기 어머니와 함께 있으니 지금 작별 인사를 드려도 되겠지요."], ["We start on Tuesday.", "우리는 화요일에 떠나요."], ["If you wish to write, you can send your letters to the housekeeper here.", "편지를 쓰고 싶으시면 여기 가정부 앞으로 보내시면 돼요."], ["I hope you will make yourself comfortable, and that you will be well.", "부디 편안히 지내시고 건강하시기를 바라요."], ["Yours affectionately, A.", "당신의 다정한 A. C.”"], ["C.\" He made no answer to it, but went that day and dined at his club.", "그는 여기에 아무 답장도 하지 않고, 그날 클럽에 가서 만찬을 들었다."]], [["\"I haven't seen you this age,\"", "“자네를 통 못 본 지 한참일세.”"], ["said Montgomerie Dobbs.", "몽고메리 돕스가 말했다."]], [["\"No.", "“그렇지."], ["My wife is going abroad with her mother, and while she is away I shall come back here again.\"", "아내가 어머니와 함께 외국으로 나가는데, 그 사람이 가 있는 동안은 내가 다시 여기로 돌아올 참일세.”"]], [["There was nothing more said to him, and no one ever made any inquiry about his domestic affairs.", "그에게 더 건네진 말은 없었고, 그의 집안일에 대해 물어보는 사람도 아무도 없었다."], ["It seemed to him now as though he had no friend sufficiently intimate with him to ask him after his wife or family.", "이제 그에게는 제 아내나 제 식구의 안부를 물어볼 만큼 가까운 벗이 하나도 없는 듯이 여겨졌다."], ["She was gone, and in a month's time he found himself again in Mount Street,--beginning the world with five hundred a year, not six.", "그녀는 가 버렸고, 한 달 뒤에 그는 다시 마운트 스트리트에 있는 저를 발견했다—한 해 육백 파운드가 아니라 오백 파운드로 세상살이를 다시 시작하면서."], ["For Mr. Gazebee, when the reckoning came, showed him that a larger income at the present moment was not possible for him.", "셈을 맞출 때가 되자 게이지비 씨가 지금 형편으로는 그보다 많은 수입은 그에게 가당치 않다는 것을 밝혀 보였기 때문이다."], ["The countess had for a long time refused to let Lady Alexandrina go with her on so small a pittance as four hundred and fifty;--and then were there not the insurances to be maintained?", "백작부인은 사백오십 파운드라는 그런 쥐꼬리만 한 돈으로는 레이디 알렉산드리나를 데려갈 수 없다고 오래도록 버텼다. 게다가 보험료도 계속 물어야 하지 않는가?"]], [["But I think he would have consented to accept his liberty with three hundred a year,--so great to him was the relief.", "그러나 나는 그가 한 해 삼백 파운드만 받고도 제 자유를 받아들이는 데 동의했으리라고 생각한다—그에게 그 안도감은 그토록 컸던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER LVII. LILIAN DALE VANQUISHES HER MOTHER.", "# 제57장. 릴리언 데일이 어머니를 이기다"]], [["Mrs. Dale had been present during the interview in which John Eames had made his prayer to her daughter, but she had said little or nothing on that occasion.", "존 임스가 그녀의 딸에게 제 간청을 올리던 그 자리에 데일 부인도 함께 있었으나, 그때 그녀는 이렇다 할 말을 거의 하지 않았다."], ["All her wishes had been in favour of the suitor, but she had not dared to express them, neither had she dared to leave the room.", "그녀의 바람은 온통 그 구혼자 편이었으되 그것을 입 밖에 낼 엄두를 내지 못했고, 그렇다고 방을 나갈 엄두도 내지 못했다."], ["It had been hard upon him to be thus forced to declare his love in the presence of a third person, but he had done it, and had gone away with his answer.", "제삼자가 보는 앞에서 이렇게 억지로 사랑을 고백해야 했던 것은 그에게 가혹한 일이었으나, 그는 그것을 해냈고 제 대답을 받아 들고 돌아갔다."], ["Then, when the thing was over, Lily, without any communion with her mother, took herself off, and was no more seen till the evening hours had come on, in which it was natural that they should be together again.", "그러고 나서 일이 끝나자 릴리는 어머니와 한마디도 나누지 않은 채 자리를 떠 버렸고, 두 사람이 다시 함께 있게 되는 것이 자연스러운 저녁 시간이 올 때까지 다시는 모습을 보이지 않았다."], ["Mrs. Dale, when thus alone, had been able to think of nothing but this new suit for her daughter's hand.", "그렇게 홀로 남은 데일 부인은 딸의 손을 두고 새로 들어온 이 청혼 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수가 없었다."], ["If only it might be accomplished!", "그저 그것이 이루어질 수만 있다면!"], ["If any words from her to Lily might be efficacious to such an end!", "그 일이 이루어지는 데에 릴리에게 건네는 제 말이 조금이라도 힘이 될 수만 있다면!"], ["And yet, hitherto, she had been afraid almost to utter a word.", "그러면서도 그녀는 여태 한마디를 꺼내는 것조차 두려워하고 있었다."]], [["She knew that it was very difficult.", "그것이 몹시 어려운 일임을 그녀는 알고 있었다."], ["She declared to herself over and over that he had come too soon,--that the attempt had been made too quickly after that other shipwreck.", "그가 너무 이르게 왔다고, 저 또 한 번의 난파가 있은 지 너무 이르게 그런 시도가 이루어졌다고 그녀는 스스로에게 몇 번이고 되뇌었다."], ["How was it possible that the ship should put to sea again at once, with all her timbers so rudely strained?", "배의 재목이 그토록 모질게 뒤틀린 마당에 어떻게 그 배가 곧바로 다시 바다로 나설 수 있단 말인가?"], ["And yet, now that the attempt had been made, now that Eames had uttered his request and been sent away with an answer, she felt that she must at once speak to Lily on the subject, if ever she were to speak upon it.", "그럼에도 이제 그 시도가 이루어진 마당에, 이제 임스가 제 청을 말하고 대답을 받아 들고 물러난 마당에, 그녀는 언젠가 이 이야기를 꺼낼 작정이라면 지금 당장 릴리에게 그 이야기를 해야 한다고 느꼈다."], ["She thought that she understood her child and all her feelings.", "그녀는 제 아이를, 그리고 그 아이의 마음을 다 이해하고 있다고 생각했다."], ["She recognized the violence of the shock which must be encountered before Lily could be brought to acknowledge such a change in her heart.", "릴리가 제 마음의 그런 변화를 인정하기까지 견뎌 내야 할 충격이 얼마나 격렬한 것인지도 그녀는 알고 있었다."], ["But if the thing could be done, Lily would be a happy woman.", "그러나 그 일이 이루어질 수만 있다면 릴리는 행복한 여자가 될 것이었다."], ["When once done it would be in all respects a blessing.", "일단 이루어지고 나면 그것은 모든 면에서 축복일 터였다."], ["And if it were not done, might not Lily's life be blank, lonely, and loveless to the end?", "그리고 그 일이 이루어지지 않는다면 릴리의 삶은 끝까지 텅 비고 외롭고 사랑 없는 것이 되지 않겠는가?"], ["Yet when Lily came down in the evening, with some light, half-joking word on her lips, as was usual to her, Mrs. Dale was still afraid to venture upon her task.", "그러나 저녁에 릴리가 여느 때처럼 가볍고 반쯤은 농담 섞인 말을 입에 물고 내려왔을 때, 데일 부인은 여전히 제 할 일에 나설 엄두를 내지 못했다."]], [["\"I suppose, mamma, we may consider it as a settled thing that everything must be again unpacked, and that the lodging scheme will be given up.\"", "“엄마, 짐을 다시 다 풀어야 하고 셋집으로 옮겨 갈 계획은 없던 일이 되었다고, 이제 그렇게 정해진 걸로 여겨도 되겠죠.”"]], [["\"I don't know that, my dear.\"", "“나는 잘 모르겠구나, 얘야.”"]], [["\"Oh, but I do--after what you said just now.", "“아이, 저는 알아요—방금 엄마가 하신 말씀을 듣고 나서는요."], ["What geese everybody will think us!\"", "사람들이 다 우리를 무슨 거위 떼로 여기겠어요!”"]], [["\"I shouldn't care a bit for that, if we didn't think ourselves geese, or if your uncle did not think us so.\"", "“우리 스스로 우리를 거위라고 여기지만 않는다면, 또 네 삼촌이 우리를 그렇게 여기지만 않는다면, 나는 그런 건 조금도 개의치 않는다.”"]], [["\"I believe he would think we were swans.", "“삼촌은 우리를 백조라고 여기실걸요."], ["If I had ever thought he would be so much in earnest about it, or that he would ever have cared about our being here, I would never have voted for going.", "삼촌이 그 일을 그토록 진심으로 여기실 줄, 또 우리가 여기 있는 것을 그토록 마음에 두실 줄 제가 조금이라도 알았더라면, 저는 결코 떠나자는 쪽에 표를 던지지 않았을 거예요."], ["But he is so strange.", "그런데 삼촌은 참 이상하세요."], ["He is affectionate when he ought to be angry, and ill-natured when he ought to be gentle and kind.\"", "화를 내셔야 할 자리에서는 다정하시고, 부드럽고 친절하셔야 할 자리에서는 심술궂으시니까요.”"]], [["\"He has, at any rate, given us reason to feel sure of his affection.\"", "“어쨌거나 삼촌은 우리에게 그분의 정을 믿어도 좋을 이유를 주셨다.”"]], [["\"For us girls, I never doubted it. But, mamma, I don't think I could face Mrs.", "“우리 딸들한테야 저는 한 번도 의심해 본 적 없어요."], ["Boyce. Mrs.", "하지만 엄마, 저는 보이스 부인을 마주할 자신이 없어요."], ["Hearn and Mrs. Crump would be very bad, and Hopkins would come down upon us terribly when he found that we had given way.", "헌 부인과 크럼프 부인도 아주 고약하겠고, 우리가 뜻을 굽혔다는 걸 알면 홉킨스는 무섭게 우리를 몰아세울 거예요."], ["But Mrs. Boyce would be worse than any of them.", "그래도 보이스 부인이 그중 누구보다 더할 거예요."], ["Can't you fancy the tone of her congratulations?\"", "그 부인이 어떤 목소리로 축하를 건넬지 짐작이 안 되세요?”"]], [["\"I think I should survive Mrs. Boyce.\"", "“보이스 부인쯤은 내가 견뎌 낼 것 같구나.”"]], [["\"Ah, yes; because we should have to go and tell her.", "“아, 그렇겠죠. 가서 그 부인한테 말할 사람은 우리일 테니까요."], ["I know your cowardice of old, mamma; don't I?", "저는 엄마가 예전부터 겁쟁이라는 걸 알아요, 엄마. 안 그래요?"], ["And Bell wouldn't care a bit, because of her lover.", "그리고 벨 언니는 애인이 있으니 눈 하나 깜짝 안 할 거예요."], ["Mrs. Boyce will be nothing to her.", "보이스 부인쯤은 언니한테 아무것도 아닐 테니까요."], ["It is I that must bear it all.", "그걸 다 감당해야 할 사람은 저예요."], ["Well, I don't mind; I'll vote for staying if you will promise to be happy here.", "뭐, 상관없어요. 엄마가 여기서 행복하게 지내겠다고 약속만 해 주시면 저는 남는 쪽에 표를 던질게요."], ["Oh, mamma, I'll vote for anything if you will be happy.\"", "아, 엄마, 엄마만 행복하시다면 저는 무엇에든 표를 던지겠어요.”"]], [["\"And will you be happy?\"", "“그러면 너는 행복하겠니?”"]], [["\"Yes, as happy as the day is long.", "“네, 하루 해가 긴 만큼이나 행복할 거예요."], ["Only I know we shall never see Bell.", "다만 우리가 벨 언니를 영영 못 보게 되리라는 것만은 알아요."], ["People never do see each other when they live just at that distance.", "딱 그만한 거리를 두고 살면 사람들은 서로 만나지 않게 되거든요."], ["It's too near for long visits, and too far for short visits.", "오래 머무는 방문을 하기에는 너무 가깝고, 잠깐 들르는 방문을 하기에는 너무 머니까요."], ["I'll tell you what; we might make arrangements each to walk half-way, and meet at the corner of Lord De Guest's wood.", "이렇게 하면 어때요. 우리가 각자 절반씩 걸어 나가서 드 게스트 경의 숲 모퉁이에서 만나기로 하는 거예요."], ["I wonder whether they'd let us put up a seat there.", "거기에 우리가 걸상 하나 놓아도 된다고 할까요?"], ["I think we might have a little house and carry sandwiches and a bottle of beer.", "작은 오두막을 하나 두고 샌드위치랑 맥주 한 병을 들고 가도 되겠어요."], ["Couldn't we see something of each other in that way?\"", "그런 식으로라면 서로 얼굴을 좀 볼 수 있지 않을까요?”"]], [["Thus it came to be the fixed idea of both of them that they would abandon their plan of migrating to Guestwick, and on this subject they continued to talk over their tea-table; but on that evening Mrs.", "그리하여 게스트윅으로 옮겨 갈 계획을 접는다는 것이 두 사람 모두에게 굳어진 생각이 되었고, 이 이야기를 두고 그들은 찻상 앞에서 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["Dale ventured to say nothing about John Eames.", "그러나 그날 저녁 데일 부인은 존 임스에 대해서는 끝내 한마디도 꺼내지 못했다."]], [["But they did not even yet dare to commence the work of reconstructing their old home.", "그러나 그들은 아직 옛집을 다시 꾸리는 일에 손을 댈 엄두는 내지 못했다."], ["Bell must come back before they would do that, and the express assent of the squire must be formally obtained.", "그러자면 먼저 벨이 돌아와야 했고, 지주의 분명한 동의도 격식을 갖추어 받아 두어야 했다."], ["Mrs. Dale must, in a degree, acknowledge herself to have been wrong, and ask to be forgiven for her contumacy.", "데일 부인은 어느 정도 제가 잘못했음을 스스로 인정하고, 제 고집을 부린 것을 용서해 달라고 청해야 했다."]], [["\"I suppose the three of us had better go up in sackcloth, and throw ashes on our foreheads as we meet Hopkins in the garden,\"", "“우리 셋이 베옷을 걸치고 올라가서, 정원에서 홉킨스를 만나면 이마에 재를 뿌리는 게 낫겠어요.” 릴리가 말했다."], ["said Lily, \"and then I know he'll heap coals of fire on our heads by sending us an early dish of peas.", "“그러면 홉킨스는 철 이른 완두 한 접시를 보내 주어서 우리 머리에 숯불을 쌓아 올릴 게 뻔하고요."], ["And Dingles would bring us in a pheasant, only that pheasants don't grow in May.\"", "딩글스는 꿩을 한 마리 들여보내 주겠죠. 다만 오월에는 꿩이 자라지 않는다는 게 문제지만요.”"]], [["\"If the sackcloth doesn't take an unpleasanter shape than that, I shan't mind it.\"", "“베옷이 그보다 더 언짢은 모양을 하고 나타나지만 않는다면 나는 개의치 않겠다.”"]], [["\"That's because you've got no delicate feelings.", "“그건 엄마한테 섬세한 감정이 없어서 그래요."], ["And then uncle Christopher's gratitude!\"", "게다가 크리스토퍼 삼촌의 고마워하시는 꼴이라니!”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“아!"], ["I shall feel that.\"", "그건 나도 마음에 걸리겠구나.”"]], [["\"But, mamma, we'll wait till Bell comes home.", "“하지만 엄마, 벨 언니가 돌아올 때까지 기다려요."], ["She shall decide.", "언니가 정하게 하는 거예요."], ["She is going away, and therefore she'll be free from prejudice.", "언니는 떠날 사람이니까 치우침 없이 판단할 테고요."], ["If uncle offers to paint the house,--and I know he will,--then I shall be humbled to the dust.\"", "삼촌이 집을 새로 칠해 주시겠다고 나서시면—그러실 게 뻔하지만—저는 흙바닥까지 납작 엎드리게 되겠죠.”"]], [["But yet Mrs. Dale had said nothing on the subject which was nearest to her heart.", "그러나 데일 부인은 제 마음에 가장 가까이 있는 그 이야기를 아직 한마디도 꺼내지 못했다."], ["When Lily in pleasantry had accused her of cowardice, her mind had instantly gone off to that other matter, and she had told herself that she was a coward.", "릴리가 우스갯소리로 그녀를 겁쟁이라고 몰아세웠을 때 그녀의 마음은 곧바로 저 다른 문제로 달려갔고, 그녀는 스스로에게 자기가 겁쟁이라고 말했다."], ["Why should she be afraid of offering her counsel to her own child?", "어찌하여 제 자식에게 충고 한마디 건네는 것을 두려워한단 말인가?"], ["It seemed to her as though she had neglected some duty in allowing Crosbie's conduct to have passed away without hardly a word of comment on it between herself and Lily.", "크로즈비의 처사를 두고 저와 릴리 사이에 이렇다 할 말 한마디 오가지 않은 채 그 일을 지나쳐 보낸 것이 무슨 의무를 저버린 일처럼 여겨졌다."], ["Should she not have forced upon her daughter's conviction the fact that Crosbie had been a villain, and as such should be discarded from her heart?", "크로즈비가 악당이었으며 그러므로 그 아이의 마음에서 내쳐 마땅하다는 사실을, 딸에게 억지로라도 납득시켰어야 하지 않았을까?"], ["As it was, Lily had spoken the simple truth when she told John Eames that she was dealing more openly with him on that affair of her engagement than she had ever dealt, even with her mother.", "실상 릴리가 존 임스에게 저 약혼 이야기를 두고는 어머니에게조차 한 적 없을 만큼 그에게 터놓고 이야기하고 있다고 말했을 때, 그것은 있는 그대로의 진실이었다."], ["Thinking of this as she sat in her own room that night, before she allowed herself to rest, Mrs. Dale resolved that on the next morning she would endeavour to make Lily see as she saw and think as she thought.", "그날 밤 제 방에 앉아 잠자리에 들기 전에 이것을 곰곰이 생각하며, 데일 부인은 이튿날 아침에는 릴리가 제가 보는 대로 보고 제가 생각하는 대로 생각하도록 애써 보리라 마음먹었다."]], [["She let breakfast pass by before she began her task, and even then she did not rush at it at once.", "그녀는 제 할 일을 시작하기 전에 아침 식사를 그냥 흘려보냈고, 그러고 나서도 곧바로 그 일에 뛰어들지는 않았다."], ["Lily sat herself down to her work when the teacups were taken away, and Mrs. Dale went down to her kitchen as was her wont.", "찻잔이 물려 나가자 릴리는 제 바느질감 앞에 앉았고, 데일 부인은 늘 하던 대로 부엌으로 내려갔다."], ["It was nearly eleven before she seated herself in the parlour, and even then she got her work-box before her and took out her needle.", "그녀가 응접실에 자리를 잡고 앉은 것은 열한 시가 다 되어서였고, 그러고도 그녀는 반짇고리를 앞으로 끌어다 놓고 바늘을 꺼내 들었다."]], [["\"I wonder how Bell gets on with Lady Julia,\"", "“벨 언니가 레이디 줄리아하고 잘 지내고 있을까요?”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Very well, I'm sure.\"", "“잘 지내고 있을 게다, 틀림없이.”"]], [["\"Lady Julia won't bite her, I know, and I suppose her dismay at the tall footmen has passed off by this time.\"", "“레이디 줄리아가 언니를 물어뜯지는 않으실 테고, 키 큰 하인들을 보고 놀랐던 것도 이제쯤은 가셨겠죠.”"]], [["\"I don't know that they have any tall footmen.\"", "“그 집에 키 큰 하인이 있는지 없는지 나는 모르겠다만.”"]], [["\"Short footmen then,--you know what I mean; all the noble belongings.", "“그럼 키 작은 하인들이라고 하죠—무슨 말인지 아시잖아요. 그 온갖 귀족다운 것들 말이에요."], ["They must startle one at first, I'm sure, let one determine ever so much not to be startled.", "아무리 놀라지 않겠다고 단단히 마음먹어도 처음에는 사람을 놀라게 하고야 말 거예요, 틀림없이. 그저 경이라는 이유만으로 경을 무서워한다는 건 물론 아주 좀스러운 짓이죠."], ["It's a very mean thing, no doubt, to be afraid of a lord merely because he is a lord; yet I'm sure I should be afraid at first, even of Lord De Guest, if I were staying in the house.\"", "그래도 저라면 처음에는 무서웠을 거예요. 그 집에 묵고 있었다면 드 게스트 경한테까지도요.”"]], [["\"It's well you didn't go then.\"", "“그러면 네가 가지 않은 것이 잘된 일이구나.”"]], [["\"Yes, I think it is.", "“네, 저도 그렇게 생각해요."], ["Bell is of a firmer mind, and I dare say she'll get over it after the first day.", "벨 언니는 마음이 더 단단하니까 하루 이틀 지나면 그런 건 이겨 낼 거예요."], ["But what on earth does she do there?", "그런데 언니는 거기서 도대체 무얼 할까요?"], ["I wonder whether they mend their stockings in such a house as that.\"", "그런 집에서도 양말을 깁기는 하는지 궁금해요.”"]], [["\"Not in public, I should think.\"", "“남들 보는 데서 깁지는 않겠지.”"]], [["\"In very grand houses they throw them away at once, I suppose.", "“아주 으리으리한 집에서는 그냥 곧바로 내다 버리겠죠."], ["I've often thought about it.", "저는 그런 걸 자주 생각해 봤어요."], ["Do you believe the Prime Minister ever has his shoes sent to a cobbler?\"", "총리대신도 구두를 구둣방에 보내신다고 보세요?”"]], [["\"Perhaps a regular shoemaker will condescend to mend a Prime Minister's shoes.\"", "“제대로 된 구두장이라면 총리대신의 구두쯤은 몸을 낮추어 고쳐 드리기도 하겠지.”"]], [["\"You do think they are mended then?", "“그러면 고쳐 신기는 하신다고 보시는 거네요?"], ["But who orders it?", "그런데 그건 누가 시키죠?"], ["Does he see himself when there's a little hole coming, as I do?", "저처럼 그분도 조그만 구멍이 나기 시작하는 걸 직접 알아보실까요?"], ["Does an archbishop allow himself so many pairs of gloves in a year?\"", "대주교는 한 해에 장갑을 몇 켤레까지 쓰기로 정해 두실까요?”"]], [["\"Not very strictly, I should think.\"", "“그리 엄격하게 정해 두지는 않을 게다.”"]], [["\"Then I suppose it comes to this, that he has a new pair whenever he wants them.", "“그러면 결국 이렇게 되겠네요. 필요할 때마다 새것을 한 켤레씩 갖는 거요."], ["But what constitutes the want?", "그런데 필요하다는 건 무얼 두고 하는 말일까요?"], ["Does he ever say to himself that they'll do for another Sunday?", "이걸로 일요일을 한 번 더 나겠다고 스스로에게 말하는 일이 있을까요?"], ["I remember the bishop coming here once, and he had a hole at the end of his thumb.", "언젠가 주교님이 여기 오셨던 게 기억나는데, 엄지손가락 끝에 구멍이 나 있었어요."], ["I was going to be confirmed, and I remember thinking that he ought to have been smarter.\"", "저는 견진을 받게 되어 있었는데, 주교님이라면 좀 더 말끔하셔야 하는 것 아닌가 하고 생각했던 게 기억나요.”"]], [["\"Why didn't you offer to mend it?\"", "“왜 기워 드리겠다고 나서지 않았니?”"]], [["\"I shouldn't have dared for all the world.\"", "“저는 무슨 일이 있어도 그럴 엄두를 못 냈을 거예요.”"]], [["The conversation had commenced itself in a manner that did not promise much assistance to Mrs.", "이야기는 데일 부인의 계획에 별 도움이 될 성싶지 않은 모양새로 시작되어 버렸다."], ["Dale's project. When Lily got upon any subject, she was not easily induced to leave it, and when her mind had twisted itself in one direction, it was difficult to untwist it.", "릴리는 어떤 이야깃거리에 한번 올라타면 좀처럼 거기서 내려오려 하지 않았고, 마음이 한쪽으로 꼬이면 그것을 다시 풀기가 어려웠다."], ["She was now bent on a consideration of the smaller social habits of the high and mighty among us, and was asking her mother whether she supposed that the royal children ever carried halfpence in their pockets, or descended so low as fourpenny-bits.", "그녀는 이제 우리네 가운데 높고 힘 있는 이들의 자잘한 생활 습관을 따져 보는 데 골몰해서, 왕실 아이들도 주머니에 반 페니짜리 동전을 넣고 다닐까요, 아니면 기껏 내려가 봐야 사 펜스짜리 은화까지일까요 하고 어머니에게 묻고 있었다."]], [["\"I suppose they have pockets like other children,\"", "“다른 아이들처럼 주머니야 있겠죠.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["But her mother stopped her suddenly,--", "그러나 어머니가 갑자기 그녀를 멈춰 세웠다—"]], [["\"Lily, dear, I want to say something to you about John Eames.\"", "“릴리야, 존 임스 이야기를 좀 하고 싶구나.”"]], [["\"Mamma, I'd sooner talk about the Royal Family just at present.\"", "“엄마, 저는 지금 당장은 차라리 왕실 이야기를 하겠어요.”"]], [["\"But, dear, you must forgive me if I persist.", "“하지만 얘야, 내가 굽히지 않더라도 용서해 다오."], ["I have thought much about it, and I'm sure you will not oppose me when I am doing what I think to be my duty.\"", "나는 이 일을 두고 많이 생각해 보았고, 내가 내 의무라고 여기는 일을 하는데 네가 나를 막지는 않으리라고 믿는다.”"]], [["\"No, mamma; I won't oppose you, certainly.\"", "“네, 엄마. 막지 않을게요, 물론이죠.”"]], [["\"Since Mr. Crosbie's conduct was made known to you, I have mentioned his name in your hearing very seldom.\"", "“크로즈비 씨의 처사가 네게 알려진 뒤로, 나는 네가 듣는 데서 그 이름을 아주 드물게만 입에 올렸다.”"]], [["\"No, mamma, you have not.", "“네, 엄마, 그러셨어요."], ["And I have loved you so dearly for your goodness to me.", "그리고 저는 엄마의 그 착하신 마음 때문에 엄마를 그토록 애틋하게 사랑했어요."], ["Do not think that I have not understood and known how generous you have been.", "제가 엄마의 너그러움을 알아듣지도 알아보지도 못했다고는 생각하지 마세요."], ["No other mother ever was so good as you have been. I have known it all, and thought of it every day of my life, and thanked you in my heart for your trusting silence.", "저는 그걸 다 알고 있었고, 제 삶의 하루하루 그것을 생각했고, 말없이 저를 믿어 주신 것을 마음속으로 엄마께 감사드렸어요."], ["Of course, I understand your feelings.", "물론 저는 엄마의 마음을 알아요."], ["You think him bad and you hate him for what he has done.\"", "엄마는 그이를 나쁜 사람이라고 여기시고, 그이가 한 짓 때문에 그이를 미워하시죠.”"]], [["\"I would not willingly hate any one, Lily.\"", "“나는 누구든 기꺼이 미워하고 싶지는 않다, 릴리야.”"]], [["\"Ah, but you do hate him.", "“아, 하지만 엄마는 그이를 미워하세요."], ["If I were you, I should hate him; but I am not you, and I love him.", "제가 엄마였다면 저도 그이를 미워했을 거예요. 하지만 저는 엄마가 아니고, 저는 그이를 사랑해요."], ["I pray for his happiness every night and morning, and for hers.", "저는 밤마다 아침마다 그이의 행복을 위해, 그리고 그 여자의 행복을 위해 기도해요."], ["I have forgiven him altogether, and I think that he was right.", "저는 그이를 온전히 용서했고, 그이가 옳게 했다고 생각해요."], ["When I am old enough to do so without being wrong, I will go to him and tell him so.", "제가 나이가 들어서 그래도 잘못이 되지 않을 만해지면, 저는 그이에게 찾아가 그렇게 말해 줄 거예요."], ["I should like to hear of all his doings and all his success, if it were only possible.", "될 수만 있다면 그이가 하는 모든 일과 그이가 거두는 모든 성공을 다 전해 들었으면 좋겠어요."], ["How, then, can you and I talk about him?", "그러니 엄마와 제가 어떻게 그이 이야기를 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["It is impossible.", "그건 될 수 없는 일이에요."], ["You have been silent and I have been silent,--let us remain silent.\"", "엄마도 말이 없으셨고 저도 말이 없었어요—그냥 우리 말없이 있어요.”"]], [["\"It is not about Mr. Crosbie that I wish to speak.", "“내가 이야기하려는 건 크로즈비 씨에 대한 게 아니다."], ["But I think you ought to understand that conduct such as his will be rebuked by all the world.", "하지만 그 사람이 한 것 같은 처사는 온 세상이 나무랄 일이라는 것쯤은 네가 알아 두어야 한다고 나는 생각한다."], ["You may forgive him, but you should acknowledge--\"", "네가 그를 용서하는 건 좋으나, 그래도 너는 인정해야—”"]], [["\"Mamma, I don't want to acknowledge anything;--not about him.", "“엄마, 저는 아무것도 인정하고 싶지 않아요—그이에 대해서는요."], ["There are things as to which a person cannot argue.\" Mrs.", "사람이 따져 볼 수 없는 일이라는 게 있잖아요.”"], ["Dale felt that this present matter was one as to which she could not argue.", "데일 부인은 지금 이 일이야말로 자기가 따져 볼 수 없는 일이라고 느꼈다. “물론 엄마,”"], ["\"Of course, mamma,\"", "릴리가 말을 이었다."], ["continued Lily, \"I don't want to oppose you in anything, but I think we had better be silent about this.\"", "“저는 어떤 일에서도 엄마를 거스르고 싶지 않아요. 하지만 이 이야기는 우리가 하지 않는 편이 낫겠어요.”"]], [["\"Of course I am thinking only of your future happiness.\"", "“물론 나는 네 앞날의 행복만을 생각하고 있는 게다.”"]], [["\"I know you are; but pray believe me that you need not be alarmed.", "“저도 알아요. 하지만 걱정하실 것 없다는 걸 부디 믿어 주세요."], ["I do not mean to be unhappy.", "저는 불행해질 생각이 없어요."], ["Indeed, I think I may say I am not unhappy; of course I have been unhappy,--very unhappy.", "아니, 저는 불행하지 않다고 말해도 될 것 같아요. 물론 저도 불행했었죠—아주 많이 불행했어요."], ["I did think that my heart would break.", "제 마음이 부서질 거라고 정말로 생각했어요."], ["But that has passed away, and I believe I can be as happy as my neighbours.", "하지만 그건 다 지나갔고, 저는 이웃 사람들만큼은 행복할 수 있다고 믿어요."], ["We're all of us sure to have some troubles, as you used to tell us when we were children.\"", "우리가 어렸을 때 엄마가 늘 말씀하셨듯이, 사람은 누구나 저마다 겪을 괴로움이 있는 법이잖아요.”"]], [["Mrs. Dale felt that she had begun wrong, and that she would have been able to make better progress had she omitted all mention of Crosbie's name.", "데일 부인은 자기가 시작을 잘못했다고, 크로즈비의 이름을 아예 입에 올리지 않았더라면 더 잘 나아갈 수 있었으리라고 느꼈다."], ["She knew exactly what it was that she wished to say,--what were the arguments which she desired to expound before her daughter; but she did not know what language to use, or how she might best put her thoughts into words.", "그녀는 제가 하고 싶은 말이 무엇인지, 딸 앞에 펼쳐 보이고 싶은 논거가 무엇인지 똑똑히 알고 있었다. 그러나 어떤 말을 써야 할지, 제 생각을 어떻게 말로 옮겨 놓는 것이 가장 나을지는 알지 못했다."], ["She paused for a while, and Lily went on with her work as though the conversation was over.", "그녀가 한동안 말을 멈추자 릴리는 이야기가 끝난 것처럼 다시 제 바느질을 계속했다."], ["But the conversation was not over.", "그러나 이야기는 끝난 것이 아니었다."]], [["\"It was about John Eames, and not about Mr. Crosbie, that I wished to speak to you.\"", "“내가 네게 말하고 싶었던 건 존 임스에 대한 것이지 크로즈비 씨에 대한 것이 아니란다.”"]], [["\"Oh, mamma!\"", "“아, 엄마!”"]], [["\"My dear, you must not hinder me in doing what I think to be a duty.", "“얘야, 내가 내 의무라고 여기는 일을 하는데 나를 막아서는 안 된다."], ["I heard what he said to you and what you replied, and of course I cannot but have my mind full of the subject.", "나는 그 아이가 네게 한 말과 네가 대답한 말을 다 들었고, 그러니 이 일로 마음이 가득할 수밖에 없지 않으냐."], ["Why should you set yourself against him in so fixed a manner?\"", "어찌하여 너는 그토록 요지부동으로 그 아이를 밀어내는 게냐?”"]], [["\"Because I love another man.\"", "“다른 사람을 사랑하니까요.”"], ["These words she spoke out loud, in a steady, almost dogged tone, with a certain show of audacity,--as though aware that the declaration was unseemly, but resolved that, though unseemly, it must be made.", "이 말을 그녀는 소리 내어, 흔들림 없이, 거의 고집스러운 어조로, 얼마쯤 대담한 태도로 내뱉었다—그런 말을 입에 올리는 것이 온당치 못하다는 것을 알면서도, 온당치 못할지언정 그 말은 해야만 한다고 마음을 다진 사람처럼."]], [["\"But, Lily, that love, from its very nature, must cease; or, rather, such love is not the same as that you felt when you thought that you were to be his wife.\"", "“하지만 릴리야, 그런 사랑은 그 성질로 보아 그치게 마련이다. 아니, 그보다도 그런 사랑은 네가 그 사람의 아내가 되리라 여기던 때에 느끼던 사랑과 같은 것이 아니란다.”"]], [["\"Yes, it is.", "“같은 거예요."], ["If she died, and he came to me in five years' time, I would still take him.", "그 여자가 죽고 그이가 오 년 뒤에 저에게 온다 해도, 저는 그이를 받아들일 거예요."], ["I should think myself constrained to take him.\"", "저는 그이를 받아들이지 않을 수 없다고 여길 거예요.”"]], [["\"But she is not dead, nor likely to die.\"", "“하지만 그 여자는 죽지도 않았고 죽을 성싶지도 않다.”"]], [["\"That makes no difference.", "“그건 아무 상관 없어요."], ["You don't understand me, mamma.\"", "엄마는 저를 이해하지 못하세요.”"]], [["\"I think I do, and I want you to understand me also. I know how difficult is your position; I know what your feelings are; but I know this also, that if you could reason with yourself, and bring yourself in time to receive John Eames as a dear friend--\"", "“나는 이해한다고 생각한다. 그리고 너도 나를 이해해 주었으면 한다. 네 처지가 얼마나 어려운지 나도 알고, 네 마음이 어떤지도 안다. 하지만 나는 이것도 안다. 네가 스스로 이치를 따져 보고, 때가 되어 존 임스를 소중한 벗으로 받아들일 수만 있다면—”"]], [["\"I did receive him as a dear friend.", "“저는 그이를 소중한 벗으로 받아들였는걸요."], ["Why not?", "왜 아니겠어요?"], ["He is a dear friend.", "그이는 소중한 벗이에요."], ["I love him heartily,--as you do.\"", "저는 그이를 진심으로 사랑해요—엄마가 그러시는 것처럼요.”"]], [["\"You know what I mean?\"", "“내 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알잖니?”"]], [["\"Yes, I do; and I tell you it is impossible.\"", "“네, 알아요. 그리고 저는 엄마께 그건 있을 수 없는 일이라고 말씀드리는 거예요.”"]], [["\"If you would make the attempt, all this misery would soon be forgotten.", "“네가 애를 써 보기만 한다면, 이 모든 괴로움도 머지않아 다 잊힐 게다."], ["If once you could bring yourself to regard him as a friend, who might become your husband, all this would be changed,--and I should see you happy!\"", "네가 한 번만 그 아이를 네 남편이 될 수도 있는 벗으로 여길 수 있게 된다면, 이 모든 것이 달라질 테고—나는 네가 행복해지는 것을 보게 될 텐데!”"]], [["\"You are strangely anxious to be rid of me, mamma!\"", "“엄마는 이상하게도 저를 떼어 놓지 못해 안달이시네요!”"]], [["\"Yes, Lily;--to be rid of you in that way.", "“그래, 릴리야—그런 식으로 너를 떼어 놓고 싶구나."], ["If I could see you put your hand in his as his promised wife, I think that I should be the happiest woman in the world.\"", "네가 그 아이의 약혼한 아내로서 그 아이의 손에 네 손을 얹는 것을 볼 수만 있다면, 나는 이 세상에서 가장 행복한 여자가 될 것 같다.”"]], [["\"Mamma, I cannot make you happy in that way.", "“엄마, 저는 그런 식으로는 엄마를 행복하게 해 드릴 수 없어요."], ["If you really understood my feelings, my doing as you propose would make you very unhappy.", "엄마가 제 마음을 정말로 아신다면, 엄마가 말씀하시는 대로 하는 것이 엄마를 몹시 불행하게 만들 거예요."], ["I should commit a great sin,--the sin against which women should be more guarded than against any other.", "저는 큰 죄를 짓게 될 테니까요—여자라면 다른 어떤 죄보다도 더 조심해야 할 죄를요."], ["In my heart I am married to that other man.", "제 마음속에서 저는 그 다른 사람과 혼인한 몸이에요."], ["I gave myself to him, and loved him, and rejoiced in his love.", "저는 저를 그이에게 내주었고, 그이를 사랑했고, 그이의 사랑을 기뻐했어요."], ["When he kissed me I kissed him again, and I longed for his kisses.", "그이가 저에게 입 맞추면 저도 그이에게 입 맞추었고, 저는 그이의 입맞춤을 애타게 기다렸어요."], ["I seemed to live only that he might caress me.", "저는 그이가 저를 어루만져 주라고 살아 있는 것만 같았어요."], ["All that time I never felt myself to be wrong,--because he was all in all to me.", "그러는 동안 내내 저는 제가 잘못하고 있다고 느껴 본 적이 없어요—그이가 저에게 전부였으니까요."], ["I was his own.", "저는 그이의 것이었어요."], ["That has been changed,--to my great misfortune; but it cannot be undone or forgotten.", "그것이 달라졌지요—저에게는 크나큰 불행이지만요. 하지만 그것을 없던 일로 돌리거나 잊어버릴 수는 없어요."], ["I cannot be the girl I was before he came here.", "저는 그이가 여기 오기 전의 그 계집아이로 돌아갈 수 없어요."], ["There are things that will not have themselves buried and put out of sight, as though they had never been.", "애초에 없었던 일인 양 파묻혀 눈앞에서 치워지기를 마다하는 것들이 있는 법이에요."], ["I am as you are, mamma,--widowed.", "저도 엄마와 같아요, 엄마—과부예요."], ["But you have your daughter, and I have my mother.", "하지만 엄마에게는 딸이 있고, 저에게는 어머니가 있잖아요."], ["If you will be contented, so will I.\"", "엄마가 그것으로 만족하시겠다면 저도 만족할게요.”"], ["Then she got up and threw herself on her mother's neck.", "그러고는 그녀가 일어나 어머니의 목을 끌어안았다."]], [["Mrs. Dale's argument was over now.", "이제 데일 부인의 논변은 끝났다."], ["To such an appeal as that last made by Lily no rejoinder on her part was possible.", "릴리가 마지막으로 내놓은 그런 호소 앞에서 그녀 쪽이 무슨 대꾸를 할 수 있겠는가."], ["After that she was driven to acknowledge to herself that she must be silent.", "그 뒤로 그녀는 잠자코 있을 수밖에 없다고 스스로에게 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Years as they rolled on might make a change, but no reasoning could be of avail.", "세월이 흐르면 달라지는 것이 있을지 몰라도 어떤 논리로도 소용이 없을 터였다."], ["She embraced her daughter, weeping over her,--whereas Lily's eyes were dry.", "그녀는 딸을 끌어안고 그 위로 눈물을 흘렸다—릴리의 눈은 말라 있었건만."], ["\"It shall be as you will,\"", "“네 뜻대로 하마.”"], ["Mrs. Dale murmured.", "데일 부인이 나직이 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, as I will.", "“네, 제 뜻대로요."], ["I shall have my own way; shall I not?", "제 마음대로 하겠어요. 그래도 되죠?"], ["That is all I want; to be a tyrant over you, and make you do my bidding in everything, as a well-behaved mother should do.", "제가 바라는 건 그것뿐이에요. 엄마 위에 군림하는 폭군이 되어서, 얌전한 어머니답게 무슨 일이든 제가 시키는 대로 하시게 만드는 거요."], ["But I won't be stern in my orderings.", "그래도 저는 그리 매섭게 시키지는 않을게요."], ["If you will only be obedient, I will be so gracious to you!", "엄마가 말만 잘 들으시면 저는 아주 자비로울 거예요!"], ["There's Hopkins again.", "저기 홉킨스가 또 왔네요."], ["I wonder whether he has come to knock us down and trample upon us with another speech.\"", "우리를 또 한바탕 연설로 때려눕히고 짓밟으러 온 걸까요.”"]], [["Hopkins knew very well to which window he must come, as only one of the rooms was at the present time habitable.", "홉킨스는 어느 창으로 와야 하는지 잘 알고 있었으니, 지금 이 집에서 사람이 들 만한 방은 하나뿐이었기 때문이다."], ["He came up to the dining-room, and almost flattened his nose against the glass.", "그는 식당방으로 올라와 유리창에 코를 거의 납작하게 눌러 붙였다."]], [["\"Well, Hopkins,\"", "“자, 홉킨스,”"], ["said Lily, \"here we are.\"", "릴리가 말했다. “우리 여기 있어요.”"], ["Mrs. Dale had turned her face away, for she knew that the tears were still on her cheek.", "데일 부인은 얼굴을 돌려 버렸으니, 뺨에 아직 눈물이 남아 있는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["\"Yes, miss, I see you.", "“예, 아가씨, 보입니다요."], ["I want to speak to your mamma, miss.\"", "마님께 여쭐 말씀이 있어서 왔습니다요, 아가씨.”"]], [["\"Come round,\"", "“돌아서 오세요.”"], ["said Lily, anxious to spare her mother the necessity of showing herself at once.", "어머니가 당장 얼굴을 내보이지 않아도 되도록 해 주고 싶어서 릴리가 말했다."], ["\"It's too cold to open the window; come round, and I'll open the door.\"", "“창을 열기에는 너무 추워요. 돌아서 오면 제가 문을 열어 줄게요.”"]], [["\"Too cold!\"", "“너무 춥다니!”"], ["muttered Hopkins, as he went.", "홉킨스가 가면서 중얼거렸다."], ["\"They'll find it a deal colder in lodgings at Guestwick.\"", "“게스트윅 셋집에서는 훨씬 더 추운 맛을 보시게 될 텐데.”"], ["However, he went round through the kitchen, and Lily met him in the hall.", "그러면서도 그는 부엌 쪽으로 돌아서 왔고, 릴리가 현관에서 그를 맞았다."]], [["\"Well, Hopkins, what is it?", "“자, 홉킨스, 무슨 일이에요?"], ["Mamma has got a headache.\"", "엄마가 머리가 아프시대요.”"]], [["\"Got a headache, has she?", "“머리가 아프시다고요?"], ["I won't make her headache no worse.", "제가 마님 머리를 더 아프게 해 드리지는 않겠습니다요."], ["It's my opinion that there's nothing for a headache so good as fresh air.", "제 소견으로는 머리 아픈 데에는 신선한 바람만 한 것이 없습니다요."], ["Only some people can't abear to be blowed upon, not for a minute.", "다만 어떤 분들은 단 일 분도 바람 쐬는 걸 못 견디시지요."], ["If you don't let down the lights in a greenhouse more or less every day, you'll never get any plants,--never;--and it's just the same with the grapes.", "온실 유리창을 날마다 얼마쯤이라도 내려 주지 않으면 화초라고는 하나도 못 얻습니다요—절대로요. 포도도 꼭 그와 같습지요."], ["Is I to go back and say as how I couldn't see her?\"", "제가 돌아가서 마님을 뵙지 못했다고 여쭐깝쇼?”"]], [["\"You can come in if you like; only be quiet, you know.\"", "“들어오고 싶으면 들어와도 돼요. 다만 조용히 해야 해요, 알죠.”"]], [["\"Ain't I ollays quiet, miss?", "“제가 늘 조용하지 않습니까요, 아가씨?"], ["Did anybody ever hear me rampage?", "제가 난동 부리는 걸 누가 들어 본 적이 있습니까요?"], ["If you please, ma'am, the squire's come home.\"", "실례합니다요, 마님, 나리께서 돌아오셨습니다요.”"]], [["\"What, home from Guestwick?", "“뭐라고요, 게스트윅에서 돌아오셨어요?"], ["Has he brought Miss Bell?\"", "벨 아가씨도 데려오셨대요?”"]], [["\"He ain't brought none but hisself, 'cause he come on horseback; and it's my belief he's going back almost immediate.", "“나리 혼자만 오셨습니다요, 말을 타고 오셨거든입쇼. 그리고 제 짐작으로는 거의 곧바로 도로 가실 것 같습니다요."], ["But he wants you to come to him, Mrs. Dale.\"", "그런데 나리께서 마님더러 좀 와 주십사 하십니다요, 데일 마님.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, I'll come at once.\"", "“아, 그래, 곧 가마.”"]], [["\"He bade me say with his kind love.", "“꼭 안부 전해 드리라고 하셨습니다요."], ["I don't know whether that makes any difference.\"", "그게 무슨 차이가 있는지야 저는 모릅니다만요.”"]], [["\"At any rate, I'll come, Hopkins.\"", "“어쨌든 가마, 홉킨스.”"]], [["\"And I ain't to say nothing about the headache?\"", "“그러면 머리 아프시다는 말씀은 안 드려도 됩니까요?”"]], [["\"About what?\"", "“무슨 말이냐?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"No, no, no,\" said Lily.", "“아녜요, 아녜요, 아녜요.”"], ["\"Mamma will be there at once.", "릴리가 말했다. “엄마가 곧 그리로 가실 거예요."], ["Go and tell my uncle, there's a good man,\"", "가서 우리 삼촌께 그리 전해 줘요, 착하기도 하지.”"], ["and she put up her hand and backed him out of the room.", "그러면서 그녀는 손을 들어 그를 방 밖으로 밀어냈다."]], [["\"I don't believe she's got no headache at all,\"", "“머리가 아프기는 무슨, 하나도 안 아프시구먼.”"], ["said Hopkins, grumbling, as he returned through the back premises.", "홉킨스는 뒷마당을 지나 돌아가면서 투덜거렸다."], ["\"What lies gentlefolks do tell!", "“지체 높은 양반님네들은 어쩌면 저리 거짓말을 잘도 하실까!"], ["If I said I'd a headache when I ought to be out among the things, what would they say to me?", "내가 밖에 나가 일감들 사이에 있어야 할 판에 머리가 아프다고 했다가는 나한테 무슨 말씀을 하실까?"], ["But a poor man mustn't never lie, nor yet drink, nor yet do nothing.\"", "그런데 가난한 사람은 절대로 거짓말도 못 하지, 술도 못 마시지, 아무것도 못 하지.”"], ["And so he went back with his message.", "그러고는 제 전할 말을 들고 돌아갔다."]], [["\"What can have brought your uncle home?\"", "“네 삼촌이 무슨 일로 집에 돌아오셨을까?”"], ["said Mrs. Dale.", "데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["\"Just to look after the cattle, and to see that the pigs are not all dead.", "“그저 가축들이 어떤지 보시고, 돼지들이 다 죽지는 않았는지 살피시려는 거죠."], ["My wonder is that he should ever have gone away.\"", "저는 삼촌이 도대체 어떻게 떠나기는 하셨는지가 더 신기해요.”"]], [["\"I must go up to him at once.\"", "“당장 올라가 봐야겠다.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, of course.\"", "“네, 그럼요.”"]], [["\"And what shall I say about the house?\"", "“그런데 집 이야기는 뭐라고 해야 하니?”"]], [["\"It's not about that,--at least I think not.", "“그 일 때문은 아니에요—적어도 저는 아니라고 생각해요."], ["I don't think he'll speak about that again till you speak to him.\"", "엄마가 먼저 꺼내시기 전에는 삼촌도 그 이야기를 다시 하지 않으실 것 같아요.”"]], [["\"But if he does?\"", "“그래도 하시면?”"]], [["\"You must put your trust in Providence.", "“섭리에 맡기셔야죠."], ["Declare you've got a bad headache, as I told Hopkins just now; only you would throw me over by not understanding.", "아까 제가 홉킨스한테 한 것처럼 머리가 몹시 아프다고 하세요. 그저 엄마가 알아듣지 못하시고 저를 저버리셨을 뿐이지만요."], ["I'll walk with you down to the bridge.\"", "다리까지는 제가 같이 걸어 드릴게요.”"], ["So they went off together across the lawn.", "그리하여 두 사람은 함께 잔디밭을 가로질러 갔다."]], [["But Lily was soon left alone, and continued her walk, waiting for her mother's return.", "그러나 릴리는 곧 혼자가 되었고, 어머니가 돌아오기를 기다리며 그대로 산책을 이어 갔다."], ["As she went round and round the gravel paths, she thought of the words that she had said to her mother.", "자갈길을 빙빙 돌면서 그녀는 어머니에게 했던 말들을 생각했다."], ["She had declared that she also was widowed.", "그녀는 자기도 과부라고 선언한 것이었다."], ["\"And so it should be,\"", "“그리고 마땅히 그래야 해.”"], ["she said, debating the matter with herself.", "그녀는 그 일을 스스로와 따져 보며 말했다."], ["\"What can a heart be worth if it can be transferred hither and thither as circumstances and convenience and comfort may require?", "“형편이나 편의나 안락함이 요구하는 대로 이리저리 옮겨 갈 수 있는 것이라면, 마음이란 게 대체 무슨 값어치가 있담?"], ["When he held me here in his arms\"--and, as the thoughts ran through her brain, she remembered the very spot on which they had stood--\"oh, my love!\"", "그이가 나를 여기서 이 팔에 안았을 때”—그리고 이런저런 생각이 머릿속을 스쳐 갈 때 그녀는 두 사람이 서 있던 바로 그 자리를 떠올렸다—“오, 내 사랑!”"], ["she had said to him then as she returned his kisses--\"oh, my love, my love, my love!\"", "그때 그녀는 그의 입맞춤에 답하며 그에게 그렇게 말했었다—“오, 내 사랑, 내 사랑, 내 사랑!”"], ["\"When he held me here in his arms, I told myself that it was right, because he was my husband.", "“그이가 나를 여기서 이 팔에 안았을 때, 나는 그이가 내 남편이니 이것이 옳은 일이라고 스스로에게 말했지."], ["He has changed, but I have not.", "그이는 변했지만 나는 변하지 않았어."], ["It might be that I should have ceased to love him, and then I should have told him so.", "내가 그이를 사랑하지 않게 되었을 수도 있고, 그랬다면 나도 그이에게 그렇게 말했겠지."], ["I should have done as he did.\"", "나도 그이가 한 대로 했을 거야.”"], ["But, as she came to this, she shuddered, thinking of the Lady Alexandrina.", "그러나 여기에 이르자 그녀는 레이디 알렉산드리나를 떠올리며 몸서리를 쳤다."], ["\"It was very quick,\"", "“너무 빨랐어.”"], ["she said, still speaking to herself; \"very, very.", "그녀는 여전히 혼잣말로 말했다. “너무, 너무나."], ["But then men are not the same as women.\"", "하지만 사내들은 여자와 같지 않으니까.”"], ["And she walked on eagerly, hardly remembering where she was, thinking over it all, as she did daily; remembering every little thought and word of those few eventful months in which she had learned to regard Crosbie as her husband and master.", "그러고는 자기가 어디에 있는지도 거의 잊은 채 열심히 걸음을 옮기며, 날마다 그러하듯 그 모든 것을 되짚어 보았다. 크로즈비를 제 남편이자 주인으로 여기게 되었던 저 파란 많던 몇 달의 자잘한 생각과 말을 하나하나 되새기면서."], ["She had declared that she had conquered her unhappiness; but there were moments in which she was almost wild with misery.", "그녀는 제 불행을 이겨 냈다고 선언했으나, 비참함에 거의 미칠 것 같은 순간들이 있었다."], ["\"Tell me to forget him!\"", "“나더러 그이를 잊으라고!”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"It is the one thing which will never be forgotten.\"", "“그것이야말로 영영 잊히지 않을 단 하나의 일인데.”"]], [["At last she heard her mother's step coming down across the squire's garden, and she took up her post at the bridge.", "마침내 그녀는 어머니의 발소리가 지주의 정원을 가로질러 내려오는 것을 듣고 다리 위에 제 자리를 잡았다."]], [["\"Stand and deliver,\"", "“서라, 가진 걸 내놓아라.”"], ["she said, as her mother put her foot upon the plank.", "어머니가 판자다리에 발을 올려놓았을 때 그녀가 말했다."], ["\"That is, if you've got anything worth delivering.", "“아니, 내놓을 만한 게 있다면 말이지만요."], ["Is anything settled?\"", "뭐가 정해졌어요?”"]], [["\"Come up to the house,\" said Mrs.", "“집으로 올라가자.”"], ["Dale, \"and I'll tell you all.\"", "데일 부인이 말했다. “가서 다 얘기해 주마.”"]], [["# CHAPTER LVIII. THE FATE OF THE SMALL HOUSE.", "# 제58장. 작은 집의 운명"]], [["There was something in the tone of Mrs. Dale's voice, as she desired her daughter to come up to the house, and declared that her budget of news should be opened there, which at once silenced Lily's assumed pleasantry.", "데일 부인이 딸에게 집으로 올라오라고 이르며 자기가 가져온 소식 보따리는 거기서 풀겠노라고 말했을 때, 그 목소리의 어조에는 릴리가 짐짓 꾸며 보이던 농기를 대번에 잠재우는 무엇인가가 있었다."], ["Her mother had been away fully two hours, during which Lily had still continued her walk round the garden, till at last she had become impatient for her mother's footstep.", "어머니는 꼬박 두 시간을 나가 있었고, 그동안 릴리는 여전히 정원을 빙빙 돌며 걷다가 마침내는 어머니의 발소리를 애타게 기다리게 되었다."], ["Something serious must have been said between her uncle and her mother during those long two hours. The interviews to which Mrs.", "그 긴 두 시간 동안 삼촌과 어머니 사이에 무언가 심상찮은 이야기가 오갔음이 틀림없었다."], ["Dale was occasionally summoned at the Great House did not usually exceed twenty minutes, and the upshot would be communicated to the girls in a turn or two round the garden; but in the present instance Mrs.", "데일 부인이 이따금 큰 집으로 불려 가 갖는 면담은 보통 이십 분을 넘지 않았고, 그 결말은 정원을 한두 바퀴 도는 사이에 딸들에게 전해지곤 했다."], ["Dale positively declined to speak till she was seated within the house.", "그러나 이번만은 데일 부인이 집 안에 들어가 자리를 잡기 전에는 한사코 입을 열려 하지 않았다."]], [["\"Did he come over on purpose to see you, mamma?\"", "“삼촌이 엄마를 보러 일부러 오신 거예요?”"]], [["\"Yes, my dear, I believe so.", "“그래, 얘야, 그런 것 같더구나."], ["He wished to see you, too; but I asked his permission to postpone that till after I had talked to you.\"", "너도 보고 싶어 하셨단다. 하지만 내가 너와 이야기를 나눈 뒤로 미뤄 달라고 청했지.”"]], [["\"To see me, mamma?", "“저를요, 엄마?"], ["About what?\"", "무슨 일로요?”"]], [["\"To kiss you, and bid you love him; solely for that.", "“너에게 입 맞추고 자기를 사랑해 달라 하시려는 거란다."], ["He has not a word to say to you that will vex you.\"", "오직 그것뿐이야. 너를 언짢게 할 말은 한마디도 없으시다.”"]], [["\"Then I will kiss him, and love him, too.\"", "“그럼 저도 삼촌께 입 맞추고 사랑해 드릴래요.”"]], [["\"Yes, you will when I have told you all.", "“그래, 내 이야기를 다 듣고 나면 그러고 싶어질 게다."], ["I have promised him solemnly to give up all idea of going to Guestwick.", "나는 게스트윅으로 갈 생각을 아주 접겠노라고 그분께 엄숙히 약속드렸다."], ["So that is over.\"", "그러니 그 일은 끝난 거야.”"]], [["\"Oh, oh!", "“어머, 어머!"], ["And we may begin to unpack at once?", "그럼 우리 당장 짐을 풀어도 되는 거예요?"], ["What an episode in one's life!\"", "사람 일생에 이런 막간극이 다 있네!”"]], [["\"We may certainly unpack, for I have pledged myself to him; and he is to go into Guestwick himself and arrange about the lodgings.\"", "“짐이야 물론 풀어도 되지. 내가 그분께 다짐을 두었으니까. 그리고 그분이 몸소 게스트윅에 들어가서 셋집 일을 정리하실 거란다.”"]], [["\"Does Hopkins know it?\"", "“홉킨스는 알아요?”"]], [["\"I should think not yet.\"", "“아직은 모를 게다.”"]], [["\"Nor Mrs. Boyce!", "“보이스 부인도 모르겠네요!"], ["Mamma, I don't believe I shall be able to survive this next week.", "엄마, 저는 이 다음 한 주를 어떻게 견딜지 모르겠어요. 우리 꼴이 얼마나 우스울까요!"], ["We shall look such fools!", "우리 이렇게 해요. 그게 제가 누릴 수 있는 유일한 위안일 테니까요."], ["I'll tell you what we'll do;--it will be the only comfort I can have;--we'll go to work and get everything back into its place before Bell comes home, so as to surprise her.\"", "벨 언니가 집에 돌아오기 전에 얼른 손을 놀려 죄다 제자리에 돌려놓는 거예요. 언니를 깜짝 놀라게 해 주게요.”"]], [["\"What!", "“뭐라고!"], ["in two days?\"", "이틀 만에?”"]], [["\"Why not?", "“왜 못 해요?"], ["I'll make Hopkins come and help, and then he'll not be so bad.", "홉킨스를 불러다 거들게 할 거예요. 그러면 그이도 그렇게 심통을 부리진 않겠죠."], ["I'll begin at once and go to the blankets and beds, because I can undo them myself.\"", "당장 시작해서 담요랑 침대부터 손댈래요. 그건 저 혼자서도 풀 수 있으니까요.”"]], [["\"But I haven't half told you all; and, indeed, I don't know how to make you understand what passed between us.", "“하지만 아직 절반도 이야기하지 않았단다. 게다가 나도 우리 사이에 오간 이야기를 너에게 어떻게 알아듣게 전해야 할지 모르겠구나."], ["He is very unhappy about Bernard; Bernard has determined to go abroad, and may be away for years.\"", "그분은 버나드 일로 몹시 상심해 계셔. 버나드가 외국으로 나가기로 마음을 굳혔고, 몇 해씩 떠나 있게 될지도 모른다는구나.”"]], [["\"One can hardly blame a man for following up his profession.\"", "“제 직분을 좇아가겠다는 사람을 나무랄 수야 없죠.”"]], [["\"There was no blaming.", "“나무라시는 건 아니었어."], ["He only said that it was very sad for him that, in his old age, he should be left alone.", "다만 늘그막에 혼자 남겨지게 되었으니 자신에게 참 서글픈 일이라고만 하셨지."], ["This was before there was any talk about our remaining.", "이건 우리가 여기 남는 이야기가 나오기 전이었단다."], ["Indeed he seemed determined not to ask that again as a favour.", "사실 그분은 그 일을 다시 청으로 꺼내지는 않겠다고 작정하신 듯했어."], ["I could see that in his eye, and I understood it from his tone.", "눈빛에서 그게 보였고, 말투에서 알아차렸지."], ["He went on to speak of you and Bell, saying how well he loved you both; but that, unfortunately, his hopes regarding you had not been fulfilled.\"", "이어서 너와 벨 이야기를 하시면서, 둘 다 얼마나 사랑하는지 모른다고, 다만 안타깝게도 너희에 대해 품었던 바람이 이루어지지 못했다고 하셨다.”"]], [["\"Ah, but he shouldn't have had hopes of that sort.\"", "“아, 하지만 애초에 그런 바람은 품지 말아야 하셨죠.”"]], [["\"Listen, my dear, and I think that you will not feel angry with him.", "“들어 보렴, 얘야. 다 듣고 나면 너도 그분께 화가 나지는 않을 게다."], ["He said that he felt his house had never been pleasant to you.", "그분은 이 집이 너희에게 한 번도 즐거운 곳이 되어 주지 못했다는 걸 느끼신다고 하셨어."], ["Then there followed words which I could not repeat, even if I could remember them.", "그다음에는, 기억이 난다 한들 옮길 수 없는 말들이 이어졌지."], ["He said much about myself, regretting that the feeling between us had not been more kindly. 'But my heart,'", "우리 사이가 좀 더 다정하지 못했던 것을 애석해하시며 나에 대해서도 많은 말씀을 하셨다."], ["he said, 'has ever been kinder than my words.'", "‘하지만 내 마음은,’ 하고 그분이 말씀하시더구나."], ["Then I got up from where I was seated, and going over to him, I told him that we would remain here.\"", "‘언제나 내 말보다는 따뜻했소.’ 그래서 나는 앉아 있던 자리에서 일어나 그분께 다가가서, 우리가 여기 남겠노라고 말씀드렸다.”"]], [["\"And what did he say?\"", "“그랬더니 뭐라고 하셨어요?”"]], [["\"I don't know what he said.", "“뭐라고 하셨는지 모르겠구나."], ["I know that I was crying, and that he kissed me.", "내가 울고 있었다는 것, 그리고 그분이 내게 입을 맞추셨다는 것만 알아."], ["It was the first time in his life.", "그분 평생에 처음 있는 일이었지."], ["I know that he was pleased,--beyond measure pleased.", "그분이 기뻐하셨다는 것도 안다. 이루 말할 수 없이 기뻐하셨어."], ["After a while he became animated, and talked of doing ever so many things.", "조금 지나자 생기가 도셔서 이런저런 일을 해 주마 하고 말씀하시더구나."], ["He promised that very painting of which you spoke.\"", "네가 말하던 바로 그 페인트칠도 해 주겠다고 약속하셨다.”"]], [["\"Ah, yes, I knew it; and Hopkins will be here with the peas before dinner-time to-morrow, and Dingles with his shoulders smothered with rabbits.", "“아, 그럼요, 저도 알고 있었어요. 그리고 내일 저녁 식사 전에는 홉킨스가 완두를 들고 나타날 거고, 딩글스는 어깨가 파묻히도록 토끼를 짊어지고 올 거예요."], ["And then Mrs. Boyce!", "그리고 보이스 부인은요!"], ["Mamma, he didn't think of Mrs.", "엄마, 삼촌은 보이스 부인 생각은 못 하셨나 봐요."], ["Boyce; or, in very charity of heart, he would still have maintained his sadness.\"", "아니면 순전한 인정으로라도 그 서글픔을 그대로 간직하고 계셨을 텐데.”"]], [["\"Then he did not think of her; for when I left him he was not at all sad.", "“그래, 그 부인 생각은 못 하셨지. 내가 자리를 뜰 때 그분은 조금도 서글퍼 보이지 않으셨으니까."], ["But I haven't told you half yet.\"", "하지만 아직 절반도 이야기하지 않았다니까.”"]], [["\"Dear me, mamma; was there more than that?\"", "“어머나, 엄마. 그것 말고 또 있었어요?”"]], [["\"And I've told it all wrong; for what I've got to tell now was said before a word was spoken about the house.", "“게다가 순서도 다 뒤죽박죽으로 말해 버렸구나. 지금 하려는 이야기는 집 문제가 나오기 전에 오간 것이니까 말이다."], ["He brought it in just after what he said about Bernard.", "버나드 이야기를 하시고 바로 이어서 꺼내셨어."], ["He said that Bernard would, of course, be his heir.\"", "버나드가 당연히 자기 상속인이 될 거라고 하시더구나.”"]], [["\"Of course he will.\"", "“당연히 그렇겠죠.”"]], [["\"And that he should think it wrong to encumber the property with any charges for you girls.\"", "“그리고 너희 아가씨들 몫으로 재산에 부담을 지우는 것은 옳지 않다고 여기신다고 하셨다.”"]], [["\"Mamma, did any one ever--\"", "“엄마, 세상에 어떻게 그런—”"]], [["\"Stop, Lily, stop; and make your heart kinder towards him if you can.\"", "“그만, 릴리, 그만하렴. 그리고 될 수 있으면 그분께 마음을 좀 더 너그럽게 가져 보렴.”"]], [["\"It is kind; only I hate to be told that I'm not to have a lot of money, as though I had ever shown a desire for it.", "“너그러워요. 다만 저는 돈을 탐낸 적도 없는데 내 몫으로 큰돈이 오지 않을 거라는 말을 듣는 게 싫을 뿐이에요."], ["I have never envied Bernard his man-servant, or his maid-servant, or his ox, or his ass, or anything that is his.", "저는 버나드의 남종이든 여종이든, 그의 소든 나귀든, 그에게 속한 그 무엇이든 부러워한 적이 없어요."], ["To tell the truth I didn't even wish it to be Bell's, because I knew well that there was somebody she would like a great deal better than ever she could like Bernard.\"", "솔직히 말하면 그게 벨 언니 몫이 되는 것조차 바라지 않았어요. 언니한테는 버나드보다 훨씬 더 좋아할 만한 사람이 따로 있다는 걸 저는 잘 알고 있었으니까요.”"]], [["\"I shall never get to the end of my story.\"", "“나는 도무지 내 이야기를 끝까지 못 하겠구나.”"]], [["\"Yes, you will, mamma, if you persevere.\"", "“아니에요, 엄마. 끈기 있게 하시면 되죠.”"]], [["\"The long and the short of it is this, that he has given Bell three thousand pounds, and has given you three thousand also.\"", "“요컨대 이런 이야기다. 그분이 벨에게 삼천 파운드를 주셨고, 너에게도 삼천 파운드를 주셨어.”"]], [["\"But why me, mamma?\"", "“그런데 왜 저한테요, 엄마?”"], ["said Lily, and the colour of her cheeks became red as she spoke.", "릴리가 말했다. 말하는 동안 두 뺨이 붉게 물들었다."], ["There should if possible be nothing more said about John Eames; but whatever might or might not be the necessity of speaking, at any rate, let there be no mistake.", "존 임스에 대해서는 될 수 있는 대로 더는 아무 말도 없어야 했다. 그러나 말을 해야 할 필요가 있든 없든, 어쨌든 오해만은 없게 해 두어야 했다."], ["\"But why me, mamma?\"", "“그런데 왜 저한테요, 엄마?”"]], [["\"Because, as he explained to me, he thinks it right to do the same by each of you.", "“그분이 내게 설명하시기를, 너희 둘에게 똑같이 해 주는 것이 옳다고 여기신다는구나."], ["The money is yours at this moment,--to buy hair-pins with, if you please.", "그 돈은 지금 이 순간부터 네 것이란다. 네가 좋다면 머리핀을 사도 되고."], ["I had no idea that he could command so large a sum.\"", "나는 그분이 그만큼 큰 돈을 마음대로 하실 수 있는 줄은 몰랐다.”"]], [["\"Three thousand pounds!", "“삼천 파운드라니!"], ["The last money he gave me was half-a-crown, and I thought that he was so stingy!", "마지막으로 저한테 주신 돈은 반 크라운이었고, 저는 삼촌이 어찌나 인색하신가 했는걸요."], ["I particularly wanted ten shillings.", "그때 저는 특히 십 실링이 필요했거든요."], ["I should have liked it so much better now if he had given me a nice new five-pound note.\"", "지금 같아서는 차라리 산뜻한 새 오 파운드짜리 지폐 한 장을 주셨더라면 훨씬 더 좋았을 텐데.”"]], [["\"You'd better tell him so.\"", "“그렇게 말씀드려 보렴.”"]], [["\"No; because then he'd give me that too.", "“안 돼요. 그러면 그것까지 주실 테니까요."], ["But with five pounds I should have the feeling that I might do what I liked with it;--buy a dressing-case, and a thing for a squirrel to run round in.", "하지만 오 파운드라면 제 마음대로 써도 된다는 기분이 들 거예요. 화장 상자도 사고, 다람쥐가 안에서 돌리며 뛰노는 쳇바퀴도 사고요."], ["But nobody ever gives girls money like that, so that they can enjoy it.\"", "그렇지만 아무도 여자애한테 그렇게 즐길 수 있는 돈은 주지 않죠.”"]], [["\"Oh, Lily; you ungrateful child!\"", "“저런, 릴리. 이 배은망덕한 것 같으니!”"]], [["\"No, I deny it. I'm not ungrateful.", "“아니에요, 저는 아니라고 우길래요."], ["I'm very grateful, because his heart was softened--and because he cried and kissed you.", "저는 배은망덕하지 않아요. 저는 아주 고마워하고 있어요."], ["I'll be ever so good to him!", "삼촌 마음이 누그러지셨으니까요."], ["But how I'm to thank him for giving me three thousand pounds, I cannot think.", "그리고 우시면서 엄마께 입을 맞추셨으니까요. 저 앞으로 삼촌께 얼마나 잘해 드릴 건데요!"], ["It's a sort of thing altogether beyond my line of life.", "하지만 삼천 파운드를 주신 걸 두고 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지는 도무지 모르겠어요."], ["It sounds like something that's to come to me in another world, but which I don't want quite yet.", "그건 아예 제 삶의 테두리 밖에 있는 일이거든요. 다른 세상에 가서야 제게 올 무엇처럼 들리는데, 저는 그걸 아직은 그리 받고 싶지 않아요."], ["I am grateful, but with a misty, mazy sort of gratitude.", "고맙기는 해요. 그런데 안개가 낀 듯 아리송한 고마움이에요."], ["Can you tell me how soon I shall have a new pair of Balmoral boots because of this money?", "이 돈 덕분에 제가 언제쯤 새 발모럴 부츠를 한 켤레 얻게 될지 엄마가 말해 주실 수 있어요?"], ["If that were brought home to me I think it would enliven my gratitude.\"", "그것만 손에 잡히게 알 수 있다면 제 고마움도 활기를 띨 것 같은데요.”"]], [["The squire, as he rode back to Guestwick, fell again from that animation, which Mrs. Dale had described, into his natural sombre mood.", "지주는 게스트윅으로 말을 몰아 돌아가면서, 데일 부인이 묘사했던 그 생기에서 다시 본래의 침울한 기분으로 가라앉았다."], ["He thought much of his past life, declaring to himself the truth of those words in which he had told his sister-in-law that his heart had ever been kinder than his words.", "그는 지나온 삶을 오래 생각하며, 자기 마음은 언제나 자기 말보다 따뜻했노라고 제수에게 했던 그 말이 참이라고 스스로에게 다짐했다."], ["But the world, and all those nearest to him in the world, had judged him always by his words rather than by his heart.", "그러나 세상은, 그리고 세상에서 그와 가장 가까운 이들은 모두 늘 그의 마음이 아니라 그의 말로 그를 판단해 왔다."], ["They had taken the appearance, which he could not command or alter, rather than the facts, of which he had been the master.", "그들은 그가 마음대로 하거나 바꿀 수 없는 겉모습을 취했을 뿐, 그가 주인이었던 사실은 취하지 않았다."], ["Had he not been good to all his relations?--and yet was there one among them that cared for him?", "그는 제 친척 모두에게 잘해 주지 않았던가. 그런데도 그중에 그를 아끼는 이가 하나라도 있었던가?"], ["\"I'm almost sorry that they are going to stay,\"", "“그들이 남기로 한 것이 차라리 안된 일이지 싶다.”"], ["he said to himself;--\"I know that I shall disappoint them.\"", "그는 혼잣말을 했다. “내가 그들을 실망시킬 게 뻔하니까.”"], ["Yet when he met Bell at the Manor House he accosted her cheerily, telling her with much appearance of satisfaction that that flitting into Guestwick was not to be accomplished.", "그러면서도 저택에서 벨을 만나자 그는 쾌활하게 말을 걸었고, 게스트윅으로 옮겨 가는 일은 이루어지지 않게 되었노라고 자못 흡족한 기색으로 알렸다."]], [["\"I am so glad,\"", "“정말 기뻐요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"It is long since I wished it.\"", "“저는 오래전부터 그러기를 바랐는걸요.”"]], [["\"And I do not think your mother wishes it now.\"", "“네 어머니도 이제는 그걸 바라지 않는 것 같더구나.”"]], [["\"I am sure she does not.", "“당연히 안 바라시죠."], ["It was all a misunderstanding from the first.", "처음부터 다 오해였어요."], ["When some of us could not do all that you wished, we thought it better--\"", "저희 가운데 누가 삼촌이 바라시는 대로 다 해 드리지 못하니까, 저희는 그게 낫겠다고 생각했던—”"], ["Then Bell paused, finding that she would get herself into a mess if she persevered.", "여기서 벨은 말을 멈췄다. 계속하다가는 곤란한 지경에 빠지겠다 싶었던 것이다."]], [["\"We will not say any more about it,\"", "“그 이야기는 더 하지 말자꾸나.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"The thing is over, and I am very glad that it should be so pleasantly settled.", "“그 일은 끝났고, 이렇게 좋게 매듭지어져서 나는 아주 기쁘다."], ["I was talking to Dr. Crofts yesterday.\"", "어제 크로프츠 선생과 이야기를 나눴다.”"]], [["\"Were you, uncle?\"", "“그러셨어요, 삼촌?”"]], [["\"Yes; and he is to come and stay with me the day before he is married.", "“그래. 혼례 전날에는 그 사람이 와서 나와 함께 묵기로 했다."], ["We have arranged it all.", "다 정해 두었지."], ["And we'll have the breakfast up at the Great House.", "그리고 피로연은 큰 집에서 차리기로 하자."], ["Only you must fix the day.", "다만 날짜는 네가 정해야 한다."], ["I should say some time in May.", "나로서는 오월 어느 날쯤이 좋겠구나."], ["And, my dear, you'll want to make yourself fine; here's a little money for you.", "그리고 얘야, 너도 곱게 차려입고 싶을 테지. 여기 얼마간 돈이 있다."], ["You are to spend that before your marriage, you know.\"", "알겠지만 그건 혼례 전에 쓰라고 주는 거다.”"], ["Then he shambled away, and as soon as he was alone, again became sad and despondent.", "그러고서 그는 어기적어기적 걸어 나갔고, 혼자가 되자마자 다시 서글프고 낙담한 사람이 되었다."], ["He was a man for whom we may predicate some gentle sadness and continued despondency to the end of his life's chapter.", "그는 우리가 그 생의 마지막 장에 이르기까지 얼마간의 잔잔한 서글픔과 끊이지 않는 낙담을 미리 점쳐 두어도 좋을 그런 사람이었다."]], [["We left John Eames in the custody of Lady Julia, who had overtaken him in the act of erasing Lily's name from the railing which ran across the brook.", "우리는 존 임스를, 개울을 가로지른 난간에서 릴리의 이름을 지우고 있던 참에 그를 붙든 레이디 줄리아의 손에 맡겨 둔 채 떠나왔었다."], ["He had been premeditating an escape home to his mother's house in Guestwick, and thence back to London, without making any further appearance at the Manor House.", "그는 게스트윅에 있는 어머니 집으로 달아났다가 거기서 다시 런던으로 가 버릴 궁리를 하던 중이었다. 저택에는 두 번 다시 얼굴을 내밀지 않을 작정이었던 것이다."], ["But as soon as he heard Lady Julia's step, and saw her figure close upon him, he knew that his retreat was cut off from him.", "그러나 레이디 줄리아의 발소리를 듣고 그 모습이 코앞에 다가온 것을 보는 순간, 그는 퇴로가 끊겼음을 알았다."], ["So he allowed himself to be led away quietly up to the house.", "그래서 그는 얌전히 이끌려 집으로 올라갔다."], ["With Lady Julia herself he openly discussed the whole matter,--telling her that his hopes were over, his happiness gone, and his heart half-broken.", "레이디 줄리아에게만은 그도 이 일을 통째로 털어놓았다. 자기 희망은 끝났고, 행복은 사라졌으며, 가슴은 반쯤 부서졌노라고."], ["Though he would perhaps have cared but little for her congratulations in success, he could make himself more amenable to consolation and sympathy from her than from any other inmate in the earl's house.", "성공했을 경우라면 그녀의 축하 따위에는 별 마음을 두지 않았을지 모르나, 위로와 동정이라면 그는 백작의 집에 머무는 어느 누구에게서보다 그녀에게서 받는 편이 더 견딜 만했다."], ["\"I don't know what I shall say to your brother,\"", "“오라버님께 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 모르겠습니다.”"], ["he whispered to her, as they approached the side door at which she intended to enter.", "그녀가 들어가려던 옆문에 다가가면서 그가 그녀에게 속삭였다."]], [["\"Will you let me break it to him?", "“제가 오라버니께 말씀드려 둘까요?"], ["After that he will say a few words to you of course, but you need not be afraid of him.\"", "그러고 나면 물론 오라버니가 몇 마디 하시겠지만, 두려워하실 것 없어요.”"]], [["\"And Mr. Dale?\"", "“그러면 데일 어르신은요?”"], ["said Johnny.", "조니가 말했다."], ["\"Everybody has heard about it.", "“다들 그 이야기를 들었을 텐데요."], ["Everybody will know what a fool I have made myself.\"", "다들 제가 얼마나 바보짓을 했는지 알게 되겠지요.”"], ["She suggested that the earl should speak to the squire, assured him that nobody would think him at all foolish, and then left him to make his way up to his own bedroom.", "그녀는 백작이 지주에게 말하게 하면 되겠다고 이르고, 아무도 그를 조금이라도 어리석다 여기지 않을 거라고 다짐해 준 뒤, 그를 놓아주어 제 침실로 올라가게 했다."], ["When there he found a letter from Cradell, which had been delivered in his absence; but the contents of that letter may best be deferred to the next chapter.", "거기서 그는 크래들에게서 온 편지를 발견했는데, 그가 없는 사이에 배달된 것이었다. 그러나 그 편지의 내용은 다음 장으로 미루는 편이 낫겠다."], ["They were not of a nature to give him comfort or to add to his sorrow.", "그것은 그에게 위안을 줄 만한 것도, 그의 슬픔을 더할 만한 것도 아니었다."]], [["About an hour before dinner there was a knock at his door, and the earl himself, when summoned, made his appearance in the room.", "저녁 식사 한 시간쯤 전에 그의 방문을 두드리는 소리가 났고, 들어오라는 말에 백작이 몸소 방 안에 모습을 나타냈다."], ["He was dressed in his usual farming attire, having been caught by Lady Julia on his first approach to the house, and had come away direct to his young friend, after having been duly trained in what he ought to say by his kind-hearted sister.", "그는 평소의 농장 일 차림 그대로였는데, 집으로 다가서던 첫걸음에 레이디 줄리아에게 붙들려, 마음씨 고운 누이에게서 무슨 말을 해야 하는지 단단히 훈련을 받은 뒤 곧장 젊은 벗에게로 온 것이었다."], ["I am not, however, prepared to declare that he strictly followed his sister's teaching in all that he said upon the occasion.", "그러나 나로서는 그가 그 자리에서 한 말이 죄다 누이의 가르침을 고스란히 따른 것이었다고 잘라 말할 준비는 되어 있지 않다."]], [["\"Well, my boy,\"", "“그래, 여보게.”"], ["he began, \"so the young lady has been perverse.\"", "그가 입을 열었다. “그 아가씨가 고집을 부렸다지.”"]], [["\"Yes, my lord.", "“예, 백작님."], ["That is, I don't know about being perverse.", "그러니까, 고집을 부린 건지는 저도 모르겠습니다."], ["It is all over.\"", "다 끝난 일입니다.”"]], [["\"That's as may be, Johnny.", "“그거야 두고 볼 일이지, 조니."], ["As far as I know, not half of them accept their lovers the first time of asking.\"", "내가 아는 한, 처음 청혼을 받고 그 자리에서 받아들이는 여자는 절반도 되지 않네.”"]], [["\"I shall not ask her again.\"", "“저는 다시 청하지 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Oh, yes, you will.", "“아니지, 자네는 다시 청할 걸세."], ["You don't mean to say you are angry with her for refusing you.\"", "거절당했다고 그 아가씨에게 화가 났다는 말은 아니겠지.”"]], [["\"Not in the least.", "“조금도 아닙니다."], ["I have no right to be angry.", "화를 낼 권리도 없고요."], ["I am only angry with myself for being such a fool, Lord De Guest.", "저는 그저 이런 바보였던 제 자신에게 화가 날 뿐입니다, 드 게스트 경."], ["I wish I had been dead before I came down here on this errand.", "이런 볼일로 여기 내려오기 전에 차라리 죽어 버렸더라면 좋았을 걸 그랬습니다."], ["Now I think of it, I know there are so many things which ought to have made me sure how it would be.\"", "이제 와 생각해 보니, 일이 어떻게 될지 진작 알아차렸어야 마땅한 일들이 그렇게나 많았는데 말입니다.”"]], [["\"I don't see that at all.", "“나는 조금도 그렇게 보지 않네."], ["You come down again,--let me see,--it's May now.", "자네 다시 내려오게. 어디 보자, 지금이 오월이지."], ["Say you come when the shooting begins in September.", "구월에 사냥철이 시작되면 오는 걸로 하세."], ["If we can't get you leave of absence in any other way, we'll make old Buffle come too.", "다른 수로 휴가를 얻어 내지 못하겠거든 늙은 버플까지 함께 오라고 하지 뭐."], ["Only, by George, I believe he'd shoot us all.", "다만, 이런, 그자는 우리를 모조리 쏘아 버릴 것 같기는 하네만."], ["But never mind; we'll manage that.", "그래도 상관없어. 그건 우리가 어떻게든 해 보겠네."], ["You keep up your spirits till September, and then we'll fight the battle in another way.", "자네는 구월까지 기운을 잃지 말고 있게. 그러면 그때 우리가 다른 방식으로 싸움을 붙여 보는 거야."], ["The squire shall get up a little party for the bride, and my lady Lily must go then.", "지주가 신부를 위해 조촐한 모임을 열 테고, 우리 릴리 아가씨도 그때는 가야 할 게 아닌가."], ["You shall meet her so; and then we'll shoot over the squire's land.", "자네는 거기서 그 아가씨를 만나는 거지. 그러고 나서 우리는 지주네 땅에서 사냥을 하는 거고."], ["We'll bring you together so; you see if we don't.", "그렇게 해서 자네들 둘을 붙여 놓겠네. 우리가 못 하나 두고 보게."], ["Lord bless me!", "원, 세상에!"], ["Refused once!", "한 번 거절당했다고!"], ["My belief is, that in these days a girl thinks nothing of a man till she has refused him half-a-dozen times.\"", "내 믿기로는, 요즘 여자애들은 한 사내를 여섯 번은 거절해 보고서야 비로소 그 사내를 마음에 두는 법일세.”"]], [["\"I don't think Lily is at all like that.\"", "“릴리는 조금도 그런 사람이 아닌 것 같습니다.”"]], [["\"Look here, Johnny.", "“여보게, 조니."], ["I have not a word to say against Miss Lily.", "나는 릴리 양을 나쁘게 말할 생각은 털끝만큼도 없네."], ["I like her very much, and think her one of the nicest girls I know.", "나는 그 아가씨를 무척 좋아하고, 내가 아는 아가씨들 가운데 가장 점잖은 축이라고 생각하네."], ["When she's your wife, I'll love her dearly, if she'll let me.", "그 아가씨가 자네 아내가 되면, 허락만 한다면 나는 그 애를 끔찍이 아낄 걸세."], ["But she's made of the same stuff as other girls, and will act in the same way.", "하지만 그 애도 다른 아가씨들과 같은 재료로 빚어졌고, 같은 식으로 행동할 걸세."], ["Things have gone a little astray among you, and they won't right themselves all in a minute.", "자네들 사이의 일이 조금 어긋났으니, 그것이 단박에 바로잡히지는 않아."], ["She knows now what your feelings are, and she'll go on thinking of it, till at last you'll be in her thoughts more than that other fellow.", "이제 그 애는 자네 마음이 어떤지 알았고, 그 일을 두고두고 생각하게 될 걸세. 그러다 마침내는 그 다른 사내보다 자네가 그 애의 생각 속에 더 크게 자리 잡게 되지."], ["Don't tell me about her becoming an old maid, because at her time of life she has been so unfortunate as to come across a false-hearted man like that.", "그 애가 저 나이에 그런 거짓된 마음을 지닌 사내와 마주치는 불운을 겪었으니 노처녀가 되고 말 거라는 소리는 내게 하지 말게."], ["It may take a little time; but if you'll carry on and not be down-hearted, you'll find it will all come right in the end.", "시간이 좀 걸릴 수는 있네. 하지만 자네가 밀고 나가면서 기죽지만 않는다면, 끝에 가서는 다 잘될 걸세."], ["Everybody doesn't get all that they want in a minute.", "누구나 원하는 것을 단박에 다 얻지는 못하는 법이야."], ["How I shall quiz you about all this when you have been two or three years married!\"", "자네가 결혼하고 두어 해나 세 해쯤 지나면 내가 이 일로 자네를 얼마나 놀려 먹을지!”"]], [["\"I don't think I shall ever be able to ask her again; and I feel sure, if I do, that her answer will be the same. She told me in so many words--; but never mind, I cannot repeat her words.\"", "“저는 다시는 그 사람에게 청할 수 없을 것 같습니다. 그리고 설령 청한다 해도 대답은 똑같으리라는 확신이 듭니다. 그 사람이 저에게 아주 분명한 말로—. 아니, 그만두겠습니다. 그 사람의 말을 옮길 수는 없습니다.”"]], [["\"I don't want you to repeat them; nor yet to heed them beyond their worth. Lily Dale is a very pretty girl; clever, too, I believe, and good, I'm sure; but her words are not more sacred than those of other men or women. What she has said to you now, she means, no doubt; but the minds of men and women are prone to change, especially when such changes are conducive to their own happiness.\"", "“나는 자네가 그 말을 옮기기를 바라지도 않고, 그 말을 제 값어치 이상으로 새기기를 바라지도 않네. 릴리 데일은 아주 어여쁜 아가씨야. 영리하기도 하겠지, 내 생각에는. 착한 것도 틀림없고. 하지만 그 애의 말이 다른 남자나 여자의 말보다 더 거룩한 것은 아닐세. 지금 자네에게 한 말은 그 애의 진심이겠지, 그야 의심할 바 없어. 하지만 남자든 여자든 마음이란 바뀌기 쉬운 법이고, 더구나 그 바뀜이 제 행복에 도움이 될 때는 더욱 그렇다네.”"]], [["\"At any rate I'll never forget your kindness, Lord De Guest.\"", "“어쨌든 저는 백작님의 온정을 결코 잊지 않겠습니다, 드 게스트 경.”"]], [["\"And there is one other thing I want to say to you, Johnny.", "“그리고 자네에게 하고 싶은 말이 하나 더 있네, 조니."], ["A man should never allow himself to be cast down by anything,--not outwardly, to the eyes of other men.\"", "사내라면 무슨 일로든 기가 꺾여서는 안 되네. 적어도 겉으로, 다른 사람들 눈에 드러나게는 말이야.”"]], [["\"But how is he to help it?\"", "“하지만 그걸 어떻게 막습니까?”"]], [["\"His pluck should prevent him.", "“배짱이 있으면 막아지는 법이지."], ["You were not afraid of a roaring bull, nor yet of that man when you thrashed him at the railway station.", "자네는 울부짖는 황소도 무서워하지 않았고, 철도역에서 그 사내를 두들겨 팰 때도 무서워하지 않았네."], ["You've pluck enough of that kind.", "그런 종류의 배짱은 자네에게 넉넉히 있어."], ["You must now show that you've that other kind of pluck.", "이제는 다른 종류의 배짱도 있다는 걸 보여 주어야 하네."], ["You know the story of the boy who would not cry though the wolf was gnawing him underneath his frock.", "자네도 그 소년 이야기를 알겠지. 이리가 겉옷 밑에서 제 살을 물어뜯는데도 울지 않았다는 아이 말일세."], ["Most of us have some wolf to gnaw us somewhere; but we are generally gnawed beneath our clothes, so that the world doesn't see; and it behoves us so to bear it that the world shall not suspect.", "우리 대부분은 어딘가에 저를 물어뜯는 이리를 하나씩 두고 있네. 다만 대개는 옷 밑에서 물어뜯기니 세상이 보지 못할 뿐이야."], ["The man who goes about declaring himself to be miserable will be not only miserable, but contemptible as well.\"", "그러니 세상이 눈치채지 못하도록 견디는 것이 우리의 도리일세. 제 입으로 나는 비참하다고 떠벌리고 다니는 사내는 비참하기만 한 것이 아니라 경멸스럽기까지 하게 된다네.”"]], [["\"But the wolf hasn't gnawed me beneath my clothes; everybody knows it.\"", "“하지만 이리는 제 옷 밑에서 저를 물어뜯은 게 아닙니다. 다들 알고 있는걸요.”"]], [["\"Then let those who do know it learn that you are able to bear such wounds without outward complaint.", "“그렇다면 아는 사람들에게, 자네가 그런 상처를 겉으로 우는소리 한마디 없이 견뎌 낼 수 있다는 것을 알게 하게."], ["I tell you fairly that I cannot sympathize with a lackadaisical lover.\"", "나는 솔직히 말하네만, 청승맞은 연인에게는 동정이 가지 않아.”"]], [["\"I know that I have made myself ridiculous to everybody.", "“저는 제가 모두에게 우스운 꼴이 되었다는 걸 압니다. 여기 오지나 말 것을."], ["I wish I had never come here. I wish you had never seen me.\"", "백작님께서 저를 만나지나 않으셨더라면 좋았을 것을.”"]], [["\"Don't say that, my dear boy; but take my advice for what it is worth.", "“그런 말은 말게, 이 사람아. 다만 내 충고를 그 값어치만큼은 받아 두게."], ["And remember what it is that I say; with your grief I do sympathize, but not with any outward expression of it;--not with melancholy looks, and a sad voice, and an unhappy gait.", "그리고 내가 하는 말을 새겨 두게. 자네의 슬픔에는 나도 마음을 같이하네만, 그것을 겉으로 드러내는 데는 그렇지 않아."], ["A man should always be able to drink his wine and seem to enjoy it.", "우울한 낯빛이며 가라앉은 목소리며 풀 죽은 걸음걸이 따위에는 말일세. 사내라면 언제나 제 술잔을 비우고 그것을 즐기는 것처럼 보일 줄 알아야 하네."], ["If he can't, he is so much less of a man than he would be otherwise,--not so much more, as some people seem to think.", "그러지 못한다면 그는 그럴 수 있었을 때보다 그만큼 덜 사내인 거야. 어떤 사람들이 생각하는 것처럼 그만큼 더 사내인 것이 아니라 말일세."], ["Now get yourself dressed, my dear fellow, and come down to dinner as though nothing had happened to you.\"", "자, 이제 옷을 갈아입게, 여보게. 그리고 자네에게 아무 일도 없었던 것처럼 저녁 식사 자리로 내려오게.”"]], [["As soon as the earl was gone John looked at his watch and saw that it still wanted some forty minutes to dinner.", "백작이 나가자마자 존은 시계를 보았고, 저녁 식사까지 아직 사십 분쯤 남은 것을 알았다."], ["Fifteen minutes would suffice for him to dress, and therefore there was time sufficient for him to seat himself in his arm-chair and think over it all.", "옷을 갈아입는 데는 십오 분이면 넉넉할 터이니, 안락의자에 앉아 이 모든 일을 곱씹어 볼 시간은 충분했다."], ["He had for a moment been very angry when his friend had told him that he could not sympathize with a lackadaisical lover.", "벗이 자기에게 청승맞은 연인에게는 동정이 가지 않는다고 말했을 때 그는 한순간 몹시 화가 났었다."], ["It was an ill-natured word.", "그것은 심술궂은 말이었다."], ["He felt it to be so when he heard it, and so he continued to think during the whole of the half-hour that he sat in that chair.", "그는 그 말을 듣는 순간에도 그렇게 느꼈고, 그 의자에 앉아 있던 삼십 분 내내 계속 그렇게 여겼다."], ["But it probably did him more good than any word that the earl had ever spoken to him,--or any other word that he could have used.", "그러나 그 말은 아마도 백작이 그에게 해 준 어떤 말보다도, 아니 그가 쓸 수 있었을 어떤 다른 말보다도 그에게 더 이로웠을 것이다."], ["\"Lackadaisical!", "“청승맞다니!"], ["I'm not lackadaisical,\"", "나는 청승맞지 않아.”"], ["he said to himself, jumping up from his chair, and instantly sitting down again.", "그는 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다가 곧바로 다시 앉으며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["\"I didn't say anything to him. I didn't tell him.", "“나는 그분께 아무 말도 하지 않았어."], ["Why did he come to me?\"", "내가 말씀드린 것도 아닌데."], ["And yet, though he endeavoured to abuse Lord De Guest in his thoughts, he knew that Lord De Guest was right, and that he was wrong.", "어째서 나를 찾아오셨담?” 그러면서도, 그는 속으로 드 게스트 경을 욕하려 애쓰면서도, 드 게스트 경이 옳고 자기가 그르다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He knew that he had been lackadaisical, and was ashamed of himself; and at once resolved that he would henceforth demean himself as though no calamity had happened to him.", "그는 자기가 청승맞았다는 것을 알았고 스스로가 부끄러웠다. 그리고 그 자리에서, 이제부터는 자기에게 아무런 재앙도 일어나지 않은 것처럼 처신하리라 결심했다."], ["\"I've a good mind to take him at his word, and drink wine till I'm drunk.\"", "“그분 말씀대로 해서 취하도록 술이나 마셔 볼까 보다.”"], ["Then he strove to get up his courage by a song.", "그러고서 그는 노래를 불러 기운을 내 보려 했다."]], [["If she be not fair for me, What care I how--", "그 여인이 나를 위해 어여쁘지 않다면, 내가 무슨 상관이랴, 그 여인이 얼마나—"]], [["\"But I do care.", "“하지만 나는 상관이 있는걸."], ["What stuff it is a man writing poetry and putting into it such lies as that!", "사내가 시라는 걸 쓰면서 저런 거짓말을 집어넣다니, 무슨 헛소리람!"], ["Everybody knows that he did care,--that is, if he wasn't a heartless beast.\"", "그자도 상관이 있었다는 건 누구나 아는 일이지. 그자가 무정한 짐승이 아니었다면 말이야.”"]], [["But nevertheless, when the time came for him to go down into the drawing-room he did make the effort which his friend had counselled, and walked into the room with less of that hang-dog look than the earl and Lady Julia had expected.", "그러나 그럼에도 응접실로 내려갈 시간이 되자 그는 벗이 일러 준 대로 애를 썼고, 백작과 레이디 줄리아가 예상했던 것보다는 풀 죽은 낯빛을 덜 하고 방으로 들어섰다."], ["They were both there, as was also the squire, and Bell followed him in less than a minute.", "두 사람 다 거기 있었고, 지주도 함께 있었으며, 벨이 채 일 분도 안 되어 뒤따라 들어왔다."]], [["\"You haven't seen Crofts to-day, John, have you?\"", "“오늘 크로프츠를 보지 못했나, 존?”"], ["said the earl.", "백작이 말했다."]], [["\"No; I haven't been anywhere his way!\"", "“못 뵈었습니다. 저는 그분 길목 쪽으로는 어디도 가지 않았으니까요!”"]], [["\"His way!", "“그분 길목이라니!"], ["His ways are every way, I take it.", "내가 보기에 그 사람 길목은 사방 천지가 다 길목일세."], ["I wanted him to come and dine, but he seemed to think it improper to eat two dinners in the same house two days running.", "나는 그 사람더러 와서 저녁을 함께 들자고 했는데, 같은 집에서 이틀을 잇달아 저녁을 두 번 먹는 건 온당치 않다고 여기는 모양이더군."], ["Isn't that his theory, Miss Dale?\"", "그게 그 사람 지론 아니오, 데일 양?”"]], [["\"I'm sure I don't know, Lord De Guest.", "“저는 정말 모르겠어요, 드 게스트 경."], ["At any rate, it isn't mine.\"", "어쨌든 제 지론은 아니고요.”"]], [["So they went to their feast, and before his last chance was over John Eames found himself able to go through the pretence of enjoying his roast mutton.", "그리하여 그들은 만찬 자리로 갔고, 마지막 남은 기회가 다 지나가기 전에 존 임스는 구운 양고기를 즐기는 시늉쯤은 해낼 수 있었다."]], [["There can, I think, be no doubt that in all such calamities as that which he was now suffering, the agony of the misfortune is much increased by the conviction that the facts of the case are known to those round about the sufferer.", "지금 그가 겪고 있는 것과 같은 온갖 재앙에서는 사정이 어떠한지를 주위 사람들이 다 알고 있다는 확신 때문에 그 불운의 고통이 훨씬 커진다는 데에, 나는 의심의 여지가 없다고 본다."], ["A most warm-hearted and intensely-feeling young gentleman might, no doubt, eat an excellent dinner after being refused by the girl of his devotions, provided that he had reason to believe that none of those in whose company he ate it knew anything of his rejection.", "마음이 몹시 따뜻하고 감정이 몹시 깊은 젊은 신사라도 제가 흠모하던 아가씨에게 거절당한 뒤에 훌륭한 만찬을 먹어 치울 수야 물론 있을 것이다. 다만 함께 먹는 이들 가운데 자기가 퇴짜 맞은 것을 아는 사람이 하나도 없다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 있어야 한다."], ["But the same warm-hearted and intensely-feeling young gentleman would find it very difficult to go through the ceremony with any appearance of true appetite or gastronomic enjoyment, if he were aware that all his convives knew all the facts of his little misfortune.", "그러나 바로 그 따뜻하고 감정 깊은 젊은 신사도, 좌중이 모두 자기의 작은 불운을 낱낱이 알고 있음을 안다면, 참으로 식욕이 있는 듯한, 혹은 미식을 즐기는 듯한 낯빛으로 그 의식을 치러 내기가 몹시 어려울 것이다."], ["Generally, we may suppose, a man in such condition goes to his club for his dinner, or seeks consolation in the shades of some adjacent Richmond or Hampton Court.", "대개 그런 처지의 사내는, 우리가 짐작하기로, 저녁을 먹으러 제 클럽으로 가거나 가까운 리치먼드나 햄프턴 코트의 그늘에서 위안을 찾는 법이다."], ["There he meditates on his condition in silence, and does ultimately enjoy his little plate of whitebait, his cutlet and his moderate pint of sherry.", "거기서 그는 말없이 제 처지를 곱씹고, 끝내는 자그마한 뱅어 요리 한 접시와 커틀릿 한 조각과 적당한 양의 셰리 한 파인트를 즐기게 된다."], ["He probably goes alone to the theatre, and, in his stall, speculates with a somewhat bitter sarcasm on the vanity of the world.", "그는 아마 혼자 극장에 가서, 제 좌석에 앉아 세상의 헛됨을 다소 씁쓸한 비꼼으로 곱씹을 것이다."], ["Then he returns home, sad indeed, but with a moderated sadness, and as he puffs out the smoke of his cigar at the open window,--with perhaps the comfort of a little brandy-and-water at his elbow,--swears to himself that, \"By Jove, he'll have another try for it.\"", "그러고서 그는 슬프기는 하되 누그러진 슬픔을 안고 집으로 돌아오며, 열린 창가에서 여송연 연기를 뿜어내며, 팔꿈치 곁에는 아마 브랜디 물 탄 것을 한 잔 놓아 위안 삼으며, 스스로에게 맹세하기를 “아무렴, 한 번 더 부딪쳐 보겠어” 하는 것이다."], ["Alone, a man may console himself, or among a crowd of unconscious mortals; but it must be admitted that the position of John Eames was severe.", "혼자 있을 때, 또는 아무것도 모르는 사람들 틈에 있을 때라면 사내는 스스로를 위로할 수 있다. 그러나 존 임스의 처지가 가혹했다는 것만은 인정해야 한다."], ["He had been invited down there to woo Lily Dale, and the squire and Bell had been asked to be present at the wooing.", "그는 릴리 데일에게 구애하라고 그곳에 초대받았고, 지주와 벨은 그 구애 자리에 함께 있어 달라는 청을 받았던 것이다."], ["Had it all gone well, nothing could have been nicer.", "만사가 잘 풀렸더라면 그보다 더 좋을 수는 없었으리라."], ["He would have been the hero of the hour, and everybody would have sung for him his song of triumph.", "그는 그날의 주인공이 되었을 테고, 모두가 그를 위해 승리의 노래를 불러 주었으리라."], ["But everything had not gone well, and he found it very difficult to carry himself otherwise than lackadaisically.", "그러나 만사가 잘 풀리지 않았고, 그는 청승맞지 않게 처신하기가 몹시 어려웠다."], ["On the whole, however, his effort was such that the earl gave him credit for his demeanour, and told him when parting with him for the night that he was a fine fellow, and that everything should go right with him yet.", "그러나 대체로 그의 애씀이 어지간했던지라 백작은 그의 몸가짐을 대견하게 여겼고, 밤에 헤어질 때 그에게 자네는 훌륭한 사내이며 언젠가는 모든 일이 다 잘될 거라고 말해 주었다."]], [["\"And you mustn't be angry with me for speaking harshly to you,\"", "“그리고 내가 자네에게 모진 말을 했다고 화내지는 말게.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"I wasn't a bit angry.\"", "“조금도 화나지 않았습니다.”"]], [["\"Yes, you were; and I rather meant that you should be.", "“아니, 자네는 화가 났어. 그리고 나는 오히려 자네가 화를 내기를 바랐네."], ["But you mustn't go away in dudgeon.\"", "하지만 앙심을 품은 채 떠나서는 안 되네.”"]], [["He stayed at the Manor House one day longer, and then he returned to his room at the Income-tax Office, to the disagreeable sound of Sir Raffle's little bell, and the much more disagreeable sound of Sir Raffle's big voice.", "그는 하루를 더 저택에 머물렀고, 그런 뒤 소득세 사무국의 제 방으로, 래플 경의 작은 종이 내는 귀에 거슬리는 소리와 그보다 훨씬 더 귀에 거슬리는 래플 경의 큰 목소리로 돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER LIX. JOHN EAMES BECOMES A MAN.", "# 제59장. 존 임스가 어른이 되다"]], [["Eames, when he was half way up to London in the railway carriage, took out from his pocket a letter and read it.", "임스는 런던으로 올라가는 기차간에서 절반쯤 왔을 무렵, 주머니에서 편지 한 통을 꺼내 읽었다."], ["During the former portion of his journey he had been thinking of other things; but gradually he had resolved that it would be better for him not to think more of those other things for the present, and therefore he had recourse to his letter by way of dissipating his thoughts.", "여정의 앞부분에서는 다른 일들을 생각하고 있었으나, 차츰 그런 다른 일들은 당분간 더 생각하지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 쪽으로 마음을 굳혔고, 그리하여 상념을 흩어 버릴 방편으로 그 편지에 기댄 것이었다."], ["It was from Cradell, and ran as follows:--", "편지는 크래들에게서 온 것으로, 내용은 이러했다—"]], [["Income-Tax Office, May --, 186--.", "소득세 사무국, 186-년 5월 -일."]], [["MY DEAR JOHN,--I hope the tidings which I have to give you will not make you angry, and that you will not think I am untrue to the great friendship which I have for you because of that which I am now going to tell you.", "친애하는 존에게,—내가 자네에게 전해야 할 소식이 자네를 노엽게 하지 않기를 바라며, 이제부터 하려는 이야기 때문에 내가 자네에게 품은 저 크나큰 우정을 저버렸다고 자네가 여기지 않기를 바라네."], ["There is no man--[and the word man was underscored]--there is no man whose regard I value so highly as I do yours; and though I feel that you can have no just ground to be displeased with me after all that I have heard you say on many occasions, nevertheless, in matters of the heart it is very hard for one person to understand the sentiments of another, and when the affections of a lady are concerned, I know that quarrels will sometimes arise.", "내가 자네만큼 그 마음을 높이 치는 사내는—[그리고 사내라는 낱말에는 밑줄이 그어져 있었다]—내가 자네만큼 높이 치는 사내는 이 세상에 없네. 그리고 자네가 여러 자리에서 한 말을 죄 들어 온 터라 자네에게 나를 언짢아할 정당한 까닭은 없으리라 여기네만, 그럼에도 마음의 일이란 한 사람이 다른 사람의 심정을 헤아리기가 여간 어려운 것이 아니고, 숙녀의 애정이 걸린 일에서는 이따금 다툼이 일어나기도 한다는 것을 나도 아네."]], [["Eames, when he had got so far as this, on the first perusal of the letter, knew well what was to follow.", "임스는 편지를 처음 훑어 내려가다 여기까지 이르렀을 때, 다음에 무엇이 올지 훤히 알았다. “가엾은 코들!”"], ["\"Poor Caudle!\"", "그는 속으로 말했다."], ["he said to himself; \"he's hooked, and he'll never get himself off the hook again.\"", "“걸려들었군. 이제 다시는 그 바늘에서 제 몸을 빼내지 못할 거야.”"]], [["But let that be as it may, the matter has now gone too far for any alteration to be made by me; nor would any mere earthly inducement suffice to change me.", "허나 그것이야 어찌 되었든, 일은 이미 내가 어떻게 고쳐 볼 수 없을 만큼 멀리 가 버렸네. 또한 이 땅의 어떤 유혹으로도 나를 돌려세우지는 못할 걸세."], ["The claims of friendship are very strong, but those of love are paramount.", "우정의 요구는 참으로 강한 것이나, 사랑의 요구는 그 위에 있네."], ["Of course I know all that has passed between you and Amelia Roper.", "자네와 어밀리아 로퍼 사이에 오간 일은 물론 나도 다 알고 있네."], ["Much of this I had heard from you before, but the rest she has now told me with that pure-minded honesty which is the most remarkable feature in her character.", "그 가운데 많은 것은 전에 자네에게서 들었네만, 나머지는 이제 그녀가 제 성품에서 가장 두드러진 특징인 저 맑은 마음의 정직함으로 나에게 털어놓았네."], ["She has confessed that at one time she felt attached to you, and that she was induced by your perseverance to allow you to regard her as your fiancy.", "그녀는 한때 자네에게 마음이 기울었노라고, 그리고 자네의 끈질김에 이끌려 자네가 그녀를 자네의 정혼녀로 여기도록 허락했노라고 고백했네."], ["[Fancy-girl he probably conceived to be the vulgar English for the elegant term which he used.] But all that must be over between you now.", "[그가 쓴 그 우아한 낱말을 상스러운 영어로 옮기면 ‘노리개 계집’이 되는 줄로 그는 아마 여기고 있었으리라.] 허나 이제 자네와 그녀 사이의 일은 죄 끝나야 하네."], ["Amelia has promised to be mine--[this also was underscored]--and mine I intend that she shall be.", "어밀리아는 내 사람이 되기로 약속했고—[이 대목에도 밑줄이 그어져 있었다]—나는 그녀를 내 사람으로 만들 작정이네."], ["That you may find in the kind smiles of L. D.", "이 일로 자네가 겪을지 모를 실망에 대하여 L."], ["consolation for any disappointment which this may occasion you, is the ardent wish of your true friend,", "D.의 다정한 미소 속에서 위로를 얻기를, 자네의 참된 벗이 간절히 바라네,"]], [["P.S.--Perhaps I had better tell you the whole. Mrs.", "추신.—아무래도 자네에게 전부 다 말해 두는 편이 낫겠네."], ["Roper has been in some trouble about her house.", "로퍼 부인이 집안일로 얼마간 곤경에 처해 있네."], ["She is a little in arrears with her rent, and some bills have not been paid.", "집세가 조금 밀렸고, 갚지 못한 계산서도 몇 장 있다네."], ["As she explained that she has been brought into this by those dreadful Lupexes I have consented to take the house into my own hands, and have given bills to one or two tradesmen for small amounts.", "저 끔찍한 루펙스네 사람들 때문에 이 지경이 되었다고 그분이 설명하기에, 나는 이 집을 내 손으로 떠맡기로 승낙했고 상인 한둘에게 소액의 어음을 끊어 주었네."], ["Of course she will take them up, but it was the credit that was wanting.", "물론 그분이 그것을 갚아 나갈 테지만, 모자랐던 것은 결국 신용이었지."], ["She will carry on the house, but I shall, in fact, be the proprietor.", "집은 그분이 계속 꾸려 가겠지만 사실상 주인은 내가 되는 셈이네."], ["I suppose it will not suit you now to remain here, but don't you think I might make it comfortable enough for some of our fellows; say half-a-dozen, or so?", "이제 자네가 여기 남아 있기는 곤란하리라 짐작하네만, 우리 사무국 사람들 몇쯤은—이를테면 여섯쯤은—내가 꽤 편안하게 지내도록 해 줄 수 있지 않겠나?"], ["That is Mrs. Roper's idea, and I certainly think it is not a bad one.", "그것이 로퍼 부인의 생각인데, 나로서도 나쁘지 않은 생각이라고 여기네."], ["Our first efforts must be to get rid of the Lupexes.", "우리가 맨 먼저 할 일은 루펙스네 사람들을 몰아내는 것일세."], ["Miss Spruce goes next week.", "스프루스 양은 다음 주에 나가네."], ["In the meantime we are all taking our meals up in our own rooms, so that there is nothing for the Lupexes to eat.", "그동안 우리는 모두 각자 제 방에서 끼니를 먹고 있어서 루펙스 부부가 먹을 것이 하나도 없다네."], ["But they don't seem to mind that, and still keep the sitting-room and best bedroom.", "그런데도 저들은 개의치 않는 눈치이고, 여전히 거실과 제일 좋은 침실을 차지하고 있네."], ["We mean to lock them out after Tuesday, and send all their boxes to the public-house.", "우리는 화요일이 지나면 저들을 문밖으로 잠가 내치고 짐짝을 죄다 선술집으로 보내 버릴 작정이네."]], [["Poor Cradell!", "가엾은 크래들!"], ["Eames, as he threw himself back upon his seat and contemplated the depth of misfortune into which his friend had fallen, began to be almost in love with his own position.", "임스는 좌석에 몸을 기대고 제 벗이 굴러떨어진 불행의 깊이를 헤아려 보다가, 제 처지가 거의 마음에 들기 시작했다."], ["He himself was, no doubt, a very miserable fellow.", "그 자신이야 물론 몹시 비참한 사내였다."], ["There was only one thing in life worth living for, and that he could not get.", "살아갈 값어치가 있는 일이라고는 이 세상에 단 하나뿐인데 그것을 얻을 수가 없었다."], ["He had been thinking for the last three days of throwing himself before a locomotive steam-engine, and was not quite sure that he would not do it yet; but, nevertheless, his place was a place among the gods as compared to that which poor Cradell had selected for himself.", "그는 지난 사흘 동안 기관차 앞에 몸을 던질 생각을 하고 있었고, 아직도 그러지 않으리라고는 아주 장담할 수 없었다. 그럼에도 가엾은 크래들이 제 손으로 골라잡은 자리에 견주면 그의 자리는 신들 틈에 낀 자리였다."], ["To be not only the husband of Amelia Roper, but to have been driven to take upon himself as his bride's fortune the whole of his future mother-in-law's debts!", "어밀리아 로퍼의 남편이 되는 것만도 모자라, 신부의 재산이랍시고 장차 장모가 될 사람의 빚을 죄다 제 어깨에 짊어지도록 떠밀리다니!"], ["To find himself the owner of a very indifferent lodging-house;--the owner as regarded all responsibility, though not the owner as regarded any possible profit!", "그것도 아주 시원찮은 하숙집의 주인이 되어서 말이다—책임으로 치면 주인이되, 있을 법한 이문으로 치면 결코 주인이 아닌 주인이!"], ["And then, above and almost worse than all the rest, to find himself saddled with the Lupexes in the beginning of his career!", "게다가 그 모든 것 위에, 아니 거의 그 모든 것보다 더 고약하게도, 제 인생의 첫머리에서 루펙스 부부를 짐짝처럼 걸머지게 되다니!"], ["Poor Cradell indeed!", "참으로 가엾은 크래들이었다!"]], [["Eames had not taken his things away from the lodging-house before he left London, and therefore determined to drive to Burton Crescent immediately on his arrival, not with the intention of remaining there, even for a night, but that he might bid them farewell, speak his congratulations to Amelia, and arrange for his final settlement with Mrs. Roper.", "임스는 런던을 떠나기 전에 하숙집에서 제 물건을 가져오지 않았으므로, 도착하는 즉시 버턴 크레슨트로 마차를 몰기로 마음먹었다."], ["It should have been explained in the last chapter that the earl had told him before parting with him that his want of success with Lily would make no difference as regarded money.", "하룻밤이라도 거기 머물 뜻이 있어서가 아니라, 작별을 고하고 어밀리아에게 축하의 말을 건네고 로퍼 부인과 마지막 셈을 매듭짓기 위해서였다. 앞 장에서 밝혀 두었어야 할 것이 하나 있다."], ["John had, of course, expostulated, saying that he did not want anything, and would not, under his existing circumstances, accept anything; but the earl was a man who knew how to have his own way, and in this matter did have it.", "드 게스트 백작이 그와 헤어지기 전에, 릴리에게서 뜻을 이루지 못한 것이 돈 문제에 관해서는 아무런 차이도 만들지 않으리라고 그에게 말해 두었다는 것이다. 물론 존은 아무것도 바라지 않으며 지금 같은 처지에서는 아무것도 받지 않겠노라고 항변했다."], ["Our friend, therefore, was a man of wealth when he returned to London, and could tell Mrs.", "그러나 백작은 제 뜻을 관철할 줄 아는 사람이었고, 이 일에서도 그렇게 했다."], ["Roper that he would send her a cheque for her little balance as soon as he reached his office.", "그러므로 우리의 벗은 런던으로 돌아올 때 재산깨나 있는 사람이었고, 사무국에 닿는 대로 남은 자잘한 셈을 치를 수표를 보내 드리겠노라고 로퍼 부인에게 말할 수 있었다."]], [["He arrived in the middle of the day,--not timing his return at all after the usual manner of Government clerks, who generally manage to reach the metropolis not more than half an hour before the moment at which they are bound to show themselves in their seats.", "그는 한낮에 도착했다—관청 서기들의 여느 방식과는 전혀 다르게 돌아온 것이니, 그들은 대개 제 자리에 얼굴을 보여야 하는 시각보다 반 시간 남짓 앞서 수도에 닿도록 맞추는 법이다."], ["But he had come back two days before he was due, and had run away from the country as though London in May to him were much pleasanter than the woods and fields.", "그러나 그는 기한보다 이틀이나 일찍 돌아왔고, 오월의 런던이 숲이며 들판보다 훨씬 더 마음에 든다는 듯이 시골에서 달아나 온 것이었다."], ["But neither had London nor the woods and fields any influence on his return.", "그렇다고 런던이나 숲과 들판이 그의 귀환에 무슨 힘을 미친 것은 아니었다."], ["He had gone down that he might throw himself at the feet of Lily Dale,--gone down, as he now confessed to himself, with hopes almost triumphant, and he had returned because Lily Dale would not have him at her feet.", "그는 릴리 데일의 발치에 몸을 던지려고 내려갔던 것이고—이제 스스로 인정하듯이 거의 승리를 확신하는 마음으로 내려갔던 것이며—릴리 데일이 그를 제 발치에 두려 하지 않았기에 돌아온 것이었다."], ["\"I loved him,--him, Crosbie,--better than all the world besides.", "“저는 그이를,—크로즈비 그이를,—이 세상 무엇보다도 사랑했어요."], ["It is still the same.", "지금도 그대로예요."], ["I still love him better than all the world.\"", "저는 아직도 그이를 이 세상 무엇보다도 사랑해요.”"], ["Those were the words which had driven him back to London; and having been sent away with such words as those, it was little matter to him whether he reached his office a day or two sooner or later.", "그를 런던으로 몰아낸 것은 이 말이었다. 그런 말을 듣고 쫓겨난 마당에, 사무국에 하루 이틀 일찍 닿든 늦게 닿든 그에게는 대수로울 것이 없었다."], ["The little room in the city, even with the accompaniment of Sir Raffle's bell and Sir Raffle's voice, would be now more congenial to him than Lady Julia's drawing-room.", "래플 경의 종소리와 래플 경의 목소리가 곁들여진다 해도, 시티의 그 자그마한 방이 이제 그에게는 레이디 줄리아의 응접실보다 더 마음 편한 곳이었다."], ["He would therefore present himself to Sir Raffle on that very afternoon, and expel some interloper from his seat.", "그러므로 그는 바로 그날 오후에 래플 경 앞에 나설 것이고, 제 자리를 차지하고 있는 침입자를 몰아낼 참이었다."], ["But he would first call in Burton Crescent and say farewell to the Ropers.", "그러나 그 전에 먼저 버턴 크레슨트에 들러 로퍼네 사람들에게 작별을 고할 것이었다."]], [["The door was opened for him by the faithful Jemima.", "문을 열어 준 것은 충직한 제미마였다."], ["\"Mr. Heames, Mr.", "“임스 나리, 임스 나리!"], ["Heames! ho dear, ho dear!\"", "아이고 저런, 아이고 저런!”"], ["and the poor girl, who had always taken his side in the adventures of the lodging-house, raised her hands on high and lamented the fate which had separated her favourite from its fortunes.", "하숙집에서 벌어진 온갖 소동에서 늘 그의 편을 들어 온 이 가엾은 처녀는 두 손을 높이 쳐들고, 제가 아끼는 분을 이 집의 운수에서 갈라놓은 팔자를 한탄했다."], ["\"I suppose you knows it all, Mister Johnny?\"", "“다 알고 계시겠지요, 조니 나리?”"], ["Mister Johnny said that he believed he did know it all, and asked for the mistress of the house.", "조니 나리는 아마 다 알고 있는 것 같다고 대답하고서 이 집 안주인을 찾았다."], ["\"Yes, sure enough, she's at home.", "“예, 그럼요, 댁에 계십니다요."], ["She don't dare stir out much, 'cause of them Lupexes.", "그 루펙스네 사람들 때문에 좀처럼 밖에 나가지도 못하십니다요."], ["Ain't this a pretty game?", "이거 참 볼만한 판이지요?"], ["No dinner and no nothink!", "만찬도 없고 아무것도 없습니다요!"], ["Them boxes is Miss Spruce's.", "저 상자들은 스프루스 양 것입니다요."], ["She's agoing now, this minute.", "그분은 이제 곧, 이 참에 떠나신답니다요."], ["You'll find 'em all upstairs in the drawen-room.\"", "다들 위층 거실에 계신 걸 보시게 될 겁니다요.”"], ["So upstairs into the drawing-room he went, and there he found the mother and daughter, and with them Miss Spruce, tightly packed up in her bonnet and shawl.", "그리하여 그는 위층 거실로 올라갔고, 거기서 어머니와 딸을, 그리고 그들과 함께 보닛과 숄로 몸을 꽁꽁 여민 스프루스 양을 보았다."], ["\"Don't, mother,\"", "“그만두세요, 어머니.”"], ["Amelia was saying; \"what's the good of going on in that way?", "어밀리아가 말하고 있었다. “그렇게 하시는 게 무슨 소용이에요?"], ["If she chooses to go, let her go.\"", "가시겠다면 가시게 두세요.”"]], [["\"But she's been with me now so many years,\" said Mrs.", "“하지만 그렇게 오랜 세월을 나하고 함께 지내셨는데,”"], ["Roper, sobbing; \"and I've always done everything for her!", "로퍼 부인이 흐느끼며 말했다. “나는 늘 이분을 위해 무엇이든 다 해 드렸고요!"], ["Haven't I, now, Sally Spruce?\"", "안 그래요, 샐리 스프루스?”"], ["It struck Eames immediately that, though he had been an inmate in the house for two years, he had never before heard that maiden lady's Christian name.", "임스는 제가 이 집에 두 해나 들어 살았으면서도 저 노처녀의 세례명을 여태 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없다는 것을 문득 깨달았다."], ["Miss Spruce was the first to see Eames as he entered the room. It is probable that Mrs.", "그가 방에 들어섰을 때 그를 맨 먼저 본 사람은 스프루스 양이었다."], ["Roper's pathos might have produced some answering pathos on her part had she remained unobserved, but the sight of a young man brought her back to her usual state of quiescence.", "아무도 보고 있지 않았더라면 로퍼 부인의 애처로움이 그녀 쪽에서도 얼마쯤 애처로움을 자아냈을지 모르나, 젊은 사내가 눈에 들어오자 그녀는 여느 때의 조용한 상태로 되돌아갔다. “나야 늙은 여자일 뿐이지요,”"], ["\"I'm only an old woman,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; \"and here's Mr. Eames come back again.\"", "“그리고 여기 임스 씨가 돌아오셨습니다.”"]], [["\"How d'ye do, Mrs. Roper?", "“안녕하십니까, 로퍼 부인?"], ["how d'ye do,--Amelia?", "안녕하세요,—어밀리아?"], ["how d'ye do, Miss Spruce?\"", "안녕하십니까, 스프루스 양?”"], ["and he shook hands with them all.", "그러고서 그는 그들 모두와 악수를 나누었다."]], [["\"Oh, laws,\" said Mrs.", "“어머나 세상에,”"], ["Roper, \"you have given me such a start!\"", "로퍼 부인이 말했다. “깜짝 놀랐잖아요!”"]], [["\"Dear me, Mr. Eames; only think of your coming back in that way,\"", "“어머, 임스 씨, 그런 식으로 돌아오시다니 이게 무슨 일이에요.”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."]], [["\"Well, what way should I come back?", "“글쎄요, 그럼 어떤 식으로 돌아와야 하나요?"], ["You didn't hear me knock at the door, that's all.", "제가 문을 두드리는 소리를 못 들으셨을 뿐이잖아요."], ["So Miss Spruce is really going to leave you?\"", "그런데 스프루스 양께서 정말로 이 댁을 떠나시는 겁니까?”"]], [["\"Isn't it dreadful, Mr. Eames?", "“기막힌 노릇 아니에요, 임스 씨?"], ["Nineteen years we've been together;--taking both houses together, Miss Spruce, we have, indeed.\"", "열아홉 해를 함께 지냈는데요.—두 집을 다 합치면 그렇지요, 스프루스 양, 정말 그렇고말고요.”"], ["Miss Spruce, at this point, struggled very hard to convince John Eames that the period in question had in truth extended over only eighteen years, but Mrs. Roper was authoritative, and would not permit it.", "이 대목에서 스프루스 양은 문제의 그 기간이 실은 열여덟 해밖에 되지 않는다는 것을 존 임스에게 납득시키려고 무던히 애를 썼으나, 로퍼 부인은 위세가 당당하여 그것을 허락하지 않았다."], ["\"It's nineteen years if it's a day.", "“하루를 더 치더라도 열아홉 해예요."], ["No one ought to know dates if I don't, and there isn't one in the world understands her ways unless it's me.", "내가 날짜를 모르면 아무도 모르는 거지요. 그리고 이분 성미를 아는 사람은 나 말고는 이 세상에 아무도 없어요."], ["Haven't I been up to your bedroom every night, and with my own hand given you--\"", "내가 밤마다 이분 침실로 올라가서 내 손으로 직접 이분께 드린 게—”"], ["But she stopped herself, and was too good a woman to declare before a young man what had been the nature of her nightly ministrations to her guest.", "그러다가 그녀는 말을 멈추었으니, 밤마다 제 손님에게 베풀어 온 시중이 어떤 성질의 것인지를 젊은 사내 앞에서 밝히기에는 너무나 마음씨 좋은 여자였던 것이다."]], [["\"I don't think you'll be so comfortable anywhere else, Miss Spruce,\"", "“다른 데 가시면 여기만큼 편하시지는 않을 겁니다, 스프루스 양.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."]], [["\"Comfortable!", "“편하다니요!"], ["of course she won't,\"", "당연히 못 하시지요,”"], ["said Amelia.", "어밀리아가 말했다."], ["\"But if I was mother I wouldn't have any more words about it.\"", "“하지만 내가 어머니라면 이 일로 더는 말을 섞지 않겠어요.”"]], [["\"It isn't the money I'm thinking of, but the feeling of it,\"", "“내가 생각하는 건 돈이 아니라 그 정이에요.” 로퍼 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs. Roper.", "“집이 그렇게 허전해질 텐데."], ["\"The house will be so lonely like.", "나도 내가 어찌 될지 모르겠어요."], ["I shan't know myself; that I shan't.", "정말 모르겠다고요. 게다가 이제는 일이 다 이렇게 잘 풀렸고 루펙스네 사람들도 화요일이면 나가야 하는데— 내가 말하지요, 샐리."], ["And now that things are all settled so pleasantly, and that the Lupexes must go on Tuesday-- I'll tell you what, Sally; I'll pay for the cab myself, and I'll start off to Dulwich by the omnibus to-morrow, and settle it all out of my own pocket.", "삯마차 삯은 내가 내겠어요. 그리고 내일 승합마차를 타고 덜리치로 가서 그 일을 죄다 내 주머니를 털어 처리하겠어요."], ["I will indeed.", "정말 그렇게 하겠어요."], ["Come; there's the cab.", "자, 삯마차가 왔군요."], ["Let me go down, and send him away.\"", "내가 내려가서 돌려보내지요.”"]], [["\"I'll do that,\"", "“그건 제가 하지요.”"], ["said Eames.", "임스가 말했다."], ["\"It's only sixpence, off the stand,\"", "“대기소에서 부른 것이니 육 펜스면 돼요.”"], ["Mrs. Roper called to him as he left the room.", "그가 방을 나서는데 로퍼 부인이 뒤에서 소리쳐 일러 주었다."], ["But the cabman got a shilling, and John, as he returned, found Jemima in the act of carrying Miss Spruce's boxes back to her room.", "그러나 마부는 일 실링을 받았고, 존이 돌아와 보니 제미마가 스프루스 양의 상자들을 다시 그 방으로 나르고 있었다."], ["\"So much the better for poor Caudle,\"", "“가엾은 코들에게는 그만큼 잘된 일이지,”"], ["said he to himself.", "그는 속으로 말했다."], ["\"As he has gone into the trade it's well that he should have somebody that will pay him.\"", "“저 장사에 뛰어든 마당이니 돈을 내줄 사람이 하나라도 있는 편이 좋고말고.”"]], [["Mrs. Roper followed Miss Spruce up the stairs and Johnny was left with Amelia.", "로퍼 부인이 스프루스 양을 따라 층계를 올라가자 조니는 어밀리아와 단둘이 남았다."], ["\"He's written to you, I know,\"", "“그이가 편지를 보냈겠지요, 다 알아요.”"], ["said she, with her face turned a little away from him.", "그녀가 얼굴을 그에게서 조금 돌린 채 말했다."], ["She was certainly very handsome, but there was a hard, cross, almost sullen look about her, which robbed her countenance of all its pleasantness.", "그녀는 분명 아주 아름다웠으나, 그 낯빛에는 어딘가 굳고 뒤틀리고 거의 뾰로통하다 할 기색이 있어서 얼굴에서 고운 맛을 죄다 앗아 가고 있었다."], ["And yet she had no intention of being sullen with him.", "그러면서도 그녀는 그에게 뾰로통하게 굴 뜻은 조금도 없었다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“그렇습니다,”"], ["said John.", "존이 말했다."], ["\"He has told me how it's all going to be.\"", "“일이 어떻게 되어 가는지 그가 다 알려 주었어요.”"]], [["\"Well?\"", "“그래서요?”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["\"Well?\"", "“그래서요?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"Is that all you've got to say?\"", "“하실 말씀이 그게 답니까?”"]], [["\"I'll congratulate you, if you'll let me.\"", "“허락하신다면 축하를 드리지요.”"]], [["\"Psha;--congratulations!", "“쳇,—축하라니요!"], ["I hate such humbug.", "나는 그런 허튼수작이 딱 질색이에요."], ["If you've no feelings about it, I'm sure that I've none.", "그쪽에 아무 감정도 없다면 나도 없어요."], ["Indeed I don't know what's the good of feelings.", "아니, 감정이란 게 무슨 소용인지 도무지 모르겠네요."], ["They never did me any good.", "그런 게 나한테 무슨 이로움을 준 적도 없고요. L."], ["Are you engaged to marry L. D.?\"", "D.와 혼인하기로 약조했나요?”"]], [["\"No, I am not.\"", "“아니요, 그렇지 않습니다.”"]], [["\"And you've nothing else to say to me?\"", "“그러면 그 밖에 나한테 하실 말씀은 없고요?”"]], [["\"Nothing,--except my hopes for your happiness.", "“없습니다,—그쪽이 행복하기를 바라는 마음 말고는요."], ["What else can I say?", "그 밖에 무슨 말을 하겠어요?"], ["You are engaged to marry my friend Cradell, and I think it will be a happy match.\"", "그쪽은 내 벗 크래들과 혼인하기로 약조했고, 나는 그것이 행복한 짝이 되리라 생각합니다.”"]], [["She turned away her face further from him, and the look of it became even more sullen.", "그녀는 얼굴을 그에게서 더 멀리 돌렸고, 그 낯빛은 한층 더 뾰로통해졌다."], ["Could it be possible that at such a moment she still had a hope that he might come back to her?", "이런 마당에도 그녀에게는 아직 그가 제게 돌아오리라는 희망이 남아 있었던 것일까?"]], [["\"Good-by, Amelia,\"", "“잘 있어요, 어밀리아.”"], ["he said, putting out his hand to her.", "그가 그녀에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["\"And this is to be the last of you in this house!\"", "“이게 이 집에서 보는 마지막 모습이 되겠군요!”"]], [["\"Well, I don't know about that.", "“글쎄요, 그건 잘 모르겠는데요."], ["I'll come and call upon you, if you'll let me, when you're married.\"", "허락하신다면 혼인하신 뒤에 찾아뵙지요.”"]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“그럼요,”"], ["she said, \"that there may be rows in the house, and noise, and jealousy,--as there have been with that wicked woman upstairs.", "그녀가 말했다. “그래서 이 집에 또 소동이 나고 시끄러워지고 시샘이 일라고요.—저 위층의 못된 여자한테 그랬듯이 말이지요."], ["Not if I know it, you won't!", "내가 알고서야 어림없어요!"], ["John Eames, I wish I'd never seen you.", "존 임스, 나는 당신을 만나지 않았더라면 좋았겠어요."], ["I wish we might have both fallen dead when we first met.", "우리가 처음 만났을 때 둘 다 그 자리에서 쓰러져 죽었더라면 좋았겠어요."], ["I didn't think ever to have cared for a man as I have cared for you.", "내가 당신을 아꼈던 것처럼 어떤 사내를 아끼게 되리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했어요."], ["It's all trash and nonsense and foolery; I know that.", "그런 건 죄다 쓰레기고 헛소리고 부질없는 짓이지요. 나도 알아요."], ["It's all very well for young ladies as can sit in drawing-rooms all their lives, but when a woman has her way to make in the world it's all foolery.", "평생 응접실에나 앉아 있을 수 있는 아가씨들한테야 다 좋은 일이겠지만, 여자가 제 앞길을 제 손으로 헤쳐 나가야 할 때는 그런 게 다 부질없는 짓이에요."], ["And such a hard way too to make as mine is!\"", "게다가 내 앞길처럼 이렇게 험한 길을 말이에요!”"]], [["\"But it won't be hard now.\"", "“하지만 이제는 험하지 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Won't it?", "“그럴까요?"], ["But I think it will.", "나는 험할 것 같은데요."], ["I wish you would try it.", "당신이 한번 겪어 봤으면 좋겠어요."], ["Not that I'm going to complain.", "그렇다고 푸념을 하려는 건 아니에요."], ["I never minded work, and as for company, I can put up with anybody.", "나는 일하는 걸 싫다고 여긴 적이 없고, 사람을 겪는 일이라면 누구하고든 견뎌 낼 수 있어요."], ["The world's not to be all dancing and fiddling for the likes of me.", "나 같은 사람한테 세상이 온통 춤추고 바이올린 켜는 자리일 수는 없지요."], ["I know that well enough.", "그쯤은 나도 잘 알아요."], ["But--\"", "하지만—”"], ["and then she paused.", "그러고서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["\"What's the 'but' about, Amelia?\"", "“그 ‘하지만’이 무엇에 관한 겁니까, 어밀리아?”"]], [["\"It's like you to ask me; isn't it?\"", "“그걸 나한테 묻다니, 당신답네요.”"], ["To tell the truth he should not have asked her.", "사실을 말하자면 그는 그것을 묻지 말았어야 했다."], ["\"Never mind.", "“됐어요."], ["I'm not going to have any words with you.", "나는 당신하고 말을 섞을 생각이 없어요."], ["If you've been a knave I've been a fool, and that's worse.\"", "당신이 악한이었다면 나는 바보였고, 그게 더 나쁘지요.”"]], [["\"But I don't think I have been a knave.\"", "“하지만 나는 내가 악한이었다고는 생각하지 않는데요.”"]], [["\"I've been both,\"", "“나는 둘 다였어요,”"], ["said the girl; \"and both for nothing.", "처녀가 말했다. “게다가 둘 다 아무 보람도 없이 말이에요."], ["After that you may go.", "그 말을 들었으면 이제 가셔도 돼요."], ["I've told you what I am, and I'll leave you to name yourself.", "내가 어떤 사람인지는 말해 드렸으니, 당신 자신의 이름은 당신이 붙이도록 남겨 두지요."], ["I didn't think it was in me to have been such a fool.", "내 속에 그렇게 바보 같은 구석이 있는 줄은 몰랐어요."], ["It's that that frets me.", "나를 애태우는 건 바로 그거예요."], ["Never mind, sir; it's all over now, and I wish you good-by.\"", "됐어요, 선생님. 이제 다 지난 일이니, 안녕히 가시라고 인사드리지요.”"]], [["I do not think that there was the slightest reason why John should have again kissed her at parting, but he did so.", "존이 헤어지면서 그녀에게 다시 입을 맞출 까닭은 조금도 없었다고 나는 생각하나, 그는 그렇게 했다."], ["She bore it, not struggling with him; but she took his caress with sullen endurance.", "그녀는 그를 뿌리치지 않고 그대로 받았으나, 그 다정한 손길을 뾰로통한 낯으로 견뎌 냈을 뿐이었다."], ["\"It'll be the last,\"", "“이게 마지막이에요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"Good-by, John Eames.\"", "“안녕히 가세요, 존 임스.”"]], [["\"Good-by, Amelia.", "“잘 있어요, 어밀리아."], ["Try to make him a good wife and then you'll be happy.\"", "그에게 좋은 아내가 되도록 애써 봐요, 그러면 행복해질 겁니다.”"], ["She turned up her nose at this, assuming a look of unutterable scorn.", "그녀는 이 말에 코를 찡그리며 이루 말할 수 없이 경멸하는 낯빛을 지었다."], ["But she said nothing further, and then he left the room.", "그러나 그 뒤로는 더 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 그는 방을 나섰다."], ["At the parlour door he met Mrs. Roper, and had his parting words with her.", "응접실 문간에서 그는 로퍼 부인과 마주쳤고, 그녀와 작별의 말을 나누었다."]], [["\"I am so glad you came,\"", "“와 주셔서 참 다행이에요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["\"It was just that word you said that made Miss Spruce stay.", "“스프루스 양을 붙든 건 바로 임스 씨가 해 주신 그 한마디였어요."], ["Her money is so ready, you know!", "그분은 셈을 꼬박꼬박 치르는 분이거든요!"], ["And so you've had it all out with her about Cradell.", "그래서 크래들 일로 저 애하고 이야기를 다 나누셨군요."], ["She'll make him a good wife, she will indeed;--much better than you've been giving her credit for.\"", "저 애는 그이한테 좋은 아내가 될 거예요. 정말 그럴 거예요.—임스 씨가 저 애를 여겨 주신 것보다 훨씬 더요.”"]], [["\"I don't doubt she'll be a very good wife.\"", "“저 아가씨가 아주 좋은 아내가 되리라는 건 나도 의심치 않습니다.”"]], [["\"You see, Mr. Eames, it's all over now, and we understand each other; don't we?", "“그러니까요, 임스 씨, 이제 다 지난 일이고 우리는 서로 마음을 알잖아요, 안 그래요?"], ["It made me very unhappy when she was setting her cap at you; it did indeed.", "저 애가 임스 씨한테 마음을 두려고 할 때 나는 참 마음이 편치 않았어요."], ["She is my own daughter, and I couldn't go against her;--could I?", "정말 그랬어요. 내 딸이니 저 애를 거스를 수도 없었고요.—안 그래요?"], ["But I knew it wasn't in any way suiting.", "하지만 나는 그게 어울리는 일이 아니라는 걸 알고 있었어요."], ["Laws, I know the difference.", "세상에, 나도 지체 차이쯤은 알지요."], ["She's good enough for him any day of the week, Mr. Eames.\"", "저 애야 그이한테라면 어느 요일에라도 넘치도록 좋은 짝이에요, 임스 씨.”"]], [["\"That she is,--Saturdays or Sundays,\"", "“정말 그렇지요,—토요일이든 일요일이든 말입니다.”"], ["said Johnny, not knowing exactly what he ought to say.", "조니가 무슨 말을 해야 좋을지 딱히 알지 못한 채 말했다."]], [["\"So she is; and if he does his duty by her she won't go astray in hers by him.", "“그렇고말고요. 그리고 그이가 저 애한테 제 도리를 다한다면 저 애도 그이한테 제 도리에서 어긋나지 않을 거예요."], ["And as for you, Mr. Eames, I am sure I've always felt it an honour and a pleasure to have you in the house; and if ever you could use a good word in sending to me any of your young men, I'd do by them as a mother should; I would indeed.", "그리고 임스 씨로 말하면, 이 집에 임스 씨를 모신 것이 나로서는 늘 영광이고 기쁨이었어요."], ["I know I've been to blame about those Lupexes, but haven't I suffered for it, Mr.", "그러니 언제라도 임스 씨가 아시는 젊은 분들 가운데 누구든 나한테 보내 주실 만한 자리가 있거든 좋은 말씀 한마디 해 주세요. 나는 그분들한테 어머니가 할 도리를 다하겠어요."], ["Eames?", "정말 그러겠어요."], ["And it was difficult to know at first; wasn't it?", "저 루펙스네 사람들 일로 내가 잘못한 걸 알고 있어요. 하지만 내가 그 값을 치르지 않았나요, 임스 씨?"], ["And as to you and Amelia, if you would send any of your young men to try, there couldn't be anything more of that kind, could there?", "그리고 처음에는 알아보기가 어려웠잖아요, 안 그래요? 그리고 임스 씨하고 어밀리아 일로 말하면, 임스 씨가 아시는 젊은 분들을 보내 주신다 해도 이제 그런 일은 더 있을 수 없잖아요, 안 그래요?"], ["I know it hasn't all been just as it should have been;--that is as regards you; but I should like to hear you say that you've found me honest before you went.", "나도 모든 일이 마땅히 그러했어야 할 대로 되지는 않았다는 걸 알아요.—그러니까 임스 씨에 관해서는요. 하지만 떠나시기 전에 나를 정직한 사람으로 여기신다는 말씀만은 듣고 싶어요."], ["I have tried to be honest, I have indeed.\"", "나는 정직하려고 애써 왔어요. 정말 그랬어요.”"]], [["Eames assured her that he was convinced of her honesty, and that he had never thought of impugning her character either in regard to those unfortunate people, the Lupexes, or in reference to other matters.", "임스는 그녀의 정직함을 자신이 믿어 의심치 않으며, 저 딱한 사람들인 루펙스 부부에 관해서든 다른 일에 관해서든 그녀의 인품을 헐뜯을 생각은 해 본 적이 없다고 그녀를 안심시켰다. “젊은 사람들이 하숙 자리를 두고 저에게 물어 올 것 같지는 않습니다만,”"], ["\"He did not think,\"", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"that any young men would consult him as to their lodgings; but if he could be of any service to her, he would.\"", "“제가 무슨 도움이 될 수 있다면 그렇게 하지요.”"], ["Then he bade her good-by, and having bestowed half-a-sovereign on the faithful Jemima, he took a long farewell of Burton Crescent.", "그러고서 그는 그녀에게 작별을 고했고, 충직한 제미마에게 반 소버린을 쥐여 준 다음 버턴 크레슨트에 오래 두고 볼 작별을 고했다."], ["Amelia had told him not to come and see her when she should be married, and he had resolved that he would take her at her word.", "어밀리아는 제가 혼인한 뒤에 찾아오지 말라고 그에게 일렀고, 그는 그 말을 그대로 받아들이기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["So he walked off from the Crescent, not exactly shaking the dust from his feet, but resolving that he would know no more either of its dust or of its dirt.", "그리하여 그는 크레슨트를 떠나 걸어 나왔다. 발에서 먼지를 털어 내지는 않았으나, 그곳의 먼지도 그곳의 때도 다시는 알고 지내지 않으리라 마음을 굳히면서였다."], ["Dirt enough he had encountered there certainly, and he was now old enough to feel that the inmates of Mrs. Roper's house had not been those among whom a resting-place for his early years should judiciously have been sought.", "때는 그가 거기서 실컷 겪었고, 로퍼 부인의 집에 든 사람들이란 그가 제 젊은 시절의 쉼터를 사려 깊게 찾았어야 할 그런 사람들이 아니었다는 것을 이제 그는 알 만한 나이가 되어 있었다."], ["But he had come out of the fire comparatively unharmed, and I regret to say that he felt but little for the terrible scorchings to which his friend had been subjected and was about to subject himself.", "그러나 그는 그 불길에서 별로 다치지 않고 빠져나왔고, 제 벗이 이미 겪었으며 앞으로 스스로 겪게 될 저 무서운 그을림에 대해 그가 느끼는 바가 거의 없었다는 것을 나는 유감스럽게 여긴다."], ["He was quite content to look at the matter exactly as it was looked at by Mrs.", "그는 이 일을 로퍼 부인이 바라보는 그대로 바라보는 데에 아주 만족하고 있었다."], ["Roper. Amelia was good enough for Joseph Cradell--any day of the week.", "어밀리아는 조지프 크래들에게라면—어느 요일에라도 넘치도록 좋은 짝이었다."], ["Poor Cradell, of whom in these pages after this notice no more will be heard!", "가엾은 크래들, 이 책장에서는 이 알림 뒤로 다시는 그의 소식을 듣지 못할 것이다!"], ["I cannot but think that a hard measure of justice was meted out to him, in proportion to the extent of his sins.", "나는 그가 지은 죄의 크기에 견주어 그에게 매겨진 심판이 모질었다고 생각하지 않을 수 없다."], ["More weak and foolish than our friend and hero he had been, but not to my knowledge more wicked.", "그는 우리의 벗이자 주인공보다 더 약하고 더 어리석기는 했으나, 내가 아는 한 더 사악하지는 않았다."], ["But it is to the vain and foolish that the punishments fall;--and to them they fall so thickly and constantly that the thinker is driven to think that vanity and folly are of all sins those which may be the least forgiven.", "그러나 벌이 떨어지는 것은 허영에 차고 어리석은 자들에게이며—그것도 어찌나 촘촘하고 끊임없이 떨어지는지, 생각하는 사람이라면 허영과 어리석음이야말로 모든 죄 가운데 가장 용서받기 어려운 것이 아닐까 하는 데까지 내몰리게 된다. 크래들에 관해서는 이것만 밝혀 두겠다."], ["As for Cradell I may declare that he did marry Amelia, that he did, with some pride, take the place of master of the house at the bottom of Mrs. Roper's table, and that he did make himself responsible for all Mrs.", "그는 정말로 어밀리아와 혼인했고, 얼마간 자랑스러운 마음으로 로퍼 부인네 식탁 끝자리에 이 집 주인의 자리를 차지했으며, 로퍼 부인의 빚을 죄다 제가 떠맡았다."], ["Roper's debts. Of his future fortunes there is not space to speak in these pages.", "그 뒤 그의 운명이 어찌 되었는지는 이 책장에서 이야기할 자리가 없다."]], [["Going away from the Crescent Eames had himself driven to his office, which he reached just as the men were leaving it, at four o'clock.", "크레슨트를 나선 임스는 마차를 몰아 제 사무국으로 갔고, 마침 네 시에 사람들이 그곳을 나서는 참에 그곳에 닿았다. 크래들은 이미 가고 없어서 그날 오후에는 그를 만나지 못했다."], ["Cradell was gone, so that he did not see him on that afternoon; but he had an opportunity of shaking hands with Mr. Love, who treated him with all the smiling courtesy due to an official bigwig,--for a private secretary, if not absolutely a big-wig, is semi-big, and entitled to a certain amount of reverence;--and he passed Mr.", "그러나 러브 씨와는 악수를 나눌 기회를 얻었는데, 러브 씨는 관청의 높으신 분에게 마땅한 온갖 웃음 띤 정중함으로 그를 대했다—개인 비서란 아주 높으신 분은 아닐지라도 반쯤은 높으신 분이며, 얼마간의 우러름을 받을 자격이 있는 것이다.—그리고 그는 복도에서 키싱 씨를 스쳐 지났는데, 그는 여느 때처럼 커다란 장부를 옆구리에 끼고 서둘러 가는 중이었다."], ["Kissing in the passage, hurrying along as usual with a huge book under his arm. Mr.", "키싱 씨는 그렇게 바쁜 와중에도 발을 끌던 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["Kissing, hurried as he was, stopped his shuffling feet; but Eames only looked at him, hardly honouring him with the acknowledgment of a nod of his head.", "그러나 임스는 그를 쳐다보기만 했을 뿐, 고개를 까딱해 아는 체해 주는 영예조차 좀처럼 베풀지 않았다. 그렇다고 키싱 씨가 언짢아한 것은 아니었다."], ["Mr. Kissing, however, was not offended; he knew that the private secretary of the First Commissioner had been the guest of an earl; and what more than a nod could be expected from him?", "그는 수석 위원의 개인 비서가 어느 백작의 손님으로 다녀왔다는 것을 알고 있었으니, 그런 사람에게서 고개 까딱 이상의 무엇을 바랄 수 있겠는가?"], ["After that John made his way into the august presence of Sir Raffle, and found that great man putting on his shoes in the presence of FitzHoward.", "그러고 나서 존은 래플 경의 지엄한 어전으로 들어갔고, 그 위대한 인물이 피츠하워드가 지켜보는 앞에서 제 신발을 신고 있는 것을 보았다. 피츠하워드는 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["FitzHoward blushed; but the shoes had not been touched by him, as he took occasion afterwards to inform John Eames.", "그러나 그 신발에 그가 손을 댄 것은 아니었으니, 그는 나중에 존 임스에게 그 사실을 알릴 기회를 얻었다."]], [["Sir Raffle was all smiles and civility. \"Delighted to see you back, Eames: am, upon my word; though I and FitzHoward have got on capitally in your absence; haven't we, FitzHoward?\"", "래플 경은 온통 웃음과 정중함 그 자체였다. “돌아와서 반갑네, 임스. 정말이지 반가워. 하기야 자네가 없는 동안에도 나와 피츠하워드가 아주 훌륭하게 해내기는 했지. 안 그런가, 피츠하워드?”"]], [["\"Oh, yes,\"", "“아, 예.”"], ["drawled FitzHoward.", "피츠하워드가 느릿하게 말했다."], ["\"I haven't minded it for a time, just while Eames has been away.\"", "“임스가 자리를 비운 동안 잠깐이었으니 저야 싫지 않았습니다.”"]], [["\"You're much too idle to keep at it, I know; but your bread will be buttered for you elsewhere, so it doesn't signify.", "“자네는 너무 게을러서 이 일을 오래 붙들고 있지 못하지, 나도 알아. 하지만 자네 밥벌이야 다른 데서 버터를 발라 줄 테니 대수로울 것 없고 말이야."], ["My compliments to the duchess when you see her.\"", "공작부인을 뵙거든 안부 전해 주게.”"], ["Then FitzHoward went.", "그러고서 피츠하워드는 갔다."], ["\"And how's my dear old friend?\"", "“그래, 내 정다운 옛 벗께서는 어떠신가?”"], ["asked Sir Raffle, as though of all men living Lord De Guest were the one for whom he had the strongest and the oldest love.", "래플 경이 물었는데, 마치 살아 있는 온 세상 사람 가운데 드 게스트 경이야말로 제가 가장 깊고 가장 오래된 사랑을 품은 사람이라는 투였다."], ["And yet he must have known that John Eames knew as much about it as he did himself.", "그러면서도 그는 존 임스가 그 속을 저만큼이나 훤히 알고 있다는 것을 알고 있었을 것이 틀림없다."], ["But there are men who have the most lively gratification in calling lords and marquises their friends, though they know that nobody believes a word of what they say,--even though they know how great is the odium they incur, and how lasting is the ridicule which their vanity produces.", "그러나 세상에는 저희가 하는 말을 아무도 믿어 주지 않는다는 것을 알면서도—아니, 그로 인해 저희가 얼마나 큰 미움을 사는지, 저희 허영이 낳는 웃음거리가 얼마나 오래가는지 알면서도—경이니 후작이니 하는 이들을 제 벗이라 부르는 데에서 더없이 생생한 즐거움을 느끼는 사람들이 있다."], ["It is a gentle insanity which prevails in the outer courts of every aristocracy; and as it brings with itself considerable annoyance and but a lukewarm pleasure, it should not be treated with too keen a severity.", "그것은 어느 귀족 사회에서든 그 바깥뜰에 퍼져 있는 순한 미친 증세이며, 적잖은 성가심을 안겨 주면서도 미지근한 즐거움밖에 주지 못하는 것이니 너무 매섭게 다룰 일은 아니다."]], [["\"And how's my dear old friend?\"", "“그래, 내 정다운 옛 벗께서는 어떠신가?”"], ["Eames assured him that his dear old friend was all right, that Lady Julia was all right, that the dear old place was all right.", "임스는 그의 정다운 옛 벗께서 아주 잘 계시고, 레이디 줄리아도 아주 잘 계시며, 그 정다운 옛 저택도 아주 잘 있다고 그를 안심시켰다."], ["Sir Raffle now spoke as though the \"dear old place\" were quite well known to him.", "이제 래플 경은 그 “정다운 옛 저택”이 제게도 아주 익숙한 곳인 양 말했다."], ["\"Was the game doing pretty well?", "“사냥감은 잘 있던가?"], ["Was there a promise of birds?\"", "새들이 많을 조짐이 보이던가?”"], ["Sir Raffle's anxiety was quite intense, and expressed with almost familiar affection.", "래플 경의 궁금증은 자못 간절했고, 거의 허물없다 할 만한 애정을 담아 표현되었다."], ["\"And, by-the-by, Eames, where are you living at present?\"", "“그런데 말이지, 임스, 자네 지금은 어디서 지내나?”"]], [["\"Well, I'm not settled.", "“글쎄요, 아직 자리를 잡지 못했습니다."], ["I'm at the Great Western Railway Hotel at this moment.\"", "지금은 그레이트웨스턴 철도 호텔에 있습니다.”"]], [["\"Capital house, very; only it's expensive if you stay there the whole season.\"", "“아주 훌륭한 집이지. 아주."], ["Johnny had no idea of remaining there beyond one night, but he said nothing as to this.", "다만 사교철 내내 거기 묵는다면 비용이 만만찮을 걸세.” 조니는 그곳에서 하룻밤 넘게 묵을 생각이 없었으나, 그 점에 관해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["\"By-the-by, you might as well come and dine with us to-morrow.", "“그건 그렇고, 내일 우리 집에 와서 만찬을 함께 하는 게 좋겠네."], ["Lady Buffle is most anxious to know you.", "레이디 버플이 자네를 몹시 알고 싶어 해."], ["There'll be one or two with us.", "우리 말고도 한두 사람쯤 더 올 걸세."], ["I did ask my friend Dumbello, but there's some nonsense going on in the House, and he thinks that he can't get away.\"", "내 벗 덤벨로에게도 청해 보기는 했네만, 의회에서 무슨 시시한 일이 벌어지고 있어서 그가 빠져나올 수 없다고 하더군.”"], ["Johnny was more gracious than Lord Dumbello, and accepted the invitation.", "조니는 덤벨로 경보다 너그러워서 그 초대를 받아들였다."], ["\"I wonder what Lady Buffle will be like?\"", "“레이디 버플은 어떤 분일까?”"], ["he said to himself, as he walked away from the office.", "사무국을 나서 걸어가면서 그는 속으로 말했다."]], [["He had turned into the Great Western Hotel, not as yet knowing where to look for a home; and there we will leave him, eating his solitary mutton-chop at one of those tables which are so comfortable to the eye, but which are so comfortless in reality.", "그는 아직 어디에 거처를 구해야 할지 모르는 채로 그레이트웨스턴 호텔에 들었고, 우리는 그를 거기 두고 떠나기로 하자. 그는 눈에는 그토록 편안해 보이면서 실상은 그토록 편안하지 못한 그런 식탁 가운데 하나에 앉아 홀로 양갈비를 먹고 있다."], ["I speak not now with reference to the excellent establishment which has been named, but to the nature of such tables in general.", "내가 지금 말하는 것은 방금 이름을 댄 그 훌륭한 집을 두고 하는 말이 아니라, 무릇 그런 식탁의 성질 일반을 두고 하는 말이다."], ["A solitary mutton-chop in an hotel coffee-room is not a banquet to be envied by any god; and if the mutton-chop be converted into soup, fish, little dishes, big dishes, and the rest, the matter becomes worse and not better.", "호텔 식당에서 홀로 먹는 양갈비란 어떤 신도 부러워할 잔치가 아니다. 그리고 그 양갈비가 수프와 생선과 작은 접시들과 큰 접시들 따위로 바뀐다면 사정은 나아지기는커녕 더 나빠진다."], ["What comfort are you to have, seated alone on that horsehair chair, staring into the room and watching the waiters as they whisk about their towels?", "저 말총 의자에 홀로 앉아 방 안을 멀거니 바라보며 웨이터들이 수건을 휘두르며 오가는 것을 지켜보고 있자니 무슨 위안이 있겠는가?"], ["No one but an Englishman has ever yet thought of subjecting himself to such a position as that!", "영국 사람 말고는 아직 아무도 제 몸을 그런 처지에 두어 볼 생각을 해 본 적이 없다!"], ["But here we will leave John Eames, and in doing so I must be allowed to declare that only now, at this moment, has he entered on his manhood.", "그러나 우리는 존 임스를 여기 두고 떠나기로 한다. 그리고 그렇게 하면서 이 말만은 하도록 허락받아야겠으니, 이제야, 바로 이 순간에야 그가 제 어른의 삶으로 들어섰다는 것이다."], ["Hitherto he has been a hobbledehoy,--a calf, as it were, who had carried his calfishness later into life than is common with calves; but who did not, perhaps, on that account, give promise of making a worse ox than the rest of them.", "여태껏 그는 풋내기였다—말하자면 송아지 티를 여느 송아지들보다 더 늦게까지 삶 속으로 끌고 온 송아지였다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 그가 다른 송아지들보다 못한 황소가 되리라는 조짐이 있었던 것은 아마 아니리라."], ["His life hitherto, as recorded in these pages, had afforded him no brilliant success, had hardly qualified him for the role of hero which he has been made to play.", "이 책장에 적힌 대로의 그의 삶은 그에게 눈부신 성공이라고는 하나도 안겨 주지 못했고, 그가 맡아 온 주인공 노릇에 어울릴 자격을 갖추어 주지도 못했다."], ["I feel that I have been in fault in giving such prominence to a hobbledehoy, and that I should have told my story better had I brought Mr. Crosbie more conspicuously forward on my canvas.", "나는 풋내기에게 그토록 큰 자리를 내어 준 것이 내 잘못이며, 크로즈비 씨를 내 화폭 앞자리에 더 두드러지게 내세웠더라면 내 이야기를 더 잘 풀어냈으리라고 느낀다."], ["He at any rate has gotten to himself a wife--as a hero always should do; whereas I must leave my poor friend Johnny without any matrimonial prospects.", "어쨌든 그는 아내를 하나 얻지 않았는가—주인공이라면 마땅히 그래야 하듯이 말이다. 그런데 나는 내 가엾은 벗 조니를 혼인의 가망이라고는 하나도 없이 남겨 두어야 하는 것이다."]], [["It was thus that he thought of himself as he sat moping over his solitary table in the hotel coffee-room.", "호텔 식당의 제 외로운 식탁 앞에서 시무룩하게 앉아 있는 동안 그가 제 자신을 두고 생각한 것이 바로 그런 것이었다."], ["He acknowledged to himself that he had not hitherto been a man; but at the same time he made some resolution which, I trust, may assist him in commencing his manhood from this date.", "그는 여태껏 자신이 어른이 아니었음을 스스로 인정했다. 그러나 그와 동시에 그는 어떤 결심을 하나 세웠으니, 바라건대 그 결심이 오늘 이날부터 그가 어른의 삶을 시작하는 데에 도움이 되기를 바란다."]], [["# CHAPTER LX. CONCLUSION.", "# 제60장. 결말"]], [["It was early in June that Lily went up to her uncle at the Great House, pleading for Hopkins,--pleading that to Hopkins might be restored all the privileges of head gardener at the Great House.", "릴리가 큰 집으로 삼촌을 찾아가 홉킨스를 위해 사정한 것은 유월 초의 일이었다—홉킨스에게 큰 집 수석 정원사의 온갖 특권을 되돌려 주십사 하는 사정이었다."], ["There was some absurdity in this, seeing that he had never really relinquished his privileges; but the manner of the quarrel had been in this wise.", "여기에는 좀 우스운 데가 있었으니, 그가 실은 제 특권을 한 번도 내려놓은 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그 다툼이 벌어진 사정은 이러했다."]], [["There was in those days, and had been for years, a vexed question between Hopkins and Jolliffe the bailiff on the matter of--stable manure.", "그 무렵에도, 또 여러 해 전부터도, 홉킨스와 관리인 졸리프 사이에는 골치 아픈 문제가 하나 있었다—마구간 두엄 문제였다."], ["Hopkins had pretended to the right of taking what he required from the farmyard, without asking leave of any one.", "홉킨스는 누구에게 허락을 구할 것도 없이 농장 마당에서 제게 필요한 만큼 가져갈 권리가 있다고 우겨 왔다."], ["Jolliffe in return had hinted, that if this were so, Hopkins would take it all.", "이에 졸리프는, 만약 그렇다면 홉킨스가 그것을 몽땅 가져가 버릴 것이라고 넌지시 비추었다."], ["\"But I can't eat it,\"", "“그렇다고 내가 그걸 먹을 수야 없잖소.”"], ["Hopkins had said.", "홉킨스는 말했다."], ["Jolliffe merely grunted, signifying by the grunt, as Hopkins thought, that though a gardener couldn't eat a mountain of manure fifty feet long and fifteen high--couldn't eat in the body,--he might convert it into things edible for his own personal use.", "졸리프는 그저 끙 소리만 냈는데, 홉킨스가 생각하기에 그 끙 소리의 뜻은 이러했다. 정원사가 길이 오십 피트에 높이 십오 피트나 되는 두엄 산을 먹을 수는 없다 하더라도—몸으로 직접 먹을 수는 없다 하더라도—그것을 제 몫으로 먹을 것으로 바꿔 놓을 수는 있지 않으냐는 것이었다."], ["And so there had been a great feud.", "그리하여 큰 반목이 생겼다."], ["The unfortunate squire had of course been called on to arbitrate, and having postponed his decision by every contrivance possible to him, had at last been driven by Jolliffe to declare that Hopkins should take nothing that was not assigned to him.", "딱한 지주는 당연하다는 듯이 중재를 맡아 달라는 부름을 받았고, 제가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 꾀로 판결을 미루다가 마침내 졸리프에게 떠밀려, 홉킨스는 제게 배정되지 않은 것은 하나도 가져가서는 안 된다고 선언하고 말았다."], ["Hopkins, when the decision was made known to him by his master, bit his old lips, and turned round upon his old heel, speechless.", "주인에게서 그 판결을 전해 들은 홉킨스는 늙은 입술을 깨물고 늙은 발꿈치로 홱 돌아섰을 뿐 말이 없었다."], ["\"You'll find it's so at all other places,\"", "“다른 데서도 다들 그렇게 하네.”"], ["said the squire, apologetically.", "지주가 변명하듯 말했다."], ["\"Other places!\"", "“다른 데라굽쇼!”"], ["sneered Hopkins.", "홉킨스가 코웃음을 쳤다."], ["Where would he find other gardeners like himself?", "자기 같은 정원사를 다른 어디서 찾을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["It is hardly necessary to declare that from that moment he resolved that he would abide by no such order.", "그 순간부터 그가 그런 명령 따위는 결코 지키지 않겠다고 마음먹었다는 것은 굳이 밝힐 것도 없다."], ["Jolliffe on the next morning informed the squire that the order had been broken, and the squire fretted and fumed, wishing that Jolliffe were well buried under the mountain in question.", "이튿날 아침 졸리프는 그 명령이 깨졌다고 지주에게 알렸고, 지주는 안달하며 씩씩거렸다. 차라리 졸리프가 그 문제의 두엄 산 밑에 곱게 파묻혀 있었으면 싶었다."], ["\"If they all is to do as they like,\"", "“다들 제멋대로 하겠다면요.” 졸리프가 말했다."], ["said Jolliffe, \"then nobody won't care for nobody.\"", "“아무도 아무한테도 아랑곳하지 않게 될 겁니다요.”"], ["The squire understood that an order if given must be obeyed, and therefore, with many inner groanings of the spirit, resolved that war must be waged against Hopkins.", "지주는 명령이란 한번 내려진 이상 지켜져야 한다는 것을 알고 있었고, 그리하여 속으로 여러 번 끙끙 앓은 끝에 홉킨스를 상대로 싸움을 벌여야겠다고 마음을 정했다."]], [["On the following morning he found the old man himself wheeling a huge barrow of manure round from the yard into the kitchen-garden.", "이튿날 아침 그는 그 늙은이가 손수 커다란 두엄 손수레를 마당에서 채마밭 쪽으로 밀고 돌아 나오는 것을 보았다."], ["Now, on ordinary occasions, Hopkins was not required to do with his own hands work of that description.", "그런데 여느 때 같으면 홉킨스가 그런 종류의 일을 제 손으로 할 필요는 없었다."], ["He had a man under him who hewed wood, and carried water, and wheeled barrows,--one man always, and often two.", "그의 밑에는 장작을 패고 물을 나르고 손수레를 미는 사내가 있었으니—늘 한 사람, 흔히는 두 사람이었다."], ["The squire knew when he saw him that he was sinning, and bade him stop upon his road.", "지주는 그를 보는 순간 그가 죄를 짓고 있음을 알아차렸고, 가던 길에 멈춰 서라고 명했다."]], [["\"Hopkins,\"", "“홉킨스.”"], ["he said, \"why didn't you ask for what you wanted, before you took it?\"", "그가 말했다. “가져가기 전에 필요한 것을 왜 청하지 않았나?”"], ["The old man put down the barrow on the ground, looked up in his master's face, spat into his hands, and then again resumed his barrow.", "늙은이는 손수레를 땅에 내려놓고 주인의 얼굴을 올려다보더니, 두 손에 침을 뱉고는 다시 손수레를 잡아 들었다."], ["\"Hopkins, that won't do,\"", "“홉킨스, 그래서는 안 되네.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["\"Stop where you are.\"", "“거기 그대로 있게.”"]], [["\"What won't do?\"", "“무엇이 안 된다는 말씀입니까요?”"], ["said Hopkins, still holding the barrow from the ground, but not as yet progressing.", "홉킨스가 말했다. 손수레는 여전히 땅에서 들어 올린 채였으나 아직 나아가지는 않았다."]], [["\"Put it down, Hopkins,\"", "“내려놓게, 홉킨스.”"], ["and Hopkins did put it down.", "그러자 홉킨스는 그것을 내려놓았다."], ["\"Don't you know that you are flatly disobeying my orders?\"", "“자네가 내 명령을 대놓고 어기고 있다는 걸 모르나?”"]], [["\"Squire, I've been here about this place going on nigh seventy years.\"", "“지주 어른, 제가 이 댁 언저리에서 지낸 것이 이제 일흔 해가 다 되어 갑니다요.”"]], [["\"If you've been going on a hundred and seventy it wouldn't do that there should be more than one master.", "“백일흔 해가 다 되어 간다 해도 주인이 둘일 수는 없네."], ["I'm the master here, and I intend to be so to the end.", "여기 주인은 나고, 나는 끝까지 그럴 작정이야."], ["Take that manure back into the yard.\"", "그 두엄을 마당으로 도로 가져가게.”"]], [["\"Back into the yard?\"", "“마당으로 도로 말입니까요?”"], ["said Hopkins, very slowly.", "홉킨스가 아주 느릿느릿 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; back into the yard.\"", "“그래, 마당으로 도로.”"]], [["\"What,--afore all their faces?\"", "“아니, 저 사람들 다 보는 앞에서 말입니까요?”"]], [["\"Yes; you've disobeyed me before all their faces?\"", "“그래, 자네는 저 사람들 다 보는 앞에서 내 말을 어기지 않았나?”"]], [["Hopkins paused a moment, looking away from the squire, and shaking his head as though he had need of deep thought, but by the aid of deep thought had come at last to a right conclusion.", "홉킨스는 잠시 말이 없었다. 지주에게서 눈길을 돌린 채, 깊이 생각할 필요가 있다는 듯, 그러나 깊이 생각한 덕분에 마침내 옳은 결론에 이르렀다는 듯 고개를 흔들었다."], ["Then he resumed the barrow, and putting himself almost into a trot, carried away his prize into the kitchen-garden.", "그러고 나서 그는 다시 손수레를 잡더니 거의 종종걸음을 치다시피 하여 제 전리품을 채마밭으로 실어 갔다."], ["At the pace which he went it would have been beyond the squire's power to stop him, nor would Mr.", "그가 내는 속도로는 지주의 힘으로 그를 멈춰 세울 수 없었을 것이고, 데일 씨 또한 제 하인과 몸으로 맞붙고 싶지도 않았다."], ["Dale have wished to come to a personal encounter with his servant.", "그러나 그는 무섭게 성이 나서 그 사내의 뒤에다 대고 소리쳤다."], ["But he called after the man in dire wrath that if he were not obeyed the disobedient servant should rue the consequences for ever.", "제 말을 듣지 않는다면 그 불복종한 하인은 두고두고 그 대가를 뉘우치게 되리라고."], ["Hopkins, equal to the occasion, shook his head as he trotted on, deposited his load at the foot of the cucumber-frames, and then at once returning to his master, tendered to him the key of the greenhouse.", "홉킨스는 그 자리에 어울리게 종종걸음을 치면서 고개를 흔들었고, 짐을 오이 온상 발치에 부려 놓은 다음 곧바로 주인에게 돌아와 온실 열쇠를 내밀었다."]], [["\"Master,\"", "“나리.” 홉킨스가 모자란 숨으로 낼 수 있는 최선의 목소리로 말했다."], ["said Hopkins, speaking as best he could with his scanty breath, \"there it is;--there's the key; of course I don't want no warning, and doesn't care about my week's wages.", "“여기 있습니다요—여기 열쇠가 있습니다요. 물론 저는 미리 통고해 주십사 할 것도 없고, 이번 주 삯도 바라지 않습니다요."], ["I'll be out of the cottage afore night, and as for the work'us, I suppose they'll let me in at once, if your honour'll give 'em a line.\"", "밤이 되기 전에 오두막에서 나가겠습니다요. 그리고 구빈원으로 말하자면, 나리께서 한 줄 적어 주시기만 하면 곧바로 들여보내 주겠지요.”"]], [["Now as Hopkins was well known by the squire to be the owner of three or four hundred pounds, the hint about the workhouse must be allowed to have been melodramatic.", "그런데 홉킨스가 삼사백 파운드를 가진 사람이라는 것은 지주도 익히 아는 바였으니, 구빈원 이야기는 지나친 연극이었다고 해 두어야겠다."]], [["\"Don't be a fool,\"", "“바보 같은 소리 말게.”"], ["said the squire, almost gnashing his teeth.", "지주가 이를 갈다시피 하며 말했다."]], [["\"I know I've been a fool,\"", "“제가 바보짓을 한 건 저도 압니다요.” 홉킨스가 말했다."], ["said Hopkins, \"about that 'ere doong; my feelings has been too much for me.", "“그 두엄 일로 말입지요. 제 속에 있는 것이 저한테는 너무 벅찼습니다요."], ["When a man's feelings has been too much for him, he'd better just take hisself off, and lie in the work'us till he dies.\"", "사람이 제 속에 있는 것을 이기지 못할 지경이 되면, 그저 제 발로 물러나서 죽을 때까지 구빈원에 누워 있는 편이 낫습지요.”"], ["And then he again tendered the key.", "그러고서 그는 다시 열쇠를 내밀었다."], ["But the squire did not take the key, and so Hopkins went on.", "그러나 지주가 열쇠를 받지 않자 홉킨스는 말을 이었다."], ["\"I s'pose I'd better just see to the lights and the like of that, till you've suited yourself, Mr. Dale.", "“아무래도 제가 유리덮개며 그런 것들이나 보아 드리는 게 낫겠습니다요."], ["It 'ud be a pity all them grapes should go off, and they, as you may say, all one as fit for the table.", "데일 어르신께서 사람을 구하실 때까지 말입지요. 저 포도가 다 못쓰게 되면 딱한 일 아니겠습니까요."], ["It's a long way the best crop I ever see on 'em.", "이제는 말하자면 죄다 상에 올려도 될 만큼 익었는데 말입니다요."], ["I've been that careful with 'em that I haven't had a natural night's rest, not since February.", "제가 여태 본 중에 단연 제일 좋은 수확입니다요. 제가 저것들한테 어찌나 마음을 쓰는지 이월 이래로 편히 잠들어 본 밤이 하루도 없습니다요."], ["There ain't nobody about this place as understands grapes, nor yet anywhere nigh that could be got at.", "이 댁 언저리에는 포도를 아는 사람이 아무도 없고, 가까이 어디에도 데려올 만한 사람이 없습니다요."], ["My lord's head man is wery ignorant; but even if he knew ever so, of course he couldn't come here.", "백작님 댁 우두머리 정원사는 아주 무식합지요. 하지만 그자가 아무리 잘 안다 한들 물론 여기로 올 수야 없는 노릇이고요."], ["I suppose I'd better keep the key till you're suited, Mr. Dale.\"", "아무래도 사람을 구하실 때까지는 제가 열쇠를 갖고 있는 게 낫겠습니다요, 데일 어르신.”"]], [["Then for a fortnight there was an interregnum in the gardens, terrible in the annals of Allington.", "그러고서 두 주 동안 정원에는 주인 없는 시절이 이어졌으니, 앨링턴의 연대기에 길이 남을 끔찍한 일이었다."], ["Hopkins lived in his cottage indeed, and looked most sedulously after the grapes.", "홉킨스는 그야 제 오두막에 살면서 포도를 더없이 부지런히 돌보았다."], ["In looking after the grapes, too, he took the greenhouses under his care; but he would have nothing to do with the outer gardens, took no wages, returning the amount sent to him back to the squire, and insisted with everybody that he had been dismissed.", "포도를 돌보다 보니 온실들도 제 손으로 맡게 되었다. 그러나 바깥 정원 일에는 손끝 하나 대려 하지 않았고, 삯도 받지 않아 보내온 것을 지주에게 도로 돌려보냈으며, 자기는 쫓겨난 것이라고 누구에게나 우겼다."], ["He went about with some terrible horticultural implement always in his hand, with which it was said that he intended to attack Jolliffe; but Jolliffe prudently kept out of his way.", "그는 늘 무슨 무시무시한 원예 연장을 손에 들고 돌아다녔는데, 그것으로 졸리프를 덮칠 작정이라는 말이 돌았다. 그러나 졸리프는 조심스럽게 그의 앞길을 피해 다녔다."]], [["As soon as it had been resolved by Mrs. Dale and Lily that the flitting from the Small House at Allington was not to be accomplished, Lily communicated the fact to Hopkins.", "앨링턴의 작은 집에서 이사하는 일을 그만두기로 데일 부인과 릴리가 정하자마자, 릴리는 그 사실을 홉킨스에게 알렸다."]], [["\"Miss,\"", "“아가씨.”"], ["said he, \"when I said them few words to you and your mamma, I knew that you would listen to reason.\"", "그가 말했다. “제가 아가씨와 마님께 그 몇 마디를 드렸을 때, 저는 아가씨가 사리에 귀를 기울이시리라는 걸 알았습니다요.”"]], [["This was no more than Lily had expected; that Hopkins should claim the honour of having prevailed by his arguments was a matter of course.", "이것은 릴리가 예상한 그대로였다. 홉킨스가 제 주장으로 일을 이겨 냈다는 영예를 제 몫으로 내세우리라는 것은 당연한 일이었다."]], [["\"Yes,\"", "“그래요.”"], ["said Lily; \"we've made up our minds to stay.", "릴리가 말했다. “우리는 남기로 마음을 정했어요."], ["Uncle wishes it.\"", "삼촌이 그러기를 바라시니까요.”"]], [["\"Wishes it!", "“바라시다니요!"], ["Laws, miss; it ain't only wishes.", "아이고, 아가씨. 바라시는 정도가 아닙지요."], ["And we all wishes it.", "그리고 우리 모두가 바라고 있습니다요."], ["Why, now, look at the reason of the thing.", "아니, 자, 이 일의 이치를 좀 보십쇼."], ["Here's this here house--\"", "여기 이 집으로 말할 것 같으면—”"]], [["\"But, Hopkins, it's decided.", "“그렇지만 홉킨스, 다 정해진 일이에요."], ["We're going to stay.", "우리는 남을 거예요."], ["What I want to know is this; can you come at once and help me to unpack?\"", "내가 알고 싶은 건 이거예요. 지금 당장 와서 짐 푸는 걸 도와줄 수 있어요?”"]], [["\"What!", "“아니!"], ["this very evening, as is--\"", "바로 오늘 저녁에 말입니까요—”"]], [["\"Yes, now; we want to have the things about again before they come back from Guestwick.\"", "“그래요, 지금요. 그분들이 게스트윅에서 돌아오기 전에 물건들을 도로 제자리에 놓고 싶어요.”"]], [["Hopkins scratched his head and hesitated, not wishing to yield to any proposition that could be considered as childish; but he gave way at last, feeling that the work itself was a good work.", "홉킨스는 머리를 긁적이며 망설였다. 어린애 같다고 여겨질 만한 제안에 넘어가고 싶지는 않았던 것이다."], ["Mrs. Dale also assented, laughing at Lily for her folly as she did so, and in this way the things were unpacked very quickly, and the alliance between Lily and Hopkins became, for the time, very close.", "그러나 그 일 자체가 좋은 일임을 느끼고 마침내 물러섰다."], ["This work of unpacking and resettling was not yet over, when the battle of the manure broke out, and therefore it was that Hopkins, when his feelings had become altogether too much for him \"about the doong,\"", "데일 부인도 그러라고 하면서 릴리의 어리석음을 두고 웃었고, 이렇게 해서 짐은 아주 빠르게 풀렸으며 릴리와 홉킨스 사이는 그동안 아주 가까워졌다. 이 짐을 풀고 다시 자리를 잡는 일이 채 끝나기도 전에 두엄 싸움이 터졌고, 그래서 홉킨스는 “그 두엄 일로”"], ["came at last to Lily, and laying down at her feet all the weight and all the glory of his sixty odd years of life, implored her to make matters straight for him.", "제 속에 있는 것이 아주 벅차 이길 수 없게 되자 마침내 릴리에게 찾아와, 예순 몇 해 인생의 온갖 무게와 온갖 영광을 그녀의 발치에 내려놓고서 제 일을 바로잡아 달라고 애원했던 것이다. “저는 그것 때문에 죽을 지경입니다요, 아가씨."], ["\"It's been a killing me, miss, so it has; to see the way they've been a cutting that 'sparagus.", "정말입니다요. 저것들이 그 아스파라거스를 잘라 내는 꼴을 보면 말입니다요."], ["It ain't cutting at all.", "그건 자르는 것도 아닙지요."], ["It's just hocking it up;--what is fit, and what isn't, all together.", "그냥 마구 파헤치는 겁니다요—될 것 안 될 것 죄다 한꺼번에 말입니다요."], ["And they've been a-putting the plants in where I didn't mean 'em, though they know'd I didn't mean 'em.", "게다가 제가 심으려던 자리가 아닌 데다 모종을 넣어 놓았습니다요. 제가 거기 심으려는 게 아니라는 걸 뻔히 알면서도요."], ["I've stood by, miss, and said never a word. I'd a died sooner.", "저는 옆에 서서, 아가씨, 한마디도 하지 않았습니다요."], ["But, Miss Lily, what my sufferings have been, 'cause of my feelings getting the better of me about that--you know, miss--nobody will ever tell;--nobody--nobody--nobody.\"", "차라리 죽는 편이 나았지요. 하지만 릴리 아가씨, 그—아가씨도 아시는 그것 때문에 제 속에 있는 것이 저를 이겨 버려서 제가 겪은 고생이 어떠했는지는 아무도 모를 겁니다요—아무도—아무도—아무도.”"], ["Then Hopkins turned away and wept.", "그러고서 홉킨스는 돌아서서 울었다."]], [["\"Uncle,\"", "“삼촌.”"], ["said Lily, creeping close up against his chair, \"I want to ask you a great favour.\"", "릴리가 그의 의자에 바싹 다가붙으며 말했다. “큰 부탁을 하나 드리고 싶어요.”"]], [["\"A great favour.", "“큰 부탁이라."], ["Well, I don't think I shall refuse you anything at present.", "글쎄, 지금 같아서는 너한테 무엇이든 거절하지는 못할 것 같구나."], ["It isn't to ask another earl to the house,--is it?\"", "백작을 또 한 사람 이 집에 부르자는 건 아니겠지—그렇지?”"]], [["\"Another earl!\"", "“백작을 또 한 사람이라니요!”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Yes; haven't you heard?", "“그래, 못 들었느냐?"], ["Miss Bell has been here this morning, insisting that I should have over Lord De Guest and his sister for the marriage.", "오늘 아침에 벨 아가씨가 여기 와서, 드 게스트 경과 그 누이를 혼례에 불러야 한다고 우겨 대더구나."], ["It seems that there was some scheming between Bell and Lady Julia.\"", "벨과 레이디 줄리아 사이에 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있었던 모양이야.”"]], [["\"Of course you'll ask them.\"", "“당연히 부르셔야죠.”"]], [["\"Of course I must.", "“당연히 그래야지."], ["I've no way out of it.", "빠져나갈 길이 없구나."], ["It'll be all very well for Bell, who'll be off to Wales with her lover; but what am I to do with the earl and Lady Julia, when they're gone?", "벨이야 제 연인과 웨일스로 떠나 버릴 테니 다 좋겠지. 그런데 그 애들이 가고 나면 나는 백작과 레이디 줄리아를 데리고 무엇을 해야 하느냐?"], ["Will you come and help me?\"", "네가 와서 나를 좀 도와주겠느냐?”"]], [["In answer to this, Lily of course promised that she would come and help.", "이에 대해 릴리는 물론 와서 돕겠노라고 약속했다."], ["\"Indeed,\"", "“아니.”"], ["said she, \"I thought we were all asked up for the day.", "그녀가 말했다. “저는 우리가 다 그날 하루 초대받은 줄 알았는데요."], ["And now for my favour.", "자, 이제 제 부탁이에요."], ["Uncle, you must forgive poor Hopkins.\"", "삼촌, 딱한 홉킨스를 용서해 주셔야 해요.”"]], [["\"Forgive a fiddlestick!\"", "“용서는 무슨 놈의 용서!”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["\"No, but you must.", "“아니에요, 그래도 하셔야 해요."], ["You can't think how unhappy he is.\"", "그이가 얼마나 불행한지 삼촌은 모르실 거예요.”"]], [["\"How can I forgive a man who won't forgive me.", "“나를 용서하지 않는 사람을 내가 어떻게 용서하느냐. 그자는 아무 일도 안 하면서 온 데를 어슬렁거리고 다녀."], ["He goes prowling about the place doing nothing; and he sends me back his wages, and he looks as though he were going to murder some one; and all because he wouldn't do as he was told.", "게다가 제 삯을 나한테 도로 보내고, 누구라도 죽일 것 같은 낯짝을 하고 다니지. 그게 다 시키는 대로 하지 않으려 해서 생긴 일이야."], ["How am I to forgive such a man as that?\"", "그런 사람을 내가 어떻게 용서한단 말이냐?”"]], [["\"But, uncle, why not?\"", "“그렇지만 삼촌, 왜 못 하세요?”"]], [["\"It would be his forgiving me.", "“그건 그자가 나를 용서하는 게 될 게다."], ["He knows very well that he may come back whenever he pleases; and, indeed, for the matter of that he has never gone away.\"", "그자는 저 오고 싶을 때 언제든 돌아와도 된다는 걸 아주 잘 알아. 게다가 사실을 말하자면 그자는 여태 나간 적도 없어.”"]], [["\"But he is so very unhappy.\"", "“그렇지만 그이가 아주 불행하잖아요.”"]], [["\"What can I do to make him happier?\"", "“그자를 더 기쁘게 해 주려면 내가 무엇을 해야 하느냐?”"]], [["\"Just go down to his cottage and tell him that you forgive him.\"", "“그냥 그이 오두막에 내려가셔서 용서한다고 말씀해 주세요.”"]], [["\"Then he'll argue with me.\"", "“그러면 나한테 따지고 들 게다.”"]], [["\"No; I don't think he will.", "“아니에요, 그러지 않을 거예요."], ["He is too much down in the world for arguing now.\"", "지금은 너무 풀이 죽어 있어서 따질 처지가 아니에요.”"]], [["\"Ah!", "“허!"], ["you don't know him as I do.", "너는 나만큼 그자를 알지 못한다."], ["All the misfortunes in the world wouldn't stop that man's conceit.", "세상의 온갖 불행이 다 닥친다 해도 그 사람의 자만심은 꺾이지 않아."], ["Of course I'll go if you ask me, but it seems to me that I'm made to knock under to everybody.", "물론 네가 청하니 가기야 가겠다만, 나는 아무한테나 다 굽히라고 생겨난 사람 같구나."], ["I hear a great deal about other people's feelings, but I don't know that mine are very much thought of.\"", "남들 속마음 이야기는 실컷 듣는데, 내 속마음을 그리 많이 헤아려 주는 것 같지는 않으니 말이다.”"], ["He was not altogether in a happy mood, and Lily almost regretted that she had persevered; but she did succeed in carrying him off across the garden to the cottage, and as they went together she promised him that she would think of him always,--always.", "그는 마냥 기분이 좋은 상태는 아니었고, 릴리는 괜히 고집을 부렸나 하고 뉘우칠 뻔했다. 그러나 그녀는 끝내 그를 이끌어 정원을 가로질러 오두막까지 데려가는 데 성공했고, 함께 가는 동안 그녀는 언제나—언제나 삼촌을 생각하겠노라고 약속했다."], ["The scene with Hopkins cannot be described now, as it would take too many of our few remaining pages.", "홉킨스와의 그 장면은 이제 와서 옮길 수가 없다. 얼마 남지 않은 우리의 지면을 너무 많이 잡아먹을 터이기 때문이다."], ["It resulted, I am afraid I must confess, in nothing more triumphant to the squire than a treaty of mutual forgiveness.", "그 결과는, 미안하지만 고백하건대, 지주에게 서로 용서하기로 하는 조약 이상의 아무런 승리도 되지 못했다."], ["Hopkins acknowledged, with much self-reproach, that his feelings had been too many for him; but then, look at his provocation!", "홉킨스는 스스로를 몹시 나무라며 제 속에 있는 것이 저한테 너무 벅찼노라고 인정했다. 하지만 그가 그토록 부아가 난 사정도 좀 보아 달라는 것이었다!"], ["He could not keep his tongue from that matter, and certainly said as much in his own defence as he did in confession of his sins.", "그는 그 문제에서 혀를 거두지 못했고, 제 죄를 고백하는 만큼이나 제 변명도 실컷 늘어놓았다."], ["The substantial triumph was altogether his, for nobody again ever dared to interfere with his operations in the farmyard.", "실속 있는 승리는 온전히 그의 것이었으니, 그 뒤로 다시는 아무도 감히 농장 마당에서 벌이는 그의 일에 간섭하지 못했기 때문이다."], ["He showed his submission to his master mainly by consenting to receive his wages for the two weeks which he had passed in idleness.", "그가 주인에게 복종한다는 뜻을 내보인 것이라고는 놀고 지낸 두 주 치 삯을 받겠다고 승낙한 것이 고작이었다."]], [["Owing to this little accident, Lily was not so much oppressed by Hopkins as she had expected to be in that matter of their altered plans; but this salvation did not extend to Mrs.", "이 자잘한 사건 덕분에 릴리는 계획이 바뀐 그 문제로 홉킨스에게 시달릴 줄 알았던 것만큼은 시달리지 않았다. 그러나 이 구원은 헌 부인에게도, 크럼프 부인에게도, 무엇보다 보이스 부인에게까지는 미치지 않았다."], ["Hearn, to Mrs. Crump, or, above all, to Mrs.", "그들은 저마다 정도의 차이는 있으나 홉킨스 소동에 관심을 두었다."], ["Boyce. They, all of them, took an interest more or less strong in the Hopkins controversy; but their interest in the occupation of the Small House was much stronger, and it was found useless to put Mrs.", "그러나 작은 집에 누가 사느냐에 대한 관심은 훨씬 더 컸으니, 헌 부인이 그 집을 바깥만이 아니라 안까지 칠하느냐를 묻고 싶어 잔뜩 부풀어 있는 판에 정원사가 삯을 한사코 마다한다는 이야기로 그녀를 따돌리려 해 봐야 소용이 없었다."], ["Hearn off with the gardener's persistent refusal of his wages, when she was big with inquiry whether the house was to be painted inside, as well as out.", "“아이고.”"], ["\"Ah,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"I think I'll go and look at lodgings at Guestwick myself, and pack up some of my beds.\"", "“나도 게스트윅에 셋집이나 보러 가서 내 침대나 몇 개 싸 두어야겠어요.” 릴리는 여기에 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["Lily made no answer to this, feeling that it was a part of that punishment which she had expected.", "이것이 자기가 각오했던 벌의 한 부분이라고 느낀 것이다. “저런, 저런.”"], ["\"Dear, dear,\" said Mrs.", "크럼프 부인이 두 아가씨에게 말했다."], ["Crump to the two girls; \"well, to be sure, we should 'a been 'lone without 'ee, and mayhap we might 'a got worse in your place; but why did 'ee go and fasten up all your things in them big boxes, just to unfasten 'em all again?\"", "“하기야 그렇지요, 아가씨들이 없으면 우리야 쓸쓸했을 테고, 아가씨들 자리에 더 나쁜 사람이 들어왔을지도 모르지요. 그렇지만 어째서 물건들을 그 큰 상자에 죄다 잡아매어 놓으셨답니까요, 그걸 다시 죄다 끌러 내시려고 말입니다요?”"]], [["\"We changed our minds, Mrs. Crump,\"", "“우리는 마음을 바꾼 거예요, 크럼프 부인.”"], ["said Bell, with some severity.", "벨이 조금 매섭게 말했다."]], [["\"Yees, I know ye changed your mindses.", "“예에, 마음들을 바꾸신 거야 저도 압니다요."], ["Well, it's all right for loiks o'", "그야 뭐 아가씨들 같은 분들이야 그래도 아무 탈이 없겠지요."], ["ye, no doubt; but if we changes our mindses, we hears of it.\"", "그렇지만 우리 같은 것들이 마음들을 바꿨다가는 소리깨나 듣습지요.”"]], [["\"So, it seems, do we!\"", "“그건 우리도 마찬가지인 모양인데요!”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."], ["\"But never mind, Mrs. Crump.", "“하지만 신경 쓰지 마세요, 크럼프 부인."], ["Do you send us our letters up early, and then we won't quarrel.\"", "편지나 일찍 올려 보내 주시면 우리도 다투지 않을게요.”"]], [["\"Oh, letters!", "“어이구, 편지요!"], ["Drat them for letters.", "편지 따위 염병할."], ["I wish there weren't no sich things.", "그런 것이 아예 없었으면 좋겠습니다요."], ["There was a man here yesterday with his imperence.", "어제는 여기 웬 사내가 와서 건방을 떨었지 뭡니까요."], ["I don't know where he come from,--down from Lun'on, I b'leeve: and this was wrong, and that was wrong, and everything was wrong; and then he said he'd have me discharged the sarvice.\"", "어디서 온 것인지도 모르겠고—런던에서 내려온 모양입디다요. 그런데 이것도 잘못됐다, 저것도 잘못됐다, 죄다 잘못됐다 하더니, 나중에는 나를 이 직에서 쫓아내겠다고 하더군입쇼.”"]], [["\"Dear me, Mrs.", "“어머나, 크럼프 부인."], ["Crump; that wouldn't do at all.\"", "그건 절대로 안 되죠.”"]], [["\"Discharged the sarvice!", "“이 직에서 쫓아낸다굽쇼!"], ["Tuppence farden a day.", "하루에 이 펜스하고 한 파딩을 받는데요."], ["So I told 'un to discharge hisself, and take all the old bundles and things away upon his shoulders.", "그래서 제가 그자더러 제 발로나 나가라고, 그 낡은 꾸러미며 물건들이나 죄다 어깨에 지고 가라고 해 주었습니다요."], ["Letters indeed!", "편지가 다 뭡니까요!"], ["What business have they with post-missusses, if they cannot pay 'em better nor tuppence farden a day?\"", "우체국장한테 하루 이 펜스 한 파딩보다 더 못 주겠다면 대체 무슨 볼일이 있다는 말입니까요?”"], ["And in this way, under the shelter of Mrs. Crump's storm of wrath against the inspector who had visited her, Lily and Bell escaped much that would have fallen upon their own heads; but Mrs.", "이렇게 해서, 저를 찾아온 감독관을 향한 크럼프 부인의 노여움이 폭풍처럼 몰아치는 그 그늘에 숨어, 릴리와 벨은 제 머리 위로 쏟아졌을 많은 것을 모면했다."], ["Boyce still remained. I may here add, in order that Mrs.", "그러나 보이스 부인은 아직 남아 있었다."], ["Crump's history may be carried on to the farthest possible point, that she was not \"discharged the sarvice,\"", "여기에 덧붙여 두거니와, 크럼프 부인의 이야기를 갈 수 있는 데까지 끌고 가 두자면, 그녀는 “이 직에서 쫓겨나지”"], ["and that she still receives her twopence farthing a day from the Crown.", "않았고 지금도 나라에서 하루 이 펜스 한 파딩을 받고 있다."], ["\"That's a bitter old lady,\"", "“고약한 늙은이로군.”"], ["said the inspector to the man who was driving him.", "감독관이 제 마차를 모는 사내에게 말했다. “그렇습니다요, 나리."], ["\"Yes, sir; they all says the same about she.", "그 여자에 대해서는 다들 똑같이 말합니다요."], ["There ain't none of 'em get much change out of Mrs. Crump.\"", "크럼프 부인한테서 재미 보는 사람은 하나도 없습죠.”"]], [["Bell and Lily went together also to Mrs. Boyce's.", "벨과 릴리는 보이스 부인네에도 함께 갔다."], ["\"If she makes herself very disagreeable, I shall insist upon talking of your marriage,\"", "“그 부인이 아주 밉살스럽게 굴면 나는 언니 혼인 이야기를 기어이 꺼낼 거야.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"I've not the slightest objection,\"", "“나야 조금도 반대하지 않아.”"], ["said Bell; \"only I don't know what there can be to say about it.", "벨이 말했다. “다만 그 얘기에 무슨 할 말이 있을지 모르겠구나."], ["Marrying the doctor is such a very commonplace sort of thing.\"", "의사와 혼인하는 건 아주 평범한 일이잖니.”"]], [["\"Not a bit more commonplace than marrying the parson,\"", "“목사와 혼인하는 것보다 조금도 더 평범하지 않은데.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes, it is. Parsons'", "“아니야, 더 평범해."], ["marriages are often very grand affairs.", "목사의 혼례는 흔히 아주 성대한 일이 되지."], ["They come in among county people.", "고을의 유지들 축에 끼어드니까."], ["That's their luck in life.", "그게 그 사람들의 인생 운이야."], ["Doctors never do; nor lawyers.", "의사는 절대 그렇지 못하고, 법률가도 마찬가지야."], ["I don't think lawyers ever get married in the country.", "법률가는 시골에서 혼인하는 법이 아예 없는 것 같아."], ["They're supposed to do it up in London.", "그건 런던에 올라가서 하기로 되어 있나 봐."], ["But a country doctor's wedding is not a thing to be talked about much.\"", "하지만 시골 의사의 혼례란 두고두고 이야깃거리가 될 만한 것이 못 돼.”"]], [["Mrs. Boyce probably agreed in this view of the matter, seeing that she did not choose the coming marriage as her first subject of conversation.", "보이스 부인도 이 문제를 그렇게 보았던 모양이니, 다가오는 혼례를 첫 화젯거리로 고르지 않은 것을 보면 그렇다."], ["As soon as the two girls were seated she flew away immediately to the house, and began to express her very great surprise,--her surprise and her joy also,--at the sudden change which had been made in their plans.", "두 아가씨가 자리에 앉자마자 그녀는 곧장 집 이야기로 날아가, 계획이 갑작스레 바뀐 데 대한 자기의 크나큰 놀라움을—놀라움과 아울러 기쁨도—늘어놓기 시작했다. “그편이 훨씬 좋지요, 아시다시피.”"], ["\"It is so much nicer, you know,\"", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, \"that things should be pleasant among relatives.\"", "“친척끼리는 일이 화기애애해야 하는 법이니까요.”"]], [["\"Things always have been tolerably pleasant with us,\"", "“우리는 늘 그럭저럭 화기애애했는걸요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Oh, yes; I'm sure of that.", "“아, 그럼요. 그건 저도 알아요."], ["I've always said it was quite a pleasure to see you and your uncle together.", "아가씨들이 삼촌하고 함께 있는 걸 보는 게 참 흐뭇하다고 저는 늘 말해 왔는걸요."], ["And when we heard about your all having to leave--\"", "그런데 아가씨들이 다 떠나야 한다는 이야기를 들었을 때는—”"]], [["\"But we didn't have to leave, Mrs. Boyce.", "“하지만 우리는 떠나야 했던 게 아니에요, 보이스 부인."], ["We were going to leave because we thought mamma would be more comfortable in Guestwick; and now we're not going to leave, because we've all 'changed our mindses,'", "엄마가 게스트윅에서 더 편안하시리라고 생각해서 떠나려던 것이었어요."], ["as Mrs. Crump calls it.\"", "그리고 이제 떠나지 않는 건 우리 모두가 크럼프 부인 말마따나 ‘마음들을 바꾸었기’ 때문이고요.”"]], [["\"And is it true the house is going to be painted?\"", "“그런데 집을 칠한다는 게 사실인가요?”"], ["asked Mrs. Boyce.", "보이스 부인이 물었다."]], [["\"I believe it is true,\"", "“사실인 것 같아요.”"], ["said Lily.", "릴리가 말했다."]], [["\"Inside and out?\"", "“안팎을 다요?”"]], [["\"It must be done some day,\"", "“언젠가는 해야 하는 일이잖아요.”"], ["said Bell.", "벨이 말했다."]], [["\"Yes, to be sure; but I must say it is generous of the squire.", "“그럼요, 그렇고말고요. 하지만 지주 어른이 후하시다는 말은 해야겠네요."], ["There's such a deal of wood-work about your house.", "아가씨들 집에는 나무로 된 데가 어찌나 많은지."], ["I know I wish the Ecclesiastical Commissioners would paint ours; but nobody ever does anything for the clergy.", "저야 교회 재산 위원회에서 우리 집도 칠해 주었으면 싶지만, 성직자를 위해서는 아무도 아무것도 해 주지 않지요."], ["I'm sure I'm delighted you're going to stay.", "아가씨들이 남게 되어 저는 정말 기뻐요."], ["As I said to Mr. Boyce, what should we ever have done without you?", "제가 보이스 씨한테도 말했지만, 아가씨들 없이 우리가 대체 어떻게 지냈겠어요?"], ["I believe the squire had made up his mind that he would not let the place.\"", "저는 지주 어른이 그 집을 세놓지 않으시겠다고 마음을 굳히셨던 것 같아요.”"]], [["\"I don't think he ever has let it.\"", "“그 집을 세놓으신 적은 여태 한 번도 없는 것 같은데요.”"]], [["\"And if there was nobody in it, it would all go to rack and ruin; wouldn't it?", "“그리고 그 안에 아무도 없으면 죄다 허물어져 못쓰게 되지 않겠어요?"], ["Had your mamma to pay anything for the lodgings she engaged at Guestwick?\"", "어머니께서 게스트윅에 얻어 놓으셨던 셋집에는 삯을 좀 무셔야 했나요?”"]], [["\"Upon my word, I don't know.", "“글쎄요, 정말 모르겠어요."], ["Bell can tell you better about that than I, as Dr. Crofts settled it.", "그건 크로프츠 선생이 정리하셨으니 저보다 언니가 더 잘 말해 드릴 수 있을 거예요."], ["I suppose Dr. Crofts tells her everything.\"", "크로프츠 선생은 언니한테 뭐든 다 말씀하시는 것 같으니까요.”"], ["And so the conversation was changed, and Mrs. Boyce was made to understand that whatever further mystery there might be, it would not be unravelled on that occasion.", "그리하여 화제는 바뀌었고, 보이스 부인은 더 캘 만한 수수께끼가 남아 있다 한들 그날은 그것이 풀리지 않으리라는 것을 알아차리게 되었다."]], [["It was settled that Dr. Crofts and Bell should be married about the middle of June, and the squire determined to give what grace he could to the ceremony by opening his own house on the occasion.", "크로프츠 선생과 벨은 유월 중순쯤에 혼인하기로 정해졌고, 지주는 그날을 위해 제 집을 열어 힘닿는 대로 예식에 격을 더해 주기로 마음먹었다."], ["Lord De Guest and Lady Julia were invited by special arrangement between her ladyship and Bell, as has been before explained.", "드 게스트 경과 레이디 줄리아는 앞서 설명한 대로 레이디 줄리아와 벨 사이의 특별한 주선으로 초대되었다."], ["The colonel also with Lady Fanny came up from Torquay on the occasion, this being the first visit made by the colonel to his paternal roof for many years.", "대령도 레이디 패니와 함께 그날을 위해 토키에서 올라왔는데, 대령이 제 아버지의 지붕 아래를 찾은 것은 여러 해 만에 처음이었다."], ["Bernard did not accompany his father.", "버나드는 아버지를 따라오지 않았다."], ["He had not yet gone abroad, but there were circumstances which made him feel that he would not find himself comfortable at the wedding.", "그는 아직 외국으로 떠나지 않았으나, 그로서는 그 혼례 자리가 편치 않으리라고 느낄 만한 사정이 있었다."], ["The service was performed by Mr. Boyce, assisted, as the County Chronicle very fully remarked, by the Reverend John Joseph Jones, M.A., late of Jesus College, Cambridge, and curate of St.", "예식은 보이스 씨가 집전했고, 《카운티 크로니클》이 아주 소상히 적어 둔 대로, 전에 케임브리지 지저스 칼리지에 있었고 지금은 게스트윅 노스게이트 세인트피터 교회의 부목사인 문학 석사 존 조지프 존스 목사가 이를 도왔다."], ["Peter's, Northgate, Guestwick; the fault of which little advertisement was this,--that as none of the readers of the paper had patience to get beyond the Reverend John Joseph Jones, the fact of Bell's marriage with Dr. Crofts was not disseminated as widely as might have been wished.", "이 자그마한 광고의 흠이라면 이런 것이었다—그 신문의 독자 가운데 존 조지프 존스 목사를 넘어서까지 참고 읽어 낸 사람이 하나도 없었던 탓에, 벨이 크로프츠 선생과 혼인했다는 사실은 바랐던 만큼 널리 퍼지지 못했다는 것이다."]], [["The marriage went off very nicely.", "혼례는 아주 좋게 치러졌다."], ["The squire was upon his very best behaviour, and welcomed his guests as though he really enjoyed their presence there in his halls.", "지주는 더할 나위 없이 훌륭하게 처신했고, 손님들이 제 집 넓은 방에 와 있는 것을 정말로 기뻐하는 사람처럼 그들을 맞았다."], ["Hopkins, who was quite aware that he had been triumphant, decorated the old rooms with mingled flowers and greenery with an assiduous care which pleased the two girls mightily.", "자기가 이겼다는 것을 잘 알고 있는 홉킨스는 꽃과 푸른 잎을 섞어 옛 방들을 꾸몄는데, 그 정성스러운 손길이 두 아가씨를 여간 기쁘게 하지 않았다."], ["And during this work of wreathing and decking there was one little morsel of feeling displayed which may as well be told in these last lines.", "그리고 이 화환을 엮고 장식을 다는 일이 이어지는 동안 자그마한 마음 한 조각이 드러났으니, 이 마지막 줄들에 적어 두어도 좋으리라."], ["Lily had been encouraging the old man while Bell for a moment had been absent.", "벨이 잠시 자리를 비운 사이 릴리가 그 늙은이에게 힘을 북돋아 주고 있었다."]], [["\"I wish it had been for thee, my darling!\"", "“이게 아가씨를 위한 것이었더라면 좋았을 것을, 우리 아가씨!”"], ["he said; \"I wish it had been for thee!\"", "그가 말했다. “이게 아가씨를 위한 것이었더라면 좋았을 것을!”"]], [["\"It is much better as it is, Hopkins,\"", "“이대로가 훨씬 좋아요, 홉킨스.”"], ["she answered, solemnly.", "그녀가 엄숙하게 대답했다."]], [["\"Not with him, though,\"", "“그래도 그 사람하고는 아니었습지요.”"], ["he went on, \"not with him.", "그가 말을 이었다. “그 사람하고는 아닙니다요."], ["I wouldn't 'a hung a bough for him.", "저라면 그 사람을 위해서는 나뭇가지 하나 걸어 주지 않았을 겁니다요."], ["But with t'other one.\"", "하지만 그 다른 분하고라면요.”"]], [["Lily said no word further.", "릴리는 더 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She knew that the man was expressing the wishes of all around her.", "그녀는 그 사람이 제 둘레에 있는 모든 이의 바람을 말하고 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["She said no word further, and then Bell returned to them.", "그녀는 더 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 그러고서 벨이 그들에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["But no one at the wedding was so gay as Lily,--so gay, so bright, and so wedding-like.", "그러나 그 혼례에서 릴리만큼 흥겨운 사람은 없었다—그만큼 흥겹고, 그만큼 환하고, 그만큼 혼례다운 사람은 없었다."], ["She flirted with the old earl till he declared that he would marry her himself.", "그녀가 그 늙은 백작에게 어찌나 살갑게 굴었던지 백작은 제가 그녀와 혼인하겠노라고 선언할 지경이었다."], ["No one seeing her that evening, and knowing nothing of her immediate history, would have imagined that she herself had been cruelly jilted some six or eight months ago.", "그날 저녁 그녀를 보고도 그녀의 가까운 지난 일을 모르는 사람이라면, 그녀 자신이 예닐곱 달 아니면 여덟 달 전에 모질게 버림받았으리라고는 짐작도 못 했을 것이다."], ["And those who did know her could not imagine that what she then suffered had hit her so hard, that no recovery seemed possible for her.", "그리고 그녀를 아는 사람들은, 그때 그녀가 겪은 일이 그녀를 그토록 세게 때려 이제 그녀에게 회복이란 있을 성싶지 않다는 것을 도무지 그려 볼 수 없었다."], ["But though no recovery, as she herself believed, was possible for her--though she was as a man whose right arm had been taken from him in the battle, still all the world had not gone with that right arm.", "그러나 그녀 자신이 믿기로는 회복이란 없었다 하더라도—그녀는 싸움터에서 오른팔을 잃은 사람과 같았다 하더라도—온 세상이 그 오른팔과 함께 사라져 버린 것은 아니었다."], ["The bullet which had maimed her sorely had not touched her life, and she scorned to go about the world complaining either by word or look of the injury she had received.", "그녀에게 모진 상처를 입힌 총알이 그녀의 목숨까지 건드리지는 않았고, 그녀는 제가 입은 상처를 말로든 낯빛으로든 하소연하며 세상을 돌아다니는 짓을 떳떳하게 여기지 않았다."], ["\"Wives when they have lost their husbands still eat and laugh,\"", "“남편을 잃은 아내들도 여전히 먹고 웃는 법이지.” 그녀는 속으로 말했다."], ["she said to herself, \"and he is not dead like that.\"", "“게다가 그이는 그렇게 죽은 것도 아니잖아.”"], ["So she resolved that she would be happy, and I here declare that she not only seemed to carry out her resolution, but that she did carry it out in very truth.", "그리하여 그녀는 행복하게 지내리라 마음먹었고, 나는 여기서 그녀가 그 결심을 지켜 낸 듯 보였을 뿐 아니라 참으로 지켜 냈다고 밝혀 둔다."], ["\"You're a dear good man, and I know you'll be good to her,\"", "“선생님은 참 좋으신 분이고, 언니한테 잘해 주시리라는 걸 저는 알아요.”"], ["she said to Crofts just as he was about to start with his bride.", "크로프츠가 막 신부를 데리고 떠나려 할 때 그녀가 그에게 말했다."]], [["\"I'll try, at any rate,\"", "“어쨌든 애써 보지요.”"], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["\"And I shall expect you to be good to me too.", "“그리고 저한테도 잘해 주셔야 해요."], ["Remember you have married the whole family; and, sir, you mustn't believe a word of what that bad man says in his novels about mothers-in-law.", "선생님은 우리 집안 전체와 혼인하셨다는 걸 잊지 마세요. 그리고 선생님, 그 못된 사람이 제 소설들에서 장모에 대해 하는 말은 한마디도 믿으시면 안 돼요."], ["He has done a great deal of harm, and shut half the ladies in England out of their daughters' houses.\"", "그 사람은 아주 큰 해악을 끼쳐서, 잉글랜드의 부인네 절반을 제 딸의 집에서 몰아내 버렸다니까요.”"]], [["\"He shan't shut Mrs. Dale out of mine.\"", "“그 사람이 데일 부인을 제 집에서 몰아내지는 못할 겁니다.”"]], [["\"Remember he doesn't. Now, good-by.\"", "“꼭 그러지 못하게 하셔야 해요."], ["So the bride and bridegroom went off, and Lily was left to flirt with Lord De Guest.", "자, 이제 안녕히 가세요.” 그리하여 신부와 신랑은 떠났고, 릴리는 남아서 드 게스트 경에게 살갑게 굴었다."]], [["Of whom else is it necessary that a word or two should be said before I allow the weary pen to fall from my hand?", "지친 펜을 손에서 떨구도록 내버려 두기 전에, 또 누구에 대해 한두 마디 해 두어야 할까?"], ["The squire, after much inward struggling on the subject, had acknowledged to himself that his sister-in-law had not received from him that kindness which she had deserved.", "지주는 그 문제로 속으로 많이 씨름한 끝에, 제수가 제게서 마땅히 받았어야 할 다정함을 받지 못했다는 것을 스스로 인정했다."], ["He had acknowledged this, purporting to do his best to amend his past errors; and I think I may say that his efforts in that line would not be received ungraciously by Mrs. Dale.", "그는 이것을 인정하면서 지난날의 제 잘못을 힘써 고쳐 보겠노라고 마음먹었고, 그 방면의 그의 노력이 데일 부인에게 곱게 받아들여지지 않을 리는 없으리라고 나는 생각한다."], ["I am inclined therefore to think that life at Allington, both at the Great House and at the Small, would soon become pleasanter than it used to be in former days.", "그러므로 앨링턴의 삶은, 큰 집에서나 작은 집에서나, 머지않아 지난날보다 한결 즐거워지리라고 나는 믿고 싶다. 릴리는 곧 발모럴 부츠를 손에 넣었다."], ["Lily soon got the Balmoral boots, or, at least, soon learned that the power of getting them as she pleased had devolved upon her from her uncle's gift; so that she talked even of buying the squirrel's cage; but I am not aware that her extravagance led her as far as that.", "아니, 적어도 그것을 제 마음대로 살 수 있는 힘이 삼촌의 선물로 제게 넘어왔다는 것을 곧 알게 되었다. 그래서 그녀는 다람쥐 우리까지 사겠다는 말도 했지만, 그녀의 사치가 거기까지 이르렀다는 이야기는 나도 알지 못한다."]], [["Lord De Courcy we left suffering dreadfully from gout and ill-temper at Courcy Castle. Yes, indeed!", "드 코시 백작은 코시 성에서 통풍과 심술로 지독히 앓고 있는 채로 우리가 두고 왔다."], ["To him in his latter days life did not seem to offer much that was comfortable.", "그렇다, 정말이지! 그의 말년에 삶은 그에게 편안한 것을 그리 많이 내어 주지 않았다."], ["His wife had now gone from him, and declared positively to her son-in-law that no earthly consideration should ever induce her to go back again;--\"not if I were to starve!\"", "아내는 이제 그를 떠나 버렸고, 사위에게 이 세상 무엇을 준다 해도 다시는 돌아가지 않겠노라고 딱 잘라 선언했다—“내가 굶어 죽는 한이 있어도 말이오!”"], ["she said.", "그녀는 말했다."], ["By which she intended to signify that she would be firm in her resolve, even though she should thereby lose her carriage and horses. Poor Mr.", "이 말로 그녀가 뜻한 바는, 그 때문에 제 마차와 말들을 잃게 된다 하더라도 제 결심을 굳게 지키겠다는 것이었다."], ["Gazebee went down to Courcy, and had a dreadful interview with the earl; but matters were at last arranged, and her ladyship remained at Baden-Baden in a state of semi-starvation.", "딱한 게이지비 씨가 코시로 내려가 백작과 지독한 담판을 벌였다. 그러나 일은 마침내 정리되었고, 백작부인은 바덴바덴에 반쯤 굶주린 채로 남았다."], ["That is to say, she had but one horse to her carriage.", "그러니까, 마차에 매는 말이 한 필밖에 없었다는 말이다."]], [["As regards Crosbie, I am inclined to believe that he did again recover his power at his office. He was Mr.", "크로즈비로 말하자면, 나는 그가 제 관청에서 다시 권세를 되찾았으리라고 믿는 쪽이다."], ["Butterwell's master, and the master also of Mr. Optimist, and the major.", "그는 버터웰 씨의 상관이었고, 옵티미스트 씨와 소령의 상관이기도 했다."], ["He knew his business, and could do it, which was more, perhaps, than might fairly be said of any of the other three.", "그는 제 일을 알았고 또 해낼 수 있었으니, 아마 나머지 세 사람 누구에 대해서도 그렇게 말하기는 어려웠을 것이다."], ["Under such circumstances he was sure to get in his hand, and lead again.", "이런 사정이니 그가 일을 제 손에 쥐고 다시 이끌어 가게 되는 것은 뻔한 일이었다."], ["But elsewhere his star did not recover its ascendancy.", "그러나 다른 데서는 그의 별이 예전의 자리를 되찾지 못했다."], ["He dined at his club almost daily, and there were those with whom he habitually formed some little circle.", "그는 거의 날마다 제 클럽에서 저녁을 먹었고, 거기에는 늘 그와 함께 자그마한 무리를 이루는 사람들이 있었다."], ["But he was not the Crosbie of former days,--the Crosbie known in Belgravia and in St. James's Street.", "그러나 그는 지난날의 크로즈비가—벨그레이비어와 세인트제임스 스트리트에서 알려졌던 그 크로즈비가 아니었다."], ["He had taken his little vessel bravely out into the deep waters, and had sailed her well while fortune stuck close to him.", "그는 제 작은 배를 씩씩하게 깊은 바다로 몰고 나가, 운이 제 곁에 붙어 있는 동안에는 배를 잘도 몰았다."], ["But he had forgotten his nautical rules, and success had made him idle.", "그러나 그는 뱃사람의 규칙을 잊었고, 성공이 그를 게으르게 만들었다."], ["His plummet and lead had not been used, and he had kept no look-out ahead.", "그는 측심줄과 추를 쓰지 않았고 앞을 살피지도 않았다."], ["Therefore the first rock he met shivered his bark to pieces.", "그리하여 처음 만난 암초가 그의 배를 산산조각 내고 말았다."], ["His wife, the Lady Alexandrina, is to be seen in the one-horse carriage with her mother at Baden-Baden.", "그의 아내 레이디 알렉산드리나는 바덴바덴에서 제 어머니와 함께 말 한 필이 끄는 마차에 앉아 있는 모습을 볼 수 있다."]]];
